FEAT-005-1-003-R010	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	Rate comparison tool shall display side-by-side analysis of proposed quote against last accepted rate, market average, cost floor, and competitor benchmark rates for the same lane	Rate comparison tool shall display side-by-side analysis of proposed quote against last accepted rate, market average, cost floor, and competitor benchmark rates for the same lane	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R011	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	System shall calculate quote profitability showing estimated margin per TEU, total margin, contribution to fixed costs, and comparison against lane-level target margins	System shall calculate quote profitability showing estimated margin per TEU, total margin, contribution to fixed costs, and comparison against lane-level target margins	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R012	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	Bulk rate upload shall accept carrier tariff sheets in CSV/Excel format with validation against existing rate structures and automatic conflict detection for overlapping validity periods	Bulk rate upload shall accept carrier tariff sheets in CSV/Excel format with validation against existing rate structures and automatic conflict detection for overlapping validity periods	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R013	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	Customer self-service portal integration shall allow quote requests submission, quote review, acceptance, and rejection with digital signature capture for accepted quotes	Customer self-service portal integration shall allow quote requests submission, quote review, acceptance, and rejection with digital signature capture for accepted quotes	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R014	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	AI model shall predict quote acceptance likelihood based on rate competitiveness, customer history, market conditions, and relationship strength to prioritize follow-up efforts	AI model shall predict quote acceptance likelihood based on rate competitiveness, customer history, market conditions, and relationship strength to prioritize follow-up efforts	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R015	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	System shall support multi-currency quotations with real-time exchange rate conversion, configurable rounding rules, and currency hedging cost inclusion in margin calculations	System shall support multi-currency quotations with real-time exchange rate conversion, configurable rounding rules, and currency hedging cost inclusion in margin calculations	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R016	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	Quote-to-booking conversion shall auto-populate booking fields from accepted quotes with rate lock guarantee for bookings made within the quote validity period	Quote-to-booking conversion shall auto-populate booking fields from accepted quotes with rate lock guarantee for bookings made within the quote validity period	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-003-R017	FEAT-005-1-003	MOD-005	API endpoint shall expose quote creation, retrieval, status update, and acceptance workflows with webhook notifications for quote status change events	API endpoint shall expose quote creation, retrieval, status update, and acceptance workflows with webhook notifications for quote status change events	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R001	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	System shall support creation of Master Line Agreements (MLA) with configurable clause library, rate annexures, volume commitments, and validity periods with digital signature workflow	System shall support creation of Master Line Agreements (MLA) with configurable clause library, rate annexures, volume commitments, and validity periods with digital signature workflow	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R002	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	SLA templates shall define measurable service parameters including transit time guarantees, equipment availability commitments, documentation turnaround times, and penalty calculations for non-complia	SLA templates shall define measurable service parameters including transit time guarantees, equipment availability commitments, documentation turnaround times, and penalty calculations for non-compliance	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R003	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Volume commitment tracking shall monitor actual TEU shipments against contracted minimum quantities with real-time utilization dashboards showing monthly and cumulative performance	Volume commitment tracking shall monitor actual TEU shipments against contracted minimum quantities with real-time utilization dashboards showing monthly and cumulative performance	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R004	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Dead freight clause management shall calculate penalties when actual shipments fall below minimum quantity commitments with configurable grace periods, penalty rates, and automatic invoice generation	Dead freight clause management shall calculate penalties when actual shipments fall below minimum quantity commitments with configurable grace periods, penalty rates, and automatic invoice generation	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R005	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	TSA (Transpacific Stabilization Agreement) compliance tracking shall monitor rate floors, surcharge adherence, and filing requirements for applicable trade lanes with deviation alerts	TSA (Transpacific Stabilization Agreement) compliance tracking shall monitor rate floors, surcharge adherence, and filing requirements for applicable trade lanes with deviation alerts	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R006	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	VSA (Vessel Sharing Agreement) management shall track slot allocation, utilization rates, partner carrier performance, and cost sharing calculations across shared vessel services	VSA (Vessel Sharing Agreement) management shall track slot allocation, utilization rates, partner carrier performance, and cost sharing calculations across shared vessel services	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R007	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Contract renewal engine shall generate alerts at configurable intervals (90/60/30 days) before expiration with AI-recommended renewal terms based on performance, market conditions, and customer value	Contract renewal engine shall generate alerts at configurable intervals (90/60/30 days) before expiration with AI-recommended renewal terms based on performance, market conditions, and customer value	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R008	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Amendment management shall support contract modifications with version control, change request workflow, approval chain, and automatic notification to all affected parties	Amendment management shall support contract modifications with version control, change request workflow, approval chain, and automatic notification to all affected parties	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R009	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	System shall maintain a clause library with standard and custom contract clauses categorized by type (liability, indemnity, force majeure, dispute resolution) with version history and legal review sta	System shall maintain a clause library with standard and custom contract clauses categorized by type (liability, indemnity, force majeure, dispute resolution) with version history and legal review status	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R010	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Contract compliance dashboard shall display aggregate metrics including active contract count, total committed volume, utilization rates, SLA adherence scores, and financial exposure from underperform	Contract compliance dashboard shall display aggregate metrics including active contract count, total committed volume, utilization rates, SLA adherence scores, and financial exposure from underperformance	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R011	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	System shall calculate contract profitability by comparing contracted rates against actual costs, surcharge recovery, volume achievement, and dead freight penalties on a per-contract basis	System shall calculate contract profitability by comparing contracted rates against actual costs, surcharge recovery, volume achievement, and dead freight penalties on a per-contract basis	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R012	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	AI model shall predict contract renewal probability based on customer satisfaction scores, volume utilization trends, rate competitiveness, and market alternatives available to the customer	AI model shall predict contract renewal probability based on customer satisfaction scores, volume utilization trends, rate competitiveness, and market alternatives available to the customer	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R013	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Automatic rate escalation engine shall apply contracted annual rate increases, index-linked adjustments, or formula-based recalculations on configured effective dates with advance notification	Automatic rate escalation engine shall apply contracted annual rate increases, index-linked adjustments, or formula-based recalculations on configured effective dates with advance notification	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R014	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Contract document generation shall produce formatted PDF agreements from templates with dynamic field population, digital watermarking, and secure distribution via customer portal or email	Contract document generation shall produce formatted PDF agreements from templates with dynamic field population, digital watermarking, and secure distribution via customer portal or email	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R015	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	System shall enforce contract exclusivity rules preventing overlapping agreements for the same customer-lane combination and flagging conflicts during new contract creation	System shall enforce contract exclusivity rules preventing overlapping agreements for the same customer-lane combination and flagging conflicts during new contract creation	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R016	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	Obligation tracking shall monitor both carrier and customer commitments including equipment provision, cargo delivery schedules, payment terms, and documentation requirements with exception alerts	Obligation tracking shall monitor both carrier and customer commitments including equipment provision, cargo delivery schedules, payment terms, and documentation requirements with exception alerts	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-1-004-R017	FEAT-005-1-004	MOD-005	API endpoint shall expose contract lifecycle operations including creation, amendment, renewal, termination, and compliance queries with role-based access control and audit logging	API endpoint shall expose contract lifecycle operations including creation, amendment, renewal, termination, and compliance queries with role-based access control and audit logging	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R002	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	AI-Driven Customer Health Score Engine	The system must compute a real-time Customer Health Score (0-100) for each account using AI models that analyze at minimum: booking frequency trends over trailing 90 days, payment timeliness (DSO variance from contracted terms), dispute/claim ratio per 100 TEU, service request responsiveness, and NPS survey scores. The scoring engine must weight factors differently per trade lane (e.g., Qatar-India corridor vs. UAE-KSA corridor) and container type mix (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer). Scores below 60 must automatically trigger a retention workflow alert to the assigned relationship manager. The model must retrain monthly on historical churn data and account for seasonal patterns such as Ramadan slowdowns and Indian festive season surges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R011	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Cross-Entity Revenue Recognition and Credit Rules	The system must define clear rules for sales credit attribution on cross-entity transactions where a booking originates in one entity but generates revenue in another. For example, a Qatar-based salesperson securing a booking from Hamad Port (Qatar entity) to JNPT (India entity) must receive primary sales credit in the Qatar entity with optional secondary credit to the India entity's account manager servicing the consignee. Revenue split rules must be configurable per trade lane direction and must handle transshipment scenarios where cargo passes through Jebel Ali as a hub. Double-counting controls must ensure consolidated group revenue reporting is accurate while individual entity P&L statements reflect appropriate revenue allocation. Commission calculations must follow the credit attribution rules with entity-specific incentive plan application.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R003	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Lead Assignment and Distribution Rules Engine	The system must automatically assign qualified leads to salespeople based on configurable distribution rules including territory alignment (geographic zone, port catchment area), industry specialization (oil/gas, FMCG, automotive, chemicals per salesperson expertise), account size tier matching (enterprise leads to senior sales, SMB leads to inside sales), round-robin rotation within matched pools for load balancing, and language preference matching (Arabic-speaking leads to Arabic-proficient salespeople). Assignment must occur within 5 minutes of lead qualification. The system must enforce maximum lead queue limits per salesperson (configurable, default 50 active leads) to prevent overload. Unaccepted leads must auto-escalate to the team manager after a configurable period (default 4 hours). Lead reassignment history must be fully auditable. For leads originating from joint marketing with port authorities, co-assignment rules must route to both the port-specific and trade-lane-specific salespeople.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R013	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Request Analytics for Customers	The portal must provide customers with analytics on their rate inquiry patterns including total requests submitted per month, average quotation acceptance rate, most frequently requested trade lanes, average rate per TEU trend over time, and comparison of accepted rates versus initial AI suggestions. Charts must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with locale-appropriate number formatting (Arabic-Indic numerals for Arabic UI, Western numerals for English UI). The analytics dashboard must allow date range filtering using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and must support data export to PDF with the customer's company branding and the entity's commercial footer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R004	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Billing Discrepancy Dispute Resolution	The system must allow customers to dispute specific line items on an invoice by selecting the invoice number, highlighting the disputed charges, entering the expected amount, and providing justification. The system must automatically compare the disputed invoice against the original rate quotation, the booking confirmation, and the bill of lading to identify discrepancies in freight charges, surcharges, currency conversion rates, or quantity billed versus quantity shipped. AI must categorize the discrepancy as a system error, rate application error, surcharge miscalculation, or exchange rate variance, and must auto-resolve discrepancies below USD 100 equivalent where the system error is confirmed. For each dispute, the system must generate a reconciliation report showing the original quoted amounts alongside the invoiced amounts with variance highlighted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R010	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Document Readiness Notifications	The system must send notifications when shipping documents are ready for customer action, including: draft bill of lading ready for review, original bill of lading released, telex release confirmed, delivery order issued, arrival notice generated, customs clearance certificate available, and certificate of origin ready for collection. Each document notification must include a secure one-click link that takes the customer directly to the document viewer in the portal without requiring re-authentication (using a time-limited signed URL valid for 72 hours). The notification must specify if the customer needs to take any action (approve, sign, collect) and the deadline for that action, with automated follow-up reminders if the action is not completed within 50% of the deadline period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R002	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Registration Invite-Only Signup	New user registration via invite link only (no open signup). Invite token (UUID v4) expires in 72h. Registration form collects: full name, work email, password (min 12 chars, complexity check), phone number. Send verification email on submit.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R003	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Contract Lifecycle Management with Multi-Currency Terms	The system must support end-to-end contract lifecycle management for freight service agreements including creation, negotiation, approval, execution, amendment, and renewal. Each contract must define rate schedules in the customer's preferred billing currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with automatic cross-currency conversion at booking time using daily Central Bank rates. Contracts must specify INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP) per trade lane, demurrage/detention free-time allowances per port, and container type rate differentials. Amendment workflows must require dual approval for rate changes exceeding 5% of base rates. Hijri calendar dates must be supported alongside Gregorian for KSA-domiciled contracts, with automatic date synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R012	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Performance Review Workflow and Documentation	The system must support structured performance review cycles (monthly check-ins, quarterly reviews, annual appraisals) with pre-populated performance data pulled from sales dashboards. Review templates must include quantitative metrics (target vs. actual across all KPI dimensions), qualitative assessments (customer relationship quality, cross-selling effectiveness, market intelligence contribution), and development objectives. The review workflow must route through the salesperson, direct manager, and skip-level manager with sign-off at each stage. Completed reviews must be stored as immutable records for HR compliance. The system must support Arabic and English review narratives with separate text fields for bilingual documentation where required by local labor law. Performance Improvement Plans (PIPs) must be trackable with milestone deadlines and automated progress check reminders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R004	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Marketing Campaign Planning and Budget Management	The system must support end-to-end marketing campaign planning with campaign types including trade shows, digital advertising, email marketing, content marketing, webinars, print media, and port authority co-marketing events. Each campaign must have a defined budget in the planning entity's currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent for consolidated reporting. Budget allocation must support line-item breakdown by expense category (venue, creative, media spend, travel, collateral production). Multi-entity campaigns must allow budget sharing with configurable contribution ratios per entity. Campaign calendars must display in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with awareness of regional event calendars (Ramadan, National Day celebrations across all four countries, major trade shows like ADIPEC, Qatar Maritime, India Maritime Week). Budget approval workflows must route based on total campaign value with entity-specific authorization limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R014	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Request API Integration for Enterprise Customers	The system must expose a RESTful API allowing enterprise customers with approved API access to programmatically submit rate requests, retrieve quotations, and accept or reject rates. The API must enforce OAuth 2.0 authentication with customer-specific API keys, rate limiting at 100 requests per minute per customer, and payload validation matching the portal's business rules. API responses must include rate details in the requested currency with the same surcharge breakdown available in the portal. The API must return responses in JSON format with bilingual field labels (English keys and optional Arabic display names) and must support webhook callbacks for asynchronous rate quotation delivery notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R005	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Acknowledgment and SLA Enforcement	The system must send an automated acknowledgment to the customer within 15 minutes of complaint submission, including the case reference number, assigned priority level (Critical, High, Medium, Low), expected resolution timeframe based on the complaint category, and the name of the assigned case handler. SLA timelines must be entity-specific and category-specific: Critical complaints (cargo damage with perishable goods) must have a 4-business-hour initial response, High priority (billing disputes above USD 10,000) within 1 business day, Medium priority within 3 business days, and Low priority within 5 business days. Business hours must follow entity-specific calendars accounting for Friday-Saturday weekends in GCC and Saturday-Sunday weekends in India, and must exclude public holidays including Hijri-calendar based Islamic holidays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R011	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification Delivery Tracking and Retry Logic	The system must track the delivery status of every notification sent, recording: queued timestamp, sent timestamp, delivery confirmation (email delivered, SMS delivered, push notification received, WhatsApp read receipt), open/click tracking for email notifications, and any failure reasons (invalid email, phone unreachable, WhatsApp number not registered). Failed notifications must trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff: first retry after 5 minutes, second after 15 minutes, third after 60 minutes. After three failed delivery attempts on the primary channel, the system must attempt delivery via the customer's secondary fallback channel. All delivery tracking data must be stored for 12 months and must be accessible to customer support staff for troubleshooting notification-related inquiries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R016	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Offline Tariff Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to published tariff schedules for field sales personnel operating in areas with limited connectivity. The offline module must cache the most recent active tariffs for the user's assigned trade lanes and entity on the local device with AES-256 encryption. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any tariff updates bidirectionally, applying conflict resolution rules where the server version takes precedence for published tariffs. The offline cache must include the full tariff detail including rate breakdowns, surcharges, and validity dates. Cache refresh must occur automatically when connectivity is detected, with a maximum staleness threshold of 24 hours before the user is warned about potentially outdated rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R001	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Type Master Data Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive master registry of all surcharge types applicable to container shipping operations. Standard surcharge types must include Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF), Terminal Handling Charges (THC), Peak Season Surcharge (PSS), Emergency Bunker Surcharge (EBS), General Rate Increase (GRI), War Risk Surcharge (WRS), Piracy Surcharge, Suez Canal Transit Surcharge, Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS), Congestion Surcharge, and Documentation Fee. Each surcharge type must have a unique code, bilingual name (Arabic/English), calculation method (fixed amount per container, percentage of freight, per TEU, per bill of lading), and applicability rules. Custom surcharge types must be creatable by authorized users for entity-specific or trade-lane-specific needs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R004	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Password Reset Flow	Forgot password: email form, send reset link (signed JWT, 1h TTL), reset form with new password and confirm, invalidate all active sessions, redirect to login. Rate-limit reset requests: max 3 per email per hour.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R004	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Automated Churn Risk Prediction and Intervention	The system must deploy an AI churn prediction model that identifies accounts at risk of attrition at least 45 days before projected volume decline. The model must analyze booking pattern deviations, rate competitiveness versus market indices (Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, Drewry WCI), outstanding receivables aging, customer complaint escalation frequency, and competitor activity signals from market intelligence feeds. When churn probability exceeds 70%, the system must auto-generate a retention action plan including recommended rate adjustments, service upgrades (priority vessel allocation, guaranteed equipment availability), and relationship manager meeting scheduling. The intervention workflow must track success rates per action type across all four operating entities and feed outcomes back into the prediction model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R013	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Win/Loss Analysis and Competitive Deal Intelligence	The system must capture win/loss outcomes for every qualified opportunity with mandatory fields for outcome reason (price, service quality, transit time, equipment availability, carrier reputation, personal relationship), competing carrier name, estimated competitor rate if known, and customer verbatim feedback. AI must analyze win/loss patterns across dimensions including trade lane, container type, customer segment, deal size, and salesperson to identify systemic issues and competitive vulnerabilities. Monthly win/loss reports must highlight trade lanes where win rates fall below 40% for management intervention. The analysis must correlate with market rate indices to distinguish price-driven losses from service-driven losses. Reports must be filterable by port pair (e.g., all opportunities involving Khalifa Port) and must render correctly in Arabic RTL for regional managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R005	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Email Marketing Campaign Execution and Compliance	The system must support email marketing campaign creation, scheduling, and execution with template management for bilingual Arabic/English email designs. The template editor must support RTL layout for Arabic content, embedded dynamic fields (recipient name, company, preferred port, relevant trade lanes), and responsive design for mobile rendering. Campaign scheduling must account for optimal send times per region (respecting Friday weekend in Qatar/UAE/KSA, Sunday weekend in India). The system must enforce compliance with anti-spam regulations including UAE Telecommunications Regulatory Authority (TRA) requirements, India's IT Act provisions, and international CAN-SPAM/GDPR requirements for contacts with European connections. Unsubscribe mechanisms must be provided in both Arabic and English. Bounce handling must automatically update lead contact records and flag invalid email addresses. Campaign analytics must track open rates, click-through rates, and conversion to lead/opportunity per campaign.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R015	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Request SLA Monitoring and Escalation	The system must enforce configurable service level agreements for rate request response times: spot rate responses within 4 business hours, contract rate responses within 2 business days. Business hours must be entity-specific (Qatar: Sunday-Thursday 07:00-17:00 AST, UAE: Sunday-Thursday 08:00-18:00 GST, KSA: Sunday-Thursday 08:00-17:00 AST, India: Monday-Friday 09:00-18:00 IST). If a rate request approaches 80% of its SLA window without a response, the system must auto-escalate to the commercial team lead. At 100% SLA breach, escalation must reach the commercial manager with a mandatory action required flag. SLA metrics must be tracked on the customer dashboard showing the entity's average response time and SLA compliance percentage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R006	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Escalation Matrix	The system must implement a configurable multi-tier escalation matrix that automatically escalates unresolved complaints based on elapsed time and severity. Tier 1 is the assigned customer service agent (initial handler), Tier 2 is the team lead (auto-escalated at 75% of SLA), Tier 3 is the department manager (auto-escalated at 100% of SLA), and Tier 4 is the country general manager (auto-escalated at 150% of SLA). Each escalation must generate a notification to the escalation recipient with the full case history, and must update the customer via their preferred communication channel that the case has been escalated. Manual escalation must be available to customers at any time via a prominently displayed escalation button on the complaint detail screen, with escalation reasons captured for process improvement analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R012	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Custom Alert Rules Engine	The system must provide an advanced alert rules engine allowing customers to create custom notification rules using a visual rule builder with conditions and actions. Conditions must include shipment attributes (origin port, destination port, container type, commodity, customer reference), event types, threshold values (delay hours, amount thresholds, weight limits), and logical operators (AND, OR, NOT). Actions must include sending notifications to specified channels, triggering webhooks to external systems, and creating tasks in the customer's project management tools via API. Each customer may create a maximum of 50 custom rules. The rule builder must validate rule logic to prevent conflicting or circular rules and must display a plain-language summary of each rule in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R001	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Creation with Customer Binding	The system must allow authorized commercial users to create rate agreements linked to specific customer accounts from the CRM module. Each rate agreement must capture the customer name and code, agreement effective and expiry dates (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), applicable trade lanes, container types, commodity scope, minimum quantity commitments (TEU-based), and the originating entity. The agreement must support binding to a parent customer account with optional extension to subsidiary accounts. AI must pre-populate agreement templates based on the customer's historical shipping patterns and suggest optimal rate levels that balance competitiveness with profitability targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R002	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	FIATA FBL Compliant Document Generation	The system must generate Multimodal Transport Documents in FIATA FBL (FIATA Multimodal Transport Bill of Lading) format with all mandatory fields per FIATA Document Model 2023. The document must include the FIATA logo and FBL serial number from the entity's allocated FIATA number range. Printing must support both original negotiable copies (typically 3 originals) and non-negotiable copies. The FBL must include ICC Uniform Rules for Multimodal Transport Documents reference, carrier liability clauses, and jurisdiction clause specifying applicable law (Qatar Civil Code, UAE Federal Law, Saudi Carriage of Goods regulations, or Indian Multimodal Transportation of Goods Act 1993). Digital watermarking must be applied to distinguish originals from copies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R005	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Service Level Agreement (SLA) Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time SLA tracking dashboard per customer account measuring agreed KPIs including transit time adherence per port pair (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT within 5-day commitment), equipment availability fulfillment rate, documentation turnaround time (Bill of Lading issuance within 24 hours of vessel sailing), customs clearance facilitation time at destination, and claim resolution SLA (acknowledgment within 4 hours, resolution within 14 days). Dashboard must render in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with identical data fidelity. SLA breach events must trigger automated escalation notifications via email and SMS with breach severity classification (minor, major, critical). Historical SLA performance trends must be exportable as PDF reports for quarterly business reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R014	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Incentive Payout Processing and Finance Integration	The system must generate approved incentive payout files for integration with entity payroll systems. Payout processing must align with entity-specific pay cycles: monthly for UAE (WPS compliant file format), monthly for KSA (GOSI-reportable), monthly for Qatar (per Qatar Labor Law), and monthly for India (with TDS calculation per Income Tax Act Section 192). The system must generate payout approval packages with commission statement detail, manager attestation, and finance controller sign-off before payroll submission. Currency-specific rounding rules must apply (QAR/SAR to 2 decimal places, INR to nearest rupee, AED to 2 decimal places). Retroactive adjustments for booking cancellations or late clawbacks must generate adjustment entries in the subsequent pay period. Full payout audit trails must be maintained for 7 years per regional retention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R006	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Trade Show and Event Management Module	The system must manage trade show participation lifecycle including event registration, booth booking, collateral planning, staff scheduling, lead capture during the event, and post-event follow-up workflow. Pre-event tasks must include target attendee list import with pre-show outreach campaign scheduling. During-event lead capture must support mobile badge scanning, business card OCR (Arabic and English), and quick-entry lead forms optimized for tablet use on trade show floors. Post-event workflows must trigger within 24 hours of event close, assigning captured leads to salespeople with event context and conversation notes. The system must track event ROI by comparing total event cost (in event currency converted to entity functional currency) against revenue generated from event-sourced leads within 6 and 12 month attribution windows. Key industry events at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT must be pre-loaded in the event calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R016	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Offline Rate Request Drafting	The portal must support offline mode where customers with intermittent connectivity can draft rate requests using a locally cached form with the most recent master data snapshot (port codes, container types, commodity list). Drafted requests must be stored in the browser's IndexedDB with AES-256 encryption and must automatically synchronize when connectivity is restored. The system must handle conflict resolution if master data has changed during the offline period by validating the draft against current data and highlighting any fields that need customer review. A visual indicator must clearly show the customer whether they are in online or offline mode, with the offline banner displayed in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R007	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Document Upload and Evidence Management	The complaint system must support uploading evidence documents in PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, DOCX, and XLSX formats with a maximum file size of 25MB per file and a maximum of 30 files per complaint case. All uploaded documents must be virus-scanned before being attached to the case record. The system must apply OCR to scanned documents and use AI to extract key data points such as invoice numbers, amounts, dates, and container numbers from uploaded evidence to auto-populate dispute fields. Documents must be stored with AES-256 encryption at rest and must be retained for a minimum of 7 years per regional regulatory requirements (Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 50/2022, Saudi Commercial Court Law, Indian Limitation Act). Each document must maintain a chain-of-custody log recording upload timestamp, uploader identity, and any access events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R013	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Regulatory and Compliance Notification Handling	The system must send mandatory compliance notifications that cannot be disabled or muted by customers, including: customs examination hold notifications requiring customer presence or documentation, port health authority inspection notices for food cargo, dangerous goods incident notifications as required by IMDG Code, trade sanction screening alerts for flagged transactions, and regulatory deadline reminders for customs bond renewals or license expirations. These notifications must be delivered through all active channels simultaneously (not just the preferred channel) and must include a read confirmation request that the customer must acknowledge. Unacknowledged compliance notifications must trigger escalation to the customer's designated compliance officer within 4 hours and must be flagged to the entity's regulatory compliance team within 8 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R002	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Volume Commitment Tiers and Graduated Pricing	The system must support multi-tier volume commitment structures within rate agreements where rates decrease as committed volume tiers are achieved. A minimum of five volume tiers must be configurable per agreement with each tier specifying a TEU range (minimum and maximum) and the corresponding rate per container type. The system must track actual shipment volumes against committed tiers in real-time and automatically apply the correct tier rate at the time of booking. Volume accumulation must be trackable on monthly, quarterly, and annual periods with carry-forward rules configurable per agreement. AI must forecast tier achievement probability based on current booking trends and alert commercial teams when customers are at risk of not meeting minimum commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R002	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Rate Definition and Effective Date Management	The system must allow definition of surcharge rates with precise effective date control supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates. Each surcharge rate entry must specify the surcharge type, applicable trade lane or port scope (origin, destination, or both), container type applicability, rate amount or percentage, currency, effective start date, and optional expiry date. The system must support future-dated surcharge rates that automatically activate on the effective date without manual intervention. Only one active rate per surcharge type per scope combination must be allowed at any time. When a new rate supersedes an existing one, the previous rate must be automatically expired at the end of the day before the new rate's effective date, maintaining a complete historical record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R006	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Customer Communication History and Interaction Log	The system must maintain a chronological interaction log for each account capturing all communications including emails, phone calls, meeting notes, WhatsApp messages (via API integration), and system-generated notifications. Each interaction must be tagged with subject category (rate negotiation, booking issue, documentation request, complaint, general inquiry), associated entity, port reference, and booking/BL numbers where applicable. Arabic-language communications must be stored in original script with optional AI-generated English translations. The interaction log must support full-text search across all communication types in both Arabic and English. Relationship managers must be able to set follow-up reminders with Hijri or Gregorian date selections. Offline-created interaction notes must sync automatically when connectivity is restored with conflict resolution for concurrent edits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R015	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Coaching and AI-Recommended Next Best Actions	The system must provide AI-generated coaching recommendations to sales managers based on individual salesperson performance patterns. Recommendations must include suggested accounts for each salesperson to prioritize based on revenue potential and churn risk, trade lanes where the salesperson has demonstrated strength versus areas needing development, optimal visit frequency per account tier, and quotation strategy guidance based on historical win rates at different price points. For each salesperson, the system must generate a weekly priority action list of the top 10 recommended activities ranked by expected revenue impact. Coaching recommendations must respect cultural norms and communication preferences across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India markets. The recommendation engine must explain its reasoning in natural language summaries available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R007	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Lead Nurturing Workflow Automation	The system must support automated lead nurturing workflows that deliver sequenced communications based on lead behavior and stage progression. Nurturing sequences must be configurable per lead segment (by industry, trade lane interest, company size, and geographic region). Content must include educational materials about shipping services, port capability highlights (Hamad Port expansion updates, Jebel Ali capacity, JNPT dedicated freight corridor benefits, Khalifa Port free zone advantages), rate trend insights, and case studies relevant to the lead's industry. Sequence timing must respect business day calendars per the lead's country with Hijri calendar awareness for KSA leads. The system must detect engagement signals (email opens, link clicks, website revisits) and accelerate or modify the nurturing sequence accordingly. Leads that achieve MQL threshold during nurturing must automatically exit the nurture flow and enter the sales pipeline. Nurturing content must be maintainable in parallel Arabic and English versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R001	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Real-Time Container Inventory Query	The system must allow customers to query available empty containers by specifying the pickup port (QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP, SAKAP, SAJED, SADAM), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR, 45HC), desired pickup date, and quantity required. The query must return the current count of available units at the specified depot, their condition grade (A: cargo-worthy, B: minor damage, C: requires repair), and the last inspection date. Results must refresh every 15 minutes from the depot management system feed and must display a timestamp showing the data freshness in the customer's local timezone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R008	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Resolution Workflow and Outcome Recording	The system must support the following resolution outcomes for each complaint: Resolved in Customer's Favor (full), Resolved in Customer's Favor (partial), Resolved Against Customer, Withdrawn by Customer, and Referred to External Arbitration. For financial resolutions, the system must capture the approved compensation amount, currency, payment method (credit note, refund to original payment method, or adjustment on next invoice), and the authorizing manager's approval with digital signature. Resolutions involving credit notes must automatically interface with the billing module to generate the credit note with proper VAT treatment per entity-specific tax rules (Qatar: 0% VAT, UAE: 5% VAT, KSA: 15% VAT, India: 18% GST). The customer must receive the resolution notification in their preferred language with a detailed explanation of the outcome.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R014	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification History and Search	The customer portal must maintain a complete notification history showing all notifications sent to the customer and their organizational users over the past 12 months. The history must be searchable by date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors), notification type, shipment reference, delivery channel, and delivery status. Each notification entry must show the rendered content as it was sent, the delivery status with timestamps, and for email notifications, the open and click tracking data. Customers must be able to re-send any previously sent notification to a different channel or email address. The notification history must support pagination with 50 entries per page and must export to CSV format with proper Unicode encoding for Arabic text content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R003	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Multi-Entity Rate Agreement Support	The system must support rate agreements that span multiple operating entities, allowing a single agreement to cover shipments originating from or destined to ports across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Multi-entity agreements must define entity-specific rate components (such as local charges in local currency) while maintaining a unified base ocean freight rate in a common currency (typically USD). Each entity's share of the agreement must be subject to that entity's approval workflow independently. Revenue allocation rules between entities must be defined within the agreement, supporting both fixed-percentage and activity-based allocation methods. The system must generate entity-specific agreement extracts for local compliance and record-keeping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R003	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Bunker Adjustment Factor Calculation Engine	The system must provide an automated BAF calculation engine that derives bunker surcharge rates based on fuel price indices. The engine must support configurable fuel price index sources (such as Singapore MOPS, Rotterdam Platts, Fujairah delivered price) with automatic or manual price feed integration. BAF calculation formulas must be configurable per trade lane and must account for vessel fuel consumption rates, voyage distance, and the applicable fuel type (VLSFO, LSMGO) in compliance with IMO 2020 sulphur regulations. The system must support both fixed BAF (set amount per period) and floating BAF (adjusted with each fuel price change) calculation methods. AI must predict BAF trends based on oil price forecasting models and alert commercial teams to significant upcoming changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R007	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Key Account Program Tier Classification	The system must support a configurable key account tiering framework with at minimum four tiers: Platinum, Gold, Silver, and Standard. Tier assignment must be computed automatically based on weighted criteria including annual TEU volume across all entities, revenue contribution in USD equivalent, payment reliability score, trade lane diversity (number of unique port pairs), and strategic alignment score (manually assessed by commercial director). Each tier must define entitlements such as dedicated relationship manager allocation, priority booking windows, preferential demurrage free-time, rate lock periods, and complimentary VAS (value-added services) quotas. Tier reassessment must occur quarterly with Hijri quarter support for KSA accounts. Tier upgrade/downgrade must require approval from the regional commercial head before customer notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R016	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Team Capacity Planning and Headcount Modeling	The system must provide capacity planning tools that model the relationship between sales team size, territory coverage, and revenue targets per entity. The model must calculate optimal salesperson-to-account ratios per segment (enterprise: 1:15, mid-market: 1:40, SMB: 1:100 configurable benchmarks), project revenue impact of adding or removing headcount per territory based on historical productivity curves, and identify under-served territories where account coverage is below threshold. The tool must factor in ramp-up periods for new hires (configurable by entity, typically 3-6 months to full productivity) and seasonal demand patterns per port. Scenario modeling must support what-if analysis for team restructuring, territory realignment, or market expansion into new trade lanes. Output reports must present financial projections in entity local currencies with consolidated USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R008	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Marketing Content Management and Asset Library	The system must provide a centralized content management repository for marketing assets including brochures, case studies, rate cards, service guides, port information sheets, vessel schedule highlights, and promotional videos. Each asset must have metadata tags for language (Arabic, English, bilingual), target market (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, regional, global), customer segment (shipper, freight forwarder, NVOCC), trade lane, and content type. Assets must support version control with approval workflows for new content publication. The library must enforce brand compliance by restricting template modifications to authorized marketing staff while allowing sales teams to generate personalized versions with customer-specific data. Arabic content must be reviewed for linguistic accuracy and cultural appropriateness by designated reviewers. Asset usage analytics must track which content is shared most frequently and correlate with lead conversion rates. The system must support offline download of frequently used assets for sales teams visiting customers at port locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R002	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Availability Forecast	The AI engine must provide a 14-day forward-looking availability forecast for each container type at each supported port, based on historical reposition patterns, inbound vessel schedules, expected empty returns from import customers, and planned repair cycles. The forecast must display a daily projected count with a confidence band (low/expected/high) and must highlight dates where availability is predicted to drop below the customer's requested quantity. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actual availability, and the system must display the model's historical accuracy percentage for each port-equipment combination to help customers assess reliability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R009	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Customer Satisfaction Survey Post-Resolution	Upon complaint resolution, the system must automatically trigger a customer satisfaction survey delivered via the customer's preferred channel within 24 hours of case closure. The survey must include a numeric rating scale (1-5) for overall satisfaction, resolution timeliness, communication quality, and outcome fairness, plus an optional free-text comment field supporting both Arabic and English input. Survey responses must be linked to the complaint case for trend analysis. If the customer rates overall satisfaction at 1 or 2, the system must automatically reopen the case for management review and flag it as a potential service recovery opportunity. Aggregate satisfaction scores must be available on entity-level dashboards with monthly trend reporting and benchmark comparisons across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R015	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	WhatsApp Business Integration for Notifications	The system must integrate with WhatsApp Business API to deliver shipping notifications as structured WhatsApp messages using pre-approved message templates registered with Meta. Templates must be created and approved in both Arabic and English, with dynamic parameter insertion for shipment-specific data. The integration must support interactive message types including quick reply buttons (e.g., Approve Draft BL / Request Changes) and list messages for selecting from multiple options. Customer opt-in and opt-out for WhatsApp notifications must comply with Meta's Business Messaging Policy and regional data privacy regulations. The system must handle WhatsApp-specific constraints including the 24-hour messaging window, template message requirements outside the window, and rate limiting per phone number to avoid account suspension.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R004	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Approval Workflow with Profitability Checks	The system must enforce an approval workflow for rate agreements that includes automated profitability analysis before submission for approval. The profitability check must calculate the estimated margin for the agreement based on the proposed rates, estimated costs (vessel slot cost, terminal handling, feeder costs, agency fees), and projected volumes. Agreements falling below entity-specific minimum margin thresholds must be escalated to senior management for approval. The approval chain must be dynamically determined based on the total contract value (annual projected revenue) and the deviation percentage from standard tariff rates. Approvers must receive bilingual (Arabic/English) summary reports with margin analysis and AI-generated risk assessment scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R004	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Currency Adjustment Factor Management	The system must manage Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF) surcharges to compensate for exchange rate fluctuations between the billing currency and the carrier's functional currency. CAF calculation must reference exchange rates from a configurable source (central bank rates, market rates) and must apply when the exchange rate deviation exceeds a configurable threshold (default: 3% from the base rate). The CAF rate must be recalculable on a configurable schedule (weekly, monthly, or quarterly) per entity. For multi-currency operations across Qatar (QAR), UAE (AED), KSA (SAR), and India (INR), the system must maintain separate CAF schedules per currency pair. CAF must be automatically excluded from rate agreements that already include exchange rate adjustment clauses to prevent double-counting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R003	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Transport Leg Segment Tracking on MBL	The system must track each transport segment referenced on the Multimodal Bill of Lading with distinct status updates: origin pickup (road), origin ICD receipt, rail loading, rail transit, rail arrival at port ICD, port terminal receipt, vessel loading, sea transit, discharge port arrival, destination ICD rail loading, destination rail transit, destination ICD arrival, and last-mile delivery. Each segment must record actual dates/times alongside planned dates with variance calculation. Status updates must propagate to all linked documents including the MBL, individual segment transport documents, and customer-facing tracking portals. The system must support both push notifications (via webhook/API) and pull-based status queries from external systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R005	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	TOTP Multi-Factor Authentication	MFA enrollment: generate TOTP secret (RFC 6238), display QR code for Google Authenticator or Authy, require 2 consecutive valid codes to confirm enrollment. Store encrypted secret. On login: prompt TOTP after password. Backup codes: generate 10 one-time codes on enrollment, allow regeneration with MFA confirmation.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R008	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Automated Contract Renewal and Rate Renegotiation Workflow	The system must initiate automated contract renewal workflows 60 days before expiry, generating AI-recommended rate proposals based on historical volume commitments versus actuals, current market rate benchmarks per trade lane, customer health score, and competitive intelligence. The renewal proposal must present rate comparisons in the contract's base currency with optional display in USD equivalent. For multi-entity customers, the system must support consolidated master service agreement renewals spanning Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations with entity-specific annexures. The workflow must route through configurable approval matrices based on total contract value thresholds (e.g., above USD 500K requires C-level approval). Auto-renewal clauses with pre-agreed escalation percentages must be supported with regulatory compliance checks per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R001	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Multi-Entity Onboarding Application Form with Dynamic Fields	The system must provide a digital onboarding application form that dynamically adjusts required fields based on the selected operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and customer type (shipper, consignee, freight forwarder, NVOCC, customs broker). For Qatar entities, the form must collect Commercial Registration number, QID of authorized signatories, and Qatar Chamber of Commerce membership. For UAE, it must collect trade license number, Emirates ID, and Dubai Chamber membership where applicable. For KSA, it must collect CR number, Saudization certificate, and ZATCA VAT registration. For India, it must collect IEC code, PAN, GST registration, and AD code. The form must render in Arabic and English with RTL support, validate all mandatory fields before submission, and save progress for incomplete applications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R009	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Marketing ROI Analytics and Attribution Dashboard	The system must provide comprehensive marketing ROI analytics calculating return on investment per campaign, channel, entity, and time period. ROI calculations must track the full funnel from marketing spend (in campaign currency converted to USD for cross-entity comparison) through lead generation, MQL conversion, opportunity creation, booking confirmation, and first-year revenue attribution. Attribution models must support configurable methodologies: first-touch, last-touch, linear, time-decay, and AI-recommended position-based models. The dashboard must display cost-per-lead (CPL), cost-per-MQL, cost-per-opportunity, and cost-per-acquisition (CPA) metrics segmented by channel, entity, trade lane, and customer segment. Year-over-year trend analysis must be available in both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal year views. Benchmark comparisons against industry averages for container shipping marketing must be maintained. All currency displays must respect the viewing user's entity preference with toggle to USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R003	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Multi-Port Availability Comparison	Customers must be able to compare container availability across multiple ports simultaneously by selecting up to four ports and a container type. The comparison view must display a tabular summary with columns for each selected port showing available units, nearest vessel departure date, estimated positioning cost if containers need to be repositioned from another port, and estimated transit time from each port to the customer's intended destination. The view must support both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with proper column mirroring and must highlight the port offering the best combination of availability and cost using an AI-weighted scoring algorithm.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R010	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	AI-Powered Complaint Pattern Detection	The AI engine must continuously analyze complaint data across all entities to identify recurring patterns such as repeated cargo damage on specific trade lanes or vessels, systematic billing errors for certain surcharge types, chronic detention issues at specific ports, or clusters of complaints from specific customer segments. When a pattern is detected with statistical significance (p-value less than 0.05 based on chi-squared testing against baseline rates), the system must generate a proactive alert to the relevant department manager with a pattern summary, affected customers, estimated financial impact, and recommended corrective actions. Pattern detection must run on a rolling 90-day window with daily batch processing and must generate reports in both Arabic and English for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R016	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification Preferences Data Privacy and Consent	The system must collect and store explicit opt-in consent for each notification channel per customer user, recording the consent timestamp, IP address, and the specific notification categories consented to. Consent records must comply with applicable data privacy regulations including Qatar Data Privacy Law No. 13 of 2016, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021 on Personal Data Protection, Saudi Personal Data Protection Law (PDPL), and India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023. Customers must be able to withdraw consent for any notification channel at any time through a one-click unsubscribe mechanism in every notification and via the portal's preference center. Consent withdrawal must take effect within 24 hours and must trigger a confirmation message via an alternative channel. The system must maintain a consent audit log for 7 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-004-R010	FEAT-006-1-004	MOD-006	Automated Payment Reminders	AI-driven payment reminder system sends escalating notifications as invoices approach and pass due dates.	must_have			["Reminders are sent at: 7 days before due date (courtesy), on due date (first reminder), 7 days overdue (second reminder), 14 days overdue (final notice)", "Reminder escalation is configurable per entity; final notice includes late payment interest calculation and potential service suspension warning", "Reminders are sent in customer's preferred language (English or Arabic) via email with PDF statement attachment", "AI suppresses reminders for invoices with active disputes or pending credit notes to avoid incorrect dunning"]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R009	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Customer Portfolio Analytics and Revenue Attribution	The system must provide portfolio-level analytics for relationship managers showing total managed revenue in local and USD equivalent currencies, TEU volume trends with year-over-year comparison, revenue concentration risk (Herfindahl index per portfolio), trade lane distribution heat maps across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and container type mix analysis (dry, reefer, OOG, tank). Revenue attribution must allocate shared revenue from co-loaded or transshipment containers to originating and destination account managers proportionally. Analytics must support filtering by Hijri and Gregorian calendar periods, entity, port, trade lane, and container type. Export functionality must generate Excel and PDF reports with Arabic headers and right-to-left number formatting when Arabic locale is selected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R002	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	AI-Powered Document Verification and OCR Processing	The system must employ AI-based Optical Character Recognition (OCR) to extract and verify information from uploaded onboarding documents including trade licenses, commercial registration certificates, VAT/GST certificates, authorized signatory identification documents (QID, Emirates ID, Iqama, Aadhaar/PAN), and bank account verification letters. The OCR engine must support both Arabic and English text extraction with minimum 95% accuracy for printed documents. Extracted data must auto-populate corresponding application fields for human review and confirmation. The system must flag discrepancies between OCR-extracted data and manually entered data (e.g., company name mismatch between trade license and application form). Document authenticity checks must validate against known templates for each jurisdiction's official documents. Processing must complete within 60 seconds per document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R010	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Social Media Integration and Social Selling Support	The system must integrate with social media platforms (LinkedIn, Twitter/X, Instagram) to capture lead interactions and support social selling workflows. LinkedIn integration must track company page follower growth, post engagement metrics, and InMail response rates. The system must identify prospects engaging with company content and auto-create lead records with social profile enrichment. Social listening capabilities must monitor shipping industry conversations mentioning the company, competitor carriers, or key trade lanes (Gulf-India, Gulf-GCC, India-GCC routes). Salespeople must be able to share approved marketing content directly to their social profiles from within the CRM with tracking links for attribution. The system must flag leads who engage across multiple social channels for priority routing. Social media analytics must aggregate engagement metrics across all four entity social accounts with entity-specific and consolidated views. Content scheduling must respect cultural sensitivities during religious observances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R004	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Reefer Container Availability with Pre-Trip Inspection Status	For refrigerated container queries (20RF, 40RF), the system must display additional attributes beyond standard availability: PTI (Pre-Trip Inspection) status (passed, pending, failed), PTI date, temperature range capability (standard -25°C to +25°C or deep freeze -60°C), genset availability at the pickup depot, and estimated PTI completion date for units currently in inspection queue. Customers requesting reefer containers for pharmaceutical cargo must be shown only units with pharma-grade certification (GDP-compliant) and calibrated data loggers. The system must enforce a minimum 72-hour lead time for reefer container pickup to ensure PTI completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R011	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Communication Audit Trail	Every communication related to a complaint case must be logged in an immutable audit trail including email correspondence, portal messages, phone call summaries (entered manually by agents), SMS notifications, and WhatsApp messages. Each entry must record the sender, recipient, timestamp with timezone, communication channel, and full content. The audit trail must be viewable by the customer showing only customer-facing communications, while internal notes and communications must be visible only to staff with appropriate role-based access. The audit trail must support full-text search in both Arabic and English and must be exportable as a chronological PDF report for use in external arbitration or legal proceedings, formatted with bilingual headers and entity-specific legal disclaimers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R017	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification Load Testing and Throttling	The notification delivery system must support a throughput of at least 10,000 notifications per minute across all channels combined, with automatic throttling to prevent channel-specific rate limits from being exceeded (SMS gateway limits, email provider sending limits, WhatsApp rate limits). During peak events such as a major vessel delay affecting hundreds of customers simultaneously, the system must prioritize notifications by severity (critical before informational) and by customer tier (premium customers before standard). The system must implement circuit breaker patterns for each delivery channel, temporarily disabling a channel if its failure rate exceeds 30% over a 5-minute window and routing notifications through fallback channels. Performance metrics including queue depth, delivery latency, and failure rates must be displayed on an internal operations dashboard updated in real time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R005	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Named Account and Key Account Pricing	The system must support designated named account or key account pricing structures with enhanced flexibility beyond standard rate agreements. Named accounts must be assignable special pricing privileges including fixed rates regardless of tariff changes, guaranteed space allocation commitments, priority equipment supply, and waived or reduced surcharges. The system must maintain a key account registry with classification levels (Platinum, Gold, Silver) each having configurable pricing rule sets. AI must continuously monitor key account profitability and utilization patterns, generating alerts when account profitability drops below threshold or when the customer's shipping patterns deviate significantly from the agreement assumptions, enabling proactive commercial engagement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R005	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Terminal Handling Charge Configuration by Port	The system must support port-specific and terminal-specific THC configuration for all operating ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA) with its three terminals, and JNPT (INNSA) with its multiple terminal operators (BMCT, NSIGT, APMT, JNPCT). THC rates must be definable separately for origin and destination, by container type and size, and must distinguish between laden and empty container handling. The THC must be configurable in the local currency of the port country (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT). The system must support THC rate variations based on cargo type such as hazardous (IMO class), reefer, out-of-gauge, and overweight containers, with appropriate premium multipliers configurable per terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R010	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Credit Risk Assessment Integration for Account Reviews	The system must integrate credit risk data into account management workflows, pulling real-time credit scores from integrated credit bureaus and internal AR aging analysis. During quarterly account reviews, the system must auto-generate a credit risk summary including current credit limit utilization percentage, average days sales outstanding versus contracted payment terms (30/60/90 days), overdue invoice count and value in account billing currency, and payment trend trajectory. For accounts exceeding 80% credit limit utilization, the system must flag the account for mandatory credit review before any new bookings are confirmed. Multi-entity customers must have consolidated and entity-level credit views. Risk thresholds must be configurable per entity to reflect local regulatory requirements (e.g., QCB guidelines for Qatar, RBI norms for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R003	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	KYC/AML Compliance Screening Integration	The system must perform automated KYC (Know Your Customer) and AML (Anti-Money Laundering) screening during onboarding by checking the applicant company and all listed beneficial owners and authorized signatories against international sanctions lists (UN, OFAC, EU), PEP (Politically Exposed Persons) databases, and adverse media screening services. For UAE onboarding, screening must additionally check against the UAE Central Bank's designated persons list. For India, ECGC cautionary list and RBI defaulter lists must be screened. For KSA, SAMA (Saudi Central Bank) compliance requirements must be met. Any positive or partial match must halt the onboarding workflow and route to the compliance officer for manual adjudication. Screening must be repeated at configurable intervals (default every 6 months) for existing customers. Full screening audit trails must be maintained per FATF recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R011	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Referral Program Management and Tracking	The system must manage a customer referral program allowing existing customers to refer new shippers and freight forwarders. The referral workflow must capture referring customer identity, referred prospect details, and track the referred lead through the full lifecycle to account activation and first booking. Referral rewards must be configurable per entity: credit notes against future invoices (in the referrer's billing currency QAR/AED/SAR/INR), booking discounts (percentage or fixed amount per TEU), or loyalty points redeemable against VAS charges. Reward triggering events must be configurable (upon referred account activation, upon first booking, upon minimum volume threshold). The system must prevent self-referrals and circular referrals through validation rules. Referral program terms must be published in Arabic and English. Monthly referral program performance reports must show active referrers, conversion rates, reward costs, and revenue generated from referred accounts per entity and port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R005	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Reservation from Availability Screen	Customers must be able to reserve available containers directly from the availability check results by selecting the desired quantity, specifying the intended pickup date and time window, and confirming the booking reference or creating a new provisional booking. The reservation must place a temporary hold on the selected units for a configurable period (default 24 hours for standard containers, 48 hours for reefers) after which unreserved units are automatically released back to the available pool. The system must enforce reservation limits per customer based on their credit status and historical no-show rate, preventing over-reservation by customers with a no-show rate exceeding 20% in the past 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R012	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Multi-Entity Complaint Jurisdiction Routing	When a complaint involves services spanning multiple entities (for example, a shipment originating in India with a transshipment issue at Jebel Ali and final delivery in Qatar), the system must determine the primary jurisdiction based on configurable rules: the entity where the incident occurred takes primary ownership, while involved entities are added as secondary stakeholders. The primary entity must coordinate the investigation and resolution, with status updates visible to all involved entities' customer service teams. Financial settlements for cross-entity complaints must follow the inter-entity transfer pricing policy, debiting the entity at fault and crediting the customer through their primary contracting entity. Jurisdictional disputes between entities must be flagged for resolution by the regional customer service director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R001	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Schedule Creation with Trade Lane Mapping	The system must allow authorized commercial users to create tariff schedules mapped to specific trade lanes defined by origin-destination port pairs. Each tariff schedule must capture the effective date range, applicable container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR, 45HC), commodity classifications using HS codes, and the operating entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India). The tariff must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date selection for effective and expiry dates. AI must auto-suggest rate levels based on historical data and market intelligence for the specific trade lane, presenting confidence scores for each suggested rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R006	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Spot Rate Quotation and Conversion to Agreement	The system must support creation of spot rate quotations for one-time or short-duration shipments outside standard tariff and agreement coverage. Spot rates must be valid for a configurable period (default 7 days) and must capture the trade lane, container details, commodity, and any special requirements. The system must track spot rate conversion rates and allow seamless conversion of repeated spot rate customers into formal rate agreements. AI must analyze spot rate request patterns to identify opportunities for agreement conversion, automatically generating agreement proposals when a customer exceeds a configurable threshold of spot bookings (default: 5 bookings per quarter per trade lane) and presenting the proposal to the responsible account manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R006	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Approval Workflow and Publication	The system must enforce an approval workflow for all surcharge rate changes before they become effective. The workflow must support different approval levels based on the surcharge type and the magnitude of the rate change. Surcharges mandated by regulatory authorities (such as port authority levies) must follow an expedited approval path. Upon approval, surcharges must be publishable to customer-facing channels including the customer portal and freight rate quotation systems. Publication must generate bilingual (Arabic/English) surcharge circulars in PDF format. The system must enforce a minimum publication lead time (configurable per surcharge type, default 30 days for BAF/CAF, 15 days for others) to ensure customers receive adequate advance notice as required by regulatory guidelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R006	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	SAML 2.0 SSO Enterprise	Support SAML 2.0 SP-initiated and IdP-initiated SSO. Admin configures: IdP entity ID, SSO URL, SLO URL, X.509 certificate. Auto-provision user on first SSO login using SAML assertions (email, name, role). Tested with: Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, Google Workspace.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R011	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Customer Feedback and NPS Survey Automation	The system must automate Net Promoter Score (NPS) surveys triggered by configurable events including shipment delivery completion, claim resolution, and contract renewal. Surveys must be delivered via email and SMS in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English) with bilingual option. Survey responses must be captured and linked to the specific transaction, account, entity, and relationship manager. NPS scores must aggregate at account, entity, port, and company-wide levels with trend visualization. Detractor responses (score 0-6) must automatically create follow-up tasks for the relationship manager within 4 hours. The system must support Hijri date display in Arabic surveys. Survey frequency must be throttled to prevent survey fatigue with a configurable minimum interval (default 30 days) between surveys per contact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R004	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Credit Application and Limit Setting Workflow	The system must integrate a credit application workflow within onboarding where customers requesting credit terms submit financial statements, bank references, and trade references. The system must auto-calculate a recommended credit limit based on AI analysis of submitted financials (revenue, net worth, current ratio, debt-to-equity), requested shipping volumes, trade reference verification outcomes, and external credit bureau scores where available (e.g., Dun & Bradstreet, CRIBIS for UAE). Credit limits must be denominated in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent display. The approval workflow must tier by limit amount: up to USD 50K equivalent requires credit manager approval, USD 50K-200K requires credit director, above USD 200K requires CFO approval. Approved limits must automatically provision in the booking and invoicing systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R012	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Market Segmentation and Target Account List Management	The system must support dynamic market segmentation based on configurable criteria including industry vertical (oil/gas, petrochemicals, FMCG, automotive, textiles, electronics, pharmaceuticals), company size (by revenue or employee count), trade lane activity (active importers/exporters on specific port pairs), container type requirements (dry, reefer, hazardous, oversized), geographic location relative to port hinterlands, and current carrier relationships. Segments must be used to define target account lists for focused marketing campaigns and sales territory planning. The system must integrate with external trade databases (customs declaration summaries, shipping manifests where publicly available) to identify high-potential prospects not yet in the CRM. Target account lists must be assignable to sales teams with progress tracking against engagement and conversion milestones. Segmentation criteria must be saveable as dynamic filters that automatically update membership as new data enters the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R006	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Special Equipment Availability Check	The system must support availability queries for special equipment types including open-top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat-rack containers (20FR, 40FR), tank containers (20TK), and 45-foot high-cube containers (45HC). For each special equipment type, the system must display the unit's specific technical specifications (internal dimensions, door opening dimensions, maximum payload, tare weight) alongside availability count. Since special equipment has limited stock, the system must display the nearest available date if no units are currently available, and must offer an automatic waitlist enrollment option that notifies the customer when a unit becomes available at their preferred port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R013	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Knowledge Base and Self-Resolution	The system must maintain a searchable knowledge base of frequently asked complaint topics, common dispute resolutions, and self-help articles covering topics such as free-time policies per port, demurrage calculation methods, cargo insurance claim procedures, and documentation requirements for different complaint types. When a customer begins typing a complaint, the AI must search the knowledge base and suggest relevant articles that may help the customer self-resolve without filing a formal complaint. The knowledge base must be available in both Arabic and English with content managed by the customer service team through a CMS interface. Articles must be tagged by entity, port, complaint category, and INCOTERMS applicability, and must be reviewed for accuracy at least quarterly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R002	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Multi-Currency Base Rate Definition	The system must support defining base freight rates in multiple currencies including USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR for each tariff line item. Each tariff entry must specify the primary billing currency and up to three alternative billing currencies with system-maintained exchange rate references. The exchange rate source and date must be captured and auditable. When a tariff is quoted in a non-primary currency, the system must apply the exchange rate valid on the date of booking confirmation. AI must flag tariff entries where currency conversion results in rate anomalies exceeding configurable thresholds per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R007	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Amendment and Addendum Management	The system must support formal amendments to active rate agreements through an addendum process that preserves the original agreement terms while documenting changes. Each addendum must specify the effective date, the specific clauses or rates being modified, the reason for amendment, and must go through the same approval workflow as the original agreement. The system must maintain a consolidated view of the agreement showing all addenda in chronological order with the currently effective terms clearly highlighted. Amendment history must be searchable and reportable. AI must assess the cumulative impact of multiple amendments on overall agreement profitability and flag agreements where cumulative amendments have reduced the projected margin below the entity's minimum threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R007	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Waiver and Exemption Rules	The system must support configurable surcharge waiver and exemption rules that can be applied to specific customers, rate agreements, trade lanes, or commodity types. Waiver rules must specify which surcharges are waived or discounted, the waiver percentage (partial or full), the applicable scope, and the authorization level required. Waivers granted within rate agreements must be automatically applied during booking and invoicing. Ad-hoc waivers outside of agreements must require individual approval with documented justification. The system must track the financial impact of all surcharge waivers for reporting purposes, calculating the total waived amount per customer, per trade lane, and per entity. AI must flag excessive waiver patterns and recommend surcharge inclusion during rate agreement renegotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R009	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Account Lockout and Brute-Force Protection	Lock account after 5 failed login attempts within 15 min. Lock duration: 15 min auto-unlock. Admin can manually unlock. Log all failed attempts with IP and timestamp. Alert user by email on lockout. Captcha after 3 failed attempts using hCaptcha.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R012	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Account Handover and Reassignment Workflow	The system must support structured account handover workflows when relationship managers change due to resignation, transfer, or portfolio rebalancing. The handover process must generate a comprehensive account briefing document including open bookings, pending quotations, active contracts with expiry dates, outstanding receivables, recent interaction history (last 90 days), open complaints or claims, and customer communication preferences. The incoming relationship manager must acknowledge receipt and review completion within 5 business days. During the transition period, both outgoing and incoming managers must have concurrent read access. Customer notification of the change must be sent in their preferred language (Arabic/English) with the new manager's contact details. The system must track handover completion metrics and flag overdue transitions to the commercial director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R005	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Onboarding Status Tracker with Customer Self-Service Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing self-service portal where applicants can track their onboarding status through defined stages: Application Submitted, Documents Under Review, KYC/AML Screening, Credit Assessment, Approval Pending, Account Activated. The portal must be accessible in Arabic and English with automatic language detection based on browser locale. Each stage must display estimated completion time and any pending actions required from the customer (e.g., missing document upload, additional information request). Push notifications via email and SMS must alert customers when their status changes or when action is required. The internal view must show the same stages plus internal-only stages (compliance review, IT provisioning) invisible to the customer. The portal must render correctly on mobile devices for customers visiting port offices at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R013	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Website Visitor Tracking and Behavioral Lead Generation	The system must integrate with the company website to track visitor behavior including pages visited (service pages, rate inquiry forms, port information pages, vessel schedules), time on site, return visit frequency, and form submissions. Anonymous visitors must be tracked via cookie/fingerprinting (compliant with UAE, Qatar, KSA, and India data protection regulations) and converted to identified leads upon form submission or email link click. The system must score website behavior signals (e.g., visiting the Jebel Ali-to-JNPT trade lane page three times in a week indicates strong purchase intent) and incorporate these into the overall lead score. High-intent behavior patterns must trigger real-time alerts to assigned salespeople or marketing automation workflows. Website analytics must distinguish traffic by geographic region, referral source, and device type. Integration must support the company website's Arabic and English language versions with behavior tracking across language switches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R007	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	SOC Container Registration and Tracking	The system must allow customers using Shipper-Owned Containers (SOC) to register their equipment in the portal by providing container number (validated against ISO 6346 format with check digit verification), container type, size, tare weight, manufacture year, and CSC plate expiry date. Registered SOC units must be tracked through the shipping cycle independently of carrier-owned equipment. The portal must display a separate SOC inventory dashboard showing each registered container's current location, status (empty at customer premises, laden in transit, at port), and upcoming CSC inspection due dates. The system must warn customers 60 days before CSC plate expiry to prevent equipment rejection at the port gate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R014	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Regulatory Compliance for Consumer Protection	The complaint management system must comply with consumer protection regulations in all operating jurisdictions: Qatar Consumer Protection Law No. 8 of 2008, UAE Federal Law No. 15 of 2020 on Consumer Protection, Saudi Consumer Protection Law, and the Indian Consumer Protection Act 2019. The system must ensure that complaint response timeframes meet or exceed regulatory minimums, that customers are informed of their right to escalate to external regulatory bodies (Qatar Ministry of Commerce, UAE DED, Saudi Ministry of Commerce, Indian Consumer Forums), and that complaint data is made available to regulators upon authorized request. For complaints involving potential safety issues (damaged containers carrying hazardous materials), the system must trigger automatic notification to the relevant port authority and maritime safety administration within 2 hours of filing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R003	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Version Control and Audit Trail	The system must maintain full version history of every tariff schedule with immutable audit trails. Each modification must create a new version with the modifier's identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, entity context, and detailed change log showing previous and new values for each modified field. The system must support concurrent active versions for different effective date windows. A maximum of one future-dated version and one currently active version per trade lane and container type combination must be enforced. AI must detect conflicting tariff versions and alert commercial managers automatically before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R008	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Commodity-Specific Rate Exceptions within Agreements	The system must support commodity-specific rate exceptions within rate agreements, allowing different rates for different commodity types shipped by the same customer on the same trade lane. Commodity exceptions must reference HS codes at minimum 4-digit heading level and must specify the exception rate, the justification, and any quantity limits. The system must apply commodity exception rates automatically during booking when the declared commodity matches the exception criteria. Where a shipment contains mixed commodities, the system must apply the appropriate rate to each container based on its declared commodity. Rate precedence rules must clearly define that commodity exceptions override the agreement base rate, which in turn overrides the standard tariff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R008	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Seasonal and Event-Based Surcharge Triggers	The system must support definition of seasonal and event-based surcharge triggers that automatically activate or deactivate surcharges based on predefined conditions. Seasonal triggers must support calendar-based rules such as Peak Season Surcharge activation during specific months (configurable per trade lane). Event-based triggers must support condition-based activation such as Congestion Surcharge when port dwell times exceed thresholds or Emergency Bunker Surcharge when fuel prices breach predefined levels. The system must support trigger conditions based on data feeds from port operations (vessel waiting times, yard utilization) and external market data. AI must predict surcharge trigger events based on historical patterns and current trend analysis, giving commercial teams advance warning to prepare customer communications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R013	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Multi-Port Activity Consolidation View	The system must provide a consolidated activity view per account aggregating operational data across all four primary ports: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA), plus any additional feeder ports. The view must display active bookings by port with vessel/voyage references, containers currently under demurrage or detention with elapsed free-time and accruing charges in local currency, pending customs clearances with estimated release dates, and historical volume by port pair for the trailing 12 months. Data must refresh at minimum every 15 minutes from port community systems and terminal operating systems. The consolidated view must support drill-down from port summary to individual booking and container level. Offline mode must cache the last-synced snapshot with a visible timestamp indicating data freshness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R006	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Trade License and Registration Validity Monitoring	The system must extract and store expiry dates from all submitted trade licenses, commercial registrations, and regulatory certificates during onboarding. An automated monitoring system must generate renewal reminders to both the customer and the assigned relationship manager at 60 days, 30 days, and 7 days before expiry. Upon expiry without renewal, the system must enforce configurable business rules: warning-only for first 30 days post-expiry, booking restriction after 30 days, and full account suspension after 60 days. Entity-specific grace periods must be configurable to account for known government processing delays (e.g., Qatar MOCIE license renewals). The system must support Hijri expiry dates for KSA documents alongside Gregorian equivalents. Re-activation after suspension must require re-submission of valid documents and compliance re-screening.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R014	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Competitor Marketing Intelligence Tracking	The system must maintain a competitor marketing intelligence database tracking competitor carriers' marketing activities in the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India markets. Tracked intelligence must include competitor rate promotions on specific trade lanes, new service launches (direct services, feeder connections, transshipment routes), capacity additions or vessel deployments affecting port calls at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, marketing campaign observations (trade show presence, advertising, digital campaigns), and customer win/loss attributable to competitor actions. Marketing team members must be able to log intelligence entries with source attribution, date observed, and confidence level assessment. AI must analyze competitor activity patterns to predict upcoming competitive moves and recommend preemptive marketing responses. Quarterly competitive marketing landscape reports must be auto-generated per entity and trade lane. Intelligence entries must support Arabic and English text with classified sensitivity levels restricting access by role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R008	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Availability Alert Subscription	Customers must be able to subscribe to availability alerts for specific container types at specific ports by setting threshold conditions such as notify when 40HC availability at Jebel Ali exceeds 50 units or when 20RF containers become available at JNPT. Alert subscriptions must support frequency controls: immediate notification, daily digest, or weekly summary. Each customer may have a maximum of 20 active alert subscriptions across all ports. The system must deliver alerts through the customer's preferred channel (email, SMS, in-app notification, or WhatsApp Business API) and must include the current availability count, the trend direction (increasing/decreasing/stable), and a direct link to the reservation screen.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R015	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Dashboard and Reporting	The customer portal must include a complaint dashboard showing all open, in-progress, and resolved complaints with real-time status tracking, assigned handler contact details, SLA countdown timers, and a timeline view of all case activities. The dashboard must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), complaint category, status, priority, and entity. Aggregate statistics must include total complaints by category, average resolution time, SLA compliance percentage, and financial impact of resolved disputes. The dashboard must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with right-aligned progress bars, mirrored timeline flow, and Arabic-localized chart labels. All dashboard data must be exportable to Excel and PDF with bilingual column headers and proper currency formatting per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R004	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Approval Workflow with Multi-Level Authorization	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for tariff creation and modification. The workflow must support at minimum three approval levels: commercial executive, commercial manager, and commercial director. Approval authority limits must be configurable per entity, allowing different thresholds for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Tariffs exceeding defined rate change thresholds (percentage increase or decrease from previous tariff) must require additional approval from the entity head. The approval workflow must support delegation during absence periods and must send bilingual (Arabic/English) notifications at each workflow stage with full tariff summary attachments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R009	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Performance Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard for monitoring rate agreement performance against commitments. Key metrics must include actual versus committed volume (TEU), revenue earned versus projected, average rate achieved versus agreed, margin performance, and container type mix. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, customer, trade lane, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar options. Visual indicators (color-coded) must highlight agreements that are underperforming, on-target, or overperforming against their volume and revenue commitments. AI must generate weekly performance summaries with natural language insights in both Arabic and English, identifying trends, risks, and opportunities for each significant agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R009	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Application Rules for Booking and Invoicing	The system must define and enforce clear rules for surcharge application during the booking and invoicing process. Rules must specify whether each surcharge is applied at booking confirmation date, vessel departure date, bill of lading date, or invoice date. The system must handle surcharge changes that occur between booking and vessel departure by applying configurable rules: either the rate at booking date is locked, or the rate at departure date applies, depending on the surcharge type and any applicable agreement terms. For transshipment cargo, surcharges must be correctly applied for each leg of the journey where applicable. The system must prevent duplicate surcharge application and must validate surcharge completeness against the mandatory surcharge list for each trade lane before allowing invoice generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R014	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Customer Document Repository and Compliance Vault	The system must maintain a secure document repository per account storing trade licenses, commercial registration certificates, power of attorney documents, authorized signatory lists, KYC/AML verification records, credit application forms, signed contracts, and rate agreement annexures. Documents must be tagged with expiry dates triggering automated renewal reminders 30 days before expiration. For KSA accounts, Saudization compliance certificates must be tracked. For India accounts, IEC (Import Export Code), GST registration, and AD code authorization must be maintained. For Qatar and UAE accounts, trade license renewal and establishment card validity must be monitored. All documents must support Arabic and English filenames. Access control must enforce entity-level segregation with audit logging of all document views and downloads per GDPR and local data protection regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R007	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	System Access Provisioning and User Account Setup	The system must automate user account provisioning upon onboarding approval, creating customer portal credentials with role-based access appropriate to the customer type. Shipper accounts must receive booking, tracking, and documentation modules. Freight forwarder accounts must additionally receive multi-party booking and sub-account management capabilities. NVOCC accounts must receive house BL issuance and container management modules. Each customer organization must have an admin user who can create and manage sub-users with configurable permissions per module. Two-factor authentication must be enforced for all customer portal accounts. User interface language preference (Arabic/English) must be set during provisioning based on the onboarding application language. Provisioning must complete within 2 hours of final approval with automated welcome email containing login instructions in the customer's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R015	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Marketing Consent and Data Privacy Management	The system must manage marketing communication consent per contact in compliance with applicable regulations across all operating jurisdictions. For each contact, explicit opt-in consent must be recorded specifying allowed communication channels (email, SMS, WhatsApp, phone), preferred language (Arabic, English), and topic preferences (general marketing, rate updates, industry news, event invitations). Consent records must be timestamped, source-attributed (web form, verbal with recording reference, written), and immutably stored for audit. The system must enforce consent before including contacts in any marketing campaign execution. Withdrawal of consent must be processed within 24 hours across all marketing automation workflows. For contacts associated with multiple entities, consent must be managed per entity unless a group-level consent is explicitly granted. The system must generate consent compliance reports for regulatory review showing opt-in rates, withdrawal trends, and any consent violations with remediation actions. Privacy notices must be available in Arabic and English per PDPL (KSA), DIFC Data Protection Law (UAE), and India DPDP Act requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R009	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Condition and Damage Report Access	When a customer selects a specific available container from the inventory results, the system must display the most recent Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) with damage annotations, photographs of any noted damage mapped to the container's six-panel diagram (front, back, left, right, top, floor), and the surveyor's assessment of each damage item (acceptable for cargo loading or requires repair). The damage report must follow IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) coding standards for damage type, location, and dimension. Customers must be able to accept or reject a specific unit based on the condition report and request an alternative unit assignment without losing their reservation slot.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R016	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Linking to Shipment Records	Each complaint must be linkable to one or more shipment records using identifiers such as bill of lading number, booking reference, container number, or invoice number. The system must automatically retrieve and display the linked shipment's complete lifecycle: booking date, vessel name and voyage, port of loading, port of discharge, container gate-in/gate-out timestamps, customs clearance dates, delivery date, and all associated invoices. This linked view must enable the complaint handler and the customer to reference specific shipment events when discussing the complaint. If a container number is provided, the system must pull the container's tracking history and Equipment Interchange Reports from both loading and discharge ports to support damage claim investigations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R005	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Port-Pair Specific Rate Structure Configuration	The system must allow tariff rates to be configured at the port-pair level with granularity down to specific terminal facilities. For Hamad Port, rates must differentiate between container terminal and general cargo berths. For Jebel Ali, rates must support zone-based pricing across its three terminals. For JNPT, rates must accommodate the different terminal operators (BMCT, NSIGT, APMT, JNPCT). Each port-pair rate must include base ocean freight, terminal handling charges at origin and destination, and any mandatory port-specific levies. The system must validate that all mandatory rate components are populated before allowing tariff activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R010	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Renewal and Renegotiation Triggers	The system must track rate agreement expiry dates and trigger renewal processes at configurable lead times (default: 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry). Renewal triggers must generate tasks assigned to the responsible account manager with a pre-populated renewal proposal based on the current agreement terms and AI-recommended rate adjustments. The AI recommendation must consider the customer's actual performance against the current agreement, market rate movements, competitive landscape, and the entity's strategic objectives for that trade lane. The system must support automatic rate escalation clauses (e.g., annual CPI-based adjustments) that can be executed without full renegotiation if specified in the original agreement terms. Renewal history must be maintained linking all successive agreements for a customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R004	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	MBL Amendment and Correction Workflow	The system must enforce a controlled amendment workflow for Multimodal Bills of Lading where changes to critical fields (consignee, notify party, cargo description, container details, delivery place) require multi-level approval. Amendment requests must capture the reason for change, supporting documents, and the requesting party. The approval chain must route through documentation controller and commercial manager with configurable escalation rules per entity. All amendments must generate an amendment log with before/after values, approver identity, timestamp, and a sequential amendment number printed on the revised document. Amendments to negotiable originals must require surrender of all previously issued originals before reissuance. The system must maintain a complete amendment history accessible in both Arabic and English audit trail views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R015	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Competitive Intelligence Tracking per Account	The system must allow relationship managers to record and track competitive intelligence per account, including known competitor carriers servicing the account, estimated competitor rate levels per trade lane and container type, competitor service advantages noted by the customer, and win/loss analysis for contested bookings. AI must analyze patterns across accounts to identify systematic competitive threats per trade lane (e.g., a competitor consistently undercutting rates on the Jebel Ali to JNPT corridor by 8-12%). The system must generate quarterly competitive landscape reports per trade lane and per port with recommendations for rate adjustments or service improvements. Intelligence entries must be time-stamped and attributed to sources. Aggregated competitive data must be accessible to commercial directors while protecting individual account-level detail from unauthorized relationship managers through role-based access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R008	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Multi-Entity Customer Linking and Master Data Deduplication	The system must detect and manage scenarios where a customer entity is being onboarded that has existing relationships with other operating entities. During onboarding, the system must run fuzzy matching against all entity databases using company name (in both Arabic and English), trade license numbers, authorized signatory identification numbers, and registered addresses to identify potential duplicates or related entities. When a match is found, the onboarding officer must be alerted with the option to link the new entity as a subsidiary of an existing master account or flag as a duplicate for resolution. Master data governance rules must prevent conflicting information across linked entities (e.g., different Arabic names for the same company). Deduplication must support approximate matching for Arabic company names accounting for common transliteration variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R016	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	AI-Generated Marketing Content and Personalization Engine	The system must provide AI-powered content generation capabilities for marketing materials including email subject lines, campaign copy, social media posts, and lead nurturing messages. Content generation must support both Arabic and English with culturally appropriate tone and terminology for the container shipping industry. The AI must personalize content based on recipient attributes including industry vertical, trade lanes of interest, historical engagement patterns, preferred container types, and operating port relevance. Personalization tokens must dynamically insert customer-specific data (company name, recent shipment routes, applicable port names, relationship manager name). Generated Arabic content must be grammatically correct with proper formal business Arabic (Fusha) usage. All AI-generated content must be reviewable and editable by marketing staff before publication. The system must track performance metrics (open rate, click rate, conversion) for AI-generated versus manually created content to continuously improve generation quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R010	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Depot Operating Hours and Pickup Scheduling	The system must display the operating hours for each container depot associated with the selected port, accounting for local weekend patterns (Friday-Saturday for Qatar, UAE, KSA; Saturday-Sunday for India) and public holidays including Islamic holidays calculated from the Hijri calendar (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Days). Customers must be able to schedule a pickup appointment within the depot's operating hours, selecting a time slot in 2-hour increments. The system must validate that the requested pickup time is at least 4 business hours in the future to allow depot preparation and must block scheduling during known port congestion windows or adverse weather closures as flagged by the port operations module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R017	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Reopening and Appeal Process	Customers must be able to request reopening of a resolved complaint within 30 calendar days of closure by submitting an appeal with new evidence or a written objection to the resolution. The reopened case must be assigned to a different handler than the original investigator to ensure impartiality. The system must track the complaint through a separate appeal workflow with its own SLA timelines: acknowledgment within 4 business hours, investigation completion within 5 business days, and final resolution within 10 business days. A complaint may be reopened a maximum of two times; after the second appeal, the system must advise the customer of external dispute resolution options including maritime arbitration under applicable rules (LMAA, ICC, or SIAC depending on the contract's governing law clause). The appeal history must be maintained as part of the permanent case record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R006	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Container Type and Size Rate Differentiation	The system must support differentiated tariff rates based on container type and size as per ISO 6346 standards. Rate differentiation must be supported for standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RF), open-top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat-rack containers (20FR, 40FR), tank containers (20TK), and special equipment. For reefer containers, the tariff must include separate line items for power supply charges at origin port, destination port, and transshipment points. AI must recommend rate differentials between container types based on historical utilization patterns and market demand analysis for each trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R011	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Document Generation and Storage	The system must generate formal rate agreement documents from approved system records in PDF format with configurable templates per entity. Documents must be generated in both Arabic and English simultaneously with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions including correct display of numbers, dates, currency amounts, and legal clauses. The document must include all commercial terms, rate tables, volume commitments, surcharge applicability, payment terms, and applicable terms and conditions. Generated documents must be stored in the document management system with version control and must be digitally linkable to the system agreement record. The system must support electronic signature integration for remote agreement execution by both the company and customer representatives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R010	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Regulatory and Government-Mandated Levy Management	The system must separately manage government-mandated levies and regulatory surcharges that are distinct from commercial surcharges. These include port dues, customs inspection fees, security charges (ISPS), environmental levies, and country-specific taxes. For Qatar operations, this includes Hamad Port authority tariffs and Qatar customs charges. For UAE operations, this includes federal port charges and Dubai Maritime City Authority levies. For KSA operations, this includes MAWANI port authority charges. For India operations, this includes Indian Customs charges, Goods and Services Tax (GST) on shipping services, and JNPT-specific handling levies. Each regulatory levy must be flagged as non-negotiable and non-waivable in the system. The system must track regulatory rate change announcements and provide advance notification to commercial and finance teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-001-R016	FEAT-005-2-001	MOD-005	Automated Quarterly Business Review (QBR) Package Generation	The system must auto-generate Quarterly Business Review presentation packages for key accounts in PDF and PowerPoint formats. The QBR package must include: volume performance versus contract commitments with variance analysis, SLA performance scorecard, financial summary showing revenue, outstanding receivables, and credit utilization in the account's billing currency, market rate trend analysis for the account's primary trade lanes, operational incident summary and resolution status, NPS trend and customer satisfaction metrics, and forward-looking volume forecast using AI prediction. The package must be brandable with company logos and must support Arabic RTL layout generation for Arabic-speaking accounts. QBR scheduling must align with both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal quarters. The relationship manager must be able to review, annotate, and customize the AI-generated package before customer distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R009	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Onboarding SLA Tracking and Bottleneck Analysis	The system must track onboarding process SLAs with defined maximum durations per stage: Document Collection (5 business days), Document Verification (2 business days), KYC/AML Screening (1 business day), Credit Assessment (3 business days), Final Approval (2 business days), and System Provisioning (1 business day). Total onboarding target must be configurable per entity (default 10 business days for standard accounts, 5 business days for pre-qualified accounts). The system must generate real-time dashboards showing average onboarding duration by entity, bottleneck stages where applications accumulate, and individual applications exceeding SLA thresholds. Breached SLA stages must trigger escalation to the department head. Monthly reports must analyze onboarding funnel conversion rates from application to activation, segmented by entity, customer type, and originating port. Business day calculations must respect entity-specific holiday calendars including Hijri holidays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R017	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Partner and Co-Marketing Campaign Management	The system must support joint marketing campaigns with partners including port authorities (Mwani Qatar, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority), free zone authorities (QFZ, JAFZA, KIZAD, NSEZ), shipping alliances, and complementary service providers (warehousing, trucking, customs brokerage). Partner campaigns must track shared lead pools with agreed lead routing rules (e.g., leads from a JAFZA co-marketing event route to the UAE entity with port-specific flag). Budget contributions from partners must be recorded in the partner's currency with reconciliation against shared campaign expenses. Joint performance reports must be generatable showing each partner's contribution and derived benefit. Co-branded content must maintain brand compliance for both parties with multi-stage approval workflows. Partner marketing agreements must be stored with terms, duration, and renewal dates. The system must prevent sharing of confidential customer data with partners beyond agreed-upon aggregate metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R011	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Availability Map Visualization	The portal must provide an interactive map view showing container availability across all supported ports in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent region. Each port must be represented by a marker with a color-coded availability indicator: green for high availability (above 80% of average demand), amber for moderate (40-80%), and red for low availability (below 40%). Clicking a port marker must expand a popup showing a breakdown by container type with unit counts. The map must support zoom, pan, and must render correctly in both Arabic and English with port names displayed in the user's selected language. The map component must degrade gracefully to a tabular list view on slow connections or when the browser does not support WebGL.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R007	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Commodity-Based Tariff Classification	The system must support commodity-based tariff classification using Harmonized System (HS) codes at minimum 6-digit level with optional extension to 8-digit level for country-specific classifications. Tariff rates must be assignable at commodity group level (HS 2-digit chapter), commodity heading level (HS 4-digit), or specific commodity level (HS 6-digit or 8-digit). The system must maintain a commodity classification master aligned with each entity's customs tariff schedule. AI must auto-classify commodities based on cargo description text and suggest the appropriate HS code with confidence scoring, supporting both Arabic and English commodity descriptions for accurate classification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R002	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Custom Role Creation	Company admins can create custom roles: name, description, select permissions from permission matrix. Max 50 custom roles per company. Custom roles can inherit from a base role. Version-controlled: track who created/modified and when.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-001-R002	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Seasonal Demand Forecasting	System forecasts seasonal demand patterns per trade lane using historical booking data and external economic indicators.	must_have			["AI model ingests minimum 3 years of historical booking data per trade lane for seasonal pattern detection", "Forecasting accounts for Ramadan, Hajj season, Diwali, and other regional demand shifts in GCC/India", "Hijri calendar events are mapped to Gregorian dates dynamically for multi-year forecasting accuracy", "Demand forecast confidence intervals are displayed at 80% and 95% levels", "Forecast accuracy is tracked monthly with MAPE below 12% after 6 months of operation"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-001-R003	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Revenue Per TEU Maximization	Yield engine calculates and optimizes blended revenue per TEU across container types and surcharge components.	must_have			["Revenue per TEU is calculated including base freight, BAF, CAF, THC, and all applicable surcharges", "System compares actual vs. potential revenue per TEU at voyage, trade lane, and entity level", "Multi-currency revenue is normalized to USD for comparison, with entity-local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) drill-down", "AI identifies underperforming trade lanes where revenue per TEU is below the 6-month rolling average", "Yield improvement recommendations are generated with estimated revenue uplift in both USD and local currency"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-001-R004	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Capacity Utilization Monitoring	Real-time monitoring of vessel capacity utilization with AI-powered alerts for underutilized or overbooked sailings.	must_have			["Vessel capacity is tracked in TEU, weight, and reefer plug utilization simultaneously", "Alert triggers when utilization falls below 70% within 7 days of sailing departure", "System recommends spot rate discounts to fill underutilized capacity with projected revenue impact", "Overbooking alerts fire when bookings exceed physical capacity plus configured overbooking threshold", "Utilization dashboard supports Arabic and English language toggle with RTL layout in Arabic mode"]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R001	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Target Setting with Multi-Dimensional KPIs	The system must support hierarchical sales target setting at company, entity, region, team, and individual levels with configurable KPI dimensions including TEU volume, revenue (in local currency and USD equivalent), new account acquisition count, customer retention rate, and trade lane diversification index. Targets must be definable for Gregorian fiscal quarters and Hijri fiscal periods for KSA operations. Each target must support stretch goals (100%, 110%, 125% achievement tiers) with associated incentive multipliers. Targets must cascade from entity-level commercial plans down to individual relationship managers with configurable allocation methods (equal split, weighted by territory potential, historical contribution). Midyear target revisions must follow an approval workflow requiring regional commercial head sign-off.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R010	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Regulatory Compliance Document Generation	The system must auto-generate jurisdiction-specific compliance documents during onboarding including: customer due diligence (CDD) forms pre-populated with verified data, FATCA/CRS declarations for US and common reporting standard tax compliance, beneficial ownership declarations, agency agreements (for freight forwarder/NVOCC relationships), and standard terms and conditions of service. Documents must generate in the legal language of the operating entity (Arabic for Qatar and KSA with English translation, English for UAE and India with optional Arabic). Generated documents must incorporate entity-specific legal clauses, arbitration venues (QICDRC for Qatar, DIAC for Dubai, SCCA for KSA, Indian courts for India), and applicable law references. Electronic signature capture must be supported with legal validity per each jurisdiction's e-signature laws. Generated documents must be stored in the account's document vault with version control.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R018	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Campaign A/B Testing and Optimization Framework	The system must support A/B and multivariate testing for marketing campaigns across all channels. For email campaigns, testable variables must include subject lines (Arabic and English variants), sender name, send time, content layout, call-to-action text, and offer positioning. For digital advertising, landing page variants, ad copy, and audience segments must be testable. The testing framework must enforce minimum sample sizes per variant to ensure statistical significance (configurable, default 95% confidence level, 5% margin of error). AI must auto-select winning variants based on the primary metric (open rate, click rate, or conversion rate as configured) and deploy the winner to the remaining audience. Test results must be archived with full statistical analysis accessible to marketing managers. The system must recommend optimal test designs based on historical campaign data and audience size per entity. Test execution must respect language-specific audience segments ensuring Arabic content variants are tested within Arabic-preference audiences and English within English-preference audiences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R012	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Availability Data Caching for Offline Access	The system must cache the most recent container availability snapshot in the customer's browser local storage, encrypted with AES-256, allowing customers to view the last-known availability data when offline. The cached data must include a clear last-updated timestamp displayed prominently in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with a warning banner stating that displayed data may not reflect current inventory levels. When the customer regains connectivity, the system must automatically refresh the availability data and visually highlight any changes from the cached version using color-coded delta indicators (green for increased availability, red for decreased). The cache must expire and auto-purge after 24 hours to prevent reliance on stale data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R001	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification Channel Configuration	The system must allow customers to configure their preferred notification delivery channels from the following options: email, SMS, in-app push notification, WhatsApp Business API, and Microsoft Teams webhook. Customers must be able to set a primary and secondary channel, where the secondary channel is used as a fallback if primary delivery fails after three retry attempts within 15 minutes. Each channel must require verification before activation: email via confirmation link, SMS via OTP code, WhatsApp via QR code opt-in per Meta Business Policy, and Teams via webhook URL validation. Channel preferences must be configurable per user within the customer organization, allowing different team members (operations, finance, management) to receive notifications through different channels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R008	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Publication and Distribution	The system must support automated tariff publication to authorized channels upon approval completion. Publication channels must include the customer self-service portal, freight forwarder portal, and internal sales systems. Published tariffs must render correctly in both Arabic (RTL layout) and English (LTR layout) with proper formatting of currency amounts, dates, and port names in the respective language. The system must generate PDF tariff circulars in both languages simultaneously for email distribution. A configurable lead time (minimum 15 days default) must be enforced between publication date and tariff effective date to comply with regional shipping regulations and FMC guidelines where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R012	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Competitive Rate Analysis for Negotiation Support	The system must provide AI-powered competitive rate analysis tools to support commercial teams during rate negotiations. The analysis must include market rate benchmarks from available industry indices (such as Drewry, Freightos Baltic Index), historical rates offered to similar customers on the same trade lanes, and win/loss analysis of recent quotations. AI must generate a recommended negotiation range showing the minimum acceptable rate (cost-plus-minimum-margin), the target rate (cost-plus-target-margin), and the opening offer rate for each trade lane and container type under discussion. The analysis must be presentable in a clean format suitable for internal review and must update dynamically as market conditions change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R011	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Impact Analysis and Simulation	The system must provide tools for analyzing the financial impact of proposed surcharge changes before implementation. The impact analysis must calculate the effect of a surcharge rate change on total freight costs per trade lane, estimated revenue impact based on current booking volumes, and the competitive positioning relative to market rates. The simulation must allow users to model multiple scenarios including different rate levels, different effective dates, and different scope definitions. AI must predict customer behavior changes (booking volume shifts) in response to surcharge changes based on historical elasticity patterns. Results must be presentable in graphical format with exportable detailed data supporting bilingual reporting in Arabic and English for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R002	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Real-Time Sales Dashboard with Pipeline Visibility	The system must provide a real-time sales dashboard showing each salesperson's performance against targets across all KPI dimensions. The dashboard must display pipeline value (total quotation value in negotiation stage) segmented by probability-weighted forecast and unweighted total. Pipeline must break down by trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, OOG), and expected closing month. Visual gauges must show target achievement percentage with color-coded thresholds (red below 60%, amber 60-85%, green above 85%). The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with right-to-left progress bars and Arabic numeral display option. Team managers must see aggregated views with drill-down to individual contributor level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R011	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Customs Authority Registration Validation	The system must validate that onboarding customers have active registrations with relevant customs authorities for their intended trade activities. For Qatar operations, validation against Qatar Customs (Al Nadeeb system) registration must be performed. For UAE, Dubai Customs (Mirsal 2) and Abu Dhabi Customs registration must be verified based on the operating port. For KSA, Saudi Customs (FASAH platform) registration must be confirmed. For India, ICEGATE registration and customs broker license validity (for customs broker customers) must be verified. The system must check registration status via API integrations where available or flag for manual verification where APIs are not accessible. Customers without valid customs registrations must be flagged with a warning indicating limited service scope (e.g., cannot be listed as importer of record). The validation must re-run quarterly to detect expired or suspended customs registrations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R001	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Spot Rate Request Submission	The system must allow authenticated customers to submit spot rate requests by specifying origin port (QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), destination port, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR), commodity description with HS code, cargo weight, required ETD range, and any special handling requirements. The form must validate that cargo weight does not exceed container payload limits per ISO 668 standards and must enforce minimum lead time of 48 hours from submission to requested ETD. Each request must generate a unique inquiry reference number with the format RR-YYYY-NNNNN for tracking purposes across both Arabic and English interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R013	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Interchange and Repositioning Cost Display	When a customer's preferred port has insufficient container availability, the system must offer repositioning options from nearby ports with surplus inventory, displaying the estimated repositioning cost in the customer's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), estimated transit time for the empty reposition, and the expected availability date at the customer's preferred port after repositioning. The system must factor in intermodal transport options (feeder vessel, road, rail) with cost comparisons for each mode. Repositioning cost calculations must include handling charges at both origin and destination depots, transport charges, and any cross-border customs fees for repositioning between GCC countries or from India to the Gulf.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R002	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Shipment Event Notification Subscriptions	Customers must be able to subscribe to notifications for specific shipment lifecycle events including: booking confirmation, shipping instruction deadline reminder, bill of lading release, vessel departure from origin, transshipment arrival and departure, vessel arrival at destination, customs clearance status change, container available for pickup, demurrage free-time warning (at 50%, 75%, and 100% of free days), and proof of delivery. Each event type must be independently toggleable per notification channel, allowing for example email notifications for BL release but SMS for demurrage warnings. Default notification templates must be provided for new customers based on their trade lane and cargo type, which they can customize after onboarding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R009	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	AI-Powered Tariff Rate Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven tariff rate optimization recommendations based on multiple data inputs including historical booking volumes, vessel utilization rates, competitor rate intelligence, seasonal demand patterns, and fuel price indices. The AI engine must generate rate recommendations for each trade lane and container type combination with projected revenue impact analysis. Recommendations must account for entity-specific market conditions, recognizing that Qatar-origin trade lanes have different competitive dynamics than India-origin trade lanes. The optimization model must present at least three scenarios (conservative, balanced, aggressive) with projected booking volume and revenue for each scenario, updated weekly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R013	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Compliance Monitoring and Enforcement	The system must continuously monitor booking and invoicing activities against active rate agreements to ensure compliance with agreed terms. Compliance checks must verify that applied rates match agreement rates, that volume commitments are being tracked correctly, that commodity restrictions are respected, and that trade lane scope is not exceeded. Any deviation detected must generate an exception record with severity classification (informational, warning, critical) and must route to the appropriate commercial user for resolution. The system must support configurable tolerance levels for rate deviations (e.g., allow up to 2% variance due to exchange rate fluctuations). AI must identify patterns of systematic non-compliance and generate root cause analysis reports to help resolve recurring issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R012	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Bundle and Package Management	The system must support creation of surcharge bundles that group multiple individual surcharges into a single all-inclusive surcharge amount for simplified customer communication and billing. Common bundles must include an All-In rate (freight plus all surcharges), a Delivered rate (including inland charges), and custom bundles per customer request. The system must maintain the individual surcharge component breakdown within each bundle for internal accounting and regulatory reporting even when the customer sees only the bundled amount. Bundle rates must be automatically recalculated when any component surcharge changes, with configurable rules for whether the bundle rate adjusts immediately or at the next review period. The system must prevent creation of circular references where a bundle contains another bundle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R003	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Permission Matrix	Permissions structured as resource:action pairs (e.g., booking:create, invoice:approve, report:export). Resources map to CS ERP modules. Actions: view, create, edit, delete, approve, export, configure. UI: checkbox grid of resource x action. Export permission matrix to PDF.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R003	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Commission Calculation Engine with Multi-Currency Payout	The system must compute sales commissions automatically based on configurable commission plans supporting flat rate per TEU, percentage of revenue, tiered progressive rates based on volume thresholds, and blended models combining multiple metrics. Commission calculations must convert all booking revenue to the salesperson's payout currency (QAR for Qatar-based, AED for UAE-based, SAR for KSA-based, INR for India-based) using the exchange rate locked at booking confirmation date. The engine must handle split commissions for shared accounts where multiple salespeople contributed, with configurable split ratios. Commission accrual must occur upon booking confirmation with clawback provisions for cancelled bookings or uncollected receivables exceeding 90 days. The system must generate monthly commission statements with line-item detail per booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R012	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Onboarding Workflow Exception Handling and Manual Override	The system must handle onboarding exceptions through a structured escalation path. Exceptions include incomplete document submissions after 3 reminder cycles, partial KYC/AML matches requiring compliance officer judgment, credit application rejection appeals, document verification failures due to poor scan quality, and trade license discrepancies requiring government verification. Each exception type must have a defined escalation matrix and maximum resolution time. Authorized users (compliance officer, credit director, commercial director) must be able to grant conditional approval with documented justification and mandatory follow-up conditions (e.g., provisional 90-day onboarding pending receipt of original trade license). Manual overrides must be logged with the overriding user's identity, timestamp, justification text, and must be included in monthly compliance audit reports. The system must support Arabic and English justification narratives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R002	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Contract Rate Request Workflow	The system must support contract rate inquiries where customers specify volume commitments (minimum quarterly TEU), contract duration (3, 6, or 12 months), preferred trade lanes, and container type mix. The portal must capture INCOTERMS 2020 basis (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP) for each rate request and allow customers to upload supporting documents such as historical volume reports or letters of intent. AI must pre-populate recommended contract terms based on the customer's historical shipment patterns, creditworthiness score, and current market conditions sourced from the freight rate intelligence engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R014	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Flexi-Bag and Liner Bag Container Filtering	The system must support filtering available containers by suitability for flexi-bag installations, showing only 20GP containers that meet the flexi-bag compatibility criteria: no internal damage to corrugation panels, functioning door gaskets, clean floor without protrusions, and a maximum age of 12 years. Similarly, for liner-bag cargo (such as bulk grains or powders), the system must filter containers meeting food-grade cleanliness certification. The availability results must display the container's last three cargo types to help customers assess contamination risk. Containers previously used for chemicals, fertilizers, or strongly odorous commodities must be flagged with a warning indicator to prevent cross-contamination of sensitive cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R003	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Billing and Financial Alert Configuration	The system must support financial notification subscriptions including: invoice generation, payment due date reminder (configurable at 7, 3, and 1 day before due date), payment receipt confirmation, credit limit utilization alerts (at 70%, 85%, and 95% thresholds), credit note issuance, statement of account availability, and overdue payment escalation notices. Financial alerts must display amounts in the customer's preferred currency with proper formatting (QAR and SAR with two decimal places and Arabic comma separator for Arabic UI, standard Western formatting for English UI). All financial notifications must include the entity name, commercial registration number, and VAT/TIN number per entity-specific invoicing requirements for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R010	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Comparison and Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide a visual dashboard for comparing tariff rates across multiple dimensions including trade lanes, time periods, entities, container types, and competitor benchmarks. The dashboard must support side-by-side comparison of current versus proposed tariff rates with percentage and absolute value variance display. Graphical trend analysis must show rate evolution over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views. The dashboard must support data export in Excel and PDF formats with bilingual headers. AI must highlight anomalies such as rates significantly below cost or above market average, with configurable sensitivity thresholds per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R014	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Freight Forwarder and NVOCC Rate Structures	The system must support specialized rate agreement structures for freight forwarders and NVOCCs (Non-Vessel Operating Common Carriers) that differ from direct shipper agreements. Forwarder agreements must support box rates (fixed all-in rates per container), FAK rates (Freight All Kinds without commodity differentiation), and NAC rates (Named Account Cargo for specific underlying shippers). The system must enforce that NVOCC customers are properly licensed by validating their FMC license number for US-bound trade or equivalent regulatory registration for other jurisdictions. Volume tracking for forwarder agreements must optionally segregate volumes by the forwarder's underlying shipper for reporting purposes while maintaining rate confidentiality between the carrier and the forwarder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R013	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Historical Analysis and Trend Reporting	The system must maintain complete historical records of all surcharge rates and provide trend analysis reporting capabilities. Historical data must be queryable by surcharge type, trade lane, port, container type, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar support. Standard reports must include surcharge rate trend over time, surcharge revenue contribution analysis, surcharge as a percentage of total freight revenue, comparison of surcharge levels across entities, and benchmark comparison against published industry surcharge indices. AI must identify statistically significant trends such as consistently increasing BAF on specific trade lanes or anomalous surcharge patterns that may indicate data entry errors. All reports must support bilingual output and data export in Excel, PDF, and CSV formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R005	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Permission Enforcement Middleware	Server-side middleware checks JWT claims against required permission for every protected route. Deny with 403 and log the attempt. Frontend: hide UI elements based on user permissions fetched on login. Never rely on frontend-only checks for security.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R004	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Incentive Plan Configuration and Administration	The system must support creation and administration of multiple concurrent incentive plans with effective date ranges, eligible participant groups, and performance criteria. Plan types must include individual performance bonuses, team-based pooled incentives, spot awards for exceptional deal closures, and annual performance bonuses aligned with entity fiscal calendars (Gregorian for Qatar/UAE/India, Hijri option for KSA). Each plan must define eligibility criteria (minimum tenure, performance threshold gates), earning mechanics (linear, accelerator curves, cliff vesting), and cap limits. Plans must comply with local labor law requirements: UAE Wage Protection System (WPS) for AED disbursements, KSA GOSI-compliant bonus treatment, India Income Tax TDS deduction at source, and Qatar Labor Law end-of-service benefit exclusion rules. Plan versioning must maintain full audit history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R013	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Customer Rate Card and Service Configuration at Onboarding	The system must allow configuration of customer-specific rate cards and service preferences during onboarding. Rate cards must define default pricing per trade lane and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR) with surcharge applicability rules (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS, low sulphur surcharge). Service preferences must capture preferred vessel services, default INCOTERMS per trade lane, standard documentation requirements (original BL, express release, seaway bill), preferred container pickup depots near operating ports, and invoicing preferences (consolidated monthly vs. per-shipment, currency, payment terms). Default preferences from the customer's segment template must pre-populate with override capability. Rate cards must specify validity periods and currency denomination with automatic rate expiry notifications. All configured rates must sync to the quotation and booking modules immediately upon account activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R003	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Multi-Currency Rate Display and Conversion	All rate quotations returned to the customer must display charges in the customer's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with real-time exchange rate conversion using treasury-approved daily rates. The system must show the base currency amount (USD), the converted amount, the applied exchange rate, and the rate effective date. Currency conversion must follow the entity-specific rounding rules: QAR and SAR to two decimal places, INR to nearest whole number for amounts above 1000 INR, and AED to two decimal places. Each quotation must include a validity timestamp after which the displayed rate and exchange rate are no longer guaranteed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R015	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Entity-Specific Inventory Segregation	Container availability must be segregated by operating entity such that the Qatar entity only displays inventory held at Qatari depots, the UAE entity shows inventory at UAE depots, the KSA entity at Saudi depots, and the India entity at Indian depots. Cross-entity inventory sharing must only be visible when the customer explicitly enables the multi-port search toggle and must clearly label which entity owns or manages each available unit. Reservation requests for containers from a different entity's inventory must trigger an inter-entity transfer workflow with appropriate transfer pricing and must require approval from both the source and destination entity's operations teams before confirming the reservation to the customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R004	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Language and Locale Preferences for Notifications	Each customer user must be able to set their preferred notification language as Arabic, English, or bilingual (both Arabic and English in the same notification). For Arabic notifications, the system must use Modern Standard Arabic with proper right-to-left text rendering in email HTML templates, correctly handle mixed LTR/RTL content (such as container numbers and port codes embedded in Arabic text using Unicode bidirectional controls), and use Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) for dates and amounts if the user selects the Arabic numeral preference. Date formats in notifications must follow the user's calendar preference: Gregorian (DD/MM/YYYY), Hijri (DD/MM/YYYY Hijri), or dual display showing both calendar dates side by side.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R011	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Bulk Tariff Upload and Validation	The system must support bulk tariff creation and modification through structured file upload in Excel and CSV formats. The upload template must be downloadable from the system with pre-populated reference data including port codes, container type codes, currency codes, and commodity classifications. The system must perform comprehensive validation upon upload including data type checks, referential integrity validation against master data, duplicate detection, rate reasonableness checks against configurable bounds, and currency format validation. Validation errors must be reported with exact row and column references in a downloadable error report. AI must auto-correct common data entry errors such as swapped origin-destination ports and suggest corrections for invalid codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R015	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Reporting and Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive reporting capabilities for rate agreement analytics including portfolio analysis across all active agreements per entity. Standard reports must include agreement utilization summary, revenue by agreement versus spot, margin analysis by agreement, volume commitment achievement rates, top 20 agreements by revenue, and agreements at risk of underperformance. Reports must be filterable by entity, region, trade lane, customer segment, and time period. Export functionality must support Excel, PDF, and CSV formats with bilingual column headers. AI must generate a monthly commercial intelligence brief summarizing portfolio performance, identifying trends, and recommending strategic actions for rate agreement management across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R014	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Communication and Customer Notification	The system must automate customer notification processes for surcharge changes. Notification templates must be maintainable in both Arabic and English with dynamic field insertion for surcharge type, old rate, new rate, effective date, and applicable scope. The system must support targeted notifications based on customer trade lane activity, ensuring customers only receive surcharge change notifications relevant to their shipping patterns. Notification channels must include email, in-app notification on the customer portal, and SMS for urgent surcharge changes. The system must track notification delivery status and maintain proof of notification for regulatory compliance. Notifications must be queued and sent according to the configured lead time before the surcharge effective date, with automated follow-up reminders at configurable intervals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R006	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Module-Level Access Control	Each CS ERP module can be enabled/disabled per company subscription tier. Users with correct role but module not enabled see upgrade prompt. Super-admin can enable individual modules per company independent of subscription.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R005	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	AI-Powered Sales Forecast and Pipeline Prediction	The system must generate AI-driven sales forecasts at individual, team, entity, and consolidated levels using historical booking patterns, pipeline stage progression rates, seasonal trade volume trends (accounting for Ramadan, Eid, Diwali, Chinese New Year cargo surges), and macroeconomic indicators relevant to container shipping. The forecast model must predict monthly TEU volume and revenue for a rolling 6-month horizon with confidence intervals. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actuals with model performance metrics (MAPE, bias) visible to commercial directors. The AI must flag deals in the pipeline with stagnation risk (no stage progression in 14+ days) and recommend next-best-actions. Forecasts must be generatable in both Gregorian and Hijri monthly aggregations for KSA regional reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R014	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Onboarding Analytics and Conversion Funnel Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive onboarding analytics including total applications by source (direct, referral, website, trade show, agent), conversion rates from application to activated account by entity and customer type, average onboarding duration trends with month-over-month comparison, rejection rates by reason category (KYC failure, credit denial, incomplete documentation, customer withdrawal), and first-booking conversion rate within 30/60/90 days post-activation. The analytics must support segmentation by originating port region, customer type, and industry vertical. AI must identify onboarding drop-off patterns and recommend process improvements (e.g., specific document requirements causing disproportionate delays). Reports must be exportable in Excel and PDF with Arabic column headers and RTL layout when Arabic locale is active. Dashboard data must refresh hourly and support both Hijri and Gregorian date range filtering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R004	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	AI-Powered Rate Suggestion Engine	The system must provide AI-generated rate suggestions within 30 seconds of a customer submitting a spot rate request. The AI engine must analyze at least 12 months of historical rate data for the specific trade lane, current vessel utilization percentages, bunker fuel surcharge indices (BAF), seasonal demand patterns for the Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade, and competitor rate benchmarks where available. The suggestion must include a confidence score (0-100%), a recommended rate range (low/mid/high), and a brief AI-generated justification in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English). Rate suggestions for hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9) must include the applicable dangerous goods surcharge.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R016	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Container Return Location Guidance	The system must display approved empty container return locations for each port, including the depot name, address with GPS coordinates for driver navigation, gate operating hours (accounting for local weekday patterns and Hijri-based holidays), accepted container types, and any current restrictions such as temporary gate closures or overflow conditions. When multiple return depots serve a single port, the system must recommend the optimal return location based on the customer's trucking origin to minimize deadhead distance, current depot gate queue times sourced from the port community system, and any depot-specific incentives such as waived lift-on charges. Return location data must be presented in both Arabic and English with addresses transliterated appropriately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R005	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Timezone-Aware Notification Scheduling	All notification timestamps must be displayed in the recipient's configured timezone with the timezone abbreviation clearly shown (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST+0 for KSA, IST for India). The system must support a quiet hours configuration where non-urgent notifications are queued and delivered in a digest format at the start of the next business day. Default quiet hours must be 22:00-06:00 local time, configurable by the customer. Urgent notifications (vessel delay exceeding 24 hours, customs hold on cargo, demurrage free-time expiry) must override quiet hours and deliver immediately. Notifications triggered by system events must calculate the correct local time for the recipient, not the server time or the port's local time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R012	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Expiry Management and Renewal Alerts	The system must track tariff expiry dates and proactively generate renewal alerts at configurable intervals before expiration (default: 60, 30, and 15 days). Alerts must be sent to the responsible commercial team members and their supervisors via in-app notifications and email in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must prevent booking acceptance against expired tariffs and display clear warnings when tariffs are within 30 days of expiry during the quotation process. AI must analyze the renewal candidate tariff against current market conditions and automatically generate a draft renewal tariff with recommended rate adjustments, reducing manual effort by populating all fields from the expiring tariff with AI-suggested modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R016	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Offline Access for Field Sales	The system must provide offline access to rate agreement summaries and customer-specific rate cards for field sales personnel. The offline module must cache active agreements for the salesperson's assigned customer portfolio with the latest rates, volume commitment status, and agreement expiry dates. When offline, users must be able to create draft spot rate quotations that synchronize to the server upon reconnection for formal approval processing. The offline cache must enforce data security through device-level encryption and remote wipe capability. Synchronization upon reconnection must handle conflicts where the server agreement has been amended during the offline period, presenting the user with a clear comparison of the cached versus current agreement terms for resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R015	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	IMO 2020 and Environmental Surcharge Compliance	The system must specifically support environmental surcharges related to IMO 2020 low-sulphur fuel regulations and other emerging environmental mandates. This includes the Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS) or Environmental Fuel Fee (EFF) calculated based on the differential cost between compliant low-sulphur fuel (VLSFO 0.5%) and traditional high-sulphur fuel (HSFO 3.5%). The calculation must support different compliant fuel cost bases for different trading regions. The system must also support future environmental surcharges such as EU Emissions Trading System (EU ETS) charges for vessels calling European ports and potential Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) related surcharges. AI must monitor regulatory developments in maritime environmental compliance and proactively alert commercial teams about new surcharge requirements that may need to be implemented.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R007	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Field-Level Visibility Control	Certain fields (e.g., salary, margin %) visible only to specific roles. Define field visibility rules per role in configuration. Applied in API response serialization, never in UI-only filtering. Finance fields hidden from non-finance roles.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R006	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Activity Tracking and Effort Metrics	The system must track granular sales activities including customer visits (with GPS check-in at port/customer office locations near Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), phone calls logged with duration, emails sent and received, quotations generated, proposals submitted, and contracts signed. Each activity must be attributable to an account, opportunity, and trade lane. The system must compute effort-to-outcome ratios such as visits per new booking, quotations-to-conversion rate, and average deal cycle time per trade lane. Activity targets must be configurable per role (hunter vs. farmer profiles) and entity. Weekly activity reports must auto-generate for team managers with AI-highlighted anomalies (e.g., high activity but low conversion suggesting pricing or service issues). Offline activity logging must be supported for areas with poor connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R015	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Bulk Onboarding for Acquisition and Migration Scenarios	The system must support bulk customer onboarding via structured data import for scenarios including agency acquisitions, portfolio transfers from competitor carriers, and system migration from legacy platforms. The bulk import must accept CSV and Excel file formats with templates matching entity-specific required fields. Imported records must undergo the same validation rules as individual onboarding (KYC screening, duplicate detection, trade license validation) with batch processing and exception reporting. The system must generate a comprehensive import reconciliation report showing successful imports, failed records with specific failure reasons, and records requiring manual intervention. Bulk-imported customers must be flagged for expedited document collection if imported without complete documentation. The import process must support Arabic company names in UTF-8 encoding and handle mixed Arabic/English data within the same import file.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R005	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Request Status Tracking Dashboard	The portal must display a real-time dashboard showing all rate requests with statuses: Submitted, Under Review, Quotation Ready, Accepted, Expired, or Rejected. Customers must be able to filter requests by status, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors), trade lane, and container type. The dashboard must show average response time per request, highlight requests pending customer action with visual badges, and provide a countdown timer for quotations nearing expiry. The dashboard layout must support Arabic RTL rendering with mirrored column ordering, right-aligned numeric fields, and Arabic-localized status labels including proper Tashkeel for ambiguous terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R001	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage Cost Estimate Creation	The system must allow creation of a detailed voyage cost estimate (VCE) for each planned voyage, capturing all anticipated cost categories including bunker fuel (VLSFO, LSMGO, HSFO), port disbursements at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA), canal transit fees (Suez Canal tolls based on SCNT tariff), crew wages, insurance premiums, and vessel hire or charter costs. The VCE must support multi-currency entry with base amounts in USD and automatic conversion to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR using configurable exchange rate sources. Each cost line item must be linked to a GL account code and cost center for downstream financial posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R010	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage Budget Template Management	The system must support creation and maintenance of reusable voyage budget templates based on trade lanes, vessel classes, and service rotations. Templates must pre-populate standard cost categories with default estimated amounts derived from AI analysis of the last 10 completed voyages on the same route. Operations planners must be able to clone templates, adjust individual line items, and save as new versions. Template versioning must maintain full history with diff comparison between versions. Templates must be entity-specific (separate templates for Qatar operations versus India operations) while also supporting a global template library accessible across entities with appropriate permission controls and multi-currency default amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R003	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Agent Fund Advance Request Processing	The system must facilitate fund advance requests from port agents based on approved PDAs. When a PDA is approved, the system must calculate the required advance amount (typically 100-110% of estimated disbursements to cover contingencies), generate a fund request linked to the PDA reference, route the request through the treasury approval workflow, and upon approval, trigger a payment instruction to the banking module. Fund advances must track the agent's bank account details, preferred currency, and any standing advance balances. The system must enforce credit limits per agent and flag situations where cumulative outstanding advances exceed configurable thresholds. Multi-entity scenarios must be handled where the disbursing entity differs from the vessel-owning entity, requiring intercompany fund transfer recording.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R012	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Automated GL Posting of Disbursements	The system must automatically generate general ledger journal entries from approved FDA line items based on configurable posting rules. Each charge category must map to a specific GL account (e.g., pilotage to account 51100, towage to 51200, berth hire to 51300), cost center (derived from vessel, voyage, and operating entity), and profit center (derived from trade lane). Journal entries must be generated in the entity's functional currency with foreign currency reference amounts preserved. The posting engine must support both real-time posting upon FDA approval and batch posting during end-of-day processing. Reversal entries for PDA-based accruals must be automatically generated when the corresponding FDA is posted. Posting validation must check for period closure, budget availability, and GL account validity before committing entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R004	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Over-Time Revenue Recognition Based on Voyage Progress	The system must recognize freight revenue over time as the transportation performance obligation is satisfied, measured by the output method based on voyage progress. Progress must be calculated as the proportion of transit time elapsed or nautical miles sailed relative to the total estimated transit time or distance for the relevant voyage leg. The system must capture vessel position data (from AIS integration or manual position reports) to automatically update voyage progress percentages. Revenue recognition journal entries must be generated daily or at configurable intervals, debiting accrued revenue (contract asset) and crediting freight revenue accounts with proper GL coding per entity, vessel, voyage, and trade lane. Month-end cut-off processing must ensure revenue is recognized up to the period end date with precise progress measurement, handling time zone differences across Gulf and Indian ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R008	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	Audit Log for Access Events	Log every access-denied event with: user_id, resource, action, timestamp, IP, reason. Log every role/permission change with: who changed, what changed, before/after state, timestamp. Retain audit logs for 7 years. Queryable via admin panel with date/user/resource filters.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R002	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	AI-Driven Bunker Cost Forecasting	The system must provide an AI-powered bunker fuel cost forecasting engine that analyzes historical consumption data, planned route distances, vessel speed profiles, weather routing inputs, and current bunker price indices from major bunkering ports (Fujairah, Singapore, Rotterdam). The forecast must account for IMO 2020 sulphur cap compliance fuel grades, calculate estimated consumption per voyage leg in metric tonnes, and apply projected price per tonne with confidence intervals. Forecasts must auto-populate the VCE bunker line items and support manual override with audit trail logging the AI-suggested versus user-entered values for variance reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R011	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Bunker ROB Tracking and Valuation	The system must track bunker Remaining on Board (ROB) quantities at voyage commencement and completion, valuing fuel inventory using FIFO, LIFO, or weighted average costing methods as configured per entity accounting policy. ROB adjustments at voyage boundaries must be recorded as inter-voyage cost transfers, ensuring accurate voyage P&L attribution. The system must capture bunker survey reports at key events (departure, arrival, bunkering operations) with quantities in metric tonnes and volumes in cubic meters at standard temperature. Valuation must handle blended fuel scenarios where VLSFO from different bunkering ports at different prices is commingled in vessel tanks, maintaining lot-level traceability for cost allocation accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R004	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	FDA Receipt and Digitization	The system must support receipt of Final Disbursement Accounts (FDA) from port agents via electronic submission (structured JSON/XML or PDF upload) and manual entry. For PDF uploads, the AI-powered document processing engine must extract line items including charge descriptions, amounts, currencies, tax components, and supporting voucher references using OCR and NLP techniques. The extraction must handle Arabic-language invoices from Gulf port authorities and English-language documents from Indian ports. Extracted data must be presented for user verification with confidence scores per field, highlighting low-confidence extractions for manual review. The FDA must be linked to the originating PDA and voyage record, with the system automatically mapping FDA line items to corresponding PDA estimate categories for variance pre-computation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R013	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	PDA Benchmarking Against Market Rates	The system must benchmark PDA estimates against market rate databases and historical averages to identify potential overcharging or anomalies before funds are advanced. The benchmarking engine must compare each PDA line item against the average cost for the same charge category at the same port for similar vessel sizes over the previous 12 months. Items exceeding the benchmark by more than a configurable threshold (default 20%) must be flagged for review with AI-generated commentary explaining whether the deviation is justified by known factors (tariff increases, vessel size differences, seasonal surcharges). This pre-advance validation reduces financial exposure from inflated PDAs and provides commercial leverage in agent negotiations. Benchmark data must be segmented by vessel size range, cargo type, and season.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R005	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Bill of Lading Revenue Allocation Engine	The system must allocate revenue to individual bills of lading (B/Ls) based on the freight rate structure in each booking or service contract. For container shipping, allocation must handle per-container rates (different for 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC), per-TEU rates, weight-based rates (applicable for heavy cargo), and ad valorem rates (for high-value shipments). The allocation engine must handle consolidation scenarios where multiple B/Ls share container space (LCL shipments), and must correctly attribute revenue when a single B/L covers multiple containers. Revenue allocation must respect INCOTERMS implications on the scope of service (e.g., CIF includes freight and insurance, while FOB freight is typically to buyer's account). All allocations must be traceable and auditable for IFRS 15 disclosure requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R012	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Service Contract Volume Commitment Revenue Adjustments	The system must manage revenue adjustments arising from volume commitment clauses in annual or multi-year freight service contracts. When a contract specifies tiered pricing based on annual TEU volumes (e.g., 0-500 TEU at USD 1,200 per TEU, 501-1000 TEU at USD 1,100, over 1000 TEU at USD 1,000), the system must estimate the expected annual volume using AI analysis of booking patterns and historical data, apply the estimated blended rate for revenue recognition throughout the contract year, and adjust retrospectively when actual volumes trigger tier changes. Revenue adjustments must be computed quarterly at minimum and at contract anniversary. The variable consideration constraint must be applied, with the system maintaining a provision for potential revenue reversals if volume projections are uncertain. Contract-level revenue tracking must aggregate all B/Ls under a service contract for volume threshold monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R003	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Address Commission and Brokerage Handling	The system must separately track and calculate address commission and brokerage commissions as distinct from agency commissions, as these are standard deductions in charter party and freight contract negotiations. Address commission (typically 2.5% deducted from charter hire or freight by the charterer) must be calculated automatically from charter hire invoices or freight invoices as applicable, with the system maintaining the payee details (charterer, freight forwarder, or designated third party). Brokerage commissions (typically 1.25% payable to the shipbroker who negotiated the fixture) must be tracked per charter party or contract with automatic calculation upon revenue recognition. The system must maintain separate GL accounts for address commission expense and brokerage expense, distinguish them from standard agency commissions, and ensure proper cost center allocation to the relevant voyage for accurate voyage P&L reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R005	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Electronic Multimodal Bill of Lading (eMBL)	The system must support issuance of electronic Multimodal Bills of Lading conforming to DCSA standards and compatible with platforms such as Bolero, essDOCS, and TradeLens (or successor platforms). The eMBL must carry the same legal validity as paper originals per the UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Transferable Records adopted by Bahrain and UAE. Digital signatures must use PKI certificates issued by a recognized Certificate Authority. Title transfer of negotiable eMBLs must be managed through a secure registry recording each transfer with transferor, transferee, timestamp, and transfer reference. The system must support parallel issuance where some parties require paper and others accept electronic originals, with a master record linking both formats to prevent double negotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R003	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Multi-Leg Voyage Budget Structure	The system must support multi-leg voyage budget structures where a single round-trip voyage (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT via Jebel Ali and return) is decomposed into discrete legs, each with independent cost budgets. Each leg must capture port-pair identifiers using UN/LOCODE, estimated sea days, port days, canal transit days, and idle days. Budget rollup must aggregate leg-level costs into a total voyage budget with drill-down capability. The system must handle complex itineraries with up to 20 legs per voyage, supporting both liner service rotations and tramp voyage patterns common in regional Gulf and Indian subcontinent trades.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R012	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage Cost Accrual Processing	The system must support automatic accrual of anticipated voyage costs at month-end and period-end closing for voyages in progress. The accrual engine must estimate unbilled costs based on voyage completion percentage (calculated by elapsed days or distance sailed versus planned totals), applying proportional cost recognition against the approved budget. Accrual journal entries must be auto-generated with proper GL coding, cost center allocation, and entity tagging. Reversal entries must be automatically posted at the start of the subsequent period. The accrual process must handle multi-entity scenarios where a single voyage incurs costs across Qatar, UAE, and India entities, generating intercompany accrual entries with matching elimination entries for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R005	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	PDA to FDA Variance Analysis	The system must perform detailed line-by-line variance analysis between the PDA estimates and FDA actuals for each port call. Variance must be computed in both absolute amount and percentage terms per charge category, with automatic classification of variances as favorable or unfavorable. The analysis must identify the top contributing categories to total variance and provide AI-generated explanations for common variance patterns (e.g., additional tug usage due to weather, extended berth stay from cargo delays, unplanned launch hire). Variance reports must be filterable by port, agent, vessel, time period, and operating entity. Historical variance trending must track agent estimation accuracy over time, computing a reliability score per agent per port to inform future PDA adjustments and agent performance evaluations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R014	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Disbursement Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive disbursement reports at multiple levels: per port call, per voyage, per vessel, per port, per agent, per entity, and consolidated across all entities. Reports must include PDA versus FDA comparisons, advance versus settlement reconciliation, outstanding advances aging, dispute status summaries, and trend analytics. All reports must support dual-language output (English and Arabic) with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. Date filtering must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selections. Export formats must include PDF, Excel, and CSV. The consolidated multi-entity report must handle intercompany disbursement eliminations and present figures in both local entity currencies and a common reporting currency (USD). Scheduled report generation and email distribution must be configurable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R006	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Contract Modification Handling	The system must handle freight contract modifications in accordance with IFRS 15 guidance, determining whether a modification should be treated as a separate contract, prospective adjustment, or cumulative catch-up. Common shipping contract modifications include route changes (e.g., transshipment via an additional port adding a new leg), cargo quantity amendments, rate renegotiations for ongoing service contracts, and change of discharge port. The system must evaluate whether the modification adds distinct services at standalone selling prices (separate contract treatment), adds distinct services not at standalone prices (prospective treatment), or does not add distinct services (cumulative catch-up). Each modification must be documented with the accounting treatment rationale, approved by the revenue accounting team, and reflected in the revenue recognition schedule with proper adjustment entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R013	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	IFRS 15 Disclosure Report Generation	The system must generate all required IFRS 15 disclosure information for financial statement preparation including disaggregation of revenue by geography (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, other), service type (ocean freight, inland transport, ancillary services), contract type (spot, service contract, volume contract), and timing of recognition (over time versus point in time). Additional disclosures must include contract balances (receivables, contract assets, contract liabilities) with opening and closing reconciliations, remaining performance obligations and expected timing of recognition, and significant judgments made in applying IFRS 15 (determination of performance obligations, methods for measuring progress, constraint on variable consideration). Reports must be produced per entity for statutory reporting and consolidated for group reporting, with multi-currency presentation and Arabic translation capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R004	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Accrual at Period End	The system must automatically generate commission accrual entries at each accounting period end for commissions earned but not yet paid. The accrual engine must compute commissions on all revenue recognized during the period that has not yet been commission-settled, applying the appropriate commission rates per agent agreement. Accrual journal entries must debit commission expense accounts and credit commission payable (accrued liability) accounts with proper entity, cost center, and voyage coding. Opening period reversals of prior period accruals must be automatically generated. The accrual must handle partial voyage scenarios where revenue is recognized over time but the commission is only payable upon voyage completion, maintaining a running accrual balance that aligns with the cumulative revenue recognized. Multi-entity accruals must be computed independently per entity with proper intercompany treatment where agents operate across entity boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R010	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Budget and Forecast Management	The system must support annual commission budgeting and rolling forecasting based on revenue projections and contracted commission rate structures. The budgeting module must allow finance teams to input projected revenue by trade lane, agent, and entity, then automatically compute expected commission expenses using the configured rate structures. AI must enhance forecasts by analyzing booking pipeline data, historical commission-to-revenue ratios, and anticipated commission rate renegotiations. Budget versus actual commission expense tracking must be available at the agent, trade lane, port, vessel, and entity levels with variance analysis highlighting areas of overcommitment or underperformance. The forecast must account for seasonality patterns specific to the Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade (Ramadan slowdowns, monsoon impacts on Indian west coast ports, Hajj season volume spikes in Saudi ports) and present projections in both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R004	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Budget Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for voyage budgets before operational commitment. Approval routing must be determined by voyage budget value thresholds (e.g., budgets exceeding USD 500,000 require CFO approval), operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India entities each having separate approval chains), and vessel type (owned versus chartered). The workflow engine must support parallel and sequential approval steps, automatic escalation after configurable SLA periods, delegation of authority during approver absence, and mobile-responsive approval interfaces. All approval actions must be timestamped with Hijri and Gregorian dates and support Arabic-language comments in the approval notes field.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R013	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage Deviation Cost Impact Analysis	The system must calculate the financial impact of voyage deviations including unscheduled port calls, weather routing diversions, emergency bunkering stops, and cargo-related delays. When a deviation is recorded, the AI engine must compute incremental costs (additional bunker consumption, extra port charges, potential canal toll differences, crew overtime) and compare the revised voyage cost against the original budget. Deviation cost reports must classify causes (weather, mechanical, commercial, regulatory) and attribute incremental costs accordingly. The system must maintain a deviation cost database for pattern analysis, enabling AI to factor historical deviation frequency and costs into future voyage budget estimates for specific trade lanes and seasonal periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R006	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Port Charge Category Standardization	The system must enforce a standardized chart of port charge categories mapped to a global taxonomy aligned with BIMCO DA guidelines, even though individual ports and agents may use varying terminology. The mapping engine must maintain aliases and local terms (in English and Arabic) that map to standard categories such as pilotage inward, pilotage outward, towage, berth hire, mooring and unmooring, port dues, light dues, anchorage dues, quarantine, immigration, customs overtime, agency fees, and miscellaneous. When FDA items are received with non-standard descriptions, the AI must suggest category mappings based on historical patterns and textual similarity. This standardization enables consistent cross-port cost comparison and accurate GL account posting regardless of local terminology variations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R015	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Disbursement Budget Integration with Voyage Costing	The system must seamlessly integrate port disbursement data with the voyage costing module, ensuring that PDA estimates feed into voyage budgets and FDA actuals update voyage actual costs in real-time. When a PDA is generated or revised, the corresponding voyage budget port cost line items must be automatically updated. When an FDA is approved and posted, the voyage actual port costs must reflect the FDA amounts, triggering budget-to-actual variance recalculation. The integration must handle scenarios where a single voyage touches multiple ports across different entities (e.g., Hamad Port under Qatar entity, Jebel Ali under UAE entity, JNPT under India entity), ensuring each entity's disbursement costs are attributed to the correct entity within the voyage cost structure while maintaining a consolidated voyage-level view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R007	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Deferred Revenue and Contract Liability Management	The system must track and manage deferred revenue (contract liabilities) arising from advance freight payments received before the performance obligation is satisfied. When freight is collected prepaid (common for import shipments where the shipper pays before vessel sailing), the system must record the receipt as a contract liability and release it to revenue over the voyage transit period proportional to performance obligation completion. The deferred revenue ledger must support aging analysis, showing balances by vessel, voyage, B/L, and customer. Period-end reporting must clearly present the opening balance, additions from new prepayments, releases to revenue, and closing balance of contract liabilities. Multi-currency tracking must maintain deferred revenue in both the transaction currency and the entity functional currency, with exchange rate remeasurement at each reporting date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R014	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Revenue Reversal and Credit Note Processing	The system must process freight revenue reversals and credit notes in compliance with IFRS 15 requirements, ensuring that revenue previously recognized is correctly reversed when performance obligations are not met, contracts are cancelled, or billing errors are identified. Revenue reversal scenarios specific to container shipping include cargo rolled to a subsequent voyage (requiring re-recognition timing adjustment), cargo short-shipped, container lost or damaged in transit, rate correction after B/L issuance, and customer dispute settlements. Each reversal must be linked to the original revenue recognition entries with clear audit trail. Credit notes must be approved through a configurable workflow with segregation of duties. The system must ensure that revenue reversals are reflected in the correct accounting period (current period if error is immaterial, prior period restatement if material) and update all affected IFRS 15 schedules and disclosure reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R005	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Statement Generation and Distribution	The system must generate detailed commission statements for each agent showing all transactions on which commission has been calculated, the applicable rates, computed commission amounts, any adjustments or deductions, withholding tax amounts, and the net payable. Commission statements must be producible monthly, quarterly, or at configurable intervals aligned with agent payment terms. Statements must be formatted in the agent's preferred language (English or Arabic) with proper RTL rendering for Arabic, include both Hijri and Gregorian dates for Gulf-based agents, and show amounts in both the transaction currency and the settlement currency. Statement distribution must support automated email dispatch with PDF attachment, secure portal download, and manual print. Each statement must include a unique reference number and reconciliation details linking back to specific B/L numbers and voyage references for agent verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R006	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Comparison and Historical Analysis	The system must allow customers to compare up to five rate quotations side by side, displaying freight charges, surcharges (THC, BAF, CAF, ISPS, documentation fees), total landed cost per INCOTERM basis, transit time, and transshipment points. A historical rate trend chart must show the customer's accepted rates over the past 12 months for the selected trade lane with an overlay of market index rates. The comparison view must clearly highlight the lowest total cost option and the fastest transit option. All comparison data must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers (Arabic/English) and the entity's commercial letterhead.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R005	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Real-Time Budget vs Actual Tracking	The system must provide real-time comparison of voyage budget estimates against actual costs as they are posted from port disbursement accounts, bunker delivery notes, invoice processing, and operational expense claims. Variance calculations must be presented as absolute amounts and percentages at each cost category level and total voyage level. The dashboard must highlight variances exceeding configurable thresholds with color-coded alerts (green within 5%, amber 5-15%, red over 15%). All amounts must be displayable in the voyage base currency (typically USD) and in the reporting currency of the controlling entity. Historical trend analysis must show budget accuracy patterns across the last 12 months of completed voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R014	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Multi-Currency Voyage Cost Consolidation	The system must consolidate voyage costs denominated in multiple currencies (USD for bunker and hire, QAR for Hamad Port charges, AED for Jebel Ali charges, SAR for Saudi port charges, INR for JNPT charges, EUR for certain insurance premiums) into a single voyage reporting currency. Exchange rate application must follow configurable rules: transaction date spot rate for actual costs, budget rate for estimate-to-actual comparisons, and month-end closing rate for balance sheet items. The system must calculate and separately report foreign exchange gains and losses arising from rate differences between cost booking date and payment date. FX impact must be excluded from operational voyage performance metrics to provide clean operational cost visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R007	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Multi-Currency FDA Settlement and Reconciliation	The system must process FDA settlements in the local currency of the disbursement while maintaining full multi-currency accounting. For example, Jebel Ali FDAs denominated in AED must be settled in AED with corresponding USD and entity functional currency (QAR for Qatar entity) postings at applicable exchange rates. The settlement process must reconcile the advance paid to the agent against the FDA total, computing the balance due to or from the agent. Credit notes for overpayments must be tracked and applied against future disbursements from the same agent. The system must handle split settlements where partial FDA amounts are disputed, allowing approved portions to be settled while disputed items remain in a suspense account pending resolution. All settlement transactions must generate proper accounting entries with exchange rate gain or loss recognition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R016	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Hijri Calendar Support for Saudi Disbursements	The system must fully support Hijri calendar dates for all port disbursement transactions at Saudi Arabian ports (King Abdulaziz Port Dammam SAKAC, Jeddah Islamic Port SAJED, King Fahd Industrial Port Jubail SAJUB). Saudi port authority invoices frequently reference Hijri dates as the primary date, and the system must capture, store, and display both Hijri and Gregorian dates for each transaction. Date conversion must be bidirectional and accurate. Financial period mapping must align with the Saudi fiscal year where applicable. The system must handle the discrepancy where Saudi government agencies may use Hijri fiscal periods while the shipping company uses Gregorian periods, providing mapping and reconciliation capabilities. All Saudi disbursement reports must offer Hijri date display as the primary option with Gregorian equivalent shown alongside.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R008	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Freight Surcharge Revenue Recognition Rules	The system must apply specific revenue recognition rules for each type of freight surcharge, determining whether each surcharge is a separate performance obligation or part of the main transportation obligation. Surcharges to be configured include Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF), Peak Season Surcharge (PSS), Emergency Bunker Surcharge (EBS), Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS), War Risk Surcharge, Congestion Surcharge, Reefer Surcharge, Overweight Surcharge, and Out-of-Gauge Surcharge. The system must maintain a surcharge-to-revenue-account mapping table where each surcharge type is assigned a recognition pattern (recognized with freight over time, or recognized at a point in time upon specific event). The configuration must be entity-specific as different entities may adopt different accounting policy elections within the IFRS 15 framework.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R015	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	AI-Powered Revenue Forecasting	The system must provide AI-driven freight revenue forecasting based on the current booking pipeline, historical revenue patterns, market rate trends, and vessel deployment schedule. Forecasts must project revenue by week, month, and quarter for a rolling 12-month horizon, segmented by trade lane, customer, vessel, and entity. The AI engine must incorporate seasonality factors (e.g., higher volumes before Ramadan, reduced activity during Eid holidays observable in Hijri calendar, Indian festival season surges), contractual minimum quantity commitments, and market intelligence on competitor pricing and capacity changes. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actuals with model performance metrics displayed. Revenue forecasts must feed into the budgeting module and treasury cash flow projections, providing confidence intervals alongside point estimates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R006	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Withholding Tax on Commission Payments	The system must calculate and apply withholding tax on commission payments according to the tax regulations of each operating jurisdiction. For India operations, Tax Deducted at Source (TDS) under Section 194H at the applicable rate (currently 5% for resident agents, 20% for non-resident agents without PAN) must be computed on commission payments exceeding the annual threshold. For KSA operations, withholding tax at 15% must be applied on commission payments to non-resident agents. The system must track withholding tax certificates (Form 16A in India), maintain cumulative annual payment and withholding records per agent, and generate returns data for tax filing. Double Tax Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) rates must be configurable where a lower treaty rate applies based on the agent's country of tax residence. Tax registration numbers (GSTIN for India, TRN for UAE and KSA) must be validated and stored per agent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R007	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Surcharge Transparency and Breakdown	Every rate quotation must include a fully itemized surcharge breakdown listing each applicable charge with its code, description in both Arabic and English, calculation basis (per container, per TEU, per bill of lading, or percentage of freight), effective date, and regulatory or tariff reference. Mandatory surcharges must include Terminal Handling Charges at origin and destination (with port-specific rates for QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), Bunker Adjustment Factor, Currency Adjustment Factor, ISPS security surcharge, and any seasonal peak surcharges. The system must flag newly introduced or recently changed surcharges with a visual indicator and link to the official tariff circular.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-001-R001	FEAT-000-2-001	\N	Tenant Company Data Model	Each tenant is a company record: id (UUID), name, trade_name_arabic, commercial_registration_number, country (QA/AE/SA/IN), data_residency_region, subscription_tier, subscription_expires_at, status (active/suspended/trial/cancelled), created_at. Company has one primary_admin user.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R006	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L Statement Generation	The system must generate a comprehensive voyage profit and loss statement consolidating all revenue streams (freight income, demurrage, detention charges, THC recoveries) against all cost components (bunker, port charges, canal tolls, agency fees, stevedoring, cargo handling, insurance, vessel operating costs). The voyage P&L must be producible at any point during the voyage lifecycle from estimation through to final settlement. Multi-currency translation must convert all foreign currency items to the voyage reporting currency using actual transaction rates for costs and contractually agreed rates for revenue. The P&L format must comply with shipping industry standard voyage accounting practices and support export to PDF with Arabic and English dual-language headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R015	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Offline Voyage Costing Capability	The system must support offline voyage cost entry and budget review for users operating in areas with limited or no internet connectivity, such as vessel-based superintendents or port captains at anchorage. The offline module must cache the current voyage budget, cost categories, GL codes, and recent exchange rates locally on the device. Cost entries made offline must be queued with local timestamps and synchronized to the central system upon connectivity restoration, with conflict detection and resolution for any concurrent edits. The offline interface must support both English and Arabic languages with proper RTL rendering and allow attachment of supporting documents (bunker delivery notes, port invoices) as offline-cached files for later upload.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R008	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Port Agent Performance Scoring	The system must maintain an AI-driven port agent performance scorecard evaluating agents across multiple dimensions: PDA estimation accuracy (measured by average PDA-FDA variance percentage), FDA submission timeliness (days between vessel departure and FDA receipt), documentation quality (completeness of supporting vouchers and receipts), cost competitiveness (agent fees and handling charges benchmarked against peer agents at the same port), and responsiveness metrics. Scores must be computed automatically from transactional data with configurable weighting per dimension. Agent scorecards must be viewable in both English and Arabic interfaces, exportable as PDF reports with bilingual headers, and accessible in a dashboard showing trend lines over rolling 6 and 12 month periods. Scores must feed into the procurement module for agent appointment and renewal decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R017	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Proforma Disbursement Account Revision Control	The system must support multiple revisions of a PDA as voyage plans change before the vessel's arrival at port. Each revision must be tracked as a separate version (PDA-R1, PDA-R2, etc.) with a clear change log showing which line items were added, modified, or removed compared to the previous version. Fund advance adjustments (additional advance requests or partial refund requests) must be automatically calculated based on the difference between the current and previous PDA revision totals. All PDA revisions must maintain linkage to the voyage schedule record and reflect changes in expected port stay duration, cargo volume adjustments, or additional services requested. The most recent approved PDA revision must be the baseline for FDA variance analysis, with earlier revisions preserved for historical audit trail and estimation accuracy improvement analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R009	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Multi-Entity Revenue Allocation for Transshipment Cargo	The system must allocate freight revenue across multiple operating entities when cargo transships between vessels or services operated by different entities within the group. For example, a container moving from JNPT (India entity vessel) to Jebel Ali (transshipment hub) to Hamad Port (Qatar entity vessel) must have its total freight revenue allocated between the India and Qatar entities based on the relative distance, cost, or standalone selling price of each leg. Intercompany freight revenue and cost allocations must be generated automatically with proper transfer pricing documentation. The system must ensure that consolidated group revenue eliminates intercompany transactions while each entity's standalone financial statements reflect the appropriate revenue share. Transfer pricing methods must be configurable and compliant with each jurisdiction's transfer pricing regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R016	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Intercompany Revenue Elimination for Consolidation	The system must automatically identify and generate elimination entries for intercompany freight revenue when cargo is transported across entities within the group. For slot purchase and sale arrangements between group entities, the system must track the buying entity's cost and the selling entity's revenue, generating elimination journal entries at consolidation. The elimination engine must handle currency differences (e.g., India entity records intercompany cost in INR while Qatar entity records intercompany revenue in QAR) using the group's consolidation exchange rate policy. Unrealized profits on intercompany transactions where cargo is still in transit at period-end must be calculated and eliminated. The system must produce an intercompany reconciliation report matching revenues and costs across entities with automated identification and flagging of unmatched balances exceeding a configurable tolerance threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R007	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Dispute and Adjustment Processing	The system must provide a structured workflow for processing commission disputes raised by agents and internal commission adjustments. When an agent contests a commission calculation, the dispute must be logged with the agent reference, affected period, claimed amount versus calculated amount, and supporting documentation. The dispute resolution workflow must route through commercial and finance reviewers with configurable approval thresholds for different adjustment amounts. Approved adjustments must generate correcting journal entries with clear linkage to the original commission transactions. Commission clawback scenarios (e.g., when freight revenue is reversed due to cargo cancellation or credit note issuance) must be automatically identified and reflected as negative commission adjustments in the next statement. All adjustments must maintain a complete audit trail with timestamps, user identifiers, and approval records accessible in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R004	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Bunker cost calculation with ROB adjustments	The system must calculate bunker costs for each voyage using actual consumption data, adjusting for Remaining on Board (ROB) at voyage start and end. Bunker types must be tracked separately: VLSFO, LSMGO, and HSFO where applicable under IMO 2020 regulations. The cost must be computed using weighted average cost method or FIFO based on entity configuration. When a vessel bunkered at multiple ports during the voyage (e.g., Jebel Ali and JNPT), each bunkering event with its unit price, quantity, and currency must be recorded. The system must convert bunker purchases in different currencies (USD, AED, INR) to the voyage base currency using the exchange rate on the bunkering date, and must reconcile opening ROB plus purchases minus closing ROB against reported consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R007	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Charter Hire Cost Allocation	The system must calculate and allocate time charter hire costs to individual voyages based on the charter party terms including daily hire rate, commission percentages (address commission and brokerage typically 2.5% and 1.25% respectively), off-hire periods, and ballast bonus provisions. For vessels on time charter, the system must prorate daily hire across voyage legs based on elapsed days, handling partial days at commencement and redelivery. The allocation engine must account for speed and consumption warranties specified in the charter party, computing claims for underperformance or overperformance. All charter hire calculations must maintain linkage to the charter party contract record and support multi-currency settlement where hire is payable in USD but recorded in the entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R016	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Voyage Cost Benchmarking and KPI Dashboard	The system must provide an AI-powered benchmarking dashboard comparing voyage costs against fleet averages, historical performance, and industry benchmarks. Key performance indicators must include cost per TEU-mile, bunker consumption per nautical mile, port cost per container move, and total voyage cost as a percentage of freight revenue. Benchmarks must be segmentable by trade lane, vessel class, season, and operating entity. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with Hijri date selectors for period filtering. AI-generated narrative summaries must explain significant cost variances in natural language, available in both English and Arabic, identifying root causes and recommending corrective actions for future voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R009	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Tax and VAT Handling on Port Disbursements	The system must correctly process tax components within port disbursements according to the tax regime of each country: UAE VAT at 5% on applicable port services, KSA VAT at 15% on applicable services, Qatar tax-exempt treatment for most port services but with specific excise duty handling, and India GST at applicable rates (IGST for inter-state, CGST plus SGST for intra-state) on port services with HSN code mapping. Each FDA line item must capture the gross amount, tax amount, tax code, and net amount separately. The system must validate that tax amounts charged by agents conform to current statutory rates and flag discrepancies. Input tax credit eligibility must be determined per line item based on the entity's tax registration status and the nature of the service, feeding into the tax module for return preparation and credit claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R001	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	IFRS 15 Five-Step Model Implementation	The system must implement the complete IFRS 15 five-step revenue recognition model for container shipping freight contracts: Step 1 - identify the contract (bill of lading, booking confirmation, or service contract); Step 2 - identify performance obligations (transportation of containers from origin to destination, with potential distinct obligations for ancillary services like fumigation, lashing, or reefer monitoring); Step 3 - determine the transaction price (base freight plus surcharges minus discounts); Step 4 - allocate transaction price to performance obligations based on standalone selling prices; Step 5 - recognize revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied over time. The system must maintain complete audit trails for each step's determination per contract, supporting both English and Arabic documentation of judgments and estimates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R010	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Revenue Recognition Schedule Generation	The system must generate detailed revenue recognition schedules showing the expected timing and amounts of revenue recognition for all active freight contracts. Schedules must be producible at the individual B/L level, voyage level, vessel level, customer level, and entity level. Each schedule must show the total transaction price, amounts already recognized, amounts deferred, and projected future recognition by accounting period. The schedule must be dynamically updated as voyage progress changes, contract modifications occur, or new information affects the estimated completion timeline. Period-end revenue recognition schedules must support the financial close process by providing auditable support for revenue balance assertions. Export capabilities must include Excel format for working paper preparation and PDF format with dual-language English and Arabic headers for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R001	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Structure Configuration	The system must support configuration of multiple commission structures applicable to different agent types and commercial arrangements in the container shipping industry. Configurable structures must include percentage-based commissions on freight revenue (standard agency commission typically 2.5-5% of ocean freight), fixed fee per B/L processed, fixed fee per container handled, tiered percentage based on revenue volume thresholds, sliding scale commissions that increase with performance milestones, and hybrid structures combining fixed fees with variable percentages. Each commission structure must be definable per agent, per port, per trade lane, and per operating entity with effective date ranges. The configuration must support currency specification for fixed-fee components and must allow exclusion of specific revenue items from the commission calculation base (e.g., excluding surcharges or THC from commissionable revenue).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R008	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Payment Processing Integration	The system must integrate with the accounts payable and treasury modules to process commission payments based on approved commission statements. Payment processing must support multiple payment methods including bank transfer (SWIFT for international, local ACH for domestic), cheque issuance, and offsetting against amounts owed by the agent (such as FDA advance balances). Payment scheduling must respect agent payment terms (net 30, net 60, or as contractually agreed) with automatic payment run generation. The system must handle situations where commissions from multiple entities are owed to the same agent, providing an option for consolidated payment from a designated paying entity with intercompany settlement. Payment files must be generated in the formats required by the company's banking partners in Qatar (QNB, Commercial Bank), UAE (Emirates NBD, ADCB), KSA (Al Rajhi, NCB), and India (SBI, HDFC).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R011	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	IFRS Cost to Obtain Contract Treatment	The system must implement IFRS 15 paragraph 91-94 treatment for commission costs as incremental costs of obtaining a contract. Agent commissions that are directly attributable to obtaining specific freight contracts and would not have been incurred without the contract must be capitalized as contract acquisition costs and amortized over the period of the contract (or expected customer relationship period if the commission relates to a renewal). The system must distinguish between commissions that qualify for capitalization (e.g., commission on a new 12-month service contract) versus those expensed immediately (e.g., commission on spot bookings where the amortization period would be one year or less and the practical expedient is elected). Capitalized commission assets must be presented on the balance sheet, tested for impairment at each reporting date, and amortized using a method consistent with the transfer of goods or services to the customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R008	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Container Slot Cost Calculation	The system must compute per-slot and per-TEU costs for each voyage leg by dividing total voyage leg costs by the number of available container slots, adjusting for vessel stowage plan capacity, weight limits, and reefer plug availability. Slot cost calculations must distinguish between 20ft TEU, 40ft FEU, 40ft high-cube, and 45ft containers with appropriate equivalency factors. The calculation must further segment costs into fixed components (hire, insurance, crew) and variable components (bunker, port charges) to support contribution margin analysis. AI must recommend optimal slot pricing based on historical utilization rates, competing carrier pricing intelligence, and current booking pipeline fill rates for the specific trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R001	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	PDA Generation from Port Call Schedule	The system must automatically generate a Proforma Disbursement Account (PDA) when a vessel port call is confirmed in the operations schedule. The PDA must be pre-populated with estimated port charges based on the port tariff database for the specific port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA,SAKAC, SADAM), vessel particulars (GRT, NRT, LOA, beam, draft), expected cargo operations (number of container moves, break-bulk tonnage), and estimated port stay duration. AI must enhance estimates by analyzing historical PDAs for the same vessel at the same port, adjusting for seasonal tariff variations, known surcharges, and recent regulatory fee changes. The generated PDA must be denominated in the port's local currency with automatic USD equivalent calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R010	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Supplementary Disbursement Account Processing	The system must handle Supplementary Disbursement Accounts (SDA) submitted by agents after the initial FDA for additional charges not included in the original final account. SDAs commonly arise from delayed invoicing by port authorities, retroactive tariff adjustments, or previously overlooked services. Each SDA must be linked to the original FDA and PDA, with the system validating that the SDA charges were not already included in the FDA to prevent duplicate billing. SDA items must go through the same approval workflow as FDA items and must update the voyage P&L accordingly. The system must track SDA frequency per agent as an input to the agent performance scoring, since excessive SDAs indicate poor initial FDA compilation quality. SDA processing must support the same multi-currency settlement flow as regular FDAs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R002	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Performance Obligation Identification Engine	The system must automatically identify distinct performance obligations within freight contracts based on configurable rules aligned with the entity's IFRS 15 accounting policy. For standard port-to-port container shipments, the primary performance obligation is the transportation service measured from container gate-in at origin to container availability at destination. For door-to-door shipments under combined transport bills of lading, the system must determine whether inland transportation legs (trucking from shipper premises to port, or from port to consignee premises) constitute separate performance obligations or are combined with the ocean leg. The engine must handle special cargo obligations such as temperature-controlled transport for reefer containers (requiring continuous monitoring as an integral service), hazardous cargo handling, and project cargo securing, assessing whether these represent distinct obligations requiring separate revenue allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R011	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Demurrage and Detention Revenue Treatment	The system must handle the revenue recognition of demurrage charges (charged when containers remain at the port beyond the free time allowance) and detention charges (charged when containers are held by the consignee beyond the free time) as variable consideration under IFRS 15. Revenue from demurrage and detention must only be recognized when it becomes highly probable that a significant reversal will not occur, which typically means when the billable event has occurred, the amount is calculable, and collectability is reasonably assured based on customer payment history. The system must compute free time based on the tariff applicable to the port, container type, and customer agreement, applying different free time allowances per port (e.g., 7 days free at Jebel Ali, 3 days free at JNPT). Automatic billing triggers must generate demurrage invoices when free time expires.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R002	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Automatic Commission Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate agent commissions based on the configured commission structures when freight revenue is recognized or when billable events occur. The calculation engine must process commission computations at the B/L level, aggregating results per agent per accounting period. For percentage-based commissions, the engine must apply the rate to the applicable revenue base after deducting any non-commissionable components as defined in the agent agreement. For tiered structures, the engine must track cumulative revenue per agent within the contract year and apply the appropriate tier rate, retroactively adjusting prior period calculations when a new tier threshold is crossed. The calculation must handle multi-currency scenarios where freight revenue is in USD but the commission is payable in the agent's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), applying the exchange rate at the revenue recognition date or as contractually specified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R009	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Sub-Agent and Override Commission Tracking	The system must support multi-level commission structures where a master agent earns an override commission on business generated by sub-agents within their territory. The system must maintain the agent hierarchy defining master agent to sub-agent relationships with effective dates and territory (port, country, or region level) assignments. When a sub-agent generates bookings, the system must calculate the sub-agent's direct commission at their contracted rate and additionally compute the master agent's override commission (typically a smaller percentage such as 0.5-1% of freight revenue). Both commissions must be independently tracked, accrued, and settled. The system must generate separate statements for sub-agents and master agents, with the master agent's statement showing aggregated sub-agent volumes. Commission hierarchy changes must be effective-dated to prevent retroactive impact on previously calculated commissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R001	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L statement generation per rotation	The system must generate a complete profit and loss statement for each voyage rotation identified by a unique voyage number, consolidating all revenue and cost line items from voyage commencement to completion. The P&L must include separate sections for freight revenue, ancillary revenue (demurrage, detention, THC surcharges), direct voyage costs (bunker consumption, port dues, pilotage, towage), and indirect costs (insurance, crew wages apportioned). Each line item must reference the source transaction. The P&L must support generation in the base currency of the operating entity (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with a consolidated USD equivalent column using the exchange rate at transaction date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-001-R002	FEAT-000-2-001	\N	Tenant Isolation Row-Level Security	All ERP data tables include company_id foreign key. PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS) policies enforce company_id = current_setting app.current_company_id on all SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. Middleware sets app.current_company_id on every DB connection from request context.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R008	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Inquiry Collaboration and Internal Notes	The system must support a threaded conversation interface on each rate request where customers can ask clarifying questions, attach supplementary documents (cargo specifications, MSDS for DG cargo, packing lists), and receive responses from the commercial team. Each message must be timestamped with timezone-aware formatting (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India) and must support Arabic and English text within the same thread. An AI assistant must auto-suggest responses to common customer queries such as free-time policy, detention charges, and container specifications, reducing manual intervention by the commercial team to under 10% of total inquiries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R004	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Settlement Period Management	The system must support configurable settlement periods per VSA agreement, including weekly, bi-weekly, monthly, and quarterly cycles. At each settlement cycle close, the system must automatically generate a settlement statement summarizing total slots purchased from partners, total slots sold to partners, net slot balance, and the net payable or receivable amount in the agreed settlement currency. Settlement statements must be generated per entity (Qatar WLL, UAE LLC, KSA Ltd, India Pvt Ltd) and must comply with local invoicing requirements including QAR for Qatar operations, AED for UAE operations, SAR for KSA operations, and INR for Indian operations with applicable GST/VAT treatment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R006	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Rate Amendment Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for amending VSA slot rates mid-agreement. Rate amendment requests must capture the reason for change (fuel price adjustment, market rate correction, seasonal surcharge, force majeure), proposed new rates per container type and trade lane, effective date of the amendment, and supporting documentation uploads. Amendments must follow a configurable approval chain where at minimum the commercial manager and finance controller of each affected entity must approve before the new rates take effect. The system must maintain a complete version history of all rate amendments with before-and-after comparisons and timestamp each change with the approver identity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R007	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Slot Exchange Rate Lock Mechanism	The system must implement a currency exchange rate locking mechanism for VSA transactions where the settlement currency differs from the operational currency. Users must be able to lock exchange rates at one of the following points: booking confirmation date, bill of lading issue date, vessel departure date, or settlement period close date, as defined in each VSA agreement. Locked rates must be sourced from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, or RBI reference rates as applicable. The system must store both the locked rate and the spot rate at settlement for variance reporting, and must support manual rate override with dual authorization for exceptional circumstances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R008	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Utilization Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time analytics dashboard showing VSA slot utilization across all active agreements. The dashboard must display utilization heatmaps by trade lane, time-series trends of slot usage over rolling 12-month periods, partner-wise utilization comparison charts, and cost-per-TEU trend analysis. Users must be able to filter by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA), container type, and date range. The dashboard must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all labels, tooltips, and axis descriptions properly mirrored. Data must refresh at maximum 15-minute intervals from the operational booking system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R016	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Capacity Forecasting with AI	The system must employ AI-based capacity forecasting models to predict future slot demand across all VSA-covered trade lanes for the upcoming 8-week rolling window. The forecasting engine must incorporate historical booking data, seasonal patterns (including Ramadan, Diwali, and Chinese New Year peak periods), macroeconomic indicators for GCC and Indian markets, and known upcoming project cargo shipments. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actual bookings with a target accuracy of 85% at the 4-week horizon. Forecasts must be entity-specific, showing predicted demand originating from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations separately, enabling proactive slot purchasing or release decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R007	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Empty Container Stacking Optimization	The system must implement specialized stacking rules for empty containers that differ from laden container logic. Empty containers must be stackable up to the maximum block tier height (typically 5-6 high) grouped by shipping line, container size (20ft, 40ft, 45ft), and container type (standard dry, high-cube, open-top, flat-rack). The system must track empty container condition grades (A-food grade, B-cargo worthy, C-wind/watertight, D-damaged) and segregate by grade to facilitate efficient pick-up for booking allocations. AI-driven compaction routines must run during off-peak hours to consolidate fragmented empty stacks and maximize usable ground slots for incoming laden cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R012	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Actual Vessel Movement Tracking and Logging	The system must record actual vessel movement milestones including arrival at anchorage, pilot boarding, first line ashore, all fast, cargo operations commenced, cargo operations completed, last line cast off, and departure from port limits. Each milestone must capture the actual date and time in UTC and local port time, the source of the data (AIS, terminal system, manual entry, port community system), and the user or system that recorded it. Variance between planned and actual times must be calculated automatically and displayed alongside schedule data. Cumulative delay statistics must be aggregated per vessel, per service loop, and per port for performance analytics dashboards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R002	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	CII Annual Rating Computation	The system must compute the annual operational Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) rating for each vessel following MEPC.352(78) resolution guidelines. The CII rating shall be derived from actual annual fuel consumption data, distance travelled, and cargo carried, producing an attained CII value compared against reference lines and reduction factors for the applicable year. Vessels must be automatically classified into ratings A through E, with the system applying the correct reduction factor percentages that increase annually through 2030. The computation must handle voyages spanning multiple calendar years by prorating fuel consumption and distance appropriately across reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-001-R003	FEAT-000-2-001	\N	Data Residency Selection	On company onboarding, admin selects data residency: Qatar (MEEZA cloud), UAE (G42 or AWS me-south-1), Saudi Arabia (STC cloud), India (AWS ap-south-1). Selection determines which DB replica is primary. Immutable after selection for legal compliance. Display data residency badge in admin panel.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R007	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Territory and Account Assignment Management	The system must manage sales territory definitions based on configurable criteria including geographic zones (per port hinterland catchment area), industry vertical (oil and gas, FMCG, automotive, chemicals, textiles), account size tier (enterprise, mid-market, SMB), and trade lane specialization. Territory assignments must map to exactly one primary salesperson with optional overlay specialists (e.g., reefer specialist, dangerous goods specialist). The system must detect and flag territory conflicts when multiple salespeople claim the same prospect. Territory rebalancing must be supported with mass reassignment tools that preserve historical performance attribution to the original salesperson for incentive calculations. Named account exclusions must override territory rules for strategic accounts managed at the entity or regional level. Territory maps must be visualizable on geographic overlays centered on the four primary port regions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R016	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Onboarding Integration with External Trade Databases	The system must integrate with external trade databases and business registries to auto-enrich onboarding applications. Integrations must include: Qatar Commercial Registration database (MOCIE) for company verification, UAE Ministry of Economy commercial license registry, KSA Ministry of Commerce (MC) commercial registration system, India Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA21) for company master data, and international shipping databases (INTTRA, CargoSmart) for existing shipper profile enrichment. The system must auto-fetch available data upon entry of registration numbers, reducing manual data entry by at minimum 40% compared to fully manual onboarding. Enrichment data must be clearly marked as externally sourced versus customer-provided for audit purposes. Integration failures must fall back to manual entry with a warning icon. API call logs must be maintained for dispute resolution and compliance verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R009	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Request Templates and Quick Repeat	Customers must be able to save frequently used rate request configurations as named templates storing origin, destination, container type, commodity, INCOTERMS basis, and special handling flags. The system must support a one-click repeat function that clones a previous rate request with updated dates and allows the customer to modify any field before submission. Templates must be entity-specific, meaning a customer operating under both the Qatar and UAE entities must maintain separate template libraries per entity. The system must limit templates to 50 per customer account and must archive templates not used for 180 days with an option to restore them within 90 days of archival.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-002-R017	FEAT-006-2-002	MOD-006	Availability Check Audit Trail	The system must log every container availability check performed by each customer, recording the search parameters (port, container type, date, quantity), the results returned (available count, units reserved), and the customer's subsequent action (reserved, no action, modified search). This audit trail must be retained for a minimum of 3 years to support dispute resolution when customers claim containers were shown as available but not provided. The audit log must be queryable by customer support staff with appropriate role-based access and must be exportable in CSV format for analysis. Each audit entry must include a tamper-evident hash linking it to the previous entry to ensure log integrity for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R006	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	AI-Optimized Notification Relevance	The AI engine must analyze each customer's notification interaction history (open rates, click-through rates, dismiss patterns, unsubscribe actions) to build a relevance profile and optimize notification delivery. Notifications consistently ignored by a customer (open rate below 10% over 30 days for a specific event type) must trigger a suggestion to the customer to disable or adjust that notification type. The AI must also detect when a customer is likely experiencing alert fatigue (defined as receiving more than 50 notifications per day) and must consolidate lower-priority notifications into periodic digest emails. The relevance engine must maintain separate models per customer user, not per customer organization, as different roles have different information needs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R013	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Multi-Entity Tariff Governance and Segregation	The system must enforce strict data segregation of tariff schedules across the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while supporting cross-entity visibility for authorized corporate users. Each entity must maintain independent tariff schedules with entity-specific approval workflows, currency defaults (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), and regulatory compliance rules. Corporate commercial users must be able to view and compare tariffs across entities but must not modify another entity's tariffs without explicit cross-entity authorization. The system must log all cross-entity tariff access for compliance auditing and must support entity-specific tariff numbering sequences to prevent conflicts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-002-R017	FEAT-007-1-002	MOD-007	Rate Agreement Cloning and Template Management	The system must support cloning of existing rate agreements to create new agreements with pre-populated data from the source agreement. Cloning must allow the user to select which elements to carry forward including trade lanes, rates, surcharge applicability, volume tiers, and special terms. The system must also support rate agreement templates that define standard commercial structures for common scenarios such as annual contracts for major accounts, seasonal agreements for specific commodity trades, or short-term promotional rates. Templates must be entity-specific and must enforce entity-level business rules. AI must recommend the most suitable template based on the customer profile and trade requirements, learning from successful agreement patterns to improve template suggestions over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R016	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Data Integration and External Feed Management	The system must support integration with external data feeds that inform surcharge calculations. Key integrations must include fuel price index feeds (Singapore MOPS, Rotterdam Platts, Fujairah spot), exchange rate feeds from central banks or financial data providers, port congestion data from terminal operating systems, and industry surcharge benchmarks from freight indices. Each data feed must be configurable with source URL, authentication credentials, polling frequency, data format mapping, and failover rules. The system must validate incoming data against reasonableness checks (such as fuel price within historical range bounds) and flag anomalous data for manual review before it affects surcharge calculations. Data feed history must be retained for audit purposes. When a feed is unavailable, the system must use the last valid data point and generate alerts to relevant teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R008	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Deal Desk Approval Workflow for Non-Standard Pricing	The system must enforce a deal desk approval workflow when salespeople propose rates below published tariff minimums or outside standard contract parameters. Approval routing must be tiered: discounts up to 5% from standard rates require team lead approval, 5-15% requires regional commercial manager, 15-25% requires entity commercial director, and above 25% requires group CEO approval. The workflow must present the approver with deal context including customer health score, historical volume, competitive rate intelligence for the specific trade lane and container type, and projected profitability impact. Approval must be time-bound (48 hours maximum per tier) with automatic escalation on expiry. Approved non-standard rates must feed into commission calculations at the discounted rate. All deal desk decisions must be logged with rationale for audit and pattern analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-003-R017	FEAT-005-2-003	MOD-005	Post-Onboarding Welcome and Orientation Workflow	The system must trigger an automated post-onboarding welcome workflow upon account activation. The workflow must send a personalized welcome communication (email and SMS) in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English) containing login credentials, assigned relationship manager contact details, key service contacts per port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT operations desks), and links to user guides and tutorial videos. The system must schedule an automated orientation session invitation within 5 business days of activation for key accounts (Gold tier and above). A first-booking assistance flag must be set on the account triggering proactive outreach from customer service if no booking is placed within 14 days of activation. The welcome workflow must generate an internal task for the relationship manager to make a personal introduction call within 3 business days. Workflow execution must be tracked with completion metrics per step.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R010	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Rate Validity and Auto-Expiry Management	Each quotation must carry a configurable validity period defaulting to 7 calendar days for spot rates and 14 calendar days for contract rates, with the validity end displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats. The system must send automated reminders at 48 hours and 24 hours before expiry via the customer's preferred notification channel. Upon expiry, the quotation status must automatically change to Expired and the system must offer the customer a one-click option to request a refreshed rate. If market conditions have changed by more than 5% since the original quotation, the AI engine must flag the refresh request for manual commercial review rather than auto-generating a new quote.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R001	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Complaint Submission with Category Classification	The system must provide a structured complaint submission form allowing customers to select a primary category (Cargo Damage, Billing Dispute, Service Delay, Equipment Issue, Documentation Error, Detention/Demurrage Dispute, Customer Service, Other) and a secondary sub-category specific to the primary selection. An AI classifier must analyze the complaint description text (supporting both Arabic and English input) and suggest the most appropriate category with a confidence score. If the AI's suggested category differs from the customer's selection, the system must present both options and allow the customer to confirm. Each complaint must generate a unique case reference number in the format CMP-ENTITY-YYYY-NNNNN where ENTITY is QA, AE, SA, or IN based on the filing entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R007	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Notification Template Management	The system must use configurable notification templates for each event type and delivery channel, with separate templates for Arabic and English. Templates must support dynamic variable insertion for shipment-specific data (BL number, vessel name, port names, dates, amounts, container numbers) using a defined placeholder syntax. Each template must include the sending entity's branding (logo, color scheme, entity legal name) based on the entity context of the shipment. Template changes must go through a review and approval workflow before activation, with version history maintained for audit purposes. The system must support A/B testing of notification templates, allowing the marketing team to compare open rates and click-through rates between two template variants for the same event type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-001-R014	FEAT-007-1-001	MOD-007	Tariff Integration with Booking and Quotation Systems	The system must expose tariff data through real-time APIs for consumption by the booking and quotation modules. When a booking or quotation request is initiated, the system must automatically retrieve the applicable tariff based on trade lane, container type, commodity, booking date, and entity. If multiple tariffs are applicable (for example, a general tariff and a commodity-specific tariff), the system must apply configurable precedence rules to determine the correct rate. The API must return the complete rate breakdown including base freight, terminal handling, and mandatory surcharges with response times not exceeding 200 milliseconds for single-rate lookups and 500 milliseconds for batch lookups of up to 50 rate combinations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R002	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Container-Level Cost Attribution	The system must attribute allocated port expenses down to individual container level, enabling per-container profitability analysis. Attribution must consider container type (dry, reefer, tank, flat-rack, open-top), size (20ft, 40ft, 45ft), weight category, and whether the container is laden or empty. Reefer containers must receive additional allocation for plug-in charges and monitoring fees. The attribution algorithm must handle transshipment containers that touch multiple ports within the same voyage differently from gateway containers, applying port costs only for ports where the container was actually handled.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R002	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Container Type and Quantity Selection	Customers select container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR) and quantities per booking with real-time equipment availability checks.	must_have			["Dropdown lists all ISO container type codes with dimensions and max payload in metric tons", "System performs real-time equipment availability check at origin port and displays available empty stock count", "When requested quantity exceeds available stock, system offers alternative ETD dates with sufficient equipment", "Reefer container selection triggers mandatory fields for temperature set-point (°C), ventilation (CBM/hr), and humidity percentage"]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R008	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Club Rules and Conditions Repository	The system must maintain a versioned digital repository of P&I Club rules, conditions, and exclusions for each club and each policy year. Rules must be searchable by keyword in both English and Arabic. The system must automatically compare rules across consecutive policy years and highlight changes relevant to the fleet's trading patterns, particularly exclusions related to sanctions-affected countries, war risk zones near the Strait of Hormuz or Gulf of Aden, and cabotage restrictions in Indian and Saudi Arabian coastal waters. Each vessel's specific coverage conditions, warranties, and endorsements must be linked to the applicable club rules version, enabling quick reference during claims handling or coverage disputes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R006	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	INCOTERMS Integration on Multimodal Documents	The system must display the applicable INCOTERMS 2020 rule on the Multimodal Bill of Lading and validate that the MBL's place of receipt and place of delivery are consistent with the selected INCOTERM. For EXW and FCA terms, the place of receipt on the MBL must match the seller's premises or named origin point. For CIP and CPT terms, the MBL must show the named place of destination. For DAP and DPU terms, the place of delivery must be the consignee's premises or unloading point. The system must warn users when an inconsistency is detected between the INCOTERM and the MBL routing. Freight payment terms (Prepaid/Collect) on the MBL must align with the INCOTERM's cost allocation: freight prepaid for C and D terms, collect for E and F terms, with override capability for non-standard commercial agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R004	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Cargo Details and Commodity Declaration	Customers declare cargo details including HS code, weight, commodity description, and hazardous goods classification per container.	must_have			["HS code field provides type-ahead search against the latest WCO Harmonized System nomenclature with Arabic and English descriptions", "System validates declared gross weight does not exceed container max payload and vessel cell weight limit", "DG cargo declaration captures UN number, proper shipping name, IMO class, packing group, flashpoint, and EmS code per IMDG Code", "System blocks booking submission when declared commodity is on the restricted import list for the destination country (KSA SABER, India DGFT)"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R005	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Booking Confirmation and Reference Generation	Upon successful validation, system confirms the booking and generates a unique booking reference number with automated notifications.	must_have			["System generates a unique alphanumeric booking reference (format: BK-{entity}-{YYYYMMDD}-{seq}) upon confirmation", "Confirmation email and SMS are sent within 60 seconds in customer's preferred language (English or Arabic)", "Booking confirmation PDF includes vessel name, voyage number, ETD, ETA, container details, rate breakdown, and cancellation terms", "Confirmed booking is immediately visible in customer's portal dashboard with status 'Booked'"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R006	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Booking Amendment and Cancellation	Customers amend or cancel bookings through the portal subject to cut-off rules and cancellation penalty policies.	must_have			["Amendment of container quantity, ETD, or port pair is permitted up to 48 hours before vessel cut-off time", "Cancellation within free cancellation window (configurable per entity: default 72 hours before ETD) incurs no penalty", "Late cancellation triggers automatic penalty fee calculation per tariff schedule and adds charge to customer ledger", "All amendments and cancellations generate an audit trail entry with timestamp, user ID, and reason code"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R007	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Multi-Entity Booking Segregation	Portal enforces entity-level data segregation so customers of the Qatar entity cannot view or interact with bookings belonging to Dubai, KSA, or India entities.	must_have			["Customer login is bound to a specific legal entity (Qatar WLL, Dubai FZE, KSA LLC, India Pvt Ltd) and can only see bookings under that entity", "Customers with cross-entity contracts see a consolidated view but each booking is tagged with its owning entity's GL code", "API responses never return booking data from entities outside the customer's authorized scope, verified by automated penetration test", "Entity-specific booking terms and conditions are displayed based on the booking's owning entity jurisdiction"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R008	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Hijri and Gregorian Calendar Support	All date fields in the booking flow support both Hijri (Umm al-Qura) and Gregorian calendar display based on customer locale preference.	must_have			["Date picker component renders in Hijri (Umm al-Qura) when customer locale is ar-SA or ar-QA", "All stored dates use ISO 8601 Gregorian internally; Hijri conversion is display-only using the approved Umm al-Qura algorithm", "Booking confirmation PDF shows both Hijri and Gregorian dates side by side when customer locale is Arabic", "Cut-off time calculations always use Gregorian internally to prevent conversion drift errors"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R009	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Responsive Arabic RTL Interface	Booking interface fully supports Arabic right-to-left layout with culturally appropriate formatting for GCC customers.	must_have			["All booking form labels, validation messages, and tooltips are available in Arabic (ar) and English (en) with zero untranslated strings", "RTL layout mirrors all UI elements including navigation, form fields, stepper progress bar, and action buttons", "Numeric fields (weights, rates, quantities) remain LTR within the RTL context per BIDI algorithm (Unicode UAX #9)", "Port names display in Arabic script with Latin transliteration in parentheses when locale is Arabic"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R010	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	AI Booking Anomaly Detection	AI monitors booking submissions for anomalies such as unusual commodity-route combinations, weight discrepancies, or booking pattern deviations.	must_have			["AI flags bookings where declared weight per TEU deviates more than 2 standard deviations from historical average for that commodity-route pair", "Anomalous bookings are held in 'Pending Review' status and routed to operations team with AI-generated risk explanation", "AI learns from operator accept/reject decisions to reduce false positive anomaly rate below 5% within 90 days of deployment", "Customer receives notification that booking is under review with expected resolution time, without exposing the anomaly detection logic"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R011	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Saved Booking Templates	Customers save frequently used booking configurations as reusable templates to accelerate repeat shipments.	must_have			["Customer can save any confirmed booking as a template with a custom name (max 100 characters, supports Arabic and English)", "Template captures origin, destination, container type/qty, commodity, cargo weight, and special instructions", "Applying a template pre-fills the booking form; customer reviews and submits as a new booking with fresh rate quotation", "Customers can store up to 50 templates per account; system warns at 45 templates and blocks creation at 50"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R012	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Vessel Schedule Lookup	Integrated vessel schedule browser allows customers to view available sailings and select preferred vessel/voyage before booking.	must_have			["Schedule search returns all available sailings for a port pair within the next 8 weeks, sortable by ETD, transit time, or transshipment count", "Each sailing displays vessel name, voyage number, ETD, ETA, transit days, transshipment ports (if any), and available space indicator", "Direct sailings are visually distinguished from transshipment routings in the results list", "Schedule data refreshes every 4 hours from the vessel operations module (MOD-003) with last-updated timestamp shown"]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R007	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Through Bill of Lading for Transshipment Cargo	The system must generate Through Bills of Lading for cargo transshipping at intermediate ports such as Jebel Ali (AEJEA) used as a hub for feeder connections to Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), or Indian subcontinent ports (INMUN, INNSA, INJNP). The Through BL must show the full routing from origin to final destination port without separate documentation at the transshipment point. Transshipment details must be recorded internally linking the mother vessel voyage to the feeder vessel voyage with container tracking continuity maintained. The system must handle split transshipments where containers from one mother vessel are distributed across multiple feeder voyages. Customs transit bond requirements at the transshipment port must be flagged to the documentation team for timely filing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-001-R004	FEAT-000-2-001	\N	Subscription Tier Enforcement	Tiers: Starter (up to 5 users, 3 modules), Professional (up to 50 users, 15 modules), Enterprise (unlimited). Enforce limits at: login (user count), module activation (module count), API (rate limits per tier). Show upgrade prompt when limit hit. Grace period: 7 days after subscription expiry before suspend.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R016	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	AI Optimal Sailing Recommendation	AI recommends the best sailing option based on customer's cargo type, urgency, cost preference, and historical performance data.	must_have			["AI ranks available sailings by a composite score weighing transit time, cost, on-time reliability, and transshipment risk per customer preference weights", "Recommendation explains the reasoning in plain language (e.g., 'This sailing is 2 days faster and has 94% on-time reliability for this route')", "Customer can override AI recommendation and select any available sailing without restriction", "AI recommendation accuracy is tracked; system targets >80% customer acceptance rate of top-ranked suggestion"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R017	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Booking Slot Allocation Visibility	Customers view their allocated booking slots per vessel/voyage and track utilization against contracted volume commitments.	must_have			["Dashboard shows contracted slot allocation per trade lane per month versus actual bookings utilized", "When utilization drops below 70% of contracted volume, system alerts customer of potential dead-freight liability", "Overbooking beyond allocation is permitted up to a configurable threshold (default 110%) with waitlist status for excess", "Volume commitment report is downloadable monthly in PDF format for contract review purposes"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R001	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Real-Time Container Milestone Tracking	Customers track individual containers through standard shipping milestones from gate-in at origin to delivery at destination.	must_have			["Tracking displays standard milestones: Gate-In, Loaded on Vessel, Vessel Departed, Transshipment Arrived/Departed, Vessel Arrived, Discharged, Gate-Out, Delivered", "Each milestone shows actual date-time (or estimated if pending) with port/terminal name and UNLOCODE", "Milestone timestamps are displayed in the local time zone of the event location with UTC offset shown", "Container status updates appear within 30 minutes of terminal operating system (TOS) event capture"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R003	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Multi-Shipment Dashboard View	Customers view all active shipments on a single dashboard with filtering, sorting, and summary statistics.	must_have			["Dashboard lists all active shipments with columns: booking ref, B/L number, vessel/voyage, origin, destination, current status, ETA", "Filters support: status (in-transit, at port, delivered, exception), origin port, destination port, date range, and container type", "Summary cards show total active shipments, shipments with exceptions, average transit time deviation, and on-time delivery percentage", "Dashboard loads within 3 seconds for customers with up to 500 active shipments"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R004	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Interactive Map Visualization	Map-based tracking shows vessel positions and container locations on an interactive world map with route overlays.	must_have			["Map displays current vessel position using AIS data with course, speed (knots), and last position update timestamp", "Planned route is drawn as a polyline from origin to destination with waypoints at transshipment ports", "Clicking a vessel icon shows a popup with vessel name, IMO number, voyage, containers on board for this customer, and next port ETA", "Map supports zoom from global view to port-level detail and clusters overlapping vessel icons at lower zoom levels"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R005	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Exception and Delay Alerts	Automated alert system notifies customers of shipment exceptions including delays, rollovers, port omissions, and vessel diversions.	must_have			["System sends alerts via email, SMS, and portal notification within 15 minutes of exception event detection", "Alert types include: vessel delay >12 hours, container rollover, port omission, customs hold, reefer temperature alarm, and damage report", "Each alert includes: exception type, affected container(s), revised ETA, recommended action, and contact details for operations team", "Customers configure alert preferences per shipment or globally: opt-in/out per alert type and per notification channel"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R006	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	B/L and Container Number Search	Customers search for shipments using Bill of Lading number, booking reference, or container number with partial match support.	must_have			["Search accepts B/L number (exact), booking reference (exact), or container number (ISO 6346 format with check digit validation)", "Partial search on B/L or booking reference returns matching results after minimum 6 characters entered", "Search results display within 2 seconds regardless of data volume and show most recent status for each match", "Search scope is restricted to the authenticated customer's shipments only; no cross-customer data leakage"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R007	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Proof of Delivery Retrieval	Customers retrieve and download Proof of Delivery (POD) documents for completed shipments directly from the tracking interface.	must_have			["POD becomes available in tracking view within 24 hours of container gate-out at destination", "POD document includes: container number, delivery date-time, receiver name, signature image, and seal condition at delivery", "POD is downloadable as PDF and viewable inline in the portal without requiring external software", "System retains PODs for a minimum of 7 years per GCC commercial record retention regulations"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R008	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Reefer Container Monitoring	Real-time monitoring dashboard for reefer containers showing temperature, humidity, and ventilation readings with alert thresholds.	must_have			["Dashboard displays current temperature (°C), humidity (%), and ventilation (CBM/hr) readings updated every 30 minutes via IoT telemetry", "Temperature chart shows readings over time with set-point band and highlights deviations exceeding ±2°C from set-point", "Alert is triggered immediately when temperature deviates beyond customer-configured threshold and persists until acknowledged", "Historical reefer data log is exportable as CSV for insurance claim or quality assurance purposes"]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R008	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Combined Transport Charges on MBL	The system must calculate and display combined transport charges on the Multimodal Bill of Lading as a single through-rate or as itemized segment charges depending on the commercial agreement. The through-rate must be sourced from the intermodal rate management module and converted to the MBL's freight currency. Itemized charges must break down ocean freight, rail haulage, road transport, terminal handling at origin and destination, and any applicable surcharges (BAF, CAF, PSS, EBS, IHC). Exchange rate applied at the time of MBL issuance must be recorded and locked for that document. All charges must support display in dual currencies: the billing currency and a user-selectable reference currency (typically USD). Prepaid and collect charge allocations must be clearly separated on the document face.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R010	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	AI Transit Anomaly Insights	AI analyzes shipment transit patterns and provides customers with proactive insights on recurring delays, optimal routing, and performance benchmarks.	must_have			["AI generates monthly transit performance report per trade lane comparing customer's shipments against fleet-wide benchmarks", "Report highlights routes with recurring delays and suggests alternative routing or timing to improve on-time performance", "Insights are presented in natural language (English and Arabic) with supporting charts for transit time distribution", "AI identifies containers that have been stationary at a port for more than 5 days and proactively alerts customer with likely cause analysis"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R011	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Customs Clearance Status Integration	Tracking view integrates customs clearance status from Qatar Customs (BAYAN), Dubai Customs (Mirsal2), KSA Customs (FASAH), and Indian Customs (ICEGATE).	must_have			["Customs status milestone shows: submitted, under assessment, inspection required, cleared, or held with timestamp per container", "System pulls customs status updates via authorized API integration with each country's customs platform at minimum 2-hour intervals", "When customs hold is detected, system displays hold reason code and links to the Document Management feature for missing document upload", "Customs duty amount and payment status are shown for destination country clearance when data is available from the customs system"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R012	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Shared Tracking Links	Customers generate shareable tracking links for specific shipments that allow third parties (consignees, freight forwarders) to view tracking without portal login.	must_have			["Customer generates a unique, time-limited tracking URL (configurable expiry: 7, 14, 30 days) for any active shipment", "Shared link provides read-only milestone view without exposing commercial data (rates, invoice amounts, customer account details)", "Customer can revoke a shared link at any time; revoked links display an 'Access Expired' message", "Shared link access is logged with IP address and timestamp for security audit purposes"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R013	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Demurrage and Detention Countdown	Tracking view displays real-time demurrage and detention free-time countdown for each container at destination port.	must_have			["Free-time countdown starts from vessel discharge date and displays remaining free days per the customer's contract terms", "Countdown distinguishes between demurrage (port storage) and detention (container use outside port) with separate timers", "System sends alerts at 2 days remaining, 1 day remaining, and upon free-time expiry with estimated daily charge amount", "Accrued demurrage/detention charges are calculated in real-time based on the applicable tariff (per entity and port) and displayed in customer's preferred currency"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R014	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Multi-Modal Tracking for Inland Legs	Tracking extends to inland transportation legs (truck/rail) from port to final destination with GPS-based position updates.	must_have			["Inland leg shows truck/rail carrier name, vehicle/wagon number, driver contact (if applicable), and estimated delivery time", "GPS position updates every 15 minutes for truck legs and every 60 minutes for rail legs during transit", "Geofence alerts trigger when container enters/exits predefined zones: port gate, inland depot, customer warehouse", "Tracking timeline seamlessly connects ocean and inland milestones into a single end-to-end journey view"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R015	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Historical Shipment Archive and Search	Customers access historical shipment records with full tracking history for completed shipments within retention period.	must_have			["Completed shipments are searchable and viewable for a minimum of 3 years from delivery date", "Historical view preserves all milestones, timestamps, documents, and exception records as they were at time of completion", "Export function generates a full shipment dossier (PDF) including milestones, documents, charges, and correspondence for audit purposes", "Search supports filters by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), trade lane, commodity, and container type across the historical archive"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R016	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Carbon Emission Tracking per Shipment	Each shipment displays estimated CO2 emissions based on vessel type, distance, cargo weight, and fuel consumption data.	must_have			["CO2 estimate is calculated per container using IMO EEOI methodology factoring vessel DWT, distance, and fuel type (VLSFO/LNG)", "Emission data is displayed on the tracking detail page with comparison to trade lane average and year-over-year trend", "Quarterly sustainability report aggregates emissions across all customer shipments, downloadable as PDF per GHG Protocol Scope 3 format", "AI suggests lower-emission routing alternatives when available (e.g., LNG-powered vessel option or slower steaming schedule)"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-002-R017	FEAT-006-1-002	MOD-006	Port Congestion and Weather Overlay	Tracking map displays real-time port congestion indicators and weather conditions affecting vessel transit and port operations.	must_have			["Port congestion indicator (green/amber/red) is shown for all served ports based on average vessel waiting time in the last 48 hours", "Weather overlay displays wind speed, wave height, and visibility conditions along the vessel's planned route from maritime weather feeds", "When severe weather is detected on route, system proactively notifies customer of potential delay with revised ETA range", "Congestion and weather data refreshes every 2 hours with source attribution and last-updated timestamp"]	pending
FEAT-006-1-003-R001	FEAT-006-1-003	MOD-006	Bill of Lading Draft Review and Approval	Customers review AI-generated draft Bills of Lading online, request amendments, and approve final versions through the portal.	must_have			["Draft B/L is generated automatically from booking and cargo data within 2 hours of vessel departure and presented in the portal for review", "Customer can highlight specific fields on the draft B/L and submit amendment requests with correction details inline", "Amendment requests are processed by AI for standard corrections (spelling, address, marks/numbers) and auto-applied within 30 minutes", "Customer approval of final B/L is captured with digital signature (timestamp + user ID) and locks the document from further edits"]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R009	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Letter of Credit Documentary Compliance Check	The system must validate Multimodal Bills of Lading against Letter of Credit documentary requirements per UCP 600 Articles 19 (Transport Document Covering at Least Two Different Modes of Transport). Validation checks must include: document title matches LC requirement, shipper name matches LC beneficiary, consignee matches LC applicant or is 'to order' as specified, notify party is correctly stated, ports and places match LC exactly including spelling, goods description is consistent with LC, shipped on board notation is present with date, and freight indication (prepaid/collect) matches LC terms. Discrepancies must be flagged with ISBP 745 reference codes and severity classification (hard discrepancy requiring amendment vs. soft discrepancy resolvable by clarification). The checker must generate a pre-submission report in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-003-R003	FEAT-006-1-003	MOD-006	Document Upload with AI Classification	Customers upload supporting documents (commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, MSDS) and AI auto-classifies and validates them.	must_have			["Upload accepts PDF, JPEG, PNG, and TIFF formats with maximum file size of 25 MB per document", "AI classifies uploaded document type (commercial invoice, packing list, COO, MSDS, fumigation certificate, etc.) with >95% accuracy", "AI extracts key fields (invoice number, amount, currency, consignee) via OCR and cross-validates against booking/SI data", "Documents in Arabic are OCR-processed with Arabic language model and extracted fields are displayed in both Arabic and English"]	pending
