FEAT-007-2-003-R014	FEAT-007-2-003	MOD-007	What-If Profitability Modeling	Interactive what-if analysis tool allowing commercial teams to model profitability impact of rate and cost changes.	must_have			["Users can adjust rate levels, surcharges, cost inputs, and volume assumptions to model profitability scenarios", "System calculates scenario impact at trade lane, entity, and group consolidated levels simultaneously", "Scenario comparison allows side-by-side view of up to 4 what-if scenarios against baseline", "AI recommends optimal scenario based on configurable objective (maximize revenue, margin, or contribution)", "Scenarios can be saved, shared across entity commercial teams, and used as basis for budget submissions"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-003-R015	FEAT-007-2-003	MOD-007	Intercompany Transfer Pricing in Profitability	System handles intercompany transfer pricing between entities to ensure accurate entity-level profitability reporting.	must_have			["Transfer pricing rules are configurable per service type (feeder, transhipment, agency, equipment) between entities", "Intercompany charges and credits are automatically calculated and posted to entity-level P&L", "System supports arm's-length transfer pricing methodology compliant with GCC and Indian tax regulations", "Intercompany elimination entries are generated for group consolidated profitability reporting", "Audit trail maintains complete record of transfer pricing calculations with regulatory compliance documentation"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R001	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	AI Rate Recommendation Generation	AI engine generates recommended freight rates for spot and contract pricing based on multi-factor optimization.	must_have			["AI considers minimum 15 input factors including demand forecast, capacity, competitor rates, costs, and customer history", "Rate recommendations are generated within 2 seconds for spot quotes and within 30 seconds for contract proposals", "Each recommendation includes confidence score (0-100%) and explanation of primary pricing drivers", "Recommendations respect entity-specific pricing floors and commercial policies for Qatar, Dubai, KSA, and India", "AI generates rate recommendations in the customer's preferred currency with automatic conversion"]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R015	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	MBL Print and Digital Distribution	The system must support multiple distribution channels for the finalized Multimodal Bill of Lading: high-security printing on pre-numbered stationery with microprint and holographic security features for negotiable originals, PDF generation with digital signatures for email distribution, EDI transmission via IFTMIN/IFTMBC message types for carrier and customs integration, and API-based distribution to customer TMS portals. Print jobs for negotiable originals must be tracked with stationery serial numbers reconciled against issued MBL numbers to prevent fraud. The system must support batch printing and distribution for multiple MBLs in a single operation. All distributed copies must be logged with recipient, channel, timestamp, and acknowledgment status. Arabic text rendering in PDF generation must use embedded fonts supporting full Arabic glyph shaping and ligatures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R003	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Real-Time Market Signal Ingestion	AI engine ingests real-time market signals to adjust pricing recommendations dynamically.	must_have			["System ingests freight rate indices, bunker prices, currency rates, and port congestion data in real-time", "Market signal processing latency does not exceed 5 minutes from source publication to model input", "AI adjusts rate recommendations within 15 minutes of significant market movement (>5% index change)", "Geopolitical risk signals (sanctions, trade disputes, canal disruptions) are factored via configurable risk premium", "Market signals are weighted per entity based on trade lane exposure relevance"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R004	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Customer-Specific Pricing Intelligence	AI incorporates customer-specific factors including booking history, payment behavior, and strategic value into rate recommendations.	must_have			["Customer lifetime value score influences recommended discount/premium level for each rate quote", "Payment history (average days-to-pay, dispute frequency) adjusts credit risk premium in recommended rate", "Volume commitment adherence from prior contracts influences renewal rate recommendations", "AI detects customer switching patterns and recommends retention pricing when churn risk is elevated", "Multi-entity customers receive coordinated pricing recommendations that align across Qatar, Dubai, KSA, and India"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R005	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Autonomous Spot Rate Pricing	AI autonomously prices spot rate inquiries within configured authority limits without human intervention.	must_have			["AI auto-prices spot inquiries where recommended rate falls within approved pricing corridor (95% automation target)", "Auto-priced quotes are issued to customers within 60 seconds of inquiry receipt", "Quotes exceeding pricing corridor are escalated to commercial team with AI recommendation and reasoning", "Auto-pricing authority limits are configurable per entity, trade lane, and customer segment", "System logs every auto-priced quote with full model input/output for audit and compliance review"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R006	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Contract Rate Optimization	AI optimizes contract rate proposals based on volume commitments, contract duration, and portfolio balance objectives.	must_have			["AI recommends rate tiers based on committed volume bands with calculated volume-discount elasticity", "Contract duration premium/discount is calculated based on market volatility and forward rate curve position", "System optimizes the contract portfolio mix to balance revenue stability against spot market upside", "AI identifies contract renewal opportunities 90 days before expiry with recommended renewal terms", "Contract proposals include multi-currency rate tables for customers booking across multiple entities"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R007	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Pricing Simulation Engine	Interactive simulation tool allowing commercial teams to test AI pricing recommendations against various market scenarios.	must_have			["Users can adjust input parameters (demand, bunker price, competitor rates) and see real-time price recalculation", "Simulation supports batch processing of up to 500 rate scenarios in a single run", "Side-by-side comparison of AI-recommended price vs. user-proposed price with projected outcome metrics", "Simulation results include projected booking volume, revenue, and profitability impact per entity", "Simulation history is saved for audit trail and model performance backtesting"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R008	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Pricing Explainability and Transparency	AI provides human-readable explanations for every pricing recommendation to build trust and support commercial decision-making.	must_have			["Each rate recommendation includes top 5 contributing factors ranked by influence on the final price", "SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values are calculated and visualized for pricing model transparency", "Explanation text is generated in both English and Arabic using natural language generation", "Commercial users can query why a specific rate was recommended via conversational AI interface", "Explainability reports satisfy internal audit requirements for AI-driven pricing decisions"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R009	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Pricing Guardrails and Compliance	System enforces pricing guardrails to prevent AI from recommending rates that violate commercial policies or regulations.	must_have			["Minimum rate floors are enforced per trade lane to prevent below-cost pricing (anti-dumping compliance)", "Maximum rate ceilings prevent excessive pricing that could trigger regulatory scrutiny in GCC/India markets", "AI cannot recommend rates that violate active contract terms or most-favored-customer clauses", "Pricing guardrails are configurable independently per entity to comply with Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India regulations", "Guardrail violations are logged and reported to compliance team with full context of attempted pricing action"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R010	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	A/B Testing for Pricing Strategies	System supports controlled A/B testing of pricing strategies to validate AI model improvements before full deployment.	must_have			["A/B tests can be configured to split traffic between current and candidate pricing models per trade lane", "Statistical significance testing determines when sufficient data has been collected to declare a winner", "Test results measure impact on win rate, revenue per TEU, and total trade lane revenue", "A/B tests can run independently per entity or across all entities for a given trade lane", "Test configuration prevents customer-visible inconsistency by maintaining consistent pricing per customer within a test"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R011	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Dynamic Surcharge Pricing	AI dynamically calculates optimal surcharge levels based on underlying cost drivers and market acceptance thresholds.	must_have			["BAF is dynamically calculated using current bunker prices with configurable recovery target percentage", "Peak season surcharge levels are AI-optimized based on demand elasticity and competitor surcharge levels", "Currency adjustment factor (CAF) is automatically recalculated when exchange rate moves beyond threshold", "Port congestion surcharge is triggered and priced dynamically based on real-time port waiting time data", "All dynamic surcharges comply with entity-specific filing and notice period requirements per regulatory jurisdiction"]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R016	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	MBL Data Integration with Carrier Platforms	The system must support bidirectional data exchange with major ocean carrier platforms (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE) for Multimodal Bill of Lading data synchronization. Outbound integration must transmit MBL booking confirmations, shipping instructions, and verified gross mass (VGM) declarations. Inbound integration must receive carrier-confirmed BL numbers, actual vessel/voyage assignments, container movement events, and freight invoice references. Data exchange must use INTTRA, CargoSmart, or carrier-specific API formats with field mapping configurable per carrier. Reconciliation reports must highlight discrepancies between the system's MBL data and carrier-confirmed data, flagging mismatches in container count, weight, port rotation, and charges for documentation team review and correction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-002-R009	FEAT-005-2-002	MOD-005	Sales Leaderboard and Gamification Engine	The system must display real-time sales leaderboards configurable by KPI dimension (TEU volume, revenue, new accounts, retention rate), time period (weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual), entity, and team. Leaderboards must support gamification elements including achievement badges (e.g., 'Trade Lane Champion' for highest volume on a specific route, 'Reefer Specialist' for reefer container quota achievement, 'Port Master' for balanced volume across all four ports). Rankings must normalize for territory potential to ensure fairness between high-potential ports like Jebel Ali and developing ports. The leaderboard must display names in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) and handle bi-directional text for mixed-language team views. Historical leaderboard snapshots must be preserved for trend analysis and annual awards ceremonies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-005-2-004-R001	FEAT-005-2-004	MOD-005	Multi-Channel Lead Capture with Source Attribution	The system must capture leads from multiple channels including website inquiry forms, trade show badge scans, email campaign responses, social media (LinkedIn, Twitter) interactions, WhatsApp business API messages, phone inquiries logged by sales/customer service, referral program submissions, and port authority event registrations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each lead must capture source channel, campaign attribution (if applicable), timestamp, and initial inquiry details. Lead forms must support Arabic and English input with automatic language detection. Duplicate lead detection must run in real-time against existing leads and customer database using fuzzy matching on company name (in both Arabic and English transliterations), phone number, email domain, and trade license number. Source attribution must support multi-touch models (first touch, last touch, linear, time-decay) for marketing ROI analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-001-R011	FEAT-006-2-001	MOD-006	Multi-Entity Rate Request Routing	When a customer submits a rate request, the system must automatically route it to the correct entity's commercial team based on the origin port: requests originating from Hamad Port (QAHAM) route to the Qatar entity, Khalifa Port (AEKHL) and Jebel Ali (AEJEA) route to the UAE entity, and JNPT (INJNP) route to the India entity. For KSA-origin requests (SAKAP, SAJED, SADAM), routing must go to the KSA entity. Cross-entity requests involving transshipment must create linked inquiry records in both origin and destination entities, maintaining referential integrity and synchronized status updates across the multi-tenant ERP architecture.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-003-R002	FEAT-006-2-003	MOD-006	Cargo Damage Claim Filing	For cargo damage complaints, the system must capture the bill of lading number, container number (validated against ISO 6346), specific damaged items with quantity and declared value in the shipment currency, nature of damage (wet damage, crushing, pilferage, contamination, temperature excursion), and the date damage was discovered. Customers must upload photographic evidence (minimum 3 photos, maximum 20, each up to 10MB in JPEG or PNG format) and supporting documents including the commercial invoice, packing list, survey report, and Equipment Interchange Receipt at delivery. The system must validate that the claim is filed within the contractual claim period (typically 3 days for apparent damage, 21 days for concealed damage per Hague-Visby Rules Article III) and warn customers if they are approaching the filing deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-2-004-R008	FEAT-006-2-004	MOD-006	Bulk Notification Preferences for Organizational Accounts	For corporate customers with multiple user accounts, the system must support an administrator role that can set organization-wide default notification preferences which are inherited by all users within that organization. Individual users must be able to override organizational defaults for their own account. The administrator must be able to enforce mandatory notifications that cannot be disabled by individual users, such as compliance-related alerts, payment overdue notices, and critical shipment exception alerts. The system must provide the administrator with a matrix view showing all users' notification settings across all event types and channels, with the ability to bulk-update preferences for selected users or the entire organization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R013	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Feedback Loop and Continuous Learning	System captures booking outcomes and commercial feedback to continuously improve AI pricing accuracy.	must_have			["Win/loss data for every quote is captured and fed back into model training pipeline", "Commercial team can flag AI recommendations as too high, too low, or appropriate via one-click feedback", "Model drift detection alerts when pricing accuracy degrades below acceptable threshold over 30-day window", "Feedback data is segmented by entity to ensure model improvements reflect local market conditions", "Quarterly model performance review report compares AI pricing accuracy across entities and trade lanes"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R014	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Multi-Entity Pricing Coordination	AI coordinates pricing across entities to prevent internal competition and ensure group-level revenue optimization.	must_have			["System detects when multiple entities are quoting the same customer for overlapping trade lanes", "AI recommends coordinated pricing to prevent entity-level undercutting that erodes group profitability", "Group pricing policies override entity-level pricing where configured for strategic accounts", "Cross-entity pricing conflicts are escalated to group commercial management with AI-recommended resolution", "Coordinated pricing respects transfer pricing regulations and arm's-length requirements in each jurisdiction"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R015	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	AI Pricing Engine API	RESTful API exposing AI pricing engine capabilities to external and internal systems for automated rate generation.	must_have			["API accepts rate request with origin, destination, container type, customer ID, and date range; returns AI-recommended rate", "Response time SLA of 2 seconds for single rate request and 30 seconds for batch of up to 100 requests", "API supports multi-currency response with rate in customer-preferred currency and entity-local currency", "Authentication uses OAuth 2.0 with entity-level API key scoping to restrict cross-entity data access", "API versioning supports backward compatibility with minimum 12-month deprecation notice for breaking changes"]	pending
FEAT-007-1-003-R017	FEAT-007-1-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Offline Access and Field Reference	The system must provide offline access to current surcharge schedules for sales and customer service personnel. The offline module must cache all active surcharges organized by trade lane and port with their effective dates, rates, and applicability rules. Users must be able to view total surcharge estimates for any trade lane and container type combination while offline. The offline surcharge data must synchronize automatically upon network reconnection with visual indicators showing which surcharges have been updated since the last sync. The offline interface must support both Arabic and English display with proper RTL formatting for Arabic. Offline surcharge data must be encrypted at rest on the device and must expire after a configurable maximum offline period (default 48 hours) requiring resynchronization for continued access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R001	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Free-Time Period Definition by Port and Container Type	The system must allow definition of free-time periods (days during which no demurrage or detention charges accrue) separately for demurrage (port/terminal storage) and detention (container usage outside port). Free-time must be configurable by port, terminal, container type (dry, reefer, special equipment), container size (20ft, 40ft, 45ft), cargo direction (import, export, transshipment), and operating entity. For Hamad Port, default free-time must align with Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani) published tariffs. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, free-time must align with DP World and Abu Dhabi Ports published schedules respectively. For JNPT, free-time must comply with Indian Shipping Ministry guidelines. Both Gregorian and Hijri calendar day counting must be supported with configurable rules for whether weekends and public holidays count toward free-time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R002	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Tiered Demurrage Rate Escalation Structure	The system must support multi-tiered demurrage rate escalation where charges increase as the duration beyond free-time extends. A minimum of five rate tiers must be configurable per tariff with each tier specifying a day range (e.g., days 1-3 after free-time, days 4-7, days 8-14, days 15-30, days 31+) and the daily rate per container type and size. Rates must be definable in the local currency of the port (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, INR for JNPT) with an optional USD equivalent for international billing. The escalation structure must clearly distinguish between import demurrage, export demurrage, and transshipment demurrage as these may have different tier structures. AI must recommend optimal tier structures based on historical container dwell time analysis and target container turnover objectives per port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R003	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Detention Tariff Configuration for Container Usage	The system must manage detention tariffs separately from demurrage, covering the period a container is in use by the customer outside the port or terminal area. Detention tariffs must be configurable by container type, size, and ownership status (carrier-owned, leased, shipper-owned). The tariff must support different detention rates for different usage scenarios: import detention (from gate-out to empty return), export detention (from empty pickup to laden gate-in), and round-trip (combined import and export). Detention free-time must be independently configurable from demurrage free-time. The system must support combined detention and demurrage tariffs where customers receive a merged free-time period covering both port storage and container usage. Currency configuration must follow the entity's default with cross-currency billing options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R004	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Customer-Specific Free-Time and Rate Exceptions	The system must support customer-specific exceptions to standard detention and demurrage tariffs as part of rate agreements or special commercial arrangements. Exceptions must allow extension of free-time periods, reduction of per-day rates, capping of maximum charges, or complete waiver for specific customers. Each exception must be linked to a rate agreement or approved independently through the surcharge waiver workflow. The system must track the financial impact of all customer exceptions including the difference between standard tariff charges and the exception-adjusted charges. AI must analyze customer exception patterns to identify customers receiving disproportionate benefits relative to their revenue contribution and recommend exception adjustments during agreement renewal. Exception terms must be visible to operations and billing teams to ensure correct charge application.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R005	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Reefer Container Special Detention and Demurrage Rules	The system must enforce special detention and demurrage rules for reefer containers that account for the higher cost and limited availability of refrigerated equipment. Reefer demurrage rates must be configurable at a premium over standard dry container rates (typically 1.5x to 3x) with shorter free-time periods reflecting the urgency of reefer container turnover. Reefer detention must additionally include power monitoring charges for containers connected to power at the port terminal beyond free-time. The system must support separate tariffs for active reefer (power required) and passive reefer (non-operating reefer used as insulated container). For perishable cargo, the system must support expedited demurrage calculation to enable rapid billing, incentivizing timely container evacuation. Port-specific reefer plug availability constraints must be factored into demurrage escalation recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R006	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Automated Demurrage and Detention Charge Calculation	The system must automatically calculate demurrage and detention charges based on container movement events received from the operations and terminal modules. The calculation must use the container gate-in, gate-out, vessel discharge, and empty return timestamps to determine the chargeable days, apply the correct free-time deductions, and compute charges using the applicable tiered tariff rates. The calculation engine must handle partial-day logic (configurable: charge full day for any part-day usage, or charge only for days where usage exceeds a threshold such as 4 hours). For import containers, demurrage calculation must start from vessel discharge date. For export containers, demurrage must apply from gate-in date. The system must recalculate charges when container event timestamps are corrected and must maintain a calculation audit trail showing the detailed computation for each container.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R002	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Slot Allocation Quota Tracking	The system must track slot allocation quotas per VSA agreement, broken down by voyage, trade lane, and port pair. For each sailing from Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), or JNPT (INNSA), the system must display total allocated slots (in TEU), utilized slots, remaining available slots, and percentage utilization. When utilization exceeds 90% of the allocated quota for any voyage leg, the system must trigger an automated alert to the commercial operations team. Quota tracking must support both owned-vessel allocations and partner-vessel allocations with separate dashboards for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R007	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Holiday and Weekend Handling in Free-Time Calculation	The system must support configurable rules for handling weekends and public holidays in free-time and chargeable day calculations. For each operating country, the system must maintain a holiday calendar that includes national holidays, religious holidays (Eid Al-Fitr, Eid Al-Adha, National Day for Qatar/UAE/KSA, Diwali and Republic Day for India), and port-specific closure days. Configurable rules must determine whether weekend days (Friday-Saturday for Qatar/KSA/UAE, Saturday-Sunday for India) and public holidays count as free-time days or are excluded from the count. The Hijri calendar must be used for calculating Islamic holiday-based exclusions with support for the annual variation in Hijri dates. Different rules must be applicable per entity and per port, as some port authorities mandate specific holiday counting rules that override the carrier's standard policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R008	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Demurrage and Detention Invoice Generation	The system must generate demurrage and detention invoices based on calculated charges with configurable billing frequency (per container event, weekly batch, monthly batch). Invoices must include detailed line items showing the container number, size, type, movement dates, free-time period, chargeable days per tier, rate applied per tier, and total charge per container. Invoices must be generated in the billing currency specified in the customer's agreement or in the port's local currency by default. Invoice documents must be producible in both Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. The system must support electronic invoice generation compliant with e-invoicing mandates in applicable jurisdictions, including Saudi Arabia's ZATCA e-invoicing requirements and India's GST e-invoicing system. Credit notes must be generatable for charge reversals or adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R009	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Demurrage Dispute Management and Resolution Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for managing customer disputes on demurrage and detention charges. Dispute initiation must be possible by the customer through the portal or by internal staff on behalf of the customer. Each dispute must capture the disputed container numbers, the disputed amount, the reason category (incorrect dates, holiday exclusion claim, free-time extension request, rate disagreement, force majeure claim), and supporting documentation uploads. The dispute must route to the appropriate commercial or operations team based on the dispute reason. Resolution options must include full waiver, partial adjustment, or rejection with documented justification. AI must assist dispute resolution by automatically validating container event timestamps against terminal records and recommending resolution actions based on historical dispute patterns and outcomes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R010	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Port Authority Tariff Alignment and Compliance	The system must maintain alignment between the carrier's demurrage and detention tariffs and the underlying port authority tariff schedules. For each operating port, the system must store the port authority's published tariff as a reference baseline. When the carrier's tariff includes a markup over port authority charges, the system must clearly separate the port authority component from the carrier's commercial component for transparency and regulatory compliance. The system must monitor port authority tariff announcements and alert commercial teams when changes are published that may require corresponding carrier tariff adjustments. For JNPT, the system must comply with Indian Container Freight Station (CFS) tariff regulations. For Jebel Ali, it must align with DP World's published free-time and storage tariff schedule. AI must automatically compare port authority tariff updates against current carrier tariffs and draft adjustment recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R011	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Combined Detention and Demurrage Reporting Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard for monitoring detention and demurrage performance metrics. Key metrics must include total D&D revenue by port, entity, and customer; average container dwell time; free-time utilization rate; number of containers in demurrage by tier; detention days outstanding; dispute rate and resolution statistics; and revenue at risk from pending disputes. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary to individual container level. Graphical visualizations must include trend charts, heat maps showing high-demurrage trade lanes, and Pareto analysis of top customers by D&D charges. Both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selection must be supported. AI must generate natural language insights highlighting anomalies, trends, and improvement opportunities in both Arabic and English for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R012	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Demurrage Amnesty and Promotional Free-Time Programs	The system must support creation of time-limited demurrage amnesty programs and promotional free-time extensions that override standard tariffs for defined periods. Amnesty programs must specify the eligible ports, customer segments, container types, and the amnesty terms (additional free days, rate reduction percentage, or charge cap). The system must automatically apply amnesty terms to qualifying containers during the program period without manual intervention. Program creation must go through the approval workflow with financial impact analysis showing the estimated revenue foregone. AI must predict the operational impact of proposed amnesty programs including projected container evacuation acceleration and yard utilization improvement. Historical program effectiveness analysis must be available to inform future program design, comparing actual results against projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R013	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Hazardous and Special Cargo Demurrage Premiums	The system must support premium demurrage and detention rates for hazardous cargo containers classified under IMO dangerous goods classes (Classes 1 through 9) and other special cargo types including out-of-gauge containers and overweight containers. Hazardous cargo demurrage rates must be configurable at multipliers over standard rates (typically 2x to 5x depending on IMO class) reflecting the higher storage costs, safety requirements, and limited designated storage areas at terminals. The system must enforce shorter free-time periods for specific high-risk IMO classes (such as Class 1 Explosives and Class 7 Radioactive) as mandated by port authority safety regulations. For JNPT, specific IMDG storage regulations must be reflected in the tariff structure. Out-of-gauge containers must have demurrage rates reflecting the additional ground slot consumption at the terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R003	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Slot Cost Calculation Engine	The system must implement an automated slot cost calculation engine that computes the effective cost per TEU and FEU for each VSA slot based on the applicable rate card, surcharges (BAF, CAF, PSS, EBS, LSS, ISPS), and any volume-based discount tiers defined in the agreement. The engine must handle partial slot usage scenarios where a 40-foot container occupies two TEU slots but is billed at the FEU rate. Currency conversion must use the exchange rate locked at the booking confirmation date or the rate specified in the VSA agreement terms. Calculations must be auditable with full breakdown stored for each transaction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R014	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Transshipment Container Demurrage Rules	The system must define specific demurrage rules for transshipment containers that differ from import and export demurrage. Transshipment free-time must be separately configurable and is typically longer than import/export free-time to account for connecting vessel schedules. The system must link transshipment demurrage calculation to the connecting vessel schedule, where free-time may be extended automatically if the scheduled connecting vessel is delayed. Demurrage for transshipment cargo must consider the mother vessel discharge date, the feeder vessel expected departure date, and the actual departure date to determine whether any delay is carrier-caused (non-chargeable) or consignee-caused (chargeable). For Jebel Ali, which serves as a major transshipment hub, the system must support complex multi-leg transshipment scenarios with demurrage correctly apportioned across service legs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R015	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Detention and Demurrage Tariff Version Control	The system must maintain full version control of all detention and demurrage tariffs with immutable history. Each tariff modification must create a new version capturing the change details, effective date, approver, and reason for change. The system must support parallel active versions for different effective date windows allowing advance preparation of future tariff changes. When charges are calculated, the system must apply the tariff version that was effective on the relevant charge date (container discharge date for demurrage, gate-out date for detention) regardless of the current active version, ensuring retrospective accuracy. The system must prevent deletion of historical tariff versions and must support audit queries that reconstruct the exact tariff that was applicable for any container at any point in time. Version comparison tools must highlight changes between any two versions side by side.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R016	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	AI-Powered Dwell Time Prediction and Proactive Alerts	The system must leverage AI to predict container dwell times and proactively alert stakeholders before containers enter chargeable demurrage periods. The prediction model must use historical dwell time data, customer behavioral patterns, cargo type, documentation clearance status from customs modules, and current port congestion levels as input features. Predictions must be generated upon vessel discharge for import containers and upon gate-in for export containers. The system must send proactive notifications to customers and their freight forwarders when containers are predicted to exceed free-time, including the estimated demurrage charges that will accrue. Notifications must be sent in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English). AI accuracy must be monitored with a target prediction accuracy of 80% or above for identifying containers that will incur demurrage charges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R017	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Offline Detention and Demurrage Tariff Reference	The system must provide offline access to current detention and demurrage tariff schedules for customer service and commercial staff who may need to advise customers in the field or in areas with limited connectivity. The offline module must cache the complete D&D tariff structure including standard tariffs per port, customer-specific exceptions for the user's assigned accounts, holiday calendars, and surcharge rules. Users must be able to perform estimated D&D charge calculations offline by entering container discharge date, expected pickup date, container type, and applicable customer. Offline calculations must clearly indicate that they are estimates subject to confirmation upon synchronization. The offline data must synchronize upon reconnection with conflict resolution for tariff changes that occurred during the offline period. Data must be encrypted at rest with a maximum offline validity of 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-1-004-R018	FEAT-007-1-004	MOD-007	Regulatory Compliance Reporting for Detention and Demurrage	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for detention and demurrage practices as required by applicable maritime regulations. For Indian operations, reports must comply with Indian Shipping Ministry directives on container detention practices including maximum detention charges and mandatory free-time provisions. For US-bound or US-origin cargo, the system must support FMC (Federal Maritime Commission) compliance requirements including the Shipping Act provisions on fair and reasonable detention and demurrage practices. Reports must include statistics on average free-time provided, percentage of containers incurring charges, dispute resolution timelines, and charge reasonableness metrics. The system must flag any D&D billing practices that may conflict with regulatory guidelines and recommend corrective actions. All compliance reports must be exportable in standard formats and must support bilingual generation in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R001	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Partner Rate Card Configuration	The system must allow authorized commercial users to configure VSA partner-specific rate cards for slot purchase and sale agreements. Each rate card must capture the VSA consortium name, partner line code (SCAC), effective date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), trade lane (origin port UN/LOCODE to destination port UN/LOCODE), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), and agreed slot rate per TEU/FEU. Rate cards must support multi-currency entry in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with the base settlement currency explicitly defined per partner agreement. The system must enforce that no two active rate cards overlap for the same partner, trade lane, container type, and date range combination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R005	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Multi-Entity VSA Cost Apportionment	When a single VSA agreement covers multiple group entities, the system must apportion slot costs across entities based on configurable allocation rules. Supported allocation methods must include: proportional to TEU volume shipped per entity, fixed percentage split defined in the inter-company agreement, actual usage per port of loading (e.g., cargo loaded at QAHMD allocated to Qatar entity, cargo loaded at AEJEA allocated to UAE entity), or a hybrid method combining fixed and variable components. Each apportionment must generate inter-company transfer pricing entries that comply with OECD arm's-length principles and produce documentation suitable for tax authority review in all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R009	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Minimum Quantity Commitment Monitoring	The system must monitor minimum quantity commitments (MQC) defined in each VSA agreement and alert stakeholders when actual utilization is projected to fall below the committed volume. Projections must use AI-driven forecasting based on historical booking patterns, seasonal trends, and current pipeline bookings. When the projected shortfall exceeds 10% of the MQC for any given period, the system must generate an early warning notification at least 30 days before the commitment period ends. The notification must include the shortfall quantity in TEU, estimated dead freight liability in the settlement currency, and recommended corrective actions such as slot swaps or repositioning empty containers to maximize utilization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R010	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Agreement Document Repository	The system must maintain a centralized document repository for all VSA agreements, addenda, rate schedules, and settlement correspondence. Each document must be tagged with metadata including the VSA consortium identifier, partner line codes, effective period, trade lanes covered, and associated entity. The repository must support full-text search in both English and Arabic, version control with check-in/check-out locking, and role-based access control ensuring that only authorized commercial and legal personnel can view or modify sensitive agreement terms. Documents must be stored with AES-256 encryption at rest and must comply with Qatar PDPL, UAE DIFC data protection, and India DPDPA requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R011	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Automated Slot Swap Proposals	The system must use AI algorithms to identify slot swap opportunities between VSA partners when one partner has excess capacity on a specific voyage leg and another partner has overflow demand. The AI engine must evaluate factors including container type compatibility, port rotation alignment, transit time impact, and cost differential. Swap proposals must be generated automatically and presented to commercial users for review, showing the financial impact (cost savings or additional expense) for each entity involved. Approved swaps must automatically update the slot allocation records and trigger amended settlement calculations. The system must log all swap proposals including those declined for audit and pattern analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R012	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Rate Benchmarking Against Market	The system must provide benchmarking capabilities that compare VSA slot rates against prevailing market freight rates for equivalent trade lanes. Market rate data must be sourced from configured external feeds (Freightos Baltic Index, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, or manually uploaded market surveys). The benchmarking report must show the premium or discount of VSA rates versus market rates per trade lane, container type, and time period. When VSA rates exceed market rates by more than a configurable threshold (default 15%), the system must flag the agreement for commercial review. All rate comparisons must be normalized to USD per TEU for consistency, with local currency equivalents displayed alongside.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R013	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	Offline VSA Rate Access for Field Staff	The system must provide offline access capability for commercial field staff who need to reference VSA slot rates during client meetings or port visits where internet connectivity may be unavailable. The offline module must synchronize the latest rate cards, slot availability snapshots, and partner contact details to the local device when connectivity is available. Any rate queries or tentative slot reservations made offline must be queued and synchronized to the central system upon reconnection. Conflict resolution rules must apply when offline reservations conflict with bookings made by other users during the disconnection period, with the earlier timestamp taking priority and the later reservation flagged for manual resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R014	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Performance Scorecard per Partner	The system must generate quarterly performance scorecards for each VSA partner evaluating key metrics including: slot utilization rate against commitment, on-time vessel arrival performance at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, documentation accuracy rate, settlement payment timeliness, equipment interchange efficiency, and dispute resolution turnaround time. Each metric must be weighted according to configurable business priorities and combined into a composite partner score on a 0-100 scale. Scorecards must be exportable as PDF reports in both English and Arabic formats, and historical scorecards must be retained for a minimum of seven years per regional record-keeping regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-001-R015	FEAT-007-3-001	MOD-007	VSA Dispute Management and Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a structured dispute management module for VSA-related disagreements including rate discrepancies, slot allocation conflicts, settlement amount variances, and service level breaches. Each dispute must be logged with a unique reference number, disputing entity, counter-party, disputed amount in settlement currency, supporting evidence attachments, and escalation timeline. The module must enforce SLA-based resolution timelines (default 15 business days for standard disputes, 5 business days for urgent disputes) and automatically escalate unresolved disputes through predefined management levels. Resolution outcomes must be linked back to the original settlement records with adjustment entries posted to the financial system for each affected entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R001	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Trigger Condition Definition	The system must allow configuration of dead freight trigger conditions per trade lane, customer, and service contract. Trigger conditions must include: booking not utilized (no-show) where the shipper fails to deliver cargo by the vessel cut-off time at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT; partial utilization where less than the booked quantity of containers is shipped; and short-shipped weight where the actual cargo weight is below the minimum billable weight defined in the tariff. Each trigger condition must have a configurable grace period (e.g., 24 hours before cut-off for cancellation without penalty) and must account for force majeure exceptions such as port closures, vessel delays, or government-imposed restrictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R002	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Charge Calculation Engine	The system must calculate dead freight charges based on the applicable rate from the customer's contract, tariff, or spot rate quotation. The calculation must support multiple methodologies: flat rate per unused TEU/FEU, percentage of the contracted freight rate for unused capacity, tiered penalty rates escalating with the number of no-show occurrences within a rolling period, and differential rates by container type (standard dry, reefer, open-top, flat-rack, tank container). For partial utilization, the charge must apply only to the unused portion. The engine must convert charges to the billing currency of the customer's contract (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) using the exchange rate effective on the vessel sailing date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R003	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Automated Dead Freight Invoice Generation	Upon confirmation of a dead freight event (vessel sailed with unused booked slots), the system must automatically generate a dead freight invoice within 48 hours. The invoice must include: booking reference number, vessel name and voyage number, port of loading UN/LOCODE, originally booked quantity and type, actually shipped quantity, dead freight charge breakdown with applicable rate, currency, VAT/GST treatment per jurisdiction (5% VAT for Qatar and UAE, 15% VAT for KSA, 18% GST for India), and payment terms. The invoice must be generated in the language of the customer's preference (English or Arabic) and conform to the e-invoicing format mandated by the local tax authority (ZATCA for KSA, FTA for UAE, GTA for Qatar).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R004	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Waiver Request Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for customers or internal sales teams to request dead freight charge waivers. Waiver requests must capture the reason category (force majeure, vessel schedule change by carrier, documentation delay at customs, port congestion, equipment unavailability, or other), supporting evidence documentation, the requested waiver amount (full or partial), and any proposed alternative remedy such as rolling the booking to the next sailing. The workflow must route through configurable approval levels: sales representative can approve waivers up to 500 USD equivalent, branch manager up to 2,000 USD, commercial director up to 10,000 USD, and CEO approval required above 10,000 USD. All waivers must be tracked against customer accounts for pattern analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R005	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Recovery Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard tracking dead freight charges, collections, waivers, and write-offs across all entities. The dashboard must display: total dead freight charged (current month, quarter, year), amount collected versus outstanding, aging analysis of unpaid dead freight invoices (30/60/90/120+ days), waiver rate as a percentage of total charges, top 10 customers by dead freight occurrence frequency, and trend analysis by trade lane. Users must be able to drill down from summary to individual transaction level. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, trade lane, customer segment, and date range. Arabic RTL layout must be fully supported with right-aligned numeric columns and mirrored chart axes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R006	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Customer No-Show Pattern Detection with AI	The system must employ AI pattern recognition to identify customers with recurring no-show or under-utilization behaviors. The AI model must analyze booking-to-shipment conversion rates per customer over rolling 6-month and 12-month periods, identify statistically significant deviations from industry norms (typically 85-90% conversion), detect seasonal patterns in no-shows, and flag customers whose no-show frequency exceeds two standard deviations from the mean. Flagged customers must be categorized into risk tiers (low, medium, high, critical) and the system must recommend mitigation actions such as requiring booking deposits, reducing allocation limits, or adjusting contract terms. Recommendations must be presented to commercial managers for review before any automated action is taken.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R007	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Allocation to VSA Partners	When dead freight occurs on a VSA vessel, the system must allocate the dead freight cost impact to the appropriate VSA partner(s) based on the agreement terms. If the unused slot was purchased from a partner under a minimum commitment, the dead freight cost must be allocated to the entity that failed to fill the slot. The system must calculate the net financial impact considering whether the slot was re-sold to another shipper (partial mitigation) or remained empty. Cross-entity dead freight allocations must generate inter-company debit/credit notes with proper transfer pricing documentation. The allocation must be reflected in the VSA settlement statement for the applicable period and reconciled during the partner settlement process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R008	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Impact on Revenue Forecasting	The system must integrate dead freight historical data into the revenue forecasting module, adjusting projected revenue figures based on expected no-show rates per trade lane and customer segment. The forecasting model must distinguish between systematic dead freight (chronic low-utilization routes) and random dead freight (occasional no-shows by otherwise reliable customers). For each revenue forecast period, the system must calculate a dead freight adjustment factor that reduces gross projected revenue to net achievable revenue. The adjustment factor must be recalculated monthly using a weighted moving average of the past 12 months of actual dead freight experience. Forecasts must be generated per entity and consolidated at the group level in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R009	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Booking Deposit Enforcement for High-Risk Customers	The system must support configurable booking deposit requirements for customers flagged as high dead freight risk. When a high-risk customer creates a booking, the system must automatically require a non-refundable deposit (configurable percentage, default 25% of estimated freight) before confirming the space allocation. The deposit must be collected via the payment gateway integrated with the system and held in a suspense account until the vessel sails. If the cargo is shipped as booked, the deposit must be applied as a credit against the freight invoice. If dead freight occurs, the deposit must be forfeited and applied against the dead freight charge. Deposit requirements must be communicated to the customer in the booking confirmation in their preferred language (English or Arabic) with clear terms and conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R010	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Regulatory Compliance per Jurisdiction	The system must ensure that dead freight charges and recovery processes comply with the commercial and tax regulations of each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar, dead freight invoices must comply with QFC commercial transaction regulations and QAR billing requirements. For UAE, charges must align with the UAE Commercial Transactions Law and DMCC free zone rules where applicable. For KSA, e-invoicing must comply with ZATCA Phase 2 requirements with XML format and QR code generation. For India, dead freight must be classified under the correct HSN/SAC code for GST purposes (SAC 996721 for coastal, 996511 for international) with proper IGST/CGST/SGST treatment based on the place of supply rules. The system must generate jurisdiction-specific tax documentation automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R011	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Automated Dead Freight Notification to Customers	The system must send automated notifications to customers at multiple stages of the dead freight lifecycle. A pre-warning notification must be sent when the booking cut-off time is approaching (configurable, default 48 hours before cut-off) and no shipping instructions have been received. A dead freight confirmation notice must be sent within 24 hours after the vessel sails with unused booked capacity, detailing the charges to be applied. A formal invoice notification must accompany the dead freight invoice with payment instructions specific to the entity's bank details (QNB for Qatar, ADCB for UAE, Al Rajhi for KSA, HDFC for India). Notifications must be sent via email and optionally SMS, in the customer's preferred language, with all amounts displayed in the contract billing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-001-R008	FEAT-000-2-001	\N	White-Label Domain Support	Each company can configure custom domain (e.g., erp.globalshipping.qa) with CNAME pointing to CS ERP. System auto-provisions SSL via Let's Encrypt. Domain verified via DNS TXT record. Company logo and name shown instead of CS ERP branding when accessed via custom domain.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R012	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Exception Handling for Transshipment Cargo	The system must handle dead freight calculations differently for transshipment cargo where containers are transferred between feeder and mainline vessels at hub ports such as Jebel Ali or Hamad Port. If a feeder vessel arrives late causing the container to miss the mainline vessel connection, the dead freight on the mainline must not be charged to the shipper but rather absorbed as an operational cost or allocated to the feeder service operator. The system must track the root cause of each transshipment dead freight event, linking it to the feeder vessel delay, port congestion, or customs hold. When the root cause is attributable to a third-party feeder operator, the system must generate a claim against that operator with supporting evidence including vessel AIS data and port terminal reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R013	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Write-Off Authorization	The system must provide a structured write-off process for dead freight charges deemed uncollectible after exhausting recovery efforts. Write-off requests must include the original dead freight amount, collection efforts documented (minimum three follow-up attempts required), customer credit status, and the recommended write-off amount. Write-off authorization must follow entity-specific financial delegation policies: up to 1,000 USD equivalent by credit controller, up to 5,000 USD by finance manager, up to 25,000 USD by CFO, and above 25,000 USD by board resolution. Approved write-offs must be posted to the bad debt expense account in the entity's general ledger and must reduce the customer's credit limit by the written-off amount. A comprehensive write-off register must be maintained per entity for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R014	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Reporting for Management Review	The system must generate monthly and quarterly dead freight management reports consolidating data across all four entities. Reports must include: total bookings versus actual shipments by volume (TEU) and revenue, dead freight percentage by trade lane and port, financial impact analysis showing charges raised, collected, waived, and written off, customer concentration analysis identifying systemic offenders, comparison against industry benchmarks and prior period performance, and corrective action tracking for previously identified issues. Reports must be available in both English and Arabic, with executive summary sections suitable for board presentation. Hijri calendar date equivalents must be shown alongside Gregorian dates for KSA and Qatar entity reports. Reports must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R015	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Overbooking Strategy Based on Dead Freight Analytics	The system must leverage historical dead freight analytics to recommend optimal overbooking strategies per vessel, trade lane, and port. The AI engine must analyze historical no-show rates, seasonal patterns, customer reliability scores, and current booking pipeline to calculate a recommended overbooking percentage for each sailing. The recommendation must balance the risk of dead freight (revenue loss from empty slots) against the risk of roll-over (operational cost of bumping confirmed cargo when all booked containers actually arrive). The system must set configurable maximum overbooking limits per trade lane (default 10% of vessel capacity) and must never recommend overbooking for hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9) or time-sensitive reefer shipments. Overbooking recommendations must be approved by the operations manager before implementation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R016	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Dead Freight Credit Note Processing	The system must support the generation of credit notes when previously charged dead freight is subsequently reversed due to waiver approval, dispute resolution, or recalculation. Credit notes must reference the original dead freight invoice number, state the reason for reversal, and comply with the same tax and e-invoicing requirements as the original charge. For KSA operations, credit notes must be submitted to ZATCA within the mandated timeframe. For Indian operations, credit notes must adjust the GST liability in the applicable return period. The system must ensure that credit notes reduce the customer's outstanding balance and update the dead freight recovery dashboard in real time. Partial credit notes must be supported for cases where only a portion of the original dead freight charge is reversed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-002-R017	FEAT-007-3-002	MOD-007	Offline Dead Freight Data Capture at Port	The system must support offline data capture for dead freight events at port locations where network connectivity may be intermittent, particularly at container yards and gate complexes at Hamad Port and JNPT. Port operations staff must be able to record no-show confirmations, partial shipment quantities, and container condition notes on mobile devices without an active internet connection. The offline module must timestamp all entries using the device's GPS-synchronized clock and queue them for synchronization when connectivity is restored. Conflict detection must identify cases where the same booking has been updated both offline and online during the disconnection period, presenting both versions to a supervisor for manual resolution. All offline-captured data must be clearly marked in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R001	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Freight Rate Compliance Scanning Engine	The system must implement an automated scanning engine that compares every freight invoice generated against the applicable rate source (contract tariff, spot quotation, or published tariff) to detect under-billing. The engine must check base ocean freight, each applicable surcharge (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS, documentation fee, seal fee, VGM fee), and any special charges against the rates effective on the bill of lading date. Discrepancies exceeding a configurable tolerance threshold (default 1% or 10 USD equivalent, whichever is greater) must be flagged as potential revenue leakage. The scan must cover all invoices across all four entities and must process newly generated invoices within 4 hours of creation, operating continuously without manual intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R002	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Surcharge Application Completeness Check	The system must verify that all mandatory surcharges applicable to a shipment have been applied on the invoice. The verification must cross-reference the shipment's characteristics (origin port, destination port, container type, commodity code, weight, vessel flag, trade lane) against the surcharge applicability matrix configured in the tariff module. Common leakage scenarios to detect include: missing peak season surcharge during configured peak periods, missing reefer electricity surcharge for refrigerated containers, missing hazardous cargo surcharge for IMO-classified goods, missing overweight surcharge for containers exceeding the standard payload, and missing war risk surcharge for designated high-risk trade lanes. Each missing surcharge must be flagged with the estimated revenue impact calculated in the invoice currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R003	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Weight and Measurement Discrepancy Detection	The system must compare declared cargo weights and measurements on the booking against verified gross mass (VGM) certificates, port weighbridge records, and terminal handling system data to identify discrepancies that result in revenue leakage. When the actual weight exceeds the declared weight by more than 2% or 500 kg (whichever is greater), the system must flag the shipment for additional freight collection based on the weight differential. For volume-based commodities, the system must compare declared cubic measurements against container scanning data where available. The revenue impact must be calculated using the applicable rate (weight or measurement basis, whichever yields higher revenue as per the freight terms) and currency of the contract. Detected discrepancies must be linked to the original booking for correction processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R004	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Contract Rate Expiry and Rollover Monitoring	The system must monitor all active customer contracts and flag instances where shipments are being rated at expired contract rates instead of the applicable current rates. When a contract expires and no renewal or extension has been processed, all subsequent bookings from that customer must be automatically flagged if they are rated below the current published tariff or the proposed renewal rate. The system must generate a daily report listing all shipments processed under expired contracts in the previous 24 hours, showing the rate applied versus the rate that should have been applied, and the revenue differential per shipment and in aggregate. The report must be broken down by entity and must highlight the cumulative exposure in both local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R005	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Free Time and Demurrage Leakage Analysis	The system must analyze container detention and demurrage billing to identify instances where free time has been incorrectly extended or demurrage charges have not been applied. The analysis must compare the actual container dwell time (from gate-out at origin to gate-in at destination, or port free time from vessel discharge to container pick-up) against the free time allowance defined in the customer's contract or the port's standard tariff. Any container exceeding the free time without a corresponding demurrage invoice must be flagged. The system must also detect unauthorized free time extensions granted without proper approval, comparing granted free days against the authorized limits per user role. Revenue leakage must be quantified per container, per customer, and per port across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R006	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	AI-Powered Anomaly Detection in Billing Patterns	The system must employ machine learning algorithms to detect anomalous billing patterns that may indicate revenue leakage. The AI model must establish baseline billing profiles per customer, trade lane, and container type, then flag statistical outliers such as: sudden drops in average revenue per TEU for a specific customer without a corresponding contract change, unusual concentration of manually adjusted invoices by a particular user, billing amounts consistently at the lower bound of acceptable ranges, and patterns of credits or refunds that deviate from historical norms. The anomaly detection must operate on a continuous basis, scoring each invoice on a risk scale of 0-100. Invoices scoring above 75 must be automatically routed for commercial audit review. The model must be retrained monthly using the latest transaction data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R007	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Commodity Misclassification Revenue Impact	The system must cross-reference the commodity description and HS code declared by the shipper against the applicable freight rate classification to detect potential revenue leakage from commodity misclassification. When a high-rate commodity (e.g., electronics, pharmaceuticals, automotive parts) is declared under a lower-rate classification, the system must flag the shipment for review. The detection engine must use both rule-based matching (HS code to commodity rate class mapping) and AI-based text analysis of the cargo description to identify likely misclassifications. The estimated revenue impact must be calculated as the difference between the rate applicable to the suspected correct classification and the rate actually applied. Flagged shipments must be routed to the documentation team for verification against the commercial invoice, packing list, and certificate of origin.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R008	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Inter-Company Revenue Leakage Detection	The system must monitor inter-company transactions between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities to detect revenue leakage at the group level. This includes verifying that transshipment charges between entities are applied at arm's-length rates, that hub feeder charges at Jebel Ali are correctly allocated to the originating entity, and that shared service costs are apportioned according to the inter-company agreement. The system must flag scenarios where one entity absorbs costs that should be charged to another entity, or where transfer prices deviate from the approved transfer pricing policy by more than 5%. Detected discrepancies must be quantified in USD and reported to the group finance controller with recommendations for corrective journal entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R009	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Revenue Leakage Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide a comprehensive revenue leakage dashboard showing detected leakage categorized by type (under-billing, missing surcharges, weight discrepancies, expired contracts, free time violations, anomalies), entity, trade lane, and time period. The dashboard must display total detected leakage amount, amount recovered, amount under investigation, and amount written off. Trend charts must show leakage as a percentage of total revenue over rolling 12-month periods with drill-down capability to individual transactions. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with properly mirrored charts and right-aligned currency columns. Executive summary reports must be auto-generated monthly in both English and Arabic, with Hijri calendar date references for GCC entity reports, and distributed to configured recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R010	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Automated Revenue Recovery Workflow	When a revenue leakage instance is confirmed, the system must initiate an automated recovery workflow. The workflow must generate a supplementary invoice or debit note for the under-billed amount, route it through the appropriate approval chain (commercial manager for amounts up to 5,000 USD, commercial director above 5,000 USD), and upon approval, dispatch it to the customer with a detailed explanation of the charge basis. The recovery invoice must reference the original invoice number, the specific rate or surcharge that was under-applied, and the contractual or tariff basis for the additional charge. VAT/GST must be correctly applied per the jurisdiction rules. The system must track recovery success rates and aging, escalating unpaid recovery invoices through the standard collections process. Recovered amounts must be tagged in the system for leakage analytics reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R011	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Rate Erosion Trend Analysis	The system must track and analyze rate erosion trends across all customers, trade lanes, and container types to identify gradual revenue leakage from declining effective rates. The analysis must compare the average revenue per TEU achieved over successive quarterly periods against the published tariff rates and contracted rates, adjusting for changes in trade lane mix and container type mix. Where the effective rate declines by more than 5% quarter-over-quarter without a corresponding tariff reduction or market rate decline, the system must flag the trade lane or customer segment for commercial review. The analysis must separate the impact of volume mix changes from actual rate erosion, presenting a waterfall decomposition showing the contribution of each factor. Reports must be generated per entity and at consolidated group level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R012	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Duplicate Payment and Credit Detection	The system must scan all credit notes, refunds, and payment allocations to detect duplicate payments to customers or erroneous credits that constitute revenue leakage. The detection logic must identify: credit notes issued against the same original invoice more than once, refunds processed for amounts exceeding the original charge, payments allocated to the same invoice multiple times resulting in overpayment, and credit notes issued without a corresponding approved reason code. For multi-entity operations, the scan must also detect scenarios where a credit is issued by one entity for a charge raised by another entity without proper inter-company reconciliation. All detected anomalies must be assigned a severity rating (low, medium, high, critical) based on the monetary value and pattern recurrence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R013	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Leakage Prevention Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable rules engine that prevents revenue leakage proactively by enforcing business rules at the point of transaction creation. Rules must include: mandatory surcharge application before invoice finalization, weight/measurement validation against VGM data before rate calculation, contract validity check before rate application, credit limit verification before booking confirmation, and minimum rate floor enforcement preventing invoices below cost. Each rule must be configurable as either a hard block (prevents the transaction from proceeding) or a soft warning (allows override with supervisor authorization). The rules engine must support entity-specific rule configurations to accommodate different regulatory and commercial practices across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R014	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Revenue Leakage Root Cause Classification	The system must classify each detected revenue leakage instance by root cause category to enable systemic improvement. Root cause categories must include: system configuration error (incorrect rate setup), manual data entry error (wrong container type or quantity), process gap (missing approval step), training deficiency (user unaware of applicable surcharge), contract ambiguity (unclear rate applicability clause), and intentional override (user deliberately reduced charges). The AI engine must suggest the most likely root cause based on the leakage characteristics, user action history, and similar past incidents. Monthly root cause analysis reports must be generated showing the distribution of leakage by root cause, with the system recommending targeted corrective actions such as system configuration fixes, user training programs, or process redesign.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R015	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Third-Party Agent Commission Leakage Monitoring	The system must monitor commission payments to third-party booking agents, freight forwarders, and sales intermediaries to detect over-payment or unauthorized commission leakage. The monitoring must verify that commission rates applied match the approved agent agreement, that commissions are calculated on the correct revenue base (gross freight, net freight, or specific charge components as per the agreement), and that no commissions are paid on charges excluded from the commission base such as terminal handling charges or government levies. The system must flag duplicate commission payments for the same shipment, commission payments to inactive or terminated agents, and instances where the effective commission rate exceeds the contractual maximum. Detected anomalies must be reported per entity with the total exposure amount in local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R016	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Currency Conversion Leakage Detection	The system must detect revenue leakage arising from incorrect or unfavorable currency conversions in multi-currency billing scenarios. The detection engine must verify that the exchange rate applied on each invoice matches the rate source and timing specified in the customer's contract (e.g., booking date rate, B/L date rate, or invoice date rate from the specified central bank). Deviations exceeding 0.5% from the contractual rate source must be flagged. The system must also identify cases where invoices are issued in a non-contracted currency resulting in conversion losses, and scenarios where the currency of surcharges differs from the base freight currency without proper rate application. Total exposure from currency conversion leakage must be tracked monthly per entity, trade lane, and currency pair, with the aggregate impact reported in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-003-R017	FEAT-007-3-003	MOD-007	Leakage Detection Audit Trail and Evidence Packaging	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every revenue leakage instance from initial detection through resolution or write-off. The audit trail must record: detection timestamp, detection method (automated scan, AI anomaly, manual report), leakage type and amount, assigned investigator, investigation findings with supporting evidence, customer communication log, recovery actions taken, and final disposition (recovered, waived, written off, disputed). All supporting documents (invoices, contracts, rate cards, email correspondence, screenshots) must be attached to the leakage record. The evidence package must be exportable as a consolidated PDF for use in customer disputes, legal proceedings, or regulatory audits. Records must be retained for a minimum of seven years per jurisdictional requirements and must be searchable by any metadata field in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R001	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Delegation of Authority Matrix Configuration	The system must allow each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to configure its own delegation of authority (DOA) matrix defining approval limits for pricing decisions. The DOA matrix must specify, for each user role and pricing action type (spot rate approval, contract rate approval, discount authorization, surcharge waiver, credit term extension, dead freight waiver), the maximum monetary value that role can approve without escalation. Limits must be defined in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to USD equivalent for cross-entity comparison. The matrix must support hierarchical escalation paths where exceeding a role's limit automatically routes to the next approval level. Changes to the DOA matrix must require dual authorization from the entity's managing director and group CFO.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R002	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Spot Rate Quotation Approval Workflow	The system must enforce an approval workflow for all spot rate quotations before they are communicated to customers. The workflow must evaluate the quoted rate against the applicable cost floor (vessel slot cost plus port charges plus minimum margin), the published tariff rate, and any applicable rate guidelines for the trade lane. Quotations below the cost floor must require commercial director approval regardless of the DOA matrix. Quotations between the cost floor and the published tariff must follow the standard DOA matrix. Quotations at or above the published tariff may be auto-approved. The workflow must capture the sales representative's justification for below-tariff pricing, competitive intelligence (competitor rate if known), volume commitment from the customer, and strategic importance rating. Approval decisions must be recorded within the configured SLA (default 4 hours for standard, 2 hours for urgent).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R003	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Contract Rate Approval with Financial Analysis	The system must provide a comprehensive financial analysis package for each contract rate submitted for approval. The analysis must include: proposed rate versus current published tariff and market rate benchmarks, projected annual revenue based on committed volume, contribution margin analysis per container type and trade lane, comparison against the customer's historical rates and volumes, impact analysis on overall trade lane profitability, and cash flow projection for the contract duration. The approval workflow must require sign-off from the sales team, commercial manager, finance analyst (for margin verification), and commercial director (for contracts exceeding configurable annual revenue thresholds). For multi-entity contracts, all affected entities must approve their respective portions before the contract is activated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R004	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Discount Authorization and Cap Enforcement	The system must enforce configurable discount caps by trade lane, container type, and customer segment. When a pricing user applies a discount, the system must verify that the discount percentage and absolute amount do not exceed the caps defined for the user's role in the DOA matrix. Discounts within the user's authority may be self-approved with mandatory reason code selection (volume commitment, competitive match, strategic account, new customer acquisition, retention). Discounts exceeding the user's authority must be automatically escalated to the next approval level. The system must maintain a real-time discount budget tracker per trade lane and per entity, showing total discounts granted year-to-date against the annual discount budget. When discount utilization reaches 80% of the budget, the system must restrict further discounts to management-level approval only.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R005	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Mobile Approval Interface for Pricing Decisions	The system must provide a mobile-responsive approval interface allowing authorized approvers to review and action pricing requests from smartphones and tablets. The mobile interface must display a summary of the pricing request including customer name, trade lane, proposed rate, comparison against benchmarks, financial impact, and the requestor's justification. Approvers must be able to approve, reject, or request modification with comments directly from the mobile interface. Push notifications must be sent for new approval requests and must include enough context for the approver to make a preliminary assessment. The mobile interface must support both English and Arabic with automatic language detection based on the user's device settings. Biometric authentication (fingerprint or face recognition) must be supported as an alternative to password entry for approval actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R006	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Approval SLA Monitoring and Auto-Escalation	The system must monitor the elapsed time for each pending pricing approval against configurable SLA targets per request type and priority level. Default SLAs must be: 2 hours for urgent spot rate approvals, 4 hours for standard spot rates, 24 hours for contract rate approvals, and 8 hours for discount and waiver authorizations. When an approval request breaches 75% of its SLA, the system must send a reminder notification to the assigned approver. When the SLA is fully breached, the system must automatically escalate the request to the next level in the approval hierarchy and notify both the original approver and the escalation recipient. Chronic SLA breaches by specific approvers must be tracked and reported in a monthly performance dashboard. The system must support temporary delegation during approver absence (vacation, travel) with automatic routing to the designated delegate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R007	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Bulk Rate Approval for Seasonal Adjustments	The system must support bulk approval workflows for seasonal rate adjustments affecting multiple customers, trade lanes, or container types simultaneously. Commercial managers must be able to submit a bulk rate change proposal specifying the adjustment type (percentage increase/decrease or absolute amount), scope (specific trade lanes, customer segments, or all), effective date range, and business justification (peak season, fuel cost change, market adjustment). The bulk proposal must display the aggregated financial impact across all affected shipments and entities before submission for approval. Approvers must be able to review the overall impact summary and drill down to individual customer-level impacts. Partial approvals must be supported, allowing the approver to exclude specific customers or trade lanes from the bulk change while approving the remainder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R008	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Surcharge Waiver Approval with Revenue Impact	The system must enforce an approval workflow for any surcharge waiver or reduction requested by sales teams on behalf of customers. The waiver request must capture: the specific surcharge being waived (THC, ISPS, documentation fee, seal fee, or other), the waiver scope (one-time, specific period, or contract duration), the estimated annual revenue impact of the waiver, and the commercial rationale. The system must display the customer's total surcharge waivers across all active contracts to prevent cumulative erosion of ancillary revenue. Waiver approvals must follow the DOA matrix with the approval level determined by the annualized revenue impact of the waiver. For waivers on regulated surcharges (e.g., ISPS security charge), the system must display a compliance warning indicating potential regulatory implications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R007	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Reefer Container Booking Temperature Specifications	The system must capture and validate reefer booking parameters including set temperature (range -35°C to +30°C), ventilation settings (CBM/hour), humidity control percentage, controlled atmosphere requirements (O2 and CO2 percentages), and genset requirements for inland transportation. The AI engine must validate temperature settings against commodity-specific guidelines (e.g., frozen meat at -18°C, pharmaceuticals at +2°C to +8°C) and flag mismatches. Pre-trip inspection (PTI) scheduling must be automatically triggered upon booking confirmation, coordinating with the equipment control team at the respective port. The booking must record whether the reefer requires continuous power monitoring and whether the commodity requires phytosanitary or veterinary clearance at the discharge port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R009	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Approval Workflow Template Management	The system must allow administrators to create, modify, and manage approval workflow templates for different pricing scenarios. Each template must define: the trigger conditions (pricing action type, threshold amounts, customer segment), the approval stages (sequential or parallel), the approver roles at each stage, the required information and documents at each stage, SLA targets per stage, escalation rules, and notification preferences. Templates must be configurable per entity to accommodate different organizational structures (e.g., the Qatar entity may require additional government affairs approval for state-owned enterprise customers, while the India entity may require a separate compliance check for shipments to sanctioned destinations). The system must support versioning of workflow templates with the ability to revert to previous versions if needed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R010	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Conditional Approval with Rate Guardrails	The system must support conditional approvals where the approver authorizes a pricing request subject to specific conditions being met. Supported condition types must include: volume commitment threshold (approved rate valid only if customer ships minimum X TEU per month), time-bound validity (approved rate expires after a specified date, requiring re-approval), payment term condition (approved rate contingent on advance payment or LC terms), and reciprocal condition (approved rate for trade lane A contingent on customer committing volume on trade lane B). The system must automatically monitor whether conditions are being met post-approval and trigger alerts when conditions are breached. When a condition breach is detected, the system must flag the customer's account for rate adjustment and notify the commercial team within 24 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R011	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Pricing Committee Review Workflow	The system must support a pricing committee review workflow for strategic pricing decisions that exceed individual DOA authority or require cross-functional input. The pricing committee workflow must aggregate relevant data into a committee review package including: the pricing proposal details, customer profile and history, trade lane profitability analysis, competitive landscape assessment, and impact on overall portfolio yield. Committee members must be able to submit their votes (approve, reject, modify) and comments asynchronously through the system within a configurable review period (default 48 hours). The system must calculate the committee decision based on configurable rules (simple majority, unanimous, or weighted voting). Meeting minutes must be auto-generated from the comments and votes recorded in the system, stored as an official record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R012	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Approval Audit Trail with Full Traceability	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for every pricing approval action capturing: the pricing request details at the time of submission (original values), each approver's identity and role, approval or rejection decision with timestamp, comments or conditions attached, any modifications made to the request during the approval process (showing before and after values), the IP address and device type used for the approval action, and the total elapsed time from submission to final decision. The audit trail must be tamper-proof using cryptographic hashing to detect any unauthorized modifications to historical records. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 10 years and must be searchable and exportable for regulatory compliance reviews, particularly for anti-trust investigations related to pricing practices in shipping consortia.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R013	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Arabic Language Support in Approval Workflows	The system must provide full Arabic language support throughout the pricing approval workflow. All approval request forms, notification emails, mobile push notifications, approval summaries, and audit reports must be available in Arabic with proper RTL text rendering. Bilingual approvers must be able to switch between English and Arabic views of the same approval request without losing context. Arabic free-text comments must be supported in approval notes and justification fields with proper Unicode handling. All currency amounts must be displayed with Arabic-Indic numerals when the Arabic locale is active, and with Western Arabic numerals in English mode. Date fields must display Hijri calendar dates as the primary format for KSA and Qatar entity workflows, with Gregorian equivalents shown in parentheses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R014	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Integration with External Rate Sources for Approval Benchmarking	The system must integrate with external rate benchmarking sources to provide approvers with market context when reviewing pricing requests. Supported sources must include Freightos Baltic Index (FBX), Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Drewry World Container Index, and internally configured competitive rate databases. When an approver opens a pricing request, the system must automatically display the current market benchmark rate for the same trade lane, the trend over the past 90 days, and the percentile position of the proposed rate relative to the market range. For trade lanes where external indices are unavailable, the system must display the entity's historical rate distribution for the past 12 months as an internal benchmark. Rate benchmarks must update at minimum daily and must cache the last known value for offline approval scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R015	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Pricing Approval KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a KPI dashboard tracking the efficiency and effectiveness of the pricing approval process across all entities. Key metrics must include: average approval turnaround time by request type and entity, SLA compliance rate (percentage of approvals completed within SLA), approval versus rejection rate by approver and pricing type, average number of approval iterations before final decision, revenue impact of approved pricing changes (monthly and year-to-date), discount budget utilization by trade lane and entity, and escalation frequency by approver. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate metrics to individual transactions. Configurable alerts must trigger when KPI thresholds are breached (e.g., SLA compliance drops below 90%). The dashboard must support both English and Arabic interfaces with proper RTL chart rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R016	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Emergency Pricing Override with Post-Facto Approval	The system must support an emergency pricing override mechanism for time-critical situations where the standard approval workflow cannot be completed before the commercial deadline. Designated senior commercial users (minimum commercial manager level) must be able to activate an emergency override that bypasses the normal approval chain and immediately applies the requested rate. The override must require the user to provide an emergency justification and select from predefined emergency categories (vessel sailing imminent, customer ultimatum with departure risk, competitor undercut requiring immediate response, or government-mandated rate). All emergency overrides must automatically trigger a mandatory post-facto approval workflow within 24 hours where the normal approval chain reviews and ratifies or reverses the decision. Overrides not ratified within 48 hours must be automatically reversed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R017	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Approval Workflow for Cross-Entity Pricing Alignment	The system must enforce a cross-entity approval workflow when pricing decisions made by one entity affect the commercial interests of another group entity. Scenarios requiring cross-entity approval must include: a UAE entity quoting a rate for cargo originating from Qatar (potentially diverting volume from the Qatar entity), rate reductions on a shared trade lane that may trigger a customer request for matching rates from other entities, and transshipment rate changes at hub ports that affect feeder service economics for other entities. The cross-entity workflow must notify the affected entity's commercial head and require their concurrence before the pricing change is finalized. Disagreements must be escalated to the group commercial director for binding resolution. The workflow must enforce a maximum 48-hour response time from the affected entity before automatic escalation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-3-004-R018	FEAT-007-3-004	MOD-007	Offline Approval Queue and Synchronization	The system must support offline approval functionality for approvers who are traveling or in areas with limited connectivity, such as during port visits to Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The offline module must download pending approval requests to the approver's device with all supporting data (financial analysis, benchmarks, customer history) when connectivity is available. Approvers must be able to review requests and record their approval or rejection decisions offline with comments. Offline decisions must be cryptographically signed using the device's secure enclave to prevent tampering. Upon reconnection, offline decisions must be synchronized to the central system in chronological order. If a request has been modified or escalated while the approver was offline, the system must flag the conflict and require the approver to re-review the updated request before their decision is applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R001	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Export Booking Creation with Multi-Entity Support	The system must allow customer service agents to create COC export bookings on behalf of shippers, freight forwarders, or NVOCCs, selecting the correct legal entity (Qatar entity for Hamad Port QAHMD, UAE entity for Khalifa Port AEKHL and Jebel Ali AEJEA, India entity for JNPT INNSA, KSA entity for Saudi ports). Each booking must capture shipper details, consignee, notify party, commodity description with HS code, requested vessel/voyage, container type and quantity (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR), port of loading, port of discharge, final destination, and INCOTERMS 2020 code (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, etc.). The AI engine must auto-suggest optimal vessel rotations based on transit time and cost.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R002	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	AI-Driven Booking Slot Availability Check	Upon booking creation, the system must perform real-time slot availability checks against vessel capacity plans, verifying weight limits, TEU allocation, reefer plug availability, and hazardous cargo segregation constraints per IMDG code. The AI engine must evaluate booking feasibility within 3 seconds and return available alternatives if the requested vessel is fully booked. The system must consider equipment repositioning costs and empty container availability at the origin port. For Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must also validate against terminal operator capacity limits published via BAPLIE/COPARN EDI messages and flag any potential roll-risk based on historical vessel utilization patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R003	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Booking Confirmation and Reference Number Generation	Upon successful slot allocation, the system must generate a unique booking reference number following the format [ENTITY]-EXP-[YYYYMMDD]-[SEQ] (e.g., QA-EXP-20260226-00145) and issue a booking confirmation to the customer via email and in-app notification. The confirmation must be available in both English and Arabic, with Arabic text rendered in RTL layout. The confirmation document must include booking reference, vessel name and voyage number, ETD/ETA, container details, free detention and demurrage days, VGM cut-off date, documentation cut-off, and port-specific gate-in instructions. Currency display must match the entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, INR for India, SAR for KSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R004	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Booking Amendment Workflow with Audit Trail	The system must support booking amendments for changes to container quantity, container type, vessel/voyage, commodity, weight, port pair, and INCOTERMS. Each amendment must be versioned with a sequential amendment number and timestamp. The AI engine must automatically assess the impact of amendments on slot availability, pricing, documentation validity, and regulatory compliance. Amendments within 48 hours of vessel ETD must trigger a supervisor approval workflow. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all amendments including the original value, new value, requesting user, approving user, and timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. All amendment notifications must be dispatched in the customer's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R005	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Booking Cancellation and No-Show Penalty Processing	The system must handle booking cancellations with configurable cancellation policies per trade lane and customer tier. Cancellations made within the free cancellation window (configurable per entity, default 72 hours before ETD) must be processed without penalty. Late cancellations must trigger automatic penalty calculation based on the contracted tariff rate, applied in the booking entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). No-show detection must be automated: if containers are not gated-in by the port cut-off time, the AI engine must flag the booking as a no-show, calculate applicable dead-freight charges, generate a debit note, and update the customer's reliability score. The cancellation reason must be captured using standardized codes for analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R006	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Hazardous Cargo Booking Compliance Validation	For bookings involving hazardous cargo, the system must validate IMDG class, UN number, proper shipping name, packing group, flashpoint, EmS code, and segregation requirements against the vessel stowage plan before confirming the booking. The system must check DG capacity limits per vessel and per port (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT each have specific DG acceptance matrices). The AI engine must auto-verify that the DG declaration form, MSDS, and container packing certificate are uploaded before documentation cut-off. For Class 1 (explosives) and Class 7 (radioactive), additional port authority pre-approval must be captured. The system must block booking confirmation if mandatory DG documents are missing or if segregation conflicts exist with already-booked cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R001	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	User Management Interface	List all company users with: name, email, roles, last_login, status (active/invited/suspended). Actions: invite user, edit roles, suspend/reactivate, reset MFA, force password reset, remove user. Bulk actions: suspend multiple, export user list to CSV. Search and filter by role, status, last login date.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R008	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Multi-Currency Freight Rate Application	The system must automatically apply the correct freight rate from the customer's contract or the applicable tariff, supporting rates denominated in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR. Rate application must consider base ocean freight, BAF (Bunker Adjustment Factor), CAF (Currency Adjustment Factor), THC (Terminal Handling Charges), documentation fees, seal charges, and any surcharges (peak season, war risk, low sulfur, Suez Canal transit). Currency conversion must use the daily exchange rate from the treasury module at the time of booking confirmation, with the option to lock the rate for 7 days. The system must support split-currency billing where ocean freight is in USD and local charges are in the entity's base currency. All rate calculations must be auditable with a breakdown visible to the customer service agent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R009	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	VGM Declaration Collection and Validation	The system must collect Verified Gross Mass (VGM) declarations in compliance with SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2, accepting submissions via EDI (VERMAS message), web portal upload, email parsing, and manual entry by customer service agents. The AI engine must validate that the declared VGM is within acceptable tolerance of the container tare weight plus the cargo weight declared in the booking (tolerance threshold configurable per entity, default ±5%). Discrepancies exceeding the tolerance must be flagged for review. The system must record the weighing method (Method 1: weighing the packed container, or Method 2: weighing cargo items plus dunnage plus container tare), the authorized signatory name, and the weighbridge certificate number. VGM must be received before the port cut-off deadline, and the system must send automated reminders at 72, 48, and 24 hours before cut-off.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R010	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Shipping Instruction Collection and BL Draft Generation	The system must collect shipping instructions (SI) from customers via EDI (IFTMIN message), web portal, or email, and the AI engine must auto-populate the draft Bill of Lading. SI must capture shipper, consignee, notify party (up to 3), marks and numbers, goods description, HS code, number of packages, package type, gross weight, measurement (CBM), and BL type (Original, Seaway, Express, Telex release). The AI must validate SI data against the booking details and flag discrepancies (e.g., weight exceeding booking tolerance, mismatched commodity). The system must support both Master BL and House BL workflows. Draft BL must be generated in PDF format with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) and sent to the customer for approval within the documentation cut-off timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R011	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Customer Communication Automation via AI	The system must automate customer communications throughout the export booking lifecycle using AI-generated messages in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic). Automated notifications must cover booking confirmation, amendment acknowledgment, documentation reminders, VGM deadline alerts, gate-in confirmations, vessel loading confirmation, vessel departure notification, and ETA updates. The AI engine must personalize messages based on customer tier (Premium, Standard, Economy) and communication channel preference (email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API, in-app notification). Message templates must support dynamic placeholders for booking reference, vessel details, dates in Gregorian and Hijri formats, and monetary amounts in the correct currency with locale-specific formatting (e.g., QAR 15,000.00 or ١٥٬٠٠٠٫٠٠ ر.ق).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R012	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Container Tracking Integration for Export Bookings	The system must provide real-time container tracking visibility for all export bookings by integrating with terminal operating systems (TOS) at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Milestones tracked must include empty container pickup, gate-in at port, customs inspection status, loaded on vessel, vessel departure, transshipment events, and vessel arrival at discharge port. Each milestone must be timestamped and automatically pushed to the customer via their preferred notification channel. The AI engine must predict potential delays based on historical patterns and proactively alert customers when the estimated vessel departure is at risk. Tracking data must be accessible via a customer-facing portal with map visualization and must support both LTR and RTL interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R013	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Offline Booking Capability for Connectivity Disruptions	The system must support offline booking creation when network connectivity to the central server is unavailable, enabling customer service agents at remote locations or during system outages to continue processing bookings. Offline mode must cache the latest vessel schedules, customer contracts, rate tariffs, and container availability data locally. Bookings created offline must be stored in the local device database and automatically synchronized with the central system upon reconnection, with conflict resolution logic for duplicate bookings or overbooked slots. The AI engine must flag any offline bookings that conflict with changes made during the offline period. Offline bookings must receive temporary reference numbers prefixed with OFF- that are replaced with permanent references upon sync completion. Maximum offline period supported must be 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R014	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Booking Dashboard with AI Analytics	The system must provide a comprehensive booking dashboard displaying real-time KPIs including total bookings by entity, bookings by status (pending, confirmed, amended, cancelled, completed), TEU count by trade lane, booking-to-loading conversion rate, average booking lead time, cancellation rate, and no-show rate. The AI engine must generate trend analysis, forecast booking volumes for the next 30/60/90 days, and identify anomalies (e.g., sudden drop in bookings from a key customer). Dashboard filters must include entity, port, vessel, trade lane, customer, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri), container type, and commodity group. All dashboard data must be exportable to Excel and PDF formats, with Arabic language support for exported reports including proper RTL column ordering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R015	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Special Cargo Booking Handling for OOG and Breakbulk	The system must support booking of out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo including over-height, over-width, and over-length containers using flat-rack (20FR, 40FR) and open-top (20OT, 40OT) equipment types. The booking must capture exact cargo dimensions (length, width, height, weight) and the system must calculate the number of additional slot positions consumed on the vessel (e.g., a 40FR with over-width cargo may consume 2 or 3 TEU slots). The AI engine must validate OOG dimensions against vessel cell guide restrictions and terminal handling equipment capabilities at both the loading and discharge ports. For Hamad Port, maximum crane lifting capacity of 65 tons must be validated. Lashing and securing requirements must be documented in the booking, and special stowage instructions must be transmitted to the vessel planner via BAPLIE EDI message.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R016	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Customer Credit Limit Validation at Booking	The system must validate the customer's available credit limit before confirming any COC export booking. The AI engine must calculate the estimated booking value including ocean freight, local charges, and surcharges in the entity's base currency and compare it against the customer's remaining credit exposure from the finance module. If the estimated booking value exceeds the available credit, the system must block booking confirmation and route an approval request to the credit control team. Customers flagged as cash-before-shipment (CBS) or cash-against-documents (CAD) must have payment receipt validated before booking confirmation. The credit check must consider all outstanding invoices, unbilled bookings, and pending debit notes across all entities where the customer has accounts, converted to a single currency using the treasury exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-001-R017	FEAT-008-1-001	MOD-008	Booking Roll-Over Management for Vessel Overcommitment	When a vessel is overcommitted or operationally disrupted, the system must manage the rollover of export bookings to the next available vessel. The AI engine must prioritize bookings for rollover based on configurable rules: customer tier (Premium customers rolled last), cargo type priority (reefer and DG cargo prioritized), booking confirmation timestamp (FIFO), and contractual commitments. Rolled bookings must be automatically re-confirmed on the next available vessel with updated ETD/ETA and the customer must be notified immediately with the new schedule. The system must track rollover history per booking, calculate any additional costs or demurrage waivers arising from the rollover, and update the BL draft if shipping instructions were already submitted. A rollover report must be available showing impacted bookings, reasons, and customer compensation actions taken.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R001	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Manifest Ingestion and Validation	The system must automatically ingest import cargo manifests received via EDI (IFTMIN/BAPLIE messages), carrier partner APIs, or manual upload in Excel/CSV format. The AI engine must parse and validate manifest data including BL number, container number, seal number, consignee details, notify party, commodity description, HS code, package count, gross weight, and measurement. Validation rules must check container number format against ISO 6346 standard including check digit verification, detect duplicate BL numbers, and flag weight discrepancies against vessel load plan data. For Hamad Port (QAHMD) and Jebel Ali (AEJEA), manifest data must be cross-referenced with port community system pre-arrival filings. Invalid records must be quarantined for manual review with AI-suggested corrections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R002	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Arrival Notice Generation and Distribution	Upon vessel arrival at the discharge port, the system must automatically generate import arrival notices for all consignees and notify parties listed on the manifest. The arrival notice must include vessel name and voyage, BL number, container number(s), cargo description, number of packages, gross weight, free detention and demurrage days (per entity tariff), last free day for container pickup, estimated cargo availability date, and applicable charges breakdown. Arrival notices must be generated in both English and Arabic with RTL formatting for Arabic recipients. Distribution must occur via the consignee's preferred channel (email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API). The AI engine must calculate expected customs clearance duration based on commodity type and historical clearance times at the specific port, providing an estimated cargo readiness date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R003	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Delivery Order Issuance and Authorization	The system must manage the delivery order (DO) issuance workflow, verifying that all prerequisites are met before authorizing container release. Prerequisites include: original BL surrender or telex release confirmation, freight payment verification (prepaid freight confirmed at origin or collect freight paid at destination), customs clearance status from the port community system, any applicable hold releases (customs hold, health authority hold, port authority hold). The DO must be generated with a unique reference number in format [ENTITY]-DO-[YYYYMMDD]-[SEQ], valid for a configurable period (default 14 days). The AI engine must auto-verify payment status against the finance module and flag any outstanding invoices. DO must be issued in bilingual format (English/Arabic) and transmitted electronically to the terminal operator for gate-out authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R004	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Detention and Demurrage Calculation Engine	The system must calculate detention and demurrage charges automatically based on the applicable tariff for each entity. Demurrage (port storage) must be calculated from the vessel discharge date to the container gate-out date, minus the contracted free days. Detention (container use outside port) must be calculated from the container gate-out date to the empty container return date. Tariff rates must support tiered pricing (e.g., Day 1-5: USD 15/day, Day 6-10: USD 25/day, Day 11+: USD 40/day per TEU) and vary by container type (standard, reefer, special equipment). Calculations must handle weekends and public holidays per entity jurisdiction (Friday-Saturday for Qatar/KSA/UAE, Saturday-Sunday for India). All charges must be computed in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent. The AI engine must proactively alert customers approaching free-day expiry at 2 days and 1 day before.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R005	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Container Release Status Tracking	The system must maintain real-time container release status for all import containers, integrating with customs authorities and port community systems at each port. Release statuses must include: under customs examination, customs cleared, health authority hold, port authority hold, freight hold (collect freight unpaid), BL hold (original BL not surrendered), DO issued, gate-out authorized, and gated-out. Each status change must be timestamped and the customer notified automatically via their preferred communication channel. The AI engine must predict clearance bottlenecks by analyzing historical hold patterns per commodity, port, and customs broker. For containers with multiple holds, all holds must be individually tracked and cleared before gate-out authorization. A consolidated release status dashboard must be available with filters by vessel, BL, container, consignee, and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R006	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Customer Inquiry Management	The system must provide a centralized import inquiry management module where customer service agents can log, track, and resolve import-related queries from consignees, customs brokers, and freight forwarders. Inquiry categories must include cargo status, DO issuance requests, detention/demurrage disputes, documentation discrepancies, customs clearance delays, container damage claims, and general information. Each inquiry must be assigned a unique ticket number, SLA-based response deadline (configurable per customer tier: Premium 2 hours, Standard 4 hours, Economy 8 hours), and responsible agent. The AI engine must auto-suggest responses based on similar historical inquiries and current container status data. Inquiries must support bilingual communication with automatic translation between English and Arabic. Escalation rules must route unresolved inquiries to supervisors after SLA breach.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R007	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Empty Container Redelivery Coordination	The system must track empty container redelivery after import cargo is destuffed, ensuring containers are returned to designated depots within the contracted free detention period. The system must maintain a list of approved empty return locations per port and entity, and validate that the customer returns the container to an authorized depot. The AI engine must predict expected empty return dates based on commodity type, destuffing complexity, and historical customer behavior. Late return alerts must be sent to customers at configurable intervals. The system must capture the empty return condition survey results (clean, damaged, requires wash, requires repair) and automatically generate repair cost debit notes where applicable. For cross-entity operations, the system must handle scenarios where a container imported via one entity's vessel is returned to a depot managed by a different entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R008	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Freight Collect Payment Tracking	For BLs designated as freight collect, the system must track payment collection at the destination before authorizing container release. The system must compute the total collect amount including ocean freight, destination THC (Terminal Handling Charges), DO fees, and any applicable surcharges, displayed in the entity's base currency. Payment must be reconcilable against bank receipts, online payment confirmations, or cash register entries. The AI engine must send payment demand notices upon vessel arrival, with escalating reminders at 3-day intervals. The system must support partial payment allocation where multiple BLs are consolidated. Credit customers with approved terms may have freight collect charges posted to their account without pre-payment, subject to credit limit validation. All transactions must record the exchange rate applied for cross-currency conversions between USD freight and local currency payment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R009	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Container Damage Claims Processing	The system must facilitate the processing of cargo damage claims for import containers, capturing damage survey reports, photographic evidence, and repair/replacement cost estimates. The claim workflow must include initial registration with claim type (cargo damage, container damage, shortage, contamination), assignment to the claims investigation team, carrier liability assessment, and settlement processing. The AI engine must assess claim validity by cross-referencing container condition at loading port (from interchange reports), vessel discharge survey, and consignee's damage report. Claim amounts must be calculated in the applicable currency with limits based on carrier liability per COGSA or Hague-Visby Rules (currently 2 SDR per kilogram or 666.67 SDR per package, whichever is higher). The system must track claim aging, generate reports on claim frequency by trade lane, and identify patterns that may indicate systematic handling issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R010	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Multi-Stop and Transshipment Import Tracking	The system must track import containers that involve transshipment at intermediate ports before arriving at the final discharge port. For each transshipment leg, the system must record the feeder vessel, mother vessel, transshipment port, estimated and actual connection times, and any delays. The AI engine must monitor transshipment connections and proactively alert customer service agents when a container is at risk of missing its connecting vessel, providing alternative routing options. For cargo transshipping through Jebel Ali hub en route to Hamad Port or JNPT, the system must coordinate with both the hub operations team and the feeder service schedule. Container tracking must display the full multi-leg journey with port-by-port milestone updates. The system must handle split BLs where containers from a single BL may travel on different vessels or routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R011	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Customs Broker Portal Integration	The system must provide a dedicated customs broker portal or API allowing authorized customs brokers to access import manifest data, container status, DO availability, and submit clearance documentation electronically. Broker access must be controlled by entity-level permissions, ensuring brokers in Qatar only access Qatar entity data and similarly for UAE, KSA, and India. The portal must support document upload for customs declarations, inspection certificates, and clearance stamps. The AI engine must validate uploaded documents for completeness and flag missing or expired documents. Broker activity logs must record all access, downloads, and submissions for audit compliance. The portal must support Arabic and English interfaces with RTL layout, and must function on mobile devices for brokers performing field inspections at the port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R012	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Charge Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for resolving disputes on import charges including demurrage, detention, THC, and DO fees. Customers or their brokers must be able to submit dispute requests with supporting documentation through the customer portal or via the customer service team. The dispute workflow must include initial assessment by the CS agent, supervisor review for disputes exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 500 equivalent), finance team validation, and final resolution (full waiver, partial waiver, or rejection). The AI engine must evaluate dispute validity by checking container event timelines, port congestion records, and customs hold durations that may justify free-day extensions. Resolution must generate the appropriate credit note or confirmation of charges in the entity's base currency. Dispute resolution SLA must be tracked with aging reports and escalation to management for overdue cases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R013	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import Regulatory Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor and enforce import regulatory compliance requirements specific to each country. For Qatar, the system must validate Qatar General Authority of Customs (QGAC) requirements including import permits for restricted goods, conformity certificates (QS mark), and food safety approvals from the Ministry of Public Health. For UAE, compliance with Federal Customs Authority regulations and Emirates Conformity Assessment Scheme (ECAS) must be validated. For KSA, Saudi Customs (ZATCA) requirements including SABER conformity certificates must be checked. For India, JNPT-specific requirements including ICEGATE EDI filing, PGA (Participating Government Agency) clearances, and FSSAI certificates for food imports must be validated. The AI engine must maintain an updated regulatory requirement matrix and flag non-compliant shipments before vessel arrival.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R003	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Tax Computation Engine for VAT and GST	The system must compute applicable taxes on each invoice line item based on the billing entity's jurisdiction. For UAE entities, 5% VAT must be applied per Federal Tax Authority (FTA) guidelines with a valid Tax Registration Number (TRN). For KSA entities, 15% VAT per ZATCA regulations must be calculated. For Indian entities, GST must be computed at applicable rates (18% for most shipping services, 5% for specific multimodal transport) with proper CGST/SGST/IGST split based on intra-state or inter-state supply determination. Qatar entities must handle zero-rate scenarios. Each tax line must reference the relevant tax code and regulation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R014	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	LCL Import Consolidation Deconsolidation Tracking	The system must handle Less-than-Container-Load (LCL) import shipments where multiple consignees share a single container. The system must track the CFS (Container Freight Station) destuffing process, individual cargo lot identification by House BL number, cargo segregation, and individual consignee notification. Each HBL consignee must receive a separate arrival notice with their specific cargo details and applicable charges. The AI engine must coordinate with the CFS operator to obtain destuffing completion confirmations and individual lot availability notifications. Delivery orders must be issued per HBL, with charges apportioned based on cargo weight or volume (revenue tons). The system must handle scenarios where partial lots are released while others remain under customs examination, and must track each lot independently through clearance and delivery. CFS demurrage charges must be tracked separately from port demurrage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R015	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Import SOB Copy and Document Management	The system must manage the receipt, verification, and storage of all import documents including Shipped-on-Board (SOB) copy of BL, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, insurance certificate, health certificates, phytosanitary certificates, and fumigation certificates. Each document must be indexed by BL number, container number, and consignee, with OCR-powered extraction of key fields by the AI engine. Document storage must comply with entity-specific retention policies (minimum 7 years for Qatar and UAE, 8 years for KSA per ZATCA regulations, 8 years for India per Indian Customs Act). The system must support both digital document workflows and scanning of physical documents received at the counter. Document access must be role-based with entity-level segregation, and document retrieval must support search by BL number, container number, vessel, consignee name, and date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R016	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Vessel Discharge Monitoring and Exception Handling	The system must monitor vessel discharge operations at each port, receiving real-time discharge event data from the terminal operating system (TOS) via EDI (COARRI message) or API integration. The AI engine must reconcile discharged containers against the import manifest and immediately flag exceptions including short-landed containers (on manifest but not discharged), over-landed containers (discharged but not on manifest), containers with mismatched seal numbers, and damaged containers noted during discharge survey. Each exception must generate an automated discrepancy report distributed to the operations team, carrier partner (for transshipment cargo), and relevant customer service agents. For short-landed containers, the system must initiate a cargo tracer to the load port and provide the consignee with an estimated resolution timeline. Discharge progress must be visible on the import dashboard showing percentage completion per vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-002-R017	FEAT-008-1-002	MOD-008	Offline Import Operations and Data Synchronization	The system must support offline import coordination workflows during network outages or connectivity disruptions at port locations. Offline capabilities must include viewing cached import manifests, issuing delivery orders using pre-authorized templates with offline reference numbers prefixed with OFF-DO-, recording container release events, and capturing customer inquiries. The local cache must store the most recent 30 days of import data including manifest details, container statuses, customer accounts, and tariff rates. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline transactions with the central database, resolving conflicts through timestamp-based priority with manual override capability for discrepancies. The AI engine must validate all offline transactions post-sync, flagging any that violate business rules that could not be checked offline (e.g., credit limit exceeded, container already released by another agent). Offline mode must support a maximum disconnection period of 48 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R001	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Container Registration and Validation	The system must maintain a registry of Shipper-Owned Containers (SOC) with comprehensive container details including container number (validated against ISO 6346 including check digit), container type and size (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, etc.), manufacturing year, maximum gross weight, tare weight, container owner details, leasing company information (if applicable), and CSC (Container Safety Convention) plate expiry date. The AI engine must validate CSC expiry against the expected voyage duration and reject containers where CSC expires before the estimated arrival at the discharge port. Registration must capture insurance details including coverage type, policy number, insurer, and validity period. The system must flag containers with expired CSC plates, missing insurance, or structural survey overdue, preventing them from being booked until compliance is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R002	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Export Booking Workflow Differentiation	The system must differentiate SOC export bookings from COC bookings in the booking workflow, applying SOC-specific business rules. SOC bookings must not allocate carrier equipment but must validate that the customer-provided container number is registered in the SOC registry and meets all compliance requirements. Freight rates for SOC shipments must be applied from SOC-specific tariffs which typically exclude equipment rental charges but may include handling differentials. The AI engine must calculate the freight difference between COC and SOC rates and display the savings to the customer service agent. SOC bookings must capture the inland transport arrangement (customer-arranged or carrier-arranged), the empty container origin location, and the container pre-inspection date. VGM responsibility for SOC containers remains with the shipper, and the system must enforce VGM submission before allowing gate-in confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R003	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Container Pre-Inspection Coordination	The system must manage the pre-inspection workflow for SOC containers before they are accepted for loading. The inspection checklist must cover structural integrity (walls, floor, roof, doors), weatherproofing (no light leaks or water ingress), cleanliness (no residue, odor, or contamination), CSC plate validity, container markings legibility, and for reefer units, cooling system functionality test results. Inspections may be performed at the port, an inland depot, or the shipper's premises. The AI engine must schedule inspections based on booking timelines, assign qualified surveyors from the approved surveyor panel, and track inspection status. Inspection results must be recorded digitally with photographs attached to the container record. Containers failing inspection must be flagged with rejection reasons, and the booking must be updated to reflect the need for a replacement container or remediation before the vessel cut-off date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R002	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	Invitation Management	Pending invitations list: email, invited_by, invited_at, expires_at, status. Actions: resend invite, revoke invite. Auto-resend reminder 24h before expiry. Max 100 pending invitations per company. Invitation link is single-use and tied to invited email only.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R004	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Import Release and Redelivery Exemption	For import shipments using SOC containers, the system must recognize that detention charges do not apply since the container belongs to the shipper. The delivery order workflow must be modified to exclude detention clauses while still enforcing demurrage (port storage) charges for the period the container occupies terminal space. The AI engine must automatically identify SOC containers from the import manifest based on the SOC registry match and apply the appropriate charge regime. The system must not send empty container return reminders or track redelivery for SOC units, as the container remains with the owner after cargo destuffing. However, the system must record the gate-out date and destination for customs and port authority reporting. If a container is flagged as SOC but not found in the registry, the system must alert the customer service agent to verify container ownership and potentially reclassify it as COC with standard detention terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R005	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Liability and Insurance Management	The system must manage liability and insurance requirements specific to SOC containers. The carrier's liability for SOC containers is limited to the period when the container is in the carrier's custody (port-to-port or CY-to-CY), unlike COC where carrier liability extends to container damage. The system must record and track the container condition at the time of receipt (gate-in interchange report) and at the time of delivery (gate-out interchange report), with photographic evidence. The AI engine must compare gate-in and gate-out condition reports and automatically flag discrepancies that may indicate damage during transit. Insurance coverage validation must ensure the container owner maintains adequate marine cargo and hull insurance. For SOC containers used across multiple entities, the system must track which entity had custody during any damage event and route claims accordingly. All liability documentation must be stored with entity-specific retention policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R006	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Fleet Analytics and Utilization Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on SOC container movements and utilization across all operating entities. Reports must include SOC container volume trends (export and import), SOC vs COC booking ratios by trade lane, top SOC customers by TEU volume, average SOC container dwell time at port, SOC booking lead times, and revenue impact analysis comparing SOC and COC freight. The AI engine must identify patterns in SOC usage, such as customers gradually shifting from COC to SOC (potential revenue risk) or seasonal SOC volume spikes. Dashboard visualizations must support entity-level filtering (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and aggregated cross-entity views. All monetary values must be displayed in the selected entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent. Reports must be exportable in Excel and PDF formats with bilingual support (English and Arabic) and proper RTL formatting for Arabic exports. Hijri calendar date ranges must be supported as filter parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R007	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Container Interchange Receipt Management	The system must generate and manage Equipment Interchange Receipts (EIR) for SOC containers at each custody transfer point: gate-in at origin port, loading on vessel, discharge from vessel, and gate-out at destination port. Each EIR must record the container condition using standard damage codes (IICL/CEDEX convention), photographic evidence (minimum 4 angles: front, back, left, right, plus any damage close-ups), seal number and condition, and the signatures or digital acknowledgments of both parties. The AI engine must use image recognition to auto-detect and classify container damage from uploaded photographs, suggesting appropriate damage codes. EIR discrepancies between consecutive interchange points must trigger automatic investigation workflows. For multi-entity transshipment routes, the system must ensure seamless EIR handover between entities, maintaining a complete chain of custody documentation accessible to the container owner through the customer portal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R008	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Reefer Container Special Handling	For SOC reefer containers, the system must manage additional operational requirements beyond standard SOC handling. The pre-inspection must include a comprehensive reefer machinery test (PTI) covering compressor performance, thermostat calibration, defrost cycle, controller software version, and data logger functionality. The system must record the reefer unit's power requirement specifications and validate compatibility with the vessel's reefer plug capacity and the port's reefer monitoring infrastructure. The AI engine must verify that the reefer unit's temperature range capability matches the booking's commodity temperature requirements. During transit, the system must integrate with the vessel's reefer monitoring system to receive temperature logs and alert customer service agents if temperature excursions occur. For SOC reefer containers at port, the system must track power connection status, reefer monitoring frequency, and any alarm events, notifying the container owner of any equipment malfunction that could affect cargo integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R009	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Customer Contract Terms Management	The system must manage SOC-specific contractual terms that differ from standard COC agreements. Contract parameters must include SOC freight rate differentials (typically a discount off COC rates), handling charge adjustments, free time allowances at port (which may differ from COC free time), liability caps, insurance requirements, and permitted trade lanes. The AI engine must validate each SOC booking against the customer's contract terms and flag deviations requiring approval. Contract terms must support multi-currency pricing (USD for ocean freight, local currency for port charges) and entity-specific variations. The system must alert customer service agents 60 and 30 days before SOC contract expiry, initiating the renewal workflow. Contract amendments must be version-controlled with full audit trails. For customers operating SOC containers across multiple entities, the system must support master agreements with entity-specific annexures, ensuring consistent pricing logic while accommodating local regulatory differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R010	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Positioning and Repositioning Tracking	The system must track the positioning (moving empty SOC containers to the loading port) and repositioning (returning empty SOC containers after import discharge) of customer-owned containers. The tracking must capture current container location, planned movement legs (truck, rail, barge, or vessel), estimated transit times, and associated logistics costs. The AI engine must optimize repositioning routes by considering available carrier services, inland transport options, and the customer's upcoming booking schedule to minimize empty positioning costs. For customers with SOC containers spread across multiple ports in the network (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), the system must provide a fleet overview map showing container locations and statuses. Repositioning bookings must be distinguishable from revenue cargo bookings in the system and may qualify for special empty repositioning rates. The system must support the customer's own repositioning requests alongside carrier-suggested repositioning to balance equipment supply and demand.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R011	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Regulatory Compliance for Cross-Border Movements	The system must ensure SOC containers comply with regulatory requirements for cross-border movements specific to each operating country. For Qatar, compliance with Qatar General Authority of Customs temporary import/export of container equipment regulations must be validated. For UAE, the system must handle FZAD (Free Zone Authority) requirements for SOC containers entering and exiting free zones at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali. For KSA, ZATCA temporary admission regulations for SOC equipment must be tracked. For India, the system must manage CBIC (Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs) requirements for SOC containers including bond and bank guarantee tracking for temporary equipment imports. The AI engine must maintain a regulatory compliance matrix updated per jurisdiction and automatically validate each SOC movement against applicable regulations, blocking non-compliant transactions and notifying the customer service agent of required remediation steps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R012	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Tank Container Operations Management	The system must support specialized SOC tank container operations for liquid, gas, and powder cargo shipments. Tank container bookings must capture additional parameters including tank capacity (in liters and gallons), maximum allowable working pressure, product compatibility certification, last cargo carried, cleaning certificate status, and heating coil requirements. The AI engine must validate product compatibility against the tank's lining material and previous cargo to prevent contamination, referencing ITCO (International Tank Container Organisation) guidelines. The system must verify that current tank test certificates (2.5-year and 5-year periodic tests per IMDG/ADR regulations) are valid for the voyage duration. For heated cargo requirements, the system must confirm vessel steam or electric heating capability. Cleaning and pre-loading inspection at designated tank cleaning depots must be coordinated through the system, with inspection certificates stored in the container's digital record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R013	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Container Maintenance History Tracking	The system must maintain a comprehensive maintenance and repair history for each registered SOC container. Records must include periodic CSC inspections (every 30 months per ACEP or per-trip), structural repairs with photographic before/after evidence, component replacements (doors, floor boards, roof panels, corner castings), painting and refurbishment, and for reefer units, compressor overhauls, refrigerant recharges, and controller upgrades. The AI engine must predict upcoming maintenance requirements based on container age, usage intensity (number of trips), and historical repair frequency, proactively alerting the container owner and customer service team. Maintenance alerts must be triggered 60 days before CSC expiry to allow scheduling. The system must support maintenance performed at any depot in the network (across all four entities) and consolidate the complete history in a single container profile accessible to authorized users. Maintenance costs must be tracked in the local currency of the depot performing the work.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R014	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Leased Container Lease Agreement Tracking	The system must distinguish between truly shipper-owned containers and leased containers operated as SOC by customers. For leased containers, the system must capture and validate the lease agreement details including lessor name, lease commencement and expiry dates, permitted trade areas, sub-lease permissions, and insurance requirements imposed by the lessor. The AI engine must validate that booked trade lanes fall within the lease's permitted trade areas and flag violations. Lease expiry alerts must be generated 90, 60, and 30 days before the lease end date, as bookings on expired leases may create liability issues. The system must support on-hire and off-hire workflows capturing interchange condition at each event. For containers leased through major leasing companies operating in the Middle East and Indian subcontinent, the system must integrate with lessor portals where available to synchronize lease status and off-hire survey requirements automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R015	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Customer Self-Service Portal	The system must provide a dedicated SOC self-service portal allowing container owners to manage their SOC fleet digitally. Portal capabilities must include SOC container registration and renewal, document upload (CSC certificates, insurance policies, inspection reports), container tracking across all network ports, booking creation for SOC export shipments, delivery order requests for SOC import shipments, EIR viewing and download, maintenance history viewing, and invoice/statement access. The portal must support English and Arabic interfaces with complete RTL rendering for Arabic. The AI engine must provide a dashboard showing the customer's SOC fleet summary: total containers registered, containers currently in transit, containers at port, containers at customer premises, and containers with upcoming compliance deadlines. Monetary values must display in the customer's preferred currency with automatic conversion. The portal must be responsive for mobile and tablet access, enabling field operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R016	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC to COC Conversion Handling	The system must handle scenarios where a customer requests conversion from SOC to COC or vice versa during the booking lifecycle. SOC-to-COC conversion may occur when a customer's SOC container fails pre-inspection and no replacement is available before the vessel cut-off. COC-to-SOC conversion may occur when the customer acquires a container after initially booking with carrier equipment. The conversion workflow must recalculate freight rates, update equipment allocation, adjust liability terms, and regenerate documentation. The AI engine must automatically compute the rate differential and generate a supplementary invoice or credit note in the entity's base currency. The system must maintain an audit trail showing the original booking type, conversion timestamp, authorizing agent, rate adjustments, and reason for conversion. Conversion after vessel loading must be restricted and require operations manager approval, as it affects stowage plans and terminal records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-003-R017	FEAT-008-1-003	MOD-008	SOC Offline Operations and Sync Management	The system must support offline SOC management operations for field agents conducting container inspections, EIR processing, or customer visits at locations with limited connectivity. Offline capabilities must include SOC container detail viewing from the locally cached registry, EIR creation with offline photograph capture and damage code annotation, pre-inspection checklist completion with digital signatures, and SOC booking request initiation. The local cache must store the customer's SOC container fleet data, active bookings, and applicable tariff rates. Offline-created records must be queued with timestamps and synchronized with the central system upon reconnection, with the AI engine validating all offline entries against current system state. Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where the same container was simultaneously processed by different agents offline, using business rule priority (gate-in records take precedence over inspection records). Offline mode must function for a maximum of 48 hours, with clear visual indicators showing the agent they are operating in offline mode.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R001	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Multi-Format Pre-Alert Ingestion	The system must ingest pre-alert cargo notifications from multiple sources and formats including EDI messages (IFTMIN, IFTMCS, COPARN), XML/JSON API feeds from carrier partners, structured email attachments (Excel, CSV, PDF), and manual entry by customer service agents. The AI engine must parse each format and extract standardized data fields including vessel name, voyage number, port of loading, port of discharge, BL number, container number, seal number, consignee name and address, notify party, commodity description, HS code, package count, package type, gross weight, and volume (CBM). For PDF pre-alerts, the AI must use OCR and NLP to extract structured data from unstructured documents with a minimum accuracy of 95%. Each ingested pre-alert must be assigned a unique processing reference number and timestamped with receipt date and time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R002	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Data Validation and Enrichment	The system must validate all ingested pre-alert data against business rules and reference databases. Validation must include container number ISO 6346 check digit verification, vessel name and voyage validation against the vessel schedule database, port code validation against UN/LOCODE registry, HS code validation against the latest harmonized tariff schedules for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and consignee name matching against the customer master database. The AI engine must enrich pre-alert data by auto-linking consignees to existing customer records using fuzzy name matching (configurable threshold, default 85% similarity), standardizing address formats, and flagging high-value or sensitive commodities (e.g., pharmaceuticals, electronics, perishables) for priority handling. Data quality scores must be calculated for each pre-alert based on completeness and accuracy of fields, with low-quality pre-alerts routed to a validation queue for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R003	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Distribution to Internal Stakeholders	Upon successful validation, the system must automatically distribute pre-alert information to relevant internal stakeholders based on configurable distribution rules. Distribution recipients must include the operations team (for vessel planning and terminal coordination), customs clearance team (for pre-clearance filing preparation), documentation team (for DO preparation), customer service team (for customer notification), and the finance team (for freight collect receivables forecasting). Distribution rules must be configurable per entity and port, with filtering based on cargo type (DG cargo alerts to DG operations team, reefer cargo alerts to reefer monitoring team, SOC containers to SOC management team). The AI engine must prioritize distribution of pre-alerts for time-sensitive cargo such as perishables, live animals, and pharmaceuticals. Distribution channels must include system dashboard notifications, email alerts, and integration with internal workflow management tools. Each distribution must be logged for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R004	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Customer Pre-Arrival Notification Generation	The system must generate and dispatch pre-arrival notifications to consignees and their authorized customs brokers upon receipt and validation of pre-alerts. Notifications must include expected vessel name and voyage, estimated time of arrival (ETA) at discharge port, BL number, number and type of containers, cargo description summary, and a checklist of documents required for customs clearance at the specific discharge port. For Hamad Port imports, the checklist must include Qatar customs requirements (Customs Declaration, Certificate of Origin, Health Certificate for food items). For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, UAE customs requirements must apply. The notification must be generated in both English and Arabic based on the consignee's language preference. The AI engine must personalize notifications based on the consignee's historical clearance patterns, highlighting any new regulatory requirements that differ from their previous imports. Notifications must include Hijri date equivalents and monetary estimates in the entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R005	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Matching with Booking and Manifest Data	The system must automatically match incoming pre-alerts against existing export bookings at the origin port and import manifests filed with port authorities. Matching must be performed using BL number, container number, and vessel/voyage combination as primary keys, with fuzzy matching on consignee name and cargo description as secondary verification. The AI engine must categorize match results as exact match (all fields align), partial match (key fields align but discrepancies exist in secondary fields), or no match (new cargo not previously booked or manifested). Discrepancies between pre-alert and booking data (e.g., different container count, weight variance exceeding 10%, changed consignee) must be flagged for investigation. The matching process must handle consolidated pre-alerts containing multiple BLs and deconsolidated pre-alerts where one BL spans multiple messages. Match results must be stored and visible in the booking and import modules for cross-reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R006	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Hazardous Cargo Pre-Alert Priority Processing	Pre-alerts containing hazardous cargo declarations must be identified and processed with priority handling. The system must extract DG-specific information including IMDG class, UN number, proper shipping name, packing group, flashpoint, EmS schedule, marine pollutant status, and limited/excepted quantity indicators. The AI engine must validate DG declarations against the port-specific DG acceptance matrix for each discharge port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT have different DG acceptance policies). Pre-alerts for Class 1 (explosives), Class 6.2 (infectious substances), and Class 7 (radioactive materials) must trigger immediate notification to the port authority liaison team for advance approval coordination. DG pre-alerts must be cross-referenced with the vessel's DG manifest to ensure segregation compliance per IMDG code. The system must generate a consolidated DG pre-alert summary report per vessel per port, distributed to terminal safety officers and emergency response teams at least 48 hours before vessel arrival.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R007	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Amendment and Update Processing	The system must handle amendments to previously received pre-alerts, maintaining version history and tracking all changes. Amendments may include changes to vessel/voyage (cargo rolled to a different vessel), container number changes, weight corrections, consignee changes, and cargo description updates. Each amendment must be timestamped and linked to the original pre-alert reference. The AI engine must assess the impact of amendments on downstream processes: a vessel change requires re-running the distribution workflow, a consignee change requires new customer notification, a cargo description change may require re-validation of customs classification. Amendment notifications must be sent to all stakeholders who received the original pre-alert, clearly highlighting what changed using a track-changes format. The system must enforce a cut-off rule where amendments received after the vessel has berthed at the discharge port are treated as post-arrival corrections subject to a different approval workflow involving the operations manager and customs compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R008	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Dashboard and Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time pre-alert monitoring dashboard displaying processing status across all entities and ports. Dashboard KPIs must include total pre-alerts received per vessel, processing status breakdown (pending validation, validated, distributed, matched, discrepant), average processing time from receipt to distribution, data quality score distribution, and pre-alerts pending manual review. The dashboard must support filters by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), vessel, carrier partner, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), and cargo type. The AI engine must highlight anomalies such as vessels with unusually low pre-alert coverage (percentage of manifested containers with pre-alerts received), sudden spikes in discrepant pre-alerts from specific partners, and overdue pre-alerts for vessels arriving within 48 hours. Dashboard data must be exportable to Excel and PDF in both English and Arabic with RTL support. Refresh interval must be configurable with default of 5 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R009	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Carrier Partner Pre-Alert SLA Tracking	The system must track and enforce pre-alert submission SLAs agreed with each carrier partner and NVOCC. SLA parameters must include the required submission lead time before vessel arrival (configurable per partner, default 72 hours), minimum data completeness requirements (mandatory fields that must be populated), data accuracy thresholds, and amendment frequency limits. The AI engine must monitor compliance in real-time and generate SLA scorecards per partner on a monthly basis, including metrics such as on-time submission rate, average submission lead time, data completeness rate, amendment frequency, and error rate by field. SLA breaches must trigger automated notifications to the partner relationship manager and the carrier partner's designated contact. Chronic SLA non-compliance (three consecutive months below threshold) must escalate to senior management with a recommended action plan. SLA performance data must feed into the partner evaluation process and may influence commercial negotiations. All SLA tracking must account for the multi-currency commercial implications of late pre-alerts causing delayed customs clearance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R010	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Customs Pre-Filing Data Preparation	The system must prepare customs pre-filing data packages from validated pre-alerts for submission to customs authorities at each discharge port. For Qatar (QGAC), the system must format data per Qatar Customs electronic filing requirements including tariff classification, declared value in QAR, and origin country. For UAE (FCA/Dubai Customs), data must be formatted for Mirsal 2 system submission at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port. For KSA (ZATCA), data must comply with FASAH platform requirements. For India (CBIC), data must be formatted for ICEGATE system import general manifest (IGM) filing requirements at JNPT. The AI engine must auto-classify commodities using HS codes based on the cargo description, suggest duty rates, and flag commodities requiring special import permits or PGA (Participating Government Agency) clearances. Pre-filing packages must be generated per customs jurisdiction with entity-specific formatting, and submission status must be tracked with acknowledgment receipts stored in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R011	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Email Parsing with AI Natural Language Processing	The system must process pre-alerts received via email using AI-powered natural language processing to extract structured cargo data from unstructured email body text, attached PDFs, Excel files, and scanned documents. The AI engine must identify and extract key fields including BL number patterns, container number patterns (four letter prefix plus seven digits), vessel and voyage references, port names and codes, commodity descriptions, weight and measurement figures, and party names with roles (shipper, consignee, notify). The NLP model must support both English and Arabic language emails, handling mixed-language content common in Middle East shipping communications. Extraction confidence scores must be assigned to each field, and fields with confidence below 90% must be highlighted for human verification. The system must learn from manual corrections to improve extraction accuracy over time. Email threading must be supported to link amendment emails to original pre-alert emails, and automated acknowledgment replies must be sent to the sender confirming receipt and any data quality issues detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R012	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert for Transshipment Cargo Coordination	The system must handle pre-alerts for transshipment cargo that arrives at a hub port (typically Jebel Ali) for onward connection to a feeder vessel serving the final discharge port (Hamad Port, JNPT, or KSA ports). Transshipment pre-alerts must capture both the mother vessel details and the planned feeder vessel details, with the AI engine validating connection viability based on vessel schedules and minimum connection times at the transshipment hub. The system must generate separate pre-alert distributions for the hub port operations team (for discharge and re-loading planning) and the final discharge port team (for customer notification and customs preparation). If the pre-alert indicates a tight connection or the mother vessel ETA suggests a potential missed connection, the AI must proactively identify alternative feeder services and alert both operations and customer service teams. Transshipment pre-alerts must be linked so that status updates on the first leg automatically flow through to the second leg tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R013	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Duplicate Detection and Consolidation	The system must detect and handle duplicate pre-alerts received from multiple sources for the same shipment. Duplicates may arise when both the carrier and the freight forwarder send pre-alerts for the same BL, or when the same partner sends the pre-alert via both EDI and email. The AI engine must identify potential duplicates using a composite matching algorithm that considers BL number, container number, vessel/voyage, and cargo details, assigning a duplicate probability score. Exact duplicates (100% match on all key fields) must be automatically merged, retaining the most complete dataset with the earliest receipt timestamp. Probable duplicates (matching on BL and vessel but differing on secondary fields) must be flagged for manual review with a side-by-side comparison view. The system must maintain source attribution for merged records, recording which partner provided which data points. Duplicate detection must operate cross-entity to catch pre-alerts for the same shipment received by different entity offices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R002	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Multi-Entity DO Numbering and Jurisdictional Compliance	The system must maintain separate DO numbering sequences per legal entity across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. Each entity must have configurable prefix formats, fiscal year reset rules, and compliance fields specific to local customs regulations. For Qatar operations under Hamad Port, DOs must include QP Customs declaration references. For UAE operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, DOs must reference Dubai Trade or ADPC portal submission IDs. For KSA, FASAH customs platform references are required. For India JNPT operations, the DO must include IGM number, line number, and sub-line number as mandated by Indian Customs ICEGATE integration requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R014	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Archival and Retrieval	The system must archive all pre-alert records including original source files (EDI messages, emails, attachments), parsed data, validation results, distribution logs, and amendment history. Archival must comply with entity-specific data retention policies: minimum 7 years for Qatar and UAE entities, 8 years for KSA entity per ZATCA regulations, and 8 years for India entity per Indian Customs Act requirements. The AI engine must index archived pre-alerts for efficient retrieval using search criteria including BL number, container number, vessel name, consignee, carrier partner, port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), commodity HS code, and processing status. Archived records must be retrievable within 5 seconds for records less than 2 years old and within 30 seconds for older records. The system must support legal hold functionality to prevent automatic purging of records under litigation or regulatory investigation. Storage must be encrypted at rest with entity-level data segregation ensuring compliance with Qatar data sovereignty requirements and India's data localization regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R015	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Integration with Terminal Planning	The system must feed validated pre-alert data to the terminal planning module and port community systems for discharge planning optimization. Integration data must include expected container count by type (standard, reefer, DG, OOG, SOC), estimated total weight for crane planning, reefer plug requirements, DG segregation requirements, and OOG handling equipment needs. The AI engine must generate a vessel discharge profile summary from pre-alerts, comparing it against the vessel's BAPLIE (Bay Plan) data to validate completeness and flag discrepancies. For Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, discharge planning data must be transmitted via the port community system API at least 48 hours before vessel arrival. For JNPT, integration must comply with the Indian port community system protocols. The system must track whether pre-alert-based discharge planning differs significantly from the final manifest, generating accuracy reports that feed back into the carrier partner SLA scoring. Terminal resource allocation suggestions must be generated by the AI based on pre-alert cargo composition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R016	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Perishable Cargo Pre-Alert Fast-Track Processing	The system must implement fast-track processing for pre-alerts containing perishable cargo to ensure all clearance preparations are completed before vessel arrival. Perishable commodities must be identified by HS code classification and commodity description keywords mapped to a configurable perishable goods database. Fast-track processing must trigger parallel workflows including health authority notification (Qatar Ministry of Public Health, UAE Municipality, FSSAI for India, SFDA for KSA), cold chain facility reservation at the port, priority customs pre-filing, and consignee notification with specific document requirements (phytosanitary certificate, health certificate, temperature logger data requirements). The AI engine must calculate the estimated shelf life remaining based on commodity type, origin, transit time, and expected clearance duration, alerting if the projected clearance time may consume more than 30% of remaining shelf life. Pre-alerts for perishable cargo must be processed within 2 hours of receipt, compared to the standard 8-hour processing SLA for general cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R017	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Offline Pre-Alert Processing Queue	The system must support offline pre-alert processing capabilities during system outages or network connectivity disruptions. The offline module must cache incoming pre-alerts from automated feeds (EDI gateway, API endpoints) in a local message queue with guaranteed delivery. Customer service agents must be able to manually enter pre-alert data using offline forms that cache locally and synchronize upon reconnection. The offline queue must maintain message ordering to ensure pre-alerts are processed in receipt order after connectivity is restored. The AI engine must perform basic validation on cached pre-alerts using locally stored reference data (vessel schedules, customer database, port codes) and flag records requiring full online validation. Upon reconnection, the system must process the queued pre-alerts in batch mode with priority given to vessels arriving within 72 hours. The offline processing status must be visible on the dashboard, showing queue depth, estimated processing time to clear the backlog, and any pre-alerts that could not be processed due to missing reference data. Maximum supported offline queue capacity must be 10,000 pre-alert records or 72 hours of accumulated data, whichever is reached first.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-1-004-R018	FEAT-008-1-004	MOD-008	Pre-Alert Compliance Screening for Sanctions and Embargoes	The system must screen all pre-alert party names (shipper, consignee, notify party) and cargo descriptions against international sanctions lists and trade embargo databases. Screening must cover UN Security Council Consolidated Sanctions List, US OFAC SDN List, EU Consolidated Sanctions List, UK HMT Sanctions List, and country-specific lists for Qatar (Qatar Financial Information Unit), UAE (Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation), KSA (Presidency of State Security), and India (MEA designated entities). The AI engine must perform fuzzy name matching with configurable sensitivity thresholds to detect aliases, transliteration variants (especially between Arabic and English names), and partial name matches. Positive matches must immediately freeze the pre-alert processing and trigger a compliance review workflow involving the entity's compliance officer. The system must generate a screening audit report for each pre-alert, recording all names screened, lists checked, match results, and disposition. Screening must be repeated when pre-alerts are amended with changed party names. False positive management must allow compliance officers to whitelist verified entities to reduce recurring false matches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R001	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Submission Portal with Multi-Channel Intake	The system must provide a unified Shipping Instructions submission portal supporting web form entry, EDI IFTMIN/IFTMBF message parsing, email-to-SI extraction via AI-powered OCR, and direct API integration. The portal must accept SI submissions in both English and Arabic with full RTL layout support for Arabic text fields including shipper name, consignee address, notify party details, and cargo descriptions. The system must auto-detect the submission language and apply appropriate Unicode normalization for Arabic diacritics. Each SI must be linked to a valid booking reference and validated against booking details for container count, type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF), weight limits, and port pair consistency across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R003	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	Company Profile Settings	Editable fields: logo (PNG/SVG max 2MB), trade name (Arabic + English), commercial registration with expiry alert, VAT number, default currency, default timezone, fiscal year start month, decimal precision for amounts. Primary language setting. Changes tracked in audit log.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R002	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	AI-Powered SI Auto-Population from Booking Data	The system must leverage AI to auto-populate Shipping Instructions fields from the associated booking confirmation, including shipper details, consignee information, notify party, port of loading (POL), port of discharge (POD), final destination, container details, commodity codes (HS codes), and package descriptions. The AI engine must learn from historical SI submissions per customer to predict cargo descriptions, marks and numbers patterns, and declared weight ranges. Auto-populated fields must be highlighted with confidence scores, and fields below 85% confidence must be flagged for manual review. The system must support auto-population for multi-entity bookings across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India legal entities with appropriate entity-specific defaults for documentation language, regulatory stamps, and local agent details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R003	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Validation Rules Engine for Regulatory Compliance	The system must enforce a configurable rules engine that validates each SI against carrier-specific requirements, port authority regulations, and customs documentation standards. Validation rules must include mandatory field checks for HS code format per destination country, dangerous goods declarations (IMO class, UN number, packing group) for DG cargo, fumigation and phytosanitary certificate references for agricultural shipments, and weight verification against SOLAS VGM requirements. The engine must apply country-specific rules: Qatar customs require Arabic commodity descriptions, UAE Federal Customs Authority mandates origin certificates for certain HS codes, KSA SABER certification references for regulated products, and India DGFT IEC codes for all export shipments. Each validation failure must generate a specific error code with remediation guidance in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R004	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Deadline Tracking and Cut-Off Management	The system must track SI submission deadlines relative to vessel cut-off dates configured per carrier, trade lane, and port. SI cut-off times must be maintained separately from cargo cut-off and VGM cut-off, with configurable lead times typically ranging from 24 to 72 hours before vessel departure. The system must send automated escalation notifications at configurable intervals (e.g., 48 hours, 24 hours, 12 hours, 6 hours before deadline) via email, SMS, and in-app push notifications in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic). Late SI submissions must trigger an exception workflow requiring carrier approval with associated late-filing surcharges tracked in the appropriate currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port, INR for JNPT). The system must support Hijri calendar date display alongside Gregorian dates for all deadline timestamps in KSA and Qatar contexts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R005	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Multi-Container SI Grouping and Split Logic	The system must support grouping multiple containers under a single SI for consolidated shipments and splitting a single booking across multiple SIs for part-shipment scenarios. When grouping, the system must validate that all containers share the same shipper, consignee, and notify party or flag discrepancies for review. Split SI logic must ensure that the total declared weight and package count across split SIs does not exceed the original booking allocation. Each split SI must maintain traceability to the parent booking with proportional weight distribution validated against individual container VGM declarations. The system must handle mixed container types within a single SI (e.g., 2x40HC + 1x20GP) and calculate total CBM and weight aggregates automatically. For multi-entity operations, split SIs may cross entity boundaries requiring inter-company documentation linkage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R006	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Shipper-Owned Container (SOC) SI Handling	The system must differentiate between Carrier-Owned Container (COC) and Shipper-Owned Container (SOC) Shipping Instructions with SOC-specific validation rules. SOC SIs must capture container ownership details, insurance certificate references, structural inspection dates compliant with CSC (Container Safety Convention) plate validity, and return depot instructions at the destination port. The system must not apply carrier demurrage or detention charges to SOC containers but must track port storage charges applicable at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT per their respective tariff schedules. SOC SIs must include additional fields for container ISO type code verification against the physical unit, last inspection survey report reference, and any special handling instructions. The AI engine must flag SOC containers approaching CSC plate expiry within 90 days of the intended vessel arrival date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R007	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Dangerous Goods SI Compliance Checks	The system must enforce IMDG Code compliance for all Shipping Instructions declaring dangerous goods cargo. DG SI fields must include UN number, proper shipping name, IMO hazard class and division, packing group (I/II/III), flash point, EmS schedule, marine pollutant status, and limited/excepted quantity indicators. The system must validate segregation rules between multiple DG containers on the same SI and cross-validate against other SIs on the same vessel voyage for incompatible hazard class stowage. Port-specific DG restrictions must be enforced: Hamad Port restrictions on Class 1 explosives, Jebel Ali DG storage zone capacity limits, JNPT AERB clearance requirements for Class 7 radioactive materials. AI must auto-classify DG declarations from commodity descriptions and flag potential undeclared dangerous goods based on HS code analysis and historical shipment patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R008	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Document Attachment and Supporting Papers	The system must allow attachment of supporting documents to each SI submission including commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, fumigation certificate, phytosanitary certificate, dangerous goods declaration form, letter of credit copy, and any trade-lane-specific documentation. Each attachment must be scanned by AI-powered OCR to extract key data points (invoice value, currency, HS codes, weights) and cross-validated against SI declared values. Discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., weight variance > 5%, value variance > 10%) must trigger automatic holds. The system must support document formats including PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, and Excel with maximum file size of 25MB per attachment. Arabic-language documents must be OCR-processed with Arabic character recognition models achieving minimum 95% accuracy. All attachments must be stored with entity-specific access controls per multi-entity data segregation policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R002	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Voyage Number Generation and Management	The system must auto-generate unique voyage numbers following a configurable pattern that includes the service loop code, direction indicator (eastbound/westbound/northbound/southbound), and sequential numbering per calendar year. Voyage numbers must be unique across the entire multi-entity structure spanning Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The system must support both internal voyage references and carrier-specific voyage references for third-party vessel operators. Voyage numbers must be immutable once a booking has been confirmed against them. The AI engine should suggest voyage number patterns based on historical naming conventions per trade lane and carrier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R009	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Status Workflow and Lifecycle Tracking	The system must manage SI through a defined workflow: Draft, Submitted, Under Review, Validated, Approved, Sent to Carrier, Carrier Confirmed, Amendment Requested, and Cancelled. Each status transition must be timestamped with the user identity, entity context, and reason code. The system must enforce business rules per transition: SIs cannot move to Approved without passing all validation rules, carrier confirmation requires EDI APERAK acknowledgment or manual carrier response entry, and cancellation after carrier confirmation requires a cancellation fee assessment. The dashboard must display SI status distribution per vessel, per customer, and per port with drill-down capability. Real-time status updates must be pushed to customer portal accounts and external systems via webhooks. The workflow must support parallel review paths where compliance team and documentation team can review simultaneously with merge-point approval logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R010	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Customer SI Templates and Preference Management	The system must allow customers to create and maintain reusable SI templates for recurring shipment patterns such as regular commodity flows between specific port pairs. Templates must store default values for shipper details, consignee information, notify party, commodity descriptions, HS codes, marks and numbers patterns, and special instructions. The AI engine must analyze customer submission history and proactively suggest template creation when it detects three or more SIs with greater than 80% field similarity within a rolling 90-day window. Templates must support entity-specific variations where a customer operating across Qatar and UAE entities may have different default notify parties, documentation languages, or regulatory certificate references. Template management must include version control with audit trail, sharing permissions within customer organizational hierarchies, and bulk application to multiple bookings. The system must render template names and descriptions in both English and Arabic based on user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R011	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	VGM Integration within SI Processing	The system must integrate Verified Gross Mass (VGM) declarations within the SI processing workflow as mandated by SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2. VGM values must be captured per container with the weighing method (Method 1: weighing the packed container, Method 2: weighing all contents and adding tare weight), authorized signatory name, weighbridge certificate reference, and weighing date. The system must validate that VGM values fall within acceptable tolerance ranges for the container type (e.g., 40HC maximum gross weight 30,480 kg) and flag containers where declared SI weight deviates from VGM by more than 5%. VGM submission deadlines must be tracked independently from SI deadlines with port-specific requirements: Jebel Ali requires VGM at least 24 hours before vessel arrival, JNPT requires VGM before gate-in. The system must transmit VGM data to terminal operators via VERMAS EDI messages and maintain a full audit trail of all VGM declarations and amendments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R012	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Freight and Charges Declaration on SI	The system must capture freight payment terms on each SI including Prepaid, Collect, or Third-Party billing with the designated payer entity. Freight charges must be displayed in the booking currency with real-time conversion display in the local currency of the port of loading (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali/Khalifa, SAR for KSA ports, INR for JNPT) using daily treasury exchange rates. The SI must capture INCOTERMS 2020 designations (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP, etc.) with the named place of delivery, and the system must validate freight payment term consistency with the declared INCOTERM (e.g., CIF shipments typically show freight prepaid). Additional charges such as THC, documentation fees, seal charges, and VGM filing fees must be itemized on the SI with applicable tax calculations (Qatar: no VAT currently, UAE: 5% VAT, KSA: 15% VAT, India: 18% GST). The system must flag any freight rate discrepancies between the SI declaration and the contracted tariff rate card for the customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R013	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI EDI Transmission to Carriers	The system must transmit approved Shipping Instructions to ocean carriers via EDIFACT IFTMIN (Instruction Message) or carrier-specific API integrations. The EDI module must support SMDG code lists for port codes, package type codes (UN/ECE Recommendation 21), and container size/type codes (ISO 6346). Outbound SI EDI messages must be queued, transmitted, and acknowledged with full message-level tracking including send timestamp, carrier receipt acknowledgment (APERAK/CONTRL), and error responses. Failed transmissions must trigger automatic retry logic with exponential backoff (retry at 5, 15, 45 minutes) and escalation to the documentation team after three failures. The system must maintain carrier-specific EDI profiles defining message versions, communication protocols (AS2, SFTP, API), and field mapping configurations. For carriers not supporting EDI, the system must generate formatted PDF SI submissions and transmit via automated email with delivery confirmation tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R014	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	Offline SI Capture and Synchronization	The system must support offline SI capture for field agents and remote warehouse locations with intermittent internet connectivity, particularly relevant for inland container depots feeding into Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The offline module must cache the most recent customer master data, booking details, and validation rules locally on the device. SIs created offline must be stored in a local encrypted database with conflict detection and resolution logic upon reconnection. When network connectivity is restored, offline SIs must synchronize automatically with the central system, applying full validation rules and flagging any conflicts where booking data has changed during the offline period. The synchronization protocol must handle partial uploads, resume interrupted transfers, and maintain data integrity through checksums. Offline mode must support both English and Arabic data entry with local spell-check and field validation for mandatory fields without requiring server-side validation calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R015	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Analytics and Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive SI analytics dashboard displaying key performance indicators including average SI processing time from submission to carrier confirmation, SI rejection rate by reason code, late SI submission percentage relative to cut-off deadlines, and AI auto-population accuracy rates. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port of loading, customer segment, carrier, and date range with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors. Trend analysis must show week-over-week and month-over-month comparisons with AI-generated insights identifying process bottlenecks and recommending workflow optimizations. Customer-specific SI scorecards must track individual shipper compliance rates, average amendment frequency, and documentation quality scores. The dashboard must export reports in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) and support scheduled automated report distribution to configurable recipient lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-001-R016	FEAT-008-2-001	MOD-008	SI Duplicate Detection and Merge Handling	The system must implement AI-powered duplicate SI detection that identifies potential duplicate submissions based on matching criteria including booking reference, shipper-consignee pair, container numbers, and cargo description similarity scores. When a potential duplicate is detected with a confidence score exceeding 90%, the system must block the submission and present a side-by-side comparison view highlighting differences between the existing and new SI. For confirmed duplicates, the system must offer merge functionality allowing the user to select the preferred value for each conflicting field from either submission. The duplicate detection engine must account for minor variations in party name spellings (including Arabic transliteration differences), address formatting, and commodity description wording. Detection must operate across entity boundaries to catch scenarios where the same physical shipment is inadvertently documented by different entity offices. All duplicate resolutions must be logged with the resolution method and authorizing user for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R001	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	AI-Powered B/L Draft Generation from Approved SI	The system must automatically generate a Bill of Lading draft upon SI approval using AI-driven document composition. The B/L draft must populate all mandatory fields from the approved SI including shipper, consignee, notify party, vessel name and voyage number, port of loading, port of discharge, place of receipt, place of delivery, container and seal numbers, cargo description, gross weight, measurement (CBM), and marks and numbers. The AI engine must format cargo descriptions per carrier-specific B/L layout requirements, apply proper CAPS conventions for party names, and structure multi-line address fields according to destination country postal standards. The generated draft must be rendered in the carrier's B/L template format with the issuing entity's letterhead (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India entity) and comply with ISS (BIMCO) or carrier-proprietary B/L formats. Arabic text fields must be rendered with proper bidirectional text handling when mixed English-Arabic content appears on the same B/L.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R002	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Type Classification and Configuration	The system must support multiple Bill of Lading types with type-specific validation rules and issuance workflows. Supported types must include Master Bill of Lading (MBL), House Bill of Lading (HBL), Seaway Bill (non-negotiable), Combined Transport B/L, Through B/L for multimodal shipments, and Switch B/L for intermediary trade. Each B/L type must have configurable field requirements: negotiable B/Ls (MBL/HBL) require original count specification (typically 3/3), Seaway Bills require named consignee (no to-order), and Switch B/Ls require original B/L surrender verification before re-issuance. The system must enforce that HBLs issued by NVOCC customers reference the corresponding MBL number for cargo traceability. B/L type selection must consider the INCOTERMS declared on the SI and flag inconsistencies such as FOB shipments with freight collect B/Ls issued by the shipper's entity. Multi-entity configurations must define which entities are authorized to issue each B/L type per their freight forwarding or NVOCC licensing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R003	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Letter of Credit B/L Compliance Validation	The system must validate B/L drafts against Letter of Credit (L/C) requirements when the payment method is documentary credit. L/C compliance checks must verify that the B/L shows the exact beneficiary name as shipper, the L/C-specified consignee or to-order notation, the correct notify party, on-board notation with specific shipped-on-board date, clean B/L status without claused remarks, and freight payment terms matching L/C stipulations (prepaid vs collect). The system must check for UCP 600 Article 20 compliance including port names matching L/C terms (not just port codes), latest shipment date adherence, transshipment allowance/prohibition verification, and full set of originals issuance. AI must flag potential discrepancies with specific UCP 600 article references and suggest corrective actions. The L/C reference number, issuing bank, and expiry date must be linked to the B/L for document presentation deadline tracking. Currency amounts on the B/L must match L/C values with tolerance checks per banking standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R004	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Multi-Party B/L Approval Workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-party approval workflow for B/L drafts supporting sequential and parallel approval chains. The approval chain must include documentation officer review, compliance team verification, customer draft approval, and final carrier confirmation. Each approver must be able to approve, reject with comments, or request amendments with specific field-level change requests. Customer approval must be obtained through a secure external portal link sent via email with a time-limited access token valid for a configurable period (default 48 hours). The system must track approval timestamps, approver identity with role, entity affiliation, and any conditions attached to the approval. Escalation rules must auto-reassign pending approvals when the assigned approver is unavailable for more than the configured SLA period. The workflow must support delegation where senior staff can approve on behalf of absent team members with full audit trail. Arabic and English notification templates must be used based on each approver's language preference setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R005	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Draft Versioning and Comparison	The system must maintain full version history of every B/L draft iteration from initial generation through final approval. Each version must capture a complete snapshot of all B/L fields, the modification timestamp, modifying user, entity context, and change reason categorization (customer request, compliance correction, carrier requirement, data error). A visual diff comparison tool must highlight changes between any two selected versions with field-level change tracking displayed in a side-by-side or inline format. The comparison view must handle Arabic text comparison with proper RTL rendering and highlight character-level changes in Arabic party names and cargo descriptions. The system must limit the maximum number of draft iterations per configurable policy (e.g., maximum 10 revisions before management escalation) to prevent excessive rework cycles. Version history must be exportable as a PDF audit report for trade compliance documentation and dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R006	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Amendment and Cancellation Processing	The system must support amendments to issued Delivery Orders for changes in consignee name, clearing agent, container substitution, or delivery location, provided the cargo has not yet been physically released from the terminal gate. Each amendment must generate a new version number while retaining the original DO number, creating a complete version history accessible via audit trail. Cancellation of a DO must require manager-level approval and must automatically trigger reversal of any associated billing entries, update the container status back to hold, and notify the port terminal system of the cancellation. The system must prevent amendments to DOs that have already been partially or fully executed at the terminal gate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R006	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Freight Manifest Data Extraction from B/L	The system must automatically extract and compile freight manifest data from approved B/Ls for submission to port authorities and customs systems. The manifest compilation must aggregate B/L-level data per vessel voyage including total container count by type, total gross weight, total package count, commodity summaries by HS chapter, and shipper-consignee pair listings. Port-specific manifest formats must be supported: Qatar Customs ASYCUDA format for Hamad Port, Dubai Trade mirsal2 integration for Jebel Ali, Saudi Customs FASAH system format for KSA ports, and Indian Customs ICEGATE EDI for JNPT. The system must validate manifest data completeness per port authority requirements and flag missing or inconsistent B/L data that would cause manifest filing rejections. Manifest generation must handle B/L amendments received after initial manifest filing by generating supplementary manifest updates. Currency conversion for declared cargo values must use the exchange rate effective on the B/L date as published by the respective central bank.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R007	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Original B/L Print and Issuance Control	The system must control the printing and issuance of original negotiable Bills of Lading with strict security controls to prevent unauthorized or duplicate issuance. Original B/L printing must be restricted to authorized personnel per entity with role-based access control and require dual authorization for print jobs. The system must track the number of originals printed (standard 3/3 full set), assign unique serial numbers with tamper-evident features, and maintain a print log with printer identification, timestamp, and authorizing officer. Void and reprint scenarios must follow a controlled workflow requiring the previous set to be physically surrendered and marked void before a replacement set is issued with new serial numbers. The system must support secure courier dispatch tracking for original B/Ls sent to consignees or banks, recording courier company, tracking number, dispatch date, and delivery confirmation. For entities operating under UAE, Qatar, KSA, or India jurisdictions, the system must comply with local commercial law requirements for negotiable document issuance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R008	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Express Release and Telex Release Processing	The system must support express release (telex release) processing as an alternative to physical original B/L surrender. The telex release workflow must require the shipper to submit a signed letter of indemnity or formal telex release request, which the system must validate against the shipper's pre-approved indemnity limit. Upon approval, the system must transmit a telex release notification to the destination agent or carrier office via EDIFACT BAPLIE or carrier-specific messaging, marking the B/L as released in the system to prevent any subsequent original B/L issuance. The system must track telex release status with confirmation from the destination office and update the cargo release status at the discharge port. For high-value shipments exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 100,000 equivalent in QAR/AED/SAR/INR), telex release must require additional management approval. The system must maintain a telex release register per entity with running sequence numbers and support audit queries by date range, customer, and vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R009	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Seaway Bill Automatic Release Configuration	The system must support Seaway Bill (non-negotiable) issuance with automatic cargo release configuration at the destination port. Unlike negotiable B/Ls, Seaway Bills must automatically authorize cargo release to the named consignee without requiring document surrender. The system must validate that Seaway Bill consignee fields contain a specific named party (not to-order or to-bearer designations which are only valid for negotiable instruments). The system must interface with destination port terminal systems to pre-authorize cargo release upon vessel arrival by transmitting release instructions via EDI COPARN or carrier API. Customer eligibility for Seaway Bill issuance must be governed by credit assessment rules: only customers with approved credit terms or prepaid freight status may use Seaway Bills. The system must clearly distinguish Seaway Bills from negotiable B/Ls in all reports, dashboards, and document archives with visual indicators and filtered views. Seaway Bill numbers must follow a separate numbering series from negotiable B/L numbers per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R010	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Clause and Remarks Management	The system must manage standard and custom clauses that appear on the Bill of Lading including carrier standard terms, special cargo remarks, clean/claused B/L notations, and regulatory stamps. A clause library must maintain pre-approved clause templates categorized by type: on-board notation, shipped-on-board date, said-to-contain declarations, shipper's load-stow-count clauses, temperature setting for reefer containers, and dangerous goods notations per IMDG Code. Custom clauses requested by customers or required by specific trade lanes must go through a compliance approval workflow before addition to the B/L. The system must flag claused B/Ls that may cause Letter of Credit discrepancies per UCP 600 Article 27 (clean transport document requirements). Clause text must support bilingual rendering where regulations require Arabic text (e.g., KSA customs requirements for Arabic cargo descriptions). The AI engine must suggest appropriate clauses based on cargo type, trade lane, and historical B/L patterns for the customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R011	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Container and Seal Number Verification on B/L	The system must verify container numbers and seal numbers on B/L drafts against gate-in records, terminal operating system data, and VGM declarations. Container number format must be validated per ISO 6346 including the owner code, equipment category, serial number, and check digit algorithm. Seal numbers must be cross-referenced against the seal issuance log to confirm the seal was assigned to the specific container and has not been reported as tampered or replaced. Discrepancies between B/L container/seal numbers and terminal records must block B/L approval until resolved through a discrepancy investigation workflow. For SOC containers, the system must verify the container number against the customer's registered SOC fleet list. The system must support multiple seal types on the B/L including carrier seal, customs seal, and shipper seal with distinct identification. When container numbers change due to container substitution at the terminal, the system must auto-update the B/L draft and re-trigger the approval workflow with change notification to all stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R012	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Compliance Hold and Customs Integration	The system must automatically place a compliance hold on any Delivery Order where the consignee or notify party matches entries on OFAC, UN sanctions, or local watchlists maintained by Qatar Financial Information Unit, UAE FIU, SAMA, or Indian FIU. Held DOs must be routed to the compliance officer queue and must not be transmittable to port systems until cleared. The system must integrate with customs clearance status feeds to prevent DO issuance for shipments with pending customs examinations, incomplete duty payments, or unresolved customs queries. Release of compliance-held DOs must require two-factor authentication and senior compliance officer approval with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R012	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Number Generation and Uniqueness Control	The system must generate unique B/L numbers following configurable numbering schemes per entity, carrier, and B/L type. The numbering format must encode the issuing entity (QA for Qatar, AE for UAE, SA for KSA, IN for India), the year, a trade lane identifier, and a sequential number with check character. The system must guarantee global uniqueness of B/L numbers across all entities and prevent duplicate issuance through database-level constraints and distributed sequence management. Reserved B/L number ranges must be supported for specific carriers or trade lanes requiring pre-allocated number blocks. The system must handle B/L number format requirements for carrier systems that impose specific prefix or length constraints. Voided B/L numbers must be permanently retired and never reused, with a void register maintaining the reason, voiding authority, and associated replacement B/L number if applicable. The numbering scheme must support annual rollover without gaps and maintain a continuous audit trail of all numbers issued, voided, or reserved per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R013	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L PDF Generation with Digital Signatures	The system must generate high-fidelity PDF renderings of B/L drafts and final approved B/Ls with digital signature capability. PDF generation must support carrier-specific B/L templates with proper logo placement, watermarking for draft copies (DRAFT overlay), and security features for originals including QR code verification links. Digital signatures must comply with local e-signature regulations: Qatar MOTC electronic signature standards, UAE Federal Law on Electronic Transactions, KSA CITC digital signature requirements, and India IT Act 2000 digital signature provisions. The PDF must embed machine-readable metadata including B/L number, vessel details, and container numbers for automated processing by banking and customs systems. Arabic text rendering in PDF must use embedded fonts supporting full Arabic character set with proper ligatures, kashida justification, and diacritical marks. The system must generate both individual B/L PDFs and consolidated PDF packages for multi-B/L shipments with a cover index page.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R014	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Surrender and Release Tracking at Destination	The system must track the full lifecycle of negotiable B/L originals from issuance through surrender at the destination port for cargo release authorization. The tracking workflow must capture original B/L courier dispatch, banking channel transit (for L/C shipments), consignee receipt, presentation to the destination agent, verification of endorsement chain for to-order B/Ls, and final surrender confirmation. The system must validate the endorsement chain: blank endorsement allows any holder to claim, specific endorsement requires the named endorsee to present, and restrictive endorsement limits further negotiability. Upon confirmed surrender of the complete set of originals, the system must automatically generate a delivery order authorization transmitted to the destination port terminal. For shipments arriving before documents (common at Jebel Ali and JNPT due to short transit times), the system must support bank guarantee or letter of indemnity processing as an alternative to original B/L surrender, with the guarantee value tracked in the appropriate local currency and linked to the B/L for eventual reconciliation upon original document receipt.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R015	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Data Transmission to Customs and Trade Platforms	The system must transmit B/L data to customs authorities and national trade platforms as required by each jurisdiction. Integration must support Qatar Single Window (Al Nadeeb) for Hamad Port, Dubai Trade portal and mirsal2 for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, Saudi FASAH platform for KSA ports, and Indian ICEGATE system for JNPT. Each integration must map B/L fields to the platform-specific data schema, handle platform-specific code lists and reference data, and process acknowledgment or rejection responses. The system must support advance cargo declaration timelines: 24-hour advance manifest rule for imports, pre-departure filing requirements for exports, and any enhanced advance data requirements for specific trade lanes (e.g., US ISF 10+2 for re-export cargo). Failed transmissions must trigger automated retry with alerting to the documentation team. The system must maintain transmission logs per B/L per platform with message content, response codes, and processing timestamps for regulatory audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R016	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	B/L Amendment Post-Approval Fee Assessment	The system must assess and track amendment fees for B/L changes requested after the B/L has been approved and issued. Fee schedules must be configurable per carrier, amendment type, and timing (pre-vessel departure vs post-departure vs post-arrival). Common amendment categories with distinct fee structures must include shipper/consignee name change, destination change, freight term change, cargo description change, and container addition or removal. The system must calculate fees in the billing currency of the customer's contract with automatic conversion display in local port currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using applicable exchange rates. Amendment fees must be linked to the corresponding invoice or debit note with appropriate tax treatment per jurisdiction (UAE VAT at 5%, KSA VAT at 15%, India GST at 18%, Qatar currently zero-rated). The system must enforce collection of amendment fees before processing the amendment or allow deferred billing for customers with approved credit terms. Fee waiver authority levels must be defined per entity with configurable approval thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-002-R017	FEAT-008-2-002	MOD-008	Switch B/L Processing for Intermediary Trade	The system must support Switch Bill of Lading processing for intermediary trade transactions where the original B/L is surrendered and a new B/L is issued with different shipper and/or consignee details. The switch B/L workflow must verify complete surrender of all originals from the first B/L set before issuing the replacement set. The system must maintain the linkage between original and switched B/L for cargo traceability while restricting visibility of the original shipper details from the new consignee per trade confidentiality requirements. Switch B/L processing must handle scenarios where the cargo description, marks and numbers, or port of discharge changes in addition to party details, with each change requiring separate validation against carrier and customs requirements. The system must calculate switch B/L fees per the applicable tariff schedule in the transaction currency. Multi-entity switch operations where the original B/L is issued by one entity (e.g., UAE) and the switch B/L by another (e.g., Qatar) must be supported with inter-entity accounting entries and proper documentary audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-002-1-001-R002	FEAT-002-1-001	MOD-002	Namespace Isolation per Module	Create dedicated Kubernetes namespaces for each ERP module (booking, documentation, billing, vessel-ops, etc.) with resource quotas and network policies enforcing module-level isolation	must_have	infra-monitoring-agent	Review namespace resource quota allocations during capacity planning	["Each ERP module runs in its own namespace with defined resource quotas (CPU, memory limits)", "Network policies restrict cross-namespace traffic to only declared service dependencies", "Namespace-level RBAC prevents unauthorized module cross-access", "Resource quota alerts fire at 80% utilization threshold"]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R001	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Request Submission and Classification	The system must provide a structured amendment request interface allowing customers and internal documentation staff to submit SI change requests with field-level specificity. Each amendment request must identify the original SI reference, the specific fields to be changed with current and proposed values displayed side by side, the business reason for the amendment selected from a configurable reason code list (customer instruction, regulatory requirement, carrier request, data correction, commercial change), and supporting documentation if applicable. The system must classify amendments by severity: minor amendments (marks and numbers, package count correction) that can be auto-approved within configurable tolerances, moderate amendments (party name spelling corrections, additional notify party) requiring single-level approval, and major amendments (consignee change, destination change, freight term change) requiring multi-level approval with compliance review. Amendment requests must support bilingual submission in English and Arabic with all changed field values captured in both languages where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R002	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	AI-Powered Amendment Impact Analysis	The system must perform automated impact analysis for each SI amendment request, identifying all downstream documents and processes affected by the proposed change. The AI engine must trace dependencies from the SI to Bill of Lading drafts, approved B/Ls, customs manifest filings, carrier EDI transmissions, VGM declarations, freight invoices, and port gate-in records. For each affected document, the system must assess the amendment feasibility considering the current document status (e.g., B/L already printed and dispatched, manifest already filed with customs, vessel already departed). The impact analysis must calculate estimated amendment costs including carrier amendment fees, customs re-filing charges, and potential demurrage or detention implications if the amendment delays cargo processing. The analysis must be presented in a clear dashboard view showing affected documents, their current status, required actions, estimated costs in the transaction currency with local currency equivalents (QAR/AED/SAR/INR), and a recommended action plan. The AI must flag high-risk amendments that could trigger regulatory penalties or documentary credit discrepancies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R003	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Approval Workflow with Authority Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable approval authority matrix for SI amendments based on amendment severity, timing relative to vessel departure, financial impact, and entity jurisdiction. The authority matrix must define approval levels per entity: for Qatar operations, amendments after customs filing require customs broker counter-signature; for UAE operations, amendments to Jebel Ali shipments after vessel loading require terminal operator acknowledgment; for India operations, amendments to JNPT export shipments after LEO (Let Export Order) require customs officer re-validation. The approval workflow must support sequential and parallel approval paths with configurable time-based escalation rules. Each approver must be presented with the impact analysis summary, cost implications, and compliance risk assessment. Approvals can be conditional (approved subject to carrier confirmation), and the system must track and enforce such conditions before executing the amendment. Rejection of an amendment must require a documented reason and trigger notification to the requestor with suggested alternatives. The workflow must handle cross-entity amendments where the issuing entity and approving entity differ.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R004	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Cascading Amendment Propagation to B/L	Upon SI amendment approval, the system must automatically propagate the changes to all linked Bill of Lading drafts that have not yet been finalized. For B/Ls in draft or under-review status, the system must apply the SI changes directly and re-trigger the B/L validation rules. For B/Ls that have been approved but not yet printed, the system must create a new B/L draft version incorporating the amendment and route it through an expedited approval workflow. For B/Ls that have been printed and issued as originals, the system must generate a B/L amendment request to the carrier with full documentation of the changes required and track the carrier's response. The propagation engine must handle partial amendments where only some fields on the SI affect the B/L (e.g., internal reference changes on SI do not propagate to B/L). Each propagation action must be logged with before and after values, propagation timestamp, and the triggering amendment reference. The system must prevent conflicting amendments where multiple pending amendments to the same SI-B/L chain would create inconsistent data, requiring sequential resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R005	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Carrier Amendment Notification and Tracking	The system must transmit SI amendments to ocean carriers via the appropriate communication channel per carrier profile: EDIFACT IFTMIN amendment messages, carrier web portal updates via API integration, or formatted email notifications for carriers without electronic integration. Each carrier notification must include the original SI reference, amendment details with before and after values, effective date, and any special instructions. The system must track carrier acknowledgment of the amendment with response categorization: accepted as-is, accepted with conditions, partially accepted (some changes accepted, others rejected), or fully rejected. Carrier response timelines must be monitored against configurable SLA periods (default 24 hours for pre-departure amendments, 48 hours for post-departure). Unacknowledged amendments approaching SLA expiry must trigger automated follow-up messages and escalation to the carrier relations team. The system must handle scenarios where the carrier imposes additional charges for the amendment, capturing the charge amount in the carrier's billing currency and linking it to the amendment record for subsequent invoice reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R006	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment to Customs and Regulatory Filings	The system must manage the cascade of SI amendments to customs and regulatory filings across all operating jurisdictions. When an amendment affects customs-declared data fields (HS code, cargo description, weight, consignee, origin), the system must assess whether a customs amendment filing is required per jurisdiction-specific rules. For Qatar Customs (Hamad Port), amendments to manifested cargo require supplementary manifest filing through Al Nadeeb. For Dubai Customs (Jebel Ali), amendments post-manifest filing require mirsal2 amendment submission with potential examination escalation. For Saudi Customs, FASAH system amendments require broker authorization and may trigger additional inspection. For Indian Customs (JNPT), post-LEO amendments require customs officer approval through ICEGATE with potential shipping bill amendment fees. The system must prepare jurisdiction-specific amendment documentation, track regulatory authority responses, and flag any amendments that would trigger cargo examination or hold. Amendment fees charged by customs authorities must be captured in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and passed through to the customer or absorbed per commercial agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R007	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Fee Calculation and Billing	The system must calculate amendment fees based on configurable fee schedules that consider the amendment type, timing, carrier charges, and regulatory filing costs. Fee components must include internal documentation processing fees, carrier amendment charges (passed through at cost or with markup per commercial policy), customs re-filing fees, and any terminal-related charges. The fee calculation engine must apply the correct currency per the customer's billing agreement: export customers typically billed in USD or the POL local currency, import customers in the POD local currency. Multi-currency fee aggregation must convert all component charges to the billing currency using the applicable exchange rate (booking date rate, amendment date rate, or contracted rate per agreement). The system must generate a fee quotation for customer acceptance before processing the amendment for major amendments, while minor amendment fees may be auto-billed per standing agreement. Fee waivers must be supported with configurable authority levels: documentation supervisor may waive up to USD 100 equivalent, operations manager up to USD 500, and country manager unlimited. All fee calculations must include applicable taxes per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R008	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Deadline and Cut-Off Enforcement	The system must enforce amendment deadlines relative to vessel operations milestones and carrier cut-off rules. Configurable amendment windows must define the latest time amendments can be accepted: free amendment period (typically until SI cut-off), paid amendment period (from SI cut-off to cargo cut-off), restricted amendment period (from cargo cut-off to vessel departure, requiring carrier special approval), and post-departure amendment period (after vessel sailing, limited to non-operational fields). Each amendment window must have associated fee tiers and approval requirements that escalate as the vessel departure approaches. The system must display the current amendment window status prominently on the amendment request screen with countdown timers showing time remaining until the next restriction level. For multi-port rotations where the vessel calls at both Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, amendment deadlines must be port-specific based on the cargo loading port. The system must handle timezone differences between ports (Qatar AST UTC+3, UAE GST UTC+4, India IST UTC+5:30) when calculating deadline compliance. Late amendment requests outside all windows must trigger an exception process with senior management approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R009	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Bulk Amendment Processing for Vessel-Level Changes	The system must support bulk amendment processing for changes affecting multiple SIs on the same vessel voyage, such as vessel schedule changes, voyage number updates, or port rotation modifications. When a vessel-level change is received from the carrier, the system must identify all affected SIs and B/Ls, generate a batch amendment proposal, and route it through an expedited approval workflow. The bulk amendment interface must display all affected documents with the proposed changes and allow reviewers to approve the batch or exclude specific documents that require individual handling. Bulk amendments must propagate to all downstream documents (B/Ls, manifests, customs filings) as a coordinated update to maintain data consistency across the document chain. The system must handle partial batch failures where some document amendments succeed while others fail, providing clear status tracking per document within the batch. Bulk amendment operations must be atomic per document chain (SI-B/L-manifest) to prevent inconsistent states. Performance requirements must ensure bulk amendments for up to 500 SIs per vessel complete within 30 minutes including all propagation and notification actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R010	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every SI amendment capturing the complete lifecycle from request through execution and all cascading effects. The audit record must include the original and amended values for every changed field, the requesting user and their organizational role, the business justification, all approval decisions with approver comments, carrier notifications and responses, customs filing updates and authority responses, fee assessments and payment status, and all system-generated actions with timestamps. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident, stored separately from the operational data to ensure forensic integrity. Compliance reports must be generated on demand or scheduled, supporting filters by entity, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), amendment type, customer, vessel, and port. Reports must include amendment frequency analysis per customer to identify customers requiring process improvement interventions, amendment reason distribution to identify systemic issues, and average amendment processing time per entity to benchmark operational efficiency. All reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R011	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Conflict Detection and Resolution	The system must detect and prevent conflicting amendments when multiple amendment requests are pending for the same SI or related document chain simultaneously. Conflict detection rules must identify field-level conflicts where two amendments propose different values for the same field, logical conflicts where one amendment invalidates another (e.g., one changes consignee while another changes the notify party who was the original consignee), and sequence conflicts where amendment order affects the final outcome. When conflicts are detected, the system must lock the document chain to prevent further amendment submissions, present a conflict resolution interface showing all pending amendments with their conflict points, and require an authorized user to determine the resolution order or merge the amendments. The conflict resolution must support three modes: sequential application in user-specified order, merged application where non-conflicting fields from both amendments are combined, and supersede where one amendment replaces another entirely. All conflict resolutions must be documented in the audit trail with the resolution rationale and authorizing user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R007	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Multi-Currency Charge Validation on DO	Before issuing a Delivery Order, the system must validate all outstanding charges against the B/L in the applicable transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR). The validation must check freight charges, terminal handling charges (THC), demurrage, detention, and any applicable surcharges such as BAF, CAF, or peak season surcharges. The system must support split payments where charges are partially settled in one currency and the balance in another, using the exchange rate locked at the time of invoice generation. Currency conversion must reference the daily exchange rate feed from the central treasury module, and any rate variance exceeding 2% from the previous day must trigger a supervisory alert before DO release.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R004	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	Module Activation Dashboard	Grid view of all 60 CS ERP modules: icon, name, status (active/inactive/coming_soon), subscription requirement. One-click activate/deactivate per module within subscription tier. Show dependency warnings. Module activation triggers provisioning job.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R012	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Customer Self-Service Amendment Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing self-service portal for submitting and tracking SI amendment requests. The portal must display the customer's active SIs with current status, allow amendment request submission with a guided field-by-field change interface, and show real-time amendment processing status with estimated completion timelines. The portal must enforce customer-level amendment permissions: regular users may request amendments, while designated customer administrators may approve amendment fee quotations and acknowledge amendment completions. The portal interface must be fully bilingual (English and Arabic) with user-selectable language preference and RTL layout for Arabic mode. The portal must display all monetary values in the customer's preferred currency with conversion tooltips showing equivalent amounts in other operating currencies. Amendment request validation in the portal must provide immediate feedback on mandatory fields, format requirements, and preliminary compliance checks before formal submission to the operations team. The portal must support mobile-responsive design for customers accessing from tablets and smartphones, particularly relevant for customer representatives at port locations such as Hamad Port and Jebel Ali container yards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R013	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Automated Amendment for Vessel Schedule Changes	The system must automatically process amendments triggered by carrier vessel schedule changes including delays, port omissions, transshipment route changes, and vessel substitutions. When a vessel schedule change notification is received via carrier EDI (IFTMIN/IFTSTA) or manual entry, the system must identify all affected SIs and B/Ls, assess the impact on each document per the change type, and initiate automated amendments for operational fields (vessel name, voyage number, ETA/ETD dates) without requiring customer approval. The system must distinguish between schedule changes that require customer notification only (minor delay, same vessel) and those requiring customer consent (vessel substitution, additional transshipment, port omission affecting cargo). For changes that require customer consent, the system must generate notifications in the customer's preferred language with clear impact descriptions and obtain acknowledgment through the self-service portal or email response. The system must recalculate all deadline-driven processes (SI cut-offs, B/L deadlines, customs filing windows) based on the revised schedule and update all countdown timers and notifications accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R014	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Rollback and Reversal Processing	The system must support amendment rollback capability allowing authorized users to reverse a previously executed amendment and restore the document to its pre-amendment state. Rollback eligibility must be assessed automatically: amendments that have already been transmitted to customs authorities and acknowledged cannot be rolled back through the standard process but require a new forward amendment. The rollback process must reverse all cascading changes made by the original amendment across B/Ls, manifests, and carrier notifications, effectively executing a counter-amendment. The system must handle partial rollbacks where only some fields of a multi-field amendment need to be reversed while others remain in effect. Rollback requests must follow the same approval workflow as the original amendment severity level and must include documentation of why the rollback is necessary. Financial implications of rollback must be assessed including any amendment fees already paid (which may or may not be refundable per carrier policy), additional carrier charges for the reversal amendment, and customs re-filing costs. The system must generate a consolidated rollback audit entry linking the original amendment, the rollback action, and all affected documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R015	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment KPI Tracking and Process Optimization	The system must track key performance indicators for the amendment management process to enable continuous improvement and identify operational inefficiencies. KPIs must include average amendment processing time from request to completion segmented by amendment severity, first-time approval rate indicating the percentage of amendments approved without rework, amendment rejection rate by reason code, carrier response time for amendment acknowledgments, customer self-service adoption rate measuring the percentage of amendments submitted via portal versus email or phone, and amendment cost per transaction averaged across all fee components. The system must provide trend analysis dashboards showing KPI evolution over configurable time periods with entity-specific views for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. AI-powered analytics must identify patterns in amendment activity such as customers with consistently high amendment rates, specific trade lanes or carriers with longer processing times, and peak amendment periods relative to vessel schedules. The system must generate automated recommendations for process improvements, such as suggesting SI template updates for customers with recurring amendments to the same fields or proposing deadline adjustment for trade lanes with systematic late amendments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-003-R016	FEAT-008-2-003	MOD-008	Amendment Integration with Financial Accounting	The system must integrate amendment-related financial transactions with the core accounting module across all operating entities. Amendment fees assessed to customers must automatically generate debit notes or supplementary invoices in the appropriate entity's books with correct revenue recognition per the entity's accounting standards (IFRS for Qatar/UAE/KSA, Indian AS for India). Carrier amendment charges received must create payable entries linked to the specific amendment record for three-way matching during carrier invoice reconciliation. The system must handle amendment fee refunds when carriers reverse previously charged fees, generating credit notes against the original debit. Multi-currency amendment transactions must be recorded at the transaction date exchange rate with month-end revaluation entries for outstanding balances. Inter-entity amendment charges (where one entity performs documentation services for another entity's customer) must generate inter-company accounting entries with proper transfer pricing treatment. Tax implications must be calculated per jurisdiction: amendment service fees are subject to VAT/GST per the standard rates in UAE (5%), KSA (15%), and India (18%), while Qatar-booked amendment fees follow Qatar's current tax framework. The system must support amendment fee accrual for amendments spanning accounting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R008	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Electronic DO Transmission to Port Systems	The system must electronically transmit approved Delivery Orders to the respective port terminal operating systems via EDI or API integration. For Hamad Port, integration must use the QTerminals Navis N4 API. For Jebel Ali, integration must interface with DP World's CargoES platform. For Khalifa Port, integration must connect to Abu Dhabi Ports ADPC system. For JNPT, integration must transmit via the Port Community System (PCS1x) using Indian Customs-approved message formats. Each transmission must receive an acknowledgment from the port system, and any rejection must be immediately flagged to the operations team with the specific rejection reason code. Failed transmissions must be queued for automatic retry with exponential backoff up to 3 attempts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R001	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Multi-Channel Case Intake and Auto-Classification	The system must support case creation from multiple customer communication channels including email, phone (via CTI integration with call logging), web portal submission, WhatsApp Business API, live chat, and walk-in counter registration at port offices. The AI classification engine must automatically categorize incoming cases by type (inquiry, complaint, dispute, service request, claim), subject area (booking, documentation, B/L, container tracking, invoicing, customs, equipment), urgency level (critical, high, medium, low), and affected entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Natural language processing must analyze the case content in both English and Arabic to extract key identifiers such as booking references, B/L numbers, container numbers, vessel names, and invoice numbers, automatically linking the case to the relevant operational records. The system must handle multi-language intake where a customer may submit a case mixing English and Arabic text, properly classifying and routing regardless of language. Auto-classification accuracy must exceed 90% as validated against manual review, with misclassified cases feeding back into the AI training pipeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R002	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	AI-Powered Case Routing and Assignment	The system must implement intelligent case routing that assigns cases to the most appropriate agent or team based on case classification, required expertise, agent workload, language proficiency, entity jurisdiction, and historical resolution performance. Routing rules must consider entity-specific team structures: Qatar operations team for Hamad Port shipments, UAE team split between Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, KSA team for Saudi port operations, and India team for JNPT operations. Language-based routing must assign Arabic-primary cases to Arabic-fluent agents and allow bilingual agents to handle both language queues. The system must support skill-based routing where specialized case types (DG cargo issues, L/C discrepancies, customs disputes) are routed to agents with certified expertise in those areas. Load balancing must distribute cases evenly across available agents while respecting maximum concurrent case limits per agent. Escalation routing must automatically re-route cases that breach SLA warning thresholds to senior agents or supervisors. The routing engine must handle cross-entity cases where a customer issue spans multiple jurisdictions by assigning a primary owner and secondary collaborators from relevant entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R003	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case SLA Definition and Monitoring	The system must enforce configurable Service Level Agreements for case resolution based on case type, priority, customer segment, and entity. SLA definitions must include first response time (time from case creation to first agent communication), resolution time (time from case creation to case closure), and intermediate milestone SLAs (e.g., investigation completion, proposed resolution communication). Default SLA targets must be configurable per entity with typical values: critical cases 4-hour first response and 24-hour resolution, high priority 8-hour first response and 48-hour resolution, medium priority 24-hour first response and 5-business-day resolution, and low priority 48-hour first response and 10-business-day resolution. SLA clocks must account for business hours per entity timezone (Qatar UTC+3, UAE UTC+4, KSA UTC+3, India UTC+5:30) and exclude local public holidays including Islamic holidays calculated from the Hijri calendar. SLA breach notifications must escalate to the team supervisor at 75% elapsed time, department manager at 100% elapsed time, and country director at 150% elapsed time. VIP customer accounts must have accelerated SLA tiers with dedicated case queues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R004	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case Investigation and Root Cause Linking	The system must provide investigation tools allowing agents to trace case issues to their root cause across all operational modules. The investigation workspace must display a consolidated timeline of all events related to the customer's shipment including booking confirmations, SI submissions, B/L issuance, container movements, customs clearance milestones, invoice generation, and payment receipts. Agents must be able to query data from booking, documentation, container tracking, billing, and customs modules directly from the case interface without navigating to separate systems. The AI assistant must suggest potential root causes based on the case description and linked operational data: for example, if a customer reports a missing container, the AI should check container tracking records, gate-in/out logs at the relevant port (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT), and terminal bay plan data to locate the unit. Root cause codes must be selected from a standardized taxonomy covering operational errors, system failures, carrier delays, customs holds, documentation errors, and customer-side issues. Each case must link to specific root cause records for pattern analysis and systemic issue identification across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R005	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Customer Sentiment Analysis and Churn Risk Detection	The system must perform real-time sentiment analysis on all customer communications within a case using natural language processing for both English and Arabic text. Sentiment must be classified on a five-point scale from very negative to very positive with granular emotion detection including frustration, urgency, satisfaction, and confusion. Arabic sentiment analysis must account for regional dialect variations common in Gulf Arabic (Qatar, UAE, KSA) versus formal Modern Standard Arabic used in official correspondence. The system must aggregate sentiment scores across all cases for a customer to generate a customer health score reflecting overall satisfaction levels. Churn risk detection must analyze patterns including increasing case frequency, deteriorating sentiment trends, unresolved case backlogs, and changes in booking volumes to flag accounts at risk of attrition. High churn-risk accounts must trigger proactive retention workflows including priority case handling, management outreach scheduling, and commercial review for potential service improvements or pricing adjustments. Sentiment dashboards must display entity-level and aggregate trends with the ability to drill down to individual customer and case level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R009	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Status Tracking and Real-Time Updates	The system must maintain real-time DO status tracking with the following statuses: Draft, Pending Approval, Approved, Transmitted, Acknowledged by Port, Partially Executed, Fully Executed, Expired, Cancelled, and Amended. Status transitions must be event-driven, updating automatically when port systems confirm gate-out events, partial container pickups, or delivery completions. The customer portal must display the current DO status with estimated remaining pickup time based on terminal operating hours and current congestion levels. Each status change must generate a timestamped audit entry and optionally trigger webhook notifications to registered customer systems for real-time supply chain visibility integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R005	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	System Configuration	Configurable settings per company: email notification preferences (digest vs real-time), default approval thresholds per module, currency display format, date format, AI agent confidence threshold (when to escalate to human), webhook endpoints for external integrations.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R006	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case Communication Management and Templates	The system must manage all case-related communications through a unified thread maintaining the complete conversation history across all channels (email, portal, chat, phone notes). Communication templates must be maintained in a template library organized by case type, subject area, and language (English and Arabic) with variable placeholders for dynamic data insertion (customer name, booking reference, B/L number, vessel details, port names, dates). AI must suggest appropriate response templates based on case classification and conversation context, pre-populating variable fields from linked operational records. Agents must be able to edit AI-suggested responses before sending, with all edits tracked for AI learning improvement. Outbound communications must be sent via the customer's preferred channel with automatic formatting per channel requirements (email supports HTML formatting, WhatsApp supports limited markdown, SMS is plain text with character limits). All communications must support bilingual content where the agent may respond in English with an Arabic translation appended or vice versa. The system must enforce quality controls including supervisor review for high-priority cases, profanity filtering, and data privacy checks preventing accidental disclosure of other customers' information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R007	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Dispute and Claims Management Workflow	The system must support formal dispute and claims management for cargo damage claims, overcharge disputes, demurrage and detention disputes, service failure claims, and documentary credit discrepancy disputes. Each claim type must have a structured workflow with required evidence collection steps: cargo damage claims require survey reports, photographs, commercial invoice, and packing list; overcharge disputes require comparative rate documentation and contract references; demurrage disputes require container movement timestamps from terminal operators. The system must calculate claim amounts in the original transaction currency and display equivalents in all operating currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Claim assessment must follow a defined authority matrix where agents can approve settlements up to a configurable limit per entity (e.g., USD 1,000 for Qatar agent, AED 5,000 for UAE agent), with higher amounts requiring management approval through escalating authority tiers. The system must track claim aging, generate provision reports for financial accounting per IFRS requirements, and support claim subrogation to carriers or third parties with linked counter-claim tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R008	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Knowledge Base Integration for Case Resolution	The system must integrate with an internal knowledge base that provides agents with relevant resolution guides, SOPs, regulatory references, and historical resolution patterns for similar cases. The knowledge base must be organized by topic area (documentation, customs, operations, billing, equipment) and tagged by entity applicability (Qatar-specific, UAE-specific, KSA-specific, India-specific, or global). AI-powered search must allow agents to query the knowledge base using natural language in English or Arabic and receive ranked relevant articles with highlighted matching sections. The system must automatically suggest knowledge base articles based on the case classification and content, presenting the top three most relevant articles in the agent workspace. Knowledge base articles must support versioning with effective dates to ensure agents reference current procedures, particularly for regulatory topics where customs rules change frequently. The system must track knowledge base article usage per case and identify gaps where agents searched but found no relevant content, triggering content creation requests to subject matter experts. Article effectiveness must be measured by tracking whether cases where a specific article was referenced achieved faster resolution times or higher customer satisfaction scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R009	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case Escalation Matrix and Management Oversight	The system must implement a multi-tier escalation matrix governing when and how cases are escalated based on SLA breach risk, customer importance, financial impact, and issue complexity. Tier 1 escalation must route to the team lead when SLA warning threshold is reached or when the agent marks the case as requiring expertise beyond their authorization level. Tier 2 escalation must route to the department manager for cases involving potential financial liability exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., QAR 50,000 for Qatar, AED 50,000 for UAE, SAR 50,000 for KSA, INR 1,000,000 for India). Tier 3 escalation must route to the country director for cases involving regulatory risk, legal threats, or strategic account impact. Cross-entity escalation must be supported where a case originating in one entity requires decision authority from another entity's management. The escalation interface must present a comprehensive case briefing including all investigation findings, communications history, customer sentiment trajectory, and proposed resolution options with financial impact analysis. De-escalation paths must also be defined allowing cases to be returned to lower tiers once the escalation trigger has been addressed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R010	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Proactive Case Generation from Operational Alerts	The system must automatically generate proactive customer cases when operational events are detected that will impact customer shipments. Triggering events must include vessel delay exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., 12 hours), port congestion alerts at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT affecting berthing schedules, customs hold notifications on customer cargo, container damage reports from terminal operators, reefer temperature deviation alerts for temperature-controlled shipments, and carrier booking rollover notifications. Each proactive case must be pre-populated with affected customer details, shipment references, the nature of the operational disruption, estimated impact on the customer's supply chain timeline, and a suggested customer communication drafted by the AI. Proactive cases must be prioritized based on the customer's service tier and the severity of the operational impact. The system must batch-create cases when a single operational event affects multiple customers (e.g., vessel delay affecting 50+ customers) and allow bulk communication with individualized details per customer. Proactive case metrics must track customer acknowledgment rates and feedback to measure the effectiveness of proactive communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R010	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Bulk DO Processing for Full Vessel Discharge	The system must support bulk generation of Delivery Orders for an entire vessel discharge list, allowing operations staff to process hundreds of DOs simultaneously for a single vessel call. The bulk processing engine must validate each B/L individually, flag exceptions for manual handling, and auto-approve DOs that meet all criteria without human intervention per the 95% AI automation target. Bulk processing must include progress tracking showing completed, pending, and failed DOs with drill-down capability to view specific failure reasons. The system must handle vessel discharge lists containing up to 5,000 B/L entries without performance degradation, completing bulk validation within 15 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R011	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Customer Feedback and Satisfaction Surveys	The system must automatically trigger customer satisfaction surveys upon case closure, collecting feedback on the resolution experience. Survey delivery must use the customer's preferred communication channel (email, portal notification, WhatsApp) and be available in both English and Arabic with appropriate RTL formatting. The survey must capture a numerical satisfaction rating (1-5 scale), resolution effectiveness assessment, agent professionalism rating, and optional free-text feedback. Survey responses must be linked to the case record and aggregated to calculate Customer Satisfaction Score (CSAT) per agent, per team, per entity, and overall organization. Net Promoter Score (NPS) surveys must be triggered at configurable intervals (e.g., quarterly) to measure overall customer loyalty beyond case-specific satisfaction. The system must analyze free-text feedback using NLP to extract themes and sentiments, identifying recurring issues or commendations. Low satisfaction scores (1-2) must automatically trigger a follow-up case assigned to a supervisor for recovery action. Survey analytics must be presented in dashboards with trend analysis, entity comparisons, and correlation analysis between case attributes (type, priority, resolution time) and satisfaction outcomes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R012	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case Reporting and Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide comprehensive case management analytics through interactive dashboards accessible to agents, supervisors, and management. Standard reports must include case volume by type and priority, SLA compliance rates, average resolution time, first-contact resolution rate, agent productivity metrics (cases handled, average handling time, resolution rate), and customer satisfaction scores. Dashboards must support multi-entity views with entity-specific filtering (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and cross-entity aggregate views. Time-based analysis must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods with year-over-year comparison accounting for Islamic calendar month variations. Trend analysis must highlight seasonal patterns in case volumes correlated with shipping peak seasons (Q4 peak season, Ramadan period effects on Middle East operations, Indian festival season logistics surges). AI-powered insights must identify emerging issue patterns before they become widespread, such as increasing documentation complaints for a specific trade lane indicating a systemic process failure. All reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers and data labels. Scheduled report distribution must support automated email delivery to configured stakeholder lists per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R013	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Inter-Entity Case Collaboration and Handoff	The system must support seamless case collaboration between entities when a customer issue spans multiple jurisdictions or requires expertise from a different entity's team. Collaboration mechanisms must include case sharing (read-only visibility to collaborating entity), case co-ownership (dual entity assignment with defined responsibilities), and case transfer (full handoff from one entity to another). Data visibility during collaboration must respect entity-level data segregation policies: the collaborating entity must only see case-relevant information, not the customer's complete transaction history with the originating entity unless explicitly authorized. Collaboration workflows must handle timezone differences between entities (Qatar UTC+3, UAE UTC+4, KSA UTC+3, India UTC+5:30) by providing follow-the-sun coverage where cases can be actively progressed across entity working hours. Communication between collaborating agents must use internal case notes (not visible to customers) with tagging and notification features. Handoff protocols must ensure no information is lost during transfer with a mandatory handoff summary including case context, investigation status, pending actions, and customer expectations documented by the transferring agent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R014	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Automated Case Resolution for Common Inquiries	The system must implement AI-driven automated case resolution for common, straightforward customer inquiries that do not require human judgment. Auto-resolvable case types must include shipment status inquiries (resolved by fetching current container tracking data and generating a status summary), B/L copy requests (resolved by attaching the B/L PDF from the document archive), invoice copy requests (resolved by attaching the invoice PDF), booking confirmation requests (resolved by forwarding the booking confirmation details), and schedule inquiries (resolved by querying the vessel schedule database). The AI must compose the response in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic) using approved response templates with dynamic data insertion. Auto-resolved cases must be flagged in the system as AI-resolved for quality monitoring, and a configurable percentage (e.g., 10%) must be randomly sampled for human review to validate resolution quality. Customer responses to auto-resolved cases indicating dissatisfaction or requesting further assistance must automatically reopen the case and route it to a human agent. Auto-resolution must be disabled for VIP customers who have contractually agreed to dedicated human agent support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R015	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case-Linked Corrective Action and Process Improvement	The system must support linking cases to formal Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) records when root cause analysis reveals systemic issues requiring process changes. CAPA creation from a case must capture the identified root cause, affected process area, proposed corrective action, responsible owner, target completion date, and effectiveness verification criteria. The system must track CAPA implementation progress with milestone updates and overdue alerts. AI must identify case patterns that warrant CAPA initiation by analyzing clusters of cases with similar root causes, calculating the cumulative financial impact, and comparing against configurable thresholds for CAPA triggers (e.g., more than 5 cases with the same root cause in 30 days, or cumulative claim value exceeding USD 10,000 equivalent). CAPA effectiveness must be measured by monitoring whether the frequency of related cases decreases after implementation. The system must maintain a CAPA register per entity and global, with periodic review workflows where operations management evaluates open CAPAs and their progress. CAPA metrics must be included in the case analytics dashboard showing the count of CAPAs initiated, implementation rates, effectiveness scores, and average time to closure per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R011	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Print and Digital Distribution	The system must generate printable DO documents in PDF format conforming to the standard layout prescribed by each port authority. The document must include a QR code linking to the real-time DO status page, barcode encoding the DO number for terminal gate scanning, and all mandatory fields including container details, validity dates, and authorized signatures. Arabic language DOs must render in RTL format with proper Arabic typography and Hijri dates for Qatar, KSA, and UAE entities. The system must support digital distribution via email with PDF attachment, WhatsApp Business API for instant delivery, and SMS with a secure short link to download the DO. All distributed copies must be watermarked with the recipient identity and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R016	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Customer Communication Preferences and Consent Management	The system must manage customer communication preferences governing how and when the case management system contacts customers. Preferences must include preferred communication channel (email, SMS, WhatsApp, phone, portal), preferred language (English, Arabic, or bilingual), preferred contact hours respecting the customer's timezone, preferred contact person per case type (operations contact for shipment issues, finance contact for billing disputes), and opt-in/opt-out status for proactive notifications. The system must comply with data protection regulations applicable to each operating jurisdiction: Qatar Personal Data Privacy Law, UAE Data Protection Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021), KSA Personal Data Protection Law, and India Digital Personal Data Protection Act. Customer consent must be explicitly obtained and recorded for each communication channel before initiating contact through that channel. Consent records must be timestamped, versioned, and auditable. The system must respect do-not-contact periods such as outside business hours or during specified blackout windows requested by the customer. Preference changes must propagate immediately to all active cases and communication automation rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R017	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Offline Case Access and Field Agent Support	The system must support offline case access for field agents and customer service representatives working at port locations, container yards, and customer premises where internet connectivity may be unreliable. The offline module must synchronize assigned cases with investigation data, communication history, and knowledge base articles to the agent's device before going offline. Agents must be able to view case details, add investigation notes, capture photographic evidence (e.g., container damage documentation at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali container yards), record customer meeting notes, and draft response communications while offline. All offline actions must be queued locally with timestamps and synchronized to the central system upon reconnection with conflict resolution for cases modified by other agents during the offline period. The offline module must support both English and Arabic text input with local language switching. Photographic evidence captured offline must include GPS coordinates and timestamp metadata for verification. The synchronization protocol must handle bandwidth-constrained environments by prioritizing text data synchronization before media uploads and supporting resume for interrupted transfers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-2-004-R018	FEAT-008-2-004	MOD-008	Case Management Integration with CRM and Sales	The system must integrate case management data with customer relationship management and sales modules to provide a 360-degree customer view. Case history must be accessible from the customer profile showing all historical and active cases with resolution outcomes, satisfaction scores, and financial impact summaries. Sales team members must receive alerts when their assigned accounts have critical or high-priority cases opened, enabling proactive engagement with the customer. Customer profitability analysis must factor in case management costs including agent time, claim payouts, and service recovery expenses to provide a true cost-to-serve metric per customer across all entities. The integration must support account planning workflows where case trends inform service improvement proposals during contract renewal discussions. Customer segmentation models must incorporate case management metrics alongside revenue and volume data to identify high-maintenance accounts requiring service plan adjustments. The system must generate customer health reports combining booking volumes, revenue trends, case patterns, and satisfaction scores with AI-generated recommendations for relationship management actions. All CRM integration must respect entity-level data boundaries while providing consolidated views for multi-entity customers who operate across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R001	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Delivery Order Creation and Issuance	The system must allow authorized users to create Delivery Orders linked to a specific Bill of Lading (B/L), booking reference, and container numbers. Each DO must capture the consignee name, clearing agent details, cargo description, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), seal numbers, and gross weight. The DO must reference the originating entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) and automatically assign a unique sequential DO number with entity prefix (e.g., QA-DO-2026-00001). The system must validate that all freight charges, demurrage, and detention fees are settled or have approved credit terms before allowing DO issuance. Integration with the billing module must confirm zero outstanding balance or approved credit hold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R003	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Validity Period and Extension Management	Each Delivery Order must have a configurable validity period defaulting to 7 calendar days from issuance, adjustable per entity and port policy. The system must automatically flag DOs approaching expiry within 48 hours via dashboard alerts and optional SMS/email notifications to the consignee and clearing agent. Expired DOs must be automatically marked as void and require re-issuance through a new approval workflow. The system must support validity extensions with audit trail capturing the extension reason, approving authority, new expiry date, and any additional charges for the extension period. Hijri calendar dates must be displayed alongside Gregorian dates for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R004	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	AI-Powered DO Auto-Generation from B/L Data	The AI engine must automatically generate draft Delivery Orders by extracting data from uploaded Bills of Lading, cargo manifests, and arrival notices using OCR and NLP processing. The system must achieve at least 95% field extraction accuracy for structured B/L documents and 85% for semi-structured or handwritten documents. Extracted fields must include consignee name, notify party, container numbers, cargo description, HS codes, weight, and volume. The AI must flag discrepancies between B/L data and vessel manifest data for manual review. Auto-generated DOs must enter a review queue where operators confirm or correct fields before final issuance, reducing manual data entry time by at least 70% compared to fully manual creation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R005	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Approval Workflow with Delegation	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for Delivery Orders based on cargo value thresholds, cargo type (hazardous/DG, reefer, OOG, standard), and customer credit status. Approval levels must be configurable per entity with a minimum of two levels: operations officer and operations manager. For DG cargo classified under IMDG codes, an additional safety officer approval must be mandatory. The system must support approval delegation where an approver can temporarily assign their approval authority to another qualified user during leave or absence, with start and end dates for the delegation period. All approval actions must be timestamped with user identity and IP address for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R006	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	Audit Log Viewer	Searchable audit log: who, what, when, where (IP), result (success/denied). Filters: date range, user, action type, resource. Export to CSV or PDF. Highlight security events. Retain 7 years. Real-time streaming for live monitoring.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R013	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	Offline DO Operations and Sync	The system must support offline Delivery Order creation and approval on mobile devices and tablets when network connectivity is unavailable at port locations or remote warehouses. Offline-created DOs must be stored locally with full data integrity using encrypted local storage. Upon network restoration, the system must automatically synchronize offline DOs with the central server using conflict resolution logic that prioritizes server-side data for status fields and preserves offline edits for data entry fields. Conflict cases requiring human judgment must be flagged in a reconciliation queue. The offline mode must support a minimum of 500 DO operations before requiring synchronization, and the sync process must complete within 60 seconds for up to 200 queued transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R014	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Analytics and Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying DO processing metrics including average issuance time, approval turnaround time, port acknowledgment latency, and pickup completion rate per entity and per port. The dashboard must support filters by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), entity, port, customer, and cargo type. Key performance indicators must include DO-to-gate-out cycle time, auto-approval rate percentage tracking toward the 95% AI automation target, exception rate by category, and expired DO percentage. The dashboard must support data export to Excel and PDF formats with bilingual headers (Arabic/English) and must refresh at maximum 30-second intervals for near-real-time operational monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R015	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Template Management for Regular Customers	The system must allow creation and management of DO templates for regular customers who ship recurring cargo types on consistent trade lanes. Templates must pre-populate consignee details, clearing agent information, preferred delivery location, standard cargo descriptions, and default container types. The AI engine must analyze historical DO patterns and proactively suggest template creation when it detects a customer has issued more than 10 similar DOs within a 90-day period. Template usage must be tracked for audit purposes, and templates must be versioned so that changes to customer master data automatically prompt template review and update. Each template must be entity-specific and support both Arabic and English field values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-001-R016	FEAT-008-3-001	MOD-008	DO Integration with Transport and Last-Mile Delivery	The system must link Delivery Orders with downstream transport booking and last-mile delivery tracking when the shipping line or agent provides door delivery services under INCOTERMS DAP, DDP, or DAT. The DO must capture delivery address with geocoordinates for route optimization, preferred delivery time window, vehicle type requirements (flatbed, skeletal trailer, lowbed for OOG), and any site-specific access restrictions. Upon container gate-out from the terminal, the system must automatically trigger transport dispatch notifications and update the DO status with GPS-based ETA for the final delivery location. Integration must support multi-leg transport where containers transit through inland container depots (ICDs) before final delivery.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R001	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Container Release Authorization Workflow	The system must implement a structured authorization workflow for container release that validates the following prerequisites in sequence: Bill of Lading surrender or telex release confirmation, customs clearance status, freight and charges settlement, and Delivery Order issuance. Each prerequisite must be independently verified against the source system (documentation module, customs integration, billing module, and DO management respectively). The workflow must support both original B/L presentation and electronic release (eB/L) via DCSA standards. The system must generate a unique release PIN code upon successful completion of all prerequisites, which the clearing agent presents at the terminal gate for container pickup. PIN codes must expire after the DO validity period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R002	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Release Hold Management with Reason Codes	The system must support placing and removing holds on container release using standardized hold reason codes including: freight hold (unpaid charges), customs hold (pending clearance or examination), carrier hold (B/L not surrendered), bank hold (LC discrepancy), damage hold (container damage assessment pending), hazmat hold (DG documentation incomplete), and regulatory hold (sanction screening or trade compliance). Multiple holds can coexist on a single container, and release must only be authorized when all holds are cleared. Each hold placement and removal must capture the responsible party, timestamp, reason narrative, and supporting document reference. The system must provide an escalation mechanism where holds older than configurable thresholds (default 72 hours) auto-escalate to management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R003	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Bank Guarantee and Letter of Credit Release Processing	For shipments under Letter of Credit (LC) terms, the system must manage container release in coordination with the advising or confirming bank. The system must track LC document presentation status, discrepancy notices, and bank acceptance or refusal. Container release under bank guarantee must capture the guarantee number, issuing bank, guarantee amount in transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), validity period, and guarantee type (shipping guarantee, indemnity letter). The system must automatically monitor bank guarantee expiry dates and alert operations 15 days before expiry. If original documents are not presented before guarantee expiry, the system must flag the container for re-hold and notify the bank liaison officer for follow-up action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R004	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Telex Release and Electronic B/L Processing	The system must support electronic release instructions received via telex release messages from origin offices or partner agents. Telex release processing must validate the sender identity against registered agent codes, match the B/L number and container details, and verify the authorization chain from the shipper or their bank. The system must parse incoming telex release messages in both structured EDI format (BAPLIE, COPARN) and unstructured email format using AI-powered text extraction with at least 92% accuracy for key fields. For electronic Bills of Lading (eB/L) processed through platforms such as WAVE BL, Bolero, or essDOCS, the system must integrate via API to verify document authenticity and title transfer status before authorizing release.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R007	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Integration with Port Community Systems	The system must integrate with Mwani Qatar's port community system for Hamad Port, Abu Dhabi Ports' SILSAL system for Khalifa Port, DP World's terminal operating system for Jebel Ali, and the Indian Customs EDI system (ICEGATE) for JNPT. Integrations must support real-time exchange of vessel schedule data including ETA notifications, berth requests, and actual arrival/departure confirmations using UN/EDIFACT BAPLIE and COPARN message standards where required. The system must handle integration failures gracefully by queuing outbound messages and retrying with exponential backoff. Failed message alerts must be routed to the operations team within five minutes of detection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R005	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Container Release PIN Code Generation and Validation	The system must generate secure, unique release PIN codes consisting of 8 alphanumeric characters for each authorized container release. PIN codes must be cryptographically random and non-sequential to prevent guessing. Each PIN must be associated with a specific container number, DO number, authorized clearing agent or transporter, and valid pickup window. The system must support PIN validation via API integration with terminal gate systems at all four port locations. PIN validation must check expiry status, authorized party identity (matching truck plate number and driver ID), and confirm no new holds have been placed since PIN generation. Failed PIN validation attempts exceeding 3 tries must trigger a security alert and temporary PIN suspension requiring operations manager intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R006	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Partial Release for Multi-Container Shipments	The system must support partial container release for shipments consisting of multiple containers under a single B/L. Partial release must track which specific containers have been released and which remain on hold, maintaining an accurate count of released versus total containers. The system must enforce business rules where partial release is only permitted if the freight terms allow it (e.g., FAK rate versus per-container rate) and if customs clearance covers the specific containers being released. Each partial release must generate its own PIN code and update the B/L release status to 'Partially Released' with a counter showing N of M containers released. The system must prevent final invoice closure until all containers under the B/L are either released or formally abandoned.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R007	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Automated Release for Pre-Cleared Trusted Customers	The system must implement an automated fast-track release program for pre-qualified trusted customers (similar to AEO - Authorized Economic Operator status). Trusted customer profiles must be maintained with approval from compliance and commercial management, specifying credit limits, cargo type restrictions, and valid port locations. For trusted customers, the system must automatically complete the release workflow within 30 minutes of vessel discharge completion, bypassing manual approval steps while still enforcing customs clearance and sanctions screening prerequisites. The AI engine must continuously monitor trusted customer behavior patterns and automatically flag anomalies such as sudden cargo type changes, unusual trade lanes, or shipment frequency deviations for compliance review. Trust status must be reviewed quarterly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R008	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Release Status Notification and Communication	The system must send automated notifications to all relevant stakeholders at each stage of the container release process. Notification triggers must include: hold placement, hold removal, all holds cleared, PIN code generated, container gate-out confirmed, and release expired. Notifications must be sent via configurable channels including email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API, and in-app push notifications. Notification templates must be available in both Arabic and English with automatic language selection based on recipient preference. Each notification must include the container number, B/L reference, current status, and next required action. The system must maintain a notification log with delivery confirmation status and must retry failed notifications up to 3 times with 15-minute intervals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R009	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Demurrage and Detention Calculation at Release	At the point of container release authorization, the system must calculate and display all applicable demurrage (port storage) and detention (container usage) charges in real time. Calculations must apply the correct tariff based on the container type, port location, customer contract terms, and number of free days consumed. The system must support different free-day policies per trade lane, per customer, and per port authority regulation. For Hamad Port, the Qatar Customs free storage period rules must be applied. For Jebel Ali, DP World's published tariff schedule must be referenced. Currency conversion must apply at the daily exchange rate for the applicable transaction currency. The calculated charges must be confirmed and either settled or approved for credit before the release PIN is generated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R010	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Release Reversal and Container Re-Hold	The system must support release reversal scenarios where a previously authorized release must be cancelled and the container placed back on hold. Valid reversal reasons include: court order or legal attachment, customs re-examination request, fraud suspicion, consignee request, and payment reversal (bounced cheque or reversed wire transfer). Release reversal must immediately invalidate any outstanding PIN codes, transmit an urgent cancellation message to the terminal operating system, and send priority notifications to all stakeholders. If the container has already been gated out, the system must log this as a post-release incident and trigger an investigation workflow. All release reversals must require senior management approval and detailed justification recorded in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R011	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Container Condition Survey at Release	The system must capture and record container condition information at the time of release, integrating with terminal gate camera systems and equipment inspection records where available. The release record must include container structural condition (good, damaged, requires repair), seal integrity status (intact, broken, replaced), reefer unit operational status for refrigerated containers including temperature reading and fuel level, and any visible cargo damage noted during the discharge survey. For damaged containers, the system must automatically create a damage case linked to the release record and trigger the insurance claims workflow. Photographic evidence must be stored as attachments to the release record with timestamps and geo-tags from the terminal gate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R012	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Release Queue Prioritization and SLA Tracking	The system must maintain a prioritized release processing queue with configurable priority rules based on cargo urgency, customer tier (platinum, gold, silver, standard), cargo type (perishable, pharmaceutical, live animals), and contractual SLA commitments. The system must track release processing time from B/L surrender to PIN generation against defined SLA targets per customer tier and cargo type. SLA breaches must trigger automatic escalation to operations management with root cause categorization (pending customs, payment delay, documentation gap, system issue). The dashboard must display real-time queue depth, average processing time, SLA compliance percentage, and agent workload distribution to enable dynamic resource allocation during peak periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R007	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	API Keys Management	Generate API keys for external integrations. Key has: name, permissions scope, expiry, IP allowlist. Revoke instantly. Last-used timestamp. Max 20 active API keys per company. Key shown only once on creation, hashed with SHA-256 for storage.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R013	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Inter-Terminal Transfer Release Coordination	The system must manage container release scenarios involving inter-terminal transfers where a container discharged at one terminal must be released from a different terminal or inland container depot (ICD). This is common at JNPT where containers may be moved between JNPCT, NSIGT, BMCT, or NSICT terminals, and at Dubai where containers may transfer between Jebel Ali terminals 1, 2, and 3. The release workflow must coordinate with both the origin and destination terminals, tracking the inter-terminal movement status, confirming container arrival at the destination terminal, and generating the release PIN specific to the destination terminal gate. Transfer charges must be automatically calculated and added to the release billing before PIN generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R014	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Empty Container Release and Repatriation	The system must manage the release of empty containers from depots to exporters for stuffing, as well as the return of empty containers after destuffing by importers. Empty release must track container grade (food grade, non-food grade, cargo-worthy), last cargo type (to prevent contamination, especially for food or chemical cargo), and cleaning/washing certificate status. The system must enforce container allocation rules ensuring empties are released according to booking priority and equipment availability. For repatriation scenarios where empty containers must be repositioned to other ports, the system must coordinate with the vessel planning module to allocate slots and generate the necessary release documentation for depot gate-out and terminal gate-in.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R015	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Release Audit Trail and Regulatory Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every container release transaction capturing the complete sequence of events from initial B/L registration through final gate-out, including all hold placements and removals, approval decisions, PIN generation and validation events, and stakeholder notifications. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. The system must generate regulatory reports required by each jurisdiction: Qatar Customs release statistics, UAE Federal Customs Authority (FCA) container movement reports, Saudi Customs (ZATCA) release compliance reports, and Indian Customs (CBIC) container tracking reports. Reports must be generated in the prescribed format for each authority and must be exportable for submission through the respective customs portals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R016	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	AI-Predicted Release Bottleneck Detection	The AI engine must analyze historical release data patterns to predict potential bottlenecks in the container release process before they materialize. Predictions must consider factors including vessel discharge volume, current terminal congestion, customs examination rates, pending payment volumes, and staffing levels. The system must generate proactive alerts when predicted release volumes are likely to exceed processing capacity within the next 24 to 48 hours, recommending specific actions such as additional staffing, pre-clearance outreach to key customers, or overtime authorization. The prediction model must achieve at least 80% accuracy in identifying bottleneck conditions 24 hours in advance, measured against actual queue depth and SLA breach metrics over rolling 30-day periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-002-R017	FEAT-008-3-002	MOD-008	Offline Release Processing and Emergency Authorization	The system must provide an offline release processing capability for scenarios where port terminal connectivity is disrupted or during system maintenance windows. Offline mode must allow authorized operations managers to generate temporary manual release authorizations using pre-allocated emergency PIN code ranges stored on encrypted local devices. Each emergency authorization must capture the authorizing officer identity via biometric or two-factor authentication, along with the justification for offline processing. Upon connectivity restoration, all offline releases must be automatically reconciled against the central system, with discrepancies flagged for immediate investigation. The system must limit offline release authority to a maximum of 50 containers per officer per 24-hour period to manage risk exposure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R001	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Port Pass Request and Issuance Workflow	The system must manage the end-to-end workflow for requesting, approving, and issuing port passes for truck drivers, clearing agent representatives, and authorized visitors entering Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT terminal areas. The workflow must capture applicant identity details (national ID, passport, or Emirates ID number), photograph, vehicle details (plate number, truck type, chassis number), purpose of visit, requested access zones within the port, and valid date/time range. The system must validate identity documents against issuing authority databases where API integration is available (UAE ICA, Qatar MOI, India Aadhaar/DigiLocker). Pass issuance must generate a QR-coded digital pass transmittable to the applicant's mobile device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R002	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Multi-Port Pass Type Management	The system must support multiple port pass types with distinct authorization levels and validity periods: single-entry pass (valid for one gate-in and gate-out within 24 hours), multi-entry recurring pass (valid for specified period up to 12 months with unlimited entries), temporary contractor pass (project-based with defined scope), emergency access pass (expedited issuance within 30 minutes for urgent operational needs), and VIP escort pass (for port authority inspections or customer visits). Each pass type must have configurable approval workflows, maximum validity durations, zone access restrictions, and associated fee structures per port authority regulations. The system must enforce quotas on active passes per company to comply with port authority limits on simultaneous terminal access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R003	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Vehicle Registration and Pre-Approval Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive vehicle registration database for all trucks and transport vehicles authorized to enter port terminals. Registration must capture vehicle plate number, emirate or state of registration, vehicle type classification (prime mover, skeletal trailer, flatbed, tanker, lowbed), gross vehicle weight, insurance validity, ISTIMARA or vehicle registration card expiry, and safety compliance certificate status (RFID tag registration for Jebel Ali, GPS tracker mandate for JNPT). The system must automatically flag vehicles with expired documentation and prevent port pass issuance until documentation is renewed. Bulk vehicle registration must be supported for transport companies registering fleet vehicles, with CSV upload capability handling up to 1,000 vehicles per import batch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-2-002-R008	FEAT-000-2-002	\N	Billing and Subscription View	Current plan, next billing date, amount, payment method (masked card or bank transfer reference). Invoice history with PDF download. Upgrade/downgrade plan. Usage metrics: active users, storage used, API calls this month vs limit.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R004	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Driver Identity Verification and Biometric Integration	The system must implement multi-factor identity verification for drivers and individuals accessing port terminals. Primary verification must use government-issued ID document scanning with OCR extraction and validation against official databases. Secondary verification must support biometric options including fingerprint scanning and facial recognition where terminal gate infrastructure supports these capabilities. For JNPT, integration with the Indian RFID-based truck tracking system must be supported. For UAE ports, Emirates ID card reader integration must validate identity in real time. The system must maintain a driver profile database with photograph, verified identity, associated transport company, and access history. Drivers flagged in security watchlists must be automatically denied access and reported to port security.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R005	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Time Slot Booking for Terminal Gate Entry	The system must integrate with terminal vehicle booking systems (VBS) to coordinate port pass issuance with gate entry time slots. For Jebel Ali terminal, integration with DP World's VBS must ensure that port passes are only valid for the booked time slot window with a configurable grace period (default 60 minutes). For JNPT, integration with the centralized DMICDC logistics data bank must synchronize container pickup appointments with port pass validity. The system must display available time slots to users during port pass application, recommend optimal slots based on terminal congestion predictions from the AI engine, and automatically adjust pass validity if the time slot is rescheduled. Slot cancellations must trigger automatic pass status updates and notifications to the pass holder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R006	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Zone-Based Access Control Configuration	The system must support granular zone-based access control within each port terminal, defining separate access zones such as container yard, reefer yard, dangerous goods storage area, customs examination zone, empty container depot, CFS (container freight station), passenger terminal area, and administrative buildings. Each port pass must specify permitted zones, and the system must transmit zone authorization data to the terminal access control system for enforcement at internal checkpoints. Zone definitions must be configurable per port location with visual mapping on a port layout diagram. Restricted zones such as DG storage areas must require additional safety certification verification (IMDG training certificate) before zone access is authorized on the port pass.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R007	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Port Pass Renewal and Expiry Management	The system must proactively manage port pass renewals by sending automated reminders to pass holders and their sponsoring companies 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry. The renewal process must re-validate all underlying documents including vehicle registration, insurance, driver license, company trade license, and security clearance. The AI engine must streamline renewals for pass holders with clean access history by pre-approving renewal applications that meet all documentary requirements, targeting 90% auto-renewal for eligible recurring passes. Expired passes must be automatically deactivated in the terminal access control system within 1 hour of expiry. The system must support bulk renewal processing for transport companies renewing multiple driver and vehicle passes simultaneously with a single approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R008	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Port Authority System Integration per Location	The system must integrate with each port authority's security and access management system using the prescribed integration method. For Hamad Port Qatar, integration must use QTerminals' access management API to submit pass requests and receive approval status. For Jebel Ali, integration must interface with DP World's gate management system and the Dubai Trade portal for customs gate pass coordination. For Khalifa Port, integration must connect with Abu Dhabi Ports Community System (Maqta Gateway). For JNPT, integration must interface with the Port Community System (PCS1x) and the terminal-specific gate automation systems of each terminal operator. Each integration must handle the port authority's specific data format requirements, acknowledgment protocols, and error handling procedures with appropriate retry logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R009	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Arabic and English Bilingual Pass Generation	All port pass documents, both digital and printable, must be generated in bilingual format with Arabic and English content displayed simultaneously. Arabic text must render in RTL direction with proper Arabic font rendering including diacritics and ligatures. The pass document must include the holder's name in both Arabic and English as registered in their identity document, company name in both languages, access zones labeled in both languages, and all dates displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. For India JNPT operations, the English-only format is acceptable, but Hindi transliteration of the pass holder name must be supported as an optional field. The QR code on the pass must encode data in a language-neutral format parseable by terminal gate systems regardless of language setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R010	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Emergency and Expedited Pass Processing	The system must provide an emergency pass processing mode for time-critical situations such as urgent vessel loading requiring immediate terminal access, emergency maintenance by port equipment vendors, government inspection teams requiring unscheduled access, or critical cargo pickup for perishable and pharmaceutical shipments. Emergency passes must be issuable within 15 minutes of request through an expedited approval workflow requiring only single senior manager authorization via mobile approval. The system must impose daily limits on emergency passes per entity (default 10 per day) to prevent abuse of the expedited process. All emergency passes must be flagged for post-issuance audit review, and the system must generate a weekly emergency pass utilization report for management review showing issuance patterns, justifications, and any anomalies detected by the AI compliance engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R011	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Port Pass Fee Collection and Invoicing	The system must manage port pass fee collection for pass types that carry charges as defined by each port authority's tariff schedule. Fee structures must be configurable per port, per pass type, and per validity duration. The system must support multiple payment methods including credit card (online gateway integration), bank transfer, prepaid wallet balance deduction, and inclusion on the customer's periodic invoice. For UAE operations, VAT at 5% must be automatically applied to pass fees. For KSA operations, VAT at 15% must be applied. For Qatar and India operations, applicable tax rules must be configurable. All fees must be collected in the local currency of the port location (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT), and consolidated invoicing must convert to the customer's preferred billing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R012	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Blacklist and Security Screening Integration	The system must screen all port pass applicants against security blacklists maintained by port authorities, national security agencies, and international watchlists. Screening must check the applicant name, identity document number, and associated company against local port authority banned persons lists, national security databases accessible via government API (where available), and international sanctions lists (OFAC SDN, EU consolidated list, UN Security Council lists). Positive matches must immediately halt the pass issuance process and generate a confidential security alert to the port security liaison officer and the company's compliance team. The system must support fuzzy name matching with configurable sensitivity to handle transliteration variations between Arabic and English names, reducing false negatives from spelling differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R013	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Real-Time Gate Movement Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing all active port pass holders currently inside each terminal, including their entry time, current duration inside the terminal, authorized zone versus detected zone (where terminal tracking systems provide location data), and associated container or job reference. The dashboard must display gate-in and gate-out events as they occur with less than 60-second latency. Alerts must be generated for pass holders exceeding their authorized time limit inside the terminal (configurable default: 4 hours for single-entry passes). The dashboard must support filtering by port location, pass type, company, and zone, and must include a map view showing terminal layout with active vehicle positions where GPS or RFID positioning data is available from the terminal operator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R014	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Contractor and Vendor Long-Term Access Management	The system must manage long-term port access for contractors, maintenance vendors, and service providers who require regular terminal access for extended project durations. Contractor pass management must include project reference linking, sponsor company accountability, safety induction completion verification, personal protective equipment (PPE) compliance records, and specific work zone authorization tied to active work permits. The system must integrate with the health and safety module to verify that contractors hold valid safety certifications (NEBOSH, IOSH, or local equivalents) before issuing terminal access. Contractor passes must have a maximum validity of 12 months with mandatory quarterly reviews. The system must automatically suspend contractor passes if the sponsoring company's trade license or insurance expires.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R015	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	Offline Pass Validation and Backup Access	The system must support offline port pass validation at terminal gates during network outages or system downtime. Each terminal gate must maintain a locally cached database of active port passes updated at configurable intervals (default every 15 minutes during normal operations). The offline cache must include pass holder identity, vehicle details, authorized zones, and validity period, enabling gate officers to validate passes without real-time server connectivity. The system must generate a printable backup pass with unique serial number and security features (holographic QR code, tamper-evident design) for scenarios where digital verification is completely unavailable. Offline gate transactions must be queued and automatically reconciled with the central system upon connectivity restoration, with any discrepancies flagged for security review within 1 hour.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-003-R016	FEAT-008-3-003	MOD-008	AI-Optimized Pass Processing and Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously analyze port pass usage patterns to optimize processing and detect anomalies. Optimization features must include predictive pre-approval of recurring pass applications based on historical patterns, intelligent time slot recommendations based on terminal congestion predictions, and automatic pass type upgrade suggestions for frequent visitors who would benefit from multi-entry passes. Anomaly detection must flag unusual patterns including: passes used at unexpected hours, vehicles entering terminals without corresponding container pickup or delivery jobs, pass holders accessing unauthorized zones, and significant deviations from normal visit duration. Detected anomalies must generate risk-scored alerts to the security team, with high-risk anomalies (score above 0.8 on a 0-1 scale) triggering immediate notification to the port security control room.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R001	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Multi-Channel Query Ingestion and Routing	The system must ingest customer queries from multiple communication channels including web portal live chat, email (dedicated inbox per entity), WhatsApp Business API, SMS, telephone (via speech-to-text integration), and the customer self-service portal. Each incoming query must be assigned a unique ticket number with entity prefix (e.g., QA-TKT-2026-00001), timestamped with receipt time, and categorized by channel source. The system must implement intelligent routing rules that direct queries to the appropriate resolution queue based on AI-classified query type, customer tier, language preference, and entity jurisdiction. Queries received outside business hours must be auto-acknowledged with estimated response time and queued for AI processing. All channels must be unified into a single agent workspace for omnichannel case management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R002	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Arabic and English Natural Language Understanding	The AI engine must process and understand customer queries in both Modern Standard Arabic and English, including mixed-language queries common in the Gulf region where customers blend Arabic and English in a single message. The NLU model must handle Arabic dialectal variations common in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and transliterated Arabic written in Latin characters (Arabizi). Intent classification must achieve at least 92% accuracy across 50+ predefined shipping-related intent categories including cargo tracking, rate inquiry, booking modification, documentation request, complaint, and claim filing. Entity extraction must identify shipping-specific entities such as container numbers (4-letter prefix + 7 digits + check digit), B/L numbers, vessel names, port codes, and date references in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with at least 95% accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R003	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	AI-Automated First Contact Resolution	The AI must autonomously resolve routine customer queries without human intervention, targeting 95% first-contact resolution rate for the following query categories: container tracking and status updates, vessel schedule and ETA inquiries, tariff and rate quotations for standard trade lanes, documentation requirements by port and cargo type, demurrage and detention calculator queries, and DO and release status inquiries. Resolution must be delivered by retrieving real-time data from the relevant system modules (operations, documentation, billing, vessel tracking) and composing a natural language response in the customer's preferred language. The AI must provide source citations for its answers (e.g., referencing specific tariff numbers, vessel schedule entries) and include confidence scores. Responses with confidence below 85% must be routed to human agents for verification before sending.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R004	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Contextual Conversation Memory and History	The AI must maintain full conversation context across interactions within a query session and across historical sessions for the same customer. When a customer contacts support, the system must immediately retrieve their customer profile, recent shipment activity (last 90 days), open tickets, pending invoices, and previous interaction summaries. The AI must reference prior conversations contextually—for example, if a customer previously asked about a delayed container and contacts again, the AI must proactively provide an update on that container's current status without requiring the customer to re-explain. Conversation history must be searchable and filterable by date range, channel, query category, and resolution status. Customer interaction data must be retained for a minimum of 3 years per regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R005	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Intelligent Escalation to Human Agents	The system must implement intelligent escalation rules that transfer conversations from AI to human agents when: the AI confidence score falls below the configured threshold (default 70%), the customer explicitly requests a human agent, the query involves a formal complaint or claim exceeding configurable value thresholds, the conversation sentiment analysis detects customer frustration or anger (negative sentiment score above 0.7), or the query requires actions outside AI authorization scope such as waiving charges or amending contracts. Escalation must transfer the complete conversation context, AI analysis summary, and recommended resolution to the human agent so the customer does not need to repeat information. The system must support warm transfer (AI remains in conversation during handoff) and cold transfer (AI exits, agent takes over) modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R006	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Complaint Management and SLA Tracking	The system must manage formal customer complaints through a structured lifecycle: registration, acknowledgment, investigation, resolution, and closure with customer satisfaction confirmation. Each complaint must be classified by category (service failure, billing dispute, cargo damage, documentation error, staff behavior, delay), severity level (critical, high, medium, low), and responsible department. SLA timelines must be enforced per severity: critical complaints must receive initial response within 2 hours and resolution within 24 hours; high severity within 4 hours response and 48 hours resolution; medium within 8 hours response and 5 business days resolution. The system must automatically escalate SLA-breaching complaints to the next management level and generate daily SLA compliance reports per entity. Root cause analysis must be documented for all resolved complaints to feed the continuous improvement knowledge base.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R007	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Proactive Customer Communication Engine	The AI must generate proactive outbound communications to customers for significant shipment events without waiting for customer inquiry. Proactive notifications must cover: vessel arrival and departure confirmations, container discharge confirmation with estimated free time expiry, customs clearance completion, DO readiness notification, demurrage warning as free days approach expiry, invoice generation, payment receipt confirmation, and service disruption advisories. Each proactive message must be personalized with the customer's shipment details and composed in their preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must manage communication frequency to prevent notification fatigue, consolidating multiple events into digest messages when more than 3 events occur within a 2-hour window for the same customer. Customers must be able to configure notification preferences per event type and channel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R008	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Knowledge Base Management for AI Training	The system must maintain a structured knowledge base that serves as the AI's reference repository for answering customer queries. The knowledge base must contain: port-specific procedures and requirements for all four port locations, tariff schedules and surcharge structures, documentation requirements by trade lane and cargo type, regulatory requirements per jurisdiction, FAQ entries covering common customer questions, and resolution templates for standard query types. Knowledge base articles must be version-controlled with approval workflow for updates, and must support both Arabic and English content. The AI must be retrained or updated whenever knowledge base content changes, with the update reflected in AI responses within a maximum of 4 hours. Content owners per department must be designated and responsible for quarterly review and accuracy verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R009	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Rate and Tariff Inquiry Resolution	The AI must handle customer rate and tariff inquiries by accessing the commercial rate management module to provide accurate quotations for standard trade lanes and container types. The system must support inquiries for ocean freight rates, local charges (THC, documentation fees, B/L fees), destination charges, and accessorial services. Rate responses must clearly specify the currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), validity period, applicable INCOTERMS basis, included and excluded services, and any surcharges (BAF, CAF, PSS, GRI) effective during the rate validity period. For non-standard requests involving special cargo (OOG, hazmat, reefer with specific temperature requirements), project cargo, or trade lanes without published rates, the AI must create a rate request ticket routed to the commercial team with all customer specifications captured.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R010	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Cargo Tracking and ETA Prediction Queries	The AI must provide comprehensive cargo tracking responses by aggregating data from multiple sources including vessel AIS tracking, terminal operating systems, customs clearance status, and inland transport tracking. When a customer inquires about cargo status, the AI must provide: current vessel position and speed, estimated port arrival time with confidence interval, predicted discharge sequence, estimated customs clearance duration based on historical patterns for the cargo type and customs office, and projected availability for pickup. The AI must proactively identify and communicate potential delays caused by port congestion, weather, customs examination selection, or vessel schedule changes. ETA predictions must use machine learning models trained on historical data specific to each port, achieving prediction accuracy within 6 hours for vessel arrival and within 24 hours for cargo availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R001	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Empty Container Demand Forecasting	The AI engine must generate rolling 4-week, 8-week, and 12-week empty container demand forecasts by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK) and location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and all configured inland depots). Forecasts must incorporate historical booking patterns, confirmed booking pipeline, seasonal trends (Hajj/Umrah peaks, Ramadan, Diwali, monsoon season), economic indicators, and vessel schedule changes. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actuals with a target MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) of less than 15% for 4-week forecasts. The system must allow manual demand overrides by authorized planners with mandatory justification notes and approval workflows, and display all forecasts with bilingual labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R011	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Sentiment Analysis and Customer Satisfaction Scoring	The system must perform real-time sentiment analysis on all customer interactions across all channels to gauge customer satisfaction and detect emerging service issues. Sentiment scoring must operate on a continuous scale from -1.0 (extremely negative) to +1.0 (extremely positive) with granular emotion detection including frustration, confusion, urgency, satisfaction, and appreciation. The system must analyze sentiment in both Arabic and English with cultural context awareness—understanding that communication styles differ across Gulf, Levantine, and South Asian cultural norms. Aggregated sentiment scores must be tracked per customer, per entity, per query category, and over time to identify trends. A customer satisfaction dashboard must display CSAT scores, Net Promoter Score (NPS) estimates derived from interaction sentiment, and trending topics associated with negative sentiment for management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R012	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Claims and Dispute Intake Processing	The AI must guide customers through the cargo claims and billing dispute intake process, collecting all required information structured for efficient processing by the claims team. For cargo damage claims, the system must collect: B/L number, container number, damage description, estimated damage value with currency, supporting photographs (uploaded via chat or email attachment), survey report reference if available, and consignee declaration of damage. For billing disputes, the system must collect: invoice number, disputed line items, customer's calculation versus invoiced amount, and supporting documentation. The AI must validate completeness of submissions against minimum requirements before creating the claim case, requesting any missing information through follow-up questions. Created claims must be automatically assigned to the claims resolution team with all collected information structured in the standard claims template per the applicable entity's legal jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R013	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Self-Service Customer Portal Integration	The AI query resolution system must be deeply integrated with the customer self-service portal, providing contextual help and guided workflows within the portal interface. When customers navigate portal sections for booking, documentation upload, invoice viewing, or DO requests, the AI must offer contextual assistance through an embedded chat widget that understands the customer's current portal context. The AI must be able to execute portal actions on behalf of the customer with their authorization, including initiating booking requests, downloading documents, requesting DO re-issuance, and scheduling delivery appointments. Portal-initiated queries must carry the full session context including the page the customer was viewing, any error messages encountered, and form data entered. The system must support SSO integration so that authenticated portal users are automatically identified without re-verification in the chat.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R014	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Multi-Entity Query Routing and Response Isolation	The system must ensure strict data isolation between entities when processing customer queries, preventing information leakage between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations even when a single customer has relationships with multiple entities. Query routing must direct inquiries to the correct entity based on the referenced shipment, port, or booking. When a customer has cross-entity queries (e.g., a shipment originating from JNPT destined for Hamad Port), the AI must be able to access data from both entities to provide a comprehensive response while clearly attributing information to the respective entity. Agent assignment must respect entity boundaries—agents in the UAE entity must not handle Qatar entity complaint resolutions unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. All responses must use the entity-specific letterhead, contact details, and regulatory disclaimers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R015	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Query Analytics and AI Performance Monitoring	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on customer query patterns, AI resolution performance, and service quality metrics. Key metrics must include: total query volume by channel, entity, and category; AI first-contact resolution rate versus target of 95%; average resolution time by query type and complexity; escalation rate and reasons; customer satisfaction scores post-resolution; AI confidence score distribution; and false positive/negative rates for intent classification. The analytics dashboard must support drill-down from summary to individual conversation level and must identify trending query topics that may indicate emerging operational issues. Weekly automated reports must be generated for customer service management with AI-generated insights highlighting areas of improvement, training needs for human agents, and knowledge base gaps identified from unresolved or low-confidence queries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R016	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	WhatsApp Business Integration for Gulf Customers	The system must integrate with WhatsApp Business API as a primary customer communication channel, reflecting the strong WhatsApp usage culture in the Gulf region. The integration must support: rich message templates with shipping-specific quick reply buttons (Track Shipment, Request DO, Get Rate Quote, Speak to Agent), media sharing for document exchange (uploading customs clearance copies, photos of damaged cargo), location sharing for delivery coordination, and interactive list messages for menu-driven self-service. The system must maintain separate WhatsApp business numbers per entity to ensure proper branding and data isolation. Message templates must be pre-approved through Meta's template review process in both Arabic and English. The system must handle WhatsApp's 24-hour messaging window policy by transitioning to approved template messages for conversations exceeding the window, with clear opt-in management for marketing and proactive notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R017	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Offline Query Queuing and Graceful Degradation	The system must handle scenarios where backend systems are temporarily unavailable due to maintenance, network issues, or port system downtime. When the AI cannot access real-time data from operational modules, it must clearly inform the customer that live data is temporarily unavailable, provide the most recent cached information with a timestamp indicating data freshness, offer to create a callback request for when systems are restored, and queue the query for automatic resolution once connectivity is restored. The system must maintain a local cache of frequently accessed data (vessel schedules, tariff rates, port procedures) refreshed every 30 minutes during normal operations. Queued queries must be automatically processed in FIFO order upon system restoration, with resolution notifications sent to the customer within 15 minutes of data availability. The system must display a service status indicator showing the health of each integrated backend system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R001	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Sidebar Navigation	Collapsible sidebar: CS ERP logo, company name and logo, primary nav items (one per active module), settings, help. Group modules by category: Commercial, Operations, Finance, Compliance. Badge counts for unread notifications per module. Keyboard shortcut to toggle sidebar. Persist collapsed state in localStorage.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-008-3-004-R018	FEAT-008-3-004	MOD-008	Continuous AI Learning and Feedback Loop	The system must implement a continuous learning pipeline where AI resolution quality improves over time based on agent feedback, customer satisfaction ratings, and resolution outcome data. Human agents must be able to rate AI-suggested responses as helpful, partially helpful, or unhelpful, with optional corrective feedback that is fed back into the training pipeline. Customer post-interaction surveys must collect satisfaction ratings on a 1-5 scale and optional free-text feedback in Arabic or English. The AI model retraining pipeline must run on a configurable schedule (default weekly) incorporating new labeled data from agent corrections and customer feedback. The system must maintain A/B testing capability to evaluate new AI model versions against production models before full deployment. Model performance metrics must be tracked over time with automatic rollback capability if a new model version shows degradation exceeding 3% on any key metric compared to the previous version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R001	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Type Selection and Creation	The system must support creation of all standard Bill of Lading types including Master Bill of Lading (MBL), House Bill of Lading (HBL), Seaway Bill (SWB), Express Release B/L, Through Bill of Lading, Combined Transport B/L, and Switch B/L. Each B/L type must enforce its specific mandatory field set as defined by carrier requirements and ISF regulations. The AI engine must auto-suggest the appropriate B/L type based on the booking details, INCOTERMS (e.g., FOB, CIF, CFR), trade lane (Qatar-India, UAE-KSA), and whether the shipment is FCL or LCL consolidation. Multi-entity support must ensure the correct legal entity letterhead and address appears based on the originating office in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R002	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	AI-Powered B/L Data Auto-Population	The system must leverage AI to auto-populate Bill of Lading fields by extracting data from linked bookings, shipping instructions, commercial invoices, packing lists, and letters of credit. The AI engine must achieve a minimum 95% accuracy rate on shipper/consignee details, cargo descriptions, container numbers, seal numbers, weights, and measurements. It must cross-validate port codes (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INNHA for JNPT) against UN/LOCODE standards. Cargo descriptions must be validated against HS tariff codes relevant to the GCC and Indian customs regimes. The system must flag any discrepancies between booking data and B/L data for human review before finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R003	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Numbering and Reference Management	The system must generate unique B/L numbers following carrier-specific prefix conventions and configurable numbering sequences per entity and trade lane. Each B/L must support multiple reference fields including booking reference, shipper reference, forwarder reference, LC reference, and customs declaration number. The numbering scheme must accommodate multi-entity operations where Qatar entity uses QA-prefix, UAE entity uses AE-prefix, KSA entity uses SA-prefix, and India entity uses IN-prefix. The system must prevent duplicate B/L numbers across all entities and maintain a global registry. AI must auto-link related B/Ls in a master-house hierarchy and support cross-referencing between switch B/L and original B/L chains.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R004	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Multi-Party B/L Approval Workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-step approval workflow for B/L issuance involving shipper verification, documentation team review, compliance check, and management authorization. Each approval step must capture the approver identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and digital signature. The workflow must support delegation rules when primary approvers are unavailable, with configurable escalation timers. For high-value shipments exceeding USD 100,000 equivalent (converted from QAR, AED, SAR, or INR at daily exchange rates), an additional senior management approval gate must be enforced. The AI engine must pre-validate all B/L data against carrier requirements and flag potential issues before the document enters the approval chain.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R005	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Amendment and Correction Processing	The system must track all amendments to issued Bills of Lading with full audit trail including original value, amended value, amendment reason, requestor identity, approver identity, and timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Each amendment must generate a new version number while preserving all historical versions. The system must enforce carrier-specific amendment cut-off rules based on vessel departure times at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Late amendment requests beyond cut-off must trigger carrier penalty fee calculations in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD). The AI must detect amendment patterns that may indicate fraud or compliance risks and flag them for investigation. Amendment fees must be tracked per entity for cost allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R006	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Electronic B/L Integration and Surrender	The system must integrate with electronic B/L platforms including INTTRA, BoleroNet, essDOCS, Wave BL, and carrier-specific e-B/L systems for paperless trade. It must support the full electronic B/L lifecycle including issuance, endorsement, transfer of title, and surrender at destination. The system must maintain compliance with MLETR (Model Law on Electronic Transferable Records) requirements applicable in UAE and the evolving regulations in Qatar, KSA, and India. For traditional paper B/L surrender, the system must track original document location, courier details, and bank routing for LC-backed shipments. The system must support telex release and express release workflows with appropriate authorization controls and must record the surrender method against each B/L for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R007	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Clause and Terms Management	The system must maintain a library of standard B/L clauses including shipped on board notations, clean/claused B/L remarks, deck cargo clauses, dangerous goods declarations, reefer temperature settings, and carrier-specific terms. The AI engine must auto-suggest relevant clauses based on cargo type, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), trade lane regulations, and INCOTERMS selected. Clauses must be available in both English and Arabic with RTL text rendering support for Arabic content. The system must enforce mandatory clause inclusion for specific commodity types such as IMO-classified dangerous goods, perishable cargo requiring temperature control, and oversized/overweight shipments requiring special equipment. Custom clause templates must be configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R002	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Top Header Bar	Fixed header: breadcrumb navigation, global search bar (Cmd+K), notifications bell with unread count badge, language toggle Arabic and English, user avatar menu (profile, settings, switch company, logout). Company name displayed. Currency and timezone shown in corner.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R008	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Freight and Charges on B/L	The system must accurately reflect freight payment terms on the B/L including Freight Prepaid, Freight Collect, and Third Party Freight. All freight amounts must support multi-currency display in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with the currency determined by the trade lane and carrier agreement. The system must calculate and display origin charges, destination charges, THC (Terminal Handling Charges) applicable at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, documentation fees, seal charges, and other surcharges as separate line items or consolidated totals per carrier format requirements. The AI engine must validate freight amounts against contracted rates and flag deviations exceeding configurable thresholds. For LC shipments, freight terms must match the LC requirements exactly to prevent discrepancies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R009	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Container and Cargo Detail Entry on B/L	The system must capture comprehensive container and cargo details on each B/L including container number, size/type (ISO 6346 codes), seal number (carrier seal and shipper seal), gross weight in kilograms, tare weight, net weight, volume in CBM, package count, package type (using UN/CEFACT package type codes), marks and numbers, and cargo description. The AI must validate container numbers against ISO 6346 check-digit algorithms and cross-reference against the booking container list. For LCL consolidation B/Ls, the system must support multiple cargo lots from different shippers within a single container. Weight discrepancies between B/L declared weights and VGM certified weights must be flagged automatically. Reefer containers must include temperature setting, ventilation, humidity, and atmosphere control parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R010	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Print and PDF Generation	The system must generate print-ready B/L documents in PDF format conforming to carrier-specific templates and BIMCO standard layouts. The generated documents must support bilingual output in English and Arabic with proper RTL layout for Arabic text sections, ensuring company names, addresses, and cargo descriptions render correctly in both languages. Watermarks must distinguish between Draft, Original, Copy Non-Negotiable, and Surrender versions. The system must support generating the standard three originals and configurable number of non-negotiable copies. Each printed B/L must include a unique QR code linking to the digital verification endpoint for authenticity validation. Templates must be configurable per entity with appropriate legal entity name, logo, registration details, and regulatory disclaimers for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R011	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Status Tracking and Lifecycle Monitoring	The system must maintain real-time status tracking for each B/L through its complete lifecycle: Draft, Pending Approval, Approved, Issued, In Transit, Arrived at Destination, Surrendered, Accomplished, and Cancelled. Status transitions must enforce business rules such as requiring VGM submission before status can move to Issued for shipments from Hamad Port and Jebel Ali per SOLAS regulations. The AI engine must predict potential delays in B/L processing based on historical patterns and proactively alert documentation teams. A centralized dashboard must display B/L status across all four entities with filtering by port, carrier, vessel, voyage, and date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Status change notifications must be sent to relevant stakeholders via email and in-app alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R012	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Switch B/L and Intermediary Trade Support	The system must support Switch B/L operations where the original B/L is surrendered at an intermediary port and a new B/L is issued with different shipper/consignee details to protect trade confidentiality. The switch B/L workflow must maintain an auditable link between the original and switched documents while restricting visibility based on user roles. Common switch B/L scenarios through Jebel Ali free zone for re-export to East Africa and Indian subcontinent must be supported with appropriate UAE free zone documentation requirements. The system must validate that cargo description, weight, and container details remain consistent between original and switch B/L. Multi-currency freight recalculation must be supported where original freight is in USD and switched freight is in AED or other local currencies based on the new trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R013	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Compliance and Sanctions Screening	The system must perform automated compliance screening on all B/L parties (shipper, consignee, notify party, and any additional parties) against international sanctions lists including OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List, UN Security Council Sanctions, and local watchlists maintained by Qatar Financial Information Unit, UAE Central Bank, SAMA in KSA, and RBI in India. Screening must occur at B/L creation and upon any amendment to party details. The AI engine must apply fuzzy matching algorithms to detect potential matches accounting for Arabic name transliteration variations and common aliases. Any positive or potential match must immediately block B/L issuance and route to the compliance officer for manual review. Full screening audit logs must be maintained for a minimum of seven years per GCC and Indian regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R014	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	Offline B/L Drafting and Synchronization	The system must support offline B/L creation and editing capabilities for scenarios where network connectivity is unavailable at port facilities, on vessel visits, or during field operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Offline-created B/Ls must be stored locally with conflict detection and resolution mechanisms when synchronizing back to the central system. The offline module must cache the most recent shipper/consignee master data, port codes, container prefixes, and clause libraries for a configurable period not exceeding 72 hours. Upon reconnection, the system must perform full validation against current data and flag any conflicts such as container reassignment or booking cancellation that occurred during the offline period. Offline B/Ls must be clearly marked as unverified until synchronization and validation complete successfully.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R015	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Data Analytics and AI Insights	The system must provide AI-driven analytics on B/L operations including average processing time per trade lane, amendment frequency analysis, clause usage patterns, carrier performance metrics, and documentation error rates across all four entities. The analytics dashboard must support drill-down from entity level to individual B/L level with data filterable by port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), carrier, commodity type, and time period in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. The AI engine must identify recurring data quality issues and recommend process improvements to the documentation team. Predictive analytics must forecast B/L processing volumes based on booking trends and seasonal patterns specific to GCC and Indian trade lanes. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual English-Arabic headers and support RTL formatting for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-001-R016	FEAT-009-1-001	MOD-009	B/L Integration with Letter of Credit Requirements	The system must validate B/L data against linked Letter of Credit terms to ensure documentary compliance under UCP 600 and ISBP 745 rules. Validation must cover LC-mandated fields including exact shipper and consignee names, port of loading and discharge matching LC stipulations, latest shipment date compliance, partial shipment and transshipment permissions, and specific document presentation requirements. The AI engine must compare B/L content character-by-character against LC requirements and highlight any discrepancy that could result in LC rejection by the advising or confirming bank. The system must support multiple currencies on the same LC where goods value may be in USD while local charges are in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR. Warnings must be generated for shipments approaching the LC latest shipment date with configurable lead-time thresholds per trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R001	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Auto-Generation from B/L Data	The system must automatically generate cargo manifests by aggregating data from all finalized Bills of Lading for a given vessel, voyage, port of loading, and port of discharge combination. The AI engine must compile cargo details including B/L numbers, shipper and consignee information, cargo descriptions with HS codes, container numbers, weights, and measurements into the manifest format required by each destination port authority. For outward manifests at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must conform to Qatar Customs CDTS and UAE Mirsal-2 formats respectively. For inward manifests at JNPT, the system must generate IGM (Import General Manifest) format per Indian Customs ICEGATE specifications. The manifest must automatically include all B/Ls and exclude cancelled or voided documents with an AI-powered completeness check before submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R002	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Multi-Port Manifest Splitting and Consolidation	The system must support manifest splitting for vessels calling at multiple ports by separating B/Ls by discharge port and generating port-specific sub-manifests. For consolidation cargo (LCL), the system must generate both the master manifest (carrier level) and house manifest (freight forwarder level) with proper linkage between master and house B/L references. The AI engine must handle complex routing scenarios including transshipment cargo at Jebel Ali hub destined for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, or onward connections to Indian subcontinent ports. Manifests must correctly classify cargo as local discharge, transshipment, or in-transit with appropriate customs codes. The system must support manifest preparation for both import and export directions with different data requirements per direction as mandated by Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India customs authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R003	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Customs EDI Manifest Submission Integration	The system must integrate electronically with customs manifest filing systems including Qatar Customs Department CDTS portal, Dubai Trade Mirsal-2 platform, Abu Dhabi Customs TAMM system for Khalifa Port, KSA Customs FASAH platform, and Indian Customs ICEGATE/ICES system for JNPT. Each integration must conform to the specific EDI message standards required: UN/EDIFACT CUSCAR for GCC ports and ICES-compliant XML for Indian customs. The system must handle submission acknowledgments, error responses, and query messages from customs authorities. Retry logic with exponential backoff must be implemented for failed transmissions with a maximum of five retry attempts before alerting the operations team. All manifest submission timestamps must be recorded in UTC with local time zone display for each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R004	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Amendment and Supplementary Filing	The system must support manifest amendments including addition of new B/Ls (supplementary manifest), deletion of B/Ls, and correction of cargo details after initial manifest submission. Each amendment must generate the appropriate EDI amendment message type per customs authority requirements. The system must track amendment cut-off times which vary by port: typically 24 hours before vessel arrival for Hamad Port, 48 hours for Jebel Ali, and as specified by JNPT customs regulations. Late manifest amendments must trigger penalty calculation in the applicable local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India). The AI engine must validate amendment consistency ensuring that cargo totals, container counts, and weight summaries are recalculated accurately after each amendment. A complete amendment history must be maintained with version control.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R005	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Dangerous Goods Manifest Preparation	The system must generate separate Dangerous Goods (DG) manifests compliant with IMDG Code requirements for vessels carrying IMO-classified hazardous cargo. The DG manifest must include UN number, proper shipping name, IMO class and division, packing group, flashpoint, EmS schedule, marine pollutant status, and container stowage position. The AI engine must validate DG cargo against segregation rules ensuring incompatible hazardous materials are not stowed in adjacent containers. DG manifests must be submitted to port authorities at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT within the prescribed timeframes which are typically earlier than general cargo manifest deadlines. The system must cross-reference DG declarations on B/Ls with the DG manifest to ensure consistency and completeness. Emergency contact details for each DG consignment must be included as mandated by SOLAS Chapter VII regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R006	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Reefer Manifest and Temperature Monitoring Integration	The system must generate reefer cargo manifests listing all refrigerated containers with their required temperature settings, ventilation parameters, humidity levels, and controlled atmosphere specifications. The manifest must include commodity-specific temperature ranges validated against carrier and port authority guidelines for perishable cargo commonly transiting GCC and Indian ports including fresh produce, seafood, pharmaceuticals, and chemical compounds. The AI engine must flag any discrepancies between booking temperature instructions and B/L declared temperature settings. For JNPT imports, the system must generate FSSAI (Food Safety and Standards Authority of India) compliant documentation for food-grade reefer cargo. The reefer manifest must integrate with IoT temperature monitoring systems to include pre-shipment temperature verification data where available from smart container devices at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R004	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Claims Registration and Tracking Workflow	The system must provide a structured claims registration workflow allowing users to file cargo damage or loss claims against active insurance policies. Each claim must capture the incident date (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar), location of loss or damage, container number, seal number, survey report references, estimated loss amount in the original policy currency, and photographic evidence as attachments. The AI must auto-classify claim severity (minor damage under 10% of cargo value, major damage 10-50%, total loss above 50%) and route to the appropriate approval authority per the entity's delegation matrix. Claims must track status through stages: Filed, Under Survey, Assessed, Approved, Settled, or Rejected, with full audit trail and SLA timers for each stage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R007	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Validation and AI Error Detection	The system must perform comprehensive validation of manifest data before submission including HS code validity per destination country tariff schedule, container number check-digit verification per ISO 6346, weight consistency between B/L declarations and VGM records, completeness of mandatory fields per customs authority requirements, and format compliance of EDI messages. The AI engine must apply pattern recognition to detect common manifest errors such as mismatched port codes, incorrect container size-type combinations, implausible weight-to-volume ratios, and missing or invalid consignee tax identification numbers (TIN for India, TRN for UAE, CR number for Qatar and KSA). Each validation error must be categorized as critical (blocks submission), warning (allows submission with acknowledgment), or informational. Validation results must be presented in a bilingual English-Arabic interface with RTL support for Arabic error descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R008	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Status Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time manifest status tracking dashboard displaying submission status for all active manifests across all four operational jurisdictions. Status categories must include Draft, Validated, Submitted, Accepted, Rejected, Amended, and Closed. The dashboard must display vessel name, voyage number, port of loading, port of discharge, submission timestamp, customs response status, total B/L count, total container count, and total cargo weight for each manifest. The AI engine must highlight manifests at risk of missing submission deadlines based on vessel ETA and port-specific filing requirements. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, carrier, date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and manifest status. Color-coded alerts must distinguish between on-time, at-risk, and overdue submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R009	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Carrier EDI Manifest Exchange	The system must support EDI manifest exchange with shipping lines using EDIFACT BAPLIE (Bayplan), COPARN (Container Pre-Announcement), and CUSCAR (Customs Cargo Report) message formats. Inbound manifest data received from carriers must be automatically parsed and loaded into the system for verification against house-level B/L data. The system must reconcile carrier manifest data with freight forwarder house manifest data and flag discrepancies for resolution before customs submission. For carriers that provide API-based data exchange instead of traditional EDI, the system must support REST API integration with JSON payload mapping to internal data structures. The AI engine must learn carrier-specific data formatting patterns and automatically apply appropriate parsing rules. All EDI messages must be archived with timestamps for a minimum retention period of seven years per GCC and Indian regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R010	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Transshipment Manifest Handling	The system must manage transshipment manifest requirements for cargo transiting through hub ports, particularly Jebel Ali which serves as a major transshipment hub for the region. Transshipment manifests must clearly identify cargo as in-transit with the original port of loading, transshipment port, and final port of discharge. The system must generate both the discharge manifest at the transshipment port and the subsequent loading manifest for the onward vessel. For cargo transshipping through UAE free zones, the system must comply with UAE Customs transit procedures including bond requirements and time-limited transit permits. The AI engine must automatically match incoming transshipment containers with outbound vessel bookings and flag any unmatched containers that risk becoming overstayed at the transshipment hub, triggering demurrage charges in AED with equivalent conversion to the origin entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R011	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Reconciliation with Port Operations	The system must reconcile manifest data against actual port operations data including gate-in records, container yard positions, vessel loading confirmations, and discharge tallies received from terminal operating systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Discrepancies such as containers listed on manifest but not loaded (short-shipped), containers loaded but not on manifest (over-shipped), and seal number mismatches must be flagged in real-time. The AI engine must analyze historical reconciliation data to predict likely discrepancy patterns per carrier, terminal, and trade lane. The system must generate discrepancy reports categorized by type, severity, and responsible party for resolution. Manifest data must be updated to reflect actual shipped cargo and resubmitted to customs authorities as amended manifest where deviations from the original filing are identified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R012	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Reporting and Regulatory Compliance	The system must generate standard and custom manifest reports required by port authorities, customs agencies, and internal management. Standard reports must include cargo summary by commodity type, container summary by size and type, weight summary for SOLAS compliance verification, dangerous goods summary, and reefer cargo summary. The system must produce port-specific statistical reports including Qatar Planning and Statistics Authority required trade data, UAE Federal Customs Authority periodic filings, KSA General Authority of Customs returns, and India DGCI&S (Directorate General of Commercial Intelligence and Statistics) export-import data submissions. All reports must support bilingual English-Arabic formatting with Arabic headers, column names, and data fields rendered in RTL layout. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation and distribution to designated recipients via email with PDF and Excel attachments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R013	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Data Security and Access Control	The system must enforce role-based access control on manifest data ensuring that users can only view and modify manifests belonging to their assigned entity, port, and trade lane. Sensitive manifest data including consignee details, cargo values, and HS codes must be encrypted at rest and in transit using AES-256 encryption. The system must maintain detailed audit logs of all manifest access, creation, modification, submission, and deletion actions with user identity, timestamp, IP address, and action details. For multi-entity operations, manifest data isolation must prevent Qatar entity users from accessing UAE entity manifests and vice versa unless explicitly granted cross-entity access permissions. The system must comply with Qatar PDPL (Personal Data Protection Law), UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 45/2021, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act 2023 for personal data contained in manifest records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R003	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Global Command Palette	Cmd+K opens searchable command palette. Searches: recent pages, all modules, quick actions (create booking, create invoice, add customer). Keyboard navigable with arrow keys and Enter. Shows keyboard shortcut hints. Results ranked by recency and role-based access. Recent searches persisted per user.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R014	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Offline Manifest Preparation and Queue Management	The system must support offline manifest preparation for scenarios where connectivity to customs EDI systems is unavailable due to network outages, system maintenance windows, or port infrastructure limitations. Manifests prepared offline must be queued for automatic submission once connectivity is restored, with priority ordering based on vessel departure schedule and customs filing deadline proximity. The offline module must cache current vessel schedules, port codes, HS code databases, and customs format specifications for up to 48 hours. The AI engine must validate offline-prepared manifests against cached rules and flag potential issues that can only be fully verified upon reconnection. The queue management interface must display pending submissions count, estimated time to clear queue, and any submissions that exceeded filing deadlines during the offline period. Offline manifest operations must be available across all four jurisdictions with jurisdiction-specific format templates cached locally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R015	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	AI-Driven Manifest Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI-driven anomaly detection on manifest data to identify potentially suspicious or non-compliant cargo declarations before customs submission. The AI engine must analyze cargo descriptions against HS codes to detect misclassification, compare declared weights against typical commodity weight ranges, identify unusual shipper-consignee-commodity combinations, and flag cargo originating from or destined to high-risk jurisdictions per FATF grey list. The anomaly detection must learn from historical manifest data across all four entities to establish baseline patterns for each trade lane. Anomaly scores must be assigned to each B/L on the manifest with configurable thresholds for automatic hold, review, and pass decisions. The system must generate anomaly investigation reports with supporting evidence for the compliance team review. All anomaly detection results must be archived for regulatory examination and must support export in formats acceptable to Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India customs investigation units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-002-R016	FEAT-009-1-002	MOD-009	Manifest Customs Duty Estimation	The system must provide AI-powered customs duty estimation for manifest cargo based on declared HS codes, cargo values, and destination country tariff schedules. Duty calculations must account for applicable preferential trade agreements including GCC Common Customs Law, GCC-India trade arrangements, and bilateral agreements between specific countries. The system must calculate duties in the destination country currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with real-time exchange rate conversion from the declared cargo value currency which may be in USD or other trading currencies. Anti-dumping duties, safeguard duties, and countervailing duties applicable to specific commodity-origin combinations must be included in the estimation. The AI must flag manifest items where duty classification is ambiguous and recommend the most accurate tariff heading based on cargo description analysis and historical classification rulings from each customs authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R001	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	US AMS Filing Automation	The system must automate the preparation and electronic submission of US Customs AMS (Automated Manifest System) filings for all ocean cargo destined to or transiting through United States ports. The AMS filing must include carrier bill data, container and seal information, shipper and consignee details, cargo description with HTS codes, and vessel/voyage information as mandated by US CBP (Customs and Border Protection) 24-hour advance manifest rule. The AI engine must extract required data from B/Ls and bookings for shipments originating from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must submit AMS data via approved ABI (Automated Broker Interface) channels at least 24 hours before vessel departure from the foreign port of loading. Response messages from CBP including status notifications, hold alerts, and Do Not Load orders must be processed automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R002	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	ISF 10+2 Importer Security Filing	The system must support ISF (Importer Security Filing) 10+2 preparation and submission for US-bound ocean cargo. The ten importer data elements must include manufacturer name and address, seller name and address, buyer name and address, ship-to party, container stuffing location, consolidator name and address, importer of record number, consignee number, country of origin, and HTS tariff number at the six-digit level. The two carrier data elements (vessel stow plan and container status messages) must be tracked separately. The AI engine must auto-populate ISF fields from B/L, booking, and purchase order data with cross-validation against AMS filing data. The system must enforce the CBP requirement to file ISF no later than 24 hours before vessel loading at the foreign port. Late filing penalties must be calculated and tracked in USD with equivalent amounts in the local currency of the originating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R003	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	EU ICS2 Pre-Loading Advance Cargo Information	The system must support EU ICS2 (Import Control System 2) filing for all ocean cargo destined to EU member states. ICS2 filings must include the ENS (Entry Summary Declaration) with master and house-level data elements as specified by the European Commission Implementing Regulation. The system must comply with ICS2 Release 3 requirements for maritime containerized cargo including the six-digit HS code, accurate goods description, weight, and complete supply chain actor identification using EORI numbers. The AI engine must validate data completeness against EU customs requirements and flag missing EORI numbers for shippers, consignees, and any intermediate parties. The system must submit ENS data to the EU customs authority of first entry within the prescribed timeline of at least 24 hours before loading at the port of departure. Multiple filing and amendment scenarios must be supported including preliminary, complete, and amendment ENS filings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R004	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Filing Deadline Management and Alerts	The system must track and enforce regulatory filing deadlines for AMS, ISF, and ICS2 across all active shipments. Deadline calculation must be based on vessel departure time from the port of loading (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT) with different lead-time requirements per regulatory regime: 24 hours before loading for AMS, 24 hours before loading for ISF, and 24 hours before loading for ICS2 maritime. The AI engine must proactively alert documentation teams about upcoming filing deadlines at configurable intervals such as 72 hours, 48 hours, and 24 hours before the deadline. Alerts must be priority-ordered by urgency with critical alerts for filings less than six hours from deadline. The system must account for time zone differences between port local times (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India) and UTC when calculating deadlines. Dashboard visualizations must display filing status across all regulatory regimes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R004	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Module Routing and Lazy Loading	Each module is a lazy-loaded route segment. Module shell renders within main content area. Module-level error boundaries so module crash does not crash shell. Loading skeleton during module load. Preserve module scroll position on back navigation. Deep-link support.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R005	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Multi-Regime Simultaneous Filing	The system must support simultaneous filing preparation for shipments that require multiple regulatory submissions such as cargo from Jebel Ali transshipping to both US and EU destinations requiring both AMS/ISF and ICS2 filings. The AI engine must identify all applicable regulatory filing requirements based on the shipment routing, ports of call, and final destination country. Data entered once for a B/L must be automatically mapped to the specific field requirements of each regulatory regime without requiring redundant data entry. The system must handle scenarios where a single container has cargo destined for multiple regulatory jurisdictions requiring split filings. Cross-validation between simultaneous filings must ensure data consistency across AMS, ISF, and ICS2 submissions for the same shipment. The system must maintain separate status tracking per regulatory filing while providing a consolidated view of all filings related to a single B/L or booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R006	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Regulatory Response Processing and Hold Management	The system must process and act upon response messages from regulatory authorities including US CBP AMS status notifications (Accept, Reject, Hold, Do Not Load), ISF acceptance and rejection notices, and ICS2 ENS risk assessment results (No Action, Request for Additional Data, Do Not Load). For Do Not Load (DNL) orders, the system must immediately alert operations teams at the relevant port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), update the B/L status to Hold, and block the container from terminal gate-out or vessel loading workflows. The AI engine must analyze rejection reasons and suggest corrective data for resubmission. Hold and DNL statuses must be visible across all related documents and workflows with appropriate access controls. Response message history must be maintained in full for audit purposes with timestamps in UTC and local port time zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R007	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	AMS ISF ICS2 Amendment Filing	The system must support amendment filings for all three regulatory regimes when cargo data changes after initial submission. For AMS amendments, the system must submit updated CUSCAR messages via ABI with the correct amendment reason code. For ISF amendments, the system must file updated ISF data reflecting changes to any of the ten importer data elements while maintaining the original ISF transaction number. For ICS2, the system must submit amendment ENS filings with proper referencing to the original filing MRN (Movement Reference Number). The AI engine must detect data changes in B/Ls that impact previously submitted regulatory filings and automatically queue amendment filings. Amendment deadlines must be enforced: AMS amendments are accepted until 24 hours after vessel arrival at US port, ISF amendments are flexible but must be filed before cargo arrival, and ICS2 amendments follow EU customs operational timelines. Each amendment must be logged with before-and-after data values for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R008	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Party Data Validation for Regulatory Filings	The system must validate all party data included in regulatory filings against authoritative reference databases. For AMS and ISF, US importer of record must be validated against active CBP importer numbers. For ICS2, all EU-based parties must have valid EORI numbers verified against the EU EORI validation service. The AI engine must validate party addresses against known formats for US ZIP codes, EU postal codes, and origin country address conventions for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Manufacturer and seller data for ISF filings must include full legal entity names and complete addresses with street, city, state/province, postal code, and country as per CBP requirements. The system must maintain a validated party master database that caches verification results to reduce repeated external lookups. Party data that fails validation must block the filing and generate a remediation task for the documentation team with specific instructions on what data elements need correction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R009	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	HS Code and Commodity Classification for Filings	The system must ensure accurate HS code classification for all regulatory filings with jurisdiction-specific requirements: six-digit HS code for ICS2, HTS ten-digit code for AMS/ISF US filings, and appropriate national tariff line codes. The AI engine must analyze cargo descriptions from B/Ls and commercial invoices to suggest the most accurate HS code classification using natural language processing trained on GCC and Indian trade commodity databases. The system must maintain harmonized tariff databases for US HTS, EU Combined Nomenclature, Qatar Customs Tariff, UAE Customs Tariff, KSA Customs Tariff, and India Customs Tariff with regular update synchronization. When B/L cargo descriptions are generic or ambiguous, the AI must flag items for manual classification review and provide the top three recommended codes with confidence scores. The system must track HS code classification rulings from previous shipments of the same commodity to ensure consistency across filings and reduce customs examination risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R010	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Regulatory Filing Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all regulatory filings including initial submissions, amendments, responses received, holds, releases, and final disposition. The audit trail must record the exact data transmitted, submission timestamp in UTC, response received, user who initiated the filing, and any AI-suggested changes that were accepted or overridden by the user. Compliance reports must be generated showing filing timeliness metrics (percentage of on-time filings per regulatory regime), rejection rates with root cause analysis, hold and DNL frequency analysis, and amendment statistics. The reports must be segmented by originating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port of loading, carrier, and trade lane. All audit data must be retained for a minimum of seven years per US CBP, EU customs, and GCC regulatory record retention requirements. Reports must support bilingual English-Arabic output with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R011	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Carrier SCAC and Vessel Validation for AMS	The system must validate carrier SCAC (Standard Carrier Alpha Code) codes and vessel information against US CBP registered carrier databases for AMS filings. Vessel names must match the exact format registered with CBP including official IMO number, vessel flag state, and call sign. The AI engine must auto-resolve common carrier name variations and abbreviations to the correct SCAC code and registered vessel name. For feeder vessel connections and transshipment scenarios where cargo is transferred between vessels at Jebel Ali or other hub ports, the system must file AMS data for the final mother vessel carrying cargo to the US port. The system must handle vessel substitution scenarios where the planned vessel changes after AMS filing by automatically generating an amendment filing with the updated vessel details. A carrier master database must be maintained with current SCAC codes, vessel registries, and trade lane mappings updated at minimum weekly from CBP and carrier data sources.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R012	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Container Stuffing Location Tracking for ISF	The system must capture and validate container stuffing location data as required for ISF Element 7 (container stuffing location). The system must record the physical address where each container was loaded including facility name, street address, city, country, and postal code. For LCL consolidation cargo, the CFS (Container Freight Station) address at the origin port must be recorded. The AI engine must auto-populate stuffing location from warehouse and CFS master data when the stuffing facility is a known location within the system. For shipper-stuffed FCL containers, the system must capture the factory or warehouse address from the shipper's booking instructions. Stuffing locations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India must include country-specific address formatting with Arabic transliteration where applicable. The system must validate that stuffing location data is complete and formatted per CBP requirements before allowing ISF submission, rejecting submissions with incomplete address fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R013	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	ICS2 Supply Chain Actor Registration	The system must manage supply chain actor registration and identification numbers required for ICS2 filings including EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) numbers for EU-based entities and Third Country Trader Identification Numbers for non-EU entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must validate EORI numbers in real-time against the EU EORI validation service API before including them in ENS filings. For non-EU shippers and consignees without EORI numbers, the system must generate appropriate third-country identifiers using the prescribed format. The AI engine must maintain a mapping between internal customer master records and their respective regulatory identification numbers across different regimes. The system must alert when customer registrations are expiring or need renewal and must support the EORI application process by pre-populating application forms with data from the customer master. Multi-entity support must ensure that each entity maintains its own set of regulatory registrations with the appropriate customs authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R014	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Offline Regulatory Filing Queue and Prioritization	The system must support offline preparation of AMS, ISF, and ICS2 filings when connectivity to regulatory submission portals is unavailable. Filings prepared offline must be validated against locally cached regulatory rules and queued for submission in priority order based on filing deadline proximity. The offline queue must display estimated time to deadline for each pending filing and highlight filings at risk of missing their regulatory window. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must automatically submit queued filings in priority order with the most urgent deadlines processed first. The AI engine must recalculate priorities dynamically as vessel schedules change during the offline period. Offline mode must cache the most recent carrier SCAC codes, vessel schedules, HS code databases, and regulatory format specifications from all three regimes. The system must log all offline operations including preparation time, queue duration, and actual submission time for compliance audit purposes across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R015	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	AI Risk Scoring for Regulatory Examination	The system must apply AI-driven risk scoring to predict the likelihood of customs examination or hold for each regulatory filing. Risk factors must include cargo commodity type, origin-destination trade lane, shipper and consignee history, container stuffing location risk profile, HS code sensitivity level, and historical examination rates for similar shipments. The AI model must be trained on historical filing outcomes from shipments originating at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Risk scores must be presented on a scale of 1-100 with configurable thresholds for low, medium, and high risk categories. High-risk filings must trigger enhanced documentation preparation including additional supporting documents such as commercial invoices, certificates of origin, and inspection certificates. The risk scoring must be available before filing submission to allow documentation teams to proactively address potential issues. Risk analytics must be available per entity with trend analysis showing risk profile changes over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R016	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	Regulatory Filing Fee Tracking and Allocation	The system must track all costs associated with regulatory filings including AMS filing fees, ISF bond costs, ICS2 submission fees where applicable, and any penalty charges for late or inaccurate filings. Filing fees must be recorded in the currency charged by the regulatory authority (USD for AMS/ISF, EUR for ICS2) and converted to the local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) at the prevailing exchange rate on the filing date. The system must allocate filing costs to the appropriate B/L, booking, or customer account based on configurable cost allocation rules. The AI engine must analyze filing cost trends and identify opportunities for cost optimization such as consolidating ISF filings or improving data quality to reduce amendment fees. Monthly and quarterly fee reports must be generated per entity with breakdowns by regulatory regime, filing type, carrier, and trade lane. The system must support automated invoice generation for filing costs that are to be charged back to customers per the applicable INCOTERMS and service agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-003-R017	FEAT-009-1-003	MOD-009	GCC and India Domestic Regulatory Filings	The system must support domestic regulatory filings beyond AMS, ISF, and ICS2 that are required for container shipments in the operating jurisdictions. For Qatar, the system must support Qatar Customs advance cargo declaration and MOFA attestation requirements. For UAE, the system must handle Dubai Trade filing requirements, DMCA (Dubai Maritime City Authority) notifications, and Abu Dhabi TAMM submissions for Khalifa Port operations. For KSA, the system must support FASAH platform filings and Saudi FDA clearance submissions for food and pharmaceutical cargo. For India, the system must generate ICEGATE filings including IGM, EGM, Shipping Bill, and Bill of Entry data components as applicable. The AI engine must determine all applicable domestic filings based on the cargo type, origin, destination, and regulatory classification. Each domestic filing must follow the specific electronic format, submission channel, and timeline requirements of the respective national authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R005	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Multi-Entity Insurance Provider Management	The system must maintain a registry of approved insurance providers per entity, as each operating entity in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India will have different local insurance partners due to regulatory requirements. Each provider record must store the company name in Arabic and English, license number, regulatory authority, coverage limits per shipment and aggregate annual limits, commission rates, settlement terms, and API integration credentials where available. The system must enforce that only locally licensed insurers are selectable for policies originating from that entity's jurisdiction. Provider performance dashboards must track claims settlement ratios, average settlement time, and dispute frequency to support AI-driven provider recommendations for new policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R001	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Declaration Creation and Method Selection	The system must support creation of VGM (Verified Gross Mass) declarations for all export containers in compliance with SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 as amended. Both VGM determination methods must be supported: Method 1 (weighing the packed container on a calibrated and certified weighbridge) and Method 2 (weighing all packages and cargo items, adding dunnage, bracing, and other securing material weights, and adding the container tare weight). The AI engine must recommend the appropriate method based on container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20OT, 40FR, 20RF, 40RF), cargo type, available weighing facilities at the stuffing location, and the port of loading requirements. For Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, port authority preferences for Method 1 verification must be highlighted. The system must capture the authorized signatory details (name, title, company) as required by SOLAS regulation for legal accountability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R002	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	Weighbridge Integration and Automated Data Capture	The system must integrate with certified weighbridge systems at ports, container freight stations, and shipper facilities to automatically capture container weight data for VGM Method 1 declarations. Integration must support common weighbridge protocols including serial data output, API-based data exchange, and manual weight ticket data entry as fallback. For Hamad Port, the system must integrate with Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani) weighbridge systems. For Jebel Ali, integration with DP World terminal weighbridge infrastructure must be supported. For Khalifa Port, Abu Dhabi Ports weighbridge integration must be implemented. For JNPT, integration with the JNPT container weighment facility systems must be supported. Each weight reading must be captured with weighbridge identification number, calibration certificate validity date, weight value in kilograms, and timestamp. The AI engine must detect anomalous weight readings that deviate significantly from expected ranges based on container type and declared cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R003	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Method 2 Calculation Engine	The system must provide a detailed calculation engine for VGM Method 2 that allows itemized entry of all cargo components including individual package weights, palletization materials, dunnage, lashing and securing materials, and any other load-securing devices. The container tare weight must be automatically populated from the container master database using the container number with validation against the tare weight stenciled on the container. The AI engine must cross-validate the total calculated VGM against the cargo details on the corresponding B/L and booking to detect discrepancies. Weight conversion between units (kilograms, metric tonnes, pounds) must be supported with the final VGM always recorded in kilograms as per SOLAS requirements. For LCL consolidation containers, Method 2 must aggregate weights from multiple consignments with clear traceability to each HBL. The system must maintain detailed weight breakdown records for audit purposes as required by maritime authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R004	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Tolerance and Discrepancy Management	The system must enforce VGM weight tolerance thresholds as specified by each port authority and carrier, which typically allow a variance of up to 5% between VGM declared weight and actual terminal weighment. Tolerance thresholds must be configurable per port: Hamad Port tolerance per Qatar Customs directive, Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port tolerance per UAE Federal Transport Authority regulations, and JNPT tolerance per Indian Directorate General of Shipping circulars. When VGM weight exceeds the tolerance threshold compared to terminal weighment, the system must automatically flag the container and block loading pending resolution. The AI engine must analyze weight discrepancy patterns per shipper, commodity type, and stuffing location to identify systematic under-declaration or over-declaration trends. Discrepancy resolution workflows must capture the investigation outcome, corrective action taken, and updated VGM value with full audit trail. Persistent offenders must be flagged in the shipper profile for enhanced scrutiny on future shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R005	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	Electronic VGM Submission to Carriers and Terminals	The system must submit VGM declarations electronically to shipping lines and terminal operators using industry-standard formats including VERMAS EDIFACT messages, carrier-specific API endpoints, and web portal submissions. For major carriers operating at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must support carrier-specific VGM submission portals including Maersk, MSC, CMA-CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, and COSCO VGM systems. The system must handle submission acknowledgments, rejections, and queries from carriers and terminals. VGM must be submitted before the carrier's VGM cut-off time which is typically 24 to 72 hours before vessel departure depending on the carrier and port. The AI engine must track VGM cut-off times per carrier and vessel and alert operations teams about approaching deadlines. Submission status must be tracked per container with clear indication of accepted, pending, and rejected VGM declarations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R006	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Cut-Off Time Management and Alerts	The system must maintain a database of VGM submission cut-off times per carrier, port of loading, and service route. Cut-off times must be automatically populated from carrier schedule data where available and manually configurable for carriers that do not provide electronic schedule feeds. The AI engine must calculate the effective VGM deadline for each container based on the booking carrier, vessel, voyage, and port of loading (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT). Proactive alerts must be sent to shippers and operations teams at configurable intervals: 72 hours, 48 hours, 24 hours, and 6 hours before the VGM cut-off. Containers without submitted VGM as of the cut-off time must be automatically flagged as at risk of being rolled to the next vessel. The system must calculate the financial impact of potential container rollovers including additional storage charges in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and potential late shipment penalties against LC deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R007	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Responsible Party Management	The system must track and manage the responsible party for VGM submission which may be the shipper, freight forwarder, or terminal operator depending on the contractual arrangement and INCOTERMS. For EXW and FCA shipments, VGM responsibility typically falls on the buyer's freight forwarder. For FOB, CFR, and CIF shipments originating from Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India, VGM responsibility typically falls on the shipper or their appointed agent. The system must clearly assign VGM responsibility at the booking level and track whether the responsible party has fulfilled their obligation. The AI engine must determine the default VGM responsible party based on INCOTERMS and booking party roles, while allowing manual override. Reminder notifications must be directed to the responsible party with escalation to the booking party if the VGM deadline is approaching without submission. The system must support scenarios where VGM responsibility is transferred between parties with proper handoff documentation and timestamp tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R008	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Certificate Generation and Document Management	The system must generate VGM certificates in PDF format containing all mandatory information per SOLAS requirements including container number, VGM value in kilograms, VGM determination method (Method 1 or Method 2), weighing equipment identification (for Method 1), authorized signatory name and title, shipper name and address, and date of VGM determination. Certificates must be available in bilingual English-Arabic format with proper RTL layout for Arabic content. The system must support electronic signature on VGM certificates using digital certificates issued by recognized certification authorities. For Method 1, the VGM certificate must include the weighbridge calibration certificate reference number and its validity period. All VGM certificates must be stored with the associated B/L and booking records and be retrievable for a minimum of three years per SOLAS record retention requirements. The system must generate batch VGM certificates for multiple containers under the same booking or B/L.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R009	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	Container Tare Weight Database and Verification	The system must maintain a comprehensive container tare weight database sourced from container owner/operator master data, BIC (Bureau International des Containers) reference databases, and actual tare weights stenciled on containers. Tare weights must be recorded per container number and validated against ISO 6346 standards for maximum gross weight by container type: 20GP maximum 30,480 kg, 40GP maximum 30,480 kg, 40HC maximum 30,480 kg. The AI engine must flag containers where the database tare weight differs from the stenciled tare weight by more than 200 kg, which may indicate container modification or database error. For Method 2 VGM calculations, the container tare weight must be sourced from the most reliable available data with priority given to the actual stenciled weight verified at the container depot. The system must track tare weight discrepancies and maintain a correction log. Container tare weight data must be accessible offline for Method 2 calculations at shipper facilities without continuous network connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R010	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Compliance Dashboard and Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time VGM compliance dashboard showing the VGM submission status for all active export containers across all four port locations. The dashboard must display key metrics including total containers pending VGM, containers with VGM submitted and accepted, containers with VGM rejected, containers at risk of missing VGM cut-off, and containers blocked from loading due to VGM non-compliance. Filtering must be available by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port of loading, carrier, vessel/voyage, booking date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and shipper. The AI engine must provide predictive analytics on VGM compliance rates by shipper, trade lane, and commodity type to identify areas requiring process improvement. The dashboard must include drill-down capability from aggregate statistics to individual container VGM details. Color-coded status indicators must visually distinguish between compliant, pending, at-risk, and non-compliant containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R011	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Override Weight for Special Equipment	The system must handle VGM requirements for special container equipment types including flat racks (20FR, 40FR), open tops (20OT, 40OT), platform containers, and tank containers where standard weighing procedures may not be applicable. For oversize cargo on flat racks that cannot be weighed on standard weighbridges, the system must support Method 2 with detailed component weight documentation including the cargo piece weight from manufacturer certificates, lashing equipment weight, and timber dunnage calculations. The AI engine must validate that the total VGM does not exceed the maximum payload for the specific equipment type accounting for the higher tare weights of special equipment. For tank containers, the system must calculate VGM based on liquid density, fill level, and tank tare weight with temperature correction factors where applicable. The system must maintain equipment-specific weight limits per carrier and port, as certain ports such as JNPT have road weight restrictions that impose lower practical limits than the container's structural capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R012	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Integration with Booking and B/L Workflows	The system must integrate VGM management with the booking and B/L lifecycle ensuring that VGM data flows seamlessly between operational modules. VGM weight must be automatically cross-referenced with the gross weight declared on the corresponding B/L, and discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerance must block B/L finalization. The system must enforce that VGM is submitted and accepted before the B/L status can transition from Draft to Issued for export shipments from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The AI engine must validate that VGM weight is consistent with the booking's declared cargo weight and flag any significant variance that might indicate incorrect cargo loading. For bookings with multiple containers, the system must track VGM submission status per container and display aggregate completion percentage. VGM data must be included in manifest preparation workflows to ensure manifest weight declarations align with verified weights.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R013	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Penalty and Non-Compliance Cost Tracking	The system must track and calculate penalties and costs associated with VGM non-compliance including carrier late VGM submission surcharges, terminal re-weighment charges, container rollover costs due to VGM cut-off miss, and storage charges for containers held pending VGM resolution. Penalty amounts must be recorded in the applicable currency: USD for carrier surcharges, QAR for Hamad Port charges, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port charges, and INR for JNPT charges. The AI engine must calculate total VGM-related costs per shipment, per shipper, and per entity for management reporting. The system must support automated charge-back of VGM penalties to the responsible party (shipper, forwarder, or agent) based on the VGM responsibility assignment. Cost reports must compare VGM penalty trends across periods, entities, and trade lanes to identify operational improvement opportunities. Multi-currency consolidation for enterprise-wide VGM cost analysis must convert all amounts to a base reporting currency (USD) at period-end exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R006	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Automatic Coverage Validation Against INCOTERMS	The system must automatically validate insurance coverage requirements based on the INCOTERMS specified in the sales contract or booking. For CIF and CIP terms, the system must ensure the seller has arranged minimum coverage per ICC clause requirements (110% of CIF value for CIF terms, Institute Cargo Clauses A minimum for CIP under INCOTERMS 2020). For FOB, FCA, and EXW terms, the system must alert the buyer's entity that insurance responsibility falls on them and prompt for coverage arrangement. The AI must detect mismatches between the INCOTERMS, insurance coverage scope, and the party responsible for arranging insurance, generating warnings before shipment departure. All validation messages must display in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R014	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	Offline VGM Capture and Synchronization	The system must support offline VGM data capture for scenarios at shipper factories, remote stuffing locations, and container yards where network connectivity is unreliable. The offline module must support both Method 1 weight entry from weighbridge ticket data and Method 2 itemized weight calculation with all component weights. Container tare weight data must be cached locally for Method 2 calculations covering at minimum the containers allocated to current active bookings. The AI engine must perform local validation of VGM data against cached business rules including weight tolerance checks and container-type weight limits. Upon network restoration, offline VGM records must synchronize with the central system with conflict detection for scenarios where the same container's VGM was entered by multiple parties during the offline period. The synchronization process must verify the VGM submission status with carriers and update the local status accordingly. All offline VGM entries must be clearly tagged with offline-entry metadata for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R015	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive VGM reports and analytics including VGM submission timeliness rates per shipper and forwarder, Method 1 versus Method 2 usage distribution by port, weight discrepancy frequency and magnitude analysis, carrier rejection rate analysis, and VGM-related cost impact summaries. The AI engine must identify trends in VGM data quality by analyzing historical submission accuracy, rejection rates, and discrepancy patterns across all four entities operating at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Predictive analytics must forecast potential VGM compliance issues for upcoming vessel sailings based on shipper historical performance and current submission progress. Reports must be available in bilingual English-Arabic format with RTL support for Arabic headers, labels, and narrative content. Standard report schedules must support daily operational reports, weekly management summaries, and monthly compliance reviews exportable in PDF and Excel formats with charts and visualizations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R016	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Shipper Self-Service Portal	The system must provide a web-based self-service portal for shippers and their agents to submit VGM declarations directly without requiring internal operations team intervention. The portal must support VGM submission for both Method 1 and Method 2 with guided data entry workflows that enforce mandatory fields and validate data in real-time. Shippers must authenticate using secure credentials linked to their customer profile in the system. The portal must display the shipper's active bookings with pending VGM requirements, VGM cut-off deadlines per container, and historical VGM submission records. The self-service portal must support Arabic and English language selection with full RTL support for Arabic interface. Mobile-responsive design must ensure the portal is accessible from tablets and smartphones used at stuffing locations and weighbridge facilities. The AI engine must pre-populate VGM fields from booking data and provide real-time validation feedback. The portal must support bulk VGM upload via CSV or Excel template for shippers submitting VGM for multiple containers simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-1-004-R017	FEAT-009-1-004	MOD-009	VGM Weighing Equipment Certification Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of certified weighing equipment used for Method 1 VGM determinations including weighbridge identification number, location, maximum capacity, minimum division, calibration certificate number, certification authority, calibration date, and next calibration due date. The system must validate that any Method 1 VGM declaration references a weighbridge whose calibration certificate is current and valid at the date of weighment. The AI engine must alert operations teams when weighbridge calibration certificates are approaching expiration, ensuring continuous compliance with national metrology standards in Qatar (QS/ISO 17025), UAE (ESMA regulations), KSA (SASO requirements), and India (Legal Metrology Act provisions). For third-party weighbridges used at shipper facilities, the system must maintain uploaded copies of calibration certificates and verify them during VGM validation. VGM declarations referencing expired or unregistered weighing equipment must be rejected with a clear error message directing the user to select a certified alternative facility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R001	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Marine Cargo Insurance Policy Creation	The system must support creation of marine cargo insurance policies for individual shipments or open-cover blanket policies. Each policy must capture insured party details, voyage information (origin port code such as QAHAD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT), container numbers, cargo description including HS codes, declared cargo value in the transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), INCOTERMS basis (CIF, CIP, FOB, EXW, etc.), and coverage type (Institute Cargo Clauses A, B, or C). The AI engine must auto-populate 90% of fields from linked booking or Bill of Lading data, reducing manual entry. Policies must support both Arabic and English text for insured party names and cargo descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R002	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	AI-Driven Premium Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate insurance premiums based on cargo type, HS code risk classification, trade lane risk profile, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), voyage duration, historical claims ratio for the route, and seasonal risk factors such as monsoon periods for Indian Ocean routes. Premium rates must be configurable per insurance provider and per entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India). The AI engine must learn from historical claims data to adjust risk scoring, flagging high-risk shipments that exceed the 110% CIF standard insurable value. All premium amounts must be calculated in the policy currency and simultaneously converted to the entity's base currency using the daily exchange rate from the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R003	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Insurance Certificate Generation and Distribution	The system must generate insurance certificates compliant with Institute of London Underwriters standards and local regulatory requirements for each jurisdiction (Qatar Central Bank insurance regulations, UAE Insurance Authority standards, SAMA regulations for KSA, and IRDAI guidelines for India). Certificates must be produced in both Arabic and English with proper RTL layout for Arabic versions, including bilingual cargo descriptions and party names. The certificate must include a unique reference number, QR code for verification, digital signature of the authorized signatory, and must be distributable via email, API integration with port community systems, and downloadable as PDF. Certificates must reference the underlying Bill of Lading number and booking reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R005	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Notification Centre	Slide-out notification panel. Notifications: system alerts, approval requests, document ready, AI escalations, due invoices, custom reminders. Mark individual or all as read. Filter by type. Notification preferences per user. Real-time via WebSocket with 30s polling fallback. Max 500 unread before auto-archive.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R007	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Insurance Document Attachment to Shipment Dossier	The system must automatically link insurance certificates and related documents (survey reports, claims correspondence, settlement letters) to the master shipment dossier. Each document must be indexed by shipment reference, Bill of Lading number, container number, and policy number for cross-referencing. When a Letter of Credit requires insurance documentation, the system must validate that the insurance certificate meets the LC terms (coverage amount, risks covered, certificate date relative to shipment date) and flag discrepancies before document presentation to the advising bank. The document repository must support versioning, with each amendment to a policy generating a new version while retaining the full history. Documents must be accessible offline for field operations at port terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R008	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Reefer Container Special Insurance Handling	The system must handle specialized insurance requirements for refrigerated containers (20RF, 40RF, 40RHHC) carrying temperature-sensitive cargo such as food products, pharmaceuticals, and chemicals. Insurance policies for reefer shipments must capture acceptable temperature range, genset fuel autonomy requirements, pre-cooling verification status, and temperature monitoring device details. The AI must calculate enhanced premium rates for reefer cargo reflecting the higher risk of spoilage and mechanical breakdown. Claims for reefer cargo must capture temperature deviation logs from IoT sensors as mandatory evidence. The system must integrate with container tracking modules to trigger automatic incident notifications to insurers when temperature excursions are detected during transit between ports like QAHAD and INJNP.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R009	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Insurance Expiry and Renewal Notifications	The system must proactively monitor open-cover and annual blanket insurance policies for upcoming expiration and trigger renewal workflows. AI-driven notifications must be sent to the risk management team at configurable intervals (default: 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry) via email and in-app alerts in the user's preferred language. The renewal workflow must pre-populate a new policy proposal using the expiring policy terms, adjusted for updated cargo volume forecasts, claims history over the policy period, and any changes in trade lane risk profiles. The system must prevent new shipment bookings from being confirmed against an expired or lapsed insurance policy when insurance is mandatory per the INCOTERMS. Notifications must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R010	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	War Risk and P&I Club Supplementary Coverage	The system must support supplementary insurance coverage types including War Risk insurance and Protection & Indemnity (P&I) Club entries for vessels transiting high-risk zones such as the Gulf of Aden, Strait of Hormuz, and Red Sea corridor. War Risk premiums must be tracked separately from standard marine cargo insurance and must be recalculated when the Joint War Committee (JWC) listed areas are updated. The system must automatically flag shipments whose routing passes through JWC-listed zones and prompt for additional War Risk coverage before vessel departure. P&I Club membership details for vessel operators must be stored and validated against the International Group of P&I Clubs registry. All supplementary coverage costs must flow to the shipment cost allocation module for accurate profitability analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R011	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Insurance Premium Accounting Integration	The system must generate accounting entries for insurance premiums payable, prepaid insurance, claims receivable, and claims income or loss upon settlement. Each accounting entry must be tagged with the correct entity code (QA, AE, SA, IN), cost center, and shipment reference for multi-entity consolidation reporting. Premium payments must integrate with the accounts payable module, supporting scheduled installment payments for annual policies. Currency conversion differences between the policy currency and the entity's functional currency must be recorded as foreign exchange gains or losses per IAS 21 and IFRS 9 standards. The system must support insurance premium allocation across multiple containers within a single shipment based on declared cargo value proportions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R012	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Cargo Valuation and Under-Insurance Detection	The AI engine must analyze declared cargo values against market benchmarks, historical shipment values for similar HS codes, and commodity price indices to detect potential under-insurance. When the declared value falls below 70% of the AI-estimated market value, the system must generate a warning to the operations team recommending value verification before policy issuance. The system must maintain a commodity valuation database updated from external market feeds for major traded goods transiting Gulf and Indian subcontinent ports. Under-insurance detection must account for currency fluctuations, as a shipment insured in INR may become under-insured if the rupee depreciates significantly against USD between policy issuance and shipment date. Detection thresholds must be configurable per entity and per cargo category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R013	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Surveyor Assignment and Report Management	The system must maintain a panel of approved cargo surveyors per port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and other configured ports) and automatically assign the nearest available surveyor when a claim is filed or pre-shipment inspection is required. Surveyor profiles must include qualifications, certifications, specializations (dry cargo, reefer, hazardous goods, bulk), availability calendar, and response time SLA. The AI must recommend the optimal surveyor based on cargo type, claim complexity, surveyor track record, and proximity to the incident location. Survey reports must be uploadable in PDF format with structured data extraction by the AI to populate claim assessment fields. Surveyor fees must be tracked per claim and allocated to the appropriate cost center.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R014	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	General Average and Salvage Contribution Tracking	The system must handle General Average (GA) declarations per York-Antwerp Rules, tracking contribution obligations for cargo interests on affected vessels. When a GA event is declared, the system must identify all shipments and containers on the affected vessel, calculate provisional GA contribution based on declared cargo values, and generate GA guarantee letters or GA bonds for submission to the average adjuster. The system must track GA deposit payments in the appropriate currency, monitor the adjustment process timeline which can span multiple years, and record final settlement amounts. All GA-related financial entries must be segregated in the accounting system with specific GL codes. The system must support Arabic language correspondence with local GA adjusters in Qatar and UAE jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R006	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Dashboard Home Page	Personalised home with greeting, date and day. Widget grid (drag-to-rearrange, save layout per user): KPI cards (booked TEUs today, revenue MTD, pending approvals, open disputes), recent activity feed, quick-action buttons (most used per user), AI insights card (anomalies, recommendations). Mobile responsive.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R015	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Insurance Regulatory Compliance Reporting	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for insurance activities as required by each jurisdiction's insurance regulator: Qatar Central Bank (QCB) for Qatar operations, Central Bank of UAE and Insurance Authority for UAE, Saudi Central Bank (SAMA) for KSA, and IRDAI for India. Reports must include aggregate insured values, premium volumes by provider, claims ratios, outstanding claims reserves, and large loss notifications exceeding jurisdiction-specific thresholds. Report formats must comply with each regulator's prescribed templates, supporting both Arabic and English submissions. The AI must monitor regulatory requirement changes and flag upcoming reporting deadlines based on each entity's fiscal calendar. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and XML formats as required by the respective regulatory portals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-001-R016	FEAT-009-2-001	MOD-009	Offline Insurance Document Access for Port Operations	The system must provide offline access to insurance certificates, policy summaries, and claims status for port operations staff working at terminal facilities where network connectivity may be intermittent. The offline cache must synchronize automatically when connectivity is restored, uploading any claims initiated offline and downloading updated policy statuses. Offline-capable documents must include a cryptographic hash for integrity verification, preventing tampering during the offline period. The offline mode must support viewing insurance documents in both Arabic and English, with proper RTL rendering for Arabic content. Field staff at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT must be able to verify insurance coverage validity by scanning the container or BL number even without network access, using locally cached policy data refreshed at configurable intervals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R001	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Declaration Auto-Generation from Shipment Data	The system must automatically generate customs import and export declarations by extracting data from the Bill of Lading, commercial invoice, packing list, and booking confirmation. The AI engine must populate all mandatory fields required by the destination customs authority including consignee details, HS tariff codes, cargo descriptions in both Arabic and English, declared values in the invoice currency with conversion to the local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), container numbers, weights (gross and net), and package counts. The auto-generation must achieve at least 95% field accuracy measured against manually prepared declarations. Draft declarations must be presented for human review before electronic submission, with AI-highlighted fields that have lower confidence scores requiring verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R002	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	AI-Powered HS Code Classification Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven Harmonized System (HS) code classification engine that recommends the most appropriate 8-digit or 10-digit tariff codes based on cargo description, commercial invoice line items, product specifications, and historical classification decisions. The engine must support the GCC Common External Tariff for Qatar, UAE, and KSA declarations, and the Indian Trade Classification (ITC-HS) for JNPT declarations. Classification suggestions must include confidence scores and alternative codes with duty rate implications. The AI must learn from customs authority rulings and binding tariff information decisions to improve accuracy over time. When multiple valid classifications exist with different duty rates, the system must present all options with a risk assessment for each, ensuring compliance with WCO classification guidelines and preventing intentional misclassification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R003	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Multi-Country Electronic Filing Integration	The system must integrate electronically with customs filing systems across all operating jurisdictions: Qatar Customs Al Nadeeb single-window platform, Dubai Trade portal and Mirsal 2 system, Saudi FASAH single-window system operated by ZATCA, and Indian Customs ICEGATE EDI system. Each integration must handle the jurisdiction-specific message formats (UN/EDIFACT, XML schemas, or REST APIs as prescribed), authentication protocols, digital signature requirements, and response processing including acceptance notifications, query messages, and rejection reasons. The system must maintain integration health monitoring with automatic retry logic for transient failures and immediate alert escalation for persistent errors. Filing timestamps must be recorded in the local timezone of the customs authority and converted to UTC for internal tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R004	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Duty and Tax Calculation Engine	The system must calculate customs duties, taxes, and fees applicable to each shipment based on the HS code, origin country, declared value, applicable trade agreements, and any anti-dumping or countervailing duties in effect. For GCC countries (Qatar, UAE, KSA), the system must apply the GCC Common Customs Tariff rate (standard 5% with exceptions) plus any applicable VAT (5% for UAE and KSA, 0% for most goods in Qatar). For India, the system must calculate Basic Customs Duty, Social Welfare Surcharge, IGST, and any applicable anti-dumping duty referencing DGTR notifications. The calculation must account for preferential duty rates under applicable FTAs such as the India-UAE CEPA, GCC-Singapore FTA, and other bilateral agreements. All duty amounts must be calculated in the local currency and cross-referenced against the customs authority's published tariff schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R005	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Free Trade Agreement Qualification Assessment	The AI engine must automatically assess whether imported goods qualify for preferential duty rates under applicable Free Trade Agreements and Preferential Trade Agreements. The system must evaluate Rules of Origin criteria including change in tariff classification, regional value content thresholds, and specific processing requirements per the relevant FTA text. For the India-UAE Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (CEPA), the system must verify that goods meet the product-specific rules listed in the agreement's annex. The system must maintain an updated database of all FTAs applicable to Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, including effective dates, product coverage, and quota limitations. When FTA qualification is confirmed, the system must auto-generate the required preferential origin documentation and duty savings must be tracked and reported per entity for management visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R002	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Dangerous Goods Declaration (DGD) Generation	The system must auto-generate IMO Dangerous Goods Declaration forms compliant with IMDG Code Chapter 5.4, pre-populating shipper details, consignee information, UN Number, proper shipping name, class/division, packing group (I, II, or III), total quantity, flash point where applicable, and EmS (Emergency Schedule) codes. The DGD must be generated in English as the primary language with an Arabic translation layer for all text fields, supporting bilingual PDF output. Each DGD must reference the booking number, BL number, container number (per ISO 6346), and vessel/voyage. The system must validate that all mandatory fields per IMO FAL Convention are populated and flag incomplete declarations before submission to port authorities at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R006	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Valuation Compliance per WTO Agreement	The system must enforce customs valuation methods in accordance with the WTO Valuation Agreement (Article VII of GATT), applying the six valuation methods in hierarchical order: transaction value, transaction value of identical goods, transaction value of similar goods, deductive method, computed method, and fallback method. The AI must analyze the declared transaction value against historical import data for the same HS code and origin to identify potential undervaluation or overvaluation that could trigger customs audit. The system must capture all value adjustments including freight costs (mandatory addition for CIF-based customs territories), insurance, royalties, license fees, buying commissions, and assists. For FOB-based declarations at JNPT India, the system must automatically add freight and insurance to arrive at the assessable value. All valuation workings must be documented and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R007	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Query and Assessment Response Management	The system must handle customs authority queries, out-of-charge delays, and reassessment notices through a structured workflow. When a customs query is received via the electronic filing integration, the system must parse the query, classify it by type (valuation query, classification query, documentation deficiency, physical examination order), and route it to the appropriate team member with a response deadline based on the customs authority's SLA. The AI must draft suggested responses to common query types based on historical successful responses, with supporting documentation auto-attached. Response submissions must be tracked with timestamps and the system must escalate overdue responses to management. For physical examination orders at ports like Hamad Port or JNPT, the system must coordinate with the container yard operations module to arrange container positioning for customs inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R008	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Temporary Import and Re-Export Declaration Management	The system must manage temporary import declarations (ATA Carnet equivalent or local temporary import regimes) for containers and goods entering a customs territory for temporary purposes such as exhibition, repair, or transshipment. For UAE and Qatar free zones, the system must handle in-bond movement declarations between free zone and mainland customs territories. The system must track the temporary import bond amount, deadline for re-export, and automatically generate re-export declarations before the deadline expires to avoid duty crystallization. For goods transshipping through Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port to final destinations in Qatar, KSA, or India, the system must generate the appropriate transshipment declarations and track the goods until they exit the customs territory. Penalty warnings must be issued at configurable intervals (default: 30, 15, 7 days) before the temporary import deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R009	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Sanctions and Denied Party Screening Integration	The system must screen all parties on customs declarations (importer, exporter, notify party, shipping agent, freight forwarder) against international sanctions lists and denied party databases before filing. Screening must cover UN Security Council Consolidated List, US OFAC SDN and non-SDN lists, EU Consolidated List, UAE Local Terrorist List, Qatar National Anti-Money Laundering list, Saudi MASAM list, and India's UAPA entity list. Screening must be performed at declaration creation, before filing submission, and when party details are amended. The AI must handle fuzzy name matching accounting for Arabic transliteration variations (e.g., Mohammed/Muhammad/Mohamed), partial name matches, and alias detection. Any positive or potential matches must block declaration filing and escalate to the compliance officer with match details for manual review and disposition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R010	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Bonded Warehouse and Duty Deferral Tracking	The system must manage goods stored in customs bonded warehouses at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali Free Zone, King Abdullah Port bonded areas, and JNPT bonded warehouses. The system must track bonded inventory by customs declaration reference, entry date, maximum storage period per local regulations (typically 1-3 years depending on jurisdiction), and remaining duty liability. When goods are removed from bond for domestic consumption, the system must generate the ex-bond declaration and calculate duties based on the rate and exchange rate applicable on the date of removal (not the date of original importation, per most GCC and Indian customs rules). The AI must optimize duty deferral strategies by recommending optimal timing for ex-bond clearance based on exchange rate trends and anticipated tariff changes. Bond cancellation workflows must be supported when goods are re-exported from the bonded warehouse.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R011	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Audit Trail and Document Retention	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all customs declarations, amendments, queries, and responses with document retention periods compliant with each jurisdiction's requirements: minimum 5 years for Qatar and UAE customs records, 5 years for KSA per ZATCA regulations, and 7 years for India per Customs Act Section 28. All declaration versions must be stored with timestamps, user identities, and reason codes for amendments. The audit trail must capture the full XML or EDIFACT message sent to and received from customs authorities. Documents must be stored in tamper-evident format with checksums and must be retrievable for customs audit proceedings. The system must support bulk export of customs records in response to audit requests, filtered by date range, HS code, origin country, or importer entity. Retention policy enforcement must automatically archive and eventually purge records per the configured schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R012	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Duty Payment and Guarantee Management	The system must manage customs duty payment workflows including direct payment, bank guarantee deductions, and duty deferral account management. For each entity, the system must track the bank guarantee or customs bond registered with the respective customs authority, monitor utilization levels against guarantee limits, and trigger alerts when utilization exceeds 80% of the guarantee value. Payment instructions must be generated in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and integrated with the treasury module for fund allocation. For Indian operations, the system must manage the ICEGATE electronic cash ledger and duty scrip utilization (MEIS/RoDTEP). The system must reconcile duty payments against customs assessment notices, flagging any discrepancies between calculated and assessed duty amounts. Late payment interest calculations must follow jurisdiction-specific rates and be automatically accrued in the accounting system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R007	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	RTL and LTR Layout Switching	Full RTL layout when Arabic selected: sidebar on right, text right-aligned, icons mirrored, date/number formatting per locale. Instant switch without page reload. RTL CSS via dir=rtl attribute on html element. All components tested for RTL correctness. Arabic font: IBM Plex Arabic. English font: Inter.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R013	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Dual-Use and Controlled Goods Export License Verification	The system must verify export license requirements for goods classified under dual-use or controlled goods categories before allowing export customs declaration filing. The system must maintain an updated database of controlled goods lists per each jurisdiction's export control regulations, including the UAE Strategic Goods List, Saudi dual-use goods regulations, Qatar export control list, and India's SCOMET (Special Chemicals, Organisms, Materials, Equipment, and Technologies) list. When the HS code or cargo description matches a controlled item, the system must block declaration filing until a valid export license number is entered and verified. The AI must perform cargo description analysis to detect potential dual-use items even when the HS code alone does not trigger a flag, using natural language processing on commercial invoice descriptions. All export control screening results must be logged for regulatory compliance audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R014	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Declaration Amendment and Cancellation Workflow	The system must support amendment of filed customs declarations when errors are discovered or cargo details change, following the amendment procedures prescribed by each customs authority. Amendments must be classified by type: pre-clearance amendments (before customs release, typically easier to process) and post-clearance amendments (after goods are released, requiring formal application). The system must track which fields are amended, calculate any additional duty liability or refund arising from the amendment, and generate the amendment application in the format required by the customs authority. For cancellation of declarations where the shipment is diverted or cancelled, the system must follow the jurisdiction-specific cancellation procedure and ensure any duty paid is refunded or credited. All amendments must be linked to the original declaration with full audit trail and must update the shipment dossier accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R015	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs KPI Dashboard and Analytics	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying key customs performance indicators per entity and per port: average clearance time from filing to out-of-charge, customs query rate as a percentage of total declarations, duty payment accuracy (assessed vs. calculated variance), FTA utilization rate showing preferential versus MFN declarations, and first-time clearance rate without queries. The AI must identify trends and anomalies such as increasing query rates for specific HS codes or origins that may indicate a need for classification review. The dashboard must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), port code, HS chapter, origin country, and customs broker. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts. Data must be exportable in CSV and PDF formats for management reporting and must include comparative analytics across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R016	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Customs Broker Performance and Fee Management	The system must manage relationships with customs brokers operating at each port, tracking their performance metrics and fee structures. Broker profiles must include license numbers issued by the respective customs authority, authorized ports of operation, service level agreements, and fee schedules which may vary by declaration type (import, export, transshipment), cargo type, and container count. The system must automatically calculate broker fees for each declaration and generate payable entries in the accounts payable module. Performance tracking must include clearance time benchmarks, query resolution speed, error rates in declaration preparation, and customer satisfaction ratings. The AI must recommend optimal broker assignment based on the cargo type, port, and broker performance history. Broker contracts must support fees in the local port currency with payment terms configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-002-R017	FEAT-009-2-002	MOD-009	Offline Customs Document Preparation and Queue Submission	The system must allow customs declaration preparation in offline mode when connectivity to the customs authority portal is unavailable, as may occur during peak filing periods or system maintenance windows. Declarations prepared offline must be validated against all local business rules and stored in a submission queue. When connectivity is restored, the system must automatically submit queued declarations in chronological order, handling any authentication token refresh required after the offline period. The offline mode must support full Arabic and English data entry with RTL text rendering for Arabic fields. Any declarations that fail submission after connectivity restoration must be flagged for manual review with the specific error returned by the customs system. The offline queue status must be visible to supervisors with counts of pending, submitted, accepted, and rejected declarations per port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R001	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Certificate of Origin Application Auto-Generation	The system must automatically generate Certificate of Origin applications by extracting relevant data from the commercial invoice, packing list, Bill of Lading, and export customs declaration. The AI engine must populate exporter details, consignee details, cargo description (in both Arabic and English), HS codes, quantity, weight, FOB value, country of origin determination, and vessel/voyage details. The auto-generation must support different CO formats required by the destination country including the standard ICC/WCO non-preferential CO, GCC Unified CO for intra-GCC trade, Arab League CO for Pan-Arab Free Trade Area (PAFTA) members, GSP Form A for exports to preference-granting countries, and bilateral FTA-specific origin certificates. Each CO application must reference the underlying export declaration number and commercial invoice number.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R002	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Chamber of Commerce Electronic Filing Integration	The system must integrate electronically with chambers of commerce and trade authorities responsible for CO issuance in each jurisdiction: Qatar Chamber of Commerce and Industry, Dubai Chamber of Commerce (eSCO platform), Federation of Saudi Chambers, and Federation of Indian Export Organisations (FIEO) and local chambers for India. The integration must support the full application lifecycle: submission, fee payment, query handling, amendment, approval, and certificate collection or download. Each chamber has different electronic filing formats and authentication methods which must be configurable per entity. The system must handle chamber of commerce working hours which follow local business days (Sunday-Thursday for GCC, Monday-Friday for India) and Hijri calendar public holidays for GCC chambers. Application status must be tracked and updated automatically via the integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-001-R008	FEAT-000-3-001	\N	Theme and Accessibility	Light and dark mode with system preference detection. Persist per-user. Colour contrast WCAG AA compliant. Keyboard-navigable all interactive elements. Focus rings visible. Screen reader labels on all icons. Font size user-adjustable (sm/md/lg). Reduced motion preference respected.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R003	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	AI-Driven Country of Origin Determination	The AI engine must determine the correct country of origin for goods based on WCO Rules of Origin, applying the substantial transformation test or specific processing rules as applicable. For manufactured goods, the system must evaluate whether the production process in the declared origin country constitutes sufficient working or processing per the applicable rules. The system must maintain a database of origin rules for each trade agreement and preference scheme applicable to exports from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. For goods with components from multiple countries, the AI must apply the appropriate origin criteria (change in tariff classification, ad valorem percentage, or specific process rule) and document the determination rationale. The system must flag goods where origin determination is ambiguous, recommending that the exporter seek an advance ruling from the customs authority before CO application.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R004	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Preferential Certificate of Origin for FTA Shipments	The system must manage preferential Certificates of Origin that entitle the importer to reduced or zero customs duty rates at the destination under applicable Free Trade Agreements. For each FTA, the system must enforce the specific CO format, data requirements, and certification procedures defined in the agreement text. This includes the India-UAE CEPA Certificate of Origin (which requires specific declarations about origin criteria met), GCC-Singapore FTA certificates, and any other bilateral or multilateral agreements in force. The system must validate that the exporter is registered or authorized to issue self-certification declarations where FTAs permit this. Preferential CO applications must include the origin criteria met (wholly obtained, substantial transformation with specific rule reference), and the AI must verify compliance against the product-specific rules of origin annex of each FTA before allowing application submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R005	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Arab League Certificate of Origin Processing	The system must handle Arab League Certificates of Origin required for goods traded within the Pan-Arab Free Trade Area (PAFTA/GAFTA) to qualify for preferential tariff treatment. The certificate must be prepared in Arabic as the primary language per Arab League requirements, with the system ensuring proper Arabic text formatting and RTL layout. The system must validate that goods meet the Arab League Rules of Origin requiring minimum 40% local or cumulated Arab origin value added. The AI must calculate the domestic value-added percentage based on input costs, manufacturing costs, and raw material origins to verify eligibility. For shipments from Qatar, UAE, or KSA to other Arab League members, the system must automatically determine PAFTA eligibility and prepare the certificate in the prescribed format. The certificate must be routed to the appropriate chamber of commerce for attestation before being included in the shipment document set.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R006	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	GSP Form A and Preferential Scheme Certificate Management	The system must generate GSP (Generalized System of Preferences) Form A certificates for exports from India to preference-granting developed countries, ensuring compliance with the specific origin criteria of each granting country's GSP scheme. The system must maintain the list of products eligible for GSP benefits by destination country and HS code, and alert when products are graduated from GSP eligibility. For UAE and Qatar exports benefiting from other preferential schemes, the system must manage the appropriate certificate format. The AI must validate that the product meets the GSP origin criteria (typically 30-50% domestic value addition depending on the granting country's scheme) and that the exporter is registered with the competent authority. Certificate numbering must follow the sequential format prescribed by the issuing authority, and the system must maintain a register of all issued certificates for post-issuance verification requests.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R007	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Legalization and Attestation Workflow Tracking	The system must track the legalization and attestation workflow for Certificates of Origin that require embassy or consulate attestation for specific destination countries. Many importing countries in the Middle East, Africa, and South America require COs to be attested by their embassy in the exporting country. The system must maintain a database of attestation requirements by destination country, listing the required steps (chamber certification, MOFA attestation, embassy legalization), estimated processing times, and fee schedules in the local currency. The workflow must track each document through each attestation stage with timestamps, physical location tracking (if documents must be physically submitted), and responsible party assignment. The AI must forecast total attestation completion time based on historical processing durations and alert when the estimated completion date risks delaying the shipment schedule. The system must support both Apostille (Hague Convention) and traditional legalization paths.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R008	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Back-to-Back Certificate of Origin for Re-Exports	The system must handle back-to-back Certificates of Origin for goods that are imported into a free zone or customs territory and then re-exported to a third country, which is a common scenario for Jebel Ali Free Zone and other UAE free zones acting as regional distribution hubs. The back-to-back CO must reference the original CO from the manufacturing country and indicate the re-export origin correctly. The system must validate that the goods have not undergone substantial transformation in the free zone (which would change the origin) unless the operation qualifies as origin-conferring under applicable rules. For goods imported into Jebel Ali and re-exported to Qatar, KSA, or India, the AI must determine whether the original origin is maintained and which CO type is required for the final destination. The system must prevent issuance of COs that would misrepresent the country of origin, maintaining full traceability from original manufacturer to final consignee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R009	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Certificate of Origin Amendment and Replacement	The system must support amendment and replacement of issued Certificates of Origin when errors are discovered or shipment details change after certificate issuance. The amendment workflow must follow the procedures prescribed by the issuing chamber of commerce, which typically require submission of the original certificate, a letter explaining the amendment reason, and supporting documents such as amended invoices. The system must track the amendment type (correction of cargo description, quantity adjustment, consignee change, vessel amendment) and generate the amendment application in the chamber's required format. For certificates that cannot be amended and must be cancelled and reissued, the system must manage the cancellation process and link the replacement certificate to the cancelled original. All amendments must be recorded in the audit trail with the original and amended values, reason for amendment, and approving authority within the organization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R010	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Multi-Currency Value Declaration on Certificates	The system must handle value declarations on Certificates of Origin in the currency required by the destination country or trade agreement. While the commercial invoice may be in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR, the CO may need to state the FOB value in a different currency per the importing country's requirements. The system must automatically convert values using the exchange rate applicable on the invoice date or the CO application date as specified by the relevant chamber of commerce rules. Currency conversion must use rates from the entity's configured rate source (central bank published rates or commercial bank rates). The system must display all monetary amounts with correct decimal precision per currency convention (2 decimals for USD/AED/QAR/SAR, 2 for INR) and include the currency code prominently. For FTA certificates requiring value-based origin criteria calculation, the system must ensure consistent currency usage throughout the calculation per the FTA's prescribed methodology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R011	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Origin Verification Request Response System	The system must manage origin verification requests received from importing country customs authorities who wish to verify the authenticity and accuracy of Certificates of Origin issued for exports. Verification requests must be logged with the requesting authority details, certificate reference number, specific information requested, and response deadline. The AI must auto-retrieve the original certificate application, supporting manufacturing or sourcing records, and draft a verification response for compliance officer review. The system must track response deadlines which are typically 90-180 days per WTO Trade Facilitation Agreement guidelines and escalate approaching deadlines. Response templates must be available in Arabic and English depending on the requesting authority's language. All verification correspondence must be stored in the document management system linked to the original CO and shipment dossier for complete traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R012	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Certificate Fee Calculation and Payment Tracking	The system must calculate chamber of commerce fees for Certificate of Origin applications based on each chamber's published fee schedule. Fee structures vary by chamber and may include base application fees, per-page charges for multi-page certificates, attestation fees, express processing surcharges, and amendment fees. Fees for GCC chambers are typically in the local currency (QAR for Qatar Chamber, AED for Dubai Chamber, SAR for Saudi chambers) while Indian chamber fees are in INR. The system must generate payment instructions or process electronic payments through the chamber's payment gateway integration. All CO-related fees must be tracked per shipment and allocated to the shipment cost center for profitability analysis. The system must maintain fee schedule versions with effective dates to handle fee changes, and the AI must flag when a chamber updates its fee structure by detecting discrepancies between calculated and invoiced fee amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R013	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Exporter Registration and Authorization Management	The system must manage exporter registrations with chambers of commerce and customs authorities across all operating entities. Many chambers require exporters to maintain an active membership or registration to apply for Certificates of Origin. The system must track registration status, validity period, renewal dates, and authorized signatory details for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India operations). For self-certification schemes under modern FTAs, the system must verify that the exporter holds Approved Exporter or Registered Exporter (REX) status with the customs authority. The AI must monitor registration expiry dates and trigger renewal workflows at configurable intervals (default: 60 days before expiry). Authorized signatories must be maintained with specimen signatures registered at the chamber, and the system must prevent CO applications listing a signatory whose authorization has expired or been revoked.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R014	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Bulk Certificate of Origin Processing for Multiple Invoices	The system must support bulk CO processing for shipments covered by multiple commercial invoices from the same exporter to the same consignee on the same vessel. Chambers of commerce may allow a single CO covering multiple invoices or may require separate COs per invoice depending on their rules. The AI must determine the appropriate approach based on the destination chamber's requirements and the trade agreement under which the CO is issued. For bulk processing, the system must consolidate cargo descriptions, values, and weights across invoices while maintaining individual invoice references. The system must handle scenarios where some items in a multi-invoice shipment qualify for preferential treatment while others do not, generating separate preferential and non-preferential COs as needed. Bulk processing must support up to 50 invoices per consolidated CO application with proper pagination and continuation sheet formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R015	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Certificate of Origin Template Management	The system must maintain a library of CO templates corresponding to different certificate types and issuing authorities. Templates must include the standard ICC non-preferential CO (white form), GCC Unified CO, Arab League CO (yellow form), GSP Form A, EUR.1 movement certificate for applicable agreements, and bilateral FTA-specific certificates. Each template must define the layout, mandatory fields, field validation rules, maximum character lengths, and printing specifications (some chambers require specific paper sizes, colors, or security features). Templates must support bilingual printing with Arabic and English text properly aligned. The system must version-control templates and automatically apply the current version when preparing a CO application. When a chamber updates its CO format, the AI must detect the format change through integration responses and alert the system administrator to update the template. Historical certificates must always display using the template version that was active at the time of issuance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-003-R016	FEAT-009-2-003	MOD-009	Origin Documentation Archival and Retrieval for Audits	The system must archive all Certificate of Origin applications, issued certificates, supporting documents (invoices, packing lists, manufacturing declarations, cost calculations for value-added determination), and correspondence with chambers and customs authorities. Archived documents must be retained for the period required by each jurisdiction's customs law (minimum 5 years for GCC countries, 7 years for India) and must be retrievable within minutes for regulatory audit response. The archival system must support full-text search across document content in both Arabic and English, including OCR-processed scanned documents. The AI must index documents by shipment reference, certificate number, HS code, destination country, and exporter entity for multi-dimensional retrieval. Archived documents must be exportable as certified copies with digital signatures for submission to auditors or legal proceedings. The system must provide certificate authenticity verification via a unique URL or QR code that third parties can use to confirm certificate validity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R001	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Phytosanitary Certificate Application Generation	The system must automatically generate phytosanitary certificate applications for export shipments containing plant products, agricultural commodities, wood packaging materials, or other items requiring plant health certification under the International Plant Protection Convention (IPPC). The AI must identify shipments requiring phytosanitary certificates based on HS code classification (chapters 06-14 primarily, plus wood products in chapter 44) and the destination country's import requirements. Application data must be extracted from the commercial invoice, packing list, and booking details, including scientific names of plant species where required, treatment details, and growing region or production facility. Applications must be prepared in the format required by the issuing authority: Ministry of Municipality and Environment for Qatar, MOCCAE for UAE, SFDA/MEWA for KSA, and Plant Quarantine Division of DPPQS for India. Bilingual Arabic-English applications must be supported for GCC authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R002	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Health Certificate Management for Food Products	The system must manage health certificates for food product shipments including processed food, beverages, dairy products, and canned goods. Health certificates must comply with the Codex Alimentarius guidelines and the specific requirements of the destination country's food safety authority. For imports into GCC countries, the system must ensure compliance with GSO (Gulf Standards Organization) food standards and halal certification requirements. The AI must identify food shipments requiring health certificates based on HS codes (chapters 02-05, 15-22) and generate applications with required data: production facility details, batch numbers, production and expiry dates, storage temperature requirements, ingredient lists in Arabic and English, and laboratory analysis results. The system must track certificate validity periods (typically tied to product shelf life) and flag certificates approaching expiry before the shipment reaches the destination port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R003	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	National Authority Electronic Integration for Certificate Filing	The system must integrate with national food safety and agriculture authorities for electronic certificate application and issuance. Integrations must cover: Qatar Ministry of Municipality (food import permits via the Qatar e-Government portal), UAE MOCCAE eFITS system for phytosanitary and health certificates, Saudi SFDA GHARS system for food import permits and MEWA for plant health certificates, and India's PQIS (Plant Quarantine Information System) for phytosanitary certificates and FSSAI Food Import Clearance System. Each integration must handle the authority's specific data format, authentication requirements, fee payment processing, and certificate status tracking. The system must manage different working days and public holidays per jurisdiction (including Hijri-based holidays for GCC authorities) and adjust submission timing accordingly. Integration health monitoring must alert operations staff when an authority's system is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R004	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Inspection Scheduling and Coordination Workflow	The system must coordinate physical inspection scheduling with the relevant authority when phytosanitary or health inspections are required before certificate issuance or upon cargo arrival at the destination port. The inspection workflow must capture the inspection type (pre-shipment inspection at origin, arrival inspection at destination, random sampling), preferred date and time, container location at the port or warehouse, and special access requirements such as reefer container power connection for temperature-sensitive inspections. The AI must optimize inspection scheduling by grouping multiple containers from the same shipment for joint inspection, considering inspector availability calendars, and minimizing demurrage charges by scheduling inspections before free time expires. At Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must coordinate with terminal operators for container positioning for inspection. Inspection results must be recorded digitally with pass, conditional pass, or rejection status and linked to the shipment dossier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R005	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	ISPM-15 Wood Packaging Compliance Verification	The system must verify ISPM-15 (International Standards for Phytosanitary Measures No. 15) compliance for wood packaging materials used in international shipments. All containers with wooden pallets, crates, dunnage, or other solid wood packaging must have documentation confirming heat treatment (HT) or methyl bromide treatment (MB) with the IPPC mark stamped on the wood. The AI must flag shipments where wood packaging compliance documentation is missing or incomplete based on the packing list analysis. For imports into Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must verify that the ISPM-15 treatment mark country code and treatment provider registration are valid. Non-compliant shipments must trigger a quarantine alert workflow, as customs authorities at ports like Hamad Port and JNPT will detain non-ISPM-15 compliant shipments for treatment or re-export. The system must maintain a register of approved treatment providers and their mark numbers for verification purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R006	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Halal Certificate Verification and Tracking	The system must manage halal certification requirements for food products imported into GCC countries and track halal certificates throughout the shipment lifecycle. The system must verify that halal certificates are issued by accreditation bodies recognized by the destination country's halal authority: ESMA-accredited bodies for UAE, SFDA-recognized bodies for KSA, and bodies recognized by Qatar's Ministry of Municipality. The AI must identify shipments requiring halal certification based on HS codes for meat products, poultry, processed foods containing animal derivatives, and cosmetics. Each halal certificate must be validated for the certifying body's accreditation status, certificate validity period, and consistency with the product description on the commercial invoice. The system must maintain a database of globally recognized halal certification bodies cross-referenced with each GCC country's recognized body list. Non-compliant or expired halal certificates must block shipment release until valid certification is provided.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R001	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	IMDG Code Classification Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered IMDG Code classification engine that automatically identifies and validates dangerous goods classes (Class 1 through Class 9 including subdivisions such as 2.1 Flammable Gas, 2.2 Non-Flammable Gas, 2.3 Toxic Gas, 4.1 Flammable Solids, 5.1 Oxidizers, 5.2 Organic Peroxides, 6.1 Toxic Substances, 6.2 Infectious Substances, 8 Corrosives, and 9 Miscellaneous) based on UN Number lookup, proper shipping name matching, and Material Safety Data Sheet (MSDS) parsing. The engine must support IMDG Code Amendment 41-22 and subsequent amendments, cross-referencing against the Qatar Civil Defence regulations, UAE Federal Authority for Nuclear Regulation (FANR), Saudi Arabia General Authority of Civil Aviation (GACA) hazmat rules, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping (DGS) circulars. Classification results must display in both English and Arabic with correct RTL rendering of chemical nomenclature.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R007	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Veterinary Certificate Management for Animal Products	The system must manage veterinary health certificates for shipments containing live animals, animal products, and animal by-products as required by the OIE (World Organisation for Animal Health) International Health Code. The system must handle export veterinary certificates from the origin country's competent veterinary authority and import permits from the destination country's authority. For live animal shipments, the system must capture species identification, quantity, origin farm or facility registration, vaccination records, quarantine period compliance, and transport container specifications meeting IATA Live Animals Regulations for air freight or appropriate container ventilation for sea freight. The AI must cross-reference the origin country's animal disease status using OIE WAHIS data to verify that the destination country permits imports from that origin, particularly for diseases such as Avian Influenza, Foot and Mouth Disease, and African Swine Fever. All veterinary certificates must be bilingual (Arabic and English for GCC destinations) and linked to the customs declaration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R008	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Laboratory Test Result Integration and Compliance Validation	The system must integrate with accredited laboratory systems to receive and process test results for food safety, phytosanitary, and product quality analyses required for certificate issuance. Test results must be matched to the specific shipment lot, container number, and sample reference. The AI must validate that test results meet the destination country's maximum residue limits (MRLs) for pesticides, maximum permitted levels for heavy metals, microbiological standards (total plate count, coliform, salmonella, listeria as applicable), and any other regulatory thresholds. For GCC imports, the system must validate compliance with GSO standards (e.g., GSO 382 for microbiological criteria, GSO 2500 series for pesticide residues). For Indian imports, validation must reference FSSAI standards. Test result failures must trigger an automatic hold on the shipment and notify the quality control team with specific parameters that exceeded limits. Results must be stored with laboratory accreditation details and analyst identification for traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R009	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Import Permit Pre-Arrival Processing	The system must manage the pre-arrival import permit process required by destination country food safety and agriculture authorities before regulated goods can arrive at the port. For UAE imports, the system must file FIRS (Food Import and Re-export System) applications with MOCCAE prior to shipment. For KSA, the SFDA import license and SABER conformity certificate must be obtained before vessel arrival. For Qatar, food import permits must be secured from the Ministry of Municipality. For India, FSSAI import licenses and DPPQS import permits must be managed. The AI must calculate the optimal submission timing based on each authority's average processing time and the vessel's estimated arrival date at the destination port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT). Late permit applications that risk cargo detention on arrival must generate priority escalation alerts. All permit applications must include the required laboratory test results, certificates, and declarations in the prescribed format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R010	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Fumigation and Treatment Certificate Management	The system must manage fumigation and pest treatment certificates for shipments requiring phytosanitary treatment either at origin before export or at destination upon quarantine authority order. Treatment types must include methyl bromide fumigation, phosphine fumigation, heat treatment, cold treatment for fruit fly control, irradiation, and vapor heat treatment. Each treatment certificate must capture the treatment provider's registration number, treatment method, chemical concentration and exposure time, temperature readings during treatment, and the pest target species. The AI must determine treatment requirements based on the commodity type, origin country pest status per IPPC pest reporting, and the destination country's phytosanitary regulations. For shipments arriving at Hamad Port or JNPT that require on-arrival fumigation, the system must coordinate with approved treatment providers, schedule the fumigation, and track the treatment completion before customs release. Treatment costs must be captured in the appropriate currency and allocated to the shipment cost center.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R011	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Reefer Container Temperature Monitoring for Perishable Compliance	The system must integrate with reefer container temperature monitoring systems to validate that temperature-sensitive cargo has maintained the required cold chain throughout the voyage, as this data is frequently required by food safety authorities for health certificate validation. The system must capture continuous temperature logs from IoT sensors, download temperature data from the reefer container's integral data logger upon arrival, and compare actual temperatures against the permitted range declared on the health certificate. Temperature excursions (deviations exceeding the permitted range for more than a configurable threshold, default 2 hours) must be flagged to the food safety team and reported to the destination authority if required. For frozen cargo (typically -18°C), chilled cargo (0-4°C), and controlled atmosphere cargo (specific O2/CO2 levels), the system must validate against product-specific temperature requirements. Temperature compliance reports must be generated in both Arabic and English and attached to the health certificate documentation for authority inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R012	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Quarantine Hold and Release Workflow Management	The system must manage quarantine hold and release workflows when cargo is detained by the phytosanitary or food safety authority at the destination port. The quarantine workflow must track the hold reason (pest detection, failed laboratory test, missing documentation, random inspection), authority reference number, estimated resolution timeline, required corrective actions (treatment, re-testing, document submission), and financial implications (storage charges accruing during hold). The AI must recommend the optimal resolution path based on the hold reason: for documentation issues, auto-generate the required documents; for pest detection, schedule approved treatment providers; for failed lab tests, coordinate re-sampling and retesting. The workflow must support escalation to management when quarantine holds exceed configurable duration thresholds (default: 5 business days) or when demurrage costs exceed a configurable amount per entity. All quarantine events must be logged with dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats and linked to the customs declaration for coordinated release processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R001	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	Company Setup Wizard	Step-by-step wizard on first login: (1) Company profile (name Arabic+English, country, registration, VAT, logo), (2) Data residency selection with latency map, (3) Fiscal year and currency defaults, (4) First admin user profile completion, (5) Team invite up to 5 initial users, (6) Module selection for first activation. Progress bar. Can skip and return later.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R013	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	ePhyto Electronic Certificate Exchange via IPPC Hub	The system must support the IPPC ePhyto solution for electronic exchange of phytosanitary certificates between national plant protection organizations (NPPOs). The system must generate outbound ePhyto XML messages compliant with the IPPC ePhyto schema for export certificates issued by the origin country's NPPO, and receive and process inbound ePhyto messages for import phytosanitary certificates. The ePhyto integration must connect through the IPPC Hub (the centralized exchange platform) and the Generic ePhyto National System (GeNS) where the country uses this solution. The AI must reconcile electronic certificates received via ePhyto with the corresponding shipment records using Bill of Lading number, container number, and commodity description matching. For countries not yet connected to the ePhyto Hub, the system must fall back to manual certificate processing with OCR-based data extraction from scanned paper certificates. Certificate authenticity verification must be performed using the ePhyto Hub's verification service.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R014	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Multi-Commodity Shipment Certificate Coordination	The system must coordinate multiple certificates for shipments containing different commodity types in separate containers, where each commodity may require different certificates (phytosanitary for fruits in one container, health certificate for processed food in another, veterinary certificate for animal products in a third). The AI must analyze the shipment's container-level cargo manifest to determine the certificate requirements per container and generate a consolidated certificate requirement checklist. The system must track certificate readiness at the container level and at the shipment level, preventing vessel booking confirmation until all required certificates are in order for every container in the shipment. For multi-commodity shipments arriving at destination ports, the system must route each container to the appropriate inspection authority (plant quarantine for phytosanitary items, food safety for health-certified items, veterinary services for animal products) and consolidate clearance status. The consolidated status dashboard must display certificate compliance in both Arabic and English with color-coded readiness indicators per container.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R015	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Shelf Life and Expiry Date Validation for Import Compliance	The system must validate remaining shelf life of food products against the destination country's minimum shelf life import requirements. GCC countries typically require a minimum of 50-60% remaining shelf life at the time of arrival for processed foods, while India requires specific minimum periods varying by product category per FSSAI regulations. The AI must calculate the estimated arrival date at the destination port based on vessel schedule, compare against the product expiry dates declared on the health certificate and commercial invoice, and flag shipments that will not meet the minimum remaining shelf life threshold upon arrival. For shipments transiting through intermediary ports (e.g., transshipping at Jebel Ali for onward delivery to Hamad Port), the additional transit time must be factored into the shelf life calculation. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri date formats for expiry dates as some GCC market products use Hijri dating. Non-compliant shipments must be flagged before vessel loading at the origin port to allow alternative routing or early shipment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R016	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Certificate Compliance Dashboard and Regulatory Alert System	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing the compliance status of all phytosanitary, health, and veterinary certificates across all entities and ports. Dashboard metrics must include: certificates pending issuance by authority and aging, inspection pass rates by commodity type and origin country, quarantine hold incidents trending over time, average certificate processing time by authority, and regulatory compliance score per trade lane. The AI must monitor regulatory changes from food safety and agriculture authorities across all four jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and generate alerts when new import requirements, banned substances, pest alerts, or standard amendments are published. Regulatory alerts must be categorized by commodity impact, assessed for operational impact on active and planned shipments, and distributed to relevant operations staff in their preferred language (Arabic or English). The dashboard must support date filtering in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R017	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Offline Certificate Document Access and Verification	The system must enable offline access to phytosanitary, health, and veterinary certificates for inspection staff and operations personnel at port facilities where connectivity may be limited. Certificates and associated inspection records must be cached locally on mobile devices used by field staff at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. The offline capability must support certificate verification by scanning a QR code or barcode on the certificate to retrieve cached certificate details, displaying the verification result with a clear valid or invalid indicator. When operating offline, inspection staff must be able to record inspection results, capture photographs of cargo condition, and input temperature readings for reefer containers, with all data queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The offline mode must render documents correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR formats, and all locally cached data must be encrypted at rest to protect sensitive shipment and certificate information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-2-004-R018	FEAT-009-2-004	MOD-009	Radiation and Contamination Certificate Processing	The system must manage radiation testing certificates and contamination-free declarations required by certain GCC and Indian import regulations for food products originating from countries or regions with known nuclear or industrial contamination risks. The system must maintain an updated list of countries and products requiring radiation testing certificates based on each destination country's regulations (notably post-Fukushima requirements still in effect for Japanese food products in some GCC countries). The AI must identify shipments requiring radiation certificates based on origin country, product type, and destination country regulations. Certificate data must include the testing laboratory's accreditation details, test method reference (typically gamma spectrometry), radionuclide levels measured (Cs-134, Cs-137, I-131 at minimum), and a declaration of compliance with the destination country's maximum permitted levels. Certificates must be in both Arabic and English for GCC destinations and must be linked to the specific production batch and shipment container. The system must reject certificates from non-accredited laboratories and flag results approaching the maximum permitted threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R002	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	Setup Completion Checklist	Persistent checklist widget on dashboard until 100% complete. Items: company profile complete, logo uploaded, first user invited, first module activated, payment method added, MFA enabled for admin, custom domain configured (optional). Dismiss permanently after 100%.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R003	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Container Packing Certificate Workflow	The system must manage the Container/Vehicle Packing Certificate workflow as mandated by IMDG Code Section 5.4.2, capturing certification that the container has been packed in accordance with the provisions of the Code. The certificate must record container number, packing date, responsible party name and signature (supporting digital signature capture), and a checklist confirming: container structurally serviceable, no incompatible goods packed together, all packages inspected for damage and leakage, proper dunnage and securing applied, placards and markings correctly applied per IMDG Code Chapter 5.3. The workflow must support multi-entity operations where the packing entity in one jurisdiction (e.g., India JNPT) differs from the booking entity in another (e.g., Qatar Hamad Port), maintaining audit chain across entity boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R004	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Segregation Rules Validation Engine	The system must enforce IMDG Code Chapter 7.2 segregation rules for dangerous goods stowage aboard vessels and within container yards. The AI engine must automatically validate segregation requirements across all nine classes using the segregation table (IMDG Code Section 7.2.4), enforcing rules such as 'Away From' (minimum 3 metres), 'Separated From' (minimum 6 metres or one container space), 'Separated by a Complete Compartment', and 'Separated Longitudinally by an Intervening Complete Compartment'. When booking multiple DG containers on the same vessel/voyage, the system must check all container-to-container segregation pairs and reject or flag violations with specific references to the violated rule. Validation must account for on-deck versus under-deck stowage differences as per IMDG Code Table 7.2.4.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R005	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Emergency Response Documentation	The system must generate and maintain Emergency Response documentation including the EmS Guide (Fire Schedule and Spillage Schedule) per IMDG Code Supplement, and the Medical First Aid Guide (MFAG). For each dangerous goods booking, the system must automatically attach the applicable EmS code pair (e.g., F-A, S-A) and generate a printable emergency response sheet in both English and Arabic. The documentation must include 24-hour emergency contact numbers for the shipper and the designated emergency response provider. For shipments involving Qatar, the system must include Hamad Port emergency coordination center contacts; for UAE, the National Emergency Crisis and Disasters Management Authority (NCEMA) contacts; for KSA, the Saudi Red Crescent Authority contacts; and for India, the Indian Coast Guard MRCC contacts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R006	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Port Authority DG Pre-Arrival Notification	The system must generate and electronically submit Dangerous Goods pre-arrival notifications to port authorities at least 24 hours before vessel arrival as required by ISPS Code and local port regulations. For Hamad Port (Qatar Ports Management Company - Mwani), the system must submit via the QPorts portal format. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (Abu Dhabi Ports/DP World), the system must comply with DP World DG approval system requirements. For JNPT, the system must submit via the Indian Port Community System (PCS) in the prescribed DGMO format. Each notification must include vessel name, IMO number, voyage number, ETA, container number, UN Number, class, packing group, quantity, stowage position, and emergency contacts. The system must track submission status and store acknowledgment receipts with timestamps for compliance audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R007	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	DG Approval Workflow with Multi-Entity Authorization	The system must implement a configurable multi-step approval workflow for dangerous goods bookings that supports the organizational structures across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. The workflow must include stages for: initial DG desk review, operations manager approval, safety officer sign-off, and port authority submission. Each approval step must capture the approver identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, approval comments in English or Arabic with full RTL support, and any conditions or restrictions imposed. The workflow must support delegation rules where an approver on leave can delegate to a substitute within the same entity. Cross-entity shipments (e.g., transhipment DG cargo from JNPT to Hamad Port via Jebel Ali) must require approval from the operations teams at both origin and destination entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R008	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	IMDG Placard and Marking Label Generation	The system must generate printable placard labels, marking labels, and UN number displays conforming to IMDG Code Chapter 5.2 (marking of packages) and Chapter 5.3 (placarding and marking of cargo transport units). Label generation must support all hazard class diamond labels (Classes 1-9), subsidiary risk labels, elevated temperature mark, marine pollutant mark, orientation arrows, and the fumigation warning sign per IMDG 5.5.2. Labels must be generated in the correct dimensions per IMDG Code specifications (minimum 100mm x 100mm for packages, 250mm x 250mm for placards). The system must produce a container-specific label set document showing exactly which placards must be affixed to which side of the container, with Arabic translations of any textual hazard communication elements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R009	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Dangerous Goods Inventory and Yard Tracking	The system must maintain a real-time inventory of all dangerous goods containers within the terminal yard at each port location, tracking container number, UN Number, IMDG class, exact yard position (block/bay/row/tier), arrival date, expected vessel loading date, and current status (awaiting inspection, approved for loading, on hold, rejected). The inventory must enforce yard segregation rules consistent with IMDG Chapter 7.2 and local port authority yard stacking rules. For Jebel Ali, the system must comply with DP World DG yard zone allocation policies. The inventory dashboard must provide a visual yard map overlay showing DG container positions color-coded by hazard class. Alerts must fire when DG containers exceed the port-specific maximum yard dwell time (configurable per entity, typically 72 hours for most GCC ports) or when incompatible goods are detected in adjacent yard slots.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R010	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Multi-Currency DG Surcharge Calculation	The system must calculate and apply dangerous goods surcharges to freight invoices based on IMDG class, quantity, packing group, and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20OT, 20FR ISO type codes). Surcharge rates must be configurable per trade lane, per carrier, and per entity with support for USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR currencies. The system must apply exchange rate conversion using the daily treasury rate from the entity's designated bank, with the conversion date being the BL date or invoice date as configured per entity policy. DG surcharges for Class 1 (Explosives) and Class 7 (Radioactive) must be flagged as requiring special approval with higher surcharge tiers. The system must generate surcharge breakdowns on commercial invoices in both English and Arabic, with currency amounts displayed in the entity's functional currency and the transaction currency where different.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R011	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Regulatory Compliance Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive, tamper-evident audit trail for every dangerous goods documentation action including creation, modification, approval, rejection, submission, and archival. Each audit entry must record the user ID, entity code, timestamp in UTC with local timezone offset (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), action performed, previous value, new value, and IP address. The audit trail must be immutable once written and must support regulatory inspection queries where port state control or flag state authorities request DG documentation history for a specific vessel, voyage, or container. The system must retain DG audit records for a minimum of seven years per IMO FAL Convention requirements and must support export in PDF, CSV, and XML formats for submission to maritime authorities across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R012	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	AI-Powered MSDS Document Parsing	The system must provide AI-powered parsing of Material Safety Data Sheets (MSDS/SDS) uploaded in PDF format, automatically extracting UN Number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group, flash point, boiling point, marine pollutant status, and special provisions from Section 14 (Transport Information) of the GHS-compliant SDS. The parser must handle MSDS documents in English, Arabic, and Hindi languages, with confidence scoring for each extracted field. When confidence falls below 85%, the system must flag the field for manual review by the DG desk operator. Extracted data must be cross-validated against the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List (Chapter 3.2) to detect mismatches between the MSDS declaration and the IMDG classification. The system must store parsed MSDS documents linked to the booking and retain them for the full cargo lifecycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R013	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	DG Exemption and Special Provision Handling	The system must support IMDG Code special provisions (SP) and exemptions as listed in IMDG Code Column 6 of the Dangerous Goods List, including limited quantity provisions (Chapter 3.4), excepted quantity provisions (Chapter 3.5), and specific special provisions (SP numbers). When a booking qualifies for limited quantity treatment, the system must adjust documentation requirements accordingly, removing the need for a full DGD while retaining the limited quantity mark requirement. The system must also handle country-specific exemptions where GCC states or India have filed reservations or additional requirements beyond the base IMDG Code. Local regulatory overrides must be configurable per entity without requiring code changes, stored as rule configurations that specify the regulatory authority, effective date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, expiry date, and affected UN Numbers or classes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R014	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Offline DG Documentation Capability	The system must support offline creation and validation of dangerous goods documentation for scenarios where vessel agents or surveyors at anchorage or remote terminal locations have limited or no internet connectivity. The offline module must cache the complete IMDG Dangerous Goods List, segregation tables, EmS Guide data, and the most recent 500 booking records for the user's assigned entity. When operating offline, the system must queue DG declarations, packing certificates, and approval requests locally with conflict detection upon reconnection. The offline sync engine must handle merge conflicts by presenting the DG desk operator with a side-by-side comparison in both English and Arabic, highlighting fields that differ between the local and server versions, and requiring explicit resolution before the record is committed to the central database.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R015	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	Multimodal Transport DG Documentation	The system must generate dangerous goods documentation for multimodal transport chains where cargo moves through road, rail, and sea segments within the Middle East and Indian subcontinent corridor. For the road segment, the system must produce ADR-equivalent documentation as adopted by GCC states under GCC Standardization Organization (GSO) transport regulations. For the rail segment within India (connecting inland container depots to JNPT), the system must produce documentation per Indian Railways IMDG-equivalent rules. The sea segment documentation follows standard IMDG Code requirements. The system must maintain a unified DG dossier that consolidates all modal documents, tracks custody transfers between road hauler, rail operator, and shipping line, and ensures that the hazard communication chain is unbroken from shipper's door to consignee's door across multiple jurisdictions and entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-001-R016	FEAT-009-3-001	MOD-009	DG Incident and Non-Conformance Reporting	The system must provide a structured incident reporting module for dangerous goods non-conformances detected during packing inspections, yard surveys, vessel loading, or transit. Each incident report must capture the container number, booking reference, UN Number, IMDG class, nature of the non-conformance (leakage, incorrect labeling, segregation violation, documentation discrepancy, packaging failure), severity classification (minor, major, critical), corrective action taken, responsible party, and photographic evidence attachments. The module must generate notifications to the entity safety officer, the DG desk, and the relevant port authority contact based on severity thresholds. For critical incidents, the system must auto-generate reports formatted per the IMO Casualty Investigation Code and the respective national maritime authority reporting templates for Qatar (TASMU Maritime), UAE (Federal Transport Authority), KSA (Transport General Authority), and India (DGS).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R001	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Pre-Trip Inspection Certificate Management	The system must manage Pre-Trip Inspection (PTI) certificates for reefer containers (ISO type codes 22R1, 42R1, 45R1, 42H1) documenting the mechanical fitness of the refrigeration unit before cargo stuffing. The PTI certificate must record container number, reefer unit manufacturer and model (Carrier, Thermo King, Daikin, Star Cool), set temperature, return air temperature, supply air temperature, humidity setting (where applicable), defrost interval, ventilation setting (CBM/h), power supply voltage, compressor operation confirmation, and inspector identity. The system must enforce that no temperature-controlled booking can proceed to gate-in at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT without a valid PTI certificate issued within the port-configurable validity window (typically 72 hours for GCC ports and 48 hours for JNPT). PTI data must be captured in both English and Arabic for GCC entity operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R001	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Rotation Sequence Definition	The system must allow voyage planners to define the port rotation sequence for each voyage, specifying the ordered list of port calls with each call identified by UN/LOCODE, terminal code, call purpose (loading, discharging, transshipment, bunkering, crew change, survey), and planned berth duration. The rotation sequence must support split calls where a vessel visits the same port multiple times within a single voyage (e.g., loading at JNPT then returning to JNPT after a Gulf loop for discharge). Port additions, removals, and resequencing must be permitted until the voyage reaches a locked status, with all changes requiring approval from a user with voyage-planner or higher role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R002	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Temperature Range Configuration by Commodity	The system must maintain a comprehensive commodity-to-temperature mapping database covering all major reefer commodities traded through the Gulf and India corridors including fresh fruits (dates, mangoes, citrus at 5°C to 8°C), fresh vegetables (0°C to 4°C), frozen meat and poultry (−18°C to −20°C for Halal-certified frozen products per GSO 993 standards), frozen seafood (−18°C to −25°C), dairy products (2°C to 4°C), pharmaceuticals (2°C to 8°C for vaccines, 15°C to 25°C for general pharma per WHO GDP guidelines), and chocolate/confectionery (12°C to 18°C). Each commodity profile must store acceptable temperature range, humidity range, ventilation requirements, and atmosphere control settings (O2/CO2 percentages for controlled atmosphere containers). The database must support entity-specific overrides where local food safety authorities (Qatar MOPH, UAE MOCCAE, Saudi SFDA, Indian FSSAI) mandate different temperature parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R003	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Continuous Temperature Monitoring Record	The system must ingest and store continuous temperature monitoring data from reefer container data loggers, supporting integration with major telematics platforms including Maersk Captain Peter, MSC iCONTAINER, CMA CGM TRAXENS, and standalone USB data logger downloads (Sensitech TempTale, Emerson GO Real-Time). Temperature records must be stored at intervals no greater than 15 minutes throughout the voyage, capturing supply air temperature, return air temperature, ambient temperature, humidity (where sensor equipped), and power status (on-grid versus genset). The system must auto-detect temperature excursions where recorded temperature deviates from the set point by more than the configurable tolerance (default ±2°C for fresh, ±3°C for frozen), generating timestamped excursion alerts with duration calculation. All temperature data must be retrievable by container number, booking number, or BL number across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R004	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Cold Chain Compliance Certificate Generation	The system must generate Cold Chain Compliance Certificates at key custody transfer points: shipper's premises to road hauler, road hauler to port terminal, port terminal to vessel, vessel to discharge terminal, and discharge terminal to consignee. Each certificate must attest that the cargo was maintained within the prescribed temperature range during that segment, referencing the temperature monitoring data as evidence. The certificate must include container number, seal number (original seal and any reseal events), commodity description, required temperature range, actual temperature range observed (min, max, average), segment start and end timestamps, and responsible party details. Certificates must be generated in bilingual format (English and Arabic for GCC entities, English and Hindi for India entity) and must carry a unique sequential document number per entity with the fiscal year prefix following the Hijri year for GCC entities and Gregorian year for India entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R005	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Reefer Cargo Booking Validation Rules	The system must enforce reefer-specific booking validation rules that prevent operational errors before cargo arrives at the terminal. Validations must include: container ISO type must be a reefer type (22R1, 42R1, 42H0, 42R1, 45R1, 45R8); set temperature must fall within the commodity profile's acceptable range; power plug type must be compatible with the vessel's reefer plug capacity (confirming vessel reefer slot availability from the vessel schedule module); genset requirements must be flagged for port pairs where terminal power infrastructure may be unavailable during transshipment. For shipments involving controlled atmosphere (CA) commodities such as bananas or avocados, the system must validate that the container unit supports CA functionality. For Halal frozen meat shipments to GCC ports, the system must validate presence of Halal certification document reference in the booking, cross-referencing approved Halal certification bodies recognized by ESMA (UAE), SFDA (KSA), and MOPH (Qatar).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R006	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Temperature Excursion Claim Documentation	The system must generate temperature excursion claim documentation packages when cargo temperature deviations result in potential cargo damage claims. The claim package must automatically compile the complete temperature data log, PTI certificate, all cold chain compliance certificates for each transport segment, the booking confirmation showing agreed temperature parameters, the bill of lading, commercial invoice showing cargo value in the transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), and any surveyor reports. The system must calculate the excursion severity metrics including total duration of excursion, maximum deviation from set point, number of discrete excursion events, and percentage of total transit time in excursion. All claim documentation must be bundled into a single downloadable PDF dossier with table of contents, page numbering, and bilingual headers (English and Arabic) for GCC entity claims. The claim value must be presented in both the transaction currency and the entity's functional currency with the exchange rate and conversion date documented.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R007	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Reefer Monitoring Dashboard and Alerts	The system must provide a real-time reefer monitoring dashboard showing all active temperature-controlled containers across all four port jurisdictions with current status indicators: normal (green), warning approaching tolerance (amber), excursion active (red), power disconnected (flashing red), and data logger offline (grey). The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, vessel, voyage, commodity type, and temperature status. Alerts must be configurable per entity with escalation rules: first alert to reefer desk operator after 15 minutes of excursion, escalation to operations manager after 30 minutes, escalation to entity head after 60 minutes. Alert notifications must be delivered via in-application notification, email, and SMS (using local telecom gateways: Ooredoo/Vodafone Qatar, Etisalat/du UAE, STC/Mobily KSA, Jio/Airtel India). The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R008	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Phytosanitary and Health Certificate Linkage	The system must link phytosanitary certificates, health certificates, and food safety clearances to temperature-controlled cargo bookings as mandatory documentation prerequisites. For fresh produce imports into Qatar, the system must validate the presence of a Qatar MOPH health certificate and phytosanitary certificate from the origin country's plant protection authority. For UAE imports through Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port, the system must check for MOCCAE approval and eCITES clearance where applicable. For KSA imports, SFDA import license and phytosanitary clearance must be present. For India exports from JNPT, the APEDA (Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority) registration and PQ (Plant Quarantine) certificate must be validated. The system must prevent gate-in of reefer containers at any terminal when mandatory certificates are missing or expired, with configurable grace periods per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R009	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Controlled Atmosphere Container Documentation	The system must manage specialized documentation for Controlled Atmosphere (CA) reefer containers that regulate O2 and CO2 levels in addition to temperature. The documentation must record initial atmosphere composition at stuffing, target O2 percentage (typically 3-5% for bananas, 2-5% for avocados), target CO2 percentage, nitrogen injection volumes, and atmosphere monitoring readings throughout the voyage. The system must generate a CA Compliance Report showing atmosphere composition trends alongside temperature trends in a unified timeline view. For CA containers arriving at GCC ports, the system must generate food safety declarations confirming that atmospheric modification gases used are food-grade per GSO standards. The CA documentation module must integrate with the cold chain compliance certificate to provide a comprehensive environmental control record covering temperature, humidity, and atmospheric composition for the entire transport chain from origin to destination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R010	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Reefer Power Consumption and Billing Documentation	The system must track and document reefer container power consumption during terminal dwell time for electricity billing purposes. Power consumption records must capture plug-in timestamp, plug-out timestamp, meter reading at plug-in, meter reading at plug-out (where metered connections exist), and calculated consumption in kWh. For ports using flat-rate reefer power charges (common at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali), the system must calculate charges based on hours connected multiplied by the applicable hourly rate. The billing documentation must be generated in the entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with the power charge appearing as a separate line item on the terminal handling invoice. For transshipment reefer containers, the system must aggregate power charges across the origin terminal, transshipment terminal, and destination terminal, presenting a consolidated power cost report. All billing documents must support bilingual output in English and Arabic with Hijri date references for GCC entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R011	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Perishable Cargo Shelf Life Tracking	The system must track remaining shelf life of perishable commodities from the date of harvest, production, or slaughter through the entire logistics chain to the estimated delivery date. For each reefer booking, the system must capture the commodity's total shelf life (in days), the production or harvest date, and calculate the percentage of shelf life consumed at each milestone (gate-in at origin, vessel departure, transshipment if applicable, vessel arrival, gate-out at destination). The system must generate alerts when projected delivery date would result in less than the configurable minimum remaining shelf life percentage (default 40%) being available to the consignee. For frozen Halal meat exports from India to GCC, the system must track both the slaughter date (relevant for Halal certification validity) and the freezing date (relevant for shelf life calculation), displaying dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Shelf life reports must be exportable and shareable with consignees in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R012	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Reefer Container Maintenance History Documentation	The system must maintain a documentation record of reefer container maintenance and repair history linked to the container number, accessible when assessing container suitability for specific cargo types. The maintenance record must capture last PTI date and result, last major service date, compressor hours, firmware version of the reefer control unit, known defects or restrictions, and the container owner or lessor (for leased reefer units from Seaco, Beacon, Triton, Textainer). When a container with known maintenance issues is allocated to a booking, the system must flag the concern to the reefer desk operator with a recommendation. The maintenance documentation must interface with the equipment management module to pull current container condition data and must support attachments for surveyor photographs and repair workshop certificates. All maintenance records must be queryable in both English and Arabic across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R013	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Multi-Commodity Reefer Consolidation Rules	The system must enforce commodity compatibility rules when multiple commodities are consolidated within a single reefer container (LCL reefer or buyer consolidation). The rules engine must validate that all commodities in the consolidation share compatible temperature ranges, humidity requirements, and ethylene sensitivity profiles (e.g., ethylene-producing fruits like apples must not be consolidated with ethylene-sensitive produce like lettuce or bananas). The system must maintain a commodity compatibility matrix covering at least 200 common perishable commodities traded through the Gulf and India corridor. When incompatible commodities are detected in a consolidation booking, the system must reject the combination and suggest alternative container allocation. Documentation for consolidated reefer shipments must list each commodity with its individual temperature requirement and the agreed compromise set point with acknowledgment from the shipper in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R014	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Offline Reefer Data Logger Download and Sync	The system must support offline download of reefer data logger information at terminals where network connectivity is intermittent, particularly at anchorage points, barge feeder terminals, and inland container depots feeding JNPT. The offline module must support USB data logger download from Sensitech TempTale Ultra, Emerson GO Real-Time, Carrier Datacorder, and Thermo King ThermoGuard units, parsing proprietary binary formats into standardized temperature records. Downloaded data must be stored locally on the operator's device with tamper-evident checksums to prevent post-download manipulation. Upon network reconnection, the system must sync data logger records to the central database, matching records to container numbers and bookings through barcode or QR code scanning. The sync process must detect duplicate uploads and resolve timestamp conflicts between the data logger's internal clock and the server's UTC reference. Offline UI must function in both English and Arabic with full RTL support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R015	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Regulatory Temperature Compliance Reporting	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for food safety authorities demonstrating that temperature-controlled cargo was transported within prescribed parameters. For Qatar (MOPH/Food Safety Department), the system must produce reports matching the Qatar Food Safety Modernization framework requirements. For UAE (MOCCAE and municipality-level food control departments in Dubai and Abu Dhabi), reports must comply with UAE.S GSO 1694 cold chain requirements. For KSA (SFDA), reports must align with the Saudi Food Safety System requirements. For India (FSSAI), reports must comply with the Food Safety and Standards (Food Recall Procedure) Regulations. Each regulatory report must include complete chain of custody documentation, continuous temperature records with graphical representation, and attestation of compliance signed by the responsible operations manager. Reports must be auto-generated on cargo arrival and stored for a minimum of five years per entity, with bilingual output and Hijri dates for GCC jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R016	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Reefer Genset Documentation and Tracking	The system must manage documentation for clip-on generator set (genset) units attached to reefer containers for inland transport or transshipment at ports without adequate reefer power infrastructure. The genset documentation must capture genset unit number, fuel type (diesel), fuel tank capacity, estimated run time, attachment date and time, detachment date and time, fuel consumption logs, and maintenance certification validity. For inland transport from JNPT to up-country Indian destinations, the system must generate a genset dispatch note linked to the reefer booking that includes the road transporter details, genset hire charges in INR, and the insurance coverage reference. For genset usage at GCC transshipment ports, charges must be documented in the local entity currency (AED, QAR, SAR) with a separate line item on the freight invoice. The system must alert when genset fuel capacity is insufficient for the estimated transport duration and when genset maintenance certification is approaching expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-002-R017	FEAT-009-3-002	MOD-009	Pharmaceutical Cold Chain GDP Compliance	The system must provide specialized documentation workflows for pharmaceutical cold chain shipments complying with WHO Good Distribution Practice (GDP) guidelines and local pharmaceutical regulatory requirements. For pharma shipments through GCC ports, the system must enforce temperature documentation per UAE MOH GDP guidelines, Qatar MOPH pharmaceutical import regulations, and KSA SFDA Drug Sector requirements. The system must generate a GDP-compliant transport validation report showing unbroken temperature monitoring with calibration certificates for all sensors used, deviation reports for any temperature excursion with root cause analysis fields, and a chain of responsibility document listing every custodian who handled the shipment. Pharmaceutical reefer documentation must maintain a separate, more stringent audit trail with electronic signatures per 21 CFR Part 11 equivalency standards. Temperature monitoring for pharma must use a tighter recording interval of maximum 5 minutes versus the standard 15-minute interval for food cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R001	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Cargo Dimension Capture and Classification	The system must capture detailed cargo dimensions for out-of-gauge shipments including length, width, height, and weight of the cargo piece, along with the over-dimensions that exceed the container or flat rack boundaries: over-height (above 2591mm for standard 20/40 containers), over-width (beyond 2438mm), over-length (beyond 6058mm for 20ft, 12192mm for 40ft), and overweight conditions exceeding the container's maximum payload. The system must classify OOG cargo into categories: over-height only, over-width only, over-length only, combination OOG, and break-bulk (cannot be containerized). For each OOG booking, the system must calculate the number of container slot equivalents consumed on the vessel (e.g., an over-width cargo occupying two cell positions). Dimensions must be captured in metric units (millimeters and kilograms) with conversion display for imperial units, and all dimension documentation must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R002	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Flat Rack and Open Top Container Allocation	The system must manage the allocation and documentation of special equipment types for OOG cargo including 20ft flat rack (22P1), 40ft flat rack (42P1), 20ft open top (22U1), 40ft open top (42U1), 20ft platform (22P0), and 40ft platform (42P3) per ISO 6346 type codes. The allocation workflow must verify equipment availability at the origin port/depot, validate that the selected equipment type can accommodate the cargo dimensions with adequate lashing points, and generate an equipment release order. For cargo requiring collapsible flat racks, the system must track whether the unit is in erected or collapsed position at the depot. The system must enforce that OOG equipment requests are placed with sufficient lead time (configurable per entity, typically 7 days for GCC ports and 5 days for JNPT) and generate alerts when equipment availability is below the minimum threshold. Equipment allocation documents must reference the carrier's tariff for special equipment surcharges in the applicable currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R003	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Stowage Plan Documentation	The system must generate specialized stowage plan documentation for OOG cargo that communicates the vessel loading requirements to the terminal operations team and vessel planner. The stowage plan must include a dimensioned diagram showing the cargo profile overlaid on the container or flat rack with exact overhang measurements on each side (port, starboard, fore, aft, top). The plan must specify required cell guide clearances, adjacent slot restrictions (marking which container positions must remain empty due to the OOG protrusion), under-deck versus on-deck stowage preference with structural weight capacity verification, and stack weight impact. For each OOG unit, the system must calculate the center of gravity position and verify it against the vessel's stability requirements as provided by the vessel operations module. The stowage plan document must be exportable as a dimensioned PDF with measurement annotations in both millimeters and feet/inches for compatibility with international vessel planners.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R004	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Lashing and Securing Plan Documentation	The system must generate lashing and securing plan documentation for OOG cargo specifying the type, quantity, and placement of securing materials required per IMO CSS Code (Code of Safe Practice for Cargo Stowage and Securing) and the vessel's Cargo Securing Manual. The plan must detail: number and type of twist locks, lashing rods, turnbuckles, chain lashings, wire rope lashings, and timber dunnage required; lashing angles ensuring they fall within the 25-75 degree acceptable range per CSS Code; calculated maximum securing load (MSL) for each lashing element based on cargo weight, expected vessel motion accelerations (rolling, pitching, heaving), and the lashing arrangement geometry. The plan must account for trade-route-specific weather conditions, with higher securing forces specified for monsoon season (June-September) on the India-Gulf trade lane. Lashing material costs must be documented in the entity's currency for billing purposes, and the securing plan must be approved by the vessel's chief officer with a digital signature capture workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R008	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Damage and Survey Recording	The system must support recording container damage surveys following IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) coding standards, capturing damage location (using IICL grid reference), damage type (dent, hole, rust, contamination), component affected (panel, rail, corner casting, door, floor), severity classification, and estimated repair cost. Surveyors must be able to attach geo-tagged photographs with Arabic or English annotations and complete surveys on mobile devices in offline mode at remote yards or anchorage points. The AI engine must analyze damage photographs using computer vision to automatically suggest IICL damage codes and estimate repair costs based on historical MNR data, achieving a minimum 85% accuracy rate for common damage types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R005	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Route Survey and Transport Permit Documentation	The system must manage route survey documentation and transport permit workflows for OOG cargo requiring special road transport from shipper premises to the port or from the port to consignee premises. For road transport in Qatar, the system must generate permit applications for Qatar Ministry of Transport (Ashghal) for loads exceeding standard dimensions. For UAE movements to Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port, the system must interface with RTA (Roads and Transport Authority) permit requirements for Dubai and ITC (Integrated Transport Centre) for Abu Dhabi. For KSA, the system must comply with Transport General Authority special load permits. For India JNPT hinterland movements, the system must generate Regional Transport Office (RTO) over-dimensional cargo (ODC) permits. Each permit application must include cargo dimensions, proposed route with waypoints, escort vehicle requirements, time-of-day movement restrictions, bridge weight and height clearances along the route, and insurance coverage documentation. The system must track permit validity dates and alert when permits are approaching expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R006	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Cargo Photography and Condition Documentation	The system must capture and manage photographic documentation of OOG cargo at multiple milestones: at shipper's premises during survey, during loading onto the container or flat rack, at terminal gate-in, during vessel loading, at discharge terminal, and at final delivery. Each photograph must be geotagged with GPS coordinates, timestamped, and linked to the booking and BL number. The system must enforce a minimum photographic documentation set per OOG booking: four side views (port, starboard, fore, aft), one top view, one close-up of each lashing point, and one overview showing dimension reference markers. Photos must be annotated with cargo dimensions, lashing positions, and any visible damage using an in-application markup tool. The condition documentation must generate a before-and-after comparison report for origin versus destination, supporting cargo damage claims. Photo storage must comply with data residency requirements for each entity jurisdiction, with Qatar data stored in-country per Qatar Data Privacy Law.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R007	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Surcharge and Special Handling Cost Documentation	The system must calculate, document, and invoice all surcharges and special handling costs associated with OOG cargo. Surcharge categories must include: OOG surcharge per carrier tariff (varying by over-dimension category and trade lane), special equipment surcharge (flat rack, open top, platform premium over standard container rates), extra slot charges for adjacent empty positions consumed, special crane charges for lifts exceeding standard spreader capacity (typically requiring mobile harbor crane or floating crane for units over 40 tonnes), lashing material costs, survey and escort fees, and port authority OOG handling fees. Each charge must be denominated in the booking currency (USD for ocean freight) and converted to the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) at the applicable exchange rate. The system must generate a detailed cost breakdown document in both English and Arabic, with line-item references to carrier tariff clauses and terminal tariff schedules. For project cargo with multiple OOG pieces, the system must aggregate costs and provide a lot-level cost summary alongside individual piece breakdowns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R008	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Marine Cargo Insurance Documentation for OOG	The system must generate and manage marine cargo insurance documentation specific to OOG shipments, which typically carry higher risk premiums than standard containerized cargo. The system must produce insurance declaration documents capturing insured value (in the shipment currency plus 10% CIF uplift per standard practice), cargo description with exact dimensions and weight, packing and securing method, vessel and voyage details, and the special conditions or warranties applicable to OOG transport. The system must support integration with insurance broker portals for major marine insurers operating in the Gulf and India market. For CIF or CIP INCOTERMS shipments where the seller arranges insurance, the system must ensure the insurance certificate references the OOG nature of the cargo and confirms cover under Institute Cargo Clauses (A) or equivalent with OOG extension. Insurance premium calculations must be available in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with the premium displayed on the commercial invoice in the transaction currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R009	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Structural Strength and Weight Distribution Calculation	The system must provide structural strength verification and weight distribution calculation documentation for OOG cargo loaded on flat racks and platforms. The calculation must verify that the cargo weight does not exceed the container's floor loading capacity (kg per linear metre), that point loads from cargo feet or supports are within the container floor's structural limits, and that the overall center of gravity remains within acceptable parameters for safe lifting and transport. The system must generate a weight distribution diagram showing load placement on the container floor with color-coded zones indicating acceptable, cautionary, and exceeded weight densities. For heavy-lift cargo pieces exceeding 25 tonnes, the system must produce a detailed lifting plan specifying the crane capacity required, sling arrangement, and lift geometry per LOLER (Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations) equivalency standards applicable in GCC and India. All structural calculations must be reviewable by a qualified marine surveyor with digital approval workflow, and calculations must be stored as part of the cargo documentation dossier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R010	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Cargo Customs Documentation Handling	The system must handle specialized customs documentation requirements for OOG and project cargo which often involves multiple packages declared on a single bill of lading with varying HS codes and values. The system must generate packing lists with individual piece dimensions, weights, and HS code classifications for each OOG component, even when multiple pieces are loaded on a single flat rack. For project cargo imports into Qatar Free Zone or Jebel Ali Free Zone (JAFZA), the system must generate temporary admission documentation (ATA Carnet equivalents or local temporary import permits) where the cargo is project equipment destined for re-export. The customs documentation must accommodate the KSA Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority (ZATCA) FASAH portal requirements for OOG declarations. For India JNPT, the system must handle the Indian Customs ICEGATE EDI filing for non-standard cargo categories. All customs values must be declared in the port's prescribed currency with the exchange rate per the customs authority's official rate for the declaration date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R003	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	Master Data Import Tool	CSV/Excel import for: customers, vendors, ports, vessels, containers, employees. Template download per entity type. Validation on upload: duplicate check, required fields, format validation. Preview first 10 rows before confirm. Background import job with progress bar. Email notification on completion with success/error summary.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R011	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Break-Bulk and Project Cargo Lot Management	The system must support lot-level management for break-bulk and project cargo shipments consisting of multiple OOG pieces shipped as a single commercial consignment. A project cargo lot must group multiple booking lines under a single lot reference, tracking each piece's individual documentation (dimensions, weight, equipment type, stowage plan, lashing plan) while maintaining a consolidated lot-level view. The lot management must support partial shipments where not all pieces ship on the same vessel or voyage, maintaining a lot completion tracker showing which pieces have been shipped, which are in transit, and which are pending. The system must generate lot-level documentation including a consolidated packing list, a master cargo manifest, and a lot progress report showing percentage completion by weight, volume, and piece count. For EPC (Engineering, Procurement, Construction) project cargo, the system must support integration with the project timeline showing cargo delivery alignment with the construction schedule. All lot documentation must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R012	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Vessel Stability Impact Assessment Documentation	The system must generate vessel stability impact assessment documentation for heavy and oversized OOG cargo that may significantly affect the vessel's trim, list, and stability parameters. The assessment must calculate the cargo's impact on the vessel's metacentric height (GM), righting lever curve (GZ), and compliance with IMO Intact Stability Code criteria. The system must interface with the vessel operations module to obtain the vessel's lightweight data, current loading condition, and tank soundings. For OOG cargo pieces exceeding 50 tonnes, the system must mandate a pre-loading stability calculation signed off by the vessel's chief officer and the company's designated person ashore (DPA). The stability assessment document must include before-loading and after-loading comparison of key stability parameters, ballast adjustment recommendations if required, and confirmation that the vessel remains within all Class society and flag state stability criteria. The document must be formatted for submission to port state control inspectors if requested during safety inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R013	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Cargo Tracking and Milestone Documentation	The system must provide enhanced tracking and milestone documentation for OOG cargo given its high-value, time-sensitive nature in project logistics. The system must track and document milestones including: factory readiness date, pre-shipment survey date, special transport permit obtained, cargo delivered to port, terminal gate-in and inspection, vessel loading commenced, vessel loading completed, vessel departure, transshipment operations (if applicable), vessel arrival at discharge port, discharge commenced, discharge completed, terminal gate-out, and delivery to project site. Each milestone must capture the actual date and time in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, the responsible party, any delay reasons categorized by cause code (weather, equipment, documentation, customs, vessel), and photographic evidence where applicable. The milestone documentation must feed into a project cargo KPI dashboard showing on-time performance by trade lane, carrier, and entity with exportable reports in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R014	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Terminal Handling Instruction Documentation	The system must generate detailed Terminal Handling Instructions (THI) for OOG cargo that communicate special handling requirements to terminal operators at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. The THI must specify: lifting equipment requirements (ship-to-shore crane, mobile harbor crane, floating crane with minimum safe working load), rigging arrangement (spreader, chains, wire slings with specified lengths and capacities), lift speed restrictions, weather limitations for handling (maximum wind speed, typically 15-20 knots for OOG lifts), designated yard storage location with ground bearing capacity confirmation, required standby emergency equipment, and personnel safety perimeter. The THI must include a step-by-step handling sequence diagram and must be issued to the terminal at least 48 hours before the vessel's scheduled arrival. The terminal must acknowledge receipt and confirm readiness through a digital acknowledgment workflow. THI documents must be bilingual (English and Arabic for GCC ports) and must reference the applicable port authority safe working practices manual.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R015	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	OOG Third-Party Surveyor Integration	The system must integrate with third-party marine surveyor workflows for OOG cargo inspections including pre-shipment surveys, loading supervision, lashing inspections, and discharge condition surveys. The system must maintain a registry of approved surveyors per port location (covering major firms such as Bureau Veritas, SGS, Cotecna, Lloyd's Register, and RINA operating across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT). Survey appointment requests must be generated from within the system and dispatched to the assigned surveyor with complete cargo details, booking information, vessel schedule, and specific inspection scope. Survey reports must be uploaded directly into the system with structured data extraction for key findings: condition assessment (satisfactory, marginal, unsatisfactory), lashing adequacy rating, dimension verification against booking declaration, and recommended actions. Survey fees must be captured in the surveyor's invoicing currency and converted to the entity's functional currency for cost allocation. The system must alert when a survey result indicates deficiencies requiring remediation before vessel loading.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-003-R016	FEAT-009-3-003	MOD-009	Offline OOG Documentation for Remote Survey Locations	The system must support offline OOG documentation creation and survey data capture at remote locations such as industrial fabrication yards, project construction sites, and inland depots where internet connectivity is unavailable or unreliable. The offline module must allow surveyors and operations staff to capture cargo dimensions via manual entry or Bluetooth-connected laser distance meter integration, take and annotate photographs, complete lashing inspection checklists, and generate draft THI documents. All offline data must be stored locally with tamper-evident timestamps and GPS coordinates. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize offline data with the central database, performing conflict resolution where multiple users may have updated the same OOG booking. The offline module must support both English and Arabic interfaces with RTL rendering, and must include a local cache of carrier tariffs, equipment specifications, and lashing material inventories to enable accurate documentation even without server connectivity. Sync completion must be confirmed via notification to both the field user and the operations desk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R004	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	User Onboarding Tour	First-login interactive product tour per role: highlight sidebar, explain key modules for their role, show first action to take. Tour skippable. Restart tour from Help menu. Contextual tooltips on key UI elements for first 7 days.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R001	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Centralized Document Repository Architecture	The system must provide a centralized document repository that stores all shipping documentation generated or received across all four entity jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) including but not limited to bills of lading, commercial invoices, packing lists, certificates of origin, customs declarations, dangerous goods declarations, reefer monitoring records, OOG documentation, insurance certificates, and vessel documentation. The repository must enforce entity-level data isolation where each entity's documents are logically partitioned while supporting cross-entity search by authorized users with appropriate permissions. Document storage must comply with data residency requirements: Qatar documents stored on infrastructure within Qatar per the Personal Data Privacy Protection Law (PDPPL), UAE documents per Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021, KSA documents per PDPL, and India documents per Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023. The repository must support a minimum storage capacity of 10 million documents with sub-second metadata query response times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R002	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	AI-Powered Document Classification and Indexing	The system must employ AI-powered document classification to automatically categorize incoming documents by type (bill of lading, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, customs declaration, DG declaration, survey report, insurance certificate, phytosanitary certificate, Halal certificate, fumigation certificate, etc.) upon upload or system generation. The AI classifier must achieve a minimum accuracy of 95% for English documents and 90% for Arabic documents, with confidence scoring and manual review routing for low-confidence classifications. Each document must be automatically indexed with extracted metadata including booking number, BL number, container number, vessel name, voyage number, shipper name, consignee name, port of loading (UN/LOCODE), port of discharge, commodity description, and document date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. The indexing engine must support Arabic text extraction with proper RTL character handling, diacritical mark normalization, and Arabic morphological analysis for search relevance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R003	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Regulatory Retention Policy Enforcement	The system must enforce document retention policies that comply with the regulatory requirements of all four operating jurisdictions. For Qatar, commercial shipping documents must be retained for a minimum of 10 years per Qatar Commercial Companies Law. For UAE, documents must be retained for a minimum of 5 years per Federal Commercial Transactions Law, with customs documents retained for 5 years per Federal Customs Law. For KSA, documents must be retained per ZATCA requirements of 6 years for tax-related documents and Saudi Commercial Court regulations. For India, documents must be retained for 8 years per Indian Companies Act Section 128 and customs documents per the Customs Act 1962 Section 14. The system must maintain a configurable retention schedule per document type per entity, with automatic retention period assignment upon document archival. Documents approaching end-of-retention must trigger review notifications 90 days before the expiry, allowing authorized users to extend retention or approve destruction. The system must prevent deletion of documents within their mandatory retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R004	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Full-Text Search with Multilingual Support	The system must provide full-text search capability across all archived documents supporting English, Arabic, and Hindi text. The search engine must support OCR-extracted text from scanned documents, typed text from system-generated documents, and metadata fields. Arabic search must handle morphological variants, root-word matching (searching for the root form must return all derived forms), and transliteration matching where users type Arabic proper nouns in Latin characters. The search must support Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT), phrase matching with quotation marks, wildcard patterns, date range filtering in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and field-specific search (e.g., BL number, container number, vessel name). Search results must display document thumbnails, key metadata, relevance score, and highlighted matching text snippets. The system must return results within 3 seconds for queries across the full document corpus. Search history and saved searches must be maintained per user with the option to set up automated search alerts for recurring queries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R005	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Version Control and Audit History	The system must maintain complete version control for all documents that undergo amendments, corrections, or reissuances. Each version must be stored as an immutable record with version number, creation timestamp in UTC with local timezone offset, creating user, entity code, and change reason. For bills of lading, the system must track the progression from draft BL to original BL to amended BL to switch BL if applicable, maintaining the full chain with clear version labeling. For customs declarations, amendment submissions must reference the original declaration number and highlight changed fields. The system must support side-by-side comparison of any two versions of the same document, with differences highlighted in both the document content and metadata fields. The audit trail for each document must record every access event (view, download, print, email, share) with user identity, timestamp, IP address, and access purpose. Version history and audit trails must be exportable as a consolidated PDF report for regulatory inspection purposes, formatted in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R006	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Bundling and Dossier Assembly	The system must enable automated and manual assembly of document bundles (dossiers) that group related documents for specific business purposes. Predefined bundle templates must include: Export Documentation Set (commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, BL, customs declaration, insurance certificate), Import Clearance Set (BL copy, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, customs declaration, import permit, health/phytosanitary certificates), DG Documentation Set (DGD, MSDS, container packing certificate, emergency response sheet, approval records), and Reefer Documentation Set (PTI certificate, temperature monitoring log, cold chain compliance certificates, commodity certificates). Each bundle must be generated as a single PDF with a cover page listing all included documents, a table of contents with page references, sequential page numbering, and entity letterhead. Bundle generation must support bilingual output (English and Arabic) with the language order configurable per entity. Users must be able to create custom bundle templates and share them within their entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R005	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	Go-Live Readiness Assessment	Admin panel Go-Live Checklist: system checks all required master data imported (min thresholds), at least 2 admin users, MFA enabled for all admins, payment method confirmed, SSL certificate valid, all purchased modules activated. Show readiness score 0-100%. Generate PDF go-live report.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R007	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Automated Document Archival Workflow	The system must implement an automated archival workflow that transitions documents from active status to archived status based on configurable triggers. Default triggers must include: shipment completion (all containers delivered and proof of delivery received), invoice settlement (all related invoices paid and reconciled), customs clearance completion (final customs release obtained at destination), and time-based archival (configurable days after BL date, defaulting to 90 days). The archival workflow must verify document completeness before archival, checking that all mandatory documents for the shipment type are present per entity-specific checklists. Incomplete document sets must be flagged to the documentation team for resolution before archival proceeds. Archived documents must be moved to a lower-cost storage tier while remaining fully searchable and retrievable. The archival action must be logged in the audit trail, and the system must send a confirmation notification to the responsible documentation officer. Archival workflows must respect entity-specific business calendars including Hijri calendar holidays for GCC entities and Indian national holidays for the India entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R008	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Rapid Document Retrieval for Regulatory Inspections	The system must provide a rapid retrieval interface specifically designed for responding to regulatory inspection requests from maritime authorities, customs authorities, port state control, and tax authorities. The interface must support query by vessel name, IMO number, voyage number, date range, port of call, container number, BL number, shipper/consignee name, and commodity type. The system must generate a regulatory response package within 15 minutes of query execution, assembling all matching documents into a structured dossier with an index page listing each document with its type, date, reference number, and page number. For port state control inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must be capable of retrieving the complete DG documentation set for any container on a vessel within 5 minutes. The retrieval interface must support access by designated compliance officers only, with all retrieval actions logged for internal audit purposes. Retrieved packages must be available for secure sharing via encrypted download links with configurable expiry periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R009	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Digitization and OCR Processing	The system must provide document digitization capabilities for converting paper-based shipping documents into searchable digital records. The OCR engine must support scanning and recognition of English, Arabic, and Hindi text from documents including handwritten customs stamps, port authority endorsements, and manually signed certificates. The OCR engine must achieve a minimum character recognition accuracy of 98% for printed English text, 95% for printed Arabic text, and 90% for Hindi Devanagari script. For handwritten annotations common on bills of lading and delivery orders, the system must extract text with confidence scoring and route low-confidence extractions for manual verification. Digitized documents must be stored in searchable PDF/A format for long-term archival compliance per ISO 19005. The system must support bulk scanning workflows where a documentation clerk can feed multiple documents through a scanner and the system automatically separates, classifies, and indexes each document. Scan quality validation must reject images below 300 DPI resolution or with excessive skew, prompting rescan.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R010	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Cross-Entity Document Sharing and Access Control	The system must enforce granular access control for document sharing across the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Access control must operate at three levels: entity level (entire entity's documents), department level (operations, finance, customs, commercial), and document type level (bills of lading, financial documents, customs declarations). The system must support role-based access where a user's role determines their default document access permissions, with the ability to grant additional temporary access for specific shipments or document sets. Cross-entity document access must require explicit authorization from the document-owning entity's compliance officer, with access grants logged and time-limited (default 30 days). For multi-entity shipments (e.g., transshipment via Jebel Ali where both UAE and Qatar entities handle documentation), the system must automatically establish shared access for the participating entities. Document watermarking must be applied to all viewed and downloaded documents showing the viewing user's name, entity, and access timestamp to deter unauthorized distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R011	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Expiry and Renewal Alert System	The system must monitor and alert on the expiry dates of time-sensitive documents linked to ongoing operations. Monitored document types must include: import/export licenses (per entity per commodity per regulatory authority), carrier contracts and rate agreements, insurance policies (hull and cargo), port authority operating licenses, customs broker licenses, Halal certification authority approvals, IMDG Code competency certificates for DG desk staff, vessel class certificates (for owned/operated vessels), and health/phytosanitary certificates linked to active shipments. The alert system must generate notifications at configurable intervals before expiry (default 90 days, 60 days, 30 days, 14 days, and 7 days). Alerts must be routed to the document owner and their department head, with overdue documents escalated to entity management. Each alert must display the document reference, current expiry date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, the regulatory consequence of lapse, and a direct link to the document in the repository. The system must maintain an entity-level compliance dashboard showing all documents by expiry status: valid, expiring soon, expired, and renewal in progress.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R012	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Secure Document Transmission and Digital Signatures	The system must support secure electronic transmission of documents to external parties including shipping lines, customs authorities, port authorities, banks (for letter of credit document presentation), insurance companies, and consignees. Transmission methods must include secure email with TLS encryption, API-based submission to port community systems (Qatar QPorts, UAE Mirsal/DHLE, KSA FASAH, India ICEGATE PCS), and secure download links with recipient authentication. The system must support digital signatures compliant with the electronic signature laws of each jurisdiction: Qatar Law No. 16 of 2010, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 46 of 2021 on Electronic Transactions, KSA Anti-Cyber Crime Law and e-transaction regulations, and India Information Technology Act 2000 Section 3. Digital signatures must use PKI-based certificates from nationally recognized certificate authorities. For letter of credit document presentations, the system must generate a presentation cover letter with the required bank reference format and transmit the document set to the nominated bank per UCP 600 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R013	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Analytics and Reporting	The system must provide analytics and reporting capabilities on the document repository covering document volumes, processing times, completeness metrics, and retrieval patterns. Standard reports must include: monthly document generation volumes by type and entity, average document processing time from draft to final by document type, document completeness rate per shipment (percentage of mandatory documents present at archival), most frequently retrieved document types and top search queries, document exception reports showing missing or overdue documents per shipment. The analytics dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), document type, and trade lane. The system must identify documentation bottlenecks through AI-powered analysis of processing time patterns, flagging systematic delays such as consistently late certificate of origin issuance for a specific trade lane. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with bilingual headers (English and Arabic). The analytics engine must support scheduled report generation and email distribution to configured recipients on daily, weekly, or monthly frequencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R014	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Document Template Management	The system must provide a template management facility for creating and maintaining standardized document templates used across the four operating entities. Templates must be configurable for entity-specific letterheads (incorporating company name in English and Arabic, CR/trade license number, VAT/TRN number, entity address), regulatory header/footer requirements, and field layouts. The template engine must support dynamic field insertion for all shipping data elements (booking details, BL details, container details, party details, financial details) with conditional sections that appear or hide based on data context (e.g., DG section only appears when the booking contains dangerous goods). Templates must support bilingual layout with English and Arabic text blocks, with configurable primary language per entity. Hijri date fields must be available alongside Gregorian dates in all templates. The system must maintain template version control where updates to a template do not retroactively affect previously generated documents. Template creation and modification must be restricted to authorized document administrators per entity, with an approval workflow for template changes that impact regulatory compliance documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R015	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Offline Document Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to critical documents for users operating in low-connectivity environments such as vessel boarding for port state control inspections, remote terminal locations, and inland transport checkpoints. Users must be able to designate specific shipments, bookings, or document bundles for offline availability, which the system will download and encrypt on the user's device. The offline cache must include full document content (PDFs), metadata, and version information. Maximum offline cache size must be configurable per device profile (default 2GB, supporting approximately 10,000 documents). When operating offline, the system must provide full search capability across cached documents with the same search interface and multilingual support (English, Arabic, Hindi) as the online system. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any annotations, notes, or status changes made to documents while offline, using last-write-wins conflict resolution for metadata updates and version branching for content modifications. The sync engine must generate a reconciliation report showing all changes made during the offline period for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R016	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Legal Hold and Litigation Support	The system must provide a legal hold capability that prevents the deletion, modification, or archival of documents subject to legal proceedings, regulatory investigations, or dispute resolution processes. A legal hold must be initiatable by authorized legal or compliance officers and must specify the scope by any combination of: entity, date range, vessel, voyage, booking numbers, BL numbers, container numbers, parties involved, and document types. Once a legal hold is activated, all documents matching the scope criteria must be frozen in their current state, with any attempted modifications blocked and logged. The legal hold must override standard retention policies, preventing destruction of held documents even if they have exceeded their normal retention period. The system must generate a legal hold notification to all users who have access to held documents, informing them of the hold and prohibiting external disclosure. For dispute resolution related to cargo damage claims, demurrage disputes, or freight payment disputes common in the Gulf-India trade, the system must export held documents in a litigation-ready format with chain of custody certification and Bates numbering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R017	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Blockchain-Anchored Document Integrity Verification	The system must provide document integrity verification through blockchain-anchored hash records that allow any party to verify that a document has not been altered since issuance. Upon finalization of critical documents (original bills of lading, customs declarations, dangerous goods declarations, certificates of origin), the system must calculate a cryptographic hash (SHA-256) of the document content and anchor this hash to a distributed ledger. The verification service must allow external parties (banks, customs authorities, consignees) to upload a document and verify its integrity against the blockchain-anchored hash without requiring system access. For electronic bills of lading, the system must comply with DCSA (Digital Container Shipping Association) standards for eBL including the DCSA eBL envelope specification. The integrity verification must operate independently of any single entity's infrastructure, ensuring that document authenticity can be confirmed even if the issuing entity's systems are temporarily unavailable. The system must maintain a verification log recording each verification request with the requester's identity, timestamp, document reference, and verification result (authentic or tampered).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R009	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Multi-Entity Container Transfer Processing	The system must support inter-entity container transfers between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations with full financial reconciliation. Transfer pricing must comply with OECD transfer pricing guidelines and each jurisdiction's tax authority requirements (GTA Qatar, FTA UAE, ZATCA KSA, CBDT India). Each transfer must generate paired debit/credit entries in both entities' ledgers with appropriate currency conversion at the transfer date spot rate. The workflow must include approval chains configurable per entity and transfer value threshold, with mandatory documentation including transfer agreement, customs declarations for cross-border movements, and container condition survey at both origin and destination. The system must maintain a complete chain-of-custody audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-3-002-R006	FEAT-000-3-002	\N	In-App Help and Support	Help centre: searchable knowledge base with markdown articles per module. Contextual help button on every page shows relevant article. Live chat widget during business hours 09:00-18:00 Gulf time. Support ticket creation from within app auto-attaches page URL, user role, last 5 actions. SLA tracking shown to user.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-009-3-004-R018	FEAT-009-3-004	MOD-009	Automated Document Disaster Recovery and Backup	The system must implement a comprehensive disaster recovery and backup strategy for the document repository ensuring zero document loss and maximum 1-hour recovery point objective (RPO) across all four entity jurisdictions. The backup strategy must include: real-time replication to a secondary data center within the same jurisdiction (for data residency compliance), daily incremental backups with 30-day retention, weekly full backups with 12-month retention, and monthly archival backups with retention matching the maximum regulatory retention period (10 years for Qatar). Backup integrity must be verified through automated daily checksum validation of a random sample of 1% of backed-up documents. The disaster recovery plan must include documented procedures for full repository restoration, individual document recovery, and cross-entity failover scenarios. Recovery drills must be executable on demand with the system generating a drill report showing recovery time, data completeness verification, and any discrepancies detected. Backup status dashboards must be available to IT administrators per entity showing last successful backup time, backup size, replication lag, and any failed backup alerts. All backup operations must maintain document encryption at rest using AES-256 and in transit using TLS 1.3.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R001	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Master Record Creation	The system must allow creation and maintenance of a comprehensive container master record conforming to ISO 6346 standards, capturing container number (owner code, equipment category, serial number, check digit), ISO size/type code (e.g., 22G1 for 20ft GP, 45R1 for 40ft HC reefer), tare weight, max gross weight, cubic capacity, manufacturing date, manufacturer details, and owning/leasing entity. The record must support multi-entity ownership tagging for Qatar (QAR), UAE (AED), KSA (SAR), and India (INR) entities with automatic currency association. Each container must be uniquely identifiable and validated against BIC (Bureau International des Containers) check-digit algorithms to prevent duplicate or invalid registrations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R002	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Real-Time Container Location Tracking	The system must track real-time container locations using GPS/IoT device integration, AIS vessel tracking feeds, and terminal operating system gate events across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Location updates must be processed within 30 seconds of receipt and displayed on an interactive map with Arabic and English labels. The tracking must support geofencing alerts when containers enter or exit defined zones such as port terminals, inland depots, customer premises, and border crossings. Offline location buffering must be supported for IoT devices in low-connectivity areas with automatic sync upon reconnection, maintaining a complete audit trail of all position updates with UTC and local timezone timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R003	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Status Lifecycle Management	The system must manage container status transitions through a configurable state machine covering statuses including: Available, Allocated, Loaded, In-Transit, Discharged, Gated-In, Gated-Out, Under-Repair, Damaged, Off-Hire, Sold, Scrapped, and Lost. Each transition must enforce business rules such as requiring a damage survey before moving to Under-Repair status, or mandating a release order before changing from Allocated to Loaded. The AI engine must predict status changes based on historical patterns and current vessel schedules, flagging anomalies such as containers stuck in a status beyond configurable SLA thresholds. All status changes must be logged with user ID, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and justification notes in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R004	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Lease and Ownership Contract Integration	The system must integrate container lease contracts from major lessors (Triton, Textainer, Beacon, Seaco, CAI) capturing lease type (master lease, spot lease, finance lease, one-way), per-diem rates in the contract currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), pickup and drop-off locations, minimum lease duration, and damage protection plan terms. The AI engine must calculate lease cost projections and recommend optimal lease-vs-buy decisions based on utilization forecasts per trade lane. The system must generate automated alerts 30, 60, and 90 days before lease expiry and support multi-currency billing reconciliation with automatic exchange rate conversion using central bank published rates from QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R005	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Fleet Utilization Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time fleet utilization dashboard showing KPIs including total fleet size, available units, units on-hire, idle days, average trip duration, turns per year, and revenue per TEU per day. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (QAHMD, AEKLP, AEJEA, INNHV), container type (GP, HC, RF, OT, FR, TK), size (20ft, 40ft, 45ft), and ownership (owned vs leased). Visualization must include heat maps, trend charts, and comparative bar graphs with bilingual Arabic/English axis labels. The AI engine must provide natural language summaries in both Arabic and English explaining fleet performance trends and recommending optimization actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R006	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	AI-Driven Fleet Size Optimization	The AI engine must continuously analyze historical booking data, seasonal demand patterns, trade lane imbalances, and market forecasts to recommend optimal fleet size per container type and geography. Recommendations must consider lead times for container procurement (typically 8-12 weeks from Chinese manufacturers), lease activation timelines, and planned vessel service changes. The system must generate quarterly fleet plans with projected surplus and deficit by type and location, factoring in Hajj/Umrah seasonal peaks for KSA, Ramadan demand shifts, Indian festival season volumes (Diwali, Navratri), and Qatar National Day logistics surges. All monetary projections must be presented in both local entity currency and consolidated USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R007	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Age and Depreciation Tracking	The system must track container age from manufacturing date and calculate depreciation based on configurable methods (straight-line, declining balance, units-of-production) per entity accounting standards. For Qatar and UAE entities, depreciation must follow IFRS standards; for KSA, it must comply with SOCPA guidelines; and for India, it must conform to Ind-AS 16 requirements. The system must flag containers approaching end-of-economic-life thresholds (typically 12-15 years for dry containers, 10-12 years for reefers) and recommend disposal, refurbishment, or conversion decisions. Book value calculations must be maintained in the entity's local currency with consolidated reporting in USD, and all depreciation schedules must support both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal year periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R010	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Insurance and Claims Management	The system must maintain insurance coverage records for each container including policy number, insurer, coverage type (all-risk, total loss only, third-party liability), coverage amount in policy currency, premium amounts, deductible thresholds, and policy validity period. When container damage exceeds configurable thresholds (e.g., repair cost exceeding 50% of insured value or USD 3,000 equivalent), the system must automatically initiate an insurance claim workflow. Claims must track submission date, claim reference, adjuster assignment, settlement amount, and resolution status. The AI engine must predict claim approval probability based on historical claim patterns and damage classification, and flag potentially fraudulent claims based on anomaly detection across repair vendor pricing and damage frequency patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R011	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Regulatory Compliance Certification Tracking	The system must track all regulatory compliance certifications for each container including CSC (Convention for Safe Containers) plate validity (maximum 30 months for periodic examination or ACEP continuous examination program), customs seal integrity records, fumigation and phytosanitary certificates, IMDG (International Maritime Dangerous Goods) approved container certifications for hazmat cargo, and food-grade certifications for containers used in edible commodity transport. The system must generate alerts 60 days before any certification expiry and automatically block allocation of containers with expired certifications. For GCC customs requirements, the system must track TIR carnet eligibility and GCC common customs transit documentation. Certificates must be stored with bilingual descriptions in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R012	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Inventory Reconciliation	The system must support periodic physical inventory reconciliation of containers at each yard and depot location. The process must allow barcode or RFID scanning of container numbers during physical counts, automatically matching against system records and highlighting discrepancies including missing containers, unrecorded containers, and status mismatches. The reconciliation workflow must support partial counts (by block, row, or container type) and full yard sweeps. Discrepancy resolution must include investigation workflows with root cause categorization (data entry error, gate system failure, unauthorized movement, theft). The AI engine must predict likely discrepancy causes based on historical patterns and suggest corrective actions. Reconciliation reports must be generated in both Arabic and English with timestamps in local timezone and UTC.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R013	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Pool and Sharing Agreements	The system must manage container pool agreements with other shipping lines and container sharing arrangements, tracking pool contributions, withdrawals, and imbalance positions. Pool interchange agreements must capture terms including per-diem charges, free-time allowances, drop-off locations, and damage liability allocation. The system must calculate real-time pool balance positions per partner, per location, and per container type, with automatic settlement generation at configurable intervals (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly). Financial settlements must handle multi-currency conversions between pool members operating in different currency zones (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). The AI engine must recommend optimal pool rebalancing strategies to minimize empty repositioning costs while maintaining adequate stock at high-demand locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R014	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	IoT Device and Smart Container Integration	The system must integrate with IoT platforms and smart container devices from vendors including Orbcomm, Globe Tracker, Nexxiot, and Traxens, receiving telemetry data via MQTT or REST API protocols. Supported data points must include GPS position, door open/close events, temperature (for reefers), humidity, shock/impact detection, light intrusion, and tilt angle. The system must process a minimum of 100,000 telemetry events per hour across the entire fleet and store historical telemetry for a minimum of 24 months for analytics purposes. The AI engine must correlate telemetry anomalies (unexpected door openings, route deviations, impact events) with cargo claims and security incidents, generating automated alerts to operations teams via configurable notification channels in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R015	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Lifecycle Cost Analysis	The system must calculate and report total cost of ownership (TCO) for each container over its entire lifecycle, aggregating acquisition cost, lease charges, repositioning costs, maintenance and repair expenditures, insurance premiums, depreciation, and disposal/residual value. TCO must be computed in the owning entity's local currency with consolidated USD equivalent for group reporting. The AI engine must benchmark individual container TCO against fleet averages by type and age band, identifying outlier containers with disproportionately high costs. Lifecycle cost projections must factor in predicted future MNR costs based on container age degradation curves and trade route severity scoring. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual Arabic/English headers and support both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal year groupings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R016	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Offline Container Operations Support	The system must provide a fully functional offline mode for container operations at locations with intermittent or no internet connectivity, including remote inland depots in India and desert staging areas in KSA. Offline capabilities must include container survey recording, gate-in/gate-out processing, damage photography and annotation, and status updates. All offline transactions must be queued locally on the mobile device with conflict detection and resolution logic for when multiple users modify the same container record offline. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must automatically synchronize queued transactions in chronological order, presenting any conflicts to supervisors for manual resolution. Offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption to protect sensitive container and cargo information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-001-R017	FEAT-010-1-001	MOD-010	Container Movement History and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete, immutable movement history for every container from the point of registration, recording every gate event, vessel loading/discharge, rail/truck transfer, yard move, status change, survey, repair, and financial transaction. The audit trail must capture the user or system process initiating the event, timestamp in UTC with local timezone conversion, geographic coordinates where available, and linked document references (bills of lading, delivery orders, survey reports). The history must be searchable and filterable by date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), event type, location, and associated entity. The system must support regulatory and auditor access with read-only views and must retain movement history for a minimum of seven years in compliance with GCC commercial record-keeping requirements and Indian Companies Act provisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R002	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Supply-Demand Imbalance Visualization	The system must provide a real-time visual dashboard showing container supply-demand imbalances across all network locations, using color-coded indicators (surplus in blue, balanced in green, deficit in amber, critical deficit in red). The visualization must include an interactive map view centered on the Middle East-India trade corridor, a tabular view sortable by location and container type, and a time-series view showing projected imbalance trends over the next 12 weeks. Each location node must display current stock, forecasted demand, confirmed inbound movements, and net position. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with right-aligned numerical displays in local conventions (Arabic-Indic numerals optionally) and must refresh at configurable intervals with a maximum latency of 5 minutes from source data update.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R003	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	AI-Optimized Repositioning Recommendations	The AI engine must generate optimized empty container repositioning plans that minimize total network cost while satisfying demand constraints at all locations. The optimization must consider transportation costs (vessel slot costs, feeder costs, truck/rail rates in local currencies), handling charges at origin and destination terminals, transit times, vessel space availability, container type substitutability (e.g., 40HC can substitute for 40GP but not vice versa), and customs/regulatory constraints for cross-border movements between GCC states and India. The algorithm must evaluate multi-leg repositioning options (e.g., Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port by truck, then Khalifa Port to JNPT by vessel) and present the top three recommended plans with cost comparison in USD and local entity currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R004	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Repositioning Cost Estimation and Budgeting	The system must calculate detailed cost estimates for each proposed repositioning movement, itemizing vessel freight (or slot cost), terminal handling charges at origin and destination, inland transportation (truck or rail), customs clearance fees where applicable, and per-diem lease charges during repositioning transit time. Costs must be estimated in the movement's operational currency and converted to both the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD using configurable exchange rates from central bank feeds. The system must maintain repositioning budgets per entity and per quarter, tracking actual spend against budget with variance analysis. The AI engine must flag repositioning proposals that exceed budget thresholds and suggest cost-reducing alternatives such as triangulation or street-turn opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R005	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Vessel Space Booking for Empty Repositioning	The system must integrate with vessel scheduling and space management modules to book vessel slots for empty container repositioning. Booking requests must specify container quantity, type, size, origin port, destination port, preferred vessel/voyage, and latest acceptable arrival date. The system must check available vessel capacity and automatically select the most cost-effective vessel option considering slot costs, transit time, and transhipment requirements. When preferred vessel space is unavailable, the AI engine must propose alternative routings including feeder vessel connections and next-available sailings. Confirmed bookings must generate corresponding export manifests and must be trackable through loading, transit, and discharge with automated status updates from the vessel tracking feed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R006	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Inland Repositioning via Truck and Rail	The system must plan and manage inland empty container repositioning via truck and rail between ports and inland container depots. For truck movements, the system must integrate with transport management to generate haulage orders specifying pickup location, delivery location, container details, requested pickup time, and delivery deadline. For Indian rail movements, the system must interface with Indian Railways FOIS (Freight Operations Information System) for wagon availability and booking. The system must support multi-modal combinations (e.g., truck from inland depot to railhead, rail to port). Cost calculations must account for distance-based trucking rates in local currency, rail freight charges, terminal handling, and waiting time penalties. The AI engine must optimize truck routing to combine empty repositioning with laden container deliveries where possible.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R007	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Street-Turn and Triangulation Optimization	The AI engine must identify and recommend street-turn opportunities where an empty container from an import delivery can be directly redirected to a nearby export customer without returning to the port or depot, thereby eliminating two truck movements and associated handling. The system must match import delivery locations with export pickup locations within configurable radius thresholds (default 50 km), compatible container types and grades, and time windows (delivery completion to export pickup requirement). Similarly, the system must identify triangulation opportunities for inter-port repositioning where containers can carry revenue cargo on at least one leg. Each recommendation must display estimated cost savings in local currency and USD, CO2 emission reduction, and time savings compared to the standard repositioning route.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R008	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Repositioning Execution Tracking	The system must provide end-to-end tracking of each repositioning movement from plan approval through execution to container availability at destination. Tracking milestones must include: plan approved, pickup order issued, container collected from origin, loaded on vessel/truck/rail, in transit, arrived at destination terminal, gated into destination depot, and available for allocation. Each milestone must record actual timestamp versus planned timestamp with delay calculation and reason coding. A real-time repositioning dashboard must display all active movements with status indicators, ETA updates, and exception alerts for movements deviating from plan. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, origin-destination pair, container type, and transport mode, with all labels and notifications available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R009	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Cross-Border Repositioning Customs Compliance	The system must manage customs documentation and compliance requirements for empty container repositioning across international borders, specifically between GCC states (Qatar, UAE, KSA) and between GCC and India. For GCC inter-state movements, the system must generate the required GCC Common Customs Transit documentation and handle temporary admission procedures. For India movements, the system must generate IGM (Import General Manifest) and EGM (Export General Manifest) data in Indian Customs EDI format. The system must track customs bond requirements, temporary import permits, and re-export obligations with automated reminders before expiry. For KSA movements, FASAH (Saudi single window) integration must be supported. The AI engine must recommend the most customs-efficient routing considering free zone advantages at Jebel Ali and Hamad Port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R010	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Repositioning Performance Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive repositioning performance analytics including total repositioning cost per TEU moved, average repositioning transit time by corridor, repositioning volume as a percentage of total fleet, empty-to-laden ratio by trade lane, and cost per avoided stock-out. Analytics must be segmentable by entity, time period (Gregorian and Hijri calendar support), container type, and corridor (origin-destination pair). The AI engine must perform root cause analysis on high-cost repositioning corridors and recommend structural changes such as fleet rebalancing, new depot locations, or alliance partner collaboration. Trend analysis must compare current period performance against prior year same period, budget, and AI-predicted targets, with variance explanations generated in natural language in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R011	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Seasonal and Event-Driven Repositioning Planning	The system must support advance repositioning planning for known seasonal demand peaks and major events. Configurable event templates must be provided for recurring events including Hajj season (Dhul Hijjah month per Hijri calendar), Ramadan, Qatar National Day (December 18), UAE National Day (December 2), Saudi National Day (September 23), Diwali season, Indian monsoon agricultural export peaks, and FIFA/sporting event logistics. Each template must specify pre-positioning requirements by container type and location with lead-time offsets. The AI engine must learn from prior year event patterns and automatically adjust pre-positioning recommendations, accounting for year-over-year growth trends and Hijri calendar date shifts for Islamic events. Plans must include budget impact analysis in entity local currencies with consolidated USD view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R012	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Container Substitution and Upgrade Rules	The system must enforce configurable container substitution rules when the exact requested container type is unavailable at a location, allowing demand fulfillment from available substitute types. Standard substitution hierarchy must include: 40HC can fulfill 40GP demand, 20GP can be paired to fulfill 40GP demand (with additional handling cost), and high-grade food-grade containers can fulfill standard-grade demand. Each substitution must have an associated cost differential and customer approval requirement flag. The AI repositioning planner must factor substitution availability when calculating net deficit positions, potentially reducing unnecessary repositioning. The system must track substitution frequency and customer acceptance rates per trade lane, feeding this data back into future demand forecasting and fleet procurement planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R013	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Repositioning Workflow Approval and Authorization	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for repositioning plans based on movement value, volume, and corridor. Approval thresholds must be configurable per entity: for example, movements up to USD 5,000 equivalent may be auto-approved by the AI engine, USD 5,001 to USD 25,000 require equipment controller approval, USD 25,001 to USD 100,000 require regional manager approval, and above USD 100,000 require director approval. The workflow must support delegation during absence periods, escalation after configurable timeout periods, and batch approval for multiple related movements. All approval actions must be logged with timestamp, approver identity, and comments. Mobile approval must be supported for managers in the field, with push notifications in Arabic or English based on user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R014	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Alliance and Partner Repositioning Coordination	The system must support coordinated repositioning planning with shipping alliance partners and slot-sharing agreement participants. The system must publish surplus container availability by type and location to configured partners via EDI (COPARN messages) or API, and receive partner availability feeds. When repositioning needs can be met by a partner's surplus at a closer or cheaper location, the AI engine must recommend partner interchange as an alternative to own-fleet repositioning. Interchange terms must be managed per partner agreement including per-diem rates, free-time allowances, damage liability, and return location obligations. Financial settlements with partners must be generated in the agreed settlement currency with automatic conversion to entity local currency for accounting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R015	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Environmental Impact of Repositioning Tracking	The system must calculate and report the environmental impact of empty container repositioning movements, specifically CO2 emissions based on transport mode (vessel, truck, rail), distance, and fuel type using IMO EEXI/CII methodology for sea legs and GLEC Framework for inland legs. The AI engine must include carbon cost as a factor in repositioning optimization, applying configurable shadow carbon pricing (default USD 50 per ton CO2). The system must track cumulative repositioning emissions per entity against annual reduction targets aligned with IMO 2030/2050 decarbonization goals. Environmental impact reports must be available in both Arabic and English and must support GCC and Indian sustainability reporting frameworks. The AI must recommend lower-emission repositioning alternatives such as rail over truck or slow-steaming vessel options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R016	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Repositioning Plan Simulation and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a simulation environment allowing planners to model what-if scenarios for repositioning decisions before committing to execution. Scenarios must support modification of demand forecasts, vessel schedule changes, port closures or congestion events, fuel price fluctuations, currency exchange rate changes, and new depot location analysis. The simulation engine must calculate the impact of each scenario on repositioning cost, container availability, customer service levels, and carbon emissions. Results must be presented in comparative dashboards showing baseline versus scenario outcomes with detailed variance breakdown. Multiple scenarios must be saveable and shareable across the planning team. The AI engine must automatically generate stress-test scenarios based on historical disruption events such as port strikes, weather events, and geopolitical tensions affecting the Middle East-India corridor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-002-R017	FEAT-010-1-002	MOD-010	Depot Network Optimization Recommendations	The AI engine must periodically analyze the depot and yard network configuration across all four operating regions and recommend optimization actions including opening new depots, closing underperforming depots, adjusting stock levels at existing depots, and reallocating container storage capacity. Analysis must consider customer location density, historical pickup and return patterns, transport cost contours, land lease costs in local currencies, and competitive landscape. Recommendations must include financial projections (NPV, IRR, payback period) calculated in entity local currency and consolidated USD, with sensitivity analysis on key variables. The system must generate a quarterly network review report with executive summary available in both Arabic and English, suitable for presentation to the group executive committee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R001	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Container Configuration and Settings Management	The system must maintain detailed technical configuration records for each reefer container including refrigeration unit manufacturer and model (Carrier PrimeLINE, Daikin, Thermo King, StarCool), firmware version, temperature range capability (standard -35°C to +30°C), atmosphere control capability (CA/MA for O2, CO2, N2 levels), humidity control range, air exchange rate settings, and power supply requirements (380V/460V, 50Hz/60Hz). Configuration settings must support commodity-specific temperature profiles following carrier's commodity database and USDA/Codex Alimentarius guidelines. The system must validate that assigned reefer units match cargo temperature requirements before allocation confirmation and reject mismatches with clear error messages displayed in Arabic or English based on the user's language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R002	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Real-Time Temperature Monitoring and Alerting	The system must receive and process real-time temperature telemetry data from reefer container monitoring units at intervals of no more than 15 minutes during yard storage and 60 minutes during vessel transit. Temperature data must include supply air temperature, return air temperature, cargo temperature (where probed), and ambient temperature. The AI engine must compare readings against the configured setpoint with configurable alarm thresholds: pre-alarm at ±2°C deviation, critical alarm at ±5°C deviation, and emergency alarm at ±8°C deviation or complete unit failure. Alarms must trigger immediate notifications to on-duty reefer technicians and operations managers via SMS, email, and in-app push notification in Arabic or English. The system must maintain an unbroken temperature log for cold chain documentation and regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R003	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Pre-Trip Inspection PTI Workflow Management	The system must manage the complete pre-trip inspection workflow for reefer containers, ensuring every reefer unit passes PTI before being allocated for cargo loading. The PTI checklist must cover refrigeration unit startup, temperature pull-down test (to setpoint within specified time), defrost cycle test, atmosphere control function test (if equipped), structural integrity of container body insulation, door gasket seal integrity, drain plug condition, and electrical connection compatibility. The system must enforce that PTI is completed no more than 72 hours before cargo loading (configurable per customer or commodity). PTI results must be digitally recorded with technician ID, timestamp, pass/fail status, and photographs of any deficiencies. Failed PTI must automatically route the container to maintenance with a repair work order and block allocation until re-inspection passes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R004	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Power Supply and Plug Management	The system must manage reefer power supply allocation at yard and terminal locations, tracking available reefer plug points by location, rack, and row. The system must maintain a real-time map of plug occupancy showing energized and available connections at each yard block. When reefer containers are gated into the yard, the system must automatically assign an available plug point considering proximity to the gate, vessel berth allocation for upcoming load, and electrical capacity constraints. The AI engine must forecast reefer plug demand based on confirmed bookings and vessel schedules, alerting yard planners when projected demand exceeds available capacity at any location. Power consumption tracking must record kilowatt-hours per container for cost allocation, with electricity costs calculated in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) based on utility tariff schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R005	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Controlled Atmosphere CA Container Management	The system must manage controlled atmosphere reefer containers that regulate O2, CO2, and N2 levels in addition to temperature, supporting commodities such as fresh fruits (bananas, avocados, mangoes), vegetables, and cut flowers. CA settings must be commodity-specific with configurable gas concentration targets and tolerance ranges following post-harvest handling standards. The system must track CA equipment calibration schedules and certification validity for gas sensors. During transit monitoring, the AI engine must detect gas concentration deviations and correlate them with temperature fluctuations and ventilation events to determine root cause. For Indian mango exports (a key trade for JNPT) and Qatari fresh food imports, the system must enforce specific CA profiles mandated by importing country phytosanitary authorities and generate compliance certificates with data logs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R006	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Monitoring During Vessel Transit	The system must receive and process reefer monitoring data from vessel-based monitoring systems during sea transit, supporting data feeds from major monitoring platforms including Emerson, Sensitech, and Carrier Lynx. Data ingestion must handle both real-time satellite transmission and batch uploads upon vessel arrival. The system must reconcile vessel reefer bay plans with container bookings to ensure all reefer containers are connected to appropriate power supply on board. When monitoring data indicates temperature excursions during transit, the AI engine must assess cargo impact severity based on commodity type, duration and magnitude of excursion, and stage of transit. The system must generate automated cargo condition advisories to the consignee and cargo insurance underwriters, with notifications in Arabic or English as appropriate for the recipient's profile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R007	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Cold Chain Documentation and Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive cold chain compliance documentation for each reefer shipment, including continuous temperature strip charts (digital equivalent), PTI certificates, vessel stowage confirmation, and delivery temperature confirmation. Documentation must comply with GDP (Good Distribution Practice) requirements for pharmaceutical shipments, HACCP principles for food shipments, and IMDG code requirements for temperature-sensitive dangerous goods. For shipments to/from GCC states, documentation must satisfy Gulf Standardization Organization (GSO) requirements and municipal food safety authority standards (Qatar MOPH, Abu Dhabi ADAFSA, Saudi SFDA). For Indian shipments, FSSAI (Food Safety and Standards Authority of India) compliance documentation must be generated. All reports must be available in bilingual Arabic/English format with timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R002	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	AI-Driven Slot Allocation Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered slot allocation engine that automatically assigns optimal yard positions to incoming containers using a multi-objective optimization algorithm. Factors must include: vessel berthing window and bay plan position, container weight class (light/medium/heavy for crane sequence optimization), destination port grouping for minimum restow moves, dwell-time forecast based on historical data, reefer power proximity, hazmat segregation compliance, and truck turn-time minimization. The engine must process allocation requests within 200 milliseconds per container and handle burst loads of 500+ containers during peak vessel discharge at Jebel Ali. Allocation results must be expressible in block-bay-row-tier (BBRT) coordinate format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R008	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Fleet Utilization and Performance Analytics	The system must track and report reefer-specific fleet performance metrics including reefer utilization rate (on-hire days versus available days), average PTI pass rate, mean time between failures (MTBF), average energy consumption per container per day (kWh), temperature excursion frequency and severity, and reefer-specific turns per year. Analytics must be segmentable by unit manufacturer, model, age band, operating location, and trade lane. The AI engine must identify underperforming reefer units by comparing individual container metrics against fleet averages, recommending maintenance interventions, technology upgrades, or retirement. Performance dashboards must support visualization in Arabic RTL layout with data export capabilities. Cost per reefer-TEU-day must be calculated in entity local currency and consolidated USD for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R009	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Spare Parts Inventory Management	The system must maintain an inventory of reefer spare parts at each maintenance depot, tracking part numbers by manufacturer compatibility, current stock levels, reorder points, lead times, and unit costs in local procurement currency. Critical spare parts (compressors, controllers, fan motors, thermostats, gas sensors) must have minimum stock thresholds that trigger automatic purchase requisitions when breached. The AI engine must forecast spare parts demand based on fleet age profile, historical failure rates by component, seasonal usage patterns (higher reefer demand in summer months across GCC), and upcoming PTI schedules. Parts costs must be tracked against maintenance budgets per entity with currency conversion for imported parts (typically procured in USD or EUR). The system must support barcode or QR code scanning for parts issue and receipt at the workshop level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R010	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Energy Efficiency Optimization	The AI engine must analyze reefer energy consumption patterns and recommend optimization strategies to reduce electricity costs and carbon footprint. Analysis must consider ambient temperature impact (particularly relevant for GCC summer temperatures exceeding 50°C), cargo pre-cooling effectiveness, optimal yard placement for shade and airflow, power supply voltage stability effects on compressor efficiency, and defrost cycle frequency optimization. The system must calculate energy cost per container per day at each location using local electricity tariff rates in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR respectively. Recommendations must include yard slot prioritization for high-ambient locations, pre-cooling protocol improvements, and identification of reefer units with degraded energy efficiency requiring maintenance. Annual energy savings projections must be presented in both local currency and USD with corresponding CO2 emission reduction estimates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R011	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Pharmaceutical Cold Chain GDP Compliance	The system must provide enhanced cold chain management capabilities for pharmaceutical and life science shipments requiring GDP (Good Distribution Practice) compliance under WHO guidelines and local regulatory frameworks (Qatar MOPH, UAE MOH, Saudi SFDA, India CDSCO). This includes mandatory temperature mapping validation of reefer containers before pharmaceutical use, continuous temperature monitoring with tamper-evident data loggers, deviation management workflows requiring quality person review and disposition decision (release, reject, quarantine) for any temperature excursion, and end-to-end chain of custody documentation. The system must support qualification of reefer containers for specific temperature ranges (+2°C to +8°C for vaccines, +15°C to +25°C for ambient pharma, -20°C for biologics) and maintain qualification records with expiry tracking. All GDP documentation must be available for regulatory audit in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R012	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Emergency Response Procedures	The system must implement automated emergency response workflows for critical reefer events including complete unit failure, refrigerant leak detection, electrical fire risk, and cargo spoilage alerts. When a critical event is detected through IoT telemetry or manual reporting, the system must immediately escalate to the on-duty reefer supervisor and generate a time-stamped incident record. The AI engine must recommend immediate corrective actions based on event type, container location, cargo commodity, and available resources (backup reefer units, gensets, nearby maintenance technicians). For perishable cargo at risk of spoilage, the system must calculate the remaining safe cargo life based on commodity type and temperature exposure duration, providing countdown alerts. Emergency procedures must account for GCC extreme heat conditions where ambient temperatures may accelerate cargo deterioration. Incident notifications must be dispatched in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R013	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Genset Management for Inland Reefer Transport	The system must manage generator set (genset) allocation and tracking for reefer containers during inland transportation where continuous power supply is required. Genset records must include unit identification, fuel type (diesel), fuel tank capacity, runtime capacity, maintenance status, and GPS tracking. The system must automatically assign an available genset when a reefer container is booked for inland haulage exceeding configurable distance thresholds. Fuel level monitoring must trigger refueling alerts before estimated depletion during transit. The AI engine must optimize genset pool allocation across depots based on demand forecasts and maintenance schedules. Genset operating costs (fuel consumption, maintenance, depreciation) must be tracked per trip and per entity in local currency for accurate reefer transport cost calculation. The system must enforce genset return and inspection workflows upon trip completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R014	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Commodity Compatibility Validation	The system must enforce commodity compatibility rules when allocating reefer containers, preventing contamination risks from residual odors, chemical traces, or biological agents from previous cargoes. The system must maintain a commodity compatibility matrix classifying commodities into groups (fresh produce, frozen meat, seafood, dairy, pharmaceuticals, chemicals) with cross-contamination risk ratings. When a reefer container is allocated, the system must check the container's cargo history for the previous three loads and flag incompatible sequences (e.g., chemicals followed by food, strong-odor commodities followed by pharmaceuticals). The AI engine must recommend pre-allocation cleaning protocols based on cargo history, including wash-out, deodorization, or food-grade sanitization requirements. Cleaning completion must be recorded with certificate generation in Arabic and English before the container is released for loading.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R015	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Data Logger Download and Archival	The system must support automated download of temperature data from reefer unit data loggers (USB, Bluetooth, or NFC interface) upon container gate-in at each terminal and depot. Downloaded data must be parsed, validated for completeness (no gaps exceeding 30 minutes), and automatically attached to the corresponding shipment record and bill of lading. The AI engine must analyze downloaded data against setpoint parameters and flag any temperature deviations, humidity excursions, or power interruptions during transit for cargo claims assessment. Data must be archived in tamper-proof format with digital signatures for legal evidentiary value in cargo damage disputes. The archival period must be configurable per entity with a minimum of five years for food shipments and ten years for pharmaceutical shipments. Data search and retrieval must support queries by container number, booking reference, date range, or commodity type with results presented in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-003-R016	FEAT-010-1-003	MOD-010	Reefer Container Washout and Sanitization Tracking	The system must manage the washout and sanitization process for reefer containers between cargo loads, tracking washout type (standard rinse, chemical wash, food-grade sanitization, pharmaceutical decontamination), cleaning agent used, water temperature, sanitization certificate validity, and next required washout date. The system must enforce washout completion before reefer allocation based on configurable rules per commodity group and customer requirements. Washout service costs must be recorded per container and allocated to the appropriate cost center in entity local currency. For food-grade shipments to GCC markets, the system must verify compliance with GSO cleaning standards and generate certificates recognized by MOPH, ADAFSA, SFDA, and FSSAI authorities. The AI engine must schedule washout appointments optimizing technician and wash bay utilization across the depot, reducing container dwell time between cargo loads.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R001	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Damage Assessment and IICL Coding	The system must support comprehensive container damage assessment following IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) 6th Edition repair standards, capturing damage using the standardized coding system: component code (four-digit IICL component identifier), damage type code (dent, hole, crack, missing, broken, bent, cut, wear), location code (using the IICL container grid reference system for panels, rails, posts, and fittings), and dimensional measurements (length, width, depth). Surveyors must be able to record multiple damage items per container on mobile devices with offline capability. Each damage entry must include severity classification (minor, moderate, major), photographic evidence with geo-tagged metadata, and timestamp in local timezone. The AI engine must validate damage coding consistency and flag potential errors such as impossible component-damage type combinations or dimensional outliers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R002	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	AI-Powered Damage Detection from Photographs	The AI engine must analyze container survey photographs using computer vision models to automatically detect and classify damage types, estimate damage dimensions, suggest IICL component and damage codes, and provide preliminary repair cost estimates. The model must achieve a minimum 85% accuracy for common damage types (panel dents, corrosion, door damage, floor damage) and 75% accuracy for complex damage (structural member deformation, refrigeration unit damage). When AI confidence is below configurable thresholds, the system must flag the assessment for human surveyor review. The AI must learn from surveyor corrections to continuously improve accuracy. Image processing must handle challenging conditions including poor lighting in container yards, dust-covered containers common in GCC desert environments, and rain-affected photographs at JNPT during monsoon season. Damage descriptions must be generated in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R003	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Repair Estimation and Cost Calculation	The system must generate detailed repair estimates for each damage item based on IICL repair method standards, calculating labor hours by skill type (welder, carpenter, painter, mechanic), material quantities (steel plate, timber, paint, gaskets, fittings), and equipment requirements (welding machine, spray booth, lifting equipment). Labor rates and material prices must be maintained per repair depot in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with periodic updates from vendor rate agreements. The system must support both customer-billable repairs (charged per IICL tariff rates or agreed DPP damage protection plan rates) and owner-account repairs (at actual cost). The AI engine must compare estimated repair costs against container current book value and flag cases where repair cost exceeds configurable percentage thresholds (typically 60-70% of depreciated value), recommending write-off and disposal instead of repair.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R004	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Repair Work Order Management	The system must generate and manage repair work orders from approved damage estimates, tracking work order through states: created, assigned to technician, in progress, quality inspection, completed, and invoiced. Each work order must specify the container number, damage items to repair, repair methods, required materials, estimated labor hours, assigned technician or vendor, target completion date, and priority level (urgent for booked containers, normal, low for stock units). The system must support work order splitting when multiple skill types are required and parallel execution tracking. Technicians must be able to update work order progress on mobile devices with photo evidence of completed repairs. The AI engine must optimize work order scheduling based on technician skills, availability, work bay capacity, material availability, and container booking priority, minimizing container out-of-service time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R005	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Third-Party Repair Vendor Management	The system must manage relationships with third-party container repair vendors at each operating location, maintaining vendor profiles including company details, approved repair categories (structural, mechanical, reefer, painting), quality certifications, rate agreements, insurance coverage, and performance history. Vendor rate agreements must capture labor rates per skill type, material markup percentages, and special rates for volume commitments, all in local entity currency with validity periods. The system must support vendor selection for outsourced repairs based on configurable criteria including repair type expertise, current workload, historical quality rating, and cost competitiveness. Vendor invoices must be validated against approved estimates with configurable tolerance thresholds (e.g., 10% cost overrun allowance). The AI engine must maintain vendor scorecards tracking quality, timeliness, and cost performance with automated alerts when scores fall below acceptable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R006	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Repair Quality Inspection and Acceptance	The system must enforce a quality inspection workflow after repair completion before a container is returned to available stock. Quality inspectors must complete a standardized checklist verifying repair completeness against work order specifications, structural integrity compliance with ISO 1496 standards, weathertight integrity (hose test or ultrasonic test results), door operation and locking mechanism function, cleanliness standards, and paint finish quality. Inspection results must be digitally recorded with photographs of repaired areas and inspector certification. Failed inspections must automatically generate rework orders linked to the original work order with deficiency details. The AI engine must analyze inspection failure patterns per technician and vendor to identify quality issues and recommend training or corrective actions. Inspection records must be archived for a minimum of five years for lessor audit and dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R007	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	MNR Cost Recovery and Billing	The system must manage the recovery of container maintenance and repair costs from responsible parties including shipping customers (for damage beyond normal wear and tear), insurance companies (for insured events), and container lessors (for owner-account repairs on leased units). Customer billing must follow the liability determination from equipment interchange receipts (EIR) at gate-in, comparing container condition at gate-out versus gate-in. The system must generate detailed repair invoices in the customer's billing currency with IICL standard format line items. For leased containers, the system must prepare lessor damage notifications (LDN) following each lessor's specific reporting format and Damage Protection Plan (DPP) terms. Multi-currency settlement must be supported with exchange rate application per invoice date. The AI engine must predict cost recovery probability based on damage type, customer history, and documentation completeness, prioritizing collection efforts accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R008	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Preventive Maintenance Scheduling	The system must implement a preventive maintenance program for the container fleet with configurable maintenance schedules based on container age, cumulative trips, operating environment severity (GCC high-heat and high-salinity environments require more frequent corrosion treatment), and container type. Preventive maintenance tasks must include structural inspection, corrosion treatment, floor inspection and repair, door mechanism lubrication and adjustment, CSC plate renewal, and data plate verification. The AI engine must generate optimized preventive maintenance schedules that minimize container downtime by aligning maintenance windows with low-demand periods and vessel schedule gaps. Maintenance alerts must be generated automatically when containers approach scheduled maintenance dates. The system must track preventive maintenance compliance rates per entity and depot, targeting configurable KPIs such as 95% on-time completion. Schedules must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar-based planning cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R009	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	MNR Materials and Consumables Inventory	The system must manage inventory of repair materials and consumables at each maintenance depot including steel plates and sections by grade and dimension, timber flooring by type (hardwood, bamboo, composite), paints and coatings, welding consumables, gaskets and seals, door hardware, corner castings, and reefer-specific spare parts. Inventory must be tracked with lot numbers, shelf life where applicable, and storage location within the depot. The system must calculate material requirements from approved work orders and check stock availability before work order release, automatically generating purchase requisitions for shortfalls. Material costs must be tracked in procurement currency with automatic conversion to entity operating currency. The AI engine must forecast material demand based on upcoming work order pipeline, historical consumption patterns, and seasonal trends, optimizing reorder quantities to balance carrying cost against stock-out risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R010	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Container Depot Workshop Capacity Planning	The system must manage repair workshop capacity at each maintenance depot, tracking available work bays by type (structural repair bay, painting bay, reefer workshop, wash bay), technician availability by skill set, and equipment availability. The AI engine must schedule work orders against available capacity to maximize workshop throughput while respecting skill-matching constraints, equipment requirements, and safety regulations (e.g., maximum concurrent welding operations in enclosed spaces). Capacity utilization dashboards must display real-time bay occupancy, technician allocation, and work-in-progress queues. When demand exceeds capacity, the system must recommend overflow options including overtime scheduling, third-party vendor outsourcing, or container transfer to a less busy depot. Capacity forecasts must project workshop loading for the next 4 weeks based on gate-in surveys and confirmed repair estimates, presented in bilingual Arabic/English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R011	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Equipment Interchange Receipt EIR Management	The system must generate and manage Equipment Interchange Receipts (EIR) at every container handover point including terminal gate-in, gate-out, rail terminal interchange, and inland depot gate events. The EIR must document container condition at the point of interchange using standardized condition codes, photographs, and digital signatures from both parties (terminal operator and trucker/customer representative). The system must support digital EIR completion on tablets or mobile devices with offline capability for locations with poor connectivity. Condition comparison between gate-out EIR and subsequent gate-in EIR must automatically identify new damage for customer liability assessment. The EIR must capture seal number, container weight (where scale is available), and any special condition notes. For GCC port operations, the EIR must integrate with customs gate systems. Digital EIR must be legally recognized as per electronic transaction laws in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R012	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	MNR Budget Planning and Cost Control	The system must support annual MNR budget planning per entity and per depot, with budget allocations by expense category (labor, materials, third-party vendors, overhead) and container segment (owned dry, owned reefer, leased dry, leased reefer). The AI engine must generate budget recommendations based on fleet age profile, historical MNR spend trends, planned preventive maintenance programs, and forecasted fleet growth. Monthly budget-versus-actual tracking must highlight variances with root cause analysis capability. Cost per TEU repaired must be benchmarked against industry standards and internal targets. Budget reports must support drill-down from entity level to individual depot and expense category, with all amounts displayed in entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated USD for group reporting. The system must enforce commitment controls preventing work order approval when cumulative commitments would exceed remaining budget without management override authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R013	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Lessor Compliance and Audit Support	The system must maintain all container repair and maintenance records in formats compliant with major lessor audit requirements (Triton, Textainer, Beacon, Seaco, CAI, Florens). For leased containers, the system must track repair history against lessor-specific maintenance obligations defined in the lease agreement, including mandatory periodic inspections, approved repair specifications (specific steel grades, paint specifications, floor materials), and maximum cumulative repair cost thresholds triggering renegotiation. The system must generate lessor-ready inspection reports and on-hire/off-hire condition surveys in the lessor's required format. During lessor audits, the system must provide read-only access to selected container maintenance histories with exportable reports. The AI engine must predict potential lessor dispute items based on repair documentation completeness and flag records requiring remediation before audit, with all reports available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R014	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Container Write-Off and Disposal Management	The system must manage the end-of-life process for containers deemed beyond economical repair (BER), including write-off approval workflow, disposal method selection (sale as-is, scrap, conversion to storage unit), and financial accounting entries. The write-off workflow must require supporting documentation including final survey report, repair cost estimate exceeding BER threshold, photograph evidence, and approval from authorized personnel based on container book value (configurable approval hierarchy per entity). For container sales, the system must record buyer details, sale price in transaction currency, and customs export documentation where applicable. The disposal process must comply with environmental regulations in each jurisdiction regarding scrap metal handling, refrigerant recovery for reefer units (per Kigali Amendment to Montreal Protocol), and hazardous material disposal. Financial entries must record the difference between book value and disposal proceeds as gain or loss in entity local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R015	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Warranty Claim Management for New Containers	The system must track manufacturer warranty terms for new containers (typically 5 years structural, 2 years paint, varying terms for reefer machinery) and manage warranty claims against manufacturers (primarily CIMC, CXIC, Singamas, Maersk Container Industry for reefers). When damage to a container within warranty period is identified as a manufacturing defect (as opposed to operational damage), the system must initiate a warranty claim workflow capturing defect description, photographs, container build date, and warranty clause reference. Claims must track manufacturer acknowledgment, inspection scheduling, settlement offers, and final resolution. Financial settlements must be recorded in the claim currency (typically USD for Chinese manufacturers) with conversion to entity local currency. The AI engine must analyze warranty claim patterns across manufacturer, production batch, and defect type to identify systematic quality issues and support fleet-wide recall or inspection campaigns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R016	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	MNR Technician Skill and Certification Management	The system must maintain records of MNR technician skills, certifications, and training history, covering competencies such as structural steel welding (coded welder certifications per AWS or ISO 9606), container floor repair, painting and coating application, reefer unit servicing (manufacturer-specific certifications for Carrier, Daimon, Thermo King), electrical systems, and CSC examination authorization. Certification expiry tracking must generate alerts 60 days before expiry and block assignment of work requiring expired certifications. The system must support training needs analysis by comparing required skills for upcoming work order pipeline against available certified technicians. For operations in GCC countries, the system must track valid work permits, trade test results recognized by the relevant labor ministry, and health and safety certifications. Technician records must be maintained in bilingual Arabic/English format for regulatory inspection purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R017	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	MNR Performance Analytics and Benchmarking	The system must provide comprehensive MNR performance analytics including average repair turnaround time, first-time-fix rate, repair cost per TEU by container type and age band, preventive-to-corrective maintenance ratio, customer damage rate per gate-in, and third-party vendor performance scores. Analytics must support comparison across entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), depots, and time periods with trend analysis over rolling 12-month windows. The AI engine must identify cost outliers, quality patterns, and efficiency improvement opportunities, generating recommendations in natural language in both Arabic and English. Benchmarking must compare entity performance against configurable industry standards and internal best practices. Dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL layout and export to PDF and Excel with bilingual headers. Automated monthly MNR performance reports must be generated and distributed to configured stakeholders per entity, with cost figures in both local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-1-004-R018	FEAT-010-1-004	MOD-010	Safety and Environmental Compliance in MNR Operations	The system must enforce health, safety, and environmental compliance in all MNR operations, tracking safety incident reports, near-miss events, safety equipment inspections (fire extinguishers, eye wash stations, ventilation systems), and hazardous material handling records (paint solvents, welding fumes, refrigerant gases). The system must maintain a risk register for each depot with risk assessments per repair activity type following ISO 45001 occupational health and safety standards. For GCC operations, compliance with OSHAD (Abu Dhabi), QCDD (Qatar), and OSHA-equivalent KSA regulations must be tracked. For Indian operations, compliance with the Factories Act and State Pollution Control Board requirements must be monitored. The AI engine must analyze incident trends and predict high-risk periods or activities, recommending preventive measures. Environmental tracking must include waste disposal records, refrigerant recovery logs (per Montreal Protocol), and VOC emission monitoring from painting operations. All safety documentation must be maintained in bilingual Arabic/English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R001	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Yard Block and Zone Configuration	The system must allow terminal administrators to configure yard blocks, zones, and sub-areas for each terminal facility (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLP, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHV). Each block must be defined with a unique alphanumeric code, GPS boundary coordinates, surface type (paved, unpaved, rail-mounted), maximum stacking height (1-6 tiers), and ground slot capacity. Configuration must support block designation as import, export, transshipment, empty, reefer, hazmat, or mixed-use. The system must enforce IMO hazmat segregation distance rules between hazmat zones and general cargo blocks. All labels and zone names must be rendered in both English and Arabic with RTL layout support for Arabic terminal operators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R003	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Reefer Slot and Power Point Management	The system must track all reefer power points across yard blocks with real-time status (available, occupied, faulty, under-maintenance). Each reefer slot must record electrical capacity (single-phase 32A, three-phase 63A), voltage (220V/380V/440V), and plug type compatibility. When a reefer container (ISO type codes 45R1, 22R1, etc.) arrives, the allocation engine must prioritize slots with functioning power points matching the container's requirements. The system must generate alerts when reefer power point utilization exceeds 85% capacity at any block, and must maintain a minimum 10% reserve buffer for emergency reefer arrivals. Power consumption data must be logged per slot for billing in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) based on the operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R004	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Hazmat Container Yard Segregation	The system must enforce IMDG Code segregation rules for hazardous cargo containers in the yard, implementing automated distance calculations between incompatible hazard classes (Classes 1 through 9 including sub-classes). The segregation matrix must follow the four-level IMDG segregation groups: Away From, Separated From, Separated By A Complete Compartment, and Separated Longitudinally. When allocating a hazmat container, the system must validate all adjacent and nearby slots against stored containers' UN numbers and hazard class designations. Any violation must block the allocation and suggest compliant alternative slots. The system must also enforce local Qatar MOEI, UAE NCEMA, KSA DGA, and India PESO regulatory overlays on top of IMDG minimums.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R005	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Real-Time Yard Occupancy Dashboard	The system must display a real-time yard occupancy dashboard with color-coded block visualization showing utilization percentage, container type distribution (dry, reefer, tank, flat-rack, open-top), and available slot counts. The dashboard must support drill-down from terminal-level overview to individual block, bay, row, and tier views. Utilization metrics must be computed every 60 seconds and include current occupancy rate, average dwell time by container category, and projected occupancy for the next 24/48/72 hours based on expected vessel arrivals and deliveries. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts, including mirrored navigation and Hijri date display alongside Gregorian dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R006	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Vessel-Linked Pre-Allocation Planning	The system must support pre-allocation of yard slots linked to expected vessel arrivals. When a vessel's bay plan (BAPLIE EDI message) is received, the system must automatically generate a pre-allocation plan assigning yard positions to all discharge containers before vessel arrival. Pre-allocation must consider the vessel's discharge sequence (hatch-cover lifts, hold vs deck), available yard equipment (RTG, RMG, reach stacker) positioning, and downstream delivery commitments. Plans must be finalized at least 4 hours before vessel berthing and locked against manual override unless authorized by a yard planning supervisor. Multi-entity operations must isolate pre-allocation plans per terminal operator while allowing cross-entity visibility for transshipment containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R008	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Yard Move Instruction Generation	The system must generate yard move instructions (shuffles, housekeeping, and repositioning) with optimized sequencing to minimize total crane and equipment travel distance. Each move instruction must specify source position (BBRT), destination position (BBRT), container ID, equipment type assigned (RTG, RMG, reach stacker, terminal tractor), priority level (urgent, normal, low), and estimated execution time. The system must detect and resolve conflicting moves that target the same destination slot or require the same equipment simultaneously. Move instructions must be transmitted to Terminal Operating System (TOS) equipment dispatch modules via REST API and must support rollback if a move fails or is cancelled mid-execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R009	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Dwell Time Monitoring and Overstay Alerts	The system must continuously monitor container dwell time from the moment of yard placement (gate-in or vessel discharge timestamp) and generate escalating alerts when containers approach or exceed configurable dwell-time thresholds. Default thresholds must be: import containers 5 days (free time) with escalation at 7, 10, and 15 days; export containers 3 days pre-vessel with alerts for early arrivals beyond 7 days; transshipment containers per connecting vessel schedule plus 2-day buffer. Overstay charges must be calculated in the operating entity's local currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali/Khalifa, SAR for KSA, INR for JNPT) using configurable tariff slabs. Demurrage and detention amounts must be posted to the billing module with Hijri and Gregorian date references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R010	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Yard Equipment Tracking Integration	The system must integrate with yard equipment tracking systems (DGPS, RFID, and IoT sensors) to capture real-time positions of RTGs, RMGs, reach stackers, empty handlers, and terminal tractors within the yard. Equipment positions must update at minimum 5-second intervals and be overlaid on the yard map visualization. The system must calculate equipment utilization rates per shift and per block, identify idle equipment, and flag equipment that has not moved within a configurable idle threshold (default 15 minutes). Integration must support standard equipment telematics protocols and provide a fallback manual position-update interface for terminals where GPS coverage is limited or equipment lacks telematics hardware.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R011	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Multi-Terminal Yard Capacity Balancing	The system must support capacity balancing across multiple terminals within the same port authority jurisdiction. When one terminal approaches capacity threshold (configurable, default 90%), the system must suggest overflow routing to partner terminals with available capacity. For Qatar operations spanning Hamad Port, the system must evaluate Old Port auxiliary yards. For UAE, balancing between Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali must be supported with inter-terminal transfer cost estimates in AED. Capacity balancing recommendations must factor in additional trucking costs, inter-terminal barge availability, and customs bond implications when containers cross terminal boundaries. All balancing transactions must maintain entity-level accounting separation with proper inter-company transfer pricing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R012	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Yard Slot Reservation for VIP Clients	The system must allow designated premium or VIP shipping lines and freight forwarders to reserve dedicated yard blocks or a guaranteed number of slots per block. Reservations must be configurable by time period (weekly, monthly, seasonal), container type, and maximum slot count. The system must enforce reservation limits and prevent general allocation from consuming reserved slots. When reserved slots are underutilized (below 60% for more than 48 hours), the system must notify the reservation holder and optionally release excess capacity back to the general pool after a configurable grace period. Reservation agreements must be tracked with contract references and billing must occur in the agreed contract currency with exchange rate handling for cross-currency arrangements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R013	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Weather and Tide Impact on Yard Operations	The system must integrate weather forecast data to proactively adjust yard planning during adverse conditions such as sandstorms (common in Qatar, UAE, KSA), heavy monsoon rains (JNPT India), extreme heat events exceeding 50°C, and high-wind conditions. During sandstorm alerts, the system must suspend empty container stacking above 3 tiers and prioritize securing flat-rack and open-top cargo. During monsoon conditions at JNPT, the system must activate waterlogging avoidance by reallocating containers away from flood-prone low-lying blocks. Reefer container monitoring frequency must increase during extreme heat events. All weather-triggered operational adjustments must be logged with timestamps and the originating weather advisory reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R014	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Offline Yard Operations Mode	The system must provide an offline-capable yard operations mode that allows yard planners and equipment operators to continue slot allocation, container positioning, and move instruction generation when network connectivity to the central server is lost. The offline module must cache the current yard inventory snapshot, active allocation rules, and pending vessel discharge lists locally on yard planning workstations and mobile devices. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize offline transactions with the central database using conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recent physical container movement. Synchronization conflicts must be flagged for manual review by a yard supervisor. The offline mode must support a minimum of 8 hours of autonomous operation for up to 2,000 container movements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R015	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Yard Planning Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all yard planning decisions, slot allocations, manual overrides, and container movements. Each audit record must capture the action type, timestamp (Gregorian and Hijri), user ID, terminal entity, source and destination positions, AI recommendation versus actual placement (if overridden), and the business reason for any deviation. Audit logs must be immutable and retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law on Commercial Transactions, KSA ZATCA requirements, and Indian Customs Act 1962 record-keeping mandates. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual Arabic-English headers and support digital signature attestation for regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-001-R016	FEAT-010-2-001	MOD-010	Container Weight Verification and Tier Validation	The system must enforce SOLAS VGM (Verified Gross Mass) compliance by validating container weights before slot allocation. Heavier containers must be allocated to lower tiers and lighter containers to upper tiers to maintain stack structural integrity and crane safety limits. The system must reject any allocation where a container's VGM exceeds the maximum permissible stack weight for its assigned tier, considering cumulative weight of containers below. Weight data must be sourced from VGM declarations (VERMAS EDI messages) and cross-referenced with weighbridge readings at the gate. Any discrepancy exceeding 5% between declared VGM and actual weighbridge measurement must trigger a hold and notification to the terminal safety officer and the relevant port authority (QP, AD Ports, Saudi Ports Authority, JNPT DPW).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R001	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Gate Lane Configuration and Management	The system must support configuration of multiple gate lanes per terminal with individual lane designations for gate-in, gate-out, or dual-direction operations. Each lane must be associated with specific equipment (OCR cameras, radiation portal monitors, weighbridges, RFID readers) and staffing assignments per shift. Lane operating hours must be configurable with Hijri and Gregorian calendar support for holidays and Ramadan-adjusted schedules in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. The system must display real-time lane status (open, closed, congested, maintenance) and queue depth per lane. Indian JNPT operations must support separate CFS and ICD gate configurations with distinct documentation requirements under Indian Customs EDI system integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R002	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	CODECO Gate-In Message Generation	The system must automatically generate UN/EDIFACT CODECO (COPARN response) messages upon successful gate-in of a container. The CODECO gate-in message must include: message reference number, container number (ISO 6346 validated), container size/type code, seal number and condition, gross weight (VGM-compliant), transport operator identification, vehicle registration number, driver ID, gate-in timestamp (UTC and local time), terminal facility code (UN/LOCODE), and full/empty indicator. The message must be transmitted to the shipping line, container lessor, and relevant customs authority within 5 minutes of gate passage. Message formatting must comply with SMDG CODECO implementation guide version 3.1 or later and support character encoding for Arabic text fields in party name and address segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R003	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	CODECO Gate-Out Message Generation	The system must automatically generate UN/EDIFACT CODECO gate-out messages when a container exits the terminal through any authorized gate lane. The gate-out CODECO must capture: container number, delivery order reference, consignee or agent identification, outbound transport mode (truck, rail, barge), vehicle and driver identification, container condition at departure, seal verification status (intact, broken, replaced, missing), and timestamp. For import deliveries, the system must verify that all customs release holds are cleared before permitting gate-out and generating the CODECO. For Qatar and UAE operations, integration with QNBP (Qatar National Billing Platform) and Dubai Trade Portal must be validated. The gate-out CODECO must be archived for a minimum of 5 years per local trade documentation retention laws.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R004	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Truck and Driver Pre-Registration	The system must support advance truck and driver pre-registration via web portal, mobile app, and EDI submission. Pre-registration must capture: truck license plate (supporting Arabic/English dual-format plates for GCC countries and Indian format), driver national ID or passport number, driver terminal access card number, trucking company details, booking or delivery order reference, expected arrival time window, and container number(s) to be picked up or delivered. Pre-registered transactions must be validated against active bookings (export) or approved delivery orders (import) before the truck arrival appointment is confirmed. The system must support biometric driver verification (fingerprint or facial recognition) integration at gate lanes for security compliance with Qatar MOI, UAE ICA, KSA MOI, and Indian CISF requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R005	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Automated Container Number Recognition	The system must integrate with Optical Character Recognition (OCR) camera systems at gate lanes to automatically read container numbers, ISO size/type codes, and hazmat placards from container surfaces. OCR results must be validated against ISO 6346 check-digit algorithm and matched to expected container numbers from pre-registration or vessel discharge lists. The system must achieve a minimum 98% first-pass recognition accuracy and provide a manual correction interface for failed reads. OCR captures must include timestamped photographic evidence stored with the gate transaction record. The system must handle containers with damaged, obscured, or non-standard markings by flagging them for manual inspection and maintaining a photographic audit trail for dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R006	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Container Damage Inspection Recording	The system must provide a digital container damage inspection interface usable on tablets and mobile devices at gate lanes. Inspectors must be able to record damage using IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) damage codes with location references mapped to the standard container damage diagram (front, rear, left, right, top, floor, interior). Each damage entry must capture: damage type (dent, hole, rust, bent, missing part), severity (minor, major, critical), estimated repair cost in local currency, photographs (minimum resolution 2MP), and inspector ID. The inspection record must be attached to the gate transaction and transmitted with the CODECO message. For leased containers, damage records must be flagged for Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) processing and lessor notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R007	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Seal Verification and Management	The system must track container seal numbers throughout the gate transaction lifecycle with full chain-of-custody documentation. At gate-in, the system must record all seal types present: shipping line high-security bolt seals (ISO 17712 H-class), customs seals, shipper seals, and electronic RFID seals. The system must validate seal numbers against the booking instruction or bill of lading manifest. At gate-out, seal integrity must be verified and any discrepancy (broken seal, missing seal, mismatched number) must generate an immediate alert to terminal security and the responsible shipping line agent. For customs-sealed containers in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, any seal breach must be reported to the respective customs authority within 30 minutes per local regulatory requirements and trigger a physical inspection hold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R008	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Customs Hold and Release Integration	The system must integrate with customs systems in all four jurisdictions to enforce container release holds and process release notifications before permitting gate-out. Integrations must include: Qatar Customs Al Nadeeb system, Dubai Customs Mirsal 2 via Dubai Trade, Abu Dhabi Customs ANJAD system, Saudi Customs FASAH platform, and Indian Customs ICEGATE EDI system. The system must support multiple concurrent hold types per container (customs examination, quarantine, port health, dangerous goods inspection, court order) from different authorities. Each hold must be individually clearable and the container must not be released until all active holds are cleared. Hold status must be visible on the gate transaction screen with Arabic and English descriptions and the originating authority name.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R009	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Weighbridge Integration and VGM Capture	The system must integrate with terminal weighbridge systems to capture container gross weight during gate-in and gate-out transactions. Weighbridge readings must be recorded with calibration certificate reference, measurement timestamp, and tolerance margin. For export gate-in, the measured weight must be compared against the declared VGM (SOLAS regulation VI/2) and any discrepancy exceeding the terminal's configured tolerance (default 5% or 1 tonne, whichever is greater) must trigger a VGM discrepancy alert. The system must support Method 1 (weighing the packed container) and Method 2 (aggregate weighing) VGM certification workflows. Weight records must be stored in kilograms as the primary unit with automatic conversion display for tonnes and pounds. Weighbridge data must feed into the yard slot allocation tier-weight validation to ensure safe stacking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R010	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Gate Transaction Appointment Scheduling	The system must provide a truck appointment scheduling system (TAS) to manage gate traffic flow and reduce congestion. Appointments must be bookable in configurable time windows (default 60-minute slots) with maximum truck count limits per slot per lane. The TAS must enforce appointment compliance by rejecting or deprioritizing trucks that arrive outside their appointment window beyond a configurable grace period (default 30 minutes). Priority scheduling must be available for reefer containers, hazmat cargo, and VIP client accounts. The system must publish real-time appointment availability via API for integration with trucking company dispatch systems and third-party logistics platforms. No-show tracking must be implemented with configurable penalty rules that can restrict future appointment booking for chronic no-show trucking companies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R011	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Radiation and Security Scanning Integration	The system must integrate with radiation portal monitor (RPM) systems installed at terminal gates to record radiation scan results for every container passing through the gate. Scan results must be classified as: clear, alarm, or equipment fault. Any radiation alarm must immediately halt the gate transaction, lock the lane, trigger security protocols, and notify terminal security, port authority CBRN team, and the relevant national nuclear regulatory body (Qatar MECC, UAE FANR, KSA NRRC, India AERB). The system must log the scan timestamp, RPM device serial number, alarm level, isotope identification if available, and resolution outcome. Integration with X-ray and gamma-ray scanning systems must also be supported for non-intrusive inspection (NII) programs mandated by customs authorities in all four countries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R012	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Gate Transaction Exception Handling	The system must manage gate transaction exceptions through a structured workflow with configurable escalation paths. Exception types must include: container number mismatch, missing booking or delivery order, expired pickup validity, weight discrepancy, undeclared hazmat, damaged container rejection, seal discrepancy, customs hold block, driver authorization failure, and truck safety violation. Each exception must generate a unique exception ticket with severity classification, automatic assignment to the responsible department (operations, customs, security, commercial), and SLA-based resolution tracking. Unresolved exceptions must escalate through configurable tiers: gate clerk to gate supervisor to terminal operations manager. The system must support exception resolution in offline mode with synchronization upon reconnection, ensuring that trucks are not indefinitely stranded at the gate during system outages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R013	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Multi-Container Truck Transaction Processing	The system must handle truck transactions involving multiple containers simultaneously, including twin-twenty (two 20ft containers on a single chassis), tandem trailer configurations, and multi-axle combinations. Each container on the truck must be individually processed with its own CODECO message, damage inspection, seal verification, and customs clearance check while maintaining a single unified truck transaction record. The system must validate that all containers on a multi-container load belong to compatible delivery orders or bookings and that the combined weight does not exceed the truck's registered maximum gross vehicle weight. For Indian operations at JNPT, the system must support the common practice of CFS-bound multi-container trips with consolidated trip sheets and e-way bill validation under GST regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R014	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Gate Throughput Analytics and Reporting	The system must provide real-time and historical gate throughput analytics including: average truck turn time (entry to exit), gate-in and gate-out transaction counts per hour/shift/day, lane utilization rates, peak congestion periods, exception rates by type, and average queue waiting times. Reports must be segmented by terminal entity, gate direction, container type, and shipping line. KPI dashboards must display against configurable targets (e.g., target truck turn time of 30 minutes for Jebel Ali). Analytics must support export to PDF and Excel with bilingual Arabic-English column headers. Hijri calendar-based reporting periods must be available alongside Gregorian for GCC operations. Historical trend analysis must cover a minimum rolling 24-month window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R015	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Electronic Delivery Order Validation	The system must validate electronic delivery orders (e-DOs) at gate-out against the original bill of lading, import manifest, and customs release records. Validation must verify: consignee or authorized representative identity, delivery order validity period (not expired), container number match, remaining balance on partial delivery orders (for LCL breakbulk), and payment clearance status for terminal charges (THC, storage, reefer monitoring) in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must support both single-use and multi-use delivery orders with automatic decrement tracking. For containers under bank letter of credit financing, the system must verify bank release authorization. Integration with shipping line e-DO platforms and freight forwarder portals must be supported via API with OAuth 2.0 authentication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R016	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Gate Operations Offline Resilience	The system must maintain gate operations continuity during network outages or central server unavailability through a local edge-computing gateway at each gate complex. The edge gateway must cache: pre-registration data for the next 24 hours, active booking and delivery order lists, customs hold status snapshots, vessel discharge lists, and container inventory. During offline mode, gate transactions must be processed locally with CODECO messages queued for transmission upon reconnection. The edge gateway must support a minimum of 500 gate transactions in offline mode. Upon reconnection, the system must perform bidirectional synchronization with conflict detection and resolution. An offline transaction indicator must be appended to all CODECO messages generated during the outage period to notify receiving parties of potential data latency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-002-R017	FEAT-010-2-002	MOD-010	Hazardous Cargo Gate Screening Protocol	The system must enforce enhanced gate screening protocols for containers declared as carrying hazardous or dangerous goods per IMDG Code classifications. At gate-in, the system must verify: valid dangerous goods declaration (DGD) document attached, IMO hazard class and UN number consistency with booking data, proper hazmat placard display on container exterior matching declared class, emergency contact information on file, and segregation compatibility with containers already in the yard. The system must cross-reference against the terminal's dangerous goods capacity limits and reject gate entry if acceptance would breach licensed storage limits imposed by Qatar Civil Defence, UAE CD, KSA CD, or Indian PESO/CIF authorities. A dedicated hazmat gate-in checklist must be completed digitally with inspector signature before the container proceeds to the allocated hazmat yard zone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R001	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Equipment Interchange Receipt Creation	The system must generate a digital Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) for every container interchange event at terminal gates, depots, rail yards, and CFS facilities. Each EIR must capture: container number (ISO 6346 validated), interchange type (gate-in, gate-out, vessel load, vessel discharge, rail load, rail unload), date and time (Gregorian and Hijri), location (UN/LOCODE and facility code), releasing party, receiving party, container size/type code, tare weight, condition status (sound, damaged, requires repair), and inspector identification. The EIR must be assigned a unique sequential reference number per terminal entity with a prefix identifying the facility (e.g., HAM for Hamad Port, JEA for Jebel Ali). EIRs must be generated within 2 minutes of the interchange event and immediately made available to both parties via portal and API.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R002	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Standardized Damage Code Recording	The system must implement IICL damage coding standards (IICL Guide for Container Equipment Inspection, 7th edition or later) for recording container damage on EIRs. Damage must be captured using a three-part code structure: component code (e.g., FR for front end, LH for left side, TP for top), damage type code (e.g., DN for dent, HL for hole, CR for corrosion), and location reference on a standardized container diagram grid. Each damage entry must include severity classification (minor/major/critical), estimated dimensional measurement (length x width x depth), and the inspector's assessment of whether the damage is pre-existing or new. The system must maintain a damage history per container showing progressive condition changes across multiple interchange events, enabling clear attribution of when and where damage occurred.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R003	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Photographic Evidence Capture and Storage	The system must require and store photographic evidence of container condition during each interchange event. A minimum of 6 photographs must be captured per container: front (door end), rear, left side, right side, top, and underside/floor. Additional close-up photographs must be mandatory for each recorded damage point. All photographs must be geotagged, timestamped, and linked to the EIR record. Image storage must comply with data residency requirements: Qatar data stored within Qatar, UAE data within UAE, KSA data within KSA, and India data within India per respective data protection regulations (Qatar Law No. 13/2016, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, India DPDP Act 2023). Photographs must be retained for a minimum of 3 years and must be accessible for dispute resolution within 30 seconds retrieval time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R004	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	AI-Powered Damage Detection from Images	The system must provide AI-powered computer vision analysis of container photographs captured during interchange to automatically detect and classify visible damage. The AI model must identify common damage types including dents, holes, corrosion patches, bent structural members, damaged door seals and gaskets, floor damage, and missing or damaged placards. Detected damage must be mapped to IICL damage codes and pre-populated on the EIR for inspector review and confirmation. The AI detection must achieve a minimum 90% recall rate for major and critical damage categories. Inspectors must be able to accept, modify, or reject AI-detected damage items. All AI detections must be flagged as machine-generated in the audit trail to distinguish from human-inspected findings for legal and liability purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R005	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Liability Attribution and Dispute Management	The system must automatically determine liability for container damage based on comparison of inbound and outbound EIR condition records. When a container is received in sound condition (clean inbound EIR) and returned with new damage (outbound EIR shows damage not present at interchange-in), the system must flag the receiving party as the responsible party for repair costs. A formal dispute workflow must be initiated when the attributed party contests liability, supporting document attachment (photographs, survey reports, correspondence), multi-party involvement (terminal, shipping line, trucker, depot), and escalation to arbitration. Dispute records must track financial amounts in the original transaction currency with support for multi-currency claims (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Settlement amounts must be posted to accounts receivable or payable in the respective entity ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R006	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Repair Estimate and Authorization Workflow	The system must generate repair cost estimates for damaged containers based on IICL damage codes, severity levels, and configurable repair rate tables. Repair rates must be maintained per depot location and per currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with periodic rate review and update capability. The estimate must itemize: labor hours by repair category (structural, panel, door, floor, reefer machinery), material costs (steel, patches, seals, paint), and any third-party subcontractor charges. Estimates must be routed through an approval workflow: estimates below a configurable threshold (e.g., USD 500 equivalent) auto-approved, above threshold requires shipping line or lessor written authorization. The system must track estimate-to-actual variance and flag repair jobs where actual costs exceed the approved estimate by more than 15% for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R007	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Container Condition Grade Classification	The system must classify containers into standardized condition grades based on EIR inspection results. Grades must include: Grade A (food-grade, suitable for food/pharmaceutical cargo, no rust, no odor, clean interior), Grade B (cargo-worthy, structurally sound, minor cosmetic damage acceptable, valid CSC plate), Grade C (wind and watertight, suitable for storage or less demanding cargo, moderate damage), Grade D (damaged, not suitable for cargo, requires repair before use), and Grade E (beyond economic repair, candidate for disposal or sale as scrap). Condition grades must be automatically suggested by the system based on the damage codes recorded but must allow inspector override with documented justification. Shipping lines must be able to define custom grade-to-usage mappings for their fleet within the standard framework.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R008	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	EIR Digital Signature and Legal Validity	The system must support digital signatures on Equipment Interchange Receipts to establish legal validity and non-repudiation. Both the releasing party representative and the receiving party representative must digitally sign the EIR at the point of interchange. Digital signatures must comply with: Qatar Law No. 16/2010 on electronic transactions, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 46/2021 on electronic transactions, KSA Anti-Cyber Crime Law and E-Transaction regulations, and India IT Act 2000 with 2008 amendments on electronic signatures. The system must support certificate-based digital signatures using PKI infrastructure as well as biometric signature capture (fingerprint or stylus signature on tablet). Unsigned EIRs must be flagged as provisional and must not be used for liability attribution until both signatures are obtained or a configurable timeout (default 48 hours) triggers automatic finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R009	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	CSC Plate and Safety Approval Tracking	The system must track Container Safety Convention (CSC) plate information for every container processed through the interchange system. CSC data must include: approval reference number, date of manufacture, maximum gross weight, allowable stacking weight, transverse racking test load, and next examination due date. The system must flag containers with expired or soon-to-expire CSC approval (within 30 days) during gate-in and interchange processing. Containers with expired CSC plates must be directed to inspection and must not be loaded onto vessels until CSC compliance is confirmed. The system must maintain examination history and support the ACEP (Approved Continuous Examination Programme) for participating shipping lines, tracking periodic examination schedules and recording examination results per CSC Convention 1972 (amended 2014) requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R010	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Reefer Equipment Specific Interchange Checks	The system must enforce additional interchange inspection requirements for reefer containers beyond standard dry container checks. Reefer-specific checks must include: compressor unit operational status (running, standby, fault), set-point temperature, actual cargo temperature, supply air and return air temperatures, humidity settings if applicable, pre-trip inspection (PTI) validity and date, power cable condition, electrical plug condition, refrigerant type and charge level indicator, controller firmware version, and defrost system functionality. All reefer readings must be captured digitally with automatic comparison against the booking's required temperature range. Any deviation between set-point and the booking requirement must trigger an alert. For pharma-grade reefer containers (e.g., PharmaClass or NaturoFresh), enhanced GDP (Good Distribution Practice) compliant inspection checklists must be enforced.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R011	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Tank Container Interchange Specialization	The system must support specialized interchange procedures for ISO tank containers (ISO type code 22T, etc.) with additional data capture requirements beyond standard dry containers. Tank-specific fields must include: last cargo carried (product name and UN number if hazmat), cleaning certificate reference and date, next test due date (2.5-year or 5-year hydraulic test per ADR/RID/IMDG), steam heating coil condition, top and bottom discharge valve status, pressure relief valve calibration date, tank shell thickness measurement date, and inner lining type and condition. The system must enforce a mandatory cleaning/purging verification before a tank container is accepted for a new cargo incompatible with the previous product. Tank container interchange records must be shared with the tank operator and any intermediate cleaning depot via automated notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R012	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Flat-Rack and Open-Top Interchange Handling	The system must accommodate specialized interchange requirements for flat-rack (ISO type codes 22P1, 42P1) and open-top (ISO type codes 22U1, 42U1) containers. For flat-racks, the inspection must cover: end-wall condition (fixed or collapsible, including locking mechanism), lashing ring and D-ring integrity (count and condition of each), stanchion socket condition, and floor structural integrity. For open-top containers, additional checks must include: tarpaulin condition and completeness, roof bow assemblies, header bar condition, and top-corner casting integrity. The system must capture over-height and over-width measurements for out-of-gauge cargo already loaded on flat-racks at gate-in, recording exact dimensions for yard allocation and vessel stowage planning. All specialized equipment types must have their own EIR templates with appropriate field layouts rendered in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R013	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Interchange Event Notification System	The system must send automated notifications to all relevant parties upon completion of each interchange event. Notification recipients must include: the container owner or lessor, the releasing party, the receiving party, the container booking party (if different), and the terminal operator. Notifications must be configurable per party preference: email, SMS, EDI (CODECO), API webhook, or in-app push notification. Each notification must contain the EIR reference number, interchange type, container number, condition summary, and a link to the full digital EIR with photographs. For containers under lease agreements, the system must additionally notify the lessor's designated fleet manager when damage is recorded that exceeds a configurable notification threshold (e.g., estimated repair cost above USD 200). Notification language must match the recipient's profile preference (Arabic or English) with automatic translation of damage descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R014	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Historical Interchange Comparison and Analytics	The system must provide analytics comparing container condition across multiple interchange events to identify patterns and trends. Analysis must include: average damage rate by container age, damage frequency by container type and size, most common damage types by terminal, damage attribution distribution across parties (terminals, truckers, depots, shipping lines), and average repair turnaround time and cost. The system must generate trend reports showing condition degradation curves per container fleet segment. Analytics must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), terminal, date range (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian calendar selection), shipping line, and container type. Dashboards must display comparative benchmarks against industry averages where available. Export to PDF and Excel with bilingual headers must be supported, along with scheduled automated report generation and distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R015	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Third-Party Survey Integration	The system must integrate with third-party container survey companies for independent condition assessments when disputes arise or when container owners require certified inspections. The integration must support: survey request submission with container details and specific inspection scope, surveyor assignment and scheduling, digital survey report upload with photographs, and automated linking of survey findings to the relevant EIR and dispute records. Survey reports must be stored as immutable documents with the surveyor's digital signature and professional credentials. The system must maintain an approved surveyor registry per jurisdiction (Lloyd's Register, Bureau Veritas, SGS, etc.) and validate surveyor qualifications before accepting their reports. Survey costs must be tracked and allocated to the responsible party as determined by the dispute resolution workflow in the applicable billing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R016	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Bulk Interchange Processing for Vessel Operations	The system must support bulk EIR processing during vessel discharge and loading operations where hundreds of containers are interchanged between vessel and terminal within compressed timeframes. Bulk processing must allow pre-population of common fields (vessel name, voyage number, berth, interchange type) with individual container-level damage recording via mobile devices by quay-side inspectors. The system must handle concurrent EIR creation by multiple inspectors working simultaneously on the same vessel operation without data conflicts or duplicate records. Performance targets must ensure EIR generation for 200+ containers per hour during peak vessel operations at Jebel Ali (the region's largest port). Bulk interchange records must be reconciled against the vessel bay plan and any container count discrepancy between the discharge list and completed EIRs must be flagged for immediate investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-003-R017	FEAT-010-2-003	MOD-010	Offline EIR Processing Capability	The system must support EIR creation and container inspection recording in offline mode for locations with limited or no network connectivity, such as remote depot facilities, inland container depots in India, or during temporary network outages at port terminals. The offline module must cache container master data, damage code libraries, inspection checklists, and party information on the mobile device or workstation. Photographs captured offline must be stored locally with the EIR record and uploaded upon reconnection. Offline EIRs must be clearly marked with an offline-generated indicator and queued for server-side validation upon synchronization. Conflicts detected during sync (e.g., container already interchanged by another party) must be flagged for manual resolution. The offline mode must support a minimum of 200 EIR records per device before requiring synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R001	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Master Lease Agreement Registration	The system must maintain a registry of master lease agreements (MLAs) between operating entities and container lessors (Triton, Textainer, Beacon, Seaco, CAI, Florens, etc.). Each MLA record must capture: agreement reference number, lessor name and SCAC code, lessee entity (with separate registrations for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities), effective date and expiry date (Gregorian and Hijri), container type and size eligibility, lease type (long-term, short-term, spot, one-way), per-diem rate schedule by container type in agreement currency (typically USD), pick-up and drop-off location lists, damage protection plan (DPP) coverage details, and liability caps. The system must alert contract managers 90, 60, and 30 days before MLA expiry. Rate schedules must support tiered pricing based on volume commitments and seasonal adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-002-1-004-R002	FEAT-002-1-004	MOD-002	User Action Audit Capture	Capture every user action across the shipping ERP — login, data view, create, update, delete, approve, reject, print, export, download — with before/after state for data modifications	must_have	audit-agent	None — fully automated capture; compliance team queries logs as needed	["Every user action logged with: user ID, entity, role, action, resource, timestamp, IP, session ID", "Data modification logs include full before-state and after-state JSON for change tracking", "Bulk operations log individual record changes — not just the bulk action metadata", "Audit capture latency under 100ms — no perceptible impact on user experience"]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R002	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	On-Hire Transaction Processing	The system must process on-hire transactions when containers are picked up from a lessor's depot or released from the lessor's available pool at a terminal. On-hire must record: container number, lessor reference, applicable MLA reference, on-hire date and time, on-hire location (UN/LOCODE), container size, type, and manufacturing year, tare weight, condition at pickup (referencing EIR), and the lessee entity responsible for per-diem charges. The system must validate that the container type and pickup location are authorized under the referenced MLA before completing the on-hire. On-hire confirmation must be transmitted to the lessor via EDI (CODECO or proprietary format) and API within 4 hours. The on-hire event must trigger per-diem billing commencement in the system's financial module and create an accounts payable accrual entry in the lessee entity's books.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R003	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Off-Hire Transaction Processing	The system must process off-hire transactions when leased containers are returned to a lessor's designated depot or redelivery location. Off-hire must record: container number, lessor reference, off-hire date and time, off-hire location (UN/LOCODE), container condition at return (referencing the gate-in EIR and inspection results), and any damage charges applicable per the MLA's damage protection plan terms. The system must validate that the off-hire location is an approved redelivery point under the MLA and flag unauthorized drop-off attempts with estimated repositioning charges. Off-hire confirmation must be transmitted to the lessor within 4 hours. The off-hire event must stop per-diem billing, calculate the final per-diem amount from on-hire to off-hire date, and generate a billing reconciliation entry. Multi-currency settlement must be supported where the per-diem is in USD but the entity's functional currency differs (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R004	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Per-Diem Calculation and Accrual Engine	The system must calculate per-diem lease charges daily for all on-hire containers, applying the correct rate from the applicable MLA rate schedule based on container type, size, and lease category. The calculation engine must handle: rate changes mid-lease (pro-rated from effective date), volume discount tiers (recalculated monthly based on average on-hire count), free days at on-hire and off-hire locations as negotiated in the MLA, and penalty rates for containers held beyond maximum lease duration. Daily accruals must be posted to the lessee entity's accounts payable in the entity's functional currency with the USD per-diem amount converted at the daily exchange rate from the Central Bank (QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI respectively). Month-end per-diem accrual reports must be generated per lessor, per entity, with aging analysis showing 30/60/90/120+ day outstanding container inventories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R005	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Lessor Invoice Reconciliation	The system must support automated reconciliation of lessor invoices against internally calculated per-diem charges and on-hire/off-hire records. Reconciliation must match: container-level per-diem line items, on-hire and off-hire dates, applicable rates, free-day adjustments, damage repair charges, and pick-up/drop-off surcharges. The system must flag discrepancies by category (date variance, rate variance, missing containers, duplicate charges) and calculate the net difference amount. A reconciliation workflow must route discrepancies to the responsible commercial team for investigation and resolution with the lessor. Approved invoice amounts must be posted for payment processing in the lessor's invoicing currency. The system must track dispute aging and generate escalation alerts when reconciliation items remain unresolved beyond 30 days. Historical reconciliation data must support trend analysis to identify chronic discrepancy patterns per lessor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R006	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Fleet Rebalancing and Repositioning Planning	The system must provide AI-driven fleet rebalancing recommendations to optimize the distribution of leased containers across the Qatar/UAE/KSA/India network. The rebalancing engine must analyze: current on-hire container inventory by location, forecasted demand based on booking projections and historical seasonal patterns, available empty container stock at each location, repositioning costs (inland trucking, feeder vessel slots, rail transport) in local currencies, and lessor preferences for redelivery locations. Recommendations must specify: containers to reposition (by number or quantity per type), origin and destination locations, recommended transport mode, estimated cost, and expected availability date at destination. The system must compare the cost of repositioning existing on-hire containers versus picking up new containers at the destination from local lessor depots to determine the most cost-effective option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R007	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Container Substitution and Interchange Between Lessors	The system must track container substitution events where a container from one lessor is used to fulfill a commitment originally allocated to another lessor's container. Substitution must record the original container number and lessor, the substitute container number and lessor, substitution reason (original unavailable, damaged, wrong type), authorization reference, and any cost differential between the two lessors' per-diem rates. The system must also manage interchange agreements between lessors where containers may be cross-utilized under bilateral arrangements. All substitution transactions must be reported to both the original and substitute lessors for billing adjustment. Financial impact of substitutions must be tracked and reported per entity to commercial management for lessor performance evaluation and contract renegotiation support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R008	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Lease Expiry and Renewal Management	The system must proactively manage lease expiry timelines for individual containers and master lease agreements. For containers approaching their maximum lease duration (as specified in the MLA), the system must generate alerts at configurable intervals (default 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry) recommending either off-hire at the nearest approved depot, lease extension request to the lessor, or container swap. Bulk renewal processing must be supported where multiple containers under the same MLA can be extended simultaneously with a single lessor authorization. The system must track extension requests and lessor responses, automatically updating per-diem rates if the extension terms differ from the original lease. For MLAs approaching expiry, the system must generate a renewal package including: current on-hire fleet size, utilization statistics, total per-diem spend, damage charge history, and comparison quotes from alternative lessors to support contract negotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R009	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Damage Protection Plan Management	The system must track Damage Protection Plan (DPP) or Container Damage Insurance (CDI) coverage for each leased container. DPP records must capture: coverage type (full, limited, catastrophic only), covered damage categories and exclusions per the MLA terms, deductible amounts per incident, maximum coverage cap, and premium rate (typically included in per-diem rate). When damage is recorded at off-hire, the system must automatically determine whether the damage qualifies for DPP coverage based on the damage type, severity, and cause. Covered damage must be processed under the DPP claim workflow with the lessor, while excluded damage must be routed to the standard repair estimate and liability process. DPP claim amounts must be tracked per container and per entity with annual summary reports for insurance renewal negotiations. Coverage status must be displayed on all EIR and off-hire screens.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R010	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Lessor Portal and API Integration	The system must provide integration with major container lessor platforms via API and EDI for real-time fleet status synchronization. Integration must support: on-hire/off-hire event exchange, container availability queries at specific locations, per-diem rate look-up, invoice and credit note exchange, damage claim submission and status tracking, and fleet inventory reconciliation. The system must maintain a unified integration layer that normalizes data from different lessor-specific formats (Triton Direct, Textainer TOPS, Beacon ContainerTrack, etc.) into the system's standard schema. API connections must use OAuth 2.0 or certificate-based mutual TLS authentication. EDI integrations must support EDIFACT message types CODECO, COPARN, and INVOIC. Integration health monitoring must track message delivery success rates and alert operations when a lessor connection fails or response latency exceeds acceptable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R011	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	One-Way Lease and Street Turn Management	The system must manage one-way lease arrangements where a container is picked up at one location and authorized for drop-off at a different designated location, potentially in a different country. One-way leases must track: origin pickup point, authorized drop-off point(s), one-way surcharge amount, validity period, and route restrictions. The system must also support street-turn transactions where an import container is reused for export without returning to the depot, reducing empty repositioning. Street turns must validate: lessor authorization for the reuse, container condition suitability for the new cargo, cleaning requirements between cargoes, and per-diem billing continuity or adjustment. Multi-entity street turns (e.g., import entity in UAE, export entity in Qatar) must handle inter-company container usage charges and transfer pricing documentation for tax compliance across GCC and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R012	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Container Fleet Utilization Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive leased container fleet utilization analytics including: total on-hire fleet size by lessor and container type, average days on-hire per trip, idle days (on-hire but not allocated to a booking), laden vs empty days ratio, geographic distribution of on-hire fleet across network locations, and cost per TEU-day analysis. Utilization must be benchmarked against MLA minimum volume commitments to identify under-utilization penalties or over-utilization requiring additional capacity. Dashboards must display trend lines showing fleet size evolution, per-diem spend trajectories, and seasonal utilization patterns over rolling 24-month periods. Analytics must be segmented by operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with consolidated group views available. Reports must support bilingual Arabic-English output with Hijri and Gregorian date columns and amounts displayed in both local currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R013	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Container Age and Retirement Tracking	The system must track container age from manufacture date and flag containers approaching or exceeding the maximum age threshold specified in the MLA (typically 12-15 years for dry containers, 10-12 years for reefers). Age tracking must be cross-referenced with CSC plate examination dates and structural condition assessments from recent EIRs. Containers exceeding the age threshold must be flagged for: return to lessor at next off-hire opportunity, exclusion from vessel loading if age exceeds carrier age policy limits, and enhanced inspection requirements at interchange. The system must generate aging fleet reports per lessor showing the age distribution of the on-hire fleet and projecting retirement volumes over the next 12/24/36 months. For containers approaching end-of-life, the system must initiate proactive replacement planning by triggering substitute container requests to the lessor and identifying suitable replacement units from available stock.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R014	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Multi-Entity Lease Cost Allocation	The system must allocate leased container costs across multiple operating entities when a container is used across different jurisdictions during a single lease period. For example, a container on-hired in UAE (AED entity), used for a shipment to India (INR entity), and off-hired at JNPT must have its per-diem charges allocated proportionally based on configurable rules: days in each entity's territory, booking entity assignment, or fixed allocation percentages. Inter-company lease cost transfers must generate proper transfer pricing documentation complying with GCC transfer pricing frameworks, India's Section 92 of the Income Tax Act (arm's length pricing), and OECD guidelines. Allocated costs must be posted in each entity's functional currency with exchange differences recognized per IAS 21 (Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates). Month-end inter-company reconciliation reports must show lease cost allocations with full audit trail of the allocation methodology applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R015	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Lessor KPI Scorecard and Performance Evaluation	The system must generate lessor performance scorecards measuring key metrics across the leasing relationship. KPIs must include: container availability rate at committed locations, average container age and condition quality at on-hire, on-time response to availability requests, invoice accuracy rate (percentage of invoices matching internal calculations without dispute), dispute resolution turnaround time, DPP claim processing speed, equipment variety and flexibility (ability to provide specialized equipment types on demand), and API/EDI integration reliability uptime. Scorecards must be calculated monthly and presented quarterly for lessor business review meetings. Historical performance trend analysis must cover a minimum of 12 months. The scorecard must support weighting of different KPIs based on business priority and must generate a composite performance score per lessor to inform contract renewal and fleet allocation decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R016	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Sale and Leaseback Transaction Support	The system must support sale-and-leaseback transactions where an operating entity sells owned containers to a lessor and simultaneously leases them back under an MLA. The transaction must record: container inventory being sold (individual container numbers with specifications), sale price per unit and total, leaseback MLA terms and effective date, per-diem rate for leased-back containers, and accounting treatment (operating lease vs finance lease classification per IFRS 16). The system must derecognize sold containers from the owned fleet register and simultaneously create on-hire records under the new leaseback MLA. Financial entries must include: sale proceeds recognition, gain or loss on disposal, right-of-use asset recognition (if finance lease), and lease liability creation. All entries must be generated in the selling entity's functional currency with proper multi-currency handling if the sale price or lease payments are denominated in a foreign currency (typically USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R017	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Off-Hire Discrepancy and Rejection Handling	The system must manage scenarios where a lessor rejects or disputes an off-hire transaction at a redelivery location. Rejection reasons must be categorized: unauthorized redelivery location, container condition below minimum acceptance standard, container type not eligible for return at that location, missing documentation, and outstanding per-diem balance. When a rejection occurs, the system must: maintain the container's on-hire status and continue per-diem billing, record the rejection reason and lessor communication, generate alternative off-hire options (nearest approved depot, repair and re-attempt, redirect to different lessor location), and alert the commercial team for resolution. If the container requires repair before off-hire acceptance, the system must estimate repair costs versus continued per-diem charges to recommend the optimal course of action. All rejection and resolution interactions must be logged with timestamps for dispute arbitration purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-2-004-R018	FEAT-010-2-004	MOD-010	Offline Lease Transaction Recording	The system must support recording of on-hire and off-hire transactions in offline mode when network connectivity to central servers is unavailable at remote depots or inland container yards, particularly relevant for Indian ICD locations and remote KSA dry ports. Offline-capable functions must include: on-hire pickup recording with container details and EIR, off-hire return recording with condition assessment, photograph capture and local storage, and per-diem calculation using cached rate tables. Upon reconnection, offline transactions must synchronize with the central system and validate against MLA terms. Conflicts such as a container being on-hired offline while already showing as on-hire in the central system must be flagged for manual resolution. The offline module must cache the complete MLA rate table, approved location lists, and container master data for a minimum of 72 hours of autonomous operation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R001	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Multi-Type Survey Registration and Scheduling	The system must support registration and scheduling of multiple survey types including pre-trip inspection (PTI), on-hire survey, off-hire survey, periodic condition survey, damage inspection, and customs hold inspection. Each survey type must have configurable checklists specific to container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK). Scheduling must account for port-specific operating hours at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, including Friday/Saturday weekends for Qatar and KSA entities versus Saturday/Sunday for India. The AI engine must auto-prioritize surveys based on vessel ETAs, container booking urgency, and inspection backlog, ensuring no container is dispatched without a valid PTI certificate within configurable validity periods per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R002	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	IICL-Compliant Damage Classification System	The system must implement the full IICL damage code matrix for container inspection reporting, covering all component groups including panels, rails, cross-members, doors, roof, floor, corner posts, and corner fittings. Each damage entry must capture component code, damage type (dent, hole, cut, crack, corrosion, missing), location coordinates using the IICL grid reference system, dimensions in metric units, and severity classification. The system must support both IICL 5th and 6th edition standards with configurable switching per leasing company agreement. Arabic translations of all IICL damage descriptions must be available for surveyors operating in Qatar, UAE, and KSA, with RTL layout rendering correctly for all damage report forms and printed outputs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R003	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	AI-Powered Photographic Damage Detection	The system must integrate an AI image recognition engine capable of analyzing container inspection photographs to automatically detect and classify damage types including dents, holes, rust, structural deformation, and missing components. The AI model must achieve a minimum 90% accuracy rate on standard damage types after initial training on at least 50,000 labeled container images. Surveyors must be able to capture photos directly through the mobile application with GPS tagging confirming the inspection location at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Each photo must be timestamped, geotagged, and linked to the specific container unit, survey record, and damage code. The system must support offline photo capture with automatic sync when connectivity is restored, storing up to 500 photos locally per device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R004	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Repair Cost Estimation in Multi-Currency	The system must generate automated repair cost estimates based on detected damage codes, applying location-specific labor rates and material costs for each operating entity. Repair estimates must be calculated in the local currency of the inspection location: QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, SAR for KSA operations, and INR for JNPT. Cross-currency conversion must use daily exchange rates sourced from the central treasury module with rates locked at the date of estimate generation. Each estimate must itemize labor hours, material costs, equipment rental, and overhead with configurable markup percentages per entity. The system must maintain a historical repair cost database enabling trend analysis and vendor rate benchmarking across all four operating regions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R005	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Surveyor Workforce Assignment and Credentialing	The system must maintain a registry of certified surveyors with their qualifications, IICL certification status, language capabilities (English, Arabic, Hindi, Urdu), and port-specific access credentials. Automatic surveyor assignment must consider surveyor availability, certification validity, proximity to inspection location within the port, current workload, and specialization (e.g., reefer-qualified surveyors for refrigerated container inspections). The system must track certification expiry dates using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and generate renewal alerts 60, 30, and 15 days before expiry. For KSA and Qatar operations, surveyor assignment must respect prayer time schedules, automatically blocking inspection slots during Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha prayer times with configurable durations per entity and season.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R006	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Digital Survey Report Generation and Distribution	The system must generate standardized digital survey reports in PDF format containing all inspection findings, photographs, damage codes, repair estimates, and surveyor certifications. Reports must be available in bilingual format (English/Arabic) with proper RTL rendering for Arabic sections, including correct mirroring of damage location diagrams for Arabic readers. Each report must include a unique QR code linking to the digital record for verification. Distribution must support automated email delivery to configurable recipient lists per survey type, including ship operators, leasing companies, insurance underwriters, and port authorities. Reports must comply with the documentation requirements of Hamad Port Authority, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World Jebel Ali, and JNPT port trust, with entity-specific headers, logos, and regulatory reference numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R007	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Liability Determination and Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must implement an automated liability determination engine that assigns responsibility for container damage based on interchange records, gate survey comparisons, and custody chain analysis. When a container is returned with new damage not present in the on-hire survey, the system must automatically generate a liability claim against the last party in custody, referencing the specific IICL damage codes and photographic evidence. A configurable dispute resolution workflow must support multi-stage escalation: initial claim, counterparty response within configurable SLA periods, independent surveyor review, and final arbitration. All dispute communications must be logged with timestamps and linked to the survey records. The system must calculate liability amounts in the currency specified in the leasing or interchange agreement, applying the agreed-upon repair rate schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R008	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Pre-Trip Inspection Certificate Management	The system must manage PTI certificates for all container types with specific validation rules for refrigerated containers including compressor test, thermostat calibration, airflow test, drain hole check, and temperature recorder verification. PTI certificates must have configurable validity periods per container type and per entity, typically 30 days for dry containers and 14 days for reefer units. The system must automatically flag containers with expired or expiring PTI certificates and prevent booking confirmation for containers without valid PTI status. For reefer containers, the system must record set-point temperature, actual temperature, supply air temperature, return air temperature, humidity settings, and atmosphere control parameters. Integration with reefer monitoring hardware at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT must enable automated PTI data capture where equipment is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R009	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Customs and Regulatory Hold Inspection Tracking	The system must track containers placed under customs hold or regulatory inspection at any of the four operating ports, recording the hold authority (Qatar Customs, UAE Federal Customs Authority, Saudi ZATCA, Indian CBIC), hold reason code, expected duration, and special handling requirements. When a container is flagged for customs examination, the system must block any movement or release instructions until clearance is recorded with the official release reference number. The system must support integration with customs single-window systems at each port: Hamad Port's Al Nadeeb system, UAE's Mirsal-2, KSA's FASAH platform, and India's ICEGATE. Inspection scheduling must coordinate with customs officer availability and the container's yard position, minimizing unnecessary repositioning moves. All hold and release events must be timestamped and auditable for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R010	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Offline Mobile Inspection Application	The system must provide a mobile application for surveyors that functions fully offline in areas within port terminals where cellular or WiFi connectivity is unreliable, which is common in large yard areas at Jebel Ali and JNPT. The offline application must cache the full damage code library, container master data for units expected to be surveyed that day, surveyor checklists, and the photo capture module. All inspection data entered offline must be stored locally with conflict resolution logic that handles scenarios where the same container is surveyed by multiple surveyors or where the container master data has been updated on the server during the offline period. Synchronization must occur automatically when connectivity is restored, with a visual indicator showing sync status and any conflicts requiring manual resolution. The application must support Arabic and English language switching without requiring reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R011	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Container Grading and Condition Classification	The system must maintain a dynamic condition grading system for every container unit, assigning grades from A (cargo-worthy, no defects) through E (requires major repair or condemned). Grading must be automatically recalculated after each survey event using a weighted scoring algorithm that considers structural integrity, cosmetic condition, watertightness, door operation, floor condition, and for reefer units, machinery condition. The grading algorithm must be configurable per entity to accommodate different customer quality expectations—for example, Qatar and UAE premium customers may require Grade A or B only, while certain India trade lanes may accept Grade C containers. Grade changes must trigger automatic updates to container availability status, booking eligibility, and estimated residual value in the asset register. Historical grading data must be retained for a minimum of seven years to support asset lifecycle analysis and depreciation calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R012	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Third-Party Survey Company Integration	The system must support integration with third-party survey companies including internationally recognized firms used across the Middle East and Indian subcontinent trade lanes. The integration must enable electronic exchange of survey requests, inspection reports, and damage photographs using standardized XML or JSON APIs conforming to the UN/EDIFACT COEDOR message standard for container survey data. Third-party surveyors must receive survey assignments through a secure portal with role-based access limited to their assigned containers and surveys only. The system must reconcile third-party survey findings against internal surveyor reports, flagging discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds for management review. Invoice matching for third-party survey fees must support multi-currency billing (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic accrual posting to the appropriate entity's general ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R013	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Survey Analytics and KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time analytics dashboard displaying key survey and inspection KPIs including average survey turnaround time, PTI pass/fail rates by container type, most common damage types by port, repair cost trends, surveyor productivity metrics, and customer damage claim ratios. Dashboards must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, container type, time period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date ranges), and surveyor. The AI engine must generate automated insights identifying patterns such as recurring damage from specific trade routes, seasonal damage trends (e.g., increased corrosion during Gulf summer months), or underperforming repair vendors. All dashboard visualizations must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts, with axis labels, legends, and data annotations switching direction appropriately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R014	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Insurance Claim Documentation Package Assembly	The system must automatically assemble comprehensive insurance claim documentation packages when container damage exceeds configurable thresholds per entity and container ownership type (owned versus leased). The package must include the original on-hire survey report, the damage survey report with photographs, the IICL damage code summary, the repair cost estimate, the custody chain showing all parties who handled the container, and the vessel stowage position if damage is suspected to be voyage-related. The system must interface with the insurance module to track claim status, adjuster assignments, and settlement amounts in the claim currency. For owned containers, the system must calculate the insurance deductible applicable per the entity's marine cargo and equipment policy, comparing repair costs against deductible thresholds before recommending whether to file a claim. All documentation must meet the evidentiary requirements of Lloyd's of London and regional insurers in the GCC and India markets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R015	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Hazardous Container Decontamination Survey Protocol	The system must enforce special survey protocols for containers that previously carried hazardous cargo classified under IMDG Code classes 1 through 9. Before a previously hazardous container can be returned to general cargo service, the system must require a decontamination survey verifying that residual contamination levels are below the thresholds specified in the IMDG Code and local regulations of the inspection port. The survey checklist must be dynamically generated based on the previous cargo's IMDG class, UN number, and packing group. For radioactive materials (Class 7), the system must record Geiger counter readings and require clearance from the national radiation authority (FANR in UAE, KACARE in KSA, AERB in India, or MECC in Qatar). The system must maintain a permanent decontamination history for each container unit, accessible to booking agents to prevent allocation of formerly hazardous containers to sensitive cargoes such as foodstuffs without verified clean status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R016	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Automated Survey Scheduling Based on Vessel Arrival	The system must automatically schedule container surveys based on vessel arrival events, creating inspection work orders for containers being discharged at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. When a vessel's actual time of arrival (ATA) is confirmed, the system must generate pre-arrival survey schedules for containers requiring off-hire inspection, import customs examination, or damage assessment following reported voyage incidents. The scheduling engine must consider berth-to-yard transit times specific to each port's layout, available surveyor capacity, daylight hours for outdoor inspections, and prayer time restrictions in GCC ports. Survey priority must be automatically escalated for containers booked on connecting vessels with tight transshipment windows, containers carrying time-sensitive cargo such as perishables in reefer units, and containers flagged by the vessel master's damage report. The system must re-optimize the survey schedule dynamically when vessel ETAs change by more than two hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-001-R017	FEAT-010-3-001	MOD-010	Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance Logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive, tamper-proof audit trail of all survey and inspection activities including creation, modification, approval, and deletion of survey records. Every field change must be logged with the before value, after value, timestamp, user identity, and IP address or device identifier. The audit trail must satisfy regulatory requirements of all four operating jurisdictions: Qatar's MCIT data retention policies, UAE Federal Law No. 2 of 2019 on IT, KSA's PDPL (Personal Data Protection Law), and India's DPDP Act 2023. Survey records must be retained for a minimum period as configured per entity, with default retention of ten years for owned container records and seven years for leased container records. The system must support authorized regulatory inspectors accessing specific audit trails through a read-only portal with multi-factor authentication, generating exportable reports in formats acceptable to each country's maritime authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R001	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Master Lease Agreement Registration and Management	The system must support registration and lifecycle management of master lease agreements (MLAs) with international container leasing companies including Triton, Textainer, Beacon, Seaco, CAI, and Florens. Each MLA record must capture the agreement number, lessor details, effective dates, renewal terms, container type allocations (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK), per-diem rates by container type and age band, pickup and drop-off locations with approved depot lists, insurance requirements, maintenance obligations, and damage liability terms referencing IICL standards. The system must support MLAs denominated in USD (industry standard) with entity-level accounting in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Automated alerts must notify the leasing team 90, 60, and 30 days before MLA expiry or renewal deadlines, with escalation to management if no renewal action is taken within 30 days of expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R002	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	On-Hire Transaction Processing and Validation	The system must process container on-hire transactions when leased containers are picked up from lessor-approved depots or interchange points. Each on-hire record must capture the container number, size/type/ISO code, manufacturing year, tare weight, maximum payload, pickup location, pickup date and time, on-hire survey reference, and the applicable MLA and rate schedule. The system must validate that the container type and pickup location are authorized under the referenced MLA before confirming the on-hire. Per-diem billing must commence automatically from the on-hire date, with the system calculating daily accruals in the MLA currency (typically USD) and converting to entity local currency for accounting purposes. The system must reject on-hire transactions for containers that fail the on-hire survey or have unresolved damage claims, requiring survey clearance before the on-hire can be finalized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R003	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Off-Hire Transaction Processing and Reconciliation	The system must process container off-hire transactions when leased containers are returned to lessor-approved depots, capturing the return location, date and time, off-hire survey reference, and container condition grade. Per-diem billing must cease on the off-hire date, and the system must automatically calculate the total lease charges for the on-hire period. If the off-hire survey identifies damage beyond normal wear and tear per the MLA terms, the system must generate a damage liability estimate and hold the off-hire as provisional until the lessor confirms acceptance. The system must support lessor rejection of off-hire with reason codes and a dispute workflow. Reconciliation of per-diem charges must compare the company's calculated charges against the lessor's invoice, flagging discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerance thresholds (default 0.5% of invoice value). Off-hire must be blocked if attempted at a depot not listed in the applicable MLA's approved return locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R004	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Per-Diem Rate Management and Tiered Pricing	The system must manage complex per-diem rate structures including flat rates, tiered rates based on lease duration (e.g., lower rate after 90 days), seasonal rates, and promotional rates offered by lessors. Rate tables must be linked to specific MLAs and configurable by container type, age band (0-5 years, 5-10 years, 10-15 years, 15+ years), and geographic zone. The system must automatically apply the correct rate based on the on-hire date, current duration, and container attributes, handling rate tier transitions without manual intervention. When lessors issue rate adjustment notices (common during market fluctuations in the Middle East-Indian Subcontinent trade), the system must support effective-dated rate changes that apply prospectively to existing on-hire units from the agreed effective date. Rate comparisons across lessors must be available to the procurement team to support negotiation of new MLAs, displaying rates in both USD and entity local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R005	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	IFRS 16 Lease Accounting Compliance Engine	The system must calculate and maintain IFRS 16 right-of-use assets and lease liabilities for all container lease portfolios across all four operating entities. For each qualifying lease (term exceeding 12 months or not classified as short-term), the system must compute the initial right-of-use asset value, lease liability at present value of future payments using the entity's incremental borrowing rate, monthly depreciation of the right-of-use asset, interest expense on the lease liability, and lease liability remeasurement upon rate changes or term modifications. The system must support the short-term lease exemption election for container leases under 12 months duration, recognizing these as operating expenses. Journal entries must be auto-generated monthly in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with the USD-denominated lease obligations translated at the closing exchange rate, and foreign exchange differences recognized per IAS 21. The system must produce IFRS 16 disclosure notes for each entity's financial statements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R006	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Lessor Invoice Matching and Payment Processing	The system must perform three-way matching of lessor invoices against on-hire records and MLA rate schedules. Invoice data must be importable via EDI (IFTMIN/INVOIC messages), CSV upload, or manual entry, supporting invoice currencies of USD, EUR, and CHF as commonly used by international lessors. The matching engine must verify container count, per-diem rates, billing period, pickup and drop-off charges, handling fees, and damage repair charges against internal records. Discrepancies must be categorized as rate variances, period variances, unit count variances, or unrecognized charges, each routing to the appropriate resolver per configurable workflow rules. Approved invoices must generate payment requests in the lessor's preferred currency with settlement through the accounts payable module, applying early payment discounts where contractually available. The system must maintain a payables aging report per lessor across all entities with consolidated and entity-level views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R007	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Leased Fleet Utilization and Cost Analytics	The system must calculate and report leased container fleet utilization metrics including average days on-hire, average idle days between trips, utilization percentage by container type and entity, cost per TEU-day, and cost per revenue move. Analytics must compare leased container costs against owned container costs to support make-versus-lease decisions for the fleet planning team. The AI engine must identify underutilized leased containers that should be off-hired to reduce per-diem costs, considering upcoming demand forecasts, repositioning costs, and minimum commitment obligations under each MLA. Dashboards must display utilization heatmaps across the four operating regions with drill-down capability to individual container level. All financial metrics must be available in both USD (for group consolidation) and entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), with currency conversion using the period average rate for income statement items and closing rate for balance sheet items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R008	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Minimum Leasing Commitment Tracking and Alerts	The system must track minimum commitment obligations specified in MLAs, including minimum fleet size (number of units by type), minimum on-hire days per period, and minimum revenue guarantees. The system must continuously monitor actual utilization against these commitments and generate alerts when utilization trends suggest a risk of falling below minimum thresholds before the commitment period ends. Shortfall penalties must be automatically calculated based on MLA terms, typically as the per-diem rate multiplied by the deficit unit-days. The system must support commitment netting across entities where MLAs permit pooling of commitments across the group's operations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Forecast reports must project commitment fulfillment based on current fleet levels, planned on-hires and off-hires, and seasonal demand patterns, enabling proactive fleet adjustments to avoid penalty charges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R009	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Damage Claim Management with Lessors	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of damage claims between the operating entities and container lessors, from initial damage detection at off-hire through to final settlement. Each claim must reference the off-hire survey report, IICL damage codes, photographic evidence, repair cost estimates, and the applicable MLA damage liability provisions. The system must distinguish between lessee-responsible damage (charged to the operating entity), normal wear and tear (lessor's responsibility), and pre-existing damage documented in the on-hire survey. A configurable approval workflow must route claims based on value thresholds: auto-approve below USD 200, team leader approval up to USD 1,000, and management approval above USD 1,000. The system must track lessor response times against MLA-stipulated SLAs and escalate overdue claims. Settlement amounts must be posted against the appropriate entity's cost center in local currency with the USD claim amount translated at the settlement date exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R010	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Container Interchange Receipt (EIR) Management	The system must generate and manage Equipment Interchange Receipts (EIRs) for every container custody transfer event between the operating entity and external parties including lessors, depots, terminals, trucking companies, and shipping lines. Each EIR must record the container number, ISO size/type code, condition at interchange (referencing the survey module), seal numbers, hazardous cargo declarations if applicable, and the transferring and receiving party details. EIRs must be sequentially numbered per entity with a unique reference format incorporating the entity code, port code, and date. Digital signatures must be supported for both parties to the interchange, with the option for wet-ink signature capture on mobile devices for parties without digital signature capability. The system must retain EIR records for the statutory period per jurisdiction: ten years in Qatar, five years in UAE, ten years in KSA, and eight years in India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R011	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Multi-Entity Lease Portfolio Consolidation	The system must provide consolidated views of the leased container portfolio across all four operating entities, enabling group-level fleet management decisions while maintaining entity-level legal and accounting separation. Consolidation must aggregate on-hire unit counts, total per-diem costs, utilization rates, and outstanding liabilities by lessor across entities. Where a single MLA covers multiple entities, the system must track allocations to each entity based on the pickup location and the entity's operational control of the container. Intercompany lease transfers must be supported when a container picked up by one entity is deployed by another, with the system generating intercompany charge records at arm's-length rates compliant with transfer pricing regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Consolidated reports must present data in USD for group management while entity-level reports use the respective local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R012	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Lease Renewal and Negotiation Decision Support	The system must provide AI-driven decision support for lease renewal negotiations by analyzing historical utilization data, market rate trends, forecasted demand by container type, current owned fleet capacity, and competitor lease rate benchmarks. The AI engine must generate recommended negotiation positions for each MLA renewal, including suggested rate targets by container type and term, optimal fleet size commitments based on demand forecasts, preferred pickup and drop-off location networks, and recommended lease term durations. Scenario modeling must allow the leasing team to compare outcomes of different negotiation scenarios including rate reductions, term extensions, fleet size adjustments, and geographic flexibility changes. All scenario outputs must display financial impacts in both USD and entity local currencies, with NPV calculations using entity-specific discount rates. The system must maintain a negotiation history log for each lessor relationship.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R013	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Depot Network Management for Lease Operations	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of container depots approved under each MLA for pickup and return operations across the Middle East and Indian subcontinent. Each depot record must include the depot operator name, physical location with GPS coordinates, supported container types and capacities, operating hours, contact information, and the MLAs under which the depot is authorized. The system must validate all on-hire and off-hire transactions against the approved depot list for the applicable MLA, preventing transactions at unauthorized locations. Depot performance metrics must be tracked including average pickup processing time, average return processing time, survey quality scores, and damage claim dispute rates. When new depots are added to or removed from MLA-approved lists, the system must update the validation rules and notify operations teams at affected entities. Integration with depot operators' systems must support electronic pickup and return confirmations where available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R014	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Lease Insurance and Indemnity Compliance Tracking	The system must track insurance and indemnity obligations specified in each MLA, verifying that the operating entity maintains adequate insurance coverage for leased containers as contractually required. Coverage requirements typically include marine cargo insurance, container physical damage insurance, and third-party liability insurance with minimum coverage amounts specified per MLA. The system must monitor insurance policy expiry dates using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, generating renewal alerts to ensure continuous coverage. Certificate of Insurance (COI) documents must be stored digitally and linked to the respective MLAs, with automated distribution to lessors upon renewal. The system must flag any gaps in insurance coverage and block new on-hire transactions under affected MLAs until coverage is reinstated. For self-insured retention portions, the system must calculate and maintain adequate provisions in each entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R015	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Arabic and Bilingual Lease Documentation	The system must generate all lease-related documents including EIRs, survey reports, damage claims, and financial statements in bilingual English/Arabic format for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities, and English/Hindi format for India entity upon request. Arabic document sections must render in proper RTL layout with numbers displayed in Eastern Arabic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) where configured by the entity, or Western Arabic numerals based on user preference. Lease agreement summaries and key terms must be translatable with legal terminology verified against each jurisdiction's commercial law vocabulary. Financial amounts must display with the appropriate currency symbol and formatting conventions: QAR and SAR with two decimal places, AED with two decimal places, INR with two decimal places and the Indian numbering system (lakhs and crores) where configured. Date fields must support display in Gregorian, Hijri, or dual format as configured per entity, ensuring MLA milestones are tracked accurately in both calendar systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R016	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Leased Container Repositioning Cost Allocation	The system must track and allocate repositioning costs for leased containers when they must be moved empty between ports to meet operational demand or to reach an approved off-hire location. Repositioning costs must include vessel slot costs, port handling charges, inland transportation fees, and any applicable customs or transit fees, all captured in the currency of the cost origin and converted to both USD and entity local currency for reporting. The system must determine whether repositioning costs are chargeable under the MLA terms—some lessors share repositioning costs for strategic relocations—and generate the appropriate billing or cost-sharing records. The AI engine must optimize repositioning decisions by comparing the cost of repositioning a leased container to an off-hire point versus continuing to pay per-diem for an underutilized unit, factoring in forecasted demand at both origin and destination locations across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-002-R017	FEAT-010-3-002	MOD-010	Lease Portfolio Risk Assessment and Concentration Analysis	The system must provide risk assessment tools for the leased container portfolio, analyzing concentration risk by lessor, geographic exposure, currency exposure, and container age profile. The AI engine must calculate a portfolio risk score considering factors such as dependence on a single lessor exceeding configurable thresholds (default 40% of total leased fleet), average container age approaching end-of-economic-life (typically 12-15 years for dry containers), currency mismatch between lease obligations (USD) and revenue currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and geopolitical risk factors in operating regions. The system must generate quarterly portfolio review reports for management with recommendations to diversify lessor relationships, rebalance container age profiles, or hedge currency exposures. Stress testing scenarios must model the financial impact of lessor rate increases, currency devaluation, and sudden demand drops on the lease portfolio across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R001	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Demand Forecasting Engine with ML Models	The system must implement a machine learning-based demand forecasting engine that predicts container requirements by type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK), location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and inland depots), and time horizon (daily for 14 days, weekly for 12 weeks, monthly for 12 months). The forecasting models must incorporate historical booking data, seasonal patterns (including Ramadan, Diwali, Chinese New Year trade impacts), economic indicators for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, commodity price trends affecting containerized cargo volumes, and vessel schedule data. The system must support multiple forecasting algorithms including ARIMA, Prophet, LSTM neural networks, and gradient boosting, with automatic model selection based on forecast accuracy metrics (MAPE, RMSE, MAE). Model retraining must occur at minimum weekly using the latest available data, with the AI monitoring for concept drift and alerting data scientists when model performance degrades below configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R002	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Real-Time Container Inventory Visibility Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing container inventory status across all operating locations with no more than 15-minute data latency. The dashboard must display total available units by type, units currently in use (loaded on vessels, at customer premises, in transit), units undergoing repair, units awaiting survey, units on customs hold, and units committed to future bookings. Inventory must be segmented by ownership category: owned, leased (by lessor), and slot-chartered. Geographic visualization must use an interactive map centered on the Middle East and Indian subcontinent showing container concentrations at each port and depot with color-coded indicators for surplus (green), balanced (amber), and deficit (red) positions. Filters must support entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), container type, ownership category, condition grade, and age band. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all labels, tooltips, and data annotations properly localized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R003	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Booking-to-Availability Matching Algorithm	The system must implement a real-time matching algorithm that validates each new booking request against current and projected container availability at the requested pickup location. When a booking is created, the algorithm must check current physical stock, containers expected to become available through inbound vessel discharges, anticipated off-hire returns, containers completing repair, and containers in transit from other locations with arrival before the requested pickup date. If a direct match is unavailable, the system must suggest alternatives including different pickup dates, nearby locations with availability, substitute container types (e.g., 40HC instead of 40GP if dimensions permit), or leased container on-hire options. The matching algorithm must consider entity-specific business rules, for example a Qatar entity booking should preferentially match against Qatar-owned containers before considering intercompany container sharing from UAE or KSA entities. Response time for the matching algorithm must not exceed three seconds for single booking validation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R004	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Seasonal and Event-Based Demand Adjustment	The system must maintain a configurable calendar of events that impact container demand patterns across the four operating regions, including Ramadan (approximately 30 days of reduced activity in GCC, varies annually by Hijri calendar), Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha holidays, Diwali and Holi festivals in India, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, Indian Republic and Independence Days, and major trade events like Expo exhibitions. The AI engine must automatically adjust demand forecasts based on the proximity and expected impact of these events, using historical data from previous occurrences. The system must handle the Islamic calendar's annual shift relative to the Gregorian calendar, recalculating Ramadan and Eid impacts for each year using Hijri date computations. Users must be able to add ad-hoc events such as port closures due to weather (shamal dust storms in the Gulf, monsoon at JNPT), geopolitical disruptions, or major infrastructure projects that temporarily affect container demand or port capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R005	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Multi-Location Inventory Balancing Optimization	The system must run a daily optimization model that recommends container repositioning actions to balance inventory across all operating locations. The optimization must minimize total system cost including per-diem costs for leased containers sitting idle, opportunity cost of unfulfilled bookings due to equipment shortage, repositioning transportation costs (vessel slot, trucking, rail), and handling costs at origin and destination ports. Constraints must include vessel capacity for empty repositioning, inland transport availability, depot storage capacity, and minimum safety stock levels per container type per location. The optimization model must produce a prioritized action list specifying which containers to reposition, from which location to which destination, by which transport mode, and by when. Actions must be convertible to movement orders with a single approval click. The system must recalculate recommendations when significant changes occur such as a vessel schedule disruption or a large booking cancellation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R010	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Schedule Calendar and Gantt Chart Visualization	The system must provide an interactive Gantt chart visualization showing vessel schedules across a configurable time horizon of up to twelve months. The Gantt chart must display port calls as colored blocks with duration proportional to planned berth time, and sea legs as connecting lines with distance and speed annotations. Users must be able to filter by service loop, vessel, port, and entity. The chart must support drag-and-drop schedule adjustment for authorized planners, with real-time validation of conflicts such as berth overlaps and minimum turnaround violations. The visualization must be exportable to PDF format for offline review. In RTL mode, the timeline axis must read right-to-left.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R006	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Container Lifecycle Stage Forecasting	The system must forecast the transition of containers through lifecycle stages including available, allocated to booking, gate-out loaded, in transit on vessel, discharged at destination, at customer premises (dwell time), gate-in empty return, awaiting survey, under repair, and available again. For each container type and location, the system must predict the average duration of each lifecycle stage using historical data, enabling accurate projection of when containers currently in use will become available for reuse. The AI engine must detect anomalies in lifecycle stage durations, such as excessive customer dwell times at JNPT indicating potential detention charge opportunities or operational bottlenecks. Forecasts must account for entity-specific operational patterns—for example, faster turnaround times at Jebel Ali's highly automated terminal versus longer dwell times at less automated facilities. The lifecycle forecasting data must feed directly into the availability calculation engine to improve the accuracy of future availability projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R007	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Safety Stock Calculation by Location and Container Type	The system must calculate and maintain dynamic safety stock levels for each container type at each operating location, adjusting based on demand variability, supply lead time for repositioning, and service level targets configured per entity. Safety stock algorithms must use statistical methods incorporating demand standard deviation, average replenishment lead time from the nearest surplus location, and the desired service level (e.g., 95% fill rate for premium trade lanes, 85% for secondary routes). The system must distinguish between strategic stock (long-term positioning for expected seasonal demand) and operational buffer stock (short-term cushion against daily variability). When actual inventory at any location falls below the calculated safety stock threshold, the system must automatically generate replenishment recommendations and alert the fleet planning team. Safety stock parameters must be reviewable and overridable by authorized users per entity, with an audit trail of all manual adjustments and the business justification provided.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R008	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Vessel Schedule Integration for Supply Forecasting	The system must integrate with vessel schedule data to forecast container supply from inbound vessels at each operating port. For every vessel scheduled to call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must import the discharge list showing containers expected to be returned empty or released after import cargo delivery. The supply forecasting engine must apply probability adjustments based on vessel schedule reliability—calculating on-time arrival probability from historical vessel performance data for each service string. When vessel delays are detected through AIS data or carrier notifications, the system must automatically adjust the expected supply dates and recalculate availability projections for affected locations. The system must also track empty containers being repositioned on feeder vessels between the four operating ports, with real-time position updates enabling accurate ETA-based supply planning. Integration must support data feeds from major carriers operating in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent trades.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R009	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Customer Demand Pattern Profiling	The system must build and maintain AI-driven demand profiles for major customers across all four operating entities, capturing their typical booking patterns by container type, preferred pickup locations, seasonal volume variations, average booking lead time, and historical reliability (booking-to-actual conversion rate). The AI engine must use these profiles to improve demand forecasting accuracy by weighting confirmed bookings based on each customer's historical conversion probability—for example, if a major Qatar petrochemical exporter consistently converts 95% of bookings to actual shipments, their bookings should be weighted at 0.95 in availability planning. Conversely, customers with high cancellation rates must have their bookings discounted in availability calculations. The system must detect changes in customer behavior patterns such as a sudden increase in booking volumes from an Indian textile exporter signaling potential new contract wins. Customer profiles must respect data segregation between entities, ensuring one entity cannot view another entity's customer data without explicit intercompany data sharing authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R010	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Availability Alert and Notification System	The system must implement a multi-channel alert system that notifies relevant stakeholders when container availability at any location reaches critical levels. Alert thresholds must be configurable per container type and location, with at minimum three severity levels: warning (inventory below 120% of safety stock), critical (inventory below safety stock), and emergency (zero available units of a requested type). Notifications must be delivered through in-application alerts, email, SMS, and integration with messaging platforms used by the operations teams. Alert messages must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) and must include the affected location, container type, current inventory count, projected demand for the next seven days, and recommended actions. Escalation rules must ensure that critical and emergency alerts reach progressively senior management if not acknowledged within configurable time windows. The system must suppress repetitive alerts for known situations where corrective action is already in progress, preventing alert fatigue among operations staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R011	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	What-If Scenario Planning for Fleet Capacity	The system must provide a scenario planning module allowing fleet planners to model the impact of hypothetical changes on container availability across the network. Scenarios must support modifications including adding or removing leased containers, purchasing new containers, disposing of aged containers, opening or closing depot locations, changing vessel service frequencies, gaining or losing major customer accounts, and varying demand growth assumptions per trade lane. Each scenario must produce a comprehensive impact analysis showing projected availability by location and type, cost implications in both USD and entity local currencies, service level impacts, and capital expenditure requirements. The system must allow side-by-side comparison of up to five scenarios simultaneously with variance highlighting. Approved scenarios must be convertible to action plans with tasks, timelines, and responsible parties. The AI engine must suggest optimal fleet composition scenarios based on demand forecasts and financial optimization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-002-2-001-R004	FEAT-002-2-001	MOD-002	Circuit breaker SHALL open after 5 consecutive failures or 50% error rate over 30-second window to any downstream service, returning cached stale vessel schedule data or a graceful degradation response	Circuit breaker SHALL open after 5 consecutive failures or 50% error rate over 30-second window to any downstream service, returning cached stale vessel schedule data or a graceful degradation response	must_have			"Circuit opens after 5 consecutive 5xx responses from vessel-schedule-service; subsequent requests receive last-known-good cached schedule data with X-Cache-Stale: true header; circuit half-opens after 30 seconds to test recovery"	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R012	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Intercompany Container Sharing Optimization	The system must optimize the sharing of container inventory across the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to maximize group-wide utilization while maintaining entity-level accountability. When one entity has surplus containers of a type that another entity needs, the system must recommend intercompany transfers with calculated transfer pricing based on arm's-length principles compliant with OECD transfer pricing guidelines and local regulations in each jurisdiction. The optimization must consider the total cost of the intercompany transfer including repositioning cost, handling charges, customs transit fees where applicable, and the per-diem savings from off-hiring leased units at the surplus entity versus the cost of on-hiring new leased units at the deficit entity. Intercompany container usage must generate automated intercompany invoices in the appropriate currency pairs (e.g., QAR-to-AED for Qatar-to-UAE transfers) at agreed transfer pricing rates. The system must maintain a clear audit trail of all intercompany container movements for tax authority review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R013	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Container Age and Condition-Based Availability Scoring	The system must assign availability scores to containers based on a composite of age, condition grade, remaining economic life, and suitability for specific cargo types, rather than treating all available containers of the same type as interchangeable. A newer, Grade A container must score higher for premium bookings, while an older Grade C container may still be suitable for certain bulk cargo trade lanes. The scoring algorithm must consider customer preferences—some customers specify maximum container age requirements in their contracts, and the system must filter availability accordingly. For refrigerated containers, the scoring must additionally consider machinery age, last PTI results, and repair history, as these significantly affect reliability on longer voyage routes. The availability score must feed into the booking-to-availability matching algorithm, ensuring optimal allocation that balances customer service quality with fleet utilization economics. The scoring weights must be configurable per entity to reflect local market conditions and customer expectations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R014	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Port Congestion and Capacity Constraint Modeling	The system must incorporate port congestion data and terminal capacity constraints into availability planning calculations. For each operating port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), the system must maintain real-time metrics on current yard occupancy, gate throughput rates, vessel berth availability, and rail/truck access constraints. When congestion levels at a port exceed configurable thresholds, the AI engine must adjust availability forecasts to account for extended container dwell times, delayed vessel operations, and potential gate restrictions. The system must integrate with port community systems to receive congestion advisories and planned maintenance outages that could affect container accessibility. During monsoon season at JNPT (typically June-September) and shamal dust storms in the Gulf (June-August), the system must apply historical congestion impact factors to availability calculations. The congestion modeling must feed into repositioning recommendations, potentially diverting empty containers to less congested alternatives among the four operating ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R015	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Availability Planning Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive availability planning reports for different stakeholder audiences: executive summary for senior management, detailed operational reports for fleet planners, and customer-specific availability confirmations for the sales team. Reports must be schedulable for daily, weekly, and monthly distribution with configurable recipient lists per entity. The executive summary must include a network-wide availability overview, key risk areas, cost projections, and AI-recommended actions, limited to two pages with visual charts. Operational reports must detail container positions, movements in transit, expected arrivals, repair pipeline, and day-by-day availability projections for each location and container type. All reports must support bilingual generation in English and Arabic for GCC entities, with proper RTL layout for Arabic versions. Financial data in reports must display in the appropriate entity currency with configurable options to include USD equivalents. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats, with the Excel version including interactive pivot tables for user analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R016	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Machine Learning Model Explainability and Audit	The system must provide explainability features for all AI-driven availability planning recommendations, enabling fleet planners to understand the rationale behind each suggestion. For demand forecasts, the system must display the top contributing factors (e.g., seasonal pattern, customer booking trend, economic indicator) with their relative influence weights. For repositioning recommendations, the system must show the cost-benefit analysis components and the constraints that shaped the optimal solution. An audit log must record every AI recommendation generated, whether it was accepted, modified, or rejected by the human operator, and the actual outcome achieved. This feedback loop must be used to continuously improve model accuracy. The explainability interface must present information in both English and Arabic, using plain language rather than technical ML terminology, to ensure accessibility for all users including Arabic-speaking fleet coordinators in Qatar, KSA, and UAE operations. Regulatory compliance documentation must be available demonstrating that AI models do not incorporate prohibited discriminatory factors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R017	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Integration with Booking and Commercial Modules	The system must maintain bidirectional integration with the booking and commercial modules to ensure availability data is consistent across all customer-facing functions. When a booking is confirmed, the system must immediately reserve the allocated container(s) reducing the available inventory count. When a booking is cancelled or modified, the reservation must be released or adjusted in real time. The commercial module must be able to query projected availability for specific future dates to support quotation generation—if a customer requests a quote for 200 x 40HC containers at Jebel Ali in three months, the system must return a confidence-scored availability response. Allocation priority rules must be configurable per entity, supporting scenarios such as VIP customer priority, contract customer guaranteed allocation, and spot market residual allocation. The system must support allocation holds where a sales team reserves tentative capacity for a pipeline opportunity, with automatic expiry if not converted to a confirmed booking within a configurable period. All API integrations must respond within two seconds to maintain user experience quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-003-R018	FEAT-010-3-003	MOD-010	Historical Accuracy Tracking and Forecast Calibration	The system must continuously track the accuracy of its availability forecasts and demand predictions, comparing predicted values against actual outcomes at each forecast horizon (daily, weekly, monthly). Accuracy metrics must include MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error), bias (systematic over or under-prediction), and hit rate (percentage of forecasts within acceptable tolerance). The system must maintain accuracy leaderboards segmented by location, container type, and forecast horizon, identifying where predictions are strongest and where improvement is needed. When forecast accuracy for a specific segment falls below configurable thresholds (default MAPE below 25% for monthly forecasts), the system must alert data scientists and trigger automated model retraining with the latest data. Calibration reports must be generated monthly for management review, showing accuracy trends and the business impact of forecast errors measured in USD and entity local currencies. The system must also perform backtesting of new model versions against historical data before deploying them to production, requiring that new models demonstrate improved accuracy on out-of-sample test periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R001	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Network Flow Optimization Model for Empty Movements	The system must implement a network flow optimization model that determines the least-cost routing of empty containers from surplus locations to deficit locations across the operating network. The model must formulate the empty repositioning problem as a minimum-cost network flow problem with nodes representing ports (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLF, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHA), inland depots, and transshipment hubs, and arcs representing available transport links (vessel slots, truck routes, rail connections). Arc costs must incorporate vessel slot charges, port handling fees, inland transportation rates, customs transit fees, and estimated carbon costs. The model must respect capacity constraints on each arc (vessel empty slot allocation, truck availability, depot storage limits) and satisfy demand constraints at each deficit node. The optimization must run at minimum daily with the ability to trigger ad-hoc runs when significant disruptions occur, producing actionable movement plans with specific container assignments to routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R002	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Multi-Modal Transport Cost Calculation Engine	The system must calculate total repositioning costs across all available transport modes including deep-sea vessel, feeder vessel, coastal shipping, rail, and road truck. Cost calculations must incorporate mode-specific rate structures: vessel costs based on slot rates per TEU/FEU varying by trade lane and carrier contract; truck costs based on distance-based rates or flat rates per route in local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India); rail costs based on Indian Railways container train tariffs for JNPT hinterland movements. The engine must account for port-specific handling charges at origin and destination, including terminal handling charges, lift-on/lift-off fees, gate processing fees, and any applicable storage charges if the container must dwell at a transshipment point. Customs transit fees and documentation costs for cross-border movements (e.g., UAE to KSA overland via Ghuweifat border crossing) must be included. All costs must be calculated in the origin currency and convertible to USD for network-wide comparison and optimization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R003	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Trade Imbalance Analysis and Prediction	The system must analyze and predict trade imbalances—the difference between import and export container volumes—at each operating location to forecast empty container surplus and deficit positions. The AI engine must model trade imbalance patterns using historical import/export ratios by trade lane, commodity flow data, economic indicators for each operating country (Qatar hydrocarbon exports, UAE re-export hub dynamics, KSA Vision 2030 industrialization impact, India manufacturing export trends), and seasonal factors. The system must classify locations as structurally surplus (more imports than exports, typical for Gulf consumer goods imports), structurally deficit (more exports than imports), or balanced. Predictions must be generated for rolling 4-week, 12-week, and 52-week horizons with confidence intervals. The trade imbalance data must feed directly into the repositioning optimizer to anticipate future needs rather than reacting to current shortages. The system must also identify emerging imbalance trends such as increasing container surplus at JNPT due to declining commodity exports or growing deficit at Jebel Ali from e-commerce import growth.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R004	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Vessel Empty Slot Booking and Allocation	The system must manage the booking and allocation of empty container slots on vessels calling at the four operating ports. For company-operated or chartered vessels, the system must reserve optimal empty slot capacity based on the repositioning plan, balancing revenue cargo priority against repositioning urgency. For third-party carrier vessels, the system must interface with carrier booking systems to request empty slot allocations at negotiated rates. The slot allocation algorithm must prioritize repositioning movements by urgency: emergency (bookings at risk within 7 days), planned (repositioning to meet forecast demand within 30 days), and opportunistic (low-cost backfill on vessels with excess capacity). The system must track slot utilization rates and costs per carrier, enabling negotiation of better empty slot rates with carriers that provide consistent capacity on key repositioning lanes such as Jebel Ali to JNPT or JNPT to Hamad Port. Slot booking confirmations must be automatically linked to the specific containers assigned for repositioning, updating the network inventory plan.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R005	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Inland Transportation Optimization for Empty Moves	The system must optimize inland transportation of empty containers between ports and inland depots, customer facilities, and intermodal terminals. For India operations centered on JNPT, the system must plan truck and rail movements to inland container depots (ICDs) at locations such as Dadri, Tughlakabad, Nagpur, and Ahmedabad, utilizing Indian Railways' container train services where cost-effective. For GCC operations, the system must plan truck movements between ports and inland yards, considering highway toll costs (Salik in Dubai, Darb in Abu Dhabi), fuel surcharges, driver hour regulations, and restricted vehicle movement times in urban areas. The optimizer must consolidate empty container movements to achieve full truckload or full train efficiency rather than moving single containers. Route optimization must consider real-time traffic conditions, road construction advisories, and border crossing wait times for cross-emirate or cross-country inland movements. All inland transport costs must be calculated in the local currency of the origin country with multi-currency reporting capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R006	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Carbon Emission Tracking for Empty Repositioning	The system must calculate and track carbon emissions generated by empty container repositioning movements across all transport modes, supporting environmental sustainability reporting for each entity. Emission calculations must follow the IMO guidelines for vessel movements, the GLEC Framework for inland transport, and the Clean Cargo Working Group methodology for end-to-end supply chain emissions. For each repositioning movement, the system must compute CO2e emissions based on the transport mode, distance, fuel type, and load factor. The AI optimizer must incorporate carbon costs as a configurable factor in repositioning decisions—for example, penalizing high-emission routing options to encourage selection of lower-emission alternatives such as rail over truck for JNPT hinterland movements. The system must generate quarterly emission reports per entity for ESG reporting, with data exportable in formats compatible with the GHG Protocol and the Carbon Disclosure Project (CDP). Emission reduction targets must be configurable per entity aligned with each country's nationally determined contributions under the Paris Agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R007	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Street Turn and Triangulation Matching Engine	The system must implement a street turn and triangulation matching engine that identifies opportunities to redirect empty containers directly from an import delivery location to a nearby export pickup location, eliminating the need to return the container to the port empty and then dispatch it again. The matching algorithm must consider the geographic proximity of the import consignee and export shipper (within configurable radius, default 50 km), container type compatibility, timing alignment (import delivery and export pickup within configurable window, default 48 hours), and container condition suitability. For each potential street turn match, the system must calculate the cost saving compared to the standard return-to-port-and-redispatch cycle, including avoided port handling charges, reduced trucking distance, and saved time. The system must generate match proposals for operations teams with one-click approval, automatically updating the transport plan and notifying all affected parties (trucking company, import consignee, export shipper). Street turn success rates must be tracked as a KPI per entity with targets to increase triangulation rates quarter-over-quarter.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R008	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Container Substitution Logic for Repositioning Efficiency	The system must evaluate container substitution opportunities during repositioning planning to reduce total empty movement costs. When a deficit location requires a specific container type, the AI engine must assess whether a different type available locally could satisfy the booking requirement—for example, using a 40HC container in place of a 40GP when the cargo dimensions permit and the customer agrees. The substitution logic must check dimensional compatibility, weight capacity compatibility, cargo type suitability (e.g., a standard container cannot substitute for a reefer), and customer contract stipulations. When substitution can avoid a repositioning movement or replace a long-distance move with a shorter one, the system must calculate the net savings and present the option to the fleet planner with customer impact analysis. The system must maintain substitution approval rates and customer acceptance data to refine future substitution recommendations. Substitution must never compromise cargo safety or regulatory compliance, and the system must block substitutions that violate IMDG Code, food safety, or customer-specified requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R009	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Repositioning Budget Management and Cost Control	The system must manage empty repositioning budgets per entity with annual, quarterly, and monthly allocation tracking. Budget amounts must be set in entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalents for group consolidation. Actual repositioning costs must be tracked against budget in real time, with variance analysis identifying overspend drivers by route, transport mode, and container type. When an entity's repositioning spend approaches budget thresholds (configurable at 80%, 90%, and 100% of period budget), the system must generate escalating alerts to fleet management. The AI optimizer must incorporate budget constraints into its recommendations—if an entity is approaching its budget limit, the optimizer must deprioritize low-urgency repositioning and focus on movements required to fulfill confirmed bookings. Budget reallocation between entities must be supported through an approval workflow when one entity has surplus budget and another faces a shortfall. Month-end and quarter-end cost accruals must be automatically calculated for unfinished repositioning movements, ensuring accurate financial reporting per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R010	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Real-Time Empty Container Tracking and ETA Updates	The system must provide real-time tracking of all empty containers in transit during repositioning, whether moving by vessel, truck, or rail. For vessel movements, tracking must leverage AIS data and carrier milestone updates to show current vessel position, estimated port arrival time, and expected container availability date at the destination. For truck movements within GCC countries and India, GPS tracking integration with contracted trucking companies must provide real-time vehicle position, estimated delivery time, and delay alerts. For rail movements in India, integration with Indian Railways' FOIS (Freight Operations Information System) must track container train positions and estimated arrival at destination ICDs. The system must automatically update the availability planning module when ETAs change, recalculating downstream availability projections. A map-based visualization must show all empty containers in transit with color-coded status indicators (on schedule in green, delayed in amber, at risk in red), filterable by entity, container type, and destination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R011	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Carrier and Vendor Rate Negotiation Support	The system must provide data-driven insights to support negotiation of repositioning rates with vessel carriers, trucking companies, and rail operators. The system must analyze historical repositioning volumes and costs by route, carrier, and time period, identifying opportunities for volume-based discounts or long-term rate agreements. Benchmarking reports must compare rates across carriers for the same routes, highlighting cost outliers and potential savings. The AI engine must forecast future repositioning volumes by route based on trade imbalance predictions, enabling procurement teams to negotiate rates based on projected volumes rather than historical data alone. Rate comparison must normalize for service quality factors including transit time reliability, equipment availability, and damage rates. All rate analysis must be presented in the relevant local currency with USD equivalents for cross-regional comparison. The system must support rate validity period tracking with automated alerts before expiry, and electronic rate tender management with structured bid comparison scoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R012	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Weather and Disruption Impact Modeling	The system must model the impact of weather events and operational disruptions on empty repositioning plans, adjusting recommendations accordingly. The system must integrate weather data feeds relevant to the four operating regions including Gulf shamal wind events (typically May-July), Indian monsoon season (June-September affecting JNPT), tropical cyclones in the Arabian Sea, and fog events affecting port operations during Gulf winter months (December-February). When severe weather is forecast, the system must simulate the impact on vessel schedules, port operations, and inland transport, adjusting repositioning timelines and suggesting pre-positioning of containers ahead of disruptions. For non-weather disruptions such as port labor actions, customs system outages, or geopolitical events affecting trade routes, the system must allow manual disruption event creation with configurable impact parameters (duration, affected routes, capacity reduction percentage). The optimizer must recalculate the repositioning plan immediately when disruptions are entered, presenting revised recommendations that minimize the cost impact while ensuring critical supply requirements are met.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R013	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Empty Repositioning Performance KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive KPI dashboard for monitoring empty repositioning performance across the network. Key metrics must include total repositioning cost as a percentage of revenue, average cost per repositioned TEU by route and mode, repositioning volume in TEU by origin-destination pair, average transit time for repositioning movements, street turn success rate, container idle days before repositioning, and forecast accuracy of trade imbalance predictions. KPIs must be displayed with month-over-month and year-over-year trend lines, with drill-down capability from network level to entity level to individual route level. Benchmarking against industry averages for the Middle East and Indian subcontinent trades must be provided where available. The dashboard must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selections for GCC entities. All financial KPIs must be available in both entity local currency and USD. The dashboard must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with proper chart axis mirroring and legend positioning for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R014	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Customs Transit and Documentation for Cross-Border Empties	The system must manage customs transit documentation for empty containers moving across international borders, which is common for repositioning between UAE, KSA, and Qatar by overland transport. The system must generate or interface with customs systems to produce temporary admission documents, transit declarations, and re-export certificates as required by each country's customs authority. For UAE-KSA overland movements, the system must handle Saudi ZATCA transit requirements through the Ghuweifat or Batha border crossings. For UAE-Qatar movements (via Saudi transit or direct sea), the system must manage Qatar Customs temporary import procedures. For India, the system must handle CBIC requirements for empty container movements between JNPT and inland container depots, including GST e-way bill generation for interstate movements. The system must pre-validate all documentation requirements before authorizing a repositioning movement, preventing containers from being dispatched without complete transit paperwork. Duty-free status of empty containers must be declared and tracked through each customs jurisdiction to avoid unexpected duty assessments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R015	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Leased Container Repositioning Cost Allocation	The system must allocate repositioning costs for leased containers according to the terms of the applicable master lease agreement and the entity responsible for the repositioning need. When a leased container is repositioned to meet demand at a specific entity, the repositioning cost must be charged to that entity's cost center. When repositioning is required to return a leased container to an approved off-hire location at lease expiry, the cost must be allocated based on the MLA terms—some lessors share repositioning costs to approved return points while others require the lessee to bear the full cost. For intercompany repositioning of leased containers (e.g., a container leased by the Qatar entity repositioned to serve the UAE entity), the system must generate intercompany charges at arm's-length transfer pricing rates. The system must compare the total cost of repositioning a leased container versus off-hiring it at the current location and on-hiring a replacement at the destination, recommending the most economical option. All cost allocations must be recorded in both USD and entity local currency with proper GL account mapping for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R016	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Collaborative Repositioning with Partner Lines	The system must support collaborative empty repositioning arrangements with partner shipping lines and vessel-sharing agreement (VSA) partners operating in the same trade lanes. The system must identify opportunities where a partner line has surplus containers at a location where the company has a deficit, and vice versa, enabling container exchange or swap agreements that reduce both parties' repositioning costs. Exchange proposals must be generated with full cost-benefit analysis, calculating the savings from avoiding separate repositioning movements for both parties. The system must track container exchange balances with each partner, ensuring reciprocal exchanges remain within agreed tolerance bands per period. Integration must support standard EDI messaging (COPARN, CODECO) for exchanging container movement instructions with partner lines. Exchange rate settlements for any financial imbalances must be processed in USD as the industry standard currency, with entity-level accounting entries in local currency. The system must maintain confidentiality of each party's demand and inventory data, sharing only the minimum information necessary to facilitate matching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R017	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Repositioning Plan Approval Workflow and Execution Tracking	The system must implement a configurable approval workflow for repositioning plans generated by the AI optimizer before execution. Approval authority must be based on the total estimated cost of the repositioning batch: auto-approve below entity-specific thresholds (default USD 5,000), operations manager approval up to USD 25,000, and fleet director approval above USD 25,000. The workflow must present the approver with the AI-generated plan summary including total cost, number of containers, routes, expected completion timeline, and the business justification (unfulfilled bookings at risk, minimum commitment obligations, or strategic pre-positioning). Upon approval, the system must automatically generate execution instructions including vessel booking requests, trucking orders, and depot notifications. Execution tracking must monitor each repositioning movement against the plan, flagging deviations in timing, routing, or cost. Post-execution reconciliation must compare planned versus actual costs, feeding variance data back to the AI model for improved future cost estimation. The complete plan-approve-execute-reconcile cycle must be auditable for financial and operational governance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-010-3-004-R018	FEAT-010-3-004	MOD-010	Offline Repositioning Decision Support for Field Operations	The system must provide offline-capable decision support for field operations staff at ports and depots who need to make repositioning-related decisions when network connectivity is unavailable. The offline module must cache the current repositioning plan, container inventory at the local facility, pending movement orders, approved substitution rules, and key contact information. Field staff must be able to confirm container dispatches, record container arrivals, flag damaged containers as unavailable for repositioning, and request emergency repositioning through the offline interface. All offline transactions must be queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution logic handling scenarios where the central plan has been updated during the offline period. The offline interface must support Arabic and English language switching and display financial data in the appropriate entity currency. The module must function on standard ruggedized tablets used in port environments, operating for at least eight hours on battery power, and must handle the extreme temperature conditions common in Gulf port operations during summer months exceeding 45 degrees Celsius.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R001	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Proforma Vessel Schedule Creation	The system must allow authorized planners to create proforma vessel schedules defining the planned sequence of port calls, estimated time of arrival (ETA), estimated time of departure (ETD), and estimated time of berth (ETB) for each port in the rotation. The proforma must support vessel identification by IMO number, MMSI, vessel name, and flag state. Each schedule entry must capture the port UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INNSA for JNPT), terminal code, and berth assignment. The system must validate that ETAs follow a chronologically consistent sequence respecting minimum sea transit times between consecutive ports based on vessel speed profiles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R003	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Schedule Versioning and Change Tracking	The system must maintain a complete version history of every vessel schedule modification, capturing the previous values, new values, timestamp of change, user who made the change, and the reason code for the modification. Reason codes must include but not be limited to: weather delay, port congestion, mechanical issue, commercial decision, regulatory hold, canal transit delay, and berth unavailability. Each version must be independently retrievable for audit purposes. The system must support comparison views between any two schedule versions, highlighting differences in ETAs, ETDs, and port call sequences. All change records must be retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Financial Centre and DMCC regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R004	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	AI-Driven ETA Prediction and Schedule Optimization	The AI engine must continuously calculate predicted ETAs for all active voyages using real-time AIS position data, historical port turnaround times, weather forecasts, current vessel speed, canal transit queues (Suez Canal), and known port congestion levels at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Predictions must update at minimum every four hours and achieve accuracy within a six-hour window for arrivals within seven days. The system must generate schedule optimization recommendations when delays cascade, suggesting revised port call sequences or speed adjustments that minimize total voyage cost while respecting contractual berth window commitments and fuel consumption targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R005	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Multi-Entity Schedule Visibility and Access Control	The system must enforce entity-level access controls so that users belonging to the Qatar entity can view and manage schedules for Hamad Port operations, UAE entity users can manage Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port schedules, KSA entity users can manage King Abdullah Port and Jeddah Islamic Port schedules, and India entity users can manage JNPT and Mundra schedules. Cross-entity schedule visibility must be configurable by role, allowing regional managers to view schedules across all entities in read-only mode while restricting edit access to the home entity. Shared vessel schedules operating across multiple entities must display a consolidated view with entity-specific annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R006	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Bilingual Schedule Display with Arabic RTL Support	All vessel schedule interfaces must render correctly in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts. Port names, vessel names, and status labels must be displayed in the user's selected language with Arabic translations sourced from the master data translation table. Date fields must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar display simultaneously, with Hijri dates calculated using the Umm al-Qura calendar standard used in Saudi Arabia. Time zones must be displayed using the port's local time (AST for Qatar/KSA, GST for UAE, IST for India) with UTC offset clearly indicated. Schedule Gantt chart visualizations must mirror correctly in RTL mode without breaking the timeline axis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R008	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Vessel Fleet Registry and Particulars Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive vessel registry containing all vessel particulars required for schedule planning, including LOA (length overall), beam, draft (summer/winter), TEU nominal capacity, TEU intake by trade lane, reefer plug count, dangerous goods capacity class, gross tonnage, net tonnage, deadweight tonnage, and crane configuration. Vessel data must be validated against Lloyd's Register or IHS Markit vessel databases upon initial entry. The system must track vessel ownership changes, flag state changes, and class society certifications. Vessel particulars must be versioned so that historical schedule records reference the vessel configuration at the time of the voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R009	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Delay Notification and Stakeholder Communication	When a vessel schedule deviation exceeds a configurable threshold (default: four hours for ETA, two hours for ETD), the system must automatically generate delay notifications to all affected stakeholders including booking customers, terminal operators, customs brokers, and internal operations teams. Notifications must be sent via email, SMS, and in-app push notification based on stakeholder communication preferences. Notification content must include the vessel name, voyage number, affected port, original and revised times, estimated delay duration, and reason code. Notifications must be generated in both English and Arabic based on the recipient's language preference. The AI engine should draft contextual delay explanations that operations staff can review and approve before dispatch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R011	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Berth Window Management and Conflict Detection	The system must track contracted berth windows at each port terminal, capturing the agreed arrival window start and end times, maximum berth occupancy duration, and priority ranking among competing vessels. When a new schedule is created or an existing schedule is modified, the system must automatically check for berth window conflicts at all ports in the rotation. Conflicts must be classified as hard conflicts (physical berth overlap) or soft conflicts (exceeding terminal vessel call capacity within a time window). The AI engine must suggest resolution options for detected conflicts, such as shifting the call by a specified number of hours, substituting an alternate berth, or resequencing port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R013	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Offline Schedule Access and Synchronization	The system must provide offline access to vessel schedule data for field personnel operating at ports with intermittent connectivity, particularly relevant for smaller Indian coastal ports and during Hamad Port operations in areas with limited coverage. Offline mode must cache the current active schedules for all vessels expected to call within the next fourteen days. Schedule updates made offline must be queued locally and synchronized automatically when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recent timestamp. The offline client must display a clear visual indicator when operating in disconnected mode and must show the timestamp of the last successful synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R014	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Service Loop Definition and Route Templates	The system must support the definition of service loops as reusable route templates specifying the standard sequence of port calls, typical transit times between ports, standard berth windows, and frequency (weekly, fortnightly, monthly). Service loops must be versioned to reflect seasonal variations such as monsoon-adjusted routings for India west coast or Ramadan-adjusted schedules for Gulf ports. When creating a new voyage, planners must be able to select a service loop template that pre-populates the schedule with standard values, which can then be adjusted for the specific voyage. Each service loop must be associated with one or more trade lanes and tagged with the operating entities to control visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R015	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Schedule Performance KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time KPI dashboard measuring schedule reliability metrics including on-time arrival percentage, on-time departure percentage, average delay per port, average delay per vessel, average berth productivity (moves per hour), and schedule integrity index (percentage of port calls completed as per proforma). KPIs must be filterable by entity, service loop, vessel, port, and date range. Threshold-based color coding must indicate performance levels: green for within target, amber for marginal deviation, and red for significant deviation. KPI targets must be configurable per entity to reflect different service level agreements. The dashboard must support export to Excel and PDF formats with proper Arabic rendering in exported documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-001-R016	FEAT-011-1-001	MOD-011	Canal and Strait Transit Schedule Integration	The system must account for Suez Canal transit scheduling in voyage plans for vessels operating between Gulf ports and Mediterranean or European destinations. Canal transit slots must be captured with the convoy direction (northbound/southbound), expected canal entry time, and estimated transit duration. The system must integrate with Suez Canal Authority transit booking data when available. For vessels transiting the Strait of Hormuz, the system must factor in TSS (Traffic Separation Scheme) routing requirements. The AI engine must monitor canal congestion reports and adjust ETAs downstream of the canal based on predicted transit delays, with a confidence interval displayed alongside the adjusted ETA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R002	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Call Pre-Arrival Documentation Management	The system must track and manage all pre-arrival documentation required at each port including the IMO FAL forms (FAL 1 through FAL 7), Port Health Declaration, ISPS security notification (96-hour and 24-hour advance), crew list, passenger list, dangerous goods manifest, and customs cargo declaration. Document submission deadlines must be configured per port based on local regulations: 48 hours for QAHMD, 24 hours for AEJEA, 72 hours for INNSA. The system must generate automated reminders when submission deadlines approach, escalating to the port operations manager if documents remain unsubmitted within two hours of the deadline. The AI engine should pre-populate forms using master data and previous port call records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R003	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Cost Estimation and Comparison	The system must calculate estimated port costs for each port call in the rotation, covering port dues, berth hire, pilotage (inbound and outbound), towage, mooring and unmooring, light dues, anchorage dues, garbage disposal, fresh water supply, and agency fees. Cost calculations must use port-specific tariff tables maintained in the system and denominated in the local currency: QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for Saudi ports, and INR for JNPT and Mundra. The system must convert all costs to USD for consolidated voyage cost reporting using the daily exchange rate feed. Cost estimates must be compared against actual invoiced amounts upon voyage completion, with variance analysis highlighting discrepancies exceeding five percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R004	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Terminal Operator Coordination Interface	The system must provide a structured communication interface with terminal operators at each port, supporting the exchange of bay plans, stowage instructions, container discharge and load lists, and reefer monitoring requirements. Communication records must be timestamped and linked to the specific port call. The system must support electronic data interchange via COPARN (container gate-in/gate-out order), BAPLIE (bayplan/stowage plan), and MOVINS (stowage instruction) message types in UN/EDIFACT format. For terminals that do not support EDI, the system must generate standardized PDF documents for manual exchange. All terminal communications must be logged as part of the voyage audit trail with entity-level access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R005	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Berth Planning and Resource Allocation	The system must support berth planning at each port call by capturing the assigned berth number, available quay crane count, crane working hours, gang deployment plan, and estimated moves per hour productivity rate. Berth changes must be tracked with reason codes and timestamps. The system must validate that the assigned berth can accommodate the vessel's LOA, beam, and maximum draft based on berth specifications stored in the port master data. For tidal ports like JNPT where draft restrictions apply at certain tidal conditions, the system must cross-reference planned arrival times with tidal prediction data to ensure safe berth access. AI must recommend optimal berth assignments based on vessel size, cargo mix, and historical berth productivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R006	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Call Status Lifecycle Tracking	The system must track each port call through a defined lifecycle of statuses: Planned, Pre-Arrival Submitted, Cleared for Entry, At Anchorage, Pilot on Board, Alongside, Operations Commenced, Operations Completed, Sailed, and Cancelled. Status transitions must be validated to follow the correct sequence, with certain transitions requiring confirmatory data (e.g., transition to Alongside requires actual berth time entry, transition to Operations Commenced requires confirmation from the terminal system). Each status change must record the timestamp, source (manual, AIS, terminal system, port authority), and responsible user. Automated status updates must be triggered by incoming AIS position data and terminal operating system events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R007	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Rotation Optimization by AI	The AI engine must analyze proposed port rotations and suggest optimizations that minimize total voyage distance, reduce port costs, improve berth window utilization, and ensure compliance with contractual transit time commitments. Optimization factors must include bunker fuel costs at current market rates, canal transit fees, port tariff differentials between alternative ports (e.g., Khalifa Port versus Jebel Ali), cargo volume at each port, and weather routing recommendations. The AI must present optimization recommendations as ranked alternatives with quantified savings in USD, additional transit days, and impact on booking commitments. Planners must be able to accept, modify, or reject each recommendation with a recorded rationale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R008	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Customs and Regulatory Clearance Tracking	The system must track customs clearance status at each port call for all cargo on board, distinguishing between import clearance, export clearance, and transshipment clearance. For Indian ports operating under ICEGATE, the system must track Import General Manifest (IGM) and Export General Manifest (EGM) submission and acknowledgment statuses. For GCC ports, the system must track compliance with GCC Customs Union requirements and free zone regulations applicable at JAFZA (Jebel Ali), QFZ (Qatar Free Zones), and KAEC (King Abdullah Economic City). Clearance holds must be flagged with severity levels and must block vessel departure approval if critical holds are unresolved. The system must maintain a per-port checklist of mandatory clearances that must all show green status before the port call is marked as completed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R009	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Call Cost Allocation to Entities	Port call costs must be allocated to the appropriate legal entity based on configurable allocation rules. When a port call serves multiple entities (e.g., a vessel at Jebel Ali discharging cargo for both UAE and Qatar entities), costs must be apportioned based on the allocation method selected: proportional to TEU volume, proportional to revenue, equal split, or custom manual allocation. Each cost allocation must generate the corresponding accounting entries in the respective entity's ledger denominated in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). Inter-entity cost transfers must generate intercompany journal entries that are reconciled through the intercompany settlement module. Allocation records must be auditable and reversible within the current accounting period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R010	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Holiday and Working Hours Calendar	The system must maintain a port-specific calendar capturing working hours, shift patterns, overtime availability, and public holidays for each port and terminal. The calendar must account for Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Mawlid) which vary by Hijri calendar and differ between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Friday must be configured as a non-working or reduced-hours day for Gulf ports while Sunday must be configured similarly for Indian ports. Ramadan working hour adjustments must be pre-configured annually for Gulf port operations. The system must use the port calendar when calculating expected berth duration, cargo operation windows, and overtime cost estimates. Calendar entries must support both Gregorian and Hijri date references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R011	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Dangerous Goods Port Call Restrictions	The system must enforce dangerous goods handling restrictions specific to each port and terminal. When a port call involves IMO-classified dangerous goods (Classes 1 through 9), the system must validate that the destination terminal is licensed to handle the specific DG class and that the planned berth supports DG operations. Segregation requirements per IMDG Code must be validated against the vessel stowage plan for the port call. Ports with specific DG restrictions (e.g., certain Hamad Port terminals restricting Class 1 explosives and Class 7 radioactive materials) must have these restrictions encoded in the port master data. The system must block port call confirmation if DG validation fails and must notify the DG compliance officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R012	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Call Deviation and Omission Management	The system must support port call deviations including unscheduled port additions (inducement calls), port omissions, and port substitutions. Each deviation must require an approval workflow with justification, commercial impact assessment, and affected booking notification. When a port is omitted from the rotation, the system must automatically identify all bookings with cargo destined for or originating from the omitted port and generate a list of affected shipments requiring rebooking or transshipment arrangements. The financial impact of deviations must be calculated in real-time, including additional port costs, fuel costs, and potential demurrage or detention claims. Deviation records must be linked to the voyage schedule version history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R013	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Agent Assignment and Communication	The system must support the assignment of port agents to each port call from an approved agent directory maintained per port. Agent assignments must include the agent company name, primary contact person, telephone numbers, email addresses, and agency agreement reference number. The system must automatically notify the assigned port agent when a port call is confirmed, providing vessel details, ETA, cargo summary, and special requirements. Agent communication must be tracked within the system, with email threads linked to the specific port call record. Agent performance ratings must be captured post-call covering responsiveness, documentation accuracy, and cost management. Agent invoices must be matched against the pre-estimated port cost budget for variance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R006	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	EU MRV and FuelEU Maritime Integration	For vessels calling at EU ports en route from or to Middle East and Indian ports (including transshipment cargo from Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, and JNPT INNSA), the system must track and report emissions under EU MRV Regulation 2015/757 and FuelEU Maritime requirements. The system shall calculate well-to-wake GHG intensity per transport work unit, track compliance balances, and compute potential penalties for non-compliant vessels. Pooling arrangements between vessels in the fleet must be supported, allowing surplus compliance from efficient vessels to offset deficits from less efficient ones across the multi-entity corporate structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R014	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Congestion Monitoring and Alerts	The system must monitor port congestion levels at all ports in the network using a combination of AIS vessel density data, terminal operating system queue lengths, and manual congestion reports submitted by port agents. Congestion levels must be classified as Normal, Moderate, High, and Critical, with configurable thresholds per port. When congestion at a port in an upcoming rotation exceeds the Moderate threshold, the system must generate an alert to voyage planners with the estimated additional waiting time. The AI engine must recommend proactive schedule adjustments such as slow steaming to delay arrival until congestion subsides, thus saving fuel costs while avoiding anchorage waiting charges. Historical congestion patterns must be analyzed seasonally to improve future voyage planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R015	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Multi-Terminal Port Call Coordination	The system must support port calls where a vessel visits multiple terminals within the same port during a single call, such as a vessel calling at both Terminal 1 and Terminal 2 within Jebel Ali or visiting both the container terminal and the general cargo berth at JNPT. Each terminal visit must be tracked as a sub-call within the parent port call, with separate berth assignments, cargo operations tracking, and terminal cost recording. The system must calculate intra-port shifting costs including pilot charges, tug charges, and linesmen fees for vessel movements between terminals. The total port call duration must aggregate all sub-call durations including shifting times for schedule management purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R016	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Master Data and Tariff Management	The system must maintain comprehensive port master data for all ports in the operational network, including port UN/LOCODE, port name in English and Arabic, country, time zone, latitude and longitude, maximum vessel size restrictions (LOA, beam, draft), available berths with specifications, terminal operators, pilotage requirements, tidal information, and approach channel details. Port tariff schedules must be stored with effective date ranges and must support complex tariff structures including tiered pricing by vessel GRT, time-based berth hire rates, and surcharges for night operations, weekend operations, and hazardous cargo. Tariff updates must be versioned so that historical port call cost calculations remain traceable to the tariff schedule that was effective at the time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-002-R017	FEAT-011-1-002	MOD-011	Port Call Reporting and Documentation Archive	The system must generate a comprehensive port call report (Statement of Facts) upon completion of each port call, capturing all milestone timestamps, cargo operations summary (containers loaded, discharged, shifted, restowed), delays encountered with reason codes, weather conditions during operations, and any incidents or exceptions. The Statement of Facts must be generated in both English and Arabic and must be digitally signed by the vessel master representative and the port agent. All port call documentation including the Statement of Facts, port disbursement account, stevedoring reports, and photographs must be archived in a document management system linked to the voyage and port call records. Archives must be retained for a minimum of seven years and be searchable by voyage, vessel, port, and date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R001	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Trade Lane Capacity Pool Definition	The system must allow capacity managers to define capacity pools for each trade lane on each voyage, specifying the maximum TEU allocation, maximum weight allocation in metric tons, reefer slot count, out-of-gauge slot count, and dangerous goods slot count per IMO class. Trade lanes must be defined by origin-destination port pairs or port group pairs (e.g., Gulf Ports to India West Coast, Hamad Port to JNPT direct). Capacity pools must be hierarchical, allowing a master pool to be subdivided into sub-pools by customer tier (Premium, Standard, Spot), by cargo type (dry, reefer, DG, OOG), and by entity. The sum of sub-pool allocations must not exceed the master pool limit, enforced by real-time validation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R002	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Contract-Based Capacity Reservation (MQC/NAC)	The system must support Minimum Quantity Commitments (MQC) and Named Account Contracts (NAC) that reserve a fixed capacity allocation on specific trade lanes and service loops for contracted customers. Each contract must specify the reserved TEU per voyage or per week, the contract period with start and end dates, the take-or-pay penalty terms, and the rate agreement reference. Reserved capacity must be ring-fenced from the general allocation pool and must be automatically released to the spot market if the customer has not booked against the reservation by a configurable cutoff time (default: 72 hours before port ETD). The system must track MQC fulfillment percentage per customer per period and generate alerts when utilization falls below the contractual minimum threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R003	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	AI-Driven Dynamic Capacity Reallocation	The AI engine must continuously monitor booking levels against allocated capacity across all trade lanes and voyages, identifying underutilized allocations and overbooked lanes. When utilization on a trade lane falls below a configurable threshold (default: sixty percent) at seven days before departure, the AI must recommend reallocation of surplus capacity to overbooked trade lanes on the same voyage. Reallocation recommendations must consider physical stowage constraints (vessel stability, stack weights, reefer plug availability), commercial priorities (revenue per TEU by trade lane), and contractual commitments. Each recommendation must be presented with projected revenue impact in USD and must require approval from the capacity manager before execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R004	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Capacity Utilization Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide a real-time capacity utilization dashboard showing percentage utilization by trade lane, voyage, vessel, entity, and cargo type. The dashboard must display utilization as both TEU count and weight, as the binding constraint may vary (e.g., heavy cargo from India may hit weight limits before TEU limits). Visualization must include heat maps showing utilization intensity across the trade lane network, trend charts showing utilization over time, and vessel cross-section diagrams showing filled versus available slots. Dashboard data must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and exportable to Excel format. Revenue per TEU by trade lane must be displayed alongside utilization to support yield management decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R008	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Real-Time CII Projection Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard projecting each vessel's year-end CII rating based on year-to-date actual performance and planned future voyages. The projection algorithm shall incorporate scheduled voyages with estimated fuel consumption, planned slow-steaming parameters, scheduled dry-dock periods, and seasonal weather routing adjustments for the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean trade lanes. The dashboard must display current attained CII trajectory versus the A/B/C/D/E boundary thresholds with visual color coding. Arabic RTL layout must be fully supported with bidirectional text rendering for vessel names, port names, and all labels without truncation or misalignment issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R005	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Multi-Currency Capacity Revenue Tracking	The system must track capacity revenue in the currency of the booking (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) and convert to the entity's functional currency and the group reporting currency (USD) using the exchange rate applicable on the booking date or the voyage departure date, as configured per entity accounting policy. Revenue projections for each capacity allocation must be recalculated when exchange rates fluctuate beyond a configurable threshold (default: two percent), with variance reports showing the impact of currency movements on projected voyage revenue. The system must support hedged rate application for customers with fixed-rate contracts denominated in local currencies. All currency conversion audit trails must be maintained for regulatory compliance across all four country jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R006	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Seasonal Capacity Planning and Forecasting	The system must support seasonal capacity planning by allowing managers to define capacity allocation profiles that vary by season, accounting for known demand patterns such as pre-Ramadan cargo surges to Gulf ports, Indian festival season peaks (Diwali, Navratri), post-Chinese New Year import waves, and summer reefer cargo peaks for perishable goods. Historical booking data from previous years must be analyzed by the AI engine to generate demand forecasts per trade lane per week, with confidence intervals. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actual bookings to continuously improve prediction models. Seasonal plans must be created annually, reviewed quarterly, and adjustable at the individual voyage level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R007	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Overbooking Policy and Management	The system must support configurable overbooking policies per trade lane, allowing capacity managers to accept bookings exceeding the physical allocation by a defined percentage to account for historical no-show and cancellation rates. The overbooking percentage must be configurable per trade lane, per cargo type, and per customer tier, with typical ranges between five and fifteen percent. The AI engine must recommend optimal overbooking levels based on historical no-show analysis specific to each trade lane. When actual bookings approach or exceed the overbooking threshold, the system must generate escalation alerts to the capacity manager. Rollover management rules must be defined to prioritize which bookings are rolled to the next available sailing if overbooking materializes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R008	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Transshipment Capacity Coordination	The system must manage capacity allocation for transshipment cargo that requires coordinated space on two or more voyage legs. When a booking involves transshipment at a hub port (e.g., Jebel Ali as a transshipment hub connecting feeder services to Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Indian ports), the system must simultaneously reserve capacity on both the mother vessel and the connecting feeder vessel. If capacity is unavailable on either leg, the booking must not be confirmed on the other leg. Transshipment capacity must be tracked separately from direct capacity in utilization reports. Connection time windows at the transshipment hub must be validated to ensure minimum dwell time (default: 48 hours) and maximum free time (default: 14 days) constraints are met.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R009	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Empty Container Repositioning Capacity	The system must allocate dedicated capacity for empty container repositioning to address trade imbalances, particularly the surplus of empty containers at import-heavy ports like JNPT and the deficit at export-heavy Gulf ports. Empty repositioning allocations must be managed separately from commercial cargo allocations but must draw from the same physical vessel capacity. The AI engine must forecast empty equipment demand by location using booking pipeline data and historical discharge patterns, recommending repositioning volumes per voyage. Repositioning capacity must have lower priority than revenue cargo and must be automatically released to commercial bookings when demand warrants, subject to a configurable minimum repositioning guarantee per voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R010	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Capacity Allocation Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a multi-level approval workflow for capacity allocation decisions. Initial capacity pool definition must be approved by the trade lane manager. Modifications to contracted customer reservations must require approval from both the trade lane manager and the commercial manager. Reallocation of capacity between trade lanes must be approved by the regional capacity director. Spot market release of reserved capacity must follow a configurable escalation path based on the volume being released. All approval workflows must support delegation during absence with configurable time-limited authority. Approval requests must be accessible via mobile interface and must auto-escalate if not actioned within four hours. Complete audit trails of all approval decisions must be maintained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R011	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Reefer Capacity Specialized Allocation	The system must manage reefer container capacity as a distinct allocation category given the physical constraint of reefer plug availability on vessels. Each vessel's reefer plug count and location (on-deck, under-deck, by bay) must be maintained in the vessel registry. Reefer allocations must consider not only plug count but also power generation capacity and temperature monitoring capability. Trade lanes with high reefer demand (e.g., perishable exports from India) must have dedicated reefer capacity pools. The system must enforce that reefer bookings do not exceed the physical plug count for the applicable stowage area. When reefer capacity is exhausted, the system must offer the customer alternative sailings with reefer availability rather than simply rejecting the booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R012	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Weight-Based Capacity Constraint Management	The system must enforce weight-based capacity constraints alongside TEU-based constraints, recognizing that certain trade lanes (particularly exports of heavy commodities from India) reach the vessel's maximum deadweight before TEU slots are fully utilized. The system must calculate remaining weight capacity per voyage leg by deducting confirmed booking weights, estimated container tare weights, bunker fuel weight, freshwater, and vessel constants from the vessel's maximum summer deadweight. Weight-based utilization must be displayed in the capacity dashboard alongside TEU utilization. When booking a heavy cargo shipment, the system must validate that the cumulative weight does not exceed the per-stack weight limit, per-tier weight limit, and overall vessel deadweight limit on any leg of the voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R013	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Capacity Allocation for Special Cargo Types	The system must manage dedicated capacity allocations for special cargo types including out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo requiring flat racks or open tops, break-bulk cargo, ISO tank containers, and project cargo requiring multiple slots. OOG capacity must account for the additional slot consumption based on the cargo dimensions: an over-width container may consume two adjacent slots, an over-height container may block the tier above. The system must calculate effective slot consumption for each special cargo booking and deduct from the available capacity accordingly. Special cargo must be flagged in the capacity allocation to alert the stowage planner. AI must analyze historical special cargo patterns per trade lane to recommend pre-allocation of special cargo slots on relevant voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R014	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Inter-Entity Capacity Sharing Agreements	The system must support capacity sharing agreements between entities where one entity (e.g., Qatar) may access unused capacity allocated to another entity (e.g., UAE) under predefined commercial terms. Sharing agreements must specify the maximum sharable TEU, the transfer price per TEU in USD, the priority rules (own entity cargo always takes priority), and the notification requirements. When an entity's own allocation is exhausted and a sharing agreement exists, the system must automatically check partner entity capacity and offer it to the booking agent with transparent markup visibility. All inter-entity capacity transfers must generate intercompany revenue entries at the agreed transfer pricing rate. Monthly reconciliation reports must track capacity shared, revenue transferred, and net settlement amounts between entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R015	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Voyage Profitability Impact of Capacity Decisions	The system must calculate the projected voyage profitability impact of capacity allocation decisions in real-time, considering revenue per TEU by trade lane, variable costs (fuel, port costs, canal fees), and allocated fixed costs (vessel hire, insurance, manning). Each capacity allocation change must update the projected voyage P&L statement, showing the marginal revenue and marginal cost impact. The AI engine must recommend the revenue-maximizing capacity allocation mix across trade lanes subject to physical and contractual constraints, using linear programming or similar optimization techniques. Profitability projections must be reported in USD with supporting detail available in entity functional currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Break-even utilization levels must be calculated and displayed per voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-003-R016	FEAT-011-1-003	MOD-011	Capacity Allocation Audit and Compliance	The system must maintain a complete audit log of all capacity allocation actions including initial pool creation, modifications, approvals, releases, and inter-entity transfers. Each audit entry must capture the action timestamp, the user identity, the entity context, the before and after values, and the business justification. Audit logs must be immutable and tamper-evident, stored with cryptographic hashing to ensure integrity. The system must support audit queries by date range, user, entity, trade lane, and voyage for internal audit and regulatory compliance reviews. Capacity allocation practices must be auditable against anti-trust compliance rules, ensuring that allocation decisions do not constitute unfair market practices. Audit reports must be exportable in formats acceptable to Qatar Financial Centre Authority, DMCC, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R001	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Real-Time Space Availability Calculation	The system must calculate and display real-time space availability for every active voyage at the trade lane level, port pair level, and individual port call level. Availability must reflect confirmed bookings, tentative bookings (with configurable aging rules), waitlisted bookings (excluded from available count), and blocked space (maintenance, operational reserves). Availability must be expressed as remaining TEU count, remaining weight in metric tons, remaining reefer plugs, and remaining special cargo slots. The calculation must account for multi-leg bookings that consume space across multiple port-to-port segments of the voyage. Space availability must refresh within three seconds of any booking transaction to ensure booking agents across all entities see consistent, up-to-date availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R002	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Confirmation Against Available Space	The system must validate every booking confirmation against available space, ensuring that the vessel has sufficient TEU capacity, weight capacity, reefer plugs (for reefer bookings), and special cargo slots (for OOG, DG, or tank bookings) on every leg of the voyage affected by the booking. Validation must occur in real-time at the point of booking confirmation and must use pessimistic locking to prevent double-booking when multiple agents book simultaneously. If space is insufficient, the system must reject the confirmation and offer alternatives: the next available sailing on the same trade lane, an alternative routing via transshipment, or waitlist placement. Confirmed bookings must immediately decrement available space and generate a booking confirmation number linked to the voyage and space allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R003	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Cut-Off Time Enforcement	The system must enforce configurable booking cut-off times that determine the latest point at which bookings can be accepted, modified, or cancelled for each port call. Cut-off types must include documentation cut-off (deadline for shipping instructions), cargo receiving cut-off (deadline for container gate-in at terminal), and VGM cut-off (deadline for verified gross mass submission as per SOLAS regulation). Cut-off times must be defined as hours before vessel ETD and must be configurable per port, per terminal, and per cargo type (standard, DG, reefer, OOG). The system must automatically transition bookings from open to closed status when cut-off times are reached. Late booking acceptance must require authorization override from a user with late-booking-approver role, with the reason documented.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R004	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Waitlist Management and Automatic Promotion	The system must maintain a prioritized waitlist for voyages where confirmed bookings have filled the available space allocation. Waitlist priority must be determined by a scoring algorithm considering customer tier (Premium customers prioritized over Spot), booking timestamp, revenue per TEU, cargo type priority (reefer and DG often prioritized for time sensitivity), and entity priority as configured. When space becomes available due to booking cancellations or capacity reallocation, the AI engine must automatically promote the highest-priority waitlisted booking to confirmed status and notify the customer within fifteen minutes. Waitlisted customers must be able to view their queue position through the customer portal. Waitlist aging rules must automatically expire waitlisted bookings that exceed a configurable maximum wait period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R005	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Space Protection and VIP Reservation Rules	The system must support space protection rules that reserve capacity for strategically important customers or cargo types beyond standard contract allocations. Protected space must be configured by trade lane managers specifying the number of protected TEU slots, the eligible customer or cargo category, the protection expiry date and time (after which slots revert to general availability), and the protection priority level. Multiple protection rules may apply to the same voyage, and the system must enforce that the total protected space does not exceed a configurable percentage of total voyage capacity (default: thirty percent). Protected space must be visually distinguished in the availability display and must not be bookable by non-eligible customers until the protection expires.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R006	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Amendment and Space Reallocation	The system must support booking amendments that affect space allocation, including changes to container quantity, container type (e.g., upgrading from 20ft to 40ft), weight adjustments, port of loading or discharge changes, and voyage rollovers. Each amendment must trigger a real-time space availability revalidation on the new parameters. If the amendment increases space consumption and insufficient space exists, the amendment must be rejected or waitlisted. If the amendment decreases space consumption, the freed space must be immediately available for other bookings. Amendment history must be maintained with before and after values, amendment timestamp, and the user who processed the change. Amendments after cargo cut-off must follow the late-booking authorization process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R007	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Cancellation and No-Show Management	The system must process booking cancellations with immediate space release and maintain cancellation records including the cancellation reason code, cancellation timestamp, cancelled-by user, and cancellation fee applicability. Cancellation fee rules must be configurable per trade lane and customer contract: no fee if cancelled more than seven days before ETD, twenty-five percent if cancelled three to seven days before, fifty percent if cancelled less than three days before. No-show detection must occur automatically when a confirmed booking has no corresponding container gate-in at the loading terminal by the cargo cut-off time. No-shows must trigger automatic cancellation, space release, and customer notification. No-show rates must be tracked per customer for use in overbooking policy calibration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R008	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Multi-Entity Booking Space Visibility	The system must provide cross-entity visibility of space availability while maintaining booking jurisdiction controls. Booking agents of the Qatar entity must be able to view space availability on voyages managed by the UAE entity and vice versa, but booking confirmation must follow inter-entity commercial rules. When a Qatar entity agent books space on a UAE entity-managed voyage, the system must apply the inter-entity transfer pricing and generate the corresponding intercompany accounting entries. Space availability displays must clearly indicate which entity manages each voyage and whether cross-entity booking is permitted under current sharing agreements. Entity-specific booking quotas must be enforced to prevent one entity from consuming another's allocated space without authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R009	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Slot Costing and Revenue Per Slot Analysis	The system must calculate the cost per slot and revenue per slot for each voyage at the trade lane level, supporting yield management decisions. Slot cost must include the allocated voyage cost (vessel hire, fuel, port costs, canal fees) divided by the total available slots, adjusted for the specific cost drivers of each trade lane leg. Revenue per slot must be calculated from the booked freight rates, inclusive of base ocean freight, surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS), and any ancillary revenue. The system must highlight trade lanes where revenue per slot falls below slot cost, indicating unprofitable allocations. AI must recommend pricing adjustments or capacity reductions for consistently loss-making trade lanes. All financial calculations must support multi-currency with conversion to USD for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R010	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Hazardous Cargo Space Segregation Control	The system must enforce IMO dangerous goods segregation requirements when allocating space for hazardous cargo bookings. The IMDG Code segregation table must be implemented to validate that incompatible DG classes are not allocated adjacent stowage positions. Space allocation for DG containers must consider the vessel's Document of Compliance specifying which holds and deck areas are approved for each DG class. The system must maintain a running count of DG containers by class per vessel compartment and reject bookings that would exceed the compartment's DG capacity or create segregation violations. Special space allocation rules must apply for Class 1 (explosives) requiring dedicated magazine space and Class 7 (radioactive) requiring minimum distance separation from crew quarters and provisions stores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R011	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Container Yard Capacity Integration	The system must integrate vessel space management with container yard capacity at origin and destination ports to ensure that vessel booking confirmations are consistent with terminal yard availability. Before confirming a booking at a loading port, the system must verify that the terminal has sufficient yard capacity to receive the container within the planned gate-in window. Yard capacity checks must account for current yard utilization, expected gate-in and gate-out volumes, and terminal-specific stack height limits. For ports where yard congestion is chronic (e.g., JNPT during peak season), the AI must recommend booking distribution across multiple gate-in windows to smooth yard utilization. Yard capacity data must be obtained via integration with terminal operating systems or manually entered by terminal coordinators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R012	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Space Allocation Optimization by AI	The AI engine must optimize the allocation of bookings to vessel space by recommending the optimal stowage position category (on-deck, under-deck, forward, aft) for each booking based on cargo weight, container type, discharge port sequence, and vessel stability requirements. The optimization must minimize the number of restow moves required at intermediate ports by placing containers bound for later discharge ports in positions that do not block earlier discharge containers. The AI must generate a preliminary space allocation plan at the booking level (not detailed bay plan, which is handled by stowage planning) that guides terminal planners. The preliminary plan must be regenerated dynamically as new bookings are added or existing bookings are amended.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R013	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Space Allocation for Vessel Sharing Agreements	The system must support Vessel Sharing Agreements (VSA) where multiple carriers share capacity on the same vessel. Each VSA partner's allocation must be defined as a fixed TEU quota or a percentage of total vessel capacity. The system must track bookings by VSA partner and enforce that each partner does not exceed their allocated space. Unused partner space may be offered to other partners through a configurable cascading release mechanism triggered at specified time intervals before departure. VSA cost and revenue sharing calculations must follow the contractual terms, which may be based on TEU utilization, proportional to allocation, or a hybrid model. The system must generate periodic VSA reconciliation reports showing each partner's utilization, revenue contribution, and settlement amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R014	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Booking Rollover Management Across Voyages	The system must manage booking rollovers where confirmed bookings are moved from one voyage to a subsequent voyage due to space constraints, vessel delays, or customer requests. Rollover processing must release space on the original voyage, book space on the new voyage (subject to availability), update all booking references and documentation, and notify affected parties including the customer, origin terminal, destination terminal, and customs brokers. The system must track the number of times a booking has been rolled over and flag bookings that have been rolled more than a configurable limit (default: two times) for management attention. Rollover reasons must be categorized (vessel delay, capacity shortage, customer request, operational decision) for root cause analysis. Compensation rules must be applied automatically based on the rollover reason and contract terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R015	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Space Management Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive space management reports including voyage utilization summary (planned versus actual TEU, weight, reefer), booking-to-confirmation conversion rate, average lead time from booking to sailing, cancellation and no-show rates by trade lane and customer, waitlist conversion rate, and overbooking materialization frequency. Reports must be available in both tabular and graphical formats with drill-down capability from summary to individual booking level. Report generation must support scheduled automated delivery to stakeholders via email. All reports must render correctly in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting. Report data must be segmentable by entity, ensuring that Qatar entity managers see Qatar-relevant data by default with optional cross-entity views subject to access permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R016	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Space Freeze and Vessel Closing Process	The system must support a vessel closing process that freezes space allocation on a voyage at a configurable time before departure (default: twenty-four hours before first loading port ETD). Once frozen, no new bookings can be confirmed, no amendments that increase space consumption are permitted, and the booking list is locked for final stowage planning. The freeze process must generate a final manifest report showing all confirmed bookings by port of loading and discharge. Unfilled space after freeze must be categorized as dead space and reported in voyage performance metrics. Emergency booking additions after freeze must require authorization from the vessel operations manager and must be logged with justification. The freeze status must be communicated to all relevant terminal operators and port agents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R017	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	API Integration for External Booking Systems	The system must expose RESTful APIs that allow authorized external systems (customer booking portals, freight forwarder platforms, digital freight marketplaces) to query space availability and submit booking requests. APIs must support OAuth 2.0 authentication with entity-level API key management. Rate limiting must be configured per API consumer to prevent abuse, with default limits of one hundred requests per minute for availability queries and twenty requests per minute for booking submissions. API responses must include real-time space availability, booking confirmation or rejection with reason codes, and waitlist position if applicable. The API must support both JSON and XML response formats. Webhook callbacks must be available for asynchronous booking status notifications. API usage analytics must be tracked per consumer for billing and capacity planning purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-1-004-R018	FEAT-011-1-004	MOD-011	Offline Booking and Space Reservation Handling	The system must support offline booking scenarios for agents operating in locations with unreliable internet connectivity, particularly relevant for smaller Indian port offices and field sales representatives. Offline mode must allow agents to create provisional bookings against a locally cached snapshot of space availability. Provisional bookings must be queued and submitted automatically when connectivity is restored, with the system validating space availability at the time of synchronization. If space is no longer available when the provisional booking syncs, the system must automatically place the booking on the waitlist and notify the agent with alternative options. The offline client must clearly display the staleness of cached availability data, showing the time elapsed since last synchronization, and must warn agents when cached data exceeds a configurable age threshold (default: four hours).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R001	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Route Definition and Management	The system must allow users to define and manage transshipment routes specifying origin port, hub port, and destination port combinations. Each route definition must include the mother vessel service loop identifier, feeder vessel service loop identifier, expected dwell time at the hub port, and allowable connection window in hours. Routes must support all regional hub ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), and JNPT (INJNP). The system must validate that feeder departure occurs after mother vessel arrival plus minimum handling time. Route definitions must be entity-specific, allowing Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities to maintain independent transshipment configurations while sharing common route data across the multi-entity structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R002	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	AI-Driven Transshipment Connection Optimization	The system must provide AI-powered optimization that automatically recommends the best transshipment connections for cargo based on transit time, cost, dwell time at hub, vessel capacity, and schedule reliability. The AI engine must evaluate at least five alternative routing options per booking and rank them using configurable weighting factors. Optimization must account for container type constraints (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, OT, FR) and hazardous cargo segregation rules per IMDG Code. The algorithm must process a minimum of 5,000 transshipment evaluations per hour and present results within 3 seconds per individual query. All optimization results must be logged with reasoning explanations for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R003	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Dwell Time Monitoring and Alerts	The system must continuously monitor transshipment cargo dwell time at hub ports and generate alerts when dwell time approaches or exceeds configurable thresholds. Default thresholds must be set at 72 hours for dry containers, 48 hours for reefer containers, and 24 hours for hazardous cargo at ports QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, and INJNP. Alerts must be dispatched via in-app notification, email, and SMS in both Arabic and English based on user language preference. The system must calculate projected dwell time using AIS vessel tracking data and port congestion indices. Dwell time calculations must account for Hijri calendar holidays and Friday/Saturday weekends in Qatar, UAE, and KSA versus Saturday/Sunday weekends in India. Escalation rules must trigger when dwell exceeds 120% of threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R004	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Multi-Entity Transshipment Cost Allocation	The system must allocate transshipment handling costs across multiple legal entities when cargo moves through hub ports operated by different group companies. Cost allocation must support inter-company transfer pricing rules compliant with Qatar Financial Centre, UAE Federal Tax Authority, ZATCA (KSA), and Indian GST regulations. Transshipment costs must be captured in the originating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with automatic conversion using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. The system must generate inter-company debit and credit notes automatically upon vessel departure from the hub port. Each cost allocation must include terminal handling charges, storage fees, reefer monitoring costs, and any port-specific surcharges. All amounts must support four decimal places for currency precision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R005	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Feeder Vessel Connection Scheduling	The system must maintain a real-time schedule of feeder vessel connections at each hub port synchronized with mother vessel arrivals. Feeder schedules must include vessel name, IMO number, service loop code, estimated time of arrival, estimated time of departure, allocated berth, and available slot capacity by container type. The system must automatically detect scheduling conflicts when a mother vessel delay causes the connection window to fall below the minimum required handling time. When conflicts are detected, the AI engine must propose alternative feeder connections or recommend cargo hold-over to the next feeder sailing. Schedule data must be refreshable from carrier EDI feeds (IFTMIN, COPARN messages) and displayed in a Gantt-style timeline view with Arabic RTL layout support for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R006	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Cargo Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time transshipment cargo tracking dashboard showing all in-transit transshipment containers across hub ports with their current status: awaiting discharge, discharged to yard, in yard storage, loaded on feeder, or departed on feeder. The dashboard must support filtering by origin port, destination port, hub port, shipping line, container type, booking reference, and bill of lading number. Visual indicators must use color coding for on-time (green), at-risk (amber), and delayed (red) connections. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with all data labels, column headers, and filter options fully localized. Dashboard data must refresh every 60 seconds and support export to PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R007	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Hazardous Cargo Segregation Compliance	The system must enforce IMDG Code segregation rules for hazardous cargo undergoing transshipment at hub ports. When planning transshipment connections, the system must validate that hazardous containers of incompatible classes are not co-loaded on the same feeder vessel bay or adjacent yard blocks during dwell. Segregation validation must reference the IMDG Code amendment 41-22 segregation table and support all nine hazard classes plus divisions. The system must block transshipment connection assignments that would violate segregation requirements and provide the operator with a detailed compliance report listing the specific IMDG rules violated. Override capability must be restricted to authorized safety officers with mandatory documentation of the override reason. All segregation checks must complete within 2 seconds per container.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R008	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Performance Analytics and KPIs	The system must calculate and display key performance indicators for transshipment operations including average dwell time by hub port, connection reliability percentage, missed connection count, average transshipment cost per TEU, and yard utilization during transshipment peaks. KPIs must be segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), by hub port (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INJNP), by shipping line, and by trade lane. Historical trend analysis must cover a minimum of 24 rolling months with month-over-month and year-over-year comparison capabilities. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date ranges. Data visualization must include bar charts, line trends, and heat maps. All analytics must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with amounts displayed in the user's preferred currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R009	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Carrier Agreement and Slot Swap Management	The system must manage slot exchange and vessel sharing agreements (VSA) between carriers for transshipment operations. Each agreement must record the participating carriers, effective date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri dates), allocated slot quantities by container type per sailing, and commercial terms including slot cost per TEU in the agreed currency. The system must track slot utilization against allocated quantities in real time and generate warnings when utilization exceeds 85% of the allocated capacity. Slot swap balancing reports must be generated monthly showing net slot positions between carriers with financial settlement amounts calculated in the agreement currency (typically USD) and converted to each entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for booking purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R010	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Offline Transshipment Planning Capability	The system must support offline transshipment planning when network connectivity is unavailable at port locations. Offline mode must cache the current vessel schedule, yard inventory, pending transshipment cargo list, and IMDG segregation tables locally on the user's device. Planners must be able to create and modify transshipment connection assignments, record container movements between yard and vessel, and flag exceptions while offline. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline transactions with the central server using conflict resolution rules that prioritize the most recent update by timestamp. Synchronization must handle concurrent modifications by multiple offline users at the same hub port and present conflict resolution options to supervisors. Offline data cache must not exceed 500MB per device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R011	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Document Generation	The system must automatically generate all required transshipment documentation including transshipment manifests, container transfer notes, customs transit declarations, and inter-terminal transfer orders. Documents must comply with Qatar Customs (QAHMD), Dubai Customs (AEJEA), Abu Dhabi Customs (AEKLP), and Indian Customs (INJNP) requirements for transshipment cargo. All documents must be generated in bilingual format (Arabic/English for GCC ports, Hindi/English for Indian ports) with proper RTL text rendering for Arabic content. Document templates must include entity-specific headers, logos, and regulatory reference numbers. The system must support batch generation of documents for an entire vessel's transshipment cargo and individual container-level document generation. Digital signatures must be supported where port authorities accept electronic documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R012	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Yard Block Pre-Allocation	The system must pre-allocate yard blocks at hub ports for expected transshipment cargo based on mother vessel discharge forecasts. Pre-allocation must consider container type (dry, reefer, hazardous, out-of-gauge), expected dwell duration, feeder vessel departure schedule, and yard equipment availability. The AI engine must optimize yard block assignments to minimize horizontal transport distance between discharge berth and allocated block and between allocated block and feeder vessel berth. Reefer containers must be allocated to blocks with adequate power plug capacity. Hazardous containers must be allocated to designated hazmat storage areas compliant with local port authority regulations at each hub. Pre-allocation plans must be adjustable up to 6 hours before mother vessel arrival and must account for current yard occupancy levels. The system must prevent over-allocation beyond 90% block capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R013	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Delay Rebooking Automation	The system must automatically initiate rebooking workflows when transshipment connections are missed due to vessel delays, port congestion, or operational disruptions. The AI engine must identify the next available feeder connection, calculate the cost differential between the original and alternative routing, and assess whether the new routing meets the cargo's delivery deadline. If the deadline cannot be met, the system must escalate to the customer service team with a pre-drafted customer notification in the appropriate language (Arabic or English). Rebooking must update all downstream documents including bills of lading, customs manifests, and inter-company cost allocations. The system must maintain an audit trail linking the original booking to all rebooking iterations with reason codes for each change. Financial impacts of rebooking must be allocated to the responsible entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R014	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment Customs Bond and Guarantee Management	The system must manage customs bonds and financial guarantees required for transshipment cargo at each hub port. Bond requirements vary by jurisdiction: Qatar General Authority of Customs requires transit bonds for certain cargo classes, Dubai Customs mandates warehouse guarantees for extended dwell periods, and Indian Customs requires transshipment bonds under the Customs Act 1962. The system must track bond amounts, validity periods, issuing bank details, and consumption against bond limits in real time. When bond utilization exceeds 80%, the system must alert the finance team. Bond amounts must be maintained in the local currency of each port jurisdiction (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT) with USD equivalent calculations. The system must support automatic bond release requests when transshipment cargo departs the hub port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R015	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Transshipment SLA and Commitment Tracking	The system must track service level agreements for transshipment operations between the company and terminal operators, shipping lines, and feeder service providers. SLA parameters must include guaranteed connection time, maximum dwell time, priority discharge and load sequencing, and reefer monitoring frequency. The system must measure actual performance against each SLA parameter and calculate compliance percentages on a per-vessel, weekly, and monthly basis. SLA breaches must trigger automated notifications to the commercial team with supporting data for penalty or claim calculations. Financial penalties and credits must be recorded in the agreement currency and converted to entity-local currencies for accounting. SLA reports must be available in both Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date options and must support digital distribution to external stakeholders via secure download links.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-001-R016	FEAT-011-2-001	MOD-011	Multi-Hub Transshipment Chain Management	The system must support complex transshipment chains involving multiple hub ports where cargo transships more than once before reaching the final destination. For example, cargo originating in India (INJNP) may transship at Jebel Ali (AEJEA), then again at Hamad Port (QAHMD) before final delivery to a KSA destination via feeder. The system must maintain end-to-end visibility of the cargo across all transshipment legs, tracking status at each hub independently. Transit time calculations must aggregate handling time at each hub port. Cost accumulation must track charges at each transshipment point attributed to the relevant legal entity. The AI engine must evaluate whether multi-hub routings remain cost-effective compared to direct service alternatives and recommend route changes when savings exceed a configurable threshold (default 15% of total freight cost).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R001	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Compilation and Auto-Generation	The system must automatically compile a preliminary loading list for each vessel voyage and port of loading based on confirmed bookings with container allocation. The loading list must include booking reference, bill of lading number, container number, container type and size (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, OT, FR, tank), gross weight in kilograms, VGM (verified gross mass), cargo description, shipper name, consignee name, POL, POD, and final destination. The system must refresh the loading list in real time as new bookings are confirmed, containers are gated in, and documentation is submitted. Loading lists must be generated separately for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and each port of loading (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INJNP). The system must support manual addition and removal of containers from the loading list by authorized operations staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R004	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Discharge Port Rotation Sequencing	The AI engine must sequence container loading to minimize unnecessary container shifts (restows) at each discharge port along the voyage rotation. The optimizer must analyze the full port rotation sequence (e.g., Jebel Ali → Khalifa Port → Hamad Port → Dammam → JNPT and return) and place containers destined for later ports below containers for earlier ports within each bay stack. The system must calculate a projected restow index (target below 3% of total moves per port) and generate alternative stowage scenarios when the restow index exceeds threshold. Each scenario must include estimated additional crane hours and cost impact in the vessel's operating currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) for comparison by the operations planner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R002	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Multi-Tier Cut-Off Deadline Configuration	The system must support configuration of multiple cut-off deadlines per vessel call at each port of loading. Required cut-off types must include: documentation cut-off (deadline for bill of lading instructions), customs cut-off (deadline for customs clearance), VGM cut-off (deadline for verified gross mass submission), reefer cut-off (deadline for reefer container pre-trip inspection and plug-in), hazardous cargo cut-off (deadline for DG declaration submission), and physical gate-in cut-off (deadline for container delivery to terminal). Each cut-off must be configurable independently per vessel, per port, and per shipping line. Cut-off times must be stored and displayed in the local timezone of the port with UTC conversion. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri date display for cut-off deadlines in GCC jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R003	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Cut-Off Compliance Validation Engine	The system must validate each container on the loading list against all applicable cut-off deadlines and flag non-compliant items. Validation must check that VGM has been submitted before VGM cut-off, shipping instructions received before documentation cut-off, customs release obtained before customs cut-off, reefer PTI completed before reefer cut-off, DG declaration approved before hazmat cut-off, and container physically present in terminal before gate-in cut-off. Non-compliant containers must be automatically moved to a hold list with the specific violation reason. The system must support configurable grace periods per cut-off type (e.g., 30-minute grace for documentation at AEJEA). Compliance status must be visible in a color-coded dashboard view showing green (compliant), amber (at risk within 4 hours of cut-off), and red (non-compliant or past cut-off).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R004	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	AI-Predicted Cut-Off Risk Assessment	The system must use AI algorithms to predict which bookings are at risk of missing their cut-off deadlines. Prediction models must analyze historical patterns of documentation submission timing, container gate-in timing, customs clearance duration, and shipper behavior profiles. Risk scores must be calculated on a scale of 0-100 for each booking and each cut-off type. The AI must generate risk assessments at least 48 hours before the earliest cut-off deadline and update predictions every 4 hours as new data becomes available. High-risk bookings (score above 75) must trigger proactive notifications to the customer service team and the shipper via email in the shipper's preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must track prediction accuracy metrics and retrain models monthly to maintain prediction accuracy above 85%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R005	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Finalization and Lock Workflow	The system must implement a formal loading list finalization workflow that transitions the loading list through stages: draft, preliminary, final, and locked. The preliminary list must be generated automatically at documentation cut-off time. The final list must be generated at the latest cut-off time (typically gate-in cut-off). Upon finalization, the system must lock the loading list to prevent further additions unless authorized by a supervisor with documented override reason. The locked loading list must be transmitted to the vessel planner for stowage planning and to the terminal operating system for yard planning. Each state transition must be timestamped and attributed to the user who initiated it. The system must support entity-specific approval chains where different entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) may require different authorization levels for loading list changes after lock.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R006	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Late Arrival and Exception Handling	The system must provide a structured exception handling workflow for containers that arrive after their respective cut-off deadlines. Late arrival requests must capture the container number, reason for delay (categorized: transport delay, customs hold, documentation incomplete, shipper fault, force majeure), expected arrival time, and requestor details. The system must route late arrival requests through an approval chain that may include operations supervisor, vessel planner, and terminal coordinator. Approved late arrivals must be reinserted into the loading list with a late-arrival flag and timestamp. The system must enforce a hard cut-off after which no exceptions are permitted (configurable per port, default 2 hours before vessel departure). All late arrival exceptions must be logged for performance reporting and shipper accountability tracking. Financial penalties for late arrivals must be calculated based on contractual terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R007	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Export and EDI Transmission	The system must export the finalized loading list in multiple formats including BAPLIE (EDI), COPARN (EDI), Excel, PDF, and CSV. EDI messages must conform to UN/EDIFACT standards and support transmission via SFTP, AS2, and API endpoints to terminal operating systems and shipping line partners. PDF exports must be available in bilingual format (Arabic/English for GCC ports, Hindi/English for INJNP) with proper RTL text rendering for Arabic content. Excel exports must include all loading list data fields with entity-specific branding and headers. The system must maintain a transmission log recording the timestamp, recipient, format, and delivery confirmation for each loading list export. Failed transmissions must trigger automatic retry (up to 3 attempts) with escalation to IT support if all retries fail. The loading list must also be publishable to a secure web portal accessible to authorized shipping line representatives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R008	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	VGM Collection and Validation Integration	The system must integrate verified gross mass (VGM) data collection in compliance with SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 requirements. VGM submissions must be accepted via EDI (VERMAS message), web portal upload, API integration, and manual entry. Each VGM submission must include container number, VGM value in kilograms, weighing method (Method 1: weighing packed container, or Method 2: aggregating cargo items), weighbridge certificate reference, authorized signatory name and contact, and submission timestamp. The system must validate that VGM does not exceed the container maximum gross weight. VGM values deviating more than 5% from the declared booking weight must trigger a review alert. Containers without VGM submission must be automatically excluded from the loading list at VGM cut-off. The system must maintain VGM records for a minimum of 3 years per regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R005	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Weight Distribution and Trim Optimization	The system must continuously calculate vessel longitudinal trim, list, shear forces, and bending moments as containers are assigned to stowage positions in the load plan. The AI optimizer must maintain trim within +/- 0.5 meters of the target sailing trim specified by the vessel master and ensure bending moments do not exceed 90% of the vessel's maximum permissible values at any frame. For multi-port voyages across the Arabian Gulf, the optimizer must compute stability at each intermediate port condition (arrival and departure) accounting for fuel consumption, ballast changes, and cargo operations. The system must integrate with vessel-specific hydrostatic tables and produce a stability booklet summary compliant with SOLAS Chapter II-1 requirements for review before departure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R009	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Customs Clearance Status Integration	The system must integrate with customs systems at each port to receive real-time customs clearance status for export containers. Integration must support Qatar General Authority of Customs at Hamad Port, Dubai Customs and Federal Authority for Identity and Citizenship at Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi Customs at Khalifa Port, and Indian Customs ICEGATE system at JNPT. Customs status must be mapped to standardized states: pending declaration, declared, under examination, examined and released, released, and held. Containers with customs hold status must be automatically flagged on the loading list and excluded from the final loading list until cleared. The system must record the customs declaration number, HS code summary, and release timestamp for each container. Customs clearance approaching the customs cut-off deadline within 6 hours must trigger alerts to the documentation team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R010	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Reefer Container Pre-Trip Inspection Tracking	The system must track pre-trip inspection (PTI) status for all reefer containers on the loading list. PTI records must include inspection date, inspector identification, set temperature, supply air temperature, return air temperature, humidity setting (if applicable), atmosphere control settings for controlled atmosphere containers, and PTI pass/fail result. Failed PTI containers must be automatically removed from the loading list and trigger a substitute container request workflow. The system must enforce that PTI is completed before the reefer cut-off deadline. For reefer containers requiring genset units, the system must verify genset allocation and fuel level adequacy for the planned voyage duration. PTI compliance reports must be generated per vessel showing pass rates, common failure reasons, and average inspection duration. All temperatures must be stored in both Celsius and Fahrenheit with conversion accuracy to one decimal place.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R011	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Hazardous Cargo Declaration Management	The system must manage dangerous goods declarations for all hazardous containers on the loading list per IMDG Code requirements. Each declaration must capture UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class and division, packing group, marine pollutant indicator, flash point (for flammable liquids), EmS (Emergency Schedule) codes, net and gross quantity, and shipper's emergency contact. The system must validate that all required fields are completed and that the declared hazard class is consistent with the UN number reference database. Declarations must be submitted before the hazardous cargo cut-off deadline. The system must cross-reference hazardous cargo against the vessel's Document of Compliance to ensure the vessel is certified to carry the declared hazard classes. Multi-class hazardous cargo must have each class individually validated. All DG declarations must be stored for 5 years per regulatory retention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R012	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Cargo Weight Summary and Validation	The system must calculate and display a real-time cargo weight summary for each vessel loading list showing total TEU count, total gross weight, total VGM weight, weight by container type, weight by port of discharge, and weight by cargo category (general, reefer, hazardous, over-dimensional). The system must validate that the total loading weight does not exceed the vessel's available deadweight tonnage for the specific port rotation considering ballast, bunkers, and freshwater. Weight distribution must be checked against the vessel's trim and stability parameters to ensure safe loading. Weight discrepancies between booking declarations and actual VGM exceeding configurable thresholds (default 3 tonnes for 20-foot, 5 tonnes for 40-foot containers) must be flagged for investigation. All weight values must be displayed in metric tonnes with conversion to long tons available for reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R013	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Change Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all changes to the loading list from initial compilation through finalization and post-lock modifications. Each audit entry must record the timestamp, user identity, user's entity affiliation, action type (add container, remove container, modify details, status change, approve exception, lock list, unlock list), the specific data fields changed with before and after values, and the reason for change where applicable. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. Audit reports must be filterable by date range (supporting Gregorian and Hijri calendars), user, action type, and container number. The audit trail must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats for regulatory review. Data retention for loading list audit records must comply with the longest applicable retention period across all four jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), which is currently 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R014	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Shipper Performance Scoring for Cut-Off Compliance	The system must calculate and maintain a shipper performance score based on historical cut-off compliance behavior. Scoring criteria must include percentage of on-time documentation submission, percentage of on-time VGM submission, percentage of on-time container gate-in, frequency of late arrival exception requests, and frequency of booking cancellations after cut-off. Scores must be calculated on a rolling 6-month window and updated weekly. The scoring algorithm must weight recent performance more heavily than older data using exponential decay. Shipper scores must be visible to booking and customer service teams during booking acceptance and must feed into the AI risk prediction engine for cut-off compliance. Low-scoring shippers (below configurable threshold, default 60/100) must trigger mandatory deposit or advance documentation requirements. Scores must be maintained per entity and per port to reflect regional performance variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R015	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Loading List Notifications and Communication Hub	The system must provide automated notifications to all relevant stakeholders throughout the loading list lifecycle. Notification triggers must include: loading list published (to shipping line, terminal), cut-off approaching (to shippers, freight forwarders at 48h, 24h, 12h, 6h intervals), container removed from loading list (to booking party), loading list finalized (to vessel planner, terminal, customs), and post-lock changes (to all stakeholders). Notifications must be delivered via email, SMS, in-app alerts, and optionally WhatsApp Business API. All notification content must be available in Arabic and English with automatic language selection based on recipient preference. Notification templates must be configurable per entity and per port. The system must track notification delivery status and provide a communication log accessible from the loading list detail view. Bulk notification sending must support at least 10,000 recipients per vessel within 5 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R016	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Booking Rollover Management for Missed Cut-Offs	The system must automatically manage booking rollovers when cargo misses cut-off deadlines and cannot be loaded on the originally planned vessel. The rollover process must identify the next available vessel on the same trade route with sufficient capacity, calculate any cost differential (freight rate changes, storage charges, additional handling fees) in the booking currency, and generate a rollover proposal for customer service review. Approved rollovers must update the booking record, reassign the container to the new vessel's loading list, and notify the shipper with revised sailing details. Financial impacts of rollovers must be categorized as carrier-caused or shipper-caused for cost attribution. Rollover charges must be calculated in the original booking currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic currency conversion where the receiving entity differs from the originating entity. The system must track rollover frequency by trade lane for capacity planning analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-002-R017	FEAT-011-2-002	MOD-011	Offline Loading List Access and Modification	The system must provide offline access to the loading list for port operations staff working in areas with unreliable connectivity at terminals. Offline mode must cache the current loading list, cut-off schedules, container status data, and pending exception requests. Operations staff must be able to update container gate-in status, record VGM values, approve or reject late arrival requests, and add notes to individual containers while offline. The offline cache must synchronize with the central server upon reconnection using optimistic concurrency control with last-writer-wins conflict resolution at the container level. Conflicts at the loading list level (e.g., simultaneous lock attempts) must be escalated to the operations supervisor. Offline sessions must automatically time out after 8 hours to prevent stale data accumulation. The system must display a prominent offline indicator in the UI and log all offline transactions with device identification for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R001	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Message Import and Parsing	The system must import and parse BAPLIE EDI messages conforming to both SMDG BAPLIE version 2.1 and version 3.0 standards. The parser must extract all container stowage positions using the standard bay-row-tier notation (e.g., 0100284 representing bay 01, row 02, tier 84), container identification (ISO 6346 format), container size and type code (ISO 6346 type codes), gross weight and VGM, port of loading, port of discharge, final destination, hazardous cargo indicators with IMDG class, reefer settings including temperature and ventilation, over-dimensional measurements (over-height, over-width, over-length), and special stowage instructions. The parser must handle BAPLIE messages up to 50,000 container positions per vessel. Parsing errors must be logged with the specific segment and element that failed validation, and partial imports must be supported with error containers quarantined for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R002	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Message Export and Generation	The system must generate BAPLIE EDI messages in both SMDG version 2.1 and version 3.0 formats for transmission to terminals, shipping lines, and port authorities. Generated messages must include complete stowage information for all containers on board the vessel at the point of export. The system must support generation of full BAPLIE (entire vessel) and partial BAPLIE (specific bays or port-of-discharge-specific subsets). Export must be triggered manually by the vessel planner or automatically upon stowage plan finalization. Generated BAPLIE files must pass validation against the SMDG implementation guide and EDIFACT syntax rules. The system must maintain version control of all generated BAPLIE messages with the ability to compare differences between successive versions. Export formats must include raw EDI, XML representation, and human-readable PDF. Transmission must be supported via SFTP, AS2, email attachment, and direct API push to terminal operating systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R003	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Visual Bay Plan Rendering Engine	The system must provide a graphical bay plan visualization that renders the vessel's container stowage arrangement in a two-dimensional cross-section view and a three-dimensional perspective view. The bay plan display must show each container cell with color coding configurable by attribute: port of discharge, container type, weight category, hazard class, reefer status, or operator/shipping line. The visualization must accurately represent the vessel's physical structure including cell guides, hatch covers, deck and hold separation, lashing bridges, and non-standard cell configurations. Users must be able to zoom into individual bays, rotate the 3D view, and click on any container cell to view detailed container information in a popup panel. The rendering engine must support vessels up to 24,000 TEU capacity and render the full bay plan within 3 seconds. Both LTR and RTL display orientations must be supported with all labels and legends properly mirrored for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R004	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Vessel Profile and Cell Configuration Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive vessel profile database defining the physical cell configuration for each vessel. Vessel profiles must include IMO number, vessel name, vessel operator, total TEU capacity (nominal and effective), number of bays, number of rows per bay, number of tiers per bay (deck and hold separately), cell dimensions and weight limits per tier, hatch cover positions, lashing bridge positions, cell guide types, reefer plug locations and power capacity, hazmat-approved cell locations, and ballast tank positions. The system must support vessels of all common types including cellular container vessels, multi-purpose vessels with container capacity, and ro-ro vessels with container deck space. Vessel profiles must be importable from BAPLIE STIF (Ship Technical Information File) format and manually editable by authorized vessel planners. Profile changes must be version-controlled with effective dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R005	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Comparison and Discrepancy Detection	The system must compare incoming BAPLIE messages (typically received from the previous port or the vessel) against the expected stowage plan and highlight discrepancies. Comparison must detect containers present in the BAPLIE but not expected (stowaways or unreported loads), containers expected but missing from the BAPLIE (potential short-shipments), containers in different positions than planned (restows or position shifts), and weight discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds. Discrepancy reports must be generated automatically upon BAPLIE import and presented in both summary and detail views. Each discrepancy must be categorized by severity: critical (missing or extra containers), major (wrong position affecting stability), and minor (data field differences). The system must support a reconciliation workflow where operators can acknowledge, investigate, or escalate each discrepancy. Reconciliation status must be tracked and reported per vessel call at each port (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INJNP).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R006	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Data Validation and Quality Checks	The system must perform comprehensive data quality validation on all imported and exported BAPLIE messages. Validation checks must include: ISO 6346 container number check digit verification, valid bay-row-tier position codes against the vessel profile, container type code validation against ISO 6346 size type codes, port code validation against UN/LOCODE database, weight value range checks (minimum 2 tonnes, maximum based on container type), IMDG class code validation against current IMDG amendment, reefer temperature range validation by cargo type, duplicate container number detection within the same vessel, and position conflict detection (two containers assigned to the same cell). Each validation failure must be logged with error code, severity level, and the specific data element that failed. The system must calculate an overall quality score (0-100) for each BAPLIE message. Messages with critical validation failures must be quarantined until corrections are applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R007	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Version History and Change Tracking	The system must maintain a complete version history of BAPLIE messages for each vessel voyage at each port of call. Each version must be stored with a timestamp, source (terminal, shipping line, vessel, internal planner), message type (pre-arrival, actual arrival, post-departure), and version sequence number. The system must provide a diff view comparing any two versions of the BAPLIE for the same vessel and port, highlighting containers that were added, removed, or repositioned between versions. Change statistics must summarize the total number of changes, number of restows, number of new loads, and number of discharges between versions. The version history must be retained for a minimum of 5 years per regulatory requirements across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Storage must be optimized using delta compression so that only changes between versions are stored rather than full copies. Version history must be searchable by container number, voyage, port, and date range with support for Hijri calendar dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R008	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Integration with Terminal Operating Systems	The system must integrate BAPLIE data exchange with terminal operating systems (TOS) at each port of operation. Integration must support common TOS platforms including Navis N4 (used at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port), TOPS (commonly used at JNPT), and other TOS systems deployable at Hamad Port. BAPLIE exchange must be bidirectional: outbound BAPLIE transmitting the planned stowage to the terminal, and inbound BAPLIE receiving the actual stowage from the terminal after vessel operations. Integration protocols must support real-time API-based exchange, scheduled batch file exchange via SFTP, and manual file upload/download. The system must handle TOS-specific BAPLIE extensions and non-standard fields by mapping them to internal data structures. Connection health monitoring must verify TOS availability every 5 minutes and alert operations staff if integration failures persist beyond 15 minutes. All data exchanged must be logged for reconciliation and dispute resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R009	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Hatch Cover and Deck Tier Weight Compliance	The system must validate that container weights within the BAPLIE do not exceed structural limits for hatch covers, deck tiers, hold tiers, and individual cell positions as defined in the vessel profile. Hatch cover weight limits must be checked by aggregating the total weight of all containers stowed on the hatch cover of each bay. Deck tier stack weight limits must be validated by summing container weights from the top tier down through each tier to the deck or hatch cover. Hold tier stack weight limits must similarly be validated from the top hold tier down to the tank top. Lashing strength calculations must consider the combined weight and height of deck container stacks. The system must generate warnings at 90% of structural limits and hard errors at 100%. Overweight conditions must be highlighted in red on the visual bay plan with specific cells and weight values displayed. Validation must be recalculated in real time as containers are added, removed, or repositioned in the stowage plan.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R010	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Hazardous Cargo Position Validation	The system must validate the stowage positions of all hazardous cargo within the BAPLIE against IMDG Code stowage and segregation requirements. Validation must check stowage category compliance (on deck only, under deck only, or any position), segregation distances between incompatible hazard classes per the IMDG segregation table (away from, separated from, separated by a complete compartment, separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment), and special stowage provisions referenced by the specific UN number. The system must also validate that hazardous containers are not stowed in positions adjacent to accommodation or engine room boundaries where prohibited. For class 1 (explosives) cargo, the system must enforce magazine stowage rules and quantity distance calculations. Validation results must be presented as a compliance report with each violation linked to the specific IMDG Code reference. The system must support override by a certified DG officer with mandatory documentation of the deviation rationale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R011	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Reefer Container Position and Power Validation	The system must validate that all reefer containers in the BAPLIE are assigned to positions with reefer power plug availability as defined in the vessel profile. Validation must verify that the total number of reefer containers per bay does not exceed the bay's reefer plug capacity, that reefer containers requiring higher power draw (e.g., controlled atmosphere or deep-freeze units below -25°C) are assigned to positions with adequate electrical capacity, and that reefer containers are not stowed in positions where airflow is obstructed by over-dimensional cargo. The system must maintain a reefer power capacity map for each vessel showing total plugs, occupied plugs, and available plugs by bay. When reefer capacity is exhausted in a bay, the system must suggest alternative bays with available capacity. Reefer position validation must also check that monitoring access routes are maintained per vessel operator requirements. The reefer capacity utilization must be displayed on the bay plan visualization with color-coded indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R012	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Over-Dimensional Cargo BAPLIE Handling	The system must handle over-dimensional (OOG - out of gauge) cargo within the BAPLIE including over-height, over-width, and over-length containers loaded on flat racks, open tops, or platforms. Over-dimensional measurements must be recorded as excess dimensions beyond standard container dimensions: over-height in centimeters above 2591mm (for standard) or 2896mm (for high cube), over-width in centimeters beyond 2438mm on port and starboard sides, and over-length in centimeters beyond 6058mm (20ft) or 12192mm (40ft) fore and aft. The system must validate that over-dimensional cargo does not conflict with adjacent container positions by checking clearance against the vessel cell guide structure. Blocked cells (positions rendered unusable by OOG cargo) must be automatically marked as unavailable in the bay plan. The system must calculate the TEU loss caused by each OOG container and include this in capacity utilization reporting. OOG cargo must be highlighted with distinctive visual indicators on the bay plan display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R013	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE-Based Port Productivity Analysis	The system must analyze BAPLIE data to calculate port productivity metrics for vessel operations at each port of call. Metrics must include planned discharge moves, planned loading moves, planned restow moves, estimated crane intensity (containers per crane per hour), estimated vessel turnaround time, and crane split plan recommendations. The system must compare pre-arrival BAPLIE with post-departure BAPLIE to calculate actual moves and compare against planned. Productivity analysis must identify operational inefficiencies such as excessive restows (threshold configurable, default >5% of total moves), under-utilized crane windows, and sub-optimal discharge sequencing. Reports must be generated per vessel call and aggregated monthly per port (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INJNP) and per entity. All reports must support bilingual output (Arabic/English) and dual calendar display (Gregorian/Hijri). Analysis must be available within 2 hours of vessel departure from the port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R014	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Archival and Regulatory Compliance Storage	The system must archive all BAPLIE messages exchanged for each vessel call at each port in compliance with regulatory retention requirements. Qatar Maritime Transport regulations, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping each have specific retention requirements; the system must enforce the longest applicable period which is currently 7 years. Archived BAPLIE messages must be stored in their original EDI format plus a parsed JSON representation for rapid query access. Archives must be searchable by vessel IMO number, vessel name, voyage number, port of call, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), container number, and bill of lading number. The system must support regulatory authority requests for BAPLIE retrieval with a maximum response time of 24 hours. Archived data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and access-controlled with role-based permissions. Archive storage must be designed for cost optimization using tiered storage (hot/warm/cold) based on data age.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R015	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	Multi-Port Rotation BAPLIE Sequence Management	The system must manage BAPLIE data across a vessel's full port rotation, maintaining the evolving stowage state as containers are discharged and loaded at each successive port. The system must track the planned stowage for each port in the rotation sequence and automatically update downstream port stowage plans when changes occur at upstream ports. For example, if a restow at Jebel Ali (AEJEA) moves a Hamad-Port-discharge container to a different position, the arrival BAPLIE for Hamad Port (QAHMD) must be updated accordingly. The system must provide a voyage-level BAPLIE timeline view showing the planned vessel stowage state at each port of call. Rotation sequences must support complex multi-port itineraries with up to 20 port calls per voyage. The system must detect and alert when stowage changes at one port create conflicts (weight, hazmat segregation, reefer capacity) at subsequent ports. Cross-port stowage optimization recommendations must be generated by the AI engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R016	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Anomaly Detection Using AI	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to identify unusual patterns in BAPLIE data that may indicate data quality issues, operational errors, or security concerns. Anomaly detection must cover: containers with implausible weight values relative to their declared cargo type, containers stowed in positions inconsistent with their port of discharge (requiring unnecessary restows), sudden changes in vessel utilization patterns compared to historical norms, containers with mismatched size/type codes versus their stowage position dimensions, and undeclared hazardous cargo indicators (unusual weight patterns for declared general cargo). The AI model must be trained on historical BAPLIE data from a minimum of 12 months of vessel operations across all four port jurisdictions. Detected anomalies must be classified by confidence level (high, medium, low) and routed to the appropriate team (operations, safety, security) for investigation. The system must achieve a false positive rate below 10% for high-confidence anomalies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-003-R017	FEAT-011-2-003	MOD-011	BAPLIE Data Access Control and Entity Separation	The system must enforce strict data access controls for BAPLIE information ensuring that users from each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) can only access BAPLIE data relevant to their operational scope. Access rules must be configurable by entity, port, vessel operator, and trade lane. Users with cross-entity roles (e.g., regional vessel planners) must be grantable multi-entity access through explicit role assignment. BAPLIE data shared between entities for transshipment coordination must be limited to the specific containers relevant to the transshipment and must not expose the full vessel stowage to non-authorized entities. Audit logging must record every BAPLIE data access including view, download, and export actions. Access control configurations must be reviewable by entity compliance officers. The system must support data sovereignty requirements where BAPLIE data for vessels calling at Indian ports must be stored on infrastructure located within India per local data localization regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R001	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	AI-Powered Stowage Plan Generation	The system must provide an AI-powered stowage planning engine that automatically generates optimized container placement plans for vessel loading operations. The engine must accept inputs including the vessel profile (cell configuration, weight limits, reefer capacity), current on-board stowage (from BAPLIE), the loading list for the current port, and the discharge plan for subsequent ports in the rotation. Optimization objectives must include: minimize restows at subsequent ports, maximize vessel utilization (TEU and weight), ensure compliance with all structural limits, maintain vessel stability within IMO-mandated parameters, respect hazardous cargo segregation rules, and optimize crane work distribution for port productivity. The engine must generate a complete stowage plan within 5 minutes for vessels up to 24,000 TEU capacity. Multiple alternative plans must be generated (minimum 3) ranked by overall optimization score for the planner to select.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R002	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Vessel Stability Calculation and Compliance	The system must calculate vessel stability parameters in real time as containers are placed in the stowage plan. Stability calculations must include displacement, draft (fore, aft, and midship), trim, metacentric height (GM), righting lever curve (GZ curve), still water bending moment (SWBM), still water shear force (SWSF), and free surface corrections for ballast and fuel tanks. Calculations must comply with the vessel's approved stability booklet and IMO International Convention on Load Lines requirements. The system must enforce minimum GM thresholds (configurable per vessel, default 0.15m for departure condition) and maximum trim limits. Wind heeling moment calculations must be included for deck-heavy loading conditions. The system must display a real-time stability dashboard showing all parameters with color-coded indicators: green (within safe limits), amber (approaching limits at 90%), and red (limit exceeded). Stability calculations must be verified against the vessel's class-approved loading computer values with acceptable variance below 2%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R003	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Restow Minimization Across Port Rotation	The system must optimize stowage plans to minimize the total number of restow (shift/overstow) movements across all ports in the vessel's rotation. The AI engine must analyze the port rotation sequence and discharge volumes at each port to determine the optimal vertical and horizontal placement strategy that avoids containers destined for later ports being stowed above containers destined for earlier ports. Restow predictions must be calculated for each proposed stowage plan and presented to the planner as a restow report showing the expected restow count, restow locations (bay, row, tier), and the reason for each restow. The system must target a restow ratio below 5% of total moves per port call (configurable threshold). When restows cannot be avoided due to capacity constraints, the system must prioritize minimizing the number of crane movements per restow. Restow cost estimates must be calculated using port-specific terminal handling rates in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) to provide financial context for stowage decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R004	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Weight Distribution and Stack Weight Optimization	The system must optimize the vertical distribution of container weights within each bay to ensure heavier containers are placed in lower tiers and lighter containers in upper tiers, while respecting the specific stack weight limits defined in the vessel profile. Stack weight calculations must account for the cumulative weight from the top container down through each tier to the deck or tank top. The system must enforce tier-specific maximum stack weights for both hold and deck stowage separately. For deck containers, lashing force calculations must validate that the combined weight and height of the stack does not exceed the lashing system's certified capacity. The optimization must balance weight distribution across the vessel's breadth (port/starboard) to minimize listing and across the vessel's length (fore/aft) to achieve target trim. Weight distribution visualizations must show color-coded stack weights per bay on the bay plan view. Overweight stacks must be automatically flagged and alternative positions suggested by the AI engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R005	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Crane Split Planning and Work Sequencing	The system must generate crane split plans that divide the vessel's loading and discharge operations among available quay cranes at each port. Crane split assignments must designate specific bay ranges for each crane considering crane spacing constraints (minimum 2 bays separation for shore cranes, configurable per terminal), tandem lift capabilities, and crane outreach limitations. The system must sequence loading operations per crane to optimize productivity by grouping containers by yard location to minimize truck travel distance and by discharge port to avoid unnecessary restacking. The AI engine must estimate total port time based on crane split plans using port-specific crane productivity rates (moves per hour) for each terminal at QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, and INJNP. Alternative crane split configurations must be generated when the primary plan yields suboptimal port time. Crane split plans must be exportable to terminal operating systems in standard EDI format and as visual PDF reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R006	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Hazardous Cargo Stowage Compliance Enforcement	The system must enforce all IMDG Code stowage and segregation requirements when placing hazardous containers in the stowage plan. Stowage category validation must ensure containers are placed on deck, under deck, or in any position as specified by their UN number stowage category. Segregation validation must enforce distance requirements between incompatible hazard classes: 'away from' (minimum one container space or one bulkhead), 'separated from' (minimum one hold or one container space on deck), 'separated by a complete compartment from' (minimum one bulkhead and one hold), and 'separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment' (fore/aft separation). The system must account for closed versus open freight containers in segregation distance calculations. Special provisions (SP) from the IMDG Dangerous Goods List must be validated for each UN number. The system must generate a DG stowage compliance certificate upon plan finalization listing all hazardous containers with their stowage positions and confirmation of compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R007	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Reefer Stowage Planning with Power Management	The system must optimize reefer container placement ensuring all reefer containers are assigned to positions with power plug availability and adequate electrical capacity. The stowage plan must consider the vessel's total reefer power capacity and individual bay-level plug counts from the vessel profile. Reefer containers must be grouped by temperature range to facilitate monitoring: frozen cargo (below -18°C), chilled cargo (-5°C to +5°C), and banana/tropical cargo (+12°C to +14°C). Controlled atmosphere (CA) containers requiring additional power and monitoring must be assigned to designated CA-capable positions. The system must validate that total reefer power demand does not exceed generator capacity for any voyage leg. Reefer placement must also ensure monitoring access walkways between reefer stacks remain passable per vessel operator safety requirements. The reefer stowage plan must include a reefer manifest showing container number, set temperature, commodity, and stowage position, exportable as a PDF for the vessel's chief engineer and in both Arabic and English for port authority inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R008	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Over-Dimensional Cargo Stowage Planning	The system must support stowage planning for over-dimensional (OOG) cargo including flat racks, open tops, and platform containers with excess height, width, or length. The stowage planner must input over-dimensional measurements (excess centimeters on each side: top, port, starboard, fore, aft) and the system must automatically calculate which adjacent cells are blocked by the protruding cargo. Blocked cells must be marked as unavailable for standard container stowage in the bay plan. The system must validate that over-dimensional cargo is placed in positions where cell guide structures can accommodate the excess dimensions. Over-height cargo must be stowed only in the top tier of a stack or in bays without overhead obstructions. Over-width cargo must have verified clearance against adjacent stacks and ship-side structures. The system must calculate the total capacity loss (in TEU) caused by OOG cargo placements. The AI engine must suggest optimal OOG positions that minimize capacity loss while maintaining access for securing with lashing equipment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R002	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	AI-Driven Supplier Selection and RFQ Generation	The system must automatically generate Request for Quotation documents to pre-approved bunker suppliers based on AI analysis of historical pricing data, supplier reliability scores, credit terms, port coverage, and fuel quality track records. The AI engine must rank suppliers using a weighted scoring algorithm considering at minimum: price competitiveness (30%), delivery reliability (25%), quality consistency (20%), credit terms (15%), and sustainability compliance (10%). RFQs must be generated in both English and Arabic for regional suppliers, and the system must support simultaneous dispatch to a minimum of five suppliers per bunkering port with configurable response deadlines in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R009	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Plan Manual Adjustment and Override	The system must allow vessel planners to manually adjust the AI-generated stowage plan by dragging and dropping containers to alternative positions in the visual bay plan editor. Each manual adjustment must trigger real-time revalidation of all constraints: structural weight limits, stability parameters, hazardous cargo segregation, reefer power availability, and over-dimensional clearances. The system must immediately display warning or error indicators if the manual placement violates any constraint. Planners must be able to override warnings with documented justification, but hard constraint violations (e.g., exceeding maximum stack weight by more than 5% or critical IMDG segregation violations) must require supervisor authorization. The system must maintain a complete history of all manual adjustments showing the original AI position, the planner's chosen position, the planner's identity and entity affiliation, and the timestamp. Undo and redo functionality must be supported for all manual adjustments with at least 50 steps of history. The system must recalculate the overall optimization score after each manual change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R010	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Plan Simulation and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a simulation mode allowing planners to explore what-if scenarios without affecting the active stowage plan. Simulations must support scenarios such as: adding or removing containers from the loading list, changing the port rotation sequence, adjusting vessel ballast conditions, modifying crane availability at a port, and evaluating the impact of expected weather conditions on stability margins. Each simulation must produce a full set of results including capacity utilization, stability parameters, restow count, estimated port time, and optimization score. Planners must be able to compare up to four simulation scenarios side by side with visual diff highlighting. Simulation results must be savable as named scenarios and shareable with colleagues across entities. The system must clearly distinguish simulation mode from live planning mode with prominent visual indicators to prevent accidental application of simulation data. Simulation runs must complete within 3 minutes for vessels up to 24,000 TEU.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R011	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Plan Approval Workflow	The system must implement a multi-step approval workflow for stowage plans before they are transmitted to the terminal for execution. The workflow must include stages: draft (planner working), submitted (ready for review), reviewed (checked by senior planner), approved (authorized for execution), and transmitted (sent to terminal). Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must be able to configure its own approval chain specifying required roles at each stage. Mandatory validation checks must be executed at submission time including stability compliance, structural limits, IMDG compliance, reefer capacity, and alignment with the finalized loading list. Failed validation checks must block plan submission until resolved. Approvers must be able to add comments, request modifications, or reject plans with reason codes. The system must notify planners of approval status changes in real time via in-app notification and email. Approval timestamps and approver identities must be recorded in an immutable audit trail. Emergency expedited approval paths must be configurable for time-critical vessel operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R012	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Optimization for Transshipment Efficiency	The system must optimize stowage positions for transshipment cargo to minimize handling time at hub ports. Containers destined for transshipment at hub ports (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP) must be placed in positions that allow direct discharge without restowing other containers. The AI engine must analyze feeder vessel schedules and connection windows at each hub to prioritize transshipment cargo that has tight connection deadlines. Transshipment containers should be grouped by feeder vessel connection to enable efficient block discharge. The system must calculate the projected handling sequence at each transshipment hub and flag stowage positions that would cause connection risks due to late discharge sequencing. Transshipment optimization must balance with the overall vessel optimization (stability, weight distribution, capacity) using configurable priority weights. The system must generate a transshipment efficiency report per voyage showing projected versus actual handling times at each hub port, enabling continuous improvement of the optimization algorithm.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R013	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Ballast Planning Integration with Stowage	The system must integrate ballast water planning with stowage planning to ensure optimal vessel stability and compliance with the IMO Ballast Water Management Convention. As the stowage plan is developed, the system must calculate required ballast water quantities and tank configurations to maintain target trim and stability parameters at departure, during the voyage (accounting for fuel consumption), and at each intermediate port of call. Ballast tank filling and emptying sequences must be generated to accompany the stowage plan. The system must validate that ballast operations at each port comply with the vessel's ballast water management plan, considering ballast water exchange zones and treatment system capacity. Ballast quantities must be factored into displacement and draft calculations displayed on the stability dashboard. When stowage changes affect ballast requirements, the system must automatically recalculate and alert the planner if the new ballast condition exceeds tank capacity or creates stability concerns. Ballast water exchange requirements for ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India territorial waters must be tracked per local maritime regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R014	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Planning Performance Metrics and Reporting	The system must track and report stowage planning performance metrics including average planning time per vessel, optimization score distribution, percentage of plans requiring manual override, restow ratio achieved versus target, stability margin utilization, capacity utilization by TEU and weight, reefer slot utilization, and hazmat compliance pass rate on first submission. Metrics must be segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), by port of loading (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INJNP), by vessel size category, and by trade lane. Historical trending must cover at least 18 rolling months with comparative analysis against industry benchmarks. Individual planner performance dashboards must be available to supervisors showing each planner's efficiency and quality metrics. All reports must be available in Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date display options. Financial impact metrics must show restow costs avoided (in USD and local currencies), capacity gains from optimization, and estimated fuel savings from trim optimization. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R015	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Special Container Type Stowage Rules	The system must enforce stowage rules specific to special container types beyond standard dry containers. Tank containers (ISO type T) must be stowed in positions with spill containment and must not be placed above food-grade cargo. Open top containers (UT) must not be stowed below deck unless empty and must account for rain protection when stowed on deck. Flat rack containers (PL) must be in positions accessible for lashing and securing equipment. Garment-on-hanger (GOH) containers must be stowed to avoid excessive heeling and rolling forces. Flexitank containers must be stowed in bottom tier only to prevent structural failure and must not be double-stacked. Livestock containers must be positioned with adequate ventilation and must not be adjacent to hazardous or reefer cargo. The system must maintain a configurable rules engine allowing authorized users to add or modify special stowage rules by container type code. Each rule must reference its regulatory or operational source. Rule violations must generate specific error messages citing the applicable regulation or standard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R016	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Plan Template and Pattern Library	The system must support creation and management of stowage plan templates based on recurring trade patterns. Planners must be able to save a completed stowage plan as a template linked to a specific vessel, trade lane, and port rotation. Templates must capture preferred bay allocations by port of discharge, standard weight distribution patterns, reefer zone designations, hazmat-approved position clusters, and crane split configurations. When creating a new stowage plan for a matching vessel and trade lane, the system must offer applicable templates as starting points for the AI optimization engine. Template effectiveness must be tracked by comparing optimization scores and restow ratios of template-based plans versus fully optimized plans. Templates must be version-controlled and entity-specific, allowing each entity to maintain its own template library. Template sharing across entities must be supported with explicit authorization. The system must recommend template retirement when a template's performance drops below the AI-generated plan by more than a configurable threshold (default 10% on optimization score).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R017	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Real-Time Stowage Plan Collaboration	The system must support real-time collaborative stowage planning where multiple planners can work on the same vessel stowage plan simultaneously. Collaborative features must include real-time cursor visibility showing each planner's active area on the bay plan, cell-level locking to prevent conflicting edits to the same container position, an integrated chat panel for planner communication within the stowage plan context, and change notifications when a collaborating planner modifies a position. The system must support cross-entity collaboration where a planner from the UAE entity and a planner from the Qatar entity work together on a vessel transiting both ports. Edit permissions must be controllable by entity and port scope, allowing planners to only modify positions relevant to their port's discharge and loading operations. Collaborative session history must be recorded with all changes attributed to the specific planner. The system must handle network latency gracefully and support a minimum of 5 concurrent planners on a single stowage plan. All collaborative interface elements must support both Arabic RTL and English LTR display modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-2-004-R018	FEAT-011-2-004	MOD-011	Stowage Plan Offline Planning Mode	The system must provide an offline stowage planning capability for use aboard vessels or at locations without reliable internet connectivity. Offline mode must pre-load the vessel profile, current BAPLIE, loading list, IMDG segregation tables, and stability calculation parameters onto the local device. Planners must be able to create and modify stowage plans, run stability calculations, validate IMDG compliance, and generate crane split plans entirely offline. The offline planning engine must replicate the core AI optimization algorithms with a performance target of generating optimized plans within 8 minutes for vessels up to 15,000 TEU on standard laptop hardware. Upon reconnection, the offline stowage plan must synchronize with the central server. Conflict resolution for concurrent modifications (offline vessel planner versus onshore planner) must present both versions for supervisor arbitration. Offline data packages must be prepared and downloadable within 5 minutes and must not exceed 2GB per vessel. The system must validate offline plan integrity upon synchronization using checksum verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R001	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Vessel Bay-Level Stowage Optimization	The system must generate AI-optimized stowage plans at the bay, row, and tier level for each vessel voyage, considering container weight distribution across all bays to maintain vessel GM (metacentric height) within safe operating limits. The optimization algorithm must process standard 20ft (TEU) and 40ft (FEU) containers including 40ft high-cube units, accounting for stack weight limits per cell guide. The engine must support vessel profiles for feeder vessels (500-2000 TEU), intra-regional vessels (2000-8000 TEU), and mainline vessels (8000-24000 TEU) commonly operating between Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA). Plans must be regenerated within 90 seconds for vessels up to 14000 TEU capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R002	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Hazardous Cargo Segregation Compliance	The AI load optimizer must enforce IMDG Code segregation rules for all nine hazardous cargo classes and their subdivisions when placing containers in the stowage plan. The system must validate segregation distances (away from, separated from, separated by a complete compartment) between incompatible hazmat classes both horizontally and vertically within the vessel. For Qatar QCDD regulations and UAE NCEMA requirements, additional buffer distances must be enforced beyond standard IMDG minimums. The engine must flag any booking combination that would make full vessel loading impossible due to segregation conflicts and suggest re-sequencing across the next two port rotations. All segregation violations must be logged with IMO class references for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R003	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Reefer Container Plug Allocation Optimization	The system must optimize reefer container placement to maximize reefer plug utilization across designated reefer bays while maintaining temperature monitoring accessibility. The engine must track available reefer plugs per bay and tier, distinguishing between standard reefer connections and high-power connections required for controlled atmosphere (CA) units. For perishable cargo moving through JNPT and Jebel Ali, the system must prioritize reefer placement in bays closest to vessel power generation to reduce voltage drop. The optimizer must consider reefer container temperature set-points ranging from -30°C to +30°C and group containers with similar temperature requirements in adjacent slots to improve energy efficiency. Plug availability must be recalculated in real-time as bookings are confirmed or cancelled.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R006	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Out-of-Gauge and Breakbulk Load Planning	The optimizer must handle out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo including over-width, over-height, and over-length containers that require adjacent slot reservations for clearance. The system must identify and reserve blocked slots (slots rendered unusable due to OOG cargo protrusion) and account for these in overall vessel utilization calculations. For flat-rack and open-top containers carrying project cargo common in KSA construction shipments, the engine must validate lashing requirements and calculate additional securing material needs. The AI must suggest optimal placement for OOG units considering crane reach limitations at each port of call, particularly for Hamad Port and JNPT where specific berth crane configurations may restrict heavy-lift operations to certain bays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R007	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Real-Time Booking Integration and Plan Updates	The load optimization engine must integrate with the booking management module to receive real-time booking confirmations, amendments, and cancellations, automatically recalculating the stowage plan within 60 seconds of any booking change. The system must handle late bookings received up to the vessel cut-off time at each port (typically 24-48 hours before ETD) and determine whether the booking can be accommodated without violating stability, weight, or segregation constraints. When a booking modification changes container type (e.g., from standard dry to reefer), the engine must trigger a targeted re-optimization of the affected bay rather than recomputing the entire vessel plan. All plan versions must be stored with timestamps and user attribution for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R008	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Multi-Entity Load Plan Collaboration	The system must support collaborative load planning across multiple legal entities operating under the ERP (Qatar entity, UAE entity, KSA entity, India entity) where each entity contributes bookings to a shared vessel voyage. The load optimizer must consolidate bookings from all entities while maintaining cargo ownership attribution for revenue allocation and cost sharing. Access control must ensure that each entity's operations team can view the full stowage plan but can only modify stowage positions for their own bookings unless granted cross-entity override permissions. Plan approval workflows must route through each entity's operations manager when changes affect more than 5% of their allocated slot capacity on the voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R009	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	AI Model Training and Optimization Feedback Loop	The system must implement a continuous learning mechanism where actual vessel loading outcomes (actual restow counts, stability readings at departure, crane productivity rates) are fed back into the AI optimization model to improve future load plan quality. The feedback loop must capture deviations between planned and actual stowage positions with reason codes (operational constraint, terminal equipment limitation, late cargo arrival, shipper request). Historical optimization performance data must be retained for a minimum of 24 months and used to calibrate port-specific adjustment factors for each terminal in the network including Hamad Port container terminal, Jebel Ali terminals 1-4, Khalifa Port container terminal, and JNPT terminals NSIGT and BMCT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R010	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Offline Load Plan Generation and Sync	The load optimization engine must support offline operation mode for vessel planners working aboard vessels or at terminals with intermittent connectivity, common during anchorage operations at JNPT or during port congestion at Jebel Ali. The offline mode must cache the current vessel profile, active bookings, and constraint parameters locally, enabling load plan generation and manual adjustments without server connectivity. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline changes with the central system using conflict resolution logic that flags any booking changes that occurred during the offline period. The offline-generated plan must be validated against current bookings upon reconnection, with discrepancies highlighted for planner review before the plan is committed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R011	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Arabic RTL Interface for Stowage Plan Visualization	The stowage plan visualization interface must fully support Arabic RTL layout, with bay plan views, container detail panels, and optimization parameter controls correctly mirrored for Arabic-speaking vessel planners. Container information overlays must display cargo descriptions in both Arabic and English simultaneously where bilingual data is available, particularly for cargo manifests originating from Qatar and KSA shippers. Navigation elements including bay selectors, deck/hold toggles, and zoom controls must maintain correct positioning in RTL mode. The color-coding legend for container status (booked, loaded, confirmed, hazmat, reefer) must use culturally appropriate color associations and include text labels in the active language. All printed stowage plan outputs must support both A3 landscape and A4 portrait formats with Arabic headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R012	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Vessel Stability Limit Override with Authorization	The system must allow authorized senior operations managers to override AI-recommended stability limits in exceptional circumstances, such as emergency cargo loading requests from government entities in Qatar or KSA. Each override must require two-factor authorization: the requesting manager's approval plus confirmation from a designated marine superintendent. The override must be time-limited (maximum 4 hours from approval) and automatically reverts the plan to standard limits upon expiration. All overrides must be logged with the override justification, the original AI recommendation, the modified parameters, and both authorizing parties' identities. Monthly override reports must be generated for the QHSSE (Quality, Health, Safety, Security, Environment) department showing override frequency, magnitude of deviation from standard limits, and correlation with any reported incidents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R013	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Container Weight Verification Integration	The load optimizer must integrate with SOLAS VGM (Verified Gross Mass) data for every container in the stowage plan, rejecting containers that lack a valid VGM declaration from loading into the plan. The system must validate VGM values against booking declared weights with a configurable tolerance threshold (default 5%) and flag significant discrepancies for investigation. For containers weighed at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT terminal scales, the system must accept terminal-certified weights as VGM source (weighing method 1). The AI optimizer must use VGM data rather than booking declared weights for all stability and stack weight calculations to ensure real-world accuracy. Containers with VGM exceeding the maximum payload for their ISO type/size code must be automatically excluded from the load plan with notification to the booking entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R014	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Dangerous Goods Stowage Category Mapping	The system must maintain a comprehensive mapping of IMDG stowage categories (A through E for general stowage, and numbered categories 1-15 for specific requirements) to vessel-specific permitted locations (on-deck, under-deck, weather deck, accommodation spaces proximity). The AI engine must enforce stowage category restrictions when assigning positions, ensuring category B cargo (on-deck only in closed containers) is never placed in hold positions and category E cargo (on-deck or under-deck) is placed optimally considering other constraints. The mapping must be configurable per vessel class and must account for flag state variations, particularly for vessels registered in Qatar (QRMA regulations), UAE (FTA maritime requirements), and India (DG Shipping circulars). Updates to IMDG code amendments must be deployable without system downtime.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R015	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Load Plan Export and Terminal Integration	The system must export finalized load plans in BAPLIE (EDIFACT) format version 2.2 and 3.0 for transmission to terminal operating systems at all ports in the rotation. The export must include all container details (booking reference, ISO size/type, weight, hazmat class, reefer settings, POD, special handling codes) and stowage positions in standard bay-row-tier notation. For Hamad Port and JNPT terminals that accept COPARN messages, the system must generate corresponding discharge/load lists. The export function must support BAPLIE validation against the vessel's bay plan template to catch position code errors before transmission. Failed transmissions must trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff and escalation notifications after three failed attempts. A preview mode must allow planners to review the BAPLIE content in a human-readable grid format before export.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R016	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Bunker and Ballast Impact on Cargo Capacity	The AI load optimizer must account for planned bunker fuel quantities (HFO, VLSFO, MGO) and ballast water distribution when calculating available cargo deadweight capacity for each voyage leg. The system must integrate with the bunker planning module to receive fuel consumption projections per voyage leg and calculate remaining cargo capacity at each port of call. For voyages transiting between the Arabian Gulf and Indian subcontinent, the optimizer must factor in ballast water exchange requirements under the BWM Convention when calculating ballast tank availability for trim correction. The system must alert planners when bunker procurement at specific ports (e.g., Fujairah bunker anchorage between Jebel Ali and JNPT) will temporarily reduce cargo capacity below committed booking volumes, enabling proactive rollover decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-001-R017	FEAT-011-3-001	MOD-011	Special Container Type Handling Rules	The load optimizer must enforce specific stowage rules for special container types prevalent in the Qatar-UAE-KSA-India trade lane, including tank containers (ISO type T-codes) carrying chemicals and foodgrade liquids, open-top containers for oversized machinery, and garment-on-hanger (GOH) containers requiring upright stowage. The engine must prevent stacking heavy containers on top of tank containers beyond the tank frame's structural rating and ensure open-top containers are placed in positions where overhead crane operations will not be impeded by adjacent over-height cargo. For livestock containers transiting through Hamad Port or Khalifa Port, the system must enforce ventilation requirements by ensuring minimum clearances on at least two sides. All special handling codes must follow the EDIFACT standard code list and be displayed in both English and Arabic in the stowage plan interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R001	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Per-Voyage Revenue per TEU Calculation	The system must calculate revenue per TEU for each completed voyage by dividing total voyage revenue by total TEU carried (with FEU counted as 2 TEU). Revenue must include base ocean freight, terminal handling charges (THC), bunker adjustment factor (BAF), currency adjustment factor (CAF), peak season surcharges, and any other applicable surcharges collected across all ports in the rotation. The calculation must handle mixed-currency invoicing by converting all revenue to the voyage's base reporting currency (typically USD) using the exchange rate locked at the booking confirmation date. For voyages spanning multiple entities (e.g., Qatar entity collecting QAR freight and India entity collecting INR freight), the system must consolidate cross-entity revenues while maintaining entity-level breakdowns for intercompany reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R002	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Trade Lane Revenue Benchmarking	The system must aggregate revenue per TEU data by trade lane (defined as origin port/region to destination port/region pairs) and provide rolling 4-week, 13-week, and 52-week moving averages for trend analysis. Standard trade lanes must include Jebel Ali–JNPT, Hamad Port–Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port–Dammam, and all permutations of the four hub ports. The benchmarking module must compare each trade lane's revenue per TEU against configurable targets set by the commercial planning team and against industry-published rate indices (SCFI, WCI, FBX) where available. Deviations beyond a configurable threshold (default ±10%) must trigger automated alerts to the commercial manager responsible for that trade lane. All benchmark displays must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date selectors for KSA and Qatar reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R003	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Customer Segment Revenue Analysis	The analytics module must segment revenue per TEU by customer tier (platinum, gold, silver, standard as defined in the CRM module), contract type (spot, short-term contract, long-term contract, MQC commitment), and cargo commodity type (using first two digits of HS code classification). The system must identify the top 20 customers by total revenue contribution and display their individual revenue per TEU trends over the selected period. For contract customers with minimum quantity commitments (MQC), the system must show actual revenue per TEU against the contracted rate and highlight any volume-based rebate thresholds that have been crossed. Segmentation must respect multi-entity boundaries, showing consolidated group-level analysis only to users with cross-entity reporting permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R004	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Revenue Leakage Detection	The system must implement AI-driven anomaly detection to identify potential revenue leakage scenarios including: containers shipped at rates below the applicable tariff minimum, surcharges not applied despite qualifying conditions (e.g., BAF not applied during high fuel price periods), weight-based charges calculated on declared weight rather than VGM-verified weight, and free-time extensions granted without proper commercial authorization. The detection engine must scan all completed voyage revenue data within 48 hours of voyage completion and generate a leakage report quantifying the estimated revenue gap per container in the booking entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). Each detected leakage instance must include a confidence score (0-100%) and root cause classification for the commercial recovery team to action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R005	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Multi-Currency Revenue Consolidation	The revenue analytics engine must consolidate revenue streams collected in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD into a single reporting currency selectable by the user. The system must support three exchange rate methodologies: booking date spot rate, invoice date spot rate, and monthly average rate, with the default methodology configurable per entity. For UAE entities where AED is pegged to USD, the system must use the fixed peg rate (3.6725 AED/USD) rather than market rates. The system must maintain a complete audit trail showing original currency amounts, applied exchange rates, and converted amounts for every revenue line item. Period-over-period comparisons must offer both nominal (current exchange rates) and constant currency (using base period rates) views to isolate operational revenue changes from currency fluctuation effects.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R006	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Container Type Revenue Yield Comparison	The system must calculate and compare revenue per TEU by container type including standard dry (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer (20RE, 40RE, 40RH), open-top (20OT, 40OT), flat-rack (20FR, 40FR), and tank containers (20TK). The analysis must account for the higher operational cost of reefer containers (plug-in electricity, monitoring) and present both gross revenue per TEU and net contribution per TEU after deducting direct variable costs. For special equipment types common in the Gulf trade (e.g., flat-racks for steel coils from India, tank containers for petrochemicals from Qatar), the system must track equipment repositioning costs and include them in the net yield calculation. Results must be exportable in Excel and PDF formats with Arabic and English column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R007	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Voyage Profitability Waterfall Analysis	The system must generate a waterfall chart analysis for each voyage showing the progression from gross revenue per TEU to net profit per TEU, with intermediate steps for: port charges and terminal handling costs, bunker fuel costs allocated per TEU, vessel charter or ownership costs per TEU, canal transit fees (Suez Canal for voyages extending beyond the regional network), agency fees, insurance costs, and administrative overhead allocation. Each cost component must be sourced from the respective ERP module (port charges from disbursement accounts, bunker costs from bunker management, etc.) and allocated to the voyage using approved cost allocation methodologies. The waterfall must support drill-down to individual cost line items and comparison against the previous three voyages on the same rotation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R008	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Spot vs Contract Revenue Mix Dashboard	The analytics module must provide a dashboard showing the revenue mix between spot market bookings and contract bookings for each vessel voyage, trade lane, and time period. The dashboard must display spot rate premiums or discounts relative to contract rates, expressed both as absolute values per TEU and as percentage differentials. For trade lanes where spot rates consistently exceed contract rates by more than 15%, the system must generate a commercial opportunity alert recommending contract rate renegotiation or reallocation of contract space to spot market. The dashboard must support filtering by origin port, destination port, container type, and commodity category. All monetary values must be displayed in the user's preferred currency with the applicable conversion rate visible on hover.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R009	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Revenue per TEU Forecasting Model	The system must provide an AI-driven forecast of revenue per TEU for the next 4, 8, and 12 weeks based on historical revenue patterns, current booking pipeline data, seasonal trends (including Ramadan period demand shifts in Gulf markets, Diwali season volume spikes for India trade), and market rate index movements. The forecast model must generate confidence intervals (80% and 95%) around point estimates and must be re-trained weekly using the latest 52 weeks of actual revenue data. Forecast accuracy must be tracked using MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) with a target below 8% for 4-week forecasts. The forecast output must be available through both the analytics dashboard interface and a REST API endpoint for integration with external financial planning systems used by entity finance teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R010	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Surcharge Revenue Component Breakdown	The system must decompose total revenue per TEU into its component surcharges and display them in a stacked visualization showing the contribution of each surcharge type. Standard surcharges tracked must include: bunker adjustment factor (BAF/LSS), currency adjustment factor (CAF), terminal handling charges at origin and destination (OTHC/DTHC), documentation fees, seal fees, peak season surcharge (PSS), war risk surcharge (applicable for certain Gulf routing), piracy surcharge, and port-specific surcharges such as Hamad Port security surcharge and JNPT infrastructure surcharge. The breakdown must enable period-over-period comparison to identify which surcharge components are driving revenue per TEU changes. Each surcharge must be categorized as either cost-recovery or margin-enhancing for profitability analysis purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R011	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Empty Container Revenue Impact Analysis	The analytics module must quantify the revenue impact of empty container repositioning by calculating the effective revenue per TEU including empty legs. For trade lanes with significant directional imbalance (e.g., westbound India-to-Gulf typically having lower volumes than eastbound Gulf-to-India), the system must compute the round-trip revenue per TEU that accounts for the cost of repositioning empty containers on the return leg. The system must model scenarios where empty repositioning costs are partially offset by triangulation opportunities (picking up export cargo from intermediate ports). This analysis must integrate with the equipment management module to track actual empty repositioning volumes and costs per trade lane, updated weekly. Results must be presented in the voyage's operating currency with cross-entity cost allocation transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R012	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Budget vs Actual Revenue Variance Reporting	The system must produce monthly and quarterly variance reports comparing actual revenue per TEU against budgeted targets set during the annual business planning cycle. Variance must be decomposed into volume variance (difference in TEU count multiplied by budgeted rate) and rate variance (difference in per-TEU rate multiplied by actual volume) to isolate the drivers of over- or under-performance. For each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the report must show variance in local currency and in USD equivalent. The reporting period must support both Gregorian calendar months and Hijri calendar months selectable by the user, with KSA reports defaulting to Hijri periods. Variance reports must be auto-distributed to entity commercial directors via email on the 5th working day of each month following period close.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R013	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Arabic Bilingual Revenue Reports	All revenue per TEU analytics reports and dashboards must be fully available in Arabic with complete RTL layout support including right-aligned numerical tables, Arabic currency formatting (e.g., ر.ق for QAR, د.إ for AED, ر.س for SAR, ₹ for INR), and Arabic month/date names for both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Chart labels, axis titles, legends, and tooltips must render in Arabic when the user's language preference is set to Arabic. PDF exports of revenue reports must support Arabic typography with correct ligature rendering and must include bilingual headers (Arabic primary, English secondary) for reports distributed to government authorities in Qatar (MOPH shipping statistics) and KSA (Saudi Ports Authority reporting). The system must handle mixed-direction text where English port codes (QAHMD, AEJEA) appear within Arabic narrative summaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R014	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Demurrage and Detention Revenue Tracking	The analytics engine must separately track demurrage (charges for container use at port beyond free time) and detention (charges for container use outside port beyond free time) revenue per TEU as distinct revenue categories. The system must calculate average demurrage and detention days by port, customer, and trade lane, and correlate extended equipment usage with revenue yield per container. For Jebel Ali Free Zone and Hamad Port where free-time policies differ between import and export, the system must apply the correct free-time allowance when calculating billable demurrage days. The module must identify chronic late-return customers generating high detention revenue and flag whether the detention revenue compensates for the equipment unavailability cost, supporting decisions on whether to enforce stricter free-time policies or continue accepting the premium revenue stream.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R015	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Revenue Analytics Access Control and Data Segregation	The revenue analytics module must enforce role-based access control ensuring that entity-level commercial staff can only view revenue data for their own entity's bookings and customers. Group-level commercial directors must have consolidated views across all entities with the ability to drill down to entity level. Revenue per TEU data at the individual customer rate level must be classified as confidential and restricted to users with the commercial_revenue_detail permission. API access to revenue analytics data must require OAuth 2.0 authentication with scope-based authorization matching the UI permission model. All revenue data queries must be logged with user identity, timestamp, query parameters, and data scope accessed for compliance with data protection requirements under Qatar Law No. 13 of 2016 on Personal Data Protection and India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-002-R016	FEAT-011-3-002	MOD-011	Real-Time Revenue Dashboard with KPI Alerts	The system must provide a real-time revenue dashboard refreshed every 15 minutes showing current-month revenue per TEU against target, with a traffic-light status indicator (green: within 5% of target, amber: 5-15% below target, red: more than 15% below target). The dashboard must display KPIs including: average revenue per TEU (current month, previous month, year-to-date), total revenue by entity, revenue by top 5 trade lanes, and booking pipeline value for the next 30 days. Configurable threshold-based alerts must be triggered when revenue per TEU drops below entity-specific minimums (set quarterly by entity commercial directors) and sent via in-app notification, email, and optionally SMS for critical alerts. The dashboard must load within 3 seconds for datasets covering up to 200,000 container movements per quarter across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R001	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	AI Demand Forecasting Model	The system must implement a machine learning-based demand forecasting model that predicts TEU volumes by trade lane, container type, and week for a rolling 26-week forecast horizon. The model must incorporate at minimum: 104 weeks of historical booking data, seasonal patterns (Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Diwali, Chinese New Year effects on Gulf-Asia trades), macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth rates for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India), and port-specific capacity constraints. The forecast must be generated separately for laden export, laden import, and empty repositioning flows for each port pair in the network. Model performance must be evaluated weekly against actual bookings, with MAPE tracked per trade lane and a retraining trigger activated when 4-week MAPE exceeds 12% for any major trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R002	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Vessel Capacity Supply Planning	The system must maintain a capacity supply model reflecting planned vessel deployments across all service rotations, including owned vessels, time-chartered vessels, and slot-charter/exchange agreements with alliance partners. The supply model must project available TEU capacity per sailing per port for the next 52 weeks, accounting for planned dry-docking periods, vessel swaps, and extra-loader deployments. When demand forecasts exceed available supply on a specific trade lane by more than 10%, the system must generate capacity gap alerts with recommended actions: deploy additional tonnage, activate slot-purchase agreements, or implement booking restrictions. The supply model must distinguish between nominal vessel capacity and effective capacity (reduced by operational factors such as weight limitations, hazmat segregation losses, and reefer bay utilization).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R003	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Booking Pipeline Analysis and Conversion Tracking	The demand planning module must ingest real-time booking pipeline data including: quotations issued (not yet booked), tentative bookings (awaiting shipper confirmation), and confirmed bookings, to build a probability-weighted demand forecast for each upcoming sailing. The system must assign conversion probability scores to pipeline entries based on historical conversion rates by customer, trade lane, and lead time. For regular customers with weekly or bi-weekly shipping patterns (common for FMCG importers in Qatar and UAE), the system must detect booking pattern deviations and proactively flag potential demand shortfalls. The pipeline analysis must update every 2 hours during business hours (Saturday-Thursday for Gulf entities, Monday-Friday for India entity) and present results in a funnel visualization showing progression from quotation to confirmed booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R004	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Seasonal Demand Pattern Recognition	The AI forecasting engine must identify and model seasonal demand patterns specific to the Gulf-India trade corridor including: pre-Ramadan consumer goods surge (typically 4-6 weeks before Ramadan start, requiring Hijri calendar integration for accurate dating), post-Eid import recovery periods, Indian festival season (September-November) driving increased exports of consumer goods and textiles, summer temperature restrictions affecting certain cargo types through Gulf ports (July-September), and Qatar National Day (December 18) and UAE National Day (December 2) periods affecting port working hours. The system must automatically adjust forecast baselines to account for these patterns and must handle the 10-11 day annual shift in Islamic calendar events relative to the Gregorian calendar. Pattern confidence scores must be displayed alongside forecasts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R005	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Port Capacity Constraint Modeling	The system must model capacity constraints at each port in the network including: berth availability windows, quay crane productivity rates (moves per hour by terminal), yard storage capacity limits, gate throughput capacity, and any regulatory restrictions on vessel size or draught. For Hamad Port, the system must account for the container terminal's maximum vessel capacity and concurrent berth occupancy limits. For JNPT, the model must factor in tidal window restrictions for deep-draught vessels and the capacity differences between NSIGT, BMCT, and APM terminals. The constraint model must feed into the demand-supply matching algorithm to ensure forecast demand does not assume port capacity that is physically unavailable, and must flag port bottleneck risks at least 4 weeks before the projected congestion period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R006	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Dynamic Space Allocation Optimization	The system must recommend optimal space allocation across customer segments (contract vs spot) and cargo types (dry vs reefer vs special) for each upcoming sailing based on forecast demand, contracted commitments, and revenue maximization objectives. The optimizer must ensure that minimum quantity commitment (MQC) contracted volumes are protected while maximizing the revenue yield from remaining spot capacity. When forecast demand exceeds vessel capacity, the system must recommend overbooking levels based on historical no-show and cancellation rates per trade lane and customer segment, with configurable maximum overbooking thresholds (default 105% for dry containers, 100% for reefer containers due to plug constraints). Allocation recommendations must be presented in a matrix view showing slots allocated by container type and customer tier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R007	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Demand Scenario Planning and Simulation	The capacity forecasting module must support what-if scenario planning allowing commercial planners to model the impact of hypothetical demand changes on capacity utilization and revenue. Scenarios must include: new customer onboarding with estimated volumes, loss of a major customer, new trade lane introduction, vessel upsizing or downsizing on an existing rotation, and external market shocks (e.g., port closure, trade sanctions). Each scenario must be modeled independently and comparatively against the baseline forecast, with impact quantified in TEU volume change, revenue per TEU impact (in USD and local currencies), and vessel utilization percentage change. The system must support saving and versioning up to 20 active scenarios per entity, with scenario comparison views showing key metrics side by side.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R008	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Intermodal Capacity Integration	The demand planning system must incorporate inland transportation capacity when forecasting end-to-end supply chain capacity, particularly for door-to-door shipments. For Qatar, the system must model truck capacity between Hamad Port and industrial areas (Mesaieed, Ras Laffan). For UAE, the system must account for truck and rail capacity between Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port and inland container depots (Dubai Logistics City, Al Ain, Abu Dhabi ICD). For India, the system must integrate rail capacity on dedicated freight corridors connecting JNPT to northern India ICDs (Tughlakabad, Dadri, Ahmedabad). The intermodal capacity model must flag when inland transportation constraints risk creating port dwell time increases that effectively reduce terminal capacity, and must include transit time estimates for each inland connection in the capacity planning calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R009	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Demand Forecast Accuracy Tracking and Reporting	The system must produce weekly and monthly forecast accuracy reports measuring deviation between forecasted and actual TEU volumes at the trade lane, port, and container type level. Accuracy metrics must include MAPE, weighted MAPE (by volume), bias (systematic over- or under-forecasting), and tracking signal (cumulative forecast error divided by mean absolute deviation). The reporting must distinguish between forecast errors attributable to demand-side changes (customer behavior, market shifts) and supply-side disruptions (vessel delays, port closures, equipment shortages). For each entity, a monthly forecast review dashboard must be generated showing the top 5 over-forecasted and under-forecasted trade lanes with root cause annotations. The accuracy reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period aggregation and must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R010	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	External Data Source Integration for Demand Signals	The forecasting model must integrate external data sources to enrich demand predictions beyond historical booking patterns. Required external data feeds include: commodity price indices (LME for metals, ICE for petrochemicals) relevant to Gulf export commodities, container throughput statistics published by Hamad Port, DP World, Abu Dhabi Ports, and JNPT on monthly or quarterly basis, purchasing managers index (PMI) data for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and vessel AIS data for tracking fleet deployment changes by competing carriers in the Gulf-India trade. The system must implement automated data ingestion pipelines with data quality validation checks, missing data imputation logic, and staleness alerts when external feeds are not updated within expected refresh intervals. Data source contribution to forecast accuracy must be measurable to justify ongoing data procurement costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R011	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Capacity Allocation Approval Workflow	The system must enforce an approval workflow for capacity allocation decisions that deviate from AI-recommended allocations by more than a configurable threshold (default 10% of total sailing capacity). The workflow must route approval requests to the entity commercial director for single-entity deviations and to the group commercial VP for deviations affecting multi-entity shared vessel capacity. The approval form must present the AI recommendation alongside the proposed manual allocation with a side-by-side comparison of projected revenue impact, utilization change, and customer service impact. Approvals must be time-bound to the specific sailing or sailing range specified in the request. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all allocation decisions, including AI recommendations that were accepted without modification, for quarterly capacity management performance reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R012	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Blank Sailing and Capacity Withdrawal Modeling	The demand planning module must model the impact of blank sailings (cancelled vessel departures) and capacity withdrawals on service reliability and customer commitments. When a blank sailing is proposed, the system must calculate: the number of confirmed bookings that must be rolled to the next sailing, the impact on MQC contract fulfillment for affected customers, the estimated cargo dwell time increase at origin ports, and the cost of potential customer claims for delayed shipment. The model must simulate cascading effects where rolled cargo from a blank sailing creates overbooking on subsequent sailings. For the Gulf-India trade where weekly frequency is standard, the system must assess whether biweekly frequency during low-demand periods (typically June-August) is viable without breaching service level agreements with key contract customers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R013	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Offline Demand Planning Capabilities	The capacity forecasting module must support offline access to forecast data and planning tools for commercial managers traveling or working from locations with limited connectivity, which is common during customer visits to industrial zones in KSA or remote project cargo sites in India. The offline mode must cache the latest forecast data, historical trends, and active allocation plans locally, allowing users to review forecasts, annotate demand assumptions, and draft scenario plans without network access. When connectivity is restored, offline annotations and scenario drafts must synchronize with the central system using last-write-wins conflict resolution for annotations and parallel version preservation for scenarios. The offline data cache must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and must automatically purge cached forecast data after 7 days without server synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R014	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Multi-Entity Demand Consolidation View	The system must provide a consolidated demand forecast view that aggregates predicted volumes from all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for shared vessel services, while maintaining clear attribution of demand to its originating entity. The consolidated view must highlight capacity contention scenarios where combined entity demands exceed vessel capacity and must provide a priority-based resolution recommendation considering entity-specific revenue per TEU, contract obligations, and strategic customer relationships. Inter-entity capacity sharing agreements must be configurable, defining how much of one entity's allocated space can be offered to another entity's overflow bookings. The consolidation must handle time zone differences (Qatar/KSA UTC+3, UAE UTC+4, India UTC+5:30) when aggregating booking pipeline data with deadline-sensitive cutoff times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R015	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Capacity Forecast API for External Partners	The system must expose a RESTful API allowing authorized external partners (feeder operators, slot-charter partners, freight forwarders with allocation agreements) to query available capacity forecasts for specific trade lanes and sailing dates. The API must support authentication via API key and OAuth 2.0, rate limiting (configurable per partner, default 100 requests per hour), and response filtering by port pair, container type, and date range. Capacity data exposed through the API must respect commercial sensitivity rules: partners must only see capacity marked as available-for-sale, not total vessel capacity or internal allocation details. API responses must include available TEU by container type (dry, reefer, special), estimated sailing dates, and port ETAs. The API must support both JSON and XML response formats, with all date fields supporting both Gregorian and Hijri representations upon request parameter specification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R016	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Equipment Demand Forecasting by Type	The demand planning module must forecast container equipment demand by ISO type and size code to support equipment repositioning planning. The forecast must project demand for standard dry (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer (20RE, 40RH), special equipment (flat-rack, open-top, tank), and empty containers required for repositioning at each port in the network for the next 13 weeks. The equipment forecast must account for equipment dwell time at destination (average days from discharge to empty return), equipment maintenance and repair cycles, and leased equipment return deadlines. For ports with chronic equipment imbalances (Jebel Ali typically has surplus empties, JNPT typically has deficit), the system must recommend inter-port repositioning volumes aligned with the vessel deployment schedule. Equipment cost implications must be calculated in the relevant local currency for each port location.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-003-R017	FEAT-011-3-003	MOD-011	Arabic Interface for Demand Planning Dashboards	All demand planning dashboards, forecast charts, scenario planning interfaces, and capacity allocation screens must be fully functional in Arabic with RTL layout support. Numerical data in tables must use Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) as a configurable option alongside Western Arabic numerals, based on entity preference (KSA entities typically preferring Arabic-Indic numerals in formal reports). Forecast period labels must display in Hijri month names (محرم، صفر، ربيع الأول، etc.) when Hijri calendar mode is selected. Interactive elements including date range pickers, dropdown filters, and slider controls for scenario parameters must maintain correct RTL behavior. The system must support Arabic text input for scenario names, demand annotations, and approval comments with proper character shaping and ligature rendering. Tooltip overlays on charts must not clip or overflow when rendered in Arabic due to typically longer text strings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R001	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Vessel Schedule Adherence Measurement	The system must calculate schedule adherence for every port call by comparing actual arrival time (ATA) and actual departure time (ATD) against the published schedule's estimated time of arrival (ETA) and estimated time of departure (ETD). A port call is classified as on-time if the vessel arrives within a configurable window (default ±12 hours) of the published ETA. The system must compute schedule reliability as the percentage of on-time port calls out of total port calls over rolling 4-week, 13-week, and 52-week periods. Reliability must be calculated at the service rotation level, individual port level, and overall network level. For benchmark comparison, the system must track the industry-standard Sea-Intelligence Global Liner Performance report methodology, allowing the company's reliability to be compared against published industry averages for the Gulf-India trade.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R002	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Delay Root Cause Classification and Analysis	The system must capture and categorize delay root causes for every port call where actual arrival or departure deviates from schedule by more than 2 hours. Root cause categories must include: port congestion (berth unavailability), terminal operational delays (crane breakdown, labor shortages), weather delays (shamal dust storms common in Gulf April-June, monsoon disruptions at JNPT June-September), vessel mechanical issues, late cargo operations (documentation delays, customs holds), canal/waterway congestion, and schedule recovery decisions (speed reduction for fuel savings). Each delay event must record the delay duration in hours, the responsible party (terminal, vessel, shipper, authority), and whether the delay was within or outside the company's control. The system must generate Pareto charts showing the top delay causes by cumulative hours lost per quarter for each port in the network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-002-2-001-R015	FEAT-002-2-001	MOD-002	Service mesh SHALL implement load balancing with least-connections algorithm for documentation-service (long-running BL generation) and round-robin for stateless tracking-service endpoints	Service mesh SHALL implement load balancing with least-connections algorithm for documentation-service (long-running BL generation) and round-robin for stateless tracking-service endpoints	should_have			"Documentation-service traffic distributes to instance with fewest active connections; tracking-service traffic distributes evenly across instances; algorithm selection is configurable per service; uneven distribution under load differs by less than 15% for round-robin"	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R003	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Predictive ETA Calculation Engine	The system must implement an AI-driven predictive ETA engine that continuously updates estimated arrival times for vessels en route based on: current vessel position and speed (from AIS data), weather and sea state forecasts along the remaining route, historical port approach and berthing delays at the destination port, and known port congestion levels. The predictive ETA must be more accurate than the static schedule ETA, with a target accuracy of ±4 hours for predictions made 48 hours before arrival and ±2 hours for predictions made 12 hours before arrival. ETA predictions must be generated for all ports remaining in the vessel's rotation and must cascade (a delay at one port automatically adjusts ETAs for subsequent ports). Updated ETAs must be published to the customer portal and transmitted via EDI (IFTMIN/IFTSTA) to customers who subscribe to milestone notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R004	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Port Turnaround Time Tracking	The system must track detailed port turnaround time components for every vessel call including: pilot boarding to berth (approach time), berth to first crane move (crane setup time), first crane move to last crane move (gross crane working time), last crane move to departure from berth (departure preparation time), and berth to pilot disembarkation (departure navigation time). For each component, the system must calculate averages and standard deviations per port terminal, vessel size class, and time of day (day shift vs night shift). The system must identify statistically significant deviations from historical norms (beyond 2 standard deviations) and trigger investigation alerts. For Hamad Port and JNPT where terminal productivity benchmarking is particularly important for contract negotiations, the system must generate monthly terminal performance scorecards comparing actual productivity against contractual guarantees in crane moves per hour.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R005	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Service Rotation Schedule Visualization	The system must provide an interactive schedule visualization showing planned versus actual vessel positions for each service rotation on a geographic map overlaid with a Gantt-style timeline. The visualization must display vessel current positions (from AIS integration), planned port call sequences with ETA/ETD bars, actual port call bars overlaid for completed calls showing on-time or delayed status with color coding, and projected future port calls using predictive ETAs. Users must be able to click on any port call to see detailed timing breakdown, delay causes, and cargo operations statistics. The map must show the primary routing through the Arabian Gulf including the Strait of Hormuz transit and across the Arabian Sea to JNPT. The visualization must support both English and Arabic labels with RTL-compatible tooltip rendering for port names and vessel details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R006	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Schedule Recovery Recommendation Engine	When a vessel falls behind schedule, the system must generate AI-driven schedule recovery recommendations considering multiple levers: increased sailing speed (with associated bunker cost increase calculated in USD per additional knot), reduced port time through operational efficiency measures, port call sequence adjustment (swapping port calls where feasible), and selective port omission as a last resort. Each recommendation must present a cost-benefit analysis showing: additional bunker fuel cost (calculated using current fuel prices at the next bunkering port), estimated time recovery in hours, impact on cargo delivery promises for containers destined for omitted or resequenced ports, and crew rest hour compliance implications under MLC 2006 requirements. Recommendations must be reviewed and approved by the fleet operations manager before execution, with all decisions logged for schedule performance audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R007	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Customer Schedule Reliability Reporting	The system must generate customer-facing schedule reliability reports that can be shared with key accounts showing the company's delivery performance for that customer's shipments. The report must include: percentage of containers delivered within the promised transit time, average delay duration for late deliveries, comparison against the published schedule reliability for each service used by the customer, and trend analysis over the past 6 months. Reports must be automatically generated monthly and distributed to customers with more than 100 TEU per quarter who opt into the reporting service. The report format must support PDF with bilingual Arabic-English content for Gulf-based customers and English-only for India-based customers. Customer-specific reports must only include data for that customer's shipments and must not reveal information about other shippers' cargo or overall vessel utilization levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R008	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Weather Disruption Impact Modeling	The system must integrate weather forecast data to model the potential impact of adverse weather conditions on vessel schedules across the Gulf-India trade network. The model must account for: shamal wind events (northwesterly winds exceeding 30 knots) that can close ports or reduce crane operations in the Arabian Gulf during June-August, monsoon season disruptions at JNPT and Indian west coast ports (June-September) causing vessel waiting time at anchorage, fog events at JNPT during winter months (December-February) affecting port approach navigation, and tropical cyclone tracks in the Arabian Sea that may require route deviation. The system must generate proactive schedule impact assessments at least 72 hours before forecasted weather events, estimating delay duration and affected vessel voyages, enabling early customer notification and contingency cargo planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R009	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Port Congestion Monitoring and Alerting	The system must monitor real-time port congestion levels at all ports in the network by tracking: number of vessels at anchorage awaiting berth (from AIS data analysis), average waiting time from anchorage to berth over the past 7 days, current berth occupancy rate at container terminals, and published port authority advisories. Congestion levels must be classified as green (normal, waiting time under 6 hours), amber (moderate congestion, 6-24 hours), or red (severe congestion, over 24 hours). The system must maintain historical congestion patterns to detect whether current conditions are seasonal norms or anomalous events. Congestion alerts must be integrated with the predictive ETA engine to automatically adjust arrival estimates. For JNPT, which frequently experiences monsoon-related congestion, the system must activate enhanced monitoring mode during June-September with hourly congestion level updates rather than the standard 4-hour interval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R013	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Booking SLA Dashboard	AI-powered dashboard shows customers their booking performance metrics and SLA adherence across all their shipments.	must_have			["Dashboard displays booking-to-confirmation time, amendment rate, cancellation rate, and no-show rate per month", "AI generates a plain-language summary of booking trends (in English or Arabic) highlighting improvements or deteriorations", "Metrics are filterable by entity, trade lane, container type, and date range (Gregorian or Hijri)", "Data is refreshed in near-real-time (within 15 minutes of booking status change)"]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R010	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Voyage Performance Benchmarking Across Rotations	The system must enable performance benchmarking across different service rotations and vessel deployments, allowing fleet managers to compare schedule reliability, port turnaround efficiency, and speed/consumption performance. The benchmarking must normalize for differences in rotation length (number of port calls), vessel size class, and seasonal conditions to enable fair comparisons. The system must rank service rotations by composite performance score (weighted combination of reliability, turnaround efficiency, and bunker efficiency) and identify best-performing rotations whose practices can be replicated. For vessel-level benchmarking, the system must compare sister vessels on the same rotation to isolate vessel-specific performance factors from route-specific factors. Benchmark reports must be generated quarterly and presented in a matrix format with conditional formatting in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R011	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Schedule Change Management and Notification	The system must manage schedule changes (proforma updates) through a controlled workflow that includes: change proposal with justification, impact assessment on active bookings and customer delivery promises, multi-level approval (operations manager for minor timing adjustments under 12 hours, VP operations for port additions/omissions), and automated stakeholder notification. When a schedule change is approved, the system must automatically: update all forward voyage ETAs/ETDs, recalculate transit times for active bookings, generate customer notifications via email and EDI, update the customer-facing sailing schedule on the booking portal, and notify port agents at affected ports. All schedule changes must maintain an audit trail showing the original schedule, each amendment, the approver, and the business reason. The system must track the frequency of schedule changes per rotation as a planning quality metric.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R012	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Transit Time Reliability by Port Pair	The system must calculate and publish transit time reliability statistics for every active port pair in the network, measuring the percentage of shipments delivered within the published transit time plus a configurable tolerance (default +1 day). Transit time must be measured from vessel departure at origin port to container availability at destination port (including terminal unloading time). The system must maintain separate transit time distributions for direct services versus services requiring transshipment at hub ports (Jebel Ali being the primary transshipment hub). For transshipment connections, the total transit time reliability must account for connection reliability (probability that the feeder/mainline connection is made as planned). Transit time statistics must be published on the customer booking portal to support booking decisions and must be updated weekly. Historical transit time trends must be available for the previous 24 months with drill-down by month, vessel, and terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R013	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	CO2 Emissions per Voyage Tracking	The system must calculate and report CO2 emissions for each completed voyage based on actual fuel consumption data, fuel type emission factors (as per IMO MEPC guidelines: HFO 3.114 tCO2/tFuel, VLSFO 3.151, MGO 3.206), and voyage distance. Emissions must be reported in total metric tonnes CO2 per voyage and normalized as grams CO2 per TEU-kilometer for benchmarking. The system must track CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) rating for each vessel per calendar year as required by IMO DCS regulations, projecting the year-end CII rating based on year-to-date performance and planned future voyages. When a vessel's projected CII rating risks falling below the required threshold (C rating or better), the system must alert the fleet manager with recommended corrective actions including slow-steaming, route optimization, or voyage reduction. Emissions data must be available for inclusion in customer sustainability reports and ESG disclosures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R014	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Hijri Calendar Integration for Schedule Reporting	All schedule reliability reports, performance dashboards, and trend analyses must support Hijri calendar date display and period aggregation as an alternative to Gregorian calendar. The system must accurately convert between Hijri and Gregorian dates using the Umm al-Qura calendar standard used in KSA for official purposes. Weekly performance summaries must align with the local work week: Saturday-Thursday for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities, and Monday-Friday for the India entity, with the system correctly handling the overlap period (Thursday-Friday) when Gulf operations continue but India operations have ended for the week. Ramadan period performance tracking must automatically identify the Ramadan start and end dates each year and calculate schedule reliability separately for Ramadan and non-Ramadan periods, as port productivity at Gulf terminals typically decreases during Ramadan due to reduced working hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R015	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Performance Data Export and Regulatory Reporting	The system must export schedule performance data in formats required by port authorities and regulatory bodies across the four operating countries. For Qatar (Mwani Qatar), the system must produce port call statistics in the prescribed XML format. For UAE (Federal Transport Authority), vessel movement reports must comply with the Smart Maritime Portal data submission requirements. For KSA (Saudi Ports Authority/Mawani), the system must generate vessel traffic and performance reports in the mandated format. For India (Director General of Shipping), the system must produce voyage performance data as required under the Merchant Shipping Act reporting obligations. All exports must be schedulable for automatic generation and submission. The system must also support ad-hoc export in CSV, Excel, and PDF formats with configurable columns, date ranges, and entity filters. PDF exports must support bilingual Arabic-English headers and footers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R016	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Real-Time Vessel Position and Event Tracking	The system must integrate with AIS (Automatic Identification System) data providers to track real-time vessel positions for all vessels in the managed fleet, updating positions at minimum every 15 minutes while at sea and every 5 minutes within port approaches. The system must automatically detect and log vessel events including: departure from berth, passing waypoints (Strait of Hormuz transit, port approach zones), anchorage arrival, pilot boarding, berth arrival, and crane operations start/completion. Each event must be timestamped in UTC and converted to local time for the relevant port (AST UTC+3 for Qatar/KSA, GST UTC+4 for UAE, IST UTC+5:30 for India). Vessel tracking data must be visualized on a map interface supporting both English and Arabic port names and navigational labels, with vessel icons showing heading, speed, and current voyage status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R017	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Schedule Performance API for Customer Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API enabling customers and freight forwarders to query schedule performance data for vessels carrying their cargo. The API must support queries by: booking reference, bill of lading number, vessel name/IMO number, and service rotation code. Response data must include current vessel position, latest predictive ETA for next ports, historical on-time performance for the service, and milestone event notifications (vessel departed, vessel arrived, container discharged). The API must support webhook subscriptions allowing customers to receive push notifications for specific milestone events rather than polling. Authentication must use API keys with rate limiting (default 500 requests per hour per customer). API responses must include timestamps in ISO 8601 format with timezone offset, and must support Accept-Language headers for Arabic (ar) and English (en) content localization in descriptive fields. API uptime SLA must be 99.9% measured monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-011-3-004-R018	FEAT-011-3-004	MOD-011	Offline Schedule Monitoring Dashboard	The schedule performance dashboard must support offline viewing for operations managers who need continuous access to vessel schedule status while working at port facilities or during travel in areas with unreliable internet connectivity, which is common at anchorage areas near JNPT and during travel between UAE ports. The offline dashboard must cache the most recent 24 hours of vessel position data, current voyage schedule status for all active voyages, and the latest delay cause entries for ongoing port calls. Users must be able to add delay event annotations and schedule notes while offline, which synchronize to the central system upon connectivity restoration using timestamp-based merge logic. The offline cache must refresh automatically when connected and must display a clear indicator showing data freshness (last sync timestamp) to prevent decision-making based on stale data. The offline mode must function in both Arabic and English interface languages without requiring additional data downloads.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R001	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Contract Creation with Type Classification	The system must support creation of charter party contracts classified by type: Time Charter (TC), Voyage Charter (VC), Bareboat Charter (BBC), Contract of Affreightment (COA), and Trip Time Charter (TTC). Each contract must capture vessel particulars (IMO number, flag state, DWT, TEU capacity, gear status), charterer and owner entity details mapped to the multi-entity structure (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), laycan dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and trading limits specifying permitted geographical zones including Arabian Gulf, Indian subcontinent, and Red Sea routes. The contract currency must default based on the owning entity (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) while supporting USD as the primary chartering currency with real-time exchange rate linkage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R002	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	BIMCO Standard Clause Library Integration	The system must provide an integrated clause library containing all BIMCO standard charter party forms including NYPE 2015, Gencon 2022, Baltime 1939 (as amended), BOXTIME 2004 for container vessels, and Shelltime 4. Each clause must be stored with version history, and the system must allow users to create custom rider clauses specific to regional requirements such as Qatar Maritime Authority regulations, UAE Federal Transport Authority directives, Saudi Ports Authority mandates, and Indian DG Shipping circulars. Clauses must be searchable in both English and Arabic with full-text indexing, and the AI engine must suggest relevant clauses based on trade route, vessel type, and counterparty history with a minimum 90% relevance accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R003	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Multi-Entity Counterparty Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive counterparty database for charter party transactions supporting owners, disponent owners, charterers, and brokers across all four operational entities. Each counterparty record must include legal entity name in English and Arabic, commercial registration numbers (CR for Qatar/KSA, trade license for UAE, CIN for India), VAT/GST registration details, sanctioned party screening status with automatic OFAC/EU/UN sanctions list checking, credit rating, and historical performance metrics including on-time delivery percentage and claims history. The system must enforce entity-level segregation so that Qatar entity contracts cannot reference KSA entity bank accounts, while still allowing consolidated group-level reporting across all entities with currency conversion at booking-date or reporting-date rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R004	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Approval Workflow Engine	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for charter party contracts with entity-specific approval matrices. Approval thresholds must be definable by contract value in local currency equivalent (e.g., contracts above USD 500,000 require C-level approval). The workflow must support sequential and parallel approval paths, delegation of authority during absence periods (with Hijri date support for Islamic holiday periods), automatic escalation after configurable SLA breaches, and digital signature capture compliant with Qatar Digital Government eSign standards, UAE PASS integration, Saudi NAFATH authentication, and Indian Aadhaar eSign. The AI module must pre-validate contracts against company policies and flag anomalies such as below-market rates, unusual trading areas, or counterparties with adverse history before routing for approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R005	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Fixture Note and Recap Generation	The system must auto-generate fixture notes and fixture recaps from negotiation data captured during the chartering process. The AI engine must parse broker correspondence (emails and messages) to extract key commercial terms including hire rate, duration, delivery/redelivery ranges, bunker prices, commission percentages, and special conditions. Generated fixture notes must conform to industry standard formats and be producible in both English and Arabic bilingual layouts with proper RTL text rendering for Arabic sections. The system must track fixture note versions through the negotiation lifecycle (subjects, clean fix, failed) and maintain a complete audit trail of all changes with timestamps, user identification, and entity context for regulatory compliance with Qatar Financial Centre Authority and DIFC record-keeping requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R006	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Vessel Particulars and Specification Database	The system must maintain a detailed vessel particulars database for all vessels under charter, owned, or commercially managed. Each vessel record must include IMO number, MMSI, call sign, flag state, classification society, P&I club details, hull and machinery insurer, TEU capacity (nominal and homogeneous), reefer plug count, dangerous goods capacity, draft restrictions for key regional ports (Hamad Port maximum 15.0m, Jebel Ali 17.0m, Khalifa Port 16.0m, JNPT 14.0m), speed and consumption tables at laden/ballast conditions, and vessel certificates with expiry tracking. The system must integrate with Lloyd's List Intelligence or equivalent maritime data provider for automatic vessel data synchronization and must alert operations teams 90, 60, and 30 days before certificate expiry dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R007	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Financial Terms Configuration	The system must capture and compute all financial terms embedded in charter party agreements including hire rate (daily or lump sum), address commission (typically 1.25-3.75%), brokerage commission, ballast bonus, deadfreight calculations based on TEU shortfall, freight prepaid versus freight collect terms aligned with INCOTERMS 2020 (particularly CIF Hamad Port, FOB JNPT, CFR Jebel Ali), and war risk premium surcharges for vessels transiting designated high-risk areas. All monetary values must be stored with currency code (ISO 4217), exchange rate at fixture date, and converted equivalent in the entity's functional currency. The system must support split-hire arrangements where hire is payable in multiple currencies simultaneously (e.g., 70% USD, 30% QAR) and compute withholding tax obligations per jurisdiction (5% WHT in India on charter hire payments to non-residents).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R008	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Amendment and Addendum Tracking	The system must support formal amendments and addenda to existing charter party contracts while preserving the original contract terms for audit and dispute resolution purposes. Each amendment must be linked to the parent charter party with a sequential addendum number, effective date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and a detailed change log showing field-level differences between original and amended values. The approval workflow for amendments must follow the same entity-specific approval matrix as the original contract, with additional escalation if the amendment increases the total contract value by more than 10% or extends duration beyond the original redelivery window. The AI system must analyze amendment patterns and flag contracts with excessive amendments (more than three within the first quarter of the charter period) as potential risk indicators for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R009	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Off-Hire and On-Hire Survey Management	The system must manage on-hire and off-hire survey processes including scheduling survey appointments at delivery and redelivery ports, tracking surveyor assignments (with preferred surveyor lists per port: Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), recording survey findings with photographic evidence attachments, and generating on-hire/off-hire statements of facts. The system must calculate off-hire periods based on charter party clause definitions (NYPE Clause 17 or equivalent) accounting for breakdown, drydocking, deviation, and other qualifying events. Off-hire deductions must be computed in the charter currency with automatic conversion to entity functional currency. The AI engine must analyze historical survey data to predict likely condition disputes and recommend pre-delivery remediation actions to minimize redelivery claims, targeting a reduction in disputed off-hire days by at least 25% compared to manual processes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R010	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Bunker Management at Charter Commencement and Termination	The system must track bunker quantities and prices at delivery and redelivery of chartered vessels, supporting all fuel grades including VLSFO (0.50% sulphur), LSMGO, HSFO (3.5% sulphur for scrubber-fitted vessels), and LNG bunkers where applicable. Bunker prices at delivery must be recorded per grade in USD per metric ton with conversion to entity currency at the prevailing rate. The system must calculate bunker adjustment amounts at redelivery based on the charter party's bunker clause (typically price at delivery or last supply price), handle bunker ROB (Remaining on Board) discrepancies with configurable tolerance thresholds (default 2% variance), and generate bunker claims for quantities outside tolerance. For vessels trading in Arabian Gulf Emission Control Areas, the system must track compliant fuel usage and maintain records for port state control inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and other regional ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R011	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Dispute and Claims Management	The system must provide a structured claims management module for charter party disputes including off-hire claims, speed and consumption underperformance claims, cargo damage claims arising from vessel condition, and deadfreight claims. Each claim must be trackable through stages: identification, notification, quantification, negotiation, settlement or arbitration referral. The system must calculate claim values based on charter party terms with supporting evidence attachments (weather routing data, noon reports, survey reports, port logs). Arbitration venue defaults must align with charter party terms (typically London LMAA or Singapore SIAC for regional trades, with Qatar International Court option for Hamad Port contracts). All claim correspondence must support bilingual English/Arabic formatting, and the AI engine must estimate claim success probability based on historical outcomes and similar precedent cases stored in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R012	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Expiry and Renewal Management	The system must proactively track charter party expiry dates and provide configurable alerts at 180, 90, 60, and 30 days before the earliest redelivery date. The AI engine must analyze current market conditions (Baltic Exchange indices where available, regional fixture reports) and recommend whether to extend, relet, or redeliver vessels based on the entity's fleet deployment strategy and current cargo commitments. Renewal negotiation tracking must support multiple concurrent offers with scenario comparison including total cost analysis across different charter durations (3, 6, 12 months), hire rate trends, and projected vessel earnings against the entity's hurdle rate. The system must account for seasonal demand patterns in regional trades (pre-Ramadan cargo surge, Indian festival season volumes, Qatar National Day logistics peaks) and factor these into renewal timing recommendations with Hijri calendar date awareness for Islamic seasonal events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R013	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Broker Performance Tracking and Commission Reconciliation	The system must maintain broker profiles with performance metrics including number of fixtures concluded, average negotiation duration, rate achievement versus market benchmark, and counterparty quality scores. Broker commissions must be calculated automatically based on charter party terms (typically 1.25% address commission plus brokerage) with support for split commissions between multiple brokers. Commission payment schedules must align with hire payment frequency and be payable in the broker's preferred currency with automatic conversion from the charter currency. The system must reconcile commission payments against hire statements, flag overpayments or underpayments exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 500), and generate commission debit notes in both English and Arabic. Each entity must maintain its own broker panel with approval status (approved, conditional, suspended) and the AI must recommend broker selection for new fixtures based on historical performance and trade route expertise.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R014	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Document Repository with Regulatory Compliance	The system must provide a secure document repository for all charter party related documents including signed charter parties, addenda, fixture notes, survey reports, bunker delivery notes, and correspondence. Documents must be stored with metadata tags (vessel name, IMO number, charter party reference, entity code, document type) enabling full-text search in English and Arabic. The repository must enforce entity-level access controls ensuring Qatar entity users cannot access KSA entity charter party documents without explicit cross-entity authorization. Document retention policies must comply with Qatar Commercial Law (15 years), UAE Commercial Transactions Law (5 years minimum), Saudi Commercial Court Law (10 years), and Indian Companies Act (8 years) with the longest applicable period applied by default. The system must support offline document caching for users operating in low-connectivity environments at anchorage or during vessel visits, with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R015	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	AI-Powered Charter Party Risk Assessment	The AI engine must perform comprehensive risk assessment for each charter party contract before execution, evaluating counterparty credit risk (integrating with external credit agencies and internal AR aging data), vessel condition risk (based on age, classification status, detention history from port state control databases), geographical risk (sanctions exposure for trading areas, piracy risk zones, weather routing risks for Arabian Sea monsoon season), and financial risk (currency exposure for non-USD denominated charters, bunker price volatility impact on voyage charter profitability). The risk assessment must produce a composite risk score on a 1-100 scale with individual component scores, generate a bilingual English/Arabic risk report, and automatically route high-risk contracts (score above 75) for enhanced due diligence. The system must update risk scores dynamically throughout the charter period as new data becomes available and alert the chartering desk when risk scores deteriorate by more than 15 points from the initial assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-001-R016	FEAT-012-1-001	MOD-012	Sanctions and Compliance Screening for Charter Operations	The system must perform real-time sanctions screening for all parties involved in charter party transactions including vessel owners, beneficial owners, charterers, sub-charterers, and intermediate brokers against OFAC SDN list, EU Consolidated Sanctions List, UN Security Council Sanctions List, and regional lists maintained by Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA (Saudi Arabia), and RBI (India). Vessel screening must check against the OFAC sanctions vessel list and flag any vessels that have called at sanctioned ports within the preceding 12 months using AIS data integration. The screening must be triggered automatically at contract creation, amendment, each hire payment, and on a scheduled weekly batch basis. Positive matches must freeze the transaction, generate an alert to the entity's compliance officer, and create an auditable investigation record. The system must support both English and Arabic name transliteration matching to reduce false negatives when screening Arabic-named entities, applying fuzzy matching algorithms with a configurable similarity threshold (default 85%).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R001	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Voyage Proforma Creation with Multi-Leg Itinerary	The system must support creation of voyage proforma estimates with multi-leg itineraries covering typical regional rotations such as Hamad Port (QAMES) to Jebel Ali (AEJEA) to JNPT (INNSA) and return. Each voyage leg must capture loading and discharge port pairs using UN/LOCODE identifiers, estimated sea passage time based on vessel speed and distance tables (incorporating Suez Canal transit where applicable), port stay durations differentiated by terminal (e.g., QTerminals at Hamad Port, DP World at Jebel Ali, APSEZ at Mundra), and cargo quantities in TEU with laden/empty split. The system must maintain a port distance database with distances in nautical miles via standard routes including Great Circle, Suez Canal, and coastal routing options. The AI engine must suggest optimal itinerary sequences to maximize vessel utilization, targeting a minimum 85% slot utilization across the round voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R002	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Bunker Cost Estimation with Multi-Grade Fuel Planning	The system must calculate estimated bunker costs for each voyage leg based on vessel-specific consumption profiles at different speeds (eco speed, normal speed, full speed) for both laden and ballast conditions. Fuel grades must include VLSFO 0.50% (default for IMO 2020 compliance), LSMGO for port operations and emission control areas, HSFO 3.5% for scrubber-fitted vessels, and LNG for dual-fuel vessels. Bunker prices must be sourced from configurable market data feeds with port-specific pricing for major bunkering locations (Fujairah as primary Gulf hub, Singapore, Colombo) with the ability to override with actual quoted prices. The estimation must account for the vessel's scrubber status and compute the HSFO-VLSFO spread benefit where applicable. Total bunker cost must be expressed in USD with conversion to the entity's functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) at the current exchange rate, and the AI engine must recommend optimal bunkering ports based on price differentials and deviation cost analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R003	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Port Cost Estimation with Regional Port Tariff Database	The system must estimate port costs for each port call in the voyage itinerary using a comprehensive port tariff database covering key regional ports including Hamad Port (Qatar), Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE), King Abdullah Port and Jeddah Islamic Port (KSA), and JNPT, Mundra, and Chennai (India). Port cost components must include port dues (based on GRT), berth hire (per hour or per day), pilotage (inward and outward), towage (number of tugs based on vessel LOA), mooring/unmooring charges, channel dues (e.g., Suez Canal tolls calculated by SCNT), terminal handling charges per TEU (laden/empty/reefer differentials), and agency fees. Each cost item must be stored in the port's local currency with automatic conversion to voyage currency (typically USD). The system must maintain tariff effective dates and automatically apply the correct tariff schedule based on the estimated port call date, with the AI flagging any tariff changes that impact proforma accuracy by more than 5%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R006	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Noon Report Digital Submission and Validation	The system must provide a structured digital noon report submission interface accessible from vessel-side applications operating in offline-capable mode for areas with limited satellite connectivity in the Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean. Each noon report must capture: position, distance run (24h), average speed, weather conditions (Beaufort, Douglas sea state, swell), fuel ROB (Remaining On Board) by grade (HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, MDO), fuel consumption breakdown (main engine, auxiliary, boiler), cargo details (TEU count, reefer units, DG containers), draft readings, and engine parameters (RPM, slip, exhaust temperatures). AI validation must automatically flag inconsistencies such as fuel consumption outliers, impossible speed/distance combinations, and ROB discrepancies exceeding 2% tolerance. Validated reports must sync to shore-side systems within 4 hours of submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R004	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Freight Revenue Calculation with Multi-Currency Support	The system must calculate estimated freight revenue for voyage charters based on cargo bookings or projected cargo volumes per port pair. Revenue calculation must support multiple freight rate structures: per TEU (dry/reefer/special), per FEU, lump sum, and ad valorem for high-value cargo. Freight rates must be configurable by trade lane, cargo type, customer tier, and seasonal factors. The system must handle freight denominated in different currencies across the same voyage (e.g., USD for Hamad-Jebel Ali leg, INR for JNPT discharge), converting all revenues to a common voyage currency for proforma comparison. Deadfreight calculations must apply when minimum quantity commitments in COA contracts are not met, computing the shortfall cost at the applicable deadfreight rate. The system must also factor in surcharges including BAF (Bunker Adjustment Factor), CAF (Currency Adjustment Factor), THC (Terminal Handling Charge), peak season surcharge, and war risk surcharge for applicable trade routes, with each surcharge trackable separately for transparency in customer billing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R005	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) Calculation and Benchmarking	The system must calculate the Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) for each voyage proforma using the standard formula: (Gross Freight Revenue minus Voyage Costs) divided by Round Voyage Days. TCE must be computed in USD per day and also converted to entity functional currencies for local reporting. The system must maintain benchmark TCE rates by vessel class (feeder 1,000-3,000 TEU, handy 3,000-5,000 TEU, sub-panamax 5,000-8,000 TEU, panamax 8,000-13,000 TEU, neo-panamax 13,000+ TEU) and trade route, sourced from market data providers. Each proforma must display the calculated TCE against the benchmark with a variance percentage. The AI engine must flag voyages where estimated TCE falls below the entity's minimum acceptable threshold (configurable per entity) or below the current time charter market rate, recommending whether to pursue the voyage or seek alternative employment. Historical TCE trends must be visualizable on a dashboard with filters by vessel, trade route, entity, and time period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R006	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Voyage Duration Estimation with Weather and Canal Transit Factors	The system must accurately estimate total voyage duration by computing individual components: sea passage time per leg (distance divided by service speed with weather margin), port stay durations (based on cargo volume and terminal productivity rates specific to each port), canal transit time (Suez Canal typical 12-16 hours with convoy scheduling), and waiting time allowances based on historical port congestion data. Weather margins must be configurable by season and route (e.g., 10% margin for Arabian Sea during southwest monsoon June-September, 5% for calm Persian Gulf waters). Port productivity rates must reflect terminal-specific performance: QTerminals Hamad Port averages 28 moves per hour per crane, DP World Jebel Ali averages 30 moves per hour, JNPT averages 22 moves per hour. The system must distinguish between SHINC (Sundays and Holidays Included), SHEX (Sundays and Holidays Excluded), and local holiday calendars including Islamic holidays calculated from Hijri calendar dates that vary by jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R007	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Voyage Proforma Scenario Comparison Engine	The system must enable creation and side-by-side comparison of multiple voyage proforma scenarios for the same vessel and cargo commitment. Users must be able to create up to ten alternative scenarios varying parameters such as itinerary sequence, vessel speed (eco vs full), bunkering port selection, cargo mix, and freight rates. The comparison view must display key financial metrics (TCE, net voyage result, daily profit, ROI percentage) in a tabular format with the best-performing scenario highlighted. The AI engine must automatically generate an optimized scenario by running Monte Carlo simulations across variable parameters (bunker price range plus/minus 15%, port congestion probability, exchange rate fluctuations within historical volatility bands) and selecting the combination that maximizes expected TCE at a 90% confidence level. All scenario data must be exportable to Excel and PDF formats with bilingual English/Arabic headers and the entity's corporate branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R008	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Cargo Commitment and Slot Allocation in Proforma	The system must integrate cargo booking data into voyage proforma calculations, allowing planners to assess voyage economics against actual and projected cargo commitments. The proforma must display booked TEUs versus vessel capacity with breakdown by cargo type (dry standard, reefer, open top, flat rack, tank container, dangerous goods IMO classes 1-9), weight utilization versus deadweight capacity, and reefer plug utilization. COA contract volumes must be overlaid on the proforma to show contract fulfillment status per voyage. The system must calculate breakeven cargo volume (minimum TEUs required to cover voyage costs at applicable freight rates) and display the load factor required for profitability. The AI engine must predict cargo uptake probability based on historical booking patterns for the specific trade lane and season, factoring in regional events such as Ramadan (reduced volumes), Diwali festival (Indian export surge), and Qatar National Day logistics requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R009	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Canal and Strait Transit Cost Estimation	The system must calculate transit costs for major waterways on the voyage route including Suez Canal (SCNT-based tolls with laden/ballast differentials and container rebate schemes), Strait of Hormuz (no direct toll but insurance and war risk premium implications), Bab el-Mandeb Strait (war risk area additional premium calculation), and any future waterway toll implementations in the region. Suez Canal toll calculation must follow the Suez Canal Authority's published tariff tables using the vessel's SCNT (Suez Canal Net Tonnage) and account for applicable discounts (loyalty rebates for frequent transit, round-trip discounts, slow steaming incentives). The system must maintain historical toll rate data to trend analysis and must alert voyage planners when Suez Canal Authority publishes toll adjustments. The proforma must also compute the alternative routing cost via Cape of Good Hope for comparison, enabling the AI to recommend Suez versus Cape routing based on total voyage economics including the additional bunker cost, time value, and hire cost differential for the longer route.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R005	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Laytime and Demurrage Calculation	The system must automatically calculate laytime used, demurrage owed, and despatch earned for each voyage performed under a COA contract. Calculations must follow the specific laytime terms defined in the COA (SHINC, SHEX, or custom terms) and account for port-specific working hours at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT including local public holidays, Friday/Saturday weekends for GCC ports, and Saturday/Sunday weekends for Indian ports. The system must handle Hijri calendar holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Day) and automatically detect overlapping exception periods. Demurrage rates must be converted to the COA settlement currency using the exchange rate applicable on the date of the event or the contractually fixed rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R010	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Insurance and P&I Cost Allocation in Voyage Estimate	The system must incorporate insurance cost allocations into voyage proforma calculations including Hull and Machinery (H&M) insurance daily cost allocation, Protection and Indemnity (P&I) club call contributions prorated to the voyage duration, war risk insurance premium for designated high-risk areas (currently including Gulf of Aden, Southern Red Sea, parts of Arabian Sea as defined by the Joint War Committee listed areas), and freight demurrage and defense (FD&D) cover allocations. Additional war risk premium must be automatically added when the voyage itinerary includes calls to ports in listed areas, with the premium calculated based on vessel value and duration in the risk zone. The system must support both annual fleet policy allocations (daily prorated cost) and voyage-specific spot insurance purchases. All insurance costs must be allocated in the policy currency (typically USD or GBP) with conversion to entity functional currency, and the system must verify that adequate cover is in place for the proposed trading area before allowing proforma finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R011	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Vessel Speed Optimization for Voyage Economics	The system must provide speed optimization analysis within voyage proforma calculations, computing the optimal vessel speed that maximizes net voyage result by balancing bunker cost savings from slow steaming against the time cost of longer voyage duration (daily hire rate for TC-in vessels or opportunity cost for owned vessels). The optimization must model bunker consumption curves using the cubic relationship between speed and consumption, calibrated to each vessel's actual performance data from noon reports. Speed scenarios must range from minimum maneuvering speed to maximum service speed in 0.5-knot increments. The system must factor in schedule constraints (cargo delivery deadlines, berth window bookings at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, tidal windows at JNPT) that establish minimum speed requirements for specific legs. The AI engine must recommend the optimal speed profile per leg, potentially varying speed across legs to meet schedule requirements while minimizing total voyage bunker expenditure, and must quantify the CO2 emission reduction achieved through slow steaming for CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R012	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Proforma Approval Workflow with Entity-Level Authority	The system must implement an approval workflow for voyage proforma estimates before they can be used as the basis for fixture negotiations or voyage orders. Approval authority must be configurable per entity with thresholds based on estimated voyage cost (e.g., Qatar entity: proformas above USD 200,000 require VP Chartering approval, above USD 1,000,000 require CFO approval). The workflow must capture approver comments, conditional approvals (e.g., approved subject to achieving minimum freight rate of USD 850 per TEU), and rejection reasons. Approved proformas must be locked with a version number, and any subsequent modifications must create a new version requiring re-approval if changes affect the net voyage result by more than 5%. The system must display proforma status (draft, pending approval, approved, expired, superseded) in both English and Arabic, with notifications sent via email and in-app alerts to approvers with mobile-responsive formatting for review on mobile devices during off-site operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R013	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Historical Voyage Performance Comparison	The system must enable comparison of new voyage proforma estimates against historical actual voyage results for similar itineraries, vessels, and trade lanes. The comparison must display side-by-side metrics including estimated versus actual bunker consumption (total MT and cost), port costs (with breakdown by port and cost component), voyage duration (sea days and port days separately), freight revenue achieved versus estimated, and net voyage result variance. The system must maintain a minimum 36-month rolling history of actual voyage data for benchmarking. The AI engine must calculate a proforma accuracy score (percentage deviation of estimate from actual) per cost category and per estimator, identifying systematic estimation biases (e.g., consistent underestimation of JNPT port costs by 12%) and recommending calibration adjustments to default assumptions. Reports must be generated in entity functional currency with drill-down capability from summary to individual voyage detail level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R014	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Emission Cost and Carbon Levy Estimation	The system must estimate carbon emission costs within voyage proforma calculations to prepare for current and forthcoming environmental regulations. The estimation must compute total CO2 emissions per voyage based on fuel consumption by grade (emission factors: VLSFO 3.151 tons CO2 per ton fuel, LSMGO 3.206, HSFO 3.114, LNG 2.750), and apply applicable carbon pricing mechanisms including EU ETS costs for voyages calling at EU ports (currently EUR 65-90 per ton CO2), IMO proposed GHG fuel standard levies, and any regional carbon pricing adopted by GCC nations or India. The system must calculate the vessel's estimated CII rating for the proposed voyage and flag if the voyage would push the vessel's annual CII below the required threshold (currently C rating minimum). The proforma must display emission costs as a separate line item with sensitivity analysis for carbon price fluctuations (plus/minus 25%). This forward-looking capability must support the entity's ESG reporting requirements in both English and Arabic for board presentation materials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R015	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Offline Voyage Estimation Capability	The system must provide offline voyage estimation capability for chartering personnel working in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, such as during vessel inspections at anchorage, broker meetings in transit, or operations at remote port locations. The offline module must cache the most recent port tariff database, distance tables, bunker prices, vessel consumption profiles, and exchange rates upon last synchronization. Users must be able to create complete voyage proformas offline with all calculation engines functioning locally. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline-created proformas to the central server, flag any data discrepancies caused by stale cached data (e.g., bunker prices changed by more than 5% during offline period), and recalculate affected proformas with current data while preserving the original offline version for reference. The offline module must support Arabic RTL interface rendering without requiring server-side resources and must function on standard tablets and laptops with minimum 4GB RAM.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R003	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Multi-Currency Bunker Price Comparison Matrix	The system must present a consolidated price comparison matrix displaying all received supplier quotations normalized to a single base currency selected by the user (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR). The matrix must show per-metric-ton pricing, total estimated cost, applicable taxes and surcharges, delivery premiums or discounts, payment terms, and net landed cost. Exchange rates must be sourced from the central treasury module in real-time or use the most recent cached rate when offline. The system must highlight the AI-recommended supplier with justification text and flag any quotation that deviates more than a configurable percentage threshold from the market benchmark price for that fuel grade and port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R016	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	Proforma Integration with Chartering and Operations Modules	The system must provide seamless integration between approved voyage proformas and downstream modules including Charter Party Management (FEAT-012-1-001) for fixture term validation, voyage operations for voyage order generation, and financial accounting for voyage P&L tracking against proforma budgets. When a proforma is linked to a charter party fixture, the system must automatically populate voyage order fields from the approved proforma data (itinerary, cargo, bunker estimates). Variance tracking must begin from voyage commencement, comparing actual costs against proforma estimates in real-time with configurable threshold alerts (default 10% variance trigger). The system must support proforma-to-actual reconciliation at voyage completion, generating a voyage result report showing variances by cost category in both absolute amount and percentage terms, denominated in voyage currency and entity functional currency. API endpoints must be provided for integration with external voyage management systems, ship management platforms, and business intelligence tools, secured with OAuth 2.0 authentication and entity-level API key segregation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-002-R017	FEAT-012-1-002	MOD-012	AI-Driven Market Rate Intelligence for Voyage Estimation	The AI engine must aggregate and analyze market rate intelligence from multiple sources to inform voyage estimation parameters. Data sources must include Baltic Exchange indices (where applicable for container assessment), broker market circulars, historical fixture databases, and proprietary company fixture data across all four entities. The AI must provide suggested freight rates per trade lane pair based on current market conditions, recent comparable fixtures, seasonal adjustments, and cargo type premiums. For time charter vessels used on voyage basis, the AI must compare the proposed voyage TCE against current TC market rates to advise on optimal vessel employment. Market intelligence must be refreshed at minimum twice daily with automated alerts when market rates for key regional trade lanes (Hamad-Jebel Ali, Jebel Ali-JNPT, Jeddah-Mundra) move by more than 8% within a rolling seven-day window. The intelligence dashboard must present data in both English and Arabic with currency display configurable between USD and entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R001	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Statement Generation with Semi-Monthly Billing Cycles	The system must generate hire statements automatically based on the payment frequency defined in the charter party (typically semi-monthly in advance: 1st-15th and 16th-end of month). Each hire statement must calculate gross hire based on the daily hire rate multiplied by the number of days in the billing period, with fractional day calculation using a 30-day month convention or actual days as specified in the charter party. The statement must be generated in the charter party currency (typically USD) with parallel display in the entity's functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) at the exchange rate on the statement date. Hire statements must be automatically numbered with entity prefix (QA/AE/SA/IN) and sequential numbering per charter party. The system must generate statements five business days before the payment due date to allow for review and approval, and must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date display on statement documents for compliance with local business practices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R002	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Off-Hire Deduction Calculation per Charter Party Terms	The system must calculate off-hire deductions within hire statements based on the specific off-hire clause in the governing charter party (e.g., NYPE 2015 Clause 17, Baltime Clause 11, BOXTIME Clause 16). Off-hire events must be categorized by cause: mechanical breakdown, drydocking, deviation for owner's purposes, crew-related delays, and force majeure events. For each off-hire event, the system must record the commencement timestamp, resumption timestamp, off-hire clause reference, and supporting documentation (master's protest note, engine room logs, class surveyor report). The off-hire deduction must be computed as the daily hire rate multiplied by off-hire duration in days and hours (minimum granularity of 15 minutes), with the option to deduct net or gross of address commission as specified in the charter party. The AI engine must cross-reference off-hire claims with vessel AIS position data to validate deviation claims and with port records to verify port delay claims, flagging discrepancies exceeding two hours for human review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R003	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Address Commission and Brokerage Deduction Computation	The system must calculate address commission deductions on each hire statement based on the percentage specified in the charter party (typically 1.25% to 3.75% of gross hire). The commission must be deducted from the gross hire amount before computing the net hire payable, unless the charter party specifies commission payable separately to the broker. The system must support split commission arrangements where multiple brokers share the brokerage (e.g., 1.25% to Broker A and 1.25% to Broker B) and track each broker's entitlement separately. Commission deductions must continue during off-hire periods if the charter party specifies commission on gross hire before off-hire deductions, versus commission only on hire actually paid (net of off-hire). The system must generate commission credit notes to brokers when hire payments are received, reconcile commission balances over the charter period, and support commission payment in the broker's preferred currency with automatic conversion from the charter currency. Commission accounting entries must be posted to the correct entity's general ledger with proper inter-entity elimination for brokers serving multiple entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R004	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Bunker Value Adjustment on Hire Statements	The system must handle bunker value adjustments on hire statements for both delivery and redelivery of chartered vessels. At delivery, the charterer pays the owner for bunkers remaining on board at the agreed delivery price per metric ton per fuel grade. At redelivery, the owner pays the charterer for bunkers ROB at the agreed redelivery price. The system must track bunker quantities by grade (VLSFO, LSMGO, HSFO, LNG) with separate pricing for each, compute total bunker adjustment amounts, and include these as line items on the first and final hire statements respectively. For intermediate bunker adjustments (when charter party requires periodic bunker reconciliation), the system must compare actual bunker consumption against the charter party's warranted consumption and compute over/under consumption claims. All bunker values must be calculated in the bunker pricing currency (typically USD per MT) with conversion to entity functional currency. The system must maintain bunker quantity audit trails with source documents (bunker delivery receipts, sounding reports, flow meter readings) attached to each adjustment entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R005	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Speed and Consumption Performance Claims on Hire Statements	The system must calculate speed and consumption underperformance claims for inclusion in or attachment to hire statements when the chartered vessel fails to meet warranted performance standards. The calculation must use good weather day criteria (typically Beaufort Force 4 or below, Douglas Sea State 3 or below, no adverse current exceeding 0.5 knots) applied to noon report data to isolate qualifying performance days. For qualifying days, the system must compare actual speed against warranted speed and actual bunker consumption against warranted consumption at the corresponding speed from the charter party's speed/consumption table. Underperformance claims must be calculated as: time lost due to speed deficit (distance divided by warranted speed minus distance divided by actual speed) multiplied by daily hire rate, plus excess bunker consumption valued at the applicable fuel price. The AI engine must apply weather routing data from third-party providers to validate good weather day determination, filter out days affected by current, shallow water effects, or port maneuvering, and generate a detailed performance analysis report with statistical confidence intervals for the claim amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R006	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Payment Scheduling and Bank Transfer Integration	The system must manage hire payment scheduling with configurable payment terms per charter party (typically 15 days in advance, semi-monthly). Payment schedules must be generated automatically at charter party commencement covering the full charter duration, with amounts adjusted for known off-hire periods and one-time deductions. The system must integrate with the entity's banking platforms for payment execution: Qatar National Bank and QNB Finansbank for Qatar entity, Emirates NBD and FAB for UAE entity, SNB and Al Rajhi for KSA entity, and SBI and HDFC for India entity. Payment files must be generated in the bank's required format (SWIFT MT103/MT202 for international transfers, local RTGS/NEFT formats for India domestic). The system must track payment status (scheduled, approved, submitted to bank, confirmed, failed) and automatically reconcile bank confirmations against hire statements. Late payment interest must be calculated at the charter party specified rate (typically LIBOR/SOFR plus 2-3%) for any hire payments not received by the due date, with interest amounts automatically included in the subsequent hire statement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R007	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Statement Dispute Management and Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a structured dispute management workflow for contested hire statement items. When a charterer or owner disputes a line item (off-hire period, bunker quantity, performance claim), the disputed amount must be segregated from the undisputed portion, allowing the undisputed balance to proceed for payment while the dispute is resolved. Each dispute must be tracked with a unique reference number, linked to the originating hire statement and charter party, categorized by dispute type, and assigned to a responsible person within the entity. The system must maintain a correspondence log for each dispute with document attachments, settlement offers, and counter-offers with timestamps. Dispute resolution outcomes (accepted, partially accepted with adjusted amount, rejected, referred to arbitration) must be recorded and automatically generate adjustment entries on subsequent hire statements. The AI engine must analyze dispute patterns per charter party, counterparty, and vessel to identify systemic issues and recommend preventive actions, achieving a target of reducing repeat dispute occurrences by 30% through proactive flagging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R008	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Multi-Currency Hire Statement with Exchange Rate Management	The system must support hire statements where the charter party hire is denominated in one currency but payment is required in another currency, as well as scenarios where different deductions are in different currencies. The system must maintain daily exchange rates for all operational currency pairs (USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR, and cross rates) sourced from the entity's treasury department or central bank published rates (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI). Each hire statement must display amounts in the charter currency as the primary denomination with a parallel column showing entity functional currency equivalents. For payment processing, the system must apply the exchange rate on the payment value date as specified in the charter party (or the rate on the hire statement date if not specified). Exchange rate gains and losses arising from timing differences between statement date and payment date must be separately identified and posted to the appropriate foreign exchange gain/loss account in each entity's chart of accounts. The system must also support frozen exchange rate agreements where parties agree to a fixed rate for the charter duration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R009	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Withholding Tax Computation on Hire Payments	The system must compute applicable withholding tax on hire payments based on the payer entity jurisdiction, payee tax residency, and applicable double tax avoidance agreements (DTAA). India entity must withhold tax at 10% (with PAN) or 20% (without PAN) on charter hire payments to non-resident vessel owners under Section 194C/195 of the Indian Income Tax Act, reduced by applicable DTAA rates (e.g., India-Singapore DTAA may reduce to 0% for shipping income under Article 8). Qatar entity must assess withholding obligations under Qatar Income Tax Law for payments to non-Qatari entities. UAE entity operations in free zones (JAFZA, KIZAD) must verify free zone tax exemption applicability. KSA entity must apply 5% withholding tax on charter payments to non-residents under Saudi Income Tax Law Article 68. The system must maintain a treaty rate database, validate tax residency certificates (TRC) before applying reduced rates, generate withholding tax certificates (Form 16A for India, equivalent forms for other jurisdictions), and integrate with entity-level tax compliance reporting. The AI engine must flag charter parties where withholding tax structuring could be optimized through entity selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R010	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Statement Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every hire statement encompassing creation timestamp, creator user ID and entity, all calculation inputs and their sources (hire rate from charter party, off-hire events from operations module, bunker quantities from vessel reports), approval chain with timestamps and approver comments, payment execution details, and any post-issuance amendments. The audit trail must be immutable once a hire statement moves beyond draft status, with amendments creating new versions rather than modifying existing records. Audit reports must be exportable in structured formats (PDF, Excel, XML) for external auditor review, with entity-specific report templates complying with Qatar Financial Markets Authority requirements, DFSA regulatory standards for UAE entities, CMA standards for KSA entities, and SEBI/MCA requirements for India entities. The system must support Arabic language audit reports with proper RTL formatting, Hijri date stamps alongside Gregorian dates, and entity-specific letterhead and legal disclaimers. Retention of hire statement records must follow the longest applicable retention period across all entity jurisdictions (minimum 15 years per Qatar Commercial Law).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R011	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Automated Hire Statement Reconciliation with Owner Statements	The system must provide an automated reconciliation engine that compares internally generated hire statements (charterer's view) against owner-issued hire statements for vessels chartered-in. The reconciliation must match line items by category (gross hire, off-hire deductions, commission, bunker adjustments, performance claims) and identify discrepancies at the individual item level. Matching tolerance thresholds must be configurable (default USD 100 for amount differences, 1 hour for time differences). The system must categorize discrepancies as: timing differences (same amounts, different periods), calculation differences (same inputs, different computation), or factual differences (different underlying data). The AI engine must suggest resolution actions for each discrepancy based on the charter party terms and historical resolution patterns. Reconciliation status must be tracked (unreconciled, partially reconciled, fully reconciled, reconciled with adjustments) and outstanding items must be escalated automatically if unresolved after a configurable period (default 30 days). Monthly reconciliation summary reports must be generated per charter party and per entity for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R012	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Ballast Bonus and Lump Sum Payment Processing	The system must handle special payment types commonly found in charter party agreements including ballast bonus (compensation for vessel positioning to the delivery port), lump sum delivery payments, redelivery bonuses, and hold cleaning costs. Ballast bonus must be calculated based on the charter party terms (fixed amount or formulaic based on distance and bunker cost from the vessel's last discharge port to the charter delivery port). These payments must be included as separate line items on the appropriate hire statement (first statement for delivery-related payments, final statement for redelivery-related payments) or processed as standalone payment documents if the charter party requires immediate payment outside the hire cycle. Each special payment must be linked to the charter party clause that authorizes it, supported by calculation workings showing the derivation of the amount, and subject to the same approval workflow, commission deductions, and withholding tax computations as regular hire. The system must track cumulative special payments per charter party for total charter cost analysis and entity-level financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R013	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Statement Template and Format Management	The system must support configurable hire statement templates per entity and per counterparty to accommodate different formatting preferences and contractual requirements. Each template must include mandatory fields (charter party reference, vessel name and IMO number, billing period, hire rate, gross hire, deductions, net payable, payment bank details, and payment due date) plus optional sections (performance summary, bunker consumption report, off-hire log). Templates must support bilingual English/Arabic layouts with entity logos, legal entity names in both languages, and registered office addresses. The system must support counterparty-specific templates when owners or charterers require hire statements in their proprietary format. PDF output must comply with PDF/A archival standards for long-term document retention. The system must generate hire statements in batch for entities managing multiple charter parties (processing all due statements for the upcoming payment cycle in a single operation) with parallel generation for performance, targeting batch completion within 5 minutes for up to 50 concurrent charter parties per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R014	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Escalation and Rate Adjustment Processing	The system must process hire rate adjustments that occur during the charter period, including annual escalation clauses (e.g., 2% increase on each anniversary), market-linked adjustments (hire adjusted quarterly based on a basket of container freight indices), profit-sharing arrangements (base hire plus percentage of voyage profits above a threshold), and currency-linked adjustments (hire adjusted if the reference exchange rate moves beyond agreed bands). Rate changes must be applied from the effective date specified in the charter party, with the system computing the exact switchover point on hire statements that span the adjustment date, prorating the old and new rates to the day. The system must maintain a rate history per charter party showing all rate changes with effective dates, and must validate rate adjustments against the charter party escalation formula before applying. The AI engine must forecast future hire obligations based on escalation provisions and market projections, feeding into entity-level cash flow forecasting. Notifications must be sent to the chartering and finance teams 30 days before scheduled rate adjustments for budget planning purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R015	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Inter-Entity Charter Hire Settlement and Transfer Pricing	The system must manage hire statement processing for inter-company charter arrangements where one entity within the group charters a vessel to another entity (e.g., Qatar entity owns the vessel, UAE entity is the time charterer). Inter-entity hire rates must comply with transfer pricing regulations in all jurisdictions: Qatar Transfer Pricing Rules (Article 20 of Qatar Income Tax Law), UAE Transfer Pricing provisions under Corporate Tax Law, Saudi Transfer Pricing Bylaws, and Indian Transfer Pricing Rules under Sections 92A-92F of the Income Tax Act. The system must maintain arm's length benchmarking documentation showing that inter-entity charter rates are consistent with rates charged to unrelated parties for similar vessels and trade routes. Hire statements for inter-entity charters must generate dual accounting entries: a payable in the charterer entity's books and a corresponding receivable in the owner entity's books, both in their respective functional currencies. The system must facilitate inter-entity settlement netting where multiple charter arrangements exist between the same entity pair, reducing the number of actual cross-border fund transfers. Transfer pricing documentation must be exportable in formats required by each jurisdiction's tax authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R005	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Weather Working Day Calculation with Meteorological Data Integration	The system must compute weather working days by excluding time periods when weather conditions prevent cargo operations, as required by charter parties specifying WWD (Weather Working Days) laytime terms. The system must integrate with meteorological data providers to obtain historical and real-time weather data for each port including wind speed, precipitation, visibility, wave height (for offshore terminals), and temperature extremes. Weather interruption thresholds must be configurable per port and terminal: for example, Hamad Port container terminal ceases operations at wind speeds above 45 km/h, Jebel Ali stops at 50 km/h, JNPT suspends during heavy rainfall exceeding 50mm/hour. The system must distinguish between weather interruptions that affect the actual cargo operation (stoppable time) and weather conditions that would have prevented operations even if the vessel was not at berth (non-stoppable time) for even if used (EIU) clause interpretation. Sand storms (shamal events) common in Arabian Gulf ports from June to August must be recognized as weather interruptions with automatic logging when meteorological data confirms conditions exceeding operational thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R016	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	AI-Powered Hire Statement Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously monitor hire statement data to detect anomalies and potential errors before statements are finalized and sent for payment. Detection rules must include: hire rate discrepancies versus the charter party fixture (tolerance zero, any difference flagged), off-hire periods that overlap with periods where AIS data shows the vessel operating normally, bunker consumption claims that deviate more than 15% from the vessel's historical average for similar voyages, commission percentages that differ from charter party terms, duplicate off-hire deductions across consecutive statements, and exchange rates that deviate more than 2% from central bank published rates on the statement date. Each anomaly must be assigned a severity level (critical, high, medium, low) and routed to the appropriate reviewer. The system must maintain an anomaly resolution log tracking false positive rates per detection rule, allowing the AI to refine detection thresholds over time and targeting a false positive rate below 10% after the initial 90-day learning period. Monthly anomaly detection effectiveness reports must be generated per entity showing detected issues, confirmed anomalies, and estimated financial impact of prevented errors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-003-R017	FEAT-012-1-003	MOD-012	Hire Statement Dashboard and Real-Time Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time dashboard for monitoring hire statement status across all active charter parties per entity, with a consolidated group view available to authorized users. The dashboard must display: total hire obligations for the current and next billing period in entity functional currency and USD equivalent, upcoming payment due dates with countdown indicators, overdue payments with aging analysis (0-15 days, 16-30 days, 31-60 days, over 60 days), unresolved disputes by value and age, and month-to-date hire expense versus budget comparison. Interactive drill-down must allow users to navigate from summary metrics to individual charter party details to specific hire statement line items. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout rendering with all labels, amounts, and dates displayed in Arabic numerals and text when the Arabic locale is selected, while maintaining Gregorian and Hijri dual-date display throughout. Dashboard data must refresh at maximum 15-minute intervals during business hours (entity-specific business hour definitions) and must be accessible on mobile devices with responsive layout for on-the-go monitoring by chartering managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R001	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Laytime Definition and Allowance Configuration per Charter Party	The system must support configurable laytime definitions per charter party including: fixed laytime (specified number of hours/days for loading and discharging separately or combined), customary quick despatch (CQD) linked to port-specific standard handling rates, and rate-based laytime calculated from cargo quantity divided by agreed handling rate (e.g., 2,500 TEU per weather working day of 24 consecutive hours). The system must distinguish between separate laytime (loading and discharging laytime calculated independently) and reversible laytime (combined allowance where time saved at one port can be used at another). Laytime allowances must be expressible in running days, running hours, weather working days (WWD), weather working days of 24 consecutive hours (WWDSHEX), or working hatches working hours (WHHW). Each laytime definition must reference the specific charter party clause and must be stored in a structured format enabling automated calculation. The system must validate laytime definitions against BIMCO Laytime Definitions for Charter Parties 2013 to ensure terms are correctly interpreted, flagging any ambiguous definitions for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R002	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Notice of Readiness (NOR) Tendering and Acceptance Tracking	The system must track Notice of Readiness (NOR) tendering and acceptance for each port call within a voyage charter or COA lifting. The system must record NOR tendered date and time (with timezone conversion to UTC and local port time), NOR acceptance date and time, any rejections of NOR with reason codes (vessel not in all respects ready, berth not available, documents not presented), and the applicable NOR acceptance clause from the charter party (e.g., whether in berth or not, whether in port or not, whether customs cleared or not — WIBON/WIPON/WIFPON). The system must calculate the waiting time between NOR tendered and laytime commencement based on the charter party's notice period requirement (typically 6 hours for SHINC ports, next working period for SHEX ports). Regional port-specific NOR requirements must be configurable: Hamad Port requires NOR at pilot boarding station, Jebel Ali accepts NOR at inner anchorage, JNPT requires NOR at port limit. The AI engine must validate NOR timing against AIS vessel position data to confirm the vessel was at the stipulated tendering point at the declared time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R003	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Time Sheet Recording with Interruption Event Classification	The system must maintain detailed time sheets for each port call recording all events from NOR acceptance to cargo completion. Events must be classified by type: cargo working time (counts against laytime), weather delays (rain, wind above Beaufort 6, sandstorms common in Gulf ports), berth unavailability (shifting, waiting for berth), vessel breakdown at berth, crane breakdown (terminal responsibility versus vessel gear), labor strikes, port authority delays, customs/quarantine inspections, and force majeure events. Each event must record start timestamp, end timestamp, duration, whether the time counts against laytime per the charter party terms, and supporting evidence (terminal daily report, vessel log extract, port authority notice). The system must support simultaneous operations (SIMOPS) where loading and discharging occur concurrently, correctly attributing time to avoid double counting. The time sheet must be editable only by authorized users (operations managers, chartering analysts) with all changes logged in the audit trail. The Arabic interface must display event descriptions and timestamps in proper RTL format with Hijri date equivalents visible.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R004	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Holiday and Working Day Calendar per Port Jurisdiction	The system must maintain comprehensive working day and holiday calendars for every operational port, essential for SHEX (Sundays and Holidays Excluded) and FHEX (Fridays and Holidays Excluded) laytime calculations. For Middle Eastern ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, Jeddah, Dammam), Friday and Saturday are standard weekend days, and the calendar must include Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Mawlid al-Nabi, Isra and Mi'raj) calculated from the Hijri calendar with dates confirmed by official government announcements per country (Qatar, UAE, KSA may observe on different days). For Indian ports (JNPT, Mundra, Chennai), Saturday and Sunday are weekend days, with national holidays (Republic Day, Independence Day, Gandhi Jayanti) and regional holidays (Maharashtra Day for JNPT, Gujarat Foundation Day for Mundra). The system must also track port-specific exceptions such as terminal-declared non-working periods during cyclone warnings (common at JNPT during June-September monsoon). The AI engine must predict likely holiday dates for future years using historical Hijri calendar patterns and adjust laytime calculations proactively when official announcements modify expected holiday dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R006	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Demurrage Rate Application and Calculation Engine	The system must calculate demurrage charges when laytime is exceeded, applying the demurrage rate specified in the charter party (expressed as a fixed daily rate in USD or other charter currency). Demurrage must be computed on time actually used in excess of allowed laytime, prorated to the minute for partial days unless the charter party specifies a minimum increment (e.g., half-day minimum). The system must support tiered demurrage rates where the charter party provides escalating rates (e.g., first 10 days at USD 25,000/day, thereafter USD 30,000/day). For reversible laytime charters, demurrage must be calculated after netting all loading and discharging time against the combined allowance. The system must handle demurrage at both ends (loading and discharging) separately for non-reversible laytime, generating distinct demurrage claims for each port. All demurrage amounts must be calculated in the charter party currency with automatic conversion to the entity functional currency at the rate specified in the charter party (contract date, claim date, or payment date rate). The AI engine must estimate potential demurrage exposure in real-time as cargo operations progress, alerting the operations team when 80% of laytime has been consumed at any port to enable contingency actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R007	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Despatch Money Calculation for Time Saved	The system must calculate despatch money (rebate) payable to the charterer when cargo operations complete within the allowed laytime. The despatch rate must be applied as specified in the charter party, which is commonly half the demurrage rate but may be a different fixed amount or percentage. The system must support multiple despatch calculation conventions: all time saved (ATS) where despatch is paid on total time between NOR acceptance and cargo completion versus laytime used, and all laytime saved (ALTS) where despatch is paid only on actual laytime saved excluding non-counting periods. For reversible laytime, the system must calculate net despatch/demurrage across all ports before generating the final claim. Despatch must be computed in the charter currency and invoiced to the vessel owner with supporting documentation showing the laytime calculation, time saved computation, and despatch rate application. The system must also track despatch receivables against owner payments in the accounts receivable module of each entity, with aging reports and follow-up workflow for outstanding despatch payments exceeding 30 days overdue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R008	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Statement of Facts (SOF) Generation and Validation	The system must generate Statements of Facts for each port call documenting the chronological sequence of events from vessel arrival to departure. The SOF must include: vessel arrival at anchorage/port limits, NOR tendered time, NOR accepted time, all fast (moored at berth), cargo commenced time, any interruptions during cargo operations with reason and duration, cargo completed time, documents on board time, all fast let go (departure from berth), and vessel sailed time. Each event entry must be recorded in local port time, UTC, and the entity's headquarters timezone. The SOF must be countersigned by the vessel master, terminal operator, and the entity's port agent (digital signature with timestamp). The system must validate SOF entries against terminal operating system data (where integration is available with QTerminals at Hamad Port, DP World at Jebel Ali) to detect inconsistencies in reported times. The AI engine must flag SOF entries where the sequence of events is logically inconsistent (e.g., cargo completed before commenced) or where reported port stay duration deviates more than 20% from the vessel's historical average at the same terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R009	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Laytime Calculation Report with Clause-by-Clause Breakdown	The system must generate a comprehensive laytime calculation report for each voyage showing the complete computation from laytime commencement to the final demurrage or despatch result. The report must include: total laytime allowed (in days, hours, and minutes), NOR tendering and acceptance details with applicable notice period deduction, a day-by-day breakdown of time used showing whether each period is counting or non-counting with the applicable charter party clause reference for each exclusion, subtotals for each port call, netting calculation for reversible laytime, total laytime used versus allowed, time in excess (demurrage) or time saved (despatch), applicable rate, and total amount due. The report must be auditable with every exclusion linked to a specific charter party clause and supporting time sheet entry. Output must be available in PDF (for formal claims), Excel (for counterparty negotiation and modification), and structured JSON (for API integration). Bilingual English/Arabic formatting must be supported with all monetary amounts displayable in charter currency and entity functional currency, and the report layout must accommodate RTL text direction when Arabic is selected as the primary language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R010	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Demurrage Claim Submission and Counter-Claim Processing	The system must manage the demurrage claim lifecycle from initial calculation through submission, negotiation, and settlement. Demurrage claims must be generated automatically when laytime calculations show time in excess, packaged with supporting documents (signed SOF, time sheets, NOR copies, weather reports, terminal handling records) into a claim dossier. The claim must be denominated in charter party currency with the applicable interest rate for late payment (typically LIBOR/SOFR plus 2%). The system must track claim submission to the counterparty, acknowledgment receipt, counter-claim responses (which typically dispute specific time entries or clause interpretations), and negotiation rounds. Settlement amounts must be recorded against the original claim with variance tracking. The system must enforce claim submission deadlines per the charter party time bar provisions (commonly 90 days from vessel completion of discharge) and alert operations teams when approaching the deadline with escalating urgency at 30, 15, and 5 days remaining. For counter-claims received from owners on charters-in, the system must provide a structured review workflow enabling the operations team to accept, partially accept, or reject each disputed item with documented reasoning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R004	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Weather Routing Efficiency Scoring	The system must integrate with weather routing services to evaluate whether vessels followed optimal routing recommendations during voyages between key trade lane ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), JNPT (INNSA), Mundra (INMUN), Dammam (SADMM), and Jubail (SAJUB). An efficiency score (0-100%) must be calculated comparing actual route distance and transit time against the weather-optimized route. The scoring algorithm must account for mandatory routing constraints such as TSS (Traffic Separation Schemes) in the Strait of Hormuz, Omani territorial waters transit permissions, and Indian coastal VTS zones. Monsoon season impacts (June-September for Indian Ocean routes) must be factored into seasonal baseline adjustments. Results must be displayed in dashboards supporting Arabic RTL layout with bilingual labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R011	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Multi-Port Laytime Aggregation for Complex Itineraries	The system must support laytime calculation across complex multi-port itineraries common in regional container trades, such as a vessel loading at JNPT and Mundra then discharging at Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, and Dammam. For non-reversible laytime, the system must calculate laytime separately at each loading port and each discharge port, applying port-specific holiday calendars, weather data, and working hours. For reversible laytime, the system must aggregate all time across all ports before computing the demurrage or despatch result. The system must handle partial cargo operations at multiple ports (part loading at JNPT, remainder at Mundra) and allocate the proportionate laytime allowance based on TEU quantity handled at each port when laytime is rate-based. Shifting time between berths at the same port must be treated as per the charter party shifting clause (typically half counting). The system must also handle transshipment port calls where cargo is discharged and loaded during the same call, correctly attributing time to loading or discharging operations based on terminal operation sequences. The AI must recommend optimal port rotation sequences that minimize aggregate laytime risk based on historical port congestion data and seasonal weather patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R012	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Laytime Exception Rules for Terminal-Specific Operations	The system must support terminal-specific laytime exception rules that override or supplement standard charter party provisions based on terminal operating conditions. For Hamad Port, the system must account for QTerminals' operational windows which may include mandatory vessel shifting for mega-vessel priority berthing. For Jebel Ali Terminal 1 versus Terminal 2 versus Terminal 3, different crane productivity rates must be applied when calculating rate-based laytime (Terminal 3 handling neo-panamax vessels averages higher crane intensity). For JNPT, the system must account for customs examination delays that may be excluded from laytime under specific charter party provisions (e.g., time lost due to Indian customs hold for DRI examination of import cargo). For Khalifa Port, KEZAD terminal-specific congestion surcharge periods must be tracked as potential force majeure exclusion events. Each terminal exception rule must be documented with its charter party clause basis, approved by the chartering manager, and applied consistently across all voyages to that terminal. The system must maintain a terminal exception rule library that is auditable and version-controlled.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R013	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Once on Demurrage Always on Demurrage Rule Application	The system must correctly implement the 'once on demurrage, always on demurrage' legal principle when applicable under the charter party terms. Under this rule, once laytime expires and demurrage commences, all time counts as demurrage regardless of exceptions that would have interrupted laytime (such as weather, holidays, or SHEX/FHEX periods). The system must identify the exact moment demurrage commences (laytime expiry timestamp) and switch the calculation methodology from laytime counting (with exclusions) to demurrage counting (continuous, without exclusions). However, the system must also support charter parties that contractually modify this common law rule (e.g., BOXTIME charters that exclude weekends from demurrage calculation or charter parties that specifically provide for exceptions to apply even during demurrage). The system must flag when a charter party contains provisions that deviate from the standard rule and prompt the user to confirm the intended calculation method. The AI engine must reference BIMCO interpretations and landmark arbitration decisions (stored in the system's legal reference database) to guide correct application of demurrage counting rules for ambiguous clause wordings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R014	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Real-Time Laytime Monitoring Dashboard with Alerts	The system must provide a real-time monitoring dashboard showing laytime status for all active voyages across all entities. The dashboard must display for each active port call: vessel name, port name, terminal, laytime allowed, laytime used to date, percentage consumed, estimated time remaining, projected demurrage or despatch amount based on current pace of operations. Color-coded indicators must show status: green (less than 50% consumed), amber (50-80% consumed), red (more than 80% consumed), and flashing red (on demurrage). Automated alerts must be triggered via email, SMS, and in-app notification when laytime consumption reaches configurable thresholds (default: 70%, 85%, 100%). The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), vessel, trade route, and terminal. Real-time updates must be fed by integration with terminal operating systems where available and manual time sheet entries where not. The Arabic language version must display all labels, vessel names (transliterated), and monetary amounts in Arabic numerals with RTL layout. Mobile-responsive design must enable operations managers to monitor laytime status from any device, with push notifications to mobile apps for threshold breach alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R015	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Demurrage and Despatch Financial Settlement and Accounting	The system must process financial settlement of demurrage claims and despatch payments through integration with the entity's accounts payable and accounts receivable modules. Demurrage receivables (from counterparties on voyage charters-out) must be booked upon claim issuance with subsequent adjustments for negotiated settlements. Demurrage payables (to owners on voyage charters-in) must be accrued upon receipt of owner's claim with provisions for disputed amounts. Despatch payments must be processed as either deductions from freight invoices or standalone payments as specified in the charter party. All demurrage and despatch transactions must post to the correct general ledger accounts per entity, with the voyage as the cost center for voyage-level P&L reporting. The system must compute VAT/GST implications on demurrage charges per jurisdiction: UAE VAT at 5% on demurrage invoiced to UAE entities, India GST at 18% on demurrage for Indian port operations, Saudi VAT at 15%, and Qatar VAT considerations based on the evolving Qatari tax framework. Currency conversion for settlement must use the rate applicable per charter party terms (typically claim date rate or payment date rate), with exchange differences posted to foreign exchange accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R005	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Hull and Propeller Fouling Trend Analysis	The system must track hull and propeller fouling degradation over time by analyzing the progressive increase in fuel consumption at constant speed and draft conditions, following ISO 19030 methodology. Fouling trend curves must be generated per vessel with projections for optimal dry-docking timing, considering available dry-dock facilities in the region (Dubai Drydocks World, Bahrain ASRY, Oman Duqm, Indian shipyards at Cochin/Mumbai). The AI engine must predict the economic breakeven point where incremental fuel cost from fouling exceeds dry-docking cost, factoring in current bunker prices at regional hubs (Fujairah, Singapore, Hamad Port) in the vessel's operating currency. Alerts must be generated when fouling penalty exceeds configurable thresholds and displayed in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R016	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	AI-Driven Laytime Dispute Prediction and Avoidance	The AI engine must analyze laytime calculation data, charter party terms, and counterparty behavior to predict likely demurrage disputes and recommend avoidance strategies. The prediction model must identify risk factors including: charter party clauses with known ambiguity (e.g., vague weather exclusion definitions, unclear NOR tendering requirements), ports with high dispute frequency (maintained as a ranked risk list based on historical data), counterparties with high dispute propensity (dispute-to-claim ratio), and specific time sheet events that historically trigger disputes (e.g., terminal shift times, crane breakdown attribution). For each identified risk, the AI must recommend proactive measures such as obtaining additional supporting documentation (independent weather station data for Hamad Port, terminal gate camera logs for Jebel Ali), requesting joint time surveys with the counterparty's agent, or clarifying ambiguous charter party clauses through pre-voyage correspondence. The system must track prediction accuracy over time, targeting a 75% accuracy rate for high-risk dispute prediction, and generate quarterly analysis reports in English and Arabic showing dispute patterns, financial exposure, and recommended process improvements per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R017	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Laytime Calculation Template Library with Precedent Database	The system must maintain a template library for laytime calculations supporting different charter party forms and trade-specific practices. Templates must be provided for standard forms including NYPE 2015 laytime terms, Gencon 2022, BOXTIME 2004, Asbatankvoy (for tanker charters if applicable to multi-purpose vessels), and custom templates for frequently used regional charter party terms. Each template must pre-configure the applicable laytime counting rules, exception categories, demurrage rate structures, and despatch calculation methods. The system must also maintain a precedent database of completed laytime calculations that have been through dispute resolution (arbitration awards or agreed settlements), tagged by the disputed clause and resolution outcome, enabling the AI engine to reference relevant precedents when similar clause interpretations arise in new calculations. The template library must support Arabic language naming and descriptions, with the AI providing suggested template selection based on the charter party form and identified clause patterns. Templates must be version-controlled with change history, and entity-specific customizations must be supported without modifying the master template.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-1-004-R018	FEAT-012-1-004	MOD-012	Offline Laytime Calculation and Time Sheet Entry	The system must provide offline capability for time sheet entry and laytime calculation, enabling port agents and operations staff at terminals to record events and compute laytime without internet connectivity. The offline module must cache the relevant charter party laytime terms, holiday calendars for the current and next month, demurrage and despatch rates, and the current time sheet state upon last synchronization. Users must be able to add time sheet entries (event type, start time, end time, counting status, remarks) and run laytime calculations locally to assess current demurrage/despatch exposure. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline entries to the central server with conflict detection and resolution for any entries that were simultaneously modified online and offline (last-write-wins with conflict flagging for manual review). The offline module must support Arabic RTL interface rendering for regional port agents, function on tablets with minimum 2GB RAM for field use, and maintain local data encryption (AES-256) to protect commercially sensitive laytime data on portable devices. Automatic synchronization must occur when the device detects network connectivity, with manual sync trigger option available to the user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R001	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Real-Time Vessel Telemetry Ingestion	The system must ingest real-time vessel telemetry data from AIS (Automatic Identification System), noon reports, and IoT sensor feeds at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes for AIS, daily for noon reports). Data points must include vessel position (latitude/longitude), SOG (Speed Over Ground), STW (Speed Through Water), draft readings (fore/aft), RPM, slip percentage, wind speed/direction, sea state (Douglas scale), and current set/drift. The ingestion pipeline must handle intermittent connectivity common in the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean corridors, buffering data locally on vessel systems and synchronizing upon reconnection. All timestamps must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar representations and store in UTC with timezone offset metadata for Doha (AST +3), Dubai (GST +4), Riyadh (AST +3), and Mumbai (IST +5:30).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R002	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Charter Party Warranted Performance Baseline Configuration	The system must allow chartering operators to configure CP-warranted performance baselines per vessel including guaranteed speed (in knots, laden and ballast), guaranteed fuel consumption (in metric tons per day for main engine HFO/VLSFO/LSMGO and auxiliary engines), and maximum acceptable weather conditions (Beaufort scale threshold, typically BF 4-5 for good weather warranty). Each baseline must be linked to a specific charter party agreement, with effective date ranges supporting both Gregorian and Hijri dates. The system must support multiple concurrent baselines for vessels operating under different CP terms across multi-entity structures (Qatar entity for Hamad Port operations, UAE entity for Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port, KSA entity for Jubail/Dammam, India entity for JNPT/Mundra). Currency for bunker cost calculations must be configurable per baseline in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R003	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Speed and Consumption Performance Analysis	The system must calculate actual versus warranted speed and fuel consumption performance on a per-voyage-leg and cumulative basis. Speed performance must be computed using both SOG and STW methodologies, applying weather correction factors per industry-standard approaches (e.g., ISO 19030 hull and propeller performance). Fuel consumption analysis must separate main engine, auxiliary engine, and boiler consumption, converting volumes to standardized energy output (kJ/kg) to account for varying fuel grades across bunkering ports like Fujairah, Hamad Port, and JNPT. The system must flag any deviation exceeding configurable thresholds (default: speed deviation >0.5 knots, consumption deviation >5%) and generate alerts in both English and Arabic with full RTL support for Arabic-language notifications sent to chartering managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R002	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Multi-leg voyage cost allocation across ports	The system must allocate shared voyage costs across individual port calls within a multi-leg rotation. When a vessel operates a rotation covering Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA), bunker costs must be allocated proportionally based on nautical miles between each leg. Canal transit fees (Suez Canal) must be allocated based on laden TEU count at transit point. The allocation engine must support configurable allocation methods: distance-based, TEU-based, revenue-based, or equal-split. Each allocation must produce an auditable trail showing the source cost, the allocation basis, and the resulting per-port or per-leg amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R007	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	AI-Driven Performance Anomaly Detection	The system must employ machine learning models trained on historical vessel performance data to detect anomalies in real-time operational metrics. Anomaly categories must include: unexpected speed loss at normal RPM (potential hull fouling or propeller damage), abnormal fuel consumption spikes (possible fuel quality issues from regional bunkering at Fujairah or Hamad Port), unusual auxiliary engine load patterns (reefer container overload common on India-Gulf trade), and draft/trim inconsistencies (potential ballast system malfunction). Each anomaly must be classified by severity (Low/Medium/High/Critical) with AI-generated root cause hypotheses presented in both English and Arabic. The system must learn from operator feedback on anomaly classifications to improve detection accuracy over time, targeting a false positive rate below 10% after 6 months of operational data collection across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R008	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Bunker Consumption Forecasting per Voyage Leg	The system must generate AI-powered bunker consumption forecasts for each planned voyage leg, considering vessel-specific performance profiles, seasonal weather patterns (monsoon for Indian Ocean, Shamal winds for Arabian Gulf), cargo load (TEU and weight), and planned speed instructions from the chartering desk. Forecasts must account for port-specific maneuvering and waiting time consumption estimates at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other regional ports. Bunker cost projections must be calculated in the vessel's operating currency (typically USD) with automatic conversion to entity-specific reporting currencies (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity) using the treasury module's daily exchange rates. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actuals with variance reporting for continuous model improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R009	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) Rating Tracking	The system must calculate and track each vessel's Carbon Intensity Indicator rating in compliance with IMO MEPC.352(78) regulations, computing the Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) based on actual CO2 emissions, deadweight tonnage, and distance traveled. The system must maintain a rolling 12-month CII calculation updated with each completed voyage, displaying the current rating (A through E) and projected year-end rating. For vessels approaching rating deterioration (projected C or below), the system must generate corrective action recommendations including speed reduction scenarios, voyage optimization suggestions, and fuel switching options from HFO to VLSFO/LNG where available at regional ports. CII data must be exportable in IMO DCS (Data Collection System) format for flag state submissions. Dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual metric labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R010	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	EEXI (Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index) Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor and record each vessel's EEXI compliance status per IMO MEPC.350(78) requirements, storing the verified EEXI value, required EEXI threshold for the vessel's type and size category, and any Engine Power Limitation (EPL) or shaft power limitation applied. The system must alert when operational parameters suggest a vessel may be exceeding its EPL limits, which is critical for container vessels operating in the time-sensitive Gulf-India trade lanes where schedule pressure can drive speed increases. Compliance documentation must be maintained per vessel including the EEXI Technical File, and the system must generate reports suitable for port state control inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and Saudi ports. All compliance records must be accessible in both English and Arabic with proper bilingual technical terminology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R011	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Vessel Performance Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive performance benchmarking dashboard comparing vessels within the managed fleet across key KPIs: average speed efficiency (actual vs warranted), fuel efficiency (g/TEU-nm), cargo utilization (TEU intake vs capacity), port turnaround time, and CII rating. Benchmarking must support filtering by trade lane (Gulf-India, Intra-Gulf, Gulf-KSA, India coastal), vessel class (feeder, intermediate, panamax), charter type (time charter, voyage charter, owned), and time period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selections). The dashboard must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with dynamic chart axis flipping and right-aligned data tables. Multi-entity views must respect data segregation rules where Qatar entity users see only their allocated vessels unless granted cross-entity visibility permissions by the system administrator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R012	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Vessel Condition Assessment Reporting	The system must support structured vessel condition assessment reports capturing hull condition (paint system, anodes, sea chest gratings), machinery status (main engine, auxiliary engines, purifiers, boilers), cargo systems (hatch covers, cell guides, reefer plugs, lashing equipment), and navigation/communication equipment status. Each assessment item must have a rating scale (Good/Fair/Poor/Critical) with photographic evidence upload capability supporting offline capture on vessels with limited bandwidth. Assessment reports must be linked to the vessel's performance profile to correlate condition degradation with performance metrics. Reports must be generated in bilingual format (English and Arabic) with standardized shipping terminology. The system must track assessment history and flag deteriorating trends that may impact charter party compliance or classification society requirements for vessels calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R013	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Port Turnaround Performance Tracking	The system must track vessel port turnaround performance at each calling port including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, Mundra, Dammam, and Jubail. Metrics must include: arrival to berth time (anchorage waiting), berth-to-berth working time, gross and net crane productivity (moves per hour), container exchange volume (load/discharge TEU), hatch cover opening/closing time, and departure clearance time. The system must calculate performance against port-specific benchmarks (e.g., Jebel Ali average crane rate vs Hamad Port average) and identify systemic delays attributable to port congestion, customs holds, or vessel-side inefficiencies. Turnaround data must feed into voyage P&L calculations and be available for charter party laytime/demurrage computations. All port names and terminal identifiers must be displayed in both English and Arabic with correct transliteration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R003	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Real-time estimated vs actual P&L tracking	The system must maintain two parallel P&L views for each voyage: an estimated P&L generated at voyage planning stage using budgeted costs and contracted freight rates, and an actual P&L updated in real-time as invoices, disbursement accounts, and revenue confirmations are posted. The variance between estimated and actual must be calculated and displayed at each line-item level with percentage deviation. Alerts must be triggered when any cost category exceeds the estimate by more than a configurable threshold (default 10%). The estimated P&L must lock once the voyage commences but remain visible for comparison throughout the voyage lifecycle and post-voyage settlement period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R014	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Offline Performance Data Synchronization	The system must support fully offline vessel performance data capture and entry for scenarios where vessels operate in areas with no or limited satellite connectivity, particularly common during Indian Ocean crossings and certain stretches of the Arabian Sea. The offline module must queue all noon reports, event logs, bunker soundings, and condition assessments locally on the vessel-side application with automatic conflict resolution upon reconnection. Synchronization must use delta-compression to minimize bandwidth consumption over expensive VSAT links. The system must handle clock drift between vessel and shore systems by reconciling timestamps using GPS-derived time. Data integrity must be maintained through checksums and the system must alert shore-side operators when synchronization gaps exceed 48 hours, with escalation notifications in both English and Arabic to the chartering operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R015	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	Vessel Performance Reporting and Export	The system must generate standardized vessel performance reports exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats for distribution to charterers, owners, technical managers, and classification societies. Report templates must include: Monthly Performance Summary, Voyage Performance Report, Annual CII/EEXI Compliance Report, and Fleet Benchmarking Report. Each report must be available in English and Arabic with correct RTL formatting for Arabic versions, including mirrored charts and right-aligned numerical tables. Reports must include watermarking with the generating entity's name (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India entity) and classification markings (Confidential/Internal/External). Automated scheduled report generation must be supported with distribution via email and in-app notifications. Currency values in reports must display in the entity's local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with USD equivalent where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-001-R016	FEAT-012-2-001	MOD-012	AI Recommendations for Speed and Route Optimization	The system must generate AI-powered recommendations for optimal vessel speed and routing decisions based on real-time market conditions, bunker prices at regional hubs (Fujairah, Hamad Port, Singapore), weather forecasts, port congestion levels, and charter party schedule requirements. Recommendations must balance commercial objectives (minimizing voyage cost, meeting laycan windows) against regulatory compliance (CII rating maintenance, ECA emission zone requirements). The AI engine must present multiple scenario options (e.g., eco-speed vs full-speed with cost/time trade-offs) with financial impact analysis in the vessel's operating currency and entity reporting currency. Recommendations must account for Strait of Hormuz transit scheduling, Suez Canal convoy timing, and Indian port tidal window constraints at JNPT. All recommendation outputs must be bilingual (English/Arabic) with clear confidence scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R001	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Event Registration and Classification	The system must support registration of off-hire events with mandatory classification by type: mechanical breakdown (main engine, auxiliary, steering gear, cargo gear), dry-docking (scheduled/emergency), port state detention (at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, Saudi ports), flag state inspection delays, crew-related incidents, deviation for crew medical emergency, deviation for bunker quality issues, and force majeure events. Each event must capture: vessel identifier, charter party reference, event start date/time (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri), location (port code or coordinates), initial estimated duration, responsible party (owner/charterer/third party), and descriptive narrative in English and/or Arabic. The system must auto-generate a unique off-hire claim reference number following the pattern OH-[Entity]-[Year]-[Sequence] (e.g., OH-QA-2026-0045 for Qatar entity). AI must suggest classification based on event description keywords.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R002	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Period Calculation Engine	The system must calculate off-hire periods with precision to the minute, applying charter party-specific calculation methodologies including net off-hire (deducting only actual time lost), gross off-hire (deducting from point of deviation to resumption of service), and period off-hire (pro-rata daily rate deduction). The calculation engine must handle complex scenarios such as overlapping off-hire events, partial off-hire (reduced speed but still operational), and off-hire during port time versus sea time. For multi-entity operations, the off-hire financial impact must be calculated in the charter party's billing currency (typically USD) and converted to entity reporting currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using the applicable exchange rate from the treasury module on the date the off-hire commenced. The engine must account for time zone differences between event location and entity headquarters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R003	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Rate and Financial Impact Computation	The system must compute the financial impact of each off-hire event by applying the charter party daily hire rate (pro-rated to the minute) to the calculated off-hire duration. Additional financial components must include: bunker savings during off-hire (calculated from last known consumption rates and current bunker prices at the nearest regional hub), port costs incurred during off-hire (agency fees, berth charges at ports like Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, or JNPT), deviation costs (additional fuel and time for off-route transit), and any third-party repair costs. The system must maintain separate accounting entries for each cost component, posting to the correct entity's general ledger (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) in both the transaction currency and the entity's functional currency. Off-hire deductions must be automatically reflected in the next hire payment statement with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R004	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Claim Document Assembly	The system must automatically assemble off-hire claim documentation packages including: the off-hire notice (formal notification to owner/charterer), supporting evidence (noon reports covering the off-hire period, engine room logs, class surveyor reports, port state detention notices), financial calculations (off-hire deduction statement with breakdowns), and correspondence history. Document templates must be available in English and Arabic with entity-specific letterheads (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India entities). The system must support attachment of scanned documents, photographs, and video evidence. AI must verify document completeness by checking against a configurable claim checklist per off-hire type and flag missing documents before claim submission. All assembled documents must be stored in a tamper-evident repository with digital signatures and timestamps for potential arbitration proceedings under English law or relevant Gulf state maritime law.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R005	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Counter-Party Claim Submission Workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-step approval workflow for off-hire claim submission to counter-parties (vessel owners for time-charter-in, charterers for time-charter-out). The workflow must include: claim preparation (chartering operator), financial review (finance department of the relevant entity), legal review (for claims exceeding configurable thresholds, default USD 50,000), management approval (chartering manager), and formal submission. Each workflow step must have configurable SLA timers with escalation rules. The system must support electronic claim submission via email with structured attachments and track delivery confirmation. For claims against owners in the Gulf region, the system must accommodate local business practices including the Friday/Saturday weekend in KSA and Friday weekend considerations in Qatar and UAE. Arabic language claim submissions must be supported for counter-parties requiring Arabic documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R006	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Dispute Resolution Tracking	The system must track the complete dispute resolution lifecycle for contested off-hire claims, supporting statuses: Submitted, Acknowledged, Under Review, Partially Accepted, Fully Accepted, Disputed, In Negotiation, In Arbitration, Settled, and Closed. Each status change must be timestamped and attributed to a user with mandatory notes. The system must maintain a complete correspondence log between parties, supporting both English and Arabic communications. For disputes proceeding to arbitration, the system must track arbitration venue (London LMAA, Singapore SIAC, Dubai DIAC, or as specified in the charter party), arbitrator appointments, submission deadlines, and hearing dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Financial provisions for disputed amounts must be automatically created in the entity's accounting system with appropriate currency handling (USD claim amount with QAR/AED/SAR/INR provision equivalent).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R007	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Scheduled Dry-Docking Off-Hire Planning	The system must support advance planning of scheduled dry-docking off-hire periods, integrating with the vessel's classification society survey schedule (Lloyd's, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK as commonly used in the Gulf-India trade). Planning must include: estimated off-hire start and end dates, selected shipyard (Dubai Drydocks World, Bahrain ASRY, Oman Duqm, Indian yards at Cochin Shipyard or Mazagon Dock), scope of work specification, estimated cost breakdown by currency (yard costs typically in USD or local currency, spares procurement in multiple currencies), and impact assessment on scheduled voyages. The system must calculate the optimal dry-docking window that minimizes commercial impact considering seasonal trade patterns (pre-Ramadan cargo surge, Indian festival season peaks). Notifications must be sent to all affected stakeholders in their preferred language (English or Arabic) with Hijri date equivalents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R008	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Port State Detention Off-Hire Handling	The system must handle port state control detention events as a specific off-hire category with enhanced tracking requirements. When a vessel is detained at any port (particularly Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, or Saudi ports where PSC inspections follow Paris MOU or Indian Ocean MOU protocols), the system must record: detention notice details, deficiency codes (per IMO resolution A.1155(32)), responsible party determination (owner for vessel condition, charterer for cargo-related issues), estimated rectification timeline, and actual release date/time. The system must automatically calculate whether the detention qualifies as off-hire under the specific charter party terms (some CPs exclude first 24 hours of detention). Financial impact must include not only hire deduction but also port demurrage charges, agency overtime fees, and potential cargo delay penalties. The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for the vessel's P&I club and flag state in the required format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R009	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Bunker Adjustment During Off-Hire Periods	The system must calculate bunker consumption and cost adjustments for off-hire periods, distinguishing between: bunkers consumed during breakdown (typically for owner's account), bunkers saved during off-hire versus what would have been consumed if vessel were on-hire (credit to charterer), and bunkers consumed during deviation for repairs. The calculation must use the bunker price applicable at the time of the off-hire event, sourced from the most recent bunker procurement at regional hubs (Fujairah, Hamad Port, Khor Fakkan, JNPT). Bunker ROB (Remaining On Board) must be reconciled at off-hire commencement and termination based on sounding reports. The system must handle multiple fuel grades (HFO 380cst, VLSFO 0.5%S, LSMGO 0.1%S) with separate pricing in USD and conversion to entity currencies. Bunker adjustment statements must be generated as part of the off-hire claim package with detailed grade-by-grade calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R010	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Impact on Voyage Schedule and Cargo	The system must assess and track the cascading impact of off-hire events on scheduled voyages, port rotations, and cargo commitments. When an off-hire event occurs, the system must automatically identify: affected port calls (with specific impact on Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT schedules), cargo bookings at risk of delay or rollover, connecting feeder service disruptions, and reefer container cargo spoilage risk for time-sensitive shipments. The system must generate impact assessment reports for commercial and operations teams in both English and Arabic, with estimated delay durations and recommended mitigation actions (vessel substitution, cargo re-routing, transshipment alternatives). Financial exposure from cargo claims must be estimated and flagged to the insurance/P&I department. The assessment must consider entity-specific contractual obligations and local regulatory requirements for cargo delay notifications at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R011	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Event Real-Time Notification System	The system must send real-time notifications when off-hire events are registered, updated, or resolved to all relevant stakeholders based on configurable notification rules. Notification recipients must include: chartering operations team, vessel technical manager, finance department (of the affected entity), legal department (for high-value events), P&I club correspondent (for detention or incident-related off-hire), and commercial team (for cargo impact assessment). Notifications must be delivered via in-app alerts, email, and SMS/WhatsApp integration for urgent events. All notifications must be available in English and Arabic based on recipient language preference. The notification must include: vessel name, event type, location, estimated duration, estimated financial impact in both USD and entity local currency, and required actions. Escalation rules must trigger management notifications if off-hire duration exceeds entity-specific thresholds (configurable per entity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R012	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Historical Off-Hire Analytics and Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on off-hire history across the managed fleet, including: total off-hire days per vessel per period, off-hire frequency by event type, average off-hire duration by category, financial impact trends (in USD and entity currencies QAR/AED/SAR/INR), and claim recovery rates. Analytics must support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), vessel class, charter type, trade lane, and time period (Gregorian and Hijri calendar). The system must generate benchmark reports comparing off-hire performance against industry averages for similar vessel types on Gulf-India trade lanes. AI must identify patterns such as recurring mechanical issues on specific vessels or seasonal detention risk increases at particular ports. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) and entity-specific branding. Dashboard visualizations must support RTL rendering for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R013	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Provision and Accrual Accounting	The system must manage financial provisions and accruals for off-hire events in compliance with IFRS 15 (Revenue Recognition) and IFRS 9 (Financial Instruments) requirements applicable to each entity's statutory reporting. When an off-hire event is registered, the system must automatically create a provision entry in the entity's general ledger for the estimated financial impact, with the provision amount calculated in the charter party currency (typically USD) and translated to the entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India). Provisions must be reviewed and adjusted monthly based on claim negotiation progress. Upon claim settlement, the system must reverse the provision and record the actual recovery or write-off. The system must generate provision aging reports showing outstanding off-hire claims by age bucket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 90+ days) with currency conversion at current exchange rates for consolidated group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R014	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Owner's Off-Hire Defense Documentation	The system must support vessel owners (for owned vessels on time-charter-out) in preparing defense documentation against off-hire claims from charterers. The defense module must allow: uploading of maintenance records proving due diligence, recording of pre-existing condition acknowledgments from on-hire surveys, documenting force majeure circumstances (regional weather events like cyclones in the Arabian Sea, port closures due to fog at JNPT, Shamal wind disruptions at Gulf ports), and preparing counter-arguments based on charter party exclusion clauses. The system must maintain a library of precedent cases and arbitration awards relevant to Gulf-India trade disputes. Defense document packages must be assembleable in English and Arabic with proper legal formatting for submission to LMAA, DIAC, or other specified arbitration forums. AI must suggest relevant defense strategies based on the off-hire event classification and applicable charter party terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R015	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Integration with Hire Payment Processing	The system must seamlessly integrate off-hire deductions with the hire payment processing workflow, automatically calculating net hire payable after off-hire deductions for each payment period (typically semi-monthly or monthly). The integration must handle: partial period off-hire (pro-rata to the minute), disputed off-hire amounts (showing gross hire, undisputed deductions, disputed deductions, and net payable), advance hire adjustments when off-hire occurs after hire has been pre-paid, and multi-currency reconciliation where hire is paid in USD but entity books are in QAR/AED/SAR/INR. The system must generate hire statements showing: base hire amount, off-hire deductions with references to specific off-hire events, bunker adjustments, address commissions, and net amount due. Payment instructions must be automatically generated for the entity's treasury department with correct banking details and currency specifications. Statements must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R016	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Event Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for all off-hire events and related financial transactions. Every action must be logged including: event creation, classification changes, duration adjustments, financial recalculations, document uploads, approval decisions, claim submissions, and settlement recordings. Each audit entry must capture the user identity, timestamp (UTC with entity timezone display), IP address, and before/after values for any data changes. The audit trail must satisfy compliance requirements for: Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA), Dubai Financial Services Authority (DFSA), Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority (SAMA), and Indian Companies Act 2013 audit requirements. The system must support audit trail export for external auditor review in both English and Arabic. Data retention for off-hire audit records must be configurable per entity jurisdiction, defaulting to 7 years for Gulf entities and 8 years for the India entity per local statutory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-002-R017	FEAT-012-2-002	MOD-012	AI-Powered Off-Hire Prediction and Prevention	The system must leverage AI and machine learning models to predict potential off-hire events before they occur, based on analysis of vessel condition data, maintenance schedules, historical breakdown patterns, and real-time performance anomalies. The prediction engine must monitor: main engine and auxiliary engine performance degradation trends, cargo system reliability indicators (hatch cover hydraulics, reefer plug failure rates), navigation equipment calibration status, and classification society survey due dates. When the probability of an off-hire event exceeds configurable thresholds (default: 70% within 30 days), the system must generate preventive action recommendations including: scheduling preemptive maintenance at the next convenient port (Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, or JNPT where service providers are readily available), ordering critical spare parts, and adjusting voyage schedules to accommodate potential delays. Predictions must be presented with confidence scores and supporting evidence in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R001	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Automated TDR Data Aggregation from Operational Sources	The system must automatically aggregate data from multiple operational sources to populate TDR (Time Charter Daily Run) reports, including: vessel noon reports (position, speed, consumption, weather), port activity logs (arrival/departure times at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other regional ports), bunker delivery notes and sounding reports, cargo operations records (loading/discharge TEU counts), and off-hire event logs. Data aggregation must reconcile timestamps across different time zones (AST +3 for Qatar, GST +4 for UAE, AST +3 for KSA, IST +5:30 for India) and normalize to a consistent reporting timezone as specified in the charter party. The system must flag missing or inconsistent data points requiring manual intervention and present these flags in both English and Arabic to the responsible chartering operator. AI must auto-fill missing data points where sufficient contextual information exists, with clear indication of AI-estimated values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R002	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Template Configuration per Charter Party	The system must support configurable TDR report templates that can be customized per charter party agreement, as different owners and charterers require varying levels of detail and formatting. Template configuration must include: selectable data fields (from a master list of 50+ standard TDR fields), field ordering and grouping, calculation formulas for derived fields (e.g., daily hire cost, cumulative bunker cost, distance remaining), header/footer customization with entity branding (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India entity logos and addresses), and output format selection (PDF, Excel, or structured email body). Templates must support bilingual rendering with English as primary and Arabic as secondary language, with proper RTL layout for Arabic sections. Each template must be version-controlled with effective dates, allowing template changes without affecting historical TDR records. The system must provide at least 5 pre-configured templates based on common industry formats used in the Gulf-India container shipping trade.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R003	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Daily Hire Calculation in TDR	The system must calculate the daily hire amount for each day reported in the TDR, applying the charter party hire rate with adjustments for: off-hire periods (deducting pro-rata hire for partial day off-hire events), address commission (typically 1.25%-3.75% as per CP terms), brokerage commission, and any hire escalation/de-escalation clauses triggered by market index movements. The hire calculation must be performed in the charter party billing currency (typically USD) with automatic conversion to the managing entity's reporting currency (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity) at the daily exchange rate from the treasury module. Cumulative hire calculations must be maintained from charter commencement to current date, with running totals for gross hire, deductions, and net hire payable. The system must handle hire rate changes mid-period for charter parties with variable rate structures and flag any calculation anomalies for operator review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R004	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Bunker Accounting in TDR Reports	The system must include detailed bunker accounting in each TDR report covering: opening ROB (Remaining On Board) by fuel grade (HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, MDO), consumption by purpose (main engine propulsion, auxiliary power generation, boiler, reefer containers, cargo heating if applicable), bunker receipts (deliveries at bunkering ports such as Fujairah, Hamad Port, Khor Fakkan, JNPT), and closing ROB. Bunker costs must be calculated using the actual procurement price from the most recent stem (delivery), with FIFO (First In First Out) or weighted average methodology as specified in the charter party. The TDR must separately track bunker costs for owner's account (during off-hire, warming/cooling) and charterer's account (normal operations). All quantities must be in metric tons with prices in USD per metric ton, with total cost conversions to entity reporting currencies. The system must validate ROB figures against vessel tank capacity and flag discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R005	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Port and Canal Charges Tracking in TDR	The system must track and allocate port charges, canal transit fees, and related disbursements within the TDR framework, categorizing each cost as owner's expense or charterer's expense per charter party terms. Port charges must be captured for all calling ports including: Hamad Port (Qatar), Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE), Dammam and Jubail (KSA), JNPT and Mundra (India), as well as transshipment hubs like Salalah, Colombo, and Singapore. The system must capture: port dues, pilotage, towage, berth hire, agency fees, customs clearance charges, and terminal handling charges. Canal transit fees for Suez Canal must be tracked when applicable for vessels on Gulf-Mediterranean rotations. Each charge must be recorded in its invoiced currency with conversion to USD and entity reporting currency. The system must integrate with port agent disbursement accounts and reconcile estimated versus actual port costs for each call.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R006	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Voyage Distance and Speed Reporting in TDR	The system must calculate and report daily voyage progress in the TDR including: distance traveled in the past 24 hours (nautical miles, based on noon-to-noon reports), cumulative voyage distance, distance remaining to next port, average speed (both SOG and STW), and estimated time of arrival at next port. Speed must be compared against charter party warranted speed, with weather allowance calculations applied per CP good weather warranty clause (typically Beaufort 4 or below, Douglas sea state 3 or below). The system must distinguish between laden and ballast passages as warranted speeds often differ. For vessels on the Gulf-India trade lane (typically Hamad Port/Jebel Ali to JNPT/Mundra), standard route distances must be pre-configured with seasonal weather adjustments for monsoon periods (June-September). All navigation data must be cross-validated against AIS positional data to ensure accuracy. Distance and speed data must be displayed in standard nautical units with Arabic translations of field labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R007	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Review and Approval Workflow	The system must implement a configurable review and approval workflow for TDR reports before distribution to external parties. The standard workflow must include: auto-generation (system aggregates data and produces draft TDR), operator review (chartering operator verifies data accuracy and makes corrections), supervisor approval (senior chartering personnel or chartering manager approves for distribution), and distribution (approved TDR is sent to designated recipients). Each workflow step must have configurable SLA timers (e.g., operator review within 4 hours of noon report receipt, supervisor approval within 2 hours of operator submission). The system must support workflow delegation for periods when approvers are unavailable, respecting entity-specific working hours and weekend patterns (Friday/Saturday for KSA, Friday for Qatar/UAE, Sunday for India). Rejected TDRs must be returned to the operator with specific rejection comments in English or Arabic. Emergency override must be available for senior management to expedite TDR release.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R008	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Automated TDR Distribution to Stakeholders	The system must automatically distribute approved TDR reports to configurable recipient lists via email, with attachments in the specified format (PDF and/or Excel). Distribution lists must be maintained per charter party and may include: vessel owner, disponent owner, time charterer, voyage charterer, ship broker(s), vessel technical manager, entity chartering department, and entity finance department. The system must support different distribution schedules: daily (standard), weekly summary, and on-demand. Email templates must be configurable per entity with bilingual subject lines and body text (English and Arabic). The system must track email delivery status (sent, delivered, bounced, opened) and alert operators to delivery failures. Distribution must respect data confidentiality rules where certain financial details (hire rate, commission) are redacted from reports sent to parties not entitled to see them. The system must maintain a complete distribution audit log for compliance purposes across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R009	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Variance Analysis and Exception Reporting	The system must perform automated variance analysis on TDR data, comparing actual performance against charter party warranties, voyage estimates, and historical averages. Key variance metrics must include: speed variance (actual vs warranted, flagged if exceeding 0.5 knots), consumption variance (actual vs warranted, flagged if exceeding 5%), port time variance (actual vs estimated, flagged if exceeding 6 hours), and hire cost variance (actual vs budgeted for the voyage). The system must generate exception reports highlighting significant variances with AI-generated explanatory notes suggesting probable causes (weather impact, port congestion at Jebel Ali or JNPT, vessel performance degradation, cargo operations delays). Exception reports must be color-coded (green/amber/red) with thresholds configurable per entity. Reports must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with directional indicators adjusted for right-to-left reading. Variance trends must be tracked over time to identify systematic performance issues versus one-off deviations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R010	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Correction and Amendment Process	The system must support a formal correction and amendment process for previously issued TDR reports when errors are discovered or updated information becomes available (e.g., revised bunker sounding figures, corrected port times, updated off-hire durations). Amended TDRs must be clearly marked as 'AMENDED' with a version number and amendment date, highlighting the specific fields that changed from the original version. The amendment must include a reason code and narrative explanation. Amended TDRs must be distributed to the same recipient list as the original, with a cover note explaining the changes in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic). The system must maintain full version history of all TDR iterations, accessible for audit purposes. Financial impacts of TDR amendments must be automatically reflected in hire statements and voyage P&L calculations. A maximum amendment window must be configurable per entity (default: 30 days from original TDR date) after which amendments require management override approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R011	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Cargo Operations Summary in TDR	The system must include a cargo operations summary section in TDR reports for days when the vessel is in port conducting loading or discharge operations. The summary must capture: port name and terminal (e.g., Jebel Ali Terminal 1, Hamad Port CT1, JNPT BMCT/NSIGT), operation type (loading/discharge/both), container counts by category (20ft dry, 40ft dry, 40ft high-cube, 20ft reefer, 40ft reefer, flat rack, open top, tank container), total TEU exchanged, dangerous goods container count with IMO class breakdown, gross crane moves and net crane productivity (moves per hour), operation start and end times, and any delays or stoppages (weather, equipment breakdown, labor issues, customs hold). The system must calculate cargo operation efficiency metrics compared to port-specific benchmarks. All container type descriptions and cargo categories must be available in both English and Arabic. The cargo summary must reconcile with terminal operator records where electronic data interchange is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R012	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Weather and Sea Condition Logging in TDR	The system must record detailed weather and sea conditions in each daily TDR entry as this data is critical for speed/consumption warranty claims and off-hire assessments. Required weather fields include: wind direction (compass bearing and cardinal direction) and force (Beaufort scale 0-12), sea state (Douglas scale 0-9), swell height and direction, visibility (nautical miles), air temperature, sea water temperature, barometric pressure and trend, and current set/drift. The system must flag days where weather conditions exceed charter party good weather warranty thresholds (typically Beaufort 4+ and/or Douglas 3+). For the Gulf-India trade lane, the system must maintain seasonal weather baselines accounting for: Shamal wind events (common June-August in the Arabian Gulf), southwest monsoon (June-September in the Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean), and tropical cyclone seasons. Weather data must be cross-referenced with meteorological service data to validate vessel-reported conditions. All weather descriptions must use standard maritime terminology with Arabic equivalents in bilingual reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R013	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Integration with Accounting and Finance Modules	The system must integrate TDR financial data with the ERP's accounting and finance modules to ensure accurate and timely financial recording of chartering operations. Daily hire accruals must be automatically posted to the entity's general ledger based on TDR data, with hire revenue (for TC-out vessels) recognized per IFRS 16 lease accounting requirements and hire expense (for TC-in vessels) recorded as operating lease expense. Bunker costs captured in the TDR must flow into the bunker inventory and consumption accounting sub-ledger. Port disbursements must be matched against agent proforma estimates and actual invoices. The integration must handle multi-currency postings: TDR calculations in charter party currency (USD) posted to entity books in functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with exchange differences recorded appropriately. Month-end TDR accrual adjustments must be automated for voyages in progress at period close. The system must generate reconciliation reports between TDR cumulative figures and general ledger balances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R014	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Laytime and Demurrage Tracking within TDR	The system must track laytime usage and demurrage/despatch calculations within the TDR framework for voyage charter vessels. For each port call, the TDR must record: Notice of Readiness (NOR) tendered time, NOR acceptance time (applying charter party-specific NOR provisions such as WIBON, WIPON, WIFPON), laytime commencement, time used for cargo operations, time excluded from laytime (weather working days, holidays at the specific port, customs delays), laytime remaining or time on demurrage/despatch. The system must apply port-specific holiday calendars including Islamic holidays (Eid Al Fitr, Eid Al Adha, National Days) for Gulf ports and Indian national/state holidays for JNPT and Mundra. Demurrage must be calculated at the CP rate per day (pro-rata per hour) in the charter party currency with entity currency conversion. Despatch (if applicable) must be calculated at the CP-specified rate (typically half demurrage). Laytime statements must be generated in English and Arabic formats compliant with BIMCO standard practices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R006	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Performance Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive COA performance dashboard displaying contract utilization percentage, revenue generated versus projected, average freight rate achieved versus contracted rate, vessel deployment efficiency, and port turnaround time metrics. Dashboards must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), trade lane, vessel, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri date range selectors. All financial metrics must be displayable in the user's preferred currency with real-time conversion using system exchange rates. The dashboard must support Arabic language rendering with proper RTL chart labels and data point annotations. AI-generated performance insights must highlight underperforming contracts and recommend corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R015	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Archival and Retrieval System	The system must maintain a complete, searchable archive of all TDR reports generated across all entities and vessels. The archive must support search by: vessel name/IMO number, charter party reference, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), entity, trade lane, and specific data fields (e.g., all TDRs where off-hire was recorded, or where speed variance exceeded a threshold). Archived TDRs must be stored in both the generated report format (PDF/Excel) and as structured data queryable for analytics. The archive must comply with data retention requirements for each entity's jurisdiction: Qatar (QFCRA minimum 5 years), UAE (DIFC minimum 6 years), KSA (SAMA minimum 10 years), and India (Companies Act 8 years). Access to archived TDRs must be controlled by entity and role-based permissions, preventing cross-entity data access unless explicitly authorized. The system must support bulk export of TDR data for external auditor requests with configurable data masking for commercially sensitive fields. Archive storage must use cost-efficient tiered storage with hot/warm/cold tiers based on data age.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R016	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	TDR Dashboard and Real-Time Fleet Overview	The system must provide a real-time TDR dashboard showing the current operational status of all vessels in the managed fleet at a glance. The dashboard must display: vessel positions on an interactive map (with correct rendering for the Gulf-India region), current voyage leg and progress percentage, last reported speed and consumption, days on/off hire, bunker ROB status with low-fuel alerts, and next port ETA. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), charter type (TC-in/TC-out/voyage charter/owned), trade lane, and vessel class. Color-coded status indicators must highlight: vessels performing within CP warranties (green), vessels with minor deviations (amber), vessels with significant performance issues or off-hire events (red). The dashboard must refresh at configurable intervals (default: every 4 hours aligned with noon report cycles) and support both English and Arabic interfaces with proper RTL rendering. Fleet-level aggregated KPIs must show total daily hire cost/revenue, fleet average speed efficiency, and total bunker consumption across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-003-R017	FEAT-012-2-003	MOD-012	Offline TDR Preparation and Synchronization	The system must support offline TDR preparation for scenarios where shore-side connectivity is limited or when operators need to work on TDR reports during travel. The offline module must allow operators to: download pending TDR data to their device, review and edit TDR entries, add comments and annotations, and queue completed TDRs for upload. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline changes with the central system, handling potential conflicts (e.g., another operator modified the same TDR while offline) through a configurable conflict resolution policy (last-write-wins, manual merge, or supervisor arbitration). The offline module must cache reference data including: vessel particulars, charter party terms, bunker price history, port benchmarks, and distribution lists. All offline-prepared TDRs must pass the same validation rules as online TDRs upon synchronization. The offline interface must support both English and Arabic with full RTL rendering capabilities, and all timestamps must properly handle timezone conversions when synchronizing data captured in different timezone contexts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R001	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage Revenue Recognition and Recording	The system must capture and record all revenue components for each voyage including: freight revenue (for voyage charters, calculated from bill of lading quantities at contracted freight rates per TEU/container type), hire income (for time charter-out vessels, based on daily hire rate multiplied by on-hire days), demurrage income (where applicable, from laytime statements), detention charges, container detention and demurrage charges levied on cargo interests, and any ancillary income such as reefer monitoring fees or hazardous cargo surcharges. Revenue must be recognized per IFRS 15 principles with performance obligation identification for each revenue stream. For multi-entity operations, revenue must be recorded in the billing currency (typically USD for international freight, with AED/QAR/SAR/INR for local charges) and translated to the entity's functional currency at the transaction date exchange rate. Revenue allocations for voyages spanning multiple entities must follow the group's intercompany pricing policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R002	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage Cost Capture and Classification	The system must capture all voyage-related costs with proper classification into direct voyage costs, vessel operating costs, and overhead allocations. Direct voyage costs must include: bunker fuel costs (separated by grade: HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, MDO with quantities and unit prices from regional bunkering at Fujairah, Hamad Port, Khor Fakkan, JNPT), port disbursements (categorized by port and cost type for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, Dammam, Jubail, and other calling ports), canal transit fees (Suez Canal when applicable), cargo handling costs (THC, stevedoring), agency fees, insurance voyage premiums, and communication costs. Vessel operating costs for owned/bareboat vessels must include: crew wages, maintenance, stores, lubricants, insurance (H&M, P&I), and management fees. Each cost must be recorded in the invoiced currency with automatic translation to USD and entity functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR). Cost classification must follow the entity's chart of accounts structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R003	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Bunker Cost Allocation Methodology	The system must implement configurable bunker cost allocation methodologies for voyage P&L calculation, supporting: FIFO (First In First Out), LIFO (Last In First Out), weighted average cost, and specific identification methods as selected per entity accounting policy. The bunker cost calculation must track: opening ROB value at voyage commencement (quantity and cost by fuel grade), bunker purchases during the voyage (with delivery port, supplier, quantity, price per MT, and total cost in purchase currency and USD), bunker consumption by purpose (propulsion, auxiliary, boiler, cargo operations, port idle), and closing ROB value at voyage end. For time charter vessels, the system must distinguish between bunker costs for charterer's account (normal consumption) and owner's account (off-hire periods, warming/cooling). Bunker cost allocation must handle multi-currency scenarios where fuel is purchased in different currencies at different ports (USD at Fujairah, QAR at Hamad Port, INR at JNPT) and consolidate into a single voyage P&L currency with exchange rate transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R001	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Purchase Requisition Creation	The system must allow operations and chartering teams to create bunker purchase requisitions specifying vessel IMO number, intended bunkering port (supporting Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHA among others), estimated bunkering date window, fuel grade (VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, MGO per ISO 8217:2024 specifications), required quantity in metric tons, and maximum acceptable price per metric ton. Each requisition must reference the associated voyage number and capture the requesting entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India subsidiary). The system must support Arabic and English language input for all free-text fields and display Hijri and Gregorian dates simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R004	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Port Disbursement Integration and Reconciliation	The system must integrate port disbursement data from port agents across all calling ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, Mundra, Dammam, Jubail, and transshipment hubs) into the voyage P&L. The integration must handle the three-stage disbursement lifecycle: proforma disbursement account (PDA - estimated costs before port call), initial disbursement account (IDA - preliminary actuals after port call), and final disbursement account (FDA - reconciled final costs). The system must automatically reconcile PDA against IDA and FDA, flagging variances exceeding configurable thresholds (default: 10% or USD 1,000 whichever is greater) for operator review. Port disbursements must be categorized by standard FONASBA cost codes for consistent reporting across ports. Disbursements invoiced in local currencies (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port, SAR for Dammam/Jubail, INR for JNPT/Mundra) must be converted to the voyage P&L reporting currency at the payment date exchange rate. Unreconciled port costs must generate accrual entries in the entity's accounting system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R005	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Time Charter Hire Cost Allocation to Voyages	The system must allocate time charter hire costs to individual voyages for TC-in vessels where the daily hire rate represents a fixed cost allocated across multiple consecutive voyages. Allocation methodologies must include: pro-rata by voyage duration (days), pro-rata by revenue contribution, and activity-based allocation considering laden versus ballast legs. The allocation must handle: partial month billing cycles where a single hire payment covers portions of two different voyages, off-hire deductions that reduce allocable hire cost, and address commission/brokerage deductions. For multi-entity scenarios where a vessel is chartered by one entity (e.g., UAE entity) but operates voyages serving multiple entities (e.g., cargo from Hamad Port for Qatar entity, cargo from JNPT for India entity), intercompany hire cost allocation must follow transfer pricing guidelines compliant with OECD principles and local tax regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The allocated hire cost must be recorded in the voyage P&L in both the charter party currency (USD) and the entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R006	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Intercompany Revenue and Cost Allocation for Multi-Entity Voyages	The system must handle intercompany revenue and cost allocation for voyages that involve cargo or services across multiple entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). When a vessel chartered by the UAE entity carries cargo booked by the Qatar entity from Hamad Port to JNPT, the system must generate intercompany transactions allocating freight revenue to the booking entity and vessel costs to the operating entity, with appropriate intercompany margins per the group's transfer pricing policy. Intercompany invoices must be automatically generated in the billing entity's currency with corresponding entries in the receiving entity's currency, using the group's agreed intercompany exchange rates (typically monthly average rates). The system must support configurable allocation keys: by TEU volume, by revenue share, by weight, or by custom formula. Intercompany eliminations must be flagged for consolidation purposes. All intercompany P&L allocations must maintain full audit trails and comply with related-party transaction disclosure requirements under IFRS and local regulations (Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Law, KSA Companies Law, Indian Companies Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R007	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage P&L Estimation at Booking Stage	The system must generate preliminary voyage P&L estimates at the voyage booking/planning stage to support go/no-go decisions for proposed voyages. The estimate must use: projected revenue based on cargo bookings and contracted freight rates, estimated bunker costs using current market prices at planned bunkering ports (Fujairah, Hamad Port, JNPT), estimated port costs based on historical averages for each calling port in the rotation, estimated canal fees (Suez Canal based on vessel tonnage), allocated time charter hire cost based on planned voyage duration, and overhead allocation. The P&L estimate must present multiple scenarios: base case (planned itinerary), optimistic (maximum cargo intake, favorable weather), and pessimistic (reduced bookings, adverse weather, potential delays). AI must compare the estimated P&L against historical voyage performance on the same trade lane to flag unrealistic assumptions. Estimates must be generated in USD with entity currency equivalents (QAR/AED/SAR/INR). The estimate must be stored as a baseline for subsequent variance analysis against actual voyage P&L.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R008	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Real-Time Voyage P&L Tracking During Execution	The system must provide real-time voyage P&L tracking that updates as voyage events occur, showing estimated final P&L based on a combination of actual incurred costs and projected remaining costs. The real-time P&L must incorporate: actual freight revenue from loaded cargo (from bill of lading data), actual bunker consumption to date (from noon reports) with projected remaining consumption, actual port costs from completed port calls with estimated costs for remaining ports, actual off-hire deductions applied, and any other actual costs posted. The system must calculate a voyage P&L completion percentage and compare the trajectory against the pre-voyage estimate, generating alerts when the projected final P&L deviates from the estimate by more than configurable thresholds (default: 10% or USD 25,000). The tracking dashboard must support entity-specific views with local currency display (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) alongside USD base amounts. Real-time P&L must update within 4 hours of new data receipt from vessel noon reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R009	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Final Voyage P&L Statement Generation	The system must generate comprehensive final voyage P&L statements upon voyage completion, consolidating all actual revenue and cost data. The final statement must include sections for: gross revenue (freight, hire, demurrage, ancillary income), less: address commission and brokerage, net revenue, direct voyage costs (bunker, port disbursements, canal fees, cargo handling), vessel operating costs allocation (for owned/bareboat vessels), time charter hire allocation (for TC-in vessels), overhead allocation, voyage contribution margin, and net voyage profit/loss. The statement must show: budgeted vs actual for each line item, variance amount and percentage, and cumulative year-to-date performance for the vessel. The P&L must be prepared in the voyage's base currency (USD) with a translated version in the entity's functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) using actual transaction date rates for individual items and period average rate for income statement presentation per IAS 21. The statement must be exportable in PDF and Excel with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) and entity-specific formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R010	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage P&L Variance Analysis and Commentary	The system must perform detailed variance analysis between estimated and actual voyage P&L, decomposing total variance into contributing factors. Variance categories must include: cargo volume variance (actual TEU vs planned), freight rate variance (actual rate vs planned rate), bunker price variance (actual fuel cost vs planned), bunker consumption variance (actual consumption vs planned based on vessel performance), port cost variance (actual vs estimated at each port including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), speed/routing variance (impact of actual routing decisions on fuel cost), off-hire variance (unplanned off-hire financial impact), and exchange rate variance (for multi-currency transactions). AI must generate natural language variance commentary explaining the top 5 variance drivers in both English and Arabic, referencing specific operational events (e.g., 'Higher bunker cost driven by USD 15/MT price increase at Fujairah between estimate date and actual stem'). The variance analysis must support drill-down from summary to transaction-level detail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R011	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Multi-Currency Voyage P&L Consolidation	The system must support multi-currency consolidation of voyage P&L data for group-level reporting across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Individual voyage P&L statements in entity functional currencies must be translated to the group reporting currency (USD) using IAS 21 methodology: income and expense items at transaction date rates or period average rates, and balance sheet items (accrued revenue, prepaid costs) at closing rates. Exchange differences arising from translation must be recorded in Other Comprehensive Income at the entity level. The consolidation must handle: intercompany elimination of revenues and costs between entities for the same voyage, minority interest adjustments where applicable, and segment reporting by trade lane (Gulf-India, Intra-Gulf, Gulf-KSA, India coastal). The system must provide consolidated voyage P&L reports in USD with comparative columns showing QAR, AED, SAR, and INR equivalents. Consolidation must be performable at any date for management reporting purposes, not limited to month-end periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R012	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage Profitability Benchmarking Across Fleet	The system must enable profitability benchmarking across all voyages in the fleet, normalizing financial performance for meaningful comparison. Benchmarking metrics must include: profit per TEU (net voyage profit divided by total TEU carried), profit per nautical mile, time charter equivalent (TCE) rate (net voyage revenue minus voyage costs divided by voyage duration), daily operating profit, return on vessel employment (ROVE), and bunker efficiency cost (USD per TEU-nm). Benchmarks must be segmented by: trade lane (Gulf-India routes, Intra-Gulf feeders, India coastal), vessel class and size, charter type (TC-in, TC-out, owned, voyage charter), and time period (monthly, quarterly, annually with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar support). The system must rank voyages by profitability and identify top and bottom performers with AI-generated insights explaining performance drivers. Dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL layout. Entity-specific benchmarking must restrict data access per user permissions (Qatar entity users see only Qatar entity voyage data unless cross-entity access is granted).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R013	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	TCE (Time Charter Equivalent) Rate Calculation	The system must calculate the Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) rate for every voyage as the industry-standard profitability metric, following the formula: TCE = (Voyage Revenue - Voyage Costs) / Voyage Duration in Days. Voyage revenue must include all freight income, demurrage earned, and other voyage-related income. Voyage costs must include bunker costs, port disbursements, canal fees, and cargo handling costs, but exclude vessel operating costs and hire payments (to enable fair comparison between owned and chartered vessels). TCE must be calculated in USD per day. The system must generate TCE comparisons against: the vessel's time charter hire rate (to assess whether a voyage charter is more profitable than continuing on time charter), market TCE rates for similar vessel types on the same trade lanes (sourced from market indices), and the entity's minimum acceptable TCE threshold (configurable per entity: Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Historical TCE trends must be tracked per vessel and per trade lane, with seasonal pattern analysis for Gulf-India routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R014	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Overhead Cost Allocation to Voyages	The system must allocate entity overhead costs to individual voyages using configurable allocation methodologies to determine fully-loaded voyage profitability. Overhead categories must include: shore-side staff costs (chartering department, operations department, finance department), office rent and facilities, IT infrastructure costs, insurance (corporate liability, D&O), depreciation of shore-side assets, and general administrative expenses. Allocation methodologies must support: allocation by revenue proportion (voyage revenue as percentage of entity total revenue), allocation by vessel-days (voyage duration as proportion of total fleet vessel-days), allocation by TEU volume, and fixed allocation per voyage. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must maintain its own overhead pool and allocation methodology as overhead structures differ by jurisdiction. The system must present overhead allocation as a separate section in the voyage P&L clearly distinguished from direct voyage costs. Allocated overheads must be recorded in the entity's functional currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with USD equivalent for group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R015	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	AI-Driven Voyage Profitability Optimization Recommendations	The system must employ AI models to analyze historical voyage P&L data and generate actionable recommendations for improving future voyage profitability. Recommendations must cover: optimal vessel speed for cost minimization on specific trade lanes (e.g., eco-speed on Hamad Port to JNPT route during low-demand season), preferred bunkering ports based on price analysis (Fujairah vs Hamad Port vs Khor Fakkan for Gulf bunkering), cargo mix optimization (recommending higher-value reefer or special cargo quotas for specific routes), port rotation sequencing to minimize ballast legs, and optimal vessel deployment across trade lanes based on seasonal demand patterns (pre-Ramadan surge, Indian festival season, year-end peaks). The AI must present recommendations with projected financial impact in USD and entity currencies, confidence levels, and risk factors. Recommendations must be contextualized for each entity's operational scope and presented in English and Arabic with bilingual executive summaries. The system must track recommendation acceptance rates and actual outcomes for model improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R016	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage P&L Approval and Lock-Down Workflow	The system must implement a formal approval workflow for final voyage P&L statements before they are locked and considered official financial records. The workflow must include: operations review (chartering operator verifies all operational data inputs), finance review (entity finance team verifies cost allocations, exchange rates, and accounting treatment), management approval (entity commercial/finance manager signs off), and final lock-down (P&L statement is frozen and no further changes permitted without formal amendment process). Each approval step must have configurable SLA timers and escalation rules. The system must enforce a deadline for voyage P&L finalization (configurable per entity, default: 30 days after voyage completion) after which the P&L is auto-locked with pending items flagged as exceptions. Locked P&L statements must be immutable with any subsequent adjustments processed through a separate voyage P&L amendment workflow requiring senior management approval. The approval interface must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual approval comments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R017	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage P&L Reporting for Tax and Regulatory Compliance	The system must generate voyage P&L reports formatted for tax and regulatory compliance in each entity's jurisdiction. For the Qatar entity, reports must comply with Qatar Tax Authority requirements and Qatar Financial Centre (QFC) reporting standards, noting Qatar's corporate tax rate on petroleum operations while other sectors may have different treatment. For the UAE entity, reports must comply with Federal Tax Authority UAE Corporate Tax requirements (effective June 2023) with correct treatment of qualifying free zone income. For the KSA entity, reports must comply with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) requirements including Zakat computation on voyage profits. For the India entity, reports must comply with Indian Income Tax Act provisions for shipping companies including tonnage tax scheme election under Section 115V-115VZC. The system must generate tax-adjusted voyage P&L reports with applicable tax treatments, transfer pricing documentation for intercompany transactions, and withholding tax tracking on cross-border payments. All reports must be available in English and Arabic (or Hindi for India entity statutory filings).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-2-004-R018	FEAT-012-2-004	MOD-012	Voyage P&L Historical Database and Analytics	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical database of all voyage P&L records across all entities, supporting advanced analytics and trend analysis. The database must enable queries by: vessel, trade lane, charter type, entity, time period (Gregorian and Hijri calendars), cargo type, and profitability range. Analytics capabilities must include: seasonal profitability patterns for Gulf-India trade (identifying peak and trough periods), correlation analysis between bunker price movements and voyage profitability, impact analysis of port efficiency on voyage margins (e.g., how JNPT congestion affects India trade profitability), and trend analysis of TCE rates by vessel class over time. The system must support custom report building with drag-and-drop field selection and configurable aggregation levels. All analytics dashboards must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual labels. The historical database must retain data for a minimum of 10 years, with archival storage for older records. Data export must be available in Excel, CSV, and API formats for integration with external business intelligence tools. Access controls must enforce entity-level data segregation with cross-entity visibility configurable by user role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R001	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Contract Creation with Multi-Entity Support	The system must allow authorized users to create new COA contracts specifying the contracting entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India legal entity), counterparty details, contract duration with both Gregorian and Hijri date support, trade lanes (e.g., Hamad Port QAHMD to JNPT INJNP), minimum and maximum TEU volume commitments per period, laytime terms, demurrage and despatch rates, and applicable freight rate schedules. The contract creation form must support Arabic RTL layout for Arabic-language users and validate all mandatory fields including IMO-compliant vessel class restrictions and container type specifications (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer units). Each COA must generate a unique reference number following entity-specific numbering sequences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R002	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Volume Commitment Tracking and Alerts	The system must continuously track actual shipped volumes against COA contractual commitments on a per-period basis (monthly, quarterly, or annually as defined in the contract). When actual volumes fall below 85% of the minimum commitment threshold or exceed 110% of the maximum commitment with 30 days remaining in the period, the AI engine must generate proactive alerts to chartering managers. Tracking must account for containers shipped from all four operational ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and aggregate volumes across multiple voyages and vessels assigned to the COA. Volume dashboards must display data in both TEU and FEU equivalents with currency-appropriate financial summaries in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R003	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Freight Rate Schedule Management	The system must support complex freight rate structures within COA contracts including base rates per TEU/FEU, surcharges (BAF bunker adjustment factor, CAF currency adjustment factor, THC terminal handling charges, PSS peak season surcharge), and volume-tiered discount schedules. Rate schedules must be definable per trade lane, container type, and cargo category. The system must handle multi-currency rate definitions allowing base rates in USD with surcharges in local currencies (AED for Jebel Ali, QAR for Hamad Port, SAR for KSA ports, INR for JNPT). Automatic rate escalation clauses tied to bunker fuel indices or CPI must be configurable with AI-suggested rate adjustments based on market trend analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R004	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Amendment and Addendum Workflow	The system must provide a formal amendment workflow for modifying active COA contracts including changes to volume commitments, rate schedules, trade lanes, vessel nominations, and contract duration extensions. Each amendment must require multi-level approval based on the financial impact threshold configured per entity (e.g., amendments exceeding USD 50,000 impact require regional director approval). The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all amendments with before-and-after values, amendment effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the approving authority chain. Amendment documents must be generatable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting and bilingual contract clause references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R007	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Nomination and Vessel Assignment	The system must support the nomination process where shippers declare cargo readiness for upcoming shipment windows under the COA and vessel operators assign appropriate tonnage. Nominations must include cargo details (container count by type, weight, hazardous goods classification per IMDG code), preferred loading port, requested laycan window, and destination port. The AI engine must recommend optimal vessel assignments based on vessel position, capacity, fuel efficiency, and schedule compatibility with other COA and spot commitments. Nomination acceptance, counter-nomination, and rejection workflows must be supported with configurable SLA timers (e.g., 48-hour response requirement). All nomination communications must be logged with timestamps in the originating entity's local timezone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R008	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Financial Settlement and Invoice Generation	The system must generate settlement statements and invoices for each voyage completed under a COA contract, calculating net amounts due after applying contracted freight rates, surcharges, demurrage or despatch, and any volume rebates earned. Invoices must be generated in the settlement currency specified in the COA with tax calculations appropriate to the billing entity jurisdiction (VAT for Qatar at 0%, UAE at 5%, KSA at 15%, GST for India at 18%). Invoice templates must support bilingual English-Arabic format with company letterhead and must comply with ZATCA e-invoicing requirements for KSA entities and GST e-invoicing for Indian entities. The system must track payment terms, due dates, and aging per COA with automated payment reminder notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R009	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Counterparty Credit Risk Assessment	The system must maintain counterparty risk profiles for all COA contract parties including credit ratings, payment history analysis, outstanding exposure calculations, and country risk factors. The AI engine must assess credit risk before COA contract execution and continuously monitor exposure during the contract term. When total outstanding receivables from a counterparty exceed a configurable threshold per entity (e.g., USD 500,000 equivalent for Qatar entity), the system must trigger automatic hold on new nominations and alert the credit control team. Risk assessments must consider multi-currency exposure by converting all outstanding amounts to a base currency (USD) using current exchange rates. Historical payment performance data across all four regional entities must be consolidated for group-level risk visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R010	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Clause Library and Template Management	The system must maintain a library of standard COA contract clauses organized by category (freight terms, laytime definitions, demurrage provisions, force majeure, arbitration, governing law) with versions applicable to each entity jurisdiction. Clause templates must be available in both English and Arabic with legally reviewed translations. Users must be able to assemble new COA contracts from template clauses, customize individual clauses with track-changes capability, and submit customized clauses for legal review approval. The AI engine must flag non-standard clause modifications and suggest alternative standard language. Clause libraries must be segregated by entity to reflect jurisdiction-specific legal requirements (Qatar Maritime Law, UAE Federal Maritime Law, KSA Maritime Regulations, Indian Merchant Shipping Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R011	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Contract Expiry and Renewal Management	The system must track COA contract expiry dates using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and generate renewal alerts at configurable intervals (90, 60, and 30 days before expiry). The AI engine must prepare renewal proposals incorporating historical performance data, market rate trends, volume forecast projections, and recommended rate adjustments. Renewal proposals must be generated in the contract's primary language (English or Arabic) and include comparative analysis of current versus proposed terms. The system must support automatic contract extension clauses where the COA rolls over for a defined period unless either party issues a termination notice. Expired contracts must be archived with full searchability and must not accept new nominations after the expiry date plus any contractual grace period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R012	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Multi-Trade Lane and Transshipment Support	The system must support COA contracts covering multiple trade lanes with different rate structures per lane, including transshipment routes through hub ports such as Jebel Ali (AEJEA) and Hamad Port (QAHMD). For transshipment cargo, the system must track the entire routing chain from origin to destination, allocate freight revenue proportionally across voyage legs, and calculate combined laytime across all ports of call. COA contracts involving transshipment must define responsibility boundaries for each leg (which entity bears the transshipment cost and risk). The system must validate that nominated vessels can service all trade lanes specified in the COA considering draft restrictions, port access permissions, and flag state requirements applicable at each port in the routing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R013	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Dispute Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a dispute management module within COA contracts to log, track, and resolve disagreements related to laytime calculations, demurrage claims, cargo damage, short shipment, and rate interpretation. Each dispute must be categorized by type, assigned a severity level, linked to the specific voyage and invoice records, and tracked through configurable resolution stages (raised, under review, mediation, arbitration, settled, closed). The system must calculate financial exposure for each open dispute in the contract settlement currency and aggregate dispute exposure at the counterparty and entity level. Dispute resolution timelines must account for jurisdiction-specific arbitration rules as specified in the COA governing law clause. All dispute correspondence must be storable in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R014	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Compliance and Sanctions Screening	The system must perform automated sanctions screening against OFAC, EU, UN, and local GCC sanctions lists for all COA counterparties, beneficial owners, and nominated vessels at contract creation and on an ongoing periodic basis (configurable, default weekly). When a match or potential match is detected, the system must immediately suspend the COA for review, block new nominations, and alert the compliance officer of the relevant entity. The screening must cover vessel flag state restrictions, port state restrictions, and cargo origin/destination restrictions relevant to Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India regulatory frameworks. All screening results must be archived with timestamps and the screening database version used, maintaining a complete audit trail for regulatory examination. The system must support manual override with dual-authorization for false positive clearance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R015	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Offline Access and Data Synchronization	The system must support offline access to active COA contract details, voyage schedules, and nomination status for chartering personnel operating in areas with intermittent connectivity such as onboard vessels or at port facilities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Offline data must be cached on the client device with AES-256 encryption and must include the last synchronized contract terms, rate schedules, vessel assignments, and pending nominations. Upon reconnection, the system must perform bidirectional synchronization with conflict detection and resolution for any nominations or status updates made simultaneously by multiple users. Offline mode must support both English and Arabic UI rendering. Data synchronization logs must record the timestamp, user, entity, and data elements synchronized for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-001-R016	FEAT-012-3-001	MOD-012	COA Reporting and Regulatory Export	The system must generate regulatory and management reports for COA contracts including contract register reports, volume commitment compliance reports, financial performance reports, and counterparty exposure summaries. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with proper Arabic text rendering in exported documents. Regulatory reports must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Authority requirements, UAE Federal Tax Authority reporting standards, KSA ZATCA requirements, and Indian DGFT reporting obligations. The system must support scheduled automatic report generation and distribution to configured recipients via email. All reports must include entity identification, reporting period in both Gregorian and Hijri dates, currency denomination, and exchange rates applied. AI-generated executive summaries must be available in both English and Arabic for each report.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R001	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC-In Charter Party Agreement Registration	The system must allow chartering managers to register new Time Charter-In agreements capturing all essential charter party terms including vessel particulars (IMO number, name, flag, DWT, TEU capacity, gear/gearless, reefer plugs), delivery and redelivery ports (supporting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT and any global port by UNLOCODE), charter period with optional extension clauses, daily hire rate in specified currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), speed and consumption warranties, trading limits and exclusions, and applicable charter party form (NYPE 2015, BOXTIME 2004, or custom). The registration must link to the contracting entity and require entity-specific approval workflows. All date fields must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar input.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R002	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC-Out Charter Party Agreement Registration	The system must support registration of Time Charter-Out agreements where owned or controlled vessels are leased to third-party charterers, capturing charterer details with credit verification, vessel assignment from the managed fleet, charter duration with minimum/maximum period ranges, daily hire rate and payment terms, charterer trading restrictions, sub-letting permissions, bunker delivery and redelivery quantities and prices, and BIMCO-standard charter party terms. The TC-Out agreement must validate that the vessel is not already committed under another TC-Out or committed to COA obligations for the proposed charter period. For multi-entity operations, the system must identify which entity owns or controls the vessel and ensure proper inter-company billing if the chartering entity differs from the vessel-owning entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R003	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	Hire Payment Schedule and Processing	The system must automatically generate hire payment schedules for TC-In agreements based on the charter party payment terms (typically 15-day advance payments) and hire collection schedules for TC-Out agreements. For TC-In, the system must calculate each hire installment including the daily rate multiplied by the payment period, deduct any off-hire time, add or subtract bunker adjustments, and generate payment instructions in the charter party currency. For TC-Out, the system must generate hire invoices to charterers with tax implications per entity jurisdiction (VAT at 5% for UAE, 15% for KSA, 0% for Qatar, GST at 18% for India). Payment schedules must account for banking holidays in the payment destination country and support automated bank transfer file generation in SWIFT MT101 format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R004	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	Off-Hire Event Recording and Calculation	The system must allow operations personnel to record off-hire events for TC-In vessels when the vessel is unable to perform the chartered service due to mechanical breakdown, dry-docking, crew issues, or other owner-attributable causes. Each off-hire record must capture the start and end date-time (with timezone, supporting AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), off-hire reason category, port or sea position at the time of the event, estimated repair duration, and supporting documentation. The system must automatically calculate the hire deduction for the off-hire period, pro-rating the daily hire rate to the exact hours and minutes of off-hire. For TC-Out vessels, equivalent off-hire tracking must capture charterer-claimed off-hire events with an owner review and acceptance workflow including dispute flagging capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R005	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	Bunker Management for Time Charters	The system must track bunker fuel quantities and values at delivery, during the charter period, and at redelivery for both TC-In and TC-Out agreements. At delivery, the system must record bunker ROB (remaining on board) quantities for each fuel type (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LNG) with the agreed price per metric ton in the charter party currency. During the charter period, the system must track bunker purchases made by the charterer (for TC-In) or by the vessel operator (for TC-Out) with actual purchase invoices and quantities. At redelivery, the system must calculate the bunker adjustment based on ROB quantities and contractually agreed prices, generating a debit or credit note in the appropriate currency. The AI engine must monitor bunker consumption against speed-consumption warranties and flag deviations exceeding 5% from warranted figures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R006	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	Vessel Delivery and Redelivery Management	The system must manage the vessel delivery and redelivery process for both TC-In and TC-Out charters including delivery/redelivery notice tracking (configurable notice periods, typically 15, 10, 7, and 3 days approximate and 3 days definite), condition survey scheduling and report attachment, bunker survey coordination, on-hire and off-hire survey document management, and holds/tanks inspection records. For delivery at GCC ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), the system must account for port-specific requirements including local agent appointment, immigration clearance timelines, and maritime authority approvals. For JNPT deliveries, Indian customs and port health clearance workflows must be supported. The system must generate delivery and redelivery statements automatically calculating prorated hire, bunker adjustments, and any outstanding claims to produce a final settlement figure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R007	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Period Extension and Early Redelivery Handling	The system must handle charter period extensions and early redelivery scenarios for both TC-In and TC-Out agreements. For extension requests, the system must validate the extension against the charter party's optional period clauses, calculate revised hire costs at the extension rate (which may differ from the base rate), and update the vessel's forward schedule and availability calendar. For early redelivery, the system must calculate any ballast bonus or early redelivery penalty as specified in the charter party, adjust the final hire statement, and update vessel availability for redeployment. The AI engine must proactively identify upcoming redelivery windows and recommend whether to extend, redeliver, or negotiate new terms based on current market rates, vessel demand forecasts, and the entity's fleet deployment strategy across all four regional operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R008	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Financial Accounting Integration	The system must integrate TC-In and TC-Out financial transactions with the general ledger module, posting hire expense (TC-In) or hire revenue (TC-Out) journal entries according to the entity's chart of accounts and IFRS 16 lease accounting requirements. For TC-In, the system must classify charters as either operating leases or finance leases based on charter duration relative to vessel useful life and present value of hire payments, creating right-of-use asset and lease liability entries for finance leases. Multi-currency hire payments must be posted at the transaction date exchange rate with monthly revaluation of outstanding balances. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must maintain separate accounting books with proper inter-company elimination entries when a vessel owned by one entity is chartered to another group entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R009	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	Speed and Performance Claims Management	The system must monitor vessel speed and fuel consumption performance against charter party warranties for TC-In vessels and track performance claims from charterers for TC-Out vessels. The AI engine must analyze vessel noon reports (daily position and performance reports) to calculate weather-adjusted vessel speed using the Beaufort scale wind force and Douglas sea state parameters, comparing actual performance against warranted speed and consumption figures. When performance falls below warranted levels by more than a configurable threshold (default 0.5 knots for speed, 3% for consumption), the system must automatically generate performance claim documentation including voyage details, weather data, calculated underperformance, and financial impact in the charter party currency. Claims must be tracked through negotiation stages with supporting evidence attachment and final settlement recording.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R010	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Vessel Insurance and P&I Compliance Tracking	The system must track insurance and P&I (Protection and Indemnity) club cover for all TC-In and TC-Out vessels, validating that coverage meets charter party requirements including hull and machinery insurance, P&I cover with minimum entry values, war risk insurance for applicable trading areas, and FDD (Freight Demurrage and Defence) cover. The system must alert when insurance certificates approach expiry (45, 30, and 15 days before expiry) and block vessel deployment if certificates lapse. For TC-In vessels, the system must store owner-provided insurance documentation and verify coverage against trading area requirements, particularly for Gulf region war risk areas. For TC-Out vessels, the system must ensure that charterer-procured additional insurances (e.g., charterer's liability) are in place before charter commencement, with document storage supporting both English and Arabic certificate formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R011	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Market Rate Benchmarking	The system must maintain a database of market charter rates sourced from industry benchmarks (Baltic Exchange indices, broker panel assessments, and historical fixture data) segmented by vessel size, type, and trading region. The AI engine must compare current TC-In hire rates against market benchmarks to assess whether the company is paying above or below market for chartered tonnage, and similarly benchmark TC-Out rates to evaluate revenue optimization. Market rate data must be updated at minimum weekly with the ability to import rates from external data feeds. Benchmarking reports must display rate trends over configurable periods (3, 6, 12, 24 months), highlight rate differentials in the user's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), and provide AI-generated market outlook commentary for the Middle East and Indian subcontinent container shipping segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R012	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Vessel Sub-Letting Controls	The system must enforce sub-letting restrictions as defined in TC-In charter party agreements, preventing unauthorized sub-chartering of hired-in tonnage. When a TC-In vessel is proposed for TC-Out or COA assignment, the system must validate that the original charter party permits sub-letting, check any geographical trading restrictions that would be violated, and ensure the sub-let rate does not trigger any profit-sharing clauses with the vessel owner. For permitted sub-letting, the system must maintain a clear chain of charter party references linking the head charter (TC-In) to the sub-charter (TC-Out), ensure proper back-to-back terms alignment, and calculate the margin between head charter cost and sub-charter revenue per day with multi-currency conversion where the head charter and sub-charter are in different currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R013	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Portfolio Dashboard and Exposure Analysis	The system must provide a consolidated TC portfolio dashboard showing all active TC-In and TC-Out agreements across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with key metrics including total hired-in capacity (TEU), total chartered-out capacity (TEU), net charter position, average daily hire cost versus revenue, forward commitment profile by month, and total financial exposure. The dashboard must display data in both summary and detailed views with entity-level drill-down capability. Financial exposure must be calculable in any supported currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) using live exchange rates. Arabic-language users must see all dashboard labels, tooltips, and AI-generated insights rendered in RTL format. The AI engine must highlight portfolio concentration risks such as excessive dependence on a single vessel owner or charterer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R014	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Regulatory and Flag State Compliance	The system must validate TC-In and TC-Out vessel deployments against flag state regulations, port state requirements, and regional maritime authority rules applicable to Qatar Maritime Administration, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. Validation must check vessel flag state acceptability at each planned port of call, crew nationality and certification requirements, vessel age restrictions (some terminals restrict vessels over 20 years), and environmental compliance certificates (EIAPP, IAPP, ballast water management). The system must maintain a configurable rules engine mapping port codes (QAHMD, AEKAL, AEJEA, INJNP) to their specific entry requirements. Non-compliant vessel deployments must be blocked with clear violation details displayed in the user's preferred language (English or Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R015	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Communication and Document Management	The system must provide a centralized communication and document repository for each TC-In and TC-Out agreement storing all related documents including signed charter party agreements, addenda and amendments, delivery and redelivery certificates, hire statements, off-hire notices, performance claim correspondence, insurance certificates, class certificates, and vessel condition survey reports. Documents must be uploadable in PDF, Word, Excel, and image formats with OCR text extraction for searchability. The system must support document tagging in both English and Arabic with full-text search across all TC documents. Version control must track document revisions with timestamps and user attribution. Access controls must enforce entity-level document segregation ensuring that personnel from one entity cannot access another entity's commercially sensitive TC agreements without explicit cross-entity authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-002-R016	FEAT-012-3-002	MOD-012	TC Offline Hire Calculation and Sync	The system must provide offline capability for calculating hire payments, off-hire deductions, and bunker adjustments when chartering staff are aboard vessels or at port locations with limited internet connectivity at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The offline module must cache active TC agreement terms, current hire rate schedules, bunker prices, and recent off-hire records with local AES-256 encryption. Users must be able to create draft hire statements, record off-hire events, and prepare bunker survey data while offline. Upon reconnection, all offline transactions must synchronize with the central system using timestamp-based conflict resolution, flagging any discrepancies for manual review. The offline interface must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R001	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Enquiry Registration and Categorization	The system must allow chartering brokers and managers to register new fixture enquiries capturing the enquiry source (direct from owner, broker, or market circular), vessel type and size requirements, cargo type and quantity (TEU, weight, hazmat classification), loading and discharge port ranges with specific port references (e.g., Hamad Port QAHMD, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INJNP), laycan window in both Gregorian and Hijri dates, preferred charter type (voyage charter, spot, TC-In, TC-Out), indicative rate level, and any special requirements (ice class, specific crane capacity, reefer plug count). Each enquiry must be categorized by urgency (routine, urgent, critical) and linked to the originating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India). The system must auto-assign a unique enquiry reference number.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R002	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Negotiation Workflow with Stage Tracking	The system must track the complete negotiation workflow through defined stages: Initial Enquiry, Indication Sent, Firm Offer, Counter-Offer (multiple rounds), Subjects (with subject lifting deadline), Fixture Confirmed (clean fix), or Failed/Withdrawn. Each stage transition must be timestamped with the user, entity, and communication method recorded. The system must support parallel negotiations for the same cargo requirement with multiple vessel owners or for the same vessel with multiple charterers, maintaining separate negotiation threads. Stage progression rules must enforce that firm offers cannot be issued without entity-specific authorization levels (e.g., offers above USD 100,000 daily hire equivalent require senior chartering manager approval). The AI engine must recommend optimal negotiation timing based on market momentum analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R003	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Offer and Counter-Offer Term Sheet Management	The system must provide structured term sheet forms for capturing and comparing offers and counter-offers including freight or hire rate, laycan dates, loading and discharge rates, laytime terms, demurrage rate, address commission percentage, brokerage percentage, cargo exclusions, trading limits, bunker delivery quantities, and any special clauses. Term sheets must support multi-currency rate entry (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to a base comparison currency. The system must generate side-by-side comparison views for up to five competing offers highlighting the key differences in rates, terms, and conditions. Each term sheet revision must be versioned with an immutable audit trail. Term sheet templates must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL layout for Arabic-language negotiators operating from GCC offices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R004	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Subjects Management and Lifting Deadlines	The system must manage the subjects phase of fixture negotiations where a fixture is agreed subject to specified conditions (e.g., subject stem confirmation, subject shipper's approval, subject board approval, subject details of cargo). Each subject must be individually tracked with its description, responsible party, deadline date and time with timezone (supporting AST, GST, IST), and current status (open, lifted, failed). The system must send automated reminder notifications at configurable intervals before subject deadlines (e.g., 24 hours, 12 hours, 4 hours, 1 hour before deadline). When all subjects are lifted, the system must automatically transition the fixture to Confirmed status and trigger downstream processes including voyage creation and vessel scheduling. If any subject fails or the deadline passes without lifting, the system must mark the fixture as Failed with reason recording.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R005	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Broker and Counterparty Database Integration	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of shipbrokers and counterparties (vessel owners, charterers, operators) used in fixture negotiations, linked to the counterparty master data module. Each broker record must include company details, individual broker contacts, preferred communication channels, commission rate agreements per entity, historical fixture performance (fixtures concluded, average negotiation duration, reliability rating), and sanctions screening status. The system must support broker panel management where chartering teams can maintain preferred broker lists per trade lane and vessel segment. When registering a new fixture enquiry, the system must auto-suggest relevant brokers based on the trade lane and vessel type using AI analysis of historical success rates. Broker data must be accessible in both English and Arabic name formats with proper transliteration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R006	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Communication Log and Correspondence Trail	The system must provide an integrated communication log for each fixture negotiation capturing all correspondence between the chartering team, brokers, and counterparties. The log must support manual entry of phone call summaries, email attachment linking (integrating with the company email system), instant message records, and formal offer documents. Each log entry must be timestamped, attributed to a specific user and entity, and categorized by communication type. The system must support email parsing to automatically extract key negotiation terms (rate, laycan, port names) from broker emails and populate offer tracking fields. Communication logs must be searchable across all negotiations with filters for entity, trade lane, vessel, broker, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and keyword. Arabic-language correspondence must be properly stored and displayed with RTL text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R007	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Financial Analysis and Voyage Estimate	The system must provide integrated financial analysis tools for evaluating fixture proposals including voyage estimate calculations showing projected revenue, bunker costs at current market prices, port disbursements for all ports of call (with specific cost databases for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT), canal transit fees where applicable (Suez Canal tolls), insurance increments, broker commissions, and net daily TCE (Time Charter Equivalent) rate. Voyage estimates must be calculable in any supported currency with the base calculation in USD and conversion to AED, QAR, SAR, or INR at current exchange rates. The AI engine must compare the projected TCE against market benchmarks and historical fixtures to provide a recommendation score (strong accept, marginal, reject). Break-even freight rate calculations must account for entity-specific overhead allocations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R008	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	AI-Assisted Rate Prediction for Negotiations	The system must provide AI-powered rate prediction to assist negotiators during fixture discussions. The AI model must analyze historical fixture data, current market indices (Baltic Exchange, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index), seasonal demand patterns for Middle East and Indian subcontinent routes, bunker fuel price trends, and current vessel supply-demand balance to predict likely achievable rates for specific trade lanes. Predictions must be presented as a range (low, expected, high) with confidence intervals and must update in near-real-time as market conditions change. The AI must factor in port-specific dynamics such as Jebel Ali hub premium, Hamad Port development capacity changes, JNPT congestion surcharges, and seasonal weather impacts on Arabian Sea routes. Rate predictions must be displayable in USD with one-click conversion to AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R009	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Register Search and Reporting	The system must provide a comprehensive searchable fixture register containing all historical and active fixtures across all four regional entities. Search filters must include fixture status, entity, trade lane, vessel name or IMO number, charterer or owner name, broker name, charter type, fixture date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection), rate range, and container type. The register must support both list and calendar views with color-coded status indicators. Standard reports must include fixture activity reports (enquiries received, offers made, fixtures concluded, hit rate), average fixture rate reports by trade lane and period, broker performance reports, and negotiation duration analysis. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel with proper Arabic text rendering. The AI engine must generate weekly market commentary summarizing fixture trends relevant to company operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R010	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Approval Workflow with Delegation Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable approval matrix for fixture confirmation based on the entity, charter type, financial commitment level, and charter duration. The approval matrix must define escalation thresholds such as fixtures below USD 25,000 per day requiring chartering manager approval, USD 25,000-50,000 requiring head of chartering approval, and above USD 50,000 requiring commercial director approval, with entity-specific variations. The system must support delegation of approval authority during absence periods with automatic routing to delegates. Approval requests must include a summary of the fixture terms, AI-generated financial analysis, market comparison data, and risk assessment. Approvers must be able to approve, reject with comments, or request modifications. The approval workflow must function on mobile devices for approvers traveling between Doha, Dubai, Riyadh, and Mumbai offices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R011	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture to Charter Party Document Generation	The system must auto-generate draft charter party documents from confirmed fixture terms, populating standard charter party forms (NYPE 2015, GENCON 2022, BOXTIME 2004) with the negotiated terms from the fixture register. The document generation engine must merge fixture data (vessel particulars, rate, laycan, ports, laytime terms) into the selected charter party template, highlight clauses that require manual review or customization, and generate the document in both English and Arabic as required. Generated documents must be editable within the system with track-changes functionality for legal review. The system must maintain a mapping between fixture register fields and charter party clause locations to ensure accurate data population. Documents must include proper entity letterhead, legal entity details, and jurisdiction-specific regulatory references for Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R012	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Competitive Intelligence and Market Positioning	The system must collect and analyze publicly available fixture data from industry sources to provide competitive intelligence on market participants, including competitor fixture activity on key trade lanes, rate trends by vessel segment, and market share estimates for the Middle East to Indian subcontinent container shipping segment. The AI engine must identify patterns in competitor behavior such as seasonal capacity adjustments, new trade lane entries, and rate strategy shifts. Market positioning reports must show the company's fixture rates relative to market averages segmented by trade lane (Hamad-JNPT, Jebel Ali-JNPT, Khalifa-JNPT, inter-GCC routes). Intelligence data must be updated at minimum weekly and presented in dashboards with both English and Arabic language support. All competitive intelligence must carry appropriate caveats regarding data source reliability and estimation methodology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R013	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Cancellation and Dead Freight Management	The system must handle fixture cancellation scenarios including full cancellation by either party, partial cargo reduction resulting in dead freight claims, and laycan cancellation when the vessel fails to present within the agreed loading window. For cancellation events, the system must calculate financial exposure including dead freight at the contracted rate for unfilled slots, damages for lost alternative fixtures, broker commission implications, and any applicable cancellation penalty as per charter party terms. Dead freight claims must be generated in the fixture's base currency with automatic conversion to the claimant entity's reporting currency. The system must maintain cancellation history per counterparty to inform the AI credit risk assessment model. Cancellation notifications must be distributable to all affected parties through the integrated communication system in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-001-R014	FEAT-006-1-001	MOD-006	Guest Booking with Account Conversion	First-time customers create a one-time booking as a guest with the option to convert to a registered portal account post-booking.	must_have			["Guest booking requires only company name, contact email, mobile number, and trade license number for identity verification", "Guest bookings are limited to a maximum of 2 TEU per submission and standard (non-DG, non-reefer) cargo only", "After booking confirmation, system prompts guest to register a full account with KYC document upload for future bookings", "Guest booking data automatically migrates to the new registered account upon successful KYC verification"]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R014	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Multi-Broker Negotiation Coordination	The system must support fixture negotiations conducted simultaneously through multiple brokers for the same cargo stem or vessel position, maintaining separate confidential negotiation threads per broker while providing the chartering team a consolidated view of all parallel negotiations. The system must prevent accidental information leakage between broker channels by enforcing strict data segregation per negotiation thread. When a fixture is concluded through one broker, the system must automatically notify other brokers that the enquiry is closed (without revealing the concluded terms) and update all related negotiation threads to withdrawn status. Commission calculations must correctly identify the concluding broker for commission payment while tracking introduction broker credits where applicable. The system must support communication with brokers in the user's and the broker's preferred language, including Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R015	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture Register Offline Access and Quick Entry	The system must provide offline access to the fixture register for chartering personnel who need to record fixture negotiations while traveling or at port locations with limited connectivity. The offline module must allow creation of new enquiry records, recording of offer and counter-offer terms, updating negotiation status, and attaching notes with timestamps. A quick-entry mode must be available for rapid fixture registration during fast-moving market situations, requiring only essential fields (vessel, rate, laycan, ports, charterer/owner) with a streamlined single-screen form. Offline data must be encrypted using AES-256 on the local device and synchronized to the central system upon reconnection with conflict detection for any negotiation updates made by other team members. The quick-entry interface must support both English and Arabic input with auto-detection of language direction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-003-R016	FEAT-012-3-003	MOD-012	Fixture KPI Tracking and Team Performance Metrics	The system must track key performance indicators for the chartering team's fixture negotiation activities including number of enquiries processed, offers issued, fixtures concluded, average negotiation duration from enquiry to fixture, fixture success rate (hit rate), average TCE achieved versus market benchmark, and revenue generated per chartering executive. KPIs must be trackable at individual, team, entity, and group-wide levels with configurable reporting periods aligned to both Gregorian fiscal quarters and Hijri calendar periods. Performance dashboards must support drill-down from group summary to individual fixture details. The AI engine must identify high-performing negotiation patterns and generate coaching recommendations for underperforming areas. All KPI displays must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL numeral formatting (supporting both Western Arabic and Eastern Arabic numerals as per user preference).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R001	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Real-Time Fleet Position and Capacity Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time fleet position dashboard displaying all managed vessels (owned, TC-In, and COA-committed) on an interactive map with current position (from AIS data feed integration), heading, speed, destination, ETA at next port, current cargo load as percentage of TEU capacity, available reefer plug utilization, and operational status (laden voyage, ballast voyage, in port loading, in port discharging, waiting, off-hire, dry-dock). The dashboard must highlight vessels approaching key operational ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) with ETA countdown displays. Vessel data must be refreshable at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes) and support both satellite view and schematic route view. All map labels and vessel information panels must support English and Arabic language switching with proper RTL text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R002	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	AI Demand Forecasting for Vessel Deployment	The AI engine must forecast container shipping demand across managed trade lanes for rolling 4, 8, 12, and 26-week horizons using historical shipment volumes, seasonal patterns (Ramadan logistics shifts, Indian festival seasons, year-end peaks), macroeconomic indicators for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India (GDP growth, trade balance data, manufacturing PMI), confirmed COA volume commitments, and pipeline of spot enquiries in the fixture register. Demand forecasts must be segmented by trade lane, container type (dry, reefer, special equipment), and cargo category. The AI must quantify forecast confidence levels and present prediction intervals rather than point estimates. Forecasts must automatically adjust when significant market events are detected (port congestion, canal disruption, sanctions changes). Demand data must be presentable in both TEU volumes and financial value terms in any supported currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R003	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Optimal Vessel-Cargo Matching Algorithm	The AI engine must implement a vessel-cargo matching algorithm that evaluates all open cargo requirements (from COA nominations, spot fixtures, and internal bookings) against available vessel capacity to recommend optimal assignments. The matching algorithm must consider vessel position and sailing distance to loading port, vessel TEU and weight capacity remaining, container type compatibility (including reefer plug availability), vessel draft constraints versus port depth at Hamad Port (15m), Khalifa Port (18m), Jebel Ali (17m), and JNPT (14m), cargo hazardous goods class compatibility with vessel certifications, loading and discharge port compatibility with vessel trading limits, laycan window feasibility, and fuel cost optimization based on vessel-specific consumption profiles. Each recommendation must include a utilization score, financial impact assessment in USD, and alternative options ranked by overall efficiency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R004	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Ballast Voyage Minimization and Triangulation	The AI engine must minimize unproductive ballast voyages by identifying triangulation opportunities where a vessel completing a discharge can pick up return cargo from a nearby port rather than ballasting empty back to the loading region. The optimizer must search across all open cargo requirements in the system, COA commitments due for lifting, and spot market opportunities to find cargo that can be economically combined into multi-leg voyages. For Middle East to India trade lanes, the system must specifically optimize the Jebel Ali/Hamad Port to JNPT route and identify backhaul cargo from Indian ports to GCC ports. The AI must calculate the financial benefit of each triangulation option including additional port costs, bunker savings versus additional consumption, time value of the vessel at current market hire rates, and impact on subsequent committed schedules. Results must be presented with net TCE comparison in the user's preferred currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R005	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Vessel Speed Optimization for Fleet Efficiency	The AI engine must recommend optimal sailing speeds for each voyage leg considering the vessel's speed-consumption curve, current and forecast bunker fuel prices at bunkering ports in the region (Fujairah, Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, JNPT), port arrival windows and berth availability schedules, laytime implications of early or late arrival, weather routing data for Arabian Sea and Persian Gulf conditions, and carbon emission targets per vessel and fleet-wide. The optimizer must calculate the cost-benefit of slow steaming versus economic speed versus full speed for each leg, presenting the analysis as a time-cost-emission trade-off matrix. Speed recommendations must account for charter party speed warranties on TC-Out vessels to avoid performance claims. The system must update speed recommendations dynamically when schedule changes occur and alert the operations team when significant deviations from recommended speed profiles are detected via AIS monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R006	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Port Rotation and Schedule Optimization	The AI engine must optimize vessel port rotation sequences to maximize cargo volume carried while minimizing total voyage time and cost. For services calling at multiple ports in the GCC and Indian subcontinent region, the optimizer must evaluate alternative port sequences considering cargo volume and destination mix, port productivity rates (moves per hour at each terminal), tidal windows and draft restrictions at each port, berth availability and terminal congestion levels from port community system data feeds, port-specific charges and tariff structures, container repositioning needs (empty container imbalances), and customs clearance processing times. The optimizer must generate recommended port rotations with projected schedule timelines, compare them against the current schedule, and quantify the improvement in vessel utilization percentage and voyage profitability. Optimization results must be viewable in both English and Arabic with Hijri calendar voyage timeline displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R007	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Fleet Composition and Chartering Recommendation Engine	The AI engine must analyze the current fleet composition (owned and TC-In vessels) against forecast demand and COA commitments to recommend fleet adjustments. Recommendations must include whether to charter-in additional tonnage specifying vessel type, size, and optimal charter period, whether to relet or redeliver underutilized TC-In vessels, whether to offer excess owned capacity for TC-Out, and the optimal mix of owned versus chartered tonnage for each entity's operations. The recommendation engine must factor in TC-In market rates, owned vessel operating costs, dry-docking schedules, regulatory compliance requirements, and seasonal demand variations. Financial impact of each recommendation must be quantified over 3, 6, and 12-month horizons in USD with entity-specific currency equivalents. Recommendations must align with each entity's budgetary constraints and fleet strategy parameters as configured by the commercial director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R008	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Container Slot Utilization Maximization	The AI engine must optimize container slot utilization on each vessel voyage by analyzing booking requests against available capacity and recommending cargo acceptance priorities. The optimizer must consider slot weight utilization versus TEU utilization (preventing weight-out scenarios where the vessel reaches maximum deadweight before all TEU slots are filled), container mix optimization (balancing 20ft, 40ft, and 45ft units for optimal stowage), reefer container concentration limits per hold, hazardous cargo segregation requirements per IMDG code affecting available slot positions, high-cube container placement constraints in under-deck holds, and revenue per slot prioritization to maximize voyage revenue. The system must calculate optimal booking limits per port pair to prevent displacement of higher-value cargo at downstream loading ports. Slot utilization analytics must display actual versus optimized scenarios with revenue differential in the voyage's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R009	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Predictive Maintenance Impact on Utilization	The AI engine must integrate with the vessel maintenance module to predict how upcoming maintenance events, class surveys, and dry-docking schedules will impact fleet utilization and proactively recommend mitigation strategies. The system must forecast vessel downtime for scheduled maintenance, predict unscheduled maintenance probability based on vessel age, running hours, and historical defect patterns, and calculate the utilization impact of each maintenance scenario. Mitigation recommendations must include rescheduling non-critical maintenance to low-demand periods, pre-positioning TC-In cover to replace vessels entering dry-dock, and adjusting COA nomination schedules to avoid commitment breaches during maintenance periods. The AI must generate a 12-month forward maintenance impact calendar showing expected fleet availability across all four regional entities with financial impact estimates for each maintenance event in the respective entity's operating currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R010	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Weather and Disruption Impact Modeling	The AI engine must model the impact of weather events, port disruptions, canal closures, and other operational disruptions on fleet utilization and schedule adherence. The system must integrate weather forecast data for the Persian Gulf, Arabian Sea, Gulf of Aden, and Indian Ocean regions, historical port congestion data for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and real-time disruption feeds. When a disruptive event is detected or forecast (e.g., tropical cyclone in Arabian Sea, sandstorm affecting port operations, Suez Canal transit delays), the AI must recalculate affected vessel schedules, identify at-risk COA commitments and charter party obligations, and recommend corrective actions including speed adjustments, port rotation changes, and vessel substitutions. Disruption impact must be quantified in terms of delay days, additional bunker cost, potential demurrage exposure, and cargo delivery delay penalties, with all financial figures in USD and converted to entity-specific currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R011	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Empty Container Repositioning Optimization	The AI engine must optimize empty container repositioning to minimize the cost of correcting equipment imbalances between surplus and deficit ports in the network. The optimizer must forecast empty container supply and demand at each port based on loaded import and export volumes, track empty container inventory at each port and depot (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and associated inland depots), and identify the lowest-cost repositioning routes considering vessel space availability on scheduled services, inland trucking costs, and container leasing alternatives (leasing versus repositioning cost comparison). The system must differentiate between container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer) as repositioning needs differ by equipment type. Repositioning recommendations must include multi-modal options where truck or rail inland transport to a nearby port may be cheaper than sea repositioning. All cost calculations must use port-specific handling tariffs in local currencies with automatic conversion to USD for comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R012	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Utilization KPI Dashboard and Benchmarking	The system must provide a comprehensive utilization KPI dashboard displaying fleet-wide and per-vessel utilization metrics including overall TEU utilization percentage per voyage, weight utilization percentage, revenue per TEU (comparing actual versus maximum achievable), vessel days at sea versus in port versus idle, ballast ratio (ballast days as percentage of total voyage days), schedule reliability (actual versus planned port arrivals), carbon efficiency (CO2 emissions per TEU-nautical mile), and operating cost per TEU. KPIs must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel ownership type (owned, TC-In), trade lane, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection. The dashboard must support benchmarking against industry averages and configurable company targets. AI-generated insights must highlight underperforming vessels with root cause analysis and improvement recommendations. All KPI labels and insights must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R013	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Multi-Entity Fleet Sharing Optimization	The AI engine must optimize vessel sharing across the four regional entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to maximize group-wide utilization. When one entity has excess vessel capacity and another has unmet cargo demand, the system must recommend vessel redeployment with proper inter-company chartering terms, transfer pricing compliant with OECD guidelines and each jurisdiction's transfer pricing regulations (Qatar Tax Authority, UAE CT rules, KSA ZATCA, India Transfer Pricing rules under Income Tax Act). The optimizer must calculate whether it is more cost-effective for the deficit entity to use a group vessel or charter from the open market, ensuring arm's length pricing. Inter-company charter agreements must be automatically generated with entity-specific legal terms. Revenue and cost allocations must be posted to each entity's books in their respective currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with proper elimination entries at the consolidated group level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R014	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Carbon Emission Optimization and CII Compliance	The AI engine must optimize fleet deployment and speed profiles to meet IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) targets for each vessel while maintaining commercial utilization. The system must calculate each vessel's current and projected annual CII rating (A through E), simulate the CII impact of each proposed voyage assignment, and flag deployments that would push a vessel's CII toward a downgrade threshold. Optimization recommendations must include speed reduction proposals with commercial impact quantification, voyage routing alternatives minimizing fuel consumption, vessel substitution recommendations assigning more fuel-efficient vessels to longer routes, and shore power utilization opportunities at equipped terminals (Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port). The system must track EU ETS and potential GCC carbon pricing implications for applicable voyages, calculating carbon cost in USD and Euros. CII compliance dashboards must display vessel ratings with trend lines and must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R015	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Scenario Planning and What-If Simulation Engine	The system must provide a scenario planning engine allowing chartering and operations managers to simulate what-if scenarios for fleet deployment decisions. Users must be able to create simulation scenarios including adding or removing vessels from the fleet, changing TC-In hire rates, modifying COA volume commitments, adjusting trade lane demand assumptions, varying bunker fuel prices, and introducing schedule disruptions. The simulation engine must calculate the utilization impact, financial impact (revenue, cost, profit) in USD with entity-specific currency breakdowns (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and operational feasibility of each scenario. Multiple scenarios must be comparable side-by-side with delta analysis highlighting the key differences. The AI engine must auto-generate optimized counter-scenarios suggesting modifications that would improve outcomes. Simulation results must be saveable and shareable across entities with proper access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R016	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Automated Vessel Deployment Recommendations	The AI engine must generate daily automated vessel deployment recommendations consolidating all optimization analyses (demand forecast, cargo matching, speed optimization, port rotation, empty repositioning, and carbon compliance) into actionable deployment plans. Each recommendation must specify the vessel, proposed cargo assignment, route with port sequence, recommended speed profile per leg, expected utilization metrics, and projected financial performance. Recommendations must be prioritized by financial impact and categorized as immediate action (within 24 hours), short-term (within 7 days), and medium-term (within 30 days). The system must present recommendations in a task-list format accessible to chartering managers across all entities with entity-specific filtering. Recommendations must be approvable through the mobile-responsive interface with one-click accept or reject with comments. All recommendation texts must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R017	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	AI Model Training and Continuous Learning Pipeline	The system must implement a continuous learning pipeline for the vessel utilization optimization AI models, retraining on new voyage data, market rate data, and operational outcomes on a configurable schedule (default weekly). The training pipeline must incorporate feedback loops where accepted or rejected recommendations are used to refine the model's understanding of operator preferences and entity-specific business rules. Model performance must be monitored through accuracy metrics comparing AI predictions against actual voyage outcomes (utilization achieved, revenue generated, schedule adherence) with dashboards showing model accuracy trends over time. New model versions must be deployed through a staged rollout process with A/B testing capability between the current and candidate model. Model explainability features must provide human-readable justification for each recommendation in both English and Arabic. All training data must be anonymized for cross-entity use while respecting entity-level data sovereignty requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-012-3-004-R018	FEAT-012-3-004	MOD-012	Offline Optimization Results Access and Manual Override	The system must provide offline access to the most recent AI-generated deployment recommendations and fleet utilization dashboards for operations personnel working at port locations (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) or aboard vessels with intermittent connectivity. Offline cached data must include current fleet positions, active voyage assignments, pending recommendations, and key utilization KPIs, encrypted with AES-256 on the local device. Users must be able to manually override AI recommendations when operational circumstances require human judgment, with each override requiring a reason code and free-text justification that feeds back into the AI learning pipeline. Override patterns must be analyzed by the AI to identify systematic recommendation gaps. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline actions, overrides, and status updates with timestamp-based conflict resolution. The offline interface must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL display modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R004	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Purchase Order Generation and Approval Workflow	The system must generate bunker purchase orders upon selection of a winning quotation, populating all commercial terms including vessel name, IMO number, bunkering port, delivery window, fuel grade and specification, quantity tolerance band (typically plus or minus 5%), agreed price per metric ton, total estimated value, currency, payment terms, and applicable INCOTERMS (typically DAP or DES for bunker deliveries). Purchase orders must pass through a configurable multi-level approval workflow based on order value thresholds that differ by entity. For example, orders below USD 50,000 may require single manager approval while orders above USD 200,000 require VP-level sign-off. The approval workflow must support mobile approvals and offline queuing with synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R005	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Supplier Master Data Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive bunker supplier master registry capturing supplier legal name in English and Arabic, trade license numbers, port coverage areas, fuel grades offered, ISO 8217 compliance certifications, MARPOL Annex VI compliance status, banking details for multiple currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), credit limit per entity, payment terms, insurance certificates, and historical performance metrics. Each supplier record must be linked to one or more bunkering ports and must store separate commercial terms per port. The system must enforce periodic review cycles (configurable, default 12 months) and automatically flag suppliers whose certifications are expiring within 60 days, sending notifications in the user's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R006	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Market Price Intelligence Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time bunker market intelligence dashboard displaying current spot prices for VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, and MGO across all configured ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The dashboard must show 30-day, 90-day, and 12-month price trend charts with the ability to overlay the company's actual procurement prices against market benchmarks. AI-driven price forecasting must project estimated prices for the next 7, 14, and 30 days with confidence intervals. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with full bidirectional text rendering and allow currency switching between USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with automatic conversion at prevailing treasury rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R007	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Contract Management and Term Agreements	The system must support creation and management of term bunker supply contracts with fixed-price, floating-price (index-linked), and hybrid pricing mechanisms. Each contract must capture the contracting entity, supplier details, covered ports, fuel grades, minimum and maximum lift quantities per month, pricing formula or fixed rate, escalation clauses, quality specifications referencing ISO 8217 tables, force majeure provisions, and contract validity period in both Gregorian and Hijri dates. The system must automatically track consumption against contracted volumes and alert procurement teams when utilization falls below 80% or exceeds 95% of the contracted monthly minimum or maximum respectively, to prevent take-or-pay penalties or supply shortfalls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R008	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Cost Allocation to Voyage and Entity	The system must allocate bunker procurement costs to the appropriate voyage, vessel, cost center, and legal entity at the time of purchase order confirmation. For vessels operating across multiple entities (e.g., a Qatar-owned vessel bunkering in Jebel Ali for a UAE subsidiary voyage), the system must support inter-company cost transfer with proper transfer pricing documentation. Cost allocation must follow configurable rules based on voyage leg, cargo allocation percentage, or time charter party terms. All allocations must generate corresponding accounting entries in the general ledger module with proper multi-currency treatment, recording both the transaction currency amount and the entity's functional currency equivalent at the booking date exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R009	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Procurement Budget Control and Forecasting	The system must enforce bunker procurement budgets at vessel, fleet, entity, and consolidated group levels. When a purchase order is created, the system must perform a real-time budget availability check and block orders that would exceed the approved budget unless an override is authorized by a designated budget controller. The AI engine must generate monthly and quarterly bunker cost forecasts based on planned voyages, historical consumption patterns, current market prices, and forward price curves. Budget variances exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10%) must trigger automatic notifications to finance controllers. Budget figures must be maintainable in each entity's functional currency with consolidated reporting in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R010	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Electronic Bunker Order Confirmation and Documentation	The system must support electronic exchange of bunker order confirmations with suppliers via EDI, API integration, or structured email templates. Order confirmation documents must be generated in PDF format with dual-language support (English and Arabic) and must include all commercial terms, vessel details, delivery instructions, safety requirements, and applicable regulatory declarations. The system must track confirmation status (sent, acknowledged, confirmed, amended, cancelled) with full audit trail including timestamps and user identifiers. For ports in Qatar and KSA, the system must generate documentation compliant with local port authority bunker delivery notification requirements, including any Arabized forms mandated by Hamad Port or Saudi Ports Authority regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R011	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Supplier Performance Scoring and Analytics	The system must automatically calculate and maintain supplier performance scores based on delivery punctuality (measured against confirmed bunkering window), quantity accuracy (measured against ordered quantity within tolerance), fuel quality compliance (measured against ISO 8217 specification and BDN declarations), documentation accuracy, responsiveness to RFQs, and claims history. Performance scores must be recalculated after each delivery and maintained as rolling 6-month and 12-month averages. The AI engine must identify underperforming suppliers and recommend alternative suppliers for future orders. Performance reports must be filterable by port, fuel grade, entity, and time period, with export capability in PDF and Excel formats supporting both English and Arabic column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R005	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Invoice Number Sequencing per Entity and Fiscal Year	The system must maintain separate, sequential, gap-free invoice number series for each billing entity across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The numbering format must be configurable per entity (e.g., QA-FI-2026-00001 for Qatar, AE-FI-2026-00001 for UAE, SA-FI-2026-00001 for KSA, IN-FI-2026-00001 for India). The sequence must reset annually based on the entity's fiscal year start date, which may follow Gregorian or Hijri calendar. The system must guarantee uniqueness under concurrent invoice generation by multiple users, using database-level sequence locks to prevent duplicate numbers even during peak billing cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R012	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Procurement Regulatory Compliance Checks	The system must perform automated compliance checks at the time of purchase order creation, verifying that the selected supplier is not on any sanctions list (OFAC, EU, UN), that the fuel grade ordered complies with the applicable MARPOL Annex VI sulphur emission control requirements for the vessel's trading area, and that the bunkering port is not subject to any active trade restrictions for the ordering entity's jurisdiction. For vessels entering Emission Control Areas (ECAs), the system must validate that the ordered fuel grade meets the 0.10% sulphur limit. The system must maintain an up-to-date regulatory reference database and flag any compliance exceptions requiring manual review before order processing can continue, logging all checks with timestamps for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R013	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Offline Bunker Order Processing and Synchronization	The system must support offline bunker procurement workflows for users operating in areas with limited or no internet connectivity, such as vessel-based procurement officers or port agents in remote locations. Offline capabilities must include creating purchase requisitions, reviewing cached supplier quotations, drafting purchase orders, and recording approvals. Upon restoration of network connectivity, the system must automatically synchronize all offline transactions with the central server, applying conflict resolution rules where the same record has been modified by multiple users. The synchronization engine must handle currency rate updates by flagging any transactions where the exchange rate has changed by more than 1% between offline creation and synchronization, requiring finance team review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R014	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Procurement Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail for every bunker procurement transaction, recording all state changes from initial requisition through final payment. Each audit entry must capture the action performed, the user identity, timestamp in UTC with entity local time reference, the before and after values of any modified fields, and the IP address or device identifier from which the action was performed. The system must generate compliance reports for internal audit, external audit, and regulatory review purposes, showing procurement process adherence metrics, segregation of duties compliance, approval workflow adherence, and budget control effectiveness. Reports must be exportable in Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date columns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R015	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Procurement Dashboard and KPI Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time procurement dashboard displaying key performance indicators including average procurement lead time by port, cost per metric ton versus market benchmark by fuel grade, supplier concentration risk index, budget utilization percentage by entity and fleet, RFQ response rate, purchase order cycle time, and percentage of AI-recommended suppliers accepted versus overridden. The dashboard must support drill-down from KPI summary to individual transaction detail. Visualizations must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper axis and label mirroring. Dashboard data must refresh at configurable intervals (default 15 minutes) and support manual refresh. All monetary KPIs must be displayable in any of the five supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-001-R016	FEAT-013-1-001	MOD-013	Bunker Credit and Payment Terms Management	The system must manage bunker supplier credit terms at both the master agreement level and the individual transaction level, supporting payment terms including advance payment, cash on delivery, and credit terms of 15, 30, 45, 60, or 90 days. For each entity, the system must track the total outstanding bunker payables by supplier and enforce credit limits to prevent new orders when a supplier's outstanding balance exceeds the approved credit threshold. The system must support letters of credit and bank guarantee instruments for high-value bunker transactions, tracking instrument validity dates and amounts. Payment scheduling must integrate with the accounts payable module to ensure timely payment processing and avoidance of late payment penalties, with currency-specific payment routing rules for USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R001	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Scheduling and Vessel Nomination	The system must allow operations teams to create bunker stem schedules by nominating a vessel for bunkering at a specific port and date window. Each stem record must capture the vessel IMO number, vessel name, bunkering port (using UN/LOCODE such as QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INNHA for JNPT), requested delivery date and time window, fuel grade and quantity, delivery method (barge, truck, pipeline, or alongside), preferred berth or anchorage location, and any special requirements such as simultaneous cargo operations or tidal restrictions. The system must validate that the bunkering schedule does not conflict with cargo operations, port authority curfews, or existing berth bookings retrieved from the port call management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R002	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	AI-Optimized Bunkering Port and Timing Selection	The AI engine must recommend the optimal bunkering port and timing for each vessel based on analysis of current fuel prices across all available ports on the vessel's route, vessel consumption forecast for upcoming voyage legs, tank capacity and current ROB levels, port call schedules and available bunkering windows, weather forecasts affecting barge delivery feasibility, and total cost of deviation if bunkering requires a route change. The optimization must consider multi-entity cost allocation rules and entity-specific tax implications. For example, bunkering in Jebel Ali versus Hamad Port may have different VAT implications for the Qatar entity. Recommendations must be presented with a cost comparison matrix showing the financial impact of each option in the vessel's operating currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R003	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Barge and Delivery Logistics Coordination	The system must manage the coordination of bunker delivery logistics including barge scheduling, truck delivery booking, and pipeline connection arrangements. For barge deliveries, the system must track barge nomination details (barge name, capacity, ETA, operator), confirm barge alongside time windows with the port authority, and record actual barge arrival and departure times. For truck deliveries common at smaller ports, the system must manage truck slot bookings and track delivery ticket numbers. The system must send automated notifications to vessel masters, port agents, and bunker suppliers at configurable milestones (e.g., 48 hours before, 24 hours before, 6 hours before delivery). All notification templates must be available in English and Arabic, with the appropriate language selected based on the recipient's profile preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R004	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Delivery Quantity Planning and Tank Allocation	The system must calculate the optimal bunker delivery quantity based on the vessel's current ROB (Remaining on Board), planned consumption for upcoming voyage legs, tank capacity constraints, minimum reserve requirements, and destination port fuel availability. Tank allocation planning must account for segregation requirements between different fuel grades (VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, MGO), tank heating coil availability for heavy fuel grades, and any tank inspection or cleaning schedules. The system must generate a pre-delivery tank plan showing which tanks will receive fuel, expected levels after delivery, and any stability implications that require ballast adjustments. The AI engine must flag deliveries where the requested quantity would result in tank overflow risk or insufficient ullage space, calculated with a configurable safety margin (default 98% tank capacity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R005	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Multi-Port Bunkering Strategy Optimization	The AI engine must generate fleet-wide bunkering strategies that optimize total fuel cost across multiple vessels and multiple ports over a configurable planning horizon (default 30 days). The optimization must consider contracted volume commitments at specific ports, volume discounts for consolidated lifts, port-specific pricing trends, seasonal demand patterns, fleet deployment schedules, and inter-entity fuel transfer possibilities. The strategy must produce a recommended bunkering plan for each vessel showing the suggested port, date, quantity, and estimated cost, with a total fleet savings projection compared to a baseline non-optimized approach. The system must allow planners to override AI recommendations with manual adjustments and must recalculate the overall strategy impact when overrides are applied. All cost projections must be calculable in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R006	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Confirmation and Supplier Communication	The system must manage the stem confirmation workflow between the company, bunker supplier, port agent, and vessel. Upon stem creation, the system must automatically dispatch a stem nomination to the confirmed supplier containing vessel details, ETA, requested delivery window, fuel grade, quantity, and delivery method. The system must track supplier acknowledgment, any counter-proposals for delivery timing or quantity, and final confirmation status. A stem status dashboard must display all active stems across the fleet with color-coded status indicators (nominated, acknowledged, confirmed, in-progress, completed, cancelled). Communication logs must capture all messages exchanged with suppliers and agents with timestamps, and the system must support both English and Arabic correspondence templates for regional suppliers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R007	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Weather and Sea State Impact on Bunkering Operations	The system must integrate with weather data feeds to assess the impact of weather conditions on planned bunkering operations, particularly for barge deliveries at anchorage or offshore locations. The system must evaluate wind speed, wave height, swell period, visibility, and tidal conditions against configurable safety thresholds for bunkering operations (default: maximum wind speed 25 knots, maximum significant wave height 1.5 meters for barge alongside operations). When weather conditions are forecast to exceed safe bunkering thresholds during the planned delivery window, the system must automatically alert the operations team and recommend alternative delivery windows or alternative bunkering ports. Weather assessments must be refreshed every 6 hours for stems within 72 hours of planned delivery and must display forecasts in both metric and imperial units based on user preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R008	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Port Authority Bunkering Permit and Notification Management	The system must generate and track port authority bunkering permits and notifications required at each bunkering port. For Hamad Port (Qatar), the system must prepare QTerminals bunkering application forms. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE), the system must generate DP World and Abu Dhabi Ports bunkering notifications respectively. For JNPT (India), the system must prepare Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust bunkering permits. Each port may have different documentation requirements, advance notice periods (typically 24-72 hours), and approval workflows. The system must store port-specific permit templates, auto-populate vessel and delivery details, track submission and approval status, and alert operations teams when permits are approaching expiry or require renewal. All documentation must support dual-language output in English and Arabic where required by the port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R009	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Delivery Monitoring and Real-Time Tracking	The system must provide real-time monitoring of active bunker delivery operations, displaying delivery start time, current flow rate, cumulative quantity delivered, estimated completion time, and any interruptions or stoppages. Data must be captured from vessel flow meters via IoT integration where available, or manually entered by the vessel's chief engineer or duty officer. The monitoring dashboard must show a live progress bar for each active delivery across the fleet, with alerts for deliveries that are running behind schedule (exceeding planned duration by more than 20%) or where the flow rate drops below a minimum threshold suggesting a delivery issue. The system must record the complete delivery timeline for each stem including all start, stop, and resume events with timestamps in UTC and local port time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R010	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Cost Estimation and Variance Tracking	The system must calculate estimated total stem cost at the time of planning, incorporating fuel price per metric ton, delivery charges (barge hire, truck fees, pipeline usage fees), port bunkering fees, agency fees, and any applicable taxes or duties. The estimation must account for the entity-specific tax regime: zero VAT for Qatar entity operations at Hamad Port, 5% VAT for UAE entity operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, 15% VAT for KSA entity operations, and applicable GST rates for India entity operations at JNPT. Upon stem completion, the system must calculate actual versus estimated cost variance and feed this data back into the AI forecasting model to improve future estimations. All cost tracking must support dual-currency recording in both transaction currency and entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R011	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Emergency Bunkering and Spot Purchase Workflow	The system must support an expedited emergency bunkering workflow for situations where a vessel requires unplanned fuel replenishment due to weather diversions, mechanical issues increasing consumption, or schedule changes. The emergency workflow must bypass standard RFQ timelines while still maintaining essential controls including budget check, sanctions screening, and single-level emergency approval. The system must allow spot price acceptance from the nearest available supplier with post-facto documentation. Emergency stems must be flagged distinctly in reporting and require a mandatory root cause explanation field. The AI engine must analyze emergency bunkering frequency and causes to identify patterns that could be prevented through better planning, generating quarterly exception reports for fleet management review in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R012	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Documentation and BDN Management	The system must manage the Bunker Delivery Note (BDN) lifecycle as mandated by MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18. Upon completion of each bunker delivery, the system must capture or receive the BDN details including supplier name, vessel name, IMO number, port of delivery, date, fuel grade designation per ISO 8217, quantity delivered in metric tons, density at 15 degrees Celsius, sulphur content declaration, and supplier representative signature. The system must validate BDN data against the purchase order and stem records, flagging discrepancies in quantity (beyond tolerance), grade, or sulphur content. BDN records must be retained for a minimum of 3 years as required by MARPOL and must be retrievable by vessel, port, date range, or fuel grade for audit and port state control inspection purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R013	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Concurrent Bunkering and Cargo Operations Safety Management	The system must assess and manage the safety implications of simultaneous bunkering and cargo operations (SIMOPS). When a bunker stem is scheduled during a port call that also involves cargo loading or discharge, the system must trigger a SIMOPS risk assessment checklist covering fire safety provisions, communication protocols, emergency shutdown procedures, spill containment readiness, and compatibility of bunker delivery method with concurrent cargo handling equipment. The risk assessment must reference the port-specific SIMOPS regulations applicable at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. The system must require sign-off from the vessel master and port safety officer before permitting concurrent operations. SIMOPS records must be stored with the stem documentation for safety audit and insurance compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R014	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Supply Chain Disruption Alerting	The AI engine must continuously monitor bunker supply chain risk factors including supplier financial health indicators, port congestion levels, geopolitical events affecting shipping lanes (particularly the Strait of Hormuz and Gulf of Aden routes critical to Qatar, UAE, and KSA operations), refinery outages, and global fuel supply-demand imbalances. When the system identifies a potential supply chain disruption that could affect planned bunkering operations, it must generate proactive alerts to operations and procurement teams with risk severity classification (low, medium, high, critical), estimated impact duration, affected ports, and recommended contingency actions such as advance purchasing or alternative port bunkering. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notifications, email, and SMS in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) with automated escalation for high and critical severity events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R015	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Calendar and Fleet Planning View	The system must provide a visual fleet bunkering calendar displaying all planned, confirmed, in-progress, and completed bunker stems across the entire fleet on a Gantt-style timeline. The calendar must support filtering by vessel, port, fuel grade, entity, and status. Users must be able to drag and drop stems to reschedule delivery windows, with the system automatically validating the new timing against vessel schedules, port availability, and supplier confirmations. The calendar must display both Gregorian and Hijri dates and support toggling between the two calendar systems as the primary display. The view must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic users with proper right-to-left timeline progression. Color coding must distinguish stems by status, entity, and fuel grade with a configurable legend.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R016	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Bunker Stem Integration with Voyage Planning Module	The system must maintain bidirectional integration with the voyage planning module (MOD-004) to ensure bunker stem schedules are synchronized with voyage itineraries. When a voyage plan is created or modified, the system must automatically evaluate whether existing bunker stems need to be adjusted and alert planners of any scheduling conflicts. Conversely, when a bunker stem is confirmed or modified, the voyage plan must be updated to reflect the bunkering port call duration and any deviation requirements. The integration must handle cross-entity scenarios where a vessel managed by one entity (e.g., Qatar) is bunkering at a port managed by another entity's operations team (e.g., UAE at Jebel Ali), ensuring proper data visibility and approval routing across entity boundaries while respecting data segregation rules for each legal entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-002-R017	FEAT-013-1-002	MOD-013	Offline Stem Planning and Field Agent Access	The system must provide offline-capable stem planning and management functionality for port agents and vessel officers who may not have reliable internet connectivity during bunkering operations. The offline module must cache active stem details, vessel tank plans, supplier contact information, and BDN templates for all stems within a configurable lookahead window (default 7 days). Users must be able to update stem status, record delivery quantities, capture BDN data, and log delivery events while offline. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize all offline changes with conflict detection and resolution, prioritizing vessel-side entries for delivery quantity fields and shore-side entries for commercial fields. Synchronization logs must be maintained for audit purposes and any conflicts must be flagged for manual review by the operations supervisor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R001	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Fuel Sample Collection and Chain of Custody Tracking	The system must manage the fuel sample collection process as required by MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18, tracking the collection of representative samples during each bunker delivery. For each delivery, the system must record the collection of a minimum of three samples: the MARPOL sample (sealed and retained onboard for 12 months), the supplier's retained sample, and the ship's analysis sample. Each sample record must capture a unique sample identification number, date and time of collection, collecting officer name, seal numbers, storage location (onboard or shore laboratory), and chain of custody transfers. The system must generate sample labels in English and Arabic and alert the vessel when MARPOL sample retention periods are approaching expiry, ensuring samples are not disposed of prematurely before the mandatory 12-month retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R002	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Laboratory Analysis Management and Result Recording	The system must manage the dispatch of bunker fuel samples to accredited testing laboratories and track analysis results. The system must support integration with major bunker testing laboratories (such as Veritas Petroleum Services, Lloyd's Register FOBAS, and Bureau Veritas) for electronic submission of sample details and receipt of test results via API or structured data file. Test results must be recorded against each sample including all ISO 8217 parameters: viscosity, density, sulphur content, flash point, pour point, water content, ash content, vanadium, sodium, aluminum plus silicon, total sediment, acid number, and compatibility characteristics. The system must automatically compare each test result against the applicable ISO 8217 specification limits for the declared fuel grade and the BDN-declared values, highlighting any parameters that are out of specification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R003	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Automated Off-Specification Fuel Detection and Alerting	The AI engine must automatically analyze laboratory test results upon receipt and detect any fuel parameters that fall outside ISO 8217 specification limits for the declared fuel grade. When off-spec fuel is detected, the system must immediately generate alerts to the vessel's chief engineer, technical superintendent, bunker procurement team, and claims manager. The alert must classify the severity of the quality failure as minor (single parameter marginally outside limits with no operational impact), moderate (parameters outside limits requiring operational adjustments such as increased purifier throughput or blending), or critical (parameters presenting safety risk such as low flash point below 60 degrees Celsius or sulphur content exceeding MARPOL limits). The alert must include specific parameter values versus specification limits, potential operational impacts, and recommended immediate actions. Alerts for critical quality failures must escalate automatically to senior management if not acknowledged within 2 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R004	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Bunker Quality Claim Initiation and Documentation	The system must support the initiation of formal quality claims against bunker suppliers when off-specification fuel is detected. A claim record must capture the delivery reference (BDN number), vessel details, supplier details, laboratory analysis report reference, specific parameters that failed specification, photographic or documentary evidence (supporting file attachments up to 50MB per claim), estimated financial impact in the transaction currency and entity functional currency, and the requested remedy (price reduction, replacement fuel, compensation for additional treatment costs, or compensation for engine damage). The system must generate claim letters in English and Arabic using configurable templates that include all required technical details and commercial references. Each claim must be assigned a unique claim number following entity-specific numbering conventions and must be linked to the original purchase order and stem records for full traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R005	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Claim Workflow and Escalation Management	The system must manage the bunker quality claim lifecycle through configurable workflow stages: draft, submitted to supplier, supplier response received, under negotiation, escalated to legal, settled, withdrawn, or rejected. Each stage transition must be recorded with timestamps, responsible user, and supporting commentary. The system must enforce configurable time limits for each stage (e.g., supplier must respond within 14 days of claim submission, negotiation must conclude within 60 days) and automatically escalate to the next level when deadlines are exceeded. Escalation paths must be configurable per entity and must support routing claims to legal counsel when negotiation fails. The system must track all correspondence related to each claim including emails, letters, and meeting notes in both English and Arabic. Claim values must be tracked in the original transaction currency with real-time conversion to the entity's reporting currency for financial reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R006	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Fuel Compatibility Testing and Cross-Contamination Prevention	The system must manage fuel compatibility testing records when a vessel is loading a fuel grade from a different supplier or a different source than the existing fuel in the receiving tanks. The system must maintain a compatibility matrix showing known compatible and incompatible fuel combinations based on historical testing data. Before each delivery, the system must check the compatibility history between the incoming fuel's source and the existing heel in the receiving tank. If no prior compatibility data exists, the system must flag the delivery as requiring onboard compatibility testing before commingling. Test results (compatible, borderline, incompatible per ASTM D4740 spot test method) must be recorded and linked to both the incoming delivery BDN and the existing tank contents record. When incompatibility is detected, the system must recommend segregation protocols and alert the chief engineer with specific tank management instructions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R007	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	MARPOL Sulphur Compliance Verification	The system must verify that all bunker fuel deliveries comply with applicable MARPOL Annex VI sulphur content regulations based on the vessel's current and planned trading areas. For vessels operating globally outside ECAs, the system must verify sulphur content does not exceed 0.50% mass by mass as per the IMO 2020 global sulphur cap. For vessels entering Emission Control Areas (North American ECA, US Caribbean ECA, North Sea ECA, Baltic Sea ECA), the system must verify sulphur content does not exceed 0.10%. The system must cross-reference the BDN-declared sulphur content with laboratory analysis results and flag any discrepancy exceeding 0.02 percentage points. For vessels equipped with exhaust gas cleaning systems (scrubbers), the system must maintain records of scrubber-compliant fuel usage and washwater monitoring data. Compliance records must be readily accessible for port state control inspections and must be formatted for each jurisdiction's requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R008	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Bunker Quality Historical Analytics and Supplier Rating	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical database of all bunker quality test results, indexed by supplier, port, fuel grade, and vessel. The AI engine must analyze this data to identify quality trends, recurring issues with specific suppliers or ports, seasonal quality variations, and correlations between fuel quality parameters and engine performance metrics. Supplier quality ratings must be calculated as a composite score incorporating the percentage of deliveries meeting full ISO 8217 specification, the severity and frequency of off-spec incidents, claim resolution responsiveness, and the accuracy of BDN declarations versus laboratory results. Quality analytics must be presented in dashboards with trend charts, heat maps showing quality by port and supplier, and exception reports. All reports must support export in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic reports, including right-aligned numerical columns and Arabic chart labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R009	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Fuel Treatment and Blending Recommendation Engine	When off-specification fuel cannot be returned to the supplier and must be consumed, the AI engine must recommend fuel treatment and blending strategies to bring the fuel within usable parameters. Recommendations must consider the specific out-of-spec parameters, available onboard treatment equipment (purifiers, homogenizers, chemical dosing systems), compatibility with other fuel parcels onboard, and the vessel's engine manufacturer guidelines. The system must calculate blending ratios when mixing off-spec fuel with compliant fuel to achieve acceptable specification levels, ensuring the resulting blend meets minimum requirements for all ISO 8217 parameters. Treatment recommendations must include estimated additional operational costs (chemical treatment costs, increased purifier running hours, reduced engine efficiency) for inclusion in the quality claim. All recommendations must be logged with approval records from the technical superintendent before implementation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R010	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Claim Financial Impact Assessment and Accounting Integration	The system must calculate the total financial impact of each bunker quality claim including direct costs (price difference for off-spec fuel, replacement fuel procurement cost), indirect costs (additional fuel treatment chemicals, increased purifier wear, engine component accelerated wear), consequential costs (off-hire time for engine-related delays, deviation costs for emergency bunkering), and administrative costs (laboratory testing fees, legal fees, survey costs). Financial impact calculations must use the applicable currency exchange rates at the time of each cost element incurrence and must generate accounting entries for claims receivable, provisions for disputed claims, and claim settlement income or write-off. The system must integrate with the general ledger module to post entries against the correct entity, cost center, and voyage, maintaining dual-currency records in both transaction currency and entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR as applicable).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R011	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Independent Surveyor and Expert Appointment Management	The system must manage the appointment and coordination of independent bunker surveyors and fuel quality experts for disputed deliveries or significant quality claims. The system must maintain a registry of approved surveyors and experts by port and specialization, including their availability, daily rates in multiple currencies, certifications, and historical engagement records. When a survey is required, the system must support the creation of survey appointment requests specifying the scope (quantity survey, quality investigation, engine damage assessment), vessel location and schedule, and urgency level. The system must track surveyor reports, integrate findings into the claim record, and manage surveyor invoicing. For ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the surveyor registry must include locally licensed surveyors who are familiar with regional port authority requirements and can produce reports in both English and Arabic as needed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R012	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Bunker Quality Certificate and Compliance Document Repository	The system must maintain a centralized, searchable document repository for all bunker quality-related certificates and compliance documents. Documents must include BDNs, laboratory analysis certificates, MARPOL sample retention records, fuel changeover logs, compatibility test certificates, scrubber compliance records, and port state control inspection reports related to fuel quality. Each document must be tagged with metadata including vessel IMO number, port code, delivery date, supplier name, fuel grade, and document type to support rapid retrieval during port state control inspections. The repository must support document upload in PDF, JPEG, and PNG formats with a maximum individual file size of 25MB. Documents must be accessible from both the shore-based system and the vessel-based offline module with automatic synchronization. The system must enforce the MARPOL-mandated 3-year retention period and prevent premature deletion of compliance documents while supporting automated archival after the retention period expires.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R013	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Engine Performance Correlation with Fuel Quality	The AI engine must correlate fuel quality test results with vessel engine performance data to identify fuel-related operational issues. The system must analyze engine parameters including fuel oil inlet temperature, viscosity at engine inlet, exhaust gas temperatures across cylinders, turbocharger performance, fuel filter differential pressure, purifier sludge quantities, and cylinder liner wear rates. When the AI detects performance anomalies correlating with a specific fuel delivery (such as abnormal cylinder exhaust temperature spread appearing after a particular bunker stem), the system must generate an investigation alert linking the performance data to the fuel quality record. This correlation data must be incorporated into quality claim documentation to support claims for consequential damages. The analysis must account for multiple variables and present findings with statistical confidence levels to strengthen the evidentiary basis for claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R014	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Regulatory Reporting for Fuel Quality Incidents	The system must support the generation of regulatory reports for fuel quality incidents as required by flag state administrations, port state authorities, and classification societies. For serious quality incidents (such as fuel contamination causing engine damage or sulphur non-compliance), the system must generate incident reports in the format required by the vessel's flag state, the port state where the fuel was delivered, and the vessel's classification society. The system must track reporting deadlines and ensure timely submission. For MARPOL non-compliance incidents, the system must prepare the required documentation for fuel oil non-availability reports (FONARs) when compliant fuel cannot be obtained. All regulatory reports must be generated in English and in Arabic when required by Middle Eastern port states or flag administrations. The system must maintain a regulatory contact database for each jurisdiction to facilitate timely notification and must record all submissions with confirmation receipts for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R015	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Claim Settlement Recording and Credit Note Processing	The system must record the final settlement of each bunker quality claim including the settlement amount, currency, payment method, and any non-monetary remedies agreed (such as replacement fuel delivery or future price credits). Settlement amounts must be recorded in the original claim currency with conversion to the entity's functional currency at the settlement date exchange rate. The system must generate or receive supplier credit notes and link them to the original purchase order and invoice for accounts payable offset processing. For partial settlements, the system must record the accepted amount, the disputed remainder, and the basis for the partial resolution. Settlement records must feed into the supplier performance scoring system, negatively impacting suppliers with frequent or high-value claims. The system must generate settlement summary reports by entity, supplier, port, and period for management review, with all monetary values displayable in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R016	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Proactive Fuel Quality Risk Prediction	The AI engine must proactively predict fuel quality risks based on analysis of multiple data sources including historical quality data by supplier and port, industry fuel quality alert bulletins, refinery changeover schedules that may affect fuel consistency, seasonal quality variations (such as cold flow property issues during Gulf winter months), and known problematic fuel supply chains. Before each planned bunker stem, the system must generate a fuel quality risk score (low, medium, high) for the planned supplier and port combination. High-risk stems must trigger recommendations for enhanced testing protocols (such as onboard drip testing before commingling), increased sample collection, or alternative supplier selection. Risk predictions must be recalibrated monthly based on new quality data and must achieve a minimum accuracy rate of 75% when measured against actual quality outcomes over a rolling 12-month period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-003-R017	FEAT-013-1-003	MOD-013	Multi-Entity Claim Coordination and Shared Supplier Issues	The system must support coordinated claims management when multiple entities within the group experience quality issues with the same supplier or the same fuel batch. The system must detect when multiple vessels across different entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) receive fuel from the same BDN batch or supplier consignment and correlate quality results across these deliveries. When a pattern of quality failures is detected across entities, the system must facilitate the creation of a consolidated group claim while maintaining separate entity-level financial records and claim documentation. The claims coordinator must have visibility across all entity claims with appropriate data access controls. Inter-entity claim cost sharing and recovery allocation must follow configurable rules agreed at the group level. All cross-entity claim communications and documentation must be available in both English and Arabic to support the diverse operational teams involved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R001	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Real-Time Fuel ROB Dashboard by Vessel and Fleet	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying the current Remaining on Board (ROB) for each fuel grade (VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, MGO) across every vessel in the fleet. The dashboard must show ROB in metric tons, as a percentage of total tank capacity, and as estimated days of remaining autonomy based on the vessel's current consumption rate. Fleet-level aggregation must display total ROB by fuel grade, entity, and vessel class. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel group, fuel grade, and ROB threshold (e.g., show only vessels with less than 7 days autonomy). ROB values must be color-coded: green for adequate levels, amber for warning (below configurable threshold, default 30% capacity), and red for critical (below configurable threshold, default 15% capacity). The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts and display vessel positions on an integrated map view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R002	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Daily Fuel ROB Reporting from Vessels	The system must receive and process daily fuel ROB reports from each vessel, submitted by the chief engineer or designated duty engineer. Each daily report must capture the reporting date and time in UTC, vessel position (latitude and longitude), ROB for each fuel grade in metric tons measured by tank sounding or flow meter readings, total fuel consumed since last report broken down by main engine, auxiliary engines, boiler, and incinerator, average vessel speed, distance sailed since last report, sea state and weather conditions, and any operational notes affecting consumption. The system must support report submission via web interface, mobile application, and structured email parsing. For vessels with intermittent connectivity, the system must accept reports submitted up to 48 hours after the reporting period with appropriate timestamping. Submitted reports must be immutable once confirmed, with any corrections requiring a separate adjustment entry with justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R003	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Automated ROB Reconciliation with Delivery and Consumption Records	The system must perform automated reconciliation of fuel ROB by comparing the reported ROB against the calculated theoretical ROB. The theoretical ROB must be computed as: previous confirmed ROB plus bunker deliveries received (from BDN records) minus reported consumption (from daily reports) minus any fuel transfers or disposals. The reconciliation must be performed for each fuel grade independently and must identify discrepancies exceeding a configurable tolerance (default 1% of total tank capacity or 5 metric tons, whichever is greater). Discrepancies must be classified as: measurement variance (within tolerance), unexplained gain, unexplained loss, or suspected reporting error. The AI engine must analyze discrepancy patterns to distinguish between systematic measurement errors, seasonal temperature effects on fuel volume, and genuine inventory anomalies requiring investigation. Reconciliation results must be available by vessel, fuel grade, reporting period, and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R004	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Tank-Level Fuel Inventory Management	The system must maintain a detailed fuel inventory at the individual tank level for each vessel. Each tank record must capture the tank identification number and location, designated fuel grade, current sounding in meters or centimeters, volume in cubic meters calculated from the vessel's tank calibration tables, quantity in metric tons using the current fuel density, temperature reading for density correction, last sounding date and time, and maximum capacity. The system must store vessel-specific tank calibration tables (ullage or sounding tables) and apply temperature-density corrections per ASTM D1250 petroleum measurement tables. Tank transfers between tanks on the same vessel must be recorded with the transferring officer's identity and reason. The system must prevent assignment of incompatible fuel grades to the same tank without a documented tank cleaning record and must alert when tank levels approach minimum suction levels or maximum safe fill levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R005	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Consumption Analysis by Voyage Leg and Operational Mode	The system must analyze and report fuel consumption at granular levels including per voyage leg (port to port), per operational mode (sea passage, port maneuvering, anchoring, loading/discharging, idle), and per consumer (main engine, each auxiliary engine, boiler, incinerator). Consumption must be reported in metric tons and as specific fuel oil consumption (grams per kilowatt-hour) for engine consumers. The AI engine must compare actual consumption against vessel-specific baseline consumption models and highlight significant deviations (exceeding configurable thresholds, default 10%) with potential explanations such as hull fouling, weather impact, speed variation, or cargo loading condition changes. Consumption analytics must support drill-down from fleet overview to entity, vessel, voyage, leg, and individual consumer level. Reports must be exportable in English and Arabic with all date fields showing both Gregorian and Hijri dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R006	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	AI-Powered Consumption Prediction and Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must generate fuel consumption predictions for planned voyages based on historical consumption data for the specific vessel, planned speed and route, weather forecast along the route, vessel's current hull and propeller condition, cargo loading condition, and fuel grade characteristics. Predictions must provide point estimates with confidence intervals and must be updated as voyage conditions change. The AI must also perform real-time anomaly detection on incoming daily ROB reports, identifying consumption patterns that deviate significantly from the vessel's historical profile or the AI prediction. Anomalies must be classified by probable cause (e.g., speed increase, weather deterioration, equipment malfunction, reporting error) and routed to the appropriate team for investigation. The prediction model must achieve a minimum accuracy of 90% (within plus or minus 5% of actual) for standard sea passage legs under normal weather conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R007	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Bunker Delivery Quantity Verification and ROB Update	The system must automatically update vessel fuel ROB records upon confirmation of a bunker delivery, using the verified delivery quantity from the BDN. The update must account for the specific tanks receiving fuel as recorded in the tank allocation plan. The system must support quantity verification by comparing three independent measurements: the BDN-declared quantity (supplier's measurement), the vessel's measurement (based on tank soundings before and after delivery), and flow meter readings where available. Discrepancies between these measurements exceeding the contractual tolerance (typically plus or minus 0.5% for quantity) must be flagged for investigation and potential shortage claim. The system must record all three measurement values and the final agreed quantity. Temperature and density corrections must be applied consistently using the agreed measurement standard (typically in-vacuum metric tons at observed temperature). The ROB update must generate a reconciliation record linking the delivery to specific tank level changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R008	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Transfer Between Vessels Recording	The system must support the recording of fuel transfers between vessels (ship-to-ship transfers), capturing the transferring vessel details, receiving vessel details, transfer date, location (coordinates and port if applicable), fuel grade, quantity transferred in metric tons, density, temperature, and authorization from both vessel masters and the fleet operations manager. Inter-vessel transfers must simultaneously debit the transferring vessel's ROB and credit the receiving vessel's ROB, maintaining tank-level accuracy on both vessels. When the transfer involves vessels belonging to different entities (e.g., a Qatar entity vessel transferring fuel to a UAE entity vessel), the system must generate inter-company transfer pricing documentation at the agreed transfer price or market value, with proper currency recording in both entities' functional currencies. Transfer records must be linked to appropriate accounting entries and must be reported separately from supplier deliveries in consumption and cost analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R009	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	EU MRV and IMO DCS Fuel Data Reporting	The system must generate fuel consumption data reports compliant with the EU Monitoring, Reporting, and Verification (EU MRV) regulation and the IMO Data Collection System (IMO DCS) requirements. For EU MRV, the system must aggregate fuel consumption, distance traveled, time at sea, cargo carried, and transport work for all voyages to, from, or between EU ports, generating annual emissions reports per vessel. For IMO DCS, the system must compile annual fuel consumption data by fuel type for submission to the vessel's flag state administration via the GISIS platform. The system must calculate CO2 emissions using the IMO-standard emission factors for each fuel grade (VLSFO 3.151 tonnes CO2 per tonne fuel, HSFO 3.114, LSMGO/MGO 3.206). Data must be validated for completeness and consistency before report generation. Reports must be reviewable and approvable by the designated company environmental officer before submission, and the system must maintain records of all submitted reports with submission timestamps and confirmation references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R010	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel ROB Physical Verification and Survey Management	The system must manage periodic physical fuel ROB verification exercises, including scheduled bunker surveys at vessel drydocking, on-hire and off-hire surveys for time charter vessels, and random spot-check surveys. Each survey record must capture the survey date, surveyor identity (internal or independent), survey method (tank sounding, ullage, flow meter calibration check), measured quantities per tank, discrepancies versus system ROB records, and corrective adjustments applied. The system must schedule surveys based on configurable rules (e.g., mandatory at drydocking, mandatory at charter party commencement and termination, random quarterly surveys). Survey results must recalibrate the system ROB baseline and any significant discrepancies (exceeding 2% of total ROB) must trigger an investigation workflow. For time charter on-hire and off-hire surveys, the system must calculate fuel values at charter party agreed rates or current market rates in the charter currency, with conversion to entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R011	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Cost Allocation to Voyage Profit and Loss	The system must allocate fuel costs to individual voyages for profit and loss calculation using configurable allocation methods including FIFO (first in first out), weighted average cost, or specific identification. The allocation must track the cost of each metric ton of fuel consumed on a voyage, considering the acquisition cost from the bunker procurement records, any quality-related adjustments from claims, and delivery charges. For vessels operating consecutive voyages, the system must properly apportion fuel costs between voyages based on consumption during each voyage leg. The system must handle fuel cost allocation for inter-entity voyages where a vessel owned by one entity operates a voyage for another entity, applying transfer pricing rules and generating inter-company charges in the appropriate currencies. All cost allocations must generate corresponding accounting entries in the general ledger module with proper cost center, entity, voyage, and currency dimensions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R012	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Theft and Unauthorized Consumption Detection	The AI engine must monitor fuel ROB patterns to detect potential fuel theft or unauthorized consumption. Detection algorithms must analyze patterns including unexplained ROB decreases during port stays when engines are not running, consumption rates that significantly exceed the vessel's established baseline profile for the operating conditions, discrepancies between flow meter readings and tank sounding measurements suggesting meter bypass, and unusual tank transfer patterns. When suspicious activity is detected, the system must generate a confidential alert to the fleet security officer and designated management personnel, classified by severity and confidence level. The alert must include the specific data anomaly, relevant time period, estimated quantity involved, and recommended investigation steps. All detection alerts must be stored in a restricted-access log with full audit trail. The system must balance sensitivity to avoid excessive false positives while maintaining detection capability, with configurable detection parameters tunable by the security team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R001	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Attained EEXI Calculation Engine	The system must calculate the attained Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) for each vessel in the fleet using the IMO MEPC.333(76) methodology. The calculation shall incorporate reference speed, installed main engine MCR, shaft/engine power limitation factors, correction factors for ice-class vessels, and fuel-specific carbon conversion factors. The engine must support multiple fuel types including VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LNG, and methanol. Results must be compared against the required EEXI baseline derived from the vessel's DWT and type classification per IMO guidelines. All calculations must persist with full audit trails showing input parameters, intermediate values, and final attained EEXI in grams CO2 per tonne-nautical mile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R013	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	CII Rating Impact from Fuel Consumption	The system must calculate and track each vessel's Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) rating based on actual fuel consumption data, as required by IMO MEPC resolution 339(76). The CII calculation must use the attained Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) formula: total CO2 emissions divided by (vessel deadweight tonnage multiplied by total distance sailed). The system must compare each vessel's attained CII against the required CII thresholds for the current year (which become progressively stricter annually through 2030) and assign a rating from A (major superior) to E (inferior). The system must project year-end CII ratings based on year-to-date consumption and planned voyages, alerting fleet managers when a vessel is trending toward a D or E rating that would require a corrective action plan. The AI engine must recommend fuel consumption optimization strategies such as speed reduction, route optimization, or fuel grade switching to improve CII trajectories. Projections must be updated weekly and available per vessel, per entity, and at fleet level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R014	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Changeover Log for Emission Control Areas	The system must manage and record fuel changeover procedures when vessels transition between high-sulphur and low-sulphur fuels for Emission Control Area (ECA) compliance. The fuel changeover log must capture the changeover commencement date, time, and vessel position, the initial fuel grade and sulphur content, the target fuel grade and sulphur content, each tank's changeover sequence and timing, the estimated and actual volume of mixed fuel during transition, the changeover completion date, time, and position confirming the vessel was burning compliant fuel before entering the ECA boundary, and the chief engineer's confirmation signature. The system must validate that changeover was completed sufficiently before ECA entry based on the vessel's speed and distance to the ECA boundary. For vessels trading in the Arabian Gulf region where ECA regulations may be implemented in future, the system must be configurable to add new ECA zones without system modification. Changeover records must be retained for a minimum of 3 years and be readily accessible for port state control verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R015	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Offline ROB Reporting and Synchronization	The system must support offline fuel ROB reporting from vessels operating in areas with limited or no satellite communication coverage. The offline module must cache the vessel's tank configuration, calibration tables, previous ROB records, and reporting templates. Engineers must be able to complete daily ROB reports, record bunker deliveries, log tank transfers, and perform consumption calculations entirely offline. All offline entries must be timestamped using the device's local clock with UTC offset and must queue for automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored. The synchronization engine must handle scenarios where multiple offline reports are queued and must process them in chronological order to maintain ROB continuity. If shore-side corrections have been applied to the same records during the offline period, the system must flag conflicts for manual resolution rather than automatically overwriting either version. The offline module must function reliably for continuous periods of up to 30 days without connectivity, storing all interim reports locally with data integrity protection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R016	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel Inventory Valuation and Financial Reporting	The system must calculate fuel inventory valuations for financial reporting purposes using entity-specific accounting policies (FIFO, weighted average, or specific identification as configured per entity). Inventory values must be calculated at each reporting date (month-end, quarter-end, year-end) for each vessel, entity, and fuel grade. The system must perform lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value assessments by comparing the carrying cost of fuel inventory against current market prices, generating write-down entries when market value falls below carrying cost. Inventory reports must present values in each entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and in the group consolidation currency (USD). The system must generate inventory movement reports showing opening balance, purchases, consumption, transfers, adjustments, and closing balance in both quantity (metric tons) and value. All inventory valuation calculations must be auditable with full traceability from the financial statement line item back to individual vessel tank quantities and acquisition costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R017	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Fuel ROB Reconciliation Audit and Compliance Reports	The system must generate comprehensive fuel ROB reconciliation audit reports that demonstrate the accuracy and completeness of fuel inventory records for internal audit, external audit, statutory compliance, and regulatory review purposes. Reports must include period-over-period ROB movement summaries, delivery-to-ROB reconciliation statements, consumption analysis versus benchmark, discrepancy investigation results and resolution records, and physical survey variance analysis. The system must support configurable reporting periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) aligned with each entity's financial calendar. Reports must highlight any unresolved discrepancies, pending investigations, or open adjustment requests. For multi-entity operations, the system must produce both entity-level reports for local statutory audit and consolidated reports for group audit. All reports must be generated in English and Arabic with Hijri and Gregorian date columns, proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions, and export options in PDF and Excel. The system must maintain an audit trail showing who generated each report, when, and what data parameters were used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-1-004-R018	FEAT-013-1-004	MOD-013	Integration with Vessel IoT Sensors for Automated ROB Updates	The system must support integration with vessel-installed IoT sensors including tank level gauges, flow meters, density meters, and temperature sensors to enable automated and continuous fuel ROB updates. The integration must support standard maritime IoT protocols and data formats, with configurable polling intervals (default every 15 minutes during sea passage, every 5 minutes during bunkering operations). Sensor data must be validated against plausibility rules (e.g., ROB cannot increase without a recorded delivery, consumption rate cannot exceed the vessel's maximum rated consumption) before updating the system ROB. When sensor data conflicts with manual reports, the system must flag the discrepancy for engineering team review rather than automatically overwriting manual entries. The IoT integration must handle intermittent connectivity by buffering readings onboard and transmitting in batches when satellite bandwidth is available. Sensor calibration records and drift correction factors must be maintained within the system, with alerts generated when sensor readings suggest calibration drift exceeding configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R003	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Corrective Action Plan Generation for C/D/E Rated Vessels	When a vessel receives a CII rating of D for three consecutive years or a rating of E in any single year, the system must automatically generate a corrective action plan as required by IMO MEPC.338(76). The plan must include specific operational measures such as speed reduction targets, route optimization recommendations, hull cleaning schedules, and engine efficiency improvement suggestions. Each corrective action must include estimated CII improvement impact in percentage terms, implementation timeline, estimated cost in the vessel's operating currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), and responsible party assignment. The plan must be exportable as a PDF document in both English and Arabic for submission to flag state administrations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R004	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	SEEMP Part III Documentation Management	The system must manage Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan (SEEMP) Part III documentation as mandated by IMO for CII compliance. This includes maintaining enhanced SEEMP documents with implementation plans for achieving required CII targets, annual self-assessment and improvement tracking, and verification records from recognized organizations. The system shall store SEEMP documents for each vessel with version control, track review and approval workflows across multi-entity operations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and generate alerts 90 days before annual verification deadlines. Document storage must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date references for KSA and Qatar regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R005	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	IMO DCS Fuel Consumption Data Collection	The system must collect and aggregate fuel consumption data in accordance with IMO Data Collection System (DCS) requirements under MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 22A. Data collection shall cover each fuel type consumed (HFO, VLSFO, MGO, LNG, LPG, methanol, ethanol), distance travelled, hours underway, and transport work for each calendar year. The system must support both automated data ingestion from vessel noon reports and manual entry with validation rules preventing negative values, consumption exceeding engine capacity limits, and distance inconsistencies with AIS tracking data. Aggregated annual reports must be generated for submission to flag state administrations and the IMO Ship Fuel Oil Consumption Database.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R007	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Voyage-Level Emissions Tracking and Breakdown	The system must track CO2, SOx, NOx, and particulate matter emissions at the individual voyage level, broken down by voyage leg segments between ports. For each leg, emissions shall be calculated based on actual fuel consumed, fuel type emission factors per IMO guidelines, and engine load profiles. The breakdown must distinguish between emissions generated at sea, during port maneuvering, and at berth for vessels calling at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and any other configured ports. Emissions data must be attributable to specific bill-of-lading cargo for cost allocation purposes and must support aggregation by trade lane, vessel class, customer, and reporting entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R009	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Carbon Offset and ETS Credit Tracking	The system must track carbon offset purchases and emissions trading scheme (ETS) credits applicable to the fleet. For EU ETS compliance affecting vessels on Middle East to Europe routes, the system shall track free allowance allocations, surrendered allowances, and outstanding obligations per vessel per year. Carbon offset purchases from voluntary markets must be recorded with certificate references, vintage year, offset project type, and verification standard. All credit and offset transactions must be recorded in their original purchase currency (USD, EUR, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with automatic conversion to the reporting entity's base currency using the exchange rate on the transaction date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R010	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Flag State Regulatory Submission Workflow	The system must support structured submission workflows for emissions compliance documents to flag state administrations of Qatar, UAE (including RAK and ADMA registries), Saudi Arabia, India (DG Shipping), and other configured flag states. Each submission workflow shall include document preparation with validation checks, internal review and approval routing based on the operating entity's organizational hierarchy, electronic submission tracking with receipt confirmation, and deficiency response management. The workflow must enforce segregation of duties such that the preparer cannot also approve submissions. Submission deadlines must be tracked in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars with configurable advance notification periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R011	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Technical Measures Tracking for EEXI Compliance	The system must maintain a registry of technical measures implemented on each vessel to achieve EEXI compliance, including engine power limitation (EPL/SHaPoLi), energy-saving devices (ESD) such as propeller boss cap fins and Mewis ducts, waste heat recovery systems, and hull air lubrication systems. Each measure record must capture installation date, classification society approval reference, measured efficiency improvement percentage, installation cost in the vessel's operating currency, and maintenance schedule. The system must recalculate the attained EEXI upon addition or modification of any technical measure and verify that the vessel remains compliant with the required EEXI threshold after the change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R012	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Emissions Benchmarking Across Fleet and Industry	The system must provide benchmarking capabilities comparing individual vessel CII performance against fleet averages and industry benchmarks published by IMO and classification societies. Benchmarking shall be segmented by vessel type (container, tanker, bulk carrier), size category (DWT range), and trade route pattern (Arabian Gulf feeder, Middle East–India, Middle East–Europe, Middle East–Far East). The benchmarking module must display percentile rankings, trend analysis over rolling 12-month periods, and identification of top and bottom performing vessels. Data visualization must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with proper number formatting using Eastern Arabic numerals when the Arabic locale is active.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R013	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Automated Regulatory Change Monitoring	The system must monitor and alert users to changes in IMO, EU, and regional emissions regulations that affect fleet compliance obligations. This includes tracking amendments to MARPOL Annex VI, updates to CII reduction factors, new emission control areas (ECAs), and regional regulations from Qatar's Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Transport General Authority, and India's Director General of Shipping. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notification and email to designated compliance officers within each operating entity. Each alert must reference the regulatory instrument, effective date, affected vessel categories, and required system configuration changes with links to the full regulatory text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R014	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Offline Emissions Data Collection and Sync	The system must support offline data collection for emissions-related measurements when vessel connectivity is unavailable in the Arabian Gulf, Indian Ocean, or during port stays at facilities without reliable internet. Officers aboard vessels must be able to record daily fuel consumption figures, engine running hours, and distance sailed using a locally cached application that stores data in an encrypted local database. Upon reconnection, the system must automatically synchronize collected data with the central server, performing conflict resolution when overlapping entries exist. The offline module must validate data entries against configurable range limits to prevent erroneous submissions and must queue regulatory report generation until all offline data is successfully merged.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R015	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Multi-Entity Consolidated Emissions Reporting	The system must generate consolidated emissions reports across all operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for corporate sustainability reporting and ESG disclosure purposes. Consolidation rules must support both operational control and equity share approaches per the GHG Protocol Corporate Standard. Reports must aggregate Scope 1 direct emissions from vessel operations and Scope 2 indirect emissions from port terminal electricity consumption where applicable. Each entity's contribution must be separately identifiable within the consolidated report. Output formats must include PDF, Excel, and machine-readable XML/JSON for integration with corporate ESG reporting platforms. All monetary values in reports must display in both local entity currency and a configurable group reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R016	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	Classification Society Verification Interface	The system must provide a secure portal or API interface for classification societies (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, Indian Register of Shipping) to access vessel EEXI and CII documentation for verification purposes. The interface shall support read-only access to attained EEXI calculations, CII rating computations with supporting data, SEEMP Part III documents, and technical file amendments. Access must be granted on a per-vessel, time-limited basis with full audit logging of all data accessed. The system must support the generation and digital signing of Statement of Compliance documents in both English and Arabic and track their validity periods with renewal alerts at 60 and 30 days before expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-001-R017	FEAT-013-2-001	MOD-013	AI-Driven CII Improvement Recommendations	The system must leverage AI to analyze historical voyage data, fuel consumption patterns, weather routing effectiveness, and port turnaround times to generate actionable recommendations for improving individual vessel CII ratings. Recommendations shall include optimal speed profiles for specific trade lanes between Arabian Gulf ports and Indian subcontinent destinations, ideal ballast voyage routing, hull cleaning timing based on fouling prediction models, and trim optimization suggestions per cargo loading configuration. Each recommendation must include a confidence score, estimated CII improvement in absolute and percentage terms, implementation cost estimate in the vessel's operating currency, and payback period calculation. The AI engine must retrain monthly using updated operational data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R001	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Bunker Delivery Note (BDN) Digital Management	The system must digitally capture, store, and manage Bunker Delivery Notes (BDN) as required by MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18.5. Each BDN record must include supplier name, vessel IMO number, port of delivery (supporting Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA, and all configured ports), date and time of delivery, fuel grade designation (VLSFO, ULSFO, MGO, LSMGO, HFO for scrubber-equipped vessels), quantity delivered in metric tonnes, sulphur content declared by supplier in percentage m/m, density at 15°C, and flash point. The system must retain BDN records for a minimum of three years as per regulation and support both scanned document attachment and structured data entry with OCR-assisted extraction from uploaded documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R002	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Sample Tracking and Lab Analysis Integration	The system must track MARPOL-mandated fuel oil samples drawn during bunkering operations, maintaining chain of custody records from sample collection to laboratory analysis and final retention. Each sample record must include a unique seal number, the corresponding BDN reference, collection point (manifold or tank), sample volume, and storage location aboard the vessel. The system must integrate with accredited testing laboratories (such as VPS, Intertek, and Bureau Veritas) to receive electronic test results including sulphur content, viscosity, catalytic fines (Al+Si), water content, and stability parameters. When lab results indicate sulphur content exceeding 0.50% m/m for non-scrubber vessels, the system must automatically generate a non-compliance alert and initiate a corrective action workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R003	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Changeover Procedure Management for ECAs	The system must manage fuel oil changeover procedures when vessels transit into and out of Emission Control Areas (ECAs) where the sulphur limit is 0.10% m/m. The changeover plan must calculate the required start time for switching from 0.50% sulphur fuel to 0.10% ECA-compliant fuel based on vessel speed, distance to ECA boundary, fuel system volume, and consumption rate to ensure full changeover before entering the ECA. The system must record opening and closing tank quantities, changeover start and completion timestamps, and the volume of ECA-compliant fuel consumed within the ECA. All changeover records must be maintained in the Oil Record Book Part I format and be exportable for port state control inspections at Middle Eastern and Indian ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R004	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Scrubber-Equipped Vessel Compliance Monitoring	For vessels equipped with Exhaust Gas Cleaning Systems (EGCS/scrubbers), the system must monitor compliance with IMO MEPC.259(68) guidelines. The system shall track continuous emissions monitoring system (CEMS) data including SOx/CO2 ratio readings, washwater discharge parameters (pH, turbidity, PAH, nitrates), and scrubber operational hours. Non-compliant readings exceeding the SOx/CO2 ratio equivalent to 0.50% sulphur fuel must trigger immediate alerts. The system must maintain scrubber operating logs, track planned maintenance schedules, and record any scrubber malfunction periods during which the vessel must switch to compliant low-sulphur fuel. Washwater discharge restrictions in specific ports (including zero-discharge zones in certain Arabian Gulf ports) must be configurable and enforced with geofence alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R005	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Oil Non-Availability Report (FONAR) Workflow	The system must support the creation and submission of Fuel Oil Non-Availability Reports (FONAR) as per MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18.2 when compliant fuel is not available at a bunkering port. The FONAR workflow must capture the vessel's attempts to procure compliant fuel including supplier quotations received, reasons for non-availability (no stock, quality issues, incompatibility), alternative fuels considered, and the planned remediation action (next compliant bunkering port). The system must pre-populate vessel details, port information, and voyage plan data. FONARs must be electronically submitted to the vessel's flag state administration and the next port of call's authorities. Historical FONAR records must be retained and analyzed to identify ports with recurring supply issues, particularly tracking availability trends at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R006	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Compatibility and Stability Management	The system must track fuel compatibility and stability parameters to prevent operational issues when commingling different fuel batches. For each fuel tank, the system shall maintain a record of the current fuel's CCAI (Calculated Carbon Aromaticity Index), compatibility number, and stability characteristics from lab analysis. When a new bunkering operation is planned, the system must check the incoming fuel's compatibility with the existing fuel in the designated receiving tank using TSP (Total Sediment Potential) prediction models. Incompatible fuel combinations must generate warnings with recommended mitigation actions such as using a dedicated segregated tank or adding fuel treatment chemicals. The system must track all commingling events and their outcomes for trend analysis across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R007	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Sulphur Compliance Document Package for PSC Inspections	The system must generate a comprehensive sulphur compliance document package ready for port state control (PSC) inspections at Arabian Gulf and Indian ports. The package must include the International Air Pollution Prevention (IAPP) certificate, valid BDNs for the current and previous bunkering operations, fuel sample retention records with seal numbers, fuel changeover procedures and logs, written fuel oil changeover plan for ECA transits, and scrubber documentation (EGCS Technical Manual, SOx Emissions Compliance Plan) where applicable. All documents must be presentable in both English and Arabic to satisfy inspection requirements at Qatar, UAE, and KSA ports. The system must maintain an inspection readiness dashboard showing document completeness status with red/amber/green indicators for each vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R008	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Multi-Grade Fuel Inventory Tracking per Vessel	The system must track fuel oil inventory across all tanks on each vessel, segregated by fuel grade and sulphur content. Tank sounding records must be captured at least daily, with automatic calculation of remaining quantity (ROB) based on tank capacity tables, temperature correction to standard 15°C density, and trim/heel corrections. The system must distinguish between HFO (above 0.50% S for scrubber vessels), VLSFO (max 0.50% S), ULSFO (max 0.10% S), and MGO/MDO grades in separate tracked inventories. Low fuel alerts must be configurable per grade per vessel, triggering bunkering requirement notifications when ROB falls below the quantity needed to reach the next planned bunkering port plus a configurable safety margin. All quantities must be recorded in metric tonnes with conversion support from cubic meters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R009	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Supplier Sulphur Content Performance Tracking	The system must maintain a performance database of bunker fuel suppliers across all bunkering ports in the operating network, tracking the deviation between declared sulphur content on BDNs and actual laboratory analysis results. Supplier performance metrics shall include average sulphur content deviation (declared vs. actual), frequency of off-specification deliveries, average delivery quantity variance, fuel quality complaint history, and response time to quality claims. The system must rank suppliers by reliability and compliance at each port, supporting procurement decisions. Suppliers with more than two off-specification deliveries within a 12-month period must be automatically flagged for review. Performance dashboards must be viewable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and aggregated across the group.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R010	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Regulatory Sulphur Limit Configuration by Zone	The system must maintain a configurable global map of sulphur emission control zones with their respective sulphur content limits and effective dates. This includes the global 0.50% m/m cap, existing ECAs (Baltic Sea, North Sea, North American, US Caribbean) at 0.10%, and emerging regional restrictions in the Mediterranean and other areas. The configuration must support future regulatory changes such as tighter global caps or new ECA designations in the Middle East region. Each zone must be defined by geographical boundaries (polygon coordinates) and linked to applicable regulatory references. The system must automatically determine which sulphur limit applies to each voyage leg based on the planned route and generate a fuel requirement plan specifying the required fuel grade for each segment of the voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R011	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Quality Claim and Dispute Management	The system must manage fuel quality claims against bunker suppliers when delivered fuel fails to meet contractual or regulatory specifications. The claims workflow shall capture claim initiation with supporting evidence (lab analysis reports, BDN discrepancy documentation, engine damage reports), supplier notification with formal claim letter generation, counterclaim tracking, negotiation history, and settlement recording. Claim amounts must be calculated in the original bunkering transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with automatic conversion to the claiming entity's base currency for financial reporting. The system must track claim aging, escalation triggers for claims unresolved after configurable periods, and maintain statistics on claim success rates by supplier and port for procurement intelligence purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R012	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Low Sulphur Fuel Surcharge Calculation	The system must calculate and apply low sulphur fuel surcharges (LSS) to freight rates and container shipping tariffs to recover the incremental cost of IMO 2020 compliant fuels versus pre-regulation HFO. The calculation engine must determine the price differential between compliant VLSFO and baseline HFO using configurable benchmark price sources (Platts, Argus), apply vessel-specific consumption factors based on vessel class and trade lane, and allocate the surcharge per TEU or per bill of lading based on the voyage routing. Surcharge calculations must be transparent and auditable for customer billing disputes. The system must support different surcharge formulas per trade lane and per customer contract, with automatic monthly or quarterly surcharge adjustments based on updated fuel price differentials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R013	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Alternative Fuel Readiness Assessment	The system must maintain an alternative fuel readiness assessment module for each vessel evaluating conversion potential to LNG, methanol, ammonia, and hydrogen propulsion. Each assessment shall record the vessel's current engine type and fuel system configuration, estimated conversion cost in USD with entity-local currency equivalents, projected timeline for conversion, impact on cargo capacity and operational range, and regulatory approval requirements from the vessel's classification society and flag state. The module must track industry developments in alternative marine fuels availability at key bunkering ports including Fujairah, Jebel Ali, and Singapore. Assessment updates must be triggered annually or when significant regulatory or technology changes are detected by the automated monitoring system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R014	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Fuel Specification Compliance Alerts for Tank Transfers	The system must monitor and alert on internal fuel tank transfer operations that could result in sulphur compliance violations. When crew initiates a transfer between tanks containing different fuel grades, the system must verify that the receiving tank's intended usage zone (global, ECA) is compatible with the blended fuel's estimated sulphur content. Transfers that would result in a blended sulphur content exceeding the applicable limit for the tank's designated use must be blocked with an override requiring senior officer authorization and documented justification. The system must calculate the resulting blend's estimated sulphur content using a weighted average formula based on the quantities and declared sulphur content of each component. All transfer events must be logged in the fuel management record with before and after tank compositions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R015	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Port State Notification of Fuel Compliance Status	The system must generate and transmit pre-arrival fuel compliance notifications to port authorities as required by certain Middle Eastern and Indian ports. The notification must include the vessel's current fuel type in use, sulphur content of fuel in service tanks, intended fuel for use during port stay, EGCS operational status for scrubber-equipped vessels, and declaration of compliance with the applicable sulphur limit. For Indian ports under DG Shipping requirements, the system must submit notifications at least 24 hours before arrival. For UAE ports regulated by the Federal Transport Authority, the system must comply with local notification timelines. Notifications must be formatted according to each port authority's required template and transmitted via the prescribed electronic channel, with confirmation receipt tracking and automated retry mechanisms for failed transmissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R016	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Hijri and Gregorian Dual-Calendar Compliance Tracking	The system must support dual-calendar display and recording for all fuel compliance dates, certificates, and regulatory deadlines. Users in KSA and Qatar offices must be able to view BDN dates, sample retention expiry dates, IAPP certificate validity periods, and FONAR submission dates in both Hijri (Umm al-Qura) and Gregorian calendar formats simultaneously. The system must correctly handle date conversions accounting for the approximate nature of Hijri-Gregorian mapping and store all dates internally in Gregorian format with Hijri equivalents computed on display. Compliance deadline alerts must fire based on the Gregorian date but display the Hijri equivalent in notifications sent to KSA and Qatar entities. All date-filtered reports must accept input in either calendar system and produce consistent results regardless of the input calendar used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-002-R017	FEAT-013-2-002	MOD-013	Offline Fuel Compliance Record Entry	The system must support offline entry of fuel compliance records including BDN data, tank sounding readings, fuel changeover logs, and sample collection records when vessel connectivity is unavailable during Arabian Sea transits or port stays at facilities with limited internet. The offline module must enforce the same validation rules as the online system including mandatory field checks, sulphur content range validation (0.01%–5.00%), quantity reasonableness checks against tank capacities, and BDN number uniqueness verification against locally cached records. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline entries with the central server using a last-writer-wins conflict resolution strategy for simple fields and manual merge review for conflicting BDN entries. Synchronization status must be clearly indicated with pending, syncing, synced, and conflict states visible per record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R001	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Voyage-Level Bunker Cost Accumulation	The system must accumulate all bunker fuel costs incurred during a voyage, from commencement port to termination port, including fuel consumed during laden and ballast legs, port stays, canal transits, and waiting/drifting periods. Cost accumulation must capture the purchase price per metric tonne from BDN records, quantity consumed per fuel grade (VLSFO, MGO, HFO for scrubber vessels), and any associated costs such as barge delivery charges, port fuel surcharges, and quality testing fees. Each cost component must be recorded in its original transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with conversion to the voyage's base currency using the exchange rate on the consumption date. The system must handle split voyages where a vessel is commercially redelivered mid-voyage by prorating costs based on distance or time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R002	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Configurable Cost Allocation Methodologies	The system must support multiple bunker cost allocation methodologies selectable per voyage, trade lane, or entity: (1) pro-rata by TEU-miles, allocating based on container count multiplied by distance carried; (2) pro-rata by weight-miles for break-bulk and heavy cargo; (3) pro-rata by revenue share, allocating fuel costs proportional to freight revenue per bill of lading; (4) actual consumption by voyage leg with even distribution to cargo on each leg; and (5) flat rate per TEU using a configurable standard cost table. Each methodology must produce allocation results traceable to source data. The system must allow different methodologies for different cargo types on the same voyage (e.g., reefer containers receiving a higher allocation factor due to additional genset fuel consumption) and maintain historical methodology selections for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R003	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Bill of Lading Level Cost Attribution	The system must attribute bunker costs to individual bills of lading based on the selected allocation methodology. Each B/L must receive a calculated bunker cost share reflecting the distance its cargo was carried, the number and type of containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer, open-top, flat-rack), and the weight factor for heavy cargo. Reefer containers must receive an additional fuel cost allocation for genset operation based on configurable kWh consumption rates per reefer unit per hour. The B/L-level cost attribution must be reconcilable back to the total voyage bunker cost with rounding differences allocated to a configurable adjustment account. Cost attribution data must be accessible via API for integration with the freight accounting module (MOD-008) and customer profitability analysis systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R004	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Multi-Entity Transfer Pricing for Shared Voyages	For voyages operated by one entity but carrying cargo booked by multiple entities within the group (e.g., vessel operated by UAE entity carrying cargo booked by Qatar and India entities), the system must calculate inter-company bunker cost transfers in accordance with configured transfer pricing policies. Transfer prices must be determined using one of the configurable methods: cost-plus with a configurable markup percentage, comparable uncontrolled price benchmarked against market charter rates, or a pre-agreed fixed rate per TEU per trade lane. All transfer pricing calculations must comply with OECD transfer pricing guidelines and generate documentation suitable for tax authority review in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must produce inter-company invoices in the receiving entity's local currency with supporting allocation breakdowns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R005	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF) Recovery Analysis	The system must compare actual bunker costs allocated per voyage or trade lane against the Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF) surcharges collected from customers. The analysis must calculate the recovery ratio (BAF collected divided by actual bunker cost) per voyage, per trade lane, per customer, and per contract. Under-recovery and over-recovery must be highlighted with configurable threshold alerts (e.g., recovery below 85% or above 115%). The system must trend recovery ratios over time and identify customers or trade lanes with consistently poor cost recovery. All amounts must be presentable in the entity's base currency and in USD for group-level comparisons. Reports must support Arabic RTL formatting with proper alignment of currency columns and percentage values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R006	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Laden vs Ballast Voyage Leg Cost Separation	The system must separately identify and allocate bunker costs incurred during laden voyage legs (carrying revenue cargo) versus ballast voyage legs (repositioning without revenue cargo). Ballast leg costs must be allocable using configurable policies: fully absorbed by the subsequent laden voyage, split equally between the preceding and subsequent laden voyages, allocated to a repositioning cost center, or distributed across all laden voyages within a configurable time period using a pooling methodology. The chosen ballast cost policy must be configurable per trade lane and per operating entity. For triangulation voyages involving multiple laden legs (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT to Colombo), the system must allocate ballast repositioning between legs to the cargo benefiting from the repositioning movement based on distance proportionality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R007	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Port Stay and Idle Time Fuel Cost Allocation	The system must separately track and allocate fuel costs incurred during port stays (cargo operations at berth), anchorage waiting time, canal transit queuing, and off-hire periods. Port stay fuel consumption for cargo operations (crane operations, reefer monitoring, ventilation) must be allocated to the cargo loaded or discharged at that port. Anchorage waiting fuel costs must be allocable to the next cargo operation or distributed across all cargo aboard. Shore power usage at ports offering cold ironing (where available at Jebel Ali or JNPT) must offset bunker cost allocations with the shore power electricity cost substituted. Off-hire periods due to mechanical breakdown or detention must be excluded from cargo cost allocation and charged to a vessel operating expense cost center with reference to the off-hire event record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R008	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Standard vs Actual Bunker Cost Variance Analysis	The system must calculate and report variances between standard (budgeted) bunker costs and actual bunker costs at the voyage level. Standard costs shall be derived from the voyage estimate module using planned consumption rates, budgeted fuel prices, and expected voyage duration. Variances must be decomposed into price variance (difference between budgeted and actual fuel price per tonne), consumption variance (difference between planned and actual consumption in tonnes), and efficiency variance (deviation from planned speed/consumption curve). Each variance component must be expressed in absolute monetary terms in the entity's base currency and as a percentage of budgeted cost. The system must support drill-down from voyage-level variances to individual leg-level details and generate exception reports for voyages exceeding configurable variance thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R009	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Bunker Cost Accrual and Prepayment Accounting	The system must generate bunker cost accrual entries for fuel consumed but not yet invoiced, and prepayment entries for fuel purchased but not yet consumed at voyage end or accounting period close. Accruals must be calculated based on consumption quantities from vessel reports multiplied by the last known purchase price for each fuel grade. Prepayments must reflect the cost of remaining on board (ROB) fuel valued using configurable methods: FIFO (first in first out), weighted average cost, or last purchase price. The system must automatically reverse accruals and prepayments at the start of the next accounting period. Journal entries must be generated in the correct entity's chart of accounts with proper inter-company elimination entries where fuel purchased by one entity is consumed on a voyage operated by another. All entries must comply with IFRS 15 revenue recognition principles for voyage accounting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R010	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Reefer Container Genset Fuel Segregation	The system must segregate fuel consumption and cost for powering refrigerated container gensets from main engine and auxiliary engine fuel consumption. Genset fuel consumption must be calculated based on the number of reefer containers plugged in, their power consumption settings (based on temperature setpoint and ambient conditions), and the duration of powered operation during sea passages and port stays. The segregated genset fuel cost must be allocated directly to the specific reefer bills of lading based on container-level power consumption records. Where actual per-container power monitoring data is unavailable, the system must use configurable standard consumption rates per reefer type (integral, clip-on, porthole) and temperature range (frozen, chilled, controlled atmosphere). The genset fuel segregation must reconcile with total vessel fuel consumption within a configurable tolerance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R011	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Canal Transit Fuel Cost Special Allocation	The system must apply special allocation rules for bunker costs incurred during canal transits (Suez Canal for Middle East–Europe routes, potential future canal transits configured by users). Canal transit fuel consumption is typically lower than open-sea steaming but the transit duration affects voyage economics. The system must allocate canal transit fuel costs to all cargo aboard during the transit proportional to TEU or weight. Canal transit waiting time (at anchorage in Suez roadstead or at Great Bitter Lake) must be tracked separately with fuel consumption during waiting periods allocated as a separate cost component. The system must support configurable canal transit standard consumption rates for each vessel class and reconcile against actual consumption. Canal toll costs must be tracked alongside but separately from bunker costs for voyage profitability analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R012	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Multi-Currency Bunker Cost Consolidation	The system must consolidate bunker costs across multiple currencies for fleet-wide and group-wide reporting. Fuel purchases may be invoiced in USD (international standard), AED (UAE bunkering), QAR (Qatar bunkering), SAR (Saudi bunkering), or INR (India bunkering). The consolidation engine must convert all costs to a configurable group reporting currency using exchange rates sourced from the central treasury module. Three exchange rate options must be supported: transaction date spot rate, monthly average rate, and voyage-weighted average rate. The system must clearly display the original transaction currency and amount alongside the converted reporting currency amount. Exchange rate gains and losses arising from timing differences between fuel purchase and cost allocation must be separately identified and allocated to a foreign exchange variance account per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R013	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Cost Allocation Audit Trail and Restatement	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all bunker cost allocations, capturing the methodology used, input data sources (BDNs, consumption reports, voyage events), calculation parameters, intermediate computation steps, and final allocation results. Authorized users must be able to restate historical allocations when errors are discovered or when allocation methodology changes are applied retrospectively. Restatements must create new allocation versions without deleting the original, with clear identification of the original and restated values, the reason for restatement, and the approving user. The system must propagate restatements to downstream financial records including voyage P&L, customer profitability reports, and inter-company invoices. Restatement access must be restricted to designated cost controllers with separate approval authority, and all restatements must generate notifications to affected entity finance managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R014	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Emissions Cost Allocation per Cargo	The system must allocate emissions-related costs (EU ETS allowance costs, carbon offset purchases, potential carbon levy charges) to individual cargo consignments alongside direct bunker fuel costs. Emissions costs shall be allocated proportionally to each cargo's attributed CO2 emissions, calculated from the fuel consumption allocated to that cargo multiplied by the applicable fuel-specific emission factor. The combined bunker and emissions cost per TEU, per tonne, and per bill of lading must be available for customer profitability analysis and environmental surcharge validation. The system must support future carbon pricing mechanisms such as IMO market-based measures or regional carbon taxes applicable in operating jurisdictions. All emissions cost allocations must be separately identifiable in financial reports to support sustainability disclosure requirements under ISSB S2 climate-related standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R015	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Voyage Profitability Dashboard with Bunker Cost Impact	The system must provide a voyage profitability dashboard showing the impact of bunker costs on voyage financial performance. The dashboard must display total revenue, total bunker cost (broken down by fuel grade), bunker cost as a percentage of total voyage cost, bunker cost per TEU carried, BAF recovery ratio, and net voyage result after bunker costs. Comparative views must show actual versus estimated performance and current voyage versus historical average for the same trade lane. The dashboard must support filtering by operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel, trade lane, and date range. All financial figures must be displayable in entity local currency or group reporting currency. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with proper alignment of financial columns, decimal separators, and thousand grouping characters appropriate to the active locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R016	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Automated Cost Allocation on Voyage Completion	The system must automatically trigger bunker cost allocation processing upon voyage completion status change. When a voyage is marked as commercially complete, the system shall validate that all fuel consumption data has been received for all voyage legs, verify that all bunkering invoices have been recorded or accrued, execute the configured allocation methodology, generate allocation entries against each bill of lading and cost center, and update the voyage profit and loss statement. If required data is missing (incomplete consumption reports or unrecorded bunkering), the system must place the allocation in a pending state with clear identification of the missing data items and notification to the responsible operations personnel. The automated allocation must be reversible and re-runnable if source data is corrected within a configurable lock-out period after initial allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-003-R017	FEAT-013-2-003	MOD-013	Bunker Cost Forecasting for Voyage Estimates	The system must provide bunker cost forecasting for voyage estimation and planning purposes. The forecast engine must estimate total bunker cost for a proposed voyage based on planned route distance, vessel-specific consumption curves at various speeds, current fuel prices at planned bunkering ports (sourced from recent purchase history and configurable market price feeds), port stay durations with auxiliary consumption estimates, and anticipated weather conditions for the planned departure window on Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean routes. The forecast must present cost in both the operating entity's base currency and USD, with sensitivity analysis showing cost variation at different speed scenarios (eco speed, normal speed, full speed) and at different fuel price assumptions (current price, plus/minus 10% and 20%). Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actual voyage costs to continuously calibrate the forecasting model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R001	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	AI-Driven Optimal Bunkering Port Selection	The system must use AI algorithms to recommend the optimal bunkering port(s) for each voyage based on multi-factor analysis including fuel prices at candidate ports (Fujairah, Jebel Ali AEJEA, Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, JNPT INNSA, Singapore, Colombo, and other configured ports), deviation distance and additional steaming cost to reach the bunkering port, port charges and barge delivery fees, fuel quality track record at each port, available fuel grades matching vessel requirements, berth availability and expected waiting time, and the vessel's consumption forecast for the remaining voyage. The optimization must produce a ranked list of bunkering options with total cost comparison in USD and the operating entity's base currency, including the deviation cost penalty. Recommendations must update dynamically as fuel prices and voyage schedules change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-006-1-003-R012	FEAT-006-1-003	MOD-006	Document Sharing with Third Parties	Customers share specific documents with authorized third parties (customs brokers, banks, consignees) through secure portal links.	must_have			["Customer selects documents to share and specifies recipient email address, access permission (view-only or download), and link expiry period", "Shared link requires recipient to verify email via OTP before accessing documents", "Document owner can revoke shared access at any time; access revocation takes effect immediately", "All third-party document access events are logged in the document audit trail with recipient identity and action performed"]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R002	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Optimal Bunkering Quantity Calculation	The system must calculate the optimal quantity of fuel to purchase at each bunkering stop, balancing the trade-off between lower fuel prices at the current port versus the cost of carrying additional fuel weight (increased consumption due to higher displacement). The optimization algorithm must consider the vessel's specific fuel consumption curve at planned speed, tank capacity constraints, minimum ROB safety margins, the price differential between the current port and the next anticipated bunkering port, and the additional fuel burned to carry the extra bunker weight. The calculation must account for trim optimization effects where strategic fuel tank filling sequence can improve vessel hydrodynamic efficiency. Output must include recommended quantity per fuel grade in metric tonnes with the calculated cost saving compared to the next cheapest bunkering alternative.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R003	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Fuel Price Forecasting Model	The system must incorporate an AI-based fuel price forecasting model that predicts VLSFO, MGO, and HFO prices at key bunkering ports over 7-day, 14-day, 30-day, and 90-day horizons. The model shall use historical price data (minimum 3 years), crude oil futures curves, regional supply/demand indicators, seasonal patterns for Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean bunker markets, and geopolitical risk factors. Forecast outputs must include point estimates, confidence intervals (80% and 95%), and directional probability (likelihood of price increase or decrease). The model must be retrained weekly using updated market data and its forecast accuracy must be tracked against actual prices with MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) and directional accuracy metrics displayed on a model performance dashboard. Users must be able to override AI forecasts with manual price assumptions for scenario planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R004	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Speed and Consumption Optimization	The system must optimize vessel speed profiles across voyage legs to minimize total fuel consumption while meeting schedule constraints. The optimization engine must use vessel-specific consumption curves calibrated from historical noon report data, accounting for hull fouling degradation over time since last dry-dock, weather and current conditions from integrated weather routing data for Arabian Gulf, Arabian Sea, and Indian Ocean passages, draft and trim variations based on cargo loading, and contractual requirements for arrival windows at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and other scheduled ports. The optimizer must output a recommended speed for each voyage leg in knots with the estimated fuel saving in metric tonnes and monetary value compared to a uniform-speed voyage. Virtual arrival scenarios must be supported where slowing down to arrive at berth availability time saves fuel versus arriving early and waiting at anchorage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R005	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Fleet-Wide Bunkering Schedule Optimization	The system must optimize bunkering schedules across the entire fleet simultaneously rather than vessel-by-vessel. The fleet optimization model must consider volume discounts available from suppliers for consolidated multi-vessel purchases at the same port, coordinated bunkering windows to minimize barge mobilization costs, fleet-wide fuel inventory levels and consumption projections, contractual term supply agreements with minimum lift obligations, and seasonal price patterns at Arabian Gulf bunkering hubs. The optimization must produce a rolling 30-day fleet bunkering plan showing recommended bunkering port, date, quantity, and estimated cost for each vessel. The plan must be recalculated daily with change highlights when the AI detects significant shifts in prices, schedules, or consumption patterns that alter the optimal plan by more than a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R006	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Vessel Performance Model Calibration	The system must maintain AI-calibrated vessel performance models for each vessel in the fleet, relating speed, draft, trim, weather conditions, and hull condition to fuel consumption rate. The model must be continuously updated using actual noon report data with anomaly detection to filter out erroneous data points (e.g., unrealistic consumption readings due to tank measurement errors). Hull fouling degradation curves must be estimated and updated, projecting the increase in fuel consumption over time since last hull cleaning or dry-dock. The model must predict the fuel consumption benefit of hull cleaning interventions, recommending cleaning when the estimated cost saving from reduced consumption exceeds the cleaning cost. Model accuracy must be monitored with predictions compared against actual consumption, and alerts generated when model error exceeds 5% on a rolling 10-voyage basis, indicating the need for model recalibration or investigation of vessel mechanical issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R007	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Weather Routing Integration for Fuel Optimization	The system must integrate with weather routing services to incorporate sea state, wind, current, and swell forecasts into fuel consumption optimization. For typical trade routes in the operating network (Arabian Gulf to Indian subcontinent, Arabian Gulf to East Africa, Arabian Gulf to Europe via Suez), the system must evaluate alternative route options considering weather forecasts and calculate the fuel consumption impact of each option using the vessel performance model. During monsoon seasons affecting Arabian Sea crossings (June–September), the system must proactively suggest route and speed adjustments that minimize fuel consumption while maintaining acceptable safety margins. Weather routing recommendations must be presented alongside the fuel cost saving estimate in the vessel's operating currency and must be logged for post-voyage analysis of weather routing effectiveness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R008	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	AI Anomaly Detection in Fuel Consumption	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to identify unusual fuel consumption patterns that may indicate mechanical problems, reporting errors, or potential fuel theft. The anomaly detection model must compare actual consumption against expected consumption from the vessel performance model for the given speed, draft, and weather conditions. Anomalies must be classified by severity (minor deviation 5-10% above expected, significant deviation 10-20%, critical deviation above 20%) and probable cause category (hull fouling, engine inefficiency, propeller damage, adverse weather beyond forecast, data entry error, or unexplained). Each detected anomaly must generate a notification to the vessel's technical superintendent and fleet performance manager with the deviation magnitude in metric tonnes and monetary value. The system must track anomaly resolution outcomes to improve future classification accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-001-R001	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Dynamic Slot Allocation Optimization	AI engine continuously optimizes container slot allocation across vessel voyages to maximize revenue yield per sailing.	must_have			["AI recalculates optimal slot allocation every 15 minutes for vessels departing within 14 days", "System balances slot mix between dry, reefer, hazardous, and OOG containers per voyage", "Allocation considers minimum commitment contracts before releasing slots to spot market", "Overbooking threshold is configurable per trade lane (default 5-12% based on historical no-show rates)", "Dashboard displays real-time slot utilization vs. optimal allocation with variance alerts"]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R009	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Bunker Procurement Contract Optimization	The system must analyze historical bunkering patterns and AI-forecasted future requirements to recommend optimal procurement contract structures. Analysis must include evaluation of spot versus term supply agreements at each bunkering port, optimal contract volume commitments based on forecasted fleet requirements at Fujairah, Jebel Ali, and other high-frequency ports, recommended price formula structures (fixed, floating linked to Platts, cap-and-floor), and supplier diversification recommendations to mitigate supply concentration risk. The system must simulate the financial impact of proposed contract terms against historical price movements and forecasted scenarios, presenting potential savings or additional costs compared to spot market procurement. Recommendations must be generated quarterly and must account for multi-entity procurement consolidation opportunities across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R010	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Trim and Ballast Optimization for Fuel Efficiency	The system must provide AI-driven trim and ballast optimization recommendations to minimize fuel consumption during each voyage leg. Using the vessel's hydrostatic data, loading condition, consumption profile, and sea state forecast, the system must calculate the optimal dynamic trim for each draft condition throughout the voyage as fuel is consumed and ballast is adjusted. Recommendations must specify target trim by the bow or stern in meters, ballast tank filling or emptying sequence, and the estimated fuel saving in metric tonnes and cost terms compared to the vessel's default trim settings. The optimization must respect stability criteria (GM, free surface effects, stress limits) and class-society-approved loading conditions. Optimization results must be presented in a simplified visual format for bridge officers with estimated daily fuel savings displayed prominently for the recommended trim setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R011	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Machine Learning Model Transparency and Explainability	All AI and machine learning models used in the bunker optimization engine must provide transparent and explainable outputs to build user trust and support decision-making. Each optimization recommendation must include the key factors driving the recommendation with their relative importance weights, a sensitivity analysis showing how changes in key inputs (fuel price plus/minus 10%, speed variation, weather changes) affect the recommendation, historical accuracy metrics for the specific model producing the recommendation, and a confidence level (high, medium, low) based on data quality and model certainty. The system must support a what-if scenario mode where users can adjust input assumptions and see the impact on recommendations without affecting production models. Explanations must be presented in plain language appropriate for maritime operations personnel in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R012	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Integration with Vessel IoT Sensor Data	The system must integrate with vessel IoT sensor systems to receive real-time data feeds for fuel optimization model inputs. Supported data points must include flowmeter readings for main engine, auxiliary engines, and boiler fuel consumption, shaft power and RPM measurements, GPS speed over ground and through-water speed log readings, draft sensors (fore, aft, midship), and exhaust gas temperature and turbocharger performance indicators. The system must handle data quality issues including sensor dropout, out-of-range readings, and communication latency for vessels in areas with poor satellite connectivity across the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean. Data must be received via configurable protocols (MQTT, REST API, satellite communication gateways) with buffering and retry mechanisms. Sensor data must be timestamped and stored at configurable intervals (minimum 15-minute granularity) for model training and real-time optimization updates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R013	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Bunker Hedge Recommendation Engine	The system must provide AI-driven recommendations for fuel price hedging strategies to protect against adverse price movements. Based on the fleet's forecasted fuel consumption over 3, 6, and 12 month horizons, current forward curve prices, and the AI price forecast model, the system must recommend hedging volumes, instruments (swaps, options, collars), and timing. The hedge recommendation must include the estimated premium cost for options strategies, the breakeven price for swap contracts, and the value-at-risk reduction achieved by the proposed hedge. Recommendations must be segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) based on each entity's fuel consumption forecast and hedging policy limits. All financial values must be presented in USD as the standard bunker market currency with entity-local currency equivalents. The system must track hedge execution and mark-to-market valuations for executed hedges against the underlying physical fuel consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R014	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	AI Model Training Data Pipeline and Governance	The system must maintain a robust data pipeline for training and updating AI models used in the bunker optimization engine. The pipeline must ingest data from vessel noon reports, bunkering records, fuel price feeds, weather data archives, port operational records, and vessel performance sensor data. Data cleansing rules must handle missing values, outliers, unit conversion errors, and temporal alignment of data from different sources. The system must enforce data governance policies including minimum data quality thresholds for model training (e.g., minimum 95% completeness for required fields), model version control with rollback capability, training/validation/test data split documentation, and model performance monitoring with automated alerts when prediction accuracy degrades below configurable thresholds. Model retraining schedules must be configurable per model type (weekly for price forecasting, monthly for consumption models, quarterly for long-term pattern models).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R015	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Multi-Entity AI Optimization Access Controls	The system must enforce entity-level and role-based access controls for AI optimization features and their outputs. Users from the Qatar entity must only see optimization recommendations, cost analyses, and vessel performance data for vessels and voyages operated by or allocated to the Qatar entity, unless granted explicit cross-entity access. Similarly, UAE, KSA, and India entity users must be restricted to their entity's scope. Fleet-level optimization recommendations that span multiple entities (such as consolidated procurement or cross-entity vessel swaps) must be visible only to users with group-level fleet management roles. AI model configuration and training parameters must be accessible only to designated data science or fleet performance roles. All access to optimization outputs must be logged with the user identity, entity, timestamp, and specific data accessed for compliance audit purposes in Arabic and English interface languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R016	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Optimization Scenario Comparison and Approval Workflow	The system must support the creation, comparison, and approval of multiple optimization scenarios for bunkering decisions. Users must be able to generate alternative scenarios by adjusting parameters such as bunkering ports, quantities, speed profiles, and fuel grade selections, and compare them side-by-side against the AI-recommended optimal scenario. Comparison views must show total cost difference, schedule impact, emissions impact, and risk assessment for each scenario. A configurable approval workflow must route selected bunkering plans through the appropriate authorization chain based on the total fuel expenditure value: plans under USD 100,000 approved by vessel manager, USD 100,000–500,000 by fleet manager, and above USD 500,000 by operations director. Approved scenarios must automatically generate purchase requisitions in the procurement module. The approval workflow must support delegation during user absence and must function in both English and Arabic interfaces with RTL text and locale-specific number formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R017	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Continuous Learning and Optimization Feedback Loop	The system must implement a continuous learning feedback loop where actual voyage outcomes are compared against AI optimization recommendations to improve future model accuracy. After each voyage completion, the system shall automatically calculate the variance between AI-recommended and actual values for bunkering cost, fuel consumption, voyage duration, and CII impact. Positive and negative variances must be analyzed to identify systematic biases in the optimization models, such as consistent underestimation of consumption in adverse weather or overestimation of fuel price changes at specific ports. The feedback data must be incorporated into the next model retraining cycle with appropriate weighting for recency. A quarterly model performance review report must be generated showing prediction accuracy trends, identified biases, model improvements implemented, and estimated cumulative financial benefit of AI-optimized decisions versus manual baseline decisions in USD and each entity's local currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-013-2-004-R018	FEAT-013-2-004	MOD-013	Offline AI Recommendation Caching for Vessel Use	The system must provide offline-capable AI recommendation caching for vessel-side use when internet connectivity is unavailable during Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean transits. Before a vessel departs from a connected port, the system must pre-compute and cache speed optimization profiles for all planned voyage legs, trim recommendations for the expected loading condition, fuel consumption benchmarks for anomaly detection thresholds, and contingency bunkering recommendations for alternative ports along the route. Cached recommendations must be stored in an encrypted local database on the vessel's onboard system with a validity period configurable per recommendation type (default 7 days for speed profiles, 14 days for contingency plans). Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize actual performance data collected during the offline period and flag any recommendations that were overridden by the vessel's officers with the reason for override for model improvement analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-001-R009	FEAT-014-1-001	MOD-014	Canal and Strait Transit Cost Management	The system must maintain a database of canal and strait transit cost schedules including Suez Canal (SCNT-based tolls calculated on vessel tonnage certificates, container count surcharges, and pilotage fees), potential Strait of Hormuz war risk insurance premiums, and any applicable waterway fees. Transit cost calculations must auto-apply based on the voyage route waypoints, pulling current tariff rates and computing total transit cost per passage. The system must track tariff revision history, support advance estimation using provisional rates, and reconcile estimated transit costs against actual invoices received from canal authorities. Special discount programs such as Suez Canal loyalty rebates must be configurable and automatically applied when eligibility criteria are met.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R002	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Port Tariff Database Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive and versioned port tariff database covering all operational ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, King Abdulaziz Port (Dammam), Jeddah Islamic Port, and JNPT Nhava Sheva. Each tariff entry must capture the charge category (pilotage, towage, berth hire, mooring, anchorage, light dues, port dues, garbage removal, fresh water), calculation basis (per GRT, per NRT, per meter LOA, per hour, fixed fee), applicable rates, currency, effective date, and expiry date. The system must support bulk tariff updates via structured file import, track all rate changes with audit history, and alert operations teams 30 days before known tariff revision effective dates. Tariff entries must accommodate both published government tariffs and negotiated contract rates with specific shipping lines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-002-R011	FEAT-014-1-002	MOD-014	Disbursement Dispute Management	The system must provide a structured dispute management workflow for contested FDA or SDA line items. When a user flags a line item as disputed, the system must record the dispute reason (overcharging, duplicate charge, unauthorized service, tariff misapplication), generate a debit note or query to the port agent, track the dispute through stages (raised, agent response received, under review, resolved, escalated), and maintain a complete communication audit trail. Disputed amounts must be excluded from settlement processing and held in a dispute suspense account. Resolution outcomes (charge accepted, charge reduced, charge reversed) must be recorded with supporting documentation. Dispute aging reports must highlight items outstanding beyond configurable thresholds (e.g., 30, 60, 90 days) for management attention and must support Arabic-language dispute correspondence generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-003-R003	FEAT-014-1-003	MOD-014	Transaction Price Determination with Variable Consideration	The system must calculate the transaction price for each freight contract incorporating all fixed and variable consideration components. Fixed components include base ocean freight, terminal handling charges (THC), documentation fees, and equipment interchange fees. Variable consideration includes potential demurrage and detention charges (recognized when billable events occur), volume rebates under service contracts (estimated using the most likely amount method based on projected annual volumes), and early payment discounts. The system must apply a constraint on variable consideration, only including amounts to the extent that it is highly probable a significant reversal will not occur. Each pricing component must be captured in its billing currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with conversion to the entity's functional currency at the transaction date rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R012	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Agent Agreement and Contract Repository	The system must maintain a digital repository of all agent agreements and commission contracts, storing executed documents with metadata including agent name, agreement effective date, expiry date, commission rates and structures, payment terms, territory scope, exclusivity provisions, and termination clauses. The repository must support document versioning for agreement amendments and renewals. Automatic alerts must be generated 90, 60, and 30 days before agreement expiry to prompt renewal negotiations. Commission calculation parameters must be extractable from stored agreements using AI-powered document parsing, with suggested configuration updates when new or amended agreements are uploaded. The repository must enforce access controls ensuring that commercial terms are visible only to authorized users, with separate read and write permissions. All documents must be searchable by agent name, port, country, date range, and commission rate range, supporting both English and Arabic document content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R013	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Reconciliation and Audit Trail	The system must provide comprehensive reconciliation capabilities for commission accounts, enabling period-end reconciliation of commission expense per the P&L against commission payable per the balance sheet, commission paid per the cash book, and commission calculated per agent statements. The reconciliation must identify timing differences (commissions calculated but not yet approved, approved but not yet paid) and present a clear bridge between the various commission balances. An unalterable audit trail must record every commission calculation, adjustment, approval, and payment event with user identification, timestamp (both Gregorian and Hijri), IP address, and before-and-after values. The audit trail must be exportable for external auditor review in standardized formats. Reconciliation reports must support multi-entity consolidation with intercompany commission elimination entries clearly identified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R014	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Analytics and Agent Performance Dashboard	The system must provide an AI-powered analytics dashboard showing commission metrics across all operating entities. Key metrics must include total commission expense as a percentage of freight revenue (segmented by trade lane, port, and agent), commission cost per TEU, agent revenue generation efficiency (freight revenue generated per commission dollar paid), year-over-year commission rate trends, and agent ranking by net revenue contribution after commission deduction. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary metrics to individual transaction details. Visualizations must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with proper chart axis mirroring for Arabic users. AI-generated insights must highlight agents delivering above-average returns, identify trade lanes with disproportionately high commission costs, and recommend commission structure optimizations to improve overall profitability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R015	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Freight Forwarder Commission Differentiation	The system must distinguish between commissions payable to different categories of intermediaries: liner agents (who represent the shipping line at a port), freight forwarders or NVOCCs (who consolidate cargo and issue their own B/Ls), customs brokers (who may earn referral fees), and slot charter partners (who earn slot utilization commissions). Each intermediary category must have distinct commission calculation rules, GL account mapping, and reporting classifications. For NVOCCs, the system must handle net rate arrangements where the commission is implicit in the difference between the NVOCC's selling rate and the shipping line's net rate, requiring the system to compute the effective commission percentage for reporting purposes. The system must also track co-loader arrangements common in the India trade where multiple freight forwarders share container space, allocating commissions proportionally based on cargo volume contribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R016	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Commission Payment in Offline Mode	The system must support offline review and approval of commission statements for users in areas with intermittent connectivity, such as remote port offices or during travel. The offline module must cache pending commission statements, agent agreement summaries, and approval queues locally on the device. Approvers must be able to review commission calculations, view supporting transaction details, add approval notes in English or Arabic, and approve or reject statements while offline. All offline actions must be timestamped with the local device clock and queued for synchronization upon reconnection. Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where the same statement is modified by different users online and offline, applying a last-write-wins policy with full conflict audit log. The offline interface must support Arabic RTL layout and render all currency amounts with proper formatting for QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-1-004-R017	FEAT-014-1-004	MOD-014	Regulatory Compliance for Commission Payments	The system must ensure all commission payments comply with local regulatory requirements across all operating jurisdictions. For Qatar, commission payments to foreign agents must comply with Qatar Central Bank foreign remittance regulations and any applicable Qatar Financial Centre Authority guidelines. For UAE, compliance with Central Bank of UAE regulations on commercial agency agreements and Federal Law No. 18 of 1981 on commercial agencies must be verified. For KSA, the system must validate compliance with Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority regulations and commercial agency law requirements. For India, compliance with FEMA (Foreign Exchange Management Act) regulations for outward remittances, RBI liberalized remittance scheme limits, and Companies Act provisions on related-party commission payments must be enforced. The system must maintain a regulatory checklist per jurisdiction that is updated when regulations change, blocking non-compliant payments and alerting the compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-002-R017	FEAT-026-2-002	MOD-026	Negotiable MBL Title Transfer Registry	The system must maintain a secure title transfer registry for negotiable Multimodal Bills of Lading. Each endorsement and transfer of title must be recorded with the endorser's identity, endorsee's identity, endorsement type (blank, special, or restrictive), date, and reference to any associated Letter of Credit or bank guarantee. The registry must support the full chain of endorsements from original issuance to final consignee presentation. The system must prevent cargo release to any party not established in the endorsement chain. For electronic MBLs, title transfer must be executed through cryptographic signing with the transferor's PKI certificate. The registry must be immutable with all entries audit-logged and accessible to authorized parties including banks, customs authorities, and legal counsel upon authenticated request.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R005	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Port disbursement account integration and reconciliation	The system must import port disbursement accounts (PDAs) from agents at each port of call and reconcile them against the proforma disbursement account (estimated costs) provided before port arrival. The PDA must capture all standard port cost categories: port dues, pilotage, towage, berth hire, mooring, launches, fresh water, garbage disposal, and agency fees. For Middle East ports (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa), the system must handle both English and Arabic-language invoices from port authorities such as Mwani Qatar, Abu Dhabi Ports, and DP World. The reconciliation must highlight variances exceeding a configurable threshold and flag disputed items for review before final settlement and posting to the voyage P&L.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R006	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Freight revenue recognition per voyage	The system must recognize freight revenue on each voyage according to the configured revenue recognition policy: percentage of completion based on voyage progress, or completed voyage method. For percentage of completion, revenue must be recognized proportionally based on the vessel's position between load port and discharge port at each reporting date. The system must handle split bills of lading where cargo is loaded at one port (e.g., JNPT) and discharged at multiple ports (e.g., Jebel Ali and Hamad Port) by allocating the freight proportionally. Revenue must be recorded in the billing currency per the contract (commonly USD for international trades) and translated to the entity's functional currency using the exchange rate at recognition date, in compliance with IFRS 15 and local GAAP requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R007	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Demurrage and detention revenue tracking per voyage	The system must track and allocate demurrage and detention revenue to the specific voyage that generated it. Demurrage charges (at port) and detention charges (outside port) must be calculated based on free-time allowances specified in the tariff or customer contract, with rates that may vary by port, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), and customer tier. The system must handle the complexity where demurrage accrues at the discharge port after the voyage is operationally complete, correctly attributing this revenue back to the originating voyage. Multi-currency billing must be supported with demurrage invoiced in the local currency of the discharge port (QAR at Hamad, AED at Jebel Ali, INR at JNPT) and consolidated into the voyage P&L in the entity base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R008	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L approval and lock workflow	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow for voyage P&L finalization. After all revenue and costs are posted and reconciled, the voyage P&L must go through a review process: initial preparation by the operations team, review by the finance controller, and final approval by the regional finance head. Each approval step must capture the approver's identity, timestamp, and any comments in both English and Arabic. Once approved, the voyage P&L must be locked against further modifications unless formally reopened through an authorized adjustment workflow. The approval workflow must support delegation rules for when approvers are unavailable and must enforce segregation of duties such that the preparer cannot also be the final approver.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R009	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L comparison across service rotations	The system must provide a comparative analysis view enabling users to compare P&L performance across multiple voyages within the same service rotation and across different service rotations. The comparison must normalize data to a per-TEU basis to enable meaningful comparison between voyages of different sizes. Key performance indicators must include revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, profit per TEU, bunker cost per nautical mile, and port cost per call. The system must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri calendar), trade lane (e.g., Arabian Gulf to Indian Subcontinent), and operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Trend analysis with graphical visualization must show performance trajectory over a user-defined number of consecutive voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R010	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	AI-driven voyage profitability prediction	The system must employ machine learning models to predict voyage profitability before voyage commencement based on historical voyage data, current bunker prices, contracted freight rates, and expected port costs. The prediction model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical voyage P&L data for the specific trade lane and must account for seasonal patterns such as increased cargo volumes during Ramadan preparation periods and Indian festival seasons. The AI prediction must generate a confidence interval and present the expected P&L range (pessimistic, most likely, optimistic scenarios). Predictions must be compared against actual results post-voyage to continuously improve model accuracy, with the system targeting at least 85% accuracy within a 5% margin of the actual result.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R011	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage cost accrual and provision management	The system must support cost accruals and provisions for voyage expenses where final invoices have not yet been received at the period-end closing date. For each open voyage, the system must automatically generate accrual entries based on estimated costs from the proforma disbursement account and known tariff rates. When actual invoices are received, the accruals must be reversed and replaced with actual costs, with any difference posted as an accrual variance. The accrual process must comply with the entity's accounting period calendar (Gregorian for all entities, with additional Hijri period tracking for KSA). The system must produce an accrual aging report showing outstanding provisions by voyage, cost category, and age, flagging accruals older than 90 days for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R012	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Slot cost and slot revenue analysis per voyage	The system must calculate the slot cost for each voyage by dividing the total voyage cost by the vessel's nominal TEU capacity, and the slot revenue by dividing total freight revenue by the number of laden TEU carried. The analysis must differentiate between owned-slot costs and chartered-slot costs for vessels under time charter or slot charter agreements. For consortium or vessel-sharing arrangements common on Gulf-India trade routes, the system must track the operator's slot allocation separately from partner slot allocations and compute profitability only for the operator's own slots. The slot analysis must break down into dry container slots and reefer container slots separately given the different revenue and cost profiles of refrigerated cargo commonly carried between India and the Gulf region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R013	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L multi-currency consolidation	The system must consolidate voyage P&L data across multiple currencies into a single reporting currency selected by the user. Voyages operating across Qatar (QAR), UAE (AED), KSA (SAR), and India (INR) will incur costs and generate revenue in each local currency plus USD for international freight. The consolidation must use configurable exchange rate types: transaction date rate for individual items, average rate for the voyage period, or closing rate at voyage completion date. Currency translation differences arising from using different rate types must be captured in a separate translation adjustment line item. The system must comply with IAS 21 requirements for foreign currency translation and must support parallel reporting in both the entity functional currency and a group reporting currency (typically USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R001	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Rate Card Structure Definition	The system must support definition of inland haulage rate cards with a hierarchical structure: rate card header (carrier, effective date, expiry date, currency, entity), rate groups (by transport mode: road or rail), and individual rate lines. Each rate line must specify origin location (port, ICD, or customer premises zone), destination location, container type/size (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR), weight slab (e.g., up to 20 tonnes, 20-24 tonnes, 24-28 tonnes, above 28 tonnes for overweight), and the base rate amount. Rate cards must be entity-specific with currency defaulting to the entity's base currency: QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India. Version control must track all rate card changes with effective date ranges preventing overlapping active versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R008	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Rate Comparison and Optimization Analysis	The system must provide a rate comparison tool that evaluates available inland haulage options for a given origin-destination-container combination across multiple dimensions: all-in cost (comparing road-only vs. rail-road multimodal options), transit time, carbon footprint estimate (CO2 per TEU-km by mode), and hauler reliability score based on historical on-time performance. The comparison must present results in a sortable table with the recommended option highlighted based on configurable weightings per entity (e.g., Qatar may weight cost at 40% and transit time at 40%, while India may weight cost at 60%). The comparison must account for all surcharges and accessorial charges in the total cost. Multi-currency normalization must convert all rates to a common comparison currency (default USD) using current exchange rates. The tool must support bulk comparison for multiple containers in a single booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R011	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Inland Haulage Charge Pre-Calculation for Quotations	The system must provide a quotation module that pre-calculates total inland haulage charges for sales teams preparing customer proposals. The quotation must accept inputs for origin, destination, container type/quantity, commodity, estimated weight, required delivery date, and INCOTERM. Based on these inputs, the system must automatically select the applicable rate card, apply all relevant surcharges, factor in seasonal adjustments, and present a total haulage cost breakdown. Quotations must be generated in the customer's preferred currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) with exchange rate locked at quotation date. Quotations must have a configurable validity period (default 15 days) and be convertible to confirmed bookings with rate lock guarantees. The system must track quotation-to-booking conversion rates per sales representative and trade lane for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R013	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Tax and Duty Calculation on Haulage Charges	The system must correctly apply taxes on inland haulage charges per the jurisdiction's tax regime. For UAE, 5% VAT must be applied per Federal Tax Authority rules with tax invoices conforming to FTA format requirements. For KSA, 15% VAT per ZATCA regulations with e-invoicing in FATOORAH format. For Qatar, no VAT currently but the system must be configurable to activate when Qatar implements VAT. For India, GST must be applied at the correct rate based on service classification: 12% for multimodal transport operator services, 18% for standalone road transport by GTA, 5% without ITC option per transporter preference. The system must handle reverse charge mechanism (RCM) for India GTA services where applicable. Tax calculations must support inter-entity transactions with proper place-of-supply rules, and generate tax-compliant invoices with SAC codes (India) or Tax Category codes (GCC).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R015	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Rate Import and Export Functionality	The system must support bulk rate import from Excel/CSV files following a predefined template format. The import process must validate all mandatory fields, check for duplicate rate lines, verify location codes against the location master, validate container type codes against ISO standards, and confirm currency codes against the system's supported currency list. Validation errors must be reported in a detailed error log specifying the row number, field name, error description, and suggested correction. Successfully validated rates must be staged for review before final import. The system must also support rate export to Excel with full formatting including Arabic text in right-to-left columns, merged header rows, and conditional formatting highlighting rates expiring within 30 days. Export must support filtering by entity, transport mode, origin, destination, and validity date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R001	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Multi-Objective Route Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI-powered route optimization engine that evaluates all feasible multimodal route combinations between a given origin and destination. The engine must optimize across multiple objectives simultaneously: total transportation cost (including all haulage charges, terminal handling, and surcharges), end-to-end transit time (including dwelling at transshipment points), carbon emissions (calculated per transport mode using IMO/GLEC framework emission factors), and reliability score (based on historical on-time performance of each segment). The optimization must use Pareto frontier analysis to present a set of non-dominated solutions rather than a single optimum, allowing the user to select the preferred trade-off. Each route option must display a detailed breakdown of cost in the entity's base currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and transit time by segment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R003	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Predictive Transit Time Modeling	The system must use machine learning models trained on historical shipment data to predict transit times for each segment of a multimodal route with confidence intervals. Predictive factors must include seasonal patterns (monsoon impact on Indian west coast ports June-September, fog disruptions at North Indian ICDs December-January, Ramadan operational slowdowns across GCC), day-of-week effects, port congestion trends, customs clearance processing times by commodity and port, and historical carrier schedule reliability. The model must provide P50 (median), P75, and P95 transit time estimates for each route option. Model accuracy must be continuously validated against actual transit times with automatic model retraining triggered when prediction error exceeds a configurable threshold (default 15% MAPE). The system must display model confidence level alongside predictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R014	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L Arabic RTL reporting and export	The system must render the complete voyage P&L report in Arabic with full right-to-left layout support, including proper alignment of numerical columns, Arabic labels for all cost and revenue categories, and Hijri date display alongside Gregorian dates. The Arabic P&L report must be exportable to PDF with correct RTL rendering, to Excel with RTL column ordering, and to CSV with UTF-8 encoding preserving Arabic characters. All financial terminology must use standardized Arabic accounting vocabulary as used in GCC maritime industry practice. The system must support bilingual report generation showing Arabic and English side by side for use in cross-border communications between Gulf and Indian operations teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R015	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L offline access and synchronization	The system must allow authorized users to access voyage P&L reports in offline mode when network connectivity is unavailable, which is common during vessel operations at anchorage or at ports with limited connectivity. The offline mode must cache the most recent version of open voyage P&L data including all posted revenue and cost items. Users must be able to enter cost estimates and notes in offline mode, which are queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The synchronization process must handle conflicts where the same voyage P&L was updated by another user during the offline period by presenting a conflict resolution interface. Offline-entered data must be clearly marked as pending synchronization and must not affect the official P&L figures until the synchronization and validation are complete.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-001-R016	FEAT-014-2-001	MOD-014	Voyage P&L audit trail and adjustment log	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for every modification to a voyage P&L record. Each entry in the audit trail must capture the previous value, new value, timestamp, user identity, IP address, and reason for change. Post-approval adjustments must follow a formal adjustment workflow requiring documented justification and separate authorization from the original approver. The audit trail must be searchable by voyage number, date range, user, and cost category. For regulatory compliance with Qatar Financial Centre Authority (QFCA), Dubai Financial Services Authority (DFSA), and Indian Companies Act requirements, the audit trail must be retained for a minimum of seven years and must be exportable in a format suitable for external audit review. The system must flag unusual adjustment patterns using AI anomaly detection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R001	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Per-container cost ledger creation and maintenance	The system must maintain an individual cost ledger for each container identified by its unique BIC/ISO container number (e.g., MSCU1234567). The ledger must record all costs incurred throughout the container's lifecycle: leasing per-diem charges from lessors like Triton, Beacon, or Textainer, positioning and repositioning costs between depots and ports, maintenance and repair (M&R) costs, cleaning and washing charges, and storage fees at depots or container yards. Each cost entry must reference the geographic location using UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INNSA for JNPT), the date incurred, the source invoice or estimate, and the currency of the original charge. The ledger must support all standard container types: 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, and 40FR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R002	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container leasing per-diem cost tracking	The system must calculate and accrue daily leasing costs (per-diem) for each leased container based on the master lease agreement terms. The per-diem rate must be sourced from the lease contract, which may vary by container type, age, and lessor. The system must distinguish between containers on long-term lease, spot lease, and one-way lease arrangements. Per-diem accrual must begin from the on-hire date and end at the off-hire date as confirmed by the interchange report. For containers repositioned empty between Gulf ports and Indian ports, the per-diem continues to accrue during transit and must be allocated to the repositioning cost center. The system must handle per-diem rate changes during the lease period and must support billing reconciliation against lessor invoices typically received monthly in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R003	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container maintenance and repair cost tracking	The system must record all maintenance and repair (M&R) costs for each container, capturing the repair estimate reference, approved repair amount, repair depot location, and whether the cost is borne by the operator or recovered from the last user under the IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) interchange standards. For reefer containers (20RF, 40RF), pre-trip inspection (PTI) costs must be tracked separately and allocated to the voyage on which the reefer is deployed. The system must integrate with depot management systems at key locations such as DP World depots in Jebel Ali, Hamad Port container depot, and JNPT CFS facilities to automatically import M&R estimates and completion records. Each M&R event must be categorized (structural, cosmetic, reefer machinery, cleaning) for cost analysis and trend reporting across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R004	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container repositioning cost calculation	The system must calculate the total cost of repositioning empty containers between locations, including inland transport from depot to port, port handling charges, vessel slot cost for empty carriage, and port handling at the destination. When empty containers are repositioned from surplus locations (e.g., JNPT where import-heavy trade creates equipment surplus) to deficit locations (e.g., Jebel Ali where export demand exists), the system must compute the full door-to-door repositioning cost. The slot cost for empty container carriage must be derived from the voyage cost model or a standard internal transfer price. Repositioning decisions may involve multiple transport modes (truck, rail, feeder vessel), and the system must aggregate costs across all legs. The repositioning cost must be recorded against the container and also allocated to the receiving location's cost pool for subsequent recovery through laden freight revenue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R005	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost allocation to bookings and BLs	The system must allocate container-level costs to specific bookings and bills of lading to enable per-shipment profitability analysis. The allocation must include the proportional leasing per-diem for the period the container was assigned to the booking (from gate-in at origin to gate-out at destination), any positioning costs to bring the container to the loading point, and any cleaning or repair costs incurred after the previous use attributable to the new booking. For FCL (Full Container Load) shipments, the full container cost is allocated to the single booking. For LCL (Less than Container Load) consolidations, container costs must be allocated proportionally based on the volume (CBM) or weight (KGS) occupied by each consignment within the container. The allocation engine must be configurable to use different allocation bases per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R006	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container fleet utilization cost analysis	The system must compute container fleet utilization metrics and their cost implications, including the ratio of laden days to total available days, idle days at depots, and days under repair. For each container type and size, the system must calculate the daily cost of idle capacity based on the average per-diem rate plus storage charges. The utilization analysis must segment the fleet by ownership (owned, long-term lease, spot lease) and by geographic region (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The system must generate AI-powered recommendations for fleet optimization, such as identifying chronic surplus locations where off-hiring leased containers would reduce costs, or deficit locations where additional leases should be negotiated. The analysis must be available as a dashboard with drill-down from fleet-wide metrics to individual container-level details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R007	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container damage cost recovery tracking	The system must track the recovery of container damage costs from responsible parties, including shippers, consignees, truckers, and terminal operators. When container damage is identified at interchange (gate-in or gate-out), the system must create a damage recovery case linked to the container cost ledger entry, the responsible party identified from the equipment interchange receipt (EIR), and the repair cost estimate. The recovery workflow must track the status from initial claim raised, through dispute if applicable, to final settlement or write-off. For damages occurring at ports governed by different legal jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, India), the system must apply the appropriate liability framework. Recovery amounts received must be posted as offsets against the container's M&R cost in the period received, with aging reports showing outstanding recovery claims by age bucket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 90+ days) and by responsible party.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R008	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container storage and detention cost accrual	The system must automatically calculate and accrue storage charges for containers held at port container yards or off-dock depots beyond the free storage period. Storage rates must be configurable per port authority (Mwani Qatar at Hamad Port, Abu Dhabi Ports at Khalifa Port, DP World at Jebel Ali, JNPT port authority) and may vary by container type and whether the container is laden or empty. The system must distinguish between port storage charges (payable to the port authority) and operator-owned depot storage costs (internal cost allocation). For import containers awaiting customs clearance, storage cost accrual must factor in the extended dwell times common during GCC customs processes. The accrued storage costs must be reconciled against actual invoices from port authorities and depot operators, with variances flagged for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R009	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Reefer container power and monitoring cost tracking	The system must track the electrical power costs for reefer containers (20RF, 40RF) at each point where power is supplied: port container yard, depot, vessel, and inland during transport. Power costs at ports must be calculated based on the plug-in duration and the port authority's published tariff per reefer per day (which varies significantly between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT). Remote monitoring costs for reefer containers equipped with telematics devices must be allocated per container per day. The system must also track costs related to reefer breakdowns including emergency technician callouts, temporary alternative power supply, and cargo claims arising from temperature deviations. All reefer-specific costs must be separately identifiable in the container cost ledger and allocated to the specific booking carrying the refrigerated cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R010	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost multi-currency handling	The system must handle container costs incurred in multiple currencies across the four operating countries. Leasing costs are typically denominated in USD, port charges in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and M&R costs may be in either local currency or USD depending on the depot operator. Each cost entry must record the original currency amount, the exchange rate applied, and the converted amount in the entity's functional currency. For containers that traverse multiple entities during a single trip (e.g., loaded in India, discharged in Qatar), the system must determine which entity bears each cost component based on configurable inter-company cost-sharing rules. Month-end revaluation of outstanding foreign currency container cost accruals must be performed automatically using closing exchange rates from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, or RBI as applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R011	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost budget versus actual comparison	The system must compare actual container costs against budgeted costs at multiple levels: per container type, per trade lane, per port, and per entity. Budgets must be definable for each cost category (leasing, M&R, repositioning, storage, handling) on a monthly or quarterly basis aligned with the entity's fiscal calendar. Variance analysis must show both absolute and percentage variances with drill-down capability from summary level to individual container transactions. The system must generate automated variance commentary using AI, explaining likely causes of significant variances (e.g., increased M&R costs due to aging fleet, higher repositioning costs due to trade imbalance). Budget revisions must follow an approval workflow and the system must maintain the original budget alongside revised budgets for full transparency in reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R012	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container lifecycle total cost of ownership report	The system must generate a total cost of ownership (TCO) report for each container over its entire lifecycle from acquisition or on-hire to disposal or off-hire. The TCO must include the initial acquisition cost or cumulative leasing costs, all M&R expenditures, repositioning costs, insurance premiums allocated per container, and any disposal or scrap value received. For owned containers, the system must calculate depreciation using the entity's depreciation policy (straight-line over 12-15 years for dry containers, 10-12 years for reefers) and include this in the TCO. The TCO report must enable comparison between owned and leased containers to support strategic fleet procurement decisions. The report must factor in the container's utilization rate to compute a per-laden-day TCO metric that normalizes for usage differences between high-utilization and low-utilization units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R003	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Automated Cost Allocation Rules Engine	The system must provide a configurable rules engine for automatic allocation of shared costs across multiple cost centres using definable allocation bases including TEU throughput, vessel calls, revenue share, headcount, square meterage, or custom-weighted formulas. Allocation rules must support both single-step and multi-step (cascading) allocation methods where overhead cost centres are distributed to operational centres in a defined sequence. Each allocation run must generate a detailed journal with full traceability from source to target cost centres, supporting reversal if errors are detected. The engine must handle cross-entity allocations for shared services such as a centralized IT function in Dubai serving all four country operations, with proper intercompany elimination entries generated automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R013	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Inter-company container cost settlement	The system must manage the inter-company settlement of container costs when containers are used across multiple entities within the group. When a container owned or leased by the Qatar entity is used for a shipment booked by the UAE entity, the system must generate inter-company charges based on a predefined transfer pricing policy. The transfer price may be based on cost-plus margin, market rate benchmarking, or a flat internal rate per container type per day. Inter-company invoices must be generated automatically at configurable intervals (weekly, monthly) and must include sufficient detail for both entities to verify and reconcile. The settlement must comply with transfer pricing regulations applicable in Qatar (QFC Tax Regulations), UAE (Corporate Tax Law), KSA (Transfer Pricing Bylaws), and India (Section 92 of the Income Tax Act) and must maintain documentation to support arm's-length pricing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R014	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost Arabic interface and localized reporting	The system must provide full Arabic language support for all container cost tracking interfaces including the container cost ledger, cost entry forms, search and filter panels, and reporting dashboards. All financial amounts must be displayed with the appropriate currency symbol in Arabic locale (e.g., ر.ق for QAR, د.إ for AED, ر.س for SAR, ₹ for INR) with correct number formatting using Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) as an optional display setting. Date fields must support dual display of Gregorian and Hijri calendars throughout the container cost module. Report headers, column labels, and chart legends must be fully translatable and rendered correctly in RTL layout. The system must support exporting Arabic container cost reports to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats while preserving RTL formatting and Arabic character integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R015	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost data offline capture and sync	The system must enable offline capture of container cost data at locations with intermittent connectivity, including remote depots, vessel-side operations, and inland container depots in areas with limited network coverage common in parts of India and at anchorage points in the Gulf. Offline-capable transactions must include container damage recording with photo capture, M&R estimate entry, equipment interchange receipt (EIR) completion with gate-in and gate-out condition records, and basic cost approval actions. The offline data must be stored securely on the local device with encryption at rest, and must synchronize automatically when connectivity is restored using a conflict resolution strategy that prioritizes server-side data for financial amounts while preserving offline-entered descriptive data. The synchronization status must be visible to users with clear indicators for pending, syncing, and confirmed states.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R016	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	AI-powered container cost anomaly detection	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to identify unusual container cost patterns that may indicate errors, fraud, or operational inefficiencies. The detection model must analyze M&R costs per container against fleet averages by container type and age, flagging containers with costs exceeding two standard deviations from the mean. Storage costs must be monitored against expected dwell times by port, with alerts for containers exceeding typical dwell times at each specific location (which may differ significantly between Hamad Port and JNPT due to different customs clearance speeds). Leasing cost anomalies such as duplicate per-diem charges or charges for off-hired containers must be detected by cross-referencing with the on-hire and off-hire register. Detected anomalies must generate prioritized alerts in both English and Arabic with recommended investigation actions and estimated financial impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-002-R017	FEAT-014-2-002	MOD-014	Container cost integration with general ledger	The system must post container cost transactions to the general ledger in real-time or in configurable batch intervals. Each cost category must map to a predefined chart of accounts structure that supports the multi-entity accounting requirements across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The GL posting must include all dimensions required for financial reporting: entity code, cost center, container type, voyage number (where applicable), and trade lane. Accrual entries and reversal entries must be paired and traceable. The integration must support the different fiscal year calendars across entities (calendar year for Qatar and UAE, potentially different for KSA and India) and must handle period-end cut-off correctly such that costs are posted to the correct accounting period regardless of the processing date. Reconciliation reports must verify that the container cost subledger balance matches the GL control account balance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R001	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead cost pool definition and configuration	The system must allow finance administrators to define and configure overhead cost pools that group related indirect expenses for allocation purposes. Standard cost pools must include: corporate office costs (rent, utilities, office supplies), IT infrastructure and systems costs, human resources and administration, shared vessel management and technical supervision, marketing and business development, and regulatory compliance costs. Each cost pool must be assignable to a specific entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) or to the group level for corporate headquarters costs. The cost pool definition must include a description, the GL accounts that feed into the pool, the allocation basis to be used, and the allocation frequency (monthly, quarterly, annually). Cost pools must support hierarchical nesting where sub-pools roll up into parent pools for reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R002	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Multiple allocation basis methods	The system must support multiple allocation basis methods for distributing overhead costs from cost pools to cost objects. Supported methods must include: revenue-based allocation (proportional to revenue generated by each cost object), TEU-based allocation (proportional to container volumes handled), headcount-based allocation (proportional to staff assigned to each business unit), direct cost-based allocation (proportional to direct costs incurred), square-footage-based allocation (for facility costs), and user-defined formula-based allocation using configurable mathematical expressions. Each allocation basis must source its data automatically from the relevant module (revenue from billing, TEU from operations, headcount from HR). The system must allow different allocation bases for different cost pools and must support changing allocation bases between periods with full audit trail of the basis used in each period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-002-R001	FEAT-007-2-002	MOD-007	Market Index Integration	System integrates with major container freight rate indices to provide real-time market benchmarking data.	must_have			["Integration with Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI) and Drewry World Container Index is operational", "Freightos Baltic Index (FBX) data is ingested daily for relevant trade lanes", "Index data is stored with historical depth of minimum 5 years for trend analysis", "Rate indices are converted to entity-local currencies (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) using daily exchange rates", "Data feed failures trigger alerts and system falls back to last known good data with staleness indicator"]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R003	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Step-down allocation method implementation	The system must implement the step-down (sequential) allocation method where service departments' costs are allocated to other service departments and operating departments in a defined sequence. For example, IT department costs may be allocated first to all other departments including HR, then HR costs (including the IT allocation received) are allocated to operating departments. The sequence of allocation must be configurable and typically ordered from the department that provides the most service to other service departments to the one that provides the least. The system must prevent circular allocations and must validate the allocation sequence before execution. Each step in the allocation must be separately recorded and auditable, showing the source cost pool, the allocation basis used, the allocation percentages, and the resulting amounts distributed to each receiving cost object.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R004	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Activity-based costing support	The system must support activity-based costing (ABC) methodology for more accurate overhead allocation. The ABC implementation must allow definition of activities (e.g., booking processing, documentation handling, customs clearance coordination, container tracking), identification of cost drivers for each activity (e.g., number of bookings processed, number of BLs issued, number of customs declarations filed), and allocation of overhead costs to cost objects based on their consumption of each activity. The activity rates must be calculated by dividing the total cost of each activity by the total volume of its cost driver. For shipping-specific activities common across Gulf and India operations, the system must provide predefined activity templates that can be customized. The ABC analysis must produce a comparison view against traditional volume-based allocation to highlight where significant distortions exist.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R005	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Inter-entity shared service cost allocation	The system must allocate shared service costs between the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) based on configurable inter-company service agreements. When the UAE entity provides shared services (e.g., centralized IT support, group treasury management, regional vessel operations management) to other entities, the cost must be allocated using methods agreed in the inter-company service agreement. The allocation must generate inter-company debit and credit entries simultaneously in both entities' books. Transfer pricing documentation must be automatically generated showing the nature of the service, the cost base, the allocation methodology, and the resulting charge, in compliance with OECD transfer pricing guidelines and local regulations in each jurisdiction. The system must support markup on shared service costs where required by transfer pricing policy and must track the arm's-length benchmarking data used to justify the pricing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R006	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation to voyage cost objects	The system must allocate overhead costs to individual voyages as cost objects to enable fully loaded voyage P&L reporting. The allocation must distribute entity-level overheads to voyages based on configurable bases such as vessel days in operation, TEU carried, or revenue generated. The system must handle the timing complexity where overhead costs are incurred monthly but voyages may span multiple months, requiring allocation based on the proportion of the voyage duration falling within each month. Overhead allocated to a voyage must be separately identified in the voyage P&L under a distinct overhead section, distinguishable from direct voyage costs. The system must allow users to view voyage profitability both with and without overhead allocation to support different analytical perspectives. Year-end overhead true-up adjustments must be supported when actual overhead differs from the rates used during the year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R007	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation to trade lanes and services	The system must allocate overhead costs to trade lanes (e.g., Gulf-India, Intra-Gulf) and service routes to enable profitability analysis at the trade lane level. Trade lane overhead allocation must consider both the direct overhead attributable to the trade lane (e.g., trade lane management staff costs, trade-specific marketing expenses) and a share of general corporate overhead. The allocation to trade lanes must be consistent with the allocation to voyages, such that the sum of overhead allocated to all voyages on a trade lane equals the total overhead allocated to that trade lane. The system must produce a trade lane profitability report showing revenue, direct costs, allocated overheads, and resulting net profit by trade lane. This analysis must support strategic decisions such as whether to increase or decrease capacity on specific trade lanes between Gulf ports and the Indian subcontinent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R008	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation schedule and automation	The system must support scheduled, automated execution of overhead allocation processes at configurable intervals. The allocation schedule must allow monthly allocation runs tied to the accounting period close process, with the ability to run preliminary allocations before period close and final allocations after all source costs are posted. The automation engine must validate all prerequisites before execution: all source GL accounts must be reconciled, all allocation bases must have current data, and no open exceptions from previous periods must exist. The system must support re-running allocations for a period if source data changes, with automatic reversal of previous allocations and generation of new allocations. Allocation run logs must record execution time, records processed, total amounts allocated, and any warnings or errors encountered, with email notifications sent to configured recipients upon completion or failure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R009	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead cost allocation audit trail	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every overhead allocation transaction including the source cost pool, allocation basis data used, allocation percentages computed, receiving cost objects, and amounts allocated. The audit trail must enable reconstruction of any allocation at any point in time, showing exactly how a specific overhead amount was derived and distributed. For regulatory compliance across all four operating jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the audit trail must be retained for a minimum of seven years and must be accessible for external audit review. Each allocation run must produce a detailed allocation journal that maps source debits to destination credits with full dimensional coding. The system must support audit queries that trace from a cost object's overhead balance back to the original expense entry in the GL, providing end-to-end visibility of the allocation chain across multiple allocation steps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R010	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Dynamic overhead rate calculation	The system must calculate dynamic overhead absorption rates that update based on actual cost and activity data rather than relying solely on static predetermined rates. At each period close, the system must compute the actual overhead rate by dividing actual overhead costs by actual activity volume (TEU handled, voyages operated, revenue earned). The system must compare the actual rate to the budgeted rate and calculate the over-absorption or under-absorption variance. Under-absorbed overhead (where actual costs exceed allocated amounts) must be reportable by cost pool and entity, with AI-generated analysis suggesting whether the variance is due to volume variance (actual activity below budget) or spending variance (actual costs above budget). The dynamic rates must be available for use in pricing decisions, enabling commercial teams to understand the true overhead cost per TEU or per booking for rate negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R011	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation multi-currency processing	The system must handle overhead allocation across entities operating in different functional currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with a group reporting currency of USD. When group-level overhead costs denominated in one currency are allocated to entities with different functional currencies, the system must convert at the appropriate exchange rate: budget rate for budgeted allocations, actual monthly average rate for actual allocations, or a specific management rate as configured. Currency translation differences arising from overhead allocation must be tracked and reported separately from operational currency gains or losses. The system must ensure that the total amount allocated in the source currency exactly equals the source cost pool balance with no rounding gaps, while the destination amounts in different currencies may have minor translation adjustments that must be captured in a designated translation difference account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R012	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation dispute and adjustment workflow	The system must provide a workflow for entity-level finance teams to review, query, and dispute overhead allocations received from shared services or corporate headquarters. When an entity finance manager reviews their allocated overhead and identifies a concern (e.g., disproportionate allocation, incorrect allocation basis data, or inclusion of costs that should not be shared), they must be able to raise a formal dispute with supporting documentation. The dispute must be routed to the group finance controller for review and resolution. Approved adjustments must generate correcting journal entries in both the source and receiving entities. The system must track dispute resolution metrics including number of disputes raised per period, average resolution time, and total financial impact of adjustments. All dispute communications must support both English and Arabic text entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R013	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation what-if scenario modeling	The system must provide what-if scenario modeling capabilities allowing finance users to simulate the impact of changes to allocation methodologies, bases, or cost structures before implementing them. Users must be able to create scenarios that modify allocation bases (e.g., switching from revenue-based to TEU-based allocation for IT costs), change allocation percentages, add or remove cost pools, or adjust the allocation hierarchy. The system must calculate the scenario results and present a comparison view showing the current allocation alongside the proposed scenario, with the impact on each receiving cost object highlighted. Multiple scenarios must be savable and comparable side by side. Scenario analysis must support the annual budgeting process by allowing finance teams to test different overhead allocation approaches and their impact on entity-level and trade-lane profitability before committing to a methodology for the fiscal year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R014	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation reporting in Arabic and English	The system must generate all overhead allocation reports in both English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering for Arabic versions. Reports must include the overhead allocation summary by cost pool, detailed allocation journal by receiving cost object, inter-entity allocation reconciliation, and variance analysis reports comparing allocated versus budgeted overhead. Arabic reports must use localized financial terminology consistent with GCC accounting standards and practices. Report date headers must display both Gregorian and Hijri dates. Numerical formatting must follow the selected locale conventions with the option to use Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) or Western Arabic numerals (0123456789). All reports must be exportable to PDF (with correct RTL rendering), Excel (with RTL worksheet orientation option), and structured data formats (JSON, XML) for integration with external reporting tools. Chart visualizations in reports must render correctly in both LTR and RTL orientations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R015	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Regulatory compliance for overhead allocation	The system must ensure overhead allocation practices comply with local regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions. For Qatar, allocations must conform to Qatar Financial Centre Tax Regulations regarding related-party transactions and transfer pricing. For UAE, the system must comply with the UAE Corporate Tax Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 47 of 2022) provisions on deductible expenses and transfer pricing. For KSA, compliance with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) transfer pricing bylaws and GAZT requirements is mandatory. For India, the system must address Section 92 of the Income Tax Act regarding transfer pricing documentation and compliance with Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS). The system must generate jurisdiction-specific transfer pricing documentation packages that include the allocation methodology description, benchmarking analysis, and contemporaneous documentation as required by each jurisdiction's tax authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R016	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	AI-optimized overhead allocation recommendations	The system must leverage AI to analyze historical allocation data and recommend optimal allocation methodologies that most accurately reflect the causal relationship between overhead costs and cost objects. The AI engine must analyze correlation between different allocation bases and the actual consumption of shared resources, identifying cases where the current allocation basis may not be the best proxy for resource consumption. For example, the system should detect if TEU volume is a better predictor of IT support costs than revenue, and recommend switching the allocation basis accordingly. Recommendations must include the expected impact on each entity's profitability and a confidence score for each suggestion. The AI must also identify overhead cost pools that have grown disproportionately relative to activity volumes and flag them for management review. All recommendations must be presented with supporting data visualizations and natural language explanations in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-003-R017	FEAT-014-2-003	MOD-014	Overhead allocation offline capability	The system must support review and approval of overhead allocation results in offline mode for executives and finance managers who may be traveling or at locations with limited connectivity within the Gulf and Indian subcontinent regions. The offline capability must cache the current period's allocation summary, detailed allocation journals for the user's entity, and pending approval items. Users must be able to review allocation details, add comments or raise preliminary disputes, and approve or reject allocation results while offline. Upon reconnection, all offline actions must synchronize to the server with conflict detection and resolution for cases where the same allocation was modified by another user during the offline period. The offline mode must enforce the same authorization and approval rules as the online system, preventing users from approving allocations beyond their authorized limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R001	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Budget structure definition and hierarchical setup	The system must allow finance administrators to define a comprehensive budget structure that mirrors the organization's cost and revenue classification hierarchy. The budget structure must support multiple dimensions: entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), department, cost center, GL account, trade lane, vessel, and container type. Budget line items must be definable at any level of the hierarchy with automatic roll-up to parent levels. The system must support both top-down budgeting (group targets disaggregated to entities) and bottom-up budgeting (entity budgets aggregated to group level) with a reconciliation mechanism to align the two approaches. The budget period structure must be configurable: monthly, quarterly, or annual buckets, with the ability to define seasonal distribution patterns for annual budgets (e.g., higher revenue allocation to Q4 for peak shipping season in the Gulf).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R002	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Multi-version budget management	The system must support multiple budget versions for each fiscal year including the original approved budget, mid-year revised budget, quarterly rolling forecasts, and stretch targets. Each budget version must be independently maintained with its own approval workflow and effective date. The system must allow variance analysis against any budget version selected by the user, enabling comparison of actuals against both the original budget and the latest forecast. Version control must prevent unauthorized modifications to approved budget versions while allowing authorized users to create new revision versions. The system must maintain a complete version history showing changes between consecutive versions at the line-item level. For the multi-entity environment, each entity may have different revision timelines, and the group consolidation must be able to combine entity budgets at different version levels with clear identification of which version is used for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R003	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Revenue variance analysis by component	The system must decompose revenue variances into meaningful components for container shipping operations. Freight revenue variance must be broken down into volume variance (difference between budgeted and actual TEU) and rate variance (difference between budgeted and actual freight rate per TEU). The analysis must further segment by trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Mundra), container type (dry, reefer, special equipment), and customer segment. Ancillary revenue variances for demurrage, detention, and terminal handling must be separately analyzed with variance attributed to volume changes, rate changes, and collection efficiency. The revenue variance report must include AI-generated natural language commentary explaining the key drivers of variance, referencing specific market conditions such as rate movements on Gulf-India trades or seasonal volume fluctuations during Ramadan and Diwali periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R004	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Cost variance analysis with flexible decomposition	The system must provide cost variance analysis that decomposes variances into price variance and efficiency variance for all major cost categories. For bunker costs, the variance must separate fuel price variance (budgeted vs actual price per metric ton) from consumption variance (budgeted vs actual consumption in metric tons). For port costs, the variance must differentiate between tariff changes (price variance) and unexpected port calls or extended stays (efficiency variance). For container leasing costs, the variance must distinguish between per-diem rate changes and fleet size or utilization changes. Each cost category's variance must be drillable from entity level down to individual transaction level. The analysis must support both absolute variance (in monetary terms in the entity's functional currency) and percentage variance, with configurable thresholds for materiality that determine which variances require management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R005	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Voyage-level budget vs actual comparison	The system must enable budget vs actual comparison at the individual voyage level, comparing the voyage pro-forma estimate (budget) against the actual voyage P&L. The comparison must be structured with matching line items for each revenue and cost category: freight revenue by cargo type, bunker costs by fuel grade, port costs by port of call, canal transit fees, and agency fees. Variances must be calculated at each line-item level and at subtotal levels (total revenue, total direct costs, total overhead, net voyage result). The system must handle the challenge of voyages that span budget periods by prorating the budget to match the actual voyage dates. For ad-hoc or extra-loader voyages not in the original budget, the system must allow creation of a benchmark budget based on the average of similar historical voyages on the same trade lane, enabling meaningful variance analysis even for unbudgeted voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R006	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Monthly management reporting with variance highlights	The system must generate monthly management reports combining actual financial results with budget variance analysis in a standardized format suitable for management review. The report must include an executive summary dashboard showing key financial metrics (revenue, EBITDA, net profit) against budget with traffic-light indicators (green for favorable or within tolerance, amber for watch, red for adverse beyond threshold). Detailed sections must cover revenue analysis by trade lane and customer segment, cost analysis by category, vessel operating expense analysis, and working capital metrics. Each section must highlight the top five favorable and adverse variances with explanatory commentary. The report must be auto-generated within one business day of period close, with AI-generated draft commentary that finance teams can review and edit before distribution. The report must support generation in both English and Arabic formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R007	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Rolling forecast integration with variance analysis	The system must integrate rolling forecasts with the variance analysis framework, allowing variance calculation against both the static annual budget and the most recent rolling forecast. The rolling forecast must cover a configurable forward-looking period (default 12 months rolling) and must be updateable monthly by entity finance teams. When calculating variance against the rolling forecast, the system must use the forecast version that was effective at the start of the period being analyzed, not the latest forecast, to ensure a fair comparison. The system must track forecast accuracy over time by comparing each month's forecast to the corresponding actual result, building a forecast reliability score by entity, department, and cost category. AI-assisted forecasting must propose adjustments to the rolling forecast based on year-to-date actuals, trend analysis, and known future events such as scheduled vessel drydockings or contracted rate changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R008	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Multi-entity consolidated variance reporting	The system must produce consolidated variance analysis across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with proper currency translation. Entity-level budgets denominated in local functional currencies must be translated to the group reporting currency (USD) using the budget exchange rate for budget figures and the actual average rate for actual figures. The consolidated report must separately identify transaction currency variances (operational performance in local currency) and translation currency variances (impact of exchange rate movements). Inter-company eliminations must be applied to the consolidated view to prevent double-counting of inter-entity revenue and costs. The consolidation must handle different fiscal year-ends across entities if applicable and must support both full consolidation and proportional consolidation for joint venture arrangements. Each entity's contribution to the consolidated variance must be clearly shown to enable accountability at the entity management level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R009	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Budget vs actual variance drill-down capability	The system must provide interactive drill-down capability in variance reports allowing users to navigate from the highest summary level (group consolidated) down to the lowest transactional detail in a seamless hierarchical path. The drill-down path must support: group total to entity, entity to department or trade lane, department to cost center, cost center to GL account, GL account to individual journal entries, and journal entry to source document (invoice, disbursement account, booking). At each drill-down level, the system must display the budget amount, actual amount, variance in absolute and percentage terms, and any comments or explanations attached. The drill-down must maintain the user's navigation context, allowing them to traverse up and down the hierarchy and across to adjacent dimensions (e.g., from one trade lane to another) without losing their analysis path. Bookmarking of specific drill-down states must be supported for revisiting or sharing specific analyses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R010	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	AI-powered variance explanation and commentary	The system must use AI to automatically generate natural language explanations for significant budget variances, reducing the manual effort in management reporting. The AI engine must analyze variance data in context, considering factors such as volume changes (TEU increase or decrease versus budget), rate movements (freight rate, bunker price, exchange rate), one-off events (vessel delays, port congestion, regulatory changes), and seasonal patterns. The generated commentary must be specific and actionable, for example: 'Bunker cost overrun of USD 245,000 (12.3% above budget) driven primarily by VLSFO price increase of USD 38/MT above budgeted rate at Jebel Ali, partially offset by 2.1% lower consumption due to slow-steaming on Gulf-India rotation.' The AI must generate commentary in both English and Arabic. Finance users must be able to edit, approve, or regenerate AI commentary before it is included in official reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R011	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Variance threshold configuration and alerting	The system must allow configuration of variance thresholds at multiple levels: by entity, department, cost category, and GL account. Thresholds must be definable as both absolute amounts and percentages, with the more restrictive threshold applying. For example, a threshold of 5% or USD 50,000 (whichever is lower) means that a variance of 4% that is USD 80,000 would still trigger an alert because it exceeds the absolute threshold. Different threshold levels must be configurable for different severity levels: informational (requires noting), warning (requires explanation), and critical (requires immediate action and escalation). When a variance exceeds a threshold, the system must generate real-time alerts delivered through the application notification system, email, and optionally SMS for critical alerts. Alert recipients must be configurable based on the dimension showing the variance (e.g., entity finance head for entity-level thresholds, trade lane manager for trade-specific thresholds).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R012	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Variance analysis Hijri calendar and multi-period support	The system must support variance analysis across both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. For KSA operations where governmental and some business reporting follows the Hijri calendar, budgets must be definable in Hijri months with variance analysis aligned to Hijri period boundaries. The system must handle the complexity that Hijri months do not align with Gregorian months, requiring pro-ration of actual data when an entity reports in Hijri periods but transactions are recorded with Gregorian dates. Comparative variance analysis must support period-over-period comparisons in both calendar systems: current Hijri month vs prior Hijri month, current Hijri month vs same Hijri month prior year, and Hijri year-to-date analysis. The system must maintain parallel period mappings and must correctly handle the approximately 11-day annual drift between Hijri and Gregorian calendars that causes Hijri fiscal years to gradually shift across Gregorian seasons over a 33-year cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R013	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Budget vs actual variance export and distribution	The system must support exporting variance analysis reports in multiple formats: PDF for formal distribution, Excel for detailed analysis, PowerPoint for management presentations, and structured data formats (JSON, XML) for integration with external BI tools. Excel exports must include preserved formulas for drill-down and pivot table functionality, enabling finance users to perform additional ad-hoc analysis. PDF exports must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with configurable cover pages showing the entity logo, report title, period, and classification markings. The system must support scheduled automatic distribution of variance reports to configured recipients upon period close, with different report variants for different audience levels (executive summary for senior management, detailed analysis for finance teams, specific sections for operational managers). Distribution lists must be configurable by entity and report type with the ability to add ad-hoc recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R014	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Variance trend analysis and pattern detection	The system must provide trend analysis capabilities showing how variances evolve over consecutive periods (minimum 12 months trailing), enabling identification of persistent issues versus one-off deviations. Trend charts must display actual-vs-budget trajectories for key financial metrics with variance bands showing acceptable ranges. The AI engine must detect recurring variance patterns such as systematic under-budgeting of specific cost categories, seasonal variance patterns correlated with shipping industry cycles in the Gulf-India trade, or progressive deterioration in a cost line suggesting structural changes. Pattern detection must classify variances as: favorable trend, adverse trend, cyclical pattern, one-off event, or emerging concern. The system must generate executive alerts when adverse trends persist for three or more consecutive periods, recommending whether the budget should be revised or corrective operational action should be taken. Trend analysis must be viewable across all dimensions including entity, trade lane, vessel, and customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R015	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Budget variance accountability and sign-off workflow	The system must implement an accountability workflow where variance owners must review, explain, and sign off on significant variances in their area of responsibility before the management reporting period closes. Each cost center and revenue stream must have a designated variance owner who receives automated notifications of variances requiring explanation. The owner must provide a structured variance explanation including: root cause category (volume, price, timing, one-off, structural), detailed narrative explanation, corrective action taken or planned, and expected future period impact. The explanation must be enterable in English or Arabic and must attach to the specific variance record. A group finance controller must have a dashboard view showing the completion status of all variance explanations across all entities, with the ability to send reminders for overdue explanations. The signed-off variance package becomes part of the official management reporting record and must be auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R016	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Capital expenditure budget variance tracking	The system must separately track capital expenditure (CAPEX) budget versus actual for shipping-specific capital items including vessel acquisitions, container fleet purchases, depot infrastructure, and IT systems. CAPEX variance must track not only the total amount variance but also timing variance (budget phasing versus actual spend pattern) and scope variance (changes in project scope affecting cost). For vessel and container purchases that may be denominated in currencies different from the entity's functional currency (typically USD for vessel purchases), the CAPEX variance must include a currency impact analysis showing how exchange rate movements between budget rate and actual rate affected the local currency cost. The system must support linking CAPEX items to approval records (board resolutions, investment committee approvals) with maximum authorized amounts that serve as hard budget ceilings regardless of business unit-level budget re-allocations. Committed but not yet spent amounts must be tracked as commitments against the CAPEX budget.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R017	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Variance analysis offline access and mobile support	The system must provide offline access to variance analysis reports and dashboards for executives and finance managers who need to review financial performance while traveling between Gulf and India operations or during periods of limited connectivity. The offline capability must cache the latest period's variance summary, the previous three months' trend data, and the key management report pages for the user's entity and areas of responsibility. Users must be able to interact with drill-down capabilities in offline mode using pre-cached data up to the cost center and GL account level. Variance explanations must be enterable in offline mode in both English and Arabic and queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The mobile interface must present variance data in a responsive layout optimized for tablet devices commonly used in management meetings, with swipe navigation between report sections and pinch-to-zoom on charts and detailed tables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-2-004-R018	FEAT-014-2-004	MOD-014	Variance analysis integration with ERP general ledger	The system must integrate seamlessly with the general ledger module to source actual financial data for variance analysis in real-time or near-real-time. The integration must map budget line items to GL account combinations using a consistent chart of accounts and dimensional structure across all four entities. When the GL is updated with new postings, the variance analysis must reflect the updated actuals within a configurable refresh interval (default: within 15 minutes for dashboard views, immediate for drill-down to transaction level). The system must handle GL adjustments and reclassifications by recalculating affected variances and flagging where previously explained variances have changed materially due to adjustments. Period-end closing entries including accruals, provisions, and allocations must be included in the actual figures, with the system supporting comparison views showing actuals before and after closing entries to help finance teams understand the impact of period-end adjustments on variance positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R001	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Hierarchical Cost Centre Tree Structure	The system must support a minimum six-level hierarchical cost centre tree structure: Entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) → Region → Port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNPT) → Business Unit (vessel operations, terminal handling, freight forwarding, warehousing) → Department → Sub-department. Each node in the hierarchy must have a unique alphanumeric code up to 20 characters, Arabic and English names, activation/deactivation dates supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and a designated responsible person. The tree must allow drag-and-drop reorganization with full audit trail of structural changes, and must prevent deletion of cost centres with posted transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R002	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Multi-Currency Cost Centre Budgeting	The system must allow annual and monthly budget allocation to each cost centre in the entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic translation to the group reporting currency (USD). Budget entry must support both top-down allocation from parent cost centres and bottom-up aggregation from child centres. The system must enforce budget tolerance thresholds configurable per cost centre (warning at 80%, hard stop at 100% by default), with override approval workflows for exceeding limits. Budget revisions must maintain version history with effective dates and approver details, and support mid-year budget reforecasting for seasonal shipping volume fluctuations such as peak Hajj cargo seasons for KSA routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R004	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Vessel Voyage Cost Centre Auto-Creation	The system must automatically create temporary cost centres for each vessel voyage upon voyage booking confirmation, linked to the voyage number, vessel IMO number, and trade lane. These voyage-specific cost centres must aggregate all related costs including bunker fuel, port charges at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, canal transit fees (Suez Canal), crew costs, container repositioning expenses, and terminal handling charges. Upon voyage completion and final cost reconciliation, the system must close the voyage cost centre, transfer any residual balances to the parent route cost centre, and generate a voyage profitability report comparing actual costs against budgeted estimates per TEU. Voyage cost centres must remain queryable for a minimum of seven financial years after closure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R005	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a multi-level approval workflow for cost centre creation, modification, deactivation, and budget allocation changes. Workflow steps must be configurable per entity and cost centre level, supporting sequential and parallel approval paths. Approvers must be assignable by role (Finance Manager, CFO, Regional Controller) with delegation rules for absence periods. The workflow must support Arabic and English notification emails and in-app alerts with full RTL rendering for Arabic users. Escalation rules must automatically route pending approvals after configurable SLA periods (default 48 hours). All workflow actions must be timestamped and logged with approver identity, IP address, and comments for SOX compliance and external audit requirements applicable to listed entities in Qatar Financial Markets Authority and UAE Securities and Commodities Authority jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R006	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Real-Time Cost Centre Balance Inquiry	The system must provide a real-time balance inquiry screen for any cost centre showing budget versus actual comparison with variance analysis in both absolute amounts and percentages. The inquiry must support drill-down from summary to individual transaction level, filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), currency (original and translated), transaction type, and posting user. Results must be displayable in the entity's functional currency or group reporting currency (USD) with exchange rate details shown for each converted amount. The interface must support Arabic RTL layout with bidirectional text for mixed Arabic-English content such as vessel names in English within Arabic descriptions. Export functionality must generate Excel, PDF, and CSV outputs with proper Arabic character encoding (UTF-8) and right-to-left formatting preserved in exported documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R007	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Inter-Entity Cost Sharing and Transfer Pricing	The system must support inter-entity cost sharing arrangements where shared resources such as vessels, containers, or equipment are used across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Transfer pricing rules must be configurable per cost type and entity pair, supporting arm's length pricing methods including comparable uncontrolled price (CUP), cost-plus, and transactional net margin methods as required by OECD guidelines and local regulations in each jurisdiction. The system must automatically generate intercompany invoices, record receivables and payables in the respective entities, and create elimination entries for group consolidation. Documentation for each transfer pricing arrangement must be maintained within the system to satisfy tax authority requirements, particularly UAE Corporate Tax regulations effective from June 2023 and KSA Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority transfer pricing documentation requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R008	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Tagging for Container Types	The system must allow tagging of cost centre transactions with container type dimensions including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, and 40FR, enabling analysis of cost distribution by container category. Each transaction posted to a cost centre must optionally carry container type, size (TEU/FEU), and cargo classification (dry, reefer, hazardous DG classes 1-9, out-of-gauge) attributes. The tagging must support reporting of cost-per-TEU by container type across ports, allowing comparison of handling costs for standard containers versus reefer units requiring power supply at Hamad Port versus Jebel Ali. Tags must be searchable and filterable in all inquiry and reporting screens, with Arabic label support for all tag categories and values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R009	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Offline Cost Centre Data Access	The system must provide offline capability for cost centre balance inquiries and budget status reviews when network connectivity is unavailable, particularly relevant for personnel at remote port facilities or aboard vessels. The offline module must synchronize cost centre master data, current period budgets, and transaction summaries to local storage upon last successful connection. Users must be able to view cost centre hierarchies, current balances, and budget utilization percentages in offline mode. Any budget approval actions initiated offline must be queued and automatically submitted upon reconnection with conflict detection if the underlying data has changed during the offline period. The offline interface must maintain full Arabic RTL support and display a clear visual indicator of the data staleness timestamp to prevent decisions based on outdated information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R010	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Performance Benchmarking	The system must support performance benchmarking across comparable cost centres, enabling comparison of operational efficiency between ports (Hamad Port versus Jebel Ali versus JNPT), between similar departments across entities, and against historical periods. Benchmarking metrics must include cost per TEU handled, cost per vessel call, overhead ratio to revenue, and budget adherence percentage. The system must automatically identify cost centres performing outside one or two standard deviations from the peer group average and flag them for management review. Benchmarking reports must be configurable to exclude non-recurring items, foreign exchange gains or losses, and intercompany charges to ensure like-for-like comparison. Results must be presentable in both tabular and graphical formats with Arabic and English labels simultaneously displayed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R011	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Dimension Analysis	The system must support multi-dimensional analysis of cost centre data along at least six configurable dimensions: entity, port, trade lane, vessel class, service type (FCL, LCL, breakbulk, ro-ro), and customer segment. Users must be able to pivot, slice, and dice cost data interactively across these dimensions without requiring IT intervention or custom report development. The analysis engine must support calculated measures such as cost per TEU by dimension combination, margin analysis by trade lane per entity, and contribution analysis by service type. Dimension hierarchies must support roll-up and drill-down navigation. The interface must allow saving of frequently used dimension combinations as personal or shared analysis templates, with access control ensuring users can only view cost centres within their authorized entity and organizational scope.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R012	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Integration with GL and Sub-Ledgers	The system must maintain real-time bidirectional integration between cost centres and the general ledger, ensuring every GL posting to an expense or revenue account carries a mandatory cost centre reference. Integration must extend to all sub-ledgers including accounts payable (vendor invoices for port charges, bunker suppliers, stevedoring), accounts receivable (freight revenue by trade lane), fixed assets (depreciation allocation), and payroll (salary distribution across cost centres). The system must validate cost centre assignments during transaction entry and reject postings to inactive or closed cost centres. Batch posting interfaces for high-volume transactions such as daily terminal handling charges at Jebel Ali processing over 50,000 container moves monthly must support bulk cost centre assignment with configurable default rules and exception handling for mismatched or missing cost centre codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R013	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Closure and Period-End Processing	The system must support systematic cost centre period-end processing including accrual calculations for incurred-but-not-invoiced costs such as pending port authority charges, estimated bunker consumption for voyages in progress, and unbilled stevedoring services. The period-end process must execute automatic allocation runs per defined rules, generate variance reports comparing budget to actuals, and create management review packages. Cost centres must support soft close (preventing new postings while allowing adjustments) and hard close (preventing all postings) with configurable close schedules per entity respecting different fiscal year-ends. The system must handle the scenario where Qatar and UAE entities operate on calendar year-end while the India entity may operate on a March 31 fiscal year-end, allowing independent period management per entity while still supporting group consolidation at common reporting dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R014	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for all cost centre master data changes, budget modifications, allocation runs, and transaction postings. The audit log must capture before and after values, timestamp, user identity, terminal/device identifier, and business justification for each change. Compliance reports must be generatable for external auditors showing cost centre structure changes over a specified period, budget revision history, allocation methodology documentation, and intercompany transaction summaries. The system must support extraction of audit data in formats required by the Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority, UAE Federal Tax Authority, KSA ZATCA, and Indian Goods and Services Tax authorities. Audit data retention must be configurable per entity with a minimum of ten years to satisfy the most stringent jurisdictional requirement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R015	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	AI-Driven Cost Centre Optimization Recommendations	The system must leverage AI and machine learning models to analyze historical cost centre data and generate optimization recommendations. The AI engine must identify cost centres with consistently underutilized budgets and suggest reallocation, detect cost centres where actual spending patterns diverge significantly from allocated budget profiles, and recommend consolidation of cost centres with overlapping functions. Recommendations must consider seasonal shipping patterns such as increased cargo volumes during Ramadan pre-stocking periods and Indian festival seasons. The AI model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical data per entity before generating recommendations, with confidence scores attached to each suggestion. Recommendations must be presented in both Arabic and English with supporting data visualizations and must require human approval before any automated action is taken on cost centre structures or budgets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-001-R016	FEAT-014-3-001	MOD-014	Cost Centre Statistical Key Figures	The system must support definition and maintenance of statistical key figures (non-monetary metrics) at the cost centre level, such as TEUs handled, vessel calls serviced, crane hours utilized, truck turnaround count, and labor hours consumed. These statistical figures must be enterable manually or receivable via automated feeds from terminal operating systems at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Statistical key figures must serve as allocation bases for the cost allocation rules engine, enabling activity-based costing approaches where overhead costs are distributed based on actual operational activity. The system must validate that statistical key figures are posted for all required cost centres before period-end allocation runs execute, generating exception reports for missing data. Historical statistical data must be retained alongside financial data for trend analysis and benchmarking, supporting both Gregorian and Hijri period aggregations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R001	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Project Registration and Classification	The system must support registration of capital expenditure projects with mandatory fields including project code, Arabic and English project names, sponsoring entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), asset category (vessels, containers, port equipment such as STS cranes and RTG cranes, terminal buildings, IT infrastructure, vehicles), expected total budget in project currency and USD equivalent, estimated start and completion dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and strategic justification. Each CAPEX project must be classifiable as new acquisition, capacity expansion, replacement, or regulatory compliance. The system must enforce a unique sequential numbering scheme per entity per fiscal year (e.g., CAPEX-QA-2026-001 for Qatar) and must prevent duplicate project registration for the same asset through validation against existing asset master records and open CAPEX projects.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R002	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Multi-Level CAPEX Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for CAPEX requests based on project value thresholds. Default thresholds must be: up to USD 50,000 requires Department Head approval; USD 50,001 to USD 500,000 requires Finance Director and COO approval; USD 500,001 to USD 5,000,000 requires CFO and CEO approval; above USD 5,000,000 requires Board of Directors approval. Threshold levels must be independently configurable per entity to accommodate local governance requirements. The workflow must support document attachments including feasibility studies, vendor quotations (minimum three quotes for projects above USD 100,000), ROI calculations, and environmental impact assessments for port infrastructure projects. Approval notifications must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with configurable escalation timelines and delegation rules for approver absence periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R003	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Budget Tracking and Commitment Accounting	The system must track CAPEX budgets at both the annual portfolio level per entity and the individual project level, distinguishing between approved budget, committed amounts (purchase orders issued), actual expenditure (invoices received), and remaining available budget. Commitment accounting must reserve budget upon purchase order approval, reducing available budget before actual cash outflow. The system must support budget carryover rules for multi-year CAPEX projects such as vessel construction spanning 24-36 months, with automatic rollover of uncommitted amounts subject to re-approval. Currency exposure tracking must show the impact of exchange rate movements on USD-denominated CAPEX budgets when actual expenditures are in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR. The system must generate alerts when cumulative project spending exceeds 90% of approved budget or when projected final cost based on current spending trajectory exceeds the approved amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R004	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Asset Capitalization from CAPEX Projects	The system must support the capitalization process converting CAPEX project costs into fixed asset records upon project completion or asset readiness for use. Capitalization must aggregate all qualifying costs including purchase price, import duties (relevant for container and equipment imports into Qatar, KSA, and India), freight charges for delivery to port locations, installation and commissioning costs, and borrowing costs eligible for capitalization under IAS 23. The system must handle partial capitalization for phased projects where some components are ready for use before the overall project completes, such as a new container yard at Hamad Port where Phase 1 areas are operational while Phase 2 is still under construction. Capitalized assets must automatically inherit the cost centre assignment from the CAPEX project and create corresponding entries in the fixed asset register with proper classification under IFRS 16 for leased assets versus owned assets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R005	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Depreciation and Amortization Calculation Engine	The system must provide a comprehensive depreciation and amortization engine supporting multiple methods: straight-line, declining balance, double declining balance, sum-of-years-digits, and units-of-production. Default useful lives must be pre-configured for shipping industry assets: vessels (20-25 years with residual value based on scrap steel prices), containers (12-15 years for standard, 10-12 years for reefer units), STS gantry cranes (25-30 years), RTG cranes (20 years), reach stackers (10-12 years), terminal buildings (30-40 years), and IT systems (3-5 years). The engine must support component depreciation where major vessel components (hull, engine, navigation systems) carry different useful lives per IAS 16. Depreciation must run automatically at month-end, posting to the correct cost centre and GL accounts, with the ability to process retroactive adjustments when assets are capitalized mid-period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R006	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Multi-GAAP Depreciation Books	The system must support parallel depreciation books to satisfy multiple reporting requirements simultaneously. At minimum, the system must maintain: a primary book per entity following local GAAP (QFRS for Qatar, UAE IFRS, KSA IFRS/SOCPA standards, Indian GAAP/Ind AS), a group consolidation book following full IFRS, and a tax depreciation book following local tax depreciation rules including UAE Corporate Tax depreciation schedules, KSA ZATCA depreciation rules, and Indian Income Tax Act depreciation rates under Section 32. Each book must independently track cost basis, accumulated depreciation, depreciation method, and useful life. The system must automatically calculate and track temporary differences between book and tax depreciation for deferred tax computation. Book-to-book reconciliation reports must be available showing the bridge between different depreciation treatments for the same asset.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R007	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Progress Monitoring and Milestone Tracking	The system must track CAPEX project progress through configurable milestones such as design approval, procurement, fabrication, delivery, installation, testing, and commissioning. Each milestone must record planned versus actual completion dates, percentage of physical progress, and associated cost to date versus milestone budget. For vessel construction projects, milestones must align with industry-standard shipbuilding stages including steel cutting, keel laying, hull completion, launching, sea trials, and delivery. The system must generate Gantt chart visualizations of project timelines with critical path highlighting, accessible in both Arabic and English interfaces. Automated alerts must notify project managers and finance controllers when milestones are overdue by configurable thresholds, and the system must support earned value management (EVM) calculations including Cost Performance Index (CPI) and Schedule Performance Index (SPI) for each CAPEX project.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R008	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Asset Impairment Testing and Revaluation	The system must support annual and triggered impairment testing of capitalized assets in accordance with IAS 36. For cash-generating units in container shipping, the system must define and track CGUs at the vessel level, route level, or terminal level as configured by the entity. Impairment testing must compare carrying amounts against recoverable amounts calculated as the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use. The system must support the discounted cash flow model for value-in-use calculations with configurable discount rates, growth rates, and projection periods. For vessel assets, the system must integrate market data for comparable vessel valuations. Impairment losses and reversals must be recorded with full documentation of assumptions, approved by appropriate authority, and posted to the correct cost centre and GL accounts. The revaluation model as an alternative to cost model must also be supported for entities electing IFRS revaluation of property, plant and equipment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R009	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Container Fleet CAPEX Tracking	The system must provide specialized CAPEX tracking for container fleet procurement, the largest recurring capital expenditure category in container shipping. Container orders must be trackable by manufacturer, purchase order number, delivery schedule, and container specification (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, special equipment). The system must handle bulk container orders where thousands of units are procured in a single purchase from manufacturers in China, tracking individual container numbers (BIC/ISO 6346 codes) as they are manufactured, tested, and delivered to designated ports. Payment milestone tracking must support typical container procurement terms: 30% upon order confirmation, 30% upon steel cutting, and 40% upon delivery and inspection. The system must calculate per-unit capitalized cost including purchase price, inland transportation to loading port, ocean freight to destination (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), customs duties, and pre-trip inspection costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R010	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Disposal and Retirement Processing	The system must manage the full disposal lifecycle for capitalized assets including sale, scrapping, trade-in, transfer to another entity, or loss/damage write-off. Disposal processing must calculate gain or loss on disposal comparing net book value against disposal proceeds, with proper tax treatment per jurisdiction. For vessel disposals, the system must comply with the Hong Kong International Convention for the Safe and Environmentally Sound Recycling of Ships where applicable, tracking required certificates and environmental compliance documentation. Container scrapping must be processable in bulk batches given the high volume, with configurable scrap value assumptions based on current steel market prices. Inter-entity asset transfers must record the transaction at appropriate transfer prices, generating intercompany entries in both the transferring and receiving entity's books. All disposals must require approval workflow with documentation of disposal justification and must update the fixed asset register, depreciation schedules, and insurance records simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R011	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Financing and Lease Integration	The system must track the financing structure of CAPEX projects distinguishing between self-funded, bank-financed, Islamic finance (Ijara, Murabaha structures commonly used in Qatar, UAE, and KSA), and lease arrangements. For financed assets, the system must record loan drawdown schedules, interest or profit rate calculations (conventional and Sharia-compliant), and principal repayment tracking linked to the specific CAPEX project and resulting asset. IFRS 16 lease accounting must be fully integrated for leased assets, calculating right-of-use assets and lease liabilities at present value of future lease payments. The system must support both lessee and lessor accounting for entities that lease containers or equipment to third parties. Sale-and-leaseback transactions, commonly used for vessel financing, must be processed in compliance with IFRS 16 requirements including proper gain/loss recognition and right-of-use asset measurement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R012	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Insurance and Warranty Tracking	The system must track insurance coverage and warranty periods for all capitalized assets. For vessels, the system must record Protection and Indemnity (P&I) club membership, hull and machinery insurance, and war risk coverage with policy numbers, coverage amounts, premium payment dates, and renewal schedules. For port equipment such as STS cranes and RTGs, warranty periods from manufacturers (typically 12-24 months) must be tracked with expiry alerts generated 90 days before warranty end to initiate maintenance contract procurement. Container fleet insurance must be trackable at both aggregate fleet level and individual unit level for high-value special equipment. The system must automatically calculate and allocate insurance premium costs to the appropriate cost centres based on asset location and usage, with amortization of annual premiums on a monthly basis. Insurance claim records must be linkable to specific assets and CAPEX projects, tracking claim amounts, deductibles, and settlement proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R013	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Report Generation and Analysis	The system must generate comprehensive CAPEX reports including: project status summary showing all active CAPEX projects with budget versus actual analysis, asset additions report by category and entity for the period, depreciation schedule showing current period charges and accumulated balances, asset register listing with net book values as at any specified date, and CAPEX forecast versus actual variance analysis. Reports must support multi-currency presentation with amounts shown in entity functional currency and USD group currency side by side. All reports must be available in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. The system must support scheduled automatic report generation and distribution via email to configured recipient lists. Report drill-down capability must allow navigation from summary totals to individual asset details and underlying source transactions including purchase orders, invoices, and payment records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R014	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Integration with Procurement and AP	The system must maintain seamless integration between CAPEX tracking and the procurement and accounts payable modules. Purchase requisitions created for CAPEX items must reference the approved CAPEX project code, with validation that the requisition amount does not exceed remaining project budget. Goods receipt for capital items must trigger work-in-progress (WIP) accounting entries, accumulating costs in the CAPEX WIP account until capitalization criteria are met. Vendor invoices matched to CAPEX purchase orders must automatically post to the CAPEX project cost accumulation, with proper handling of foreign currency invoices from international suppliers (e.g., crane manufacturers in China, container builders in Qingdao/Shanghai). Retention amounts typically held at 5-10% on construction contracts must be tracked separately and released upon satisfactory completion of defects liability periods. The integration must handle partial deliveries and progress billing common in large infrastructure projects at port facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R015	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Tax Incentive and Grant Tracking	The system must track tax incentives, government grants, and investment allowances applicable to CAPEX projects in each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must handle Qatar Free Zone tax exemptions for qualifying assets in Hamad Port free zone areas. For UAE, tracking of free zone versus mainland asset classifications affecting corporate tax treatment must be supported. For KSA, investment incentives under Vision 2030 programs for logistics infrastructure must be recordable against qualifying CAPEX projects. For India, Special Economic Zone (SEZ) benefits at JNPT and accelerated depreciation allowances under the Income Tax Act must be tracked. Government grants received for infrastructure development must be accounted for per IAS 20, supporting both the deferred income approach and the netted-against-asset-cost approach as elected by each entity. The system must generate reports showing the tax benefit realized from each CAPEX project across all applicable jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R016	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	Offline CAPEX Approval and Data Entry	The system must support offline CAPEX approval processing for stakeholders who may be at remote port locations, aboard vessels, or traveling between country operations where internet connectivity is unreliable. The offline module must cache pending CAPEX approval requests with full supporting documentation locally on the user's device, allowing review and approval or rejection decisions to be recorded offline. Upon reconnection, offline decisions must synchronize with the central system with conflict resolution for cases where the same CAPEX request was modified by another user during the offline period. Offline data entry must be supported for CAPEX progress updates including milestone completion reporting and physical progress percentage updates from project sites. The offline interface must maintain full Arabic RTL support and bilingual display capabilities, ensuring that document attachments cached offline are accessible without network connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-002-R017	FEAT-014-3-002	MOD-014	CAPEX Environmental and Sustainability Tracking	The system must track environmental and sustainability attributes of CAPEX projects to support ESG reporting requirements and IMO 2030/2050 decarbonization targets. For vessel CAPEX, the system must record Energy Efficiency Design Index (EEDI) and Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) ratings, tracking whether new vessel acquisitions or retrofits improve the fleet's environmental performance. For port infrastructure CAPEX, sustainability features such as shore power (cold ironing) installations, solar panel arrays, electric RTG conversions, and LNG bunkering facilities must be tagged for separate ESG disclosure reporting. The system must calculate the green CAPEX ratio (environmentally beneficial investments as a percentage of total CAPEX) per entity and consolidated group. Cost savings attributable to sustainability investments (fuel efficiency improvements, reduced emissions penalties) must be trackable against the original CAPEX business case projections over the asset's useful life.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R001	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Real-Time Cost Transaction Monitoring Engine	The system must implement a real-time monitoring engine that analyzes every cost transaction as it is posted, comparing it against established baselines derived from historical patterns. The engine must process transactions from all cost categories including port charges at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, ocean freight rates by trade lane, bunker fuel costs by vessel and route, terminal handling charges, inland transportation, customs duties, and agency fees. Processing latency must not exceed 30 seconds from transaction posting to anomaly assessment. The engine must maintain rolling baselines calculated from the most recent 12 months of data, automatically adjusting for known seasonal patterns such as increased volumes during pre-Ramadan periods and Indian festival seasons. Each transaction must receive an anomaly score from 0 (normal) to 100 (highly anomalous), with configurable thresholds per cost category triggering different alert levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R002	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Statistical Anomaly Detection Models	The system must implement multiple statistical anomaly detection models operating in ensemble: Z-score analysis for detecting individual transaction amounts deviating significantly from the mean for that cost type, Isolation Forest algorithm for identifying outliers in multi-dimensional cost data considering amount, vendor, port, container type, and timing dimensions simultaneously, and ARIMA-based time series models for detecting unusual trends or level shifts in cost series over time. Each model must be independently trainable and tunable per entity, cost category, and port combination. The ensemble scoring mechanism must weight individual model outputs based on their historical detection accuracy, with weights recalibrated monthly. The system must achieve a minimum precision of 85% (at least 85% of flagged anomalies are true positives when investigated) to minimize alert fatigue among finance teams processing high transaction volumes across four country operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R003	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Port Charge Anomaly Detection	The system must provide specialized anomaly detection for port authority charges, which represent a major cost category in container shipping. Detection rules must compare actual port charges against published tariff schedules for Hamad Port (Qatar Ports Management Company tariffs), Khalifa Port (Abu Dhabi Ports tariffs), Jebel Ali (DP World published tariffs), and JNPT (Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority tariff orders). The system must flag discrepancies between invoiced amounts and expected amounts calculated from published tariffs applied to the vessel's actual specifications (GT, LOA, draft) and cargo volume. Seasonal surcharges, congestion levies, and special handling charges for hazardous cargo (IMO DG classes 1-9) must be validated against published schedules. The system must also detect unusual patterns such as a sudden increase in anchorage charges suggesting operational delays, or unexpected pilotage charges indicating vessel size or draft issues at a specific port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R004	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Bunker Fuel Cost Anomaly Detection	The system must monitor bunker fuel costs at granular level, detecting anomalies in fuel price per metric ton relative to benchmark indices (Platts, Baltic Exchange), fuel consumption per nautical mile relative to vessel specifications and historical performance, and total voyage fuel cost relative to route distance and vessel class. The AI model must account for variables affecting fuel consumption including weather conditions, vessel speed, cargo load factor, hull fouling status, and port waiting times. Anomalies indicating potential fuel theft, unreported bunkering, or inefficient vessel operation must be flagged with specific diagnostic insights. The system must compare bunkering costs across different supply ports (Fujairah being a major hub near Jebel Ali, Singapore, Colombo) to detect pricing anomalies from specific suppliers. Alerts must include recommended actions such as supplier verification, vessel inspection, or route optimization, presented in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R005	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Freight Rate Anomaly Detection	The system must detect anomalies in freight rates charged to customers and rates contracted with shipping line partners. Rate anomaly detection must consider trade lane (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Jeddah Islamic Port), container type (20GP, 40HC, reefer), commodity classification, contract versus spot rate indicators, and INCOTERMS basis (CIF, FOB, CFR, FCA). The system must flag instances where actual booked rates fall significantly below or above the expected range for the specific trade lane and period, potentially indicating pricing errors, unauthorized discounting, or market rate shifts. Comparison against market rate indices such as the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI) and Drewry World Container Index must be integrated where available. The AI must learn entity-specific pricing strategies and customer tier structures, detecting when premium customers are charged below their negotiated contract rates or when spot rates are booked below the current market floor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R006	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Demurrage and Detention Cost Monitoring	The system must implement AI-powered monitoring of demurrage and detention charges, which represent a significant and often controllable cost in container shipping. The system must track free-time allowances per shipping line, port, and container type, automatically flagging containers approaching free-time expiry to prevent avoidable charges. Historical demurrage patterns must be analyzed by customer, trade lane, port, and clearing agent to identify systemic causes of delays such as customs clearance bottlenecks at JNPT, documentation issues at Hamad Port, or warehouse congestion at Jebel Ali. The AI must distinguish between unavoidable demurrage (port congestion, regulatory holds) and controllable demurrage (customer collection delays, documentation errors) and calculate the potential savings if controllable demurrage is reduced. Anomaly alerts must fire when demurrage charges for a specific shipment, customer, or port exceed normal patterns by more than two standard deviations, with root cause analysis suggestions provided automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R007	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Vendor Invoice Fraud Detection	The system must implement AI-based fraud detection patterns for vendor invoices related to shipping costs. Detection rules must identify: duplicate invoices from the same vendor with matching amounts but different invoice numbers, invoices with amounts just below approval thresholds suggesting intentional splitting, vendors with addresses matching employee addresses, unusual payment terms deviating from master vendor agreements, round-number invoices lacking supporting documentation, and invoices from newly created vendors with high-value first transactions. The system must implement Benford's Law analysis on invoice digit distributions per vendor to detect manufactured invoice amounts. For port agency disbursement accounts (PDA), the system must compare estimated versus actual disbursements and flag cases where actual costs exceed estimates by more than a configurable tolerance (default 15%) across multiple port calls. All fraud detection alerts must be routed to a dedicated fraud investigation queue with restricted access limited to compliance officers and senior finance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R008	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Multi-Currency Anomaly Detection	The system must detect anomalies specifically related to multi-currency operations. This includes monitoring for unusual foreign exchange gains or losses on cost transactions relative to expected rates, detecting transactions booked at exchange rates significantly different from the prevailing market rate on the transaction date, and flagging patterns suggesting potential exchange rate manipulation such as consistent use of off-market rates with specific vendors. The system must track currency pair volatility for USD/QAR (pegged at 3.64), USD/AED (pegged at 3.6725), USD/SAR (pegged at 3.75), and USD/INR (floating rate), applying different anomaly thresholds for pegged currencies (where rate deviations beyond the peg band are highly anomalous) versus floating currencies (where wider volatility is expected). Intercompany transactions must be monitored for transfer pricing compliance, flagging cross-entity charges that deviate materially from arm's length benchmarks established in the transfer pricing documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R009	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Anomaly Alert Management and Workflow	The system must provide a comprehensive alert management interface where detected anomalies are presented as prioritized cases for investigation. Each alert must display: the anomalous transaction details, the expected normal range, the statistical confidence of the anomaly classification, historical context showing similar past transactions, and suggested investigation steps. Alerts must be categorizable as confirmed anomaly, false positive, or under investigation, with workflow routing to appropriate personnel based on anomaly type and entity. The interface must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual content display. Alert resolution must require documented findings and corrective actions. The system must learn from false positive classifications, automatically adjusting model parameters to reduce future false alerts on similar patterns. Alert aging reports must track the time from detection to resolution, with escalation rules triggering management notification for alerts unresolved beyond configurable SLA periods (default 72 hours for high-severity, 7 days for medium-severity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R010	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Cost Pattern Recognition and Trend Analysis	The system must employ pattern recognition algorithms to identify cost trends and emerging patterns that may not be apparent at the individual transaction level. Pattern analysis must detect: gradual cost creep where a specific cost category increases slowly but persistently over multiple periods, seasonal pattern breaks where costs do not follow expected seasonal patterns, correlation anomalies where costs that normally move together (such as bunker prices and fuel surcharges) diverge, and structural breaks indicating a fundamental change in cost behavior such as a new port tariff regime. The system must present detected patterns as trend alerts with visualization showing the identified pattern overlaid on historical data. Pattern analysis must operate across entities to identify whether a trend is localized to a single country operation or represents a broader market shift. AI confidence intervals must be displayed for each identified pattern, with drill-down capability to examine the underlying transactions driving the pattern.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R011	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Anomaly Detection Model Training and Retraining	The system must support initial model training on historical cost data with a minimum of 24 months of transaction history per entity and automated periodic retraining to adapt to evolving cost patterns. Model retraining must execute monthly using the most recent data window, with the ability to trigger ad-hoc retraining after significant events such as new trade lane launches, port tariff changes, or fuel price regime shifts. The training pipeline must include data preprocessing, feature engineering, model fitting, validation against a holdout dataset, and performance comparison against the current production model. New models must only be promoted to production if they demonstrate equal or superior performance metrics (precision, recall, F1-score) compared to the incumbent model. The system must maintain a model registry tracking all trained model versions, their training data periods, hyperparameters, and performance metrics. Model explainability reports must be generatable showing which features (cost type, vendor, port, season, currency) are most influential in the anomaly scoring decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R012	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Terminal Handling Charge Anomaly Detection	The system must provide specialized anomaly detection for terminal handling charges (THC) which vary significantly by port, terminal operator, container type, and cargo nature. The system must maintain a reference database of expected THC ranges per port terminal (Hamad Port Container Terminal, Khalifa Port Container Terminal, Jebel Ali Terminal 1-4, JNPT Gateway Terminals and Nhava Sheva International Container Terminal), container specification (20ft/40ft, standard/reefer/open-top/flat-rack), and handling type (full import, full export, empty repositioning, transshipment). Anomaly detection must flag THC invoices deviating more than 10% from the expected rate, accounting for published tariff revisions and contractual volume discount tiers. The system must identify patterns of THC overcharging by specific terminal operators or trends of increasing THC that may necessitate rate renegotiation. Comparison dashboards must show THC benchmarking across the four primary ports to inform commercial decision-making on port selection and route optimization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R001	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	AI-Driven Invoice Auto-Generation from Booking Data	The system must automatically generate freight invoices by extracting and reconciling data from confirmed bookings, bills of lading (B/L), and container tracking records. The AI engine shall match booking reference numbers, container numbers (ISO 6346 format), voyage details, and port pair combinations (e.g., QAHMD–INJNP, AEJEA–QAHMD) to produce line items covering ocean freight, terminal handling charges (THC), bunker adjustment factor (BAF), and currency adjustment factor (CAF). The auto-generation must achieve at least 95% accuracy, flagging discrepancies for human review only when confidence falls below the configured threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R013	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Customs Duty and Tax Anomaly Detection	The system must detect anomalies in customs duty, import taxes, and GST/VAT charges across all four country operations. For India JNPT, the system must validate customs duty calculations against the applicable Harmonized System (HS) tariff codes, checking that duty rates applied match the published customs tariff schedule and that assessable values are consistent with declared invoice values. For Qatar, UAE, and KSA, GCC Common Customs Law compliance must be validated including the standard 5% customs duty rate with detection of incorrect exemption claims or miscategorized goods. The system must flag shipments where customs duty paid deviates significantly from expected amounts based on declared commodity, origin country (considering preferential trade agreements such as GCC-Singapore FTA), and cargo value. Patterns suggesting potential customs fraud such as consistent undervaluation of cargo, misclassification of goods to lower duty categories, or abuse of temporary import provisions must trigger high-priority alerts routed to the compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R014	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Predictive Cost Anomaly Forecasting	The system must go beyond reactive anomaly detection to provide predictive forecasting of potential future cost anomalies. Using time series forecasting models (Prophet, LSTM neural networks), the system must predict expected cost levels for the next 30, 60, and 90 days by category, entity, and port. When predicted costs show significant deviation from budget or historical norms, early warning alerts must be generated allowing proactive management intervention. The forecasting engine must incorporate external data signals including crude oil price futures for bunker cost prediction, published port tariff revision announcements, currency forward rates for exchange rate impact assessment, and shipping market indices for freight rate trends. Forecast accuracy must be tracked and reported, with the system targeting a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) below 15% for monthly cost category predictions. Forecast results must be visualized in interactive dashboards with Arabic and English toggle, showing prediction intervals at 80% and 95% confidence levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R015	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Anomaly Detection Audit and Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive audit reports on anomaly detection activities for internal and external compliance review. Reports must include: total transactions monitored per period, number and percentage of anomalies detected by severity and category, investigation outcomes (confirmed versus false positive), average resolution time, financial impact of confirmed anomalies (overcharges recovered, fraud prevented), and model performance metrics. The audit trail must record every anomaly detection decision, investigation action, and resolution outcome with timestamps and user identities. Reports must be available in formats acceptable to external auditors and regulatory authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must track and report the return on investment of the anomaly detection capability, quantifying the total cost savings, fraud prevention, and efficiency gains attributable to AI-detected anomalies over configurable periods. Annual effectiveness reviews must compare detection rates year-over-year and benchmark against industry standards for financial anomaly detection in the logistics sector.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R016	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Anomaly Notification Channels and Escalation	The system must support multiple notification channels for anomaly alerts including in-application notifications with bilingual Arabic and English display, email notifications with formatted alert summaries and direct links to investigation screens, SMS alerts for critical severity anomalies, and integration with enterprise messaging platforms (Microsoft Teams, WhatsApp Business). Notification preferences must be configurable per user, allowing individuals to subscribe to specific anomaly categories, severity levels, entities, or ports of interest. Escalation chains must be definable per anomaly severity: low-severity anomalies route to cost analysts, medium-severity to finance managers, and high-severity to CFO and compliance officers. Time-based escalation must automatically elevate unacknowledged alerts to the next management level after configurable intervals. The system must respect user timezone settings across the four country operations (Qatar GMT+3, UAE GMT+4, KSA GMT+3, India GMT+5:30) for notification scheduling and must suppress non-critical notifications during configured quiet hours per user preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-003-R017	FEAT-014-3-003	MOD-014	Cost Anomaly Root Cause Analysis Engine	The system must provide an automated root cause analysis engine that, upon anomaly detection, performs systematic investigation to identify the most probable cause. The engine must analyze related transactions, compare against reference data (tariff schedules, contract rates, market benchmarks), check for recent changes in master data (new vendor, modified payment terms, exchange rate changes), and correlate with operational events (port congestion, weather delays, equipment breakdowns). Root cause categories must include: data entry error, vendor overcharging, tariff change, contract non-compliance, operational inefficiency, market rate movement, currency fluctuation, and potential fraud. The engine must present its findings as a ranked list of probable causes with supporting evidence for each hypothesis, enabling investigators to efficiently confirm or eliminate possibilities. Root cause analysis must complete within 60 seconds of anomaly detection for standard cases, with complex cross-entity analyses completing within five minutes. Findings must be presented in both Arabic and English with supporting transaction details and reference documents accessible through drill-down navigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R001	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Executive Financial Dashboard Overview	The system must provide an executive-level financial dashboard displaying key financial metrics for the consolidated group and each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) on a single screen. Mandatory KPIs on the overview dashboard must include: total revenue, EBITDA, EBITDA margin percentage, net profit, revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, operating ratio (operating expenses as percentage of revenue), vessel utilization rate, and container fleet utilization rate. All metrics must be displayable in USD for group consolidation with the ability to toggle to entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The dashboard must support configurable time period comparison (current month versus prior month, current month versus same month prior year, year-to-date versus prior year-to-date). Visual indicators using color coding (green for favorable, red for unfavorable variance) must highlight performance against budget targets. The dashboard must load within three seconds and support both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with automatic switching based on user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R002	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Voyage Profitability Reporting	The system must generate voyage profitability reports analyzing financial performance at the individual voyage level. Each voyage report must show: total revenue (freight, surcharges, demurrage collected), total costs (bunker fuel, port charges at each call port, canal transit fees, terminal handling, crew costs, vessel daily operating cost allocation, container repositioning), and resulting voyage contribution margin. The report must support both completed voyages with actual figures and in-progress voyages with a combination of actuals-to-date and projected costs to completion. Profitability must be calculable on a per-TEU and per-FEU basis. The system must support drill-down from voyage summary to individual bill of lading-level revenue and individual cost transaction detail. Comparative analysis must show the same vessel's performance across multiple voyages on the same route, identifying trends in voyage profitability. Reports must handle multi-leg voyages calling at multiple ports (e.g., Jebel Ali → Hamad Port → Jeddah → JNPT) with proper cost allocation to each voyage leg.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R003	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Trade Lane Profitability Analysis	The system must provide trade lane profitability analysis showing financial performance for each origin-destination pair in the network. Trade lanes must be analyzable at multiple granularity levels: port-to-port (e.g., Jebel Ali AEJEA to JNPT INNPT), country-to-country (UAE to India), and region-to-region (GCC to South Asia). For each trade lane, the system must display: revenue by freight type (FCL, LCL), average revenue per TEU, total direct costs, contribution margin, and loaded versus empty TEU ratio. The analysis must support segmentation by container type showing the profitability difference between dry cargo, reefer, hazardous, and special equipment shipments per trade lane. Directional imbalance analysis must quantify the financial impact of cargo volume disparity between head-haul and back-haul directions. The system must generate AI-powered recommendations for trade lanes requiring rate adjustments, marketing focus, or potential discontinuation based on sustained negative contribution margins. All analysis must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with dual Arabic-English header options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R004	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Port Performance Financial KPIs	The system must track and display financial KPIs specific to each port operation: Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Port-level KPIs must include: revenue per vessel call, cost per vessel call, revenue per TEU throughput, terminal handling cost per move, average demurrage and detention cost per container, average customs clearance cost, and truck turnaround cost. The dashboard must compare actual port costs against budgeted benchmarks and against the other ports in the network for cross-port benchmarking. Port cost trend analysis must show month-over-month and year-over-year changes with identification of cost drivers. For each port, the system must track free zone versus non-free zone financial metrics separately where applicable (Hamad Port Free Zone, Jebel Ali Free Zone, JNPT SEZ). The system must generate port performance scorecards rated on cost efficiency, revenue generation, operational productivity, and customer satisfaction financial metrics, with composite scores enabling ranking of port operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R005	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Customer Profitability Reporting	The system must produce customer profitability reports analyzing the financial contribution of each customer relationship. Customer profitability must be calculated as customer revenue (all freight, surcharges, and ancillary service charges) minus directly attributable costs (sales commissions, customer-specific transport arrangements, special handling, dedicated equipment) minus allocated shared costs (based on the customer's proportion of TEU throughput or revenue). The system must rank customers by total contribution margin and by margin percentage, identifying the top 20% of customers generating 80% of profits and the bottom customers with negative margins. Cost-to-serve analysis must break down the cost of serving each customer by activity: documentation, customs clearance, warehousing, last-mile delivery, and customer service interactions. The system must support customer profitability analysis across entities for customers operating in multiple countries (e.g., a customer shipping from Jebel Ali and collecting at JNPT), providing a consolidated cross-entity customer view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R006	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Cash Flow Forecasting Dashboard	The system must provide a cash flow forecasting dashboard projecting cash inflows and outflows for the next 30, 60, 90, and 180 days. Cash inflow projections must incorporate: expected freight revenue collections based on payment terms by customer, expected demurrage and detention collections, and expected ancillary service revenues. Cash outflow projections must include: scheduled vessel operating cost payments, port charge payments based on expected vessel call schedules, bunker fuel purchase commitments, container lease payments, CAPEX payment milestones, loan principal and interest payments (both conventional and Islamic finance structures), employee salary payments, tax payment obligations (UAE Corporate Tax, KSA Zakat, India GST/Income Tax), and intercompany settlement schedules. The dashboard must show cash position by entity and by currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), with consolidated group cash position in USD. AI-assisted forecasting must incorporate historical payment pattern analysis to predict actual collection timing versus contractual due dates, particularly for markets with historically longer collection cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R007	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Budget Variance Analysis Reports	The system must generate detailed budget variance analysis reports at multiple organizational levels: group consolidated, entity, cost centre, department, and individual cost category. Variance reports must present budget, actual, and variance amounts with percentage calculation, decomposing total variance into price/rate variance and volume/quantity variance where applicable. For revenue variances, the system must separate the impact of TEU volume changes from rate changes per trade lane. For cost variances, the system must isolate the effects of input price changes (e.g., bunker fuel price increase) from consumption quantity changes (e.g., increased fuel usage). The system must flag material variances exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10% or USD 50,000 whichever is lower) for management attention with required commentary fields. Variance trend analysis must show whether variances are improving or deteriorating over consecutive periods. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods and must be generatable in Arabic and English with all variance narratives translatable between languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R008	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	AI-Generated Management Commentary	The system must leverage AI to automatically generate narrative management commentary for financial reports, transforming numerical data into readable executive summaries. The AI commentary engine must analyze monthly financial results and produce: a group performance summary highlighting key drivers of revenue and cost changes, entity-specific performance narratives explaining significant variances against budget and prior year, risk alerts for deteriorating financial metrics with suggested corrective actions, and forward-looking statements based on booking pipeline and cost forecasts. Commentary must be generated in both Arabic and English, with the Arabic version being a culturally appropriate translation rather than a literal word-for-word conversion. The AI must learn the organization's preferred reporting terminology, commonly referenced KPIs, and management reporting style through initial configuration and ongoing feedback. Generated commentary must be editable by finance team members before publication, with track changes showing AI-generated versus human-modified text. The system must maintain a library of approved commentary templates for recurring scenarios such as seasonal volume fluctuations or currency impact explanations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R009	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Container Fleet Financial Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a dedicated dashboard for monitoring the financial performance of the container fleet, the most capital-intensive asset class in the operation. Fleet financial KPIs must include: total fleet book value by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, special equipment), average revenue per container per day (per diem equivalent), container utilization rate (days loaded versus days idle), maintenance and repair cost per container per annum, depreciation cost per container per day, total cost of ownership versus leasing alternative analysis, and return on capital employed for the fleet by type and age category. The dashboard must show fleet age profile analysis with financial implications of aging units including increased repair costs, reduced market value, and optimal replacement timing recommendations. Container damage and write-off tracking must quantify the financial impact of container losses, damage, and premature retirement by cause (port handling damage, cargo damage, structural failure, theft). The AI must generate fleet investment recommendations suggesting optimal fleet size and mix based on trade lane demand forecasts and financial return analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R010	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Intercompany Financial Reconciliation Dashboard	The system must provide a dashboard for monitoring and reconciling intercompany financial positions between the four country entities. The dashboard must display: outstanding intercompany receivables and payables by entity pair (Qatar-UAE, Qatar-KSA, Qatar-India, UAE-KSA, UAE-India, KSA-India), aging analysis of intercompany balances, unmatched intercompany transactions requiring resolution, and the status of periodic intercompany settlement netting. The system must automatically match intercompany transactions between entities based on reference numbers, amounts (with configurable tolerance for exchange rate differences), and dates. Unmatched items must be flagged for investigation with aging triggers. The dashboard must show the impact of intercompany eliminations on consolidated financial statements, quantifying the total eliminated revenue, costs, receivables, payables, and unrealized profits on intercompany transactions. For transfer pricing compliance, the dashboard must track whether intercompany charges are within the approved arm's length ranges established in the group transfer pricing policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R011	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Working Capital Management Dashboard	The system must provide a working capital management dashboard tracking the key components of working capital for each entity and consolidated. Metrics must include: Days Sales Outstanding (DSO) by entity, customer segment, and trade lane; Days Payable Outstanding (DPO) by entity and vendor category; inventory days for consumables and spare parts; and the resulting Cash Conversion Cycle (CCC). The dashboard must show trend analysis of working capital metrics over 12 rolling months with traffic light indicators for metrics outside target ranges. For accounts receivable, the dashboard must display aging brackets (current, 30 days, 60 days, 90 days, over 90 days) by entity with separate identification of disputed invoices, credit notes pending, and collections on payment plans. For accounts payable, the system must show upcoming payment obligations by due date, early payment discount opportunities with calculated benefit of taking discounts, and vendor relationship risk scores for overdue payments. The dashboard must support currency-specific working capital analysis showing the QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD components separately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R012	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Custom Report Builder	The system must provide a self-service custom report builder allowing authorized finance users to create ad-hoc reports without IT assistance. The builder must offer a drag-and-drop interface for selecting dimensions (entity, port, cost centre, trade lane, vessel, customer, container type, time period) and measures (revenue, cost, margin, volume in TEU, utilization percentage) from a governed data catalog. Calculated fields must be definable using formula expressions with basic arithmetic, conditional logic (IF/THEN/ELSE), and aggregation functions (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT). Reports must support tabular, pivot table, chart (bar, line, pie, waterfall, scatter), and combined layouts. The builder must enforce data security, ensuring users can only access data for entities and cost centres within their authorization profile. Custom reports must be savable as templates, shareable with other users or groups, and schedulable for automatic periodic execution with email distribution. The interface must be fully functional in Arabic RTL mode with proper handling of mixed-direction content such as English port codes within Arabic report titles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R013	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Regulatory Financial Reporting Package	The system must generate regulatory financial reporting packages required by authorities in each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must produce reports formatted for Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority requirements and QFMA listed entity disclosures if applicable. For UAE, reports must comply with UAE Central Bank reporting requirements for licensed entities, FTA Corporate Tax return supporting schedules, and ADGM or DIFC regulatory submissions. For KSA, the system must generate ZATCA Zakat return supporting schedules, SOCPA-compliant financial statement formats, and Capital Market Authority disclosures for listed entities. For India, reports must support MCA annual filing formats, GST return reconciliation schedules (GSTR-1, GSTR-2B, GSTR-3B, GSTR-9), Income Tax return supporting schedules, and RBI reporting for foreign exchange transactions. All regulatory reports must support the specific date formats, currency presentations, and language requirements mandated by each authority, with Hijri date support for GCC regulatory filings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R014	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Operational Financial KPI Drill-Down	The system must support unlimited drill-down from any KPI on any dashboard to the underlying transaction-level detail. Starting from a group-level metric such as consolidated EBITDA, a user must be able to drill down through entity EBITDA, then to cost centre contribution, then to individual GL account balances, and finally to individual posted transactions with their source documents (invoices, voyage records, port call records). The drill-down path must be visually indicated as a breadcrumb trail, allowing users to navigate back to any previous level. Cross-dimensional drill-down must be supported, allowing a user viewing revenue by entity to pivot to view the same entity's revenue by trade lane, then by customer within a trade lane, all without returning to the top level. Performance must remain responsive with sub-second drill-down transitions regardless of the data volume, utilizing pre-aggregated OLAP cubes or columnar storage for analytical queries. The drill-down interface must maintain consistent Arabic or English formatting throughout all levels based on the user's language selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R015	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Scheduled Report Distribution	The system must support automated scheduled report generation and distribution. Authorized users must be able to configure report schedules at daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual frequencies with specific execution times configurable in each entity's local timezone (Qatar GMT+3, UAE GMT+4, KSA GMT+3, India GMT+5:30). Report output formats must include PDF, Excel (xlsx with formatted tables and charts), CSV for data extraction, and interactive HTML for web-based consumption. Distribution must support email delivery to individual recipients and distribution lists, upload to shared document repositories, and secure API endpoints for integration with external business intelligence platforms. Each scheduled report must support parameterization so a single report template can generate entity-specific versions (e.g., separate monthly performance reports for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India distributed to their respective management teams). The scheduling engine must handle dependencies where one report must complete before another begins, such as the consolidation report waiting for all entity reports to finalize. Execution logs must track each scheduled run's status, output file location, distribution confirmation, and any errors encountered.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R016	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Hijri Calendar Financial Reporting	The system must fully support Hijri (Islamic) calendar-based financial reporting alongside Gregorian calendar reporting. Users must be able to select Hijri periods for all reports and dashboards, with the system correctly aggregating financial data by Hijri month (Muharram through Dhul Hijjah) and Hijri fiscal year. The system must handle the complexity that Hijri months do not align with Gregorian months, requiring pro-rata allocation of daily transaction data to the correct Hijri period. Hijri-based budget setting must be supported for entities that operate on Hijri fiscal calendars, with automatic mapping to Gregorian periods for group consolidation. Reports must display Hijri dates in proper Arabic format alongside Gregorian dates where both are shown. The Hijri calendar module must account for the annual variation in Hijri month lengths (29 or 30 days based on lunar observation) with configurable advance calendar entries that can be adjusted when actual month start dates are officially announced by each country's moon-sighting authority. Period comparisons must correctly handle Hijri year-over-year analysis accounting for the approximately 11-day annual shift relative to the Gregorian calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R017	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	Mobile-Responsive KPI Dashboard	The system must deliver a fully mobile-responsive version of the KPI dashboard optimized for tablet and smartphone devices used by executives and port managers. The mobile dashboard must present the top-level KPIs in a card-based layout optimized for touch interaction, with swipe gestures for navigating between entity dashboards and tap-to-expand for KPI drill-down. Critical financial alerts and anomaly notifications must appear as push notifications on mobile devices. The mobile interface must support offline caching of the most recent dashboard data, allowing executives to review KPIs during travel between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations where connectivity may be intermittent. Data refresh must occur automatically upon network reconnection with visual indication of data timestamp. The mobile dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with proper touch gesture direction reversal (swipe right-to-left for forward navigation in Arabic mode versus left-to-right in English mode). Biometric authentication (fingerprint, Face ID) must be supported for secure dashboard access on mobile devices, with automatic session timeout after configurable inactivity period (default 15 minutes).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-014-3-004-R018	FEAT-014-3-004	MOD-014	ESG and Sustainability Financial Reporting	The system must support Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) financial reporting integrated with the management reporting framework. Environmental financial metrics must include: carbon emission costs per TEU by route (including EU ETS costs for European trade lanes), fuel efficiency cost metrics by vessel class, environmental compliance expenditure by port, and green investment as a percentage of total CAPEX. Social financial metrics must include: employee cost ratios (total compensation as percentage of revenue by entity), training investment per employee, safety-related expenditure, and community investment spending. Governance financial metrics must include: audit and compliance costs, regulatory penalty tracking by jurisdiction, and board and management remuneration disclosures. The system must generate reports aligned with GRI Standards (Global Reporting Initiative), SASB (Sustainability Accounting Standards Board) metrics for the marine transportation industry, and the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards (ISSB S1 and S2). ESG dashboard visualizations must show trend analysis and peer benchmarking, with all content available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R002	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Surcharge and Accessorial Charge Management	The system must support configurable surcharges and accessorial charges layered on top of base inland haulage rates. Standard surcharge types must include fuel surcharge (percentage-based or fixed amount, updateable monthly), toll charges (per-route fixed amounts for toll roads such as UAE Salik, India NHAI toll plazas), weekend/holiday surcharges (percentage uplift for deliveries on non-working days), peak season surcharges (date-range based), hazardous cargo surcharges (per IMDG class), overweight surcharges (per weight slab above standard), and genset charges for reefer containers requiring power during road transit. Each surcharge must have independent effective dates, applicability rules (by route, container type, or commodity), and currency. The system must calculate compound surcharges in the correct sequence as defined by the surcharge priority order.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R002	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Invoice Denomination and Exchange Rate Application	The system must support invoice generation in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with automatic exchange rate application sourced from the central treasury module or configured external rate feeds (e.g., Qatar Central Bank, RBI reference rates). Each invoice line item must display the original currency amount and the converted equivalent in the billing entity's functional currency. Exchange rates must be locked at the date of invoice generation or at a user-configurable rate date (booking date, B/L date, or vessel arrival date). Rounding rules must comply with each currency's decimal precision—two decimals for USD/AED/QAR/SAR and two decimals for INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R004	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Bilingual Invoice Rendering in Arabic and English	The system must generate invoices in both Arabic and English, supporting right-to-left (RTL) layout for Arabic text rendering. All field labels, charge descriptions, terms and conditions, and entity details must be presented in dual-language format on the same invoice document. Arabic numerals (Eastern Arabic ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) must be optionally supported based on entity configuration. The invoice template must properly handle mixed-direction text where English container numbers, ISO codes, and port codes appear within Arabic narrative sections. PDF output must embed Arabic fonts (e.g., Noto Naskh Arabic) to ensure consistent rendering across all platforms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R006	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Container-Level Charge Breakdown on Invoice	The system must itemize charges at the individual container level, distinguishing between 20-foot (TEU), 40-foot (FEU), 40-foot high-cube (40HC), and reefer container types. Each container line must display the container number, ISO size/type code, seal number, gross weight, and associated charges including ocean freight, THC at origin and destination ports, documentation fees, and any surcharges (peak season, hazardous cargo, overweight). For reefer containers, additional charges for power supply, pre-trip inspection (PTI), and genset usage must be separately itemized. The total must aggregate all container-level charges with subtotals by charge category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R007	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Electronic Invoice Dispatch via Multiple Channels	The system must support automated invoice dispatch through email with PDF attachment, EDI (IFTMIN/INVOIC D.95B message format), API integration with customer portals, and direct upload to government e-invoicing platforms such as ZATCA Fatoora for KSA and India's GST e-invoicing portal via NIC. Email dispatch must support configurable recipient lists per customer with CC/BCC options. The system must track dispatch status (sent, delivered, bounced, opened) and maintain a complete audit trail of all dispatch attempts. Failed dispatches must trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff up to three attempts before escalating to the billing team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R008	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	ZATCA Phase 2 E-Invoicing Compliance for KSA	For invoices generated by KSA billing entities, the system must comply with ZATCA Phase 2 (Integration Phase) requirements including generating invoices in XML format conforming to UBL 2.1 standard, embedding a cryptographic stamp (QR code containing seller name, VAT number, timestamp, invoice total, and VAT amount), and submitting invoices to the ZATCA Fatoora platform via API for clearance before dispatch to the customer. The system must handle ZATCA validation responses, store the clearance status and Invoice Counter Value (ICV), and prevent dispatch of invoices that have not received ZATCA clearance. Retry logic must handle temporary ZATCA platform unavailability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R009	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	India GST E-Invoicing and IRN Generation	For invoices generated by Indian billing entities, the system must integrate with the NIC (National Informatics Centre) e-invoicing portal to generate an Invoice Reference Number (IRN) and QR code for all B2B invoices exceeding the applicable turnover threshold. The system must submit the invoice payload in the prescribed JSON schema, receive and store the signed JWT containing the IRN, digitally signed QR code, and acknowledgement number. The e-invoice must be generated before or simultaneously with the PDF invoice. Cancellation of IRN within the 24-hour window must be supported. The system must handle NIC portal downtime with offline queuing and automatic retry mechanisms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R010	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Freight Charge Tariff Integration and Rate Application	The system must integrate with the tariff management module to automatically apply correct freight rates based on the trade lane (port pair), container type, commodity code, customer contract, and applicable INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, CFR, FCA, etc.). Rate application must consider tiered pricing based on volume commitments, spot rate agreements, and long-term contract rates. Where multiple rate agreements exist for the same customer and trade lane, the system must apply the most specific applicable rate following a defined precedence hierarchy: spot rate > contract rate > tariff rate. Any rate overrides must be logged with the approving user's identity and justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R011	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Hijri and Gregorian Dual-Date Display on Invoices	The system must display both Hijri (Islamic calendar) and Gregorian dates on invoices for KSA and Qatar billing entities, as required by local business practices and regulatory expectations. The invoice date, due date, and B/L date must each show the Hijri equivalent calculated using the Umm al-Qura calendar. The date format must be configurable per entity (e.g., DD/MM/YYYY for Gregorian, DD/MM/YYYY Hijri with Arabic month names). For UAE and India entities, Hijri dates must be optional but configurable. The conversion algorithm must account for the one-day variation that can occur in Hijri date computation across different regional conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R012	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Invoice Approval Workflow with AI Anomaly Detection	The system must implement a configurable approval workflow for invoices exceeding defined thresholds (by amount, variance from estimate, or customer risk category). The AI engine must automatically flag invoices with anomalies such as charges deviating more than a configurable percentage from historical averages for the same trade lane, unusual surcharge combinations, weight discrepancies between booking and actual, or charges applied to containers not listed on the B/L. Flagged invoices must be routed to designated approvers with a summary of detected anomalies. Approvers must be able to approve, reject, or return for correction with mandatory comments. Escalation timers must ensure invoices are not stalled in approval queues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R013	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Batch Invoice Generation for Vessel Discharge Operations	The system must support batch invoice generation triggered by vessel discharge completion at ports such as Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INJNP). Upon receiving discharge confirmation from the terminal operations module, the system must auto-generate invoices for all consignments on the vessel manifest, grouping charges by customer account. The batch process must handle vessels carrying 500+ containers efficiently, completing invoice generation within 15 minutes. Progress tracking must display the number of invoices generated, pending, and failed. Failed individual invoices must not block the batch, and error details must be logged for each failure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R014	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Offline Invoice Draft and Sync Capability	The system must support offline invoice drafting when network connectivity is unavailable, particularly relevant for operations at remote port facilities or during network outages. Users must be able to create invoice drafts with locally cached customer data, tariff rates, and exchange rates (last synced values). Once connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize drafted invoices with the central server, validate against current data, flag any discrepancies (e.g., exchange rate changes, tariff updates), and present a reconciliation summary to the user before finalizing. Conflict resolution must prioritize server-side data for rates and taxes while preserving user-entered notes and adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R015	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Revenue Recognition Tagging on Invoice Generation	The system must tag each invoice line item with the appropriate revenue recognition criteria in accordance with IFRS 15 (Revenue from Contracts with Customers). For ocean freight charges, revenue must be recognized over the voyage period (percentage of completion based on vessel transit). For THC and port charges, revenue must be recognized at the point of service delivery (container lift-on/lift-off). The system must generate the accounting entries for deferred revenue and recognized revenue simultaneously with invoice generation, posting to the appropriate GL accounts per the entity's chart of accounts. Multi-leg voyages involving transshipment must allocate revenue proportionally across each leg.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R016	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Customer Credit Limit Validation Before Invoice Finalization	The system must validate the customer's outstanding receivable balance plus the new invoice amount against their approved credit limit before allowing invoice finalization. If the credit limit would be exceeded, the system must block finalization and route the invoice to the credit control team for review. The validation must consider all outstanding invoices, unbilled accruals, and pending debit notes across all entities where the customer has accounts (consolidated group credit limit). For customers with entity-specific credit limits, validation must apply at the entity level. AI-driven credit risk scoring must provide a recommendation to approve or deny the credit limit override based on customer payment history, aging profile, and market conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-001-R017	FEAT-015-1-001	MOD-015	Invoice Template Customization per Entity and Customer	The system must support customizable invoice templates allowing each billing entity to configure their own branding (logo, colors, header/footer text), legal disclaimers, bank account details for payment, and terms and conditions. Customer-specific templates must be supported for key accounts requiring bespoke invoice formats. Templates must accommodate variable-length charge tables, multiple tax summary sections, and dual-language content without layout distortion. The template engine must support conditional sections that appear only when relevant (e.g., hazardous cargo declarations, reefer monitoring summaries, transshipment details). All templates must produce consistent output across PDF, HTML, and XML formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R001	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Debit Note Generation for Additional Charges	The system must support generation of debit notes to bill customers for additional charges not included in the original freight invoice. Common scenarios include detention charges for containers held beyond free days at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, demurrage charges at JNPT or Khalifa Port, container damage recovery, re-weighing fees due to VGM discrepancies, and storage charges. Each debit note must reference the original invoice number, B/L number, and container numbers. The system must auto-calculate detention/demurrage based on configurable free-day policies per port, container type, and customer agreement, applying the correct daily rate with automatic escalation tiers for extended holding periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R002	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Credit Note Generation for Overcharges and Service Failures	The system must support generation of credit notes to refund or adjust charges on previously issued freight invoices. Credit notes must be issued for overcharges identified during post-billing audit, service failures (e.g., missed vessel connection causing delay, container damage attributable to carrier), negotiated rate reductions applied retroactively, and cancellation of bookings after invoicing. Each credit note must reference the original invoice number and specific line items being credited. The system must enforce that a credit note cannot exceed the net outstanding amount of the referenced invoice, preventing over-crediting. Tax adjustments must be automatically computed to match the original invoice's tax treatment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R003	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Automated Debit Note Trigger from Detention/Demurrage Events	The system must automatically trigger debit note generation when container detention or demurrage events exceed the configured free-day threshold. Integration with the container tracking module must detect when a container's gate-out date at the destination port (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA, INJNP) exceeds the allotted free days. The AI engine must calculate the chargeable days, apply the correct tariff tier (e.g., days 1-5 at rate X, days 6-10 at rate Y, days 11+ at rate Z), factor in any customer-specific free-day extensions, and generate a draft debit note. Weekends and public holidays (per the port country's calendar, including Hijri-based holidays for Qatar/KSA/UAE) must be optionally excluded from the detention calculation based on policy configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R004	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Multi-Level Approval Workflow for Credit Notes	The system must enforce a multi-level approval workflow for credit notes based on configurable thresholds. Credit notes below a defined amount (e.g., USD 500 equivalent) may be auto-approved by the billing team. Credit notes between USD 500 and USD 5,000 must require manager approval. Credit notes exceeding USD 5,000 must require director-level approval. Threshold amounts must be configurable per entity and convertible to local currency equivalents (AED/QAR/SAR/INR) using prevailing exchange rates. The approval chain must support delegation during approver absence, with automatic escalation if not actioned within the configured SLA period. All approval actions must be recorded with timestamps, approver identity, and comments for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R005	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Tax Adjustment Notes for VAT/GST Compliance	Debit notes and credit notes must carry appropriate tax adjustments compliant with the jurisdiction's tax regulations. For UAE entities, tax credit notes must conform to FTA requirements including the mandatory reference to the original tax invoice number and TRN. For KSA entities, credit notes must be reported to ZATCA as per e-invoicing Phase 2 requirements with proper linkage to the original invoice's ICV. For Indian entities, credit notes must be issued within the time limit prescribed under GST law (30th November of the following financial year or date of filing annual return, whichever is earlier) and must adjust the GST liability in the return period of issuance. The system must warn users when approaching tax note issuance deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R006	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Separate Number Sequencing for Debit and Credit Notes	The system must maintain separate, sequential, gap-free number series for debit notes and credit notes, distinct from the freight invoice number series. Each entity must have its own debit note prefix (e.g., QA-DN-2026-00001) and credit note prefix (e.g., QA-CN-2026-00001). The numbering must comply with local regulatory requirements—for KSA, ZATCA mandates sequential numbering with no gaps; for India, GST regulations require distinct document series. The system must ensure no number collisions under concurrent issuance by multiple users across different entities. Annual reset must align with the entity's fiscal year, which may differ between entities (e.g., January for UAE, April for India, Hijri year for KSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R007	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Bulk Credit Note Processing for Rate Renegotiations	The system must support bulk credit note generation when a rate renegotiation retroactively reduces freight rates for a customer across multiple invoices. The user must be able to select a customer account, specify the date range and trade lanes affected, input the new agreed rates, and the system must automatically identify all applicable invoices, compute the rate differential for each line item, and generate individual credit notes referencing each original invoice. The bulk process must handle hundreds of invoices efficiently, provide a preview summary showing total credit amount per currency before execution, and require a single approval action for the entire batch while maintaining individual credit note documents for each original invoice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R008	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Automatic GL and Subledger Posting for Debit/Credit Notes	Upon approval and finalization of a debit note or credit note, the system must automatically generate and post the corresponding accounting entries to the general ledger and accounts receivable subledger. For debit notes, the system must debit the customer's receivable account and credit the appropriate revenue account (e.g., detention income, demurrage income). For credit notes, the system must credit the customer's receivable account and debit the revenue adjustment account. Tax entries must be reversed or adjusted proportionally. The posting must respect the entity's chart of accounts structure, cost center assignments, and intercompany accounting rules when the debit/credit note involves services rendered by a different entity than the billing entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R009	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Debit/Credit Note Linkage and Balance Tracking	The system must maintain a complete linkage chain between original invoices, debit notes, and credit notes, enabling full traceability of the billing lifecycle for each shipment. A dashboard view must display for any given invoice the total original amount, cumulative debit note additions, cumulative credit note reductions, and net outstanding balance. The system must prevent the total of credit notes from exceeding the original invoice amount plus any debit notes (net positive constraint) unless explicitly overridden by an authorized user with documented justification. This linkage must work across entities in cases where a credit note from one entity relates to an invoice from another entity within the same corporate group.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R010	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Customer Self-Service Portal for Debit/Credit Note Queries	The system must provide a customer-facing self-service portal allowing customers to view their debit notes and credit notes, download PDF copies in Arabic and English, raise disputes electronically, and track dispute resolution status. The portal must support authentication via SSO or credentials, display documents filtered by date range, vessel, B/L number, or document type. Customers must be able to upload supporting documents when raising disputes (e.g., photographs of container condition, gate-in receipts contradicting detention calculations). The system must route disputes to the appropriate billing team based on the originating entity and charge type, with SLA tracking for response time compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R011	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	AI-Powered Dispute Prediction and Preemptive Resolution	The AI engine must analyze patterns in historical debit note and credit note disputes to predict which newly generated debit notes are likely to be disputed by customers. The prediction model must consider factors including the customer's dispute history, charge type (detention/demurrage disputes being most common), amount relative to historical norms, port of discharge (certain ports having higher dispute rates), and time elapsed since container discharge. Debit notes with a high dispute probability score must be flagged for proactive review by the billing team before dispatch, with AI-generated recommendations for preemptive adjustments (e.g., waiving marginal free-day overruns for premium customers).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R012	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Handling for Cross-Entity Notes	When a debit note or credit note involves a different currency than the original invoice, the system must handle the currency conversion with full transparency. The exchange rate applied must be configurable to use either the rate from the original invoice date, the current date rate, or a manually specified rate with appropriate approval. The document must display amounts in both the original invoice currency and the note currency with the applied exchange rate clearly stated. For intercompany scenarios where a Qatar entity issues a credit note against a UAE entity's invoice, the system must generate the corresponding intercompany settlement entries in both QAR and AED with the applicable transfer pricing rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R013	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Automated Netting of Debit and Credit Notes Against Invoices	The system must support automatic and manual netting of outstanding debit notes and credit notes against open invoices for the same customer. The netting process must match documents by currency, entity, and aging priority (oldest first). When a credit note is approved, the system must suggest netting against the oldest outstanding invoice for the same customer and currency, subject to user confirmation. Cross-currency netting must be supported with exchange rate conversion at the netting date rate. The netting transaction must generate a netting statement document for the customer's records and update the accounts receivable subledger to reflect the reduced outstanding balance for both the invoice and the credit/debit note.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R014	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Regulatory Reporting Integration for Debit/Credit Notes	The system must include debit notes and credit notes in all applicable regulatory reports. For UAE entities, notes must appear in the FTA VAT return in the adjustment section. For KSA entities, notes must be transmitted to ZATCA as linked documents to the original invoice via the e-invoicing API. For Indian entities, credit notes must be included in GSTR-1 filing with proper linkage to original invoice details and must be reflected in GSTR-3B monthly returns. For Qatar entities, notes must be included in any applicable customs or tax reporting. The system must generate pre-filing reconciliation reports showing all notes issued in the reporting period with their tax impact, allowing the finance team to verify before submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R015	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Audit Trail and Document Versioning for Notes	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every debit note and credit note, recording creation, each approval step, amendments, dispatch events, customer acknowledgement, dispute actions, and netting transactions. Each state change must capture the user identity, timestamp (in both Gregorian and Hijri for KSA/Qatar), IP address, and reason/comments. Document versioning must preserve all prior versions when a note is amended, with the ability to compare any two versions side by side showing field-level differences. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident, supporting compliance requirements for financial document retention mandated by local regulations (minimum 5 years for UAE, 6 years for India, as prescribed by ZATCA for KSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R016	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Configurable Reason Codes for Debit and Credit Notes	The system must enforce selection of a standardized reason code when creating debit or credit notes, ensuring consistent categorization for reporting and analytics. Reason codes must be configurable per entity and must include at minimum: detention charges, demurrage charges, rate correction, weight discrepancy, service failure, booking cancellation, cargo damage claim, documentation error, duplicate billing, and negotiated discount. Each reason code must map to a specific GL account and revenue category for accurate financial reporting. The AI engine must suggest the most probable reason code based on the context (e.g., automatically suggesting 'detention' when the note is triggered by a container overstay event). Bilingual labels in Arabic and English must be maintained for each reason code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-002-R017	FEAT-015-1-002	MOD-015	Threshold-Based Automatic Write-Off for Minor Credit Notes	The system must support configurable threshold-based automatic write-off for minor credit amounts that are not economically viable to process as formal credit notes. When a billing discrepancy falls below the configured threshold (e.g., USD 10 or local currency equivalent), the system must offer the option to write off the amount directly to a designated expense account rather than generating a credit note. The threshold must be configurable per entity and per currency. All write-offs must be logged with the original discrepancy details, auto-tagged with reason codes, and included in a monthly write-off summary report for management review. The cumulative write-off per customer per period must be monitored, and the system must alert when cumulative write-offs exceed a configurable ceiling to prevent abuse.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R001	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Invoice Amendment Request Initiation and Reason Capture	The system must provide a structured process to initiate invoice amendments, requiring the user to specify the original invoice number, select specific line items to amend, and provide a mandatory reason code and free-text justification. Permitted amendment reasons must include rate correction, quantity adjustment, charge reclassification, port code correction, container number correction, B/L reference update, customer entity change, and tax rate correction. The system must distinguish between minor amendments (non-financial, such as typographical corrections to names or addresses) and material amendments (affecting amounts, tax, or revenue recognition). Material amendments must trigger the full approval workflow, while minor amendments may follow an expedited approval path configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R002	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Automatic Impact Analysis Before Amendment Approval	Before an amendment is approved, the system must automatically calculate and present a comprehensive impact analysis showing: the financial difference between the original and amended invoice (per line item and total), the tax impact (additional VAT/GST or refund due), the effect on revenue recognition entries already posted, the impact on the customer's outstanding receivable balance and credit limit utilization, and any downstream effects on intercompany settlements if the invoice involves cross-entity charges. The impact analysis must be presented in all relevant currencies, displaying both the original and amended amounts with exchange rate details. This analysis must be attached to the approval workflow so approvers can make informed decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R003	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Amendment Versioning with Full Change History	The system must maintain a complete version history for every amended invoice, preserving the original document and each subsequent amendment as separate, immutable versions. Each version must be individually retrievable and printable. The system must support a visual diff view showing field-by-field changes between any two versions, highlighting modified values in a color-coded format. The version history must record the amendment initiator, approver(s), timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, reason codes, and justification text. A maximum amendment count may be configured per entity (e.g., maximum 3 amendments) after which the invoice must be cancelled and reissued as a new document to maintain audit integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R004	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Regulatory-Compliant Reissue for KSA ZATCA E-Invoicing	When amending an invoice originally cleared through ZATCA's Fatoora platform, the system must comply with ZATCA amendment regulations. If the amendment is material (affecting the invoice amount or VAT), the system must issue a credit note against the original invoice and generate a new invoice with the corrected details, both submitted to ZATCA for clearance. The original invoice's ZATCA clearance status must be preserved, and the credit note must reference the original Invoice Counter Value (ICV). The new invoice must receive its own ICV and QR code. For non-material amendments, the system must follow ZATCA's prescribed process for corrections without requiring a full cancel-and-reissue cycle, if such a process is available in the current ZATCA specifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R005	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	GST-Compliant Amendment Process for Indian Entity	For invoices issued by the Indian billing entity, the amendment process must comply with GST regulations governing invoice amendments. Amendments to B2B invoices must be reported in GSTR-1 under the amendment tables (Table 9A for invoice amendments, Table 9B for credit/debit note amendments). The system must track whether the original invoice was reported in a filed GSTR-1 return and determine the correct reporting period for the amendment. If the amendment crosses a financial year boundary, the system must validate that the amendment is filed before the statutory deadline (30th November of the following financial year or the date of filing annual return). The system must prevent amendments that would violate GST input tax credit matching rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R006	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Cancel-and-Reissue Workflow for Major Corrections	When an amendment is too complex for in-place correction (e.g., change of customer entity, reversal of entire charge categories, or change of billing currency), the system must support a cancel-and-reissue workflow. This process must generate a full credit note against the original invoice, void the original invoice with a status of 'Cancelled – Reissued', and generate a new invoice with the corrected details linked to the original via the credit note reference chain. The new invoice must carry its own sequential number and the original invoice number must be referenced in a 'Supersedes' field. All documents in the chain (original, credit note, reissued invoice) must be viewable together in a transaction summary screen.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R007	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Automatic Accounting Reversal and Re-Posting on Amendment	Upon finalization of an invoice amendment, the system must automatically reverse the original invoice's accounting entries and post new entries reflecting the amended amounts. The reversal must be dated either on the original posting date (if the amendment occurs within the same accounting period) or on the amendment date (if the original period is closed), based on entity-specific accounting policy configuration. Revenue recognition adjustments must be recalculated based on the amended amounts and voyage completion status. The system must generate a journal entry summary showing the net effect of the amendment on each affected GL account, cost center, and intercompany balance. The posting must be atomic—either all entries succeed or none are posted—to prevent partial state.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R008	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Customer Notification of Invoice Amendment	The system must automatically notify the customer when an invoice is amended, sending the amended invoice document through the same dispatch channel used for the original (email, EDI, portal, or API). The notification must clearly indicate that the document is an amended version, reference the original invoice number and date, highlight the changes made, and state the revised total amount and payment due date. For email notifications, the subject line must include an '[AMENDED]' prefix in both English and Arabic. The customer portal must display amended invoices with a visual indicator distinguishing them from original invoices, and must allow the customer to view the original document alongside the amendment for comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R009	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Amendment Lock Period and Fiscal Period Constraints	The system must enforce configurable lock periods beyond which invoices cannot be amended through the standard workflow. Lock periods must be definable per entity based on fiscal period close dates, regulatory filing deadlines, and audit requirements. For example, once the UAE FTA VAT return for a period is filed, invoices within that period must be locked from amendment unless a special override is granted by the finance controller. Similarly, after GSTR-1 filing for Indian entities, amendments to reported invoices must follow the statutory amendment process. The system must clearly display the lock status of an invoice and the reason (fiscal period closed, tax return filed, audit freeze, etc.) when an amendment is attempted on a locked invoice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R010	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	AI-Assisted Amendment Recommendation Engine	The AI engine must proactively identify invoices that may require amendment by continuously monitoring for discrepancy triggers such as: updated vessel manifests showing different container counts than invoiced, VGM corrections received after invoicing, exchange rate corrections from treasury, tariff rate updates applied retroactively, and customer master data changes (e.g., VAT registration number update). When a potential amendment trigger is detected, the system must generate an amendment recommendation with the specific changes needed, estimated financial impact, and suggested priority level. Recommendations must be presented in a queue for the billing team to review, accept, or dismiss. Accepted recommendations must pre-populate the amendment form to streamline processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R011	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Partial Amendment for Multi-Container Invoices	The system must support partial amendments to invoices containing multiple containers or line items, allowing specific line items to be amended while others remain unchanged. The amended invoice must clearly show which line items have been modified (with a visual indicator such as an asterisk or highlight) and which remain at their original values. The total invoice amount must be recalculated to reflect only the amended line items' changes. Tax must be recomputed on the entire invoice to ensure accuracy of the total tax amount. The system must prevent amendments that would result in negative line item amounts unless the line item is being fully credited, in which case it must guide the user to issue a credit note instead of amending.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R012	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Amendment Audit Report and Compliance Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive amendment audit report showing all invoice amendments within a configurable date range, filterable by entity, customer, amendment type (minor/material), reason code, approver, and financial impact range. The report must include the original invoice amount, amended amount, net change, and current payment status. A compliance dashboard must display key metrics including amendment frequency rate (as a percentage of total invoices), average amendment processing time, top amendment reason codes, and amendments pending approval beyond SLA. The dashboard must highlight trends and anomalies—for example, a sudden increase in amendment rates for a specific trade lane or customer that may indicate systematic billing errors requiring root cause analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R006	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma DA Approval Workflow	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow for proforma DAs before they are sent to port agents or principals. The workflow must support configurable approval chains based on estimated DA total amount thresholds (e.g., below USD 25,000 requires single approval, above USD 25,000 requires dual approval, above USD 100,000 requires management sign-off). Each approval step must capture the approver identity, timestamp, comments, and decision (approve, reject, request revision). Rejected proformas must be returned to the originator with mandatory rejection reasons and the full audit trail must be preserved across all revisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R013	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Amendment Impact on Payment Allocations	When an invoice that has partial or full payment allocated is amended, the system must handle the payment reallocation automatically. If the amendment increases the invoice amount, the existing payment allocation must remain and the additional amount must become a new outstanding balance. If the amendment decreases the invoice amount, the system must detect overpayment scenarios and offer options: generate a credit note for the overpaid amount, apply the excess to another outstanding invoice for the same customer, or initiate a refund process. The system must prevent finalization of an amendment that would create an inconsistent state between the invoice amount and allocated payments. All payment reallocation actions must be logged in both the invoice and payment audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R014	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Batch Amendment Processing for Systematic Errors	The system must support batch amendment processing when a systematic error affects multiple invoices—for example, an incorrect THC rate applied to all invoices for Jebel Ali port during a specific period, or a wrong exchange rate used for all QAR-denominated invoices on a particular date. The user must be able to define the error criteria (date range, port, charge type, rate value), preview all affected invoices with the proposed corrections and aggregate financial impact, and execute the batch amendment with a single approval action. The batch process must generate individual amendment documents for each invoice while maintaining a batch reference linking them together. The batch must be reversible as a unit if errors are discovered post-execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R015	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Digital Signature and Tamper Protection for Amended Invoices	All amended invoices and their associated version history must be digitally signed to ensure document integrity and non-repudiation. The digital signature must cover the complete invoice content including line items, amounts, tax computations, and amendment metadata. Any tampering with a finalized amended invoice must be detectable through signature verification failure. For KSA entities, the digital signature must comply with ZATCA's cryptographic requirements. For Indian entities, the amended invoice must carry the updated IRN and digitally signed QR code from the NIC e-invoicing portal. The system must support certificate management for signing keys, including key rotation, expiry alerts, and revocation handling across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R016	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Reissued Invoice Payment Terms Recalculation	When an invoice is reissued through the cancel-and-reissue process, the system must recalculate payment terms based on configurable policy: either retain the original invoice's due date (preserving the customer's original payment timeline), reset the payment terms from the reissue date (granting a fresh credit period), or apply a prorated approach where the remaining credit period from the original invoice is applied to the reissue date. The policy must be configurable per entity and overridable per customer. The system must account for payment terms that reference Hijri calendar durations (e.g., 'Net 30 Hijri days') when applicable for KSA and Qatar entities. Early payment discount terms, if applicable, must also be recalculated based on the chosen payment term recalculation policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-003-R017	FEAT-015-1-003	MOD-015	Amendment Restriction Based on Collection Status	The system must enforce amendment restrictions based on the invoice's collection status. Invoices that are in active legal collection, referred to a collection agency, or subject to a court order must be locked from amendment unless authorized by the legal department. Invoices with pending bank guarantee or letter of credit claims must require treasury department approval before amendment. The system must check for liens, holds, or encumbrances on the invoice from other modules (e.g., cargo release hold from operations, customs hold) and prevent amendments that would conflict with these holds. A clear explanation must be displayed to the user when an amendment is blocked, identifying the specific hold or restriction and the department or process that must clear it.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R001	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Invoice Creation from Booking Quotation	The system must support creation of proforma invoices directly from accepted booking quotations, automatically populating shipment details including origin port (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA), destination port (e.g., INJNP, AEKLP), container type and quantity (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), estimated vessel and voyage, commodity description, gross weight, and all applicable charges from the tariff module. The proforma must itemize ocean freight, THC at origin and destination, documentation fees, BAF, CAF, and any applicable surcharges. The system must apply the quoted exchange rate and lock it for the proforma's validity period. INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, CFR, FCA, EXW, etc.) must be prominently displayed as they determine the charge allocation between shipper and consignee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R002	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Validity Period and Expiry Management	Each proforma invoice must carry a configurable validity period (e.g., 7, 14, or 30 days) after which it automatically expires if not converted to a confirmed booking and freight invoice. The validity period must be configurable per entity, customer, and trade lane. The system must send automated reminders to both the sales team and the customer at configurable intervals before expiry (e.g., 3 days before, 1 day before). Upon expiry, the proforma status must change to 'Expired' and any associated rate locks or tentative booking holds must be released. Expired proformas must be re-quotable with a single action that creates a new proforma with updated rates and a new validity period, referencing the original proforma number for traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R003	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Proforma with Rate Lock Mechanism	The system must support proforma invoice generation in any of the operational currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with an exchange rate lock mechanism that guarantees the quoted rate for the proforma's validity period. The rate lock must reserve the exchange rate at the time of proforma creation or at a user-specified rate, sourced from the treasury module. If the proforma is converted to a freight invoice after the rate lock expires, the system must flag the rate discrepancy and require user confirmation to proceed with either the original locked rate (if approved by treasury) or the current prevailing rate. The proforma must display a clear statement indicating whether the quoted amount is subject to exchange rate fluctuation or is rate-locked, as this is critical for customer decision-making on high-value shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R004	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma-to-Invoice Conversion Workflow	The system must support seamless conversion of approved proforma invoices to freight invoices upon booking confirmation and shipment execution. The conversion process must carry forward all charge details, customer information, and shipment references from the proforma to the invoice. Differences between proforma estimates and actual shipment data must be automatically identified and highlighted—for example, actual container count differing from estimated, actual weight differing from declared, or additional surcharges triggered by actual vessel allocation. The user must review and accept these differences before the invoice is generated. Partial conversion must be supported when only some containers from a multi-container proforma are actually shipped, generating an invoice for the shipped containers and optionally retaining the proforma for the remainder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R005	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Invoice Number Series and Distinction from Tax Invoices	The system must maintain a separate number series for proforma invoices, clearly distinguished from tax invoice numbers to prevent confusion in financial and tax reporting. The proforma number format must include a 'PI' identifier (e.g., QA-PI-2026-00001 for Qatar, AE-PI-2026-00001 for UAE). The document must be prominently watermarked as 'PROFORMA INVOICE' in both English and Arabic to prevent misuse as a tax document. Proforma invoices must not appear in VAT/GST return reports or accounts receivable aging reports, as they represent pre-billing estimates, not tax obligations. The system must enforce that proforma invoices cannot be used for customs clearance in jurisdictions where only tax invoices are accepted (e.g., India customs requires a commercial/tax invoice).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R006	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Advance Payment Collection Against Proforma	The system must support recording advance payments collected against proforma invoices, particularly for new customers or high-risk accounts where prepayment is required before shipment execution. The advance payment must be linked to the proforma number and held in a customer advance/deposit account in the GL. Upon conversion of the proforma to a freight invoice, the advance payment must be automatically applied against the invoice, reducing the outstanding receivable. If the advance payment exceeds the final invoice amount (due to rate adjustments or quantity changes), the system must identify the excess and offer options to refund, hold as credit balance, or apply to the next invoice. The system must generate a payment receipt for the advance in the appropriate format for each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R007	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Revision and Version Control	The system must support revision of proforma invoices when customer negotiations require rate adjustments, charge modifications, or shipment detail changes before final acceptance. Each revision must create a new version of the proforma while preserving all previous versions for reference. The revision number must be appended to the proforma number (e.g., QA-PI-2026-00001-R1, QA-PI-2026-00001-R2). The system must display a comparison view showing the differences between the current and previous versions. A maximum configurable number of revisions must be enforced (e.g., 5 revisions) after which a new proforma must be created. Each revision must reset the validity period. The AI engine must track negotiation patterns to recommend optimal pricing that balances win rate and margin targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R008	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Bilingual Proforma Document with Arabic and English Content	Proforma invoices must be rendered in bilingual format supporting both Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR) text, consistent with the freight invoice rendering standards. All charge descriptions, terms and conditions, customer details, port names, and legal disclaimers must appear in both languages. The proforma must include entity-specific bank details for advance payment in both Arabic and English, with IBAN and SWIFT codes formatted per banking standards. The Arabic content must use proper shipping terminology (e.g., بوليصة الشحن for Bill of Lading, رسوم مناولة المحطة for Terminal Handling Charges). The template must handle variable-length content gracefully, preventing page break issues when Arabic text expansion causes content to exceed the English equivalent's space allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R009	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Approval Workflow with Margin Validation	The system must enforce an approval workflow for proforma invoices that validates profitability before issuance. The system must calculate the estimated margin for the shipment by comparing the proforma charges against estimated costs (vessel slot cost, port charges, inland transportation, agent commissions). Proformas with margins below a configurable threshold (e.g., less than 10%) must be escalated to the commercial manager for approval. Negative-margin proformas must require director-level approval with documented strategic justification (e.g., market penetration, volume commitment, strategic customer relationship). The approval screen must display margin analysis per container, per trade lane, and benchmarked against similar recent bookings. Approved below-threshold proformas must be tagged for post-shipment profitability review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R010	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma-Based Customs and Letter of Credit Documentation	The system must support generation of proforma invoices formatted specifically for customs pre-clearance and letter of credit (LC) application purposes. For LC applications, the proforma must include fields required by banks such as beneficiary details, LC reference number placeholder, goods description matching HS code classifications, and total amount in both figures and words (in English and Arabic). For customs pre-clearance in Qatar (QA Customs) and UAE (Dubai Customs), the proforma must include the HS tariff codes, country of origin, declared value breakdown per container, and consignee details in the format required by the respective customs authority. The system must validate that all mandatory customs fields are populated before allowing the proforma to be dispatched for customs or LC purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R011	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Analytics and Conversion Rate Tracking	The system must track and report on proforma invoice conversion metrics to support commercial performance analysis. Key metrics must include: proforma-to-booking conversion rate (by entity, trade lane, customer, and sales representative), average time from proforma issuance to conversion, revenue value of converted versus expired proformas, most common reasons for non-conversion (tracked via a mandatory reason code when a proforma expires or is cancelled), and win/loss analysis by trade lane and container type. The AI engine must identify patterns in conversion data to recommend optimal pricing strategies—for example, detecting that proformas for the QAHMD-INJNP trade lane have a 20% higher conversion rate when priced within a specific range. Dashboard visualizations must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R012	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Templates for Recurring Shipments	The system must support creation of proforma templates for customers with recurring shipment patterns, enabling rapid proforma generation for standard trade lane, container type, and charge configurations. Templates must be linkable to customer contracts and automatically apply contracted rates, agreed surcharge waivers, and customer-specific payment terms. When a template is used, the system must check for any rate changes since the template was last updated and alert the user if the template rates differ from current tariff or contract rates. Templates must support seasonality adjustments where certain surcharges (e.g., peak season surcharge) are automatically added or removed based on the shipment date. Each entity must be able to maintain its own set of templates, and templates must be shareable across entities with appropriate rate and currency localization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R013	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Digital Proforma Dispatch and Customer Acceptance Tracking	The system must support electronic dispatch of proforma invoices via email, customer portal, and API integration with customer procurement systems. The dispatch must track delivery status and customer engagement (opened, viewed, downloaded). The customer portal must enable digital acceptance of proforma invoices with an electronic signature or acceptance button, triggering an automatic workflow to convert the proforma to a booking request. The system must support a customer counter-offer workflow where the customer can respond with requested rate modifications through the portal, which are routed to the commercial team for review. All customer interactions with the proforma (view, download, accept, counter-offer, reject) must be logged with timestamps for sales pipeline reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R014	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma for Intercompany and Cross-Entity Shipments	The system must support proforma invoice generation for intercompany shipments where the booking entity differs from the billing entity—for example, a booking made through the Qatar entity for a shipment originating from Jebel Ali (UAE) and destined for JNPT (India). The proforma must apply transfer pricing rules to determine the inter-entity charge allocation, showing the customer-facing charges and the internal cost allocation separately. The system must validate that intercompany rates comply with arm's-length pricing requirements for tax purposes across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. Upon proforma-to-invoice conversion, the system must automatically generate intercompany billing documents between the involved entities with proper currency conversion at the configured transfer pricing exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R015	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Offline Proforma Creation and Synchronization	The system must support offline proforma invoice creation for sales teams operating without reliable network connectivity, such as during customer visits or at remote port locations. The offline module must cache the latest tariff rates, customer master data, exchange rates, and proforma templates locally. Users must be able to create, revise, and preview proforma invoices offline with full charge calculation capabilities. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize offline proformas with the central server, validating against current rates and flagging discrepancies. If tariff rates changed during the offline period, the system must present a reconciliation screen showing the offline rate versus current rate per charge line, allowing the user to accept current rates or retain offline rates with manager approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R016	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Cancellation and Reason Tracking	The system must support controlled cancellation of proforma invoices with mandatory reason code selection and optional comments. Cancellation reason codes must include: customer declined, rate not competitive, shipment rerouted, booking cancelled by customer, duplicate proforma, superseded by revision, and market conditions changed. Cancelled proformas must retain full visibility in the system with a 'Cancelled' status watermark and must not be convertible to invoices. If an advance payment was received against the proforma, the cancellation process must trigger a refund workflow or reallocation to another proforma/invoice. The system must track cancellation patterns and the AI engine must provide insights on cancellation reasons by trade lane, customer segment, and competitor pricing intelligence where available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R017	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Validity Extension and Re-Quotation Workflow	The system must support extending the validity period of a proforma invoice beyond its original expiry date when customer negotiations are ongoing. The extension must require approval if rates have changed since the original proforma issuance, as extending at outdated rates may impact profitability. The approval screen must show the current tariff rate versus the proforma rate and the margin impact of honoring the original rate for the extended period. A maximum number of extensions must be configurable (e.g., 2 extensions of 7 days each). After the maximum extensions are exhausted, the proforma must either be converted or a re-quotation must be initiated, which creates a new proforma with current rates referencing the original proforma for tracking the customer relationship lifecycle. Re-quotations must be flagged in analytics to measure the impact of rate changes on conversion probability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-1-004-R018	FEAT-015-1-004	MOD-015	Proforma Integration with CRM and Sales Pipeline	The system must integrate proforma invoice data with the CRM module to provide a unified view of the sales pipeline. Each proforma must be linked to a CRM opportunity record, updating the opportunity stage automatically (e.g., 'Quoted' when proforma is issued, 'Negotiating' when counter-offer is received, 'Won' when converted, 'Lost' when expired/cancelled). The integration must enable sales managers to view proforma values in pipeline reports segmented by trade lane, customer segment, entity, and expected conversion month. Revenue forecasting must incorporate proforma values weighted by historical conversion probability for each customer and trade lane combination. The AI engine must recommend follow-up actions for proformas approaching expiry based on customer engagement signals (e.g., whether the proforma PDF was opened, how many times the portal was accessed).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R001	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	IFRS 15 Five-Step Revenue Recognition Model	The system must implement the full IFRS 15 five-step revenue recognition model for every freight contract: (1) identify the contract with the customer based on the booking confirmation and Bill of Lading, (2) identify distinct performance obligations such as ocean freight, terminal handling at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, documentation fees, and container detention, (3) determine the transaction price in the contracted currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), (4) allocate the transaction price to each performance obligation using standalone selling prices, and (5) recognize revenue as each obligation is satisfied over time or at a point in time. The engine must generate journal entries automatically per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R002	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Percentage-of-Completion Voyage Revenue Accrual	The system must calculate voyage percentage-of-completion based on vessel GPS position data, comparing distance sailed against total planned voyage distance for routes such as QAHMD (Hamad Port) to INJNP (JNPT) or AEJEA (Jebel Ali) toSAKAC (King Abdullah Port). Revenue accrual journal entries must be generated daily at end-of-day processing for each active voyage, proportionally recognizing ocean freight revenue across the voyage duration. The calculation must handle transshipment legs separately, treating each leg as a distinct performance obligation when the Bill of Lading specifies through-transport. Multi-currency conversion must use the daily exchange rate from the Central Bank feed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R003	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Deferred Revenue Liability Tracking	The system must maintain a deferred revenue liability ledger for all prepaid freight collections where the performance obligation has not yet been satisfied. When a customer pays an advance deposit or full freight prepayment for a booking at Khalifa Port or JNPT, the system must credit a deferred revenue account rather than recognizing income. As the vessel departs and voyage milestones are achieved (vessel departure, canal transit, port arrival, cargo discharge), the system must systematically release deferred revenue to earned revenue. Each release must reference the original invoice number, booking reference, and BL number. The deferred revenue balance must be reportable by customer, trade lane, and aging bucket in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R004	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Multi-Entity Intercompany Revenue Elimination	For shipments involving multiple group entities (e.g., Qatar entity books origin charges at Hamad Port while UAE entity books destination charges at Jebel Ali), the system must automatically generate intercompany revenue and cost elimination entries during the consolidation process. The elimination logic must identify matching intercompany invoices by booking reference and container number, reconcile amounts across currencies using the group-approved transfer pricing rate, and post elimination journals to the consolidation entity. Any unmatched intercompany balances exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 100) must be flagged for manual review. The system must support both simultaneous and sequential closing across entities operating in different fiscal periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R005	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Period-End Revenue Cut-Off Processing	The system must enforce strict revenue cut-off at each period-end (monthly, quarterly, annually) ensuring that revenue is recognized in the correct accounting period based on the actual date of service delivery, not the invoice date. For voyages in transit at period-end, the system must calculate accrued revenue up to midnight of the closing date using the latest available vessel position. The cut-off engine must handle time zone differences between Qatar (AST, UTC+3), UAE (GST, UTC+4), KSA (AST, UTC+3), and India (IST, UTC+5:30). A cut-off report must list all adjusting entries with full audit trail showing the vessel position, percentage completion, and exchange rate used at the cut-off moment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R006	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Variable Consideration and Constraint Estimation	The system must estimate variable consideration components of freight revenue such as demurrage, detention, port congestion surcharges, and volume rebates using the expected value or most likely amount method as configured per customer contract. For container detention at Hamad Port or JNPT where free days vary by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC), the system must calculate probable detention revenue based on historical return patterns and constrain the estimate to amounts highly probable of not reversing. The AI engine must analyze the last 12 months of detention data per trade lane to refine variable consideration estimates. All estimates must be reviewed and adjustable at period-end before final revenue posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R007	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Contract Modification Revenue Adjustment	When a freight contract is modified after booking confirmation (e.g., change of destination port from Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port, addition of reefer surcharge for temperature-controlled containers, or amendment of INCOTERMS from CIF to FOB), the system must evaluate whether the modification creates a separate contract or modifies the existing one per IFRS 15 guidance. For modifications treated as separate contracts, new performance obligations and pricing must be established. For cumulative catch-up modifications, the system must recalculate revenue recognized to date and post an adjusting entry. All modifications must maintain a complete audit trail linking the original booking, amendment request, and revised revenue schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R008	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Revenue Recognition for Bundled Services	The system must unbundle freight service packages that combine ocean transport with ancillary services such as customs brokerage at JNPT, inland trucking from Jebel Ali to Dubai warehouses, container fumigation at Hamad Port, or cargo insurance. Each bundled component must be identified as a separate performance obligation if it is distinct, with revenue allocated based on relative standalone selling prices derived from the published tariff or market rate database. The system must maintain a standalone selling price master updated quarterly per trade lane and service type. When standalone prices are not directly observable, the system must support the adjusted market assessment, expected cost plus margin, or residual approaches for price allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R009	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Lease Component Separation for Container Rentals	For freight arrangements that include an embedded lease component (such as long-term container leasing or dedicated equipment agreements for SOC — Shipper Owned Container — operations), the system must separate the lease component from the service component per IFRS 16 and recognize each under the applicable standard. The lease component related to container rental must be recognized on a straight-line basis over the lease term, while the freight service component follows IFRS 15 performance obligation completion. The system must track container asset IDs, lease start and end dates, daily rental rates in the contracted currency, and generate separate journal entries for lease revenue versus freight service revenue for each entity in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R010	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	AI-Driven Revenue Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously monitor revenue recognition patterns and flag anomalies such as unusually high accruals relative to voyage progress, revenue recognized on cancelled or rolled bookings, duplicate accrual entries for the same container on the same voyage, or sudden spikes in deferred revenue drawdown. The anomaly detection model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical revenue data across all four operating entities and must account for seasonal patterns (e.g., peak season pre-Ramadan cargo rush, Indian festival season volumes through JNPT). Detected anomalies must generate alerts to the revenue accounting team with a severity score, recommended corrective action, and supporting data visualization. False positive rates must remain below 5% after the initial 3-month calibration period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R011	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Revenue Translation and Reporting	The system must support revenue recognition in the transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) and translate recognized revenue into each entity's functional currency and the group reporting currency using IAS 21 rules. Revenue transactions must be translated at the exchange rate on the date of recognition (spot rate), while period-end balances of deferred and accrued revenue must be retranslated at the closing rate with the resulting exchange difference posted to other comprehensive income. The system must source exchange rates automatically from the Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, and RBI feeds. Manual rate override must be restricted to authorized treasury users with full audit logging of any override including justification text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R012	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Revenue Disclosure and Footnote Data Generation	The system must generate all data required for IFRS 15 revenue disclosure notes including: disaggregation of revenue by geography (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), service type (ocean freight, THC, documentation, detention, demurrage), timing of recognition (over time vs. point in time), and contract balances (receivables, contract assets, contract liabilities). The disclosure engine must produce comparative period data and reconciliation of opening to closing contract balances. Output must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic reports. All monetary amounts must display in the entity's reporting currency with the option to drill down to transaction-currency detail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R013	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Hijri Calendar Revenue Period Alignment	The system must support revenue reporting aligned to the Hijri (Islamic) calendar for KSA and Qatar regulatory submissions that require Hijri-dated financial statements. Revenue accruals and deferrals must be recalculable for Hijri monthly and annual periods, which do not align with Gregorian months. The system must maintain dual-calendar period mapping tables updated annually, handle Hijri year-end cut-off processing independently from Gregorian year-end, and produce revenue schedules showing recognized and deferred amounts per Hijri month. The Hijri period close must trigger the same cut-off validations, voyage accrual calculations, and intercompany eliminations as the Gregorian close. Users must be able to toggle between Hijri and Gregorian views in all revenue dashboards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R014	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Offline Revenue Accrual Calculation and Sync	The system must support offline revenue accrual calculations for field offices or vessel-based finance teams operating without reliable internet connectivity at remote ports or during canal transits. The offline module must cache the latest voyage data, exchange rates, and contract terms, allowing users to run provisional accrual calculations locally. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline-generated accrual entries with the central ledger, detect and resolve conflicts (e.g., voyage data updated centrally during the offline period), and flag any entries where the exchange rate used offline deviates by more than 1% from the central rate. Conflict resolution must present both versions to the user for manual adjudication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R015	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Revenue Audit Trail and Restatement Support	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for every revenue recognition event, capturing the triggering transaction (invoice, voyage milestone, period-end close), the accounting rule applied (IFRS 15 step reference), the user or automated process that initiated the entry, timestamp in UTC, and the resulting journal entry details. In the event of a revenue restatement due to error correction or accounting policy change, the system must support retrospective restatement by recalculating revenue for affected prior periods, generating adjustment entries, and producing a restatement impact report showing the effect on each period's revenue, profit, and contract balances. Restated periods must be clearly marked in all reports with the restatement date and authorizing officer reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-001-R016	FEAT-015-2-001	MOD-015	Automated Revenue Reconciliation to Sub-Ledgers	The system must perform daily automated reconciliation between the revenue recognition sub-ledger and the general ledger, accounts receivable sub-ledger, and billing system. Any discrepancy exceeding a configurable tolerance (default USD 1.00 equivalent per entity currency) must generate an exception report with the affected booking references, container numbers, invoice numbers, and the variance amount in both transaction and functional currencies. The reconciliation must cover all revenue accounts including ocean freight, THC at Hamad Port and JNPT, documentation fees, detention and demurrage, and intercompany revenue. Unresolved exceptions older than 3 business days must be escalated automatically to the Chief Accountant with a summary of attempted auto-resolution actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R001	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Lodgment and Registration	The system must allow customers, agents, and internal users to lodge invoice disputes through multiple channels: web portal, email integration, API, and manual entry. Each dispute must be assigned a unique dispute reference number with the format DIS-{entity}-{YYYYMM}-{sequence} (e.g., DIS-QA-202602-00145). The registration form must capture the disputed invoice number, disputed line items, disputed amount in original invoice currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), dispute category (rate variance, quantity error, surcharge disagreement, duplicate billing, service not rendered, documentation error), supporting document uploads, and free-text description in English or Arabic. The system must validate that the referenced invoice exists and is in a disputable state (not already fully credited or written off).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R002	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	AI-Powered Dispute Classification and Routing	The AI engine must automatically classify incoming disputes into predefined categories and sub-categories based on natural language analysis of the dispute description, historical dispute patterns for the same customer and trade lane, and the invoice line items in question. The classification must achieve at least 90% accuracy after initial training on 6 months of historical dispute data. Based on classification, the system must auto-route disputes to the responsible department: rate disputes to the commercial team, quantity disputes to operations at the relevant port (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT), surcharge disputes to the pricing team, and documentation errors to the shipping documentation unit. Routing rules must be configurable per entity and overridable by dispute managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R012	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Pricing Performance Analytics	Comprehensive analytics measuring AI pricing engine performance against key commercial KPIs.	must_have			["Dashboard tracks quote-to-booking conversion rate for AI-priced vs. manually-priced quotes", "Revenue lift from AI pricing is measured against control group baseline per entity and trade lane", "Average discount from list rate is tracked showing trend of AI pricing discipline over time", "Pricing response time SLA compliance is reported (target: 95% of spot quotes within 60 seconds)", "Monthly pricing performance report is generated per entity in local language (Arabic/English) with Hijri date option"]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R003	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Investigation Workflow Stages	The system must enforce a configurable multi-stage dispute resolution workflow with a minimum of: (1) Registered — dispute logged and acknowledged, (2) Under Investigation — assigned to handler with investigation deadline, (3) Pending Customer Response — awaiting additional information from the disputing party, (4) Resolution Proposed — internal resolution recommendation prepared, (5) Approved/Rejected — management decision on proposed resolution, (6) Closed — financial adjustment posted or dispute rejected with reason. Each stage transition must require mandatory fields (e.g., investigation notes, supporting evidence, approver comments) and enforce role-based access so that only authorized users can advance disputes. SLA timers must track time spent in each stage with configurable escalation thresholds per entity and dispute category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R004	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute SLA Management and Escalation	The system must enforce configurable SLA targets for dispute resolution based on entity, dispute category, and disputed amount tier. Default SLAs must be: disputes under USD 1,000 resolved within 5 business days, USD 1,000 to USD 10,000 within 10 business days, and over USD 10,000 within 15 business days. The system must calculate SLA deadlines considering each entity's business day calendar (excluding Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, Indian public holidays, and weekends which are Friday-Saturday for Qatar/UAE/KSA and Saturday-Sunday for India). When a dispute exceeds 75% of its SLA target without advancing to the resolution stage, the system must send an automated escalation notification to the dispute handler's supervisor. Breached SLAs must be highlighted in red on all dispute dashboards and included in weekly management reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R005	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Automated Evidence Collection for Disputes	Upon dispute registration, the system must automatically gather and attach relevant evidence from connected modules: the original freight invoice and all line item details, the booking confirmation and any amendments, the Bill of Lading (original and any switched BLs), container tracking events from the TOS integration showing gate-in, loading, discharge, and gate-out timestamps at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, the applicable tariff or contract rate card in effect at the time of shipment, vessel manifest data confirming container on board, and any prior correspondence related to the shipment. This automated evidence package must be compiled within 60 seconds of dispute registration and made available to the assigned investigator as a single downloadable dossier in PDF format with bilingual headers in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R006	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Credit Note and Debit Note Generation from Disputes	When a dispute resolution results in a financial adjustment, the system must automatically generate a credit note (for overcharge disputes) or debit note (for undercharge disputes) linked to the original invoice. The credit/debit note must comply with the tax invoice requirements of the issuing entity's jurisdiction: Qatar VAT format, UAE FTA e-invoicing requirements, KSA ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoicing with QR code and XML submission, or India GST credit note format with IRN from the e-invoice portal. The adjustment amount must be recorded in the original invoice currency with the exchange rate at the date of resolution. Partial dispute resolutions must be supported, allowing a credit note for a portion of the disputed amount while the remainder is rejected with documented reasoning. Generated credit notes must flow to the accounts receivable module for customer balance adjustment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R007	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Communication and Correspondence Management	The system must maintain a complete correspondence trail for each dispute, supporting both internal notes (visible only to staff) and external communications (shared with the customer or agent). External communications must be sendable via email directly from the dispute interface, with templates available in English and Arabic. Templates must cover: dispute acknowledgment, request for additional information, resolution proposal, resolution acceptance, and dispute rejection. Each template must auto-populate with dispute details including invoice number, container numbers, vessel name, voyage number, and disputed amount. All sent and received correspondence must be timestamped and linked to the dispute record. The system must support RTL text rendering for Arabic correspondence and allow attachments up to 25 MB per communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R008	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Analytics and Root Cause Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time analytics dashboard showing dispute metrics including: total disputes by status, disputes by category and sub-category, average resolution time by entity and category, dispute rate as a percentage of total invoices, total disputed amount versus total billed revenue, top 10 customers by dispute frequency and value, and disputes by trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Mundra). The AI engine must perform root cause analysis identifying systemic issues such as recurring rate card discrepancies on specific trade lanes, frequent THC disputes at particular ports, or documentation errors correlated with specific booking channels. Root cause insights must be presented with recommended corrective actions and must be exportable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL layout for Arabic reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R009	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Aging and Provision Calculation	The system must calculate and report dispute aging in configurable buckets (0-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, over 90 days) from the dispute lodgment date. For open disputes, the system must calculate a provision amount representing the probable financial exposure, using AI-estimated resolution outcomes based on historical dispute resolution patterns for similar dispute types, amounts, and customers. The provision engine must generate recommended provision journal entries in the entity's functional currency, classified by dispute category. Provisions must be updated monthly at period-end and reversed automatically when disputes are closed. The provision report must show movement analysis (opening balance, new provisions, releases, utilizations, FX adjustments, closing balance) per entity and consolidated for group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R010	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Bulk Dispute Management for Systematic Errors	The system must support bulk dispute creation and resolution when a systematic billing error affects multiple invoices (e.g., an incorrect BAF surcharge applied to all shipments on a specific trade lane for an entire month). The bulk dispute wizard must allow users to define selection criteria (date range, trade lane, surcharge type, customer group), preview all affected invoices and the calculated adjustment per invoice, and submit the entire batch as a single dispute case. Bulk resolution must generate individual credit or debit notes per original invoice to maintain proper audit trail while allowing single-approval workflow for the batch. The system must enforce a configurable batch size limit (default 500 invoices) and require senior management approval for bulk adjustments exceeding a configurable total threshold (default USD 50,000 equivalent).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R011	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Customer Self-Service Dispute Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing web portal where registered customers and freight forwarders can view their invoices, lodge disputes online, upload supporting documents, track dispute status in real time, and view resolution outcomes. The portal must support English and Arabic language selection with full RTL layout for Arabic. Authentication must integrate with the customer portal SSO. Customers must only see disputes related to their own account and entity. The portal must display dispute SLA countdown timers, allow customers to respond to information requests directly within the portal, and send email notifications at each dispute stage transition. The portal must be responsive for mobile devices and must enforce the same document upload size limits and file type restrictions as the internal system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R012	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Impact on Revenue Recognition	When a dispute is lodged against a recognized revenue item, the system must assess the impact on revenue recognition per IFRS 15 variable consideration constraints. If the dispute creates significant uncertainty about the collectability of the invoiced amount, the system must recommend a revenue adjustment to constrain recognized revenue to the amount highly probable of not reversing. The assessment must consider the dispute amount as a percentage of the invoice total, the customer's historical dispute resolution rate, and the dispute category. Revenue adjustments related to disputes must be posted as separate adjustment entries (not reversals of the original recognition) to maintain audit trail. When the dispute is resolved, any revenue constraint must be released and the final revenue amount recognized, with the timing difference properly disclosed in IFRS 15 revenue reconciliation notes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R013	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Workflow Delegation and Absence Management	The system must support temporary delegation of dispute handling responsibilities when the assigned handler is on leave or unavailable. Managers must be able to reassign individual disputes or configure automatic delegation rules based on handler absence calendars. When a dispute is reassigned, the new handler must receive a complete context package including all investigation notes, correspondence history, evidence dossier, and any draft resolution proposals. The system must track delegation history as part of the dispute audit trail. Escalation rules must automatically bypass absent handlers and route to the next available authorized user. Delegation rules must respect entity boundaries so that Qatar entity disputes are only delegable to Qatar-authorized handlers, and similarly for UAE, KSA, and India entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R014	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Resolution Knowledge Base	The system must maintain an AI-curated knowledge base of dispute resolution precedents, searchable by dispute category, trade lane, port, customer segment, and resolution outcome. When a new dispute is assigned, the AI must suggest relevant precedents from the knowledge base, showing how similar disputes were resolved including the resolution amount as a percentage of the disputed amount, the average resolution time, and the key factors that influenced the outcome. The knowledge base must be automatically enriched with each closed dispute. Handlers must be able to rate the relevance of suggested precedents to improve future recommendations. The knowledge base must support entries in both English and Arabic and must segregate precedents by entity jurisdiction where resolution practices differ due to local regulations or customs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R015	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute Reporting and Regulatory Compliance	The system must generate comprehensive dispute reports for management review and regulatory compliance, including: monthly dispute summary by entity with trend analysis, dispute resolution rate and average settlement percentage, aging analysis with provision adequacy assessment, top disputed charge categories with root cause annotations, and customer satisfaction metrics based on resolution survey responses. Reports must be available in English and Arabic with Hijri and Gregorian date options. For KSA ZATCA compliance, any credit notes issued through dispute resolution must be included in the periodic VAT return filing data. For India GST, dispute-related credit notes must be reported in GSTR-1 with proper linking to the original invoice. The system must retain dispute records for a minimum of 7 years per regional record retention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R016	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Offline Dispute Registration and Sync	The system must allow dispute registration in offline mode for users at remote port locations or during network outages. The offline-capable dispute form must cache customer data, recent invoice references, and dispute category lists locally. When registering a dispute offline, the system must assign a provisional dispute reference with an offline prefix (e.g., DIS-OFF-QA-202602-00001) and store all entered data including uploaded documents in local encrypted storage. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline disputes to the central system, validate invoice references, assign permanent dispute reference numbers, and trigger the standard routing and SLA workflows. If the referenced invoice was modified or paid during the offline period, the system must flag the dispute for manual review before activating the standard workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-002-R017	FEAT-015-2-002	MOD-015	Dispute-Linked Customer Risk Scoring	The system must calculate and maintain a dispute risk score for each customer account, factoring in dispute frequency relative to invoice volume, average disputed amount as a percentage of total billings, dispute outcome ratio (upheld versus rejected), average resolution time driven by customer responsiveness, and repeat dispute patterns on the same charge categories. The risk score must be updated in real time as disputes are opened and closed. High-risk customers (score exceeding a configurable threshold) must trigger alerts to the commercial and credit control teams. The dispute risk score must feed into the overall customer credit risk assessment used by the collections module. Scores must be calculable per entity and consolidated across all entities to identify customers that dispute selectively with specific regional offices such as Hamad Port operations versus Jebel Ali operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R001	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	AI-Powered Collection Priority Scoring	The AI engine must calculate a dynamic collection priority score for every overdue invoice, considering: days past due, invoice amount in USD equivalent, customer payment history over the last 24 months, customer credit limit utilization, existence of active disputes on the same account, trade lane profitability, seasonal payment patterns (e.g., slower payments during Ramadan in Qatar/UAE/KSA or Diwali season in India), and the probability of collection estimated from a machine learning model trained on historical payment data. The priority score must be recalculated daily and must drive the collector's work queue ordering. Invoices with declining collection probability must be escalated automatically, while those with improving probability (e.g., partial payment received) must be deprioritized to focus collector effort where it is most needed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R002	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Configurable Multi-Stage Dunning Sequences	The system must support configurable dunning sequences with multiple escalation stages, each defining: the trigger (days past due or days since last action), communication channel (email, SMS, portal notification, physical letter), message template, sender identity (department or individual), and required approvals before sending. Default dunning sequence must include: (1) friendly payment reminder at 7 days past due, (2) formal overdue notice at 30 days, (3) escalation warning at 60 days mentioning potential service hold, (4) final demand at 90 days referencing legal action, and (5) legal notice at 120 days. Dunning sequences must be configurable per entity, customer segment (direct shippers, freight forwarders, NVOCCs), and currency. Each entity must have sequences available in English and Arabic with proper formatting and RTL support for Arabic communications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R003	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	AI-Personalized Dunning Communication	The AI engine must personalize dunning communications based on the customer's profile and historical response patterns. Personalization must include: preferred language (English or Arabic), optimal send time based on the customer's time zone and historically observed email open times, preferred communication channel (some customers respond better to email while others to phone calls), tone adjustment based on customer relationship value (strategic accounts receive softer language while transactional accounts receive standard language), and inclusion of specific invoice details most likely to prompt payment (e.g., container numbers, vessel names, BL references). The AI must learn from each interaction, tracking which communication approaches lead to faster payment for each customer segment. Personalization rules must comply with data privacy regulations applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R004	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Collector Workload Management and Assignment	The system must assign overdue accounts to collectors based on configurable rules including: entity affiliation, customer segment, language capability (Arabic or English speaking collectors), trade lane specialization, and current workload balance. Each collector must have a dashboard showing their assigned accounts sorted by AI priority score, with key metrics per account: total overdue amount, oldest overdue invoice, last collection action taken, last customer response, promised payment dates, and recommended next action. The system must support workload rebalancing when collectors are added, removed, or on leave, automatically redistributing accounts while maintaining relationship continuity where possible. Supervisors must have a team dashboard showing collector performance metrics: total amount collected, number of accounts worked, average days to collect, and promise-to-pay conversion rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R005	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Promise-to-Pay Tracking and Follow-Up	When a customer commits to a payment date during collection follow-up, the system must record the promise-to-pay with: promised amount, promised currency, promised date, payment method (wire transfer, cheque, LC), and any conditions attached. The system must automatically schedule a follow-up action for the day after the promised payment date to verify receipt. If payment is not received by the promised date plus a configurable grace period (default 2 business days), the system must automatically escalate the account and flag the broken promise in the customer's collection history. Repeat broken promises must increase the customer's risk score and trigger a recommendation to the credit control team to review the customer's credit terms. The system must track promise-to-pay fulfillment rates per customer and use this data to train the AI collection probability model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R006	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Automated Payment Matching and Allocation	The system must automatically match incoming payments received via bank statement feeds from banks in Qatar (QNB, CBQ), UAE (Emirates NBD, ADCB), KSA (Al Rajhi, SNB), and India (SBI, HDFC) to outstanding invoices using intelligent matching rules. Matching criteria must include: payment reference containing invoice number, amount matching (exact or within a configurable tolerance for bank charges, default USD 5.00 equivalent), customer bank account identification, and remittance advice parsing using AI-powered OCR for scanned remittance documents. When a payment cannot be auto-matched (e.g., bulk payment from a customer covering multiple invoices without clear allocation), the system must suggest the most probable invoice allocation based on due dates, amounts, and customer payment patterns, presenting the suggestion to the collector for confirmation. Unallocated payments exceeding 5 business days must be escalated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R007	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Service Hold and Release for Delinquent Accounts	The system must support automated and manual placement of service holds on customer accounts when overdue balances exceed configurable thresholds per entity. Hold types must include: booking hold (preventing new bookings), BL release hold (preventing release of Bills of Lading for shipped cargo), and full account freeze. The system must respect INCOTERMS implications — for example, under CIF terms where the shipper is responsible for freight, a hold should not block the consignee's unrelated shipments. When a hold is placed, the system must immediately notify the customer via email and portal notification in their preferred language, the sales account manager, and the operations team at the relevant port (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT). Holds must be automatically released within 30 minutes of confirmed payment receipt matching the overdue amount. Manual hold overrides must require manager approval with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R008	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Collection Tracking	The system must track collections in the original invoice currency and provide real-time conversion to each entity's functional currency and USD equivalent for consolidated reporting. When a customer makes payment in a currency different from the invoice currency (e.g., paying a USD-invoiced freight charge in AED or QAR), the system must calculate the exchange gain or loss based on the rate at payment date versus the rate at invoice date, posting the difference to the appropriate foreign exchange gain/loss account. The collection dashboard must display overdue balances in both original currency and functional currency with the option to switch views. For customers with invoices in multiple currencies, the system must support partial payments allocated across currencies based on customer instruction or configured default allocation rules (e.g., oldest invoice first regardless of currency, or within same currency first).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R009	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Legal Escalation and Bad Debt Management	When collection efforts are exhausted per the configured dunning sequence and the account remains delinquent beyond a configurable threshold (default 180 days), the system must initiate legal escalation workflow. This must include: generating a formal legal demand letter template compliant with the jurisdiction of the customer's registered entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India commercial law requirements), creating a legal case file containing all collection correspondence, proof of debt documents (signed BLs, delivery receipts, acknowledged invoices), and a timeline of collection efforts. The system must track legal proceedings stages and associated legal costs. When an account is deemed uncollectable, the system must support bad debt write-off workflow requiring CFO-level approval, generating the write-off journal entry, and adjusting the expected credit loss provision per IFRS 9. Written-off amounts must remain trackable for potential future recovery.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R010	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	AI-Predicted Days Sales Outstanding Optimization	The AI engine must predict the Days Sales Outstanding (DSO) for each entity, customer segment, and trade lane, providing forward-looking DSO estimates based on the current receivables portfolio composition and predicted payment dates for each outstanding invoice. The prediction model must factor in customer-specific payment behavior, seasonal patterns, economic indicators for the shipping industry in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent, and the effectiveness of recent dunning actions. The system must recommend specific collection actions that would have the highest impact on reducing DSO, such as targeting specific large-balance accounts or offering early payment discounts to customers predicted to delay. DSO predictions must be updated weekly and presented alongside actual DSO trends in a management dashboard with entity-level and consolidated group-level views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R011	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Expected Credit Loss Provision Calculation (IFRS 9)	The system must calculate the Expected Credit Loss (ECL) provision per IFRS 9 using a forward-looking model that considers historical loss rates by customer segment and aging bucket, current economic conditions in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and forward-looking macroeconomic indicators such as GDP growth forecasts, oil prices, and trade volume indices. The ECL calculation must use a provision matrix approach with loss rates applied to the aged receivables balance, with the option to override rates for specific customers or segments based on individual credit assessments. The system must generate ECL provision journal entries at each month-end, calculate the movement analysis (opening, charges, releases, write-offs, recoveries, FX translation, closing), and produce IFRS 7 disclosure data including sensitivity analysis showing the impact of changes in key assumptions on the ECL estimate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R012	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Dunning Calendar and Cultural Sensitivity Rules	The system must enforce cultural sensitivity rules that prevent dunning communications during culturally inappropriate periods. Configurable blackout periods must include: Ramadan (especially the last 10 days and Eid Al-Fitr) for Qatar, UAE, and KSA customers, Eid Al-Adha for all Muslim-majority regions, Diwali and other major Indian festivals for India-based customers, National Day holidays for each country, and Friday prayer hours (12:00-14:00 local time) for GCC region customers. The dunning calendar must be updated annually with confirmed dates including Hijri-to-Gregorian date conversions for Islamic holidays. During blackout periods, dunning communications must be queued and automatically released on the next eligible business day. The system must still track aging and escalation internally during blackout periods but suppress external communications. Emergency collections above a configurable threshold (default USD 100,000) may override blackout rules with manager approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R013	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Collection Activity Logging and Audit Trail	The system must log every collection activity with immutable records capturing: activity type (email sent, phone call made, SMS sent, meeting conducted, promise-to-pay recorded, payment received, hold placed, hold released, legal action initiated), timestamp in UTC with local time zone display, the collector who performed the action, the customer contact interacted with, detailed notes in English or Arabic, and any system-generated actions (automated dunning sends, AI priority changes, automatic escalations). All email and SMS content sent through the dunning system must be archived with delivery confirmation status. Phone call logs must support voice recording attachment where legally permitted. The audit trail must be searchable by account, collector, date range, and activity type. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 7 years and must be exportable for internal and external audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R014	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Collector Performance Analytics and Incentive Tracking	The system must track and report individual collector performance metrics including: total amount collected per period, collection rate (amount collected versus amount assigned), average days to collect from first dunning action to payment receipt, number of customer contacts made per day, promise-to-pay conversion rate, dispute referral rate (collections identified as disputes and redirected), and reduction in overdue balance for assigned portfolio. The system must support configurable collection targets per collector per period and display performance against target in real-time dashboards. An incentive calculation module must compute collector bonuses based on a configurable formula that may include collection amount tiers, speed of collection, and reduction of accounts in high-aging buckets. Performance reports must be available per entity and consolidated, viewable by collectors, supervisors, and finance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R015	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Automated Collection Correspondence in Arabic and English	The system must generate and send collection letters, emails, and SMS messages in both English and Arabic based on the customer's preferred language setting. Arabic communications must render correctly in RTL format with proper Arabic numeral handling (supporting both Eastern Arabic and Western Arabic numerals based on entity preference). Templates must be legally reviewed and entity-specific, referencing the correct legal entity name, commercial registration number, and jurisdiction. The AI must dynamically adjust the formality level of Arabic correspondence based on the customer's cultural context (e.g., formal classical Arabic for government entities in KSA versus modern standard Arabic for commercial entities in UAE). All templates must include mandatory legal notices required by each jurisdiction's commercial debt collection laws. The system must track delivery status for emails (sent, delivered, opened, bounced) and SMS (delivered, failed) to optimize channel selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R016	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Offline Collection Activity Recording	The system must allow collectors to record collection activities (phone call notes, meeting outcomes, promise-to-pay commitments) while offline, such as when visiting customer premises or working from locations with poor connectivity near port areas at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The offline module must cache the collector's assigned account list with key receivable data, recent activity history, and customer contact details. Activities recorded offline must be timestamped using the device clock and flagged as offline entries. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline activities to the central system, validate any promise-to-pay dates against the current calendar, and trigger any automated follow-up workflows that should have been initiated during the offline period. Conflicts between offline and online updates to the same account must be presented to the collector for resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-003-R017	FEAT-015-2-003	MOD-015	Integration with Credit Insurance and Factoring	The system must integrate with credit insurance providers and factoring arrangements to support insured receivables collection. For credit-insured receivables, the system must track the insured percentage, policy limits, and notification requirements — automatically notifying the insurer when an account exceeds the pre-claim notification threshold (typically 60-90 days overdue) as required by policy terms. For factored receivables where invoices have been assigned to a factoring bank, the system must redirect collection activities to the factor's collection process while maintaining visibility of the receivable status. The system must calculate the net exposure (total receivable minus insured portion minus factored portion) for each customer and entity, feeding this into the ECL provision calculation. Claim filing workflows for credit insurance must include all required documentation: proof of debt, evidence of collection efforts, and commercial dispute status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R001	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Booking-Based Short-Term Revenue Forecast	The system must generate short-term revenue forecasts (1-4 weeks ahead) based on confirmed bookings, calculating expected revenue by summing the contracted freight rates, applicable surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS, low-sulphur surcharge), and estimated ancillary charges (documentation fees, container detention based on historical averages) for all confirmed bookings with ETD within the forecast horizon. The forecast must be segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Mundra), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), and customer segment. The system must account for historical booking cancellation and rollover rates per trade lane to adjust the gross confirmed revenue to a net expected revenue figure. Forecasts must be generated daily and compared against the previous day's forecast to show movements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R002	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	AI Pipeline Revenue Estimation	The system must estimate revenue from pipeline opportunities (quotes issued but not yet confirmed, recurring customer patterns without formal bookings, and spot market inquiries) using AI prediction models. The pipeline model must assign a probability of conversion to each opportunity based on: quote age, customer's historical conversion rate on the same trade lane, current market rate competitiveness of the quoted rate, vessel capacity utilization on the target sailing, and the customer's booking pattern regularity. Pipeline revenue must be categorized into confidence tiers: high confidence (over 80% probability), medium confidence (50-80%), and low confidence (below 50%). The total weighted pipeline revenue must be added to confirmed booking revenue to produce a blended forecast. The AI model must be retrained monthly using the latest 12 months of quote-to-booking conversion data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R003	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Long-Term Revenue Projection with Seasonal Modeling	The system must produce long-term revenue projections covering 3-month, 6-month, and 12-month horizons using time-series forecasting models that incorporate: historical revenue patterns over at least 36 months, seasonal factors (Ramadan, Eid, Chinese New Year, Indian festive season, European summer), trade lane growth trends, contracted volume commitments from MQC (Minimum Quantity Commitment) agreements, planned service additions or withdrawals on specific routes, and macroeconomic indicators affecting Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade volumes such as oil prices, GDP growth, and manufacturing PMI indices. Long-term projections must be presented with confidence intervals (P10, P50, P90) and must be recalculated weekly. The system must flag when actual revenue deviates from the projection by more than a configurable threshold (default 10%) for management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R004	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Forecast by Trade Lane and Service Loop	The system must disaggregate revenue forecasts by trade lane and service loop, enabling commercial and operations teams to analyze expected revenue at a granular route level. Each trade lane forecast must show: number of expected TEUs (twenty-foot equivalent units), average revenue per TEU, total forecast revenue, and comparison to the same period in the prior year and to budget. Service loop forecasts must aggregate all trade lanes served by the same vessel rotation. The system must automatically identify trade lanes with declining revenue forecasts and cross-reference with market rate intelligence to determine whether the decline is due to volume loss or rate erosion. Trade lane forecasts must be viewable on an interactive map visualization showing the ports of Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other connected ports with color-coded revenue forecast indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R005	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Scenario Analysis for Revenue Planning	The system must support scenario analysis allowing users to model the revenue impact of hypothetical changes such as: rate increases or decreases on specific trade lanes, addition or removal of a vessel service, changes in fuel prices affecting BAF surcharges, currency fluctuations impacting the USD equivalent revenue from QAR, AED, SAR, or INR denominated contracts, loss of a major customer or win of a new contract, and changes in port terminal handling charges at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT. Users must be able to create, save, and compare multiple scenarios side-by-side, with each scenario showing the delta versus the baseline forecast in absolute amounts and percentages. The system must support combining multiple scenario adjustments (e.g., simultaneous rate increase plus currency devaluation) and calculating the compound effect on forecast revenue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R007	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Port Agent Communication Integration	The system must support electronic transmission of approved proforma DAs to nominated port agents via configurable channels including structured email with PDF attachment, EDI messages conforming to BIMCO DA format standards, and API-based integration for agents with compatible systems. The transmitted proforma must include all vessel particulars, expected cargo operations (number of containers to load/discharge by type: 20ft dry, 40ft dry, 20ft reefer, 40ft reefer, OOG, hazardous IMO class), estimated dates, and a unique proforma reference number. The system must track transmission status and log all communication timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R006	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Budget Variance Analysis	The system must compare actual recognized revenue against the approved budget at every level of the organizational hierarchy: group consolidated, entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), trade lane, customer segment, and charge type. Variance analysis must decompose the total variance into volume variance (difference in TEU volume at budget rate), rate variance (difference in average rate at actual volume), mix variance (change in container type or trade lane mix), surcharge variance (BAF, CAF, THC changes), and currency variance (exchange rate movements). Each variance component must be calculable in the entity's functional currency and in USD for consolidated reporting. The system must produce monthly budget variance reports with year-to-date cumulative variances and full-year forecast versus budget projections. AI must generate narrative explanations for significant variances, identifying the root causes and suggesting corrective commercial actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R007	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Customer Revenue Concentration Risk Analysis	The system must continuously monitor and report customer revenue concentration across all entities. The analysis must identify: the top 10 and top 20 customers by revenue contribution with their percentage of total revenue, the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI) for customer concentration per entity and consolidated, trade lanes where a single customer represents more than 30% of revenue, customers whose revenue contribution is declining quarter-over-quarter, and new customers that are growing rapidly. The system must flag concentration risk alerts when any single customer exceeds a configurable revenue share threshold (default 15% per entity, 10% consolidated). The AI must recommend diversification strategies based on the pipeline opportunities and market intelligence for under-penetrated customer segments or trade lanes. Reports must be presentable in English and Arabic with data exportable to Excel for board presentation preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R008	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Freight Rate Intelligence Integration	The system must integrate with external freight rate intelligence sources (such as the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, Freightos Baltic Index, and Drewry World Container Index) to provide market context for revenue forecasting. Rate intelligence must be mapped to the company's operating trade lanes, showing the relationship between market rates and the company's average achieved rates. The AI must identify trade lanes where the company's rates are significantly above or below market averages and predict the likely direction of rate movements over the next 30, 60, and 90 days based on market trends, vessel capacity supply data, and demand indicators. Rate intelligence must feed into the long-term revenue projection model as an external factor. Historical rate trends must be displayed alongside the company's booking rates on interactive charts with the ability to filter by container type, trade lane, and date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R009	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Forecast Accuracy Measurement	The system must track forecast accuracy by comparing each forecast vintage (the date the forecast was produced) against actual realized revenue for the same period once actuals are available. Accuracy metrics must include: Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), weighted MAPE (giving more weight to higher-revenue trade lanes), bias measurement (systematic over- or under-forecasting), and accuracy by forecast horizon (1-week ahead typically more accurate than 12-month ahead). The system must maintain a forecast accuracy dashboard showing accuracy trends over time per entity, trade lane, and forecast type (booking-based, pipeline-based, long-term projection). When forecast accuracy for a specific dimension falls below a configurable threshold (default MAPE below 85%), the system must trigger a review alert to the forecasting team with AI-generated analysis of the likely causes of inaccuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R010	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Multi-Currency Revenue Forecast Consolidation	The system must consolidate revenue forecasts from all entities into a group-level view, handling multi-currency translation consistently. Entity-level forecasts in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR must be translated to the group reporting currency (USD) using either budget exchange rates (for budget comparison purposes) or forecast exchange rates (for realistic projections). The system must support treasury-provided forward exchange rates for future periods and must show the sensitivity of the consolidated forecast to exchange rate movements via a configurable range of FX scenarios (e.g., +/-5% movement in each currency). The consolidated forecast must eliminate intercompany revenue between group entities (e.g., revenue booked by Qatar entity for services rendered to UAE entity). Currency impact analysis must quantify how much of the forecast variance versus prior period is attributable to organic growth versus FX translation effects.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R011	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Capacity Utilization and Revenue Optimization	The system must correlate revenue forecasts with vessel capacity utilization to identify revenue optimization opportunities. For each sailing, the system must calculate: forecast TEU utilization, forecast revenue per available TEU slot, forecast weight utilization (important for heavy cargo on Gulf-India routes), and the marginal revenue opportunity from filling remaining capacity. The AI must recommend optimal pricing strategies for remaining capacity: whether to offer spot rates below published tariff to fill empty slots, hold capacity for higher-yielding bookings expected closer to sailing, or reallocate capacity between trade lanes. The analysis must consider container repositioning costs (empty container return from JNPT to Jebel Ali is a significant cost factor) in the revenue optimization calculation. Recommendations must be generated at least 14 days before sailing to allow commercial teams to act.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R012	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Forecast Workflow and Approval	The system must support a formal revenue forecast submission and approval workflow for management reporting purposes. The workflow must include: (1) initial forecast generation by the AI engine, (2) review and adjustment by the trade lane commercial managers at each entity, (3) consolidation and review by entity finance controllers, (4) group-level review and adjustment by the group CFO office, and (5) final approval and publication. Each stage must support commentary fields where reviewers can document their rationale for adjustments. Adjustments must be tracked as overlays on top of the system-generated forecast so that the variance between the AI forecast and the management-adjusted forecast is visible. The approved forecast must become the reference baseline for subsequent variance analysis. The workflow must enforce stage deadlines aligned with the group financial reporting calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R013	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Impact of External Disruptions	The system must model the revenue impact of external disruptions to shipping operations, including: port congestion or closure at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT due to weather or operational issues, canal or strait disruptions (Suez Canal, Strait of Hormuz), geopolitical events affecting specific trade routes, pandemic-related restrictions on crew or cargo, and regulatory changes such as new emissions regulations or sanctions. The AI must use historical disruption data to estimate the duration and revenue impact magnitude for each type of disruption, applying adjustments to the baseline forecast when a disruption event is flagged. Users must be able to manually trigger disruption scenarios, specifying the affected ports, expected duration, and estimated capacity reduction percentage. The system must calculate the cascading revenue impact across connected trade lanes and generate adjusted forecasts for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R014	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Forecast API for Downstream Systems	The system must expose a RESTful API endpoint for downstream systems to consume revenue forecast data. The API must support queries by: entity, trade lane, time period, currency, customer segment, container type, and forecast type (confirmed, pipeline, blended). Response format must be JSON with consistent schema including forecast amount, confidence interval, forecast vintage date, and comparison to budget and prior year. The API must support both point-in-time queries (latest forecast for a specific period) and time-series queries (all forecast vintages for a specific period to enable accuracy tracking). Authentication must use OAuth 2.0 tokens with entity-level access control so that a Qatar entity API consumer cannot access India entity forecast details without explicit cross-entity authorization. Rate limiting must be configurable per consumer with a default of 1000 requests per hour. The API must support response caching with configurable TTL to reduce load during peak reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R015	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Hijri Period Revenue Forecast Reporting	The system must produce revenue forecasts aligned to Hijri calendar periods for KSA and Qatar regulatory and management reporting. Hijri period forecasts must translate the Gregorian-based forecast data into Hijri monthly periods, handling the misalignment between Hijri and Gregorian months by proportionally allocating daily forecast amounts. Revenue budgets set in Hijri periods must be comparable to Hijri period forecasts for variance analysis. The system must display Hijri month names in Arabic script alongside English transliterations and must handle the variation in Hijri month lengths (29 or 30 days determined by lunar observation). Forecast reports in Hijri format must include all the same drill-down dimensions (entity, trade lane, customer, charge type) as Gregorian reports. The Hijri forecast module must support the annual Hijri budget cycle which starts on 1 Muharram.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R016	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Dashboard with Real-Time KPIs	The system must provide an executive revenue dashboard displaying real-time and near-real-time KPIs including: month-to-date recognized revenue versus budget and prior year, revenue run-rate extrapolation for the full month, quarter-to-date revenue performance, revenue per TEU trend by trade lane, top 5 revenue-contributing customers this month, top 5 revenue growth customers month-over-month, booking pipeline conversion funnel (quotes to bookings to shipments to invoices to collections), and DSO trend. The dashboard must support entity-level views and consolidated group view. All currency amounts must be displayable in the entity's functional currency or USD equivalent with a toggle switch. The dashboard must support RTL layout when Arabic is selected as the display language. Data refresh frequency must be configurable with a minimum of every 15 minutes for booking-based metrics and hourly for financial metrics. The dashboard must be accessible on desktop and tablet devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R017	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Offline Revenue Forecast Snapshot Access	The system must allow authorized users to download the latest approved revenue forecast as an offline-accessible snapshot for use during management meetings, board presentations, or travel to port locations with limited connectivity. The offline snapshot must be available in PDF and Excel formats, containing: summary forecast by entity and trade lane, variance analysis against budget and prior year, key assumptions and scenario analysis results, and trend charts. The Excel version must include structured data tables enabling further offline analysis and pivot table creation. The snapshot must be time-stamped with the forecast vintage date and approval status. Access to offline snapshots must be controlled by the same role-based permissions as the online system. Snapshots must be automatically generated upon forecast approval and distributed to configured recipients via email. The system must retain historical snapshots for at least 24 months for trend comparison and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-015-2-004-R018	FEAT-015-2-004	MOD-015	Revenue Forecast Sensitivity to Port Tariff Changes	The system must model the impact of port tariff changes on revenue forecasts, specifically for terminal handling charges (THC), wharfage, and port dues at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. When a port authority announces a tariff revision, users must be able to input the new rates with effective dates, and the system must automatically recalculate affected revenue forecast line items showing the incremental revenue or cost impact. The analysis must distinguish between tariff changes that can be fully passed through to customers via surcharge adjustments (with a configurable pass-through lag in days) and those that must be absorbed by the company. The system must calculate the net revenue impact after pass-through and show the working in a transparent waterfall analysis from gross impact to net impact. Historical tariff change data must be maintained to support trend analysis of port cost inflation across the operating region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R001	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Multi-Entity Customer Account Creation	The system must support creation of customer AR accounts under each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with entity-specific chart of accounts mapping. Each customer account must capture the legal entity code, trade license number, VAT/GST registration number (GST for India, VAT for GCC), primary currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD), and default payment terms. The system must prevent duplicate account creation across entities by cross-referencing the customer master using a globally unique customer identifier, tax registration number, and trade license number. AI must auto-suggest matching existing customers with a confidence score above 85% before allowing new account creation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R002	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Classification by Shipping Role	The system must classify each customer AR account by its shipping industry role including but not limited to: shipping line, NVOCC, freight forwarder, customs broker, consignee, shipper, container lessor, port terminal operator, and trucking company. Each classification must map to a predefined set of billing rules, credit terms, and default revenue recognition accounts. A single customer may hold multiple role classifications across different entities, for example acting as a freight forwarder in Qatar (Hamad Port operations) and as an NVOCC in UAE (Jebel Ali operations). The classification must drive downstream credit scoring and aging bucket configurations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R003	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Bilingual Customer Account Display with Arabic RTL Support	All customer account screens, forms, and printed statements must render correctly in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts. Customer names, addresses, and notes must be stored in both languages with Arabic as a mandatory field for GCC entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA) and optional for India entity. The UI must dynamically switch between LTR and RTL based on user language preference without page reload. All numeric fields including account balances, invoice amounts, and credit limits must display with locale-appropriate thousand separators and decimal formats. Arabic text must use proper Tashkeel diacritics storage capability for legal names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R004	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Hierarchical Grouping	The system must support hierarchical grouping of customer accounts at minimum three levels: global parent, regional parent, and local subsidiary. This hierarchy must enable consolidated statement generation, group-level credit exposure calculation, and inter-company offset capabilities. For example, a global shipping line like MSC may have regional offices in Dubai (Jebel Ali), Doha (Hamad Port), Dammam (King Abdulaziz Port), and Mumbai (JNPT), each as separate AR accounts but linked under a single group for credit and reporting purposes. Group-level payment application must be configurable to allow or restrict cross-entity allocation based on intercompany agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R005	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Suspension and Reactivation Workflow	The system must provide an AI-assisted workflow for suspending customer accounts when predefined triggers are met including: credit limit breach exceeding 10% tolerance, aging beyond the maximum allowed days per entity policy, returned checks or failed electronic payment attempts, or regulatory sanctions list matches. Suspension must immediately block new booking acceptance, BL issuance, and container release orders across all ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) within 60 seconds of activation. Reactivation must require a configurable multi-level approval chain with mandatory documentation upload such as payment proof, bank guarantee, or management override justification. The AI engine must recommend suspension severity levels (soft block versus hard block) based on customer payment history patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R006	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Default Payment Terms Configuration	Each customer AR account must have configurable default payment terms that include: payment due days (net 7, 15, 30, 45, 60, 90), early payment discount percentage and qualifying period, late payment penalty rate per entity jurisdiction (Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 50/2022, KSA Commercial Court regulations, Indian Contract Act provisions), and accepted payment methods (wire transfer, check, letter of credit, cash against documents, bank guarantee). Payment terms must be overridable at the individual invoice level with appropriate authorization. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all payment term modifications including the approver identity, timestamp, and business justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R007	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account KYC Document Management	The system must manage mandatory KYC (Know Your Customer) documents for each AR account including trade license, certificate of incorporation, VAT/GST certificate, authorized signatory list, bank account verification letter, and beneficial ownership declaration. Document expiry dates must be tracked with AI-powered alerts generated 60, 30, and 14 days before expiration. Expired KYC documents must trigger configurable business rules ranging from warning notifications to automatic account suspension depending on entity-specific regulatory requirements. For GCC entities, the system must validate commercial registration numbers against the respective Ministry of Commerce databases where API integrations are available. All documents must support bilingual Arabic and English storage with OCR-based data extraction capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R008	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Currency and Exchange Rate Configuration	Each customer account must define a primary billing currency and up to five alternate accepted currencies from the supported set (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP, CNY). The system must store the exchange rate source preference per customer: Central Bank rate (QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI), contracted fixed rate with validity period, or real-time market rate with configurable markup percentage. For invoices issued in a currency different from the customer primary currency, the system must automatically calculate and display the equivalent amount in the primary currency. Exchange rate variance tolerance must be configurable per customer account with alerts triggered when actual rate deviates beyond the threshold from the contracted rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R009	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Contact and Communication Preferences	The system must store multiple contacts per customer AR account with role-based designations including: accounts payable contact, credit controller contact, commercial contact, and escalation contact. Each contact must have configurable communication preferences for statement delivery (email, WhatsApp Business API, physical mail, EDI), invoice delivery format (PDF, XML e-invoice, EDI IFTMIN/INVOIC), and preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must track communication delivery status and automatically retry failed deliveries through alternate channels. For customers operating at multiple ports, contacts may be port-specific, for example a different AP contact for Jebel Ali versus Hamad Port operations, and the system must route communications to the appropriate port-level contact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R010	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Bank Details and Beneficiary Management	The system must securely store customer bank details for refund processing and direct debit arrangements including IBAN (mandatory for GCC entities per Central Bank regulations), SWIFT/BIC code, bank name and branch, and account holder name. Multiple bank accounts per customer must be supported with designation of a primary account for each currency. Bank details must be validated against IBAN checksum algorithms for GCC countries and IFSC code validation for Indian bank accounts. Any modification to bank details must trigger a maker-checker approval workflow with mandatory cool-down period of minimum 24 hours before the new details become active for outbound payments, protecting against payment fraud. All bank detail fields must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R011	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Offline Data Synchronization	The system must support offline access to customer account data for field collection agents and commercial teams operating in areas with limited connectivity, particularly relevant for port-area operations at Hamad Port, JNPT, and remote depot locations. Offline capability must include read access to customer balance summaries, recent transaction history (last 90 days), contact details, and credit status. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize any offline-captured data (payment promises, visit notes, dispute records) using a conflict resolution strategy that prioritizes server-side data for financial figures while merging text notes with timestamps. The maximum offline data staleness must be configurable per entity with a default of 24 hours before mandatory re-sync is required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R012	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Audit Trail and Change History	Every modification to a customer AR account must generate an immutable audit trail record capturing the field changed, previous value, new value, user identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, IP address, and change reason category (administrative, commercial, regulatory, correction). The audit trail must be queryable by date range (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian date inputs), user, field name, and change type. Bulk account modifications (such as mass payment term updates) must generate individual audit entries per account while also being linked to a batch operation identifier. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 10 years per GCC commercial record-keeping requirements and Indian Companies Act Section 128 provisions. AI must flag unusual account modification patterns such as repeated credit limit increases for the same customer within a short period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R013	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Integration with Booking and BL Modules	The customer AR account must integrate bidirectionally with the booking module and Bill of Lading module to enable real-time credit validation at booking creation and BL release stages. When a new booking is created at any port (Hamad Port code QAHMD, Jebel Ali code AEJEA, Khalifa Port code AEKHL, JNPT code INNSA), the system must query the customer AR account to validate credit availability, account status (active, suspended, or blocked), and KYC compliance status. The integration must return a credit decision within 2 seconds for online transactions. Any holds placed on the AR account must immediately propagate to the operations modules, preventing container release, delivery order issuance, or FCL/LCL cargo handover until the hold is resolved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R014	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Tax Registration and Compliance Mapping	The system must store and validate tax registration details specific to each entity jurisdiction: VAT registration number for Qatar (formatted per GTA requirements), TRN (Tax Registration Number) for UAE per FTA format, VAT registration for KSA per ZATCA requirements, and GSTIN for India per GST Council format with state-level registration codes. The system must automatically determine tax applicability and rates based on the customer registration status, transaction type (domestic, export, re-export, transit), and service category (freight, THC, detention, demurrage, documentation fees). For reverse charge mechanism transactions applicable under GCC VAT frameworks, the system must flag the invoice appropriately and adjust the AR posting. AI must auto-validate tax numbers against government databases where APIs are available (ZATCA, FTA) and flag mismatches within the customer account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R015	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Dashboard and AI Health Scoring	The system must provide an AI-generated customer account health dashboard displaying a composite score (0-100) derived from weighted factors including: payment punctuality (last 12 months trend), credit utilization ratio, dispute frequency and resolution time, volume trend (TEU throughput across all ports), relationship tenure, and financial statement indicators where available. The dashboard must render in both Arabic and English with interactive drill-down capability from summary score to individual factor analysis. Health score changes exceeding 15 points in either direction within a rolling 30-day window must trigger automated alerts to the assigned credit controller. The scoring algorithm weights must be configurable per entity to accommodate regional business practices, for example higher weight on relationship tenure for GCC entities versus stricter financial metrics for India entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-001-R016	FEAT-016-1-001	MOD-016	Customer Account Closure and Archival Process	The system must enforce a structured account closure workflow requiring zero outstanding balance (verified across all currencies), no pending unapplied receipts or credit notes, no active bookings or in-transit containers at any port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), and completion of final statement reconciliation signed off by both the customer and internal credit controller. Closed accounts must transition to an archived state where all historical data remains queryable for audit and reporting purposes but no new transactions can be posted. The archival must comply with data retention policies per jurisdiction: minimum 5 years for India (GST Act), 7 years for KSA (ZATCA), and as specified by QFC/QFCRA regulations for Qatar. AI must predict potential account closures 90 days in advance based on declining activity patterns and alert relationship managers for retention intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R001	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	AI-Driven Credit Scoring Model	The system must implement an AI-powered credit scoring engine that generates a risk score (AAA to D scale, mapping to numeric 0-1000) for each customer account. The model must incorporate minimum 15 weighted factors including: audited financial statements (balance sheet strength, profitability ratios, cash flow adequacy), trade payment history from the ERP (DSO trend, payment punctuality index), external credit bureau data where available (Dun & Bradstreet, Etihad Credit Bureau for UAE, SIMAH for KSA, CIBIL for India), shipping volume trajectory (TEU throughput across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), industry risk classification per NACE/ISIC codes, and geopolitical risk scoring for the customer domicile country. The AI model must be retrained quarterly using the latest 24 months of payment behavior data and must achieve minimum 85% accuracy in predicting payment default within 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R002	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Multi-Entity Credit Limit Structure	The system must support a hierarchical credit limit structure with limits defined at global group level, regional level, and individual entity level (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each entity-level limit must be denominated in the entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR respectively) with an aggregate group limit typically in USD. The system must enforce that the sum of entity-level limits does not exceed the group-level limit, with configurable tolerance of 0-10%. Real-time credit exposure calculation must aggregate outstanding invoices, unbilled accruals (for containers in-transit with known freight charges), and provisional bookings not yet invoiced. Credit limits must be reviewed automatically by AI every 90 days with recommendation to increase, maintain, or decrease based on the updated credit score and trading pattern analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R003	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Limit Approval Workflow with Delegation Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable multi-tier approval workflow for credit limit setting and modification based on a delegation of authority matrix. Approval tiers must be defined by limit amount bands per entity currency, for example: up to AED 500,000 requires Credit Controller approval, AED 500,001 to AED 2,000,000 requires Credit Manager approval, above AED 2,000,000 requires CFO approval. Cross-entity credit limit requests exceeding the group threshold must require Group Credit Committee approval with a minimum quorum of three approvers. The workflow must support parallel and sequential approval routing, automatic escalation after configurable SLA breach (default 48 hours per tier), and delegation of approval authority during planned absences. All approvals must be digitally signed with full audit trail including comments and supporting documents reviewed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R004	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Real-Time Credit Exposure Monitoring	The system must calculate and display real-time credit exposure per customer across all entities, updated within 30 seconds of any transaction posting. Exposure must include: outstanding AR invoices (by currency with USD equivalent), unmatched debit notes, unbilled revenue accruals for active voyages and containers at port (pending THC, detention, demurrage charges at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), provisional booking commitments not yet invoiced, and pending freight charges for containers in-transit based on expected routing. The system must deduct available credit notes, unapplied cash receipts, and active bank guarantees from the gross exposure. A color-coded utilization gauge (green 0-70%, amber 71-90%, red 91-100%, critical above 100%) must be displayed on the customer account dashboard in both Arabic and English interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R005	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Temporary Credit Limit Override with Expiry	The system must support temporary credit limit increases with mandatory expiry dates, commonly required during peak shipping seasons (Q4 pre-Ramadan inventory build-up, Hajj season logistics surge, Indian festival season import peaks). Temporary overrides must specify the override amount (additional limit above permanent), effective date, expiry date (maximum 90 days), business justification, and approver chain which must be minimum one tier higher than the permanent limit authority. Upon expiry, the system must automatically revert to the permanent limit and generate alerts if the customer current exposure exceeds the reverted limit. AI must proactively recommend temporary limit increases 30 days before anticipated peak periods based on historical seasonal patterns for each customer, reducing manual credit review workload by at least 60%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R006	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Bank Guarantee and Security Instrument Management	The system must track credit risk mitigants including bank guarantees, standby letters of credit, corporate guarantees, cash deposits, and insurance credit policies. Each instrument must record: issuing bank (with SWIFT code), instrument number, face value and currency, effective date, expiry date (Gregorian and Hijri), beneficiary entity, auto-renewal terms if applicable, and claim procedure reference. The system must reduce the customer net credit exposure by the instrument face value (or a configurable haircut percentage based on instrument type and issuing bank rating). Expiry alerts must be generated at 90, 60, 30, and 14 days before maturity. AI must assess the quality of guarantees based on issuing bank credit rating and country risk, applying appropriate haircut percentages automatically. For Islamic finance instruments like Kafalah guarantees common in GCC, the system must accommodate Sharia-compliant guarantee structures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R007	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Hold and Release Automation	The system must automatically place credit holds on customer operations when credit exposure exceeds the approved limit, configurable with a tolerance band (default 5% soft hold, 10% hard hold). Soft holds must prevent new booking acceptance but allow in-progress shipments to complete including BL release and container delivery at destination ports. Hard holds must block all new transactions and require explicit credit controller release for each pending operation. The AI engine must intelligently prioritize hold actions, for example allowing release of a high-margin FCL shipment at Jebel Ali while holding a low-margin LCL consolidation at JNPT if partial release would bring exposure below the hard hold threshold. Credit release must support bulk release for multiple held transactions with a single approval action, and the system must log the financial impact (estimated revenue at risk) of each hold decision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R008	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Customer Credit Application Processing	The system must provide a digital credit application workflow for new and existing customers requesting credit facilities. The application form must capture: requested credit amount per entity, requested payment terms, trade references (minimum three), bank references, latest audited financial statements (mandatory for limits above USD 100,000 equivalent), business description including shipping lanes and commodity types, and expected monthly TEU volume per port. AI must auto-populate available fields from existing customer data and external sources (company registries, credit bureaus). The AI credit engine must generate a preliminary credit recommendation within 4 hours of complete application submission, including suggested limit amount, payment terms, security requirements, and risk rating. Applications must support bilingual Arabic and English submission with all attachments accepted in either language with AI-powered OCR extraction for Arabic financial documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R009	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Portfolio-Level Credit Risk Dashboard	The system must provide a portfolio-level credit risk dashboard for management reporting showing aggregate credit exposure by entity, customer segment (shipping lines, freight forwarders, consignees, etc.), industry sector, country of domicile, risk rating band, and currency. The dashboard must display concentration risk metrics including largest single customer exposure as percentage of total portfolio, top 10 customer exposure concentration, and sector concentration ratios. Stress testing scenarios must be configurable, for example modeling the impact of a 20% currency devaluation of INR on the India entity portfolio or a major shipping line default scenario. All dashboard visualizations must render in both Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR) with data exportable to Excel and PDF formats. The dashboard must refresh automatically every 15 minutes during business hours with manual refresh capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R008	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma DA Template Management	The system must support creation and management of proforma DA templates for frequently called ports and vessel types. Templates must capture standard charge items, default calculation parameters, typical ancillary costs, and port-specific notes. Users must be able to create templates from scratch or clone from historical DAs. Templates must be versioned with effective dates and must be entity-specific (separate templates for Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, and India operations). The system must suggest the most appropriate template when a new proforma is initiated based on matching port code, vessel size class, and cargo type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R010	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	External Credit Bureau Integration	The system must integrate with relevant external credit information providers through API or file-based interfaces: Etihad Credit Bureau (Al Etihad Credit Bureau) for UAE customers, SIMAH for KSA customers, CIBIL/TransUnion for India customers, and international providers (Dun & Bradstreet, Coface) for cross-border credit intelligence. Integration must support automated periodic credit report retrieval (configurable frequency, default quarterly) and on-demand queries for new credit applications. Retrieved credit scores and reports must be stored within the customer account with full version history. The AI engine must normalize disparate scoring scales from different bureaus into a unified internal score for cross-entity comparison. Data handling must comply with each jurisdiction data protection regulations including Qatar Law No. 13 of 2016, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021 on personal data protection, KSA Personal Data Protection Law, and India Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R011	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Insurance Policy Management	The system must manage credit insurance policies from providers such as Euler Hermes, Atradius, and Coface that cover accounts receivable portfolio risk. Policy details must include: insurer name, policy number, coverage percentage (typically 80-90%), maximum single buyer limit, aggregate policy limit, premium rate, policy period, excluded countries or sectors, and claims filing procedures. The system must automatically allocate insured credit limits per customer based on the insurance policy buyer limits and track cumulative exposure against the aggregate policy cap. When a customer account enters overdue status exceeding the policy notification threshold (typically 60-90 days), the system must auto-generate a preliminary insurance claim notification per the policy terms. AI must optimize credit limit allocation between insured and uninsured portions to maximize coverage efficiency while minimizing premium costs based on the customer risk profile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R012	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Country and Sovereign Risk Assessment	The system must incorporate country-level risk scoring into customer credit assessments, particularly relevant for cross-border shipping transactions involving multiple jurisdictions along Middle East and South Asian trade routes. Country risk factors must include: sovereign credit rating (from Moody's, S&P, Fitch), political stability index, currency convertibility and transfer restrictions, sanctions screening status (OFAC, EU, UN sanctions lists), and bilateral trade agreement status. For customers domiciled in high-risk countries, the system must automatically require enhanced security (bank guarantee, letter of credit, or cash-in-advance) regardless of individual credit score. The AI engine must monitor geopolitical developments and automatically adjust country risk scores, alerting credit controllers when a country risk downgrade affects existing customer credit exposures. Sanctions list screening must occur in real-time at both account creation and every transaction initiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R013	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Limit Currency Conversion and Hedging	The system must handle credit limit calculations across multiple currencies with configurable exchange rate methodologies. For customers trading across entities in different currencies, the system must convert all exposures to a common reporting currency (configurable, default USD) using either: daily central bank rates (QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI), monthly average rates, or contracted fixed rates per customer agreement. The system must alert credit controllers when significant currency movements (configurable threshold, default 5% within 30 days) materially impact a customer credit utilization ratio in the reporting currency. For the QAR-USD and AED-USD pegged currency pairs, the system must use the fixed peg rates (QAR 3.64 per USD, AED 3.6725 per USD) while for floating currencies (SAR within band, INR fully floating), dynamic rate updates must be applied. AI must predict potential credit limit breaches arising from currency volatility for INR-denominated exposures and recommend preemptive limit adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R014	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Committee Meeting Support and Documentation	The system must generate comprehensive credit committee meeting packs including: agenda with customer cases requiring committee decision, individual customer credit proposals with AI-generated risk summaries, portfolio risk analysis with changes since last meeting, overdue and watch-list account reviews, and credit policy compliance metrics. Meeting packs must be generated in both Arabic and English with financial data presented per entity-specific accounting standards. The system must record committee decisions (approved, rejected, deferred, conditionally approved), meeting minutes, voting records, and action items with assigned owners and due dates. Post-meeting, approved credit decisions must automatically update customer credit limits and terms with committee reference numbers. Meeting scheduling must support Hijri calendar date selection for GCC entities that follow Hijri-based meeting cycles. AI must prioritize cases by risk urgency and estimated revenue impact to optimize committee agenda ordering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R015	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Early Warning System for Credit Deterioration	The AI-powered early warning system must continuously monitor leading indicators of customer credit deterioration including: gradual DSO increase trend (exceeding 10% increase over rolling 3 months), reduced shipping volumes compared to committed minimums, increasing dispute frequency, industry-specific distress signals (such as shipping rate collapse on customer key trade lanes), negative news sentiment analysis from maritime and financial news sources, and peer comparison deterioration. The system must generate tiered alerts: Level 1 (Watch - informational to credit controller), Level 2 (Concern - mandatory review within 7 days), Level 3 (Action Required - immediate credit hold recommendation). Each alert must include AI-generated narrative explaining the trigger factors, potential financial exposure at risk, and recommended mitigation actions. The early warning system must achieve a minimum 75% detection rate for eventual defaults with at least 60 days advance notice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R016	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Credit Terms Negotiation Tracking and History	The system must maintain a complete history of credit terms negotiations per customer including all proposals, counterproposals, and final agreed terms. Each negotiation record must capture: date (Gregorian and Hijri), initiating party (customer or internal), proposed terms (limit amount, payment days, security requirements, pricing concessions), negotiation notes, and outcome status. The system must track concessions made during negotiations (extended payment terms, reduced security requirements, higher unsecured limits) and their cumulative financial impact. AI must analyze negotiation patterns to recommend optimal starting positions for credit term discussions, factoring in the customer total relationship value across all entities and ports (container volumes at Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT, plus freight revenue and ancillary charges). The system must generate alerts when negotiated terms deviate significantly from the credit policy guidelines, requiring documented exception approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-002-R017	FEAT-016-1-002	MOD-016	Intercompany Credit Exposure Netting	The system must support netting of credit exposures across entities for customers that have both receivable and payable relationships with different group companies. For example, a shipping line may owe freight charges to the Qatar entity while simultaneously being owed agency commission by the UAE entity. The system must calculate net exposure per customer group considering: gross receivables per entity, gross payables per entity (from AP module integration), enforceable netting agreements with legal jurisdiction validation, and currency of each exposure component. Netting must only be applied when a legally enforceable netting agreement exists, validated per the governing law (Qatar Financial Centre regulations, UAE Federal Law, KSA Commercial Law, or Indian Contract Act as applicable). AI must identify netting opportunities and quantify the credit risk reduction benefit, recommending netting arrangements to credit controllers for customers where bilateral exposures exceed a configurable threshold (default USD 100,000 equivalent).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R001	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Configurable Aging Bucket Definitions	The system must support fully configurable aging bucket definitions per entity, allowing each operating company (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to define distinct aging periods reflecting local business practices and regulatory reporting requirements. Default aging buckets must include: Current (not yet due), 1-30 days overdue, 31-60 days overdue, 61-90 days overdue, 91-180 days overdue, 181-365 days overdue, and over 365 days overdue. Bucket boundaries must be adjustable by authorized users with audit trail logging. The system must support parallel aging bucket configurations for different reporting purposes, for example management reporting buckets versus regulatory reporting buckets (IFRS 9 expected credit loss staging: Stage 1 performing, Stage 2 underperforming, Stage 3 non-performing). Aging calculation must use the invoice due date as the baseline, not the invoice date, with configurable grace period days per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R002	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Multi-Currency Aging Report with Real-Time Conversion	The aging report must display balances in both the original invoice currency and a configurable reporting currency (entity functional currency or group consolidation currency in USD). Exchange rates for aging report conversion must be selectable: current spot rate (default), invoice date rate, period-end rate, or average rate for the reporting period. The report must clearly display unrealized foreign exchange gains and losses per aging bucket resulting from rate movements since invoice issuance. For pegged currencies (QAR/USD at 3.64, AED/USD at 3.6725), fixed rates must be used. For floating currencies (INR, SAR band), the system must source rates from the configured central bank feed (RBI, SAMA). The report must flag invoices where unrealized FX loss exceeds a configurable percentage threshold (default 5%) of the original invoice value, alerting the credit controller to potential collection shortfalls when receiving payment in a depreciated currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R003	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Aging Analysis by Shipping Transaction Type	The system must support aging analysis segmented by shipping transaction type including: ocean freight (FCL and LCL separately), terminal handling charges (THC) by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), detention charges, demurrage charges, documentation fees (BL fees, telex release, switch BL), customs brokerage fees, inland transportation charges, container surveying fees, and reefer monitoring charges. Each transaction type must be independently analyzable to identify systematic collection issues by service category. For example, detention and demurrage charges at JNPT may show higher aging than freight charges at Jebel Ali, indicating port-specific collection challenges. The AI engine must identify statistically significant aging pattern differences across transaction types and ports, generating actionable insights for the credit control team about which service categories and port combinations require focused collection efforts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R004	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Hijri Calendar Aging Report Support	The system must support aging analysis with Hijri calendar dates as the primary date reference for GCC entity reports, particularly for customers and regulatory submissions that follow the Islamic calendar. Aging bucket calculations must correctly handle Hijri month boundaries (which vary in length between 29 and 30 days per lunar observation). The aging report header must display both Hijri and Gregorian dates for the report period. Users must be able to filter and generate aging reports by Hijri date ranges, Hijri month-end periods, and Hijri fiscal year boundaries. For entities using a fiscal year aligned with the Hijri calendar (relevant for some KSA government-linked entities and Qatari ministries), the aging report must correctly calculate period-over-period comparisons on the Hijri calendar. The system must maintain a validated Hijri-Gregorian date mapping table updated annually with Umm al-Qura calendar data for accurate conversions across both calendar systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R005	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Customer Statement of Account Generation	The system must generate customer statements of account in both detailed and summary formats, available in Arabic and English with bilingual headers and footers. Detailed statements must list every transaction (invoices, credit notes, debit notes, payments, adjustments) in chronological order with running balance. Summary statements must show opening balance, total debits, total credits, and closing balance per aging bucket. Statements must include: entity legal name and address (Arabic and English), VAT/GST registration number, customer account number and name (Arabic and English), statement period, currency, and due amounts by aging bucket. Statement delivery must be automated per customer communication preferences (email PDF, WhatsApp, postal mail) with configurable frequency (monthly default, weekly or bi-weekly for high-risk accounts). The system must track statement delivery confirmation and log customer acknowledgment of statement receipt where available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R006	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	IFRS 9 Expected Credit Loss Provisioning	The system must calculate Expected Credit Loss (ECL) provisions per IFRS 9 requirements, applicable across all four entities for consolidated group reporting. The ECL model must implement: Stage 1 (12-month ECL for performing receivables), Stage 2 (lifetime ECL for receivables with significant credit risk increase), and Stage 3 (lifetime ECL for credit-impaired receivables). The AI engine must determine stage classification based on quantitative triggers (days past due thresholds configurable per entity, default: Stage 2 at 30 DPD, Stage 3 at 90 DPD) and qualitative factors (customer credit score deterioration, industry distress signals, country risk downgrades). Probability of Default (PD), Loss Given Default (LGD), and Exposure at Default (EAD) parameters must be calculated using historical loss data from the ERP with minimum 3-year lookback period. ECL calculations must be performed monthly with full audit trail and supporting documentation suitable for external auditor review. The system must generate IFRS 9 disclosure reports showing ECL movements, stage migration analysis, and provision adequacy assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R007	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Aging Report Drill-Down to Source Documents	Every line item in the aging report must support drill-down navigation to the source transaction document. For invoices, drill-down must display the full invoice detail including linked Bill of Lading number, booking reference, container numbers, vessel name and voyage, port pair (origin and destination from UNLOCODE-coded ports such as QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), INCOTERMS basis, and commodity description. For credit notes and debit notes, drill-down must show the original invoice reference and adjustment reason. For payments, drill-down must navigate to the cash receipt record showing bank reference, deposit date, and allocation details. The drill-down must operate within the same browser session without losing the aging report context, allowing users to navigate between summary and detail views. All drill-down data must respect entity-level data access controls, ensuring users from the Qatar entity cannot drill into India entity source documents unless granted cross-entity access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R008	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Automated Aging Report Scheduling and Distribution	The system must support scheduled automatic generation and distribution of aging reports to configurable recipient lists. Schedules must be definable per entity with options for: daily (for management), weekly (for credit controllers), monthly (for month-end close), and on-demand generation. Reports must be distributable via email (PDF and Excel attachments), saved to a shared document management system, and available on the web dashboard. Each scheduled report must have a configurable recipient list based on organizational role (Credit Manager, CFO, Regional Controller) with entity-level filtering ensuring recipients only receive reports for their authorized entities. The system must log report generation timestamps, recipient lists, and delivery status. Failed deliveries must trigger automatic retry with escalation to system administrators after three consecutive failures. Report generation for the full portfolio (all entities, all customers) must complete within 5 minutes for datasets up to 50,000 active customer accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R009	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	AI Predictive Collection Forecasting	The AI engine must generate predictive collection forecasts per customer and in aggregate, estimating the expected cash inflow per week for the next 12 weeks based on historical payment behavior analysis. The prediction model must consider: customer historical payment patterns (average days to pay, consistency of payment timing), invoice characteristics (amount, transaction type, port of origin, whether disputed), current aging bucket, customer credit health score, seasonal payment patterns (delays during Ramadan, Eid holidays, Diwali, Indian fiscal year-end March), and macro-economic indicators (shipping rate indices, currency movements). Forecasts must be presented with confidence intervals (70% and 90% probability bands) and must be updated daily during business hours. The AI must achieve a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of less than 15% on 4-week-ahead collection forecasts measured on a rolling quarterly basis. Forecast reports must be available in both Arabic and English with amounts in entity functional currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R010	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Bad Debt Write-Off Reporting and Tracking	The system must provide comprehensive reporting on bad debt write-offs including: write-off amount per customer and entity, original invoice details (date, amount, transaction type, port reference), write-off date (Gregorian and Hijri), approval chain and authorization level, reason classification (bankruptcy, absconding debtor, statute-barred, commercial settlement, uneconomical to collect), recovery expectation percentage, and tax implications per entity jurisdiction. For Qatar and GCC entities, the report must identify write-offs that qualify for VAT bad debt relief per respective tax authority guidelines (GTA, FTA, ZATCA) and generate the required supporting documentation. For India entity, the system must track write-offs against the GST credit note requirements and Section 36(1)(vii) of the Income Tax Act provisions. AI must flag potential bad debt candidates when receivables exceed entity-specific aging thresholds (configurable, default 365 days) and have shown no payment activity for 180 consecutive days, generating preliminary write-off recommendations with supporting evidence packages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R011	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Comparative Aging Analysis with Trend Visualization	The system must generate comparative aging analysis showing period-over-period trends with visual charts and graphs. Comparison periods must be configurable: month-over-month, quarter-over-quarter, year-over-year, and Hijri year-over-year for GCC entities. Visualizations must include: stacked bar charts showing aging bucket distribution evolution, trend lines for total overdue amounts, DSO (Days Sales Outstanding) trend per entity and consolidated, and collection effectiveness index (CEI) trends. The system must highlight statistically significant deteriorations using AI analysis, for example flagging when the 91-180 day bucket has grown by more than 20% month-over-month or when DSO has increased by more than 5 days compared to the trailing 6-month average. All charts must support bilingual rendering with proper Arabic RTL text placement for axis labels, legends, and annotations. Data must be exportable in tabular format for further analysis in external tools with currency and date format preservation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R012	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Port-Specific Aging Analysis and Benchmarking	The system must generate aging analysis segmented by port of operation, enabling comparison of collection performance across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Port-specific metrics must include: average DSO per port, overdue percentage by port, most problematic customer-port combinations, and port-specific charge categories with highest aging (e.g., demurrage at JNPT versus detention at Jebel Ali). The system must benchmark each port aging performance against configurable targets set by management, highlighting ports that are underperforming relative to their targets. AI must analyze port-specific factors influencing aging such as local customs clearance delays, port congestion impact on demurrage disputes, and regulatory processing times, providing contextual explanations for aging variances between ports. The report must support filtering by container type (20ft, 40ft, 40ft HC, reefer, flat rack, open top) to identify whether specific equipment types drive aging patterns at particular ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R002	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Automated Dunning Communication Generation	The system must auto-generate dunning communications in both Arabic and English, selecting the appropriate language based on the customer's preferred language and the originating entity's locale. Arabic communications must render correctly in RTL format across email, PDF, and SMS channels. Each communication must include the outstanding invoice references, original shipment details (BL number, container numbers, vessel name, voyage number), port of loading, port of discharge, and a detailed aging breakdown in the customer's base currency. Templates must comply with Qatar Financial Centre, DIFC, and SAMA communication standards for debt collection notices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R013	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Disputed Invoice Aging Segregation	The system must segregate disputed invoices from the main aging analysis and present them in a separate aging report section with dispute-specific attributes. Disputed invoice aging must show: original invoice details, dispute raised date, dispute category (rate discrepancy, service failure, documentation error, cargo damage claim, incorrect weight or measurement, wrong port charges, duplicate billing), assigned dispute handler, dispute resolution SLA status (days elapsed versus target resolution days), and estimated financial impact of the dispute (amount under dispute versus total invoice). The aging of disputed invoices must be calculated from the dispute date rather than the invoice due date for management reporting purposes, while maintaining the original due-date-based aging for regulatory and IFRS 9 provisioning purposes. AI must predict dispute resolution likelihood and expected settlement amounts based on historical dispute resolution outcomes by category, customer, and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R014	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Regulatory Aging Report Formats per Jurisdiction	The system must generate aging reports in formats mandated by regulatory authorities per jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must produce reports per Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA) guidelines and Qatar Central Bank reporting templates. For UAE, reports must comply with Central Bank of UAE prudential reporting requirements. For KSA, reports must align with ZATCA and Saudi Central Bank (SAMA) reporting templates. For India, reports must satisfy Reserve Bank of India (RBI) non-banking financial company reporting requirements if applicable, and Companies Act Schedule III balance sheet format for trade receivables disclosure. Each jurisdictional report format must be version-controlled to accommodate regulatory updates. The system must alert compliance officers when regulatory format changes are detected or announced. AI must automatically map internal aging data to each regulatory format template, reducing manual reformatting effort by at least 90%. Reports must include all mandatory fields per jurisdiction including entity registration numbers, authorized signatory details, and certification statements in the appropriate language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R015	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Aging Data Export and API Access	The system must provide multiple data export options for aging analysis data including: Excel export with preserved formatting, cell-level formulas for calculated fields, and multi-sheet workbooks for multi-entity reports; PDF export with configurable page layout (A4, Letter), orientation, and digital watermarking indicating confidentiality classification; CSV export for data integration with external analytics tools; and RESTful API endpoints for programmatic access to aging data with OAuth 2.0 authentication and rate limiting. API responses must support JSON and XML formats with pagination for large datasets (configurable page size, default 500 records). The API must provide filtering capabilities by entity, customer, aging bucket, currency, port, transaction type, and date range (Gregorian and Hijri). All export and API access must enforce entity-level and user-level security permissions, ensuring users cannot extract data beyond their authorization scope. API usage must be logged with requester identity, query parameters, and data volume for security audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R016	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Aging Analysis Integration with Collection Workflow	The aging report must integrate directly with the collection action workflow, allowing credit controllers to initiate collection actions from within the aging report interface. Actions available must include: generate dunning letter (with templates per aging bucket and entity language), schedule customer follow-up call with calendar integration, record payment promise with expected date and amount, escalate to senior credit controller or legal department, place or lift credit hold on the customer account, and create a collection case for concentrated follow-up. Each action must be logged against the customer account with timestamps and handler identity. The system must generate a daily collection action summary report showing actions taken versus overdue balance per credit controller, enabling management oversight of collection productivity. AI must recommend prioritized collection actions based on the customer aging profile, outstanding amount, credit health score, and historical response patterns to different collection approaches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-003-R017	FEAT-016-1-003	MOD-016	Consolidated Multi-Entity Aging Summary	The system must generate a consolidated aging summary across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with proper elimination of intercompany balances. Consolidation must convert all entity balances to the group reporting currency (USD) using configurable exchange rates (period-end rate for balance sheet items per IAS 21). The consolidated report must display: total group receivables by aging bucket, entity-level subtotals with currency of origin, top 20 customers by group exposure, intercompany receivables identified and eliminated with reference to corresponding AP balances, and group-level ECL provision adequacy. The report must reconcile to the general ledger AR control account balances per entity with variance explanations for any differences. Access to the consolidated report must be restricted to authorized group-level finance personnel. The consolidation engine must handle timing differences where entities close at different calendar points (India March fiscal year-end versus GCC December year-end) by using aligned snapshot dates for like-for-like comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R001	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	AI-Powered Automatic Payment Matching	The system must implement an AI-driven payment matching engine that automatically matches incoming bank receipts to outstanding customer invoices with a target straight-through processing rate of 95%. The matching engine must use multiple matching criteria in weighted combination: bank reference matching to invoice number, remittance advice parsing (structured MT940/CAMT.053 and unstructured text), payment amount matching (exact, partial, and overpayment scenarios), customer bank account identification against stored bank details, and historical payment pattern recognition. For container shipping specific scenarios, the engine must recognize BL numbers, booking references, container numbers, and voyage references embedded in payment narratives to facilitate matching. The AI confidence score must be displayed for each proposed match, with auto-posting enabled above a configurable threshold (default 90% confidence). Matches below the threshold must be queued for manual review with AI-suggested alternatives ranked by likelihood.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R003	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Automatic Credit Hold Trigger on Escalation	When an overdue invoice reaches a configurable escalation tier (default: 45 days past due), the system must automatically place a credit hold on the customer account, blocking new booking confirmations, Bill of Lading releases, and Delivery Order issuances across all entities. The credit hold must propagate in real-time to the booking module (MOD-003), documentation module (MOD-005), and container tracking module (MOD-007). The system must generate an Arabic/English notification to the customer, the responsible sales representative, and the credit controller, specifying the blocked services and the outstanding balance in all applicable currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R002	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Bank Statement Import and Processing	The system must support automated import of bank statements from all banking partners across the four entities via SWIFT MT940, MT942, ISO 20022 CAMT.052/053/054 formats, and entity-specific formats required by local banks (QNB and Qatar Islamic Bank for Qatar, Emirates NBD and FAB for UAE, Al Rajhi and NCB for KSA, SBI and HDFC for India). Import must be schedulable at configurable intervals (minimum every 2 hours during banking hours per entity timezone: AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India). Each imported statement must be validated for completeness (opening/closing balance verification, transaction count reconciliation) before processing. The system must handle multi-currency bank accounts and automatically identify the transaction currency versus the account base currency. Duplicate statement detection must prevent re-import of previously processed statements using a combination of statement number, date, and bank identifier. Failed imports must generate immediate alerts to the treasury team with diagnostic details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R003	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Partial Payment Application and Tracking	The system must handle partial payments where the received amount is less than the outstanding invoice total. Partial payment application must support two modes: proportional allocation (distributing the payment across multiple invoices proportionally to their outstanding amounts) and specific allocation (applying the payment to specified invoices in a user-defined or AI-recommended sequence). The default allocation sequence must be configurable per entity: oldest invoice first (FIFO), highest priority invoice first, or highest interest-bearing invoice first. For partial payments against shipping invoices, the system must track the remaining unpaid portion per invoice and maintain this as a separate aging item linked to the original invoice with cross-reference to the partial payment receipt. The system must not allow partial payment application that would leave a residual balance below a configurable minimum threshold (default USD 10 equivalent), to prevent uneconomical collection of trivial amounts. AI must predict the likelihood of the remaining balance being collected based on the customer payment history and remaining amount significance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R004	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Overpayment Handling and Refund Processing	When a customer payment exceeds the total outstanding balance or the specified invoice amounts, the system must identify the overpayment and provide configurable handling options: hold as unapplied credit for future invoice offset, hold as advance payment against upcoming known shipments (linked to booking references at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT), or initiate a refund workflow. Refund requests must follow a maker-checker approval process with the authorization level determined by refund amount per entity delegation matrix. Refund must be processed to the customer original payment source bank account by default, with alternative bank account refunds requiring enhanced verification. The system must generate the appropriate accounting entries: debit unapplied cash or customer advance account, credit bank account, with correct VAT/GST treatment per entity jurisdiction. AI must analyze overpayment patterns to identify customers who systematically overpay and suggest process improvements such as updated standing instruction amounts or automated advance allocation rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R005	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Multi-Currency Payment Allocation with FX Handling	The system must allocate payments received in currencies different from the invoice currency, calculating and posting realized foreign exchange gains or losses. For example, a customer paying USD for an invoice issued in QAR must use the configured exchange rate to determine the QAR equivalent and post any variance to the realized FX gain/loss account. The exchange rate for conversion must be sourced from the payment value date rate per the configured rate source (Central Bank rate as default). For cross-currency payments involving pegged currencies (QAR-USD, AED-USD), the system must use fixed peg rates. The system must handle payment allocation where a single multi-currency payment covers invoices in different currencies, calculating individual FX gain/loss per invoice line. Rounding differences arising from currency conversions must be posted to a configurable rounding tolerance account when below a threshold (default USD 1 equivalent). AI must flag unusual FX rate discrepancies between the applied rate and market rate that may indicate data entry errors or potential fraudulent transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R006	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Remittance Advice Processing and Reconciliation	The system must process customer remittance advices received through multiple channels: email attachments (PDF, Excel, CSV), EDI REMADV messages, customer portal uploads, and SWIFT MT110 advices. AI-powered OCR and NLP must extract payment allocation instructions from unstructured remittance advices, identifying invoice numbers, BL references, booking numbers, container numbers, deducted amounts, and reason codes for deductions. The extraction must handle bilingual remittance advices containing both Arabic and English text with a minimum accuracy of 95% for structured fields (invoice numbers, amounts) and 85% for unstructured notes. Extracted data must be presented to the cash application team for confirmation before auto-matching, with discrepancies highlighted. The system must track remittance advice receipt against bank statement entries, flagging payments received without corresponding remittance advice (requiring investigation) and remittance advices received without matching bank entries (potential upcoming payments). For shipping-specific remittances that reference BL numbers rather than invoice numbers, the system must resolve BL numbers to the corresponding invoices through integration with the BL module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R007	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Deduction and Short Payment Management	The system must manage customer deductions and short payments where customers reduce their payment from the invoiced amount with or without prior notification. Common shipping industry deductions must be categorized including: rate dispute (contracted rate versus invoiced rate), cargo damage claim deduction, weight/measurement discrepancy, duplicate billing claim, service failure deduction (late delivery, container damage), previously issued credit note offset, and unauthorized deduction. Each deduction must generate a deduction case linked to the original invoice, customer account, and payment receipt. The deduction workflow must route cases to the appropriate handler based on deduction category: rate disputes to commercial team, cargo claims to claims department, billing errors to AR team. AI must classify deductions automatically based on the remittance advice narrative and historical deduction patterns for the customer, achieving minimum 80% correct classification rate. Deductions must age separately in the aging report and must be resolved within configurable SLA periods (default 30 days for billing disputes, 90 days for cargo claims).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R008	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Cross-Entity Payment Application	The system must support payment application where a customer makes a single payment to one entity that covers invoices across multiple entities. For example, a customer may remit payment to the UAE entity bank account covering invoices from both the UAE entity (Jebel Ali port operations) and the Qatar entity (Hamad Port operations). The system must create intercompany settlement entries: crediting the receiving entity AR and debiting the intercompany receivable from the owing entity, while posting the corresponding intercompany payable and AR clearing in the owing entity. Cross-entity allocation must comply with transfer pricing regulations applicable in each jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and generate the required intercompany documentation. The system must maintain a cross-entity settlement ledger tracking all such transactions for intercompany reconciliation. Currency conversion between entities must use intercompany transfer rates as defined in the group transfer pricing policy. AI must validate that cross-entity allocations are commercially reasonable and flag transactions that may raise transfer pricing concerns under OECD guidelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R009	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Unapplied Cash Management and Aging	Payments that cannot be immediately matched to specific invoices must be posted to an unapplied cash account per entity and customer. The system must separately age unapplied cash items and generate daily reports of unresolved unapplied items with aging beyond configurable thresholds (default alert at 3 days, escalation at 7 days, management review at 14 days). Unapplied cash must be displayed on the customer account dashboard alongside the regular aging analysis. The system must provide a dedicated unapplied cash workbench allowing cash application staff to review, match, and allocate pending items with AI-suggested matching options. For shipping industry scenarios, unapplied cash often arises from advance payments for upcoming shipments, container deposits, or consolidated payments covering multiple BLs across ports. AI must proactively suggest likely matching candidates by analyzing expected upcoming invoices from active bookings and in-transit shipments. The system must prevent unapplied cash items from being included in customer balance confirmation letters unless explicitly authorized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R010	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Payment Reversal and Re-Application Workflow	The system must support payment reversal for scenarios including: returned checks (bounced), chargeback on card payments, bank error reversals, incorrect initial application requiring reallocation, and fraudulent payment detection. Reversal must restore the original invoice balances, recalculate aging from the original due dates, and update the customer credit exposure in real-time. Reversed items must be flagged on the customer account with a reversal reason code and must generate automatic notification to the credit controller for risk assessment. For check returns (common in India and parts of GCC), the system must automatically apply a returned check penalty fee per entity policy and update the customer credit score negatively. Re-application workflow must allow cash application staff to reallocate a payment to different invoices without requiring full reversal and re-receipt, maintaining the original receipt date for cash flow reporting purposes. All reversals must generate complete audit trail entries including original application details, reversal justification, and approver identity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R011	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Advance Payment and Deposit Tracking	The system must manage advance payments and container deposits received from customers before invoice generation. Advance payments must be categorized as: freight prepayment (for CIF/CFR INCOTERMS shipments where freight is paid before shipment from origin ports), container deposit (refundable deposit for SOC - Shipper Owned Container or leased container usage), customs duty advance (for customers using the entity as customs broker at JNPT or GCC ports), and general advance against future services. Each advance must be linked to specific booking references, container numbers, or service agreements where identifiable. The system must automatically apply advances against corresponding invoices when generated, following configurable matching rules (booking reference match, container number match, or chronological FIFO). Unapplied advances beyond configurable aging thresholds (default 90 days) must trigger review for potential forfeiture per the customer agreement terms or refund processing. The accounting treatment must separate advance payments from trade receivables per IFRS 15 contract liability recognition requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R012	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Cash Application Performance Metrics and Reporting	The system must track and report cash application efficiency metrics including: straight-through processing rate (target 95% for AI-matched payments), average time to apply (from bank statement import to full allocation), unapplied cash ratio (unapplied amount as percentage of total receipts), deduction resolution rate and average resolution time, payment matching accuracy (correct matches versus total AI-proposed matches), and cash application staff productivity (items processed per person per day). Metrics must be reportable by entity, by cash application team member, by customer segment, and by payment channel (wire transfer, check, LC proceeds, etc.). Trend analysis must be available showing month-over-month performance improvement correlating with AI model enhancements. The AI system must provide self-assessment metrics including false positive rate (incorrect auto-matches that required manual correction) and false negative rate (matches missed by AI that were later identified manually). Reports must be available in both Arabic and English with dashboard visualization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R013	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Letter of Credit Proceeds Application	The system must handle allocation of Letter of Credit (LC) proceeds, which are a significant payment mechanism in container shipping trade. When LC proceeds are received from the advising or confirming bank, the system must match the proceeds to the specific invoices covered by the LC based on the LC number, beneficiary drawing reference, and presented document details (BL number, commercial invoice number). The system must handle LC discrepancies where the bank payment is reduced due to documentary discrepancies (late presentation, BL clausing, missing documents), posting the discrepancy amount as a deduction case for follow-up with the customer. For deferred payment LCs (common in 60-90 day import LCs for India trade), the system must track the maturity date and expected payment date, creating a contingent receivable that converts to an actual receivable upon LC acceptance. Usance LC discounting proceeds must be correctly allocated with the discount charges posted to the appropriate finance cost account per entity. The system must support back-to-back LC and transferable LC scenarios common in NVOCC and trading house operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R014	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Automated Bank Reconciliation for AR	The system must perform automated reconciliation between the AR cash application records and bank statement entries, identifying and categorizing discrepancies. Reconciliation must match: total receipts per bank statement versus total receipts posted in AR, individual transaction matching between bank entries and AR receipts, and timing differences where bank credits appear before or after AR posting. Common discrepancies must be automatically classified: bank charges deducted from credited amount, intermediary bank charges on international wire transfers (common for cross-border shipping payments routed through correspondent banks), value date versus booking date differences, and partial bank credits for LC drawings. The reconciliation must produce a bank reconciliation statement per bank account per entity, formatted per the entity auditor requirements. Unreconciled items exceeding configurable thresholds (default USD 100 equivalent or 5 business days unresolved) must be escalated. AI must learn from historical reconciliation patterns to improve auto-classification accuracy, targeting 90% automatic reconciliation of standard discrepancy types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R015	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Payment Plan and Installment Tracking	The system must support creation and monitoring of structured payment plans for customers with overdue balances who have negotiated installment settlements. Each payment plan must define: total overdue amount covered, number of installments, installment amounts and due dates (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar scheduling), interest or late payment charges included (where permitted per entity jurisdiction and Sharia compliance requirements for Islamic banking arrangements), security or guarantee requirements, and consequences of default (typically automatic credit hold and plan cancellation). The system must generate automated reminders 7 days and 2 days before each installment due date via the customer preferred communication channel. Missed installments must trigger immediate alert to the credit controller with AI-recommended escalation action based on the payment plan performance history. The system must distinguish payment plan receivables from regular trade receivables in aging reports and IFRS 9 ECL calculations, applying modified staging criteria per entity policy. Payment plans must be approved through the credit authorization workflow with appropriate delegation levels based on the plan total amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R016	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Cash Application Workflow for Offline Scenarios	The system must support cash application operations during network outages or system unavailability through an offline-capable module. The offline module must allow cash application staff to: view pre-cached customer balances and outstanding invoices (synchronized within the last 24 hours), record payment receipts with basic allocation (customer, amount, currency, bank reference, and manual invoice selection), and queue allocations for server synchronization upon connectivity restoration. Offline recorded transactions must be stamped with offline mode indicators and require mandatory post-synchronization review by a supervisor within 4 hours. Conflict resolution for offline-applied payments must handle scenarios where the same invoice was paid by a different receipt during the offline period, flagging duplicates for investigation. The offline module must operate on standard web browsers with local storage capability and must not require separate application installation. This capability is particularly critical for operations at JNPT and remote port locations in India where connectivity interruptions are more frequent than at GCC port facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R017	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Netting and Offset Settlement Processing	The system must support netting and offset settlements where a customer has both AR (receivable) and AP (payable) balances with the same or related group entity. Netting must be executed through a formal netting agreement workflow that creates matching debit and credit entries in both AR and AP subledgers, eliminating the need for cash movement. The netting process must validate: existence of a legally enforceable netting agreement per the governing law of the relevant entity jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India), matching currencies or agreed cross-currency netting rates, both parties agreement to the specific invoices being netted (with digital confirmation workflow), and correct VAT/GST treatment where netting triggers deemed supply provisions. For container shipping, common netting scenarios include: freight forwarder commission offset against freight invoices, shipping line rebate offset against THC charges, and depot repair charges offset against container lease payments. AI must identify netting opportunities proactively by scanning both AR and AP ledgers for common counterparties and recommend optimal netting combinations that maximize cash flow efficiency while maintaining minimum exposure thresholds. The system must generate netting statements in both Arabic and English for counterparty confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-1-004-R018	FEAT-016-1-004	MOD-016	Cash Application Audit and Compliance Controls	The system must enforce comprehensive audit and compliance controls over the cash application process. Segregation of duties must be maintained: the user who records a bank receipt must not be the same user who applies it to invoices (configurable per entity based on team size). All manual cash application overrides (applying a payment against AI recommendation, forcing a match below the confidence threshold, or applying to a disputed invoice) must require documented justification and supervisor approval for amounts exceeding configurable thresholds (default USD 5,000 equivalent). The system must maintain a complete audit trail of every cash application action including: timestamp, user, action type (auto-match, manual match, reversal, reallocation), source document references, and any overrides applied. Period-end cash application completeness checks must verify that all bank statement entries within the period have been processed, all unapplied items have been reviewed, and the AR control account reconciles to the subledger within tolerance. AI must monitor cash application patterns for anomalies indicative of fraud or error, such as repeated application of payments to the same customer by the same operator, or systematic override of AI matching recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R001	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Configurable Multi-Tier Dunning Schedule	The system must support configurable dunning schedules with a minimum of six escalation tiers per entity (e.g., friendly reminder at 7 days, formal notice at 15 days, demand letter at 30 days, credit hold at 45 days, collection agency referral at 60 days, legal action at 90 days). Each tier must be independently configurable per legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to reflect local commercial practices and regulatory requirements. Schedules must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar calculations for KSA entities. The system must allow overrides for VIP shipping clients with custom payment terms tied to specific port operations at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), or JNPT (INNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R004	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Role-Based Escalation Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a role-based approval workflow for each escalation tier transition. Moving from friendly reminder to formal notice may be auto-approved, but transitions to credit hold, collection agency referral, or legal action must require explicit approval from designated authority levels (e.g., Credit Manager for credit hold, Finance Director for collection agency, CFO or Legal Head for legal action). Approval requests must be routed based on the outstanding amount thresholds configurable per entity and per currency. The workflow must support delegation of authority when approvers are unavailable, with automatic escalation to the next authority level after a configurable timeout period (default 48 hours).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R005	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Collection Task Assignment and Tracking	The system must automatically assign collection tasks to credit controllers based on configurable rules including customer portfolio assignment, outstanding amount ranges, geographic region (GCC vs. Indian subcontinent), and current workload balancing. Each task must track all collection activities including phone calls, emails, meetings, payment promises, and dispute resolutions with timestamps and outcomes. The system must provide a consolidated task queue with priority scoring based on invoice age, amount, customer risk rating, and shipment criticality (e.g., reefer containers with perishable cargo at Hamad Port requiring urgent release). Tasks must be accessible offline with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R006	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Payment Promise Tracking and Breach Detection	When a customer makes a payment promise during the collection process, the system must record the promised amount, promised date (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date entry), currency, and the specific invoices covered by the promise. The system must automatically monitor for promise fulfillment by matching incoming payments against recorded promises. If a payment promise is breached (no payment received by the promised date or partial payment below a configurable threshold of 90%), the system must automatically advance the escalation tier, notify the assigned credit controller, and update the customer's reliability score. Promise breach history must be retained for credit assessment purposes for a minimum of 36 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R007	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Dispute Management Integration	The system must allow credit controllers to flag invoices under dispute during the collection workflow, automatically pausing dunning actions for disputed amounts while continuing collection on undisputed balances. Dispute categories must include freight rate discrepancies, demurrage/detention charge disputes, THC (Terminal Handling Charges) disagreements, documentation fee conflicts, currency conversion rate disputes, and damage claims on cargo. Each dispute must be linked to the originating shipment (BL number, container ID, vessel, voyage) and routed to the appropriate department (Operations, Documentation, Claims) for resolution. Disputed invoices must be excluded from aging calculations for credit limit assessment but must remain visible in total exposure reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R008	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Multi-Entity Consolidated Collection View	The system must provide a consolidated collection view showing a customer's outstanding balances across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) in a single dashboard. Balances must be displayed in each entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with a consolidated total in the group reporting currency (USD). The view must show the highest escalation tier across all entities and allow cross-entity collection coordination where a single credit controller can manage a customer's global exposure. Entity-level segregation must be maintained for accounting purposes while enabling group-level credit decisions. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with properly aligned numeric columns and currency symbols.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R009	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Automated Escalation to External Collection Agency	When an overdue account reaches the collection agency referral tier and receives the required approval, the system must generate a standardized handover package containing the customer's KYC details, all relevant invoices with supporting shipping documentation (Bills of Lading, Delivery Orders, signed PODs), complete communication history, payment promise records, and a summary of the outstanding balance with aging breakdown. The package must be exportable in PDF format with Arabic/English bilingual content and in structured data format (JSON/XML) for electronic submission to registered collection agencies. The system must track the agency assignment, recovery progress, and fees, and must automatically reverse the assignment if the customer pays directly during the agency collection period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R010	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Legal Action Preparation and Tracking	Upon escalation to the legal action tier, the system must compile a comprehensive legal case file including the original contract/credit agreement, all invoices with shipping documentation (BL copies, container manifests, proof of delivery at destination ports), complete dunning communication trail, payment promise records and breaches, and any dispute resolution correspondence. The file must be formatted according to the jurisdiction of the applicable entity: Qatar courts (Arabic mandatory), DIFC courts (English accepted), KSA commercial courts (Arabic mandatory with Hijri dates), or Indian commercial courts (English). The system must track legal case numbers, hearing dates, legal costs, and outcomes, and must update the customer's credit profile based on legal proceedings status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R011	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	SMS and WhatsApp Notification Integration for Collections	The system must support sending collection reminders and payment due notifications via SMS and WhatsApp Business API in addition to email. Messages must be sent in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English) and must include a deep link or payment reference enabling quick payment. SMS and WhatsApp messages must comply with local telecommunications regulations in Qatar (CRA), UAE (TRA), KSA (CITC), and India (TRAI) including opt-out mechanisms and time-of-day restrictions. The system must maintain delivery receipts and read receipts where available, logging all communication attempts in the collection activity timeline. Message templates must be pre-approved per channel and per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R012	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Escalation SLA Monitoring and Alerts	The system must monitor escalation SLAs ensuring each tier transition occurs within the configured timeframe. If an escalation action (e.g., sending a dunning letter, obtaining approval, placing a credit hold) is overdue beyond a configurable grace period, the system must generate alerts to the responsible credit controller, their supervisor, and the credit management team. SLA metrics must be tracked per credit controller, per entity, and per customer segment (liner agency, NVOCC, freight forwarder, direct shipper). A real-time SLA compliance dashboard must display current performance with drill-down capability from entity level to individual collection cases. The dashboard must support both Arabic and English display with RTL-compatible progress indicators and status badges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R013	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	AI-Recommended Collection Strategy	The system must leverage AI to recommend optimal collection strategies for each overdue account based on historical payment behavior, customer segment, outstanding amount, shipment patterns, seasonal trends (e.g., increased shipping volumes during Hajj season or Indian festival periods), and macroeconomic indicators. Recommendations must include suggested communication channel (email, phone, SMS, in-person visit), optimal contact timing, recommended escalation pace (accelerated or extended), and likelihood of recovery at each tier. The AI model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical collection data across all entities and must be retrained quarterly. Recommendations must be presented as suggestions with confidence scores, allowing credit controllers to accept, modify, or reject them.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R014	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Interest and Late Fee Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate late payment interest and penalty fees based on configurable rules per entity and per customer contract. Calculations must comply with local regulations: Qatar Commercial Law interest caps, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 50/2022 on commercial transactions, KSA Sharia-compliant alternative penalty structures (which may prohibit interest-based charges in favor of fixed penalty amounts or charitable donation mechanisms), and Indian GST implications on late fees. The engine must support compound and simple interest methods, grace periods, tiered penalty rates based on days overdue, and maximum penalty caps. Calculated charges must be presented in the invoice currency with automatic multi-currency conversion for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R015	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Collection Performance Analytics and Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive collection performance reports including Days Sales Outstanding (DSO) by entity, customer segment, and trade lane, Collection Effectiveness Index (CEI), aging bucket distribution (current, 1-30, 31-60, 61-90, 90+ days), recovery rates by escalation tier, and collector productivity metrics. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. All monetary values must be displayed in local currency with USD equivalent. The system must support scheduled report generation (daily, weekly, monthly) aligned with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods, with automated distribution to configurable recipient lists. Historical trend analysis must cover a minimum of 24 rolling months with year-over-year comparison capabilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-001-R016	FEAT-016-2-001	MOD-016	Offline Collection Activity Recording	The system must allow credit controllers to record collection activities (call notes, meeting outcomes, payment promises, dispute details) while offline during customer visits or in areas with limited connectivity, particularly relevant for port area visits at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT where network coverage may be intermittent. Offline records must be stored locally on the device with encryption at rest, and must automatically synchronize with the central system when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution rules must handle scenarios where the same customer record was updated both offline and online simultaneously, with the system flagging conflicts for manual review rather than silently overwriting data. Offline capability must support both Arabic and English text entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R001	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	IFRS 9 Expected Credit Loss Model Implementation	The system must implement the IFRS 9 three-stage Expected Credit Loss (ECL) model for trade receivables arising from container shipping operations. Stage 1 must calculate 12-month ECL for performing receivables, Stage 2 must calculate lifetime ECL for receivables with significant increase in credit risk, and Stage 3 must calculate lifetime ECL for credit-impaired receivables. The model must incorporate forward-looking macroeconomic variables relevant to the shipping industry including global trade volume indices, oil prices (critical for Gulf operations), container freight rate indices (SCFI, WCI), and GDP growth forecasts for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The probability of default (PD), loss given default (LGD), and exposure at default (EAD) parameters must be configurable per customer segment and per trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R002	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Automated Provision Matrix by Aging Bucket	The system must maintain a simplified provision matrix as permitted under IFRS 9 for trade receivables without a significant financing component, which covers the majority of container shipping invoices with standard 30-60 day payment terms. The matrix must define provision percentages by aging bucket (current, 1-30 days past due, 31-60, 61-90, 91-180, 181-365, over 365 days) with separate matrices per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), per customer segment (liner principals, NVOCCs, freight forwarders, direct shippers), and per trade lane (intra-GCC, GCC-India, international). The matrix must be reviewed and updated at least quarterly based on actual loss experience, with AI-assisted recommendations for adjustment. Historical provision adequacy must be tracked to validate matrix accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R003	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Individual Assessment for Large Exposures	Receivables exceeding a configurable threshold per entity (e.g., QAR 500,000 for Qatar, AED 500,000 for UAE, SAR 500,000 for KSA, INR 10,000,000 for India) must be individually assessed for impairment rather than included in the collective provision matrix. The individual assessment must consider customer-specific factors including financial health indicators, payment history across all entities, current dispute status, shipping volume trends, and any collateral or guarantees held (bank guarantees, corporate guarantees, cargo liens). The system must generate a standardized individual assessment template in Arabic/English capturing the assessor's analysis, recommended provision percentage, and supporting evidence. Individual assessments must be reviewed and approved by the Credit Committee before being recorded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R004	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Automated Provision Journal Entry Generation	The system must automatically generate accounting journal entries for bad debt provisions at each reporting period (monthly, quarterly, or as configured per entity). Provision increase entries must debit Bad Debt Expense and credit Allowance for Doubtful Accounts, while provision decrease entries (reversals) must debit the Allowance and credit Bad Debt Recovery income. Journal entries must be generated per entity with the correct chart of accounts mapping, cost center allocation (by department, trade lane, or port of origin such as QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNPT), and currency denomination. The system must support both the local statutory currency and USD for group consolidation purposes. All generated entries must be staged for review and approval before posting to the General Ledger module (MOD-015).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R005	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Write-Off Request and Approval Workflow	The system must provide a formal write-off request workflow with multi-level approval based on the write-off amount. Configurable approval tiers must be defined per entity, for example: Credit Manager up to QAR 50,000, Finance Director up to QAR 200,000, CFO up to QAR 1,000,000, and Board approval for amounts exceeding QAR 1,000,000 (with equivalent thresholds in AED, SAR, INR). The write-off request must include a comprehensive case summary documenting all collection efforts undertaken, external agency results, legal action outcomes, customer financial status evidence, and the recommended write-off amount with supporting justification. The workflow must maintain a complete audit trail of all approvals and rejections with timestamps, approver identity, and comments in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R006	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Partial Write-Off Processing	The system must support partial write-offs where only a portion of an outstanding receivable is deemed uncollectible while the remainder continues to be pursued through collection efforts. Partial write-offs must be allocatable at the invoice line level, allowing write-off of specific charges (e.g., demurrage charges disputed and deemed uncollectible) while retaining the freight and documentation charges as active receivables. The system must maintain the relationship between the original invoice, the written-off portion, and the remaining collectible balance. Partial write-offs must trigger proportional adjustments to the existing provision balance, and the remaining balance must continue through the normal collection and aging process with updated provision calculations reflecting the reduced exposure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R007	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Write-Off Accounting Entry and Tax Treatment	Upon approval of a write-off, the system must automatically generate the appropriate accounting entries: debit Allowance for Doubtful Accounts (to the extent previously provisioned) and debit Bad Debt Expense (for any unprovisioned amount), with a credit to Accounts Receivable. The system must simultaneously handle VAT/GST recovery where applicable: in UAE and KSA, VAT on bad debts may be recoverable after 12 months under specific conditions per FTA and ZATCA regulations respectively; in India, GST credit notes must be issued within the prescribed time limits. The system must flag write-offs that qualify for tax deduction under each entity's jurisdiction and generate the required supporting documentation for tax filing purposes including original invoice details, collection evidence, and write-off approval records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R008	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Post-Write-Off Recovery Tracking	The system must maintain a register of all written-off receivables and continue to track any subsequent recoveries. If a payment is received against a previously written-off invoice, the system must automatically generate a recovery journal entry (debit Cash/Bank, credit Bad Debt Recovery income) and update the write-off register. Recovery entries must be allocated to the correct entity, period, and cost center matching the original write-off. The system must support both full and partial recoveries, maintaining a running balance of the unrecovered written-off amount. Recovery statistics must be tracked per customer, per entity, and per write-off period to inform future provisioning models. Alerts must be generated to credit controllers when payments are received from customers with written-off balances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R009	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Provision Adequacy Backtesting	The system must perform quarterly backtesting of provision adequacy by comparing actual credit losses experienced during the period against the provisions that were held at the start of the period. Backtesting results must be segmented by entity, customer segment, aging bucket, and trade lane to identify systematic over- or under-provisioning. The system must calculate and report the provision coverage ratio (actual losses divided by provisions held), with alerts generated when the ratio falls outside configurable thresholds (e.g., below 80% indicating under-provisioning or above 150% indicating excessive conservatism). Backtesting results must feed into the AI-assisted provision matrix adjustment recommendations and must be documented for external auditor review with full methodology transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R010	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Regulatory Reporting for Provisions	The system must generate provision-related regulatory reports as required by each entity's jurisdiction. For Qatar entities, reports must comply with Qatar Central Bank guidelines and QFC Authority requirements. For UAE entities, reports must meet Central Bank of UAE prudential standards and ADGM/DIFC reporting requirements where applicable. For KSA entities, reports must conform to SAMA guidelines and ZATCA requirements with all figures reported in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar periods. For India entities, reports must comply with RBI guidelines and Companies Act requirements. All reports must be available in the local language (Arabic for GCC entities, English for India) with a consolidated English version for group reporting. The system must maintain a regulatory reporting calendar with automated reminders and submission tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R011	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Credit Insurance Claim Integration	For receivables covered by trade credit insurance (common in container shipping for protecting against buyer insolvency), the system must track insurance coverage limits per customer, calculate the net exposure after insurance recovery, and adjust provision calculations to reflect the insured portion. When a receivable becomes delinquent, the system must generate insurance claim documentation including the policy number, insured event details, outstanding invoice information with shipping documentation (BL number, container details, cargo description), and collection effort evidence as required by the insurer. The system must track claim submission dates, insurer responses, approved claim amounts, and actual recoveries, adjusting provisions and write-offs based on insurance outcomes. Net exposure (gross receivable minus insured amount) must be used for provision matrix calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R012	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Collateral and Guarantee Offset in Provisioning	The system must incorporate collateral and guarantees held against customer exposures when calculating ECL provisions. Supported collateral types must include bank guarantees (common for liner agency agreements), corporate guarantees from parent companies, cash deposits, cargo liens (particularly for CFS/warehouse operations at ports like JNPT and Jebel Ali), and pledged assets. The system must maintain collateral records with expiry dates, coverage amounts in original currency with USD equivalent, and the issuing institution's credit rating. Provision calculations must reduce the Loss Given Default (LGD) component based on the estimated recoverable value of collateral, applying configurable haircuts by collateral type. The system must generate alerts 60 and 30 days before collateral expiry to enable timely renewal or replacement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R013	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Multi-Currency Provision Calculation and Reporting	The system must calculate provisions in the currency of each outstanding receivable and translate to the entity's functional currency and the group reporting currency (USD) using the closing exchange rate at the reporting date. Foreign exchange differences arising on provision balances between reporting periods must be separately identified and reported, distinguishing between changes due to revised provision estimates and changes due to currency movements. For customers with receivables in multiple currencies (e.g., a freight forwarder billed in QAR for Hamad Port operations and AED for Jebel Ali operations), the provision calculation must be performed at the individual invoice currency level before consolidation. The system must support revaluation of provision balances at interim reporting dates with configurable frequency (daily, weekly, monthly).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R014	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Audit Trail and External Auditor Access	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all provision calculations, adjustments, write-off requests, approvals, and reversals. Each audit record must capture the user identity, timestamp, action performed, values before and after the change, and the business justification. External auditors must be provided with a read-only portal access to review provision models, calculation parameters, individual assessments, approval workflows, and backtesting results. The portal must support the generation of audit confirmation letters and provision movement schedules in formats compatible with major audit firm methodologies. All audit trail data must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with the strictest applicable retention requirement across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R015	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Provision Reversal on Payment or Dispute Resolution	When a previously provisioned receivable is settled through payment, credit note, or dispute resolution, the system must automatically calculate and record the provision reversal. The reversal must be proportional to the settlement amount in cases of partial payment. For multi-currency settlements where the payment currency differs from the invoice currency, the provision reversal must account for any exchange rate differences between the original provision booking rate and the settlement date rate. The system must generate reversal journal entries with clear cross-references to the original provision entries and the triggering settlement transaction. Monthly provision movement reports must separately disclose new provisions, reversals due to settlements, reversals due to write-offs, exchange rate adjustments, and net movement, presented in both local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R016	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Intercompany Receivable Provisioning Rules	The system must handle provisioning rules for intercompany receivables between group entities (e.g., Qatar entity billing UAE entity for shared vessel space or transhipment services via Hamad Port). Intercompany receivables must be subject to separate provisioning rules that may differ from third-party receivable policies, typically with lower provision rates reflecting the controlled nature of the counterparty. However, the system must still track intercompany aging and escalate overdue intercompany balances through a separate workflow. At the group consolidation level, intercompany receivables and their provisions must be eliminated. The system must generate intercompany reconciliation reports showing outstanding balances, aging, and any provisions held, flagging discrepancies between the receivable and payable sides for resolution before consolidation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-002-R017	FEAT-016-2-002	MOD-016	Provision Dashboard with Drill-Down Capability	The system must provide an interactive provision dashboard displaying total provisions by entity, by IFRS 9 stage (Stage 1, 2, 3), by aging bucket, by customer segment, and by trade lane. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary level to individual customer and invoice level. Visual indicators must highlight significant period-over-period movements (provisions increasing more than 20% must be flagged amber, more than 50% flagged red). The dashboard must include trend charts showing provision levels over the past 24 months with overlay of actual write-off experience for backtesting visualization. All dashboard elements must support Arabic RTL rendering with properly formatted currency amounts in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. The dashboard must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats for board and audit committee presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R001	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Invoice-Level Payment Date Prediction	The system must predict the expected payment date for each outstanding invoice using a machine learning model trained on historical payment data. Prediction features must include customer historical payment patterns (average days to pay, standard deviation, trend direction), invoice characteristics (amount, currency, charge type such as ocean freight, THC, demurrage, detention), shipment attributes (trade lane, port pair e.g. QAHMD-INNPT, container type FCL/LCL, INCOTERM), seasonal patterns (Ramadan, Eid, Diwali, Chinese New Year effects on payment cycles), and current aging status. The model must output a predicted payment date with a confidence interval (e.g., 80% probability of payment between Day 35 and Day 42). Predictions must be updated daily as new data becomes available and must achieve a mean absolute error of less than 5 business days after the initial 6-month training period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R002	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Dynamic Customer Credit Risk Score	The system must calculate and maintain a dynamic credit risk score for each customer on a scale of 0-1000, updated daily. The scoring model must incorporate internal factors (payment history across all entities, current outstanding balance, credit utilization ratio, dispute frequency, collection escalation history, shipping volume trends) and external factors where available (company financial filings, industry credit ratings, country risk ratings for the customer's domicile, shipping market conditions). The score must be segmented into risk bands (e.g., 800-1000 Excellent, 600-799 Good, 400-599 Fair, 200-399 Poor, 0-199 Critical) with each band mapped to recommended credit terms, required approvals, and collection intensity levels. Score changes exceeding configurable thresholds must trigger alerts to credit controllers and auto-adjust collection priority queues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R003	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Payment Probability Scoring per Invoice	The system must assign a payment probability score to each outstanding invoice indicating the likelihood of collection within the standard payment terms, within 30 days of due date, within 60 days, within 90 days, and the probability of eventual write-off. The probability model must consider invoice-specific factors including the customer's historical behavior for similar invoice types (freight vs. local charges vs. demurrage), whether the shipment has been completed and cargo delivered, any open disputes or claims linked to the shipment, the customer's current financial stress indicators, and the effectiveness of collection actions already taken. Probability scores must be recalculated daily and must be used by the collection workflow to prioritize outreach efforts, focusing credit controller attention on invoices where timely intervention has the highest probability of accelerating payment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R004	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Seasonal and Cyclical Pattern Recognition	The AI model must identify and incorporate seasonal and cyclical payment patterns specific to the container shipping industry in the Middle East and India corridor. Key patterns must include Ramadan period slowdowns in GCC countries (payment processing delays during reduced working hours), post-Eid payment surges, Indian fiscal year-end cash conservation (January-March), Diwali festival period impacts, Chinese New Year effects on Far East trade lane payments, Hajj season logistics surges affecting Saudi operations, and monsoon season disruptions at JNPT affecting Indian payment cycles. The system must automatically detect these patterns from historical data without manual configuration and must apply seasonal adjustments to payment predictions and risk scores. Pattern detection must span at least 3 years of historical data to distinguish genuine seasonal effects from one-time anomalies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R005	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Early Warning System for Credit Deterioration	The system must implement an AI-powered early warning system that detects leading indicators of customer credit deterioration before invoices become overdue. Warning signals must include gradual extension of payment days (even if still within terms), reduction in shipping volumes, increased frequency of invoice disputes, changes in shipping patterns (e.g., switching from FCL to LCL suggesting reduced cargo volumes), delayed document retrieval (BL or DO pickup delays), requests for extended payment terms, changes in authorized signatories or contact persons, and negative news sentiment from external sources. When the model detects a deterioration pattern, it must generate a proactive alert to the credit controller with a risk assessment summary, recommended actions (e.g., reduce credit limit, request additional security, increase monitoring frequency), and the specific signals that triggered the alert. Alerts must be generated in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R006	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Model Training and Retraining Pipeline	The system must implement an automated machine learning pipeline for training and retraining payment prediction and credit scoring models. Initial model training must use a minimum of 24 months of historical transaction and payment data across all entities. Retraining must occur automatically on a configurable schedule (default: monthly) incorporating the latest payment data, write-off outcomes, and updated macroeconomic indicators. The pipeline must support A/B testing of model versions, allowing new models to run in shadow mode alongside production models for a configurable validation period before promotion. Model performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, mean absolute error for date predictions) must be tracked and reported, with automatic rollback to the previous model version if performance degrades below configurable thresholds. All model versions must be archived with full reproducibility metadata for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R007	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Explainable AI for Credit Decisions	All AI-generated predictions and scores must include explainability features showing the top contributing factors that influenced the prediction or score. For payment date predictions, the system must display factors such as 'customer average payment delay: 12 days', 'Ramadan seasonal adjustment: +5 days', 'high-value invoice adjustment: +3 days' in a ranked list. For credit risk scores, the system must provide SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) or equivalent feature importance values showing how each factor contributed positively or negatively to the score. Explanations must be presented in business-friendly language (not technical ML terminology) in both Arabic and English. This explainability is critical for regulatory compliance in KSA (SAMA AI governance guidelines) and for enabling credit controllers to validate and override AI recommendations with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R008	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Customer Segment Clustering for Peer Analysis	The system must use unsupervised learning techniques to automatically cluster customers into behavioral segments based on payment patterns, shipping characteristics, and financial profiles. Clusters must be generated separately for each entity and at the group level, enabling peer comparison analysis. The system must identify customers whose behavior deviates significantly from their cluster peers (e.g., a historically good payer in the 'reliable liner agent' cluster suddenly exhibiting delayed payment behavior). Peer analysis must be available on the customer credit profile screen showing the customer's position relative to cluster averages for key metrics: average days to pay, dispute rate, credit utilization, and payment predictability. Cluster definitions must be updated quarterly and must be reviewable by credit managers who can label clusters with business-meaningful names in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R009	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	External Data Source Integration for Scoring	The system must integrate with external data sources to enrich credit scoring models. Required integrations include commercial credit bureaus (Al Etihad Credit Bureau for UAE, SIMAH for KSA, CIBIL/TransUnion for India), company registry databases for financial filing data, vessel tracking data (AIS) for verifying shipment activity claims, commodity price indices relevant to shipped cargo types, and country risk rating providers (e.g., Euler Hermes, Coface). The system must handle scenarios where external data is unavailable or stale, applying appropriate confidence adjustments to scores that rely on incomplete external data. Data refresh frequencies must be configurable per source (daily for market data, weekly for credit bureau, monthly for financial filings). All external data usage must comply with data protection regulations including Qatar Law No. 13 of 2016, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act 2023.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R010	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Real-Time Score Recalculation on Payment Events	The system must trigger real-time recalculation of customer credit risk scores and invoice payment predictions when significant events occur, rather than waiting for the daily batch update. Trigger events must include payment receipt (full or partial), new invoice generation, credit note issuance, dispute registration or resolution, collection escalation tier change, credit limit adjustment, new shipment booking, and external credit event notifications. Real-time recalculation must complete within 30 seconds of the triggering event and must update all downstream displays and workflows that rely on the score or prediction, including the collection priority queue, credit limit utilization warnings, and the cash flow forecasting dashboard. The system must maintain a recalculation event log showing the trigger, previous score, new score, and the delta for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R011	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Credit Limit Recommendation Engine	The system must provide AI-driven credit limit recommendations for new and existing customers based on their predicted payment behavior, financial capacity, requested shipping volumes, and the entity's risk appetite. For new customers without historical payment data, the recommendation must rely on industry benchmarks, peer group analysis, external credit data, and the proposed trade lane characteristics (e.g., higher risk for certain commodity types or destination countries). For existing customers, recommendations must factor in the current credit score, utilization patterns, payment prediction accuracy, and any recent changes in shipping volumes or patterns. Recommendations must include a suggested credit limit amount in the relevant currency, a confidence level, and a detailed rationale. The engine must also recommend periodic credit limit reviews when customer behavior changes significantly, supporting both credit limit increases and decreases with appropriate justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R012	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Portfolio Risk Heatmap Visualization	The system must provide an interactive portfolio risk heatmap displaying the distribution of receivables across risk score bands and aging buckets in a matrix format. The heatmap must use color coding (green for low risk/current, yellow for moderate risk/slightly overdue, orange for elevated risk, red for high risk/severely overdue) with cell sizes proportional to the exposure amount. The heatmap must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), customer segment, trade lane, and port of origin (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNPT). Clicking on any cell must drill down to show the individual customers and invoices in that risk/aging intersection. The heatmap must support Arabic RTL layout with properly positioned axes and legends. A time slider must allow viewing historical snapshots of the portfolio risk distribution over the past 12 months to identify migration trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R013	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Prediction Accuracy Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a model performance monitoring dashboard showing prediction accuracy metrics updated in real-time as actual payments are received and compared against predictions. Key metrics must include mean absolute error for payment date predictions (by entity, customer segment, and charge type), precision and recall for on-time payment predictions, credit score calibration curves showing predicted vs. actual default rates per score band, and model drift indicators detecting when prediction accuracy degrades over time. The dashboard must display a comparison of the current production model against the most recent shadow model to support promotion decisions. Alerts must be generated when any accuracy metric falls below a configurable minimum threshold, triggering a model retraining cycle. The dashboard must support bilingual display in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R014	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Manual Score Override with Justification	The system must allow authorized users (Credit Manager and above) to manually override AI-generated credit risk scores and payment predictions when they possess information not captured by the model. Overrides must require a mandatory justification text field (minimum 50 characters, supporting Arabic and English input) and must be classified by reason (e.g., known financial restructuring, insider industry knowledge, pending large contract, government-backed entity status, relationship management decision). Overridden scores must be visually distinguished from AI-generated scores throughout the system. The override must have a configurable expiry period (default 90 days) after which the score reverts to the AI-calculated value unless the override is renewed. All overrides must be logged in the audit trail and must be reported to the Credit Committee monthly, showing override frequency by user, reason distribution, and outcome comparison (was the override justified based on subsequent payment behavior).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R015	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Batch Scoring API for External System Integration	The system must expose a secure REST API endpoint for batch credit scoring and payment prediction requests from external systems such as the booking portal, customer self-service platform, and partner shipping line systems. The API must accept customer identifiers and invoice references and return credit risk scores, payment predictions, and risk band classifications in JSON format with bilingual labels (Arabic and English). The API must support authentication via OAuth 2.0 and API key mechanisms, rate limiting per consumer (configurable, default 1000 requests per minute), and must respond within 2 seconds for batch sizes up to 100 records. The API must include versioning to support backward compatibility during model upgrades. API usage must be logged for billing, auditing, and performance monitoring purposes. Error responses must include meaningful error codes and descriptions in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R016	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	Bias Detection and Fairness Monitoring	The system must implement fairness monitoring to detect and prevent discriminatory bias in credit scoring and payment prediction models. The system must regularly analyze model outputs across protected dimensions including customer nationality, company size (SME vs. large enterprise), geographic location, and industry sub-sector to ensure that no group is systematically disadvantaged by model predictions. Statistical fairness metrics (demographic parity, equalized odds, predictive parity) must be calculated and reported quarterly. If significant bias is detected (disparity exceeding configurable thresholds), the system must flag the finding for review by the model governance committee and suggest mitigation strategies such as feature removal, reweighting, or recalibration. This requirement supports compliance with emerging AI governance frameworks in the UAE (National AI Strategy), KSA (SDAIA AI ethics guidelines), and India (proposed AI regulation). Bias monitoring reports must be retained for a minimum of 5 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-003-R017	FEAT-016-2-003	MOD-016	What-If Scenario Simulation for Credit Decisions	The system must provide a what-if simulation tool allowing credit managers to model the impact of hypothetical scenarios on customer credit scores and payment predictions. Scenarios must include changes in credit limit (increase or decrease), modification of payment terms (e.g., shifting from 30 to 60 days), addition or removal of collateral (bank guarantee, cash deposit), changes in shipping volume, currency devaluation events (critical for INR and regional currency fluctuations), and macroeconomic shocks (oil price drops affecting GCC trade volumes, monsoon disruptions at JNPT). The simulation must show the projected impact on the customer's credit score, payment prediction dates, expected credit loss provision, and portfolio-level risk metrics. Simulation results must be saveable and attachable to credit committee presentation documents. The tool must support running simulations across multiple customers simultaneously to assess portfolio-level impact of systemic scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R001	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	AI-Enhanced Cash Inflow Projection	The system must generate daily cash inflow projections for a configurable forecast horizon (default: 90 days, maximum: 365 days) by combining AI payment predictions from FEAT-016-2-003 with deterministic scheduled receipts (post-dated cheques, confirmed bank transfers, LC maturity dates). Each projected inflow must be categorized as 'confirmed' (backed by a confirmed payment instrument), 'high confidence' (AI prediction confidence above 80%), 'medium confidence' (50-80%), or 'low confidence' (below 50%). Projections must be generated per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), per currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), and per customer segment. The system must recalculate projections daily incorporating the latest payment receipts, new invoice generations, and updated AI predictions, with the ability to trigger ad-hoc recalculation on demand.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R002	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Multi-Currency Consolidated Forecast View	The system must present cash flow forecasts in a consolidated multi-currency view showing expected inflows in each operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with automatic conversion to the group reporting currency (USD) using configurable exchange rate assumptions (spot rate, budget rate, or forward rate). The dashboard must display a currency breakdown chart showing the proportion of expected inflows by currency, enabling treasury teams to plan foreign exchange conversions and hedging strategies. Users must be able to switch between individual entity views and consolidated group views with a single click. Exchange rate sensitivity analysis must show how forecast totals change under configurable currency movement scenarios (e.g., INR depreciating 5%, oil price affecting QAR/AED peg stability). All currency amounts must be formatted according to locale-specific conventions with proper Arabic numeral display when in Arabic language mode.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R003	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Rolling Forecast Accuracy Tracking	The system must track forecast accuracy by comparing projected cash inflows against actual receipts on a daily, weekly, and monthly basis. Accuracy metrics must include Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), forecast bias (systematic over or under-prediction), and accuracy by confidence category (confirmed, high, medium, low). The system must maintain a rolling 12-month accuracy history with trend visualization showing whether forecast reliability is improving or deteriorating. Accuracy must be segmented by entity, currency, customer segment, and forecast horizon (1-week accuracy vs. 1-month accuracy vs. 3-month accuracy). When forecast accuracy falls below configurable thresholds (default: MAPE exceeding 15% for 1-week forecasts, 25% for 1-month forecasts), the system must generate alerts to the treasury team and trigger a review of the underlying AI prediction model parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R004	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Interactive Time-Series Visualization	The dashboard must display cash flow forecasts using interactive time-series charts with daily granularity for the first 30 days and weekly granularity for days 31-90, with optional monthly granularity for longer horizons. Charts must show stacked area bands representing confidence levels (confirmed, high, medium, low confidence) with distinct color coding. Users must be able to zoom into specific date ranges, hover over data points to see detailed breakdowns (top customers contributing to that day's expected inflows, invoice counts, currency composition), and toggle between cumulative and daily views. The chart must overlay actual receipts for past periods against the forecast that was active for those dates, enabling visual assessment of forecast accuracy. The visualization must support Arabic RTL layout with right-to-left date axis progression and Arabic numeral display options, and must render correctly on desktop browsers and tablet devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R005	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Customer-Level Cash Flow Drill-Down	The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate forecast views to individual customer-level cash flow projections. The customer drill-down must show all outstanding invoices for the selected customer with their individual predicted payment dates, confidence levels, and amounts in both the invoice currency and entity functional currency. The view must include the customer's credit risk score from FEAT-016-2-003, current collection status from FEAT-016-2-001, any recorded payment promises, and the historical payment pattern graph showing actual vs. terms payment behavior over the past 24 months. Users must be able to manually adjust predicted payment dates at the customer level for specific invoices where they have additional information (e.g., customer has confirmed payment date verbally), with adjustments flowing through to the aggregate forecast. Manual adjustments must be logged with the user's identity and justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R006	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Scenario-Based Forecast Modeling	The system must support creation and comparison of multiple forecast scenarios beyond the base AI-driven forecast. Users must be able to define scenarios such as 'optimistic' (all customers pay within terms), 'pessimistic' (payment delays increase by configurable percentage), 'stress test' (top 5 customers default on all outstanding balances), and custom scenarios with user-defined assumptions. Each scenario must generate a complete cash flow projection that can be overlaid on the base forecast for visual comparison. Scenario parameters must include payment delay adjustments per customer segment, default probability overrides, currency movement assumptions, and seasonal adjustment modifications (e.g., extended Ramadan slowdown scenario for GCC entities). Scenarios must be saveable with descriptive names in Arabic or English, shareable with other users, and includable in treasury reports. The system must calculate the variance between each scenario and the base forecast.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R007	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Cash Flow Gap Analysis and Alerts	The system must integrate projected AR cash inflows with known cash outflow commitments (payable due dates, payroll schedules, loan repayments, vessel charter payments, port dues) to identify projected cash flow gaps or surpluses for each entity and currency. When a cash flow gap (negative net cash position) is projected within the forecast horizon, the system must generate a proactive alert to the treasury team specifying the entity, currency, date, projected shortfall amount, and the key drivers of the gap (e.g., large receivable from Customer X predicted to pay late, upcoming vessel charter payment of USD 2M due at Jebel Ali). Alerts must include AI-recommended remedial actions such as accelerating collection efforts on specific high-value invoices, requesting early payment discounts, or arranging intercompany fund transfers. The gap analysis must be updated in real-time as payment receipts and new commitments are recorded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R008	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Aged Receivables Waterfall Chart	The dashboard must include an interactive waterfall chart showing how the current accounts receivable balance is expected to convert to cash over the forecast period. The waterfall must start with the total outstanding AR balance, then show projected collections by week or month, new invoices expected to be generated (based on confirmed bookings and historical billing patterns), provisions for bad debts, credit notes and adjustments, resulting in a projected closing AR balance at the end of each period. Each waterfall segment must be drillable to show the contributing invoices and customers. The chart must be filterable by entity, currency, customer segment, and port of origin (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNPT). The waterfall visualization must support Arabic RTL rendering with correctly positioned labels and directional flow indicators appropriate for right-to-left reading.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R009	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Treasury Integration Export Formats	The system must export cash flow forecast data in formats compatible with common treasury management systems and banking platforms used in the GCC and India regions. Supported export formats must include CSV with configurable delimiters and encoding (UTF-8 with BOM for Arabic text compatibility), Excel (XLSX) with formatted sheets per entity and currency, SWIFT MT940/MT942 compatible formats for bank reconciliation integration, and JSON/XML for API-based integration with treasury management systems. The export must include forecast metadata (generation date, model version, confidence distribution, assumptions used). Scheduled exports must be configurable to run daily at specified times aligned with each entity's business hours and time zone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India). Export files must be delivered via secure channels (SFTP, encrypted email) with configurable recipient lists per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R010	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Hijri Calendar and Regional Holiday Adjustment	The cash flow forecast must account for non-business days when predicting payment receipt dates, adjusting predictions to the next business day when a predicted payment falls on a weekend or public holiday. The system must maintain holiday calendars for all operating jurisdictions: Qatar (Friday-Saturday weekend, Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Day, Sports Day), UAE (Saturday-Sunday weekend since 2022, same Islamic holidays plus Commemoration Day, National Day), KSA (Friday-Saturday weekend, Islamic holidays, National Day, Founding Day), and India (Sunday weekend, Diwali, Holi, Republic Day, Independence Day, plus state-specific holidays for Maharashtra where JNPT operates). Islamic holidays must be managed using Hijri calendar integration with support for both calculated and officially announced dates. The system must handle the variability of Islamic holiday dates (shifting approximately 10-11 days earlier each Gregorian year) and must allow manual adjustment of holiday dates when official announcements differ from calculated dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R011	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Real-Time Dashboard Refresh and Performance	The cash flow forecasting dashboard must load within 3 seconds for the default view (current entity, current month, all currencies) and must support real-time data refresh without full page reload. Dashboard widgets must update independently as new data becomes available (e.g., payment receipt recorded, new invoice generated, AI prediction updated). The system must support at least 50 concurrent dashboard users across all entities without performance degradation. For complex views (group consolidation across all entities, full 365-day forecast horizon, all customer segments), the maximum acceptable load time must be 8 seconds. The dashboard must implement intelligent data caching with configurable cache invalidation policies (default: 5-minute cache for aggregate views, real-time for transaction-level data). Performance metrics (page load times, query response times, cache hit ratios) must be monitored and reported to the system operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R012	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Mobile-Responsive Dashboard Layout	The cash flow forecasting dashboard must provide a fully responsive layout optimized for mobile devices and tablets, enabling CFOs and treasury managers to review cash positions while traveling between offices in Doha, Dubai, Riyadh, and Mumbai. The mobile layout must prioritize key summary metrics (total projected inflows by entity, cash flow gap alerts, confidence distribution) with progressive disclosure for detailed drill-downs. Touch-optimized interactions must replace hover-based desktop interactions (e.g., tap to expand instead of hover for tooltips). Charts must be simplified for smaller screens while retaining the ability to pinch-zoom for detail. The mobile view must support both portrait and landscape orientations and must handle Arabic RTL layout correctly on mobile browsers (Safari, Chrome, Samsung Internet). Offline access must cache the most recent forecast data for viewing when connectivity is unavailable during transit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R013	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Configurable Dashboard Widgets and Personalization	The system must allow each user to customize their dashboard layout by selecting, arranging, and resizing widgets from a library of available forecast visualization components. Available widgets must include forecast summary cards, time-series charts, waterfall charts, currency breakdown pie charts, customer concentration tables, aging distribution bars, forecast accuracy gauges, and alert notification panels. Users must be able to save multiple dashboard configurations as named profiles (e.g., 'Daily Treasury Review', 'Monthly Board Report', 'Qatar Entity Focus') and switch between them. Widget configurations must persist across sessions and devices for each user. Default dashboard layouts must be definable per role (CFO view, Treasury Manager view, Credit Controller view, Entity Finance Manager view). Widget titles and labels must support both Arabic and English with the user's language preference applied consistently across all widgets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R014	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Forecast Variance Commentary and Collaboration	The system must allow users to add commentary and annotations to specific forecast data points, time periods, or variance items. When the actual cash inflow for a period differs significantly from the forecast (variance exceeding a configurable threshold, default 10%), the system must prompt the responsible entity finance manager to provide a variance explanation. Comments must support Arabic and English text input with @mention capability to tag other users for follow-up. A threaded discussion capability must allow collaborative analysis of forecast variances, with the full discussion thread visible alongside the relevant chart or data point. Comments must be categorizable by type (customer-specific, market-driven, one-time event, model limitation) and must be searchable and reportable. Monthly forecast variance reports must automatically compile all variance commentary for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R015	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Automated Cash Flow Report Generation	The system must automatically generate scheduled cash flow forecast reports for distribution to configurable stakeholder groups. Report types must include daily cash position summary (sent each morning before business hours in each entity's time zone), weekly forecast update with accuracy tracking and variance analysis, monthly cash flow forecast report with scenario comparisons for CFO and Board review, and quarterly liquidity adequacy assessment. Reports must be generated in PDF format with bilingual Arabic/English content, properly formatted charts and tables, and entity-specific branding. The report generation engine must support Hijri date headers for KSA reports alongside Gregorian dates. Reports must be distributed via email with optional secure link access for board members. The system must maintain a report archive accessible for at least 7 years to support regulatory and audit requirements across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R016	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Intercompany Cash Flow Netting View	The dashboard must provide a dedicated view for intercompany cash flows between group entities, showing receivables and payables between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities with netting opportunities. The system must identify offsetting intercompany positions (e.g., Qatar entity owes AED 500,000 to UAE entity while UAE entity owes QAR 450,000 to Qatar entity) and recommend netting settlements to minimize actual cross-border fund transfers. Net settlement amounts must be calculated using agreed intercompany exchange rates with the resulting FX exposure clearly displayed. The netting view must show a visual flow diagram of intercompany positions with directional arrows indicating net payment flows between entities. Netting proposals must be exportable as settlement instructions and must integrate with the intercompany reconciliation process in FEAT-016-2-002-R016. The view must account for regulatory restrictions on cross-border payments applicable in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R017	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Cash Flow Forecast Audit Trail and Versioning	The system must maintain a complete version history of all cash flow forecasts generated, including the date and time of generation, the user or automated process that triggered the forecast, the AI model version used, the input parameters and assumptions, and the resulting projection values. Users must be able to retrieve and compare any two historical forecast versions side-by-side to analyze how the forecast evolved over time and identify the drivers of changes (new invoices, payment receipts, model updates, manual overrides, exchange rate changes). The audit trail must be immutable and must support regulatory and internal audit requirements across all four jurisdictions. Forecast version data must be retained for a minimum of 5 years. The comparison view must highlight material differences between versions with drill-down capability to the individual invoice or customer level that caused the forecast change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-016-2-004-R018	FEAT-016-2-004	MOD-016	Integration with Bank Statement Reconciliation	The cash flow dashboard must integrate with the bank statement reconciliation module to automatically update forecast accuracy as actual bank credits are matched to predicted inflows. When a bank statement is imported and credits are matched to customer payments, the system must immediately update the forecast to reflect the actual receipt, remove the corresponding predicted inflow, and recalculate the remaining forecast period. Unmatched bank credits must be flagged for investigation and excluded from forecast accuracy calculations until matched. The integration must support multiple bank accounts per entity across different banks commonly used in the operating jurisdictions (QNB, Commercial Bank for Qatar; Emirates NBD, FAB for UAE; Al Rajhi, SNB for KSA; SBI, HDFC for India). Reconciliation status must be visible on the dashboard showing the percentage of predicted inflows confirmed by actual bank receipts for the current period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R001	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Multi-Entity Vendor Registration Portal	The system must provide a self-service vendor registration portal accessible in both Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR) with automatic layout switching based on user language preference. Vendors must be able to register against one or more entities (Qatar entity for Hamad Port operations, UAE entity for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali operations, KSA entity, and India entity for JNPT operations). The registration form must capture trade license numbers, CR numbers (for GCC), GSTIN (for India), VAT/TIN certificates, and bank details per entity. Each registration must generate a unique vendor code following the pattern VND-{entity}-{category}-{sequence} and must support document uploads in PDF, JPG, and PNG formats up to 25MB per file.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R002	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Shipping-Specific Vendor Categorization	The system must enforce a hierarchical vendor categorization taxonomy specific to container shipping operations. Primary categories must include but not be limited to: Terminal Operators (port-specific: QICT for Hamad, KCTPL for Khalifa, DPW for Jebel Ali, JNPCT/BMCTPL for JNPT), Shipping Lines, Freight Forwarders (IATA/FIATA licensed), Custom House Agents, Bunker Fuel Suppliers, Container Lessors (dry, reefer, tank, flat-rack, open-top), Marine Surveyors, Stevedoring Companies, Inland Haulage Providers, Container Freight Stations, and Ship Chandlers. Each category must have configurable mandatory document checklists and compliance requirements that vary by operating jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R003	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	AI-Powered KYC/AML Vendor Screening	The system must integrate an AI-powered screening engine that automatically validates vendor identities against international sanctions lists (OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated, UN Security Council), PEP databases, and adverse media feeds upon registration and on a configurable periodic basis (default: quarterly). For GCC-based vendors, the system must additionally screen against local regulatory blacklists from Qatar Financial Centre, UAE Central Bank, and SAMA. The AI engine must assign a risk score from 0 to 100, flag vendors scoring above a configurable threshold (default: 70) for manual compliance review, and maintain a full audit trail of all screening decisions including false-positive resolutions with timestamps and reviewer identities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R004	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Multi-Currency Bank Account Validation	The system must capture and validate multiple bank accounts per vendor, supporting USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR as primary currencies with the ability to configure additional currencies. For each bank account, the system must store and validate the IBAN (mandatory for GCC accounts following ISO 13616), SWIFT/BIC code (validated against the SWIFT directory), account holder name matching the registered vendor name with fuzzy tolerance for transliteration differences between Arabic and English, and correspondent bank details for USD wire transfers. The system must support Indian IFSC code validation for JNPT-related vendors and NEFT/RTGS routing. A micro-deposit verification workflow must be available for new bank accounts before they are approved for payment disbursement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R005	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Document Expiry Tracking and Renewal Alerts	The system must track expiry dates for all vendor compliance documents including trade licenses, CR certificates, insurance policies (P&I Club certificates, cargo insurance), IATA/FIATA licenses, port passes, customs broker licenses, and VAT registration certificates. AI-driven alerts must be generated at configurable intervals before expiry (default: 90, 60, 30, and 7 days). The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date tracking for document expiry, particularly for KSA-issued documents that use Hijri dates. Upon document expiry without renewal, the system must automatically change the vendor status to 'Suspended' and block new purchase order creation while allowing existing committed POs to be processed through to payment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R006	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Credit Rating and Payment Terms Assignment	The system must maintain an AI-computed vendor credit rating based on historical payment performance, delivery reliability, dispute frequency, and external credit bureau data where available. Payment terms must be assignable per vendor per entity, supporting standard shipping industry terms including Net 30, Net 45, Net 60, Net 90, Cash Before Delivery (CBD), Cash on Delivery (COD), Letter of Credit (LC), and milestone-based payments for long-term charter party agreements. The system must enforce entity-specific approval hierarchies for payment terms exceeding configurable thresholds: for example, terms beyond Net 60 for the Qatar entity must require CFO approval. Default payment terms must auto-populate on new POs based on the vendor's credit rating and category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R007	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Dual-Language Vendor Communication Templates	The system must provide a library of pre-configured bilingual communication templates in Arabic and English for all vendor lifecycle events including onboarding confirmation, document request, rejection notification, suspension notice, reactivation confirmation, and payment remittance advice. Templates must support dynamic field insertion for vendor name, vendor code, entity name, amounts with correct currency formatting (Arabic: comma as decimal separator, period as thousands; English: standard), and dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. The RTL Arabic templates must correctly handle mixed-direction text where English trade terms, vessel names, or port codes (QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP) are embedded within Arabic paragraphs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R008	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Performance Scorecard with AI Analytics	The system must generate automated vendor performance scorecards using AI analytics across five weighted dimensions: delivery timeliness (weight: 25%), service quality including container condition and documentation accuracy (weight: 25%), pricing competitiveness benchmarked against market rates (weight: 20%), compliance adherence including timely document renewals (weight: 15%), and dispute resolution responsiveness (weight: 15%). Scorecards must be computed monthly per entity and consolidated quarterly across all entities. Vendors scoring below a configurable threshold (default: 40 out of 100) for two consecutive quarters must be automatically flagged for review and potential deactivation. The AI must provide natural language recommendations for vendor relationship optimization in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R009	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Multi-Entity Vendor Deduplication and Linking	The system must employ AI-based fuzzy matching to detect and manage duplicate vendor records across entities using trade license numbers, tax registration IDs, IBAN/bank account details, phone numbers, and name similarity with Arabic transliteration awareness (e.g., matching 'محمد' with 'Mohammed', 'Mohammad', 'Mohamed'). When a potential duplicate is detected with a confidence score above 85%, the system must prompt the user to either merge records (designating one as primary), link records as related entities (parent-subsidiary), or confirm as distinct vendors with documented justification. The deduplication engine must handle vendors operating across multiple jurisdictions who legitimately have separate registrations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India while maintaining a unified relationship view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R010	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Tax Configuration per Jurisdiction	The system must store and enforce jurisdiction-specific tax configurations per vendor. For UAE vendors, this includes 5% VAT registration with TRN validation against FTA records. For KSA vendors, 15% VAT with ZATCA-compliant TIN validation. For Qatar vendors, no VAT but withholding tax rules for specific service categories. For Indian vendors, GST configuration including CGST, SGST, IGST rates by HSN/SAC code with GSTIN validation against the government portal. The system must support reverse charge mechanism (RCM) applicability flags per vendor per service category, particularly for freight and transportation services under GCC VAT frameworks and Indian GST Section 9(3). Tax configuration must auto-populate on invoices during the matching process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R011	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Insurance and Liability Verification	The system must track and validate mandatory insurance coverages for shipping-specific vendor categories. Terminal operators and stevedores must have minimum Public Liability and Workmen's Compensation coverages. Freight forwarders must maintain Cargo Legal Liability (CLL) or Freight Forwarder's Liability insurance with minimum coverage amounts configurable per entity. Bunker suppliers must carry Environmental Liability insurance. Marine surveyors must hold Professional Indemnity coverage. The AI engine must parse uploaded insurance certificates to extract coverage amounts, policy periods, and covered perils, flagging any gaps against the configured minimum requirements. Insurance adequacy must be re-evaluated automatically when a vendor is assigned to higher-value contracts or operations involving hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R012	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Approval Workflow with Delegation	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for vendor onboarding that varies by vendor category, risk score, and operating entity. The default workflow must include: L1 — Procurement Officer review of completeness, L2 — Compliance Officer KYC/AML clearance, L3 — Finance Manager bank account and payment terms validation, L4 — Department Head final approval for vendors with annual spend projection exceeding entity-specific thresholds (default: QAR 500,000 / AED 500,000 / SAR 500,000 / INR 5,000,000). The system must support approval delegation during absence with date-range-based proxy assignments, escalation rules for approvals pending beyond configurable SLA (default: 48 hours per level), and mobile push notifications for pending approvals accessible offline with sync-on-reconnect capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R013	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Portal with Self-Service Capabilities	The system must provide an external-facing vendor portal accessible via web browser with responsive design for mobile devices, supporting Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts. Vendors must be able to view their profile information, update bank details (subject to re-verification), upload renewed documents, view purchase order status and history, submit invoices electronically, track invoice processing and payment status, download payment remittance advices, and raise disputes or queries through a ticketing system. The portal must enforce two-factor authentication (2FA) via SMS or authenticator app, support SSO via SAML 2.0 for enterprise vendors, and provide a dashboard showing outstanding invoices, upcoming payments, and overdue items with amounts displayed in the vendor's preferred currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R014	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Master Data Change Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for every change made to vendor master data including creation, modification, approval, suspension, reactivation, and deletion events. Each audit record must capture the timestamp in UTC with entity-local time display (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), the user ID and role performing the action, the IP address and device fingerprint, the field changed with before and after values, and the business justification. The audit trail must be searchable and filterable by vendor code, entity, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri), change type, and user. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with commercial record-keeping requirements across all four jurisdictions and must be exportable in PDF and CSV formats for regulatory audit submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R015	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Offline Vendor Data Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to vendor master data for procurement and operations staff working at port facilities (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) where network connectivity may be intermittent. The offline capability must cache the most recently accessed 500 vendor records with key fields including vendor name, code, category, contact details, payment terms, and compliance status. Users must be able to initiate vendor searches, view cached profiles, and draft new vendor registration requests while offline. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline-created records with conflict detection — if the same vendor record was modified by another user during the offline period, the system must present a side-by-side comparison and require manual resolution. The offline cache must be encrypted at rest using AES-256.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-001-R016	FEAT-017-1-001	MOD-017	Vendor Blacklisting and Restricted Party Management	The system must maintain an internal blacklist of vendors who have been debarred due to contractual violations, fraud, safety incidents, or regulatory sanctions. Blacklisted vendors must be completely blocked from new PO creation, invoice submission, and payment processing across all entities with no override possible except by the Chief Compliance Officer role. The system must support temporary restriction periods with automatic review dates and permanent blacklisting. When a vendor is blacklisted, all pending POs must be flagged for review and reassignment. The system must also cross-reference vendor beneficial ownership data against restricted party lists, detecting indirect relationships where a blacklisted entity may be a shareholder or director of a new vendor attempting registration. Blacklist events must generate compliance reports in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R001	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Shipping-Specific PO Type Configuration	The system must support configurable purchase order types specific to container shipping operations. Standard PO types must include: Terminal Handling Orders (THC-based, linked to specific ports — QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), Bunker Fuel Procurement (spot and contract, with quantity in metric tons), Container Lease Agreements (per diem rates by container type — 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK), Vessel Agency Appointments, Inland Haulage Contracts (per TEU/FEU rates by route), Survey and Inspection Orders, Ship Chandlery, Stevedoring, and General Services. Each PO type must have a configurable document template, mandatory field set, approval routing, and accounting code mapping. The AI must recommend the correct PO type based on the selected vendor category and line item descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R002	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Multi-Currency PO Creation with Exchange Rate Control	The system must allow purchase orders to be created in any of the supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to the entity's base currency for budgetary control. The exchange rate must be locked at the time of PO approval using either the system's daily rate feed (sourced from the Central Bank of the respective entity's jurisdiction) or a manually entered contracted rate for long-term agreements. The PO must display amounts in both the transaction currency and the base currency. For USD-denominated POs created by the India entity (common for shipping line charges at JNPT), the system must apply the RBI reference rate. The system must prevent PO creation if the exchange rate variance between the system rate and any manually entered rate exceeds a configurable threshold (default: 2%).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R003	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	AI-Assisted PO Line Item Generation	The system must provide AI-assisted line item generation where the user can describe the required service or goods in natural language (Arabic or English) and the AI engine auto-generates structured PO line items with appropriate descriptions, unit of measure (per TEU, per container, per MT, per vessel call, lump sum), estimated quantities based on historical consumption patterns, and suggested unit prices based on the last 12 months of pricing history with the selected vendor and market benchmarking data. The AI must flag any line items where the suggested price deviates more than 15% from the historical average and provide a confidence score for each auto-generated line. Users must be able to accept, modify, or reject each AI suggestion while maintaining a record of AI-generated versus manually entered items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R004	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Budget Availability Check and Commitment Accounting	The system must perform a real-time budget availability check against the relevant cost center and GL account at the time of PO creation. The check must consider the entity's approved annual budget, year-to-date actual expenditure, and existing PO commitments (encumbrances). If the PO amount would cause the budget to be exceeded, the system must block submission and display the available budget balance. A budget override workflow must be available for authorized users (minimum: Finance Manager level) with mandatory justification text. Upon PO approval, the system must create an encumbrance entry in the commitment ledger, reserving the budgeted amount. The encumbrance must be released proportionally as invoices are matched and processed. Budget checks must respect the entity's fiscal year configuration (January-December for Qatar/UAE, April-March for India, varying for KSA based on Hijri calendar).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R005	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Approval Workflow with Amount-Based Routing	The system must enforce a multi-tier approval workflow for purchase orders with routing rules based on PO total amount, PO type, vendor risk category, and operating entity. Default approval tiers must be configurable per entity, with a minimum structure of: Tier 1 (up to QAR/AED/SAR 50,000 or INR 500,000) — Department Manager, Tier 2 (up to QAR/AED/SAR 250,000 or INR 2,500,000) — Department Head plus Finance Manager, Tier 3 (up to QAR/AED/SAR 1,000,000 or INR 10,000,000) — VP Operations plus CFO, Tier 4 (above Tier 3 threshold) — CEO approval required. The system must support parallel and sequential approval steps, automatic escalation after configurable SLA breach, and complete approval history with timestamps and comments. Emergency POs for vessel-related urgencies must have an expedited single-approver pathway with mandatory post-facto review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R006	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Amendment and Version Control	The system must support PO amendments after initial approval, maintaining a complete version history with change tracking. Amendments that increase the PO value by more than a configurable threshold (default: 10% or QAR/AED/SAR 10,000 / INR 100,000, whichever is lower) must trigger a re-approval workflow following the same tier structure as the original PO based on the new total value. Each amendment must record the amendment reason (quantity change, price adjustment, scope addition, term extension, or correction), the fields changed with before and after values, the amendment requestor and approver, and the amendment date. The system must support a maximum of 10 amendments per PO before requiring closure and re-issuance. The vendor portal must display the current approved version and allow vendors to acknowledge amendments electronically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R007	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Blanket and Framework PO Management	The system must support blanket purchase orders (BPOs) and framework agreements for recurring shipping services such as container terminal handling at specific ports, regular bunker fuel supply contracts, ongoing container lease arrangements, and standing inland haulage agreements. BPOs must define maximum quantities and/or values for a specified period (monthly, quarterly, or annually) with the ability to generate release orders against the blanket PO. The system must track cumulative consumption against the BPO limit and alert when utilization reaches configurable thresholds (default: 75%, 90%, 100%). Upon reaching 100%, the system must block further release orders until the BPO is amended or renewed. The AI must analyze consumption patterns and recommend optimal BPO quantities and renewal terms based on historical usage data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R008	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO-to-Voyage and Container Linking	The system must support linking purchase orders to specific voyages, vessel calls, bills of lading, and/or container numbers for cost allocation and traceability. Terminal handling POs must be linkable to vessel call IDs and specific berth assignments at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Container lease POs must be linkable to individual container numbers with tracking of on-hire and off-hire dates. Inland haulage POs must reference specific booking numbers, container numbers, and delivery locations. The linking must support one-to-many relationships (one PO serving multiple voyages) and many-to-one (multiple POs for a single voyage). This linkage must propagate through to invoice matching to enable accurate voyage-level profitability analysis and per-container cost tracking across the entire supply chain.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R009	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Automated PO Dispatch to Vendors	The system must automatically dispatch approved purchase orders to vendors through multiple channels: email with PDF attachment (bilingual Arabic/English), vendor portal notification, and optionally via EDI (EDIFACT ORDERS message) for integrated shipping line and terminal operator vendors. The PDF must include a legally compliant format with entity letterhead, authorized signatory details, terms and conditions (entity-specific and PO-type-specific), and a unique QR code linking to the vendor portal for electronic acknowledgment. The system must track vendor acknowledgment status with configurable reminders at 24-hour and 48-hour intervals for unacknowledged POs. Vendors must be able to acknowledge, request clarification, or formally decline a PO through the portal, with each response triggering appropriate workflow notifications to the procurement team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R010	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Goods/Service Receipt Processing	The system must support recording goods receipts (GRN) and service completion confirmations against purchase order line items. For physical goods (bunker fuel, spare parts, provisions), the system must capture received quantity, delivery date, condition assessment, and warehouse/location (bonded or free zone). For services (terminal handling, stevedoring, surveying), the system must capture service completion date, service report reference, and quality rating. Partial receipts must be supported with automatic tracking of outstanding quantities. The system must enforce tolerance limits for over-delivery (configurable, default: 5% for physical goods, 0% for services) and under-delivery. GRN data must automatically feed into the 3-way match process. Port-specific receipt workflows must be configurable, for example, bunker delivery notes at Jebel Ali must capture BDN number, density at 15°C, and sulphur content percentage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R011	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Closure and Cancellation Management	The system must support both automatic and manual PO closure and cancellation with appropriate controls. Automatic closure must occur when all line items are fully received and matched invoices are processed to payment. Manual closure must be available for POs where the remaining undelivered quantity or value is below a configurable threshold (default: 5% of PO value) and must require a closure reason from a predefined list. Cancellation of approved POs must follow a separate approval workflow requiring minimum Finance Manager authorization and must reverse any associated budget encumbrances. The system must prevent cancellation of POs that have associated approved invoices or completed payments. Partially fulfilled POs must support line-level closure while keeping other lines active. All closure and cancellation events must be reflected in commitment accounting in real time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R012	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	INCOTERMS and Delivery Terms on POs	The system must capture and enforce INCOTERMS 2020 delivery terms on each purchase order, with the applicable INCOTERM determining cost allocation, risk transfer point, and required documentation. Supported INCOTERMS must include EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, and DDP with the named place/port specified using UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHAM for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNP for JNPT). The selected INCOTERM must drive downstream processes: for example, a CIF purchase must not require a separate freight PO for the same shipment, while an FOB purchase must trigger a linked freight procurement requirement. The system must validate that the selected INCOTERM is commercially appropriate for the goods/service type and transportation mode (sea, road, multimodal) and warn if an incompatible combination is detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R013	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Tax Computation and Withholding on POs	The system must automatically compute applicable taxes on PO line items based on the vendor's tax configuration, the purchasing entity's jurisdiction, and the nature of the goods or services. For UAE entities, 5% VAT must be applied with correct treatment for zero-rated exports and exempt supplies per FTA guidelines. For KSA entities, 15% VAT with ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoicing compliance preparation. For India entities, GST must be computed at the correct rate (5%, 12%, 18%, or 28%) based on HSN/SAC classification with automatic determination of CGST+SGST for intra-state or IGST for inter-state transactions. TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) applicability must be evaluated for Indian POs under relevant sections (194C for contractors, 194J for professional services). The computed tax must be displayed separately per line item and in the PO total summary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R014	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Print and Export with Bilingual Support	The system must generate purchase order documents in configurable bilingual formats supporting side-by-side Arabic and English layout or single-language versions. The PO document must include entity header with logo and registration details, vendor details with bilingual name display, PO metadata (number, date in Gregorian and Hijri, revision number, payment terms), line item details with descriptions in both languages, tax breakdown, total amounts in transaction and base currencies with amounts in words (supporting Arabic numeral-to-words conversion), terms and conditions, and authorized signatory block. Export formats must include PDF (for print and email), Excel (for internal analysis), and XML (for EDI integration). The system must maintain a configurable template library allowing different PO layouts per entity and PO type while ensuring all templates comply with local commercial document regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R015	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Analytics and Spend Intelligence Dashboard	The system must provide an AI-powered analytics dashboard for purchase order data offering insights across all four entities. Key metrics must include: total PO value by entity, vendor, category, and period; PO cycle time from creation to approval (average, median, P90); vendor concentration risk analysis showing dependency on single vendors per service category; price trend analysis for key cost categories (THC rates per port, bunker fuel prices per MT, container per diem rates by type); budget versus actual commitment comparison per cost center; and PO amendment frequency analysis. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate views to individual PO details, date range filtering with both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, currency normalization to a single display currency, and export of all charts and data tables in PDF and Excel formats. The AI must proactively surface anomalies and cost-saving opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R016	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	PO Integration with Procurement Planning	The system must integrate with upstream procurement planning modules to enable automatic PO generation from approved procurement requisitions. When a voyage plan or vessel schedule is confirmed, the system must auto-generate draft POs for standard port call services (pilotage, towage, berth hire, terminal handling) at the designated ports using pre-negotiated rates from framework agreements. The AI must recommend optimal vendor selection based on the vendor performance scorecard, pricing competitiveness, and current capacity or availability. Auto-generated POs must be routed through the standard approval workflow but flagged as AI-generated for reviewer awareness. The system must track the conversion rate from requisitions to POs and identify bottlenecks in the procurement-to-PO pipeline across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-002-R017	FEAT-017-1-002	MOD-017	Recurring PO Scheduling and Automation	The system must support the creation of recurring purchase orders for periodic services such as monthly vessel agency retainers, quarterly equipment maintenance contracts, annual software license renewals, and regular bunker fuel deliveries at fixed intervals. The recurring PO schedule must define the recurrence pattern (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually), start and end dates, line item template with fixed or escalating prices (supporting annual escalation percentages), and auto-approval rules for POs within pre-defined value bounds. The system must automatically generate the next PO instance at a configurable lead time before the scheduled date (default: 5 business days). Each generated instance must inherit the parent schedule's configuration but allow per-instance modifications. The AI must monitor recurring PO patterns and alert when actual consumption deviates significantly from the scheduled quantities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R001	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	AI-Powered Multi-Format Invoice Ingestion	The system must support automated invoice ingestion from multiple channels including email (parsing attachments from registered vendor email addresses), vendor portal upload, EDI (EDIFACT INVOIC messages from shipping lines and terminals), API integration (for large-volume vendors like terminal operators), and manual scan/upload. Supported document formats must include PDF (including scanned images), TIFF, JPEG, PNG, XML (UBL 2.1 and ZATCA-compliant e-invoice format), and Excel-based invoice templates. The AI-powered OCR engine must achieve a minimum 95% field extraction accuracy for printed invoices and 90% for handwritten elements. The system must handle invoices in Arabic, English, and mixed Arabic-English layouts with correct RTL/LTR text direction detection. Duplicate invoice detection must occur at ingestion, blocking re-submission of invoices matching existing records by vendor code, invoice number, and invoice date combination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R002	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Intelligent Invoice Data Extraction and Classification	The AI engine must automatically extract and classify key invoice fields including vendor name and code (cross-referenced with the vendor master), invoice number, invoice date (handling both Gregorian and Hijri date formats), due date, currency, line item descriptions, quantities, unit prices, line totals, tax amounts (VAT/GST broken down by rate), total amount, PO reference number, vessel name, voyage number, bill of lading numbers, and container numbers. For shipping-specific invoice types, the AI must recognize and extract port-specific charges such as THC, wharfage, storage/demurrage, detention, documentation fees, seal charges, and surveyor fees. The extraction confidence score must be provided per field, and fields below a configurable confidence threshold (default: 85%) must be highlighted for manual verification. The system must learn from manual corrections to improve extraction accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R001	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Vessel-Based Proforma DA Creation	The system must allow users to create a new proforma disbursement account by selecting a vessel from the registered fleet or entering ad-hoc vessel particulars including IMO number, vessel name, GRT, NRT, LOA, beam, draft, and flag state. The proforma DA must be linked to a specific voyage number, port of call (selected from UNLOCODE-based port master including QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA), and estimated arrival/departure dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The system must auto-populate known vessel charges from the port tariff database and calculate preliminary totals in the port's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R003	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Coding and GL Account Assignment	The system must automatically assign general ledger account codes, cost centers, and profit centers to each invoice line item using AI-driven classification based on the PO reference (if available), vendor category, line item description, and historical coding patterns. The GL account structure must support the multi-entity chart of accounts with entity-specific account segments (entity code, department, cost center, GL account, project/voyage, intercompany). For invoices linked to specific voyages, the system must allocate costs to the correct voyage cost center enabling voyage-level profitability analysis. The AI must achieve a minimum 90% auto-coding accuracy after a 3-month learning period. Users must be able to override AI-suggested coding with the override recorded for audit purposes. The system must flag any coding that would result in cross-entity booking without a corresponding intercompany journal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R004	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Multi-Currency Invoice Processing and Conversion	The system must process invoices in any currency and perform conversion to the entity's base currency using the exchange rate applicable on the invoice date. The system must support the following exchange rate determination hierarchy: (1) contracted rate from the linked PO, (2) invoice-date spot rate from the central bank feed, (3) manually entered rate requiring Finance Manager approval. Foreign currency invoices must track the transaction amount, base currency equivalent at booking, and any exchange rate gain/loss realized at the time of payment. The system must handle the common shipping scenario where a vendor invoices in USD for services rendered at an Indian port (JNPT), and the India entity must book the expense in INR while the payment is made in USD, correctly computing and posting the forex impact. Unrealized foreign exchange gains/losses must be computed during month-end revaluation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R005	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Tax Validation and Compliance Check	The system must validate tax amounts on vendor invoices against the expected tax computation based on the vendor's tax registration, the purchasing entity's jurisdiction, and the nature of supply. For UAE invoices, the system must verify that VAT at 5% is correctly applied and that the vendor's TRN on the invoice matches the vendor master record. For KSA invoices, ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoice XML validation must be performed including QR code verification containing seller name, VAT number, timestamp, total, and VAT amount. For Indian invoices, GST validation must verify correct rate application, GSTIN format, place of supply determination for IGST versus CGST/SGST split, and Input Tax Credit (ITC) eligibility per Section 16 of CGST Act. The system must flag invoices with tax discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerance (default: INR 1 / QAR 0.01 / AED 0.01 / SAR 0.01) for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R006	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Approval Workflow with Exception Handling	The system must route invoices through a configurable approval workflow based on invoice amount, match status (auto-matched, partial match, no match), exception type, and operating entity. Fully auto-matched invoices within tolerance (see 3-way match requirements) below a configurable threshold (default: QAR/AED/SAR 10,000 or INR 100,000) must be auto-approved without manual intervention, achieving the 95% AI automation target. Invoices requiring manual approval must be routed to the appropriate approver based on the cost center owner and amount tier. The workflow must support hold/release capability for disputed invoices, split approval (different approvers for different line items when cost centers differ), and configurable escalation timers. The system must provide a consolidated approval inbox showing all pending invoices across entities for users with multi-entity roles, with priority sorting based on payment due date and early payment discount deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R007	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Credit Note and Debit Note Processing	The system must support the processing of vendor credit notes (reducing the amount owed) and debit notes (increasing the amount owed) with full linkage to the original invoice. Credit notes must be applicable against specific invoice line items or as general credits against the vendor's account. The system must validate that credit note amounts do not exceed the original invoice amount and that the tax treatment mirrors the original invoice. For UAE and KSA entities, credit notes must comply with VAT credit note requirements including reference to the original tax invoice number and date. For Indian entities, credit notes must comply with GST Section 34 provisions and must be issued within the statutory time limit. The system must support automatic offset of credit notes against outstanding invoices during payment processing and maintain a running vendor credit balance visible in the vendor portal and AP aging reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R008	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Demurrage and Detention Invoice Verification	The system must provide specialized processing for demurrage and detention invoices from shipping lines and terminal operators, which represent significant cost items in container shipping. The system must automatically verify charged free days against the contracted free-time terms in the applicable tariff or service agreement. Container-level verification must cross-reference the detention/demurrage period against actual container movement data (gate-in, gate-out timestamps from terminal operating systems). The AI must flag invoices where the charged period overlaps with port congestion events (where the carrier or terminal has issued force majeure notices), weekends/holidays (if excluded per agreement), or periods where containers were under customs hold (not attributable to the consignee). The system must calculate the correct chargeable days per container and highlight variances against the invoiced amount, generating a dispute report template for overcharges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R009	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Freight and Haulage Invoice Rate Verification	The system must automatically verify freight and inland haulage invoice rates against contracted tariff schedules stored in the system. For ocean freight, verification must check the charged rate per TEU/FEU against the applicable tariff based on trade lane, container type (dry, reefer, hazardous), weight slab, and contract validity period. For inland haulage, the system must verify per-trip or per-container rates based on the origin-destination pair, container size, and any applicable surcharges (fuel adjustment factor, toll charges, overweight penalties). The AI must detect and flag common billing errors including: wrong container size charged (20' rate applied to 40' movement), duplicate charges for the same container trip, charges for cancelled bookings, and surcharges not covered by the contract. Rate discrepancies must be quantified per line item with a total variance summary for the invoice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R010	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Dispute Management and Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a structured dispute management workflow for invoices where discrepancies are identified during processing or matching. Dispute creation must capture the dispute category (pricing variance, quantity mismatch, duplicate charge, unauthorized charge, quality issue, missing documentation), affected line items, disputed amount, and supporting documentation. The system must automatically notify the vendor through the vendor portal and email with dispute details in the vendor's preferred language (Arabic or English). Resolution tracking must support multiple resolution types: vendor issues credit note, vendor provides revised invoice, dispute resolved in vendor's favor (invoice approved as-is with justification), or partial resolution with agreed settlement amount. The system must enforce SLA tracking for dispute resolution (configurable, default: 15 business days) with escalation to senior management for aged disputes. Analytics must show dispute trends by vendor, category, and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R011	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Document Attachment and Compliance Verification	The system must validate that all required supporting documents are attached to or linked with each invoice based on the PO type and entity-specific requirements. Required documents by invoice type must include: for freight invoices — bill of lading copy, for terminal handling — container move confirmation, for customs brokerage — customs declaration (Bayan for Qatar/UAE, Sadad for KSA, Bill of Entry for India) and duty payment receipt, for bunker supply — Bunker Delivery Note (BDN) per MARPOL Annex VI requirements, for survey services — survey report, and for haulage — proof of delivery with GPS tracking confirmation. The AI must verify document completeness and flag missing attachments, blocking invoice approval until mandatory documents are provided. Document verification must include basic authenticity checks such as validating that the BL number on the freight invoice matches an actual BL in the documentation module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R012	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Recurring and Accrual Invoice Processing	The system must handle recurring invoices for periodic services (office rent, equipment leases, software subscriptions, vessel agency retainers) with automated matching against recurring PO schedules. For month-end close, the system must support accrual invoice creation for services received but not yet invoiced, based on GRN/service receipt data and PO commitments. The AI must identify POs with receipts recorded but no matching invoice received by the accrual cut-off date (configurable per entity) and auto-generate accrual journal entries. When the actual invoice is subsequently received, the system must automatically reverse the accrual and process the invoice normally. The accrual process must correctly handle multi-currency POs by applying the month-end exchange rate for accrual booking and tracking the exchange rate difference upon actual invoice receipt. Accrual reports must be generated per entity for review by the finance team before month-end close.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R013	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	E-Invoicing Compliance for KSA ZATCA	The system must fully comply with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) Phase 2 e-invoicing requirements for all invoices received by the KSA entity. Incoming e-invoices must be validated against the ZATCA UBL 2.1 XML schema, verifying the presence and correctness of mandatory fields including the seller's and buyer's VAT registration numbers, invoice type code (388 for standard, 381 for credit note), cryptographic stamp, QR code containing the TLV (Tag-Length-Value) encoded fields, and digital signature. The system must integrate with the ZATCA Fatoora portal API for invoice clearance (B2B) and reporting (B2C). Non-compliant invoices must be rejected with a clear error description referencing the specific ZATCA validation rule that failed. The system must maintain a compliance dashboard showing ZATCA submission status, rejection reasons, and clearance timestamps for all KSA entity invoices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R014	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Processing SLA Monitoring and Dashboard	The system must track and display real-time SLA metrics for invoice processing across all entities. Key SLAs must include: time from invoice receipt to data extraction completion (target: 4 hours for digital, 24 hours for scanned), time from extraction to match completion (target: 4 hours for auto-match, 48 hours for manual), time from match to approval (target: 24 hours for auto-approved, 72 hours for manual), and total invoice-to-payment-ready cycle time (target: 5 business days end-to-end). The dashboard must show SLA performance by entity, vendor category, invoice type, and processing channel (portal, email, EDI). The AI must identify bottlenecks in the processing pipeline and recommend process improvements. SLA breaches must trigger automated escalation notifications to the AP manager. Monthly SLA compliance reports must be generated automatically with trend analysis comparing current month performance against the trailing 6-month average.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R015	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Vendor Statement Reconciliation	The system must support periodic reconciliation of the entity's accounts payable ledger against vendor statements received via the vendor portal, email, or manual upload. The AI must automatically match vendor statement line items (invoices, credit notes, payments) against the entity's AP sub-ledger records using invoice numbers, dates, and amounts with configurable matching tolerance (default: 1% of line amount or QAR/AED/SAR 100 / INR 1000, whichever is lower). Unmatched items must be categorized as: invoices on statement not in AP (potentially unprocessed invoices), items in AP not on statement (timing differences or vendor omissions), and amount mismatches. The system must generate a reconciliation report showing matched items, open items by aging bucket, and total reconciling differences. This reconciliation must be schedulable (monthly or quarterly) per vendor based on vendor tier, with higher-spend vendors reconciled more frequently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R016	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Batch Invoice Upload and Processing	The system must support batch processing of multiple invoices uploaded simultaneously via CSV/Excel template, zip file containing multiple PDFs, or API batch endpoint. The batch upload must validate each invoice independently, providing a processing status report showing successful extractions, failed extractions with error reasons, and duplicate detections. For large terminal operators (like DP World at Jebel Ali or JNPCT at JNPT) who issue consolidated monthly invoices covering hundreds of container moves, the system must parse and create individual charge records per container while maintaining the link to the consolidated invoice. Batch processing must support scheduling for off-peak hours to manage system load, with email notification upon completion. The system must handle batches of up to 1,000 invoices per upload with a maximum processing time of 4 hours for the complete batch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-003-R017	FEAT-017-1-003	MOD-017	Invoice Aging and AP Balance Reporting	The system must generate accounts payable aging reports showing outstanding vendor balances categorized by aging buckets: Current (not yet due), 1-30 days past due, 31-60 days past due, 61-90 days past due, and over 90 days past due. The aging must be calculated based on the invoice due date derived from the vendor's payment terms. Reports must be available per entity, consolidated across entities (with intercompany eliminations), per vendor, per vendor category, and per cost center. All amounts must be displayed in the entity's base currency with the option to view in original invoice currency. The report must differentiate between disputed and undisputed overdue amounts. The AI must provide a cash flow forecast based on the aging schedule, highlighting weeks with unusually high payment obligations. The report must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with support for Arabic and English headers and right-to-left number formatting for Arabic versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R001	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Automated PO-GRN-Invoice Matching Engine	The system must provide a fully automated 3-way match engine that compares vendor invoice line items against the corresponding purchase order line items and goods receipt/service completion records. The matching must operate at the line item level, comparing: quantity invoiced versus quantity ordered (from PO) versus quantity received (from GRN), unit price invoiced versus unit price on PO, and line total invoiced versus expected amount. The AI engine must handle fuzzy description matching where invoice line item descriptions do not exactly match PO descriptions but refer to the same service or goods (e.g., 'THC charges — 40ft' matching 'Terminal Handling Charge — 40HC container'). The matching engine must process invoices within 60 seconds of data extraction completion and must achieve a minimum 85% auto-match rate for invoices linked to valid PO references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R002	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Configurable Matching Tolerance Rules	The system must support configurable tolerance rules for the 3-way match at multiple levels: global default, per entity, per vendor, per PO type, and per line item category. Tolerance must be definable as both a percentage and an absolute amount, with the match passing if either condition is met. Default tolerances must be: quantity tolerance of +/- 5% for physical goods and 0% for services, price tolerance of +/- 2% or QAR/AED/SAR 50 / INR 500, and total tolerance of +/- 3% or QAR/AED/SAR 500 / INR 5,000. For shipping-specific scenarios, special tolerances must apply: bunker fuel quantity tolerance of +/- 0.5% (per industry standard for mass measurement), freight rates with zero tolerance (must match contract exactly), and container per diem charges with +/- 1 day tolerance for on-hire/off-hire date rounding. Tolerance rules must be version-controlled with approval workflow for any changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R003	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Partial Match and Progressive Matching	The system must support partial matching scenarios common in container shipping where a single PO receives multiple deliveries and/or multiple invoices. The matching engine must track cumulative quantities and amounts matched against each PO line item, preventing over-invoicing beyond the PO value plus tolerance. Progressive matching must handle scenarios such as: a bunker PO for 500 MT receiving three deliveries (200 MT, 150 MT, 150 MT) each with a separate invoice; a container lease PO receiving monthly per diem invoices over a 6-month period; and a terminal handling PO receiving separate invoices per vessel call. The system must maintain a match status per PO line showing: total ordered, total received, total invoiced, total matched, and remaining open balance. When the cumulative invoiced amount approaches the PO limit, the system must alert the AP team and block further matching beyond PO value plus tolerance without an amendment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R004	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	2-Way Match for Non-PO Invoices	The system must support a 2-way match process (invoice against receipt/approval only, without PO) for categories of expenditure that do not require purchase orders, such as government fees (port authority charges, customs duty payments), utility invoices, statutory payments, and emergency procurement below a configurable threshold per entity. The 2-way match must validate the invoice against a pre-approved expense authorization or standing approval. Non-PO invoices must require additional approval compared to PO-matched invoices, with a minimum of Department Head and Finance Manager dual approval. The system must track non-PO invoice spend as a percentage of total AP and flag to management when the ratio exceeds a configurable threshold (default: 15%), as high non-PO spending may indicate procurement process bypass. Analytics must identify recurring non-PO vendors who should be brought under PO coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R005	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Match Exception Categorization and Routing	The system must automatically categorize match exceptions into predefined types and route them to the appropriate resolver. Exception categories must include: price variance (invoice price differs from PO price beyond tolerance), quantity variance (invoiced quantity differs from received quantity), over-invoicing (cumulative invoiced amount exceeds PO value), missing GRN (invoice received but no goods receipt recorded), missing PO (invoice references a non-existent or incorrect PO number), tax discrepancy (invoiced tax differs from computed tax), and currency mismatch (invoice currency differs from PO currency). Each exception type must have a configurable routing rule: price variances to Procurement for rate negotiation, quantity variances to Operations/Warehouse for receipt verification, tax discrepancies to Tax/Compliance, and missing GRN to the requesting department. The system must provide exception aging tracking with SLA targets per exception type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R006	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	AI-Powered Match Suggestion for Unlinked Invoices	For invoices that do not contain a valid PO reference, the AI engine must attempt automatic PO matching using a multi-factor similarity analysis. Matching factors must include: vendor code (exact match required), time proximity between PO date and invoice date, description similarity between invoice line items and PO line items using NLP, amount proximity (invoice total within 20% of open PO balance), referenced vessel names or voyage numbers, and container numbers mentioned in the invoice cross-referenced against PO-linked containers. The AI must present the top 3 candidate PO matches with a confidence score per candidate. Matches with confidence above 90% and amount within tolerance must be auto-applied with a flag for post-processing review. Matches between 70-90% must be presented to the AP clerk for manual confirmation. Below 70%, the invoice must be flagged as unmatched for manual PO assignment or non-PO processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R007	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Multi-Currency Match with Forex Tolerance	The system must handle 3-way matching for transactions where the PO, GRN, and invoice involve different currencies or exchange rates. Common scenarios in container shipping include: PO issued in USD at a budgeted exchange rate, invoice received in USD but needing to match against a QAR/INR-denominated GRN, or PO in AED with invoice in USD requiring conversion. The matching engine must first compare amounts in the transaction currency (original currency of each document) and then in the entity's base currency. A forex tolerance must be applied separately from the price/quantity tolerance, configurable per entity (default: 3% for currencies pegged to USD like AED/QAR/SAR, 5% for floating currencies like INR). Forex variances within tolerance must auto-clear with the difference posted to a designated forex gain/loss GL account. Forex variances exceeding tolerance must create a separate exception requiring Treasury/Finance approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R008	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Service-Based Matching with Milestone Verification	The system must support milestone-based matching for service POs where payment is contingent on completion of defined deliverables rather than physical goods receipt. For container shipping, this applies to vessel agency appointments (payment upon completion of port call), ship repair contracts (payment per milestone: drydocking, hull work, sea trial), classification society surveys (payment upon certificate issuance), and IT/consulting projects. Each milestone must have defined acceptance criteria, a designated verifier (operations manager, technical superintendent, or project owner), and a payment percentage linked to the PO value. The 3-way match for service milestones must validate: milestone completion certification signed by the designated verifier, completion date within the contractual timeline, and invoiced amount matching the milestone payment percentage. Partial milestone acceptance must be supported with proportional payment calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R009	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Automated Tolerance Override and Approval Escalation	The system must provide a structured override mechanism for match exceptions that fall outside configured tolerances but have valid business justifications. Override authority must be tiered: Level 1 — AP Supervisor may override variances up to 5% above tolerance or QAR/AED/SAR 1,000 / INR 10,000, Level 2 — AP Manager up to 10% above tolerance or QAR/AED/SAR 5,000 / INR 50,000, Level 3 — Finance Controller for any amount with CFO notification for overrides exceeding QAR/AED/SAR 50,000 / INR 500,000. Each override must require a mandatory justification text (minimum 20 characters), attachment of supporting documentation (amended agreement, email confirmation, market rate evidence), and the selection of a variance reason code from a predefined list. The system must track override frequency per user and flag users who override more than a configurable percentage (default: 20%) of their processed matches for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R010	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Retroactive Price Adjustment Matching	The system must handle retroactive price adjustments common in container shipping where tariff rates are renegotiated mid-contract and applied retrospectively to previous transactions. For example, when a terminal handling charge tariff is revised at Jebel Ali effective retroactively from the beginning of the quarter, the system must: identify all POs and invoices affected by the rate change within the effective period, compute the price differential per transaction, match incoming credit notes or supplementary invoices from the vendor against the computed adjustments, and post the variance to the appropriate GL accounts with clear linkage to the original transactions. The system must support both vendor-initiated adjustments (credit notes received) and buyer-initiated adjustments (debit notes raised). A retroactive adjustment report must summarize all affected transactions, the net financial impact, and the accounting entries generated, segmented by entity and cost center.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R011	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Matching Dashboard and Real-Time Status Visibility	The system must provide a real-time matching dashboard accessible to AP staff, procurement, and finance managers showing the current status of all invoices in the matching pipeline. The dashboard must display: total invoices pending match, auto-matched and approved count, invoices with exceptions by category, aging of unmatched invoices (1-3 days, 4-7 days, 8-14 days, 15+ days), match rate percentage (auto-matched / total invoices) trending over time, and value of invoices blocked in matching by entity. Drill-down capability must allow users to navigate from dashboard metrics to the individual invoice or PO detail. Filters must include entity, vendor, PO type, match status, exception type, and date range (with Hijri calendar support for KSA). The dashboard must be optimized for large data volumes, rendering within 3 seconds even with over 10,000 invoices in the pipeline across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R012	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Goods Receipt Reversal and Rematch Handling	The system must correctly handle scenarios where a goods receipt or service completion record is reversed or amended after an invoice has already been matched. If a GRN is reversed (due to rejected goods, incorrect quantity recording, or quality failure), the system must automatically flag the associated matched invoice for re-evaluation. The re-evaluation must determine whether the invoice can be rematched against a corrected GRN, must be partially reversed with a credit note request to the vendor, or must be fully reversed with the invoice returned to the vendor. The rematch process must maintain a complete audit trail showing the original match, the GRN reversal event, the rematch decision, and the final resolution. For shipping-specific scenarios such as bunker fuel quantity disputes discovered after initial receipt (e.g., survey reveals short delivery), the system must support quantity adjustment workflows that propagate through to invoice matching and payment adjustment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R013	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Intercompany PO and Invoice Cross-Entity Matching	The system must support 3-way matching for intercompany transactions where one entity issues a PO to another entity within the group. For example, the Qatar entity (Hamad Port operations) may procure container repositioning services from the UAE entity (Jebel Ali operations). The matching engine must recognize intercompany vendor codes and apply intercompany pricing rules (transfer pricing at arm's length). Both the AP entry in the purchasing entity and the AR entry in the selling entity must be generated and matched simultaneously to maintain intercompany balance consistency. The system must validate that intercompany invoices use the agreed transfer pricing methodology and flag deviations. Month-end intercompany reconciliation must automatically identify unmatched intercompany transactions across all four entities, generating a reconciliation report with aging analysis and a one-click journal proposal for resolving differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R014	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Match Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a detailed, immutable audit trail for every matching action including auto-match execution results, manual match confirmations, exception creation and resolution, tolerance overrides, and match reversals. Each audit record must capture the timestamp, user ID (or 'SYSTEM' for automated actions), the matching rule applied, all compared values (PO amount, GRN amount, invoice amount), the tolerance applied, the match decision (pass, fail, override), and any comments. The audit trail must support regulatory audit requirements across all four jurisdictions: Qatar Commercial Companies Law record retention, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 of 2021, KSA ZATCA audit requirements, and India Companies Act 2013 and GST audit provisions. Compliance reports must be generatable showing match accuracy rates, exception trends, override patterns, and processing cycle times. All reports must be available in both Arabic and English with entity-specific regulatory formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R015	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Configurable Matching Rules Engine	The system must provide a rules engine allowing authorized users (minimum: AP Manager role) to configure and modify matching rules without code changes. The rules engine must support: defining which fields are compared in the match (quantity, price, amount, description, dates), setting match priority (which PO lines are matched first when multiple lines could match an invoice line), configuring auto-approval conditions (match within tolerance AND invoice below threshold AND vendor in approved category), defining sequential or parallel matching steps, and creating entity-specific or vendor-specific rule overrides. Rules must be version-controlled with an effective date range, approval workflow for rule changes, and rollback capability. The system must provide a simulation mode where proposed rule changes can be tested against historical invoice data to predict the impact on match rates and exception volumes before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R016	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Batch Matching and Scheduled Processing	The system must support both real-time individual invoice matching (triggered upon invoice data extraction completion) and scheduled batch matching for high-volume periods. Batch matching must be schedulable at configurable intervals (default: every 4 hours during business hours, nightly for full reconciliation) and must process all unmatched invoices in the queue. The batch must prioritize invoices by payment due date urgency, early payment discount eligibility, and vendor priority tier. Batch processing must handle a minimum of 5,000 invoices per run with completion within 2 hours. The system must provide batch processing reports showing: invoices processed, auto-matched count and value, exceptions generated by category, and processing duration. During batch processing, the system must remain available for manual invoice processing and ad-hoc matching operations without performance degradation exceeding 10% on response times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R017	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Early Payment Discount Detection and Optimization	The AI engine must automatically detect early payment discount terms on vendor invoices (e.g., 2/10 Net 30 — 2% discount if paid within 10 days) by parsing invoice text and cross-referencing against vendor master payment terms. When a discount-eligible invoice passes the 3-way match, the system must calculate the annualized return on early payment (e.g., 2% discount for 20 days early = 36.5% annualized) and compare it against the entity's cost of capital or short-term investment return rate. If the annualized return exceeds the entity's threshold (configurable, default: 10%), the system must flag the invoice for expedited payment processing and route it to the Treasury team with a recommendation to capture the discount. The system must track captured versus missed early payment discounts per entity, vendor, and period, reporting the financial impact of discount capture performance in both the transaction currency and the entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-1-004-R018	FEAT-017-1-004	MOD-017	Machine Learning Match Accuracy Improvement	The system must implement a continuous learning mechanism where the AI matching engine improves its accuracy over time based on user corrections and exception resolutions. When an AP clerk manually resolves a match exception (e.g., confirming that 'Container handling — import' on an invoice matches 'THC Import 40HC' on the PO), the system must capture this as a training signal for the NLP-based description matching model. The ML pipeline must retrain on accumulated corrections at configurable intervals (default: weekly) with model performance metrics tracked across versions. Key metrics must include: auto-match rate (target: 85% baseline increasing to 95% within 12 months), false positive rate (incorrect auto-matches, target: below 0.5%), and average confidence score for auto-matched invoices. The system must support A/B testing of new matching models against production models before deployment and must provide an explainability interface showing why the AI made specific matching decisions for compliance and audit transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R001	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Multi-Currency Payment Execution Engine	The system must execute vendor payments in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with real-time exchange rate integration from central bank feeds (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI). Each payment transaction must record the base currency amount, target currency amount, applied exchange rate, rate source timestamp, and any bank margin. For cross-border payments related to container shipping services such as port handling at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), or JNPT (INNHV), the system must automatically determine the optimal payment currency based on vendor preference, contract terms, and current FX rates. Settlement must support T+0, T+1, and T+2 value dating with automatic holiday calendar adjustments per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R002	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Automated Payment Run Scheduling	The system must support configurable automated payment runs that can be scheduled daily, weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each payment run must aggregate approved invoices by vendor, currency, and payment method, applying early payment discounts where applicable. The AI engine must recommend optimal payment run timing based on cash flow forecasts, vessel arrival schedules at ports like Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, and discount capture opportunities. Payment runs must respect entity-specific cut-off times aligned with local banking hours—for example, QNB cut-off at 13:00 AST for same-day SWIFT transfers. Users must be able to override AI recommendations with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R002	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Port Tariff Schedule Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive port tariff schedule database for each supported port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) containing all published tariff items such as port dues, pilotage, towage, berth hire, mooring/unmooring, anchorage dues, light dues, and container handling charges. Each tariff item must have an effective date range, calculation basis (per GRT, per NRT, per meter LOA, per TEU, flat rate, or per hour), applicable currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Khalifa/Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT), and any applicable tax rates including Indian GST at 18% and UAE/Qatar exemptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R003	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	SWIFT and Wire Transfer Integration	The system must generate SWIFT MT103 (single customer credit transfer) and MT101 (request for transfer) messages for international vendor payments, particularly for overseas container line operators, foreign port authorities, and international freight forwarders. Each SWIFT message must include correct BIC codes, IBAN validation for GCC banks, and IFSC codes for Indian beneficiaries. The system must support STP (Straight Through Processing) rates above 95% by ensuring all mandatory fields are pre-validated before submission. Failed transfers must trigger automatic retry logic with configurable retry limits (default 3 attempts) and escalation workflows notifying the treasury team with full error details from the banking middleware.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R004	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Letter of Credit Payment Settlement	The system must process LC-based payments for container shipping operations where vendors require documentary credit, commonly used for vessel charter hire, bulk bunker fuel purchases, and high-value equipment procurement. Upon presentation of compliant documents (Bill of Lading, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin), the system must match LC terms against presented documents and flag discrepancies per UCP 600 rules. Payment settlement must integrate with the trade finance module, recording LC utilization against the credit facility. The system must track LC expiry dates using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, sending automated alerts 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry to prevent lapses on critical vendor commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R005	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Cheque Generation and Management	The system must generate printed cheques for vendors who require physical payment instruments, which remains common for certain local suppliers at Hamad Port and smaller Indian freight agents. Cheque generation must support Arabic and English bilingual printing with amount in words in both languages, compliant with local banking standards. The system must maintain a cheque register per bank account per entity, tracking cheque numbers sequentially, recording issuance dates, clearance dates, and stale-cheque thresholds (default 180 days for GCC, 90 days for India per Negotiable Instruments Act). Post-dated cheques must be supported with automatic liability recognition on the cheque date and release tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R006	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Early Payment Discount Optimization	The AI engine must analyze all pending vendor invoices and recommend which invoices to pay early to capture discount terms such as 2/10 Net 30 or 1/15 Net 45, commonly offered by container liner agents, terminal operators, and marine supply vendors. The recommendation engine must calculate the annualized return on early payment against the entity's weighted average cost of capital (WACC) and current cash position. For multi-entity operations, the system must evaluate cross-entity treasury pooling opportunities where a Qatar entity might fund an early payment for the UAE entity if the discount capture exceeds internal transfer costs. All discount decisions must be auditable with full financial justification trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R007	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Payment Approval Workflow Engine	The system must enforce configurable multi-level approval workflows for payment processing based on amount thresholds, vendor category, entity, and currency. For example, payments above SAR 500,000 to vessel operators in KSA may require CFO and GM approval, while routine port handling payments below QAR 50,000 at Hamad Port may require only department head approval. The workflow engine must support parallel and sequential approval chains, delegation of authority during leave periods, and mobile approval via push notifications. Approval must capture digital signatures with timestamps, IP addresses, and device identifiers for audit compliance with local regulations including QFCRA, DFSA, and CMA requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R008	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Payment Status Tracking and Notifications	The system must provide real-time payment status tracking through the complete lifecycle: initiated, approved, submitted-to-bank, acknowledged, settled, and reconciled. Each status transition must generate notifications to relevant stakeholders including the requesting department, accounts payable team, and optionally the vendor via email or SMS. For container shipping operations where timely payments affect vessel turnaround—such as port dues at Khalifa Port or container detention charges at JNPT—the system must flag payment delays that could impact operations and escalate to operations managers. The tracking dashboard must display payments in both list and timeline views with Arabic RTL layout support and currency-appropriate decimal formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R009	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Withholding Tax Deduction at Source	The system must automatically calculate and deduct withholding tax (WHT) from vendor payments based on the vendor's tax residency, service type, and applicable treaty rates. For Indian operations, TDS must be deducted per Income Tax Act sections 194C (contractor payments at 1-2%), 194J (professional services at 10%), and 194I (rent at 10%). In KSA, WHT at 5-20% must apply to non-resident vendor payments per ZATCA regulations. The system must generate WHT certificates (Form 16A for India, ZATCA-compliant certificates for KSA), track quarterly filing deadlines, and maintain a WHT rate master that accounts for double taxation avoidance agreements (DTAA) between GCC countries and India for shipping-related services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R010	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Batch Payment File Generation	The system must generate bank-specific batch payment files for bulk vendor payment processing, supporting formats required by major banking partners: QNB (proprietary XML), Emirates NBD (ENBD H2H format), Al Rajhi Bank (SAR payment flat file), and State Bank of India (NEFT/RTGS batch format). Each batch file must include proper header and trailer records with control totals, payment counts, and hash totals for reconciliation. The system must validate all beneficiary account details before file generation, checking IBAN structure for GCC payments (QA prefix for Qatar, AE for UAE, SA for KSA) and IFSC/account number combinations for Indian payments. Failed validations must prevent file generation and route exceptions to AP analysts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R011	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Recurring Payment Template Management	The system must support creation and management of recurring payment templates for regular vendor obligations such as monthly terminal handling agreement payments at Jebel Ali, quarterly vessel agency retainer fees, annual port concession charges at Hamad Port, and periodic container depot lease rentals. Each template must define the vendor, amount (fixed or variable with formula), currency, payment method, frequency, start date, end date, and approval workflow. The AI engine must detect anomalies in recurring payments—for example, a 20% increase in a monthly terminal charge that historically remained stable—and flag for review before auto-processing. Templates must support Hijri-based frequencies for KSA entities where fiscal periods align with the Islamic calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R012	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Payment Reversal and Void Processing	The system must support payment reversal for erroneous or duplicate payments, generating appropriate SWIFT MT192 cancellation requests for wire transfers, stop-payment instructions for cheques, and debit notes for already-settled transactions. Each reversal must require documented justification and senior approval (minimum one level above original approver). The reversal process must automatically create the corresponding accounting entries to reverse the original payment posting, adjust vendor balances, and reinstate the original invoice to payable status. For container shipping scenarios where a payment was made against incorrect Bill of Lading references or wrong container numbers (e.g., paying for TCLU containers instead of MSCU containers), the system must link the reversal to the corrected documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R013	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Vendor Payment Portal Self-Service	The system must provide a secure external portal where registered vendors can view their payment status, download remittance advices, update banking details, and submit payment inquiries. The portal must support Arabic and English with automatic RTL layout switching based on language selection. Vendors must authenticate using multi-factor authentication and can only access their own payment data scoped to the relevant entity. For major shipping vendors such as container line operators (MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM) who operate across multiple entities, the portal must offer a consolidated view across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations with currency conversion summaries. Bank detail changes submitted through the portal must trigger a verification workflow requiring AP team confirmation before activation to prevent payment fraud.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R014	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Payment Compliance and Sanctions Screening	The system must screen all outbound vendor payments against international sanctions lists including OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List, UN Security Council sanctions, and local lists from Qatar Financial Information Unit, UAE Executive Office for AML/CTF, and India's UAPA list. Screening must occur at payment initiation and again at execution for any payments queued more than 24 hours. Matches must halt payment processing and route to the compliance team with match details, confidence scores, and vendor background information. For container shipping, the system must additionally screen vessel names associated with vendor invoices (e.g., a bunker fuel payment referencing a sanctioned vessel IMO number) and flag payments to vendors in high-risk jurisdictions as defined by FATF grey/black lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R015	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Offline Payment Processing Capability	The system must support offline payment processing for scenarios where network connectivity is unavailable, which can occur at remote port locations or during system maintenance windows. In offline mode, users must be able to queue payment approvals, generate cheques from local cache, and record manual payment confirmations. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline transactions with the central database, detecting and resolving conflicts such as duplicate approvals or payments against invoices already paid by another entity. Offline queued payments must be clearly marked with a pending-sync status in the UI, and the system must prevent the same invoice from being paid twice through optimistic locking with server-side validation upon sync completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-001-R016	FEAT-017-2-001	MOD-017	Dynamic Payment Priority Assignment	The AI engine must dynamically assign payment priorities based on multiple weighted factors including vendor criticality score, invoice aging, operational impact, contractual penalties, and relationship value. For container shipping operations, payments to port authorities (Mawani, Abu Dhabi Ports, QTerminals) must receive elevated priority since delayed port payments can result in vessel holds and significant demurrage costs exceeding USD 30,000 per day. The system must allow manual priority overrides with documented reasoning and must generate a prioritized payment queue dashboard showing the recommended payment sequence within available cash balances per entity and currency. Priority algorithms must be configurable per entity to reflect different business conditions in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India markets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R001	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Automated Statement Ingestion and Parsing	The system must automatically ingest vendor account statements in multiple formats including PDF, Excel, CSV, EDI, and structured XML. The AI parser must extract key fields: statement date, transaction date, reference numbers, amounts, currency, and running balance. For container shipping vendors, the parser must recognize industry-specific reference formats such as Bill of Lading numbers (MAEU format for Maersk, MSCU for MSC), booking references, container numbers (ISO 6346 format), and voyage numbers. The parser must handle bilingual Arabic-English statements common from GCC-based vendors, extracting data correctly regardless of text direction. Parsed statements must be stored with the original document for audit reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R002	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	AI-Powered Transaction Matching Engine	The system must employ AI algorithms to match vendor statement lines against internal AP ledger entries using multi-factor matching criteria: amount (exact and tolerance-based), date proximity, reference number similarity, and transaction description analysis. The matching engine must handle common container shipping scenarios including partial payments against multiple invoices, consolidated payments for multiple Bills of Lading, and split invoices for a single shipment across CIF/FOB INCOTERMS components. The engine must achieve a minimum 85% automatic match rate with a false positive rate below 2%. Unmatched items must be categorized as: vendor-only, ledger-only, or partial-match with suggested resolution actions ranked by confidence score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R003	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Multi-Currency Reconciliation Handling	The system must reconcile vendor accounts where transactions occur in different currencies, correctly handling exchange rate differences between the vendor's recorded rate and the entity's booked rate. For a typical scenario where a Jebel Ali terminal operator invoices in AED but the Indian entity books in INR, the reconciliation must identify legitimate FX differences versus actual discrepancies. Tolerance thresholds must be configurable per currency pair—for example, 0.5% for USD/QAR (pegged) and 2% for USD/INR (floating). The system must separately classify FX gain/loss differences from true transaction discrepancies and automatically generate FX adjustment journal entries for immaterial differences below entity-defined thresholds, recording the adjustment rationale for auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R004	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Vendor Balance Confirmation Workflow	The system must generate and manage vendor balance confirmation requests for periodic reconciliation, supporting both positive confirmation (vendor confirms stated balance) and negative confirmation (vendor responds only if balance differs) methods. Confirmation letters must be generated in Arabic and English with entity letterhead, showing outstanding invoice details, credit notes, advance payments, and net balance as of the confirmation date. The system must track confirmation responses, automatically match confirmed balances against ledger balances, and flag discrepancies for investigation. For year-end audits aligned with Qatar Financial Year (January-December), UAE fiscal year, KSA fiscal year (aligned to Gregorian since 2017), and India fiscal year (April-March), the system must support batch confirmation generation across all active vendors per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R005	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Aging Analysis and Dispute Management	The system must generate vendor-side aging analysis showing outstanding payables in standard buckets (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 91-120, 120+ days) with drill-down to individual invoices. When reconciliation identifies disputed items—such as a container line charging demurrage for 15 days at JNPT when the shipper claims only 10 days of container detention—the system must create a formal dispute record linked to the reconciliation, assign it to the responsible department (operations for shipping disputes, procurement for pricing disputes), and track resolution through to final settlement or write-off. Dispute aging must be tracked separately from invoice aging, and unresolved disputes older than 90 days must trigger automatic escalation to senior management with a financial impact summary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R006	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Automated Reconciliation Scheduling	The system must support automated reconciliation scheduling based on vendor tier classification: Tier 1 vendors (container lines, port authorities, major freight forwarders) must be reconciled weekly, Tier 2 vendors (local transporters, warehouse operators, customs brokers) monthly, and Tier 3 vendors (office supplies, miscellaneous services) quarterly. Reconciliation schedules must be configurable per entity and vendor, with the AI engine recommending frequency adjustments based on transaction volume changes and historical discrepancy rates. The scheduler must distribute reconciliation workload evenly across the AP team, accounting for team capacity, and send reminders 3 days before scheduled reconciliation dates. Missed reconciliations must generate compliance alerts visible to the AP manager dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R007	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Debit Note and Credit Note Reconciliation	The system must accurately reconcile debit notes issued to vendors and credit notes received from vendors against the original invoices and payment records. In container shipping, credit notes commonly arise from container damage claims, overcharged THC (Terminal Handling Charges) at ports like Khalifa Port, freight rate adjustments for short-shipped cargo, and rebates on annual volume commitments with container lines. The reconciliation engine must link credit notes to their originating invoices using reference chains, validate that credit amounts do not exceed the original invoice value, and ensure that credits are properly offset against future payments. Unlinked credit notes older than 60 days must be flagged for vendor follow-up, and the system must track the net vendor position inclusive of all outstanding credits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R008	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Three-Way Match Reconciliation for Shipping	The system must perform three-way matching for shipping-related vendor invoices by comparing the purchase order, goods receipt or service acknowledgment, and vendor invoice. For container shipping, this includes matching vessel discharge tallies against carrier manifests and freight invoices at ports such as Hamad Port and JNPT. The matching must verify quantities (TEU counts, weight in metric tons), rates (per-container, per-CBM, or lump sum), and applicable surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, documentation fees). Tolerance thresholds must be defined per charge type—for example, zero tolerance on container counts but 1% tolerance on weight-based charges due to scale calibration differences between origin and destination ports. Failed three-way matches must block payment and route to the procurement team for resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R009	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Intercompany Reconciliation for Multi-Entity	The system must reconcile intercompany payable and receivable balances across the four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) where one entity acts as a vendor to another for shared shipping services such as vessel slot purchases, shared container pool usage, or consolidated freight bookings. Intercompany reconciliation must match transactions from both sides simultaneously, identifying timing differences where one entity has booked a transaction but the counterpart has not yet recorded it. The system must generate intercompany reconciliation reports showing matched items, unmatched items from each side, and currency translation differences. Reconciliation must support transfer pricing documentation requirements by linking intercompany charges to arm's-length pricing benchmarks as required by GCC and Indian tax authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R010	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Reconciliation Exception Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive reconciliation exception dashboard displaying all unresolved discrepancies across vendors, entities, and currencies. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout and allow filtering by entity, vendor tier, exception type (amount mismatch, missing transaction, duplicate, timing difference), aging bucket, and materiality threshold. Each exception must display the vendor name, statement reference, ledger reference, variance amount in both original and base currency, days outstanding, and assigned analyst. The AI engine must suggest resolution actions for each exception based on historical patterns—for instance, if a QAR 500 difference with a Hamad Port vendor has historically been a rounding difference, the system should recommend automatic write-off. The dashboard must include trend charts showing exception volumes and resolution times over rolling 12-month periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R011	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Bank Statement to Vendor Payment Reconciliation	The system must reconcile outbound payment entries on bank statements with the internal payment records to confirm that payments recorded as made to vendors have actually been debited from the entity's bank account. For each payment, the reconciliation must match the bank debit amount, value date, and beneficiary reference against the payment run details. This is critical for container shipping operations where large payments to container lines (often exceeding USD 1 million per payment run) must be confirmed cleared before vessel departure from ports like Jebel Ali. The system must identify payments that appear in the ledger but not on the bank statement (outstanding payments) and bank debits without matching payment records (unidentified debits), routing each category to the appropriate team for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R012	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Tolerance-Based Auto-Reconciliation Rules	The system must support configurable auto-reconciliation rules that automatically clear minor discrepancies within defined tolerance levels without manual intervention. Tolerances must be definable as absolute amounts (e.g., differences below QAR 50 auto-reconcile), percentages (e.g., differences below 0.1% of invoice value), or combined thresholds. Rules must be entity-specific and vendor-specific—for example, a stricter tolerance for large container line payments versus a more lenient tolerance for small local transport vendors. Auto-reconciled items must be clearly flagged in the reconciliation report with the applied rule reference, and cumulative auto-reconciliation amounts per vendor per period must be monitored to prevent systematic small discrepancies from accumulating into material misstatements. The system must generate monthly summary reports of all auto-reconciled differences for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R013	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Reconciliation Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all reconciliation activities including statement uploads, matching decisions (automatic and manual), exception resolutions, write-offs, and adjustments. Each audit entry must record the user, timestamp, action taken, and supporting rationale. For regulatory compliance, the system must retain reconciliation records for minimum periods per jurisdiction: 10 years for Qatar (QFC requirements), 5 years for UAE (Federal Law No. 2 of 2015), 10 years for KSA (ZATCA requirements), and 8 years for India (Companies Act 2013). The audit trail must be tamper-proof using sequential hashing, and reconciliation reports must be exportable in formats acceptable to external auditors including Big Four firms commonly engaged by shipping conglomerates in the GCC region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R014	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Vendor Ledger Sub-Ledger to GL Reconciliation	The system must perform automated reconciliation between the vendor sub-ledger (detailed AP records per vendor) and the general ledger control account (aggregated AP balance) on a daily basis. Any difference between the sub-ledger total and GL control account must trigger an immediate alert to the financial controller. The reconciliation must account for timing differences caused by batch posting schedules, in-transit payments, and pending approval items. For multi-entity operations, the reconciliation must run independently per entity with a consolidated group-level view. The system must generate a formal sub-ledger-to-GL reconciliation report as of any user-specified date, which is essential for month-end close procedures and external audit requirements common across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India subsidiaries of shipping groups.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R015	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Duplicate Payment Detection and Recovery	The AI engine must proactively scan for potential duplicate payments using multi-dimensional analysis: same vendor with same amount within a configurable window (default 30 days), same invoice number across entities, same bank reference, or similar amounts to the same vendor within short intervals. For container shipping, the system must recognize that identical amounts to the same carrier can be legitimate—for example, two identical THC payments for different vessels at Hamad Port—and use additional context (container numbers, BL references, voyage numbers) to differentiate true duplicates from valid repeat payments. Confirmed duplicates must trigger an automated recovery workflow generating a debit note to the vendor, tracking the recovery through to collection, and preventing future occurrences by flagging the root cause pattern.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R016	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Advance Payment and Prepayment Reconciliation	The system must track and reconcile vendor advance payments and prepayments against subsequent invoices to ensure proper amortization and balance clearance. In container shipping, advances are common for annual port concession fees at Khalifa Port, prepaid freight contracts with container lines, and advance customs duty deposits at JNPT. The reconciliation must match advances against the invoices they were intended to cover, calculate remaining unapplied balances, and flag advances outstanding beyond contractual delivery periods. For each entity, the system must generate an advance payment aging report showing original advance date, amount, applied portions, and remaining balance in both local currency and USD equivalent. Tax implications of advances must be considered—particularly in India where GST is applicable on advances received.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-002-R017	FEAT-017-2-002	MOD-017	Reconciliation KPI and Performance Metrics	The system must track and report key reconciliation performance metrics including: reconciliation completion rate per scheduled cycle, average time to resolve exceptions, auto-match success rate, number of open exceptions by aging bucket, and total unreconciled balance as a percentage of total vendor spend. Metrics must be viewable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), per AP analyst, and per vendor tier. The AI engine must establish baseline performance levels and alert management when metrics deviate significantly—for example, if the auto-match rate for a Jebel Ali terminal operator drops below 70% indicating a potential systematic invoicing change. Monthly reconciliation scorecards must be generated automatically and distributed to AP managers and financial controllers, with trend analysis comparing current period performance against the trailing 6-month average.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R001	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Multi-Format Invoice Document Ingestion	The system must accept vendor invoices through multiple ingestion channels: email attachment parsing (monitoring designated AP mailboxes per entity), direct upload via web interface, bulk upload via SFTP folder, API integration from vendor portals, and mobile camera capture. Supported document formats must include PDF (both native and scanned), JPEG, PNG, TIFF, Microsoft Word, and Excel. For container shipping operations, the system must also accept EDI-based invoices (EDIFACT INVOIC D96A message type) commonly used by major container lines for freight invoices. Each ingested document must be assigned a unique tracking ID, timestamped, and queued for OCR processing with estimated completion time communicated to the uploader. Maximum supported file size must be 50MB to accommodate multi-page commercial invoices with attached Bills of Lading.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R002	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Arabic-English Bilingual OCR Processing	The OCR engine must accurately extract text from bilingual Arabic-English invoices with a minimum accuracy rate of 97% for printed text and 90% for handwritten annotations. The engine must correctly handle mixed text directions within a single document—Arabic RTL content alongside English LTR fields—which is standard for invoices from GCC-based vendors such as QTerminals, Abu Dhabi Ports, and local customs brokers. The system must recognize Arabic numerals (Eastern Arabic digits ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) and Western Arabic numerals interchangeably, correctly mapping them to numeric values. Date fields must be detected in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, with automatic conversion to the entity's standard calendar. Diacritical marks in Arabic vendor names must be handled to ensure correct vendor matching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R003	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Intelligent Field Extraction and Mapping	The AI extraction engine must identify and extract the following fields from vendor invoices with field-level confidence scores: vendor name, vendor tax registration number (QID/TRN/VAT/GSTIN), invoice number, invoice date, due date, currency, line item descriptions, quantities, unit prices, line totals, tax amounts (VAT/GST), total amount, payment terms, bank details, and purchase order reference. For container shipping invoices, the engine must additionally extract: Bill of Lading number, container numbers, vessel name, voyage number, port of loading, port of discharge, INCOTERMS, and individual charge breakdowns (ocean freight, THC, BAF, CAF, documentation fee, seal charges). Each extracted field must include a confidence score from 0 to 100, with fields scoring below 80 flagged for human review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R004	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Shipping-Specific Charge Code Classification	The AI engine must automatically classify extracted line items from shipping invoices into standardized charge codes used across the ERP system. The classification must map vendor-specific charge descriptions to internal codes—for example, mapping 'Terminal Handling Origin' from Maersk invoices and 'THC at Load Port' from MSC invoices to the same internal charge code THC-ORIGIN. The system must maintain a learning model that adapts to each vendor's invoicing patterns, achieving 95% correct classification after processing 50 invoices from a given vendor. Charge codes must align with standard shipping industry practices and cover categories including: ocean freight (basic, BAF, CAF, PSS, GRI), terminal charges (THC, wharfage, stevedoring), documentation (BL fee, telex release, AMS/ACI filing), and inland logistics (pre-carriage, on-carriage, container haulage).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R005	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Tax Registration Number Validation	The system must extract and validate vendor tax registration numbers against the format rules and official registries of each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must validate QID (11-digit format) and cross-reference against the General Tax Authority registry. For UAE, TRN validation must follow the 15-digit format with check digit verification per FTA specifications. For KSA, VAT registration numbers (15-digit starting with 3) must be validated against ZATCA's VATIN lookup service. For India, GSTIN (15-character alphanumeric) must be validated for structure, state code, PAN linkage, and active status via the GST portal API. Invalid or inactive tax numbers must block invoice processing and trigger vendor master update requests. The system must also detect when a vendor's tax registration has changed between invoices and alert the AP team for vendor record update.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R006	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Invoice Template Learning and Adaptation	The AI engine must learn vendor-specific invoice templates through supervised initial training and continuous unsupervised adaptation. When a new vendor's invoice is first processed, the system must present the extracted data alongside the source document for human verification, using this feedback to create a vendor-specific extraction template. After 5 verified invoices from the same vendor, the system must achieve 95% field-level accuracy without human intervention. The template library must handle vendor invoice format changes gracefully—for instance, when a container line like Hapag-Lloyd updates their invoice layout, the system must detect the format change, flag the first occurrence for human review, and adapt the template accordingly. Templates must be shareable across entities so that learning from processing a CMA CGM invoice in the Qatar entity benefits the UAE, KSA, and India entities automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R007	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Duplicate Invoice Detection During Extraction	The system must check each newly extracted invoice against the existing invoice database to detect duplicates before the invoice enters the AP workflow. Detection must use multi-factor matching: vendor ID, invoice number, invoice date, total amount, and currency. The system must handle vendor-specific numbering quirks—for example, some shipping agents prefix invoice numbers with the year while others use sequential numbering that resets annually, and port authorities like Mawani may issue invoices with similar numbers across different service types. Detected potential duplicates must display side-by-side comparison showing the extracted invoice and existing records, with the AI providing a duplicate probability score. True duplicates must be automatically rejected with notification to the submitter, while false positives must be learnable to improve future detection accuracy per vendor pattern.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R008	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Purchase Order Auto-Matching from Extracted Data	The system must automatically match extracted invoices to existing purchase orders using the PO reference field, vendor ID, and amount correlation. For container shipping, where a single PO may generate multiple invoices (e.g., a freight PO covering multiple shipments over months) or a single invoice may reference multiple POs, the matching engine must handle one-to-many and many-to-one relationships. The system must calculate PO utilization showing how much of the PO value has been invoiced to date and flag over-invoicing beyond PO value plus a configurable tolerance (default 5%). For shipping services ordered without formal POs—common for ad-hoc port charges at Hamad Port or emergency container repairs at JNPT—the system must route to a non-PO invoice workflow requiring additional approval levels before processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R009	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Handwritten Annotation and Stamp Recognition	The OCR system must recognize and extract information from handwritten annotations and stamps commonly found on shipping invoices. This includes handwritten approval signatures, date stamps from customs authorities at ports like Jebel Ali and JNPT, received stamps with dates from the entity's mail room, rubber stamps with vessel agent codes, and handwritten container condition notes. The system must distinguish between informational annotations (which should be captured as metadata) and approval stamps (which affect workflow status). For Arabic handwritten notes, the system must achieve minimum 80% recognition accuracy for common terms used in shipping document annotation. Recognized stamps must be categorized (customs clearance, goods receipt, approval) and linked to the appropriate workflow stage for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R010	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Multi-Page Invoice and Attachment Processing	The system must correctly process multi-page invoices, identifying page boundaries, continuation pages, and distinguishing between the invoice itself and supporting attachments within a single document package. For container shipping, a vendor submission may include a 2-page invoice, a 30-page container list detailing individual container numbers and weights, a Bill of Lading copy, and a delivery order—all in a single PDF. The system must parse each section appropriately, extracting line items across pages with correct total reconciliation, linking container list details to invoice line items, and storing supporting documents as attachments to the invoice record. The extraction engine must handle page numbers, continuation markers, and sub-totals carried forward across pages, flagging any discrepancy between line item totals and the stated invoice total.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R011	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Currency and Amount Extraction Accuracy	The system must extract monetary amounts with 100% accuracy after confidence-based validation, correctly handling different currency notation formats across jurisdictions. This includes: Arabic/Indian numbering with lakhs and crores notation (e.g., INR 12,45,678.00 for twelve lakh forty-five thousand), European-style comma decimals that may appear on some vessel operator invoices (EUR 1.234,56), and standard Western notation (USD 1,234.56). The system must detect the invoice currency from currency symbols (QR, AED, SR, Rs, $), ISO codes, or contextual clues when currency is not explicitly stated. For multi-currency invoices where charges are split across currencies—common in shipping where ocean freight is in USD but local charges are in QAR or INR—the system must extract each currency segment separately and validate that the total converts correctly at the stated exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R012	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Tax Amount Extraction and Validation	The system must extract tax amounts from invoices and validate them against applicable tax rules per jurisdiction. For Qatar (currently 0% VAT but monitoring for future implementation), UAE (5% VAT per FTA), KSA (15% VAT per ZATCA), and India (GST at 5%/12%/18% depending on service HSN code), the system must verify that the stated tax amount matches the expected calculation based on the taxable base amount and applicable rate. For Indian shipping invoices, the system must separately extract CGST, SGST, and IGST components and validate based on whether the transaction is intra-state or inter-state. Reverse charge mechanism applicability must be detected for services from unregistered vendors. Tax calculation mismatches exceeding QAR 1 or equivalent must flag the invoice for tax team review before processing, as incorrect input tax credit claims can result in penalties during VAT/GST audits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R013	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Extraction Confidence Scoring and Human Review	The system must assign confidence scores at both the document level and individual field level, routing invoices through different processing paths based on confidence thresholds. Invoices with all fields above 95% confidence must be auto-processed (straight-through processing). Invoices with any field between 80-95% must be routed to a quick-review queue where the reviewer only validates low-confidence fields. Invoices with any field below 80% must enter a full manual review queue. The human review interface must display the original document image alongside extracted data with low-confidence fields highlighted in amber or red, allowing reviewers to correct values with single-click acceptance or manual override. Each human correction must feed back into the AI model for continuous improvement. The system must track extraction accuracy metrics per vendor, document type, and field to identify systematic issues requiring model retraining.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R014	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Email Invoice Forwarding and Auto-Processing	The system must monitor designated AP email inboxes per entity (e.g., ap-qatar@company.com, ap-uae@company.com) and automatically extract invoice attachments for OCR processing. The system must parse email metadata to identify the sending vendor, extract invoice attachments while ignoring non-invoice attachments (signatures, logos, disclaimers), and handle forwarded emails where the original sender differs from the forwarder. For container shipping operations where vessel agents often send multiple invoices for different services (port charges, agency fees, crew handling) in a single email, the system must detect and separate individual invoices from multi-invoice attachments. Auto-reply confirmation must be sent to the vendor acknowledging receipt, including the assigned tracking numbers. Emails with unreadable or corrupt attachments must generate exception notifications to both the AP team and the sender requesting resubmission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R015	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Document Quality Assessment and Enhancement	The system must automatically assess the quality of ingested invoice documents and apply image enhancement techniques to improve OCR accuracy. Quality assessment must evaluate resolution (minimum 200 DPI required), skew angle, contrast, noise level, and text clarity, assigning a quality score from 0 to 100. Documents scoring below 60 must be automatically enhanced using deskewing, contrast adjustment, noise reduction, and sharpening algorithms before OCR processing. For container shipping documents that have been faxed, photocopied multiple times, or scanned from thermal paper (common for container weighing tickets at ports like JNPT), the system must apply specialized enhancement profiles. Documents that remain below acceptable quality after enhancement must be routed to manual entry with the original and enhanced images provided for reference. Quality metrics must be tracked per vendor to identify vendors consistently submitting poor-quality documents for proactive communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R016	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	Extracted Data Export and ERP Integration	The system must export extracted and validated invoice data in structured formats compatible with the ERP's accounts payable module, generating proper accounting entries based on the extracted information. Each extracted invoice must produce a complete AP voucher including vendor account, expense account mapping based on charge classification, tax account postings, cost center allocation derived from the shipment or project reference, and entity identification. The export must handle the chart of accounts specific to each entity while maintaining group-level consistency. For container shipping, cost allocation must support distribution across multiple cost objects—for example, a single freight invoice covering 50 containers may need allocation across 30 different customer shipments. The integration must be real-time with acknowledgment from the AP module confirming successful voucher creation, and must include rollback capability if downstream validation fails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-003-R017	FEAT-017-2-003	MOD-017	OCR Model Training and Continuous Improvement	The system must support continuous AI model improvement through supervised and unsupervised learning mechanisms. Human corrections made during the review process must be captured as training data, with the model retraining on a configurable schedule (default weekly). The system must track extraction accuracy trends per vendor, per field type, and per document quality level, alerting the AI operations team when accuracy drops below threshold for any category. A/B testing capability must be provided to evaluate new model versions against the production model before deployment. For container shipping document types that evolve over time—such as when the International Maritime Organization (IMO) introduces new documentation requirements or when ports like Hamad Port change their invoice formats—the system must detect format drift and trigger proactive retraining. Model performance dashboards must show precision, recall, and F1 scores segmented by document category and extraction field.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R001	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Real-Time Spend Dashboard with Multi-Entity View	The system must provide an interactive real-time dashboard displaying total vendor spend across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with drill-down capability from group level to entity, department, cost center, vendor category, and individual vendor. The dashboard must support dual currency display showing amounts in both local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and a user-selectable base currency (default USD). Dashboard widgets must include: total spend YTD, spend by vendor category (container lines, port operators, freight forwarders, customs brokers, inland transport), month-over-month trend, top 10 vendors by spend, and budget versus actual comparison. The interface must support Arabic RTL layout with all labels, tooltips, and data formatted correctly for right-to-left reading when Arabic is selected as the display language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R002	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	AI-Powered Spend Categorization and Taxonomy	The AI engine must automatically categorize all vendor spend into a standardized shipping industry taxonomy with a minimum of three hierarchy levels: category (e.g., Sea Freight), sub-category (e.g., FCL Import), and line item type (e.g., Basic Ocean Freight, BAF, CAF, THC). The categorization must achieve 95% accuracy after initial training on 6 months of historical data. Uncategorized or low-confidence items must be routed for human classification, which feeds back into the AI model. The taxonomy must cover all spend categories relevant to container shipping including vessel operations, port and terminal charges, inland transportation, customs and compliance, container management (leasing, repair, repositioning), marine insurance, and general administrative costs. Cross-entity consistency must be enforced so that identical spend types in Qatar and India map to the same taxonomy node.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R003	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Vendor Performance Scorecard Generation	The system must generate automated vendor performance scorecards evaluating key metrics across four dimensions: quality (invoice accuracy rate, dispute frequency, documentation completeness), delivery (on-time service completion, SLA adherence for port operations), cost (rate competitiveness versus market benchmarks, price stability, discount utilization), and compliance (tax document validity, insurance coverage, regulatory certification status). Each vendor must receive a composite score from 0 to 100 with individual dimension scores. For container shipping vendors, quality metrics must include container condition quality for lessors, vessel schedule reliability for carriers calling at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and customs clearance accuracy for brokers. Scorecards must be generated quarterly with trend analysis showing score progression over the last 8 quarters, and must be exportable as PDF reports in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R004	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Predictive Spend Forecasting Engine	The AI engine must generate spend forecasts at vendor, category, entity, and group levels using historical patterns, seasonal trends, and external data inputs. For container shipping, the forecast must incorporate shipping volume projections, published GRI (General Rate Increase) announcements from carriers, fuel price trends affecting BAF surcharges, and known regulatory changes (such as IMO emission regulations impacting fuel costs). Forecasts must project monthly spend for the next 12 months with confidence intervals (80% and 95% bands). The system must detect forecast deviations in real-time, alerting procurement and finance teams when actual spend exceeds forecast by more than 10%. Forecasts must account for Hijri calendar periods affecting KSA spending patterns and Indian fiscal year budget cycles running April to March.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R005	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Budget Variance Analysis and Alerts	The system must track actual vendor spend against approved budgets at multiple granularity levels: entity, department, cost center, expense category, and individual vendor contract. Variance analysis must show absolute and percentage differences with drill-down to the contributing line items. For container shipping operations, budget tracking must accommodate the highly variable nature of freight costs—for example, spot rate fluctuations at Jebel Ali can cause 50% month-to-month variance in ocean freight spend. The system must distinguish between volume-driven variances (more containers shipped than planned) and rate-driven variances (higher per-container cost than budgeted). Automated alerts must trigger at configurable thresholds (default: amber at 80% budget utilization, red at 95%) and must be entity-specific to account for different fiscal year calendars and budget approval processes across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R006	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Vendor Spend Concentration Risk Analysis	The AI engine must analyze vendor spend concentration to identify supply chain risks from over-dependence on single vendors. The analysis must calculate Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI) per spend category per entity and flag categories where a single vendor accounts for more than 40% of total category spend. For container shipping, this is critical for services like container line freight where the entity may be over-reliant on one carrier for a specific trade lane (e.g., Qatar to India West Coast via Jebel Ali transshipment). The system must recommend diversification strategies, identifying alternative vendors in the approved vendor database and estimating switching costs. Risk reports must consider geopolitical factors—for example, concentration risk with vendors from a single country that may be subject to trade restrictions—and present mitigation recommendations to procurement management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R007	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Payment Terms Optimization Analytics	The system must analyze payment term patterns across all vendors and identify optimization opportunities that improve the entity's working capital position. Analysis must include: average days payable outstanding (DPO) by vendor category, early payment discount capture rate, late payment penalty incidence, and comparison of negotiated terms versus actual payment timing. For container shipping operations where cash flow is tied to vessel sailing schedules and freight collection cycles, the system must model the impact of payment term changes on cash flow. The AI engine must recommend vendor-specific payment term renegotiation targets—for instance, extending standard 30-day terms to 45 days for a terminal operator at Khalifa Port if market benchmarks support it. Impact projections must quantify the working capital benefit in local currency and USD, accounting for opportunity cost at the entity's cost of capital.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R005	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Historical DA Benchmarking	The system must provide a benchmarking feature that compares the current proforma DA estimate against historical actual DAs for the same port and similar vessel class (within a configurable GRT/NRT range tolerance of plus or minus 10%). The comparison must display a side-by-side view of each charge category showing the proforma estimate, historical average, historical minimum, and historical maximum from the last 12 months of finalized DAs. Variance percentages must be calculated and any line item exceeding a configurable threshold (default 15%) must be flagged with a visual warning indicator for review by the operations or finance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R008	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Cost Benchmarking Against Industry Standards	The system must enable benchmarking of key cost categories against industry standards and market rates for container shipping services. Benchmarkable categories must include: ocean freight rates per TEU per trade lane, terminal handling charges per TEU at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, customs brokerage fees per declaration, inland transport cost per km per TEU, and container depot charges per TEU per day. The system must integrate with market rate indices (Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, Drewry World Container Index) where available and support manual benchmark entry for local services. Benchmark comparison reports must highlight vendors whose rates exceed benchmark by more than 15%, identifying potential renegotiation candidates. Reports must be generated per entity with cross-entity comparison showing whether the UAE entity achieves better rates than the Qatar entity for similar services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R009	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Regulatory and Tax Compliance Reporting	The system must generate jurisdiction-specific regulatory reports related to vendor payments and spend. For UAE, this includes FTA VAT return support with input tax detail per vendor. For KSA, ZATCA e-invoicing compliance reports and withholding tax certificates per vendor per quarter must be generated. For India, GST reconciliation reports (GSTR-2A matching), TDS return data (quarterly Form 26Q), and MSME payment compliance reports (per Section 43B(h) of Income Tax Act requiring payment within 45 days to micro/small enterprises) must be produced. For Qatar, reports must support QFC and QFCA regulatory requirements. All reports must be generated in the prescribed format for electronic filing, support both Gregorian and Hijri date formats as required by the authority, and include digital signature capability for authorized signatories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R010	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Custom Report Builder with Shipping Context	The system must provide a drag-and-drop report builder allowing authorized users to create custom spend reports without technical assistance. Available data dimensions must include: entity, time period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), vendor, vendor category, spend category, cost center, project or shipment reference, port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), trade lane, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), INCOTERMS, and payment method. Measures must include: gross spend, net spend (after credits), tax amount, count of invoices, average invoice value, and payment cycle days. Reports must support charts (bar, line, pie, waterfall), pivot tables, and tabular formats. Generated reports must be exportable to Excel, PDF, and CSV with Arabic language support including right-to-left formatted Excel exports using proper Arabic column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R011	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Spend Trend Analysis and Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must perform continuous trend analysis on vendor spend patterns, detecting anomalies that deviate from established baselines by more than two standard deviations. Anomaly detection must consider seasonality—for example, higher freight spend during pre-Ramadan peak season and Diwali festival shipping periods is expected and should not trigger false alerts. The system must categorize detected anomalies as: volume anomaly (unusual number of transactions), amount anomaly (unusual transaction values), frequency anomaly (unusual payment timing), and pattern anomaly (unusual vendor or category combinations). For container shipping, the system must correlate spend anomalies with operational events—for instance, a spike in demurrage charges at JNPT may correlate with a port congestion event. Each anomaly must generate an investigation ticket with AI-provided context and recommended resolution steps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R012	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Savings Tracking and Procurement ROI Reporting	The system must track realized savings from procurement activities including vendor renegotiations, competitive bidding outcomes, early payment discount captures, consolidation benefits, and spend under contract versus spot purchasing. Savings must be calculated using a defined baseline methodology (prior period rates, budget rates, or market benchmark rates as configured per category). For container shipping, savings categories must include: freight rate reductions per TEU, THC negotiation outcomes at specific ports, volume commitment rebates from container lines, and inland transport route optimization savings. The system must generate quarterly savings reports per entity and consolidated group level, attributing savings to specific procurement initiatives and team members. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis must be supported for major vendor categories, comparing not just the invoice price but including quality costs, administrative costs, and supply risk costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R013	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Aged Payables Reporting with Operational Context	The system must generate aged payables reports segmented by standard aging buckets (current, 1-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, 90+ days overdue) with additional context specific to container shipping operations. Each overdue invoice must display the associated shipment reference, container numbers, and operational status to help prioritize payments based on business impact. The report must highlight invoices where delayed payment may trigger operational consequences—such as a port authority placing a lien on containers at Hamad Port, a container line applying booking restrictions, or a customs broker holding clearance at JNPT. Reports must be filterable by entity, vendor, and urgency level, and must calculate projected late payment penalties based on vendor contract terms. A separate section must show invoices on hold due to disputes, quality issues, or missing documentation, distinguishing operational holds from cash management deferrals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R014	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Executive Summary and Board-Level Reporting	The system must generate automated executive summary reports suitable for C-suite and board presentation, consolidating AP and spend data across all four entities. The executive report must include: total group spend by entity with YoY comparison, key vendor relationship health indicators, working capital metrics (DPO trend, cash conversion cycle contribution), top 5 risks and opportunities identified by the AI engine, and compliance status across jurisdictions. Visualizations must use professional chart formatting suitable for board decks with support for the entity's branding guidelines. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English, with financial figures presented in thousands or millions with appropriate currency symbols. The report must include a one-page AI-generated narrative summary highlighting the most significant AP trends and recommended management actions for the upcoming quarter, referencing specific ports, trade lanes, and vendor categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R015	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Data Export and Integration with BI Tools	The system must provide standardized data export capabilities for integration with external business intelligence platforms including Microsoft Power BI, Tableau, and SAP Analytics Cloud commonly used by GCC shipping conglomerates. Export must support ODATA feeds, REST API endpoints with OAuth 2.0 authentication, and scheduled flat file exports in CSV and Parquet formats. The data model must expose curated spend analytics dimensions and measures as a star schema with proper foreign key relationships, supporting efficient slicing by all relevant business dimensions. Real-time streaming feeds must be available for critical metrics to power live dashboards in operations centers at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali offices. Data security must enforce entity-level access control ensuring that India entity users cannot access UAE spend data through the BI tool unless explicitly authorized. All exports must respect data residency requirements per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R016	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Contract Spend Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor actual vendor spend against contracted terms to identify maverick spending (purchases outside of contracted agreements) and contract leakage (purchases at non-contracted rates). For container shipping, the system must track whether freight bookings use contracted rates from volume agreements with container lines versus expensive spot market rates, whether terminal handling charges match negotiated tariff schedules at ports like Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, and whether preferred vendors are being used as specified in procurement policies. The system must calculate contract utilization percentages and alert procurement when utilization is below 70% (indicating potential commitment shortfall and associated penalties) or above 95% (indicating need for contract extension or new procurement). Monthly compliance reports must be generated per entity showing total spend, contracted spend percentage, maverick spend value, and top deviations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R017	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Sustainability and ESG Spend Reporting	The system must track and report on spend categories related to environmental, social, and governance (ESG) objectives, which are increasingly important for container shipping operations in the GCC region aligned with Qatar National Vision 2030 and UAE Net Zero 2050. The system must categorize spend by ESG relevance including: green fuel and low-sulfur bunker purchases (IMO 2020 compliant), carbon offset program contributions, environmentally certified vendor spend, SME and local content spend percentages per entity, and women-owned business enterprise utilization. The AI engine must calculate the carbon cost embedded in shipping spend using emission factors per trade lane and vessel type. ESG reports must align with GRI Standards and ISSB sustainability disclosure requirements, generating data needed for the entity's annual sustainability report. The system must track progress against entity-defined ESG procurement targets with quarterly milestone reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-017-2-004-R018	FEAT-017-2-004	MOD-017	Offline Report Access and Cached Analytics	The system must support offline access to previously generated reports and cached analytics data for users who may be working at port locations with intermittent connectivity such as remote terminal areas at JNPT or during vessel inspections at Hamad Port anchorage. The offline capability must cache the most recent versions of configured dashboards and standard reports to the user's device, synchronized automatically when connectivity is restored. Cached data must include at minimum the last 3 months of spend analytics at the summary level and the last 30 days at the transaction detail level. Reports accessed offline must display a clear timestamp indicating when the data was last refreshed and a visual indicator that the user is viewing cached data. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any report bookmarks, annotations, or filter configurations made offline back to the server.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R003	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Automated Charge Calculation Engine	The system must provide an automated calculation engine that computes each line item of the proforma DA based on the vessel particulars and applicable tariff schedule. Calculations must handle different computation methods: GRT-based charges with sliding scale brackets (e.g., first 5000 GRT at one rate, excess at another), time-based charges for berth hire calculated in 6-hour or 24-hour increments depending on port rules, per-TEU charges differentiated by laden versus empty containers and 20ft versus 40ft units, and flat-rate charges for services like pilotage. The engine must apply port-specific surcharges such as Jebel Ali infrastructure fee and JNPT vessel-related charges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R004	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Multi-Currency Proforma Conversion	The system must calculate proforma DA totals in the port's local currency (QAR, AED, INR) and simultaneously convert to the principal's preferred reporting currency (typically USD) using configurable exchange rates. The exchange rate source must be selectable between Central Bank published rates, company treasury rates, or manually entered rates. Each proforma line item must display both local currency and converted amounts. The system must support rounding rules per currency (QAR to 2 decimals, INR to nearest paisa, AED to 2 decimals) and clearly indicate the exchange rate date used for the conversion to ensure transparency in multi-entity consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R003	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Contract Rate vs Spot Rate Management	The system must differentiate between contract rates (agreed under annual or multi-year transport contracts with specific haulers) and spot rates (negotiated per-shipment for ad-hoc requirements). Contract rates must be linked to a transport service agreement with the hauler, referencing the agreement number, contracted volume commitments, and penalty clauses for volume shortfalls. Spot rates must be captured via a quotation workflow where multiple hauler quotes can be compared side-by-side with automatic ranking by cost, transit time, and hauler performance score. The system must default to the applicable contract rate when a matching contract exists and fall back to spot rate workflow when no contract covers the required route, container type, or date range. Rate application precedence rules must be configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R009	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Hauler Performance-Based Rate Adjustment	The system must support performance-based rate adjustments where hauler rates are dynamically modified based on measured service quality. Performance metrics must include on-time pickup percentage, on-time delivery percentage, cargo damage incident rate, equipment availability, and documentation accuracy. Each metric must have a configurable weight and threshold. Haulers exceeding performance targets may receive incentive rate reductions (e.g., 2% discount for >98% on-time delivery), while underperforming haulers may incur penalty surcharges as defined in the transport service agreement. Performance-based adjustments must be calculated automatically at the end of each review period (monthly or quarterly), generating adjustment credit/debit notes in the appropriate currency. The system must provide a hauler scorecard dashboard viewable in Arabic and English showing trend data over 12 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R009	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Ancillary and Miscellaneous Cost Estimation	The system must allow inclusion of estimated ancillary costs beyond standard port tariff items in the proforma DA. These must include launch hire, fresh water supply, garbage removal, crew-related charges (shore passes, immigration fees), cargo documentation fees, customs overtime charges, environmental compliance fees (ballast water treatment certificate verification), and any port-specific mandatory charges such as ISPS security charges at JNPT or Hamad Port security levy. Each ancillary item must be individually line-itemized with quantity, unit rate, and total. Users must be able to add custom line items not present in the tariff schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R010	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Arabic RTL Proforma DA Rendering	The system must generate proforma DA documents and on-screen displays in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts simultaneously. Arabic rendering must correctly handle right-to-left text flow for all charge descriptions, port names, vessel names transliterated to Arabic, and agent details. Numeric values and currency symbols must follow Arabic locale conventions with Arabic-Indic numerals as an option. Bilingual PDF output must be available with Arabic and English on the same document in a dual-column layout or as separate pages. All date fields must display both Gregorian and Hijri (Umm al-Qura) calendar dates throughout the proforma.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R011	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Voyage Cost Budgeting Integration	The system must integrate proforma DA estimates with the voyage cost budgeting module to provide a consolidated view of estimated port costs across all ports in a vessel's rotation. When a proforma DA is created or updated, the corresponding voyage budget must be automatically refreshed to reflect the latest port cost estimate. The system must support splitting DA costs between multiple cargo interests based on bill of lading allocation rules, differentiating between owner's costs (port dues, pilotage) and cargo-related costs (container handling, wharfage). Integration must support both real-time and batch synchronization modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R012	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma Revision and Version Control	The system must maintain full version history of all proforma DA revisions. Each modification to a proforma after initial creation must generate a new version with an incremental revision number (e.g., PDA-2024-001-R01, R02, R03). The system must track and display differences between versions highlighting added, removed, or modified line items and amount changes. Only the latest approved version may be transmitted to port agents. Previous versions must remain accessible for audit and reference purposes. The system must enforce that revisions to an already-transmitted proforma trigger a notification to the port agent indicating the superseding version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R013	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma DA Duplication and Bulk Operations	The system must support duplication of an existing proforma DA to create a new proforma for a similar port call, automatically adjusting dates, voyage reference, and recalculating charges based on any tariff changes between the original and new effective dates. Bulk proforma creation must be supported for multi-port voyage planning where a user can generate proformas for all ports in a rotation from a single voyage itinerary input. The bulk operation must process each port sequentially using the appropriate tariff schedule and currency, generating individual proforma documents while maintaining a consolidated voyage-level summary view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R014	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Offline Proforma DA Capability	The system must support offline creation and editing of proforma DAs for users operating in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, such as onboard vessel or remote port locations. The offline mode must cache the latest port tariff schedules, vessel particulars database, exchange rates, and proforma templates locally. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline-created proformas with the central server using conflict resolution logic that prioritizes server-side tariff updates while preserving user-entered custom line items. The offline mode must clearly indicate the staleness of cached data with last-sync timestamps and warn users if tariff data is older than 7 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R015	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma DA Reporting and Analytics	The system must provide a dashboard and reporting suite for proforma DA analytics including total estimated port costs by port, by vessel class, by trade lane, and by entity over configurable date ranges. Reports must support filtering by currency with cross-currency aggregation at a specified reporting rate. Trend analysis must show month-over-month and year-over-year changes in estimated port costs per TEU handled. The reporting engine must export data in PDF (bilingual Arabic/English), Excel (with separate currency columns), and CSV formats. All reports must support entity-level segregation for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations with a consolidated group-level rollup option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R016	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Tax and Regulatory Compliance in Proforma	The system must correctly apply all applicable taxes and regulatory levies in proforma DA calculations based on the port jurisdiction. For Indian ports (JNPT), the system must calculate GST at the applicable rate (currently 18%) on eligible services with proper HSN/SAC code mapping for each charge type. For UAE ports (Jebel Ali, Khalifa), standard VAT at 5% must be applied where applicable. Qatar operations must reflect the current zero-VAT regime but support future tax implementation. KSA ports must apply 15% VAT on applicable services. The system must track tax registration numbers (GSTIN, TRN, VAT ID) for each entity and include them on proforma documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-001-R017	FEAT-018-1-001	MOD-018	Proforma DA Access Control and Entity Segregation	The system must enforce strict entity-level data segregation ensuring users from the Qatar entity can only view and create proformas for Qatar port calls, UAE entity users for UAE ports, and similarly for KSA and India entities, unless granted explicit cross-entity access by a super administrator. Role-based access control must define permissions for proforma creation, editing, approval, transmission, and deletion separately. The system must log all access attempts including denied access for security audit purposes. Multi-entity users must select their active entity context before performing any proforma operations and the selected entity must be prominently displayed in the UI header.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R001	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Final DA Receipt and Registration	The system must allow registration of final disbursement accounts received from port agents by matching them to existing proforma DAs using the proforma reference number, vessel IMO, voyage number, or port call reference. The registration process must capture the agent's DA reference number, DA date, received date, total amount in original currency, and attach the agent's supporting documentation (scanned invoices, receipts, port authority bills). The system must support multiple input methods: manual entry, structured data import from Excel/CSV, OCR-assisted extraction from scanned DA documents, and API-based receipt from integrated port agent systems. Each final DA must be tagged with the receiving entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R002	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Line-Item Variance Analysis	The system must perform automatic line-by-line comparison between the proforma DA estimate and the final DA received from the port agent. For each charge category, the system must calculate the absolute variance (in local currency and USD equivalent) and percentage variance. Variances must be color-coded: green for within acceptable tolerance (configurable, default plus or minus 5%), amber for moderate variance (5-15%), and red for significant variance exceeding 15%. The system must generate an exception report listing all red-flagged items requiring investigation. Each variance line must allow the user to record an explanation code (tariff change, additional service, calculation error, exchange rate difference, agent surcharge) and free-text notes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R003	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Multi-Level Reconciliation Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for final DA reconciliation based on total variance amount and percentage thresholds. If total variance is within the entity's auto-approval threshold (configurable, default plus or minus 3% and under USD 500), the system may auto-approve with logging. Variances between 3-10% must require operations manager approval. Variances exceeding 10% or USD 5,000 must require finance controller approval. Variances exceeding USD 25,000 must escalate to entity CFO. Each approval level must capture approver comments, conditional approvals with action items, and timestamps. The workflow must support delegation and out-of-office routing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R004	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Disputed Charge Management	The system must support flagging individual line items in a final DA as disputed when the reconciling user disagrees with the agent's charge. Disputed items must be tracked in a dedicated dispute register with fields for dispute reason (overcharge, unauthorized service, incorrect calculation, duplicate charge, inapplicable tariff), disputed amount, supporting evidence attachments, and communication log with the port agent. The dispute workflow must support multiple resolution statuses: open, under negotiation, credit note requested, credit note received, written off, and escalated. The system must calculate the undisputed portion of the DA separately to allow partial payment processing while disputes are ongoing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R005	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Exchange Rate Reconciliation	The system must reconcile exchange rate differences between the proforma DA (estimated at proforma creation rate) and the final DA (which may use the agent's applied rate or the rate at date of actual port call). The system must calculate exchange rate variance separately from service charge variance to provide clear visibility into cost drivers. Users must be able to specify the applicable exchange rate for final DA booking from multiple sources: agent's rate, Central Bank rate on port call date, company treasury rate, or contractual rate with the agent. Exchange gains or losses must be separately identified and posted to the appropriate foreign exchange gain/loss account per entity accounting policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R006	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Credit Note and Debit Note Processing	The system must support issuance and receipt of credit notes and debit notes related to final DA reconciliation. When a dispute is resolved in the principal's favor, the system must track the expected credit note from the agent, match it upon receipt, and adjust the DA balance accordingly. The system must generate debit notes for additional charges identified during reconciliation. Each credit/debit note must reference the original DA number, specific line items affected, adjustment amounts in original and reporting currencies, and reason codes. The system must prevent final closure of a DA until all pending credit/debit notes are resolved or explicitly waived by an authorized approver.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R007	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	AI-Powered Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to automatically flag unusual charges in final DAs by comparing against historical patterns for the same port, vessel class, and cargo type. The AI model must learn from at least 24 months of historical DA data to establish baselines for each charge category at each port. Anomalies must be scored on a confidence scale (0-100) and those exceeding a configurable threshold (default 75) must be presented to the reconciler as suggested investigation items. The system must detect patterns such as consistently inflated charges from specific agents, sudden tariff increases not matching published schedules, and duplicate charges across consecutive port calls at the same port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R008	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Batch Reconciliation Processing	The system must support batch reconciliation of multiple final DAs simultaneously for efficiency during high-volume periods. The batch process must allow import of multiple final DAs from a structured file (Excel template or CSV), automatically match each to its corresponding proforma, perform variance calculations, and present a consolidated exception dashboard. Batch processing must handle mixed-currency DAs across different entities, correctly segregating QAR-denominated DAs to the Qatar entity, AED to UAE, SAR to KSA, and INR to India. The system must provide a batch summary report showing total DAs processed, auto-matched count, exception count, and aggregate variance statistics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R009	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Final DA Document Management	The system must provide comprehensive document management for all supporting documentation related to final DAs. Each final DA record must support attachment of port authority invoices, agent fee breakdowns, third-party service receipts, overtime authorization forms, and any correspondence related to the port call. Documents must be stored with metadata including document type, upload date, uploader identity, and file size. The system must support PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, and Excel file formats with a configurable maximum file size (default 25 MB per file). Full-text search across attached documents must be available using OCR indexing for scanned documents in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R010	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Reconciliation SLA Tracking	The system must track reconciliation timelines against configurable service level agreements (SLAs). The SLA clock must start when the final DA is received from the port agent and end when the DA is fully approved and ready for payment. Default SLA targets must be configurable per entity: 5 business days for DAs under USD 10,000, 10 business days for DAs between USD 10,000 and USD 50,000, and 15 business days for DAs exceeding USD 50,000. The system must provide SLA compliance dashboards showing current aging of unreconciled DAs, breach alerts sent via email and in-app notification, and monthly SLA compliance percentages by entity, port, and agent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R011	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Accounting Integration for Approved DAs	The system must generate accounting entries upon final DA approval for integration with the general ledger module. The accounting entry must debit the appropriate voyage cost or port expense account and credit the port agent payable account, with proper entity and cost center coding. Multi-currency DAs must generate entries in both the transaction currency and the entity's functional currency using the approved reconciliation exchange rate. Any exchange rate differences from the proforma accrual must generate adjustment entries. The system must support configurable chart of accounts mapping per entity to accommodate different accounting structures across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R012	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	DA Reconciliation Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail for every action taken during the reconciliation process. The audit log must capture: who performed the action (user ID, entity, role), what action was taken (status change, amount modification, line item addition/deletion, approval, rejection, dispute creation), when the action occurred (timestamp in UTC with local time display), and the before/after state of any modified data. The audit trail must be exportable in a format suitable for external audit review. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 7 years in compliance with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Commercial Transactions Law, and Indian Companies Act record retention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R013	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Partial DA Approval and Split Processing	The system must support partial approval of final DAs where undisputed line items can be approved for payment while disputed items remain under investigation. The partial approval must generate a payment request for the approved portion only, with clear tracking of the remaining balance under dispute. When disputed items are subsequently resolved (via credit note, write-off, or acceptance), the system must generate supplementary payment requests for the resolved amounts. The system must maintain a running balance view for each DA showing original total, approved amount, disputed amount, credit notes received, and net payable, all displayed in both local currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R014	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Reconciliation Dashboard and KPIs	The system must provide a real-time reconciliation dashboard displaying key performance indicators including: total DAs pending reconciliation by entity, average reconciliation turnaround time, total variance amount (proforma vs final) by port and agent, dispute rate as a percentage of total DA line items, SLA compliance rate, and top agents by variance. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary KPIs to individual DA records. Filters must include date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), entity, port, vessel, agent, and reconciliation status. The dashboard must refresh automatically and support role-based visibility restricting entity-specific data to authorized users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R015	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Agent Performance Scoring	The system must calculate and maintain a performance score for each port agent based on their final DA reconciliation history. Scoring criteria must include: average variance from proforma (weighted 30%), timeliness of final DA submission (weighted 25%), dispute frequency (weighted 20%), dispute resolution time (weighted 15%), and documentation completeness (weighted 10%). Scores must be calculated on a rolling 12-month basis and presented on a 0-100 scale. The system must generate quarterly agent performance reports for procurement and operations review. Agents scoring below a configurable threshold (default 60) must trigger an automatic alert to the entity's operations manager for review of the agency appointment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R016	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Hijri Calendar Reconciliation Periods	The system must support reconciliation period management using both Gregorian and Hijri (Umm al-Qura) calendars. For KSA operations, reconciliation periods and SLA calculations must align with Hijri months where required by local regulations. The system must correctly handle Hijri month boundaries when calculating aging and SLA compliance, accounting for variable month lengths in the Hijri calendar. Reconciliation reports must display dates in both calendar systems side by side. The system must account for Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Saudi National Day, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Indian public holidays) when calculating business days for SLA purposes across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R017	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Offline Reconciliation Review Capability	The system must support offline review and preliminary reconciliation of final DAs for finance users working without reliable internet connectivity. The offline mode must download pending final DAs with their matched proformas, supporting documents, and historical benchmarks for local review. Users must be able to annotate variance items, add preliminary approval or rejection notes, and flag disputed items while offline. Upon reconnection, all offline annotations and decisions must synchronize with the central system, entering the standard approval workflow from the point of the user's offline decision. The system must prevent conflicting edits by locking DA records assigned to offline reviewers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-002-R018	FEAT-018-1-002	MOD-018	Regulatory Withholding Tax Handling	The system must correctly apply withholding tax requirements during final DA reconciliation based on the jurisdiction and nature of charges. For India operations, TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) must be calculated on applicable agent commission and service charges at the prescribed rates under Indian Income Tax Act Section 194C (currently 1-2% depending on payee type). The system must generate TDS certificates (Form 16A) for Indian agents. For KSA, withholding tax at 5% on service fees to non-resident agents must be calculated per ZATCA regulations. Qatar and UAE operations must apply withholding tax rules where applicable. The system must track withholding tax liability separately and integrate with tax filing modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R001	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Master Data Structure	The system must maintain a hierarchical port cost category master with at least three levels of classification. Level 1 must include primary categories: port authority charges, pilotage and towage, berth and anchorage, cargo handling, agency fees, regulatory and compliance, and miscellaneous. Level 2 must break down each primary category into specific charge types (e.g., under cargo handling: container lift-on/lift-off, reefer monitoring, hazardous cargo surcharge, over-dimensional cargo handling). Level 3 must capture granular sub-types where applicable. Each cost category must be mappable to the entity's chart of accounts and support bilingual Arabic/English descriptions. The master must be entity-specific with a global template option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R002	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Real-Time Port Cost Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying current port cost status across all active vessel operations. The dashboard must show: total port costs incurred month-to-date and year-to-date by entity, costs broken down by port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), costs per TEU handled at each port, top 10 vessels by port expenditure, and budget versus actual variance indicators. All monetary values must be displayable in the user's preferred currency with live conversion. The dashboard must support interactive drill-down from summary tiles to individual cost records. Auto-refresh interval must be configurable with a minimum of 5-minute intervals and must indicate the last refresh timestamp prominently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R003	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Budget vs Actual Cost Tracking	The system must support creation of annual port cost budgets at multiple granularity levels: by entity, by port, by vessel class, and by cost category. Budget amounts must be entered in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD conversion for consolidated reporting. The system must track actual costs against budget in real-time, displaying committed (proforma DA), accrued (final DA pending approval), and confirmed (approved DA) amounts separately. Variance reporting must show both absolute and percentage deviations with configurable alert thresholds. Monthly budget phasing must be supported to account for seasonal port call patterns, with the ability to redistribute unused budget across remaining months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R004	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Cost Allocation to Cargo and Voyages	The system must support flexible allocation of port costs to individual voyages, cargo bookings, and bills of lading. Allocation methods must include: direct assignment (specific charges to specific cargo), pro-rata by TEU count, pro-rata by cargo weight, pro-rata by cargo revenue, and custom allocation percentages. The system must handle split costs where a single port call serves multiple services or trade lanes, allocating shared costs (pilotage, port dues) proportionally while assigning direct costs (container handling) to the specific cargo. For transshipment cargo at Jebel Ali or JNPT, the system must correctly allocate costs between the inbound and outbound voyage legs based on configurable transshipment cost-sharing rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R005	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Accrual Management	The system must manage port cost accruals for financial reporting purposes, creating accrual entries when a vessel completes a port call based on the proforma DA estimate and reversing accruals when the final DA is approved and booked. The accrual process must run automatically at each month-end closing, identifying all port calls completed in the period without finalized DAs and creating accrual entries at the latest proforma estimate. The system must track the aging of accruals and escalate items where the proforma-to-final cycle exceeds entity-specific thresholds (default 30 days). Accrual amounts must be calculated in both local and reporting currencies using month-end exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R006	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Trend Analysis	The system must provide trend analysis capabilities showing port cost movements over configurable periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) with the ability to analyze trends by specific cost category, port, vessel class, and entity. The analysis must include year-over-year comparison charts, rolling 12-month averages, and seasonality detection showing cost patterns aligned with peak shipping seasons in the Arabian Gulf and Indian subcontinent. Trend reports must identify statistically significant cost increases or decreases using a configurable significance threshold (default 2 standard deviations from the rolling mean). AI-assisted narrative summaries must be generated explaining the key drivers behind observed cost trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R007	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Multi-Entity Cost Consolidation	The system must provide consolidated port cost reporting across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with proper elimination of inter-entity charges. Consolidation must convert all entity-level costs to a common reporting currency (USD) using configurable exchange rates (month-end spot, period average, or weighted average). The consolidated view must clearly identify the contributing entity for each cost item and support drill-down to entity-level detail. Inter-entity port cost recharges (e.g., when the UAE entity's vessel calls at a Qatar port managed by the Qatar entity) must be identified and eliminated in the consolidated view to prevent double-counting while maintaining full visibility in entity-level reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R008	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Benchmarking Across Ports	The system must provide a port cost benchmarking tool that compares costs across different ports for similar operations to support port selection and negotiation decisions. The benchmarking must normalize costs to a per-TEU or per-GRT basis to enable fair comparison between ports of different sizes and throughput. Comparisons must be available between Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, as well as against configurable external benchmark data. The system must display benchmarking results in interactive charts with the ability to filter by vessel class, cargo type, and time period. Currency effects must be isolated from operational cost differences in the benchmarking analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R009	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Tariff Change Impact Analysis	The system must provide an impact analysis tool that calculates the projected cost effect of announced or anticipated port tariff changes across the fleet's port call schedule. When a new tariff schedule is entered for any port, the system must calculate the incremental cost impact by applying the new tariffs to the planned port calls for the next 6 and 12 months, comparing against the current tariff projection. The analysis must be broken down by vessel, voyage, cost category, and entity. The system must generate a management summary report showing total annual cost impact in each entity's local currency and USD, enabling proactive budget adjustments and commercial negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R010	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Cost Exception and Outlier Detection	The system must implement automated exception detection rules that flag port cost entries exceeding predefined thresholds. Rules must be configurable at multiple levels: global, entity-specific, port-specific, and cost-category-specific. Detection criteria must include absolute amount thresholds (e.g., any single line item exceeding USD 10,000), relative thresholds compared to historical averages (e.g., exceeding the 6-month average by more than 25%), and pattern-based rules (e.g., same charge appearing more than once for a single port call). Flagged exceptions must generate notifications to the designated cost controller and must require explicit acknowledgment with explanation before the cost can proceed through the approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R011	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Forecasting Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered cost forecasting engine that predicts future port costs based on historical patterns, known tariff schedules, planned vessel deployments, and seasonal factors. The forecast must project costs at entity, port, and fleet levels for periods of 3, 6, and 12 months ahead. The forecasting model must incorporate variables including vessel call frequency, average cargo volumes, currency exchange rate trends, announced tariff changes, and inflation indices for each operating country. Forecast accuracy must be tracked by comparing predictions against actual costs, with the model self-adjusting based on prediction errors. Forecast results must be exportable for integration with corporate financial planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R012	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Approval and Authorization Matrix	The system must enforce a cost authorization matrix that defines spending limits and approval requirements for port-related expenditure by role, entity, and cost category. The matrix must support multiple authorization levels: auto-approved (routine charges within tariff), single approval (standard charges within budget), dual approval (charges exceeding budget or non-standard items), and executive approval (exceptional or high-value items). Authorization limits must be denominated in each entity's local currency with USD equivalent thresholds for cross-entity consistency. The system must prevent booking of any port cost that has not passed through the appropriate authorization level and must log all authorization decisions for compliance audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R013	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Reefer Container Port Cost Tracking	The system must provide specialized tracking for reefer (refrigerated) container-related port costs, which are typically higher and more variable than standard container costs. Reefer-specific charges must include power connection and monitoring fees calculated on an hourly or daily basis, pre-trip inspection charges, temperature deviation handling costs, and genset fuel charges where shore power is unavailable. The system must track reefer plug-in and plug-out times to verify billed durations against actual usage. For ports with limited reefer capacity (relevant at certain berths in Hamad Port and JNPT), the system must track waiting time surcharges. Reefer costs must be allocable to specific booking references and reefer container numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R014	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Hazardous Cargo Port Cost Surcharges	The system must track and manage additional port costs associated with hazardous cargo handling classified under IMO dangerous goods classes 1 through 9. Surcharges must be linked to specific container numbers and their declared IMDG class, UN number, and packing group. The system must apply port-specific hazardous cargo surcharge schedules which vary significantly between ports (Jebel Ali has specific DG zone charges, JNPT applies DG storage surcharges, Hamad Port applies flat-rate DG handling premiums). The system must validate that declared hazardous cargo surcharges match the manifest-declared DG containers and flag discrepancies. Cost records must maintain full traceability to the dangerous goods manifest for regulatory compliance and incident investigation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R015	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Data Export and Integration	The system must support export of port cost data in multiple formats and through multiple channels for integration with external systems. Supported export formats must include structured Excel workbooks with predefined templates matching each entity's reporting requirements, CSV files with configurable delimiters and encoding (UTF-8 for Arabic character support), JSON for API consumers, and XML for EDI integration. The system must support scheduled automatic exports (daily, weekly, monthly) to designated file shares or SFTP endpoints for each entity. Real-time API endpoints must expose cost data following RESTful conventions with OAuth 2.0 authentication, supporting filtering by entity, port, vessel, voyage, date range, and cost category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R016	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Currency Hedging Support	The system must integrate with treasury operations to support currency hedging for anticipated port costs. When proforma DAs are created in foreign currencies relative to the entity's functional currency, the system must feed projected foreign currency exposures to the treasury module for hedging decisions. The system must track the hedged rate versus the actual settlement rate for each DA, calculating hedge effectiveness. For Indian operations where INR volatility impacts USD-denominated cost reporting, the system must support forward contract rate application to projected JNPT costs. The system must generate monthly currency exposure reports showing net unhedged port cost exposure by currency pair across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-003-R017	FEAT-018-1-003	MOD-018	Port Cost Audit and Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive audit-ready reports for port cost compliance reviews by internal audit, external auditors, and regulatory authorities. Reports must include a complete cost trail from proforma estimate through final DA approval to payment, with all intermediate reconciliation steps documented. The system must support generation of VAT/GST compliance reports per entity jurisdiction: UAE VAT returns showing input tax on port costs, India GST returns with ITC (Input Tax Credit) details for JNPT costs, and KSA VAT reports per ZATCA requirements. Audit reports must be available in both English and Arabic, include digital signatures of approvers, and support extraction for specific date ranges, entities, and cost categories with full supporting documentation bundles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R001	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Port Agent Master Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive port agent master registry containing all active and historical port agents used across the four operating entities. Each agent record must include: company name (English and Arabic), registration number, tax identification numbers (GSTIN for India, TRN for UAE, VAT ID for KSA), bank details for multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR accounts), contact persons with roles, communication preferences, contracted commission rates, service level agreements, port coverage (which ports each agent serves), and appointment validity dates. The registry must support agent status management (active, suspended, terminated) and maintain a complete history of all changes to agent records with timestamps and change reasons.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R002	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent Statement of Account Ledger	The system must maintain a running statement of account ledger for each port agent in their primary transaction currency with parallel tracking in the entity's reporting currency. The ledger must record all financial transactions including: advance payments (funds on account), proforma DA accruals, final DA bookings, credit notes, debit notes, dispute adjustments, write-offs, and settlement payments. Each transaction must be cross-referenced to the source document (DA number, payment reference, credit note number). The ledger must calculate and display running balances after each transaction. Month-end and year-end balances must be snapshotted for period-over-period reconciliation and must be immutable once the accounting period is closed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R003	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Advance Payment (Funds on Account) Management	The system must manage advance payments made to port agents as funds on account for anticipated vessel port calls. The advance request workflow must capture the requesting entity, purpose (specific vessel/voyage or general operating fund), requested amount and currency, beneficiary bank details (pulled from agent master), and expected utilization period. Advance payments must be tracked in the agent's SOA as a debit balance reducing the agent's net payable. The system must automatically offset advances against approved final DAs on a FIFO basis or allow manual allocation. Aging of unallocated advances must be monitored with alerts when advances remain unallocated beyond a configurable threshold (default 60 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R004	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Periodic Statement Generation and Distribution	The system must generate periodic statements of account for each port agent at configurable intervals (monthly, quarterly, or on-demand). The statement must list all transactions within the period in chronological order, showing opening balance, itemized transactions with dates and references, and closing balance. Statements must be generated in the agent's primary currency with a supplementary section showing entity reporting currency equivalents. The statement format must support bilingual Arabic/English rendering for Middle East agents and English for Indian agents. Generated statements must be automatically distributed to agents via email with PDF attachment or made available through a secure agent portal. The system must track statement delivery and agent acknowledgment status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R005	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent Balance Confirmation Process	The system must support a formal balance confirmation process where the agent is requested to confirm or dispute the outstanding balance as of a specific date. The confirmation request must be generated as a formal document including the balance breakdown by currency, list of open items, and a response form for the agent to sign and return. The system must track confirmation requests sent, responses received, confirmed balances, and disputed amounts. Disputed items must be escalated to the reconciliation workflow for investigation. The balance confirmation process must be schedulable to run automatically at fiscal year-end for all agents or ad-hoc for specific agents. Response deadlines must be tracked with automated reminder notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R006	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Multi-Currency Agent Account Reconciliation	The system must support reconciliation of agent accounts across multiple currencies where a single agent may handle port calls billed in different currencies (e.g., a UAE agent handling both AED-denominated Khalifa Port DAs and USD-denominated charges). The reconciliation must maintain separate sub-ledgers by currency with independent balances, while providing a consolidated view in a common reporting currency. Exchange rate differences arising from timing of advances (sent at one rate) versus DA settlement (recorded at another rate) must be clearly identified and reconciled. The system must support netting of cross-currency balances where the entity and agent agree to settle net positions, calculating the netting amount at agreed exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R007	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent Commission Tracking and Calculation	The system must calculate and track port agent commissions based on contracted commission structures stored in the agent master. Commission structures must support multiple calculation methods: percentage of DA total (excluding port authority charges), flat fee per port call, percentage of specific charge categories only, tiered rates based on volume of port calls per period, and hybrid structures combining fixed and variable components. The system must calculate commissions automatically when final DAs are approved, creating a commission payable entry in the agent's SOA. Commission amounts must be clearly separated from DA cost reimbursements in the agent ledger. The system must track commission accruals for financial reporting and support commission rate renegotiation with effective date management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R008	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent Account Aging Analysis	The system must provide aging analysis of outstanding balances with each port agent, categorizing open items into configurable aging buckets (default: current, 1-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, over 90 days). Aging must be calculated based on the transaction date or due date of each open item. The aging report must be available by entity, by agent, and consolidated across all entities. The system must distinguish between amounts owed to agents (DA settlements pending) and amounts owed by agents (unallocated advances, credit notes receivable). Aging data must drive automated collection or payment priority actions: items aged beyond 90 days must trigger escalation notifications, and items beyond the entity's provisioning threshold must feed into the bad debt provisioning calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R009	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Payment Instruction Generation	The system must generate payment instructions for approved agent payables based on the reconciled SOA balance. Payment instructions must include all details required for bank processing: beneficiary name, bank name and branch, SWIFT/BIC code, IBAN (for GCC agents), IFSC code (for Indian agents), account number, payment currency, amount, and payment reference. The system must support generation of payment batches grouping multiple agent payments for the same entity and currency. Payment files must be exportable in bank-specific formats (SWIFT MT101, local payment file formats for QNB, Emirates NBD, ADCB, SBI, and other supported banks). The system must prevent duplicate payments by checking against recently generated payment instructions for the same agent and DA references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R010	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Dispute Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a dedicated dispute resolution module within the agent SOA framework for managing disagreements over specific transactions. When the agent's confirmed balance differs from the system balance, the variance must be itemized and each disputed item entered into the dispute register with: disputed amount, entity's position, agent's position, supporting documentation from both sides, and assigned resolver. The dispute workflow must support statuses: open, under investigation, proposed resolution, resolution accepted by agent, resolution accepted by entity, and closed. Resolved disputes must automatically generate the appropriate adjusting entries (credit notes, debit notes, or write-offs) in the agent's SOA ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R001	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Voyage-Level Cost Allocation Engine	The system must provide a voyage-level cost allocation engine that distributes all port disbursement expenses across individual voyage legs. Allocation must support configurable keys including TEU count, cargo weight (MT), revenue proportion, container count by type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer), and time-in-port duration. The engine must handle multi-leg voyages spanning Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNHV). Each allocation run must produce an auditable trail showing the allocation method, input parameters, and resulting cost distribution per voyage leg with amounts in the voyage base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R011	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Inter-Entity Agent Account Handling	The system must handle scenarios where a port agent serves multiple entities within the group and maintain proper segregation of accounts per entity while supporting a consolidated group view. When the same agent (e.g., a Jebel Ali agent serving both UAE and Qatar entities' vessels), the system must maintain separate SOA ledgers for each entity's transactions with the agent. The consolidated agent view must show the total exposure across all entities with clear entity breakdown. Inter-entity settlements must be supported where one entity pays the agent on behalf of another, creating appropriate inter-company receivable/payable entries. The system must prevent unauthorized cross-entity access to agent SOA details while allowing designated group finance users to view consolidated positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R012	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Arabic Bilingual Statements	The system must generate all agent statements of account, balance confirmations, and payment advices in bilingual Arabic/English format for agents operating in Arabic-speaking jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA). Arabic content must follow proper RTL text rendering with correct number formatting. Transaction descriptions must be available in both languages with Arabic descriptions sourced from the bilingual cost category master. The Arabic statement layout must comply with local commercial practice conventions and include entity details in Arabic (company name, commercial registration, address). For Indian agents, statements must be generated in English only. The system must allow agents to specify their preferred language for communications, defaulting to bilingual for GCC and English for India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R013	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent Contract and SLA Monitoring	The system must track port agent contractual obligations and service level agreements, integrating this data with the SOA management. Monitored SLAs must include: final DA submission timeliness (within X days of vessel departure), documentation completeness (all required supporting docs attached), response time to queries (within X business hours), balance confirmation turnaround (within X days of request), and advance fund utilization reporting frequency. The system must calculate SLA compliance scores monthly for each agent across all contracted SLA metrics. Non-compliance instances must be logged and must be cross-referenced with the agent performance scoring feature. SLA compliance history must be retained for the duration of the agent contract plus a configurable archive period for dispute resolution and contract renewal decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R014	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Year-End Processing	The system must support fiscal year-end processing for all agent statements of account, including balance carry-forward, provision calculation, and statutory reporting. Year-end balances must be frozen and carried forward as opening balances for the new fiscal year. The system must calculate provisions for doubtful advances based on aging analysis and entity-specific provisioning policies. Year-end confirmation letters must be auto-generated for all agents with outstanding balances. The system must support different fiscal year-ends across entities (India: March 31, GCC entities: December 31 or as per company policy) and handle the complexity of year-end processing running at different times for different entities while maintaining consistent inter-entity balances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R015	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Reporting Suite	The system must provide a comprehensive reporting suite for agent SOA management including: agent-wise outstanding summary (current and aging), entity-wise agent exposure report, advance utilization report showing allocated versus unallocated advances, commission summary report by agent and period, settlement history report showing payment trends, and currency exposure report showing net positions by currency. All reports must support filtering by entity, agent, port, currency, date range, and balance type (receivable/payable). Reports must be exportable in PDF (with bilingual Arabic/English support), Excel (with proper Arabic text encoding), and CSV formats. The reporting suite must include a scheduled report feature allowing users to configure automatic generation and email distribution of standard reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R016	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Integration with Accounts Payable	The system must integrate the agent SOA ledger with the accounts payable module to ensure consistency between the sub-ledger (agent SOA) and the general ledger (AP control account). Every transaction posted to the agent SOA must generate a corresponding AP entry with proper entity coding, cost center allocation, and currency conversion. The system must provide a reconciliation report comparing agent SOA balances with AP ledger balances, identifying and flagging any discrepancies. The integration must support both real-time posting (each transaction immediately reflected in AP) and batch posting (accumulated transactions posted at end-of-day or end-of-period). The system must enforce that no manual AP entries are created for port agent payables outside the SOA module to maintain ledger integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R017	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Offline Access and Synchronization	The system must provide offline access to agent SOA data for finance users who need to review agent balances and transaction history while traveling or in areas with limited connectivity. The offline mode must cache selected agent SOA ledgers with full transaction history for a configurable period (default last 6 months). Users must be able to generate provisional statements, review aging, and draft dispute notes while offline. The offline cache must include related documents such as DA summaries and payment references. Upon reconnection, drafted items must synchronize with the server and enter the standard workflow. The system must display clear visual indicators when operating in offline mode and show the timestamp of the last successful synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-1-004-R018	FEAT-018-1-004	MOD-018	Agent SOA Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain a comprehensive and tamper-proof audit trail for all agent SOA transactions and balance modifications. The audit trail must capture the complete lifecycle of each transaction: creation, modifications (with before/after values), approvals, reversals, and archival. All audit records must include user identification, timestamp in UTC, originating entity, IP address, and action type. The audit trail must be queryable for compliance investigations and exportable for external auditor review. Retention of audit records must comply with the most stringent applicable regulation across operating jurisdictions: 7 years under Indian Companies Act, 5 years under UAE Commercial Companies Law, and 10 years for Qatar Financial Centre regulated entities. The system must support legal hold capabilities to preserve audit trails beyond standard retention when litigation is pending.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R003	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Multi-Currency Expense Consolidation	The system must consolidate port expenses denominated in multiple currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) into a single voyage currency for allocation purposes. Exchange rates must be sourced from the treasury module using either the booking date rate, port call date rate, or invoice date rate based on entity-level configuration. Realized and unrealized exchange differences arising from rate fluctuations between PDA estimation and FDA settlement must be tracked separately. The system must support triangulated currency conversion for non-direct pairs and maintain full currency conversion audit trails compliant with IFRS 21 / IAS 21 foreign currency translation requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R004	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Allocation Method Configuration by Port	The system must allow configuration of different allocation methods per port and per expense category. For example, pilotage at Jebel Ali (AEJEA) may be allocated by vessel GRT while wharfage at JNPT (INNHV) may be allocated by cargo tonnage. Configuration must support at least six allocation bases: TEU equivalent, cargo weight, vessel GRT/NRT, revenue share, equal distribution, and time-based proration. Each port-expense combination must have a default allocation method with the ability to override at voyage level. Configuration changes must be version-controlled with effective dates and must not retroactively alter previously completed allocations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R005	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Shared Voyage Cost Splitting Between Entities	The system must handle cost splitting for shared voyages where multiple group entities (Qatar entity, UAE entity, KSA entity, India entity) share vessel space or slot charter arrangements. Inter-company cost allocation must follow predefined sharing agreements specifying percentage splits or TEU-based proportional sharing. The system must automatically generate inter-company debit and credit entries when port expenses paid by one entity are partially allocated to another entity's cargo. Transfer pricing rules must be respected, and all inter-company allocations must produce documentation suitable for tax authority review in each jurisdiction including Qatar Tax Authority, UAE FTA, ZATCA (KSA), and Indian GST requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R006	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Cargo-Type Weighted Allocation Factors	The system must support weighted allocation factors based on cargo type classifications. Hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9) must carry higher allocation weights reflecting additional port handling costs, segregation requirements, and regulatory surcharges. Overweight and over-dimensional cargo must receive proportionally higher cost allocation for special handling equipment usage. Reefer cargo must include power supply and temperature monitoring cost components. The weighting factors must be configurable per port and must reflect actual cost differentials observed at Middle East and Indian subcontinent ports. Default weight tables must be provided with the ability to customize per shipping line agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R007	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Retroactive Allocation Adjustment Processing	The system must support retroactive adjustment of voyage cost allocations when late-arriving invoices or corrected disbursement accounts are received after initial allocation completion. Adjustment processing must recalculate affected container-level and B/L-level cost attributions without disturbing already-invoiced or already-settled customer accounts. The system must generate adjustment journal entries that clearly reference the original allocation and the reason for adjustment. A configurable tolerance threshold (percentage and absolute amount per currency) must determine whether adjustments trigger automatic reprocessing or require manual approval. All retroactive adjustments must maintain complete audit trail with timestamps and user identification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R008	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Voyage Leg Proration for Canal Transit Costs	The system must prorate canal transit costs (Suez Canal, Strait of Hormuz navigation aids) across voyage legs based on configurable methods. Suez Canal transit tolls must be allocated based on either laden TEU count at time of transit, proportional distance of pre-transit and post-transit legs, or cargo value proportion for high-value shipments. The proration engine must handle split transits where a vessel enters the canal with cargo destined for multiple discharge ports across different voyage legs. Special handling must be provided for ballast legs where canal costs are allocated entirely to the immediately preceding or following laden leg as per company policy configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R009	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Bill of Lading Level Cost Reporting	The system must produce cost reports at the Bill of Lading (B/L) level showing all allocated port disbursement expenses attributable to each B/L. The report must break down costs by expense category (pilotage, towage, berth hire, wharfage, stevedoring, agency fees, documentation charges) for each port of loading and discharge referenced on the B/L. For through B/Ls covering multiple port pairs, costs must be accumulated across all relevant port calls. The B/L cost report must support export to PDF and Excel formats with both English and Arabic headers. Currency display must show both original currency and B/L currency equivalent amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R010	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Allocation Simulation and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a simulation mode allowing users to test different allocation methods and parameters before committing an allocation run. Users must be able to compare results of at least three different allocation scenarios side by side, viewing the impact on per-container costs and voyage profitability. Simulation must support changing allocation keys, weighting factors, and currency conversion rates independently. Simulation results must be exportable for management review and must include variance analysis showing the difference between each simulated scenario. Simulations must not create any accounting entries or affect live voyage records until explicitly promoted to production allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R011	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Automated Allocation Trigger on FDA Receipt	The system must automatically trigger cost reallocation when a Final Disbursement Account (FDA) is received and approved for any port call within a voyage. The AI engine must determine whether the FDA variance from the Proforma Disbursement Account (PDA) is significant enough to warrant reallocation based on configurable materiality thresholds set per entity and currency. If the variance exceeds the threshold, the system must queue a reallocation job, notify the voyage accountant, and provide a preview of the expected allocation changes before execution. If the variance is within tolerance, the system must auto-approve the FDA and adjust allocations without manual intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R012	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Allocation Lock and Period Close Integration	The system must integrate with the financial period close process to lock voyage cost allocations at period end. Once a financial period is closed, allocations for voyages completed within that period must be locked and any subsequent adjustments must be posted to the current open period as prior-period adjustments. The locking mechanism must support partial voyage locking where a voyage spans multiple periods, locking only the port calls and costs applicable to the closed period. Integration with the general ledger must ensure that locked allocations match posted journal entries, and any discrepancies must be flagged for reconciliation before period close approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R013	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Arabic RTL Allocation Reports and UI	The system must render all allocation reports and user interfaces in Arabic right-to-left (RTL) layout when the user's language preference is set to Arabic. Port names, vessel names, and expense descriptions must display in Arabic with transliterated English equivalents where applicable. Numeric values, currency codes, and date fields must follow Arabic locale formatting conventions including Eastern Arabic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) as a configurable option. Hijri calendar dates must be displayed alongside Gregorian dates in all allocation reports. Table column ordering must mirror for RTL layout. PDF export must generate properly formatted RTL documents with Arabic typography supporting Naskh font family.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R014	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Offline Allocation Calculation Support	The system must support offline allocation calculations for scenarios where connectivity to the central ERP server is unavailable, particularly relevant for onboard vessel systems or remote port offices. Offline mode must cache the latest allocation configuration, exchange rates, and voyage data locally. Users must be able to run provisional allocation calculations offline with results stored locally and synchronized upon reconnection. Conflict resolution must handle cases where voyage data was updated centrally during the offline period, presenting discrepancies to the user for manual resolution. Offline allocations must be clearly marked as provisional until server synchronization and validation completes successfully.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R015	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Allocation Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every allocation run capturing the user who initiated it, timestamp, allocation method used, input data snapshot, exchange rates applied, and resulting distribution. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using hash chaining. Compliance reports must be generatable for tax authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India showing how port costs were allocated across entities and jurisdictions. The audit data must support drill-down from any allocated amount back to the source disbursement invoice and the original PDA or FDA document. Retention period for audit data must be configurable per jurisdiction with a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-001-R016	FEAT-018-2-001	MOD-018	Allocation Dashboard with Real-Time KPIs	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying key allocation metrics including total allocated costs per voyage, average cost per TEU by port, allocation completion status across active voyages, and pending FDA receipts requiring reallocation. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, vessel, trade lane, and date range. Visual indicators must highlight voyages with unusually high or low per-TEU costs compared to historical benchmarks. The dashboard must refresh automatically at configurable intervals and support drill-through to underlying allocation details. Both English and Arabic versions of the dashboard must be available with consistent data presentation across language settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R001	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Line-Item Variance Calculation Engine	The system must calculate variance between PDA and FDA at the individual line-item level for every expense category including pilotage, towage, berth hire, wharfage, stevedoring, launch hire, fresh water, garbage disposal, customs overtime, immigration fees, agency fees, and miscellaneous charges. Variance must be computed as absolute amount, percentage deviation, and currency-adjusted amount where the PDA and FDA are in different currencies. The engine must handle partial FDA submissions where only some line items have been finalized while others remain at PDA estimates, clearly distinguishing settled from estimated amounts in all variance displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R002	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Multi-Level Variance Aggregation	The system must aggregate variances at multiple hierarchical levels: line-item, port call, voyage leg, complete voyage, vessel, trade lane, and company-wide portfolio. Each aggregation level must show both absolute variance totals and weighted average percentage variances. Drill-down capability must allow users to navigate from portfolio-level summary down to individual line-item variances in no more than four clicks. Aggregation must respect entity boundaries, showing consolidated group views only to users with multi-entity access permissions. Cross-entity variance reporting must convert all amounts to a common reporting currency (configurable as USD, QAR, or AED) using period-end exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R003	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Threshold Alerting System	The system must support configurable variance threshold alerts at entity, port, and expense category levels. When an FDA is received and the variance from PDA exceeds the configured threshold (defined as both percentage and absolute amount), the system must automatically generate alerts to designated recipients. Alert channels must include in-app notifications, email, and integration with Microsoft Teams or Slack. Thresholds must be configurable separately for favorable (FDA < PDA) and unfavorable (FDA > PDA) variances. Critical thresholds exceeding a second tier must escalate to senior management. Alert suppression rules must prevent notification fatigue for known seasonal variations at specific ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R004	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	AI-Powered Variance Pattern Detection	The AI engine must analyze historical PDA vs FDA variance data to detect systematic patterns including consistent over-estimation by specific port agents, seasonal cost fluctuations at particular ports, vessel-size-dependent variance trends, and expense categories with chronic deviation. Pattern detection must operate across a rolling historical window of at least 24 months of data. Detected patterns must be presented with statistical confidence scores and must include recommended corrective actions such as adjusting PDA estimation factors for specific agents or ports. The AI must distinguish between random variance noise and statistically significant systematic bias using appropriate statistical tests with p-value thresholds configurable by the user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R005	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Agent Performance Variance Scorecard	The system must generate port agent performance scorecards based on PDA vs FDA variance history. Each agent must be scored on estimation accuracy (average absolute percentage variance), consistency (standard deviation of variances), timeliness (average days between port departure and FDA submission), and completeness (percentage of line items accurately estimated). Scorecards must cover configurable periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) and must support comparison across agents serving the same port. The scorecard must highlight agents whose performance has degraded over time versus those showing improvement. Scorecards must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual English/Arabic headers for sharing with agent management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R006	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Impact on Voyage P&L Calculation	The system must calculate and display the impact of PDA-to-FDA variances on voyage profit and loss statements. When an FDA replaces a PDA estimate, the voyage P&L must be automatically recalculated showing the before-and-after impact. Cumulative variance impact across all port calls within a voyage must be summarized with clear indication of whether the voyage profitability improved or deteriorated. The system must track running estimates of voyage profitability as FDAs arrive progressively, allowing operations managers to see real-time convergence of estimated versus actual voyage results. Materiality flags must indicate variances that shift voyage profitability beyond acceptable margin bands.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R007	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Supplementary Disbursement Account Handling	The system must handle Supplementary Disbursement Accounts (SDA) that arrive after the initial FDA, representing additional charges not captured in the original FDA. SDAs must be tracked as separate variance layers, distinguishable from PDA-to-FDA variance. The system must support multiple SDAs per port call with sequential numbering and cumulative tracking. Variance analysis must show three-way comparison: PDA versus initial FDA versus cumulative FDA+SDA total. SDA items must be categorized as genuinely new charges versus corrections to existing FDA line items. Approval workflow for SDAs must include validation against port tariff schedules and historical norms before acceptance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R008	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Currency Variance Isolation	The system must isolate currency-related variance from operational variance when PDA and FDA involve different exchange rates or when port charges are denominated in a currency different from the PDA base currency. The variance decomposition must separate the total variance into three components: volume variance (quantity differences), rate variance (unit price differences), and currency variance (exchange rate differences). This three-way decomposition must be applied to each line item and aggregated consistently at higher levels. Currency variance must be further classified as economic (market rate movement) versus timing (rate date selection difference) for treasury reporting purposes compliant with IFRS 9 hedge accounting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R009	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Trend Visualization and Charting	The system must provide interactive charts and visualizations for variance trend analysis. Required chart types include time-series line charts showing variance trends by port over rolling 12-month periods, box plots showing variance distribution by expense category, heat maps showing variance severity across port-agent combinations, and waterfall charts decomposing total voyage variance into contributing port calls. All charts must support interactive filtering by entity, port, vessel class, and trade lane. Charts must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with properly mirrored axis labels. Chart data must be exportable to CSV for external analysis tools.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R010	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Root Cause Classification for Variances	The system must support classification of variances by root cause to enable structured analysis and corrective action tracking. Standard root cause categories must include: tariff change (port authority updated charges), volume change (actual cargo differed from estimated), service scope change (additional services required), agent estimation error, weather delay impact, regulatory surcharge, labor action impact, and exchange rate movement. Users must be able to assign root causes to individual variance line items or bulk-assign at the port call level. AI must suggest probable root causes based on historical patterns and contextual data such as known tariff changes or weather events during the port call period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R011	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	PDA Accuracy Improvement Recommendations	The AI engine must generate actionable recommendations for improving PDA estimation accuracy based on variance analysis findings. Recommendations must be specific and measurable, such as suggesting adjustment factors for particular expense categories at specific ports. The system must track recommendation acceptance and measure whether accepted recommendations actually improved subsequent PDA accuracy. Recommendations must consider seasonal factors (Ramadan port schedule changes, Indian monsoon impacts on JNPT operations, summer heat restrictions at Gulf ports), vessel-specific factors (draft restrictions, LOA-based charges), and market factors (fuel price impacts on towage costs). Each recommendation must include expected accuracy improvement percentage and confidence interval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R012	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Approval and Dispute Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for reviewing, approving, or disputing FDA variances. When variance exceeds configurable thresholds, the FDA must enter a review queue requiring approval before being accepted into the accounting system. Dispute handling must support formal communication with port agents including attachment of supporting documentation, back-and-forth comments, and resolution tracking. Disputed amounts must be segregated in accounting as provisions until resolution. The workflow must support parallel approval paths where financial and operational approvers can review simultaneously. Escalation rules must auto-escalate unresolved disputes beyond configurable aging thresholds to senior management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R013	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Hijri Calendar Variance Period Reporting	The system must support variance reporting aligned to Hijri calendar periods in addition to Gregorian calendar periods for entities operating in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. Hijri period reports must correctly handle the variable length of Hijri months (29-30 days) and the annual drift of Hijri calendar relative to Gregorian. Year-over-year variance comparisons using Hijri periods must include appropriate normalization for period length differences. Ramadan period analysis must be available as a standard reporting dimension given its significant impact on port operating hours and overtime charges at Gulf ports. The system must support dual-calendar display showing both Hijri and Gregorian dates simultaneously in all variance reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R014	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Benchmark Variance Against Industry Standards	The system must compare PDA-to-FDA variances against industry benchmark data where available. Integration with BIMCO or similar industry data sources must allow comparison of the company's variance performance against peer group averages. Where external benchmarks are unavailable, the system must use internal historical data to establish proprietary benchmarks by port, vessel size, and expense category. Benchmark comparisons must be presented as percentile rankings showing where the company's variance performance sits relative to benchmarks. The system must flag port-agent combinations where variance performance is consistently below industry median, triggering review of agent appointment and potential retendering of agency services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R015	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Data Export and API Access	The system must provide comprehensive data export capabilities for variance analysis data. Export formats must include Excel (XLSX with formatted worksheets and pivot-ready data tables), CSV, PDF (formatted reports with company branding), and JSON (for system integration). A RESTful API must expose variance data with filtering, pagination, and sorting capabilities for integration with external business intelligence tools. The API must support OAuth 2.0 authentication and must enforce entity-level data access controls. Bulk export must handle large datasets (10,000+ port calls) without timeout, using asynchronous job processing with download notification upon completion. All exports must include metadata showing generation timestamp, filter criteria applied, and exchange rates used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R016	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Real-Time FDA Receipt Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard tracking FDA receipt status across all active voyages and port calls. The dashboard must show port calls awaiting FDA submission with aging indicators (days since vessel departure), FDAs received but pending review, FDAs under dispute, and fully settled port calls. Visual indicators must use traffic light coloring for aging: green (within SLA), amber (approaching SLA), red (overdue). The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, vessel, and port agent. Automated reminders must be sent to port agents for overdue FDAs based on configurable SLA periods (default: 30 days for Gulf ports, 45 days for Indian ports). Summary statistics must show FDA receipt rate and average settlement cycle time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-002-R017	FEAT-018-2-002	MOD-018	Variance Analysis Report Scheduling	The system must support scheduled generation and distribution of variance analysis reports. Users must be able to configure recurring report schedules at daily, weekly, monthly, or custom intervals. Scheduled reports must support parameterized generation where each entity receives its own filtered version of the report. Distribution channels must include email with attached PDF or Excel, storage to document management system, and push to shared network locations. Report templates must be configurable by authorized users including selection of variance metrics, aggregation levels, chart inclusions, and language (English, Arabic, or bilingual). Failed report generation must trigger alerts to system administrators with error details for troubleshooting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R001	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Historical Port Cost Database Construction	The system must automatically construct and maintain a comprehensive historical port cost database from all processed PDA and FDA records. The database must normalize costs by vessel size (TEU capacity, GRT, NRT, LOA, beam), cargo profile (laden TEU, empty TEU, reefer count, hazmat presence), and temporal factors (date, season, day of week). Data must span all operational ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV) with minimum 36 months of history for reliable benchmarking. The database must update incrementally as new FDAs are processed and must maintain data quality scores indicating completeness and reliability of each port's benchmark dataset.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R002	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	AI Cost Prediction Model Training	The system must train machine learning models to predict expected port costs for upcoming port calls based on historical benchmarks. Models must incorporate features including vessel particulars (GRT, NRT, LOA, draft), expected cargo operations (containers to load/discharge, reefer count, hazmat count), port-specific factors (tariff schedules, known surcharges), temporal factors (season, day of week, holiday periods including Ramadan and Diwali), and market conditions (fuel prices affecting towage). Model accuracy must be tracked using MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) with target accuracy of ±10% for well-established ports. Models must be retrained automatically on a configurable schedule as new FDA data becomes available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R003	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Anomaly Detection in Port Charges	The AI engine must detect anomalous charges in incoming PDAs and FDAs by comparing each line item against historical benchmarks and statistical norms. Anomaly detection must use both statistical methods (z-score, IQR-based outlier detection) and machine learning approaches (isolation forest, autoencoder-based detection) to identify charges that deviate significantly from expected ranges. The system must distinguish between genuine anomalies (potential overcharges, billing errors) and legitimate cost increases (tariff updates, regulatory changes). Each detected anomaly must be assigned a confidence score and categorized by likely cause. Anomaly alerts must be prioritized by financial impact to focus reviewer attention on the highest-value discrepancies first.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R004	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Port-to-Port Cost Comparison Analytics	The system must provide port-to-port cost comparison analytics that normalize for vessel size, cargo volume, and service scope to enable fair comparison. Comparison dimensions must include total port call cost per TEU, individual expense category costs, port efficiency metrics (cost per hour in port), and total cost of port call by vessel class. The comparison must cover all operational ports and must support both absolute cost comparison and cost-adjusted-for-service-quality comparison where service quality metrics (turnaround time, damage rates) are available. Interactive visualization must allow users to select comparison ports, time periods, and normalization methods. Results must highlight ports where costs are significantly above regional averages for similar operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R005	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Tariff Change Impact Analysis	The system must detect and quantify the impact of port tariff changes on disbursement costs. When a pattern of increased charges is detected at a specific port that is consistent with a tariff update, the system must flag this as a probable tariff change and estimate the annualized cost impact based on projected port call frequency and cargo volumes. The system must maintain a tariff change registry where confirmed tariff updates are recorded with effective dates, affected charge categories, and percentage or absolute changes. Impact analysis must project forward 12 months showing expected cost increase by entity, vessel, and trade lane. Integration with port authority tariff publication feeds must be supported where available for proactive impact assessment before tariff effective dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R006	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Cost Optimization Recommendation Engine	The AI engine must generate actionable cost optimization recommendations based on benchmark analysis. Recommendations must include: alternative port selection where cost savings exceed route deviation costs, optimal berth timing to avoid overtime charges (particularly relevant for Gulf ports during restricted working hours), cargo consolidation opportunities to reduce per-TEU port costs, agent renegotiation targets where benchmark comparisons indicate above-market agency fees, and vessel scheduling adjustments to avoid peak-period surcharges. Each recommendation must include estimated annual savings in the entity's reporting currency, implementation complexity rating, and prerequisite conditions. Recommendations must be tracked through acceptance, implementation, and realized savings verification stages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R007	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Seasonal Cost Pattern Modeling	The system must model seasonal cost patterns for each port to improve PDA accuracy and cost budgeting. Seasonal factors must capture Ramadan impacts on Gulf port operations (reduced working hours, overtime premiums at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port), Indian monsoon season impacts on JNPT (weather delays, additional tug requirements), summer heat restrictions in the Arabian Gulf (reduced daytime working hours from June to September), and holiday period surcharges (Eid, Diwali, Christmas/New Year). Seasonal models must be automatically updated annually based on observed data and must provide confidence intervals around seasonal adjustment factors. Budget planning reports must incorporate seasonal adjustments to provide more accurate monthly cost projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R008	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Vessel Class Cost Profiling	The system must build and maintain cost profiles by vessel class showing typical port cost structures for different vessel sizes and types. Vessel classes must include feeder (500-2000 TEU), sub-Panamax (2000-5000 TEU), Panamax (5000-8000 TEU), post-Panamax (8000-14000 TEU), and ultra-large container vessels (14000+ TEU). Cost profiles must show expected ranges for each expense category at each port, enabling quick identification of outlier charges. Profiles must account for vessel-specific factors such as whether shore cranes or ship gear is used for cargo operations, draft restrictions affecting tug requirements, and LOA-based berth allocation charges. Profiles must be automatically recalibrated quarterly as new data accumulates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R009	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	External Data Integration for Benchmarking	The system must support integration with external data sources to enrich benchmarking capabilities. Supported integrations must include port authority published tariff schedules (where available via API or structured data feed), bunker fuel price indices (for towage cost correlation analysis), currency exchange rate feeds (for cross-currency benchmark normalization), and industry benchmarking databases. The system must handle data format variations across different port authorities (Mawani for Saudi ports, Abu Dhabi Ports for Khalifa Port, DP World for Jebel Ali, JNPT port authority) and must normalize external data into the internal benchmark format. Data quality validation must flag stale or inconsistent external data and must fall back to internal benchmarks when external data is unavailable or unreliable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R010	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Cost Benchmarking Dashboard with AI Insights	The system must provide an interactive benchmarking dashboard displaying AI-generated insights alongside traditional cost comparisons. The dashboard must feature a cost heat map showing relative expense levels across the port network, trend indicators showing cost trajectory (rising, stable, declining) for each port, AI confidence indicators for prediction accuracy, and a prioritized list of cost optimization opportunities ranked by potential savings. Dashboard widgets must be configurable and arrangement must be user-personalizable. The dashboard must support real-time filtering and must update as new FDA data is processed. A natural language summary section must provide AI-generated narrative explaining key cost trends and anomalies in both English and Arabic languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R011	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Agent Fee Benchmarking and Negotiation Support	The system must benchmark port agent fees across the network to support agency fee negotiations. Agent fees must be compared by port, separating fixed fees (attendance fee, documentation fee) from variable fees (husbandry services, crew-related services). The system must identify agents whose fees are above the port average and quantify the potential savings from renegotiation or agent replacement. Historical agent fee trends must be displayed showing whether agents have increased fees above inflation rates. For ports served by multiple agents, the system must provide comparative analysis to inform agent selection decisions. Negotiation support reports must be generated in PDF format with benchmark data, peer comparisons, and recommended target fee ranges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R012	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Multi-Currency Benchmark Normalization	The system must normalize all benchmark costs to enable fair comparison across ports operating in different currencies (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for Saudi ports, INR for JNPT, USD as common reference). Normalization must use either current exchange rates, period-average rates, or purchasing power parity adjustments based on the analysis purpose selected by the user. The system must account for currency volatility when comparing costs across periods, isolating real cost changes from exchange rate movements. Benchmark reports must allow display in any supported currency with clear indication of the conversion methodology used. Historical benchmark trends must offer both nominal and real (inflation-adjusted) views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R013	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Predictive Cost Budgeting for Voyage Planning	The system must provide predictive port cost estimates for voyage planning purposes, integrating with the voyage planning module. When a new voyage is being planned, the AI must generate expected port costs for each planned port call based on the vessel assigned, expected cargo profile, scheduled dates, and historical benchmarks. Cost predictions must include confidence intervals showing best-case, expected, and worst-case cost scenarios. The predictions must automatically adjust as voyage parameters change during the planning process. Comparison between AI-predicted costs and PDA estimates from agents must be displayed to highlight potential estimation discrepancies before voyage commencement. Predicted costs must feed into voyage profitability projections for go/no-go decision support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R014	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Benchmark Data Quality and Governance	The system must implement data quality controls and governance processes for the benchmarking database. Data quality checks must include outlier exclusion rules (configurable standard deviation thresholds), minimum sample size requirements for statistical validity (configurable per port, default minimum 20 port calls), data recency weighting (more recent data weighted higher in benchmarks), and completeness validation (flagging ports with insufficient data for reliable benchmarking). A data governance dashboard must show benchmark coverage across the port network, data quality scores per port, and alerts for ports where benchmark reliability has degraded. Authorized users must be able to manually exclude specific port calls from benchmarks with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R015	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Benchmark Reporting with Arabic Localization	The system must generate all benchmarking reports with full Arabic language support including proper RTL text rendering, Arabic port and vessel names, and bilingual column headers. Report narratives generated by the AI must be available in both English and Arabic, with the Arabic version being a proper contextual translation rather than literal word-for-word translation. Charts and visualizations must adapt to RTL layout with axis labels, legends, and data labels rendered in Arabic when the Arabic language is selected. All monetary amounts must format according to the selected locale with proper thousands separators and decimal conventions. Report cover pages must include both Gregorian and Hijri dates with the company logo and entity-specific branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R016	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Machine Learning Model Explainability	The system must provide explainability features for all AI-generated cost predictions and anomaly detections to ensure user trust and regulatory compliance. For each cost prediction, the system must display the top contributing factors (feature importance) that drove the prediction, such as vessel size accounting for a specific percentage of the predicted cost and seasonal factor contributing another percentage. For anomaly detections, the system must explain which benchmark dimensions the flagged charge deviated from and by how much. Explainability output must be presented in business-friendly language rather than technical ML terminology. Users must be able to provide feedback on AI predictions (correct, overestimate, underestimate) to support model improvement through active learning loops.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-003-R017	FEAT-018-2-003	MOD-018	Regulatory Surcharge Tracking and Analysis	The system must track and analyze port-related regulatory surcharges separately from base port costs to provide clean benchmark comparisons. Regulatory surcharges include environmental levies (IMO 2020 compliance surcharges), security surcharges (ISPS code compliance), infrastructure development levies, congestion surcharges, and government-mandated fees. Each surcharge must be mapped to its regulatory basis and effective date range. The system must monitor for new surcharge introductions and existing surcharge modifications across the port network. Impact analysis must project the annual cost of regulatory surcharges by entity and trade lane. Comparison of regulatory burden across ports must be available to inform port selection and trade route optimization decisions in conjunction with the voyage planning module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R001	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Group-Level Consolidated Disbursement Report	The system must generate a group-level consolidated disbursement report aggregating all port costs across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Consolidation must eliminate inter-company transactions where one entity paid port charges on behalf of another, preventing double-counting. The report must present totals in the group reporting currency (configurable, default USD) with sub-totals by entity in their respective local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Consolidation exchange rates must be sourced from the treasury module using period-end rates for balance sheet items and average rates for income statement items in compliance with IFRS 10 consolidation requirements. The report must support both monthly and quarterly consolidation periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R002	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Multi-Dimensional Reporting Cube	The system must construct a multi-dimensional reporting cube enabling ad-hoc analysis of disbursement data across dimensions including port, entity, vessel, voyage, trade lane, expense category, port agent, time period (Gregorian and Hijri), currency, and container type. Users must be able to pivot, slice, and dice data across any combination of dimensions without requiring IT support. The cube must support standard OLAP operations including roll-up, drill-down, slice, dice, and pivot. Pre-aggregated summary tables must ensure query response times under three seconds for standard dimension combinations. The reporting cube must refresh incrementally as new disbursement data is processed, with full rebuild capability on demand.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R003	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Executive Summary Dashboard	The system must provide an executive summary dashboard presenting key port disbursement metrics for senior management. Dashboard components must include total port expenditure by entity with month-over-month trend, cost per TEU by port with benchmark comparison, top ten highest-cost port calls in the current period, PDA versus FDA variance summary with traffic-light indicators, agent performance overview showing top and bottom performing agents, and projected port costs for the next quarter based on AI predictions. The dashboard must load within five seconds and must support responsive layout for desktop, tablet, and mobile viewing. Executive users must be able to customize which widgets appear and their arrangement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R004	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Trade Lane Cost Analysis Report	The system must produce trade lane cost analysis reports showing total port disbursement costs attributable to each trade lane operated by the group. Trade lanes must be defined as origin-destination port pairs or port rotation patterns. The report must break down costs by each port within the trade lane, showing both absolute costs and cost per TEU carried on each leg. Comparison across trade lanes must be normalized by distance and cargo volume to identify relative cost efficiency. The report must support time-series comparison showing trade lane cost evolution over configurable periods. Trade lane definitions must be sourced from the voyage planning module and must handle changes in port rotation mid-period by prorating costs appropriately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R003	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Calendar Interval Maintenance Scheduling	The system must support calendar-based maintenance scheduling using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar systems. Maintenance intervals must be configurable in days, weeks, months, or years. The system must handle Hijri date conversions accurately for entities operating under KSA and Qatar regulatory frameworks where official documentation may require Hijri dates. Scheduling must account for weekends varying by entity: Friday-Saturday for Qatar/KSA/UAE entities, Saturday-Sunday for India entity. The system must generate maintenance due lists sortable by vessel, equipment group, criticality, and port of next call to optimize maintenance execution during scheduled port stays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R005	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Regulatory and Tax Authority Reporting	The system must generate port disbursement reports formatted for regulatory and tax authority submissions in each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar, reports must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority requirements. For UAE, reports must support UAE Corporate Tax filing requirements effective from 2024. For KSA, reports must align with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) e-invoicing requirements including Fatoora format compliance. For India, reports must support GST Input Tax Credit reconciliation for port services. Each jurisdiction-specific report must include the appropriate tax identification numbers, currency formatting, and date conventions. Reports must be generated on demand or scheduled to align with filing deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R006	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Vessel Performance Disbursement Report	The system must generate vessel-level disbursement performance reports showing total port costs incurred by each vessel across all ports visited during a reporting period. The report must compare actual costs against budgeted costs for each vessel, highlighting vessels with consistent budget overruns. Per-voyage cost trends must be displayed for each vessel showing whether port costs are increasing or decreasing over time. The report must segment costs into fixed components (pilotage, berth hire based on vessel characteristics) and variable components (stevedoring based on cargo volume) to isolate controllable versus uncontrollable cost elements. Fleet-wide averages and medians must be provided as reference points for individual vessel assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R007	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Accrual and Provision Reporting	The system must generate accrual and provision reports for port disbursement costs at period-end. For port calls where FDA has not yet been received, the system must report the outstanding PDA-based accrual amount. For partially settled port calls with disputed items, the system must report the provision for expected settlement. The report must age accruals by days outstanding and flag aged accruals exceeding configurable thresholds (default 90 days). Accrual reversal tracking must show accruals created in prior periods that have been settled in the current period with variance commentary. The report must reconcile to the general ledger accrual accounts with clear identification of any discrepancies. Month-end accrual adjustment journal entries must be auto-generated from this report.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R008	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Port Agent Accounts Payable Aging Report	The system must produce port agent accounts payable aging reports showing outstanding balances owed to each port agent across all entities. Aging buckets must be configurable with defaults of current, 30 days, 60 days, 90 days, and over 90 days. The report must distinguish between undisputed amounts due for payment and disputed amounts under review. Multi-currency balances must be shown in both original currency and entity reporting currency. Consolidated group-level views must be available showing total exposure to each port agent across all entities. The report must integrate with payment processing to show scheduled payment dates for outstanding invoices. Agent credit limit utilization must be displayed where credit limits have been configured.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R009	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Budget vs Actual Port Cost Reporting	The system must produce budget versus actual port cost reports at entity, port, vessel, and trade lane levels. Budget data must be imported from the budgeting module or entered directly into the disbursement system. Variance analysis must show absolute and percentage deviations from budget with color-coded indicators (green within ±5%, amber ±5-15%, red beyond ±15%). Year-to-date and full-year forecast views must be provided, with the forecast combining actuals for elapsed months with budget or AI-predicted costs for remaining months. The report must support rolling forecast methodology where the forecast is updated monthly based on latest actuals and AI predictions. Budget restatement capability must handle mid-year budget revisions without losing original budget reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R010	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Consolidated Cash Flow Impact Report	The system must generate reports showing the cash flow impact of port disbursements across the group. The report must distinguish between cash advances paid to agents before port calls, agent invoices paid after port calls, and outstanding commitments creating future cash requirements. Cash flow timing must be projected based on standard payment terms per agent and historical payment patterns. Multi-currency cash flow projections must be available showing when payments in each currency are expected. The report must integrate with treasury management to support cash position forecasting and foreign currency hedging decisions. Net cash flow from port operations must be reconciled to bank statements through the payment module integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R011	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Bilingual Report Generation Engine	The system must support generation of all consolidated reports in English, Arabic, or bilingual (English and Arabic side by side) formats. The report generation engine must handle RTL text layout for Arabic including proper rendering of mixed-direction content where English port codes or vessel names appear within Arabic text. Table headers, column labels, and report titles must have maintained translation mappings with the ability for authorized users to customize translations. Numeric formatting must adapt to the selected language locale. PDF output must embed Arabic fonts to ensure consistent rendering across different viewer applications. The bilingual format must present English content on the left page and Arabic equivalent on the right page for print-ready output.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R012	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Automated Report Distribution System	The system must support automated generation and distribution of consolidated reports on configurable schedules. Distribution lists must be configurable per report type with different recipients for different entity subsets. Reports must be distributed via email with PDF or Excel attachments, uploaded to SharePoint or document management systems, and made available through a self-service report portal. Schedule options must include daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and custom cron-based schedules. Each scheduled report must support parameterization allowing the same report template to generate entity-specific versions distributed to respective entity management. Report generation failures must trigger alerts to administrators with error details and must automatically retry once before escalating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R001	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Agent Registry & Discovery Service	Implement a centralized agent registry that supports dynamic registration, health-check heartbeats every 10 seconds, capability advertisement, and automatic deregistration of agents unresponsive for 3 consecutive heartbeat cycles	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R013	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Interactive Report Drill-Down Capability	The system must provide interactive drill-down capability in all consolidated reports allowing users to navigate from summary totals to underlying detail. Drill-down paths must support navigation from group total to entity to port to voyage to port call to individual disbursement line item. Each drill-down level must maintain the filter context from the parent level. Users must be able to open drill-down details in new browser tabs for side-by-side comparison. The drill-down must ultimately reach the source document (PDA or FDA) with ability to view the original scanned document where available. Breadcrumb navigation must show the current drill-down path and allow quick return to any intermediate level. Drill-down must work consistently across all report types and must respect entity-level data access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R014	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Cross-Entity Reconciliation Report	The system must generate cross-entity reconciliation reports identifying and quantifying inter-company disbursement transactions. When one entity pays port charges that should be partially or fully borne by another entity, the reconciliation report must track the original payment, the inter-company charge allocation, and the settlement status. The report must highlight unmatched inter-company items where one entity has recorded a charge but the counterparty entity has not yet acknowledged it. Aging of unsettled inter-company balances must be tracked with escalation alerts for items exceeding configurable thresholds. The reconciliation must support quarterly close processes where inter-company balances are formally confirmed between entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R015	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Custom Report Builder Interface	The system must provide a custom report builder allowing authorized users to create ad-hoc reports without developer assistance. The builder must offer drag-and-drop field selection from the disbursement data model, configurable grouping and sorting, calculated field definitions using formula expressions, conditional formatting rules, and chart insertion capabilities. Custom reports must be saveable as templates for reuse and shareable with other users subject to access controls. The builder must support both tabular and cross-tab (pivot) report layouts. Preview capability must show report output with sample data before execution against the full dataset. Custom reports must have access to the same data security model as standard reports, ensuring users can only include data they are authorized to view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R016	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Report Performance Optimization	The system must optimize report generation performance to handle large consolidated datasets spanning multiple entities and thousands of port calls per reporting period. Reports covering a full fiscal year of group-wide data must generate within 60 seconds for standard reports and within 180 seconds for complex custom reports. Performance optimization must include materialized views for common aggregation patterns, intelligent query caching for frequently accessed report combinations, and progressive rendering for interactive dashboards. The system must support concurrent report generation for at least 50 simultaneous users without degradation. Resource-intensive reports must be queued and processed asynchronously with progress indicators and completion notifications rather than blocking the user interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R017	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Consolidated Reporting Data Validation	The system must perform comprehensive data validation before generating consolidated reports to ensure accuracy and completeness. Validation checks must include: completeness verification confirming all entities have submitted data for the reporting period, exchange rate availability for all currencies in the reporting period, inter-company transaction matching verification, accrual completeness for unreceived FDAs, and general ledger reconciliation status. Validation results must be presented as a pre-generation checklist with pass/fail indicators for each check. Reports generated with validation warnings must be watermarked as provisional until all warnings are resolved. Critical validation failures must block report generation until resolved, while non-critical warnings must allow generation with appropriate disclaimers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-018-2-004-R018	FEAT-018-2-004	MOD-018	Historical Report Archive and Retrieval	The system must maintain an archive of all generated consolidated reports with full retrieval capability. Each generated report must be stored as a versioned immutable document with metadata including generation timestamp, parameters used, user who generated it, and data snapshot identifier. The archive must support search by report type, date range, entity, and generating user. Archived reports must be retrievable in their original format (PDF, Excel) for at least seven years to comply with the most stringent document retention requirement across operating jurisdictions (India requires eight years for certain tax documents). Storage must use compressed format with automatic tiering to cold storage for reports older than configurable threshold. Comparison between archived reports from different periods must be supported to track reporting evolution over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R001	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Maintenance Job Template Library	The system must provide a comprehensive library of maintenance job templates organized by equipment category (main engine, auxiliary engines, deck machinery, navigation equipment, cargo handling gear, reefer systems, ballast water treatment). Each template must include default intervals (running hours and calendar-based), estimated spare parts, required certifications for personnel, and step-by-step work instructions. Templates must support bilingual content in Arabic and English with proper RTL rendering for Arabic text. The library must be pre-populated with manufacturer-recommended maintenance schedules for common marine equipment found on container vessels operating in the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R002	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Running Hours Based Scheduling Engine	The system must automatically schedule maintenance jobs based on equipment running hours counters. It must support configurable thresholds with early-warning alerts at 80% and 95% of the interval. The scheduling engine must account for varying operational profiles between vessels trading on Qatar-UAE-India routes versus KSA coastal routes. Running hours must be captured via integration with vessel machinery monitoring systems or manual entry with validation. When a vessel is in port at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), or JNPT (INNHA), the system must flag overdue jobs and recommend execution windows based on port stay duration and berth availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R004	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Inter-Agent Communication Bus	Provide asynchronous message bus for agent-to-agent communication supporting publish-subscribe, request-reply, and broadcast patterns with message ordering guarantees, at-least-once delivery, and dead-letter queue for undeliverable messages	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R004	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Condition Based Maintenance Triggers	The system must support condition-based maintenance triggers derived from sensor data, oil analysis results, vibration monitoring, thermography readings, and visual inspection reports. Trigger thresholds must be configurable per equipment type and must generate automatic work orders when breached. The AI engine must analyze historical condition data to predict equipment degradation trends and recommend proactive maintenance before failure occurs. Condition monitoring data must be storable offline on vessel systems and synchronized when connectivity is restored at port or via VSAT links. All condition data must be timestamped in UTC with vessel local time display and linked to the vessel's position for correlation with operating conditions in Arabian Gulf high-temperature environments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R005	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Work Order Generation and Assignment	The system must automatically generate work orders from scheduled maintenance jobs, condition-based triggers, or manual requests. Each work order must capture: job description in Arabic and English, assigned personnel with certification validation, required spare parts with inventory check against vessel stores, estimated duration, safety precautions, permit-to-work requirements, and relevant drawings or manuals. Work orders must be assignable to vessel crew, riding squads, or shore-based contractors. The system must enforce that critical machinery work orders (main engine, steering gear, fire systems) require Chief Engineer approval before execution. Multi-entity work orders must track costs in the vessel's operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with conversion to USD for consolidated fleet reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R006	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Spare Parts Integration for Maintenance Jobs	The system must link each maintenance job to required spare parts with part numbers, quantities, and specifications. When a work order is generated, the system must automatically check vessel onboard inventory and shore warehouse stock levels at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT logistics hubs. If parts are insufficient, the system must generate purchase requisitions routed to the appropriate entity's procurement department based on vessel flag and operating entity. Spare parts costs must be tracked in the purchasing currency with automatic conversion to the vessel's operating budget currency using daily exchange rates for USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The system must maintain minimum-maximum stock levels per vessel and alert when reorder points are reached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R007	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Maintenance Completion and Sign-off Workflow	The system must enforce a structured completion workflow for all maintenance jobs. The executing officer must record actual work performed, parts consumed, running hours at completion, and any deviations from the planned procedure. Photographic evidence must be attachable as proof of completion. The Chief Engineer must digitally sign off completed jobs for critical equipment categories as defined by ISM Code. Shore-based superintendent review and approval must be required for Class-related maintenance items. The system must support offline completion recording on vessels with synchronization to the central system when connectivity is available. Completion timestamps must be recorded in UTC with automatic Hijri date conversion for QA/KSA entity reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R008	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Overdue Maintenance Tracking and Escalation	The system must track all overdue maintenance jobs with automatic escalation based on configurable rules. Level 1 escalation (1-7 days overdue) must notify the Chief Engineer and vessel superintendent. Level 2 escalation (8-14 days overdue) must notify the fleet manager and generate a risk assessment. Level 3 escalation (15+ days overdue) must notify the DPA (Designated Person Ashore) and flag the item in the ISM non-conformity register. Overdue items related to Class-critical equipment must be immediately flagged to the classification society liaison. The system must calculate an overdue index per vessel and per fleet segment, displayed on the AI-driven fleet dashboard with trend analysis. Notifications must be delivered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R009	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Maintenance Budget Planning and Cost Tracking	The system must support annual maintenance budget planning per vessel with line items for each equipment category. Actual costs must be tracked against budget with variance analysis at equipment, vessel, and fleet levels. Budgets must be created in the vessel's operating currency (QAR for Qatar-flagged vessels, AED for UAE-flagged, SAR for KSA-flagged, INR for India-flagged) with consolidated reporting in USD. The AI engine must analyze historical maintenance spending patterns to forecast future budgets and identify cost optimization opportunities. Dry dock provision costs must be accrued monthly based on planned dry dock intervals. The system must generate multi-entity consolidated maintenance cost reports with intercompany elimination for vessels managed by one entity but owned by another.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R010	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Regulatory Compliance Maintenance Matrix	The system must maintain a regulatory compliance matrix mapping maintenance requirements to ISM Code, SOLAS, MARPOL, MLC 2006, and flag state regulations for Qatar (QMSA), UAE (FTA), KSA (TGA), and India (DGS) maritime administrations. Each maintenance job must be tagged with applicable regulatory references. The system must generate compliance status reports showing percentage of regulatory maintenance completed, due, and overdue per vessel. The AI engine must monitor regulatory updates from IMO, flag states, and classification societies, alerting technical managers when new maintenance requirements are introduced. The compliance matrix must be auditable and exportable in formats acceptable to port state control inspectors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R011	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Maintenance History and Equipment Lifecycle Tracking	The system must maintain a complete maintenance history for every piece of equipment across its entire lifecycle. History records must include all work orders executed, parts replaced, condition monitoring readings, failures, and modifications. Equipment history must be transferable when vessels are bought or sold, maintaining full traceability. The system must calculate mean time between failures (MTBF) and mean time to repair (MTTR) per equipment type and compare against fleet averages and industry benchmarks. The AI engine must analyze maintenance history to identify recurring failures, recommend design modifications, and predict remaining useful life. All historical data must be searchable in both Arabic and English with full-text search capability across work descriptions and failure narratives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R006	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Circuit Breaker & Fault Tolerance	Implement circuit breaker pattern per agent type with configurable failure thresholds (default 5 failures in 60s), half-open probe intervals, automatic recovery detection, and fallback routing to secondary agent pools	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R012	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Critical Equipment Redundancy Monitoring	The system must identify and monitor critical equipment with redundancy requirements per SOLAS and classification society rules. For container vessels, this includes main engine, steering gear, generators, fire pumps, bilge systems, and navigation equipment. The system must track the operational status of both primary and backup systems, alerting when redundancy is compromised. If a critical system and its backup are both under maintenance simultaneously, the system must block the work order and require DPA override authorization. The monitoring dashboard must show real-time redundancy status per vessel with color-coded indicators. For vessels calling at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, the system must verify that all port-required safety systems are operational before generating arrival documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R013	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Maintenance KPI Dashboard and AI Analytics	The system must provide an AI-powered KPI dashboard showing: PMS completion rate (target >95%), overdue maintenance index, mean time between failures, maintenance cost per TEU carried, planned vs unplanned maintenance ratio, and superintendent response time. KPIs must be viewable at vessel, fleet segment, entity, and consolidated group levels. The AI engine must identify anomalies, predict maintenance trends, and generate natural language insights in both Arabic and English. The dashboard must support drill-down from KPI to individual work orders. Benchmarking against industry standards and historical fleet performance must be available. The dashboard must render correctly in RTL mode for Arabic-language users with all charts and graphs properly mirrored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R014	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Vessel Crew Certification Validation for Maintenance	The system must validate that personnel assigned to maintenance tasks hold the required certifications and competencies. Certification requirements must be configurable per job type: electrical work, confined space entry, hot work, working at height, refrigeration systems, and high-voltage equipment. The system must block work order assignment if the assigned crew member's certification has expired or is not recognized by the vessel's flag state administration. Certification data must be synchronized from the crew management module. For riding squads and shore contractors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must verify contractor qualifications against port authority requirements and vessel operator standards before allowing work authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R015	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Offline PMS Operation and Data Synchronization	The system must support full PMS functionality in offline mode for vessels at sea without reliable internet connectivity. Vessel-side systems must store all maintenance data locally including job schedules, work orders, spare parts inventory, equipment history, and completion records. When connectivity is restored via VSAT, port WiFi at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, or cellular networks, the system must automatically synchronize all offline transactions with the central shore-based system using conflict resolution rules. Priority synchronization must be given to safety-critical items and overdue maintenance alerts. The offline system must support the same bilingual Arabic-English interface with Hijri calendar functionality as the online system. Data integrity checksums must verify successful synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-001-R016	FEAT-019-1-001	MOD-019	Permit to Work Integration for Hazardous Maintenance	The system must integrate a permit-to-work module for hazardous maintenance activities including hot work, confined space entry, work on electrical systems, and work on pressurized systems. Each permit must capture risk assessment, safety precautions, required PPE, atmospheric testing results (for confined spaces), and authorization chain. Permits must be time-limited and automatically expire, requiring renewal for extended jobs. The system must enforce that no hazardous maintenance work order can be started without an active, approved permit. Permit templates must comply with ISM Code requirements and flag state regulations for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. All permit documentation must be available in Arabic and English with digital signatures for the authorizing officer, executing officer, and safety officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R001	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Schedule Planning and Forecasting	The system must maintain a rolling 5-year dry dock schedule for all fleet vessels based on classification society requirements (typically 2.5-year intermediate and 5-year special surveys). The schedule must account for vessel trading patterns on Qatar-UAE-India routes, seasonal weather windows in the Arabian Gulf, port congestion at major container terminals, and shipyard availability in the region (Drydocks World Dubai, Nakilat-Keppel Erhama Bin Jaber Al Jalahma Shipyard in Ras Laffan, Cochin Shipyard, and other regional yards). The AI engine must optimize scheduling to minimize off-hire time and coordinate with cargo booking commitments. Schedules must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars for KSA and Qatar entity reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R002	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Specification Development	The system must support creation of detailed dry dock repair specifications organized by work categories: hull treatment (blasting, coating, anode replacement), machinery overhauls, steel renewals, piping repairs, electrical work, safety equipment servicing, and class survey items. Each specification item must include scope description in English and Arabic, estimated quantities, quality standards, reference drawings, and acceptance criteria. The system must auto-populate specifications from outstanding PMS items, class survey requirements, and defect reports. Specifications must be exportable in standard shipyard tender formats (PDF and structured data). The AI engine must analyze historical dry dock records to suggest commonly missed items and recommend scope additions based on vessel age, type, and trading area conditions in the Arabian Gulf high-salinity environment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R003	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Shipyard Tender and Selection Process	The system must manage the shipyard tender process including RFQ generation, distribution to pre-qualified shipyards, bid collection, and comparative analysis. The tender evaluation matrix must consider price, delivery time, yard capabilities, past performance ratings, proximity to vessel trading route, and flag state acceptance. Bids must be normalized to a common currency (USD) from shipyard local currencies (AED for UAE yards, QAR for Qatar yards, INR for Indian yards, SAR for KSA yards). The AI engine must score shipyard bids against historical performance data and recommend optimal selection. The system must maintain a pre-qualified shipyard register with capability matrices, certifications, and blacklist status. Award letters and contracts must be generated in bilingual format with proper Arabic legal terminology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R007	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Agent Scaling & Pool Management	Auto-scale agent pool sizes based on queue depth, average processing time, and SLA targets with minimum and maximum pool bounds, cool-down periods between scale events, and pre-warming of agent instances during predicted peak hours	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R004	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Budget Estimation and Approval Workflow	The system must generate detailed dry dock cost estimates based on specification items, historical cost data, and shipyard quotations. Estimates must include direct repair costs, docking and undocking charges, classification society survey fees, superintendent travel and accommodation, stores and spares, and contingency provisions (typically 10-15% for container vessels). Budget approval must follow a multi-level workflow configurable per entity: Technical Manager, Fleet Director, CFO, and Board approval for amounts exceeding entity-specific thresholds. Budgets must be prepared in the contracting currency with conversion to the vessel owner entity's reporting currency. The system must track dry dock provision accruals from the maintenance budget module and reconcile actual costs against provisions upon dry dock completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R005	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Progress Tracking and Reporting	The system must provide real-time dry dock progress tracking with daily status updates on each specification item. Progress must be tracked as percentage complete with milestones: vessel arrival, docking, tank cleaning, survey completion, hull treatment, machinery work, undocking, sea trials, and delivery. The system must capture daily reports from the attending superintendent including work completed, delays encountered, additional items discovered, and weather impacts. A Gantt chart view must show planned vs actual progress with critical path analysis. The AI engine must predict completion date based on current progress rate and flag potential delays. Daily cost tracking must compare actual expenditure against budget with variance alerts. All reports must be available in Arabic and English for stakeholder distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R006	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Additional Work Item Management During Dry Dock	The system must handle additional work items discovered during dry dock that were not in the original specification. Each additional item must capture: description, justification (safety, class requirement, operational necessity, or owner's extra), estimated cost, and impact on schedule. Additional items must follow an expedited approval workflow with configurable thresholds: superintendent authority up to a limit, technical manager authority up to a higher limit, and escalation to fleet director above that. Cost thresholds must be currency-specific based on the contracting entity. The system must distinguish between class-mandated additions (non-discretionary) and optional improvements. Running totals of additional work must be visible on the dry dock dashboard with projected total cost versus original budget.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R007	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Quality Assurance and Inspection Tracking	The system must track quality inspections throughout the dry dock process including hull coating thickness measurements (DFT readings), steel thickness gauging results, machinery alignment records, pressure test certificates, and trial results. Each inspection must record: inspector name, date and time, measurement values, acceptance criteria, pass/fail status, and photographic evidence. Non-conforming items must trigger corrective action requests with tracking to resolution. The system must maintain coating system specifications (manufacturer, product, scheme) with DFT acceptance ranges per zone (boot-top, vertical sides, flat bottom, cargo holds). Classification society surveyor attendance and findings must be recorded with direct linkage to survey status tracking. All quality records must be archived for the vessel's lifetime and accessible for future dry dock reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R008	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Vendor and Subcontractor Management	The system must manage all vendors and subcontractors engaged during dry dock including riding squads, specialist contractors (propeller polishing, underwater welding, tank coating), equipment OEM service engineers, and classification society surveyors. Each vendor must have a profile with qualifications, insurance certificates, port security clearances (required at Ras Laffan, Jebel Ali, and JNPT), and rate agreements. The system must generate vendor purchase orders linked to specific dry dock specification items. Vendor invoices must be matched against purchase orders and verified by the attending superintendent before payment processing. Vendor performance must be rated after each dry dock engagement on quality, timeliness, safety, and value, feeding into the pre-qualified vendor register for future selections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R009	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Hull Performance Monitoring Pre and Post Dry Dock	The system must capture hull performance metrics before and after dry dock to quantify the benefit of hull treatment. Metrics must include speed-power curves, fuel consumption at reference speed and draft, hull roughness index, and propeller performance. Pre-dock data must be collected over the 30-day period before dry dock from vessel performance monitoring systems. Post-dock data must be collected during sea trials and over the first 30 days of commercial operation. The AI engine must calculate the improvement in fuel efficiency and CO2 emissions reduction attributable to the dry dock hull treatment. Performance improvement must be converted to estimated annual fuel cost savings in USD and reported per entity. This data must feed into future dry dock planning decisions including optimal coating system selection for vessels trading in the warm, high-fouling waters of the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R010	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Safety Management	The system must enforce safety management protocols throughout the dry dock period. A dry dock safety plan must be generated for each project covering: fire prevention (especially critical in shipyards in hot climates of Qatar, UAE, and KSA where temperatures exceed 50°C), confined space entry procedures, working at height protocols, hot work management, and emergency evacuation procedures. The system must track daily toolbox meetings, safety observations, near-miss reports, and incident reports. Safety KPIs (lost time injury frequency rate, total recordable case frequency) must be monitored against targets. Safety non-conformities must trigger stop-work orders until resolved. All safety documentation must be available in Arabic, English, and Hindi (for Indian shipyard workers at Cochin Shipyard and JNPT-area facilities) to ensure comprehension by all workforce nationalities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R011	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Sea Trial Planning and Execution Tracking	The system must support sea trial planning and execution following dry dock completion. Trial plans must include: main engine performance trials at various loads, steering gear tests, anchor windlass tests, navigation equipment calibration, safety equipment function tests, speed trials, and crash stop maneuvers as required by the classification society. Each trial item must have acceptance criteria linked to class and flag state requirements for Qatar (QMSA), UAE (FTA), KSA (TGA), or India (DGS) administrations. Trial results must be recorded with actual measurements against acceptance criteria. Failed trial items must generate corrective action work orders with tracking back to the shipyard. The system must generate sea trial reports in the format required by the relevant classification society (Lloyd's, DNV, Bureau Veritas, or Indian Register of Shipping).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R012	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Cost Reconciliation and Final Settlement	The system must manage dry dock cost reconciliation and final settlement with shipyards. All shipyard invoices must be matched against the contracted specification, approved additional work orders, and verified quantities. Disputed items must be tracked with resolution status. Retention amounts (typically 5-10%) must be calculated and held pending warranty period completion. Final settlement statements must be generated in the contracting currency with conversion to the vessel owner entity's reporting currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The system must compare actual dry dock cost against the original budget and dry dock provision with variance explanations. Cost data must feed into the fleet maintenance cost analytics for benchmarking future dry dock budgets. Payment milestones must be configured per shipyard contract terms with automatic payment request generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R013	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Document Management and Archival	The system must maintain a complete document repository for each dry dock project. Required documents include: repair specifications, shipyard contracts, daily progress reports, quality inspection records, class survey reports, photographs (before, during, after), coating certificates, steel renewal certificates, machinery test certificates, sea trial reports, and final settlement documentation. Documents must be categorized, version-controlled, and searchable. The system must enforce document naming conventions per organizational standards. All documents must support bilingual metadata in Arabic and English. The archive must be retained for the vessel's lifetime plus 5 years per maritime record-keeping requirements. Documents must be accessible offline for attending superintendents at shipyards and synchronizable when connectivity is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R014	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Environmental Compliance Tracking	The system must track environmental compliance during dry dock operations including: anti-fouling system (AFS Convention) compliance for hull coatings, ballast water management system installation or upgrade tracking per BWM Convention, hazardous material inventory (IHM) updates per EU Ship Recycling Regulation and Hong Kong Convention, waste disposal records for removed coatings, oily waste, and hazardous materials. The system must verify that coating products comply with IMO AFS Convention restrictions and regional environmental regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. VOC emission tracking must be maintained for coating application activities. Environmental non-compliance items must be flagged as critical and escalated to the DPA. All environmental records must be formatted for inspection by port state control officers at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R015	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	AI-Driven Dry Dock Optimization Recommendations	The AI engine must analyze historical dry dock data across the fleet to generate optimization recommendations. Analysis must include: optimal dry dock intervals based on hull fouling rates in Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean waters, cost-benefit analysis of premium vs standard coating systems, shipyard performance benchmarking across regional yards, optimal timing considering seasonal weather patterns and shipyard pricing, and grouping of vessels for volume discounts at preferred shipyards. The system must predict future dry dock costs based on vessel age degradation curves and market price trends. Recommendations must be presented as actionable insights with estimated cost savings in USD. The AI must learn from each completed dry dock to improve future predictions and must account for multi-entity consolidation opportunities where vessels from different group entities can be scheduled at the same shipyard for better commercial terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R016	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Dry Dock Warranty Tracking and Claims Management	The system must track warranty periods for all dry dock work items, typically 12 months from delivery for general work and 36-60 months for hull coatings. Each warranty must record: scope covered, start date, expiry date, shipyard obligations, and claim procedures. When defects are reported within warranty periods, the system must generate warranty claims with supporting evidence (photographs, inspection reports, and original dry dock quality records). Claims must be tracked through notification, shipyard acknowledgment, resolution proposal, and settlement stages. The system must alert technical managers 30 and 60 days before warranty expiry dates to ensure final inspections are conducted. Warranty claim values must be tracked in the original contract currency with conversion to the claiming entity's reporting currency for financial reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-002-R017	FEAT-019-1-002	MOD-019	Multi-Entity Dry Dock Cost Allocation	The system must support complex cost allocation for dry docks involving multiple group entities. When a vessel is owned by one entity (e.g., Qatar holding company) but technically managed by another (e.g., UAE operating company), costs must be allocated per the intercompany management agreement. The system must separate owner's account items (hull treatment, steel renewal, statutory surveys) from manager's account items (riding squad costs, superintendent expenses). Intercompany invoices must be generated automatically in the appropriate currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with proper transfer pricing documentation. Tax implications must be flagged for cross-border dry dock services, particularly India GST requirements for vessels docked at Indian shipyards and UAE/Qatar VAT for regional yards. Consolidated group reports must eliminate intercompany transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R001	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Survey Schedule Management and Window Tracking	The system must maintain the complete survey schedule for each vessel based on classification society rules and flag state requirements. Survey types must include: annual surveys, intermediate surveys, special surveys (class renewal), bottom surveys, boiler surveys, tailshaft surveys, and continuous machinery surveys. Each survey must have a defined window period (typically 3 months before and after the due date for annual surveys, 6 months for intermediate). The system must display survey windows graphically on a timeline view with color coding: green (within window), amber (approaching due date), red (overdue). The AI engine must optimize survey scheduling to align with port calls at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT where classification society surveyors are readily available, minimizing dedicated survey calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R002	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Multi-Classification Society Support	The system must support vessels classed with multiple classification societies including Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, American Bureau of Shipping, Indian Register of Shipping, and other IACS members. Each society's specific survey requirements, terminology, certificate formats, and reporting standards must be configurable. The system must maintain surveyor contact databases for each society's offices at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other regional ports. Survey request workflows must be tailored to each society's booking procedures and lead time requirements. Class society fee schedules must be maintained per society and updated annually, with fees tracked in the society's invoicing currency and converted to the vessel entity's reporting currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R003	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Flag State Inspection and Certificate Tracking	The system must track all flag state certificates and inspections for vessels registered in Qatar (QMSA), UAE (FTA - Federal Transport Authority), KSA (TGA - Transport General Authority), and India (DGS - Directorate General of Shipping). Certificates tracked must include: Certificate of Registry, Safety Management Certificate, ISSC (International Ship Security Certificate), Safety Equipment Certificate, Safety Radio Certificate, Load Line Certificate, IOPP Certificate, Cargo Ship Safety Construction Certificate, and Minimum Safe Manning Certificate. Each certificate must have issue date, expiry date, issuing authority, and endorsement requirements. The system must generate alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry. Flag state specific requirements must be configurable, such as Qatar's additional port state compliance documentation and India's DGS circular compliance requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R004	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Conditions of Class Tracking and Resolution	The system must track all conditions of class imposed by the classification society with full lifecycle management. Each condition must record: date imposed, surveyor name, description of the condition, due date for rectification, associated equipment or structural item, and estimated cost of rectification. The system must categorize conditions by urgency: immediate (vessel cannot sail), before departure from port, within a specified timeframe, or at next scheduled survey. Resolution workflow must track: planned corrective action, parts ordered, work completed, and surveyor verification. Overdue conditions must trigger automatic escalation to the DPA and generate notifications to the classification society liaison. The system must maintain historical condition of class records per vessel for trend analysis and identify recurring issues across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R005	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Port State Control Inspection Readiness	The system must maintain port state control (PSC) inspection readiness dashboards for each vessel. The dashboard must show: all certificates with validity status, outstanding conditions of class, overdue maintenance items, crew certification status, ISM audit findings status, and ISPS compliance status. The system must calculate a PSC risk score based on vessel age, flag state performance (Paris MoU, Tokyo MoU, Indian Ocean MoU target factors), classification society performance, and historical inspection results. The AI engine must identify high-risk areas before vessel arrival at ports known for rigorous PSC enforcement. Pre-arrival checklists must be generated specific to the port state MoU regime: Riyadh MoU for Arabian Gulf ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port), and Indian Ocean MoU for JNPT. All PSC-related documentation must be available in English as the international maritime language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R006	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Survey Cost Tracking and Budgeting	The system must track all survey-related costs including classification society fees, flag state inspection fees, surveyor travel expenses, and any repair costs mandated by survey findings. Annual survey budgets must be prepared per vessel and consolidated at entity and group levels. Classification society fee invoices must be matched against the fee schedule and verified before payment authorization. Costs must be tracked in the invoicing currency and converted to the vessel entity's operating currency using daily exchange rates. The system must distinguish between routine survey costs (budgetable) and unplanned additional survey costs triggered by incidents or conditions of class. Multi-year cost projections must account for the survey cycle with special survey years typically having significantly higher costs than annual survey years. The AI engine must benchmark survey costs against fleet averages and industry data to identify optimization opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R007	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Continuous Survey Machinery Program Management	The system must support Continuous Survey Machinery (CSM) programs where class surveys are distributed over the 5-year cycle rather than concentrated at special survey. The system must maintain the CSM item list per vessel with each item assigned to a specific year in the cycle. Progress tracking must show items completed, due, and overdue within each annual period. The system must coordinate CSM item surveys with vessel port calls to minimize disruption, preferring survey completion during regular port stays at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT where classification society surveyors are stationed. If a vessel transfers from conventional survey to CSM or vice versa, the system must recalculate the survey schedule and redistribute items accordingly. Credit for maintenance already performed under PMS must be tracked for CSM recognition by the class society.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R008	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	ISM Code Audit Tracking and Non-Conformity Management	The system must track ISM Code audit schedules, findings, and corrective actions for both vessel and company (DOC) audits. Audit types must include: initial audit, annual verification, intermediate verification, and renewal audit. Each audit finding must be classified as: major non-conformity, non-conformity, or observation. The corrective action workflow must track: root cause analysis, planned corrective action, implementation evidence, and verification by the auditing body. The system must maintain the ISM non-conformity register accessible to the DPA at all times. Audit schedules must be coordinated with the flag state administration (QMSA, FTA, TGA, or DGS) or the Recognized Organization (RO) delegated to perform audits. All ISM documentation must be maintained in the vessel's working language and the company's official language, supporting bilingual Arabic-English documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R009	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	ISPS Code Compliance and Security Audit Tracking	The system must track International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code compliance including Ship Security Plan (SSP) review and approval, ship security assessments, ISSC certificate management, and security drills. Security levels (1, 2, 3) must be trackable per vessel with escalation procedures documented. The system must maintain records of all security incidents and drill exercises with completion dates and findings. Security audit schedules must be tracked with the flag state or RSO (Recognized Security Organization). For vessels calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must verify that PFSO (Port Facility Security Officer) notifications are completed with the required advance notice per port-specific regulations. Declaration of Security (DoS) records must be maintained for ship-to-ship and ship-to-port interactions. All security documentation must follow the confidentiality requirements of the ISPS Code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R008	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Distributed Tracing & Correlation	Propagate correlation IDs across all agent interactions enabling end-to-end trace reconstruction, with OpenTelemetry-compatible span emission, parent-child span linking, and trace sampling configurable per agent type	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R010	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Enhanced Survey Program ESP Tracking for Hull and Machinery	The system must support Enhanced Survey Programs (ESP) as required by classification societies for container vessels including hull structural surveys and machinery condition assessments. For hull ESP, the system must track: close-up survey areas, thickness measurement locations, tank testing requirements, and structural assessment results. For machinery ESP, the system must track: main engine component inspections, auxiliary machinery surveys, and boiler internal examinations. The system must maintain coating condition assessments (GOOD, FAIR, POOR) per tank and compartment. Survey planning must identify areas requiring staging or access arrangements and coordinate with vessel operations to ensure cargo-free tanks are available during port calls. ESP reports must be generated in the classification society's required format with all measurements, photographs, and surveyor comments linked to structural drawings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R011	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Certificate Digital Repository and Verification	The system must maintain a digital repository of all vessel certificates with scanned originals, metadata (issue date, expiry date, issuing authority, certificate number), and verification status. The system must support electronic certificates where accepted by flag states and classification societies. Certificate authenticity must be verifiable against classification society online databases where available. The system must generate certificate status reports per vessel showing all valid certificates, upcoming renewals, and any gaps in certification. Port agents at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT must be able to access vessel certificate status via a secure portal for pre-arrival documentation. The system must support certificate endorsement tracking where annual endorsements are required. All certificate data must be exportable in formats required by port state control inspectors, with Arabic translations available for certificates required by QMSA, FTA, and TGA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R012	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Survey Findings Integration with Maintenance System	The system must automatically create maintenance work orders from classification society survey findings and recommendations. When a surveyor identifies a deficiency or recommends corrective action, the system must generate a linked work order in the PMS with the survey finding reference, required completion date, and class condition details. Work order completion must be linked back to the survey finding for closure tracking. The system must distinguish between findings that can be addressed by vessel crew, those requiring shore contractor support at the next port of call, and those requiring dry dock intervention. Cost estimates for survey-mandated repairs must be generated and allocated to the appropriate budget category. The integration must support offline work order creation on vessel systems with synchronization to the central system when connectivity is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R013	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Ballast Water Management Convention Compliance Tracking	The system must track Ballast Water Management (BWM) Convention compliance for all fleet vessels. Tracking must include: BWMS (Ballast Water Management System) type approval certificate, installation date, commissioning test results, and annual survey status. The system must maintain records of ballast water exchange or treatment operations with GPS coordinates, volumes, and sampling results. Compliance deadlines for BWMS installation (based on IOPP Certificate renewal date) must be tracked with alerts. For vessels calling at ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must verify that BWM requirements per local port state regulations are met, as some jurisdictions may have requirements exceeding IMO BWM Convention minimums. The system must track BWMS manufacturer maintenance requirements and spare parts availability at regional service stations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R014	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Statutory Regulation Change Monitoring and Impact Assessment	The AI engine must continuously monitor regulatory changes from IMO, flag state administrations (QMSA, FTA, TGA, DGS), classification societies, and regional port authorities. When new regulations are identified that affect fleet vessels, the system must generate impact assessments detailing: which vessels are affected, what modifications or surveys are required, compliance deadlines, estimated costs, and recommended action plans. Recent regulatory areas must include: EEXI/CII carbon intensity regulations, cyber security requirements (IMO MSC.428(98)), SOLAS amendments for container weight verification, and regional emission control area requirements. Impact assessments must be distributed to technical managers, vessel masters, and compliance officers in their preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must maintain a regulatory change log with implementation status tracking per vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R015	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Survey Attendance and Surveyor Coordination	The system must manage surveyor attendance requests and coordination for all classification society and flag state surveys. Survey request workflows must include: preferred date and port, scope of survey items, required documentation preparation, and surveyor assignment confirmation. The system must track surveyor availability at key ports including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Lead time requirements must be configurable per classification society and port (typically 7-14 days advance notice). The system must support splitting surveys across multiple port calls when the full scope cannot be completed in a single visit. Surveyor attendance records must include: surveyor name, society, attendance date, items surveyed, findings, and fees charged. For remote surveys where accepted by the classification society, the system must support video survey scheduling and evidence submission. All survey communications must be logged for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-003-R016	FEAT-019-1-003	MOD-019	Fleet Survey Compliance Dashboard and AI Analytics	The system must provide a fleet-wide survey compliance dashboard showing: overall fleet compliance percentage, vessels with overdue surveys, upcoming survey windows, outstanding conditions of class count, and PSC detention risk scores. The dashboard must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), flag state, classification society, and vessel type. The AI engine must generate trend analysis showing compliance improvement or degradation over time and predict potential compliance issues 6 months ahead. Benchmarking against industry detention rates from Paris MoU, Tokyo MoU, and Indian Ocean MoU annual reports must be available. The dashboard must support drill-down from fleet overview to individual vessel survey status. Executive summary reports must be generated monthly in both Arabic and English for board-level reporting with proper RTL layout for Arabic version and Hijri date references for GCC entity reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R009	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Agent Versioning & Canary Deployment	Support concurrent execution of multiple agent versions with traffic splitting by percentage, automatic rollback when error rate exceeds baseline by 2x, and version-specific configuration isolation	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R001	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Reporting and Initial Classification	The system must provide a structured defect reporting interface accessible to vessel crew, superintendents, and shore-based personnel. Each defect report must capture: equipment identifier from the vessel's equipment register, defect description in Arabic or English with automatic translation, date and time discovered, reporter name and rank, location on vessel (using standard compartment coding), photographs or video evidence, and initial severity classification (critical, major, minor). Critical defects affecting vessel seaworthiness must trigger immediate notifications to the Chief Engineer, vessel superintendent, DPA, and if class-related, the classification society duty surveyor. The reporting interface must function offline on vessel systems with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, or via VSAT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R002	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Risk Assessment and Prioritization	The system must enforce a risk assessment for every reported defect using a configurable risk matrix combining likelihood of failure progression and consequence severity. Consequence categories must include: safety (crew injury potential), environmental (pollution risk), operational (vessel delay or off-hire), commercial (cargo damage to containerized goods), and regulatory (class condition or PSC detention risk). The AI engine must assist in risk scoring by analyzing historical defect data for similar equipment types and failure modes across the fleet. Risk scores must determine repair priority: P1 (immediate action required), P2 (repair at next port call), P3 (repair at next convenient opportunity), P4 (monitor and plan). High-risk defects on vessels approaching Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT must trigger pre-arrival repair resource mobilization. Risk assessments must be reviewable and overridable by the superintendent with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R003	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Temporary Repair Authorization and Tracking	The system must manage temporary repairs when permanent repair cannot be immediately executed. Each temporary repair must be documented with: repair method, materials used, expected service life, monitoring requirements, and conditions under which the temporary repair must be replaced with a permanent solution. Temporary repairs on class-critical items must be reported to the classification society with a time limit for permanent repair. The system must maintain an active temporary repair register per vessel with countdown timers to permanent repair due dates. Escalation alerts must be generated when temporary repairs approach their expiry. The system must track classification society and flag state (QMSA, FTA, TGA, DGS) requirements for temporary repair acceptance and any operational restrictions imposed. For container-specific equipment (cell guides, lashing bridges, reefer plugs), temporary repairs must be assessed for impact on container stowage capacity and cargo safety.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R004	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Repair Work Order Creation and Resource Planning	The system must generate detailed repair work orders from assessed defect reports. Each work order must include: repair method statement, required spare parts with procurement status, specialist tools or equipment needed, estimated manhours, safety precautions and permit requirements, and quality acceptance criteria. The system must determine the optimal repair strategy: vessel crew capability, riding squad at next port, shore contractor engagement, or deferral to next dry dock. For port-based repairs, the system must identify available contractors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT from the pre-qualified vendor register. Repair costs must be estimated in the local port currency with conversion to the vessel's operating budget currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The AI engine must recommend repair timing based on vessel schedule, parts availability, and contractor mobilization lead times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R005	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Emergency Repair Coordination and Response	The system must support emergency repair coordination when critical defects occur at sea or require immediate response in port. An emergency repair workflow must bypass standard approval chains with post-incident ratification. The system must maintain emergency contact lists for: classification society emergency response, flag state administration duty officers, salvage and towing companies in the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean, emergency spare parts suppliers at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, and specialist emergency repair contractors. For machinery breakdowns requiring towing, the system must integrate with the vessel's position tracking to provide real-time location to responding parties. Emergency repair costs must be captured with flags for insurance claim categorization (H&M, P&I, or owner's account). The system must generate post-incident reports per ISM Code requirements with root cause analysis and preventive action tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R006	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Root Cause Analysis and Trend Identification	The system must support structured root cause analysis (RCA) for all major and critical defects using methodologies such as 5-Why, fishbone diagram, or fault tree analysis. RCA findings must be linked to corrective actions and preventive measures tracked to completion. The AI engine must analyze defect data across the fleet to identify trends: recurring equipment failures, systemic maintenance gaps, environmental factors (e.g., increased corrosion rates in Arabian Gulf high-temperature, high-humidity conditions), and age-related degradation patterns. Trend reports must be generated quarterly per vessel and annually per fleet with comparisons across entities. The system must identify when a defect trend indicates a potential class-wide issue requiring fleet-wide inspection or modification. Root cause categories must be standardized using marine industry taxonomies with bilingual Arabic-English descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R007	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Repair Cost Tracking and Multi-Currency Settlement	The system must track all costs associated with defect repairs including: spare parts, contractor labor, riding squad expenses, superintendent travel, equipment rental, and classification society fees for survey attendance. Costs must be captured in the invoicing currency and converted to the vessel's operating budget currency at the daily exchange rate. Supported currencies must include USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and other currencies for repairs at non-home ports. The system must support split cost allocation when repairs are shared between owner's account and charterer's account per charter party terms. Insurance-recoverable repair costs must be flagged and tracked separately with claim reference numbers. Budget variance reports must show actual repair costs against maintenance budget provisions. The AI engine must analyze repair cost trends and predict annual repair budgets based on vessel age, type, and trading pattern.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R010	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Orchestration State Machine	Implement durable state machine for multi-step orchestration workflows with checkpoint persistence, replay capability from any checkpoint, compensation actions for partial failures, and maximum workflow duration enforcement	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R008	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Class-Related Defect and Condition Management	The system must provide specialized handling for defects that affect classification society requirements. When a defect is identified that may impact class status, the system must trigger mandatory notification to the classification society with defect details, photographs, and risk assessment. The system must track the classification society's response: acknowledgment, surveyor attendance requirement, condition of class imposition, or acceptance of owner's repair plan. Integration with the survey tracking feature must automatically update the vessel's condition of class register. The system must manage memoranda issued by class societies imposing operational restrictions (speed, route, cargo limitations) until permanent repair is completed. For vessels trading between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must verify that class conditions do not prevent the vessel from calling at any scheduled port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R009	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Repair Quality Verification and Sign-off	The system must enforce quality verification for all completed repairs with a structured sign-off process. Verification must include: visual inspection records, functional test results, measurement data where applicable (alignment readings, pressure tests, thickness measurements), and photographic evidence of completed repair. The sign-off workflow must require: executing officer confirmation, Chief Engineer verification for critical equipment, superintendent approval for major repairs, and classification society surveyor attendance for class-related items. The system must maintain repair quality records linked to the original defect for warranty tracking and future reference. For repairs performed by external contractors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, the system must capture contractor warranty terms and certificates of completion. Quality records must be stored in the vessel's permanent equipment history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R010	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Backlog Management and Reporting	The system must maintain a real-time defect backlog per vessel showing all open defects with their current status, priority, planned repair date, and estimated cost. The backlog must be sortable and filterable by equipment system, severity, repair location (at sea, in port, dry dock), and budget status. Fleet-wide defect backlog reports must aggregate across all vessels with drill-down capability. The AI engine must analyze backlog trends and alert when the defect count or total estimated repair cost exceeds configurable thresholds per vessel or fleet segment. Backlog aging reports must identify defects that have been open beyond acceptable periods based on their priority classification. Monthly defect backlog reports must be generated for technical committee review in both Arabic and English formats with RTL layout support for Arabic version. The system must track defect backlog reduction targets and progress.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R011	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Cargo Equipment Defect Specialization	The system must provide specialized defect handling for container cargo-specific equipment including: cell guides, lashing bridges, twist locks, container securing devices, hatch covers, hatch coamings, reefer plug connections, and container gantry cranes (for geared vessels). Defects in cargo equipment must be assessed for impact on container stowage capacity and vessel's ability to safely carry maximum TEU. The system must calculate the operational impact of cargo equipment defects in terms of TEU capacity reduction and estimated revenue loss. Integration with the cargo planning system must be supported so that defective bay or cell positions are blocked from loading. For reefer plug defects at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT where high reefer cargo volumes exist, the system must prioritize repairs and calculate the impact on reefer container acceptance. Cargo equipment defect reports must be sharable with P&I clubs when cargo damage claims arise.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R012	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Structural Defect Assessment and Steel Renewal Planning	The system must handle structural defects with specialized assessment workflows including thickness measurement recording, wastage rate calculations, and remaining life estimation. Structural defect locations must be mapped on vessel general arrangement drawings and structural profiles. The system must maintain classification society minimum thickness requirements per structural member and flag items approaching or below minimum. Steel renewal planning must include: steel grade specification, quantity estimation, weld procedure requirements, and coating reinstatement. The AI engine must analyze structural defect patterns to identify areas of accelerated corrosion, particularly relevant for vessels trading in the Arabian Gulf where water temperatures and salinity accelerate hull degradation. Steel renewal costs must be estimated for budget planning with options for repair at next dry dock or emergency in-water repair.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R013	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Photographic Evidence Management	The system must support comprehensive photographic evidence management for all defects and repairs. Each defect must have associated photographs showing: the defect from multiple angles, reference scale indicators, surrounding area context, and any temporary measures applied. Repair progress photographs must be captured at key stages. Photographs must be geotagged with vessel position, timestamped in UTC with local time conversion, and tagged with equipment identifier and defect reference number. The system must support photograph annotation tools for marking up defect extent and measurements directly on images. Photograph storage must support offline capture on vessel devices with batch upload when connectivity is available at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT. Image compression must be configurable to balance quality requirements with bandwidth limitations on VSAT connections. AI-powered image analysis must assist in defect severity classification from photographs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R014	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Warranty Defect Tracking for New Builds and Repairs	The system must track warranty claims for defects on newly built vessels and recently repaired equipment. Warranty periods must be configurable per shipyard, manufacturer, and contract terms. The system must automatically identify when a reported defect falls within an active warranty period and flag it for warranty claim processing. Claim documentation must include: original specification or contract reference, defect description with evidence, timeline from delivery or repair to defect manifestation, and commercial impact assessment. The system must track claim status through stages: notification to warrantor, acknowledgment, inspection arrangement, resolution agreement, and financial settlement. Warranty claim values must be tracked in the original contract currency with conversion to the claiming entity's currency. The system must generate warranty expiry reports per vessel alerting technical managers to conduct comprehensive inspections before warranty periods end.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-001-1-001-R011	FEAT-001-1-001	MOD-001	Rate Limiting & Throttling per Agent	Enforce per-agent and per-tenant rate limits using token bucket algorithm with configurable burst capacity, graceful degradation under load shedding, and priority-based exemption rules for critical shipping operations	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R015	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Integration with Vessel Vetting and Inspections	The system must integrate defect data with vessel vetting and third-party inspection systems. When external inspections are conducted (SIRE, CDI, OVID, or terminal-specific inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT), the system must import inspection findings and create corresponding defect records. Observations from oil major vetting inspections must be tracked to closure with evidence of corrective action. The system must pre-populate vessel condition data for upcoming vetting inspections to ensure transparency and readiness. Defect status must be available to commercial teams when responding to vessel nomination enquiries, ensuring that material defects are disclosed per industry standards. The system must track the impact of open defects on vessel vetting approval status and flag risks to commercial operations in the Qatar-UAE-KSA-India container trade.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R016	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	AI-Driven Predictive Defect Analytics	The AI engine must analyze historical defect data across the fleet to predict potential equipment failures before they occur. Predictive models must consider: equipment age and running hours, maintenance history, operating conditions (Arabian Gulf high-temperature environment, monsoon season for India-bound vessels), manufacturer reliability data, and fleet-wide failure patterns. The system must generate predictive maintenance recommendations with confidence scores and estimated time to failure. Recommendations must be prioritized by risk and cost-benefit analysis comparing predictive intervention cost versus unplanned failure cost. The AI must continuously learn from actual outcomes, refining predictions as more fleet data is accumulated. Predictive alerts must be integrated into the PMS scheduling engine to proactively adjust maintenance intervals. Predictive analytics dashboards must show fleet-wide equipment health indices with drill-down to individual vessel and equipment level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R017	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Defect Reporting to P&I Club and Insurance Integration	The system must support defect and incident reporting to Protection and Indemnity (P&I) clubs and Hull and Machinery (H&M) insurers. When defects result in cargo damage, third-party liability, or hull and machinery claims, the system must generate structured incident reports per the P&I club's reporting format. Reports must include: chronological event narrative, defect history and maintenance records, photographic evidence, crew statements, and cost documentation. The system must maintain P&I club and H&M insurer contact details, claim reference numbers, and correspondence logs. Claim costs must be tracked in the original currency with conversion to the vessel entity's reporting currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) and to USD for consolidated group reporting. Deductible amounts must be calculated per policy terms and allocated to the appropriate entity's accounts. The system must generate loss prevention statistics aggregated quarterly and annually for insurer reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-1-004-R018	FEAT-019-1-004	MOD-019	Offline Defect Management and Synchronization Protocol	The system must provide full defect management functionality in offline mode for vessels at sea. Vessel-side systems must support defect reporting, risk assessment, work order creation, repair recording, photograph capture, and completion sign-off without internet connectivity. Data must be stored locally on the vessel's onboard server with encrypted backup. When connectivity is restored via VSAT, port WiFi at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, or cellular network, the system must automatically synchronize all offline defect records with the shore-based central system. Conflict resolution rules must prioritize shore-side updates for fields like priority classification and budget approval, while preserving vessel-side data for execution records. The synchronization protocol must handle intermittent connectivity gracefully, supporting partial synchronization and resumption. Data integrity verification must confirm successful synchronization with checksums and record counts. Critical defect notifications must be queued for immediate transmission when connectivity is first available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-002-1-001-R001	FEAT-002-1-001	MOD-002	Kubernetes Cluster Provisioning	Provision production-grade Kubernetes clusters (EKS/AKS) in primary regions (ME-South-1 for Qatar/Dubai, ME-Central-1 for KSA, AP-South-1 for India) with node pools sized for shipping ERP microservices workloads	must_have	infra-monitoring-agent	Approval required for cluster sizing decisions and region selection exceeding budget thresholds	["K8s clusters provisioned in all 4 target regions with minimum 3 worker nodes each", "Cluster version is within one minor version of latest stable Kubernetes release", "Node pools configured with appropriate instance types for ERP microservices (min 8 vCPU, 32GB RAM per node)", "Cluster health checks pass with all system pods in Running state"]	pending
FEAT-002-1-001-R012	FEAT-002-1-001	MOD-002	Metrics and Monitoring with Prometheus/Grafana	Prometheus metrics collection and Grafana dashboards for all ERP services with shipping-specific KPIs — booking throughput, BL processing times, container tracking latency, and customs clearance durations	must_have	infra-monitoring-agent	Operations team configures alert thresholds based on SLA agreements with shipping customers	["Prometheus scrapes metrics from every microservice at 15-second intervals", "Grafana dashboards display per-module health, latency percentiles (p50, p95, p99), and error rates", "Shipping KPI dashboard shows real-time booking volume, avg BL processing time, and tracking query latency", "PagerDuty/OpsGenie integration for critical alerts with escalation policies"]	pending
FEAT-002-1-002-R004	FEAT-002-1-002	MOD-002	Delegation of Authority Hierarchy	Implement DOA matrix mapping approval authorities to financial thresholds, operational scopes, and entity boundaries — e.g., branch manager approves bookings up to $50K, country head up to $500K, regional director unlimited	must_have	auth-agent	CFO and regional directors define and approve DOA threshold limits per entity	["DOA matrix supports financial threshold tiers per role, entity, and transaction type", "Approval chains auto-route to next authority level when transaction exceeds current level's threshold", "DOA overrides require dual approval from two authorities at the target level", "DOA matrix version-controlled with effective dates — changes take effect on specified future date"]	pending
