FEAT-007-2-001-R005	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Booking Pace Analysis	System tracks booking velocity against historical pace to identify sailings that are ahead or behind expected fill rates.	must_have			["Booking pace is measured as cumulative TEU booked vs. days-before-departure curve per trade lane", "AI classifies each sailing as ahead-of-pace, on-pace, or behind-pace with color-coded visual indicators", "Behind-pace sailings trigger automated rate adjustment suggestions within configured authority limits", "Pace analysis accounts for entity-specific booking patterns (e.g., India origin vs. Qatar origin)", "Historical pace curves adjust for Hijri calendar holidays and regional public holidays automatically"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-001-R006	FEAT-007-2-001	MOD-007	Trade Lane Yield Comparison	Comparative yield analysis across trade lanes with drill-down by origin-destination pair, container type, and booking entity.	must_have			["Yield comparison matrix displays revenue per TEU for all active trade lanes sortable by performance", "Filtering supports entity-level comparison (Qatar vs. Dubai vs. KSA vs. India operations)", "Container type breakdown shows yield differential between 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, and reefer containers", "Year-over-year yield trend lines are displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views", "Export to Excel includes multi-currency columns with conversion rates used at time of export"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-002-R012	FEAT-007-2-002	MOD-007	Forward Rate Curve Benchmarking	System tracks forward freight rate curves from market sources to benchmark forward contract pricing and future rate expectations.	must_have			["Forward rate curves are ingested for 3-month, 6-month, and 12-month horizons per major trade lane", "Internal forward contract rates are plotted against market forward curves for gap analysis", "AI identifies trade lanes where forward curves suggest rate increases, recommending shorter contract terms", "Forward curve data supports scenario modeling for budget planning across all four entities", "Currency forward rates are incorporated to assess multi-currency forward rate exposure in QAR/AED/SAR/INR"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-003-R007	FEAT-007-2-003	MOD-007	Contribution Margin Analysis	Multi-level contribution margin analysis from gross margin through to net profit at each profitability dimension.	must_have			["System calculates CM1 (revenue minus direct variable costs), CM2 (minus semi-variable), and CM3 (minus fixed allocated)", "Contribution margin waterfall chart visualizes margin erosion from gross revenue to net profit", "Margin analysis is available at voyage, trade lane, customer, and entity dimensions", "AI highlights margin compression trends with early warning when CM1 drops below configurable threshold", "Contribution margin reports render in Arabic with proper RTL number formatting (Arabic-Indic numerals optional)"]	pending
FEAT-007-2-004-R002	FEAT-007-2-004	MOD-007	Machine Learning Model Training Pipeline	Automated pipeline for training and retraining pricing ML models using historical booking and market data.	must_have			["Model retraining runs weekly using latest 24 months of historical booking, rate, and market data", "Training pipeline validates model performance against holdout test set before promoting to production", "Model accuracy metrics (RMSE, MAPE) are tracked and must meet configurable thresholds before deployment", "Separate models are trained per major trade lane cluster to capture lane-specific pricing dynamics", "Training data includes entity-specific features accounting for local market conditions in Qatar, Dubai, KSA, and India"]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R001	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Vessel-Specific Spare Parts Catalog Management	The system must maintain a hierarchical spare parts catalog for each vessel in the fleet, organized by machinery system, equipment group, and individual component. Each catalog entry must store IMPA catalog number, maker part number, drawing reference, unit of measure, minimum stock level, maximum stock level, reorder point quantity, lead time in days, criticality classification (critical, essential, non-critical), and associated class society requirement references. The catalog must support bilingual entries in English and Arabic with RTL rendering for Arabic part descriptions and technical specifications. Catalog entries must be linkable to planned maintenance system jobs defined in Part 1 of this module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R002	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Real-Time Onboard Inventory Stock Tracking	The system must track real-time stock quantities of spare parts onboard each vessel, supporting stock receipt, consumption against work orders, stock adjustments, and physical inventory counts. Each stock transaction must record the vessel IMO number, transaction date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, quantity, responsible officer, and linked work order or purchase order reference. The system must operate in offline mode when vessels are at sea between ports such as Hamad Port (QAHMD) and JNPT (INJNP), synchronizing all inventory transactions automatically when connectivity is restored at port. Stock valuations must be maintained in the vessel operating currency and consolidated in USD for fleet-level reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R003	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Shore-Side Warehouse Inventory Management	The system must manage shore-side spare parts warehouses located at Doha (Qatar entity), Dubai (UAE entity), Dammam (KSA entity), and Mumbai (India entity). Each warehouse must track stock levels independently with multi-entity segregation ensuring that inventory owned by the Qatar entity at a Dubai warehouse is accounted separately from UAE entity stock. Inter-warehouse transfer orders must be supported with full audit trails. Warehouse stock valuations must be maintained in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to USD using daily exchange rates. Physical inventory count workflows must support cycle counting and full wall-to-wall counts with variance reporting and approval thresholds per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R004	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Automated Reorder Point Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate optimal reorder points for each spare part based on historical consumption rates, supplier lead times, vessel trading patterns, and criticality classification. The calculation engine must factor in longer lead times for parts destined for vessels trading in the Arabian Gulf versus those calling at JNPT, accounting for customs clearance delays at Indian ports. Critical spare parts as classified by the vessel class society (DNV, Lloyd's, Bureau Veritas) must have safety stock levels set at minimum 150 percent of average consumption during maximum lead time. The system must generate automated reorder alerts when stock levels fall below calculated reorder points, distributing notifications to the fleet technical superintendent and purchasing department.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R005	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Purchase Requisition Generation from Inventory	The system must automatically generate purchase requisitions when spare part stock levels reach reorder points or when manual requisitions are raised by vessel chief engineers through the onboard module. Each purchase requisition must specify the requesting vessel, delivery port preference from configured port list (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, King Abdulaziz Port), required delivery date, part specifications including IMPA code and maker reference, requested quantity, estimated unit cost in the procuring entity currency, and budget code allocation. Requisitions from vessels must be validated against the vessel annual spare parts budget before submission. The system must support requisition creation in both English and Arabic interfaces with all technical specifications maintained bilingually.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R006	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Spare Parts Consumption Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard showing spare parts consumption trends across the fleet, broken down by vessel, equipment category, machinery maker, and time period. The dashboard must display consumption cost analysis in USD with drill-down capability to individual entity currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Comparative analysis must be available showing actual consumption versus budgeted consumption per vessel per quarter. The dashboard must identify high-consumption items, abnormal usage patterns indicating potential equipment problems, and cost optimization opportunities through bulk purchasing. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with proper chart axis orientation for Arabic users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R007	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Critical Spare Parts Compliance Tracking	The system must maintain a dedicated tracking register for class-mandated critical spare parts per vessel, as required by IACS Unified Requirements and individual class society rules. Each critical spare part entry must record the class requirement reference, required minimum quantity onboard, current quantity onboard, last survey verification date, and next survey due date. The system must generate alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before class survey dates if critical spare part quantities are below mandated minimums. Non-compliance status must be escalated automatically to the designated person ashore (DPA) as required under ISM Code Section 4. The register must produce class-ready reports formatted per DNV, Lloyd's, and Bureau Veritas submission requirements for vessels calling at ports under respective flag state jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R008	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Barcode and QR Code Inventory Scanning	The system must support barcode and QR code scanning for spare parts inventory management both onboard vessels and in shore-side warehouses. Each spare part must be assignable a unique barcode or QR code that encodes the part number, IMPA code, vessel assignment, and lot or batch number. The mobile scanning interface must operate in offline mode on vessels, queuing scanned transactions for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The scanning module must support stock receipt verification against purchase orders, consumption recording against work orders, and physical inventory counting. Scanned data must automatically update stock records and generate discrepancy alerts when scanned quantities differ from system records by more than the configured tolerance percentage per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R009	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Spare Parts Inter-Vessel Transfer Management	The system must support spare parts transfers between vessels in the fleet, particularly when vessels are at the same port or anchorage simultaneously. Transfer requests must be initiated by the receiving vessel chief engineer and approved by the fleet technical superintendent. The system must automatically identify vessels with surplus stock of requested parts based on current inventory levels relative to minimum stock requirements. Transfer documentation must include a parts transfer note with quantities, condition assessment, and value for inter-entity accounting purposes. When transfers occur between vessels belonging to different entities (e.g., Qatar entity vessel to UAE entity vessel), the system must generate intercompany accounting entries with proper transfer pricing in both entity currencies using the exchange rate on the transfer date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R010	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Supplier Parts Cross-Reference Database	The system must maintain a cross-reference database linking original equipment manufacturer (OEM) part numbers to equivalent or compatible parts from alternative suppliers. Each cross-reference entry must record the OEM part number, alternative supplier part number, compatibility verification status (verified, pending verification, rejected), cost comparison in USD, quality grade assessment, and approval status from the technical superintendent. The cross-reference database must be searchable from purchase requisition forms to enable cost-effective procurement decisions. Class-critical spare parts must be flagged to restrict alternative sourcing to class-approved manufacturers only. The database must support Arabic descriptions for suppliers operating in Qatar, UAE, and KSA markets alongside English technical specifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R011	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Spare Parts Budget Allocation and Monitoring	The system must support annual spare parts budget allocation per vessel, broken down by equipment category and quarter. Budget amounts must be set in the vessel operating currency with automatic consolidation to USD for fleet-level monitoring. Budget consumption tracking must update in real-time as purchase orders are raised and goods are received. The system must enforce configurable budget threshold warnings at 70 percent, 85 percent, and 95 percent utilization, with automatic escalation to the fleet manager when thresholds are exceeded. Budget reallocation between equipment categories within the same vessel must require technical superintendent approval. Cross-vessel budget transfers must require fleet manager approval. Budget reports must display amounts in both the vessel entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent, with Hijri date references for KSA entity vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R012	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Customs and Import Documentation for Parts Delivery	The system must generate customs and import documentation required for spare parts delivery to vessels at each configured port. For deliveries to Hamad Port Qatar, the system must produce Qatar Customs declaration forms. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port UAE, Dubai Customs and Abu Dhabi Customs forms must be generated. For JNPT India, Indian Customs Bill of Entry details must be prepared referencing the applicable HSN codes for marine spare parts. The system must store copies of customs clearance documents linked to each purchase order delivery. Duty and tax amounts must be captured in local currency and allocated to the spare part landed cost. The system must track bonded warehouse options at each port for duty deferral on parts held in transit between vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R013	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Shelf Life and Expiry Date Tracking	The system must track shelf life and expiry dates for perishable spare parts including rubber gaskets, seals, chemical compounds, paints, lubricants, and safety equipment components. Each perishable item must record the manufacture date, expiry date, storage condition requirements (temperature, humidity), and remaining shelf life percentage. The system must generate alerts at configurable intervals (180, 90, 30 days) before expiry for items onboard vessels and in shore-side warehouses. Expired items must be automatically flagged as non-usable in inventory and excluded from available stock calculations. Disposal of expired items must follow entity-specific waste management procedures with proper documentation for environmental compliance at ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Expiry dates must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R014	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Warranty Tracking for Installed Spare Parts	The system must track warranty status for spare parts installed on vessel machinery and equipment. Each warranty record must capture the part number, serial number, installation date, warranty start date, warranty end date, warranty terms and conditions, supplier warranty contact details, and claim history. When a warranted part fails, the system must automatically generate a warranty claim draft with installation records, running hours since installation, failure description, and supporting photographs. The warranty claim workflow must route through the technical superintendent for review before submission to the supplier. Warranty cost recovery amounts must be tracked in the procurement currency and reflected in the vessel operating cost reports. The system must alert users when warranty periods are approaching expiry within 30 days to enable final inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R015	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Emergency Spare Parts Fast-Track Ordering	The system must provide an expedited ordering workflow for emergency spare parts required to prevent vessel detention or off-hire situations. Emergency orders must bypass standard approval hierarchies with post-facto approval within 48 hours from the fleet manager or DPA. The system must automatically identify the nearest supplier with stock availability and calculate fastest delivery options to the vessel current port or next port of call from the configured port rotation (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, Jubail). Emergency order costs must be flagged separately in vessel operating cost reports with justification documentation requirements. The system must maintain a log of all emergency orders for quarterly review by management, categorized by root cause (breakdown, survey finding, safety critical) to identify preventive improvement opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-001-R016	FEAT-019-2-001	MOD-019	Audit Trail and Inventory Reconciliation Reports	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all spare parts inventory transactions including receipts, issues, adjustments, transfers, and disposals. Each audit record must capture the user identity, timestamp in UTC with local time zone reference, transaction type, quantity before and after, value impact in local entity currency and USD, and authorization reference. Monthly inventory reconciliation reports must compare system stock levels against physical count results for each vessel and shore-side warehouse. Reconciliation variances exceeding configurable thresholds (default five percent by value) must trigger investigation workflows. Annual inventory audit reports must be generated per entity for statutory audit compliance in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions with amounts in respective local currencies and consolidation currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R001	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Multi-Entity Procurement Workflow Configuration	The system must support configurable procurement workflows per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with entity-specific delegation of authority matrices. Each entity must define approval thresholds in local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic USD equivalent calculation for consolidated reporting. The workflow must support minimum two-level and maximum five-level approval chains based on purchase order value bands. Each approval level must specify the approving role, maximum approval amount, and whether co-approval is required. The system must enforce strict separation of duties ensuring that the requisitioner, quotation evaluator, and final approver are different users. Workflow templates must be clonable across entities with currency and threshold adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R002	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Request for Quotation Generation and Distribution	The system must generate Request for Quotation (RFQ) documents from approved purchase requisitions, supporting distribution to multiple suppliers simultaneously via email and supplier portal. Each RFQ must include detailed technical specifications, required delivery port from the configured port list (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, King Abdulaziz Port), delivery deadline, payment terms preference, and quality certification requirements. RFQ documents must be generated in English as standard with optional Arabic translation for regional suppliers in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. The system must track RFQ response deadlines and automatically send reminders to non-responding suppliers at configurable intervals. A minimum of three quotations must be required for purchases exceeding entity-defined thresholds before proceeding to evaluation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R003	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Quotation Comparison and Evaluation Matrix	The system must provide a structured quotation comparison matrix enabling side-by-side evaluation of supplier responses against the RFQ specifications. The comparison must normalize all quoted prices to a common currency (USD) using the exchange rate on the quotation date for fair comparison across suppliers quoting in different currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, EUR, USD). Evaluation criteria must include unit price, total price, delivery lead time, delivery port accessibility, payment terms, warranty offered, supplier quality rating from historical performance, and technical compliance score. The system must calculate a weighted overall score based on configurable evaluation criteria weights per entity. Evaluation results must be documented with justification narrative for audit purposes and regulatory compliance with government procurement rules applicable in Qatar and KSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R004	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Purchase Order Creation and Management	The system must generate purchase orders from approved quotation evaluations, automatically populating supplier details, agreed pricing, delivery terms, and payment conditions. Each purchase order must reference the originating purchase requisition and quotation evaluation for full traceability. Purchase orders must specify delivery INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, DAP, DDP) appropriate for maritime spare parts delivery to vessel or port warehouse. The PO must include bank details for the procuring entity, tax registration numbers (Qatar QID, UAE TRN for VAT, KSA VAT number, India GSTIN), and applicable tax rates. Purchase order amendments must follow a controlled revision process with change tracking and re-approval requirements for changes exceeding five percent of original PO value. PO documents must render correctly in both English and Arabic for bilingual supplier communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R005	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Supplier Master Database and Performance Rating	The system must maintain a comprehensive supplier master database with entries categorized by supply category (spare parts, lubricants, provisions, deck stores, engine stores, safety equipment, services). Each supplier record must store company registration details, contact information, bank details per currency, tax identification numbers, quality certifications (ISO 9001, class society approvals), geographical coverage indicating which ports they can deliver to, and blacklist status. The system must calculate and maintain supplier performance ratings based on delivery timeliness, quality compliance, pricing competitiveness, documentation accuracy, and warranty claim resolution history. Ratings must be updated automatically after each completed purchase order. Suppliers with performance ratings below configurable thresholds must be flagged for review and optionally restricted from new RFQ distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R006	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Dry-Docking Project Procurement Management	The system must support specialized procurement workflows for vessel dry-docking projects, handling the unique requirements of shipyard selection, repair specification tendering, and project cost management. Dry-dock procurement must support multi-stage tendering with preliminary budget quotations followed by detailed specification-based quotations from shortlisted shipyards. The system must manage dry-dock project budgets in USD with cost breakdown by work category (hull, machinery, piping, electrical, class renewal items, owner additional items). Change order management during dry-docking must track scope additions and deletions against the original contract with impact on total project cost. Settlement workflows must support progress payments, retention amounts, and final account reconciliation. All dry-dock procurement documents must be linkable to the vessel class survey schedule from the compliance module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R002	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Performance Scoring Dashboard	The system must calculate and display a composite performance score for each manning agency based on weighted KPIs including crew supply lead time (days from request to onboard), crew retention rate per vessel assignment, number of disciplinary incidents involving supplied crew, documentation accuracy rate (percentage of crew documents submitted without errors), and crew feedback scores. Scores must be computed per entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) and consolidated globally. The AI engine must automatically flag agencies scoring below a configurable threshold (default 60%) and recommend alternative agencies from the registry for upcoming crew requirements at ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R007	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Technical Service Contract Management	The system must manage ongoing technical service contracts for vessels including main engine service agreements, turbocharger overhaul contracts, navigation equipment maintenance agreements, and communication system support contracts. Each service contract must record the contracting entity, vessel or fleet coverage, contract start and end dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, service scope description, annual contract value in contract currency, payment schedule, performance KPIs, and penalty clauses. The system must generate alerts for contract renewal 120, 90, and 60 days before expiry. Contract cost allocation must distribute charges to individual vessels proportionally when fleet-wide contracts exist. The system must track service delivery against contracted SLAs and flag underperformance for management review. Arabic contract summaries must be supported for regional service providers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R008	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Three-Way Invoice Matching for Technical Purchases	The system must perform three-way matching between purchase orders, goods received notes, and supplier invoices for all technical procurement transactions. Matching must validate quantities received against ordered quantities with configurable tolerance percentages per entity (default plus or minus two percent for quantity, plus or minus five percent for value). Price variances between PO price and invoice price must be flagged for review when exceeding entity-defined thresholds. The system must handle partial deliveries with multiple goods received notes matched against a single purchase order and multiple invoices. Currency conversion differences between PO currency and invoice currency must be captured as exchange rate variances. Matched invoices must automatically generate accounts payable entries in the procuring entity ledger with proper tax treatment (Qatar no VAT, UAE five percent VAT, KSA fifteen percent VAT, India variable GST rates based on HSN classification).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R009	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Procurement Budget Control and Commitment Tracking	The system must enforce budget control at the purchase requisition stage, preventing creation of requisitions that would exceed the allocated vessel technical budget. Budget commitment tracking must reserve budget amounts when purchase orders are approved, reducing available budget accordingly. The system must distinguish between committed budget (approved POs not yet delivered), accrued budget (goods received but not yet invoiced), and actual expenditure (matched and approved invoices). Budget utilization dashboards must show real-time status per vessel and per entity in both local currency and USD. Budget transfers between vessels within the same entity must follow an approval workflow. The system must support mid-year budget revisions with proper authorization trails. Fleet-level budget consolidation must aggregate all entity budgets with currency conversion at period-end rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R010	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Preferred Supplier Agreements and Framework Contracts	The system must support preferred supplier agreements and framework contracts that establish pre-negotiated pricing and terms for frequently purchased items across the fleet. Framework contracts must specify the contracting entity, item catalog with agreed unit prices in contract currency, volume discount tiers, delivery lead times per port, validity period, and maximum contract value. Purchase orders raised against framework contracts must automatically apply agreed pricing without requiring the full RFQ process. The system must track cumulative spend against each framework contract and alert procurement when approaching maximum contract value thresholds at 80 percent and 95 percent utilization. Contract terms must be reviewed and renewed annually. Multi-entity framework contracts must specify which entities can order under the agreement and handle intercompany cost allocation for centralized procurement arrangements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R011	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Procurement Document Archival and Retrieval	The system must archive all procurement documents including purchase requisitions, RFQs, supplier quotations, evaluation matrices, purchase orders, goods received notes, and invoices in a searchable document repository. Each document must be indexed by vessel name, vessel IMO number, supplier name, PO number, date range, and procurement category for rapid retrieval. Document retention periods must comply with entity-specific regulatory requirements: Qatar Commercial Companies Law retention of ten years, UAE Commercial Transactions Law retention of five years, KSA ZATCA requirements, and India Companies Act retention periods. The archival system must support both English and Arabic document search with full-text search capability across scanned documents using OCR. Archived documents must be accessible for external audit review with controlled access permissions per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R012	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Vendor Portal for Quotation Submission	The system must provide a web-based vendor portal allowing registered suppliers to receive RFQs, submit quotations, acknowledge purchase orders, and update delivery status electronically. The vendor portal must support bilingual interface in English and Arabic with RTL layout for Arabic-language users. Suppliers must be able to upload quotation documents, technical datasheets, and quality certificates through the portal. Quotation submissions must be time-stamped and locked after the RFQ deadline to ensure fairness. The portal must provide suppliers with visibility into their purchase order status, payment status, and performance rating. Supplier registration through the portal must include a verification workflow with document upload requirements for trade license, tax registration, bank details verification, and quality certification validation before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R013	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Emergency Procurement Authorization Workflow	The system must support an emergency procurement authorization workflow for urgent technical purchases required to maintain vessel seaworthiness or prevent port state control detentions. Emergency procurement must allow single-source purchasing without the standard RFQ process, subject to post-facto justification and approval within 72 hours. The system must capture the emergency justification reason categorized by type (safety critical, class condition, port state control deficiency, imminent breakdown, charterer requirement). Emergency purchases must have a separate elevated approval authority defined in each entity delegation of authority matrix. All emergency procurements must be logged in a dedicated register for quarterly management review. The system must track the ratio of emergency to planned procurements per vessel as a KPI for maintenance planning effectiveness, with targets below ten percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R014	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Tax and Duty Calculation for Cross-Border Procurement	The system must automatically calculate applicable taxes and duties for cross-border technical procurement transactions based on the supplying country, receiving country, and goods classification. For imports into Qatar, the system must apply Qatar Customs duty rates. For UAE, five percent VAT and applicable customs duties must be calculated per Federal Tax Authority rules. For KSA, fifteen percent VAT per ZATCA regulations and customs duties must be applied. For India, IGST, customs duty, and applicable cess must be calculated based on HSN code classification of marine spare parts and equipment. The system must support duty exemption claims for vessels in transit and temporary importation of parts for repair and return. Tax calculation results must be stored with each purchase transaction for VAT return filing and customs audit compliance across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R015	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Procurement Analytics and Spend Analysis	The system must provide comprehensive procurement analytics covering spend analysis by category, supplier, vessel, entity, and time period. Spend analysis must present data in USD with drill-down to local entity currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The analytics module must identify procurement cost trends, seasonal price variations for key spare parts categories, supplier concentration risks where single suppliers account for more than thirty percent of category spend, and savings opportunities through consolidation. Comparative dashboards must show procurement performance metrics including average PO processing time, average supplier lead time, percentage of on-time deliveries, and average cost variance against budget. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic rendering for RTL reports. Scheduled report distribution must be configurable per user role and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-002-R016	FEAT-019-2-002	MOD-019	Procurement Compliance and Anti-Fraud Controls	The system must enforce procurement compliance controls to prevent fraud and ensure adherence to entity-specific procurement policies. Controls must include mandatory segregation of duties between requisition, approval, and payment functions with system-enforced restrictions preventing the same user from performing multiple roles in a single transaction. The system must detect and flag potential duplicate invoices based on supplier, amount, invoice number, and date proximity. Unusual procurement patterns such as repeated just-below-threshold purchases to avoid higher approval levels (split ordering) must be identified and reported. Conflict of interest declarations must be captured from procurement personnel annually and cross-referenced against the supplier database. All procurement compliance violations must be logged and reported to the entity compliance officer with full transaction details for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R001	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	SMS Document Control and Version Management	The system must provide a controlled document management system for the company Safety Management System (SMS) as required by ISM Code Section 11. All SMS documents including policies, procedures, checklists, and forms must be maintained with version control tracking document number, revision number, revision date, author, reviewer, approver, and distribution list. The system must enforce a formal document change request workflow requiring review by the designated person ashore (DPA) and approval by the company safety committee before publication. Superseded document versions must be archived but remain accessible for audit reference. Document distribution to vessels must be tracked with acknowledgment receipts from vessel masters confirming receipt and implementation of updated procedures. The system must support bilingual SMS documents in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R002	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Document of Compliance and SMC Certificate Management	The system must track the lifecycle of Document of Compliance (DOC) certificates for each operating entity and Safety Management Certificates (SMC) for each vessel in the fleet. Certificate records must store the issuing administration or recognized organization (flag state, classification society), issue date, expiry date, annual verification due dates, and intermediate verification due dates. The system must generate escalating alerts at 180, 120, 90, 60, and 30 days before certificate expiry or verification due dates. Certificate renewal workflows must trigger preparation checklists, audit scheduling, and corrective action completion verification. The system must maintain a certificate validity matrix showing real-time compliance status across the fleet, accessible to port state control inspection preparation teams and the DPA. Expired or suspended certificates must immediately flag the affected vessel as non-compliant.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R003	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	ISM Internal Audit Planning and Execution	The system must manage the ISM Code internal audit program as required by Section 12, supporting annual audit planning, auditor assignment, audit checklist management, and finding documentation. The audit plan must schedule internal audits for each vessel and shore-based entity at intervals not exceeding twelve months. Auditor qualification records must be maintained including ISM auditor training certificates, audit experience log, and independence verification ensuring auditors do not audit their own department. Audit checklists must be configurable per vessel type and must cover all ISM Code sections (1 through 13). The system must support audit execution on tablets with offline capability for onboard audits when vessels are between ports such as Hamad Port and Jebel Ali. Completed audit reports must be reviewed by the DPA and presented to company management as required by ISM Code Section 12.4.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R004	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Non-Conformity and Observation Tracking	The system must track all non-conformities (NCs), observations, and opportunities for improvement identified during internal audits, external audits, port state control inspections, and vetting inspections. Each non-conformity record must capture the finding source, ISM Code section reference, description of the non-conformity, root cause analysis, immediate corrective action, preventive action to address the root cause, responsible person, target completion date, and verification of effectiveness. The system must categorize findings as major non-conformity, non-conformity, or observation per ISM Code definitions. Major non-conformities must trigger immediate notification to the DPA and company management. Corrective action completion must be verified with objective evidence (photographs, documents, test records) before closure. The system must prevent closure of major NCs without DPA authorization and must track overdue corrective actions with automatic escalation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R005	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Management Review Meeting Documentation	The system must support the ISM Code Section 12.1 requirement for company management review of the SMS at defined intervals. Management review meeting records must capture attendees, agenda items covering audit results, non-conformity trends, accident and incident analysis, effectiveness of the SMS, resource requirements, and improvement actions. The system must generate management review input reports automatically, compiling data from internal audit findings, external audit findings, port state control deficiency trends, near-miss reports, accident investigation outcomes, maintenance performance indicators, and crew competency metrics. Action items from management reviews must be tracked with assigned owners, deadlines, and completion verification. The system must maintain a history of all management review meetings with trend analysis showing improvement or deterioration in SMS performance metrics over consecutive review periods. Meeting minutes must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R006	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Emergency Preparedness and Drill Management	The system must manage emergency preparedness procedures and drill programs as required by ISM Code Section 8. The system must maintain a library of shipboard emergency procedures covering fire, flooding, collision, grounding, pollution, piracy, medical emergency, and abandon ship scenarios specific to vessel trading areas including the Strait of Hormuz, Gulf of Oman, and Arabian Sea approaches to JNPT. Drill schedules must be generated per vessel ensuring compliance with SOLAS drill frequency requirements with minimum monthly lifeboat drills and fire drills. Drill execution records must capture drill type, date, participants, scenario description, performance assessment, deficiencies identified, and corrective actions. The system must track drill completion rates per vessel and flag non-compliance when mandatory drill frequencies are not met. Drill performance trend analysis must be available for fleet safety performance review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R007	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Accident and Incident Investigation Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for reporting and investigating accidents, incidents, and near-misses as required by ISM Code Section 9. Initial incident reports must capture the date, time, location (with port code or sea coordinates), vessel, persons involved, injury classification per IMO casualty investigation code, environmental impact assessment, and immediate actions taken. Investigation workflows must follow a root cause analysis methodology (such as the Reason model or fault tree analysis) with structured fields for direct causes, contributing factors, and root causes. Investigation findings must link to corrective and preventive actions tracked through the non-conformity management system. The system must classify incidents by severity and type for statistical analysis and trend reporting. Flag state notification requirements must be automatically identified based on incident severity and the vessel flag state, with notification templates prepared per maritime authority requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R008	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Crew Competency and Training Matrix Management	The system must manage crew competency requirements and training matrices as required by ISM Code Section 6 for ensuring that personnel are qualified and trained for their duties. Each vessel position must have defined minimum competency requirements including STCW certificates, flag state endorsements, company-specific training, and equipment-specific familiarization. The system must track individual crew member qualifications against position requirements, identifying gaps and generating training needs reports. STCW certificate expiry tracking must generate alerts at 180 and 90 days before expiry. The system must maintain records of all onboard training and familiarization activities including safety induction for new joiners. Training completion rates must be reported as a KPI per vessel for SMS performance monitoring. Certificate records must be verifiable against issuing authority databases where API integration is available, such as Indian DG Shipping CDC verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R009	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Port State Control Inspection Preparedness	The system must support port state control (PSC) inspection preparation for vessels calling at ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India under the respective memoranda of understanding (Indian Ocean MOU, Riyadh MOU). The system must maintain a PSC readiness checklist per vessel, tracking the status of commonly inspected items including certificates validity, crew documentation, safety equipment condition, MARPOL compliance, and ISM SMS implementation evidence. Pre-arrival PSC preparation reports must be generated automatically 48 hours before scheduled port arrival, highlighting any items requiring attention. Historical PSC inspection results must be recorded with deficiency codes per IMO harmonized system, corrective actions taken, and verification of closure. The system must analyze PSC deficiency trends across the fleet and compare against regional PSC detention statistics to identify areas of elevated risk requiring management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R010	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Safety Meeting Minutes and Action Tracking	The system must manage onboard safety committee meeting records as required by ISM Code and flag state regulations. Safety meeting templates must include standard agenda items covering review of previous meeting actions, accident and near-miss review, safety observation reports, maintenance safety concerns, crew welfare items, and upcoming drills. Meeting minutes must be recorded digitally with attendance registration, discussion summaries, and action items with assigned responsible persons and deadlines. The system must track action item completion with overdue alerts escalated to the vessel master and shore-based technical superintendent. Safety meeting frequency compliance must be monitored ensuring minimum monthly meetings are held per vessel. Meeting records must be accessible to shore-based management in real-time when vessel connectivity permits, with offline storage and batch synchronization when operating between ports. Meeting templates and action tracking must support both English and Arabic input.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R011	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	MARPOL Compliance Documentation Tracking	The system must track compliance with MARPOL Annexes I through VI documentation requirements for each vessel. This includes Oil Record Book Part I and Part II entries, Garbage Record Book entries, Ballast Water Record Book entries, and Ozone Depleting Substances Record Book entries. The system must validate that entries are made at required frequencies and that disposal operations comply with discharge criteria for the vessel operating area including the Persian Gulf Special Area under MARPOL Annex I and Annex V. Bunker Delivery Notes must be recorded with sulphur content verification for MARPOL Annex VI compliance, particularly important for vessels trading in emission control adjacent areas. The system must generate alerts when record books approach capacity for replacement. IOPP Certificate, IAPP Certificate, and BWM Certificate validity must be tracked alongside SMC certificates with coordinated renewal scheduling.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R012	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Vetting Inspection Management for Tanker Operations	The system must manage vetting inspection preparation and results tracking for container vessels that may carry dangerous goods or for any tanker vessels in the fleet. The system must support SIRE 2.0 (Ship Inspection Report Programme) inspection frameworks with configurable questionnaire libraries. Pre-vetting preparation checklists must be generated based on the inspecting organization requirements (OCIMF, CDI, or charterer-specific). Inspection findings must be recorded with photographic evidence, corrective action plans, and closure verification. The system must calculate vessel vetting readiness scores based on outstanding findings and historical performance. Vetting inspection schedules must be coordinated with commercial vessel scheduling to ensure inspections occur at operationally convenient ports. Vetting performance reports must be available for commercial chartering teams to support vessel marketing efforts to oil major charterers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R013	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Continuous Improvement and KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive safety and quality KPI dashboard supporting ISM Code Section 1.2.2.2 requirement for continuous improvement. KPIs must include lost time injury frequency rate (LTIFR), total recordable case frequency (TRCF), near-miss reporting rate per vessel per month, PSC deficiency rate, PSC detention rate, internal audit non-conformity closure rate, overdue maintenance percentage, drill completion rate, and crew training compliance rate. KPI targets must be configurable per entity and per vessel category. The dashboard must show trend analysis over rolling twelve-month and thirty-six-month periods with red-amber-green status indicators against targets. KPI data must be presented in both English and Arabic dashboard views with proper RTL chart rendering for Arabic users. Automated monthly KPI reports must be generated and distributed to company management, DPA, and vessel masters as input for management review meetings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R014	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	External Audit Preparation and Follow-Up	The system must support preparation for external ISM audits conducted by flag state administrations and recognized organizations (classification societies) for DOC and SMC certification. Pre-audit preparation workflows must generate a comprehensive readiness assessment covering all ISM Code sections with evidence compilation for each requirement. The system must create an audit evidence pack including current SMS procedures, recent management review minutes, internal audit reports, non-conformity registers, accident investigation records, maintenance records, drill records, and crew competency documentation. During external audits, findings must be recorded in real-time with classification as major NC, NC, or observation. Post-audit corrective action plans must be submitted to the auditing body within agreed timeframes, tracked through the non-conformity management system with DPA oversight. The system must maintain external audit history per entity DOC and per vessel SMC for trend analysis and certification risk assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R015	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Master Review and Override Authorization	The system must implement the ISM Code Section 5.2 requirement establishing the master's overriding authority on safety matters. The system must provide a formal mechanism for vessel masters to document decisions to override SMS procedures in the interest of safety, capturing the circumstances, the procedure overridden, the alternative action taken, the safety justification, and subsequent reporting to the DPA. Master override events must trigger immediate notification to the DPA and company management. The system must ensure that no commercial pressure can be applied through the system to override the master's safety decisions, with system-enforced restrictions preventing commercial department users from modifying or countermanding master safety override records. All master override events must be reviewed in the next management review meeting. The override log must support Arabic and English documentation with proper RTL formatting for Arabic narrative entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-003-R016	FEAT-019-2-003	MOD-019	Designated Person Ashore Dashboard and Notifications	The system must provide a dedicated dashboard for the Designated Person Ashore (DPA) as defined in ISM Code Section 4, consolidating all safety-critical information requiring DPA attention. The dashboard must display overdue non-conformity corrective actions, upcoming certificate expiry dates, overdue drills, recent accident and incident reports, PSC inspection results, master override events, and critical equipment failure notifications. DPA notifications must be delivered via in-system alerts, email, and SMS for urgent safety matters. The system must support DPA delegation to a deputy DPA during absence periods with proper handover documentation. DPA activity logs must record all actions taken in response to safety notifications for audit trail purposes. The dashboard must provide fleet-wide visibility across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with entity-level filtering capabilities and must render correctly in both English and Arabic interface modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R001	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Sensor Data Ingestion and Normalization Pipeline	The system must ingest real-time and near-real-time sensor data from vessel machinery monitoring systems including main engine, auxiliary engines, boilers, turbochargers, purifiers, compressors, and deck machinery. Data ingestion must support industry-standard protocols including NMEA 2000, Modbus TCP, OPC-UA, and proprietary engine maker interfaces (MAN, Wartsila, Caterpillar). The ingestion pipeline must normalize sensor readings to standard units, handle missing data points through interpolation, detect and flag anomalous readings caused by sensor malfunction versus genuine equipment issues, and timestamp all data in UTC. Data must be buffered onboard when vessel connectivity is unavailable between ports such as Hamad Port and JNPT, with automatic batch upload when satellite or port connectivity is restored. The system must process minimum 10,000 data points per vessel per hour across all monitored parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R002	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Equipment Digital Twin Model Management	The system must maintain digital twin models for critical vessel equipment including main engine, auxiliary engines, turbochargers, exhaust gas boilers, and cargo handling systems. Each digital twin must incorporate the equipment manufacturer specifications, performance curves, degradation models based on operating hours and conditions, and historical maintenance and failure data. Digital twin models must be calibrated using the first 90 days of operational data from each vessel joining the fleet. The system must update digital twin parameters continuously as new operational data is received, adjusting predicted performance baselines for equipment aging. Digital twin accuracy must be validated quarterly against actual equipment condition assessments performed during maintenance activities. The system must support equipment-specific digital twin templates for common machinery types across the fleet, reducing model setup time for new vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R003	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Failure Prediction Algorithm for Main Propulsion	The system must implement machine learning algorithms to predict main engine and propulsion system failures with a minimum lead time of 14 days before predicted failure occurrence. Prediction models must analyze exhaust gas temperatures per cylinder, turbocharger RPM and vibration, scavenge air pressure and temperature, fuel oil consumption rate deviations, cylinder liner wear rates, crankcase oil mist detector trends, main bearing temperature trends, and torsional vibration patterns. The prediction engine must classify failure probability on a five-level scale from normal operation through watch, warning, alarm, to critical. Each prediction must include the estimated time to failure window, confidence level percentage, recommended maintenance action, and estimated cost impact of failure versus planned intervention. Prediction models must account for varying operating conditions between Arabian Gulf high ambient temperatures and monsoon season sea states on the India coast approach to JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R004	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Auxiliary Systems Predictive Monitoring	The system must provide predictive maintenance monitoring for auxiliary vessel systems including auxiliary engines and generators, boilers, air compressors, freshwater generators, sewage treatment plants, oily water separators, refrigeration systems, and HVAC systems. Each auxiliary system must have defined monitoring parameters with normal operating ranges, trend analysis algorithms, and failure prediction models trained on fleet-wide historical data. The system must correlate auxiliary system performance with environmental conditions, distinguishing between performance degradation caused by equipment wear versus external factors such as high seawater temperatures in the Arabian Gulf during summer months exceeding 35 degrees Celsius. Predictive alerts for auxiliary systems must be integrated with the planned maintenance system from Part 1 of this module, automatically generating recommended work orders with suggested scheduling windows aligned to vessel port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R005	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Vibration Analysis and Trending Engine	The system must process vibration data from permanently installed accelerometers and periodic portable vibration measurements on rotating machinery. Vibration analysis must support frequency domain analysis (FFT), time waveform analysis, and envelope analysis to identify specific fault types including imbalance, misalignment, bearing defects, gear mesh problems, and structural looseness. The system must maintain vibration trend databases per measurement point with automatic baseline establishment and alarm threshold setting using ISO 10816 and ISO 20816 standards as reference. Trend analysis must detect gradual degradation patterns and predict the time window when vibration levels will exceed alarm thresholds or class society acceptable limits. Vibration analysis reports must be generated in formats acceptable to classification societies (DNV, Lloyd's, Bureau Veritas) for condition-based class survey extensions. The system must support vibration route data upload from portable analyzers used during vessel port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R006	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Lubricating Oil Condition Monitoring Integration	The system must integrate lubricating oil analysis results from laboratory testing and onboard test kits to support predictive maintenance decisions. Oil analysis parameters must include viscosity, total base number (TBN), total acid number (TAN), water content, insolubles, wear metal analysis (iron, copper, lead, tin, aluminum, chromium, nickel), and particle count. The system must trend oil analysis results per equipment item, comparing against OEM condemning limits and fleet-specific experience-based limits. Abnormal oil analysis results must automatically trigger predictive maintenance alerts correlated with other sensor data from the same equipment to improve diagnosis accuracy. The system must recommend oil change intervals based on condition rather than fixed time periods, optimizing lubricating oil consumption and reducing environmental waste. Oil analysis data must be linked to lubricant procurement records to track lubricant brand and grade performance across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R007	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Thermographic Analysis Data Processing	The system must process thermal imaging data from routine thermographic surveys of electrical switchboards, main engine components, boiler refractory, and cargo hold structures. Thermal images must be stored with metadata including equipment identification, survey date, ambient temperature, emissivity setting, and surveyor identification. The system must perform automated hot-spot detection comparing thermal images against baseline reference images, identifying temperature anomalies exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10 degrees Celsius above reference baseline). Trend analysis must track temperature profiles of identified hot spots over time to predict failure progression. Thermographic survey scheduling must be integrated with the planned maintenance system with recommended survey frequencies per equipment type. Survey reports must be generated in classification society acceptable formats supporting condition-based maintenance survey credits. The system must support Arabic annotations on thermographic reports for regional maintenance teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R008	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Hull and Propeller Performance Monitoring	The system must monitor hull and propeller performance degradation through analysis of vessel speed, power consumption, draft, trim, sea state, wind conditions, and current data. The system must calculate hull fouling indicators by comparing actual vessel performance against the clean hull baseline established from sea trial data or last dry-docking, normalized for environmental conditions. Propeller performance analysis must detect efficiency losses indicating propeller roughness or damage. The system must predict optimal hull cleaning intervals and recommend in-water cleaning at specific ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port) based on cost-benefit analysis comparing cleaning cost in local currency versus fuel penalty from continued fouling. Hull performance reports must calculate the fuel cost impact of fouling in USD per day, supporting dry-docking timing decisions. The system must account for seasonal variations in marine growth rates between Arabian Gulf warm waters and Indian Ocean monsoon conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R009	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Predictive Maintenance Scheduling Optimizer	The system must optimize maintenance scheduling by combining AI-predicted maintenance needs with vessel operational schedules, port call itineraries, spare parts availability, and service engineer availability. The optimizer must consider maintenance windows available at each port of call (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT) based on port stay duration, alongside available shore-based maintenance support at each location. Maintenance activities must be grouped to maximize work completion during available port stay windows, reducing the need for separate dedicated maintenance port calls. The optimizer must calculate the cost impact of deferring predicted maintenance activities versus performing them at the next available opportunity, factoring in failure probability progression, potential off-hire costs in vessel charter currency, and spare parts logistics costs. Optimization results must be presented as recommended maintenance plans with alternative scheduling scenarios for fleet manager decision-making.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R010	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Fleet-Wide Failure Pattern Recognition	The system must analyze equipment failure data across the entire fleet to identify common failure patterns, systematic defects, and emerging reliability issues affecting specific equipment types, manufacturers, or models. Pattern recognition must correlate failures with equipment age, operating hours, maintenance history, operating environment (Arabian Gulf high-temperature operations versus Indian Ocean monsoon conditions), and crew experience levels. When a failure pattern is identified across multiple vessels, the system must generate a fleet-wide alert recommending preventive inspection or maintenance for all vessels with the same equipment type. The system must maintain a fleet reliability database tracking mean time between failures (MTBF) and mean time to repair (MTTR) per equipment category. Failure pattern reports must support root cause categorization (design defect, material defect, maintenance error, operational error, environmental factor) for manufacturer feedback and warranty claims processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R011	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	AI Model Training and Continuous Learning	The system must support continuous learning and retraining of predictive maintenance AI models as new operational and failure data becomes available. Model training must be performed on aggregated fleet data with privacy controls ensuring vessel-specific data attribution is maintained per entity ownership (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India entities). The system must track model performance metrics including prediction accuracy, false positive rate, false negative rate, and average lead time of valid predictions. Model retraining must be triggered automatically when prediction accuracy drops below configurable thresholds (default 85 percent accuracy). The system must support A/B testing of new model versions against production models before full deployment. Model version history must be maintained with performance comparison reports. All AI model decisions must be explainable, providing human-readable justification for each predictive maintenance recommendation to support superintendent review and approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R012	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Condition-Based Class Survey Support	The system must generate condition monitoring reports formatted to support applications for condition-based class survey extensions from classification societies (DNV Predictive Vessel program, Lloyd's Register SHCM, Bureau Veritas VCBM). Reports must compile equipment condition data including vibration trends, oil analysis trends, thermographic survey results, performance monitoring data, and predictive maintenance model outputs into classification society required formats. The system must track the status of condition-based survey applications per vessel including application submission date, class approval status, approved survey extension period, and conditions attached to the approval. Cost savings from condition-based survey extensions must be calculated per vessel comparing standard dry-docking costs versus extended interval costs, reported in the vessel operating currency and USD. The system must alert management when conditions attached to survey extensions require specific monitoring actions to maintain the approval validity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R013	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Environmental Operating Condition Correlation	The system must correlate equipment performance and degradation rates with environmental operating conditions specific to the vessel trading routes. Environmental parameters must include sea water temperature (critical for Arabian Gulf operations where temperatures exceed 35 degrees Celsius in summer), ambient air temperature, humidity, sea state, and port-specific conditions such as high dust and sand exposure at Middle Eastern ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port). The system must adjust predictive maintenance model parameters based on seasonal operating profiles, recognizing that equipment stress profiles differ significantly between summer Gulf operations and monsoon season Indian Ocean transits. Environmental condition data must be sourced from vessel weather routing systems, port meteorological services, and onboard environmental sensors. Correlation analysis must be presented in fleet performance reports showing the impact of operating environment on equipment reliability and maintenance costs across different trading areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R014	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Predictive Maintenance Cost-Benefit Analysis Engine	The system must provide a cost-benefit analysis engine that quantifies the financial impact of predictive maintenance recommendations. For each predicted maintenance action, the system must calculate the estimated cost of planned intervention including parts cost, service engineer cost, and vessel operational impact, versus the estimated cost of failure including emergency repair cost, potential off-hire cost at the vessel charter rate, port delay costs, potential cargo claims, and regulatory penalty exposure from port state control detentions. Cost calculations must be performed in the vessel operating entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalents for fleet-level analysis. The system must maintain historical accuracy metrics comparing predicted cost impacts against actual costs incurred for both planned interventions and failures, enabling continuous calibration of cost estimation models. Annual cost avoidance reports must quantify the financial benefit of the predictive maintenance program per vessel and fleet-wide.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R015	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Predictive Maintenance Alert Notification and Escalation	The system must implement a multi-tier alert notification and escalation framework for predictive maintenance findings. Alert severity levels must include informational (normal degradation trending), advisory (approaching maintenance threshold within 30 days), warning (maintenance required within 14 days), and critical (imminent failure risk within 7 days). Each alert level must have configurable notification recipients: informational alerts to vessel chief engineer only, advisory alerts to chief engineer and technical superintendent, warning alerts adding the fleet manager, and critical alerts adding the DPA and operations director. Notifications must be delivered via in-system dashboard, email, and SMS for warning and critical levels. The system must track alert acknowledgment and response actions taken by each recipient. Unacknowledged critical alerts must auto-escalate to the next management level after configurable timeout periods. Alert history must be maintained per vessel for trend analysis and management review reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R007	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Multi-Entity Crew Pool Management	The system must maintain a unified crew pool across all operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India) while preserving entity-level data segregation for regulatory and financial reporting purposes. Each seafarer record must be associated with a primary employing entity but must be available for cross-entity assignment subject to configurable business rules. The system must track entity-specific employment contracts, social insurance obligations (GOSI for KSA, WPS for UAE, QNSS considerations for Qatar, PF/ESI for India), and tax implications of cross-entity assignments. The AI engine must optimize crew allocation across entities to minimize mobilization costs, reduce travel distances, and balance crew utilization rates while respecting nationality and visa constraints specific to each Gulf Cooperation Council member state and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R016	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Predictive Maintenance Reporting and Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive predictive maintenance analytics dashboard presenting fleet-wide equipment health status, prediction accuracy metrics, cost avoidance calculations, and maintenance optimization KPIs. The dashboard must display equipment health scores per vessel on a fleet map overlay showing vessel positions relative to ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT). Drill-down capability must allow navigation from fleet overview to individual vessel, to equipment group, to individual equipment item with full sensor data history and prediction timeline visualization. The dashboard must present financial analytics showing predictive maintenance program ROI per entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated USD. Comparative analytics must show planned versus unplanned maintenance ratios, prediction accuracy trends over rolling twelve-month periods, and equipment reliability improvements attributed to predictive maintenance interventions. All dashboard components must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with proper Hijri calendar date display options for KSA entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-019-2-004-R017	FEAT-019-2-004	MOD-019	Integration with OEM Remote Monitoring Services	The system must integrate with original equipment manufacturer (OEM) remote monitoring and diagnostic services for major machinery including main engine makers (MAN Energy Solutions, Wartsila, Caterpillar), turbocharger makers (ABB, MHI), and navigation equipment providers. Integration must support bidirectional data exchange where vessel sensor data is shared with OEM monitoring centers and OEM diagnostic assessments and recommendations are received back into the predictive maintenance system. OEM recommendations must be automatically cross-referenced with the system internal predictions to provide corroborating or conflicting analysis for superintendent decision-making. Data sharing agreements must be configurable per entity to comply with data sovereignty requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must track OEM service agreement coverage levels to ensure remote monitoring services are utilized within contractual entitlements. API integration must support secure data transmission protocols with data encryption in transit and at rest meeting entity-specific cybersecurity requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R001	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Vessel Manning Plan Configuration	The system must allow creation and maintenance of vessel-specific manning plans that define the minimum and optimal crew complement for each vessel in the fleet. Each manning plan must specify required ranks (Master, Chief Officer, Second Officer, Third Officer, Chief Engineer, Second Engineer, Third Engineer, Bosun, AB, OS, Oiler, Cook, etc.), the number of positions per rank, required certifications per position, and nationality restrictions if imposed by the flag state. Manning plans must support vessels registered under Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian flags, and must be version-controlled so that historical manning configurations can be audited. The AI engine must flag any vessel operating below minimum safe manning levels as defined by the flag state Administration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R002	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Rotation Cycle Planning Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven rotation cycle planning engine that automatically calculates crew change dates based on configurable contract durations per rank and nationality. Standard rotation cycles must support 4-month, 6-month, 8-month, and 9-month durations with configurable extension allowances up to a maximum defined by the MLC 2006 (11 months). The engine must factor in vessel itinerary data from the voyage management module to identify optimal crew change ports among Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHA). The system must generate rotation forecasts at least 90 days in advance and automatically trigger crew sourcing workflows when a planned rotation date approaches the 60-day threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R003	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Assignment & Nomination Workflow	The system must implement a multi-step crew assignment workflow comprising nomination, approval, and confirmation stages. When a rotation vacancy is identified, the AI engine must automatically shortlist eligible seafarers from the crew pool based on rank, certification validity, medical fitness, nationality, contract status, and rest period compliance. The nomination must be reviewed by the Crewing Superintendent and approved by the Fleet Manager before confirmation. The workflow must support multi-entity operations where crew may be employed by the Qatar entity but assigned to a vessel managed by the UAE or KSA entity. All workflow steps must be captured in an audit trail with timestamps, approver identity, and any override justifications. Arabic and English bilingual notifications must be sent at each workflow transition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R004	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Change Port Logistics Coordination	The system must manage all logistical arrangements for crew changes at designated ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. This includes travel booking requests (flights, ground transport), hotel accommodation for transit crew, port pass and security clearance applications, launch boat arrangements for anchorage crew changes, and immigration document preparation. The system must integrate with the port call module to synchronize crew change activities with vessel arrival and departure schedules. For each crew change event, the system must generate a crew change checklist with estimated costs in the local currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Khalifa and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) and equivalent USD amounts using the prevailing exchange rate from the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R005	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	AI-Powered Crew Availability Forecasting	The system must provide AI-powered forecasting of crew availability across the entire seafarer pool, projecting availability at least 180 days forward. The forecast must account for current onboard assignments and their expected end dates, mandatory rest periods between contracts (minimum as per MLC 2006), pending training or certification renewal periods, approved leave periods, medical hold statuses, and visa processing lead times for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The AI model must identify potential crew shortages by rank and department at least 90 days before they materialize and recommend mitigation actions such as contract extensions, accelerated recruitment, or inter-entity crew sharing. The forecast dashboard must display data in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R006	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Minimum Rest Hours Compliance Tracking	The system must track and enforce compliance with STCW rest hour requirements and MLC 2006 provisions for each crew member onboard. The minimum rest periods of 10 hours in any 24-hour period and 77 hours in any 7-day period must be continuously monitored using work/rest hour data submitted from vessels. The system must support both the standard two-watch and three-watch systems and account for exceptional circumstances such as emergency drills, port state inspections, and cargo operations at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Non-compliance instances must be flagged immediately to the vessel Master and shore-side Crew Manager with severity classification. The AI engine must analyze patterns of non-compliance and recommend crew augmentation or watch schedule adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R008	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Travel Document & Visa Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of all travel documents for each seafarer including passport, seaman's book, continuous discharge certificate (CDC), and country-specific visas. For each document, the system must track document number, issuing authority, issue date, expiry date, and scanned copy. The system must automatically calculate visa requirements based on the vessel's planned itinerary and crew nationality, specifically for Qatar business visa, UAE seafarer visa, KSA transit visa, and Indian e-visa requirements. Expiry alerts must be generated at 180, 90, 60, and 30 days before document expiration. The AI engine must identify crew members who cannot be assigned to specific trade routes due to visa restrictions and suggest alternative assignments. All document data must be searchable in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R009	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Change Budget Planning & Cost Tracking	The system must support annual and quarterly crew change budget planning with actual cost tracking at the vessel, fleet, and entity level. Budget line items must include flight costs, hotel accommodation, ground transportation, port agent fees, launch boat charges, visa fees, and medical examination costs. Budgets must be maintained in the vessel's operating currency and consolidated in USD for group reporting, with support for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR at both budget and actual exchange rates. The system must track budget variance by vessel and flag deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10%). The AI engine must analyze historical crew change cost data to optimize future crew change port selection, recommending the most cost-effective crew change locations along planned vessel routes through the Arabian Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R010	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Offline Crew Planning Synchronization	The system must support offline crew planning operations for scenarios where shore-side connectivity is unavailable or when vessel-based planning is performed in areas with limited satellite communication bandwidth. Offline mode must allow viewing of the current crew list, upcoming rotation dates, crew document summaries, and planned crew changes. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline changes with the central database using conflict resolution rules that prioritize shore-side entries for administrative data and vessel-side entries for operational data such as work/rest hours. The synchronization engine must handle concurrent edits by multiple users across different time zones spanning the Gulf region (AST/GST UTC+3/+4) and Indian Standard Time (IST UTC+5:30) and present conflicts for manual resolution with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R011	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Performance Rating & History	The system must maintain a comprehensive performance history for each seafarer across all contracts and vessel assignments. Performance ratings must be captured at the end of each contract using a configurable rating matrix covering technical competence, safety awareness, teamwork, leadership (for officers), and English/Arabic language proficiency. Ratings must be submitted by the vessel Master and department head (Chief Officer for deck, Chief Engineer for engine) and reviewed by the shore-side Crewing Superintendent. The AI engine must calculate a composite performance score and rank seafarers within their peer group by rank and department. Performance history must influence the AI crew nomination algorithm, with higher-rated seafarers prioritized for premium vessel assignments. Performance data must be accessible in both Arabic and English with RTL layout support for Arabic-language reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R012	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Embarkation & Disembarkation Processing	The system must manage the embarkation and disembarkation process for each crew member with status tracking through all stages: travel initiated, arrived at port, security clearance obtained, boarded vessel, and signed on articles (for embarkation) or signed off articles, cleared vessel, exited port, and travel completed (for disembarkation). Each stage transition must be timestamped and attributed to the responsible user. The system must generate the required crew change documentation including sign-on/sign-off reports, crew list updates for port authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and immigration notification forms. The system must support simultaneous processing of joining and departing crew members during a crew change window and calculate handover overlap periods. All forms must be available in English and Arabic bilingual format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R013	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Succession & Emergency Replacement Planning	The system must maintain succession plans for all critical positions onboard each vessel (Master, Chief Officer, Chief Engineer, Second Engineer) and generate emergency replacement plans that can be activated when unplanned crew changes occur due to medical emergencies, disciplinary actions, or personal reasons. The AI engine must identify the nearest available qualified replacement seafarer considering their current location, travel document readiness, certification validity, and mobilization time to the vessel's current or next port of call within the Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT network. Emergency replacement activation must trigger expedited workflows with reduced approval chains and automated travel booking requests. The system must maintain a standby crew register per entity with guaranteed response times and track standby crew costs in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR based on the employing entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R014	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Contract Duration & Extension Management	The system must track individual crew contracts with defined start dates, planned end dates, and maximum allowable duration per MLC 2006 (not exceeding 11 months plus one month repatriation). Contract extensions must be managed through a formal approval workflow requiring justification, seafarer consent confirmation, and flag state notification if applicable. The system must enforce hard limits on maximum contract duration and generate warnings at configurable intervals (30, 15, and 7 days before planned end date). When contracts approach the MLC maximum, the system must automatically escalate to the DPA (Designated Person Ashore) and block further extensions. Contract tracking must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date displays, and contract values must be stored in the seafarer's wage currency with conversion to the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R001	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Manning Agency Master Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of all manning agencies with fields for agency name (English and Arabic), license number, licensing authority (e.g., QMAA for Qatar, UAE FTA for Dubai, DG Shipping India for JNPT-region agencies), license validity dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, registered address, communication details, and principal officer information. Each agency record must support multi-entity tagging so that a single agency can be linked to Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India operating entities simultaneously. The registry must enforce uniqueness on license number per jurisdiction and flag agencies whose licenses expire within 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R015	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Planning Dashboard & KPI Reporting	The system must provide a real-time crew planning dashboard displaying key performance indicators including fleet manning status (percentage of positions filled vs. plan), average days onboard by rank, crew change compliance rate (on-time vs. delayed), crew cost per vessel per day in USD with drill-down to local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), nationality mix compliance, and upcoming crew change workload by port. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel, fleet segment, and time period. AI-generated insights must highlight anomalies such as vessels with unusually high crew turnover, ranks with chronic shortage patterns, or ports with consistently delayed crew changes. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts and support data export in Excel and PDF formats with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R016	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Crew Nationality & Diversity Compliance	The system must enforce flag state and company-specific nationality requirements for crew composition. Certain flag states mandate minimum percentages of nationals among the crew, and the system must track and enforce these quotas at the vessel level. For vessels operating under Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian flags, the system must maintain configurable nationality rules including minimum local national percentages, restricted nationalities, and preferred nationality mixes. The AI engine must validate crew nominations against nationality rules before allowing assignment approval and flag non-compliant nominations with specific rule violations. The system must generate nationality diversity reports per vessel and fleet, track trends over time, and alert when planned crew changes would result in non-compliance. Reports must display nationality names in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-001-R017	FEAT-020-1-001	MOD-020	Integration with Manning Agency Systems	The system must support integration with external manning agencies used for crew sourcing across multiple geographies. The integration must support electronic exchange of crew requisitions (specifying rank, certifications, experience requirements, and contract terms), candidate profiles with document scans, interview scheduling, and selection confirmation. The system must maintain a registry of approved manning agencies per entity and per flag state, track agency performance metrics (response time, candidate quality scores, document accuracy), and enforce usage of approved agencies only. API-based integration must support JSON and XML message formats, with asynchronous processing for agencies in regions with intermittent connectivity. Agency invoices must be processed in the agency's local currency with conversion to the employing entity's currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) for cost allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R001	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	STCW Certificate Registry & Master Data	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of all STCW certificates and competencies required for each rank and vessel type in the fleet. The registry must include Certificates of Competency (CoC) for deck and engine officers at all levels (Management, Operational, Support), Certificates of Proficiency (CoP) for ratings, and all mandatory short courses including Basic Safety Training (BST), Advanced Firefighting (AFF), Medical First Aid (MFA), Medical Care (MC), Proficiency in Survival Craft (PSC), Security Awareness (SA), and Ship Security Officer (SSO). Each certificate type must be configurable with validity periods, renewal requirements, flag state endorsement requirements, and prerequisite certificates. The registry must support certificates issued by Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, and all other major flag state administrations recognized under STCW.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R002	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Individual Seafarer Certificate Portfolio Management	The system must maintain a complete digital certificate portfolio for each seafarer, storing certificate type, certificate number, issuing authority and country, date of issue, date of expiry, revalidation date (if different from expiry), and high-resolution scanned copies of original certificates. Each certificate entry must track verification status (unverified, verification requested, verified, verification failed) with the date and method of verification. The system must support bulk upload of certificate scans via mobile capture with AI-powered OCR extraction of certificate details from scanned images in both English and Arabic. Duplicate certificate detection must prevent the same certificate from being recorded multiple times. The portfolio must calculate an overall compliance score per seafarer indicating the percentage of required certificates that are valid and current.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R003	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Flag State Endorsement Tracking & Management	The system must track flag state endorsements (also known as Recognition or Confirmation of Certificates) for each Certificate of Competency held by officers. For vessels in the fleet registered under Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian flags, the system must maintain the specific endorsement requirements of each flag state administration. The system must track endorsement application status through stages: not applied, application submitted, under review, approved, issued, and rejected. The AI engine must automatically identify when a seafarer requires a new flag state endorsement based on vessel assignment and trigger the application process at least 90 days before the planned embarkation date. Processing time estimates must be maintained per flag state and factored into crew planning. The system must alert when an endorsement is about to expire and block assignment to vessels of that flag if no valid endorsement exists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R004	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Certificate Expiry Monitoring & Renewal Workflow	The system must implement a multi-tier certificate expiry monitoring system that generates alerts at 180, 120, 90, 60, and 30 days before certificate expiration. Alerts must be directed to the seafarer, the assigned Crewing Officer, and the Training Superintendent via in-app notifications and email in the user's preferred language (English or Arabic). When a certificate approaches the 120-day threshold, the system must automatically initiate a renewal workflow that includes identifying approved training centers in the seafarer's home country or current port region (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), estimating renewal costs in local currency, and scheduling the training slot to align with the seafarer's next rotation. The AI engine must prioritize renewal of certificates critical to the seafarer's current or planned assignment and group multiple renewals together to minimize training downtime and travel costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R003	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Crew Supply Agreement Contract Management	The system must store and manage crew supply agreements (CSAs) between the shipping company entities and manning agencies, including contract effective and expiry dates in Gregorian and Hijri formats, crew categories covered (officers, ratings, cadets), agreed supply lead times per rank, commission rates per crew rank in the contract currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), penalty clauses for late supply or documentation errors, and auto-renewal terms. Each CSA must be linked to the specific legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India) that is the contracting party. The system must alert contract managers 60 days before CSA expiry and AI must recommend renewal or replacement based on agency performance data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R005	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	STCW Competency Matrix Validation	The system must validate each seafarer's certificate portfolio against the STCW competency matrix for their assigned rank and vessel type. The competency matrix must define all required certificates, endorsements, and sea service requirements for each position on each vessel type (bulk carrier, container vessel, tanker, general cargo). The validation engine must identify gaps between a seafarer's current qualifications and the requirements for their assigned or nominated position and classify gaps as critical (prevents assignment), warning (assignment possible with flag state dispensation), or advisory (recommended but not mandatory). Validation must run automatically during the crew nomination workflow and produce a compliance report that can be presented to port state control inspectors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. The report must be generated in English with Arabic translation available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R006	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Sea Service Record Tracking for Certificate Eligibility	The system must maintain detailed sea service records for each seafarer tracking vessel name, IMO number, flag state, vessel type and gross tonnage, rank held, sign-on date, sign-off date, and total qualifying sea service days. Sea service records must be automatically populated from embarkation and disembarkation events processed in the crew planning module. The system must calculate accumulated qualifying sea service towards the next level Certificate of Competency as defined by STCW requirements (e.g., 12 months qualifying sea service for Second Officer to Chief Officer upgrade, 36 months for Officer of the Watch certification). The AI engine must identify seafarers approaching eligibility thresholds for certificate upgrades and recommend them for promotion pathways. Sea service records must distinguish between near-coastal and unlimited voyage service, and between different vessel type categories for tanker endorsement eligibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R007	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Training Needs Analysis & Planning	The system must perform AI-driven training needs analysis across the entire crew pool by comparing current certificate portfolios against fleet requirements, upcoming regulatory changes, and company-specific training standards. The analysis must identify training gaps at individual, vessel, fleet, and entity levels and generate prioritized training plans. Training plans must consider budget constraints per entity (in QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), training center availability in the Gulf region and Indian subcontinent, seafarer availability windows between contracts, and certificate renewal urgency. The system must maintain a catalog of approved training centers per geography with course schedules, fees in local currency, and quality ratings. Training plans must be reviewed and approved by the Training Superintendent and DPA before execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R008	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Certificate Verification & Authentication	The system must support verification of seafarer certificates through multiple channels including direct verification with issuing authorities, STCW Information System (STCW-IS) database queries where available, and manual verification by document reviewers. The verification workflow must track verification request date, method used, response received, verification result (authentic, fraudulent, inconclusive), and verifier identity. For certificates issued by Qatar Maritime Administration, UAE Federal Transport Authority, KSA Transport General Authority, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping, the system must support direct API integration where available. Fraudulent certificate detection must trigger immediate alert to the Crewing Manager, DPA, and Legal department with automatic suspension of the seafarer's assignment eligibility. The system must maintain a verification audit trail compliant with ISM Code requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R009	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Dispensation & Exemption Management	The system must manage flag state dispensations and exemptions where a seafarer may serve in a capacity for which they do not hold the full required certification. Dispensation requests must be tracked through a formal workflow including justification, risk assessment, flag state application, approval or rejection, and validity period. The system must enforce STCW Regulation I/10 limits on dispensations (not exceeding 6 months, not for Master or Chief Engineer on vessels over certain tonnage thresholds). Active dispensations must be prominently displayed on the vessel's crew compliance dashboard and included in port state control preparation reports for inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. The system must generate expiry alerts for dispensations at 60, 30, and 15 days before expiration and automatically initiate certification completion workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R010	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Medical Certificate & Fitness Tracking	The system must track medical fitness certificates for all seafarers as required by STCW Regulation I/9 and MLC 2006 Standard A1.2. Medical certificates must include the issuing medical practitioner details, examination date, fitness classification (fit, fit with restrictions, temporarily unfit, permanently unfit), any restrictions or limitations, and validity period (maximum 2 years, 1 year for seafarers under 18). The system must track approved medical examination centers in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India with their accreditation status and fee schedules in local currency. Medical fitness status must be validated as a prerequisite in the crew assignment workflow, and seafarers with expired medical certificates must be blocked from embarkation. The AI engine must schedule medical examinations to align with certificate renewal and rotation planning to minimize separate trips.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R011	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Drug & Alcohol Testing Record Management	The system must maintain records of drug and alcohol testing for all seafarers in compliance with company policy, flag state requirements, and Oil Companies International Marine Forum (OCIMF) standards for vessels operating in the tanker trade. Testing records must include test date, test type (pre-employment, random, post-incident, reasonable cause), testing laboratory and accreditation, substances tested, results (negative, positive with substance identified, inconclusive), and chain of custody documentation. The system must support scheduling of random tests with AI-generated randomized selection ensuring statistical validity and non-discrimination. Positive test results must trigger immediate workflow notifications to the Master, DPA, and HR department with automatic suspension of the seafarer's active assignment eligibility. Testing frequency must comply with requirements of port states in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R012	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Competency Assessment & Onboard Training Records	The system must support recording and tracking of onboard competency assessments and training activities as required by the ISM Code and STCW Code Section B. Each assessment record must include the assessor identity and qualification, assessment date and location (vessel or shore), competency standard assessed (mapped to STCW Table of Competence), assessment method (practical demonstration, oral examination, simulator, written test), result (competent, not yet competent), and evidence references. The system must maintain onboard training matrices per vessel showing planned vs. completed training activities including emergency drills, equipment familiarization, and bridge/engine resource management exercises. Training completion rates must be reported per vessel and aggregated at fleet and entity level. All records must support bilingual data entry in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R013	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Regulatory Change Impact Assessment	The system must monitor and assess the impact of changes to STCW regulations, flag state requirements, and port state control focus areas on the fleet's crew certification compliance. When a regulatory change is registered in the system (manually or via integration with regulatory update feeds), the AI engine must automatically analyze the change against the current crew pool's certificate portfolios and identify all affected seafarers, vessels, and positions. The impact assessment must quantify the number of affected crew, estimated compliance cost in USD with breakdown by entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), timeline to achieve compliance, and risk exposure if non-compliant. The system must generate a remediation plan with prioritized actions and track implementation progress. Impact assessments must be presented in both English and Arabic with RTL support for Arabic text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R014	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Port State Control Inspection Readiness	The system must generate port state control (PSC) inspection readiness reports for each vessel prior to port calls at Hamad Port (Qatar EMSA/Paris MoU equivalent), Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE Federal Transport Authority under Riyadh MoU), and JNPT (Indian DG Shipping under Indian Ocean MoU). The report must identify any crew certification deficiencies that could result in PSC detention or observations, including expired certificates, missing flag state endorsements, incomplete training records, expired medical certificates, and rest hour non-compliance. The AI engine must assign a risk score to each vessel based on historical PSC performance, current deficiency count, and the inspection targeting criteria of the relevant MoU regime. The system must recommend corrective actions prioritized by detention risk severity and generate the report in the language required by the inspecting authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R015	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Certificate Document Archival & Retrieval	The system must provide secure document archival for all certificate scans, verification records, and training documentation with configurable retention periods per document type (minimum 5 years after seafarer's last contract, or as required by the applicable flag state). Documents must be stored with AES-256 encryption at rest and accessible only to authorized users based on role-based access control. The document management system must support full-text search across document metadata in both Arabic and English, filtering by certificate type, issuing authority, validity status, and seafarer demographics. Bulk export functionality must be available for audit purposes, generating structured ZIP archives with an index file in both English and Arabic. The system must maintain document integrity checksums and flag any unauthorized modifications. Archival storage costs must be allocated to the relevant entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India) based on the seafarer's employing entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R016	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Simulator Training Record Integration	The system must integrate with maritime simulator training centers to capture and record simulator-based training and assessment results. Simulator training records must include simulator type and classification (full mission, multi-task, or part-task as per STCW Section A-I/12), training course name and STCW reference, training dates and duration, instructor details and qualifications, exercises completed with performance scores, and final assessment outcome. The system must support integration with major simulator training providers in the Gulf region (Qatar, UAE) and India via API or structured file import (CSV, XML). Simulator training completion must automatically update the seafarer's competency matrix and count towards revalidation requirements for Certificates of Competency. Training costs must be recorded in the local currency of the training center with conversion to the employing entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R017	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Certificate Compliance Analytics & Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics and reporting on certificate compliance across the fleet and crew pool. Standard reports must include fleet compliance overview (percentage of positions filled with fully compliant crew), certificate expiry forecast by month for the next 12 months, training budget utilization per entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD, flag state endorsement status summary, and PSC deficiency trends by inspection region. The AI engine must generate predictive analytics identifying future compliance risks based on certificate expiry clustering, budget constraints, and training center capacity. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation and distribution in PDF and Excel formats. All reports must support bilingual generation in English and Arabic with appropriate RTL rendering, Hijri calendar date display alongside Gregorian dates, and entity-specific branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-002-R018	FEAT-020-1-002	MOD-020	Offline Certificate Data Access & Sync	The system must provide offline access to critical certificate data for use during vessel inspections or when shore connectivity is unavailable. The offline data package must include crew certificate summaries for all onboard personnel, scanned certificate copies, STCW competency matrix validation results, and the latest PSC readiness report. The offline package must be downloadable as an encrypted file accessible via the vessel's onboard system or a mobile application. Data synchronization upon reconnection must merge any offline annotations or updates (such as inspection outcomes or new certificates received onboard) with the central database using timestamp-based conflict resolution. The offline package must be refreshable at configurable intervals (default every 7 days) and must alert the Master when the offline data is older than 14 days. All offline interfaces must support both English and Arabic display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R001	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Crew Wage Scale Configuration & Management	The system must support configuration and management of crew wage scales per entity, flag state, rank, and vessel type. Each wage scale must define base monthly wage, fixed overtime rate, leave pay accrual rate, guaranteed overtime hours, and any rank-specific allowances (command money for Master/Chief Engineer, refrigeration allowance, tanker allowance, etc.). Wage scales must be version-controlled with effective dates to support historical payroll recalculations. The system must support wage scales in multiple currencies including USD (industry standard for seafarer wages), QAR, AED, SAR, and INR for locally employed crew. CBA (Collective Bargaining Agreement) rates from ITF and national unions must be configurable as minimum benchmarks, and the AI engine must flag any wage scale configured below applicable CBA minimums.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R002	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Monthly Payroll Calculation Engine	The system must calculate monthly crew payroll for all onboard and on-leave seafarers with proration for partial months based on sign-on and sign-off dates. The calculation must include base wage (prorated by days onboard), guaranteed overtime, excess overtime (calculated from approved work/rest hour records), leave pay accrual, seniority increments, performance bonuses as configured, and deductions (allotments, cash advances, slop chest purchases, union dues). The payroll engine must support both 30-day fixed month and calendar month calculation bases as configured per entity. Payroll must be calculated in the seafarer's contract currency (typically USD for international crew) with parallel calculation in the employing entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) using the exchange rate applicable on the payroll date from the treasury module. The AI engine must flag anomalies such as unusual overtime spikes or deductions exceeding configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R003	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Allotment Management & Distribution	The system must manage crew allotment instructions whereby seafarers designate portions of their wages to be remitted to named beneficiaries (typically family members) in their home countries. Each seafarer may configure up to five allotment instructions specifying beneficiary name, relationship, bank name and branch, account number or IBAN, SWIFT/BIC code, remittance currency, and fixed amount or percentage of net wages. The system must process allotment payments monthly after payroll finalization, generating bank remittance files in the format required by the remitting bank. Allotment currencies must support USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, PHP, IDR, BDT, LKR, and other currencies common for seafarer remittances to South and Southeast Asian countries. The system must track remittance confirmation receipts and flag failed transfers for immediate follow-up by the payroll department.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R004	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Leave Pay Accrual & Settlement Processing	The system must calculate and track leave pay accrual for each seafarer based on the applicable leave entitlement per their contract and CBA terms. Standard leave accrual rates must be configurable per entity and rank, with common rates being 8 days per month for officers and 6-8 days for ratings. Leave pay must accrue continuously during the onboard period and be settled either as a lump sum upon sign-off, as monthly payments during the leave period, or as a combination per the seafarer's election. The system must maintain a running leave balance per seafarer showing accrued leave, leave taken, leave paid, and outstanding leave balance in both days and monetary value. Leave pay settlement must be calculated in the contract currency with conversion to the payment currency at the prevailing exchange rate. The AI engine must project leave liability across the fleet for financial reporting and provision calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R005	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Overtime Calculation & Approval Workflow	The system must calculate overtime based on work/rest hour records submitted from vessels and the applicable overtime rate structure from the crew wage scale. Overtime calculation must distinguish between guaranteed overtime (included in the fixed monthly compensation) and excess overtime (compensated at hourly rates). The system must support different overtime rate structures including flat rate, tiered rates (e.g., 1.25x for first 40 excess hours, 1.5x thereafter), and day-type differentiation (weekday, Saturday, Sunday/holiday at different multipliers). Overtime claims must be approved by the vessel Master and verified by the shore-side payroll department before inclusion in the monthly payroll. The AI engine must analyze overtime patterns across the fleet and flag vessels with consistently high overtime claims for review, potentially indicating undermanning or inefficient watch scheduling.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R006	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Multi-Entity Payroll Consolidation & Reporting	The system must support payroll processing independently for each employing entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while providing consolidated payroll reporting at the group level. Entity-level payroll reports must include individual payslips in the entity's base currency, payroll summary by vessel, department, and rank, bank payment instructions in the format required by the entity's banking partner, and statutory deduction reports. Group-level consolidated reports must convert all entity payrolls to USD using the month-end exchange rate and present total crew cost analysis by vessel, fleet, rank, nationality, and entity. The system must support intercompany crew cost recharging when a seafarer employed by one entity serves on a vessel managed by another entity, generating appropriate intercompany invoices in the relevant currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with supporting documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R007	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Tax & Social Insurance Calculation Engine	The system must calculate applicable taxes and social insurance contributions for crew based on the employing entity's jurisdiction and the seafarer's tax residency. For the KSA entity, the system must calculate GOSI contributions (employer and employee portions) for Saudi nationals and expat employees. For the UAE entity, the system must process WPS (Wage Protection System) compliant payments and calculate pension contributions for UAE/GCC nationals. For the Qatar entity, the system must handle end-of-service gratuity calculations per Qatar Labour Law. For the India entity, the system must calculate income tax (TDS), Provident Fund (PF), and Employee State Insurance (ESI) contributions based on applicable slabs and thresholds. The system must support seafarer tax exemption rules (many jurisdictions exempt seafarers serving more than 183 days outside the country) and maintain configurable tax tables updated per fiscal year. All calculations must be auditable with full breakdown documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R008	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Payslip Generation & Distribution	The system must generate individual payslips for each seafarer at the end of each payroll cycle showing gross earnings breakdown (base wage, overtime, allowances, leave pay), deductions breakdown (allotments, cash advances, tax, social insurance, other deductions), net pay, and allotment distribution summary. Payslips must be generated in the seafarer's preferred language (English or Arabic) with currency amounts displayed in both the contract currency and the employing entity's base currency. Payslips must be distributed electronically via the crew self-service portal and email, and must be accessible offline through the vessel's onboard system for crew without personal internet access. Historical payslips must be retained for a minimum of 5 years and accessible through the crew self-service portal. The payslip format must comply with MLC 2006 Standard A2.2 requirements for itemized wage accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R009	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Cash Advance & Onboard Account Management	The system must track cash advances provided to seafarers during their onboard service period. Cash advances may be drawn against wages at designated ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT) or from the vessel's onboard cash. Each advance must record the amount in the currency disbursed, equivalent USD value at the exchange rate applied, date of advance, authorizing officer (Master), purpose, and seafarer's acknowledgment. The system must enforce configurable limits on cumulative cash advances as a percentage of earned wages (default maximum 50% of accrued net wages). The Master must be able to view each seafarer's running wage account balance showing earnings to date, deductions to date, advances drawn, and remaining balance. Onboard slop chest purchases must be recorded as deductions with itemized listings. All advance records must synchronize with shore-side payroll upon vessel connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R010	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	End-of-Contract Settlement Calculation	The system must calculate comprehensive end-of-contract settlement for each seafarer upon sign-off, including final wage payment for the partial month, accumulated leave pay balance, repatriation travel entitlement, gratuity or bonus per contract terms, outstanding cash advance recovery, and any other pending adjustments. The settlement must be processed within the timeframe mandated by MLC 2006 (promptly and in full upon termination). The system must generate a detailed settlement statement in the seafarer's preferred language (English or Arabic) showing all components with amounts in both contract currency and payment currency. Where the employing entity is in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India, the settlement must additionally comply with local labor law requirements for end-of-service benefits. The AI engine must verify settlement calculations against contract terms and flag any discrepancies for payroll officer review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R011	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Crew Cost Budgeting & Variance Analysis	The system must support annual crew cost budgeting per vessel with monthly tracking of actual costs against budget. Budget categories must include wages (base, overtime, leave pay), travel and crew change costs, training and certification costs, insurance premiums (P&I crew cover), victualling costs, and recruitment costs. Budgets must be prepared in USD per vessel with supporting detail in local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must calculate budget variance monthly and highlight categories exceeding configurable thresholds (default 5% monthly, 3% cumulative). The AI engine must analyze variance patterns and attribute variances to root causes such as exchange rate movements, unplanned crew changes, overtime overruns, or wage scale increases. Variance reports must be generated per vessel, per fleet segment, per entity, and consolidated at group level with drill-down capability from summary to transaction level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R012	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Payroll Approval & Authorization Workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-level payroll approval workflow before payment execution. The standard workflow must include preparation by Payroll Officer, review by Senior Payroll Officer, approval by Crewing Manager, and final authorization by Finance Manager. Each approval level must verify different aspects: data completeness, calculation accuracy, budget compliance, and payment authorization. The workflow must support delegation of authority during absence and escalation rules for delayed approvals. Approval must be entity-specific, with each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) running independent approval cycles with different authorized approvers. The system must enforce segregation of duties ensuring that the person preparing payroll cannot also approve it. All approval actions must be digitally signed with timestamp and user identity for audit compliance. Approved payrolls must be locked against modification, with any post-approval changes requiring a separate adjustment workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R013	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Bank Payment File Generation & Reconciliation	The system must generate bank payment files for crew wage disbursement in the formats required by each entity's banking partners. Payment file formats must include SWIFT MT103 for international wire transfers, local ACH formats for domestic transfers in Qatar (QCB), UAE (UAEFTS/WPS), KSA (SARIE), and India (NEFT/RTGS/IMPS), and CSV/XML formats for bulk payment processing. Each payment file must include all required fields for straight-through processing including beneficiary details, bank routing codes, payment references, and value dates. The system must support payment splitting where a seafarer's net pay is distributed across multiple payment methods (retained wages, allotments, cash). Upon receiving bank confirmation files, the system must automatically reconcile payments against the payroll, matching by reference number and amount, and flag unmatched or rejected payments for immediate follow-up.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R014	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Retroactive Payroll Adjustment Processing	The system must support retroactive payroll adjustments for scenarios including back-dated wage scale revisions, overtime corrections, late-reported deductions, exchange rate adjustments, and CBA renegotiation impacts. Retroactive adjustments must specify the affected period, original calculation values, corrected values, and net adjustment amount. The system must automatically recalculate affected payrolls for the retroactive period and compute the net adjustment to be applied in the current payroll cycle. Adjustments must be clearly itemized on the affected seafarer's payslip with reference to the original pay period being corrected. The AI engine must model the financial impact of proposed retroactive adjustments before execution, including currency conversion implications when the exchange rate has changed between the original and adjustment periods. All retroactive adjustments must follow the same approval workflow as regular payroll with additional justification documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R015	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	MLC 2006 Wage Protection Compliance	The system must enforce MLC 2006 Title 2 (Conditions of Employment) requirements related to seafarer wages. This includes ensuring wages are paid at regular monthly intervals not exceeding one month, providing each seafarer with a monthly account of payments due and amounts paid including exchange rates applied, ensuring the right to allot wages to family members, protecting against unauthorized deductions, and maintaining records of all wage payments for the duration of the seafarer's employment plus 3 years. The system must generate MLC-compliant wage account statements in both English and Arabic. For port state control inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must produce on-demand evidence of wage payment compliance including bank transfer confirmations and seafarer acknowledgment records. The AI engine must monitor payroll processing timeliness and alert management when payment deadlines are at risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R016	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Crew Insurance Premium & Claims Processing	The system must manage crew insurance coverage including P&I club crew cover, personal accident insurance, and supplementary medical insurance. For each seafarer, the system must track active insurance policies, coverage limits, premium amounts, and claims history. Premium calculations must consider risk factors including rank, vessel type, trading area (Arabian Gulf, Indian Ocean), age, and claims history. The system must support claims processing workflows from incident reporting through documentation collection, claims submission to insurers, and settlement tracking. Claims amounts must be recorded in the currency of incurrence (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) with conversion tracking for settlement in different currencies. The AI engine must analyze claims patterns to identify risk factors and recommend loss prevention measures. Insurance cost allocation must be distributed to the vessel's operating cost center with entity-level aggregation for renewal negotiation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-003-R017	FEAT-020-1-003	MOD-020	Crew Self-Service Payroll Portal	The system must provide a crew self-service portal accessible via web browser and mobile application where seafarers can view their payslips, wage account history, allotment status, leave balances, and tax deduction summaries. The portal must support bilingual operation in English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering for Arabic content. Seafarers must be able to update their allotment instructions through the portal subject to approval by the Crewing department, download payslips and wage statements in PDF format, submit queries regarding payroll discrepancies, and view their P&I and insurance coverage details. The portal must function with low bandwidth requirements to accommodate access from vessels via satellite communication. Authentication must use multi-factor methods. The portal must display all monetary amounts in both the contract currency and the local currency of the seafarer's selected home country with real-time exchange rate conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R001	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Flag State Regulatory Requirements Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of flag state crew-related regulatory requirements for all flag states under which the fleet's vessels are registered, with detailed coverage for Qatar (Qatar Maritime Administration), UAE (Federal Transport Authority - Land & Maritime), KSA (Transport General Authority), and India (Directorate General of Shipping). Requirements must be categorized by type: manning, certification, training, medical, documentation, and reporting. Each requirement must specify the regulatory reference, applicability criteria (vessel type, tonnage, trading area), compliance evidence required, and consequences of non-compliance. The database must be version-controlled to track regulatory changes over time. The AI engine must automatically assess the impact of new or amended regulations on the current fleet and generate compliance gap reports. Requirements must be accessible in both English and Arabic with accurate legal terminology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R002	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Safe Manning Document Management	The system must manage Safe Manning Documents (SMD) issued by flag state administrations for each vessel. The SMD specifies the minimum number and qualifications of crew required to operate the vessel safely. For each vessel, the system must store the current SMD with all amendments, track the manning levels specified per position and qualification, and continuously validate the actual crew complement against SMD requirements. Any deviation from SMD manning levels must generate immediate alerts to the Master, Fleet Manager, and DPA with severity classification (critical if below minimum, warning if at minimum without backup). The system must support SMD amendment request workflows when vessel operations change (e.g., new trading area requiring additional qualified crew). For vessels registered in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must format amendment requests according to each flag state's specific submission requirements and track application status through to approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R003	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Port State Control Inspection History & Analytics	The system must maintain a complete history of port state control inspections for each vessel, recording inspection date, port (with specific tracking for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT), MoU regime (Paris, Tokyo, Indian Ocean, Riyadh, etc.), inspector details, inspection type (initial, expanded, concentrated), crew-related deficiencies identified with ISM/STCW/MLC codes, corrective actions required, detention status, and resolution dates. The AI engine must analyze inspection history to identify recurring deficiency patterns per vessel, fleet, and flag state. Predictive analytics must estimate the probability of crew-related deficiencies at upcoming port calls based on the vessel's inspection history, current crew compliance status, and the targeting criteria of the port state's MoU regime. The system must generate trend reports showing improvement or deterioration in crew compliance performance across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R004	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Pre-Arrival Compliance Checklist Generation	The system must automatically generate comprehensive crew compliance checklists prior to each vessel's port call at major ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The checklist must verify crew certificate validity and flag state endorsement status for all onboard personnel, rest hour compliance for the preceding 30 days, Safe Manning Document compliance, MLC 2006 documentation availability (Seafarer Employment Agreements, crew list, wage records), crew complaint procedures posting, and vessel-specific requirements based on the port state's known focus areas. The checklist must be generated at least 72 hours before the estimated time of arrival and distributed to the Master and DPA. The AI engine must prioritize checklist items by risk severity and recommend corrective actions for any identified deficiencies. Checklists must be available in English and Arabic with port-specific regulatory references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R005	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Crew List Generation for Port Authorities	The system must generate port authority crew lists in the format required by each port's immigration and maritime authority. For Hamad Port (Qatar), the crew list must comply with Qatar Ministry of Interior requirements. For Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali (UAE), the format must satisfy UAE Federal Authority for Identity and Citizenship requirements. For JNPT (India), the format must meet Indian Immigration Bureau and DG Shipping requirements. Standard IMO FAL Form 5 (Crew List) must be supported as a baseline with port-specific extensions. The crew list must include all required fields: name, nationality, rank, date and place of birth, travel document details, and embarkation port. The system must generate crew lists automatically from the current vessel manning data and allow the Master to review and approve before submission. Lists must support generation in English and Arabic as required by the receiving authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R006	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	MLC 2006 Maritime Labour Certificate Compliance	The system must track Maritime Labour Certificate (MLC) and Declaration of Maritime Labour Compliance (DMLC) status for each vessel subject to MLC 2006. The system must maintain DMLC Part I (flag state requirements) and DMLC Part II (company measures) documentation for the fourteen areas of MLC compliance. For crew-related compliance areas (minimum age, medical certificates, qualifications, seafarer employment agreements, hours of work/rest, manning levels, accommodation, recreational facilities, food and catering, health and safety, on-board complaint procedures, and payment of wages), the system must provide continuous evidence of compliance through automated data collection from relevant modules. The system must generate MLC inspection-ready documentation packages that can be presented during port state control inspections at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Compliance status must be monitored per vessel with dashboards accessible to the DPA and Fleet Manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R007	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Seafarer Employment Agreement Management	The system must manage Seafarer Employment Agreements (SEA) as required by MLC 2006 Standard A2.1 for all crew members. Each SEA must contain the mandatory elements: seafarer's name and date of birth, shipowner's name and address, place and date of agreement, capacity (rank), amount of wages, paid annual leave, termination conditions, health and social security benefits, and repatriation entitlement. The system must generate SEA documents from templates configured per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and flag state, incorporating applicable CBA terms. SEAs must be generated in English and Arabic bilingual format where required. The system must track SEA signing, amendment, and termination with digital signature support. A copy of each signed SEA must be available onboard the vessel and accessible to the seafarer at all times through the crew self-service portal. The AI engine must validate SEA terms against applicable minimum standards and flag non-compliant clauses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R008	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Crew Complaint & Grievance Procedure Compliance	The system must implement the onboard complaint procedure required by MLC 2006 Regulation 5.1.5, providing seafarers with a mechanism to lodge complaints related to any breach of MLC requirements including wages, working conditions, accommodation, and victimization. The complaint workflow must support filing at vessel level (to the Master), escalation to shore-side management (Crewing Manager and DPA), further escalation to the flag state authority, and external referral to port state control at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT. Each complaint must be assigned a unique reference number, categorized by type, tracked through investigation stages, and resolved within configurable SLA timelines. The system must protect complainant confidentiality and prevent victimization by restricting access to complaint details. Complaint forms and procedures must be available in English, Arabic, Hindi, Filipino, and other languages common among the crew nationalities employed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R009	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	ISM Code Crew Competence Documentation	The system must support ISM Code Section 6 (Resources and Personnel) requirements by maintaining documented evidence that all crew members are qualified, certified, and medically fit for their assigned duties. The system must generate ISM audit-ready reports showing crew competence verification records, familiarization training completion for each vessel assignment, safety training drills participation, emergency duty assignments and readiness, and language proficiency assessments (working language of the vessel). The system must track vessel-specific familiarization requirements and ensure that newly joined crew members complete familiarization checklists before assuming operational duties. For multi-entity operations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must maintain entity-specific ISM documentation that satisfies the requirements of each entity's Document of Compliance (DoC) issued by the respective flag state. All ISM reports must be generated in English with Arabic translation available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R010	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Deficiency Tracking & Corrective Action Management	The system must implement a comprehensive deficiency tracking and corrective action management system for all crew-related deficiencies identified during port state control inspections, flag state audits, internal ISM audits, and management reviews. Each deficiency must be recorded with the source inspection details, deficiency code (IMO/ILO reference), description, risk severity (detention-level, observation, recommendation), assigned responsible person, root cause analysis, corrective action plan, target completion date, and evidence of closure. The workflow must support immediate corrective actions for detention-level deficiencies and planned corrective actions for observations. The AI engine must perform root cause analysis across all deficiencies to identify systemic issues such as training gaps, procedural weaknesses, or resource constraints. Deficiency reports must be aggregated at vessel, fleet, entity, and group level with trend analysis spanning inspections at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and all other ports of call.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R011	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Flag State Reporting & Notification Submissions	The system must manage all mandatory flag state reporting and notification requirements related to crew matters. This includes crew change notifications, serious injury and fatality reports, crew complaint escalation reports, manning shortfall notifications, and annual crew statistics submissions. For vessels registered under Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian flags, the system must format reports according to each flag state administration's specific requirements and submission channels (online portals, email, or physical submission). The system must track submission deadlines using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates where applicable (particularly for KSA and Qatar regulatory submissions), maintain evidence of submission receipt, and follow up on pending responses. The AI engine must generate draft reports from system data and present them for review before submission. Notification templates must be maintained in both English and Arabic as required by each flag state.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R012	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Repatriation Rights & Entitlement Management	The system must manage seafarer repatriation rights as required by MLC 2006 Standard A2.5. The system must track each seafarer's repatriation entitlement including designated repatriation destination, maximum period of service before repatriation becomes due (aligned with contract duration), and specific circumstances triggering repatriation rights (contract expiry, illness, injury, shipwreck, owner insolvency, or vessel sold/flag change). The system must calculate estimated repatriation costs per seafarer including travel fare, accommodation during transit, subsistence allowance, and personal effects transport. Repatriation cost provisions must be maintained per vessel in the vessel's operating currency and consolidated in USD for group financial reporting. For crew changes at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must coordinate repatriation logistics with the crew change management module. Financial security for repatriation must be documented as required by MLC 2006 Standard A2.5.2.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R013	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Crew Working Hour Regulatory Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor crew working hours against applicable regulatory limits from multiple sources: STCW (minimum rest hours), MLC 2006 (maximum work hours and minimum rest hours), and flag state specific requirements for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian registered vessels. The system must support both the maximum hours of work framework (14 hours in any 24-hour period, 72 hours in any 7-day period) and the minimum hours of rest framework (10 hours in any 24-hour period, 77 hours in any 7-day period) and apply the more restrictive standard. Work/rest hour records must be maintained per seafarer per day in the format prescribed by the flag state and available for inspection. The AI engine must identify non-compliance trends, predict future violations based on current patterns, and recommend schedule adjustments. Monthly and quarterly compliance reports must be generated per vessel with Hijri and Gregorian date formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R014	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Sanctions & Travel Restriction Screening	The system must screen all crew members against international sanctions lists and travel restriction databases before assignment to vessels and before each port call. Screening databases must include UN Security Council sanctions lists, US OFAC SDN list, EU consolidated sanctions list, and country-specific restricted persons lists for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must perform name matching using fuzzy logic algorithms to account for transliteration variations between Arabic and English names. Screening must be performed automatically when a seafarer is added to the system, when a crew change is planned, when a vessel's itinerary changes to include a new port, and on a periodic batch basis (minimum weekly). Any positive or potential match must trigger an immediate alert to the Compliance Officer and Legal department with automatic hold on the crew assignment pending clearance. The screening audit trail must be maintained for a minimum of 7 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R015	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Crew Accommodation & Living Conditions Compliance	The system must track and verify compliance with MLC 2006 Title 3 (Accommodation, Recreational Facilities, Food and Catering) requirements for each vessel. The system must maintain vessel accommodation records including cabin assignments (with maximum occupancy limits per vessel tonnage and type), cabin dimensions and facilities, mess room capacity, recreational facility inventory, hospital/sick bay equipment, and sanitary facility counts. The system must validate accommodation assignments against MLC requirements when processing crew changes, ensuring no cabin is over-occupied and that officer and rating accommodations meet minimum standards. Inspection checklists for accommodation condition must be completable from the vessel with photo documentation. Deficiencies must feed into the corrective action workflow with tracking through to resolution. Accommodation records must satisfy inspection requirements at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R016	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Regulatory Audit Trail & Evidence Repository	The system must maintain a comprehensive, tamper-proof audit trail of all crew management actions that may be subject to regulatory scrutiny. Auditable events must include crew assignments and changes, certificate verifications, payroll calculations, rest hour records, complaint handling, deficiency closures, and dispensation approvals. Each audit entry must record the event type, timestamp (in UTC with display in local time zones for Gulf and India), user identity, entity context, before and after values for data changes, and the business justification where applicable. The audit repository must support full-text search across all fields in both English and Arabic. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 7 years in compliance with the most stringent applicable flag state requirement. The system must support audit data export in formats suitable for flag state auditors, port state control inspectors, and external classification society auditors. Audit reports must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R017	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Multi-Flag Compliance Dashboard & Risk Scoring	The system must provide a unified compliance dashboard showing the crew compliance status of every vessel in the fleet, grouped by flag state (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, and others). Each vessel must display an AI-calculated composite compliance risk score based on weighted factors including manning level adequacy, crew certificate validity rates, rest hour compliance percentage, open deficiency count and severity, MLC documentation completeness, and time since last PSC inspection. The dashboard must highlight vessels in the red zone (high detention risk) requiring immediate attention and provide drill-down capability to specific compliance gaps. Risk scores must be recalculated in real-time as underlying data changes. The dashboard must support comparison views across entities and flag states, trend visualization over configurable time periods, and alert configuration for threshold breaches. Display must support English and Arabic with Hijri date formatting alongside Gregorian dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-1-004-R018	FEAT-020-1-004	MOD-020	Classification Society & Vetting Inspection Crew Requirements	The system must track crew-related requirements from classification societies (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, etc.) and oil major vetting inspections (SIRE, CDI) that go beyond statutory minimums. These may include additional training requirements for specific vessel types or cargoes, enhanced competency requirements for officers on tankers trading in the Arabian Gulf, specific crew experience levels required for terminal approvals at Hamad Port, Ras Laffan, Jebel Ali, or Indian refineries, and additional manning requirements imposed by charterers or terminal operators. The system must maintain a register of these additional requirements linked to each vessel's class and trading profile, validate crew assignments against these enhanced requirements, and flag gaps that could affect the vessel's commercial readiness. The AI engine must analyze vetting inspection outcomes to predict likely crew-related observations and recommend preemptive actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R004	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Commission Calculation and Multi-Currency Settlement	The system must calculate manning agency commissions based on the agreed CSA rates per crew rank, applying the correct commission structure (fixed fee per crew member, percentage of crew wages, or tiered volume-based rates). Commission invoices must be generated in the agency's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with automatic FX conversion using the daily exchange rate from the ERP treasury module. The system must support split commission scenarios where a crew member is sourced through a sub-agent, requiring proportional commission allocation. All commission calculations must include applicable VAT/GST rates per jurisdiction (5% VAT for UAE/KSA/Qatar, 18% GST for India) and generate accounting entries mapped to the correct entity chart of accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R005	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Document and Certification Tracking	The system must track all mandatory documents for each manning agency including maritime recruitment license, ISO 9001 certification, MLC 2006 recruitment and placement service certificate, financial guarantee or insurance bond, labor ministry approvals per country of operation, and POEA/GAMCA/Protector of Emigrants clearances where applicable. Each document must have fields for document type, issuing authority, issue date, expiry date (both Gregorian and Hijri), digital copy attachment (PDF up to 25MB), and verification status. The AI engine must automatically validate document authenticity patterns and flag anomalies. The system must block crew assignments from agencies with any expired mandatory document and generate compliance exception reports for port state control readiness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R006	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Crew Requisition Workflow from Vessel to Agency	The system must support a structured crew requisition workflow initiated by the vessel master or fleet personnel manager specifying vessel name, IMO number, port of crew change (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT), required rank, STCW certificates needed, preferred nationality, joining date, and contract duration. The AI engine must automatically match the requisition to the best-fit manning agency based on historical performance, available crew pool, geographical proximity to the crew change port, and current CSA terms. The requisition must flow through configurable approval levels (fleet manager, crewing superintendent, operations director) with SLA timers and escalation rules. Offline requisition submission must be supported for vessels at sea with automatic sync upon connectivity restoration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R007	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Crew Pool Visibility and Availability Tracking	The system must provide real-time visibility into each manning agency's available crew pool, displaying seafarer profiles with current availability status (available, on leave, assigned, in training), rank, certificates held, nationality, nearest port for mobilization, and expected availability date. Agencies must be able to update their crew pool data via a secure API integration or a dedicated agency portal with Arabic and English language support. The system must maintain a minimum data refresh interval of 24 hours and flag agencies that have not updated their pool data within 72 hours. AI must predict crew availability gaps for upcoming vessel schedules and proactively trigger requisitions to agencies with adequate pool depth for the required ranks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R008	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Manning Agency Blacklisting and Suspension Workflow	The system must support a formal blacklisting and suspension workflow for manning agencies that violate contractual terms, supply unqualified crew, or fail regulatory audits. The workflow must require documented justification with evidence attachments, approval from the crewing director and legal department, and a defined suspension period or permanent blacklist designation. Blacklisted agencies must be automatically excluded from all crew requisition matching, and any in-progress crew assignments from suspended agencies must trigger replacement requisitions. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all blacklisting actions with timestamps, approving officers, and reasons. Reinstatement from suspension must require a separate approval workflow with mandatory re-verification of all agency documents and a probationary performance monitoring period of configurable duration (default 6 months).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R009	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Communication and Correspondence Log	The system must maintain a centralized communication log for each manning agency capturing all correspondence including emails, portal messages, phone call notes, and meeting minutes. Each communication entry must record the date and time (with timezone support for Qatar AST, UAE GST, KSA AST, India IST), the communicating parties, subject, content summary, and any attached documents. The system must support bilingual correspondence in Arabic and English with automatic language detection. AI must analyze communication patterns to identify agencies with deteriorating responsiveness (increasing response times, unacknowledged requisitions) and generate early warning alerts. The communication log must be accessible offline with synchronization upon reconnection, supporting vessel-based crewing staff operating in limited connectivity environments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R010	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Audit and Inspection Scheduling	The system must manage periodic audit and inspection schedules for all registered manning agencies in compliance with MLC 2006 Regulation 1.4 requirements for recruitment and placement services. Audit schedules must be configurable per agency risk category (high risk: every 6 months, medium: annually, low: every 2 years) with automatic calendar generation in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Each audit must have a structured checklist covering recruitment practices, fee transparency, crew welfare provisions, complaint handling procedures, document management, and data protection compliance. Audit findings must be recorded with severity levels (critical, major, minor, observation), corrective action requirements, and follow-up deadlines. The AI engine must analyze audit history to predict agencies likely to fail upcoming audits and recommend preemptive corrective measures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R011	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Multi-Entity Agency Cost Allocation	The system must allocate manning agency costs (commissions, recruitment fees, travel reimbursements, training charges) to the correct operating entity based on the vessel's registration entity and the crew member's employment contract entity. In cases where a crew member serves across vessels belonging to different entities during a single contract period (e.g., transferring from a Qatar-flagged vessel at Hamad Port to a UAE-entity vessel at Jebel Ali), costs must be prorated based on days served per entity. The system must generate inter-company charge-back entries in the respective entity currencies (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity) with proper transfer pricing documentation. All cost allocations must be auditable and reconcilable against agency invoices and crew payroll records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R012	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency KPI Reporting and Benchmarking	The system must generate comprehensive KPI reports for manning agencies with drill-down capability from global fleet level to individual vessel level. Key metrics must include average crew mobilization time (from requisition to vessel boarding), first-time documentation accuracy rate, crew retention rate beyond initial contract, crew welfare complaint rate, and cost per hire by rank and nationality. Reports must support benchmarking across agencies supplying to the same trade routes (e.g., Gulf-India, Gulf-Far East) and must be exportable in PDF (with Arabic RTL layout support) and Excel formats. The AI engine must identify statistically significant performance trends and generate natural language summaries in both Arabic and English, highlighting agencies that are improving or deteriorating relative to fleet averages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R013	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Portal Self-Service Access Management	The system must provide a secure self-service web portal for manning agencies allowing them to view crew requisitions assigned to them, submit crew nominations with supporting documents, update crew availability pools, submit commission invoices, and track payment status. The portal must support Arabic and English language toggle with full RTL layout support for Arabic. Access must be controlled through multi-factor authentication with role-based permissions (agency admin, recruiter, finance officer). Each agency user action must be logged in an immutable audit trail. The portal must function in low-bandwidth environments typical of offices in smaller ports and must support document uploads up to 25MB. Session timeout must be configurable per agency with a default of 30 minutes of inactivity, and the portal must comply with GDPR and regional data protection regulations (Qatar Law No. 13 of 2016, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, India DPDP Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R014	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	AI-Driven Agency Recommendation Engine	The system must implement an AI recommendation engine that suggests the optimal manning agency for each crew requisition based on multi-factor analysis including historical crew quality scores, supply reliability for the specific rank and nationality requested, geographical proximity of the agency's crew pool to the planned crew change port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLP, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA), current commission rates versus budget, active CSA terms, and recent audit findings. The engine must provide a ranked list of top three agencies with confidence scores and explanatory reasoning in natural language (Arabic or English based on user preference). The recommendation must be overridable by authorized personnel with mandatory justification logging. The model must be retrained quarterly using updated performance data and must achieve at least 85% acceptance rate for its top recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R015	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Regulatory Compliance Cross-Reference Matrix	The system must maintain a regulatory compliance cross-reference matrix mapping each manning agency's certifications and licenses against the regulatory requirements of every flag state and port state the fleet operates in. For example, an agency supplying crew to vessels calling at Hamad Port must hold Qatar MOIA approval, while agencies supplying to Indian-flagged vessels must hold DG Shipping RPL license. The matrix must automatically identify compliance gaps when a new trade route or flag state is added to the fleet profile. The system must generate a compliance readiness report before each crew change operation, confirming the assigned agency meets all applicable regulatory requirements. Non-compliant assignments must be blocked with a mandatory override workflow requiring crewing director approval and documented risk acceptance. The matrix must be updated automatically when new regulations are published via integration with maritime regulatory feeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-001-R016	FEAT-020-2-001	MOD-020	Agency Dispute and Claims Management	The system must track and manage disputes and claims between the shipping company and manning agencies covering scenarios such as commission disagreements, crew quality complaints, documentation errors causing vessel delays, and breach of CSA terms. Each dispute must have a structured record including dispute type, amount in dispute (in the original transaction currency with USD equivalent), parties involved, chronological event log, evidence attachments, and resolution status. The workflow must support escalation levels from crewing operations to legal department with configurable SLA timers at each level. Financial claims must integrate with the accounts payable module to place holds on pending agency payments until dispute resolution. The AI engine must analyze dispute patterns to identify systemic issues with specific agencies and recommend preventive contract clause amendments for future CSA negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R001	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Crew Visa Application Workflow Engine	The system must manage end-to-end visa application workflows for seafarers joining vessels at Hamad Port (Qatar), Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali (UAE), Dammam and Jeddah (KSA), and JNPT (India). Each workflow must capture the visa type (transit, crew, employment, visit), destination country, seafarer nationality, required documents per destination immigration authority, application submission date, expected processing time, and approval status. The workflow must support parallel visa applications when a vessel itinerary requires crew to transit multiple countries (e.g., Indian seafarer flying to Doha via Dubai requires both UAE transit visa and Qatar crew visa). AI must predict visa processing times based on historical data per nationality-destination combination and flag applications at risk of delay relative to the vessel's crew change schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R002	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Immigration Document Repository and Validation	The system must maintain a centralized repository of all immigration-related documents for each seafarer including passport (with bio page scan), seaman's book, CDC (Continuous Discharge Certificate), visa copies, port entry permits, shore leave passes, and letter of guarantee from the shipping company. Each document must be stored with metadata including document type, issuing country, issue and expiry dates in Gregorian and Hijri formats, and verification status. The AI engine must perform automated validation checks including passport MRZ (Machine Readable Zone) parsing for data accuracy, expiry date validation (minimum 6 months validity for most GCC destinations), and cross-referencing nationality against destination country visa requirements. Documents must be accessible offline for vessel-based personnel and synchronized upon connectivity restoration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R009	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Multi-Entity Demurrage Allocation	When a shipment involves multiple legal entities within the group (e.g., Qatar entity as principal, UAE entity as handling agent, India entity as destination), the system must allocate demurrage charges to the responsible entity based on configurable allocation rules. Allocation may be by port of occurrence (demurrage at JNPT allocated to India entity), by BL consignee party, or by a custom split percentage. Inter-company demurrage allocations must automatically generate inter-company debit/credit entries in the respective entity's ledger using the correct transfer pricing currency and FX rate. Each entity must see only its allocated portion in its entity-filtered dashboard view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R003	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Travel Booking and Itinerary Management	The system must manage travel bookings for crew joining and departing vessels, capturing flight details (airline, flight number, departure/arrival airports and times), ground transportation arrangements (port transfers at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), hotel accommodations for transit crew, and travel insurance details. The system must support integration with corporate travel management companies via API and manual booking entry. Each itinerary must be linked to the crew change plan, vessel schedule, and visa status. AI must optimize travel routes and costs by analyzing historical fare data, recommending cost-effective routing options (e.g., direct flights versus connections), and consolidating bookings when multiple crew members travel on the same date to the same port. All costs must be captured in the booking currency with automatic conversion to the entity base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R004	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Crew Change Port Operations Coordination	The system must coordinate crew change operations at designated ports by generating a comprehensive crew change plan that includes joining crew travel itineraries, departing crew repatriation schedules, port agent contact details, launch boat or gangway arrangements, immigration clearance timelines, and baggage logistics. The plan must account for port-specific constraints such as Hamad Port's crew change anchorage procedures, Jebel Ali's 48-hour advance crew list submission requirement, and JNPT's immigration office operating hours. The system must generate the crew change plan in both English and Arabic and distribute it automatically to the vessel master, port agent, manning agency, and crewing operations team. AI must predict optimal crew change windows based on vessel port stay duration, berth availability, and immigration processing times, alerting operations when planned crew changes risk exceeding the vessel's port stay.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R005	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	MLC 2006 Repatriation Entitlement Tracking	The system must track each seafarer's repatriation entitlements in strict compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A2.5, including maximum service period (default 11 months, configurable per flag state), repatriation destination (seafarer's choice of home country port, port of engagement, or other agreed location), mode of transport entitlement (air economy class as minimum), baggage allowance (minimum 30kg), and transit accommodation entitlement. The system must automatically calculate the repatriation due date based on the seafarer's sign-on date and maximum service period, generating alerts at 90, 60, and 30 days before the due date. For each operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the system must apply the more favorable provision between the flag state requirements, CBA (Collective Bargaining Agreement) terms, and company policy. Any extension beyond the maximum service period must trigger a mandatory approval workflow with documented seafarer consent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R006	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Travel Cost Budgeting and Variance Analysis	The system must support annual travel cost budgeting per vessel, per trade route, and per operating entity with monthly phasing. Budget amounts must be maintained in the entity base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with a consolidated USD view for group reporting. Actual travel costs must be captured against each budget line as bookings are confirmed, with real-time variance analysis showing budget versus actual at vessel, fleet, entity, and group levels. The AI engine must forecast end-of-year travel spend based on remaining crew change schedules, historical cost patterns, and seasonal fare fluctuations (e.g., peak season surcharges during Hajj for KSA routes, Diwali for India routes). Variance reports must be exportable in PDF with Arabic RTL support and Excel formats, and must highlight cost overruns exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10% over budget).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R007	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Emergency Repatriation and Compassionate Travel	The system must handle emergency repatriation scenarios including medical evacuation, compassionate leave (family bereavement, critical illness), vessel abandonment, and disciplinary sign-off. Each emergency case must be processed through an expedited workflow bypassing standard approval timelines while maintaining audit trail integrity. The system must capture the emergency type, medical or compassionate justification, nearest feasible disembarkation port, emergency contact details, and insurance claim reference where applicable. For medical evacuations from vessels at sea in the Arabian Gulf, the system must maintain a directory of MEDEVAC service providers covering Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port regions. Travel arrangements must be bookable within 4 hours of emergency declaration. Cost tracking must distinguish emergency repatriation costs from routine crew changes for insurance recovery purposes, with amounts recorded in transaction currency and entity base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R008	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Visa Fee Tracking and Recovery Management	The system must track all visa-related fees per seafarer per visa application including government visa fees, service agent charges, medical examination fees (GAMCA for GCC, panel physician for other destinations), attestation fees, and courier charges. Each fee must be recorded in the payment currency with automatic conversion to the entity base currency using the daily exchange rate. The system must determine fee recoverability based on the applicable CBA, employment contract terms, and jurisdiction-specific labor laws (Qatar Labor Law prohibiting recruitment fee charging to workers, UAE Ministerial Decree No. 1283 of 2010, Indian Emigration Act provisions). For recoverable fees, the system must generate payroll deduction schedules within permissible limits. For non-recoverable fees, the system must allocate costs to the correct entity cost center. AI must analyze visa fee trends per destination country and nationality combination to forecast annual visa budgets and identify cost optimization opportunities such as bulk visa processing or alternative visa categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R009	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Travel Document Expiry Monitoring and Renewal Alerts	The system must continuously monitor expiry dates of all crew travel documents including passports, seaman's books, visas, and travel insurance policies. Alerts must be generated at configurable intervals (default: 12 months, 6 months, 3 months, 1 month before expiry) and distributed to the seafarer, manning agency, and crewing operations team. For passports, the system must enforce a minimum 6-month validity rule for GCC destinations and flag passports that will fall below this threshold during the planned contract period. The AI engine must predict document renewal processing times based on the seafarer's nationality and issuing authority, recommending optimal renewal initiation dates to avoid crew change disruptions. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri date tracking for documents issued by Saudi and Qatari authorities where Hijri dates are the primary reference. Offline access to document expiry dashboards must be available for vessel-based personnel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R010	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Shore Leave Pass and Port Entry Permit Management	The system must manage shore leave passes and port entry permits for crew members at each port of call, capturing the permit type, issuing authority, validity period, permitted areas, and any restrictions. For GCC ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), the system must track the specific shore leave visa requirements which vary by seafarer nationality, with some nationalities requiring advance approval from immigration authorities. The system must generate the required crew lists in the format specified by each port authority (IMO FAL Form 5 as the base, with port-specific addenda) and submit them electronically where port community systems support it. AI must flag crew members who may face shore leave restrictions based on nationality-destination combinations (e.g., certain nationalities facing enhanced screening at specific GCC ports) and recommend alternative crew change arrangements. The system must maintain a historical log of all shore leave passes issued for port state control inspection readiness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R011	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Travel Insurance and P&I Club Coordination	The system must track travel insurance coverage for all seafarers during transit periods (from home to vessel and vessel to home), including policy number, insurer name, coverage type (medical, accident, baggage, trip cancellation), coverage limits in USD, deductible amounts, and policy validity dates. The system must verify that every traveling crew member has active insurance coverage before travel confirmation and block travel bookings for uninsured personnel. For incidents during travel, the system must capture claim details and coordinate with P&I club correspondents at the relevant port. The system must maintain P&I club contact directories for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, Dammam, Jeddah, and JNPT with 24/7 emergency contact numbers. Insurance costs must be allocated to the correct entity and vessel cost center, with multi-currency support for premium payments in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R012	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Crew Travel Advance and Expense Settlement	The system must manage travel cash advances issued to crew members for incidental expenses during transit, including advance amount in the travel currency, exchange rate applied, advance approval workflow, and settlement deadline. Upon completion of travel, the seafarer or manning agency must submit expense receipts through the system for reconciliation against the advance. The system must support receipt capture via mobile photograph upload with AI-powered OCR extracting merchant name, amount, currency, and date from receipts in English, Arabic, and Hindi. Unsettled advances beyond the configurable deadline (default 14 days post-joining or post-repatriation) must trigger automatic payroll deduction requests. All advance and settlement transactions must generate proper accounting entries in the entity's base currency with foreign exchange gain/loss calculations. The system must enforce per-diem limits based on the transit country and company travel policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R013	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Group Travel Coordination for Bulk Crew Changes	The system must support bulk crew change scenarios where multiple seafarers (typically 5-25) join or depart a vessel simultaneously, optimizing group travel arrangements. The system must consolidate travel bookings to leverage group fare discounts, coordinate shared ground transportation from airports to ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), and arrange block hotel bookings for transit accommodation. The group travel plan must account for seafarers arriving from different origin cities and nationalities with varying visa requirements, generating a consolidated timeline showing each crew member's travel status, estimated arrival time at the crew change port, and immigration clearance progress. AI must identify the optimal crew change window that minimizes total waiting time and accommodation costs while ensuring all joining crew are available before departing crew sign off, maintaining minimum safe manning requirements throughout the transition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R014	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Travel Restriction and Sanctions Screening	The system must screen all crew travel arrangements against applicable sanctions lists (UN Security Council, US OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List, UK HMT), travel ban databases, and port state immigration blacklists. Screening must be performed at the time of travel booking and again 24 hours before departure, with results logged for compliance audit. Any positive or partial match must trigger an immediate hold on the travel booking and escalation to the compliance officer with details of the match including the seafarer's full name, date of birth, nationality, passport number, and the specific sanctions list entry matched. The system must support fuzzy name matching to catch transliteration variations between Arabic and Latin scripts (e.g., Mohammed/Muhammad/Mohamed). False positive resolution must be documented with the compliance officer's clearance and supporting evidence. The screening engine must be updated within 24 hours of any sanctions list revision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R015	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Repatriation Cost Accrual and Financial Provisioning	The system must calculate and accrue repatriation cost provisions for each active seafarer on a monthly basis in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A2.5.2 requirements for financial security. The accrual calculation must estimate the repatriation cost based on the seafarer's contractual repatriation destination, current vessel position (nearest likely repatriation port), economy class airfare estimates, transit accommodation costs, and incidental allowances per the applicable CBA. Accruals must be maintained per seafarer, per vessel, and per operating entity in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with a consolidated USD provision for group financial reporting. When actual repatriation occurs, the system must reverse the accrual and book the actual cost, generating a variance report. The AI engine must update repatriation cost estimates quarterly based on actual fare data and flag vessels or entities where the accrued provision is insufficient relative to estimated actual costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R016	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Digital Crew Change Checklist and Sign-Off	The system must provide a digital crew change checklist for each joining and departing crew member encompassing all travel, visa, documentation, and operational handover steps. For joining crew, the checklist must include visa confirmed, travel booked, travel insurance active, advance issued, port agent notified, vessel master notified, cabin allocated, and safety familiarization scheduled. For departing crew, the checklist must include repatriation travel booked, sign-off medical completed, wage account settled, personal effects packed, shore pass returned, and debriefing completed. Each checklist item must support electronic sign-off with timestamp, signing officer identity, and optional comments. The checklist must be accessible on mobile devices in offline mode for use at anchorage or during launch boat transfers. Incomplete checklists must block the crew change confirmation and escalate to the crewing superintendent. The system must generate a completed crew change report in English and Arabic for filing with the vessel's continuous synopsis record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-002-R017	FEAT-020-2-002	MOD-020	Historical Travel Analytics and Route Optimization	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical database of all crew travel transactions including routes, airlines, fares paid, booking lead times, delays encountered, and visa processing durations. The AI engine must analyze this data to identify cost optimization opportunities such as preferred airline agreements for high-volume routes (e.g., Mumbai to Doha for JNPT-Hamad Port crew changes, Manila to Dubai for Jebel Ali crew changes), optimal booking lead times for lowest fares per route, and alternative routing options that reduce total travel time or cost. The system must generate quarterly travel analytics reports showing cost trends by route, airline, booking channel, and entity, with recommendations for travel policy updates. Reports must support Arabic RTL layout and bilingual content. The AI must also detect anomalous travel costs (significantly above historical averages for the same route) and flag them for review before payment approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R001	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Seafarer Medical Records Management	The system must maintain comprehensive electronic medical records for each seafarer including pre-employment medical examination (PEME) results, onboard medical log entries, port medical facility visit records, prescription history, allergy information, blood type, chronic condition tracking, and vaccination records (including Yellow Fever, COVID-19, and region-specific requirements for GCC and India). Medical records must comply with ILO/WHO Guidelines for Conducting Pre-sea and Periodic Medical Fitness Examinations for Seafarers and must be stored with strict access controls limiting visibility to authorized medical officers, the seafarer, and designated crewing personnel. Records must support document attachments (lab reports, X-rays, specialist referrals) up to 50MB per document. Data encryption at rest and in transit must meet healthcare data protection standards applicable in Qatar (Law No. 13 of 2016), UAE (Health Data Law), KSA (PDPL), and India (DPDP Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R002	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Pre-Employment Medical Examination Workflow	The system must manage the PEME workflow from medical appointment scheduling through result evaluation to fitness certificate issuance. The workflow must support PEME appointments at approved medical facilities near major crew supply ports and manning agency locations, with a directory of approved clinics for Doha, Dubai, Mumbai, Chennai, Manila, and Colombo. PEME results must be entered using standardized ILO medical fitness categories (fit, fit with restrictions, temporarily unfit, permanently unfit) with detailed findings per examination component (vision, hearing, cardiovascular, respiratory, musculoskeletal, mental health). AI must analyze PEME results against the seafarer's intended rank and vessel type to flag potential fitness concerns (e.g., color vision deficiency for navigating officers, hearing loss for engine room ratings). Failed PEME results must automatically cancel pending crew assignments and trigger replacement requisitions. PEME certificates must be tracked with validity dates (maximum 2 years, 1 year for seafarers over 50) in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R003	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Onboard Medical Inventory and Dispensary Management	The system must track medical inventory onboard each vessel in compliance with the flag state medical stores requirements and WHO International Medical Guide for Ships. The inventory must cover medicines (categorized by therapeutic class), medical equipment, first aid supplies, and controlled substances with batch numbers, expiry dates, storage temperature requirements, and minimum stock levels. The system must generate replenishment orders when stock falls below minimum levels or items approach expiry, routing orders to the ship chandler at the next scheduled port of call (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT) with lead time calculations. Controlled substance usage must be tracked with a double-entry log requiring the master's countersignature. AI must analyze consumption patterns per vessel to optimize stock levels, reducing wastage from expired medicines while maintaining compliance. The inventory must be auditable for port state control inspections and flag state medical survey requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R004	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Telemedicine and Remote Medical Consultation	The system must support telemedicine consultations for seafarers requiring medical advice while the vessel is at sea, integrating with TMAS (Telemedical Maritime Assistance Service) providers. Each consultation must be logged with the date and time (UTC and vessel local time), presenting symptoms, vital signs transmitted by the onboard medical officer, TMAS physician identity and license number, diagnosis, treatment instructions, and follow-up requirements. The system must support video consultation booking with automatic bandwidth assessment for the vessel's satellite communication link. For vessels operating in the Arabian Gulf between Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must maintain a directory of regional TMAS providers with Arabic language capability. AI must triage symptom reports from onboard medical officers, suggesting preliminary diagnostic considerations and flagging cases that may require medical evacuation based on severity assessment algorithms. All telemedicine records must be integrated into the seafarer's medical file.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R005	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Port Medical Facility Directory and Appointment Scheduling	The system must maintain a comprehensive directory of approved medical facilities near all regularly called ports, with detailed entries for Hamad Medical Corporation facilities near Hamad Port (Doha), Cleveland Clinic Abu Dhabi and NMC hospitals near Khalifa Port, Mediclinic and Aster facilities near Jebel Ali, and government and private hospitals near JNPT (Navi Mumbai). Each facility entry must include specialties available, emergency department availability (24/7 or limited hours), distance from port gate, estimated transfer time, accepted insurance providers, language capabilities (Arabic, English, Hindi, Urdu), and contact details. The system must support appointment scheduling via API integration where available or manual booking with confirmation tracking. AI must recommend the optimal facility based on the medical condition, urgency level, facility capabilities, and estimated cost, considering the entity's insurance coverage and preferred provider network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R001	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Certificate and DMLC Registry	The system must maintain a registry of Maritime Labour Certificates (MLC) and Declaration of Maritime Labour Compliance (DMLC) Parts I and II for every vessel in the fleet. Each entry must capture the certificate number, issuing authority (flag state or recognized organization), issue date, expiry date (in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), last endorsement date, next endorsement due date, DMLC Part I national requirements, and DMLC Part II company measures. The system must generate alerts at 6 months, 3 months, and 1 month before certificate expiry and before annual or intermediate endorsement deadlines. AI must cross-reference the vessel's trading pattern with port state control MLC inspection frequency data for ports including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to prioritize vessels at highest risk of inspection and ensure their documentation is current. Expired or unendorsed certificates must trigger an automatic operational hold advisory to the fleet operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R006	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Mental Health and Psychological Welfare Program	The system must support a structured mental health and psychological welfare program for seafarers in compliance with MLC 2006 Guideline B4.3.1 and IMO/ILO Guidelines on the Mental Health of Seafarers. The program must include periodic mental health screening questionnaires (PHQ-9 for depression, GAD-7 for anxiety) administered at sign-on, mid-contract, and pre-repatriation, with results stored confidentially and accessible only to the designated welfare officer and the seafarer. The system must provide access to a 24/7 multilingual helpline directory (supporting Arabic, English, Hindi, Tagalog, and other major seafarer nationalities) and confidential counseling appointment booking. AI must analyze anonymized welfare screening trends across the fleet to identify vessels or trade routes with elevated mental health risk factors (e.g., long sea passages, isolation, high-stress port operations) and recommend targeted welfare interventions. Individual screening results triggering concern thresholds must generate confidential alerts to the company-designated welfare officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R007	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Work and Rest Hours Monitoring for Health Compliance	The system must integrate with the vessel's work and rest hours recording system to monitor crew fatigue levels and compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A2.3 (minimum 10 hours rest in any 24-hour period, minimum 77 hours rest in any 7-day period) and STCW Section A-VIII/1 requirements. The system must flag individual seafarers whose rest hour records show non-compliance patterns, generating alerts to the vessel master and shore-based crewing superintendent. AI must predict fatigue risk by analyzing work patterns, rest period fragmentation, and voyage-specific factors (frequent port calls at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with intensive cargo operations reducing rest opportunities). The system must generate monthly fatigue risk reports per vessel ranked by crew member exposure, with recommendations for watch schedule adjustments. Persistent non-compliance must trigger a welfare intervention workflow and may result in mandatory additional manning recommendations for the affected vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R008	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Onboard Recreational Facilities and Welfare Provisions Tracking	The system must track the availability and condition of onboard recreational and welfare facilities for each vessel in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A3.1, including crew mess and galley facilities, recreation room equipment (TV, library, gym, games), internet and communication facilities (satellite phone, Wi-Fi access hours and bandwidth allocation), laundry facilities, and religious observance provisions (prayer room/musalla, Qibla direction indicator, prayer time schedule for Muslim crew which is significant for vessels operating in the GCC region). The system must support periodic welfare inspections with structured checklists, condition ratings, and photograph documentation. Deficiency items must generate corrective action work orders routed to the vessel's technical superintendent. AI must benchmark welfare facility provision across the fleet and flag vessels falling below company standards or MLC minimum requirements, recommending specific improvements prioritized by crew satisfaction survey feedback data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R009	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Food and Catering Standards Management	The system must manage food and catering standards onboard vessels in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A3.2 and Regulation 3.2, tracking provisions budgets per vessel per day per person in the vessel's operating currency, menu planning capabilities with nutritional balance analysis, food safety inspection records, ship's cook qualification certificates (STCW Regulation VI/3), and galley equipment maintenance schedules. The provisioning module must support menu planning that accommodates diverse dietary requirements including halal certification (mandatory for GCC-operating vessels), vegetarian options (significant for Indian crew), and medical dietary restrictions. Provisions ordering must be integrated with ship chandler procurement at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with cost comparison across approved suppliers. AI must analyze per-capita food costs across the fleet, flag anomalies suggesting wastage or quality issues, and recommend optimal provisioning quantities based on crew composition, voyage duration, and historical consumption patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R010	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Occupational Health and Injury Reporting	The system must capture and manage all occupational health incidents, injuries, and near-misses occurring onboard vessels or during crew travel, in compliance with ISM Code requirements and flag state reporting obligations. Each incident must be recorded with structured fields including date, time, location on vessel, activity at time of incident, injury type (using ILO classification codes), body part affected, severity level (first aid, medical treatment, lost time, fatality), root cause analysis (using the maritime industry standard SHELL model), and corrective actions taken. The reporting workflow must support initial report submission from the vessel (including offline capability with sync), shore-based review and investigation, flag state notification where required, and P&I club notification for potential claims. AI must analyze incident data across the fleet to identify patterns (e.g., increased slip-and-fall incidents during monsoon season on India-Gulf routes) and recommend preventive measures. Reports must be generatable in English and Arabic for regulatory submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R011	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Medical Cost Tracking and Insurance Claims Management	The system must track all medical costs incurred for each seafarer including PEME costs, onboard treatment costs, port medical facility costs, hospitalization expenses, specialist consultation fees, medical evacuation costs, and long-term treatment costs for injuries sustained during service. Each cost entry must record the amount in the transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR, or other), the entity responsible for payment, and the applicable insurance or P&I coverage. The system must generate insurance claim packages with all supporting documentation (medical reports, invoices, treatment records) formatted per P&I club requirements. AI must estimate claim recovery likelihood based on historical claim outcomes and flag claims at risk of denial due to documentation gaps. Multi-entity cost allocation must apply when a seafarer is injured on one entity's vessel but receives treatment at a port associated with a different entity (e.g., injured on Qatar-entity vessel, treated at Jebel Ali port hospital under UAE entity arrangements).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R003	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Transit Time Matrix Calculation	The system must automatically compute port-to-port transit times for every origin-destination pair within a service loop based on the defined rotation schedule and planned dwell times. Transit times must be expressed in calendar days and hours, accounting for intermediate port calls. The matrix must be recalculated whenever the rotation schedule changes. For the Hamad Port to JNPT corridor, for instance, the system must calculate direct transit time as well as transit via Jebel Ali transshipment if applicable. The transit time matrix must be exportable in CSV and PDF formats and must support display in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R012	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Vaccination and Health Certificate Compliance Tracking	The system must track all required vaccinations and health certificates for each seafarer based on the vessel's trading area, port state requirements, and flag state regulations. For vessels calling at GCC ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), the system must verify compliance with GCC-specific health screening requirements including GAMCA medical clearance for certain nationalities. For vessels trading to endemic regions, the system must verify Yellow Fever vaccination certificates with 10-year validity tracking. The system must maintain an immunization schedule per seafarer showing administered vaccines, booster due dates, and pending vaccinations required before the next voyage. AI must cross-reference the vessel's planned itinerary with destination port health requirements and generate a vaccination compliance gap report per crew member before each voyage, allowing sufficient lead time for vaccinations that require multiple doses. Vaccination dates must be tracked in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars for GCC-issued certificates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R013	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Complaint and Grievance Handling System	The system must implement a structured onboard complaint and grievance handling procedure in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A5.1.5, providing seafarers with a fair, effective, and expeditious process. Complaints must be submittable through multiple channels (vessel-based terminal, mobile app, shore-based portal, or via the manning agency) in Arabic, English, Hindi, or Tagalog. Each complaint must be assigned a unique tracking number, categorized by type (working conditions, food quality, accommodation, wages, discrimination, harassment, safety concerns), and routed through a defined escalation chain (onboard welfare officer, master, shore-based designated person, independent external body). The system must enforce response SLAs at each escalation level (initial acknowledgment within 24 hours, investigation completion within 14 days). Complaint data must be kept confidential with access restricted to authorized investigation personnel. AI must analyze complaint patterns to identify systemic welfare issues per vessel or fleet-wide and recommend corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R014	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Communication Facilities and Connectivity Management	The system must track and manage crew communication facilities onboard each vessel including satellite phone availability and call credit allocation, internet access provisions (bandwidth allocation per crew member, access schedules), crew Wi-Fi hotspot configuration, and video calling facilities for family communication. The system must support prepaid communication credit management with crew self-service top-up in their preferred currency (USD, INR, PHP, or other) via mobile payment integration. Usage tracking must capture per-crew-member data consumption and call minutes for fair usage policy enforcement. For vessels operating in the Arabian Gulf region between Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must log connectivity coverage quality by position for route-based bandwidth optimization. AI must recommend optimal communication service provider packages based on vessel trading patterns, crew nationality distribution (determining peak usage times for different time zones), and historical consumption data. The system must ensure minimum communication access as per MLC 2006 Guideline B3.1.11.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R015	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Welfare Fund Management and Disbursement	The system must manage the crew welfare fund for each operating entity, tracking fund contributions (from company allocation, vessel budgets, and any shore-based welfare organization grants), fund balances per entity in the entity base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and disbursements for welfare activities. Disbursement categories must include port welfare facility access fees, crew recreational events, religious celebration provisions (Eid, Diwali, Christmas, and other cultural observances relevant to the crew nationalities), educational materials, and crew family support programs. Each disbursement must follow an approval workflow appropriate to the amount (welfare officer up to a configurable threshold, crewing manager above threshold). The system must generate quarterly welfare fund utilization reports per vessel and per entity showing fund movements, activity participation rates, and crew satisfaction scores. AI must analyze fund utilization patterns and recommend optimal allocation across welfare categories based on crew demographic profiles and satisfaction survey feedback.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R016	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Seafarer Death and Serious Injury Case Management	The system must provide a specialized case management workflow for seafarer death or serious injury incidents, coordinating multiple concurrent processes including flag state notification (within 24 hours as per most flag state requirements), P&I club notification, family notification through designated next-of-kin contacts, repatriation of remains or injured seafarer, personal effects inventory and return, investigation coordination with maritime authorities, and compensation claim processing. The workflow must generate required statutory reports in the formats specified by the vessel's flag state and the port state where the incident occurred. For incidents at GCC ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), the system must coordinate with local police and coroner requirements which involve Arabic documentation and Sharia-compliant procedures for Muslim seafarers. Financial provisions including death compensation, outstanding wages, and family support must be calculated per the applicable CBA, employment contract, and statutory minimums in the entity's operating currency with USD equivalent for insurance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-003-R017	FEAT-020-2-003	MOD-020	Crew Satisfaction Survey and Welfare Analytics	The system must administer periodic crew satisfaction surveys covering all MLC 2006 welfare areas including accommodation, food, recreation, communication, medical care, work-life balance, and management responsiveness. Surveys must be available in Arabic, English, Hindi, Tagalog, and other languages representing major crew nationalities, accessible via vessel-based terminals and mobile devices. The system must support anonymous submission to encourage honest feedback while allowing optional identification for follow-up. AI must perform sentiment analysis on free-text responses (supporting multilingual analysis including Arabic), identify emerging welfare concerns before they escalate to formal complaints, and generate actionable insights per vessel, per trade route, and fleet-wide. Survey results must feed into vessel welfare improvement plans with tracked corrective actions. Trend analysis must show welfare score progression over time with correlation to retention rates and incident frequency, enabling data-driven welfare investment decisions across all operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R002	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Title 1 - Minimum Age and Seafarer Documentation Compliance	The system must verify and continuously monitor compliance with MLC 2006 Title 1 requirements for every seafarer in the fleet. This includes minimum age verification (no person under 16 employed, no person under 18 engaged in hazardous work or night work unless authorized for training), valid seafarer employment agreement on file, valid medical fitness certificate within validity period, competency certificates matching assigned duties per STCW requirements, and personal documentation adequacy. The system must calculate the seafarer's age at sign-on date and throughout the contract period, flagging any assignment that would place a minor (under 18) in a non-compliant role. For each operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the system must apply the stricter of the flag state requirements and the MLC standards. AI must perform daily compliance scans across all active crew records and generate a non-compliance exception report sorted by severity and vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R003	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Title 2 - Employment Conditions Compliance Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time compliance dashboard for MLC 2006 Title 2 (Conditions of Employment) covering seafarer employment agreements (Standard A2.1), wages (Standard A2.2), hours of work and rest (Standard A2.3), entitlement to leave (Standard A2.4), repatriation (Standard A2.5), compensation for vessel loss or foundering (Standard A2.6), and manning levels (Standard A2.7). The dashboard must display per-vessel compliance status using traffic light indicators (green: fully compliant, amber: minor gaps identified, red: critical non-compliance). Each indicator must be drillable to the specific requirement, showing the evidence of compliance or the nature of the gap. The system must pull live data from the crew payroll module for wage compliance, the work-rest hours recording system for hours compliance, and the manning plan for minimum safe manning verification. Dashboard views must be available in English and Arabic with RTL layout support, and filterable by entity, vessel, flag state, and compliance area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R004	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Title 3 - Accommodation and Recreational Facilities Standards	The system must track compliance with MLC 2006 Title 3 (Accommodation, Recreational Facilities, Food and Catering) for each vessel, maintaining a structured checklist of requirements including cabin floor area minimums per rank, berth dimensions, sanitary facilities ratios, mess room capacity, hospital accommodation provision, laundry facilities, and recreational space adequacy. Each checklist item must reference the specific MLC Standard and Guideline number (e.g., Standard A3.1 paragraph 6(a) for sleeping room floor area). The system must store the results of annual accommodation inspections with photographs, measurements, and condition assessments. For vessels on order or undergoing modification, the system must validate proposed accommodation plans against MLC requirements before construction approval. AI must compare vessel accommodation standards against fleet averages and industry benchmarks, flagging vessels that meet minimum MLC requirements but fall below company standards. Non-compliance items must generate corrective action plans with target completion dates and cost estimates in the vessel's operating currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R005	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Title 4 - Health Protection and Medical Care Compliance	The system must monitor compliance with MLC 2006 Title 4 (Health Protection, Medical Care, Welfare and Social Security Protection) across the fleet. This includes tracking onboard medical equipment per WHO/ILO standards and flag state medical stores list, verifying medical officer qualifications on vessels carrying 100+ persons, ensuring access to medical care ashore at ports of call (with the port medical facility directory cross-referenced per FEAT-020-2-003-R005), verifying occupational health protection measures per ISM Code, and confirming social security protection arrangements per applicable flag state and seafarer nationality. For GCC-operating vessels, the system must verify that onboard medical stores comply with the specific requirements of Hamad Port health authority, Dubai Maritime City Authority, and Abu Dhabi Ports Authority medical inspection checklists. AI must identify gaps between the vessel's current medical provisions and the requirements of each port on the planned itinerary, generating a compliance preparation report at least 14 days before each port call.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R006	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Title 5 - Port State Control Inspection Readiness	The system must maintain continuous port state control (PSC) inspection readiness for MLC 2006 Title 5 requirements across all vessels. The system must generate a pre-arrival MLC compliance package for each port call including current MLC certificate and DMLC copies, crew list with employment agreement summaries, hours of work and rest records for the preceding 30 days, evidence of wage payment, accommodation inspection records, medical stores inventory, and onboard complaint procedure posting verification. For high-inspection-risk ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT have active Paris MoU or Indian Ocean MoU PSC regimes), the system must perform an automated pre-arrival compliance check 72 hours before estimated arrival and generate a deficiency prediction report. AI must analyze historical PSC inspection data from the port state MoU databases (Tokyo MoU, Indian Ocean MoU, Riyadh MoU) to predict likely inspection focus areas based on vessel type, flag state, and recent detention statistics. The report must be available in English and Arabic for the master's review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R007	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Seafarer Employment Agreement Compliance Checker	The system must validate every Seafarer Employment Agreement (SEA) against MLC 2006 Standard A2.1 requirements, verifying that each agreement contains all mandatory terms including seafarer's full name, date of birth, and birthplace; shipowner's name and address; place and date of agreement; capacity in which the seafarer is employed; amount of wages; paid annual leave; health and social security coverage; repatriation entitlement; termination conditions; and the applicable CBA if any. The system must maintain SEA templates per operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) that incorporate both MLC minimums and entity-specific legal requirements (Qatar Labour Law, UAE Maritime Code, KSA Labor Law, Indian Merchant Shipping Act). AI must compare each signed SEA against the applicable template and flag deviations, missing clauses, or terms that fall below MLC minimums. SEAs must be stored in both English and Arabic versions where the employment contract law requires Arabic as the governing language (applicable for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R006	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Commission tier structure for agents	The system must support multi-tier commission structures for shipping agents with calculation methods including flat amount per TEU, percentage of freight revenue, tiered rates based on volume thresholds, and hybrid models combining fixed and variable components. Commission tiers must be definable per trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), and cargo type (general, hazardous, reefer, out-of-gauge). Each commission structure must have effective date ranges and the system must support future-dated commission changes that automatically activate on the specified date without manual intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R008	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Wage Compliance Monitoring Against MLC and CBA Minimums	The system must continuously monitor crew wage payments against MLC 2006 Standard A2.2 minimum wage requirements (updated annually by the ILO Joint Maritime Commission) and applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement minimums (ITF TCC, IBF, or company CBA). For each seafarer, the system must verify that the base wage meets or exceeds the MLC minimum for the rank, overtime is calculated and paid per the CBA formula, leave pay is accrued correctly, and allotment arrangements are processed as per the seafarer's instructions. Wages must be verified in the payment currency (typically USD for international seafarers) with cross-checks against entity base currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for local-hire crew. AI must detect wage payment anomalies including late payments (beyond the contractual monthly date), unexplained deductions, and systematic underpayment patterns. Non-compliance flags must generate alerts to the crewing manager and payroll department with detailed deviation calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R009	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Hours of Work and Rest Compliance Recording and Analysis	The system must record and analyze hours of work and rest for each seafarer in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A2.3 and STCW Section A-VIII/1, enforcing the applicable limits: minimum 10 hours rest in any 24-hour period (split into no more than two periods, one of which must be at least 6 hours), minimum 77 hours rest in any 7-day period, and maximum 14 hours work in any 24-hour period. The system must accept work-rest hour data from vessel-based recording systems (electronic or manual entry with officer verification) and perform automated compliance calculations. For flag states that permit exceptions (e.g., hours of rest may be reduced to 70 hours per week for a maximum of two consecutive weeks), the system must track exception periods and enforce the compensatory rest requirements. AI must identify vessels with chronic work-rest compliance issues, correlate non-compliance with safety incident data, and recommend manning level adjustments or watch schedule modifications for specific trade routes (e.g., vessels on the short-sea Gulf feeder route calling Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali multiple times weekly).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R010	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Annual Leave Entitlement and Accrual Compliance	The system must calculate and track annual leave entitlements for each seafarer in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A2.4 (minimum 2.5 calendar days per month of employment). Leave accrual must be calculated daily based on the seafarer's employment start date, with separate tracking for annual leave, compensatory leave for public holidays worked, and any additional leave per the applicable CBA. The system must display each seafarer's leave balance in days, with projections showing the accumulated leave at contract end date. For multi-entity operations, the system must apply the more favorable leave entitlement between the MLC minimum, the flag state requirement, and the entity-specific CBA. Leave encashment calculations must use the correct wage basis per the CBA and generate payments in the seafarer's contracted currency. AI must identify seafarers with leave balances approaching or exceeding the maximum carry-forward limit per the applicable CBA and recommend scheduling shore leave or contract completion to prevent forfeiture and compliance issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R011	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Onboard Complaint Procedure Compliance Verification	The system must verify and monitor compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A5.1.5 onboard complaint procedures for each vessel. The system must confirm that each vessel has a documented and posted complaint procedure, the procedure is available in the working language of the vessel and in English, a designated onboard complaint handler is appointed and trained, the complaint log is maintained and reviewed, and shore-based contact details (company DPA, flag state competent authority, port state inspection authority) are posted in crew areas. The system must track all complaints filed through the onboard procedure, monitoring resolution timelines against the company SLA (initial response within 24 hours, resolution within 14 days) and the flag state requirements. For vessels operating in GCC waters, the complaint procedure must also reference local labor complaint mechanisms (Qatar MOIA labor complaints, UAE MOHRE complaints, KSA labor court procedures). AI must analyze complaint resolution data fleet-wide, flagging vessels with above-average complaint rates or resolution delays and recommending management intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R012	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Flag State Inspection and Audit Compliance Coordination	The system must manage flag state MLC inspections and audits for each vessel, tracking initial certification inspections, annual endorsement inspections, intermediate inspections, renewal inspections, and any additional ad hoc inspections triggered by complaints or PSC detentions. Each inspection must be recorded with the inspection type, inspecting authority (flag state administration or RO such as Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas), inspector name, inspection date and port, findings categorized by MLC Title and Standard, deficiency classification (observation, non-conformity, major non-conformity), corrective action plan with responsible officer and deadline, and verification closure date. The system must maintain the vessel's MLC compliance history as a continuous timeline, enabling trend analysis. AI must predict inspection outcomes based on the vessel's current compliance posture and historical finding patterns, highlighting areas most likely to attract findings. Pre-inspection preparation checklists must be auto-generated and distributed to the vessel 30 days before scheduled inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R013	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Social Security Protection Compliance Per Seafarer Nationality	The system must track social security protection arrangements for each seafarer in compliance with MLC 2006 Standard A4.5, ensuring that every seafarer has access to social security protection no less favorable than that enjoyed by shore workers in the flag state. The system must map each seafarer's nationality to the applicable bilateral or multilateral social security agreements between the flag state and the seafarer's country of residence. For the operating entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must track compliance with local social security requirements: GOSI contributions for KSA-based seafarers, WPS compliance for UAE-based seafarers, Qatar social insurance for Qatari nationals, and EPF/ESI contributions for Indian seafarers. The system must calculate employer and employee contribution amounts in the applicable currency, generate contribution payment schedules, and reconcile payments against statutory requirements. AI must flag any gaps in social security coverage and recommend corrective enrollment actions before flag state or PSC inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R014	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Continuous Improvement and Corrective Action Tracking	The system must implement a structured corrective and preventive action (CAPA) tracking mechanism for all MLC compliance gaps identified through internal audits, PSC inspections, flag state inspections, crew complaints, and AI-driven compliance scans. Each CAPA must have a unique tracking number, source of finding, MLC Standard reference, description of the non-compliance, root cause analysis (using the 5-Why or Ishikawa method), immediate corrective action taken, long-term preventive action planned, responsible officer, target completion date, verification method, and closure status. The system must enforce that CAPAs are not closed without evidence of effective implementation, verified by a person independent of the original non-compliance. AI must analyze CAPA data fleet-wide to identify systemic issues requiring management system changes rather than vessel-specific fixes, and must track the effectiveness of closed CAPAs by monitoring for recurrence of similar findings. CAPA reports must be exportable in English and Arabic for management review meetings and regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R015	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Compliance Document Version Control and Archival	The system must maintain strict version control for all MLC compliance documents including DMLC Part II measures, onboard complaint procedures, SEA templates, company policies referenced in the DMLC, and crew welfare plans. Each document must have a version number, effective date, approval authority, review due date, and change history log. When a new version is published, the system must distribute it to all applicable vessels with electronic acknowledgment tracking by the master and relevant officers. Superseded versions must be archived with full retrieval capability for historical audits and legal proceedings. The system must enforce a maximum review period of 12 months for all MLC compliance documents and generate review reminders 60 days before the due date. Document templates must support bilingual content (English and Arabic) and Hijri calendar dates for KSA and Qatar entity documents. AI must detect when regulatory changes (MLC amendments, flag state circulars) necessitate document updates and automatically queue affected documents for review, linking the specific regulatory change to the impacted document sections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R016	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	Multi-Flag-State Compliance Comparison and Gap Analysis	The system must support comparison of MLC compliance requirements across multiple flag states under which the fleet vessels are registered. For each MLC Standard, the system must display the baseline MLC requirement alongside any flag state variations (stricter requirements or permitted exceptions) for all flag states in the fleet. The comparison must generate a gap analysis report identifying where a vessel changing flag would face additional compliance requirements or where a single company policy could satisfy multiple flag state requirements simultaneously. For the operating entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must also incorporate the respective national maritime legislation that supplements or implements MLC requirements (Qatar Maritime Law, UAE Federal Law No. 26 of 1981, KSA Ports Authority regulations, Indian Merchant Shipping Act 1958 as amended). AI must recommend a harmonized compliance approach that meets the strictest requirement across all flag states in the fleet, minimizing the administrative burden of maintaining flag-state-specific procedures while ensuring no compliance gaps exist.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R017	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Compliance Analytics and Executive Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive MLC compliance analytics and executive reports for senior management and regulatory submissions. Reports must include fleet-wide MLC compliance score (percentage of requirements fully met), compliance trend over time (monthly, quarterly, annually), top non-compliance areas ranked by frequency and severity, PSC detention risk assessment per vessel, financial exposure from potential non-compliance penalties, and comparative benchmarking against industry average detention and deficiency rates from MoU annual reports (Tokyo MoU, Indian Ocean MoU, Riyadh MoU). Reports must be available in dashboard format for real-time monitoring and in exportable PDF format with Arabic RTL layout support for board presentations. The AI engine must generate natural language executive summaries in both English and Arabic, highlighting critical compliance risks, improvement achievements, and recommended strategic actions. Reports must support drill-down from fleet level to entity level to individual vessel level, with data filterable by MLC Title, Standard, vessel type, flag state, and operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-020-2-004-R018	FEAT-020-2-004	MOD-020	MLC Regulatory Update Monitoring and Impact Assessment	The system must continuously monitor for MLC 2006 amendments, ILO maritime session resolutions, flag state implementation updates, and port state control regime changes that affect MLC compliance obligations. Monitoring sources must include the ILO official gazette, flag state maritime administration circulars, MoU secretariat publications (Paris MoU, Tokyo MoU, Indian Ocean MoU, Riyadh MoU), and recognized organization technical circulars. Each regulatory update must be captured with the source, publication date, effective date, summary of changes, and affected MLC Standards. AI must automatically perform an impact assessment against the current fleet compliance posture, identifying specific vessels, policies, procedures, or crew management practices that require modification. Impact assessments must be translated into actionable work items assigned to responsible officers with implementation deadlines aligned to the regulatory effective date. The system must maintain a regulatory change log accessible to all compliance stakeholders in English and Arabic, serving as the single source of truth for MLC regulatory evolution affecting the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R001	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Service Loop Creation with Port Rotation	The system must allow users to create a new service loop by defining a unique loop code, loop name in both English and Arabic, a frequency (weekly, fortnightly, monthly), and an ordered sequence of port calls using UN/LOCODE identifiers (e.g., QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INNSA for JNPT). Each port call in the rotation must specify the terminal code, estimated arrival day offset from the loop start, estimated departure day offset, planned dwell hours, and whether the call is a loading port, discharge port, or transshipment hub. The system must enforce a minimum of two port calls per loop and a maximum of forty.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R002	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Vessel Assignment to Service Loops	The system must support assigning one or more vessels to a service loop, specifying each vessel's IMO number, name, flag state, nominal TEU capacity, reefer plug count, and dangerous goods class certifications. When a vessel is assigned, the system must calculate the required number of vessels to maintain the loop frequency based on total round-voyage days. For example, a 42-day round-voyage weekly service requires six vessels. The system must warn if insufficient vessels are assigned to maintain the declared frequency and must prevent double-assignment of a vessel to overlapping loops without explicit override approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R004	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Service Loop Lifecycle State Management	The system must enforce a lifecycle state machine for each service loop with the following states: Draft, Proposed, Active, Suspended, and Retired. Transitions must follow business rules: Draft to Proposed requires at least one complete rotation defined; Proposed to Active requires at least the minimum vessel count assigned and commercial approval from the trade manager; Active to Suspended requires a reason code (weather, geopolitical, demand shortfall, vessel breakdown) and an estimated resume date; Suspended to Active requires re-validation of vessel availability; any state to Retired requires confirmation that no outstanding bookings exist on future voyages. All state transitions must be audit-logged with timestamp, user ID, and entity code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R005	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Multi-Entity Loop Ownership and Visibility	The system must support multi-entity ownership of service loops across the four operating entities: Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Each service loop must have a designated owning entity and may grant visibility or operational rights to other entities. For example, a loop owned by the UAE entity operating from Jebel Ali may grant booking rights to the Qatar entity for Hamad Port calls. Entity-level access controls must ensure that financial data (costs, revenue allocation) for a loop is only visible to entities with explicit financial visibility rights. The owning entity must approve any changes to the loop rotation or vessel assignments proposed by partner entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R006	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Proforma Schedule Generation	The system must generate proforma (planned) voyage schedules from the service loop rotation template for a configurable future horizon of up to 52 weeks. Each proforma voyage must be assigned a unique voyage number following the convention LOOPCODE-YYWW-DIR (e.g., AGS-2601-E for eastbound week 01 of 2026). The proforma must include ETA and ETD at each port call, adjusted for known port holidays, Friday closures in Qatar/KSA, and Hijri calendar observances such as Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. Users must be able to manually override individual port call dates within a proforma voyage while the system highlights deviations from the standard rotation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R007	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	AI-Powered Loop Optimization Recommendations	The system must provide AI-driven recommendations for optimizing service loop rotations based on historical cargo demand patterns, bunker fuel price forecasts, port congestion indices, and seasonal trade volume fluctuations. The AI engine must analyze at least 24 months of historical booking data per trade lane and generate recommendations such as adding or removing a port call, adjusting rotation frequency, or swapping vessel size. Each recommendation must include a projected impact on transit times, estimated cost savings or revenue uplift in USD, and a confidence score between 0 and 100. Recommendations must be reviewable and approvable by the trade route manager before implementation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R008	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Rotation Change Impact Analysis	When a user proposes a change to an active service loop rotation (adding a port, removing a port, or resequencing calls), the system must perform an automated impact analysis before the change is committed. The analysis must identify: number of existing bookings affected, cargo that would require rebooking or transshipment rerouting, transit time changes for all port pairs, effect on vessel scheduling and bunker consumption, and contractual obligations under active slot agreements that may be violated. The impact report must be presented in a structured format with severity indicators (critical, warning, informational) and require explicit acknowledgment before the rotation change is applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R009	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Offline Service Loop Data Access	The system must support offline access to service loop data for operations personnel at ports with intermittent connectivity, particularly relevant for certain terminals in the Indian subcontinent region. When connectivity is lost, the system must cache the most recently synchronized service loop definitions, active proforma schedules for the next four weeks, and vessel assignments locally on the user's device. Users must be able to view loop details, rotation schedules, and transit time matrices in offline mode. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize any locally created notes or flagged issues back to the central server with conflict detection and resolution prompts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R010	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Arabic RTL Interface for Loop Management	The system must render the entire service loop management interface in a fully functional Arabic right-to-left (RTL) layout when the user's language preference is set to Arabic. All form labels, field descriptions, validation messages, tooltips, and navigation elements must be displayed in Arabic. Port rotation diagrams must mirror horizontally so that the reading flow proceeds from right to left. Date fields must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar inputs with a toggle control. Numeric fields must accept both Western Arabic numerals (0-9) and Eastern Arabic-Indic numerals (٠-٩). All user-entered Arabic text must be stored in UTF-8 encoding with proper bidirectional text markers for mixed Arabic-English content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R011	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Service Loop KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time KPI dashboard for each active service loop displaying: schedule reliability percentage (on-time arrival within a configurable threshold, defaulting to 24 hours), average port dwell time versus planned, vessel utilization as a percentage of nominal TEU capacity, average transit time deviation from proforma, and bunker consumption per TEU-mile. KPIs must be aggregable by time period (weekly, monthly, quarterly), by direction (eastbound, westbound, northbound, southbound), and by entity. The dashboard must support currency display in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with exchange rates sourced from the ERP's central treasury module. All KPI tiles must be exportable as PNG images or PDF reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R012	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Integration with Vessel Schedule Module	The system must integrate with the Vessel Schedule and Fleet Management module to automatically receive vessel position updates (AIS data), actual arrival and departure times, and vessel availability changes. When a vessel assigned to a service loop reports a delay exceeding a configurable threshold (default 12 hours), the system must automatically recalculate downstream ETAs for all subsequent port calls in the current voyage and trigger notifications to affected booking parties. The integration must use asynchronous event-driven messaging to ensure that service loop schedule displays are updated within five minutes of a vessel event being recorded in the fleet management system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R013	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Seasonal and Ramadan Schedule Adjustments	The system must support seasonal schedule variants for service loops that operate differently during specific periods. In particular, the system must allow definition of Ramadan schedule overlays where port call dwell times are extended to account for reduced working hours at terminals in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. The Ramadan period must be automatically determined based on the Hijri calendar with the ability for manual date override. Additionally, the system must support monsoon season adjustments for Indian ports (typically June through September) where weather-related delays increase average transit times. Schedule variants must be pre-configured and automatically activated based on date triggers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R014	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Service Loop Cloning and Templating	The system must allow authorized users to clone an existing service loop to create a new loop with the same rotation structure, optionally modifying specific port calls, frequencies, or vessel assignments during the cloning process. Cloning must generate new unique identifiers for the loop and all associated proforma voyages while preserving the source loop's configuration as a reference. Additionally, the system must support saving any loop configuration as a reusable template. Templates must be categorized by trade corridor (e.g., Gulf-India, Intra-Gulf, Gulf-Red Sea) and tagged with metadata including typical vessel size range, expected weekly TEU volume, and primary commodity types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R015	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every service loop, recording all create, read, update, and delete operations with full before-and-after snapshots of changed data fields. Each audit record must capture the timestamp in UTC, the authenticated user's identity, the originating entity code, the IP address or device identifier, and the business justification if one was required by the state transition. Audit logs must be immutable and retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Financial Centre (QFC) regulations, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 47 of 2022 on corporate tax, and Indian Companies Act 2013 record retention requirements. The audit log must be searchable by date range, user, entity, and loop code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-001-R016	FEAT-021-1-001	MOD-021	Capacity Allocation per Port Call	The system must allow the trade route manager to define capacity allocation limits at each port call within a service loop. Allocation must be specified in TEU and weight (metric tonnes) for each container category: dry standard 20ft, dry standard 40ft, dry high-cube 40ft, reefer 20ft, reefer 40ft, flat-rack, open-top, and tank containers. The system must enforce that total allocated capacity across all port calls does not exceed the assigned vessel's nominal capacity minus a configurable operational buffer (default 5%). When bookings approach 90% of the allocated capacity at any port call, the system must trigger an AI-generated alert recommending whether to increase allocation, waitlist bookings, or suggest alternative routing via another service loop.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R001	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Definition and Configuration	The system must allow users to define trade lanes as directional port pairs using UN/LOCODE identifiers, specifying an origin port and a destination port. Each trade lane must have a unique code combining origin and destination (e.g., AEJEA-INNSA for Jebel Ali to JNPT), a descriptive name in both English and Arabic, and classification tags including trade corridor (Intra-Gulf, Gulf-Indian Subcontinent, Gulf-Red Sea), primary commodity types (petrochemicals, consumer goods, foodstuffs, building materials, automobiles), and container type mix expectations. The system must support both direct trade lanes (single service loop) and indirect trade lanes (requiring transshipment) and must automatically identify which active service loops serve each defined trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R002	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Historical Volume Analysis with Trend Detection	The system must provide historical cargo volume analysis for each trade lane displaying TEU counts, weight in metric tonnes, and revenue in the trade lane's base currency over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) for up to five years of historical data. The AI engine must automatically detect and highlight statistically significant trends including seasonal patterns, year-over-year growth or decline rates, and anomalous volume spikes or drops. Trend detection must account for Hijri calendar events (Ramadan, Hajj season) that affect trade volumes in Gulf ports. The analysis must distinguish between laden and empty container movements and must be filterable by container type (dry, reefer, special), customer segment, and booking entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R003	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Freight Rate Benchmarking and Index Tracking	The system must track average freight rates per TEU for each trade lane and benchmark them against industry indices such as the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Drewry World Container Index (WCI), and Freightos Baltic Index (FBX) where applicable. Freight rates must be stored in USD as the base currency with automatic conversion display capability for AED, QAR, SAR, and INR using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. The system must display rate trends on interactive charts with the ability to overlay competitor rate intelligence (manually entered or imported from market data feeds). Rate analysis must support segmentation by contract type (spot, short-term contract, long-term contract) and by cargo type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R004	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	AI-Driven Demand Forecasting per Trade Lane	The system must generate AI-powered demand forecasts for each trade lane projecting TEU volumes for the next 4, 8, 12, and 26 weeks. The forecasting model must incorporate historical booking patterns, macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth rates for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India), commodity price indices relevant to the trade lane's primary cargo types, port infrastructure developments (e.g., Hamad Port Phase 2 expansion), and geopolitical risk factors. Each forecast must include a confidence interval (expressed as a range at 80% and 95% confidence levels) and must identify the top three factors driving the forecast. Forecasts must be automatically refreshed weekly and must track forecast accuracy against actual volumes with a rolling MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) metric.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R005	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Market Share Analysis by Trade Lane	The system must calculate and display the organization's market share for each trade lane based on internally tracked TEU volumes versus estimated total market volume. Total market volume estimates must be sourced from configurable external data feeds or manually entered by the commercial intelligence team. Market share must be presented as a percentage and visualized over time to show trend direction. The system must support competitive landscape mapping where major competitors on each trade lane are listed with their estimated capacity, service frequency, and transit times. The AI engine must flag trade lanes where market share has declined by more than two percentage points in any rolling quarter and recommend corrective actions such as rate adjustments, service frequency changes, or targeted marketing campaigns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R006	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Commodity Flow Mapping and Visualization	The system must provide an interactive geographic map visualization showing commodity flows between port pairs with line thickness proportional to TEU volume and color coding by commodity category. The map must be zoomable from a regional overview (Arabian Gulf to Indian subcontinent) down to individual port terminal views. Users must be able to filter flows by time period, commodity type, container type, and customer segment. The visualization must support animation showing flow changes over time (monthly snapshots) and must render correctly in both LTR and RTL interface modes. Map layers must include port infrastructure indicators, free trade zone boundaries (JAFZA in Jebel Ali, QFZ in Qatar, KASEZ in India), and customs territory demarcations relevant to trade lane analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R007	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Connectivity and Transshipment Analysis	The system must analyze and display all possible routing options between any two ports in the network, including direct connections and transshipment routings via hub ports such as Jebel Ali and Hamad Port. For each routing option, the system must show: total transit time, number of transshipment moves, estimated additional cost per TEU for each transshipment, reliability score based on historical connection performance, and carbon emission estimate per TEU. The AI engine must recommend the optimal routing based on a weighted scoring model combining transit time, cost, reliability, and emissions factors with user-configurable weights. When a direct service is unavailable, the system must automatically identify the best transshipment routing and display it as the recommended alternative.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R008	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Seasonal Demand Pattern Recognition	The system must identify and catalog recurring seasonal demand patterns for each trade lane using at least three years of historical data. Patterns must be classified by type: Ramadan pre-stocking surge (typically increased consumer goods imports to Gulf ports four to six weeks before Ramadan), monsoon season decline (reduced volumes at Indian west coast ports during June through September), Diwali festive season spike (increased exports from India in September through November), year-end inventory building, and post-Hajj demand normalization. Each recognized pattern must include its typical duration in weeks, average volume deviation from baseline as a percentage, and historical consistency score. The system must use these patterns to generate automated capacity planning alerts at least eight weeks before the expected onset of each seasonal demand shift.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R009	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Comparison and Ranking Dashboard	The system must provide a comparative dashboard allowing users to select up to ten trade lanes simultaneously and compare them across standardized KPIs: total TEU volume, revenue per TEU, average freight rate, utilization percentage, transit time, schedule reliability, and year-over-year growth rate. The comparison must be displayable in both tabular and radar chart formats. Users must be able to rank trade lanes by any single KPI or by a composite score using customizable weighting. The dashboard must support currency normalization to any of the five supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) for fair comparison. Export functionality must generate formatted PDF reports with the organization's letterhead and bilingual (English/Arabic) headers suitable for management presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R010	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Imbalance Detection and Empty Repositioning Analysis	The system must automatically detect trade imbalances between directional trade lanes (e.g., AEJEA-INNSA versus INNSA-AEJEA) by comparing laden TEU volumes in each direction on a rolling weekly and monthly basis. When an imbalance exceeds a configurable threshold (default 30% differential), the system must calculate the implied empty container repositioning cost based on current repositioning rates and flag the imbalance for review. The AI engine must recommend mitigation strategies such as triangulation routing, reduced export rates to attract backhaul cargo, or container leasing adjustments. The analysis must account for different container types since reefer and special equipment imbalances have higher repositioning costs than standard dry containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R011	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Regulatory and Compliance Impact per Trade Lane	The system must maintain a regulatory profile for each trade lane documenting applicable customs regulations, import/export restrictions, required documentation (bills of lading format, certificates of origin, phytosanitary certificates), and sanctions screening requirements for the origin and destination countries. For trade lanes involving Saudi Arabia, the system must flag compliance with SABER certification requirements. For Indian ports, ICEGATE (Indian Customs EDI Gateway) documentation requirements must be referenced. For Qatar, the system must account for Qatar Single Window (Al Nafitha) requirements. When regulations change, the system must alert trade lane managers and update the compliance profile within 48 hours. The regulatory profile must be viewable in both English and Arabic with reference links to official regulatory portals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R012	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Customer Concentration Risk Analysis	The system must analyze customer concentration risk for each trade lane by calculating the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI) based on TEU volume distribution among customers. The system must flag trade lanes where the top three customers account for more than 60% of total volume or where HHI exceeds 2500 (indicating high concentration). For each flagged trade lane, the system must display the customer breakdown with percentage shares, contract expiration dates, and historical booking consistency scores. The AI engine must recommend diversification strategies and estimate the revenue impact if the largest customer were to reduce volumes by 25% or 50%. Reports must be generatable per entity to support multi-entity risk assessment across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R013	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Automated Trade Lane Performance Alerts	The system must support configurable alert rules for trade lane performance metrics. Users must be able to define alert thresholds for: volume drop exceeding X% versus prior period, average freight rate falling below a minimum floor, utilization dropping below a target percentage, transit time exceeding service level agreement limits, and market share declining below a defined floor. Alerts must be deliverable via in-app notification, email, and SMS (supporting international mobile numbers with country codes for Qatar +974, UAE +971, KSA +966, India +91). Each alert must include a concise AI-generated summary of the likely cause and a suggested action. Alert rules must be configurable per entity and per user role, and users must be able to snooze alerts for a defined period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R014	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Data Import and External Feed Integration	The system must support importing trade lane data from external sources including: container tracking platforms (CargoSmart, INTTRA), port community systems (Hamad Port's Mwani, Dubai Trade, ICEGATE), and commercial market data providers (Container Trades Statistics, Alphaliner). Import formats must include CSV, Excel (XLSX), JSON, and XML with configurable field mapping templates. The system must validate imported data for completeness, detect duplicate records, and flag anomalies (e.g., volumes exceeding historical norms by more than three standard deviations). Scheduled automatic imports must be configurable with retry logic for failed connections and email notifications upon import completion or failure. All imported data must be tagged with its source and import timestamp for traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R015	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Carbon Emission Estimation	The system must calculate estimated carbon emissions (CO2e in metric tonnes) for each trade lane based on the IMO Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) methodology. The calculation must use vessel-specific fuel consumption data, actual voyage distances between port pairs (sourced from the service loop rotation), cargo weight, and fuel type (VLSFO, MGO, LNG). The system must display carbon intensity per TEU-kilometer for each trade lane and flag lanes that exceed the IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) thresholds. Emission data must be aggregable by trade corridor, entity, customer, and time period to support EU ETS reporting requirements for vessels calling at European ports in extended rotations and to align with IMO 2030 greenhouse gas reduction targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R016	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Offline Trade Lane Report Caching	The system must support generating and caching trade lane analysis reports for offline access by commercial teams traveling between the four operating regions. Users must be able to select specific trade lanes and report types (volume analysis, rate trends, demand forecast, competitor analysis) for offline synchronization. Cached reports must be stored in an encrypted format on the local device with AES-256 encryption and must automatically expire after a configurable period (default seven days). When the user reconnects, the system must display a notification indicating the age of cached data and offer to refresh. The offline cache must support a maximum of 50 trade lane reports and must not exceed 500 megabytes of local storage. Reports in the cache must retain bilingual display capability without requiring an active network connection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-002-R017	FEAT-021-1-002	MOD-021	Trade Lane Scenario Modeling and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a what-if scenario modeling tool that allows trade managers to simulate the impact of hypothetical changes on trade lane performance. Scenarios must support variables including: freight rate changes (percentage increase or decrease), volume shifts (absolute TEU or percentage change), new competitor entry with specified capacity and frequency, port infrastructure changes (new terminal, berth closures), fuel price fluctuations, and currency exchange rate movements. Each scenario must calculate the projected impact on revenue, cost, margin, market share, and vessel utilization. Users must be able to save scenarios with descriptive names, compare up to four scenarios side by side, and share scenarios with colleagues across entities. The AI engine must validate scenario inputs for reasonableness and flag implausible combinations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R001	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Trade Lane Revenue Recognition and Tracking	The system must track and recognize revenue for each trade lane by aggregating all freight income from bookings that traverse the trade lane, including ocean freight, bunker adjustment factor (BAF), currency adjustment factor (CAF), terminal handling charges (THC), documentation fees, container cleaning charges, and any other surcharges defined in the tariff structure. Revenue must be recorded in the billing currency of each booking and consolidated into the trade lane's base reporting currency using the exchange rate applicable on the bill of lading date. The system must support revenue recognition under both IFRS 15 (performance obligation completion) and Indian Accounting Standard (Ind AS) 115, ensuring that revenue is recognized when the vessel departs the loading port for each leg of the trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R002	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Voyage Cost Allocation to Trade Lanes	The system must allocate voyage costs to individual trade lanes based on configurable allocation methodologies. Supported allocation methods must include: TEU-mile proportional allocation (costs distributed based on each trade lane's share of total TEU-miles on the voyage), revenue-proportional allocation, and direct assignment for costs specifically attributable to a single trade lane. Voyage costs to be allocated must include bunker fuel consumption, port dues, pilotage and towage fees, canal transit fees (Suez Canal tolls where applicable), stevedoring charges at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, and vessel charter hire or depreciation. The system must maintain full traceability from each cost line item back to the source voyage and the allocation methodology applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R003	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Multi-Currency P&L Consolidation	The system must consolidate trade lane P&L statements across multiple currencies, supporting USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR as both transaction and reporting currencies. Exchange rates must be sourced from the central treasury module and applied consistently using the organization's defined rate type policy: transaction date rate for revenue items, average period rate for operating expenses, and closing rate for balance sheet items. The system must separately track and display foreign exchange gains and losses arising from currency movements between the booking date and the settlement date. P&L reports must be generatable in any of the five supported currencies with full drill-down capability from the consolidated total to individual booking-level transactions. Currency conversion methodology must comply with IAS 21 (The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R004	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Contribution Margin Calculation and Display	The system must calculate and display contribution margin at multiple levels for each trade lane: Contribution Margin 1 (revenue minus variable voyage costs including bunker, port charges, and cargo handling), Contribution Margin 2 (CM1 minus semi-variable costs including feeder costs, transshipment charges, and empty repositioning), and Contribution Margin 3 (CM2 minus allocated fixed costs including vessel depreciation or charter hire, crew costs, insurance, and overhead allocations). Each margin level must be displayed as both an absolute amount and a percentage of revenue. The system must highlight trade lanes where any contribution margin level falls below configurable thresholds (e.g., CM1 below 15%, CM2 below 8%) using color-coded indicators (red, amber, green) and must trigger AI-generated margin improvement recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R005	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Per-TEU Profitability Breakdown	The system must compute per-TEU profitability for each trade lane by dividing total revenue, total variable cost, total fixed cost, and net profit by the number of TEUs carried. Per-TEU calculations must be available for standard 20ft equivalent, with 40ft containers counted as two TEU and 45ft high-cube containers counted as 2.25 TEU. The per-TEU profitability must be further segmentable by container type (dry, reefer, special), by customer tier (premium, standard, economy), and by contract type (spot, short-term, long-term). The system must display per-TEU profitability trends over time as interactive line charts and must benchmark per-TEU margins against configurable targets set by the trade route management team. Reports must support both English and Arabic display with proper number formatting for each locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R006	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	AI-Driven Margin Optimization Recommendations	The system must employ AI algorithms to analyze trade lane P&L data and generate actionable margin optimization recommendations. The AI engine must evaluate at least the following optimization levers: freight rate adjustments (comparing current rates against market benchmarks and elasticity models), cargo mix optimization (shifting container type ratios toward higher-margin categories), slow steaming analysis (calculating bunker savings versus transit time impact), port call sequence optimization (testing alternate rotations for cost reduction), and empty repositioning cost reduction through triangulation. Each recommendation must include a quantified projected impact on trade lane margin expressed in both absolute currency amounts (USD) and percentage points. Recommendations must be ranked by estimated impact and implementation feasibility, and the system must track which recommendations were accepted, rejected, or deferred along with actual realized impact versus projected impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R007	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Budget versus Actual Variance Analysis	The system must support definition of annual budgets for each trade lane with monthly or quarterly breakdowns for both revenue and cost line items. The variance analysis must compare actual P&L results against budget for each period, calculating both absolute variance and percentage variance. Variances exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10% unfavorable) must be automatically flagged and require a variance explanation to be recorded by the responsible trade manager. The system must support budget revisions with version tracking (original budget, revised forecast 1, revised forecast 2) and must calculate variance against both the original budget and the latest forecast. Budget data must be importable from Excel templates formatted per entity and must support multi-currency budgets aligned to each entity's functional currency: QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, and INR for India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R008	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Inter-Entity Transfer Pricing for Shared Trade Lanes	The system must handle inter-entity transfer pricing for trade lanes where revenue and costs span multiple operating entities. When cargo is booked by the UAE entity but loaded at Hamad Port (Qatar entity's terminal), the system must apply configurable transfer pricing rules to allocate revenue between the booking entity and the operating entity. Transfer pricing methodologies must include: cost-plus markup, comparable uncontrolled price (CUP), and profit split methods, selectable per entity pair and per trade lane. The system must generate inter-entity invoices or credit notes based on the applied transfer pricing rules, denominated in the receiving entity's functional currency. All transfer pricing calculations must maintain an audit trail and comply with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines, Qatar Tax Authority regulations, UAE Corporate Tax requirements, and Indian Transfer Pricing provisions under Section 92 of the Income Tax Act.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R009	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Bunker Cost Impact Analysis	The system must provide detailed bunker fuel cost analysis for each trade lane, calculating bunker cost as a percentage of total voyage cost and as a per-TEU amount. The analysis must support multiple fuel types (VLSFO, HSFO with scrubber, MGO for emission control areas, LNG for dual-fuel vessels) and must track the bunker surcharge (BAF) recovery rate, defined as the ratio of BAF revenue collected to actual bunker cost incurred. When the BAF recovery rate falls below a configurable threshold (default 85%), the system must flag the trade lane and recommend BAF formula adjustments. The system must also perform bunker price sensitivity analysis showing the impact on trade lane margin for bunker price changes of plus or minus 10%, 20%, and 30% from the current price level. Bunker prices must be sourced from port-specific bunker price feeds for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R010	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Slot Cost Accounting for VSA Partners	The system must accurately account for slot costs and slot revenues arising from vessel sharing agreements (VSAs) in the trade lane P&L. When the organization utilizes slots on a partner vessel, the slot cost must be recorded based on the agreed slot rate (per TEU or per slot) as defined in the VSA contract. When the organization provides slots to partners, the slot revenue must be recognized based on the same contractual terms. The system must perform slot cost reconciliation comparing the contracted slot rate against the imputed cost of operating the equivalent capacity on an owned or chartered vessel. The P&L must clearly segregate own-vessel trade lane performance from VSA-slot trade lane performance to provide visibility into whether VSA participation is accretive or dilutive to trade lane profitability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R011	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Reefer and Special Cargo Premium Analysis	The system must separately track and analyze the financial performance of reefer containers and special cargo (out-of-gauge, hazardous goods DG classes 1 through 9, break-bulk) on each trade lane. For reefer cargo, the system must calculate the reefer premium as the difference between actual reefer freight rate and the equivalent dry container rate, and track reefer-specific costs including power consumption, pre-trip inspection costs, and genset fuel. For special cargo, the system must track surcharges (hazardous goods surcharge, out-of-gauge surcharge, heavy-lift surcharge) and additional handling costs. The analysis must determine whether the reefer and special cargo segments are margin-accretive on each trade lane and must recommend optimal reefer slot allocation based on demand patterns and margin contribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R010	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Bulk Demurrage Recalculation Engine	The system must provide a bulk recalculation function that re-computes demurrage charges for a selected set of containers when rate schedules are retroactively corrected, holiday calendars are updated, or free time allocations are changed. The recalculation must process up to 10,000 containers in a single batch, comparing the previously calculated amount against the newly computed amount, and generating a variance report showing the delta per container. Recalculated charges must not overwrite the original calculation; instead, the system must create adjustment entries that link back to the original charge, maintaining a full audit trail. Recalculation must be triggerable by authorized users only and must log the initiator, reason, and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R012	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Trade Lane P&L Report Generation and Scheduling	The system must support generation of standard P&L reports for each trade lane in multiple formats: detailed P&L statement (all line items with full drill-down), summary P&L (key aggregated figures), and executive P&L dashboard (visual with charts and KPI tiles). Reports must be generatable on demand and on a scheduled basis (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly). Scheduled reports must be automatically distributed via email to configurable recipient lists per entity. All reports must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. Report output formats must include PDF (for formal distribution), Excel (for further analysis), and interactive HTML (for in-app viewing). The system must support report watermarking with confidentiality classifications (Public, Internal, Confidential, Strictly Confidential) as required by the organization's information security policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R013	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Historical P&L Trend and Comparative Analysis	The system must provide historical P&L trend analysis for each trade lane over configurable periods up to five years. Trend visualization must include revenue, cost, and margin metrics plotted on time-series charts with the ability to overlay multiple trade lanes for comparison. The system must support year-over-year and quarter-over-quarter comparative views with automatic calculation of growth rates and trend lines. Seasonality decomposition must isolate recurring patterns from underlying trends and one-time events. The AI engine must automatically annotate significant inflection points in the P&L trend with probable causes (e.g., rate war, demand spike from Ramadan pre-stocking, bunker price shock, new competitor entry). Users must be able to add manual annotations to supplement the AI-generated ones. All trend data must be available in inflation-adjusted terms using configurable CPI indices for each operating country.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R014	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Cost Accrual and Estimation for Open Voyages	The system must support cost accrual and estimation for voyages that are in progress and not yet financially closed. For each open voyage contributing to a trade lane, the system must estimate pending costs based on the voyage's proforma cost budget adjusted for known actuals (e.g., actual bunker consumption to date, port charges already invoiced). The estimated costs must be clearly distinguished from actual settled costs in the P&L display using visual indicators. The system must recalculate accruals daily as new actual cost data becomes available and must automatically reverse accruals when actual invoices are received and matched. At period-end (month-end, quarter-end), the system must generate an accrual report listing all estimated cost items by trade lane for review by the finance team before the entity's books are closed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R015	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Tax and Regulatory Cost Allocation per Trade Lane	The system must allocate applicable taxes and regulatory costs to each trade lane based on the jurisdictions involved. Tax items must include: Qatar corporate income tax (currently 10% under QFC or general tax law), UAE corporate tax (9% above AED 375,000 threshold), KSA income tax (20% for non-GCC entities) and Zakat (2.5% for Saudi-owned entities), and Indian corporate tax (25% standard rate) plus GST on domestic services. Regulatory costs must include ISPS (International Ship and Port Facility Security) code compliance charges, emissions-related costs (EU ETS allowances for applicable voyages, IMO levies), and port state control inspection costs. The system must maintain a configurable tax rule engine that can be updated when tax rates or regulations change without requiring code modifications, and must generate tax-compliant P&L reports suitable for submission to local tax authorities in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R016	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	P&L Data Access Controls and Entity Segregation	The system must enforce strict data access controls on trade lane P&L data based on the user's entity affiliation and role. Users from the Qatar entity must only see P&L data for trade lanes where the Qatar entity has financial visibility rights; similarly for UAE, KSA, and India entity users. Group-level users with cross-entity authorization must be able to view consolidated P&L across all entities with drill-down to individual entity contributions. The system must log all P&L data access events including view, export, and print actions for security audit purposes. Role-based access must distinguish between view-only access, data entry access (for cost inputs and accruals), and approval access (for budget sign-off and variance explanations). All access control rules must be configurable by the system administrator without requiring developer intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R017	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	Hijri and Gregorian Dual Calendar P&L Reporting	The system must support P&L reporting aligned to both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. For Gregorian reporting, standard monthly, quarterly, and annual periods must be used as the primary fiscal calendar aligned with each entity's fiscal year (Qatar: January to December, UAE: January to December or June to May configurable, KSA: Gregorian fiscal year January to December following recent reforms, India: April to March). For Hijri reporting, the system must generate P&L summaries by Hijri month using the Umm al-Qura calendar officially adopted in Saudi Arabia. The system must handle the mismatch between Gregorian and Hijri period boundaries by prorating revenue and cost items based on the number of days of overlap. Hijri period reports must display dates using both Hijri and Gregorian equivalents with Arabic month names displayed correctly in RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-003-R018	FEAT-021-1-003	MOD-021	P&L Simulation for New Trade Lane Evaluation	The system must provide a P&L simulation tool for evaluating the financial viability of launching a new trade lane before committing resources. The simulation must accept inputs including: estimated weekly TEU volume (low, base, high scenarios), projected freight rate per TEU by container type, estimated voyage costs based on vessel type and rotation distance, port charges sourced from the ERP's port tariff database for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and any other ports in the proposed rotation, and overhead allocation rates. The simulation must output a projected P&L statement for the first 12 months with monthly granularity, calculate the breakeven TEU volume, and determine the payback period for any required capital investment. The AI engine must benchmark the simulated P&L against comparable existing trade lanes and provide a viability rating (highly favorable, favorable, marginal, unfavorable) with supporting rationale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R009	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Customer port preference and routing defaults	Each customer entity must have configurable default port preferences for origin and destination, preferred vessel services, standard container type requirements, and INCOTERMS preferences (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, FCA). These defaults must auto-populate booking screens to accelerate data entry. The system must support multiple preference sets per customer keyed by trade lane. For example, a customer may prefer CIF terms with 40HC containers for Qatar-India trade but FOB with 20GP containers for UAE-KSA trade. Port preferences must reference valid UN/LOCODE entries and validate against active service routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R001	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Contract Registration and Storage	The system must allow authorized users to register new Vessel Sharing Agreement contracts by capturing: agreement reference number, agreement type (VSA, slot charter, slot exchange, slot purchase), contracting parties with their SCAC codes and legal entity names, effective date and expiry date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, covered service loops, covered trade lanes, and the agreement's governing law jurisdiction (typically English law, UAE law, or Indian law). The system must store the signed agreement document as an attachment (PDF, maximum 50 megabytes) and must support versioning for amendments. Each VSA must be linked to the relevant service loop(s) defined in the Service Loop Management feature, and the system must prevent registration of overlapping VSAs for the same service loop and trade direction unless explicitly approved by the commercial director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R002	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Slot Allocation Definition and Tracking	The system must allow definition of slot allocations for each VSA specifying the number of slots (in TEU) allocated to each party per voyage per port pair and direction. Allocations must be definable as: fixed allocation (guaranteed slots regardless of utilization), flexible allocation (base allocation with the right to request additional slots subject to availability), and proportional allocation (percentage of total vessel capacity). The system must track slot utilization in real time as bookings are confirmed against allocated slots, displaying current utilization as both absolute TEU count and percentage of allocation. When utilization approaches the allocation limit (configurable threshold, default 90%), the system must alert the slot manager and recommend actions such as requesting additional slots from the partner or redirecting bookings to an alternative service.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R003	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Partner Performance Monitoring	The system must monitor and report on VSA partner performance against agreed service level metrics defined in the agreement. Monitored metrics must include: schedule reliability of partner-operated vessels (percentage of port calls within the agreed arrival window), vessel substitution frequency and compliance with minimum vessel size requirements, cargo handling incident rate, documentation accuracy and timeliness, and response time for slot request approvals. The system must maintain a partner performance scorecard updated after each voyage with automatic calculation of a composite performance score on a scale of 0 to 100. When a partner's performance score falls below a configurable threshold (default 70), the system must generate an automated alert to the commercial director and compile a performance report suitable for formal partner review meetings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R004	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Slot Cost and Revenue Settlement Calculation	The system must calculate slot costs and revenues for financial settlement between VSA partners based on the contractual terms. Settlement calculations must support multiple pricing models: per-TEU slot rate (fixed rate per TEU utilized), per-slot lump sum (fixed fee per voyage regardless of utilization), cost-sharing model (proportional share of actual voyage costs based on slot ratio), and hybrid models combining fixed and variable components. The system must generate settlement statements per voyage and per period (monthly or quarterly as defined in the agreement) showing: slots allocated, slots utilized, applicable rate, gross amount, any adjustments (dead freight for unutilized guaranteed slots, overage charges for excess utilization), and net settlement amount. All settlements must be calculated in the agreement's base currency with conversion to each entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R005	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Lifecycle State Management	The system must enforce a lifecycle for each VSA contract with the following states: Draft (under negotiation, not yet effective), Pending Approval (terms agreed, awaiting internal sign-off), Active (fully operational and bookings permitted against allocated slots), Suspended (temporarily non-operational, no new bookings but existing bookings honored), Under Renewal (approaching expiry with renewal negotiations in progress), Expired (past expiry date with no renewal), and Terminated (ended early by mutual agreement or breach). State transitions must follow defined business rules: Draft to Pending Approval requires all mandatory contract fields to be populated and the agreement document to be attached; Pending Approval to Active requires sign-off from the commercial director and the finance director of the owning entity. The system must send automated notifications at configurable intervals (default 90, 60, and 30 days) before VSA expiry to initiate renewal discussions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R006	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Slot Swap and Exchange Agreement Tracking	The system must support tracking of slot swap arrangements where the organization exchanges slots on one service loop for equivalent slots from a partner on a different service loop. Each swap arrangement must record: the number of slots exchanged in each direction, the service loops involved, the trade lanes covered, the period of the swap, and any balancing payment if the exchange is not of equal value. The system must maintain a running balance of slot swaps per partner showing net slots received versus net slots provided, valued at the imputed market rate for each slot. When the net balance exceeds a configurable threshold (e.g., 500 TEU in aggregate), the system must flag the imbalance for review and recommend either a balancing payment or an adjustment to the swap terms. All swap transactions must be fully auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R007	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Capacity Pool Management for VSA Consortiums	The system must support capacity pool management for VSAs involving three or more shipping lines operating as a consortium. In a pooled arrangement, each party contributes a defined number of vessel deployments and receives a proportional share of total pool revenue or capacity. The system must track each party's vessel contributions (number of voyages, vessel size in TEU), calculate the pool equity share based on contributed capacity, and distribute revenue or capacity credits according to the pooling formula. The system must handle adjustments for vessel size differentials (e.g., a party deploying a 14,000 TEU vessel versus the standard 12,000 TEU vessel contributes proportionally more). Pool settlement statements must be generated monthly and must reconcile each party's contributed capacity against their utilization to determine net receivable or payable positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R011	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Estimation for Pre-Arrival Shipments	The system must provide an AI-powered estimation of potential demurrage exposure for shipments that have not yet arrived at the destination port. The estimation must consider the vessel ETA, historical average customs clearance time for the consignee at that port, the applicable free time, and the projected dwell time based on similar past shipments on the same trade lane. The estimate must be presented as a range (optimistic, most likely, pessimistic) with confidence intervals and must auto-update as the vessel ETA changes. Estimates must be viewable in the shipper's preferred currency and must support drill-down into the assumptions and historical data points used in the projection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R008	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	AI-Driven Slot Utilization Optimization	The system must employ AI algorithms to optimize slot utilization across all active VSAs. The AI engine must analyze historical booking patterns per trade lane, forecast demand for the next eight weeks, and recommend optimal slot allocation strategies. Recommendations must include: whether to release underutilized guaranteed slots back to the partner (and the latest date by which this must be communicated per contract terms), whether to request additional slots from partners for high-demand trade lanes, and whether to shift bookings between own-vessel services and VSA partner services based on relative cost and transit time. Each recommendation must quantify the expected financial benefit in USD and must consider contractual constraints such as minimum utilization requirements and slot release deadlines. The system must track recommendation acceptance rates and realized benefits versus projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R009	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Contract Amendment Management	The system must support formal amendment management for active VSA contracts. When a change is required (e.g., slot allocation adjustment, addition or removal of a port call, change in settlement terms), the user must create an amendment record linked to the parent agreement. The amendment must capture: amendment number (sequential), effective date, description of changes in English and Arabic, revised terms with tracked changes highlighting additions and deletions, and the approval workflow status. The system must maintain the full amendment history as an integral part of the VSA record so that the current effective terms can be reconstructed at any point in time by applying all amendments chronologically. Amendment approval must require sign-off from both the commercial and legal departments of the owning entity. The system must validate that amendments do not create conflicts with other active VSAs on the same service loop.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R010	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Competition Law Compliance Checks	The system must perform automated compliance checks to flag potential competition law concerns arising from VSA arrangements. The system must calculate the combined market share of all VSA partners on each covered trade lane and flag situations where the combined share exceeds configurable thresholds aligned with regulatory frameworks: 30% for EU Consortia Block Exemption Regulation (Commission Regulation EU 2024/1395 or successor), and analogous thresholds for Competition Commission of India (CCI) guidelines. When a threshold is exceeded or approached (within five percentage points), the system must generate an alert to the legal compliance team with a detailed market share calculation methodology and data sources. The system must maintain a compliance log documenting all checks performed, results, and any remedial actions taken, stored for a minimum of five years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R011	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Multi-Entity VSA Participation and Rights	The system must support scenarios where multiple entities within the organization participate in the same VSA. For example, the UAE entity may be the primary contracting party for a Gulf-India VSA, while the Qatar entity and India entity may have sub-allocation rights to book against the UAE entity's slot allocation. The system must track: which entity holds the primary contractual relationship, which entities have been granted sub-allocation rights and the quantum of their allocations, the internal transfer pricing mechanism for inter-entity slot usage, and the settlement workflow between entities. Bookings made by a sub-allocated entity must count against both the sub-allocation limit and the primary allocation. Financial settlement between entities must follow the inter-entity transfer pricing rules configured in the Trade Lane P&L module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R012	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Slot Release and Buyback Mechanism	The system must implement a slot release and buyback workflow as commonly required in VSA contracts. When the organization forecasts that it will not utilize its full slot allocation on a specific voyage, the system must allow the slot manager to release a defined number of slots back to the VSA partner by a contractual deadline (typically seven to fourteen days before vessel arrival at the loading port). The system must track released slots and any dead-freight charges that may apply if release occurs after the contractual deadline. Conversely, when the organization needs additional slots beyond its allocation, the system must support a buyback request workflow with approval routing to the partner via integrated EDI messaging (EDIFACT IFTMIN or equivalent). The system must maintain a ledger of all slot releases and buybacks per VSA with running totals for reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R013	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Financial Reconciliation and Dispute Management	The system must support financial reconciliation of VSA settlements by comparing the organization's calculated settlement amounts against partner-provided settlement statements. The reconciliation process must identify discrepancies by line item (voyage number, slot count, rate applied, adjustment type) and categorize them as: matched (within a configurable tolerance of 0.5%), minor discrepancy (between 0.5% and 2%), or major discrepancy (exceeding 2%). Unresolved discrepancies must be escalated through a dispute management workflow with states: Identified, Under Review, Disputed (sent to partner), Response Received, Resolved, and Written Off. Each dispute must track the financial amount in contention, supporting documentation, correspondence history, and resolution outcome. Settlement reconciliation must be performed per voyage and aggregated per period with an aging report showing unresolved disputes by age bucket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, over 90 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R014	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	EDI Integration for VSA Operations	The system must support electronic data interchange (EDI) with VSA partners for key operational messages. Supported EDI message types must include: BAPLIE (bayplan/stowage plan) for sharing vessel loading information, COPARN (container announcement) for pre-advising container movements, IFTMIN (booking confirmation) for slot booking requests and confirmations, IFTSTA (shipment status) for container tracking updates, and INVOIC (invoice) for settlement statement exchange. The system must support both EDIFACT (UN/CEFACT) and ANSI X12 message formats as well as emerging API-based alternatives using DCSA (Digital Container Shipping Association) standards. EDI messages must be processed through a configurable mapping engine that handles partner-specific variations in message structure. Failed EDI transmissions must be automatically retried with exponential backoff and must trigger alerts after three consecutive failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R013	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Customer payment terms and method defaults	The system must store default payment terms per customer entity including credit days (net 30, 60, 90), early payment discount percentages and cutoff days, accepted payment methods (bank transfer, letter of credit, cash against documents, open account), and preferred settlement currency from supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). Payment terms must be overridable at the booking level with appropriate approval workflow if deviating from the master. The system must support Islamic finance payment terms including Murabaha-based deferred payment structures commonly used in Qatar, UAE, and KSA shipping transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R015	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	VSA Dashboard with KPI Summary	The system must provide a comprehensive VSA management dashboard displaying key performance indicators across all active agreements. The dashboard must show: total number of active VSAs by type, aggregate slot allocation versus utilization as a percentage, top five most utilized and least utilized VSAs ranked by utilization rate, financial summary showing total slot revenue earned versus slot costs incurred with net position, partner performance league table ranked by composite score, upcoming VSA expiries within the next 90 days, and unresolved settlement disputes by value. The dashboard must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), by service loop, by partner, and by time period. All financial figures must be displayable in any of the five supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). The dashboard must support an Arabic RTL interface mode with proper formatting of all labels, legends, and data values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R016	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Slot Agreement Expiry and Renewal Workflow	The system must implement a structured renewal workflow for VSAs approaching expiry. The workflow must begin automatically at a configurable lead time (default 120 days before expiry) by creating a renewal task assigned to the responsible commercial manager. The renewal workflow must include stages: Performance Review (auto-populated with the partner's performance scorecard and financial summary for the agreement period), Terms Evaluation (AI-generated comparison of current terms against market benchmarks and alternative partner offerings), Negotiation (tracking of proposed terms, counteroffers, and meeting notes), Internal Approval (routing through commercial director and finance director for sign-off), and Execution (capture of signed renewal agreement). If no renewal is executed by the expiry date, the system must automatically transition the VSA to Expired status and alert the operations team to rebook any cargo on affected trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-1-004-R017	FEAT-021-1-004	MOD-021	Audit Trail and Regulatory Reporting for VSAs	The system must maintain a comprehensive and immutable audit trail for all VSA-related activities including contract registration, amendments, slot allocations, booking decisions, settlement calculations, dispute resolutions, and state transitions. Each audit entry must record the action performed, the before and after values of changed fields, the authenticated user, the user's entity affiliation, the timestamp in UTC, and the source system or interface (web UI, API, EDI). The audit trail must be searchable and exportable for regulatory reporting purposes. The system must support generation of regulatory reports in formats required by: Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Transport General Authority, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of ten years and must be stored in a tamper-evident manner using cryptographic hashing to ensure integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R001	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Vessel Sharing Agreement Registration	The system must support registration and lifecycle management of Vessel Sharing Agreements (VSAs) between alliance partners operating on routes connecting Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Each VSA record must capture agreement reference number, effective date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar), participating carriers with SCAC codes, allocated slot counts per vessel class (feeder, Panamax, post-Panamax, ULCV), minimum quantity commitments (MQC), and penalty clauses for underutilization. The system must support multi-currency financial terms in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with exchange rate locking at agreement date. All VSA documents must be storable in both English and Arabic with full RTL support for Arabic contract text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R002	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Slot Allocation & Utilization Tracking	The system must track real-time slot allocation and utilization percentages per voyage leg for each alliance partner. Slot inventory must be maintained at the TEU and FEU level, distinguishing between dry containers, reefer slots (with power plug availability), dangerous goods slots (IMO classes 1-9), and out-of-gauge cargo positions. The system must calculate rolling utilization rates over configurable periods (weekly, monthly, quarterly) and generate automatic alerts when a partner's utilization falls below the agreed MQC threshold by more than 10%. Utilization dashboards must display data in both English and Arabic with RTL-compatible chart rendering, and must aggregate across multiple entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India subsidiaries) with entity-level drill-down capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R003	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Consortium Cost Sharing Calculation	The system must compute cost-sharing allocations among alliance partners based on configurable methodologies including pro-rata by slot count, proportional to TEU-miles, or hybrid formulas defined per agreement. Cost categories must include bunker fuel (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO with sulphur cap compliance), port charges at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, canal transit fees (Suez Canal tolls based on SCNT), terminal handling charges, and vessel operating expenses. Calculations must handle multi-currency settlement with automatic conversion using daily Central Bank rates for QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. The system must generate settlement statements in both English and Arabic, with Hijri date references for KSA-entity settlements, and support offline calculation capability with reconciliation upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R004	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Partner Performance Scorecard	The system must maintain performance scorecards for each alliance partner measuring schedule reliability (percentage of on-time port calls within a 24-hour window), booking confirmation accuracy, documentation timeliness (bill of lading release within 6 hours of vessel departure), cargo damage rates per 1000 TEU, and payment settlement adherence. Scorecards must be configurable per trade lane (e.g., Jebel Ali–JNPT vs. Hamad Port–Khalifa Port feedering) and must support weighting factors for each KPI. Historical trend analysis must cover rolling 12-month periods with Hijri and Gregorian calendar toggles. The Arabic UI must render all scorecard labels, axis titles, and legend entries in RTL layout with proper Arabic numeral formatting where entity locale preferences dictate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R005	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Revenue Pooling & Settlement Engine	The system must support revenue pooling mechanisms where freight revenues collected by individual alliance partners are aggregated and redistributed based on agreed formulas. The engine must handle revenue recognition across multiple entities—Qatar operations billing in QAR, UAE in AED, KSA in SAR, and India in INR—with intercompany elimination entries for consolidated reporting. Settlement cycles must be configurable (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly) with automatic netting of receivables and payables between partners. The system must generate SWIFT-compatible payment instructions and support integration with treasury management systems. Dispute resolution workflows must allow line-item contestation with Arabic and English annotation support and audit trail preservation for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R006	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Joint Schedule Coordination	The system must provide a shared schedule coordination workspace where alliance partners can propose, review, and confirm vessel deployment changes affecting joint services. Schedule modifications such as port rotation changes (adding or omitting Khalifa Port on an existing Jebel Ali–Hamad Port–JNPT string), sailing day adjustments, and vessel substitutions must follow a configurable approval workflow requiring sign-off from all affected partners. The system must detect schedule conflicts including berth window overlaps at shared terminals, tidal restrictions at JNPT, and Hamad Port gate hour constraints during Ramadan periods. Notifications must be delivered in both Arabic and English based on each partner's language preference, with all timestamps shown in the recipient's local timezone (AST, GST, IST).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R007	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Slot Exchange & Swap Management	The system must facilitate slot exchange transactions between alliance partners where one carrier transfers unused slot capacity to another on specific voyage legs. Each swap transaction must record the originating partner, receiving partner, voyage reference, port pair (e.g., AEJEA to INNSA), number of TEU/FEU slots exchanged, container type restrictions (dry, reefer, hazardous), and any financial compensation. The system must enforce business rules preventing slot exchanges that would breach regulatory antitrust provisions or exceed maximum capacity thresholds per carrier on any single voyage. Swap confirmations must be digitally signed and stored with immutable audit trails. Financial compensation for swaps must be calculated in the settlement currency of the receiving partner with automatic FX conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R008	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Alliance Communication Portal	The system must provide a secure communication portal for alliance partner coordination supporting structured message types including operational notices, schedule change requests, dispute notifications, and settlement queries. Messages must support bilingual content in Arabic and English with automatic RTL/LTR layout switching based on the primary language of each message section. File attachments must support common shipping documents (PDF, Excel, EDI messages including BAPLIE, COPARN, IFTMIN). The portal must maintain message threading with full conversation history, read receipts, and escalation workflows. Access control must enforce partner-level visibility ensuring each carrier can only view communications relevant to their alliance agreements. Offline message queuing must be supported with automatic synchronization upon connectivity restoration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R009	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Antitrust Compliance Monitoring	The system must include antitrust compliance monitoring capabilities to ensure alliance activities remain within the boundaries of applicable competition laws in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The module must flag communications or data sharing between partners that could constitute price-fixing, market allocation, or bid-rigging as defined by the Qatar Competition Protection Law, UAE Federal Law No. 4 of 2012, Saudi Competition Law, and India's Competition Act 2002. Automated screening must analyze slot exchange patterns, rate discussions, and capacity coordination for potential compliance violations. The system must generate quarterly compliance reports in both Arabic and English for legal review, with all flagged items requiring mandatory disposition recording before report finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R010	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Capacity Forecasting for Alliance Planning	The system must provide AI-driven capacity forecasting for alliance planning horizons of 4, 8, 12, and 26 weeks. Forecasting models must incorporate historical booking patterns per trade lane, seasonal demand variations (Ramadan slowdown, Diwali peak in India trade, year-end cargo rush), vessel maintenance schedules, and planned blank sailings. Forecasts must be segmented by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF) and cargo category (general, hazardous, overweight, out-of-gauge). The system must present forecasts in a multi-entity view showing demand projections for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India origin/destination cargo separately. All forecast dashboards must support Arabic and English toggling with proper number formatting (Eastern Arabic numerals for Arabic locale in KSA entity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R011	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Blank Sailing Coordination	The system must manage blank sailing decisions within alliance services, tracking the proposal, approval, and execution of voyage cancellations. Each blank sailing record must capture the affected voyage number, service loop identifier, skipped ports (with UN/LOCODE references), impacted booking count and TEU volume, estimated cost savings, and customer impact assessment. The system must enforce minimum notice periods per regulatory jurisdiction—14 days for Qatar Maritime Authority, 14 days for UAE FTA, 21 days for Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), and 14 days for Indian DG Shipping. Automated customer notification workflows must generate alerts in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English) with rebooking options on the next available sailing. Financial impact must be calculated per entity in local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R012	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Multi-Entity Alliance Accounting	The system must maintain separate alliance accounting ledgers for each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while supporting consolidated alliance-level reporting. Each entity's ledger must comply with local accounting standards—IFRS as adopted in Qatar and UAE, SOCPA standards in KSA, and Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS). Intercompany transactions arising from alliance cost sharing and revenue pooling must be automatically identified and tagged for elimination in consolidation. The system must support multi-currency journal entries with translation to each entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using closing rates for balance sheet items and average rates for income statement items. VAT and GST treatment must be applied per jurisdiction with correct tax codes for alliance-related charges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R013	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Alliance KPI Dashboard with AI Insights	The system must provide a real-time alliance KPI dashboard presenting key metrics including aggregate fleet utilization, combined schedule reliability index, average port-to-port transit times across alliance services, bunker cost per TEU-mile, and customer satisfaction scores. The dashboard must support multi-entity filtering allowing managers to view KPIs for the entire alliance, a specific trade lane (e.g., Arabian Gulf–Indian Subcontinent), or a single port pair. AI-powered insights must automatically identify anomalies such as sudden utilization drops on specific legs, recurring delay patterns at particular ports, or cost variances exceeding configurable thresholds. Insights must be generated in both Arabic and English with natural language summaries. Dashboard data must be cacheable for offline viewing with a staleness indicator showing last refresh timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R014	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Alliance Document Repository	The system must provide a centralized document repository for all alliance-related legal agreements, amendments, operational guidelines, and correspondence. Documents must be categorized by type (VSA, slot charter agreement, cost-sharing addendum, operational manual, regulatory filing), by trade lane, and by partner carrier. The repository must support version control with full change history and diff comparison between document versions. Access permissions must be granular—certain documents visible only to specific partner combinations as defined by the agreement scope. Document metadata must be searchable in both Arabic and English, and OCR-processed scans of physical Arabic documents must be indexed for full-text search. The system must enforce document retention policies per jurisdiction (minimum 5 years for Qatar, 7 years for KSA, 8 years for India) with automated archival workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R015	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Emergency Vessel Substitution Protocol	The system must manage emergency vessel substitution workflows when an alliance vessel becomes unavailable due to breakdown, drydocking, detention, or force majeure events. The protocol must automatically identify substitute vessel candidates from the alliance fleet based on matching criteria including vessel size range (within 15% TEU capacity), draft restrictions for destination ports (JNPT max draft 14.5m, Hamad Port 16m), crane compatibility, and reefer plug count requirements. The system must calculate financial implications of substitution including additional bunker costs, speed differential penalties, and terminal productivity impacts. Notifications to all affected parties must be dispatched within 30 minutes of substitution confirmation in both Arabic and English. The substitution record must link to the original VSA terms governing force majeure provisions and associated cost liability allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-001-R016	FEAT-021-2-001	MOD-021	Alliance Benchmarking Against Market	The system must enable benchmarking of alliance performance against broader market indicators including average market utilization on the same trade lanes, competitor transit times published in carrier schedules, industry-average bunker consumption per TEU-mile, and port productivity benchmarks from terminal operators at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Benchmarking data must be sourced from integrated market intelligence feeds and updated at minimum weekly frequency. The system must present comparative visualizations showing alliance performance relative to market averages and top-quartile performers. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual Arabic/English headers and data labels. Multi-currency financial benchmarks must normalize values to USD for comparison while allowing drill-down to local currency amounts per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R001	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Port Call Event Tracking	The system must capture and timestamp all port call milestone events for vessels calling at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), JNPT (INNSA), and all other configured ports. Events must include pilot boarding (PBRD), first line ashore (FLAS), all fast (ALFT), cargo operations commenced (COPC), cargo operations completed (COCD), last line cast off (LLCO), and pilot disembark (PDIS). Timestamps must be recorded in UTC with automatic conversion to local port time (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE/Oman, AST+3 for KSA, IST for India). Each event must be attributable to a source—AIS data, terminal operating system feed, port authority notification, or manual entry—with confidence scoring. Events must synchronize across all entity systems within 5 minutes of capture, with offline buffering when connectivity is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R002	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Berth Productivity Measurement	The system must calculate berth productivity metrics including gross crane moves per hour (GCMPH), net crane moves per hour (NCMPH), ship-to-shore crane utilization percentage, and berth occupancy ratio. GCMPH must be computed as total container moves divided by elapsed time from first lift to last lift. NCMPH must exclude operational delays such as crane breakdowns, hatch cover movements, lashing/unlashing time, and vessel-induced delays (e.g., Bay planning errors, twist-lock jams). The system must categorize delay causes using standardized codes aligned with BIMCO vessel/terminal delay definitions. Productivity must be tracked per crane, per shift, and per vessel call, with comparative visualization across terminals in the network. Arabic UI must render all metric labels and delay category descriptions with proper RTL alignment and Eastern Arabic numerals where configured.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R003	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Turnaround Time Analysis	The system must compute vessel turnaround time (TAT) broken down into component segments: waiting time (anchorage to berth), berthing time (pilot boarding to all fast), cargo working time (first lift to last lift), idle time at berth (non-working periods including prayer times, shift changes, weather stoppages), and unberthing time (cargo complete to departure). Each segment must be measurable individually and as a percentage of total port stay. The system must establish target TAT benchmarks per port and vessel size category—feeder vessels (<3000 TEU) at Hamad Port target 18 hours, Panamax at Jebel Ali target 24 hours, ULCV at JNPT target 36 hours—and flag deviations exceeding 15% of target. TAT analysis must be available per entity, per port, and per trade route, with multi-currency cost impact quantification (demurrage, port dues, bunker at berth) in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R004	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Terminal Performance Benchmarking	The system must enable side-by-side benchmarking of terminal performance metrics across all ports in the network. Benchmarking dimensions must include average GCMPH, vessel TAT by size category, truck turnaround time for container pickup and delivery, reefer monitoring responsiveness, and dangerous goods handling compliance rates. The system must maintain rolling 30-day, 90-day, and 12-month averages for each metric per terminal. Benchmarking dashboards must support filtering by vessel operator, vessel size range, time of day (to identify shift-based performance variations), and cargo mix (dry vs. reefer vs. hazardous). Results must be exportable as PDF reports with bilingual Arabic/English formatting including proper Hijri date rendering for KSA-entity reports. AI-generated recommendations must suggest optimal terminal selection when multiple options exist at a port (e.g., DP World vs. Abu Dhabi Terminals at Khalifa Port).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R012	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Charge Dispute Tracking	The system must allow users to flag a demurrage charge as disputed, capturing the dispute reason, supporting evidence documents, and the disputing party (consignee, shipping line, or terminal operator). Disputed charges must be segregated from confirmed charges in all financial reports and dashboards. The dispute workflow must support status transitions: Open, Under Review, Escalated, Resolved-Accepted, Resolved-Waived, Resolved-Partial. Each status change must be timestamped and attributed to the acting user. The system must calculate the aging of open disputes and generate escalation alerts when disputes remain unresolved beyond configurable SLA thresholds (e.g., 7 days for standard, 3 days for high-value over USD 10,000). Dispute records must be bilingual in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R005	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Weather & Tidal Impact Assessment	The system must integrate weather and tidal data to assess their impact on port stay duration and productivity. For each port call, the system must capture wind speed and direction, visibility, wave height (for anchorage operations), tidal windows (critical for JNPT where draft-restricted vessels require high tide for transit through the Nhava Sheva channel), and temperature extremes affecting reefer container operations. The system must correlate weather events with productivity deviations to distinguish weather-related delays from operational inefficiencies. Fog-related delays at JNPT during December–February, shamal wind stoppages at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port during June–August, and extreme heat protocols (above 50°C outdoor work prohibition in Qatar and UAE) must be automatically classified. Historical weather-productivity correlation models must feed into predictive port stay estimation for future voyage planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R006	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Gang and Resource Productivity Tracking	The system must track labor gang productivity at each terminal including gang size, moves per gang-hour, gang deployment patterns (number of gangs per vessel simultaneously), and gang changeover efficiency during shift transitions. The system must capture terminal resource utilization including quay crane allocation versus availability, yard equipment deployment (RTGs, straddle carriers, reach stackers), and internal transfer vehicle (ITV) cycle times. Resource constraints impacting vessel operations must be flagged—such as when a terminal operates below agreed crane intensity due to equipment breakdown or labor shortages. Data must be collected from terminal operating system integrations where available (Navis N4, TOPS) or via manual entry forms supporting offline data capture with Arabic and English field labels. Productivity data must respect Ramadan working hour adjustments and Friday prayer time schedules applicable at KSA and Qatar ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R007	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Port Cost Analysis per Call	The system must compute total port call costs broken down by category: pilotage, towage, berth hire, channel dues, light dues, port authority charges, mooring/unmooring, terminal handling charges (THC per container by type—20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, hazardous surcharges), wharfage, and anchorage fees. Each cost must be captured in the local port currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) with automatic conversion to USD and the reporting entity's functional currency. The system must compare actual costs against budgeted/estimated costs per voyage plan and highlight variances exceeding 5%. Port tariff tables must be maintainable per port with effective date ranges supporting scheduled tariff increases. Multi-entity allocation must distribute shared port costs (e.g., vessel-level charges) across entities based on cargo volume attributable to each entity's bookings on that voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R008	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Congestion Monitoring & Prediction	The system must monitor port congestion levels in real-time using AIS vessel position data, port authority queue reports, and terminal operating system berth allocation feeds. Congestion metrics must include number of vessels at anchorage awaiting berth, average waiting time over the trailing 7 days, berth occupancy percentage, and estimated time to berth for inbound vessels. The system must provide AI-driven congestion predictions for 24-hour, 48-hour, and 7-day horizons using historical patterns, known vessel arrival schedules, and seasonal factors (e.g., pre-Eid cargo surges at Jebel Ali, monsoon season impacts at JNPT from June through September). Congestion alerts must be distributed to voyage planners and operations teams in Arabic and English based on user preference. Predicted congestion cost impacts must be quantified in the vessel's operating currency including bunker at anchorage, schedule delay penalties, and potential late-arrival surcharges at downstream ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R009	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Berth Window Compliance Tracking	The system must track vessel compliance with allocated berth windows at each port, measuring the difference between the terminal's berthing appointment and the vessel's actual arrival alongside. Compliance must be categorized as early arrival (>2 hours before window), on-time (within +/- 2 hours), late arrival (>2 hours after window), and missed window (>6 hours late or no-show requiring reberthing). The system must track the financial impact of non-compliance including early arrival waiting costs, late arrival penalties imposed by terminals, and reberthing fees. For Jebel Ali's automated appointment system, the integration must submit berth requests via the DP World API and track confirmation status. At JNPT, integration with the Vessel Traffic Management System (VTMS) must capture actual pilot boarding times for compliance calculation. Compliance reports must be generated per trade route, per port, and per vessel with Arabic and English bilingual output.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R010	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Cargo Mix Impact on Productivity	The system must analyze the impact of cargo composition on port call productivity, quantifying how different container type ratios affect crane moves per hour. Analysis must account for reefer container handling overhead (plug-in/plug-out time, pre-trip inspection delays), hazardous cargo segregation requirements (IMO class stowage rules requiring additional bay shifts), over-dimensional cargo requiring special gear (flat racks, open tops with out-of-gauge loads), and transshipment container restow moves versus gateway cargo direct discharge. The system must build port-specific productivity adjustment factors—for example, each 1% increase in reefer mix at Hamad Port may reduce GCMPH by 0.3 moves. These factors must feed into port stay duration estimation models used in voyage planning. Reports must display cargo mix breakdowns with bilingual labels and support filtering by entity to show cargo composition differences between Qatar-origin, UAE-origin, KSA-origin, and India-origin shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R011	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Shift-Level Performance Reporting	The system must generate shift-level performance reports capturing productivity metrics for each working shift at a terminal during a vessel's port stay. Shift definitions must be configurable per port—Hamad Port operates three 8-hour shifts, Jebel Ali uses two 12-hour shifts, and JNPT follows a three-shift pattern with a 30-minute overlap for handover. Each shift report must include moves completed, crane hours worked, crane hours lost (with delay reason codes), gang productivity, and percentage of planned work completed. The system must identify performance patterns across shifts—such as consistently lower productivity during night shifts or during Friday afternoon periods at Gulf ports. Shift reports must display timestamps in local port time with Hijri date alongside Gregorian date for Gulf port reports. Data must be available for offline review by vessel superintendents with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R012	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Demurrage and Detention Risk Assessment	The system must assess demurrage and detention risk arising from port stay overruns by comparing estimated remaining port time against container free-time allowances. For import containers, the system must track the free-time clock starting from vessel discharge completion and alert when containers approach the end of free days (typically 3-5 days at Gulf ports, 3 days at JNPT) factoring in Friday/Saturday non-working days at Qatar and UAE ports, and Friday non-working at KSA ports. Demurrage charges must be calculated using carrier-specific tariff tables in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with tiered rate structures (e.g., days 1-5 at base rate, days 6-10 at 150%, beyond 10 days at 200%). The system must project total demurrage liability per port call and per entity, flagging high-risk situations where port congestion or productivity shortfalls threaten to push containers into penalty periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R013	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Historical Port Performance Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical database of port performance records spanning a minimum of 5 years of data for trend analysis and planning. Records must be indexed by port (UN/LOCODE), terminal, berth number, vessel IMO number, vessel size category, cargo volume (TEU), cargo mix, date and time (both Gregorian and Hijri), and weather conditions. The database must support complex analytical queries such as average TAT at JNPT for vessels over 8000 TEU during monsoon season, or crane productivity trend at Hamad Port since terminal expansion in a given year. Query results must be exportable in CSV, Excel, and PDF formats with bilingual Arabic/English column headers. Data retention must comply with the most stringent applicable requirement across all entities (8 years per Indian regulatory requirements). The system must support data archival to cold storage with on-demand retrieval capability within 24 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R014	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Terminal Service Level Agreement Monitoring	The system must track terminal performance against Service Level Agreements (SLAs) defined in terminal handling agreements between the shipping line entities and terminal operators. SLA parameters must include guaranteed minimum crane intensity (cranes per vessel by size category), maximum vessel waiting time, container dwell time targets, reefer monitoring frequency, truck appointment compliance, and documentation processing times. Each SLA must be recorded with the contracting entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India subsidiary), the terminal operator, effective dates, and penalty/bonus clauses with amounts in the contract currency. The system must compute SLA achievement percentages monthly and generate claim documentation when terminals fail to meet agreed thresholds. Claim letters must be auto-generated in the language of the contract (Arabic or English) with supporting performance data appendices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R015	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Real-Time Port Stay Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing the current status of all vessels in port or at anchorage across the network—Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and any additional configured ports. The dashboard must display each vessel's current port call phase (at anchorage, berthing, cargo operations, idle, unberthing), elapsed time in current phase, percentage of cargo work completed, estimated time of departure (ETD), and variance against the planned schedule. Dashboard data must refresh at minimum 5-minute intervals from terminal operating system feeds and AIS position data. The UI must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with seamless toggling. Vessel icons on the port map must be color-coded by status (green for on-schedule, amber for minor delay, red for significant delay exceeding 4 hours). Offline mode must display the last-known status with a prominent staleness warning banner indicating time since last successful data refresh.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R016	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Port Productivity Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to identify unusual port stay or productivity patterns that deviate significantly from historical norms. Anomalies must include unexpectedly long idle periods at berth, sudden drops in crane productivity mid-operation, abnormal waiting times at anchorage inconsistent with reported congestion levels, and unexplained cost variances. Each detected anomaly must be classified by severity (informational, warning, critical) and assigned a probable cause category using machine learning models trained on historical port call data. The system must generate anomaly alerts to relevant operations personnel in their preferred language (Arabic or English) with recommended investigation actions. Anomaly detection thresholds must be tunable per port and per vessel size category to avoid false positives in ports with inherently variable operations such as JNPT during monsoon season or Gulf ports during Ramadan scheduling adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-002-R017	FEAT-021-2-002	MOD-021	Bunker Consumption at Port Analysis	The system must track and analyze vessel bunker fuel consumption during port stays separately from sea passage consumption. Port consumption must be categorized by operational phase: maneuvering (arrival and departure), cargo operations (powering cranes on geared vessels, maintaining reefer containers), auxiliary systems at berth (hotel load, ballast operations), and idle time consumption. The system must compare actual port bunker consumption against vessel-specific benchmarks and flag anomalies exceeding 10% of expected values. Shore power (cold ironing) utilization at equipped berths must be tracked as an alternative to generator running, with cost comparison between shore power tariffs and bunker fuel costs. All consumption must be recorded in metric tons with cost calculation in the bunkering port's currency (QAR, AED, INR) and converted to USD for fleet-level analysis. Reports must support multi-entity aggregation with Arabic and English formatting options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R001	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Multi-Objective Route Optimization Model	The system must implement a multi-objective optimization model for liner route design that simultaneously optimizes for transit time competitiveness, vessel utilization, bunker fuel cost minimization, and schedule reliability. The model must accept configurable weighting factors for each objective, allowing planners to prioritize cost savings over speed or vice versa depending on trade lane strategy. Constraints must include maximum and minimum port-to-port transit times (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT maximum 5 days for competitive positioning), vessel draft limitations per port (JNPT 14.5m, Hamad Port 16m, Jebel Ali 17m), berth window availability at each port, and emission control requirements. The optimization must consider multiple vessel classes simultaneously and output Pareto-optimal solutions with trade-off visualizations. All optimization parameters and results must be displayable in Arabic and English with proper numerical formatting per entity locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R002	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Dynamic Speed Optimization	The system must provide dynamic speed optimization recommendations for each voyage leg based on current conditions including bunker fuel prices (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO at major bunkering ports—Fujairah, Jebel Ali, Mumbai), weather forecasts along the route, port congestion levels at destination, and schedule buffer requirements. The engine must calculate optimal sailing speeds using vessel-specific fuel consumption curves (speed-consumption tables per vessel IMO number) and recommend speed adjustments that balance fuel savings against schedule commitments. Speed recommendations must respect minimum maneuvering speed in traffic separation schemes (Strait of Hormuz, approach channels), and maximum speed limits in emission control areas. The system must present fuel cost savings in the operating entity's currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with CO2 emission reduction estimates in metric tons for ESG reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R003	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Port Rotation Optimization	The system must evaluate and recommend optimal port rotation sequences for liner services calling at ports in the Arabian Gulf, Indian subcontinent, and connecting regions. The optimization must consider cargo demand volumes between each origin-destination port pair, transshipment connectivity requirements at hub ports (Jebel Ali as regional hub, JNPT as Indian gateway), port operational hours and restrictions (Hamad Port Ramadan schedule, JNPT tidal windows), and competitive positioning against rival carrier services on the same trade lane. The engine must simulate multiple rotation scenarios—such as comparing a direct Hamad Port–JNPT service versus a multi-stop Hamad Port–Khalifa Port–Jebel Ali–JNPT rotation—and present revenue, cost, and transit time comparisons. Simulation results must handle multi-entity revenue attribution, allocating projected freight income to the originating entity in its local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R004	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Vessel Deployment Recommendation	The system must recommend optimal vessel deployment to trade routes based on fleet composition, demand forecasting, and operational constraints. The AI engine must match vessel characteristics (TEU capacity, reefer plug count, fuel type—conventional, dual-fuel LNG, methanol-ready, crane configuration for geared vessels) against route requirements including port size restrictions, cargo mix demands, and service frequency targets. The system must evaluate fleet redeployment scenarios when demand patterns shift—for example, recommending upsizing from 4500 TEU to 6500 TEU vessels on the Gulf–India route during peak season while downsizing during Ramadan slowdown. Deployment recommendations must consider charter market rates for supplementary tonnage in USD/day and compare against owned fleet operating costs. Multi-entity cost allocation must distribute vessel costs based on revenue-weighted cargo attribution across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R005	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Schedule Reliability Prediction	The system must predict schedule reliability outcomes for proposed route configurations using machine learning models trained on historical operational data. Prediction factors must include seasonal weather patterns (Arabian Gulf shamal winds, Indian Ocean monsoon, fog at JNPT), port congestion probability distributions per port and time of year, vessel mechanical reliability records by age and class, and buffer time adequacy between consecutive port calls. The model must output a reliability score (percentage of port calls expected to be on-time within a 24-hour window) for each proposed schedule variant. The system must identify high-risk legs where schedule disruption probability exceeds configurable thresholds (default 20%) and recommend mitigation strategies such as additional buffer time, alternative ports of refuge, or speed reserve allocation. Reliability predictions must be recalculated weekly with updated input data and presented in bilingual dashboards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R006	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Demand-Driven Capacity Adjustment	The system must analyze booking demand patterns and recommend capacity adjustments to route configurations including extra loader deployment, sweeper vessel scheduling, and service frequency changes. Demand analysis must segment by trade lane, cargo type (dry, reefer, hazardous, special equipment—flat racks, open tops, tank containers), and customer tier (contract vs. spot). The AI engine must detect demand surges—such as pre-Diwali export rushes from JNPT, pre-Ramadan consumer goods imports to Gulf ports, or petrochemical cargo spikes from Jubail—and recommend proactive capacity additions at least 4 weeks in advance. Recommendations must include financial projections showing incremental revenue versus additional vessel deployment costs, calculated per entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with consolidated USD P&L impact. Demand data must be visualizable in Arabic and English dashboards with Hijri calendar overlays for Gulf market seasonality analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R007	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Emission-Optimized Routing	The system must incorporate emission reduction objectives into route optimization, calculating CO2, SOx, NOx, and particulate matter emissions for each route variant based on vessel engine specifications, fuel type, sailing speed, and distance. The engine must evaluate routing alternatives that reduce emissions—such as weather routing to avoid adverse currents, slow steaming strategies, and optimal port rotation to minimize total voyage distance. The system must assess compliance with IMO 2023 CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) rating requirements, predicting each vessel's annual CII rating based on proposed deployment patterns and flagging routes that would push vessels from rating C to D or E, triggering mandatory corrective action plans. Emission calculations must align with IMO DCS (Data Collection System) reporting methodology and EU MRV where applicable for vessels subsequently trading in European waters. Reports must support Arabic and English output with unit conversions between metric tons CO2 and grams CO2 per TEU-mile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R008	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Canal Transit Optimization	The system must optimize Suez Canal transit planning for vessels on routes connecting the Arabian Gulf and Indian subcontinent with Mediterranean and European destinations that form extensions of the core Gulf–India network. Optimization must consider Suez Canal transit booking slots, convoy schedules (northbound and southbound), SCNT (Suez Canal Net Tonnage) based toll calculations, and waiting time minimization at Port Said or Suez anchorage. The engine must evaluate whether to route vessels via the new Suez Canal channel (reduced transit time) versus the original channel based on current traffic conditions. For purely Gulf–India route segments not transiting Suez, the system must still track Suez-related costs for end-to-end voyage economics on services extending beyond the regional network. Canal transit costs must be allocated to entities based on the proportion of cargo destined beyond the canal transit point, with amounts in USD and converted to entity functional currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R009	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	What-If Scenario Simulation Engine	The system must provide a what-if simulation engine allowing planners to model hypothetical scenarios and assess their impact on route performance. Scenarios must include adding or removing a port call from the rotation, changing vessel size or type, modifying service frequency (weekly to bi-weekly or vice versa), adjusting sailing speeds across all legs, introducing a new competing service on the trade lane, and varying bunker fuel prices by +/- 10%, 25%, and 50%. Each simulation must produce a comprehensive output including estimated voyage revenue, total voyage cost, vessel utilization projection, schedule reliability prediction, emission forecast, and competitive transit time positioning. The engine must support side-by-side comparison of up to 5 scenarios simultaneously. Simulation parameters and results must be fully operable in both Arabic and English interfaces, with financial outputs in multiple currencies selectable by the user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R010	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Machine Learning Model Training Pipeline	The system must implement an automated machine learning model training pipeline that continuously improves route optimization accuracy based on operational outcomes. Training data must include historical voyage results (actual vs. planned transit times, fuel consumption, port productivity, revenue, and costs) spanning at minimum 24 months of operations across all entities. The pipeline must support model versioning, A/B testing of model variants, and automated rollback when a new model version underperforms the incumbent by more than a configurable threshold. Feature engineering must incorporate shipping-specific variables including seasonal cargo patterns per trade lane, port infrastructure changes (new terminal openings, crane additions), regulatory changes (emission regulations, port tariff revisions), and macroeconomic indicators affecting trade volumes (GDP growth rates for Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Model performance dashboards must display accuracy metrics in bilingual format with entity-level breakdowns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R011	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Feeder Network Integration Optimization	The system must optimize feeder network design connecting smaller Gulf ports with mainline hub ports. The optimization must determine optimal feeder vessel deployment to connect Hamad Port (Doha), Khalifa Port (Abu Dhabi), Dammam (SADMM), Sohar (OMSOH), and other regional ports with the mainline hub at Jebel Ali. Feeder schedules must be synchronized with mainline vessel arrivals and departures to minimize transshipment dwell time at the hub—target connection time of 24-48 hours for import relay and 12-36 hours for export relay. The system must evaluate dedicated feeder loops versus multi-port milk-run configurations, considering cargo volumes, vessel costs, and customer transit time expectations. Cost analysis must separate feeder economics per entity, with Qatar feeder costs in QAR, UAE feeder costs in AED, and KSA feeder costs in SAR, while maintaining consolidated USD reporting for network-level optimization decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R012	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Real-Time Route Deviation Advisory	The system must provide real-time advisory recommendations when en-route deviations from the planned voyage become necessary due to weather events, port closures, security incidents, or mechanical issues. The advisory engine must evaluate alternative routing options—such as omitting a scheduled port call, substituting an alternative port (e.g., diverting from congested JNPT to Mundra INMUN), adjusting speed to make up lost time, or proceeding to a port of refuge. Each deviation option must be presented with estimated time impact, fuel cost implication, cargo rerouting requirements (number of containers affected, alternative delivery arrangements), and customer notification actions required. Advisories must be generated within 15 minutes of triggering event detection and delivered to operations teams in Arabic and English based on user preference. All deviation decisions must be logged with full audit trail including decision maker, timestamp, rationale, and outcome tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R013	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Bunker Planning Integration	The system must integrate route optimization with bunker procurement planning, recommending optimal bunkering ports and quantities for each voyage based on current fuel prices at available bunkering locations (Fujairah, Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, Mumbai/JNPT, Khor Fakkan), vessel tank capacities, planned consumption per leg at recommended speeds, and price forecasting models. The optimizer must consider fuel quality requirements including sulphur content limits (0.50% global cap, 0.10% in ECAs), compatibility between fuel batches from different suppliers, and vessel engine specifications for fuel type (VLSFO, HSFO with scrubber, LNG for dual-fuel vessels). Bunkering recommendations must minimize total fuel cost across the full voyage cycle while maintaining minimum safety reserves per class society requirements. Cost outputs must be presented in USD (standard bunker trading currency) with conversion to entity functional currencies for cost allocation across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R014	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Competitive Route Benchmarking	The system must benchmark the organization's route offerings against competitor services on the same trade lanes. Benchmarking parameters must include port-to-port transit times, frequency of service (weekly, bi-weekly), vessel sizes deployed, port coverage (direct calls vs. transshipment), published schedule reliability (from sources such as Sea-Intelligence Global Liner Performance), and estimated market rates. Competitor data must be sourced from publicly available sailing schedules, AIS vessel tracking, and integrated market intelligence feeds. The system must generate competitive positioning maps showing the organization's services relative to top 5 competitors on each trade lane (e.g., Gulf–India westbound). The AI engine must identify competitive gaps—such as a rival offering 2-day faster transit on Jebel Ali–JNPT—and recommend route adjustments to improve positioning. Benchmarking reports must be available in Arabic and English with competitor carrier names, service codes, and transit time comparisons.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R015	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Intermodal Connectivity Optimization	The system must optimize route design by considering intermodal connectivity at each port, including rail connections, truck transport networks, and inland container depot (ICD) linkages. For JNPT, the system must factor in rail connectivity to major ICDs (Tughlakabad Delhi, Dadri, Nagpur, Ahmedabad) and the dedicated freight corridor capacity. For Gulf ports, the system must consider truck transport networks—Jebel Ali to inland destinations across UAE and Oman, Hamad Port to Qatar industrial zones, Dammam to Riyadh ICD via rail. The optimization must evaluate whether shifting cargo from a direct port call to a transshipment plus inland transport combination offers superior total transit time and cost. Intermodal cost calculations must include terminal handling, transport, customs clearance, and last-mile delivery in the destination country's currency. The system must factor in INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, DDP, DAP) to determine which transport segments fall under the carrier's responsibility versus the shipper or consignee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R016	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Optimization Audit Trail & Explainability	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all AI-generated route optimization recommendations including the input parameters, model version used, optimization objectives and weights, constraints applied, alternative solutions considered, and the final recommendation with supporting rationale. The explainability module must provide human-readable justifications for each recommendation—for example, explaining why the AI suggests adding Khalifa Port to a rotation by citing cargo demand projections, competitive transit time improvements, and incremental cost analysis. Audit records must be immutable and timestamped, stored for a minimum of 7 years per the most stringent entity requirement. The system must support regulatory inquiry responses where authorities (Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE FTA, Indian DG Shipping) request justification for service changes. Explanations must be available in both Arabic and English with proper technical terminology translation for shipping-specific terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-003-R017	FEAT-021-2-003	MOD-021	Seasonal Pattern Recognition Engine	The system must employ machine learning to identify and predict seasonal demand patterns affecting route optimization across the Gulf–India trade network. Recognized patterns must include Ramadan and Eid al-Fitr demand cycles (typically a pre-Ramadan import surge followed by reduced activity during Ramadan at all Gulf ports), Indian festival seasons (Diwali, Navratri driving export peaks from JNPT), annual petrochemical maintenance shutdowns at Jubail and Yanbu affecting Saudi exports, summer produce export peaks from India, and year-end inventory build-up across all markets. The pattern recognition engine must quantify demand impact as percentage deviation from baseline volumes, segmented by cargo type and trade lane direction. Predictions must be available at minimum 8 weeks ahead to enable proactive route and capacity adjustments. Seasonal calendars must display both Gregorian and Hijri dates with automatic year-to-year Hijri date shifting for Islamic holiday-driven patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R001	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Freight Rate Index Integration	The system must integrate with major container freight rate indices including the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Drewry World Container Index (WCI), Freightos Baltic Index (FBX), and Platts Container Rate benchmarks. Rate index data must be ingested at the published frequency—typically weekly for SCFI and WCI, daily for FBX—and stored historically for trend analysis spanning at minimum 5 years. The system must map index routes to the organization's trade lanes (e.g., SCFI Middle East/India subcontinent routes mapped to Gulf–India services) and display rate trends with interactive charting. Rate indices must be presented in their native currency (typically USD per TEU or FEU) with conversion capability to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR for entity-level commercial analysis. The Arabic UI must render index names, chart axes, and trend descriptions in proper RTL format with Eastern Arabic numerals where configured for KSA entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R002	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Trade Volume Statistics Integration	The system must ingest and display trade volume statistics from authoritative sources including port authority published throughput data (Hamad Port QTerminals reports, DP World Jebel Ali statistics, JNPT traffic figures), national customs statistics (Qatar Planning and Statistics Authority, UAE Federal Competitiveness and Statistics Centre, India DGCIS), and international bodies (UNCTAD, World Bank trade data). Volume data must be normalized to TEU equivalents and categorized by trade direction (import, export, transshipment), cargo type (containerized, break-bulk converted to TEU equivalent), and commodity group. The system must compute market share estimates by comparing the organization's volumes against total port throughput. Historical trend analysis must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period comparisons. Data freshness indicators must show the publication date and source for each data series, alerting users when data is more than 30 days old.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R003	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Competitor Service Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor competitor liner service offerings on trade lanes relevant to the Qatar/UAE/KSA/India corridor. Monitored attributes must include service name and loop identifier, port rotation sequence, published transit times between key port pairs, vessel names and sizes deployed, sailing frequency, and published schedule reliability. Data sources must include carrier website schedule APIs, industry databases (such as Alphaliner, MDS Transmodal), and AIS-derived vessel movement analysis. The system must detect and alert on competitive changes including new service launches, port rotation modifications, vessel upgrades or downgrades, frequency changes, and service suspensions. Alerts must be delivered in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) with impact assessment indicating which of the organization's services face increased competition. Competitive intelligence must be restricted to commercially authorized users with role-based access controls per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R004	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Bunker Price Feed Integration	The system must integrate real-time bunker fuel price feeds from major pricing agencies including Platts (S&P Global Commodity Insights), Argus Media, and Ship & Bunker. Price data must cover VLSFO (0.50% sulphur), HSFO (3.50% sulphur), MGO (0.10% sulphur), and LNG bunker prices at key bunkering ports: Fujairah (AEFUJ), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Hamad Port (QAHMD), Mumbai (INBOM), and Singapore (SGSIN) as a benchmark reference. Prices must be stored historically with daily granularity for trend analysis and forecasting. The system must calculate bunker cost impact on route economics by combining price data with vessel-specific consumption curves and planned voyage distances. Price alerts must be configurable—triggering when prices at a specific port cross user-defined thresholds or when price differentials between ports exceed thresholds that make bunkering port selection decisions commercially significant. All prices must be displayed in USD per metric ton (industry standard) with optional conversion to entity currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R005	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Macroeconomic Indicator Dashboard	The system must present macroeconomic indicators relevant to container shipping demand in the Gulf–India trade corridor. Indicators must include GDP growth rates (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), manufacturing PMI indices, consumer confidence indices, import/export trade balance figures, currency exchange rate trends (USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR), and commodity price indices for key traded goods (petrochemicals, construction materials, consumer electronics, textiles). Data must be sourced from central bank publications (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI), international organizations (IMF, World Bank), and financial data providers. The dashboard must enable correlation analysis between macroeconomic indicators and the organization's historical cargo volumes to identify leading indicators of demand changes. All indicator names, descriptions, and data labels must be available in both Arabic and English with proper RTL layout for Arabic users. Hijri calendar quarterly reporting periods must be supported alongside Gregorian for KSA fiscal year alignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R006	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Rate Forecasting Model	The system must provide AI-driven freight rate forecasting for key trade lanes in the Gulf–India network with prediction horizons of 2 weeks, 4 weeks, 8 weeks, and 12 weeks. Forecasting models must incorporate historical rate index data, supply-side factors (fleet capacity on the trade lane, blank sailing announcements, newbuilding deliveries, scrapping rates), demand-side factors (trade volume trends, seasonal patterns, macroeconomic indicators), and disruption events (port congestion, geopolitical risks, canal closures). The model must output point forecasts with confidence intervals (80% and 95% bands) and provide direction indicators (rising, stable, falling) for rapid interpretation. Forecast accuracy must be continuously tracked against actual realized rates, with accuracy metrics published monthly showing MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) per trade lane. Forecasts must be presented in USD per TEU and per FEU with conversion to entity currencies, and must be displayable in Arabic and English with proper number and chart formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R007	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Supply-Demand Balance Analysis	The system must analyze the supply-demand balance on each trade lane by comparing deployed fleet capacity (total TEU slots offered per week or month) against estimated cargo demand. Supply data must be derived from published carrier schedules and vessel deployment records, aggregated across all carriers serving each trade lane. Demand data must combine the organization's own booking volumes with market volume estimates from trade statistics and port throughput data. The system must compute capacity utilization rates and identify lanes where overcapacity (utilization below 75%) or undercapacity (utilization above 95%) conditions exist. Trend analysis must show how the supply-demand balance has evolved over the past 24 months and project forward 12 months based on known fleet order book and demand forecasts. Analysis must be segmented by container type (dry, reefer) as reefer capacity constraints differ from overall TEU capacity. Reports must support multi-entity views with entity-specific demand attribution in local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R008	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Port Tariff Intelligence Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of port tariffs, terminal handling charges, and ancillary port fees for all ports in the network and competing alternative ports. Tariff data must be structured by charge type (pilotage, towage, berth hire, port dues, THC, wharfage, anchorage, reefer monitoring, hazardous cargo surcharges) with rates in local currency and USD equivalent. The database must track tariff effective dates, revision history, and upcoming published changes. For each port, tariffs must distinguish between vessel-related charges (based on GRT/NRT/LOA) and cargo-related charges (based on TEU/weight/commodity). The system must enable cost comparison across alternative ports—for example, comparing total port call costs at Khalifa Port versus Jebel Ali for a 6500 TEU vessel with 2000 moves. Tariff data must be maintained by authorized users with approval workflow for updates, and must be viewable in both Arabic and English with proper currency formatting per locale. Offline access must be supported with last-synced tariff data available when connectivity is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R009	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Geopolitical Risk Assessment Feed	The system must integrate geopolitical risk assessment data relevant to shipping routes in the Gulf–India corridor. Risk categories must include maritime security threats (piracy risk zones near Gulf of Aden and Somali coast, drone and missile threats in the Red Sea and Strait of Hormuz), trade sanctions and embargo restrictions (OFAC, UN Security Council, EU sanctions lists affecting cargo or port calls), political instability indicators for countries along the route, and diplomatic events affecting bilateral trade (e.g., trade agreements, tariff impositions, diplomatic disputes). Risk data must be sourced from maritime security providers (UKMTO, MSCHOA), government advisories, and specialized risk intelligence services. The system must overlay geopolitical risk layers on route maps and generate risk-adjusted voyage cost estimates accounting for insurance premium variations (war risk, K&R), routing diversions, and convoy requirements. Alerts for significant risk changes must be bilingual (Arabic and English) and distributed to operations and commercial teams across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R010	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Customer Demand Intelligence	The system must aggregate and analyze customer booking behavior to generate demand intelligence insights for route planning. Analysis dimensions must include booking-to-shipment conversion rates by customer and trade lane, advance booking lead times (days between booking creation and vessel departure), cancellation and no-show rates, cargo type preferences by customer segment, and price sensitivity indicators. The system must identify customers at risk of churning to competitor services based on declining booking volumes or increasing rate negotiation aggressiveness. AI models must predict customer-level demand for 4-week and 8-week horizons using historical booking patterns and CRM data. Customer intelligence must be attributed to the servicing entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and presented in the entity's functional currency for revenue impact assessment. All customer data handling must comply with PDPL (Qatar Personal Data Protection Law), UAE PDPL, Saudi PDPL, and India's DPDP Act, with appropriate consent management and data minimization controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R011	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Commodity Flow Analysis	The system must analyze commodity flow patterns between origin and destination regions served by the Gulf–India route network. Key commodity categories must include petrochemical products (polyethylene, polypropylene from Gulf to India), construction materials (steel, cement, aggregates), consumer goods (electronics, textiles, FMCG), food products (rice, spices, frozen meat—requiring reefer containers), automobiles and automotive parts, and project cargo for infrastructure developments. For each commodity, the system must track volume trends (TEU per month), seasonal patterns, key shipper concentrations, and price sensitivity to freight rates. The system must correlate commodity market prices (sourced from commodity exchanges and price reporting agencies) with containerized shipping demand to identify leading indicators. Commodity flow maps must be interactive, displayable in Arabic and English, with trade direction arrows showing volume magnitudes. Multi-currency value analysis must present commodity trade values in origin and destination currencies alongside USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R013	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Port Authority Tariff Import and Validation	The system must support importing port authority tariff schedules from structured files (CSV, Excel, JSON) and validating them against a predefined schema that enforces required fields: port code, container type, tier ranges, daily rate, currency, effective date, and expiry date. The import process must detect overlapping effective date ranges for the same port and container type combination and must reject the import with a detailed error report if conflicts are found. Successfully imported tariffs must be placed in a pending-approval state and require authorization from a tariff administrator before becoming active. The system must maintain a complete version history of all tariff imports with the ability to compare two versions side-by-side.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R012	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Market Share Tracking & Reporting	The system must track the organization's market share on each trade lane by comparing own-carrier volumes against total market volumes. Market share must be computed at multiple granularity levels: overall trade lane, port pair, cargo type (dry, reefer, special equipment), and customer segment (BCO direct, NVOCC, freight forwarder). Data sources for market volume estimation must include port throughput statistics from port authorities (QTerminals, DP World, APM Terminals JNPT), customs declaration data where accessible, and industry analyst estimates. Market share trends must be tracked monthly with rolling 12-month averages to smooth seasonal fluctuations. The system must set target market share objectives per trade lane and entity, generating alerts when actual share deviates from targets by more than 2 percentage points. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual Arabic/English headers, supporting Hijri date formatting for KSA entity reports and Gregorian for others.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R013	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Regulatory Change Monitoring	The system must monitor and alert on regulatory changes affecting liner shipping operations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, and international waters. Monitored regulations must include port state control inspection regimes (Paris MOU, Indian Ocean MOU, Riyadh MOU), emission regulations (IMO MEPC amendments, national emission standards), customs and trade facilitation changes (Qatar Single Window updates, UAE Customs modernization, Indian ICEGATE changes, Saudi FASAH platform), cabotage restrictions, and manning regulations. The system must capture regulation effective dates, compliance deadlines, and impact assessment summaries. For each regulatory change, the system must evaluate operational impact on existing route configurations and flag services requiring schedule, vessel, or documentation adjustments. Regulatory alerts must be bilingual (Arabic for Gulf/KSA regulations, English for Indian and international regulations, bilingual for cross-jurisdictional impacts) with links to official gazette publications or authority notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R014	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Exchange Rate Impact Analysis	The system must analyze the impact of currency exchange rate movements on route profitability and commercial competitiveness. Analysis must cover the primary currency pairs relevant to operations: USD/QAR (fixed peg at 3.64), USD/AED (fixed peg at 3.6725), USD/SAR (fixed peg at 3.75), and USD/INR (floating rate with significant volatility). While QAR, AED, and SAR pegs are historically stable, the system must still monitor for peg adjustment announcements and model hypothetical depegging scenarios. For INR, the system must track daily exchange rate movements, calculate realized vs. budgeted FX gains/losses on India-origin and India-destination freight charges, and provide hedging recommendation alerts when INR volatility (30-day rolling standard deviation) exceeds configurable thresholds. FX impact must be computed per entity and per trade lane direction, with consolidated group-level exposure summaries. All analysis dashboards must support Arabic and English rendering with currency symbol and decimal formatting appropriate to each locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R015	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Market Intelligence Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive market intelligence reports combining rate indices, trade volumes, competitor analysis, macroeconomic indicators, and route performance metrics into executive-ready documents. Report templates must be configurable per audience—executive summary for C-suite (1-2 pages), detailed commercial review for trade lane managers (5-10 pages), and operational briefing for vessel planners (3-5 pages). Reports must be auto-generated on configurable schedules (weekly, monthly, quarterly) with ad-hoc generation capability. Each report must include AI-generated narrative summaries interpreting data trends and recommending commercial or operational actions. Report generation must support full bilingual output—complete Arabic version with RTL layout, proper Hijri date references, and culturally appropriate chart formatting alongside a parallel English version. Multi-entity reporting must allow entity-specific sections (e.g., KSA market conditions chapter, India trade analysis chapter) with a consolidated group overview. Reports must be distributable via email, in-app notification, and downloadable as PDF with digital signatures for authenticity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R016	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Real-Time Market Alerts Engine	The system must provide a configurable real-time alert engine that monitors market intelligence data streams and triggers notifications when predefined conditions are met. Alert conditions must include rate index movements exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., SCFI Gulf route drops more than 5% week-over-week), competitor service changes (new service launch, port addition/removal, frequency change), significant port congestion events (average waiting time exceeding 48 hours at any network port), bunker price spikes or crashes (more than 10% change within 7 days), and regulatory deadline reminders (30-day, 14-day, 7-day warnings before compliance deadlines). Users must be able to create custom alert rules combining multiple conditions with AND/OR logic. Alert delivery channels must include in-app notifications, email, SMS (to Gulf and India mobile numbers), and integration with enterprise messaging platforms. Alert messages must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) with relevant data summaries and recommended actions. Offline alert queuing must ensure no alerts are lost during connectivity outages, with batch delivery upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R017	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Data Quality & Source Management	The system must implement data quality controls for all market intelligence data sources ensuring accuracy, timeliness, and consistency. Each data source must be registered with metadata including provider name, update frequency, data format (API, file feed, manual entry), authentication credentials (stored securely), SLA for data delivery, and designated data steward per entity. The system must monitor data feed health—detecting missed deliveries, format changes, value anomalies (e.g., rate index values outside historical range by more than 3 standard deviations), and source contradictions (conflicting data from multiple providers). Data quality scores must be computed for each source using dimensions of completeness, timeliness, accuracy (validated against cross-references), and consistency. When data quality falls below configurable thresholds, the system must flag affected reports and dashboards with data quality warnings in both Arabic and English. Fallback data strategies must be defined per source—such as using the previous period's data with a staleness indicator or switching to an alternative provider—to ensure business continuity when primary sources are unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-021-2-004-R018	FEAT-021-2-004	MOD-021	Market Intelligence API for Internal Systems	The system must expose a secure RESTful API allowing other internal ERP modules to consume market intelligence data programmatically. API endpoints must include current and historical rate indices by trade lane, bunker prices by port and fuel type, competitor service schedules, port congestion metrics, macroeconomic indicators, and AI-generated forecasts. The API must support JSON and XML response formats with pagination for large datasets. Authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with entity-level API keys ensuring each subsidiary (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) can only access data authorized for their entity plus shared global data. Rate limiting must be enforced to prevent system overload—minimum 1000 requests per minute per entity. API responses must include data freshness timestamps and quality scores. The API must return localized content based on an Accept-Language header supporting 'ar' for Arabic and 'en' for English, with currency values formatted according to the requesting entity's locale. API documentation must be maintained in OpenAPI 3.0 specification format with bilingual descriptions and example payloads.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R001	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Tiered Demurrage Rate Schedule Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure tiered demurrage rate schedules per port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA) with escalating daily rates across configurable time bands (e.g., days 1-5 at base rate, days 6-10 at 1.5x, days 11+ at 2x). Each tier must support per-container-type rates for 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, and 45HC. Rate schedules must be versioned with effective date ranges using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and the system must retain historical rate versions for audit and recalculation purposes. Currency for each rate must be configurable in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R002	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Real-Time Demurrage Accrual Computation	The system must compute demurrage charges in real time as containers dwell at the terminal, updating accrued amounts at least every 15 minutes using the vessel discharge timestamp or gate-in timestamp as the starting reference. The calculation must factor in the applicable free time period, any granted grace extensions, weekends, public holidays per jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), and Hijri-based holidays such as Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. The engine must handle partial-day calculations where a port tariff specifies that any part of a calendar day counts as a full day, and must support both calendar-day and working-day counting modes as configured per port authority tariff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R003	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Multi-Currency Demurrage Calculation with FX Conversion	The system must calculate demurrage in the port's local currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) and simultaneously convert the amount into the billing entity's base currency using the daily exchange rate from the configured FX rate provider. The conversion must use the rate effective on the date of charge accrual, not the invoice date. Rounding rules must be configurable per currency (e.g., INR rounded to nearest paisa, QAR to two decimal places). The system must store both the original port-currency amount and the converted amount, along with the FX rate applied, for full traceability and reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R004	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	AI-Powered Demurrage Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously monitor demurrage accruals and flag anomalies where charges deviate more than a configurable threshold (default 20%) from the historical average for the same port, container type, and trade lane combination. Anomaly alerts must classify the root cause probability (e.g., customs hold, documentation delay, port congestion, shipper negligence) using historical pattern analysis. Each alert must include a confidence score, suggested corrective action, and estimated financial exposure if unresolved. Alerts must be dispatched to the assigned operations controller via in-app notification and optionally via email or SMS. The system must support both English and Arabic alert content with proper RTL rendering for Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R005	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Container Dwell Time Tracking Integration	The demurrage engine must integrate with the terminal operating system (TOS) and port community system (PCS) at each configured port to receive automated container milestone events including vessel discharge, gate-out, customs release, and examination hold timestamps. For Hamad Port, integration must use the Mwani Qatar PCS API; for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, the system must interface with Dubai Trade and Abu Dhabi Ports PCS respectively; for JNPT, integration with the Indian PCS (PCS1x) is required. Where API connectivity is unavailable, the system must support manual milestone entry with maker-checker approval and must queue events for synchronization when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R006	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Holiday and Non-Working Day Calendar Management	The system must maintain separate holiday calendars for each jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and each port terminal, supporting both Gregorian fixed holidays (e.g., India Republic Day on 26 January) and Hijri floating holidays (e.g., Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Mawlid al-Nabi). The demurrage engine must reference these calendars to determine whether non-working days are excluded from chargeable days, as per the applicable port tariff rule. Administrators must be able to add ad-hoc non-working days (e.g., cyclone closure at JNPT, sandstorm closure at Jebel Ali). Calendar entries must display correctly in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats, and the Arabic UI must render Hijri dates using the Umm al-Qura calendar variant.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R007	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Calculation for Reefer Containers	The system must apply separate demurrage rate schedules for refrigerated (reefer) containers (20RF, 40RF) that include an additional power supply surcharge component for each day the reefer remains plugged in at the terminal. The power surcharge rate must be configurable per port and must be calculated independently of the base demurrage rate, then aggregated into the total demurrage charge. The engine must track whether the reefer was plugged in or unplugged on each chargeable day based on TOS event data. If the reefer was disconnected due to a terminal-side fault, those days must be excluded from the power surcharge but still count for base demurrage unless a waiver is applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R008	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Waiver and Discount Application	The system must support applying demurrage waivers (full or partial) and discounts at the individual container level, the BL level, or the shipment level. Each waiver must require an approval workflow with configurable authority thresholds (e.g., operations manager up to USD 5,000, branch director up to USD 25,000, regional director above USD 25,000). The waiver record must capture the reason code (e.g., port congestion, force majeure, carrier fault, customer goodwill), the approving user, approval timestamp, and supporting document references. AI must suggest optimal waiver amounts based on historical approval patterns, customer tier, and the probability of successful recovery from the shipping line or terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R014	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Dashboard and KPI Visualization	The system must provide a real-time demurrage dashboard showing total accrued demurrage by port, by entity, by container type, and by customer. The dashboard must include KPIs: average dwell time, total demurrage exposure (current month, quarter, year), top 10 customers by demurrage charges, and demurrage-to-revenue ratio. Charts must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), port, entity, and currency. The dashboard must be fully responsive and render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts. Data must refresh at a maximum interval of 5 minutes, and the dashboard must support export to PDF and Excel with proper Arabic text rendering including right-to-left column ordering in Excel exports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R015	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Offline Demurrage Calculation Capability	The system must support offline demurrage calculation at locations with intermittent internet connectivity, particularly relevant for field operations at smaller Indian ports or remote customs facilities. The offline module must cache the current tariff schedules, holiday calendars, container dwell data, and free time allocations locally on the user's device. Calculations performed offline must be timestamped and queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored. Upon reconnection, the system must reconcile offline-calculated charges with any server-side updates, flagging conflicts where the same container's demurrage was modified both offline and online. Conflict resolution must present both values and require manual selection by an authorized user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R016	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Automated Demurrage Alert Notifications	The system must send automated notifications when demurrage charges reach configurable threshold amounts or when containers approach the end of their free time period. Notification triggers must include: 24 hours before free time expiry, free time expired, demurrage exceeds USD 1,000 (or equivalent in local currency), and daily accrual summary at end of business day. Notifications must be sent via in-app alerts, email, and SMS, with the channel selection configurable per user and per notification type. All notification content must be available in English and Arabic based on the recipient's language preference. The system must track notification delivery status and retry failed deliveries up to three times over a 30-minute period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-001-R017	FEAT-022-1-001	MOD-022	Demurrage Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every demurrage calculation, recalculation, waiver, dispute, and adjustment event. Each audit entry must record the action type, the before and after values, the user who performed the action, the timestamp in UTC and local port time, the IP address, and the session identifier. Audit records must be immutable and stored for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Financial Centre regulatory requirements, UAE Federal Tax Authority VAT record-keeping rules, KSA ZATCA retention mandates, and India GST record retention requirements. The audit log must be searchable by container number, BL number, customer, date range, and action type, and must support export in CSV format for external auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R001	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Standard Free Time Configuration per Port and Container Type	The system must allow configuration of standard free time days for each port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA) broken down by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 45HC) and by cargo direction (import vs export). Standard free time must be expressed in calendar days or working days as per the port authority's tariff rules. For import containers, free time must start from the vessel discharge completion date; for export containers, from the gate-in date. The configuration must support different free time values for the same port depending on whether the cargo is transshipment, local delivery, or bonded warehouse movement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R002	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Customer-Specific Free Time Agreements	The system must support creating customer-specific free time agreements that override the standard port tariff free time for designated customers. Each agreement must specify the customer account, the applicable ports, container types, direction (import/export), the extended free time in days, the agreement effective date range (both Gregorian and Hijri), and the approving authority. Agreements must be linked to the customer master record and automatically applied when demurrage is calculated for containers consigned to that customer. The system must enforce a maximum free time extension limit configurable per entity to prevent unauthorized generous terms. Expired agreements must cease to apply automatically but remain visible in the historical agreement register.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R003	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Shipping Line Free Time Allocation Rules	The system must manage free time allocations granted by shipping lines to consignees, which operate independently of port terminal free time. Shipping line free time must be configurable per carrier (by SCAC code), per trade lane, per container type, and per service contract reference. The system must support scenarios where shipping line free time differs from port free time and must calculate the effective chargeable period by comparing both: port demurrage applies based on port free time, while carrier detention applies based on shipping line free time. When the shipping line extends free time via an amendment, the system must retroactively recalculate any previously accrued charges against the updated free time window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R004	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Grace Period Request and Approval Workflow	The system must provide a workflow for requesting additional grace periods beyond the standard or contractual free time. The request must capture the container numbers, BL reference, reason for the extension (selectable from a configurable list including customs delay, documentation pending, port congestion, vessel schedule change, force majeure), the number of additional days requested, and supporting documentation. The approval workflow must route through configurable approval levels based on the grace period duration and estimated financial impact. Grace period requests must be trackable with statuses: Submitted, Pending Approval, Approved, Rejected, Expired. Approved grace periods must automatically adjust the demurrage calculation start date for the affected containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R005	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Force Majeure Free Time Extension Mechanism	The system must support a force majeure event declaration that automatically extends free time for all containers at a specified port or terminal during the declared event period. The declaration must capture the event type (natural disaster, pandemic, strike, political unrest, severe weather), the affected port or terminal, the start and end dates, and the number of additional free days granted. Once declared, the system must recalculate demurrage for all affected containers by excluding the force majeure period from chargeable days. The declaration must be authorized by a senior operations director and must generate an audit record. The system must support retroactive force majeure declarations where the event start date is in the past.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R006	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Countdown Tracker per Container	The system must display a real-time countdown of remaining free time days for each active container at every port terminal. The countdown must account for the applicable free time type (standard, customer-specific, or shipping line allocation) and must deduct non-working days if the tariff rule specifies working-day counting. The tracker must display the countdown in a color-coded format: green for more than 3 days remaining, amber for 1-3 days remaining, red for expired free time. The tracker must be visible on the container detail screen, the port operations dashboard, and the customer shipment tracking portal. Arabic UI must display the countdown with Hijri reference dates alongside Gregorian dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R007	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Utilization Analytics and Reporting	The system must generate analytics on free time utilization across all ports, entities, and customers. Reports must include: average free time used vs. allotted per port and container type, percentage of containers exceeding free time, top customers by free time overrun frequency, and financial impact of free time extensions granted. Reports must be filterable by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), port, entity, customer, and container type. The AI engine must identify patterns where certain trade lanes or customers consistently exceed free time and must recommend adjustments to standard free time allocations or customer terms. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R008	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Automated Free Time Expiry Notification	The system must send automated notifications to the consignee, the clearing agent, and the internal operations team when a container's free time is approaching expiry. Notifications must be triggered at configurable intervals: default at 72 hours before expiry, 48 hours before expiry, 24 hours before expiry, and upon expiry. Each notification must include the container number, BL reference, port location, free time expiry date and time, estimated demurrage rate that will apply after expiry, and instructions for requesting a grace period extension. Notifications must be delivered via email and in-app notification, with SMS as an optional additional channel. All notifications must be available in English and Arabic with correct RTL formatting for Arabic recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R009	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Agreement Bulk Import and Validation	The system must support bulk import of customer free time agreements from structured files (CSV, Excel) to accommodate large-scale contract onboarding or annual tariff resets. The import template must enforce mandatory fields: customer code, port code, container type, direction, free time days, effective from date, effective to date, and approval reference. The import process must validate that no overlapping agreements exist for the same customer-port-container type-direction combination within the effective date range. Validation errors must be returned as a downloadable error report with row numbers and specific error descriptions. Successfully validated records must be placed in a pending-approval queue before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R010	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Multi-Entity Free Time Policy Governance	The system must enforce entity-level governance over free time policies, ensuring that each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) can define its own maximum allowable free time extensions and approval thresholds while adhering to group-level minimums. A group administrator must be able to set a global minimum standard free time that no entity can reduce below. Entity-level administrators must only modify free time policies for their own entity. Cross-entity visibility must be available to group-level users for benchmarking purposes. Policy changes must go through an approval workflow and must be effective from a future date to prevent mid-cycle disruptions to ongoing demurrage calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R011	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Calculation for Transshipment Containers	The system must handle free time calculation for transshipment containers that arrive at a hub port (e.g., Hamad Port, Jebel Ali) and are subsequently loaded onto a connecting vessel for onward carriage. Transshipment free time must be calculated from the discharge of the first vessel to the loading on the second vessel. The applicable free time for transshipment must be separately configurable and is typically longer than import free time (e.g., 14 days vs. 7 days). If the connecting vessel is delayed beyond the transshipment free time, the system must determine whether the delay is attributable to the carrier (no demurrage) or the terminal (demurrage applies) based on the vessel schedule data. The carrier attribution logic must be configurable per port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R012	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Audit and Change History Log	The system must log every change to free time configurations, customer agreements, and grace period grants with full audit detail. The log must capture the previous value, the new value, the user who made the change, the timestamp in both UTC and local port time, the authorization reference, and the business justification. For customer-specific free time agreements, the log must also record the sales representative who requested the agreement and the commercial justification. The audit log must be immutable and retained for a minimum of seven years. Authorized auditors must be able to search the log by customer, port, date range, and change type, and export filtered results in CSV format for compliance review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R013	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Hijri Calendar Integration for Free Time Computation	The system must fully support Hijri calendar dates in free time computation for operations in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. When a port tariff specifies free time in Hijri days, the system must compute the free time window using the Umm al-Qura Hijri calendar. Hijri weekends (Friday-Saturday in KSA, Friday in Qatar pre-2024, Saturday-Sunday in UAE) must be correctly identified for working-day calculations. The system must handle the discrepancy between Hijri and Gregorian month boundaries correctly, especially for free time periods that span the end of a Hijri month. All date inputs and displays must offer a Hijri date picker alongside the Gregorian picker, and free time reports must optionally display dates in Hijri format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R014	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	AI-Recommended Free Time Optimization	The AI engine must analyze historical container dwell patterns, customer pickup behaviors, and port congestion trends to recommend optimal standard free time durations per port and container type. Recommendations must balance the commercial objective of minimizing demurrage disputes with the operational objective of incentivizing faster container retrieval. The AI must present recommendations with supporting data: projected change in average dwell time, estimated demurrage revenue impact, and customer satisfaction impact score. Recommendations must be generated quarterly and must be reviewable by the commercial and operations teams before any changes are applied. The recommendation report must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R015	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Weekend and Working Day Configuration per Jurisdiction	The system must support jurisdiction-specific weekend definitions for free time working-day calculations: Friday-Saturday for Qatar and KSA, Saturday-Sunday for UAE (post-2022 calendar reform), and Saturday-Sunday for India. The configuration must be effective-dated to handle historical changes (e.g., UAE's shift from Friday-Saturday to Saturday-Sunday effective January 2022). For ports operating across jurisdiction boundaries (e.g., a UAE entity managing operations at Hamad Port in Qatar), the applicable weekend must follow the port's physical jurisdiction, not the managing entity's jurisdiction. The system must allow administrators to override the standard weekend for specific terminals that operate on non-standard schedules, such as 24/7 terminals where no weekends are observed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R016	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Extension Impact Simulation	The system must provide a simulation tool that calculates the financial impact of granting a proposed free time extension before it is approved. The simulation must take the proposed extension in days, the affected containers or customer scope, and the current demurrage rate schedule as inputs, and must output the total demurrage revenue that would be foregone, the number of containers affected, and the average per-container financial impact. The simulation must be available to commercial managers during customer negotiation and to operations managers during grace period approval. Results must be displayed in the entity's base currency with the option to view in the port's local currency. Simulation results must be attachable to the grace period approval workflow as supporting evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R017	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Offline Free Time Data Synchronization	The system must ensure that free time configurations, customer agreements, and grace period records are available offline for field operations staff who may operate in areas with limited connectivity, such as remote customs yards in India or port facilities during network outages. The offline module must cache the latest free time parameters and must synchronize changes bidirectionally when connectivity is restored. If a grace period was approved offline and a conflicting update was made online during the disconnection period, the system must flag the conflict and require manual resolution by a supervisor. The offline cache must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption to protect commercially sensitive customer agreement data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-002-R018	FEAT-022-1-002	MOD-022	Free Time Compliance with Port Authority Regulations	The system must enforce minimum free time periods mandated by port authority regulations at each configured port. For example, JNPT mandates a minimum of 3 free days for import containers as per Indian Customs regulations, while Jebel Ali follows DP World's published tariff minimums. The system must prevent administrators from configuring standard free time below these regulatory minimums and must display a warning when customer-specific agreements set free time at exactly the regulatory minimum. When port authority regulations change, the system must support a regulatory update workflow where the new minimum is reviewed, approved, and applied to all affected configurations. The system must generate a compliance report listing all active free time configurations and their compliance status against current regulatory minimums.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R001	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Container Gate-Out and Return Event Tracking	The system must track the complete lifecycle of each container from gate-out at the port terminal to return at the designated empty container depot or terminal. The gate-out timestamp must be captured from the terminal operating system integration or manual entry with maker-checker validation. The return event must record the depot code, return timestamp, container condition (clean, damaged, requires repair), and the receiving inspector's ID. The system must support multiple return locations per port (e.g., Jebel Ali has multiple inland container depots) and must validate that the return depot is an authorized location for the applicable shipping line. Any gap between gate-out and return constitutes the detention period subject to charge calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R002	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Carrier-Specific Detention Tariff Management	The system must maintain detention tariff schedules per shipping line (identified by SCAC code), per port, and per container type. Each tariff must define tiered daily rates with configurable time bands (e.g., days 1-4 free, days 5-10 at USD 25/day for 20GP, days 11+ at USD 50/day). Tariffs must be versioned with effective date ranges and must support currency specification per carrier preference (some carriers bill in USD regardless of port location). The system must support importing tariff updates from carrier notifications in PDF or structured formats, with AI-assisted extraction of rate data from PDF tariff circulars. Tariff changes must go through a review and approval workflow before becoming effective in detention calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R003	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Real-Time Detention Charge Accrual	The system must calculate detention charges in real time for all containers that have been gated out from the terminal and not yet returned. The calculation must use the gate-out timestamp as the starting point, apply the carrier's detention free time, and then compute daily charges per the applicable tiered tariff schedule. The system must handle partial-day rules per carrier policy (some carriers charge for any partial day, others prorate). Detention charges must be computed in the carrier's billing currency and simultaneously converted to the entity's base currency using the daily FX rate. The system must recalculate detention automatically when a container return event is recorded, finalizing the total detention charge for that container.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R004	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Exposure Dashboard and Alerts	The system must provide a real-time detention exposure dashboard showing all containers currently outside the terminal with their elapsed detention days, accrued charges, and projected charges if returned in 1, 3, or 7 days. The dashboard must support filtering by port, entity, customer, shipping line, and container type. Color-coded indicators must highlight containers in free time (green), approaching free time expiry (amber), and in chargeable detention (red). The system must trigger automated alerts when a container's detention crosses configurable financial thresholds (e.g., USD 500, USD 1,000, USD 5,000 equivalent). Alerts must be sent to the assigned operations controller and the customer's registered contact in their preferred language (English or Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R005	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Waiver and Carrier Credit Tracking	The system must support recording detention waivers granted by shipping lines, including the waiver amount, the carrier reference number, the containers covered, and the validity period. When a carrier issues a detention waiver, the system must automatically reduce the calculated detention charge for the specified containers. If the waiver is received after an invoice has already been issued, the system must generate a credit note linked to the original invoice. The system must also track pending waiver requests submitted to carriers, with aging reports showing requests outstanding beyond configurable SLA periods (default 14 days). The AI engine must predict the likelihood of waiver approval based on the carrier, reason code, and historical approval patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R006	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Multi-Trip and Round-Trip Container Detention Handling	The system must correctly handle detention for containers used in multi-trip or round-trip scenarios, which are common in the India-Gulf trade. When a container is gated out for delivery at one location and then picked up again for stuffing at another location before being returned to the port, the system must track each trip segment separately. Detention-free time may apply to the total out-of-terminal duration or to each individual trip, depending on the carrier's policy, and the system must be configurable for both models. The system must prevent double-counting of detention days when a container passes through an intermediate depot that records both a return and a re-dispatch event within a single overall detention cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R007	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Container Condition and Damage-Related Detention Handling	The system must track container condition upon return and manage detention implications when a container is returned in a damaged state. If the shipping line rejects the container return due to damage, the detention clock must continue running until the container is repaired and accepted. The system must record the damage type (structural, cosmetic, reefer machinery), estimated repair duration, and whether the damage is attributable to the consignee (detention continues) or was pre-existing (detention should be waived from the first return attempt date). A dispute resolution workflow must be available when the damage attribution is contested, with photo evidence and surveyor report uploads. Damage-related detention charges must be separately identifiable in financial reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R008	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Depot Integration for Container Return Validation	The system must integrate with authorized empty container depots at each port location to receive automated container return notifications. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, integration with major depot operators (e.g., Unipac, Emirates Container Services) must be supported via API or EDI. For JNPT, integration with CFS/ICD operators must follow the Indian Container Freight Station data exchange standards. For Hamad Port, the system must interface with Qatar-designated depots. Where depot integration is not available, the system must support manual return entry with mandatory fields: depot code, return date/time, container number, seal number, condition status. Manual entries must be validated against the expected return depot per the carrier's depot nomination list.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R009	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Charge Allocation to Cost Centers	The system must allocate detention charges to the appropriate cost center based on configurable rules. Allocation can be by BL-level responsibility (consignee bears detention), by shipment job (allocated to the job P&L), by customer account, or by internal department (operations, commercial). When detention is incurred due to operational delays attributable to the company (e.g., delayed customs documentation preparation), the charge must be allocated to the internal cost center responsible for the delay. The system must support split allocations where multiple parties share responsibility, with configurable percentage splits. All allocations must flow through to the general ledger module with the correct account codes per the entity's chart of accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R010	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	SOC and COC Container Detention Differentiation	The system must differentiate between Shipper-Owned Container (SOC) and Carrier-Owned Container (COC) detention tracking. For COC containers, standard carrier detention tariffs apply, and the system must track charges per the carrier's published tariff. For SOC containers, detention does not apply in the traditional sense, but the system must track the container's out-of-terminal duration for utilization reporting purposes. The system must identify the container ownership type from the booking or BL data and automatically apply the correct detention logic. If a container's ownership type changes mid-cycle (rare but possible in container leasing scenarios), the system must handle the transition and apply the correct tariff for each period based on the ownership change effective date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R011	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Liability Tracking by INCOTERM	The system must determine the party liable for detention charges based on the INCOTERM specified in the shipment. Under EXW, FCA, and FAS terms, the buyer typically bears detention at the destination; under CIF, CFR, and CPT terms, liability may shift depending on the contract. The system must maintain a configurable INCOTERM-to-detention-liability mapping table that can be overridden at the customer agreement level. When generating detention invoices or allocating charges, the system must reference this mapping to identify the correct billable party. For complex INCOTERM scenarios like DDP where the seller bears all risk until delivery, the system must track detention against the seller's account and support cross-entity billing if the seller and buyer are different group entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R012	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Duration Prediction Using AI	The AI engine must predict the expected detention duration for containers at gate-out based on historical patterns for the consignee, the delivery location, the container type, and the trade lane. The prediction must be presented as an estimated return date with a confidence score and must be used to proactively calculate the expected detention charge exposure. If the AI predicts that a container is likely to exceed the free time by more than a configurable threshold (default 5 days), the system must automatically trigger an early warning notification to the operations team and the customer. The prediction model must be continuously retrained using actual return data and must report its prediction accuracy as a rolling 30-day MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) metric visible on the analytics dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R013	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Tracking for Cross-Border Movements	The system must handle detention tracking for containers involved in cross-border movements within the GCC region (e.g., a container gated out from Jebel Ali for delivery in Oman, then returned to a Dubai depot). The detention clock must continue across border crossings and must not reset when the container crosses a customs territory. The system must track the container's geographic location history using GPS data from container tracking providers where available, and must calculate detention based on the original gate-out timestamp from the first port terminal. For containers moving between GCC countries under the GCC Customs Union transit arrangements, the system must identify the applicable customs regime and any duty-free transit periods that might affect the container's return timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R014	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Reconciliation with Carrier Invoices	The system must support reconciliation of internally calculated detention charges against invoices received from shipping lines. The reconciliation process must match carrier invoice line items to internal container-level detention calculations by container number and date range. Variances must be categorized as: rate difference, free time difference, date calculation difference, or unmatched container. The system must generate a reconciliation report showing matched items, variance items with detailed breakdowns, and unmatched items from both the internal calculation and the carrier invoice. Where AI detects a pattern of systematic overcharging by a carrier (e.g., consistently using fewer free days than contracted), it must flag this for commercial team review with supporting evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R015	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Reports by Entity, Port, and Carrier	The system must generate detention reports aggregated by entity, port, carrier, and customer with drill-down capability to individual container level. Standard reports must include: total detention charges by period (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly), average detention duration by carrier and port, top 10 customers by detention charges, and detention charge recovery rate (billed vs. collected). Reports must support multi-currency presentation where the user can select to view all amounts in a single currency with FX conversion, or in original currencies. All reports must render correctly in both English and Arabic, with Arabic reports using RTL layout, Arabic numerals optionally, and Hijri date references. Reports must be schedulable for automated generation and distribution via email.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R016	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Data Offline Access and Sync	The system must provide offline access to detention tracking data for transport coordinators who may be at inland depots or customer warehouses with limited connectivity. The offline module must cache the list of containers currently in detention, their gate-out dates, applicable free time, and accrued charges. Users must be able to record container return events offline with mandatory fields including depot code, timestamp, and condition. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize offline return events with the server, validate them against any conflicting updates, and recalculate detention charges accordingly. Offline data must be encrypted using AES-256 at rest on the device, and the offline session must auto-expire after 72 hours requiring re-authentication and fresh data sync.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R017	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention Charge Reversal and Credit Processing	The system must support full and partial reversal of detention charges when a shipping line grants a retroactive waiver, when a dispute is resolved in the customer's favor, or when a calculation error is identified. Reversals must not delete the original charge; instead, a credit entry must be created referencing the original charge ID, the reversal reason, the approving user, and the authorization reference. If the original charge has already been invoiced, the system must automatically generate a credit note for the reversed amount. If the charge was part of an inter-company allocation, the reversal must cascade to the inter-company entries, generating corresponding inter-company credit and debit adjustments. All reversals must be logged in the audit trail with the full before-and-after financial impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-003-R018	FEAT-022-1-003	MOD-022	Detention KPI Monitoring and SLA Compliance	The system must track detention-related KPIs against configurable SLA targets per entity and per port. Key KPIs must include: average container turnaround time (gate-out to return), percentage of containers returned within free time, total detention cost as percentage of revenue, and detention invoice recovery rate. The system must display KPI performance on the operations dashboard with trend indicators (improving, stable, deteriorating) based on rolling 30-day, 90-day, and 12-month averages. When a KPI breaches its SLA threshold, the system must generate an automated alert to the entity's operations director. KPI definitions and thresholds must be configurable per entity, and the group-level dashboard must show a consolidated view across all entities with the ability to benchmark entity performance side by side.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R001	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Automated D&D Invoice Generation from Accrued Charges	The system must automatically generate demurrage and detention invoices from finalized charge records when a container's demurrage period ends (gate-out event) or detention period ends (container return event). The invoice generation must aggregate charges at the configurable level: per container, per BL, per shipment job, or per customer account per period. Each invoice line item must detail the container number, the charge type (demurrage or detention), the chargeable period (from date to date), the number of chargeable days, the daily rate applied, the total amount in original currency, and the converted amount in the invoice currency. The system must support both on-demand invoice generation by authorized users and scheduled batch generation at configurable intervals (daily, weekly, monthly).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R002	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Multi-Currency Invoice Generation with Tax Compliance	The system must generate invoices in the customer's agreed billing currency, which may differ from the port's local currency where the charge was incurred. Supported currencies must include USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The FX conversion rate used must be the rate on the invoice date or the charge accrual date, configurable per entity policy. Each invoice must include the applicable tax: VAT at 5% for UAE and KSA invoices, no VAT for Qatar (until mandated), and GST at the applicable rate (currently 18% for services) for India invoices. Tax calculations must reference the tax master configuration from the ERP's tax module and must handle tax-exempt scenarios (e.g., exports, free zone entities in UAE, SEZ units in India) by applying the correct exemption codes and references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R003	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Bilingual Arabic-English Invoice Document Layout	The system must generate invoice documents in a bilingual format displaying both English and Arabic content. The invoice layout must comply with each jurisdiction's regulatory requirements: UAE FTA e-invoicing guidelines, KSA ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoicing requirements, and India GST invoice rules. The Arabic content must render correctly in RTL format with Arabic numerals optionally configurable per entity preference. The invoice must include: entity legal name in Arabic and English, customer name in Arabic and English, invoice number with entity prefix, invoice date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, line item descriptions in both languages, total amounts in words in both languages, and the entity's TRN (Tax Registration Number) or GSTIN. The PDF output must use Unicode-compliant Arabic fonts that support all required diacritical marks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R004	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	KSA ZATCA Phase 2 E-Invoice Compliance	For invoices generated by the KSA entity, the system must comply with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) Phase 2 e-invoicing requirements (Fatoorah). Each invoice must be digitally signed using the entity's ZATCA-issued cryptographic stamp, must include a QR code containing the seller name, VAT registration number, invoice date and time, total with VAT, and VAT amount encoded in TLV (Tag-Length-Value) format as per ZATCA specifications. The system must generate the invoice in UBL 2.1 XML format and submit it to the ZATCA Fatoorah platform for clearance before dispatching to the customer. The system must handle ZATCA rejection responses by flagging the invoice for correction and resubmission. All ZATCA submission logs must be retained for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R005	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	India GST-Compliant Invoice Generation	For invoices generated by the India entity, the system must comply with India GST invoice requirements including mandatory fields: GSTIN of supplier and recipient, HSN/SAC code for the service (SAC 996719 for container storage/demurrage services), place of supply, CGST/SGST amounts for intra-state supply or IGST amount for inter-state supply, and invoice serial number in the prescribed format. The system must determine the tax rate based on the place of supply rules: if the supplier and recipient are in the same state (e.g., both in Maharashtra for JNPT operations), CGST and SGST apply; if in different states, IGST applies. For exports and supplies to SEZ units, the invoice must apply zero-rated GST with the appropriate LUT (Letter of Undertaking) reference. Invoices must be compatible with the GST portal's e-invoice schema for IRN (Invoice Reference Number) generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R006	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	UAE VAT-Compliant Invoice Generation	For invoices generated by the UAE entity, the system must comply with UAE Federal Tax Authority (FTA) VAT invoice requirements. Each tax invoice must include the entity's TRN, the customer's TRN if VAT-registered, the invoice date, a sequential invoice number, a description of the services (demurrage/detention), the taxable amount, the VAT rate (5%), and the VAT amount in AED. For supplies to entities within designated free zones (e.g., JAFZA, KIZAD), the system must determine whether the supply is zero-rated or standard-rated based on the nature of the service and the free zone's VAT status. The system must also support generating simplified tax invoices for amounts below AED 10,000 where the customer's TRN is not mandatory. Currency conversion to AED must use the UAE Central Bank exchange rate for the invoice date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R007	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	D&D Invoice Numbering and Sequencing	The system must generate unique, sequential invoice numbers per entity using a configurable numbering format. The format must support entity prefix, fiscal year, location code, and sequential number components (e.g., QAT-2526-DOH-000001 for Qatar entity, UAE-2526-JEA-000001 for UAE entity at Jebel Ali). The numbering sequence must guarantee no gaps in production invoices, as required by tax authorities in all four jurisdictions. Draft invoices must use a separate temporary numbering sequence. When an invoice is cancelled, its number must be retained in the sequence with a cancelled status, not reused. The system must support fiscal year rollover with automatic sequence reset and must allow configurable sequence starting numbers for each new fiscal year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R008	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Automated Invoice Dispatch via Email	The system must automatically dispatch generated invoices to the customer's registered email addresses upon approval. The dispatch must include the invoice PDF as an attachment and a configurable email body template in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic). For KSA ZATCA-compliant invoices, the XML file must also be attached. The system must support multiple email recipients per customer (e.g., accounts payable, operations contact, clearing agent). Email dispatch must use the entity's configured SMTP server or email service provider and must include the entity's email signature and legal disclaimers. The system must track email delivery status (sent, delivered, bounced, opened) using read receipts or email tracking integration, and must retry bounced emails after verifying the email address with the customer records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R009	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	D&D Credit Note Generation	The system must support generating credit notes for previously issued D&D invoices when charges are reversed, waivers are applied post-invoicing, disputes are resolved in the customer's favor, or calculation errors are corrected. The credit note must reference the original invoice number, detail the containers and charge periods being credited, the credit amount per line item, and the total credit in the invoice currency with applicable tax adjustments. Credit notes must follow the same tax compliance rules as invoices: ZATCA-compliant credit notes for KSA, GST-compliant credit notes for India (with original invoice IRN reference), and FTA-compliant credit notes for UAE. Credit note numbers must follow a separate sequential numbering series per entity and must be dispatched to the customer using the same channels as the original invoice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R010	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Inter-Company D&D Invoice Generation	The system must generate inter-company invoices when demurrage or detention charges incurred by one entity are allocated to another entity within the group. For example, if demurrage at JNPT is the commercial responsibility of the Qatar entity under a principal-agent arrangement, the India entity must generate an inter-company invoice to the Qatar entity. Inter-company invoices must use transfer pricing rules configured in the multi-entity module and must comply with the tax regulations of both the issuing and receiving entity's jurisdictions. The system must handle withholding tax implications (e.g., India TDS on services at 10% for payments to non-residents) and must generate the appropriate withholding tax certificates. Inter-company invoices must be automatically reconciled during the inter-company settlement process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R011	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Invoice Approval Workflow Before Dispatch	The system must enforce a configurable approval workflow for D&D invoices before they are dispatched to customers. The workflow must support single-level or multi-level approval based on invoice amount thresholds: invoices below a configurable threshold (e.g., USD 1,000) may be auto-approved, invoices between USD 1,000 and USD 10,000 require operations manager approval, and invoices above USD 10,000 require finance manager approval. Approvers must be able to review the invoice details, the underlying charge calculations, and any associated waivers or disputes before approving. Rejected invoices must be returned to the preparer with rejection comments and must support amendment and resubmission. The approval workflow must enforce segregation of duties: the user who generates the invoice cannot approve it.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R012	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Batch Invoice Generation and Processing	The system must support batch invoice generation for processing large volumes of D&D charges efficiently. A batch run must allow selection criteria including: port, date range of charges, customer, carrier, and entity. The batch process must aggregate eligible charges per the configured invoicing level (per container, per BL, per customer-period), generate draft invoices, and present them for bulk review before finalization. The batch must process up to 5,000 invoices in a single run with progress tracking and the ability to pause and resume. If errors occur during batch processing (e.g., missing customer billing address, invalid tax registration), the system must skip the errored invoice, log the error, and continue processing the remaining invoices. A batch completion report must summarize total invoices generated, total amount, errors encountered, and skipped items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R013	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Invoice Aging and Outstanding Balance Tracking	The system must track the aging of outstanding D&D invoices using configurable aging buckets (e.g., current, 1-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, 90+ days). The aging report must be generated per entity, per customer, and per port, showing the invoice number, date, original amount, payments received, credit notes applied, and outstanding balance. The aging calculation must use the invoice due date as the reference point, with due date determined by the customer's payment terms (e.g., net 30, net 60). The system must automatically flag invoices that are overdue and must trigger dunning notifications at configurable intervals (e.g., 7 days overdue, 14 days overdue, 30 days overdue). Aging data must feed into the customer credit risk assessment and may trigger credit hold recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R014	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	D&D Invoice Integration with Accounts Receivable	The system must automatically post generated D&D invoices to the accounts receivable sub-ledger in the ERP's finance module. The posting must include the correct GL account codes for demurrage revenue, detention revenue, VAT/GST output tax, and any applicable withholding tax. The accounts receivable entry must carry the customer account code, invoice number, due date, and entity code for multi-entity reporting. When payments are received against D&D invoices, the system must support automatic matching by invoice number and must update the D&D module's invoice status to paid, partially paid, or overpaid. Unmatched payments must be flagged for manual allocation. The integration must be real-time or near-real-time with a maximum latency of 5 minutes between invoice generation and AR posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R015	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	D&D Revenue Recognition and Reporting	The system must support configurable revenue recognition policies for D&D charges per entity, compliant with IFRS 15 (Revenue from Contracts with Customers). Revenue may be recognized at the point of charge accrual (daily as demurrage/detention accrues), at the point of invoicing, or at the point of cash collection, as configured per entity's accounting policy. The system must generate revenue recognition journal entries in the correct accounting periods and must handle period-end cut-off accurately, recognizing accrued but uninvoiced D&D revenue as a receivable. Revenue reports must break down D&D revenue by port, entity, customer, charge type (demurrage vs. detention), and currency. Reports must support consolidation across entities with inter-company elimination for group-level financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R016	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Proforma D&D Invoice Generation	The system must support generating proforma D&D invoices for customers who request advance billing estimates or where prepayment is required before cargo release. Proforma invoices must include all the same details as final invoices (container details, charge periods, rates, amounts, taxes) but must be clearly marked as PROFORMA and must use a separate numbering series. Proforma invoices must not post to the accounts receivable ledger and must not be included in tax returns. When the actual charges are finalized, the system must allow conversion of a proforma invoice to a final invoice, retaining the proforma reference. If the actual charges differ from the proforma estimate, the final invoice must show the variance and the system must handle any excess prepayment as a credit balance on the customer's account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R017	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	D&D Invoice Audit Trail and Document Retention	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every D&D invoice from generation through approval, dispatch, payment, and cancellation. Each audit entry must record the action (created, approved, rejected, dispatched, paid, cancelled, credit noted), the acting user, the timestamp in UTC and local time, and the before-and-after state of the invoice. All invoice documents (PDF, XML, email dispatch records) must be stored in the document management system with retention periods compliant with each jurisdiction: Qatar QFC requires 6 years minimum, UAE FTA requires 5 years, KSA ZATCA requires 6 years, and India GST requires 6 years from the due date of the annual return. The system must prevent deletion of invoice records within the retention period and must support retrieval by invoice number, customer, date range, and entity for audit and regulatory examination purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-1-004-R018	FEAT-022-1-004	MOD-022	Offline Invoice Draft and Queue for Dispatch	The system must allow authorized users to generate draft D&D invoices in offline mode when internet connectivity is unavailable. The offline module must cache the necessary data including charge records, customer billing details, tax rates, and invoice number sequences (with a pre-allocated block of sequence numbers to avoid conflicts). Draft invoices created offline must be stored locally with encrypted storage (AES-256) and must be queued for server synchronization upon connectivity restoration. The synchronization process must validate each queued invoice against the server-side charge records and customer data, flagging any discrepancies (e.g., charge was modified online during the offline period). Validated invoices must enter the standard approval workflow upon synchronization. The system must prevent duplicate invoice generation by cross-referencing queued offline invoices against server-side invoices created during the offline period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R001	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Case Initiation and Registration	The system must allow authorized users including shippers, consignees, customs brokers, and freight forwarders to initiate a D&D dispute case by selecting the specific invoice line items being contested. Each dispute case must be assigned a unique case reference number following the pattern DSP-{ENTITY}-{YYYY}-{SEQ} where ENTITY is QA, AE, SA, or IN. The system must capture mandatory fields including disputed amount in original invoice currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR), dispute reason category from a configurable taxonomy, container numbers, BL reference, vessel voyage, and port of discharge (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP). Supporting documents must be attachable at initiation with minimum one document required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R002	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Multi-Stage Dispute Workflow Configuration	The system must support a configurable multi-stage dispute resolution workflow with a minimum of six stages: Filing, Acknowledgement, Investigation, Evidence Review, Committee Decision, and Resolution. Each stage must have configurable SLA timers measured in business hours with separate calendars for each entity jurisdiction considering local holidays including Eid Al Fitr, Eid Al Adha, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, and Indian Republic Day. Stage transitions must enforce mandatory checklist completion before progression. Workflow configuration must be entity-specific allowing different dispute processes for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations while maintaining a unified reporting structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R003	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Evidence Collection and Document Management	The system must provide a structured evidence collection framework for each dispute case supporting document categories including gate-in/gate-out records, container tracking data, port authority clearance certificates, customs release orders, vessel discharge reports, terminal operator confirmations, and force majeure declarations. Each evidence item must be timestamped, tagged with its source entity, and cryptographically hashed for tamper detection. The system must support document formats PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, and XML with maximum file size of 25MB per document. Arabic-language documents must be accepted and indexed. AI-powered OCR must extract key dates and reference numbers from uploaded evidence documents automatically for cross-referencing against system records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R004	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Investigation Assignment and Tracking	The system must automatically assign dispute cases to investigation officers based on configurable rules including port of operation, dispute amount threshold, container type (dry, reefer, tank, flat-rack, open-top), and customer tier classification. Investigators must receive the complete case file including original D&D calculation breakdown, free-time allocation details, container movement history, and all submitted evidence. The assignment engine must support load balancing across investigators with maximum concurrent case limits configurable per entity. Reassignment must be possible by supervisors with full audit trail. Investigation progress must be tracked with mandatory milestone updates at configurable intervals defaulting to every 48 business hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R005	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Committee Review and Voting	The system must support formation of dispute review committees for cases exceeding configurable amount thresholds per entity and currency. Committee composition must be configurable requiring minimum three members with defined roles: chairperson, operations representative, and finance representative. The system must provide a structured voting mechanism supporting outcomes of Approve Full Reversal, Approve Partial Reversal, Reject, and Escalate. Each committee member must submit individual assessment with justification text minimum 50 characters. Quorum rules must be enforced before final decision recording. Committee meetings must support both synchronous review with calendar integration and asynchronous review with individual deadline tracking. All committee decisions must be recorded with Hijri and Gregorian dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R006	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Resolution Actions and Credit Processing	Upon dispute resolution the system must automatically generate the appropriate financial transaction based on the decision outcome. For full or partial reversals the system must create credit notes in the original invoice currency with proper exchange rate handling for multi-currency scenarios using the rate from the original invoice date or current rate based on entity-specific policy configuration. Credit notes must reference the original invoice number, dispute case number, and committee decision reference. The system must integrate with the accounts receivable module to apply credits against outstanding balances or generate refund requests. Partial reversals must specify the exact line items and amounts being reversed with recalculated tax implications per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R007	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	SLA Monitoring and Escalation Engine	The system must continuously monitor dispute case SLAs at each workflow stage and trigger automated escalations when thresholds are breached. Three escalation levels must be supported: Warning at 75 percent of SLA, Breach at 100 percent of SLA, and Critical at 150 percent of SLA. Each escalation level must trigger configurable actions including email notifications, SMS alerts, dashboard highlighting, and manager notifications. Escalation recipients must be configurable per entity, port, and dispute amount band. The system must calculate SLA elapsed time excluding non-business hours and entity-specific holidays. SLA pause functionality must be available for cases pending external information with documented pause reasons and maximum cumulative pause duration limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R008	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Analytics and Reporting Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dispute analytics dashboard showing key metrics including total open disputes by entity and port, average resolution time by dispute category, dispute success rate by customer segment, financial impact of approved reversals in each operating currency, and aging analysis of pending disputes. Dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port code, date range using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, dispute category, customer, and amount band. Trend analysis must show dispute volume and resolution patterns over configurable time periods. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with proper number formatting per locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R002	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	EmS Schedule Integration for Fire and Spillage Response	The system must provide instant access to Emergency Schedules (EmS) for every substance listed in the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List, covering both fire schedules (F-A through F-J) and spillage schedules (S-A through S-Z). Each EmS entry must detail the specific response actions, including firefighting agents to use or avoid, personal protective equipment required, spillage containment procedures, and environmental protection measures. The system must cross-reference EmS codes with the specific cargo loaded at each port (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA) so that port emergency response teams can instantly access the relevant EmS for containers currently at the terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R009	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Customer Self-Service Dispute Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing web portal allowing registered customers to file disputes, upload supporting evidence, track dispute status in real-time, view historical dispute records, and receive resolution notifications. The portal must authenticate customers via integration with the customer management module supporting SSO and two-factor authentication. The portal interface must be fully bilingual Arabic and English with automatic language detection based on user profile preferences. All monetary values displayed must show the customer's preferred currency with conversion rates where applicable. The portal must be responsive supporting desktop and mobile browsers and must function in degraded mode during intermittent connectivity showing cached dispute status information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R010	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Precedent Database and Search	The system must maintain a searchable database of all resolved dispute cases to serve as precedent references for investigators and committee members reviewing new disputes. The precedent search must support filtering by dispute category, resolution outcome, port, container type, shipping line, customer segment, and dispute amount range. AI-powered similarity matching must identify the top five most relevant precedent cases when a new dispute is filed based on contextual factors including route, commodity type, delay reason, and free-time terms. Each precedent record must include the original dispute details, evidence summary, committee rationale, resolution outcome, and lessons learned notes. Access to the precedent database must be role-restricted to internal dispute resolution staff only.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R011	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	External Stakeholder Communication Tracking	The system must track all communications related to a dispute case including emails, portal messages, phone call summaries, and meeting notes with external stakeholders such as shipping lines, terminal operators, port authorities, and customs agencies. Each communication must be logged with timestamp, participants, communication channel, language used (Arabic or English), and content summary. The system must support templated responses in both Arabic and English for common dispute communication scenarios including acknowledgement, information request, status update, and resolution notification. Communication templates must include proper formal Arabic business language conventions. All outbound communications must be reviewed and approved by authorized personnel before dispatch for disputes exceeding configurable amount thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R012	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Workflow Offline Capability	The system must support offline dispute processing capabilities for field operations personnel at ports including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT where network connectivity may be intermittent. Offline mode must allow viewing of assigned dispute cases, adding investigation notes, uploading photographic evidence from mobile devices, and recording preliminary assessments. Upon network restoration the system must automatically synchronize offline data with conflict detection and resolution for concurrent modifications. Offline-capable data must be encrypted at rest on the local device using AES-256 encryption. The system must clearly indicate to users when operating in offline mode and display the timestamp of last successful synchronization. Maximum offline operation period must be configurable with a default of 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R013	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Regulatory Compliance and Statute of Limitations	The system must enforce jurisdiction-specific dispute filing deadlines and statute of limitations rules for demurrage and detention claims. For Qatar Maritime Authority regulations disputes must be filed within the configured period from invoice date. For UAE Federal Maritime Law and DMCA regulations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port separate limitation periods must apply. For Saudi Ports Authority MAWANI governed operations at Dammam and Jeddah the system must enforce KSA-specific dispute windows. For Indian operations at JNPT the system must comply with Indian Merchant Shipping Act provisions and Major Port Trusts Act regulations. The system must display remaining filing window prominently on D&D invoices and warn customers when deadlines approach within configurable threshold days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R014	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Multi-Entity Dispute Jurisdiction Routing	The system must automatically determine the correct entity jurisdiction for dispute resolution based on the port of discharge, booking entity, and contractual terms. Disputes involving transhipment cargo moving between entities such as Hamad Port to JNPT via Jebel Ali must be routed to the entity holding the primary contract with the customer. Where disputes involve charges from multiple entities the system must split the dispute into entity-specific sub-cases linked under a parent case with coordinated resolution tracking. Cross-entity dispute resolution must respect each entity's approval authority matrix and currency denomination. Consolidated dispute reports must present amounts in both local entity currency and a common reporting currency configurable as USD or QAR with exchange rates sourced from the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R015	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	Dispute Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for every dispute case capturing all state transitions, user actions, document uploads, communications, committee decisions, and financial transactions with microsecond timestamps. Each audit entry must record the acting user identity, role, entity affiliation, IP address, and action description in both English and Arabic where applicable. The audit trail must be tamper-evident using sequential hash chaining to detect any unauthorized modifications. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of ten years in compliance with Qatar Financial Centre regulatory requirements, UAE Commercial Companies Law, Saudi ZATCA regulations, and Indian Companies Act provisions. Authorized auditors must be able to export complete dispute case audit trails in structured formats including PDF, CSV, and XML.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-001-R016	FEAT-022-2-001	MOD-022	AI-Assisted Dispute Validity Pre-Screening	The system must employ an AI model to pre-screen newly filed disputes and provide an initial validity assessment score between zero and one hundred based on analysis of the dispute reason, supporting evidence quality, container movement timeline consistency, historical dispute patterns for the customer, and comparison with similar resolved cases from the precedent database. The AI pre-screening must complete within thirty seconds of dispute submission and must present its assessment with explanatory factors to the assigned investigator. The AI must flag potentially fraudulent dispute patterns such as repeated disputes on the same container across different entities or systematic disputes from the same broker exceeding statistical norms. Pre-screening results must be advisory only and must not automatically reject any dispute case.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R001	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Request Initiation and Classification	The system must allow authorized users to initiate waiver requests against specific D&D invoices or charge line items. Each waiver request must be classified into configurable categories including Contractual Concession, Commercial Goodwill, Force Majeure, Operational Disruption, Port Authority Delay, Customs Hold, and Regulatory Compliance. The waiver request must capture the requested waiver type as either full waiver or partial waiver with specific amount or percentage. For partial waivers the system must allow specification at individual container level supporting mixed waiver amounts across containers on the same booking. Currency of the waiver must match the original invoice currency from USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR. Each request must receive a unique reference WAV-{ENTITY}-{YYYY}-{SEQ}.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R002	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Tiered Approval Authority Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable tiered approval authority matrix for waiver requests based on waiver amount, waiver percentage of original charge, customer classification, and entity. The matrix must support a minimum of four approval tiers: Operations Manager for waivers up to a configurable threshold, Commercial Director for mid-range waivers, Country Manager for high-value waivers, and Regional CEO for waivers exceeding the highest threshold. Each tier must be configurable independently per entity with separate thresholds in local currency. The system must support both sequential and parallel approval routing configurable per tier. Approvers must be able to partially approve waivers by reducing the requested amount with mandatory justification. Delegation of approval authority must be supported with configurable validity periods and maximum delegation depth of two levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R003	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Contractual Concession Agreement Management	The system must maintain a repository of customer contractual concession agreements that define pre-approved D&D waiver terms including extended free-time days, reduced tariff rates, maximum annual waiver caps, and specific route or port exemptions. Each concession agreement must be linked to a customer account and optionally to specific trade lanes such as Hamad Port to JNPT or Jebel Ali to Mundra. Agreement validity periods must be tracked with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates. The system must automatically apply contractual concessions during D&D calculation before invoice generation, reducing the need for post-invoice waiver requests. Concession utilization must be tracked against annual caps with automatic alerts at 75 percent and 90 percent utilization thresholds. Expired or modified agreements must be versioned with full history maintained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R004	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Force Majeure Waiver Processing	The system must support bulk waiver processing for force majeure events affecting multiple containers, customers, and potentially multiple ports simultaneously. Authorized administrators must be able to declare a force majeure event specifying the affected port codes (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP), date range, event description in both Arabic and English, and applicable container categories. Upon declaration the system must automatically identify all D&D charges incurred during the force majeure period at affected ports and generate bulk waiver proposals. The bulk waiver must support configurable rules including full waiver for the event period, partial waiver based on percentage, or cap per container. Force majeure declarations must require dual authorization from senior management. The system must generate regulatory compliance reports documenting the force majeure event and associated waivers for port authority and maritime regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R005	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Financial Impact Calculation	The system must calculate and display the complete financial impact of each waiver request before approval including the waiver amount in original currency, equivalent amount in reporting currency using current exchange rates from the treasury module, applicable tax reversal implications per jurisdiction, impact on the customer's year-to-date D&D revenue, and comparison against the customer's contracted annual waiver allowance if applicable. The calculation must account for multi-currency scenarios where a single booking may have charges denominated in different currencies due to transhipment across entities. The system must maintain running totals of waiver amounts by customer, entity, port, and period to enable trend analysis. Financial impact summaries must be presented to approvers as part of the approval workflow with clear visualization of cumulative waiver exposure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R006	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Credit Note Generation	Upon waiver approval the system must automatically generate credit notes against the original D&D invoices reflecting the approved waiver amounts. Credit notes must comply with the invoicing requirements of each jurisdiction including Qatar Tax Authority e-invoicing format, UAE Federal Tax Authority requirements for VAT credit notes, Saudi ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoicing XML schema with QR code generation, and Indian GST credit note regulations. The credit note must reference the original invoice number, waiver case reference, approval authority, and approval date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. For partial waivers the credit note must itemize each container and the specific charges being waived. The system must handle tax recalculation for jurisdictions where D&D charges are subject to VAT or GST ensuring correct tax credit amounts on the credit note.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R007	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Concession Utilization Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing concession utilization across all active contractual agreements. The dashboard must display for each agreement the total contracted concession value, utilized amount to date, remaining balance, utilization percentage with visual progress indicators, and projected exhaustion date based on current consumption rate. Filters must include entity, customer tier, trade lane, port, and agreement validity period. The dashboard must support drill-down from agreement level to individual waiver transactions. Alert indicators must highlight agreements approaching exhaustion thresholds. The dashboard must render in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with currency amounts formatted according to locale conventions. Export functionality must support PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic text rendering and right-to-left table layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R007	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Carrier DG Policy and Embargo Enforcement	The system shall enforce carrier-specific DG policies and embargoes that may be more restrictive than IMDG Code requirements. Each carrier or vessel operator may define policies prohibiting certain UN numbers, restricting hazard classes to specific trade lanes, imposing lower quantity limits than IMDG permits, requiring additional documentation such as weathering certificates or stability data, or embargo DG cargo during specific periods such as Hajj season for KSA-bound shipments. The system must maintain a carrier DG policy register that is checked during booking acceptance screening. When a booking conflicts with a carrier policy, the system must display the specific carrier restriction to the operator in both English and Arabic, suggesting alternative carriers or routing options where available across the multi-entity network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R008	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Policy Rule Engine	The system must include a configurable rule engine that evaluates waiver requests against predefined business policies before routing to manual approval. Rules must support conditions based on customer attributes such as credit rating, annual revenue, payment history, dispute frequency, and relationship tenure. Additional rule conditions must include container type (standard dry 20ft and 40ft, high-cube, reefer, tank container, flat-rack, open-top), cargo commodity classification, trade lane, seasonal factors, and current port congestion status. Rules can produce outcomes including auto-approve with notification, auto-reject with reason, route to specific approval tier, or flag for enhanced review. Each rule must have configurable validity dates, entity applicability, and priority ordering. Rule changes must require authorized approval and maintain version history with rollback capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R009	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Customer Waiver History and Pattern Analysis	The system must maintain a comprehensive waiver history for each customer across all entities showing every waiver request, its outcome, amount, category, approving authority, and processing duration. The AI engine must analyze customer waiver patterns to identify trends such as increasing waiver frequency, seasonal waiver clustering, correlation between waiver requests and specific trade lanes or container types, and comparison against peer group averages. The analysis must flag anomalous patterns such as a customer whose waiver rate exceeds two standard deviations above their segment average. Pattern analysis results must be presented to commercial teams during contract renewal preparation and to approvers during waiver request review. Historical data must be retained for minimum seven years across all entities and must be searchable using both English and Arabic customer names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R010	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Operational Disruption Waiver Automation	The system must automatically identify and process waiver eligibility for D&D charges caused by operational disruptions within the company's control including vessel schedule delays, terminal equipment breakdowns, system outages, and internal processing errors. Integration with the vessel operations module must detect when vessel arrival deviates from scheduled ETA by more than a configurable threshold causing demurrage to accrue beyond contracted free time. The system must calculate the excess demurrage attributable to the operational disruption versus customer-caused delays. Automated waiver proposals must be generated for the disruption-attributed portion and routed to the appropriate approval authority. For port-wide disruptions at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, the system must support bulk processing with configurable rules per disruption category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R011	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Budget Management and Controls	The system must support annual waiver budget allocation by entity, port, customer segment, and waiver category. Budget amounts must be maintained in each entity's local currency with consolidated reporting in a common currency. Real-time budget consumption tracking must show approved waivers against allocated budgets with alerts at configurable threshold percentages. When waiver approvals would exceed the remaining budget for a category the system must enforce additional approval requirements routing to a higher authority tier. Budget reallocation between categories within an entity must be supported with appropriate authorization. Monthly and quarterly budget variance reports must compare actual waivers against budget with drill-down to individual transactions. Budget periods must align with each entity's fiscal year which may differ across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R012	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Workflow Notifications and Reminders	The system must send automated notifications at each stage of the waiver approval workflow to relevant stakeholders via configurable channels including email, SMS, in-app notification, and Microsoft Teams or Slack integration. Notification templates must be maintained in both Arabic and English with automatic language selection based on recipient profile preferences. Pending approval reminders must be sent at configurable intervals with escalation to delegate or supervisor after a configurable timeout period. Customers must receive notifications upon waiver request acknowledgement, approval, partial approval, or rejection with appropriate detail level excluding internal deliberation information. Bulk notification suppression must be available during force majeure events to prevent notification flooding. All notifications must include proper entity branding and comply with communication preferences registered by each recipient.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R013	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Reversal and Cancellation Processing	The system must support reversal of previously approved waivers in scenarios including erroneous approval, customer contract breach, or fraud detection. Waiver reversal must generate a debit note against the original credit note restoring the D&D charges to the customer's account. The reversal must recalculate any tax implications and generate corrected tax documents per jurisdiction requirements. Reversal requests must go through an approval workflow with a minimum approval tier one level above the original waiver approver. The system must notify the customer of the reversal with detailed explanation in their preferred language Arabic or English. Reversed waivers must be tracked separately in reporting to distinguish from standard D&D charges. The system must prevent duplicate reversals and maintain referential integrity between original waiver, credit note, reversal, and debit note records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R014	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Waiver Reporting and Regulatory Compliance	The system must generate comprehensive waiver reports meeting regulatory and internal audit requirements across all four jurisdictions. Reports must include total waivers by category, entity, and period with year-over-year comparisons. Revenue impact analysis must show waiver amounts as percentage of gross D&D revenue per entity and consolidated. Related-party waiver reporting must separately identify and detail waivers granted to group companies or affiliated entities as required by Qatar Financial Centre, UAE Federal Tax Authority transfer pricing rules, Saudi ZATCA transfer pricing documentation, and Indian Transfer Pricing regulations under Section 92 of Income Tax Act. Reports must support generation in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. All reports must be exportable in PDF with proper Arabic RTL rendering, Excel with multi-sheet entity breakdowns, and structured XML for regulatory electronic submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R015	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	Offline Waiver Request Capability	The system must support offline waiver request creation and preliminary processing for field operations staff at remote port locations or during network outages. Offline capability must allow creation of waiver requests with all required fields, attachment of supporting evidence from mobile device cameras, and local storage of draft requests. Upon connectivity restoration the system must synchronize offline waiver requests with the central system, validate all data against current business rules, and initiate the approval workflow. Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where the underlying D&D invoice was modified during the offline period. The offline module must cache relevant customer concession agreement data and approval authority matrices for reference during offline operation. Maximum offline period must be configurable per entity with default of 48 hours and data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 on the local device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-002-R016	FEAT-022-2-002	MOD-022	AI-Recommended Waiver Disposition	The system must employ an AI model to analyze each waiver request and provide a recommended disposition to the approver including suggested waiver amount or percentage, confidence score, and supporting rationale. The AI recommendation must consider factors including customer lifetime value, payment behavior score, dispute history, current market conditions for the trade lane, port congestion levels, competitor concession benchmarks where available, and the financial impact on entity profitability. The recommendation must also assess the precedent impact of the waiver decision by identifying similar customer profiles that may request comparable waivers. AI recommendations must be presented alongside the manual approval interface as advisory information and must never automatically approve or reject waiver requests. The model must be retrained quarterly using the latest twelve months of waiver decision data across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R001	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Predictive D&D Risk Scoring Model	The system must implement a machine learning model that assigns a D&D risk score from zero to one hundred to each active shipment based on multiple input features including vessel schedule reliability for the specific service loop, historical port congestion patterns at the destination port (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP), customer's historical container dwell time patterns, customs clearance complexity based on commodity HS code and origin country, weather forecast data for the port region, and current container yard utilization levels. The risk score must be updated at least every four hours as new data becomes available. The model must achieve a minimum area under ROC curve of 0.85 validated against historical data. Risk scores must be generated for all container types including standard, reefer, tank, and out-of-gauge.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R002	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Vessel Schedule Deviation Analysis	The system must continuously monitor vessel schedule data from AIS feeds, carrier EDIFACT messages, and port community systems to detect deviations from planned vessel arrival times at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. When a vessel's projected ETA deviates by more than a configurable threshold from the scheduled arrival the system must recalculate the free-time impact for all containers on that vessel and generate early warning alerts. The deviation analysis must consider the cascading impact on connecting vessel services for transhipment cargo. The system must maintain a vessel schedule reliability database tracking historical on-time performance by carrier, service loop, and port pair to improve prediction accuracy. Analysis must run continuously with alert generation latency not exceeding fifteen minutes from receipt of updated vessel position data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R003	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Port Congestion Prediction Engine	The system must predict port congestion levels at each operating port for a rolling fourteen-day forecast horizon using historical congestion patterns, scheduled vessel arrivals, current yard utilization, berth occupancy rates, gate throughput metrics, and seasonal traffic patterns including Ramadan period adjustments and major holiday impacts. The congestion prediction must output expected container dwell time multipliers compared to normal operations. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port the model must account for inter-port traffic redistribution patterns during congestion events. For JNPT the model must factor in monsoon season impacts on vessel and terminal operations from June through September. Congestion predictions must be presented on an interactive map visualization with color-coded severity levels and must feed into the D&D risk scoring model as a primary input feature.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R004	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Customs Clearance Delay Prediction	The system must predict the likelihood and expected duration of customs clearance delays for each shipment based on analysis of commodity classification, origin country risk profile, consignee's customs compliance history, documentation completeness assessment, current customs authority processing backlogs, and regulatory inspection campaign patterns. For Qatar customs the model must account for Qatar General Authority of Customs inspection protocols. For UAE operations the system must integrate with Dubai Customs and Abu Dhabi Customs risk assessment indicators. For Saudi operations ZATCA customs clearance patterns must be analyzed. For Indian operations at JNPT the model must consider ICEGATE processing patterns and DPD (Direct Port Delivery) eligibility status. Predictions must include confidence intervals and must identify specific risk factors contributing to potential delay.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R005	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Estimated D&D Cost Projection	The system must generate estimated D&D cost projections for each at-risk shipment combining the predicted delay duration with applicable tariff rates from the D&D tariff module. Cost projections must be calculated in the billing currency for the shipment with conversion to reporting currency. Projections must present three scenarios: best case based on optimistic delay resolution, most likely case based on median prediction, and worst case based on pessimistic delay estimate. Each scenario must show the projected daily cost accumulation curve with the free-time boundary clearly marked. For reefer containers the system must add estimated power supply costs to the D&D projection. Cost projections must be aggregated at customer, trade lane, and entity levels to provide portfolio-level exposure views. Projections must be recalculated whenever underlying risk factors change with changes exceeding configurable thresholds triggering updated alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R006	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Pre-Arrival DG Notification Compliance	The system must automatically trigger pre-arrival DG notifications to destination port authorities within the mandated timeframes: 24 hours before arrival for Gulf ports (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL) and 48 hours for Indian ports (INNSA) as per Indian Maritime DG Rules. The notification must include vessel particulars, complete DG manifest summary grouped by IMDG class, total DG container count, any class 1 explosives or class 7 radioactive materials flag, and emergency contact details for the vessel operator and local DG agent. The AI engine must monitor vessel tracking data and automatically adjust notification timing if ETA changes, ensuring compliance windows are maintained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R006	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Early Warning Alert Generation and Distribution	The system must generate tiered early warning alerts based on configurable risk score thresholds: Green for low risk scores below thirty, Yellow for moderate risk between thirty and sixty, Orange for high risk between sixty and eighty, and Red for critical risk above eighty. Alerts must be distributed to configurable recipient lists based on entity, port, customer tier, and risk level through multiple channels including in-app dashboard notifications, email digests configurable as real-time or batched, SMS for critical alerts, and webhook integrations for external systems. Alert content must include shipment identification details, predicted delay cause, estimated D&D cost exposure, and recommended intervention actions generated by the AI. Alerts must be available in both Arabic and English based on recipient language preference. Alert fatigue prevention must be implemented through intelligent suppression of redundant alerts for the same risk factor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R007	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	AI-Recommended Intervention Actions	For each high-risk shipment the AI system must generate specific recommended intervention actions that operations teams can execute to mitigate or prevent D&D charges. Recommendations must be contextual to the identified risk factors and may include contacting the customer to expedite documentation, pre-arranging customs clearance, coordinating with terminal operator for priority discharge, arranging alternative transport from port, requesting free-time extension from terminal or port authority, or initiating container repositioning to avoid detention. Each recommendation must include an estimated cost-benefit analysis showing the intervention cost versus projected D&D savings. Recommendations must prioritize actions by expected impact and urgency. The system must track which recommendations were accepted, rejected, or partially implemented and feed this data back into the AI model for continuous learning and improvement of future recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R008	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Customer D&D Risk Profile Dashboard	The system must provide a customer-level D&D risk profile dashboard showing each customer's current at-risk shipments, historical D&D performance metrics, predicted D&D exposure for the next thirty days, and trend analysis of D&D risk scores over time. The dashboard must segment risk by port, container type, and trade lane. A comparison feature must benchmark each customer against their peer group average within the same customer segment. The dashboard must display the customer's contractual free-time terms, concession agreements, and remaining waiver budget alongside the risk projections to provide complete context. Heat map visualization must show risk concentration across the customer's active shipment portfolio. The dashboard must support both English and Arabic with RTL layout rendering and must allow export of risk reports for sharing with customer relationship managers during account reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R009	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Machine Learning Model Training and Validation	The system must support automated periodic retraining of the D&D prediction models using updated historical data from all four operating entities. Model training must use a minimum of twenty-four months of historical container movement, D&D charge, and external factor data. Training pipelines must implement cross-validation with separate training, validation, and test datasets maintaining chronological ordering to prevent data leakage. Model performance metrics including precision, recall, F1 score, and calibration curves must be logged and compared against previous model versions before deployment. A model governance framework must require human approval before any new model version is promoted to production. A/B testing capability must support running new model versions in shadow mode against the production model before switchover. Model bias testing must verify prediction fairness across customer segments, entity jurisdictions, and container types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R010	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	External Data Source Integration for Predictions	The system must integrate with external data sources to enhance prediction accuracy including AIS vessel tracking data providers for real-time vessel position and speed, weather forecast APIs for port regions covering Arabian Gulf, Arabian Sea, and Indian Ocean, port community systems at Hamad Port (Mwani Qatar), Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (Maqta Gateway), and JNPT (PCS1x), carrier schedule reliability databases, and global trade disruption monitoring feeds. Each data source integration must include health monitoring with automatic fallback to degraded prediction mode using cached or historical data when external feeds are unavailable. Data quality scoring must assess the reliability and freshness of each external input. Integration adapters must handle different data formats including EDIFACT, XML, JSON, and CSV. Data refresh frequencies must be configurable per source with defaults appropriate to each data type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R011	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Seasonal and Event-Based Pattern Recognition	The AI model must identify and incorporate seasonal and event-based patterns that affect D&D risk across the operating region. Seasonal patterns must include Ramadan period impacts on customs processing and port operations across Qatar, UAE, and KSA, monsoon season effects on JNPT vessel and terminal operations from June through September, Eid Al Fitr and Eid Al Adha holiday period slowdowns, peak shipping seasons including pre-Ramadan import surge and Q4 year-end volume increases, and Hajj season logistics impacts on Saudi port operations. The model must detect emerging patterns from real-time data that deviate from seasonal norms such as unexpected congestion spikes or unusual customs hold patterns. Historical event impact data must be maintained and tagged for model feature engineering including past port closures, extreme weather events, and geopolitical disruptions affecting regional shipping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R012	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	D&D Exposure Portfolio Risk Dashboard	The system must provide a portfolio-level risk dashboard showing aggregate D&D exposure across all operating entities with drill-down capability by entity, port, customer segment, trade lane, and container type. The dashboard must display total predicted D&D exposure amount in each entity currency and consolidated reporting currency, number of shipments by risk tier, projected daily D&D cost accumulation for the portfolio, and comparison of predicted versus actual D&D outcomes to track model accuracy. Geographic visualization must show risk distribution on a regional map covering Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India port locations. Time-series charts must show risk trend evolution over configurable periods. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with proper chart axis orientation, number formatting per locale, and Hijri calendar date axis options. Automated daily and weekly portfolio risk summary reports must be generated and distributed to senior management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R013	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Container Dwell Time Anomaly Detection	The system must implement real-time anomaly detection for container dwell times at each operating port, identifying containers whose dwell time is deviating from predicted patterns and may be at risk of incurring unexpected D&D charges. The anomaly detection must use statistical process control methods combined with machine learning to establish dynamic baseline dwell time expectations per port, container type, customer, and commodity category. Containers exceeding their expected dwell time by more than one standard deviation must be flagged for review with escalating severity as the deviation increases. The system must distinguish between systemic anomalies affecting multiple containers such as a customs hold on a commodity category versus individual container anomalies. Anomaly alerts must include root cause hypothesis generated by the AI based on correlation with known factors such as documentation status, customs channel assignment, and transport arrangement status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R014	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Prediction Accuracy Tracking and Reporting	The system must continuously track and report the accuracy of D&D predictions by comparing predicted outcomes against actual D&D charges incurred. Accuracy metrics must be calculated and reported at multiple granularity levels including individual shipment, customer, port, trade lane, and entity. Key accuracy metrics must include mean absolute error of cost predictions in local currency, percentage of correctly classified risk tiers, lead time accuracy measuring how far in advance warnings were generated before D&D charges began, and false positive rate tracking warnings that did not result in actual D&D charges. Monthly accuracy reports must be generated per entity and consolidated with trend analysis showing improvement or degradation over time. When prediction accuracy falls below configurable thresholds per entity the system must automatically trigger model retraining workflows and notify the data science team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R015	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	What-If Scenario Simulation Engine	The system must provide a what-if scenario simulation capability allowing operations managers to model the D&D cost impact of hypothetical scenarios such as a vessel delay of N additional days, port congestion increasing by a specified percentage, customs processing time extending by N days for a specific commodity category, or a force majeure event closing a port for a defined period. Simulations must use the same prediction models and tariff data as the live system but operate on modified input parameters. Results must show the projected impact on D&D charges by customer, container, and entity with comparison against the baseline prediction. Simulations must support multi-currency output in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. Users must be able to save and share simulation scenarios for collaborative planning. The simulation engine must complete scenario calculations for up to ten thousand containers within sixty seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R016	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Predictive Model Explainability Interface	The system must provide an explainability interface for all AI predictions showing the key factors contributing to each risk score and cost projection in a format understandable to non-technical operations staff. Explanations must use SHAP values or equivalent feature importance methodology to rank the top contributing factors for each prediction. Visualizations must include waterfall charts showing how each factor increases or decreases the risk score from a baseline. Natural language explanations must be generated in both Arabic and English describing the prediction rationale in business terms such as stating that the vessel has historically arrived three days late at this port and customs clearance for this commodity typically takes five additional days during Ramadan. The explainability interface must be accessible from any prediction display in the system including dashboards, alerts, and reports. Audit logging must capture which users accessed prediction explanations for compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-003-R017	FEAT-022-2-003	MOD-022	Real-Time Prediction API for Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API endpoint allowing external systems and partner platforms to request D&D risk predictions for specific shipments or containers. The API must accept inputs including container number, BL reference, port of discharge, expected vessel arrival, commodity code, and customer identifier. Response must include the risk score, predicted delay duration, estimated D&D cost in requested currency, top risk factors, and recommended actions. API authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with entity-specific API keys and rate limiting configurable per consumer. Response time must not exceed two seconds for single container queries and thirty seconds for batch queries of up to five hundred containers. The API must support both JSON and XML response formats. API usage must be metered and logged per consumer for billing and audit purposes. Webhook callback registration must be supported for asynchronous notification when risk scores change significantly for previously queried containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R001	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Notification Template Management System	The system must provide a comprehensive notification template management interface supporting creation, editing, versioning, and approval of notification templates across all D&D lifecycle events. Templates must be maintained in paired Arabic and English versions with mandatory synchronization ensuring both language versions convey equivalent information. Each template must support dynamic variable placeholders for shipment-specific data including container numbers, BL references, vessel names, port names in both Arabic and English, dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, monetary amounts with currency-appropriate formatting, and customer-specific salutations. Template categories must include Pre-Arrival Advisory, Free-Time Commencement, Free-Time Expiry Warning, Daily Charge Accrual, Invoice Generation, Payment Reminder, Dispute Status Update, and Waiver Decision. Each entity must be able to customize templates while inheriting from a global base template structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R002	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Multi-Channel Notification Delivery Engine	The system must support notification delivery through multiple channels including email with HTML and plain text variants, SMS with Arabic and English character set support, WhatsApp Business API integration, in-app push notifications for mobile applications, and customer portal inbox messages. Channel selection must be configurable per notification type and customer preference with fallback channel routing if the primary channel delivery fails. Email notifications must support entity-specific branding with correct logos, colors, and sender addresses for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. SMS messages must comply with length limitations and support Arabic Unicode encoding. WhatsApp notifications must use pre-approved message templates per WhatsApp Business API requirements. Delivery confirmation and read receipts must be tracked where supported by the channel. Batch sending must be throttled to comply with channel-specific rate limits and anti-spam regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R003	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Pre-Arrival Free-Time Advisory Notification	The system must automatically send pre-arrival advisory notifications to consignees and their nominated customs brokers a configurable number of days before vessel expected arrival at the destination port. The advisory must include vessel name and voyage number, expected arrival date and time at the port, container details including numbers quantities and types, applicable free-time period in days per the customer's contractual terms or port tariff, estimated free-time expiry date calculated from expected discharge completion, and any special instructions for the specific port such as Hamad Port documentation requirements, Jebel Ali gate procedures, Khalifa Port customs pre-clearance options, or JNPT DPD eligibility information. The notification must be sent in the customer's preferred language with amounts shown in the applicable billing currency. The system must resend updated advisories if the vessel ETA changes by more than a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R004	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Free-Time Expiry Warning Notifications	The system must send graduated warning notifications as the container's free-time period approaches expiry. A minimum of three warning stages must be configurable: first warning at a configurable number of days before expiry defaulting to three days, second warning at one day before expiry, and final warning on the day of expiry. Each warning must clearly state the remaining free-time hours, the exact date and time of free-time expiry in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with the customer's local timezone, the applicable demurrage or detention tariff rate that will commence upon expiry in the billing currency, the estimated daily charge amount, and actionable steps the customer can take to avoid or minimize charges such as collecting the container or submitting required customs documentation. Warning escalation must increase urgency in tone and may include additional recipients such as the customer's account manager based on configurable rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R005	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Daily D&D Charge Accrual Notifications	The system must send daily notifications to customers for containers actively accruing demurrage or detention charges. The daily notification must include a summary of all containers currently incurring charges grouped by booking or BL reference, the number of days each container has been in demurrage or detention status, the daily charge rate per container in the applicable billing currency, cumulative charges to date per container and total across all containers, any applicable tariff rate escalation approaching such as tier changes from standard to penalty rates, and projected charges for the next seven days if the container status remains unchanged. Customers must be able to configure their preference for receiving individual container notifications or consolidated daily digests. The notification must include a direct link to the customer portal where detailed charge breakdowns can be viewed. Notifications must respect customer-configured quiet hours and timezone preferences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R006	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Invoice Generation and Delivery Notification	The system must automatically notify customers when D&D invoices are generated and ready for review. The notification must include a summary of the invoice including invoice number, total amount in billing currency with equivalent in customer's preferred reporting currency if different, period covered, number of containers billed, and payment due date calculated per entity-specific credit terms. A secure link or attachment must provide access to the full invoice document in PDF format. For Saudi operations the notification must include the ZATCA-compliant e-invoice XML reference and QR code. For Indian operations the GST invoice reference must be included. The notification must clearly indicate available payment methods per entity including bank transfer details with IBAN for Gulf entities and IFSC for India, online payment portal links, and any early payment discount applicability. Notifications must be sent to all designated billing contacts for the customer account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R007	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Payment Reminder and Overdue Notifications	The system must send automated payment reminder notifications following a configurable escalation schedule for unpaid D&D invoices. The reminder sequence must include a courtesy reminder before the due date, a first overdue notice on the day after the due date, and subsequent escalating reminders at configurable intervals. Each reminder level must use progressively urgent language while maintaining professional tone in both Arabic and English. The notification must show the outstanding amount in original billing currency with any applicable late payment surcharges calculated per entity-specific policies. For invoices overdue beyond a configurable threshold the system must notify the customer's account manager and credit control team in addition to the customer. The system must suppress payment reminders for invoices that have active dispute cases or pending waiver requests. Integration with the accounts receivable module must ensure reminders reflect real-time payment status to avoid sending reminders for recently paid invoices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R008	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Customer Notification Preference Management	The system must allow customers to configure their notification preferences through the customer portal including preferred language for each contact (Arabic or English), preferred notification channels per notification type, notification consolidation preferences (individual versus daily digest versus weekly summary), quiet hours during which non-critical notifications should be queued, contact-specific routing allowing different contacts within the same customer organization to receive different notification types such as operations staff receiving pre-arrival advisories while finance staff receive invoice notifications, and opt-out preferences for specific non-mandatory notification categories. Preference changes must take effect within one hour of modification. The system must enforce mandatory notification delivery for regulatory and contractual compliance notifications regardless of customer opt-out preferences. Default notification preferences must be configurable per entity to comply with local communication regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R009	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	AI-Personalized Notification Content	The system must use AI to personalize notification content based on customer behavior patterns and communication effectiveness analysis. Personalization must include adjusting notification timing based on historical open rates and response patterns per customer, emphasizing information elements that the specific customer has previously engaged with such as cost projections versus deadline dates, adapting the call-to-action based on the customer's typical response to D&D situations whether they typically pay promptly dispute or request waivers, and adjusting the detail level based on customer sophistication from simplified summaries for occasional shippers to detailed breakdowns for major freight forwarders. The AI must A/B test notification variants tracking open rates, click-through rates, and desired action completion rates per variant. Personalization must respect cultural communication norms appropriate to the entity and customer region including formal Arabic business correspondence conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R010	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Notification Delivery Tracking and Analytics	The system must comprehensively track notification delivery status and engagement metrics across all channels. Tracked metrics must include delivery status (sent, delivered, bounced, failed), open or read status where available, click-through rates on embedded links, response actions taken after notification such as portal login, payment initiation, or dispute filing, and time-to-action measuring elapsed time between notification delivery and customer response. Analytics dashboards must show notification effectiveness by template type, channel, entity, customer segment, and language. Bounce management must automatically flag invalid email addresses and phone numbers for customer data quality remediation. Delivery failure patterns must trigger automatic channel fallback attempts. Monthly notification performance reports must be generated per entity comparing effectiveness metrics across notification types and channels. All tracking must comply with data protection regulations applicable in each entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R011	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Bulk Notification Management for Port Events	The system must support bulk notification generation and delivery when port-wide events affect multiple customers simultaneously such as port closures, terminal congestion causing extended free-time grants, force majeure declarations, or system-wide tariff changes. Bulk notification creation must allow specification of the affected port, date range, impacted container categories, and notification message with merge fields for customer-specific data. Preview functionality must show sample rendered notifications in both Arabic and English before broadcast. Bulk sends must be staggered to prevent channel congestion with configurable sending rate limits. The system must generate a bulk notification delivery report showing successful deliveries, failures, and bounces. Deduplication logic must prevent a customer from receiving multiple bulk notifications for the same event when they have multiple affected shipments while still providing shipment-specific detail in a consolidated message.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R012	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Regulatory and Compliance Notification Requirements	The system must ensure all D&D notifications comply with jurisdiction-specific regulatory requirements for commercial communications. For Qatar operations notifications must comply with Qatar Central Bank communication guidelines for financial notices and CRA data protection requirements. For UAE operations notifications must meet Dubai Trade portal integration requirements and TDRA regulations for electronic communications. For Saudi Arabia notifications must comply with CITC communication regulations and include ZATCA-required reference information on tax-related notices. For Indian operations notifications must comply with TRAI regulations for commercial communications including DND registry checking for SMS and RBI guidelines for payment-related notifications. All notifications must include legally required elements such as company registration details, unsubscribe options for non-mandatory communications, and data privacy notices. The system must maintain compliance configuration per entity that is updateable without code deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R013	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Notification Scheduling and Timezone Management	The system must manage notification scheduling across multiple timezones relevant to the operating entities and their customers. Default sending timezones must be configurable per entity: AST (UTC+3) for Qatar, GST (UTC+4) for UAE, AST (UTC+3) for Saudi Arabia, and IST (UTC+5:30) for India. Customer-specific timezone preferences must override entity defaults when configured. The system must enforce configurable business hours for notification delivery with non-urgent notifications queued for delivery during the next business hours window. Timezone calculations must correctly handle daylight saving time transitions for customers in regions that observe DST noting that Gulf states do not observe DST. Scheduled notifications such as daily charge accrual summaries must be delivered at consistent local times for the recipient. The scheduling engine must handle batch processing of notifications across timezones ensuring all daily notifications are delivered within appropriate local morning hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R014	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Notification Audit Trail and Archival	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all notifications sent including the rendered notification content in the delivered language, recipient details, delivery channel, timestamp of sending and delivery, template version used, dynamic data values merged into the template, and any delivery error details. Notification audit records must be linked to the related business entities including container, booking, invoice, dispute case, or waiver request enabling complete communication history retrieval from any business context. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with the most stringent retention requirement across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. The audit trail must support search by recipient, notification type, date range, entity, and related business reference. Archived notifications must be retrievable in their original rendered format for dispute resolution evidence or regulatory inquiry responses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R015	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Escalation Notification for Internal Stakeholders	The system must generate internal escalation notifications to company stakeholders when customer D&D situations require management attention. Escalation triggers must include D&D charges exceeding configurable amount thresholds per customer tier and currency, containers dwelling beyond configurable maximum days at any operating port, customers with multiple concurrent containers in demurrage or detention status, payment defaults on D&D invoices beyond configurable overdue thresholds, and high-value customers showing deteriorating D&D performance trends. Internal escalation notifications must include customer context summary, financial exposure in both local and reporting currencies, AI-generated risk assessment, and recommended actions. Escalation routing must follow entity-specific hierarchies with configurable escalation chains. The system must track escalation acknowledgment and resolution ensuring escalated issues are not dropped. Internal notifications must support Arabic and English based on the recipient staff member's profile language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R006	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Limited and Excepted Quantities Handling	The system shall support booking and documentation of dangerous goods shipped under IMDG Code limited quantities (Chapter 3.4) and excepted quantities (Chapter 3.5) provisions. For limited quantities, the system must verify that inner packaging quantities do not exceed the limits in Column 7a of the DGL, that outer packaging gross mass does not exceed 30 kg for most goods, and that packages bear the correct limited quantity mark. For excepted quantities, the system must verify compliance with the maximum net quantity per inner and outer packaging as specified in the excepted quantities table, and must assign the correct excepted quantity code (E0 through E5). The system must differentiate limited and excepted quantity shipments from full DG shipments in booking records, as these categories have reduced segregation and documentation requirements affecting stowage planning at all operating ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R016	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Notification Testing and Simulation Environment	The system must provide a notification testing and simulation environment allowing operations and marketing teams to preview and test notification templates before deployment to production. The test environment must support rendering sample notifications with realistic test data in both Arabic and English languages verifying correct RTL layout, dynamic variable population, currency formatting per locale, date rendering in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and embedded link functionality. Channel-specific previews must show how notifications will appear in email clients, SMS on various device screens, WhatsApp message format, and in-app notification panels. Test notifications must be sendable to designated test recipients without affecting production notification metrics or customer communication logs. A/B test configuration must be testable in simulation mode before live deployment. The testing environment must validate compliance with regulatory requirements for each entity jurisdiction before allowing template promotion to production.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-022-2-004-R017	FEAT-022-2-004	MOD-022	Offline Notification Queue and Retry Management	The system must implement a robust notification queue management system that handles delivery failures and system outages gracefully. Failed notification deliveries must be automatically retried with configurable retry intervals using exponential backoff starting from five minutes up to a maximum of twenty-four hours. The queue must prioritize notifications based on business urgency with free-time expiry warnings and payment-critical notifications receiving highest priority. During system outages or channel provider downtime the queue must buffer notifications and process them in chronological order upon service restoration without duplicate delivery. Dead letter queue handling must capture permanently undeliverable notifications after maximum retry attempts for manual review and alternative delivery. Queue depth monitoring must trigger alerts when notification backlogs exceed configurable thresholds indicating potential delivery issues. The system must maintain queue state across service restarts ensuring no notifications are lost during deployment or infrastructure maintenance events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R001	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	IMDG Dangerous Goods List Database Maintenance	The system shall maintain a complete, versioned database of the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List (DGL) including all UN numbers (UN0001 through UN3550+), proper shipping names in both English and Arabic, hazard classes (1 through 9 including subdivisions such as 1.1 through 1.6, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 5.1, 5.2, 6.1, 6.2, 8, 9), packing groups (I, II, III), subsidiary risks, special provisions, limited and excepted quantities, and EmS schedules. The database must support amendment tracking so that when IMDG Code amendments are published by IMO, administrators can import updated lists while preserving historical records of prior versions used for previously booked shipments across all four operating entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R002	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	AI-Powered UN Number Classification Assistance	The system shall provide an AI-powered classification assistant that helps operations staff identify the correct UN number and proper shipping name for dangerous goods based on natural language descriptions entered in English or Arabic. The AI engine must analyze chemical composition, physical properties, and intended use descriptions to suggest the top five most likely IMDG classifications ranked by confidence score. Each suggestion must include the UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group, and any special provisions. The system must log all AI-assisted classifications with the confidence score and the operator who accepted or overrode the suggestion for audit purposes at ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R003	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Dangerous Goods Declaration Validation	The system shall validate all incoming Dangerous Goods Declarations (DGD) per IMO FAL Form 7 and IMDG Code Section 5.4 requirements. Validation must check that the UN number matches the proper shipping name, that the declared hazard class and packing group are consistent with the IMDG DGL entry, that the declared net and gross quantities do not exceed permitted limits for the specified container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20TK, 20OT), and that all mandatory fields including emergency contact, signatory details, and transport document references are populated. Validation errors must be displayed in both English and Arabic with specific IMDG Code section references. The AI engine shall auto-correct minor formatting inconsistencies while flagging substantive discrepancies for human review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R004	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Special Provisions Enforcement	The system shall enforce all IMDG Code special provisions (SP) referenced in Column 6 of the Dangerous Goods List for each declared UN number. When a special provision imposes quantity limits, packaging requirements, transport mode restrictions, or documentation obligations, the system must automatically verify compliance before allowing booking confirmation. For example, SP63 restricts certain Class 1 goods from maritime transport, SP188 limits lithium battery watt-hour ratings, and SP961 mandates competent authority approvals. The system must maintain a machine-readable special provisions registry supporting all numeric and alphabetic SP codes, and must display applicable special provisions in both English and Arabic during the booking acceptance workflow for operators at all four regional entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R005	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Packing Instruction Compliance Verification	The system shall verify that the declared packaging for each dangerous goods item complies with the applicable IMDG Code packing instruction (PI) as referenced in Columns 8 and 9 of the Dangerous Goods List. Verification must check UN packaging specification marks against the declared packing group, confirm that inner and outer packaging combinations are authorized under the applicable PI, and validate that the declared quantity per package does not exceed the maximum permitted under the packing instruction. The system must support verification of Intermediate Bulk Containers (IBCs) per IBC packing instructions and portable tanks per tank instructions (T-codes and TP provisions). Compliance results must be recorded in the shipment DG profile for regulatory inspection at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R007	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Multi-Entity Regulatory Overlay Management	The system shall support entity-specific regulatory overlays on top of the base IMDG Code requirements for each of the four operating entities: Qatar (QA entity operating at Hamad Port), UAE (AE entity operating at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali), KSA (SA entity), and India (IN entity operating at JNPT). Each entity may have additional national regulations that supplement or restrict IMDG provisions, such as Qatar Civil Defence requirements, UAE Federal Transport Authority directives, Saudi SFDA restrictions on certain chemicals, or Indian PESO (Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation) rules. The system must allow administrators to configure entity-specific rule overlays that are applied in addition to IMDG base rules during compliance checking, with clear audit logging of which overlay triggered any rejection or restriction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R008	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	IMDG Code Amendment Version Control	The system shall maintain version control for IMDG Code amendments, supporting at minimum the current effective amendment and the immediately prior amendment for transitional periods as permitted by IMO. When a new IMDG Code amendment enters force (typically January 1 of the effective year with a one-year transitional period), the system must allow administrators to activate the new amendment version while retaining the prior version for shipments booked during the transitional period. Each DG booking must record the IMDG Code amendment version under which it was validated. The system must generate transition reports listing bookings validated under the outgoing amendment that have not yet been shipped, flagging any that would fail validation under the incoming amendment so operations teams can take corrective action before the transitional period expires.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R009	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Competent Authority Approval Tracking	The system shall track and enforce competent authority approvals required for certain dangerous goods as mandated by IMDG Code provisions. For UN numbers requiring competent authority approval (e.g., new self-reactive substances under Class 4.1, certain organic peroxides under Class 5.2, or articles requiring special transport conditions), the system must maintain a registry of approval certificates including issuing authority, approval reference number, validity dates, and approved conditions of transport. The system must block booking acceptance for goods requiring competent authority approval unless a valid, non-expired approval certificate is recorded and linked to the booking. Approval expiry alerts must be generated 90, 60, and 30 days before expiration, sent to the responsible entity's DG compliance officer with notifications in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R010	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Marine Pollutant Identification and Marking	The system shall automatically identify substances classified as marine pollutants per IMDG Code Chapter 2.9 and Appendix A of the Index, and enforce the additional marking, documentation, and stowage requirements applicable to marine pollutants. When a UN number entry in the DGL includes the marine pollutant indicator (column marked PP or P), the system must flag the booking for marine pollutant marking requirements, ensure the Dangerous Goods Declaration includes the marine pollutant designation, and apply stowage category SW1 or other applicable stowage requirements. The system must also identify severe marine pollutants (PP designation) and enforce the stricter requirements including prohibition of overboard jettisoning. Marine pollutant status must be clearly indicated in all printed and electronic documentation in both English and Arabic languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R011	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	EmS Emergency Response Schedule Reference	The system shall associate each declared dangerous goods item with the correct Emergency Schedule (EmS) codes as specified in the IMDG Code Supplement EmS Guide. Each UN number entry must be linked to the applicable fire schedule (F-A through F-J) and spillage schedule (S-A through S-Z) codes. When generating shipping documents, container packing certificates, or stowage plans, the system must include the EmS codes for each DG item. The system must also maintain a complete database of EmS procedural instructions so that in an emergency scenario, port operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT can immediately retrieve the correct firefighting and spillage response procedures for any container with declared DG cargo. EmS data must be accessible in offline mode for vessel and terminal operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R012	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Multimodal Transport DG Compliance Cross-Check	The system shall perform cross-modal compliance checks for dangerous goods shipments that involve multimodal transport legs, verifying that the goods are permitted not only under IMDG Code for the sea leg but also under applicable regulations for road (ADR), rail (RID), or air (IATA DGR) transport legs in the door-to-door supply chain. When a booking includes inland transport from origin to port of loading or from port of discharge to destination, the system must flag any UN numbers or quantity limits that are permitted under IMDG but restricted under the relevant inland transport regulation applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India. This cross-check must cover at minimum the transport prohibition checks and any modal-specific quantity limits, alerting the booking operator to potential non-compliance on non-maritime legs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R013	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Fumigated Container and Unit Compliance	The system shall enforce IMDG Code requirements for fumigated cargo transport units (CTUs) classified under UN 3359 per IMDG Code Section 5.5.2. When a container is declared as fumigated, the system must capture the fumigant chemical name, date and time of fumigation, and the ventilation status. The system must verify that the required fumigation warning sign is indicated in documentation, and that the container is not loaded aboard the vessel until the safe entry period has elapsed or the container has been ventilated as per the IMDG Code requirements. The system must also enforce that fumigated CTU declarations are separate from standard DGDs and that fumigation details are communicated to the vessel master and terminal operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT for crew safety.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R014	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Dangerous Goods Compliance Audit Trail	The system shall maintain an immutable, timestamped audit trail of all DG compliance checks, validation results, overrides, and approvals for each dangerous goods booking. Every compliance check must record the rule evaluated, the input data checked, the pass/fail result, and the IMDG Code section reference. When an operator overrides a compliance warning, the system must capture the override reason, the operator's identity, the approving supervisor's identity, and the entity under which the override was performed (QA, AE, SA, or IN). Audit records must be retained for a minimum of seven years per maritime regulatory requirements and must be queryable by UN number, booking reference, vessel, voyage, port, date range, entity, and compliance status. Audit reports must render correctly in both English and Arabic with RTL layout support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R015	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Offline IMDG Compliance Check Capability	The system shall support offline IMDG compliance checking for terminal and vessel operations that may experience intermittent connectivity at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The offline module must cache the complete IMDG Dangerous Goods List, special provisions, packing instructions, and segregation tables locally on the terminal workstation or mobile device. When operating offline, the system must perform all standard compliance validations against the cached data and queue results for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The system must detect version mismatches between cached offline data and the central database upon reconnection and alert the operator if any offline compliance checks were performed against outdated IMDG data, triggering automatic revalidation of affected bookings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-001-R016	FEAT-023-1-001	MOD-023	Hazard Class Quantity Aggregation per CTU	The system shall automatically calculate and enforce aggregate quantity limits for dangerous goods packed within a single cargo transport unit (container). When multiple DG items of the same or different hazard classes are consolidated into one 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, or other container type, the system must aggregate net explosive quantities for Class 1 goods per compatibility group, total volumes or masses per hazard class for quantity-limited provisions, and verify that combined quantities do not exceed any applicable thresholds defined in IMDG Code packing provisions or special provisions. The system must also enforce the IMDG Code mixed packing provisions of Section 4.1.10, verifying that different UN numbers packed in the same outer packaging are permitted to be mixed and that any segregation requirements within the CTU are satisfied. Aggregation calculations must be logged for audit at each operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R001	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Booking Request Intake and Validation	The system shall provide a structured DG booking request intake form that captures all mandatory information required for dangerous goods screening, including shipper details, UN number(s), proper shipping name(s), hazard class(es), packing group(s), net and gross quantities, packaging type and specification marks, flashpoint (where applicable), marine pollutant status, and emergency contact information. The form must support entry in both English and Arabic with real-time field validation ensuring data completeness and format correctness. For multi-DG bookings containing more than one UN number per container, the system must capture each item separately with individual quantities and packaging details. The intake form must auto-populate known data from the shipper's profile and previous DG bookings to accelerate data entry, while still requiring explicit confirmation of all safety-critical fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R002	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	AI-Powered DG Booking Risk Scoring	The system shall apply an AI-driven risk scoring algorithm to each DG booking request, generating a composite risk score from 0 (lowest risk) to 100 (highest risk) based on multiple weighted factors including hazard class severity, quantity relative to container capacity, shipper DG compliance history, route-specific risk factors (e.g., transit through environmentally sensitive waters), port of loading and discharge DG handling capabilities, seasonal temperature considerations for thermally sensitive goods, and historical incident data for the specific UN number. The AI model must be trained on historical DG incident data and booking patterns. Bookings scoring above a configurable threshold (default 75) must be routed for mandatory manual review by a certified DG officer, while bookings below the threshold may proceed through automated acceptance at all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R003	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Port Authority DG Restriction Enforcement	The system shall maintain a configurable database of port-specific DG restrictions for each operating port including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Port restrictions may include outright prohibition of certain UN numbers or hazard classes, quantity limits per vessel call, special documentation requirements, advance notification periods (typically 24 to 72 hours before vessel arrival), and designated DG berth or storage area assignments. During booking acceptance screening, the system must verify the DG cargo against both the port of loading and port of discharge restrictions. If the booking involves transshipment, the transshipment port restrictions must also be checked. Restriction violations must generate clear rejection messages referencing the specific port authority regulation in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R004	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Vessel Class and Flag State DG Approval Verification	The system shall verify that the nominated vessel is approved to carry the declared class and quantity of dangerous goods based on the vessel's classification society certification, flag state approvals, and SOLAS Chapter II-2 compliance status. The system must maintain a vessel DG capability register recording each vessel's Document of Compliance (DOC) for DG carriage, including approved hazard classes, maximum quantities per class, approved stowage locations (on deck, under deck, or both), and any vessel-specific restrictions imposed by the classification society or flag state. During booking acceptance, the system must cross-reference the declared DG against the vessel's capability register and reject bookings that exceed the vessel's DG carriage approval. The system must alert operations when vessel DG certificates approach expiry within 60 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R005	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Shipper DG Compliance History Assessment	The system shall maintain a DG compliance history profile for each shipper, tracking metrics including the number of DG bookings submitted, DGD error rates, documentation rejection rates, misdeclaration incidents, packaging deficiency reports, and any regulatory penalties associated with the shipper's DG shipments. The AI screening engine must factor the shipper's compliance history into the booking risk score, applying elevated scrutiny to shippers with poor compliance records. Shippers with misdeclaration incidents within the past 24 months must be flagged for mandatory manual document review regardless of the overall risk score. The system must support shipper blacklisting at the entity level (QA, AE, SA, IN) where a shipper is prohibited from booking DG cargo through a specific entity due to repeated violations, while potentially remaining eligible through other entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R006	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Advance Notification to Port Authorities	The system shall automatically generate and transmit DG advance notifications to the relevant port authority within the required timeframe before vessel arrival. Notification formats must comply with each port's specific requirements: Qatar MOPH and Customs format for Hamad Port, Abu Dhabi Ports format for Khalifa Port, Dubai Maritime City Authority format for Jebel Ali, and Mumbai Port Trust and Indian Maritime Administration format for JNPT. Notifications must include complete DG manifest data including UN numbers, proper shipping names, hazard classes, quantities, container numbers, stowage positions, and EmS codes. The system must track notification acknowledgment status and alert operations if acknowledgment is not received within the expected timeframe. All notifications must be generated in the port authority's required language and format, supporting both English and Arabic for Gulf ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R007	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Amendment and Revision Control for DG Manifests	The system must support amendment workflows for DG manifests after initial submission, maintaining a full audit trail of changes including who initiated the amendment, the reason code (cargo change, error correction, additional booking, cancellation), and timestamp in the local timezone of the submitting entity. Each amendment must generate a new manifest version number and be re-submitted to the relevant port authority with a change summary highlighting added, removed, or modified DG entries. The system must enforce a cutoff rule preventing amendments after vessel departure from the port of loading, configurable per port (e.g., 2 hours before departure for Jebel Ali, 4 hours for JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R008	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Temperature-Controlled DG Screening	The system shall perform additional screening checks for dangerous goods requiring temperature control during transport, including self-reactive substances (Class 4.1 Type B through F), organic peroxides (Class 5.2 Type B through F), and other temperature-sensitive DG items with defined control and emergency temperatures in the IMDG Code. Screening must verify that the declared control temperature and emergency temperature match the IMDG Code requirements for the specific UN number, that the container is a reefer unit (20RE or 40RH) with appropriate temperature range capability, and that the temperature monitoring and alarm systems meet IMDG requirements. The system must also verify availability of power points at the declared stowage location for reefer DG containers and check transit time against the cargo's thermal stability, factoring in ambient temperatures along the voyage route through the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R009	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Booking Financial Surcharge Calculation	The system shall automatically calculate DG surcharges applicable to the booking based on the hazard class, quantity, and route, supporting multi-currency calculation in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. DG surcharge rates must be configurable per entity, carrier, hazard class, and route combination. The system must support tiered surcharge structures where rates vary based on quantity thresholds and container type (e.g., higher rates for tank containers carrying Class 3 flammable liquids). Surcharges must be itemized separately on the booking confirmation and freight invoice, clearly labeled as DG handling surcharge, DG documentation surcharge, and DG storage surcharge where applicable. Currency conversion must use the treasury-approved exchange rates from the multi-currency module. The system must apply applicable VAT or GST based on the entity's tax regime (Qatar zero-rate, UAE 5% VAT, KSA 15% VAT, India 18% GST).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R010	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Multi-DG Container Compatibility Pre-Check	The system shall perform automated compatibility pre-checks when multiple dangerous goods items are declared for packing within a single container (mixed loads). The compatibility check must evaluate IMDG Code segregation requirements between all pairs of declared UN numbers within the container, verifying that no prohibited combinations exist per the segregation table in IMDG Code Chapter 7.2. For Class 1 explosives, the system must verify compatibility group mixing rules per IMDG Code Section 7.2.6. The system must also check that the aggregate quantities of all DG items in the container do not trigger requirements for a different transport category or additional documentation. If incompatible combinations are detected, the system must clearly identify which specific pair of UN numbers conflicts and suggest splitting the cargo into separate containers, calculating the additional container and surcharge costs for the operator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R011	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Booking Approval Workflow with Escalation	The system shall implement a configurable multi-tier approval workflow for DG bookings based on risk level and hazard classification. Tier 1 (low risk, common DG classes 8 and 9 in limited quantities) may be auto-approved by the AI engine. Tier 2 (moderate risk, standard DG bookings) requires approval by a certified DG operations officer. Tier 3 (high risk, Class 1 explosives, Class 6.1 toxic substances, Class 7 radioactive materials) requires sequential approval by the DG officer, port safety manager, and entity compliance director. The workflow must enforce time-based escalation: if an approval request is not actioned within the configurable SLA (default 4 hours for Tier 2, 8 hours for Tier 3), the system must escalate to the next-level authority with notifications in both English and Arabic. Approvers must be able to approve, reject, or return with conditions via mobile interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R012	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Documentation Completeness Gate	The system shall enforce a documentation completeness gate that prevents DG booking confirmation until all required documents have been uploaded and validated. Required documents include the signed Dangerous Goods Declaration (DGD/IMO FAL Form 7), Material Safety Data Sheet (MSDS/SDS) for each UN number, Container Packing Certificate (CPC) per IMDG Code Section 5.4.2, competent authority approvals where required by special provisions, weathering certificate for certain Class 5.2 organic peroxides, and any entity-specific or port-specific additional documentation. The system must validate document format (PDF, scanned image with minimum resolution), check that document dates are current and not expired, and use AI-powered OCR to extract and cross-reference key data fields from uploaded documents against the booking declaration. Missing or invalid documents must block the booking from proceeding past the screening stage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R013	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Transshipment DG Screening for Intermediate Ports	The system shall perform comprehensive DG screening not only for the origin and destination ports but also for all intermediate transshipment ports in the routing. When a DG booking involves transshipment at one or more intermediate ports, the system must verify DG acceptance at each transshipment port, check dwell time restrictions for DG cargo at transshipment terminals, verify that the transshipment terminal has appropriate DG storage facilities for the declared hazard classes, and confirm that the connecting vessel at each transshipment point is also approved to carry the declared DG. For bookings routing through Jebel Ali as a transshipment hub to Hamad Port or JNPT, the system must apply both the Jebel Ali terminal DG restrictions and the final destination port restrictions. The screening must also factor in cumulative transit time for temperature-controlled DG to ensure the cargo remains within safe parameters throughout the multileg journey.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R014	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Booking Amendment and Cancellation Controls	The system shall enforce strict controls on amendments to confirmed DG bookings, requiring re-screening of any amendment that changes the UN number, hazard class, quantity, packaging type, or container type. Amendments that increase the DG risk profile (e.g., higher quantity, higher hazard class) must go through the full approval workflow again. Amendments that reduce risk (e.g., lower quantity) may be processed with simplified re-screening. The system must maintain a complete amendment history showing the original booking details, each amendment with timestamp and operator identity, and the re-screening results. DG booking cancellations must trigger automatic cancellation of associated port authority advance notifications with confirmation tracking. All amendment and cancellation actions must be recorded in the compliance audit trail with the responsible entity identifier (QA, AE, SA, or IN).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R015	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Seasonal and Calendar-Based DG Restrictions	The system shall support configuration and enforcement of seasonal and calendar-based DG restrictions applicable to specific entities, ports, or trade lanes. Examples include Hajj and Umrah season restrictions on certain DG classes for KSA-bound cargo, Ramadan period enhanced scrutiny requirements, summer temperature restrictions in the Arabian Gulf region where ambient temperatures may exceed 50 degrees Celsius affecting thermal stability of certain DG classes, and monsoon season restrictions for JNPT operations affecting open-top containers with DG cargo. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date ranges for restriction periods and must automatically activate and deactivate restrictions based on the configured dates. Restriction periods must be configurable per entity with advance notification to booking teams at least 30 days before a seasonal restriction takes effect, with alerts displayed in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R016	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	DG Screening Dashboard and Analytics	The system shall provide a real-time DG screening dashboard displaying key metrics including total DG bookings screened today, auto-approved versus manually reviewed counts, rejection rate by hazard class, average screening time, pending approvals by tier, and screening queue depth. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (QA, AE, SA, IN), port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), hazard class, and risk score range. The system must generate weekly and monthly analytics reports showing DG booking trends, common rejection reasons, shipper compliance patterns, and port utilization for DG capacity. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both English and Arabic with RTL layout support. The dashboard must include predictive analytics using AI to forecast DG booking volumes by hazard class for the upcoming 30-day period to assist terminal DG storage capacity planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-002-R017	FEAT-023-1-002	MOD-023	Integration with Global DG Regulatory Databases	The system shall integrate with external regulatory databases and watch lists to enhance DG booking screening. Integration sources must include the UN Consolidated Sanctions List for dual-use chemical precursors, country-specific controlled substance lists for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the Rotterdam Convention Prior Informed Consent (PIC) list for restricted chemicals, and the Stockholm Convention Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs) list. When a declared DG substance matches an entry on any watch list, the system must flag the booking for enhanced scrutiny including verification of end-use certificates, import permits at the destination country, and export licenses from the origin country. The system must update its local cache of watch list data at least weekly and log all watch list check results in the compliance audit trail for each booking screened.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R001	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	IMDG Segregation Table Implementation	The system shall implement the complete IMDG Code general segregation table from Section 7.2.4, defining the segregation requirements between all pairs of hazard classes and divisions. The table must encode the four segregation levels: 'Away from' (minimum one container space or 3 meters horizontally), 'Separated from' (minimum one container space or 6 meters horizontally), 'Separated by a complete compartment or hold from', and 'Separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment or hold from'. The system must correctly apply these segregation distances in both the horizontal and vertical dimensions for on-deck and under-deck stowage scenarios. The segregation table must be maintained as a configurable matrix to accommodate future IMDG Code amendments and must support the subsidiary risk-based segregation rules where subsidiary risks trigger additional segregation beyond the primary hazard class requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R002	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	UN Number Specific Segregation Override Rules	The system shall support UN number-specific segregation requirements that override or supplement the general hazard class-based segregation table. Certain UN numbers have specific segregation provisions specified in Column 16b of the Dangerous Goods List (e.g., SG codes) that require segregation from specific substances or groups of substances beyond what the general table mandates. The system must maintain a database of all SG segregation group codes (SG1 through SG75 and beyond) and evaluate them during stowage planning. When a UN-specific segregation rule conflicts with the general table, the more restrictive requirement must prevail. The system must display the applicable segregation rule source (general table or UN-specific) in the stowage plan validation report for operator review in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R003	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Stowage Category Assignment and Enforcement	The system shall automatically assign and enforce the correct IMDG Code stowage category for each DG container based on the stowage and handling codes in Column 16a of the Dangerous Goods List. Stowage categories range from Category A (on deck or under deck on cargo ships and passenger ships) through Category E (on deck or under deck on cargo ships only, prohibited on passenger ships). The system must enforce the stowage category restrictions during bay plan creation, preventing DG containers from being assigned to prohibited stowage locations. For vessels carrying passengers (including vessel crew above a specified number), the system must apply the more restrictive passenger vessel stowage categories. The stowage category must be clearly displayed in the container's DG profile and communicated to the vessel planner and terminal operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R004	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Special Stowage Provision (SW) Enforcement	The system shall enforce all IMDG Code special stowage provisions (SW codes) assigned to dangerous goods in Column 16a of the Dangerous Goods List. Special stowage provisions include requirements such as SW1 (protected from sources of heat), SW2 (clear of living quarters), SW3 (shaded from direct sunlight), SW4 (surface ventilation required), SW5 (mechanical ventilation required), and numerous other provisions specifying environmental conditions for safe stowage. The system must map each SW code to physical vessel characteristics and bay plan positions, ensuring that containers assigned SW provisions are placed in compliant locations. For example, containers requiring shade (SW3) must not be assigned to top-tier open deck positions in the Arabian Gulf region where solar radiation is intense. The system must alert the planner when no compliant stowage position is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R008	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	DG Manifest Approval Workflow with Multi-Entity Authorization	The system must implement a configurable approval workflow for DG manifests before submission, requiring sign-off from the DG safety officer, operations manager, and compliance officer based on entity-specific rules. For Qatar entity operations at Hamad Port, an additional approval from the Qatar Civil Defence liaison may be required for IMDG Class 1 and Class 7 cargo. The workflow must support delegation of authority, escalation timers (configurable per entity, defaulting to 2 hours), and mobile approval via push notification. All approvals must capture the approver's digital identity, timestamp, and any conditions or remarks in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R005	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Handling Code (H) Requirement Enforcement	The system shall enforce IMDG Code handling codes (H codes) that specify special handling requirements for DG containers during transport and port operations. Handling codes include H1 (keep as dry as reasonably practicable), H2 (keep as cool as reasonably practicable), H3 (during transport, temperature of the substance shall be maintained between specified limits), and other codes specifying operational handling requirements. The system must communicate applicable handling codes to terminal operations at each port in the container's routing, generating handling instruction sheets for crane operators, yard planners, and truck drivers. Handling instructions must be displayed in both English and Arabic and must be included in the terminal operating system work order for the container. The system must track handling code compliance through the terminal operation lifecycle and flag any deviations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R006	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Vessel Bay Plan DG Segregation Validation	The system shall validate the vessel bay plan for DG segregation compliance before the plan is finalized and before vessel loading operations commence. The validation engine must check every pair of DG containers in the bay plan against the applicable segregation requirements (general table, UN-specific SG codes, and class-specific rules) considering the three-dimensional positions of containers in the bay plan including bay, row, and tier coordinates. The system must calculate physical distances between DG container positions factoring in container dimensions (TEU/FEU), cell gaps, and structural boundaries (bulkheads, decks). Segregation violations must be displayed graphically on the bay plan view with color-coded severity indicators and specific descriptions of which segregation rule is violated between which pair of containers. The validation must complete within 30 seconds for vessels with up to 500 DG containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R007	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Terminal Yard DG Segregation for Storage	The system shall enforce DG segregation rules within the terminal container yard at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT for DG containers in temporary storage. Yard segregation rules must comply with both IMDG Code requirements and local port authority regulations, which may be more restrictive than IMDG for static storage. The system must maintain designated DG storage zones within each terminal yard, with zone assignments based on hazard class compatibility. When assigning yard positions for DG containers, the system must verify that the proposed position maintains required segregation distances from incompatible DG containers already in the yard and from non-DG sensitive cargo such as foodstuffs and refrigerated containers. The system must support configurable yard segregation rules per terminal, as each terminal may have different physical layouts and local authority requirements for DG storage areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R008	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Class 1 Explosives Compatibility Group Segregation	The system shall enforce the specialized segregation rules for Class 1 (Explosives) compatibility groups as defined in IMDG Code Section 7.2.6. The system must implement the Class 1 compatibility group segregation table specifying which compatibility groups (A through S, excluding I) may be stowed together and which must be segregated. Compatibility group A (primary detonating explosives) and compatibility group L (articles containing primary detonating explosives) must always be segregated from all other compatibility groups. The system must also enforce the net explosive quantity (NEQ) limits per compartment and per vessel as specified in the IMDG Code, calculating aggregate NEQ for all Class 1 containers in each hold or on-deck zone. When Class 1 explosives are booked through any operating entity, the system must verify that the vessel has specific Class 1 carriage approval from its flag state and classification society.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R009	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Class 7 Radioactive Material Stowage and Segregation	The system shall enforce the specialized stowage and segregation requirements for Class 7 (Radioactive Materials) as defined in IMDG Code Section 7.2.9 and the transport index (TI) system. The system must calculate the cumulative transport index for all Class 7 containers in any single group, ensuring the total TI does not exceed the limits specified in IMDG Code Table 7.2.9.1 for the applicable segregation group. The system must enforce minimum separation distances from persons (crew quarters, regularly occupied spaces) based on the cumulative TI and exposure duration tables. The system must also enforce segregation of Class 7 containers from undeveloped photographic film and plates. For Class 7 bookings through any of the four operating entities, the system must verify that the shipper holds a valid competent authority certificate and that the consignee has the required radioactive material import license for the destination country.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R010	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	AI-Optimized DG Stowage Position Recommendation	The system shall provide AI-powered stowage position recommendations for DG containers that optimize vessel stability, port rotation efficiency, and segregation compliance simultaneously. The AI engine must evaluate all available stowage positions and rank them based on segregation compliance with all other DG containers already planned, proximity to required ventilation or temperature control infrastructure, weight distribution impact on vessel stability, accessibility based on the container's discharge port in the rotation sequence, and distance from crew accommodation and navigation bridge. The recommendation engine must present the top three stowage positions to the planner with a compliance and efficiency score for each option. The system must learn from planner selections over time to improve future recommendations specific to each vessel and trade route operating from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R011	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Closed versus Open Container Segregation Differentiation	The system shall differentiate segregation distance requirements based on whether DG containers are stowed in closed (under deck in enclosed cargo holds) or open (on deck or in open-top holds) stowage positions, per IMDG Code Section 7.2.4.2. Segregation distances differ between closed and open freight containers, and the system must apply the correct distance matrix based on the stowage location. For on-deck stowage, the system must consider both horizontal and vertical separation using the on-deck container spacing rules. For under-deck stowage, the system must evaluate compartment boundaries (bulkheads and decks) to determine if containers are in the same compartment, adjacent compartments, or separated by an intervening compartment. The system must maintain vessel-specific compartment definitions derived from the vessel's general arrangement plan, linked to the bay plan structure for each vessel calling at the four operating ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R012	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Segregation for Goods of Class 1 Transported with Other DG	The system shall enforce the segregation requirements between Class 1 explosives and all other dangerous goods classes as specified in IMDG Code Section 7.2.6.3. Class 1 goods generally require 'Separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment or hold from' most other DG classes, which is the most stringent segregation level. The system must apply the Class 1 versus other classes segregation matrix accurately, considering the compatibility group of the Class 1 goods, as certain compatibility groups have modified segregation requirements with specific classes. The system must prevent any stowage plan from being finalized where Class 1 containers violate these segregation rules. When segregation compliance cannot be achieved for a planned vessel due to physical constraints, the system must suggest offloading one of the conflicting DG containers and rebooking it on an alternative vessel service.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R013	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	DG Stowage and Segregation Exception Management	The system shall support documented exceptions to standard segregation and stowage rules where permitted by the IMDG Code or authorized by the competent authority. The IMDG Code permits certain exemptions for specific UN number pairs based on scientific evidence that the combination does not create additional hazard. The system must maintain an exception register that records the UN number pair, the exemption basis (IMDG Code reference or competent authority approval number), the approving authority, the validity period, and the conditions under which the exception applies. When an exception is applied during stowage planning, the system must log the exception usage with full traceability. Only designated DG compliance managers may add or modify entries in the exception register, with all changes recorded in the audit trail. Exception registers may differ between operating entities based on differing national competent authority rulings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R014	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Real-Time Segregation Conflict Detection During Loading	The system shall perform real-time segregation conflict detection during vessel loading operations, rechecking segregation compliance as each DG container is physically loaded onto the vessel. If the actual loading sequence deviates from the planned bay plan (e.g., a container is placed in a different position due to operational constraints), the system must immediately recalculate segregation compliance for the modified configuration and alert the terminal operations team if a violation is created. Alerts must be displayed on the terminal operating system interface, the vessel planner's dashboard, and as push notifications to the DG safety officer's mobile device. The alert must clearly identify which containers are in conflict, the required segregation distance, the actual distance, and the remediation options. This real-time checking must function with low latency (under 5 seconds) at all four operating terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R015	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	DG Stowage Plan Documentation and Reporting	The system shall generate comprehensive DG stowage plan documentation including the Dangerous Goods Manifest (required by SOLAS Chapter VII and IMDG Code Section 5.4.3), vessel DG stowage plan showing the physical locations of all DG containers color-coded by hazard class, and a segregation compliance summary certifying that all segregation requirements have been verified. The documentation must be generated in formats compliant with port authority requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The DG manifest must include all mandatory data fields: booking reference, container number, UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group, quantity, stowage position, and EmS codes. Documents must be available in both English and Arabic, with the Arabic version supporting RTL layout. The system must support electronic transmission of DG manifests to port authorities and vessel masters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R016	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Multi-Port Rotation Segregation Planning	The system shall perform segregation planning across the full vessel port rotation, not just for a single port call. When planning DG stowage for a vessel calling at multiple ports (e.g., JNPT to Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port to Hamad Port), the system must consider the cumulative effect of loading DG containers at each port and discharging them at subsequent ports. The system must verify that at every point in the rotation, the remaining DG containers maintain segregation compliance after containers are discharged at intermediate ports. The planner must be able to view the segregation compliance status at each port call in the rotation. The system must flag scenarios where loading a DG container at one port would create a segregation violation with containers destined for a later discharge port, requiring either stowage adjustment or cargo deferral to a subsequent vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R017	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Segregation Rule Visualization and Interactive Planning	The system shall provide an interactive graphical interface for DG segregation planning, displaying the vessel cross-section and bay plan with DG containers color-coded by hazard class. When a planner selects a DG container for placement, the system must visually highlight all positions on the bay plan where the container may be legally stowed (green), positions that would create a segregation conflict (red), and positions with marginal compliance requiring additional review (amber). The visualization must show segregation distance circles or zones around existing DG containers indicating the exclusion areas for each incompatible hazard class. The interface must support zoom, pan, and layer toggles for on-deck versus under-deck views. All labels and legends must render in the user's selected language (English or Arabic with RTL support) and the interface must perform smoothly with up to 1000 DG containers displayed simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-003-R018	FEAT-023-1-003	MOD-023	Segregation Compliance Certificate Generation	The system shall generate a formal Segregation Compliance Certificate for each vessel departure that certifies all DG containers have been stowed in compliance with IMDG Code segregation requirements. The certificate must list each DG container with its stowage position, the nearest incompatible DG container and the actual segregation distance versus the required minimum, and the segregation rule reference. The certificate must be digitally signed by the designated DG officer responsible for the vessel at the loading port. The certificate must be generated in both English and Arabic and must be available for inspection by port state control officers at the loading port and at subsequent ports of call. The system must archive all segregation compliance certificates for a minimum of five years and link them to the vessel, voyage, and booking records for retrieval during audits or incident investigations at any of the four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R001	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	IMDG Hazard Label Database and Specification	The system shall maintain a complete database of all IMDG Code hazard labels as specified in Section 5.2.2, including the nine primary hazard class labels (Classes 1 through 9 with all subdivisions), subsidiary risk labels, and special labels such as the marine pollutant mark, elevated temperature mark, and environmentally hazardous substance mark. Each label entry must store the label model number, dimensions (minimum 100mm x 100mm for packages, 250mm x 250mm for placards), color specifications (including exact color codes for the background and symbol), orientation requirements, and the conditions under which each label is required. The database must include high-resolution vector images of each label suitable for printing and digital display. Label names and descriptions must be stored in both English and Arabic to support bilingual documentation and operator interfaces across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R002	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Automatic Label Determination per UN Number	The system shall automatically determine the required hazard labels for each dangerous goods item based on the UN number entry in the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List. The system must assign the primary hazard label based on the hazard class in Column 3 of the DGL, assign subsidiary risk labels based on the subsidiary risk(s) in Column 4, and add any additional labels or marks required by special provisions in Column 6 or stowage and handling codes in Column 16. For example, UN 1203 (Gasoline) requires a Class 3 Flammable Liquid label as primary, while UN 2813 (Water-reactive solid) requires a Class 4.3 primary label and potentially a Class 8 subsidiary risk label. The system must display the complete label set required for each package and each cargo transport unit, supporting both the package-level labeling requirements of Chapter 5.2 and the CTU-level placarding requirements of Chapter 5.3.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R003	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Container Placard Position and Orientation Rules	The system shall enforce the IMDG Code requirements for placard placement on cargo transport units as specified in Section 5.3.1. Placards must be affixed on each side and each end of the container (four placards minimum per container), positioned so they are visible and not obscured by container fittings or other markings. The system must generate a visual placard placement diagram for each DG container showing the required placard type and position on the container's four faces. For containers carrying multiple hazard classes, the system must determine the correct combination of placards per IMDG Code rules including the requirement for subsidiary risk placards and the marine pollutant mark. The system must validate that container placard requirements are communicated to the packing station or CFS (container freight station) prior to container packing at facilities serving Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R004	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	UN Number Marking Requirements for CTUs	The system shall enforce the IMDG Code requirement for displaying the UN number on orange rectangular panels on cargo transport units as specified in Section 5.3.2. Each DG cargo transport unit must display the UN number (preceded by the letters 'UN') in black digits at least 65mm high on an orange background panel, on each side and each end of the unit adjacent to the corresponding placard. For containers with multiple UN numbers of the same hazard class, the system must determine whether individual UN number panels are required or whether a single placard without UN number marking is acceptable per IMDG Code provisions. The system must generate marking specifications including panel dimensions, digit height requirements, and positioning relative to the placard. For tank containers carrying single substances, the system must enforce the additional requirement for UN number marking on the tank shell.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R005	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Package Marking Requirements Enforcement	The system shall enforce IMDG Code package marking requirements as specified in Section 5.2.1, ensuring that all DG packages bear the correct UN number (preceded by 'UN'), proper shipping name, and the shipper and consignee name and address. The system must validate that the declared packaging bears the correct UN packaging specification mark indicating the packaging type, performance standard, and packing group approval. For inner packagings within combination packagings, the system must verify that marking requirements for inner packaging are communicated to the shipper. The system must generate package marking specifications for each DG booking including the exact text required on the package, the minimum marking size requirements, and the durability requirements (markings must withstand 3 months immersion in sea water). Marking specifications must be produced in both English and Arabic for Gulf region shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R006	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Limited Quantity and Excepted Quantity Marking	The system shall enforce the distinct marking requirements for dangerous goods shipped under limited quantity (Chapter 3.4) and excepted quantity (Chapter 3.5) provisions. For limited quantities, the system must verify that packages bear the limited quantity mark (diamond shape with upper and lower black portions) per Section 3.4.7, and that CTUs containing limited quantity packages bear the enlarged limited quantity placard per Section 3.4.8 if they contain packages destined for multiple consignees. For excepted quantities, the system must verify that packages bear the excepted quantity mark per Section 3.5.4 showing the applicable hazard class and shipper/consignee details. The system must track these distinct marking categories separately from full DG markings and ensure that documentation correctly reflects the limited or excepted quantity status throughout the transport chain across all four operating entities (QA, AE, SA, IN).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R007	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Marine Pollutant Mark Application Rules	The system shall enforce the marine pollutant marking requirements as specified in IMDG Code Section 5.2.1.6 for packages and Section 5.3.3 for cargo transport units. Packages containing marine pollutants must bear the marine pollutant mark (triangle with fish and tree symbol) adjacent to the UN number marking. Cargo transport units containing marine pollutant packages must bear the enlarged marine pollutant mark on all four sides. The system must automatically identify bookings requiring marine pollutant marking based on the marine pollutant indicator in the Dangerous Goods List and from the AI classification engine's analysis of the substance properties. For bulk containers and tank containers carrying marine pollutant substances, the system must enforce the additional marking requirements specified in IMDG Code Chapter 5.3. The system must include marine pollutant mark requirements in all packing instruction documents and placard specifications generated for terminal operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R008	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Elevated Temperature and Fumigation Warning Signs	The system shall enforce marking requirements for elevated temperature substances per IMDG Code Section 5.3.3 and fumigated cargo transport units per Section 5.5.2. For substances transported at or above 100 degrees Celsius in liquid state or 240 degrees Celsius in solid state, the system must require the elevated temperature mark (thermometer symbol in red triangle) on all four sides of the cargo transport unit. For fumigated containers (UN 3359), the system must require the fumigation warning sign per Section 5.5.2.3 showing the fumigant name, date and time of fumigation, and warning text. The system must generate fumigation warning sign templates pre-populated with booking data, ready for printing in both English and Arabic. The system must verify that fumigation signs are correctly declared in documentation and that terminal operations at all four ports are notified of fumigated containers to ensure worker safety during handling and inspection operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R009	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Orientation Arrows for Liquid DG Packages	The system shall enforce the requirement for orientation arrows on combination packagings containing inner packagings of liquids and on single packagings fitted with vents as specified in IMDG Code Section 5.2.1.8. Orientation arrows (two black arrows pointing upward on white or contrasting background) must be placed on two opposite sides of the package to indicate the correct upright position. The system must identify bookings requiring orientation arrows based on the physical state of the dangerous goods (liquid) and the packaging configuration (combination packaging with inner liquid containers). The system must include orientation arrow requirements in packing instruction documents generated for shippers and CFS operations. For packages containing both liquid DG and solid DG in combination packagings, the system must verify that orientation arrow placement accounts for the liquid inner packagings. Exemptions from orientation arrow requirements for certain packaging types must be enforced per IMDG provisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R010	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Overpack Marking and Label Transfer Requirements	The system shall enforce the IMDG Code overpack marking requirements as specified in Section 5.1.2. When packages are assembled into an overpack (such as a pallet wrapped in shrink film), the system must ensure that the word 'OVERPACK' is marked on the outside in letters at least 65mm high, along with the UN number and proper shipping name unless the inner package markings are visible through the overpack. If inner package labels are not visible through the overpack, the overpack must bear labels representative of all inner packages. The system must track overpacking status in the booking and packing records, ensuring that label and marking transfer requirements are communicated to the packing station. For overpacks containing packages from multiple shippers, the system must verify that all shippers' DGD information is consolidated and that the overpack labeling reflects the combined hazard profile. These requirements apply to all packing operations across the four entity operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R011	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Placard and Label Compliance Inspection Checklist	The system shall generate digital inspection checklists for verifying placard and label compliance at container gate-in at terminals. The checklist must be specific to each DG container, listing every required placard, label, mark, and sign with their specified positions on the container or package. Terminal gate inspectors must be able to confirm or reject each item on the checklist using a mobile device interface available in both English and Arabic. When a placard or label deficiency is identified during gate-in inspection, the system must block the container from entering the terminal DG storage area until the deficiency is rectified. The checklist must support photo capture functionality allowing inspectors to photograph placards and labels as evidence of compliance or non-compliance. Completed inspection checklists must be stored in the container's DG compliance record with inspector identity, timestamp, and entity reference for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R012	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	AI-Powered Placard Recognition from Gate Camera	The system shall integrate with terminal gate camera systems to perform AI-powered optical recognition of DG placards and labels on containers entering the terminal at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The AI vision model must identify the hazard class from placard colors and symbols, read the UN number from orange panels, detect the presence of marine pollutant marks, and verify that placards are affixed on visible container faces. Recognition results must be cross-referenced against the container's DG booking declaration to detect mismatches such as missing placards, incorrect hazard class placards, or undeclared DG containers bearing placards. Detection confidence scores must be generated for each recognition, with low-confidence results flagged for manual inspection. The system must operate in real-time at gate processing speeds of at least 25 containers per hour per lane, supporting day and night conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R013	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Label and Placard Print Generation Service	The system shall provide a label and placard print generation service that produces correctly formatted, regulation-compliant hazard labels, placards, UN number panels, and marking templates ready for printing. Print outputs must conform to IMDG Code dimension, color, and symbol specifications. The service must support multiple print formats including adhesive labels for packages, weather-resistant placards for container affixing, and paper documentation inserts. Print specifications must include exact dimensions (100mm x 100mm for labels, 250mm x 250mm for placards), CMYK color codes for print production, and scalable vector formats for different print sizes. The service must generate print-ready files in PDF and SVG formats. For operations at all four entities, the service must support both English and Arabic text elements on labels and marks, with Arabic text conforming to RTL layout rules. The system must maintain a print job log tracking which labels were printed, for which booking, and by whom.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R014	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Placard Removal Tracking After DG Cargo Discharge	The system shall track placard and label removal requirements after DG cargo has been discharged from a container. Per IMDG Code Section 5.3.1.1.6, placards must be removed or covered when the container no longer contains dangerous goods. The system must generate placard removal work orders when a DG container is unstuffed at the destination terminal or CFS, tracking that removal is completed before the container is released for non-DG use. If a container that previously carried DG is returned to the container pool without placard removal confirmation, the system must flag the container as requiring inspection before reuse. The system must also enforce placarding requirements for empty uncleaned containers and residue containers per IMDG provisions, ensuring that containers that previously held DG and have not been cleaned still bear the appropriate residue hazard markings. This tracking applies at all terminal operations across the four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R015	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Subsidiary Risk Label and Placard Requirements	The system shall correctly determine and enforce subsidiary risk label and placard requirements for dangerous goods with multiple hazards. When a substance has one or more subsidiary risks listed in Column 4 of the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List, the system must require the corresponding subsidiary risk labels on packages in addition to the primary hazard label, and subsidiary risk placards on the cargo transport unit in addition to the primary hazard class placard. Subsidiary risk labels must be placed adjacent to the primary label. The system must handle substances with up to two subsidiary risks, ensuring all three labels (one primary plus two subsidiary) are listed in the marking and labeling requirements. The system must also address the special case where a substance's subsidiary risk is the same as another DG item's primary risk in the same container, affecting the segregation and placarding combination rules. All subsidiary risk labeling requirements must be clearly documented in operator-facing packing instructions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R016	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Class-Specific Label Design Variations	The system shall correctly manage the label design variations within hazard classes where different divisions require distinct label designs. For Class 1 (Explosives), the system must select the correct label variant based on division (1.1 through 1.6) and compatibility group, each having specific symbol and numbering requirements. For Class 2 (Gases), the system must differentiate between the flammable gas (2.1, red), non-flammable non-toxic gas (2.2, green), and toxic gas (2.3, white) label designs. For Class 5, the system must distinguish between oxidizing substances (5.1, yellow) and organic peroxides (5.2, split red-yellow). The system must maintain the complete set of label design variants and select the correct one based on the specific division and hazard characteristics declared in the booking. The design database must be kept current with any label design changes introduced in IMDG Code amendments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-1-004-R017	FEAT-023-1-004	MOD-023	Placard and Label Regulatory Reporting	The system shall generate regulatory reports on placard and label compliance across all operating entities for submission to port authorities and internal audit teams. Reports must include statistics on total DG containers processed per period, placard inspection pass/fail rates per terminal, common placard deficiency types (missing placard, wrong class, damaged or illegible, incorrect position), AI camera detection accuracy metrics, and time-to-rectification for identified deficiencies. Reports must be filterable by entity (QA, AE, SA, IN), port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA), hazard class, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar), and shipper. The system must support scheduled automatic report generation (daily, weekly, monthly) with distribution to configured recipients via email. Report formats must include PDF for official submissions and CSV/Excel for data analysis. All reports must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R001	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Automated DG Manifest Generation from Booking Data	The system must automatically generate a Dangerous Goods manifest by extracting DG cargo details from confirmed bookings, including UN numbers, proper shipping names, IMDG class and division, packing group, flash point, net and gross weights, and container numbers. The AI engine must cross-reference booking data against the IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List to validate completeness and accuracy. Manifests must be generated per vessel voyage, per port of loading, supporting multi-entity operations where Qatar entity (QAR), UAE entity (AED), KSA entity (SAR), and India entity (INR) bookings may appear on the same vessel. The system must handle both FCL and LCL consolidation scenarios for DG cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R002	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Multi-Port Authority Submission Format Support	The system must support electronic DG manifest submission in the specific formats required by each port authority: Mwani Qatar format for Hamad Port (QAHAM), Abu Dhabi Ports VASPP format for Khalifa Port (AEKHL), DP World ZODIAC format for Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT PCS (Port Community System) format for Indian ports (INNSA). Each submission must include the port-specific header fields, vessel IMO number, voyage reference, ETA/ETD in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats where required by Gulf port authorities, and digital signatures or authentication tokens as mandated by the respective port authority systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R003	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	DG Manifest Validation Engine with IMDG Cross-Check	The system must provide an AI-powered validation engine that checks every line item on the DG manifest against IMDG Code Chapter 3.2 Dangerous Goods List, verifying that the UN number matches the proper shipping name, the packing group is correct for the substance, the IMDG class and subsidiary risks are accurately declared, and the EmS (Emergency Schedule) codes are populated. The validation must flag incompatible DG segregation within the same container or adjacent containers per IMDG Code Chapter 7.2 segregation table rules. Validation errors must be displayed in both English and Arabic with severity levels (critical, warning, informational).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R004	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Container-Level DG Stowage Position Mapping	The system must map each DG container to its stowage position on the vessel bay plan and validate that the stowage location complies with IMDG Code stowage and segregation requirements. This includes checking stowage category (on deck, under deck, or both), distance from accommodation and machinery spaces, segregation from incompatible DG classes per the segregation table in IMDG Code 7.2.4, and whether the container is positioned away from heat sources. The manifest must include the bay-row-tier position for each DG container, and any violations must be flagged with recommended alternative positions before submission to port authorities at QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, or INNSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R005	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Multimodal DG Transport Document Chain	The system must maintain a complete document chain for multimodal DG shipments covering sea-road-rail legs, linking the DG manifest to the DG declaration form (IMO FAL Form 7), the Multimodal Dangerous Goods Form, the container packing certificate, and the bill of lading. For shipments transiting through UAE free zones or KSA bonded warehouses, the system must generate additional customs DG declarations in the format required by UAE Federal Customs Authority or Saudi ZATCA. All documents must be stored with version history, support bilingual Arabic/English content, and be retrievable by booking reference, container number, or UN number across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R009	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Automated Segregation Conflict Detection on Manifest	The system must automatically detect segregation conflicts within the DG manifest by analyzing all declared DG cargo against the IMDG Code segregation groups and the segregation table in Chapter 7.2.4. When two or more containers carry incompatible DG classes (e.g., Class 3 flammable liquids and Class 5.1 oxidizing substances requiring 'separated from' distance), the system must flag the conflict, calculate the minimum required separation distance in TEU positions, and suggest re-stowage options. The conflict detection must run in real-time as new DG bookings are added to the manifest and must account for the specific vessel cell guide configuration and container size (20ft/40ft/45ft).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R010	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Submission Status Tracking and Port Authority Response Handling	The system must track the submission status of each DG manifest through its lifecycle: draft, pending approval, submitted, acknowledged, accepted, rejected, or amendment requested. When a port authority responds with a rejection or amendment request, the system must parse the response, map rejection codes to specific manifest line items, and route the issue to the responsible operations team with AI-generated remediation suggestions. For Jebel Ali (AEJEA) and Khalifa Port (AEKHL), the system must integrate with the port's API for real-time status updates. For JNPT (INNSA), the system must poll the PCS portal at configurable intervals and extract status from response messages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R011	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Offline DG Manifest Preparation with Sync Capability	The system must support offline DG manifest preparation for operations personnel working in areas with limited connectivity, such as remote port terminals or vessel-side inspections. The offline module must cache the current IMDG Code Dangerous Goods List, vessel bay plan, and active bookings for local validation. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline-prepared manifests with the central database, detect and resolve conflicts where the same manifest was modified by multiple users, and queue submissions to port authorities. The offline mode must support Arabic RTL interface rendering and maintain all validation rules locally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R012	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	DG Manifest Reporting and Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard for DG manifest operations, displaying metrics such as total DG containers per period, breakdown by IMDG class, submission acceptance rate per port authority, average time from manifest creation to acceptance, and top rejection reasons. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), vessel, voyage, and date range using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Currency-related metrics for DG surcharges must display in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent. All dashboard labels and data must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R013	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Integration with Vessel EDI and Carrier DG Systems	The system must integrate with major container shipping lines' DG declaration systems via INTTRA, EDIFACT IFTDGN message type, and carrier-specific APIs (e.g., Maersk BAPLIE, MSC DG portal, CMA CGM eSolutions). The integration must support both outbound transmission of DG manifest data from the ERP and inbound receipt of carrier-confirmed DG stowage positions. The system must map internal DG data fields to each carrier's specific format requirements, handle acknowledgment messages (APERAK/CONTRL), and log all EDI transmissions with timestamps for audit purposes. Failed transmissions must trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff and alert the operations team after three failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R014	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Regulatory Update Tracking for DG Manifest Requirements	The system must track and notify operations teams of changes to DG manifest requirements from port authorities and regulatory bodies across all four jurisdictions. This includes updates to IMDG Code amendments (biennial cycle), Qatar MOTC circulars, UAE FTA DG regulations, Saudi SFDA chemical import rules, and Indian DG Shipping Directorate notices. The AI engine must analyze regulatory updates, identify impacted manifest fields or submission procedures, and generate impact assessments with recommended system configuration changes. Notifications must be delivered in both English and Arabic, with priority flags for changes effective within 30 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R015	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	DG Manifest Archival and Regulatory Retention Compliance	The system must archive all submitted DG manifests, including all versions, approval records, submission confirmations, and port authority responses, for the retention period mandated by each jurisdiction: 5 years for Qatar (MOTC requirement), 7 years for UAE (Federal Law No. 8), 10 years for KSA (ZATCA retention rules), and 8 years for India (DG Shipping rules). Archived manifests must be searchable by vessel, voyage, port, UN number, IMDG class, date range, and container number. The archive must support legal hold functionality to prevent deletion during regulatory investigations and must generate tamper-evident checksums for each archived document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-001-R016	FEAT-023-2-001	MOD-023	Bulk DG Manifest Operations for Multi-Vessel Scenarios	The system must support bulk operations for DG manifest management when multiple vessels are loading simultaneously at the same port. Operations personnel must be able to view a consolidated DG cargo pool across all active vessels, allocate DG containers to specific vessels based on segregation compatibility and sailing schedule, and generate manifests for multiple vessels in a single workflow. The bulk operation interface must display real-time capacity utilization for DG slots per vessel, highlight cross-vessel segregation opportunities (e.g., moving incompatible cargo to different sailings), and support drag-and-drop re-allocation with automatic manifest regeneration. This feature must handle concurrent access by multiple users across different entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R001	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Digital MFAG Database with Real-Time Lookup	The system must maintain a comprehensive digital database of the Medical First Aid Guide (MFAG) as published by IMO, covering all MFAG table numbers and their associated treatments for DG exposure incidents. The lookup must support search by UN number, proper shipping name, IMDG class, or MFAG table number, returning treatment protocols within 2 seconds. The database must be kept current with the latest IMDG Code amendment cycle and display all medical procedures in both English and Arabic. Each MFAG entry must include symptoms, immediate first aid measures, antidotes where applicable, and instructions for contacting the vessel's P&I club medical advisory service.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R003	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Port-Specific Emergency Response Plan Integration	The system must integrate with port-specific emergency response plans for each operational port: Hamad Port fire and hazmat response protocols (Qatar Civil Defence coordination), Jebel Ali Emergency Response Center procedures (Dubai Civil Defence), Khalifa Port HAZMAT team protocols (Abu Dhabi Civil Defence), and JNPT Disaster Management Plan (Indian Coast Guard coordination). The integration must include port emergency contact directories, assembly point locations, evacuation route maps, and nearest medical facility details. Each port's emergency plan must be stored with version control and reviewed quarterly. The system must display the appropriate port plan automatically based on the user's current operational location or selected port context.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R004	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Real-Time DG Cargo Locator for Emergency Responders	The system must provide a real-time DG cargo locator that enables emergency responders to instantly identify the location, contents, and hazard profile of all DG containers at a given port terminal or vessel. The locator must display an interactive map showing container positions within the terminal yard or vessel bay plan, color-coded by IMDG class, with drill-down capability to view UN number, proper shipping name, quantity, packing group, and special provisions. For Jebel Ali and JNPT where terminal operating systems provide live container position feeds, the data must update within 5 minutes. The interface must support touch-screen operation for emergency tablet devices and render correctly in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R005	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Automated Emergency Alert Notification System	The system must provide an automated emergency alert notification system that broadcasts DG incident alerts to relevant stakeholders based on configurable escalation matrices per port and entity. Alert recipients must include internal operations staff, port authority emergency desks, shipping line DG desks, local civil defence authorities, and designated emergency response contractors. Notifications must be sent via multiple channels simultaneously: SMS, email, push notification, and WhatsApp Business API. Each alert must include incident location, DG class involved, UN number, recommended EmS procedures, wind direction data (if available from port weather station), and evacuation zone radius. Alerts must be generated in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R006	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	DG Compatibility Assessment for Adjacent Cargo During Emergencies	The system must instantly assess DG compatibility risks for cargo adjacent to an incident location by analyzing the IMDG segregation table and the actual cargo manifest. When an emergency is reported for a specific container or terminal area, the system must identify all DG containers within a configurable radius (default 50 meters for terminal, 2 bay positions for vessel), evaluate chain-reaction risks based on the combination of DG classes present, and recommend priority containers for relocation or additional monitoring. The assessment must consider environmental factors such as ambient temperature at Gulf ports (which can exceed 50°C in summer), proximity to fuel storage, and drainage patterns for liquid spill scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R007	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Personal Protective Equipment Guidance per IMDG Class	The system must provide detailed PPE (Personal Protective Equipment) guidance for emergency responders based on the IMDG class and specific substance involved in an incident. The guidance must specify minimum PPE levels (Level A through D), respiratory protection type (SCBA, air-purifying respirator, or dust mask), chemical-resistant suit material compatibility, glove type, and eye/face protection requirements. For each port location, the system must maintain an inventory cross-reference showing available PPE at the port's emergency stores, highlighting any shortfalls. The PPE guidance must be accessible offline on mobile devices and display clearly on small screens with visual icons and color coding. Arabic translations must use standardized Gulf region safety terminology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R008	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Emergency Response Drill Scheduling and Tracking	The system must manage the scheduling, execution, and evaluation of DG emergency response drills at each port as required by ISPS Code, port authority regulations, and company safety management system. Drill schedules must account for port-specific requirements: quarterly drills at Hamad Port per Qatar MOTC directive, semi-annual drills at Jebel Ali per DP World safety protocol, and annual comprehensive exercises at JNPT per Indian Merchant Shipping Act. The system must generate drill scenarios based on the most common DG classes handled at each port, assign participant roles, capture drill performance metrics (response time, procedure adherence, communication effectiveness), and generate post-drill reports with improvement recommendations. Drill records must be maintained for port state control inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R009	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Hazard Zone Calculation and Visualization	The system must calculate and visualize hazard zones for DG incidents based on the substance properties, quantity, release scenario (fire, explosion, toxic cloud, liquid spill), and local meteorological conditions. The calculation engine must implement recognized dispersion models for toxic gas releases and thermal radiation models for fire scenarios, producing exclusion zones, evacuation zones, and shelter-in-place zones. The visualization must overlay on the port terminal map, showing affected areas with color-coded severity bands. For Gulf ports (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL) the model must account for high ambient temperatures and prevailing wind patterns. The output must be shareable as a static image for distribution to emergency services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R010	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Emergency Communication Log and Timeline Tracking	The system must maintain a real-time emergency communication log that records all actions, communications, and decisions during a DG incident response. Each log entry must capture timestamp (in local port timezone), author, communication type (radio, phone, email, in-person), recipient, message content, and any decisions made. The log must support concurrent entries from multiple users at different locations and consolidate into a single chronological timeline. The timeline must distinguish between operational actions (e.g., container moved, fire suppressed), communications (e.g., port authority notified, ambulance requested), and status updates (e.g., wind shift, secondary leak detected). The log must be exportable in PDF format for post-incident investigation and regulatory reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R011	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Offline Emergency Response Access on Mobile Devices	The system must provide full offline access to emergency response procedures on mobile devices (tablets and smartphones) for use in areas with limited connectivity at port terminals or onboard vessels. The offline package must include the complete MFAG database, all EmS schedules, port-specific emergency plans, DG cargo manifests for the current vessel or terminal area, PPE guidance, and hazard zone calculation capabilities. The offline data must synchronize automatically when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution for any concurrent updates. The mobile interface must support both portrait and landscape orientations, Arabic RTL rendering, and high-contrast mode for outdoor visibility in bright Gulf sunlight conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R012	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Regulatory Authority Notification Compliance for DG Incidents	The system must generate and submit mandatory regulatory notifications for DG incidents to the relevant authorities within prescribed timeframes: Qatar MOTC and Qatar Civil Defence within 1 hour, UAE National Emergency Crisis and Disasters Management Authority (NCEMA) within 2 hours, Saudi Transport General Authority (TGA) within 2 hours, and Indian DG Shipping Directorate within 4 hours. Each notification must follow the authority-specific format, including incident classification (near miss, minor, major, catastrophic), DG details, environmental impact assessment, casualties if any, and initial response actions taken. The system must track notification acknowledgment and any follow-up requests from authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R013	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Emergency Response Resource Coordination Dashboard	The system must provide a coordination dashboard for managing emergency response resources during a DG incident, displaying real-time status of available fire tenders, hazmat vehicles, ambulances, tugs, oil spill response boats, and specialized DG response teams at each port. The dashboard must integrate with port operations systems to show vessel movements that may need to be halted or redirected, terminal areas that require evacuation, and road access routes for emergency vehicles. Resource allocation must be tracked with assignment status (available, dispatched, on-scene, returning), and the dashboard must support multi-agency coordination between port authority, civil defence, coast guard, and environmental protection agency. All interface elements must display in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R014	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Post-Emergency Decontamination and Clearance Workflow	The system must manage the post-emergency decontamination and clearance workflow for DG incident sites, tracking decontamination activities, environmental sampling results, air quality monitoring readings, and safety officer clearance sign-offs before normal operations resume. The workflow must enforce sequential steps: initial hazard assessment, decontamination execution, verification sampling, independent safety review, and formal clearance. Each step must capture responsible person, timestamp, test results (with threshold comparisons), and photographic evidence. For incidents involving marine pollution, the system must track compliance with MARPOL notification requirements. Clearance records must be archived per the retention requirements of each entity jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R015	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	AI-Powered Emergency Response Recommendation Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered recommendation engine that analyzes the specific DG incident parameters (substance, quantity, release type, location, weather, surrounding cargo) and generates prioritized response recommendations. The AI must consider historical incident data from the system's database, cross-reference with IMO casualty investigation reports, and factor in port-specific infrastructure and resource availability. Recommendations must include immediate actions (first 15 minutes), short-term containment measures (1-4 hours), and recovery planning steps. The engine must adapt recommendations in real-time as incident conditions change and must present recommendations in both English and Arabic with confidence scores indicating the reliability of each recommendation based on available data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R016	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Emergency Response Training Records and Competency Tracking	The system must maintain comprehensive training records for all personnel involved in DG emergency response across all four entity jurisdictions. Training records must track IMDG Code DG awareness certification, HAZMAT response team qualifications, first aid and MFAG training, port-specific safety inductions, and refresher training due dates. The system must enforce that only personnel with valid and current DG response certifications can be assigned to emergency response roles in drill schedules and actual incident responses. Expiring certifications must trigger automated reminders 60 and 30 days before expiry. Training records must comply with ISM Code documentation requirements and be available for port state control inspections in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-002-R017	FEAT-023-2-002	MOD-023	Weather and Environmental Condition Monitoring for DG Areas	The system must integrate with meteorological data sources to provide real-time weather and environmental conditions for DG storage and handling areas at each port. Monitored parameters must include temperature, humidity, wind speed and direction, precipitation, and sea state. The system must generate automatic alerts when conditions exceed safe thresholds for specific DG classes, for example when ambient temperature exceeds 45°C at Gulf ports affecting Class 2 compressed gases or Class 5 organic peroxides. Wind direction changes must trigger reassessment of hazard zone calculations for any active DG incidents. Weather data must be displayed on the emergency response dashboard and archived with incident records for post-investigation analysis purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R001	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Safety Data Sheet Repository with Version Control	The system must maintain a centralized repository of Safety Data Sheets (SDS) for all chemical substances and dangerous goods handled across all four entity jurisdictions. Each SDS must be stored with full version control, tracking the issue date, revision number, supplier, and the 16 mandatory GHS sections. The repository must support storage in multiple languages (English, Arabic, Hindi) and associate each SDS with the corresponding UN number, CAS number, and IMDG Code entry. The system must enforce that no DG booking can be confirmed without a valid, non-expired SDS on file, with configurable expiry rules (default 5 years per GHS revision cycle). Duplicate detection must prevent the same SDS from being uploaded multiple times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R002	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Automated SDS Parsing and Data Extraction	The system must employ AI-powered document parsing to automatically extract key safety data from uploaded SDS documents in PDF, Word, or scanned image formats. Extracted fields must include product identification, hazard classification (GHS categories), composition and ingredient information, first aid measures, firefighting measures, accidental release measures, handling and storage conditions, exposure controls and PPE requirements, physical and chemical properties, stability and reactivity, toxicological information, ecological information, disposal considerations, transport information (including UN number, proper shipping name, transport hazard class), and regulatory information. Extraction accuracy must exceed 95% for standard GHS-compliant SDS formats, and all extracted data must be presented for human verification before being committed to the database.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R003	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	GHS Classification Cross-Reference and Validation	The system must validate that the GHS (Globally Harmonized System) classification stated in each SDS is consistent with the IMDG Code classification for the same substance. When discrepancies are detected between the GHS hazard category and the IMDG class or packing group, the system must flag the inconsistency and route it to the DG compliance officer for resolution. The validation must check signal words (Danger vs. Warning), hazard statements (H-codes), precautionary statements (P-codes), and GHS pictograms against the expected classification for the declared UN number. The system must support both GHS Revision 7 and Revision 9 formats and handle jurisdiction-specific implementation variations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R004	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Chemical Compatibility Database for Storage Planning	The system must maintain a chemical compatibility database that maps incompatible chemical groups and provides storage segregation guidance for DG warehousing and container freight station operations. The database must support compatibility lookups by UN number, chemical family, or IMDG class, returning a compatibility rating (compatible, conditionally compatible, incompatible, highly reactive) with specific storage distance requirements. For warehouse operations at Hamad Port free zone, Jebel Ali Free Zone (JAFZA), and JNPT CFS facilities, the system must generate storage layout recommendations that maximize space utilization while maintaining required segregation distances. Compatibility rules must be configurable to incorporate facility-specific constraints and local fire code requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R005	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Exposure Limit Monitoring and Workplace Safety Compliance	The system must store and manage occupational exposure limits (OELs) for all chemical substances in the SDS repository, supporting the specific regulatory frameworks of each jurisdiction: Qatar Labour Law exposure standards, UAE OSHAD SF framework, KSA OSHA equivalent standards, and Indian Factories Act threshold limit values. The system must display the most restrictive applicable limit for each substance when personnel are working in multi-entity port environments. Integration with workplace air quality monitoring systems must be supported where available, with the system automatically alerting operations managers when monitored exposure levels approach 50% of the applicable OEL. All exposure data and alerts must be recorded for regulatory compliance reporting in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R006	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Regulatory Chemical Registration Tracking	The system must track chemical registration and import permit status for DG substances across all four jurisdictions: Qatar Ministry of Environment chemical registration, UAE ESMA product registration for chemicals, KSA SFDA and SASO chemical import requirements, and India CDSCO and BIS chemical clearances. Each DG booking must be validated against the registration database to ensure the substance is registered and approved for import in the destination country. The system must maintain expiry dates for registrations and permits, generate renewal reminders 90 days before expiry, and block booking confirmations for unregistered substances. Registration documents must be linked to the corresponding SDS and stored with bilingual Arabic/English metadata.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R007	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	SDS Distribution to Stakeholders with Audit Trail	The system must automate the distribution of relevant SDS documents to all stakeholders in the DG cargo handling chain, including terminal operators, warehouse staff, transport companies, customs brokers, and port authority inspectors. Distribution must be triggered at key milestones: booking confirmation, container gate-in at origin port, vessel loading, transshipment at intermediate ports, and delivery to consignee. Each distribution event must be logged with recipient, timestamp, delivery channel (email, portal access, API), and read confirmation where available. The system must support bulk distribution for vessel-level SDS packages and allow recipients to request SDS in their preferred language (English, Arabic, or Hindi). Access controls must ensure that confidential formulation details in Section 3 are redacted when distributing to non-authorized parties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R008	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Chemical Inventory Tracking Across Port Facilities	The system must maintain a real-time chemical inventory for all DG substances present at each port facility, including containers in the terminal yard, cargo in warehouses, and goods in transit through the port gate. The inventory must be aggregated by IMDG class, UN number, and quantity, enabling compliance with port-specific DG quantity limits (e.g., Jebel Ali maximum DG tonnage per terminal block, JNPT maximum DG container stack height). The inventory must update automatically as containers are gated in, loaded, discharged, or gated out, integrating with terminal operating systems via API. Inventory reports must be available in the local currency of each entity for insurance valuation purposes (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and must be exportable for submission to port authority DG inspectors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R009	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Hazardous Substance Threshold Alerting for Seveso Compliance	The system must monitor cumulative quantities of hazardous substances at each port facility against threshold values defined by applicable major accident hazard regulations, analogous to the EU Seveso III Directive. For each jurisdiction, the system must apply the relevant threshold framework: Qatar Environmental Protection Law hazardous substance limits, UAE Civil Defence storage quantity limits, KSA Saudi Civil Defence hazardous materials regulations, and Indian Chemical Accidents (Emergency Planning, Preparedness and Response) Rules. When cumulative quantities approach 75% of any threshold, the system must generate warning alerts to the facility safety manager and compliance officer. At 90%, the system must block further DG container acceptance until quantities are reduced. Threshold calculations must account for aggregation rules where substances of similar hazard are combined.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R010	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	SDS Expiry Management and Renewal Workflow	The system must track SDS validity periods and proactively manage the renewal process by alerting the procurement and compliance teams when SDS documents approach expiry. The renewal workflow must start 120 days before expiry, sending automated requests to the chemical manufacturer or supplier for updated SDS. If no updated SDS is received within 60 days of expiry, the system must escalate to the DG compliance officer and flag all active bookings using the affected substance. Expired SDS must trigger a soft block on new bookings (overridable by DG compliance officer with documented justification) and a hard block if the SDS has been expired for more than 30 days. Renewal tracking must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats and support Arabic correspondence templates for supplier communications in the Gulf region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R011	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Chemical Risk Assessment Generation for DG Operations	The system must generate AI-assisted chemical risk assessments for DG cargo operations based on the SDS data, IMDG Code requirements, and site-specific conditions. Risk assessments must cover handling and lifting operations, storage conditions (including temperature sensitivity critical for Gulf summer operations exceeding 50°C), loading and discharge procedures, fumigation protocols where applicable, and emergency response preparedness. Each risk assessment must include hazard identification, risk rating matrix (likelihood x severity), control measures, residual risk rating, and reviewer sign-off. The AI engine must recommend control measures based on the specific substance properties and the operational context at the selected port. Risk assessments must be generated in both English and Arabic and linked to the corresponding SDS and DG booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R012	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Restricted and Prohibited Substance Screening	The system must screen all DG bookings against restricted and prohibited substance lists maintained by each jurisdiction and international sanctions regimes. This includes the OPCW Chemical Weapons Convention schedules, UN Security Council sanctions lists, Qatar Strategic Goods Control List, UAE Strategic Goods list under Federal Decree-Law No. 13, KSA dual-use and controlled items list, and India SCOMET (Special Chemicals, Organisms, Materials, Equipment and Technologies) list. The screening must run automatically at booking creation and at each border crossing point, checking the declared substance CAS numbers, UN numbers, and chemical names against all applicable lists. Any match must trigger an immediate hold on the booking and route it to the compliance officer with full details of the matched list entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R013	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Environmental Impact Data for DG Substances	The system must store and present environmental impact data for all DG substances from SDS Section 12 (Ecological Information), including aquatic toxicity, persistence, bioaccumulation potential, and mobility in soil. This data must be integrated with the emergency response module to inform environmental protection decisions during DG spill incidents. For marine environments at Gulf ports (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL), the system must flag substances with high marine toxicity and cross-reference with local marine protected area designations. For JNPT, the system must flag substances that may impact the Thane Creek flamingo habitat. Environmental impact data must be included in pre-arrival DG notifications where required by port environmental authorities, and must be available in summary format for customs environmental clearance applications in all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R014	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Supplier SDS Compliance Scoring and Management	The system must evaluate and score chemical suppliers based on the quality, completeness, and timeliness of their SDS submissions. Scoring criteria must include GHS compliance of SDS format, completeness of all 16 sections, accuracy of classification data verified against known substance properties, responsiveness to update requests, and availability of multilingual versions. The AI engine must analyze each submitted SDS against a quality checklist and assign a compliance score from 0 to 100. Suppliers with scores below 70 must be flagged for review, and their DG bookings must require additional verification steps. Compliance scores must be visible to procurement teams when selecting suppliers and must be tracked over time with trend analysis. Reports must be filterable by entity and displayable in both English and Arabic with currency values for associated cargo in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R015	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Chemical Safety Data API for Third-Party Integration	The system must expose a secure RESTful API for third-party systems to query chemical safety data, enabling integration with warehouse management systems, transport management systems, and customs declaration platforms used across the four entity jurisdictions. The API must support queries by UN number, CAS number, product name, or IMDG class and return structured safety data including GHS classification, handling precautions, storage requirements, PPE requirements, and emergency response information. API access must be controlled via OAuth 2.0 authentication with role-based permissions determining which SDS sections are accessible to each consumer. The API must support response formats in JSON and XML, handle concurrent requests from multiple entities, and provide rate limiting configurable per API consumer. All API transactions must be logged for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R016	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Temperature-Sensitive Chemical Monitoring Integration	The system must integrate with IoT temperature monitoring devices installed on reefer containers and DG warehouses to track ambient and internal temperatures for temperature-sensitive dangerous goods. The SDS repository must define temperature thresholds for each substance (control temperature, emergency temperature per IMDG Code 7.7), and the monitoring system must generate alerts when temperatures approach or exceed these limits. For Gulf ports where summer ambient temperatures regularly exceed 50°C, the system must apply region-specific handling windows that restrict DG operations to cooler hours when necessary. Temperature logs must be archived with the shipment record for regulatory compliance and insurance claim purposes. The monitoring dashboard must display readings in real-time with historical trend charts, accessible in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R014	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Lessons Learned Repository and Knowledge Sharing	The system must maintain a searchable lessons learned repository derived from completed DG incident investigations. Each lessons learned entry must include an anonymized incident summary, root causes identified, corrective actions implemented, effectiveness assessment, and applicability guidance (which ports, DG classes, or operational scenarios the lesson applies to). The AI engine must automatically suggest relevant lessons learned when new incidents are reported based on similarity matching of incident parameters. Lessons must be categorized by IMDG class, incident type, port, and root cause category, and must be accessible in both English and Arabic. The repository must support subscription-based notifications where operations teams at each port can subscribe to lessons relevant to their DG cargo profile and receive alerts when new relevant lessons are published.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-003-R017	FEAT-023-2-003	MOD-023	Multi-Language SDS Translation and Standardization	The system must support SDS documents in English, Arabic, and Hindi, providing AI-assisted translation services to convert SDS content between these languages while maintaining technical accuracy of chemical nomenclature and safety terminology. Translations must preserve GHS-standardized hazard statements (H-codes) and precautionary statements (P-codes) as defined in each language version of GHS. For Arabic translations, the system must use IUPAC Arabic chemical naming conventions and ensure proper RTL rendering of chemical formulas, hazard pictograms, and safety data tables. Translated SDS documents must be clearly marked as translations with reference to the source language version. The system must maintain a chemical terminology glossary in all three languages to ensure consistency across all translated documents and allow domain experts to review and approve translations before publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R001	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Multi-Channel DG Incident Reporting Interface	The system must provide multiple channels for reporting DG incidents: a web-based form accessible from desktop and mobile browsers, a dedicated mobile app with camera integration for photo/video evidence capture, a voice-to-text reporting option for hands-free reporting during active incidents, and an API endpoint for automated incident creation from IoT sensor alerts (e.g., gas detectors, temperature monitors). Each channel must capture the mandatory incident data fields: date/time (with timezone and Hijri date equivalent), location (port code, terminal, bay/row/tier or yard position), DG details (UN number, IMDG class, proper shipping name), incident type (leak, fire, exposure, misdeclaration, structural damage), severity level, and initial reporter identity. All channels must support Arabic and English input.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R002	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Incident Classification and Severity Assessment Engine	The system must automatically classify DG incidents using an AI-powered severity assessment engine that considers the substance hazard profile, quantity involved, release scenario, proximity to personnel and populated areas, environmental sensitivity of the location, and potential for escalation. The classification must follow a five-tier severity scale: Near Miss (no actual release), Minor (contained release, no injuries), Moderate (limited release, minor injuries or environmental impact), Major (significant release, serious injuries or major environmental impact), and Catastrophic (uncontrolled release, fatalities or widespread contamination). The AI engine must cross-reference the incident substance against the MFAG and EmS databases to factor in the specific hazard characteristics. Classification must be provisional initially and updatable as the incident evolves or investigation reveals additional information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R003	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Regulatory Incident Notification Automation	The system must automate the generation and submission of mandatory incident notifications to regulatory authorities based on the incident severity and jurisdiction. For Qatar, notifications must be sent to MOTC, Qatar Civil Defence, and Ministry of Environment per their respective formats and timeframes. For UAE, notifications must comply with NCEMA reporting requirements and Abu Dhabi OSHAD or Dubai Municipality protocols depending on the port. For KSA, the system must notify the Transport General Authority and Saudi Civil Defence. For India, notifications must be sent to the DG Shipping Directorate, state pollution control board, and district emergency authority per the Chemical Accidents Rules. Each notification must be generated in the authority-mandated language (Arabic for Gulf states, English for India) and track submission confirmation and acknowledgment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R004	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Root Cause Analysis Framework with Investigation Templates	The system must provide a structured root cause analysis (RCA) framework supporting multiple investigation methodologies: 5-Why analysis, fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram, fault tree analysis, and bow-tie analysis. Investigation templates must be pre-configured for common DG incident types in container shipping: container leakage due to improper packing, misdeclaration of DG class, segregation violation, temperature excursion, structural failure during handling, and fumigation gas exposure. Each template must guide investigators through evidence collection, witness statements, timeline reconstruction, contributing factor identification, and root cause determination. The framework must support collaborative investigation where team members from different entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) can contribute findings simultaneously, with all entries timestamped and attributed to the contributor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R005	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Evidence Management and Chain of Custody Tracking	The system must provide comprehensive evidence management for DG incident investigations, supporting the capture, storage, and retrieval of photographs, videos, audio recordings, sensor data logs, documents (SDS, DG declarations, packing certificates), container inspection reports, and witness statements. Each evidence item must be assigned a unique identifier, timestamped with tamper-proof metadata, geotagged where applicable, and linked to the incident record. The system must enforce chain of custody tracking, recording who captured, accessed, modified, or transferred each evidence item. Evidence must be stored in jurisdiction-compliant encrypted storage, with retention periods aligned to each entity's legal requirements. For incidents that may result in legal proceedings, the system must support legal hold to prevent evidence deletion or modification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R006	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) Management	The system must manage the full lifecycle of corrective and preventive actions (CAPA) arising from DG incident investigations. Each CAPA must include a clear description of the action required, responsible person, target completion date, priority level, and linkage to the root cause identified in the investigation. The CAPA workflow must support assignment across entity boundaries (e.g., a CAPA arising from an incident at Jebel Ali may be assigned to the Qatar entity's packing team), with multi-entity visibility and escalation. Progress tracking must include status updates, evidence of completion (photos, documents, system configuration screenshots), and effectiveness verification after implementation. Overdue CAPAs must trigger escalation notifications to the responsible entity's management and the group DG compliance director. CAPA statistics must be reportable in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R006	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Route Survey and Site Assessment Management	The system must manage route surveys for inland transport of heavy lift cargo from factory or yard to port. Survey records must capture road dimensions, bridge load limits, overhead clearance restrictions, turning radius constraints, and required road modifications. Each survey must be geotagged and linked to specific cargo pieces. The system must support uploading photographic evidence and engineering drawings. For Middle East routes, the system must account for summer heat restrictions on asphalt roads (typically above 50°C ambient) that may limit transport windows. Survey reports must be generated in Arabic and English with measurements in both metric and imperial units where required by local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R007	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	DG Misdeclaration Incident Detection and Workflow	The system must support a specific workflow for DG misdeclaration incidents, which represent a significant safety risk in container shipping. When a misdeclaration is detected (through port inspection, container scanning, or in-transit discovery), the system must capture the declared vs. actual DG classification, identify the shipper responsible, cross-reference against the shipper's declaration history to identify patterns of repeat misdeclaration, and initiate the appropriate response workflow. The workflow must include notification to the vessel operator, port authority, and flag state; quarantine of the affected container; re-assessment of vessel stowage plan for segregation violations; and generation of a misdeclaration report for submission to the IMO DG reporting system. Financial penalties must be calculated based on entity-specific tariff schedules in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R008	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Incident Timeline Reconstruction and Visualization	The system must support the reconstruction of a detailed incident timeline from multiple data sources, presenting events in a visual chronological format. Data sources for timeline construction must include container tracking events (gate-in, loading, discharge), temperature monitoring logs, vessel AIS position data, port CCTV footage timestamps, access control records, communication logs, weather station data, and manual entries from investigators. The timeline visualization must support zooming from a macro view (days before and after the incident) to a micro view (minute-by-minute during the incident), with the ability to filter by data source type. The timeline must be exportable as a standalone HTML file for sharing with regulatory investigators and must render correctly in both English and Arabic with proper RTL alignment for Arabic date and time labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R009	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	DG Incident Cost Tracking and Insurance Claim Support	The system must track all costs associated with a DG incident, including emergency response costs, environmental cleanup costs, cargo damage, third-party liability claims, regulatory fines, vessel delay costs, and business interruption losses. Costs must be recorded in the local currency of the entity incurring the expense (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated reporting using the exchange rate on the date of expense. The system must generate insurance claim documentation packages including incident report, investigation findings, evidence summaries, cost breakdowns, and third-party correspondence. For P&I club claims, the system must produce documentation in the format specified by the vessel's P&I club. Cost tracking must integrate with the ERP's financial module for proper ledger posting across multi-entity accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R010	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Shipper and Carrier Performance Tracking for DG Safety	The system must maintain safety performance records for all shippers, carriers, and packing facilities involved in DG cargo operations, tracking incident frequency, misdeclaration rates, container condition compliance, documentation completeness, and response cooperation during investigations. Performance data must be aggregated from incidents across all four entity jurisdictions, providing a global view of each stakeholder's DG safety record. The system must calculate a DG safety score for each entity on a scale of 0-100, with configurable weightings for different incident types. Shippers with scores below a configurable threshold (default 60) must be flagged for enhanced scrutiny including mandatory container inspection for all DG shipments. Safety scores must be shared with booking teams to inform acceptance decisions and must be reviewable in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R011	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	AI-Powered Incident Pattern Analysis and Prediction	The system must employ AI and machine learning algorithms to analyze historical DG incident data across all four entity jurisdictions, identifying patterns, trends, and leading indicators of potential future incidents. Pattern analysis must consider variables including DG class, origin country, shipper, packing facility, season and temperature correlation (critical for Gulf summer months), vessel type, port handling equipment, and time-of-day patterns. The AI engine must generate monthly predictive risk assessments identifying the highest-risk DG shipments, routes, and operational periods. Predictions must be presented as risk heat maps overlaid on operational dashboards, with drill-down capability to view the contributing factors for each high-risk area. The system must track prediction accuracy over time and continuously refine models based on actual incident outcomes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R012	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Regulatory Investigation Cooperation and Documentation	The system must support cooperation with regulatory investigations by providing controlled access to incident data for authorized external investigators. Access must be configurable per investigation, granting read-only views to specific incident records, evidence items, and investigation findings without exposing broader system data. The system must generate investigation cooperation packages in the format required by each regulatory authority: Qatar MOTC investigation format, UAE National Transport Authority format, KSA TGA investigation protocol, and Indian DG Shipping inquiry format. All data shared with external investigators must be logged with timestamps and access records maintained for legal compliance. The system must support legal privilege markers to protect attorney-client privileged communications within the investigation record from inadvertent disclosure during regulatory cooperation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R013	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	DG Incident Reporting Dashboard with KPI Analytics	The system must provide a comprehensive incident reporting dashboard displaying key performance indicators for DG safety management across all entities and ports. KPIs must include total reportable incidents per period, incident frequency rate (per 1000 DG containers handled), severity distribution, average time to initial report, average investigation closure time, CAPA completion rate, misdeclaration detection rate, and cost per incident. The dashboard must support comparison views across entities (Qatar vs. UAE vs. KSA vs. India), ports (QAHAM vs. AEJEA vs. AEKHL vs. INNSA), and time periods (month-over-month, year-over-year) using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar references. Trend analysis must highlight statistically significant changes in incident rates. All dashboard elements must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with appropriate currency formatting for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R001	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Certification Profile Management	The system must maintain a dedicated AEO certification profile for each legal entity operating across Qatar (QCGA AEO), UAE (FCA AEO), KSA (ZATCA AEO), and India (CBIC AEO-T1/T2/T3/LO). Each profile shall store the AEO certificate number, date of issuance in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, expiry date, tier classification, authorized customs broker linkages, and the designated AEO compliance officer contact. The profile must support Arabic and English bilingual data entry with RTL layout for Arabic fields. When a certificate approaches expiry within 90 days, the system shall trigger automated renewal workflow notifications to the compliance team and senior management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R015	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Third-Party Liability and Claims Management for DG Incidents	The system must manage third-party liability claims arising from DG incidents, tracking claimant details, claim type (personal injury, property damage, environmental damage, business interruption), claimed amount in the relevant currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), supporting evidence, and claim status through the lifecycle (received, under review, accepted, disputed, settled, closed). The claims management workflow must route claims to the appropriate entity's legal and insurance team, support multi-currency claim valuations with exchange rate date stamping, and generate settlement documentation. Integration with the ERP's accounts payable module must support payment processing for approved claims. The system must maintain a claims reserve register for financial reporting purposes and generate actuarial-ready data exports for the group's insurance brokers to support annual DG liability policy renewals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R016	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Whistleblower and Anonymous DG Safety Reporting Channel	The system must provide a confidential and anonymous reporting channel for DG safety concerns, enabling employees, contractors, and third parties to report unsafe practices, misdeclarations, regulatory violations, or safety culture issues without fear of retaliation. The anonymous channel must strip all identifying metadata from submissions, assign each report a unique tracking code for follow-up communication without revealing reporter identity, and route reports to the group DG compliance director and the relevant entity's ethics officer. Reports must be classifiable by urgency (routine, urgent, critical), and critical reports must trigger immediate review within 4 hours. The channel must comply with Qatar Law No. 20 of 2008 on whistleblower protection, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 of 2021, and equivalent protections in KSA and India. The interface must support Arabic and English submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R017	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	Automated Incident Report Generation and Distribution	The system must automatically generate formatted incident reports at configurable stages of the investigation lifecycle: initial notification report (within 2 hours of incident), preliminary investigation report (within 48 hours), interim report (weekly during active investigation), and final investigation report (upon closure). Each report format must include an executive summary, incident description, timeline, affected parties, environmental impact assessment, root causes, corrective actions, and lessons learned where available. Reports must be generated in both English and Arabic with proper formatting, company branding per entity, and attachment of key evidence items. Distribution must be automated based on the incident severity and entity: internal management reports for minor incidents, port authority and regulatory submission for moderate and above, and board-level briefing format for major and catastrophic incidents. Report templates must be configurable per entity and regulatory jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-023-2-004-R018	FEAT-023-2-004	MOD-023	DG Incident Simulation and Scenario Planning Tool	The system must provide a simulation tool that allows safety managers to model hypothetical DG incident scenarios for preparedness planning and training purposes. Simulations must accept input parameters including DG substance (by UN number), quantity, release type (instantaneous vs. continuous), location (selectable from port facility maps for QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), weather conditions, and time of day. The simulation engine must calculate projected hazard zones, estimate casualty exposure based on personnel location data, predict environmental impact using substance ecotoxicity data from the SDS database, and estimate response resource requirements. Simulation results must be compared against actual emergency response capabilities at the selected port to identify gaps. Completed simulations must be archived and linked to emergency response drill planning. The simulation interface must support Arabic and English with all calculations using metric units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R001	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Reefer Booking Request Creation	The system must allow shippers and freight forwarders to create reefer booking requests specifying commodity type from a standardized HS code list, required set-point temperature in Celsius or Fahrenheit, humidity percentage range, ventilation settings in CBM/hour, container size (20RF or 40RH per ISO 6346), and desired pickup and delivery dates. The booking form must support both English and Arabic RTL layouts with proper field alignment. Each booking must be assigned a unique reference number prefixed with the entity code (QA/AE/SA/IN) and linked to the originating port (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNP for JNPT). The system must validate that the requested temperature range is physically achievable by the selected container type before allowing submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R002	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Commodity-Temperature Matrix Validation	The system must maintain a comprehensive commodity-temperature matrix mapping HS codes to their required transport temperature ranges, humidity levels, and ventilation settings. For example, fresh fruits (HS 0803) require -1°C to +14°C depending on variety, frozen meat (HS 0202) requires -18°C to -22°C, pharmaceuticals (HS 3004) require +2°C to +8°C per WHO GDP guidelines. When a booking is created, the AI engine must automatically validate the requested temperature against the commodity matrix and flag discrepancies with severity levels (warning for minor deviations within 2°C, error for deviations exceeding 2°C). The matrix must be configurable per entity to accommodate local regulatory variations such as SFDA requirements in KSA and FSSAI standards in India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R003	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	AI-Driven Booking Acceptance Decision	The AI engine must automatically evaluate each reefer booking request against multiple criteria including current reefer container availability at the origin port, power plug availability at origin and destination terminals, route transit time versus commodity shelf life, historical rejection rates for similar commodity-route combinations, and weather forecast data for the planned voyage. The system must generate an acceptance confidence score between 0 and 100, automatically accepting bookings scoring above 85, rejecting those below 40, and routing scores between 40-85 to human operators for review. The decision rationale must be logged in both English and Arabic for audit trail purposes, and the AI model must be retrained monthly using actual booking outcome data from all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R007	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Lashing and Securing Plan Generation	The system must generate detailed lashing and securing plans for heavy lift cargo on vessels, trailers, and rail wagons in accordance with IMO CSS Code Annex 13 and CTU Code guidelines. The plan must calculate required lashing forces based on cargo weight, dimensions, friction coefficients, and expected accelerations for the specific voyage route. The system must recommend lashing materials (chains, wire ropes, turnbuckles, welded stoppers) with specifications and quantities. Plans must be approved by a designated cargo surveyor before loading commences. Each lashing plan must be stored as a versioned document linked to the booking, with change history tracking any modifications made after initial approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R004	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Multi-Currency Reefer Surcharge Calculation	The system must calculate reefer surcharges based on container type, temperature range category (frozen below -15°C, chilled -15°C to +2°C, controlled ambient +2°C to +25°C), voyage duration, and fuel cost indices. Surcharges must be computed in the booking currency which may be USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR, using real-time exchange rates from the treasury module with rates locked at the time of booking confirmation. The system must support differential surcharge structures per entity, allowing Hamad Port operations to apply QAR-based tariffs while Jebel Ali uses AED-based schedules. All surcharge calculations must include applicable VAT rates (5% for UAE and KSA, 0% for Qatar free zones, 18% GST for India) and generate itemized breakdowns compliant with local tax authority formats including ZATCA e-invoicing for KSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R005	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Reefer Equipment Availability Check	Upon booking request submission, the system must perform real-time availability checks against the reefer container inventory across all managed depots and terminals within the booking origin port cluster. The check must consider containers currently in depot, containers expected to be released from import deliveries within the booking lead time, and containers in transit that will arrive before the required pickup date. The system must differentiate between standard reefer units, controlled atmosphere (CA) units capable of O2/CO2 regulation, and super-freezer units rated to -60°C. If no suitable equipment is available at the origin, the AI must automatically search adjacent ports (e.g., Khalifa Port if Jebel Ali has no stock) and propose repositioning with estimated cost and time, presenting options in the user's preferred language and currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R006	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Dangerous Goods Compatibility for Reefer Cargo	The system must validate dangerous goods declarations for reefer cargo against IMDG Code requirements, specifically for commodities that require refrigeration and carry DG classifications such as certain chemicals (UN numbers in Class 6.1 or Class 9) or dry ice used as a refrigerant (UN 1845, Class 9). The validation must check segregation rules to ensure incompatible DG classes are not booked on the same reefer stack or bay. For bookings originating from or transiting through UAE ports, additional ADR compliance checks per UAE Federal Transport Authority regulations must be applied. The system must generate the required DG documentation including the Dangerous Goods Declaration form and container packing certificate, auto-populating fields from the booking data and presenting them in English with Arabic translation for GCC port authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R007	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Booking Amendment and Cancellation Workflow	The system must support structured amendment workflows for reefer bookings allowing changes to temperature set-point, pickup date, container size, or destination port with full audit trail. Each amendment must trigger re-validation of commodity-temperature compatibility, equipment availability, and surcharge recalculation. Amendments requested within 48 hours of vessel cut-off must require supervisor approval via a configurable workflow engine. Cancellation policies must be entity-specific: Qatar operations may allow free cancellation up to 72 hours before gate-in, while India JNPT operations may apply a 25% cancellation fee after booking confirmation per local trade customs. All cancellation charges must be calculated in the original booking currency and reflected in the accounts receivable module. The system must support Hijri calendar date references for cancellation deadline calculations in KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R008	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Shipper Commodity Declaration Capture	The system must capture detailed shipper commodity declarations for each reefer booking including exact commodity description, packaging type (cartons, pallets, drums), total gross weight in kilograms, total number of packages, required atmosphere composition for CA shipments (O2 percentage, CO2 percentage, N2 percentage), pre-cooling requirements specifying whether cargo must arrive at a target pulp temperature before stuffing, and any special handling instructions such as top stow or away from engine room heat. The declaration form must support file attachments for phytosanitary certificates, health certificates, and certificates of origin in PDF format up to 10MB each. For India-origin bookings, APEDA registration number and FSSAI license number must be mandatory fields. The system must validate that the declared weight does not exceed the container maximum payload rating of 27,400 kg for a standard 40RH unit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R009	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Route Feasibility Analysis for Perishables	The AI engine must perform route feasibility analysis for each reefer booking by calculating total transit time including port dwell times, transshipment durations, and customs clearance estimates at origin and destination. The calculated total transit time must be compared against the commodity's maximum allowable transport duration from the commodity-temperature matrix. For multi-leg routes involving transshipment (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT via Jebel Ali), the system must verify reefer plug availability at each transshipment terminal and calculate the maximum allowable unplug duration during terminal transfers. The analysis must factor in seasonal variations such as extreme summer temperatures in the Arabian Gulf region exceeding 50°C ambient, which may affect reefer unit performance. Results must be presented as a risk score with green, amber, or red status indicators, with amber and red bookings requiring explicit shipper acknowledgment of transit risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R010	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Automated Booking Confirmation Notification	Upon booking acceptance, the system must generate and dispatch booking confirmation documents via email, SMS, and in-app notification to the shipper, consignee, and nominated freight forwarder. The confirmation must include booking reference number, vessel and voyage details, allocated container number if pre-assigned, required gate-in date and time at the origin terminal, temperature set-point and ventilation settings, applicable reefer surcharges with currency breakdown, and terms and conditions per the entity's standard trading terms referencing applicable INCOTERMS 2020 rules. Notifications must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) and must include QR codes linking to the real-time booking status portal. For KSA and Qatar entities, the confirmation must include Hijri date equivalents alongside Gregorian dates. The system must retry failed notification deliveries up to three times with exponential backoff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R002	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Multi-currency rate definition per tariff	Each tariff code must support rate definitions in multiple currencies simultaneously. The base rate must be defined in USD with equivalent rates in AED, QAR, SAR, and INR either manually entered or auto-calculated from the live exchange rate feed. The system must flag rates that deviate more than a configurable percentage from the exchange-rate-derived equivalent. Rate entry must support per-unit bases including per TEU, per FEU, per CBM (for LCL), per revenue ton, and per container for special equipment (flat rack, open top, tank container). Each rate must carry a minimum and maximum threshold to prevent data entry errors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R011	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Reefer Booking Capacity Planning Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time capacity planning dashboard showing reefer slot utilization per vessel voyage, per port, and per week for a rolling 12-week horizon. The dashboard must display total reefer plugs on each vessel, currently booked plugs, remaining available plugs, and waitlisted bookings. AI-based demand forecasting must predict reefer booking volumes using historical data, seasonal commodity patterns (e.g., mango season from India April-June, date exports from KSA August-October), and market intelligence feeds. The dashboard must support drill-down from port cluster level to individual berth level and must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts. Data must be filterable by entity, port code, vessel operator, commodity group, and temperature category. Export functionality must support PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic text rendering including right-aligned numeric columns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R012	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Offline Booking Capability for Remote Locations	The system must support offline booking creation for field agents operating in areas with limited connectivity such as inland container depots feeding into JNPT or remote agricultural regions in India and KSA. The offline module must cache the commodity-temperature matrix, tariff schedules, and container availability snapshots locally on the agent's device. Bookings created offline must be queued and automatically synchronized when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution logic that handles cases where equipment became unavailable during the offline period. The offline mode must support a maximum queue of 50 pending bookings per device and must preserve all mandatory field validations including weight limits and DG checks. Synchronized bookings must be clearly flagged in the system with their original offline creation timestamp and the synchronization timestamp for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R013	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Multi-Entity Booking Transfer and Sharing	The system must support transferring reefer bookings between operating entities when operational circumstances require it, such as when a vessel skips Hamad Port and cargo must be rerouted through Jebel Ali. The transfer process must automatically recalculate surcharges in the receiving entity's base currency, apply the receiving entity's tax rules, and update the booking reference to include the new entity prefix while maintaining a cross-reference to the original booking. Inter-entity settlements must be recorded in the intercompany accounting module with proper transfer pricing documentation. The transfer workflow must require approval from both the originating and receiving entity operations managers, with configurable SLA timers (default 4 hours) after which the request escalates to regional management. All transfer documentation must be generated bilingually in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R014	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Regulatory Compliance Pre-Screening	The system must perform automated regulatory compliance pre-screening for each reefer booking against applicable import and export regulations of the origin and destination countries. For Qatar-bound perishable imports, the system must verify that the commodity is not on the Ministry of Municipality restricted list and that the exporting country is approved for the specific commodity category. For KSA-bound shipments, SFDA import permit requirements must be validated against the Saudi FDA product registration database. For India-bound cargo, FSSAI licensing and Plant Quarantine clearance requirements must be checked based on HS code classification. The pre-screening must also verify that the shipper and consignee are not on any international sanctions lists (OFAC, EU, UN). Failed pre-screening checks must block booking confirmation and generate a detailed compliance report listing each failed check with remediation guidance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R015	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Booking Analytics and Rejection Analysis	The system must maintain comprehensive analytics on reefer booking patterns including acceptance rates, rejection reasons, amendment frequencies, cancellation rates, and average lead times segmented by entity, port, commodity group, and customer. The AI engine must identify trends in booking rejections and generate proactive recommendations such as suggesting additional reefer equipment procurement when rejection rates due to equipment shortage exceed 15% at any port for two consecutive weeks. Analytics must be presented through configurable dashboards supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar aggregations. The system must generate automated weekly reports distributed to operations managers in their preferred language, with KPI comparisons against configurable targets. Data retention for booking analytics must comply with local data protection laws: five years for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities, and eight years for India entities per Indian customs record-keeping requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-001-R016	FEAT-024-1-001	MOD-024	Customer Credit Check for Reefer Bookings	The system must integrate with the accounts receivable module to perform real-time credit checks before confirming reefer bookings. The credit check must evaluate the customer's outstanding balance, credit limit, payment terms, and aging profile. Reefer bookings carry higher financial exposure due to surcharges and potential cargo claims, so the system must apply a reefer-specific credit multiplier (configurable, default 1.5x) to the booking value when evaluating against available credit. Customers exceeding their credit limit must be blocked from new reefer bookings unless a credit override is approved by the entity's finance manager. The system must support prepayment workflows where customers with insufficient credit can make advance payments in any supported currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) to release blocked bookings. Credit check results must be cached for 15 minutes to avoid excessive calls to the finance module during peak booking periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R001	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	IoT Sensor Data Ingestion Pipeline	The system must ingest real-time telemetry data from reefer container IoT sensors including temperature (supply air, return air, and cargo probe), relative humidity percentage, O2 and CO2 levels for controlled atmosphere units, door open/close status, power supply status, and GPS coordinates. The ingestion pipeline must support industry-standard protocols including MQTT v5.0, REST API webhooks, and proprietary protocols from major reefer manufacturers (Carrier PrimeLINE, Daikin, Thermo King, MCI). The system must handle a sustained throughput of at least 10,000 sensor readings per second across all monitored containers with a maximum ingestion latency of 5 seconds. Data must be timestamped in UTC with millisecond precision and tagged with the container number per ISO 6346 format, the associated booking reference, and the current location context (terminal code, vessel IMO number, or inland location).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R002	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Real-Time Temperature Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time monitoring dashboard displaying the current temperature status of all active reefer containers grouped by location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), vessel, or customer. Each container must show current supply air temperature, return air temperature, set-point temperature, and the deviation between actual and set-point. Containers must be color-coded: green for within tolerance (±1°C of set-point), amber for minor deviation (±1°C to ±3°C), and red for critical deviation (beyond ±3°C). The dashboard must auto-refresh every 30 seconds without requiring manual page reload and must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with proper number formatting (Eastern Arabic numerals option for Arabic UI). Users must be able to filter by entity, port, temperature category, alarm status, and commodity type with results updating in under 2 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R003	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	AI-Driven Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously analyze incoming reefer telemetry data to detect anomalies beyond simple threshold breaches. The anomaly detection model must identify patterns such as gradual temperature drift indicating refrigeration unit degradation, abnormal defrost cycle frequency suggesting evaporator icing, humidity spikes correlating with door opening events, and unexpected power consumption patterns indicating compressor issues. The model must be trained on historical data from each container unit type and commodity combination to establish normal operating baselines. Detected anomalies must be classified by severity (informational, warning, critical) and predicted impact on cargo quality using remaining shelf-life calculations. The system must generate anomaly alerts within 60 seconds of pattern detection and must achieve a false positive rate below 5% after the initial 90-day learning period across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R004	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Multi-Channel Alert Notification System	The system must dispatch temperature and humidity alerts through multiple channels including email, SMS, push notifications, WhatsApp Business API, and in-app notifications. Alert routing must be configurable per entity and per customer, allowing different escalation paths based on alert severity and time of day. Critical temperature alarms (cargo temperature exceeding commodity tolerance for more than 30 minutes) must trigger immediate phone call notifications to on-duty operations staff via integration with the VoIP system. Alert messages must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) and must include the container number, current temperature, set-point, deviation magnitude, container location, commodity description, and recommended corrective action. For containers at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, alerts must also be sent to the terminal operating system (TOS) to prioritize inspection. Alert acknowledgment must be tracked with timestamps and responder identification for SLA compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R005	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Historical Temperature Data Logging and Retrieval	The system must store all reefer temperature and humidity readings in a time-series database with a minimum retention period of three years for active regulatory compliance and seven years in cold archive storage for dispute resolution purposes. Historical data must be queryable by container number, booking reference, vessel voyage, date range, and commodity type with query response times under 5 seconds for any 30-day data range. The system must generate downloadable temperature log reports in PDF and CSV formats showing readings at configurable intervals (minimum 15-minute resolution for PDF charts, raw data for CSV). Temperature logs must include chain-of-custody annotations marking when the container was under terminal responsibility versus vessel responsibility versus inland transport. For pharmaceutical shipments subject to WHO GDP requirements, the system must generate GDP-compliant temperature excursion reports with mean kinetic temperature (MKT) calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R006	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Geofenced Monitoring Zone Configuration	The system must support geofenced monitoring zones that automatically adjust monitoring parameters and alert thresholds based on the container's current location. For containers within terminal boundaries (Hamad Port coordinates, Khalifa Port coordinates, Jebel Ali coordinates, JNPT coordinates), monitoring frequency must be set to maximum resolution with immediate alert escalation to terminal operations. For containers on vessels at sea, the system must switch to satellite-based data transmission intervals (configurable, default every 15 minutes) with alerts routed to vessel operations teams. For containers on inland transport, cellular-based monitoring must be activated with alerts sent to the trucking company and cargo owner simultaneously. Each geofence zone must be configurable with entity-specific parameters including ambient temperature baselines that affect alarm threshold calculations, particularly for Gulf region ports where ambient temperatures regularly exceed 45°C in summer months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R007	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Temperature Excursion Event Management	The system must manage temperature excursion events through a structured lifecycle: detection, notification, investigation, corrective action, resolution, and post-incident review. When a temperature excursion is detected (actual temperature deviating from set-point beyond the commodity-specific tolerance for a duration exceeding the commodity-specific grace period), the system must automatically create an excursion event record linked to the container, booking, and cargo details. The event must capture the excursion start time, peak deviation, duration, estimated cargo impact based on commodity sensitivity curves, and GPS location at excursion onset. Investigation workflows must support attachment of photographic evidence, technician notes, and root cause classification from a configurable taxonomy (equipment failure, power interruption, incorrect set-point, door left open, external heat source). Completed excursion events must feed into the AI learning model to improve future anomaly detection accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R008	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Offline Data Buffering and Synchronization	The system must handle connectivity interruptions gracefully by implementing offline data buffering at the IoT gateway level. When network connectivity between the reefer monitoring gateway and the central platform is lost (common during vessel transit in certain ocean regions or at remote inland depots), the gateway must buffer all sensor readings locally with a minimum buffer capacity of 72 hours of data at full monitoring frequency. Upon connectivity restoration, buffered data must be automatically synchronized to the central platform in chronological order with timestamps preserved from the original reading time, not the synchronization time. The system must flag data gaps in monitoring dashboards when no data has been received for more than the expected transmission interval plus a configurable tolerance (default 5 minutes for terminal, 30 minutes for vessel, 15 minutes for inland). Post-synchronization, the AI anomaly detection engine must retroactively analyze buffered data and generate any alerts that would have been triggered during the offline period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R009	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Humidity Monitoring and Dew Point Calculation	The system must monitor relative humidity inside reefer containers and calculate dew point temperature in real-time to prevent condensation damage to cargo. For commodities sensitive to humidity (fresh produce, paper products, electronics), the system must maintain humidity within the booking-specified range and alert when humidity deviates by more than 5% from the target for longer than 20 minutes. The dew point calculation must use the Magnus formula incorporating supply air temperature and relative humidity readings. When the dew point temperature approaches within 2°C of the cargo surface temperature, the system must generate a condensation risk warning and recommend ventilation adjustments. Humidity data must be displayed alongside temperature on all monitoring dashboards and included in all temperature log reports. The system must support both standard humidity sensors and psychrometric calculations derived from wet-bulb and dry-bulb temperature sensor pairs for older reefer units at JNPT that lack dedicated humidity sensors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R010	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Controlled Atmosphere Gas Monitoring	For controlled atmosphere (CA) reefer containers, the system must monitor O2, CO2, and N2 concentration levels in addition to temperature and humidity. Gas concentration thresholds must be configurable per commodity, for example fresh bananas typically require 2-5% O2 and 2-5% CO2, while apples may require 1-3% O2 and 0.5-2.5% CO2. The system must alert when gas concentrations deviate from the specified ranges and must track total gas injection volumes for cost allocation and billing purposes. CA reefer monitoring data must be displayed on a dedicated dashboard tab showing gas concentration trends over time with clear visualization of target bands. The AI engine must predict gas consumption rates based on commodity respiration models and ambient conditions, alerting operations teams when gas reserves in the CA unit are projected to deplete before the end of the voyage. All CA monitoring data must be included in the cargo outturn documentation for quality assurance at destination ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R011	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Temperature Mapping and Hot Spot Detection	For reefer containers equipped with multiple internal temperature sensors (minimum four probe positions: front-top, front-bottom, rear-top, rear-bottom), the system must generate temperature distribution maps showing thermal gradients within the container. The AI engine must identify hot spots where temperature consistently exceeds the container average by more than 2°C, which may indicate poor air circulation due to improper cargo stacking or blocked T-bar floor channels. Hot spot detection must trigger automated recommendations for cargo restacking or ventilation adjustment. Temperature mapping data must be stored and retrievable for post-voyage analysis to support cargo claims investigations where localized temperature damage is alleged. The visualization must render correctly on both desktop and mobile interfaces with responsive layout supporting Arabic RTL orientation, and must use color gradient overlays interpretable by users with common forms of color vision deficiency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R012	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Regulatory Temperature Compliance Reporting	The system must generate regulatory compliance temperature reports formatted to meet the requirements of food safety authorities in all operating jurisdictions: Qatar Ministry of Municipality and Environment, UAE Municipality food safety departments, KSA SFDA, and India FSSAI. Each report format must include the mandatory data fields specified by the respective authority, such as continuous temperature records at intervals not exceeding 30 minutes, excursion summaries with duration and magnitude, and chain-of-custody timestamps. For pharmaceutical cargo, the system must generate reports compliant with WHO Technical Report Series No. 961 Annex 9 requirements including mean kinetic temperature calculations and cumulative excursion analysis. Reports must be digitally signed with the organization's certificate for authenticity and must be generatable in both English and Arabic. The system must support automated submission of compliance reports to regulatory portals where electronic submission APIs are available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R013	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Sensor Health and Calibration Monitoring	The system must monitor the health status of all IoT sensors attached to reefer containers, detecting sensor malfunctions including stuck readings (same value for more than 30 consecutive readings), out-of-range values (below -60°C or above +60°C for temperature sensors), excessive noise (standard deviation exceeding 3°C over a 10-reading window), and complete sensor failure (no data received). Malfunctioning sensors must be flagged on the monitoring dashboard with a distinct icon and must trigger maintenance work orders through integration with the equipment maintenance module. The system must track sensor calibration schedules based on manufacturer recommendations (typically annual for temperature sensors, semi-annual for humidity sensors) and generate calibration due alerts 30 days in advance. Calibration records including calibration certificates, reference standards used, and before/after accuracy measurements must be stored and linked to the sensor asset record for a minimum of five years per ISO 17025 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R014	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Customer Self-Service Temperature Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing web portal and mobile application allowing shippers and consignees to view real-time temperature and humidity status of their reefer containers without requiring access to the full operational system. The portal must authenticate users via the customer identity management module and restrict visibility to containers associated with their active bookings only. Customers must be able to view current temperature readings, historical temperature charts for the voyage duration, alert history, and estimated time of arrival. The portal must support customizable alert subscriptions where customers can set their own notification thresholds independent of the operational monitoring thresholds. The interface must be fully responsive, supporting desktop browsers and mobile devices with both English and Arabic language options. Temperature data displayed to customers must have a configurable delay (default 15 minutes) to prevent real-time operational data exposure, with the delay configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R007	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Health certificate tracking for perishable cargo	The system must track health certificates for all perishable cargo categories including frozen meat (HS codes 0201-0210), fresh fish and seafood (HS codes 0301-0307), dairy products (HS codes 0401-0406), and fresh fruits and vegetables (HS codes 0701-0814). Each health certificate record must capture issuing authority, certificate number, date of issue, expiry date, laboratory test results, approved establishment number, and halal certification status where applicable. For imports into Qatar and KSA, the system must verify halal slaughter certification from approved international halal bodies (JAKIM, IFANCA, HFA). The tracking module must alert operations staff 48 hours before certificate expiry for shipments still in transit. The system must integrate with the Qatar MOPH e-services portal and KSA SFDA Rasid platform for electronic certificate verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R015	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Energy Consumption Monitoring per Reefer Unit	The system must track electrical power consumption of each reefer container unit in kilowatt-hours (kWh) using data from smart power meters at terminal plug points and vessel reefer rack monitoring systems. Power consumption data must be correlated with temperature set-point, ambient temperature, container age, and cargo load factor to establish energy efficiency baselines per unit type. The AI engine must identify reefer units consuming more than 20% above their baseline, indicating potential refrigeration system degradation that may lead to future temperature failures. Energy consumption data must feed into the reefer surcharge calculation module for accurate per-container power cost recovery. The system must generate energy efficiency reports per terminal comparing power consumption across the reefer fleet, supporting sustainability reporting requirements. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port operations, energy data must integrate with the UAE carbon tracking framework. Power consumption must be convertible to CO2 equivalent emissions using entity-specific grid emission factors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R016	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Predictive Maintenance Alert Generation	The AI engine must analyze reefer telemetry patterns to generate predictive maintenance alerts before equipment failures occur. The predictive model must monitor indicators including compressor run-time ratios, defrost cycle frequency and duration, discharge pressure trends, suction pressure trends, condenser and evaporator temperature differentials, and power consumption patterns. When the model predicts a probability of failure exceeding 70% within the next 72 hours, it must automatically generate a maintenance alert classified by predicted failure type (compressor, controller, fan motor, refrigerant leak, electrical). The alert must include the predicted remaining useful life, recommended maintenance action, estimated repair duration, and whether the container cargo should be transferred to a backup unit. Predictive alerts must be routed to the maintenance planning team at the container's current or next port of call. The model accuracy must be validated quarterly against actual failure data with a target precision of 80% and recall of 75% across all reefer unit types in the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-002-R017	FEAT-024-1-002	MOD-024	Multi-Entity Consolidated Monitoring View	The system must support a consolidated monitoring view allowing regional operations managers to view reefer container status across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) simultaneously on a single dashboard. The consolidated view must show total monitored containers per entity, alarm counts by severity, top ten critical containers requiring immediate attention, and key performance indicators including average temperature compliance rate and mean time to alarm response. The dashboard must display data in the user's selected currency for any financial metrics and must support toggling between Gregorian and Hijri calendar views for date-based aggregations. Access to the consolidated view must be restricted to users with cross-entity authorization roles, and data access must comply with each entity's data residency requirements, meaning that while aggregated metrics can be displayed centrally, drill-down to individual container data must be served from the entity's local data center. The view must render within 3 seconds even when displaying data from all entities concurrently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R001	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Work Order Generation	The system must automatically generate Pre-Trip Inspection (PTI) work orders when a reefer container is allocated to a confirmed booking. The work order must be created a minimum configurable lead time before the booking gate-in date (default 72 hours for Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, 48 hours for JNPT due to faster turnaround requirements). Each work order must specify the required inspection type based on the cargo requirements: standard PTI for general reefer cargo, enhanced PTI for pharmaceutical shipments including calibration verification, and CA-PTI for controlled atmosphere shipments including gas-tightness testing. The work order must include the booking set-point temperature, required humidity range, ventilation settings, and any special instructions from the shipper's commodity declaration. Work orders must be assigned to certified reefer technicians based on their qualification level, current workload, and physical location within the terminal, optimized by the AI scheduling engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R002	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Digital PTI Checklist Execution	The system must provide a comprehensive digital PTI checklist accessible on ruggedized tablets and mobile devices used by reefer technicians in the yard. The checklist must include all inspection points as defined by the Institute of International Container Lessors (IICL) Reefer Inspection guidelines including external container condition (walls, floor, roof, doors, door gaskets), refrigeration unit visual inspection (compressor, condenser, evaporator, fans, drain), electrical system checks (power cable condition, plug integrity, controller display), and operational performance tests (cool-down test to set-point, defrost cycle test, fresh air ventilation operation). Each checklist item must support pass/fail/not-applicable status, free-text remarks in English or Arabic, and photographic evidence capture directly from the device camera. The checklist must enforce a mandatory completion sequence preventing technicians from skipping critical safety items such as electrical insulation resistance tests.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R003	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Temperature Pull-Down Test Automation	The system must automate the temperature pull-down test by setting the reefer unit to the booking-specified set-point temperature and monitoring the cool-down curve against expected performance benchmarks. The system must record supply air temperature at 5-minute intervals during the pull-down period and calculate the time required to reach within 1°C of the set-point from ambient temperature. The measured pull-down time must be compared against the manufacturer's specification for the unit type (e.g., Carrier PrimeLINE should reach -25°C from +38°C ambient in under 6 hours). If the actual pull-down time exceeds the benchmark by more than 20%, the system must flag the unit for enhanced mechanical inspection and potentially reject it for the booking. The ambient temperature at the start of the test must be recorded, with adjustments made for Gulf region ports where ambient temperatures may exceed 50°C, which naturally extends pull-down times. All pull-down test data must be stored and linked to the PTI record and the container's maintenance history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R001	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Pre-Load Survey Request Creation	The system must allow authorized users to create pre-load cargo survey requests by specifying the booking reference, container number (ISO 6346 format), commodity description, HS code, declared cargo weight, port of loading (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), and requested survey date/time. The form must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date selection for KSA and Qatar entities. Each request must be linked to a specific entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) and auto-assign the applicable currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR/USD) based on entity configuration. The system must validate that the survey request date falls within the vessel's cargo cut-off window and reject requests that fall outside this window with a clear error message referencing the cut-off date and time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R004	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	AI-Assisted Visual Defect Detection	The system must integrate with camera-based AI visual inspection stations positioned at the PTI yard entrance to automatically detect external defects on reefer containers. The computer vision model must identify defects including dents deeper than 25mm, corrosion patches larger than 100cm², damaged door gaskets with visible gaps, cracked or missing T-bar floor sections, damaged or illegible CSC safety approval plates, and refrigeration unit external damage such as bent condenser fins or damaged fan guards. Each detected defect must be classified by severity (cosmetic, minor, major, critical) according to IICL damage criteria and automatically populated into the PTI checklist. Critical defects (e.g., compromised structural integrity or refrigerant leak indicators) must immediately flag the container as PTI-failed and block booking allocation. The AI model must achieve a minimum detection accuracy of 92% for major and critical defects, validated quarterly using manually inspected ground-truth datasets from each operating terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R005	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Pass/Fail Decision Workflow	The system must implement a structured PTI pass/fail decision workflow with the following outcomes: Pass (container meets all requirements and is approved for cargo loading), Conditional Pass (minor defects identified that do not affect cargo integrity, with repair to be completed before next use), Fail-Repairable (significant defects that can be repaired on-site, container returned to maintenance workshop with estimated repair time), and Fail-Rejected (unit has irreparable defects or performance issues, must be replaced with alternative equipment). For each outcome, the system must automatically trigger downstream actions: Pass triggers status update in booking module and gate-in authorization; Conditional Pass triggers a maintenance work order for post-voyage repair; Fail-Repairable triggers immediate equipment substitution search and booking re-allocation; Fail-Rejected triggers container flagging for surveyor inspection and potential insurance claim. All PTI decisions must be digitally signed by the inspecting technician and countersigned by a supervisor for Fail outcomes, with the approval workflow supporting both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R006	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Certificate Generation and Storage	Upon successful PTI completion (Pass or Conditional Pass), the system must generate a digital PTI certificate containing the container number, inspection date and time in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, inspecting technician name and certification number, all checklist items with their status, pull-down test results including ambient start temperature and time to set-point, photographs of the container exterior and refrigeration unit data plate, any defects noted with severity classification, and the overall PTI outcome. The certificate must be digitally signed and stored with a unique certificate number cross-referenced to the booking and container asset records. PTI certificates must be retrievable by container number, booking reference, or certificate number with search response time under 2 seconds. Certificates must be generated in English with Arabic translation available on demand. The system must retain PTI certificates for a minimum of five years with immutable audit trails preventing post-issuance modification. Certificates must be shareable via secure link with shipper representatives upon request.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R007	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Technician Qualification and Certification Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of reefer technicians authorized to perform PTI inspections, tracking their qualification levels, manufacturer-specific certifications (Carrier, Daikin, Thermo King), certification expiry dates, and inspection performance metrics. Technicians must be classified into tiers: Level 1 for basic PTI on standard reefer units, Level 2 for enhanced PTI including CA system testing, and Level 3 for pharmaceutical and specialized cargo PTI. The system must prevent assignment of PTI work orders to technicians whose certifications have expired or who lack the required tier for the inspection type. Certification renewal reminders must be generated 60 days before expiry and escalated to the maintenance supervisor if not renewed within 30 days of expiry. The system must track each technician's inspection throughput, average inspection duration, defect detection rate, and post-PTI failure rate (containers that pass PTI but experience issues during the voyage). Performance metrics must be viewable by maintenance supervisors with drill-down to individual inspection records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R008	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Scheduling and Resource Optimization	The AI scheduling engine must optimize PTI scheduling across available technicians, inspection bays, and power outlets to maximize throughput while meeting booking deadlines. The optimizer must consider technician availability shifts (including prayer time breaks for GCC operations), inspection bay capacity at each terminal (e.g., Jebel Ali may have 20 bays while Hamad Port has 8), estimated inspection duration based on container type and inspection tier, and the priority of the associated booking (express bookings receiving higher scheduling priority). The system must generate a visual schedule (Gantt chart format) showing planned PTI activities for the next 7 days, rendereable in both LTR and RTL layouts. When a PTI fails and a replacement container requires inspection, the scheduler must automatically insert an expedited PTI slot and notify the affected technician. The system must achieve a scheduling efficiency target of 85% bay utilization during peak hours and must predict daily PTI workload volumes using historical booking patterns and seasonal commodity trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R009	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Spare Parts Inventory Integration for PTI Repairs	The system must integrate with the spare parts inventory module to check availability of common reefer repair parts when a PTI identifies repairable defects. Common parts include door gaskets, power cables, controller boards, fan motors, and compressor valves. When a PTI Fail-Repairable outcome is recorded, the system must automatically check parts availability at the local warehouse and, if parts are not available locally, search inventory at other entity locations (e.g., if Hamad Port lacks a Carrier controller board, check Jebel Ali stock). Parts reservation must be automatically created against the repair work order, reducing available inventory count. The system must track parts consumption per PTI repair for cost allocation to the container owner (shipping line or leasing company) and must generate parts usage reports aggregated by port, defect type, and container manufacturer. Low stock alerts must be generated when any critical spare part quantity falls below the configured minimum level, with reorder suggestions based on AI-predicted consumption rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R010	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Third-Party Surveyor PTI Integration	The system must support integration with third-party surveyor companies who perform independent PTI inspections on behalf of shipping lines or cargo insurers. Surveyors must be able to access a dedicated portal to view assigned PTI requests, execute inspection checklists with the same digital tools available to internal technicians, and submit their findings for review. The system must support divergent PTI outcomes where the third-party surveyor's assessment differs from the internal PTI result, triggering an automatic dispute resolution workflow requiring senior maintenance manager adjudication. Surveyor access must be restricted to only the specific containers they are authorized to inspect with time-limited access tokens expiring 24 hours after the scheduled inspection. Surveyor companies must be registered in the vendor master with their accreditation details, insurance coverage, and performance ratings. All surveyor interactions must be logged for audit purposes, and surveyor reports must be stored alongside internal PTI records with clear provenance marking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R011	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Cost Tracking and Billing	The system must track all costs associated with PTI activities including technician labor hours, parts consumed, power consumption during pull-down tests, and inspection bay rental charges. Costs must be allocated to the appropriate cost center based on the container ownership: shipping line-owned containers charged to the line's account, leased containers charged per the lease agreement terms, and shipper-owned containers charged to the shipper's booking account. The system must generate PTI cost invoices in the entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with conversion to the customer's preferred billing currency using the treasury module's exchange rates. VAT and GST must be applied according to entity-specific tax rules. PTI cost reports must be available showing average PTI cost per container by type, port, and period, supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. For failed PTI requiring equipment substitution, the additional costs of the replacement PTI must be tracked and reported separately to enable recovery from the defective container's owner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R012	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Data Analytics and Fleet Health Scoring	The system must analyze PTI outcomes across the entire reefer fleet to generate health scores for individual containers, container batches (by manufacturer and build year), and the overall fleet. The fleet health score must be calculated based on PTI pass rates, average defect counts per inspection, severity distribution of defects found, pull-down test performance trends, and the frequency of repeat defects. The AI engine must identify containers with deteriorating health scores and recommend preemptive withdrawal from service before they fail a PTI with cargo allocated, which would cause booking disruptions. Fleet health analytics must be segmented by container owner (shipping line), manufacturer, age band (0-5 years, 5-10 years, 10-15 years, 15+ years), and home port. The system must generate quarterly fleet health reports for each shipping line customer showing their equipment performance compared to fleet averages, supporting commercial discussions about equipment upgrade investments. Dashboard visualizations must support both English and Arabic with exportable charts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R013	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Refrigerant Leak Detection and Environmental Compliance	During PTI, the system must record refrigerant charge levels and flag units showing refrigerant loss exceeding 10% of rated capacity, indicating potential leaks. For containers using HFC refrigerants (R-134a, R-404A) subject to Kigali Amendment phase-down schedules, the system must track cumulative refrigerant top-up quantities per container for F-gas regulation reporting. The system must maintain a refrigerant tracking register per entity recording all refrigerant additions, recoveries, and disposals with technician identification and date stamps. For containers transitioning to lower-GWP refrigerants (R-452A, R-513A), the system must verify technician certification for handling the specific refrigerant type. Environmental compliance reports must be generatable per entity showing total refrigerant consumption, leak rates, and estimated CO2-equivalent emissions. For UAE operations, reports must align with the UAE Ministry of Climate Change reporting format. The system must alert when a container's cumulative refrigerant loss in a 12-month period exceeds regulatory thresholds, recommending unit retirement or major overhaul.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R014	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Mobile Offline PTI Execution	The system must support full PTI checklist execution in offline mode on mobile devices for scenarios where yard Wi-Fi coverage is inadequate, particularly common in large terminal yards at JNPT and during new terminal construction phases. The offline mode must pre-download the assigned PTI work orders, digital checklists with all inspection point definitions, reference images showing acceptable versus defective conditions, and the container's last known maintenance history. Technicians must be able to complete the entire inspection including checklist marking, photo capture, remarks entry in English or Arabic, and pull-down test data recording without network connectivity. Upon reconnection, the completed PTI data must synchronize to the central system with conflict detection in case the same container was reassigned during the offline period. Offline PTI records must be clearly time-stamped with both the local execution time and the synchronization time, and the system must validate data integrity using checksums to ensure no data corruption occurred during offline storage on the mobile device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R015	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	PTI Compliance Audit Trail	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for all PTI activities including work order creation, checklist completion, pass/fail decisions, supervisor approvals, certificate generation, and any subsequent modifications or disputes. The audit trail must capture the user identity, timestamp in UTC, action performed, previous state, new state, and the IP address or device identifier from which the action was performed. Audit records must be tamper-proof using cryptographic hashing where each record includes a hash of the previous record creating a chain of integrity. The audit trail must be searchable by container number, technician, date range, PTI outcome, and entity. For regulatory inspections by port authorities (Qatar Ports Management Company, Abu Dhabi Ports, Saudi Ports Authority, Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority), the system must generate comprehensive audit reports showing the complete PTI history for any specified container or time period. The system must support data export in formats required by each port authority and must provide read-only access grants to authorized regulatory inspectors with time-limited credentials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-003-R016	FEAT-024-1-003	MOD-024	Automated PTI Scheduling Based on Container Lifecycle	The system must automatically schedule PTI inspections based on configurable container lifecycle rules beyond booking-triggered PTIs. These include periodic PTIs for containers sitting idle in depot for more than 30 days to ensure readiness, post-repair PTIs after any maintenance work on the refrigeration system, re-PTI requirements when a container's previous PTI certificate is older than the shipping line's maximum PTI validity period (typically 30 to 90 days depending on the line), and mandatory PTI before any repositioning movement of empty reefer containers between terminals. The lifecycle rules must be configurable per container owner (shipping line) to accommodate varying standards, for example one line may require re-PTI after 30 days idle while another accepts 60 days. The system must calculate the optimal PTI timing considering upcoming booking demand forecasts from the AI engine, preferring to schedule PTIs during low-demand periods to minimize peak-time bay congestion. Lifecycle-triggered PTI work orders must be distinguishable from booking-triggered PTIs in reporting and must carry a lower scheduling priority unless a booking is imminent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R001	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Terminal Reefer Plug Inventory Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive inventory of all reefer electrical plug points across each terminal including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each plug point must be registered with its unique identifier, physical location coordinates within the yard grid (block, row, tier, slot), electrical specifications (voltage: 380V/440V/460V, phase: 3-phase, frequency: 50Hz or 60Hz), maximum amperage rating, current operational status (available, occupied, out of service, under maintenance), and the date of last electrical safety inspection. The inventory must support different plug types including standard CEE/IEC 60309 industrial plugs and terminal-specific configurations. The system must track plug point lifecycle including installation date, cumulative operating hours, and maintenance history. Plug points marked as out of service must include the reason and estimated return-to-service date. The inventory must be visually represented on an interactive yard map with color-coded status indicators, supporting both English and Arabic label rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R002	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	AI-Optimized Plug Allocation Algorithm	The AI engine must automatically allocate reefer containers to optimal plug points when they enter the terminal yard, considering multiple optimization criteria: minimizing travel distance from gate-in point to allocated slot, grouping containers by vessel to facilitate efficient loading sequences, prioritizing containers with critical cargo (pharmaceuticals, high-value perishables) for plug points with backup power supply coverage, avoiding allocation to plug points with recent intermittent fault history, and balancing electrical load across transformer zones to prevent circuit overload. The algorithm must also consider the container's planned dwell time and departure sequence to minimize re-handling when containers on the same vessel are stacked together. Allocation decisions must be made within 30 seconds of the container entering the terminal and communicated to the yard tractor driver via the terminal operating system integration. When no optimal plug is available, the system must queue the container and provide the yard planner with reallocation suggestions including which currently-plugged containers could be relocated based on their cargo temperature criticality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R003	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Real-Time Power Load Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the electrical power load across the terminal's reefer power distribution infrastructure at multiple levels: individual plug point, distribution panel, transformer, and total terminal reefer power capacity. Real-time power consumption data must be collected from smart meters at minimum 1-minute intervals and displayed on a power management dashboard showing current load in kilowatts, load factor as a percentage of rated capacity, power factor, and voltage quality metrics (voltage deviation, harmonic distortion). The system must generate alerts when any distribution panel exceeds 80% of rated capacity (warning) or 90% (critical), enabling proactive load redistribution before circuit breakers trip. For terminals experiencing peak load during summer months in the Gulf region (June-September) when ambient temperatures exceed 45°C and reefer units draw maximum power, the system must activate demand management protocols such as staggering defrost cycles across reefer units on the same circuit. Power quality data must be logged for analysis of trends that may indicate infrastructure degradation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R004	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Power Interruption Detection and Response	The system must detect power interruptions to reefer containers within 60 seconds of occurrence through a combination of IoT sensor heartbeat monitoring and smart meter disconnect signals. Upon detecting a power interruption, the system must immediately classify the interruption as planned (scheduled maintenance window) or unplanned, and determine the scope (single plug, distribution panel, transformer zone, or terminal-wide). For unplanned interruptions affecting containers with active cargo, the system must calculate the estimated time before cargo temperature exits the safe range based on the commodity's thermal inertia model, ambient temperature, and container insulation rating. Containers whose cargo is estimated to exceed safe temperature limits within 4 hours must be flagged as priority for emergency power restoration or physical relocation to an energized plug point. The system must automatically notify the terminal electrical team, the reefer operations supervisor, and affected cargo owners via the multi-channel alert system. Post-interruption, the system must log the total unplugged duration per container and generate a power interruption report for SLA compliance monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R005	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Vessel Reefer Plug Planning	The system must support reefer plug planning for vessel stowage by interfacing with the vessel stowage planning module to ensure that reefer containers are placed in bays with adequate power supply capacity. For each vessel call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must retrieve the vessel's reefer plug configuration including total reefer plugs per bay, electrical rating per circuit, and any known defective plugs reported by the vessel operator. The stowage recommendation engine must ensure that the total power demand of reefer containers planned for a bay does not exceed the bay's electrical capacity, considering that deep-frozen containers (-18°C and below) typically draw 7-10 kW while chilled containers draw 4-6 kW. The system must also verify that high-amperage units (e.g., CA reefers with gas injection systems) are allocated to plugs rated for their peak draw. Any mismatch between planned reefer load and vessel capacity must generate a pre-stowage alert to the vessel planner with recommendations for redistribution across bays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R006	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Generator Backup Power Management	The system must manage backup generator sets (gensets) available at each terminal for providing temporary power to reefer containers during planned electrical maintenance windows, grid power outages, or when plug demand exceeds fixed infrastructure capacity. The genset inventory must track each unit's capacity in kVA, fuel type, current fuel level, maintenance status, and physical location. When backup power is required, the AI engine must calculate the number of gensets needed based on the total load of affected reefer containers, deploy gensets to optimal locations minimizing cable run distances, and schedule fuel replenishment based on predicted runtime. The system must track genset operating hours per deployment for maintenance scheduling (oil changes every 250 hours, major service every 1,000 hours) and fuel consumption for cost allocation. Genset deployment costs must be billable to the appropriate party: terminal operator for infrastructure maintenance windows, shipping line for vessel-related delays, or force majeure allocation for grid outages. Cost calculations must use the entity's base currency with conversion available to customer billing currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R007	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Plug Point Maintenance Scheduling	The system must manage preventive and corrective maintenance schedules for all reefer plug points across managed terminals. Preventive maintenance must be scheduled based on configurable intervals: electrical safety testing (insulation resistance, earth continuity, RCD trip test) every 6 months, visual inspection and cleaning every 3 months, and thermal imaging survey annually. The scheduling engine must optimize maintenance windows to minimize reefer plug point downtime, preferring low-occupancy periods identified through historical demand patterns and upcoming vessel schedule analysis. When a plug point requires corrective maintenance (reported fault or failed inspection), the system must automatically update its status to out-of-service, trigger container relocation for any currently-connected reefer, and generate a maintenance work order with priority based on the number of bookings that will require the plug point within the next 72 hours. Maintenance completion must trigger a return-to-service inspection workflow, and the plug point must only be marked available after passing all post-maintenance checks. All maintenance records must be stored for regulatory compliance with local electrical safety standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R008	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Reefer Yard Capacity Forecasting	The AI engine must forecast reefer plug demand for each terminal on a daily basis for a rolling 14-day horizon, using inputs including confirmed booking volumes, historical seasonal patterns (e.g., higher reefer demand during Ramadan food import season, Indian mango export season April-June, Saudi date harvest August-October), vessel schedule changes, and current plug inventory status. The forecast must predict peak concurrent reefer plug occupancy for each day and compare against available capacity to identify potential shortfall dates. When predicted demand exceeds 90% of available capacity, the system must automatically recommend mitigation actions such as expediting outbound reefer container dispatch, activating overflow yard areas, deploying mobile genset power banks, or negotiating with vessel operators to advance or delay vessel calls. Forecasts must be presented on a dashboard with confidence intervals and must be recalculated every 4 hours as booking data changes. Forecast accuracy must be tracked weekly with a target mean absolute percentage error below 15%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R009	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Plug-In and Plug-Out Event Tracking	The system must record every plug-in and plug-out event for each reefer container with precise timestamps, capturing the plug point identifier, container number, technician or automated system that performed the connection, initial power draw reading, and the container's temperature at the time of connection. Plug-out events must additionally record the reason for disconnection (departure for vessel loading, relocation to different slot, maintenance, or unplanned interruption) and the container's temperature at disconnection. The total plugged-in duration must be calculated per container per terminal visit for power consumption billing purposes. Events must be captured automatically through smart plug integration where available, or through manual technician input via mobile devices for terminals without smart plug infrastructure (applicable to certain berths at JNPT during infrastructure upgrade phase). The system must flag containers that remain unplugged for more than 30 minutes during their terminal stay when they contain temperature-sensitive cargo, generating immediate alerts to the reefer monitoring team. All event data must be exportable for billing reconciliation and SLA reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R010	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Electrical Safety Compliance Management	The system must enforce electrical safety compliance for all reefer plug operations in accordance with local electrical codes: Qatar KAHRAMAA regulations, UAE ADDC/DEWA standards, KSA SEC regulations, and India CEA (Central Electricity Authority) rules. Before a container is connected to a plug point, the system must verify that the container's power cable has a valid inspection sticker (not older than the entity-specific maximum validity period), the plug point has passed its most recent safety inspection, and the connection is compatible (voltage, phase, frequency matching). The system must maintain records of all electrical safety inspections including test results for insulation resistance (minimum 1 megaohm), earth continuity (maximum 0.5 ohm), and residual current device functionality. Safety inspection certificates must be generated with bilingual content (English and Arabic) and stored for the retention period mandated by each jurisdiction. The system must block plug-in operations for containers or plug points with expired safety certifications and generate work orders for re-inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R011	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Power Consumption Billing Integration	The system must calculate reefer power consumption charges per container based on metered kWh consumption multiplied by the applicable tariff rate. Tariff rates must be configurable per entity and may vary by time of day (peak/off-peak for terminals with time-of-use electricity pricing, particularly JNPT which follows MSEDCL tariff structures), season (summer surcharge for Gulf terminals), and customer contract terms (volume discount tiers). The system must generate itemized power consumption invoices showing each container's plugged-in duration, total kWh consumed, applicable tariff rate, and calculated charge in the billing currency. For containers where smart metering data is unavailable, the system must apply estimated consumption based on the container type, set-point temperature, and ambient temperature using standardized consumption models. Power charges must be posted to the customer's account in the accounts receivable module with proper tax calculations (VAT for UAE and KSA, GST for India). The system must support power charge disputes where customers can challenge metered readings, triggering a review workflow with meter calibration verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R012	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Renewable Energy Integration Tracking	The system must track the proportion of reefer power consumption sourced from renewable energy installations at each terminal, supporting sustainability reporting and carbon footprint calculations. For terminals with solar panel arrays or other renewable installations (Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali have active solar programs), the system must calculate the renewable energy fraction applied to reefer operations based on the terminal's overall renewable mix. Customers must be able to view the estimated carbon footprint of their reefer operations per container in kg CO2-equivalent, calculated using entity-specific grid emission factors and renewable energy offset data. The system must generate sustainability certificates for customers who opt into green energy programs showing verified renewable energy usage for their reefer containers. Reports must align with GCC sustainability frameworks and India's Bureau of Energy Efficiency reporting standards. The system must support carbon credit tracking where applicable, allocating renewable energy benefits proportionally across reefer customers based on their consumption share, with financial values displayed in the customer's preferred currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R013	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Emergency Power Priority Matrix	The system must maintain a configurable priority matrix for reefer power allocation during emergency situations such as partial grid failures or generator capacity constraints where not all reefer containers can be simultaneously powered. The priority matrix must rank containers based on cargo criticality (pharmaceutical and life-saving supplies highest, followed by frozen goods, chilled goods, and controlled ambient lowest), remaining thermal buffer time (containers closest to exceeding safe temperature range prioritized), cargo value (insurance declared value as a secondary ranking factor within the same criticality class), and contractual SLA commitments with shipping lines. During an emergency, the AI engine must generate an optimized power allocation plan that maximizes the number of high-priority containers maintained within safe temperature ranges given the available power capacity. The plan must be presented to the operations manager for approval within 5 minutes of emergency declaration, with container-level recommendations for which units to keep powered and which can safely be temporarily unplugged. The emergency protocol must also include notification templates in English and Arabic for communicating with affected cargo owners.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R014	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Yard Map Visualization with Plug Status	The system must provide an interactive yard map visualization showing the physical layout of reefer plug points overlaid on the terminal yard grid. Each plug point must be represented by an icon whose color indicates its current status: green for available, blue for occupied with container in normal temperature range, amber for occupied with container showing temperature deviation, red for out of service or power fault, and grey for under maintenance. Clicking on a plug point must show a detail panel with the plug specifications, currently connected container details (if occupied) including temperature reading, booking reference, and planned departure time. The yard map must support zoom levels from full terminal overview to individual block detail and must include layer toggles for showing electrical infrastructure (transformer zones, distribution panel boundaries, cable routes). The map must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts with Arabic labels when the user's language preference is Arabic. The visualization must update in near-real-time (maximum 60-second refresh) and must perform smoothly on standard desktop browsers even for large terminals with over 2,000 plug points like Jebel Ali.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R015	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Cross-Terminal Plug Utilization Reporting	The system must generate cross-terminal plug utilization reports comparing reefer plug usage metrics across all managed terminals (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT). Reports must include metrics such as average daily plug occupancy rate, peak utilization percentage, average plugged-in duration per container, power consumption per TEU-day, plug point availability rate (excluding maintenance downtime), and mean time to plug-in after container gate-in. Reports must support time-period comparisons (month-over-month, year-over-year) and must benchmark each terminal against configurable target KPIs. The AI engine must identify terminals with consistently high utilization (above 85%) and recommend infrastructure expansion investments with estimated cost-benefit analysis. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with charts and tables rendering correctly in both English and Arabic, and in Excel format for further analysis. Financial metrics in reports must be presentable in any of the five supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) using the selected reporting date's exchange rates. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation and distribution to configured recipients on daily, weekly, or monthly cadences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R016	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Smart Plug IoT Device Management	The system must manage the fleet of smart plug IoT devices deployed at terminal reefer plug points, tracking each device's firmware version, hardware model, communication status (online, offline, intermittent), last successful data transmission timestamp, and battery level for wireless units. The system must support over-the-air firmware updates for smart plug devices, allowing updates to be scheduled during low-activity periods and rolled out progressively (configurable percentage of devices per batch, default 10% per batch with 24-hour monitoring between batches) to minimize operational risk. Device health monitoring must detect and alert on devices showing communication failures for more than 10 consecutive minutes, measurement drift compared to adjacent devices, or hardware error codes reported through the device diagnostic protocol. The system must maintain a device lifecycle log from commissioning through decommissioning, including all firmware updates applied, calibration events, and maintenance interventions. Device inventory reports must be filterable by terminal, device model, firmware version, and health status to support fleet management decisions and budget planning for device replacement cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R008	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Customs cold chain declaration automation	The system must auto-generate customs cold chain declarations for import and export clearance at all operating ports (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP). Declarations must include the continuous temperature record summary, any temperature exceedance events with timestamps and corrective actions taken, commodity-specific storage requirement compliance confirmation, and container pre-trip inspection (PTI) results. For Qatar customs (QAHMD), the system must format declarations per Qatar General Authority of Customs electronic submission standards. For India customs (INJNP), declarations must comply with ICEGATE electronic filing format. The system must calculate and display all customs duties and fees in the local currency of the destination port (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with real-time exchange rate conversion from USD base rates. Declarations must be printable in A4 format with Arabic or English selectable as primary language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-1-004-R017	FEAT-024-1-004	MOD-024	Reefer Container Pre-Cooling Power Reservation	The system must support advance reservation of reefer plug points for containers requiring pre-cooling before cargo stuffing at the terminal. When a booking specifies that the container must be pre-cooled to a target temperature before the shipper delivers cargo for stuffing, the system must reserve a plug point for the estimated pre-cooling duration (calculated based on the target temperature, expected ambient temperature, and container type cooling performance data). The reservation must block the plug point from allocation to other containers during the reserved window, and the AI scheduler must account for these reservations in its capacity planning calculations. If the container arrives later than the reserved slot, the system must attempt to reallocate to the next available plug and adjust the estimated pre-cooling completion time, notifying the shipper of any delay to the cargo stuffing appointment. Pre-cooling power consumption must be tracked separately from regular storage power consumption for billing purposes, as some entities apply different tariff rates for pre-cooling services. The pre-cooling completion event (container reaching target temperature) must be automatically detected by the temperature monitoring system and trigger notification to the shipper that the container is ready for stuffing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R001	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Cold chain document template registry	The system must maintain a configurable registry of cold chain document templates mapped to each regulatory authority across operating jurisdictions: Qatar MOPH (Ministry of Public Health), UAE Municipality / SFDA, KSA SFDA (Saudi Food and Drug Authority), and India FSSAI (Food Safety and Standards Authority of India). Each template must support bilingual content in Arabic and English with proper RTL/LTR rendering. Templates must be versioned with effective dates tracked in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. The registry must support at minimum 50 distinct document types including phytosanitary certificates, veterinary health certificates, certificate of origin for perishables, temperature compliance declarations, and fumigation certificates. Each template must be tagged by commodity type (frozen meat, fresh produce, dairy, pharmaceuticals, chemicals) and applicable port codes (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNP for JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R002	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	AI-powered document completeness validation	The system must provide AI-driven validation of cold chain documents before submission to regulatory authorities, checking for completeness, consistency, and compliance with destination country requirements. Validation must cross-reference the container booking details (container number, seal number, BL number, commodity description, HS code) against the submitted documents to detect mismatches. For pharmaceutical shipments under GDP (Good Distribution Practice), the system must verify that WHO temperature guidelines are referenced. The AI engine must achieve a minimum 97% accuracy rate in detecting documentation gaps and must flag missing fields, inconsistent dates, mismatched commodity codes, and absent regulatory stamps. Validation results must be presented in both Arabic and English with severity levels (critical, warning, informational) and must include corrective action suggestions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R003	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Automated phytosanitary certificate generation	The system must auto-generate phytosanitary certificate requests for fresh produce and plant-based cargo shipments based on booking data, commodity classification, and destination port requirements. For shipments arriving at QAHMD (Hamad Port) from Indian ports like INJNP (JNPT), the system must pre-populate Qatar MOPH phytosanitary requirements including pest-free area declarations and treatment records. The generation engine must support ISPM-15 (International Standards for Phytosanitary Measures) wood packaging compliance declarations. Generated certificates must include QR codes linking to the digital verification portal, support dual-language output in Arabic and English, and carry digital signatures from authorized personnel. The system must track certificate validity periods and send renewal alerts 72 hours before expiration for multi-leg shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R004	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Temperature log digitization and archival	The system must digitize and archive continuous temperature logs from reefer container data loggers (Ryan, Emerson, Sensitech, Carrier Transicold) in a tamper-proof digital repository. Each log record must capture timestamp (UTC and local timezone), temperature reading (Celsius and Fahrenheit), humidity percentage, GPS coordinates, container number, booking reference, and BL number. The archival system must retain records for a minimum of 7 years as required by SFDA and FSSAI regulations. Logs must be searchable by container number, voyage, date range, commodity type, and temperature threshold breaches. The system must support bulk import of USB data logger downloads and real-time IoT streaming ingestion. Archived logs must be exportable in PDF, CSV, and XML formats with digital certification of authenticity for use in regulatory audits and cargo claim disputes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R005	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Regulatory submission workflow with approval chain	The system must implement a multi-step approval workflow for regulatory document submissions, supporting role-based approval chains that vary by document type, commodity classification, and destination port authority. For high-risk commodities (pharmaceuticals, live cultures, restricted chemicals), the workflow must require a minimum of three approval levels: operations officer, compliance manager, and authorized signatory. Each approval step must capture the approver identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, digital signature, and optional comments in Arabic or English. The workflow must enforce SLA timers configurable per document type (default 4 hours for standard, 2 hours for urgent) with automatic escalation to the next authority level upon SLA breach. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all workflow actions and support delegation of approval authority during planned absences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R006	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Multi-entity compliance matrix management	The system must maintain a compliance requirements matrix that maps regulatory obligations across all operating entities: Qatar entity (QAR-denominated operations under Qatar Commercial Companies Law), UAE entity (AED-denominated operations under UAE Federal Law), KSA entity (SAR-denominated operations under Saudi Companies Law), and India entity (INR-denominated operations under Indian Companies Act). Each entity must have distinct compliance profiles reflecting local cold chain regulations, permitted commodity lists, and documentation requirements. The matrix must track which documents are mandatory versus optional per entity-port-commodity combination. For cross-entity shipments (e.g., transshipment from Jebel Ali AEJEA to Hamad Port QAHMD), the system must identify the union of all applicable compliance requirements from both origin and destination entities and present a consolidated checklist to the operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R009	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	GDP compliance tracking for pharmaceutical reefers	The system must implement WHO GDP (Good Distribution Practice) compliance tracking for pharmaceutical reefer shipments including temperature-sensitive vaccines, biologics, and insulin. GDP tracking must enforce 2-8°C storage range for standard pharmaceuticals and -20°C to -70°C for ultra-cold chain vaccines with zero tolerance for exceedance. Each pharmaceutical reefer must have a documented qualification protocol including temperature mapping, alarm verification, and backup power validation. The system must generate GDP-compliant shipping reports that include mean kinetic temperature (MKT) calculations, time-temperature integration curves, and deviation impact assessments. For shipments between India (INJNP) and Gulf ports, the system must track compliance with both Indian CDSCO (Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation) export requirements and destination country pharmaceutical import regulations. All GDP records must be retained for a minimum of 5 years with full traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R010	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Offline documentation capture with sync	The system must support offline capture of cold chain documentation in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, commonly encountered at remote port yards, vessel holds during loading/discharge, and inland container depots. Offline mode must allow operations staff to photograph physical documents using mobile devices, enter key metadata fields (document type, container number, BL reference, commodity code), and record temperature readings manually. All offline-captured data must be stored locally on the device with AES-256 encryption and automatically synchronized to the central system upon connectivity restoration. The sync mechanism must handle conflict resolution when the same document has been updated by multiple users, applying a last-write-wins strategy with full conflict audit logging. Offline mode must support a minimum of 500 document captures before requiring sync, and must display clear visual indicators (in both Arabic and English UI) showing sync status and pending upload count.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R011	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Automated compliance audit report generation	The system must generate comprehensive compliance audit reports on demand and on a scheduled basis (daily, weekly, monthly) summarizing the cold chain documentation status across all operating entities. Reports must include metrics such as: total reefer shipments processed, documentation completeness percentage per port, average document processing time, regulatory submission success rate, rejected documents with rejection reasons, and overdue document counts. Reports must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port code, commodity type, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection), and compliance status. The AI engine must provide trend analysis identifying recurring documentation gaps and recommending process improvements. Reports must be generated in PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic RTL formatting when Arabic language is selected, and must include charts and visual dashboards showing compliance KPIs against configurable target thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R012	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Digital certificate of origin for perishables	The system must manage electronic certificates of origin (eCO) for perishable cargo shipments, integrating with chambers of commerce electronic platforms in Qatar (Qatar Chamber), UAE (Dubai Chamber, Abu Dhabi Chamber), KSA (Saudi Chamber), and India (FIEO/DGFT). The eCO module must auto-populate origin details from booking data including exporter information, consignee details, commodity description with HS codes, country of origin, and INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, CFR, DDP as applicable). For preferential trade agreements (GCC common market, India-UAE CEPA, India-GCC FTA), the system must identify applicable tariff preferences and flag when a certificate of origin could reduce customs duties. The module must track eCO application status through issuance lifecycle stages (draft, submitted, under review, approved, rejected) with estimated processing times. All monetary values on certificates must display in the transaction currency with USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R013	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Fumigation and treatment certificate management	The system must track fumigation and pest treatment certificates required for reefer containers carrying fresh produce, grains, and plant-based products. Certificate records must capture treatment type (methyl bromide, phosphine, heat treatment, irradiation), treatment date and duration, chemical concentration levels, treatment facility certification number, and ISPM-15 compliance status for wooden packaging. For imports into KSA, the system must validate that fumigation certificates meet SFDA and Ministry of Environment requirements including approved fumigant types and maximum residue levels. For imports into Qatar, MOPH approved treatment protocols must be referenced. The system must alert operations 24 hours before a fumigation certificate expires for containers awaiting customs clearance. Where re-fumigation is required due to delays, the system must generate a re-treatment order with cost estimates in the applicable local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and track the re-treatment workflow to completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R014	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Blockchain-anchored document integrity verification	The system must anchor critical cold chain document hashes to a blockchain or distributed ledger to provide tamper-proof integrity verification for regulatory audits and dispute resolution. Documents eligible for blockchain anchoring include temperature logs, health certificates, phytosanitary certificates, customs declarations, and GDP compliance reports. Each anchored document must have a unique hash generated using SHA-256 algorithm, an anchoring timestamp, and a verification URL that allows third parties (customs authorities, insurance companies, cargo claimants) to independently verify document authenticity. The system must support batch anchoring to optimize transaction costs, processing a minimum of 1000 document hashes per batch. Verification must be accessible via a public-facing portal supporting Arabic and English interfaces without requiring authentication. The anchoring history must be displayed in the document detail view showing chain of custody from creation through all modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R015	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	AI-driven document classification and extraction	The system must employ AI-powered optical character recognition (OCR) and natural language processing (NLP) to automatically classify uploaded cold chain documents and extract key data fields. The AI engine must support document recognition in Arabic, English, Hindi, and Urdu languages commonly encountered in shipping documentation across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India trade lanes. Classification accuracy must exceed 95% for the top 20 document types including health certificates, phytosanitary certificates, temperature logs, customs declarations, and bills of lading. Extracted fields must include document number, issuing authority, dates, commodity descriptions, container numbers, temperature ranges, and regulatory reference numbers. The system must present extracted data for human verification with confidence scores per field, highlighting low-confidence extractions (below 85%) in amber for manual review. Verified extractions must feed back into the AI training pipeline to continuously improve accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-001-R016	FEAT-024-2-001	MOD-024	Regulatory change notification and impact assessment	The system must monitor and notify users of changes to cold chain regulations across all operating jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) that impact reefer cargo documentation requirements. Notification sources must include SFDA regulatory bulletins, MOPH circulars, FSSAI notifications, and port authority advisories from QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, and INJNP. Each notification must include an AI-generated impact assessment identifying affected commodity types, impacted trade lanes, required documentation changes, and a recommended compliance timeline. Notifications must be delivered via in-app alerts (supporting Arabic RTL and English LTR display), email, and SMS. The system must maintain a regulatory change log with effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. For high-impact regulatory changes (new banned substances, modified temperature requirements, additional certification mandates), the system must automatically update document templates and validation rules upon administrator approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R001	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Real-time reefer malfunction detection engine	The system must continuously monitor reefer container telemetry data to detect mechanical and electrical malfunctions in real time. Detection algorithms must identify anomalies across key parameters: compressor discharge pressure, suction pressure, evaporator fan speed, condenser fan operation, defrost cycle timing, refrigerant charge level, supply air temperature, return air temperature, and power consumption. The engine must support monitoring of all major reefer unit manufacturers (Carrier Transicold PrimeLINE, Daikin, Thermo King Magnum Plus, Star Cool by MCI). Malfunction detection must achieve less than 5-minute latency from anomaly occurrence to alert generation. The system must distinguish between critical failures (compressor seizure, total power loss, refrigerant leak) requiring immediate intervention and degraded performance conditions (reduced cooling capacity, intermittent fan operation) allowing scheduled maintenance. Detection thresholds must be configurable per commodity type and container age.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R002	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Automated breakdown severity classification	The system must automatically classify reefer breakdowns into severity levels using AI analysis of telemetry patterns, ambient conditions, cargo type, and remaining voyage time. Severity levels must include: Critical (P1) for total cooling loss with perishable cargo at risk of spoilage within 4 hours, High (P2) for significant cooling degradation with cargo risk within 12 hours, Medium (P3) for partial system failures with cargo safe for 24+ hours, and Low (P4) for minor issues not affecting cargo temperature. Classification must account for the specific commodity's temperature sensitivity — frozen meat at -18°C has different urgency than fresh produce at +2°C. The AI classifier must consider external factors including ambient temperature at the container location (desert temperatures in Qatar/KSA may exceed 50°C), humidity levels, and whether the container is on-vessel, at-port, or in-transit by road. Classification accuracy must be validated quarterly against actual cargo outcome data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R003	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Emergency response team dispatch workflow	The system must implement an automated dispatch workflow for reefer emergency response teams based on breakdown severity, container location, and available technician resources. For breakdowns at Hamad Port (QAHMD), the system must route to Qatar-based reefer technician teams with estimated response times. For Jebel Ali (AEJEA) and Khalifa Port (AEKHL), UAE-based teams must be dispatched. For JNPT (INJNP), India-based teams must be mobilized. The dispatch workflow must include: automatic work order generation with breakdown details, technician skill matching (Carrier-certified, Daikin-certified, Thermo King-certified), GPS-based nearest available technician identification, estimated time of arrival calculation, and customer notification. Dispatch orders must display repair cost estimates in the local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent. The workflow must support manual override by operations supervisors for reprioritization and must log all dispatch decisions with timestamps for SLA tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R004	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Cargo salvage decision support system	The system must provide AI-driven decision support for cargo salvage scenarios when reefer breakdowns cannot be repaired within safe temperature windows. The decision engine must evaluate: current cargo core temperature versus commodity-specific spoilage thresholds, estimated time to repair completion, availability of substitute reefer containers at the current location, cost of cargo transfer versus potential spoilage loss, and insurance coverage implications. For pharmaceutical cargo under GDP requirements, the system must apply zero-tolerance rules and recommend immediate transfer to a qualified backup unit. The system must generate salvage action recommendations ranked by cost-effectiveness including: transfer to backup reefer container, temporary cold storage facility placement, emergency truck dispatch to nearest cold chain facility, or cargo condemnation with insurance claim initiation. All salvage cost estimates must be calculated in the applicable transaction currency and must account for INCOTERMS to determine which party bears the financial responsibility (CIF versus FOB versus DDP).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R005	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Spare parts inventory integration for reefer repairs	The system must integrate with spare parts inventory systems at each operating port to verify parts availability before dispatching repair technicians. The parts catalog must cover common reefer failure components including compressors, condensers, evaporator coils, expansion valves, refrigerant (R-134a, R-404A, R-452A), electronic controllers, temperature sensors, fan motors, and power cable assemblies. Inventory must be tracked per port location (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP) with real-time stock levels, reorder points, and lead times from OEM suppliers. When a breakdown is diagnosed, the system must automatically check local parts availability and, if unavailable, search adjacent port inventories and calculate inter-port transfer time and cost. Parts costs must be maintained in multi-currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion at daily exchange rates. The system must track parts consumption trends using AI to predict future demand and generate procurement recommendations to prevent stockouts of critical components.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R008	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Regulatory Compliance Document Generation	The system must automatically generate all required regulatory compliance documents for OOG cargo acceptance at each port jurisdiction. For Hamad Port, this includes Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani) special cargo permits. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, this includes DP World and Abu Dhabi Ports OOG declarations respectively. For JNPT, this includes Indian Maritime Administration OOG annexures. Each document must be generated in the jurisdiction's required language (Arabic for Qatar/UAE/KSA, English for India, bilingual where mandated). The system must pre-populate all dimensional data, equipment details, and shipper information from the booking record. Documents must be digitally signed using the entity's authorized certificate and transmitted to port community systems via EDI or API as configured per port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R006	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Power supply failure management for reefer stacks	The system must detect and manage power supply failures affecting reefer container stacks at port terminals and on vessels. Detection must identify individual container power disconnections, reefer rack bus bar failures, generator outages affecting multiple containers, and shore power interruptions during vessel berth operations. For multi-container power failures at Hamad Port (QAHMD) or Jebel Ali (AEJEA), the system must prioritize power restoration based on cargo value, commodity temperature sensitivity, and time-to-spoilage calculations. The system must maintain a backup power allocation plan that maps available generator capacity to critical reefer containers. Power failure events must be logged with start time, end time, duration, number of containers affected, ambient temperature during outage, and estimated cargo temperature rise rate. The system must generate power failure impact reports within 30 minutes of resolution, including temperature trend charts for all affected containers, to support insurance claims and customer notifications. Reports must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R007	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Breakdown root cause analysis with AI diagnostics	The system must perform AI-powered root cause analysis for each reefer breakdown event using historical telemetry data, maintenance records, container age, manufacturer defect bulletins, and environmental conditions. The AI diagnostic engine must identify probable root causes from a taxonomy including: compressor mechanical failure, refrigerant undercharge or leak, dirty condenser coils, frozen evaporator, defective electronic controller, power supply voltage irregularity, door seal deterioration, and insulation damage. Diagnostic confidence levels must be provided for each probable cause. The system must correlate breakdown patterns across the reefer fleet to identify systemic issues such as batch defects in specific container manufacturing lots or recurring failures in specific trade lane conditions (e.g., high humidity Gulf routes versus temperate Indian Ocean legs). Root cause analysis reports must be generated within 2 hours of breakdown resolution and must include preventive maintenance recommendations. Reports must support Arabic and English output with technical terminology glossaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R008	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Vendor and third-party repair service management	The system must maintain a registry of approved third-party reefer repair vendors at each operating port and inland location, tracking vendor certifications (OEM-authorized for Carrier, Daikin, Thermo King), service level agreements, response time commitments, hourly labor rates, and performance ratings. Vendor contracts must be stored with commercial terms in the applicable local currency (QAR for Qatar vendors, AED for UAE vendors, SAR for KSA vendors, INR for India vendors). The system must support automated vendor selection based on breakdown location, required expertise, SLA compliance history, and cost competitiveness. Purchase orders for repair services must be generated automatically upon dispatch approval, with multi-level authorization thresholds (e.g., repairs under 5000 USD equivalent auto-approved, above 5000 USD requiring manager approval). Vendor performance must be scored monthly on metrics including response time adherence, first-time fix rate, customer satisfaction, and billing accuracy. Low-performing vendors must be flagged for contract review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R009	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Container substitution workflow for irreparable units	The system must implement a container substitution workflow when a reefer unit is deemed irreparable within the required timeframe. The workflow must search available empty reefer containers at the current location matching the required specifications: container size (20ft or 40ft HC), temperature capability range, controlled atmosphere capability if needed, and USDA/EU/halal certification status. The substitution workflow must automate: cargo transfer work order generation, updated booking and BL amendment notifications to all parties (shipper, consignee, customs broker, shipping line), temperature continuity documentation showing chain of custody during transfer, and updated customs declarations. Transfer costs must be calculated including crane lifts, labor, and any waiting time charges, presented in the local currency with USD equivalent. The system must update all downstream systems including vessel stowage plans, terminal operating system, and customs manifests. For CIF and DDP shipments, the system must identify who bears the substitution cost per the applicable INCOTERMS.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R010	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Emergency response SLA monitoring and escalation	The system must track response time SLAs for every reefer breakdown event from detection through resolution, with configurable SLA targets per severity level and per port location. Default SLA targets must be: P1 Critical — technician on-site within 1 hour, resolution within 4 hours; P2 High — technician on-site within 2 hours, resolution within 8 hours; P3 Medium — technician on-site within 4 hours, resolution within 24 hours; P4 Low — scheduled within 48 hours. The system must implement automatic escalation when SLA milestones are at risk: first escalation to shift supervisor at 75% of SLA elapsed, second escalation to operations manager at 90%, and third escalation to country director at SLA breach. Escalation notifications must be delivered via push notification, SMS, and email in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). SLA performance dashboards must display real-time and historical metrics by port, severity level, technician team, and vendor, with drill-down capability to individual breakdown event timelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R011	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Breakdown event communication hub	The system must provide a centralized communication hub for each reefer breakdown event, enabling real-time collaboration between all stakeholders including terminal operations, vessel operations, reefer technicians, cargo owners, insurance surveyors, and port authority representatives. The hub must support threaded messaging in Arabic and English with automatic language detection and optional AI-powered translation between the two languages. All communications must be timestamped and stored as part of the breakdown event record for audit and dispute resolution purposes. The hub must support file attachments including photographs of damage, repair work in progress, temperature logger screenshots, and technician diagnostic reports. Push notifications must alert relevant stakeholders when new messages are posted or when breakdown status changes. For P1 Critical breakdowns, the system must automatically initiate a group notification to all pre-configured stakeholders within 5 minutes of detection. The communication hub must function in low-bandwidth environments common at remote yard areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R012	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Insurance notification and survey coordination	The system must automatically notify cargo insurance providers when a reefer breakdown event meets configurable notification thresholds (default: any P1 or P2 event, or any event with temperature exceedance exceeding 2 hours). Insurance notifications must include: container number, cargo description with HS code, insured value in the policy currency (typically USD), breakdown type and severity, current temperature readings, estimated time-to-repair, and preliminary cargo condition assessment. The system must coordinate insurance surveyor appointments by sharing breakdown event access with surveyor firms, providing real-time container location and access instructions per port (gate procedures for QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP). Survey reports must be uploaded to the breakdown event record and linked to subsequent cargo claim files. For multi-entity shipments, the system must identify the insuring entity and route notifications to the correct insurance policy based on the entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India) responsible under the booking terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R013	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Preventive maintenance scheduling based on breakdown data	The system must use breakdown event data to continuously update and optimize preventive maintenance schedules for the reefer container fleet. AI analysis must identify containers with increasing failure frequency, components approaching end-of-life based on operating hours and environmental stress (Gulf summer heat cycles degrade components faster than temperate routes), and maintenance intervals that should be shortened or extended based on actual reliability data. Preventive maintenance work orders must be auto-generated and scheduled during container idle periods at operating ports, targeting completion before the next loaded voyage. The scheduling engine must consider port-specific maintenance facility availability and technician capacity. Maintenance costs must be tracked per container, per component category, and per operating entity in the respective local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with consolidated fleet-level reporting in USD. The system must calculate total cost of ownership per reefer container and flag units where cumulative maintenance costs exceed economically justified thresholds for replacement consideration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R014	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Hazardous refrigerant leak detection and safety protocol	The system must implement specialized handling protocols for refrigerant leak events, which pose environmental and safety hazards in addition to cargo temperature risks. When a refrigerant leak is detected through pressure sensor anomalies or IoT gas detection sensors, the system must automatically trigger safety protocols including: hazard zone demarcation notifications to terminal operations, personal protective equipment (PPE) requirements for responding technicians, environmental reporting obligations under Qatar Ministry of Environment, UAE MOCC&E, KSA PME, and Indian CPCB regulations, and refrigerant recovery requirements under Montreal Protocol / Kigali Amendment. The system must track refrigerant gas type (R-134a, R-404A, R-407C, R-452A, R-744 CO2) and leaked quantity estimates for environmental compliance reporting. Leak events must be logged with GPS coordinates and reported to the applicable port authority safety department. Safety incident reports must be generated in Arabic and English within 24 hours of the event, with regulatory submission tracking per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R015	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Breakdown cost tracking and financial allocation	The system must capture all costs associated with each reefer breakdown event including technician labor hours, spare parts consumed, crane and equipment hire for container moves, temporary cold storage charges, cargo transfer costs, emergency generator fuel, and third-party vendor invoices. Costs must be recorded in the local currency of the port where the breakdown occurred (QAR at QAHMD, AED at AEJEA/AEKHL, INR at INJNP) with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated reporting. The system must allocate breakdown costs to the responsible party based on contractual terms: shipping line for equipment defects, terminal operator for power supply failures, cargo owner for pre-existing cargo conditions, and third parties for external damage. Cost allocation must reference applicable INCOTERMS and container interchange agreements. Monthly and quarterly breakdown cost summary reports must be generated per entity, per port, and per cost category, supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selection. The system must calculate breakdown cost as a percentage of reefer revenue to track profitability impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R016	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Offline breakdown reporting for vessel operations	The system must support offline reefer breakdown reporting for containers aboard vessels at sea where internet connectivity is unavailable or limited to satellite bandwidth. Vessel reefer technicians must be able to record breakdown details including container number, unit manufacturer and model, fault code, visual inspection findings, temperature readings (supply air, return air, cargo probe), repair actions attempted, and parts used. The offline module must include a fault code library for major reefer manufacturers (Carrier, Daikin, Thermo King, Star Cool) accessible without connectivity. Photographs of the malfunction must be capturable and stored locally with AES-256 encryption. Upon vessel reaching port or satellite connectivity restoration, all offline breakdown reports must automatically synchronize with the central system, triggering appropriate escalation workflows if unresolved breakdowns are detected. The offline interface must support Arabic and English with an intuitive touch-friendly design suitable for use with protective gloves in cold environments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-002-R017	FEAT-024-2-002	MOD-024	Emergency reefer genset deployment tracking	The system must track the deployment and availability of emergency diesel generator sets (gensets) used to power reefer containers during breakdowns, port power outages, and inland transport. Genset inventory must be maintained per location (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP, and key inland depots) with specifications including power output capacity (number of reefer plugs supported), fuel tank capacity, current fuel level, last maintenance date, and operational status. When a power-related reefer emergency occurs, the system must automatically identify the nearest available genset with sufficient capacity, generate a deployment work order, and track genset connection to the affected containers. Fuel consumption must be monitored and refueling alerts generated at 25% remaining capacity. Genset operating costs (fuel, maintenance, operator labor) must be tracked in the local currency and allocated to the appropriate cost center or customer account. The system must maintain genset utilization reports to support fleet sizing decisions and capital expenditure planning across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R001	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Configurable temperature threshold profiles per commodity	The system must maintain configurable temperature threshold profiles for each commodity type transported in reefer containers, with profiles mapped to HS code classifications. Profiles must specify acceptable temperature range (minimum and maximum in Celsius), critical deviation threshold (degrees above or below acceptable range triggering immediate escalation), warning threshold (early warning before critical breach), acceptable temperature fluctuation rate (degrees per hour), and humidity range where applicable. Standard profiles must be pre-configured for common commodities including frozen meat (-18°C to -22°C), fresh produce (+1°C to +4°C), dairy products (+2°C to +6°C), pharmaceuticals (+2°C to +8°C for standard, -20°C for frozen biologics, -70°C for mRNA vaccines), chocolate (+12°C to +18°C), and fresh flowers (+0.5°C to +2°C). Profiles must be overridable per shipment based on shipper instructions. Each profile must be auditable with version history and effective date tracking in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R002	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	AI-predictive temperature trend analysis	The system must employ AI/ML models to predict temperature trends inside reefer containers based on current telemetry data, historical patterns, ambient weather conditions at the container location, and reefer unit performance characteristics. The predictive engine must forecast container internal temperature for the next 1, 4, 12, and 24-hour windows with confidence intervals. When the prediction indicates a probable temperature exceedance within the forecast window, the system must generate a pre-emptive alert enabling operations teams to take corrective action before an actual breach occurs. The model must account for external factors including ambient temperature (critical in Gulf summer where external temperatures at ports like QAHMD and AEJEA can exceed 50°C), solar radiation on container surfaces, container position in stack (top tier versus ground level), and whether the container is plugged into shore power versus operating on vessel power. Prediction accuracy must be validated monthly with a target of 90% accuracy for 4-hour forecasts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R003	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Multi-tier escalation matrix configuration	The system must support configuration of multi-tier escalation matrices that define notification recipients, communication channels, and response requirements for temperature exceedance events at each severity level. The escalation matrix must support a minimum of 5 tiers with configurable time intervals between escalations. Each tier must specify: role-based recipients (reefer monitoring operator, shift supervisor, operations manager, country director, regional VP), individual named contacts, notification channels (in-app alert, email, SMS, voice call, WhatsApp), required acknowledgment within a specified timeframe, and required response actions. Escalation matrices must be configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), per port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), per commodity category, and per customer priority level. The system must support time-of-day routing so that after-hours escalations go to on-call personnel. Matrix configurations must require dual-authorization to modify, preventing unauthorized changes to critical alert routing. All matrix changes must be logged with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R004	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Real-time temperature exceedance alert generation	The system must generate real-time alerts within 60 seconds of detecting a temperature exceedance relative to the configured commodity threshold profile. Each alert must contain: container number, booking reference, BL number, commodity description, set temperature, actual temperature reading, deviation magnitude, duration of exceedance so far, container location (GPS coordinates and port/terminal/bay/row/tier reference), reefer unit manufacturer and model, current reefer unit operating status, ambient temperature, and the specific threshold profile that was breached. Alerts must be formatted for display in both Arabic and English with the recipient's preferred language applied automatically. For containers on vessels at sea, alerts must include vessel name, voyage number, estimated time of arrival at next port, and vessel reefer technician contact details. The system must suppress duplicate alerts for the same ongoing exceedance event but must generate follow-up alerts at configurable intervals (default every 30 minutes) showing the updated temperature trend if the exceedance persists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R005	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Stakeholder-specific notification content customization	The system must customize alert notification content based on the recipient's role and information needs. Operations team members must receive full technical details including reefer unit diagnostics, recommended corrective actions, and nearby technician availability. Customer-facing notifications to shippers and consignees must use simplified language appropriate for cargo owners, omitting internal operational details but including cargo condition assessment, expected impact, and actions being taken. Insurance company notifications must include policy-relevant information such as insured value, temperature deviation magnitude and duration, and preliminary damage probability. Regulatory authority notifications (for pharmaceutical cargo) must include GDP compliance status and mean kinetic temperature calculations. Each notification template must support Arabic and English bilingual formatting with proper RTL layout for Arabic. Notification templates must be configurable per entity and per customer, allowing key accounts to receive customized communication formats. The system must track notification delivery and read receipts across all channels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R006	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Temperature exceedance event lifecycle management	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of each temperature exceedance event from initial detection through resolution and post-event analysis. Event states must include: Detected (temperature threshold breached), Acknowledged (operator has seen the alert), Investigating (root cause being determined), Action In Progress (corrective action underway), Resolved (temperature restored to acceptable range), and Closed (post-event documentation completed). State transitions must be timestamped and attributed to the user performing the action. The system must enforce that P1 Critical events cannot be closed without documented root cause, corrective action taken, cargo condition assessment, and supervisor sign-off. Average time in each state must be tracked for SLA reporting. Events that remain in Detected or Acknowledged state beyond configurable time limits must automatically escalate to the next tier. The system must prevent premature closure of events where temperature has temporarily recovered but the AI predicts continued instability. All event lifecycle data must be available for reporting in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R007	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Geofence-based alert rule adjustment	The system must support geofence-based dynamic adjustment of temperature alert rules based on container location within the supply chain. Different alert thresholds and escalation paths must be configurable for containers in the following zones: at origin port loading area, aboard vessel at sea, at transshipment port (e.g., containers transshipping through Jebel Ali AEJEA from India INJNP to Qatar QAHMD), at destination port discharge area, in customs inspection hold, and during last-mile inland transport. For containers passing through extreme heat zones (Gulf region ports during summer months May-September), the system must apply adjusted thresholds that account for normal temperature spikes during plug-in/plug-out operations and stack repositioning. Geofences must be configurable per port using GPS polygon coordinates covering terminal yards, quay areas, inspection zones, and gate areas. The system must automatically switch alert rules as the container crosses geofence boundaries. Geofence alert rule configurations must be auditable with change history and must support multi-entity configurations where different entities have jurisdiction over different geofenced zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R008	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Cumulative temperature deviation tracking	The system must track cumulative temperature deviation across the entire shipment journey, calculating total degree-hours of exceedance from origin to destination. Cumulative tracking must compute: total time outside acceptable range, maximum deviation peak, mean kinetic temperature (MKT) using the Arrhenius equation for pharmaceutical cargo, total degree-hours above upper threshold, total degree-hours below lower threshold, and number of separate exceedance events. For commodities with regulatory shelf-life implications (frozen food under Codex Alimentarius, pharmaceuticals under WHO GDP), the system must calculate remaining shelf-life reduction based on cumulative deviation. Cumulative deviation reports must be generated automatically upon container arrival at destination port and must be formatted for regulatory submission to SFDA (KSA), MOPH (Qatar), FSSAI (India), and UAE Municipality. Reports must display all monetary impacts (estimated cargo value reduction) in the shipment's commercial currency with conversion to local destination currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) at the applicable exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R009	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Voice call escalation for critical alerts	The system must implement automated voice call escalation for P1 Critical temperature exceedance events that remain unacknowledged after the configured SMS and push notification timeouts (default 10 minutes). Voice calls must deliver a pre-recorded or text-to-speech message in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) summarizing the alert details: container number, location, commodity type, current temperature, and required action. The voice system must support interactive response via DTMF (keypad) input allowing the recipient to acknowledge the alert (press 1), request 15-minute callback (press 2), or escalate immediately to next tier (press 3). If the voice call goes unanswered after 3 attempts spaced 2 minutes apart, the system must automatically escalate to the next tier in the escalation matrix. Voice call records must be logged including call time, duration, recipient response, and DTMF input. The system must support local phone numbers for Qatar (+974), UAE (+971), KSA (+966), and India (+91) to maximize answer rates by avoiding international number rejection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R010	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Temperature alert dashboard with real-time map visualization	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying all active temperature exceedance events on an interactive map overlaid with port terminal layouts for QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, and INJNP. Each active alert must be represented by a color-coded marker (red for P1 Critical, orange for P2 High, yellow for P3 Medium, blue for P4 Low) positioned at the container's current GPS location or last known terminal position (bay-row-tier). The dashboard must support filtering by severity level, commodity type, customer, vessel, and event status. A sidebar panel must display a scrollable list of active alerts sorted by severity and time, with one-click access to full event details. The map must update container positions every 60 seconds for shore-based monitoring and every 15 minutes for vessel-based tracking via satellite. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with Arabic map labels and place names when Arabic language is selected. Historical playback mode must allow operators to replay temperature events over a selectable time range to support post-event analysis. Dashboard access must be role-based with configurable view permissions per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R011	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Customer self-service temperature monitoring portal	The system must provide a customer-facing self-service portal allowing shippers and consignees to monitor the temperature status of their reefer shipments in real time. The portal must display current temperature, set temperature, temperature trend graph for the past 24 hours, active alerts if any, and shipment milestone timeline. Customers must be able to configure their own secondary alert thresholds (tighter than or equal to the operational thresholds) and receive notifications via email or SMS. The portal must support Arabic and English language selection with full RTL support for Arabic interface elements. Customer access must be scoped to their own shipments only, identified by booking reference or BL number, with authentication via email-based OTP or SSO integration. The portal must be responsive for mobile device access and must function with degraded features (static temperature snapshot updated hourly) when real-time streaming is unavailable. Temperature data displayed must align with the data available in the operational system with a maximum delay of 5 minutes. The portal must include a feedback mechanism for customers to report perceived temperature issues not yet detected by automated monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R012	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Alert fatigue prevention through intelligent suppression	The system must implement intelligent alert suppression mechanisms to prevent operator alert fatigue while ensuring critical events are never missed. Suppression rules must include: deduplication of alerts for the same container within a configurable window (default 30 minutes) for non-critical events, correlation of related alerts (e.g., multiple containers on the same reefer rack experiencing simultaneous exceedances due to a common power failure should be grouped into a single parent event), and suppression of known transient events (brief temperature spikes during container repositioning or plug-in operations typically lasting under 15 minutes). The system must learn suppression patterns from operator actions — if operators consistently dismiss certain alert types within seconds, the AI must recommend new suppression rules for administrator review. Alert suppression must never apply to P1 Critical events or pharmaceutical cargo alerts. The system must maintain suppression audit logs showing which alerts were suppressed, the rule applied, and provide monthly suppression analytics reports showing suppression rate, false positive rate, and any suppressed alerts that should not have been suppressed based on outcome data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R013	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Regulatory temperature compliance reporting	The system must generate regulatory-compliant temperature reports for submission to food safety and pharmaceutical authorities across all operating jurisdictions. For SFDA (KSA), reports must comply with the SFDA imported food control system requirements including continuous temperature records from port of origin to final delivery. For MOPH (Qatar), reports must meet Qatar food safety standards for cold chain verification. For FSSAI (India), reports must satisfy the Food Safety and Standards (Food Recall Procedure) Regulations temperature documentation requirements. For UAE Municipality and ESMA, reports must comply with UAE.S GSO standards for chilled and frozen food transport. Each report must include: shipper and consignee details, container number, complete temperature log with timestamps in local timezone, any exceedance events with duration and magnitude, corrective actions taken, and a compliance declaration. Reports must be generated in the required language (Arabic for Gulf authorities, English for FSSAI) and formatted per each authority's submission template. The system must track submission deadlines and send reminders 48 hours before due dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R014	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Multi-sensor data fusion for alert accuracy	The system must fuse data from multiple temperature sensors within each reefer container to improve alert accuracy and reduce false positives. Data sources must include: supply air temperature sensor, return air temperature sensor, cargo probe temperature sensor (when installed), container door sensor (open/closed status), and external IoT sensors affixed to cargo pallets. The fusion algorithm must weigh sensor readings based on reliability — cargo probe readings are most representative of actual cargo temperature, while supply air readings indicate reefer unit output. When sensor readings diverge significantly (more than 3°C difference between supply and return air), the system must generate a sensor diagnostic alert separate from the temperature exceedance alert. For containers equipped with multi-zone monitoring (partitioned reefer containers), the system must track and alert independently for each temperature zone. The system must detect and flag faulty sensors that report static readings (stuck sensor), readings outside physically possible ranges, or readings that contradict correlated sensors, preventing faulty sensor data from triggering false exceedance alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R015	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Exceedance event correlation with supply chain milestones	The system must automatically correlate temperature exceedance events with supply chain milestones to identify patterns and root causes. Milestones to correlate against include: container gate-in at origin terminal, container loading onto vessel, vessel departure, transshipment operations (common at Jebel Ali AEJEA for India-Qatar cargo), vessel arrival at destination port, container discharge from vessel, customs inspection (containers may be unplugged during physical examination at ports like QAHMD and INJNP), container gate-out for delivery, and last-mile transport to consignee warehouse. The system must generate pattern reports showing frequency of exceedances at each milestone stage, enabling operations to identify systemic issues such as: prolonged plug-out durations during vessel loading at specific terminals, consistent temperature rises during customs inspections at particular ports, or inadequate pre-cooling before container stuffing at origin. Pattern reports must be filterable by trade lane (e.g., INJNP to QAHMD), commodity type, shipping line, terminal operator, and time period with Gregorian and Hijri calendar support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R016	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Automated corrective action recommendation engine	The system must provide AI-generated corrective action recommendations when a temperature exceedance alert is generated, based on the specific exceedance characteristics, container status, and available resources. Recommendations must include actionable steps ranked by effectiveness and feasibility, such as: adjusting the reefer set point (if the deviation is minor and within operating parameters), checking for obstructed airflow or improperly stowed cargo (if return air temperature is elevated while supply air is normal), dispatching a technician for reefer unit inspection (if compressor performance metrics indicate degradation), moving the container to a shaded position (if solar gain is a contributing factor at Gulf ports during summer), or initiating emergency cargo transfer (if the reefer unit has failed completely). Each recommendation must include an estimated time to implement, probability of resolving the exceedance, and estimated cost in the local port currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must track which recommendations were implemented and their actual outcomes to continuously improve the recommendation engine through reinforcement learning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-003-R017	FEAT-024-2-003	MOD-024	Integration with vessel reefer monitoring systems	The system must integrate with vessel-based reefer monitoring systems (Emerson RMS, Carrier USDA RMS, Daikin Active CA, and proprietary shipping line systems like Maersk RCM and MSC Reefer Management) to receive temperature data for containers while aboard vessels. Integration must support data receipt via API polling, SFTP file exchange, and satellite-based IoT platforms (Orbcomm, TRAXENS, Nexxiot). For vessels with limited satellite bandwidth, the system must support compressed data formats that prioritize exceedance events over normal readings. Data latency tolerance must be configurable per commodity type — pharmaceutical shipments requiring near-real-time monitoring (15-minute intervals) versus frozen goods acceptable at 1-hour intervals. The integration must handle data format normalization across different vessel systems, converting proprietary temperature scales, timestamp formats, and container identification schemes to the system's standard format. When vessel data indicates an exceedance on a container destined for one of the operating ports (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), the system must pre-alert the destination port operations team to prepare intervention resources before vessel arrival.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R001	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Historical cargo claim data repository	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical repository of all reefer cargo claims across all operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), storing claim records with full details including: claim number, claimant (shipper, consignee, or insurance subrogation), container number, booking reference, BL number, voyage details, commodity type with HS code, claimed damage description, claim amount in original currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), settlement amount and currency, root cause classification (temperature exceedance, mechanical failure, power outage, pre-shipment condition, documentation deficiency, handling damage), liability determination (carrier, terminal, shipper, third party), and resolution timeline. The repository must support data import from legacy systems and must be searchable across all fields with advanced filtering by entity, port, trade lane, commodity category, claim value range, root cause, and date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. Data retention must comply with the longest applicable statute of limitations across all jurisdictions (minimum 6 years).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R002	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	AI-powered claim risk scoring for active shipments	The system must calculate real-time claim risk scores for every active reefer shipment using an AI model trained on historical claim data, current temperature telemetry, operational conditions, and shipment characteristics. Risk scores must be expressed as a percentage probability (0-100%) of a claim arising from the shipment, updated hourly throughout the shipment lifecycle. The scoring model must consider factors including: commodity temperature sensitivity, current and historical temperature deviation events, reefer unit age and maintenance history, trade lane claim history, season (Gulf summer months carry higher risk), transit duration, number of transshipment operations, customs inspection likelihood at destination port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), and shipper/consignee claim filing history. Shipments scoring above a configurable threshold (default 70%) must trigger proactive intervention alerts to operations teams. The model must be retrained quarterly with new claim outcome data and must maintain explainability by providing the top 5 contributing risk factors for each score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R003	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Claim pattern identification and trend analysis	The system must perform automated pattern analysis across the historical claims dataset to identify recurring claim trends, emerging risk areas, and systemic operational weaknesses. Analysis dimensions must include: claim frequency and severity trends by trade lane (e.g., INJNP to QAHMD route analysis), seasonal claim patterns (Ramadan period logistics disruptions, Gulf summer peak, Indian monsoon season impacts), commodity-specific claim rates, terminal-specific claim correlation (identifying if certain terminals have higher claim-generating operations), vessel-specific patterns (older vessels or specific shipping lines with higher reefer failure rates), and customer-specific filing patterns. The AI must detect statistical anomalies such as sudden claim spikes on previously stable routes or new claim types not previously observed. Trend reports must be generated monthly in both Arabic and English with data visualizations including heat maps, trend lines, and Pareto charts. Reports must present financial impact in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must provide AI-generated narrative summaries explaining the trends in business context.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R004	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Pre-shipment risk assessment and mitigation recommendations	The system must generate a pre-shipment risk assessment for every reefer booking at the time of confirmation, evaluating the probability of a temperature-related cargo claim based on booking characteristics. Assessment factors must include: commodity sensitivity classification, origin and destination ports (risk profiles for QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), planned routing and transshipment points, total transit duration, season and expected weather conditions en route, allocated container age and maintenance status, and the shipper's historical claim rate. The assessment must output a risk rating (Low, Medium, High, Critical) with specific mitigation recommendations such as: requesting a newer reefer container, adding a backup data logger, scheduling a pre-trip inspection (PTI) with enhanced checklist, inserting additional monitoring checkpoints in the routing, arranging priority discharge at destination, or recommending the shipper obtain enhanced cargo insurance coverage. Mitigation recommendations must include estimated cost in the booking currency and expected risk reduction percentage. All pre-shipment assessments must be stored and later correlated with actual shipment outcomes to validate and improve the assessment model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R005	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Container performance scoring for claim prevention	The system must calculate and maintain a performance score for each reefer container in the fleet based on its operational history, maintenance record, and claim involvement. The scoring algorithm must evaluate: number of temperature exceedance events in the past 12 months, breakdown frequency and severity, mean time between failures, pre-trip inspection pass rate, claim history (number of claims, total claim value), age of the reefer unit, last major component replacement date, and OEM reliability ratings for the specific model. Scores must range from 0 to 100 with classification bands: Excellent (85-100), Good (70-84), Acceptable (55-69), Watch List (40-54), and Restricted (below 40). Containers scoring in the Watch List band must receive enhanced monitoring during loaded voyages. Containers scoring in the Restricted band must be flagged for mandatory maintenance or retirement review. Scores must be recalculated weekly and must be visible during the container allocation process so that booking teams can preferentially assign higher-scoring containers to high-value or sensitive shipments. Score histories must be maintained for fleet management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R006	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Root cause taxonomy and classification engine	The system must implement a standardized root cause taxonomy for reefer cargo claims with AI-assisted classification. The taxonomy must support at minimum four levels of detail: Level 1 (category: mechanical, electrical, operational, environmental, pre-existing), Level 2 (subcategory: e.g., compressor failure, power disconnection, improper set point, extreme ambient heat, pre-shipment cargo condition), Level 3 (specific cause: e.g., compressor valve plate failure, terminal reefer rack circuit breaker trip, shipper set incorrect temperature on booking, container placed in direct sun at QAHMD yard during August, cargo pre-cooled to insufficient temperature at origin), and Level 4 (contributing factors: e.g., deferred maintenance, operator training gap, terminal congestion causing delayed plug-in). The AI classification engine must suggest root cause codes based on the claim description, temperature log analysis, and breakdown event records, achieving a minimum 85% suggestion accuracy. The taxonomy must be maintained by a designated compliance team and must be periodically benchmarked against industry standards including TT Club loss prevention categories and IUMI cargo claims classifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R007	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Financial impact modeling and loss projection	The system must provide financial impact modeling capabilities that project potential cargo claim losses based on current operational trends and risk indicators. The model must calculate projected monthly and quarterly claim costs per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), per port, per trade lane, and per commodity category using time-series forecasting on historical claim data combined with current risk score distributions. Financial projections must be presented in USD for consolidated reporting and in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for entity-level management. The model must support scenario analysis allowing operations managers to simulate the financial impact of operational changes: e.g., what would be the claim cost reduction if average plug-out duration during transshipment at Jebel Ali (AEJEA) were reduced from 45 minutes to 20 minutes, or if all containers above 10 years old were retired. Scenario results must quantify expected annual savings and return on investment for proposed operational improvements. The system must compare actual claim costs against projections monthly and recalibrate the model automatically when actual results deviate by more than 15% from projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R008	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Operational practice benchmarking across entities	The system must benchmark cold chain operational practices across all operating entities and ports to identify best practices and underperforming operations contributing to higher claim rates. Benchmarking metrics must include: average reefer plug-in time after vessel discharge, pre-trip inspection thoroughness scores, temperature monitoring response times, breakdown repair completion times, customs clearance duration for reefer cargo, and last-mile delivery temperature compliance rates. Each metric must be compared across ports (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP) and presented in a comparative dashboard with quartile rankings. The system must identify statistically significant performance gaps where one entity or port performs materially worse than the best-performing counterpart on claim-correlated metrics. For each identified gap, the system must generate an AI-produced improvement recommendation citing the specific practices of the better-performing entity. Benchmarking reports must be generated quarterly in Arabic and English, must be entity-aware to show relevant comparisons, and must include trend lines showing improvement or degradation over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R009	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Shipper behavior analysis for claim prevention	The system must analyze shipper behavior patterns that correlate with higher cargo claim rates, providing insights for targeted shipper engagement and education. Analysis must cover: shipper pre-cooling practices (cargo temperature at container stuffing versus required set point), accuracy of commodity declarations (actual HS code versus declared, impacting temperature requirements), packing quality (cargo stowage affecting airflow within the container), documentation completeness at origin, and shipper responsiveness to pre-shipment risk advisories. The system must score each shipper on a cold chain compliance index (0-100) based on these behavioral factors, with historical trending. Shippers scoring below 50 must be flagged for proactive engagement including cold chain best practice training offers. The system must generate shipper-specific claim risk reports shareable with the commercial team to inform contract negotiations, rate adjustments, and additional surcharge requirements. Reports must be available in Arabic and English and must present claim values in the trade lane's primary commercial currency. The system must protect data privacy by ensuring shipper analytics are only accessible to authorized commercial and operations roles within the responsible entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R010	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Predictive cargo damage probability assessment	The system must calculate real-time cargo damage probability for each active reefer shipment experiencing or having experienced a temperature exceedance, estimating the likelihood and extent of physical cargo damage based on commodity-specific spoilage models. The assessment must use scientific degradation models appropriate to each commodity type: Arrhenius-based shelf-life reduction for pharmaceuticals, microbial growth prediction (using modified Gompertz or Baranyi models) for fresh meat and seafood, ethylene exposure impact for fresh produce, and texture/appearance degradation models for frozen foods. Input variables must include: cumulative time-temperature exposure (degree-hours), commodity initial quality at shipment (if known), packaging type and insulation properties, and humidity levels. The assessment must output: probability of rejection at destination inspection (0-100%), estimated percentage of cargo affected, projected cargo value loss in the shipment's commercial currency, and recommended inspection scope upon arrival. Damage probability assessments must be shared with the destination port operations team 24 hours before vessel arrival to pre-arrange survey and inspection resources.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R011	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Claim prevention KPI dashboard	The system must provide an executive KPI dashboard for claim prevention performance monitoring, accessible to senior management across all operating entities. Dashboard KPIs must include: total claim value (current month, quarter, year) with comparison to prior periods and budget, claim frequency per 1000 reefer TEUs, average claim value, claim-to-revenue ratio per entity, top 5 root causes by value, top 5 trade lanes by claim frequency, proactive intervention success rate (percentage of high-risk shipments where intervention prevented a claim), pre-shipment risk assessment accuracy (predicted versus actual claim outcomes), and claim settlement turnaround time. Each KPI must be displayable at consolidated level (all entities in USD) or drilled down to individual entity level in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The dashboard must support date range selection in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Trend arrows and color-coding must indicate improvement or deterioration against targets. The dashboard must support PDF export for board reporting, with Arabic RTL formatted version available. Role-based access must restrict entity-level data visibility to authorized users of each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R012	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Automated evidence package assembly for claim defense	The system must automatically compile a comprehensive evidence package when a cargo claim is filed, assembling all relevant documentation to support the claim defense. The package must include: complete container temperature log from origin to destination with exceedance events highlighted, pre-trip inspection (PTI) report confirming reefer unit serviceability before loading, container condition report at origin, photographs of cargo at stuffing (if available from shipper), all cold chain compliance documents (health certificates, phytosanitary certificates), breakdown event records and repair logs if any mechanical issues occurred during transit, GPS tracking history showing container movements, power connection logs at all port terminals, customs inspection records, and delivery receipt with consignee remarks. The system must organize the evidence package chronologically and generate a timeline summary narrative in the claim's applicable language (Arabic or English). All documents must be cross-referenced and indexed for easy navigation. The package must identify any gaps in the evidence chain and flag them for operations follow-up before formal claim response. Cost data must be presented in the claim currency with supporting exchange rate documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R009	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Historical OOG Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide an analytics dashboard showing OOG cargo acceptance trends across all four operating entities. Metrics must include total OOG TEU volume by port, acceptance-to-rejection ratio, average overage dimensions by cargo category, surcharge revenue by currency, and AI classification accuracy rates. The dashboard must support date range filtering using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Data must be segmentable by entity, port, trade lane, equipment type, and OOG sub-type. All monetary values must display in the user's preferred currency with conversion rates shown. The dashboard must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with right-aligned charts and Arabic numeral options. Export functionality must support Excel and PDF formats with entity-specific letterheads and branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R013	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Trade lane risk profiling and route optimization	The system must develop and maintain risk profiles for all active reefer trade lanes across the operating network, combining historical claim data with operational and environmental factors to score each route's claim risk. Trade lane profiles must cover at minimum: INJNP to QAHMD, INJNP to AEJEA, INJNP to AEKHL, AEJEA to QAHMD (transshipment), AEJEA to AEKHL, and all reverse routing. Each profile must include: average claim rate per 100 shipments, average claim value, seasonal risk variation (charted monthly), most common root causes, average transit temperature compliance rate, transshipment risk factors, and customs clearance impact. Based on risk profiles, the system must recommend route optimizations such as: alternative transshipment ports with lower claim histories, preferred shipping lines with better reefer fleet reliability, optimal sailing schedules that minimize time in extreme Gulf summer heat, and recommended container age limits for high-risk routes. Route optimization recommendations must include financial impact estimates showing projected claim cost reduction versus any increased freight costs in USD and applicable local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R014	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Terminal operator performance impact on claims	The system must analyze and report the correlation between terminal operator performance metrics and cargo claim rates at each operating port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP). Terminal metrics to analyze must include: average reefer plug-in delay after container discharge from vessel, reefer monitoring check frequency and thoroughness, power outage frequency and duration, container handling incident rates (drops, collisions affecting reefer unit integrity), pre-delivery inspection compliance, and temperature documentation accuracy. The system must calculate a terminal performance score weighted by claim correlation strength, identifying which terminal practices most significantly contribute to or prevent claims. For ports with multiple terminal operators (e.g., DP World and Abu Dhabi Terminals at AEKHL, JNPCT and APMT at INJNP), the system must provide comparative analysis. Terminal performance reports must be generated quarterly in Arabic and English and must be shareable with terminal operators as part of service quality discussions. The system must track terminal performance trends and alert when a terminal's claim-correlated metrics deteriorate beyond statistical control limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R015	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Claim prevention ROI tracking and reporting	The system must track and report the return on investment (ROI) of claim prevention activities, quantifying the financial benefit of proactive interventions versus the cost of implementing them. Tracked activities must include: enhanced reefer monitoring investments (additional IoT sensors, monitoring software subscriptions), fleet maintenance programs (preventive maintenance costs versus breakdown repair costs avoided), operational process improvements (additional staff for faster plug-in, enhanced PTI procedures), training programs for operations and partner staff, and technology investments (AI analytics platform costs). The ROI calculation must compare: baseline claim costs (projected claims without intervention) versus actual claims after intervention, attributing the reduction to specific prevention activities using statistical control methods. ROI reports must be generated quarterly per entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD. The system must support multi-year ROI tracking to demonstrate long-term trends and compound benefits of sustained prevention investments. Reports must include visualizations suitable for executive presentations, available in Arabic and English formats, and must be exportable as PDF and PowerPoint.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R016	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Insurance premium optimization analytics	The system must provide analytics to support cargo insurance premium optimization by demonstrating cold chain risk management maturity to insurance underwriters. The analytics module must generate insurer-ready reports showing: claim frequency trends (demonstrating year-over-year improvement), average claim severity trends, proactive monitoring coverage percentage (percentage of reefer shipments under real-time temperature monitoring), mean time to detect and respond to temperature exceedances, preventive maintenance compliance rates, cold chain documentation completeness scores, and investment in risk mitigation technologies. Reports must benchmark the company's claim performance against industry averages published by IUMI (International Union of Marine Insurance) and TT Club. The system must calculate the estimated premium saving potential based on demonstrated risk improvements, presenting potential savings in the policy currency (typically USD) with equivalent amounts in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The analytics must support insurance renewal preparation by generating pre-formatted loss run reports compatible with major marine cargo insurance platforms. Reports must be exportable in Arabic and English with full audit trail of underlying data sources.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R017	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Machine learning model performance monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the performance of all AI and machine learning models used in cargo claim prevention analytics, ensuring prediction accuracy and model reliability over time. Monitoring must track: claim risk scoring model accuracy (predicted risk versus actual claim outcome), pre-shipment risk assessment calibration (predicted probabilities versus observed frequencies), cargo damage probability model accuracy (predicted damage versus survey results), pattern detection false positive and negative rates, and root cause classification accuracy. The system must implement model drift detection using statistical tests (Population Stability Index, KL divergence) comparing current prediction distributions against baseline distributions, alerting the data science team when drift exceeds configurable thresholds. Model retraining must be triggered automatically when performance metrics fall below minimum thresholds (configurable per model, default 85% accuracy). A model performance dashboard must display all monitored metrics with historical trends, accessible in Arabic and English. The system must maintain a model registry tracking all deployed model versions, training datasets, hyperparameters, and A/B test results, with full lineage traceability for regulatory audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R010	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Shipper Self-Service OOG Declaration Portal	The system must provide a web-based self-service portal allowing shippers and freight forwarders to submit OOG cargo declarations prior to gate-in. The portal must accept dimension inputs with photo or technical drawing uploads, support drag-and-drop file attachment for cargo profiles and MSDS sheets if applicable, and provide instant preliminary AI-based acceptance assessment. The portal must be fully bilingual (Arabic/English) with seamless language switching. Submitted declarations must flow into the OOG acceptance workflow automatically. The portal must authenticate users via the ERP's identity management system and enforce entity-level data isolation—a shipper registered under the Qatar entity must not see UAE entity tariff structures. The portal must function on mobile devices for on-site submissions from warehouses and freight stations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-024-2-004-R018	FEAT-024-2-004	MOD-024	Collaborative claim prevention knowledge base	The system must maintain a searchable knowledge base of claim prevention best practices, lessons learned, case studies, and standard operating procedures accumulated across all operating entities. Knowledge base articles must be categorized by: commodity type, root cause category, port/terminal, trade lane, season, and prevention strategy. Each article must support bilingual content in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic text. The knowledge base must include: post-claim analysis reports with anonymized case details and prevention recommendations, standard operating procedures for high-risk operations (reefer handling during Gulf summer, pharmaceutical cold chain protocols, Ramadan period logistics adjustments), training materials for reefer monitoring operators and technicians, and vendor-specific technical bulletins for reefer unit models in the fleet. The system must implement an AI-powered search and recommendation engine that surfaces relevant knowledge base articles when operators are handling active temperature events or high-risk shipments. Content must be reviewed and approved by designated subject matter experts before publication, with version control and expiry date tracking to ensure currency. Usage analytics must track article views, search queries, and user feedback ratings to continuously improve content relevance and identify knowledge gaps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R001	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	OOG Dimensional Data Capture	The system must capture and store precise cargo dimensions including length, width, height, and gross weight for every OOG shipment declaration. Measurements must support metric (meters, kilograms) and imperial (feet, pounds) units with automatic conversion. Each measurement record must include the measurement source (shipper-declared, surveyor-verified, LiDAR-scanned, or AI-vision-estimated), timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the operator identity. The UI must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts. All dimensional data must persist with full audit trail per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and comply with ISO 668 container dimension standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R002	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	AI-Powered Overage Classification	The system must employ an AI classification engine that automatically categorizes incoming cargo into OOG sub-types: over-height (OH), over-width (OW), over-length (OL), over-weight (OWT), and combined overage (COMBO). Classification must occur within 3 seconds of dimension submission and achieve at least 97% accuracy against historical surveyor determinations. The AI model must account for port-specific gauge envelopes—for example Jebel Ali permits different stack heights than JNPT due to gantry crane clearance profiles. Classification results must be reviewable by operators with one-click override capability and all overrides must be logged for compliance audit across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R003	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Port-Specific Gauge Limit Enforcement	The system must maintain configurable gauge limit profiles for each supported port: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV). Gauge limits must define maximum permissible over-height, over-width, and over-length in centimeters beyond standard 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, and 45HC container envelopes. Each port profile must include rail gauge limits for intermodal transfers, road transport height restrictions for last-mile delivery, and berth-specific crane outreach constraints. When cargo exceeds any limit, the system must block acceptance and generate an exception notification in both Arabic and English to the operations supervisor. Profile updates must require dual authorization from port operations and safety departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R004	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	3D Visualization of OOG Protrusions	The system must generate a real-time 3D visualization showing the cargo item placed on its assigned equipment type (flat rack, open top, or platform) with protrusion zones highlighted in color-coded overlays: red for exceeding port limits, amber for within 10% of limits, and green for within safe margins. The visualization must support rotation, zoom, and cross-section views. It must render on both desktop and tablet interfaces with Arabic RTL text labels for dimension callouts when the user locale is set to Arabic. The 3D model must be exportable as a PDF attachment for inclusion in booking confirmation documents and must be cached locally for offline access at port gate facilities where network connectivity may be intermittent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R005	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	LiDAR and AI Vision Integration	The system must integrate with LiDAR scanning hardware and AI-powered camera vision systems deployed at port gate-in facilities to automatically capture OOG cargo dimensions upon truck arrival. The integration must support MQTT and REST API protocols for real-time data ingestion. Captured measurements must be compared against shipper-declared dimensions with a configurable tolerance threshold (default ±5cm for length/width/height, ±500kg for weight). Discrepancies exceeding the tolerance must trigger an automatic hold on the booking and generate a discrepancy alert to the gate supervisor. The system must store both shipper-declared and physically-measured values for dispute resolution. Integration must function across all four port locations with location-specific calibration profiles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R006	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	OOG Surcharge Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate OOG surcharges based on the degree of overage, equipment type, trade lane, and port pair. Surcharge rules must be configurable per shipping line tariff agreement and must support tiered pricing—for example, over-height up to 30cm at one rate, 31-60cm at a higher rate, and beyond 60cm requiring special quotation. All surcharges must be computed in the booking currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with real-time exchange rate conversion from a configured treasury feed. Surcharge line items must appear on the cargo acceptance confirmation with bilingual Arabic/English descriptions. The engine must support retroactive recalculation when physical measurements differ from initial declarations, generating credit or debit adjustments linked to the original invoice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R007	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Multi-Entity OOG Acceptance Workflow	The system must enforce entity-specific approval workflows for OOG cargo acceptance. Each legal entity (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations) must have independently configurable approval chains with role-based authorization levels. For example, Qatar entity may require safety officer approval for any over-height exceeding 50cm while UAE entity may set that threshold at 40cm. The workflow engine must support sequential and parallel approval steps, automatic escalation after configurable SLA timeouts, and delegation rules for approver absence. All workflow actions must be timestamped in the entity's local timezone and recorded in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats. Cross-entity OOG transshipment cargo must trigger approval workflows in both the discharge and load port entities simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R011	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	OOG Booking Amendment Handling	The system must support amendments to OOG bookings when cargo dimensions change between initial declaration and physical gate-in. Amendment requests must trigger a re-evaluation of equipment suitability, gauge limit compliance, stowage feasibility, and surcharge calculations. If the amended dimensions cause an equipment type change (for example from 40FR to 40OT), the system must check equipment availability at the load port and update the booking accordingly. Amendment history must be maintained with full version tracking showing who changed what and when. Financial impacts of amendments—surcharge increases or decreases—must be automatically calculated in the original booking currency and linked to existing invoices. The system must support bulk amendments for project cargo shipments where multiple OOG units in a single booking require simultaneous updates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R012	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Weight Verification and VGM Compliance	The system must enforce Verified Gross Mass (VGM) requirements per SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 for all OOG shipments. For overweight OOG cargo, the system must validate that the declared VGM does not exceed the maximum payload capacity of the assigned equipment unit (flat rack, open top, or platform) accounting for the tare weight of any securing materials. The system must integrate with weighbridge systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to receive actual weighed VGM values. Discrepancies between declared and weighed VGM exceeding 5% or 1 tonne (whichever is lesser, per IMO guidelines) must trigger an automatic acceptance hold. VGM certificates must be generated in the format required by each port authority and stored with the booking record for a minimum retention period of three years per regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R013	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Hazardous OOG Cargo Cross-Validation	The system must cross-validate OOG declarations against the dangerous goods module (MOD-026) to identify OOG cargo that is also classified as hazardous under IMDG Code. When an OOG shipment carries an IMDG classification, the system must enforce combined OOG and DG acceptance rules including segregation requirements that account for the cargo's physical protrusion zone. The system must prevent acceptance of hazardous OOG cargo at ports where the combination is restricted—for example certain IMDG classes may be prohibited from open-top stowage at specific terminals. Combined OOG-DG surcharges must be calculated as a composite and displayed as separate line items. Emergency response information must be appended to the OOG acceptance documentation. Alerts must be generated in Arabic and English for terminal safety officers at the accepting port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R014	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	Offline OOG Acceptance Capability	The system must support offline OOG cargo acceptance at gate facilities when network connectivity to the central ERP is unavailable. The offline module must cache the most recent gauge limit profiles, equipment inventory, and tariff rules for the local port. Gate operators must be able to capture dimensions, perform preliminary classification, and issue conditional acceptance receipts while offline. All offline transactions must be queued with cryptographic integrity hashes and automatically synchronized to the central system when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution rules for any bookings modified concurrently on the server. The offline interface must support Arabic and English languages and display date fields in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Offline mode must be available on ruggedized tablets used at gate inspection points across all four port locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R015	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	OOG Acceptance SLA Monitoring	The system must track and enforce service level agreements for OOG cargo acceptance processing times. Configurable SLA targets must be defined per entity and port—for example Jebel Ali may require acceptance decisions within 2 hours while JNPT allows 4 hours. The SLA clock must start when a complete OOG declaration is submitted (all dimensions, documentation, and VGM provided) and stop when final acceptance or rejection is communicated to the shipper. The system must provide real-time SLA dashboards showing pending OOG acceptances color-coded by urgency (green within SLA, amber approaching SLA, red breached SLA). Automated escalation notifications must be sent to supervisors when SLA breach is imminent. SLA performance reports must be generated monthly per entity with trend analysis displayed in both Arabic and English for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R016	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	AI Risk Scoring for OOG Acceptance	The system must generate an AI-driven risk score (0-100) for each OOG cargo acceptance request evaluating factors including dimensional extremity relative to equipment capacity, cargo commodity type fragility index, shipper historical damage claim rate, route-specific weather and sea state forecasts, and transshipment handling complexity. The risk model must be trained on historical OOG incident data across all four operating entities and continuously updated via federated learning without sharing raw data between entities to comply with data sovereignty requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Risk scores above a configurable threshold (default 75) must trigger mandatory senior operations review. The scoring rationale must be explainable with factor contribution breakdowns visible to operators. Risk scores must be included in the acceptance confirmation document in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-001-R017	FEAT-025-1-001	MOD-025	OOG Photo Documentation Management	The system must mandate and manage photographic documentation for all OOG cargo at defined checkpoints: pre-shipment at origin, gate-in at load port, after lashing on vessel, discharge at destination, and gate-out delivery. Each photo must be geotagged and timestamped with tamper-evident metadata. The system must use AI image analysis to automatically verify that photographed cargo matches the declared dimensions within a 10% visual tolerance and flag discrepancies for manual review. Photos must be stored in the document management system linked to the booking reference and accessible across entities involved in the shipment chain. Storage must comply with data residency requirements—Qatar entity photos stored in Qatar data centers, UAE photos in UAE data centers, and so forth. The photo gallery interface must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual captions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R001	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	OOG Cell Blockage Calculation	The system must automatically calculate which adjacent container cell positions are blocked by an OOG unit's protrusion envelope. For over-height cargo, the system must determine how many tiers above are blocked. For over-width cargo, the system must calculate lateral blockage in adjacent bays or rows. For over-length cargo spanning multiple bays, the system must map all affected cell positions across the bay range. Blockage calculations must reference the specific vessel's cell guide dimensions and bay plan geometry as loaded from the vessel profile database. The AI engine must optimize OOG placement to minimize total cell blockage while respecting weight distribution and port rotation constraints. Blocked cell revenue loss must be calculated in the booking currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) for commercial decision support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R002	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	AI-Optimized OOG Stowage Position Selection	The system must employ an AI optimization algorithm to select the optimal stowage position for each OOG unit considering multiple constraints: minimize cell blockage, maintain vessel stability (GM within class-approved limits), respect port rotation sequence to avoid restows, comply with dangerous goods segregation if applicable, and honor cargo-specific stowage preferences (e.g., under-deck prohibited for certain OOG). The optimization must run within 30 seconds for a full vessel plan containing up to 50 OOG units. The algorithm must support manual override by vessel planners with automatic re-validation of all constraints after override. Results must be presented in a visual bay plan interface supporting both English and Arabic labels with the ability to toggle between Gregorian and Hijri date displays for ETD/ETA information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R003	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Vessel Stability Impact Assessment	The system must calculate the impact of OOG cargo placement on vessel stability parameters including metacentric height (GM), trim, list, shear forces, bending moments, and torsional moments. OOG units must be modeled with their actual center of gravity positions accounting for protrusion heights and asymmetric weight distributions that differ from standard container assumptions. The stability calculation must integrate with the vessel's loading computer data via BAPLIE EDI messages or API. When OOG placement causes any stability parameter to approach within 10% of its class-approved limit, the system must generate a warning and suggest alternative positions. Stability reports must be generated per port call and stored with the stowage plan. The system must support stability calculations for vessel types commonly calling at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT including feeder vessels, Panamax, and Ultra Large Container Vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R004	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Flat Rack Stowage Rule Engine	The system must enforce a configurable rule engine for flat rack container stowage encompassing: collapsible versus non-collapsible flat rack placement restrictions, minimum clearance requirements between adjacent flat rack OOG protrusions, prohibition of stacking standard containers on top of loaded flat racks unless specifically approved, wind load considerations for exposed deck flat rack cargo on specific trade routes (e.g., Arabian Sea monsoon season routes between JNPT and Gulf ports), and lashing point load limits per flat rack ISO type (20FR, 40FR). Rules must be configurable per vessel operator and trade lane. Rule violations must be displayed as visual alerts on the bay plan with Arabic and English explanatory text. The rule engine must support seasonal rule variations—for example stricter wind exposure limits during June-September monsoon period for Indian Ocean routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R005	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Open Top Container Stowage Constraints	The system must enforce stowage constraints specific to open top containers carrying OOG cargo. Over-height cargo in open tops must not be stowed below deck unless the vessel's hatch cover clearance permits it, which must be validated against the vessel profile. The system must ensure no containers are stowed directly above loaded open tops if the cargo protrudes above the container height. Rain and spray exposure rules must be enforced—open tops with weather-sensitive cargo must be assigned tarpaulin-covered positions or deck positions with protective arrangements as declared in the booking. The system must track tarpaulin inventory at each port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and alert when tarpaulin allocation exceeds available stock. Stowage positions for open tops must account for crane hook height clearance during lift-on/lift-off operations at each port's quay crane specifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R006	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Break-Bulk on Flat Rack Stowage Integration	The system must handle stowage planning for break-bulk cargo loaded onto flat rack containers, which may have irregular shapes and multiple pieces per flat rack. The system must accept detailed piece-level dimensions and weight distributions including center of gravity offsets from the flat rack centerline. The AI stowage planner must account for asymmetric loading where the cargo weight is not centered, adjusting vessel stability calculations accordingly. For project cargo shipments comprising multiple flat racks that must be loaded and discharged together, the system must enforce co-stowage rules ensuring all related units are accessible at the same port without restowing unrelated cargo. The stowage plan must visually distinguish break-bulk-on-flat-rack units from containerized flat rack cargo using distinct color coding in the bay plan interface, with labels rendered in the operator's selected language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R007	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Restow Impact Analysis for OOG Cargo	The system must perform restow impact analysis when OOG cargo is planned for positions that may require shifting at intermediate ports. Given that OOG restows are significantly more expensive and time-consuming than standard container moves due to special crane rigging requirements, the system must flag any OOG unit whose stowage position would necessitate a restow at intermediate ports along the vessel rotation. The analysis must calculate restow cost in the vessel operator's base currency with conversion to local currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) at each port, estimated restow time impact on port stay, and crane resource requirements. The AI optimizer must prioritize stowage positions that eliminate OOG restows entirely, accepting increased cell blockage as a trade-off when necessary. Restow warnings must be prominently displayed in the planner interface with bilingual explanations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R008	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Multi-Port OOG Stowage Sequencing	The system must optimize OOG stowage positions considering the full vessel rotation across multiple ports in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent region. For a typical rotation such as JNPT–Hamad Port–Jebel Ali–Khalifa Port, OOG units loading at JNPT and discharging at Khalifa Port must be stowed in positions accessible without disturbing OOG units destined for Hamad Port or Jebel Ali. The sequencing algorithm must process the complete OOG manifest for all ports of loading and discharge in a single optimization pass. When optimal sequencing is impossible due to vessel capacity constraints, the system must present trade-off scenarios ranked by total cost impact including restow charges and cell blockage losses. The system must recalculate the stowage sequence whenever an OOG booking is added, amended, or cancelled at any port in the rotation, providing updated plans within 60 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R001	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Registration with Vessel Particulars	The system must allow agents to register a new port call by capturing vessel IMO number, MMSI, vessel name, flag state, LOA, beam, draft (arrival and departure), GRT, NRT, DWT, vessel type (container, bulk, tanker, Ro-Ro, general cargo), and call sign. The registration must auto-populate vessel particulars from the Lloyd's Register or Equasis integration when the IMO number is entered. For vessels calling at Hamad Port (QAHMD) or Jebel Ali (AEJEA), the system must validate that the vessel dimensions are within the port's maximum allowable LOA and draft restrictions. All vessel data must be stored in both English and Arabic transliterations for regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R009	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Crane Operational Feasibility Validation	The system must validate that planned OOG stowage positions are operationally feasible given the crane equipment available at each port of loading and discharge. Validation must check: quay crane safe working load (SWL) against OOG unit gross weight including lifting gear, crane outreach capability to reach the planned bay and row position, hook height clearance above the planned tier position considering OOG over-height, and spreader compatibility with flat rack and open top lifting points. Crane specifications must be maintained per terminal at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, with support for multiple terminals per port. When a planned position fails crane feasibility, the system must suggest alternative positions that pass validation. Crane feasibility reports must be included in the stowage plan documentation distributed to port operations teams in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R010	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Deck versus Hold Stowage Decision Engine	The system must implement an AI decision engine that recommends deck versus under-deck (hold) stowage for each OOG unit based on multiple factors: cargo weather sensitivity, over-height clearance against hatch coaming and cell guide heights, ventilation requirements, dangerous goods class stowage mandates per IMDG Code, and cargo value-based insurance preferences. The decision engine must respect vessel-specific constraints—some vessels may prohibit all OOG in holds while others have specially designed hold spaces for flat racks. The system must display the deck/hold recommendation with confidence score and contributing factors in a bilingual panel. Operators must be able to override the recommendation with mandatory reason capture. For each operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), different override authorization levels may be configured to reflect local operational governance structures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R011	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	OOG Stowage Plan Versioning and Audit	The system must maintain a complete version history of OOG stowage plans for each voyage and port call. Every modification—whether AI-generated or manual—must create a new version with a timestamp, operator identity, entity affiliation, and change description. The system must support side-by-side visual comparison of any two plan versions highlighting moved OOG units, changed blocked cells, and altered stability parameters. Versions must be immutable once created and retained for a minimum of five years to support dispute resolution and regulatory audits. The version history interface must support Arabic RTL layout with Hijri date display. Authorized users must be able to designate a plan version as the official working plan and generate a digitally signed PDF export of any version for transmission to vessel masters, port authorities, and terminal operators. Each entity must have separate version access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R012	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Stowage Plan Conflict Detection	The system must continuously monitor stowage plans for conflicts that arise when multiple planners work on the same vessel across different operating entities. For example, a planner in Qatar may position an OOG unit at Hamad Port load while a planner in UAE simultaneously plans an OOG unit at Jebel Ali load into a conflicting position. The system must implement optimistic concurrency control with real-time conflict detection and notification. When a conflict is detected, both planners must receive an alert in their respective languages showing the conflicting positions and affected OOG units. The system must suggest resolution options ranked by overall plan quality. A designated lead planner role must have authority to resolve conflicts with final override capability. All conflict events and resolutions must be logged for operational analysis and process improvement reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R013	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Weather Route Impact on OOG Stowage	The system must integrate with weather routing services to assess the impact of forecasted sea conditions on OOG cargo stowage positions. For exposed deck positions, the system must evaluate predicted wind forces, wave heights, and roll angles against the securing arrangement capacity of each OOG unit. When weather forecasts indicate conditions exceeding the securing arrangement design limits for a planned stowage position, the system must recommend repositioning the OOG unit to a more sheltered position or additional securing measures. The weather assessment must consider seasonal patterns on key routes—Arabian Gulf summer heat impact on cargo, Indian Ocean monsoon swell on JNPT-Gulf routes, and Red Sea wind conditions. Alerts must be generated with sufficient lead time (minimum 48 hours before vessel departure) to allow stowage plan adjustments. Weather assessments must be presented in both Arabic and English with visual route overlays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R014	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Reefer OOG Combined Stowage Handling	The system must support stowage planning for OOG cargo on reefer-capable flat racks and specialized temperature-controlled open tops. These units require stowage positions with access to vessel power receptacles while simultaneously accounting for OOG protrusion blockage. The system must map reefer plug positions against OOG-suitable stowage positions on each vessel and identify compatible intersections. When reefer OOG units compete with standard reefer containers for power receptacles, the system must enforce priority rules configurable per entity and trade lane. Power consumption calculations must account for the higher thermal load of OOG reefer units with exposed surfaces. The stowage plan must clearly indicate reefer-OOG positions with distinct symbology in the bay plan view and include power cable routing instructions for terminal operations teams at all four port locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R015	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Stowage Plan Distribution and Acknowledgment	The system must distribute finalized OOG stowage plans to all relevant stakeholders via configurable channels: EDI MOVINS messages to terminal operating systems, BAPLIE updates to vessel operators, email notifications to vessel masters, and API pushes to port community systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each distribution must be tracked with delivery confirmation and recipient acknowledgment. The system must support acknowledgment workflows where terminal planners can accept or raise objections to OOG stowage positions within a configurable time window (default 4 hours). Objections must route back to the originating planner with the terminal's concern documented. Distribution documents must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). Distribution audit trails must be maintained per entity with timestamps in local timezone and both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R016	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	OOG Stowage Template Management	The system must support creation and management of OOG stowage templates for recurring vessel services and trade lanes. Templates must capture preferred OOG stowage zones, pre-approved positions for common OOG types, and blocked positions reserved for specific OOG categories on regular rotation vessels. Templates must be versioned and associated with specific vessel classes and service strings. Planners must be able to apply a template as a starting point and then adjust for the specific voyage's OOG mix. The AI optimizer must learn from planner adjustments to templates over time and suggest template refinements. Templates must be shareable across entities with entity-specific override layers—for example, a Gulf Feeder Service template may have common base positions but entity-specific DG segregation rules applied at each port. Template management interfaces must support Arabic and English with full RTL compatibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-002-R017	FEAT-025-1-002	MOD-025	Stowage Utilization and Revenue Analytics	The system must provide analytics on OOG stowage efficiency including: cell utilization loss ratio (cells blocked by OOG versus total vessel capacity), OOG revenue per blocked cell metric, comparative analysis of AI-optimized versus manually planned OOG stowage efficiency, and trends across vessel services and ports. Analytics must be filterable by entity, port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), vessel service, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and OOG type. Revenue figures must be displayed in the user's preferred currency with multi-currency consolidation supporting USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The system must generate monthly entity-level and consolidated group-level reports. The analytics dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with bidirectional text for mixed Arabic-English content such as vessel names and port codes. Data must be exportable to Excel and business intelligence tools via API.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R001	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Special Equipment Inventory Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of all special equipment units across the four operating entities including flat racks (20FR, 40FR), open tops (20OT, 40OT), platforms (20PL, 40PL), bolsters, and mafi trailers. Each unit record must include: unique equipment number, ISO type code, owner (shipping line, lessor, or own-fleet), current location (port, depot, vessel, or in-transit), condition status (serviceable, damaged, under repair), last inspection date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, maximum payload capacity, and tare weight. The registry must support real-time updates from terminal operating systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Equipment owned by different legal entities must be segregated with cross-entity visibility governed by configurable sharing agreements. The interface must render correctly in Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R002	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Availability Tracking	The system must provide real-time availability tracking of special equipment at each port and depot location. Availability must account for current stock on hand, equipment committed to confirmed OOG bookings, equipment in repair or inspection, and equipment in transit (on vessels or trucks en route). The availability calculation must project forward using confirmed booking data and vessel schedules to show forecasted availability for future dates. When a new OOG booking requires specific equipment, the system must check availability at the load port for the required date and automatically suggest alternatives if the preferred type is unavailable—for example offering a 40FR when no 40OT is available for an over-height shipment. Availability data must be displayed with multi-currency leasing costs (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) for equipment sourced from external lessors. Low-stock alerts must be generated when availability falls below configurable thresholds per port and equipment type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R003	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	AI-Driven Equipment Demand Forecasting	The system must employ AI models to forecast special equipment demand at each port for rolling 30, 60, and 90-day horizons. The forecast must consider historical OOG booking patterns, seasonal trends (e.g., increased project cargo during construction season in Qatar and KSA), confirmed and provisional booking pipeline, vessel service schedules, and regional economic indicators. The model must generate demand forecasts segmented by equipment type and port, with confidence intervals. When forecasted demand exceeds projected availability, the system must automatically generate repositioning recommendations specifying which equipment types to move between which ports and the optimal timing. Forecasts must be presented in dashboards supporting both Arabic and English with date axes configurable between Gregorian and Hijri calendars. The AI model must retrain monthly on actual versus forecasted data and expose accuracy metrics per port and equipment type to operations managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R004	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Repositioning Management	The system must manage the repositioning of empty special equipment between ports and depots. Repositioning orders must capture: origin and destination locations, equipment type and quantity, requested arrival date, transportation mode (vessel, truck, or rail), and the authorizing entity. The system must calculate repositioning costs including: transport charges in the relevant currency, port handling fees, customs duties if crossing borders (e.g., UAE to India), and insurance. The AI optimizer must consolidate repositioning needs across entities to achieve economies of scale—for example combining Qatar and UAE entity empty flat rack repositioning onto the same feeder vessel. Repositioning status must be trackable in real-time with milestone updates (picked up, loaded on vessel, discharged, delivered to depot). Cross-border repositioning must generate the required customs documentation automatically including bilingual Arabic/English commercial invoices and packing lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R005	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Condition Inspection Workflow	The system must enforce a structured inspection workflow for special equipment condition assessment. Inspections must be triggered at defined events: gate-in after return from customer, gate-out before allocation to a new booking, and periodic maintenance intervals. The inspection checklist must be configurable per equipment type and must include structural integrity checks (frame, corner castings, forklift pockets), lashing ring and securing point condition assessment, floor and deck condition, and ISO marking legibility. Inspectors must record findings on mobile devices with photo evidence and damage location marking on equipment diagrams. AI image analysis must assist in identifying damage severity from uploaded photos. Inspection records must be stored with the equipment unit history and accessible across entities when equipment is shared. The inspection interface must fully support Arabic language input for damage descriptions and Arabic RTL layout for the inspection form.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R006	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Maintenance and Repair Tracking	The system must track maintenance and repair activities for all special equipment units. When an inspection identifies damage, the system must automatically create a repair work order routed to the appropriate maintenance provider (in-house workshop or external vendor) at the equipment's current location. Work orders must include damage details, required parts, estimated repair cost in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), and target completion date. The system must track repair status through stages: work order created, parts ordered, repair in progress, quality inspection, and returned to service. Repair costs must be allocated to the owning entity and, where applicable, recovered from the responsible party (shipper, terminal operator) via damage billing integrated with the invoicing module. Equipment downtime due to repairs must be factored into availability calculations. Maintenance history must be retained for the equipment's entire lifecycle with Arabic and English bilingual records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R007	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Leased Equipment Contract Management	The system must manage lease contracts for special equipment sourced from external lessors including container leasing companies and other shipping lines. Contract records must include: lessor details, equipment types and quantities, per diem rental rates in the contract currency, free time allowances, pick-up and drop-off depot locations, damage protection terms, and contract validity periods. The system must automatically calculate accrued lease charges based on actual usage days and generate lease billing reconciliation reports matching lessor invoices. Off-hire notifications must be generated when leased equipment is returned or becomes unserviceable. The system must alert when contracts approach expiry and support renewal or extension workflows. Multi-currency lease charge accruals must be maintained in the contract currency and converted to each entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) at month-end using treasury-provided exchange rates. Contract documents must be stored in the document management system with bilingual metadata.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R008	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Allocation to OOG Bookings	The system must implement intelligent equipment allocation logic that assigns specific special equipment units to confirmed OOG bookings. Allocation must consider: equipment type compatibility with cargo requirements, unit condition and maintenance due dates, proximity to the gate-in location, ownership priority (own-fleet preferred over leased), and lease cost optimization. The AI allocator must batch-process allocation for multiple bookings simultaneously to achieve global optimization rather than greedy first-come-first-served assignment. Allocation must be performed automatically at a configurable lead time before gate-in (default 48 hours) but support manual override by equipment controllers. When allocation is confirmed, the system must update equipment availability, notify the gate operations team, and generate equipment preparation instructions. Allocation records must track the full chain: booking reference, equipment unit number, allocation timestamp, allocating entity, and allocating officer for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R009	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Tracking via IoT Integration	The system must integrate with IoT tracking devices attached to special equipment units to provide real-time location and condition monitoring. Supported IoT data streams must include GPS coordinates, shock and tilt sensor readings (to detect mishandling), temperature sensors for reefer-capable special equipment, and door open/close events for open tops. IoT data must be ingested via MQTT or REST API with support for major tracking device manufacturers. The system must plot equipment locations on a map interface covering all four port regions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with geofence alerts when equipment enters or exits designated zones (ports, depots, customer premises). Abnormal sensor readings (excessive shock, unexpected tilt) must trigger automated alerts to the equipment management team with location details. IoT data must be retained for 90 days for operational analysis and one year for insurance claim support. The map interface must support Arabic place names and right-to-left legend panels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R010	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Detention and Demurrage Billing	The system must calculate and bill detention and demurrage charges for special equipment based on contractual free time allowances and tariff rates. Free time must be calculated from the gate-out event at the port or depot to the gate-in return event. The system must support complex free time rules including: different free time periods for different equipment types, shipper-specific free time agreements, and port-specific public holiday exclusions (including Islamic holidays calculated from the Hijri calendar for Qatar, UAE, and KSA). Tariff rates must be configurable per entity and equipment type with tiered escalation (e.g., first 5 days at rate A, days 6-10 at rate B). Invoices must be generated in the billing currency agreed with the customer (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with Arabic and English bilingual descriptions. The system must support detention waiver workflows for extenuating circumstances with entity-specific approval authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R011	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Cross-Entity Equipment Sharing Agreements	The system must support formal equipment sharing agreements between the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to optimize special equipment utilization across the group. Sharing agreements must define: which equipment types are shared, priority access rules (owning entity has first right of use), inter-company transfer pricing for equipment usage, and settlement currency and frequency. When an entity requests equipment that is unavailable locally but available at a sister entity's location, the system must automatically propose a cross-entity transfer with cost visibility showing both the repositioning cost and the inter-company usage charge. Transfers must generate inter-company accounting entries in both entities' functional currencies with proper elimination entries for consolidated reporting. The sharing agreement configuration interface must support Arabic and English and enforce dual authorization from both entities for agreement modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R012	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Lashing Gear Inventory and Kit Management	The system must manage inventory of lashing and securing gear associated with special equipment including chains, turnbuckles, D-rings, wire ropes, timber dunnage, and protective materials. Inventory must be tracked at the individual item level with attributes: type, size, safe working load (SWL), certification expiry date, and current location. The system must support kit assembly—grouping individual lashing items into pre-configured securing kits for common OOG types (e.g., standard over-height kit, heavy-lift flat rack kit). Kit allocation must be linked to OOG bookings so that when a securing plan specifies a kit, the system reserves the required items from inventory. Low stock alerts must be generated per port when lashing gear inventory falls below configurable reorder points. Procurement requisitions must be generated automatically in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and routed to the entity's procurement workflow. Certification expiry warnings must be generated 30 days in advance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R013	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Lifecycle Cost Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive lifecycle cost analytics for special equipment across all four operating entities. Analytics must include: total cost of ownership per equipment unit and type (acquisition, maintenance, repair, repositioning, insurance), revenue generated per equipment unit derived from OOG surcharges and equipment rental charges, utilization rate (days in revenue service versus total days), average turnaround time per port, and depreciation schedules per entity's accounting policy. The analytics must support comparison between own-fleet and leased equipment economics to inform make-versus-lease decisions. All monetary values must be presentable in any of the five operating currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with consistent exchange rate application. Dashboards must support Arabic RTL layout with Hijri date filtering. Reports must be exportable in formats compatible with each entity's financial reporting system and must support both standalone entity and consolidated group-level views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R014	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Customs and Temporary Import Tracking	The system must track the customs status of special equipment units that cross international borders, particularly equipment moving between UAE, Qatar, KSA, and India. The system must manage temporary import permits (TIP) recording: permit number, issuing customs authority, validity period, equipment covered, and bond or guarantee amounts in local currency. The system must alert operations when a temporary import permit is approaching expiry, requiring the equipment to be re-exported or the permit extended. For equipment moving between GCC countries, the system must determine whether GCC customs union provisions apply, eliminating the need for temporary import procedures. For India-bound equipment, the system must generate the required customs documentation including bills of entry for temporary import under Chapter 98 of the Indian Customs Tariff. All customs documents must be stored with audit trails and linked to the equipment master record. Alerts must be generated in Arabic and English per the entity's operating language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R015	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Damage Liability Assessment	The system must support damage liability assessment for special equipment returned in damaged condition. When a gate-in inspection identifies damage, the system must correlate the damage against the gate-out inspection record to determine if the damage occurred during the customer's possession. The system must apply the UIIA (Uniform Intermodal Interchange Agreement) or equivalent regional interchange rules to assess liability. AI-assisted damage assessment must compare gate-out and gate-in photographs to identify new damage with confidence scoring. Liability determinations must generate damage billing records linked to the responsible party's account with repair cost estimates in the entity's functional currency. Disputed damage claims must enter a resolution workflow with configurable escalation paths per entity. The damage assessment interface must support Arabic text input for damage descriptions, Arabic RTL layout, and photographic evidence annotation. Historical damage patterns must feed into the AI risk scoring model for OOG acceptance decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R016	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Offline Equipment Operations Support	The system must support offline equipment management operations at port gates and depot facilities when network connectivity is unavailable. The offline module must cache current equipment inventory for the local facility, pending allocation instructions, and inspection checklists. Gate operators must be able to record equipment gate-in and gate-out events, perform inspections with photo capture, and update equipment condition status while offline. All offline transactions must be queued with timestamps and operator identification, and automatically synchronized to the central system when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution rules must handle scenarios where equipment was allocated to a different booking on the server while the gate was offline. The offline interface must support Arabic and English language switching and display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Synchronization status must be clearly indicated to operators to prevent duplicate entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-003-R017	FEAT-025-1-003	MOD-025	Equipment Intermodal Transfer Coordination	The system must coordinate special equipment transfers between maritime, road, and rail transport modes at each port location. For Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali, intermodal transfers primarily involve truck movements between port and inland depots or customer sites. For JNPT, the system must additionally support rail transfers via the Dedicated Freight Corridor to inland container depots. Transfer coordination must include: scheduling pickup and delivery appointments, generating equipment interchange receipts at each transfer point, tracking transit status with estimated arrival times, and calculating transfer costs in local currency. The system must enforce vehicle and rail wagon capacity limits for special equipment—for example, over-height flat racks may require special rail wagon allocation or route permits for road transport. Transfer documentation must be generated bilingually and must integrate with each port's truck appointment system to avoid gate congestion during peak periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R001	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Automated Securing Plan Calculation Engine	The system must implement a calculation engine that automatically generates cargo securing plans based on the IMO/ILO/UNECE Code of Practice for Packing of Cargo Transport Units (CTU Code) and the CSS Code Annex 13 standard method for assessing securing arrangements. Inputs must include: cargo dimensions and weight, center of gravity position, friction coefficient between cargo and equipment deck, securing equipment properties (SWL, material safety factor), and vessel-specific acceleration factors. The engine must calculate required securing forces in longitudinal, transverse, and vertical directions and determine the number, type, and arrangement of lashing components needed to resist sliding and tipping forces. Calculations must be performed within 10 seconds per OOG unit. The engine must support manual parameter overrides by certified lashing engineers with all overrides logged for audit. Output must include a detailed force balance summary presentable in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R002	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Vessel-Specific Acceleration Factor Management	The system must maintain a database of vessel-specific acceleration factors used in securing force calculations. Acceleration factors must be configurable per vessel and stowage position (bay, row, tier) reflecting the vessel's motion characteristics as documented in the vessel's Cargo Securing Manual (CSM). When vessel-specific data is unavailable, the system must apply IMO CSS Code standard acceleration tables based on vessel length, GM, stowage position relative to midship, and tier height above keel. The system must support seasonal route-specific acceleration adjustments—for example, higher transverse accelerations for monsoon season voyages between JNPT and Gulf ports. Acceleration factor updates must require authorization from the marine operations department. The system must alert when a securing plan is generated using default values rather than vessel-specific factors, flagging the plan for additional review. All acceleration data must be version-controlled and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R003	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Material Selection and Specification	The system must automatically select appropriate securing materials and components from the lashing gear inventory based on the calculated securing force requirements. Selection logic must consider: chain versus wire rope versus webbing strap suitability for the cargo type, securing point safe working load (SWL) compatibility with the flat rack or open top's lashing ring capacity, material availability at the load port, and cost optimization. The system must generate a complete bill of materials listing each securing component with its specification: type, size, SWL, quantity, and unit cost in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) or booking currency (USD). When preferred materials are unavailable at the load port, the system must suggest alternatives with recalculated securing arrangements. Material specifications must reference recognized standards (EN 12195, ISO 1161) and be presented in bilingual Arabic/English format for procurement and operations teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R004	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Visual Securing Arrangement Drawing Generation	The system must generate visual securing arrangement drawings showing the plan view, side elevation, and end elevation of the cargo on its equipment with all securing components illustrated. Drawings must clearly indicate: lashing attachment points on the cargo and equipment, lashing angles (horizontal and vertical), tensioning direction, and component identification labels cross-referenced to the bill of materials. The AI system must automatically determine optimal lashing angles to maximize securing effectiveness while respecting practical installation constraints. Drawings must be generated in scalable vector format suitable for printing at A3 size. All text labels on drawings must be rendered in the operator's selected language (Arabic or English). Drawings must include a title block with booking reference, vessel name, voyage number, and plan version. The drawing generation engine must produce output within 15 seconds per OOG unit and support batch generation for project cargo shipments with multiple units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R005	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Friction Coefficient Database and Validation	The system must maintain a database of static friction coefficients (mu values) for common cargo-to-deck surface material combinations used in securing force calculations. Standard combinations must include: steel on steel (dry, oiled, wet), steel on timber, rubber on steel, concrete on timber, and various anti-slip mat materials. Each friction coefficient entry must include the source reference (e.g., CSS Code Table 5, manufacturer test certificate). When the default friction coefficient is used, the system must apply the conservative CSS Code values. When anti-slip mats or timber dunnage are specified in the securing plan, higher friction values may be applied, but the system must cross-validate that the specified friction-enhancing materials are included in the bill of materials. The system must flag securing plans where the selected friction coefficient significantly impacts the lashing quantity—if using a lower friction value would require more than 20% additional lashing, a warning must be displayed to the planner in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R006	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Center of Gravity Estimation and Validation	The system must capture and validate the cargo center of gravity (CoG) position for accurate securing calculations. The CoG must be specified in three dimensions relative to the equipment platform: longitudinal distance from one end, transverse distance from centerline, and vertical height above deck. When the shipper provides CoG data, the system must validate it against the cargo dimensions and weight for physical plausibility—for example, a homogeneous rectangular cargo should have its CoG near the geometric center. When CoG data is not provided, the AI engine must estimate it based on the cargo commodity type using historical data patterns and engineering heuristics. Estimated CoG positions must be flagged with a confidence level and the securing plan must apply safety factors proportional to the estimation uncertainty. For high-risk OOG cargo (risk score above 60 from FEAT-025-1-001), the system must mandate surveyor-verified CoG data rather than accepting estimates. CoG data must be displayed in the visual securing arrangement drawings with both Arabic and English annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R007	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Tipping and Sliding Force Balance Verification	The system must perform separate verification of securing arrangements against both sliding and tipping failure modes in all three axes (longitudinal, transverse, and vertical). For sliding resistance, the calculation must balance applied acceleration forces against friction forces plus lashing component forces resolved into the sliding direction. For tipping resistance, the calculation must verify that the restoring moment (from cargo weight and lashing hold-down forces) exceeds the overturning moment (from transverse and longitudinal acceleration forces) about the tipping edge. The system must identify the critical failure mode (sliding or tipping) for each direction and ensure the securing arrangement addresses the most demanding case. Safety factors must be applied per CSS Code requirements: minimum 1.0 for advanced calculation method. Force balance results must be presented in a structured report format with clear pass/fail status per direction and mode. Reports must be generated in Arabic and English and included as annexures to the OOG acceptance documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R008	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Plan Compliance with Port Authority Rules	The system must validate generated securing plans against port-specific securing requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each port authority may impose additional securing standards beyond the baseline CSS Code requirements—for example, Dubai Maritime City Authority may require specific lashing component certifications, while JNPT may mandate particular timber dunnage treatment standards compliant with ISPM-15 for phytosanitary requirements. The system must maintain a configurable port authority rule database that is applied as an additional validation layer after the core securing calculation. Non-compliant plans must display specific violations with port authority regulation references. The system must support rule updates when port authorities issue revised securing guidelines. Compliance validation results must be included in the securing plan documentation and presented bilingually. Plans that pass core calculation but fail port-specific rules must be flagged for remediation before the plan can be approved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R004	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Project Cargo Milestone Tracking	The system must provide a milestone-based project tracker for complex project cargo shipments that span multiple legs and extended durations. Each project must support configurable milestones including factory readiness, pre-shipment survey, inland transport commencement, port arrival, vessel loading, sea transit, discharge, and final delivery. Milestones must capture planned dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, actual dates, responsible party, and status (pending, in-progress, completed, delayed). The system must calculate critical path and highlight milestones at risk of delay. Automated notifications must be sent to stakeholders in their preferred language (Arabic or English) when milestones are updated or overdue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R009	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Heavy Lift Securing Plan Specialization	The system must provide specialized securing plan calculations for heavy lift OOG cargo exceeding 30 tonnes per unit, which is common for project cargo through Gulf and Indian ports. Heavy lift securing plans must account for: concentrated load distribution on the flat rack or platform deck requiring load spreading calculations, specialized lifting and lashing equipment (heavy-duty chains, steel wire ropes, welded securing points), and multi-point securing arrangements with load equalization requirements. The system must calculate deck strength adequacy by comparing the cargo footprint pressure against the equipment's rated floor loading capacity. When deck strengthening measures are required (e.g., timber spreader beams, steel grillages), these must be included in the securing plan bill of materials with engineering specifications. Heavy lift securing plans must be flagged for mandatory review by a certified lashing surveyor before approval. The plan must include crane lifting instructions for terminal operations at all four port locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R010	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Plan Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a multi-stage approval workflow for cargo securing plans before they are considered final. The workflow stages must include: AI-generated plan creation, technical review by a qualified lashing planner, marine operations approval for vessel-specific compliance, and optional port authority submission where required. Each approval stage must capture the reviewer's identity, entity affiliation, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and approval or rejection decision with mandatory comments for rejections. Rejected plans must be returned to the originating stage with specific deficiency notes. The workflow must support delegation and escalation rules configurable per operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Emergency approval paths must be available for time-critical situations where cargo is already at the port gate, requiring senior operations authorization to expedite the review cycle. Approval status must be visible in real-time on the OOG booking dashboard with bilingual Arabic and English status labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R011	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Plan Version Control and Revision History	The system must maintain full version control for every cargo securing plan with immutable revision history. Each revision must record: version number, revision date and time, revising user identity and entity, nature of changes (parameters modified, materials changed, arrangement altered), and the trigger for revision (initial creation, measurement update, equipment change, reviewer feedback). The system must support comparison between any two versions highlighting all differences in calculation parameters, force balance results, material specifications, and visual arrangements. When a securing plan is revised after initial approval, the system must invalidate the previous approval and re-trigger the approval workflow for the new version. The latest approved version must be clearly identified in the plan repository. Version history must be retained for a minimum of seven years per maritime regulatory record-keeping requirements. The version control interface must support Arabic RTL layout and Hijri date display alongside Gregorian dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R012	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Plan Template Library	The system must maintain a library of pre-approved securing plan templates for commonly shipped OOG cargo types. Templates must be categorized by cargo commodity (e.g., construction machinery, industrial boilers, yacht transport, wind turbine components), equipment type (20FR, 40FR, 20OT, 40OT, 20PL, 40PL), and approximate weight range. Each template must include a reference securing arrangement, standard bill of materials, and base calculation parameters that can be adapted to specific shipment dimensions and weights. The AI engine must recommend the most suitable template based on the OOG booking details and automatically adjust the template parameters to match actual cargo specifications. Template usage must reduce securing plan generation time by at least 60% compared to creating plans from scratch. Templates must be authored and approved by certified lashing engineers and tagged with applicable port jurisdictions. The template library interface must support bilingual Arabic and English search and navigation with category labels in both languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R013	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Execution Verification at Port	The system must support on-site verification that the physical cargo securing execution matches the approved securing plan. Verification must be performed by terminal lashing teams using a mobile application that displays the approved securing arrangement drawing and bill of materials. The mobile app must provide a step-by-step checklist for verifying each securing component: correct type and size, correct attachment point, correct tensioning, and correct lashing angle within tolerance. Lashing teams must photograph each securing point and the overall arrangement from multiple angles. The system must use AI image recognition to compare photographed arrangements against the plan drawings and flag discrepancies. Verification results must be digitally signed by the supervising lashing foreman and transmitted to the vessel planner and marine operations team. The mobile verification interface must function offline at port locations where cellular connectivity is unreliable and must support Arabic and English language selection for lashing team operators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R014	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Dynamic Securing Plan Adjustment for Route Changes	The system must support dynamic recalculation of securing plans when a vessel's intended route changes after cargo loading. Route changes may alter the expected sea conditions and acceleration factors—for example, a vessel originally planned to transit via the Suez Canal being rerouted around the Cape of Good Hope would experience significantly different weather patterns and voyage duration. When a route change is notified via the vessel operations module, the system must automatically recalculate all OOG securing plans on the affected vessel using the acceleration factors applicable to the new route. If any securing arrangement is found insufficient for the revised conditions, the system must generate an urgent alert to marine operations at the next port of call recommending additional securing measures. The alert must specify exact additional lashing requirements with materials and installation instructions. Alerts must be distributed to relevant entities along the vessel's rotation in both Arabic and English with appropriate urgency classification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R005	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Multi-Party Coordination Workspace	The system must provide a shared digital workspace for coordinating between multiple parties involved in heavy lift operations including shipping lines, port terminal operators, crane operators, surveyors, freight forwarders, and customs brokers. Each party must have role-based access to relevant documents, schedules, and communications. The workspace must support threaded discussions, file sharing with version control, and task assignments with due dates. All interactions must be logged with timestamps for audit purposes. The workspace must function across entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India with entity-specific branding. Offline mode must cache the latest workspace state and sync changes when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R015	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Cost Estimation and Allocation	The system must calculate the total cost of cargo securing for each OOG unit including: lashing materials (purchase or rental), labor hours for securing and unsecuring at load and discharge ports, supervisor and surveyor fees, and any special equipment (cranes, forklifts) required for securing operations. Costs must be calculated in the local currency of each port where securing work is performed (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) and converted to the booking currency for customer billing. The system must support cost allocation rules—securing costs may be charged to the shipper, absorbed by the shipping line, or split according to contractual terms stored in the customer agreement. Cost estimates must be generated alongside the securing plan and included in the OOG surcharge calculation from FEAT-025-1-001. Actual costs must be captured post-execution for variance analysis against estimates. Cost reports must be generated per entity with bilingual Arabic/English formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R016	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Securing Plan Digital Archive and Retrieval	The system must maintain a searchable digital archive of all cargo securing plans generated across all four operating entities. The archive must support search by booking reference, vessel name, voyage number, cargo commodity, equipment type, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), port of loading, and approval status. Each archived plan must include the complete securing package: calculation report, visual arrangement drawings, bill of materials, approval records, and execution verification photographs. The archive must enforce data residency rules—plans generated by the Qatar entity must be stored in Qatar-based infrastructure, UAE entity plans in UAE infrastructure, and similarly for KSA and India. Cross-entity access to archived plans must be governed by role-based permissions with audit logging of all access events. The archive must support legal hold functionality to preserve plans involved in cargo damage claims or regulatory investigations. Retrieval interfaces must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual search results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R017	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	AI Continuous Learning from Securing Outcomes	The system must implement a continuous learning feedback loop where actual cargo securing outcomes inform and improve future AI-generated securing plans. Outcome data must be collected from: cargo damage reports at discharge ports, lashing inspection findings during voyage, securing execution verification discrepancies, and cargo insurance claim data. The AI model must identify patterns such as recurring damage on specific trade routes, cargo types that consistently require stronger-than-calculated securing, and seasonal factors that affect securing effectiveness. Learning must be federated across the four operating entities—insights from JNPT damage patterns must improve securing plans generated at Jebel Ali without sharing raw shipment data across entity boundaries to comply with data sovereignty regulations. Model updates must be reviewed by marine operations before deployment. Performance metrics including plan accuracy rate, damage incident correlation, and cost optimization trends must be reported quarterly in dashboards supporting Arabic and English with multi-currency financial impact analysis in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-1-004-R018	FEAT-025-1-004	MOD-025	Regulatory Reporting for Securing Compliance	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for cargo securing as required by maritime authorities in each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar Maritime Administration, the report must demonstrate compliance with Qatar Ports regulations on OOG cargo securing. For UAE Federal Transport Authority, the report must satisfy DP World and Abu Dhabi Ports securing standards. For Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), KSA-specific securing requirements must be addressed. For JNPT under the Indian Directorate General of Shipping, compliance with Indian Merchant Shipping Act provisions on cargo securing must be documented. Reports must include statistical summaries of securing plan volumes, approval cycle times, execution compliance rates, and any incidents or near-misses. All regulatory reports must be generated in the mandated language and format for each jurisdiction (Arabic for Qatar, UAE, KSA and English for India, with bilingual options where accepted). Reports must be digitally signed by the entity's designated person ashore and transmitted via the prescribed regulatory submission channel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R001	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Heavy Lift Cargo Registration and Classification	The system must allow operators to register heavy lift cargo items with detailed attributes including gross weight in metric tonnes, individual piece dimensions (length, width, height in meters), center of gravity coordinates, lifting point specifications, and cargo classification codes per IMO guidelines. The registration form must support both English and Arabic (RTL) input with bilingual labels. Each heavy lift item must be linked to a parent booking reference, consignee entity, and origin/destination port pair (e.g., QAHMD to INJNP). The system must auto-classify cargo into weight tiers: standard heavy (50-100t), super heavy (100-500t), and ultra heavy (500t+), each triggering different approval workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R002	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Crane and Lifting Equipment Planning	The system must provide a crane planning module that matches heavy lift cargo pieces to available crane capacity at origin and destination ports. For Hamad Port, the system must reference shore crane SWL (Safe Working Load) limits of up to 100 tonnes and mobile crane availability up to 600 tonnes. For Jebel Ali, the system must support the mega-crane capacity of 2000 tonnes. The module must calculate required crane configurations including tandem lifts, crawler crane positioning, and boom radius constraints. Planners must be able to generate crane rigging plans as PDF documents in both Arabic and English, with weight distribution diagrams and lift sequence animations stored against the booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R003	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Vessel Stability and Stowage Calculation	The system must integrate vessel stability calculations for heavy lift cargo placement on breakbulk and semi-submersible vessels. The module must compute the impact of heavy lift cargo on vessel trim, list, bending moments, and shear forces using cargo weight, dimensions, and proposed stowage position. Results must be compared against vessel class society limits (DNV, Lloyd's, BV). The system must generate stowage plans showing cargo footprint on deck or in hold with lashing pattern recommendations. All calculations must be auditable with version history, and any parameter change must trigger automatic recalculation. Multi-currency cost estimates (USD/QAR/AED/SAR/INR) for stowage must be attached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R008	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Heavy Lift Cost Estimation and Budgeting	The system must provide a comprehensive cost estimation engine for heavy lift operations covering crane hire, special equipment rental, stevedoring labor, vessel modifications, route engineering, permits, and insurance premiums. Costs must be estimated in the local currency of the operation port (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for KSA ports, INR for JNPT) with automatic conversion to the booking currency using daily exchange rates. The estimation must support margin markup by entity and customer tier. Historical cost data from previous similar lifts must be accessible as benchmarking references. Budget vs actual tracking must provide variance analysis with alerts when actual costs exceed estimates by a configurable threshold percentage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R009	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Pre-Shipment Inspection and Survey Workflow	The system must manage the complete pre-shipment inspection workflow for heavy lift cargo including survey appointment scheduling, surveyor assignment, inspection checklist execution, deficiency reporting, and clearance certification. Inspection checklists must be configurable per cargo type (e.g., turbines, transformers, pressure vessels, structural steel) and must cover dimensional verification, weight confirmation, lifting point integrity, packaging adequacy, and marking compliance. The system must support mobile-based inspections with photo capture and digital signature capability for use in offline environments at remote fabrication yards. Survey reports must be generated in PDF format with bilingual Arabic/English content and automatically attached to the cargo booking record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R010	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Spreader Bar and Special Equipment Tracking	The system must maintain an inventory of special lifting equipment including spreader bars, lifting beams, modular trailers (SPMT), jack-up systems, and skidding equipment across all operational locations. Each equipment item must have records of load capacity, certification expiry dates, current location, and availability status. The system must support equipment reservation against specific heavy lift bookings with conflict detection for overlapping reservations. Certification renewal reminders must be generated 30, 60, and 90 days before expiry. Equipment utilization reports must be available per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) showing usage rates, idle time, and maintenance history to support capital expenditure planning decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R011	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	AI-Powered Lift Risk Assessment	The system must employ AI algorithms to assess risk levels for planned heavy lift operations by analyzing cargo characteristics, weather forecasts, historical incident data, equipment condition, and crew experience levels. The AI engine must produce a composite risk score on a scale of 1 to 100 with contributing factor breakdown. Risk assessments must be automatically triggered when a heavy lift booking is confirmed and updated as conditions change. High-risk operations (score above 75) must require additional senior management approval before proceeding. The system must learn from completed operations by comparing predicted risk factors against actual outcomes, continuously refining the risk model. Risk reports must be presentable in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R012	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Weather Window Planning for Heavy Lifts	The system must integrate real-time weather data feeds for all operational ports to assist in planning weather-dependent heavy lift operations. Critical weather parameters must include wind speed (sustained and gusts), wave height, swell period, temperature, and humidity. The system must define configurable weather limits per operation type (e.g., maximum 15 knots wind for crane lifts above 200 tonnes). A weather window calendar must display forecasted suitable windows for planned operations over the next 14 days. When weather conditions are forecasted to exceed safe limits during a planned lift window, the system must automatically alert operations teams and suggest alternative windows. Historical weather pattern analysis must help planners select optimal scheduling periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R013	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Subcontractor Management for Heavy Lift Operations	The system must manage a registry of qualified subcontractors for heavy lift operations including crane operators, rigging crews, transport companies, and marine surveyors. Each subcontractor record must include company details, insurance certificates, safety records, equipment inventories, certifications, and performance ratings. The system must support a prequalification workflow requiring document submission, review, and approval before a subcontractor can be assigned to operations. Subcontractor rates must be maintained in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with contract validity periods. Performance evaluation forms must be completed after each engagement and feed into an aggregate rating system. Subcontractors with expired insurance or poor safety records must be automatically flagged and blocked from new assignments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R014	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Heavy Lift Safety Method Statement Management	The system must manage safety method statements for all heavy lift operations, requiring preparation and multi-level approval before any operation commences. Method statements must follow a configurable template covering scope of work, hazard identification, risk mitigation measures, emergency procedures, personnel competency requirements, and communication protocols. The approval chain must include the operations manager, safety officer, and client representative at minimum. The system must enforce that no lift operation booking can proceed to execution status without an approved method statement. Version control must track all revisions with reason-for-change documentation. Method statements must be viewable on mobile devices at the lift site and available in both Arabic and English languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R015	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Post-Lift Incident and Near-Miss Reporting	The system must provide a structured incident and near-miss reporting module for heavy lift operations with mandatory fields including date, time, location, personnel involved, equipment used, description of event, root cause analysis, and corrective actions. Reports must be classifiable by severity (near-miss, minor incident, major incident, fatality) with escalation rules per classification level. Photographic and video evidence must be attachable. The system must generate statistical dashboards showing incident trends by port, operation type, subcontractor, and time period. Corrective action tracking must ensure all identified actions are assigned owners and completion deadlines. Regulatory reporting requirements for QHSE authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India must be supported with jurisdiction-specific report templates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-001-R016	FEAT-025-2-001	MOD-025	Heavy Lift Insurance and Liability Management	The system must track insurance coverage for heavy lift operations including marine cargo insurance, third-party liability, equipment all-risk, and professional indemnity policies. Each heavy lift booking must be assessed against existing insurance coverage and any gaps must be flagged for additional cover arrangement. The system must calculate insurable values in the booking currency and convert to policy currency using applicable exchange rates for multi-currency operations across QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. Insurance certificates must be linked to bookings and verified before operations commence. Claims management must track incidents from initial notification through adjustment to settlement with full document trail. Premium allocation must be distributable across entities based on configurable rules for inter-company cost sharing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R001	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Multi-Modal Route Planning Engine	The system must provide a route planning engine that calculates optimal multi-modal transport routes for OOG cargo between origin and destination considering sea, road, rail, and inland waterway options. The engine must evaluate route feasibility based on cargo dimensions and weight against infrastructure constraints including bridge heights, road weight limits, tunnel dimensions, and rail loading gauge restrictions. Routes must be calculable for corridors including Qatar-UAE (via Hamad Port to Jebel Ali), UAE-India (Khalifa Port to JNPT), and intra-GCC movements. The engine must present multiple route options ranked by cost, transit time, and risk level with detailed leg-by-leg breakdowns. All route costs must be computed in USD with breakdowns in local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R002	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Intermodal Transfer Point Management	The system must maintain a database of intermodal transfer points (ports, rail yards, transshipment hubs) with their OOG handling capabilities including maximum piece weight, available crane capacity, storage area dimensions, and operating hours. For each transfer point, the system must store contact details, booking procedures, and applicable tariffs in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Transfer point capacity must be bookable through the system with confirmation workflows. The system must detect scheduling conflicts when multiple OOG shipments require the same transfer facility simultaneously. Seasonal constraints such as Ramadan operating hour changes and national holiday closures must be reflected in availability calendars. Transfer point data must be viewable in both Arabic and English with map-based visualization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R003	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Road Transport Permit Coordination for OOG	The system must manage abnormal load road transport permits across multiple jurisdictions including Qatar (Ministry of Transport), UAE (ITC/RTA), KSA (Ministry of Transport), and India (NHAI/State PWDs). Each jurisdiction must have a configurable permit application template capturing required fields such as vehicle configuration, axle loads, cargo dimensions, proposed route, travel dates, and escort requirements. The system must track permit application status through stages: drafted, submitted, under review, approved, or rejected. Permit validity periods and approved travel windows (typically nighttime only for super-heavy loads) must be enforced against transport scheduling. Automated reminders must be sent 7 days before permit expiry for recurring routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R004	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Rail Wagon Allocation for OOG Cargo	The system must interface with rail operators to manage wagon allocation for OOG cargo moving by rail, particularly relevant for Indian rail corridors to and from JNPT. The module must support wagon type selection based on cargo dimensions and weight including standard flat wagons, well wagons, and special low-bed wagons. The system must validate that cargo plus wagon dimensions fall within the rail loading gauge for the specific route including Over Dimensional Cargo (ODC) classifications used by Indian Railways. Wagon booking requests must capture cargo details, preferred loading and unloading dates, origin and destination rail terminals, and special handling instructions. Cost estimates must be generated in INR with conversion to the shipment's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R005	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Barge and Inland Waterway Transport Planning	The system must support planning and booking of barge transport for OOG cargo where inland waterway options exist, particularly for movements to and from Indian ports where river or coastal barge options may be viable. The module must capture barge specifications including deck strength (tonnes per square meter), beam width, draft restrictions, and available deck space. Route planning must account for tidal windows, lock dimensions, bridge air drafts, and seasonal water depth variations. The system must generate barge stowage plans showing cargo placement with stability calculations. Barge transport costs must be estimable in local currency with comparison against road alternatives. All barge bookings must be tracked with real-time status updates through the multi-modal shipment lifecycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R006	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Equipment Compatibility Validation Across Modes	The system must validate equipment compatibility when OOG cargo transitions between transport modes. Validation checks must include: trailer-to-vessel ramp capacity matching, rail wagon deck height compatibility with port crane reach, barge freeboard adequacy for port-side crane operations, and container spreader compatibility for flat rack handling. The system must maintain equipment specification databases for each transport mode and automatically flag incompatibilities when a multi-modal route is planned. When incompatibilities are detected, the system must suggest alternative equipment configurations or additional handling steps (e.g., requiring a mobile crane at the transfer point). Compatibility reports must be generated as part of the shipment planning documentation in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R007	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Real-Time OOG Shipment Tracking Across Modes	The system must provide unified real-time tracking of OOG cargo across all transport modes on a single dashboard. Tracking data sources must include vessel AIS feeds for sea legs, GPS tracking devices on road trailers, rail wagon tracking systems, and manual checkpoint updates for locations without automated tracking. The dashboard must display current cargo position on a map with estimated arrival times at the next transfer point and final destination. Status updates must be pushed to stakeholders via configurable channels (email, SMS, in-app notification) in their preferred language. The tracking module must maintain a complete movement history log with timestamps, locations, and responsible carrier per leg. Offline tracking data must be cached locally and synchronized when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R008	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Multi-Modal Cost Allocation and Settlement	The system must allocate total multi-modal transport costs across individual legs and settlement between carriers, agents, and subcontractors. Each transport leg must have its own cost structure including base freight, OOG surcharges, permit fees, escort costs, and handling charges in the local currency of the operation. The system must support cost settlement in the carrier's preferred currency with exchange rate application at the agreed rate date (booking date, loading date, or invoice date as per contract terms). Inter-company charges between group entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India must follow transfer pricing rules with proper documentation. Consolidated cost reports must show total shipment cost by mode, by leg, and by cost category with budget-to-actual variance analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R009	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Escort Vehicle and Police Coordination	The system must manage escort vehicle requirements and police coordination for abnormal load road movements across all operating jurisdictions. The module must determine escort requirements based on cargo dimensions and local regulations: typically front and rear escort vehicles for loads exceeding specific width or length thresholds (varying by jurisdiction). Police escort requirements must be identified for movements through urban areas or on specific highway segments. Escort vehicle bookings must capture vehicle type, driver details, communication equipment, and shift schedules for multi-day movements. The system must generate movement schedules showing departure times coordinated between the cargo vehicle, escort vehicles, and police units. Cost tracking must capture escort fees in local currency per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R010	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Multi-Modal Schedule Optimization with AI	The system must use AI algorithms to optimize scheduling of multi-modal OOG shipments considering vessel sailing schedules, port berth availability, road transport windows (nighttime restrictions), rail slot availability, weather windows for loading and discharge, and Hijri calendar public holiday closures. The optimizer must minimize total transit time while respecting all operational constraints and cost budgets. When schedule disruptions occur (vessel delay, permit rejection, weather cancellation), the AI must automatically propose alternative schedule scenarios with impact analysis on cost, transit time, and downstream leg connections. The system must learn from historical schedule performance to improve future scheduling accuracy. Schedule outputs must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R011	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Temperature and Environmental Monitoring for OOG Transit	The system must support environmental monitoring for sensitive OOG cargo during multi-modal transit including temperature, humidity, shock, and tilt sensors. Sensor data must be ingested from IoT devices attached to cargo pieces with configurable alert thresholds per cargo type. When environmental parameters exceed defined limits, the system must generate immediate alerts to operations staff and the shipper with recommended corrective actions. Historical sensor data must be stored and presentable as time-series graphs for the complete multi-modal journey. The monitoring module must support diverse sensor hardware manufacturers and communication protocols. Environmental monitoring reports must be generated as proof-of-condition documents for insurance and claims purposes with data integrity validation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R012	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Customs Transit Bond Management for Multi-Modal OOG	The system must manage customs transit bonds for OOG cargo moving through multiple customs jurisdictions in multi-modal configurations. For cargo transiting through free zones (e.g., Jebel Ali Free Zone) or moving under temporary import bonds, the system must track bond amounts in local currency, validity periods, guarantor details, and release conditions. When cargo crosses from one customs territory to another (e.g., Qatar to UAE via road), the system must ensure proper bond coverage for each jurisdiction and manage the handoff documentation. Bond amounts must be calculated based on cargo declared value in the customs currency of the relevant jurisdiction. The system must alert users when bonds approach expiry and track bond release requests through to confirmed release and bank guarantee discharge.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R013	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Consolidation and Deconsolidation Planning for OOG	The system must support planning of consolidation (grouping multiple OOG pieces for shared transport) and deconsolidation (separating grouped cargo) operations at intermodal transfer points. The consolidation planner must evaluate compatible cargo pieces based on dimensions, weight, destination proximity, and handling requirements to identify grouping opportunities that reduce per-unit transport costs. Weight and dimension limits for the proposed consolidated load must be validated against the transport equipment capacity for each subsequent leg. The system must generate consolidated loading plans showing piece placement sequence and must track individual piece identity throughout consolidation and deconsolidation. Cost savings from consolidation must be calculated and allocated proportionally across individual bookings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R014	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Last Mile Delivery Planning for OOG Cargo	The system must manage last-mile delivery planning for OOG cargo from the final port or rail terminal to the project site or warehouse. Last-mile planning must capture site access constraints including gate dimensions, internal road capacity, unloading area specifications, and available site cranage. The system must support site survey records with photographs, dimensional drawings, and access route details. Delivery scheduling must coordinate with site readiness status and receiving party availability. For deliveries in urban areas across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the system must account for municipal restrictions on oversized vehicle movements during peak traffic hours. Delivery confirmation must capture proof of delivery with photographic evidence, recipient signature, and condition assessment in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R015	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Multi-Modal Carbon Emission Calculation for OOG	The system must calculate and report carbon emissions for OOG cargo shipments across all transport modes in the multi-modal chain. Emission factors must be maintained per transport mode, vehicle or vessel type, fuel type, and route distance following IMO DCS and GLEC Framework methodologies. The system must compute emissions per transport leg and aggregate total shipment emissions in tonnes of CO2 equivalent. Comparative analysis must show emission differences between alternative routing options to support sustainable transport decisions. Emission reports must be generated per shipment, per customer, per trade lane, and per entity for corporate sustainability reporting requirements applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Reports must be exportable in standard formats for submission to regulatory authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R016	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Carrier Performance Scoring for OOG Multi-Modal	The system must maintain carrier performance scorecards for all transport providers (shipping lines, road hauliers, rail operators, barge operators) involved in OOG multi-modal logistics. Performance metrics must include on-time pickup and delivery rates, damage incident frequency, documentation accuracy, communication responsiveness, and pricing competitiveness. Scores must be calculated automatically from shipment data with manual adjustment capability for qualitative factors. The system must support quarterly performance review reports per carrier with trend analysis. Carrier scores must influence the AI route planning engine's carrier selection recommendations. Performance data must be segregated by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while allowing consolidated group-level analysis. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-002-R017	FEAT-025-2-002	MOD-025	Contingency Planning for Multi-Modal OOG Disruptions	The system must support pre-defined contingency plans for common multi-modal OOG disruption scenarios including port congestion, vessel omission, road closure, rail cancellation, customs hold, and equipment breakdown. Each contingency plan must define trigger conditions, alternative actions, responsible parties, cost implications, and communication templates. When a disruption event is logged against a shipment, the system must automatically suggest applicable contingency plans and facilitate execution tracking. The system must maintain a library of contingency plans categorized by disruption type and geographic region. AI must analyze historical disruption patterns to recommend proactive contingency planning for upcoming shipments on high-risk routes. Contingency plans must be reviewable and editable in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R001	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	OOG Shipping Instruction Template Management	The system must provide configurable shipping instruction templates specifically designed for OOG cargo that capture additional fields beyond standard container shipments. Templates must include fields for cargo dimensions per piece (length, width, height, weight), flat rack or open top container assignments, overstow prohibition flags, special stowage instructions, and lashing requirements. Templates must be maintained per trade lane and per shipping line as each carrier has specific OOG documentation requirements. The system must support bilingual template rendering in Arabic and English with proper RTL layout for Arabic content. Template versioning must track changes over time with the ability to revert to previous versions. Auto-population from booking data must minimize manual data entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R002	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Technical Drawing and Cargo Plan Repository	The system must provide a centralized repository for technical drawings, cargo plans, and engineering documents associated with OOG shipments. Supported document formats must include PDF, DWG, DXF, and standard image formats. Each document must be tagged with metadata including cargo reference, document type, revision number, author, and approval status. The repository must support version control with the ability to compare revisions side by side. Access control must restrict viewing and editing based on user roles and entity affiliation (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India entities). The system must generate thumbnail previews for quick browsing and support full-text search across document metadata. Storage must comply with data residency requirements applicable to each operating jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R003	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Bill of Lading Generation for OOG Cargo	The system must generate bills of lading specifically formatted for OOG cargo including detailed cargo descriptions with individual piece dimensions, flat rack container numbers, and special clauses for oversized cargo liability. The BL must comply with the Hague-Visby Rules and any applicable local maritime law requirements. OOG-specific clauses must address shipper-loaded and lashed cargo responsibility, deck stowage consent, and weather risk acceptance as applicable. The system must support both original and waybill BL types with configurable number of originals. BL generation must support multi-currency freight display (prepaid in origin currency or collect in destination currency). The BL must render correctly in both Arabic and English with proper font handling for Arabic characters and numerals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R004	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Customs Declaration Preparation for OOG Cargo	The system must prepare customs declarations for OOG cargo tailored to each jurisdiction's requirements: Qatar Customs (HS code classification with QAR values), Dubai Customs (AED values with JAFZA specific formats), Saudi Customs (FASAH system compatible SAR declarations), and Indian Customs (INR values with ICEGATE EDI format). OOG-specific customs fields must include detailed dimension declarations, temporary import indicators for project cargo, re-export bonds where applicable, and duty exemption references for industrial equipment under development incentive programs. The system must validate HS code consistency across origin and destination declarations. Exchange rates for customs valuation must use the official rates prescribed by each customs authority on the applicable date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R005	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Survey Certificate Management and Tracking	The system must track all survey certificates required for OOG shipments including pre-shipment survey certificates, weight certificates, packing certificates, lashing certificates, and condition survey certificates at loading and discharge. Each certificate must be linked to the specific cargo booking and must capture surveyor name, company, qualification reference, survey date, survey location, and findings summary. The system must enforce that mandatory certificates are uploaded and verified before key operational milestones (e.g., no loading without approved lashing certificate). Certificate expiry tracking must alert operations when time-limited certificates approach expiration. The system must maintain a registry of approved survey companies per port location across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R006	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Packing List Generation for Project Cargo Shipments	The system must generate detailed packing lists for project cargo shipments containing multiple OOG pieces. Each packing list entry must include piece mark number, description, gross weight, net weight, dimensions (L x W x H), volume, packaging type, and associated container or flat rack number. The packing list must be sortable by piece mark, weight, or container assignment. Totals must be automatically calculated for weight, volume, and piece count. The system must support hierarchical packing where sub-components are grouped under main equipment items. Packing lists must be exportable in Excel, PDF, and CSV formats with bilingual Arabic/English headers. The system must validate that packing list totals reconcile with booking quantities and weights.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R007	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Dangerous Goods Documentation for OOG Hazmat Cargo	The system must manage dangerous goods documentation for OOG cargo that also carries hazardous material classifications (e.g., large transformers with oil fill, industrial batteries, chemical processing equipment with residual contents). Documentation must comply with IMDG Code requirements including DG declaration forms, emergency procedures (EmS), and material safety data sheets (MSDS). The system must validate that DG class, UN number, packing group, and quantity are correctly declared and that OOG stowage positions comply with segregation rules from the IMDG Code. For multi-modal shipments, the system must generate mode-specific DG documents (IMO declaration for sea, ADR/local equivalent for road). All DG documentation must be available in English as the international standard and Arabic for local authority submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R008	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Insurance Certificate and Policy Documentation	The system must generate and manage insurance documentation for OOG cargo shipments including insurance certificates, cover notes, and policy endorsements. The module must capture insured values per cargo piece in the shipment currency and convert to the policy currency using the applicable exchange rate. Special coverage clauses for OOG risks (heavy lift operations, open deck stowage, inland transit of oversized loads) must be documented with specific policy references. The system must validate that insurance coverage matches the declared cargo value and covers all transport legs including intermodal transfers. Certificate generation must follow Institute Cargo Clauses (A), (B), or (C) as applicable with OOG-specific extensions. Certificates must be printable in both Arabic and English for presentation to customs and port authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R009	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Letter of Credit Documentation for OOG Shipments	The system must support letter of credit compliance checking for OOG shipment documentation ensuring all documents conform to LC terms and UCP 600 requirements. The module must validate that cargo descriptions on all documents (BL, invoice, packing list, certificate of origin) are consistent with LC terms and do not contain discrepancies. OOG-specific LC clauses regarding deck stowage, transshipment permissions, and partial shipment allowance must be flagged for attention. The system must maintain a checklist of required documents per LC and track submission status. Document presentation deadlines must be calculated from the BL date per LC terms and displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. Multi-currency LC amounts must be validated against invoice values with exchange rate tolerance checking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R010	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Certificate of Origin for OOG Cargo	The system must generate certificates of origin for OOG cargo in formats required by destination countries including standard CO, Form A (GSP), GCC unified CO, and bilateral FTA certificates. For cargo moving between GCC countries (Qatar, UAE, KSA), the GCC unified certificate of origin must be generated with proper Arabic text and attestation language. For cargo destined for India from GCC origins, applicable FTA certificate formats must be supported. The system must capture manufacturing origin details per cargo piece when a single shipment contains items from multiple origins. Chamber of Commerce attestation workflow must be supported with document tracking through submission, processing, and collection stages. Fees must be tracked in the local currency of the attesting chamber.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R011	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Fumigation and Phytosanitary Certificate Management	The system must manage fumigation certificates and phytosanitary documentation for OOG cargo using wooden packaging materials (crates, dunnage, blocking, bracing) in compliance with ISPM-15 standards. The module must track which cargo pieces use wooden packaging and verify that fumigation treatment (methyl bromide or heat treatment) has been completed with valid certification. Fumigation certificate details must include treatment date, chemical used, concentration, exposure duration, and temperature. The system must alert when cargo with wooden packaging is booked to destinations with strict phytosanitary enforcement (particularly India and KSA). Treatment provider details and certification validity must be recorded. Documents must be generated in English as the international standard with Arabic translations available for local authority submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R012	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	OOG Cargo Photo Documentation Workflow	The system must provide a structured photo documentation workflow for OOG cargo capturing images at each stage: pre-loading condition at origin, loading onto vessel or transport, stowage position, lashing arrangement, discharge operation, and delivery condition. Each photo must be timestamped, geotagged, and linked to the specific cargo piece and booking reference. The workflow must define minimum required photo angles and counts per stage (e.g., minimum 4 angles for pre-loading condition). Photos must be stored at original resolution for evidentiary purposes while generating compressed thumbnails for quick viewing. The system must support offline photo capture on mobile devices with batch upload when connectivity is restored. Photo documentation packages must be compilable into PDF reports for surveyor review and insurance claim support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R013	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Automated Document Compliance Checker	The system must provide an AI-powered document compliance checker that validates OOG shipment documentation packages against regulatory requirements, LC terms, and customer-specific documentation instructions. The checker must verify consistency of cargo descriptions, weights, dimensions, and values across all documents in the package. Missing mandatory documents must be identified based on the shipment route, cargo type, and applicable regulations. The checker must flag common discrepancies such as weight mismatches between BL and packing list, dimension inconsistencies between shipping instructions and survey certificates, and currency or amount discrepancies between commercial invoice and customs declaration. Compliance check results must be presented as a pass/fail checklist with specific remediation guidance for each failed item in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R014	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Electronic Document Submission to Authorities	The system must support electronic submission of OOG documentation to port and customs authorities through their respective digital platforms. Integration must be supported for Qatar Single Window (Al Nadeeb), Dubai Trade Portal, Saudi FASAH system, and Indian ICEGATE/PCS platforms. The system must format and transform internal document data to match each authority's required submission format (XML, JSON, EDI as applicable). Submission status must be tracked with automated retry for transient failures and escalation for rejected submissions. Acknowledgment receipts from authorities must be stored against the shipment record. The system must handle multi-entity submissions where the declaring entity differs by jurisdiction. Submission logs must capture timestamps, user identity, and authority response for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R015	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Document Archival and Retention Management	The system must implement document archival and retention policies for OOG shipment documentation in compliance with legal requirements across all operating jurisdictions. Retention periods must be configurable per document type and jurisdiction: typically 7 years for commercial documents in GCC countries and 8 years for Indian customs documents. Archived documents must be retrievable through search by booking reference, BL number, vessel name, date range, or cargo description. The system must support both hot storage (immediately accessible) and cold storage (archived with retrieval delay) tiers to optimize storage costs. Automated purging must delete documents beyond their retention period with audit-logged destruction certificates. Data residency requirements must ensure documents related to each jurisdiction are stored in compliant data center locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R016	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	Power of Attorney and Authorization Letter Management	The system must manage powers of attorney (POA) and authorization letters required for customs clearance, port operations, and permit applications for OOG cargo across all operating jurisdictions. Each POA must capture the grantor entity, authorized representative, scope of authority, validity period, and applicable jurisdiction. The system must track POA validity and alert users 30 days before expiration. For operations in KSA and Qatar, the system must support POA documents with Arabic text and notarization tracking. The module must generate authorization letter templates for specific operations such as customs clearance, cargo release, and permit applications with auto-populated shipment and entity details. POA documents must be verifiable by port and customs authorities through a secure reference code system linked to each authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-003-R017	FEAT-025-2-003	MOD-025	OOG Cargo Manifest Preparation and Submission	The system must generate OOG-specific cargo manifest entries with enhanced detail fields beyond standard container manifests. OOG manifest entries must include per-piece dimensions, overhang measurements (over-length, over-width, over-height from flat rack edges), total weight including dunnage and lashing materials, and stowage position references on the vessel. The manifest must comply with IMO FAL Convention format requirements and any port-specific supplementary format requirements for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Pre-arrival manifest submission timelines must be enforced per destination port regulations (typically 24-48 hours before vessel arrival). The system must validate manifest data against booking and BL data for consistency. Manifest amendments must be trackable with reason codes and authority approval status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R001	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Authority Approval Request Workflow	The system must provide a configurable workflow engine for managing port authority approval requests for OOG cargo operations. Each approval request must capture the requesting entity, port of operation, operation type (heavy lift, oversized cargo discharge, special equipment usage), proposed dates, cargo details, and supporting documentation. Workflows must be configurable per port authority with Hamad Port (Mwani Qatar), Khalifa Port (Abu Dhabi Ports), Jebel Ali (DP World), and JNPT (JNPT Trust) each having distinct approval steps, required documents, and processing timelines. The system must support multi-stage approvals where initial technical review precedes commercial approval. Status tracking must show current approval stage, pending actions, and estimated completion dates with notifications in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R002	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Berth Allocation Request for Heavy Lift Vessels	The system must manage berth allocation requests for vessels carrying heavy lift OOG cargo requiring specialized berth characteristics. Requests must specify required berth parameters including quay crane capacity, minimum quay length, water depth alongside, bollard pull capacity for mooring, and quay surface load bearing capacity in tonnes per square meter. The system must interface with port berth planning systems to check availability and submit allocation requests. For Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must support priority berth requests for project cargo vessels with justification workflows. Berth allocation confirmations must capture the assigned berth number, approved operational window (start and end datetime), and any restrictions. Berth allocation costs must be tracked in the port's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R003	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Quay Strength Assessment Request Management	The system must manage quay strength assessment requests required when heavy lift cargo ground pressure exceeds standard quay design loads. Assessment requests must include detailed cargo weight distribution data, proposed grounding arrangement (timber spreaders, steel plates, concrete pads), contact area dimensions, and transport equipment (trailer axle loads). The system must track assessment requests through submission, engineering review, conditional approval, and final clearance stages. Port engineering department feedback including required load spreading measures must be captured and communicated to operations teams. The system must maintain historical quay assessment records per berth and port to expedite repeat assessments for similar cargo. Assessment documentation must be stored in both Arabic and English as required by the respective port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R004	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Harbor Master Operational Window Approval	The system must manage harbor master approvals for operational windows during which OOG cargo operations (heavy lifts, oversized cargo movements) will be conducted. Approval requests must specify the proposed operation start and end times, type of operation, impact on adjacent berth operations, and any navigational restrictions required during the operation. For operations requiring vessel crane usage or floating crane alongside, the system must include stability and mooring assessments. The system must track harbor master response with approved window times, conditions imposed, and any navigation notices to be issued. Tidal window constraints for draft-restricted vessels must be automatically calculated and included in the request. Approved windows must integrate with the operational scheduling calendar showing both Gregorian and Hijri dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R005	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Terminal Equipment Request and Allocation	The system must manage requests for terminal equipment needed for OOG cargo operations including shore cranes, mobile cranes, reach stackers with special attachments, rubber-tired gantries, and special lifting equipment (spreader bars, slings). Each request must specify equipment type, required capacity, duration of use, and operational location within the terminal. The system must check equipment availability against the terminal's equipment database and existing reservations. For equipment not available at the terminal, the system must support third-party equipment sourcing with subcontractor management. Equipment allocation confirmations must include operator assignment, mobilization schedule, and applicable tariff rates in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must track actual usage hours against the original allocation for billing reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R006	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Security Approval for OOG Operations	The system must manage ISPS Code compliant security approvals for OOG operations that require non-standard port access, extended gate hours, or escort within restricted terminal areas. Security approval requests must capture personnel details for non-regular port visitors (crane operators, surveyors, riggers), vehicle registration for non-standard equipment entering the terminal, and proposed access schedules. The system must interface with port security systems for ID pass issuance tracking. For operations at Hamad Port and JNPT, advance security clearance requirements (typically 48-72 hours) must be enforced through the workflow. The system must manage temporary access cards with automatic expiry and return tracking. Security incident reporting during OOG operations must be integrated with the main incident reporting module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R007	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Environmental Compliance Approval for Port Operations	The system must manage environmental compliance approvals required for OOG operations at ports, including noise level assessments for nighttime operations, dust suppression measures for open cargo handling, and oil spill prevention plans for heavy equipment operations near water. Environmental impact assessments must be completed per port authority requirements before approval is granted. For operations in Qatar and UAE, the system must address specific environmental regulations regarding marine environment protection in the Arabian Gulf. The system must track environmental monitoring obligations during operations and post-operation cleanup confirmations. Environmental compliance documents must be maintained in both Arabic and English. The system must generate environmental compliance checklists tailored to each port's specific requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R008	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Free Zone Authority Approval for OOG Cargo	The system must manage approvals from free zone authorities for OOG cargo operations within free zone boundaries including Jebel Ali Free Zone (JAFZA), Khalifa Industrial Zone Abu Dhabi (KIZAD), Qatar Free Zones Authority, and Indian SEZ authorities near JNPT. Approval requests must address free zone specific requirements including temporary storage permits for oversized cargo, special transport route permits within the free zone, and handling equipment authorization. The system must track free zone duty status of OOG cargo (in-zone, ex-zone, transit) and ensure proper documentation for zone entry and exit. Free zone tariffs and handling charges must be captured in the applicable local currency. Integration with free zone portal systems must support electronic submission and status tracking of approval requests.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R009	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Pilotage and Towage Special Requirements Approval	The system must manage special pilotage and towage requirement approvals for vessels carrying significant deck cargo that affects vessel windage area, visibility, and maneuverability. The system must capture vessel details including OOG cargo stowage plan, resulting vessel air draft, additional windage calculations, and any visibility obstructions from deck cargo. Pilotage authority requests must be submitted to harbor pilot services at each port with sufficient advance notice (configurable per port, typically 72 hours). Towage requirements including number and capacity of tugs must be determined based on vessel size plus OOG cargo windage factors. Approval confirmations must include assigned pilot boarding time, tug rendezvous schedule, and any speed or daylight restrictions. Pilotage and towage charges must be estimated and tracked in the port's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R010	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Tariff Calculation for OOG Operations	The system must calculate and apply port tariffs specific to OOG cargo operations based on each port authority's published tariff schedule. OOG tariff components must include oversized cargo surcharges, special handling fees, extended berth occupation charges, special equipment usage fees, and additional labor charges. Tariff schedules must be maintained per port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) in their respective local currencies. The system must handle tariff structures that vary by cargo weight tiers, dimension categories, and operation complexity levels. Tariff calculations must be auditable with line-item breakdown showing the applicable tariff rule for each charge. The system must support tariff schedule updates when port authorities publish revised rates and must flag any bookings affected by rate changes between quotation and operation dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R011	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Storage and Yard Allocation for OOG Cargo	The system must manage storage and yard space allocation for OOG cargo that cannot be stored in standard container stacks. Storage requests must specify required ground area (length x width), surface load bearing requirements, security fencing needs, and weather protection requirements. The system must track available open storage areas at each port with their specifications and current utilization. Storage duration estimates must be provided with tariff calculations in local currency including any free storage period applicable per the port tariff. The system must alert when OOG cargo approaches free storage expiry to avoid demurrage charges. For long-term project cargo storage, the system must support storage lease agreements with periodic billing. Yard allocation maps must be visualizable showing OOG cargo placement relative to crane reach and transport routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R012	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Multi-Port Authority Coordination for Transshipment OOG	The system must coordinate approvals across multiple port authorities when OOG cargo requires transshipment. For example, cargo discharged at Jebel Ali for transshipment to Hamad Port requires approvals from both DP World and Mwani Qatar. The system must manage parallel approval workflows with dependency tracking (e.g., destination port approval may require origin port discharge confirmation). Transshipment timelines must be coordinated to ensure connecting vessel berth windows align with discharge and reload operations. The system must track customs transit status during transshipment to maintain cargo bonded status between jurisdictions. Communication between port authorities must be facilitated through the system with shared document visibility. All costs must be segregated by port in respective local currencies with consolidated total in the shipment base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R013	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Authority Communication Log and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive communication log of all interactions with port authorities regarding OOG cargo operations. Each communication entry must capture the date, time, port authority contact person, communication channel (email, portal, phone, in-person), subject, content summary, and any commitments or action items arising. Emails exchanged with port authorities must be automatically captured and linked to the relevant booking and approval request. The communication log must be searchable by booking reference, port, date range, and topic. All approval decisions must have a documented audit trail showing who approved, when, under what conditions, and based on which documents. The audit trail must be exportable for regulatory review purposes and must comply with data retention policies for each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R014	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Authority SLA Monitoring and Escalation	The system must monitor port authority response times against expected service level agreements for OOG approval requests. Configurable SLA timelines must be maintained per port authority and per approval type (e.g., berth allocation response within 48 hours, quay assessment within 5 working days). The system must generate automated reminders to internal teams when SLA thresholds approach without response. Escalation workflows must be triggered when SLAs are breached, routing to senior management with context about the pending request and its impact on operational schedules. SLA performance dashboards must show approval turnaround metrics per port authority with trend analysis. Hijri calendar working days must be used for SLA calculation in GCC jurisdictions, accounting for Friday-Saturday weekends in Qatar and UAE, and Friday-only weekends in KSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R015	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Vessel Nomination and Acceptance for OOG at Port	The system must manage the vessel nomination and acceptance process with port authorities for vessels carrying OOG cargo. Vessel nominations must include vessel particulars (name, IMO number, flag, class, dimensions), expected arrival date, OOG cargo manifest summary, and required terminal services. The port authority must be able to review and accept or request modifications to the vessel nomination. For vessels requiring special berth assignments due to OOG cargo crane operations, the nomination must include vessel gear specifications and planned operation methodology. The system must track nomination status through submitted, under review, conditionally accepted, and fully confirmed stages. Nomination deadlines relative to vessel ETA must be enforced per port requirements. All nomination correspondence must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R016	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Authority Fee Payment Tracking	The system must track payment of all port authority fees related to OOG cargo operations including approval fees, inspection fees, special handling charges, berth overtime charges, and penalty fees. Payments must be tracked per port authority in their respective local currencies (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT). The system must support multiple payment methods as accepted by each port authority including direct bank transfer, port authority online payment portals, and credit facilities. Payment due dates must be calculated from approval or operation dates per port authority payment terms. Overdue payments must trigger alerts and must be flagged as potential blockers for future approval requests. Payment receipts must be stored against the relevant booking and approval record. Reconciliation reports must match port authority invoices against system-calculated amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R017	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Regulatory Change Monitoring for Port OOG Rules	The system must provide a regulatory change monitoring module that tracks updates to port authority regulations, tariffs, and procedures affecting OOG cargo operations across all operating ports. The module must maintain a registry of applicable regulations per port with effective dates and version history. When regulatory changes are identified (through manual input or automated feed monitoring), the system must assess impact on existing OOG bookings and pending approval requests. Affected bookings must be flagged and operations teams notified with a summary of required actions. The system must support AI-assisted analysis of regulatory documents to extract relevant changes. A regulatory compliance calendar must show upcoming effective dates for new regulations. Regulatory content must be stored and searchable in both Arabic and English to support the bilingual operating environment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-025-2-004-R018	FEAT-025-2-004	MOD-025	Port Authority Digital Integration and API Management	The system must support digital integration with port authority systems through APIs, EDI, or web service interfaces where available. Integration must be configurable per port authority to accommodate varying levels of digital maturity: full API integration for advanced ports like Jebel Ali (DP World), portal-based submission for Hamad Port, and manual submission fallback for authorities without digital interfaces. The system must manage API credentials, rate limits, and connectivity monitoring for each integration endpoint. Integration message formats must comply with port community system standards applicable to each port. Failed submissions must be retried automatically with exponential backoff and manual intervention alerts after configurable retry limits. Integration logs must capture all request and response payloads for troubleshooting and audit purposes. The system must support offline queuing of submissions when port authority systems are temporarily unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R001	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Facility Master Registration	The system must maintain a comprehensive ICD facility master register supporting multiple inland container depots across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each ICD record must capture facility code (aligned with UN/LOCODE), geographic coordinates, total yard capacity in TEU, number of rail sidings, truck bays, reefer plug points, hazmat storage zones, and bonded area designations. The facility master must support bilingual naming in Arabic and English with RTL rendering for Arabic UI. Facility status must track operational, under-maintenance, and decommissioned states. The system must auto-populate Jebel Ali ICD (AEJEA-ICD), JAFZA South, and Dubai Logistics Corridor facilities as default seed data upon deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R002	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Container Yard Slot Allocation with AI Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven yard slot allocation for containers arriving at any registered ICD facility. The allocation engine must consider container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), weight class, hazmat classification (IMDG classes 1-9), dwell-time prediction, and onward transport mode (rail, truck, or barge). The AI model must optimize for minimum rehandling moves, grouping containers by next destination port (AEJEA, AEKHL, QAHAM, INNSA) and vessel cut-off dates. Slot assignments must be displayed on an interactive yard map with real-time occupancy heat maps, supporting both Arabic and English labels. The system must handle edge cases where yard capacity exceeds 90% by triggering overflow alerts and suggesting alternative ICD facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R003	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Container Gate-In Processing at ICD	The system must record every container gate-in event at an ICD facility capturing container number (ISO 6346 validated), seal number, truck plate number, driver ID (Emirates ID, QID, Iqama, or Aadhaar), gross weight from weigh-bridge integration, and container condition survey (CSC inspection). Gate-in timestamps must be recorded in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats with timezone awareness for UAE (GST+4), Qatar (AST+3), KSA (AST+3), and India (IST+5:30). The system must auto-match gate-in containers against pre-advised delivery orders (DO) and flag any discrepancies in container number, size/type, or consignee. Offline gate-in must be supported with local data capture and automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored, ensuring no duplicate entries through idempotent transaction processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R004	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Container Gate-Out Authorization and Release	The system must enforce a multi-checkpoint gate-out authorization workflow before releasing any container from the ICD. Gate-out prerequisites must include valid delivery order status, customs clearance confirmation (linked to customs declaration number), payment clearance for all ICD charges (storage, handling, reefer monitoring), and transport booking confirmation. The system must validate that the collecting truck and driver match the pre-authorized transport booking. Gate-out charges must be calculated in the local currency of the ICD entity (AED for UAE, QAR for Qatar, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic FX conversion for cross-entity billing at the daily treasury rate. The system must generate a gate-out pass with QR code containing container number, destination, and authorization reference, printable in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R005	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Dwell Time Monitoring and Demurrage Calculation	The system must continuously monitor container dwell time at each ICD from gate-in to gate-out, calculating free-time allowances based on the applicable tariff schedule per entity and customer contract. Free-time rules must support differentiation by container type (dry, reefer, hazmat, over-dimensional), import versus export, and customer tier (standard, premium, VIP). Demurrage charges must be computed automatically in the entity's base currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with configurable slab-based escalation rates (e.g., days 1-5 free, days 6-10 at rate X, days 11+ at rate Y). The system must generate AI-powered alerts at 80% and 100% of free-time consumption, recommending actions such as expediting customs clearance or arranging transport. Demurrage invoices must integrate with the ERP billing module and support Arabic and English invoice rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R006	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	CFS Destuffing and Restuffing Operations	The system must manage Container Freight Station (CFS) operations including destuffing (unloading LCL cargo from containers) and restuffing (consolidating LCL cargo into containers). Each CFS job must capture the parent container number, individual cargo package details (marks and numbers, package count, weight, volume in CBM), consignee allocation, and warehouse bin location assignment. The system must generate a CFS tally sheet for each operation with line-item reconciliation against the original bill of lading manifest. Discrepancies such as shortage, excess, or damage must be recorded with photographic evidence attachments. CFS charges must be computed per package, per CBM, or per weight ton (whichever yields higher revenue), supporting multi-currency billing where the shipper and consignee operate in different entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R007	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Reefer Container Monitoring at ICD	The system must provide continuous temperature monitoring for reefer containers stored at ICD facilities, integrating with IoT temperature sensors and reefer unit telemetry via API. Temperature readings must be logged at configurable intervals (default every 15 minutes) with automatic alerting when temperature deviates beyond the prescribed range specified in the booking (e.g., -25°C for frozen cargo, +2°C to +8°C for pharmaceuticals). The system must maintain a complete temperature audit trail per container compliant with FSMA and UAE ESMA food safety regulations. Reefer plug-in and plug-out events must be timestamped and linked to the container's ICD stay record. Power consumption must be metered per container for accurate billing. The monitoring dashboard must support Arabic and English with RTL layout switching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R008	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Hazmat Container Segregation Enforcement	The system must enforce IMDG Code segregation rules for hazardous cargo containers stored at any ICD facility. When a hazmat container is assigned a yard slot, the system must validate the proposed location against all adjacent containers' IMDG classifications, ensuring compliance with segregation categories (away from, separated from, separated by one compartment, separated by two compartments). The system must reference the IMDG segregation matrix covering all nine hazard classes and their divisions. Violations must be blocked with a clear error message in Arabic or English indicating the conflicting container and the specific IMDG rule violated. The system must maintain an emergency response plan reference for each hazmat container, linked to the MSDS (Material Safety Data Sheet) on file, accessible offline for emergency responders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R009	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Inventory Dashboard and Real-Time Analytics	The system must provide a real-time ICD inventory dashboard displaying current container count by status (awaiting customs, cleared, ready for dispatch, under inspection), container type, dwell-time distribution, and yard utilization percentage. The dashboard must support filtering by ICD facility, entity, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and container owner (shipping line). AI-powered analytics must include dwell-time trend prediction, throughput forecasting for the next 7/14/30 days, and bottleneck identification (e.g., customs clearance delays, transport booking shortages). All charts and data tables must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts. The dashboard must auto-refresh at configurable intervals and support export to PDF and Excel with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R010	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Multi-Entity ICD Billing and Inter-Company Settlement	The system must support ICD billing across multiple legal entities where a container may be received at a UAE ICD but billed to a Qatar or KSA entity. Inter-company charges must be recorded with proper transfer pricing references, generating matching debit and credit entries in each entity's ledger. Billing must support multi-currency with automatic conversion using configurable exchange rate sources (Central Bank rates, treasury fixed rates, or spot rates). VAT/GST treatment must follow each jurisdiction's rules: 5% VAT in UAE and KSA, 0% in Qatar free zones, and 18% GST in India. Invoice generation must comply with ZATCA e-invoicing requirements for KSA, FTA requirements for UAE, and GST e-invoice requirements for India. All financial documents must be renderable in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R011	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD-to-Port Container Movement Tracking	The system must track the complete movement chain of containers from ICD to destination port, providing end-to-end visibility. Each movement leg must record origin ICD, destination port (AEJEA, AEKHL, QAHAM, INNSA), transport mode (truck or rail), carrier details, departure timestamp, estimated arrival, and actual arrival. The system must integrate with port community systems (e.g., Dubai Trade for Jebel Ali, Dhow portal for Hamad Port) to confirm container receipt at the port terminal. Movement status updates must be pushed to stakeholders (shipper, consignee, customs broker, freight forwarder) via configurable notification channels (email, SMS, WhatsApp, in-app). GPS tracking data from transport providers must be aggregated to display container position on a map interface. Any delay beyond the estimated arrival must trigger AI-driven root cause analysis and revised ETA calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R012	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Appointment Scheduling and Slot Management	The system must provide an appointment scheduling module for truck arrivals and departures at ICD facilities to prevent congestion and optimize gate throughput. Time slots must be configurable per ICD facility with capacity limits per 30-minute window for both gate-in and gate-out operations. Appointment booking must be available via web portal and mobile app with Arabic and English interfaces. The system must enforce appointment compliance by rejecting trucks arriving outside their booked window unless manually overridden by an authorized gate supervisor. No-show appointments must be automatically cancelled after a configurable grace period (default 60 minutes) and the slot released for rebooking. AI must analyze historical arrival patterns to recommend optimal slot capacity distribution across peak and off-peak hours, adjusting for day-of-week, Hijri calendar events (Ramadan, Eid), and seasonal cargo volume variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R013	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Equipment and Resource Management	The system must track all material handling equipment (MHE) at each ICD facility including reach stackers, forklifts, top lifters, side loaders, and trailer chassis. Each equipment record must capture asset ID, type, capacity (tonnage), current operational status (available, in-use, under-maintenance, decommissioned), and maintenance schedule. The system must auto-assign equipment to ICD jobs (gate-in lift, yard shift, loading/unloading) based on container weight, type (standard, reefer requiring genset, over-dimensional requiring special spreaders), and equipment availability. Equipment utilization reports must be generated daily showing hours operated, idle time, and jobs completed. Maintenance alerts must be triggered based on operating hours or calendar intervals, integrating with the ERP asset management module. Equipment costs must be allocated to ICD operations for accurate job costing in the entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R014	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Labour and Shift Management	The system must manage ICD workforce allocation across shifts, tracking gang composition for destuffing, stuffing, and yard operations. Shift patterns must be configurable per ICD facility respecting local labour laws (UAE, Qatar, KSA, India) including maximum working hours, mandatory rest periods, and summer outdoor work restrictions (UAE midday ban June-September). Each labour gang must be assigned to specific jobs with start and end timestamps for productivity measurement. The system must calculate labour costs per job based on shift rates, overtime multipliers, and allowances in the entity's local currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR). Productivity KPIs must include containers handled per gang-hour, average destuffing time, and delay attribution. The Arabic UI must display worker names and gang identifiers in Arabic script where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R015	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	ICD Compliance and Regulatory Reporting	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports required by each jurisdiction's port and customs authorities. For Jebel Ali ICD, reports must conform to Dubai Customs and JAFZA requirements including periodic inventory declarations and bonded cargo movement statements. For KSA ICDs, reports must comply with ZATCA and Saudi Ports Authority formats. For Qatar, reports must align with Qatar General Authority of Customs (QGAC) requirements. For India, reports must comply with CBIC (Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs) ICD licensing conditions including monthly throughput returns and bonded warehouse registers. All reports must support Hijri date formatting where required by the regulatory authority, dual-language output in Arabic and English, and digital signature for electronic submission. The system must maintain an audit trail of all report submissions with timestamp, submitter identity, and authority acknowledgment reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-001-R016	FEAT-026-1-001	MOD-026	Offline ICD Operations and Data Synchronization	The system must support full offline operation of critical ICD functions including gate-in, gate-out, yard slot assignment, and CFS tally recording when network connectivity to the central ERP is unavailable. Offline mode must utilize a local data store on the ICD workstation or mobile device, capturing all transaction data with locally generated unique identifiers to prevent duplication upon sync. When connectivity is restored, the system must perform automatic bi-directional synchronization with conflict resolution rules: server-side data wins for master data updates, while transactional data from offline operations is always preserved. Synchronization must handle multi-currency amounts by applying the exchange rate valid at the original transaction timestamp, not the sync timestamp. The offline capability must support Arabic and English UI rendering without requiring server-side language resource loading. Sync status indicators must clearly show last-sync time and pending upload count.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R001	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Operator and Route Master Configuration	The system must maintain a master registry of rail operators, routes, and schedules relevant to container intermodal operations. Rail operators must include Etihad Rail (UAE), Saudi Arabia Railways (SAR), Qatar Rail (future), and Indian Railways/CONCOR with their respective operating licenses, contact details, and commercial contract references. Each route must define origin and destination terminals (mapped to ICD facility codes and port UN/LOCODEs), intermediate stops, total distance in kilometers, standard transit time, maximum train length (number of wagons), and axle load limits per route segment. Route schedules must support recurring timetables (daily, specific weekdays) and ad-hoc special services. All route and schedule data must be displayed in Arabic and English with support for Hijri calendar date selection when planning future services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R002	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Wagon Fleet Management and Availability Tracking	The system must track the complete wagon fleet available for container transport, recording wagon number, type (flat wagon, well wagon, spine wagon), TEU capacity, maximum payload in tonnes, current location (ICD, port, in-transit, maintenance depot), and ownership (own fleet, leased, rail operator pool). Wagon status must cycle through available, loaded, in-transit, at-destination-awaiting-unload, under-maintenance, and condemned states. The system must provide real-time wagon availability counts by location, type, and status for train planners. Maintenance schedules must trigger automatic withdrawal of wagons approaching periodic overhaul dates based on kilometers run or calendar intervals. The AI engine must predict wagon availability at each location 7 days forward based on current transit schedules and historical turnaround times, accounting for cross-entity wagon interchange between UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R003	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	AI-Driven Train Consist Planning	The system must provide AI-powered train consist planning that optimally assigns containers to wagons and sequences wagons into a complete train formation. The AI planner must consider container weight distribution to maintain train balance and comply with axle load limits per route segment, container destination sequencing to minimize shunting at intermediate stops, hazmat segregation requirements between wagons carrying IMDG-classified containers, and reefer container placement near power supply points. The planner must respect the maximum train length for the specific route, maximum trailing tonnage for the assigned locomotive class, and any height/width gauge restrictions for the corridor. The system must generate a train plan document showing wagon sequence, container-to-wagon assignments, total train weight, and estimated departure and arrival times. Plans must be reviewable in both Arabic and English with graphical train formation diagrams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R004	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Slot Booking and Capacity Reservation	The system must enable booking of rail transport slots with integrated capacity management across all configured rail operators. Bookings must specify origin terminal, destination terminal, preferred departure date and time, number of TEUs, container type mix (dry, reefer, hazmat, OOG), and total estimated weight. The system must check real-time slot availability against the rail operator's published capacity and existing bookings, displaying available slots in a calendar view supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date formats. Overbooking must be prevented by enforcing hard capacity limits per train service. Booking confirmation must generate a unique rail consignment note number following the operator's numbering convention. Cancellation and amendment policies must be configurable per rail operator with automatic penalty charge calculation in the operator's billing currency (AED, SAR, INR). Booking data must synchronize with the rail operator's system via EDI or API integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R005	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Container-to-Wagon Loading Instruction Generation	The system must generate detailed loading instructions for each wagon in a planned train, specifying which containers load onto which wagon positions. Loading instructions must include container number, size/type (ISO code), gross weight, stacking position (for double-stack routes in India), and any special handling codes (hazmat placard numbers, reefer temperature settings, OOG dimensions). The instruction document must include a wagon diagram showing container placement with orientation (door-end direction). For Indian rail operations, instructions must comply with CONCOR's loading pattern guidelines including maximum stack weight for double-stack dwarf containers on dedicated freight corridors (DFC). The system must validate that no individual wagon exceeds its rated payload capacity and that the overall train weight remains within the locomotive's haulage capability. Instructions must be printable in Arabic and English and exportable as PDF for field use.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R006	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Train Departure and Arrival Event Processing	The system must record train departure and arrival events at each terminal along the route with timestamps in local timezone (GST+4, AST+3, IST+5:30). Departure events must capture actual departure time, locomotive number, crew details, total wagons, total TEUs, total gross weight, and any last-minute consist changes (containers added, removed, or swapped). Arrival events must record actual arrival time, condition report (any incidents en route), and initiate the unloading workflow. The system must compare actual versus planned departure and arrival times, calculating delay duration and requiring delay reason codes from a configurable master list. AI-driven analysis must identify recurring delay patterns by route, operator, time-of-day, and season, feeding into future transit time predictions. Event data must integrate with the ICD operations module to trigger container availability updates at the destination facility. All events must be logged with Gregorian and Hijri timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R007	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Freight Tariff and Cost Management	The system must maintain rail freight tariff schedules per operator, route, container type, and weight slab. Tariff structures must support per-TEU rates, per-tonne rates, minimum charge per wagon, and surcharges for hazmat, reefer power supply, over-dimensional cargo, and peak-season supplements. Tariffs must be stored in the rail operator's billing currency (AED for Etihad Rail, SAR for SAR, INR for Indian Railways/CONCOR) with automatic conversion to the booking entity's base currency using the applicable exchange rate. The system must compute estimated freight cost at booking time and actual freight cost upon train completion, highlighting any variance. Volume discount tiers based on monthly TEU commitments must be applied automatically. Fuel surcharge adjustments must be updateable periodically without modifying base tariff structures. All tariff displays and invoices must support Arabic and English rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R008	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Container Tracking and ETA Prediction	The system must provide real-time tracking of containers in rail transit by integrating with rail operator tracking systems (GPS on locomotives, wayside readers, terminal gate systems). Container status must update through milestones: loaded-on-wagon, train-departed-origin, in-transit, arrived-at-intermediate, train-arrived-destination, unloaded-from-wagon. Each milestone must record timestamp, location coordinates, and reporting source. The AI engine must calculate dynamic ETA for each container based on current train position, speed, scheduled stops, historical route performance data, and known disruptions (track maintenance, weather). ETA predictions must achieve at least 85% accuracy within a 2-hour window for routes under 500km. Tracking data must be shareable with customers via a web portal and API, with notifications configurable per customer in Arabic or English. The tracking map must show the rail corridor with real-time train positions across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R009	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Wagon Interchange and Cross-Border Operations	The system must manage wagon interchange events at border crossings or inter-operator transfer points, particularly for the GCC rail network connecting UAE, KSA, and Qatar. Interchange records must capture transferring operator, receiving operator, wagon numbers, container manifest, interchange timestamp, wagon condition inspection results, and any seal verification notes. Customs transit documentation for cross-border rail movement must be generated automatically, including T1-equivalent transit declarations for GCC Customs Union movements. The system must track wagon hire charges for foreign wagons on the entity's network, calculating daily hire rates from the interchange-in to interchange-out timestamps. Cross-border movements must handle currency conversion between AED, SAR, and QAR for interchange billing. All interchange documentation must be bilingual in Arabic and English and comply with GCC railway regulatory framework requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R010	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Safety and Dangerous Goods Compliance	The system must enforce dangerous goods transport regulations specific to rail operations, including RID (Regulations concerning the International Carriage of Dangerous Goods by Rail) equivalents applicable in each jurisdiction. When planning a train carrying hazmat containers, the system must validate wagon-level segregation (minimum wagon separation between incompatible IMDG classes), placarding requirements per wagon, and emergency information card availability. The system must prevent loading of prohibited IMDG classes on specific routes where regulatory restrictions apply (e.g., tunnel restrictions, populated area transit limitations). Train documentation must include a dangerous goods summary showing total quantity per IMDG class, emergency contact numbers, and response procedures. The system must maintain a dangerous goods adviser (DGA) approval workflow where trains exceeding configurable hazmat thresholds require DGA sign-off before departure authorization. All safety documentation must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R011	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Reefer Power Supply Management	The system must manage reefer container power supply throughout rail transit, tracking plug-in points on generator wagons or trainline power systems. When planning a train with reefer containers, the system must verify sufficient power supply capacity (number of reefer outlets, total generator kVA) and assign reefer containers to powered wagon positions. Power supply start and stop times must be recorded for accurate billing of reefer power consumption during rail transit. Temperature monitoring must continue during rail transit through IoT sensor integration, with alerts routed to the train crew and origin/destination terminal operators if temperature excursions occur. The system must calculate reefer power costs per container-hour in the entity's local currency and include them in the rail freight invoice. Pre-trip inspection (PTI) status of reefer units must be verified before rail loading, blocking containers with failed PTI from loading onto the train.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R012	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Operations Performance Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive rail operations analytics covering service reliability, asset utilization, and financial performance. Service reliability metrics must include on-time departure percentage, on-time arrival percentage, average delay minutes by route and operator, and service cancellation rate. Asset utilization must track wagon turnaround time (loading to next loading), average loaded versus empty wagon-kilometers, and locomotive utilization percentage. Financial analytics must include revenue per TEU-kilometer, cost per TEU-kilometer, and margin analysis by route, operator, and container type. All metrics must be segmentable by entity (UAE, Qatar, KSA, India), time period (with Hijri calendar support), and rail operator. AI-driven insights must highlight underperforming routes, recommend tariff adjustments, and predict capacity shortfalls for the upcoming quarter. Dashboards must render in both Arabic and English with RTL-compatible chart layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R013	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Manifest and Documentation Generation	The system must generate all required rail transport documentation including train manifest (complete list of wagons and containers), rail consignment notes per shipment, dangerous goods declaration summary, customs transit documents, and commercial invoices. The train manifest must list wagons in sequence order with their container loads, total weight per wagon, and aggregate train statistics. Rail consignment notes must comply with the applicable rail operator's format requirements and include shipper, consignee, commodity description (harmonized system code), weight, and freight payment terms. Documents must be generated in both Arabic and English and support electronic transmission to the rail operator's system via API or EDI (EDIFACT IFTMIN message). Physical copies must be printable with QR codes linking to the digital version. Document templates must be configurable per rail operator and entity to accommodate varying regulatory formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R014	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Empty Wagon Repositioning Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven empty wagon repositioning planning to balance wagon supply across the network. The AI engine must analyze forecasted demand at each terminal (based on booking pipeline, historical patterns, and seasonal trends), current wagon positions, and in-transit wagon arrivals to recommend optimal empty wagon movements. Repositioning plans must minimize total empty-running kilometers while ensuring each terminal has sufficient wagon supply to meet demand within a configurable planning horizon (default 7 days). The system must differentiate wagon types (flat, well, spine) in repositioning as demand varies by container type and route. Repositioning costs must be estimated using deadhead tariff rates and allocated to the benefiting entity's cost center. The planner must flag situations where leasing additional wagons from external pools would be more cost-effective than long-distance repositioning, comparing daily lease rates against repositioning transport costs in the relevant currencies (AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R015	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Service Schedule Publication and Customer Booking Portal	The system must publish rail service schedules to customers via a self-service web portal and mobile app, enabling direct slot booking. The schedule display must show departure terminal, arrival terminal, days of operation, cut-off time for container delivery to the rail terminal, estimated transit time, and available capacity (remaining TEU slots). Customers must be able to book rail slots online by selecting a service, specifying container details (number, size, type, weight, hazmat classification), and receiving instant booking confirmation. The portal must support Arabic and English language switching with full RTL layout support for Arabic. Pricing must be displayed in the customer's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with transparent FX conversion references. The portal must provide booking history, shipment tracking, and invoice download. API access must be available for freight forwarders to integrate rail booking into their own TMS systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R016	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Rail Incident and Disruption Management	The system must provide a rail incident management module for recording and responding to operational disruptions including derailments, equipment failures, track closures, weather events, and security incidents. Each incident record must capture incident type, severity level (minor, major, critical), location (GPS coordinates and nearest station), affected train services, impacted container count, estimated resolution time, and responsible parties. The system must trigger automated notifications to affected customers listing their impacted containers and revised ETAs. For major disruptions, the system must support contingency planning by identifying alternative transport options (re-routing via alternative rail corridor, switching to road transport) and estimating cost and time impact of each option. Incident resolution must be tracked through status updates until service restoration. Post-incident analysis must feed into the AI risk model to improve future disruption predictions. All incident communications must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-002-R017	FEAT-026-1-002	MOD-026	Offline Rail Terminal Operations Support	The system must support offline operations at rail terminals and sidings where network connectivity is unreliable, particularly at remote inland terminals in India and KSA. Offline functionality must cover wagon inspection recording, container loading and unloading confirmation, train departure and arrival logging, and container condition surveys. Data entered offline must be stored locally with timestamps and operator identification, then synchronized automatically when connectivity is restored. The synchronization engine must handle conflict resolution where the same train service may have been updated from multiple terminals during an offline period, applying business rules to determine the authoritative record (e.g., departure terminal data takes precedence for departure events). Offline mode must support Arabic and English UI without requiring server-side resource loading. Local validation rules must prevent data entry errors such as duplicate container loading or exceeding wagon weight limits even without server connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R001	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Carrier and Fleet Master Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of approved transport carriers and their vehicle fleets across all operating entities. Each carrier record must capture company name (Arabic and English), trade license number, registration jurisdiction (UAE, Qatar, KSA, India), insurance policy details with expiry dates, safety rating, and approved operating routes. Vehicle records must include plate number, chassis number, vehicle type (prime mover, trailer, skeletal trailer, flatbed, side-loader), maximum payload in tonnes, container size compatibility (20ft, 40ft, 45ft), and current registration status. The system must enforce compliance by blocking bookings to carriers with expired trade licenses, lapsed insurance, or vehicles with overdue inspection dates. Fleet capacity must be queryable in real-time showing available vehicles by type, location, and entity for AI-driven carrier selection algorithms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R002	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Booking Request and Workflow	The system must support transport booking requests initiated from multiple sources: manual entry by operations staff, automatic generation from ICD gate-out workflows, customer self-service portal, or API from external freight management systems. Each booking request must specify pickup location (port gate, ICD, customer warehouse with full address), delivery location, container details (number, size, type, gross weight), pickup date and time window, delivery deadline, and any special requirements (hazmat escort, reefer genset, police permit for OOG). The booking must flow through a configurable approval workflow based on value thresholds and entity-specific authorization matrices. Approved bookings must be published to the carrier assignment engine. Booking requests must support Arabic and English data entry with addresses in both scripts. The system must validate that the requested pickup time aligns with port appointment windows where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R003	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	AI-Powered Carrier Assignment and Rate Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven carrier assignment that selects the optimal transport carrier for each booking based on multiple weighted criteria: contracted rate for the route, carrier performance score (on-time delivery percentage, damage rate, customer complaints), current vehicle availability at the pickup location, carrier capacity utilization to honor minimum volume commitments, and any customer-specific carrier preferences or exclusions. The AI must support multi-stop trip optimization where multiple containers for nearby pickup or delivery points are consolidated into a single trip. Rate optimization must compare spot market rates against contracted rates, recommending the most cost-effective option. The system must handle multi-currency rate comparison where carriers in different entities quote in AED, QAR, SAR, or INR, converting to a common base currency for comparison. Assignment results must be presentable in Arabic and English for dispatcher review and approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R004	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Port Appointment Integration for Truck Operations	The system must integrate with port terminal appointment systems to coordinate truck arrivals for container pickup and delivery. For Jebel Ali (AEJEA), the system must interface with DP World's truck appointment system via API. For Khalifa Port (AEKHL), integration must connect with Abu Dhabi Ports' MAQTA platform. For Hamad Port (QAHAM), the system must interface with QTerminals' vessel and gate appointment system. For JNPT (INNSA), integration must connect with the port community system for truck slot booking. The system must synchronize booked port appointment windows with the transport booking to ensure trucks arrive within the allocated slot. Late arrival risk must be calculated based on real-time traffic conditions and distance, with automatic alerts when a truck may miss its port appointment. Port appointment reference numbers must be linked to the transport booking and displayed on the driver's trip sheet. The system must handle appointment rescheduling when transport delays are detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R005	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Driver Trip Sheet and Mobile App Assignment	The system must generate electronic trip sheets for assigned drivers accessible via a mobile application. Each trip sheet must contain pickup and delivery addresses with GPS coordinates, container details, customer contact information, special handling instructions, port gate appointment time, required documents checklist (delivery order, customs release, LPC/EIR), and route navigation link. The mobile app must support Arabic and English with automatic language selection based on driver preference. Drivers must acknowledge trip acceptance through the app, triggering status updates visible to dispatchers and customers. The app must function in offline mode for areas with poor connectivity, caching trip details locally and syncing status updates when connectivity returns. Trip sheets must be printable from the app in the event of device failure. The app must capture driver location at configurable intervals for real-time tracking without excessive battery drain.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R006	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Real-Time GPS Tracking and Geofence Monitoring	The system must provide real-time GPS tracking of all trucks executing transport bookings, integrating with vehicle-mounted GPS devices, carrier fleet management systems, or the driver mobile app. Tracking data must update at minimum every 5-minute intervals during active trips. The system must support configurable geofences around key locations: port gates, ICD facilities, customer warehouses, customs checkpoints, and restricted zones. Geofence events (entry, exit, dwell time) must be logged and trigger automated status updates on the transport booking. Unauthorized route deviations beyond a configurable corridor width must generate alerts to the dispatch team. Speed violations exceeding posted limits must be flagged for safety reporting. The tracking interface must display truck positions on a map supporting both Arabic and English place names with the ability to filter by entity, carrier, and trip status. Historical track playback must be available for dispute resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R007	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Proof of Delivery and Electronic POD Capture	The system must capture electronic proof of delivery (ePOD) at each delivery point through the driver mobile application. ePOD must include delivery timestamp with GPS coordinates confirming location at the delivery address, recipient name and signature captured on the device touchscreen, photographic evidence of container condition at delivery (minimum four photos: front, back, left, right), container seal number verification (intact, broken, replaced), and any exception notes (damage, shortage, refusal). The ePOD must be linked to the transport booking and accessible to the customer, operations team, and billing department. For deliveries to port terminals, the system must capture the terminal's equipment interchange receipt (EIR) reference number as the delivery confirmation. ePOD data must sync immediately when online or queue for upload when offline. Completed ePODs must be immutable and timestamped for audit trail purposes. ePOD forms must render in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R008	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Cost Calculation and Multi-Currency Billing	The system must calculate transport costs based on configurable tariff structures supporting per-trip flat rates, per-kilometer rates, per-TEU rates, and combination models. Tariff lookups must consider route (origin-destination pair), container size and type, cargo classification (standard, hazmat, reefer, OOG), and day type (weekday, weekend, public holiday including Hijri-based holidays). Additional charges must be itemizable including waiting time beyond free hours at pickup and delivery, detention charges, police escort for OOG, genset rental for reefer, toll charges, and border crossing fees. All charges must be computed in the carrier's contracted currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to the booking entity's base currency for financial posting. The system must handle split billing where transport costs are shared between shipper and consignee according to INCOTERMS rules (e.g., DAP, CIF, FOB). VAT/GST must be applied according to each jurisdiction's rules and reverse charge mechanism for cross-entity transport.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R009	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Order Integration with Customs and Documentation	The system must link transport bookings with customs clearance status to ensure containers are only dispatched when customs release is confirmed. For import containers, the system must verify that the customs out-of-charge (OOC) order or customs release instruction has been received before authorizing truck dispatch from the port or ICD. For export containers, the system must ensure that the shipping bill or customs export declaration has been filed and the Let Export Order (LEO) obtained where applicable (India). The system must generate or receive electronic delivery orders from shipping lines and link them to transport bookings. For bonded movements between customs-bonded locations, the system must generate customs transit documents (bond-to-bond transfer notes) and track bond liability. All documentation must be available in Arabic and English, with customs reference numbers cross-linked to the transport booking for end-to-end traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R010	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Multi-Stop Trip Planning and Route Optimization	The system must support multi-stop trip planning where a single truck executes multiple pickup or delivery tasks in a single journey. The AI route optimizer must sequence stops to minimize total distance traveled, total time, and fuel consumption while respecting delivery time windows at each stop, driver working hour limits (as per UAE, Qatar, KSA, or India road transport regulations), and container compatibility constraints (e.g., cannot carry hazmat and food-grade containers on the same chassis simultaneously). The optimizer must integrate real-time traffic data and road restriction information (weight limits, height restrictions, time-based access controls in urban areas) to generate practical routes. Each multi-stop trip must have a consolidated trip sheet showing the stop sequence, estimated arrival at each stop, and cumulative distance. The system must recalculate the route dynamically if a stop is cancelled or a new urgent stop is inserted mid-trip, pushing updated instructions to the driver's mobile app in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R011	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Driver and Vehicle Compliance Management	The system must enforce regulatory compliance for drivers and vehicles before allowing trip assignment. Driver compliance checks must verify valid driving license for the vehicle category, medical fitness certificate, hazmat handling certification (ADR equivalent) for dangerous goods transport, and any country-specific permits (UAE RTA permit, KSA SFDA permit for food transport, India CMVR compliance). Vehicle compliance must verify valid registration (Istimara/Mulkiya), insurance, roadworthiness certificate, and calibrated weighbridge certificate. The system must maintain a compliance calendar showing upcoming expiry dates across all drivers and vehicles with automated alerts at 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry. Non-compliant drivers or vehicles must be automatically blocked from new trip assignments with a clear reason displayed in Arabic or English. Compliance document uploads must support Arabic-language scanned documents with OCR-assisted data extraction for expiry date recognition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R012	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Exception and Incident Handling	The system must provide a structured exception handling workflow for transport incidents including truck breakdowns, accidents, cargo damage, container seal breaches, customs holds during transit, and delivery refusals. Each exception must be recorded with incident type, severity, location (GPS coordinates), timestamp, photographic evidence, and initial assessment by the driver via the mobile app. The system must trigger automated escalation workflows based on incident severity: minor exceptions notify the dispatcher, major exceptions alert the operations manager and customer, and critical exceptions (accidents, hazmat spills) trigger emergency response protocols with relevant authority notifications. The system must support replacement vehicle dispatch when breakdowns occur, automatically generating a new trip assignment and notifying the customer of the revised ETA. Exception costs (towing, repair, penalties) must be tracked against the original transport booking for margin analysis. All exception records and communications must support Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R005	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Rate Validity and Escalation Rules	The system must enforce rate validity periods with automatic expiry notifications sent to the commercial team 30 days before a rate card expires. Expired rate cards must be blocked from application to new bookings while remaining applicable to shipments booked during the validity period. Annual escalation rules must be configurable per rate card with options including fixed percentage increase, CPI-linked adjustment sourced from national statistics authorities (Qatar QSA, UAE FCSC, KSA GASTAT, India MOSPI), fuel-price-linked adjustment indexed to local diesel prices, or custom formula-based escalation. Escalation must generate a draft new-version rate card for review and approval before activation. The system must support retroactive rate adjustments for billing corrections with automatic credit/debit note generation in the appropriate entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R013	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport KPI Dashboard and Carrier Performance Scoring	The system must calculate and display carrier performance metrics on a real-time dashboard covering on-time pickup percentage, on-time delivery percentage, average transit time versus standard by route, damage incident rate, customer complaint frequency, and invoice accuracy. Performance scores must be computed per carrier, per route, and per entity (UAE, Qatar, KSA, India) with trend analysis over configurable time periods. AI-driven scoring must weight metrics according to business priorities configurable by each entity, generating a composite carrier rating (A/B/C/D) that influences future carrier assignment algorithms. The dashboard must flag carriers whose performance drops below configurable thresholds, triggering corrective action workflows including warning notices, rate renegotiation, or suspension from the approved carrier panel. Financial analytics must show transport cost per TEU by route, carrier, and entity with multi-currency normalization. All dashboard elements must render correctly in Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R014	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Customer Self-Service Transport Booking Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing web portal and mobile app for self-service transport booking, tracking, and documentation. Customers must be able to create transport requests specifying pickup and delivery details, view real-time tracking of their booked transports on a map, download ePODs and delivery confirmations, and access transport invoices and payment history. The portal must display transport rates applicable to the customer's contract, with instant quotation for standard routes and request-for-quote workflow for non-standard movements. Booking must be available in Arabic and English with full RTL support, and the portal must adapt to the customer's entity context (showing relevant ports, ICDs, and currency for their jurisdiction). The portal must support customer user management with role-based access (admin, booking, view-only). API integration must be available for customers to embed transport booking into their own supply chain systems, with API documentation in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R015	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Fuel Cost Management and Carbon Emission Tracking	The system must track fuel costs associated with transport operations either through actual fuel receipts uploaded by carriers or calculated estimates based on trip distance and vehicle fuel efficiency ratings. Fuel cost allocation must link to individual transport bookings for accurate job costing, with separate tracking for owned fleet versus carrier-managed fuel. The system must calculate carbon emissions per transport trip based on vehicle type, fuel consumed, and distance traveled using emission factors from recognized standards (GHG Protocol, GLEC Framework). Emission reports must aggregate by route, carrier, entity, and customer to support sustainability reporting. The system must support carbon offset tracking where emission credits are purchased and allocated against specific transport movements. Fuel prices must be maintainable per entity in local currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with periodic updates. Carbon emission dashboards and reports must render in Arabic and English with metric units (kg CO2e per TEU-km).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R016	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Overweight and Over-Dimensional Cargo Transport Planning	The system must provide specialized planning for over-weight (OW) and over-dimensional (OOG) container transport that requires special permits, route surveys, and escort arrangements. For OOG cargo, the system must capture exact dimensions (length, width, height, overhang measurements) and check them against route restrictions including bridge heights, tunnel clearances, road width limitations, and overhead cable heights for each entity's road network. Police escort and pilot vehicle requirements must be determined based on cargo dimensions and local regulations (UAE RTA, Qatar MOI, KSA MOT, India NHAI rules). Permit application generation must be automated with pre-filled forms for each jurisdiction's transport authority. The system must calculate special transport surcharges including permit fees, escort costs, and route survey charges in the applicable currency. Route survey reports with photographic evidence must be uploadable and linked to the transport booking. All planning documents and permits must support Arabic and English output.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R017	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Transport Billing Reconciliation and Carrier Payment	The system must support end-to-end transport billing reconciliation from carrier invoice receipt through verification, approval, and payment processing. Carrier invoices must be matched against completed transport bookings, validating trip details (route, container, dates), agreed rates from the contract tariff, and additional charges against supporting evidence (waiting time logs, toll receipts, permit invoices). Discrepancies must be flagged for dispute resolution with a structured workflow for negotiation and settlement. Approved invoices must be posted to the accounts payable module with proper entity allocation and multi-currency handling (carrier invoice currency versus entity base currency). The system must track payment terms per carrier (net 30, net 45, net 60) and generate payment batches respecting each entity's payment cycle. Self-billing (where the ERP generates the carrier invoice based on completed trips) must be supported as an alternative to carrier-submitted invoices. Reconciliation reports must show billed versus actual cost variance by carrier, route, and period in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-003-R018	FEAT-026-1-003	MOD-026	Offline Transport Operations and Connectivity Resilience	The system must ensure transport operations continue seamlessly during connectivity outages affecting the central ERP, dispatch terminals, or driver mobile devices. Dispatch workstations must cache active transport bookings and carrier assignments locally, allowing dispatchers to view pending trips, print trip sheets, and record manual status updates offline. The driver mobile app must store trip details, capture ePOD data (photos, signatures, timestamps), and log GPS breadcrumbs locally when offline, automatically uploading all captured data upon connectivity restoration. Offline data synchronization must use conflict resolution rules ensuring no duplicate trip assignments or contradictory status updates. The system must handle scenarios where a transport booking is modified on the server while the driver is offline, presenting the dispatcher with a conflict resolution interface upon sync. Offline functionality must support Arabic and English UI rendering from locally cached language resources. Battery optimization must be maintained during offline GPS logging to prevent device shutdown during long hauls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R002	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Multi-Port Call Rotation Planning	The system must support planning of multi-port call rotations where a single voyage covers multiple ports in sequence such as JNPT to Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port to Hamad Port. Each port call within the rotation must maintain its own independent ETA, ETB (Estimated Time of Berthing), ETD, and EOSP (End of Sea Passage) timestamps. The rotation sequence must be editable with drag-and-drop reordering in the UI. When an ETA at one port changes, the AI engine must automatically recalculate downstream ETAs based on configured sea passage times between port pairs, factoring in canal transit delays (Suez Canal) and weather routing adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R001	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bonded Warehouse License and Configuration Management	The system must maintain a registry of all customs bonded warehouse licenses held by each entity, recording the licensing authority (Dubai Customs, Abu Dhabi Customs, QGAC, ZATCA, CBIC), license number, license validity dates (Gregorian and Hijri), approved commodity categories (general cargo, hazmat, temperature-controlled, high-value), maximum storage capacity in metric tonnes and cubic meters, and security and surveillance requirements. Each bonded warehouse must be linked to a physical facility in the ICD master with designated bonded zones mapped to specific yard blocks or warehouse sections. The system must generate alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before license expiry, initiating the renewal workflow. Configuration must support multiple bonded areas within a single ICD facility, each potentially under different customs authority jurisdiction. All license details must be displayable in Arabic and English, and the system must store copies of the license document in both languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R002	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	In-Bond Cargo Receipt and Bond Register Entry	The system must record every cargo receipt into a bonded warehouse as an entry in the customs bond register, capturing the customs entry reference number (bill of entry for India, customs declaration for GCC), container or package details, commodity description with HS (Harmonized System) tariff code at 8-digit level, quantity (units, weight in kg, volume in CBM), CIF value in the customs declaration currency, and the applicable duty rate. Each bond register entry must be assigned a unique bond reference number following the customs authority's prescribed format. The system must calculate the contingent customs duty liability for each entry (CIF value multiplied by applicable duty rate plus any anti-dumping or safeguard duties) and maintain a running total of total bond liability per warehouse. Bond entries must be time-stamped in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. The system must support both FCL (full container load) and LCL (less than container load) bond entries with appropriate quantity tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R003	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	In-Bond Inventory Management and Stock Control	The system must provide real-time inventory management for all goods held under customs bond, tracking each item's physical location within the bonded warehouse (zone, aisle, rack, bin level). Inventory must be searchable by bond reference, customs declaration number, HS code, consignee, container number, and date range. Stock movements within the bonded area (re-slotting, consolidation, quality inspection sampling) must be recorded with before-and-after locations and timestamps. The system must enforce strict FIFO (first-in-first-out) or FEFO (first-expiry-first-out) rules for perishable and time-sensitive bonded goods. Physical stock count procedures must be supported with variance reporting against the bond register, requiring customs authority notification for discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., 0.5% by value). Inventory dashboards must show stock aging analysis, capacity utilization, and commodity breakdown, rendered in both Arabic and English with multi-currency value display (original declaration currency and entity base currency).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R004	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Ex-Bond Processing and Duty Payment Integration	The system must manage the ex-bond process when goods are released from the bonded warehouse for domestic consumption, triggering customs duty payment. The ex-bond request must specify the bond register entries being released (full or partial quantities), the customs out-of-charge order or release instruction reference, and the intended consignee. The system must calculate the exact customs duty payable based on the declared value, applicable tariff rate at the time of ex-bond (which may differ from the rate at bond entry if tariff changes occurred), and any preferential duty rates under applicable free trade agreements (GCC FTA, India-GCC potential agreements, bilateral agreements). Duty payment must integrate with the customs authority's electronic payment systems (Dubai Customs e-payment, ZATCA FASAH, ICEGATE for India). Upon payment confirmation, the bond register must be debited and the bond liability reduced accordingly. Multi-currency handling must convert duty amounts from the declaration currency to the payment currency at the customs-prescribed exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R005	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Re-Export from Bonded Warehouse Processing	The system must support re-export workflows where goods stored under customs bond are exported without payment of import duties. The re-export process must capture the export destination country, shipping details (vessel, voyage, container), export customs declaration reference, and the bond register entries being re-exported. The system must generate re-export documentation including packing lists, commercial invoices, and customs re-export declarations. For GCC Customs Union member states (UAE, Qatar, KSA), the system must handle intra-GCC transfers where goods move between bonded facilities in different member states under customs transit arrangements. The bond register must be debited upon confirmed re-export, reducing the contingent duty liability. The system must enforce that re-exported goods are physically verified by customs (if required by the authority) before bond discharge. Re-export documentation must be generated in Arabic and English and comply with each customs authority's prescribed format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R006	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bond-to-Bond Transfer Management	The system must manage bond-to-bond transfers where goods move from one bonded warehouse to another without duty payment. Transfer requests must specify the origin bonded warehouse, destination bonded warehouse (potentially in a different entity or customs jurisdiction), transferring bond register entries, transport arrangement details, and customs transit document references. The system must generate customs bond-to-bond transfer applications for the origin customs authority and corresponding bond entry advice for the destination customs authority. During transit, the bond liability must remain with the origin warehouse until the destination warehouse confirms receipt and registers the goods in its bond register. For cross-border bond transfers (e.g., UAE to KSA), the system must handle dual customs declarations (export from origin, transit, and import bond at destination). Transfer costs must be allocated and billed in the appropriate entity currencies (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with inter-company settlement entries. Transit time monitoring must trigger alerts if goods are not received at the destination within the permitted transit period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R002	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	AI-Powered Surveyor Auto-Assignment	The system must implement an AI engine that automatically assigns the most suitable surveyor to each pre-load survey request based on surveyor availability, geographic proximity to the terminal or warehouse, surveyor certification level, commodity specialization (hazardous goods IMDG certified, reefer cargo qualified, break-bulk experienced), current workload balance, and historical performance ratings. The AI must consider traffic patterns and estimated travel time to the survey location within the port zone. For Jebel Ali Free Zone and JNPT, the system must account for port gate entry permit requirements and pre-approve surveyors who hold valid gate passes. Assignment must complete within 30 seconds of request submission for 95% of cases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R007	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Duty Deferment and Periodic Payment Scheme Management	The system must support duty deferment schemes where approved importers defer customs duty payment to a periodic settlement date rather than paying at the time of ex-bond. The system must track each entity's deferment account with the customs authority, recording the approved credit limit, utilized amount, available balance, and payment due dates. Each ex-bond transaction under deferment must debit the deferment account and be included in the periodic duty payment statement. The system must generate periodic payment summaries (weekly, fortnightly, or monthly as configured per customs authority) reconciling all ex-bond transactions within the period. Payment reminders must be triggered before the due date to avoid late payment penalties. The system must handle deferment guarantees (bank guarantees or insurance bonds) tracking their validity, amount coverage, and renewal requirements. Deferment account balances and statements must be displayable in the customs authority's prescribed currency with Arabic and English formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R008	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bonded Warehouse Value-Added Services Tracking	The system must track value-added services (VAS) performed on goods within the bonded warehouse under customs supervision, including relabeling, repackaging, quality testing, sampling, and minor processing that does not change the HS tariff classification. Each VAS activity must be recorded against the relevant bond register entry with activity type, date, quantity processed, before-and-after descriptions, and customs officer supervision reference (where required by the authority). The system must enforce that VAS activities do not result in a change of customs classification that would require re-assessment of duty liability without proper customs approval. Material consumption during VAS (labels, packaging materials) must be tracked for cost allocation. VAS charges must be billed to the consignee or cargo owner in the entity's base currency. The system must generate VAS activity reports for customs authority review, with all records maintained in Arabic and English to satisfy audit requirements in all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R009	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Customs Examination and Inspection Workflow	The system must support the customs examination process for bonded goods selected for physical inspection by the customs authority. When a customs examination order is received, the system must create an examination job linked to the bond register entry, assign warehouse staff for container or package retrieval and presentation, and schedule the examination at a designated inspection bay. The examination record must capture the customs officer's identity, examination findings (conforming, discrepancy found, samples taken), photographic evidence, and the officer's release or hold decision. For containerized cargo, the system must record seal breaking and re-sealing events with new seal numbers. Examination-related charges (handling, re-packing, overtime for after-hours inspections) must be computed and billed to the cargo owner. The system must track examination rates (percentage of consignments examined) by customs authority and commodity type for compliance analytics. All examination records must be in Arabic and English with timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R010	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bond Liability Reporting and Risk Management	The system must provide comprehensive bond liability reporting showing the total contingent duty liability across all bonded warehouses per entity. Reports must break down liability by customs authority, bonded warehouse, commodity category (HS chapter), and consignee. Aging analysis must show bond entries by duration in warehouse (0-30 days, 31-90 days, 91-180 days, 181-365 days, over 365 days) with highlighting for entries approaching the maximum bonding period permitted by the customs authority (typically 1-3 years depending on jurisdiction). The system must calculate the bond guarantee utilization ratio (total liability versus total guarantee amount) and alert when utilization exceeds 80%, recommending guarantee top-up. AI-driven risk analysis must identify patterns of long-dwelling bonded goods that may indicate abandoned cargo requiring customs authority notification. All liability reports must display amounts in both the declaration currency and the entity base currency, with Arabic and English formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R011	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Customs Authority Electronic Filing Integration	The system must integrate electronically with each entity's customs authority systems for bonded warehouse operations. For UAE, integration must connect with Dubai Trade and Abu Dhabi MAQTA platforms for bond register submissions, ex-bond requests, and customs examination results. For Qatar, integration must interface with the Qatar Single Window (Al Nadeeb) system. For KSA, integration must connect with ZATCA's FASAH platform for customs declarations, bond management, and e-invoicing compliance. For India, integration must interface with ICEGATE for bond register maintenance, ex-bond bills of entry, and re-export shipping bills. Electronic filing must handle message formats prescribed by each authority (XML, JSON, EDIFACT) with proper digital signature and authentication. Filing responses (acknowledgments, queries, approvals, rejections) must be automatically processed and linked to the corresponding bond register entries. All transmitted data must maintain Arabic content where required by the authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R012	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bonded Warehouse Security and Access Control	The system must manage security access controls for bonded warehouse areas in compliance with customs authority requirements for bonded facility security. Access control must enforce that only authorized personnel (customs officers, bonded warehouse operators with valid customs clearance) enter bonded zones. The system must integrate with physical access control systems (badge readers, biometric scanners) to log every entry and exit from bonded areas with timestamp, person identity, and purpose of visit. Visitor management for customs officers and external auditors must record visit purpose, areas accessed, and duration. CCTV monitoring integration must provide camera feeds for bonded zones accessible through the system dashboard, with recording retention periods complying with each customs authority's minimum requirements (typically 90-180 days). Security breach events (unauthorized access attempts, alarm triggers) must be logged and escalated immediately. Access control reports must be generatable for customs authority inspections in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R002	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Self-Assessment Questionnaire Automation	The system must provide a structured self-assessment questionnaire aligned with each jurisdiction's AEO requirements: QCGA's customs compliance criteria, FCA's SAFE Framework implementation, ZATCA's AEO program guidelines, and CBIC's AEO circular 33/2016. Each questionnaire shall contain jurisdiction-specific sections covering customs compliance history, financial solvency, security and safety standards, and record-keeping practices. The AI engine must pre-populate answers from existing ERP data such as shipment records at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA), flagging gaps where documentation is missing or non-compliant. All questionnaires must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R013	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Bonded Warehouse Storage Tariff and Billing	The system must compute storage charges for goods in bonded warehouses based on configurable tariff structures that may vary by warehouse, commodity type, storage method (floor stacking, racking, open yard), and container type (dry, reefer, hazmat). Tariff slabs must support escalating rates by storage duration (e.g., days 1-15 at base rate, days 16-30 at 1.5x, days 31+ at 2x) to incentivize cargo clearance and maximize warehouse throughput. Free storage periods must be configurable per customer contract and commodity type. The system must generate periodic storage invoices (weekly or monthly) with detailed line items per bond register entry showing storage days, rate applied, and amount. Billing must be in the entity's base currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with automatic application of the correct VAT/GST rate for bonded warehouse services. Credit notes for overcharges or early release must follow proper approval workflows. Invoice generation must support Arabic and English with e-invoicing compliance for ZATCA (KSA) and GST (India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R014	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Customs Audit Trail and Compliance Documentation	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all bonded warehouse transactions satisfying customs authority audit requirements across all four operating jurisdictions. Every bond register entry, modification, ex-bond, re-export, and transfer must be logged with the exact timestamp (Gregorian and Hijri), user identity, workstation identifier, and the before-and-after state of all modified fields. Audit records must be immutable and protected from deletion or modification, stored for a minimum retention period compliant with each jurisdiction's requirements (UAE: 5 years, Qatar: 5 years, KSA: 7 years per ZATCA, India: 8 years per CBIC). The system must support customs authority audit access through a read-only portal allowing authorized customs officers to query bond register data, transaction history, and supporting documents. Audit reports must be exportable in formats acceptable to each customs authority. All audit records and reports must be available in Arabic and English with proper Unicode support for Arabic names and descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R015	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Abandoned Cargo and Customs Auction Processing	The system must manage the abandoned cargo process for bonded goods that remain unclaimed beyond the customs-prescribed maximum bonding period. The system must generate notifications to the consignee and importer of record at configurable intervals (e.g., 30, 15, and 7 days before the maximum period expires) warning of impending abandonment status. Upon expiry, the system must generate the customs authority notification of abandoned cargo, including a detailed inventory list, estimated duty liability, and storage charges accrued. If the customs authority directs auction, the system must support auction lot creation from abandoned bond entries, catalogue generation with photographs and commodity descriptions in Arabic and English, and auction result recording (winning bid, buyer details, auction fees). Auction proceeds must be applied first against customs duty liability, then storage and handling charges, with any surplus returned to the cargo owner. The bond register must be closed upon completion of the abandonment or auction process with full audit trail documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R016	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Temperature-Controlled Bonded Storage Management	The system must manage temperature-controlled bonded storage for goods requiring refrigerated, frozen, or climate-controlled conditions while under customs bond. Temperature zones within the bonded warehouse must be configurable with target temperature ranges, humidity levels, and air circulation parameters. Continuous temperature monitoring must log readings at intervals not exceeding 15 minutes, integrating with cold room management systems and IoT sensors. Temperature excursion alerts must be triggered when readings deviate beyond the prescribed range, notifying warehouse operations and the customs officer responsible for the bonded facility. The system must maintain temperature records as part of the bond register entry, satisfying food safety regulations (ESMA in UAE, SFDA in KSA, FSSAI in India, and Qatar public health authority). For pharmaceutical products, temperature monitoring must comply with GDP (Good Distribution Practice) requirements. Reefer container monitoring within the bonded area must track plug-in time, power consumption, and temperature for accurate billing. All temperature records and certificates must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-1-004-R017	FEAT-026-1-004	MOD-026	Offline Bonded Warehouse Operations Continuity	The system must support critical bonded warehouse operations during network connectivity outages to prevent customs compliance violations. Offline operations must include bond register entry recording for arriving cargo, physical inventory verification and count recording, ex-bond release processing with locally cached customs release instructions, and storage charge accrual calculations. The offline system must enforce bond register integrity by maintaining a local copy of the bond register synchronized at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes when online). Customs-sensitive operations performed offline must be flagged for mandatory review upon reconnection, generating a reconciliation report for the bonded warehouse supervisor. The system must prevent data conflicts by reserving sequential bond reference number ranges for each offline workstation. Offline operation duration must be logged and reported as a compliance metric, as extended offline periods may require customs authority notification in some jurisdictions. Arabic and English UI must function fully offline using locally cached language and font resources.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-003-R006	FEAT-026-2-003	MOD-026	Customer-Specific Rate Agreements	The system must support customer-specific inland haulage rate agreements that override standard rate cards for nominated customers. Customer rates must be linked to the customer master record and may be defined per origin-destination pair, container type, commodity group, and volume tier. Volume-based tiered pricing must support cumulative volume tracking against tier thresholds calculated monthly, quarterly, or annually as defined in the customer agreement. When a customer's cumulative volume crosses a tier threshold, the system must recalculate charges for the current period at the new tier rate and generate a retrospective credit note for the rate differential. Customer rate agreements must display in the customer's preferred currency with conversion to the entity's base currency for accounting. The system must alert the commercial team when a customer approaches a tier threshold within 10% of the next volume band.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R002	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Scheduled and on-demand rate capture	The system must support configurable rate capture schedules with options for multiple captures per day at defined times aligned with the business hours of each entity's local market. Default schedules must include pre-market-open capture (before 8 AM local time), mid-day capture (12 PM), and end-of-day capture (5 PM) for each operating entity. The system must also support on-demand manual rate capture triggered by authorized treasury personnel. Each captured rate must record the source provider, capture timestamp in UTC and local time, bid and ask rates, mid-market rate, and the system user or automated process that initiated the capture.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R005	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Carbon Emission Optimization Mode	The system must provide a dedicated carbon emission optimization mode that prioritizes routes minimizing CO2 equivalent emissions per TEU-kilometer. Emission calculations must follow the Global Logistics Emissions Council (GLEC) framework version 3.0, applying mode-specific emission factors: road transport using regional truck emission factors differentiated by vehicle Euro/BS emission standard, rail using national grid electricity emission factors for electrified routes and diesel factors for non-electrified routes, and sea using IMO EEXI-based vessel emission factors. The system must quantify the emission savings of rail-road multimodal routes compared to all-road alternatives for each origin-destination pair. Carbon cost monetization must be supported using configurable carbon prices per tonne CO2e (aligned with EU ETS, voluntary carbon market prices, or internal carbon pricing). Reports must show emissions per entity for Scope 3 reporting requirements in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R006	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Container Consolidation Opportunity Detection	The system must use AI to identify container consolidation opportunities across multiple bookings sharing similar origin-destination pairs, compatible commodities, and overlapping delivery windows. The consolidation engine must evaluate LCL-to-FCL consolidation at origin ICDs, multi-shipper container sharing for partial loads, and hub consolidation at transshipment points like Jebel Ali where containers from multiple origins can be grouped onto a single feeder or rail service. Consolidation proposals must calculate the per-shipper cost saving compared to individual shipment routing, accounting for the additional handling charges at the consolidation point. The system must respect cargo compatibility rules (no hazmat with food, no odorous cargo with sensitive goods) and customer consent requirements for shared containers. All monetary calculations must use the entity-specific currency with consolidated billing splitting charges proportionally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R007	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Seasonal and Event-Based Route Adjustment	The system must incorporate a seasonal intelligence layer that automatically adjusts route recommendations based on known seasonal patterns and scheduled events. Seasonal factors must include Indian monsoon season (June-September) affecting west coast port operations and hinterland road conditions, Qatar summer extreme heat (June-August) affecting road transport windows to cooler hours, Hajj season affecting Saudi transport capacity and routing around Makkah/Madinah restricted zones, Ramadan affecting GCC operational hours and labor availability, Diwali/Eid affecting Indian and GCC port throughput, and Chinese New Year affecting inbound cargo volumes. The system must maintain a configurable event calendar with pre-defined routing rule adjustments per event. Route optimization must automatically apply the relevant seasonal adjustments based on the shipment's planned transit dates, showing the user which seasonal factors influenced the recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R008	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Regulatory Compliance Route Filtering	The system must filter route options to exclude combinations that violate regulatory requirements. Compliance checks must include cabotage restrictions (e.g., foreign-registered trucks cannot perform inland haulage in India without permits, GCC cabotage rules for domestic transport), hazardous cargo routing restrictions per municipality regulations (specific roads banned for IMDG class cargo in Dubai, Doha, Riyadh, Mumbai), overweight and over-dimensional cargo routing restrictions per road authority permits, customs bonded transit requirements where cargo must move under customs seal between approved points, and sanitary/phytosanitary inspection requirements at specific border crossings or ICDs. The system must maintain a regularly updated regulatory rules database per jurisdiction. Routes violating any regulatory constraint must be excluded from optimization results with the specific violated rule displayed as the exclusion reason.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R009	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Network Graph Model for Route Planning	The system must maintain a comprehensive transport network graph model representing all available nodes (ports, ICDs, rail terminals, customer premises zones, border crossings) and edges (transport connections between nodes with mode, capacity, cost, and transit time attributes). For the target geography, the network must include Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), JNPT (INJNP), Mundra (INMUN), and all connected ICDs including ICD Tughlakabad Delhi, ICD Dadri, ICD Sanand Ahmedabad, Riyadh Dry Port, and Dammam ICD. Network edges must have time-variant attributes reflecting schedule-based services (vessel sailings, rail departures) and continuously available services (road transport). The graph must support dynamic edge addition and removal for new services or discontinued routes without requiring full system redeployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R010	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Customer Preference Learning and Personalization	The system must learn and apply individual customer routing preferences through analysis of historical booking patterns and explicit preference configurations. Learned preferences must include preferred transport modes (some customers prefer all-road for time-sensitivity, others prefer rail for cost savings), preferred haulers and carriers, preferred ICDs and transshipment points, acceptable transit time ranges, and cost sensitivity thresholds. The AI must assign learned preference weights to the optimization objectives, personalizing the route recommendation ranking per customer. Explicit preferences set by the customer or account manager must override learned preferences. The system must maintain preference profiles per customer-entity combination (a customer's Qatar imports may have different preferences than their India imports). Preference learning models must be explainable, showing which historical patterns drove each learned preference in the customer profile viewable in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R011	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	What-If Scenario Analysis for Route Planning	The system must provide a what-if scenario analysis tool that allows planners to simulate the impact of hypothetical changes on route optimization outcomes. Scenario variables must include rate changes (e.g., what if rail rates increase by 15%), capacity changes (e.g., what if Jebel Ali adds a new feeder service to Hamad Port), transit time changes (e.g., what if JNPT customs processing improves by 2 days), new node additions (e.g., what if a new ICD opens near Sohar), and disruption scenarios (e.g., what if Suez Canal transit is disrupted for 2 weeks). The tool must run the optimization across the modified network and compare results against the baseline scenario, showing cost, time, and emission deltas. Scenarios must be saveable and shareable across users. Results must be presentable in multi-currency views for cross-entity impact assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R012	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Batch Route Optimization for Volume Planning	The system must support batch optimization where multiple shipments are optimized simultaneously to exploit network synergies. Batch optimization must consider shared capacity allocation (distributing limited rail wagon space across multiple bookings), combined trucking opportunities (multi-drop routes serving nearby consignees), and balanced ICD utilization (distributing volume across alternative ICDs to avoid congestion at a single facility). The batch optimizer must handle volumes up to 500 shipments per optimization run with results available within 5 minutes. Input data must include booking forecasts from the sales module for forward planning. The optimizer must generate a capacity demand heatmap across the network showing where bottlenecks are likely to occur in the planned period. All cost outputs must be in entity-specific currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD-equivalent totals for group-level volume planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R013	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	AI Model Training and Performance Monitoring	The system must provide a model management interface for monitoring and maintaining the AI optimization models. Key metrics displayed must include prediction accuracy (MAPE for transit time, cost estimation accuracy), optimization quality (percentage of recommendations accepted by dispatchers, percentage of recommended routes that met delivery commitments), and model freshness (date of last training, volume of new data since last training). The system must support scheduled automatic retraining (configurable, default monthly) and manual on-demand retraining triggered by operations managers. Training data must be filtered to exclude anomalous periods (major disruptions, pandemic impacts) that could skew normal pattern learning. Model versioning must allow rollback to a previous model version if a newly trained model underperforms. The monitoring dashboard must display metrics in bilingual Arabic/English format with Hijri and Gregorian date selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R014	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Integration with External Route and Map Services	The system must integrate with external mapping and route planning services for road segment distance, travel time, and route geometry data. Supported providers must include Google Maps Platform, HERE Technologies, and OpenStreetMap-based routing engines as configurable options. The integration must retrieve accurate road distances accounting for truck-specific routing (avoiding low bridges, weight-restricted roads, and residential zones), real-time traffic conditions for ETA calculations, and turn-by-turn route geometry for display on the planning map. For GCC countries, the system must use map providers that include Arabic place names and street names. Road toll calculations must be integrated for India NHAI electronic toll collection and UAE Salik/Darb tolling systems. The system must cache frequently queried routes to minimize API calls and must function with degraded accuracy using cached data during external service outages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R015	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Optimization Explainability and Recommendation Justification	The system must provide transparent explanations for every route recommendation generated by the AI optimization engine. Each recommended route must be accompanied by a justification narrative explaining why it was selected over alternatives, which optimization objectives it best satisfies, and what trade-offs were made. The explanation must include quantified comparisons: cost difference versus the cheapest alternative, transit time difference versus the fastest alternative, and emission difference versus the greenest alternative. Constraint satisfaction must be explained: which capacity limits, regulatory rules, or customer preferences influenced the recommendation. The explanation must be generated in both Arabic and English, using natural language that non-technical users can understand. The system must support a drill-down mode for technical users showing the underlying optimization weights, scores per objective, and Pareto ranking of each evaluated route option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R016	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Continuous Learning from Dispatcher Overrides	The system must capture and learn from dispatcher decisions to override AI-recommended routes. When a dispatcher selects a route different from the AI recommendation, the system must prompt for an override reason from a configurable list (customer request, capacity knowledge not in system, carrier relationship, cost negotiation in progress, regulatory change not yet updated, other with free text). Override data must be fed back into the optimization model as training signals, with the system adjusting recommendation weights over time to reduce override frequency. The system must track override rate as a key performance metric, targeting less than 15% overrides as a measure of AI recommendation quality. Monthly override analysis reports must categorize reasons and identify systematic gaps in the optimization model's knowledge. Reports must be available in Arabic and English with entity-level filtering for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-026-2-004-R017	FEAT-026-2-004	MOD-026	Intermodal Route Cost Allocation Across Entities	The system must allocate optimized route costs across multiple entities when a single intermodal movement spans jurisdictions managed by different entities. For example, a container moving from Jebel Ali (UAE entity) by road to a KSA ICD (KSA entity) for rail transit to Riyadh (KSA entity) must allocate the UAE road haulage cost to the UAE entity and the KSA rail and last-mile cost to the KSA entity. Transfer pricing rules must be configurable per entity pair with options for cost-plus markup, arm's-length market rate, or agreed fixed transfer prices. Inter-entity invoices must be automatically generated in the respective entity currencies (AED for UAE leg, SAR for KSA leg) with consolidated USD reporting for group management. Tax implications of cross-border inter-entity charges must be flagged including GCC customs union considerations and India IGST for inter-state movements. Audit trails must track all cost allocation decisions for transfer pricing compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R001	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Multi-Channel Claim Intake Portal	The system must provide a unified claim intake portal supporting web, mobile, email-to-claim parsing, and EDI (IFTMAN/IFTDGN) message ingestion for registering cargo claims. The portal must accept claims from shippers, consignees, freight forwarders, and insurance underwriters. Each claim must capture mandatory fields: bill of lading number, container number (ISO 6346 format), voyage reference, port of loading (QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, INJNP), port of discharge, commodity description, declared cargo value with currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), damage description, and date of discovery. The UI must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with full right-to-left text entry support for damage descriptions and claimant details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R006	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Mutual Recognition Agreement Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of Mutual Recognition Agreements (MRAs) between AEO programs relevant to the operating jurisdictions, including GCC Customs Union mutual recognition, India-UAE CEPA AEO provisions, and any bilateral MRA arrangements with major trading partners. For each MRA, the system shall track effective dates, eligible benefit categories, partner country AEO equivalents, and operational procedures for claiming MRA benefits at destination ports. When processing shipments between MRA-partner countries, the AI engine must automatically identify eligible consignments and pre-populate MRA benefit claim documentation, reducing manual intervention. The system must alert compliance officers when new MRAs are signed or existing ones are amended.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R007	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Corrective Action Plan Management	The system must support creation, assignment, tracking, and closure of corrective action plans (CAPs) arising from AEO self-assessments, customs authority audits, or internal compliance reviews. Each CAP shall have a unique identifier, root cause classification, assigned responsible party, target completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, priority level (critical/high/medium/low), and supporting evidence requirements. The AI engine must recommend corrective actions based on historical resolution patterns for similar findings across all four jurisdictions. Overdue CAPs must trigger escalation notifications to entity compliance managers and regional heads. The system must maintain a CAP effectiveness register tracking whether implemented actions prevented recurrence of identified non-conformities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R008	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Tier Upgrade Readiness Assessment	The system must provide an AI-driven readiness assessment for entities seeking to upgrade their AEO tier level, such as moving from CBIC AEO-T1 to AEO-T2 or from ZATCA basic AEO to enhanced AEO status. The assessment shall compare current compliance metrics, financial stability indicators, security infrastructure ratings, and operational performance against the target tier requirements. Gap analysis results must be presented as a prioritized action list with estimated effort, cost implications in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and timeline recommendations. The system must generate a formal upgrade application package with pre-filled forms and supporting evidence when readiness score exceeds 90%, outputting documents in the language required by the target customs authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R009	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Supply Chain Partner Validation	The system must maintain a validated supply chain partner registry for AEO compliance, screening all freight forwarders, customs brokers, warehouse operators, and transport carriers against AEO requirements. Each partner shall be assessed on their own AEO or equivalent certification status, customs compliance history, security protocols, and financial standing. The AI engine must continuously monitor partner risk profiles using data from customs violation databases, sanction lists, and commercially available risk intelligence feeds. Partners operating at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT must have port-specific security clearance verification. When a partner's risk rating deteriorates below acceptable thresholds, the system must flag affected shipments and recommend alternative AEO-compliant partners.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R010	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Annual Renewal Workflow	The system must automate the annual AEO certification renewal process for each entity and jurisdiction, initiating the workflow 120 days before expiry. The renewal workflow shall include updated self-assessment completion, financial document compilation (audited statements in local currency), security audit scheduling, compliance metric aggregation, and application form generation in the format required by QCGA, FCA, ZATCA, or CBIC respectively. Each workflow step must have configurable SLA timers with escalation rules. The system must track renewal application status through submission, customs authority review, site inspection scheduling, and final decision stages. All renewal correspondence must be stored with bilingual Arabic/English support and Hijri date references where required by the authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R011	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Compliance Training Record Management	The system must track AEO compliance training requirements and completion records for all personnel involved in customs operations across the four jurisdictions. Training modules shall cover customs procedures, security awareness, record-keeping obligations, and jurisdiction-specific AEO program rules. Each training record must capture employee details, training type, completion date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, certification validity period, and assessment scores. The AI engine must identify training gaps by comparing role-based requirements against completed training for staff at each port location (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT). Automated reminders must be sent 30 days before training certifications expire, and the system must prevent assignment of customs-critical tasks to personnel with expired or incomplete AEO training records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R012	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO KPI Benchmarking Across Entities	The system must enable cross-entity benchmarking of AEO key performance indicators including customs declaration error rates, physical inspection percentages, clearance time averages, duty dispute frequency, and compliance cost per TEU. Benchmarks shall compare performance between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities against each other and against industry standards published by WCO SAFE Framework guidelines. All financial KPIs must be convertible between QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD using configurable exchange rates from the ERP treasury module. Benchmark reports must render in both Arabic and English with RTL-compatible charts and tables. The AI engine must identify underperforming entities and recommend specific improvement actions derived from best practices observed in higher-performing sister entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R013	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Document Expiry and Validity Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the validity status of all documents required for AEO compliance including trade licenses, customs registration certificates, security certifications (ISO 28000, TAPA, C-TPAT), insurance policies, and financial guarantees/bonds. Each document shall be stored with metadata including issuing authority, issue date, expiry date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, applicable entity, and linked port operations. The system must generate a tiered alert schedule at 90, 60, 30, and 7 days before document expiry, escalating to senior management for documents expiring within 7 days without renewal action initiated. Expired documents must automatically flag the affected entity's AEO profile as at-risk and block new AEO benefit claims until the document is renewed or a waiver is documented.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R014	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Customs Authority Communication Log	The system must maintain a structured communication log for all interactions with customs authorities regarding AEO matters across QCGA, FCA, ZATCA, and CBIC. Each communication entry shall record the authority name, contact person, communication channel (email, portal submission, in-person meeting, phone), date and time in local timezone and UTC, subject matter classification, action items arising, and follow-up deadlines. Communications in Arabic must be stored with original Arabic text and optional English translation. The system must link communications to related AEO profiles, audit findings, or corrective action plans. AI-powered summarization must generate weekly digests of authority communications for compliance leadership review, highlighting urgent items requiring immediate attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R015	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Offline Compliance Checklist Mode	The system must provide an offline-capable AEO compliance checklist module for use during port facility inspections, warehouse audits, or customs authority site visits where internet connectivity may be unreliable at locations such as remote container yards at Hamad Port or inland container depots near JNPT. The offline module shall cache the latest AEO self-assessment questionnaire, facility security checklists, document verification templates, and corrective action forms. Users must be able to complete checklists, capture photographic evidence, record GPS-tagged observations, and annotate findings in Arabic or English while offline. Upon reconnection, all offline data must synchronize with the central system with conflict resolution for any records modified concurrently, maintaining full audit trail integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-001-R016	FEAT-027-2-001	MOD-027	AEO Suspension and Revocation Risk Alerting	The system must implement an AI-driven early warning mechanism that identifies conditions which could lead to AEO certification suspension or revocation by any of the four customs authorities. Risk triggers shall include repeated customs violations exceeding authority thresholds, failure to submit annual renewals, unresolved critical audit findings beyond grace periods, financial instability indicators, and security breaches at port facilities. The AI engine must calculate suspension probability scores using historical data from all jurisdictions and alert compliance officers when probability exceeds configurable thresholds (default 25%). Alert notifications must include the specific risk factors, recommended immediate mitigation actions, and estimated financial impact of AEO suspension in the entity's operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R001	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Port Facility Security Plan PFSP Management	The system must maintain digital Port Facility Security Plans (PFSPs) for each terminal and berth operated at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA) in compliance with ISPS Code Part A Section 16. Each PFSP shall contain security organization structure, access control procedures, restricted area definitions, cargo handling security measures, security communication protocols, and incident response procedures. Plans must be stored in both Arabic and English versions with version control tracking all amendments. The system must enforce access restrictions ensuring only authorized personnel (Port Facility Security Officers and designated alternates) can view or modify PFSPs, with full audit logging of all access events including timestamps in local port timezone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R002	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Maritime Security Level Management	The system must support real-time management of ISPS security levels (Level 1 Normal, Level 2 Heightened, Level 3 Exceptional) for each port facility independently. When a contracting government (Qatar MOT, UAE FTA, KSA TGA, India DG Shipping) issues a security level change directive, the system must immediately propagate the change to all affected facility security plans, trigger corresponding security measure activation checklists, notify all Port Facility Security Officers (PFSOs) and Company Security Officers (CSOs), and update vessel pre-arrival notification requirements. Security level transitions must be logged with the directive reference number, issuing authority, effective timestamp, and acknowledgment records from all notified parties. The system must maintain separate security level tracking per berth when facilities operate under split-level conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R003	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Declaration of Security DOS Processing	The system must automate the preparation, exchange, and archival of Declarations of Security (DoS) between port facilities and vessels as required by ISPS Code Part A Section 5. For each vessel call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system shall determine whether a DoS is required based on current security levels, vessel flag state risk assessment, and port-specific requirements. The AI engine must pre-populate DoS forms with vessel particulars from AIS data, port facility security measures in effect, and agreed security activities between the Ship Security Officer (SSO) and Port Facility Security Officer (PFSO). Completed DoS documents must be stored for the mandatory retention period of the port's flag state requirements, searchable by vessel IMO number, call date, and port facility code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R004	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Security Drill and Exercise Scheduling	The system must manage the scheduling, execution, and recording of ISPS-mandated security drills and exercises for all port facilities. Drills shall be scheduled at intervals not exceeding three months as per ISPS Code Part A Section 18, covering scenarios including unauthorized access attempts, bomb threats, cargo tampering detection, and cyber security incidents. Each drill record must capture drill type, date and time in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, participating personnel with roles, scenario description in Arabic and English, lessons learned, and corrective actions identified. The AI engine must analyze drill performance trends across facilities and recommend focus areas for upcoming drills. The system must generate automated alerts when drill scheduling gaps approach the three-month regulatory maximum, escalating to the PFSO and entity security manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R005	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Port Facility Security Assessment PFSA Automation	The system must support the conduct and documentation of Port Facility Security Assessments (PFSAs) as required by ISPS Code Part A Section 15 for each terminal at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The PFSA module shall include asset identification and prioritization, threat scenario modeling, vulnerability analysis of physical security infrastructure, consequence assessment matrices, and countermeasure gap identification. The AI engine must leverage historical security incident data, regional threat intelligence, and vessel traffic patterns to generate risk-rated threat profiles specific to each port facility. Assessment outputs must map directly to PFSP sections requiring update, with auto-generated recommendations for security enhancement prioritized by risk reduction impact and estimated implementation cost in the facility's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R006	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Audit and Verification Readiness	The system must maintain continuous audit readiness for ISPS verification inspections conducted by recognized security organizations (RSOs) and flag state administrations. An always-current readiness dashboard shall display compliance status against all ISPS Code Part A and Part B requirements for each port facility, highlighting non-conformities, pending corrective actions, and documentation gaps. The system must generate a pre-audit checklist customized for the inspecting authority's known focus areas, derived from AI analysis of previous audit reports and regional inspection trends. Mock audit simulation capability must allow PFSOs to conduct internal verification rounds with scored checklists that mirror actual RSO audit protocols. All readiness metrics and reports must be available in Arabic and English with export capability to PDF and Excel formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R007	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Vessel Pre-Arrival Security Notification Processing	The system must process and validate vessel pre-arrival security notifications for all container vessels calling at operated terminals, verifying ISPS compliance documentation including valid International Ship Security Certificates (ISSCs), continuous synopsis records, and last ten port of call security information. The system shall cross-reference vessel IMO numbers against sanctioned vessel databases, port state control detention records, and ISPS non-compliance histories. For vessels arriving from ports with elevated security levels or those flagged by regional maritime security centers (UKMTO, MSCHOA for Gulf of Aden transit), the system must automatically recommend enhanced security measures and DoS requirements. Processing must handle high volumes during peak season at Jebel Ali (up to 30+ container vessel calls daily) with sub-second response times for security clearance decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R008	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Access Control Integration	The system must integrate with physical access control systems at each port facility to enforce ISPS-mandated restricted area management. Access permissions shall be configurable per security level, differentiating between permanent staff, temporary workers, visitors, seafarers on shore leave, customs officials, and other government authorities. The system must track access card issuance, biometric enrollment status, and access event logs for each individual entering restricted areas of the container terminal. For multi-entity operations where staff may work across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must maintain federated identity management with port-specific access profiles. Access denial events must trigger real-time alerts to the duty security officer with the individual's identification details, attempted access point, and reason for denial in Arabic or English based on the facility's primary language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R009	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Security Incident Reporting and Investigation	The system must provide a structured security incident reporting module compliant with ISPS Code requirements and local maritime authority regulations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Incident reports shall capture incident type classification (unauthorized access, stowaways, contraband detection, cyber intrusion, bomb threat, piracy/armed robbery), location within the port facility using geo-referenced coordinates, date and time in local timezone and UTC, personnel involved, immediate actions taken, and photographic or video evidence. The AI engine must auto-classify incident severity, recommend escalation paths based on the incident type and jurisdiction, and generate regulatory notifications to the appropriate maritime authority (Qatar MOT, UAE FTA, KSA TGA, India DG Shipping). Investigation workflows must track root cause analysis, corrective actions, and PFSP amendments resulting from incident findings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R010	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Security Equipment Maintenance Tracking	The system must track the maintenance, calibration, and operational status of all ISPS-related security equipment deployed across port facilities including CCTV systems, X-ray scanners, radiation portal monitors, perimeter intrusion detection systems, vessel tracking radar, and communication equipment. Each equipment item shall have a maintenance schedule aligned with manufacturer specifications and port authority requirements. The system must generate work orders for preventive maintenance, track equipment downtime periods, and assess the security impact of non-operational equipment against the facility's PFSP requirements. When critical security equipment is offline, the AI engine must recommend compensating security measures as defined in the facility's contingency procedures. Equipment inventories and maintenance records must be available for ISPS verification audits with documentation in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R011	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Ship-Port Interface Security Coordination	The system must facilitate security coordination at the ship-port interface for every vessel call, managing communication between the Ship Security Officer (SSO), Port Facility Security Officer (PFSO), and Company Security Officer (CSO). The coordination module shall handle pre-arrival security information exchange, gangway and access point security arrangements, cargo operations security protocols for container loading and discharge, and bunkering security procedures. For each ship-port interface event, the system must generate a security activity timeline synchronized with the vessel's cargo operations schedule from the terminal operating system. The system must support real-time secure messaging between SSO and PFSO with message encryption, read receipt tracking, and Arabic/English translation assistance for communications between Arabic-speaking port staff and international vessel crews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R012	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Training and Certification Compliance	The system must manage ISPS security training programs and personnel certification for all port facility security staff across the four jurisdictions. Training requirements shall align with ISPS Code Part B Section 18 and local maritime authority mandates from Qatar MOT, UAE FTA, KSA TGA, and India DG Shipping. The system must track PFSO certification validity (five-year cycle), security guard training completion, and specialized training for X-ray operators, access control personnel, and security patrol teams. Training records must include course name, provider accreditation, completion date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, certificate number, and next renewal date. The AI engine must forecast training demand based on staff turnover rates and certification expiry schedules, generating quarterly training plans with budget estimates in local currency for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R013	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	Cyber Security Risk Management for Port Facilities	The system must incorporate maritime cyber security risk management into the ISPS framework as per IMO MSC-FAL.1/Circ.3 guidelines on maritime cyber risk management. The module shall assess cyber threats to operational technology (OT) systems at container terminals including terminal operating systems, container tracking, CCTV networks, access control systems, and vessel traffic management interfaces. Cyber risk assessments must be conducted quarterly for each port facility with vulnerability scanning results, threat intelligence feeds, and mitigation action tracking. The system must maintain a cyber incident response plan integrated with the physical security incident reporting module, ensuring coordinated response when cyber attacks affect port operations. Reports must support multi-language output for submission to the respective national maritime cyber security agencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R014	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Compliance Reporting to Maritime Authorities	The system must generate and submit periodic ISPS compliance reports to the designated maritime authorities in each jurisdiction: Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, KSA Transport General Authority, and India Directorate General of Shipping. Reports shall include security level change histories, DoS execution summaries, drill completion statistics, security incident summaries, equipment maintenance status, and personnel training compliance rates. Each authority has different reporting formats, frequencies, and submission channels which the system must support through configurable report templates. Reports for Qatar and KSA authorities must be generated in Arabic with Hijri calendar date formatting as the primary format, while UAE and India reports shall use English with Gregorian dates. The system must maintain submission receipts and authority acknowledgments for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R015	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Offline Security Operations Mode	The system must provide an offline-capable mode for critical ISPS security operations during network outages or communication disruptions at port facilities. The offline module shall cache current security levels, active vessel call security profiles, access control authorization lists, emergency contact directories, and security incident reporting forms. Security personnel must be able to record access control events, log security patrols, document incidents with photographic evidence, and complete DoS forms while offline. Upon network restoration, all offline transactions must synchronize with the central system using conflict resolution logic that prioritizes security-critical data. The offline mode must support Arabic and English interfaces and function on ruggedized tablets used by security patrol teams at outdoor container yard locations across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-002-R016	FEAT-027-2-002	MOD-027	ISPS Inter-Facility Security Level Coordination	The system must coordinate security level management across multiple facilities operated by the same entity within a single port complex or across the four operating ports. When a security level change is issued affecting one facility at Jebel Ali Free Zone, the system must evaluate whether adjacent or operationally linked facilities at the same port should adopt aligned security measures even if not formally directed. The AI engine must analyze vessel movement patterns between facilities, shared access points, common perimeter boundaries, and supply chain dependencies to recommend coordinated security responses. Cross-entity security coordination protocols must be supported for scenarios where Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities share operational interfaces such as transshipment cargo moving between Hamad Port and Jebel Ali. All coordination decisions must be logged with justification for audit review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R001	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Inspection Targeting Risk Assessment	The system must calculate PSC inspection targeting risk scores for all container vessels scheduled to call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT using the targeting criteria of the applicable MOU regime (Indian Ocean MOU for Qatar/UAE/India, Riyadh MOU for KSA). Risk factors shall include vessel age, flag state performance from MOU white/grey/black lists, classification society recognized organization status, company performance history from THETIS/Asia-Pacific databases, previous PSC deficiency and detention records, and vessel type-specific risk parameters for container ships. The AI engine must predict inspection probability within a confidence range and recommend priority preparation actions. Risk scores must update dynamically as MOU databases refresh and vessel inspection histories change, with Arabic and English display options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R002	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	Pre-Inspection Readiness Checklist Generation	The system must auto-generate comprehensive pre-inspection readiness checklists tailored to each vessel's characteristics, flag state requirements, and the PSC regime of the calling port. Checklists shall cover IMO convention compliance areas: SOLAS (safety of life at sea) including fire safety, life-saving appliances, and navigation equipment; MARPOL (pollution prevention) covering Annexes I through VI; MLC 2006 (seafarer working conditions); STCW (crew certification); and ISM Code (safety management). Each checklist item must reference the specific convention article, regulation, and resolution. The AI engine must highlight high-risk checklist items based on the vessel's deficiency history and current industry-wide PSC campaign focus areas such as SOLAS Chapter II-2 fire safety or MARPOL Annex VI sulfur cap compliance. Checklists must be available in Arabic and English for multi-lingual crew usage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R003	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Deficiency History Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of PSC deficiency records for all vessels in the managed fleet and chartered tonnage, sourced from THETIS (Paris MOU), Asia-Pacific Computerized Information System (APCIS), Indian Ocean MOU database, and Riyadh MOU records. Each deficiency entry shall store the inspection port and country, inspection date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, PSC officer details, deficiency code per IMO harmonized system, nature of deficiency description, action taken code, and detention status. The database must support advanced search and filtering by vessel IMO number, deficiency category, inspection port (including Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT specifically), time period, and severity. The AI engine must identify recurring deficiency patterns across the fleet and generate trend analysis reports in both Arabic and English for fleet safety management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R003	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Exchange rate spread and margin configuration	The system must support configurable spread or margin application on top of raw feed rates for commercial use. Spread rules must be definable per currency pair, transaction type (booking, invoice, payment, GL posting), customer segment, and entity. For example, the Qatar entity may apply a 0.5% spread on USD/QAR for customer invoicing but use the mid-market rate for inter-company settlements. Spread can be defined as a fixed pip amount or a percentage of the base rate. The system must preview the effective commercial rate after spread application and maintain audit records distinguishing between raw feed rates and commercially applied rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R004	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Inspection Outcome Recording and Analysis	The system must capture real-time PSC inspection outcomes as they occur at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, recording inspector identity, inspection type (initial, more detailed, expanded), deficiencies found with IMO codes, detention decisions, and conditions of class imposed. Each inspection outcome must be linked to the vessel's operational record, the pre-inspection readiness checklist completion status, and the inspection targeting risk score that was calculated prior to the inspection. The AI engine must perform root cause analysis on each deficiency, correlating it with vessel maintenance records, crew change history, and previous corrective actions. Inspection outcomes must trigger automated workflows for deficiency rectification with deadlines, responsible party assignment, and follow-up inspection scheduling. Financial impact of detentions must be calculated in USD and the local port currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R005	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	Vessel Certificate and Document Validity Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the validity of all statutory and classification certificates required for PSC compliance, including Safety Equipment Certificate, Safety Construction Certificate, Safety Radio Certificate, International Load Line Certificate, IOPP Certificate, IAPP Certificate, International Energy Efficiency Certificate, Minimum Safe Manning Certificate, Document of Compliance (ISM), Safety Management Certificate (ISM), and Maritime Labour Certificate. Each certificate shall be tracked with issuing authority, issue date, expiry date, annual/intermediate survey due dates, and endorsement status. The system must generate tiered alerts at 90, 60, 30, and 14 days before certificate expiry and immediately flag any vessel arriving at a managed port with expired or soon-to-expire certificates. Certificate data must be presentable in both Arabic and English to support multi-lingual port operations teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R006	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Campaign and CIC Preparation Module	The system must track active and upcoming PSC Concentrated Inspection Campaigns (CICs) announced by the Paris MOU, Tokyo MOU, Indian Ocean MOU, and Riyadh MOU regimes. For each active CIC, the system shall identify the campaign topic (e.g., stability compliance, STCW hours of rest, MARPOL Annex VI), applicable vessel types, campaign duration, and inspection questionnaire details. The AI engine must assess each vessel in the fleet against the active CIC criteria, generating vessel-specific preparation guides highlighting potential non-compliance areas and recommended corrective actions. CIC readiness dashboards must show fleet-wide compliance percentages with drill-down to individual vessel status. All CIC preparation materials must be available in Arabic and English for distribution to vessel crews and shore-based technical superintendents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R007	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	Crew Competency and Certification PSC Verification	The system must verify crew competency documentation against STCW Convention requirements and flag state endorsement rules for PSC readiness. For each crew member aboard managed vessels calling at the four operating ports, the system shall validate certificates of competency, flag state endorsements, STCW certificates (basic safety training, advanced firefighting, medical first aid, survival craft), tanker endorsements where applicable for multi-purpose vessels, and hours of rest compliance records. The AI engine must cross-reference crew certification data against the vessel's minimum safe manning requirements and flag any gaps that would constitute PSC deficiencies. Crew change records must be analyzed to ensure new joiners have completed familiarization training within STCW-mandated timeframes. The system must handle crew documents issued in multiple languages including Arabic, Hindi, and Filipino with English translations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R008	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Detention Prevention Analytics	The system must employ AI-driven predictive analytics to identify vessels at elevated detention risk before they arrive at PSC-active ports in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The detention prediction model shall incorporate vessel age and type coefficients, flag state detention ratios from the latest MOU annual reports, recognized organization performance metrics, company detention history, time since last PSC inspection, outstanding deficiency rectification status, and crew nationality-based performance statistics. The model must output a detention probability percentage with contributing factor weights and specific recommended mitigation actions for each risk factor. Vessels exceeding a configurable detention risk threshold (default 15%) must trigger pre-arrival alert workflows notifying the technical superintendent, fleet manager, and commercial operations to assess schedule contingency plans with financial exposure estimates in USD and local port currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R009	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Corrective Action and Deficiency Rectification Tracking	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of PSC deficiency rectification from initial finding through corrective action to verification and closure. Each deficiency shall be assigned to a responsible technical superintendent with a rectification deadline based on the PSC action taken code: immediate rectification for detainable deficiencies, rectification before departure for code 16/17 actions, and rectification within 14 days for code 15 actions. The system must track parts procurement, service engineer scheduling, classification society surveyor attendance, and flag state notification where required. Rectification evidence including photographs, survey reports, and class condition closure letters must be stored against each deficiency record. The AI engine must estimate rectification costs in USD and local currency, track actual expenditure, and compile monthly deficiency rectification cost analysis reports per vessel and per fleet segment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R010	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Performance KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive PSC performance dashboard displaying fleet-wide and individual vessel KPIs including total inspections per period, deficiency index (deficiencies per inspection), detention rate, nil-deficiency inspection rate, average deficiencies per inspection by category, and comparison against MOU regime averages. The dashboard must support filtering by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), vessel, flag state, time period using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors, and deficiency category. Trend lines must show performance trajectory over rolling 12-month windows with year-over-year comparison. KPIs must benchmark against published MOU annual report statistics for the vessel's flag state and recognized organization. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL and English LTR display modes with exportable reports in PDF format for management review and charterer due diligence responses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R011	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Inspector Interaction Protocol Management	The system must provide structured guidance for vessel and shore staff on PSC inspector interaction protocols specific to each port jurisdiction. For Hamad Port, the system shall reference Qatar Maritime Administration inspection procedures; for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, UAE FTA maritime inspection protocols; for KSA ports, TGA inspection guidelines; and for JNPT, Indian DG Shipping PSC procedures. Each protocol shall include inspector reception procedures, document presentation sequence, restricted area escort requirements, crew interview preparation guidance, and escalation contacts for disputed deficiency findings. The AI engine must generate jurisdiction-specific briefing documents for the master and chief officer before each port call, incorporating any known PSC inspector preferences or focus areas derived from historical inspection data. Briefings must be generated in English with Arabic translation for GCC port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R012	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	Classification Society Survey Alignment with PSC	The system must align classification society survey schedules with PSC inspection readiness, ensuring that annual, intermediate, and special surveys are completed before vessels trade in PSC-intensive port regions. The system shall integrate with classification society portals (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, Indian Register of Shipping) to track survey status, conditions of class, and outstanding recommendations. When a vessel has overdue survey items or open conditions of class, the AI engine must flag these as high-probability PSC deficiency triggers and recommend expedited survey completion. The system must map classification society findings to IMO deficiency codes to predict which class items would appear as PSC deficiencies. Survey completion certificates and condition of class reports must be stored with bilingual Arabic/English metadata for quick retrieval during PSC inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R013	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	MARPOL Compliance Verification for PSC Readiness	The system must verify vessel compliance with all applicable MARPOL Annexes before port arrival to prevent pollution-related PSC deficiencies. Verification shall cover Annex I oil discharge monitoring equipment and oil record book entries, Annex II NLS cargo record book compliance, Annex IV sewage treatment plant operational status, Annex V garbage management plan and garbage record book, and Annex VI emission control including sulfur content compliance for fuel oil used within emission control areas and IMO 2020 global sulfur cap. The system must validate fuel oil bunker delivery notes against MARPOL Annex VI requirements, cross-checking sulfur content declarations with port-specific fuel quality requirements. For vessels calling at ports within the Arabian Gulf emission monitoring zones, the AI engine must verify fuel switchover records and calculate compliance margins. MARPOL documentation must be maintained in English with Arabic annotations where required by local port authority regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R014	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Follow-Up Inspection Scheduling and Tracking	The system must track and manage PSC follow-up inspection requirements arising from initial inspections at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and any other port globally. When a PSC inspection results in deficiency codes requiring follow-up verification (action taken codes 15, 16, 17, or 30), the system must record the follow-up deadline, next port of call inspection requirements, and classification society involvement needs. The AI engine must coordinate follow-up scheduling with the vessel's commercial itinerary, recommending optimal follow-up ports based on port state authority availability, classification society surveyor presence, and repair facility proximity. For detentions requiring re-inspection before departure, the system must manage the reinspection request workflow including flag state notification, classification society attendance confirmation, and port authority appointment scheduling. All follow-up records must maintain currency references in USD and local port currency for cost tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R015	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Data Integration with External MOU Databases	The system must integrate with external PSC databases including THETIS (Paris MOU and cooperating MOUs), Asia-Pacific Computerized Information System, Indian Ocean MOU Information System, and Riyadh MOU database to retrieve and synchronize vessel inspection records. Integration shall support automated polling for new inspection records related to managed fleet vessels, with data mapping from each database's schema to the internal deficiency tracking format. The system must handle data discrepancies between databases such as delayed entries, coding differences, and incomplete records, flagging inconsistencies for manual review. API rate limits and database availability windows must be managed with retry logic and offline caching. Data retrieved from external databases must be stored with source attribution, retrieval timestamp, and data freshness indicators. The integration must comply with data sharing agreements and privacy regulations applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R016	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	PSC Preparation Offline Vessel Module	The system must provide an offline-capable PSC preparation module for use aboard vessels where satellite connectivity may be limited or expensive. The module shall cache the vessel's complete certificate inventory, deficiency history, pre-inspection checklists, crew certification data, MARPOL record book templates, and PSC inspector interaction protocols for all four operating port jurisdictions. Ship staff must be able to conduct self-assessments, update checklist completion status, record maintenance activities affecting PSC readiness, and capture photographic evidence of rectified deficiencies while offline. Upon connectivity restoration, data must synchronize bidirectionally with the shore-based system, with conflict resolution prioritizing vessel-side updates for operational records and shore-side updates for certificate validity data. The offline module must support English as the primary interface language with Arabic language support for GCC port-specific documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-003-R017	FEAT-027-2-003	MOD-027	Charterer and P&I Club PSC Reporting	The system must generate PSC performance reports formatted for submission to charterers conducting vetting assessments and P&I Club correspondents managing claims exposure. Charterer reports shall include vessel PSC inspection history for the trailing 24 months, deficiency index trends, detention records, current outstanding deficiencies, and rectification completion status in formats compatible with SIRE 2.0 and CDI inspection regimes. P&I Club reports shall summarize detention incidents with root cause analysis, estimated financial exposure in USD for detention costs including port charges, crew overtime, bunker consumption, and off-hire claims, as well as corrective action implementation status. The system must support automated report generation triggered by charterer enquiries or P&I Club questionnaires, with configurable report templates per recipient. Reports must include multi-currency conversion for port charges incurred in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R001	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	IMO Document Repository and Classification	The system must maintain a structured repository of IMO documents including circulars from MSC, MEPC, FAL, LEG, and TC committees, resolutions, guidelines, codes, and amendments to international conventions (SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, MLC, ISPS, ISM, IMDG, CSC). Each document shall be classified by committee of origin, document number, issue date, subject matter taxonomy (safety, environment, security, facilitation, legal), applicable vessel types, and implementation deadline. The repository must support full-text search in English with metadata tagging for Arabic keyword search. The AI engine must automatically categorize new IMO documents upon ingestion, linking them to affected operational areas within the container shipping ERP such as vessel operations, cargo handling, port facility management, and customs compliance. Document storage must handle PDF, DOCX, and HTML formats with version tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R002	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Automated IMO Document Ingestion Pipeline	The system must implement an automated pipeline for ingesting newly published IMO documents from official IMO sources including IMODOCS, GISIS databases, and maritime administration notifications from Qatar MOT, UAE FTA, KSA TGA, and India DG Shipping. The pipeline shall check for new publications at configurable intervals (default daily), download new documents, extract metadata using NLP processing, classify documents by relevance to container shipping operations, and queue high-relevance documents for compliance officer review. The AI engine must assign an initial impact assessment rating (critical/high/medium/low/informational) based on the document's scope, mandatory versus recommendatory nature, and applicability to the managed fleet and port operations. Documents classified as critical or high impact must trigger immediate notification to the regulatory compliance manager and affected department heads.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R003	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Regulation Amendment Tracking and Timeline Management	The system must track the lifecycle of IMO regulation amendments from initial proposal through committee discussion, adoption, and entry into force, with specific focus on amendments affecting container shipping such as SOLAS container weight verification (VGM), IMDG Code amendments, MARPOL Annex VI decarbonization measures, and maritime autonomous surface ship regulations. Each amendment shall have a tracked timeline showing proposal date, committee session references, adoption date, expected entry into force date with tacit acceptance deadline, and transitional provisions. The system must calculate days remaining to implementation for each applicable amendment and generate countdown dashboards viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. For amendments with transitional provisions, the system must track which vessels or facilities in the managed fleet have completed transitional compliance and which remain pending.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R004	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Regulatory Impact Assessment on Operations	The system must perform AI-driven impact assessments for each new or amended IMO regulation against current container shipping operations across the four jurisdictions. Impact assessment shall evaluate effects on vessel technical compliance (structural modifications, equipment upgrades), operational procedures (cargo handling, navigation, pollution prevention), documentation requirements (new certificates, amended record-keeping), crew training needs (new competency requirements, updated familiarization), and financial implications (capital expenditure for modifications, operational cost changes). Financial impacts must be estimated in USD with local currency equivalents (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and broken down by entity. The AI engine must generate prioritized implementation recommendations considering the regulation's entry into force date, fleet renewal schedule, and available dry-docking windows. Impact assessments must be reviewable and adjustable by compliance officers before finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R005	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	National Implementation Tracking per Jurisdiction	The system must track how each operating jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) implements IMO regulations into national law, as implementation timelines, scope interpretations, and additional requirements often vary between flag and port states. For each IMO regulation, the system shall record the national implementation status for all four jurisdictions including the national legislative instrument reference, local effective date which may differ from IMO entry into force date, additional national requirements beyond IMO minimums, and designated enforcement authority. The AI engine must identify discrepancies between jurisdictions, such as KSA implementing stricter emission standards than IMO minimums or India requiring additional documentation beyond SOLAS requirements. Discrepancy alerts must be generated for vessels and facilities trading across multiple jurisdictions, recommending compliance with the most stringent applicable standard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R006	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Compliance Gap Analysis Generator	The system must auto-generate compliance gap analyses comparing current vessel and facility compliance status against newly adopted or upcoming IMO regulations. For each regulation, the gap analysis shall identify specific areas of non-compliance or potential non-compliance across the managed fleet and port facilities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Gap items must be categorized by severity (critical gap requiring immediate action, significant gap requiring action before regulation entry into force, minor gap addressable during routine maintenance, and no gap/already compliant). Each gap item shall include the specific regulation clause reference, current compliance status evidence, required corrective action description, estimated cost in USD and local currency, and recommended implementation timeline. The AI engine must prioritize gaps by regulatory enforcement risk, operational safety impact, and cost-efficiency of remediation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R007	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	IMO Committee Session Monitoring and Reporting	The system must track IMO committee session schedules (MSC, MEPC, FAL, LEG, TC, sub-committees) and their agendas, identifying items relevant to container shipping operations. For each relevant agenda item, the system shall record the proposal summary, sponsoring member states or organizations, expected outcome, and potential operational impact on managed fleet and port facilities. Post-session, the system must capture adopted decisions, new work program items, and circulated draft amendments. The AI engine must generate post-session briefing reports summarizing key outcomes relevant to the organization's operations in Arabic and English, distributed to compliance officers, fleet managers, and senior management. Session monitoring must include IMO Assembly sessions and diplomatic conferences where major convention amendments are adopted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R008	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Regulatory Compliance Calendar and Deadline Management	The system must maintain a unified regulatory compliance calendar displaying all upcoming IMO regulation implementation deadlines, national maritime authority submission deadlines, certificate renewal dates, and survey windows for the managed fleet and port facilities. The calendar must support both Gregorian and Hijri date display with configurable views (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual). Each calendar entry shall link to the associated regulation, compliance gap analysis, responsible person or department, and completion status. The AI engine must identify deadline conflicts and resource bottlenecks, recommending schedule adjustments to prevent multiple high-impact compliance activities from overlapping. Calendar entries for port-specific deadlines must be tagged with the relevant port code (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA) and rendered in the appropriate language (Arabic for Qatar/KSA, English for UAE/India by default).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R009	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	IMDG Code Amendment Tracking for Container Cargo	The system must provide specialized tracking for International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code amendments that directly impact container shipping operations including classification changes, packing instruction updates, segregation table modifications, documentation requirement changes, and new special provisions. Each IMDG amendment shall be mapped to affected UN numbers, proper shipping names, and hazard classes commonly transported through Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must identify container bookings and existing cargo manifests that may be affected by upcoming IMDG amendments, alerting operations teams to reclassification requirements or changed stowage and segregation rules. Amendment implementation guides must be generated for container planning teams, dangerous goods inspectors, and documentation staff in both Arabic and English, with specific attention to regional DG cargo patterns such as petrochemical products prevalent in GCC ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R010	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	MARPOL Decarbonization Regulation Tracking	The system must provide dedicated tracking for IMO decarbonization regulations under MARPOL Annex VI including the Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI), Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII), IMO GHG Strategy milestones, and emerging market-based measures such as the IMO maritime carbon levy or emissions trading proposals. For each managed container vessel, the system shall track current EEXI attained value versus required value, CII rating trajectory (A through E), and corrective action plan status for vessels rated D or E. The AI engine must model future CII rating trajectories based on current trading patterns between GCC and Indian ports, fuel consumption trends, and planned operational efficiency measures. Financial impact projections must account for potential carbon pricing in USD per tonne of CO2 equivalent, with fleet-wide exposure calculated in USD and broken down by entity in local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R011	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Container Safety Convention CSC Regulation Tracking	The system must track amendments and interpretations of the International Convention for Safe Containers (CSC) 1972 and its annexes as they affect container fleet management and port inspection procedures. The system shall monitor CSC plate compliance for managed container equipment, track ACEP (Approved Continuous Examination Programme) implementation status, and identify containers requiring re-examination based on age, condition reports, and CSC amendment requirements. When new CSC circular interpretations are published regarding structural testing, marking requirements, or examination procedures, the AI engine must assess impact on the managed container fleet and generate compliance action items for container maintenance depots at or near Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must track container rejection rates at PSC inspections attributable to CSC non-compliance and correlate them with depot maintenance quality metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R012	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships MASS Regulation Monitoring	The system must monitor emerging IMO regulations on Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships (MASS) and assess their potential impact on container shipping operations, port infrastructure, and regulatory compliance frameworks. Tracking shall cover the MASS regulatory scoping exercise outcomes, MASS Code development progress, proposed amendments to SOLAS, COLREG, STCW, and other conventions for autonomous vessel operations, and national autonomous vessel trial frameworks in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The AI engine must assess readiness of port facilities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to handle autonomous or remotely operated container vessels, identifying infrastructure and procedural gaps. The system must maintain a MASS regulatory timeline showing expected regulatory milestones against the organization's fleet renewal and port modernization schedules, presented in both Arabic and English with Gregorian and Hijri calendar support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R013	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	FAL Convention and Single Window Regulation Tracking	The system must track IMO FAL Convention amendments and maritime single window implementation mandates affecting port call documentation and customs processes at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The FAL Convention 2024 amendments requiring electronic information exchange and the IMO Maritime Single Window concept must be monitored against each jurisdiction's implementation timeline. The system shall assess compliance of current ERP document exchange capabilities against FAL standard forms (FAL 1-7), electronic data exchange format requirements (IMO Reference Data Model), and national single window integration specifications for Qatar Customs (QCGA), UAE Trade Connect, Saudi FASAH platform, and India PCS 1x (Port Community System). Gap analysis must identify any document format, data standard, or connectivity gaps, with recommended system enhancement specifications and estimated implementation costs in USD and local currency per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R014	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Regulatory Change Notification and Distribution Engine	The system must implement a configurable notification engine that distributes IMO regulatory change alerts to relevant stakeholders based on their role, responsibility area, and jurisdictional scope. Notification rules shall be configurable by regulation category (safety, environment, security, facilitation), affected operational area (fleet, port facilities, cargo operations, customs), jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, or all), and impact severity level. Notifications must be delivered through multiple channels including in-app alerts, email digests (daily/weekly configurable), SMS for critical regulatory changes, and integration with enterprise collaboration tools. Each notification must include a concise AI-generated summary of the regulatory change, relevance to the recipient's operational area, key compliance deadlines, and a link to the full impact assessment. Notifications must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) with appropriate date formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R015	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Regulatory Compliance Evidence Collection and Archival	The system must provide a structured evidence collection framework for demonstrating compliance with IMO regulations during audits by flag state administrations, port state control inspections, classification society surveys, and internal compliance reviews. Evidence items shall include compliance certificates, implementation records, training completion records, equipment test certificates, operational procedures documented in the SMS, and management review meeting minutes. Each evidence item must be linked to the specific regulation clause it satisfies, with metadata including collection date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, collecting entity, validity period, and next review date. The system must generate compliance evidence packages on demand for specific regulations or inspection scenarios, compiled as indexed document bundles in PDF format with table of contents in Arabic and English. Evidence archival must comply with IMO-mandated retention periods and national record-keeping requirements across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R016	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Offline Regulatory Reference Library	The system must provide an offline-accessible regulatory reference library for use aboard vessels and at port facilities where internet connectivity is unreliable. The library shall cache the complete IMO regulation database relevant to container shipping operations, including convention texts, code amendments, circulars, guidelines, and the organization's compliance gap analyses and implementation status records. Users must be able to search and browse regulations by convention, topic, vessel applicability, and port jurisdiction while offline. The offline library must synchronize with the central repository upon network restoration, downloading newly published documents and updated compliance status information. The library interface must support Arabic and English with RTL layout support, and must function on standard maritime-grade tablets and laptops used by vessel crews and port facility staff at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R017	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	AI Regulatory Horizon Scanning and Forecasting	The system must employ AI-driven horizon scanning to identify emerging regulatory trends and forecast future IMO regulations that may impact container shipping operations before formal proposals are submitted to IMO committees. The AI engine shall analyze IMO working group reports, member state submissions, industry association position papers (WSC, BIMCO, ICS), academic maritime regulatory research, and regional maritime authority strategic plans from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Horizon scanning outputs must include identified emerging regulatory themes, estimated timeline to formal regulation adoption, potential operational and financial impact ranges in USD, and recommended proactive preparation measures. Forecasts must be presented with confidence levels and updated quarterly as new information becomes available. Horizon scanning reports must be generated in both Arabic and English for distribution to senior management and strategic planning teams, with Hijri calendar milestones for GCC jurisdiction-specific regulatory forecasts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-2-004-R018	FEAT-027-2-004	MOD-027	Multi-Entity Regulatory Compliance Status Consolidation	The system must provide a consolidated regulatory compliance status view across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for each applicable IMO regulation, enabling group-level compliance oversight. The consolidation dashboard shall display each entity's compliance status (compliant, partially compliant, non-compliant, not applicable) for every tracked regulation, with drill-down capability to entity-specific gap analyses, corrective action plans, and evidence packages. The AI engine must identify regulations where entities have achieved different compliance levels and recommend knowledge transfer or resource sharing between entities to accelerate compliance across the group. Financial consolidation must aggregate compliance costs and investments across entities in USD, with breakdown views in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The dashboard must support Arabic RTL and English LTR display modes with Hijri and Gregorian date selectors, and generate executive summary reports suitable for board-level regulatory compliance oversight presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R003	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Mobile Offline Survey Data Capture	The system must provide a mobile application interface that allows surveyors to capture pre-load survey data in offline mode when network connectivity is unavailable inside port terminals or warehouse facilities. The offline capability must support capturing photographs (minimum 5 megapixel resolution) with automatic GPS geotagging, recording cargo dimensions (length, width, height in both metric and imperial units), weight readings, packaging condition assessments, and free-text observations in both English and Arabic. All offline data must be stored locally on the device with AES-256 encryption and automatically synchronized to the central server when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution logic that preserves the most recent surveyor entry and flags discrepancies for supervisor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R004	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	SOLAS VGM Weight Verification Integration	The system must integrate with certified weighbridge systems at each port to capture Verified Gross Mass (VGM) readings in compliance with SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 requirements. The system must support both Method 1 (weighing the packed container) and Method 2 (weighing all packages and cargo items plus container tare weight) VGM determination methods. For each container surveyed, the system must record the weighbridge certificate number, calibration expiry date, authorized signatory details, and calculate the VGM by adding cargo weight plus dunnage plus container tare weight. If the VGM exceeds the container maximum gross weight indicated on the CSC plate, the system must block loading approval and generate an alert to the operations team with the variance percentage and recommended corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R005	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Pre-Load Survey Certificate Generation	The system must auto-generate a pre-load survey certificate upon surveyor completion and submission of all required survey data fields. The certificate must be produced in bilingual format (English and Arabic) with proper RTL text alignment for Arabic content. The certificate must include a unique sequential certificate number per entity per calendar year, survey date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, container number, seal numbers (original and surveyor-applied), cargo description, weight details, surveyor name and license number, photographic evidence thumbnails, and a QR code linking to the full digital certificate. The certificate must be generated as a PDF/A-compliant document for long-term archival and must be digitally signed using the entity's authorized certificate authority credentials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R006	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Cargo Damage Assessment and Grading	The system must provide a standardized cargo damage assessment framework where surveyors classify observed damage using predefined categories including water damage, crushing, puncture, contamination, shortage, pilferage, and temperature deviation (for reefer cargo). Each damage type must be graded on a severity scale of 1 to 5, where 1 represents superficial cosmetic damage and 5 represents total loss. The system must calculate an estimated damage value in the survey currency and convert to USD equivalent using the daily exchange rate feed. For each damage entry, the system must capture photographic evidence with mandatory annotation tools allowing surveyors to mark damaged areas on the photographs. The AI engine must analyze uploaded photographs and suggest damage categories and severity grades with a confidence score, allowing the surveyor to accept or override the AI recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R007	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Hazardous Cargo Pre-Load Survey Protocol	The system must enforce a specialized survey protocol for IMDG-classified hazardous cargo that requires additional checklist items beyond standard pre-load surveys. The protocol must verify correct UN number marking, proper placarding per IMDG Class (1 through 9), segregation compliance with the IMDG segregation table, packaging group integrity (I, II, or III), presence and legibility of the Dangerous Goods Declaration, emergency contact information availability, and compliance with the specific port authority hazardous cargo handling regulations applicable at Hamad Port (QTerminals rules), Jebel Ali (DP World regulations), Khalifa Port (ADPC guidelines), and JNPT (Mumbai Port Trust hazmat protocols). The system must prevent survey completion if any mandatory hazmat checklist item is marked as non-compliant until a deviation approval is obtained from the port safety officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R008	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Survey Scheduling Calendar and Resource Planning	The system must provide a visual calendar interface displaying all scheduled pre-load surveys across all ports and entities with filtering capabilities by port, terminal, surveyor, commodity type, and survey status. The calendar must support drag-and-drop rescheduling with automatic conflict detection that prevents double-booking a surveyor for overlapping time slots. The interface must display in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with culturally appropriate date formatting. The system must account for local public holidays including UAE National Day, Qatar National Day, Saudi National Day, Indian Republic Day, and Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha) which follow the Hijri calendar and may shift annually. When a survey is rescheduled, the system must automatically notify all stakeholders including the shipper, freight forwarder, terminal operator, and assigned surveyor via email and in-app notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R009	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Multi-Entity Survey Cost Allocation	The system must support configurable survey fee structures per entity with rates defined per survey type, commodity category, container size (20ft, 40ft, 40ft HC, 45ft), and port location. Survey costs must be captured in the local entity currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated reporting using configurable exchange rate sources (Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI reference rates). The system must support split cost allocation where survey fees can be charged to the shipper, consignee, or shipping line based on INCOTERMS rules (e.g., under FOB the seller bears pre-load survey costs, under CIF the seller also bears costs). Each survey cost entry must generate an accounting voucher linked to the appropriate entity's chart of accounts with proper inter-company elimination entries when the surveying entity differs from the billing entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R010	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Survey Photo and Document Management	The system must provide a document management subsystem for each pre-load survey that stores all photographs, videos (maximum 2 minutes per clip), scanned documents, and surveyor notes in a structured folder hierarchy organized by survey certificate number. Each uploaded document must be tagged with metadata including document type, upload timestamp, GPS coordinates (for photos taken on-site), file size, and the uploading user identity. The system must enforce minimum photo requirements per survey type (e.g., minimum 4 exterior container photos showing all four sides, minimum 2 interior photos showing cargo stowage, close-up photos of any damage observed). The storage must comply with data residency requirements where Qatar entity data remains in Qatar-based servers, KSA entity data in KSA-based servers per NDMO regulations, and UAE and India entity data in their respective regional data centers. Total storage per survey must not exceed 500 MB with automatic image compression applied to photos exceeding 10 MB.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R011	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	AI-Based Cargo Condition Anomaly Detection	The system must deploy a computer vision AI model that analyzes pre-load survey photographs in real-time to detect cargo condition anomalies including visible moisture or water stains, torn or punctured packaging, crushed cartons, leaking containers or drums, incorrect stacking patterns that may cause cargo shift during transit, and missing or damaged securing arrangements (lashing, dunnage, chocking). The AI model must achieve a minimum detection accuracy of 90% for common damage types and must flag detected anomalies with bounding boxes overlaid on the original photographs. Each AI-detected anomaly must be presented to the surveyor for confirmation or dismissal, and the surveyor's decision must be logged for model retraining purposes. The system must support model versioning and allow operations managers to compare detection accuracy across model versions to ensure continuous improvement of the anomaly detection capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R012	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Survey Workflow Approval Chain	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for pre-load survey reports. The default workflow must include surveyor submission, senior surveyor review, operations manager approval, and client notification. Each approval level must have configurable SLA timers (e.g., senior surveyor review within 2 hours, operations manager approval within 4 hours) with automatic escalation to the next management level if the SLA is breached. The workflow must support conditional routing where surveys reporting damage exceeding a configurable threshold value (default USD 5,000) are automatically routed to the claims department for parallel review. Approvers must be able to add comments, request additional photographs or information from the surveyor, or reject the survey report with mandatory rejection reasons. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all approval actions including timestamps, user identity, IP address, and device information for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R013	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Integration with Terminal Operating Systems	The system must integrate via API with terminal operating systems (TOS) at each port including Navis N4 at Jebel Ali and JNPT, and relevant TOS platforms at Hamad Port and Khalifa Port to receive real-time container movement events such as gate-in, yard placement, vessel loading, and gate-out. When a container that has a pending pre-load survey request arrives at the terminal gate, the system must automatically trigger a notification to the assigned surveyor with the container's yard location and estimated time available for survey. The integration must also push survey completion status back to the TOS so that the terminal can include the container in the vessel loading sequence only after the pre-load survey is marked as completed and approved. The API integration must support both REST and EDI COPARN/BAPLIE message formats and must handle TOS downtime gracefully with message queuing and automatic retry mechanisms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R014	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Survey KPI Dashboard and Analytics	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying key performance indicators for pre-load survey operations including average survey turnaround time (from request to certificate issuance), surveyor utilization rates, surveys completed per day per port, damage detection rates by commodity type, SLA compliance percentages for each workflow approval level, and survey cost per TEU. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate KPIs to individual survey records and must allow filtering by entity, port, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date pickers), surveyor, and commodity type. The system must generate automated weekly and monthly performance reports in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual (English/Arabic) headers and labels. All dashboard data must refresh at intervals not exceeding 5 minutes and must support export to CSV for further analysis. Historical trend charts must display data for a configurable lookback period defaulting to 12 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R015	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Regulatory Compliance and Audit Trail	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for every pre-load survey transaction that satisfy regulatory requirements across all four operating jurisdictions. For Qatar operations, audit records must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority guidelines. For UAE operations, records must satisfy Federal Tax Authority VAT audit requirements and Dubai Maritime City Authority inspection standards. For KSA operations, records must align with ZATCA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority) electronic documentation mandates and Saudi Ports Authority regulations. For India operations, records must comply with Customs Act 1962 documentation requirements and Indian Register of Shipping standards. Each audit entry must capture the action performed, timestamp in UTC and local timezone, user identity with role, originating IP address, before and after values for any data modification, and a tamper-proof hash chain linking sequential audit entries. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 7 years and must be retrievable within 30 seconds for any individual survey record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-001-R016	FEAT-028-1-001	MOD-028	Surveyor Credential and Certification Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of all surveyors with their professional credentials including surveyor license number, issuing authority, license validity period, commodity specializations (general cargo, hazardous goods, reefer, bulk, livestock), port-specific access permits and their expiry dates, professional indemnity insurance policy details and coverage limits, and training certifications (IMDG Code, CTU Code, SOLAS VGM). The system must automatically alert the operations manager 30 days before any credential expires and must prevent assignment of surveyors to survey types for which they lack valid credentials. For each port, the system must track gate pass validity and renewal requirements specific to that port authority (QTerminals at Hamad Port, DP World at Jebel Ali, ADPC at Khalifa Port, JNPT trust at Nhava Sheva). The surveyor profile must display performance metrics including average survey completion time, damage detection accuracy compared to subsequent outturn surveys, and client satisfaction ratings aggregated over the trailing 12-month period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R001	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Container Condition Survey Initiation	The system must allow container condition surveys to be initiated through multiple channels: manual creation by depot operators, automatic triggering upon container gate-in at a depot or terminal, bulk import via CSV or EDI CODECO messages, and API-triggered creation from partner systems. Each survey initiation must capture the container number (validated against ISO 6346 check digit algorithm), container type and size (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR, 20TK), owning or leasing shipping line, current location (depot or terminal code), and the survey purpose (gate-in, gate-out, on-hire, off-hire, periodic, pre-trip inspection for reefer units). The system must cross-reference the container number against the master container registry to auto-populate container specifications including tare weight, maximum gross weight, internal dimensions, and manufacturing year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R002	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	IICL 6 Standard Damage Code Mapping	The system must implement the complete IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) Repair Manual 6th Edition damage code structure as the foundation for container condition recording. Every damage entry must capture the component code (e.g., panel, rail, corner post, door, floor), location code using the IICL grid reference system (front, rear, left, right, top, bottom with positional subdivisions), damage type code (dent, hole, cut, bent, missing, broken, corroded), and dimensional measurements of the damage (length, width, depth in millimeters). The system must support combined damage entries where multiple damage types affect the same component and location. The damage code catalog must be configurable to accommodate shipping line-specific extensions to the standard IICL codes, with each extension mapped back to the base IICL code for standardized reporting. All damage code descriptions must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering for Arabic text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R003	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	AI-Powered Container Damage Detection from Photos	The system must provide an AI-based computer vision module that analyzes container survey photographs to automatically detect and classify structural damage. The AI model must be trained to identify common container defects including dents exceeding IICL threshold dimensions, holes and cuts in panels, corrosion patches, damaged or missing door gaskets, bent corner castings, cracked welds, floor damage, and roof punctures. Upon processing each photograph, the system must overlay detected damage areas with color-coded bounding boxes (red for major damage, yellow for minor damage, green for cosmetic) and generate preliminary IICL damage codes with dimensional estimates. The surveyor must review and confirm or modify each AI-generated damage entry before it is finalized. The system must track AI detection accuracy metrics per damage type and container type, achieving a target accuracy of 85% for common damage categories, and must retrain the model quarterly using confirmed surveyor annotations as ground truth data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R004	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	MNR Repair Estimate Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate repair cost estimates based on confirmed damage entries using configurable repair rate tables. Repair rates must be maintained per entity and per depot location with separate labor rates (per man-hour), material costs (per component), and overhead percentages. The rate tables must support multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion using daily exchange rates. For each damage entry, the system must determine the applicable repair method (straighten, section, weld, replace) based on damage severity and IICL guidelines, then calculate the total repair cost as the sum of labor time multiplied by labor rate plus material cost plus overhead. The system must apply shipping line-specific maximum repair cost thresholds (e.g., if total repair cost exceeds 60% of container current value, recommend condemn rather than repair). Estimates must be generated within 10 seconds of damage entry completion and must include a detailed cost breakdown exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R005	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Repair Authorization Workflow	The system must implement a configurable repair authorization workflow that routes MNR estimates to the appropriate approver based on the container owner (shipping line or leasing company), repair cost threshold, and depot location. The workflow must support multi-tier approval where repairs below a configurable threshold (e.g., USD 200) are auto-approved, repairs between USD 200 and USD 1,000 require depot manager approval, and repairs exceeding USD 1,000 require shipping line or lessor approval via an external portal or EDI message. The system must track authorization response SLAs with configurable escalation rules (e.g., if no response within 48 hours, auto-escalate to the regional equipment manager). Each authorization decision must be logged with the approver identity, timestamp, approved or rejected status, and any modifications to the original estimate such as partial approvals or rate adjustments. The system must support bulk authorization for multiple containers with similar damage profiles to improve operational efficiency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R006	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Reefer Container Pre-Trip Inspection	The system must provide a specialized survey module for refrigerated container pre-trip inspections (PTI) that captures all functional test results required before a reefer unit can be released for cargo loading. The PTI checklist must include compressor operation verification, thermostat calibration test (set-point versus actual temperature differential must be within 1 degree Celsius), defrost cycle test, refrigerant gas pressure readings (high side and low side), electrical insulation resistance test results, power cable and plug condition, controller display functionality, data logger download and verification, and door gasket seal integrity test using light penetration or vacuum methods. The system must record the reefer unit model, serial number, software version, and total running hours. Failed PTI results must automatically generate an MNR repair order for the defective components. The system must maintain a PTI history per reefer container to track recurring failures and support predictive maintenance alerts when failure frequency exceeds configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R007	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Container Grade Classification	The system must assign a condition grade to each surveyed container based on the aggregate damage assessment results. The grading system must support at minimum five grades: Grade A (food-grade, suitable for all cargo types), Grade B (cargo-worthy, minor cosmetic damage only), Grade C (cargo-worthy with restrictions, suitable for specific cargo types only), Grade D (requires repair before use), and Grade X (condemned, beyond economical repair). The grading algorithm must consider the total number of damage points, severity distribution, container age, and specific damage locations that affect structural integrity versus cosmetic appearance. The system must enforce shipping line-specific grading criteria where different lines may have different thresholds for each grade. The container grade must be displayed prominently on the survey report and must be communicated to the equipment control module to update container availability status. When a container is graded D or X, the system must automatically restrict it from booking allocation until repair completion or formal condemnation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R008	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Survey Photo Evidence Requirements by Container Component	The system must enforce mandatory photographic documentation requirements for each container survey based on container type and survey purpose. For a standard gate-in survey, the minimum photo set must include: front end with doors closed showing container number and CSC plate (1 photo), rear end with doors open showing interior (1 photo), left side full view (1 photo), right side full view (1 photo), interior floor condition (1 photo), interior roof condition (1 photo), and close-up of each identified damage area (1 photo per damage). For reefer containers, additional photos must include the machinery end showing unit data plate, controller display with set temperature, and all cable connections. Each photo must be captured through the mobile app with automatic timestamping and GPS tagging that cannot be manually overridden. The system must reject surveys submitted with fewer than the minimum required photos and must use AI to verify that submitted photos match the expected container number by reading the number from the photograph using OCR technology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R009	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Third-Party Liability Damage Attribution	The system must support damage attribution to responsible parties when container damage is identified during condition surveys. The attribution workflow must compare the current survey findings against the previous survey record for the same container to identify new damage occurring between survey points. For each new damage item, the system must allow the surveyor to attribute responsibility to one of the following parties: shipping line (pre-existing), terminal operator (handling damage), trucking company (road transport damage), depot operator (storage damage), or unknown. The system must automatically generate a damage notification to the attributed party including photographic evidence, damage description, estimated repair cost in the attributed party's preferred currency, and a response deadline. If the attributed party disputes the damage, the system must support a dispute resolution workflow with evidence submission by both parties and escalation to an independent surveyor for arbitration. All liability records must be retained and linked to subsequent invoice and recovery transactions in the financial module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R010	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Depot Survey Queue Management	The system must provide a real-time queue management interface for depot operations showing all containers awaiting condition survey, currently under survey, and survey completed pending approval. The queue must display container arrival sequence, priority level (urgent, normal, low), container type, owning line, and estimated survey duration based on historical averages for similar container types and conditions. The system must support priority override rules where specific shipping lines or container types (e.g., reefer units) are automatically elevated in the queue. The interface must display the current queue depth and estimated wait time for each priority level. The system must support multiple simultaneous survey lanes within a depot and automatically distribute containers across available lanes based on surveyor specialization and lane equipment availability. The queue display must update in real-time via WebSocket connections and must function in both English and Arabic UI modes with proper RTL layout for Arabic including right-aligned queue position numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R011	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Container CSC Plate and Safety Compliance Check	The system must validate container safety compliance during condition surveys by recording and verifying CSC (Container Safety Convention) plate information. The surveyor must capture the CSC plate data including approval number, date of manufacture, maximum operating gross mass, allowable stacking weight for 1.8g, transverse racking test force value, and the next examination due date. The system must flag containers where the CSC examination date has expired or will expire within 30 days and must prevent release for cargo loading until a valid CSC examination is recorded or renewed. For containers requiring CSC re-examination, the system must generate a work order for the ACEP (Approved Continuous Examination Programme) inspection if the container owner participates in an ACEP scheme, or schedule a periodic examination per the CSC convention requirements. The system must maintain a registry of ACEP program enrollments per shipping line and automatically apply the correct examination regime based on the container's enrollment status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R012	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Repair Completion and Quality Verification Survey	The system must support a post-repair verification survey workflow that confirms all authorized repairs have been completed to the required standard before a container is returned to serviceable inventory. The verification survey must compare each original damage entry and approved repair method against the post-repair condition, recording whether the repair meets IICL quality standards. For welded repairs, the surveyor must verify weld quality, paint coverage, and dimensional compliance. For replaced components, the surveyor must verify correct material specification and proper installation. The system must generate a repair completion certificate showing before and after photographs for each repaired damage item, total actual repair cost versus the original estimate, and the verification surveyor's sign-off. If the verification surveyor identifies substandard repairs, the system must create a rework order with specific deficiency descriptions and route it back to the repair shop. The container must remain in non-serviceable status until all rework items are cleared through a subsequent verification survey.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R013	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Container Survey History and Lifecycle Tracking	The system must maintain a complete survey history for every container in the system, creating a continuous lifecycle record from the container's first survey entry through its eventual condemnation or sale. Each survey record in the history must be immutable once finalized and must include the full damage assessment, repair records, condition grade, survey location, survey date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and surveyor identity. The lifecycle view must provide a timeline visualization showing the container's condition trajectory over time, highlighting recurring damage patterns and total maintenance expenditure. The system must calculate and display the total cost of ownership (TCO) per container including all MNR costs, with values converted to USD for cross-entity comparison. The system must support exporting the complete container survey history as a structured PDF report or JSON data file for handover to new owners or lessors when containers change ownership. Container history queries must return results within 5 seconds for containers with up to 100 historical survey records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R014	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Integration with Shipping Line Equipment Systems	The system must integrate with major shipping line equipment management systems to exchange container condition data bidirectionally. Outbound integrations must transmit survey results, damage codes, repair estimates, and repair completion notifications in standard CEDEX (Container Equipment Data Exchange) format or shipping line-specific formats as required. Inbound integrations must receive container master data updates, repair authorization responses, and rate table updates from shipping line systems. The system must maintain integration adapters for major container shipping lines operating at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT including but not limited to MSC, Maersk, CMA-CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, COSCO, and ZIM. Each integration adapter must support configurable message mapping, protocol selection (REST API, SFTP, EDI AS2), and retry logic with dead-letter queue handling for failed message deliveries. The system must log all inbound and outbound integration messages with full payload capture for troubleshooting and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R015	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	MNR Cost Analytics and Benchmarking	The system must provide comprehensive analytics for maintenance and repair costs across all entities and depot locations. The analytics module must calculate and display average repair cost per container type, average repair cost per damage category (IICL component and damage type combination), repair cost trends over time by entity and depot, cost variance analysis comparing actual repair costs against estimates, top 10 most expensive damage types by total spend, and depot-level cost benchmarking showing each depot's average MNR cost per survey compared to the network average. All monetary values must be presentable in any of the supported currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with conversion at historical transaction rates for accuracy. The system must generate automated monthly MNR performance reports per entity with executive summary sections in both English and Arabic. The analytics engine must support ad-hoc queries with configurable dimensions and measures, exportable to Excel with pivot table formatting preserved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R016	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Container Condemnation and Disposal Workflow	The system must implement a formal condemnation workflow for containers assessed as beyond economical repair (Grade X). The condemnation process must require submission of a condemnation survey report with comprehensive photographic evidence, a repair cost estimate demonstrating that repair cost exceeds the configurable percentage threshold of container current market value (default 75%), and approval from the container owner or lessor. Upon condemnation approval, the system must update the container status to condemned, remove it from all active inventory pools and booking availability, and generate a condemnation certificate with a unique reference number. The system must track condemned containers through the disposal process including sale to scrap dealers, recycling facilities, or conversion to storage units, recording the disposal method, disposal date, sale price in local currency and USD equivalent, and buyer details. The financial module must be notified to write off the remaining book value and record any disposal proceeds. The condemnation register must be reportable by entity, port, container type, and disposal method for fleet management analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-002-R017	FEAT-028-1-002	MOD-028	Survey Data Validation and Completeness Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable business rules engine that validates container condition survey data for completeness and consistency before allowing submission. Validation rules must include: container number must pass ISO 6346 check digit verification, all mandatory photo categories must be populated, at least one damage entry must exist if the container is graded below Grade A, damage dimensions must fall within physically plausible ranges for the specified container component (e.g., a dent depth on a side panel cannot exceed the panel depth of approximately 50mm for a standard container), repair cost estimate must be non-zero if any damage entries exist, and surveyor certification must be valid for the survey type being submitted. The rules engine must support entity-specific rule sets where Qatar entity may require additional customs compliance fields, KSA entity may require Hijri date population, and India entity may require GST registration number of the repair vendor. Validation failures must display clear error messages in the user's selected language (English or Arabic) with field-level highlighting indicating which specific entries require correction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R001	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Draft Survey Request and Scheduling	The system must allow users to create draft survey requests specifying the vessel name, IMO number, voyage number, berth location, port (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), survey type (initial, intermediate, final, continuous), cargo type (bulk, breakbulk, liquid bulk), and requested survey commencement time. The system must validate that the vessel is currently in port or expected within 24 hours based on AIS vessel tracking data or port call schedule integration. The AI scheduling engine must auto-assign qualified marine surveyors based on their availability, draft survey certification status, and proximity to the assigned berth. The request form must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date selection and must display times in the port's local timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India). The system must generate a pre-survey checklist specific to the vessel type and cargo that the surveyor must acknowledge before proceeding to the berth.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R002	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Vessel Particulars and Hydrostatic Data Management	The system must maintain a database of vessel particulars and hydrostatic tables for all vessels that regularly call at the four operating ports. For each vessel, the system must store the vessel name, IMO number, flag state, classification society, deadweight tonnage, lightweight, length between perpendiculars (LBP), breadth, depth, number of holds and hatches, tank arrangement diagrams, and the complete set of hydrostatic tables including displacement tables, TPC (tonnes per centimeter), MTC (moment to change trim by 1 cm), LCF (longitudinal center of flotation), and LCB (longitudinal center of buoyancy) values for each draft increment. Hydrostatic data must be importable from classification society documents in PDF format with AI-assisted table extraction and manual verification. The system must support multiple hydrostatic table versions per vessel to account for vessel modifications and must flag when the most recent hydrostatic data is older than 5 years, recommending an updated survey before relying on the existing tables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R003	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Six-Point Draft Reading Data Entry	The system must capture draft readings at six standard points on the vessel: forward port, forward starboard, midship port, midship starboard, aft port, and aft starboard. For each reading, the surveyor must record the observed draft in meters and centimeters, the water density at the reading location measured by hydrometer (in tonnes per cubic meter), sea water temperature, and the time of reading. The system must calculate the mean forward draft, mean aft draft, mean midship draft, and true mean draft corrected for hull deflection (hogging or sagging). The data entry interface must highlight any draft readings that deviate from the expected range based on the vessel's hydrostatic characteristics and current loading condition. The system must support entry of draft readings in both metric (meters) and imperial (feet and inches) units with automatic conversion, and must record whether readings were taken from the inside (dock water mark) or outside of the vessel using binoculars or other means.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R004	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Draft Survey Displacement Calculation Engine	The system must implement a mathematical calculation engine that computes the vessel's displacement from draft readings using the vessel's hydrostatic tables with the following sequential corrections: interpolation of displacement from hydrostatic tables using the corrected mean draft, first trim correction using the LCF (longitudinal center of flotation) position, second trim correction (Nemoto correction) for non-linear waterplane changes, density correction from observed dock water density to standard salt water density (1.025 t/m3), hull deflection correction for hogging (positive deflection) or sagging (negative deflection) conditions, and propeller immersion correction where applicable. Each calculation step must be shown transparently in the survey report with intermediate values displayed so that the calculation can be verified manually. The cargo quantity must be determined by subtracting the lightship displacement, ballast water, fresh water, fuel oil, diesel oil, lubricating oil, and other deductible weights from the corrected total displacement. The engine must handle edge cases including vessels with significant trim exceeding the hydrostatic table range by applying safe extrapolation methods with warning flags.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R005	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Ballast and Consumables Deduction Management	The system must capture detailed deductions for all non-cargo weights on the vessel at the time of each draft survey. Deductible items must include ballast water (per tank with sounding or ullage readings and tank calibration table references), fresh water (per tank), heavy fuel oil (per tank with temperature and density corrections), marine diesel oil (per tank), lubricating oil (per tank and sump), slop water, bilge water, crew and personal effects (standard allowance), and stores and provisions. For each liquid deduction, the system must record the sounding or ullage measurement, liquid temperature, observed density, and apply volume-to-weight conversions using tank calibration tables stored in the vessel particulars database. The system must support automatic ingestion of the vessel's sounding report if provided electronically. Where tank calibration tables are unavailable, the system must allow manual entry of volumetric estimates with a mandatory flag indicating reduced accuracy. The total deductions must be itemized in the draft survey report and any discrepancy exceeding 2% between the vessel's declared figures and surveyor's independently verified figures must be flagged for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R006	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Multi-Stage Draft Survey Tracking for Loading/Discharge	The system must support multi-stage draft survey tracking for cargo operations that span multiple tidal cycles, shifts, or days. The system must link initial survey (before commencement), intermediate surveys (during operation), and final survey (after completion) into a single draft survey case file. For each stage, the system must record the survey timestamp, draft readings, displacement calculation, cumulative cargo quantity loaded or discharged, and remaining quantity estimate. The system must calculate and display the running cargo quantity at each stage and compare it against the bill of lading quantity or shipper's declaration. For loading operations, the system must provide real-time cargo remaining estimates to assist the terminal in planning the final loading stages to achieve the target cargo quantity without exceeding the vessel's maximum permissible draft at the berth (considering tidal conditions and under-keel clearance requirements). The system must generate stage-wise comparison reports showing the cargo quantity progression and any variance trends between surveyor calculations and the vessel's declared figures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R007	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Tidal and Environmental Corrections	The system must integrate with tide prediction databases or port authority tide gauge feeds for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT to obtain accurate tidal height data at the time of each draft reading. The system must apply tidal corrections to normalize draft readings to a standard reference level (chart datum) when draft readings are taken at different times during a survey where significant tidal change has occurred (more than 10 centimeters between first and last reading). The system must also record and account for environmental factors that may affect draft accuracy including wind force and direction (which can cause heel), current effects, swell conditions, and water salinity variations in estuarine ports like JNPT where fresh water influx from the Ulhas River can significantly alter dock water density. The system must allow surveyors to record weather conditions at the time of survey and must flag surveys conducted in conditions exceeding safe survey parameters (e.g., wind force above Beaufort 6 or significant swell) with a reduced confidence notation on the survey certificate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R008	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Draft Survey Certificate Generation	The system must auto-generate a comprehensive draft survey certificate upon completion and verification of all calculations. The certificate must be produced in bilingual format (English and Arabic) with proper RTL text alignment for Arabic sections. The certificate must include: survey case reference number, vessel particulars, port and berth details, date and time of survey in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, all six draft readings with corrections, step-by-step displacement calculation showing all corrections applied, itemized deductions table, final cargo quantity determination, comparison with bill of lading or shipper's declared quantity showing any variance, surveyor's remarks and observations, and surveyor's signature with professional credentials. The certificate must include a digital signature using the entity's certificate authority and a QR code linking to the online verification portal. The system must generate the certificate in PDF/A format and must support watermarked draft versions during the review stage and finalized versions after approval. Certificate numbering must follow a sequential scheme per entity per calendar year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R009	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Cargo Quantity Variance Analysis and Dispute Support	The system must automatically calculate and analyze cargo quantity variances between the draft survey result and other quantity determination methods including bill of lading weight, shore scale weight (for terminals with conveyor belt weighing systems), vessel's cargo manifold meter readings (for liquid bulk), and shipper's declared weight. For each variance, the system must express the difference in both absolute terms (metric tonnes) and percentage terms and must classify the variance against configurable tolerance thresholds (e.g., within 0.5% is acceptable, 0.5%-1.0% requires investigation, above 1.0% triggers a formal dispute). When a variance exceeds the dispute threshold, the system must automatically generate a letter of protest template pre-populated with the survey details and variance figures, available in both English and Arabic. The system must maintain a historical variance database that tracks variance patterns by vessel, cargo type, port, and terminal to identify systematic measurement biases that may indicate calibration issues or operational problems requiring corrective action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R010	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Draft Survey Cost and Invoice Management	The system must manage the commercial aspects of draft survey services including surveyor fee calculation, cost allocation, and invoice generation. Survey fees must be configurable per entity with rate structures based on vessel size (deadweight range brackets), survey type (initial, intermediate, final), port location, and whether the survey is conducted during normal working hours or overtime periods (evenings, weekends, public holidays including Islamic holidays per Hijri calendar). The system must determine the party responsible for survey costs based on the charter party terms or commercial agreement: under voyage charter the charterer typically bears survey costs, under time charter the shipowner bears the cost. The system must generate invoices in the local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with applicable tax calculations (VAT at the entity-specific rate) and must support multi-currency billing where the principal requests invoicing in USD. Each invoice must reference the survey certificate number and must be exportable in both PDF format and structured e-invoice formats compliant with ZATCA Phase 2 requirements for KSA and FTA requirements for UAE.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R011	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Real-Time Collaboration Between Ship and Shore Surveyors	The system must support real-time collaboration features enabling shore-based senior surveyors to assist on-site surveyors during draft survey execution. The collaboration module must include secure video calling capability allowing the on-site surveyor to stream live video of draft readings from the vessel to the shore office, a shared digital workspace where both parties can simultaneously view and annotate draft reading data, chat functionality supporting both English and Arabic with automatic language detection, and the ability for the shore-based surveyor to remotely verify calculation inputs before the on-site surveyor finalizes the survey. The system must log all collaboration sessions with timestamps and participant identities for audit trail purposes. For situations where the on-site surveyor has limited connectivity (common aboard vessels in port), the system must support low-bandwidth mode that transmits only text and compressed images rather than video. The collaboration feature must comply with data residency requirements and route communications through regional servers for each entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R012	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Integration with Port Vessel Traffic Systems	The system must integrate with port vessel traffic management systems (VTMS) and port community systems (PCS) at each operating port to receive real-time vessel arrival and departure notifications, berth allocation updates, and vessel movement schedules. When a vessel with a pending draft survey request is allocated a berth, the system must automatically update the survey schedule with the confirmed berth number and expected berthing time. The system must receive AIS (Automatic Identification System) data feeds to track vessel positions and estimated arrival times. For JNPT, the system must integrate with the Indian Port Community System (PCS1x) for vessel schedule data. For Jebel Ali, integration with Dubai Trade portal must be supported. For Hamad Port, integration with QTerminals vessel management system is required. For Khalifa Port, integration with Maqta Gateway (Silsal platform) must be implemented. Each integration must handle system downtime gracefully with local caching of the last known vessel schedule and manual override capability when automated feeds are unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R013	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Bulk Cargo Specific Survey Extensions	The system must provide specialized survey extensions for different bulk cargo categories that require additional measurements beyond standard draft survey procedures. For dry bulk commodities (grain, coal, iron ore, clinker, cement), the system must support cargo hold-by-hold volumetric calculations using laser scanning or manual ullage measurements as a cross-check against the draft survey quantity. For liquid bulk (petroleum products, chemicals, LPG), the system must integrate with shore tank gauging systems and vessel manifold meter readings to provide three-way quantity reconciliation. For containerized breakbulk, the system must support tally-based quantity verification alongside the draft survey. The system must maintain commodity-specific stowage factors and cargo density tables that are used to validate the reasonableness of the draft survey quantity result against the cargo volume available in the vessel's holds. Commodity-specific density correction factors for temperature must be applied for liquid bulk surveys using ASTM (American Society for Testing and Materials) Table 54 or equivalent standard tables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R014	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Draft Survey Audit and Quality Assurance	The system must implement a quality assurance module that reviews draft survey accuracy through systematic comparison of survey results against final outturn quantities at the discharge port. The QA module must calculate the survey accuracy percentage as the ratio of draft survey quantity to actual outturn quantity and must track this metric per surveyor, per vessel class, per cargo type, and per port over time. Surveys where the accuracy falls below a configurable threshold (default 99%) must trigger an automatic review case requiring the surveyor to provide a written explanation for the variance. The system must support periodic calibration audits where a senior surveyor independently repeats a sample of draft surveys conducted by junior surveyors and the results are compared. The system must generate quarterly quality reports per entity showing survey accuracy trends, surveyor performance rankings, and identification of systematic issues. All quality records must be retained for a minimum of 5 years and must be accessible for external auditor review within 24 hours of request.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R015	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Vessel Trim Optimization Advisory	The system must provide an AI-powered advisory module that analyzes draft survey data during loading operations to recommend optimal vessel trim and cargo distribution across holds to maximize cargo intake while respecting the vessel's structural limits and port navigational constraints. The advisory must consider the vessel's maximum permissible draft at the loading berth (accounting for tidal windows and under-keel clearance requirements at the port channel), the vessel's maximum draft at the intended discharge port, hull stress limits (bending moment and shear force at each frame) from the vessel's loading computer data if available, and cargo segregation requirements for multiple cargo parcels. The system must display a visual cross-section of the vessel showing current fill levels per hold and recommended final distribution. The advisory calculations must execute within 30 seconds and must produce recommendations in both English and Arabic. The system must clearly label these as advisory outputs requiring master's approval and must include a disclaimer that the vessel's own loading computer remains the authoritative reference for structural safety limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R016	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Historical Draft Survey Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard for draft survey operations displaying historical metrics including total surveys conducted per period (daily, weekly, monthly, annually) by port and entity, average cargo quantity per vessel class, survey turnaround time distribution (from request to certificate issuance), average cargo quantity variance percentage by cargo type and port, revenue generated from draft survey services per entity in local and USD currencies, surveyor productivity metrics (surveys completed per surveyor per month), and seasonal cargo volume patterns. The dashboard must support interactive filtering by entity, port, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date selection), vessel type, cargo type, and surveyor. All charts and tables must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts with bilingual (English/Arabic) axis labels and legends. The system must support scheduled report generation and automatic email distribution to configurable recipient lists. Historical data must be available for a minimum of 10 years and dashboard queries must return results within 10 seconds for any standard filter combination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-003-R017	FEAT-028-1-003	MOD-028	Offline Draft Survey Calculation Capability	The system must provide full draft survey calculation capability in offline mode for use aboard vessels or at berth locations where internet connectivity is unavailable or unreliable. The offline module must pre-download and cache the hydrostatic tables for all vessels scheduled for survey within the next 48 hours, along with tide prediction data and the current dock water density records. The surveyor must be able to input all six draft readings, deduction figures, and environmental observations while offline and execute the complete displacement calculation locally on the mobile device. All calculation logic must produce identical results to the online server-based engine with bit-level numerical precision consistency. Offline surveys must be stored securely on the device with encryption and must synchronize to the central server when connectivity is restored, with the system detecting and flagging any data conflicts that may arise from concurrent modifications. The offline module must function on both iOS and Android tablets with a minimum screen size of 10 inches and must support operation in bright sunlight conditions common at Middle Eastern and Indian ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R001	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	On-Hire Survey Initiation and Equipment Registration	The system must support initiation of on-hire surveys when leased containers or equipment are delivered to the lessee at any depot or terminal across the four operating entities. The on-hire survey initiation must capture the lease agreement reference number, lessor name and entity, lessee name and entity, equipment type (dry container, reefer container, genset, chassis, flat-rack, open-top, tank container), equipment identifier (container number validated per ISO 6346, or serial number for non-container equipment), pick-up location (depot code or terminal code at QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), and the agreed on-hire date which may be in Gregorian or Hijri format. The system must cross-reference the equipment against the master equipment registry to verify that the equipment is currently available for hire and not already allocated to another active lease. If the equipment is flagged as requiring repair (Grade D status from condition survey), the system must block on-hire initiation and alert the equipment controller with the outstanding repair work order reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R002	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Off-Hire Survey Initiation and Return Processing	The system must support initiation of off-hire surveys when leased containers or equipment are returned by the lessee to a designated drop-off depot or terminal. The off-hire survey must be linkable to the original on-hire survey record to enable condition comparison between hire-start and hire-end states. The system must capture the return location, actual return date and time, lease agreement reference, and the reason for return (lease expiry, early return, equipment swap, lease termination). The system must calculate the actual hire duration from the on-hire date to the off-hire date in calendar days and compare it against the minimum hire period specified in the lease agreement. If the actual hire duration is less than the minimum hire period, the system must automatically flag this for billing purposes so that the minimum period charge is applied. The system must support off-hire at a different location than the original on-hire location and must calculate any repositioning charges applicable based on the lease agreement terms and the distance between the on-hire and off-hire locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R003	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Comparative Condition Assessment Between On-Hire and Off-Hire	The system must provide a side-by-side comparison interface displaying the equipment condition at on-hire versus off-hire to identify damage or deterioration that occurred during the hire period. The comparison must align damage entries by component and location using IICL grid references, highlighting new damage items that appear in the off-hire survey but not in the on-hire survey, worsened damage items where existing damage has increased in severity or dimensions, repaired items where damage present at on-hire has been fixed during the hire period, and unchanged items where pre-existing damage remains in the same condition. For each new or worsened damage item, the system must calculate the estimated repair cost using the applicable MNR rate table and attribute the cost to the lessee as a damage liability. The comparison interface must display photographs from both surveys side by side for each component, with overlay capability to visually highlight changes. The comparison must be viewable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL layout, and must generate a printable PDF comparison report that can be shared with both lessor and lessee for agreement or dispute.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R004	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	On-Hire/Off-Hire Certificate Generation	The system must generate formal on-hire and off-hire certificates as legally binding documents recording the equipment condition at the point of handover. The certificate must include the certificate type (on-hire or off-hire), unique sequential certificate number per entity per year, lease agreement reference, equipment identification details, survey date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, survey location with depot or terminal name and address, complete condition assessment with all damage entries coded per IICL standards, photographic evidence gallery with thumbnail index, condition grade assignment, surveyor name and professional credentials, and digital signature fields for surveyor, lessor representative, and lessee representative. The certificate must be generated in bilingual format (English and Arabic) with proper RTL alignment for Arabic text sections. The system must support electronic acknowledgment workflow where both lessor and lessee can digitally sign the certificate through a secure web portal. For cases where one party refuses to sign, the system must record the refusal reason and escalate to the dispute resolution workflow while still preserving the survey data as evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R005	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Hire Period Charge Calculation Engine	The system must calculate hire period charges based on configurable rate structures defined in each lease agreement. The calculation engine must support multiple rate models including fixed daily rate per equipment unit, tiered daily rates that decrease with longer hire durations (e.g., first 30 days at rate A, 31-90 days at rate B, 91+ days at rate C), and seasonal rate variations. Rates must be stored in the lease agreement's base currency with support for all operating currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must calculate the total hire charge by multiplying the applicable daily rate by the number of hire days, adding any applicable surcharges (drop-off location surcharge, repositioning charge, late return penalty), and subtracting any agreed credits or discounts. The calculation must prorate partial days at the start and end of the hire period according to the lease agreement terms (full day, half day, or hourly proration). The system must recalculate charges whenever the off-hire date is amended and must maintain a charge revision history showing each calculation version with the reason for revision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R006	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Damage Liability Cost Recovery Processing	The system must manage the process of recovering damage repair costs from lessees for damage identified during off-hire surveys that was not present at on-hire. The liability calculation must use the repair estimate generated from the comparative condition assessment and must apply the damage liability terms specified in the lease agreement, which may include deductibles, damage caps per container, aggregate annual damage caps, and exclusions for fair wear and tear. The system must generate a damage liability invoice in the agreed billing currency with full supporting documentation including the off-hire survey report, comparative condition assessment, repair cost estimates, and photographic evidence. The invoice must comply with local tax requirements including VAT calculations at the applicable entity rate (5% for Qatar, 5% for UAE, 15% for KSA, 18% GST for India). The system must track invoice status through issuance, acknowledgment, dispute, partial payment, and full settlement stages. For disputed damage items, the system must support a line-item dispute workflow where the lessee can accept some damage charges while disputing others.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R007	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Lease Agreement Terms and Conditions Repository	The system must maintain a comprehensive repository of lease agreement terms and conditions that govern the on-hire and off-hire survey requirements and commercial terms for each lessor-lessee relationship. Each agreement record must store the agreement reference number, lessor and lessee entity details, effective date range, equipment types and quantities covered, agreed depot locations for on-hire and off-hire, daily hire rates per equipment type and size (20ft, 40ft, 40HC, 45ft), currency of the agreement, minimum hire period, maximum hire period, damage liability terms (deductible amounts, fair wear and tear definitions, damage caps), insurance requirements and coverage limits, cleaning and washing requirements at off-hire, and applicable law and dispute resolution jurisdiction. The system must support versioning of agreement terms where amendments are tracked with effective dates, and the system applies the correct version of terms based on the survey date. Agreement documents must be uploadable in PDF format and linked to the structured terms data for reference. The repository must be searchable by lessor, lessee, equipment type, and port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R008	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Bulk On-Hire and Off-Hire Processing	The system must support bulk processing of on-hire and off-hire surveys for scenarios where large numbers of containers are simultaneously handed over between lessor and lessee. Bulk on-hire must allow import of a container list via CSV file or EDI message containing container numbers, types, and sizes for batch on-hire survey creation. The system must validate all container numbers against the master registry and flag any containers that fail ISO 6346 check digit validation, are already on active hire, or have outstanding repair requirements. Bulk off-hire must support batch return processing where containers are scanned at the depot gate using barcode or OCR technology and automatically matched to their active on-hire records. The system must generate batch-level summary reports showing total containers processed, condition grade distribution, total damage liability value in aggregate and per container, and average hire duration. Bulk processing must handle up to 500 containers per batch without performance degradation and must complete validation within 60 seconds for the full batch. Individual containers within a bulk batch must still maintain complete individual survey records accessible for detailed review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R009	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Intermodal Equipment On-Hire/Off-Hire for Non-Container Assets	The system must extend on-hire and off-hire survey management beyond standard containers to cover intermodal equipment including chassis (skeletal trailers, flatbed trailers, extendable trailers), gensets (generator sets for powering reefer containers during inland transport), container handling equipment, and special equipment such as flexi-tanks, bulk liners, and tank containers. For each non-container equipment type, the system must support equipment-specific survey checklists that capture the relevant condition parameters: for chassis this includes tire tread depth and condition, brake system inspection, lighting and reflector status, twist lock functionality, and chassis frame structural integrity; for gensets this includes engine hours, fuel tank level, electrical output test, noise level measurement, and emission compliance. The system must maintain equipment-specific rate cards and depreciation schedules for damage liability calculation. Survey forms must be dynamically generated based on the equipment type selected, displaying only relevant fields and checklist items. All non-container equipment survey capabilities must support the same multi-entity, multi-currency, bilingual, and offline functionality as standard container surveys.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R010	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Fair Wear and Tear Guidelines Engine	The system must implement a configurable fair wear and tear (FWT) assessment engine that automatically distinguishes between normal equipment deterioration acceptable under lease terms and damage that constitutes chargeable lessee liability. The FWT engine must evaluate each off-hire damage entry against configurable criteria including equipment age (years since manufacture), total hire days accumulated, container type and usage category (e.g., SOC containers used in harsh environments may have higher FWT allowances), geographic operating region (Middle East environments have higher corrosion and UV degradation rates), and the specific IICL damage type and component. The engine must apply lessor-specific FWT guidelines where major container lessors such as Triton, Beacon, Florens, Textainer, and Seaco each define their own FWT standards. When the FWT engine classifies a damage item as fair wear and tear, it must be automatically excluded from the damage liability invoice with a coded reason. The lessee must be able to invoke the FWT engine to challenge damage charges they believe should be classified as FWT, triggering a review by the lessor's equipment inspector.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R004	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Deficiency Tracking and Rectification Monitoring	The system must maintain a comprehensive deficiency register for each inspected hold and hatch cover, categorizing findings by severity levels including critical, major, minor, and observation. Each deficiency record shall include a unique tracking identifier, photographic evidence with annotations, location coordinates within the vessel general arrangement plan, and estimated rectification timeframe. Workflow automation must route critical deficiencies to vessel master, P&I correspondents, and charterer representatives simultaneously. Rectification verification inspections shall be triggered automatically upon notification of completion. The system must support offline deficiency recording for inspections conducted in areas without network connectivity at remote berths, synchronizing data when connection is restored. Currency for any cost estimates must default to the entity operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R011	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Container Cleaning and Washing Assessment at Off-Hire	The system must include a cleaning assessment component in the off-hire survey that evaluates the container interior cleanliness against the lease agreement's return condition standards. The cleaning assessment must categorize the container interior condition as: clean and ready for use (no cleaning required), light cleaning required (sweeping of minor debris or dust), heavy cleaning required (washing to remove cargo residue, stains, or odors), and chemical cleaning required (specialized treatment for contamination from hazardous or strongly odorous cargo). For each cleaning category, the system must maintain configurable cost rates per entity and per depot location in local currency. The cleaning cost must be added to the lessee's damage liability invoice unless the lease agreement includes a cleaning allowance or the container was returned within the agreed acceptable cleanliness standard. For reefer containers, the cleaning assessment must additionally evaluate the interior for biological contamination, assess the condition of the T-bar floor gratings, and verify that no cargo residue has accumulated on the evaporator coils. The system must support photographic evidence of interior condition with AI analysis to automatically suggest the applicable cleaning category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R012	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	On-Hire/Off-Hire Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must implement a structured dispute resolution workflow for handling disagreements between lessors and lessees regarding equipment condition assessments, damage attribution, repair cost estimates, or hire period charges arising from on-hire and off-hire surveys. The dispute workflow must support the following stages: dispute initiation (either party can raise a dispute with supporting evidence), evidence exchange (both parties upload relevant documents, photographs, and statements), independent surveyor appointment (system selects a mutually acceptable third-party surveyor from a pre-approved panel for independent assessment), independent survey execution (third-party surveyor conducts a fresh inspection and submits findings), determination (system presents all three survey reports for comparison and facilitates negotiation), and settlement (final agreed amounts are recorded and invoiced or credited). The system must track dispute aging and enforce configurable escalation timelines (e.g., if no resolution within 30 days, escalate to senior management; within 60 days, escalate to legal). All dispute communications and documents must be maintained in a tamper-proof audit trail. The dispute workflow must support bilingual communication threads in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R013	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Lessor Portal for External Survey Access	The system must provide a secure external web portal through which lessors can access on-hire and off-hire survey data for their equipment without requiring direct access to the main ERP system. The portal must support SSO (Single Sign-On) integration with major lessor identity providers and must enforce role-based access where lessor users can only view surveys related to their own equipment. Portal features must include: viewing on-hire and off-hire survey certificates and photographs, reviewing comparative condition assessments, approving or disputing damage liability charges, downloading survey reports in PDF and Excel formats, accessing equipment-level survey history, and viewing aggregate fleet analytics for their containers across all four operating entities. The portal must support bilingual (English and Arabic) interface with user-selectable language preference. The portal must be responsive and functional on desktop, tablet, and mobile devices. Data displayed on the portal must be consistent with the internal system data with a maximum synchronization delay of 15 minutes. The portal must comply with the data residency requirements of each entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R014	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) Integration	The system must integrate with equipment interchange receipt (EIR) processes at depot and terminal gates to link gate-level container condition assessments with the formal on-hire and off-hire survey records. When a container passes through a depot gate for on-hire pick-up, the system must verify that a valid on-hire survey has been completed and approved before allowing the gate-out transaction. When a container arrives at a depot gate for off-hire return, the system must automatically initiate the off-hire survey workflow and generate a preliminary EIR from the gate camera system's automated damage detection (if available) or from the gate clerk's quick visual inspection. The EIR data must be mapped to IICL damage codes for consistency with formal surveys. The system must capture EIR data including container number (validated by OCR from gate camera images), seal numbers and condition, visible external damage notes, and gate transaction timestamp. For terminals using automated gate systems at Jebel Ali and JNPT, the system must receive container image sets from the gate camera system and feed them to the AI damage detection module for pre-screening before the formal surveyor inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R015	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Fleet Utilization and Hire Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive analytics for leased equipment fleet utilization and hire performance. The analytics must include: fleet composition breakdown by equipment type and size per entity, equipment utilization rate (percentage of fleet on active hire versus idle in depot), average hire duration by equipment type and lessee, revenue per container day (hire income plus damage recovery divided by total available container days) in local and USD currencies, idle container aging showing the duration each off-hire container has been waiting in depot for the next on-hire, top 10 lessees by hire volume and revenue contribution, damage frequency analysis showing average number of off-hire damage items per container per hire cycle, and net yield analysis showing hire revenue minus MNR costs per container per annum. The analytics must support segmentation by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, lessor, lessee, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri date support. Dashboards must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic users. The system must support scheduled distribution of fleet utilization reports to lessor management in automated email format with attached PDF reports containing bilingual content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R005	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Hold Condition Survey Photography Management	The system must provide a structured photography protocol for hold inspections requiring minimum photograph counts per hold area including forward bulkhead, aft bulkhead, port side shell, starboard side shell, tank top, hatch coaming, and under-deck areas. Each photograph must be automatically tagged with vessel IMO number, hold number, inspection date in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, surveyor identifier, and GPS coordinates. AI-powered image classification must automatically categorize photos by deficiency type such as rust, coating failure, contamination, or structural damage. The photography module must support offline capture with batch upload capability when connectivity is restored. Image compression must balance quality retention with storage optimization, maintaining minimum 2MP effective resolution for evidentiary purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R016	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Multi-Currency Hire Settlement and Reconciliation	The system must manage the financial settlement process for hire charges and damage liabilities across multiple currencies and entities. The settlement engine must support scenarios where the lease agreement is denominated in USD but the invoicing entity operates in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), requiring currency conversion at the settlement date exchange rate. The system must generate periodic hire charge invoices (weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly per agreement terms) consolidating all active hires under a single lease agreement into one invoice with line-item detail per container. Damage liability charges from off-hire surveys must be invoiced separately or consolidated with hire charges based on the agreement configuration. The system must support netting of charges where damage liabilities can be offset against overpayments on hire charges within the same account. The reconciliation module must match incoming payments against outstanding invoices and generate aged receivables reports per lessor showing current, 30-day, 60-day, 90-day, and 120-day outstanding balances. All currency conversions must use the rate source specified in the lease agreement (e.g., Reuters, Bloomberg, Central Bank) and must record the rate used for each conversion for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R017	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Insurance Claim Integration for Hire Period Damage	The system must support integration with insurance claim processes when significant damage identified during off-hire surveys qualifies for insurance recovery. The system must automatically flag off-hire damage entries where the estimated repair cost exceeds the insurance claim threshold defined in the lease agreement or internal policy (configurable per entity, default USD 2,000). For flagged items, the system must generate an insurance claim preparation package containing the complete off-hire survey report, comparative condition assessment showing the damage was not present at on-hire, photographic evidence organized chronologically, repair cost estimate with detailed breakdown, and relevant lease agreement terms regarding insurance coverage and liability allocation. The system must track the insurance claim lifecycle from preparation through submission, insurer acknowledgment, assessment, approval or denial, and settlement payment receipt. When insurance proceeds are received, the system must automatically credit the corresponding damage liability receivable and adjust the lessee's outstanding balance accordingly. The system must maintain an insurance claim register per entity showing all active claims, their status, claimed amounts, and settlement amounts in both local currency and USD for cross-entity consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-1-004-R018	FEAT-028-1-004	MOD-028	Automated Hire Period Expiry Notifications and Renewals	The system must implement an automated notification engine that monitors all active hire periods and generates proactive alerts as hire agreements approach their expiry dates. The notification schedule must be configurable per lease agreement with default milestones at 90 days, 60 days, 30 days, 14 days, and 7 days before the hire expiry date. Each notification must be sent to configurable recipient lists including the lessee contact, lessor account manager, and internal equipment controller via email and in-app notification, with message content available in both English and Arabic based on recipient language preference. The system must support hire period extension requests initiated by the lessee through the external portal, capturing the requested extension duration, any revised rate proposals, and business justification. Extension requests must route through a configurable approval workflow (equipment controller review, commercial manager approval, lessor confirmation) with SLA timers for each approval stage. Upon extension approval, the system must automatically update the hire end date, recalculate projected hire charges at the agreed extension rate (which may differ from the original rate), and generate an addendum to the lease agreement. If no extension is processed and the hire period expires, the system must generate overdue return alerts with escalating urgency levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R001	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Hatch Cover Weathertightness Inspection Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for hatch cover weathertightness inspections including ultrasonic testing, chalk testing, and hose testing methods. Each inspection type shall have configurable pass/fail criteria aligned with IMO MSC.1/Circ.1178 and IACS Rec.14 standards. The workflow must capture compression bar condition, rubber gasket wear measurements, cleating mechanism functionality, and cross-joint drainage channel status. Inspectors at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), and JNPT (INNHV) must be able to record findings with geo-tagged photographs. The system shall automatically flag deficiencies that require rectification before cargo loading is authorized, supporting both Arabic and English reporting formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R002	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Cargo Hold Cleanliness Grading System	The system must implement a multi-grade cargo hold cleanliness assessment framework supporting grain clean, hospital clean, and normal clean standards as defined by FOSFA, GAFTA, and NIBA inspection protocols. Each grade shall have a detailed checklist covering residue from previous cargo, paint condition, bilge well status, sounding pipe caps, ventilation duct covers, and tank top coating integrity. AI-assisted image analysis shall provide preliminary cleanliness scoring based on surveyor photographs, with confidence levels displayed in both Arabic and English interfaces. The grading outcome must integrate with voyage planning modules to validate hold suitability for nominated next cargo. Results shall be stored with Hijri and Gregorian date timestamps for regional compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R003	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Hold Inspection Scheduling and Surveyor Assignment	The system must enable automated scheduling of hold inspections based on vessel ETA notifications received from port community systems at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Scheduling logic shall consider surveyor availability, certification scope, travel time between berths, and vessel priority classification. AI recommendation engine must suggest optimal surveyor assignments based on historical performance metrics, specialization in specific cargo types such as grain, coal, or chemicals, and language proficiency in Arabic, English, or Hindi. Conflict detection must prevent double-booking surveyors and alert operations when inspection windows overlap with tidal or berth allocation constraints. Multi-entity operations must respect entity-specific surveyor pools while allowing cross-entity resource sharing when explicitly authorized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R006	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Pre-Loading Inspection Certificate Generation	The system must generate pre-loading inspection certificates upon successful completion of all hold and hatch inspection requirements. Certificate templates must comply with BIMCO standard forms and be customizable per charterer requirements. Certificates shall include vessel particulars, hold dimensions, inspection findings summary, cleanliness grade achieved, hatch cover test results, and surveyor credentials. The system must support digital signatures with PKI-based authentication for authorized surveyors. Certificates must be generated in bilingual Arabic-English format with proper RTL text rendering for Arabic content. QR codes embedded in certificates shall link to the full digital inspection record. Automatic distribution to vessel master, charterer, shipper, and port authority must be configurable per entity. Certificates must reference the applicable port code (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, or INNHV) and include entity-specific letterhead.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R007	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Historical Hold Condition Trend Analysis	The system must maintain historical records of all hold inspections per vessel, enabling trend analysis of hold condition deterioration over time. AI-driven analytics shall identify patterns such as recurring deficiencies in specific holds, coating degradation rates, and correlation between cargo types and hold damage. Dashboard visualizations must present condition trends with configurable time ranges in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar views. Predictive models shall estimate when holds will require major maintenance based on inspection history, supporting fleet maintenance planning. The analytics engine must aggregate data across all four operating entities while respecting data segregation requirements. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with Arabic and English language options and proper RTL formatting for Arabic output.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R008	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Bunker Tank and Void Space Inspection Integration	The system must extend inspection capabilities to include bunker tanks, ballast tanks, void spaces, and chain lockers when required by survey scope. Each space type shall have dedicated checklists covering structural integrity, coating condition, cathodic protection status, and cleanliness standards. Integration with vessel stability systems must ensure that tank inspection scheduling does not conflict with ballasting operations or cargo loading sequences. The inspection module must support confined space entry permits with gas-free certificate verification and safety equipment checklists. Multi-entity compliance rules must accommodate varying port state control requirements across Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority, and Indian DG Shipping regulations. Offline inspection capability must be available for below-deck spaces where connectivity is unreliable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R009	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Hatch Cover Maintenance History and Spare Parts Tracking	The system must track hatch cover maintenance history including gasket replacements, compression bar adjustments, hydraulic system repairs, and cleating mechanism overhauls. Integration with vessel technical management systems shall synchronize maintenance records and spare parts inventory. The system must alert operations teams when hatch cover maintenance is overdue based on manufacturer recommended intervals or when inspection findings indicate deteriorating performance. Spare parts requisition workflows shall be triggered automatically when critical components fall below minimum stock thresholds. Cost tracking must support multi-currency operations with automatic conversion between USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR using daily exchange rates. Procurement workflows must route through entity-specific approval hierarchies defined by the multi-entity organizational structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R010	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Third-Party Surveyor Portal Access Management	The system must provide a secure web portal for third-party surveyors to access assigned inspection tasks, submit findings, and upload supporting documentation. Portal access shall be governed by role-based permissions restricting visibility to only assigned vessels and inspections. Multi-factor authentication must be enforced for all external portal users. Surveyors must be able to submit inspection reports through the portal in either Arabic or English with automatic translation assistance powered by AI. The portal must function on mobile devices and tablets commonly used in field conditions, with responsive design supporting both LTR and RTL layouts. Session management must include automatic timeout after configurable inactivity periods. Portal activity logging must capture all access events for audit trail compliance across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R011	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Vessel General Arrangement Plan Integration	The system must integrate vessel general arrangement plans as interactive reference documents during hold inspections. Surveyors shall be able to tap on specific holds, tanks, or spaces on the GA plan to initiate or resume inspection records. Deficiency locations must be plottable directly on the GA plan with customizable markers indicating deficiency type and severity. The system must support standard CAD file formats including DXF and DWG for GA plan import, as well as PDF overlays for vessels where CAD files are unavailable. Multiple vessel types must be accommodated including bulk carriers, container vessels, general cargo ships, and multi-purpose vessels operating across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. GA plans must be cached locally on surveyor devices for offline accessibility during inspections conducted in areas without network coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R012	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Automated Inspection Compliance Validation	The system must automatically validate that all mandatory inspection steps have been completed before allowing certificate generation or loading authorization. Compliance rules shall be configurable per cargo type, charterer requirements, and applicable trade-specific regulations such as Australian AMSA hold inspection standards for grain exports. The validation engine must check for minimum photograph counts, mandatory checklist completions, required measurement recordings, and surveyor qualification verification. Non-compliance shall trigger escalation notifications to operations managers with specific details of outstanding requirements. Override capabilities must be restricted to authorized senior personnel with mandatory reason documentation. Validation rules must be maintainable through a configuration interface accessible to quality assurance managers without requiring system developer intervention. Multi-entity operations must support entity-specific compliance rule sets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R013	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Integration with Port State Control Databases	The system must interface with port state control inspection databases including Tokyo MOU, Paris MOU, Indian Ocean MOU, and Riyadh MOU to retrieve vessel detention history and targeted inspection records. Vessels with recent PSC deficiencies related to hatch covers or cargo holds shall be automatically flagged for enhanced inspection protocols. The integration must pull data using standard THETIS-MR and APCIS API formats where available. Risk scoring algorithms shall combine PSC history with internal inspection records to generate comprehensive vessel risk profiles. High-risk vessels must trigger automatic notifications to port facility security officers and operations management. Data retrieval must comply with data privacy regulations across all four operating jurisdictions including Qatar, UAE, Saudi Arabia, and India. The system must cache PSC data locally to support offline risk assessment during inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R014	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Inspection Cost Allocation and Invoicing	The system must track all costs associated with hold and hatch inspections including surveyor fees, travel expenses, equipment rental, and laboratory testing charges. Cost allocation rules shall distribute expenses to the appropriate cost center based on inspection type, requesting party, and charter party terms. Multi-currency invoicing must support USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with automatic exchange rate application at the date of service. Integration with the financial accounting module shall generate accounts receivable entries for recoverable inspection costs and accounts payable entries for third-party surveyor invoices. VAT and GST calculations must apply entity-specific tax rules including Qatar zero-rate VAT, UAE 5% VAT, KSA 15% VAT, and India 18% GST on survey services. Invoice templates must support bilingual Arabic-English formatting with entity-specific branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R015	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	AI-Powered Inspection Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI algorithms to analyze inspection data patterns and detect anomalies that may indicate inspection quality issues or vessel condition concerns. Machine learning models shall be trained on historical inspection datasets across all four operating ports to identify statistically significant deviations in deficiency rates, inspection durations, and cleanliness grading distributions per surveyor. Anomaly alerts must be routed to quality assurance managers with supporting data visualizations showing the deviation from expected norms. The AI engine must also detect potential fraudulent inspection practices such as photograph reuse across different vessels or impossibly short inspection durations relative to vessel size. Model performance metrics including precision, recall, and false positive rates must be tracked and reported monthly. All AI recommendations must include confidence scores and explainability narratives in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-001-R016	FEAT-028-2-001	MOD-028	Multi-Entity Inspection Standards Harmonization	The system must support harmonized inspection standards across all four operating entities while accommodating entity-specific regulatory requirements. A master inspection standard library shall define the baseline requirements applicable across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Entity-specific overrides must be configurable to add or modify checklist items based on local port authority requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Version control must track all changes to inspection standards with approval workflows requiring quality manager sign-off. The system must generate compliance gap reports when standards are updated, identifying inspections that were conducted under previous versions. Standard synchronization across entities must be managed through a central governance dashboard accessible to regional quality directors. Arabic and English versions of all standards must be maintained in parallel with translation verification workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R001	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Inspection Workflow and Checklist Management	The system must provide a comprehensive Pre-Trip Inspection workflow for refrigerated containers covering all major reefer unit manufacturers including Carrier, Daikin, Thermo King, and Star Cool. PTI checklists shall include compressor operation verification, condenser and evaporator coil condition, refrigerant charge levels, electrical connection integrity, controller software version validation, and defrost cycle testing. Each checklist item must have configurable pass/fail criteria with measurement value recording where applicable such as supply air temperature, return air temperature, and discharge pressure. The workflow must enforce sequential completion of safety-critical items before allowing progression to operational testing phases. Container type codes (22R1, 42R1, 45R1, 45R8 for high-cube reefers) must be validated against inspection checklists to ensure appropriate testing protocols are applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R002	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Scheduling and Capacity Planning	The system must enable automated PTI scheduling based on container booking data, vessel cut-off times, and depot capacity constraints. Scheduling algorithms shall optimize PTI slot allocation considering available power plug points at each facility, technician availability by shift, and estimated inspection duration per container unit type. AI-driven demand forecasting shall predict PTI volumes by analyzing historical booking patterns, seasonal cargo trends for perishables from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and shipping line release schedules. The scheduling engine must handle peak season capacity constraints where reefer PTI demand at JNPT and Jebel Ali can exceed normal capacity by 200-300 percent. Multi-entity coordination must enable PTI load balancing between Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali facilities when capacity constraints arise. Calendar displays must support both Hijri and Gregorian date formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R003	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Reefer Technician Qualification and Assignment	The system must maintain a registry of qualified reefer technicians with certifications, specialization areas, and authorization levels for each reefer unit manufacturer. Assignment algorithms shall match technician qualifications to the specific reefer unit model requiring inspection, preventing assignment of technicians to units outside their certification scope. Certification expiry tracking must generate renewal reminders 60, 30, and 15 days before expiration, with automatic de-authorization upon expiry. Workload balancing must distribute PTI assignments equitably among available technicians while considering skill levels for complex units such as controlled atmosphere reefers. Multi-entity operations must maintain separate technician pools per operating entity while supporting temporary cross-entity assignments during peak periods. Technician profiles must be maintained in both Arabic and English with proper RTL text handling for Arabic name fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R004	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Temperature Recorder Verification and Calibration Tracking	The system must manage temperature recording device verification as part of the PTI process, covering both integrated data loggers and standalone devices such as Ryan and Sensitech units. Calibration status of each recording device must be verified against equipment calibration databases before PTI approval. The system shall record temperature set-point programming, operating mode selection including chilled, frozen, banana, and controlled atmosphere modes, and ventilation setting configuration. Deviation alarm thresholds must be documented and verified against booking requirements and shipper instructions. Historical calibration certificates shall be linked to individual PTI records for traceability. The system must flag containers where temperature recorder firmware requires updating and integrate with manufacturer portals for firmware version validation. All temperature values must be recorded in Celsius with Fahrenheit conversion available for reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R005	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Deficiency Repair Workflow	The system must manage the complete repair workflow for reefer containers that fail PTI inspection, from deficiency identification through repair completion to re-inspection. Repair categorization shall distinguish between mechanical failures, electrical faults, refrigerant leaks, structural damage, and controller malfunctions. Automated spare parts availability checks must query depot inventory systems before generating repair work orders. Repair cost estimation shall use entity-specific labor rates and parts pricing in the local operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with automatic conversion to USD for shipping line billing. Repair authorization workflows must route to container owners or shipping line representatives for approval when estimated costs exceed configurable thresholds. Re-inspection scheduling must be automatically triggered upon repair completion notification, with priority allocation to minimize container dwell time at the depot.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R006	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Controlled Atmosphere Container PTI Procedures	The system must support specialized PTI procedures for controlled atmosphere (CA) reefer containers including Carrier NaturaFresh, Daikin Active CA, and Star Cool CA systems. CA-specific inspection items shall include oxygen and carbon dioxide sensor calibration verification, nitrogen generator performance testing, membrane integrity assessment, and atmosphere composition stability monitoring over minimum two-hour test periods. The system must record baseline gas composition readings and verify that the CA system can achieve and maintain target atmosphere conditions specified in the booking requirements. Integration with shipper commodity databases shall provide recommended CA settings for specific produce types such as mangoes from India, dates from KSA, and avocados transiting through Jebel Ali. CA container PTI records must include extended data fields not present in standard reefer PTI checklists. Technician CA-specific certification must be validated before assignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R007	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Certificate and Release Documentation	The system must generate PTI certificates upon successful completion of all inspection requirements, including container number, reefer unit serial number, inspection date in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, set-point temperature, operating mode, and technician details. Certificates shall comply with shipping line-specific templates configurable per carrier. Digital PTI release must integrate with terminal operating systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to update container status from PTI-pending to PTI-passed, enabling gate-out or vessel loading operations. The system must support bulk certificate generation for multiple containers inspected in the same session. QR codes on certificates shall enable rapid verification by terminal operators and customs officials. Bilingual Arabic-English certificate formatting must handle RTL text alignment correctly. Electronic distribution to booking parties must be automated upon certificate generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R008	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Reefer Monitoring Integration During PTI	The system must integrate with remote container monitoring platforms including Carrier LYNX, Maersk Captain Peter, and MSC EYES to retrieve real-time reefer unit telemetry during PTI operations. Telemetry data shall include supply air temperature, return air temperature, ambient temperature, compressor suction and discharge pressures, and power consumption. Automated comparison between telemetry readings and manual inspection measurements must flag discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerance thresholds. The integration must support API-based data retrieval from monitoring platforms and direct Bluetooth connectivity to reefer controller units for on-site diagnostics. Historical telemetry data for the preceding 30 days shall be retrievable to assess unit performance trends prior to PTI. Offline mode must cache the most recent telemetry snapshot for use during inspections in depot areas without network connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R009	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Shipping Line PTI Standard Configuration	The system must maintain shipping line-specific PTI standards and requirements configurable per carrier, including inspection scope variations, reporting formats, deficiency classification thresholds, and cost recovery rates. Major shipping lines operating at the four ports including MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, OOCL, Evergreen, ONE, and ZIM shall have pre-configured PTI templates that can be customized by operations managers. Standard updates received from shipping lines must be version-controlled with effective date tracking to ensure inspections apply the correct standards based on inspection date. The configuration interface must support Arabic and English with entity-specific default settings. Shipping line approval matrices for repair authorizations must be configurable with escalation thresholds defined in USD with automatic conversion to local entity currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) based on prevailing exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R010	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Performance Analytics and KPI Dashboard	The system must provide comprehensive analytics dashboards tracking PTI operational performance across all four operating entities. Key performance indicators shall include PTI pass rate by depot location, average inspection duration, first-time pass rate, repair turnaround time, and technician productivity measured in units inspected per shift. Trend analysis must identify deteriorating performance patterns and correlate them with potential root causes such as seasonal demand spikes, technician shortages, or equipment age distributions. Comparative analytics across entities must benchmark Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT operations against each other and against industry standards. AI-generated insights shall proactively highlight improvement opportunities and predict capacity constraints before they impact operations. Dashboard displays must support Arabic and English language switching with RTL layout adjustment. Data export must be available in Excel and PDF formats with entity-specific branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R011	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Power Plug Point Monitoring and Allocation	The system must track power plug point availability and allocation at reefer PTI yards across all operating depots. Real-time visibility of plug point occupancy shall enable efficient scheduling by avoiding allocation conflicts where containers cannot be connected for testing. Integration with yard management systems must provide automated plug point assignment based on container location, electrical phase requirements for different reefer unit types, and estimated PTI duration. Power consumption monitoring shall detect abnormal draw patterns that may indicate reefer unit malfunctions during testing. The system must track plug point maintenance schedules and automatically exclude offline points from allocation pools. Capacity planning tools must model plug point utilization against forecast PTI volumes to identify infrastructure investment needs. Multi-entity reporting must aggregate plug point utilization data while maintaining entity-level granularity for operational management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R012	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Reefer Spare Parts Inventory Integration	The system must integrate with spare parts inventory management systems to verify component availability before authorizing PTI repairs. Parts catalogs shall be organized by reefer unit manufacturer, model, and component category including compressors, fans, controllers, sensors, gaskets, and refrigerant. Automated reorder triggers must generate purchase requisitions when critical spare parts fall below safety stock levels. Parts consumption tracking per PTI repair must update inventory in real-time and generate usage analytics for procurement planning. Multi-entity inventory visibility shall enable cross-depot parts transfers when local stock is depleted and the part is available at another entity location. Parts pricing must be maintained in both local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD for shipping line cost recovery calculations. Vendor lead time tracking shall inform repair timeline estimates provided to container owners and shipping lines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R013	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Environmental Compliance for Refrigerant Handling	The system must track refrigerant handling activities during PTI operations in compliance with Montreal Protocol obligations and local environmental regulations applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Refrigerant type identification must be recorded for each reefer unit using ISO 817 nomenclature including R-134a, R-404A, R-452A, and R-513A. Refrigerant recovery, recycling, and reclamation quantities shall be logged per technician with equipment calibration verification. The system must flag units containing phased-down HFC refrigerants requiring special handling procedures. Environmental compliance reports must be generated per entity jurisdiction showing total refrigerant consumption, recovery rates, and atmospheric release estimates. Technician refrigerant handling certifications must be verified before authorizing service work. Integration with national environmental reporting portals shall enable automated submission of refrigerant use data where required by regulatory authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R014	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	PTI Cost Recovery and Billing Management	The system must manage PTI service billing including standard inspection fees, repair labor charges, spare parts costs, and ancillary service charges such as washing, genset testing, and pre-cooling. Billing rates shall be configurable per shipping line agreement, container type, and operating entity. Automated invoice generation must aggregate all charges per container or per batch with detailed line-item breakdowns. Multi-currency billing must support invoicing in the shipping line's preferred currency with automatic conversion from the entity operating currency at the daily exchange rate. Credit note processing for disputed charges must follow approval workflows with audit trail documentation. Integration with the accounts receivable module shall track invoice aging and payment status. Tariff schedules must be version-controlled with effective dates to ensure correct rate application. VAT and GST must be applied per entity jurisdiction rules on all billable services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R015	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Mobile PTI Application for Field Technicians	The system must provide a mobile application for reefer technicians to conduct PTI inspections on tablets and smartphones in field conditions. The application must support offline operation with local data caching for inspection checklists, container information, and reference documentation. Camera integration shall enable in-app photography with automatic tagging by container number, reefer unit serial number, and inspection step. Bluetooth connectivity must support direct communication with reefer unit controllers for diagnostic data retrieval and parameter programming. The mobile interface must support both Arabic and English with runtime language switching and proper RTL layout for Arabic mode. Barcode and QR code scanning must enable rapid container identification and spare parts lookup. Data synchronization upon connectivity restoration must handle conflict resolution when multiple technicians have updated related records. Battery optimization features must extend usable session time during extended yard operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R016	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Automated PTI Prioritization and Urgency Scoring	The system must implement AI-driven prioritization of PTI requests based on multiple factors including vessel cut-off deadlines, cargo booking priority, commodity perishability risk, shipping line SLA requirements, and current depot workload. Urgency scoring algorithms shall assign numerical priority values that dynamically update as vessel schedules change or new bookings are received. Containers with perishable cargo bookings for temperature-sensitive commodities such as pharmaceuticals, fresh produce, or frozen seafood must receive elevated priority weighting. The system must automatically reschedule lower-priority PTIs when urgent requests are received and capacity is constrained. Priority override capabilities must be available to authorized operations managers with mandatory justification documentation. AI models shall learn from historical patterns to predict which containers are most likely to fail PTI and may require additional repair time, enabling proactive scheduling of buffer time. Notifications in Arabic and English must alert technicians when priority assignments change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-002-R017	FEAT-028-2-002	MOD-028	Genset PTI and Testing Coordination	The system must manage Pre-Trip Inspections for clip-on generator sets (gensets) used to power reefer containers during inland transportation. Genset PTI checklists shall cover engine condition, fuel system integrity, alternator output verification, automatic start-stop functionality, and emission compliance. Genset-to-reefer compatibility verification must confirm power output specifications match reefer unit electrical requirements including voltage, frequency, and phase configuration. Fuel consumption testing results must be recorded and compared against manufacturer specifications to identify units with degraded efficiency. Integration with transport planning modules shall coordinate genset availability with inland haulage schedules. Genset fleet inventory must track location, maintenance status, and certification validity across all four operating entities. Cost tracking for genset PTI must support multi-currency operations with entity-specific billing rates in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R001	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Classification Society Registry and API Integration	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of recognized maritime classification societies including Lloyd's Register (LR), Bureau Veritas (BV), DNV, American Bureau of Shipping (ABS), ClassNK (NK), Korean Register (KR), China Classification Society (CCS), Indian Register of Shipping (IRS), and RINA. For each society, the system shall store API endpoint configurations, authentication credentials, data exchange format specifications, and communication protocols. Real-time API integrations must be established with societies that offer digital interfaces for certificate verification, survey status queries, and condition of class notifications. Where API integration is not available, the system must support structured email-based data exchange with parsing capabilities. Connection health monitoring must alert administrators when API endpoints become unreachable. Multi-entity configurations must support different classification society relationships per operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R002	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Statutory Survey Schedule Tracking and Alerts	The system must track all statutory survey schedules for managed vessels including annual surveys, intermediate surveys, special surveys (class renewal), drydocking surveys, and continuous hull and machinery surveys. Survey windows shall be calculated based on class rules with configurable lead times for planning purposes. The alert engine must generate notifications at 180, 90, 60, and 30 days before survey due dates, escalating to senior management when surveys approach overdue status. Survey schedule data must be synchronized with classification society databases where API integration is available. The system must handle survey window extensions granted by flag state administrations or classification societies, updating schedules accordingly with extension documentation attached. Calendar views must support both Hijri and Gregorian date formats with entity-specific display preferences. Survey scheduling must account for port rotation plans to optimize survey locations across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R003	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Class Certificate Digital Repository	The system must provide a centralized digital repository for all classification and statutory certificates including class certificate, safety construction certificate, safety equipment certificate, load line certificate, IOPP certificate, IAPP certificate, ISM DOC and SMC, ISPS certificate, and MLC certificate. Each certificate record shall capture issuing authority, issue date in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, expiry date, endorsement history, and associated survey records. Automated expiry monitoring must flag certificates approaching renewal with sufficient lead time for survey planning. The repository must support document versioning to maintain historical certificate records. Digital certificate verification against classification society databases must be performed periodically to detect discrepancies between locally stored and society-held records. Access controls must restrict certificate modification to authorized document controllers while providing read access to operations teams. Multi-entity operations must maintain separate certificate registries per fleet entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R004	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Condition of Class Item Management	The system must track all condition of class items imposed by classification societies on managed vessels, including imposed dates, required rectification deadlines, and imposed conditions for trading. Each condition item shall be categorized by urgency level including immediate rectification, before next port, within specified timeframe, and at next scheduled survey. Workflow automation must route new conditions to technical superintendents and vessel masters with required action plans. Progress tracking shall monitor rectification efforts with evidence documentation requirements including photographs, repair reports, and material test certificates. The system must interface with classification society systems to confirm condition clearance upon submission of rectification evidence. Overdue conditions must trigger escalation alerts to fleet directors and flag state liaison officers. Cost tracking for condition rectification must support multi-currency recording in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. Historical condition analysis shall identify systemic issues across vessel classes or fleet segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R005	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Flag State Administration Liaison Tracking	The system must manage relationships and communication with flag state administrations for all managed vessels, tracking registration details, recognized organizations (ROs), and delegated authority scopes. Supported flag states shall include at minimum Qatar, UAE, Saudi Arabia, India, Panama, Liberia, Marshall Islands, Malta, and Singapore. Each flag state record must include contact details for maritime administration departments, applicable national maritime legislation references, and port state control targeting profiles. The system must track dual-flag arrangements and parallel registration requirements where applicable. Communication logs with flag state authorities must be maintained with document attachments and response tracking. Flag state-specific compliance requirements that exceed international conventions must be documented and linked to vessel operational procedures. Multi-entity fleet management must handle vessels registered under different flags within the same operational oversight, with entity-specific reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R006	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Survey Attendance Coordination and Logistics	The system must coordinate survey attendance logistics including classification society surveyor availability confirmation, berth booking coordination with port authorities, and attendance schedule alignment with vessel operational commitments. Survey attendance requests must be submitted to classification societies through integrated APIs or structured communication templates with minimum required notice periods. The system shall track surveyor assignment details including name, qualification scope, and estimated attendance duration. Coordination with vessel port calls must identify optimal survey windows that minimize operational disruption, considering cargo operations, bunkering schedules, and crew changes. Multi-port survey planning must optimize cost efficiency by grouping survey items across consecutive port calls at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Travel and accommodation arrangements for attending surveyors must be trackable with cost allocation to appropriate cost centers. Survey attendance costs must be recorded in local currency with USD equivalent for consolidated fleet reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R002	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Terminal and Berth Configuration	The system must allow creation and management of terminal records nested under each port, with each terminal having a unique identifier, name in English and Arabic, terminal operator details, and one or more berth definitions. Berth records must capture berth number, maximum vessel LOA (length overall) in meters, maximum draft in meters, maximum beam, quay length, number of quay cranes, crane outreach specifications, and maximum vessel capacity in TEUs. For example, Hamad Port Terminal 1 must support berths handling vessels up to 400m LOA and 16m draft. Each terminal must be linkable to one or more operating entities across the multi-entity structure (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R007	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Class Notation and Descriptive Note Management	The system must maintain a complete record of class notations and descriptive notes for each managed vessel as assigned by the classification society. Notation categories shall include hull notation, machinery notation, additional class notations such as ICE class, green passport, and cyber security, plus descriptive notes for specific operational capabilities. Changes to class notations must be tracked with effective dates, governing survey references, and approval documentation. The system must validate that vessel operations comply with the scope of assigned notations, alerting operations when trade patterns may require additional notations such as polar code notation for extreme weather routes. Integration with chartering modules must verify that tendered class notations match charterer requirements specified in fixture notes. Notation comparisons across fleet vessels must support fleet standardization planning. Display of notations must handle the complex alphanumeric symbology used by different classification societies with accurate rendering in system interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R008	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Thickness Measurement Report Management	The system must manage hull and structural thickness measurement reports generated during classification surveys. Measurement data shall be organized by vessel structural member including shell plating, deck plating, frames, longitudinals, transverse bulkheads, and hatch coamings. Each measurement point must be geo-referenced to the vessel structural plan with historical values enabling wastage rate calculation. AI-powered analysis shall predict when structural members will reach minimum allowable thickness based on corrosion rate trends, supporting proactive maintenance planning. Comparison against classification society minimum thickness standards shall automatically identify members approaching renewal criteria. Thickness measurement surveys conducted at different ports across the network including drydocking facilities used by vessels calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT must be consolidated into unified vessel structural records. Report generation must support Arabic and English formatting with tabular and graphical wastage trend presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R009	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	IACS Common Structural Rules Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor vessel compliance with IACS Common Structural Rules (CSR) for bulk carriers and oil tankers, tracking structural assessment requirements throughout the vessel lifecycle. CSR compliance records shall include as-built scantling data, design loading conditions, fatigue assessment results, and coating condition assessments. The system must integrate CSR compliance monitoring with cargo loading calculations to ensure structural loading limits are not exceeded during commercial operations. Updated CSR requirements published by IACS must be tracked with impact assessment against managed fleet vessels. Training record management shall verify that relevant technical staff maintain current knowledge of CSR requirements. Compliance reporting must aggregate fleet-wide CSR status for classification society annual reviews. The system must differentiate between CSR-BC (bulk carrier) and CSR-OT (oil tanker) requirements applying appropriate rules per vessel type. Multi-entity fleet data must be consolidatable for group-level structural compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R010	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Electronic Certificate Verification Service Integration	The system must integrate with classification society electronic certificate verification services to enable real-time validation of vessel certificates. Integration with IACS Electronic Certificate Delivery (ECD) system shall support automated download and storage of electronic certificates with digital signatures. The system must verify certificate authenticity by cross-referencing local records against society-held electronic certificates at configurable intervals. Port state control preparation workflows shall include automated certificate verification runs before vessel arrival at ports with known targeting risk. Discrepancy alerts must be generated when local certificate records do not match electronic verification results, triggering investigation workflows. The verification service must support certificates issued by all major classification societies operating in the Middle East and Indian subcontinent region. Verification results must be logged with timestamps for audit trail purposes. Bulk verification capabilities must enable fleet-wide certificate status checks with summarized reporting for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R011	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Survey Cost Budgeting and Tracking	The system must provide comprehensive budgeting and cost tracking for classification society surveys including annual survey fees, special survey costs, drydocking expenses, and ad-hoc inspection charges. Budget templates shall be configurable per vessel type, age bracket, and classification society fee schedule. Actual costs must be tracked against budgets with variance analysis and forecasting for the remaining budget period. Multi-currency cost management must handle survey invoices in classification society billing currencies while consolidating reports in entity operating currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and group reporting currency (USD). The system must support accrual accounting for survey costs that span multiple financial periods. Cost benchmarking across similar vessel types within the managed fleet shall identify cost optimization opportunities. Integration with procurement modules must track drydock and repair yard quotation processes. Budget approval workflows must follow entity-specific authorization hierarchies with supporting documentation requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R012	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Regulatory Convention Amendment Tracking	The system must track amendments to international maritime conventions and regulations that impact vessel classification and statutory compliance. Monitored conventions shall include SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, MLC, BWM Convention, and the Hong Kong Convention. Amendment tracking must capture effective dates, transitional provisions, flag state implementation status, and impact assessment on managed vessels. AI-powered analysis shall evaluate each amendment against fleet characteristics to identify affected vessels and required compliance actions. Compliance action plans must be generated with task assignments, deadlines, and budget implications. The system must differentiate between mandatory requirements and recommendatory guidelines. Regional regulatory requirements specific to Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE FTA, Saudi Ports Authority, and Indian DG Shipping must be tracked separately with cross-references to parent international conventions. Amendment notifications must be distributed in both Arabic and English to relevant stakeholders across all operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R013	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Drydocking Planning and Survey Integration	The system must integrate drydocking planning with classification society survey requirements to optimize the scope and timing of drydock visits. Planning tools shall consolidate all pending survey items, condition of class rectifications, maintenance requirements, and owner-requested modifications into a unified drydock specification. Drydock yard selection must be supported by a database of approved yards in the Middle East and Indian subcontinent region with capability matrices, historical performance ratings, and current availability. Tender management workflows shall distribute specifications to shortlisted yards and manage quotation comparisons with scoring against quality, cost, and schedule criteria. Multi-currency quotation comparison must normalize prices to a common currency for evaluation while maintaining original currency records. Project timeline planning must align drydock entry with classification society surveyor availability. Integration with vessel scheduling modules must identify optimal drydocking windows that minimize commercial disruption. Post-drydock survey completion must update all relevant certificate and condition of class records automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R014	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Classification Society Performance Benchmarking	The system must enable performance benchmarking of classification society services across the managed fleet. Performance metrics shall include survey response times, surveyor competency ratings, finding consistency scores, certificate processing turnaround, and dispute resolution effectiveness. Benchmarking data must be aggregated from operational records across all four entities to provide statistically meaningful comparisons. Service level agreement compliance tracking shall measure classification society performance against contractual commitments for survey attendance, certificate issuance, and technical inquiry response times. AI-driven analysis must identify trends in classification society service quality that may impact fleet operational efficiency. Benchmarking reports must support strategic decisions regarding classification society selection for newbuilding orders or re-classification of existing tonnage. Report generation must support bilingual Arabic-English formatting with data visualization components. Anonymized benchmarking data sharing with industry associations must be configurable per entity governance policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R015	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	Cyber Security Notation Compliance Management	The system must manage classification society cyber security notation requirements for vessels with networked operational technology systems. Compliance tracking shall cover IMO MSC.428(98) cyber risk management requirements and classification society-specific cyber notations such as DNV Cyber Secure, LR ShipRight Cyber, and BV Cyber Managed. Asset inventories of vessel OT and IT systems must be maintained with network topology diagrams and vulnerability assessment records. Cyber security survey preparation checklists must ensure all documentation and evidence requirements are ready before classification society cyber audits. The system must track cyber security incident reports and their impact on class notation validity. Integration with vessel network monitoring systems shall provide continuous compliance evidence collection. Training records for vessel and shore-based personnel on maritime cyber security must be maintained per classification society requirements. Multi-entity cyber security policies must be harmonized while accommodating jurisdiction-specific regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-003-R016	FEAT-028-2-003	MOD-028	GHG and EEXI/CII Rating Management	The system must track vessel Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) attainment and Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) ratings as managed by classification societies under IMO MEPC.328(76) and MEPC.352(78). EEXI technical files must be stored with classification society approval records and engine power limitation (EPL) or shaft power limitation (SHaPoli) documentation where applicable. CII operational ratings shall be calculated continuously using voyage data with projections of year-end ratings updated in real-time. Corrective action plans for vessels rated D or E must be tracked with implementation timelines and effectiveness assessments. Integration with classification society CII verification processes must streamline annual rating confirmation. The system must model the CII impact of different operational strategies including slow steaming, weather routing, and port efficiency improvements across routes serving Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Fleet-wide emissions dashboards must present CII trends in both Arabic and English interfaces with entity-level filtering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R001	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Survey Report Template Library Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive library of survey report templates covering all inspection types including hold inspections, hatch surveys, reefer PTI, on-hire and off-hire surveys, bunker surveys, draft surveys, damage surveys, and condition surveys. Each template shall define mandatory sections, required data fields, photograph placement guidelines, and signature blocks. Templates must be version-controlled with approval workflows for modifications requiring quality manager authorization. Entity-specific template variations must be supported to accommodate local regulatory requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Bilingual Arabic-English templates must maintain parallel content structure with proper RTL formatting for Arabic sections. AI-assisted template recommendations shall suggest the appropriate template based on survey type, vessel characteristics, and client requirements. Template customization must be achievable through a configuration interface without developer involvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R002	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	AI-Assisted Survey Report Generation	The system must provide AI-powered assistance for generating survey reports from inspection data, photographs, and field notes. Natural language generation shall convert structured inspection findings into professional narrative report text in both Arabic and English. AI shall automatically organize photographs within reports based on inspection sequence and deficiency categorization. Draft reports must incorporate standardized terminology consistent with industry practices and classification society conventions. The AI engine must learn from historical approved reports to improve generation quality over time, adapting to entity-specific writing styles and formatting preferences. Human review and approval must always be required before report finalization, with AI-generated content clearly marked during the review process. The system must support voice-to-text input for field surveyors dictating observations, with automatic transcription in Arabic or English based on detected language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R003	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Multi-Stage Report Approval Workflow	The system must implement configurable multi-stage approval workflows for survey reports with routing rules based on report type, survey category, financial implications, and entity-specific governance requirements. Standard workflow stages shall include surveyor submission, peer review, technical review, quality assurance check, and management approval. Each stage must capture reviewer identity, timestamp in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, comments, requested modifications, and approval or rejection status. Rejection workflows must route reports back to designated stages with revision tracking and comparison views highlighting changes between versions. Parallel approval branches must be supported for reports requiring sign-off from multiple departments such as technical, commercial, and legal. Escalation rules must trigger when reports remain at any approval stage beyond configurable time thresholds. Delegation capabilities must allow approvers to assign temporary deputies during absence periods. Approval workflows must be entity-specific while sharing common structural elements across the multi-entity organization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R004	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Digital Archive Storage and Retention Policy	The system must implement a digital archive for all survey reports and supporting documentation with configurable retention policies per document type and entity jurisdiction. Retention periods shall comply with maritime regulatory requirements including minimum seven-year retention for statutory survey records, five-year retention for commercial survey reports, and permanent retention for structural survey records. Archive storage must use redundant cloud infrastructure with geographic distribution ensuring data availability across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operating regions. Document integrity verification using cryptographic hashing shall detect any unauthorized modifications to archived records. Automated lifecycle management must transition documents from active storage to archive tiers based on age and access frequency. Legal hold capabilities must override standard retention policies when documents are subject to litigation or regulatory investigation. Storage costs must be tracked per entity in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with allocation based on storage consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R005	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Advanced Search and Retrieval Engine	The system must provide advanced search capabilities for retrieving archived survey reports using multiple criteria including vessel name, IMO number, container number, survey type, date range in both Hijri and Gregorian calendars, surveyor name, port location, deficiency category, and keywords within report content. Full-text search shall index all report content including embedded text in photographs using OCR technology. AI-powered semantic search must enable natural language queries such as finding reports mentioning specific types of structural damage or particular cargo contamination incidents. Search results must be sortable and filterable with preview capabilities showing relevant excerpts with highlighted search terms. Saved search configurations must be shareable across team members within the same entity. Cross-entity search must be available to authorized users with role-based access controls limiting result visibility. Search performance must return results within three seconds for queries spanning up to ten years of archived reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R006	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Report Distribution and Notification Management	The system must manage automated distribution of finalized survey reports to designated recipients based on configurable distribution lists per report type and entity. Distribution channels shall include secure email with encrypted attachments, secure portal download links with expiration dates, and API-based delivery to client systems. Recipient categories must include vessel owners, charterers, P&I clubs, classification societies, port authorities, cargo interests, and regulatory bodies. Distribution tracking must confirm delivery status and record when recipients access or download reports. Selective distribution must enable sending different report versions or sections to different recipient groups based on confidentiality requirements. Mass distribution capabilities must support sending batch notifications when multiple reports are finalized simultaneously. Distribution templates must support bilingual Arabic-English cover letters with entity-specific branding. Undeliverable notification handling must alert operations when distribution fails and provide retry mechanisms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R007	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Survey Report Version Control and Audit Trail	The system must maintain complete version control for all survey reports from initial draft through final approved version including all intermediate revisions. Each version shall capture the full report content, modification details with granular change tracking at the paragraph level, modifier identity, and timestamp in both Hijri and Gregorian formats. Side-by-side comparison views must enable reviewers to identify changes between any two versions with visual highlighting of additions, deletions, and modifications. The audit trail must be tamper-proof using append-only storage with cryptographic chaining to detect any unauthorized modifications. Audit records must capture all access events including reads, downloads, prints, and distribution actions. Regulatory auditors must be able to independently verify the integrity of the audit chain. Version control must extend to embedded photographs and attachments with visual diff capabilities for image modifications. Multi-entity audit trails must be consolidated for group-level compliance reporting while maintaining entity-specific access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R008	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Photographic Evidence Management and Enhancement	The system must provide comprehensive management of photographic evidence associated with survey reports including storage, organization, annotation, and enhancement capabilities. Photographs must be stored at original resolution with metadata preservation including EXIF data, GPS coordinates, capture timestamp, and device information. AI-powered image enhancement shall improve visibility of deficiencies in poorly lit conditions common during hold and tank inspections. Annotation tools must enable surveyors to add measurement overlays, directional indicators, and deficiency markers on photographs. Automatic duplicate detection must identify photographs that are identical or near-identical to prevent report padding. Image authentication shall detect manipulated photographs using digital forensics techniques. Photograph organization must support categorization by inspection area, deficiency type, and severity level. Batch processing capabilities must handle upload of hundreds of photographs per inspection efficiently. Storage optimization must apply intelligent compression while maintaining evidentiary quality standards required by P&I clubs and courts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R009	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Cross-Reference and Linking Between Survey Records	The system must enable cross-referencing between related survey records to build comprehensive vessel history profiles. Linking capabilities shall connect pre-loading surveys with cargo damage claims, on-hire surveys with off-hire surveys, periodic surveys with condition of class items, and damage surveys with repair completion records. Automatic link suggestions shall be powered by AI algorithms that identify related records based on vessel identity, timeframe proximity, deficiency similarity, and cargo type correlations. Bi-directional navigation must enable users to traverse linked records seamlessly without losing context. Link integrity must be maintained when records are archived or migrated between storage tiers. Cross-entity linking must be supported for vessels that operate across multiple entity jurisdictions such as a vessel surveyed at Jebel Ali under UAE entity and subsequently at JNPT under India entity. Relationship visualization must display survey record networks graphically with timeline-based navigation. Orphan detection must identify survey records that should have corresponding linked records but currently do not.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R010	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Regulatory Compliance Evidence Package Generation	The system must generate consolidated compliance evidence packages for regulatory inspections including port state control examinations, flag state audits, ISM external audits, and ISPS verifications. Evidence packages shall automatically compile relevant survey reports, certificates, maintenance records, and training documentation based on the inspection type and scope. Package configuration must be customizable per regulatory authority requirements with pre-built templates for common audit scenarios at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must maintain a compliance calendar tracking upcoming regulatory inspection windows based on vessel trading patterns and port state control targeting criteria. AI-assisted gap analysis shall identify missing or expired documentation before compliance packages are finalized. Package generation must support both digital delivery with indexed PDF compilation and physical printing with professional formatting. Bilingual Arabic-English packages must be generatable with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content. Package delivery tracking must confirm receipt by the requesting regulatory authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R011	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Knowledge Extraction and Lessons Learned Database	The system must extract knowledge from historical survey reports to build a searchable lessons learned database supporting continuous improvement in inspection quality and vessel maintenance. AI-powered text mining shall identify recurring deficiency patterns, successful repair methodologies, and emerging equipment failure modes across the fleet. Knowledge articles must be automatically generated from significant survey findings with categorization by vessel type, equipment category, deficiency type, and geographic region. Human curation workflows shall validate AI-extracted knowledge before publication to the lessons learned database. Search and recommendation engines must surface relevant lessons when surveyors are preparing for inspections of similar vessel types or investigating similar deficiency categories. Knowledge sharing across entities must be governed by configurable visibility rules respecting proprietary information boundaries. The database must support contributions in both Arabic and English with cross-language search capabilities. Usage analytics must track which lessons are most frequently accessed to prioritize knowledge curation efforts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R012	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	External Party Report Sharing Portal	The system must provide a secure external portal for sharing survey reports with authorized third parties including vessel owners, charterers, P&I club correspondents, cargo underwriters, and classification society representatives. Portal access shall be governed by invitation-based registration with multi-factor authentication and configurable access expiration periods. Report access permissions must be granularly controllable allowing specific reports, report sections, or time-limited viewing windows. Watermarking must be applied to all documents accessed through the portal with recipient-specific identifiers to trace unauthorized distribution. Download tracking must record all document access events with IP address logging and device fingerprinting. The portal interface must support Arabic and English with automatic RTL layout switching based on user language preference. Portal branding must be customizable per operating entity with entity-specific logos and color schemes. Usage analytics must provide insights into which reports are most frequently accessed and by which recipient categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R013	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Survey Report Analytics and Business Intelligence	The system must provide business intelligence dashboards analyzing survey report data across multiple dimensions including deficiency frequency by vessel type, inspection pass rates by port location, average report turnaround times, and cost analysis by survey category. Trend analysis shall identify improving or deteriorating vessel condition patterns across the managed fleet with statistical significance indicators. Comparative analytics must benchmark entity performance across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT operations with drill-down capabilities from summary to individual report level. Predictive analytics shall forecast survey volumes, expected deficiency rates, and resource requirements based on fleet age profiles and trading patterns. AI-generated insight narratives must accompany dashboard visualizations explaining significant findings in business context. Report data must be exportable to standard business intelligence tools through API endpoints and scheduled data feeds. Dashboard displays must support Arabic and English with dynamic RTL layout adjustment. Currency normalization must convert multi-currency survey costs to a common reporting currency for cross-entity comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R014	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Legal Hold and Litigation Support Features	The system must support legal hold procedures for survey reports and associated documentation that may be relevant to ongoing or anticipated litigation. Legal hold activation must freeze retention policies for designated documents preventing automatic deletion or archival transitions. Hold scope must be definable by vessel, date range, survey type, and related parties with the ability to expand scope as litigation requirements evolve. Chain of custody documentation must track all access to held documents with certified timestamps and accessor identity verification. Expert witness packages must be compilable from held documents with annotation capabilities for legal review without modifying original records. Integration with legal matter management systems shall synchronize hold status and matter references. Court-admissible export formats must be supported with metadata preservation and integrity certification. Multi-jurisdiction hold requirements must accommodate differing legal discovery obligations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. Hold release workflows must require dual authorization from legal counsel and records management officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R015	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Automated Report Quality Assurance Checks	The system must perform automated quality assurance checks on survey reports before they enter the approval workflow. QA checks shall verify completeness of mandatory sections, presence of required photographs, consistency of vessel particulars across report sections, mathematical accuracy of calculated values such as draft readings and cargo quantities, and adherence to formatting standards. AI-powered language quality checks must assess report text for grammatical errors, ambiguous statements, and inconsistencies between narrative findings and tabulated data in both Arabic and English report sections. Terminology consistency checks shall verify that standard maritime terms are used correctly and consistently throughout the report. Photograph quality assessment must flag images that are too dark, blurry, or lack required metadata. QA check results must be presented as a categorized list of findings with severity levels distinguishing between blocking issues that prevent submission and advisory items for surveyor consideration. QA rule sets must be configurable per entity and report type without requiring technical system modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R016	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Offline Report Drafting and Synchronization	The system must support offline survey report drafting for surveyors working in environments without reliable network connectivity such as vessel cargo holds, engine rooms, and remote berths at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Offline capabilities must include access to report templates, vessel particulars from cached data, photograph capture and annotation, and structured data entry for all standard inspection findings. The offline module must store draft reports securely on the local device with encryption at rest to protect sensitive survey data. Upon network reconnection, automatic synchronization must upload draft reports and associated media to the central system with conflict detection and resolution when multiple surveyors have contributed to the same report. Data integrity verification must confirm successful synchronization with checksums validated against the uploaded content. Offline session duration must support extended periods of up to 72 hours accommodating multi-day vessel inspections. The offline interface must maintain full Arabic and English bilingual functionality with RTL support independent of network connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-028-2-004-R017	FEAT-028-2-004	MOD-028	Survey Report Data Export and Integration API	The system must provide robust data export capabilities and integration APIs enabling survey report data to be consumed by external systems including vessel management platforms, insurance management systems, and regulatory reporting portals. RESTful API endpoints must expose report metadata, structured finding data, and document download capabilities with OAuth 2.0 authentication and rate limiting. Data export formats shall include JSON, XML, CSV, and PDF with configurable field mappings per integration partner. Webhook notifications must alert subscribed systems when reports are finalized, status changes occur, or new deficiency items are recorded. API versioning must ensure backward compatibility when interface changes are deployed. Data transformation capabilities must map internal data structures to partner-specific schemas used by major P&I clubs, classification societies, and port community systems operating at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. API usage analytics must track consumption patterns per integration partner with billing support for chargeable data access. Multi-entity API key management must enforce entity-level data segregation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R002	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Unique Claim Reference Generation	The system must generate a globally unique claim reference number upon registration following the pattern CLM-{ENTITY}-{YYYY}{MM}-{SEQ} where ENTITY is the two-letter code for the operating entity (QA for Qatar, AE for UAE, SA for KSA, IN for India). The sequence must be zero-padded to six digits and must never be reused even if a claim is cancelled or voided. The generation mechanism must be thread-safe and support concurrent claim registration across all four entities without collision. Each claim reference must be immutable once assigned, and the system must maintain an audit trail recording the timestamp, user, and source channel of registration in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R007	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claim Priority Routing and Assignment	After triage classification, the system must automatically route claims to the appropriate claims handler or team based on configurable routing rules. Routing criteria must include: claim severity, port jurisdiction (Hamad Port claims to Qatar team, Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port to UAE team, JNPT to India team), commodity type (hazardous goods to specialized handlers), claim value threshold, and claimant VIP status. The system must support workload balancing across handlers within a team, ensuring no single handler exceeds the configurable maximum active claim count. If no handler is available, the claim must be escalated to the team supervisor. Routing rules must be maintainable through an admin UI without code changes, supporting both English and Arabic rule descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R012	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claim Acknowledgment and SLA Timer	Upon successful registration, the system must automatically generate and send a claim acknowledgment to the claimant via email and in-app notification in the claimant's preferred language (English or Arabic). The acknowledgment must include the claim reference number, summary of the claim details, the assigned claims handler name and contact information, and the expected response timeline based on the SLA. The system must start an SLA timer at registration with configurable SLA targets per claim severity: Critical claims must have initial response within 4 hours, High within 24 hours, Medium within 3 business days, and Low within 5 business days. SLA breaches must trigger escalation notifications to the team supervisor and must be tracked as KPI metrics. Business days must be calculated per the entity's local calendar, accounting for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian public holidays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R016	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Integration with Insurance and P&I Club Systems	The system must provide API integration endpoints for notifying cargo insurance underwriters and Protection & Indemnity (P&I) club correspondents upon claim registration. The integration must support transmission of claim summary data in ACORD XML and JSON formats, including claim reference, vessel IMO number, voyage details, container number, damage description, and estimated claim value in USD. The system must track acknowledgment receipts from external systems and flag claims where insurer acknowledgment is not received within 48 hours. For claims involving P&I club liability, the system must auto-populate the vessel's P&I club details from the voyage record and initiate the notification workflow. The integration must support configurable endpoint URLs per insurer and P&I club, with OAuth 2.0 authentication and TLS 1.3 encryption for all transmissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R001	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	P&I Club Master Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive master registry of all P&I Clubs with which the organization holds or has held memberships, including International Group clubs (e.g., Gard, Skuld, Standard Club, West of England, Britannia) and fixed-premium clubs. Each registry entry must store club name, registered address, contact details, club rules version, policy year cycle (typically February 20 renewal), correspondent offices in Doha, Dubai, Jeddah, and Mumbai, and communication preferences. The registry must support both English and Arabic club names with RTL rendering for Arabic correspondence templates. Historical records must be retained for a minimum of 10 years for regulatory audit purposes across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R002	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Policy Lifecycle Management	The system must track the complete lifecycle of each P&I policy from initial application through renewal or cancellation. Each policy record must capture the policy year (aligned with the International Group's February 20 cycle), entered vessels (by IMO number and vessel name), coverage type (mutual or fixed premium), tonnage declarations, deductible levels, and special conditions. The system must automatically generate renewal reminders 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry. Policy status transitions (draft, active, renewed, cancelled, run-off) must be tracked with full audit trail. Multi-entity support must allow separate policies per legal entity in Qatar (QR), UAE (AED), KSA (SAR), and India (INR) while providing a consolidated fleet-wide view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R003	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Premium Call Tracking and Allocation	The system must record and track all premium calls from P&I Clubs, including advance calls, supplementary calls, release calls, and overspill calls. Each call must be associated with the specific policy year and allocated proportionally across entered vessels based on gross tonnage or net tonnage as specified by the club. Premium amounts must be stored in the club's billing currency (typically USD) with automatic conversion to the local entity currency (QAR for Qatar operations at Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port operations, SAR for KSA operations, INR for JNPT operations) using the exchange rate on the call date. The system must generate payment schedules aligned with club-specified installment dates and integrate with the accounts payable module for settlement tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R004	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Vessel Entry and Withdrawal Management	The system must manage the entry and withdrawal of vessels into and from P&I Club cover, including mid-year entries and withdrawals. For each vessel entry, the system must capture the vessel's IMO number, flag state, classification society, DWT, GT/NT, trading area restrictions, and any special conditions or warranties imposed by the club. Pro-rata premium calculations must be automatically computed for mid-year entries or withdrawals based on the number of days of cover. The system must enforce business rules preventing gaps in P&I coverage for any vessel actively trading in the Arabian Gulf, Red Sea, or Indian Ocean routes, generating alerts if a vessel is withdrawn without confirmed replacement cover from another club or policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R005	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Club Correspondence Tracking	The system must provide a structured correspondence management facility for all communications with P&I Clubs, including emails, letters, circulars, and club bulletins. Each correspondence item must be tagged with the relevant vessel, policy year, claim reference (if applicable), and correspondence category (underwriting, claims, loss prevention, legal, accounting). The system must support bilingual correspondence in English and Arabic, with Arabic text rendered in RTL format and proper Unicode handling for mixed-language documents. Correspondence templates must be maintained for standard communications such as vessel entry notifications, premium queries, and claim notifications. Inbound club circulars must be parsed and flagged if they reference any entered vessel, port (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT), or trading area relevant to the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R006	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Coverage Verification and Certificate Generation	The system must provide real-time verification of P&I coverage status for any vessel in the fleet, accessible via API for integration with port call management and cargo booking modules. Blue Cards and Certificates of Entry must be generated on demand in PDF format, supporting both English and Arabic versions as required by port state authorities in Qatar (MWANI Qatar), UAE (Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World), KSA (Saudi Ports Authority), and India (Mumbai Port Trust). The system must validate that coverage limits meet minimum requirements under the International Convention on Civil Liability for Bunker Oil Pollution Damage (Bunkers Convention 2001) and CLC 1992 before allowing a vessel to be scheduled for port calls at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R007	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	AI-Driven Premium Benchmarking	The system must utilize AI models to benchmark P&I premium rates against market trends and historical data. The AI engine must analyze at least 5 years of premium history per vessel class (container feeder, container main-liner, bulk carrier, tanker), club performance metrics, and International Group pool claims data to recommend optimal club placement and negotiation strategies. The benchmarking must consider vessel-specific risk factors including age, flag state, classification society, trading area (Arabian Gulf, Indian subcontinent, Red Sea), and claims history. Results must be presented in dashboard format with both English and Arabic labels, supporting QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD currency displays with automatic conversion at configurable exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R009	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Renewal Workflow and Approval Process	The system must implement a multi-stage renewal workflow with configurable approval gates. The workflow must begin with an AI-generated renewal strategy recommendation at least 120 days before the February 20 renewal date, followed by management review, broker engagement (if applicable), club negotiation tracking, and final board or management committee approval. Each approval stage must capture the approver's identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, comments, and digital signature. The workflow must support parallel approvals across entities (Qatar head office, UAE branch, KSA branch, India branch) with entity-level authority matrices. Escalation rules must automatically notify senior management if approvals are not completed within defined SLA periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R001	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Multi-level customer hierarchy tree	The system must support a minimum of six hierarchical levels for customer organizations: Global Parent, Regional HQ, Country Entity, Branch Office, Department, and Individual Contact. Each level must carry its own set of attributes including trade license number, tax registration (VAT/GST), preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), and default port preferences (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEAUH for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT). The hierarchy must support drag-and-drop reorganization with full audit trail logging of every structural change, including timestamp, user, and reason for modification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R010	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Loss Prevention Integration	The system must integrate with P&I Club loss prevention programs by tracking condition surveys, loss prevention bulletins, and recommended corrective actions for each entered vessel. Survey findings from club-appointed surveyors at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other ports of call must be recorded with photographs, deficiency descriptions, and deadline dates for rectification. The system must generate automated follow-up reminders and track completion status. Non-compliance with loss prevention recommendations must trigger risk alerts that are factored into the AI premium benchmarking model. Historical loss prevention data must be accessible for trend analysis across the fleet, supporting both tabular and graphical representations with Arabic and English language options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R011	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Multi-Entity Policy Consolidation Dashboard	The system must provide a consolidated dashboard view of all P&I policies across the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), showing total entered tonnage, aggregate premium exposure, coverage gaps, and upcoming renewal milestones. The dashboard must support drill-down from group level to entity level to individual vessel level. Premium totals must be displayed in both the entity's local currency and a user-selectable base currency (defaulting to USD). The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts, with all numerical formats respecting the selected locale (e.g., Arabic-Indic numerals for Arabic locale). Offline access must cache the most recent dashboard snapshot for viewing when connectivity to the central server is unavailable, particularly useful for operations at anchorage areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R012	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Regulatory Compliance Tracking	The system must track regulatory compliance requirements related to P&I insurance across all four operating jurisdictions. This includes Qatar Maritime Administration requirements, UAE Federal Transport Authority (FTA) regulations, Saudi Arabian General Authority of Transport (GAT) directives, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping (DGS) mandates. Each regulatory requirement must be mapped to specific policy conditions to verify compliance. The system must automatically flag policies that do not meet minimum cover requirements for specific port state jurisdictions, including minimum CLC limits, bunker convention compliance, and wreck removal coverage thresholds. Compliance reports must be generatable in both English and Arabic, with dates shown in both Gregorian and Hijri formats per the user's preference settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R013	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Broker Management and Commission Tracking	The system must manage relationships with insurance brokers involved in P&I placement, tracking broker appointment letters, scope of authority, commission rates, and performance metrics. Commission calculations must be automated based on agreed rates applied to advance call premiums, with adjustments for supplementary and release calls. The system must support different broker arrangements per entity (e.g., a Qatar-based broker for Gulf placements, an India-based broker for JNPT-related coverage). Commission payments must be tracked in the broker's agreed currency with conversion to entity local currency for accounting integration. Broker performance dashboards must show placement success rates, negotiation outcomes versus AI benchmarks, response time metrics, and claim handling support quality scores across policy years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R014	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Sanctions and Exclusion Screening	The system must automatically screen all P&I policy activities against current international sanctions lists, including OFAC, EU, and UN sanctions, as well as club-specific exclusion lists. Before processing any vessel entry, coverage confirmation, or claim notification, the system must verify that the vessel, its beneficial owner, charterer, cargo interest, and counterparties are not on any sanctioned entity list. The screening must consider the specific sanctions landscape relevant to the Arabian Gulf region, including restrictions on certain Iranian, Syrian, and Yemeni trades. Screening results must be logged with timestamps and retained for at least 7 years. Positive matches must trigger an immediate workflow alert to the compliance officer and block further processing until manual clearance is provided with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R015	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Offline Correspondence Drafting	The system must support offline drafting and queuing of P&I Club correspondence when network connectivity is unavailable, which may occur during vessel visits, port inspections at remote berths, or operations in areas with limited internet coverage. Drafted correspondence must be stored locally on the user's device with encryption at rest, including all attachments and metadata. When connectivity is restored, queued correspondence must be automatically synchronized with the central system, validated against current policy data, and submitted for approval workflow processing. The offline module must support bilingual drafting in English and Arabic with RTL text rendering. Conflict resolution rules must handle scenarios where policy data has changed between offline draft creation and synchronization, notifying the user of any discrepancies requiring manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-001-R016	FEAT-029-1-001	MOD-029	Audit Trail and Document Versioning	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for all P&I policy-related activities, including policy creation, modification, renewal, correspondence, premium payments, and coverage verifications. Each audit entry must capture the user identity, action performed, timestamp in UTC with local timezone annotation, entity context, and before/after values for any data modification. Document versioning must be applied to all policy documents, correspondence, and club rules, retaining all versions with the ability to compare any two versions side by side. The audit trail must support export in both PDF and CSV formats for review by internal auditors, external auditors, and regulatory inspectors. Arabic language audit reports must properly render RTL text with correct date formatting in Hijri calendar where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R001	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	H&M Policy Registration and Data Capture	The system must support the registration and comprehensive data capture for each Hull and Machinery insurance policy, including insured vessel details (IMO number, vessel name, flag state, classification society, year of build, hull material, main engine type and power), policy period, insured value (agreed value basis or market value basis), premium amount and rate, deductible structure (standard deductible, age-related additional deductible, machinery damage deductible), and underwriter details. Each policy must be linked to the corresponding vessel record in the fleet management module. The insured value and premium must be stored in the policy currency (typically USD) with automatic conversion to entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for consolidated financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R002	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Underwriter and Co-Insurance Panel Management	The system must manage underwriter panels for H&M policies, supporting both single-insurer and co-insurance arrangements where multiple underwriters share the risk. For co-insurance panels, the system must record each underwriter's participation percentage (line), leading underwriter designation, and claims agreement authority. Panel member details must include company name, Lloyd's syndicate number (if applicable), local representative offices in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, and claims contact information. The system must calculate and track premium distribution across panel members based on their participation percentages. When processing premium payments, the system must generate separate payment instructions for each panel member in their preferred settlement currency, with exchange rate conversion applied at the policy-agreed rate or spot rate as contractually specified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R003	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Vessel Valuation Tracking	The system must track vessel valuations used for H&M insurance purposes, including agreed valuations at policy inception, periodic market value assessments, and classification society valuations. The system must integrate with or accept data feeds from vessel valuation services to monitor market value fluctuations. When a vessel's market value deviates by more than 15% from the insured value, the system must generate an alert recommending policy adjustment to avoid under-insurance or over-insurance implications. Valuation records must be maintained in USD as the primary currency with historical conversion rates to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR at each valuation date. The AI engine must predict valuation trends based on vessel age, type (container vessel, tanker, bulker), market conditions, and comparable sales data to support insurance renewal negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R004	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Premium Calculation and Payment Scheduling	The system must calculate H&M premiums based on configurable rating factors including insured value, vessel age, vessel type, classification society, flag state, trading area (Arabian Gulf, Indian Ocean, Red Sea), and claims experience. Premium payment schedules must support annual lump sum, semi-annual, quarterly, or monthly installment options as agreed with underwriters. The system must generate payment reminders at configurable intervals before each installment due date (default: 30, 15, and 7 days). Late payment penalties must be calculated automatically per policy terms. Each premium transaction must record the amount in policy currency (USD) and local entity currency, with the applicable exchange rate sourced from the treasury module. Payment reconciliation with bank statements must be supported through automated matching on reference numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R005	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Trading Area and Warranty Compliance Monitoring	The system must monitor vessel trading patterns against H&M policy warranties and exclusions related to geographical trading areas. Standard Institute Warranties (e.g., Institute Warranties concerning trading, towage, salvage, etc.) must be stored per policy, and the system must cross-reference vessel AIS position data and port call schedules to detect potential warranty breaches. Specific monitoring must be implemented for Joint War Committee (JWC) listed areas in the Arabian Gulf region, Gulf of Aden, and areas near the Strait of Hormuz. When a vessel enters or is scheduled to enter a restricted or additional-premium zone, the system must automatically calculate the additional premium obligation and generate a notification to underwriters as required by policy terms. All warranty compliance checks must be logged for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R006	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Classification Society Survey Integration	The system must integrate with classification society survey schedules and outcomes for all insured vessels. Survey types must include annual surveys, intermediate surveys, special surveys (every 5 years), bottom surveys, boiler surveys, and tail shaft surveys as defined by major classification societies (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, ABS). The system must track survey due dates, completed surveys, conditions of class, and outstanding recommendations. Overdue surveys or unresolved conditions of class that may affect H&M coverage must trigger automated alerts to both the technical management team and the insurance department. Survey completion certificates must be stored in the document management system and linked to the corresponding H&M policy record for underwriter reference during renewal negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R007	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	War Risk Insurance Management	The system must manage war risk insurance as a separate but linked policy to the standard H&M cover, tracking war risk premiums, coverage limits, and territorial exclusions. War risk policies must be linked to the underlying H&M policy and vessel record. The system must maintain a current copy of the Joint War Committee (JWC) Hull War, Piracy, Terrorism and Related Perils Listed Areas and automatically cross-reference vessel itineraries against these areas. For vessels trading through the Strait of Hormuz, Gulf of Oman, or Gulf of Aden, the system must calculate additional war risk premiums (breach premiums) based on the number of transits and days in listed areas. War risk premium tracking must support separate currencies and underwriter panels from the standard H&M policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R008	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Increased Value and Excess Liabilities Management	The system must support Increased Value (IV) and Excess Liabilities policies placed in addition to the primary H&M policy. These supplementary policies must be linked to the underlying H&M policy and vessel record, with separate tracking of insured amounts, premiums, and deductibles. The system must enforce the standard market practice that the combined insured value under H&M plus IV policies does not exceed 125% of the vessel's agreed value. Premium calculations for IV policies must reference the H&M policy premium rate with configurable adjustment factors. When calculating total loss scenarios, the system must aggregate recoveries across H&M, IV, and excess liability policies while ensuring no over-recovery. Dashboard views must display the total insurance structure per vessel across all policy layers in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R009	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Renewal Strategy and AI Recommendation Engine	The system must generate AI-driven renewal strategy recommendations for each H&M policy at least 90 days before policy expiry. The AI engine must analyze the vessel's claims history over the current and prior four policy years, market premium rate trends for comparable vessels, vessel condition survey results, classification society status, and fleet-wide loss ratios. Recommendations must include suggested insured value adjustments, optimal deductible levels, panel restructuring opportunities, and estimated premium ranges. The AI must factor in regional market conditions specific to the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean insurance markets. Renewal strategy reports must be generated in both English and Arabic, with monetary values displayed in USD and the relevant entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R010	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	H&M Policy Document Management	The system must manage all documents associated with H&M policies including policy wordings (Marine Insurance Act 1906 / Institute clauses based), endorsements, addenda, slip copies, cover notes, and debit/credit notes. Each document must be versioned, with full change history and the ability to compare any two versions. Documents must be stored with metadata tags including vessel name, IMO number, policy year, document type, underwriter, and entity. The system must support document formats including PDF, DOCX, and scanned images with OCR text extraction for searchability. Bilingual documents containing both English and Arabic text must be rendered correctly with proper RTL/LTR handling. Automated document retention policies must comply with the longest statutory requirement across all four jurisdictions (typically 10 years for insurance documents in India under the Marine Insurance Act, 1963).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R011	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Loss of Hire Insurance Tracking	The system must track Loss of Hire (LOH) insurance policies as an optional extension to H&M cover. LOH policy records must capture the daily indemnity amount, franchise period (deductible period, typically 14 days), maximum indemnity period (typically 90 or 180 days), and covered perils. The daily indemnity amount must be stored in the policy currency with conversion to entity local currencies. When an H&M claim is filed that may result in vessel off-hire, the system must automatically calculate the potential LOH recovery based on estimated repair duration, franchise period, and daily rate. The system must cross-reference off-hire records from the voyage management and charter party modules to validate LOH claim periods. LOH premium must be tracked separately from H&M premium for cost allocation and profitability analysis per vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R012	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Fleet-Wide H&M Portfolio Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive analytics for the entire H&M insurance portfolio across all operating entities. Key metrics must include total insured value, total premium, average rate (premium as percentage of insured value), loss ratio (claims paid versus premium), frequency ratio (number of claims per vessel-year), and average cost per claim. Analytics must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel type, vessel age bracket, classification society, and policy year. Trend analysis must show at least 5 years of historical data to identify deteriorating or improving performance patterns. All analytics views must support export to Excel and PDF formats, display in both English and Arabic, and render monetary values in user-selected currencies with real-time conversion rates. The AI engine must highlight anomalies and generate natural language insights about portfolio performance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R013	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Machinery Damage Additional Deductible Management	The system must manage machinery damage additional deductibles which are commonly applied to older vessels or vessels with specific machinery configurations. The system must store and apply age-related additional deductibles (typically applying to vessels over 15 years old with escalating rates for 20+ and 25+ year-old vessels) and machinery-specific deductibles for main engine, auxiliary engines, boilers, and electrical systems. When a machinery damage claim is initiated, the system must automatically determine the applicable deductible based on the vessel's age at the date of casualty, the machinery component involved, and any special deductible endorsements. Deductible calculations must account for series of casualties and the application of common or uncommon cause provisions under the Institute Time Clauses (Hulls) 1/10/83 or 1/11/95 as specified in the policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R014	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Drydocking and Repair Cost Estimation Integration	The system must integrate with drydocking and repair planning modules to correlate H&M insurance coverage with planned and unplanned repair activities. When a repair is required that may give rise to an H&M claim (e.g., collision damage, grounding damage, machinery breakdown), the system must capture repair yard quotations from yards in the Arabian Gulf (e.g., ASRY Bahrain, Drydocks World Dubai, Nakilat-Keppel Offshore & Marine Qatar) and Indian subcontinent (e.g., Cochin Shipyard, Mazagon Dock). Repair cost estimates must be compared against policy deductibles to determine claim viability. The system must track whether repairs are carried out alongside routine drydocking (enabling betterment deductions by underwriters) versus emergency repairs, as this distinction impacts claim recovery calculations under H&M policy terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R015	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Mortgagee Interest and Assignment Tracking	The system must track mortgagee interests registered against each insured vessel, including bank or financial institution name, mortgage amount, mortgage currency, and loss payee clause details. Notices of Assignment must be generated and tracked for each vessel where the H&M policy has been assigned to a mortgagee bank. The system must ensure that mortgagee interest endorsements are maintained on all H&M policies for financed vessels and generate alerts when policies are due for renewal to ensure continuous mortgagee protection. Letters of Undertaking from underwriters to mortgagee banks must be managed with version control. The system must support the specific requirements of banks commonly financing vessels in the region, including Qatar National Bank, Emirates NBD, National Commercial Bank (KSA), and State Bank of India, each of which may have distinct loss payee clause wordings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R016	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	Offline H&M Policy Access and Updates	The system must provide offline access to H&M policy summaries, coverage details, and key documents for use during vessel inspections, port calls, or when operating in areas with limited connectivity such as anchorage areas at Hamad Port outer anchorage, Jebel Ali anchorage, or JNPT anchorage. The offline module must cache the most recent policy data including insured values, deductible levels, underwriter contact information, and coverage certificates for all vessels in the user's assigned fleet. Any updates made offline (e.g., recording damage observations, initiating preliminary claim notifications) must be encrypted and stored locally until connectivity is restored, at which point they must be synchronized with the central system. Conflict resolution must handle cases where policy data was updated centrally during the offline period, alerting the user to review and reconcile any discrepancies before finalizing the synchronized records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R004	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Pilot Booking and Scheduling	The system must manage pilot booking requests for vessel arrival and departure at each port. For Hamad Port, pilot requests must be submitted to Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani) at least 24 hours before the vessel's ETA. For Jebel Ali, pilot requests follow DP World's booking portal requirements with a 12-hour advance notice window. The system must capture pilot boarding station coordinates, pilot ladder side (port or starboard), freeboard height, and any pilot exemption certificates held by the vessel master. AI must predict optimal pilot boarding times based on historical tide and current data, vessel maneuvering characteristics, and traffic density at the pilot station to suggest the most efficient scheduling slots.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-002-R017	FEAT-029-1-002	MOD-029	H&M Premium Allocation Across Entities	The system must support the allocation of H&M premiums across multiple legal entities when a vessel is beneficially owned by one entity but commercially managed or operated by another. Allocation rules must be configurable based on vessel ownership structure, bareboat charter arrangements, or management agreements between group entities. The system must generate inter-company charge records for premium allocations, with proper transfer pricing documentation as required by tax authorities in Qatar (Qatar Tax Authority), UAE (Federal Tax Authority for corporate tax), KSA (ZATCA), and India (Income Tax Department transfer pricing rules under Section 92). Premium allocation reports must display amounts in both the policy currency and each entity's local currency, with exchange rate details and allocation methodology clearly documented for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R001	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Open Cover Policy Registration	The system must support the registration and management of cargo open cover policies, which provide blanket coverage for all shipments meeting defined criteria without requiring individual negotiation per shipment. Each open cover must capture underwriter details, policy period, maximum single shipment limit, annual aggregate limit, covered commodities (with HS code references), covered trade routes (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Dammam), applicable Institute Cargo Clauses (A, B, or C), war and strikes clauses, and special conditions. The system must support multiple concurrent open covers per entity to handle different commodity types or trade lanes. Policy limits must be tracked in real-time against declared shipments to prevent exceeding maximum accumulation limits at any single location or on any single vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R002	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	INCOTERMS-Based Coverage Determination	The system must automatically determine cargo insurance coverage obligations based on the INCOTERMS 2020 rule specified in the sales contract or booking. For CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) and CIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To) terms, the system must flag that the seller is responsible for procuring insurance and verify that appropriate cover is in place. For CIP shipments, the system must enforce the INCOTERMS 2020 requirement for minimum Institute Cargo Clauses (A) all-risks coverage. For FOB, CFR, FCA, and other terms where the buyer arranges insurance, the system must facilitate buyer-side policy management. The coverage determination engine must consider the full transport chain from origin to destination, including inland transport segments from container freight stations to Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT container terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R003	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Shipment Declaration and Certificate Issuance	The system must automate the declaration of individual shipments under open cover policies, generating insurance certificates for each declared shipment. Declaration data must be auto-populated from the booking and bill of lading modules, including shipper, consignee, vessel name, voyage number, container numbers and types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), bill of lading number, cargo description, HS code, and declared value. Insurance certificates must be generated in PDF format supporting both English and Arabic text, with the certificate layout conforming to market standards acceptable to banks for letter of credit presentations. The system must assign unique certificate numbers, calculate premiums based on the open cover rate schedule, and update the cumulative declarations against policy limits in real-time. Certificates must be issuable in currencies specified in the letter of credit (commonly USD, but supporting QAR, AED, SAR, and INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R004	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Cargo Valuation and Sum Insured Calculation	The system must calculate the sum insured for cargo insurance based on configurable valuation methods. The standard marine insurance valuation (CIF value plus 10% anticipated profit, i.e., CIF + 10%) must be the default, but the system must support alternative methods including invoice value, replacement value, and agreed value. For shipments under letters of credit, the sum insured must comply with UCP 600 requirements (minimum 110% of CIF or CIP value). The system must handle multi-currency invoices, converting to the insurance currency using the exchange rate on the date of declaration or the date of the bill of lading as specified by the open cover terms. Special valuation rules must be supported for high-value cargo such as electronics, pharmaceuticals, and machinery, which may require specific agreed values. The system must flag declarations where the declared value appears anomalous compared to historical averages for the same commodity code.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R005	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Commodity-Specific Coverage Rules	The system must maintain a database of commodity-specific coverage rules and exclusions that apply to cargo insurance policies. Rules must be categorized by HS code and must define applicable Institute Cargo Clauses, additional clauses (e.g., Institute Frozen Food Clauses for reefer containers, Institute Container Clauses), packaging requirements, and special survey or inspection requirements. For common commodities shipped through the target ports (e.g., petrochemicals from Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, foodstuffs and textiles through JNPT, building materials through Khalifa Port), pre-configured rule sets must be provided. The system must automatically validate that declared cargo is covered under the applicable policy and alert users when cargo falls under policy exclusions such as inherent vice, inadequate packaging, or specific commodity exclusions. Commodity rules must support Arabic language descriptions for Gulf-origin cargo documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R006	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Multi-Modal Transport Coverage Tracking	The system must track cargo insurance coverage across multiple transport modes within a single shipment journey, including ocean transport, inland road transport, rail transport, and warehouse storage. Coverage must be aligned with the warehouse-to-warehouse clause typically included in Institute Cargo Clauses, tracking the cargo from the point of origin (e.g., factory or warehouse in the shipper's country) through inland transport to the loading port, ocean voyage, discharge at destination port, and final delivery to the consignee's premises. Transit duration limits specified in the policy (e.g., 60 days from discharge at the final port) must be monitored, and the system must alert when cargo storage at destination ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT) approaches the transit clause time limit, which could jeopardize coverage. Each transport leg must record the carrier, conveyance details, and estimated transit times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R007	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Accumulation Control and Risk Management	The system must provide real-time accumulation monitoring to ensure that total cargo values at risk at any single location, on any single vessel, or in any single conveyance do not exceed policy-specified maximum accumulation limits. Accumulation calculations must aggregate all declared and in-transit shipments by location (port, warehouse, vessel), considering the latest container tracking positions. When accumulation at a location approaches 80% of the policy limit, the system must generate a warning alert. When accumulation would exceed the policy limit, the system must block further declarations and route to an underwriter notification workflow for additional cover or risk acceptance. Specific accumulation monitoring must be configured for the high-value container yards at Jebel Ali Port (one of the world's busiest container ports), Hamad Port container terminal, Khalifa Port KIZAD logistics zone, and JNPT's Nhava Sheva container terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R008	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Premium Rate Schedule Management	The system must manage complex premium rate schedules associated with each cargo open cover or specific policy. Rate schedules must support rate determination based on multiple factors including commodity type (by HS code grouping), packaging type (containerized FCL, LCL, break-bulk), trade route (port pair), vessel age and type, and value band. The system must support flat rates, tiered rates (with breakpoints based on cargo value), and minimum premiums per declaration. Rate schedules must accommodate different rates for Institute Cargo Clauses A, B, and C, with separate war and strikes premium rates. The AI engine must analyze historical declaration patterns and loss experience to recommend rate optimizations during open cover renewal negotiations. Rate schedules must be versioned with effective dates to ensure correct premium calculation for declarations made during rate transition periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R009	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Letter of Credit Insurance Compliance	The system must validate cargo insurance certificates against letter of credit (L/C) requirements to ensure compliance before document presentation to banks. Validation checks must include verification that the insurance cover date is no later than the bill of lading date (as required by UCP 600 Article 28), the currency and amount meet minimum requirements (typically 110% of CIF value), the risks covered match L/C stipulations, and the certificate is issued by an acceptable insurance company or underwriter. The system must flag discrepancies that would cause L/C document rejection, including mismatched vessel names, ports, or goods descriptions between the insurance certificate and other shipping documents. Support must be provided for L/C operations in all four entity jurisdictions, with integration to trade finance modules handling QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD denominated letters of credit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R010	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Specific Voyage Policy Management	The system must support the creation and management of specific voyage (spot) cargo insurance policies for shipments that fall outside the scope of existing open covers, such as exceptional high-value consignments, unusual commodities, or non-standard trade routes. Specific voyage policy requests must capture all shipment details and be routed through an underwriter quotation workflow. The system must support quotation comparison when multiple underwriters are approached, tracking quoted rates, terms, and conditions side by side. Once a quotation is accepted, the system must generate the policy and certificate documents. The workflow must accommodate the time-sensitive nature of spot placements, with target turnaround times tracked and escalation alerts when quotation responses from underwriters are delayed beyond configurable thresholds. All specific voyage policies must be reported to the overall cargo insurance portfolio analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R011	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Reefer Container Cargo Special Conditions	The system must handle special insurance conditions applicable to refrigerated (reefer) container shipments, which are common for foodstuff imports through Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Special conditions must include temperature maintenance warranties (specifying acceptable temperature ranges in both Celsius and Fahrenheit), pre-cooling requirements, genset fuel adequacy, and continuous power supply provisions during port storage and inland transport. The system must integrate with container IoT monitoring data to record temperature logs and alert when temperature deviations occur that may impact insurance coverage. Insurance certificates for reefer cargo must include temperature warranty clauses and reference to Institute Frozen Food Clauses (A) 01/01/86 or Institute Frozen Meat Clauses (A) 01/01/86 as applicable. Pre-shipment survey requirements for reefer containers must be tracked and documented as a condition precedent to coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R012	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Dangerous Goods Cargo Insurance Handling	The system must manage insurance requirements specific to dangerous goods (DG) cargo classified under the IMDG Code. For each DG shipment declaration, the system must capture the UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class and division, packing group, and flash point where applicable. Additional premium loadings for DG cargo must be automatically applied based on the hazard classification and the policy's DG rate schedule. The system must validate that the declared DG commodity is not excluded under the cargo policy's standard or specific exclusions (e.g., certain Class 1 explosives or Class 7 radioactive materials may be excluded). Documentation requirements including Material Safety Data Sheets (MSDS) must be linked to the insurance declaration. The system must also track DG-specific regulatory requirements for ports including Jebel Ali DG storage regulations, JNPT DG handling rules, and Hamad Port's DG manifest requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R013	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Cargo Insurance Portfolio Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive portfolio reports for cargo insurance programs across all four operating entities. Reports must include declarations summary (count, total value, total premium), loss ratio analysis (claims versus premiums by period, trade route, commodity, and entity), premium leakage analysis (shipments not declared under open covers), and concentration risk reports showing accumulation by location, vessel, and commodity group. Reports must support date range filtering using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Financial summaries must present values in the policy currency (typically USD) with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). All reports must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL layout, exportable to Excel and PDF formats. AI-powered insights must be embedded in reports highlighting trends, anomalies, and optimization opportunities for the cargo insurance program.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R014	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Subrogation Rights Preservation Tracking	The system must track actions required to preserve subrogation rights against carriers, terminal operators, and other responsible third parties when cargo damage occurs. The system must ensure that time-bar deadlines for filing claims against carriers under the Hague-Visby Rules (one year from delivery or when goods should have been delivered), Hamburg Rules, or Rotterdam Rules are tracked and enforced. For inland transport segments, applicable local limitation periods must be monitored (e.g., India's Carriage of Goods by Sea Act, UAE Maritime Commercial Law, Saudi Maritime Commercial Law). The system must generate timely reminders for filing protective notices and legal proceedings. Carrier liability assessment must consider whether containers were shipped under shipper-owned container (SOC) or carrier-owned container (COC) terms, as this affects the allocation of responsibility for container condition-related damage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R015	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Automatic Declaration from Booking Integration	The system must support automatic cargo insurance declaration generation when integrated with the booking and shipping instruction modules. When a booking is confirmed for a trade route covered under an active open cover policy, the system must automatically create a draft declaration pre-populated with vessel, voyage, container, cargo, and value details from the booking. The draft declaration must be routed for review and approval before being finalized and the insurance certificate generated. The system must handle scenarios where booking details change after initial declaration (e.g., vessel change, roll-over to next sailing, container substitution) by generating amendment declarations and revised certificates. For shipments from JNPT, Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port, port-specific data fields (terminal operator, berth, container yard location) must be auto-populated from port community system integrations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R016	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Offline Certificate Generation Capability	The system must provide offline capability for generating cargo insurance certificates when network connectivity is unavailable, which is particularly relevant during operations at port areas, container freight stations, and warehouses. The offline module must cache the current open cover policy details, rate schedules, and certificate templates locally on the user's device. Certificates generated offline must be assigned from a pre-allocated certificate number range to avoid numbering conflicts. Offline-generated certificates must be clearly marked as provisionally issued pending synchronization with the central system. When connectivity is restored, offline certificates must be automatically validated against current policy status, accumulation limits, and rate schedules, with any discrepancies flagged for manual review. The offline module must support bilingual certificate generation in English and Arabic with all required fields including insured value in the L/C-specified currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-003-R017	FEAT-029-1-003	MOD-029	Third-Party Logistics Cargo Coverage Coordination	The system must coordinate cargo insurance coverage with third-party logistics (3PL) providers and freight forwarders who may arrange their own cargo insurance or operate under the organization's master open cover. The system must track whether each 3PL partner has their own insurance (and verify its adequacy) or operates as a declared interest under the organization's policy. For 3PL-arranged insurance, the system must record policy details, coverage limits, and certificate copies for verification. Liability boundaries between the organization's coverage and the 3PL's coverage must be clearly defined in the system based on INCOTERMS, handling agreements, and warehouse operator liability terms. The system must support the common scenario in the Gulf region where free zone warehouse operators (e.g., JAFZA, KIZAD, QFZ) have specific insurance requirements and liability caps that must be considered in the overall cargo insurance coverage design.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R001	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Surveyor Registry and Qualification Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of approved marine surveyors and survey firms, categorized by survey type (cargo damage, hull condition, P&I condition, pre-loading, draft survey, container inspection, reefer pre-trip inspection). Each surveyor record must capture full name, company affiliation, qualifications and certifications (e.g., IIMS membership, Lloyd's Agency appointment, classification society authorization), geographic coverage area, available ports (specifically Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and surrounding anchorages), contact details, languages spoken (with mandatory Arabic and English capability for Gulf-based surveyors), and hourly or per-survey fee schedule in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The registry must support both active and inactive surveyor statuses, with reasons for deactivation tracked for compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R002	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	AI-Powered Surveyor Selection and Assignment	The system must implement an AI-driven surveyor selection engine that automatically recommends the most suitable surveyor for each survey requirement based on multiple weighted criteria. Selection criteria must include surveyor proximity to the survey location, expertise match for the specific survey type and cargo commodity, historical performance ratings, current workload and availability, surveyor fee competitiveness, language capabilities matching the involved parties, and any underwriter or P&I Club preferences or requirements for surveyor appointment. The AI must learn from historical survey outcomes (timeliness, report quality, claim correlation) to continuously refine its selection recommendations. For urgent surveys at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT where vessels have limited port stays, the system must prioritize surveyors with confirmed immediate availability and proven rapid response history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R003	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Request Initiation Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for initiating survey requests that captures all necessary information for surveyor briefing. The request form must include survey type, urgency level (routine, urgent within 24 hours, emergency within 4 hours), vessel details (name, IMO number, berth or anchorage location), cargo details (commodity, container numbers, bill of lading references), nature of damage or concern, parties to be notified of survey (underwriters, P&I Club, cargo interests, ship owner), access arrangements, safety requirements, and any special equipment needed. The workflow must support initiation by multiple stakeholders (operations team, claims department, cargo receivers, vessel master) with appropriate authorization levels. Survey requests must be auto-linked to relevant insurance policies (H&M, cargo, P&I) based on the vessel and cargo details provided. All request fields must support bilingual entry in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R004	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Scheduling and Calendar Management	The system must provide integrated scheduling capabilities for coordinating survey appointments with vessel port call schedules, tide windows, terminal operating hours, and surveyor availability. The scheduling engine must access real-time vessel ETA/ETD data from the port call management module for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to ensure surveys are scheduled within the vessel's port stay window. Calendar views must show all scheduled surveys across all ports with filtering by port, survey type, surveyor, and entity. The system must handle time zone differences between operating regions (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India) and display times in the user's local timezone. Scheduling conflicts must be detected and flagged automatically. Rescheduling due to vessel delays, weather disruptions, or surveyor unavailability must be managed through a modification workflow that notifies all interested parties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R005	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Surveyor Notification and Briefing Distribution	The system must automate the generation and distribution of surveyor appointment notifications and briefing packages. Notification methods must include email, SMS, and in-app messaging, with delivery confirmation tracking. The briefing package must be auto-generated and include the survey mandate (scope of instructions), vessel particulars, cargo details and stowage plan extracts, relevant policy details (redacted to show only information necessary for the survey), previous survey reports for the same vessel or cargo type if relevant, port-specific access procedures (gate pass requirements, safety induction requirements for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT), and contact details for the local agent and vessel representative. Briefing documents must be generated in both English and Arabic for Gulf port surveys. The system must track acknowledgment of survey instructions by the surveyor and escalate if acknowledgment is not received within the configured timeframe.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R006	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Joint Survey Coordination	The system must support the coordination of joint surveys where multiple parties appoint their own surveyors to attend simultaneously. Joint survey scenarios are common in cargo damage claims where the ship-owner's P&I Club surveyor, the cargo underwriter's surveyor, and the receiver's surveyor may all need to attend together. The system must manage the scheduling logistics of aligning multiple surveyors' availability with the vessel's port stay, track each party's surveyor appointment separately, and generate a unified joint survey attendance record. Communication must be facilitated between all appointed surveyors to agree on survey timing and methodology. The system must record whether each invited party attended the joint survey or declined attendance, as non-attendance may have legal implications for subsequent claim disputes. Joint survey reports must cross-reference findings from different surveyors for comparative analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R007	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Progress Tracking and Real-Time Updates	The system must enable real-time tracking of survey progress from appointment through completion and report submission. Survey status stages must include appointed, briefing acknowledged, surveyor en route, survey commenced, survey completed on-site, preliminary findings submitted, draft report submitted, report finalized, and report distributed. Surveyors must be able to update status via a mobile-responsive web interface or native mobile application, including the ability to upload photographs, short videos, and voice notes from the survey site. GPS location tracking must be available (with surveyor consent) to confirm surveyor arrival at the port or vessel. Real-time status updates must be visible to all authorized stakeholders through the dashboard. Automated alerts must be triggered when survey milestones are missed against SLA timelines (e.g., preliminary findings not submitted within 24 hours of survey completion).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R008	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Report Management and Storage	The system must manage the lifecycle of survey reports from draft through final submission, review, and archival. Reports must be uploaded in PDF format with support for embedded photographs and diagrams. The system must extract and index key data from survey reports using AI-powered document analysis, including damage descriptions, estimated repair costs or cargo loss values, probable cause assessments, and recommendations. Reports must be tagged with structured metadata including survey type, vessel IMO number, port location (using UN/LOCODE: QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP), date of survey in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, cargo commodity codes, and associated policy and claim references. Full-text search must be supported across all stored reports in both English and Arabic. Retention policies must ensure reports are stored for the longer of the policy's contractual requirement or the applicable statute of limitations period in the relevant jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R009	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Surveyor Fee Management and Invoice Processing	The system must manage surveyor fee agreements, track invoices, and process payments for survey services. Fee structures must support per-survey fixed fees, hourly rates with minimum charges, travel and subsistence allowances, overtime rates for surveys outside normal working hours, and weekend or holiday premiums (considering Friday-Saturday weekends in Qatar, UAE, and KSA versus Saturday-Sunday weekends in India). The system must validate surveyor invoices against agreed fee schedules and the recorded survey duration, flagging discrepancies for review. Invoice amounts must be processed in the surveyor's local currency (QAR for Qatar-based surveyors, AED for UAE-based, SAR for KSA-based, INR for India-based) with conversion to the policy currency for claims cost allocation. Aggregate fee reports must show surveyor costs by port, survey type, entity, and period for budget management and cost optimization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R010	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Surveyor Performance Rating and Feedback	The system must implement a structured performance evaluation framework for surveyors, tracking metrics across multiple dimensions. Performance dimensions must include response time (time from appointment to acknowledgment and to survey commencement), report quality (completeness, clarity, technical accuracy as rated by claims handlers), report turnaround time (time from survey completion to final report submission), adherence to survey instructions, and professional conduct feedback from vessel or cargo interests. After each survey, the assigned claims handler or operations coordinator must complete a standardized feedback form rating the surveyor on a 1-5 scale across each dimension with mandatory comments for scores below 3. The AI engine must aggregate ratings to maintain a dynamic performance score for each surveyor that feeds back into the surveyor selection algorithm. Surveyors with consistently low ratings must be flagged for review and potential removal from the approved registry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R011	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Pre-Loading Survey Coordination	The system must manage pre-loading surveys conducted before cargo is loaded into containers or onto vessels, which are often required by cargo underwriters for high-value or sensitive shipments. Pre-loading survey requests must be triggered automatically based on cargo insurance policy conditions, commodity type, and shipment value thresholds configured in the system. The system must coordinate the survey timing with the cargo stuffing schedule at container freight stations (CFS) or shipper premises, ensuring the surveyor is present to verify cargo condition, packaging adequacy, container condition (including CSC plate validity), and proper stowage and securing. For reefer containers, pre-loading surveys must include verification of pre-cooling, temperature settings, ventilation settings, and genset fuel levels. Survey findings must be recorded against the shipment and linked to the cargo insurance declaration. Any adverse findings must trigger an alert to the insurance department before the shipment proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R012	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Discharge and Tally Survey Management	The system must coordinate discharge and tally surveys at destination ports to verify cargo condition and quantity upon vessel discharge. The system must integrate with terminal operating system data from Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT to obtain container discharge sequences and timing. Tally survey records must capture container numbers, seal numbers and seal condition (intact, broken, missing, replaced), container external condition (damage codes per IICL standards), and any visible cargo damage observed during discharge. The system must generate automated survey appointment requests when containers with high-value cargo or damage-prone commodities are scheduled for discharge, based on configurable rules linked to the cargo insurance policy conditions. Discrepancies between ship figures and shore tally must be highlighted and escalated immediately to preserve claim rights against the carrier under applicable carriage conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R013	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Evidence and Photography Management	The system must provide structured management of photographic and video evidence collected during surveys. Surveyors must be able to upload photographs directly from mobile devices with automatic geotagging (GPS coordinates) and timestamping. Each photograph must be categorized by evidence type (container exterior, container interior, cargo condition, damage detail, vessel hold condition, documentation, seal condition, temperature logger readout) with mandatory captioning in English and optional Arabic captioning. The system must enforce minimum photograph requirements per survey type (e.g., minimum 20 photographs for a cargo damage survey covering all six sides of the container plus detail shots). AI-powered image analysis must be available to automatically detect and tag visible damage patterns (e.g., water damage, crushing, contamination, rust) in uploaded photographs. All evidence must be stored with chain-of-custody metadata to ensure admissibility in legal proceedings or arbitration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R014	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Third-Party Survey Appointment Tracking	The system must track survey appointments made by third parties (P&I Clubs, cargo underwriters, classification societies, port authorities, customs) that the organization needs to be aware of and potentially attend. When notified of a third-party survey appointment, the system must record the appointing party, surveyor name and firm, survey purpose, scheduled date and time, and vessel or cargo subject. The system must determine whether the organization should appoint its own representative to attend the third-party survey to protect its interests and generate appropriate recommendations. Attendance tracking must record who attended from each party. The system must request and file copies of third-party survey reports when issued, linking them to the relevant vessel, cargo, and insurance records. Third-party survey schedules at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT must be integrated into the organization's survey calendar to avoid conflicts and enable coordination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R015	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Template and Checklist Management	The system must maintain a library of survey templates and checklists tailored to different survey types, cargo commodities, and vessel configurations. Templates must include structured data entry fields, photographic requirement checklists, measurement recording forms (e.g., draft survey calculation sheets, temperature recording logs, moisture content measurement records), and standardized damage assessment forms. Templates must be available in both English and Arabic, with bilingual field labels and input validation rules. The AI engine must recommend the most appropriate template based on the survey type, cargo commodity, and vessel type specified in the survey request. Custom templates must be creatable by authorized users for specialized survey requirements. Template versioning must be maintained to ensure that the correct version is used for each survey, with completed surveys permanently linked to the template version that was active at the time of the survey.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R016	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Offline Survey Data Collection	The system must support offline survey data collection for surveyors working in areas with limited or no internet connectivity, which commonly occurs inside vessel holds, container yards, warehouses, and certain berth areas at ports. The offline-capable mobile survey application must pre-download the assigned survey briefing, applicable templates and checklists, and relevant reference data before the surveyor goes on-site. All data entered offline, including form responses, photographs, voice notes, and GPS coordinates, must be stored securely on the device with encryption at rest. When connectivity is restored, the offline data must synchronize automatically with the central system, with conflict detection and resolution for any data that may have been updated centrally during the offline period. The offline module must function for a minimum of 72 hours without connectivity to accommodate surveys during extended vessel anchorage periods or multi-day inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R017	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Cost Allocation and Recharging	The system must manage the allocation and recharging of survey costs to the appropriate cost centers, insurance policies, or third parties. Survey costs must be allocatable to specific claim files (when the survey relates to an existing or potential claim), cargo insurance policies (for routine pre-loading or discharge surveys), H&M insurance policies (for hull condition surveys), P&I Club matters, or operational budgets. The system must support cost sharing scenarios where multiple parties agree to split survey costs, calculating each party's share and generating appropriate debit notes or invoices. For surveys conducted on behalf of cargo interests, the system must determine whether survey costs are recoverable under the cargo policy or must be borne by the consignee based on the applicable INCOTERMS and policy conditions. Cost allocation reports must present data in both the survey currency and the entity's reporting currency, with date-specific exchange rates applied for accurate financial reporting across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-1-004-R018	FEAT-029-1-004	MOD-029	Survey Analytics and Trend Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on survey activities across all operating ports and entities. Key analytics must include survey volume by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), survey type, and time period; average survey cost by type and port; surveyor utilization rates; average time from survey request to completion; correlation between survey findings and claim outcomes; and recurring damage patterns by commodity, trade route, and container type. The AI engine must identify trends in survey data that indicate systemic issues, such as recurring cargo damage at specific terminals, damage patterns linked to particular vessels or trade routes, or seasonal variations in claim frequency. Trend reports must be presented in both English and Arabic with interactive visualizations supporting Hijri and Gregorian calendar date selections. Predictive analytics must forecast future survey demand by port and type to support surveyor capacity planning and budget forecasting across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R001	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Multi-Channel Claim Registration Portal	The system must provide a unified claim registration interface supporting web, mobile, and API-based submissions for all entity types (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The portal must capture claim type classification (cargo damage, container loss, delay, theft, natural disaster, collision) with mandatory fields including bill of lading number, container ID (ISO 6346 format), voyage reference, port of origin/destination (QAHAM, AEKLP, AEJEA, INJNP), estimated loss value in original transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), and incident date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The interface must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual field labels and validation messages. Offline claim registration must queue submissions locally and synchronize upon connectivity restoration with conflict detection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R002	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Automated Claim Number Generation with Entity Prefix	The system must generate unique claim reference numbers using a structured format: {ENTITY}-CLM-{YEAR}-{SEQUENCE} where ENTITY is QA/AE/SA/IN corresponding to the filing entity jurisdiction. The sequence must be auto-incrementing per entity per calendar year and must support both Gregorian and Hijri year references. Duplicate detection must run at registration time, comparing new claims against existing records using bill of lading, container number, voyage, and incident date combinations. When a potential duplicate is detected within a configurable threshold (default 90% similarity), the system must flag the claim for manual review and present the matching records side by side in both Arabic and English. Claims registered offline must reserve sequence numbers from a pre-allocated block to prevent numbering conflicts upon synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R003	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Evidence and Documentation Upload Management	The system must support uploading and categorizing evidence documents for each claim including survey reports, photographs of damage, bills of lading, packing lists, commercial invoices, certificates of origin, temperature logs for reefer containers (container types 22R1, 42R1, 45R1), GPS tracking data exports, port gate-in/gate-out records, and tally sheets. Each document must be tagged with metadata including upload timestamp, uploader identity, document type classification, and chain-of-custody audit trail. Maximum file size must be configurable per entity (default 50MB per file, 500MB per claim). The system must support image formats (JPEG, PNG, TIFF), PDF, Excel, and video (MP4) with automatic virus scanning. Arabic-named files and documents with Arabic content must be fully supported without encoding issues. Offline uploads must be queued with integrity checksums verified upon synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R004	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	AI-Powered Damage Assessment from Photographic Evidence	The system must integrate an AI image analysis engine that processes uploaded photographs of container and cargo damage to automatically estimate damage severity on a 5-point scale (minimal, minor, moderate, severe, total loss). The AI model must identify damage types including dents, corrosion, holes, water ingress, structural deformation, and seal tampering for standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC) and specialized units (flat racks, open tops, tank containers). The assessment must generate a preliminary damage report with estimated repair or replacement cost in the claim's base currency, automatically converted to USD equivalent using the prevailing exchange rate. Confidence scores must be displayed alongside AI assessments, and any assessment below 75% confidence must be automatically escalated for human surveyor review. The AI must learn from surveyor corrections to improve accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R005	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Surveyor Assignment and Coordination Workflow	The system must maintain a registry of approved marine surveyors across all operational ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) with their certifications, specializations (hull, cargo, P&I, condition surveys), availability calendars, and performance ratings. When a claim requires physical inspection, the system must automatically recommend the top three surveyors based on proximity to the incident location, specialization match, current workload, historical performance score, and response time SLA. The surveyor assignment must generate notifications in the surveyor's preferred language (Arabic or English) with all claim details, required inspection scope, and deadline. The workflow must track surveyor acceptance, inspection scheduling, report submission, and payment processing. If no surveyor accepts within the configurable SLA (default 24 hours), the system must auto-escalate to the claims manager and expand the search radius.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R006	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Investigation Task Management and Timeline Tracking	The system must provide a structured investigation workflow with configurable task templates per claim type. Each investigation must track milestones including initial notification, evidence collection, surveyor appointment, survey completion, third-party statements, root cause determination, liability assessment, and settlement recommendation. Tasks must have assignable owners, due dates (displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), priority levels, and dependency chains. The system must calculate and display elapsed time against SLA benchmarks set per entity and claim type (e.g., cargo damage claims at JNPT must complete initial assessment within 48 hours per Indian Insurance Regulatory guidelines). Overdue tasks must trigger automated escalation notifications to supervisors in both Arabic and English. A Gantt-style timeline visualization must show investigation progress with critical path highlighting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R007	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Third-Party Liability Determination Engine	The system must support structured liability assessment across multiple parties including carrier, shipper, consignee, port operator, stevedore, freight forwarder, and warehouse operator. Liability determination must consider INCOTERMS 2020 rules (EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP) to establish risk transfer points and allocate responsibility. The engine must reference the applicable legal framework based on claim jurisdiction: Qatar Maritime Law, UAE Federal Maritime Law, Saudi Maritime Regulations, or Indian Carriage of Goods by Sea Act. Shared liability scenarios must support percentage-based allocation across multiple parties with automated notification to each liable party. The system must maintain a knowledge base of precedent decisions for similar claim types to assist investigators in consistent liability determination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R008	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Automated Notification to Insurers and P&I Clubs	The system must automatically generate and transmit claim notifications to relevant insurance underwriters and P&I clubs upon claim registration or when claim value exceeds policy-specific notification thresholds. Notifications must conform to Lloyd's Market Association (LMA) electronic claim file (ECF) standards where applicable. The system must support multiple notification channels including API integration with major marine insurers, EDI messaging, structured email with PDF attachments, and ACORD XML format. Each notification must include claim summary, policy reference, estimated quantum in both original currency and USD equivalent, incident narrative in English (with Arabic translation where the filing entity requires), and preliminary liability assessment. The system must track insurer acknowledgment and response within configurable SLA windows (default 5 business days), auto-escalating unacknowledged notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R009	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Claim Status Tracking and Stakeholder Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time claim status dashboard accessible to all authorized stakeholders including claimants, adjusters, surveyors, legal counsel, and management. The dashboard must display claims categorized by status (registered, under investigation, surveyed, liability determined, settlement proposed, settled, closed, reopened, disputed) with drill-down capability. Filtering must support multiple dimensions: entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port, claim type, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), currency, value range, assignee, and aging bracket. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with all numerical values, currency symbols, and date formats appropriately localized. Real-time notifications must be sent to stakeholders upon status transitions via in-app alerts, email, and SMS where configured. A mobile-responsive view must provide critical claim details in low-bandwidth scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R010	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Claim Valuation and Quantum Assessment	The system must calculate claim quantum based on documented evidence, applying the appropriate valuation methodology per policy terms: agreed value, market value, replacement cost, or CIF value plus agreed percentage. For cargo claims, valuation must reference the commercial invoice value, adjusting for partial loss using weight or unit-based proration. Container damage claims must reference the current book value or replacement cost from the container asset register (integrated with MOD inventory). Currency conversion must use the exchange rate applicable on the date of loss, sourced from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority, or Reserve Bank of India as appropriate to the claim entity. The system must calculate deductibles and policy excess amounts per coverage terms, displaying net claimable amounts. All quantum calculations must maintain a full audit trail showing input values, formulas applied, and any manual adjustments with justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R011	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Fraud Detection Indicators and Scoring	The system must implement an AI-driven fraud detection module that assigns a fraud risk score (0-100) to each registered claim. The scoring model must evaluate indicators including claim frequency per claimant, claim value relative to cargo value, timing patterns (claims filed just before policy expiry), inconsistencies between reported damage and photographic evidence, historical claim patterns for specific trade routes (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT corridor), GPS track anomalies, and discrepancies between bill of lading declarations and actual cargo. Claims scoring above a configurable threshold (default 70) must be automatically flagged for special investigation unit (SIU) review. The system must generate a fraud indicator summary report in both Arabic and English highlighting specific risk factors. False positive feedback from investigators must be used to continuously retrain the model. All fraud scoring algorithms must comply with data protection regulations across QA, AE, SA, and IN jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R012	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Reefer Container Specific Claim Handling	The system must provide specialized claim handling workflows for refrigerated container (reefer) cargo claims involving temperature-sensitive goods. The workflow must integrate with temperature monitoring systems to retrieve continuous temperature logs for the entire transit chain from origin port gate-in through vessel voyage to destination port gate-out. The system must automatically identify temperature excursions against the shipper's declared set-point temperature and acceptable deviation range, plotting these on a time-temperature graph with port call annotations (QAHAM, AEKLP, AEJEA, INJNP). For perishable cargo claims, the system must calculate spoilage percentage using commodity-specific degradation curves (fruits, pharmaceuticals, chemicals, frozen goods). The claim must capture responsible party for each temperature excursion segment (terminal operator, shipping line, or pre-carriage trucker). Power supply interruption events must be flagged separately from equipment malfunction claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R013	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Multi-Entity Claim Consolidation and Splitting	The system must support scenarios where a single incident generates claims across multiple entities. For example, a vessel collision at Jebel Ali may generate cargo claims against the UAE entity and hull claims against the Qatar entity. The system must allow linking related claims into a claim group with a master reference while maintaining independent processing workflows per entity. Conversely, a single claim may need to be split across multiple policies or coverage types (hull, cargo, liability) with proportional cost allocation. Consolidated reporting must aggregate linked claims showing total exposure across entities in USD equivalent while preserving individual entity views in local currency. Intercompany settlement entries must be automatically generated when claims cross entity boundaries, respecting transfer pricing rules applicable under GCC and Indian tax regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R014	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Regulatory Reporting for Maritime Authorities	The system must generate regulatory incident and claim reports required by maritime authorities in each operational jurisdiction. For Qatar Maritime Administration, the system must produce incident reports conforming to QMSA format requirements. For UAE Federal Transport Authority and Abu Dhabi Maritime, reports must follow their prescribed electronic submission formats. Saudi Transport General Authority (TGA) reporting requirements must be met for KSA-registered claims. Indian Director General of Shipping (DGS) prescribed formats must be supported for claims at JNPT. All regulatory reports must be generated in the authority's required language (Arabic for QA/AE/SA, English for IN) with supporting documentation attachments. The system must track submission deadlines, maintain filing receipts, and flag claims requiring mandatory authority notification based on value thresholds or incident severity classification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R015	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Claim Communication Log and Correspondence Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive communication log for each claim capturing all inbound and outbound correspondence including emails, letters, phone call summaries, meeting notes, and system-generated notifications. Each communication entry must record timestamp, participants, communication channel, direction (inbound/outbound), language (Arabic or English), and content summary. The system must support generating templated correspondence in both Arabic and English for standard claim communications: acknowledgment of claim receipt, request for additional documentation, survey appointment notification, liability determination notice, settlement offer, and claim closure confirmation. Templates must be entity-specific with appropriate legal disclaimers per jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). All correspondence must be linked to the claim file and searchable via full-text search supporting both Arabic and English queries. The system must enforce a communication SLA tracking mechanism alerting adjusters when claimant communications remain unanswered beyond the entity-configured response window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-001-R016	FEAT-029-2-001	MOD-029	Claim Reserve Management and Financial Provisioning	The system must support setting and adjusting claim reserves (financial provisions) for outstanding claims. Initial reserves must be automatically set based on claim type and estimated quantum from the AI damage assessment or manual adjuster input. Reserve amounts must be recorded in the claim's original currency with parallel tracking in USD and the entity's functional currency. Reserve adjustments must follow an approval workflow where changes exceeding a configurable percentage (default 20%) or absolute threshold require supervisor authorization. The system must maintain a complete reserve history showing each adjustment with timestamp, adjusted amount, reason code, and approver identity. Month-end reserve adequacy reports must be generated per entity comparing total outstanding reserves against actuarial benchmarks. The system must integrate with the general ledger module to post reserve entries, reversals, and adjustments in compliance with IFRS 17 insurance contract accounting standards applicable across QA, AE, SA, and IN operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R001	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Subrogation Opportunity Identification Engine	The system must automatically analyze settled claims to identify subrogation recovery opportunities based on liability determination outcomes. When a claim settlement identifies a third party (carrier, stevedore, port operator, freight forwarder) as partially or fully liable, the system must generate a subrogation case linked to the original claim. The engine must evaluate recovery viability by considering factors including the liable party's financial standing, applicable limitation periods per jurisdiction (three years under Qatar Maritime Law, two years under Indian Carriage of Goods by Sea Act, one year under Hague-Visby Rules for cargo claims), evidence strength score, and estimated recovery cost versus potential recovery amount. Cases with a net recovery ratio below a configurable threshold (default 1.5x) must be flagged for cost-benefit review. The system must support bulk identification across multiple settled claims and prioritize opportunities by expected recovery value in USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R002	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Recovery Agent Assignment and Performance Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of approved recovery agents, law firms, and correspondents across all operational jurisdictions with their specializations (maritime law, cargo recovery, hull claims), jurisdiction coverage (Qatar courts, UAE DIFC courts, Saudi Board of Grievances, Indian Admiralty courts at Bombay High Court), fee structures (contingency percentage, fixed fee, hourly rate), and historical recovery success rates. Agent assignment must consider the claim jurisdiction, liable party location, claim value, and agent's track record for similar claim types. The system must track agent performance metrics including average recovery time, recovery percentage achieved, cost efficiency ratio, and communication responsiveness. Fee calculations must support multi-currency billing with automatic conversion at the agent-agreed exchange rate date. Performance reviews must trigger automatic ranking adjustments affecting future assignment priority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R003	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Demand Letter Generation and Tracking	The system must auto-generate subrogation demand letters addressed to liable third parties using jurisdiction-specific legal templates. Each template must incorporate the correct legal basis citations: Qatar Maritime Law provisions, UAE Federal Law references, Saudi Commercial Maritime Law articles, or Indian Carriage of Goods by Sea Act sections as applicable. Demand letters must be produced in the appropriate language (Arabic for QA/AE/SA jurisdictions with English translation, English for Indian jurisdiction) and include claim summary, liability basis, quantum demanded with currency specification, supporting evidence references, response deadline (configurable per jurisdiction, default 30 days), and escalation consequences. The system must track demand letter delivery (postal tracking, email read receipts), response receipt, and counterparty replies. Follow-up reminders must be automatically generated at configurable intervals. A complete correspondence trail must be maintained with version control for each demand iteration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R005	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Tug Assistance Coordination	The system must manage tug booking and coordination for vessels requiring tug assistance during berthing and unberthing operations. Tug requirements must be auto-calculated based on vessel DWT, LOA, wind speed thresholds, and port-specific regulations. For example, vessels above 200m LOA at Hamad Port require a minimum of two tugs for berthing. The system must interface with tug operators to check availability, book tugs with specified bollard pull requirements, and confirm tug assignments. Tug cost estimates must be generated in the port's local currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT) and converted to the vessel principal's preferred billing currency using the configured exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R004	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Negotiation and Settlement Tracking Workflow	The system must provide a structured negotiation workflow for subrogation cases supporting multiple rounds of offer and counter-offer between the recovering entity and the liable party. Each negotiation round must capture the offer amount in original currency, USD equivalent, percentage of original claim, terms and conditions proposed, response deadline, and negotiator notes. The system must calculate and display the negotiation progression showing movement from initial demand to current position as a percentage. Settlement authority levels must be configurable per entity: adjusters up to a specified amount, managers up to a higher threshold, and director approval required above that. Settlement agreements must be digitally signed with jurisdiction-appropriate electronic signature validity (Qatar eCommerce Law, UAE Electronic Transactions Law, Saudi Electronic Transactions Law, India IT Act 2000). Accepted settlements must automatically trigger recovery payment tracking workflows and update the original claim's financial records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R005	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Litigation Management and Court Proceedings Tracker	The system must support tracking subrogation cases that escalate to litigation across multiple court systems including Qatar Civil Courts, UAE Federal Courts, DIFC Courts, Saudi Commercial Courts, Board of Grievances, and Indian Admiralty Courts at Bombay High Court for JNPT-related claims. Case tracking must capture filing date, case number, court name, assigned judge (where public), appointed legal counsel, hearing dates displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and procedural milestones (filing, service, defense, discovery, hearing, judgment, appeal). The system must calculate and track limitation periods per jurisdiction with advance warning notifications at 180, 90, 30, and 7 days before expiry. Legal cost tracking must capture attorney fees, court fees, expert witness costs, and translation costs in local currency with USD equivalent. Court document uploads must support Arabic documents with OCR text extraction for searchability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R006	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Salvage Recovery Value Tracking	The system must track salvage and recovery operations for damaged cargo and containers, recording recovered goods inventory, condition assessment, and realized salvage value. For damaged containers, the system must interface with the container asset register to update container status (damaged, under repair, scrapped) and track repair cost versus scrap value decisions. Salvage sale proceeds must be recorded against the original claim with automatic reduction of net claim cost. The system must support multiple salvage disposal methods: auction, private sale, scrap, and donation, each with appropriate documentation requirements. Currency handling must support recording salvage proceeds in the sale currency with automatic conversion to the claim's base currency and USD equivalent using the exchange rate on the date of salvage sale. Salvage buyers registry must be maintained per port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) with buyer ratings and historical transaction records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R007	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	General Average Contribution Claims Processing	The system must handle General Average (GA) declarations under York-Antwerp Rules, processing both claims made by the entity and contributions owed. When a GA is declared, the system must identify all cargo interests from the affected voyage's bill of lading manifest and calculate contributory values based on CIF value at destination. The system must track GA adjuster appointments (typically appointed by the shipowner), bond and guarantee submissions from cargo interests, and provisional payment requirements. For the entity's own cargo, the system must generate GA guarantee letters referencing the applicable insurance policy. The system must track the GA adjustment process which may span multiple years, maintaining interim payment records and final settlement calculations. Currency handling must manage GA contributions calculated in the adjustment currency (typically USD) with conversion to each entity's functional currency. The system must generate GA status reports showing outstanding contributions receivable and payable across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R008	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Recovery Payment Receipt and Reconciliation	The system must track recovery payments received from liable third parties, matching them against outstanding subrogation demands. Payment receipt recording must capture payer details, payment date, amount received in payment currency, applicable exchange rate, bank charges deducted, and net amount credited. The system must support partial recoveries with automatic calculation of remaining balance and recovery percentage achieved. Multi-currency reconciliation must handle scenarios where the original claim was in QAR, the demand was issued in USD, and payment is received in AED, performing necessary conversions using the rates applicable on the payment date. Recovery proceeds must be automatically allocated: first to deductible reimbursement to the insured, then to the insurer's claim payment recovery, following the jurisdiction-specific subrogation priority rules. The system must generate recovery accounting entries for posting to the general ledger, distinguishing between principal recovery, interest recovery, and cost recovery components.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R009	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Limitation Period and Prescription Monitoring	The system must maintain a comprehensive limitation period tracking engine for all recovery actions across jurisdictions. The engine must calculate prescription deadlines based on the applicable legal framework: one year from delivery date under Hague-Visby Rules for cargo claims, two years under the Indian Limitation Act for maritime claims, three years under Qatar Civil Code for general commercial claims, and varying periods under UAE and Saudi law depending on claim category. The system must account for limitation period extensions, suspensions (e.g., during negotiation under Saudi law), and interruptions (e.g., by filing suit under Qatar law). A dashboard must display all recovery cases approaching limitation with color-coded urgency indicators (green >90 days, yellow 30-90 days, red <30 days). Automated alerts must notify recovery managers and assigned legal counsel at configurable intervals. The system must support recording protective filing actions (filing suit to preserve rights even while negotiating) and track their status separately. All limitation calculations must account for Hijri calendar impacts where limitation periods are defined in Hijri years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R012	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Cargo Operations Planning Integration	The system must integrate port call planning with cargo operations by linking each port call to its associated cargo manifest, stowage plan, and container loading or discharge lists. For container vessels, the system must display expected container moves (load, discharge, restow, shift) in TEU and FEU broken down by category (full, empty, reefer, hazmat, out-of-gauge). For bulk vessels, the cargo plan must show parcel details with commodity, quantity in MT, and hold allocation. The AI engine must use cargo complexity data (number of moves, hazmat presence, reefer monitoring requirements) to predict cargo operations duration and factor this into the overall port call timeline. Any changes to the cargo plan must trigger automatic recalculation of estimated berth occupancy time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R010	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Inter-Entity Recovery Claim Settlement	The system must manage recovery claims between group entities where one entity has a subrogation right against another entity within the same corporate group. For example, the UAE entity operating at Jebel Ali may have a recovery claim against the Qatar entity for damage caused during transshipment at Hamad Port. Inter-entity claims must follow a simplified settlement workflow with automated liability determination based on operational records (gate surveys, tally reports, container tracking). Settlement amounts must be calculated in USD as the intercompany reference currency and then converted to each entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using the group's approved transfer pricing exchange rates. The system must generate intercompany debit and credit notes, track approval from both entity controllers, and create the corresponding accounting entries. All intercompany recovery transactions must be flagged for elimination during group consolidation reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R011	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Recovery Cost-Benefit Analysis Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time cost-benefit analysis dashboard for all active and completed recovery actions. For each recovery case, the dashboard must display original claim value, total recovery costs incurred (legal fees, surveyor fees, agent commissions, court costs, translation costs), amounts recovered to date, net recovery value, and return on recovery investment ratio. Aggregate views must summarize recovery performance by entity (QA/AE/SA/IN), by claim type, by liable party category, by recovery agent, and by jurisdiction. The dashboard must include trend analysis showing recovery rate changes over configurable periods (monthly, quarterly, annual) with both Gregorian and Hijri period options. Projections must estimate expected recovery from active cases based on historical completion rates for similar cases. All monetary values must be displayable in the user's preferred currency with real-time conversion. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with appropriate chart labeling and data formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R012	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Waiver of Subrogation Rights Management	The system must support recording and enforcing contractual waivers of subrogation rights that exist in service agreements with ports, terminals, and shipping lines. When a claim is settled and subrogation opportunity identification runs, the system must check the liable party against the waiver registry and automatically flag cases where subrogation rights have been contractually waived. Waiver records must capture the contract reference, waiver scope (full or limited to specific claim types or value thresholds), effective period, applicable entity, and authorizing signatory. The system must alert claim managers when a waiver blocks a potentially high-value recovery (above configurable threshold, default USD 50,000) for review of waiver validity and scope. Waiver expiry must trigger notifications to commercial and legal teams for renegotiation consideration. The system must support Arabic and English contract references and display waiver terms in the user's selected interface language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R013	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Recovery Proceeds Distribution to Co-Insurers	The system must manage distribution of recovery proceeds to co-insurers and reinsurers based on their participation shares in the original risk. When a claim was covered under a co-insurance arrangement, recovery proceeds must be allocated proportionally to each insurer's participation percentage. The system must generate distribution statements showing gross recovery, deductions for recovery costs (allocated proportionally or as agreed in the co-insurance terms), and net distributable amount per participant. Distribution payments must be tracked with each co-insurer's payment confirmation. For claims with reinsurance recoveries, the system must calculate the treaty or facultative reinsurer's share of subrogation recoveries and generate the appropriate credit advice. All distributions must be calculated in the policy currency and converted to each participant's settlement currency using the agreed exchange rate basis. The system must maintain a full audit trail of distribution calculations and payment records for regulatory inspection by insurance supervisory authorities in QA, AE, SA, and IN.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R014	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Bulk Recovery Campaign Management	The system must support organizing and executing bulk recovery campaigns targeting a specific liable party across multiple related claims. For example, if a terminal operator at Jebel Ali is found liable for damage across 50 separate cargo claims over a quarter, the system must enable grouping these claims into a single recovery campaign with consolidated demand. Campaign creation must filter claims by liable party, port, date range, claim type, and value threshold. The system must generate a consolidated demand schedule listing each underlying claim with reference, quantum, and supporting summary. Campaign-level negotiation must allow block settlement with proportional allocation back to individual claims. Campaign performance tracking must show total demanded versus recovered with breakdowns by individual claim. Financial settlement of campaign recoveries must handle scenarios where individual claims are in different currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) while the campaign settlement may be in USD. The system must support both Arabic and English campaign correspondence and reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R015	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Recovery Action Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a tamper-proof audit trail for all recovery and subrogation actions capturing every state change, decision, communication, and financial transaction. The audit log must record actor identity, action timestamp (UTC with local timezone display), action type, previous state, new state, and justification. Compliance reports must be generatable per regulatory requirements: Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority guidelines for insurance recovery practices, UAE Insurance Authority directives, Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority (SAMA) insurance regulations, and Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) subrogation reporting requirements. The system must support regulatory examination workflows where auditors can be granted read-only access to specific recovery case files with time-limited access tokens. All audit reports must be available in both Arabic and English with certified translation attestation capability. Data retention for recovery audit trails must comply with the most stringent applicable retention period across all four jurisdictions (minimum 10 years).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R011	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Multi-Entity Crew Change Allocation	The system must correctly allocate crew change operations and their associated costs to the appropriate legal entity based on the port of operation. Crew changes at Hamad Port must be allocated to the Qatar entity, operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port to the UAE entity, operations at Saudi ports (SAKAC, SADMM) to the KSA entity, and JNPT operations to the India entity. When a single crew change involves multiple ports across entities (e.g., sign-off at Jebel Ali and sign-on at JNPT), the system must split costs proportionally and generate inter-company charges with proper transfer pricing documentation. Each entity must have its own chart of accounts mapping for crew change expense categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-002-R016	FEAT-029-2-002	MOD-029	Automated Recovery Workflow Escalation and SLA Monitoring	The system must enforce configurable SLA timelines for each stage of the recovery process: subrogation identification within 7 days of claim settlement, demand letter issuance within 14 days of identification, follow-up at 30-day intervals, escalation to litigation review at 90 days without response, and case closure review at 365 days. SLA parameters must be independently configurable per entity (QA/AE/SA/IN) to account for differing legal process timelines in each jurisdiction. Breached SLAs must trigger automated escalation: first to the assigned recovery officer, then to the recovery team lead, and finally to the claims director. The escalation chain must be entity-specific and support out-of-office delegation. The system must generate weekly SLA compliance dashboards showing on-time performance by entity, by recovery agent, and by claim type. SLA calculations must account for public holidays in each jurisdiction including Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha) that vary by country, UAE National Day, Qatar National Day, Saudi National Day, and Indian public holidays. Dashboard rendering must support Arabic RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R001	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Predictive Claim Risk Scoring for Shipments	The system must assign a real-time claim risk score (0-100) to each active shipment based on a multi-factor AI model. Input factors must include cargo commodity type and value, container type and age (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, flat rack, open top, tank), origin-destination trade lane risk profile, seasonal weather patterns affecting the route, vessel age and classification society rating, historical claim frequency for the specific carrier and trade lane, port congestion levels at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and declared INCOTERMS 2020 terms affecting risk transfer points. The risk score must be recalculated at each shipment milestone (booking confirmed, gate-in, vessel loaded, transshipment, discharge, gate-out). Shipments scoring above a configurable high-risk threshold (default 75) must generate automated alerts to operations, insurance, and commercial teams in both Arabic and English. The model must achieve minimum 80% prediction accuracy validated against historical claim data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R002	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Weather and Natural Hazard Impact Prediction	The system must integrate real-time weather data and natural hazard forecasting services to predict weather-related claim risks for active voyages and port operations. The system must monitor tropical cyclones in the Arabian Sea affecting routes between JNPT and Gulf ports, shamal wind events affecting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali operations, fog events impacting port visibility and berthing operations, and extreme heat events affecting reefer container performance during summer months in the Gulf region where ambient temperatures exceed 50°C. When weather hazards are predicted to impact active shipments, the system must calculate the incremental claim risk, estimate potential exposure value in USD across all affected cargo, and recommend preventive actions (rerouting, delayed sailing, additional securing, reefer temperature adjustment). Historical weather-claim correlation models must be continuously updated. Alerts must be generated in both Arabic and English with hazard maps showing affected trade lanes and ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R003	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Container Condition Predictive Maintenance Alerts	The system must predict container condition deterioration that may lead to cargo damage claims by analyzing container lifecycle data including age, last inspection date and results, repair history, previous cargo types carried (particularly hazardous or corrosive commodities), and cumulative mileage. For reefer containers (types 22R1, 42R1, 45R1), the system must additionally analyze compressor runtime hours, refrigerant levels, historical temperature deviation frequency, and power unit age. The predictive model must identify containers with elevated failure probability and recommend preemptive withdrawal from service for inspection or repair before they are assigned to high-value cargo bookings. Alerts must be generated when a container predicted to have greater than 30% failure probability within the next 90 days is allocated to a booking at any operational port (QAHAM, AEKLP, AEJEA, INJNP). The system must calculate the expected claim cost avoidance from each preemptive intervention. Maintenance recommendations must integrate with the container maintenance scheduling system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R004	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Trade Lane Risk Profiling and Trend Analysis	The system must build and maintain dynamic risk profiles for each trade lane connecting operational ports, analyzing historical claim data to identify patterns and trends. Trade lanes must be defined at multiple granularity levels: port-to-port (e.g., AEJEA to INJNP), country-to-country (UAE to India), and regional (GCC to South Asia). Risk profiles must capture claim frequency per TEU, average claim severity, predominant claim types, seasonal variation patterns, and trend direction (improving, stable, deteriorating). The system must identify emerging risk patterns such as increasing pilferage on specific routes, new congestion-related damage patterns at specific terminals, or carrier-specific performance degradation. Risk profiles must factor in INCOTERMS distribution for the trade lane to distinguish shipper-risk versus carrier-risk claims. The analysis must be refreshed weekly and presented in both Arabic and English with interactive visualizations showing heatmaps of claim density across the operational port network. Anomaly detection must flag statistically significant deviations from baseline risk levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R005	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	AI-Recommended Preventive Actions Engine	The system must generate specific, actionable preventive recommendations based on predicted claim risks. Recommendations must be categorized by type: operational (additional lashing, dunnage requirements, container pre-trip inspection), routing (alternative transshipment port, weather avoidance waypoints), timing (delayed shipment to avoid monsoon peak, avoiding port congestion windows), packaging (additional moisture protection, shock indicators, temperature monitors), and insurance (additional coverage for high-risk segments, specific exclusion waivers). Each recommendation must include the predicted risk reduction percentage, estimated cost of implementing the preventive measure, expected claim cost avoidance, and net benefit calculation in USD with equivalent in the entity's functional currency. Recommendations must reference the specific risk factors they address and prioritize by net benefit. The system must track recommendation acceptance or rejection by operations teams and correlate subsequent claim outcomes to validate recommendation effectiveness over time. Recommendations must be presentable in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R006	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Dynamic Insurance Premium Optimization	The system must use predictive analytics to recommend optimal insurance coverage levels and structures based on the entity's risk profile and claim history. The optimization engine must analyze the correlation between premium spend and claim recoveries across coverage types (marine cargo, hull and machinery, P&I, war risks, strikes) for each entity (QA, AE, SA, IN). The system must model alternative program structures including varying deductible levels, coverage limits, and co-insurance percentages to identify the optimal risk retention versus transfer balance. Premium optimization must consider multi-currency impacts where premiums are paid in USD but claims are settled in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with exchange rate volatility factored in. The system must generate annual renewal preparation reports showing loss ratio trends, benchmark comparisons against industry averages for the GCC and Indian maritime sectors, and recommended program adjustments. Reports must be produced in both Arabic and English for presentation to insurance brokers and underwriters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R007	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Real-Time Cargo Monitoring Integration for Early Warning	The system must integrate with IoT cargo monitoring devices and platforms to receive real-time alerts that may indicate an impending claim situation. Monitored parameters must include container GPS location deviations from planned route, door opening sensors indicating unauthorized access, shock and tilt sensors exceeding configured thresholds for fragile cargo, temperature excursions for reefer containers, and humidity levels for moisture-sensitive cargo. When monitoring data indicates a potential claim-triggering event, the system must automatically create a pre-claim incident record capturing the sensor data, timestamp, location (geocoded with nearest port reference), affected container and booking details, and cargo value at risk. The system must calculate the probability of the incident resulting in a claim based on historical sensor-to-claim correlation data. High-probability incidents (above configurable threshold, default 60%) must trigger immediate notification to the claims team, operations, and the relevant insurer per the policy's incident notification requirements. All IoT data must be preserved as potential evidence for subsequent claim processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R008	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Carrier and Vendor Risk Assessment Model	The system must maintain AI-driven risk assessments for all carriers, terminal operators, stevedoring companies, trucking firms, and warehouse operators used across the operational network. Risk assessment factors must include historical claim frequency attributed to the vendor, claim severity distribution, response time to claim notifications, cooperation level during investigations (rated by adjusters), financial stability indicators, safety certifications (ISM Code compliance, ISO 28000, AEO status), and regulatory compliance record. The model must generate a composite vendor risk score (A through E rating) updated monthly. Vendor risk scores must be accessible to procurement and operations teams when selecting service providers for specific shipments. The system must flag when a high-value cargo booking is allocated to a vendor with a risk rating below a configurable threshold (default C). Vendor risk trend reports must show rating changes over time with contributing factor analysis. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English with vendor names displayed in both scripts where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R009	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Seasonal and Cyclical Claim Pattern Forecasting	The system must develop and maintain forecasting models for seasonal and cyclical claim patterns specific to the GCC-India maritime corridor. The model must identify recurring patterns including monsoon-season damage increases for JNPT operations (June through September), Ramadan period operational changes affecting handling quality, extreme summer heat impacts on cargo at Gulf ports (May through October), year-end volume surges creating congestion-related damage, and holiday period staffing reductions affecting cargo security. The forecasting model must produce monthly claim volume and value predictions for each entity (QA, AE, SA, IN) with confidence intervals, broken down by claim type. Forecasts must be used to proactively adjust insurance coverage limits, reserve allocations, and claims team staffing levels. The system must compare actual claim outcomes against forecasts and display forecast accuracy metrics. Hijri calendar events must be incorporated into the seasonal model as they shift through the Gregorian calendar. All forecast reports must be available in Arabic and English with appropriate calendar format display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R010	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Machine Learning Model Training and Validation Pipeline	The system must provide a managed pipeline for training, validating, and deploying the AI prediction models used across claims prediction features. The pipeline must support model versioning with rollback capability, A/B testing of model variants against production traffic, and automated retraining on a configurable schedule (default monthly) using the latest claim data. Training data must be sourced from historical claims across all four entities with appropriate data anonymization per each jurisdiction's data protection requirements (Qatar Personal Data Privacy Law, UAE Data Protection Law, Saudi Personal Data Protection Law, India Digital Personal Data Protection Act). Model validation must use held-out test sets representing each entity proportionally and evaluate accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score per claim type. Models must be validated for bias across entities, trade lanes, and cargo types to ensure no systematic under-prediction or over-prediction for any segment. Model performance dashboards must display metrics in both Arabic and English. An approval workflow must gate model deployment to production requiring sign-off from the claims analytics team lead.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R011	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Port Congestion Impact on Claims Prediction	The system must model the relationship between port congestion levels and claim frequency and severity for each operational port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT). The model must ingest real-time port congestion indicators including vessel waiting time, berth occupancy rate, yard utilization percentage, truck turnaround time, and gate queue length. Historical analysis must establish correlation coefficients between congestion metrics and claim types (container damage from double-stacking under pressure, reefer power supply interruptions from yard congestion, delay claims from missed connections, cargo deterioration from extended dwell time). When real-time congestion indicators exceed thresholds associated with elevated claim risk (configurable per port), the system must proactively alert operations and insurance teams with estimated incremental exposure value. The system must recommend contingency actions such as diverting cargo to less congested alternative terminals within the same port complex or adjusting reefer monitoring frequency. All alerts and recommendations must be generated in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R012	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Claim Causation Network Analysis	The system must perform root cause network analysis across historical claims to identify systemic causation patterns that span multiple operational touchpoints. The analysis must map causal chains from upstream events (booking errors, incorrect cargo declarations, inadequate packaging at origin) through operational events (rough handling, improper stowage, temperature excursions, transit delays) to claim outcomes (cargo damage, shortage, contamination, deterioration). The network must identify common root causes that contribute to multiple claim types and quantify the total financial impact of each root cause across all entities in USD. The system must generate causal pathway visualizations showing the frequency and financial impact of each link in the causation chain. When a new root cause is identified (appearing in more than a configurable number of claims, default 5, within a 90-day window), the system must alert the quality and operations management teams. Causation analysis must distinguish between port-specific patterns (e.g., handling issues at a specific terminal) and systemic patterns (e.g., packaging inadequacy for a commodity type across all ports). Reports must support Arabic and English display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R013	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Customer Claim Propensity Scoring	The system must maintain a claim propensity score for each customer (shipper, consignee, freight forwarder) based on their historical claim behavior and cargo characteristics. The scoring model must consider claim frequency per shipment volume, average claim value relative to cargo value, claim types distribution, documentation quality at booking (complete versus incomplete cargo declarations), packaging compliance rate, and claim outcome patterns (approved versus rejected). The propensity score must inform commercial decisions by flagging customers with elevated claim likelihood during booking acceptance, enabling proactive risk mitigation measures such as mandatory pre-shipment surveys, enhanced packaging requirements, or adjusted freight rates incorporating insurance risk loading. Customer scores must be entity-specific (a customer may have different risk profiles across QA, AE, SA, IN operations due to different trade lanes and cargo types). Privacy controls must ensure propensity scores are only accessible to authorized commercial and claims personnel. The system must comply with customer data processing requirements under applicable data protection laws across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R014	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Geopolitical and Sanctions Risk Impact on Claims	The system must incorporate geopolitical risk factors and sanctions compliance considerations into claims prediction models. The system must monitor trade disruption risks including regional geopolitical tensions affecting shipping lanes in the Arabian Gulf and Gulf of Oman, sanctions lists (UN, US OFAC, EU, GCC) that may affect cargo insurance coverage validity, war risk area designations by the Joint War Committee (JWC) that trigger additional premium requirements, and piracy risk areas reported by the International Maritime Bureau (IMB). When a shipment transits a newly designated high-risk area, the system must reassess the claim risk score and verify that insurance coverage remains valid, alerting the insurance team if additional war risk or strikes coverage is required. The system must track war risk premium surcharges applied to specific voyages and correlate with actual claim outcomes. All geopolitical risk alerts must be generated in both Arabic and English with referenced source citations and effective dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R015	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Automated Underwriting Data Package Generation	The system must automatically generate comprehensive data packages for insurance underwriting submissions using predictive analytics outputs. The underwriting package must include five-year claims history summarized by entity, trade lane, cargo type, and claim cause with trend analysis; current risk profile scores for major trade lanes and customer segments; loss ratio analysis by coverage type (marine cargo, hull, P&I) and entity; predictive claims forecast for the upcoming policy period with confidence intervals; risk improvement actions implemented and their measured effectiveness; and fleet and container condition summaries from predictive maintenance data. The package must be formatted per major marine insurance market requirements including Lloyd's of London, Dubai International Financial Centre (DIFC) insurers, and Indian marine insurance market standards. Data must be presented in both tabular and graphical formats with executive summaries in Arabic and English. The system must support generating the package in presentation format (PDF) and machine-readable format (structured JSON/XML) for automated underwriting platforms. All financial data must be presented in USD with local currency equivalents per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R016	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Predictive Model Explainability and Transparency Dashboard	The system must provide an explainability layer for all AI prediction outputs, enabling claims managers and underwriters to understand and trust the model's predictions. For each shipment risk score, the system must display the top contributing factors ranked by impact magnitude with SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) or equivalent feature attribution values. The explanation must show how each factor (weather, carrier rating, cargo type, route history, container condition) contributed positively or negatively to the risk score relative to the baseline. The system must support what-if analysis allowing users to modify input parameters (e.g., change carrier, adjust routing, add preventive measures) and see the predicted impact on the risk score in real time. An overall model health dashboard must display aggregate metrics including prediction accuracy, calibration curves, feature importance rankings, and data quality indicators for the training pipeline. All explanations and dashboards must be fully localized in Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts. The explainability features must satisfy regulatory requirements for AI transparency where applicable under emerging AI governance frameworks in the GCC region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-003-R017	FEAT-029-2-003	MOD-029	Offline Prediction Capability for Remote Operations	The system must provide lightweight offline prediction capability for operations teams at locations with unreliable internet connectivity, particularly relevant for inland depot operations in India feeding JNPT and remote warehouse locations in Saudi Arabia. The offline module must include a cached version of the current prediction model with the most recent risk parameters for active trade lanes. Offline risk scoring must function for new shipment assessments using locally cached reference data with a clearly displayed data freshness indicator showing last synchronization timestamp. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize offline risk assessments with the central system, reconcile any scoring differences due to model updates that occurred during the offline period, and flag assessments where the score changed by more than a configurable threshold (default 15 points). The offline module must support both Arabic and English interfaces and handle date display in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Cache size must be optimized to function on standard mobile devices with limited storage capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R001	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Executive Loss Prevention Dashboard	The system must provide an executive-level dashboard summarizing loss prevention performance across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with drill-down capability to individual port, trade lane, and customer levels. Key performance indicators must include total claims value (current period versus prior period with percentage change), loss ratio by entity and coverage type, claims frequency per 1000 TEU handled, average claim processing time, recovery rate percentage, and claims reserve adequacy ratio. The dashboard must support period selection in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats with standard periods (monthly, quarterly, annual, YTD) and custom date ranges. All monetary values must be displayable in USD (default), QAR, AED, SAR, or INR based on user preference with real-time conversion. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with all chart labels, legends, and tooltips properly localized. Executive commentary sections must support bilingual input and display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R002	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Loss Ratio Trend Analysis and Benchmarking	The system must calculate and display loss ratio trends (claims incurred divided by premiums earned) across multiple dimensions: by entity, by coverage type (marine cargo, hull, P&I, war risks), by trade lane, by cargo commodity category, and by customer segment. Trend analysis must cover configurable historical periods up to ten years with the ability to overlay multiple dimension views for comparison. The system must support benchmarking against industry averages sourced from published maritime insurance market reports for the GCC region (from the Emirates Insurance Association, Qatar Financial Centre Authority) and Indian market (from IRDAI annual reports). Benchmark data must be manually updatable by authorized users with source documentation. The system must identify statistically significant deviations from benchmarks and historical trends, generating automated commentary explaining potential contributing factors. Loss ratio calculations must handle multi-currency scenarios correctly, converting all values to a common reporting currency using the average exchange rate for the reporting period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R003	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Claims Heat Map by Port and Terminal	The system must generate interactive geographic heat maps showing claim concentration and severity across all operational ports and their individual terminals. The heat map must overlay claim data onto port layout maps for Hamad Port (including container terminals and general cargo berths), Khalifa Port (ADPC terminals and KIZAD logistics zone), Jebel Ali (DP World terminals 1-4 and Jebel Ali Free Zone), and JNPT (BMCT, NSIGT, APMT, and JNPCT terminals). Claim density must be visualized using color gradients representing claim frequency per TEU throughput, with separate layers for claim value severity. Users must be able to filter by time period, claim type, container type, and cargo category. The heat map must identify specific high-risk zones within each port such as particular yard blocks, berth segments, or gate areas with elevated claim frequency. Clicking on a hot zone must drill down to the underlying claim records. The map interface must support Arabic and English labels and place names with proper transliteration consistency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R004	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Cargo Category Loss Analysis Report	The system must produce detailed loss analysis reports segmented by cargo category using the Harmonized System (HS) code classification at chapter level (2-digit) with drill-down to heading level (4-digit). For each cargo category, the report must display total shipment volume and value, number of claims filed, total claim value, claim frequency rate, average claim severity, predominant claim causes, and trend over the reporting period. The report must highlight the top 10 highest-loss cargo categories across each entity with specific analysis of contributing factors. Special attention must be given to high-risk categories prevalent in the GCC-India trade including petrochemicals (HS 27-29), foodstuffs (HS 01-24), electronics (HS 84-85), automobiles (HS 87), and textiles (HS 50-63). Commodity-specific loss prevention recommendations must be generated based on the analysis. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English with HS code descriptions displayed bilingually. All financial figures must show original transaction currency alongside USD equivalent for cross-entity comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R005	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Prevention Measure Effectiveness Tracking	The system must track and measure the effectiveness of implemented loss prevention measures by comparing claim metrics before and after intervention. Each prevention measure must be registered with its implementation date, applicable scope (entity, port, trade lane, cargo type, or customer), nature of intervention (process change, equipment upgrade, training program, packaging standard update, vendor change), estimated implementation cost in local currency with USD equivalent, and expected claim reduction target. The system must calculate actual claim reduction by comparing the configured baseline period (default 12 months pre-intervention) against the post-intervention period, controlling for volume changes and seasonal variations. ROI calculation must compare implementation cost against documented claim cost avoidance. The system must generate quarterly effectiveness reports ranked by ROI, identifying the most and least effective interventions. When a measure shows no statistically significant improvement after a configurable evaluation period (default 6 months), the system must alert the loss prevention team for review. Reports must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R006	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Regulatory Insurance Compliance Reporting	The system must generate all mandatory insurance and claims reports required by regulatory authorities in each operational jurisdiction. For Qatar: Qatar Central Bank insurance supervision reports and Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority returns covering premium volumes, claims statistics, and solvency positions. For UAE: Central Bank of UAE insurance reporting requirements and DFSA regulatory returns for DIFC-based operations. For KSA: Saudi Central Bank (formerly SAMA) quarterly and annual insurance statistical returns including claims development triangles and reserve adequacy reports. For India: IRDAI prescribed formats for marine insurance business statistics, claims ratio reports, and policyholder grievance reports. Each report must be generated in the authority's prescribed format and language (Arabic for QA/AE/SA with English translations, English for India). The system must maintain a regulatory calendar tracking submission deadlines with advance notifications at 30, 14, and 7 days. Report generation must be automated with manual review and approval workflow before submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R007	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Container Damage Pattern Analysis	The system must analyze container damage claims to identify recurring damage patterns by container type, age, manufacturer, and operational history. The analysis must categorize damage by location on the container (roof, walls, floor, doors, corner castings, locking mechanisms) and damage type (dents, holes, corrosion, structural deformation, refrigeration unit failure). For each pattern identified, the system must correlate with operational factors including handling equipment type used, terminal operator, vessel stowage position (on-deck versus under-deck, tier, bay), and transit route. The system must generate container type-specific damage frequency reports comparing standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC) against specialized units (flat racks 20FR/40FR, open tops 20OT/40OT, tank containers 22T0, and reefers 22R1/42R1/45R1). Damage pattern reports must identify root causes amenable to preventive action such as specific handling equipment causing above-average damage rates at a particular terminal. Reports must include photographic evidence aggregation showing typical damage examples for each identified pattern. All reports must support Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R008	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Claims Development Triangle Reporting	The system must generate actuarial claims development triangles (also known as loss development triangles or run-off triangles) for each entity and coverage type. Development triangles must show claims by accident year (both Gregorian and Hijri year options) with development periods at quarterly intervals showing paid claims, incurred claims (paid plus reserved), and claim counts. The system must calculate age-to-age development factors (link ratios) and apply standard actuarial projection methods (chain ladder, Bornhuetter-Ferguson, expected loss ratio) to estimate ultimate claims cost. IBNR (incurred but not reported) reserves must be calculated using the selected projection method with confidence intervals at 75th, 90th, and 95th percentiles. The system must support manual actuarial adjustments with documented justification overlaid on the algorithmic projections. Reports must comply with IFRS 17 insurance contract measurement requirements applicable across all four jurisdictions. All monetary values must be presented in the entity's functional currency with USD equivalent columns. Triangle reports must be exportable to Excel format for external actuarial review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R009	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Customer Claims Experience Report	The system must generate customer-level claims experience reports for use in commercial negotiations, premium setting, and customer risk management. For each customer, the report must show total shipment volume and value over a configurable period, number of claims filed and their outcomes (approved, partially approved, rejected, withdrawn), total claim payments made, loss ratio relative to freight revenue attributed to the customer, claim frequency per TEU, and comparison against the portfolio average. The report must highlight customers with deteriorating claims experience trends and those with improving trends. For customers operating across multiple entities (QA, AE, SA, IN), the report must offer both consolidated and entity-specific views. Customer reports must be shareable with the customer in a sanitized format excluding internal investigation notes, fraud scores, and proprietary risk assessments. Reports must be generated in the customer's preferred language (Arabic or English) with appropriate currency display (customer's billing currency alongside USD equivalent). The system must support automated distribution of quarterly claims experience reports to key account managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R010	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Carrier Performance Claims Scorecard	The system must produce comparative carrier performance scorecards based on claims data across all operational entities and ports. For each carrier (shipping line, feeder operator), the scorecard must display total TEU carried, number of claims attributed, total claim value in USD, claim frequency per 1000 TEU, average claim severity, predominant claim types, average response time to claim notifications, and cooperation rating during investigations. Carriers must be ranked overall and within each trade lane served, with performance bands (top quartile, above average, average, below average, bottom quartile). The scorecard must identify specific vessels within a carrier's fleet that generate above-average claims for container damage or cargo loss. Trend lines must show carrier performance changes over quarterly periods. Scorecards must be usable by commercial teams during carrier contract negotiations and by operations teams for vessel planning and allocation decisions. The system must integrate carrier scorecard data with the booking system to flag high-claims-risk carrier allocations. Reports must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R011	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Monthly Loss Prevention Management Report	The system must auto-generate a comprehensive monthly loss prevention management report consolidating key metrics across all entities. The report structure must include: executive summary with key highlights and lowlights, total claims registered and settled during the month by entity, claims pipeline aging analysis showing open claims by age bracket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 91-180, 181-365, >365 days), reserve movement summary (opening reserves, new reserves set, reserve adjustments, settlements, closing reserves) by entity in local currency and USD, recovery activity summary showing amounts recovered and pending by entity, top 10 largest open claims with current status and expected resolution timeline, prevention measures implemented and their early effectiveness indicators, and upcoming risk alerts from the AI prediction system. The report must be generated in both Arabic and English versions with consistent formatting and data. Report distribution must be automated to a configurable stakeholder list with entity-specific sections visible only to authorized recipients. The report must support PDF export with corporate branding per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R012	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Ad-Hoc Query and Report Builder	The system must provide a self-service ad-hoc query and report builder enabling authorized users to create custom analyses from the claims data warehouse without requiring technical SQL knowledge. The query builder must offer a drag-and-drop interface supporting dimensions including entity, port, terminal, trade lane, vessel, carrier, customer, cargo category, container type, claim type, claim cause, claim status, date period, and coverage type. Measures must include claim count, claim value, reserve amount, recovery amount, loss ratio, claim frequency, and average severity. The builder must support calculated fields using standard arithmetic operations. Output formats must include tabular data, bar charts, line charts, pie charts, and pivot tables. Saved queries must be shareable across teams with configurable access permissions. The query interface must function in both Arabic RTL and English LTR modes with dimension and measure labels localized. Exported reports must support Excel, PDF, and CSV formats with Arabic text encoding properly handled in CSV exports using UTF-8 BOM.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R013	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Annual Insurance Program Performance Review Report	The system must generate a comprehensive annual insurance program performance review for each entity and consolidated across the group. The review must cover all coverage lines (marine cargo, hull and machinery, P&I, war risks, loss of hire, freight demurrage and defense) with analysis including: premium spend by coverage line and entity in local currency and USD, claims incurred versus premiums (loss ratio) with five-year trend, claims development showing current year versus prior years at the same development stage, large loss analysis for claims exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 100,000) with individual case summaries, recovery performance showing net loss ratio after recoveries, coverage gap analysis identifying incidents not covered or underinsured, broker and insurer service performance evaluation, and recommendations for the upcoming renewal. The report must incorporate AI prediction outputs showing expected claims for the coming year to inform renewal negotiations. Report format must follow insurance market conventions suitable for presentation to Lloyd's, DIFC, and Indian market underwriters. Dual language versions in Arabic and English must be generated simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R014	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Environmental and Sustainability Claims Impact Report	The system must track and report on claims with environmental impact dimensions including oil spills, chemical leaks, hazardous material incidents, and container losses at sea. For each environmental claim, the report must capture the environmental impact classification (minor, moderate, major per IMO MARPOL categories), remediation actions taken, environmental cleanup costs, regulatory fines imposed by maritime authorities in QA, AE, SA, or IN, and third-party environmental damage claims. The system must calculate the entity's environmental claims footprint including total environmental incident count, aggregate environmental claim cost in USD, and trend analysis. The report must track compliance with each jurisdiction's environmental regulations: Qatar Environmental Protection Law, UAE Federal Law on Environmental Protection, Saudi Environmental Law, and India Merchant Shipping Act environmental provisions. The system must generate ESG-aligned environmental risk metrics suitable for inclusion in corporate sustainability reports. Reports must be available in Arabic and English with environmental incident location mapping across operational ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R015	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Claims Data Export and Integration API	The system must provide a comprehensive data export and integration API enabling claims analytics data to be consumed by external business intelligence tools, actuarial software, and corporate reporting platforms. The API must support RESTful endpoints with JSON and XML response formats, and bulk data export in CSV and Parquet formats for data warehouse integration. Data export must respect entity-level access controls ensuring that a user authorized for Qatar entity data cannot access UAE, KSA, or India entity records unless explicitly granted multi-entity access. The API must support parameterized queries filtering by entity, date range, claim type, status, and value range. Currency conversion must be available as an API parameter allowing consumers to receive all monetary values in a specified target currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Date fields must be available in both Gregorian ISO 8601 format and Hijri calendar format based on a request parameter. The API must enforce rate limiting (configurable, default 1000 requests per hour per API key), require OAuth 2.0 authentication, and log all data access for audit compliance. API documentation must be available in English with Arabic field name mappings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R016	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Automated Anomaly Detection in Claims Patterns	The system must implement automated statistical anomaly detection across all claims data streams to identify unusual patterns requiring management attention. Anomaly detection must monitor multiple metrics simultaneously including daily claim registration volume, average claim value, claim type distribution shifts, geographic concentration changes, carrier-specific claim spikes, and customer claim frequency deviations. The detection engine must use configurable statistical methods (Z-score, IQR, isolation forest) with entity-specific sensitivity tuning to account for different operational scales at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. When an anomaly is detected, the system must generate an alert containing the anomalous metric, its current value versus expected range, severity classification (informational, warning, critical), potential explanations based on correlated events (weather events, vessel incidents, port disruptions), and recommended investigation actions. Alerts must be delivered in both Arabic and English to configurable recipient lists per entity. The system must maintain an anomaly log with investigation outcome feedback to improve detection accuracy and reduce false positive rates over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R017	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Board-Level Risk and Insurance Summary Report	The system must generate a quarterly board-level summary report designed for presentation to the board of directors and audit committee. The report must provide a consolidated group view across all entities (QA, AE, SA, IN) with a maximum length of 10 pages. Content must include: total insured asset value and premium spend as percentage of asset value with year-over-year comparison, key risk exposures by category (natural catastrophe, operational, geopolitical, cyber) with estimated maximum probable loss scenarios, loss performance summary showing actual versus budgeted claims cost by entity, significant claims and incidents during the quarter with status updates on claims exceeding USD 500,000, insurance market outlook and renewal strategy summary, regulatory compliance status across all four jurisdictions, and AI prediction highlights including top emerging risks and recommended board-level actions. The report must use executive-appropriate visualizations (consolidated trend charts, risk heat matrices, geographic exposure maps). Financial figures must be presented in USD millions with entity breakdowns available as appendices. The report must be generated in both Arabic and English with identical formatting and content, suitable for printing and digital distribution to board members.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R013	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Weather and Tide Impact Assessment	The system must integrate weather forecast data (wind speed, wind direction, wave height, visibility, precipitation) and tide tables for each port to assess impact on port call operations. The system must define configurable thresholds per port for safe berthing conditions: for example, maximum wind speed of 25 knots for container vessels at Jebel Ali, maximum wave height of 1.5 meters for pilot boarding at JNPT during monsoon season. When forecast conditions exceed configured thresholds during the planned port call window, the system must automatically generate weather alerts and recommend schedule adjustments. Tide windows for draft-restricted vessels must be calculated using UKC (Under Keel Clearance) requirements specific to each berth and channel, with the system suggesting optimal arrival and departure windows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-029-2-004-R018	FEAT-029-2-004	MOD-029	Scheduled Report Distribution and Notification Engine	The system must provide a comprehensive report scheduling and distribution engine enabling automated generation and delivery of all analytics reports on configurable schedules. Users must be able to schedule any standard or custom report for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, or annual generation with specific delivery dates and times accounting for business days in each entity's jurisdiction. Distribution must support multiple channels including email (with PDF or Excel attachment), secure file share upload, in-app notification with report link, and API webhook notification for system integrations. Each report schedule must define the recipient list with entity-based access validation ensuring recipients only receive data for their authorized entities. The scheduler must account for calendar differences across jurisdictions: weekend days (Friday-Saturday for QA/AE/SA, Saturday-Sunday for IN), public holidays per entity, and Hijri calendar date triggers for reports aligned to Islamic calendar periods. Failed report generation must trigger automated retry with exponential backoff and notify the system administrator after three consecutive failures. Report generation audit logs must record schedule execution, generation status, distribution confirmations, and access tracking for compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R003	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Berth Allocation Request and Confirmation	The system must generate berth allocation requests to the respective port authority or terminal operator for each port call. The request must include vessel particulars, cargo type, estimated cargo volume (TEU for containers, MT for bulk), requested berth window (from-to), preferred berth number or terminal, and any special requirements (reefer plugs, hazmat berth, crane specifications). The system must track the berth allocation status through stages: Requested, Tentatively Allocated, Confirmed, Modified, and Cancelled. Berth allocation confirmations received from port authorities must be parsed and matched to the correct port call automatically. Notifications must be sent to the operations team upon each status change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R006	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	AI-Driven ETA Prediction and Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must continuously refine vessel ETA predictions by ingesting AIS position data, weather forecasts (wind, wave height, current), vessel speed profiles, and historical port approach patterns. The system must detect anomalies such as unexpected speed reductions, course deviations, or prolonged anchorage waits and automatically flag these to the port agency team with severity levels (informational, warning, critical). For each flagged anomaly, the AI must generate a probable cause analysis and a recommended action. ETA predictions must achieve a target accuracy of plus or minus 2 hours when the vessel is within 48 hours of arrival, and this accuracy metric must be tracked and reported monthly per port and per vessel operator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R007	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Timeline and Milestone Tracking	The system must maintain a structured timeline for each port call with the following mandatory milestones: EOSP (End of Sea Passage), NOR Tendered (Notice of Readiness), Pilot on Board, First Line Ashore, All Fast, Cargo Operations Commenced, Cargo Operations Completed, Pilot on Board (Departure), Last Line Cast Off, and COSP (Commencement of Sea Passage). Each milestone must record planned time, actual time, variance in hours, and the user or system that recorded the timestamp. The AI engine must learn from historical milestone data to predict future milestone timings for new port calls. The timeline must be viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats with full Arabic localization for display in Qatar, KSA, and UAE entity interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R008	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Anchorage Management and Waiting Time Tracking	The system must track vessels waiting at anchorage before berthing, recording anchor drop time, anchor aweigh time, anchorage position coordinates, and the reason for anchorage (berth congestion, tide restriction, cargo readiness, documentation delay, customs hold). Anchorage waiting time must be calculated automatically and classified as either counting toward laytime or not, based on the charter party terms configured for the voyage. The system must display a real-time anchorage dashboard showing all vessels at anchorage per port with color-coded urgency indicators. For ports with designated anchorage areas (such as Hamad Port outer anchorage or JNPT anchorage zones A through D), the system must validate that reported positions fall within the correct designated zone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R009	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Multi-Entity Port Call Ownership and Billing Segregation	The system must support port calls being owned by different legal entities within the multi-entity structure (Qatar entity, UAE entity, KSA entity, India entity). When a port call is created, the owning entity must be automatically determined based on the port of call but must be overridable by authorized users for cases like cross-entity agency appointments. All costs, revenues, and disbursement accounts associated with the port call must be segregated by entity for financial reporting. Inter-entity charges for shared services (such as a Qatar-based coordinator managing a UAE port call) must be automatically generated as inter-company transactions with the appropriate transfer pricing markup as configured in the entity management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R010	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Dashboard with Real-Time Vessel Positions	The system must provide a real-time port call dashboard displaying all active and upcoming port calls across all four ports on an interactive map. Vessel positions must be updated via AIS data feed at intervals no greater than 5 minutes. The dashboard must support filtering by port, vessel operator, vessel type, cargo type, port call status, and date range. Each vessel marker on the map must show a tooltip with vessel name, IMO number, current status, ETA or ETD, and assigned berth. The dashboard must be accessible in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts, with all labels, status descriptions, and date formats properly localized. Dashboard must remain functional in offline mode by caching the last known vessel positions and port call data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R011	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Document Repository	The system must maintain a document repository for each port call, organizing all related documents into predefined categories: Vessel Certificates, Crew Documents, Cargo Documents, Port Authority Submissions, Customs Declarations, Health and Safety, and Miscellaneous. Each document must be tagged with upload timestamp, uploader identity, document type, expiry date (where applicable), and verification status (unverified, verified, expired, rejected). The system must enforce mandatory document requirements per port: for example, ISPS (International Ship and Port Facility Security) certificates and maritime declarations of health are required for all ports, while specific food import permits are additionally required for JNPT. The AI must scan uploaded documents using OCR to extract key data fields and auto-populate related forms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R014	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Cost Estimation and Proforma DA Generation	The system must generate proforma Disbursement Account (DA) estimates for each port call based on the port tariff schedules configured for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Cost categories must include port dues, berth hire, pilotage, towage, mooring and unmooring, anchorage dues, agency fees, launch hire, fresh water supply, and miscellaneous charges. Each cost line must calculate in the local port currency (QAR, AED, INR) and convert to USD and the principal's preferred currency using the exchange rate effective on the ETA date. The proforma DA must be auto-generated when the port call is confirmed and must be revisable as port call parameters change. The system must maintain version history of all proforma DA revisions with change reasons tracked.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R015	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Port Call Status Workflow and Approval Gates	The system must enforce a configurable workflow for port call status transitions: Draft, Submitted, Confirmed, Vessel Arrived, Operations In Progress, Operations Completed, Vessel Departed, and Closed. Each status transition must be governed by approval gates that validate prerequisite conditions: for example, transitioning to Confirmed requires berth allocation confirmation and approved proforma DA; transitioning to Vessel Arrived requires actual arrival timestamp and NOR tendered. Role-based permissions must control which users can trigger each transition per entity. The workflow must support parallel approval paths where multiple stakeholders (operations manager, finance controller, compliance officer) must approve before certain transitions. All status changes must be logged in an immutable audit trail with timestamp, user, previous status, new status, and any comments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-001-R016	FEAT-030-1-001	MOD-030	Automated Notifications and Stakeholder Alerts	The system must send automated notifications to configured stakeholders at each port call milestone and status change. Notification channels must include email, SMS, in-app notifications, and WhatsApp Business API integration. Notification templates must be configurable per port and per event type, supporting both English and Arabic content with dynamic field substitution (vessel name, port, ETA, berth number). Stakeholder notification groups must be definable per vessel operator and per port, allowing different distribution lists for different principals. The system must support notification escalation rules: if a critical milestone is missed by more than a configurable threshold (for example, vessel not arrived within 6 hours of confirmed ETA), the system must escalate notifications to senior management. Notification delivery status must be tracked and failed deliveries must be retried with configurable retry policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R001	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Crew Change Request Management	The system must manage crew sign-on and sign-off requests for vessels calling at any of the four ports. Each crew change request must capture seafarer details (full name, nationality, passport number, CDC number, rank, joining or departing), travel itinerary (flight details, hotel bookings if overnight stay required), visa requirements based on nationality and port country, and transport arrangements (launch hire for anchorage crew changes or road transport for alongside crew changes). The system must validate visa requirements against the port country's immigration rules: for example, Indian nationals require a pre-arranged visa for Qatar and UAE, while GCC nationals have visa-free entry. The AI must auto-detect potential visa issues and flag them before the crew change request is submitted to immigration authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R002	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Immigration and Shore Pass Processing	The system must generate immigration pre-arrival documentation for crew changes at each port, complying with local immigration authority requirements. For Qatar (Hamad Port), the system must interface with Qatar's Ministry of Interior e-services for shore pass applications. For UAE ports (Jebel Ali and Khalifa), the system must support Federal Authority for Identity and Citizenship (ICA) smart gate registration. For JNPT, the system must generate FRRO (Foreigners Regional Registration Office) documentation for crew sign-on and sign-off. Each shore pass application must track status through stages: Application Submitted, Under Review, Approved, Denied, Expired. The system must maintain a database of nationality-specific visa and shore pass requirements for each port country, with automated reminders when regulations change. All immigration forms must support bilingual Arabic and English data entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R003	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Medical Assistance and Evacuation Coordination	The system must manage medical assistance requests for crew members requiring medical attention during port calls. The request must capture patient details, symptoms or medical condition description, urgency level (routine, urgent, emergency), and whether the crew member can walk or requires stretcher evacuation. The system must maintain a directory of approved medical service providers (hospitals, clinics, dentists, pharmacies, ambulance services) for each port area with their contact details, operating hours, and specialties. For medical evacuations (MEDEVAC), the system must coordinate launch services, ambulance booking, and hospital admission in a structured workflow. Medical expenses must be captured with supporting documentation (invoices, medical reports) and categorized for P&I Club insurance claims. All medical records must be handled in compliance with data privacy regulations applicable in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R004	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Cash to Master Disbursement Processing	The system must process cash-to-master (CTM) requests where the vessel principal requests cash delivery to the vessel master during a port call. Each CTM request must capture the requested amount, requested currency (USD, EUR, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or other), delivery method (cash, bank transfer to master's account, or pre-paid card), and delivery timing. The system must apply the configured exchange rate for currency conversion, calculate the agency's CTM handling fee, and generate a CTM receipt for the master's signature. For cash deliveries, the system must enforce anti-money-laundering (AML) thresholds per jurisdiction: amounts exceeding the configured threshold must trigger enhanced due diligence checks and compliance officer approval. CTM advances must be tracked as receivables against the principal's account and included in the final Disbursement Account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R001	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Crew Change Request Registration	The system must allow port agents to register crew change requests specifying vessel IMO number, voyage reference, port of crew change (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNHA,SAKAC, SADMM), expected arrival date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, number of sign-on crew, number of sign-off crew, and nationalities of all seafarers involved. Each request must capture the vessel's flag state, P&I club details, and the manning agency contact information. The system must generate a unique crew change reference number with port prefix and sequential numbering, and must support bulk upload of crew lists in CSV or Excel format conforming to IMO FAL Form 5 structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R005	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Spare Parts and Ship Stores Delivery Coordination	The system must manage the delivery of spare parts, ship stores, and provisions to vessels during port calls. Each delivery request must capture item descriptions, quantities, dimensions, weight, customs classification (HS codes), origin country, and whether the items require customs clearance or qualify for ship stores exemption under international maritime conventions. The system must coordinate with port security for gate passes and vehicle entry permits to the terminal area. For items requiring customs clearance, the system must generate or link to customs declarations and track clearance status. Delivery scheduling must be coordinated with vessel cargo operations to avoid conflicts with crane movements or restricted access periods. The system must capture proof of delivery (signed receipts, photographs) and associate delivery costs with the correct port call for disbursement accounting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R006	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Bunker Coordination and Fuel Oil Delivery	The system must coordinate bunkering operations for vessels requiring fuel oil, diesel oil, or lubricating oil during port calls. The bunker coordination record must capture fuel type (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LSMGO), quantity ordered in metric tons, delivery method (barge, pipeline, truck), nominated bunker supplier, and delivery window. The system must verify that the nominated bunker supplier holds valid licenses for the respective port and that the fuel specification complies with IMO 2020 sulphur cap regulations (0.50% maximum sulphur content unless the vessel has a scrubber fitted). Bunker delivery reports (BDR) must be uploaded and linked to the port call record. Any quantity disputes between vessel figures and barge figures must be flagged for resolution. Bunker costs must be captured in the supplier's invoicing currency and converted to the principal's currency for disbursement purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R007	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Vessel Repair and Maintenance Service Coordination	The system must manage requests for vessel repair and maintenance services during port calls, including hull cleaning, propeller polishing, underwater inspection, engine repairs, electrical work, and safety equipment servicing. Each service request must capture the scope of work description, required specialists or trades, estimated duration, safety requirements (hot work permits, confined space entry permits), and whether the work requires class surveyor attendance. The system must maintain a directory of approved repair contractors and service providers for each port with their certifications, insurance coverage, and performance ratings. Quotations from multiple vendors must be managed within the system for comparison and principal approval. Completed work must be documented with service reports, photographs, and invoices linked to the port call disbursement record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R008	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Launch and Boat Hire Service Management	The system must manage launch (boat) hire services for transporting personnel, stores, and equipment between shore and vessels at anchorage or outer harbor positions. Launch bookings must capture the purpose (crew change, stores delivery, surveyor visit, agent boarding), number of passengers, cargo weight and dimensions, requested pickup time and location, and vessel anchorage position. The system must interface with approved launch service providers at each port to check availability and confirm bookings. Launch hire charges must be calculated based on the port's tariff structure (which may include base hire, waiting time charges, overtime rates for bookings outside normal working hours, and surcharges for hazardous cargo). For ports with multiple launch operators (such as JNPT), the system must support operator selection based on availability, cost, and service quality ratings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R009	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Crew Welfare and Shore Leave Coordination	The system must support crew welfare services including shore leave arrangements, crew transport to and from designated seafarer welfare centers, SIM card procurement, currency exchange facilitation, and crew recreation bookings. For each port, the system must maintain information about available crew welfare facilities (seafarer missions, international seafarer centers, recreation areas) with operating hours and available services. Shore leave requests must be validated against immigration restrictions: some nationalities may be denied shore leave at certain ports based on bilateral agreements. The system must generate shore leave pass requests to port security and immigration, tracking approval status. Transport arrangements for crew going ashore must be coordinated with approved transport providers, and associated costs must be captured per port call.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R010	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Husbandry Service Provider Registry and Rating	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of husbandry service providers across all four port locations, categorized by service type (medical, transport, launch hire, provisions, ship chandlery, repair contractors, bunker suppliers, immigration agents, customs brokers). Each provider entry must include company name in English and Arabic, trade license number, license validity dates, contact persons, phone numbers, email addresses, physical address, bank account details for payments, and performance rating. The AI must analyze historical service delivery data (response time, quality ratings from vessel masters, cost competitiveness, complaint frequency) to generate provider performance scores and recommend preferred providers for each service type. Providers with expired licenses or consistently poor ratings must be automatically flagged for review and potential deactivation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R011	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Husbandry Service Cost Capture and Invoicing	The system must capture all costs associated with husbandry services and link them to the respective port call for inclusion in the Disbursement Account. Each cost entry must record the service provider, service description, invoice number, invoice date, invoice amount in the provider's currency, exchange rate applied, equivalent amount in the port call's base currency and the principal's billing currency. The system must support uploading scanned copies of original invoices and receipts as supporting documentation. Cost entries must be categorized according to a standardized chart of husbandry cost categories (BIMCO Disbursement Account format) to ensure consistency across all four port locations. The AI must detect unusual cost patterns (charges significantly above historical averages for the same service at the same port) and flag them for review before inclusion in the final DA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R004	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Shore Pass & Port Entry Permit Management	The system must manage shore pass applications for crew members requiring temporary port area access or shore leave. For Hamad Port, the system must interface with QTerminals security protocols and generate shore pass requests in Arabic and English. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, the system must comply with DP World and Abu Dhabi Ports security requirements respectively. For JNPT, the system must generate CISF-compliant port entry permits. Each shore pass record must capture the seafarer's photograph, passport details, vessel berth number, validity period (maximum 72 hours unless extended), and sponsoring agent details. The system must auto-expire shore passes and send return-to-vessel reminders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R012	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Vessel Provisions and Fresh Water Supply	The system must manage orders for vessel provisions (food supplies, bonded stores, cabin stores) and fresh water supply during port calls. Provision orders must capture item lists with quantities, delivery date and time, and any dietary or religious requirements (halal certification is mandatory for vessels loading provisions in Qatar, UAE, and KSA). Fresh water supply requests must specify quantity in metric tons, delivery method (barge or pipeline), and required quality standard. The system must coordinate with approved ship chandlers and water supply companies, generating purchase orders and tracking delivery confirmations. For bonded stores (duty-free cigarettes, alcohol), the system must ensure compliance with customs bonded stores regulations at each port, noting that alcohol provisions may be restricted or prohibited at certain ports in the GCC region depending on local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R013	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Surveyor and Inspection Visit Coordination	The system must coordinate visits by classification society surveyors, P&I Club correspondents, flag state inspectors, PSC (Port State Control) inspectors, and cargo surveyors during port calls. Each visit request must capture the surveyor organization, surveyor name, purpose of survey (annual survey, intermediate survey, damage survey, cargo survey, ISM audit, ISPS audit, MLC inspection), requested date and time, estimated duration, and access requirements (which holds, tanks, or areas need to be accessed). The system must coordinate gate passes, terminal access permits, and transport arrangements for surveyors. Survey reports and findings must be uploaded and linked to the port call and vessel records. For PSC inspections, the system must track any detentions or deficiencies noted and their rectification status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R014	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Waste Disposal and Slop Reception Coordination	The system must manage waste disposal and slop reception services for vessels during port calls in compliance with MARPOL regulations (Annexes I through VI). The system must capture the types and estimated quantities of waste for disposal: oily waste (Annex I), noxious liquid substances (Annex II), garbage categories as per revised Annex V (plastics, food waste, domestic waste, cooking oil, operational waste, cargo residues), and sewage (Annex IV). For each port, the system must maintain a list of approved waste reception facility operators with their licenses and accepted waste types. Waste disposal requests must be submitted to port authorities as required, and waste disposal receipts must be collected and linked to the vessel's garbage record book entries. Costs for waste disposal must be captured per waste category and included in the port call disbursement account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R015	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Husbandry Service Request Workflow and Principal Approval	The system must enforce a workflow for husbandry service requests that includes principal approval for services exceeding configurable cost thresholds. The workflow stages must be: Request Received, Cost Estimated, Awaiting Principal Approval, Approved, Service Ordered, Service Delivered, Invoice Received, and Closed. For services below the cost threshold, automatic approval must be applied to avoid delays. Principals must be able to approve or reject requests via email response, web portal, or mobile application. Rejected requests must capture the rejection reason and allow for resubmission with modifications. The system must track SLA metrics for each workflow stage (time from request to estimation, time from estimation to approval, time from approval to delivery) and generate performance reports. Escalation rules must be configurable so that requests pending approval beyond a specified duration are escalated to alternative approvers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-002-R016	FEAT-030-1-002	MOD-030	Offline Husbandry Service Recording	The system must support offline recording of husbandry service activities for agents operating in areas with limited or no internet connectivity, such as onboard vessels at anchorage or in remote port areas. The offline mode must allow agents to create service requests, record service delivery confirmations, capture photographs of receipts and delivery documents, and enter cost data on mobile devices without network connectivity. All offline data must be stored locally on the device with encryption and automatically synchronized with the central system when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution rules must handle scenarios where the same port call record was modified both offline and online: the system must flag conflicts for manual resolution rather than silently overwriting data. The offline capability must function for a minimum of 72 hours without synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R001	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Dynamic Pre-Arrival Checklist Generation	The system must automatically generate a pre-arrival checklist for each port call based on the port of call, vessel type, cargo type, vessel flag state, and any special conditions (first call at port, hazmat cargo, livestock carrier, vessel under PSC detention at previous port). The checklist must be composed of configurable checklist items drawn from a master checklist template library. Each checklist item must have a title, description, responsible party (agent, vessel, principal, port authority), due date relative to ETA (for example, 72 hours before ETA, 48 hours before ETA, 24 hours before ETA), priority level (mandatory, recommended, optional), and completion status. The AI engine must learn from historical port call data to suggest additional checklist items that were frequently added manually for similar port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R002	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Port-Specific Regulatory Checklist Templates	The system must maintain port-specific regulatory checklist templates for each of the four ports. For Hamad Port (Qatar), the template must include Qatar Customs pre-arrival manifest submission (72 hours), Mwani port entry application, QCDD (Qatar Civil Defence Department) hazmat notification if applicable, and Ministry of Public Health notification for vessels from disease-affected regions. For Jebel Ali (UAE), the template must include Dubai Customs manifest submission, DP World terminal booking confirmation, and FTA (Federal Transport Authority) vessel entry permit. For JNPT (India), the template must include Indian Customs IGM (Import General Manifest) filing (24 hours), DGLL (Directorate General of Lighthouses and Lightships) light dues payment, and Port Health Officer clearance. Each template must be version-controlled with effective dates to track regulatory changes over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R005	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Transportation & Hotel Booking Coordination	The system must coordinate ground transportation for signing-on and signing-off crew between the airport, hotel, and port. Transportation requests must capture pickup location, drop-off location, number of passengers, luggage count, preferred vehicle type, and scheduled time. For hotel bookings, the system must record hotel name, room type, check-in and check-out dates, meal plan, and cost per night in local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India). The system must maintain an approved vendor list for transportation and hotels per port, with negotiated rates stored in the system. AI must recommend optimal logistics plans based on flight arrival times, port distance, and cost efficiency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R003	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Automated Pre-Arrival Document Collection from Vessels	The system must automatically request required pre-arrival documents from the vessel master or vessel operator via email at configurable intervals before the ETA. The document request must specify exactly which documents are needed, the required format (PDF, Excel, specific IMO form numbers), and the submission deadline. Required documents typically include crew list (IMO FAL Form 5), passenger list (IMO FAL Form 6), ship's stores declaration (IMO FAL Form 3), crew effects declaration (IMO FAL Form 4), maritime declaration of health (IMO FAL Form 7), cargo declaration (IMO FAL Form 2), dangerous goods manifest, last ten ports of call, and ballast water reporting form. The system must track which documents have been received, which are overdue, and send automated reminder notifications for missing documents. Received documents must be validated for completeness using AI-based document analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R004	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Checklist Progress Dashboard and Completion Tracking	The system must provide a visual dashboard showing the completion status of pre-arrival checklists across all active port calls. The dashboard must display each port call with a progress bar indicating the percentage of mandatory checklist items completed, a count of overdue items, and the time remaining until the vessel's ETA. Clicking on a port call must expand to show all individual checklist items with their status (not started, in progress, completed, waived, blocked). The dashboard must support filtering by port, vessel operator, due date range, and completion status. Color-coded indicators must highlight port calls where mandatory items are overdue (red), approaching deadline (amber), or all completed (green). The dashboard must be accessible in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with proper alignment of progress indicators and status labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R005	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Pre-Arrival Notification to Port Authorities	The system must automatically generate and submit pre-arrival notifications to port authorities at the required advance notice periods. For vessels calling at Hamad Port, a 72-hour advance notice must be submitted to Mwani Qatar including vessel particulars, ETA, cargo summary, crew and passenger counts, and last ports of call. For Jebel Ali, a 48-hour vessel arrival notification must be submitted through DP World's CATOS or equivalent terminal operating system. For JNPT, pre-arrival notifications must be submitted to the Mumbai Port Trust and JNPT traffic department. Each notification must be formatted according to the receiving authority's specified format and transmitted via the required channel (email, web portal submission, EDI, or API). The system must track submission confirmation receipts and flag any notifications that were not acknowledged by the port authority within the expected timeframe.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R006	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Customs Pre-Arrival Manifest Submission	The system must manage the submission of pre-arrival customs manifests to the customs authority at each port. For Qatar Customs, the advance cargo manifest must be filed electronically via the Qatar Single Window (Al Nadeeb) system at least 24 hours before vessel arrival. For UAE ports, the customs manifest must be submitted through Dubai Trade or the relevant emirate's customs portal. For JNPT, the Import General Manifest (IGM) must be filed with Indian Customs through ICEGATE (Indian Customs Electronic Gateway) at least 24 hours prior to vessel arrival. The system must generate the manifest data from linked cargo documentation, validate it against customs requirements (mandatory fields, HS code validation, consignee details), and submit it electronically. Amendment submissions for manifest corrections must be supported with tracking of amendment numbers and approval status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R007	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Health and Quarantine Pre-Arrival Screening	The system must assess health and quarantine risk for each arriving vessel based on the vessel's recent port call history, reported illness on board, and current WHO health advisories. The Maritime Declaration of Health (MDH) submitted by the vessel master must be analyzed by the AI engine to detect any reported illnesses, deaths, or symptoms among crew and passengers that may require quarantine intervention. For vessels arriving from ports in countries with active disease outbreaks (as per WHO IHR notifications), the system must automatically flag the port call for enhanced health screening and notify the port health authority. The system must generate port health officer notification forms specific to each port's health authority requirements. Vessels with clean MDH and no risk factors must be flagged for free pratique eligibility to expedite port entry clearance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R008	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	ISPS Security Pre-Arrival Notification	The system must manage the International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code pre-arrival security notifications for all vessel port calls. The ISPS notification must include the vessel's current ISPS security level, the last ten ports of call with their ISPS security levels, any special or additional security measures taken during the last ten port calls, and details of any ship-to-ship activities conducted. The notification must be submitted to the port's Designated Authority (DA) at least 24 hours before arrival or, for voyages of less than 24 hours duration, before departure from the previous port. The system must validate that the vessel's International Ship Security Certificate (ISSC) is valid and not expired before allowing the ISPS notification to be submitted. If the vessel has called at a port operating at ISPS Security Level 2 or 3 within the last ten calls, the system must automatically escalate the security notification to the port facility security officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R009	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Ballast Water Management Pre-Arrival Reporting	The system must manage ballast water management reporting as required by the IMO Ballast Water Management Convention (BWM Convention) and local port regulations. The pre-arrival ballast water report must capture ballast water tanks on board with their capacity and current volume, ballast water source ports and coordinates, ballast water exchange positions (latitude, longitude, date, method used: sequential, flow-through, or dilution), and whether the vessel is equipped with a type-approved ballast water management system (BWMS). For ports in regions with invasive species concerns, the system must validate that ballast water exchange was conducted in open ocean (minimum 200 nautical miles from shore and 200 meters depth) or that BWMS treatment was completed to D-2 standard. The ballast water report must be submitted to the port authority as part of the pre-arrival documentation package, and any non-compliance must be flagged for port state control attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R010	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Dangerous Goods Pre-Arrival Declaration	The system must manage pre-arrival declarations for dangerous goods (DG) and hazardous materials being carried on board or planned for loading at the port. The DG declaration must capture IMDG Code class, UN number, proper shipping name, packing group, flash point where applicable, total quantity, packaging type, stowage location on the vessel, and emergency contact details. For each port, the system must validate the DG declaration against port-specific restrictions: certain IMDG classes may be prohibited or restricted at specific berths or terminals. For Hamad Port, DG notifications must comply with QCDD requirements. For JNPT, DG declarations must be filed through the Container Freight Station Management System. The system must generate DG stowage plans showing container positions and segregation compliance according to IMDG Code segregation table requirements. The AI must verify segregation rules between incompatible DG classes and flag any violations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R011	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Automated Stakeholder Notification Scheduling	The system must maintain a configurable notification schedule that automatically dispatches notifications to relevant stakeholders at predefined intervals relative to the vessel's ETA. The notification schedule must support multiple notification tiers: initial notification (typically 72 hours pre-arrival), update notification (48 hours), confirmation notification (24 hours), final notification (12 hours), and imminent arrival notification (6 hours). Each notification tier must be configurable per port and per stakeholder category (port authority, terminal operator, customs broker, stevedore, bunker supplier, ship chandler, crew change agent, P&I correspondent). The notification content must be dynamically generated based on the current port call data and updated at each tier. If the vessel's ETA changes by more than a configurable threshold (default 4 hours), the notification schedule must be automatically recalculated and affected stakeholders re-notified with updated timing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R012	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Checklist Item Dependency and Sequencing Rules	The system must support dependency relationships between checklist items where certain items cannot be started or completed until prerequisite items are finished. For example, customs manifest submission cannot be completed until the cargo manifest is received from the vessel operator, and berth confirmation cannot proceed until the port entry permit is approved. Dependencies must be defined as either hard (blocking: the dependent item cannot proceed at all) or soft (advisory: the dependent item can proceed with a warning). The system must visualize dependencies in a Gantt-chart style timeline view showing the critical path of checklist completion. When a prerequisite item is delayed, the system must automatically adjust the due dates of all dependent items and send updated notifications. The AI must detect circular dependencies during template configuration and prevent them from being saved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R013	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Pre-Arrival Checklist Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every checklist item action: creation, assignment, status change, waiver, and completion. Each audit entry must record the timestamp, user identity, action performed, and any supporting comments or documents. The system must generate compliance reports showing checklist completion rates by port, by vessel operator, by vessel type, and by time period. Reports must highlight patterns of late completions, frequently waived items, and common bottlenecks in the pre-arrival process. The AI must analyze compliance data to identify systemic issues and recommend process improvements. For regulatory audits (by port authorities or classification societies), the system must produce a formatted compliance report for a specified port call or date range demonstrating that all mandatory pre-arrival requirements were fulfilled with timestamps and responsible party identification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R014	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Hijri and Gregorian Dual Calendar Support for Deadlines	The system must support deadline calculation and display in both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar systems. For entities operating in Qatar, UAE, and KSA, checklist item due dates must be displayable in Hijri format (using the Umm al-Qura calendar as the standard Hijri reference) alongside the Gregorian date. During Ramadan and Eid periods, the system must automatically account for reduced port authority working hours when calculating deadline dates: for example, if a checklist item is due during Eid al-Fitr holidays in Qatar, the due date must be adjusted to the next working day with appropriate advance notice. The system must maintain a configurable public holiday calendar for each country (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) including both fixed and movable holidays (Islamic holidays, Diwali, Indian national holidays). All date inputs must accept both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and the system must validate cross-calendar date conversions for accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R015	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	AI-Powered Pre-Arrival Risk Assessment	The AI engine must perform an automated risk assessment for each port call during the pre-arrival phase, analyzing multiple risk dimensions: regulatory compliance risk (missing or expired certificates, incomplete documentation), operational risk (weather conditions, berth congestion, cargo complexity), security risk (ISPS level of previous ports, high-risk flag states, sanction screening), health risk (ports visited in disease-affected regions), financial risk (principal payment history, estimated DA amount versus credit limit), and environmental risk (ballast water compliance, emission control area requirements). Each risk dimension must be scored on a standardized scale (low, medium, high, critical) with AI-generated explanatory narratives. The composite risk score must be displayed prominently on the port call record and dashboard. Port calls classified as high or critical risk must trigger mandatory review by a senior operations manager before the vessel is accepted for agency services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R016	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Vessel Certificate Validity Verification	The system must verify the validity of all mandatory vessel certificates as part of the pre-arrival checklist. Mandatory certificates include Safety Management Certificate (SMC), Document of Compliance (DOC), International Ship Security Certificate (ISSC), International Oil Pollution Prevention Certificate (IOPPC), International Load Line Certificate, International Tonnage Certificate, SOLAS Safety Equipment Certificate, SOLAS Safety Radio Certificate, Civil Liability Convention (CLC) Certificate, and Bunker Convention Certificate. For each certificate, the system must check the expiry date against the vessel's planned arrival date, verify that the issuing authority is a recognized organization (RO) or flag state administration, and check whether the vessel's flag state is on the Paris MOU or Tokyo MOU grey or black lists. Expired or soon-to-expire certificates (within 30 days) must be flagged with warnings. The AI must cross-reference certificate data with public databases (Equasis, IMO GISIS) where API access is available to validate certificate authenticity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-003-R017	FEAT-030-1-003	MOD-030	Notification Template Management with Multilingual Support	The system must provide a notification template management interface allowing administrators to create, edit, and version-control notification templates for each pre-arrival notification type and each port. Templates must support both English and Arabic content with the ability to define the template body in both languages simultaneously. Dynamic placeholders must be supported for auto-populating vessel name, IMO number, port name, ETA, ETD, berth number, agent name, agent contact details, and any other port call data fields. Templates must support conditional content blocks that are included or excluded based on port call attributes: for example, dangerous goods notification paragraphs should only appear if the vessel is carrying hazmat cargo. Each template must have an effective date range to support version transitions when regulatory requirements change. The template preview function must render both the English and Arabic versions with placeholder data before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R001	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Centralized Communications Inbox and Threading	The system must provide a centralized communications inbox that aggregates all inbound and outbound communications with port authorities, customs, immigration, coast guard, and other regulatory bodies across all four ports. Communications must be automatically threaded by port call reference, linking related messages, submissions, responses, and follow-ups into a coherent conversation thread. Each communication must be tagged with the originating port authority or body, communication type (application, notification, declaration, response, query, approval, rejection), port call reference, and priority level. The inbox must support filtering by port, authority type, communication status (pending response, responded, closed), and date range. Unread and pending-response communications must be highlighted with visual indicators. The inbox must function in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with automatic detection of the language of incoming communications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R002	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Port Authority Application Submission Tracking	The system must track all applications and submissions made to port authorities with full lifecycle visibility. Application types must include port entry permit, berth allocation request, pilot booking, tug booking, hot work permit, diving permit, crane hire request, hazmat handling permit, and waste disposal request. Each application must track submission date and time, submission method (email, web portal, EDI, physical submission), reference number assigned by the port authority, current status (submitted, acknowledged, under review, approved, conditionally approved, rejected, withdrawn), expected response date based on the authority's published SLA, and actual response date. When responses are overdue (exceeding the expected SLA plus a configurable tolerance), the system must automatically generate follow-up reminders and escalation notifications. Historical application data must be analyzable to calculate average processing times per application type per port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R003	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	AI-Assisted Communication Drafting in Arabic and English	The AI engine must assist port agency staff in drafting formal communications to port authorities in both Arabic and English. When a user initiates a new communication, the AI must suggest a draft based on the communication type, the target authority, and the port call context. The draft must follow the formal correspondence conventions expected by each specific port authority: for example, communications to Mwani Qatar must follow their prescribed format and include the correct departmental address and reference conventions. The AI must support translation between Arabic and English, allowing the user to draft in one language and generate a properly formatted version in the other language. The AI must learn from previously sent and approved communications to improve draft quality over time. All AI-generated drafts must be clearly marked as drafts requiring human review and must not be auto-submitted without explicit user confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R004	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Regulatory Submission Calendar and Deadline Management	The system must maintain a regulatory submission calendar showing all upcoming submission deadlines for each active port call across all ports. The calendar must display deadlines for customs manifest submissions, port entry permit applications, ISPS notifications, dangerous goods declarations, health declarations, immigration notifications, and any other regulatory submissions. Each deadline must show the submission type, target authority, port call reference, vessel name, deadline date and time, and current preparation status (not started, in preparation, ready for review, submitted). The calendar must support views by day, week, and month in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Automated alerts must be generated at configurable intervals before each deadline (for example, 48 hours, 24 hours, 12 hours, and 2 hours before deadline). Missed deadlines must be escalated immediately to the operations manager and compliance officer with the potential regulatory consequences highlighted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R005	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Port Authority Fee and Tariff Reference Management	The system must maintain a reference database of port authority fee schedules and tariff structures for each of the four ports. The tariff database must include fee categories (port dues, berth hire, pilotage, towage, mooring, anchorage, light dues, garbage disposal), rate structures (per GRT, per NRT, per meter LOA, per hour, flat rate), applicable currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT), and effective dates. The system must support multiple concurrent tariff versions with effective date ranges to handle tariff revisions. When port authorities publish tariff updates, the system must allow administrators to enter new rates with future effective dates without disrupting current calculations. The system must calculate estimated port authority charges for each port call by applying the relevant tariff rates to the vessel's particulars and planned port call duration, supporting proforma DA preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R006	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Customs Authority Electronic Communication Interface	The system must interface with customs authority electronic systems at each port for manifest filing, customs declarations, and clearance status tracking. For Qatar, the system must integrate with the Al Nadeeb Single Window platform for all customs submissions. For UAE ports, integration must be with Dubai Trade (for Jebel Ali) and the relevant emirate customs portal (for Khalifa Port in Abu Dhabi). For JNPT, integration must be with ICEGATE for IGM filing, bill of entry processing, and shipping bill submissions. Each interface must handle authentication, message formatting according to the customs system's specifications (typically UN/EDIFACT or XML), submission, acknowledgment receipt, and error handling. When electronic submission fails, the system must log the error details, notify the responsible agent, and provide guidance on manual fallback procedures. All customs communications must be retained for the statutory retention period applicable in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R007	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Immigration Authority Communication Workflows	The system must manage structured communication workflows with immigration authorities at each port for crew change notifications, shore pass applications, and passenger disembarkation approvals. For Qatar, immigration communications must comply with Ministry of Interior requirements including advance crew list submission with passport copies and visa documentation. For UAE ports, the workflow must align with ICA (Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs and Port Security) procedures for seafarer visa issuance and shore pass processing. For JNPT, communications must follow FRRO and Bureau of Immigration requirements for seafarer sign-on and sign-off endorsements. Each immigration communication must track the complete lifecycle from initial submission through approval or rejection, with the ability to handle requests for additional information from the immigration authority. The system must auto-generate immigration departure clearance requests when a vessel's departure is confirmed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R008	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Coast Guard and Maritime Safety Authority Reporting	The system must manage required reports and notifications to coast guard and maritime safety authorities at each port. Report types must include vessel movement notifications, maritime incident reports, pollution incident reports (as required under MARPOL), search and rescue (SAR) coordination communications, and vessel traffic service (VTS) communications. For each port, the system must maintain the correct contact details and reporting formats for the relevant coast guard or maritime safety authority: Qatar Coast Guard, UAE Critical Infrastructure and Coastal Protection Authority (CICPA), Saudi Border Guard for KSA port calls, and Indian Coast Guard for JNPT. Incident report forms must capture incident type, date and time, location (coordinates), vessels involved, description, actions taken, and supporting evidence (photographs, AIS tracks). The system must ensure that time-critical reports (pollution incidents, SAR situations) can be transmitted immediately via the fastest available channel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R009	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Port Authority Response Parsing and Auto-Classification	The AI engine must automatically parse incoming communications from port authorities and classify them by type (approval, rejection, conditional approval, request for information, general notification, tariff update, regulatory change notice). For email-based communications, the AI must extract key data points such as reference numbers, dates, vessel names, approval conditions, and required actions. The parsed data must be used to automatically update the relevant port call record: for example, when a berth allocation approval email is received, the system must update the port call's berth allocation status to confirmed and populate the assigned berth number. The AI must handle communications in both English and Arabic, applying appropriate NLP models for each language. Parsing confidence scores must be displayed, and communications with low confidence parsing must be flagged for manual review. The system must learn from manual corrections to improve parsing accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R010	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Sanctions and Compliance Screening for Communications	The system must perform automated sanctions and compliance screening on all port call participants (vessel, vessel owner, operator, charterer, cargo shipper, cargo consignee) before generating official communications to port authorities. The screening must check against OFAC (US), EU sanctions list, UN Security Council sanctions list, and local sanctions lists for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Any positive or potential match must immediately block the communication from being sent and escalate to the compliance officer for review. The system must maintain an audit log of all screenings performed, including the lists checked, the date of the list version used, the results, and the disposition (cleared, escalated, blocked). False positive resolutions must be documented with the compliance officer's justification. The sanctions database must be updated automatically at least daily from official sources, and the system must alert administrators if the database has not been refreshed within the configured update interval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R011	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Communication SLA Monitoring and Escalation	The system must monitor response times for all communications sent to port authorities and track them against defined SLA targets. SLA targets must be configurable per authority and per communication type: for example, berth allocation requests at Jebel Ali may have a 12-hour expected response SLA, while customs manifest acknowledgments at JNPT may have a 4-hour SLA. The system must display a real-time SLA compliance dashboard showing pending communications with elapsed time, percentage of SLA consumed, and a visual traffic-light indicator (green under 50% SLA consumed, amber between 50% and 100%, red when SLA exceeded). When the SLA threshold is approached (configurable warning at 80% of SLA), the system must automatically generate a polite follow-up reminder communication to the authority. When the SLA is exceeded, the system must escalate to the operations manager with a recommendation to contact the authority through alternative channels (phone, personal visit). Monthly SLA compliance reports must be generated per port authority for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R012	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Formal Letter and Official Document Generation	The system must generate formal letters and official documents for port authority submissions using entity-specific letterhead templates. Each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must have its own letterhead template with the entity's legal name in English and Arabic, commercial registration number, address, logo, and authorized signatory details. Document types must include cover letters, undertakings, guarantees, indemnity letters, and formal requests. The system must support digital signature integration for authorized signatories, with configurable signatory authority levels (certain document types may require senior management signature). Generated documents must be available in PDF format with embedded fonts for proper Arabic text rendering. The system must maintain a document numbering sequence per entity with a configurable format (for example, QA-PAC-2026-00001 for Qatar entity Port Authority Communication number 1 of 2026). All generated documents must be automatically archived in the port call document repository.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R006	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Flight Booking Integration & Tracking	The system must track flight details for all arriving and departing crew members including airline, flight number, departure airport IATA code, arrival airport IATA code, scheduled departure time, scheduled arrival time, PNR reference, and ticket cost in the booking currency. The system must support multi-leg itineraries where crew transit through hub airports such as DOH, DXB, AUH, JED, or BOM. AI must monitor flight status changes and automatically adjust ground transportation schedules when flights are delayed or cancelled. The system must calculate total travel cost per crew change in the vessel's operating currency (typically USD) with exchange rate conversion from local currencies at the booking date rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R013	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Port Authority Portal Integration and Screen Scraping	The system must integrate with port authority web portals to submit applications and retrieve status updates where API-based integration is not available. For port authorities that only provide web portal access (no API or EDI), the system must support robotic process automation (RPA) or controlled screen scraping to automate repetitive form submissions and status checks. Portal login credentials must be stored securely using encryption at rest with access restricted to authorized system service accounts. The integration must handle portal session management, CAPTCHA challenges (by escalating to a human operator when encountered), form field mapping from the port call data model to the portal's form fields, and submission confirmation capture. When portal integration encounters errors (portal downtime, form structure changes, authentication failures), the system must immediately notify the IT support team and fall back to manual submission procedures. Portal integration configurations must be maintainable by technical administrators without requiring code changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R014	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Communication Archival and Legal Retention	The system must archive all communications with port authorities and regulatory bodies in compliance with legal retention requirements for each jurisdiction. Retention periods must be configurable per country and per communication type: Qatar commercial records require a minimum 10-year retention, UAE commercial records require a minimum 5-year retention, KSA records follow ZATCA requirements, and Indian records must comply with the Companies Act and Customs Act retention rules. Archived communications must be stored in a tamper-evident format with cryptographic integrity verification to ensure they can serve as legal evidence if required. The archive must support full-text search across all communications in both Arabic and English, with filters for date range, port, authority, communication type, and port call reference. Retrieval of archived communications must be achievable within 30 seconds for records up to 5 years old and within 2 minutes for older records. Data export in legally admissible formats must be supported for litigation or regulatory audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R015	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Multi-Channel Communication Routing	The system must support routing communications to port authorities through multiple channels based on the authority's preferred or mandated communication method. Supported channels must include email (with configurable SMTP settings per entity), web portal submission (via browser automation or API), EDI (UN/EDIFACT messages via SFTP or AS2), API (RESTful or SOAP web services), fax (via internet fax gateway for authorities still requiring fax submissions), and physical document delivery (generating a print-ready document with delivery tracking). The system must maintain a communication channel registry specifying the preferred and fallback channels for each port authority and each communication type. When the primary channel fails (email bounces, portal timeout, API error), the system must automatically attempt the configured fallback channel and log the channel switch. Communication delivery confirmations must be tracked per channel: read receipts for email, submission confirmation numbers for portals, functional acknowledgments for EDI, and HTTP response codes for API submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R016	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Real-Time Port Authority Regulation Change Monitoring	The AI engine must monitor port authority websites, official gazettes, and maritime regulatory news sources for changes to regulations, procedures, tariffs, and requirements that affect port agency operations at the four target ports. When a regulatory change is detected, the AI must generate a change notification to the compliance team summarizing the change, its effective date, the affected ports and operations, and recommended actions for compliance. The system must maintain a regulatory change log with entries for each detected change, tracking its status through analysis, impact assessment, system update required (checklist template changes, tariff updates, document template modifications), and implementation. Detected changes must be linkable to the affected checklist templates and notification templates so that updates can be propagated efficiently. The monitoring must cover sources in both Arabic and English, including Qatar's Official Gazette (Al Jarida Al Rasmiyya), UAE Federal Authority for Government Human Resources portal for public holiday announcements, and India's Directorate General of Shipping circulars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-1-004-R017	FEAT-030-1-004	MOD-030	Emergency Communication Protocols and Priority Override	The system must support emergency communication protocols for time-critical situations such as maritime emergencies (vessel grounding, collision, fire, pollution), security incidents (piracy, stowaway discovery, bomb threats), and medical emergencies (MEDEVAC requests). Emergency communications must bypass normal workflow approvals and be sent immediately to the relevant authorities with a single-click initiation by any authorized operations user. Emergency communication templates must be pre-configured for each emergency type and each port, pre-populated with the vessel and port call details, and formatted according to the relevant authority's emergency reporting format (such as POLREP format for pollution reports). The system must simultaneously notify all relevant internal stakeholders (operations manager, compliance officer, entity CEO, insurance contacts) when an emergency communication is initiated. A post-emergency debrief checklist must be generated after the emergency is resolved to ensure all required follow-up reports and documentation are completed within regulatory timeframes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R002	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Seafarer Document Verification Workflow	The system must implement an AI-driven document verification workflow that validates each seafarer's passport, seaman's book, STCW certificates, medical fitness certificate, and yellow fever vaccination record against the destination country's immigration requirements. For Qatar ports, the system must verify Qatar visa eligibility based on nationality and check against MOPH health requirements. For UAE ports (Jebel Ali, Khalifa), it must validate ICA entry permit requirements. For KSA ports, it must check MOFA visa requirements. For Indian ports (JNPT), it must verify FRRO registration requirements. The AI engine must flag document discrepancies with at least 95% accuracy and generate a compliance checklist per seafarer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R003	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Immigration & Visa Processing Tracker	The system must track visa application status for each crew member through stages: application submitted, documents received, visa approved, visa rejected, appeal filed, and visa collected. For each jurisdiction the system must store the relevant authority reference numbers — MOI reference for Qatar, ICA reference for UAE, MOFA reference for KSA, and FRRO reference for India. The system must calculate visa processing lead times per nationality per port and use AI prediction to estimate approval probability based on historical data. Automated alerts must be sent 72 hours, 48 hours, and 24 hours before vessel ETA if any crew visa remains unapproved, with escalation to the operations manager after the 24-hour threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R007	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Crew Change Cost Estimation & Budgeting	The system must generate pre-arrival cost estimates for each crew change operation covering visa fees, transportation, hotel accommodation, port charges, agent fees, and miscellaneous expenses. Estimates must be generated in both the local port currency and USD equivalent using configurable exchange rates. The AI engine must compare estimates against historical crew change costs at the same port for similar vessel types and flag anomalies where estimated costs exceed the historical average by more than 15%. The system must support approval workflows where estimates exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 5,000 per crew change) require principal approval before proceeding. Budget variance reports must be generated comparing estimates to actuals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R008	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Medical Assistance & Emergency Coordination	The system must manage medical assistance requests for crew members requiring onshore medical attention at any of the serviced ports. Each medical case must capture symptoms, urgency level (routine, urgent, emergency), recommended medical facility, attending physician details, diagnosis, treatment plan, and estimated medical costs in local currency. For emergency medical evacuations (MEDEVAC), the system must maintain emergency contact lists for hospitals near each port — Hamad Medical Corporation for Doha, Rashid Hospital for Dubai, Sheikh Khalifa Medical City for Abu Dhabi, and Navi Mumbai municipal hospitals for JNPT. The system must generate medical expense reports linked to the vessel's P&I club claim reference and track reimbursement status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R009	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	COVID & Health Protocol Compliance	The system must enforce applicable health screening protocols as mandated by each port state authority. Health declaration forms must be generated in both Arabic and English for GCC ports and in English and Hindi for Indian ports. The system must maintain a configurable health protocol ruleset per port that can be updated as regulations change without code deployment. The AI engine must cross-reference crew travel history from the past 14 days against any active travel restriction lists maintained per port authority. The system must generate health clearance certificates once all protocols are satisfied, and must block crew disembarkation if mandatory health checks are incomplete or failed, with override capability restricted to designated health officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R010	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Manning Agency Communication Portal	The system must provide a secure communication portal for manning agencies to submit crew change nominations, upload seafarer documents, and track the status of ongoing crew changes. The portal must support Arabic and English language interfaces with RTL layout for Arabic users. Manning agencies must authenticate via two-factor authentication and can only view crew changes related to their vessels. The system must send automated status update notifications via email and SMS when crew change milestones are reached (visa approved, transportation booked, crew embarked/disembarked). The portal must support document exchange with versioning, ensuring the latest version of each seafarer document is always referenced by the port agent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R012	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Sign-On & Sign-Off Checklist Automation	The system must generate dynamic sign-on and sign-off checklists for each crew member based on the port, vessel flag state, seafarer nationality, and rank. Sign-on checklists must include items such as document handover, safety familiarization acknowledgment, cabin assignment, and PPE issuance. Sign-off checklists must include items such as final wage settlement confirmation, personal effects inventory, cabin inspection, and repatriation flight confirmation. The AI engine must learn from completed checklists to suggest additions for specific port-flag-nationality combinations. Each checklist item must capture completion timestamp, responsible person, and digital signature or acknowledgment. Incomplete checklists must block the crew change from being marked as completed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R013	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Crew Change Timeline & Gantt Visualization	The system must display a visual timeline (Gantt-style) for each crew change operation showing all activities from initial request through final completion. Timeline elements must include visa processing duration, flight schedules, hotel stay periods, transportation movements, port entry windows, vessel boarding time, and handover period. The visualization must support both LTR and RTL layouts depending on the user's language preference (English or Arabic). Users must be able to drag and adjust timeline elements to reschedule activities, with the system automatically recalculating dependent activities. Critical path items must be highlighted in red, and the AI engine must predict potential delays based on historical processing times at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R014	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Offline Crew Change Data Capture	The system must support offline data entry for crew change operations when agents are working at anchorage or in port areas with limited connectivity. The offline module must cache the current crew change details, vessel information, and pending checklists locally on the agent's device. Agents must be able to record crew embarkation and disembarkation times, complete checklist items, capture seafarer photographs, and log notes while offline. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline entries with the central database using conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recent timestamp. The offline module must function for up to 48 hours without connectivity and must encrypt all locally stored data using AES-256 encryption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R015	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Crew Change Analytics & KPI Dashboard	The system must provide an analytics dashboard showing crew change KPIs including average crew change duration per port, cost per crew change by vessel type, visa approval rate by nationality and port, transportation cost trends, and agent performance metrics. The dashboard must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), port, entity, vessel type, and manning agency. AI-generated insights must highlight cost reduction opportunities, such as identifying ports where advance visa processing significantly reduces last-minute premium transportation charges. The dashboard must be exportable to PDF with bilingual headers in Arabic and English, and must support scheduled email distribution of weekly and monthly crew change performance reports to configurable stakeholder lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-001-R016	FEAT-030-2-001	MOD-030	Regulatory Compliance Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every crew change operation recording all actions taken, documents submitted, approvals granted, and communications exchanged. Each audit entry must capture the user ID, timestamp in UTC, action performed, entity context, and IP address. The audit trail must satisfy the record-keeping requirements of the Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Transport General Authority, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. Audit records must be immutable and retained for a minimum of seven years. The system must generate compliance reports on demand showing that all crew changes at a given port followed the prescribed regulatory workflow, with any deviations flagged and explained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R001	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Cash to Master Request Creation	The system must allow port agents to create cash-to-master (CTM) requests specifying the vessel IMO number, voyage reference, port of delivery, requested amount, requested currency (USD, EUR, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or other supported currencies), preferred denomination breakdown, and delivery date/time. Each CTM request must be linked to the vessel's proforma disbursement account and must capture the master's full name and rank for identification purposes. The system must generate a unique CTM reference number with port prefix and must validate that the requested amount does not exceed the vessel principal's configured CTM limit per call. Requests exceeding the limit must trigger an automatic escalation to the principal for approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R002	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Multi-Currency CTM Processing	The system must support CTM deliveries in multiple currencies simultaneously for a single vessel call — for example, a master may request USD 5,000 plus QAR 10,000 at Hamad Port. The system must apply the exchange rate valid at the date of cash purchase from the money exchanger or bank, not the system's default treasury rate. Each currency component must be tracked separately with its own purchase rate, source (bank name or exchange house), and receipt reference. The system must calculate the total CTM value in USD equivalent for reporting purposes and must record any exchange rate margin or commission charged by the currency provider. Variance between the applied rate and the Central Bank reference rate exceeding 2% must be flagged for review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R003	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	CTM Approval Workflow Configuration	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for CTM requests based on amount thresholds, currency, port, and entity. For example, CTM requests below USD 3,000 equivalent may be auto-approved by the port captain, requests between USD 3,000 and USD 10,000 require operations manager approval, and requests above USD 10,000 require finance director approval. Approval thresholds must be independently configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to reflect local policies and regulatory requirements. The system must support delegation of approval authority during absences, with delegation records maintained in the audit trail. Pending approvals must trigger escalation notifications after a configurable timeout period (default 4 hours).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R004	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Cash Procurement & Delivery Tracking	The system must track the physical cash procurement process from bank or exchange house withdrawal through delivery to the vessel master. Each procurement transaction must record the source institution, withdrawal date and time, amount per currency, denomination breakdown (e.g., 50x USD 100 bills, 20x USD 50 bills), security seal number for cash bags, and the name of the agent collecting the cash. The delivery phase must record the delivery date and time, delivery location (berth number, anchorage position, or vessel agent office), the name of the person delivering, and the master's acknowledgment with digital signature or signed receipt scan. The system must enforce that cash is delivered within 24 hours of procurement to minimize holding risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R005	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Master's Receipt & Acknowledgment	The system must generate a printable CTM receipt in duplicate — one for the master and one for the agent's records — showing vessel name, IMO number, port, date, amount delivered per currency, denomination breakdown, exchange rates applied, total USD equivalent, and the agent's company details with stamp. The receipt must be available in English with optional Arabic translation for GCC port deliveries. The master must sign the receipt, and the system must store a scanned or photographed copy linked to the CTM record. If the master disputes the amount or refuses to sign, the system must allow the agent to record a discrepancy note with details of the dispute and trigger a notification to the operations manager and the vessel principal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R006	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Petty Cash Fund Initialization & Limits	The system must allow finance administrators to initialize petty cash funds per port office with defined maximum balance limits in local currency — for example, QAR 20,000 for Doha office, AED 25,000 for Dubai office, SAR 15,000 for Dammam office, and INR 200,000 for JNPT office. Each petty cash fund must be assigned a custodian (responsible person), a backup custodian, and a replenishment threshold (e.g., replenish when balance falls below 30% of maximum). The system must prevent any single petty cash disbursement from exceeding a configurable percentage of the fund maximum (default 25%). Fund initialization must require finance manager approval and must be recorded with effective date, initial balance, and funding source (bank transfer reference or parent company advance).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R007	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Petty Cash Disbursement Recording	The system must allow petty cash custodians to record each disbursement with the following mandatory fields: disbursement date, payee name, purpose category (transportation, provisions, stationery, port charges, crew welfare, miscellaneous), description of expense, amount in local currency, and the vessel or overhead cost center to be charged. Supporting documents such as vendor receipts, taxi receipts, or purchase invoices must be attached as scanned images or photographs. The system must enforce that the total of all unreconciled disbursements plus the physical cash remaining equals the fund balance at all times. Any disbursement without a supporting document must be flagged as unsubstantiated and must be approved by the finance manager before being posted to the ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R008	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Petty Cash Replenishment Process	The system must automate the petty cash replenishment process by generating a replenishment request when the fund balance falls below the configured threshold. The replenishment request must summarize all disbursements since the last replenishment, categorized by expense type, and must calculate the exact replenishment amount needed to restore the fund to its maximum balance. The request must be routed through the approval workflow configured for the entity and must generate a payment instruction to the treasury module specifying the bank account, amount, and beneficiary (petty cash custodian). Upon receipt of the replenishment funds, the custodian must confirm receipt in the system with the bank transfer reference or cheque number, at which point the fund balance is automatically updated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R006	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Arabic RTL Dashboard Rendering	The system must fully support Arabic right-to-left (RTL) rendering for all executive dashboard components including KPI labels, data tables, charts, and navigation elements. All numeric values must remain in Western Arabic numerals (0-9) but directional flow of dashboard components must mirror for RTL mode. Chart axes must flip appropriately with time series progressing from right to left in Arabic mode. All KPI titles, tooltips, and descriptive text must be available in both English and Arabic with the ability to toggle language without losing dashboard state. Date displays must support both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar formats with dual-date display option showing both calendar systems simultaneously per entity preference settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R009	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Petty Cash Physical Count & Reconciliation	The system must support periodic physical cash counts where the custodian records the actual denomination breakdown of cash on hand (e.g., 10x QAR 500 notes, 5x QAR 100 notes, coins totaling QAR 45). The system must compare the physical count total against the book balance and flag any discrepancy. Discrepancies must be categorized as shortage or surplus, and the custodian must provide an explanation. Shortages exceeding a configurable threshold (default equivalent of USD 50) must trigger a mandatory investigation workflow involving the finance manager. Physical counts must be performed at least monthly, and the system must send automated reminders to custodians when a count is overdue. All count records must be stored with timestamps and the counter's digital acknowledgment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R010	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	CTM Advance Recovery Tracking	The system must track the recovery of CTM advances from vessel principals through the disbursement account settlement process. Each CTM delivery must be posted as a receivable against the vessel's disbursement account, and the system must monitor the aging of outstanding CTM receivables per principal. The system must generate aging reports showing CTM advances outstanding for 0-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, and over 90 days, with amounts in both the original delivery currency and USD equivalent. Automated dunning notices must be sent to principals when CTM receivables exceed the configured payment terms (default 30 days). The AI engine must predict collection probability based on the principal's historical payment behavior and flag high-risk receivables for proactive follow-up.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R011	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Anti-Money Laundering Compliance Checks	The system must enforce anti-money laundering (AML) controls on all cash transactions in compliance with the regulatory requirements of Qatar Financial Information Unit (QFIU), UAE Central Bank AML regulations, Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority (SAMA) guidelines, and India's Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA). CTM deliveries exceeding the equivalent of USD 10,000 in a single transaction or USD 25,000 cumulative per vessel per port call must be flagged for enhanced due diligence review. The system must verify the master's identity against international sanctions lists and must generate Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) for transactions exceeding local reporting thresholds. All AML-flagged transactions must be reviewed and cleared by the compliance officer before cash procurement can proceed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R012	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Cash Handling Security Protocols	The system must enforce security protocols for physical cash handling including dual-control requirements (two persons present) for cash amounts exceeding the equivalent of USD 5,000, mandatory use of tamper-evident security bags with unique serial numbers, and GPS tracking of cash in transit from the bank or exchange house to the vessel. The system must record the security bag serial number at each handover point (bank to agent, agent to delivery person, delivery person to master). Insurance coverage verification must be checked before any cash movement, ensuring the transit insurance policy covers the amount being transported. Any deviation from security protocols must be logged as a compliance incident and must trigger immediate notification to the security officer and finance manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R013	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Hijri Calendar & Islamic Finance Compliance	The system must display all CTM and petty cash transaction dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats throughout the user interface. For the KSA entity, the Hijri date must be the primary display date as required by Saudi financial regulations. Financial reports for the KSA entity must show Hijri fiscal periods alongside Gregorian equivalents. The system must ensure that all interest-related calculations or charges comply with Islamic finance principles for entities operating under Sharia-compliant financial frameworks. Cash procurement from Islamic banks must be recorded without any interest component, and exchange transactions must comply with the Sharia requirement of same-day settlement (spot rate) to avoid elements of riba. The system must support configurable financial calendar mappings per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R014	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Offline CTM & Petty Cash Operations	The system must support offline recording of CTM deliveries and petty cash disbursements when agents are aboard vessels at anchorage or in areas without network connectivity. The offline module must pre-load approved CTM requests, vessel details, and the current petty cash fund status. Agents must be able to record cash delivery confirmations, capture master signatures via touchscreen, photograph receipts, and log petty cash expenses while offline. All offline transactions must be queued with timestamps and synchronized to the central database upon reconnection using a conflict-resolution mechanism that prevents duplicate postings. The offline module must enforce the same validation rules as the online system, including amount limits and mandatory fields, and must store data encrypted on the local device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R015	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	CTM & Petty Cash Reporting Suite	The system must provide comprehensive reporting for CTM and petty cash operations including: CTM summary report per vessel per port call, CTM aging analysis per principal, petty cash fund status report per port, petty cash expense analysis by category, monthly cash movement reconciliation, and AML compliance report. All reports must support filtering by entity, port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), currency, and vessel. Reports must be generated in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. The AI engine must generate executive summaries highlighting unusual patterns such as unusually high CTM requests for specific vessel types or petty cash categories with growing expense trends. Reports must be exportable to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-002-R016	FEAT-030-2-002	MOD-030	Tax & VAT Treatment on Cash Transactions	The system must correctly apply tax treatment to petty cash disbursements and CTM-related expenses based on the operating entity's jurisdiction. For Qatar operations, the system must track zero-rated and exempt supplies pending future VAT implementation. For UAE operations, the system must apply 5% VAT on taxable supplies and generate tax invoices compliant with FTA requirements. For KSA operations, the system must apply 15% VAT and comply with ZATCA e-invoicing requirements including QR code generation. For India operations, the system must apply applicable GST rates (5%, 12%, 18%, 28%) based on the HSN code of the expense category and generate GST-compliant invoices. The system must calculate input tax credit eligibility for each petty cash expense and feed this data to the tax module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R001	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Pre-Arrival Notification Submission	The system must generate and submit pre-arrival notifications (PAN) to the relevant port authority at least 72 hours before the vessel's estimated time of arrival (ETA). The PAN must include vessel particulars (name, IMO number, flag, gross tonnage, net tonnage, LOA, beam, draft), voyage details (last port, next port, cargo summary), crew list, passenger list if applicable, and dangerous goods manifest if carrying hazardous cargo per IMDG Code classifications. For Hamad Port, the PAN must be submitted through QTerminals' port community system. For Jebel Ali, through DP World's DUBAITRADE portal. For Khalifa Port, through ADPORTS Maqta Gateway (Maqta'a). For JNPT, through the Indian PCS 1x system. The system must store submission confirmations and track acknowledgment from each authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R002	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	IMO FAL Convention Forms Generation	The system must auto-generate all seven IMO FAL Convention standardized forms from vessel and voyage data: FAL Form 1 (General Declaration), FAL Form 2 (Cargo Declaration), FAL Form 3 (Ship's Stores Declaration), FAL Form 4 (Crew's Effects Declaration), FAL Form 5 (Crew List), FAL Form 6 (Passenger List), and FAL Form 7 (Dangerous Goods Manifest). Each form must be populated from the vessel's master data, voyage data, and crew database, with the AI engine pre-filling fields based on historical data and flagging any missing mandatory information. Forms must be generated in English as the primary language with Arabic translations for GCC port submissions. The system must support the electronic FAL (e-FAL) format where accepted by the port authority and must maintain version control of submitted forms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R003	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Customs Inward Clearance Processing	The system must manage the customs inward clearance process by submitting the required documentation to the customs authority at each port. For Qatar Customs (General Authority of Customs), the system must submit the import manifest, cargo declaration, and any temporary admission requests for ship stores. For UAE Federal Customs Authority, submissions must comply with the Dubai Trade single window requirements at Jebel Ali and Abu Dhabi Customs requirements at Khalifa Port. For Saudi Customs (ZATCA), submissions must include the unified customs declaration. For Indian Customs (ICEGATE), the Import General Manifest (IGM) must be filed electronically. The system must track customs clearance status through stages: documents submitted, under review, queries raised, queries responded, clearance granted, with timestamps for each stage transition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R004	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Immigration Clearance Coordination	The system must coordinate immigration clearance by submitting crew and passenger lists to the immigration authority prior to vessel arrival. For Qatar (MOI), the system must submit crew lists through the approved channel with passport details and visa status of each crew member. For UAE (ICA / GDRFA), the system must comply with smart gate requirements at Dubai and Abu Dhabi ports. For KSA (Jawazat), the system must submit through the Absher or MAWANI-approved system. For India (Bureau of Immigration), the system must file through the Immigration Check Post system at JNPT. The system must flag crew members with expired documents, mismatched visa types, or nationalities requiring special entry clearance. Immigration clearance status must be tracked independently from customs clearance with separate status workflows per authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R005	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Port Health Clearance & Maritime Declaration of Health	The system must generate the Maritime Declaration of Health (MDH) as required by the International Health Regulations (IHR 2005) and submit it to the port health authority prior to vessel arrival. The MDH must include information on the health status of all persons on board, any illness or death during the voyage, ports visited in the last 30 days, and the sanitation measures applied. For GCC ports, the system must comply with the Gulf Cooperation Council health clearance protocols. For Indian ports, the system must submit to the Port Health Organization under the Directorate General of Health Services. The system must check whether the vessel has a valid Ship Sanitation Certificate (SSC) and flag if it has expired or will expire within 30 days. Port health clearance must be obtained before any person is allowed to disembark.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R006	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Port Authority & Harbour Master Clearance	The system must manage communications with the harbour master's office for vessel arrival and departure clearance. Arrival clearance must confirm berth allocation, pilot boarding arrangement, tug requirements, and mooring plan. The system must submit the vessel's arrival condition details including draft forward, draft aft, air draft, displacement, and any deficiencies noted in the last Port State Control inspection. For departure clearance, the system must submit the vessel's departure condition, next port of call, estimated sailing time, and confirmation that all port dues and charges have been settled or guaranteed. The system must track pilot request submissions and confirmations, recording the assigned pilot name and boarding time. All harbour master communications must be logged with timestamps for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R007	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Coast Guard & Maritime Security Clearance	The system must manage security clearance requirements as mandated by the International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code. The vessel's ISPS security level, Ship Security Certificate details, and the Continuous Synopsis Record (CSR) must be captured and submitted to the port facility security officer (PFSO). For Qatar, the system must interface with the Qatar Coast Guard requirements. For UAE, the system must comply with CICPA (Critical Infrastructure and Coastal Protection Authority) regulations. For KSA, the system must satisfy Saudi Border Guard requirements. For India, the system must comply with the Indian Coast Guard and CISF port security protocols. The system must generate pre-arrival security notifications as per SOLAS Chapter XI-2 and track security clearance status independently of other clearances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R008	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Clearance Status Dashboard & Tracking	The system must provide a unified dashboard showing the clearance status of all vessels currently at port or expected to arrive within the next 7 days. The dashboard must display each vessel as a row with columns for: customs clearance status, immigration clearance status, port health clearance status, harbour master clearance status, and security clearance status. Each status cell must show a color-coded indicator (green for cleared, yellow for in progress, red for pending or blocked, grey for not applicable). The dashboard must support filtering by port, date range, vessel type, and clearance authority. Real-time updates must be reflected within 60 seconds of any status change. The AI engine must predict clearance completion times based on current status and historical processing durations at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R009	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Outward Clearance Document Generation	The system must generate all required outward clearance documents including the Port Clearance Certificate application, updated crew list reflecting any crew changes during the port stay, updated ship's stores declaration reflecting any provisions received, export cargo declaration, and confirmation of outstanding dues settlement. For Hamad Port, the outward clearance must be obtained from QTerminals and Qatar Customs. For Jebel Ali, from DP World and Dubai Customs. For Khalifa Port, from ADPORTS and Abu Dhabi Customs. For JNPT, the Export General Manifest (EGM) must be filed through ICEGATE and Port Clearance obtained from the MMD (Mercantile Marine Department). The system must verify that all inward clearance conditions have been satisfied before allowing outward clearance submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R010	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Vessel Deficiency & Detention Management	The system must track any deficiencies identified during Port State Control (PSC) inspections or Flag State inspections that affect the vessel's clearance status. Each deficiency must be recorded with the inspection date, inspector details, deficiency code per the Paris MOU or Tokyo MOU classification, description, corrective action required, and deadline for rectification. If a vessel is detained due to serious deficiencies, the system must block outward clearance processing and maintain a detention record with the detention order reference, issuing authority, and conditions for release. The system must track rectification progress and generate re-inspection requests to the relevant authority once corrections are completed. AI must analyze deficiency patterns across the fleet to predict high-risk vessels before port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R011	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Dangerous Goods Clearance Handling	The system must implement specialized clearance workflows for vessels carrying dangerous goods as classified under the International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code. The system must generate the Dangerous Goods Manifest (FAL Form 7) with correct UN numbers, proper shipping names, hazard classes, packing groups, and stowage locations. For each port, the system must verify that the declared dangerous goods are permitted entry based on port-specific restrictions — for example, certain Class 1 explosives or Class 7 radioactive materials may be restricted at specific terminals. The system must submit dangerous goods notifications to the port authority at least 24 hours before arrival in addition to the standard PAN. Emergency response information including MSDS references must be available for all declared dangerous goods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R012	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Clearance Fee Calculation & Port Dues	The system must calculate all applicable port dues and clearance-related fees based on the vessel's gross tonnage, net tonnage, LOA, and duration of stay at each port. Fee calculations must reference the latest tariff schedules published by each port authority: QTerminals tariff for Hamad Port, DP World tariff for Jebel Ali, ADPORTS tariff for Khalifa Port, and JNPT/TAMP tariff for Indian ports. The system must apply correct currency for each port's tariff (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and convert to USD equivalent for the principal's disbursement account. Fee components must include port dues, berth hire, pilotage, towage, mooring/unmooring, anchorage charges, and any overtime surcharges for operations outside normal working hours. The AI engine must validate calculated fees against historical actuals for similar vessel calls and flag significant variances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R013	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Arabic & Bilingual Document Submission	The system must generate all clearance documents in the language required by each authority. For Qatar, UAE, and KSA port authorities, documents must be submitted in Arabic with English translations where required, using proper RTL formatting for Arabic text. Vessel names, port names, and cargo descriptions must be transliterated or translated accurately per the authority's conventions. The system must maintain a bilingual glossary of maritime and customs terminology to ensure consistent translation across all documents. For Indian port submissions, documents must be in English. The user interface for document review and approval must support seamless switching between Arabic and English views. Digital stamps and signatures on Arabic documents must be positioned according to Arabic document conventions (right side for stamps, left side for signatures where applicable).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R014	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Clearance Workflow Automation & AI Routing	The system must implement AI-driven workflow automation that determines the correct clearance sequence and routing based on the vessel type, cargo type, port, and flag state. For tankers arriving at petroleum terminals, the system must include additional safety clearances from the terminal operator. For container vessels, the system must coordinate clearance timing with container terminal slot allocation. For vessels arriving from high-risk origins, the system must add enhanced security screening steps. The AI engine must learn from historical clearance processing patterns to optimize submission timing — for example, submitting customs documentation during periods of lowest queue volume at the customs office. The system must automatically retry failed electronic submissions with exponential backoff and must alert the agent after three consecutive failures for manual intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R015	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Multi-Entity Clearance Cost Allocation	The system must allocate all clearance-related costs (port dues, government fees, agent service charges, overtime fees) to the correct legal entity based on the port where the clearance activity occurs. Clearance costs at Hamad Port are allocated to the Qatar entity's accounts, Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port costs to the UAE entity, Saudi port costs to the KSA entity, and JNPT costs to the India entity. When a vessel transits between ports managed by different entities (e.g., arriving at Jebel Ali under UAE entity and departing from Hamad Port under Qatar entity), the system must correctly split the inward and outward clearance costs between the respective entities. Inter-entity charge records must be generated for vessels whose principals are contracted with a different entity than the port of call entity, with proper transfer pricing and supporting documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R016	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Clearance Document Archive & Retrieval	The system must archive all clearance-related documents including submitted forms, authority responses, clearance certificates, and correspondence in a structured document repository linked to the vessel visit record. Documents must be indexed by vessel IMO number, voyage reference, port, clearance type, and date for rapid retrieval. The archive must comply with document retention requirements of each jurisdiction — minimum 5 years for Qatar, 5 years for UAE, 10 years for KSA (ZATCA requirement), and 8 years for India (Customs Act requirement). Archived documents must be retrievable within 30 seconds for any query. The system must support bulk export of clearance documents for audit purposes and must provide a search interface supporting both English and Arabic keywords. Document integrity must be ensured through digital checksums verified at retrieval time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-003-R017	FEAT-030-2-003	MOD-030	Offline Clearance Form Preparation	The system must support offline preparation of clearance forms when port agents are working aboard vessels or in port areas without internet connectivity. The offline module must pre-cache vessel master data, crew lists, voyage details, and blank form templates for all IMO FAL forms and port-specific submission forms. Agents must be able to complete form fields, attach supporting documents from the device camera, and queue submissions for later transmission. Upon reconnection, the system must validate all queued forms against the latest vessel data to detect any changes made by other users during the offline period. Conflicts must be presented to the agent for resolution before submission. The offline module must function for up to 24 hours and must encrypt all cached data using AES-256 encryption to protect sensitive vessel and crew information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R001	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Proforma Disbursement Account Creation	The system must allow port agents to create a Proforma Disbursement Account (PDA) for each expected vessel call specifying the vessel IMO number, voyage reference, expected port of call, estimated arrival date, estimated departure date, and cargo operations planned (loading, discharging, or both). The PDA must be structured according to the FONASBA (Federation of National Associations of Ship Brokers and Agents) standard DA format with itemized cost categories. The AI engine must auto-populate estimated costs based on the vessel's particulars (GT, NT, LOA, draft), expected duration of stay, and historical actual costs for similar vessel calls at the same port. Each PDA line item must show the estimated amount in local port currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R002	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	PDA Cost Category Structure	The system must support a comprehensive and configurable cost category structure for disbursement accounts aligned with industry-standard port agency accounting practices. Mandatory cost categories must include: port dues, berth hire, pilotage (inward and outward), towage (inward and outward), mooring and unmooring, anchorage charges, agency fees, communication charges, launch hire, fresh water supply, provisions supply, bunker delivery charges, waste disposal fees, cargo documentation fees, customs clearance charges, immigration charges, port health charges, crew change costs, cash to master, surveys and inspections, and miscellaneous charges. Each category must have a configurable sub-category structure per port to reflect local variations. The system must enforce that every cost posted to the DA is assigned to exactly one cost category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R003	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Proforma to Actual DA Conversion	The system must support the conversion of a Proforma DA to an Actual (Final) Disbursement Account upon completion of the vessel's port call. During conversion, each PDA line item must be updated with the actual cost incurred, and the system must calculate the variance between estimated and actual amounts per line item and in total. New line items not in the original PDA must be flagged as additional charges with mandatory justification notes. The AI engine must highlight line items where the actual cost exceeds the proforma estimate by more than 10% and require the agent to provide an explanation. The conversion process must lock the PDA from further edits and create a new editable FDA (Final DA) document. The system must maintain full version history showing all changes from PDA to FDA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R004	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Multi-Currency DA Processing	The system must handle disbursement accounts in multiple currencies reflecting the actual currencies in which expenses were incurred. Each DA line item must record the expense amount in the original vendor invoice currency, the exchange rate used for conversion, and the equivalent amount in the DA's base currency (typically USD). The system must support configurable exchange rate sources — vessel principal's agreed rate, daily treasury rate, Central Bank rate, or actual purchase rate — with the applicable source defined per principal contract. Currency conversion must be applied at the date of expense incurrence, not the DA finalization date. The system must display DA totals in both the local port currency and USD, and must support principals who require DAs in their own reporting currency (e.g., EUR, GBP, JPY) with a third currency column.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R005	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Supporting Document Management	The system must require and manage supporting documents for every line item in the disbursement account. Acceptable supporting documents include port authority invoices, vendor receipts, official fee receipts, CTM acknowledgment receipts, crew change cost breakdowns, and internal service charge calculations. Each document must be scanned or uploaded and linked to the specific DA line item it supports. The AI engine must perform optical character recognition (OCR) on uploaded documents to extract key data points (invoice number, date, amount, vendor name) and cross-validate against the manually entered DA line item values. Discrepancies between OCR-extracted amounts and DA line item amounts must be flagged for agent review. The system must enforce that the FDA cannot be finalized if any line item exceeding USD 100 equivalent lacks a supporting document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R006	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Principal Approval & DA Submission	The system must generate a formatted DA statement for submission to the vessel principal (owner or operator) showing all cost items with supporting document references. The DA statement must be generated in the FONASBA standard format with the agent's company header, vessel details, port details, arrival and departure dates, and a categorized cost breakdown. The system must submit the DA electronically to the principal via email or through a principal portal with secure access. Principals must be able to review each line item, view supporting documents, and either approve the DA, approve with deductions, or dispute specific items. The system must track the principal's response with timestamps and maintain a complete communication trail. If no response is received within the configured period (default 30 days), the system must send automated reminders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R007	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must implement a structured dispute resolution workflow when a principal raises objections against specific DA line items. Each disputed item must be recorded with the principal's objection reason, the disputed amount, and the agent's response with supporting evidence. The system must support multiple rounds of negotiation with each round tracked and timestamped. Dispute categories must include: amount overcharged, service not rendered, duplicate charge, rate disagreement, currency conversion disagreement, and documentation insufficient. The AI engine must suggest resolution options based on historical dispute outcomes for similar line items and principals. Disputes unresolved after a configurable period (default 60 days) must be escalated to the operations director. The system must calculate the financial impact of outstanding disputes and include them in management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R008	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Payment Tracking & Funds Application	The system must track payments received from principals against submitted disbursement accounts. Payment receipt must be recorded with the payment date, amount received, currency, bank reference, and the DA reference(s) the payment applies to. The system must support partial payments where a principal pays less than the full DA amount, with the remaining balance tracked as outstanding. When a principal sends a lump-sum payment covering multiple DAs, the system must allow the agent to allocate the payment across specific DAs. The AI engine must suggest payment allocation based on aging (oldest DA first) or principal instructions. Overpayments must be recorded as credit balances available for offset against future DAs. The system must reconcile received payments with bank statement entries and flag any unmatched receipts for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R009	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Advance Funds Management	The system must manage advance funds (funds on deposit, FOD) received from principals before or during the vessel's port call to cover anticipated disbursements. Each advance receipt must be recorded with the principal reference, amount, currency, bank details, and the vessel call it relates to. The system must apply advance funds against the final DA upon completion, showing the advance received, total DA amount, and the balance due to or from the principal. If the DA amount exceeds the advance, the system must generate a balance invoice. If the advance exceeds the DA amount, the system must generate a refund request or credit note per the principal's preference. The system must calculate interest on advance funds held beyond a configurable threshold period (default 90 days) if required by the principal's contract terms. Advance fund balances must be reported per principal with aging analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R010	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Agency Fee Calculation Engine	The system must calculate agency fees based on configurable fee schedules that may vary by port, vessel type, vessel size (GT bands), cargo type, and principal contract terms. Fee types must include: attendance fee (fixed per port call), vessel arrival fee, vessel departure fee, cargo operations supervision fee, husbandry services fee, and overtime fees for services outside normal working hours. The system must support different fee structures: fixed amount per call, percentage of total disbursements, GT-based sliding scale, or a combination. Each principal's contracted fee terms must be stored in the system and automatically applied when generating their DAs. The AI engine must detect when a vessel call's characteristics would result in a significantly different fee under alternative fee structures and alert the commercial team to potential contract renegotiation opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R011	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Inter-Entity DA Settlement	The system must handle inter-entity settlements when a vessel's principal has a contract with one entity (e.g., Qatar entity) but calls at a port managed by another entity (e.g., UAE entity at Jebel Ali). The port entity must create the DA and incur the local costs, then generate an inter-company invoice to the contracting entity for reimbursement. The system must apply the agreed transfer pricing policy — either at cost, at cost plus a configurable margin, or at the standard agency fee tariff. Inter-entity DAs must be reconciled monthly with automatic generation of netting statements. The system must track inter-entity receivables and payables per DA and provide aging analysis. Each entity's general ledger must reflect the correct revenue, cost, and inter-company balance for every vessel call. Transfer pricing documentation must comply with the tax regulations of all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R012	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Revenue Recognition & Accounting Integration	The system must generate accounting entries for each DA event — PDA creation (contingent receivable), advance funds receipt (liability), FDA finalization (revenue recognition and receivable), payment receipt (receivable clearance), and credit note issuance (revenue reversal). Revenue recognition must follow the applicable accounting standard (IFRS 15) with the performance obligation satisfied upon completion of the port call services. The system must post journal entries to the general ledger module with the correct entity, cost center, revenue account, and tax treatment per jurisdiction. For the India entity, the system must generate entries compliant with Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS). The system must support both accrual and cash basis reporting and must provide a DA-to-GL reconciliation report ensuring every DA amount is fully reflected in the financial statements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R013	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Aging & Collections Management	The system must generate DA aging reports showing outstanding receivables from principals categorized by aging buckets: current (0-30 days), 31-60 days, 61-90 days, 91-180 days, and over 180 days. Aging must be calculated from the FDA submission date to the principal. The system must support configurable collection workflows including automated reminder emails at 30, 60, and 90 days past due, escalation to senior management at 90 days, and referral to legal at 180 days. The AI engine must predict collection probability based on the principal's payment history, dispute status, and industry payment trends, assigning each outstanding DA a collection risk score. The system must calculate expected credit loss provisions per IFRS 9 requirements and feed the provision amounts to the accounting module. Collection actions must be logged with dates and outcomes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R014	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Analytics & Profitability Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard for DA management showing key metrics including: total DA revenue by port and entity, average DA value by vessel type, DA-to-cash conversion cycle (days from FDA submission to full payment), PDA accuracy ratio (PDA vs FDA variance percentage), dispute rate by principal and cost category, and agency fee margin analysis. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary metrics to individual DAs and from DA line items to supporting documents. AI-generated insights must identify trends such as increasing disbursement costs at specific ports, principals with deteriorating payment patterns, or cost categories where vendor negotiations could reduce expenses. The dashboard must support Gregorian and Hijri date filtering and must be exportable in bilingual format (English and Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R015	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	DA Template & Benchmarking Library	The system must maintain a library of DA templates per port per vessel type that serve as benchmarks for new PDA creation. Templates must be auto-updated by the AI engine based on a rolling average of the last 20 actual DAs for each port-vessel type combination, weighted by recency. Each template must show the expected cost range (minimum, average, maximum) for every line item with the standard deviation to help agents identify when a PDA estimate falls outside normal bounds. The system must support manual template overrides for known future changes such as published port tariff increases effective from a specific date. Templates must be versioned with effective dates, and the system must automatically apply the correct template version based on the vessel's expected arrival date. Template accuracy must be measured and reported monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R016	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Principal Portal & Self-Service Access	The system must provide a secure web portal for vessel principals (owners and operators) to view and manage their disbursement accounts. The portal must allow principals to view all PDA and FDA documents for their vessels, download DA statements and supporting documents, submit approvals or disputes online, track payment status, and view their account statement with running balance. Access must be secured with two-factor authentication, and principals must only see data related to their own vessels. The portal must support English and Arabic language interfaces with proper RTL layout for Arabic. The system must log all principal portal activities in the audit trail. The AI engine must provide principals with a cost comparison tool showing how their DA costs compare to anonymized industry averages for similar vessel calls at the same port, encouraging transparency and trust.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R017	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Offline DA Preparation & Synchronization	The system must support offline preparation of proforma and final disbursement accounts when port agents have limited or no internet connectivity. The offline module must cache vessel master data, principal contract terms, port tariff schedules, fee calculation rules, and historical DA templates. Agents must be able to create new PDAs, convert PDAs to FDAs, add line items with cost details, and attach supporting documents while offline. All changes must be queued locally with timestamps and synchronized to the central database upon reconnection. The synchronization engine must detect conflicts when multiple agents have modified the same DA offline and present a merge interface for resolution. The offline module must enforce the same validation rules, approval thresholds, and mandatory field requirements as the online system. Locally stored data must be encrypted with AES-256 and the module must function for up to 72 hours without connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-030-2-004-R018	FEAT-030-2-004	MOD-030	Regulatory & Tax Compliance on DAs	The system must ensure all disbursement accounts comply with the tax and regulatory requirements of each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must apply any applicable customs duty exemptions for vessel stores and provisions under the GCC Common Customs Law. For UAE, the system must correctly apply 5% VAT on taxable agency services and generate FTA-compliant tax invoices. For KSA, the system must apply 15% VAT on taxable services, generate ZATCA-compliant e-invoices with QR codes, and ensure withholding tax deductions on payments to foreign principals per the Saudi income tax law. For India, the system must apply GST on agency services with correct SAC codes, generate e-invoices compliant with GSTN requirements, and handle TDS deductions as per the Income Tax Act Section 194C or 194J as applicable. The system must maintain a tax treatment matrix per cost category per jurisdiction and automatically apply the correct treatment during DA preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R001	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Real-Time TEU Throughput KPI Widget	The system must display a real-time TEU throughput widget on the executive dashboard showing total TEUs handled across all four operational entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with breakdown by port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLP, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA). The widget must support hourly, daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual aggregation intervals. Data must refresh at configurable intervals with a minimum of every 60 seconds. The widget must show current period vs. prior period variance as both absolute numbers and percentage change, with color-coded indicators for positive and negative trends. TEU counts must distinguish between laden and empty containers, and between 20-foot and 40-foot equivalent units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R002	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Multi-Currency Revenue Consolidation Display	The system must consolidate revenue figures from all entities into a single dashboard view supporting display in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR currencies. Revenue conversion must use configurable exchange rates sourced from the ERP treasury module with support for daily spot rates, monthly average rates, and budget rates. The dashboard must allow executives to toggle between reporting currencies with instant recalculation. When displaying consolidated revenue, the system must clearly indicate the exchange rate type being used (spot, average, or budget) and the rate effective date. Revenue must be broken down by freight revenue, surcharge revenue, demurrage and detention revenue, and other ancillary revenue streams per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R003	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Gross Profit Margin Calculation and Display	The system must calculate and display gross profit (GP) margins at multiple hierarchical levels including consolidated group level, entity level (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port level, service route level, and individual voyage level. GP calculation must account for all direct costs including vessel operating costs, port charges, terminal handling charges (THC), bunker fuel costs, canal transit fees, and agency fees. The system must support both actual GP based on realized costs and estimated GP based on budgeted or standard costs. GP margins must be displayed as both absolute monetary values in the selected reporting currency and as percentage of revenue, with configurable threshold alerts when margins fall below entity-specific target thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R004	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Vessel and Container Utilization Gauges	The system must display vessel utilization and container fleet utilization as visual gauge widgets on the executive dashboard. Vessel utilization must be calculated as the ratio of loaded TEUs to nominal vessel capacity for each voyage, aggregated across the active fleet. Container utilization must track the ratio of containers currently in revenue-generating use versus total owned and leased container fleet. The system must differentiate utilization by container type including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, and flat rack containers. Utilization metrics must include idle time analysis showing average days containers remain empty at each port location (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and flag containers idle beyond configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R005	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Dashboard Layout Customization and Personalization	The system must allow each executive user to customize their dashboard layout by rearranging, resizing, adding, or removing KPI widgets. Personalized layouts must persist across sessions and devices for each authenticated user. The system must provide a default dashboard template per user role (CEO, CFO, COO, Regional Director) that can be used as a starting point. Widgets must support drag-and-drop repositioning within a responsive grid layout that adapts to screen sizes from 1024px to 4K resolution displays. Users must be able to save multiple named dashboard configurations and switch between them. The system must support both left-to-right (English) and right-to-left (Arabic) layout orientations with proper widget mirroring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R007	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Multi-Entity Data Filtering and Segregation	The system must enforce strict multi-entity data segregation on the executive dashboard ensuring users only see data for entities they are authorized to access. Entity-level filters must be available as persistent filter controls allowing selection of one or more entities (Qatar entity, UAE entity, KSA entity, India entity) for consolidated or individual viewing. When multiple entities are selected, all KPIs must reflect the consolidated totals with the ability to expand into per-entity breakdowns. The system must respect legal entity boundaries for financial KPIs ensuring inter-company transactions are properly eliminated in consolidated views. Role-based access control must determine which entity data each user can view, with super-admin roles able to see all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R008	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	KPI Threshold Alerts and Notifications	The system must support configurable threshold alerts for each KPI displayed on the executive dashboard. Thresholds must be definable as absolute values or percentage deviations from target, budget, or prior period benchmarks. When a KPI breaches a threshold, the system must visually highlight the widget using color-coded severity levels (green for on-target, amber for warning, red for critical). Additionally, the system must trigger push notifications to designated recipients via email and in-app notification channels when critical thresholds are breached. Alert configurations must be entity-specific allowing different threshold values for Qatar operations versus India operations given their different operational scales and market conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R009	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Historical Trend Analysis with Period Comparison	The system must provide historical trend analysis for all executive KPIs with the ability to compare across configurable time periods. Executives must be able to overlay current period performance against any historical period including same month last year, same quarter last year, rolling 12-month average, and custom date ranges. Trend lines must be rendered as interactive line charts with data point tooltips showing exact values. The system must support Hijri calendar period comparisons for KSA and Qatar entities alongside Gregorian comparisons. Trend data must be available for a minimum of 36 months of historical data. Seasonal adjustment options must be available to normalize for known shipping industry cyclical patterns such as pre-Ramadan cargo surges and Chinese New Year volume drops.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R010	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Automated Executive Report Generation	The system must generate automated executive summary reports from dashboard KPI data in PDF and Excel formats. Reports must be schedulable on daily, weekly, monthly, and quarterly frequencies with configurable distribution lists per entity. The PDF report must include the company logo, bilingual headers (English and Arabic), and formatted charts matching the dashboard visualizations. Report generation must support both Gregorian and Hijri date stamps in report headers and footers. Generated reports must be archived and accessible through a report repository with search and filter capabilities. The system must support watermarking reports with classification levels (Confidential, Internal, Public) as per each entity's information security policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R011	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Drill-Down Navigation from KPIs to Source Data	The system must support multi-level drill-down navigation from any executive KPI widget to progressively detailed source data. For example, clicking a consolidated revenue KPI must drill into entity-level revenue, then port-level, then service-level, then voyage-level, and finally individual bill-of-lading-level detail. Each drill-down level must maintain the context of applied filters (date range, entity, currency). The drill-down path must be displayed as a breadcrumb trail allowing executives to navigate back to any intermediate level. Data at the lowest drill-down level must link to the source transaction in the operational module (e.g., booking, invoice, voyage record) enabling executives to investigate anomalies without leaving the analytics platform.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R012	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Budget vs Actual Variance Dashboard Panel	The system must display a dedicated budget versus actual variance panel on the executive dashboard comparing actual KPI values against approved budget targets. Variance must be shown in both absolute terms and percentage deviation with configurable favorable and unfavorable indicators. The system must support multiple budget versions (original budget, revised forecast Q1, revised forecast Q2, etc.) with the ability to switch between versions for comparison. Budget data must be importable from the ERP financial planning module or uploadable via standardized Excel templates. The variance panel must support multi-currency budgets with automatic conversion to the selected reporting currency using the designated budget exchange rates per entity fiscal year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R013	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Offline Dashboard Caching for Executive Access	The system must support offline access to the executive dashboard by caching the most recent dashboard snapshot locally on the user's device. When network connectivity is unavailable, the dashboard must display cached data with a clear timestamp indicating when the data was last refreshed and a visual indicator showing offline mode. Cached data must include all KPI values, chart data, and drill-down data up to the second level. The system must automatically synchronize and refresh all dashboard data when connectivity is restored. Offline caching must comply with data security policies including encryption of cached data at rest on the device and automatic cache expiration after a configurable period (default 72 hours).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R014	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	AI-Powered KPI Anomaly Detection and Insights	The system must employ AI-driven anomaly detection to automatically identify unusual patterns or deviations in executive KPIs without requiring manual threshold configuration. The AI engine must analyze historical KPI trends to establish dynamic baselines and flag statistically significant deviations in TEU throughput, revenue, GP margins, and utilization rates. Detected anomalies must be surfaced as insight cards on the dashboard with natural language explanations in both English and Arabic describing the anomaly, potential root causes, and recommended investigative actions. The system must learn from user feedback (confirmed relevant or dismissed) to improve anomaly detection accuracy over time. Anomaly sensitivity must be configurable per KPI and per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R001	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Container Volume Forecasting by Trade Lane	The system must provide AI-driven container volume forecasting for all active trade lanes connecting Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA) with global destinations. Forecasts must cover 7-day, 30-day, 90-day, and 365-day horizons with confidence intervals at 80%, 90%, and 95% levels. The model must account for seasonal variations including Ramadan, Eid, Diwali, and National Day periods that significantly impact shipping volumes. Results must be broken down by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, OT, FR) and direction (import/export/transshipment). Historical accuracy metrics must be displayed alongside each forecast.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R015	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Dashboard Data Export and API Access	The system must provide data export capabilities for all executive dashboard KPI data in CSV, Excel (XLSX), and JSON formats. Exports must respect the current filter context including selected entities, date ranges, and currency. The system must also expose a RESTful API endpoint allowing authorized external systems (such as board reporting tools or investor portals) to programmatically access dashboard KPI data with OAuth 2.0 authentication. API responses must support pagination for large datasets and include metadata such as data freshness timestamps and currency conversion rates applied. Rate limiting must be enforced on API endpoints with configurable limits per API consumer. All data exports and API access must be audit-logged with user identity, timestamp, and data scope accessed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-001-R016	FEAT-031-1-001	MOD-031	Role-Based Dashboard Access Control	The system must enforce role-based access control (RBAC) for all executive dashboard features ensuring that KPI visibility, drill-down depth, and export capabilities are restricted based on the authenticated user's assigned role and entity access permissions. The system must support hierarchical roles including Group CEO (all entities, all KPIs), Entity Managing Director (single entity, all KPIs), Finance Director (all entities, financial KPIs only), Operations Director (all entities, operational KPIs only), and Regional Manager (single port, limited KPIs). Dashboard widgets must dynamically render or hide based on role permissions. Unauthorized drill-down attempts must be gracefully blocked with an appropriate access denied message in the user's preferred language. Permission changes must take effect immediately without requiring dashboard reload.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R001	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Profitability Analysis Report	The system must generate a detailed voyage profitability analysis report for each completed voyage showing total revenue versus total direct costs broken down by cost category. Revenue must include ocean freight, BAF, CAF, THC recovery, demurrage, detention, and other surcharges. Costs must include bunker fuel consumption (VLSFO, LSMGO), port dues, pilotage, towage, terminal handling charges, canal transit fees (Suez Canal), agency fees, and crew costs allocated to the voyage. The report must calculate voyage contribution margin, voyage GP percentage, and revenue per TEU. Multi-currency transactions must be converted to the voyage reporting currency using the applicable exchange rates. The report must compare actual results against the voyage estimate (pre-fixture estimate) highlighting favorable and unfavorable variances exceeding configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R002	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Schedule Reliability Metrics Dashboard	The system must calculate and display schedule reliability metrics for each shipping service showing on-time arrival percentage, on-time departure percentage, and average delay in hours at each port of call. On-time must be defined as arriving or departing within a configurable window (default: 24 hours) of the published schedule. Reliability metrics must be calculated on a rolling basis for the last 4 weeks, 12 weeks, 26 weeks, and 52 weeks. The dashboard must show reliability trends over time and allow comparison between different services. Port-specific reliability must identify chronic delay points such as congestion at JNPT or tidal restrictions at specific berths. The system must flag services with reliability below configurable thresholds (e.g., below 80%) and generate alerts to the operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R003	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Bunker Fuel Consumption Analytics	The system must provide detailed bunker fuel consumption analytics per voyage, per vessel, and per service route. Analytics must track actual consumption of VLSFO (Very Low Sulphur Fuel Oil), LSMGO (Low Sulphur Marine Gas Oil), and any other fuel grades against planned consumption from the voyage estimate. The system must calculate fuel efficiency metrics including consumption per nautical mile, consumption per TEU carried, and consumption per TEU-mile. Fuel consumption must be correlated with vessel speed, weather conditions, and cargo load factors to identify optimization opportunities. The system must track bunker costs in USD with conversion capability to entity reporting currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). ECA (Emission Control Area) fuel switching events must be tracked and reported separately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R004	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Port Productivity and Turnaround Analysis	The system must analyze and display port productivity metrics for each port call including gross crane moves per hour, net crane moves per hour, berth-to-berth turnaround time, total port stay duration, and cargo exchange volume (loads and discharges) in TEUs. Productivity must be tracked for each terminal at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT separately. The system must calculate average, median, and percentile distributions (P25, P75, P90) for each productivity metric over configurable time periods. Port productivity trends must be visualizable as time-series charts enabling identification of improving or deteriorating terminal performance. The system must benchmark port productivity against industry standards and flag ports or terminals where turnaround times consistently exceed acceptable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R005	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Service Route Capacity Utilization Trends	The system must track and display capacity utilization trends for each shipping service route on a voyage-by-voyage and weekly aggregated basis. Utilization must be measured as the percentage of available TEU slots filled with laden containers on each voyage leg. The system must distinguish between northbound and southbound (or eastbound and westbound) utilization for round-trip services to identify directional imbalances. Utilization analytics must separate dry containers from reefer containers given their different slot configurations and revenue characteristics. The system must project future utilization based on current bookings versus available capacity for upcoming voyages. Utilization below configurable thresholds must trigger yield management alerts recommending rate adjustments or slot reallocation actions to commercial teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R006	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Delay Root Cause Classification	The system must capture and classify root causes for all voyage delays exceeding a configurable threshold (default: 6 hours) per port call. Standard delay reason codes must include weather delays, port congestion, berth unavailability, crane breakdowns, labor disputes, customs holds, vessel mechanical issues, and waiting for cargo. Each delay event must record the delay duration in hours, the responsible party (terminal, port authority, customs, vessel operator), and free-text notes for additional context. The system must generate Pareto analysis charts showing the most frequent and most costly delay causes by service, by port, and by vessel over configurable time periods. Delay analytics must be available in both English and Arabic to support operational teams across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R007	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Vessel Performance Benchmarking	The system must enable comparative benchmarking of vessel performance across the managed fleet and any chartered-in tonnage. Benchmarking metrics must include revenue per TEU-day, cost per TEU-day, fuel consumption per nautical mile, average voyage GP margin, cargo capacity utilization, and schedule adherence percentage. Vessels must be groupable by vessel class, size range, age, and ownership type (owned, time-chartered, voyage-chartered) for meaningful comparisons. The system must generate ranking tables showing best and worst performing vessels on each metric with the ability to filter by time period and service deployment. Performance trends for individual vessels must be trackable over time to identify vessels with declining performance that may require maintenance or repositioning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R008	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Cost Variance Drill-Down	The system must provide drill-down variance analysis comparing actual voyage costs against estimated voyage costs at the individual cost line item level. For each voyage, the system must display estimated versus actual amounts for bunker fuel, port charges, canal dues, terminal handling, agency fees, insurance allocations, and other cost categories. Variances must be shown in both absolute monetary terms and percentage deviation with color-coded indicators for favorable (under budget) and unfavorable (over budget) results. The system must support multi-currency cost tracking with actual costs recorded in the local currency of each port (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa/Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) and converted to the voyage reporting currency. Users must be able to annotate variances with explanatory comments that are retained for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R009	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	CO2 Emissions Tracking per Voyage	The system must calculate and report CO2 emissions for each voyage based on actual fuel consumption data using IMO-approved emission factors for each fuel type (VLSFO: 3.151 tonnes CO2 per tonne fuel, LSMGO: 3.206 tonnes CO2 per tonne fuel). Emissions must be reported in absolute tonnes of CO2 and as intensity metrics including grams of CO2 per TEU-kilometre and the Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) as defined by IMO MEPC guidelines. The system must track emissions against the vessel's Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) rating trajectory to identify vessels at risk of falling into unfavorable CII categories (D or E). Emissions data must be exportable in formats compatible with IMO Data Collection System (DCS) reporting and EU MRV requirements. The system must support both English and Arabic emission report formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R010	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Service Network Visualization Map	The system must provide an interactive geographic map visualization showing all active shipping service routes with port-to-port connections rendered as voyage paths on a world map. The map must display vessel positions in near real-time (updated at minimum every 4 hours via AIS integration) showing vessel name, current speed, heading, and next port of call. Port markers must be color-coded to indicate current congestion levels and display summary statistics on hover including vessel count at berth, vessels at anchor, and average waiting time. Users must be able to click on any service route to view its schedule, current voyage status, and key performance metrics. The map must support zoom, pan, and search functionality with special focus on the Gulf region (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali) and Indian subcontinent (JNPT) ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R011	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Revenue Per TEU Waterfall Analysis	The system must generate waterfall chart visualizations decomposing voyage revenue per TEU into its constituent components for detailed revenue quality analysis. The waterfall must start with the base ocean freight rate and sequentially add or subtract surcharge components including Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF), Terminal Handling Charges (THC), Peak Season Surcharge (PSS), War Risk Surcharge, Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS), and any trade-specific surcharges. The chart must show the final effective revenue per TEU after all adjustments. Users must be able to compare revenue waterfalls across voyages on the same service to track rate evolution over time. The system must support filtering by cargo direction, container type, and shipper to analyze revenue quality by segment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R012	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Proforma vs Actual Voyage Schedule Comparison	The system must provide a visual comparison of proforma (planned) voyage schedules against actual voyage execution timelines displayed as Gantt-style timeline charts. For each port call, the system must show planned arrival, actual arrival, planned departure, actual departure, and the resulting variance in hours. Sea passage segments must display planned versus actual transit times with speed comparisons. The visual must clearly highlight port calls where deviations exceeded acceptable thresholds using color-coded bars. The system must calculate cumulative schedule drift across a complete round voyage to quantify the total impact of individual port delays. Historical proforma adherence data must be retained and analyzable for trend identification across multiple consecutive voyages on the same service rotation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R013	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Demurrage and Detention Revenue Analytics	The system must provide analytics on demurrage and detention (D&D) revenue generated per voyage, per port, and per service route. The system must track total D&D charges invoiced, D&D charges collected, D&D charges waived, and D&D charges outstanding by aging bucket (0-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, over 90 days). Analytics must identify chronic late-returning customers and ports with high free-time exceedance rates. The system must calculate average container dwell time at each port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and compare against granted free-time allocations. D&D revenue must be reportable in both the local entity currency and consolidated reporting currency. The system must flag anomalies such as unusually high waiver rates that may indicate commercial policy issues requiring management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R014	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Analytics Data Refresh and Latency SLA	The system must ensure that voyage analytics data is refreshed with a maximum latency of 15 minutes from the time operational data is updated in source systems (vessel operations, port operations, finance modules). Data refresh status must be visible to users via a last-updated timestamp displayed on each analytics view. If data refresh fails or exceeds the latency SLA, the system must display a warning banner indicating stale data and notify the system administrator. The data pipeline must support incremental updates rather than full data reloads to minimize processing time and system resource consumption. Historical data corrections in source systems must propagate to analytics within the next scheduled refresh cycle. The system must maintain an audit log of all data refresh events including start time, end time, record counts processed, and any errors encountered.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R015	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	Voyage Comparison Side-by-Side Analysis	The system must allow users to select two or more voyages on the same service route and display a side-by-side comparison of key performance metrics. Comparable metrics must include total revenue, total cost, voyage GP, TEUs carried, utilization percentage, bunker consumption, average speed, total voyage duration, and schedule adherence. The comparison must highlight the best and worst performing voyage for each metric using visual indicators. Users must be able to compare voyages across different time periods to assess seasonal patterns and year-over-year performance trends. The comparison view must support export to PDF and Excel for management reporting. Voyage comparison must work across entity boundaries when the same service route spans multiple entity jurisdictions (e.g., a Gulf-India service crossing Qatar and India entities).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-002-R016	FEAT-031-1-002	MOD-031	AI-Driven Voyage Optimization Recommendations	The system must leverage AI algorithms to generate voyage optimization recommendations based on historical voyage performance data. Recommendations must cover optimal sailing speed profiles balancing fuel cost against schedule requirements, recommended port call sequences to minimize ballast legs, and cargo mix suggestions to maximize voyage revenue per TEU. The AI engine must analyze at least 24 months of historical voyage data per service route to identify patterns and optimization opportunities. Each recommendation must include an estimated financial impact quantified in USD showing potential cost savings or revenue uplift. Recommendations must be presented in both English and Arabic with contextual explanations referencing specific voyage parameters. The system must track recommendation acceptance rates and actual outcomes versus predicted outcomes to continuously improve AI model accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R001	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Definition and Configuration	The system must allow authorized users to define and configure trade lanes as logical groupings of origin-destination port pairs for analytical purposes. Each trade lane must have a unique code, descriptive name in both English and Arabic, and a list of constituent port pairs using UN/LOCODE port codes (e.g., QAHMD-INNSA for Hamad Port to JNPT). Trade lanes must support hierarchical grouping such as Gulf-Indian Subcontinent (parent) containing Hamad-JNPT, Jebel Ali-JNPT, Khalifa-JNPT (children). The system must support bidirectional trade lane definition where the same trade lane encompasses both export and import flows. Trade lane configurations must be versionable with effective dates to track how trade lane definitions evolve over time as new port pairs are added or removed from service coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R002	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Volume Trend Analysis	The system must display TEU volume trends for each configured trade lane over configurable time periods with monthly, quarterly, and annual aggregation options. Volume trends must be visualized as time-series charts showing laden TEUs, empty TEUs, and total TEUs separately. The system must support overlay of multiple trade lanes on the same chart for comparison purposes. Year-over-year growth rates must be automatically calculated and displayed for each trade lane. The system must differentiate volume by container type (dry, reefer, special equipment) and by cargo direction (headhaul versus backhaul). Trade lane volume analytics must aggregate data across all entity bookings regardless of which entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) originated the booking, ensuring a complete corridor view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R003	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Revenue and Yield Analytics	The system must calculate and display revenue metrics per trade lane including total revenue, revenue per TEU, revenue per container, and revenue per TEU-kilometre. Yield metrics must be broken down by freight component (base ocean freight, surcharges, ancillary charges) to enable revenue quality analysis. The system must track yield trends over time to identify rate erosion or improvement on specific trade lanes. Yield analytics must support multi-currency display allowing users to view revenue metrics in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR with conversion using configurable exchange rate types. The system must compare actual yield against published tariff rates and contracted rates to measure discount levels and commercial effectiveness. Yield comparisons must be available against market benchmarks when external market rate data is integrated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R004	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Profitability Ranking Matrix	The system must generate a trade lane profitability ranking matrix displaying all active trade lanes sorted by configurable profitability metrics such as GP per TEU, GP margin percentage, contribution margin, or total GP. The matrix must use a color-coded heat map visualization with green indicating high-performing trade lanes, amber for moderate, and red for underperforming trade lanes based on configurable threshold values. Each trade lane row must show volume, revenue, cost, and profit metrics in a single consolidated view. The system must allow filtering by entity, service route, time period, and container type. Users must be able to click on any trade lane to drill into detailed voyage-level and booking-level profitability data. The matrix must support export to Excel with full formatting preserved for board reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R005	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Cargo Mix Analysis	The system must analyze and display the cargo mix composition for each trade lane showing the distribution of cargo types including dry bulk containerized, refrigerated perishables, hazardous goods (IMO classes 1-9), out-of-gauge cargo, and project cargo. Cargo mix must also be analyzable by commodity code (HS code) showing top commodity categories transported on each trade lane. The system must track cargo mix changes over time to identify shifting trade patterns such as increasing reefer volumes on Gulf-India corridors due to growing food exports. Revenue contribution by cargo type must be displayed alongside volume distribution to highlight high-value cargo segments. The analysis must identify trade lanes with high concentration risk where a single commodity or cargo type dominates, potentially exposing the business to sector-specific downturns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R006	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Transit Time Performance	The system must track and report port-to-port and door-to-door transit times for each trade lane comparing actual transit times against published and committed transit times. Transit time metrics must include average transit time, median transit time, standard deviation, and on-time delivery percentage (within committed transit window). The system must separate ocean transit time from port dwell time to identify whether delays are caused by sea passage or port operations. Transit time analysis must be available for specific origin-destination pairs within a trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port CY to JNPT CY). The system must track transit time competitiveness by comparing against known competitor transit times on the same trade lanes where market intelligence data is available. Transit time degradation trends must trigger automated alerts to commercial teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R007	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Equipment Imbalance Tracking	The system must track and visualize container equipment imbalances across trade lanes showing the net surplus or deficit of empty containers at key locations. For each trade lane, the system must display the ratio of export (laden) to import (laden) containers to identify directional imbalances. Equipment imbalance must be tracked by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer) as imbalances vary significantly by equipment type. The system must calculate the repositioning cost associated with empty container movements needed to correct imbalances on each trade lane. Forecasted imbalance projections must be generated based on booking pipeline data for the next 4 to 8 weeks. The system must highlight trade lanes with chronic imbalances requiring structural solutions such as foldable containers, triangulation routing, or strategic empty depot positioning at Gulf and India ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R008	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Market Share Estimation by Trade Lane	The system must provide market share estimation capabilities for each trade lane by comparing the company's TEU volumes against total market volumes sourced from port authority statistics or industry data providers (e.g., Container Trades Statistics, Alphaliner). Market share must be displayed as a percentage of total market volume for each trade lane on a monthly and quarterly basis. The system must track market share trends over time to identify lanes where market position is strengthening or eroding. Market share analytics must support both direct carrier volumes and any slot-sharing or vessel-sharing arrangement volumes. Where external market data is not available, the system must allow manual entry of estimated total market volumes with clear data source attribution. Market share data must be restricted to senior commercial and strategy roles via role-based access controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R009	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Seasonality Pattern Recognition	The system must identify and display seasonal patterns for each trade lane using at least 24 months of historical data to establish reliable seasonal indices. Seasonal patterns must be visualized as monthly indices showing which months typically experience above-average or below-average volumes relative to the annual mean. The system must account for regional calendar events affecting shipping patterns including Ramadan (variable dates per Hijri calendar), Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Diwali, Chinese New Year, and Western holiday seasons. The system must overlay actual current-year volumes against the seasonal forecast to identify deviations from expected patterns. Seasonal patterns must be generated separately for different cargo types as reefer cargo (perishables) and dry cargo often exhibit different seasonal profiles on Gulf-India trade corridors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R010	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Competitive Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide a competitive benchmarking dashboard for each trade lane allowing users to compare the company's service offering against known competitors. Benchmarking dimensions must include number of direct services, weekly frequency, published transit times, vessel sizes deployed, and port coverage. Competitor data must be maintainable through a combination of manual data entry by commercial teams and automated ingestion from public schedule databases where available. The dashboard must highlight competitive advantages (faster transit, more frequency) and disadvantages (fewer direct calls, older vessels) for each trade lane. Competitive intelligence must be timestamped and version-controlled showing how the competitive landscape evolves over time. Access to competitive benchmarking data must be restricted to commercial strategy and senior management roles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R011	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane INCOTERMS Distribution Analysis	The system must analyze the distribution of INCOTERMS used on bookings within each trade lane to understand the scope of service responsibility. The analysis must categorize bookings by INCOTERMS 2020 terms including EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, and DDP. The system must show the percentage of bookings and TEUs under each INCOTERMS category per trade lane. Trends in INCOTERMS usage must be trackable over time to identify shifts toward more or fewer carrier-controlled logistics scopes. Revenue per TEU must be correlated with INCOTERMS to identify higher-yielding service arrangements. The system must flag trade lanes where high percentages of FOB or EXW shipments indicate opportunities to upsell to more comprehensive logistics products covering door-to-port or door-to-door services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R012	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Sanctions and Trade Compliance Risk Overlay	The system must provide a compliance risk overlay on trade lane analytics flagging trade lanes that involve countries, ports, or regions subject to international trade sanctions or enhanced regulatory scrutiny. The system must reference sanctions lists from OFAC (US), EU, UN Security Council, and local regulatory authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Trade lanes involving sanctioned or high-risk jurisdictions must be visually flagged with warning indicators on all analytics views. The system must track the volume and value of cargo moving on flagged trade lanes for compliance reporting purposes. Sanctions data must be updated at minimum weekly from authoritative sources. The system must generate compliance reports showing total exposure by sanctioned jurisdiction for review by the compliance officer and senior management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R013	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Forecast vs Actual Comparison	The system must compare forecasted trade lane volumes and revenue against actual results to measure forecasting accuracy and enable continuous forecast improvement. Forecasts must be importable from the commercial planning module or uploadable via Excel templates with monthly granularity per trade lane. The system must calculate forecast accuracy metrics including Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), forecast bias (systematic over or under prediction), and tracking signal. Forecast accuracy must be reported per trade lane, per entity, and per forecast horizon (1-month, 3-month, 6-month ahead). The system must visually overlay forecast versus actual data on time-series charts with shaded confidence intervals showing forecast uncertainty ranges. Historical forecast accuracy trends must be maintained for at least 12 months to assess whether planning accuracy improves over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R014	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Hinterland Connectivity Analysis	The system must analyze hinterland connectivity metrics for key ports on each trade lane including inland transportation mode split (road versus rail), average inland transit time from major cargo origin or destination cities, and inland logistics cost as a proportion of total door-to-door cost. For the Gulf region, the system must track intermodal connectivity between Hamad Port and Doha industrial areas, Khalifa Port and Abu Dhabi/KIZAD, Jebel Ali and Dubai/Sharjah hinterland, and for India, JNPT connectivity to Mumbai, Pune, and northern India via dedicated freight corridors. Hinterland analysis must identify bottlenecks or capacity constraints in inland logistics chains that may affect port throughput. The system must support bilingual reporting in English and Arabic for Gulf region connectivity and English for India connectivity reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R015	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	AI-Powered Trade Lane Growth Opportunity Scoring	The system must use AI algorithms to score each trade lane on growth opportunity potential based on multiple factors including historical volume growth trajectory, GDP growth rates of origin and destination countries, commodity trade flow projections, competitive intensity, current market share headroom, and capacity deployment feasibility. Each trade lane must receive a composite growth opportunity score on a scale of 1-100 with contributing factor scores visible for transparency. The AI model must be retrained quarterly using the latest available macroeconomic and trade data. Growth opportunity scores must be displayed alongside profitability rankings to enable balanced strategic portfolio decisions. The system must generate natural language growth opportunity summaries in English and Arabic for each trade lane explaining the key drivers behind the assigned score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R016	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Hijri Calendar Period Analytics Support	The system must fully support Hijri (Islamic) calendar-based period analytics for trade lane reporting to accommodate business planning cycles in Qatar, UAE, and KSA that reference Hijri dates. Users must be able to select Hijri months and years as reporting periods for trade lane volume, revenue, and profitability analytics. The system must correctly map Gregorian date-stamped operational data to Hijri periods using accurate Hijri-Gregorian calendar conversion algorithms. Hijri period analytics must support year-over-year comparisons accounting for the approximately 11-day annual drift between Hijri and Gregorian calendars. Ramadan-period analysis must be available as a predefined analytical period given its significant impact on shipping patterns in the Gulf region. Report headers must display both Hijri and Gregorian date ranges when Hijri period analytics are selected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-003-R017	FEAT-031-1-003	MOD-031	Trade Lane Data Quality Monitoring	The system must implement data quality monitoring for trade lane analytics identifying and flagging records with missing, inconsistent, or potentially erroneous data. Quality checks must include validation of port codes against UN/LOCODE master data, detection of duplicate bookings or shipments, identification of revenue or cost outliers that exceed three standard deviations from the trade lane mean, and verification of container type consistency with reported commodity types. Data quality issues must be surfaced via a dedicated data quality dashboard showing the percentage of clean records versus flagged records per trade lane. The system must assign data quality scores to each trade lane's analytics to indicate the reliability of reported metrics. Users must be able to drill into flagged records to review and correct data quality issues. Quality monitoring results must be available in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R001	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Revenue Ranking and Tiering	The system must generate customer revenue rankings displaying all active customers sorted by total revenue contribution over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annually, trailing 12 months). The system must automatically classify customers into configurable tiers (e.g., Platinum, Gold, Silver, Bronze) based on revenue thresholds defined per entity. Customer revenue must be aggregated across all entities where the customer has active business to provide a consolidated group-level view as well as entity-specific views. Rankings must be displayable in any supported currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion. The system must track tier movement showing customers who have moved up or down in tier classification compared to the prior period. Revenue rankings must respect multi-entity access controls ensuring entity-level commercial teams only see their authorized customer data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R002	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Profitability Analysis	The system must calculate customer-level profitability by allocating direct costs to individual customers based on their shipment characteristics including trade lanes used, container types, cargo volumes, and service consumption patterns. Profitability metrics must include gross revenue, allocated direct costs (ocean freight cost, terminal handling, inland transportation, documentation fees), customer gross profit, and customer GP margin percentage. The system must identify customers who generate high revenue but low or negative profitability due to excessive empty repositioning, high claim rates, or unfavorable trade lane mix. Profitability analysis must be available at both the consolidated group level and individual entity level with inter-company cost allocations properly handled. The system must generate customer profitability scatter plots showing revenue versus margin to visually identify strategic customer segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R003	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Volume Trend and Forecast Dashboard	The system must display monthly TEU volume trends for each customer over a configurable historical period with a minimum of 24 months of history. Trend charts must show actual volumes alongside any contracted volume commitments (MQC - Minimum Quantity Commitments) from the customer's service contract. The system must automatically calculate contract fulfillment rates showing whether customers are meeting their committed volumes. Volume forecasts must be generated using AI algorithms analyzing historical patterns, seasonal adjustments specific to the customer's cargo type, and booking pipeline data for upcoming periods. Forecast accuracy must be tracked and displayed alongside the forecast. The dashboard must alert commercial teams when customers show declining volume trends exceeding a configurable threshold (e.g., greater than 15% decline over three consecutive months) potentially indicating customer attrition risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R004	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Trade Lane Distribution Analysis	The system must analyze and display the distribution of each customer's shipment volumes across trade lanes showing which corridors generate the most revenue and TEU volume per customer. The distribution must be visualized as both tabular data and Sankey diagram or treemap charts showing the flow of customer cargo across origin-destination port pairs. The system must identify customers with high concentration on a single trade lane (above 80% of volume) as a potential risk factor if that trade lane experiences disruption. Trade lane distribution analysis must compare current period against prior periods to detect shifts in customer shipping patterns. The system must flag customers whose trade lane mix is shifting toward lower-yielding corridors requiring proactive commercial engagement. Analysis must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL chart rendering for Arabic users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R005	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Payment Behavior Analytics	The system must track and analyze customer payment behavior including average days to pay (DSO - Days Sales Outstanding), payment punctuality rate (percentage of invoices paid within agreed credit terms), and aging analysis of outstanding receivables by bucket (current, 1-30 days overdue, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, over 90 days). Payment analytics must be correlated with customer revenue tier to identify high-value customers with deteriorating payment patterns that may indicate financial distress. The system must calculate credit utilization rates showing how much of the customer's approved credit limit is currently consumed. Payment behavior trends must be trackable over time with alerts when a customer's average DSO exceeds their agreed payment terms by a configurable threshold. Multi-currency receivables must be tracked in both original invoice currency and entity reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R006	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Wallet Share Estimation	The system must estimate the company's wallet share (share of customer's total shipping spend) for key customers based on available data including known competitor usage, total market volumes on the customer's trade lanes, and customer-declared total shipping budgets where available. Wallet share must be estimated as a percentage range (e.g., 30-40%) given the inherent uncertainty in competitor intelligence. The system must track wallet share trends over time to measure commercial effectiveness in growing share with key accounts. Where customers operate across multiple entities, the consolidated wallet share across all entities must be calculated. The system must allow commercial managers to manually input or adjust wallet share estimates when they have direct customer intelligence. Wallet share data must be accessible only to authorized commercial and management roles given its competitive sensitivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R007	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Churn Risk Prediction	The system must employ AI-driven predictive analytics to identify customers at risk of churning (reducing or ceasing business) based on behavioral signals including declining booking volumes, increasing booking cancellation rates, decreasing shipment frequency, deteriorating payment behavior, and rising claim or complaint frequency. Each customer must receive a churn risk score on a configurable scale (e.g., Low, Medium, High, Critical) updated at least weekly. The AI model must analyze patterns from historically churned customers to identify leading indicators of churn. When a customer's churn risk score changes to High or Critical, the system must automatically generate alerts to the assigned account manager and commercial director. The system must provide explainable AI outputs showing which specific factors are driving the elevated churn risk score for each customer to enable targeted retention interventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R008	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Segmentation and Clustering	The system must support multidimensional customer segmentation using configurable criteria including revenue contribution, volume, profitability, trade lane diversity, cargo type mix, service product usage (FCL, LCL, reefer, hazardous), payment reliability, and growth trajectory. The system must provide AI-driven clustering capabilities that automatically group customers into behavioral segments based on their transactional patterns without requiring manual rule definition. Each segment must be visualizable on interactive scatter plots and radar charts showing the distinguishing characteristics of each cluster. Segment membership must be refreshed periodically (configurable, default monthly) as customer behavior evolves. The system must support user-defined custom segments based on business rules in addition to AI-generated segments. Segmentation results must be exportable for integration with CRM systems and targeted marketing campaign tools.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R009	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Contract Performance Monitoring	The system must monitor and report on customer contract performance including volume commitment fulfillment, rate adherence, and contract profitability. For each active service contract, the system must display committed volumes versus actual shipped volumes on a cumulative basis through the contract period with projection to contract end. The system must calculate whether the customer is on track to meet their Minimum Quantity Commitment (MQC) and flag contracts at risk of underperformance. Rate analysis must compare contracted rates against spot market rates for the same trade lanes to identify contracts where the company is over or under market. Contract profitability must be calculated incorporating all shipments under the contract against allocated costs. The system must generate contract renewal recommendations based on performance data including suggested volume commitments and rate adjustments for the renewal negotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R010	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Cross-Entity Customer Consolidation Logic	The system must implement customer consolidation logic to recognize and merge analytics for customers who transact across multiple entities under different local customer codes. The system must support a master customer record concept that links entity-specific customer accounts (e.g., same freight forwarder may have separate accounts in Qatar, UAE, and India entities). Consolidated analytics must aggregate revenue, volume, and profitability across all linked entity accounts for a unified customer view. The consolidation logic must handle parent-subsidiary corporate structures where a holding company's analytics should include all subsidiary entity accounts. Data access for consolidated views must enforce the intersection of entity-level permissions ensuring users only see data for entities they are authorized to access. Customer consolidation mappings must be maintainable by authorized master data administrators with full audit trail of all mapping changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R011	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Revenue by Container Type Analysis	The system must analyze and display customer revenue broken down by container type including 20-foot general purpose (20GP), 40-foot general purpose (40GP), 40-foot high cube (40HC), 20-foot reefer (20RF), 40-foot reefer (40RF), 20-foot open top (20OT), 40-foot open top (40OT), flat rack, and tank container. Revenue per container type must be shown alongside TEU volume to enable revenue density analysis (revenue per TEU by container type). The system must identify customers whose container type mix is shifting over time, for example increasing reefer usage indicating growing cold chain logistics needs. Revenue by container type must be comparable across customers within the same tier or segment to identify cross-selling opportunities. The analysis must support multi-currency display with conversion at configurable exchange rates and must be accessible in both English and Arabic interface languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R012	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Complaint and Claims Correlation	The system must correlate customer complaint and claims data with revenue analytics to provide a complete view of customer health. For each customer, the system must display total claims filed, claims value, claims-to-revenue ratio, average claim resolution time, and claim outcome distribution (paid, partially paid, rejected). Complaint frequency and severity trends must be trackable over time to identify customers with increasing dissatisfaction. The system must identify correlation patterns between claim rates and churn risk, flagging customers where high claim rates may be a leading indicator of future volume decline. Claims analytics must cover all claim types relevant to container shipping including cargo damage, cargo shortage, container damage, delay claims, and mis-delivery. The system must generate customer health scorecards combining revenue, profitability, payment behavior, and claims metrics into a unified customer quality index.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R013	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Top Customer Executive Summary Report	The system must generate automated executive summary reports for top customers (configurable number, default top 50 by revenue) providing a comprehensive one-page overview per customer. The report must include customer profile information, revenue and volume trends (12-month chart), profitability metrics, trade lane distribution, contract status summary, payment behavior summary, and any open claims or complaints. Reports must be generated in PDF format with professional layout supporting both English and Arabic bilingual content with proper RTL formatting for Arabic sections. The report must include the company logo and be formatted for executive presentation. Automated generation must be schedulable on monthly or quarterly frequencies with email distribution to assigned account managers and commercial directors. Historical reports must be archived and retrievable through the analytics platform for comparative review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R014	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Revenue Concentration Risk Analysis	The system must calculate and display customer revenue concentration metrics including the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI) for the customer portfolio and the percentage of total revenue attributable to the top 5, top 10, top 20, and top 50 customers. Concentration analysis must be available at group consolidated level and per entity to identify entities with dangerously high customer concentration. The system must track concentration trends over time to assess whether the customer portfolio is diversifying or becoming more concentrated. Risk thresholds must be configurable per entity with visual alerts when concentration exceeds acceptable levels (e.g., when top 5 customers exceed 40% of entity revenue). The system must model the revenue impact of hypothetical customer loss scenarios showing the financial exposure if specific major customers were to churn. Concentration analytics must be restricted to senior commercial and finance management roles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R015	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Acquisition Cost and Lifetime Value Analytics	The system must calculate and track Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) estimates for all active customers based on historical revenue patterns, projected future revenue using AI-driven forecasting, estimated customer relationship duration, and applicable discount rate. CLV calculations must account for the entity-specific cost of capital and currency-adjusted revenue streams for multi-entity customers. The system must also track customer acquisition cost (CAC) where data is available from the commercial team's expense tracking, including sales team costs, marketing campaign attributions, and onboarding costs. The CLV-to-CAC ratio must be calculated for each customer segment to assess the return on commercial investment. The system must identify high-CLV customers for premium service treatment and low-CLV customers where service costs may need optimization. CLV models must be refreshable quarterly as new transactional data becomes available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R016	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Analytics Arabic Localization	The system must provide full Arabic language localization for all customer revenue analytics interfaces including navigation menus, filter labels, chart titles, axis labels, tooltips, data table headers, and report content. Arabic text must render in proper right-to-left (RTL) direction with appropriate font selection supporting Arabic typography. All customer names must be displayable in both English and Arabic where dual-language customer master data is maintained. Numerical values must use Western Arabic numerals (0-9) consistently across both English and Arabic interfaces for data consistency. Date displays must support dual Hijri-Gregorian format when the user's locale is set to Arabic. Chart visualizations must properly mirror for RTL layout including reversing bar chart directions and time-series axis orientation. The system must support instant language switching between English and Arabic without data loss or page reload.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R017	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Analytics Data Access Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all customer analytics data access events recording the user identity, timestamp, customer records accessed, analytics features used, filters applied, and any data exports performed. The audit trail must capture both interactive dashboard access and API-based data retrieval. Audit records must be stored for a minimum retention period of 7 years to comply with regulatory requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must provide audit trail query interfaces for compliance officers to search and review access patterns by user, customer, date range, or access type. Suspicious access patterns such as bulk data exports or access to customer data outside a user's normal entity scope must be automatically flagged for compliance review. Audit trail data must be tamper-proof with integrity verification mechanisms preventing unauthorized modification or deletion of audit records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-1-004-R018	FEAT-031-1-004	MOD-031	Customer Upsell and Cross-Sell Opportunity Scoring	The system must use AI algorithms to identify and score upsell and cross-sell opportunities within the existing customer portfolio. Upsell opportunities must include identification of customers currently using standard service tiers who could benefit from premium services (e.g., priority loading, guaranteed space, fast-track documentation). Cross-sell opportunities must identify customers using only ocean freight who could be offered inland logistics, customs brokerage, warehousing, or reefer services based on their cargo profile and trade lane patterns. Each opportunity must receive a probability score and estimated annual revenue impact in USD with entity currency equivalents. Opportunities must be ranked and presented to account managers as actionable recommendations with supporting data. The AI model must learn from historical conversion data (accepted vs. declined offers) to improve scoring accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R002	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Freight Rate Prediction Engine	The system must implement a freight rate prediction engine that forecasts spot and contract rates for major container shipping routes involving Middle East and Indian subcontinent ports. Predictions must be generated in multiple currencies including USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with real-time exchange rate integration. The model must incorporate supply-demand dynamics, bunker fuel price trends, carrier capacity announcements, and geopolitical risk factors. Rate predictions must be segmented by container size (TEU/FEU), cargo type (dry, reefer, hazardous, over-dimensional), and service type (FCL/LCL). The engine must achieve a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of less than 8% on 30-day forecasts validated against actual market rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R003	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Port Congestion and Dwell Time Prediction	The system must predict port congestion levels and average container dwell times at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT using real-time AIS vessel tracking data, historical berth occupancy rates, and weather forecasts. Predictions must be updated every 4 hours and provide 24-hour, 48-hour, and 7-day forward estimates. The model must factor in known disruptions such as planned port maintenance windows, regulatory inspection surges during customs audit periods, and seasonal volume spikes during pre-Ramadan cargo rushes. Alerts must be generated when predicted congestion exceeds configurable thresholds per entity, and alternative routing suggestions must be provided to operations teams in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R003	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	AI-Driven Claim Triage Classification	The system must apply an AI triage engine that automatically classifies incoming claims into severity categories: Critical (estimated value exceeding USD 100,000 or total loss), High (USD 25,000–100,000 or significant partial damage), Medium (USD 5,000–25,000 or moderate damage), and Low (below USD 5,000 or cosmetic damage). The AI model must analyze damage descriptions in both English and Arabic, cross-reference historical claim patterns for the same trade lane, commodity type, and vessel, and produce a confidence score between 0 and 1. Claims with confidence below 0.7 must be flagged for manual triage review. The triage must complete within 30 seconds of claim submission and must account for currency conversion to USD using the daily exchange rate for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R008	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Duplicate Claim Detection	The system must detect and flag potential duplicate claims at registration time by comparing the new claim against existing claims using a composite matching algorithm. The algorithm must evaluate similarity across container number, bill of lading number, voyage reference, claimant, damage description, and claim date with configurable matching thresholds for each field. When a potential duplicate is detected with a similarity score above 0.85, the system must alert the registering user, display the existing claim details side-by-side, and require explicit confirmation to proceed with registration. The duplicate detection must work across entities—for example, detecting that a claim already filed under the UAE entity for a Jebel Ali discharge matches a new claim filed under the India entity for the same container arriving at JNPT on a transshipment routing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R013	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claim Registration Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for every claim from the moment of registration. The audit trail must record every field value captured at registration, the user or system process that created the record, the timestamp in UTC and local entity timezone, the IP address and device identifier, and the source channel (web portal, mobile app, EDI, email). Any subsequent modification to registration data must be logged as a separate audit entry preserving the before and after values. The audit trail must be queryable by claim reference, user, date range, and field name. All audit entries must be stored in compliance with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority, UAE Federal Law, KSA ZATCA, and Indian Information Technology Act data retention requirements, with a minimum retention period of 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R001	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Hague-Visby Rules Knowledge Base	The system must maintain a comprehensive, structured knowledge base of the Hague-Visby Rules (Brussels Protocol 1968) including all Articles, with particular emphasis on Article III (carrier responsibilities for seaworthiness, care of cargo, and bill of lading issuance), Article IV (carrier defenses and immunities including the 17 excepted perils such as act of God, act of war, quarantine, inherent vice, insufficiency of packing, and fire), and Article IV Rule 5 (package and unit limitation of liability at 666.67 SDR per package or 2 SDR per kilogram gross weight). The knowledge base must also incorporate local maritime law variations: Qatar Maritime Law (Law No. 15 of 1980), UAE Federal Maritime Law (Federal Law No. 26 of 1981), KSA Ports Authority regulations, and Indian Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1925, identifying where local provisions supplement or deviate from the Hague-Visby baseline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R004	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Package Limitation Calculation Engine	The system must calculate the carrier's maximum liability under the Hague-Visby package limitation rules (Article IV Rule 5) for every claim. The calculation must determine the number of packages or shipping units as declared on the bill of lading, compute the limitation using the higher of 666.67 SDR per package or 2 SDR per kilogram of gross weight of the goods, and convert the SDR amount to the claim currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) using the IMF's official SDR exchange rate on the date of the bill of lading. The system must handle containerized cargo correctly—identifying whether the bill of lading enumerates individual packages within the container (in which case each package is a unit) or lists the container itself as the unit. The calculation result must be compared to the claimed amount to determine if limitation applies, and the system must flag claims where breaking the limitation under Article IV Rule 5(e) may be arguable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R007	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Multi-Party Liability Apportionment	The system must support liability apportionment across multiple parties when a claim involves shared responsibility. Parties must include the ocean carrier, feeder vessel operator, port terminal operator (at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT), inland haulier, freight forwarder, and shipper. The apportionment engine must consider the stage of transport at which damage occurred based on survey evidence, the contractual relationships defined by the transport document type (combined transport B/L, port-to-port B/L, or through B/L), and applicable INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP) that determine risk transfer points. The system must calculate each party's liability share as a percentage and compute the monetary amount in the claim currency. Apportionment decisions must be documented with reasoning and must be reviewable and adjustable by claims handlers, with all changes recorded in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R004	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Revenue and Cash Flow Forecasting	The system must generate multi-entity revenue and cash flow forecasts for each legal entity operating in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Forecasts must be produced in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated reporting currency (USD) with proper intercompany elimination logic. The forecasting model must incorporate booked shipments, pipeline opportunities weighted by probability, historical collection patterns, credit terms by customer segment, and seasonal billing cycles. Hijri calendar date alignment must be supported for KSA and Qatar entities where fiscal reporting follows Hijri milestones. Forecasts must reconcile with actual GL postings within a configurable tolerance threshold, defaulting to 5% variance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R005	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Vessel Arrival Time Prediction (ETA AI)	The system must predict vessel estimated time of arrival (ETA) at destination ports using a combination of AIS positional data, historical voyage performance, weather and sea-state forecasts, canal transit schedules (Suez Canal), and port schedule data. Predictions must be continuously refined as the vessel progresses along its route, with accuracy targets of ±4 hours for vessels within 48 hours of arrival and ±12 hours for vessels more than 7 days out. The system must support prediction for all vessel types including container ships, bulk carriers, and roll-on/roll-off vessels calling at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. ETA predictions must trigger downstream workflow updates for customs pre-clearance, yard planning, and trucking dispatch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R006	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Demand Sensing from External Data Sources	The system must ingest and process external data sources for demand sensing including global trade indices (Baltic Dry Index, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index), commodity price feeds, GDP growth forecasts for GCC and India, PMI indicators, and port throughput statistics published by regional port authorities. Data ingestion pipelines must handle API-based feeds, scheduled file imports (CSV, XML, JSON), and web scraping where APIs are unavailable. Each external data source must be tagged with reliability scores and staleness thresholds. When a source becomes unavailable or exceeds its staleness threshold, the system must gracefully degrade by falling back to historical trend extrapolation and must log the degradation event for analyst review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R007	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Model Training and Retraining Pipeline	The system must implement an automated machine learning model training and retraining pipeline that supports scheduled retraining (daily, weekly, monthly) and event-triggered retraining when model drift is detected. The pipeline must support multiple algorithm families including time-series models (ARIMA, Prophet, LSTM), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM), and ensemble methods. Feature engineering must automatically derive shipping-specific features such as vessel utilization rates, trade lane seasonality indices, port pair transit times, and INCOTERMS distribution shifts. Each model version must be tracked with full lineage metadata including training data range, hyperparameters, validation metrics (RMSE, MAE, MAPE), and approval status. Only models that pass automated validation gates may be promoted to production.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R008	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Scenario Planning and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a scenario planning interface that allows analysts to create what-if scenarios by adjusting key variables such as freight rates, fuel costs, exchange rates, trade volumes, and port capacity. Each scenario must be saveable, shareable across entities, and comparable side-by-side with baseline forecasts and other scenarios. The interface must support both manual parameter adjustment via sliders and input fields, and AI-suggested scenarios based on historical analogues (e.g., similar geopolitical disruptions, pandemic impacts, canal blockages). Scenario results must be viewable in both Arabic and English with proper RTL layout support, and all monetary values must be displayed in the user's preferred currency with conversion rates clearly shown.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R009	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Forecast Accuracy Tracking and Model Performance Dashboard	The system must maintain a forecast accuracy tracking dashboard that compares predicted values against actuals for every forecast category (volume, rate, congestion, revenue, ETA). Accuracy metrics must include MAPE, weighted MAPE, bias indicators, and tracking signal values. The dashboard must display accuracy trends over time with drill-down capability by trade lane, port, entity, container type, and forecast horizon. When forecast accuracy falls below configurable thresholds (default: MAPE exceeding 15% for volume forecasts, 10% for rate forecasts), automated alerts must be sent to the analytics team. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts and support Hijri date selectors for KSA/Qatar users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R010	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Anomaly Detection in Shipping Patterns	The system must implement real-time anomaly detection algorithms that identify unusual patterns in container shipping operations including unexpected volume spikes or drops on specific trade lanes, abnormal dwell times at ports, unusual booking-to-shipment conversion rates, and irregular pricing patterns that may indicate market manipulation or data quality issues. Anomalies must be classified by severity (informational, warning, critical) and by type (volume, timing, financial, operational). Each detected anomaly must generate an investigation ticket with contextual data showing the normal range, the anomalous value, potential root causes suggested by AI, and recommended corrective actions. Anomaly detection sensitivity must be configurable per entity to accommodate different operational profiles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R011	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Multi-Currency Forecast Consolidation	The system must consolidate forecasts across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) into unified multi-currency views. Consolidation must support both current-rate and historical-rate translation methods as required by IFRS and local GAAP standards. The system must handle forecast currency conversion using forward exchange rates for future periods and spot rates for current-period estimates. When exchange rate volatility exceeds configurable thresholds, the system must generate sensitivity analyses showing forecast impact at ±5%, ±10%, and ±15% currency movement scenarios. All consolidated forecasts must properly eliminate intercompany transactions between entities and must be auditable with full drill-down from consolidated figures to source entity forecasts in their local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R012	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Seasonal Pattern Library for GCC and India Markets	The system must maintain and continuously update a seasonal pattern library specific to GCC and Indian subcontinent shipping markets. Patterns must capture Ramadan-related volume shifts (typically 15-25% reduction in general cargo, increase in food and consumer goods), Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha pre-shipment surges, Diwali season demand spikes for Indian trade lanes, Qatar National Day and UAE National Day logistics impacts, Indian budget season effects on import duties and consequent volume changes, and summer heat restrictions on certain hazardous cargo movements. Each seasonal pattern must be tagged with historical confidence levels and must automatically adjust based on Hijri calendar date calculations for Islamic holidays that shift annually on the Gregorian calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R013	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Inventory and Equipment Repositioning Forecast	The system must forecast empty container availability and repositioning needs across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT based on predicted import-export imbalances per trade lane and container type. The forecasting model must account for typical equipment turnaround times at each port, inland depot capacities, and carrier equipment interchange agreements. Forecasts must be generated for 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, and reefer container categories separately, as the repositioning economics differ significantly by type. The system must recommend optimal repositioning routes considering triangulation opportunities (e.g., moving empties from surplus import markets like Jebel Ali to deficit export markets) and must calculate repositioning cost estimates in local currency for each recommended movement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R014	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Offline Forecast Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to the most recent forecast data sets when network connectivity is unavailable, which is common during vessel inspections at anchorage, remote warehouse visits, and travel between Gulf states. Users must be able to download forecast reports and dashboards for offline viewing through a progressive web application or dedicated mobile cache. When connectivity is restored, any annotations, scenario adjustments, or notes made offline must synchronize with the central system using conflict resolution logic that preserves the most recent edit. Offline data packages must be configurable by entity and role to minimize storage requirements on mobile devices while ensuring users have access to the forecasts most relevant to their operational scope.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R015	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Natural Language Forecast Query Interface	The system must provide a natural language query interface that allows users to ask forecast-related questions in both Arabic and English, such as 'What is the expected volume for Jebel Ali reefer imports next month?' or the Arabic equivalent. The NLP engine must understand shipping-specific terminology including port codes (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA), container type codes, INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, EXW, DDP), and trade lane references. Query results must be returned as formatted data visualizations (charts, tables) with the option to export as PDF or Excel. The system must maintain a query history per user and suggest related follow-up queries based on the current question context and the user's role and entity affiliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-001-R016	FEAT-031-2-001	MOD-031	Forecast Alert and Notification Configuration	The system must allow users to configure personalized forecast alert rules based on threshold breaches, trend changes, and significant deviations from plan. Alert conditions must support compound logic such as 'notify me when Hamad Port import volume forecast exceeds 5000 TEU AND average dwell time prediction exceeds 7 days.' Notifications must be deliverable via in-app notification, email, SMS, and Microsoft Teams or Slack integration. Alert messages must be generated in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) and must include contextual summary data, a direct link to the relevant forecast dashboard, and AI-generated recommended actions. Users must be able to snooze, acknowledge, or escalate alerts, with full audit logging of alert lifecycle events across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R001	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Global Freight Rate Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide a freight rate benchmarking dashboard that compares the organization's actual rates against market indices (Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, Drewry World Container Index, Freightos Baltic Index) and estimated competitor rates on matching trade lanes. Benchmarking must be segmented by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer), service type (FCL, LCL), and INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, EXW, CFR, DDP). Rate comparisons must be displayed in multiple currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with the ability to toggle between them. Historical rate evolution must be visualizable over selectable time ranges from 1 month to 5 years, with trend lines and moving averages to identify market cycles and the company's relative positioning within those cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R002	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Competitor Activity Tracking and Profiling	The system must maintain competitor profiles for major shipping lines and freight forwarders operating on trade lanes served by the organization across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each profile must track estimated market share by trade lane, service frequency, vessel deployment patterns derived from AIS data analysis, published rate changes, new service announcements, and customer win/loss intelligence gathered from CRM integration. Competitor profiles must be automatically enriched from public data sources including carrier websites, port authority statistics, and industry publications. Users must be able to tag and annotate competitor activities with strategic notes, and the system must generate quarterly competitor landscape reports comparing market position changes across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R003	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Trade Flow Analysis and Visualization	The system must provide interactive trade flow visualization maps showing container movement volumes and values between origin-destination pairs relevant to GCC and India trade corridors. Visualization must support filtering by commodity type (using HS codes at 2-digit and 4-digit levels), container type, time period (Gregorian and Hijri calendar support), and value thresholds in any supported currency. Trade flows must be overlaid on geographic maps with animated directional indicators showing volume intensity. The system must identify emerging trade patterns such as new commodity flows, shifting origin countries, and growing destination markets. Data must be sourced from customs declarations, bill of lading databases, and port throughput statistics, with clear attribution of data freshness and source reliability for each visualization layer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R004	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Market Share Calculation and Trend Analysis	The system must calculate the organization's market share on each active trade lane using internal shipment data compared against total market volumes obtained from port authority statistics and customs data. Market share must be calculated by TEU volume, revenue value, and number of shipments. Trends must be trackable over monthly, quarterly, and annual periods with drill-down by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), customer segment, and cargo type. The system must automatically flag trade lanes where market share has declined by more than 2 percentage points over a rolling 3-month period and generate AI-recommended recovery strategies. Market share reports must support multi-entity consolidation with proper handling of intercompany shipments to avoid double-counting volumes in consolidated views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R005	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Regulatory Change Impact Assessment	The system must monitor and assess the business impact of regulatory changes across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India that affect container shipping operations. Monitored regulations must include customs duty rate changes, import/export restrictions, free zone policy updates, cabotage rules, environmental regulations (IMO 2020 sulfur cap compliance, carbon intensity indicator requirements), and bilateral trade agreement modifications. Each regulatory change must be scored for business impact (low, medium, high, critical) with AI-generated analysis of affected trade lanes, estimated financial impact in local currency, and recommended operational adjustments. The system must provide advance warning where regulatory changes have announced effective dates and must track compliance deadlines with automated reminders sent to relevant entity compliance officers in their preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R006	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Commodity Price Tracking and Correlation Analysis	The system must track commodity prices for key goods transported through Middle East and India trade lanes including crude oil, refined petroleum products, steel, aluminum, chemicals, food grains, textiles, and electronics components. Commodity prices must be correlated with container shipping volumes and rates to identify leading indicators for demand changes. The system must display commodity price trends in USD and local currencies (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with correlation coefficients and lag analysis against shipping volume data. When significant commodity price movements are detected (configurable threshold, default ±10% in 30 days), the system must generate impact assessments predicting the likely effect on container volumes for affected trade lanes within 30 to 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R007	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Port Performance Benchmarking Across GCC and India	The system must benchmark operational performance metrics across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA) as well as competitor ports in the region including King Abdullah Port (SAKAP), Mundra (INMUN), and Salalah (OMSLL). Benchmarking metrics must include average vessel turnaround time, container moves per crane per hour, truck turnaround time, average dwell time by container status (import, export, transshipment), and customs clearance processing time. Data must be gathered from port authority published statistics, operational data where available, and estimated from AIS and vessel schedule analysis. The benchmarking dashboard must support side-by-side comparison of up to 6 ports simultaneously and must be viewable in both Arabic and English with RTL layout support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R008	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Customer Churn Risk Intelligence	The system must analyze customer booking patterns to identify churn risk indicators such as declining booking frequency, shrinking shipment volumes, increasing rate negotiation pressure, longer payment cycles, and increased usage of competitor services detected through market intelligence. A churn risk score must be calculated for each active customer account using machine learning classification models trained on historical churn data. Customers must be segmented into risk categories (low, moderate, high, critical) with entity-specific thresholds for the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. For high and critical risk customers, the system must generate AI-recommended retention actions including suggested rate adjustments, service improvement proposals, and relationship management escalation protocols. Churn risk dashboards must be accessible to sales teams with proper entity-level data isolation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R009	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	New Market Opportunity Identification	The system must use AI algorithms to identify new market opportunities including underserved trade lanes, emerging commodity flows, and growing customer segments that represent expansion potential for the organization. Opportunity identification must analyze global trade data, regional economic indicators, new infrastructure developments (e.g., new port terminals, free zone expansions, railway connections such as Etihad Rail), and changing trade policy landscapes. Each identified opportunity must be scored by estimated revenue potential in USD and local currency, required investment level, competitive intensity, and strategic alignment. Opportunities must be presented with supporting evidence and data visualizations and must be filterable by target entity, required capability, and time-to-market estimate to enable strategic planning teams across all four operating regions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R010	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Industry News and Event Aggregation	The system must aggregate shipping industry news, press releases, and event announcements from major industry sources including Lloyd's List, The Journal of Commerce, Splash247, Container News, and regional sources covering GCC and Indian subcontinent maritime markets. News items must be automatically categorized by relevance (trade lane, port, carrier, regulation, technology) and sentiment (positive, negative, neutral) using NLP analysis. Arabic-language news sources must be supported alongside English sources, with automatic translation capability in both directions. Users must be able to subscribe to topic-specific news feeds and receive daily or weekly digest emails. The system must highlight news items that have potential operational impact on the organization's trade lanes and must link relevant news to affected forecasts, customer accounts, or trade lanes in the analytics system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R011	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Service Level Benchmarking Against Market Standards	The system must benchmark the organization's service level performance against industry standards and competitor offerings across key metrics including transit time reliability, booking confirmation speed, documentation accuracy, claims processing time, and end-to-end shipment visibility coverage. Service level data must be gathered from internal operational systems and compared against published carrier service commitments, customer satisfaction surveys, and industry benchmark reports. Benchmarking must be performed separately for each trade lane and service type (door-to-door, port-to-port, CY-CY, CFS-CFS). Results must be presented as scorecards with color-coded indicators showing areas of competitive advantage and areas requiring improvement. The system must track service level trends over time and correlate service quality metrics with customer retention rates and market share changes per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R012	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Geopolitical Risk Assessment for Trade Lanes	The system must provide continuous geopolitical risk assessment for all active trade lanes with specific focus on risks relevant to Middle East and Indian subcontinent shipping routes. Risk factors must include regional conflicts, sanctions regimes (US OFAC, EU, UN Security Council), piracy risk zones (Gulf of Aden, Strait of Hormuz, Indian Ocean), canal disruption risks (Suez Canal), trade war escalation indicators, and political instability in origin or destination countries. Each trade lane must be assigned a composite risk score updated daily, with historical trend visualization. When risk scores exceed configurable thresholds, the system must generate alerts with AI-recommended mitigation strategies including alternative routing options, insurance coverage adjustments, and inventory buffer recommendations. Risk assessments must be available in both Arabic and English with proper terminology localization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R013	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Carrier Alliance and Service Network Analysis	The system must map and analyze carrier alliance structures (2M, Ocean Alliance, THE Alliance) and their service network coverage on trade lanes relevant to GCC and India operations. The analysis must track alliance membership changes, service rotation modifications, vessel upgrades or downsizes, and new direct service launches. The system must visualize how alliance service networks connect to feeder services at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, showing transshipment connectivity and transit time implications. When carriers announce service changes, the system must automatically assess the impact on the organization's routing options and competitive positioning. Analysis reports must calculate the effective capacity changes on each trade lane and must estimate the impact on freight rate dynamics based on supply-demand balance shifts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R014	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Market Intelligence Data Quality and Freshness Monitoring	The system must implement a comprehensive data quality and freshness monitoring framework for all market intelligence data sources. Each data source must be assigned a quality score based on completeness, accuracy, timeliness, and consistency metrics. Data freshness must be tracked with configurable staleness thresholds per source type (e.g., freight rate indices must not be older than 24 hours, port throughput statistics must not be older than 30 days). When data quality falls below acceptable thresholds or data becomes stale, the affected dashboards and reports must display clear warnings indicating degraded data quality. The system must maintain a data source catalog showing all active feeds, their update frequencies, last successful ingestion timestamps, and historical reliability metrics. Data quality reports must be generated monthly for review by the analytics governance team across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R015	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Competitive Rate Intelligence and Win-Loss Analysis	The system must track competitive rate intelligence by analyzing tender outcomes, customer-reported competitor pricing, and market rate surveys. Win-loss analysis must be performed for every significant tender and rate negotiation, capturing the competitor(s) involved, their estimated rate levels, service differentiators cited by the customer, and the final outcome. The system must calculate win rates by trade lane, customer segment, container type, and entity. AI models must identify patterns in wins and losses to recommend optimal pricing strategies and service packaging. Rate intelligence must be stored securely with access restricted to authorized commercial team members per entity. All competitor rate data must be handled in compliance with local competition law requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, with automated data retention policies and audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R016	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Arabic RTL Market Intelligence Interface	The system must provide a fully functional Arabic RTL (right-to-left) interface for all market intelligence dashboards, reports, and analysis tools. All charts, tables, and data visualizations must render correctly in RTL mode with proper Arabic numeral display options (Western Arabic or Eastern Arabic numerals based on user preference). Map-based trade flow visualizations must support Arabic labels for ports, countries, and trade lanes. Search and filter functionality must support Arabic text input with proper morphological analysis for shipping terminology. Date displays must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats simultaneously. Report exports in PDF and Excel formats must maintain proper RTL formatting. The interface must support seamless switching between Arabic and English without loss of context or filter state, and all market intelligence terminology must use industry-standard Arabic translations approved by the organization's localization team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-002-R017	FEAT-031-2-002	MOD-031	Market Intelligence API for External Integrations	The system must expose a RESTful API that allows authorized external systems and third-party applications to consume market intelligence data programmatically. The API must support OAuth 2.0 authentication with entity-level access control ensuring that API consumers can only access data for entities they are authorized to view. API endpoints must cover freight rate benchmarks, market share data, trade flow statistics, competitor profiles, and risk assessments. Response formats must support JSON and XML with proper Unicode handling for Arabic content. The API must implement rate limiting (configurable per consumer, default 1000 requests per hour), pagination for large datasets, and webhook subscriptions for real-time alerts. API documentation must be auto-generated using OpenAPI 3.0 specification and must be available in both Arabic and English. All API access must be logged for audit and billing purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R001	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	End-to-End Shipment Cycle Time Analysis	The system must measure and analyze end-to-end shipment cycle times from booking creation to final delivery for every shipment across all four entities. Cycle time must be decomposed into discrete process stages including booking confirmation, documentation preparation, customs pre-clearance, container stuffing, port gate-in, vessel loading, ocean transit, discharge, customs examination, container destuffing, and last-mile delivery. Each stage must be measured against configurable benchmarks by trade lane, port pair, and INCOTERMS basis (FOB, CIF, EXW, DDP). The system must identify the top bottleneck stages contributing to cycle time delays and must calculate the financial impact of delays using demurrage, detention, and opportunity cost models in the relevant local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R001	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	UN/LOCODE Port Registry	The system must maintain a complete registry of global ports using the United Nations Code for Trade and Transport Locations (UN/LOCODE) standard. Each port record must include the five-character UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEAUH for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT Nhava Sheva), country code, port name in both English and Arabic, geographic coordinates (latitude/longitude in decimal degrees), timezone offset from UTC, and operational status (active, temporarily closed, decommissioned). The system must support bulk import of UN/LOCODE updates published biannually by UNECE and flag any discrepancies with existing records for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R002	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Port Terminal Productivity Metrics Dashboard	The system must provide real-time and historical port terminal productivity dashboards for operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Key metrics must include berth utilization rate, crane moves per hour (gross and net), truck turnaround time at gate, container yard occupancy by block and tier, reefer plug utilization rate, and rail siding throughput where applicable. Dashboards must support drill-down from port-level summary to individual terminal, berth, and crane performance. Metrics must be displayed with trend indicators, target comparison, and peer benchmarking. The system must support configurable shift-based reporting aligned with local port operating schedules and must handle time zone differences correctly when displaying cross-entity comparisons between GCC ports (UTC+3/UTC+4) and Indian ports (UTC+5:30).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R003	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Documentation Processing Efficiency Metrics	The system must track and analyze documentation processing efficiency including bill of lading issuance time, certificate of origin processing time, customs declaration preparation time, letter of credit document submission accuracy rate, and invoice generation speed. Metrics must be broken down by document type, entity, and processing method (manual vs AI-automated). The system must calculate the documentation error rate by type (data entry errors, classification errors, regulatory compliance gaps) and must quantify the cost of documentation errors in terms of delay penalties, amendment fees, and customer satisfaction impact. AI automation rate must be tracked showing the percentage of documents processed with zero human intervention versus those requiring manual review. Efficiency targets must be configurable per entity to account for different regulatory complexity levels in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R004	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Customs Clearance Performance Analytics	The system must analyze customs clearance performance across all four operating countries with specific tracking of clearance times by commodity type (using HS codes), customs channel assignment (green, yellow, red), inspection frequency rates, and duty assessment accuracy. The system must identify patterns in customs delays such as specific commodity codes that consistently trigger red channel inspections at JNPT or documentation deficiencies that cause holds at Jebel Ali. Analytics must track the effectiveness of customs broker performance where third-party brokers are used and must compare pre-clearance versus on-arrival clearance success rates. The system must monitor compliance with Saudi SABER certification requirements, Qatar TASMU smart customs initiatives, UAE trade facilitation programs, and Indian ICEGATE electronic customs processing to ensure maximum utilization of fast-track clearance schemes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R005	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Transportation and Haulage Efficiency Analysis	The system must analyze inland transportation and haulage efficiency for first-mile and last-mile container movements at all four operating locations. Metrics must include average pickup and delivery times, truck utilization rates, empty running percentages, fuel consumption per TEU-kilometer, and on-time pickup and delivery performance. The system must identify optimal trucking windows based on port gate congestion patterns, traffic conditions, and applicable driving hour restrictions. Route optimization analytics must consider regional factors such as extreme summer heat restrictions on certain cargo types in GCC countries, toll road economics on Indian highway corridors (e.g., Mumbai-Pune Expressway), and free zone boundary crossing procedures. Cost per TEU-kilometer must be calculated in local currency with benchmarking against market rates for each operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R006	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Container Utilization and Load Optimization Analytics	The system must analyze container space utilization rates across all shipments to identify optimization opportunities. Metrics must include average volume utilization percentage, weight utilization percentage, and revenue per TEU by trade lane and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, OT, FR). The system must identify shipments with suboptimal container utilization (below 70% volume or weight) and recommend consolidation opportunities where compatible cargo can share container space. Analysis must account for cargo compatibility rules including hazardous materials segregation requirements (IMO/IMDG Code), temperature requirements for reefer cargo, and weight distribution constraints. The system must calculate potential savings from improved utilization in USD and local currencies and must track utilization improvement trends over time per entity and trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R007	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	AI Process Automation Rate Tracking	The system must track the AI automation rate across all operational processes to measure progress toward the 95% AI automation target. Automation metrics must be calculated for each process category including booking processing, documentation generation, customs classification, rate quotation, shipment tracking updates, invoice generation, exception management, and customer communication. For each process, the system must distinguish between fully automated (zero human touch), semi-automated (AI-assisted with human approval), and manual processing. The dashboard must show automation rate trends over time with drill-down by entity, process type, and exception category. When processes fall below target automation rates, the system must identify the most common reasons for human intervention and must recommend AI model improvements or process design changes to increase automation. Automation savings must be quantified in labor hours and monetary value per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R008	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Energy and Sustainability Performance Metrics	The system must track and report energy consumption and sustainability metrics for container shipping operations aligned with IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) requirements and ESG reporting standards. Metrics must include CO2 emissions per TEU-mile, energy consumption per container handled at port terminals, fuel efficiency of inland transportation fleet, and waste reduction metrics for warehouse operations. The system must support Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 emissions tracking with proper allocation methodology for shared vessel space. Sustainability dashboards must display progress against organizational targets and must support regulatory reporting requirements for each operating country. The system must generate carbon offset recommendations and must integrate with carbon credit trading data where applicable. Reports must comply with GCC sustainability reporting frameworks and India's Business Responsibility and Sustainability Reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R009	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Exception Management and Resolution Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on operational exceptions including shipment delays, cargo damage claims, documentation errors, customs holds, container detention overruns, and billing disputes. Each exception type must be tracked by frequency, average resolution time, financial impact in local currency, root cause category, and resolution method (AI-automated vs manual). The system must identify recurring exception patterns and must recommend preventive measures using AI pattern recognition. Exception resolution efficiency must be benchmarked across entities to identify best practices that can be replicated. The system must support Pareto analysis showing the top 20% of exception types that cause 80% of operational disruption and financial loss. Exception trend dashboards must display data in both Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting and must support filtering by Hijri and Gregorian date ranges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R010	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Workforce Productivity and Capacity Analytics	The system must measure workforce productivity across operational functions including operations coordinators, documentation specialists, customs brokers, customer service representatives, and yard planners. Productivity metrics must include transactions processed per person per day, error rates per processor, average handling time per transaction type, and capacity utilization percentages. The system must predict workforce capacity requirements based on forecasted shipment volumes and must identify periods of over-staffing or under-staffing. Analytics must respect local labor law requirements in Qatar (kafala system considerations), UAE (WPS compliance), KSA (Saudization/Nitaqat quotas), and India (labor code variations by state). All employee performance data must be anonymized and aggregated in analytics dashboards to comply with data privacy regulations, with individual-level data accessible only to authorized HR and management personnel per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R011	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Vessel Schedule Reliability Impact Analysis	The system must analyze the impact of vessel schedule reliability on downstream operational efficiency across all four operating entities. Analysis must correlate vessel delay data with yard congestion, gate queue lengths, labor overtime costs, warehouse storage overflows, and customer delivery commitments. The system must calculate the cascading cost impact when vessels arrive outside their scheduled berth windows, including additional demurrage and detention charges in local currencies, missed intermodal connections, and wasted pre-positioned transportation resources. The system must maintain a carrier reliability scorecard ranking shipping lines by on-time performance at each port and must recommend optimal carrier selection strategies that balance rate competitiveness with schedule reliability. Impact analysis must factor in the specific operating hours and berth allocation rules at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R012	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Warehouse and CFS Throughput Analytics	The system must provide analytics for warehouse and Container Freight Station (CFS) operations measuring throughput efficiency including cargo receipt processing time, storage utilization by zone and rack, order picking accuracy rates, cargo consolidation and deconsolidation cycle times, and outbound dispatch performance. Metrics must be tracked for each warehouse and CFS facility across all four entities with benchmarking between facilities. The system must identify peak throughput periods and correlate them with staffing levels to optimize shift scheduling. Storage cost per cubic meter per day must be calculated in local currency and compared against market warehouse rates. The system must detect slow-moving inventory and generate alerts when cargo dwell time exceeds configurable thresholds (default: 15 days for general cargo, 7 days for reefer, 3 days for hazardous goods). Analytics must support bonded warehouse inventory tracking with proper customs bond value calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R013	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Payment and Collection Efficiency Metrics	The system must track payment and collection efficiency metrics including Days Sales Outstanding (DSO) by entity and customer segment, invoice-to-payment cycle time, payment method distribution (bank transfer, letter of credit, cash against documents), dispute rate and resolution time, and bad debt provision trends. Metrics must be segmented by operating entity with proper multi-currency handling showing outstanding receivables in both local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and reporting currency (USD). The system must identify customers with deteriorating payment patterns and must correlate payment behavior with credit risk scoring. Collection efficiency must be benchmarked against industry standards for each country and must account for local payment culture differences such as post-dated cheque prevalence in UAE, SADAD payment system usage in KSA, and UPI adoption in India. The system must track the effectiveness of AI-automated collection reminders versus manual follow-ups.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R014	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Process Mining and Bottleneck Identification	The system must implement process mining capabilities that automatically discover actual operational workflows from system event logs and transaction data. Process mining must visualize the actual flow of shipments through operational stages compared against the intended standard operating procedures, highlighting process variants, loops, rework cycles, and unauthorized shortcuts. The system must identify the top bottleneck activities that create the greatest delays in shipment processing and must quantify the financial impact of each bottleneck in local currency. AI-powered root cause analysis must suggest specific process improvements and must estimate the potential efficiency gains from implementing each recommendation. Process mining must cover cross-entity workflows such as intercompany shipments that involve handoffs between Qatar and India entities, with proper visualization of entity boundary transitions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R015	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Real-Time Operational KPI Monitoring Wall	The system must provide a real-time operational KPI monitoring wall designed for display on large screens in operations control centers at each entity location. The monitoring wall must show live metrics including active shipments in transit, vessels at berth and at anchor, containers pending customs clearance, documentation completion rates, exception counts by severity, and AI automation system health indicators. Metrics must update at intervals no longer than 60 seconds for operational data and 5 minutes for financial data. The display must support configurable layouts with drag-and-drop widget arrangement per control center. Visual alerts must use color coding (green, amber, red) consistent with local cultural expectations and must include audible alert options for critical threshold breaches. The monitoring wall must support both Arabic and English display modes and must gracefully handle temporary data source disconnections by showing last-known values with staleness indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-003-R016	FEAT-031-2-003	MOD-031	Operational Efficiency Trend Reporting with Hijri Calendar Support	The system must generate periodic operational efficiency trend reports on configurable schedules (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually) with full support for both Gregorian and Hijri calendar reporting periods. Monthly reports for KSA entity must support alignment with Hijri months (Muharram, Safar, etc.) as required by local regulatory reporting. The system must calculate period-over-period efficiency improvements using matching calendar systems to ensure meaningful comparisons. Trend reports must include executive summary sections generated by AI that highlight the most significant efficiency improvements and deteriorations in natural language narrative. Reports must be distributable as interactive HTML dashboards, static PDF documents, and Excel workbooks. All report templates must support bilingual generation in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting and currency localization for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R001	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Template Designer with Drag-and-Drop Interface	The system must provide a visual report template designer that allows business users to create custom BI report templates using drag-and-drop components including tables, charts (bar, line, area, pie, scatter, heatmap, waterfall, treemap), KPI cards, maps, images, and text blocks. Templates must support dynamic data binding to any analytics metric or dataset available in the system. The designer must enforce consistent branding with configurable themes per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and must support both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layout orientations within the same template. Templates must be versioned with change history and must support role-based access control determining which users can create, modify, or use each template. The designer must include a preview mode showing the report populated with sample data before publishing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R002	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	AI-Generated Narrative Report Insights	The system must use natural language generation AI to automatically compose written narrative sections for BI reports in both Arabic and English. Narratives must translate numerical data and trends into coherent business language, highlighting key findings such as 'Jebel Ali transshipment volume increased 12% month-over-month driven by new feeder connections to Hamad Port' or the Arabic equivalent. The AI must identify and articulate the most significant changes, anomalies, and patterns in the data without requiring manual analysis. Narrative style must be configurable between executive summary (concise, strategic), operational briefing (detailed, action-oriented), and regulatory reporting (formal, compliance-focused) tones. The system must ensure factual accuracy by grounding all narrative statements in underlying data with traceable references and must flag any data quality issues that may affect narrative reliability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R003	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Scheduled Report Generation and Distribution	The system must support automated report generation on configurable schedules including daily, weekly, biweekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual frequencies. Schedules must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar triggers for entities operating on Islamic calendar reporting cycles. Reports must be distributable via email (with configurable attachment format: PDF, Excel, HTML), in-app notification with embedded viewer, shared network folder deposit, and API webhook delivery for integration with external systems. Distribution lists must be configurable per report with support for dynamic recipient resolution based on organizational roles and entity membership. The system must handle schedule conflicts and peak generation loads by implementing a priority-based queue ensuring critical management reports are generated before routine operational reports. Failed generation attempts must trigger automatic retry with escalation notification after three consecutive failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R004	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Multi-Entity Consolidated Reporting	The system must generate consolidated reports spanning multiple entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with proper multi-currency aggregation, intercompany elimination, and entity-level drill-down capability. Consolidated financial metrics must be presented in a common reporting currency (USD) with clear disclosure of exchange rates used for each entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must support both management reporting consolidation (operational metrics) and statutory reporting consolidation (financial metrics with IFRS compliance). Entity-level sections within consolidated reports must respect data access controls ensuring users can only drill into entities they are authorized to view. The consolidation engine must handle different fiscal year-end dates across entities and must properly align reporting periods when entities follow different calendar systems (Gregorian vs Hijri). Consolidated reports must include an intercompany reconciliation section showing eliminated transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R005	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Personalized Report Recommendations	The system must use AI to recommend relevant reports to each user based on their role, entity affiliation, recent activity patterns, and current operational context. Recommendations must consider factors such as the user's department (operations, commercial, finance, compliance), their most frequently viewed reports, reports viewed by peers in similar roles, and current operational events that may require attention (e.g., recommending a port congestion report when vessel delays are detected at the user's primary port). The recommendation engine must learn from user feedback including report open rates, time spent viewing reports, and explicit ratings. Recommendations must be displayed on the user's personalized dashboard with brief explanations of why each report was recommended. The system must avoid recommending reports the user is not authorized to view and must respect entity-level data isolation in its recommendation logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R006	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Interactive Report Viewer with Drill-Down and Filtering	The system must provide an interactive web-based report viewer that allows users to dynamically filter, sort, drill-down, and cross-filter report data without requiring report regeneration. Interactive capabilities must include clicking on chart segments to filter the entire report to that dimension, expanding summary rows to see detailed records, toggling between different aggregation levels (daily, weekly, monthly), and switching currency display between USD and the user's local currency. The viewer must support bookmarking specific filter states as personal saved views. Cross-report navigation must allow users to click on a KPI in one report and navigate to a detailed report explaining that metric. The viewer must perform well with reports containing up to 100,000 data rows with filtering operations completing within 2 seconds. The viewer must render correctly in both LTR and RTL modes and must support touch-screen interaction for tablet users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R007	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Automated Data Quality Validation Before Report Generation	The system must perform automated data quality validation checks before generating any BI report to ensure accuracy and completeness. Validation checks must include data completeness verification (ensuring all expected data sources have provided data for the reporting period), cross-source consistency checks (verifying that shipment counts in operational data match financial transaction records), currency conversion accuracy validation (confirming exchange rates are current and within reasonable bounds), and temporal consistency checks (ensuring no gaps in time-series data). When validation issues are detected, the system must classify them by severity (warning: proceed with caveat, error: require manual override, critical: block report generation). Reports generated with warnings must include a data quality disclaimer section listing all detected issues. Validation rules must be configurable per report template and must account for entity-specific data characteristics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R008	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Export in Multiple Formats	The system must support report export in multiple formats including PDF (with full formatting, charts, and images preserved), Microsoft Excel (with formulas, pivot tables, and cell formatting), Microsoft PowerPoint (with each report section mapped to slides), CSV (for raw data consumption by external tools), and HTML (for web embedding and email body inclusion). PDF exports must support A4 and Letter page sizes with proper pagination and must preserve RTL Arabic layout including correct bidirectional text handling in mixed Arabic-English content. Excel exports must include separate worksheets for summary and detail data with named ranges for easy formula reference. All exports must include metadata headers showing generation timestamp, data freshness, report version, entity scope, and currency basis. Export file sizes must be optimized through compression and resolution management to not exceed 25MB for email distribution compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R009	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Role-Based Report Access Control and Distribution	The system must enforce role-based access control for all BI reports at multiple levels: report template access (who can create or modify templates), report instance access (who can view generated reports), and data-level access (which entity and trade lane data appears in the report based on the viewer's permissions). Access control must integrate with the organization's identity management system and must support entity-level isolation ensuring that India entity users cannot access Qatar entity financial data unless explicitly authorized for cross-entity viewing. Report distribution lists must be validated against access controls to prevent accidental distribution of sensitive reports to unauthorized recipients. The system must maintain an audit log of all report access events including generation, viewing, export, and forwarding actions. Emergency report access override must be available for designated senior executives with mandatory logging and post-access review notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R010	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Real-Time Dashboard Report Mode	The system must support real-time dashboard mode for designated high-priority reports where data refreshes automatically at configurable intervals ranging from 30 seconds to 30 minutes without requiring manual page reload. Real-time dashboards must display data freshness timestamps for each widget showing the last update time. The system must handle real-time data stream interruptions gracefully by displaying the last known value with a visual staleness indicator and must automatically reconnect when the data source becomes available. Real-time dashboards must support simultaneous viewing by up to 500 concurrent users without performance degradation. The system must implement efficient data push mechanisms (WebSocket or Server-Sent Events) to minimize bandwidth consumption. Real-time dashboards must be optimizable for large display screens in operations centers as well as desktop and tablet browsers, with responsive layout adaptation for each form factor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R011	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Automated Report Anomaly Highlighting	The system must automatically detect and visually highlight anomalies and significant deviations in report data to draw user attention to items requiring review. Anomaly detection must identify values that deviate more than configurable standard deviations from historical norms, period-over-period changes exceeding threshold percentages, metrics that breach predefined target boundaries, and trend reversals in previously stable metrics. Highlighted anomalies must be annotated with AI-generated explanations suggesting probable causes based on correlated events and contextual data. For example, if Hamad Port throughput drops 20% in a weekly report, the system should annotate whether this correlates with a known vessel delay, public holiday, or weather event. Anomaly severity must be color-coded consistently across all reports and must be accessible to color-blind users through secondary indicators such as icons or patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R012	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Version History and Comparison	The system must maintain a complete version history for all generated report instances allowing users to access any previously generated version of a report. Version history must include the exact data snapshot used, generation parameters, filter settings, and any manual annotations added by reviewers. The system must support side-by-side comparison of two report versions highlighting data changes, metric movements, and narrative differences between versions. This is particularly critical for monthly management reports where stakeholders need to understand what changed between the draft version circulated for review and the final approved version. Version comparison must handle currency display changes (e.g., comparing a version viewed in QAR with one viewed in USD) by normalizing to a common currency for valid comparison. The system must retain report versions according to configurable retention policies with minimum retention of 7 years for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R013	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Regulatory and Compliance Report Templates	The system must include pre-built report templates for regulatory and compliance reporting requirements across all four operating jurisdictions. Templates must cover Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA) reporting, UAE Federal Tax Authority VAT returns data, KSA ZATCA e-invoicing compliance reports, India GST return reconciliation reports, customs duty payment summaries per port, and international maritime regulatory compliance reports (ISM Code, ISPS Code, MLC). Each regulatory template must be mapped to specific filing deadlines with automated generation schedules that allow sufficient review time before submission. Templates must be updated when regulatory requirements change and must include validation rules that check for common compliance errors. Report outputs must conform to the exact format specifications required by each regulatory authority, including specific date formats (Hijri for KSA ZATCA), currency precision requirements, and mandatory field validations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R014	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Self-Service Ad-Hoc Report Builder	The system must provide a self-service ad-hoc report builder that enables business users without technical skills to create one-time analytical reports by selecting data dimensions, measures, filters, and visualization types from a guided interface. The builder must present available data fields organized by business domain (operations, finance, commercial, compliance) with plain-language descriptions in both Arabic and English. Users must be able to join data across multiple domains (e.g., combining shipment volume data with financial revenue data and customer satisfaction scores) without writing queries. The system must enforce data access controls dynamically, showing only fields and dimensions the user is authorized to access based on their entity and role. Ad-hoc reports must be saveable as personal or shared reports and must be convertible into scheduled report templates. Query performance must be optimized through intelligent caching and pre-aggregation to return results within 10 seconds for queries spanning up to 12 months of data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R015	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Subscription Management and Preferences	The system must provide a user-facing subscription management interface where users can browse available reports, subscribe to or unsubscribe from report distribution lists, and configure personal delivery preferences. Preferences must include preferred delivery channel (email, in-app, Teams, Slack), preferred format (PDF, Excel, HTML), preferred language (Arabic, English, or bilingual), preferred currency for financial data, preferred calendar system (Gregorian, Hijri, or dual display), and preferred delivery time window. Users must be able to set vacation holds that pause report delivery during absence periods and resume upon return. Managers must be able to subscribe their team members to mandatory reports that cannot be unsubscribed. The system must send a monthly subscription digest showing all active subscriptions and recent delivery status. Subscription changes must take effect immediately for future report generations without affecting in-progress report distribution cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R016	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Performance Optimization and Caching	The system must implement intelligent report caching and performance optimization to ensure that report generation and viewing meets performance SLAs: simple reports must generate within 15 seconds, complex multi-entity consolidated reports within 60 seconds, and real-time dashboards must refresh within 5 seconds. The caching strategy must include pre-computed aggregation tables refreshed on configurable schedules, query result caching with automatic invalidation when underlying data changes, and rendered report caching for frequently accessed reports. Cache warming must be scheduled before peak access times (typically 7:00-9:00 AM local time at each entity). The system must provide cache hit rate monitoring and must alert administrators when cache effectiveness drops below 70%. Memory allocation for caching must be configurable per entity based on their report consumption patterns. Cache entries must respect data access controls ensuring that cached data is never served to unauthorized users even if the report content matches a cached version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R017	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Offline Report Package Generation	The system must support generation of offline report packages that bundle multiple related reports with their data into a downloadable package for consumption without network connectivity. Offline packages must be generated in self-contained HTML format with embedded data and JavaScript-powered interactivity including filtering, sorting, and chart interaction capabilities. Package generation must support selective inclusion of reports based on user-specified criteria such as entity, time period, and report category. Each package must include a table of contents and search functionality for navigating between bundled reports. Offline packages must be encrypted with password protection to secure sensitive business data during transport on personal devices. Package sizes must be optimized through data compression and selective detail level inclusion, with users able to choose between summary-only (smaller) and full-detail (larger) package options. Offline packages must render correctly on devices without internet connectivity including proper Arabic RTL display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-031-2-004-R018	FEAT-031-2-004	MOD-031	Report Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all report-related activities to support internal governance and regulatory compliance requirements across all four operating entities. Audited events must include report template creation and modification, report generation (scheduled and ad-hoc), report viewing with viewer identity and timestamp, report export with format and destination, report distribution list changes, report access control modifications, and report data source configuration changes. Audit logs must capture the user identity, timestamp in UTC and local timezone, source IP address, entity context, and action details for every event. Logs must be immutable and must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with the most stringent applicable retention requirement across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. Audit data must be searchable and exportable for internal audit reviews and must support regulatory examination requests. The audit system must generate monthly compliance summary reports showing access patterns and potential policy violations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R003	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Operating Hours and Calendar	The system must store port and terminal operating schedules including standard working hours, shift patterns (day/night/24-hour), and holiday calendars. Holiday calendars must support both Gregorian and Hijri date systems to accommodate Islamic holidays such as Eid Al-Fitr, Eid Al-Adha, and national days (Qatar National Day on 18 December, UAE National Day on 2 December, Saudi National Day on 23 September, Indian Republic Day on 26 January). The system must calculate effective working hours excluding holidays and allow override schedules for Ramadan operating hours when port productivity typically adjusts. AI recommendation engine must predict optimal berthing windows based on historical operating patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R004	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Facility and Equipment Registry	The system must maintain a detailed inventory of port facilities and cargo-handling equipment at each terminal. This includes container yards (capacity in TEU ground slots and stacking height), reefer plug points (count and power specifications in volts/amps), dangerous goods storage areas classified by IMDG class, tank farms for liquid bulk, rail connectivity status, and intermodal transfer zones. Equipment records must cover ship-to-shore gantry cranes, rubber-tyred gantry cranes (RTGCs), reach stackers, terminal tractors, and chassis with their operational status, maintenance schedules, and maximum lift capacity in tonnes. This data feeds into AI-driven terminal capacity optimization and vessel planning algorithms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R005	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Charges and Tariff Structure	The system must store port tariff schedules for each port and terminal, supporting multi-currency pricing in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. Tariff categories must include vessel-related charges (port dues per GRT, berth hire per hour, pilotage, towage), cargo-related charges (wharfage per TEU, storage per TEU per day with free time periods, lift-on/lift-off charges), and ancillary charges (documentation fees, security surcharges, environmental levies). Each tariff must have effective date ranges, approval references from the respective port authority, and support for seasonal or promotional rate variations. The system must flag tariff expiry 30 days in advance and maintain full tariff version history for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R006	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Authority and Regulatory Contacts	The system must store regulatory authority details for each port including the port authority name (e.g., Mwani Qatar for Hamad Port, Abu Dhabi Ports for Khalifa Port, DP World for Jebel Ali, JNPT Board), customs authority references, maritime authority contacts, coast guard liaison details, and health/quarantine authority information. Each contact record must include department name in English and Arabic, contact person, email, phone with country code, fax, physical address, and operating hours. The system must support role-based access so that only authorized users in the respective entity (Qatar entity for Qatari ports, UAE entity for Emirati ports) can modify their port authority records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R004	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Container and Voyage Auto-Linking	Upon claim registration, the system must automatically link the claim to the corresponding container movement record, booking, bill of lading, and voyage schedule by querying MOD-002 (Container Tracking) and MOD-005 (Voyage Management) services. The auto-linking must validate that the container number exists in the system, that the voyage referenced is valid, and that the port pair matches the actual routing. If discrepancies are found—such as a container not listed on the declared voyage or a port of loading mismatch—the system must flag the claim for manual verification and generate an alert to the claims handler. This linking must work across multi-entity boundaries when a shipment traverses entities, for example Hamad Port (QA entity) to JNPT (IN entity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R009	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Offline Claim Registration with Sync	The system must support offline claim registration for surveyors and port agents operating in areas with limited connectivity at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The offline mode must allow complete claim data entry including damage descriptions in Arabic and English, photograph capture, and document attachment on mobile devices. All data must be stored locally with encryption and automatically synchronized with the central system when connectivity is restored. The sync mechanism must handle conflict resolution when the same claim has been modified both offline and online, using a last-write-wins strategy with full conflict audit trail. Offline registrations must receive provisional claim reference numbers that are reconciled to permanent references upon successful sync.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R014	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Bulk Claim Registration for Multi-Container Shipments	The system must support bulk claim registration when a single incident affects multiple containers on the same voyage, such as a vessel grounding or a stack collapse at port. The bulk registration interface must allow the user to enter common claim attributes once (voyage, incident date, damage cause, claimant) and then specify per-container details (container number, cargo description, damage extent, claimed amount in the applicable currency). The system must generate individual claim records for each container while linking them under a parent incident reference for consolidated tracking and reporting. Bulk registration must support up to 500 containers per incident and must validate each container number against ISO 6346 check digit rules. The interface must function in both English and Arabic with proper RTL alignment for Arabic tabular data entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R002	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	AI Liability Opinion Generation	The system must generate an AI-driven preliminary liability opinion for each registered claim within 60 seconds of triage completion. The opinion must analyze the claim facts against applicable Hague-Visby Rules provisions and produce a structured assessment containing: the applicable liability regime (Hague, Hague-Visby, or Hamburg Rules based on the bill of lading clause paramount), the carrier's likely liability exposure (full, limited, or nil), applicable defenses with supporting reasoning, package limitation calculations where relevant, and an overall liability confidence score from 0 to 1. The AI must process damage descriptions in both English and Arabic and must cite specific Article and Rule references in its reasoning. Opinions with confidence below 0.65 must be automatically escalated to a senior claims assessor for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R005	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Clause Paramount and Regime Detection	The system must automatically detect the applicable liability regime by analyzing the bill of lading's clause paramount. The detection engine must parse uploaded bill of lading documents using OCR and NLP to identify references to the Hague Rules 1924, Hague-Visby Rules 1968, Hamburg Rules 1978, or Rotterdam Rules. Where no clause paramount is identified, the system must determine the applicable regime based on the country of shipment's mandatory legislation—applying Qatar Maritime Law for shipments from Hamad Port, UAE Federal Maritime Law for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, KSA regulations for Saudi ports, and the Indian COGSA 1925 for JNPT. The detected regime must be recorded on the claim with an option for manual override by a qualified claims assessor, and the liability assessment must adjust its analysis framework accordingly based on the detected or overridden regime.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R008	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Seaworthiness Due Diligence Checker	The system must evaluate whether the carrier exercised due diligence to make the vessel seaworthy before and at the beginning of the voyage, as required under Hague-Visby Article III Rule 1. The checker must integrate with vessel management data to verify: the vessel's valid class certificates from recognized classification societies (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas), the date of last dry-dock survey, Port State Control inspection results at QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, and INJNP, and any outstanding deficiency notices. For reefer cargo claims, the system must verify pre-trip inspection (PTI) records for the specific container. The seaworthiness evaluation must produce a risk rating (low, medium, high) indicating the strength of a seaworthiness defense, with high-risk ratings triggering automatic escalation to the legal review team. Evidence links must be maintained for all data sources consulted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R007	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Anchorage and Waiting Area Definitions	The system must define anchorage zones and designated waiting areas for each port, capturing geographic boundaries as polygon coordinates, maximum vessel count capacity, depth restrictions in meters, anchorage type (general, hazardous cargo, quarantine, STS transfer), and applicable regulations. For Jebel Ali, the system must support inner and outer anchorage definitions with different draft restrictions. For JNPT, the system must capture monsoon-season anchorage restrictions effective June through September. Each anchorage zone must track real-time occupancy status and estimated waiting times to feed into the AI-powered vessel scheduling module for optimal port call sequencing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R008	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Connectivity and Hinterland Links	The system must capture hinterland connectivity data for each port including road network connections (highway identifiers, distance to major logistics hubs in kilometers), rail connections (rail gauge, railhead capacity, rail operator details), inland waterway links, and free zone or special economic zone adjacency. For Jebel Ali, this includes connectivity to JAFZA free zone. For JNPT, this includes the Dedicated Freight Corridor connectivity. For Hamad Port, the logistics zone and warehousing district connections must be recorded. Each connectivity record must specify transit time estimates, capacity constraints, and applicable toll or transit charges in the local currency to enable end-to-end supply chain cost calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R009	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Arabic Bilingual Port Data Display	All port and terminal master data must support full Arabic localization with right-to-left (RTL) text rendering. Port names, terminal names, berth identifiers, facility descriptions, and regulatory contact details must be stored in both English and Arabic. The UI must dynamically switch between LTR and RTL layouts based on user language preference without data loss or formatting corruption. Arabic transliteration of international port names must follow the UNGEGN romanization standard. Search functionality must support querying in both Arabic and English simultaneously, returning results regardless of which language the search term is entered in. All printed reports and PDF exports must render Arabic text correctly with proper ligatures and diacritical marks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R010	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Data Version Control and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete version history for every port and terminal master data record, capturing the previous value, new value, timestamp (in UTC and local port timezone), user who made the change, entity context (which legal entity the user belongs to), and reason for change. All modifications must be immutable once committed and accessible through an audit trail viewer. The system must support point-in-time retrieval of port data as it existed on any historical date, enabling retrospective analysis of billing disputes or regulatory inquiries. Version history must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with GCC commercial record retention regulations and Indian Companies Act requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R011	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Multi-Entity Port Access Control	The system must enforce entity-level access control for port master data. Users belonging to the Qatar entity must have full read-write access to Qatari ports (Hamad Port, Doha Port) and read-only access to ports in other jurisdictions unless explicitly granted cross-entity permissions. Similarly, UAE entity users manage Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, KSA entity users manage their respective ports, and India entity users manage JNPT and other Indian ports. A global administrator role must exist with read-write access across all entities. Cross-entity data sharing agreements must be configurable to allow selective field-level visibility, ensuring that commercially sensitive tariff data is not exposed between competing entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R012	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Data Import and External Integration	The system must support automated import of port data from external authoritative sources including the World Port Source database, IHS Markit port reference data, and respective port authority published datasets. Import formats must include CSV, JSON, XML, and EDI BAPLIE/COPARN message extracts. Each import must go through a validation pipeline that checks for duplicate UN/LOCODEs, missing mandatory fields, coordinate plausibility (latitude between -90 and 90, longitude between -180 and 180), and referential integrity with existing vessel and route master data. Failed validations must generate detailed error reports with line-level diagnostics. AI must auto-suggest corrections for common data quality issues such as transposed coordinates or misspelled port names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R013	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Risk and Compliance Flags	The system must support risk and compliance flag assignment to ports, including sanctions screening status (OFAC, EU, UN sanctions lists), war risk zone classification, piracy risk zone proximity (as defined by Joint War Committee listed areas), environmental compliance ratings (IMO MARPOL Annex VI emission control area status), and port state control detention rates. Each flag must have an effective date, expiry date, source reference, and severity level (informational, warning, critical). The system must automatically trigger alerts when a voyage plan or booking references a flagged port and must prevent cargo bookings to sanctioned ports unless overridden by a compliance officer with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R014	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Offline Port Data Availability	The system must support offline access to port and terminal master data for users operating in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, such as vessel-based operations or remote terminal locations. A configurable subset of port data (user-selectable by trade lane or region) must be downloadable to local devices and synchronized with the central database upon reconnection. Conflict resolution rules must apply when the same record has been modified both locally and centrally during the offline period, with the central record taking precedence for system-generated fields and user-modified fields flagged for manual reconciliation. Offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and automatically purged after 30 days without synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R015	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	AI-Powered Port Performance Analytics	The system must leverage AI to analyze historical port performance data and generate predictive insights including average vessel turnaround time by vessel size category, berth productivity measured in crane moves per hour, yard utilization trends, gate-in and gate-out throughput patterns, and congestion probability forecasts by day of week and time of day. The AI engine must compare actual performance against published port KPIs and highlight deviations exceeding configurable thresholds. Insights must be presented in dashboard widgets with drill-down capability, exportable in PDF and Excel formats. Predictions must achieve at least 85% accuracy when validated against actual performance data over a rolling 90-day window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-001-R016	FEAT-032-1-001	MOD-032	Port Geographic Boundary Definitions	The system must store geographic boundary definitions for each port as GeoJSON polygon objects, enabling precise geofencing for vessel tracking and automated arrival/departure detection. Port limits, pilotage boarding areas, vessel traffic service (VTS) zones, and customs jurisdiction boundaries must each be defined as separate named polygons with altitude constraints where applicable. The system must validate that terminal and berth coordinates fall within the parent port boundary polygon, rejecting entries that fail spatial containment checks. Boundary data must integrate with the vessel tracking module to automatically trigger port call events when AIS-tracked vessel positions intersect defined geofences, supporting automated arrival notification generation per FAL Convention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R001	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	IMO Number Based Vessel Identification	The system must uniquely identify each vessel by its seven-digit IMO number as assigned by the International Maritime Organization under the IMO Ship Identification Number Scheme (Resolution A.1117(30)). The IMO number must be validated using the standard check-digit algorithm (weighted sum of first six digits modulo 10 equals the seventh digit). Duplicate IMO numbers must be rejected at the point of entry. The system must also store MMSI (Maritime Mobile Service Identity) number for AIS tracking integration, ITU call sign, vessel name, and former names with date of each name change. Vessels without valid IMO numbers (e.g., barges, inland craft) must be assignable a system-generated temporary identifier with a flag for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R002	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Technical Specifications	The system must capture and store detailed technical specifications for each vessel including: length overall (LOA) in meters, beam (breadth) in meters, depth in meters, summer draft and tropical draft in meters, gross tonnage (GT), net tonnage (NT), deadweight tonnage (DWT) in metric tonnes, lightship weight, TEU capacity (nominal and homogeneous 14-tonne), reefer plug count and power specifications, number of cargo holds, number of hatches, number of bays (on-deck and under-deck), tier and row configuration, cell guide specifications, and maximum stack weight per bay position. Tonnage values must comply with the International Convention on Tonnage Measurement of Ships 1969 (London Convention). Changes to technical specifications must trigger revalidation of all active berth compatibility checks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R003	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Classification and Certification	The system must record vessel classification society details (e.g., Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, ABS) including class notation, classification status (in class, suspended, withdrawn), date of last class survey, and next survey due date. Additionally, the system must maintain records of all mandatory statutory certificates including Safety Equipment Certificate (SEC), Safety Construction Certificate (SCC), International Load Line Certificate (ILLC), IOPP Certificate, ISPS Code Certificate, Document of Compliance (DOC), Safety Management Certificate (SMC), and International Tonnage Certificate. Each certificate must have fields for issuing authority, issue date, expiry date, and document attachment capability. The system must generate alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before certificate expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R004	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Ownership and Operator Details	The system must maintain a complete ownership and operator chain for each vessel including registered owner, beneficial owner, technical manager, commercial operator, ISM manager, and P&I club details. Each party record must include company name in English and Arabic, registered address, country of incorporation, IMO company number, contact details, and relationship effective dates. The system must support ownership transfer workflows where a change of owner triggers review of all active contracts, port call authorizations, and sanctions screening against the new owner entity. Historical ownership records must be retained indefinitely for compliance traceability, and the system must integrate with sanctions databases (OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List) to screen vessel ownership structures for sanctioned entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R005	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Flag State and Registration	The system must record the vessel's flag state (country of registration), port of registry, official registration number, and flag state authority details. The system must maintain a reference list of all recognized flag states classified by Paris MOU, Tokyo MOU, and Indian Ocean MOU performance lists (white, grey, black). When a vessel's flag state is on a black or grey list, the system must automatically flag the vessel record with a port state control risk indicator. Flag state changes (re-flagging) must be tracked with effective dates, and the system must validate that re-flagging does not violate any cabotage restrictions applicable in the operational jurisdictions (e.g., Indian coastal cargo restrictions under the Merchant Shipping Act, GCC cabotage regulations). AI must predict likelihood of port state control inspection based on flag state risk profile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R006	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Capacity and Bay Plan Template	The system must store vessel bay plan templates defining the exact container stowage configuration including bay numbers (fore to aft), row numbers (port to starboard using odd numbering convention), tier numbers (bottom to top), and cell positions. Each cell position must specify whether it accepts 20-foot or 40-foot containers, maximum stack weight in tonnes, height restrictions (standard vs high-cube), reefer capability, dangerous goods acceptance class by IMDG code, and out-of-gauge clearances. Bay plan templates must support both above-deck and below-deck configurations with hatch cover positions clearly delineated. The system must calculate and display total nominal TEU capacity, effective capacity at various draft restrictions, and reefer capacity from the bay plan template.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R007	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Engine and Fuel Specifications	The system must record propulsion and fuel system details including main engine type and make, engine power in kilowatts, number of engines, maximum service speed in knots, economical speed in knots, fuel types consumed (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LNG, methanol, ammonia), daily fuel consumption rates at various speeds (laden and ballast), auxiliary engine specifications, and boiler fuel consumption. Fuel consumption data must support IMO Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) and Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) calculations as mandated by MARPOL Annex VI amendments effective January 2023. The system must calculate and display the vessel's CII rating (A through E) and flag vessels rated D or E for operational review. Fuel type compatibility must be validated against port-specific fuel availability data and emission control area requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R008	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Trading and Sanction Restrictions	The system must maintain a configurable trading restriction profile for each vessel, specifying countries, ports, or regions where the vessel is prohibited from calling due to sanctions, insurance restrictions, war risk exclusions, or owner/charterer instructions. Restrictions must reference the applicable regulation or policy (e.g., OFAC Iran sanctions, EU Russia maritime restrictions) and include effective dates. When a voyage plan, port call, or cargo booking is created involving a restricted vessel-port combination, the system must automatically block the transaction and generate a compliance alert to the designated compliance officer. The system must support automated daily synchronization with updated sanctions lists and war risk area definitions from Lloyd's Market Association Joint War Committee circulars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R009	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Inspection and Vetting Records	The system must store historical inspection and vetting records for each vessel including port state control (PSC) inspection results (deficiency codes per Paris MOU classification, detention status, detention duration), SIRE (Ship Inspection Report Programme) inspection reports for tankers, CDI (Chemical Distribution Institute) inspections, and customer-specific vetting results. Each inspection record must capture inspection date, port of inspection (linked to port master data via UN/LOCODE), inspector or vetting body name, overall result (pass, conditional pass, fail), number and severity of deficiencies, and corrective action deadlines. The system must calculate a vessel risk score using AI based on inspection history, age, flag state performance, and classification society record, and present this score in vessel selection workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R010	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Hire and Commercial Parameters	The system must store commercial parameters for vessels under charter or fleet management, including charter type (time charter, voyage charter, bareboat, COA), charter party reference, daily hire rate with currency denomination (supporting USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), hire commencement and termination dates, redelivery range (list of acceptable ports), speed and consumption warranty terms, off-hire event definitions, and address commission percentages. For owned vessels, the system must capture daily operating cost (OPEX) breakdown including manning, insurance, maintenance, stores, and administration. Commercial parameters must integrate with the voyage estimation module to enable accurate profit and loss calculations per voyage and per vessel over configurable time periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R011	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Communication and Tracking Systems	The system must record the communication and tracking equipment installed on each vessel including AIS transponder details (Class A or B, MMSI number), LRIT (Long Range Identification and Tracking) compliance status, SSAS (Ship Security Alert System) configuration, GMDSS (Global Maritime Distress and Safety System) area classification (A1 through A4), satellite communication systems (Inmarsat Fleet, VSAT provider, Iridium), and email communication addresses for master, chief officer, and operations. The system must validate MMSI number format (nine digits, first digit indicating type of station) and cross-reference with the IMO number to detect data inconsistencies. Communication system data must support the vessel tracking integration module for real-time position monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R012	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Environmental Compliance Data	The system must capture and maintain vessel environmental compliance data including ballast water management system type and approval status (per BWM Convention), anti-fouling system certificate (AFS Convention), International Energy Efficiency Certificate (IEE Certificate), Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan (SEEMP), NOx Technical Code compliance tier (Tier I, II, or III), SOx scrubber installation status and type (open loop, closed loop, hybrid), and Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) certificate status under the EU Ship Recycling Regulation and Hong Kong Convention. For vessels trading in IMO-designated Emission Control Areas (ECAs), the system must verify fuel switching capability and validate compliance documentation. Non-compliant vessels must be flagged with prohibitions against ECA transit in voyage planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R013	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Photo and Document Repository	The system must provide a structured document repository for each vessel record supporting upload, storage, retrieval, and version control of vessel photographs (profile, bow, stern, bridge views), general arrangement plans (GA plans), capacity plan drawings, stability booklet extracts, P&I club certificates, class certificates, and charter party documents. Each document must be tagged with document type, upload date, uploader identity, expiry date (where applicable), and confidentiality level (public, internal, restricted). The system must enforce maximum file size limits (configurable, default 50MB per file) and supported format restrictions (PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, DWG). Documents marked as confidential must be accessible only to users with explicit vessel-level document access permissions within their entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R014	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Data Synchronization and External Feeds	The system must support automated synchronization of vessel particulars from external maritime databases including IHS Markit Sea-web, Equasis, and classification society registries. Synchronization must run on a configurable schedule (default daily) and compare incoming data against existing records field by field. Discrepancies must be categorized as minor (name formatting differences, rounding variations in measurements) or major (tonnage changes, ownership changes, classification status changes) with minor discrepancies auto-resolved using configurable rules and major discrepancies queued for manual review. The system must maintain a synchronization log with timestamps, source identifiers, fields updated, and previous values. API-based integration must use RESTful endpoints with OAuth 2.0 authentication and rate limiting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R015	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Sister Ship and Fleet Grouping	The system must support grouping vessels into fleets, sub-fleets, and sister ship classes. A fleet group must be assignable to one or more operating entities (e.g., the Qatar-flag fleet under Qatar entity, the India-flag fleet under India entity). Sister ship grouping must link vessels sharing identical technical specifications and bay plan configurations, enabling bay plan template inheritance where a template created for one sister ship automatically applies to all vessels in the group. Fleet grouping must support operational views showing all vessels in a fleet with their current position, next port, ETA, cargo status, and charter status. The system must allow dynamic fleet composition where vessels can be added or removed from groups with effective date tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R016	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Dry Dock and Maintenance Schedule	The system must record planned and historical dry dock events for each vessel including dry dock yard name and location, scheduled start and end dates, actual start and end dates, scope of work summary, cost estimate and actual cost in the vessel's operating currency, and off-hire days incurred. The system must enforce classification society survey cycles (typically five-year special survey cycle with intermediate survey at 2.5 years) and generate automated alerts when a vessel is approaching its survey window. Dry dock scheduling must integrate with the voyage planning module to ensure vessels are not scheduled for commercial voyages during planned dry dock periods. AI must recommend optimal dry dock timing based on vessel trading patterns, yard availability, and cost predictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-002-R017	FEAT-032-1-002	MOD-032	Vessel Crew Nationality and Manning Requirements	The system must record minimum safe manning requirements per flag state regulations including number and rank of officers and ratings, required certifications per STCW Convention, and nationality restrictions imposed by the flag state or port state. For vessels calling at Indian ports, the system must validate compliance with Director General of Shipping circulars regarding manning. For GCC-flagged vessels, Qatarization or Emiratization manning ratio requirements must be configurable. The system must track crew change port preferences (linked to port master data) and visa requirement flags for crew nationalities at each port of call. Manning data must feed into voyage cost estimation for crew-related expenses including travel, victualing, and wage calculations in the applicable currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R001	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	WCO Harmonized System Code Hierarchy	The system must maintain the complete World Customs Organization Harmonized System (HS) nomenclature structured as a hierarchical tree: Sections (I through XXI using Roman numerals), Chapters (01 through 99 as two-digit codes), Headings (four-digit codes), and Subheadings (six-digit codes). Each node in the hierarchy must store the code, official description in English and Arabic, section/chapter notes, and legal notes that govern classification interpretation. The system must support the current HS 2022 edition and provide a migration pathway for future HS edition updates (typically every five years) with automated mapping of old codes to new codes where one-to-one correspondence exists. HS codes that have been split or merged in new editions must be flagged for manual reclassification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R002	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Regional Tariff Line Extensions	The system must extend the six-digit HS code with regional tariff line codes: eight-digit codes for the GCC Unified Customs Tariff (applicable to Qatar, UAE, and KSA), and eight-digit codes for the Indian Customs Tariff (ITC-HS). Each tariff line must store the national tariff code, description in English and Arabic (for GCC) or English and Hindi transliteration (for India), applicable duty rate as a percentage or specific rate, preferential duty rates under applicable free trade agreements (e.g., GCC-Singapore FTA, India-UAE CEPA, India-GCC FTA), and any applicable anti-dumping or safeguard duty references. The system must flag discrepancies where the same HS six-digit code has different duty treatments across the four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R003	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Description and Attributes	The system must allow creation of commodity records that link to one or more HS codes and capture shipping-relevant attributes including: common trade name in English and Arabic, scientific or technical name where applicable, physical state (solid, liquid, gas, powder), temperature requirements (ambient, chilled with range in Celsius, frozen with range in Celsius), humidity sensitivity flag, stackability constraints (maximum stack weight, fragile indicator), density range in kg per cubic meter for volume-weight calculations, and shelf life in days for perishable commodities. Each commodity must be classified by cargo type (general, reefer, dangerous goods, out-of-gauge, bulk in container, liquid in flexitank/ISO tank) to drive container type selection logic in the booking module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R004	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Dangerous Goods Classification Linkage	The system must link commodity records to IMDG (International Maritime Dangerous Goods) Code classifications where applicable. Each dangerous goods commodity must capture the UN Number (four-digit identifier), proper shipping name, IMDG class and division (e.g., Class 3 Flammable Liquids, Class 8 Corrosives), subsidiary risk classes, packing group (I, II, or III), marine pollutant flag, EmS (Emergency Schedule) fire and spillage codes, segregation group, limited quantity threshold, and excepted quantity provisions. The system must enforce IMDG stowage and segregation rules by flagging incompatible commodity combinations when loaded on the same vessel. Dangerous goods classification must be validated against the current IMDG Code Amendment (Amendment 41-22 effective 1 January 2024) and updated biennially.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R005	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Trade Restriction and Compliance	The system must maintain trade restriction flags for commodities including: export control classifications (dual-use items per Wassenaar Arrangement and UAE Federal Law on Strategic Goods, Saudi GAMI controlled items, India SCOMET list), import prohibition flags by country (e.g., pork products prohibited in KSA and Qatar, certain chemicals restricted in India), quota-controlled commodities with quota period and threshold quantities, and phytosanitary or health certificate requirements by destination country. Each restriction must reference the applicable regulation, have an effective date range, and specify the action (block, warn, require documentation). The system must automatically evaluate trade restrictions during the cargo booking process and prevent bookings for prohibited commodity-country combinations unless overridden by a compliance officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R006	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	HS Code Search and AI Classification	The system must provide intelligent HS code search supporting keyword search in English and Arabic, code-based search (partial or full HS code entry), and hierarchical browsing through the HS tree. An AI-powered classification engine must suggest the most likely HS codes based on a free-text commodity description input by the user, presenting the top five recommendations with confidence scores. The AI engine must be trained on historical classification data from the organization's customs declarations and must achieve a minimum 80% accuracy for first-suggestion match rate when validated against customs-accepted classifications. The system must log all AI classification suggestions with the user's final selection to create a feedback loop for continuous model improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R007	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Customs Duty Rate Management	The system must store and maintain customs duty rates applicable to each tariff line code for each of the four operational jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Duty types must include basic customs duty (BCD), additional customs duty or countervailing duty (India-specific), special additional duty (India-specific), value-added tax or GST rate, excise duty where applicable, and any applicable anti-dumping duty or safeguard duty. Each duty rate must have an effective from date, effective to date, notification or gazette reference number, and calculation method (ad valorem percentage, specific rate per unit of measure, or compound rate). The system must support multi-currency duty calculation converting CIF value from invoice currency to the local customs currency using exchange rates from the respective central bank.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R008	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Free Trade Agreement Preferential Rates	The system must maintain preferential duty rates under applicable free trade agreements and bilateral trade agreements. For GCC states, this includes the GCC Unified Economic Agreement, GCC-Singapore FTA, and Greater Arab Free Trade Area (GAFTA) preferences. For India, this includes the India-UAE Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (CEPA), India-ASEAN FTA, India-Japan CEPA, and others. Each preferential rate must specify the agreement name, qualifying conditions (rules of origin criteria such as change in tariff heading, value addition percentage, or regional value content threshold), required documentation (certificate of origin form type), and rate differential from the MFN (Most Favoured Nation) rate. The system must automatically identify applicable preferential rates during customs declaration preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R009	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Unit of Measure and Quantity Standards	The system must maintain a standardized unit of measure (UOM) registry for commodity quantity reporting aligned with customs requirements. Primary UOM must follow the WCO recommended standards including kilograms (KGM), metric tonnes (TNE), liters (LTR), square meters (MTK), cubic meters (MTQ), units/pieces (PCE), pairs (PR), and dozens (DZN). Each commodity record must specify the primary customs reporting UOM and allow secondary UOMs with conversion factors. The system must validate quantity entries against plausible ranges per commodity type (e.g., a 20-foot container of rice should not exceed approximately 28 metric tonnes) and flag outliers for verification. UOM codes must align with UN/ECE Recommendation 20 for electronic data interchange compatibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R010	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Pricing Reference Data	The system must maintain reference pricing data for key traded commodities to support customs valuation validation and commercial benchmarking. Reference prices must be sourced from recognized commodity exchanges or industry indices (e.g., Platts for petrochemicals, London Metal Exchange for metals, USDA for agricultural products) and stored with effective date, price per standard unit, and currency. The system must support multi-currency storage with rates in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with automatic conversion using daily exchange rates from the respective central banks (Qatar Central Bank, Central Bank of UAE, SAMA, Reserve Bank of India). Reference pricing data must flag customs declarations where the declared value deviates by more than a configurable percentage (default 20%) from the reference price, indicating potential undervaluation or overvaluation for customs audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R011	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Image and Documentation Library	The system must support attachment of reference images, technical data sheets, material safety data sheets (MSDS/SDS for dangerous goods), phytosanitary certificate templates, fumigation certificate templates, and halal certification references to commodity master records. Each attachment must be tagged with document type, language (English, Arabic, or bilingual), upload date, validity period where applicable, and access control level. The system must enforce that all dangerous goods commodity records have a mandatory MSDS attachment before the commodity can be used in cargo bookings. For food products destined to GCC countries, the system must require halal certification reference attachment. Documents must be retrievable by commodity code, HS code, or trade name search, supporting both English and Arabic search terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R012	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Embargo and Sanction Screening	The system must maintain commodity-level embargo and sanction data aligned with OFAC, EU, UN Security Council, and national sanctions regimes of Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Embargoed commodities must be flagged with the sanctioning authority, sanction program reference (e.g., OFAC Iran Sanctions, UN DPRK Sanctions), commodity scope description, and effective dates. The system must perform real-time sanction screening when a commodity is selected in a booking, bill of lading, or customs declaration, cross-referencing the commodity HS code, origin country, destination country, and parties involved. Screening must cover both primary sanctions (direct prohibition) and secondary sanctions (risk of US secondary sanctions for non-US entities). Screening results must be logged immutably for compliance audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R013	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	HS Code Change History and Impact Analysis	The system must track all changes to HS codes including code additions, deletions, splits, merges, and description amendments across WCO HS edition updates and national tariff schedule revisions. When an HS code is amended or retired, the system must perform an impact analysis identifying all active commodity records, contracts, tariff agreements, and standing booking templates referencing the affected code. The impact analysis report must list each affected record with its current status, the nature of the change, and a recommended action (reclassify, archive, merge). AI must suggest the most likely replacement code for retired HS codes based on description similarity analysis and historical classification patterns. All HS code transitions must maintain a complete mapping history for retrospective customs audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R014	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Seasonal and Volume Analytics	The system must leverage AI to analyze historical booking and shipment data to identify seasonal commodity volume patterns across the four operational regions. Analytics must cover peak and trough periods for major commodity groups (e.g., dates and food products peaking before Ramadan in GCC, mangoes and spices peaking in Indian summer for export), average monthly TEU volumes by commodity-trade lane combination, year-over-year growth trends, and commodity mix shifts. The system must generate forward-looking demand forecasts by commodity category and trade lane with configurable forecast horizons (1, 3, 6, and 12 months). Forecast accuracy must be tracked and reported against actuals, with the AI model automatically retraining monthly on the latest shipment data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R015	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Commodity Master Data Governance Workflow	The system must enforce a maker-checker approval workflow for commodity and HS code master data changes. New commodity record creation, HS code assignment changes, dangerous goods classification modifications, and trade restriction updates must require initiation by an authorized maker and approval by a separate checker before becoming effective. The workflow must capture maker identity, submission timestamp, checker identity, approval or rejection timestamp, and comments. Rejected changes must be returned to the maker with rejection reasons for revision. Urgent changes (e.g., newly announced sanctions) must support an expedited approval path with post-facto audit review. The governance workflow must generate monthly reports summarizing master data changes by type, entity, and approval turnaround time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R016	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Offline Commodity Data Access and Sync	The system must enable offline access to the commodity and HS code master data for users at customs clearance offices, port terminals, or remote locations with unreliable connectivity. A downloadable data package must include all active HS codes with descriptions in both English and Arabic, linked commodity records, duty rates for the user's operational jurisdiction, and dangerous goods classifications. The offline package must be versioned with a generation timestamp and maximum validity period (configurable, default 7 days). Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any commodity lookups or classification queries performed offline and reconcile against the current central dataset. Offline data must be encrypted using AES-256 and accessible only after user authentication with cached credentials validated against a time-limited token.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-003-R017	FEAT-032-1-003	MOD-032	Multi-Entity Commodity Classification Harmonization	The system must support commodity classification harmonization across the four operating entities while respecting jurisdiction-specific differences. A central commodity catalog must serve as the golden record with entity-specific overlays for local tariff codes, duty rates, and regulatory requirements. When the Qatar entity classifies a new commodity, the system must prompt for classification under GCC Unified Tariff. When the India entity classifies the same commodity, it must prompt for ITC-HS classification. The system must highlight classification discrepancies where the same physical commodity has been assigned different HS codes by different entities, flag these for review by a central classification team, and track resolution status. Harmonization reports must show classification consistency metrics across entities on a monthly basis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R001	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	ISO 6346 Container Type Code Registry	The system must maintain a complete registry of container type codes following the ISO 6346 standard for the coding, identification, and marking of freight containers. Each container type must be identified by the four-character size/type code where the first two characters denote size (length and height per ISO 668) and the last two characters denote type and characteristics. Standard entries must include 22G1 (20ft standard dry), 42G1 (40ft standard dry), 45G1 (40ft high-cube dry), 22R1 (20ft reefer), 42R1 (40ft reefer), 45R1 (40ft high-cube reefer), 22T1 (20ft tank), 42T1 (40ft tank), 22U1 (20ft open-top), 42U1 (40ft open-top), 22P1 (20ft flat-rack), and 42P1 (40ft flat-rack). The system must support addition of new ISO type codes as they are published by the BIC (Bureau International des Containers).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R002	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Dimensional Specifications	The system must store precise dimensional specifications for each container type code including external length, width, and height in both metric (millimeters) and imperial (feet and inches) units, internal length, width, and height, door opening width and height, tare weight in kilograms, maximum gross weight in kilograms (typically 30,480 kg for standard 20ft, 30,480 kg for standard 40ft), maximum payload in kilograms (calculated as gross minus tare), and cubic capacity in cubic meters and cubic feet. Dimensions must comply with ISO 668 tolerances. The system must validate that tare weight plus payload does not exceed maximum gross weight and must flag containers where country-specific road weight limits (e.g., India's 20.32 tonne per axle limit, GCC road weight regulations) effectively reduce the usable payload below the ISO maximum.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R003	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Reefer Container Technical Specifications	The system must capture extended technical specifications for refrigerated container types including temperature range (minimum and maximum in degrees Celsius, typically -35°C to +30°C for modern reefer units), refrigeration unit make and model, refrigerant gas type (e.g., R-134a, R-452A), power supply requirements (voltage, frequency, amperage — typically 380/460V, 50/60Hz, 3-phase), fresh air ventilation settings (cubic meters per hour), controlled atmosphere capability (O2 and CO2 percentage control for fruit and vegetable shipments), humidity control capability and range, USDA cold treatment program compliance flag, and pre-trip inspection (PTI) interval requirements. Reefer specifications must be validated against commodity temperature requirements during the booking process to ensure equipment suitability for cargo such as pharmaceuticals, frozen meat, and fresh produce.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R004	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Tank Container Specifications	The system must store detailed specifications for ISO tank containers (type code T-series) including tank capacity in liters, maximum working pressure in bar, test pressure in bar, tank material (stainless steel grade, carbon steel, lined), valve type and configuration (top discharge, bottom discharge), heating system type (steam coil, electrical), insulation type and thickness, UN portable tank instruction reference (e.g., T11, T14 per IMDG Code Chapter 4.2), approved product list by UN number, last hydraulic test date and next test due date, and cleaning certificate requirements. The system must validate that commodities assigned IMDG tank instructions are booked only with compatible tank container types. Tank containers used for food-grade products must be flagged to prevent subsequent loading with non-food-grade chemicals without documented cleaning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R005	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Special Container and Out-of-Gauge Equipment	The system must support registration of special container types beyond standard dry and reefer units including flat-rack containers (fixed-end and collapsible), open-top containers, platform containers (bolsters), half-height containers, ventilated containers, garment-on-hanger (GOH) containers, and livestock containers. For flat-rack and platform containers, the system must capture floor dimensions, stanchion positions, lashing ring positions and safe working load (SWL) per ring in tonnes, and maximum load distribution diagrams. For out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo equipment, the system must define maximum over-width, over-height, and over-length tolerances beyond the standard container envelope. Each special equipment type must specify bay plan stowage restrictions (e.g., flat-racks requiring adjacent empty slots, open-tops requiring no stack above).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R006	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Weight Verification (VGM) Parameters	The system must store Verified Gross Mass (VGM) parameters per container type as required by SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 (effective July 2016). Each container type record must specify the tare weight as stated on the CSC (Container Safety Convention) plate, acceptable VGM submission methods (Method 1: weighing packed container, Method 2: weighing all contents and adding tare), VGM tolerance thresholds per port authority regulations (e.g., JNPT allows 5% tolerance for Method 2, Jebel Ali specifies 3% maximum deviation), and authorized weighbridge/scale references. The system must validate submitted VGM values against container maximum gross weight and reject values exceeding the rated capacity. VGM submission deadlines relative to vessel cut-off must be configurable per port and terminal, and the system must flag bookings where VGM has not been submitted before the deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R007	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Grade and Condition Standards	The system must define container condition grading standards specifying acceptable condition for different cargo types. Grade definitions must include: cargo-worthy (CW) — suitable for general cargo per IICL-6 standards, food-grade — suitable for direct food contact with documented cleaning certificate, pharma-grade — suitable for pharmaceutical cargo with temperature mapping validation, IMDG-approved — certified for dangerous goods with valid CSC plate and placarding provisions, and wind-and-watertight (WWT) — structurally sound but not cargo-worthy, suitable for storage only. Each grade must define the inspection criteria (wall condition, floor condition, door seal integrity, odor, pest-free status, coating condition), acceptable damage thresholds per IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) repair standards, and the grade's validity period before re-inspection is required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R008	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container CSC Plate and Safety Certification	The system must record Container Safety Convention (CSC) plate data for each container type as a template and for individual containers as specific records. CSC data must include date of manufacture, manufacturer identification, maximum gross weight, allowable stacking weight for 1.8g (the maximum weight that can be stacked on top), transverse racking test force in kilonewtons, and approval reference number from the certifying authority. The system must track CSC plate examination validity (initial examination within five years of manufacture, subsequent examinations per the approved continuous examination programme or periodic scheme). Containers with expired CSC examination must be automatically flagged as non-cargo-worthy in the equipment inventory module and excluded from booking allocation until re-examination is documented.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R009	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Leasing and Ownership Categories	The system must categorize container types by ownership and leasing model including: company-owned (COC — Carrier Owned Container), shipper-owned (SOC — Shipper Owned Container), and leased containers (master lease, spot lease, one-way lease, round-trip lease). Each category must define different data capture requirements — leased containers must record lessor name, lease agreement reference, per diem rate in the applicable currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), pick-up and drop-off depot locations linked to port master data, and free days before per diem commences. SOC containers must capture shipper-provided specifications and a liability disclaimer for structural adequacy. The system must maintain separate equipment pools per ownership category and entity, preventing COC equipment from one entity being inadvertently allocated to another entity without inter-entity transfer authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R010	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Depot and Interchange Location Registry	The system must maintain a registry of container depots, inland container depots (ICDs), container freight stations (CFS), and empty container yards linked to each operating port. Each depot record must include depot name in English and Arabic, operator name, physical address, geographic coordinates, services offered (storage, repair, cleaning, fumigation, PTI for reefers, VGM weighing), operating hours, contact details, and the container types and sizes accepted. Interchange locations at port terminals must specify gate numbers, appointment system requirements, and interchange inspection standards applied (IICL or custom). For JNPT, the system must support CFS locations in the Nhava Sheva hinterland. For Jebel Ali, inland container depot connectivity to JAFZA and Dubai Logistics Corridor must be recorded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R011	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Intermodal Compatibility Matrix	The system must maintain an intermodal compatibility matrix defining which container types are compatible with each transport mode — deep-sea vessel, feeder vessel, barge, rail wagon (by wagon type: flat wagon, well wagon, spine wagon), road chassis, and trailer. Compatibility must factor in dimensional constraints (e.g., 45ft containers not accepted on standard Indian rail wagons, high-cube containers requiring low-bed trailers on certain GCC road routes), weight restrictions per mode and route, and special handling requirements (e.g., reefer containers requiring genset or power supply on rail). The matrix must be configurable by trade lane and route segment, enabling the booking and routing module to validate end-to-end transport feasibility for the selected container type. Incompatible combinations must generate clear rejection messages with alternative container type suggestions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R012	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Demand Forecasting	The system must leverage AI to forecast demand for each container type by trade lane, port, and time period. Forecasting must analyze historical booking patterns, seasonal variations (e.g., reefer demand increase during GCC summer for food imports, open-top demand spikes for project cargo in KSA NEOM construction), committed contract volumes, and market intelligence inputs. Forecasts must be generated at weekly and monthly granularity with a minimum 12-week forward horizon. The AI model must produce demand forecasts with confidence intervals (80% and 95%) and compare forecasted versus actual demand to calculate forecast accuracy metrics (MAPE, bias). Demand forecasts must feed into the equipment repositioning module to optimize empty container logistics and minimize repositioning costs across the four operational regions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R013	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Pricing and Surcharge Rules	The system must define pricing rules and surcharges associated with each container type for use in rate management and quotation modules. Container-type-specific charges must include base ocean freight differential (e.g., 40ft HC premium over 40ft standard, reefer premium over dry), equipment imbalance surcharge by trade lane direction (e.g., higher charge for SOC in deficit areas), special equipment surcharge for flat-racks, open-tops, and tank containers (typically quoted per unit rather than per TEU), and cleaning surcharges for food-grade or chemical-grade requirements. All charges must support multi-currency definition in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with effective date ranges and customer-category-level overrides (contract customers, spot customers, NVOCC, freight forwarder). Pricing rules must be auditable with a complete change history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R014	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Marking and Identification Standards	The system must define and validate container marking standards per ISO 6346 including: owner code (three letters registered with BIC), equipment category identifier (U for freight container, J for detachable equipment, Z for trailers/chassis), serial number (six digits), and check digit (calculated using ISO 6346 Annex A algorithm). The system must validate the check digit upon container number entry and reject invalid numbers with a clear error message showing the expected check digit. For bulk container operations, the system must support BIC-registered owner codes for all major shipping lines operating in the Qatar-UAE-KSA-India trade (e.g., MAEU for Maersk, MSCU for MSC, CMAU for CMA CGM, HLCU for Hapag-Lloyd). The system must also capture size/type code markings, CSC plate reference, and maximum gross weight marking in kilograms and pounds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R015	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Image and Technical Drawing Repository	The system must provide a document and image repository for each container type record supporting storage of: exterior and interior photographs from multiple angles, dimensional drawings with measurement annotations, floor plan diagrams showing lashing ring and forklift pocket positions, door-end and side-panel structural drawings, and equipment-specific technical manuals (reefer unit operating manuals, tank container cleaning procedures). Each document must be tagged with container type code, document category, file format (PDF, JPEG, PNG, DWG, AutoCAD), upload date, and source (manufacturer, BIC, internal). The repository must support version control so that when a container type specification is updated (e.g., new reefer unit model), the historical technical drawings remain accessible. Documents must be accessible offline as part of the container type data synchronization package.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R016	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Regulatory Compliance by Jurisdiction	The system must maintain jurisdiction-specific regulatory compliance requirements for each container type across the four operating countries (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Compliance data must include maximum road transport weight per axle configuration for each country (e.g., India permits 36 tonnes gross vehicle weight on a two-axle trailer, UAE permits 45 tonnes on a tri-axle), fumigation and pest control requirements (ISPM-15 wood packaging standards, methyl bromide prohibition status), food safety container certification requirements per national food authority regulations (Qatar MOPH, UAE FSSA, Saudi SFDA, India FSSAI), and customs seal type requirements (RFID, bolt, cable, high-security per ISO 17712). Each compliance rule must have an effective date, regulatory reference, and enforcement level (mandatory, recommended, advisory).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R017	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Master Data Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a governance workflow for container type master data ensuring all new container type registrations, specification modifications, and deactivation requests follow a maker-checker approval process. The workflow must require submission by an authorized equipment data specialist (maker) with mandatory fields validated before submission, followed by review and approval by an equipment manager (checker) from the same or parent entity. New container types affecting vessel stowage compatibility must additionally require approval from the operations planning team. The system must maintain a complete workflow audit trail with submission timestamp, checker identity, approval or rejection with reasons, and time to approval metrics. Bulk updates (e.g., updating all reefer container types with new refrigerant gas specifications) must support batch approval with individual record-level review capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-1-004-R018	FEAT-032-1-004	MOD-032	Container Type Data Synchronization and Offline Access	The system must support synchronization of container type master data to offline-capable devices used at port terminals, container depots, and customs inspection stations. The synchronization package must include all active container type codes with full dimensional specifications, weight limits, ISO code mappings, intermodal compatibility flags, and reference images. Synchronization must be incremental (only transmitting changes since the last sync) to minimize bandwidth consumption in low-connectivity environments common at remote depots in GCC and Indian hinterland locations. Conflict resolution for offline modifications must follow a last-write-wins policy for non-critical fields (e.g., description text) and require manual reconciliation for critical fields (e.g., weight limits, safety specifications). The offline dataset must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 and automatically expire after a configurable period (default 14 days) requiring re-synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R003	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Credit limit inheritance and override rules	The system must implement credit limit inheritance from parent to child entities within the customer hierarchy. A parent entity's credit limit in its base currency must cascade to child entities with configurable allocation percentages. Individual child entities must support credit limit overrides that cannot exceed the parent's remaining unallocated balance. The system must calculate real-time credit exposure across all entities using live exchange rates when aggregating multi-currency exposures (e.g., QAR bookings at Hamad Port combined with AED bookings at Jebel Ali). An AI recommendation engine must suggest optimal credit allocation based on historical booking patterns and payment behavior.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R005	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Duplicate customer detection with AI matching	The system must employ AI-powered fuzzy matching to detect potential duplicate customer records during creation and import. The matching algorithm must consider name similarity in both Arabic and English (accounting for transliteration variations such as Mohammed/Muhammad/Mohamed), tax registration numbers, phone numbers, email domains, and physical addresses. The system must present a confidence score (0-100%) for each potential match and allow the user to merge, link as related, or confirm as distinct. Merge operations must consolidate booking history, outstanding invoices, and credit limits while maintaining full audit trail of the merge action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R007	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Customer group tagging and segmentation	The system must allow tagging of customers into multiple overlapping groups for segmentation purposes. Standard group categories must include trade lane affinity, cargo type specialization, payment behavior rating (AI-generated from historical data), strategic importance tier (Platinum, Gold, Silver, Bronze), and industry vertical (oil and gas, construction, FMCG, automotive, textiles). Each group assignment must carry an effective date range and the system must maintain historical group membership records. Group tags must be usable as filters in tariff assignment, credit policy application, and reporting across all four country entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R008	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Bilingual data entry with Arabic RTL support	All customer and agent data entry forms must support simultaneous Arabic and English input with proper right-to-left rendering for Arabic fields. The system must auto-detect input language direction and switch field alignment accordingly. Customer name, address, and contact fields must have paired Arabic and English versions with mandatory validation ensuring at least one language version is completed. Search functionality must support cross-language queries where searching in English returns Arabic-named records and vice versa. Print outputs including customer statements, credit notes, and correspondence must render in the language preferred by the customer entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R010	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Multi-entity customer visibility and data sharing	The system must support cross-entity customer visibility where a customer registered in the Qatar entity can be visible to operators in the UAE, KSA, or India entities based on configurable sharing rules. Shared customer records must maintain entity-specific attributes (local tax registration, local credit limit, local payment terms) while sharing common attributes (global parent reference, group memberships, KYC status). Data sharing permissions must be granular allowing view-only, booking-allowed, or full-edit access levels per entity. Changes to shared attributes must synchronize across entities in real-time with conflict resolution rules favoring the owning entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R011	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Customer blacklist and sanctions screening	The system must integrate with international sanctions lists (OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List, UN Security Council) and local regulatory blacklists for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Every new customer creation and periodic batch re-screening must check entity names, aliases, and beneficial owners against these lists. Positive matches must trigger an automatic hold on the customer account preventing new bookings, with mandatory compliance officer review workflow. The screening must handle Arabic name transliteration variants and partial name matches with configurable sensitivity thresholds. Screening results must be retained for a minimum of five years for regulatory audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R012	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Offline customer data capture and sync	The system must support offline customer data capture for field sales representatives operating in areas with limited connectivity, particularly at port facilities and industrial zones near Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Offline-captured records must queue locally on the device with full validation rules applied client-side. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize offline records with conflict detection comparing against records created during the offline period. Conflicts must be presented to the user for manual resolution with side-by-side comparison of the offline and online versions. Offline mode must support a minimum of 500 customer records cached locally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R014	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Agent performance dashboard and KPI tracking	The system must calculate and display real-time key performance indicators for each shipping agent including booking volume (TEU count), revenue generated, average freight rate achieved versus tariff card rate, booking-to-shipment conversion ratio, documentation accuracy rate, and average days to collect receivables. KPIs must be calculable across configurable time periods aligned with both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal calendars. An AI engine must generate performance trend analysis and predictive forecasts for each agent. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate KPIs to individual booking details and must export to PDF and Excel with bilingual Arabic-English formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R015	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Bulk customer and agent data import with validation	The system must support bulk import of customer and agent master records from Excel and CSV files with comprehensive validation. Import templates must include all mandatory fields with dropdown validations for coded fields such as country, port (UN/LOCODE), currency, and INCOTERMS. The import engine must validate data completeness, referential integrity against existing master data, duplicate detection against existing records, and format compliance for tax registration numbers per jurisdiction. Import results must provide a detailed error report with row-level error descriptions. Successfully validated records must be staged for review before final commit, supporting partial import of valid records while rejecting invalid ones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R016	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Customer contract and agreement tracking	The system must maintain a registry of customer contracts and service agreements linked to customer hierarchy nodes. Each contract record must capture agreement type (volume commitment, rate agreement, service level agreement), validity period with Gregorian and Hijri dates, minimum quantity commitment in TEUs, applicable trade lanes and ports, special rate entitlements, and free-time allowances at origin and destination. The system must monitor actual performance against contracted commitments and trigger alerts when customers fall below minimum volume thresholds. Contract renewal workflows must initiate automatically 90 days before expiry with AI-generated renewal recommendations based on historical utilization patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-001-R017	FEAT-032-2-001	MOD-032	Audit trail and change history for master records	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every change made to customer and agent master records. Each audit entry must record the field changed, old value, new value, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, user identity, IP address, entity context (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India), and business justification where required by workflow rules. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. Audit records must be searchable and filterable by date range, user, field, and entity. Retention period must be configurable per jurisdiction with a default minimum of seven years to comply with the strictest applicable regulation across all operating countries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R001	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Tariff code structure and classification	The system must support a hierarchical tariff code structure with at minimum four classification levels: Service Type (FCL, LCL, breakbulk, RORO), Trade Direction (import, export, transshipment, cross-trade), Trade Lane (defined by origin-destination port pairs using UN/LOCODE), and Cargo Classification (general, hazardous per IMDG class, reefer, out-of-gauge, special cargo). Each tariff code must carry a unique alphanumeric identifier, effective date range, and status (draft, active, expired, superseded). The system must prevent activation of overlapping tariff codes covering the same service-direction-lane-cargo combination to avoid ambiguous rate lookups.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R003	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Surcharge and accessorial charge management	The system must support an extensible catalog of surcharges and accessorial charges attachable to base tariff codes. Standard surcharge types must include BAF (Bunker Adjustment Factor), CAF (Currency Adjustment Factor), THC (Terminal Handling Charges) specific to each port (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT), PSS (Peak Season Surcharge), ERS (Emergency Risk Surcharge), low-sulphur fuel surcharge, war risk premium, and piracy surcharge. Each surcharge must be independently activatable with its own effective date range, calculation method (fixed amount, percentage of base freight, per TEU/FEU), and applicable currency. Surcharges must support geographic applicability rules at the port, country, or trade lane level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R004	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Container type rate differentiation matrix	The system must maintain rate differentiation factors for all standard ISO container types relative to a 20GP base rate. Supported container types must include 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR, 20TK, and 45HC. Each container type must carry a multiplication factor applied to the base rate (e.g., 40HC at 1.85x of 20GP rate, 40RF at 2.5x). Differentiation factors must be configurable per trade lane as market conditions vary by route. The system must support special equipment surcharges for non-standard containers including out-of-gauge cargo requiring special stowage, with rate additions definable by over-height, over-width, and over-length increments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R005	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	INCOTERMS-based rate component bundling	The system must support bundling of rate components based on INCOTERMS 2020 rules to present all-inclusive rates matching the terms of sale. For CIF terms, the system must bundle ocean freight, insurance, and origin charges. For FOB terms, only ocean freight and destination charges apply. For DDP terms, all charges including destination customs clearance and inland delivery must be included. The system must maintain a mapping matrix defining which charge components are included under each INCOTERM (EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP). Rate quotations generated from tariff codes must automatically assemble the correct charge components based on the selected INCOTERM.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R006	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Seasonal and dynamic rate adjustments	The system must support scheduled seasonal rate adjustments that automatically modify base tariff rates during defined periods. Seasonal profiles must be configurable per trade lane with start and end dates supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Adjustment methods must include percentage increase or decrease, fixed amount addition, and complete rate replacement. The AI pricing engine must analyze historical booking volumes, vessel utilization data, and market rate indices to recommend dynamic rate adjustments. Recommended adjustments must pass through a configurable approval workflow before activation. The system must maintain a complete history of all rate adjustments with rollback capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R007	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Commodity-based rate exceptions	The system must support commodity-specific rate exceptions linked to HS Code (Harmonized System) classifications at the 6-digit or 8-digit level. Certain commodities may warrant premium rates (hazardous chemicals per IMDG Code, pharmaceuticals requiring temperature control) or discounted rates (government-subsidized exports, humanitarian cargo). Each commodity exception must reference the applicable HS Code range, rate adjustment method, and regulatory basis where applicable. The system must validate commodity exceptions against customs tariff schedules of Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Commodity exceptions must be stackable with other rate adjustments following a configurable precedence hierarchy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R008	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Rate versioning and effective date management	The system must implement comprehensive version control for all tariff records. Each rate modification must create a new version while preserving the complete prior version history. Version transitions must be governed by effective dates with the system automatically activating new versions at midnight (local time of the originating entity) on the effective date. The system must support future-dated rate entries allowing commercial teams to prepare rate changes weeks in advance. Booking rate lookups must always resolve to the version effective on the booking date. The system must prevent backdated rate modifications that would affect already-invoiced bookings and must flag bookings made during the transition period for review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R009	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Customer-specific contract rate overrides	The system must support customer-specific contract rates that override standard tariff card rates. Contract rates must be linkable to specific customer hierarchy nodes (individual customer, customer group, or global parent) with inheritance rules. Each contract rate must specify the applicable trade lanes, container types, validity period, minimum quantity commitment, and whether the rate is a fixed override or a discount percentage from the prevailing tariff rate. The system must implement a rate resolution waterfall: first checking customer contract rates, then customer group rates, then standard tariff rates. Rate lookups must log which level of the waterfall was applied for transparency and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R010	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Tariff comparison and benchmarking tools	The system must provide tools for comparing tariff rates across multiple dimensions including trade lane, time period, container type, and customer segment. Comparison views must support side-by-side display of current versus proposed rates with calculated impact analysis showing projected revenue change based on historical booking volumes. Benchmarking capabilities must allow comparison against market rate indices where available. The system must generate variance reports highlighting tariff codes where actual achieved rates deviate significantly from published tariff rates, indicating excessive discounting. All comparison outputs must support multi-currency display with conversion at both current and historical exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R011	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Bulk tariff upload and validation engine	The system must support bulk upload of tariff rate data from structured Excel templates. The upload engine must validate all rate entries against business rules including mandatory field completeness, referential integrity of port codes against the UN/LOCODE master, container type validity against the ISO 6346 standard, currency code validity against ISO 4217, and rate reasonableness checks against configurable minimum and maximum thresholds per trade lane. Validation must produce a detailed error report with row and column references. Successfully validated entries must be staged in a review queue with side-by-side comparison against current active rates before final approval and activation. The upload must support incremental updates without requiring full tariff replacement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R012	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Tariff approval workflow with multi-level authorization	All tariff rate changes must pass through a configurable multi-level approval workflow before activation. The workflow must support at minimum three approval levels: commercial team entry, regional pricing manager review, and senior management authorization for changes exceeding a configurable threshold. Threshold rules must be definable by percentage change from current rate, absolute rate amount, and trade lane strategic importance classification. The approval workflow must capture approver comments, conditional approvals with modifications, and rejection reasons. Escalation rules must auto-escalate pending approvals after a configurable timeout period. The system must support delegation of approval authority during approver absence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R013	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Tariff expiry alerts and renewal management	The system must proactively monitor tariff code validity periods and generate alerts at configurable intervals before expiry (default 60, 30, and 15 days). Alerts must be routed to the responsible pricing team based on trade lane ownership. The system must distinguish between tariffs requiring renewal (ongoing trade lanes) and tariffs expected to expire (seasonal or promotional rates). For tariffs requiring renewal, the AI engine must generate rate renewal recommendations based on market trends, booking volume analysis, vessel utilization patterns, and competitor rate intelligence where available. Expired tariffs must be clearly marked and excluded from rate lookups while remaining accessible for historical reference and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R014	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Free time and demurrage tariff integration	The system must maintain detention and demurrage tariff schedules linked to the main freight tariff structure. Free time allowances must be configurable per port (Hamad Port standard 7 days, Jebel Ali standard 5 days, JNPT standard 3 days), container type (reefer containers typically receiving shorter free time), and customer tier (premium customers receiving extended free time). Demurrage and detention rates must be definable as daily rates with escalating tiers (e.g., days 1-3 at base rate, days 4-7 at 1.5x, days 8+ at 2x). The system must integrate free time and demurrage tariffs with the billing module to automatically calculate storage and detention charges based on actual container dwell times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R015	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Tariff code search and rate lookup interface	The system must provide an intuitive rate lookup interface allowing users to retrieve applicable rates by entering search criteria including origin port, destination port, container type, cargo commodity, customer reference, and shipment date. The lookup must resolve through the complete rate hierarchy including base tariff, applicable surcharges, customer-specific discounts, and seasonal adjustments, presenting a fully built-up rate breakdown. The interface must support both Arabic and English with bilingual field labels and search terms. Search results must display rates in the user's preferred currency with the option to toggle between all supported currencies. The lookup must return results within two seconds for optimal user experience.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R016	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	AI-powered rate optimization recommendations	The system must provide AI-generated rate optimization recommendations by analyzing historical booking data, market demand patterns, vessel space utilization, and competitive rate intelligence. The AI engine must identify trade lanes with suboptimal pricing where rate adjustments could improve revenue yield or market share. Recommendations must be presented with supporting data including projected volume impact, revenue impact in multiple currencies, and confidence score. The system must learn from accepted and rejected recommendations to improve future suggestion quality. Recommendations must respect configured business rules including minimum margin thresholds, maximum discount percentages, and strategic trade lane protection policies defined by management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-002-R017	FEAT-032-2-002	MOD-032	Regulatory surcharge compliance tracking	The system must track regulatory-mandated surcharges and ensure tariff compliance with port authority fee schedules, customs duty structures, and government-imposed levies across all operating jurisdictions. Regulatory surcharges must be flagged as non-negotiable in the tariff structure, preventing commercial teams from waiving or discounting them. The system must maintain a regulatory change log capturing the gazette or official notification reference, effective date, and impacted tariff codes. When regulatory surcharge changes are published, the system must automatically identify all affected tariff codes and generate a batch update proposal for review. Compliance reports must be available for audit by port authorities and customs regulators in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R001	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Multi-source exchange rate feed integration	The system must support integration with multiple external exchange rate feed providers including central bank feeds (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI), commercial rate providers (Reuters, Bloomberg), and configurable REST API endpoints. Each feed source must be configurable with connection parameters, authentication credentials, polling frequency, and priority ranking. The system must support automatic failover to secondary sources when the primary feed is unavailable. Feed responses must be parsed and normalized into a standard internal format supporting all required currency pairs: USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR, and all cross-rate combinations among these five currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R004	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Rate variance monitoring and anomaly detection	The system must continuously monitor incoming exchange rates for anomalies by comparing each new rate against configurable variance thresholds. If a newly captured rate deviates from the previous rate by more than the configured threshold (default 2% for major currency pairs, 5% for volatile pairs), the system must flag the rate for manual review before it becomes available for transactional use. The AI engine must learn normal rate movement patterns and adjust anomaly detection sensitivity accordingly. Anomaly alerts must be routed to treasury personnel via in-app notification, email, and SMS. The system must hold transactions pending rate confirmation when anomaly review is in progress.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R005	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Historical exchange rate repository	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical repository of all captured exchange rates with unlimited retention. Historical rates must be queryable by currency pair, date range, source provider, and rate type (raw feed, commercial with spread, central bank official). The repository must support retrieval of the rate applicable at any specific historical date and time for retrospective booking valuation and audit purposes. Historical rate data must support export to Excel and CSV with proper formatting for financial analysis. The system must pre-calculate and store daily, weekly, and monthly average rates for reporting purposes. Storage must be optimized with appropriate indexing to ensure sub-second query response times across the full historical dataset.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R006	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Rate locking for booking and invoice transactions	The system must support exchange rate locking at configurable transaction lifecycle points. Rate lock options must include lock-at-booking (rate fixed when booking is confirmed), lock-at-sailing (rate fixed on vessel departure date), lock-at-invoice (rate fixed on invoice generation date), and lock-at-payment (rate fixed on payment receipt date). The locked rate must be stored with the transaction record and used consistently for all financial calculations related to that transaction. Rate lock policies must be configurable per entity, customer, and trade lane. The system must calculate and report the gain or loss arising from the difference between the locked rate and the rate prevailing at settlement date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R007	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Pegged currency handling for QAR and AED	The system must recognize and appropriately handle currency pegs, specifically QAR pegged to USD at approximately 3.64 and AED pegged to USD at approximately 3.6725. For pegged currency pairs, the system must support configurable fixed rates that override feed-sourced rates, with optional narrow bands allowing minor fluctuation within the peg. The system must alert treasury personnel if feed rates deviate from the known peg rate beyond a configurable tolerance (default 0.1%). For financial reporting and revaluation purposes, the system must support using either the pegged fixed rate or the market feed rate based on entity-level accounting policy configuration. Cross-rate calculations involving pegged currencies must maintain consistency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R008	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Exchange rate API for internal system consumption	The system must expose a RESTful API endpoint enabling other ERP modules (booking, invoicing, accounts receivable, accounts payable, GL posting) to retrieve current and historical exchange rates. The API must support queries by currency pair, date, rate type, and entity context. Response format must include the rate, source, capture timestamp, and applicable spread. The API must enforce rate freshness validation, returning an error if the most recent rate capture is older than a configurable staleness threshold (default 24 hours for business days). The API must support both synchronous request-response and event-driven notification patterns where subscribing modules receive pushed updates when new rates are captured.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R009	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Multi-entity rate governance and entity-specific rates	The system must support both centralized and decentralized rate governance models. In centralized mode, the headquarters entity captures and publishes rates used by all entities. In decentralized mode, each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) captures and maintains its own rates from local sources. A hybrid mode must allow centralized rates for major pairs (USD-based) with entity-specific rates for local currency pairs. The system must enforce that only one authoritative rate exists per currency pair per entity per timestamp to prevent conflicting rate usage. Inter-company transaction rates must be governed by transfer pricing policies with the system maintaining a separate rate table for inter-entity settlements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R010	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Weekend and holiday rate handling	The system must handle rate availability gaps during weekends and public holidays across all operating jurisdictions. For Islamic calendar-based holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Days of Qatar, UAE, and KSA) and Indian public holidays (Republic Day, Independence Day, Diwali), the system must apply the last available business day rate with a configurable option to apply a forward adjustment factor. The system must maintain a comprehensive holiday calendar for all four countries supporting both Gregorian and Hijri dates. Friday-Saturday weekends (Qatar, UAE, KSA) and Saturday-Sunday weekends (India) must be independently configured. The system must clearly indicate when a carried-forward rate is being used rather than a fresh market rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R011	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Exchange rate impact simulation tool	The system must provide a simulation tool allowing finance teams to model the impact of exchange rate changes on outstanding bookings, unbilled revenue, accounts receivable, and accounts payable. Users must be able to input hypothetical rate scenarios (e.g., INR depreciates 5% against USD) and view the calculated impact across all entities in their base reporting currency. The simulation must cover unrealized foreign exchange gains and losses on open positions. Results must be exportable in a format compatible with financial reporting requirements. The tool must support saving simulation scenarios for comparison and must integrate with the AI engine to generate probability-weighted impact assessments based on rate trend analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R012	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Offline rate caching and synchronization	The system must cache the most recent exchange rates locally on client devices to support continued operations during network connectivity interruptions. The cached rate set must include all active currency pairs with the last three business days of rate history. Transactions created using cached rates must be flagged with a cached-rate indicator and the timestamp of the cached rate used. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must reconcile transactions made with cached rates against the current live rates and flag any transactions where the rate variance exceeds a configurable threshold for treasury review. The cache must be encrypted at rest on the local device to protect commercially sensitive rate information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R013	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Regulatory reporting rate compliance	The system must support regulatory requirements for exchange rate reporting across all jurisdictions. For Qatar Financial Markets Authority, UAE Securities and Commodities Authority, Saudi GAZT, and Indian RBI compliance, the system must identify and store official central bank reference rates used for customs valuation, tax computation, and statutory financial reporting. The system must distinguish between commercial transaction rates and regulatory reference rates, ensuring that statutory reports use the prescribed official rates. For Indian GST compliance, the system must apply the CBIC-notified exchange rates effective on the date of invoice. Rate sources used in statutory filings must be fully traceable and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R014	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Exchange rate feed health monitoring dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying the health status of all configured exchange rate feed sources. The dashboard must show for each source: connection status (connected, disconnected, degraded), last successful rate capture timestamp, number of failed capture attempts in the last 24 hours, average response latency, and data quality score based on anomaly frequency. The dashboard must highlight sources requiring attention with color-coded severity indicators. Historical feed reliability metrics must be available for trending analysis over configurable periods. The dashboard must support both Arabic and English display with proper RTL layout and must be accessible to treasury and IT operations personnel based on role permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R015	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Forward rate and hedging rate support	The system must support entry and management of forward exchange rates for hedging purposes. Forward rates must be associable with specific future-dated transactions or groups of transactions (hedge designation). Each forward rate entry must capture the deal date, settlement date, forward rate, counterparty bank, deal reference number, and notional amount. The system must calculate forward points (premium or discount) relative to the spot rate. Mark-to-market valuation of outstanding forward contracts must be calculable using current spot rates. The system must support hedge effectiveness testing calculations in compliance with IFRS 9 financial instruments standards applicable across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R016	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Currency conversion calculation engine	The system must provide a centralized currency conversion calculation engine used by all modules requiring rate conversion. The engine must support direct conversion (e.g., QAR to USD), triangulated conversion via a base currency (e.g., QAR to INR via USD), and inverse rate calculation with configurable rounding rules. Rounding must be configurable per currency following local conventions: QAR to 2 decimal places, AED to 2 decimal places, SAR to 2 decimal places, INR to 2 decimal places, and USD to 2 decimal places. The engine must handle conversion of both amounts and unit rates with appropriate precision management. All conversions must be logged with the rate used, source, and timestamp for full auditability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-003-R017	FEAT-032-2-003	MOD-032	Automated GL revaluation rate distribution	The system must automatically distribute month-end and period-end exchange rates to the General Ledger module for foreign currency revaluation purposes. At each configured period close, the system must publish the official closing rate for each currency pair to be used for balance sheet revaluation and the average rate for the period to be used for income statement translation. Rate distribution must be automated with configurable timing aligned to each entity's financial close calendar. The system must generate a rate distribution confirmation report listing all rates published, their sources, and the receiving GL periods. Distribution must be reversible within the close period to accommodate rate corrections before final period close.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R001	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Multi-entity chart of accounts structure	The system must support a unified chart of accounts framework with entity-specific extensions for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The global chart must define a common account numbering scheme with reserved ranges for asset (1xxxx), liability (2xxxx), equity (3xxxx), revenue (4xxxx), and expense (5xxxx-9xxxx) categories. Each entity must be able to extend the global chart with local accounts required by their jurisdiction's regulatory requirements without modifying the global structure. The system must support a minimum of seven segment account codes combining entity, account, cost centre, profit centre, trade lane, vessel, and voyage dimensions. Account creation must enforce uniqueness within each entity and validate against the global numbering conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R002	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Shipping-specific account classifications	The system must provide pre-configured account classifications specific to container shipping operations. Required shipping account categories must include ocean freight revenue (segmented by trade lane and container type), slot charter revenue, demurrage and detention revenue, terminal handling income, container leasing costs, vessel operating expenses (fuel, port dues, crew), voyage-specific expenses, container maintenance and repair, depot operating costs, inland transportation costs, and customs brokerage fees. Each shipping-specific account must carry metadata linking it to the operational module that primarily drives its transactions (booking module for freight revenue, equipment module for container costs, vessel module for voyage expenses). This metadata enables automated posting rule configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R003	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Cost centre hierarchy management	The system must support a multi-level cost centre hierarchy with configurable depth. Standard hierarchy levels for shipping operations must include Company, Division (commercial, operations, finance, IT), Region (Middle East, Indian Subcontinent), Country (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), Port/Location (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), Department, and Team. Each cost centre must carry attributes including responsible manager, budget allocation, active date range, and reporting currency. Cost centre creation must validate against the hierarchy rules ensuring proper parent-child relationships. The system must support both top-down budgeting (allocation from parent to children) and bottom-up cost aggregation (rollup from children to parent) for reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R004	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Profit centre definition for trade lane analysis	The system must support profit centre definitions aligned with shipping trade lanes to enable profitability analysis by route. Each trade lane profit centre must be defined by origin port, destination port, service loop, and direction (headhaul/backhaul). The system must support virtual profit centres that aggregate multiple trade lanes for regional profitability analysis (e.g., all Gulf-India trade lanes). Revenue and cost allocations to profit centres must follow configurable rules including direct assignment, volume-based allocation, and revenue-weighted allocation. The system must prevent posting transactions without a valid profit centre assignment when profit centre tracking is enabled for the account. Profit centre definitions must support effective date ranges for organizational restructuring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R005	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Bilingual account descriptions in Arabic and English	Every GL account, cost centre, and profit centre must support dual-language descriptions in Arabic and English. Arabic descriptions must be stored with proper Unicode encoding supporting all Arabic character forms (initial, medial, final, isolated). The system must render descriptions in the user's preferred language throughout all interfaces including transaction entry, reports, and financial statements. Financial statement generation must support bilingual output with Arabic and English descriptions appearing side by side or selectable per output. Search functionality must support querying account descriptions in either language and returning matches regardless of the search language used. Import and export templates must include columns for both language versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R007	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	ZATCA E-Invoicing Phase 2 Integration Reporting	The system must generate compliance reports specific to KSA ZATCA Fatoora Phase 2 (Integration Phase) requirements for all container shipping invoices processed through the KSA entity. Reports must cover clearance invoice volumes, debit and credit note reconciliation, QR code generation success rates, and XML validation error summaries. The system must track the Universal Unique Identifier (UUID) for each invoice, the Invoice Counter Value (ICV), and the Previous Invoice Hash (PIH) chain integrity. Monthly reconciliation reports must compare ERP invoice totals against ZATCA-acknowledged totals in SAR, flagging any discrepancies exceeding 0.01 SAR for investigation and resolution within the mandated timeframe.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R006	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Multi-GAAP account mapping	The system must support mapping of accounts to multiple accounting standards simultaneously. Each account must be mappable to IFRS account classifications (as required across all four countries), local GAAP requirements (Qatar Financial Markets Authority standards, UAE Federal Law, KSA SOCPA standards, Indian Accounting Standards), and tax reporting classifications per jurisdiction. The mapping must support one-to-many relationships where a single operational account maps to different reporting accounts under different standards. The system must validate that all active accounts have complete mappings for all applicable standards before allowing period close. Mapping changes must be version-controlled with effective dates to support retrospective standard transitions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R007	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Account group and rollup configuration	The system must support configurable account groupings for financial statement presentation. Groups must be definable at multiple summary levels to support trial balance, detailed financial statements, management reporting, and regulatory filing formats. Each account must be assignable to exactly one primary group within each reporting hierarchy, but the system must support multiple parallel reporting hierarchies for different output formats. For example, the same accounts may be grouped differently for IFRS reporting versus management reporting versus tax filing. Group definitions must include sequence numbers controlling the display order in financial statements. The system must validate that all active accounts are assigned to groups in each active reporting hierarchy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R008	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Voyage and vessel cost centre auto-generation	The system must automatically generate temporary cost centres for each voyage when a voyage is created in the vessel operations module. Voyage cost centres must follow a standardized naming convention incorporating the vessel code, voyage number, and direction (e.g., CC-VSL-HAMAD01-V2401-NB for northbound voyage 2401 of vessel HAMAD01). Auto-generated cost centres must inherit the parent cost centre of the operating entity and must be automatically closed upon voyage completion and final cost settlement. The system must prevent new postings to closed voyage cost centres while maintaining historical data accessibility. Voyage cost centres must aggregate all voyage-related expenses including bunker fuel, port dues, canal fees, crew costs, and container handling charges for per-voyage profitability analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R009	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Inter-company account configuration	The system must support configuration of inter-company accounts used for transactions between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. A matrix of inter-company account pairs must be maintained defining the receivable and payable accounts for each entity combination (Qatar-UAE, Qatar-KSA, Qatar-India, UAE-KSA, UAE-India, KSA-India). The system must enforce balanced inter-company postings where every debit in one entity generates a corresponding credit in the counterparty entity. Inter-company accounts must support multi-currency postings with exchange rate differences posted to designated FX gain/loss accounts. The system must generate inter-company reconciliation reports highlighting unmatched or out-of-balance positions across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R010	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Account access control and posting restrictions	The system must enforce granular access controls on GL accounts and cost centres. Access levels must include view-only, post-manual-journal, post-system-generated, and full-administration. Posting restrictions must be configurable by entity, user role, transaction type, and date range. The system must support account-level posting rules such as debit-only or credit-only restrictions, mandatory narration requirements for specific accounts, required supporting document attachment for expense accounts exceeding configurable thresholds, and mandatory second-approver requirements for postings above amount thresholds. Period-based posting restrictions must prevent entries to closed accounting periods while allowing authorized adjustments through a controlled re-opening process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R011	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Automated account validation rules	The system must enforce configurable validation rules during transaction posting to GL accounts. Validation rules must include mandatory cost centre assignment for all expense and revenue accounts, mandatory profit centre assignment for all revenue accounts, valid combination checks ensuring that specific account and cost centre combinations are permitted (e.g., vessel operating expense accounts must only be posted to vessel-type cost centres), currency restriction rules limiting certain accounts to specific currencies, and contra-account validation ensuring proper accounting entry pairing. Validation rules must be testable in a simulation mode before activation and must provide clear error messages in both Arabic and English when posting violations are detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R012	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Hijri fiscal calendar support for KSA entity	The system must support Hijri calendar-based fiscal periods for the KSA entity while simultaneously maintaining Gregorian calendar periods for Qatar, UAE, and India entities. The Hijri fiscal calendar must handle the variable length of Hijri months (29 or 30 days) and the drift of the Hijri year relative to the Gregorian year. Period close processes must operate independently per entity allowing the KSA entity to close on Hijri period boundaries while other entities close on Gregorian month-end. Financial reports for the KSA entity must display period labels in both Hijri and Gregorian formats. Consolidation reports combining all entities must map Hijri periods to equivalent Gregorian periods using configurable alignment rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R013	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Account master data import and migration tools	The system must provide comprehensive tools for initial migration and ongoing bulk maintenance of GL account, cost centre, and profit centre master data. Import templates must support Excel format with built-in validation rules including account number format compliance, hierarchy integrity verification, mandatory field completeness checks, and duplicate detection. The migration tool must support mapping from legacy account structures to the new chart of accounts with a mapping table that transforms old account numbers to new ones. Trial balance import functionality must allow loading of opening balances with multi-currency support. The system must generate a migration reconciliation report comparing source system totals against imported totals by account category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R008	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Data Transformation Mapping	The system must provide a visual mapping interface for administrators to define data transformations between the ERP internal schema and external endpoint schemas. Transformation rules must support field mapping, data type conversion, value translation (e.g., mapping internal container type codes to ISO 6346 codes like 22G1 for 20ft dry, 42G1 for 40ft dry, 45R1 for 40ft reefer), and conditional logic based on entity or port context. The mapping interface must display field labels in both English and Arabic and must support import/export of mapping configurations in JSON format to enable replication across multi-entity deployments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R014	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Statistical account support for non-financial KPIs	The system must support statistical (non-financial) accounts for tracking operational metrics alongside financial data. Statistical accounts must accommodate shipping-specific metrics including TEU volumes, vessel capacity utilization percentages, container turnaround days, empty container repositioning counts, and customs clearance processing times per port. Statistical postings must be linked to the same cost centre and profit centre dimensions as financial postings to enable KPI analysis alongside financial performance. The system must support both manual statistical entry and automated capture from operational modules. Statistical accounts must be clearly distinguished from financial accounts in all reports and must be excluded from trial balance and financial statement outputs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R015	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Account lifecycle management and deactivation	The system must implement controlled account lifecycle management with states including draft, active, suspended, and closed. Transition between states must follow configurable workflow rules: draft to active requires approval, active to suspended requires justification and zero balance confirmation for balance sheet accounts, suspended accounts prevent new postings but allow viewing, and closed accounts are fully locked. The system must prevent deactivation of accounts that have open balances or pending unposted transactions. Mass account status changes must be supported for organizational restructuring with preview of affected accounts and transactions. Reactivation of suspended or closed accounts must require senior-level authorization and must create an audit trail entry documenting the reason for reactivation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R016	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Default account assignment rules for automation	The system must support configuration of default GL account assignment rules used by automated posting engines across the ERP. Default account rules must be definable by transaction type (freight invoice, demurrage charge, agency commission), entity, port of operation, customer segment, and container type. For example, freight revenue from Hamad Port import bookings must default to a specific revenue account with the Hamad Port cost centre. The rule engine must support a priority hierarchy resolving conflicts when multiple rules match a transaction. Default assignments must be overridable by authorized users during manual journal entry. The system must validate that all transaction types have complete default account mappings before allowing system go-live.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R017	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Chart of accounts comparison and consolidation mapping	The system must provide tools for comparing charts of accounts across entities and managing consolidation mappings. A visual comparison view must highlight accounts present in one entity but absent in others, accounts with different classifications across entities, and accounts with mismatched reporting hierarchy positions. Consolidation mappings must define how entity-level accounts roll up into the group consolidated chart for preparing group financial statements. The system must support elimination account definitions for inter-company balances, unrealized profits, and other consolidation adjustments. Mapping validation must ensure completeness by flagging entity accounts without consolidation mappings. The comparison tool must export difference reports in Excel format with bilingual Arabic and English account descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-032-2-004-R018	FEAT-032-2-004	MOD-032	Tax account configuration per jurisdiction	The system must support jurisdiction-specific tax account configurations for VAT (Qatar, UAE, KSA) and GST (India). Tax accounts must be structured to capture input tax, output tax, reverse charge tax, and tax adjustments separately for each tax type and rate. For UAE and KSA VAT, standard rate (5%), zero-rated, exempt, and out-of-scope categories must each have designated accounts. For Indian GST, separate accounts must be maintained for CGST, SGST, IGST, and cess components at all applicable rate slabs. The system must map tax accounts to the relevant tax return line items for automated tax filing preparation. Tax account postings must be validated against the tax registration status of the transacting entity to prevent incorrect tax treatment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R001	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Visual Workflow Designer with Drag-and-Drop Canvas	The system must provide a visual drag-and-drop workflow designer canvas that allows authorized users to create, edit, and manage approval workflows for container shipping operations. The canvas must support nodes for approval steps, conditional branches, parallel paths, merge points, delay timers, and termination endpoints. Each node must be configurable with entity-specific parameters for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The designer must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts, maintaining proper node alignment, connector directionality, and label positioning. Workflow diagrams must be exportable as PDF with bilingual labels for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R002	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Shipping Document Type Workflow Templates	The system must provide pre-built workflow templates for at least 20 common container shipping document types including Bill of Lading release, Delivery Order approval, Freight Invoice verification, Container Booking Confirmation, Customs Declaration (SAD), Certificate of Origin, Phytosanitary Certificate, Dangerous Goods Declaration (IMO classes 1-9), Vessel Nomination approval, Demurrage/Detention claim approval, and Letter of Credit document presentation. Each template must be entity-aware, pre-configured with typical approval chains for Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA) operations. Templates must be clonable and customizable without affecting the original template definition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R003	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Conditional Branching Based on Shipment Attributes	The workflow engine must support conditional branching based on shipment attributes including but not limited to: shipment value thresholds in multiple currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), INCOTERMS 2020 terms (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, DAP, FCA), cargo classification (general, hazardous per IMDG code, reefer, out-of-gauge, break-bulk), port of loading/discharge, shipping line, and customer credit rating. Conditions must support complex boolean logic with AND/OR/NOT operators and nested grouping. Currency comparisons must use real-time exchange rates from the ERP treasury module with configurable tolerance bands.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R011	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint SLA Compliance Monitoring	The system must track and report endpoint SLA compliance metrics including uptime percentage, average response time, error rate, and throughput. SLA thresholds must be configurable per endpoint and per entity, reflecting contractual obligations with port authorities such as Mawani (KSA), Qatar Ports Management Company, DP World (Jebel Ali), and Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust. When an endpoint breaches its SLA threshold, the system must automatically generate an incident record, notify the designated support team, and display the breach on the admin dashboard. SLA reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual headers in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R004	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Multi-Entity Workflow Isolation and Cross-Entity Linking	Each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must maintain isolated workflow definitions respecting local regulatory requirements including Qatar Financial Centre rules, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32/2021 on commercial companies, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013. The system must support cross-entity workflow linking where an approval in one entity triggers a dependent workflow in another entity, for example a freight invoice approved in UAE entity triggering a corresponding payable workflow in India entity for inter-company settlement. Cross-entity workflows must maintain full audit trails with entity-specific timestamps and must respect data residency requirements where entity data remains within jurisdictional boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R005	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	AI-Recommended Workflow Optimization	The system must leverage AI to analyze historical workflow execution data and recommend optimizations such as removing redundant approval steps, suggesting parallel execution of independent approvals, identifying bottleneck approvers, and recommending approval threshold adjustments. The AI engine must process at minimum 12 months of historical data across all entities and provide recommendations with confidence scores. Recommendations for workflows involving hazardous cargo (IMDG classes), high-value shipments exceeding USD 500,000 equivalent, or customs-bonded cargo must be flagged for mandatory human review before implementation. The system must generate bilingual (Arabic/English) optimization reports with projected time savings and compliance impact assessments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R006	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Versioning and Rollback Capability	The system must maintain a complete version history of every workflow definition with immutable audit records. Each version must capture the full workflow graph, all conditional rules, approver assignments, SLA parameters, and the user who made the modification along with Hijri and Gregorian timestamps. Active workflow instances must continue executing under the version that was current when they were initiated, while new instances use the latest published version. The system must support rollback to any previous version with a mandatory approval step from a workflow administrator. Rollback operations must generate compliance notifications to all affected approvers and must be logged with reason codes per entity-specific audit requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R007	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Parallel and Sequential Approval Path Configuration	The workflow builder must support configuring both parallel and sequential approval paths within a single workflow. Parallel paths must support configurable completion rules including all-must-approve, majority-approval with configurable threshold percentage, first-response-wins, and weighted-voting where senior approvers carry higher weight. For container shipping scenarios such as BL release requiring both operations manager and finance controller approval simultaneously, the system must track each parallel path independently and merge results according to the configured completion rule. Timeout handling for parallel paths must be configurable per branch, allowing different SLA durations for operations versus finance approval branches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R008	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Trigger Configuration for Shipping Events	The system must support configuring workflow triggers based on shipping lifecycle events including vessel arrival notification, container gate-in at terminal, customs examination order, Bill of Lading issuance, Delivery Order generation, freight invoice creation, demurrage charge threshold breach, container detention period commencement, transhipment booking confirmation, and vessel ETD/ETA changes exceeding configurable thresholds. Triggers must be filterable by port code (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), shipping line SCAC code, service route, container size/type ISO code, and customer segment. Each trigger must support debounce intervals to prevent duplicate workflow instantiation from rapid-fire event emissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R009	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Simulation and Testing Sandbox	The system must provide a sandbox environment where workflow designers can simulate workflow execution using synthetic or anonymized historical shipment data without affecting production operations. The simulation must model realistic approval response times based on historical averages per approver, simulate SLA breaches, test escalation paths, and validate conditional branching logic across different shipment scenarios including multi-currency invoices, hazardous cargo declarations, and cross-entity inter-company transactions. Simulation results must display a visual timeline with projected completion dates in both Hijri and Gregorian calendars, bottleneck analysis, and compliance validation reports for each entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R010	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Status Dashboard with Real-Time Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time workflow monitoring dashboard displaying all active workflow instances with their current step, pending approvers, elapsed time, SLA status (on-track, at-risk, breached), and associated shipment reference numbers. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, workflow type, shipment value range, and SLA status. Dashboard widgets must include workflow completion rate trends, average cycle time by workflow type, top bottleneck approvers, and SLA compliance percentages. The dashboard must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with proper alignment of charts, tables, and progress indicators. Auto-refresh interval must be configurable with a minimum of 10-second polling.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R011	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Offline Workflow Initiation and Sync	The system must support offline workflow initiation for field operations at port terminals where network connectivity may be intermittent, particularly at Hamad Port container yards and JNPT gate complexes. Users must be able to queue workflow approvals and initiation requests locally on their device using a progressive web application with IndexedDB storage. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize queued actions with conflict detection and resolution, applying server-side validation rules before committing. If a workflow definition has been updated during the offline period, the system must flag the queued action for manual review rather than auto-processing against an outdated workflow version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R012	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Access Control and Role-Based Permissions	The system must enforce role-based access control on workflow builder functions with granular permissions for creating, editing, publishing, deactivating, and deleting workflow definitions. Permissions must be entity-specific, allowing a workflow administrator in the UAE entity to manage UAE workflows without visibility into Qatar or KSA workflow definitions unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. The system must support segregation of duties rules preventing the same user from both designing a workflow and being the sole approver within that workflow. All permission changes must be logged with the modifier identity, timestamp in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, and the IP address of the session for compliance auditing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R013	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Integration with External Regulatory Systems	The system must support workflow steps that invoke external regulatory system checks including Qatar Customs (ASYCUDA), Dubai Trade portal, Abu Dhabi Maqta Gateway for Khalifa Port, Saudi FASAH customs platform, and Indian ICEGATE system. Workflow steps must handle asynchronous responses from these systems with configurable timeout periods and retry logic. If an external system is unavailable, the workflow must enter a configurable hold state with automatic retry at exponential backoff intervals. The workflow must capture and store external system response codes, reference numbers, and timestamps as part of the workflow instance audit trail, supporting both XML and JSON response formats per the respective regulatory system specifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R014	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Cloning Across Entities with Localization	The system must allow cloning an existing workflow definition from one entity to another with automatic localization adjustments. When cloning a UAE workflow to KSA entity, the system must prompt for re-mapping of approver roles to KSA equivalents, adjust currency references from AED to SAR, update port codes from Jebel Ali (AEJEA) or Khalifa Port (AEKHL) to appropriate KSA ports such as Jeddah Islamic Port (SAJED) or King Abdulaziz Port (SADMM), and adapt regulatory compliance steps to KSA requirements. The cloning wizard must present a side-by-side comparison of source and target workflows highlighting all changes requiring manual review. Cloned workflows must be created in draft status requiring explicit publication approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R015	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Workflow Performance Analytics and Reporting	The system must generate detailed analytics on workflow performance including average completion time per workflow type segmented by entity and port, approval step duration distribution, rejection rate analysis with reason code categorization, escalation frequency trends, and SLA compliance rates over configurable time periods. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date ranges and must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with proper Arabic character encoding (UTF-8). The system must provide AI-generated executive summaries in both Arabic and English highlighting key performance indicators, anomalies, and actionable recommendations for workflow optimization across all four operational entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-001-R016	FEAT-033-1-001	MOD-033	Bulk Workflow Operations for Peak Season Management	The system must support bulk operations on workflow instances during peak shipping seasons such as Ramadan pre-stocking periods and Indian festival season (Diwali/Navratri). Bulk operations must include mass re-assignment of pending approvals from unavailable approvers, bulk SLA extension for all workflows of a specific type, batch approval of low-risk workflows meeting AI-determined auto-approval criteria, and bulk escalation of overdue workflows. Bulk operations must enforce a configurable maximum batch size per entity, require dual authorization for batches exceeding 100 workflow instances, and generate a comprehensive audit report with before/after states for each affected workflow instance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R001	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Template Definition with Shipping Context Parameters	The system must allow definition of SLA templates parameterized by shipping context attributes including document type (BL, DO, freight invoice, customs declaration), port of operation (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), container type (dry, reefer, hazardous, out-of-gauge), shipment value tier, INCOTERMS 2020 term, and customer service level agreement tier (standard, priority, VIP). Each SLA template must specify target completion duration, warning threshold percentage, escalation tiers with designated recipients, and breach consequence actions. Templates must support time units of minutes, hours, and business days with entity-specific business day calendar references. SLA durations must be expressible in both working hours and calendar hours with configurable selection per template.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R002	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Port-Specific Working Hours and Holiday Calendar Management	The system must maintain separate working hour calendars for each port of operation reflecting actual operational schedules: Hamad Port operating 24/7 for vessel operations but standard business hours for documentation, Jebel Ali with Friday-Saturday weekends and UAE public holidays, Khalifa Port aligned with Abu Dhabi government working hours, and JNPT following Indian public holidays with Saturday half-days. Calendars must incorporate Hijri-based holidays (Eid Al-Fitr, Eid Al-Adha, Prophet's Birthday) with dates auto-calculated from Hijri calendar conversion, and Gregorian fixed holidays (Indian Republic Day, Independence Day, UAE National Day, Qatar National Day, Saudi National Day). SLA timers must pause during non-working hours and holidays according to the applicable port calendar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R003	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Real-Time SLA Timer with Pause and Resume Capabilities	The system must maintain real-time SLA countdown timers for every active workflow step. Timers must automatically pause when a workflow step enters a hold state due to external dependencies such as pending customs examination results, awaiting carrier confirmation, or port authority documentation clearance. Paused time must be tracked separately and excluded from SLA elapsed time calculations. The timer must support manual pause by authorized users with mandatory reason code selection from a configurable list including customer-requested-hold, regulatory-hold, force-majeure, and system-dependency-wait. All pause and resume events must be logged with operator identity, timestamp in Hijri and Gregorian formats, and the associated reason code for SLA compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R004	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Multi-Tier Escalation on SLA Warning and Breach	The system must support configurable multi-tier escalation when SLA thresholds are approached or breached. At minimum three escalation tiers must be supported: warning tier (configurable at 70-90% of SLA target), critical tier (at 90-100% of SLA target), and breach tier (at 100%+ of SLA target). Each tier must trigger independently configurable actions including email notification, SMS alert, in-app push notification, Microsoft Teams message, dashboard color coding change (green to amber to red), automatic re-assignment to backup approver, and escalation to next management level. Escalation recipients must be resolvable dynamically based on the entity, department, and document type of the workflow instance, supporting both named users and role-based resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R012	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Access Control by Entity and Role	The system must enforce role-based access control for endpoint management operations, restricting who can register, modify, activate, deactivate, or delete endpoints. Access must be scoped by entity so that a Qatar entity administrator cannot modify endpoints belonging to the UAE or KSA entity without explicit cross-entity permissions. The system must support at minimum three roles for endpoint management: Endpoint Viewer (read-only), Endpoint Manager (CRUD operations), and Endpoint Administrator (full control including credential management). All access control changes must be logged in the audit trail with both Gregorian and Hijri dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R005	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	AI-Powered SLA Breach Prediction	The system must employ AI models to predict potential SLA breaches before they occur by analyzing current workflow progress, historical completion patterns for similar document types and approvers, current approver workload, time of day and day of week patterns, and upcoming holiday periods. Predictions must be generated with a minimum lead time of 2 hours before projected breach and must include a confidence score percentage and recommended mitigation actions such as re-assignment to a less loaded approver or parallel routing. The AI model must be trained separately for each entity to account for entity-specific operational patterns and must achieve a minimum prediction accuracy of 85% as measured by monthly model performance reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R006	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Compliance Dashboard with Entity Comparison	The system must provide an SLA compliance dashboard presenting real-time and historical SLA performance metrics across all entities. The dashboard must display SLA compliance rate as a percentage, average completion time versus target, breach count and trend, top breached SLA categories, and worst-performing workflow steps. A cross-entity comparison view must allow management to benchmark performance between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations using normalized metrics that account for different working hour calendars and holiday schedules. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with properly mirrored chart axes, right-aligned data tables, and Arabic numeral display option. Date range selectors must support both Hijri and Gregorian calendar inputs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R007	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Measurement for Multi-Currency Invoice Approvals	The system must apply differentiated SLA targets for invoice approval workflows based on invoice value tiers in the entity's functional currency. For UAE entity (AED), tiers must be configurable with defaults of: Tier 1 under AED 50,000 with 4-hour SLA, Tier 2 AED 50,000-500,000 with 8-hour SLA, Tier 3 over AED 500,000 with 24-hour SLA. Equivalent tiers must be maintained for QAR, SAR, INR, and USD with automatic recalculation when exchange rates fluctuate beyond a configurable threshold (default 2%). When a shipment invoice spans multiple currencies due to multi-leg routing, the system must convert to a single reference currency using the treasury module exchange rate for SLA tier determination and must log the applied exchange rate for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R008	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Exception Management for Force Majeure Events	The system must support SLA exception declarations for force majeure events affecting port operations such as severe weather disruptions, port congestion emergencies, labor actions, or vessel mechanical failures. An authorized user must be able to declare a force majeure event specifying the affected port(s), start time, estimated end time, and impacted workflow types. During an active force majeure event, all matching SLA timers must be automatically suspended with a force-majeure pause reason. Upon event closure, timers must resume and a reconciliation report must be generated listing all affected workflow instances, total suspended duration, and revised SLA target dates. Force majeure declarations must require approval from entity-level management and must be logged per regulatory compliance requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R009	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Customer-Facing SLA Visibility Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing portal view displaying the real-time SLA status of their shipment-related workflows without exposing internal approval chain details. Customers must see the current stage (e.g., documentation review, customs processing, finance approval), estimated completion time, and actual versus target SLA performance for each workflow associated with their shipments. The portal must support Arabic and English language toggle, display dates in both Hijri and Gregorian formats based on customer preference, and show amounts in the customer's preferred currency with real-time conversion from the entity's functional currency. Customer access must be authenticated via the ERP customer portal module and must enforce data isolation preventing customers from viewing other customers' workflow statuses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R010	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Reporting with Regulatory Compliance Metrics	The system must generate SLA compliance reports aligned with regulatory requirements in each jurisdiction. For Qatar operations, reports must include metrics required by Qatar Financial Centre Authority for financial document processing times. For UAE operations, reports must track Federal Tax Authority VAT invoice processing SLAs. For KSA operations, reports must include ZATCA e-invoicing compliance turnaround metrics. For India operations, reports must cover GST invoice processing timelines and customs clearance SLAs mandated by CBIC. Reports must be generatable on demand and on configurable schedules (daily, weekly, monthly) with automatic distribution to compliance officers. All reports must include digital timestamps, entity identification, and must be generated in both Arabic and English where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R011	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Dependency Chain Tracking for End-to-End Shipment Lifecycle	The system must track SLA dependencies across the entire shipment lifecycle from booking confirmation through to final delivery and invoice settlement. When a customs clearance workflow at JNPT is dependent on a BL amendment approval at Jebel Ali, the system must link these SLA timers and calculate the cumulative end-to-end SLA exposure. Dependency chains must be visualized as a Gantt-style timeline showing critical path items, slack time on non-critical paths, and projected end-to-end completion dates. If a predecessor workflow step breaches its SLA, the system must automatically recalculate downstream SLA targets and trigger proactive notifications to all stakeholders in the dependency chain with revised estimated completion times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R012	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Penalty and Incentive Calculation Engine	The system must calculate financial penalties and incentives based on SLA performance for customer-facing service level agreements. Penalty calculations must support tiered structures where the penalty amount increases with the severity and duration of the breach, configurable as a percentage of the shipment freight value or as fixed amounts per time unit of delay. Calculations must be performed in the contract currency and converted to the entity's functional currency using the exchange rate applicable at the date of breach. The system must generate penalty/incentive accrual entries for integration with the ERP finance module and must produce customer-facing SLA performance statements in both Arabic and English with detailed breach chronologies for dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R013	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Automated SLA Threshold Adjustment Using AI Analytics	The system must use AI analytics to recommend SLA threshold adjustments based on historical performance data, seasonal patterns, and operational capacity changes. For example, during peak pre-Ramadan shipping periods at Jebel Ali and Hamad Port, the AI must detect increased average processing times and recommend temporary SLA relaxation for specific workflow types with a proposed adjustment percentage and effective date range. Recommendations must be presented as draft adjustments requiring management approval before activation. The AI must also identify workflows where current SLA targets are consistently beaten by large margins and recommend tightening to drive continuous improvement. All AI-recommended adjustments must include the statistical basis, confidence interval, and projected impact on customer satisfaction scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R014	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Clock Synchronization Across Distributed Systems	The system must ensure accurate SLA time measurement across distributed microservices and geographically dispersed data centers serving Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. All SLA timer events must be recorded with UTC timestamps synchronized via NTP with a maximum clock drift tolerance of 100 milliseconds. The system must handle timezone conversions for display purposes: AST (UTC+3) for Qatar, GST (UTC+4) for UAE, AST (UTC+3) for KSA, and IST (UTC+5:30) for India. SLA elapsed time calculations must be timezone-agnostic using UTC internally, with timezone-aware presentation in user-facing displays. The system must detect and alert on clock synchronization failures that could compromise SLA measurement accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R015	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Audit Trail with Tamper-Proof Logging	The system must maintain a tamper-proof audit trail for all SLA-related events including timer start, pause, resume, escalation trigger, breach declaration, exception application, and manual override. Each audit entry must contain the event type, UTC timestamp, local timestamp in the entity timezone, operator identity, workflow instance reference, associated shipment and document references, and the before/after state of the SLA timer. Audit records must be stored in an append-only data structure with cryptographic hash chaining to detect any unauthorized modifications. The audit trail must be retained for a minimum of 7 years per GCC commercial record retention requirements and 8 years for India operations per Companies Act 2013 provisions. Audit data must be exportable in structured formats for external auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R016	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	SLA Integration with Carrier and Terminal Performance Tracking	The system must correlate internal workflow SLA performance with external carrier and terminal performance metrics. When a BL release workflow SLA is breached due to delayed vessel berthing information from the terminal operator at Hamad Port or JNPT, the system must capture the external dependency as a contributing factor. The system must maintain a carrier and terminal performance scorecard tracking on-time information provision, response time to documentation queries, and EDI message delivery reliability. These external performance metrics must feed into the AI breach prediction model as input features and must be included in SLA compliance reports to distinguish between internally controllable and externally dependent SLA performance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-002-R017	FEAT-033-1-002	MOD-033	Offline SLA Timer Continuity and Reconciliation	The system must ensure SLA timer continuity even when the user's client application is offline or disconnected from the server. SLA timers are server-side and must continue counting regardless of client connectivity. When a user operates offline at a port terminal (common at JNPT container freight stations and Hamad Port yard areas), any approval actions taken offline must be timestamped locally on the device and reconciled with the server-side SLA timer upon reconnection. If the offline approval timestamp falls within the SLA target, the approval must be recognized as timely. Discrepancies between client and server clocks exceeding 5 minutes must be flagged for manual review by the SLA compliance officer. The system must handle network partitions gracefully without creating duplicate SLA timer entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R001	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Matrix Structure with Multi-Dimensional Authority Levels	The system must support a multi-dimensional DOA matrix where authority levels are defined across dimensions including transaction type (procurement, freight payment, customer credit, customs duty, demurrage settlement), monetary value tier, entity jurisdiction, department, and cost center. Each cell in the matrix must specify the authorized role or individual, maximum approval amount in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), whether sole or joint approval is required, and any conditional constraints such as requiring additional approval for transactions involving related parties. The matrix must support unlimited authority levels per dimension and must enforce that no transaction can proceed without matching a valid DOA matrix entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R002	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Multi-Currency Approval Threshold Management	The system must define and enforce DOA approval thresholds in each entity's functional currency while supporting transaction amounts in any operational currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP, CNY). When a freight invoice of USD 150,000 requires approval in the UAE entity, the system must convert to AED using the applicable exchange rate from the treasury module to determine the correct DOA authority level. The system must handle exchange rate volatility by applying the rate at the time of workflow initiation and must re-evaluate the DOA level if the rate changes beyond a configurable threshold (default 3%) before approval is completed. Split-currency transactions common in container shipping (e.g., ocean freight in USD plus local charges in AED) must aggregate the total equivalent value for DOA threshold determination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R003	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Temporary Delegation for Leave and Travel Coverage	The system must support temporary delegation of authority from one user to another for specified date ranges, covering scenarios such as annual leave, business travel, sick leave, and Hajj/Umrah leave common in GCC operations. Temporary delegations must specify the delegator, delegate, effective start date and time, expiry date and time, scope of delegated authority (all authorities or specific transaction types), and maximum delegated amount which cannot exceed the delegator's own authority limit. The system must support both Hijri and Gregorian date selection for delegation periods. Upon delegation expiry, all pending workflow items must automatically revert to the original authority holder. Active delegations must be visible on the DOA dashboard and must be auditable with the original delegator's identity always recorded alongside the delegate's approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R004	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Joint and Sequential Approval Enforcement	The system must enforce joint approval requirements where the DOA matrix specifies that certain transactions require approval from multiple authorities. Joint approval types must include any-two-of-three, all-must-approve, sequential-chain (A then B then C), and department-head-plus-finance patterns. For container shipping operations, the system must support common joint approval scenarios such as: demurrage claims exceeding AED 100,000 requiring both operations director and finance controller approval, hazardous cargo bookings (IMDG classes 1, 6, and 7) requiring both safety officer and operations manager, and customer credit limit increases requiring both sales director and CFO. The system must prevent approval by the same person in multiple roles within a joint approval chain to maintain segregation of duties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R005	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Matrix Version Control with Board Resolution Tracking	The system must maintain versioned DOA matrices linked to corporate governance records such as board resolutions, management committee minutes, and power of attorney documents. Each DOA matrix version must record the authorizing document reference, effective date, approving authority (board of directors, management committee, or entity CEO), and digital copy of the authorizing document. Changes to authority levels exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., increasing any single approval limit by more than 25%) must require board-level authorization. The system must ensure that only one version of the DOA matrix is active per entity at any time and must retain historical versions for a minimum of 10 years for corporate governance compliance across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R006	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Role-Based and Named-Individual Authority Assignment	The system must support authority assignment to both organizational roles (e.g., Finance Manager, Operations Director, Regional Head) and named individuals. Role-based assignments must automatically apply to any user holding that role in the HR/organizational module, including users with acting or interim role assignments. Named-individual assignments must override role-based limits when specified, allowing exceptions such as a senior shipping manager being granted higher authority than their role default for a specific port operation. The system must detect and alert on conflicts where a named-individual assignment contradicts their role-based authority (either higher or lower) and must require explicit acknowledgment from the DOA administrator. Authority effective dates must support future-dated assignments for planned organizational changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R007	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Compliance Validation at Workflow Runtime	The system must perform real-time DOA compliance validation at every approval step within a workflow. Before presenting an approval task to a user, the system must verify that the user has sufficient authority for the transaction type and amount as defined in the active DOA matrix for their entity. If a user's authority is insufficient, the system must automatically route to the next higher authority level or trigger an escalation workflow. The validation must account for cumulative approval exposure, preventing a user from approving multiple related transactions that individually fall within their limit but cumulatively exceed it (e.g., split invoices from the same vendor for the same shipment). Validation failures must be logged with detailed reason codes for compliance auditing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R008	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Cross-Entity DOA for Inter-Company Transactions	The system must handle DOA requirements for inter-company transactions between the four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). When a container shipment involves freight booked by the UAE entity with discharge at JNPT handled by the India entity, the inter-company settlement transaction must satisfy DOA requirements in both entities. The system must support configurable rules for which entity's DOA takes precedence or whether both must be satisfied. For transfer pricing adjustments between entities, the system must enforce enhanced DOA requirements with higher authority levels than standard transactions, as required by GCC transfer pricing regulations and Indian transfer pricing provisions under Section 92 of the Income Tax Act. Cross-entity DOA validation must be atomic, ensuring both entity approvals are obtained before the transaction proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R009	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Emergency Override Authority with Enhanced Audit Controls	The system must provide an emergency override mechanism for time-critical shipping operations where the designated approver is unavailable and the standard delegation chain is exhausted. Emergency overrides must be restricted to a configurable list of senior management roles (minimum CEO or CFO level per entity). The override must require the emergency approver to provide a mandatory justification text, select from predefined urgency reason codes (vessel-sailing-cutoff, customs-deadline, customer-critical, regulatory-deadline), and acknowledge that the override will be flagged in the next board compliance report. Emergency overrides must trigger immediate notifications to the original authority holder, the entity compliance officer, and the group internal audit function. The system must enforce a maximum number of emergency overrides per entity per calendar month, configurable by entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R010	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Matrix Visualization and Gap Analysis	The system must provide a visual representation of the DOA matrix showing authority hierarchies, approval chains, and coverage gaps. The visualization must display the complete authority structure as an interactive organizational tree with filterable dimensions (entity, department, transaction type, value tier). A gap analysis function must identify scenarios where no authority is defined, such as a new transaction type lacking DOA coverage or a value tier between defined thresholds without an assigned approver. The visualization must highlight potential segregation of duties conflicts where the same individual appears in conflicting roles within an approval chain. All visual elements must support Arabic RTL layout with proper right-to-left hierarchy rendering and Arabic labels alongside English for bilingual organizations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R011	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Threshold Auto-Adjustment for Inflation and Currency Fluctuation	The system must support periodic review and adjustment of DOA monetary thresholds to account for currency fluctuation and inflation. An AI-assisted review process must analyze transaction volume distribution across authority tiers and recommend threshold adjustments when significant shifts are detected, such as when currency depreciation causes a disproportionate number of routine transactions to escalate to higher authority tiers. For example, if INR depreciates against USD causing standard container freight invoices to breach the Tier 2 threshold in INR terms, the system must recommend adjusting the INR threshold proportionally. Recommended adjustments must be presented with supporting data, require DOA administrator approval, and must not take effect until the next DOA matrix version is formally activated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R012	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Segregation of Duties Enforcement and Conflict Detection	The system must enforce segregation of duties rules within the DOA matrix to prevent fraud and comply with internal control requirements. The system must detect and prevent configurations where the same individual can initiate and approve a transaction, approve both the purchase order and the corresponding invoice, or approve transactions with related parties (customers or vendors linked to the approver per the conflict of interest register). Segregation rules must be entity-specific to comply with local regulations: Qatar QFC Anti-Money Laundering rules, UAE Federal Law requirements, KSA Corporate Governance Regulations, and Indian Companies Act Section 188 related-party provisions. The system must run a daily automated segregation compliance check and generate exception reports for the internal audit team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R013	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Integration with HR Organizational Changes	The system must automatically respond to HR organizational changes that affect DOA assignments. When an employee is promoted, transferred to a different entity, placed on long-term leave, or terminated, the system must identify all DOA entries referencing that employee and take appropriate action: for promotions, flag for DOA limit review; for transfers, remove from source entity DOA and flag for target entity DOA setup; for long-term leave, activate standing delegation or escalate to next authority; for termination, immediately revoke all DOA authorities and re-route pending approvals. The system must process these changes within one business hour of the HR event and must generate a reconciliation report for the DOA administrator listing all actions taken and any manual interventions required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R014	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Audit Report Generation for External Auditors	The system must generate comprehensive DOA audit reports suitable for external auditor review during annual financial audits. Reports must include the complete active DOA matrix with all authority levels and thresholds, a log of all DOA matrix changes during the audit period with authorizing references, a list of all temporary delegations granted with utilization statistics, emergency override usage with justifications, and a segregation of duties compliance summary. Reports must be filterable by entity, audit period (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian fiscal years), and transaction type. The report format must comply with International Standards on Auditing (ISA) requirements and must be exportable in PDF with digital signatures and in machine-readable XML format for auditor analysis tools.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R015	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	Mobile DOA Approval with Biometric Authentication	The system must support DOA-validated approvals via mobile devices (iOS and Android) with enhanced authentication for high-value transactions. For transactions exceeding a configurable threshold per entity (default: QAR 100,000 / AED 100,000 / SAR 100,000 / INR 2,000,000), the mobile approval must require biometric authentication (fingerprint or facial recognition) in addition to the standard session authentication. The mobile approval interface must display the complete transaction summary including shipment reference, vendor/customer name, amount in transaction and entity functional currencies, DOA authority being exercised, and any special conditions. The mobile interface must support both Arabic and English with proper RTL rendering and must function in low-bandwidth environments common at port terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-003-R016	FEAT-033-1-003	MOD-033	DOA Compliance Scorecard by Entity and Department	The system must maintain a DOA compliance scorecard tracking key metrics per entity and department including: percentage of transactions approved within DOA limits, number and value of emergency overrides, delegation utilization rate, segregation of duties violation attempts, and average time to complete DOA-required approvals. The scorecard must provide trend analysis over configurable periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) aligned with both Hijri and Gregorian calendars. Departments with compliance scores below configurable thresholds must be automatically flagged for enhanced monitoring. The scorecard must be accessible to entity compliance officers, group internal audit, and entity CEOs, with role-based visibility restrictions ensuring each entity can only view its own detailed data while group-level users see consolidated cross-entity comparisons.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R001	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Rule-Based Assignment Engine with Shipping Domain Attributes	The system must provide a rule-based assignment engine that routes workflow items based on configurable shipping domain attributes including port of loading/discharge (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), shipping line, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 45HC), cargo commodity code (HS code first 4 digits), INCOTERMS 2020 term, shipment value range, customer account tier, trade lane (e.g., Gulf-India, Gulf-Europe, Gulf-Far East), and document type. Rules must be evaluable in a configurable priority order where the first matching rule determines assignment. A default catch-all rule must be mandatory for each entity to ensure no workflow item remains unassigned. Rules must support both simple attribute matching and complex expressions with AND/OR/NOT logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R002	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Round-Robin Assignment with Weighted Distribution	The system must support round-robin assignment across a pool of qualified approvers with configurable weighting. Weights must be assignable per pool member based on factors such as experience level, working hours availability, and entity assignment. For example, a senior documentation officer at Jebel Ali handling BL verification may receive a weight of 1.5 compared to a junior officer's weight of 1.0, resulting in 60/40 task distribution. The round-robin algorithm must account for each member's current working status (online, offline, on-leave, in-meeting) obtained from the HR calendar and presence module. The distribution must be tracked with counters that reset at configurable intervals (daily, weekly, monthly) and must exclude items that were manually reassigned from the distribution count to maintain fair allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R003	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	AI-Driven Workload Balancing Across Approver Pools	The system must use AI to dynamically balance workload across approver pools by considering each approver's current queue depth, average processing time per workflow type, historical approval throughput, current online status, and projected availability based on their calendar. The AI must factor in workflow urgency (SLA remaining time) and complexity (estimated from document type, shipment value, and cargo classification) when making assignment decisions. During peak periods such as end-of-month closing at Indian operations or pre-Eid shipping surges at GCC ports, the AI must proactively redistribute pending tasks from overloaded approvers to those with capacity. The system must provide a workload heatmap visualization showing current load distribution across all approver pools with drill-down capability by entity, department, and individual.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R004	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Skill-Based Routing for Specialized Shipping Operations	The system must support skill-based routing where workflow items requiring specialized knowledge are assigned to approvers with verified competencies. Skills must be maintainable in an approver profile and must include categories such as hazardous cargo handling (with IMDG code class specificity), reefer cargo management, out-of-gauge/project cargo, livestock shipments, Letter of Credit documentation, customs bonded warehouse operations, free zone regulations (JAFZA, QFZ, SAGIA), and trade-specific expertise (pharmaceuticals, food products, chemicals). The routing engine must match required skills derived from the shipment attributes with approver skill profiles and must route only to approvers possessing all required skills. If no skilled approver is available, the system must escalate to the team lead with a skill-gap notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R005	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Geography-Based Routing with Time Zone Awareness	The system must support geography-based routing that assigns workflow items to approvers in the most appropriate timezone for timely processing. For a customs clearance workflow initiated at JNPT (IST, UTC+5:30) outside of India business hours, the system must be configurable to either hold for the next India business day or route to a UAE-based approver (GST, UTC+4) with cross-entity approval authority if the SLA requires immediate action. The routing engine must maintain a follow-the-sun configuration where workflow items can be routed to approvers across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India based on current business hours at each location. Time zone routing must respect DOA matrix constraints, only routing to approvers with sufficient authority in the target entity, and must log the geographic routing decision rationale in the workflow audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R006	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Customer Account-Based Routing to Dedicated Teams	The system must support routing workflow items to dedicated customer service teams based on customer account assignments. Key accounts such as major trading houses, NVOCC operators, and government entities must be assignable to dedicated teams who handle all workflow items for that customer across document types. The routing must support hierarchical customer grouping where a parent company routing rule applies to all subsidiaries unless overridden. For customers with operations across multiple entities (e.g., a trading company with shipments through both Jebel Ali and JNPT), the system must route to the entity-specific team member within the dedicated team. Customer routing assignments must integrate with the CRM module for automatic updates when account ownership changes occur.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R007	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Automatic Re-Assignment on Approver Unavailability	The system must automatically detect approver unavailability and re-assign pending workflow items according to configurable rules. Unavailability triggers must include: approver not responding within a configurable time threshold (default 2 hours), approver's HR calendar showing out-of-office status, approver's session inactive beyond a timeout period, or approver explicitly declining the assignment. Re-assignment must follow a priority sequence: first to the configured backup approver, then to the next available team member by workload, then escalation to the team lead. For time-sensitive operations such as vessel sailing cutoff documentation at Hamad Port or JNPT, the re-assignment threshold must be reduced to a configurable minimum (default 30 minutes). All re-assignments must preserve the full workflow context and audit trail including the reason for re-assignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R008	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Priority-Based Queue Management with Shipping Urgency Factors	The system must implement priority-based queue management for each approver where workflow items are ordered by calculated priority scores. Priority calculation must factor in SLA remaining time (highest weight), shipment value, vessel sailing cutoff proximity, customer tier, cargo perishability (reefer cargo priority), hazardous cargo regulatory deadlines, customs examination response deadlines at JNPT and Jebel Ali, and explicit priority overrides by operations management. The system must display the prioritized queue in the approver's task list with visual indicators (color coding and priority labels in both Arabic and English). Queue priority must be recalculated dynamically at configurable intervals (default every 15 minutes) to reflect changing conditions such as approaching vessel cutoffs or SLA warning thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R009	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Routing Rule Testing and Impact Analysis	The system must provide a routing rule testing tool that allows administrators to simulate the effect of new or modified routing rules before activation. The simulation must process a configurable sample of recent historical workflow items (default last 30 days) through the proposed rules and display the projected assignment distribution compared to actual assignments. Impact analysis must highlight changes in workload distribution per approver, potential SLA impact from different assignment patterns, and any workflow items that would be unassigned under the new rules. The testing tool must support what-if scenarios such as removing a team member from the pool or adding a new skill requirement. Test results must be exportable as a report for management review before rule activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R010	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Multi-Level Routing for Complex Approval Chains	The system must support multi-level routing where each step in a multi-step approval workflow can have independent routing rules. For a freight invoice approval workflow that requires operations verification, then finance review, then management approval, the system must apply operations-specific routing rules at step one (routing to operations team members based on port and shipping line), finance-specific rules at step two (routing based on invoice currency and value tier), and DOA-based rules at step three (routing to the lowest authority level sufficient for the invoice amount). Each routing level must independently apply its assignment algorithm (round-robin, skill-based, workload-balanced) as configured. Routing decisions at each level must be logged separately in the workflow audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R011	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	AI-Powered Assignment Recommendation with Approval Prediction	The system must leverage AI to recommend the optimal approver for each workflow item based on historical patterns of approval decisions. The AI must analyze past approval/rejection patterns per approver, average response time, domain expertise demonstrated through historical assignments, and relationship with the requesting party. For borderline cases such as demurrage claims near the contestable threshold, the AI must predict approval likelihood per potential assignee and flag potential biases. The AI recommendation must be presented alongside the rule-based assignment with a confidence score, and the system must default to the rule-based assignment unless the AI recommendation has a confidence score above a configurable threshold (default 90%). All AI recommendations must be logged for model performance monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R012	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Routing Exception Handling for Unmatched Workflow Items	The system must provide comprehensive exception handling for workflow items that cannot be matched to any routing rule or assigned to any available approver. Exception scenarios must include: no routing rule matches the workflow attributes, all matched approvers are unavailable, the required skill combination has no qualified approvers in the entity, and DOA constraints eliminate all potential assignees. For each exception type, the system must support configurable fallback actions including assignment to entity-level supervisor, assignment to a cross-entity shared services team, placement in a monitored exception queue, or triggering an urgent notification to the workflow administrator. Exception queue items must have accelerated SLA tracking with reduced warning thresholds and must be included in daily operational reports for all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R013	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Seasonal and Event-Based Routing Profile Switching	The system must support configurable routing profiles that can be activated based on seasonal patterns or specific events. Routing profiles must be definable for scenarios such as Ramadan operations (reduced staffing, adjusted working hours across GCC entities), Indian festival periods (Diwali, Holi closures), year-end closing (increased financial approval routing to senior staff), port congestion events (load distribution to backup teams), and new service route launches. Profile activation must support both manual triggering by operations management and automatic activation based on Hijri/Gregorian calendar date ranges. When a seasonal profile is active, it must override the default routing rules for specified workflow types while maintaining all other rules unchanged. Profile transitions must be logged with activation timestamps and the authorizing user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R014	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Routing Analytics and Assignment Effectiveness Reporting	The system must generate routing analytics reports measuring assignment effectiveness including: first-assignment success rate (percentage of items completed by the initially assigned approver without re-routing), average assignment-to-action time per routing method, workload distribution equity index (Gini coefficient across approver pools), skill utilization rate per approver, and cross-entity routing frequency. Reports must be segmented by entity, port, workflow type, and time period supporting both Hijri and Gregorian calendar selections. The system must identify routing rule conflicts where multiple rules match the same workflow attributes and provide deduplication recommendations. All reports must be available in both Arabic and English with proper RTL rendering for Arabic versions including right-aligned numeric columns and Arabic chart labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R015	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Manual Override with Justification for Auto-Assigned Items	The system must allow authorized users such as team leads and operations managers to manually override auto-assigned workflow items and re-route them to a different approver. Manual overrides must require a mandatory justification selected from a configurable reason code list including workload-rebalancing, subject-matter-expertise-required, customer-relationship-requirement, conflict-of-interest-avoidance, and training-opportunity. The override must validate that the target approver meets DOA authority requirements and possesses required skills for the workflow type. The system must track override frequency per user and generate monthly reports flagging users with override rates exceeding a configurable threshold (default 15% of assignments) for management review. Override patterns must feed into the AI routing model to improve future automatic assignments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R016	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Real-Time Assignment Notification with Contextual Shipment Summary	The system must deliver real-time assignment notifications to approvers through multiple channels (in-app notification, email, SMS, mobile push notification, Microsoft Teams) when a workflow item is assigned to them. Each notification must include a contextual summary containing: shipment reference number, BL or document number, vessel name and voyage, port of loading and discharge, container count and types, cargo description, customer name, transaction amount with currency, SLA deadline with countdown, and a direct deep-link to the approval screen. Notifications must be rendered in the approver's preferred language (Arabic or English) with proper RTL formatting for Arabic. The system must support notification delivery preferences per user allowing channel selection by urgency level and must implement delivery confirmation tracking with retry logic for failed notification attempts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-1-004-R017	FEAT-033-1-004	MOD-033	Offline Routing Rule Cache and Local Assignment	The system must support offline operation for routing and assignment in environments with intermittent connectivity at port terminals. The progressive web application must cache the current routing rules, approver availability matrix, and DOA matrix locally. When a workflow item requires assignment while offline, the system must apply cached routing rules to determine the assignment and queue the assignment action for server synchronization upon reconnection. The local cache must be refreshed at configurable intervals (default every 30 minutes when online) and must include a cache staleness indicator warning users when routing data exceeds a configurable age threshold (default 4 hours). Upon reconnection, the server must validate the locally determined assignment against current state and flag any conflicts for manual resolution, such as when the assigned approver went on leave during the offline period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R001	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Bilingual Email Template Authoring Interface	The system must provide a WYSIWYG template authoring interface that supports simultaneous Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR) content editing within a single template definition. Each template must allow separate subject lines, body content, headers, and footers for Arabic and English versions. The editor must correctly render mixed-direction text including port names (e.g., Hamad Port, ميناء حمد), INCOTERMS codes (FOB, CIF, CFR, DDP), container identifiers (TRIU1234567), and BL numbers. Templates must be versioned with full audit trail showing which user created or modified each version, with timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The interface must support preview rendering before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R002	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Dynamic Data Binding with Shipping Context Variables	The template engine must support dynamic variable binding from all ERP modules using a standardized placeholder syntax such as {{booking.vessel_name}}, {{container.number}}, {{bl.release_date}}. The system must provide a complete variable catalogue organized by shipping domain including Booking, BL (Bill of Lading), Container Tracking, Customs Declaration, Invoice, DO (Delivery Order), and Vessel Schedule modules. Variable resolution must handle multi-currency formatting where amounts like freight charges are displayed in the correct currency symbol and decimal precision (USD 2 decimals, QAR 2 decimals, INR 2 decimals, AED 2 decimals, SAR 2 decimals). Unresolved variables must be flagged during template preview and must not render as raw placeholders in production emails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R003	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Entity-Specific Email Branding and Sender Configuration	Each legal entity (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations) must have independently configurable email branding including company logo, color scheme, registered address, CR/trade license numbers, and legal disclaimers. Sender addresses must be configurable per entity and per notification type (e.g., operations@entity-qa.com for vessel updates, finance@entity-ae.com for invoice remittances). The system must support DKIM, SPF, and DMARC authentication for each sending domain. Entity-specific footers must include jurisdiction-appropriate legal text such as QFCRA disclaimers for Qatar, JAFZA references for UAE, ZATCA compliance notes for KSA, and GST registration details for India JNPT operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R004	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Shipping Document Email Attachment Handling	The email engine must support automatic attachment of shipping documents including Bills of Lading (original and copies), commercial invoices, packing lists, certificates of origin, customs declarations, delivery orders, and container inspection reports. Attachments must be generated as PDF from ERP document modules and attached inline or as downloadable links based on file size thresholds (configurable, default 5MB per attachment, 15MB total). For documents exceeding the threshold, the system must generate secure time-limited download links (default 72 hours expiry). The system must support watermarking of draft documents with DRAFT or مسودة overlay, and final documents must carry digital signatures where required by port authority regulations at Hamad Port (QTerminals) or JNPT (Mumbai Port Trust).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R005	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Bulk Email Dispatch and Rate Limiting	The system must support bulk email dispatch for scenarios such as vessel schedule changes affecting multiple bookings, port congestion advisories at Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port, and mass invoice distribution at month-end closing. Bulk dispatch must implement configurable rate limiting per SMTP provider to avoid blacklisting, with default limits of 500 emails per hour per sending domain. The system must queue bulk dispatches with priority levels (critical for BL release notifications, high for customs deadline alerts, normal for marketing updates, low for periodic reports). Queue processing must be resumable after system interruption, ensuring no duplicate emails are sent. Dispatch logs must record message ID, recipient, template used, entity, timestamp, and delivery status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R006	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Email Delivery Tracking and Bounce Management	The system must track email delivery status through the full lifecycle: queued, sent, delivered, opened, clicked, bounced (soft/hard), and complained (spam). Bounce management must automatically categorize bounces and implement retry logic for soft bounces (up to 3 retries over 24 hours) and suppression list addition for hard bounces. When a critical shipping notification (BL release, customs clearance deadline, DO expiry) bounces, the system must automatically escalate by attempting alternate contact channels (SMS, WhatsApp) and alerting the assigned operations agent. Delivery analytics must be available per template, per entity, and per time period, with exportable reports showing delivery rates, open rates, and bounce rates for compliance auditing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R007	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Conditional Content Blocks in Templates	The template engine must support conditional content blocks that render different sections based on shipping context data. Conditions must include container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF reefer containers), trade lane (intra-GCC, GCC-India, international), INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, FCA), customs regime (transit, re-export, temporary import, free zone), and entity jurisdiction. For example, a cargo arrival notification for reefer containers at Hamad Port must include temperature monitoring data and cold chain instructions, while a dry container notification omits these sections. Conditional blocks must support nested logic (AND/OR operators) and must be testable through the template preview with sample data sets representing each major shipping scenario.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R008	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Hijri and Gregorian Date Formatting in Emails	All date fields rendered in email templates must support dual calendar display showing both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) dates based on recipient preferences and entity configuration. The default for QAR and SAR entities must display Hijri as the primary date with Gregorian in parentheses, while USD and INR entities default to Gregorian primary. Date formatting must respect locale conventions: dd/MM/yyyy for GCC entities and dd-MM-yyyy for India. Critical shipping dates such as free time expiry, demurrage start date, customs declaration deadline, and BL surrender cutoff must include timezone-aware timestamps showing both origin port and destination port local times. The system must correctly calculate Hijri dates including month variations and must not rely on simple arithmetic conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R009	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Email Template Approval Workflow	New or modified email templates must undergo an approval workflow before activation in production. The workflow must support at least a two-level approval chain: content reviewer (verifies accuracy of shipping terminology, port references, and regulatory text) and compliance approver (verifies legal disclaimers, data privacy statements per PDPL for KSA, NDPD for Qatar, and IT Act for India). Templates containing financial data (invoice amounts, demurrage charges, freight rates) must additionally require finance department approval. The approval workflow must support delegation during approver absence, deadline-based auto-escalation (configurable, default 48 hours), and rejection with mandatory comments. Approved templates must be immutable; any changes create a new version requiring re-approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R010	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	AI-Generated Email Content Suggestions	The system must leverage AI capabilities to suggest email content for common shipping notification scenarios, achieving the 95% automation target. AI suggestions must cover subject line optimization, body text generation from structured shipment data, and automatic summary generation for complex multi-container bookings. The AI engine must generate contextually appropriate content in both Arabic and English, understanding shipping terminology including vessel names, voyage numbers, port codes (QAHAD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKLP for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT), and trade-specific jargon. All AI-generated content must be reviewable before dispatch, with confidence scores displayed to the operator. Low-confidence suggestions (below 85%) must be flagged for human review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R011	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Multi-Recipient and CC/BCC Routing Rules	The system must support intelligent multi-recipient routing based on shipment stakeholder roles. Each notification type must have configurable recipient rules mapping roles (shipper, consignee, notify party, customs broker, freight forwarder, shipping line agent) to TO, CC, and BCC fields. For example, a BL release notification at Jebel Ali must automatically TO the consignee, CC the customs broker and freight forwarder, and BCC the operations manager. Rules must support entity-level overrides (KSA operations may require additional CC to ZATCA-registered broker). The system must validate email addresses against suppression lists and hard-bounce records before dispatch. Distribution lists must be maintainable per trade lane and per customer account, with changes audited and requiring operations supervisor approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R012	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Email Retry and Failover SMTP Configuration	The system must support primary and failover SMTP gateway configurations per entity to ensure email delivery continuity. Each entity must have at least one primary SMTP provider and one backup. If the primary SMTP gateway fails (connection timeout, authentication failure, or server error), the system must automatically failover to the backup gateway within 30 seconds. Retry logic must implement exponential backoff starting at 60 seconds for transient failures. The system must log all SMTP interactions including TLS handshake details for security auditing. Failover events must trigger system administrator alerts. The configuration must support OAuth2 authentication for modern SMTP providers and traditional username/password for legacy systems commonly used by port authorities at Hamad Port and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R013	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Personalization Tokens and Recipient Preferences	The system must support personalization tokens beyond standard merge fields, including recipient salutation preferences (Mr., Mrs., Sheikh, Dr.), preferred language (Arabic or English), preferred communication time windows, and preferred summary level (detailed or brief). Recipient preferences must be stored at the contact level within the CRM module and automatically applied during template rendering. Arabic name rendering must follow culturally appropriate conventions including proper use of honorifics and family name formats common in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must respect opt-out preferences per notification category (marketing, operational, financial, regulatory) in compliance with anti-spam regulations across all four jurisdictions. Preference changes must propagate within 15 minutes across all notification channels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R014	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Email Template Performance Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a dedicated analytics dashboard for email template performance metrics across all entities. Metrics must include delivery rate, open rate, click-through rate, bounce rate, unsubscribe rate, and average time to open, segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), notification type (operational, financial, regulatory, marketing), language (Arabic, English), and time period (daily, weekly, monthly, custom range). The dashboard must identify underperforming templates (delivery rate below 90% or open rate below 30% for operational emails) and generate AI-powered recommendations for improvement. All metrics must be exportable in CSV and PDF format. The dashboard must support currency conversion for any financial metrics displayed, using the ERP's configured exchange rates for USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R015	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Offline Email Queue and Sync for Remote Port Operations	The system must support offline email queuing for scenarios where port operations staff at remote locations (e.g., container yards at JNPT or satellite offices at Khalifa Port) experience intermittent connectivity. Emails composed or triggered during offline periods must be stored locally in an encrypted queue with full template rendering preserved. Upon connectivity restoration, the queue must sync with the central email dispatch service, resolving any data conflicts (e.g., shipment status changed during offline period triggers re-rendering with updated data). The offline queue must support a maximum of 500 queued emails per client instance and must display pending email count to the user. Emails older than 24 hours in the offline queue must be flagged for review before dispatch, as shipping data may have become stale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-001-R016	FEAT-033-2-001	MOD-033	Regulatory Compliance Email Archiving	All outbound emails related to customs declarations, BL issuance, delivery orders, and financial transactions must be archived in compliance with regulatory retention requirements: 7 years for Qatar (QFC regulations), 5 years for UAE (Federal Commercial Transactions Law), 7 years for KSA (ZATCA requirements), and 8 years for India (Companies Act 2013). The archive must store the complete email including headers, body, attachments, and delivery metadata. Archived emails must be searchable by BL number, booking reference, container number, vessel name, sender, recipient, date range, and entity. The archival system must support legal hold functionality preventing deletion of emails related to active disputes or audits. Archive storage must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 and must support entity-specific data residency requirements where Qatar data remains in Qatar-hosted infrastructure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R001	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Business API Multi-Entity Configuration	The system must support separate WhatsApp Business API accounts for each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with dedicated phone numbers, business profiles, and verified sender identities. Each entity account must display the correct company name, logo, business description in both Arabic and English, and registered address. API configuration must support both Cloud API (Meta-hosted) and On-Premises API deployment models. The system must manage API rate limits per entity (default 80 messages per second for Cloud API) and implement queuing when limits are approached. Entity-specific webhook endpoints must be configured to receive delivery receipts, read receipts, and incoming message callbacks. Each entity must have independently manageable message template catalogues approved by Meta.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R002	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	Shipping Event-Triggered WhatsApp Notifications	The system must automatically trigger WhatsApp notifications for key shipping milestones based on configurable event mappings. Mandatory events must include: booking confirmation, vessel departure from origin port, vessel arrival at destination port (Hamad Port QAHAD, Jebel Ali AEJEA, Khalifa Port AEKLP, JNPT INJNP), customs clearance initiation, customs clearance completion, BL release, delivery order issuance, container gate-in at terminal, container gate-out, free time expiry warning (48 hours before), and demurrage charge notification. Each event must map to a pre-approved WhatsApp message template with dynamic parameter insertion. The AI engine must determine optimal send timing to avoid message fatigue, grouping related updates for multi-container shipments into consolidated messages rather than per-container alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R003	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	Rich Media Shipping Document Sharing via WhatsApp	The system must support sending shipping documents as WhatsApp media messages including PDF attachments (BL copies, invoices, delivery orders, packing lists), images (container condition photos, seal verification images), and location pins (vessel current position, terminal gate locations). PDF documents must be generated with appropriate headers showing entity branding and must not exceed WhatsApp's 100MB file size limit for documents. For container condition photos captured during gate-in surveys at terminals, the system must automatically compress images to WhatsApp-optimal resolution while maintaining readability of container numbers and seal numbers. Location sharing for vessel tracking must use WhatsApp's native location message format with vessel name, voyage number, ETA, and last reported position coordinates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R004	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	Interactive WhatsApp Message Templates for Shipping Actions	The system must implement interactive WhatsApp message templates using quick reply buttons and call-to-action buttons for common shipping actions. Quick reply templates must include: shipment status inquiry (buttons: Track Container, View Documents, Contact Agent), delivery order actions (buttons: Accept DO, Request Amendment, Escalate Issue), and booking confirmation (buttons: Confirm Booking, Modify Details, Cancel). Call-to-action templates must include: click-to-call for assigned operations agent, click-to-open for web portal shipment tracking page, and click-to-email for document requests. All interactive templates must be submitted for Meta approval in both Arabic and English variants. Button labels must be concise (maximum 20 characters) and must use shipping-standard terminology understood across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R005	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	Bilingual Arabic-English WhatsApp Message Handling	The system must determine message language based on recipient contact preferences stored in the CRM module, defaulting to the entity's primary language (Arabic for Qatar/KSA, English for India, bilingual for UAE). When recipient preference is bilingual, messages must include both Arabic and English content separated by a clear visual divider within the same message. Arabic text must render correctly in RTL format within WhatsApp messages including proper display of Arabic numerals (Eastern Arabic ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩ or Western Arabic 0123456789 based on preference), currency amounts in QAR/SAR/AED with Arabic formatting, and shipping terms transliterated appropriately. Container numbers, BL references, vessel IMO numbers, and other international standard identifiers must always render in LTR regardless of surrounding text direction to prevent reference number corruption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R006	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Chatbot for Shipment Status Inquiries	The system must implement an AI-powered WhatsApp chatbot capable of handling inbound shipment status inquiries in both Arabic and English. The chatbot must authenticate users by matching their WhatsApp number against registered contact records and support reference-based lookups by BL number, booking reference, container number, or delivery order number. Responses must include current shipment status, vessel name and voyage, current port location, ETA at destination, customs clearance status, and any pending actions required from the stakeholder. The chatbot must handle at least 85% of standard inquiries without human escalation, supporting the 95% AI automation target. For queries the chatbot cannot resolve (disputes, rate negotiations, document discrepancies), it must seamlessly transfer the conversation to a human agent with full conversation context preserved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R007	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Message Template Version Management	The system must maintain a centralized catalogue of WhatsApp message templates with full version control and Meta approval status tracking. Each template must have a unique identifier, category (utility, authentication, marketing), language variants (ar, en), parameter definitions with data type validation, and sample content for Meta review. The system must track template approval status (pending, approved, rejected) per language per entity and must prevent sending messages using unapproved templates. Template quality score from Meta must be monitored; if a template's quality rating drops to low, the system must alert administrators and automatically pause usage to prevent account quality degradation. Template changes must follow the email template approval workflow with content and compliance review stages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R008	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Delivery Receipt Processing and Escalation	The system must process WhatsApp delivery receipts (sent, delivered, read) through webhook callbacks and update notification status in real-time. For critical shipping notifications (customs deadline alerts, free time expiry warnings, BL release notifications), if the message is not delivered within 30 minutes, the system must automatically escalate by attempting SMS delivery as a fallback channel. If the message is delivered but not read within 2 hours for time-sensitive notifications, the system must trigger a follow-up reminder message. Read receipt timestamps must be stored for compliance auditing, particularly for delivery order acknowledgments at Hamad Port and customs clearance notifications at JNPT. Delivery analytics must be available per entity showing message delivery rates, read rates, and average response times segmented by notification type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R009	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Contact Opt-In and Consent Management	The system must enforce WhatsApp Business Policy compliance by managing explicit opt-in consent for each recipient before sending any messages. Opt-in must be captured through ERP user interface checkboxes during contact registration, web portal preferences page, or inbound WhatsApp keyword trigger (e.g., sending START to the entity's WhatsApp number). Consent records must store opt-in method, timestamp, IP address (for web opt-ins), and the specific notification categories authorized (operational, financial, marketing). Opt-out must be supported via WhatsApp keyword (STOP) and must be processed within 5 minutes, immediately suppressing all future messages. Consent management must comply with Qatar's NDPD, KSA's PDPL, UAE's Federal Decree-Law No. 45, and India's DPDP Act 2023, with entity-specific consent language templates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R010	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Session and 24-Hour Window Management	The system must correctly manage WhatsApp's 24-hour messaging window policy, distinguishing between user-initiated conversations (customer service window) and business-initiated conversations (using approved templates). When a customer sends an inbound message, the system must open a 24-hour session window during which free-form messages can be sent. The system must track window expiry per conversation and automatically switch to template-based messaging when the window closes. For shipping scenarios requiring urgent free-form communication (e.g., customs hold at Khalifa Port requiring additional documentation), the system must alert the operations agent to the remaining window time and prompt them to send messages before expiry. Conversation session costs must be tracked per entity for billing reconciliation against Meta's per-conversation pricing tiers across the four supported countries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R011	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	Multi-Container Shipment Alert Consolidation	When a booking involves multiple containers (common for FCL shipments of 5-50 containers through Jebel Ali or JNPT), the system must intelligently consolidate WhatsApp alerts rather than sending individual per-container notifications. Consolidation rules must group containers by booking reference, vessel-voyage combination, and event type. A consolidated message must list container numbers in a formatted table showing individual statuses (e.g., 15 of 20 containers cleared customs at QAHAD, 5 pending inspection). The system must send exception-based alerts for containers deviating from the group status (e.g., one container held for inspection while others cleared). Consolidation must be configurable per customer preference with options for fully consolidated, exception-only, or individual alerts. Maximum message length must comply with WhatsApp's 4096 character limit, with overflow handled through pagination or document attachment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R012	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Group Messaging for Trade Lane Stakeholders	The system must support creating and managing WhatsApp groups for recurring trade lane stakeholder communication, such as weekly vessel schedule groups for the Jebel Ali to JNPT corridor or Hamad Port feeder service coordination groups. Group creation must be automated based on trade lane configuration and stakeholder role assignments. The system must post automated updates to relevant groups including vessel schedule changes, port congestion advisories, customs regulation updates, and holiday closure notifications for GCC entities (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, National Days) and India (Republic Day, Diwali, state-specific holidays affecting JNPT operations). Group membership must be synchronized with ERP stakeholder records, automatically adding new partners and removing deactivated contacts. Admin controls must prevent unauthorized manual posting to automated groups.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R013	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Notification Cost Tracking and Budget Management	The system must track WhatsApp messaging costs per entity based on Meta's conversation-based pricing for each country (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Cost tracking must categorize conversations by type (utility, authentication, marketing, service) and calculate costs using the applicable per-conversation rates in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with cross-currency consolidation in USD for group reporting. Monthly budget limits must be configurable per entity with alerts at 80% and 95% thresholds. When budget is exhausted, the system must prioritize critical shipping notifications (customs deadlines, BL releases, safety alerts) and suppress non-critical messages (marketing, routine status updates). Cost reports must be integrated with the ERP financial module for automated expense allocation to the correct cost centre per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R014	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp API Failover and High Availability	The system must implement high availability for WhatsApp Business API connectivity with automatic failover mechanisms. For On-Premises API deployments, the system must support primary and standby node configurations with health check monitoring every 30 seconds. For Cloud API, the system must handle Meta API outages gracefully by queuing messages locally and retrying with exponential backoff (initial 60 seconds, maximum 1 hour). During API unavailability, the system must automatically reroute critical notifications to SMS as a fallback channel. Message queue integrity must be maintained during failover, ensuring no message loss or duplication. The system must log all failover events with timestamps, duration, affected message count, and recovery confirmation. Monthly uptime metrics must be tracked per entity with a target SLA of 99.5% message delivery success rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R015	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Integration Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all WhatsApp communications including outbound messages, inbound responses, delivery receipts, chatbot interactions, and opt-in/opt-out events. Audit records must capture message content, template ID, parameters, sender entity, recipient WhatsApp number (masked for display as +974****1234), timestamp in UTC and local timezone, delivery status chain, and the ERP event that triggered the notification. Audit logs must be retained per entity regulatory requirements (matching the email archival periods: Qatar 7 years, UAE 5 years, KSA 7 years, India 8 years). The audit trail must be searchable by all shipping reference numbers (BL, booking, container, DO) and must support export for regulatory examination. Access to audit logs must be restricted by entity and role with segregation of duties enforced.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-002-R016	FEAT-033-2-002	MOD-033	WhatsApp Notification Priority and Throttling Engine	The system must implement a notification priority engine that classifies WhatsApp messages into priority tiers: P1-Critical (customs hold notices, safety alerts, regulatory deadlines), P2-High (BL releases, DO issuance, free time expiry), P3-Normal (vessel arrival/departure, gate-in/gate-out confirmations), and P4-Low (marketing, periodic reports, service surveys). During high-volume periods such as peak season at Jebel Ali or monsoon-related delays at JNPT, the throttling engine must ensure P1 and P2 messages are delivered within 60 seconds while P3 and P4 messages may be deferred up to 30 minutes. Per-recipient throttling must prevent sending more than 10 messages per hour to any single contact to avoid spam perception and account quality degradation. Throttling rules must be configurable per entity and must log all deferred or suppressed messages for audit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R001	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Multi-Carrier SMS Gateway Configuration per Entity	The system must support configuring multiple SMS gateway providers per entity to ensure delivery reliability and cost optimization across Qatar (Ooredoo, Vodafone Qatar), UAE (Etisalat, du), KSA (STC, Mobily, Zain), and India (Airtel, Jio, BSNL via aggregators like Gupshup, Kaleyra). Each entity must have a primary and secondary SMS gateway with automatic failover when the primary provider reports errors or delivery failures exceeding a configurable threshold (default 10% failure rate over 5 minutes). Gateway configuration must include API endpoint, authentication credentials (API key or OAuth2), sender ID registration details, and supported message types (transactional, promotional). The system must support HTTP/HTTPS REST API and SMPP protocol for legacy gateway integration commonly used by port authority communication systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R002	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	AI-Powered Document Classification Engine	The system must implement an AI-powered classification engine achieving minimum 95% accuracy that automatically categorizes uploaded documents into predefined shipping document types including Bill of Lading (Master/House), Sea Waybill, Delivery Order, Shipping Instruction, Certificate of Origin, Phytosanitary Certificate, Dangerous Goods Declaration (IMO DGD), Commercial Invoice, Packing List, Insurance Certificate, Letter of Credit, Customs Declaration (Qatar Single Window, UAE Mirsal2, KSA FASAH, India ICEGATE), Container Inspection Report, Vessel Stowage Plan, and Terminal Handling Receipts. Classification must work for documents in both English and Arabic languages, including mixed-language documents common in GCC trade corridors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R002	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Registered Sender ID Management per Jurisdiction	The system must manage registered sender IDs (alphanumeric and numeric) per jurisdiction in compliance with local telecom regulations. For Qatar (TRA-regulated), sender IDs must be pre-registered through the Communications Regulatory Authority. For UAE (TRA/TDRA), sender IDs require approval from the Telecommunications and Digital Government Regulatory Authority. For KSA, sender IDs must be registered through the CST (Communications, Space & Technology Commission). For India, sender IDs must comply with TRAI DLT (Distributed Ledger Technology) platform registration with entity ID, header, and template registration. Each entity must have separate sender IDs for transactional messages (e.g., QTRSHP for Qatar Shipping) and promotional messages where permitted. The system must prevent sending from unregistered sender IDs and must track sender ID renewal dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R003	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Arabic Unicode SMS Encoding and Character Management	The system must handle Arabic Unicode character encoding correctly, understanding that standard GSM-7 encoding supports 160 characters per SMS segment while Unicode (UCS-2) encoding required for Arabic text supports only 70 characters per segment. The system must intelligently manage message length, providing character count warnings during template creation and automatically splitting long messages into concatenated multi-part SMS with proper UDH (User Data Header) handling. Port names, vessel names, and shipping references containing Arabic text (e.g., ميناء حمد for Hamad Port, ميناء جبل علي for Jebel Ali) must render correctly. Mixed Arabic-English messages must handle bidirectional text within SMS character limits. The system must estimate SMS segment count and cost before dispatch, considering that Arabic messages consume segments at approximately 2.3x the rate of English-only messages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R004	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Template Registration for Indian DLT Compliance	For India operations targeting JNPT stakeholders, the system must integrate with the TRAI DLT platform for mandatory SMS template registration. Every SMS content template must be pre-registered with the DLT platform through an authorized telecom operator, with a unique template ID assigned. The system must store DLT entity ID, template ID, header (sender ID), and consent template ID for each message template. SMS dispatch to Indian numbers must include the DLT template ID in the API call to the gateway. Template scrubbing by telecom operators must be accounted for; the system must ensure message content exactly matches the registered template with only approved variable parameters substituted. Template registration status (pending, approved, rejected) must be tracked, and rejected templates must trigger alerts to the notification administrator for remediation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R005	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Critical Shipping Alert SMS with Escalation Chains	The system must define critical shipping alert categories that mandate SMS delivery regardless of other channel availability. Critical alerts include: customs examination hold notices (requiring immediate document submission at Hamad Port or JNPT), vessel schedule changes affecting cargo cutoff times, hazardous cargo (DG container) safety notifications, port emergency alerts (weather closures at Jebel Ali during shamal wind events, monsoon disruptions at JNPT), and payment overdue notices affecting cargo release. Each critical alert must have a configured escalation chain: primary contact SMS, wait 15 minutes, if no acknowledgment then escalate to secondary contact, wait 15 minutes, then escalate to operations manager. Acknowledgment must be supported via SMS reply keywords (OK, ACK, CONFIRM) parsed by the system. Unacknowledged critical alerts after full escalation must create a high-priority task in the operations dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R006	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Delivery Report Processing and Analytics	The system must process delivery reports (DLRs) from SMS gateways tracking message status through submitted, delivered, failed, rejected, and expired states. DLR processing must handle asynchronous callbacks from gateway providers with correlation to original message IDs. Failed deliveries must be categorized by error code (invalid number, subscriber absent, network error, content filtering, DND/DNC registered) with appropriate retry or suppression logic. Analytics must track delivery success rates per gateway provider, per entity, per country, and per notification type. The system must detect degraded delivery rates (below 85% for transactional messages) and trigger automatic gateway switching to the failover provider. Monthly SMS analytics reports must include message volumes, segment counts, delivery rates, and cost summaries per entity in local currency with USD consolidated view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R007	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Do-Not-Disturb and Quiet Hours SMS Scheduling	The system must respect Do-Not-Disturb (DND) and quiet hours regulations across all four jurisdictions. For India, the system must check TRAI NCPR (National Customer Preference Register) DND status before sending promotional SMS and must not send promotional messages between 9:00 PM and 9:00 AM IST. For GCC entities, non-critical messages must not be sent during Friday prayer times (approximately 12:00-1:30 PM local time, configurable per entity). The system must queue non-critical messages falling within quiet hours and dispatch them at the next available window. Critical shipping alerts (customs deadlines, safety notifications) must override quiet hours restrictions with a mandatory audit log entry justifying the override. The system must handle timezone differences correctly when scheduling, particularly for cross-border notifications between GCC (UTC+3/+4) and India (UTC+5:30) operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R008	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Cost Optimization and Routing Intelligence	The system must implement intelligent SMS routing to optimize delivery cost across multiple gateway providers. Route selection must consider per-message cost by destination country and carrier network, delivery success rate history per route, message priority level, and current gateway health status. For high-volume scenarios such as vessel schedule change notifications affecting hundreds of bookings, the system must calculate estimated cost per gateway and select the most cost-effective route while maintaining delivery SLA (95% delivery within 60 seconds for critical messages). The system must support long-code and short-code routing where applicable. Cost tracking must record per-message cost in the entity's currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with real-time budget consumption visibility. Monthly cost comparison reports across gateways must be generated to support periodic provider renegotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R009	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Two-Way SMS for Shipment Status Queries	The system must support two-way SMS enabling stakeholders to query shipment status by sending keyword-based SMS messages to the entity's registered number or short code. Supported commands must include: STATUS followed by container number (e.g., STATUS TRIU1234567), ETA followed by BL number (e.g., ETA QABL2024001234), DO followed by delivery order number for release status, and HELP for available commands list. The system must parse inbound messages, authenticate the sender by matching the originating number against registered contacts, query the relevant ERP module, and respond with a formatted SMS containing current status, location, and next expected milestone. Response formatting must be concise to minimize SMS segments while remaining informative. Arabic keyword variants must be supported (حالة for STATUS, وصول for ETA). The system must handle malformed queries gracefully with helpful error messages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R010	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Number Validation and International Format Handling	The system must validate all recipient phone numbers against international format standards before SMS dispatch. Validation must verify country code presence and correctness (Qatar +974, UAE +971, KSA +966, India +91), number length per country (Qatar 8 digits, UAE 9 digits, KSA 9 digits, India 10 digits), and mobile number prefix validity per carrier. Numbers stored without country codes must be automatically prefixed based on the contact's associated entity. The system must maintain a number portability awareness where available (particularly for India where MNP is common) to ensure correct carrier routing. Invalid numbers must be flagged and excluded from dispatch with notifications to the operations team for data cleanup. Duplicate number detection must prevent sending the same message multiple times to a contact who has multiple roles (e.g., both shipper representative and customs broker contact).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R011	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	Bulk SMS Campaign Management for Port Advisories	The system must support bulk SMS campaigns for port-wide advisories affecting multiple stakeholders simultaneously. Use cases include: Jebel Ali terminal congestion advisories affecting all active bookings, Hamad Port Ramadan operating hour changes, JNPT customs system maintenance windows, and KSA National Day holiday schedule notifications for all active shipments at Saudi ports. Bulk campaigns must support recipient list generation from ERP data filters (all active bookings at port X, all consignees with pending DOs, all customs brokers registered for entity Y). Campaign scheduling must support immediate dispatch, future-dated dispatch, and recurring schedules. The system must provide campaign progress tracking showing total recipients, messages sent, delivered, failed, and pending. Campaign templates must be pre-approved through the template approval workflow and must include mandatory opt-out instructions per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R012	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Concatenation and Long Message Handling	The system must handle long SMS messages requiring concatenation across multiple segments with proper UDH (User Data Header) management. For English messages exceeding 160 characters (GSM-7) or Arabic messages exceeding 70 characters (UCS-2), the system must automatically split into concatenated segments with maximum 4 segments per logical message (640 characters English, 280 characters Arabic). If message content exceeds the maximum segment limit, the system must truncate with a URL link to the full notification in the web portal. Segment count must be pre-calculated during template design and displayed to template authors. The system must track cost per logical message (sum of all segments) rather than per segment for accurate cost reporting. Concatenated messages must be delivered in correct order; the system must verify gateway support for concatenation before dispatch and fall back to single-segment truncated messages for gateways that do not support UDH.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R013	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Consent and Opt-Out Management	The system must manage SMS consent records per recipient per entity in compliance with telecom regulations across all four jurisdictions. Consent collection must capture opt-in source (web form, in-person at port office, phone call recording reference, contractual agreement), timestamp, consent scope (transactional only, transactional plus promotional), and consenting entity. Opt-out must be supported via SMS keyword (STOP, UNSUB, إلغاء for Arabic) processed within 5 minutes of receipt. The system must maintain a global suppression list synchronized across all SMS gateways. For India, consent must be mapped to TRAI DLT consent categories (explicit, implicit via existing business relationship). Re-consent campaigns must be triggerable for contacts approaching consent expiry. The system must generate consent compliance reports per entity showing opt-in rates, opt-out rates, and consent coverage percentage for auditing by telecom regulators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R014	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Fallback from Failed Email and WhatsApp Channels	The system must serve as a fallback notification channel when primary channels (email and WhatsApp) fail to deliver critical shipping notifications. Fallback rules must be configurable per notification type and priority level. When an email bounces and WhatsApp message is undelivered after the configured timeout (default 30 minutes for critical alerts), the system must automatically trigger SMS delivery with a condensed version of the original notification content. The SMS fallback message must include the essential information (reference number, action required, deadline if applicable) and a shortened URL to view full details in the web portal. Fallback cascading must be logged in the unified notification audit trail showing the original channel, failure reason, fallback trigger time, and SMS delivery status. The system must prevent notification storms by implementing a cooldown period (minimum 5 minutes) between fallback attempts to the same recipient.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R015	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Gateway Health Monitoring and Alerting	The system must continuously monitor SMS gateway health and performance with automated alerting for degraded service. Health checks must run every 60 seconds for each configured gateway, testing API connectivity, authentication validity, and sending capability via test message to a monitoring number. Monitored metrics must include: API response time (alert if average exceeds 5 seconds over 5-minute window), delivery success rate (alert if drops below 90% over 15-minute window), queue depth (alert if exceeds 1000 pending messages), and credit/balance status (alert at 20% remaining for prepaid gateways). Gateway health status must be displayed on the ERP system administration dashboard with green/yellow/red indicators per entity per provider. Automated failover to secondary gateway must trigger when primary health score drops below threshold, with recovery-based automatic failback when primary health restores. All health check results and failover events must be logged for SLA compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R016	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	OTP and Verification SMS for Shipping Document Access	The system must support OTP (One-Time Password) delivery via SMS for secure access to sensitive shipping documents and portal functions. OTP use cases include: BL surrender verification, delivery order release authorization, high-value freight invoice access (amounts exceeding configurable thresholds per entity), and first-time portal login verification. OTP messages must be delivered within 10 seconds of generation with a configurable expiry period (default 5 minutes). OTP format must be 6-digit numeric codes with collision avoidance ensuring no duplicate active OTPs per recipient. The system must enforce rate limiting (maximum 5 OTP requests per number per hour) to prevent abuse. OTP delivery must use the highest priority queue and must not be subject to quiet hours restrictions. Failed OTP verification attempts (3 consecutive failures) must trigger account lockout and security team notification. OTP SMS content must include the entity name and purpose to prevent phishing confusion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-003-R017	FEAT-033-2-003	MOD-033	SMS Notification Scheduling with Timezone Intelligence	The system must implement timezone-aware SMS scheduling that considers the recipient's local timezone for optimal delivery timing. For shipping notifications involving cross-timezone stakeholders (e.g., shipper in India IST UTC+5:30, consignee in Qatar AST UTC+3, customs broker in UAE GST UTC+4), the system must schedule each recipient's message according to their local business hours (default 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM). The scheduler must handle daylight saving time transitions for any stakeholders in DST-observing regions. Scheduled messages must be cancellable up to 60 seconds before dispatch. Recurring scheduled notifications (e.g., daily shipment status summaries) must maintain consistent delivery times despite timezone offset changes. The system must display all scheduled times in both UTC and recipient local time in the notification management interface, with Hijri date display for GCC recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R001	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Real-Time Notification Delivery via WebSocket	The system must deliver in-app notifications in real-time using WebSocket connections to all active user sessions. WebSocket connections must be established upon user login and maintained with heartbeat pings every 30 seconds. Notification delivery latency from event trigger to user screen display must not exceed 3 seconds under normal load conditions. The system must support simultaneous connections for users logged into multiple devices (desktop browser, mobile app, tablet) with notification state synchronized across all sessions. When WebSocket connection is interrupted (network instability common at port locations like JNPT container yard), the system must implement automatic reconnection with exponential backoff and deliver any missed notifications upon reconnection. The notification payload must include notification ID, type, priority, title, summary, timestamp in UTC, and deep-link URL to the relevant ERP module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R002	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Centre UI with Arabic RTL Layout	The notification centre must provide a responsive UI panel accessible from a persistent bell icon in the ERP application header bar showing unread notification count as a badge. The panel must support both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with correct text alignment, icon positioning, and scrolling direction based on the user's selected language preference. Notification items must display: priority indicator (color-coded icon), notification title, summary text (truncated to 2 lines with expand option), relative timestamp (e.g., 5 minutes ago, قبل 5 دقائق), source entity name, and action buttons where applicable. The panel must support infinite scrolling with lazy loading of older notifications in batches of 20. A clear visual separator must distinguish unread from read notifications. The notification bell must animate briefly when new notifications arrive to draw user attention without being disruptive during focused operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R003	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Role-Based Notification Filtering and Routing	The system must route in-app notifications based on user roles and responsibilities within the ERP. Defined roles must include: Operations Manager (receives all operational alerts for assigned trade lanes), Customs Officer (receives customs clearance status updates and regulatory alerts), Finance Controller (receives payment notifications, demurrage charges, invoice approvals), Documentation Specialist (receives BL amendments, DO releases, document verification requests), Port Coordinator (receives vessel schedule changes, berth assignments, gate activity for assigned terminals at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT), and System Administrator (receives system health alerts and configuration changes). Users with multiple roles must receive the union of all role-based notifications without duplicates. Entity-level filtering must ensure users only see notifications for entities they have access to per the ERP's multi-entity security model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R004	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Categorization and Priority Management	The system must categorize notifications into distinct types with configurable priority levels. Categories must include: Workflow Actions (pending approvals, task assignments, deadline reminders), Shipment Updates (vessel movements, customs status changes, container milestones), Financial Alerts (payment due notices, demurrage charges, credit limit warnings), Document Notifications (BL releases, DO issuance, amendment requests), System Alerts (maintenance windows, integration errors, performance warnings), and Regulatory Updates (customs regulation changes, port authority circulars). Each category must have a default priority (Critical, High, Normal, Low) overridable by administrators. Critical notifications for events like customs holds at Hamad Port or hazardous cargo alerts at Jebel Ali must bypass user notification preferences and always display prominently. The notification centre must allow filtering by category, priority, entity, and date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R005	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Actionable Notifications with Inline Decision Buttons	Workflow-triggered notifications must support inline action buttons enabling users to take immediate action without navigating away from their current screen. Supported actions must include: Approve/Reject for workflow approval notifications (e.g., credit note approval, BL amendment approval, rate quotation approval), Acknowledge for informational alerts requiring confirmation (e.g., vessel delay acknowledged, customs hold noted), Assign/Delegate for task notifications that can be reassigned (e.g., document verification task delegation during staff absence), and View Details for navigating to the full context in the relevant ERP module. Action buttons must enforce the same authorization checks as the underlying ERP function. Completed actions must update the notification status to actioned with a timestamp and must trigger downstream workflow progression. Multi-step approvals must show current stage progress (e.g., Step 2 of 3: Finance Approval pending).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R006	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Preferences and Channel Configuration per User	Each user must be able to configure notification preferences controlling which notifications they receive through which channels (in-app, email, WhatsApp, SMS). The preferences interface must present a matrix of notification categories versus channels with toggleable switches. Users must be able to set preferences at the category level (e.g., all financial alerts via email and in-app) and override at the individual notification type level (e.g., demurrage charge alerts also via SMS). A minimum delivery channel must be enforced for critical notifications, ensuring they always appear in-app regardless of user preferences. Preferences must be entity-specific, allowing a user operating across Qatar and UAE entities to configure different preferences per entity. Default preference profiles must be definable per role by system administrators. Preference changes must take effect within 60 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R007	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification History and Search with Shipping Context	The notification centre must maintain a searchable history of all notifications received by the user for the past 12 months, with archived notifications accessible for the full regulatory retention period per entity. Search must support free-text search across notification title and body, filtering by category, priority, date range, entity, read/unread status, and actioned/pending status. Shipping-context search must allow lookup by BL number, booking reference, container number, vessel name, voyage number, and delivery order number, instantly showing all notifications related to a specific shipment. Search results must be sortable by date (newest/oldest first), priority, and category. The history view must support bulk operations: mark all as read, bulk delete (soft delete with audit trail), and export notification history to CSV with columns for timestamp, category, title, body, status, entity, and related shipping references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R008	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Push Notifications for Mobile ERP Application	The system must deliver push notifications to the ERP mobile application (iOS and Android) using Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) for Android and Apple Push Notification Service (APNs) for iOS. Push notification payloads must include title, body, notification type, priority, deep-link URI to the relevant mobile app screen, and entity identifier. Users must be able to register multiple devices with independent push notification settings. Rich push notifications must be supported, showing container tracking status updates with progress indicators and vessel position map thumbnails where available. Push notifications must respect device-level Do Not Disturb settings while ensuring critical shipping alerts (customs deadlines, safety alerts) use iOS critical alert entitlement and Android high-priority channel to bypass DND. Device token management must handle token refresh and expired token cleanup automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R009	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Grouping and Threading for Shipments	The notification centre must intelligently group related notifications into threads based on shipping context to prevent information overload. Grouping criteria must include: same BL number (all BL lifecycle notifications in one thread), same booking reference (from booking creation through cargo delivery), same vessel-voyage (all cargo on the same sailing grouped), and same workflow instance (all approval chain notifications threaded). Each group must show the latest notification prominently with an expandable view of the full thread history. Thread badges must show total and unread counts. When a shipment progresses through multiple milestones rapidly (common during cargo operations at Jebel Ali where gate-in, customs scan, and yard placement happen within minutes), the system must collapse sequential status updates into a timeline view within the thread rather than showing individual notification cards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R010	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	AI-Prioritized Notification Feed	The notification centre must leverage AI to intelligently prioritize and surface the most relevant notifications for each user based on their role, current active tasks, and historical interaction patterns. The AI engine must learn from user behavior patterns such as which notifications are opened immediately versus ignored, which actions are taken most frequently, and which shipping routes or customers the user primarily handles. The AI must identify time-sensitive notifications requiring immediate attention, such as free time expiring within 4 hours at Hamad Port or customs documentation deadline approaching at JNPT, and promote them to the top of the feed with visual urgency indicators. AI confidence scores for prioritization must be logged for model improvement. Users must retain the ability to switch between AI-prioritized view and chronological view, and must be able to provide explicit feedback (pin, snooze, mark not relevant) to improve future prioritization accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R011	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Snooze and Reminder Functionality for Pending Actions	Users must be able to snooze notifications for later follow-up with configurable reminder times. Snooze options must include: 30 minutes, 1 hour, 4 hours, tomorrow morning (next business day 9:00 AM in user's timezone), and custom date/time. Snoozed notifications must be removed from the main notification feed and stored in a Snoozed section, reappearing at the scheduled reminder time as new notifications with a snoozed indicator. The system must prevent snoozing beyond the action deadline for time-sensitive notifications (e.g., cannot snooze a customs document submission deadline beyond the actual deadline). Recurring reminders must be supported for ongoing tasks such as pending container detention charges accruing daily. The system must track snooze patterns per user to identify notification types that are frequently snoozed, feeding this data back to the AI prioritization engine and alerting managers if critical workflow items are being consistently deferred.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R012	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Sound and Visual Alert Customization	The system must support customizable notification sounds and visual alerts per notification category and priority level. Default sound profiles must differentiate between critical alerts (distinct urgent tone), workflow approvals (moderate attention tone), informational updates (subtle notification tone), and system alerts (technical alert tone). Users must be able to customize sounds per category or mute specific categories while maintaining visual indicators. Visual alert customization must include desktop browser notification pop-ups (using the Web Notification API with user permission), notification badge colors per priority (red for critical, orange for high, blue for normal, grey for low), and optional screen flash for critical alerts during focused operations at port terminals. Sound settings must be independent per device (desktop may have sounds enabled while mobile relies on device push notification settings). All customization preferences must sync across sessions via the user profile stored in the ERP backend.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R013	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Multi-Entity Notification Aggregation Dashboard	For users with cross-entity access (e.g., regional operations managers overseeing Qatar and UAE, or group finance controllers across all four entities), the notification centre must provide an aggregated view with clear entity segregation. The dashboard must display notification counts per entity with visual cards showing entity name, logo, and colour coding (configurable per entity). Users must be able to toggle between aggregated view (all entities interleaved chronologically) and entity-filtered view (single entity at a time). Entity-specific notification formatting must apply automatically, including currency display (QAR for Qatar notifications, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), date format preferences, and language default per entity. Cross-entity notifications (e.g., a shipment transiting from Jebel Ali UAE to Hamad Port Qatar involving both entities) must appear in both entity views with contextual information relevant to each entity's perspective.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R014	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification API for Third-Party and Custom Integrations	The system must expose a REST API for external systems and custom integrations to publish notifications into the in-app notification centre. The API must support authenticated POST requests with notification payload including target user IDs or role-based targeting, notification category, priority, title (Arabic and English), body (Arabic and English), deep-link URL, and optional action button definitions. API authentication must use OAuth2 bearer tokens with scoped permissions per integration. Rate limiting must be enforced per API client (default 100 notifications per minute) to prevent notification flooding. The API must validate notification payloads against schema requirements and return appropriate error codes for malformed requests. Use cases include: port community systems (PCS) at Hamad Port publishing customs status updates, shipping line APIs publishing vessel tracking updates, and bank payment gateways publishing payment confirmation notifications. API usage analytics must be tracked per integration client.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R015	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Offline Notification Caching and Sync for Mobile Users	The mobile application must support offline notification caching for users operating in areas with poor connectivity, such as inside container yards at JNPT, warehouse areas at Khalifa Port, or during vessel operations at Hamad Port. The system must cache the most recent 200 notifications locally on the device with full content, action status, and thread context. Upon connectivity restoration, the mobile app must synchronize bidirectionally: downloading new notifications from the server and uploading any offline actions taken by the user (mark as read, snooze, inline approvals queued offline). Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where the same notification was actioned both offline and by another user or system process during the offline period, with the most recent action taking precedence and the user notified of the conflict. Offline mode must display a clear visual indicator showing the user they are viewing cached data with the last sync timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R016	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Analytics and User Engagement Metrics	The system must provide administrative analytics for notification effectiveness across the platform. Metrics must include: notification volume per category per entity per time period, average time from notification delivery to user read, average time from notification delivery to user action (for actionable notifications), notification dismissal rates per category, snooze frequency and patterns, and channel preference distribution across the user base. Analytics must identify notification fatigue indicators such as increasing dismissal rates or decreasing open rates for specific categories. The system must provide AI-generated recommendations for notification optimization, such as consolidating high-frequency low-engagement notifications or adjusting delivery timing. Entity-level comparison dashboards must show how notification engagement varies across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations, enabling regional managers to identify and address communication effectiveness gaps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R017	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Notification Retention and Archival Policy Management	The system must implement configurable notification retention policies per entity aligned with regulatory requirements. Active notifications must be retained in the primary database for 12 months with full search capability. Beyond 12 months, notifications must be archived to cold storage with on-demand retrieval capability (retrieval within 5 minutes). Archival retention periods must be configurable per entity: 7 years for Qatar, 5 years for UAE, 7 years for KSA, and 8 years for India per regulatory requirements. Notifications linked to active legal disputes, customs audits, or regulatory investigations must support legal hold preventing archival or deletion regardless of retention policy. Automated purge processes must run outside business hours, processing entity-specific retention rules. Purge operations must generate audit logs recording notification count deleted, date range, entity, and authorizing policy. Archived notifications must be retrievable by shipping reference numbers for regulatory examination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-033-2-004-R018	FEAT-033-2-004	MOD-033	Unified Notification Audit Trail Across All Channels	The system must maintain a unified audit trail correlating notifications across all delivery channels (in-app, email, WhatsApp, SMS) for each shipping event. The audit trail must show the complete notification lifecycle: event trigger source (ERP module, workflow engine, external API), notification creation timestamp, channel dispatch decisions (which channels were selected and why, based on user preferences and channel availability), per-channel delivery status with timestamps, user interaction events (read, clicked, actioned, snoozed, dismissed), and any escalation or fallback channel activations. The audit trail must be accessible from the notification centre detail view and from the related shipping document view (e.g., viewing a BL should show all notifications sent about that BL across all channels). Audit data must be exportable for compliance reporting per entity, supporting regulatory examinations at Qatar Financial Centre, JAFZA, ZATCA, and Indian customs authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R001	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Centralized Multi-Entity Document Storage	The system must provide a centralized document repository that supports multi-entity storage isolation across Qatar (Hamad Port, QAHAM), UAE (Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA), KSA, and India (JNPT INNSA) entities. Each entity must have its own logical partition with configurable access controls while allowing cross-entity document sharing when authorized. Storage must support documents up to 500MB per file, with automatic replication across at least two geographic zones for disaster recovery. The repository must maintain entity-specific retention policies complying with Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32, KSA ZATCA regulations, and Indian Customs Act 1962 requirements for document preservation periods ranging from 5 to 10 years depending on jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R003	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Metadata Extraction and Auto-Tagging	The system must automatically extract and tag metadata from uploaded documents using OCR and NLP capabilities. Extracted metadata must include vessel name and IMO number, voyage number, Bill of Lading number, container numbers (ISO 6346 format validation), port of loading and discharge (UN/LOCODE format), shipper and consignee details, INCOTERMS 2020 references (FOB, CIF, CFR, DDP, etc.), HS tariff codes, cargo weight and measurements, and document dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The extraction engine must handle Arabic text with RTL orientation, support mixed Arabic-English documents, and achieve a minimum 90% extraction accuracy for structured fields. Failed extractions must be queued for manual review with AI-suggested corrections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R004	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Hierarchical Folder Structure with Voyage Linking	The system must support a configurable hierarchical folder structure organized by entity, year (Gregorian and Hijri), vessel/voyage, Bill of Lading number, and container number. Each document must be linkable to multiple business objects including voyage records, booking references, container tracking entries, customs declarations, and financial transactions. The folder structure must support both automatic organization based on extracted metadata and manual reclassification by authorized users. When a voyage is created in the operations module, the system must automatically generate the corresponding folder structure with standard sub-folders for customs documents, commercial documents, transport documents, and compliance certificates specific to the trade lane (e.g., Qatar-India corridor requiring additional food safety documentation).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R005	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Format Support and Conversion	The repository must accept and render documents in all standard formats including PDF, PDF/A (for archival), TIFF, JPEG, PNG, Microsoft Office formats (DOCX, XLSX, PPTX), OpenDocument formats, XML (for EDIFACT/BAPLIE/IFTMIN messages), JSON, plain text, and EML/MSG for email attachments. The system must provide automatic conversion to PDF/A format for long-term archival storage while retaining the original file. For EDI messages commonly used in container shipping such as COPARN, BAPLIE, IFTMIN, IFTMBC, and CUSCAR, the system must render human-readable previews alongside the raw message format. Arabic documents must maintain proper RTL formatting, ligatures, and diacritical marks throughout conversion processes without corruption or character loss.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R006	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Full-Text Search with Bilingual Support	The system must provide full-text search across all documents in the repository with support for both English and Arabic languages including transliterated search terms. Search must cover document content (via OCR-indexed text), metadata fields, tags, annotations, and comments. Advanced search filters must include document type, entity, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), vessel/voyage, port (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), container number, B/L number, customer name, and classification status. Search results must be ranked by relevance with configurable weighting. The search engine must support Boolean operators, wildcard matching, fuzzy matching for Arabic names with variant spellings, and proximity search. Search response time must not exceed 3 seconds for repositories containing up to 10 million documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R007	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Role-Based Access Control for Documents	The system must enforce granular role-based access control at entity, folder, document type, and individual document levels. Permissions must include view, download, upload, edit metadata, delete, share, print, and administer with the ability to create custom permission sets. Entity-level isolation must ensure that Qatar entity users cannot access UAE or KSA entity documents unless explicitly granted cross-entity permissions. Sensitive documents such as Letters of Credit, pricing agreements, and customs duty assessments must support additional classification levels (Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). The system must maintain a complete access audit trail recording who accessed which document, when, from which IP address, and what action was performed, retained for a minimum of 7 years per GCC regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R008	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Bulk Upload and Batch Processing	The system must support bulk document upload via drag-and-drop interface, ZIP archive extraction, email ingestion (monitoring designated mailboxes), and FTP/SFTP folder watching for automated intake from shipping lines, port authorities, and customs brokers. Batch processing must handle a minimum of 500 documents simultaneously with progress tracking, error reporting, and partial failure recovery. Each document in a batch must be independently classified, tagged, and filed without blocking the processing of other documents in the same batch. The bulk upload interface must support Arabic file names with proper encoding, handle duplicate detection based on content hash and metadata matching, and provide a review queue for documents that fail automatic classification with confidence scores below the configurable threshold (default 85%).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R009	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Linking to Shipping Operations	The system must enable bi-directional linking between documents and operational records across all ERP modules. A Bill of Lading document must automatically link to the corresponding booking, vessel schedule, container tracking record, customs declaration, freight invoice, and delivery order. When users view any operational record (e.g., a container at JNPT port), all associated documents must be accessible in a contextual document panel. The linking engine must support automatic linking based on extracted reference numbers (B/L number, booking reference, container number ISO 6346, customs declaration number) and manual linking by authorized users. Orphaned documents without any operational links must be flagged for review within 48 hours of upload, and the system must suggest potential links using AI matching of document content against active shipment records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R010	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Offline Document Access and Synchronization	The system must provide offline document access capabilities for users operating in areas with limited connectivity, such as vessel-side operations at Hamad Port or container yard inspections at Jebel Ali. Users must be able to mark specific document sets (e.g., all documents for a particular voyage or B/L) for offline availability, with automatic download and local encrypted storage. When connectivity is restored, any documents created or modified offline must synchronize with the central repository using conflict resolution rules: server version wins for metadata conflicts, and both versions are retained with manual merge required for content conflicts. Offline storage must be AES-256 encrypted with automatic purge after a configurable period (default 30 days) or upon user session termination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R011	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Retention and Archival Policies	The system must enforce configurable document retention policies per entity, document type, and regulatory jurisdiction. Qatar entity documents must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority retention requirements of minimum 5 years for commercial documents and 10 years for customs records. UAE entity documents must follow DMCC and Abu Dhabi Global Market retention schedules. KSA documents must comply with ZATCA archival requirements. India entity documents must follow CBIC (Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs) retention mandates of 5 years from date of customs clearance. Upon retention period expiry, documents must be moved to cold storage with reduced access, and final deletion must require dual authorization from entity administrator and compliance officer. The system must generate monthly retention compliance reports per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R012	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Preview and Annotation	The system must provide in-browser document preview for all supported formats without requiring external software or plugins. Preview must support zooming, rotation, multi-page navigation, and side-by-side comparison of two documents (e.g., comparing original B/L with amended version). Users must be able to add annotations including text comments, highlights, stamps (Approved, Rejected, Under Review, Original, Copy), freehand drawings, and rectangular region selections on document previews. Annotations must be stored as a separate layer preserving the original document integrity. Each annotation must record the author, timestamp (both Gregorian and Hijri), entity context, and must support replies creating threaded discussions on specific document sections. Arabic text annotations must render correctly in RTL with proper font support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R013	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Expiry Tracking and Alerts	The system must track expiry dates for time-sensitive shipping documents including vessel classification certificates, P&I club insurance certificates, crew certifications, dangerous goods approvals, trade licenses, customs broker authorizations, phytosanitary certificates, fumigation certificates, and Letters of Credit. The system must generate automated alerts at configurable intervals before expiry (default: 90, 60, 30, 15, and 7 days) via email, in-app notification, and SMS to designated recipients per entity. Expired documents must be visually flagged with a red indicator and must trigger business rule validations preventing their use in new transactions (e.g., an expired Certificate of Origin cannot be attached to a new customs declaration at QAHAM or INNSA ports). The alert system must account for Hijri calendar dates and GCC public holidays when calculating business-day-based deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R014	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Import from External Systems	The system must integrate with external document sources commonly used in container shipping including carrier EDI portals (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd via INTTRA/CargoSmart), port community systems (Qatar Single Window Dhareeba, UAE Mirsal2, KSA FASAH, India PCS1x/ICEGATE), banking SWIFT networks for trade finance documents, and customs authorities for electronic clearance certificates. Import must be automated via scheduled polling or webhook-based push notifications. Each imported document must be logged with source system identifier, import timestamp, and data integrity hash (SHA-256). Failed imports must retry with exponential backoff (max 3 retries) and generate alerts to the integration monitoring dashboard. The system must handle character encoding conversions between systems ensuring Arabic text integrity across all integration points.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R015	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Storage Quota Management and Cost Allocation	The system must enforce configurable storage quotas per entity with multi-currency cost allocation for storage consumption. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must have independent storage quotas with usage tracking and threshold alerts at 70%, 85%, and 95% utilization. Storage costs must be allocated in the entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. The system must provide storage analytics dashboards showing consumption by entity, document type, department, and time period. Automatic tiered storage must move documents older than configurable thresholds (default 2 years) from hot storage to cold storage to reduce costs. Compressed and deduplicated storage metrics must be reported alongside raw file sizes for accurate capacity planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-001-R016	FEAT-034-1-001	MOD-034	Document Watermarking and Copy Protection	The system must apply configurable watermarks to documents when viewed, downloaded, or printed. Watermarks must include the viewing user's name, entity, timestamp, and a unique access tracking code. For sensitive documents such as original Bills of Lading, Letters of Credit, and bank guarantees, the system must enforce copy-controlled viewing that prevents screenshots via dynamic watermarking patterns and restricts printing to authorized users only. Downloaded copies must embed invisible digital watermarks traceable to the requesting user and session for leak investigation purposes. Watermark text must support both English and Arabic rendering with proper bidirectional text handling. The watermarking configuration must be document-type-specific with entity-level overrides, allowing Qatar entity to enforce stricter watermarking policies than other entities if required by local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R001	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Automatic Version Numbering and Tracking	The system must automatically assign sequential version numbers to documents following a major.minor versioning scheme (e.g., v1.0, v1.1, v2.0). Major version increments must be triggered by significant content changes such as amendment to Bill of Lading cargo details, correction of container numbers, or shipper/consignee changes. Minor version increments must apply to metadata updates, annotation additions, or formatting changes. Each version must record the author, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, entity context, IP address, reason for change (mandatory free-text field with minimum 10 characters), and the change category (Amendment, Correction, Update, Supersede). The system must retain all previous versions indefinitely for documents related to customs declarations at QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, and INNSA ports as required by respective customs authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R004	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Structured data extraction from commercial invoices	The system must extract from commercial invoices: invoice number, date, seller details, buyer details, item descriptions, HS codes (Harmonized System), quantities, unit prices, total amounts, currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), INCOTERMS, payment terms, and bank details. The extraction engine must handle multi-currency invoices where line items may be quoted in different currencies. For invoices in Arabic, the system must correctly extract Arabic company names, addresses, and descriptions while mapping them to the corresponding English transliterations stored in the ERP master data. The system must flag discrepancies between extracted invoice values and the corresponding purchase order or booking record values exceeding a configurable tolerance threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R002	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Check-In and Check-Out Workflow	The system must implement a check-in/check-out mechanism to prevent concurrent editing conflicts on editable documents such as Shipping Instructions, Booking Confirmations, and Draft Bills of Lading. When a user checks out a document, it must be locked for editing by other users with a visible lock indicator showing the lock holder's name, entity, and checkout timestamp. Automatic lock expiry must release documents after a configurable timeout (default 4 hours) with a 30-minute warning notification to the lock holder. Forced unlock must be available to entity administrators with mandatory reason documentation. The check-out status must be visible across all entities if the document is shared cross-entity, and the system must queue edit requests from other users with notification when the document becomes available. Checked-out documents must remain viewable (read-only) by other authorized users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R003	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Visual Document Comparison Tool	The system must provide a side-by-side visual comparison tool that highlights differences between any two versions of a document. For text-based documents (PDF with extractable text, DOCX, XML, EDI messages), the comparison must show added text in green, removed text in red, and modified text in amber with change markers in the margin. For image-based documents (scanned PDFs, TIFF, JPEG), the system must overlay differences using pixel-level comparison with configurable sensitivity thresholds. The comparison view must support synchronized scrolling, navigation between changes (next/previous change buttons), and a summary panel listing all changes categorized by type and location. Arabic text comparisons must correctly handle RTL rendering, ligature changes, and diacritical mark differences. Users must be able to export the comparison report as a PDF with annotations for dispute resolution or customs inquiry responses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R004	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Document Amendment Workflow with Approvals	The system must enforce configurable approval workflows for document amendments based on document type, amendment category, and entity. Bill of Lading amendments must require approval from at least two authorized personnel: the operations manager and the documentation supervisor. Amendments to customs declaration documents for QAHAM, AEJEA, or INNSA must additionally require compliance officer approval before the amended document can be transmitted to the respective port authority system. The workflow must support sequential and parallel approval chains, automatic escalation after configurable timeout periods (default 24 hours for standard, 4 hours for urgent), delegation of approval authority during absence, and rejection with mandatory comments. Each approval step must be timestamped with both Gregorian and Hijri dates and recorded in the document's immutable audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R005	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Immutable Audit Trail for Document Changes	The system must maintain a tamper-proof, immutable audit trail for every document action including creation, view, download, print, edit, version creation, metadata change, classification change, access permission change, annotation, sharing, deletion request, and archival. Each audit entry must record the action type, actor (user ID, name, role, entity), timestamp in UTC with millisecond precision (displayed in local timezone with both Gregorian and Hijri dates), source IP address, device fingerprint, and before/after values for any changed fields. The audit trail must be stored in an append-only data structure that prevents modification or deletion even by system administrators. Audit records must be searchable and filterable by all recorded fields, exportable in CSV and PDF formats for regulatory inspection, and retained for a minimum of 10 years across all entities as required by the most stringent applicable regulation (India CBIC requirement).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R006	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Document Rollback and Restoration	The system must allow authorized users to rollback a document to any previous version with full audit trail preservation. Rollback must create a new version (not overwrite history) that contains the content of the target historical version, clearly marked as a rollback with reference to the source version number. Rollback of documents already submitted to external systems (e.g., a customs declaration submitted to Qatar Single Window or India ICEGATE) must be blocked with a warning message explaining that external submission cannot be undone and directing the user to the amendment workflow instead. The rollback authorization must require at least entity-level documentation supervisor approval for standard documents and dual approval (documentation supervisor plus compliance officer) for customs and financial documents. Rollback requests must include a mandatory justification field with minimum 20 characters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R007	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Branch and Merge for Collaborative Editing	The system must support document branching for scenarios where multiple parties need to propose changes simultaneously, such as when both the shipper and the carrier's agent need to amend a Bill of Lading. A branch must create an independent copy linked to the parent version, allowing edits without affecting the main document line. The system must provide a merge interface that presents all branch changes for review, highlights conflicts where multiple branches modify the same section, and requires manual resolution of conflicts by an authorized user. Merged results must be saved as a new version on the main branch with complete lineage tracking. Branch and merge operations must be limited to editable document types and must not be available for sealed, signed, or externally submitted documents. Each branch must display its origin version, creator entity, and creation timestamp in both calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R008	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Version Lifecycle State Machine	Each document version must progress through a defined lifecycle with states: Draft, Under Review, Approved, Issued, Amended, Superseded, Revoked, and Archived. State transitions must enforce business rules: a Draft can move to Under Review or be discarded; Under Review can be Approved or returned to Draft with comments; Approved documents can be Issued (triggering distribution); Issued documents can be Amended (creating a new version) or Revoked with mandatory reason. The Superseded state must be automatically applied to previous versions when a new Approved version exists. Only the latest Issued version must be available for external transmission to port systems (QAHAM, AEJEA, INNSA). The state machine must be configurable per document type and entity, with the India entity requiring additional states for IGM (Import General Manifest) documents per CBIC regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R009	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Change Notification and Subscription System	The system must provide a subscription-based notification system allowing users to subscribe to change notifications for specific documents, document folders, voyage document sets, or document types. Notifications must be delivered via configurable channels: in-app notification center, email, SMS, and Microsoft Teams/Slack webhook integration. Each notification must include the document name, version number, change summary, actor name and entity, and a direct link to view the changes. Users must be able to configure notification preferences including immediate, hourly digest, or daily digest delivery. Cross-entity notifications must respect access control — users must only receive notifications for documents they are authorized to view. The notification content must be bilingual (English and Arabic) based on the recipient's language preference setting, with proper RTL formatting for Arabic notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R010	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Regulatory Version Freeze and Legal Hold	The system must support regulatory version freeze that locks a specific document version from any further modification when it is referenced in a legal proceeding, customs dispute, insurance claim, or regulatory audit. A legal hold must be initiated by authorized compliance or legal personnel and must specify the hold reason, reference case number, applicable jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), expected hold duration, and the authorizing officer. Documents under legal hold must display a prominent visual indicator and must not be deletable, archivable, or have their metadata modified. The hold must cascade to all linked documents (e.g., freezing a B/L must also freeze associated commercial invoices, packing lists, and certificates of origin). Legal holds must persist across system upgrades and data migrations, and release of a hold must require dual authorization from legal and compliance departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R011	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Automated Change Detection for External Documents	The system must automatically detect when externally received documents (from carriers, port authorities, customs brokers) are updated versions of previously received documents. Detection must use matching algorithms based on document reference numbers (B/L number, customs declaration number, container number), content similarity scoring, and source system identifiers. When a new version of an external document is detected, the system must automatically link it to the existing document chain, create a new version entry, run the visual comparison tool to generate a change summary, and notify subscribed users. For documents received from port community systems (Qatar Dhareeba, UAE Mirsal2, KSA FASAH, India PCS1x), the system must parse status changes and map them to internal document states. False positive matches must be reviewable and dismissible with the AI model learning from corrections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R012	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Version History Export and Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive version history reports for any document or document set, formatted for regulatory submission to customs authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Reports must include complete version timeline with timestamps (Gregorian and Hijri), change summaries between each version, approval chain details, digital signature verification status, and full audit trail entries. Export formats must include PDF (with entity letterhead and compliance certification footer), Excel (for data analysis), and XML (for electronic submission to customs authorities). The report must support entity-specific regulatory formats: Qatar customs requires Arabic-primary bilingual reports, India CBIC requires reports in English with specific section numbering, and UAE reports must include Dubai Trade reference numbers. Batch report generation must be supported for multiple documents simultaneously with progress tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R013	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Document Relationship and Dependency Mapping	The system must maintain and visualize document relationships and dependencies as a directed graph. Relationship types must include: Parent-Child (e.g., Master B/L to House B/Ls), Supersedes (amended document replacing original), References (commercial invoice referencing L/C number), Requires (customs declaration requiring certificate of origin), and Attachments (email with attached shipping instructions). The dependency map must be viewable as an interactive graph visualization showing document nodes with version numbers, relationship edges with labels, and status indicators. When a document is amended, the system must identify all dependent documents that may require corresponding updates and generate alerts to responsible users. For example, amending container weights on a packing list must trigger review alerts for the associated Bill of Lading, customs declaration, and vessel stowage plan at the relevant port (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, or INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R014	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Concurrent Version Access and Conflict Prevention	The system must handle concurrent access to document versions by multiple users across different entities without data corruption or version conflicts. Optimistic concurrency control must be implemented using version tokens — if two users load the same version and one saves changes, the second user must be notified of the conflict before their save is processed. The system must support a minimum of 50 concurrent viewers and 5 concurrent editors per document without performance degradation. Real-time presence indicators must show which users are currently viewing or editing a document, their entity affiliation, and their cursor position for text-based documents. When concurrent edits cannot be automatically merged, the system must present both versions side-by-side with conflict highlighting and require the later editor to manually resolve conflicts. All conflict resolution actions must be logged in the audit trail with both users' identities and entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R015	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Version Metadata and Custom Properties	Each document version must support both system-managed and user-defined custom metadata properties. System-managed properties must include version number, creation date (Gregorian and Hijri), author, entity, file size, file hash (SHA-256), content type, page count, and lifecycle state. User-defined custom properties must be configurable per document type and entity, supporting data types including text (with Arabic/English validation), number, date (Gregorian and Hijri with automatic conversion), currency amount with currency code (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), dropdown selection from configurable lists, and multi-value tags. Custom property schemas must be version-controlled themselves, with schema changes propagating to existing documents without data loss. The system must validate that mandatory custom properties are populated before allowing a document version to transition from Draft to Under Review state, with entity-specific mandatory fields (e.g., India entity requiring IEC code on all import documents).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R016	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Bulk Version Operations and Batch Amendment	The system must support bulk version operations for scenarios such as vessel schedule changes affecting multiple Bills of Lading or port rotation changes requiring updates to all associated customs declarations. Bulk amendment must allow selection of multiple related documents (e.g., all B/Ls for voyage VY2024-0456), application of a common change set (e.g., update ETA at QAHAM from March 15 to March 18), and creation of new versions for all affected documents in a single transactional operation. The bulk operation must generate a batch amendment report listing all documents affected, changes applied, and approval status. Approval workflows for bulk amendments must allow single approval for the entire batch rather than requiring individual approval per document, with the batch treated as an atomic unit — if any document in the batch fails validation, the entire batch must be rolled back. Batch operations must support up to 200 documents per transaction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-002-R017	FEAT-034-1-002	MOD-034	Version Archival and Cold Storage Migration	The system must automatically migrate older document versions to cost-effective cold storage while maintaining instant access to the current and immediately previous versions in hot storage. The migration policy must be configurable per entity and document type with default thresholds: versions older than 6 months move to warm storage (retrieval within 30 seconds), versions older than 2 years move to cold storage (retrieval within 5 minutes). Documents under legal hold or regulatory freeze must be exempt from cold storage migration regardless of age. When a user requests access to a cold-stored version, the system must initiate automatic retrieval with a progress indicator and estimated time display. Retrieval must be logged in the audit trail. Storage cost allocation must track hot, warm, and cold storage consumption separately per entity with costs in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for chargeback reporting to the finance module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R001	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	PKI-Based Digital Signature Infrastructure	The system must implement a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) based digital signature framework supporting X.509 certificates from recognized Certificate Authorities in all operating jurisdictions. Supported CAs must include Qatar's Ministry of Transport and Communications approved CAs, UAE's Emirates Identity Authority (EIDA) certificates, KSA's National Center for Digital Certification (NCDC) certificates, and India's Controller of Certifying Authorities (CCA) approved providers (eMudhra, Sify, NSDL, CDAC). The system must support RSA 2048-bit and 4096-bit keys, ECDSA P-256 and P-384 curves, and SHA-256/SHA-384/SHA-512 hash algorithms. Certificate validation must check revocation status via OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol) and CRL (Certificate Revocation List) in real-time before accepting any signature. The infrastructure must support hardware security module (HSM) integration for server-side signing operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R002	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Multi-Party Signing Workflow Engine	The system must support configurable multi-party signing workflows where documents require signatures from multiple parties in defined sequences. For a Bill of Lading at Hamad Port (QAHAM), the typical workflow requires signatures from: shipper or authorized agent, carrier's documentation department, and optionally the customs broker. The workflow engine must support sequential signing (Party A must sign before Party B), parallel signing (multiple parties can sign simultaneously), and conditional signing (Party C signs only if cargo is dangerous goods per IMDG Code). Each signing step must have a configurable deadline with automatic reminders at 75% and 90% of elapsed time. Expired signing requests must escalate to the workflow administrator. The system must support delegation of signing authority with full audit trail, allowing a port operations manager to delegate signing to a deputy during leave periods while maintaining accountability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R003	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Electronic Stamp Integration with Government Systems	The system must integrate with government-approved e-stamping services in each operating jurisdiction. For India, integration with Stock Holding Corporation of India Limited (SHCIL) e-stamping system is required for documents requiring stamp duty payment. For Qatar, integration with the Ministry of Justice e-authentication portal must be supported. For UAE, integration with the ICA (Federal Authority for Identity and Citizenship) and Tasdeeq must be implemented. For KSA, integration with the Ministry of Commerce e-stamping platform is required. Each integration must handle the complete e-stamp lifecycle: stamp duty calculation based on document type and value, payment processing in local currency (INR for India, QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA), stamp certificate retrieval, and stamp verification. The system must cache stamp certificates locally and verify their validity during document retrieval. Failed e-stamping must not block document creation but must flag the document as pending stamping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R004	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Signature Verification and Validation Service	The system must provide comprehensive signature verification capabilities that validate the mathematical integrity of digital signatures, verify the signer's certificate chain up to the trusted root CA, check certificate revocation status in real-time, confirm the signing timestamp against a trusted timestamp authority (TSA), and verify that the document content has not been modified since signing. Verification results must be displayed with clear visual indicators: green checkmark for fully valid signatures, yellow warning for signatures with expired certificates (but valid at time of signing), and red cross for invalid or tampered signatures. The verification report must include signer identity details, certificate issuer, signing timestamp (Gregorian and Hijri), signature algorithm, hash value, and certificate validity period. Batch verification must support checking all signatures across a voyage document set (up to 200 documents) with a consolidated validity report exportable in PDF and XML formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R005	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Long-Term Signature Validation (LTV)	The system must implement Long-Term Validation (LTV) for digital signatures ensuring that signature validity can be verified years after the signing certificate has expired. LTV implementation must embed all necessary validation data within the signed document including the complete certificate chain, OCSP responses or CRL snapshots captured at signing time, and trusted timestamp tokens from RFC 3161 compliant Timestamp Authorities. The system must use PAdES (PDF Advanced Electronic Signatures) format for PDF documents conforming to PAdES-LTV profile (ETSI EN 319 142-1). For non-PDF documents, the system must use CAdES (CMS Advanced Electronic Signatures) or XAdES (XML Advanced Electronic Signatures) as appropriate. LTV-enabled signatures must remain verifiable for a minimum of 10 years after signing, satisfying the longest document retention requirement across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R006	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Mobile and Remote Signing Capabilities	The system must support digital signing from mobile devices (iOS and Android) and remote locations, enabling vessel masters, port agents, and customs brokers to sign documents in the field. Mobile signing must support biometric authentication (fingerprint, face recognition) as a second factor before signature application. The system must integrate with cloud-based signing services that allow users to sign using certificates stored in secure cloud HSMs, eliminating the need to carry physical USB tokens. For scenarios at Jebel Ali port (AEJEA) or JNPT (INNSA) where network connectivity is intermittent, the system must support queued signing — the user authenticates and approves the signing action, which is executed when connectivity is restored, with the signing timestamp reflecting the approval time, not the execution time. Mobile signing must support both portrait and landscape orientations with properly scaled signature placement for Arabic RTL documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R007	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Signature Placement and Appearance Configuration	The system must provide configurable visual signature placement on documents with support for both graphical signature images and text-based signature blocks. Signature appearance must be configurable per entity and document type, including options for: company logo, signer name (in English and Arabic), designation/title, signing date (Gregorian and Hijri), location (port name and UN/LOCODE), and a QR code linking to the online verification portal. For Bills of Lading, the signature block must conform to carrier-specific formatting requirements with the carrier's standard signature position. The system must support pre-defined signature zones on document templates where signatures must be placed, preventing signatures from obscuring critical document content. Multiple signatures on the same document must be visually arranged without overlapping. The signature appearance must include a tamper-evident seal indicator that visually changes if the document is modified after signing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R008	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Qualified Electronic Signature Compliance	The system must support Qualified Electronic Signatures (QES) as defined by applicable regulations in each jurisdiction to ensure legal equivalence with handwritten signatures. For UAE, compliance with Federal Decree-Law No. 46 of 2021 on Electronic Transactions and Trust Services is required, supporting the UAE Pass national digital identity. For KSA, compliance with the Saudi Anti-Cyber Crime Law and Electronic Transactions Law is required with NCDC-issued qualified certificates. For Qatar, compliance with Law No. 16 of 2010 on Electronic Commerce and Transactions is required. For India, compliance with the Information Technology Act 2000 (Section 3A for electronic signatures and Section 5 for secure digital signatures) and the India Evidence Act provisions for electronic records is required. The system must maintain a jurisdiction mapping table that determines which signature level (simple, advanced, qualified) is legally required for each document type in each country.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R009	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Bulk Signing and Automated Signature Application	The system must support bulk signing operations for scenarios where an authorized signatory needs to sign multiple related documents simultaneously, such as all documentation for a vessel call at Hamad Port including multiple Bills of Lading, Dangerous Goods Manifests, and Cargo Manifests. The bulk signing interface must display a summary of all documents pending signature with key metadata (document type, reference number, parties involved), allow the signer to review each document or sign all with a single authentication action, and apply individual signatures to each document (not a batch signature). A single authentication via certificate PIN or biometric must authorize signing of up to 50 documents in a batch. Server-side automated signing must be supported for system-generated documents such as automated customs status notifications and container release orders using entity-level certificates stored in HSMs, with each automated signature logged with the triggering business event.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R010	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Timestamp Authority Integration	The system must integrate with trusted Timestamp Authorities (TSAs) compliant with RFC 3161 to provide cryptographic proof of the exact time a document was signed. TSA integration must support multiple providers for redundancy, with automatic failover if the primary TSA is unavailable. Timestamp tokens must be embedded within the signature structure (not stored separately) to ensure they travel with the document. The system must maintain a list of trusted TSAs approved for each jurisdiction — using jurisdiction-appropriate TSAs for regulatory compliance (e.g., India's CCA-approved TSAs for Indian customs documents, UAE-approved TSAs for Dubai Trade submissions). Timestamp accuracy must be within one second of UTC. The system must verify timestamp token validity during signature verification, checking the TSA certificate chain and confirming the token's integrity. Timestamp costs must be tracked per entity and allocated in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) for cost accounting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R011	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Counter-Signature and Endorsement Chain	The system must support counter-signatures and endorsement chains essential for negotiable shipping documents such as negotiable Bills of Lading and Letters of Indemnity. An endorsement chain tracks the sequential transfer of document rights from one party to another — for example, when a negotiable B/L is endorsed from the shipper to a bank (for L/C negotiation) and then to the final consignee. Each endorsement must be a separate digital signature layer that does not invalidate previous signatures. The system must display the complete endorsement chain chronologically with signer identities, timestamps, and endorsement notes. For to-order Bills of Lading commonly used in Qatar-India and UAE-India trade corridors, the system must enforce that endorsements follow the documentary credit requirements specified in the associated Letter of Credit (UCP 600 compliance). The endorsement chain must be verifiable independently at each link.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R012	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Signature Revocation and Document Recall	The system must provide a controlled process for revoking signatures on documents that have not yet been externally transmitted or acted upon by third parties. Signature revocation must require authentication by the original signer or a designated authority (entity compliance officer), a mandatory reason for revocation (dropdown with categories: Error in Document, Unauthorized Signing, Fraudulent Content, Changed Circumstances, Other with free text), and creation of an audit trail entry that permanently records the revocation. Revoked signatures must be visually distinguished (strikethrough with red indicator) but not removed from the document to maintain audit integrity. If a document with a revoked signature has already been transmitted to external systems (Qatar Single Window, Mirsal2, FASAH, ICEGATE), the system must block revocation and instead guide the user to the amendment workflow. The system must track revocation rates per entity and user for compliance monitoring and flag unusual patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R013	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Digital Seal for Organizational Authentication	The system must support organizational digital seals (distinct from individual signatures) that authenticate documents as officially issued by the entity. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must have its own organizational seal certificate issued by a recognized CA in the entity's jurisdiction. The digital seal must include the entity's official name (in English and Arabic for GCC entities), commercial registration number, trade license number, and the entity's official logo. Seals must be applied automatically to system-generated official documents such as Freight Invoices, Delivery Orders, Container Release Orders, and Arrival Notices. The seal application must be triggered by document lifecycle transitions (e.g., when a Delivery Order moves to Issued state). Seal certificates must be managed by designated entity security administrators with HSM-backed key storage. The system must support dual sealing for documents issued jointly by two entities, such as inter-company transactions between the Qatar and UAE entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R014	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Electronic Bill of Lading Signature Compliance	The system must comply with electronic Bill of Lading standards and regulatory frameworks including UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Transferable Records (MLETR), adopted by UAE, KSA, and under consideration by Qatar and India. The system must support integration with approved eBL platforms including DCSA (Digital Container Shipping Association) standards, Bolero, essDOCS, and WAVE BL for issuing and transferring electronic Bills of Lading with legal validity equivalent to paper B/Ls. Signatures on eBLs must satisfy the singularity requirement (only one valid holder at any time), the exclusive control requirement (only the holder can transfer), and the integrity requirement (any unauthorized change is detectable). The system must support both carrier-issued and freight forwarder-issued eBLs with appropriate signature authorities. For trade lanes where paper B/Ls are still required (certain India JNPT imports), the system must support printing signed documents with embedded verification QR codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R015	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Signature Audit Dashboard and Compliance Reporting	The system must provide a comprehensive signature audit dashboard showing real-time statistics and historical trends for digital signature operations across all entities. Dashboard metrics must include: total signatures applied (daily, weekly, monthly), signatures by entity and document type, signature verification success/failure rates, average signing turnaround time per workflow, pending signature requests with aging analysis, certificate expiry warnings (30, 60, 90 days), e-stamp consumption and costs by entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and signing authority utilization. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary metrics to individual transaction details. Compliance reports must be generated monthly per entity, formatted for submission to regulatory authorities where required (e.g., India's CBIC requires periodic digital signature usage reports for customs-related documents). Reports must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for GCC entity reports, and exportable in PDF, Excel, and XML formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R016	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Certificate Lifecycle Management	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of digital certificates used for signing operations including issuance requests, installation, renewal, suspension, revocation, and replacement. The system must maintain a central certificate registry tracking all active certificates across all entities with details including certificate owner, issuing CA, key algorithm, validity period, usage scope (which document types and entities the certificate is authorized for), and storage location (USB token, smart card, HSM, cloud). Automated alerts must be generated for certificates approaching expiry at 90, 60, 30, and 7 days before expiry date with escalation to entity IT administrators. When a certificate is revoked or expired, all documents signed with that certificate must be retroactively flagged for verification review. The system must support emergency certificate replacement procedures for compromised keys with automatic re-signing of documents signed with the compromised certificate using the replacement certificate, limited to documents not yet externally transmitted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-003-R017	FEAT-034-1-003	MOD-034	Cross-Border Signature Recognition and Validation	The system must handle cross-border digital signature recognition for documents that traverse multiple jurisdictions in container shipping operations. For a shipment from JNPT India to Hamad Port Qatar, documents signed with an Indian CCA-approved certificate must be validated and accepted by the Qatar entity with appropriate trust mapping. The system must maintain a cross-jurisdiction trust matrix defining which foreign CAs are recognized in each operating jurisdiction. Where mutual recognition agreements exist (such as GCC cross-border e-signature frameworks), the system must automatically validate foreign signatures. Where no mutual recognition exists, the system must flag the document for manual verification and provide the option to request a counter-signature from a locally recognized certificate. The trust matrix must be updateable by entity compliance officers as new bilateral or multilateral recognition agreements are enacted across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R001	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Repository with Categorization	The system must maintain a centralized template repository organized by categories including Transport Documents (Bill of Lading, Sea Waybill, Delivery Order, Mate's Receipt, Shipping Order), Customs Documents (Import/Export Declaration, Transit Declaration, Warehouse Entry, Free Zone Entry), Commercial Documents (Commercial Invoice, Proforma Invoice, Packing List, Weight Certificate), Certificates (Certificate of Origin, Phytosanitary Certificate, Fumigation Certificate, Quality Certificate, Insurance Certificate), Finance Documents (Freight Invoice, Debit/Credit Note, Statement of Account, Letter of Credit Application), and Compliance Documents (Dangerous Goods Declaration, ISM/ISPS certificates, Sanctions Screening Report). Each template must be tagged with applicable entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), applicable ports (QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), and the regulatory authority that prescribes the format. The repository must support a minimum of 200 active templates across all categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R005	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Extraction of customs declaration data for GCC and India	The system must extract data from customs declarations specific to each jurisdiction: Qatar Customs (Single Administrative Document), UAE Federal Customs Authority declarations, Saudi ZATCA customs forms, and Indian Customs Bill of Entry and Shipping Bill formats. Extracted fields must include: declaration number, date, importer/exporter IDs (CR number for Qatar, trade license for UAE, CR for KSA, IEC for India), HS tariff codes, assessable value, duty amounts, and duty payment status. The system must handle both electronic and scanned paper declarations, recognizing official stamps, QR codes, and barcode identifiers used by each country's customs authority for document authentication and verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R002	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Bilingual Template Design with RTL Support	The system must provide a template designer supporting bilingual English-Arabic layouts with proper right-to-left (RTL) rendering for Arabic text sections. Templates must support mixed directionality within a single document — for example, a Bill of Lading with English field labels on the left and Arabic translations on the right, or alternating language sections. The template designer must handle Arabic typographic requirements including proper ligature rendering, kashida justification, diacritical marks (tashkeel), and Indic numeral display options (both Arabic-Indic ٠١٢ and Western 012 numeral systems). Templates must support dynamic text expansion where Arabic text may be 20-30% longer than English equivalents, with automatic layout adjustment to prevent overflow. Font embedding must be supported to ensure consistent Arabic rendering across different viewing environments. Hindi Devanagari script support must be available for India entity templates where required by local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R003	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Entity-Specific Template Branding and Customization	Each template must support entity-specific branding including company logo, letterhead, footer, color scheme, and corporate typography for each of the four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The system must support template inheritance where a master template defines the base layout and content, and entity-specific variants override branding elements, add entity-required fields, or adjust formatting to comply with local requirements. For example, the Commercial Invoice template master must define the standard structure while the KSA variant adds QR code placement for ZATCA e-invoicing compliance and the India variant includes GST-related fields per Indian tax law. Template variants must be independently version-controlled, and changes to the master template must cascade to entity variants with a review and approval step. Entity administrators must be able to customize templates within guardrails (branding only, no structural changes) without IT involvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R004	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	AI-Powered Template Auto-Population	The system must provide AI-powered automatic population of templates from existing operational data across ERP modules. When a user initiates document generation from a template, the system must identify the relevant data sources (booking records, vessel schedules, container tracking, customer master data, tariff tables) and pre-fill all available fields. For a Bill of Lading template, auto-population must include shipper/consignee details from customer master, vessel/voyage from schedule module, container numbers and seal numbers from operations module, cargo description and weights from booking data, port of loading and discharge (UN/LOCODE), freight charges from tariff module in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), and INCOTERMS from the sales contract. The AI engine must detect and flag data inconsistencies (e.g., container weight exceeding VGM limits, mismatched port codes) before generating the document. Auto-populated fields must be highlighted for user review before finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R005	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Version Control and Approval Workflow	All templates must be version-controlled with a complete change history tracking who modified the template, when, what changed, and why. Template modifications must follow an approval workflow: template designer creates or modifies a template (Draft state), submits for review (Under Review), entity compliance officer validates regulatory compliance (Approved or Rejected with comments), and entity administrator activates the template (Active). Only Active templates must be available for document generation. The system must prevent modification of templates that are currently in use for pending document workflows — a new version must be created instead. Template version transitions must record timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. When a regulatory authority (e.g., India CBIC or Qatar Customs) mandates a format change, the system must support urgent fast-track approval with single-approver override capability for compliance-critical updates affecting port operations at QAHAM, AEJEA, AEKHL, or INNSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R006	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Dynamic Field Configuration and Conditional Logic	Templates must support dynamic fields that show, hide, or change based on conditional logic tied to business context. Conditions must evaluate shipment attributes including trade direction (import/export/transshipment), cargo type (general, reefer, dangerous goods IMDG classes 1-9, out-of-gauge, break-bulk), INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, DDP, EXW, FCA), port pair (origin/destination combination), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR per ISO 6346), and customer type (shipper, consignee, freight forwarder, NVOCC). For example, a Dangerous Goods Declaration template must conditionally display UN Number, Proper Shipping Name, Packing Group, EmS Schedule, and Marine Pollutant fields only when cargo classification indicates IMDG goods. Conditional sections for Reefer containers must include temperature settings, ventilation requirements, and atmosphere control parameters. The conditional logic engine must support AND/OR/NOT operators with up to 10 nested conditions per field.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R007	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Regulatory Template Compliance Validation	The system must validate that generated documents from templates comply with regulatory requirements specific to each port and jurisdiction. For Qatar Customs (Hamad Port QAHAM), validation must ensure mandatory fields per Qatar General Authority of Customs circulars are populated including HS code at 8-digit level, country of origin, and declared value in QAR. For UAE (Jebel Ali AEJEA, Khalifa Port AEKHL), validation must enforce Dubai Trade and Abu Dhabi Customs requirements including FTA-specific fields. For KSA, ZATCA e-invoicing requirements must be validated including QR code generation per Phase 2 specifications. For India (JNPT INNSA), validation must check CBIC-mandated fields including IEC code, GSTIN, HSN code at 8-digit level, and BCD/IGST applicable duties. Validation must run automatically before document finalization with clear error messages indicating which fields fail which regulatory requirement, preventing submission of non-compliant documents to customs authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R008	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Output Format and Rendering Engine	The template rendering engine must generate documents in multiple output formats including PDF (for official document distribution and printing), PDF/A-3 (for long-term archival with embedded original data), HTML (for web-based preview and email distribution), XML (for electronic submission to customs and port community systems including EDIFACT mapping), Excel (for data-centric templates like cargo manifests and container lists), and Word (for templates requiring recipient editing such as draft Letters of Indemnity). The rendering engine must produce pixel-perfect output matching the template design across all formats, with proper Arabic font rendering and RTL layout preservation in all output types. PDF output must support A4 and US Letter paper sizes with configurable margins, headers, footers, page numbering (including Arabic page numbers), and automatic page breaks that respect section boundaries. Generated documents must be optimized for file size (maximum 2MB for a standard 5-page document) to facilitate email transmission and upload to customs portals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R009	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Cloning and Rapid Customization	The system must support template cloning to enable rapid creation of new templates from existing ones. When an entity requires a variation of a standard template (e.g., a specialized Certificate of Origin for Qatar-India bilateral trade requiring additional fields per the Qatar-India trade agreement), an authorized user must be able to clone the base template, apply modifications, and submit the clone through the standard approval workflow. The cloning operation must preserve all formatting, conditional logic, field mappings, and branding while allowing modifications. The system must track the lineage of cloned templates (parent-child relationship) and optionally propagate critical updates from parent to child templates with review. Bulk cloning must be supported for scenarios such as setting up templates for a new entity — cloning all active templates from an existing entity (e.g., UAE) and adapting them for the new entity (e.g., KSA) with batch branding replacement and field mapping adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R010	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Data Source Mapping and Field Binding	The template designer must provide a visual field binding interface that maps template placeholders to data sources across the ERP. Supported data sources must include all ERP modules: Vessel Schedule (vessel name, IMO number, flag, voyage number, ETA/ETD per port), Booking (cargo details, container assignments, shipper/consignee references), Container Tracking (container number, size/type, seal number, VGM weight, status, location), Customer Master (company name in English and Arabic, address, registration numbers, IEC code for India, CR number for GCC), Tariff and Rating (freight charges, surcharges, currency, exchange rates), and Financial (invoice numbers, payment terms, bank details in applicable currency QAR/AED/SAR/INR/USD). Field bindings must support data transformations including date format conversion (Gregorian to Hijri), currency formatting per locale, number formatting (Western vs Arabic-Indic numerals), text case conversion, and concatenation of multiple source fields. Binding validation must verify data source availability and type compatibility at template design time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R011	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Batch Document Generation from Templates	The system must support batch document generation for producing multiple documents from a single template using different data sets. Typical use cases include generating Bills of Lading for all bookings on a vessel call (up to 500 B/Ls per vessel at Jebel Ali), creating Arrival Notices for all consignees of an arriving vessel at JNPT, or producing Freight Invoices for all shipments in a billing cycle. The batch generation engine must process documents asynchronously with a progress tracker showing estimated completion time, completed count, failed count, and error details. Failed individual documents must not block the batch — they must be logged with error details and available for retry after correction. Generated documents must be individually reviewable before final approval, with an option for bulk approval by authorized personnel. The system must handle entity-specific formatting within a single batch when documents span multiple entities, applying the correct branding, currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and regulatory fields per document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R012	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Localization and Translation Management	The system must support template localization for all operating languages with primary focus on English and Arabic. Each template must have a localization layer where all static text elements (labels, headers, footers, instructions, legal disclaimers) are stored as translatable strings with language-specific values. The system must provide a translation management interface allowing authorized translators to update template text without modifying the template structure. Translation completeness must be tracked per template and language with warnings for templates that have untranslated strings. For templates requiring Hindi localization (India entity customs documents), Devanagari script support must be available. The system must support locale-specific formatting rules: date formats (DD/MM/YYYY for GCC, DD-MM-YYYY for India), number formats (comma as thousands separator in English, Arabic formatting for Arabic), and currency symbol placement (before amount for USD/AED/QAR/SAR, after amount for INR per Indian convention). Legal disclaimer text must be jurisdiction-specific and reviewed by entity legal departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R013	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Testing and Preview Environment	The system must provide a template testing sandbox where template designers can preview generated documents with sample data before activating templates for production use. The sandbox must include pre-configured test data sets representing common shipping scenarios: standard FCL export from QAHAM to INNSA, reefer container import at AEJEA from Southeast Asia, dangerous goods transshipment through Jebel Ali, LCL consolidation from multiple KSA origins, and out-of-gauge cargo with special handling requirements. Preview must render the document in all supported output formats (PDF, HTML, XML, Excel) with both English and Arabic content to verify bilingual layout correctness. The testing environment must validate conditional logic by showing which fields are visible or hidden for each test scenario. Print preview must show exact paper output including margins, headers, footers, and page breaks. Performance metrics must be displayed showing template rendering time, output file size, and data source query execution time to identify optimization opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R014	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Clause and Paragraph Library for Legal Content	The system must maintain a library of reusable clauses, paragraphs, and legal text blocks that can be inserted into templates. The clause library must be organized by category: Terms and Conditions (freight payment terms, demurrage clauses, lien clauses), Liability Limitations (Hague-Visby Rules Article IV, Hamburg Rules references), Jurisdiction Clauses (applicable law and arbitration venue per entity), Force Majeure definitions, Dangerous Goods declarations (IMDG Code compliance statements), Sanctions Compliance statements (OFAC, EU, UN Security Council sanctions screening declarations), and Data Protection notices (Qatar Data Privacy Law, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, India DPDP Act 2023). Each clause must be available in English and Arabic with version control and legal department approval workflow. Clauses must support parameterization — for example, a jurisdiction clause template where the applicable law and arbitration city are parameters filled based on the entity (Qatar law with Doha arbitration for Qatar entity, UAE law with DIFC Courts for UAE entity, Indian law with Mumbai jurisdiction for India entity).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R015	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Usage Analytics and Optimization	The system must track and report comprehensive usage analytics for all templates including generation frequency per template (daily, weekly, monthly), average generation time, error rates during auto-population, most frequently manually corrected fields (indicating potential data quality or mapping issues), template adoption rates per entity, and user satisfaction ratings. The analytics dashboard must identify underutilized templates that may be candidates for retirement and high-error templates requiring redesign. AI-powered optimization suggestions must be generated based on usage patterns — for example, if users consistently add the same manual field to a template, the system should suggest adding that field permanently. Template performance benchmarks must track rendering time trends and flag templates that exceed the 10-second generation threshold. Analytics must be filterable by entity, template category, time period, and user role, with reports exportable in PDF and Excel formats with data in both English and Arabic as per user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R016	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Import and Export with Industry Standards	The system must support template import and export to facilitate template sharing between entities and with external parties. Export format must preserve all template elements including layout, field bindings (as abstract mappings), conditional logic rules, branding assets, and localization strings in a portable package format (ZIP containing JSON metadata and asset files). Import must validate the template package structure, identify unmapped data source references, and provide a mapping wizard to connect abstract field bindings to the target system's data sources. The system must support import of industry-standard template formats including BIMCO standard document layouts for charter party documents, FIATA document standards for freight forwarding (FBL, FCR, FWR), ICC document standards for trade finance (UCP 600 compliant L/C applications), and WCO data model-aligned customs declaration formats. Imported templates must go through the standard approval workflow before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R017	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Offline Template Access and Generation	The system must support offline template access and document generation for users operating in disconnected environments such as vessel-side operations at Hamad Port, container yard inspections at Jebel Ali, or remote offices in KSA with intermittent connectivity. Authorized users must be able to download a designated set of templates with their associated clause libraries and recent data snapshots for offline use. Offline document generation must function using the locally cached template and data, with the generated document stored locally in encrypted storage (AES-256) until synchronization is possible. Upon reconnection, offline-generated documents must sync to the central repository with conflict detection — if the underlying template version has changed while offline, the system must flag the document for re-generation review. The offline capability must limit data exposure by only caching data the user is authorized to access per their role and entity permissions, with automatic purge of cached data after a configurable period (default 7 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-1-004-R018	FEAT-034-1-004	MOD-034	Template Compliance Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all template lifecycle events including creation, modification, cloning, approval, activation, deactivation, and retirement. Each audit entry must record the actor (user ID, name, role, entity), timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, action performed, before and after states for modifications, and approval chain details. For documents generated from templates, the audit trail must link the generated document to the exact template version used, the data sources queried, the auto-populated field values, any manual overrides applied by the user, and the conditional logic evaluation results. This linkage is critical for regulatory inquiries — when Qatar Customs or India CBIC requests documentation about how a customs declaration was prepared, the system must reproduce the exact template version, data inputs, and generation parameters. Audit records must be immutable, stored in append-only format, and retained for a minimum of 10 years consistent with the longest applicable regulatory requirement across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R001	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Multi-language OCR engine supporting Arabic and English	The system must provide a dual-language OCR engine capable of accurately recognizing both Arabic and English text within the same document, including mixed-language documents commonly found in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India shipping operations. The engine must handle Arabic right-to-left text direction, Arabic numerals (Eastern and Western), diacritical marks, and ligatures. Accuracy must exceed 95% for printed text and 85% for handwritten annotations. The system must process documents from all four port jurisdictions: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA), each having distinct document formats and regulatory stamps that the OCR engine must be trained to recognize.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R002	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Automatic document type classification upon upload	Upon document upload, the system must automatically classify the document into one of the predefined categories: Bill of Lading (B/L), commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, customs declaration, delivery order, container inspection report, phytosanitary certificate, dangerous goods declaration (IMO DG), insurance certificate, letter of credit, bank guarantee, or port authority permit. Classification must use AI-based pattern recognition analyzing document layout, header fields, logos, and key terms in both Arabic and English. The system must achieve a classification accuracy of at least 92% and present a confidence score to the user, allowing manual override when confidence falls below 80%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R003	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Structured data extraction from Bills of Lading	The system must extract structured fields from Bills of Lading including: B/L number, shipper name and address, consignee name and address, notify party, vessel name, voyage number, port of loading (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA), port of discharge, container numbers, seal numbers, gross weight, number of packages, description of goods, INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, etc.), freight payment terms (prepaid/collect), and date of issue. Extracted data must be mapped to the ERP's internal data model and validated against existing booking records. For containers of types 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, and reefer units, the system must cross-reference extracted container numbers against the container tracking module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R006	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Batch OCR processing for bulk document uploads	The system must support batch processing of documents where users can upload up to 500 documents simultaneously via drag-and-drop or folder upload. Each document in the batch must be individually processed through the OCR pipeline: classification, text extraction, data mapping, and indexing. The system must provide a real-time progress dashboard showing the status of each document in the batch (queued, processing, completed, failed). Failed documents must be quarantined with detailed error descriptions. Batch processing must support mixed document types and languages within the same batch. Processing priority must be configurable based on document type, with customs declarations and B/Ls receiving higher priority than general correspondence documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R007	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Auto-indexing with metadata tagging	After OCR extraction, the system must automatically generate metadata tags for each document including: document type, related shipment reference, booking number, container numbers, vessel/voyage, port pair (origin-destination), customer name, date range, currency, INCOTERMS, and entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India). Tags must be stored in both Arabic and English where applicable. The auto-indexing engine must link documents to existing ERP records such as bookings, invoices, and container movements using fuzzy matching algorithms with configurable similarity thresholds. The system must support custom metadata fields defined per entity, allowing each country operation to add jurisdiction-specific index fields such as Qatar Free Zone references or Indian GST numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R008	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	OCR confidence scoring and manual verification workflow	Each extracted field must be assigned an individual confidence score ranging from 0 to 100. Fields with confidence scores below a configurable threshold (default 75%) must be highlighted in the verification interface for manual review. The verification UI must display the original document image alongside the extracted data in a side-by-side view, supporting Arabic RTL layout. Reviewers must be able to correct extracted values, and corrections must feed back into the AI model as training data to improve future accuracy. The system must track verification statistics per document type, per language, and per user to identify systematic extraction issues and monitor operator performance across all four country entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R009	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Template-based extraction for recurring document formats	The system must allow administrators to define extraction templates for frequently received document formats from specific trading partners, shipping lines, or port authorities. Templates must map specific zones on the document to data fields, supporting both fixed-position and relative-position zone definitions. When a new document matches a known template (via layout fingerprinting), the system must apply the template for faster and more accurate extraction. Templates must be versioned, allowing updates when trading partners change their document formats. The system must support at least 200 active templates per entity and must automatically suggest template creation when it detects recurring unmatched document layouts appearing more than five times from the same source.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R010	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Handwritten annotation and stamp recognition	The system must recognize handwritten annotations commonly found on shipping documents such as manual corrections, approval signatures, date stamps, customs officer notes, and port authority endorsements. For Arabic handwriting, the system must support connected cursive script recognition with a minimum accuracy of 80%. The system must identify and classify official stamps including customs clearance stamps from Qatar General Authority of Customs, UAE Federal Customs Authority, Saudi ZATCA, and Indian CBIC. Recognized stamps must be tagged with the issuing authority, date (both Gregorian and Hijri where applicable), and location. The system must differentiate between original stamps and photocopied stamps, flagging potential document authenticity concerns for compliance review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R011	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	QR code and barcode extraction from shipping documents	The system must detect and decode QR codes, Code 128, Code 39, and PDF417 barcodes embedded in shipping documents. This includes container tracking barcodes, customs declaration QR codes (used extensively by ZATCA in KSA and CBIC in India), electronic B/L verification codes, and port gate pass barcodes from Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Decoded data must be cross-referenced with the ERP database to auto-link documents to shipments, containers, or transactions. The system must handle partially damaged or low-resolution barcodes by applying image enhancement algorithms before decoding. Multiple barcodes within a single document must each be individually extracted and mapped to their respective data fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R012	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Extraction validation against ERP master data	All extracted data must be validated against the ERP master data in real time. Customer names must be matched against the customer master, vessel names against the vessel registry, port codes against the UN/LOCODE database, HS codes against the tariff master for each jurisdiction, and container numbers against ISO 6346 check-digit validation. Currency codes must be validated against ISO 4217 (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). The system must flag any extracted value that does not match master data, presenting suggested corrections from the master data. Validation rules must be entity-specific, as each country operation may have different acceptable value ranges for weights, container counts, and monetary amounts based on local trade patterns and regulatory limits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R013	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Offline OCR processing capability for field operations	The system must provide an offline OCR module for field operations at ports and container yards where network connectivity may be intermittent. The offline module must run on tablets and laptops, supporting document scanning via device camera with real-time OCR processing. Extracted data must be stored locally in an encrypted SQLite database and synchronized with the central ERP system once connectivity is restored. The offline module must support at least the five most common document types: B/L, delivery order, container inspection report, gate pass, and equipment interchange receipt. Synchronization must handle conflict resolution when the same document has been processed both offline and online, using timestamp-based precedence with manual override capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R003	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Multi-Entity Report Consolidation	The system must support consolidated regulatory reporting across multiple legal entities operating under the ERP including the Qatar parent entity, UAE branch, KSA subsidiary, and India operations. Consolidation must handle inter-company elimination entries for container leasing, freight forwarding commissions, and shared vessel slot arrangements. Currency consolidation must convert all amounts to the reporting entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) using the closing rate for balance sheet items and average rate for income statement items. The system must maintain entity-level detail for drill-down while producing consolidated group reports as required by IFRS 10.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R014	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Multi-page document handling and page-level extraction	The system must handle multi-page documents of up to 200 pages, correctly associating all pages as a single document unit. For documents where different pages contain different data types (e.g., a customs declaration with attached annexures, packing lists spanning multiple pages, or a B/L with rider clauses), the system must perform page-level classification and extraction. The system must detect and handle duplicate pages within a multi-page upload, preventing data duplication in extraction results. Page ordering must be automatically determined even if pages are scanned out of sequence, using content analysis and page numbering detection. The system must support extraction from documents containing both landscape and portrait oriented pages within the same file.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R015	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	Dangerous goods document extraction per IMDG Code	The system must extract specific fields from dangerous goods declarations as per the International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code requirements. Extracted fields must include: UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group, marine pollutant status, flashpoint, EmS schedule, segregation requirements, and emergency contact details. The system must validate extracted UN numbers against the IMDG dangerous goods list and flag any inconsistencies. For container shipping at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must extract container-specific DG information including container packing certificate details, placard requirements, and stowage category. Extracted DG data must be cross-referenced with the vessel stowage plan module to verify compliance with segregation rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-001-R016	FEAT-034-2-001	MOD-034	AI model training and continuous improvement pipeline	The system must implement a continuous learning pipeline where manually corrected OCR results are fed back into the AI model for retraining. Training data must be segregated by entity and document type to allow entity-specific model fine-tuning. The system must track extraction accuracy metrics over time, generating weekly reports showing accuracy trends per document type, per language, and per source. Model retraining must occur automatically when accuracy drops below configurable thresholds or when a minimum of 500 new corrections have been accumulated. The training pipeline must support A/B testing of model versions, allowing parallel operation of current and candidate models with automatic promotion when the candidate outperforms the current model by a statistically significant margin across all supported document types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R001	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Configurable document expiry tracking per document type	The system must maintain an expiry tracking registry for all document types that have validity periods, including but not limited to: trade licenses, commercial registrations, customs broker licenses, insurance policies (P&I, H&M, cargo), classification society certificates, ISPS security certificates, port authority permits, dangerous goods handling permits, fumigation certificates, phytosanitary certificates, health certificates, and free zone permits. Each document type must have configurable alert lead times (e.g., 90, 60, 30, 15, 7 days before expiry). Lead times must be independently configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) as renewal processing times differ by jurisdiction. The system must support both fixed-date expiry and rolling-period expiry based on date of issue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R002	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Dual calendar support for Gregorian and Hijri expiry dates	The system must support document expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar systems. Documents issued by Saudi Arabian authorities (ZATCA, MAWANI, Saudi Customs) and some Qatari authorities frequently use Hijri dates. The system must accurately convert between Hijri and Gregorian calendars using the Umm al-Qura calendar for Saudi dates and astronomical calculation for Qatari dates. Alert calculations must work correctly regardless of which calendar system the expiry date was originally recorded in. The UI must display expiry dates in the user's preferred calendar system with the alternate calendar date shown in parentheses. All date comparisons, sorting, and filtering operations must handle both calendar systems correctly without conversion errors at month boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R003	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Multi-channel alert notification delivery	Expiry alerts must be delivered through multiple channels: in-app notifications, email, SMS, and integration with Microsoft Teams and WhatsApp Business API. Each user must be able to configure their preferred notification channels and quiet hours. Alert messages must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the primary language determined by user preference. Email alerts must include a direct link to the document record and the renewal workflow. SMS alerts must be concise and include the document type, entity name, and days until expiry. The system must support escalation chains where if the primary document owner does not acknowledge the alert within a configurable timeframe, the alert escalates to their supervisor and then to the compliance department, ensuring no critical document expiry is missed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R004	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Automated renewal workflow initiation	When a document reaches its first alert threshold, the system must automatically initiate a renewal workflow in the ERP's workflow engine. The renewal workflow must include: identification of the renewal authority, required supporting documents, estimated processing time, associated fees (in the local currency: QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), and assigned responsible person. For documents requiring government portal submissions (e.g., Qatar Ministry of Commerce, UAE Ministry of Economy, Saudi ZATCA, Indian DGFT), the workflow must include links to the relevant government portals. The system must pre-populate renewal application forms with existing document data where possible, reducing manual data entry. Workflow SLAs must be entity-specific, reflecting each country's typical government processing timeframes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R005	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Compliance dashboard with entity-wide expiry overview	The system must provide a compliance dashboard showing the expiry status of all tracked documents across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The dashboard must display documents in categories: expired, expiring within 7 days, expiring within 30 days, expiring within 90 days, and valid. Each category must show counts and drill-down capability to individual documents. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, document type, department, and responsible person. A traffic-light color coding system (red for expired/critical, amber for warning, green for valid) must be applied. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout and display all monetary values (renewal fees, insurance values) in the entity's local currency. A printable compliance report must be exportable in PDF format for board and regulatory reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R006	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Insurance certificate expiry tracking with coverage gap detection	The system must specifically track marine insurance certificates including Protection & Indemnity (P&I), Hull & Machinery (H&M), cargo insurance, and freight liability policies. For each policy, the system must track: insurer name, policy number, coverage amount, deductible, territorial limits, and vessel/container coverage scope. The system must detect potential coverage gaps where a new policy start date does not immediately follow the previous policy end date, raising critical alerts to prevent uninsured operations. Coverage amounts must be tracked in the policy currency (typically USD) with equivalent values calculated in local currencies (AED, QAR, SAR, INR) using daily exchange rates. The system must validate that coverage amounts meet minimum requirements specified by port authorities at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R007	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Trade license and commercial registration renewal tracking	The system must track trade licenses and commercial registrations for each legal entity across jurisdictions: Qatar Commercial Registration (CR) from Ministry of Commerce, UAE Trade License from Department of Economic Development, Saudi Commercial Registration from Ministry of Commerce, and Indian Import Export Code (IEC) from DGFT. Each registration must track: registration number, issue date, expiry date, registered activities (mapped to shipping operations), registered capital, and authorized signatories. The system must alert when registered activities need updating due to business expansion (e.g., adding dangerous goods handling). For Qatar and UAE free zone entities (e.g., Qatar Free Zones Authority, JAFZA, DMCC), the system must separately track free zone permits which often have different renewal cycles than mainland licenses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R008	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Customs bond and guarantee expiry management	The system must track customs bonds, bank guarantees, and financial securities lodged with customs authorities across all four jurisdictions. Tracked fields must include: bond type (transit bond, warehouse bond, temporary admission bond), issuing bank, bond amount, currency (USD/AED/QAR/SAR/INR), beneficiary (customs authority), effective date, expiry date, and bond utilization percentage. The system must alert when bond utilization exceeds 80% of the guaranteed amount, as this may require bond top-up before expiry. For India-specific customs bonds (e.g., B-17 bonds for customs brokers), the system must track the annual renewal cycle aligned with the Indian financial year (April-March). The system must generate a bond renewal request document pre-populated with current bond details for submission to the issuing bank.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R009	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Port authority permit and access card renewal tracking	The system must track port-specific permits and access credentials including: port entry passes for Hamad Port (issued by Mwani Qatar), Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port (issued by Abu Dhabi Ports/DP World), and JNPT (issued by Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority). Tracked items must include employee port access cards, vehicle entry permits, equipment operator licenses, and restricted area access authorizations. The system must link each permit to the employee or asset record in the ERP, automatically flagging when an employee with an expiring port pass has upcoming scheduled port operations. The system must handle the scenario where a permit renewal requires re-submission of background check documents, medical fitness certificates, or safety training completion records, initiating sub-workflows for each prerequisite document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R010	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Vessel and classification society certificate tracking	For shipping companies operating owned or chartered vessels, the system must track classification society certificates including: Certificate of Class, Safety Equipment Certificate, Safety Construction Certificate, Load Line Certificate, International Oil Pollution Prevention Certificate (IOPP), ISPS Certificate, and Minimum Safe Manning Certificate. Each certificate must track the issuing classification society (Lloyd's, DNV, Bureau Veritas, etc.), issue date, expiry date, survey due dates (annual, intermediate, special), and conditions of class. The system must generate alerts not only for certificate expiry but also for upcoming survey windows. Port State Control inspection history must be linked, as overdue certificates increase PSC inspection risk at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R011	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Automated document validity check before shipment operations	The system must perform automated pre-operation document validity checks before allowing critical shipping operations to proceed. Before a booking confirmation, the system must verify that all required documents (trade license, customs bond, insurance certificates) are currently valid and will remain valid through the expected shipment completion date. Before customs declaration submission, the system must verify that the customs broker license and relevant bonds are valid. The system must block operations when critical documents are expired, displaying a clear message in both Arabic and English identifying the expired documents and linking to the renewal workflow. A configurable override mechanism must exist for authorized users (with audit trail) to proceed with operations when documents are in active renewal status, with a maximum override period of 15 business days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R012	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Renewal cost tracking and budgeting integration	The system must track actual and estimated renewal costs for all managed documents. For each document renewal, the system must capture: government fees, agent/broker fees, attestation costs, translation costs, and any other associated expenses. Costs must be recorded in the local currency of the issuing authority and converted to the entity's base currency using the prevailing exchange rate. The system must generate an annual renewal cost forecast based on historical data and known upcoming renewals, broken down by entity, document type, and quarter. This forecast must integrate with the ERP's budgeting module (if available) to ensure renewal costs are included in departmental budgets. The system must flag any renewal where actual costs exceed the estimated budget by more than 10%, requiring management approval before proceeding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R013	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Regulatory change monitoring and compliance alerts	The system must support a regulatory change management feature where administrators can record changes to document requirements imposed by port authorities, customs agencies, or government bodies across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. When a new document requirement is introduced (e.g., a new type of security certificate required by Hamad Port), the system must allow bulk creation of tracking records for all affected entities. When an existing document requirement is modified (e.g., validity period reduced from 2 years to 1 year by ZATCA), the system must automatically recalculate expiry dates and regenerate alerts for all affected documents. An audit trail must record all regulatory changes with the source reference, effective date, and the administrator who recorded the change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R014	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Dependent document chain management	The system must manage document dependency chains where the renewal of one document depends on the validity of other documents. For example, a customs broker license renewal in India requires a valid GST registration, PAN card, and police clearance certificate. The system must model these dependencies as a directed graph and alert users when a prerequisite document is expiring before the dependent document, which could block the dependent document's renewal. The dependency graph must be configurable per entity and document type. The system must generate a consolidated renewal timeline showing the optimal sequence for renewing interdependent documents, ensuring no document renewal is blocked by an expired prerequisite. Visual dependency mapping must be available in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R015	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Grace period and penalty tracking for overdue renewals	The system must track jurisdiction-specific grace periods allowed after document expiry. For example, Qatar Commercial Registration allows a 30-day grace period, UAE trade licenses may have different grace periods per emirate, and Indian IEC codes have their own renewal windows. The system must calculate and display the grace period end date alongside the expiry date. After the grace period, the system must track applicable penalties and late fees in the relevant local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Penalty rates must be configurable per document type and jurisdiction. The system must generate a financial exposure report showing potential penalties for all documents currently in grace period or overdue status, helping management prioritize renewals based on financial impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R016	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Bulk renewal processing for annual document cycles	The system must support bulk renewal processing for documents that share common renewal cycles, such as annual trade license renewals in January (UAE) or annual customs bond renewals aligned with fiscal years. Administrators must be able to select multiple documents for batch renewal workflow initiation, with the system generating a consolidated checklist of all prerequisite documents and fees. The bulk renewal interface must allow entity-specific processing (e.g., renew all Qatar documents simultaneously) or cross-entity processing for group-level documents. The system must generate a bulk payment request summarizing all renewal fees in a single currency-converted total for treasury approval, showing amounts in both local currencies and a consolidated USD equivalent for multi-entity operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-002-R017	FEAT-034-2-002	MOD-034	Historical renewal audit trail and compliance reporting	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all document renewals including: previous document version, new document version, renewal date, processing duration (days from initiation to completion), renewal cost, approving authority, and any compliance notes. This history must be retained for a minimum of 10 years to satisfy regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions. The system must generate compliance audit reports showing renewal timeliness metrics: percentage of documents renewed before expiry, average renewal processing time, and number of operations affected by expired documents. Reports must be filterable by entity, time period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date ranges), and document type. The audit trail must be tamper-proof with cryptographic hashing to ensure data integrity for regulatory inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R001	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Jurisdiction-specific retention policy engine	The system must implement a configurable retention policy engine that enforces document retention periods specific to each jurisdiction. Qatar Commercial Law requires 10 years for commercial records, UAE Commercial Transactions Law requires 5 years, Saudi Commercial Court Law requires 10 years, and Indian Customs Act Section 14 requires 5 years for import/export records. The system must allow administrators to define retention periods per document type, per entity, and per regulatory requirement. When multiple retention rules apply to the same document (e.g., a customs declaration subject to both customs retention and tax retention), the system must apply the longest applicable retention period. Retention periods must be calculated from configurable trigger dates: document creation date, transaction completion date, or financial year end date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R002	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Automated document lifecycle state management	The system must manage documents through defined lifecycle states: Active, Semi-Active, Archived, Pending Destruction, and Destroyed. Active documents are in current operational use. Semi-Active documents have completed their operational purpose but are within retention period. Archived documents are in long-term storage. Pending Destruction documents have completed their retention period and are awaiting authorized disposal. The system must automatically transition documents between states based on configurable rules linked to shipment completion, financial year closure, or elapsed time. State transitions must generate audit log entries. Documents in Archived and Pending Destruction states must be moved to lower-cost storage tiers while remaining retrievable within defined SLA times (e.g., archived documents retrievable within 4 hours, semi-active within 1 hour).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R003	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Legal hold capability preventing document destruction	The system must support legal hold functionality that prevents the destruction of documents involved in active or anticipated litigation, regulatory investigations, or audit proceedings. Legal holds must be placeable on individual documents, entire shipment document sets, customer document portfolios, or time-period-based document collections. When a legal hold is active, all affected documents must be excluded from automated retention disposal regardless of their retention period expiry. The system must track legal hold metadata: hold name, initiating authority (legal department, external counsel, regulatory body), affected scope, initiation date, and expected duration. Only authorized legal department users must be able to place or release legal holds. The system must generate reports of all documents under legal hold across all four entities for corporate counsel review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R004	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Tiered storage management for cost optimization	The system must implement tiered storage to optimize costs across the document lifecycle. Tier 1 (hot storage) must host active documents with sub-second retrieval times. Tier 2 (warm storage) must host semi-active documents with retrieval times under 5 minutes. Tier 3 (cold storage) must host archived documents with retrieval times under 4 hours. The system must support cloud storage backends (AWS S3 with Glacier, Azure Blob with Cool/Archive tiers, or equivalent) and on-premises storage. Storage tier assignment must be automatic based on document lifecycle state. The system must generate monthly storage cost reports per entity showing volume and cost by tier, with currency conversion to local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for entity-level budgeting. Data residency requirements must be enforced, keeping Qatar entity documents in Qatar-region storage and similarly for other jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R005	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Automated archive migration for completed shipments	Upon shipment completion (final delivery confirmed and all financial transactions settled), the system must automatically initiate the archival process for the shipment's complete document set. The archival process must verify document completeness against a configurable checklist per shipment type (FCL, LCL, breakbulk) and trade lane. Missing documents must be flagged and the archive process paused until resolved or overridden by an authorized user. The archived shipment document package must include a manifest listing all contained documents with their metadata. The system must handle multi-leg shipments (e.g., transshipment at Jebel Ali for cargo moving from JNPT to Hamad Port) by coordinating archival across the entities involved, ensuring each entity's document set is complete before the shipment archive is finalized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R006	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Document destruction workflow with regulatory compliance	When documents reach the end of their retention period and are not under legal hold, the system must initiate a destruction workflow. The workflow must require multi-level approval: document owner, department head, compliance officer, and entity general manager. The destruction request must list all documents proposed for destruction with their retention policy justification. Physical document destruction (for original paper documents stored in warehouses) must generate a destruction certificate signed by authorized witnesses. Digital document destruction must use secure deletion methods compliant with NIST SP 800-88 guidelines. The system must maintain a permanent destruction log recording: document identifiers, destruction date, destruction method, retention policy applied, and approving authorities. This log must be retained indefinitely as proof of compliant disposal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R007	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Physical document warehouse integration and tracking	The system must track physical document locations for original paper documents that must be retained (e.g., original Bills of Lading, stamped customs declarations, signed contracts). The system must record: storage facility name, room/zone, shelf/rack, box number, and position within box. Barcode or RFID labels must be generated for physical document containers. The system must support check-out and check-in workflows when physical documents are retrieved for customs audits, legal proceedings, or operational needs. Location must be tracked per entity with facilities in Doha (Qatar), Dubai/Abu Dhabi (UAE), Riyadh/Jeddah (KSA), and Mumbai/Nhava Sheva (India). The system must generate retrieval requests to warehouse staff with expected turnaround times and must track actual retrieval performance against SLAs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R008	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Retention policy compliance reporting and auditing	The system must generate comprehensive retention compliance reports for internal audit and regulatory inspection purposes. Reports must show: total documents under management by lifecycle state, documents approaching retention expiry within the next quarter, documents overdue for archival, documents under legal hold, and any policy violations (e.g., documents destroyed before retention period completion). Reports must be segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), document type, and department. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri date ranges in report parameters for Saudi and Qatari regulatory reporting. Compliance reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats. The system must maintain report generation history showing which reports were generated, when, by whom, and for what purpose, creating an audit trail of compliance monitoring activities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R009	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Cross-entity document archival for group operations	For shipments involving multiple entities (e.g., a Qatar entity as shipper, UAE entity as transshipment handler, and India entity as destination agent), the system must coordinate archival across entities while maintaining each entity's autonomous retention policies. A master archival record must link all entity-specific document sets for the same shipment. When one entity's retention period expires before another's, the system must prevent destruction of linked documents if the partner entity still requires retention. The system must handle scenarios where entities operate in different fiscal years (Qatar: January-December, India: April-March, UAE: varies by entity) by calculating retention periods based on each entity's relevant fiscal year. Inter-entity archival reports must display amounts in a common reporting currency (USD) alongside local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R010	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Document integrity verification for archived records	The system must ensure the integrity of archived documents throughout their retention period using cryptographic hash verification. Upon archival, each document must be hashed using SHA-256 and the hash stored in a tamper-proof audit log. Periodic integrity checks must run automatically (configurable interval, default monthly) to verify that archived documents have not been altered or corrupted. Any integrity failure must generate critical alerts to the IT security team and compliance department. The system must support digital signatures for archived documents using PKI certificates, meeting the requirements of Qatar's Digital Signature Law, UAE's Electronic Transactions and Trust Services Law, Saudi Arabia's Electronic Transactions Law, and India's Information Technology Act Section 5. Integrity verification reports must be available for regulatory inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R011	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Bulk archival operations with progress tracking	The system must support bulk archival operations for end-of-period processing, such as archiving all documents related to shipments completed in a specific quarter or fiscal year. Bulk operations must process up to 100,000 documents in a single batch. The system must provide a real-time progress tracker showing: total documents, processed documents, successful archives, failed archives, and skipped documents (with reasons). Failed archival attempts must be retried automatically up to three times with exponential backoff before being flagged for manual intervention. The bulk archival process must not impact the performance of active system operations and must be schedulable during off-peak hours. Each bulk operation must generate a completion report summarizing results, processing duration, and storage utilization changes across tiers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R012	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Regulatory audit access with controlled document retrieval	The system must provide a dedicated audit access portal for regulatory authorities (Qatar customs auditors, UAE Federal Tax Authority, ZATCA inspectors, Indian customs audit teams) to request and receive archived documents. Audit access must be time-limited and scope-limited based on a formal audit notification. The system must create a virtual audit room containing only the documents within the audit scope, preventing browsing of unrelated records. All document views and downloads by audit users must be logged with timestamps and IP addresses. The system must support generation of certified true copies with digital watermarks indicating the document is an official system extract. The audit portal must support Arabic and English interfaces and must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats as required by the requesting authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R013	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Data residency and sovereignty compliance for archived documents	The system must enforce data residency requirements ensuring that archived documents are stored in compliance with each jurisdiction's data sovereignty laws. Qatar's Personal Data Privacy Law requires certain data to remain within Qatar. UAE's data protection regulations may require data to stay within UAE or approved jurisdictions. Saudi Arabia's PDPL requires personal data to remain in KSA unless specific conditions are met. India's DPDP Act has data localization requirements for certain categories. The system must maintain a data residency map showing where each document category must be stored. Cross-border document sharing between entities must use encrypted transfer channels with audit logging. The system must prevent archive migration operations that would violate data residency rules, blocking any storage tier change that would move data to a non-compliant geographic region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R014	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Disaster recovery and backup for archived documents	The system must implement disaster recovery procedures for all archived documents with a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of 24 hours and Recovery Time Objective (RTO) of 4 hours for Tier 2 storage and 24 hours for Tier 3 storage. Backups must be stored in a geographically separate location from the primary archive while still complying with data residency requirements. The system must support automated backup verification where a random sample of archived documents is restored and integrity-verified weekly. Backup status must be monitored and any backup failure must generate immediate alerts. The system must maintain a documented and tested disaster recovery plan with annual DR drill results recorded. For multi-entity operations, each entity's disaster recovery must be independently manageable while maintaining cross-entity document linkages in the recovery process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R015	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Archive storage capacity planning and forecasting	The system must provide storage capacity planning tools that forecast future archive storage requirements based on historical document ingestion rates, average document sizes, and retention period configurations. Forecasts must project storage needs for 1, 3, and 5 year horizons, segmented by entity and storage tier. The system must generate cost projections for each forecast period using current storage pricing and configurable annual cost escalation rates. Capacity reports must show storage utilization trends and alert when utilization exceeds 80% of provisioned capacity for any tier or entity. The system must recommend optimal storage tier configurations based on access patterns, showing potential cost savings from migrating infrequently accessed documents to lower-cost tiers. Reports must present costs in local entity currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated USD for group-level planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-003-R016	FEAT-034-2-003	MOD-034	Retention policy versioning and retroactive application	The system must support versioning of retention policies, maintaining a complete history of all policy changes with effective dates, change descriptions, and authorizing personnel. When a retention policy is updated (e.g., a regulatory change extends the required retention period from 5 to 7 years), the system must allow administrators to choose whether to apply the new policy retroactively to existing documents or only to documents created after the effective date. Retroactive application must recalculate retention expiry dates for all affected documents and regenerate any pending destruction workflows that are now invalid under the new policy. The system must generate an impact analysis report before retroactive application, showing the number of affected documents per entity, the change in retention periods, and the estimated additional storage cost. Policy changes must require dual authorization from compliance and IT departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R001	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Natural language search in Arabic and English	The system must support natural language search queries in both Arabic and English, allowing users to type queries such as 'find all bills of lading for shipments from Jebel Ali to JNPT in Q3 2025' or the Arabic equivalent. The NLP engine must understand shipping domain terminology including port names (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer), INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, DDP), document types, and common shipping abbreviations (B/L, D/O, THC, BAF). The system must handle code-switching where users mix Arabic and English in a single query, which is common in GCC business communication. Query understanding must include date parsing supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date expressions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R002	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Semantic search using vector embeddings	The system must implement semantic search using document vector embeddings that capture the meaning and context of document content beyond simple keyword matching. The embedding model must be trained or fine-tuned on shipping domain documents to understand industry-specific terminology and relationships. For example, a search for 'hazardous cargo certificates' must also return dangerous goods declarations and IMDG documentation even if those exact keywords are not used. The system must generate embeddings for both the full document text and structured metadata, combining both for relevance ranking. Embeddings must be generated in both Arabic and English with cross-lingual retrieval capability (an Arabic query must find English documents and vice versa). The vector database must support at least 10 million document embeddings with sub-second query response times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R003	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Faceted search with shipping-specific filters	The system must provide faceted search allowing users to progressively narrow results using shipping-specific filters: document type, entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port of loading, port of discharge, vessel name, voyage number, container number, customer name, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), currency, INCOTERMS, shipment status, and document lifecycle state (active/archived). Facets must dynamically update to show available filter values and document counts based on the current search results. The system must support saved filter combinations as named search presets that users can share within their entity. Multi-select must be supported within each facet (e.g., selecting multiple ports). The UI must render facets correctly in Arabic RTL layout with Arabic translations for all filter labels and values where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R004	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Customs Declaration Compliance Reporting	The system must generate customs compliance reports for all four port jurisdictions: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Reports must include container movement summaries by HS code classification, INCOTERMS distribution analysis (FOB, CIF, CFR, DDP), duty payment reconciliation in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and bonded warehouse inventory aging. The system must flag discrepancies between declared and actual container weights exceeding SOLAS VGM tolerance thresholds of 5% or 500 kg whichever is less. Reports must be exportable in formats accepted by each port's customs authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R004	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Cross-entity search with access control enforcement	The system must support cross-entity search allowing authorized users to search documents across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) in a single query. Search results must respect the user's role-based access control permissions, only returning documents the user is authorized to view. Entity-level data isolation must be enforced such that a Qatar entity user cannot see UAE entity documents unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. The search results must clearly indicate which entity each document belongs to using entity-specific color coding or badges. For group-level management users with cross-entity access, search results must show entity-wise result counts and allow toggling entity visibility. The system must log all cross-entity search activities for compliance auditing, recording the searching user, query parameters, entities searched, and number of results accessed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R005	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Full-text search within document content including OCR text	The system must provide full-text search capability across all document content, including OCR-extracted text from scanned documents, native text from digital documents, and structured metadata fields. The full-text search engine must support: exact phrase matching, wildcard searches, proximity searches (finding words within N words of each other), Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT), and field-specific searches (e.g., searching only within B/L numbers or only within consignee names). For Arabic text, the search must handle morphological variations, removing prefixes and suffixes to match root words. The search index must be updated in near real-time (within 60 seconds of document upload or modification). Search highlighting must show matching terms in context within the result snippets, supporting both Arabic and English text highlighting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R006	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	AI-powered document similarity and related document suggestions	When a user views a document, the system must automatically suggest related documents using AI-based similarity analysis. Related documents must be identified through multiple dimensions: same shipment (sharing booking number, B/L number, or container numbers), same trade lane (same port pair), same customer, similar document type, and similar content. The similarity engine must use the vector embeddings from the semantic search module to find contextually related documents even when they share no common metadata fields. Related document suggestions must be ranked by relevance and grouped by relationship type. The system must also detect potential duplicate documents (same content uploaded multiple times, possibly with different filenames) and alert users to prevent redundancy. Similarity analysis must work across entities for multi-entity shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R007	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Saved searches and automated search alerts	Users must be able to save search queries as named saved searches with all parameters including natural language query, facet filters, sort order, and entity scope. Saved searches must be executable with a single click from the user's dashboard. The system must support automated search alerts where saved searches are executed periodically (configurable: hourly, daily, weekly) and users are notified when new documents matching their criteria appear. Alert notifications must be delivered via email and in-app notifications in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English). Saved searches must be shareable within teams and must support parameterized templates where date ranges automatically adjust relative to the current date. Each user must be able to maintain up to 50 saved searches, and team-shared searches must be manageable by team leads.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R008	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Search result preview with inline document viewer	Search results must include an inline document preview capability allowing users to view documents without leaving the search interface. The preview must support: PDF rendering, image display (TIFF, JPEG, PNG), and structured data display for electronic documents. The preview must highlight search terms within the document content, scrolling to the first match location. For multi-page documents, the preview must show the page containing the search match with navigation to view other pages. The preview must support zoom, rotation, and annotation tools. For Arabic documents, the viewer must correctly render RTL text and Arabic typography. The preview must load within 3 seconds for documents up to 10MB. A side-by-side comparison mode must allow users to view two documents simultaneously for verification purposes, which is essential when comparing original and amended shipping documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R009	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Voice search capability for hands-free operation	The system must support voice-based search input in both Arabic and English for hands-free operation scenarios common in port and warehouse environments. The voice recognition engine must accurately interpret shipping terminology, port names, vessel names, and alphanumeric identifiers (container numbers, B/L numbers) in both languages. The system must provide visual confirmation of the interpreted query before executing the search, allowing users to correct misinterpretations. Voice search must function in noisy environments typical of port operations at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, with noise-cancellation capabilities. The system must support voice commands for navigating search results (next result, open document, go back). Voice search activity must be logged with the same audit trail requirements as text-based searches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R010	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Search analytics and usage intelligence	The system must collect and analyze search usage data to improve search quality and understand information needs. Analytics must track: most frequent search queries, search terms with zero results, average search response time, click-through rates on search results, most frequently accessed document types, and search abandonment patterns. The system must use zero-result queries to identify gaps in document indexing or OCR quality. Search analytics dashboards must be available to administrators showing usage patterns per entity, per department, and per user role. The system must use machine learning to identify trending search topics and proactively surface relevant documents on user dashboards. Analytics data must be retained for 12 months and must be anonymizable for sharing across entities while complying with data privacy requirements of each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R005	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Digital Signature and Authentication for Submissions	The system must support digital signature standards required by each jurisdiction for regulatory submissions. For KSA, the system must integrate with ZATCA's Fatoora platform using ECDSA cryptographic signing with SHA-256 hashing for e-invoicing compliance. For UAE, submissions to FTA must use the authorized digital certificate format. For Qatar, MOTC submissions must support the Qatar National PKI infrastructure. For India, the system must support DSC Class 2 and Class 3 digital signatures for DGFT and customs filings. All signed documents must be timestamped using RFC 3161 compliant timestamping authorities and archived with tamper-evident seals for a minimum retention period of 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R011	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Bulk document retrieval and export for audits and legal requests	The system must support bulk document retrieval where users can select multiple documents from search results and export them as a single compressed archive (ZIP) with an index manifest. The manifest must list all exported documents with their metadata in both Arabic and English. Export formats must include: original format, PDF/A for long-term archival, and a structured metadata CSV/Excel file. For customs audit requests at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, the system must support generating audit-ready document packages organized by the auditor's required structure (by date, by shipment, by document type). Bulk exports must support up to 10,000 documents per request. Large exports must be processed asynchronously with notification upon completion. Export packages must include a digital signature certificate attesting to document authenticity and completeness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R012	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Contextual search suggestions and auto-completion	The search interface must provide real-time suggestions and auto-completion as the user types, drawing from: recent search history, popular searches within the user's entity, document metadata values (customer names, vessel names, port names, B/L numbers), and shipping industry terminology. Suggestions must appear within 200 milliseconds of user input. The auto-complete engine must support both Arabic and English input simultaneously, recognizing when the user switches languages mid-query. For structured identifiers like container numbers (e.g., MSCU1234567), B/L numbers, and booking references, the system must offer format-aware suggestions that validate the identifier structure. The suggestion ranking must be personalized based on the user's role, entity, and search history, prioritizing suggestions most relevant to the user's typical document access patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R013	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Document retrieval from archived storage tiers	When search results include documents in archived (cold) storage tiers, the system must transparently handle retrieval with appropriate user communication. For Tier 2 (warm) documents, retrieval must complete within 5 minutes with a progress indicator. For Tier 3 (cold/Glacier) documents, the system must display the estimated retrieval time (up to 4 hours), offer to notify the user when the document is available, and allow the user to continue searching while retrieval is in progress. The system must cache recently retrieved archived documents in hot storage for a configurable period (default 24 hours) to avoid repeated retrieval delays. Retrieval requests must be queued and prioritized based on user role and request urgency. The system must track retrieval costs per entity as cold storage retrievals incur additional cloud provider charges, reporting costs in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R014	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	AI-powered document summarization in search results	The system must generate AI-powered summaries for documents in search results, providing users with a concise overview without needing to open the full document. Summaries must highlight the key information relevant to the search query: for a B/L, the summary must show shipper, consignee, port pair, container count, and cargo description; for an invoice, the summary must show parties, amount, currency, and INCOTERMS; for customs declarations, the summary must show declarant, HS codes, and duty amounts. Summaries must be generated in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) regardless of the document's original language, providing cross-lingual summarization. The system must cache generated summaries to avoid reprocessing and must regenerate summaries when document content is updated. Summary generation must complete within 2 seconds per document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R015	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Offline search capability with local index synchronization	The system must provide offline search capability for field operations at ports and container yards where network connectivity may be unavailable. The offline module must maintain a local search index synchronized from the central system, containing metadata and text indices for the most recently accessed and most frequently needed documents. The local index must support a configurable scope (e.g., last 90 days of documents, specific shipments, specific customers) with a maximum local index size of 10GB. Synchronization must occur incrementally when connectivity is available, transferring only delta changes. Offline search must support the same query syntax as online search (natural language, faceted filters, Boolean operators) but limited to the locally indexed document set. Search results for documents not available locally must be flagged with an indication that the full document requires connectivity to retrieve.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R016	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Search API for integration with external systems	The system must expose a RESTful search API enabling external systems (customs portals, shipping line platforms, banking systems, trade finance platforms) to search and retrieve documents programmatically. The API must support authentication via OAuth 2.0 and API keys with rate limiting per client. API endpoints must accept JSON request bodies with the same search parameters available in the UI: natural language query, faceted filters, pagination, and sort order. Response payloads must include document metadata, relevance scores, and download URLs for authorized documents. The API must enforce the same access control rules as the UI, with API clients mapped to entity and role permissions. API usage must be metered and logged for billing and compliance purposes. The API must support webhook callbacks for asynchronous operations like bulk retrieval and archived document restore, notifying the calling system when results are ready.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R017	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Search performance optimization and scalability	The system must deliver search results within 2 seconds for simple queries and within 5 seconds for complex multi-faceted queries across a document corpus of up to 50 million documents. The search infrastructure must scale horizontally to handle up to 500 concurrent search users across all four entities. Search index sharding must be implemented by entity and document date range to optimize query routing. The system must implement query result caching with intelligent cache invalidation when underlying documents change. Search performance must be monitored with automated alerts when response times exceed SLA thresholds. The system must support query optimization suggestions when users construct inefficient queries. Load testing results must demonstrate sustained performance under peak load conditions corresponding to end-of-month customs filing deadlines when all four port operations simultaneously generate heavy search traffic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-034-2-004-R018	FEAT-034-2-004	MOD-034	Search audit trail and compliance logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all search activities for compliance and security purposes. The audit log must capture: user identity, timestamp (UTC and local timezone), search query text, applied filters, result count, documents viewed from results, documents downloaded, and session duration. For cross-entity searches, the log must record which entities were searched and which entity's documents were accessed. Audit logs must be tamper-proof using append-only storage with cryptographic chaining. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 5 years across all jurisdictions. The system must support audit log export for regulatory inspections by Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian authorities. Suspicious search patterns (e.g., bulk downloading, accessing documents outside normal job scope, after-hours access) must trigger automated alerts to the information security team for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R001	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Annual Audit Plan Creation with AI Risk Scoring	The system must allow authorized audit managers to create annual audit plans for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with AI-generated risk scoring that prioritizes auditable areas based on historical findings, transaction volumes at ports (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHV), and regulatory change indicators. The AI engine must analyze at least 24 months of operational data including container throughput, customs clearance rates, demurrage incidents, and financial transaction anomalies to produce risk heat maps. Plans must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar scheduling with entity-specific fiscal year configurations. Each plan must be approvable through a configurable workflow with at least three approval tiers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R002	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Universe Definition and Categorization	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit universe that catalogs all auditable processes, departments, entities, and port operations across the shipping ERP. Each auditable unit must be tagged with risk category (high, medium, low), entity affiliation (QA, AE, SA, IN), port code association (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNHV), functional area (operations, finance, customs, logistics, HR, IT), and last audit date. The audit universe must support hierarchical grouping so that container terminal operations, vessel scheduling, bill of lading processing, INCOTERMS compliance, and freight forwarding can be organized under parent categories. The system must allow bulk import of auditable units via Excel or CSV with Arabic column header support and RTL text fields for Arabic entity names and descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R003	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Engagement Scheduling and Resource Allocation	The system must support scheduling individual audit engagements within the approved annual plan, assigning lead auditors and team members based on availability, qualifications, and entity-specific expertise. Resource allocation must consider auditor certifications (CIA, CISA, ISO 27001 Lead Auditor), language proficiency (Arabic and English), and jurisdiction familiarity for Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping regulations. The scheduling engine must detect conflicts when auditors are assigned to overlapping engagements and must respect entity-specific public holiday calendars including Hijri-based holidays such as Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. Engagement timelines must display in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats based on user locale preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R004	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Fieldwork Documentation and Working Papers	The system must provide structured working paper templates for audit fieldwork that capture testing procedures, sample selections, evidence attachments, and preliminary findings. Working papers must support bilingual documentation in Arabic and English with RTL text rendering for Arabic narrative sections. Auditors must be able to attach evidence files including scanned documents, photographs of container inspections, screenshots of ERP transactions, and exported reports in PDF, JPEG, PNG, and XLSX formats with a maximum file size of 50MB per attachment. Working papers must maintain version control with full audit trail showing who modified what and when, and must support offline data entry with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored at remote port locations where internet access may be intermittent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R005	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Finding Classification and Severity Rating	The system must allow auditors to document findings with a structured classification framework that includes finding type (control deficiency, non-compliance, process gap, fraud indicator), severity rating (critical, high, medium, low, observation), and affected business process (container booking, customs declaration, bill of lading issuance, freight invoicing, demurrage calculation, container tracking). Each finding must reference the specific control objective tested, the condition observed, the criteria or standard violated (such as ISM Code, ISPS Code, SOLAS regulations, local customs regulations, or internal SOPs), the root cause analysis, and the potential financial or operational impact quantified in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to USD at the prevailing exchange rate for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R006	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Corrective Action Plan Tracking and Follow-up	The system must enable management to submit corrective action plans (CAPs) for each audit finding, specifying the responsible person, target completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and detailed remediation steps. CAPs must have configurable status workflows (draft, submitted, accepted, in-progress, implemented, verified, closed) with automated email and in-app notifications at each status transition. The system must support automated follow-up reminders at configurable intervals (e.g., 7, 14, 30 days before target date) and must escalate overdue CAPs to the next management level after a configurable grace period. Auditors must be able to verify CAP implementation by attaching verification evidence and documenting re-testing results. The system must calculate CAP closure rates by entity, department, and finding severity for executive dashboard reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R007	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	AI-Powered Continuous Auditing and Anomaly Detection	The system must provide AI-driven continuous auditing capabilities that automatically monitor high-risk transaction streams across all four entities for anomalies and potential control failures. Monitored transaction types must include container booking modifications after cutoff, unusual demurrage waivers exceeding entity-specific thresholds (e.g., above 500 QAR for Qatar, 500 AED for UAE, 500 SAR for KSA, 5000 INR for India), freight rate deviations beyond two standard deviations from contracted rates, duplicate payment requests, and unauthorized changes to master data such as customer credit limits or INCOTERMS defaults. The AI engine must generate automated exception reports with confidence scores and must learn from auditor feedback on false positives to continuously improve detection accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R008	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Report Generation with Bilingual Support	The system must generate formal audit reports in both Arabic and English, with configurable templates that include executive summary, scope and methodology, detailed findings with severity ratings, management responses, corrective action plans, and appendices. Reports must support RTL layout for Arabic versions with proper formatting of tables, charts, and numbering systems including both Western Arabic numerals and Eastern Arabic-Indic numerals based on locale settings. The report generation engine must support automated translation suggestions using AI while allowing manual override by the audit team. Reports must be exportable in PDF, DOCX, and XLSX formats and must include digital signatures from the lead auditor and audit director with timestamps in the entity's local timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R009	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Committee Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide an executive dashboard for the audit committee that displays key audit metrics across all four entities including total audits planned versus completed, finding trends by severity over rolling 12-month periods, open corrective action aging analysis, and risk coverage maps showing audited versus unaudited areas of the audit universe. The dashboard must support drill-down from consolidated multi-entity views to individual entity, port, or department levels. All monetary values on the dashboard must be displayable in the user's preferred currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with real-time conversion using system exchange rates. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts and must support export to PDF for offline board meeting presentation packages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R010	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Workflow Approval and Digital Authorization	The system must implement configurable multi-level approval workflows for audit plans, engagement letters, draft reports, and final reports. Approval workflows must be entity-specific, allowing Qatar operations to require three approval levels while India operations may require four levels based on local governance requirements. Each approval step must capture the approver's digital signature, timestamp, and optional comments in Arabic or English. The system must support delegation of approval authority during approver absence with configurable delegation rules and expiry dates. Rejected items must be returned to the originator with mandatory rejection reasons, and the system must maintain a complete approval audit trail that is immutable and tamper-evident for regulatory examination purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R011	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Integration with ERP Modules for Audit Data Extraction	The system must integrate with other ERP modules including Financial Accounting, Container Operations, Customs & Trade Compliance, Vessel Management, and Freight Billing to extract transactional data for audit sampling and testing. Data extraction must support configurable filters by entity, port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), transaction type, currency, customer, and vessel. Extracted datasets must be importable into audit working papers with automatic cross-referencing to source transactions. The integration must use the internal service mesh architecture with proper authentication and must respect data access controls so auditors from the Qatar entity cannot access raw transactional data from the India entity without explicit cross-entity audit authorization granted by the respective entity compliance officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R012	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Sampling Methodology Configuration	The system must support multiple audit sampling methodologies including statistical sampling (random, systematic, stratified), judgmental sampling, and AI-recommended sampling based on risk factors. For statistical sampling, the system must calculate required sample sizes based on population size, confidence level (90%, 95%, 99%), tolerable error rate, and expected deviation rate. The AI sampling engine must prioritize transactions with higher risk indicators such as large-value container bookings exceeding 100,000 USD equivalent, transactions involving sanctioned country flags, shipments with hazardous cargo classifications (IMO classes 1-9), and transactions processed outside normal business hours. Sample selection must be documented with full methodology justification for regulatory review and must be reproducible with the same parameters and random seed values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R013	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Quality Assurance and Peer Review	The system must support a quality assurance program for the internal audit function itself, including peer review checklists, supervisory review sign-offs on working papers, and periodic external quality assessment tracking. Each working paper must have a preparer and reviewer sign-off with timestamp before it can be finalized. The system must track quality metrics such as average time per audit phase, finding acceptance rate by management, CAP implementation timeliness, and stakeholder satisfaction survey scores. Quality review findings must be tracked separately from operational audit findings with their own corrective action workflow. The system must support the Institute of Internal Auditors (IIA) Quality Assurance and Improvement Program (QAIP) framework requirements including both internal and external assessment scheduling and results documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R014	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Time and Expense Tracking	The system must allow audit team members to log time spent on each audit engagement broken down by audit phase (planning, fieldwork, reporting, follow-up) and activity type (document review, interview, data analysis, travel, administration). Time entries must capture the date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, hours worked, and optional narrative descriptions in Arabic or English. The system must track audit-related expenses including travel costs for inter-port audit visits (e.g., Qatar team auditing Jebel Ali operations), accommodation, per diem allowances in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and other direct costs. Budget versus actual analysis must be available at the engagement, entity, and annual plan levels with variance explanations required when actual costs exceed budgeted amounts by more than 10 percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R015	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Audit Evidence Chain of Custody and Integrity	The system must maintain a tamper-evident chain of custody for all audit evidence including documents, data extracts, photographs, and interview records. Each piece of evidence must be cryptographically hashed upon upload using SHA-256 to ensure integrity verification at any future point. The system must log every access to evidence files including views, downloads, and shares with user identity, timestamp, IP address, and action type. Evidence must be retained according to entity-specific retention policies: minimum 7 years for Qatar per Qatar Financial Markets Authority requirements, 5 years for UAE per ADGM regulations, 10 years for KSA per ZATCA requirements, and 8 years for India per Companies Act 2013. The system must prevent deletion of evidence linked to open or recently closed audits and must support legal hold functionality to preserve evidence when litigation or regulatory investigation is anticipated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-001-R016	FEAT-035-1-001	MOD-035	Offline Audit Capability for Remote Port Locations	The system must provide offline audit functionality for auditors conducting fieldwork at port facilities where internet connectivity may be unreliable or unavailable, particularly at container yards, vessel berths, and warehouse inspection areas at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The offline module must allow auditors to view pre-downloaded audit programs, complete checklists, capture photographic evidence with GPS tagging, and draft findings on mobile devices (iOS and Android tablets). All offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and must synchronize automatically with the central system when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution logic that preserves the most recent version while flagging discrepancies for manual review. The offline capability must support a minimum of 30 days of operation without connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R001	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Multi-Jurisdiction Compliance Obligation Registry	The system must maintain a master registry of all regulatory compliance obligations applicable to container shipping operations across Qatar (Qatar Maritime Authority, Qatar Financial Markets Authority, Ministry of Commerce and Industry), UAE (Federal Transport Authority, Abu Dhabi Ports regulations, Dubai Maritime City Authority, DMCA), KSA (Saudi Ports Authority Mawani, ZATCA, Ministry of Transport and Logistic Services), and India (Directorate General of Shipping, CBIC Customs, Major Port Authorities Act). Each obligation must be categorized by jurisdiction, regulatory body, obligation type (filing, reporting, license renewal, certification, inspection), applicable entity, frequency (one-time, monthly, quarterly, semi-annual, annual), and consequence of non-compliance including potential monetary penalties in local currency and operational impact such as port access restrictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R002	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Dual Calendar Display with Hijri and Gregorian Support	The system must display all compliance deadlines in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar formats simultaneously, with the primary calendar format configurable per entity (Hijri primary for KSA, Gregorian primary for India, user-selectable for Qatar and UAE). Calendar views must support daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual perspectives with color-coded status indicators: green for on-track obligations (more than 30 days to deadline), yellow for approaching deadlines (15-30 days), orange for urgent deadlines (7-14 days), and red for overdue items. The calendar must correctly handle Hijri month variations and must support the Umm al-Qura calendar variant used officially in Saudi Arabia. Weekend configurations must be entity-specific: Friday-Saturday for Qatar, UAE, and KSA; Saturday-Sunday for India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R003	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Automated Compliance Deadline Reminders and Escalation	The system must generate automated reminders for upcoming compliance deadlines at configurable intervals, with default escalation tiers: initial reminder at 60 days before deadline to the assigned compliance officer, second reminder at 30 days to the compliance officer and their manager, third reminder at 14 days escalating to the entity compliance director, and critical alert at 7 days escalating to the Chief Compliance Officer and entity General Manager. Reminders must be delivered via email, in-app notifications, and optionally SMS for critical alerts. Reminder content must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the primary language matching the recipient's profile preference. The system must track reminder acknowledgment and must re-send unacknowledged reminders after 48 hours with escalation to the next tier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R004	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Regulatory Change Monitoring and Impact Assessment	The system must support tracking regulatory changes that affect container shipping operations, including new regulations, amendments to existing laws, and regulatory guidance updates from maritime authorities across all four jurisdictions. Each regulatory change entry must capture the issuing authority, effective date (Hijri and Gregorian), summary in Arabic and English, affected business processes (container operations, customs clearance, freight billing, vessel management, hazardous cargo handling), and compliance gap analysis documenting what operational or system changes are required. The system must support AI-assisted impact assessment that cross-references new regulations against the existing compliance obligation registry and policy library to identify potential gaps. Impact assessments must be routable through an approval workflow where the entity compliance officer confirms the analysis and assigns action items with target completion dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R005	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	License and Certification Expiry Tracking	The system must track all business licenses, operating permits, and professional certifications required for container shipping operations including port operator licenses, customs broker licenses, freight forwarder registrations, dangerous goods handling certifications, ISO certifications (9001, 14001, 27001, 28000), ISPS Code compliance certificates, ISM Code Document of Compliance, and vessel class certificates. Each license or certification must store the issuing authority, issue date, expiry date (in Hijri and Gregorian), renewal lead time, associated entity and port, responsible person, and digital copy of the certificate. The system must automatically calculate renewal initiation dates by subtracting the configured lead time from expiry dates and must generate renewal tasks assigned to the responsible person. The system must flag any license that expires without documented renewal as a critical compliance breach.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R006	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Statutory Filing Submission Tracking and Evidence Capture	The system must track the submission status of all statutory filings including customs declarations, port authority statistical reports, tax filings (VAT returns for UAE and KSA, GST returns for India, corporate tax returns for all entities), financial regulatory reports, and maritime safety reports. Each filing must capture the submission deadline, actual submission date and time, submission method (electronic portal, physical submission, email), confirmation or acknowledgment reference number, and copies of submitted documents. The system must support marking filings as submitted with mandatory attachment of submission evidence such as portal screenshots, email confirmations, or stamped receipt copies. For filings involving monetary values, the system must record the filing currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and equivalent USD amount for consolidated compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R001	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Port and Terminal Master Data Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive port and terminal master data registry containing UN/LOCODE identifiers (QADOH for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INNSA for JNPT), terminal codes, berth numbers, draft restrictions, crane availability, operating hours, and contact details. Each port record must support entity-specific operational parameters such as free storage days (typically 3-7 days varying by port), demurrage tariff schedules, and customs cut-off times. Port data must be editable only by users with Master Data Administrator role and all changes must trigger a review workflow before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R007	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Calendar Integration with External Regulatory Portals	The system must support integration with external regulatory portals and e-government platforms where available, including UAE FTA e-services for VAT filing, KSA ZATCA Fatoora platform for e-invoicing compliance, India ICEGATE for customs filings, and Qatar Ministry of Commerce and Industry online services. Integration must support automated retrieval of filing deadlines, submission status confirmations, and penalty notifications where APIs are available. For portals without API access, the system must support manual deadline entry with AI-assisted suggestions based on historical filing patterns. The integration layer must handle authentication token management, session timeout recovery, and must log all integration transactions with timestamp, status code, and response payload for troubleshooting. The system must gracefully degrade to manual mode when external portal connectivity is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R008	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Status Dashboard with Multi-Entity Consolidation	The system must provide a real-time compliance status dashboard showing overall compliance health across all four entities with drill-down capability to individual entity, jurisdiction, and obligation type levels. Dashboard metrics must include total obligations tracked, obligations due within the next 30, 60, and 90 days, overdue obligations count and aging, compliance rate percentage (on-time submissions divided by total due), expiring licenses within 90 days, and pending regulatory change impact assessments. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNHV), regulatory body, and obligation category. All dashboard elements must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic users and LTR for English users, with automatic layout switching based on the active user interface language setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R009	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Task Assignment and Workflow Management	The system must allow compliance managers to create and assign compliance tasks to team members for each regulatory obligation, with task details including description in Arabic and English, priority level, due date synchronized with the compliance calendar, required deliverables, and approval chain. Tasks must support status tracking through configurable stages (assigned, in-progress, under-review, approved, submitted, confirmed). The workflow engine must enforce sequential stage progression and must require mandatory fields at each transition, such as submission evidence at the submitted stage. Task reassignment must be supported with full history tracking of all assignments. The system must calculate task completion metrics by assignee, entity, and obligation type, and must flag individuals with consistently late task completion for management attention through automated monthly performance summaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R010	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Penalty and Fine Tracking for Non-Compliance	The system must track all penalties, fines, and sanctions imposed by regulatory authorities for non-compliance events, capturing the regulatory body, violation reference number, violation description in Arabic and English, penalty amount in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD equivalent calculation, penalty date, payment deadline, payment status, and root cause analysis linking back to the specific compliance obligation that was breached. The system must support appeal tracking for contested penalties with appeal submission date, hearing dates, legal counsel assignment, and outcome recording. Historical penalty data must be analyzable by entity, jurisdiction, violation type, and time period to identify trends. The system must calculate total cost of non-compliance including direct penalties, legal fees, and estimated operational impact for executive reporting and must integrate with the Financial Accounting module for penalty payment processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R011	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	AI-Driven Compliance Risk Prediction	The system must employ AI algorithms to predict compliance risks by analyzing historical compliance performance, upcoming deadline density, resource availability, regulatory change velocity, and external risk indicators such as port congestion levels and geopolitical developments affecting maritime trade routes through the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean. The AI engine must generate compliance risk scores for each entity on a scale of 1-100, with scores above 75 triggering automated alerts to the Chief Compliance Officer. Risk predictions must consider seasonal patterns such as increased filing obligations during fiscal year-end periods and reduced staff availability during Ramadan and major public holidays across all four jurisdictions. The AI model must be retrained monthly using the latest compliance performance data and must provide explainable risk factors for each prediction to support informed decision-making by compliance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R012	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Regulatory Body Contact and Communication Management	The system must maintain a directory of regulatory body contacts for each jurisdiction including authority name in Arabic and English, department, contact person, title, phone number, email, physical address, and office hours. The system must log all formal communications with regulatory authorities including correspondence reference numbers, dates, subject, direction (inbound or outbound), communication channel (letter, email, portal message, meeting), and attached documents. Communication logs must be linkable to specific compliance obligations, audit findings, or penalty records. The system must support generating formal correspondence templates in Arabic and English with entity letterhead, proper salutations following local business customs, and reference numbering conforming to each entity's document control standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R013	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Training Tracking and Certification Management	The system must track compliance-related training requirements for employees across all four entities, including mandatory training topics (anti-money laundering, sanctions screening, customs regulations, port safety, ISPS Code awareness, hazardous goods handling), required frequency of refresher training, and employee completion status. Each training record must capture the employee name, entity, role, training topic, training provider, completion date, certification validity period, and next renewal date. The system must automatically identify employees with expiring or expired compliance certifications and must generate notifications to both the employee and their manager. Training completion rates must be reportable by entity, department, and training topic, and must feed into the compliance dashboard to show overall workforce compliance readiness percentages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R014	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Cross-Entity Compliance Harmonization Reporting	The system must generate cross-entity compliance harmonization reports that compare compliance maturity, obligation fulfillment rates, and regulatory burden across the four operating entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Reports must identify common obligations that apply across multiple jurisdictions such as IMO conventions, SOLAS requirements, and IMDG Code compliance, and must highlight entity-specific obligations unique to each jurisdiction. The harmonization report must recommend standardization opportunities where entities can adopt common compliance procedures to reduce duplication of effort. Reports must present financial data in a unified currency (USD) with breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and must be generated in both Arabic and English versions with proper RTL formatting for Arabic output including correctly mirrored charts and right-aligned table columns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R015	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Document Version Control and Repository	The system must maintain a centralized repository of all compliance-related documents including regulatory texts, official guidance notes, authority circulars, compliance certificates, filing records, and correspondence. Each document must support versioning with check-in and check-out functionality to prevent concurrent editing conflicts. Documents must be searchable by full-text search in both Arabic and English, with metadata filters for jurisdiction, regulatory body, document type, effective date range, and entity applicability. The repository must support document classification using a configurable taxonomy aligned with the compliance obligation registry. Access controls must enforce entity-level and classification-level restrictions so that confidential regulatory correspondence for one entity is not accessible to users from another entity without explicit cross-entity access grants approved by both entity compliance officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R016	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Automated Compliance Obligation Recurrence Scheduling	The system must automatically generate recurring compliance obligation instances based on configured schedules, supporting monthly, quarterly, semi-annual, annual, and custom recurrence patterns. For Hijri-based deadlines used in KSA and optionally Qatar, the system must correctly calculate recurring dates accounting for the varying length of Hijri months (29 or 30 days) and the 11-day annual drift relative to the Gregorian calendar. Each generated instance must inherit the parent obligation's assigned responsible person, approval workflow, required deliverables, and escalation rules, while allowing per-instance overrides. The system must generate all instances for at least 18 months in advance and must automatically extend the schedule as time progresses. Cancelled or suspended obligations must stop generating future instances while preserving historical records of previously completed instances for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-002-R017	FEAT-035-1-002	MOD-035	Compliance Breach Incident Recording and Investigation	The system must support recording compliance breach incidents when regulatory obligations are not met, capturing the breached obligation, breach discovery date, breach occurrence date, severity assessment (critical, major, minor), root cause category (process failure, system error, human error, resource constraint, external factor), detailed description in Arabic and English, immediate containment actions taken, and long-term remediation plan. Each breach must trigger a configurable investigation workflow involving the compliance officer, entity manager, and potentially the legal department. The investigation must document evidence collected, interviews conducted, timeline reconstruction, and final determination of accountability. Breach records must be linked to any resulting regulatory penalties and must feed into the risk register module for risk score recalculation. The system must produce monthly and quarterly breach trend reports showing breach frequency, severity distribution, and mean time to resolution by entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R001	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Enterprise Risk Register with Multi-Entity Structure	The system must maintain a centralized enterprise risk register that organizes risks hierarchically by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port facility (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHV), and functional area (container operations, vessel management, customs compliance, financial management, IT security, HSE). Each risk entry must have a unique identifier, risk title in Arabic and English, detailed description, risk category (strategic, operational, financial, compliance, reputational, environmental), risk owner, date identified (Hijri and Gregorian), current status (open, mitigating, accepted, closed), and linkages to related risks for correlation tracking. The risk register must support a minimum of 10,000 risk entries without performance degradation and must provide instantaneous search and filter capabilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R002	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Assessment Methodology Configuration	The system must support configurable risk assessment methodologies including qualitative assessment (5x5 likelihood-impact matrix), semi-quantitative assessment (scored scales with weighted factors), and quantitative assessment (monetary value-at-risk calculations). The likelihood scale must be configurable with default values: rare (1-10% probability), unlikely (11-30%), possible (31-50%), likely (51-70%), and almost certain (71-100%). The impact scale must cover financial impact thresholds configurable per entity in local currency (e.g., minor below 50,000 QAR for Qatar, moderate 50,000-500,000 QAR, major 500,000-5,000,000 QAR, catastrophic above 5,000,000 QAR with equivalent thresholds in AED, SAR, and INR for respective entities). Risk scores must be calculated as likelihood multiplied by impact, and the system must support custom weighting factors for different impact dimensions including financial, operational, reputational, and safety impacts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R003	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Heat Map Visualization with Interactive Drill-Down	The system must generate visual risk heat maps displaying risks on a color-coded matrix with likelihood on one axis and impact on the other, using a traffic light color scheme (green for low risks, yellow for medium, orange for high, red for critical). Heat maps must be filterable by entity, port, risk category, risk owner, and date range. Users must be able to click on any cell in the heat map to drill down and view the list of individual risks within that likelihood-impact combination. The heat map must support comparative views showing risk profile changes between assessment periods, with arrows or indicators showing risk movement direction. The visualization must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts, with axis labels and legends displayed in the active user interface language (Arabic or English). Heat maps must be exportable as PNG or SVG images for inclusion in board presentation materials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R004	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Treatment Plan Documentation and Tracking	The system must support documenting risk treatment plans for each identified risk, with treatment strategy options including risk avoidance, risk mitigation, risk transfer (insurance), risk acceptance, and risk sharing. Each treatment plan must specify the treatment actions, responsible person, target completion date in Hijri and Gregorian formats, estimated treatment cost in local currency with USD equivalent, expected residual risk level after treatment, and key performance indicators for monitoring treatment effectiveness. Treatment plans must go through an approval workflow configurable per entity, with the risk owner proposing the plan and the entity risk committee or authorized approver providing approval. The system must track treatment plan implementation progress through configurable milestones and must automatically flag treatment plans that fall behind schedule for management escalation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R005	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	AI-Powered Risk Identification and Correlation Analysis	The system must employ AI algorithms to continuously scan operational data across the ERP ecosystem to identify emerging risks and correlations between existing risks. The AI engine must analyze data from container operations (booking patterns, vessel delays, port congestion), financial transactions (payment delays, credit limit breaches, exchange rate volatility in QAR, AED, SAR, INR pairs), customs compliance (declaration rejection rates, inspection failure rates), and external data sources (maritime incident databases, weather patterns affecting Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean shipping lanes, geopolitical risk indices for Middle East and South Asian trade routes). Identified risk correlations must be presented with confidence scores and supporting evidence, and must be reviewable by risk managers before being added to the risk register. The AI must update correlation analysis weekly and must highlight newly detected risk clusters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R006	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Key Risk Indicator Definition and Monitoring	The system must support defining Key Risk Indicators (KRIs) for each risk or risk category, with configurable thresholds that trigger alerts when breached. Each KRI must specify the data source within the ERP, calculation formula, measurement frequency (real-time, daily, weekly, monthly), acceptable threshold range, warning threshold, and critical threshold. Shipping-specific KRIs must be supported including container dwell time exceeding port authority limits (e.g., 14 days at Jebel Ali, 30 days at JNPT), customs declaration rejection rate exceeding 5 percent, demurrage costs exceeding 2 percent of freight revenue, vessel turnaround time exceeding scheduled berth window, and cargo damage claim frequency per 1,000 TEUs handled. KRI dashboards must display current values with trend charts showing historical performance, and must support automated data collection from integrated ERP modules via the internal service bus.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R007	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Assessment Workshop Facilitation Tools	The system must provide digital tools to facilitate risk assessment workshops including structured templates for risk identification brainstorming, online voting and consensus-building for likelihood and impact scoring, and real-time risk matrix updates during workshop sessions. Workshop sessions must support both in-person and virtual participation with screen-sharing compatible displays. The system must allow workshop facilitators to create sessions, invite participants from multiple entities, define the scope and objectives, and distribute pre-read materials. During the workshop, participants must be able to submit risk nominations, provide independent scores before consensus discussion (to avoid groupthink), and record action items with assigned owners. Workshop minutes and outcomes must be automatically documented in both Arabic and English, capturing all participant contributions, scoring rationale, and decisions made. Workshop artifacts must be linked to the corresponding risks in the risk register for traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R008	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Appetite and Tolerance Framework Management	The system must support defining and managing the organization's risk appetite and tolerance framework at the enterprise, entity, and functional area levels. Risk appetite statements must be configurable for each risk category (strategic, operational, financial, compliance) with quantitative thresholds expressed in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and qualitative descriptions in Arabic and English. Risk tolerance levels must define the acceptable variation around risk appetite for each risk category. The system must automatically compare current risk exposures against defined appetite and tolerance levels and must generate exception reports when risks exceed tolerance boundaries. The risk appetite framework must be version-controlled with board approval dates tracked, and must be reviewable annually with the system generating reminders to the Chief Risk Officer 60 days before the annual review date. Changes to risk appetite must require multi-level approval including the entity Risk Committee and Group Board authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R009	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Inherent and Residual Risk Scoring Comparison	The system must capture and display both inherent risk scores (risk level before considering existing controls) and residual risk scores (risk level after considering existing controls and treatment plans) for each risk in the register. The difference between inherent and residual scores must be calculated automatically to demonstrate control effectiveness. The system must support documenting the existing controls that reduce each risk, linking to control descriptions in the policy and procedure management feature, and rating each control's effectiveness on a configurable scale (effective, partially effective, ineffective). Bow-tie analysis visualization must be available showing threats on the left, the risk event in the center, consequences on the right, preventive controls above, and mitigating controls below, with all elements labeled in the user's preferred language. When controls are assessed as ineffective, the system must automatically flag the associated risk for reassessment and must notify the risk owner and the control owner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R010	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Reporting and Board-Level Summaries	The system must generate comprehensive risk reports at multiple levels: entity-level risk reports for local management, consolidated enterprise risk reports for group management, and board-level executive summaries for the Board Risk Committee. Board-level reports must include a top-10 risks summary with movement indicators since the last reporting period, risk appetite utilization dashboard, emerging risk highlights, key risk indicator status summary, and treatment plan progress overview. All reports must be available in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions, including right-to-left table rendering and mirrored chart axes. Financial values in reports must display in both the entity's local currency and USD. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation on configurable dates aligned with board meeting calendars (Hijri or Gregorian) and must be distributable via secure email to designated board members with access-controlled download links.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R011	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Scenario Analysis and Stress Testing	The system must support scenario analysis and stress testing for identified risks, allowing risk managers to model the potential impact of adverse events on container shipping operations. Scenarios must be definable with variable parameters including container volume fluctuation (increase or decrease by configurable percentage), exchange rate shock (devaluation or appreciation of QAR, AED, SAR, INR against USD), port closure duration (1 day to 6 months), fuel price change, trade sanctions imposition on specific routes, and cyber-attack on port management systems. For each scenario, the system must calculate estimated financial impact across all four entities in local currencies and consolidated USD, operational impact on container throughput, and recovery time estimates. Scenario results must be comparable side-by-side to evaluate relative severity and must be stored with version history to track how scenario outcomes evolve as the business environment changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R012	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Event and Incident Linkage	The system must support linking materialized risk events and actual incidents to their corresponding risk register entries, capturing the event date (Hijri and Gregorian), description in Arabic and English, actual financial impact in local currency and USD, operational consequences (e.g., container throughput reduction, vessel delays, customs clearance backlogs), root cause analysis, and lessons learned. When a risk event occurs, the system must prompt the risk owner to reassess the risk's likelihood and impact scores based on the actual experience. The system must maintain statistics on risk materialization rates showing how often each risk category actually manifests compared to its assessed likelihood, enabling calibration of the risk assessment methodology over time. Incident linkage must also cross-reference related compliance breaches, audit findings, and insurance claims for holistic risk event management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R013	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Third-Party and Supply Chain Risk Assessment	The system must support assessing risks associated with third-party relationships critical to container shipping operations, including shipping lines, port terminal operators, customs brokers, freight forwarders, trucking companies, container leasing firms, and technology service providers operating across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Each third-party risk assessment must evaluate the vendor's financial stability, operational reliability, regulatory compliance history, information security posture, and business continuity capability. The system must support configurable risk assessment questionnaires that can be distributed to third parties electronically in Arabic and English, with automated scoring based on responses. Third-party risk scores must feed into the enterprise risk register, and the system must automatically flag high-risk vendors for enhanced monitoring or contract review. Annual reassessment scheduling must be configurable by vendor risk tier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R014	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Register Audit Trail and Change History	The system must maintain a complete, immutable audit trail for all changes to the risk register including risk creation, score modifications, status changes, treatment plan updates, owner reassignment, and risk closure. Each audit trail entry must capture the user who made the change, the timestamp in the entity's local timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), the field changed, the previous value, and the new value. The audit trail must be searchable and filterable by risk ID, user, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and change type. Export functionality must produce formatted reports in PDF and Excel showing the complete change history for any selected risk or set of risks. The audit trail must be tamper-proof, storing records in an append-only data structure that prevents retroactive modification, and must be retained for a minimum of the longest applicable retention period across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R015	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Operational Risk Indicators for Port-Specific Hazards	The system must include pre-configured operational risk indicators specific to container port operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, covering hazards such as extreme weather events (sandstorms in Gulf region, monsoons at JNPT), vessel collision in port approaches, container stack collapse, hazardous cargo spill (tracking IMO dangerous goods classes 1 through 9), crane equipment failure, port worker safety incidents, and cybersecurity threats to port management systems. Each pre-configured risk must include default likelihood and impact scores based on industry benchmarks, suggested control frameworks referencing ISPS Code, ISM Code, and local port authority safety regulations, and recommended KRIs with typical threshold values. Port-specific risks must be automatically instantiated for each port entity during initial system setup and must be adjustable by the local risk manager to reflect actual port conditions and risk profiles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R016	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Risk Register Integration with Insurance Management	The system must integrate risk register data with insurance management processes, mapping identified risks to applicable insurance coverage types including marine cargo insurance, hull and machinery insurance, protection and indemnity (P&I), port operator liability, cyber insurance, and business interruption insurance. For each insured risk, the system must track the insurance policy reference, coverage limits in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent, deductible amounts, policy expiry dates (Hijri and Gregorian), and the insurer or broker contact information. The system must identify gaps where high or critical risks exist without corresponding insurance coverage and must flag these gaps in the risk reporting dashboard. When risk scores change significantly, the system must automatically notify the insurance coordinator to review whether coverage adjustments are needed with the underwriter.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-003-R017	FEAT-035-1-003	MOD-035	Emerging Risk Radar and Horizon Scanning	The system must provide an emerging risk radar feature that captures and tracks potential future risks that have not yet materialized but could significantly impact container shipping operations in the medium to long term (1-5 year horizon). Emerging risk categories must include geopolitical shifts affecting Gulf trade corridors and India Ocean routes, technological disruption (autonomous vessels, blockchain in trade documentation), climate change impacts on port infrastructure (sea level rise, extreme temperature affecting container integrity), regulatory evolution (carbon emission regulations, digital customs mandates), and economic shifts (currency regime changes in GCC countries, Indian trade policy reforms). Each emerging risk must be assessed on a plausibility-impact matrix and must be reviewed quarterly by the enterprise risk committee. The system must support AI-assisted scanning of maritime industry publications and regulatory announcements to suggest emerging risk topics for consideration, presenting suggestions in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R001	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Document Lifecycle Management	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of policy documents from drafting through approval, publication, periodic review, revision, and retirement. Each policy must progress through configurable lifecycle stages: draft, under-review, pending-approval, approved, published, under-revision, superseded, and retired. Transitions between stages must be controlled by role-based permissions with the policy author able to submit for review, designated reviewers able to approve or return with comments, and the entity policy committee or authorized approver providing final approval. The system must enforce that no policy can be published without completing all required approval steps. Retired policies must remain accessible in read-only mode for historical reference with clear visual indicators showing their retired status and the date of retirement in both Gregorian and Hijri formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R002	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Bilingual Policy Authoring with RTL Support	The system must provide a rich text editor for authoring policy documents in both Arabic and English, with full RTL text support for Arabic content including proper rendering of mixed-direction text (Arabic narrative containing English technical terms such as INCOTERMS codes like FOB, CIF, DDP, and container type designations like 20GP, 40HC, 45HC). The editor must support formatted text (bold, italic, underline), numbered and bulleted lists, tables with configurable column alignment for RTL and LTR content, embedded images and diagrams, hyperlinks to other policies or external references, and section cross-references. Each policy must maintain paired Arabic and English versions with a linking mechanism that ensures both language versions are updated and approved together. The system must track translation completeness, flagging policies where one language version has been updated but the corresponding translation has not.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R003	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Standard Operating Procedure Templates for Shipping Operations	The system must provide pre-configured SOP templates specific to container shipping operations including container booking procedures, bill of lading issuance (covering straight, order, and bearer types), customs declaration processing, container inspection and survey, hazardous cargo handling per IMDG Code requirements, reefer container monitoring, container damage reporting, vessel loading and discharge planning, freight rate quotation, demurrage and detention calculation, and customer credit management. Each template must include sections for purpose, scope, applicable entities and ports, responsible roles, step-by-step procedures, required forms and documents, quality checkpoints, escalation procedures, and references to governing regulations. Templates must be available in Arabic and English and must be customizable at the entity level to accommodate jurisdiction-specific requirements while maintaining a consistent group-wide structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R004	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Version Control and Change Tracking	The system must implement comprehensive version control for all policy and procedure documents, maintaining a complete version history with major version numbering (1.0, 2.0) for substantive content changes and minor version numbering (1.1, 1.2) for formatting or editorial corrections. Each version must capture the version number, change description in Arabic and English, author of changes, date of change (Hijri and Gregorian), and approval status. The system must provide a visual diff comparison between any two versions of the same document, highlighting additions in green, deletions in red, and modifications in yellow with proper RTL rendering for Arabic text comparisons. The current effective version must be clearly identified and must be the only version accessible to general users, while policy administrators and auditors must have access to all historical versions for compliance review and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R005	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Approval Workflow with Entity-Specific Routing	The system must support configurable approval workflows for policy documents that route through appropriate stakeholders based on the policy scope, entity applicability, and risk classification. Entity-specific policies must be approvable by the entity policy committee, while group-wide policies must require approval from entity representatives across all applicable entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) plus the group policy committee. The workflow must support parallel and sequential approval paths, allowing multiple reviewers to comment simultaneously while requiring sequential sign-off from approvers in a defined order. Each approval action must capture the approver identity, digital signature, timestamp in local timezone, and optional comments in Arabic or English. The system must support conditional routing where policies affecting regulated activities (customs operations, financial controls, safety procedures) automatically include the compliance officer and legal department in the approval chain.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R006	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Distribution and Acknowledgment Tracking	The system must manage the distribution of approved policies to relevant employees and must track individual acknowledgment of receipt and understanding. Distribution lists must be configurable by entity, department, role, port location, and specific named individuals. When a new policy is published or an existing policy is revised, the system must automatically notify all personnel on the distribution list via email and in-app notification in their preferred language (Arabic or English) with a link to the published document. Each employee must be required to electronically acknowledge that they have read and understood the policy, with the acknowledgment recording the employee name, entity, role, date and time of acknowledgment, and IP address. The system must generate compliance reports showing acknowledgment rates by entity and department, highlighting employees who have not acknowledged within a configurable deadline (default 14 calendar days from publication).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R007	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Periodic Policy Review Scheduling and Reminders	The system must enforce periodic review of all published policies and procedures at configurable intervals, with default review periods based on policy category: annual review for operational procedures, biennial review for corporate policies, and immediate review triggered by significant regulatory changes. The system must automatically calculate review due dates based on the last approval date and the configured review period, using the entity's calendar preference (Gregorian or Hijri). Automated review reminders must be sent to the policy owner at 90, 60, and 30 days before the review due date, with escalation to the policy owner's manager at 15 days and to the entity compliance director when the review is overdue. The system must flag overdue policy reviews on the compliance dashboard and must prevent any policy that is more than 60 days overdue for review from being cited as a current reference in audit working papers or compliance filings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R008	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Compliance Attestation Campaigns	The system must support periodic compliance attestation campaigns where designated employees or managers certify that their departments are operating in compliance with applicable policies and procedures. Attestation campaigns must be configurable by scope (specific policies, policy categories, or all policies), target audience (by entity, department, role), attestation period (quarterly, semi-annually, annually), and response deadline. Each attestation response must require the responder to confirm compliance or declare exceptions with mandatory explanation, supporting evidence, and remediation plan for each declared exception. The system must aggregate attestation results and produce summary reports showing overall compliance rates, common exception areas, and trend analysis over multiple attestation periods. Non-responses after the deadline must be escalated automatically to the responder's manager and must be treated as non-compliant for reporting purposes until the attestation is completed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R009	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Mapping to Regulatory Requirements	The system must support mapping each policy and procedure to the specific regulatory requirements it addresses, creating a traceability matrix between internal policies and external regulations. Regulatory references must include the regulation name in Arabic and English, issuing authority, specific section or article number, and applicability to each entity. The mapping must support many-to-many relationships where a single policy may address multiple regulatory requirements and a single regulation may be covered by multiple policies. The system must generate gap analysis reports identifying regulatory requirements that are not addressed by any current policy and must flag these gaps for the compliance team. When a regulatory requirement changes (as tracked in the Regulatory Compliance Calendar feature), the system must automatically identify all mapped policies that may require updates and must create review tasks assigned to the respective policy owners.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R010	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Search and Knowledge Base Interface	The system must provide an intelligent search interface that enables employees to find relevant policies and procedures using natural language queries in both Arabic and English. The search engine must support full-text search across policy content, metadata search by policy number, title, category, entity, and effective date, and AI-powered semantic search that understands shipping domain concepts (e.g., a search for 'container damage' must return policies about container inspection, damage reporting, and claims handling). Search results must be ranked by relevance with the most applicable policies appearing first, and must display snippets showing the matching content sections. The system must track search queries and click-through patterns to identify areas where employees frequently seek guidance, which may indicate training needs or policy communication gaps. The knowledge base must be accessible from all ERP modules via a contextual help button that pre-filters results based on the current operational context.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R011	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Exception and Waiver Management	The system must support formal exception and waiver requests when business circumstances require deviation from published policies. Exception requests must capture the policy being deviated from, the specific sections or requirements subject to the exception, the business justification in Arabic and English, the requested exception duration (one-time, temporary with end date in Hijri and Gregorian, or permanent), the risk assessment of operating under the exception, and proposed compensating controls. Exception requests must route through a configurable approval workflow that includes at minimum the policy owner, the requestor's management chain, and the entity compliance officer. Approved exceptions must be documented in a central exception register with regular review of active exceptions (at least quarterly). The system must automatically expire temporary exceptions on their end date and must notify the requestor and compliance officer 30 days before expiry to evaluate whether the exception should be renewed, converted to a policy amendment, or allowed to expire.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R012	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	AI-Assisted Policy Drafting and Consistency Checking	The system must provide AI-assisted policy drafting capabilities that suggest policy language based on the document type, applicable regulations, industry best practices for container shipping, and the organization's existing policy library. The AI engine must check draft policies for consistency with existing approved policies, flagging potential conflicts or contradictions such as different approval thresholds for the same transaction type across entities, inconsistent terminology usage, or conflicting procedural steps. The AI must also verify that mandatory sections defined in the policy template are completed and that regulatory references cited in the policy are valid and current. Suggested text must be provided in both Arabic and English, with the AI respecting the linguistic conventions and formal register appropriate for policy documents in each language. All AI suggestions must be clearly marked as machine-generated and must require human review and approval before incorporation into the policy document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R013	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Impact Assessment for Operational Changes	The system must support policy impact assessments when operational changes are proposed that may affect existing policies, such as introducing a new container handling process at Jebel Ali, changing customs declaration workflows to comply with updated ZATCA requirements in KSA, or modifying freight billing procedures for India GST compliance. Impact assessments must identify all policies and procedures affected by the proposed change, describe the required modifications to each affected document, estimate the effort and timeline for policy updates, and identify training requirements for personnel affected by the changed procedures. The impact assessment must be linkable to the originating change request and must generate a policy update project plan with tasks assigned to respective policy owners. The system must track the completion of all identified policy updates and must prevent the operational change from being implemented until all critical policy updates are published.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R006	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Submission Status Tracking and Acknowledgment	The system must track the complete lifecycle of each regulatory submission from draft creation through final acceptance or rejection by the regulatory authority. Status states must include Draft, Under Review, Submitted, Acknowledged, Accepted, Rejected, Amendment Required, and Resubmitted. Each status transition must capture the timestamp, user or system actor, and any reference numbers provided by the authority. For rejected submissions, the system must parse and display rejection reasons in both Arabic and English, link them to the original filing data, and create automated remediation tasks. The system must maintain a dashboard showing submission status across all entities and authorities with color-coded deadline proximity indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R014	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Access Control and Confidentiality Classification	The system must enforce granular access controls on policy documents based on confidentiality classification levels: public (accessible to all employees across all entities), internal (accessible to employees within the applicable entity), confidential (accessible only to specified roles or departments), and restricted (accessible only to named individuals). Access controls must be entity-aware so that policies classified as internal for the Qatar entity are not visible to users from the UAE, KSA, or India entities without explicit cross-entity access grants. The system must support time-limited access grants for external parties such as auditors, regulators, or consultants, with automatic access revocation upon expiry. All access to confidential and restricted policies must be logged with the user identity, access timestamp, action type (view, download, print), and IP address. The system must generate access audit reports for security review and must alert the policy owner when access patterns deviate from normal, such as bulk downloads or access from unusual locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R015	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Training Material Linkage and Competency Tracking	The system must support linking policy documents to associated training materials including e-learning modules, video tutorials, classroom training schedules, and competency assessments. When a policy is published or significantly revised, the system must automatically identify linked training materials that may require updates and must notify the training coordinator. The system must track which employees have completed policy-related training, recording the training completion date, assessment score if applicable, and certification validity period. For safety-critical policies such as hazardous cargo handling procedures conforming to IMDG Code or ISPS Code security procedures, the system must enforce that employees in relevant roles cannot perform the associated operational functions in the ERP until they have completed mandatory training and achieved the minimum assessment score. Training compliance statistics must be reportable by entity, port, department, and policy category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R016	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Repository Organizational Taxonomy and Navigation	The system must organize the policy repository using a configurable hierarchical taxonomy that allows users to browse policies by category (corporate governance, operations, finance, HR, IT, HSE, compliance), sub-category (e.g., under operations: container handling, vessel operations, yard management, gate operations, customs procedures), entity applicability, port applicability, and regulatory domain. The taxonomy must support multiple classification paths so that a single policy can appear under multiple categories without duplication. Navigation must include a tree-view browser, breadcrumb trails showing the current location in the taxonomy hierarchy, and quick-access bookmarking for frequently referenced policies. The taxonomy structure must be administrable by policy administrators without requiring technical support, and must support Arabic and English labels for all taxonomy nodes. The system must display policy counts at each taxonomy node and must highlight nodes containing recently updated or newly published policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R017	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Obsolete Policy Sunset and Archival Process	The system must provide a structured sunset and archival process for policies that are being retired, superseded, or consolidated. When a policy is marked for retirement, the system must require documentation of the retirement reason (superseded by new policy, regulatory requirement removed, business process discontinued, consolidated into another policy), the effective retirement date (Gregorian and Hijri), and the reference to any replacement policy. The system must automatically notify all personnel on the retiring policy's distribution list about the retirement and must redirect any existing hyperlinks or cross-references to the replacement policy or display a retirement notice if no replacement exists. Archived policies must be retained in a read-only archive accessible to authorized personnel (auditors, compliance officers, legal team) for the duration of the applicable retention period as defined by entity-specific regulations (minimum 7 years for Qatar, 5 years for UAE, 10 years for KSA, 8 years for India). The system must prevent permanent deletion of archived policies during the retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-1-004-R018	FEAT-035-1-004	MOD-035	Policy Effectiveness Metrics and Analytics	The system must collect and analyze metrics that indicate policy effectiveness across all four entities, including policy acknowledgment completion rates, exception and waiver request frequency by policy, compliance attestation results, audit finding frequency related to policy areas, training completion rates for policy-linked courses, and employee search query patterns indicating confusion or lack of awareness. The analytics engine must generate trend reports showing policy effectiveness indicators over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) with entity-level and consolidated views. The system must calculate a composite policy health score for each policy based on weighted metrics and must highlight policies with declining health scores for prioritized review. Effectiveness data must be presentable in interactive dashboards with filters for entity, port, policy category, and time period, rendering correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts. The system must support exporting analytics data to Excel for ad hoc analysis with proper Arabic text encoding in exported files.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R001	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Regulatory Authority Registry Management	The system must maintain a configurable registry of all regulatory authorities across operating jurisdictions including Qatar Ministry of Transport (MOTC), UAE Federal Transport Authority (FTA), Saudi Transport General Authority (TGA), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping (DGS). Each authority record must store the authority code, jurisdiction, contact details, submission portal URLs, accepted file formats (XML, XBRL, PDF/A, CSV), preferred language (Arabic or English), and required digital signature standards. The registry must support addition of new authorities without code deployment and must be auditable with full change history tracking per SOC-2 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R002	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Automated Filing Schedule Generation	The system must automatically generate filing schedules for each regulatory authority based on configured reporting frequencies (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual). Schedules must account for both Gregorian and Hijri calendar deadlines, with automatic conversion between calendar systems. For KSA ZATCA VAT returns, the system must calculate filing deadlines based on the Hijri month-end plus 30 days. Weekend variations across jurisdictions must be handled: Friday-Saturday for Qatar and KSA, Saturday-Sunday for UAE, and Saturday-Sunday for India. The system must generate advance notifications at configurable intervals (30, 15, 7, 3, 1 days before deadline).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R008	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Automated Regulatory Change Detection	The system must provide an AI-powered regulatory change detection engine that monitors official gazette publications, regulatory authority websites, and subscribed legal databases for changes affecting container shipping operations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Detected changes must be classified by impact severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low) and mapped to affected ERP modules, business processes, and compliance controls. The system must generate impact assessment reports in both Arabic and English, identifying specific configuration changes, workflow modifications, or new reporting requirements. Change notifications must be routed to designated compliance officers per jurisdiction with acknowledgment tracking and escalation to senior management if unacknowledged within 48 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R009	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Port Authority Vessel and Container Reporting	The system must generate standardized vessel and container movement reports for submission to port authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Reports must include vessel call details (IMO number, flag state, ETA/ETD, berth allocation), container discharge and loading manifests categorized by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 20FR), hazardous cargo declarations per IMDG code classification, and reefer container temperature monitoring logs. For Indian JNPT operations, the system must generate reports compatible with the Indian Customs EDI System (ICES) format. Reports must be automatically generated upon vessel departure and submitted within the port authority's mandated timeframe.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R010	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Anti-Money Laundering Transaction Reporting	The system must generate Suspicious Transaction Reports (STRs) and Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) for container shipping financial transactions that trigger AML thresholds. For Qatar, reports must comply with Qatar Financial Information Unit (QFIU) requirements with thresholds at QAR 55,000. For UAE, reports must follow goAML format for the Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU) with thresholds at AED 55,000. For KSA, SAFIU reporting standards apply with thresholds at SAR 60,000. For India, FIU-IND reporting applies with thresholds at INR 1,000,000. The system must automatically screen all freight payment counterparties against OFAC, EU, and UN sanctions lists and flag matches for compliance officer review before processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R011	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	VAT and GST Return Data Preparation	The system must prepare VAT return data for Qatar, UAE, and KSA operations and GST return data for India operations from container shipping transaction records. For UAE, the system must categorize transactions into standard-rated (5%), zero-rated (international freight), exempt, and reverse charge supplies per FTA VAT return Form 201. For KSA, the system must handle the 15% VAT rate and prepare data for ZATCA VAT return submission. For India, the system must prepare GSTR-1, GSTR-2A reconciliation, and GSTR-3B data covering IGST on international freight, CGST/SGST on domestic services, and input tax credit reconciliation. All amounts must be computed in local currency with exchange rate references for cross-border transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R012	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Regulatory Report Template Management	The system must provide a template management engine for regulatory report formats, allowing compliance administrators to create, version, and manage report templates without developer intervention. Templates must support dynamic field mapping to ERP data sources, conditional logic for jurisdiction-specific sections, and embedded validation rules. Each template must support bilingual output with Arabic right-to-left formatting for headers, labels, and narrative sections alongside English content. Template versioning must track effective dates aligned to regulatory amendment cycles, and the system must automatically switch to the correct template version based on the reporting period. Templates must support output in PDF/A for archival, XBRL for financial regulators, XML for customs authorities, and Excel for internal review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R013	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Cross-Border Transaction Compliance Reporting	The system must generate cross-border transaction compliance reports covering all container shipments moving between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Reports must detail transfer pricing documentation for inter-company freight charges, withholding tax applicability on cross-border service fees, and Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) benefit claims. For GCC intra-trade, the system must report on GCC customs union preferential treatment eligibility and certificate of origin validation. The system must reconcile BOL-level shipment values against commercial invoice values and flag discrepancies exceeding 2% for compliance review. Reports must include INCOTERMS analysis showing responsibility transfer points and associated duty and tax liability allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R014	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Offline Regulatory Report Preparation	The system must support offline preparation of regulatory reports when connectivity to central servers or regulatory portals is unavailable, particularly relevant for port-side operations at Hamad Port and JNPT during network outages. Offline mode must cache the most recent master data including exchange rates, HS code classifications, regulatory templates, and entity configuration. Users must be able to draft, validate, and locally store regulatory reports with full field-level validation against cached rules. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline-prepared reports, detect conflicts with centrally updated data, and present reconciliation options. Offline-prepared reports must carry a visual indicator and require online re-validation before final submission to any regulatory authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R015	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Regulatory Submission Audit Trail and Evidence Pack	The system must generate comprehensive audit trail evidence packs for each regulatory submission, bundling the submitted report, all supporting source documents, data extraction queries, calculation worksheets, approval chain records, and digital signature certificates into a single tamper-evident archive. Evidence packs must be stored in compliance with data retention requirements: 7 years for Qatar MOTC, 5 years for UAE FTA, 6 years for KSA ZATCA, and 8 years for India DGFT. Each evidence pack must include a SHA-256 hash manifest for integrity verification. The system must support retrieval of evidence packs by regulatory authority, reporting period, entity, or submission reference number with sub-second search response times across the full retention archive.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R001	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Feature Flag CRUD Operations	The system must provide full create, read, update, and delete operations for feature flags through the Admin Portal UI and a corresponding REST API. Each feature flag must include a unique key identifier, a human-readable name in both English and Arabic, a description field supporting bilingual content, a flag type selector (boolean, percentage rollout, multivariate, or JSON payload), and a default value. The creation form must validate that flag keys follow a namespace convention such as module.feature.subfeature (e.g., mod012.booking.autoAllocation) to prevent naming conflicts across the ERP modules serving Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-001-R016	FEAT-035-2-001	MOD-035	Penalty and Fine Tracking for Late Submissions	The system must track penalties and fines imposed by regulatory authorities for late or non-compliant submissions across all operating jurisdictions. Each penalty record must capture the authority code, submission reference, original deadline, actual submission date, penalty amount in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), penalty calculation basis (flat fee, percentage, daily accrual), appeal status, and payment status. The system must automatically calculate estimated penalties for overdue submissions based on each authority's published penalty schedule. Dashboard views must show total penalty exposure by entity and jurisdiction with trend analysis. The system must generate root cause analysis reports linking penalties to specific process failures and recommend preventive controls to compliance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R001	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Control Activity Catalog and Classification	The system must maintain a comprehensive catalog of all internal controls over financial reporting (ICFR) organized by financial statement assertion (Existence, Completeness, Valuation, Rights & Obligations, Presentation & Disclosure). Each control must be classified by type (Preventive, Detective, Corrective), nature (Manual, Automated, IT-Dependent Manual), frequency (Transaction-level, Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Quarterly, Annual), and risk level (Key Control, Non-Key Control). Controls must be mapped to specific ERP transaction flows including freight revenue recognition, container lease accounting, port charges accrual, bunker fuel hedging, and inter-company settlements. The catalog must support multi-entity control variations where Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations require jurisdiction-specific control procedures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R002	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Segregation of Duties Matrix and Enforcement	The system must enforce segregation of duties (SoD) policies across all financial transaction processing within the container shipping ERP. The SoD matrix must define conflicting role combinations including but not limited to: freight invoice creation versus payment approval, container inventory adjustment versus financial posting, bank reconciliation versus journal entry posting, and vendor master data maintenance versus purchase order approval. The system must perform real-time SoD conflict detection during user role assignment and provide compensating control recommendations when conflicts cannot be avoided due to organizational constraints. SoD violations must be logged with justification, approved by a compliance officer, and reported in quarterly SOX compliance packages. The matrix must cover all four entity jurisdictions with entity-specific role mappings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R003	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Automated Control Testing Workflow	The system must support automated testing of IT application controls embedded in the ERP including three-way matching for container freight invoices (purchase order, goods receipt, invoice), automated revenue recognition calculations per IFRS 15 for shipping contracts, foreign exchange gain/loss computations across QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD transactions, and system-enforced approval hierarchies. Test scripts must be configurable to define test objectives, sample selection criteria (statistical or judgmental), expected results, and pass/fail thresholds. The system must automatically execute scheduled test runs, capture evidence screenshots, record test results, and route failed tests to control owners for remediation. Test execution must support parallel testing across all four entity instances without cross-entity data leakage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R004	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Control Deficiency Classification and Tracking	The system must classify identified control deficiencies into three severity levels per PCAOB AS 2201 standards: Material Weakness, Significant Deficiency, and Control Deficiency. Classification must consider both likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement, with magnitude thresholds configurable by entity and currency. For example, a material weakness threshold might be set at QAR 5,000,000 for the Qatar entity and INR 50,000,000 for the India entity. Each deficiency must be linked to the specific control, business process, financial statement line item, and responsible entity. The system must track remediation plans with milestones, target completion dates, assigned owners, and validation testing requirements. Escalation workflows must automatically notify the CFO and audit committee for material weaknesses and significant deficiencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R005	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Management Assertion and Certification Workflow	The system must support quarterly and annual management certification workflows aligned with SOX Section 302 requirements. Entity-level controllers must certify the operating effectiveness of controls within their jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) before group-level consolidation. The certification workflow must include a checklist of required evidence, sub-certifications from process owners, representation letters confirming no unreported control failures, and disclosure of any known fraud indicators. Certifications must be digitally signed with timestamps and stored with the same retention requirements as regulatory submissions. The system must prevent certification submission if outstanding material weakness remediation items remain open and must generate a certification status dashboard showing completion percentages across all entities and process areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R006	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	IT General Controls Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor IT General Controls (ITGCs) that underpin the reliability of automated application controls within the ERP. Monitored ITGCs must include logical access controls (user provisioning, deprovisioning, privileged access reviews), change management (code deployment approvals, emergency change procedures, rollback verification), computer operations (batch job scheduling for freight billing runs, backup completion for financial databases, disaster recovery testing), and program development (test-to-production migration controls). The system must integrate with identity management platforms to detect unauthorized access changes and with CI/CD pipelines to verify change management compliance. ITGC monitoring dashboards must display control health scores by category with drill-down to individual control evidence across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R007	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Journal Entry Testing and Approval Controls	The system must enforce and test controls over manual journal entries, which represent a significant fraud risk in container shipping operations involving multi-currency transactions. All manual journal entries must require dual approval for amounts exceeding configurable thresholds per entity (e.g., QAR 100,000 for Qatar, AED 100,000 for UAE, SAR 100,000 for KSA, INR 2,000,000 for India). The system must flag unusual journal entries for enhanced review including entries posted on weekends or holidays per jurisdiction calendar, entries to seldom-used accounts, entries with round amounts, reversing entries within the same period, and entries posted by users not typically performing journal entry functions. Monthly reports must summarize journal entry testing results with trend analysis by entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R008	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Revenue Recognition Control Framework	The system must implement and test controls specific to container shipping revenue recognition under IFRS 15, covering the five-step model: contract identification (booking confirmations, service contracts), performance obligation identification (ocean freight, terminal handling, documentation fees, customs brokerage), transaction price determination (base freight, surcharges including BAF, CAF, THC, PSS in multiple currencies), allocation of transaction price to performance obligations, and recognition timing (percentage of voyage completion, point-in-time for ancillary services). Controls must validate that revenue is not recognized before vessel departure from origin port, that demurrage and detention charges follow contractual escalation schedules, and that bill of lading surrender triggers final revenue recognition adjustments. Each control must have documented testing procedures with sample sizes based on transaction volumes per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R009	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Accounts Payable and Procurement Controls	The system must enforce and monitor controls over the procure-to-pay cycle specific to container shipping operations including port charges at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, vessel fuel procurement (bunker purchases), container depot maintenance charges, customs duty payments, and freight forwarding sub-contractor payments. Controls must include purchase order authorization limits by entity and currency, three-way matching tolerance thresholds (quantity variance up to 2%, price variance up to 1%), duplicate invoice detection across all entities using vendor tax ID, invoice number, amount, and date matching, and payment run approval segregation from invoice processing. The system must generate monthly control effectiveness reports showing match rates, exception volumes, override frequencies, and aging of unmatched items per entity and vendor category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R010	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Bank Reconciliation and Cash Controls	The system must enforce controls over bank reconciliation processes for all bank accounts across the four operating entities, covering multiple currencies including QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD, EUR, and GBP. Controls must require daily automatic bank statement import via MT940 or CAMT.053 formats, automated matching of bank transactions to ERP cash entries with configurable tolerance thresholds, mandatory review and approval of reconciling items older than 30 days, and escalation of unreconciled differences exceeding entity-specific materiality thresholds. The system must detect and flag unusual cash transactions including payments to new beneficiaries, payments to countries on enhanced due diligence lists, and cash receipts from non-customer counterparties. Monthly bank reconciliation completion status must be tracked by entity with deadline enforcement and exception reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R011	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Fixed Asset and Container Fleet Controls	The system must implement controls over fixed asset accounting with specific attention to container fleet management, which represents the largest asset class in the shipping ERP. Controls must cover container capitalization decisions (owned versus leased per IFRS 16), depreciation method and useful life assignment (typically 12-15 years for dry containers, 10-12 years for reefer units), impairment testing triggers (container utilization below 60% for two consecutive quarters), and disposal authorization. Physical verification controls must require annual container fleet reconciliation between the asset register and container tracking system, with discrepancy investigation and resolution within 30 days. The system must enforce approval workflows for asset write-offs exceeding USD 50,000 equivalent across all currencies, with audit committee notification for write-offs exceeding USD 500,000.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R012	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Period-End Close Controls and Checklist	The system must manage period-end financial close processes with a structured checklist of mandatory control activities for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The close checklist must include foreign exchange revaluation of open receivables and payables (QAR/AED/SAR/INR against USD), accrual of unbilled freight revenue based on vessel tracking data, provision for doubtful debts based on aging analysis with shipping-industry-specific criteria, inter-company balance reconciliation and elimination, and management review of significant estimates. Each checklist item must have an assigned owner, deadline relative to period-end (T+1 through T+10), required evidence attachments, and reviewer sign-off. The system must block period closure if mandatory checklist items remain incomplete and must generate a close status dashboard showing real-time completion progress across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R013	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	External Auditor Portal and Evidence Sharing	The system must provide a secure external auditor portal allowing authorized audit firm personnel to access control documentation, testing evidence, and deficiency tracking information without requiring full ERP user licenses. The portal must support role-based access controlling visibility to specific entities, process areas, and document types. Auditors must be able to download evidence packages, add review comments, request additional documentation through structured queries, and mark items as reviewed. All portal access must be logged with IP address, timestamp, documents accessed, and download records. Portal sessions must enforce multi-factor authentication and automatic timeout after 30 minutes of inactivity. The system must support concurrent access by multiple audit firms when different auditors serve different entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R014	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Control Environment Risk Assessment	The system must support annual and event-driven risk assessments of the control environment covering entity-level controls, fraud risk factors, and tone-at-the-top indicators across all four operating jurisdictions. Risk assessment questionnaires must be configurable in both Arabic and English with jurisdiction-specific questions addressing local regulatory requirements, cultural factors, and business practices. The system must calculate inherent risk scores based on transaction volumes, financial statement materiality per entity, complexity of accounting estimates (e.g., container residual values, voyage revenue accruals), and prior year audit findings. Residual risk scores must factor in control design and operating effectiveness ratings. Risk heat maps must be generated by entity and process area, with automatic linkage between high-risk areas and required control testing scope increases per PCAOB AS 2201 guidance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R011	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Cross-Entity Audit Correlation	The system must support cross-entity audit log correlation to track business processes that span multiple entities, such as a shipment originating in India (JNPT) destined for Qatar (Hamad Port) involving documentation processed by the UAE entity. The correlation engine must link audit entries across entities using common business identifiers such as master bill of lading number, booking reference, or shipment ID. Cross-entity correlation searches must respect entity-level access controls, only showing audit entries from entities the requesting administrator is authorized to view. Results must clearly indicate which entity each audit entry belongs to with both English and Arabic entity labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R015	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	Whistleblower and Ethics Hotline Integration	The system must integrate with whistleblower and ethics hotline channels to capture and track reports of suspected financial fraud, control overrides, and compliance violations related to container shipping operations. Reports must be classifiable by category (financial misstatement, asset misappropriation, bribery and corruption, sanctions violations, revenue manipulation) and severity. The system must support anonymous reporting with bilingual interfaces (Arabic and English) and must comply with Qatar Law No. 20 of 2008, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 of 2021, and India's Whistleblower Protection Act 2014. Each report must trigger an investigation workflow with assigned investigators, evidence collection tasks, interim containment actions, and final disposition categories. Investigation records must be restricted to authorized compliance committee members with enhanced access controls beyond standard ERP permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-002-R016	FEAT-035-2-002	MOD-035	SOX Compliance Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide a comprehensive SOX compliance dashboard presenting the overall control environment health across all four entities with drill-down capabilities. Dashboard metrics must include total controls by category and status (Effective, Ineffective, Not Yet Tested), testing completion percentage against the annual plan, open deficiency counts by severity and aging, remediation plan adherence rates, and management certification readiness scores. The dashboard must support views by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), by process area (Order-to-Cash, Procure-to-Pay, Record-to-Report, Container Fleet Management), and by financial statement line item. Trend analysis must show quarter-over-quarter improvement or deterioration. All dashboard data must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats with the option to generate board-level executive summaries in both Arabic and English for audit committee presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R001	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	ISO Certification Registry and Scope Definition	The system must maintain a centralized registry of all active, expired, and pending ISO certifications across the container shipping organization. Each certification record must capture the ISO standard reference (e.g., ISO 9001:2015, ISO 14001:2015, ISO 27001:2022, ISO 45001:2018, ISO 28000:2022), certification body name and accreditation details, certificate number, scope statement describing covered activities (e.g., container freight operations at Hamad Port, freight forwarding services at Jebel Ali), certified entity legal name, physical sites covered, initial certification date, current cycle expiry date, and certification status (Active, Suspended, Withdrawn, Expired, Pending). The registry must support bilingual scope statements in Arabic and English and must display Hijri calendar dates alongside Gregorian dates for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R002	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Surveillance and Recertification Audit Scheduling	The system must automatically calculate and schedule surveillance audits and recertification audits based on the ISO certification cycle. For a standard 3-year certification cycle, the system must schedule two surveillance audits (approximately at months 12 and 24) and one recertification audit (at month 33-36). Scheduling must account for jurisdiction-specific holiday calendars including Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, and Indian public holidays. The system must send automated notifications to site managers, quality managers, and senior management at configurable intervals (90, 60, 30, 14 days before scheduled audit). Audit scheduling must support multi-site sampling plans where not all sites require annual surveillance, with automated rotation ensuring each site (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) is audited within the certification cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R003	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Certification Body and Auditor Management	The system must maintain records of accredited certification bodies and individual auditors engaged for ISO audits across all operating jurisdictions. Certification body records must include accreditation body affiliation (e.g., DAC for Qatar, EIAC for UAE, SAC for KSA, NABCB for India), accreditation scope, contract terms, fee schedules in applicable currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), and performance ratings based on audit quality assessments. Individual auditor records must capture qualifications, sector-specific competencies (maritime and logistics), language capabilities (Arabic, English, Hindi), audit-day rates, and conflict-of-interest declarations. The system must enforce auditor rotation rules where the same lead auditor cannot conduct more than three consecutive surveillance or recertification audits for the same certified site, ensuring independence as required by ISO 17021.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R004	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Nonconformity and Corrective Action Tracking	The system must track nonconformities identified during ISO surveillance and recertification audits, classifying them as Major Nonconformity, Minor Nonconformity, or Opportunity for Improvement per ISO 17021-1 clause 9.4. Each nonconformity must be linked to the specific ISO standard clause violated, the audited process or site, and the responsible entity. For major nonconformities, the system must enforce a maximum 90-day corrective action deadline with certification suspension risk escalation at 60 days. For minor nonconformities, a maximum 12-month resolution deadline must apply with surveillance audit verification. Corrective actions must follow the PDCA cycle with mandatory root cause analysis, containment action, corrective action implementation, and effectiveness verification steps. The system must generate bilingual (Arabic/English) corrective action reports for submission to the certification body.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R005	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Document Control for ISO Management Systems	The system must provide ISO-compliant document control functionality for all management system documentation including quality manuals, procedures, work instructions, forms, and records across all certified entities and sites. Document control must enforce version management with approval workflows, distribution lists per document scope, and automatic notification of document changes to affected personnel. The system must maintain the documented information requirements specified by each ISO standard: quality policy and objectives for ISO 9001, environmental aspects register for ISO 14001, information security risk treatment plan for ISO 27001, hazard identification records for ISO 45001, and supply chain security threat assessments for ISO 28000. Documents must support bilingual content with proper Arabic RTL formatting and must be accessible offline for port operations staff at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT during connectivity outages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R006	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Management Review Meeting Tracking	The system must facilitate and track ISO-required management review meetings for each certified management system across all entities. Meeting agendas must automatically populate with required inputs per each ISO standard including audit results, customer feedback analysis (shipping line satisfaction scores, freight forwarder complaints), process performance metrics (container throughput, on-time delivery rates, cargo damage claims), nonconformity status summaries, and continual improvement project updates. Meeting minutes must capture decisions, action items with assigned owners and deadlines, and resource allocation approvals in both Arabic and English. The system must track meeting frequency compliance ensuring reviews occur at minimum annually per ISO requirement, with dashboards showing overdue reviews by entity and standard. Output records must demonstrate that management has reviewed system effectiveness and allocated adequate resources for maintaining certifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R007	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Internal Audit Program Management	The system must manage the internal audit program required for maintaining ISO certifications, ensuring all processes and clauses are audited within each certification cycle. The audit program must generate risk-based annual audit schedules covering all certified processes at all sites including container handling at Hamad Port, freight forwarding at Jebel Ali, customs brokerage at JNPT, and warehouse operations at Khalifa Port. Internal auditor competency records must be maintained with training histories, qualified standards, and independence requirements preventing auditors from auditing their own work areas. Audit checklists must be auto-generated from applicable ISO standard clauses with shipping-industry-specific assessment criteria. The system must track audit completion rates, finding trends by clause and site, and generate program effectiveness reports for management review input across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R008	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	ISO 28000 Supply Chain Security Specific Controls	The system must implement tracking and evidence collection specifically for ISO 28000:2022 Supply Chain Security Management System requirements relevant to container shipping operations. Security threat assessments must be maintained for each port location (Hamad Port risk profile, Khalifa Port risk profile, Jebel Ali risk profile, JNPT risk profile) covering cargo theft, piracy zones, smuggling, tampering, and cyber threats. The system must track implementation of security controls including container seal integrity verification (ISO 17712 high-security seals), yard access control logs, CCTV coverage assessments, personnel security screening records, and business partner security evaluations per C-TPAT and AEO criteria. Security incident reports must be linked to nonconformity records and feed into the continuous improvement cycle. Drill and exercise records must demonstrate preparedness for security scenarios specific to each port jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R009	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	ISO 14001 Environmental Compliance Tracking	The system must track environmental management system compliance specific to container shipping operations under ISO 14001:2015 certification scope. Environmental aspects and impacts must be cataloged for each operational site covering vessel emissions at berth (SOx, NOx, PM), container washing effluent discharge, reefer container refrigerant gas management (HFC phase-down tracking), waste oil and bilge water disposal, noise pollution at port terminals, and packaging waste from cargo operations. The system must track compliance with local environmental regulations: Qatar Ministry of Environment requirements, UAE Federal Law No. 24 of 1999, KSA General Authority for Meteorology and Environmental Protection standards, and India Coastal Regulation Zone notifications. Environmental KPI dashboards must show performance trends by site with targets aligned to the organization's carbon neutrality roadmap and IMO 2030/2050 emission reduction targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R010	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Certification Cost and Budget Management	The system must track all costs associated with ISO certification maintenance across entities and standards. Cost categories must include certification body audit fees, internal auditor time allocation, training and competency development costs, documentation system maintenance, corrective action implementation costs, and management time for review meetings. Costs must be captured in the local entity currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with USD-equivalent consolidation for group reporting. The system must support annual certification budget planning with variance analysis against actuals, cost allocation to certified sites and departments, and ROI analysis linking certification status to customer requirements (many shipping lines mandate ISO 9001 and ISO 28000 from their logistics partners). Budget approval workflows must route through entity finance controllers with group compliance officer oversight.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R011	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Competency and Training Records for ISO Compliance	The system must maintain competency and training records demonstrating that personnel involved in certified processes meet the competence requirements specified by each applicable ISO standard. For container handling staff at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, competency records must cover crane operation certifications, dangerous goods handling (IMDG Code), reefer container pre-trip inspection qualifications, and container damage assessment skills. For management system roles, records must track lead auditor qualifications (ISO 19011), standard-specific training completions, and refresher training schedules. The system must generate competency gap analysis reports by site and role, trigger automated training reminders for expiring qualifications, and link training records to nonconformity root cause analysis where competency gaps contributed to process failures. All training records must be maintained in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R012	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Integrated Management System Cross-Standard Mapping	The system must provide cross-reference mapping between ISO standards using the Annex SL High Level Structure, enabling integrated management system administration across ISO 9001, ISO 14001, ISO 27001, ISO 45001, and ISO 28000. The mapping must identify common clause requirements (Context of Organization, Leadership, Planning, Support, Operation, Performance Evaluation, Improvement) and highlight standard-specific additions. Integrated audit planning must allow a single audit to cover multiple standards simultaneously, reducing audit fatigue at port operation sites. The system must track evidence items that satisfy multiple standard requirements, preventing duplicate documentation efforts. Cross-standard dashboards must show compliance posture across all certifications for each entity with the ability to identify gaps where one standard is significantly weaker than others, enabling targeted improvement resource allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R013	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Certification Expiry Risk Alerting and Escalation	The system must implement a multi-tier alerting and escalation framework for ISO certification expiry risk management. Risk levels must be calculated based on days until certificate expiry, outstanding nonconformity status, scheduled audit completion status, and management review currency. Green status indicates more than 180 days to expiry with no outstanding major nonconformities. Amber status triggers at 90 days to expiry or presence of unresolved major nonconformities. Red status activates at 30 days to expiry or when the certification body has issued a suspension notice. Escalation chains must route alerts to progressively senior management: site quality manager at Green, entity compliance director at Amber, and group CEO and board audit committee at Red. The system must calculate business impact of potential certification loss including at-risk customer contracts that mandate specific ISO certifications, with total revenue exposure quantified in USD across all affected entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R014	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Customer and Stakeholder Certification Evidence Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing portal where shipping line clients, freight forwarders, and regulatory authorities can verify the organization's current ISO certification status. The portal must display active certificates with scope details, certification body information, and validity dates without requiring authentication for basic verification. Detailed certificate copies and audit summary reports must be available upon authenticated access with customer-specific permissions. The portal must support QR code generation on physical certificates linking to real-time online verification. Portal content must be presented in both Arabic and English with automatic language detection based on browser settings. The system must track portal access analytics showing which customers and stakeholders verify certifications most frequently, providing business intelligence on certification value to customer retention across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India markets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R015	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Regulatory Alignment Between ISO and Local Standards	The system must map ISO certification requirements to local regulatory standards in each operating jurisdiction to identify overlaps and gaps. For Qatar, ISO 9001 must be mapped against Qatar Quality Assurance standards and QP-HSE requirements for port operations. For UAE, ISO 45001 must be aligned with OSHAD SF requirements in Abu Dhabi and DMCC safety regulations in Dubai. For KSA, ISO 14001 must be cross-referenced with RCER (Royal Commission for Environmental Regulations) standards. For India, ISO 28000 must be aligned with AEO (Authorized Economic Operator) program requirements under CBIC. The system must generate gap analysis reports showing where ISO compliance automatically satisfies local regulatory requirements and where additional local-specific controls are needed. This mapping must be reviewed and updated whenever either the ISO standard or local regulation undergoes revision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-003-R016	FEAT-035-2-003	MOD-035	Continuous Improvement Project Tracking	The system must track continuous improvement projects arising from ISO management system activities including nonconformity corrective actions, audit findings, management review decisions, and employee suggestions. Each improvement project must be categorized by ISO standard relevance, linked entity and site (Qatar Hamad Port, UAE Khalifa Port or Jebel Ali, KSA operations, India JNPT), implementation priority, estimated cost in local currency, expected benefit quantification, and project status. The system must support PDCA (Plan-Do-Check-Act) methodology with milestone tracking for each phase. Improvement project dashboards must show active project counts by entity and standard, completion rates against targets, realized benefits versus projections, and trend analysis of improvement velocity. Annual management review inputs must automatically aggregate improvement project summaries demonstrating the organization's commitment to continual improvement as required by Clause 10 across all applicable ISO standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R001	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Multi-Dimensional Risk Scoring Engine	The system must implement a configurable AI-powered risk scoring engine that evaluates shipping transactions across multiple risk dimensions including financial risk (invoice amount anomalies, unusual payment patterns, currency conversion irregularities across QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), operational risk (route deviations, container weight discrepancies, port congestion impacts at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), compliance risk (sanctions screening matches, HS code classification errors, INCOTERMS misapplication), and counterparty risk (new trading partners, adverse media mentions, beneficial ownership opacity). Each dimension must contribute a weighted sub-score to an overall composite risk score on a 0-100 scale. Weights must be configurable by entity and jurisdiction, allowing KSA operations to weight sanctions risk higher than India operations, for example.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R002	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Transaction Anomaly Detection Using Machine Learning	The system must deploy machine learning models trained on historical container shipping transaction data to identify anomalous patterns indicative of fraud, errors, or compliance violations. Models must analyze freight invoice amounts against historical norms by trade lane (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT typical TEU rates), container booking patterns by customer, payment timing deviations from contractual credit terms, and unusual combinations of INCOTERMS with trade routes. The system must support multiple ML model types including isolation forests for outlier detection, LSTM networks for sequential pattern analysis of booking-shipping-invoicing cycles, and gradient boosted trees for classification of risk categories. Models must be retrained monthly on rolling 24-month data windows and must achieve a minimum precision of 85% and recall of 75% for high-risk transaction identification. Model performance metrics must be tracked and reported to compliance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R003	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Real-Time Sanctions and Watchlist Screening	The system must perform real-time AI-enhanced screening of all transaction counterparties, vessel names, and beneficial owners against global sanctions and watchlists including OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated List, UN Security Council sanctions, Qatar National Anti-Terrorism Committee lists, UAE Executive Office of AML/CFT lists, and India's UAPA designations. The AI engine must employ fuzzy name matching algorithms supporting transliteration variations between Arabic, English, and Hindi scripts (e.g., matching Mohammed/Muhammad/محمد variations). Screening must cover all parties on bills of lading including shipper, consignee, notify party, and freight payer. Match results must be categorized as Confirmed Match, Potential Match, or False Positive with AI-assigned confidence scores. Potential matches must be routed to compliance officers for manual review within a 4-hour SLA. The system must maintain a false positive whitelist to reduce repeat manual reviews while logging all whitelist decisions for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R004	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Trade-Based Money Laundering Pattern Detection	The system must implement AI models specifically designed to detect trade-based money laundering (TBML) patterns in container shipping operations. Detection patterns must include over and under-invoicing compared to market commodity prices and historical trade lane rates, multiple invoicing for the same shipment using different bill of lading numbers, phantom shipments where documentation exists but container tracking shows no physical movement, round-tripping where goods are exported and re-imported through intermediary ports (e.g., Jebel Ali free zone transit), and unusual trade patterns such as high-value goods on routes typically associated with low-value commodities. The system must cross-reference declared cargo values against customs tariff databases for each jurisdiction and flag deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (default 20% above or below expected values). TBML risk alerts must comply with FATF Recommendation 20 reporting requirements applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R005	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Counterparty Risk Profiling and Scoring	The system must maintain AI-generated risk profiles for all counterparties (customers, vendors, agents, shipping lines) involved in container shipping operations across all four entities. Risk profiles must incorporate financial health indicators (credit scores, payment history analysis, financial statement ratios), compliance history (prior sanctions matches, customs violations, regulatory penalties), operational behavior (booking cancellation rates, documentation error frequency, cargo inspection failure rates), and external intelligence (adverse media screening in Arabic and English, corporate registry changes, beneficial ownership updates). Risk scores must be dynamically updated with each new transaction and must decay over time if no negative indicators are detected. The system must classify counterparties into risk tiers (Low, Medium, High, Prohibited) with automated due diligence requirements escalating by tier. New counterparties must default to Medium risk until sufficient transaction history enables AI-driven reclassification after a minimum of 10 completed shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R006	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Route and Port Risk Assessment	The system must calculate and maintain dynamic risk scores for shipping routes and ports based on AI analysis of multiple risk factors. Route risk factors must include passage through high-risk maritime zones (Gulf of Aden, Strait of Hormuz, Malacca Strait), transit through sanctioned country waters, piracy incident frequency from IMB Piracy Reporting Centre data, and historical customs detention rates at destination ports. Port-specific risk scores must consider customs efficiency indices, corruption perception scores, cargo theft statistics, port state control detention rates, and political stability indicators. The system must automatically adjust risk scores based on current events including geopolitical tensions, weather disruptions, port labor strikes, and pandemic-related restrictions. Route risk scores must influence cargo insurance premium calculations and must trigger enhanced documentation requirements for shipments on routes scoring above 70 on the 0-100 risk scale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R007	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Predictive Compliance Failure Forecasting	The system must implement predictive AI models that forecast potential compliance failures before they occur, enabling proactive remediation. Forecasting areas must include customs declaration rejection probability based on HS code selection patterns, documentation completeness at time of booking, and historical clearance rates at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must predict SOX control failure likelihood based on transaction volume trends, staffing changes, system access pattern anomalies, and seasonal processing peaks. Regulatory filing deadline miss probability must be calculated based on data readiness indicators, preparer workload analysis, and historical filing timing patterns per jurisdiction. Each prediction must include a confidence interval and recommended preventive actions ranked by expected risk reduction impact. Predictions must be recalculated daily and presented in a forward-looking compliance risk dashboard with 7-day, 30-day, and 90-day horizon views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R008	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Natural Language Processing for Document Risk Analysis	The system must employ NLP models to analyze unstructured text in shipping documents including bills of lading, letters of credit, insurance certificates, customs declarations, and commercial invoices for compliance risk indicators. NLP analysis must detect inconsistencies between document fields (e.g., cargo description on BL versus commercial invoice), identify prohibited or dual-use goods descriptions requiring export license verification, flag vague or deliberately obscured cargo descriptions that may indicate smuggling attempts, and extract entity names for sanctions screening from narrative fields. The NLP engine must process documents in both Arabic and English, handling mixed-language documents common in Gulf region shipping operations. Document risk scores must be integrated into the overall transaction risk score and must trigger automated holds on high-risk shipments pending compliance officer review. The system must support continuous learning from compliance officer feedback on NLP-flagged documents to improve detection accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R009	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	AI Model Governance and Explainability Framework	The system must implement a comprehensive AI model governance framework ensuring all risk detection models are transparent, fair, and auditable. Each model must have documented model cards specifying training data sources, feature descriptions, performance metrics (precision, recall, F1 score, AUC-ROC), known limitations, and bias assessment results. The system must provide explainability for every risk score using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) or LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) values, showing which factors contributed most to each risk assessment. Model drift detection must continuously monitor input data distributions and model performance metrics, triggering automated alerts when accuracy degrades below configured thresholds. A model approval committee workflow must require sign-off from compliance, data science, and business stakeholders before deploying new models or significant model updates to production. All model decisions must be logged for regulatory audit purposes with retention aligned to jurisdiction-specific requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R007	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	User Group and Team Assignment	The system must support creation of user groups that aggregate multiple users for bulk permission assignment and notification routing. Groups must be definable by entity, department, port of operation, or custom criteria. For container shipping operations, pre-defined group templates must include: Vessel Operations Team (per port), Documentation Team (per entity), Customs Clearance Team (per port authority — Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs ZATCA, Indian Customs ICEGATE), and Finance & Settlement Team (per entity). Users may belong to multiple groups simultaneously. Group membership changes must take effect immediately without requiring user re-login. The system must support temporary group assignments with automatic expiry dates for seasonal or project-based shipping operations such as peak season reinforcement at Jebel Ali.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R010	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Automated Risk-Based Audit Sample Selection	The system must use AI risk scores to intelligently select audit samples for internal and external audit testing programs. Sample selection must consider the transaction risk score, monetary amount stratification in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), entity jurisdiction, transaction type (freight revenue, port charges, inter-company settlements, container lease payments), and temporal distribution across the audit period. The system must generate stratified samples that ensure coverage of high-risk transactions while maintaining statistical validity for extrapolation to the population. Sample sizes must be dynamically calculated based on control reliance strategy, population size, expected error rate, and desired confidence level (typically 95% for SOX testing). The system must prevent cherry-picking by generating tamper-evident sample lists with hash-sealed selection criteria. Audit sampling history must be maintained to ensure rotation of tested transactions across audit periods and to enable trend analysis of risk score accuracy versus actual audit findings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R011	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Container Cargo Misdeclaration Detection	The system must deploy AI models to detect potential cargo misdeclaration in container shipments across all four port jurisdictions. Detection methods must include weight discrepancy analysis comparing declared VGM (Verified Gross Mass) against historical weight distributions for the declared commodity and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), X-ray scan anomaly correlation where port authority scan results are available via API integration, trade pattern analysis identifying unusual commodity flows (e.g., high-value electronics declared on routes typically carrying raw materials), and tariff arbitrage detection where HS code selections consistently minimize duty exposure. The system must cross-reference declared cargo values against international commodity price indices adjusted for INCOTERMS and delivery point. Misdeclaration risk scores must be generated per container with visual indicators on the container tracking dashboard. High-risk containers must trigger automated notification to customs compliance teams at the relevant port for pre-clearance inspection coordination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R012	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Behavioral Analytics for Internal Fraud Detection	The system must implement behavioral analytics to detect internal fraud and unauthorized activities by ERP users across all four entity jurisdictions. Monitored behaviors must include unusual login patterns (off-hours access, concurrent sessions from different locations, access from unrecognized devices), abnormal transaction volumes or values compared to peer group baselines, systematic override of automated controls (approval limit bypasses, three-way match force-closes, manual exchange rate entries deviating from market rates), and data extraction patterns indicating potential data exfiltration. User behavior profiles must be established using unsupervised learning algorithms that adapt to role-specific normal patterns across different entity offices and time zones. Behavioral anomaly alerts must be routed confidentially to the entity compliance officer and group internal audit director, bypassing the suspected user's direct management chain to prevent tip-offs. Alert investigation workflows must support secure evidence preservation meeting forensic admissibility standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R013	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Risk Score Trend Analysis and Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive trend analysis and reporting capabilities for all AI-generated risk scores across the container shipping operation. Trend reports must show risk score evolution over configurable time periods (weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual) segmented by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), transaction type, counterparty category, and trade lane. The system must identify statistically significant risk score increases using control chart methodology (upper and lower control limits at 2 and 3 sigma) and generate automated narrative explanations for trending changes. Comparative analytics must benchmark entity risk profiles against each other and against industry baselines where available. Risk trend reports must be generated in both Arabic and English with appropriate RTL formatting and must be exportable as PDF, Excel, and interactive HTML dashboards. Executive summary reports must highlight the top 10 risk movers in each period with recommended action items for compliance management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R014	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Integration with External Risk Data Sources	The system must integrate with external risk data providers to enrich AI risk assessments with real-time intelligence. Required integrations must include maritime tracking services (AIS data for vessel position and route verification), global trade compliance databases (customs tariff classifications, restricted party lists, dual-use goods controls), credit risk agencies (Dun & Bradstreet, Bureau van Dijk for corporate risk data), adverse media monitoring services (multilingual news screening in Arabic, English, and Hindi), sanctions list update feeds (OFAC provides daily updates, EU provides weekly updates), and commodity price feeds for cargo value benchmarking. Each integration must have configurable refresh frequencies, data quality validation rules, and fallback mechanisms for service unavailability. The system must maintain a data lineage trail showing which external data sources contributed to each risk score calculation. API rate limits and costs per external data provider must be monitored and optimized to balance risk intelligence quality against operational cost across all entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R015	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Risk-Based Workflow Automation and Escalation	The system must automate compliance workflows based on AI-generated risk scores, routing transactions through appropriate review and approval chains. Low-risk transactions (score 0-30) must proceed through standard automated processing with post-transaction monitoring. Medium-risk transactions (score 31-60) must trigger enhanced due diligence requirements including additional documentation collection and supervisor review. High-risk transactions (score 61-85) must require compliance officer approval before processing with mandatory supporting evidence. Critical-risk transactions (score 86-100) must automatically halt processing and escalate to the entity compliance director and group chief compliance officer. Escalation timeframes must be configurable by entity with SLA targets: 4-hour response for critical-risk during business hours, 24-hour response for high-risk. The system must track escalation compliance rates and generate SLA adherence reports by entity and risk tier. Workflow automation rules must support jurisdiction-specific variations where KSA or Qatar regulations impose stricter review requirements than other entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R016	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Offline Risk Score Caching and Synchronization	The system must support offline risk assessment capabilities for port-side operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT where network connectivity may be intermittent. The offline cache must store the most recent risk scores for all active counterparties, the latest sanctions and watchlist data (updated at each successful synchronization), pre-computed risk thresholds and workflow rules per entity, and the trained ML model artifacts enabling local inference for basic risk scoring. Offline-computed risk scores must be flagged with an offline indicator and the timestamp of the last data synchronization. Upon reconnection, the system must perform delta synchronization, re-evaluate all offline-scored transactions against current data, and update risk scores accordingly. Any transactions that change risk tier upon re-evaluation must trigger retrospective compliance review notifications. The offline cache must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 and must be automatically purged if the device reports a security compromise event.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-035-2-004-R017	FEAT-035-2-004	MOD-035	Compliance Risk Heatmap and Geographic Visualization	The system must provide interactive geographic heatmap visualizations showing compliance risk concentrations across the container shipping network. The heatmap must overlay risk scores on a world map highlighting trade lanes between Hamad Port (Qatar), Khalifa Port (UAE), Jebel Ali (UAE), JNPT (India), and their global trading partner ports. Color intensity must reflect aggregate risk scores for each trade lane, port, or geographic region. Users must be able to filter the heatmap by risk dimension (financial, operational, compliance, counterparty), time period, entity, and container type. Drill-down capability must allow users to click on any port or trade lane to view underlying risk score distributions, top contributing risk factors, and trending indicators. The heatmap must support both Arabic and English labels with dynamic language switching. Geographic risk data must be exportable for integration with executive presentation materials and board reporting packages. The visualization must refresh at configurable intervals with minimum 15-minute granularity during business hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R001	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Multi-Entity User Provisioning	The system must support creation, modification, suspension, and deletion of user accounts across all four legal entities (Qatar entity operating at Hamad Port QAHMD, UAE entity at Khalifa Port AEKHL and Jebel Ali AEJEA, KSA entity at Dammam SADMM and Jeddah SAJED, and India entity at JNPT INJNP and Mundra INMUN). Each user must be associated with a primary entity and may be granted cross-entity access with explicit secondary entity assignments. User provisioning must capture full name in both English and Arabic, national ID or passport number, employee ID, department, designation, reporting manager, and contact details. The system must enforce unique email and employee ID constraints globally across all entities and maintain a complete audit trail of all provisioning actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R002	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Hierarchical Role Definition Framework	The system must support a hierarchical role structure with at minimum five levels: Super Admin, Entity Admin, Department Head, Operational Manager, and Operational User. Roles must be definable per entity with inheritance from parent roles. Each role must specify accessible modules (from MOD-001 through MOD-036), permitted actions (create, read, update, delete, approve, reject, escalate), and data scope (own records, department, entity, or global). Shipping-specific roles must be pre-configured including Vessel Operations Manager, Container Yard Supervisor, Customs Documentation Officer, Freight Rate Analyst, B/L Release Authority, and Port Liaison Officer. Role definitions must support Arabic display names and descriptions alongside English equivalents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R003	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Granular Permission Matrix Configuration	The system must provide a permission matrix that maps roles to specific features and sub-features within each module. Permissions must be configurable at field level, allowing administrators to make specific fields read-only, hidden, or mandatory per role. For example, the Freight Rate Analyst role may view and edit rate cards in MOD-008 but cannot access cost breakdowns in MOD-012. The permission matrix must support conditional permissions based on transaction value thresholds in multiple currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), and trade lane (intra-GCC, GCC-India, international). All permission changes must require maker-checker approval before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R004	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Entity-Level Data Isolation and Cross-Entity Access	The system must enforce strict data isolation between legal entities by default, ensuring that users of the Qatar entity cannot view or modify data belonging to the UAE, KSA, or India entities unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. Cross-entity access must be configurable at the role level with read-only or read-write granularity. When a user accesses data from a secondary entity, all monetary values must be displayed in both the source entity's base currency and the user's primary entity currency using the configured exchange rate from MOD-015. Audit logs must record every cross-entity data access event including the source entity, target entity, user ID, timestamp, and accessed record identifiers for regulatory compliance across QFCRA, ADGM, SAMA, and RBI jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R005	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Multi-Factor Authentication and Session Management	The system must enforce multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all user logins using at minimum two of: password, OTP via SMS or authenticator app, and biometric verification. Password policies must be configurable per entity to comply with local regulations: minimum 12 characters, complexity requirements, maximum age of 90 days, and prevention of last 12 password reuses. Session timeout must be configurable per role with default of 30 minutes for operational users and 15 minutes for admin roles. Concurrent session limits must be enforceable per user with a default maximum of 2 active sessions. The system must support IP whitelisting per entity to restrict access from authorized office networks and VPN endpoints at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT operational centers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R006	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Arabic RTL User Interface for RBAC Administration	The entire RBAC administration interface must be fully functional in right-to-left (RTL) Arabic layout. All role names, permission descriptions, user profile fields, and system messages must display correctly in Arabic with proper text alignment, mirrored navigation panels, and RTL-compatible data tables. Form labels and input fields must support bidirectional text entry for mixed Arabic-English content such as port names (ميناء حمد / Hamad Port) and container numbers (MAEU1234567). Date pickers must support both Gregorian and Hijri (Um Al-Qura) calendar systems simultaneously, displaying both dates where applicable. Error messages, validation prompts, and confirmation dialogs must render in the user's selected language. The RTL layout must maintain full functionality including drag-and-drop permission assignment and role hierarchy visualization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R008	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Role Assignment Workflow with Maker-Checker	All role assignments and modifications must follow a maker-checker workflow where the initiator (maker) cannot also approve (checker) the same request. The system must support configurable approval chains: single-level approval for standard role assignments and multi-level approval for privileged roles such as Super Admin, Entity Admin, and Financial Approver. Role assignment requests must capture justification text, effective date, and optional expiry date. The checker must be able to view the complete impact analysis showing what additional access the role change would grant, including module access, data scope expansion, and approval authority changes. Emergency role assignments must be supported with a bypass mechanism requiring documented justification, automatic 72-hour expiry, and mandatory post-hoc review notification to the Entity Admin and compliance officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R009	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Delegation and Acting Authority Management	The system must support temporary delegation of user authority when personnel are on leave, traveling between port locations, or otherwise unavailable. A user with appropriate permissions must be able to delegate their approval authority and operational access to a designated alternate for a specified date range. Delegations must be constrained to the delegator's own permission scope and cannot escalate privileges beyond what the delegator holds. The system must prevent circular delegations and limit delegation chains to a maximum depth of two. All actions performed under delegated authority must be clearly marked in the audit trail with both the acting user's identity and the delegating user's identity. Delegation rules must respect entity boundaries unless cross-entity delegation is explicitly enabled by the Entity Admin, which is particularly relevant for regional managers overseeing both Hamad Port and Jebel Ali operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R010	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	User Activity Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail of all user activities within the RBAC module including login attempts (successful and failed), role changes, permission modifications, group membership updates, and delegation events. Each audit record must capture user ID, IP address, timestamp in UTC with entity-local time conversion, action type, affected record, before-state, after-state, and browser or device fingerprint. Audit logs must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with QFCRA, ADGM, SAMA, and RBI regulatory requirements. The system must provide pre-built compliance reports for: user access reviews (quarterly), privileged access reports (monthly), dormant account identification (accounts inactive for 90+ days), and segregation of duties violation detection. Reports must be exportable in PDF (with Arabic support) and Excel formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R011	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Segregation of Duties Enforcement	The system must enforce segregation of duties (SoD) rules that prevent conflicting role combinations from being assigned to the same user. Pre-configured SoD rules must include: a user cannot both create and approve the same purchase order, a user cannot both issue and release a Bill of Lading (B/L), a user cannot both set freight rates and approve customer contracts, and a user with vendor master data maintenance access cannot also process vendor payments. Administrators must be able to define custom SoD rules specifying conflicting permission pairs. When a role assignment would violate an SoD rule, the system must block the assignment and display the specific conflict. An exception mechanism must exist requiring approval from the Entity Admin and compliance officer with documented business justification, compensating controls, and a review expiry date not exceeding 180 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R012	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	API and Integration Access Token Management	The system must manage API access tokens and service accounts used for system-to-system integrations with port community systems (QPorts at Hamad Port, DMCA Dubaitrade at Jebel Ali, FASAH in KSA, PCS1x at JNPT), shipping line EDI connections (INTTRA, CargoSmart, GSBN), and customs authorities (Qatar Customs ASYCUDA, Dubai Customs Mirsal-2, ZATCA FASAH, ICEGATE). Each API token must be associated with a service account that has defined role-based permissions limiting accessible endpoints and data scope. Tokens must support configurable expiry periods (30, 60, 90, 180, 365 days), IP restriction, and rate limiting. The system must alert administrators 14 days before token expiry and automatically disable expired tokens. Token creation and rotation must follow the maker-checker workflow and all API calls must be logged with the service account identity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R013	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Bulk User Operations and Import/Export	The system must support bulk user operations for efficient onboarding during entity setup or organizational restructuring. Bulk import must accept CSV and Excel files with a standardized template containing user details, entity assignment, role assignment, and group membership. The import process must validate all records before committing, reporting errors with specific row numbers and field-level validation failures. Duplicate detection must check against existing users by email, employee ID, and national ID. Bulk export must generate a complete user directory per entity with role and permission summaries. Bulk operations must support: activation, deactivation, role reassignment, entity transfer, and password reset. All bulk operations must generate a summary audit record and individual audit records per affected user. The system must limit bulk operations to a maximum of 500 users per batch to prevent system overload.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R014	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Self-Service User Profile and Preference Management	The system must provide a self-service portal where authenticated users can manage their own profile information within permitted boundaries. Users must be able to update contact details (phone, secondary email), change password, configure MFA settings, set preferred language (English or Arabic), select preferred calendar system (Gregorian or Hijri), choose default currency display preference from entity-supported currencies (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity, with USD as universal option), set timezone, and configure notification preferences (email, SMS, in-app). Users must not be able to modify their own role assignments, entity associations, or employee ID. Profile photo upload must be supported with a maximum file size of 2 MB and acceptable formats of JPG and PNG. All self-service changes must be logged in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R001	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Registration with Protocol Configuration	The system must allow administrators to register new integration endpoints by specifying the endpoint URL, communication protocol (REST, SOAP, SFTP, AS2, EDIFACT), authentication method (OAuth2, API Key, mTLS, SAML), and associated entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each endpoint registration must capture the target system type such as port community system (e.g., Maqta Gateway for Khalifa Port, NVOCC system for JNPT), customs authority (Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi ZATCA, Indian CBIC), or shipping line API. The registration form must support both English and Arabic labels with proper RTL alignment for Arabic field entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R015	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	Offline Access Mode and Synchronization for RBAC	The system must support a limited offline access mode for users operating in areas with intermittent connectivity, such as container yards at Hamad Port, remote warehouses, or vessel-side operations. The offline mode must cache the user's current role, permissions, and recent authorization decisions locally using encrypted storage. While offline, the system must allow read access to previously loaded data and queue write operations for synchronization when connectivity is restored. Offline sessions must have a maximum duration of 8 hours, after which re-authentication is mandatory. The synchronization process must handle conflicts using a last-write-wins strategy with conflict logging for administrative review. Offline access must be an opt-in feature configurable per role, and Super Admin and Entity Admin roles must be excluded from offline access for security reasons. The system must log all offline access periods with duration and actions performed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-001-R016	FEAT-036-1-001	MOD-036	User Lifecycle and Offboarding Automation	The system must automate the user offboarding process when an employee exits the organization or transfers between entities. Upon triggering offboarding (via HR integration or manual initiation), the system must immediately revoke all active sessions, disable the user account, revoke all API tokens associated with the user, reassign pending approval tasks to the user's reporting manager or designated alternate, transfer ownership of open shipment bookings, pending B/L releases, and in-progress customs declarations to a designated successor, and archive the user's access history. The offboarding process must generate a compliance report listing all access the user held, data they accessed in the last 90 days, and any pending items transferred. For cross-entity users, offboarding must be coordinated across all associated entities. Offboarded accounts must be retained in a disabled state for 7 years per regulatory requirements before permanent anonymization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R001	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Entity-Level Module Activation Control	The system must provide a centralized dashboard for Super Admins and Entity Admins to activate or deactivate individual ERP modules (MOD-001 through MOD-036) per legal entity. Module activation must be independent per entity: for example, the Reefer Container Monitoring module (MOD-023) may be active for the UAE entity at Jebel Ali but inactive for the KSA entity if no reefer operations exist there. Activating a module must automatically enable its mandatory dependencies (e.g., activating Freight Invoicing MOD-018 must ensure Base Finance MOD-015 is already active). Deactivating a module must warn administrators of dependent modules that will be affected and require confirmation. Module activation status changes must take effect within 60 seconds without requiring system restart. The dashboard must display module health status, active user count per module, and last configuration change timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R002	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Feature Flag Management Within Modules	Each module must expose a configurable set of feature flags that administrators can toggle to enable or disable specific capabilities. Feature flags must support four states: enabled for all users, enabled for specific roles only (progressive rollout), disabled, and experimental (beta). For example, within the Booking module (MOD-005), features like automated slot allocation at Hamad Port, hazardous cargo DG booking workflow, and reefer temperature pre-set might each be independently togglable. Feature flags must support scheduled activation with future effective dates for planned go-live events. The system must maintain a history of all feature flag changes with before and after states, changed-by user, and business justification. Feature flags must be manageable via both the admin UI and a REST API for CI/CD integration during phased deployments across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R003	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Dependency Graph Visualization	The system must display an interactive dependency graph showing relationships between all ERP modules. The graph must visually distinguish mandatory dependencies (hard links where one module cannot function without another) from optional integrations (soft links that enhance functionality). For example, the Customs Declaration module (MOD-011) has mandatory dependencies on Document Management (MOD-009) and Customer Master (MOD-003), and optional integrations with AI Classification (MOD-030) and Reefer Monitoring (MOD-023). The graph must highlight circular dependency risks, show activation status per entity using color coding, and allow administrators to simulate the impact of activating or deactivating a module before committing changes. The visualization must support both English and Arabic labels, render correctly in RTL mode, and be exportable as PNG or SVG for documentation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R004	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Configuration Parameter Management	The system must allow administrators to configure module-specific operational parameters without code changes. Configuration parameters must be typed (string, integer, decimal, boolean, date, dropdown selection, multi-select) and validated against defined constraints. Examples of configurable parameters include: default container free days per port (Hamad Port typically 7 days, Jebel Ali 5 days, JNPT 3 days), demurrage rate tiers in local currency, auto-release threshold amounts for B/L release, customs declaration auto-filing cut-off hours before vessel ETA, and maximum TEU capacity per booking per vessel call. Each parameter must have a description, default value, permissible range, and entity override capability. Parameter changes must follow a maker-checker approval workflow and the system must support rollback to previous parameter values with one-click revert functionality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R005	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Entity-Specific Localization Configuration	The system must provide configuration for entity-specific localization settings including: primary language (Arabic for Qatar/UAE/KSA entities, English and Hindi for India entity), secondary language, default calendar system (Hijri for GCC entities, Gregorian for India entity), date format preferences (dd/MM/yyyy for GCC, dd-MM-yyyy for India), number formatting (Arabic-Indic numerals ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩ option for Arabic interface), currency decimal precision (2 decimals for QAR/AED/SAR, 2 for INR, 2 for USD), and timezone (AST UTC+3 for Qatar, GST UTC+4 for UAE, AST UTC+3 for KSA, IST UTC+5:30 for India). These localization settings must cascade as defaults to all modules within the entity but individual modules must be able to override specific settings where operationally required, such as using UTC for vessel scheduling modules regardless of entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R002	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Health Check Scheduling	The system must support configurable health check intervals for each registered endpoint, with minimum granularity of 30 seconds and maximum interval of 24 hours. Health checks must verify connectivity, response time, SSL certificate validity, and expected response schema. For critical port system endpoints such as Hamad Port vessel scheduling API or Jebel Ali container tracking gateway, the system must enforce a maximum health check interval of 5 minutes. Health check results must be stored for a minimum of 90 days to support SLA compliance reporting across all four country operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R006	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Health Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time health monitoring dashboard for all active modules across all entities. For each module, the dashboard must display: service uptime percentage (current day, 7-day, 30-day), average response time in milliseconds, error rate percentage, active user count, pending transaction queue depth, last successful database sync timestamp, and integration endpoint health for connected external systems (port community systems, customs authorities, shipping lines). Health status must be classified as healthy (green), degraded (yellow), or critical (red) based on configurable thresholds. The dashboard must support drill-down from module level to individual service endpoint level. Alerts must be configurable to notify administrators via email, SMS, or in-app notification when module health degrades below defined thresholds. The monitoring data must be retained for 90 days for trend analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R007	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module License and Subscription Management	The system must track module licensing and subscription status per entity, supporting various licensing models: per-user, per-transaction (e.g., per B/L issued, per customs declaration filed), per-TEU processed, and flat-rate entity-wide licenses. The system must display current usage against licensed limits and alert administrators when usage reaches 80% and 95% of licensed capacity. For transaction-based modules, the system must track consumption across billing periods and support automatic license renewal notifications 30 days before expiry. The licensing dashboard must show cost allocation per entity in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD. When a module reaches its licensed capacity, the system must enforce the configured policy: either soft block (warn and allow) or hard block (prevent new transactions until license is upgraded). License compliance reports must be generated monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R008	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Environment Configuration Management	The system must support multiple environment configurations (production, staging, UAT, development) with the ability to promote configuration sets between environments. Configuration promotion must support selective promotion where administrators choose specific parameter changes to promote rather than full environment sync. The system must display a diff view comparing configuration values between any two environments, highlighting differences in module activation, feature flags, parameter values, and role definitions. Configuration export must produce a versioned JSON or YAML manifest file that can be imported into another environment. The manifest must exclude sensitive data such as API keys, passwords, and connection strings, replacing them with placeholder tokens. Import validation must check compatibility before applying and provide a dry-run mode showing what would change without committing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R009	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Custom Field and Form Extension Configuration	The system must allow administrators to add custom fields to standard module forms without code deployment. Custom fields must support types including: text, number, decimal, date (Gregorian and Hijri), dropdown (with configurable options in English and Arabic), multi-select, file attachment, and lookup reference to other entities. Custom fields must be assignable to specific entities, allowing the Qatar entity to have additional fields for Qatar Free Zone classification on the booking form that do not appear for other entities. Each custom field must have configurable validation rules (required, regex pattern, min/max values, conditional visibility based on other field values). Custom fields must be indexable for search, includable in reports, and exposable via API. The system must support a maximum of 50 custom fields per module per entity to maintain performance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R010	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Workflow and Business Rule Configuration	The system must provide a visual workflow designer for administrators to configure business process workflows within modules without code changes. Workflows must support sequential steps, parallel branches, conditional routing based on field values (e.g., if container type is reefer, add temperature verification step), approval gates, automated actions (send notification, update status, call external API), timer-based escalations, and loop-back for rework. Workflow templates must be pre-configured for common shipping processes: booking approval, B/L amendment, freight rate approval, customs declaration review, and demurrage waiver request. Workflows must be configurable per entity with entity-specific steps such as mandatory ZATCA e-invoicing validation for KSA entity or GST compliance check for India entity. The designer must display correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R011	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Notification Template Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure notification templates for system-generated communications across all modules. Templates must support multiple channels: email (HTML and plain text), SMS, in-app notification, and WhatsApp Business API. Each template must be configurable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content. Templates must support dynamic placeholders for shipping-specific data: vessel name, voyage number, container number, B/L number, port of loading, port of discharge, ETA/ETD, consignee name, and shipper name. Notification triggers must be configurable per module event (e.g., booking confirmed, vessel arrived, container gated-in, customs cleared, invoice generated). Administrators must be able to define recipient rules based on role, department, or specific user assignments. Template versioning must be maintained with the ability to preview rendered templates before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R012	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Data Retention Policy Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure data retention policies per module per entity to comply with varying regulatory requirements across jurisdictions. Retention periods must be configurable for: active data (readily accessible), archived data (stored but retrievable on request), and purged data (permanently deleted). Pre-configured retention defaults must reflect regulatory minimums: Qatar QFCRA requires 10 years for financial records, UAE Commercial Companies Law requires 5 years for trade documents, KSA ZATCA requires 6 years for tax-related records, and India GST Act requires 6 years plus current year. Shipping-specific documents such as Bills of Lading, manifests, and customs declarations must have separate retention rules from transactional data. The system must automate archival processes running during off-peak hours and generate compliance certificates confirming retention policy adherence for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R013	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Performance Threshold Configuration	The system must allow administrators to define performance thresholds and SLA targets per module. Configurable metrics must include: maximum acceptable page load time (default 3 seconds), API response time (default 500ms for read, 2000ms for write), report generation time (default 30 seconds for standard reports, 5 minutes for complex analytics), batch processing throughput (minimum records per minute), and concurrent user capacity. Thresholds must be settable per entity to account for varying infrastructure (cloud region proximity to Doha, Dubai, Riyadh, Mumbai). When a module breaches its configured threshold, the system must generate alerts to technical administrators with diagnostic information including the specific operation, response time recorded, and server resource utilization at the time. Historical performance data against thresholds must be viewable in trend charts for capacity planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R014	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Third-Party Integration Endpoint Configuration	The system must provide a centralized interface for configuring all external integration endpoints used by modules. For each integration, administrators must configure: endpoint URL, authentication method (API key, OAuth 2.0, certificate-based), timeout values, retry policy (count and backoff interval), and circuit breaker thresholds. Pre-defined integration profiles must exist for: QPorts API (Hamad Port), Dubaitrade (Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port), FASAH (KSA ports), PCS1x/ICEGATE (Indian ports JNPT, Mundra), INTTRA (booking and tracking), CargoSmart (visibility), shipping line APIs (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, COSCO), and payment gateways. Each integration must support environment-specific configurations (production vs sandbox endpoints). Health check pings must be configurable to periodically verify endpoint availability and the system must maintain a 30-day log of all integration call success and failure rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R015	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Version and Update Management	The system must track and display the current version of each deployed module and support controlled update rollout across entities. Version information must include: current version number (semantic versioning), deployment date, changelog summary, and database migration status. Updates must support phased rollout where a new module version can be deployed to one entity (e.g., Qatar entity as pilot) before rolling out to remaining entities. The system must perform pre-update compatibility checks verifying that dependent modules meet minimum version requirements and that custom configurations (custom fields, workflows, templates) remain compatible with the new version. Rollback capability must be available within 24 hours of update deployment, restoring both code and database schema to the previous version. Update deployment windows must be configurable per entity to respect local business hours and avoid peak shipping operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R016	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Usage Analytics and Adoption Tracking	The system must collect and display usage analytics for each module and feature to help administrators understand adoption patterns and identify underutilized capabilities. Tracked metrics must include: daily/weekly/monthly active users per module per entity, most frequently accessed features, average session duration per module, feature adoption rate (percentage of available features actively used), and user journey heatmaps showing navigation patterns. Analytics must segment data by entity, department, role, and port location. The system must identify features with zero usage in the last 30 days and flag them for review. Comparative analytics between entities must help identify best practices where one entity achieves higher automation rates in a module. Reports must support export in PDF with Arabic support and Excel formats, and data must be accessible via API for integration with external BI tools such as Power BI or Tableau.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-002-R017	FEAT-036-1-002	MOD-036	Module Maintenance Mode and Scheduled Downtime	The system must allow administrators to place individual modules into maintenance mode for planned updates, data migrations, or infrastructure changes without affecting other modules. When a module enters maintenance mode, active users must receive a configurable advance notification (default 15 minutes) in their selected language (English or Arabic). During maintenance mode, the module must display a customizable maintenance page showing estimated restoration time and alternative contact information. Maintenance windows must be schedulable in advance with automatic entry and exit at configured times. The system must prevent scheduling overlapping maintenance windows for dependent modules. Emergency maintenance mode must be available for immediate activation by Entity Admin or Super Admin. All maintenance events must be logged with start time, end time, initiating user, reason, and affected entity. Users with in-progress transactions must receive specific guidance on how their work will be preserved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R001	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Autonomy Level Configuration Per Module	The system must provide a configuration interface to set AI agent autonomy levels for each module on a scale from Level 1 (AI suggests, human decides) to Level 5 (fully autonomous AI with post-action audit). Each module must have an independently configurable autonomy level per entity, allowing the Qatar entity to operate booking automation at Level 4 (AI decides with human notification) while the India entity, with more complex customs requirements at JNPT, operates at Level 3 (AI decides within predefined boundaries). The target autonomy distribution must trend toward the 95-5 model: 95% of transactions processed at Level 4 or 5 (fully automated), and 5% escalated to human review at Level 1-3. The system must display current autonomy distribution metrics per module per entity against the 95-5 target in a real-time dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R002	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Confidence Threshold Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure confidence score thresholds that determine when AI agents can act autonomously versus when they must escalate to human review. Thresholds must be configurable per decision type within each module. For example, in the Customs Classification module (MOD-011), HS code classification decisions with AI confidence above 95% may proceed automatically, decisions between 80-95% require human verification, and below 80% must be escalated to a senior customs specialist. Thresholds must be independently configurable for different container types (standard dry containers vs hazardous DG cargo vs reefer vs out-of-gauge), trade lanes (intra-GCC vs intercontinental), and transaction values (below USD 10,000 vs above). The system must provide historical accuracy data to help administrators calibrate thresholds appropriately, showing false positive and false negative rates at each threshold level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R003	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Decision Boundary Rule Configuration	The system must provide a rule engine for defining explicit boundaries within which AI agents may operate autonomously. Rules must be expressible as conditions on transaction attributes with configurable actions (auto-approve, escalate, reject, hold for review). Example rules for shipping operations: AI may auto-approve booking amendments if the change is within 2 TEU of original booking and vessel departure is more than 72 hours away; AI may auto-classify HS codes for commodities previously classified with 99%+ accuracy in the last 100 transactions; AI may auto-calculate demurrage charges for standard containers but must escalate reefer containers (20RF, 40RF) and out-of-gauge (40FR, 20OT) for human verification. Rules must support compound conditions using AND/OR logic, entity-specific overrides, and effective date ranges. Each rule must have a unique identifier, description in English and Arabic, and be testable in simulation mode before production activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R004	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Escalation Path and Human Review Queue Configuration	The system must allow configuration of escalation paths when AI agents encounter decisions exceeding their autonomy boundaries. Each escalation path must define: the target human reviewer (by role, specific user, or team), escalation priority (normal, high, critical), maximum response time SLA (e.g., 30 minutes for vessel-related decisions, 4 hours for documentation reviews, 24 hours for rate approvals), and auto-escalation rules if the primary reviewer does not respond within the SLA. The human review queue must be configurable per entity with filters for module, decision type, AI confidence score, and transaction value. Queue assignment algorithms must support round-robin, load-balanced, and expertise-based routing (e.g., route DG cargo decisions to certified IMDG-trained staff). The system must track escalation volumes and resolution times to identify modules where AI boundaries need adjustment to achieve the 95-5 target.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R005	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Training Data and Learning Configuration	The system must provide configuration for AI agent learning and model improvement processes. Administrators must be able to configure: whether AI agents learn from human override decisions (feedback loop toggle per module), the minimum number of consistent human overrides before AI auto-adjusts its behavior (default 10), data sources the AI agent can access for decision-making per module, and prohibited data fields that must be excluded from AI processing (e.g., personal identification numbers for data privacy compliance). The system must support configuration of model retraining schedules (daily, weekly, monthly) per AI agent. Administrators must be able to review and approve AI model updates before deployment using an A/B testing framework where the updated model runs in shadow mode alongside the current production model. Model performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score) must be displayed per module with trend analysis over configurable time periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R006	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Exception Pattern Recognition Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure patterns that AI agents should recognize as exceptions requiring mandatory human intervention regardless of confidence score. Exception patterns for container shipping must include: first-time shipper or consignee (no transaction history), sanctioned country or entity involvement (OFAC, EU, UN sanctions lists), unusually high-value cargo exceeding entity-defined thresholds (e.g., above USD 500,000 per container), dangerous goods classifications (IMO classes 1, 6.2, and 7), transshipment through high-risk ports, and discrepancies between declared cargo and AI-detected anomalies in documentation. Exception patterns must be configurable per entity to reflect varying risk appetites: the KSA entity may have additional ZATCA-specific exception rules, while the India entity may require exceptions for cargo destined for restricted areas. Each exception pattern must log the trigger reason and route to the appropriate specialist reviewer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R007	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Performance Monitoring and 95-5 KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard tracking AI agent performance against the 95-5 automation target. Key metrics must include: overall automation rate (percentage of transactions processed without human intervention), automation rate per module and per entity, human escalation rate and reasons, AI decision accuracy (measured against subsequent human overrides), average AI processing time versus human processing time for comparable decisions, and cost savings from automation. The dashboard must display real-time data with hourly, daily, weekly, and monthly aggregation views. Trend analysis must show automation rate trajectory with projected date to achieve 95% automation per module. The system must generate automated weekly reports for Entity Admins highlighting modules below 90% automation rate with root cause analysis of common escalation reasons. All dashboard text and labels must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R008	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Per-Transaction Override Logging	The system must log every instance where a human overrides an AI agent decision, capturing: the original AI recommendation, AI confidence score, the human decision, reason for override (selected from configurable categories plus free-text), time taken for human review, the identity of the human reviewer, and the module and transaction context. Override categories must include: AI incorrect classification, missing context that AI could not assess, regulatory requirement not captured in AI rules, customer relationship consideration, commercial negotiation factor, and port-specific operational constraint. The system must aggregate override data to identify systematic AI weaknesses: if more than 5% of AI decisions in a specific category are overridden in a rolling 30-day window, the system must alert the AI configuration administrator with a recommendation to adjust confidence thresholds or decision boundary rules for that category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R009	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Multi-Currency Decision Configuration	The system must configure how AI agents handle multi-currency decisions across the four entities. AI agents must be configurable to: evaluate transaction values in a common reference currency (default USD) for consistent threshold comparison while displaying in local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), apply entity-specific tolerance ranges for exchange rate fluctuations when comparing quoted rates against market rates, and use configurable exchange rate sources (central bank rates from QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI versus commercial bank rates). AI decision boundaries that involve monetary thresholds must automatically adjust based on configured exchange rates: for example, an auto-approval threshold of USD 50,000 must dynamically convert to approximately QAR 182,000, AED 183,650, SAR 187,500, or INR 4,175,000 based on current rates. The system must alert administrators when exchange rate volatility exceeds configured bounds, as this may affect AI decision accuracy for value-based rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R010	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Simulation and What-If Testing Mode	The system must provide a simulation environment where administrators can test AI agent configurations before deploying to production. The simulation mode must allow: replaying historical transactions through modified AI configurations to compare outcomes, injecting synthetic test scenarios representing edge cases (e.g., a booking for 500 TEU of hazardous cargo from Hamad Port to JNPT with transshipment at Jebel Ali), adjusting confidence thresholds and observing the impact on automation rates and accuracy, and stress-testing AI decision boundaries with high-volume concurrent transaction scenarios. Simulation results must display side-by-side comparisons of current production configuration versus proposed configuration, showing metrics for automation rate change, escalation volume change, estimated accuracy change, and projected impact on the 95-5 target. Simulation environments must be isolated from production data and must not affect live operations or generate real notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R011	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Shipping Document Processing Configuration	The system must provide specific AI configuration for document processing agents that handle Bills of Lading, commercial invoices, packing lists, certificates of origin, phytosanitary certificates, and customs declarations. Configurable parameters must include: OCR confidence threshold for field extraction (default 90%), mandatory field validation rules per document type, cross-document consistency checks (e.g., weights on packing list must match B/L within 2% tolerance), auto-matching rules between purchase orders and shipping documents, and language detection and translation configuration for documents received in Arabic, English, Hindi, or other languages. The system must support configuration of document templates per shipping line (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd each have unique B/L formats) and per port authority (Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, ZATCA, ICEGATE each require different declaration formats). AI processing must be configurable to flag discrepancies above threshold rather than auto-correcting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R012	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Compliance and Regulatory Guardrails	The system must enforce non-overridable compliance guardrails that AI agents must respect regardless of autonomy level or confidence score. These guardrails must include: mandatory sanctions screening for all parties in every transaction (no AI bypass allowed), customs valuation rules per WTO Valuation Agreement that AI must apply for declared values, SOLAS VGM (Verified Gross Mass) requirements for all export containers, ISPS (International Ship and Port Facility Security) code compliance checks, IMDG code compliance for all dangerous goods classifications, and trade embargo restrictions applicable to each entity's jurisdiction. Guardrails must be maintained by the compliance team with Super Admin approval and must be versioned with effective dates to handle regulatory changes. Any AI agent attempting to process a transaction that violates a compliance guardrail must be automatically blocked with a detailed violation report. The system must track guardrail trigger frequency for regulatory reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R013	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Load Balancing and Failover Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure AI agent resource allocation and failover behavior across the multi-entity deployment. Configuration must include: maximum concurrent AI processing requests per module (to prevent resource starvation), priority queuing rules (e.g., time-sensitive vessel operations at Jebel Ali take priority over routine documentation tasks), AI processing timeout limits per decision type (default 30 seconds for real-time decisions, 5 minutes for batch processing), and failover behavior when AI services are unavailable (options: queue for later processing, fall back to manual workflow, apply default conservative decision). Load balancing must distribute AI workload across entities based on configured weights reflecting transaction volumes (Jebel Ali typically highest volume, followed by JNPT, Hamad Port, then KSA ports). The system must monitor AI agent resource utilization and auto-scale within configured limits. All failover events must be logged and reported.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R014	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Audit and Explainability Configuration	The system must configure AI agent explainability requirements to ensure transparency and regulatory compliance. For every autonomous AI decision, the system must be configurable to generate: a human-readable explanation of the decision rationale (in both English and Arabic), the key data points that influenced the decision with their relative weights, alternative decisions considered and reasons for rejection, applicable rules and thresholds that were evaluated, and a confidence breakdown by decision factor. Explainability detail level must be configurable per module: summary only for routine decisions (booking confirmations, standard rate applications), detailed explanation for significant decisions (customs classification, freight rate adjustments above USD 5,000), and full forensic trail for high-value or disputed decisions. Explanations must reference specific business rules, regulatory requirements, and historical precedents used in the decision. All AI decision explanations must be stored and searchable for audit purposes with a minimum retention of 7 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R015	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Cross-Module Orchestration Configuration	The system must allow configuration of AI agent orchestration for cross-module workflows where multiple AI agents collaborate on end-to-end shipping processes. Configurable orchestration scenarios must include: booking-to-delivery workflow (spanning MOD-005 Booking, MOD-007 Container Tracking, MOD-011 Customs, MOD-018 Invoicing), import clearance workflow (spanning MOD-011 Customs Declaration, MOD-009 Document Management, MOD-015 Finance, MOD-023 Reefer Monitoring if applicable), and exception handling cascade (where an AI exception in one module triggers review requirements in dependent modules). The orchestration configuration must define: data handoff points between AI agents, aggregate confidence scoring across the workflow chain (overall confidence is the product of individual agent confidences), circuit breaker conditions where a failure in one agent halts the entire workflow, and compensation actions for partial workflow rollbacks. Orchestration flows must be visualizable in a workflow diagram.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R003	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Authentication Credential Vault	The system must store all endpoint authentication credentials in an encrypted vault using AES-256 encryption at rest with HSM-backed key management. Credentials must include API keys, OAuth client secrets, mTLS certificates, and SFTP private keys. Each credential set must be associated with a specific entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) and must support automatic rotation scheduling. The vault must maintain an audit trail of all credential access events including accessor identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the specific endpoint for which the credential was retrieved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R016	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Agent Entity-Specific Behavioral Customization	The system must support entity-specific AI behavioral customization that reflects the distinct operational characteristics and regulatory environments of each entity. For the Qatar entity at Hamad Port, AI agents must be configurable with Qatar-specific customs tariff rules, Qatar Free Zone regulations, and QCB financial compliance requirements. For the UAE entity at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, AI must accommodate Dubai Multi Commodities Centre (DMCC) rules, JAFZA free zone procedures, and ADGM regulations. For the KSA entity, AI must incorporate ZATCA VAT and e-invoicing requirements, Saudi Customs FASAH platform rules, and Vision 2030 compliance metrics. For the India entity at JNPT and Mundra, AI must handle GST calculations, ICEGATE electronic filing requirements, and RBI foreign exchange regulations (FEMA). Each entity's AI customization must be independently versioned, deployable, and testable without affecting other entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-003-R017	FEAT-036-1-003	MOD-036	AI Continuous Improvement Feedback Loop Configuration	The system must provide a configurable feedback loop mechanism that continuously improves AI agent performance toward the 95% automation target. The feedback loop must capture: human decisions on AI-escalated cases as training data, user satisfaction ratings on AI-automated decisions (optional thumbs-up/down), operational outcome data (did the AI-approved booking result in successful delivery, did the AI-classified HS code pass customs without query), and time-series performance data showing improvement trajectories. The system must support configurable improvement campaigns where administrators can focus AI learning on specific weak areas: for example, a 30-day campaign to improve AI accuracy on reefer container temperature range validation at Jebel Ali. Improvement targets must be settable per module with milestone tracking (e.g., achieve 90% automation by end of Q1, 93% by end of Q2, 95% by end of Q3). The system must generate monthly AI improvement reports with recommendations for configuration changes that could increase automation rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R001	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Multi-Dimensional Approval Matrix Definition	The system must support definition of approval matrices across multiple dimensions: transaction type (booking, freight rate, credit note, demurrage waiver, B/L amendment, vendor payment, capital expenditure), transaction value range, entity, department, cost center, and trade lane. Each cell in the matrix must specify the required approver role(s), number of approval levels, and whether approvals are sequential or parallel. For example, a freight rate discount exceeding 15% on the Hamad Port to JNPT trade lane for a transaction value above USD 100,000 may require sequential approval from: Regional Sales Manager (Level 1), Commercial Director (Level 2), and CFO (Level 3). The matrix must support conditional branching where the approval path changes based on transaction attributes such as container type (standard vs reefer vs DG), customer credit rating, or whether the shipment involves sanctioned-adjacent routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R002	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Multi-Currency DOA Threshold Configuration	The system must support Delegation of Authority thresholds configured in multiple currencies with automatic cross-currency conversion for approval determination. Each approver role must have DOA limits defined in the entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with equivalent USD limits for cross-entity comparisons. For example, an Operations Manager at Hamad Port may have a DOA limit of QAR 200,000 (approximately USD 54,900) for booking amendments, while the same role at Jebel Ali has a DOA limit of AED 200,000 (approximately USD 54,500). The system must automatically convert transaction values to the approver's DOA currency using the configured exchange rate source and apply the appropriate threshold. Currency conversion must use the exchange rate applicable at the transaction date, not the approval date. The system must handle edge cases where exchange rate fluctuations cause a transaction to cross a DOA threshold between initiation and approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R003	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Entity-Specific Approval Hierarchy Management	The system must maintain separate approval hierarchies per legal entity reflecting the organizational structure and local regulatory requirements of each jurisdiction. The Qatar entity must support approval hierarchies aligned with QFCRA governance requirements including board-level approvals for transactions exceeding QAR 5,000,000. The UAE entity must support dual hierarchies for JAFZA and mainland operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port respectively. The KSA entity must incorporate ZATCA compliance approvals for VAT-related transactions and e-invoicing. The India entity must include approvals mandated by the Companies Act 2013 and RBI regulations for foreign exchange transactions under FEMA. Each entity hierarchy must support a minimum of 6 approval levels from operational staff through to board of directors. Cross-entity transactions (e.g., intercompany charges between Qatar and UAE entities for transshipment services at Jebel Ali) must trigger approval requirements in both the originating and receiving entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R004	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Shipping-Specific Approval Category Definition	The system must provide pre-configured approval categories specific to container shipping operations. Categories must include: freight rate negotiation and discount approval, demurrage and detention charge waiver, container damage claim settlement, B/L amendment after vessel departure, cargo insurance claim approval, empty container repositioning cost approval, vessel charter or slot purchase agreement, port terminal handling charge negotiation, customs duty payment authorization, container equipment interchange, transshipment route change approval, hazardous cargo acceptance approval, reefer container temperature deviation acceptance, and force majeure claim processing. Each category must have configurable attributes: applicable module(s), default DOA thresholds per entity, required supporting documentation, SLA for approval turnaround (e.g., B/L amendments require 4-hour turnaround before vessel cut-off), and whether AI agents can auto-approve within configured limits or must always escalate to human approvers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R005	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Sequential and Parallel Approval Workflow Engine	The system must support both sequential and parallel approval workflows with configurable routing logic. Sequential approvals must enforce strict ordering where Level N+1 approver can only see and act on the request after Level N approval is complete. Parallel approvals must allow multiple approvers at the same level to review simultaneously with configurable consensus rules: all must approve, majority must approve, or any one must approve. Mixed workflows must be supported where some levels are sequential and others are parallel. For example, a vessel charter agreement at Jebel Ali may require: parallel approval from Operations Director and Commercial Director (Level 1, both must approve), followed by sequential CFO approval (Level 2), followed by CEO approval (Level 3) if the value exceeds AED 5,000,000. The workflow engine must support recall (initiator withdraws request), return for rework (approver sends back with comments), and conditional skip (auto-skip a level if the approver at a higher level has already provided informal pre-approval).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R006	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Escalation and Timeout Management	The system must enforce configurable SLA timers on each approval level with automatic escalation when timeouts are breached. Default SLA timers must be configurable per approval category: 2 hours for time-sensitive vessel operations (B/L release, container hold/release at gate), 4 hours for booking amendments, 24 hours for commercial approvals (rate discounts, credit notes), 48 hours for financial approvals (vendor payments, capital expenditure), and 72 hours for policy changes. The escalation path must be configurable with up to 3 escalation levels: first escalation to the approver's direct manager, second escalation to the entity head, third escalation to the Super Admin with a critical alert. The system must send reminder notifications at 50% and 80% of the SLA timer. For approvals pending during non-business hours (configured per entity timezone: AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), the SLA timer must pause and resume at the start of the next business day. Hijri calendar weekends (Friday-Saturday for GCC entities) must be respected in SLA calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R007	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Authority Delegation and Acting Capacity	The system must support formal delegation of approval authority from an authorized approver to a designated delegate for a specified time period. Delegations must capture: delegator identity, delegate identity, scope of delegation (specific approval categories or all categories within DOA limits), effective start and end dates (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date selection), maximum single-transaction value the delegate may approve (which may be lower than the delegator's DOA limit), and cumulative value ceiling during the delegation period. The system must prevent delegation to users who would create segregation of duties conflicts, delegation to users outside the delegator's entity without cross-entity delegation permission, and cascading delegation (delegate further delegating to a third party) unless explicitly enabled. When a delegate approves a transaction, the audit trail must record both the delegate's identity and the delegator's identity. Active delegations must be visible on a centralized delegation dashboard accessible to Entity Admins.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R008	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Cumulative Approval Threshold Tracking	The system must track cumulative approval values per approver per approval category over configurable rolling periods (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually) and enforce cumulative DOA limits. For example, an Operations Manager at JNPT may have a per-transaction DOA limit of INR 5,000,000 for demurrage waivers but a monthly cumulative limit of INR 25,000,000, preventing unlimited small waivers that individually fall within limits but collectively exceed acceptable risk. Cumulative tracking must account for multi-currency transactions by converting to a common reference currency (USD) for aggregation. The system must alert the approver when they approach 80% and 95% of their cumulative limit. When a cumulative limit would be breached, the transaction must automatically escalate to the next approval level. Cumulative limits must reset according to the configured period using the entity's fiscal calendar which may differ between entities (Qatar fiscal year: April-March, India fiscal year: April-March, UAE and KSA fiscal year: January-December).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R009	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Request Initiation and Documentation	The system must provide a standardized approval request form that captures all information required for an informed approval decision. The form must include: request type (mapped to approval category), transaction reference number (booking number, B/L number, invoice number as applicable), requested amount in transaction currency with auto-converted display in approver's entity currency, detailed justification text (minimum 50 characters, supporting Arabic and English input), impact analysis (e.g., revenue impact, customer relationship impact, operational impact), supporting document attachments (contract, correspondence, calculation sheet), related transaction history for context (previous similar approvals for the same customer or trade lane), and AI recommendation with confidence score if the transaction was escalated from AI processing. The form must auto-populate available fields from the source transaction and validate completeness before submission. Incomplete requests must be blocked from entering the approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R010	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Decision Recording and Audit Trail	The system must record comprehensive audit information for every approval decision. Each approval record must capture: approver identity, approval level, decision (approve, reject, return for rework, approve with conditions), decision timestamp in UTC with entity-local time, time elapsed from request to decision, conditions or comments attached to the approval (in English or Arabic), approver's DOA limit at time of decision, transaction value relative to DOA limit (percentage utilization), whether the decision was made under delegated authority, and the device and IP address from which the decision was made. For rejected or returned requests, the approver must provide a mandatory reason from configurable categories (insufficient justification, exceeds policy, requires additional documentation, pricing discrepancy, compliance concern) plus optional free-text explanation. All approval audit records must be immutable, retained for a minimum of 10 years, and queryable for regulatory reporting across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R011	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Matrix Version Control and Change Management	The system must maintain full version control of the approval matrix with the ability to view, compare, and restore any historical version. Every change to the approval matrix (threshold modification, approval level addition/removal, role change, category addition) must create a new version with: version number, effective date, changed-by user identity, change description, and before/after comparison. Changes to the approval matrix must themselves go through an approval workflow requiring sign-off from the Entity Admin and CFO. The system must support scheduled activation of new matrix versions aligned with fiscal year changes, regulatory updates, or organizational restructuring. For audit purposes, the system must be able to determine which version of the approval matrix was in effect for any historical transaction. Draft versions must be shareable with stakeholders for review before activation. The system must support a maximum of 4 concurrent draft versions per entity to facilitate planning for different scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R004	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Rate Limiting and Throttle Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure rate limits per endpoint to comply with third-party API usage policies. Rate limiting must be configurable as requests per second, per minute, or per hour with burst allowances. For government customs endpoints such as Qatar Customs HS code validation or ZATCA e-invoicing API, the system must respect published rate limits and queue excess requests with retry logic. Rate limit thresholds must be independently configurable per entity to accommodate different SLA tiers negotiated with port authorities across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R012	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Mobile and Offline Approval Capability	The system must provide mobile-optimized approval interfaces accessible via responsive web or dedicated mobile application for approvers who are frequently away from their desks at port terminals, vessel berths, or in transit between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The mobile interface must display approval requests with full context including transaction details, supporting documents, AI recommendations, and approval chain status. Approvers must be able to approve, reject, or return requests with comments from the mobile interface. Push notifications must alert mobile users of pending approvals with configurable urgency indicators. For offline scenarios (common in container yard areas with limited connectivity), the system must queue approval decisions locally and synchronize when connectivity is restored. Offline approvals must be validated against current DOA limits upon synchronization, with automatic flagging if the approver's DOA was modified during the offline period. The mobile interface must support both English and Arabic with RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R013	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Analytics and Bottleneck Identification	The system must provide analytics dashboards for monitoring approval workflow efficiency across all entities. Key metrics must include: average approval turnaround time per category per entity, SLA breach rate per approval level, approval volume trends (daily, weekly, monthly), rejection rate per category with common rejection reasons, bottleneck identification showing which approver roles or individuals consistently delay the workflow, comparison of AI-recommended decisions versus final human approval decisions, and cost of delay analysis estimating financial impact of approval delays (e.g., vessel demurrage accruing while waiting for cargo release approval at Jebel Ali). The system must generate automated weekly bottleneck reports identifying the top 5 approval bottlenecks per entity with recommended actions such as DOA limit adjustments, additional delegate assignments, or approval workflow simplification. Analytics must be segmentable by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), trade lane, container type, and customer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R014	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Regulatory Compliance Approval Rules	The system must enforce mandatory approval rules derived from regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions. For Qatar: transactions subject to QFCRA regulations must include compliance officer approval when the transaction involves parties from high-risk jurisdictions identified by Qatar Financial Information Unit (QFIU). For UAE: transactions processed through JAFZA must comply with free zone authority approval requirements for specific commodity categories. For KSA: ZATCA e-invoicing compliance must be verified as an automated approval checkpoint before any invoice-related approval is finalized, and transactions above SAR 40,000 must include VAT validation. For India: foreign exchange transactions must comply with FEMA regulations with RBI-mandated approvals for amounts exceeding specified thresholds, and GST reconciliation must be validated before payment approvals. The system must automatically update regulatory threshold values when regulatory bodies publish changes, with administrator review and confirmation before the updates take effect.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R015	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Intercompany Transaction Approval Coordination	The system must handle approvals for intercompany transactions between the four legal entities with dual-entity approval coordination. When a transaction involves charges between entities (e.g., the Qatar entity charges the UAE entity for transshipment handling at Hamad Port for cargo ultimately destined for Jebel Ali), the approval workflow must obtain approval from authorized approvers in both the originating entity and the receiving entity. Transfer pricing compliance must be enforced by requiring that intercompany rates fall within arm's length ranges configured per service type. The system must support configurable rules for which entity's approval takes precedence in case of conflict, with default being the receiving entity for cost items and the originating entity for revenue items. Intercompany approvals must track the corresponding debit and credit entries in both entities' ledgers and must not allow one-sided posting. The approval matrix must support specific intercompany approval categories with DOA limits defined in USD for cross-entity consistency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R016	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Emergency and Override Approval Mechanism	The system must provide a controlled mechanism for emergency approvals that bypass the standard approval matrix when operational urgency requires immediate action. Emergency approval scenarios in container shipping include: vessel departure imminent (less than 2 hours) and cargo release approval pending, port authority-mandated immediate container evacuation due to safety concern, customs authority deadline for declaration amendment response, and force majeure events (port closure, severe weather, geopolitical disruption). Emergency approvals must be restricted to designated Emergency Approver roles (minimum Entity Admin level). Every emergency approval must capture: the emergency justification with mandatory category selection, the standard approval path that was bypassed, the time constraint that necessitated the bypass, and supporting evidence (screenshots, emails, port authority notices). Post-hoc review must be mandatory within 48 hours where the bypassed approvers review and ratify or flag the emergency decision. The system must track emergency approval frequency per entity and alert compliance officers if the rate exceeds configurable thresholds indicating potential process abuse.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R017	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Matrix Template and Cloning	The system must support approval matrix templates that can be cloned and customized to accelerate setup for new entities, departments, or approval categories. Pre-built templates must be provided for common container shipping organizational structures: single-port operation, multi-port national operation, and multi-country regional operation. Templates must include standard DOA limits, approval levels, and category definitions that serve as a starting baseline. When cloning a template, administrators must be able to modify: currency and DOA thresholds (with automatic proportional scaling when changing base currency), approval roles (mapping template roles to entity-specific roles), entity-specific regulatory checkpoints, and trade lane or port-specific conditions. The system must validate cloned matrices for completeness, ensuring all required approval categories have assigned approvers and no gaps exist where a transaction could be submitted without a defined approval path. Templates must be importable and exportable as JSON files with schema validation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-1-004-R018	FEAT-036-1-004	MOD-036	Approval Matrix Conflict Detection and Resolution	The system must automatically detect and flag conflicts or ambiguities in the approval matrix configuration. Conflict types must include: overlapping transaction value ranges within the same category and entity (e.g., two rules both covering USD 10,000-50,000 range with different approval requirements), missing coverage gaps where a valid transaction has no matching approval rule, circular approval paths where approver A requires approver B who requires approver A, rules that reference roles with no active users assigned (orphan approver roles), and rules where the configured SLA for a lower level exceeds the SLA for the complete approval chain. The system must run conflict detection automatically when any matrix change is proposed and display all detected conflicts with severity classification (critical conflicts that block activation, warnings that should be reviewed, and informational notes). Resolution suggestions must be provided for each conflict type. The conflict detection engine must consider cross-entity interactions and validate that intercompany approval paths do not create deadlocks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R005	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Failover and Redundancy Configuration	The system must support primary and secondary endpoint configuration for critical integrations, enabling automatic failover when the primary endpoint becomes unresponsive after a configurable number of consecutive failures (default 3). Failover must be supported for shipping line booking APIs (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd), port community systems, and banking payment gateways processing multi-currency transactions in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The failover mechanism must log all switchover events with timestamps, affected transactions, and must send real-time notifications to designated administrators via email and in-app alerts in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R006	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Request and Response Logging	The system must log all requests and responses for each integration endpoint with configurable verbosity levels (headers only, headers and body, full trace). Logs must capture HTTP method, URL, request headers, request body, response status code, response body, and round-trip latency in milliseconds. For endpoints handling sensitive data such as banking credentials or customs declarations, the system must automatically mask fields containing credit card numbers, bank account details, and personal identification numbers. Log retention must comply with data residency requirements for each entity jurisdiction: Qatar (MDPS regulations), UAE (DIFC data protection), KSA (PDPL), and India (DPDP Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R007	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Versioning and Deprecation Management	The system must support multiple active versions of the same logical endpoint to facilitate gradual migration between API versions offered by port authorities and customs systems. Administrators must be able to mark endpoint versions as active, deprecated, or retired. When an endpoint version is marked deprecated, the system must display a warning banner in both English and Arabic on all admin screens referencing that endpoint and must send automated notifications 30, 14, and 7 days before the scheduled retirement date. Traffic splitting between endpoint versions must be configurable by percentage to allow phased rollout for critical integrations like JNPT ICEGATE or Dubai Trade portal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R009	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Testing and Sandbox Environment	The system must provide a built-in endpoint testing tool allowing administrators to send test requests to any registered endpoint using sandbox or production credentials. The testing tool must support constructing requests with custom headers, query parameters, and JSON or XML body content. For port community system endpoints, the tool must provide pre-built test templates for common operations including vessel schedule query, container release order, delivery order generation, and bill of lading verification. Test results must display response status, headers, body with syntax highlighting, and response time. All test executions must be logged with the administrator's identity and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R010	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Dependency Graph Visualization	The system must generate and display an interactive dependency graph showing relationships between integration endpoints and the ERP modules that consume them. The graph must visually indicate endpoint health status using color coding (green for healthy, amber for degraded, red for down) and must highlight cascading impact when a critical endpoint fails. For example, if the Hamad Port container tracking endpoint goes down, the graph must show affected downstream modules including vessel operations, container yard management, and delivery order processing. The visualization must support both English and Arabic labels with proper bidirectional text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R013	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Bulk Import and Export	The system must support bulk import of endpoint configurations from CSV or JSON files to facilitate rapid onboarding when setting up a new entity or migrating from legacy systems. The import process must validate all required fields including URL format, protocol type, authentication method, and entity assignment before committing changes. Duplicate detection must flag endpoints with matching URLs and protocols. The export function must generate a complete configuration package excluding sensitive credentials, suitable for backup or replication to disaster recovery environments. Import and export operations must support Arabic characters in endpoint descriptions and metadata fields using UTF-8 encoding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R014	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Circuit Breaker Configuration	The system must implement a configurable circuit breaker pattern for each integration endpoint to prevent cascading failures across the ERP platform. Administrators must be able to set the failure threshold count, monitoring window duration, and recovery timeout for each endpoint independently. When a circuit breaker trips (e.g., after 5 consecutive failures to the ZATCA e-invoicing endpoint within 60 seconds), the system must immediately stop sending requests to that endpoint, return a cached response or graceful degradation message, and attempt recovery after the configured timeout. Circuit breaker state transitions (closed, open, half-open) must be visible on the admin dashboard in real time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R015	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Endpoint Webhook and Callback Registration	The system must allow administrators to register webhook callback URLs that external systems can invoke to push data into the ERP, such as vessel arrival notifications from Hamad Port, container gate-in events from Jebel Ali, or customs clearance confirmations from Indian ICEGATE. Each webhook registration must specify the expected payload schema, allowed source IP ranges, authentication mechanism (HMAC signature, bearer token, or mutual TLS), and the target internal processing queue. The system must validate incoming webhook payloads against the registered schema and reject malformed requests with appropriate HTTP error codes. Webhook processing must support idempotency keys to prevent duplicate processing of the same event.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-001-R016	FEAT-036-2-001	MOD-036	Offline Endpoint Queue Management	The system must provide a queue management interface for handling requests that could not be delivered due to endpoint unavailability or network connectivity loss, which is particularly relevant for operations at remote container yards or during network disruptions at port facilities. Queued requests must display the target endpoint, payload summary, queue timestamp, retry count, and priority level. Administrators must be able to manually retry, reorder, or discard queued requests. The queue must support automatic retry with exponential backoff and must respect the original request ordering for transactional sequences such as customs declaration submission followed by duty payment notification across multi-currency transactions in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R002	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Container Type and ISO Code Registry	The system must maintain a master registry of container types aligned with ISO 6346 size and type codes including standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RE, 40RE, 40RH), open top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat rack containers (20FR, 40FR), and tank containers (20TK). Each container type record must include dimensions, tare weight, maximum payload, cubic capacity, and applicable IMDG restrictions for hazardous cargo. The registry must support entity-specific pricing tiers and must display container type descriptions in both English and Arabic. Administrators must be able to activate or deactivate container types per entity without affecting other entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R003	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Currency Exchange Rate Administration	The system must provide an interface for managing daily exchange rates for all operational currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with support for both system-imported rates from central bank feeds and manual override entries. Each exchange rate record must capture the base currency, target currency, buy rate, sell rate, mid-market rate, effective date, and source (Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI, or manual). Historical exchange rates must be preserved indefinitely to support retroactive financial reconciliation. The system must alert administrators when exchange rates deviate more than a configurable threshold (default 2%) from the previous day's rate to catch data entry errors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R004	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Customer and Vendor Master Data Governance	The system must enforce data governance policies for customer and vendor master records including mandatory field validation (trade license number, VAT/TIN registration, bank details), duplicate detection using fuzzy matching on company name and registration number, and approval workflows for new record creation and material changes. Customer records must support multi-entity relationships where the same customer may operate across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India with different legal entities, tax registrations, and credit terms. The system must maintain a golden record linking all entity-specific customer profiles and must display customer names in both English and Arabic script where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R005	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	HS Code and Tariff Schedule Management	The system must provide tools for managing Harmonized System (HS) codes used for customs classification across all four country operations. The HS code registry must support the standard 6-digit international classification plus country-specific extensions (8-digit for Qatar, 8-digit for UAE, 8-digit for KSA, 8-digit for India ITC-HS). Each HS code entry must include the duty rate, applicable exemptions, restricted or prohibited status, and required permits or licenses. Administrators must be able to import updated tariff schedules in bulk from government publications and the system must flag changes that affect existing active shipments. HS code descriptions must be maintained in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R006	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Vessel and Voyage Master Registry	The system must maintain a vessel master registry containing IMO numbers, vessel names, flags of registration, vessel types (container, multi-purpose, feeder), TEU capacity, LOA (length overall), beam, draft, and operational status. The registry must support linking vessels to shipping lines and alliances (2M, Ocean Alliance, THE Alliance). Voyage master records must capture the rotation of port calls with estimated and actual arrival/departure times for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and all intermediate ports. Vessel data must be updatable via AIS feed integration or manual entry. All vessel and voyage data must be searchable in both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R007	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	INCOTERMS and Trade Terms Reference Management	The system must maintain a reference table of INCOTERMS (2020 and prior versions) including EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, and DDP with detailed descriptions of buyer and seller obligations, risk transfer points, and cost allocation rules. Each INCOTERM record must specify applicability by transport mode (sea only, any mode) and must include entity-specific default terms commonly used for trade lanes involving Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must allow administrators to configure which INCOTERMS are available for selection in booking and quotation modules per entity. Descriptions must be available in both English and Arabic with contextual examples relevant to Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R008	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Master Data Change Request Workflow	The system must enforce a maker-checker workflow for all master data modifications where a change request is submitted by one user (maker) and must be approved by a different authorized user (checker) before the change takes effect. The workflow must capture the current value, proposed new value, justification for change, supporting documentation, and requested effective date. For critical master data such as tariff rates, exchange rates, and HS codes, the system must require a second-level approval from entity management. The workflow must support Arabic text in justification fields and must display all dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R009	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Master Data Versioning and History	The system must maintain a complete version history for every master data record, capturing the full record state at each change point including the field values before and after modification, the user who made the change, the approver, and the effective date range. Version history must be queryable by date range allowing administrators to view the exact state of any master data record as it existed on any historical date. This is critical for resolving disputes related to tariff rates applied to specific shipments or exchange rates used for invoice conversion between QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. The version comparison view must support side-by-side display with highlighted differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R010	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Regulatory Code and Permit Type Administration	The system must allow administrators to manage regulatory codes and permit types required for container shipping operations across all four jurisdictions. This includes Qatar Ministry of Commerce permits, UAE Federal Customs Authority codes, KSA ZATCA classification codes, and Indian DGFT IEC codes. Each regulatory code must specify the issuing authority, validity period, applicable commodity categories, and renewal requirements. The system must automatically flag regulatory codes approaching expiry (30, 14, and 7 days before) and must prevent booking creation for commodities requiring expired permits. All regulatory code descriptions and authority names must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R011	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Shipping Line and Agent Master Management	The system must maintain a master registry of shipping lines with SCAC codes, carrier codes, alliance memberships, service routes covering Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade lanes, equipment availability by port, and local agent contact details for each entity jurisdiction. Each shipping line record must include negotiated rate agreement references, credit terms in the applicable currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), and preferred booking channels (EDI, API, web portal). The system must support linking multiple local agents to a single shipping line across different entities and must track agent commission structures and payment terms per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R012	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Commodity and Cargo Classification Master	The system must provide a commodity classification master supporting multiple classification systems including HS codes, IMDG classes for dangerous goods (Classes 1-9 with divisions), temperature-controlled cargo categories, and oversized/overweight cargo classifications. Each commodity entry must specify handling requirements, stowage restrictions, documentation requirements (Material Safety Data Sheet, dangerous goods declaration, phytosanitary certificate), and any country-specific import/export restrictions for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must validate that commodity classifications are consistent across linked HS codes and IMDG classes and must prevent conflicting entries. Commodity names and handling instructions must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R013	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Location and Geofence Master Data	The system must maintain a location master including container depots, container freight stations, inland container depots, bonded warehouses, and free zone facilities across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Each location must have GPS coordinates for geofencing, physical address in both English and Arabic, operating entity, customs zone classification (free zone, bonded area, domestic), and operating hours including Friday/Saturday weekend for Gulf states and Saturday/Sunday for India. The system must support geofence polygon definitions for major port facilities including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to enable automated container movement tracking and event triggering when containers enter or exit defined zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R014	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Tax and Duty Configuration by Jurisdiction	The system must provide a jurisdiction-specific tax and duty configuration interface supporting Qatar's zero-income-tax regime with 5% customs duty, UAE's 5% VAT and 5% customs duty, KSA's 15% VAT and variable customs duties, and India's complex GST structure (IGST, CGST, SGST) plus Basic Customs Duty and Social Welfare Surcharge. Each tax configuration must specify applicability rules by commodity type, origin country, trade agreement (GCC Customs Union, India-UAE CEPA), and free zone exemptions. Tax rates must be effective-dated to support historical lookups and future scheduling. All tax descriptions and authority references must be maintained in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R015	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Master Data Bulk Operations and Data Quality	The system must support bulk creation, update, and deactivation of master data records via CSV and Excel file uploads with comprehensive validation including data type checks, referential integrity verification, business rule compliance, and duplicate detection. The bulk operation interface must display a preview of changes with row-by-row validation results before committing. Failed rows must be exportable with error descriptions for correction and re-upload. The system must provide data quality dashboards showing completeness, accuracy, and consistency metrics for each master data domain. Bulk operations must support Arabic text fields encoded in UTF-8 and must correctly handle right-to-left text in preview displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R016	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	Master Data Synchronization Across Entities	The system must support configurable synchronization policies for master data shared across entities, distinguishing between global reference data (vessel registry, shipping line master, HS codes) that is centrally managed and entity-specific data (local customers, local tariffs, entity-specific tax rules) that is managed independently. Synchronization must support real-time push for critical data changes and scheduled batch synchronization for non-critical updates. Conflict resolution must be configurable as either central-wins or entity-wins per data domain. The synchronization log must record all data propagation events with source entity, target entity, record identifiers, and timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-002-R017	FEAT-036-2-002	MOD-036	AI-Assisted Master Data Enrichment	The system must provide AI-assisted tools for enriching master data records by automatically suggesting missing field values based on patterns in existing data. For example, when a new customer is registered with a trade license from Dubai, the AI must suggest the likely customs registration number format, applicable VAT registration prefix, default currency (AED), and default port (Jebel Ali AEJEA). The AI enrichment must achieve a minimum 85% suggestion accuracy and must present all suggestions for human review before application. The AI must learn from accepted and rejected suggestions to improve accuracy over time. Suggestions must be presented in the user's preferred language (English or Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R001	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Comprehensive Event Capture Scope	The system must capture audit log entries for all user authentication events (login, logout, failed login, session timeout, MFA challenges), all data creation, modification, and deletion operations across every ERP module, all API endpoint invocations (inbound and outbound), all workflow state transitions (booking approval, customs declaration submission, payment authorization), and all system configuration changes. Each audit entry must record the event type, timestamp in UTC with millisecond precision, user identity, source IP address, entity context (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), affected resource type and identifier, and the before/after state for data modification events. Entries must be immutable once written.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R002	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Advanced Search with Multi-Criteria Filtering	The system must provide an advanced search interface allowing administrators to filter audit logs by any combination of: date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar input), user identity or role, entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), event type category (authentication, data modification, API call, workflow, configuration), affected module (booking, customs, finance, vessel operations), affected resource ID (e.g., booking number, BL number, container number), action outcome (success/failure), and source IP range. Filters must support AND/OR logic with the ability to save frequently used filter combinations as named presets. The search interface must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R003	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Full-Text Search Across Audit Payloads	The system must support full-text search across audit log entry payloads including data field values captured in before/after states. This enables administrators to search for specific container numbers (e.g., MSCU1234567), booking references, bill of lading numbers, customer names in English or Arabic script, vessel IMO numbers, and any other business identifiers that appear in audit trail data. Full-text search must support exact match, wildcard, and phrase queries with results ranked by relevance. Search must return results within 3 seconds for queries spanning up to 90 days of audit data. Arabic text search must support both exact and normalized forms accounting for common character variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R004	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Audit Log Timeline Visualization	The system must provide a chronological timeline visualization for audit events related to a specific business entity such as a booking, shipment, container, or financial transaction. The timeline must display events from all modules that touched the entity, creating a complete audit trail from creation to completion. For example, a container shipment timeline would show booking creation, documentation submission, customs declaration, vessel loading at Jebel Ali, vessel discharge at JNPT, customs clearance, and delivery order issuance. Each timeline event must be expandable to show full audit details. The timeline must support both English and Arabic display with proper date formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R005	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	User Activity Session Reconstruction	The system must enable administrators to reconstruct a complete user session by selecting a user and time range, displaying all actions performed by that user in chronological order with the ability to replay the sequence of screens visited and data accessed. Session reconstruction must show page navigation, data queries executed, records viewed, modifications made, reports generated, and documents downloaded. This capability is essential for investigating security incidents such as unauthorized access to sensitive shipping manifests, fraudulent booking modifications, or suspected data exfiltration. Session data must include entity context to distinguish actions performed under different entity profiles (Qatar vs UAE vs KSA vs India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R006	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Data Modification Diff Viewer	The system must provide a detailed diff viewer for data modification audit entries showing the exact fields that were changed, the previous value, and the new value in a side-by-side or inline comparison format. The diff viewer must handle complex data types including nested objects (e.g., a booking with multiple containers, each with cargo details), arrays (e.g., adding or removing container numbers from a shipment), and multi-currency amounts showing both the amount and currency code. For Arabic text fields, the diff viewer must correctly render RTL text with highlighted changes. Numeric changes must show the magnitude and direction of change (e.g., freight rate increased by 15% from 1,200 USD to 1,380 USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R007	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Compliance Report Generation from Audit Logs	The system must generate compliance reports from audit log data to satisfy regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions. Required reports include: user access reports showing who accessed what data and when (required by Qatar MDPS, UAE NESA, KSA NCA, India CERT-In), data modification reports for financial records (required for VAT/GST audit trails), failed authentication reports for security compliance, and privileged action reports for administrator oversight. Reports must be generatable for configurable date ranges with entity-level filtering. Each report must include a report header with generation timestamp, requestor identity, and scope parameters. Reports must be exportable in PDF and CSV formats with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R008	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Audit Log Retention and Archival Policy	The system must enforce configurable retention policies per entity to comply with jurisdiction-specific data retention regulations: Qatar requires minimum 5 years for financial records, UAE requires minimum 5 years, KSA requires minimum 10 years for commercial records under ZATCA regulations, and India requires minimum 8 years for customs and GST records. The system must automatically archive audit logs beyond the active retention period (configurable, default 1 year) to cold storage while maintaining searchability through an archive search interface. Archived logs must be retrievable within 4 hours of request. The system must prevent deletion of audit logs within the mandatory retention period regardless of administrator actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R009	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Real-Time Audit Event Streaming	The system must support real-time streaming of audit events to the admin dashboard using WebSocket connections, allowing administrators to monitor live system activity. The streaming interface must support configurable filters so administrators can focus on specific event types such as failed login attempts, high-value transaction modifications (above configurable thresholds per currency: 100,000 QAR, 100,000 AED, 100,000 SAR, 5,000,000 INR, 25,000 USD), or changes to critical master data. The streaming view must auto-scroll with new events while allowing administrators to pause the stream and inspect specific entries. Stream connections must be entity-scoped to ensure data isolation between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R010	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Anomaly Detection in Audit Patterns	The system must apply AI-driven anomaly detection algorithms to audit log patterns to identify suspicious activities such as unusual login times for a user, abnormal volume of data exports, access to records outside a user's normal scope, rapid sequential modifications suggesting automated manipulation, or access from unexpected geographic locations. Detected anomalies must be flagged with a severity rating (low, medium, high, critical) and must trigger notifications to security administrators. The anomaly detection must establish baseline behavior per user over a rolling 30-day window and must account for legitimate variations such as Ramadan schedule changes and different working hours across Gulf and Indian time zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R012	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Audit Log Export and Integration	The system must support exporting audit logs in multiple formats including CSV, JSON, and PDF for external analysis, legal proceedings, or regulatory submissions. Exports must support configurable field selection, date range filtering, and entity scoping. For SIEM integration, the system must support streaming audit events via syslog (RFC 5424), webhook push, or Apache Kafka topics. Export operations for large datasets (exceeding 100,000 entries) must run asynchronously with progress tracking and email notification upon completion. All export files must include metadata headers specifying the export parameters, timestamp, and the identity of the administrator who initiated the export for chain-of-custody documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R013	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Audit Log Integrity Verification	The system must implement cryptographic integrity verification for audit logs using hash chaining where each audit entry includes a SHA-256 hash of the previous entry, creating a tamper-evident chain. Administrators must be able to run integrity verification checks on any date range to confirm that no audit entries have been modified, deleted, or inserted after the fact. The verification report must identify the exact point of any detected tampering. For regulatory compliance, the system must support generating digitally signed audit attestations confirming log integrity for a specified period, suitable for submission to customs authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT during compliance audits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R014	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Privileged Action Audit Highlighting	The system must automatically highlight and categorize privileged administrative actions in the audit log including user account creation/deletion, role assignment changes, permission modifications, system configuration changes, master data overrides bypassing approval workflows, endpoint credential access, and audit log export operations. Privileged actions must be displayed with a distinct visual indicator in the audit log viewer and must be included in a dedicated privileged action summary accessible from the admin dashboard. The system must enforce dual-notification for privileged actions, alerting both the acting administrator and a designated security officer. Notifications must be sent in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R015	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Audit Log Performance and Scalability	The system must handle a minimum throughput of 10,000 audit events per second during peak operations (such as simultaneous vessel arrivals at multiple ports) without degrading ERP system performance. Audit log writes must be asynchronous to prevent impact on transaction response times. The search interface must return results within 5 seconds for queries spanning up to 30 days and within 15 seconds for queries spanning up to 1 year against a dataset of at least 500 million audit entries. The system must support horizontal scaling of the audit log storage and search infrastructure. Index optimization must run during off-peak hours respecting the different business calendars across Gulf (Sunday-Thursday) and India (Monday-Saturday) operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-003-R016	FEAT-036-2-003	MOD-036	Contextual Audit Links from Business Screens	The system must provide contextual audit log links from all business transaction screens, allowing users with appropriate permissions to view the complete audit trail for the record they are currently viewing. For example, when viewing a booking record, a user must be able to click an audit icon to see all changes made to that booking since creation including field-level modifications, status transitions, document attachments, and related financial entries. The contextual audit view must filter relevant entries automatically based on the current record's identifier and must support pagination for records with extensive audit histories. The audit link must be accessible via keyboard shortcut and must render properly in both English and Arabic UI layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R001	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Real-Time Service Health Overview	The system must display a real-time health overview dashboard showing the operational status of all ERP microservices including booking service, customs declaration service, vessel operations service, finance service, documentation service, and all other module services. Each service must show current status (healthy, degraded, down), uptime percentage for the current day and rolling 30 days, current request throughput (requests per second), average response time, error rate percentage, and active instance count. The dashboard must auto-refresh at configurable intervals (minimum 10 seconds) and must use color-coded status indicators following a consistent scheme across both English and Arabic interface versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R002	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Infrastructure Resource Monitoring	The system must monitor and display infrastructure resource utilization metrics including CPU usage, memory consumption, disk I/O, network throughput, and database connection pool status for all servers and containers hosting ERP services. Metrics must be displayed as time-series charts with configurable time ranges (last 1 hour, 6 hours, 24 hours, 7 days, 30 days). The dashboard must show resource utilization segmented by deployment region corresponding to entity operations: Qatar (Middle East primary), UAE (Middle East secondary), KSA (Middle East tertiary), and India (South Asia). Threshold-based alerts must be configurable for each metric (e.g., CPU above 80%, memory above 85%, disk above 90%) with notification routing to designated administrators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R003	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Database Performance Metrics	The system must monitor and display database performance metrics for all ERP databases including query throughput, slow query count (queries exceeding configurable threshold, default 2 seconds), active connections versus connection pool limit, replication lag for read replicas, transaction lock wait times, and table-level statistics for the largest tables. The dashboard must highlight databases approaching capacity limits such as storage utilization above 75% or connection count above 80% of maximum. For multi-entity database architectures, metrics must be displayed per entity schema to identify entity-specific performance issues, such as the India entity experiencing higher query volumes during JNPT peak hours (0800-1800 IST) compared to Gulf entity off-peak hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R004	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	API Gateway and Traffic Analytics	The system must display API gateway metrics including total request volume, requests per endpoint, response time distribution (P50, P95, P99 percentiles), HTTP status code distribution (2xx, 3xx, 4xx, 5xx), top consumers by API key or client certificate, and geographic distribution of requests. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, time range, and specific API endpoint groups (public APIs, internal APIs, port authority integrations, customs integrations). Rate limiting status must be visible showing current consumption against limits for each consumer. Traffic analytics must identify unusual patterns such as sudden traffic spikes from specific clients that might indicate integration errors or potential denial-of-service conditions against endpoints connecting to Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R005	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Message Queue and Event Bus Monitoring	The system must monitor and display the health of all message queues and event bus topics used for asynchronous processing including queue depth (current message count), consumer lag, processing throughput (messages per second), dead letter queue counts, and oldest unprocessed message age. Critical queues such as customs declaration submission, booking confirmation, vessel schedule updates, and payment processing must have dedicated monitoring panels with entity-specific views. The dashboard must alert when queue depth exceeds configurable thresholds indicating processing backlogs, when dead letter queue counts increase indicating systematic failures, or when consumer lag exceeds acceptable limits (configurable per queue, default 5 minutes for critical queues).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R006	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Integration Endpoint Health Heatmap	The system must display an integration endpoint health heatmap showing the status and performance of all external integration points organized by category (port systems, customs authorities, shipping lines, banking gateways, government portals). The heatmap must use color intensity to represent response time degradation and must show the last successful communication timestamp for each endpoint. For critical port authority integrations (Hamad Port Maqta, Khalifa Port KEZAD, Jebel Ali DP World, JNPT PCS), the heatmap must show separate cells for each integration function (vessel schedule, container tracking, gate operations, documentation). Hovering over any cell must display detailed metrics and clicking must navigate to the endpoint management interface. The heatmap must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R007	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Automated Alert Rules and Escalation	The system must support configurable alert rules that trigger notifications when performance metrics breach defined thresholds. Alert rules must support compound conditions using AND/OR logic across multiple metrics (e.g., CPU above 80% AND response time above 2 seconds). Each alert rule must define severity (informational, warning, critical, emergency), notification channels (email, SMS, in-app, webhook to incident management), and escalation paths specifying who gets notified after configurable time intervals if the alert is not acknowledged. Alert rules must support entity-specific thresholds to account for different operational scales. Alert messages must be generated in both English and Arabic based on the recipient's language preference and must include relevant multi-currency transaction impact estimates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R008	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Historical Performance Trend Analysis	The system must provide historical performance trend analysis with the ability to compare metrics across different time periods (day-over-day, week-over-week, month-over-month, year-over-year). Trend charts must support overlay comparisons showing how current performance compares to the same period in previous weeks or months. The system must identify seasonal patterns such as increased load during peak shipping seasons (Q4 for Gulf imports, pre-Ramadan periods for food and consumer goods), vessel bunching events at major ports, and month-end financial processing peaks. Trend data must be retained for a minimum of 2 years to support capacity planning. All trend visualizations must correctly display Hijri calendar month labels when the administrator's locale is set to Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R009	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	AI-Powered Predictive Health Alerts	The system must implement AI-powered predictive analytics that analyze historical performance patterns to forecast potential issues before they impact operations. Predictions must cover disk space exhaustion (projected date when storage will reach capacity), memory leak detection (gradual memory consumption increase over time), database performance degradation (query time trends indicating index fragmentation or data growth), and integration endpoint reliability forecasting. Predictions must achieve a minimum 80% accuracy rate for issues predicted 24 hours in advance. Predictive alerts must be clearly distinguished from threshold-based alerts in the dashboard UI. Predictions must account for entity-specific patterns and seasonal variations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R010	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Custom Dashboard Layout and Widget Management	The system must allow administrators to customize their dashboard layout by adding, removing, resizing, and rearranging monitoring widgets. Available widgets must include metric charts (line, bar, area), status grids, heatmaps, gauges, counters, and log streams. Each administrator must have a personal dashboard configuration that persists across sessions. The system must also support shared team dashboards that can be created by senior administrators and published for team viewing. Widget configurations must support entity filtering so a Qatar operations manager sees Qatar-specific metrics by default. The dashboard layout engine must support both LTR and RTL arrangements and must correctly mirror widget positions when switching between English and Arabic interface languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R011	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Service Dependency Map and Impact Analysis	The system must generate and display an interactive service dependency map showing how ERP microservices depend on each other, on databases, on message queues, and on external integration endpoints. The dependency map must update in real time to reflect current service health, overlaying health status indicators on each node. When a service experiences issues, the map must highlight all upstream and downstream dependencies that may be affected. For example, if the customs declaration service is down, the map must show impact on booking completion, container release at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, and delivery order generation. Administrators must be able to simulate failure scenarios by clicking on a service node to see projected impact cascading across dependent services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R012	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Transaction Volume and Business Metrics	The system must display real-time business transaction metrics including active bookings count, customs declarations in progress, containers in transit, delivery orders pending, invoices awaiting payment, and total transaction value segmented by currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Business metrics must be filterable by entity and by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT). The dashboard must show transaction processing rates (bookings per hour, declarations per hour) and compare them against historical baselines to identify abnormal patterns. During peak operations such as large vessel arrivals handling 5,000+ TEU, the dashboard must provide real-time progress tracking of container discharge and customs clearance throughput.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R013	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Incident Timeline and Resolution Tracking	The system must provide an incident management timeline that logs all system incidents including service outages, performance degradations, integration failures, and security events. Each incident must capture start time, detection method (automated alert or manual report), affected services and entities, impact description, assigned responder, resolution actions taken, and resolution time. The incident timeline must correlate with performance metrics to show the metric behavior before, during, and after the incident. Post-incident reports must be generatable with root cause analysis fields and preventive measure recommendations. The system must calculate and display MTTR (Mean Time to Resolution) metrics per service and per entity. Incident reports must support bilingual content in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R014	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Capacity Planning and Resource Forecasting	The system must provide capacity planning tools that project future resource requirements based on historical growth trends, planned business expansions, and seasonal patterns. Projections must cover compute resources (CPU, memory), storage capacity, database size, network bandwidth, and API call volumes. The planning tool must allow administrators to model scenarios such as onboarding a new shipping line, adding a new port of call, increasing transaction volumes by a specified percentage, or expanding to a new entity. Capacity forecasts must be presented as time-series projections with confidence intervals. The tool must flag resources projected to reach capacity within configurable horizons (30, 60, 90 days) to enable proactive procurement across all four entity deployments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R015	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Network Latency and Connectivity Monitoring	The system must monitor network latency between all ERP service components, between deployment regions (Gulf and India), and between the ERP platform and external integration endpoints. Latency measurements must be continuous with configurable sampling intervals (minimum 30 seconds). The dashboard must display a latency matrix showing round-trip times between all major components and must highlight connections exceeding acceptable thresholds (configurable per connection type, default 200ms for intra-region, 500ms for cross-region Gulf-to-India). Connectivity monitoring must detect and alert on packet loss, DNS resolution failures, and SSL certificate expiry warnings. For port authority VPN connections (commonly used for Hamad Port and JNPT integrations), the dashboard must show tunnel status and throughput metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R016	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Scheduled Maintenance Window Management	The system must provide a maintenance window scheduling interface that allows administrators to define planned downtime periods for services, databases, and infrastructure components. Scheduled maintenance windows must be displayed on the health dashboard with countdown timers and must suppress alerting for affected components during the maintenance period. The scheduler must validate against operational calendars to prevent scheduling maintenance during critical business periods such as vessel arrival windows, customs cut-off times, or end-of-month financial closing. Maintenance windows must be entity-aware, respecting different working weeks (Sunday-Thursday for Gulf states, Monday-Friday for India) and public holidays including Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, KSA National Day, and Indian Republic Day.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-2-004-R017	FEAT-036-2-004	MOD-036	Mobile-Responsive Health Dashboard Access	The system must provide a mobile-responsive version of the health dashboard optimized for smartphones and tablets, enabling administrators to monitor system health while away from their desks or during port visits. The mobile dashboard must display critical health indicators, active alerts, and incident status with touch-friendly navigation. Push notifications must be supported for critical and emergency alerts via mobile browser or progressive web app. The mobile layout must support both portrait and landscape orientations and must correctly render in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) modes. Dashboard data on mobile must refresh at the same frequency as the desktop version and must function with reduced bandwidth conditions commonly encountered at port facilities and container yards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R002	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Entity-Scoped Flag Overrides	The system must support entity-level overrides for each feature flag, allowing administrators to enable or disable specific features independently for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India legal entities. When an override is configured for a specific entity, it must take precedence over the global default value. The override configuration screen must display the entity name, trade license number, and jurisdiction clearly. Administrators must be able to set different values for percentage rollout flags per entity, enabling for example a 50% rollout at Jebel Ali while maintaining 100% at JNPT. The system must track override creation timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R005	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claimant Identity Verification and KYC	The system must verify the claimant's identity and standing before accepting a claim for processing. Verification must confirm that the claimant is a registered party on the bill of lading (shipper, consignee, or notify party) or holds a valid letter of subrogation from the cargo insurer. The system must integrate with the customer master (MOD-010) to validate trade license numbers, commercial registration numbers (CR for KSA, QID/CR for Qatar, DED license for UAE, GSTIN for India), and ensure the claimant entity is not on any sanctions or blacklist. For claims submitted in Arabic, the system must perform name matching using fuzzy Arabic transliteration algorithms to account for variations in romanization of Arabic names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R010	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Multi-Currency Claim Value Capture	The system must capture the claimed amount in the original transaction currency as stated on the commercial invoice, supporting at minimum USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. Upon registration the system must automatically calculate and store equivalent values in USD (as the base reporting currency) and the local entity currency using exchange rates sourced from the central treasury module (MOD-038). The exchange rate used must be the rate effective on the date of the bill of lading or the date of loss, whichever the claims handler selects, and this rate must be locked and recorded with the claim for audit purposes. The system must support manual rate override by authorized users with mandatory justification entry, and all currency amounts must display with the correct decimal precision (2 decimals for USD/AED/QAR/SAR, 2 decimals for INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R003	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Port-Level Flag Targeting	The system must allow feature flags to be targeted by port code, enabling administrators to activate or deactivate features at specific ports such as QAHMD (Hamad Port), AEKHL (Khalifa Port), AEJEA (Jebel Ali), or INJNP (JNPT Mumbai). Port-level targeting must support inclusion and exclusion lists, so a flag can be enabled globally but excluded at specific ports, or disabled globally but enabled only at selected ports. This is critical for scenarios where port-specific regulatory requirements in Qatar versus India demand different operational workflows, such as customs pre-clearance automation or container scanning protocols unique to each port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R004	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Role-Based Flag Targeting	The system must support targeting feature flags based on user roles defined in the ERP role hierarchy. Administrators must be able to specify which roles see a particular feature, such as enabling an AI-based container damage detection feature only for surveyors and operations managers while hiding it from data entry operators. Role targeting must support both individual role selection and role group selection. The UI must display role names in both English and Arabic with RTL-compatible layout. When combined with entity and port targeting, role targeting must follow an AND logic where all conditions must be satisfied for the flag to evaluate as active for a given user session.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R005	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Percentage-Based Gradual Rollout	The system must support percentage-based gradual rollout for feature flags, allowing administrators to expose new features to a configurable percentage of users. The percentage allocation must use consistent hashing based on user ID to ensure the same users consistently see the feature across sessions, preventing confusion during partial rollouts. Administrators must be able to adjust the percentage in increments of 1% from 0 to 100. The system must display real-time statistics showing how many active users are currently in the enabled cohort versus the disabled cohort. Rollout percentage changes must be logged with the administrator identity and timestamp for audit compliance required by Qatar Financial Centre and DIFC regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R006	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Feature Flag Scheduling	The system must allow administrators to schedule feature flag activation and deactivation at specific dates and times. Scheduling must support timezone-aware configuration with at least AST (Arabia Standard Time, UTC+3), GST (Gulf Standard Time, UTC+4), and IST (Indian Standard Time, UTC+5:30) options. Scheduled changes must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views. The system must support recurring schedules for flags that need to be active only during specific windows, such as enabling enhanced vessel tracking AI during peak shipping seasons at Jebel Ali or during Hajj season freight surges at Jeddah Islamic Port. A countdown timer must display the time remaining until the next scheduled state change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R007	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Feature Flag Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all feature flag operations including creation, modification, override changes, schedule updates, and deletions. Each audit entry must record the administrator identity, IP address, timestamp in UTC with local timezone conversion, the previous flag state, and the new flag state. The audit log must be immutable and retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Commercial Companies Law and UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 on commercial companies. The audit trail must be searchable by flag key, administrator, date range, entity, and change type. Export of audit records must be available in PDF and CSV formats with Arabic and English column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R008	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Flag Dependency Management	The system must support defining dependencies between feature flags, preventing administrators from enabling a flag unless its prerequisite flags are already active. For example, the AI-based Bill of Lading auto-generation flag must not be activatable unless the OCR document processing flag is already enabled. The dependency graph must be visualized in the Admin Portal UI showing parent-child relationships with clear directional arrows. When an administrator attempts to disable a flag that other flags depend on, the system must display a warning listing all dependent flags and require explicit confirmation. Circular dependency detection must prevent invalid configurations, and the dependency validation must execute within two seconds for flag graphs containing up to 500 nodes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R009	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Environment-Aware Flag Configuration	The system must support separate feature flag configurations for development, staging, UAT, and production environments. Flag values set in a lower environment must not automatically propagate to production, requiring explicit promotion by an authorized administrator. The promotion workflow must include a mandatory review step where a second administrator approves the change before it takes effect in production. The Admin Portal must display a clear visual indicator (color-coded banner) showing which environment the administrator is currently viewing. Environment-specific flag values must be exportable as JSON snapshots for backup and disaster recovery purposes, supporting the multi-region deployment topology across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India data centers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R010	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Real-Time Flag Evaluation Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing the current evaluation status of all feature flags across entities and ports. The dashboard must display the total number of active flags, inactive flags, flags in partial rollout, and scheduled flags. Each flag entry must show its current state per entity with a color-coded matrix view. The dashboard must support filtering by module, entity, port, and flag type. Real-time metrics must include the number of flag evaluations per minute, average evaluation latency, and error rates. The dashboard must auto-refresh every 30 seconds and support Arabic RTL layout. Performance data must be visualized with trend charts covering the last 24 hours, 7 days, and 30 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R011	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Emergency Kill Switch Capability	The system must provide an emergency kill switch mechanism allowing authorized administrators to instantly disable one or more feature flags across all entities and ports simultaneously. The kill switch must bypass normal approval workflows and take effect within five seconds of activation. The system must maintain a dedicated kill switch audit log separate from the standard audit trail with mandatory reason-for-disablement field. After a kill switch activation, the system must send immediate notifications to all configured administrators via email and in-app alerts in both Arabic and English. A restoration workflow must require dual-administrator approval to re-enable a flag that was disabled via kill switch, preventing premature reactivation of problematic features that could disrupt shipping operations at critical ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R012	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Flag Impact Analysis	The system must provide an impact analysis tool that shows which modules, screens, API endpoints, and business processes are affected by a specific feature flag. The impact analysis must be populated from a flag registry where developers declare flag usage points during development. Administrators must be able to view impact analysis before toggling a flag, understanding for example that disabling the container-tracking-ai flag would affect the vessel schedule module, the yard management dashboard, and the customer shipment visibility portal. The analysis must include estimated user impact counts per entity and port, helping administrators make informed decisions about flag changes during peak operational hours at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R013	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Feature Flag API and SDK Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API for programmatic feature flag evaluation, allowing all ERP microservices to query flag states with sub-50-millisecond latency. The API must support batch flag evaluation where a service can request the state of multiple flags in a single call, reducing network overhead for modules that check numerous flags during request processing. A server-side SDK must be provided for Node.js and Python services with local caching and automatic refresh capabilities. The SDK must support offline fallback behavior where cached flag values are used when the flag service is unreachable, critical for maintaining operations at ports with intermittent connectivity. API authentication must use service-to-service JWT tokens with entity-scoped permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R014	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Flag Lifecycle State Machine	The system must enforce a lifecycle state machine for feature flags with the following states: Draft, Active, Deprecated, and Archived. New flags must start in Draft state where they can be configured but not evaluated in production. Transitioning from Draft to Active requires approval from a designated flag owner. Deprecated flags must display a warning banner in the Admin Portal and emit deprecation notices in API responses. Archived flags must be read-only and excluded from evaluation queries but retained for historical audit purposes. The system must automatically suggest deprecation for flags that have been at 100% rollout for more than 90 days across all entities, indicating the feature is fully launched and the flag overhead should be removed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R015	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Multivariate Flag Testing Support	The system must support multivariate feature flags where multiple variant values can be defined and distributed among user segments. For example, a multivariate flag for the container booking UI could have variants A (standard form), B (AI-assisted form), and C (conversational chatbot form) with configurable traffic distribution percentages. Each variant must have a name, description in English and Arabic, and a JSON payload defining variant-specific configuration. The system must track variant-level analytics showing which variant each user was assigned to and must integrate with the ERP analytics pipeline for measuring conversion metrics such as booking completion rates, time to complete a Bill of Lading, or customs declaration accuracy rates across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-001-R016	FEAT-036-3-001	MOD-036	Bulk Flag Operations	The system must support bulk operations on feature flags including bulk enable, bulk disable, bulk export, and bulk import. Administrators must be able to select multiple flags using checkbox selection, filter-based selection, or by uploading a list of flag keys. Bulk operations must execute atomically where either all flags in the batch are updated or none are, preventing partial updates that could leave the system in an inconsistent state. The bulk import format must be JSON with validation against the flag schema before processing. Bulk operations must be subject to the same audit trail requirements as individual flag changes, with each flag change recorded as a separate audit entry linked to a common batch operation identifier. Progress indicators must display during long-running bulk operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R001	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	License Key Generation and Activation	The system must generate unique cryptographically signed license keys for each entity-module combination. License keys must encode the entity identifier, licensed module IDs, validity period, and maximum user count. The activation process must verify the key signature, check expiry dates, and validate that the entity ID matches the requesting organization. License keys must support offline activation for deployment scenarios at ports with limited internet connectivity such as remote container freight stations linked to JNPT. The system must maintain an activation registry recording the activation timestamp, server fingerprint, and activating administrator identity. Duplicate activation attempts on different server instances must be detected and blocked unless explicitly authorized for high-availability deployment configurations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R002	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Subscription Tier Definition	The system must support configurable subscription tiers including Starter, Professional, Enterprise, and Custom plans. Each tier must define the included modules, maximum concurrent users, API call quotas, storage limits, and available AI automation features. The tier configuration must specify which ports are included in the license scope, allowing for example a Professional tier that covers Hamad Port and Khalifa Port but not Jebel Ali or JNPT. Tier definitions must include pricing in multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with exchange rate references. Each tier must have bilingual (English and Arabic) marketing descriptions and feature comparison matrices. Custom tiers must allow administrators to cherry-pick individual modules and set bespoke limits for large shipping conglomerate clients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R003	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Usage Metering and Tracking	The system must track real-time usage metrics against subscription entitlements including concurrent user sessions, API calls per hour, storage consumption in gigabytes, number of active vessel bookings, Bill of Lading documents generated, and AI inference requests consumed. Usage data must be aggregated per entity, per port, and per module. The metering system must operate with eventual consistency, updating counters within a maximum delay of 60 seconds. Historical usage data must be retained for 24 months to support subscription right-sizing recommendations. The system must calculate usage trends and project when current entitlement limits will be reached based on trailing 90-day growth patterns. Usage dashboards must display data in the user-selected currency and support both Arabic and English number formatting conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R004	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Entitlement Enforcement and Soft Limits	The system must enforce subscription entitlements using a two-tier warning approach with soft limits at 80% utilization and hard limits at 100%. When a soft limit is reached, the system must display a warning banner in the Admin Portal and send notification emails to designated license administrators in both Arabic and English. When a hard limit is reached for non-critical features, the system must block new resource creation while allowing existing operations to continue. Critical shipping operations such as active vessel tracking, customs declaration submissions to Qatar Customs (QC), Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs (ZATCA), or Indian Customs (ICEGATE) must never be blocked by entitlement enforcement. The system must log all enforcement actions with timestamps and affected user details for dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R005	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Subscription Renewal Workflow	The system must manage subscription renewal workflows with automated reminders starting 90 days before expiration. Renewal notifications must be sent at 90, 60, 30, 14, and 7 days before expiry, escalating to senior management contacts as the deadline approaches. The renewal form must display the current subscription details, usage statistics for the current term, and recommended tier adjustments based on actual utilization patterns. Renewal pricing must be calculated in the entity's preferred currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with applicable VAT rates (currently 5% for Qatar and UAE, 15% for KSA, 18% GST for India). The system must support multi-year renewal discounts and generate renewal quotations as downloadable PDF documents with bilingual Arabic and English content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R006	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Module-Level License Assignment	The system must allow administrators to assign individual module licenses to specific users or user groups within an entity. Not all users within a licensed entity should automatically have access to all licensed modules. For example, yard management staff at Hamad Port may only need access to MOD-015 (Yard Management) and MOD-012 (Container Tracking), while commercial teams need MOD-008 (Booking Management) and MOD-010 (Pricing & Tariff). The assignment interface must display available module licenses, currently assigned count versus maximum allowed, and provide drag-and-drop or checkbox-based assignment capabilities. Module license reassignment must take effect immediately without requiring user session restart. The system must prevent assignment of more licenses than the subscription entitlement permits for any given module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R007	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Multi-Entity License Consolidation View	The system must provide a consolidated dashboard view showing license and subscription status across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) in a single screen. The consolidation view must display each entity's active subscription tier, license utilization percentages, upcoming renewal dates, and outstanding payment status. Administrators with cross-entity access must be able to compare usage patterns across entities to identify over-provisioned or under-provisioned licenses. The dashboard must support currency normalization, displaying all values in a selected base currency with conversion rates from QAR, AED, SAR, and INR to USD or any other chosen reference currency. Entity-level drill-down must show port-specific usage breakdowns, highlighting for example whether Jebel Ali consumes more API quotas than Khalifa Port within the UAE entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R008	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Grace Period and Expiry Handling	The system must implement a configurable grace period after subscription expiration, defaulting to 15 days, during which full functionality remains available while renewal is pending. During the grace period, the system must display a persistent banner in both Arabic and English indicating the expired status and days remaining in the grace period. After the grace period expires, the system must transition to a read-only mode where users can view existing data including historical shipment records, Bills of Lading, and customs declarations but cannot create new transactions. Critical integrations with port community systems such as Qatar Customs Portal (Nadeeb), Dubai Trade, ZATCA Fasah, and ICEGATE must remain functional for mandatory regulatory reporting even after grace period expiry. A reactivation workflow must allow instant restoration upon payment confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R009	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Subscription Upgrade and Downgrade Management	The system must support mid-term subscription upgrades and downgrades with pro-rated billing calculations. When upgrading, the system must calculate the remaining value of the current subscription and apply it as a credit toward the new tier, computing the differential amount in the entity's local currency. When downgrading, the system must verify that current usage does not exceed the lower tier's limits before allowing the change. If usage exceeds the target tier's limits, the system must display a detailed report showing which limits are exceeded and suggest remediation actions such as reducing active users or archiving old shipment data. Upgrade changes must take effect immediately, while downgrades must be scheduled for the next billing cycle start date. All pricing calculations must account for entity-specific VAT rates and display amounts in both the local currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R010	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	AI Feature Consumption Tracking	The system must separately track and meter AI feature consumption as AI automation constitutes a significant cost component of the ERP platform. Tracked AI features must include automated container damage detection inference calls, AI-generated customs classification suggestions, natural language Bill of Lading generation, predictive vessel ETA calculations, and AI-powered freight rate optimization. Each AI feature consumption must be recorded with the requesting entity, port, user, timestamp, and computational cost units. The subscription tier must define monthly AI credit allocations, and the system must display a real-time AI credit balance gauge in the Admin Portal. When AI credits reach 90% consumption, the system must offer options to purchase additional credits, throttle AI features, or upgrade to a higher tier with larger AI allocations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R011	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	License Compliance Reporting	The system must generate license compliance reports for internal audit and vendor management purposes. Compliance reports must include peak concurrent user counts per month, maximum storage utilization, API call volumes by module, and any entitlement enforcement events that occurred during the reporting period. Reports must be available in PDF format with Arabic and English bilingual headers and must include charts showing monthly usage trends over the subscription term. The compliance report must highlight any periods where usage exceeded entitlement limits and the duration of such exceedances. Reports must be automatically generated on the first day of each month and stored for seven years to comply with commercial record retention requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Report generation must support Hijri calendar date ranges for KSA compliance needs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R012	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Trial License Management	The system must support trial licenses that allow potential customers or new entity deployments to evaluate specific ERP modules for a configurable trial period defaulting to 30 days. Trial licenses must include configurable limitations such as maximum number of container bookings (default 50), maximum Bill of Lading generations (default 20), and restricted port access (one port only). The trial activation process must collect company information including trade license number, primary contact details, preferred language, and target ports of operation. Trial usage analytics must track feature engagement patterns to inform sales follow-up strategies. At trial expiry, the system must preserve all data created during the trial and make it accessible upon subscription purchase. Trial extensions must be approvable by sales administrators with a maximum of two 15-day extensions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R013	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Concurrent Session Enforcement	The system must enforce maximum concurrent session limits as defined by the subscription tier. When a user attempts to log in and the concurrent session limit has been reached, the system must display a message in both Arabic and English explaining that the maximum number of active sessions has been reached and provide options to either wait or terminate an existing session from the same user account. The system must distinguish between named user licenses where each user has a dedicated seat and concurrent user licenses where any user can log in up to the floating limit. Session tracking must account for active browser tabs, mobile application sessions, and API integration sessions separately. Administrators must have access to a real-time active sessions dashboard showing connected users, their roles, connected ports, and session duration to facilitate license management decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R014	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	License Transfer Between Entities	The system must support the transfer of unused or surplus licenses between entities within the same corporate group. For example, if the Qatar entity has five unused yard management module licenses while the UAE entity needs three additional licenses, an authorized administrator must be able to initiate a transfer. The transfer workflow must include a request, approval by both the source entity administrator and the destination entity administrator, and execution steps. Transfer requests must document the business justification and specify whether the transfer is permanent or temporary with a defined return date. The system must maintain a transfer history log recording all inter-entity license movements. License transfers must not affect the overall corporate license count and must validate that the source entity retains minimum required licenses after the transfer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R015	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	Subscription Payment Integration	The system must integrate with payment processing to track subscription payment status including pending, paid, overdue, and disputed states. Payment records must store the invoice number, amount in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), equivalent USD amount at the time of invoicing, payment method, and transaction reference. The system must generate proforma invoices for upcoming renewals with correct VAT calculations based on the entity's jurisdiction. Payment reminders must be sent to configured billing contacts when invoices are overdue by 7, 14, and 30 days. The Admin Portal must display a payment history timeline with filtering by entity, date range, and payment status. All financial displays must support right-to-left layout for Arabic-language users and show amounts with the correct currency symbol and decimal formatting per locale conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-002-R016	FEAT-036-3-002	MOD-036	License Dashboard and Health Indicators	The system must provide a license health dashboard with color-coded indicators showing the overall licensing status for each entity. Green indicates utilization below 70%, amber indicates utilization between 70% and 90%, and red indicates utilization above 90% or approaching expiry within 30 days. The dashboard must display key metrics including days until next renewal, current utilization percentage for each metered resource, number of available unused licenses per module, and any active entitlement enforcement events. Health indicators must be accessible from the main Admin Portal home screen and must update in real-time. The dashboard must include a recommendation engine that suggests optimal subscription adjustments based on historical usage patterns, considering seasonal fluctuations in shipping volumes typical at Gulf ports during Q4 peak season and Indian monsoon-related volume dips at JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R001	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Supported Import File Formats	The system must support data import from the following file formats: CSV (with configurable delimiters including comma, semicolon, tab, and pipe), Microsoft Excel (XLSX and XLS), JSON, XML, and UN/EDIFACT message types commonly used in container shipping (BAPLIE for bay plan, COPARN for container announcements, IFTMIN for booking instructions, COPINO for container pre-notification). Each format must have a dedicated parser with configurable encoding support including UTF-8, UTF-16, Windows-1256 (Arabic), and ISO-8859-1. The system must auto-detect file encoding when possible and allow manual override. Maximum import file size must be at least 500 megabytes for CSV and Excel formats and 200 megabytes for structured formats like JSON and XML.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R002	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Import Template Generation	The system must generate downloadable import templates for each data entity type in the ERP, including but not limited to vessels, containers, bookings, Bills of Lading, customers, tariff rates, port terminals, and container yard locations. Templates must include column headers in both English and Arabic, data type annotations, mandatory field indicators, allowed value lists for enumerated fields such as container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR), and example data rows. Templates for financial data must include currency code columns with supported values (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Templates must be available in Excel format with data validation dropdowns and CSV format with an accompanying field specification document. Each template must include a version identifier to ensure compatibility with the current system schema.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R003	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Column Mapping Interface	The system must provide an interactive column mapping interface that allows administrators to map source file columns to target system fields. The mapping interface must display a preview of the first 20 rows of the uploaded file alongside the target field definitions. Auto-mapping must be attempted based on column header name matching supporting both English and Arabic header names. Administrators must be able to manually override auto-mapped columns, skip irrelevant source columns, and specify default values for target fields not present in the source file. The mapping configuration must be saveable as a named template for reuse in recurring import tasks. The interface must support concatenation mapping where multiple source columns are combined into a single target field, and split mapping where a single source column is separated into multiple target fields using a configurable delimiter.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R004	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Data Validation Engine for Imports	The system must validate all imported data against configurable business rules before committing to the database. Validation rules must include data type checks, mandatory field enforcement, referential integrity verification against existing master data, format pattern matching for codes like IMO vessel numbers (seven digits with check digit), container numbers (ISO 6346 format with check digit validation), BIC codes, and port UN/LOCODE format. Financial amount fields must be validated for correct decimal precision per currency (two decimals for USD, QAR, AED, SAR; two decimals for INR). Date fields must accept both Gregorian (YYYY-MM-DD, DD/MM/YYYY, MM/DD/YYYY) and Hijri calendar formats. Validation must produce a detailed error report listing each invalid row with the row number, column name, invalid value, and specific validation rule that failed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R005	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Duplicate Detection and Resolution	The system must detect potential duplicate records during import by comparing incoming data against existing database records using configurable matching rules. For customer records, matching must check against trade license number, company name similarity using fuzzy matching with configurable threshold (default 85%), and contact email addresses. For container records, matching must use the container number and current status. For vessel records, matching must use IMO number as the primary key. When duplicates are detected, the system must present the administrator with options: skip the duplicate record, overwrite the existing record with imported data, merge records by selecting individual field values from either source, or create the record as new despite the potential duplication. Duplicate resolution decisions must be logable as part of the import audit trail with the administrator identity and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R006	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Bulk Import Transaction Management	The system must process bulk imports within transactional boundaries to ensure data consistency. Administrators must be able to choose between all-or-nothing mode where the entire import fails if any record has validation errors, and partial-commit mode where valid records are committed and invalid records are skipped. In partial-commit mode, the system must generate a rejection file containing all skipped records with their error details, formatted in the same file format as the source import. Import transactions must support rollback within 24 hours of completion, allowing administrators to undo an entire import batch if data issues are discovered post-import. The rollback mechanism must restore all affected records to their pre-import state. Each import batch must be assigned a unique batch identifier for tracking, auditing, and rollback reference across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R007	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Scheduled and Recurring Imports	The system must support scheduling recurring imports from preconfigured file sources including SFTP directories, cloud storage buckets (AWS S3 compatible), and shared network drives. Scheduled imports must be configurable with cron-like expressions supporting intervals from every 15 minutes to monthly. Each scheduled import must have a named profile specifying the source location, file format, column mapping template, validation rules, duplicate handling strategy, and target entity. The scheduling interface must display times in the administrator's local timezone (AST, GST, or IST) and support Hijri calendar date selection for KSA users. Failed scheduled imports must trigger automated alerts to configured administrator email addresses and must retry up to three times with exponential backoff. The system must maintain a history of all scheduled import executions showing status, record counts, error summaries, and processing duration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R008	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Export Data Selection and Filtering	The system must allow administrators to configure data exports with granular selection and filtering capabilities. Exportable data entities must include all master data tables and transaction tables across all ERP modules. Filtering must support date range selection using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, entity-level filtering (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port-level filtering using UN/LOCODE codes, status filtering, and custom field-level criteria with AND/OR logic. Column selection must allow administrators to choose which fields to include in the export and configure the column order. The system must support calculated export columns where derived values such as currency-converted amounts (converting from transaction currency to a reporting currency like USD) can be included. Export previews must display the first 50 rows before initiating the full export to allow administrators to verify the output format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R009	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Export File Format Options	The system must support data export in the following formats: CSV with configurable encoding and delimiter, Microsoft Excel XLSX with formatted headers and auto-fitted column widths, JSON with configurable nesting depth, XML with configurable schema and namespace declarations, and PDF for human-readable formatted reports. Excel exports must support multiple worksheets where related data can be split across tabs. PDF exports must support Arabic RTL text rendering, bilingual headers, and company letterhead templates per entity. All export formats must support Unicode character encoding for proper Arabic text representation. For container shipping specific needs, the system must support export in UN/EDIFACT message formats including BAPLIE for bay plan exchange with terminal operators, and COPARN for container release communication. File naming must follow configurable patterns including entity code, data type, date, and sequence number.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R010	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Large Dataset Export Optimization	The system must handle export of large datasets containing up to 10 million records without causing system performance degradation. Exports exceeding 100,000 records must be processed asynchronously with a progress indicator showing estimated completion time and percentage complete. The system must use streaming techniques to avoid loading the entire dataset into memory simultaneously. Completed export files must be made available for download from a secure temporary storage area with configurable retention defaulting to 72 hours. For very large exports, the system must support automatic splitting into multiple files with configurable maximum rows per file (default 500,000). Export processing must be assigned to a dedicated background worker queue to prevent impact on real-time ERP operations at active ports such as Hamad Port and Jebel Ali where uptime requirements are critical during vessel arrival windows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R011	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Data Transformation During Import/Export	The system must support configurable data transformations during both import and export operations. Supported transformations must include currency conversion using configurable exchange rates between USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with rate date specification, date format conversion between Gregorian and Hijri calendars, unit conversion for container weights between kilograms and pounds, temperature conversion between Celsius and Fahrenheit for reefer container data, and string transformations including uppercase, lowercase, trim, and transliteration between Arabic and Latin characters. Administrators must be able to define custom transformation rules using a formula builder interface supporting arithmetic operations, conditional logic, and lookup references. Transformation rules must be testable against sample data before applying to the full dataset. Each transformation must be logged for audit traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R012	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Import Progress Monitoring and Control	The system must display real-time progress information during bulk import operations including total records in file, records processed, records successfully imported, records failed validation, records identified as duplicates, and estimated time remaining. A progress bar must visualize overall completion percentage. Administrators must be able to pause a running import operation and resume it from the point of interruption without reprocessing already imported records. Administrators must also be able to cancel a running import, which must trigger a rollback of all records imported in the current batch. The progress monitor must be accessible from the Admin Portal dashboard so administrators can check import status while working on other tasks. Progress data must persist across browser sessions allowing an administrator to close their browser and check progress later.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R013	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Import/Export Access Control	The system must enforce granular access control over import and export operations based on user roles and entity assignments. Import permissions must be configurable per data entity type, so a user might have permission to import customer master data but not tariff rates or financial transactions. Export permissions must similarly be configurable per data entity with additional restrictions on whether sensitive fields such as customer credit terms, negotiated freight rates, and internal cost allocations can be included in exports. Entity-level restrictions must ensure that administrators of the Qatar entity cannot export data belonging to the India entity unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. All import and export operations must be logged in the audit trail with the user identity, operation type, data scope, record count, and timestamp. The access control configuration must support Arabic UI labels for role and permission names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R014	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Data Migration Wizard	The system must provide a guided data migration wizard for initial system deployment and when onboarding new entities. The wizard must guide administrators through a sequenced import process that respects data dependencies, importing master data before transactional data. The recommended sequence must be: port and terminal master data, vessel and voyage data, customer and vendor master data, contract and tariff data, then transactional data such as bookings, Bills of Lading, and invoices. The wizard must validate referential integrity at each step, ensuring for example that a booking import references valid customer IDs and vessel voyage IDs that were imported in previous steps. Progress must be displayed as a multi-step pipeline showing completed, current, and pending phases. The wizard must support saving and resuming migration sessions across multiple days for large-scale deployments involving historical data from legacy systems at ports like JNPT or Jebel Ali.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R015	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Export Scheduling and Automation	The system must support scheduled automatic exports that generate and deliver data files at configured intervals. Delivery methods must include SFTP upload to specified server paths, email attachment to configured recipient lists (with file size limits configurable per entity, defaulting to 25 megabytes), and storage in a cloud-accessible bucket with presigned download URLs. Scheduled exports must be configurable with daily, weekly, monthly, or custom cron schedules with timezone awareness for AST, GST, and IST. Each scheduled export must specify the data scope, filters, format, transformation rules, and delivery target. The system must support conditional exports that only execute if data has changed since the last export run, preventing empty file generation and unnecessary network traffic. Scheduled export execution history must be retained for 12 months showing execution timestamps, record counts, file sizes, and delivery confirmation statuses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R016	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Import Dry Run and Preview	The system must provide a dry run mode for imports that executes all parsing, mapping, validation, duplicate detection, and transformation steps without committing any data to the database. The dry run must produce a comprehensive preview report showing the total records that would be created, updated, skipped, and rejected, along with a sample of the first 100 processed records displayed in a data grid format. The preview must highlight fields that were transformed, default values that were applied, and duplicate matches that were found. Administrators must be able to transition directly from a successful dry run to actual import execution without re-uploading the file. Dry run results must be downloadable as an Excel report for offline review by stakeholders. The dry run must execute with performance characteristics representative of the actual import, providing accurate time estimates for the real import operation, especially important for large historical data migrations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-003-R017	FEAT-036-3-003	MOD-036	Multi-Entity Import Routing	The system must support importing a single file that contains data for multiple entities, automatically routing records to the correct entity based on a configurable entity identifier column. The routing rules must map entity codes or names to the internal entity identifiers for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Records with unrecognized entity identifiers must be flagged for manual review rather than rejected outright. The system must validate that the importing administrator has permission to import data for each entity referenced in the file. Cross-entity import summary must show record counts per entity and any routing errors. When importing financial data, the system must validate that currency codes align with the target entity's configured currencies (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and flag mismatches such as a QAR amount routed to the India entity for administrator review before committing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R001	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Template CRUD Operations	The system must provide full create, read, update, and delete operations for notification templates through the Admin Portal UI. Each template must have a unique identifier, a human-readable name in both English and Arabic, a category classification (operational, financial, regulatory, marketing, system alert), a channel type (email, SMS, in-app push, WhatsApp), and a status (draft, active, inactive, archived). Templates must be organized in a hierarchical folder structure allowing grouping by module, entity, or business process. The template list view must support searching by name, category, channel, and status with results displayed in a sortable, paginated data grid. Administrators must be able to clone existing templates as starting points for new templates. Template deletion must be soft-delete with the ability to restore within 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R002	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Bilingual Template Content Editor	The system must provide a rich text editor for creating notification content in both English and Arabic simultaneously. The editor must display English content in a left-to-right panel and Arabic content in a right-to-left panel side by side. For email templates, the editor must support HTML formatting including headers, paragraphs, bold, italic, lists, tables, images, and hyperlinks. For SMS and WhatsApp templates, the editor must enforce character limits (160 characters for SMS, 1024 for WhatsApp) with real-time character counters displayed for both language versions. The Arabic editor must support Arabic typography conventions including proper letter joining and diacritical marks. The editor must include a spell-check function for both English and Arabic content. Template content must support directionality markers to handle mixed-language content where English terms like container numbers or INCOTERMS appear within Arabic text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R003	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Dynamic Placeholder System	The system must support dynamic placeholders within notification templates that are replaced with actual data values when notifications are generated. Placeholders must follow a double-curly-brace syntax such as {{booking.referenceNumber}}, {{container.number}}, {{vessel.name}}, {{customer.companyName}}, {{invoice.totalAmount}}, and {{invoice.currency}}. The system must provide a searchable placeholder catalog organized by data domain (booking, container, vessel, customer, invoice, customs, port) displaying all available placeholders with descriptions and sample values. Placeholders for financial amounts must support format specifiers for currency symbol inclusion, decimal precision, and thousand separator style. Date placeholders must support format specifiers for Gregorian and Hijri output such as {{vessel.eta|hijri}} or {{vessel.eta|gregorian:dd/MM/yyyy}}. The system must validate templates to detect undefined placeholders and warn administrators before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R004	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Entity-Specific Template Branding	The system must support entity-level branding customization for notification templates. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must be able to configure its own company logo, color scheme, email header and footer templates, physical address, contact information, and legal disclaimers. Email templates must automatically apply the correct entity branding based on the originating entity of the notification trigger. The branding configuration must include separate Arabic and English versions of company names, taglines, and legal text. Footer templates must support entity-specific regulatory notices such as Qatar Financial Centre registration numbers, UAE Trade License details, Saudi Commercial Registration numbers, and Indian CIN (Corporate Identity Number). The system must preview templates with each entity's branding applied so administrators can verify the visual appearance before activating a template.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R005	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Notification Channel Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure notification delivery channels including email SMTP settings, SMS gateway credentials, in-app notification service endpoints, and WhatsApp Business API integration parameters. Each entity must have independently configurable channel settings, allowing the Qatar entity to use a different SMTP server and SMS provider than the India entity. Email configuration must include SMTP host, port, authentication credentials, TLS settings, sender email address, and sender display name in both English and Arabic. SMS configuration must support international number formatting with country codes (+974 for Qatar, +971 for UAE, +966 for KSA, +91 for India). The system must validate channel configurations by sending test messages and must display the last successful connection timestamp for each channel. Channel-specific rate limits must be configurable to prevent exceeding provider quotas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R006	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Template Versioning and History	The system must maintain a complete version history for each notification template, recording every change with the version number, modification timestamp, modifying administrator, and a diff view highlighting what changed between versions. Administrators must be able to view any historical version and revert to a previous version if needed. Version comparison must display side-by-side differences for both English and Arabic content with added text highlighted in green and removed text in red. The system must support version-specific activation where the currently active version can be different from the latest version, allowing administrators to prepare new template versions without immediately deploying them. Version history must be retained indefinitely and must not be deleted even when a template is archived. Each version must store the complete template state including content, placeholders, branding, and conditional logic to enable accurate historical reproduction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R007	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Conditional Content Logic	The system must support conditional content blocks within notification templates that show or hide content based on data attributes. Conditions must be definable using a visual rule builder interface without requiring programming knowledge. Supported conditions must include comparisons on placeholder values such as showing different content when {{shipment.incoterm}} equals CIF versus FOB, when {{container.type}} is a reefer type (20RF or 40RF) to include temperature monitoring instructions, or when {{invoice.currency}} is QAR versus INR to display appropriate payment bank details. Conditional blocks must work independently for English and Arabic content sections, allowing different conditional logic per language where necessary. Nested conditions up to three levels deep must be supported. The system must validate conditional logic for completeness, warning if certain condition branches produce empty content in either language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R008	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Template Testing and Preview	The system must provide a template testing interface where administrators can preview how notifications will appear with sample data before deploying templates to production. The preview must render the template for each supported channel (email with full HTML rendering, SMS as plain text with character count, WhatsApp with formatting preview, in-app as it would appear in the notification panel). Administrators must be able to enter custom test data values for each placeholder or select from predefined test data scenarios representing common shipping operations such as a standard FCL booking confirmation at Hamad Port, a reefer container temperature alert at Jebel Ali, or a customs clearance completion at JNPT. Test notifications must be sendable to specified test email addresses or phone numbers without affecting production notification logs. Preview must render correctly for both Arabic and English content including RTL layout verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R009	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Notification Trigger Mapping	The system must allow administrators to map notification templates to specific system events (triggers) that cause notifications to be sent. Triggers must include business events across all ERP modules such as booking creation, booking amendment, Bill of Lading issuance, container gate-in at yard, container gate-out from yard, vessel departure from port, vessel arrival at port, customs declaration submission, customs clearance granted, invoice generation, payment received, and demurrage charge notification. Each trigger must be configurable with the target template, recipient rules (who receives the notification), delivery channel, and entity scope. Multiple templates can be assigned to the same trigger for different channels, enabling for example an email and an SMS to be sent simultaneously when a vessel arrives at Khalifa Port. Trigger mappings must be activatable and deactivatable per entity independently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R010	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Recipient Rule Configuration	The system must support configurable recipient rules that determine who receives each notification. Recipient types must include static email addresses or phone numbers, dynamic recipients derived from data relationships (e.g., the booking agent's email, the customer's operations contact, the vessel master's phone number), role-based recipients where all users with a specific role at the relevant port receive the notification, and distribution lists that group multiple recipients. The system must support CC and BCC fields for email notifications. Recipient rules must allow filtering by entity and port, so a customs clearance notification at JNPT is sent only to the India operations team, not the Qatar team. The system must handle recipient deduplication to prevent the same person receiving multiple copies of the same notification when they appear in multiple recipient rule matches. Recipient rules must support language preference detection, sending Arabic or English content based on the recipient's profile language setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R011	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Notification Delivery Tracking	The system must track the delivery status of every notification sent through the platform. Tracked statuses must include queued, sent, delivered, opened (for email), clicked (for email links), failed, and bounced. For SMS notifications, the system must track sent, delivered, and failed statuses. Delivery tracking must be viewable in a centralized notification log with filtering by template, channel, recipient, date range, entity, and delivery status. Failed notifications must display the failure reason (invalid email, SMS gateway error, recipient opted out, etc.) and must support manual retry by administrators. The system must calculate and display delivery rate metrics per template and per channel including delivery rate percentage, open rate for emails, and click-through rate. Delivery metrics must be aggregated in daily, weekly, and monthly summaries. The notification log must retain delivery records for 12 months with archival beyond that period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R012	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Notification Frequency and Throttling Rules	The system must support configurable throttling rules to prevent notification fatigue and excessive messaging to recipients. Administrators must be able to set maximum notification frequency per recipient per channel, such as no more than five SMS messages per day or no more than 20 emails per hour to a single recipient. Throttling must support quiet hours configuration where non-critical notifications are held and batched, with configurable quiet hours per timezone (for example, 10 PM to 7 AM AST for Qatar recipients). Critical notifications such as vessel safety alerts, customs hold notices, or hazardous cargo warnings must be exempt from throttling rules and delivered immediately regardless of frequency limits. The system must support notification digest mode where multiple notifications of the same type are combined into a single summary notification, useful for high-volume events like container gate movements at busy terminals at Jebel Ali which may process thousands of containers daily.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R013	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Template Approval Workflow	The system must enforce an approval workflow for notification templates before they can be activated in production. The workflow must support configurable approval chains where templates are routed to designated approvers based on the template category and entity. For example, regulatory notification templates for customs-related events may require approval from the compliance manager, while marketing templates require approval from the communications director. The approval interface must display the full template content including both Arabic and English versions, all placeholder mappings, conditional logic, and branding. Approvers must be able to approve, reject with comments, or request modifications. Rejection comments must be visible to the template author for revision. The system must track approval history including timestamps, approver identities, and decisions. Templates that fail approval must return to draft status. Emergency template changes must support an expedited approval path with post-facto review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R014	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Attachment Template Configuration	The system must support configuring file attachments for email notification templates. Configurable attachments must include system-generated documents such as PDF Bills of Lading, commercial invoices, packing lists, customs declarations, container inspection reports, and booking confirmations. Administrators must be able to specify which document attachments are included with each notification template based on the notification trigger context. Attachment size limits must be configurable per entity with a default maximum of 10 megabytes per email. When attachments exceed the size limit, the system must automatically generate a secure download link instead of attaching the file directly. Attachment file naming must be configurable using placeholder-based patterns such as BOL_{{booking.referenceNumber}}_{{date}}.pdf. The system must support attaching static files such as terms and conditions documents, port tariff schedules, or regulatory requirement guides specific to Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, ZATCA, or ICEGATE.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R015	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Unsubscribe and Preference Management	The system must provide notification preference management allowing recipients to control which notification types they receive per channel. An unsubscribe link must be automatically appended to all non-regulatory email notifications in compliance with anti-spam regulations. The unsubscribe page must be available in both Arabic and English and must allow recipients to unsubscribe from specific notification categories rather than only offering all-or-nothing unsubscription. Regulatory and compliance-mandated notifications such as customs hold alerts, sanctions screening results, and mandatory port authority communications must be exempt from unsubscription and clearly marked as mandatory in the preference center. The system must maintain an unsubscribe registry that is checked before every notification dispatch. Preference changes must take effect within five minutes. Administrators must have access to an unsubscribe analytics dashboard showing opt-out rates per notification category and channel to identify potentially unwanted notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R016	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	WhatsApp Business Template Compliance	The system must ensure that WhatsApp notification templates comply with Meta WhatsApp Business Platform policies. Templates submitted for WhatsApp delivery must follow the WhatsApp-required approval process where template content is submitted to Meta for review before becoming usable. The system must track WhatsApp template approval status (pending, approved, rejected) separately from the internal template workflow. WhatsApp templates must use the required format with a header (text or media), body (up to 1024 characters), footer (up to 60 characters), and optional quick-reply or call-to-action buttons. The system must manage WhatsApp message categories (utility, authentication, marketing) and ensure correct categorization to optimize delivery rates and minimize per-message costs. Arabic WhatsApp templates must be submitted separately from English templates as Meta reviews each language version independently. Template rejection feedback from Meta must be displayed to administrators with guidance on how to modify the template for compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R017	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Notification Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive notification analytics dashboard showing aggregate metrics across all notification channels and templates. Key metrics must include total notifications sent per day, week, and month segmented by channel and entity, delivery success rates, bounce rates for email, failure rates for SMS, average delivery latency from trigger event to recipient delivery, and recipient engagement metrics (email open rates, link click rates). The dashboard must support date range filtering using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Comparative views must allow administrators to benchmark notification performance across entities, for example comparing email delivery rates for the Qatar entity versus the India entity. Top-performing and underperforming templates must be highlighted with recommendations for improving deliverability. All dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL layout and bilingual labels. Data must be exportable in Excel and PDF formats for management reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-036-3-004-R018	FEAT-036-3-004	MOD-036	Offline Notification Queuing	The system must queue notifications when delivery channels are temporarily unavailable due to network outages, SMTP server downtime, or SMS gateway failures. The queue must persist notifications in durable storage to prevent message loss during system restarts or crashes. When connectivity is restored, the queue must automatically process pending notifications in chronological order with configurable concurrency limits to prevent overwhelming the delivery channel. The system must implement exponential backoff retry logic with a maximum of five retry attempts for transient failures. Permanently failed notifications (e.g., invalid email address, deactivated phone number) must be moved to a dead-letter queue for administrator review. The offline queue status must be visible in the Admin Portal showing queue depth, oldest pending message age, and estimated drain time. This capability is particularly critical for port operations at remote container freight stations linked to JNPT and smaller feeder port offices in Qatar where internet connectivity may be intermittent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R001	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Multi-Entity Employee Record Creation	The system must support creation of employee master records linked to one or more legal entities operating across Qatar (Hamad Port), UAE (Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), KSA, and India (JNPT). Each employee record must capture the primary employing entity, secondary entity assignments, and inter-entity transfer history. The record must store entity-specific employee IDs following configurable numbering schemes (e.g., QA-EMP-00001 for Qatar, AE-EMP-00001 for UAE). The system must enforce mandatory field validation per entity jurisdiction including national ID formats, tax registration numbers, and labor card references. Duplicate detection must run across all entities using configurable matching rules on national ID, passport number, and mobile phone number to prevent ghost employees.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R002	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Bilingual Arabic-English Profile Fields	The system must store all employee profile fields in both Arabic and English simultaneously, supporting right-to-left (RTL) rendering for Arabic text fields and left-to-right (LTR) for English. Employee names must be captured in both scripts with separate fields for first name, father name, grandfather name, and family name following GCC naming conventions. The Arabic name fields must support full Unicode Arabic character set including diacritics (tashkeel). Search functionality must work across both language fields with transliteration-aware matching so that searching in English returns Arabic-named records and vice versa. All printed documents, ID cards, and export reports must render the appropriate language based on user locale preference or destination authority requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R003	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Visa and Work Permit Lifecycle Tracking	The system must track the complete lifecycle of work visas and labor permits for each employee per jurisdiction: Qatar residence permit (QID), UAE residence visa and Emirates ID, KSA Iqama, and India employment visa where applicable. Each visa record must store issue date, expiry date (in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats for GCC jurisdictions), sponsoring entity, visa type, and permit number. Automated alerts must trigger at configurable thresholds (90, 60, 30, and 14 days) before expiry, routed to the HR manager of the respective entity. The system must block assignment of employees to port operational roles at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, or Jebel Ali if their work permits have expired or are within the critical threshold window. Renewal workflow must capture submission date, medical fitness results, and approval status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R004	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Employee Document Repository with Expiry Management	The system must provide a structured document repository per employee record supporting upload, versioning, and categorization of documents including passport copies, educational certificates, professional certifications, medical fitness certificates, port security clearance cards, and customs broker licenses. Each document must have metadata fields for document type, issue date, expiry date, issuing authority, and verification status. Documents required for port access (e.g., Hamad Port security badge, JNPT CISF clearance) must be flagged as critical with separate escalation workflows when approaching expiry. The repository must enforce maximum file size limits per document type, support PDF, JPEG, and PNG formats, and provide AI-powered OCR extraction to auto-populate metadata fields from uploaded scans. All documents must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R005	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Employee Classification and Position Hierarchy	The system must support configurable employee classification taxonomies per entity including employment type (permanent, contract, probationary, temporary, seconded), job grade structures, position hierarchies, and department trees. Each entity must maintain its own organizational structure reflecting local labor law categories: Qatar Labor Law classifications, UAE MOHRE categories, KSA Nitaqat Saudization bands, and India Shops and Establishments Act categories. The position hierarchy must support matrix reporting structures common in shipping operations where a terminal operations supervisor may report to both a local port manager and a regional operations director. The system must maintain historical records of all classification changes with effective dates and approval audit trails to support labor compliance reporting and workforce analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R006	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Multi-Currency Compensation Structure Definition	The system must store employee compensation structures in the local currency of their employing entity: QAR for Qatar operations, AED for UAE operations, SAR for KSA operations, and INR for India operations. Each compensation record must define base salary, housing allowance, transportation allowance, port-specific hardship allowance, and any entity-specific mandatory allowances per local labor law. The system must also store a USD-equivalent shadow salary for consolidated group reporting, automatically calculated using configurable exchange rates sourced from the treasury module. For employees on inter-entity secondment, the system must track split compensation across home and host entity currencies with defined allocation percentages. All monetary fields must support decimal precision appropriate to each currency (2 decimals for QAR/AED/SAR/USD, 2 decimals for INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R007	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Emergency Contact and Next-of-Kin Management	The system must capture multiple emergency contacts per employee with mandatory minimum of one contact. Each contact record must store full name in both Arabic and English, relationship type, primary and secondary phone numbers with international dialing codes, email address, physical address, and preferred contact language. For employees working in port terminal operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must enforce a minimum of two emergency contacts as per port safety authority regulations. The system must integrate with the vessel operations and incident management modules to enable rapid emergency contact notification via SMS and email during port incidents. Contact details must be exportable in a standardized format compatible with port authority emergency response systems and must be reviewable by the employee through self-service portal on an annual basis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R008	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Professional Certification and Maritime Qualification Tracking	The system must track professional certifications and maritime-adjacent qualifications for shore staff including customs brokerage licenses, dangerous goods handling certificates (IMDG Code compliance), terminal equipment operator certifications, port safety officer credentials, and freight forwarding qualifications (FIATA diplomas). Each certification record must store certification name, issuing body, certificate number, issue date, expiry date, and renewal requirements. The system must maintain a configurable matrix mapping job positions to required certifications per entity and port, automatically flagging non-compliant employees who lack required certifications for their assigned roles. AI-driven recommendations must suggest upcoming certification renewals and relevant new certifications based on employee role, career development path, and regulatory changes in the maritime logistics sector. Integration with external certification verification APIs must be supported where available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R009	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Employee Self-Service Portal Profile Updates	The system must provide a responsive self-service portal accessible via web and mobile where employees can view their profile, request updates to personal information, upload renewed documents, and track the status of pending requests. Profile update requests for sensitive fields (bank account details, national ID, emergency contacts) must route through a configurable approval workflow with maker-checker controls. The portal must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR modes with automatic language detection based on user preference or browser locale. Offline capability must allow employees at remote port locations such as outer anchorage offices or container freight stations to draft profile updates that synchronize when connectivity is restored. The self-service portal must comply with WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards and support biometric authentication on mobile devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R010	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Inter-Entity Employee Transfer and Secondment	The system must support formal employee transfer and secondment workflows between legal entities across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The transfer process must capture transfer type (permanent transfer, temporary secondment, project-based assignment), effective dates, compensation adjustment details including currency conversion, reporting line changes, and benefit continuity rules. For cross-border transfers, the system must generate checklists for visa cancellation at the source entity and new visa processing at the destination entity, linked to the visa tracking feature. End-of-service benefit calculations at the source entity must be computed per local labor law (Qatar Labor Law Article 54, UAE EOSB rules, KSA Labor Law Article 84, India Gratuity Act). The system must maintain a continuous employment record across entities for group-level seniority calculations while preserving entity-specific employment dates for local compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R011	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Hijri and Gregorian Dual Calendar Date Management	The system must support simultaneous storage and display of dates in both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar formats across all employee profile date fields including date of birth, hire date, contract start and end dates, and probation end date. The Hijri calendar implementation must use the Umm al-Qura calendar standard mandated by Saudi authorities and commonly used across GCC jurisdictions. Date conversion between Gregorian and Hijri must be automatic and bidirectional, with the system accepting input in either format and computing the other. All date fields in reports destined for GCC labor authorities must include the Hijri date alongside Gregorian. The system must handle edge cases where Hijri month sighting may differ between Qatar, UAE, and KSA, allowing entity-specific Hijri date overrides for religious holiday calculations. Date pickers in the UI must offer toggle between Gregorian and Hijri views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R012	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Employee Probation Period Tracking and Alerts	The system must track probation periods per employee per entity with configurable durations reflecting local labor law maximums: Qatar up to 6 months (non-renewable), UAE up to 6 months, KSA up to 90 days (extendable to 180 days by written agreement), and India as per state-specific Shops and Establishments Act provisions. The system must automatically calculate probation end dates from the employment start date, generate reminder alerts to line managers and HR at configurable intervals (30 days, 14 days, 7 days before expiry), and trigger a probation review workflow. The workflow must capture evaluation outcomes (confirm, extend where legally permitted, or terminate) with mandatory documentation of performance assessment notes. If no action is taken by probation end date, the system must escalate to the HR director and flag the employee record as requiring immediate attention to avoid deemed-confirmation under applicable labor law.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R013	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Nationalization Quota Compliance Tracking	The system must track and report on nationalization compliance metrics per entity: Qatarization percentages for Qatar operations, Emiratization targets for UAE operations, and Nitaqat Saudization bands for KSA operations. The system must maintain real-time dashboards showing current nationality mix versus mandated targets per job category and entity, with drill-down capability to individual department and position level. When new hires or terminations would cause an entity to breach nationalization thresholds, the system must generate proactive warnings during the recruitment approval workflow. Historical trend analysis must show quarterly nationalization progress with AI-generated recommendations for meeting targets such as identifying roles suitable for national candidates based on qualification requirements and availability in the local labor market. Reports must be exportable in formats required by MOLSS Qatar, MOHRE UAE, and HRSD KSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R014	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Employee Data Privacy and Access Control	The system must enforce role-based access control (RBAC) on employee profile data with granular field-level permissions. HR administrators in one entity must not access employee records of another entity unless granted explicit cross-entity access. Sensitive fields including salary information, medical records, disciplinary history, and national ID numbers must be masked by default and only visible to authorized roles. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all profile views, edits, and exports with timestamp, user identity, IP address, and fields accessed. Data retention policies must be configurable per entity to comply with Qatar Data Protection Law, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act 2023. The system must support data subject access requests and right-to-erasure workflows while maintaining legally required records per labor law retention mandates which typically override deletion requests for employment records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R015	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	AI-Powered Employee Profile Completeness Scoring	The system must calculate a profile completeness score for each employee record using a weighted scoring algorithm that considers mandatory fields per entity jurisdiction, document upload status, certification currency, and emergency contact completeness. The AI engine must analyze patterns across the workforce to identify systemic gaps such as departments with consistently low profile completeness or document categories frequently missing for specific employee types. Weekly automated reports must be generated for HR managers highlighting the top incomplete profiles requiring attention, prioritized by operational risk (e.g., employees in port-facing roles at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali with missing security clearance documents scored higher than back-office staff with missing educational certificates). The scoring model must be configurable per entity with adjustable field weights and must track completeness trends over time to measure HR data quality improvement initiatives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-001-R016	FEAT-037-1-001	MOD-037	Bulk Employee Data Import and Migration	The system must support bulk import of employee records from Excel spreadsheets, CSV files, and integration feeds from legacy HR systems during initial data migration and ongoing batch operations. The import engine must validate all records against entity-specific mandatory field rules, data format constraints, and referential integrity checks before committing any records to the database. Validation errors must be reported in a detailed error log with row numbers, field names, and specific error descriptions in both Arabic and English. The import must support mapping of legacy system codes to new system reference data through configurable transformation rules. For container shipping operations transitioning from manual or spreadsheet-based HR management at smaller port offices, the system must provide import templates pre-configured with the correct field structures for each entity jurisdiction. A dry-run mode must be available to preview import results without committing changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R001	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Jurisdiction-Specific Leave Type Configuration	The system must support configuration of leave types per legal entity reflecting local labor law mandates: Qatar Labor Law (annual leave of 3 weeks increasing to 4 weeks after 5 years, 2 weeks sick leave at full pay plus 4 weeks at half pay), UAE MOHRE regulations (30 days annual, 90 days sick, 5 days bereavement, 5 days parental), KSA Labor Law (21 days annual increasing to 30 days after 5 years, 120 days sick), and India (as per applicable state Shops and Establishments Act plus central Maternity Benefit Act). Each leave type must be configurable with accrual rules, carry-forward limits, encashment eligibility, pro-rata calculation methods for partial year employment, and whether the leave type is paid, unpaid, or partially paid. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar-based accrual cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R002	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Religious and Public Holiday Calendar Management	The system must maintain entity-specific public holiday calendars reflecting official government announcements for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must support both fixed Gregorian holidays and variable Hijri-based holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Islamic New Year, Prophet's Birthday) whose exact dates are confirmed by moon sighting announcements. An administrative workflow must allow HR to update Hijri holiday dates once officially announced by each country's authority, with the ability to adjust dates per entity since GCC countries may observe holidays on different days. For port operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must distinguish between holidays where port operations continue with skeleton staffing versus full shutdown days, automatically adjusting shift rosters and overtime eligibility calculations accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R003	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Request and Multi-Level Approval Workflow	The system must provide a leave request workflow where employees submit requests via self-service portal or mobile application, specifying leave type, start date, end date, and optional supporting documents (medical certificates, travel itineraries). The approval workflow must support configurable multi-level routing based on leave type and duration: short leaves (1-3 days) may require only direct manager approval, while extended leaves (over 2 weeks) must route through department head and HR. For employees in critical port operational roles (terminal operations, vessel planning, customs clearance), the system must enforce a mandatory handover checklist completion before leave approval can be finalized. Approvers must see team leave calendar overlays showing concurrent absences to prevent understaffing of critical shipping operations. Rejection must require mandatory reason documentation, and employees must be notified in real-time via push notification and email in their preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R004	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Balance Calculation and Accrual Engine	The system must maintain real-time leave balances per employee per leave type, computed through a configurable accrual engine that processes monthly or annual accruals based on entity labor law requirements. The accrual engine must handle pro-rata calculations for mid-year joiners, deductions for unpaid leave periods, and adjustments for entity-specific rules such as Qatar's leave accrual suspension during probation. Carry-forward rules must be configurable per entity and leave type with maximum carry-forward limits and use-by deadlines (e.g., UAE mandates employees take annual leave within the year or carry forward only with employer agreement). The balance engine must correctly handle leave year boundaries which may differ across entities (calendar year, Hijri year, or fiscal year). All balance calculations must produce auditable computation logs showing each accrual transaction, deduction, adjustment, and carry-forward with date, source rule, and resulting balance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R005	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Sick Leave with Medical Certificate Integration	The system must enforce jurisdiction-specific sick leave rules including mandatory medical certificate requirements. For Qatar, sick leave beyond 2 days must be supported by a medical report from a government-approved healthcare facility. For UAE, sick leave requires a medical certificate from the first day if company policy mandates it, with statutory entitlement of 15 days full pay, 30 days half pay, and 45 days unpaid. For KSA, the system must track the 120-day sick leave entitlement with tiered pay rates (30 days full, 60 days three-quarters). The system must support upload of medical certificates via mobile camera capture with AI-powered document validation that checks for required elements (doctor name, facility stamp, diagnosis indication, recommended leave duration). Consecutive sick leave exceeding configurable thresholds must trigger automated notifications to HR for fitness-to-work review before the employee returns to port operational duties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R006	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Hajj and Umrah Leave Entitlement Processing	The system must support Hajj leave as a distinct leave type for Muslim employees in GCC entities as mandated by labor laws: KSA Labor Law Article 115 grants 10-15 days paid Hajj leave once during employment tenure, Qatar and UAE have similar provisions for Muslim employees who have not previously performed Hajj. The system must track whether an employee has already availed Hajj leave across their entire employment history including inter-entity transfers to prevent duplicate entitlements at the group level. Hajj leave requests must be linked to the Hajj season calendar dates with appropriate buffer periods for travel. For port operations, the system must provide workforce impact analysis when multiple Hajj leave requests overlap, showing coverage gaps in critical roles such as vessel operations coordination, container yard management, and customs documentation processing at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The system must also support Umrah leave where applicable per company policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R007	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Maternity and Paternity Leave Compliance	The system must enforce maternity and paternity leave entitlements per jurisdiction: Qatar (50 days maternity leave with full pay), UAE (60 days maternity: 45 full pay and 15 half pay, plus 5 days paternity), KSA (70 days maternity with full pay, plus 3 days paternity), and India (26 weeks maternity under Maternity Benefit Act 2017 for first two children, 12 weeks for subsequent children, plus 15 days paternity under Central Civil Services rules where applicable). The system must track eligibility prerequisites such as minimum service duration before maternity leave entitlement. Leave balance calculations must correctly exclude maternity and paternity leave from annual leave deductions. The system must support optional childcare leave extensions per company policy. Return-to-work dates must be calculated automatically with consideration for medical extensions, and post-maternity flexible working arrangement requests must be capturable with HR approval workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R008	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Encashment and End-of-Service Leave Calculation	The system must calculate leave encashment amounts for unused annual leave balances upon employment termination or during annual encashment windows where permitted by entity policy and labor law. Encashment calculations must use the correct salary basis per jurisdiction: Qatar uses basic salary, UAE uses basic salary plus allowances for leave salary calculation, KSA uses actual wage, and India uses average daily wages. The system must compute encashment amounts in the employee's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and also provide USD equivalents for group-level financial reporting. For end-of-service scenarios, the system must calculate accrued but unused leave days from the last leave anniversary to the termination date using pro-rata methods specified by each jurisdiction's labor law. All encashment calculations must generate detailed computation worksheets showing the base salary used, daily rate derivation, number of encashable days, and resulting amounts for payroll processing and employee transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R009	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Team Leave Calendar and Operational Coverage View	The system must provide an interactive team leave calendar showing approved and pending leave for all team members in a department or operational unit. The calendar must support daily, weekly, and monthly views with color-coded leave type indicators and must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts. For port operational teams, the calendar must overlay minimum staffing requirements so that managers can immediately identify potential coverage gaps. When staffing falls below configured minimum thresholds for critical functions such as vessel berthing coordination at Hamad Port, container handling operations at Jebel Ali, or customs clearance processing at JNPT, the calendar must display visual warnings. The calendar must support filtering by entity, department, job grade, and leave type. A print-friendly version must be available for posting in port operational control rooms. Integration with the shift management system must show leave alongside shift rosters for a unified workforce availability view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R010	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Compensatory Off and Time-Off-in-Lieu Management	The system must manage compensatory time-off (comp-off) earned by employees who work on public holidays, weekends, or beyond standard shift hours during peak port operational periods such as vessel surge days or quarter-end customs clearance rushes. Comp-off credits must be automatically calculated based on attendance and overtime records with configurable rules per entity defining eligibility criteria, accumulation limits, and use-by-expiry periods. For employees at port terminal operations, comp-off earned during critical operations like emergency vessel handling at Khalifa Port or monsoon-season operations at JNPT must be tagged with the source event for audit purposes. The system must allow employees to request comp-off usage through the standard leave workflow with manager approval. Expired unused comp-off balances must be handled per entity policy: either forfeited, encashed, or escalated to HR for manual decision. Balance reports must distinguish between comp-off and regular leave entitlements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R011	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Policy Rule Engine with Entity Overrides	The system must implement a configurable rule engine for leave policies that supports global base policies with entity-level overrides. The rule engine must evaluate conditions including employee entity, job grade, employment type, tenure, nationality, and department to determine applicable leave entitlements and restrictions. Policy rules must support effective dating so that changes to leave policies apply prospectively without retroactively affecting existing balances. The rule engine must handle complex scenarios such as employees on inter-entity secondment who may be subject to either home or host entity leave policies based on the secondment agreement terms. Policy simulation capability must allow HR administrators to model the impact of proposed policy changes on leave liability and workforce availability before activation. All rule evaluations must produce traceable decision logs explaining which rules were applied and why, supporting employee queries and labor dispute resolution in shipping operations across multiple jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R012	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	AI-Driven Leave Pattern Analytics and Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI algorithms to analyze leave patterns across the workforce and detect anomalies indicative of policy abuse, chronic absenteeism, or burnout risk. Pattern analysis must identify behaviors such as frequent single-day sick leaves on specific weekdays, leave taken consistently before or after public holidays to create extended breaks, and sudden changes in leave frequency. For port operational staff, the system must correlate leave patterns with vessel schedules and peak operational periods to identify potential gaming of leave timing around high-workload periods at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The AI must generate monthly reports for HR managers with risk-scored employees and recommended interventions. All analytics must operate within the bounds of entity-specific data privacy regulations and must anonymize data used for aggregate trend analysis while only providing individual-level insights to authorized HR personnel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R013	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Offline Leave Request Submission from Remote Port Locations	The system must support offline leave request submission for employees working at remote port locations, container freight stations, or inland container depots where internet connectivity may be intermittent or unavailable. The mobile application must allow employees to complete leave request forms including date selection, leave type, and reason while offline, storing the request locally with timestamp and device signature. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must automatically synchronize pending requests to the server, applying the original submission timestamp for policy compliance calculations (e.g., minimum advance notice requirements). Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where leave balance was consumed by another approved request during the offline period, notifying both the employee and approver of the conflict. The offline mode must support viewing of cached leave balances and team calendar data with clear indicators that the data may not reflect real-time changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R014	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Impact on Payroll Integration	The system must generate leave transaction records that integrate seamlessly with the payroll processing module for accurate salary calculations. Each approved leave record must produce payroll-relevant data including leave type pay category (full pay, half pay, unpaid), number of working days absent, and applicable salary deduction or allowance adjustment rules per entity labor law. For unpaid leave, the system must compute daily salary rates per jurisdiction-specific formulas: Qatar uses basic salary divided by working days, UAE uses gross salary divided by 30, and KSA uses actual daily wage. The system must handle month-spanning leave correctly by splitting leave transactions across payroll periods. Leave encashment amounts must be computed and transmitted to payroll with correct GL coding per entity chart of accounts. Currency-specific calculations must be performed in the employee's entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with proper rounding rules per currency precision requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R004	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Saudi Arabia WPS and MUDAD Integration	The system must integrate with the Saudi Arabian MUDAD wage protection platform for all KSA-based shore staff payroll processing. Payroll files must comply with the Ministry of Human Resources and Social Development (MHRSD) format specifications, including employee Iqama number, GOSI subscription number, bank IBAN in SAR currency, and detailed salary component breakdown. The system must validate that each employee salary meets the minimum wage thresholds defined by the Saudization (Nitaqat) program and flag any discrepancies. For container shipping operations staff at Saudi ports, the system must handle special allowances including hazardous material handling premiums and overtime rates computed per Saudi Labour Law Article 107. Failed MUDAD submissions must trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff and notify the payroll administrator after three consecutive failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R015	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Annual Leave Travel Ticket Entitlement Tracking	The system must track annual leave travel ticket entitlements for expatriate employees in GCC entities as mandated by Qatar, UAE, and KSA labor laws. The system must record ticket entitlement frequency (annual or biennial based on contract terms and labor law), eligible destination (home country or as per contract), class of travel, number of dependents covered, and monetary ticket allowance amounts in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR). The system must link ticket entitlements to annual leave requests, ensuring that travel ticket benefits are only processed alongside qualifying leave periods of minimum statutory duration. For employees who opt for ticket encashment in lieu of actual travel, the system must calculate the cash value based on prevailing airfare rates or fixed contract amounts. The system must generate reports for finance departments showing upcoming ticket liabilities for budget forecasting, broken down by entity, department, and destination country. Unused ticket entitlements must be tracked with configurable carry-forward and forfeiture rules per entity policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R016	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Cancellation and Recall from Leave Workflow	The system must support both employee-initiated leave cancellation and employer-initiated recall from leave. Employees must be able to cancel approved but not-yet-commenced leave through the self-service portal, with automatic restoration of leave balance and notification to the approving manager. For leave already in progress, the employer must be able to initiate a recall-from-leave request with mandatory business justification, typically for critical port operational needs such as vessel emergency handling at Hamad Port, peak season surge at Jebel Ali, or regulatory audit response requirements. The recalled employee must receive notification in their preferred language with details of the recall reason. The system must track recall events for compliance reporting and must calculate compensation adjustments where labor law mandates additional pay for recalled employees. Any unused portion of recalled leave must be automatically re-credited to the employee's balance and made available for future scheduling.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-002-R017	FEAT-037-1-002	MOD-037	Leave Liability Reporting and Financial Provisioning	The system must calculate and report leave liability (the monetary value of accrued but unused leave) per employee, department, entity, and consolidated group level for financial provisioning under IFRS and local accounting standards. Leave liability calculations must use current salary rates and apply jurisdiction-specific valuation rules: Qatar leave salary includes basic salary only, UAE includes basic plus regular allowances, KSA uses actual wage inclusive of all allowances. The system must compute liability in entity local currency and provide USD consolidated values using exchange rates from the treasury module. Monthly liability movement reports must show opening balance, accruals, utilization, encashment, adjustments, and closing balance. For container shipping operations with seasonal workforce fluctuations around peak vessel traffic periods, the system must provide trend analysis showing projected liability growth and recommend optimal leave utilization campaigns to manage financial exposure. Reports must be exportable in formats compatible with the general ledger and financial reporting modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R001	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Multi-Modal Attendance Capture Methods	The system must support multiple attendance capture methods to accommodate diverse work environments across port terminals and corporate offices: biometric fingerprint and facial recognition scanners at fixed locations (port gate houses, terminal buildings, office lobbies), GPS-enabled mobile check-in for field staff at container yards and freight stations, NFC/RFID badge tap at controlled access points, and manual timesheet entry with supervisor attestation for exceptional situations. Each attendance event must record employee ID, timestamp (synchronized to UTC with local timezone conversion), capture method, device identifier, and GPS coordinates where applicable. The system must validate GPS check-ins against predefined geofence boundaries for each work location including Hamad Port terminal areas, Khalifa Port container yards, Jebel Ali free zone offices, and JNPT dock areas. Failed biometric reads or GPS validation failures must be logged with reason codes and escalated through configurable exception handling workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R002	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Shift Schedule Definition and Rotation Management	The system must support definition and management of complex shift patterns used in 24/7 port terminal operations. Shift types must include fixed day shifts for office staff, rotating 8-hour shifts (morning, afternoon, night) for terminal operations, 12-hour continental shift patterns, split shifts for peak vessel arrival windows, and flexible working hours for corporate headquarters staff. Each shift pattern must define start time, end time, break periods (including prayer break times configurable per entity for GCC operations), minimum rest intervals between shifts per labor law requirements, and overtime trigger thresholds. The system must support rotation schedules across shift types with configurable cycle lengths (weekly, fortnightly, monthly). Shift templates must be assignable at individual employee, team, department, or port location level. The system must validate shift assignments against labor law constraints including maximum daily working hours (Qatar: 8 hours/48 per week, UAE: 8 hours standard with Ramadan reduction, KSA: 8 hours reduced to 6 during Ramadan).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R003	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Ramadan Working Hours Adjustment	The system must automatically adjust working hour calculations and shift schedules during the month of Ramadan for GCC entities as mandated by labor law. For UAE and KSA, the standard working day must be reduced by 2 hours during Ramadan, and the system must recalculate overtime thresholds accordingly so that overtime only applies after the reduced Ramadan working hours. The system must handle the variable start and end dates of Ramadan based on Hijri calendar moon sighting announcements, allowing HR administrators to set the exact Ramadan period per entity once officially declared. Shift schedules for port terminal operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali must be automatically regenerated for the Ramadan period with adjusted timings while maintaining minimum operational coverage. The system must differentiate between Muslim and non-Muslim employees where labor law permits different working hour rules during Ramadan (KSA applies reduced hours to all workers, while some entities may vary). Prayer break times must be adjusted to reflect Ramadan prayer schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R004	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Overtime Calculation and Compliance Engine	The system must automatically calculate overtime hours and applicable premium rates per jurisdiction labor law: Qatar mandates 125% of hourly wage for overtime and additional premiums for night work, UAE requires 125% for regular overtime and 150% for overtime between 9 PM and 4 AM, KSA mandates 150% for all overtime hours, and India follows applicable state-specific Factories Act or Shops and Establishments Act rates (typically double time). The overtime engine must correctly compute hourly rates from monthly salaries using jurisdiction-specific divisors. The system must enforce maximum overtime limits per labor law (Qatar: not to exceed 2 hours per day, UAE: not to exceed 2 hours per day with annual maximum, KSA: not to exceed 720 hours per year). Overtime must be categorized as regular overtime, holiday overtime, night shift premium, and rest-day work with different premium rates. Port operational managers at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali must be able to pre-approve overtime for vessel surge operations through batch approval workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R005	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Real-Time Attendance Dashboard and Alerts	The system must provide a real-time attendance dashboard showing current workforce presence status across all port locations and offices. The dashboard must display total headcount, present count, absent count, late arrivals, early departures, and employees currently on approved leave for each location including Hamad Port terminal zones, Khalifa Port operations center, Jebel Ali free zone offices, and JNPT dock facilities. Visual heat maps must show attendance density across different zones within large port facilities. Automated alerts must trigger for configurable events: employee not checked in within grace period after shift start, critical-role absence without approved leave, consecutive unauthorized absences exceeding threshold, and minimum staffing levels breached for essential port operations. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR modes with real-time data refresh intervals no longer than 60 seconds. Historical attendance trend charts must be available with filtering by entity, department, location, and date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R006	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Late Arrival and Early Departure Policy Enforcement	The system must enforce configurable late arrival and early departure policies per entity. Grace periods must be definable per shift type (e.g., 10-minute grace for office shifts, 5-minute grace for port operational shifts requiring precise handover). Late arrivals beyond grace periods must be automatically categorized by severity: minor late (within 30 minutes), moderate late (30-60 minutes), and serious late (over 60 minutes) with each category triggering different consequences per entity policy. The system must maintain cumulative late arrival counts per month with configurable thresholds that trigger escalation actions such as warning notifications, salary deductions per entity labor law provisions, or referral to disciplinary workflow. For port terminal shift operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali where precise shift handover timing is critical for operational safety, the system must send immediate notifications to shift supervisors when incoming shift personnel are late, enabling them to extend the departing shift or arrange coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R007	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Geofence Configuration for Port Work Locations	The system must support definition of geofence boundaries for all work locations using GPS polygon coordinates or radius-based zones. Each port facility must support multiple granular geofence zones: Hamad Port must have separate zones for the container terminal, general cargo berths, port administration building, and gate complex. Similarly, Jebel Ali must have zones for different terminal operators, and JNPT must have zones for each berth cluster, CFS areas, and the administrative block. The geofence configuration must specify allowed check-in methods per zone, minimum GPS accuracy thresholds for mobile check-ins, and whether the zone requires additional validation such as NFC tap confirmation. The system must handle GPS drift and signal interference common in port environments with large metal structures (container stacks, ship-to-shore cranes) by applying configurable accuracy tolerance buffers. Geofence violation events (employee checking in from outside the defined boundary) must be flagged for supervisor review rather than automatically rejected, as legitimate edge cases occur when employees are at port perimeter facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R008	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Attendance Regularization and Exception Handling	The system must provide an attendance regularization workflow for handling exceptions where normal attendance capture failed or produced incorrect records. Common scenarios include biometric device malfunction at port gate houses, employee forgetting to check out after emergency vessel operations, GPS failure in covered container yard areas, and power outages affecting time clock systems. Employees must be able to submit regularization requests through the self-service portal specifying the date, claimed check-in or check-out time, reason for exception, and supporting evidence. Regularization requests must route through line manager approval with optional secondary approval by HR for frequent requestors. The system must maintain statistics on regularization frequency per employee, flagging excessive regularization requests that may indicate attendance policy circumvention. All regularized records must be permanently marked as manually adjusted with full audit trail showing the original captured data, the adjustment made, approver identity, and approval timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R009	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Working Hours Calculation with Break Deductions	The system must calculate net working hours per shift by deducting break periods according to entity labor law requirements and shift configuration. For Qatar, a minimum 1-hour break must be deducted for shifts exceeding 6 consecutive hours. For UAE, break periods totaling at least 1 hour must be deducted and not counted as working time. For KSA, employees must not work more than 5 consecutive hours without a 30-minute break. For India, the Factories Act mandates breaks after 5 hours of continuous work. The system must support both fixed break schedules (deducted automatically) and actual break tracking via check-out and check-in at break facilities. Prayer break handling must be configurable per entity: either included within the standard break allocation or provided as additional time. The calculation engine must produce daily, weekly, and monthly working hour summaries per employee, correctly handling scenarios spanning midnight, cross-day shifts, and partial attendance days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R001	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Master Lease Agreement Creation with Multi-Entity Support	The system must allow authorized users to create master lease agreements (MLAs) linked to one or more legal entities across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Each MLA must capture the lessor name, lessor code, agreement effective date, expiry date, auto-renewal clause flag, notice period in days, and governing law jurisdiction. The agreement must support assignment to specific container types including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, and 20FR. The system must validate that no overlapping active MLA exists for the same lessor-entity-container type combination and must store both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R010	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Biometric Device Integration and Health Monitoring	The system must integrate with biometric attendance hardware from major manufacturers through standard protocols (ZKTeco SDK, Suprema BioStar API, HID Global OSDP) for fingerprint, facial recognition, and iris scan devices deployed at port terminals and office locations. The integration must support real-time event push from devices to the central attendance server as well as periodic batch synchronization as fallback. Device health monitoring must track connectivity status, battery levels for wireless devices, storage capacity for offline record buffers, and biometric template synchronization status. The system must alert IT administrators when devices go offline or report errors, with priority escalation for devices at critical port access points such as Hamad Port main gate, Jebel Ali Terminal 1 gate, or JNPT dock entry. The system must handle device failover scenarios where if a primary biometric device fails, employees can use a designated backup device or mobile check-in as a temporary alternative without disrupting port access workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R011	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Attendance Data Offline Synchronization	The system must ensure continuous attendance tracking even during network connectivity interruptions at port locations. Biometric devices at port gates and terminal buildings must buffer attendance records locally when server connectivity is lost, with automatic synchronization once connectivity is restored. The local buffer must support storage of at least 10,000 attendance events per device. Mobile attendance applications must similarly cache check-in and check-out events with secure local storage and background synchronization. The synchronization engine must handle conflict resolution for scenarios where duplicate records exist from manual override entries made during the outage period. All offline-buffered records must be clearly marked with an offline flag and the time delta between capture and synchronization. For remote port facilities such as outer anchorage control stations or inland container depots connected via satellite or cellular links, the system must support low-bandwidth synchronization modes that prioritize attendance event transmission over less critical data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R012	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Attendance Integration with Access Control Systems	The system must bidirectionally integrate with physical access control systems at port facilities to correlate employee attendance with security gate entry and exit events. Port access control systems at Hamad Port (operated under Qatar Ports Management Company regulations), Khalifa Port (under Abu Dhabi Ports), Jebel Ali (under DP World), and JNPT (under Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority) each maintain their own gate access logs. The system must ingest gate access events and reconcile them with attendance records to identify discrepancies such as employees who checked in via biometric but have no gate entry record or vice versa. Reconciliation exceptions must be flagged for security team review. The integration must respect each port authority's data sharing protocols and comply with their information security requirements. For employees requiring access to multiple port facilities, the system must aggregate access events from all locations into a unified attendance record linked to the correct shift and work location assignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R013	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Roster-Based Attendance Compliance Reporting	The system must generate compliance reports comparing planned roster schedules against actual attendance data. Reports must calculate key metrics including attendance rate, absenteeism rate, overtime utilization rate, late arrival frequency, and schedule adherence percentage per employee, team, department, and entity. For port operations, specific reports must show shift handover compliance measuring whether incoming shift personnel arrived before the outgoing shift departure, critical for maintaining continuous vessel operations at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali container terminals. Monthly summary reports must be produced in formats meeting labor authority requirements: MOLSS Qatar periodic returns, MOHRE UAE WPS compliance data, and HRSD KSA wage protection evidence. The system must support configurable report templates with both Arabic and English headers and content. Reports must be available through scheduled automatic generation with email delivery to designated recipients and on-demand generation through the reporting interface with export to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R014	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	AI-Powered Attendance Anomaly Detection	The system must utilize AI and machine learning models to detect attendance anomalies and patterns that may indicate buddy punching (one employee clocking in for another), habitual tardiness trends, attendance manipulation, or operational scheduling inefficiencies. The AI engine must analyze biometric confidence scores to flag low-confidence matches that could indicate buddy punching attempts at port gate biometric terminals. Pattern recognition must identify employees whose attendance closely mirrors another specific employee, suggesting coordinated fraudulent check-ins. For GPS-based mobile attendance, the system must detect implausible location jumps (e.g., check-in at Hamad Port and check-out at Jebel Ali within an unreasonable timeframe). The system must generate weekly anomaly reports scored by confidence level and potential financial impact, enabling HR and operations managers to investigate high-priority cases. All AI detections must be presented as alerts requiring human review, not automatic disciplinary actions, to ensure fairness and comply with labor law due process requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R015	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Night Shift and Split Shift Attendance Handling	The system must correctly handle attendance for shifts that span midnight (night shifts) and split shifts common in port operations. Night shift attendance records where check-in occurs on one calendar date and check-out on the next must be attributed to the correct shift date based on configurable rules (typically the date the shift starts). Split shifts where an employee works a morning port operation window, takes an extended midday break, and returns for an afternoon/evening customs clearance window must capture all check-in and check-out pairs and aggregate total working hours correctly. The system must compute night shift premiums as applicable per jurisdiction labor law alongside regular overtime calculations. Shift changeover periods at port terminals must be handled with overlapping attendance windows to prevent false absence flags during handover periods. The system must support configurable shift date attribution rules per entity to accommodate different operational practices at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R002	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Per-Diem Rate Schedule Configuration	The system must support configuring tiered per-diem rate schedules within each lease agreement. Rates must be definable per container type, per size (20-foot vs 40-foot), and per age bracket (0-5 years, 5-10 years, 10-15 years, 15+ years). Each rate schedule must specify the currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) and the applicable billing frequency (daily, monthly, or per-voyage). The system must support rate escalation clauses with annual percentage increases and effective dates. AI must recommend optimal rate negotiations by analyzing historical market benchmarks and current fleet utilization ratios across all four operating ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R016	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Attendance-Payroll Integration Transaction Generation	The system must generate monthly attendance summary transactions for payroll processing containing calculated working days, absent days categorized by type (authorized, unauthorized, partial day), overtime hours by premium category, late deduction incidents, night shift premium days, and holiday work days. Each transaction must be in the employee's entity currency and reference the applicable salary components. The payroll integration must support a review and approval workflow where HR validates the attendance summary before releasing it to payroll, with the ability to adjust exceptions identified during the review. The system must produce reconciliation reports comparing attendance system totals against payroll input files to identify discrepancies. For multi-entity operations, attendance transactions must be generated per entity with correct entity coding for intercompany billing scenarios where employees from one entity work at another entity's port facility. The cutoff date processing must handle attendance records received after the payroll cutoff through a subsequent adjustment cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-003-R017	FEAT-037-1-003	MOD-037	Facial Recognition Temperature and Health Screening Integration	The system must support integration with facial recognition attendance devices that include health screening capabilities such as temperature measurement and mask detection, deployed at port facility entry points as required by port health and safety regulations. Temperature readings and health screening results must be captured alongside attendance events with configurable thresholds that trigger automated responses: employees with elevated temperature readings at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali terminal gates must be flagged for secondary health screening before being permitted to access operational areas. Health screening data must be stored in compliance with health data privacy regulations per jurisdiction, with access restricted to authorized health and safety personnel only. The system must generate daily health screening reports for port facility managers showing screening volumes, flag rates, and follow-up action outcomes. Integration must support the major thermal screening device manufacturers and comply with port authority health protocols at all four operational ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R001	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Configurable Appraisal Cycle and Template Management	The system must support definition of multiple appraisal cycles per entity with configurable frequency (annual, semi-annual, quarterly), cycle start and end dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and phase timelines for goal setting, mid-cycle review, self-assessment, manager evaluation, calibration, and final rating communication. Appraisal templates must be configurable per entity, department, and job grade with sections for KPI-based objectives, competency assessments, behavioral indicators, and development goals. Templates must support both Arabic and English content with the ability for evaluators to enter comments in either language. The system must allow HR administrators to create, modify, and version-control templates with effective dating so that changes to appraisal structures apply to future cycles without affecting in-progress evaluations. Standard shipping industry competency frameworks must be available as template building blocks, including competencies specific to terminal operations, freight forwarding, customs brokerage, and maritime logistics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R002	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	KPI Definition and Cascading from Organizational Objectives	The system must support hierarchical KPI definition that cascades from organizational-level strategic objectives down through entity, department, team, and individual employee levels. For container shipping operations, organizational KPIs such as vessel turnaround time at Hamad Port, container throughput at Jebel Ali, customs clearance time at JNPT, and customer satisfaction scores must cascade to relevant departments and individuals with appropriate weighting. Each KPI must define measurement methodology, target value, threshold ranges (below expectations, meets expectations, exceeds expectations), data source for actual value measurement, and measurement frequency. The system must support both quantitative KPIs with numerical targets and qualitative KPIs with rubric-based assessment criteria. KPI weights must be assignable per role so that a terminal operations supervisor might have 60% operational KPIs and 40% behavioral competencies while a finance analyst might have a different mix. The system must validate that all individual KPI weights within an appraisal sum to 100%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R003	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Automated KPI Data Collection from Operational Systems	The system must integrate with operational modules of the container shipping ERP to automatically collect actual performance data for quantitative KPIs. Integration sources must include the vessel operations module for berth productivity and turnaround metrics, the container yard management module for throughput and dwell time statistics, the customs and documentation module for clearance processing times, the customer service module for complaint resolution rates, and the financial module for budget adherence data. Automated data collection must populate actual KPI values at configurable intervals (daily, weekly, monthly) with the ability for employees and managers to view real-time KPI dashboards showing progress against targets. The system must handle data availability delays gracefully, showing the last available data point with a freshness timestamp. For KPIs sourced from external systems such as port authority performance reports from Hamad Port or Jebel Ali terminal operating systems, the system must support manual data entry with source document upload as evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R004	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Self-Assessment and Manager Evaluation Workflow	The system must implement a structured workflow where employees first complete a self-assessment against their assigned KPIs and competencies, followed by manager evaluation with the ability to view the employee's self-assessment. Both self-assessment and manager evaluation forms must capture rating scores per KPI and competency using a configurable rating scale (e.g., 1-5 or descriptive scale), narrative comments justifying the rating, and evidence references (documents, metrics, project outcomes). The workflow must enforce sequential completion: self-assessment must be submitted before the manager evaluation opens, and the manager evaluation must be completed before the review meeting phase activates. For multi-entity operations, the system must handle matrix reporting scenarios where an employee at JNPT reports to both a local port operations manager and a regional director based in Dubai, requiring evaluation input from both managers with configurable weighting of each manager's rating. The system must send automated reminders at configurable intervals to employees and managers with pending evaluations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R005	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	360-Degree Feedback Collection and Anonymization	The system must support 360-degree feedback collection from multiple rater categories: direct manager, skip-level manager, peers, direct reports, and internal customers (other departments or entities within the shipping group). The feedback nomination process must allow both manager-selected and system-suggested raters based on organizational relationships and project collaboration history. Feedback forms must be tailored per rater category with relevant competency questions, and must support both Arabic and English response entry. Peer and direct report feedback must be anonymized in the final appraisal report, showing aggregated scores and themes rather than individual responses, with a minimum respondent threshold (configurable, typically 3) below which category results are suppressed to protect anonymity. For cross-entity feedback scenarios common in shipping operations (e.g., Qatar customs team rating UAE documentation team's responsiveness), the system must facilitate cross-entity rater selection while maintaining data access controls. Feedback completion rates must be tracked with automated reminders to non-respondents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R006	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Rating Calibration and Normalization Process	The system must support a formal calibration process where managers at the same level convene to review and normalize performance ratings across their teams to ensure consistency and reduce rating bias. The calibration module must display a configurable nine-box grid (performance vs. potential matrix) showing all employees in a calibration group, with the ability to drag-and-drop employees between boxes during calibration discussions. The system must highlight statistical outliers such as managers who consistently rate all employees at the highest level or significant rating discrepancies between self-assessment and manager evaluation. Calibration must be performed per entity, department, or job grade band with configurable grouping rules. The system must enforce a target distribution curve where organizational policy mandates forced ranking (common in some shipping companies), displaying current distribution against target distribution in real-time during calibration. All rating adjustments made during calibration must be logged with justification notes, and the pre-calibration and post-calibration ratings must both be preserved in the employee record for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R007	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Performance Improvement Plan Creation and Tracking	The system must support creation and tracking of Performance Improvement Plans (PIPs) for employees whose appraisal ratings fall below acceptable thresholds. PIP templates must define improvement objectives with measurable targets, support resources and training to be provided, review checkpoint dates, and consequences of not meeting improvement targets. Each PIP must be linked to the originating appraisal record and must specify a duration aligned with entity labor law requirements for fair performance management process documentation. For GCC entities, PIP documentation must meet the evidentiary requirements for lawful termination under Qatar Labor Law Article 61, UAE Labor Law provisions for termination due to poor performance, and KSA Labor Law Article 80 conditions. The system must track PIP progress through scheduled review meetings with structured evaluation forms capturing improvement assessment at each checkpoint. Automated escalation must notify HR when PIP deadlines approach or when review meetings are not conducted on schedule. PIP outcome recording must support results of successful completion, extension, or recommendation for further action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R008	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Career Development Plan and Competency Gap Analysis	The system must generate individual career development plans based on performance appraisal outcomes, competency gap analysis, and employee career aspirations. The competency gap analysis engine must compare an employee's current assessed competency levels against the required competency profile for their target role, identifying specific development areas. For container shipping operations, the system must maintain competency profiles for roles across the value chain including terminal operations, vessel planning, container logistics, customs brokerage, freight forwarding, and port facility management at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Development plans must recommend specific actions including training courses, on-the-job assignments, mentoring relationships, and cross-entity rotations. The system must integrate with the training management module to track completion of recommended development activities. AI-powered career path suggestions must analyze the employee's current competencies, performance trajectory, and available positions across all entities to recommend viable career progression options within the shipping group.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R009	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Performance-Linked Compensation Recommendation Engine	The system must generate compensation adjustment recommendations based on appraisal outcomes using configurable rules that consider final rating, current salary position within grade band, entity budget allocations, and market benchmark data. The recommendation engine must produce salary increment percentages, bonus amounts, and promotion eligibility flags per employee. Recommendations must be generated in the employee's entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with consolidated budget impact reports in USD for group-level management review. The system must support configurable compensation matrices mapping rating levels to increment ranges per entity, with separate matrices for different job grade bands. For multi-entity operations, the engine must account for different market conditions: a high performer in India may receive a different percentage increase than an equivalent performer in Qatar due to market salary dynamics. The recommendation output must feed into an approval workflow where HR reviews and department heads approve within allocated budgets before final compensation changes are committed to the payroll master.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R010	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Probation Period Performance Evaluation	The system must support distinct probation evaluation processes separate from the regular annual appraisal cycle. Probation evaluations must be triggered automatically based on the probation end dates tracked in the employee master, with configurable evaluation timing (e.g., at 30 days, 60 days, and final evaluation at probation end). Probation evaluation forms must be tailored with role-specific onboarding KPIs such as completion of port safety orientation at Hamad Port or JNPT, successful completion of terminal operating system training, achievement of initial productivity targets, and cultural fit assessment. The evaluation workflow must result in one of three outcomes: confirm employment, extend probation (where legally permitted per entity jurisdiction), or terminate employment during probation. For each outcome, the system must generate appropriate documentation templates compliant with the entity's labor law requirements. The system must track probation evaluation completion rates and escalate incomplete evaluations to HR directors when probation end dates are approaching without a recorded evaluation decision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R011	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Continuous Feedback and Recognition Log	The system must support continuous feedback beyond formal appraisal cycles, allowing managers and peers to record positive recognition and constructive feedback at any time. Feedback entries must capture the feedback type (recognition, coaching, concern), related competency or KPI, specific situation description, and recommended actions. For port operations scenarios, real-time recognition must be possible for outstanding performance during critical events such as achieving record vessel turnaround time at Jebel Ali, successful handling of hazardous cargo incidents at Hamad Port, or exceptional customer service during peak season at JNPT. The continuous feedback log must be accessible during formal appraisal preparation, providing managers with a comprehensive record of employee performance observations throughout the review period. The system must support feedback in both Arabic and English with proper RTL rendering for Arabic text. Recognition entries must optionally be shareable on an internal recognition wall visible to the team or entity. AI analysis of continuous feedback themes must generate periodic summary reports identifying trending strengths and development areas per team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R012	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Multi-Entity Performance Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide executive dashboards showing performance distribution analytics across entities, departments, and job grades. Dashboards must display rating distribution histograms, average ratings by department, calibration adjustment statistics, goal completion rates, and year-over-year performance trends. For container shipping operations, cross-entity benchmarking must allow comparison of operational role performance across ports: comparing terminal operations team performance at Hamad Port versus Jebel Ali versus JNPT to identify best practices and underperforming areas. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate metrics to department and team levels while respecting entity-level data access controls (Qatar HR can see Qatar employee details but only aggregated data for other entities). Visual elements must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts. Export functionality must produce presentation-ready reports in PowerPoint and PDF formats for board-level review. The system must provide AI-generated narrative summaries of key performance insights and trends for each entity and the consolidated group.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R013	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Appraisal Document Generation and Digital Signing	The system must generate formal performance appraisal documents from completed evaluation data in both Arabic and English, formatted per entity requirements and branding guidelines. Generated documents must include employee details, KPI scores with evidence summaries, competency ratings, overall rating, manager comments, employee self-assessment excerpts, development plan summary, and compensation recommendations where applicable. The document generation engine must support configurable templates per entity with appropriate legal disclaimers and employee acknowledgment clauses per jurisdiction labor law. Digital signing capability must allow both the appraising manager and the employee to electronically sign the appraisal document with timestamp and identity verification. The system must handle scenarios where the employee disagrees with the appraisal, allowing recording of employee grievance notes attached to the signed document. Signed appraisal documents must be automatically filed in the employee document repository and retained per entity-specific document retention policies. For employees who cannot access digital signing due to port field operations, the system must support print, wet-sign, and scan-upload workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R014	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	AI-Driven Performance Prediction and Attrition Risk Scoring	The system must employ machine learning models to predict future performance trajectories and identify employees at risk of attrition based on historical performance data, attendance patterns, leave utilization trends, compensation competitiveness, tenure, and engagement survey results. The AI model must generate risk scores at individual and team levels, highlighting employees whose predicted performance decline or attrition probability exceeds configurable thresholds. For container shipping operations, the model must weight factors specific to the industry such as port operational role burnout indicators, impact of shift pattern assignments on retention at 24/7 terminal facilities like Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and market demand for specialized roles like vessel planners and customs brokers. Predictions must be presented to HR managers as actionable insights with recommended interventions (compensation review, role rotation, development opportunity, management attention). All AI predictions must include confidence intervals and must be clearly labeled as decision-support tools requiring human judgment, not automated decisions, to comply with emerging AI governance regulations across GCC and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R015	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Goal Setting and Objective Alignment Workflow	The system must provide a structured goal-setting workflow at the beginning of each appraisal cycle where employees propose individual objectives aligned with their department and entity-level strategic goals. Each goal must capture the objective description in both Arabic and English, measurable key results with quantifiable targets, timeline with milestone dates, required resources, and alignment linkage to the parent department or entity objective. The workflow must route proposed goals through manager review and approval, with the ability for the manager to suggest modifications before finalizing. For container shipping operational roles, the system must provide goal libraries with pre-defined operational objectives relevant to terminal efficiency, vessel operations, documentation accuracy, and customs compliance at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Mid-cycle goal revision capability must allow adjustments for significant operational changes such as new shipping route launches, port expansion projects, or regulatory changes, with approval workflow and change documentation. The system must track goal achievement progress through the cycle with visual progress indicators on employee and manager dashboards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R005	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	India Payroll Statutory Compliance	The system must compute payroll for India-based shore staff at JNPT (Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust) in INR, incorporating all statutory deductions including Professional Tax (state-specific slabs for Maharashtra), Income Tax (TDS as per prevailing Finance Act slabs), Employee Provident Fund (EPF at twelve percent of basic), Employee State Insurance (ESI at 0.75 percent for applicable salary brackets), and Labour Welfare Fund contributions. The system must generate Form 16, Form 24Q, and monthly ECR files for EPF submission. Payroll calculations must handle the old tax regime and new tax regime options per employee election. The system must support mid-month advances common in port operations during peak shipping seasons and reconcile these against final monthly payroll. All INR computations must round to the nearest rupee as per Indian statutory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R016	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Succession Planning Integration with Performance Data	The system must integrate performance appraisal data with succession planning processes by identifying high-potential employees based on sustained performance ratings, leadership competency assessments, and development plan progress. The succession planning module must allow HR and senior leadership to map successor candidates for critical roles across the container shipping organization, particularly for specialized port operational leadership positions at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each successor candidate profile must show their performance history, readiness assessment (ready now, ready in 1-2 years, development needed), identified gaps with development actions, and cross-entity mobility willingness. The nine-box grid from calibration must feed directly into succession talent pools with configurable criteria for each quadrant. The system must generate succession depth reports showing the number of ready successors for each critical role across entities, highlighting single points of failure where key roles have no identified successors. AI-powered recommendations must suggest non-obvious successor candidates from across entities based on transferable competencies and career trajectory analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R017	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Performance Appraisal Grievance and Appeal Process	The system must provide a formal grievance and appeal workflow for employees who disagree with their performance appraisal outcomes. The appeal process must capture the employee's written grievance detailing specific areas of disagreement, supporting evidence, and requested resolution. Appeals must route to a configurable review panel which may include the skip-level manager, HR representative, and an independent reviewer from another department or entity. The system must enforce timeline requirements for each stage: appeal submission window after appraisal communication (configurable, typically 14 days), panel review period, and resolution communication deadline. For GCC entities, the grievance documentation must meet labor law requirements for fair process evidence that may be presented in labor dispute proceedings before the Qatar Labor Dispute Committee, UAE MOHRE mediation, or KSA Labor Courts. The system must track all grievance communications, panel meeting minutes, and resolution decisions with timestamps and participant identities. Grievance outcomes including upholding original rating, adjusting the rating, or ordering a re-evaluation must be recorded with detailed justification and reflected in the employee's appraisal record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-1-004-R018	FEAT-037-1-004	MOD-037	Appraisal Cycle Administration and Compliance Monitoring	The system must provide comprehensive administration tools for HR to manage appraisal cycles across all entities from initiation to completion. Administration capabilities must include cycle launch with bulk notification to all participants, real-time progress tracking showing completion percentages per phase (goal setting, self-assessment, manager evaluation, calibration, sign-off) per entity and department, and automated escalation for overdue actions. The system must generate compliance reports showing which managers have not completed evaluations for their direct reports, which employees have not submitted self-assessments, and which calibration sessions are pending. For container shipping operations where port staff may be on rotational schedules or deployed at different terminals, the system must accommodate flexible deadlines per work location while maintaining overall cycle target dates. The administration dashboard must display progress in both Arabic and English with exportable status reports. Historical cycle data must be archived and searchable for trend analysis across multiple appraisal periods. The system must support configuration of cycle-specific rules such as minimum evaluation narrative length and mandatory development goal count.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R001	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Multi-Entity Payroll Run Initiation	The system must support initiating separate payroll runs for each legal entity operating across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKLF), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INJNP) jurisdictions. Each payroll run must be entity-specific, respecting local pay periods (monthly for Qatar/UAE/KSA, monthly or biweekly for India). The payroll initiation screen must display entity name in both Arabic and English, show the pay period start and end dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and allow authorized payroll officers to select specific departments such as port operations, vessel scheduling, container yard management, customs clearance, and administration. The system must prevent duplicate payroll runs for the same entity and period combination and must log all initiation actions with timestamp, user ID, and entity code for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R002	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	WPS SIF File Generation for UAE Entities	The system must generate Salary Information Files (SIF) in the exact format mandated by the UAE Central Bank Wage Protection System for all UAE-based shore staff at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali facilities. The SIF file must include employee MOL number, bank routing code (IBAN), salary amount in AED, and allowance breakdowns. The system must validate all IBAN numbers against the UAE banking format before file generation, reject records with incomplete bank details, and produce a discrepancy report listing all rejected employee records with reasons. SIF files must be generated no later than the 28th of each month and support both electronic submission and manual download. The system must maintain a complete archive of all generated SIF files with version history for Central Bank audit requirements spanning a minimum seven-year retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R003	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Qatar WPS Compliance and QCB Submission	The system must generate WPS-compliant payroll files for Qatar-based shore staff at Hamad Port in accordance with Qatar Central Bank (QCB) regulations. Salary disbursements must be processed exclusively in QAR through approved banking channels. The system must capture and validate each employee Qatar ID (QID) number, bank account details, and labor contract reference number. The WPS file must include basic salary, housing allowance, transportation allowance, and any port-specific shift differentials for employees working container terminal operations, vessel berthing coordination, and customs liaison roles. The system must flag any salary payment that deviates more than ten percent from the contracted amount and require payroll manager approval before inclusion. All WPS submissions must comply with Qatar Labour Law Articles 64-71 regarding timely payment of wages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R002	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	AI-Powered Requisition Auto-Classification	The system must provide an AI engine that automatically classifies purchase requisitions into procurement categories such as vessel spares, container repair materials, bunker fuel, port handling equipment, safety equipment, provisions, IT hardware, and general office supplies. The AI classifier must achieve at least 92% accuracy based on the item description, historical purchase data, and vendor catalog mapping. Misclassified requisitions must be flagged for manual review. The classification must determine the downstream sourcing strategy, including whether the item requires a formal RFQ process, a direct purchase order, or a blanket order release under an existing framework contract.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R006	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Multi-Currency Salary Conversion and Disbursement	The system must support salary disbursement in multiple currencies where employees at container shipping entities may hold contracts denominated in USD but receive payment in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The exchange rate applied must be sourced from the entity treasury module or an approved central bank reference rate locked at a payroll-officer-approved date no earlier than five business days before the pay date. The system must compute gross-to-net calculations in the contract currency first, then convert the net payable amount using the locked exchange rate. Any foreign exchange gain or loss arising from rate fluctuations between payroll computation date and actual bank transfer date must be recorded in a separate FX variance ledger. The system must display all currency amounts with proper formatting including the correct decimal precision for each currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R007	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Overtime and Shift Differential Calculation	The system must compute overtime pay for shore-based staff working extended hours at container terminals, vessel berth coordination centers, and port customs clearance offices. Overtime rates must comply with local labor law in each jurisdiction: UAE Labour Law mandates 125 percent for daytime overtime and 150 percent for nighttime overtime; Qatar Labour Law Article 74 mandates 125 percent minimum; Saudi Labour Law Article 107 mandates 150 percent; and Indian Factories Act mandates double the ordinary wage rate. The system must integrate with attendance and time-tracking data to automatically identify overtime hours exceeding the standard work week (48 hours for GCC, 48 hours for India). Shift differentials for night operations at ports handling 24/7 vessel arrivals must be configurable per entity and per job role, with separate rates for weekend and public holiday shifts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R008	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Payroll Approval Workflow and Segregation of Duties	The system must enforce a multi-level approval workflow for payroll processing with strict segregation of duties. At minimum, the workflow must include four stages: payroll preparation by the payroll officer, first review by the HR manager, second review by the finance controller, and final approval by the entity general manager or authorized signatory. No single user may perform more than one stage in the same payroll cycle. The system must generate a variance report comparing current payroll against the previous period highlighting any changes exceeding five percent in total cost, new hires, terminations, and salary adjustments. Each approver must digitally acknowledge review of the variance report before advancing the workflow. The approval workflow must support delegation with time-bound authority and maintain a complete audit trail of all approval actions including timestamp, IP address, and user identity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R009	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Payslip Generation in Arabic and English	The system must generate individual payslips for each employee in both Arabic and English, formatted for RTL display when Arabic is selected. Payslips must include entity legal name, employee name in both scripts, employee ID, designation, department (mapped to shipping operations function such as terminal operations, vessel planning, container tracking, or customs brokerage), pay period in both Gregorian and Hijri dates, and a detailed breakdown of all earnings and deductions. Earnings must separately itemize basic salary, housing allowance, transport allowance, port operation allowance, overtime, and any ad hoc payments. Deductions must itemize social insurance, tax withholdings, loan repayments, and any disciplinary deductions. Payslips must be available as downloadable PDF documents and accessible via the employee self-service portal. The system must send automated notifications via email and SMS when payslips are published.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R010	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Bank File Formatting and Transmission	The system must generate bank transfer files compatible with the specific file formats required by major banks operating in Qatar (QNB, Commercial Bank), UAE (Emirates NBD, ADCB, Mashreq), KSA (Al Rajhi, SNB, Riyad Bank), and India (SBI, HDFC, ICICI). Each bank file format must include the correct header records, detail records with beneficiary IBAN or account number, payment amount, currency code per ISO 4217, payment reference, and trailer records with hash totals. The system must support SWIFT MT103 format for cross-border salary transfers to employees repatriating portions of their salary. File transmission must support both secure FTP upload and API-based submission where banks offer such interfaces. The system must verify file integrity using checksums before transmission and maintain delivery receipts with bank acknowledgment references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R011	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Payroll Reversal and Correction Processing	The system must support payroll reversal and correction scenarios that commonly arise in container shipping operations, such as when an employee transfer between entities (e.g., from Hamad Port to Jebel Ali) is backdated, or when retrospective salary adjustments are mandated by union agreements or regulatory changes. A full payroll reversal must create contra entries that zero out the original payroll transaction and generate a new corrected payroll run. Partial corrections must allow adjustment of individual salary components without reversing the entire payroll. All reversals and corrections must flow through the same approval workflow as regular payroll and must update the WPS submission records accordingly. The system must recalculate all statutory deductions (social insurance, tax) based on corrected figures and generate amended regulatory filings. Reversal transactions must reference the original payroll run ID and maintain bidirectional traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R012	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Payroll Journal Entry and GL Integration	The system must automatically generate general ledger journal entries from each completed payroll run, mapped to the entity chart of accounts. Journal entries must debit salary expense accounts segmented by department (terminal operations, vessel scheduling, container freight station, customs, administration) and cost center (specific port facility code), and credit payable accounts for net salary, statutory deductions, and employer contributions. The system must support multi-currency journal entries where the payroll entity operates in local currency but the group consolidation requires USD reporting. Intercompany journal entries must be automatically created when employees are seconded between entities, for example a Qatar-hired operations manager temporarily assigned to Jebel Ali. All journal entries must include payroll run reference, period, and entity code, and must be exportable in formats compatible with SAP, Oracle Financials, and the ERP native accounting module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R013	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Leave Encashment and Accrual Processing	The system must calculate leave encashment amounts for shore staff who opt to cash out accrued but unused annual leave days, as permitted by their employment contracts and local labor law. In Qatar, leave encashment must be computed on basic salary per Qatar Labour Law Article 79. In the UAE, encashment must use the basic salary rate per Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021 Article 29. In KSA, encashment must be based on the actual wage per Saudi Labour Law Article 111. In India, encashment must follow the Payment of Gratuity Act and company policy. The system must track accrued leave balances monthly, account for any leave consumed or forfeited, and compute the financial liability for accrued leave for balance sheet provisioning. Leave encashment payments processed through payroll must be subject to the same WPS and bank file generation processes as regular salary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R014	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Retroactive Pay Adjustment Processing	The system must handle retroactive pay adjustments triggered by backdated salary revisions, promotions, or regulatory minimum wage changes effective from a prior date. When a retroactive adjustment is entered, the system must recalculate the net pay difference for each affected pay period, including recalculation of all proportional statutory deductions (social insurance contributions, income tax, EPF) and employer contributions. Arrears must be computed as a separate line item on the payslip and clearly identified as retroactive adjustment with reference to the applicable period range. For WPS compliance, retroactive adjustments must be reported as supplementary payments in the current period WPS file with appropriate coding. The system must prevent double payment of arrears by tracking retroactive adjustments against a unique adjustment reference ID and linking to the original salary revision authorization document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R015	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Offline Payroll Computation and Sync	The system must support offline payroll computation capabilities for scenarios where network connectivity is disrupted at remote port facilities or during maritime emergencies affecting shore-based IT infrastructure. The offline module must cache the latest approved salary master data, tax tables, social insurance rates, and exchange rates locally on the payroll officer workstation. When operating offline, the system must allow full gross-to-net computation, payslip preview, and draft bank file generation. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline computations with the central server, detect any conflicts arising from concurrent master data changes (such as an employee termination processed at headquarters while payroll was computed offline), and present a reconciliation screen requiring manual resolution of each conflict. Offline-generated payroll data must be digitally signed with a timestamp to ensure integrity during the sync process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-001-R016	FEAT-037-2-001	MOD-037	Payroll Analytics and Cost Reporting Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time payroll analytics dashboard showing total payroll cost by entity, department, port facility, and job category for container shipping operations. Key metrics must include total payroll cost as a percentage of revenue, cost per TEU (twenty-foot equivalent unit) handled for labor cost benchmarking against industry standards, overtime cost trends correlated with vessel call volumes, and headcount versus payroll cost variance analysis. The dashboard must support drill-down from entity level to individual employee level and allow filtering by date range using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors. All monetary values must be displayable in the entity local currency or converted to USD for group-level comparison. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with proper right-aligned numerical formatting and Arabic number representation. Export options must include PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with proper Unicode encoding for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R001	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	GOSI Contribution Calculation for KSA Employees	The system must calculate General Organization for Social Insurance (GOSI) contributions for all KSA-based shore staff. For Saudi nationals, the system must compute the Annuities Branch contribution at the prescribed rate (currently 9.75 percent employee and 9.75 percent employer on basic salary plus housing allowance, capped at SAR 45,000 monthly) and the Occupational Hazards Branch at 2 percent employer-only. For non-Saudi employees, only the Occupational Hazards Branch at 2 percent applies. The system must track each employee GOSI subscription number, validate that the contribution base salary matches the GOSI-registered salary, and flag discrepancies for HR review. Contribution calculations must account for mid-month joiners and leavers on a pro-rata daily basis. The system must apply updated GOSI rate changes within one payroll cycle of official gazette publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R002	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	GOSI Monthly Filing and XML Submission	The system must generate GOSI monthly contribution files in the XML format prescribed by the GOSI electronic portal for all KSA entity employees. The filing must include establishment number, employee GOSI subscription number, Iqama or national ID, contribution month, basic salary, housing allowance, total contribution base, employee share, employer share, and occupational hazard premium. The system must validate the file against GOSI schema requirements before submission, checking for data completeness, format compliance, and mathematical accuracy of contribution totals. Any validation errors must be presented in a clear report with corrective action suggestions. The system must support automated submission via GOSI API where available and manual XML file download for portal upload. Filing must be completed by the 15th of the following month, and the system must generate reminder notifications starting five days before the deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R003	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Qatar PIFSS Contribution Management	The system must compute Public Institution for Social Security (PIFSS) contributions for Qatari national employees working at Hamad Port and other Qatar-based facilities. PIFSS contributions must be calculated on the total pensionable salary, which includes basic salary and social allowance, at the prescribed rates (currently 5 percent employee and 10 percent employer). The system must track each Qatari employee PIFSS membership number, date of first enrollment, and contribution history. For employees who have transferred from government service to the private shipping sector, the system must maintain records of prior service periods that count toward pension eligibility. The system must generate monthly PIFSS returns in the format required by the institution and support electronic submission. Non-Qatari employees are exempt from PIFSS but the system must maintain records confirming their exemption status linked to their residency permit type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R004	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	UAE GPSSA and Pension Contribution Processing	The system must calculate General Pension and Social Security Authority (GPSSA) contributions for UAE national employees at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali shore operations. Contributions must be computed on the contribution salary (basic salary plus allowances as defined by GPSSA) at the prescribed rates: 5 percent employee share, 12.5 percent employer share, and 2.5 percent government share for Abu Dhabi entity employees, with variations for Dubai entity per DEWS (DIFC Employee Workplace Savings) if applicable. The system must validate that the contribution salary reported to GPSSA matches the actual salary paid and flag any discrepancies exceeding the permissible tolerance. For non-UAE national employees, the system must track that they are excluded from GPSSA but may be subject to home country social insurance obligations. The system must support the GPSSA online portal file format for monthly submissions and maintain contribution history for pension calculation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R005	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	India EPF Contribution and ECR Generation	The system must calculate Employee Provident Fund (EPF) contributions for India-based shore staff at JNPT at the statutory rate of 12 percent employee share and 12 percent employer share on basic wages plus dearness allowance, subject to the statutory ceiling of INR 15,000 per month for employer EPF contribution (with 8.33 percent of employer share diverted to EPS - Employee Pension Scheme). The system must generate the Electronic Challan cum Return (ECR) file in the format mandated by the Employees Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO) including UAN (Universal Account Number), member name, gross wages, EPF wages, EPF contribution, EPS contribution, and EDLI contribution. The ECR must be validated against EPFO schema before submission. The system must handle voluntary provident fund (VPF) contributions where employees elect to contribute above the statutory minimum and track the combined balance for each employee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R006	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	India ESI Contribution Processing	The system must calculate Employee State Insurance (ESI) contributions for eligible JNPT shore staff whose gross monthly wages fall at or below the ESI applicability threshold (currently INR 21,000 per month). The employee contribution rate is 0.75 percent and the employer contribution rate is 3.25 percent of gross wages. The system must automatically determine ESI eligibility based on the employee current gross wage and flag any employees whose salary crosses the threshold mid-year, applying the prescribed transition rules (coverage continues for the contribution period once enrolled). The system must generate ESI contribution challans and monthly returns in the format required by the ESIC portal, including IP number, name, days worked, wages paid, and contribution amounts. The system must maintain contribution records to support ESI benefit claims for medical coverage, sickness benefits, and maternity benefits for eligible port staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R007	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Rate Configuration and Versioning	The system must maintain a configurable rate table for all social insurance schemes across jurisdictions (GOSI, PIFSS, GPSSA, EPF, ESI) with effective date versioning. When a government authority changes contribution rates, ceiling limits, or applicability rules, the payroll administrator must be able to create a new rate version with the effective date without altering historical calculations. The system must automatically apply the correct rate version based on the payroll period being processed, ensuring that mid-period rate changes are handled correctly by pro-rating contributions. Each rate configuration must store employee percentage, employer percentage, applicable salary components, ceiling amount, minimum contribution floor, and any special rates for specific worker categories such as expatriate maritime operations staff. All rate changes must go through an approval workflow and maintain audit history showing who made the change, when, and the authorizing regulatory reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R008	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Employee Social Insurance Registration Automation	The system must automate the social insurance registration process for new shore staff hires across all entities. Upon completion of the hiring workflow, the system must prepare registration data for submission to the appropriate authority: GOSI for KSA hires, PIFSS for Qatari nationals in Qatar, GPSSA for UAE nationals in UAE, and EPFO/ESIC for India hires. Registration data must include employee personal details, nationality, identification numbers, employment start date, initial salary, job title mapped to the social insurance occupation classification, and the employer establishment number. The system must track registration status (pending, submitted, confirmed, rejected) and display outstanding registrations on the HR dashboard. For rejected registrations, the system must capture the rejection reason from the authority portal and create a task for the HR officer to remediate. Registration must be completed within the statutory timeframe: within 30 days for GOSI, within the first month for EPFO.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R009	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Deregistration on Termination	The system must initiate social insurance deregistration when an employee exits the organization, whether through resignation, termination, end of contract, or inter-entity transfer. The deregistration process must compute final contributions up to the last working day on a pro-rata basis and include these in the final settlement payroll. For GOSI, the system must generate the termination notification with reason code (resignation, termination, transfer, retirement) and last contribution month. For EPF, the system must facilitate the generation of Form 10C and Form 19 for withdrawal claims or Form 13 for EPF transfer to a new employer. The system must prevent final settlement payment from being processed until social insurance deregistration data has been prepared and queued for submission. For employees transferring between group entities across jurisdictions (e.g., from JNPT to Hamad Port), the system must close the origin country social insurance record and initiate registration in the destination country within the same workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R010	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Employer Contribution Accrual and Liability Tracking	The system must track employer social insurance contributions as accrued liabilities on the entity balance sheet, segregated by scheme type (GOSI annuities, GOSI occupational hazard, PIFSS pension, GPSSA pension, EPF employer share, EPS, ESI employer share, EDLI). Monthly accrual journal entries must be generated automatically as part of the payroll posting process, debiting the appropriate expense accounts and crediting the respective liability accounts. When actual payments are remitted to the social insurance authorities, the system must match the payment against the accrued liability and clear the balance. Any variances between accrued contributions and actual payments (due to pro-rata adjustments, rate corrections, or penalties) must be recorded in a reconciliation account and reported to the finance controller. The system must produce a monthly social insurance liability aging report showing outstanding contributions by entity, scheme, and period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R011	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Salary Ceiling Management	The system must enforce salary ceiling limits applicable to social insurance contribution calculations in each jurisdiction. For GOSI in KSA, contributions must be capped at the prescribed maximum monthly contributable salary of SAR 45,000. For GPSSA in UAE, the contribution salary ceiling must be applied as defined by the authority. For EPF in India, the statutory ceiling for employer contribution calculation is INR 15,000 basic wages (though employee may contribute on actual basic if higher under VPF). The system must automatically identify employees whose salary exceeds the applicable ceiling and compute contributions only on the capped amount while recording the excess salary excluded from the calculation. When ceiling limits are revised by government notification, the system must allow updating the ceiling effective date and recalculate prospective contributions accordingly. The system must generate a report listing all employees at or near the ceiling threshold to assist HR in workforce cost planning for port operations expansion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R012	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Compliance Audit Report	The system must generate comprehensive social insurance compliance audit reports for each entity on a monthly and annual basis. The report must compare registered employees against the active payroll headcount and identify discrepancies such as employees on payroll but not registered with social insurance, or registered employees no longer on payroll. The report must verify that contribution amounts submitted to each authority match the payroll records and highlight any variances. For GOSI, the report must include Nitaqat compliance metrics showing the Saudization ratio by establishment and whether contribution patterns support the claimed nationalization levels. For EPF, the report must reconcile ECR submissions against payroll data and flag any UAN mismatches. The report must be presented in both Arabic and English with amounts in local currency, include a summary dashboard with compliance status indicators (green, amber, red), and support digital signature by the compliance officer for regulatory submission purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R013	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Cross-Border Social Insurance Coordination	The system must manage social insurance obligations for employees who are seconded or transferred between group entities across jurisdictions, a common scenario in multi-national container shipping operations where expertise is shared between Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must determine which country social insurance regime applies based on bilateral social security agreements (e.g., India-Qatar bilateral agreement, if applicable) and the employee home country status. Where dual coverage is possible, the system must recommend the optimal social insurance arrangement and flag cases requiring Certificate of Coverage (COC) from the home country to claim exemption in the host country. The system must maintain a secondment register tracking the origin entity, destination entity, secondment start and end dates, applicable social insurance regime, and any inter-entity cost-sharing arrangements for employer contributions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R014	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Penalty and Late Fee Tracking	The system must track penalties and late fees imposed by social insurance authorities for delayed registration, late contribution payments, or filing non-compliance. For GOSI, penalties may include fines for late submission beyond the 15th of the following month. For EPFO, penalties include interest at 12 percent per annum for delayed deposits and damages under Section 14B of the EPF Act. The system must automatically calculate estimated penalty exposure when a contribution deadline is approaching and the filing has not been submitted, displaying this as a risk alert on the HR and finance dashboards. Actual penalties received must be recorded against the entity, period, and scheme, with root cause categorization (system error, data issue, approval delay, bank processing delay). The system must generate a penalty trend analysis report to support process improvement initiatives for the port HR operations team, and all penalty amounts must be excluded from operational cost centers and charged to a dedicated compliance penalty account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R015	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	AI-Driven Social Insurance Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI algorithms to detect anomalies in social insurance contribution patterns that may indicate errors, fraud, or non-compliance. Anomaly detection must cover scenarios including sudden changes in an employee contribution base salary without corresponding HR approval, contribution amounts that deviate significantly from peer group averages within the same port operation department, employees approaching retirement age with irregular contribution histories, and patterns suggesting salary splitting to avoid crossing contribution ceiling thresholds. The AI model must learn from historical contribution data across all entities and flag suspicious patterns with a confidence score. Flagged anomalies must be routed to the compliance officer for investigation through a dedicated review queue. The system must support Arabic and English case notes for each investigated anomaly and maintain a resolution log. False positive rates must be tracked to continuously improve model accuracy, targeting less than fifteen percent false positive rate within six months of deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R016	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Offline Social Insurance Data Capture	The system must support offline data capture for social insurance registration and contribution tracking when connectivity to government portals or the central ERP server is unavailable, a critical requirement for port facilities that may experience network outages during severe weather or infrastructure maintenance. The offline module must allow HR officers to enter new employee social insurance registration details, record contribution adjustments, and prepare monthly filing data locally. Data captured offline must be validated against locally cached business rules including contribution rate tables, salary ceilings, and mandatory field requirements. Upon network restoration, the system must synchronize offline data with the central server and the relevant government portal, performing conflict detection if the same employee record was modified both offline and online. The synchronization log must be auditable, timestamped, and require HR supervisor sign-off for any conflict resolutions affecting contribution amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R003	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Multi-Currency Budget Validation	Upon requisition submission, the system must validate the requested amount against the available budget in the requisitioner's cost center. Budget checks must support multi-currency operations in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with real-time exchange rate conversion sourced from the treasury module or an integrated FX rate feed. If the requisition amount exceeds the remaining budget by any margin, the system must block submission and display the available balance, requested amount, and deficit in both the transaction currency and the entity's base reporting currency. Budget validation must account for committed but not yet invoiced amounts from existing purchase orders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-002-R017	FEAT-037-2-002	MOD-037	Social Insurance Employee Self-Service Portal	The system must provide an employee self-service portal where shore staff can view their social insurance contribution history, current registration status, and projected pension or end-of-service benefits. The portal must display contribution details by month showing employee share, employer share, and cumulative totals in both the local entity currency and USD equivalent. For EPF members in India, the portal must show the current EPF balance estimate including interest accrual. For GOSI subscribers, the portal must display the estimated monthly pension based on current contribution levels and years of service. The portal interface must support Arabic RTL layout and English LTR layout with language toggle, display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and be accessible on mobile devices used by port operations staff. The portal must clearly explain in plain language what each social insurance scheme provides, using jurisdiction-specific benefit descriptions, to help employees understand their entitlements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R001	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	UAE End of Service Gratuity Calculation	The system must calculate end-of-service gratuity for UAE-based shore staff at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali in accordance with UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021. For employees with unlimited contracts: 21 calendar days of basic salary for each year of the first five years of service and 30 calendar days of basic salary for each additional year, with the total gratuity not exceeding two years total remuneration. For employees who resign, the system must apply the reduced gratuity scale: one-third if service is between one and three years, two-thirds if between three and five years, and full entitlement if over five years. The daily wage must be computed as basic salary divided by 30. The system must handle partial year calculations on a pro-rata basis, counting actual days served in the final incomplete year. All calculations must use AED as the base currency with the option to display equivalent in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R002	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Qatar End of Service Benefit Calculation	The system must calculate end-of-service benefits for Qatar-based shore staff at Hamad Port in accordance with Qatar Labour Law No. 14 of 2004, Article 54. Employees who have completed one year of continuous service are entitled to a gratuity of not less than three weeks basic wage for each year of service. The basic wage used for calculation must be the last drawn basic salary. The system must handle the distinction between Qatari nationals (who are covered under PIFSS pension scheme and may be exempt from EOSB) and non-Qatari employees who are entitled to the gratuity. For employees terminated for cause under Article 61 grounds, the system must allow HR to flag reduced or forfeited gratuity with documented justification. Pro-rata calculation for partial years must be based on the actual number of days worked in the final year divided by 365. The system must compute the benefit in QAR and generate the payment instruction as part of the final settlement process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R003	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	KSA End of Service Award Calculation	The system must calculate the end-of-service award for KSA-based shore staff in accordance with Saudi Labour Law Articles 84-86. The award must be computed as half month actual wage for each of the first five years of service and one full month actual wage for each year beyond five years. The actual wage includes basic salary plus all fixed allowances (housing, transport) as defined in the employment contract. If the employee resigns, the system must apply the reduced scale per Article 85: one-third of the award for service between two and five years, two-thirds for service between five and ten years, and full award for service exceeding ten years. Employees terminated during probation or dismissed under Article 80 grounds forfeit the award entirely. The system must calculate the award in SAR, accounting for any unpaid leave periods that may be excluded from the service duration per company policy, and generate the payment as part of the final settlement batch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R004	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	India Gratuity Calculation under Payment of Gratuity Act	The system must calculate gratuity for India-based shore staff at JNPT in accordance with the Payment of Gratuity Act 1972. Gratuity is payable to employees who have completed a minimum of five years of continuous service (with the exception of death or disability where the minimum service requirement is waived). The calculation formula must be: last drawn wages (basic salary plus dearness allowance) multiplied by 15 days multiplied by years of service, divided by 26 (working days in a month). The maximum gratuity payable must be capped at the statutory limit of INR 20,00,000 (twenty lakh rupees) or the prevailing limit as updated by government notification. The system must handle the rounding rule where service exceeding six months in the final year is rounded up to a full year. Tax exemption on gratuity must be computed per Section 10(10) of the Income Tax Act, and the taxable portion must be included in the final settlement TDS computation. The system must generate Form I as required under the Gratuity Act rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R005	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Provision Accrual and Monthly Liability	The system must calculate and maintain monthly gratuity provision accruals for all active employees across all entities, reflecting the estimated end-of-service liability on the balance sheet as required by International Accounting Standard IAS 19 (Employee Benefits). The actuarial provision must consider each employee current salary, completed years of service, expected future salary increases, employee turnover rate by entity and department, and the applicable labor law formula for the jurisdiction. The system must generate monthly journal entries debiting gratuity expense and crediting the gratuity provision liability account, segmented by entity and cost center (port operations, administration, commercial). The provision must be recalculated at each payroll cycle to reflect salary changes, new hires, and departures. At year-end, the system must support actuarial valuation adjustments provided by external actuaries and reconcile the actuarial liability against the accounting provision, posting any differences through other comprehensive income as per IAS 19 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R006	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Multi-Scenario Gratuity Simulation	The system must provide a gratuity simulation tool that allows HR managers and employees to model end-of-service benefit amounts under different scenarios. Scenarios must include: voluntary resignation at a specified future date, employer-initiated termination, retirement at normal retirement age, and early retirement. The simulation must project the gratuity amount based on current salary with optional annual increment assumptions, reflect the applicable labor law formula for the employee entity jurisdiction, and display the result in the employee contract currency with optional conversion to USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR. For managerial staff in container shipping operations who may be considering relocation between entities, the simulation must show comparative gratuity outcomes if the employee transfers versus completes service at the current entity. The simulation tool must display results in both Arabic and English and allow saving and printing scenario comparisons for discussion during employee retention reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R007	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Offset Against Social Insurance Benefits	The system must handle the offset rules between end-of-service gratuity and social insurance pension benefits where applicable. In Qatar, Qatari national employees enrolled in PIFSS are generally exempt from employer-paid EOSB as the pension scheme substitutes this benefit, and the system must enforce this exclusion automatically based on the employee PIFSS registration status. In the UAE, employees enrolled in GPSSA pension scheme (UAE nationals) may have their gratuity obligation substituted by the pension scheme, and the system must track which benefit regime applies. In KSA, GOSI subscribers are entitled to both GOSI pension benefits and end-of-service award under the Labour Law, and the system must compute both independently. In India, gratuity under the Payment of Gratuity Act is separate from EPF and both are payable. The system must clearly distinguish these overlapping or exclusive benefit structures and prevent double-counting or omission in the final settlement calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R008	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Continuous Service Period Computation	The system must accurately compute the continuous service period for each employee, which forms the basis of all gratuity calculations. Continuous service must be calculated from the date of joining to the last working day, accounting for approved leave periods (annual leave, sick leave, maternity leave) that count toward service and excluding periods that do not count (unpaid leave exceeding the permitted threshold, unauthorized absences exceeding the tolerance defined in each jurisdiction labor law). The system must handle employment breaks where an employee left the organization and was rehired, applying the local labor law rules on whether prior service periods are aggregated or forfeited. For employees transferred between group entities (e.g., from Jebel Ali to Hamad Port), the system must support configurable rules on service continuity: either maintaining continuous service across entities (if the group policy so provides) or resetting service at the new entity with settlement at the old entity. The computation must be accurate to the day level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R009	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Final Settlement Statement Generation	The system must generate a comprehensive final settlement statement for departing employees that consolidates all terminal benefits into a single document. The statement must include: unpaid salary for the final work period, end-of-service gratuity amount with calculation methodology shown, leave encashment for accrued unused leave, any repatriation allowance payable per contract terms (applicable for expatriate staff at GCC port operations), notice period payment or deduction, recovery of outstanding loans or advances, recovery of any company property deposits, and net amount payable or recoverable. The statement must be generated in both Arabic and English with amounts in the entity local currency and employee home currency where different. The statement must reference the applicable labor law articles and the calculation formula applied. It must go through a multi-step approval workflow (HR, Finance, Legal) before payment authorization. The system must generate the payment instruction in the appropriate bank file format for final disbursement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R010	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Payment Tax Implications	The system must compute the tax implications of end-of-service gratuity payments in jurisdictions where gratuity is taxable. In India, gratuity received by employees covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act is exempt up to the statutory limit (INR 20,00,000); amounts exceeding this limit are taxable as salary income. For employees not covered under the Act (typically managerial staff exceeding the wage threshold), exemption is calculated as the least of actual gratuity received, half month salary for each year of service (based on last ten months average salary), or the statutory limit. The system must determine the employee coverage status, compute the exempt and taxable portions, and include the taxable gratuity in the final month TDS computation generating an amended Form 16. In the GCC jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA), gratuity payments are generally tax-free for employees, but the system must track these payments for employer tax reporting in the employee home country where required and generate appropriate certificates for the employee to use in home country tax filings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R011	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Bulk Gratuity Processing for Workforce Restructuring	The system must support bulk gratuity computation and processing for scenarios where multiple employees are separated simultaneously, such as during organizational restructuring, contract completions for project-based port expansion staff, or entity closure. The bulk processing module must accept a list of employees by department or project code, compute individual gratuity amounts using the applicable formula for each employee jurisdiction and contract type, generate individual settlement statements, and produce a summary report showing total financial liability by entity and currency. The system must support phased payment scheduling where the total gratuity disbursement is spread across multiple payment dates as negotiated with employee groups or unions. Budget impact analysis must be available showing the effect of the bulk separation on monthly payroll cost, social insurance obligations, and headcount by port facility. All bulk processing must maintain individual-level audit trails and support the same approval workflows as individual terminations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R012	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Fund Investment Tracking	The system must support entities that maintain a dedicated gratuity fund or trust to pre-fund end-of-service obligations. The system must track fund contributions made by the employer, investment returns earned by the fund, and withdrawals made to settle employee gratuities upon separation. The fund balance must be reconciled against the total gratuity provision liability to determine whether the fund is over-funded or under-funded, and this status must be reported on the entity financial statements as per IAS 19 requirements. The system must generate alerts when the fund coverage ratio falls below a configurable threshold (e.g., 80 percent of total liability) to prompt additional employer contributions. For Indian entities, the system must support the Gratuity Trust approved under the Income Tax Act Section 4A and track the trust separate legal entity registration, audit requirements, and annual return filing. Fund transaction records must be maintained with full audit trail in both local currency and USD equivalent for group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R004	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Configurable Multi-Level Approval Workflow	The system must support configurable multi-level approval workflows with a minimum of five approval tiers based on requisition value thresholds, procurement category, entity, and port location. For example, requisitions under 5,000 QAR at Hamad Port may require only department head approval, while requisitions exceeding 500,000 QAR must route through procurement manager, finance controller, and general manager. Approval delegation must be supported for users on leave with automatic time-bound delegation expiry. Each approval step must capture the approver identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, comments, and digital signature hash.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R013	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Contractual Gratuity Enhancement Handling	The system must support enhanced gratuity terms that exceed the statutory minimum where provided by employment contracts, collective bargaining agreements, or entity HR policies. Common in the container shipping industry for senior operations staff, enhancements may include higher multiplier rates (e.g., one month per year instead of the statutory 21 days per year in UAE), inclusion of additional salary components beyond basic salary in the computation base, or removal of the resignation penalty reduction. The system must allow configuration of contractual gratuity rules at the employee level, grade level, or entity level, with the contractual terms taking precedence over statutory minimums. However, the system must validate that contractual terms never result in a benefit lower than the statutory minimum and must apply the higher of the two calculations. Contractual gratuity rules must be linked to the digital copy of the employment contract stored in the document management system for auditability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R014	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Hijri Calendar Service Period for GCC Gratuity	The system must support gratuity calculation based on Hijri calendar years where required by contract or company policy in GCC jurisdictions. While most GCC labor laws reference service in Gregorian years, some employment contracts, particularly for government-seconded employees working in port authority liaison roles, may specify service periods in Hijri years. The system must accurately convert between Gregorian and Hijri dates using the Umm al-Qura calendar for Saudi Arabia and the standard Hijri calendar for Qatar and UAE. Gratuity computation using Hijri years must account for the approximately 11-day shorter Hijri year compared to Gregorian, resulting in marginally different gratuity amounts. The system must display the service period in both calendar systems on the final settlement statement. Date conversion must handle edge cases including dates falling in the overlap period between Hijri months (where sighting-based determination may differ from the calculated calendar) by using the official government-published calendar for each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R015	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	AI-Powered Gratuity Liability Forecasting	The system must use AI-based predictive analytics to forecast the organization total end-of-service gratuity liability over short-term (12 months), medium-term (3 years), and long-term (5 years) horizons. The forecasting model must consider current workforce demographics including age distribution and tenure distribution at each port facility, historical turnover rates by entity, department, and job grade, projected salary increments based on historical trends and budgeted increases, anticipated headcount changes from port expansion or contraction plans, and macroeconomic factors affecting exchange rates between settlement currencies. The forecast must be presented by entity, currency, and confidence interval (50th, 75th, and 95th percentile). The AI model must update its projections monthly as actual separation data becomes available, and the system must track forecast accuracy over time. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English with visualizations showing liability trajectories and be exportable for inclusion in board of directors financial presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R016	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Dispute and Appeal Management	The system must provide a workflow for managing disputes and appeals related to end-of-service gratuity calculations. When an employee or their legal representative challenges the computed gratuity amount, the dispute must be logged with the employee ID, disputed amount, claimed amount, and the grounds for dispute (e.g., disagreement on service period, salary base, or applicable law interpretation). The system must support attaching supporting documentation from both parties, including employment contracts, salary records, attendance records, and legal opinions. A dispute resolution workflow must route the case to the HR manager, legal counsel, and senior management with escalation timelines. The system must track the dispute status through stages: received, under review, mediation, resolved, or escalated to labor court. For disputes referred to labor court in Qatar (Labour Disputes Settlement Committee), UAE (MOHRE mediation), KSA (Labour Court), or India (Labour Commissioner), the system must record case numbers, hearing dates, and outcomes. Financial provisions for disputed amounts must be maintained separately from the regular gratuity provision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-003-R017	FEAT-037-2-003	MOD-037	Gratuity Calculation Audit Trail and Recalculation	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail for every gratuity calculation performed, capturing all input parameters (joining date, last working day, basic salary, applicable allowances, service breaks, separation reason), the formula applied, intermediate computation steps, and the final gratuity amount. The audit trail must record which labor law version and which company policy version were applied at the time of calculation. When a recalculation is triggered due to data correction (e.g., corrected joining date discovered in employee file), the system must preserve the original calculation, compute the revised amount, show the delta, and require approval before updating the settlement. Recalculation history must be maintained indefinitely for each employee, with the ability to reconstruct any historical calculation using the parameters and rules effective at the original calculation date. The audit trail must be accessible to internal auditors and external regulatory inspectors, and all entries must include timestamp, user ID, and system version identifier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R001	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Visa Application Workflow Initiation	The system must support initiating visa and work permit applications for new expatriate hires joining container shipping operations at Hamad Port (Qatar), Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali (UAE), and JNPT (India). The workflow must capture all required data fields per jurisdiction: for Qatar, Ministry of Interior (MOI) requirements including job offer approval, medical fitness certificate, and QID application; for UAE, Ministry of Human Resources (MOHRE) work permit application with Tasheel reference; for KSA, Ministry of Human Resources block visa allocation and Iqama application via Muqeem; for India, Employment Visa or Business Visa application via the FRRO/eFRRO portal. The system must generate a checklist of required documents per visa type and jurisdiction, track document collection status, and prevent application submission until all mandatory documents are uploaded. The workflow must support multiple concurrent applications for bulk hiring scenarios common during new terminal commissioning or peak shipping season staffing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R002	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Qatar QID and Work Permit Management	The system must manage the complete Qatar work permit and Qatar ID (QID) lifecycle for expatriate staff at Hamad Port and other Qatar-based facilities. This includes tracking the initial work visa entry permit, medical examination scheduling and results, fingerprint appointment scheduling, QID card issuance with card number and expiry date, and annual QID renewal. The system must integrate with Qatar Ministry of Interior (MOI) e-services portal (Metrash2) data formats for application submission and status tracking. Work permits must be linked to the sponsoring entity establishment card number and tracked against the entity approved staff quota. The system must generate renewal alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before QID expiry and create automated renewal tasks assigned to the PRO (Public Relations Officer). For employees requiring exit permits (where applicable under the Qatar exit permit system), the system must manage exit permit requests with supervisor approval workflow. All dates must be displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with Arabic and English text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R003	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	UAE Work Permit and Emirates ID Management	The system must manage the UAE work permit and Emirates ID lifecycle for shore staff at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali. The system must track the end-to-end process: MOHRE work permit application (new or transfer), entry visa issuance, status change (residency stamping) within the mandatory 60-day window, medical fitness test, Emirates ID application and biometric enrollment, and labor card issuance. Each stage must have configurable SLA timelines with escalation alerts when deadlines approach. The system must handle the distinction between mainland UAE entities (under MOHRE) and free zone entities (under respective free zone authority such as JAFZA for Jebel Ali Free Zone) as visa processing rules and portals differ. Work permit categories must be mapped to the MOHRE occupation classification including shipping-specific roles such as port operations manager, vessel planner, container terminal supervisor, and customs clearing agent. The system must track work permit costs including MOHRE fees, typing center charges, medical fees, and Emirates ID fees for cost allocation to the appropriate entity and cost center.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R004	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	KSA Iqama and Work Visa Management	The system must manage Saudi Arabia work visa and Iqama (residency permit) processes for shore staff via integration with Muqeem and Qiwa platform data formats. The system must track block visa quota allocation per entity, individual visa issuance against the quota, and Iqama issuance after entry into the Kingdom. Iqama details including number, issue date, expiry date (Hijri), profession as registered in Muqeem, and sponsor transfer status must be maintained. The system must enforce Iqama renewal processing before the Hijri expiry date and generate alerts using the Hijri calendar at 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry. The system must track the Nitaqat (Saudization) compliance status, calculating the ratio of Saudi to non-Saudi employees per establishment and alerting when the ratio approaches the minimum threshold for the entity current Nitaqat band (Platinum, Green High, Green Medium, Green Low, Yellow, Red). Profession changes on Iqama, which require Ministry approval, must be tracked through a separate sub-workflow with document requirements and approval status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R005	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	India FRRO Registration and Visa Tracking	The system must manage visa and Foreigners Regional Registration Office (FRRO) registration for expatriate staff working at JNPT and other India-based port operations. The system must track employment visa details including visa number, type, validity dates, port of entry, and sponsoring entity. For foreign nationals requiring FRRO/FRO registration within 14 days of arrival, the system must generate registration reminders and track registration certificate issuance. The system must manage visa extension applications through the eFRRO portal, maintaining records of all extensions with approved validity periods. For expatriate staff from specific nationalities requiring Protected Area Permits (PAP) or Restricted Area Permits (RAP) for certain port zones, the system must track permit status and validity. The system must handle the distinction between Employment Visa, Business Visa, and Project Visa categories, each with different documentation requirements and stay conditions. Visa cost tracking must include government fees, agent charges, and any travel costs in INR with conversion to the employee home entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R006	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Document Expiry Dashboard and Alert Engine	The system must provide a centralized dashboard displaying the expiry status of all visa, residency, and government documentation for shore staff across all entities. The dashboard must show documents approaching expiry within configurable warning periods (default 90, 60, 30, 15, and 7 days), expired documents, and documents in renewal processing. Document types tracked must include work permits, residency permits (QID, Emirates ID, Iqama), passports, medical fitness certificates, port access passes, security clearances for restricted port zones handling sensitive cargo, and professional licenses (customs broker licenses, dangerous goods certifications). The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port location, department, and document type. Critical alerts for expired documents that may result in regulatory penalties or employee inability to access port facilities must be escalated to entity HR director and legal counsel automatically. The dashboard must display in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with color-coded status indicators and support export to Excel for offline review by PRO teams at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R007	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Visa Cost Tracking and Allocation	The system must track all costs associated with visa and residency processing for each employee, including government fees, medical examination fees, typing and translation charges, agent/PRO service fees, travel costs for visa runs, and any penalties or fines for late renewals. Costs must be recorded in the currency of payment (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and converted to the entity base currency and group reporting currency (USD) using the applicable exchange rate. The system must support allocation of visa costs to the appropriate general ledger expense account and cost center, distinguishing between new hire visa costs (capitalizable as recruitment cost), renewal costs (period expense), and penalty costs (compliance cost). The system must generate reports showing total visa expenditure by entity, nationality, visa type, and period. Budget versus actual tracking must be supported, with the annual visa cost budget set at the entity level and monitored against actual spending with variance alerts when spending exceeds 80 percent of the budgeted amount for the period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R008	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Sponsorship Transfer Processing	The system must manage employee sponsorship transfers which occur when an employee moves between legal entities within the group, or when hiring an employee who is currently sponsored by another employer in the same country. For UAE transfers, the system must track the MOHRE transfer process including NOC (No Objection Certificate) from the current sponsor, new work permit application, and status change. For Qatar transfers, the system must manage the MOI transfer process under the current labor mobility provisions. For KSA transfers, the system must handle the Muqeem-based sponsorship transfer process including clearance from the current sponsor and GOSI transfer. The system must calculate any financial implications of the transfer including settlement of benefits at the releasing entity and the commencement of a new service period or continuation of service at the receiving entity per group policy. Transfer processing must coordinate between the HR departments of both entities with a shared workflow visible to authorized users at each entity. The system must maintain a complete transfer history for each employee across all group entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R009	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Visa Cancellation and Exit Processing	The system must manage the visa cancellation and exit process for departing employees across all jurisdictions. The cancellation workflow must integrate with the final settlement process and ensure that visa cancellation is initiated only after all financial obligations are cleared. For UAE exits, the system must generate the MOHRE cancellation request, track the 30-day grace period for departure, and generate the exit report. For Qatar exits, the system must process the QID cancellation through MOI and track the exit permit if applicable. For KSA exits, the system must process the final exit visa through Muqeem and ensure GOSI deregistration is completed concurrently. For India FRRO deregistration, the system must ensure the departure registration is completed. The system must enforce a dependency check preventing visa cancellation from proceeding if any of the following are outstanding: company property not returned, outstanding loan balance, pending legal cases, or incomplete knowledge transfer handover for critical port operations roles. The system must track the employee actual departure date and flag any cases where the employee remains in-country beyond the grace period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R010	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Family Visa and Dependent Management	The system must manage family visa and dependent residency permits for employees entitled to family sponsorship under their employment contracts. The system must track dependent details including name, relationship, date of birth, nationality, passport details, visa type, residency permit number, and expiry date. Family visa processing workflows must be managed through the same document tracking system as employee visas, with separate checklists for spouse visa, child visa, and parent visa where applicable. The system must enforce minimum salary thresholds required for family sponsorship in each jurisdiction: UAE requires a minimum salary as per MOHRE regulations, Qatar has income requirements set by MOI, and KSA has minimum salary requirements for dependent visa eligibility. Medical insurance enrollment for dependents must be triggered automatically upon family visa approval in jurisdictions where employer-provided dependent medical insurance is mandatory (UAE). The system must generate renewal alerts for dependent visas independently of the employee visa and handle dependent visa cancellation as part of the employee exit process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R011	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Port Security Access Pass Integration	The system must integrate visa and residency status verification with port security access pass management at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Shore staff requiring access to restricted port areas (container yards, vessel berths, customs bonded zones, dangerous goods storage facilities) must have valid residency permits and security clearances as prerequisites for access pass issuance. The system must automatically flag when an employee residency permit expires and trigger a security access pass suspension workflow, notifying port security administration and the employee department manager. For JNPT, the system must track the Dock Labour Board registration where applicable. For Jebel Ali Free Zone (JAFZA), the system must track the JAFZA access card linked to the employee free zone visa. The system must maintain records of port-specific security clearance levels mapped to the employee job role and the types of cargo areas they are authorized to access (general cargo, refrigerated containers, hazardous materials under IMDG Code classifications, and high-value cargo zones).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R012	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Nationality and Quota Management	The system must track employee nationality distribution across all entities and enforce any applicable nationality quota requirements. For KSA, the system must monitor Nitaqat (Saudization) ratios per establishment and per occupational category, alerting when hiring decisions would push the entity below the required Saudi national percentage for its size band. For UAE, the system must track Emiratization targets where applicable, particularly for semi-government port entities. For Qatar, the system must monitor compliance with any Qatarization requirements for port operations roles. The system must provide a nationality distribution dashboard showing headcount by nationality, entity, department, and job grade, with visual indicators when quota thresholds are approached. When HR initiates a new hire request for a non-national position, the system must verify that the hire will not breach any quota requirements and block the requisition if it would result in non-compliance. The dashboard must display in both Arabic and English and show data for all four operating jurisdictions on a single consolidated view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R013	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Government Portal Integration and Status Sync	The system must integrate with or support data exchange formats for government e-services portals used for visa and residency processing in each jurisdiction. For Qatar, integration must support Metrash2 and MOI Hukoomi portal data formats. For UAE, integration must support MOHRE Tasheel portal, ICA (Federal Authority for Identity and Citizenship) portal, and relevant free zone portals (JAFZA, KIZAD). For KSA, integration must support Muqeem, Qiwa, and Absher portal data formats. For India, integration must support eFRRO portal formats. Where direct API integration is available, the system must support automated status polling to update application status in real time. Where API access is not available, the system must provide structured data export in the portal required format and support manual status update with timestamp tracking. The system must cache the latest known status of each application with last sync timestamp and alert when status has not been updated beyond the expected processing timeline. All government reference numbers, transaction IDs, and receipt numbers must be stored and linked to the employee visa record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R014	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Medical Fitness and Health Card Management	The system must manage medical fitness examinations and health card requirements that are prerequisites for visa issuance and renewal in GCC jurisdictions and for certain port operation roles in India. The system must track medical appointment dates, examination results (fit, temporarily unfit, permanently unfit), health card issuance and expiry, and any follow-up medical requirements. For UAE, the system must track the mandatory medical fitness test for residency visa including the approved medical center, test date, and result linked to the DHA (Dubai Health Authority) or HAAD (Health Authority Abu Dhabi) health card. For Qatar, the system must track the medical commission examination required for QID issuance. For KSA, the system must track the medical examination for Iqama. For JNPT port operations staff handling food-grade containers or working in ship provisioning, the system must track food handler health certificates. Medical records must be stored with appropriate access controls limiting visibility to HR medical officer and the employee, in compliance with data privacy regulations. The system must generate medical appointment reminders and renewal alerts aligned with visa renewal timelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R015	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Passport and Travel Document Custody	The system must manage passport and travel document custody where company retention of passports is permitted by law and requested by the employee in writing. In jurisdictions where passport retention by employers is restricted or prohibited (per recent labor law reforms in Qatar and UAE), the system must enforce the legal requirement by not permitting passport custody records without a valid voluntary consent form signed by the employee. The system must track passport submission and return with date, time, receiving officer, and employee acknowledgment signature (digital or physical). When a passport is in company custody, the system must support employee requests for temporary passport release for personal travel, medical appointments, or embassy visits, with an approval workflow and expected return date tracking. The system must generate alerts if a passport is not returned within the agreed timeframe. For all shore staff, the system must maintain passport details including number, nationality, issue date, expiry date, and issuing authority, and generate renewal alerts when passport expiry approaches within twelve months. Passport expiry must be cross-referenced with visa validity to ensure coherent document timelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R016	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	AI-Powered Visa Processing Time Prediction	The system must use AI and machine learning to predict visa and residency permit processing times based on historical application data. The prediction model must consider factors including application type (new, renewal, transfer, cancellation), jurisdiction, employee nationality, applying entity, time of year (accounting for seasonal variations in government processing capacity, Ramadan period slowdowns, and national holiday closures), and the current government portal reported backlog where available. The system must display predicted processing time ranges with confidence intervals when a new application is initiated, helping HR planners at container shipping operations schedule new employee start dates and plan for workforce availability at port facilities. The model must continuously learn from actual processing outcomes, comparing predicted versus actual timelines and adjusting future predictions accordingly. Prediction accuracy metrics must be tracked and displayed to system administrators, with the target prediction accuracy within plus or minus five business days for 80 percent of applications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R017	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Visa Compliance Violation Reporting	The system must detect and report visa and residency compliance violations across all entities to minimize regulatory risk for the container shipping group. Violations tracked must include: employees working with expired residency permits, employees performing job functions not matching their visa occupation classification, employees working at a port location not covered by their visa or work permit (e.g., an employee with a Khalifa Port free zone visa working at a non-free-zone facility), family dependents with expired residency, and medical fitness certificates that have lapsed. The system must generate a monthly compliance violation report for the entity legal counsel and group compliance officer, classifying violations by severity (critical requiring immediate action, major requiring action within 7 days, minor requiring action within 30 days). For repeated violations of the same type, the system must trigger an escalation to entity general manager with a recommended corrective action plan. All violation records must be maintained for a minimum of seven years for regulatory audit purposes. The report must be available in both Arabic and English with entity-specific sections and a group-level executive summary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-037-2-004-R018	FEAT-037-2-004	MOD-037	Offline Visa Document Access and Processing	The system must support offline access to visa and residency document records for PRO (Public Relations Officer) staff who frequently visit government offices, embassies, and typing centers that may have limited or no internet connectivity. The offline module must synchronize the latest visa records, employee document images, and pending application checklists to the PRO mobile device or laptop before each government office visit. While offline, the PRO must be able to update application statuses, record government reference numbers, capture receipt images, and add processing notes. Upon reconnection, the offline updates must synchronize with the central system with conflict detection for any records modified concurrently by other users. The offline cache must include encrypted storage of sensitive document data (passport copies, visa copies, medical records) with device-level PIN or biometric authentication. If the device is reported lost or stolen, the system administrator must be able to remotely trigger a wipe of the cached visa data. The synchronization log must maintain a complete audit trail of all offline activities with timestamps and device identifiers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R001	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Multi-Entity Requisition Creation	The system must allow authorized users to create purchase requisitions under any of the configured legal entities (Qatar entity registered at Hamad Port, UAE entity at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, KSA entity at King Abdullah Port, and India entity at JNPT Mumbai). Each requisition must capture the originating entity code, port location, department, cost center, and budget line item. The entity selection must automatically apply the correct chart of accounts, approval hierarchy, and local regulatory constraints. Requisitions created under one entity must not be visible to unauthorized users of another entity unless cross-entity visibility permissions are explicitly granted by the system administrator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R005	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Emergency Requisition Fast-Track Processing	The system must provide an emergency requisition workflow for urgent procurement needs such as critical vessel spare parts required to avoid port detention, emergency safety equipment replacement, or time-sensitive container repair materials needed before vessel departure. Emergency requisitions must bypass standard approval tiers and route directly to a designated emergency approver per entity. The system must enforce a mandatory justification field of at least 100 characters, require supporting documentation upload, and automatically flag emergency requisitions for post-facto audit review. Emergency requisitions must be capped at a configurable percentage of the monthly procurement budget per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R006	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Arabic and English Bilingual Requisition Forms	All purchase requisition forms, labels, validation messages, and approval notifications must render correctly in both Arabic and English languages. The Arabic interface must support full right-to-left text rendering including bidirectional text in fields where Arabic and English content coexist, such as item descriptions containing both Arabic names and English part numbers. Date fields must display both Gregorian and Hijri dates simultaneously. Currency amounts must follow locale-specific formatting with Arabic-Indic numerals as an optional display preference. Form field labels must be professionally translated and reviewed, not machine-translated, to ensure maritime and shipping terminology accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R007	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Line Item Detail Capture	Each requisition must support multiple line items with a minimum capacity of 200 lines per requisition. Each line item must capture: item description in Arabic and English, material code from the integrated catalog, UNSPSC commodity code, requested quantity with unit of measure, estimated unit price, preferred currency, required delivery date, delivery port or warehouse location (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, or any configured location), and vessel or equipment assignment if applicable. Line items must support free-text descriptions for non-cataloged items with a mandatory field for the suggested vendor or brand specification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R008	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Consolidation and Grouping	The system must provide an AI-driven requisition consolidation engine that identifies similar or identical requisition line items across multiple requisitions within a configurable time window, defaulting to seven calendar days. The consolidation engine must group line items by commodity code, delivery location, and required delivery date to enable bulk sourcing and volume discount negotiation. Consolidated groups must be presented to the procurement team with the estimated savings from bulk purchasing. Users must be able to accept or reject consolidation suggestions. The system must never auto-consolidate requisitions across different legal entities without explicit procurement manager approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R009	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Offline Requisition Drafting and Sync	The system must support offline requisition creation for users operating in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, such as vessel crew members at sea or port staff in areas with unreliable network coverage. Offline-drafted requisitions must be stored locally on the user's device in an encrypted format and automatically synchronized with the central server when connectivity is restored. The sync process must handle conflict resolution when the same budget line has been consumed by other requisitions during the offline period, alerting the user to budget shortfalls and requiring re-confirmation before final submission to the approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R010	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Status Tracking and Notifications	The system must provide real-time requisition status tracking through the following states: Draft, Submitted, Under Approval, Approved, Rejected, Partially Sourced, Fully Sourced, Cancelled, and On Hold. Each status transition must trigger configurable notifications via email, SMS, and in-app push notifications to the requisitioner and relevant stakeholders. Notifications must be delivered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all status changes with timestamps, user identities, and comments. A dashboard must display requisition aging metrics with alerts for requisitions pending approval beyond configurable SLA thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R011	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Recurring Requisition Templates	The system must allow procurement users to create and manage recurring requisition templates for regularly purchased items such as monthly vessel provisions, quarterly safety equipment inspections supplies, or annual container maintenance materials. Templates must store all line item details, preferred vendors, delivery schedules, and budget allocations. Recurring templates must support configurable generation frequencies including weekly, bi-weekly, monthly, quarterly, and custom cron-based schedules aligned to both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. Auto-generated requisitions from templates must enter the standard approval workflow and must be distinguishable from manually created requisitions in all reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R012	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Attachment and Document Management	Each purchase requisition must support attachment of supporting documents including technical specifications, equipment photographs, safety data sheets, previous quotations, and regulatory compliance certificates. The system must accept file formats including PDF, DOCX, XLSX, JPG, PNG, and DWG with a maximum individual file size of 50 MB and a total attachment limit of 200 MB per requisition. All attachments must be virus-scanned before storage. Attachments must be versioned so that updates to specifications during the approval process are tracked. The system must support optical character recognition on uploaded documents to extract key data points for auto-population of requisition fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R013	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Cross-Entity Requisition Visibility and Transfer	Authorized procurement managers must be able to view requisitions across all entities for strategic sourcing purposes. The system must support requisition transfer between entities when a procurement need can be better fulfilled by a different entity's local vendor network. For example, a spare part requisitioned by the Qatar entity at Hamad Port may be more cost-effectively sourced by the UAE entity at Jebel Ali due to vendor proximity. Transferred requisitions must retain the original cost center allocation, with an inter-company settlement record created automatically. Transfer must require approval from both the originating and receiving entity procurement managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R014	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	AI Demand Forecasting for Proactive Requisitions	The system must include an AI-based demand forecasting module that analyzes historical purchase requisition data, vessel maintenance schedules, container fleet age profiles, seasonal port throughput patterns, and upcoming voyage plans to predict future procurement needs. The AI engine must generate proactive requisition suggestions at least 30 days before the anticipated need date. Suggestions must include recommended quantities based on consumption trends, preferred vendors based on past performance scores, and estimated costs in the relevant entity currency. Procurement staff must be able to convert AI suggestions into formal requisitions with a single action, pre-populated with all forecasted details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R015	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Analytics and Reporting Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive requisition analytics dashboard displaying key performance indicators including: average requisition-to-PO cycle time per entity, requisition approval rate, rejection reasons distribution, top requested commodity categories, budget utilization percentage by cost center, emergency requisition frequency, and AI classification accuracy metrics. All reports must be filterable by entity, port, department, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), currency, and requisition category. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with Arabic and English column headers. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary metrics to individual requisition records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-001-R016	FEAT-038-1-001	MOD-038	Requisition Integration with Inventory Module	Before allowing requisition submission, the system must automatically check available inventory levels in the integrated warehouse management module across all entity warehouses and port stores at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. If sufficient stock exists at the requesting location, the system must suggest an inventory transfer instead of a new purchase. If stock is available at a different entity location, the system must present an inter-entity stock transfer option with estimated logistics cost and delivery timeline. Only when no existing inventory can fulfill the requirement should the requisition proceed to the procurement sourcing workflow. This check must complete within three seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R001	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Master Database with Shipping Specialization	The system must maintain a comprehensive vendor master database supporting categorization by maritime and shipping specializations including vessel spare parts suppliers, bunker fuel providers, container repair workshops, port equipment manufacturers, marine paint and coating suppliers, safety equipment vendors, provisions and catering suppliers, and general industrial suppliers. Each vendor record must store: legal entity name in Arabic and English, trade license number, commercial registration number, VAT or GST registration number per jurisdiction (Qatar QID, UAE TRN, KSA VAT, India GSTIN), bank details for multi-currency payments, port delivery capabilities, and compliance certification expiry dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R002	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	AI-Driven Vendor Recommendation Engine	When a purchase requisition is approved and ready for sourcing, the system must automatically recommend the top five vendors using an AI scoring algorithm that weighs the following factors: historical delivery performance score (on-time delivery rate), quality rating from past inspections, price competitiveness based on historical quotes, geographic proximity to the delivery port, current compliance and certification status, credit terms offered, and capacity to fulfill the requested quantity within the required timeline. The recommendation must be recalculated in real-time and must display a confidence score for each vendor. Procurement users must be able to override AI recommendations with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R003	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	RFQ Creation and Distribution	The system must enable procurement users to create Request for Quotation documents from approved purchase requisitions with automatic population of item descriptions, quantities, technical specifications, required delivery dates, and delivery port locations. RFQs must be distributable to selected vendors via email, vendor portal invitation, and EDI integration. Each RFQ must include configurable terms such as INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, and DAP as commonly used in container shipping), payment terms, warranty requirements, and inspection conditions. RFQs must support bilingual content in Arabic and English simultaneously, with the vendor receiving the document in their preferred language as configured in the vendor master.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R004	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Portal for Bid Submission	The system must provide a secure web-based vendor portal where invited vendors can view RFQ details, download technical specifications and attachments, submit their quotations with line-item pricing, upload supporting documents such as product catalogs and compliance certificates, and ask clarification questions. The vendor portal must support Arabic and English interfaces with RTL layout for Arabic users. Vendors must be able to submit partial bids covering only some line items of a multi-line RFQ. The portal must enforce the RFQ submission deadline with automatic closure, preventing late submissions. All vendor portal communications must be encrypted and must maintain a complete audit trail of vendor interactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R005	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Sealed Bid Management and Opening Ceremony	For high-value procurements exceeding a configurable threshold per entity (e.g., 1,000,000 QAR for Qatar entity), the system must support sealed bid functionality where submitted vendor quotations are encrypted and inaccessible to all users until a formal bid opening event. The bid opening must require simultaneous authentication by at least two authorized personnel using multi-factor authentication. The system must record the bid opening timestamp, the identities of the opening committee members, and generate a bid opening report listing all received bids with their total values. This process must comply with Qatar Government Procurement Regulatory Authority guidelines and equivalent KSA and UAE public procurement regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R006	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Multi-Currency Bid Comparison and Normalization	The system must normalize all received vendor quotations to a common comparison currency, configurable per RFQ but defaulting to USD for international marine procurement. Currency conversion must use the exchange rate as of the RFQ closing date sourced from the integrated treasury module or central bank reference rates for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The bid comparison matrix must display each vendor's pricing in both their quoted currency and the normalized comparison currency. Total cost of ownership calculations must include freight charges, customs duties based on the applicable INCOTERMS, insurance, and estimated clearing charges at the destination port. The system must highlight the lowest total cost bid and flag any bid that deviates more than 20% from the average.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R007	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	AI-Powered Bid Evaluation Scoring	The system must provide an AI-powered bid evaluation engine that scores vendor quotations across configurable weighted criteria including price competitiveness (default 40% weight), technical compliance (default 25%), delivery timeline adherence (default 15%), vendor historical performance (default 10%), and commercial terms favorability (default 10%). The AI engine must analyze uploaded technical documents from vendors to assess compliance with specifications and automatically flag non-compliant bids. Evaluation results must be presented as a ranked scorecard with detailed breakdowns per criterion. The procurement committee must be able to adjust weights before final scoring and must provide written justification if the award is made to a vendor other than the highest-scored bidder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R008	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Prequalification and Compliance Checks	Before any vendor can participate in an RFQ, the system must verify their prequalification status by checking trade license validity, ISO certification status (ISO 9001, ISO 14001, ISO 45001 as applicable for maritime suppliers), insurance coverage adequacy, financial stability assessment based on uploaded financial statements, and sanctions screening against OFAC, EU, and UN consolidated sanctions lists. For vendors supplying to vessels, the system must additionally verify RINA, Lloyd's, Bureau Veritas, or DNV classification society approvals. Expired or missing certifications must automatically disqualify the vendor from new RFQs while sending a notification to both the vendor and the procurement team with the specific compliance gap identified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R009	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Reverse Auction Capability	The system must support online reverse auction events for commodity procurements such as bunker fuel, marine lubricants, container paint, and standard consumables where multiple qualified vendors compete by submitting progressively lower bids in real-time. The auction module must enforce configurable parameters including auction duration (minimum 30 minutes), bid decrement rules (minimum reduction per bid), automatic time extensions when bids are received in the final minutes, and reserve price below which the auction result requires additional management approval. The auction interface must display anonymized bid rankings to participating vendors without revealing competitor identities or exact bid amounts. All auction events must be recorded with complete bid histories for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R010	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Performance Scorecard Integration	The system must maintain a continuously updated vendor performance scorecard that feeds into the sourcing recommendation engine. The scorecard must track metrics including: on-time delivery percentage calculated against the committed delivery date at the destination port, quality acceptance rate based on goods receipt inspection results, invoice accuracy rate, responsiveness measured as average time to respond to RFQs, claims and warranty resolution timeliness, and compliance documentation currency. Scores must be calculated on a rolling 12-month basis and must be segmented by entity, port, and commodity category. Vendors scoring below a configurable threshold must be automatically flagged for review and temporarily suspended from new RFQ invitations pending procurement manager evaluation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R011	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Single Source Justification Workflow	When procurement requires a single-source or sole-source purchase bypassing the competitive RFQ process, the system must enforce a formal justification workflow. Valid justification categories must include: OEM-exclusive spare parts for specific vessel equipment, proprietary technology with no alternative suppliers, emergency procurement under critical time constraints, and existing contract coverage. The justification must require supporting documentation, market research evidence of supplier exclusivity, and approval by a senior manager one level above the standard approval authority. The system must maintain a register of all single-source purchases with quarterly reporting to the procurement compliance committee, tracking single-source spend as a percentage of total procurement volume per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R012	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	RFQ Template Library and Reuse	The system must provide a searchable library of RFQ templates categorized by procurement type, commodity group, and entity. Templates must store standard terms and conditions, evaluation criteria weights, required vendor certifications, INCOTERMS defaults, and payment term structures commonly used in container shipping procurement. Users must be able to create new RFQs from templates with pre-populated fields, modifying only the specific requirements for the current sourcing need. Templates must support versioning with change tracking so that updates to standard terms are propagated to all future RFQs while preserving the original terms on previously issued RFQs. Each entity must be able to maintain entity-specific template variations reflecting local regulatory and business requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R013	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Geographical Vendor Mapping for Port Proximity	The system must maintain a geographical mapping of vendors in relation to major port locations (Hamad Port Doha, Khalifa Port Abu Dhabi, Jebel Ali Dubai, King Abdullah Port Jeddah, JNPT Nhava Sheva Mumbai) to enable proximity-based sourcing decisions. The mapping must calculate estimated delivery times based on vendor warehouse locations and standard logistics routes to each port. When sourcing for urgent vessel requirements with tight turnaround times, the system must prioritize vendors within a configurable radius of the delivery port. The AI recommendation engine must factor in port-specific delivery constraints such as customs clearance timelines, port gate operating hours, and free zone regulations at Jebel Ali Free Zone and Hamad Port Free Zone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R014	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Communication Audit Trail	All communications between procurement users and vendors during the sourcing process must be logged in an immutable audit trail. This includes RFQ invitations sent, clarification questions and responses, bid submission timestamps, negotiation emails and portal messages, and award or regret notifications. The audit trail must capture the communication channel (email, vendor portal, EDI), sender and recipient identities, timestamp in UTC with local time zone conversion, and the full content or attachment references. The audit trail must be searchable by vendor name, RFQ number, date range, and communication type. This data must be retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Decree Law on Commercial Transactions, and India Companies Act record retention requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R015	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Sourcing Event Analytics and Savings Tracking	The system must track and report procurement savings achieved through the competitive sourcing process. Savings must be calculated as the difference between the initial estimated cost on the purchase requisition and the final awarded price, broken down by negotiation savings, volume consolidation savings, and competitive bidding savings. Analytics must include: average number of bids received per RFQ, RFQ cycle time from issuance to award, vendor participation rates, and sourcing strategy distribution across competitive bid, single source, reverse auction, and framework contract calloff. All analytics must be segmented by entity, port, commodity category, and time period with support for both Gregorian and Hijri calendar reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R016	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	Vendor Diversity and Local Content Reporting	The system must track vendor diversity metrics to support local content requirements in Qatar (Qatar National Vision 2030 Tawteen program), KSA (IKTVA local content program), UAE (National In-Country Value program), and India (Make in India initiative). Each vendor must be tagged with ownership nationality, SME classification status, and local content certification. The system must generate reports showing procurement spend distribution by vendor nationality, local versus international sourcing ratios, and SME participation rates per entity. When sourcing decisions are made, the system must display the local content impact of each vendor selection to help procurement managers meet entity-specific local content targets established by government mandates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-002-R017	FEAT-038-1-002	MOD-038	RFQ Amendment and Addendum Management	The system must support formal amendment of issued RFQs when requirements change before the submission deadline. Amendments must be versioned, tracked, and automatically distributed to all invited vendors with clear highlighting of the changed sections. Vendors who have already submitted bids must be notified of amendments and given the option to revise their submissions. The system must extend the RFQ deadline by a configurable minimum period (default 48 hours) whenever a material amendment is issued. The amendment history must be preserved in the RFQ record showing the original content, each amendment version, the reason for the amendment, and the approver identity. All vendors must acknowledge receipt of amendments before their bids can be considered valid.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R001	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Automated PO Generation from Sourcing Awards	Upon completion of the vendor sourcing and award process, the system must automatically generate a purchase order pre-populated with all awarded line items, negotiated pricing, selected INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, or DAP), payment terms, delivery schedule, and delivery port location. The auto-generated PO must inherit the originating purchase requisition reference, budget allocation, cost center coding, and vessel or equipment assignment. The PO must be created under the correct legal entity with entity-specific terms and conditions, tax treatment (Qatar zero-rate VAT, UAE 5% VAT, KSA 15% VAT, India GST at applicable slab rates), and sequential PO numbering following entity-specific format conventions. Users must be able to review and modify the auto-generated PO before submitting for approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R002	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Multi-Currency PO with Exchange Rate Locking	Purchase orders must support creation in any of the operational currencies: USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. When a PO is issued in a currency different from the entity's base currency, the system must record the exchange rate at PO creation and provide an option to lock the rate for budget commitment purposes. The locked exchange rate must be used for all budget consumption calculations until the PO is invoiced. At invoice time, the system must support both the locked rate and the actual payment rate, recording any foreign exchange gain or loss. The PO must display amounts in both the transaction currency and the entity's base reporting currency on all printed and digital documents. Currency conversion must follow the exchange rate source hierarchy configured in the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R003	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Approval Workflow with Delegation	The system must enforce a configurable approval workflow for purchase orders separate from the requisition approval workflow. PO approval tiers must be based on PO total value, procurement category, entity, and vendor risk classification. The workflow must support parallel approval paths where both procurement and finance approvers must approve simultaneously for POs above a configurable threshold. Approval delegation must be time-bound with automatic expiry and must log the delegation chain for audit purposes. Approvers must receive notification in their preferred language (Arabic or English) with a PO summary including total value, vendor name, key line items, and delivery schedule. Mobile approval must be supported through the responsive web interface and native mobile push notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R004	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Dispatch and Vendor Acknowledgment Tracking	Upon approval, the system must dispatch the purchase order to the vendor via the configured communication channel: email with PDF attachment, vendor portal notification, or EDI transmission. The PO document must be generated in the vendor's preferred language (Arabic or English) with entity letterhead, authorized signatory details, and all applicable legal terms. The system must track vendor acknowledgment of the PO with a configurable acknowledgment deadline defaulting to 48 hours. If the vendor does not acknowledge within the deadline, the system must escalate to the procurement manager and send a reminder to the vendor. Vendor acknowledgment must be recorded with a timestamp and the acknowledging representative's name. The PO must not be considered active for goods receipt purposes until vendor acknowledgment is recorded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R005	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Blanket and Framework Purchase Orders	The system must support blanket purchase orders and framework agreements that establish pricing, terms, and conditions for repeated purchases over a defined period (typically 6 to 24 months). Blanket POs must define maximum quantity limits and maximum value limits per line item and in total. Individual release orders must be created against the blanket PO with automatic deduction from the available balance. The system must alert procurement users when blanket PO utilization reaches configurable thresholds (e.g., 75% and 90% of quantity or value). Blanket POs must support price escalation clauses linked to commodity indices for items like bunker fuel and marine lubricants. Expiring blanket POs must trigger renewal notifications 60 days before expiry with a comparison of actual consumption versus forecasted consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R006	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Multi-Port Delivery Schedule Management	Each purchase order must support multiple delivery schedules across different port locations for the same line item. For example, a PO for container lashing rods may specify delivery of 500 units to Jebel Ali by March 15, 300 units to Hamad Port by March 22, and 200 units to JNPT by April 1. Each delivery schedule line must capture the destination port code, warehouse or berth location within the port, required delivery date, receiving contact person, and any port-specific delivery instructions such as gate pass requirements or restricted delivery hours. The system must track delivery performance against each schedule line independently and calculate on-time delivery percentage per port location for vendor performance scoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R007	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Amendment and Change Order Management	The system must support formal PO amendments through a change order process. Amendments may include quantity changes, price adjustments, delivery date modifications, addition of new line items, or changes to delivery locations. Each amendment must create a new PO version with a sequential change order number while preserving the full version history. Amendments exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., value increase greater than 10% or delivery delay beyond 14 days) must require re-approval through the PO approval workflow. The vendor must receive an amended PO document clearly highlighting all changes from the previous version. The system must track the cumulative impact of all amendments on budget consumption and alert when amendments cause budget overruns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R008	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Goods Receipt and Three-Way Matching	The system must support goods receipt recording against purchase orders with mandatory three-way matching between the PO, goods receipt note, and vendor invoice before payment authorization. Goods receipt must capture received quantity, condition assessment (accepted, rejected, or conditionally accepted), receiving warehouse or port location, vessel assignment if applicable, and receiving inspector identity. Partial receipts must be supported with automatic tracking of remaining open quantities. The three-way match must validate that invoice quantities do not exceed received quantities, invoice unit prices do not exceed PO prices beyond a configurable tolerance (default 2%), and the total invoice amount does not exceed the PO value. Mismatches must be flagged for procurement and finance review with clear identification of the specific discrepancy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R009	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Cancellation and Closure Procedures	The system must support PO cancellation at any stage in the lifecycle with appropriate controls. Cancellation of a PO that has already been acknowledged by the vendor must require a cancellation reason, procurement manager approval, and must trigger an automatic cancellation notification to the vendor. For partially received POs, the system must support line-level closure where individual line items can be closed while the PO remains open for other items. Automatic PO closure must occur when all line items are fully received, inspected, and invoice-matched. The system must also support force-closure of stale POs that have been open beyond a configurable period (default 180 days) with no activity, subject to finance approval. Cancelled or closed PO amounts must automatically release the corresponding budget commitment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R010	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Tax Calculation and Compliance per Jurisdiction	The system must automatically calculate applicable taxes on purchase orders based on the entity jurisdiction, vendor location, goods or service category, and applicable trade agreements. For Qatar entities, the system must apply zero-rate or exempt VAT treatment per Qatar Tax Authority guidelines. For UAE entities, standard 5% VAT must be applied with support for reverse charge mechanism on imported goods. For KSA entities, 15% VAT must be calculated with proper treatment of port-related services. For India entities, GST must be calculated at the applicable slab rate (5%, 12%, 18%, or 28%) with correct CGST, SGST, and IGST bifurcation based on the supply type (intra-state or inter-state). All tax calculations must generate the data required for entity-specific tax return filing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R011	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Print and Digital Document Generation	The system must generate purchase order documents in both printable PDF and structured digital formats (PDF/A for archival, XML for EDI integration). PO documents must include the entity legal header with commercial registration details, PO number and version, vendor address in both Arabic and English, line item details with item codes and descriptions, pricing with tax breakdowns, INCOTERMS, payment terms, delivery schedule, and applicable terms and conditions. The document must support entity-specific branding with configurable logos, color schemes, and authorized signatory blocks. Digital signatures conforming to Qatar Digital Government standards and UAE PASS integration must be supported. QR codes for document verification must be embedded in all generated PO documents linking to the PO record in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R012	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Expediting and Delivery Tracking	The system must provide automated PO expediting capabilities that monitor delivery dates and proactively alert procurement users to potential delays. Expediting notifications must be sent to vendors at configurable intervals before the delivery due date (e.g., 14 days, 7 days, and 3 days before). Vendors must respond with delivery confirmation or revised delivery date through the vendor portal. The system must integrate with shipping line tracking APIs and logistics provider systems to automatically update expected delivery dates for goods in transit. For vessel spare parts and critical equipment, the system must support priority expediting workflows with escalation to senior management when delivery delays threaten vessel operations or port schedules. An expediting dashboard must show all open POs with color-coded delivery status indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R013	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Inter-Company Purchase Order Processing	The system must support inter-company purchase orders where one entity procures goods or services from another entity within the organization. For example, the Qatar entity at Hamad Port may issue a PO to the UAE entity at Jebel Ali for container spare parts held in the UAE warehouse. Inter-company POs must automatically generate corresponding inter-company sales orders and invoices in the supplying entity. Transfer pricing must be applied according to configurable rules compliant with OECD transfer pricing guidelines and applicable bilateral tax treaties between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Inter-company settlement must flow through designated inter-company receivable and payable accounts with automatic reconciliation. Currency conversion for cross-entity transactions must use the group-approved transfer exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R014	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Budget Impact and Commitment Accounting	Upon PO approval, the system must create a budget commitment (encumbrance) entry in the financial system equal to the PO value in the entity's base currency. The commitment must reduce the available budget in the associated cost center, preventing over-commitment of funds. As goods are received and invoices are matched, the commitment must be progressively relieved and converted to actual expenditure entries. The system must maintain a real-time budget position showing original budget, total commitments from open POs, actual expenditure from invoiced POs, and available balance. Budget impact reports must be available at the cost center, department, entity, and consolidated group levels. PO amendments that increase the committed value must trigger a re-validation against the available budget before amendment approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R015	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Vendor Return and Debit Note Processing	The system must support goods return processing when received items are defective, damaged, or do not match the purchase order specifications. Return requests must be initiated from the goods receipt record with mandatory documentation of the return reason, inspection findings, and photographic evidence. Approved returns must generate a debit note to the vendor in the same currency as the original PO with correct tax treatment reversal. The system must track return shipment logistics and update the PO received quantities upon vendor confirmation of return receipt. For partial returns, the PO line item must reflect the net received quantity. Return metrics must feed into the vendor performance scorecard, and vendors with return rates exceeding a configurable threshold per commodity category must be automatically flagged for vendor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R016	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	PO Lifecycle Analytics and KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive PO lifecycle analytics dashboard displaying key metrics including: PO creation to approval average cycle time, PO to delivery lead time by vendor and commodity, on-time delivery percentage by port location, three-way match success rate, PO amendment frequency and value impact, average cost variance between PO and invoice amounts, open PO aging distribution, and PO closure rate. Analytics must support comparison across entities, time periods (Gregorian and Hijri calendars), commodity categories, and vendor segments. Trend analysis must highlight improving or deteriorating metrics with AI-generated insights recommending procurement process improvements. The dashboard must support scheduled email distribution of summary reports to procurement leadership in both Arabic and English formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-003-R017	FEAT-038-1-003	MOD-038	Duplicate PO Detection and Prevention	The system must implement AI-driven duplicate purchase order detection that identifies potential duplicates before PO creation. Detection criteria must include matching vendor, similar item descriptions using natural language processing, overlapping delivery dates, and matching requisition references. When a potential duplicate is detected, the system must alert the user with a comparison view showing the existing PO details alongside the proposed PO, highlighting the similarities. The user must explicitly acknowledge the duplicate warning and provide justification before proceeding with PO creation. The duplicate detection engine must also identify split-purchase patterns where a procurement need is artificially divided into multiple smaller POs to circumvent approval thresholds, alerting the compliance team when such patterns are detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R001	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Repository with Metadata Classification	The system must provide a centralized contract repository storing all procurement contracts across all legal entities. Each contract record must capture comprehensive metadata including: contract type (master service agreement, supply agreement, annual maintenance contract, rate contract, charter party supplement), contracting entity, vendor details, contract value and currency, effective and expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, auto-renewal flag, notice period for termination, INCOTERMS applicable, payment terms, and performance bond or bank guarantee details. The repository must support full-text search across contract documents in both Arabic and English. Contracts must be classified by commodity category, risk level, and strategic importance for portfolio management purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R002	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	AI-Powered Contract Drafting Assistance	The system must provide an AI-powered contract drafting assistant that generates initial contract drafts based on the procurement category, vendor profile, entity jurisdiction, and selected template. The AI must populate standard clauses appropriate for the contract type and jurisdiction, including force majeure provisions referencing maritime-specific scenarios (port closures, canal blockages, piracy zones), limitation of liability clauses calibrated to the contract value, intellectual property provisions for technology procurement, and dispute resolution clauses specifying the appropriate arbitral institution (Qatar International Court, DIAC for Dubai, SCCA for KSA, or Mumbai High Court for India). The AI-drafted contract must highlight sections requiring human review and approval, particularly commercial terms, pricing schedules, and entity-specific regulatory compliance provisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R003	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Approval and Execution Workflow	The system must enforce a multi-stage contract approval workflow comprising legal review, commercial review, finance review, and final authority sign-off. Each review stage must support redline markup with tracked changes visible to all reviewers. The approval routing must be configurable based on contract value, type, entity, and risk classification. Contracts above configurable thresholds must require board-level or committee approval with supporting documentation package generation. Upon final approval, the system must support digital execution via electronic signature integration compliant with Qatar Electronic Commerce and Transactions Law, UAE Federal Decree Law No. 46/2021 on Electronic Transactions, KSA Electronic Transactions Law, and India Information Technology Act 2000. Executed contracts must be automatically watermarked and archived with tamper-evident sealing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R004	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Obligation and Milestone Tracking	The system must parse executed contracts to identify and track all obligations, milestones, and deliverables for both the organization and the vendor. Obligations must be categorized as financial obligations (payment schedules, performance bond renewals), operational obligations (delivery milestones, service level commitments), compliance obligations (certification renewals, insurance maintenance), and reporting obligations (periodic performance reports, audit access). Each obligation must have a responsible party, due date, notification schedule, and escalation path. The AI engine must automatically extract obligations from contract text with at least 90% accuracy, presenting extracted obligations for human verification. Overdue obligations must trigger escalation notifications to the contract manager and their supervisor with the number of days past due and potential contractual penalties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R005	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Financial Tracking and Budget Linkage	The system must maintain a real-time financial view of each contract showing the total contract value, cumulative spend to date from linked purchase orders and invoices, remaining uncommitted value, and projected spend based on historical utilization rates. Financial tracking must support multi-currency contracts where different deliverables may be priced in different currencies, with consolidated reporting in the entity's base currency. The system must automatically link purchase orders to their parent contracts and validate that PO values do not exceed contract limits. When contract utilization reaches configurable thresholds (e.g., 80% of value or 75% of term), the system must alert the contract manager to initiate renewal or re-procurement planning. Budget impact must flow through to the cost center and departmental budget reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R006	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Renewal and Expiry Management	The system must provide proactive contract renewal management with configurable notification cascades starting at 180, 120, 90, 60, and 30 days before contract expiry. Renewal notifications must include a contract performance summary, total spend analysis, vendor scorecard data, and market benchmark comparison. The system must support three renewal paths: automatic renewal for contracts with auto-renewal clauses (with opt-out capability before the notice period deadline), negotiated renewal with a streamlined re-negotiation workflow, and re-procurement trigger that initiates a new sourcing event. For critical contracts such as bunker fuel supply agreements, port handling equipment maintenance, or container leasing arrangements, the system must flag renewal with high-priority status and assign to senior procurement management. Expired contracts must be automatically locked to prevent new PO creation against them.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R007	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Service Level Agreement Monitoring	For service contracts, the system must track vendor compliance against defined service level agreements including response time SLAs, resolution time SLAs, availability percentages, and quality benchmarks specific to container shipping operations such as container repair turnaround time, equipment uptime percentages, and vessel provisioning delivery accuracy. SLA measurements must be automatically calculated from operational data captured in the goods receipt, service confirmation, and incident management modules. The system must generate monthly SLA compliance reports per contract with red, amber, and green status indicators. SLA breaches must trigger automatic penalty calculations as defined in the contract terms, generate debit notes for financial penalties, and update the vendor performance scorecard. Recurring SLA breaches must escalate to the contract manager for formal vendor performance review initiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R008	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Risk Assessment and Monitoring	The system must provide a contract risk assessment framework that evaluates each contract across financial risk (vendor financial stability, currency exposure, price volatility), operational risk (single-source dependency, delivery reliability, capacity constraints), compliance risk (regulatory changes, certification expiry, sanctions exposure), and strategic risk (market shifts, technology obsolescence). Risk assessments must be performed at contract creation and updated quarterly using AI analysis of vendor performance data, market intelligence, and news monitoring. High-risk contracts must be flagged on the procurement dashboard with recommended mitigation actions. The system must monitor external risk indicators including vendor credit rating changes, country risk index updates for supplier locations, and commodity price volatility indices relevant to container shipping procurement categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R009	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Amendment and Variation Management	The system must support formal contract amendments through a structured variation order process. Each amendment must capture the amendment scope, commercial impact (value increase or decrease), revised terms, effective date, and business justification. Amendments must be linked to the parent contract with full version history maintaining the original contract and all subsequent amendments as a complete chain. The amendment approval workflow must mirror the original contract approval workflow for amendments exceeding configurable thresholds. Minor amendments below the threshold may follow a simplified approval path. All parties must execute amendments with the same digital signature formality as the original contract. The system must calculate the cumulative amendment impact on total contract value, timeline, and scope, alerting management when cumulative amendments exceed a configurable percentage of the original contract value.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R010	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Compliance with Local Regulations	The system must enforce jurisdiction-specific contract compliance requirements for each entity. For Qatar, contracts must comply with Qatar Civil Code provisions on commercial contracts, Qatar Financial Centre regulations where applicable, and Qatar Energy sector requirements for fuel-related procurement. For UAE, contracts must comply with UAE Civil Transactions Law, DMCC or JAFZA free zone regulations for Jebel Ali-based operations, and UAE Federal procurement guidelines. For KSA, contracts must comply with Saudi Government Tenders and Procurement Law and its implementing regulations. For India, contracts must comply with Indian Contract Act 1872, GST invoicing requirements, and JNPT-specific port trust regulations. The system must maintain a configurable compliance checklist per jurisdiction that must be satisfied before contract execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R011	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Performance Bond and Guarantee Management	The system must track all performance bonds, bank guarantees, letters of credit, and insurance policies associated with procurement contracts. Each guarantee instrument must capture the issuing bank or institution, instrument number, value, currency, issue date, expiry date, beneficiary entity, and conditions for invocation. The system must send renewal notifications at configurable intervals before guarantee expiry (default 60 and 30 days). For performance bonds linked to active contracts, the system must prevent bond release until all contract obligations are confirmed as fulfilled by the contract manager. The system must support guarantee value reduction aligned with contract milestone completion. A guarantee register must provide a consolidated view across all entities showing total guarantee exposure by vendor, currency, and entity with alerts for guarantees expiring within the next 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R003	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Multi-Currency Reconciliation with Tolerance Thresholds	The system must reconcile lessor statement amounts denominated in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR against internal accruals computed in the entity's functional currency. Configurable tolerance thresholds must be supported at both absolute amount (e.g., within 5 USD) and percentage (e.g., within 0.5%) levels, settable per lessor, per entity, and per charge type. Exchange rate variance analysis must compare the lessor's applied rate against the system's ECB/Central Bank reference rate on the transaction date. Items within tolerance must be auto-approved while items exceeding tolerance must be escalated with full variance breakdown.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R012	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	AI Contract Intelligence and Clause Analytics	The system must provide AI-powered contract intelligence that analyzes the full contract portfolio to identify patterns, risks, and optimization opportunities. The AI must detect non-standard clauses that deviate from approved templates, identify contracts with unfavorable terms compared to market benchmarks, flag conflicting obligations across multiple contracts with the same vendor, and highlight contracts approaching automatic renewal that may benefit from renegotiation. The AI must generate natural language summaries of complex contracts in both Arabic and English for executive review. Contract clause analytics must enable procurement managers to compare terms across similar contracts to identify best practices and standardization opportunities. The AI must learn from user feedback on its recommendations to continuously improve accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R013	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Termination and Close-Out Process	The system must support a structured contract termination process covering both natural expiry and early termination scenarios. For early termination, the system must enforce notice period requirements as defined in the contract, calculate termination penalties or settlement amounts, and generate formal termination notices in the appropriate language (Arabic or English) for the jurisdiction. The close-out process must verify that all outstanding obligations are resolved: pending deliveries are either completed or cancelled, all invoices are received and processed, retention amounts are released or forfeited per contract terms, and performance bonds are returned or claimed. The system must generate a contract close-out report summarizing total spend, vendor performance over the contract term, lessons learned, and recommendations for future procurement. Closed contracts must be archived but remain searchable for reference purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R014	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Template and Clause Library	The system must maintain a versioned library of approved contract templates and standard clauses organized by contract type, jurisdiction, and procurement category. The clause library must include pre-approved clauses for common contractual provisions such as payment terms, warranty, indemnification, confidentiality, force majeure, dispute resolution, anti-bribery and corruption, data protection, and health safety and environment compliance specific to port and maritime operations. Each clause must have metadata indicating applicable jurisdictions, risk rating, mandatory versus optional status, and last legal review date. Procurement users must be able to assemble contracts by selecting clauses from the library with AI-assisted recommendations for clause selection based on the contract context. Clause modifications outside the pre-approved text must trigger mandatory legal review before contract execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R015	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Multi-Entity Contract Consolidation	The system must support group-level contracts that cover procurement needs across multiple entities, enabling consolidated volume-based pricing while maintaining entity-level order processing and invoicing. A master contract may be signed between the group holding company and a vendor, with entity-specific schedules defining local delivery terms, invoicing currency, tax treatment, and operational contacts. The system must track utilization against the master contract at both the individual entity level and the consolidated group level. Volume tier pricing must be evaluated based on aggregate consumption across all entities. Each entity must be able to raise purchase orders referencing the master contract while maintaining compliance with local entity regulations. Financial reporting must support both entity-level contract spend and consolidated group-level contract analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R016	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive immutable audit trail for every contract lifecycle event including creation, each approval stage, execution, amendments, obligation tracking updates, financial transactions, SLA measurements, risk assessment updates, and termination or close-out. The audit trail must capture the user identity, timestamp in UTC with local time zone display, action performed, and before-and-after values for any data modifications. Compliance reports must be generated for internal audit, external audit, and regulatory submission purposes covering: total contract portfolio value by entity, vendor concentration analysis, contract approval compliance rates, amendment frequency analysis, and expired or unsigned contract identification. All audit data must be retained for a minimum of ten years and must be exportable in formats compatible with major audit management platforms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-1-004-R017	FEAT-038-1-004	MOD-038	Contract Dashboard and Portfolio Overview	The system must provide an executive contract dashboard presenting a portfolio overview across all entities with the following visualizations: total active contract count and value by entity and category, contracts expiring within 30, 60, and 90 days, top ten contracts by value with utilization percentages, vendor concentration heat map showing spend distribution, SLA compliance trend charts, amendment frequency trends, and risk distribution across the contract portfolio. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary visualizations to individual contract records. Filters must include entity, port location, vendor, contract type, value range, date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, risk rating, and commodity category. The dashboard must support personalized views where each user can configure their preferred layout and metrics. Data must refresh in near-real-time with a maximum latency of five minutes from underlying transaction processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R001	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Multi-Warehouse Stock Register with Port-Level Granularity	The system must maintain a centralized stock register that tracks inventory across all warehouse locations at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Each stock item must be associated with a specific warehouse code, storage zone (bonded, free-zone, hazmat-rated, refrigerated), and bin location. The register must support hierarchical location structures allowing drill-down from entity level (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India) to port, warehouse, zone, rack, and bin. Stock quantities must be tracked in both primary and alternate units of measure with configurable conversion factors. The system must handle container repair materials categorized by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, flat-rack, open-top) and ISO container codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R013	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment data drill-down and audit trail	Every segment report line item must support drill-down from the summary level through sub-segments, trade lanes, individual journal entries, and ultimately to the source transaction (bill of lading, invoice, port disbursement). The drill-down must preserve the segment context so users can trace how a specific container's freight revenue at Jebel Ali contributes to the UAE entity's trade lane profitability. Each drill-down level must display the allocation percentage applied if the amount was distributed. The audit trail must capture who generated the report, timestamp, parameters used, and any filters applied. Drill-down must function in offline mode using cached segment data no older than 24 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R002	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Real-Time Stock Valuation in Multi-Currency	The system must compute real-time stock valuation using configurable costing methods including weighted average cost, FIFO, and standard cost per item category. Valuations must be calculated and displayed simultaneously in the local entity currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and in the group reporting currency (USD). Exchange rates must be sourced from the central treasury module with daily rate updates, and the system must support both spot rates and monthly average rates for inventory revaluation. When stock is transferred between entities, the system must handle transfer pricing rules and record any currency conversion gain or loss. The valuation report must support Arabic RTL rendering with proper number formatting for each locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R003	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	AI-Driven Automatic Reorder Point Calculation	The system must use AI/ML models to dynamically calculate reorder points (ROP) and economic order quantities (EOQ) for each stock item at each warehouse location. The AI engine must analyze historical consumption patterns over a minimum 24-month rolling window, seasonal demand fluctuations linked to shipping peak seasons (Q4 pre-holiday surge, Ramadan scheduling impacts, Indian monsoon season disruptions), and supplier lead time variability. The model must factor in port-specific constraints such as customs clearance delays at JNPT which average 3-5 days longer than GCC free-zone ports. Reorder recommendations must be generated as draft purchase requisitions with suggested quantities, preferred suppliers, and estimated landed costs. The system must allow manual override of AI-calculated parameters with audit trail logging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R004	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Stock Movement Tracking and Transaction Ledger	The system must record every stock movement as an immutable ledger entry with transaction types including goods receipt, goods issue, stock transfer between warehouses, stock transfer between entities, stock adjustment (positive/negative), stock write-off, and stock return to supplier. Each transaction must capture the movement date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, the user who initiated the transaction, the approver (for adjustments exceeding configurable thresholds), quantity moved, unit cost, total value in local and USD currencies, and reference document number (PO, work order, vessel maintenance request). The ledger must support full traceability from current stock balance back to the original goods receipt and purchase order.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R005	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Inter-Entity Stock Transfer with Customs Documentation	The system must support stock transfers between entities across different countries (e.g., from Jebel Ali warehouse to Hamad Port warehouse) with automated generation of required customs documentation. For GCC-to-GCC transfers, the system must generate the GCC Unified Customs Declaration form. For transfers involving India (JNPT), the system must generate the Bill of Entry or Shipping Bill as applicable under Indian Customs regulations. Transfer pricing must be calculated based on configurable rules (cost-plus, market rate, or arm's-length pricing) and recorded for transfer pricing compliance. The system must track in-transit inventory as a separate stock status and automatically update warehouse balances upon confirmed receipt at the destination. All documentation must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R006	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Hazardous Materials Inventory Compliance	The system must enforce IMDG Code (International Maritime Dangerous Goods) classification for all hazardous materials stored in port warehouses. Each hazmat item must carry its UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group, and compatibility group. Storage location assignments must validate against warehouse zone hazmat ratings and enforce segregation rules per IMDG Chapter 7.2 (e.g., Class 3 flammable liquids must not be stored adjacent to Class 5.1 oxidizers). The system must maintain maximum allowable quantities per storage zone as defined by port authority regulations at each location. Automated alerts must trigger when hazmat stock levels approach zone capacity limits or when materials approach expiry dates, with notifications sent in both Arabic and English to designated safety officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R007	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Physical Stock Count and Cycle Counting	The system must support both full physical inventory counts and ABC-classified cycle counting programs. For cycle counting, the system must automatically schedule count frequencies based on item classification: A-class items (top 20% by value) counted monthly, B-class items counted quarterly, and C-class items counted semi-annually. Count sheets must be generated with current system quantities hidden to ensure blind counting. The system must support barcode and RFID scanning for count entry via mobile devices operating in potentially low-connectivity warehouse environments with offline data capture and sync capabilities. Variance reports must be generated comparing physical counts to system balances, with automatic adjustment approval workflows based on configurable tolerance thresholds per item category and entity. Variances exceeding thresholds must escalate to finance controllers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R008	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Minimum and Maximum Stock Level Alerts	The system must support configurable minimum stock level, maximum stock level, and safety stock parameters for each item at each warehouse location. When stock falls below the minimum level, the system must generate automated alerts to procurement officers via in-app notification, email, and SMS. When stock approaches the maximum level (configurable threshold, default 90%), the system must warn against additional procurement to prevent overstocking. Safety stock calculations must consider port-specific lead times: Jebel Ali averages 7-day supplier lead time for local items, Hamad Port averages 10 days, and JNPT averages 14-21 days due to customs processing. Alerts must display in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) and include item details, current balance, minimum level, recommended order quantity, and estimated days of stock remaining based on average daily consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R009	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Shelf-Life and Expiry Date Management	The system must track shelf-life and expiry dates for perishable inventory items including safety equipment (fire extinguishers, life rafts, flares), chemicals (paints, cleaning agents, water treatment compounds), provisions, and medical supplies. Batch-level tracking must be enforced for all expiry-managed items with FEFO (First Expired, First Out) picking rules applied automatically during goods issue. The system must generate graduated alerts at configurable intervals before expiry (default: 90, 60, 30, and 7 days). Items within 30 days of expiry must be flagged for accelerated usage or disposal. Expired items must be automatically blocked from issuance and flagged for write-off processing. Disposal records must comply with port authority environmental regulations and maintain full audit trails including disposal method, authorized personnel, and regulatory reference numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R010	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Spare Parts Catalog for Container Maintenance	The system must maintain a comprehensive spare parts catalog specifically designed for container maintenance and repair operations at port depots. The catalog must be organized by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, flat-rack, open-top, tank container) and component category (door assembly, floor panels, roof panels, corner castings, locking mechanisms, refrigeration unit components, electrical systems). Each part must cross-reference OEM part numbers, alternative supplier part numbers, and universal replacement compatibility codes. The catalog must link to standard repair cost estimates per IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) guidelines for damage assessment. Stock availability must show real-time balances across all port depot locations to enable cross-depot fulfillment when local stock is depleted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R011	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Bunker Fuel Inventory Tracking	The system must track bunker fuel inventory (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LNG) at each port's fuel storage facility with quantity tracking in metric tons and cubic meters with temperature-adjusted volume calculations. The system must record each bunkering operation including vessel name, IMO number, quantity delivered, BDN (Bunker Delivery Note) reference, sulphur content percentage for IMO 2020 compliance verification, and sampling details. Stock reconciliation must account for measurement variances between shore tank readings and vessel ullage reports within configurable tolerance limits (default ±0.5%). The system must integrate with port fuel metering systems where available and support manual entry with mandatory dual-witness verification for ports without automated metering. Fuel inventory valuations must track both purchase cost and current market replacement cost to support hedge accounting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R012	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Consignment and Vendor-Managed Inventory	The system must support consignment inventory arrangements where stock physically located in company warehouses remains the property of the supplier until consumed. Consignment stock must be displayed separately from owned stock in all inventory reports and valuations to ensure accurate financial reporting. The system must automatically generate consumption reports to consignment suppliers at configurable intervals (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly) with self-billing invoice generation for consumed quantities. For vendor-managed inventory (VMI) programs, the system must provide suppliers with controlled read-access to real-time stock levels and consumption data through a secure supplier portal. VMI suppliers must be able to submit replenishment proposals that enter the standard purchase order approval workflow. All consignment and VMI transactions must maintain entity-level segregation across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R013	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Inventory Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The system must provide a comprehensive inventory dashboard displaying key metrics including total stock value by entity and consolidated, stock turnover ratio, slow-moving and obsolete inventory value, stockout incidents count and trend, fill rate percentage, and days of supply on hand. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper mirroring of charts, tables, and navigation elements. Numeric values must be formatted according to locale conventions: Arabic numerals or Eastern Arabic-Indic numerals based on user preference, with appropriate thousands separators and decimal markers. Date displays must support simultaneous Gregorian and Hijri dates. All dashboard widgets must support drill-down from summary metrics to underlying transaction details. The dashboard must refresh data at configurable intervals with a maximum latency of 60 seconds from source transaction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R014	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Offline Inventory Operations for Remote Locations	The system must support offline inventory operations for warehouse staff working in areas with limited or no network connectivity, particularly in remote port yard areas and onboard vessels. The mobile application must cache the local warehouse item master, current stock balances, and pending transaction queue for offline use. Users must be able to perform goods receipt, goods issue, stock count, and stock adjustment transactions while offline with full data entry validation against cached master data. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline transactions with the central database using conflict resolution rules: if a stock balance conflict is detected (another user modified the same item), the system must flag the transaction for manual review rather than automatically overwriting. Offline sessions must be limited to a configurable maximum duration (default 72 hours) after which re-synchronization is mandatory.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R015	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Inventory Aging Analysis and Obsolescence Management	The system must generate inventory aging reports categorizing stock by age buckets: 0-90 days, 91-180 days, 181-365 days, and over 365 days since last receipt or last movement. Slow-moving inventory must be automatically identified based on configurable criteria (default: no movement in 180 days) and flagged for review by procurement and warehouse managers. The system must calculate obsolescence provisions following entity-specific accounting policies: for example, 25% provision for items with no movement in 6-12 months, 50% for 12-18 months, and 100% for items exceeding 18 months of inactivity. AI analysis must correlate slow-moving inventory patterns with vessel fleet changes, route modifications, and equipment retirement schedules to proactively identify items at risk of obsolescence. Write-off proposals must generate multi-level approval workflows with configurable approval thresholds per entity and must record the disposal or scrap value for financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-001-R016	FEAT-038-2-001	MOD-038	Multi-Entity Inventory Consolidation and Reporting	The system must produce consolidated inventory reports across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with elimination of inter-entity stock transfers to prevent double-counting. Consolidated reports must present values in the group reporting currency (USD) using period-end exchange rates for balance sheet items and average rates for cost of goods consumed. The system must support entity-level, regional (GCC combined vs India), and group-level reporting views with appropriate access controls ensuring users only see data for their authorized entities. Report outputs must be available in PDF (with Arabic and English formatting), Excel, and CSV formats. The consolidation engine must reconcile inter-entity transfer balances and highlight any unmatched transfer-in/transfer-out pairs for investigation. Regulatory reporting for each jurisdiction must be extractable: Qatar Financial Markets Authority format, UAE Federal Tax Authority format, ZATCA format for KSA, and Indian GST-compliant format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R001	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Purchase Order-Based Goods Receipt Note Creation	The system must enable creation of Goods Receipt Notes (GRN) linked to approved purchase orders. Upon initiating a GRN, the system must display all open PO lines eligible for receipt with outstanding quantities. Users must be able to receive partial quantities against a PO line with the system tracking cumulative receipts and remaining open quantities. Over-receipt must be controlled by configurable tolerance percentages per item category (default: 5% for bulk materials, 0% for serialized equipment). The GRN must capture delivery note reference, transporter details, vehicle/vessel identification, receipt date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and receiving warehouse location down to bin level. For items delivered under INCOTERMS DAP or DDP, the system must automatically flag if delivery documentation includes proof of customs clearance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R002	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Three-Way Matching Engine for Invoice Verification	The system must perform automated three-way matching between the purchase order, goods receipt note, and supplier invoice to validate quantities and prices before payment authorization. Quantity matching must compare PO ordered quantity, GRN received quantity, and invoice billed quantity with configurable tolerance thresholds (default: quantity ±2%, price ±1%). The matching engine must handle partial receipts and partial invoices by maintaining cumulative match status at the PO line level. Currency conversion must be applied when PO currency differs from invoice currency using the exchange rate applicable on the invoice date. Matched invoices must be automatically routed for payment processing. Mismatched invoices must generate exception reports with specific mismatch details (quantity variance, price variance, UOM mismatch) and route to the designated procurement officer for resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R003	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Quality Inspection Workflow for Maritime Safety Equipment	The system must enforce mandatory quality inspection workflows for configurable item categories, with maritime safety equipment (life-saving appliances, fire protection equipment, navigation instruments) requiring 100% inspection per SOLAS (Safety of Life at Sea) Chapter III and IV requirements. Upon goods receipt of inspection-required items, the system must automatically create a Quality Inspection (QI) order and place the received stock in a blocked 'quality hold' status preventing issuance until inspection is completed. The QI order must present item-specific inspection checklists with pass/fail criteria, measurement fields for dimensional and performance parameters, and mandatory photograph upload capability. Inspection results must be recorded by qualified inspectors whose certifications are validated against the personnel master. Failed inspections must trigger supplier notification and initiate the return-to-supplier or rework workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R004	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Sampling-Based Quality Inspection Plans	The system must support configurable sampling plans for quality inspection based on AQL (Acceptable Quality Level) standards per ISO 2859-1. Sampling plans must define sample size relative to lot size, acceptance number, and rejection number for each inspection characteristic. The system must support normal, tightened, and reduced inspection levels with automatic switching rules: two consecutive lot rejections trigger switch to tightened inspection; five consecutive lot acceptances under tightened inspection allow switch back to normal; ten consecutive lot acceptances under normal allow switch to reduced inspection. The switching rules must be maintained per supplier per item combination. Lot disposition (accept, reject, or conditional accept with concession) must be recorded with full audit trail. Conditional acceptance must require approval from a designated quality authority and must record the concession conditions and any price adjustments negotiated with the supplier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R005	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Goods Receipt for Import Shipments with Customs Integration	The system must handle goods receipt for imported materials with integration to customs clearance workflows at each port. For goods arriving at JNPT (India), the system must capture the Bill of Entry number, customs duty assessment, IGST (Integrated GST) amount, and customs broker details. For goods arriving at GCC ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali), the system must capture the customs declaration number and any applicable customs duty for non-GCC origin goods. The system must support bonded warehouse receipts where customs duty payment is deferred until goods are released from the bonded zone. Landed cost calculation must automatically aggregate FOB cost, freight charges, insurance, customs duty, port handling charges, and inland transportation to compute the total landed cost per unit in both local currency and USD. INCOTERMS (EXW, FOB, CIF, CFR, DAP, DDP) applicable to the purchase order must drive which cost components are supplier-borne versus buyer-borne.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R006	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Automated Goods Receipt from EDI and Supplier Portal	The system must support automated goods receipt creation through EDI (Electronic Data Interchange) messages, specifically DESADV (Despatch Advice) and RECADV (Receiving Advice) message types per UN/EDIFACT standards. When a supplier transmits an ASN (Advanced Shipping Notice) via EDI or through the supplier portal, the system must auto-create a pending goods receipt pre-populated with shipment details, expected quantities, and tracking references. Warehouse staff must confirm or adjust quantities upon physical receipt. The system must validate ASN data against the corresponding PO and flag discrepancies for review. For suppliers using the web-based supplier portal instead of EDI, the system must provide an ASN entry form with PO lookup, line selection, and quantity specification. The AI engine must learn typical receipt patterns and flag anomalies such as unexpected delivery timing, unusual quantities, or receipts from substitute delivery addresses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R007	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Serialized and Batch-Tracked Item Receipt	The system must enforce serial number capture for items configured as serialized (e.g., container chassis, refrigeration units, high-value spare parts). Each serial number must be unique within the item master and the system must reject duplicate serial numbers during goods receipt entry. For batch-tracked items (chemicals, paints, lubricants, provisions), the system must capture batch number, manufacturing date, expiry date, and supplier certificate of analysis reference. The system must validate that expiry dates meet minimum remaining shelf-life requirements configurable per item (e.g., received goods must have at least 75% of total shelf-life remaining). Batch and serial records must maintain full lifecycle traceability from receipt through storage, issuance, and final consumption or disposal. Certificate of conformance documents must be uploaded and linked to the corresponding batch or serial number for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R008	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Goods Receipt Reversal and Correction	The system must support goods receipt reversal for scenarios including incorrect quantity entry, wrong item received, duplicate receipt posting, and receipt against incorrect purchase order. Reversal must create a compensating stock movement that restores the previous stock balance and reversal of any associated accounting entries. Partial reversal must be supported, allowing correction of specific line items without affecting the entire GRN. The system must enforce time-based reversal restrictions: receipts can be freely reversed within 48 hours of posting; reversals after 48 hours but within the current accounting period require supervisor approval; reversals after period close require finance controller approval and generate adjustment entries in the current period. All reversals must maintain the original GRN record intact with a linked reversal document for audit trail purposes. Reversed quantities must become available again on the originating purchase order.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R009	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Port Gate Integration for Delivery Vehicle Tracking	The system must integrate with port gate management systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to track delivery vehicle entry and exit. When a delivery vehicle is registered at the port gate with a reference to a purchase order, the system must notify the receiving warehouse of the inbound delivery and estimated time of arrival at the warehouse dock. The system must capture vehicle registration number, driver identification, container or truck seal numbers, and gate-in timestamp. Upon goods receipt completion, the system must update the gate system with receipt confirmation to enable vehicle release processing. For deliveries requiring customs inspection at the port gate, the system must track the inspection status and prevent goods receipt processing until customs clearance is confirmed. Average turnaround times from gate-in to gate-out must be tracked for logistics performance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R010	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Return to Supplier and Rejection Processing	The system must manage the complete return-to-supplier workflow for rejected goods. When quality inspection fails or goods are found defective after receipt, the system must generate a Return Material Authorization (RMA) request linked to the original GRN and PO. The RMA must specify rejection reason codes (quality failure, wrong specification, damaged in transit, expired goods, documentation missing), rejected quantity, and requested resolution (replacement, credit note, or refund). The system must block rejected stock from being issued and move it to a designated rejection holding area in the warehouse. For international returns, the system must generate re-export documentation including customs declaration amendments at the originating port. Financial entries must record the return and any credit note received from the supplier. The supplier's quality scorecard must be automatically updated with the rejection incident.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R011	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Free-Issue Material Receipt Handling	The system must support receipt of free-issue materials that are provided by clients or partners without a purchase order, commonly encountered in container shipping for owner-furnished equipment, liner-supplied spare parts, or charterer-provided stores. Free-issue receipts must be processed through a separate document type that does not create financial liability entries but does update physical stock balances. The system must track ownership attribution for all free-issue stock, ensuring these items are segregated in inventory reports from company-owned materials. Consumption of free-issue materials must be reported back to the providing party at configurable intervals. The system must prevent free-issue stock from being included in inventory valuation calculations or in automatic reorder computations. Custody responsibility documentation must be generated upon receipt, signed by both parties, and archived with the receipt record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R012	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Goods Receipt Accounting and Tax Posting	The system must automatically generate accounting entries upon goods receipt posting. For standard-cost items, the system must post the receipt at standard cost and record any purchase price variance to a designated variance account. For moving-average or FIFO items, the receipt must update the item cost based on the actual receipt value. Tax handling must be jurisdiction-specific: UAE VAT at 5% with Input Tax recovery per Federal Tax Authority rules; KSA VAT at 15% per ZATCA regulations; India GST at applicable slab rates (5%, 12%, 18%, 28%) with proper CGST/SGST or IGST classification based on inter-state or intra-state supply; Qatar currently zero-rated but with configurable tax rate for future implementation. Reverse charge mechanism entries must be generated for applicable import transactions. All accounting entries must reference the GRN number, PO number, and supplier details for reconciliation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R013	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Certificate and Document Management for Received Goods	The system must manage all documents associated with goods receipt including supplier delivery notes, packing lists, certificates of origin, certificates of conformance, material safety data sheets (MSDS), test certificates, calibration certificates, and classification society certificates for marine equipment. Each document type must have configurable mandatory/optional status per item category: for example, MSDS must be mandatory for all chemical receipts, and classification society certificates (Lloyd's, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ABS) must be mandatory for critical marine equipment. The system must validate document completeness before allowing goods receipt to be finalized. Documents must be stored with metadata including document type, issue date, expiry date, issuing authority, and document language. Expired certificates must trigger alerts for renewal before the associated stock can be issued. Document retrieval must support full-text search across uploaded PDFs and images using OCR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R014	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Goods Receipt Notification and Workflow Automation	The system must send automated notifications at key stages of the goods receipt process. Upon GRN creation, the originating purchase requisitioner must be notified that their requested materials have arrived. The quality inspection team must be notified when inspection-required items are received and placed on quality hold. Warehouse managers must receive daily summary reports of all goods received, pending inspections, and items awaiting put-away. Finance must be notified when GRN posting triggers accounting entries or when three-way match exceptions require attention. All notifications must support bilingual content delivery in Arabic and English based on each recipient's language preference. Notification channels must include in-app alerts, email with PDF attachment summaries, and SMS for critical alerts. The system must support workflow escalation rules where unactioned inspection orders escalate after configurable SLA periods (default: 24 hours for standard items, 4 hours for vessel-critical items).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R015	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Goods Receipt Performance Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time analytics dashboard tracking goods receipt performance metrics including average receipt processing time (from delivery to GRN posting), inspection pass rate by supplier and item category, three-way match success rate, receipt accuracy rate (GRN quantity versus PO quantity variance), and supplier on-time delivery performance measured against PO delivery dates. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port location, time period (with Hijri and Gregorian date range selectors), supplier, and item category. Trend analysis must show month-over-month and year-over-year comparisons. The system must highlight bottlenecks such as items pending inspection for more than SLA duration or receipts pending put-away. All charts and visualizations must support RTL rendering for Arabic language users with proper axis mirroring and right-aligned legends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R016	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	Bulk and Liquid Cargo Receipt with Measurement Reconciliation	The system must handle goods receipt for bulk and liquid commodities (bunker fuel, lubricating oil, fresh water, ballast water treatment chemicals) with specialized measurement reconciliation. Receipt quantities must support capture from multiple measurement methods: flow meter readings, tank ullage calculations, weighbridge tickets, and draft surveys. The system must calculate and record the measurement variance between supplier-stated quantity and receiver-measured quantity, applying configurable acceptable tolerance limits per commodity type (e.g., ±0.5% for fuel oil, ±1.0% for fresh water). Temperature correction factors must be applied for petroleum products to normalize volumes to standard reference temperature (15°C). When measurement variance exceeds tolerance, the system must flag the receipt for dispute resolution and prevent full quantity acceptance until the variance is investigated. Shore tank or storage vessel calibration tables must be maintained within the system for accurate ullage-to-volume conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-002-R017	FEAT-038-2-002	MOD-038	GRN Mobile Application with Barcode and Photo Capture	The system must provide a mobile application for warehouse receiving staff to process goods receipts on handheld devices directly at the delivery dock. The mobile app must support barcode scanning (1D and 2D/QR codes) for PO lookup, item identification, and bin location assignment. Photo capture must be integrated for documenting delivery condition, packaging integrity, and any visible damage for claims processing. The mobile app must function in offline mode for warehouse areas with poor connectivity, queuing transactions for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The interface must support both Arabic and English with switchable RTL/LTR layouts and must accommodate large-format text for outdoor readability. Voice-to-text input must be available for capturing notes and observations. Scanned data must validate against the PO in real-time (when online) to prevent receipt of incorrect items or quantities exceeding the open PO balance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R001	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Unified Spend Cube with Multi-Dimensional Analysis	The system must construct a unified spend cube that consolidates all procurement expenditure across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. The spend cube must support analysis across at minimum the following dimensions: supplier, item category, cost center, vessel/asset, port location, purchase order type (spot, contract, blanket), INCOTERMS, currency, time period (Gregorian and Hijri), and entity. All spend amounts must be normalized to USD using transaction-date exchange rates for accurate cross-entity comparison. The spend cube must be refreshed at least daily with incremental updates processing within 30 minutes of source transaction posting. Users must be able to create custom pivot views combining any dimensions, with the ability to drill down from summary totals to individual transaction details. Spend categorization must follow a standardized taxonomy aligned with UNSPSC (United Nations Standard Products and Services Code) for benchmarking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R002	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	AI Spend Classification and Categorization Engine	The system must employ AI/ML models to automatically classify and categorize all procurement spend into a standardized category hierarchy. The classification engine must analyze purchase order descriptions, item master data, supplier profiles, and historical classification patterns to assign each transaction to the correct spend category. The system must support a minimum four-level category hierarchy (e.g., Marine Equipment > Propulsion > Spare Parts > Bearings) with shipping-industry-specific categories including container repair materials, bunker fuel, port equipment, vessel stores, stevedoring services, and maritime insurance. The AI model must achieve a minimum 95% classification accuracy with confidence scoring, routing low-confidence classifications (below 85%) for human review. The system must learn from human corrections to continuously improve classification accuracy. Manual reclassification must be supported with bulk operations for correcting systematic misclassifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R003	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Maverick Spend Detection and Policy Compliance Analysis	The system must use AI to detect maverick spend, defined as purchases made outside of established contracts, preferred supplier agreements, or standard procurement processes. The AI engine must analyze each purchase transaction against active contract coverage, comparing actual purchase prices to contracted rates and flagging deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (default: 5% above contract price). The system must identify bypass purchasing patterns where requisitioners circumvent procurement controls, such as splitting orders to stay below approval thresholds, using non-preferred suppliers when contract suppliers are available, or creating emergency purchase orders for items that could have been planned. Maverick spend reports must quantify the potential savings lost due to non-compliance, calculated as the difference between actual prices paid and available contract prices. Reports must be segmented by entity, department, and user with trend analysis showing compliance improvement or deterioration over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R004	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Bunker Fuel Spend Optimization and Hedging Analytics	The system must provide specialized analytics for bunker fuel procurement, which typically represents the largest single spend category in container shipping operations. The AI engine must analyze historical bunker purchase data across all ports (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and correlate with global fuel price indices (Platts, Argus) to identify optimal purchasing timing and port location strategies. The system must model bunker fuel price forecasts using AI/ML trained on historical price data, crude oil futures, refinery utilization rates, and seasonal demand patterns. Cost comparison analysis must evaluate the total bunkering cost across ports, factoring not just the per-metric-ton price but also port charges, deviation costs for off-route bunkering, and fuel quality premiums. The system must support hedging scenario analysis showing potential cost savings from fixed-price contracts, futures contracts, and collar strategies with break-even calculations and risk exposure quantification in the entity's local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R005	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Supplier Consolidation Opportunity Identification	The system must use AI to identify supplier consolidation opportunities where multiple entities or departments are purchasing similar items from different suppliers. The analysis must compare pricing, quality metrics, lead times, and total cost of ownership across suppliers providing equivalent items. The system must generate consolidation recommendations with projected savings calculated from volume-based pricing improvements, reduced administrative costs from fewer supplier relationships, and potential logistics efficiency gains. Recommendations must account for strategic supplier diversification requirements, ensuring critical categories maintain at least two qualified suppliers to mitigate supply chain risk. The system must consider geographic constraints: local content requirements in KSA (IKTVA compliance), Qatar's preferential procurement policies for local suppliers, and India's Make in India procurement preferences. Consolidation recommendations must be presented with implementation roadmaps including timeline, change management requirements, and risk mitigation strategies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R006	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Spend Anomaly Detection and Fraud Risk Alerts	The system must deploy AI-based anomaly detection models to identify unusual spending patterns that may indicate procurement fraud, errors, or process breakdowns. The system must monitor for known fraud patterns including duplicate invoice payments, round-number invoice amounts suggesting fabrication, invoices just below approval thresholds, unusual payment terms deviating from contracts, significant price increases without justification, new suppliers with no competitive bidding, and excessive sole-source justifications. Each detected anomaly must be assigned a risk score (low, medium, high, critical) based on the financial exposure amount and pattern severity. High and critical risk alerts must be immediately escalated to the internal audit team with a detailed analysis package including the flagged transactions, comparison benchmarks, and suggested investigation steps. The system must maintain a false-positive feedback loop where investigators can mark alerts as confirmed or false-positive to improve model accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R007	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Contract Utilization and Leakage Analysis	The system must analyze contract utilization rates by comparing actual purchase volumes against contracted quantities or values for each active supplier agreement. The system must identify contract leakage where purchases of in-scope items are being made outside of existing contracts, quantifying the financial impact of this leakage. For contracts approaching volume commitments, the system must forecast whether minimum purchase obligations will be met based on current consumption trends and alert procurement managers to potential shortfall penalties. For contracts approaching maximum values, the system must warn of pending contract exhaustion and trigger renewal or re-tender workflows. The system must track contract price competitiveness by benchmarking contracted rates against market indices and recent spot purchase prices for equivalent items. The AI engine must recommend contract restructuring opportunities such as converting high-volume spot purchases to contracts or renegotiating underperforming contracts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R008	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Total Cost of Ownership Modeling	The system must calculate total cost of ownership (TCO) for major procurement categories going beyond the purchase price to include all associated costs throughout the item lifecycle. TCO components must include purchase price, inbound freight and logistics, customs duties and taxes, quality inspection costs, warehousing and handling, installation or commissioning costs, maintenance over useful life, training costs for specialized equipment, and disposal or recycling costs at end of life. For container shipping-specific assets, TCO must factor in classification society survey costs, regulatory compliance costs (IMO, flag state requirements), and downtime costs when critical equipment fails and requires replacement. The AI engine must build TCO models using historical data and predictive analytics, enabling procurement teams to compare supplier bids on a TCO basis rather than just initial purchase price. TCO comparisons must be presented in the buyer's local currency with sensitivity analysis showing how key variables (exchange rates, duty rates, maintenance frequency) affect the total cost.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R009	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Predictive Demand Forecasting for Procurement Planning	The system must use AI/ML models to forecast procurement demand for all major spend categories over 3, 6, and 12-month horizons. The forecasting engine must incorporate historical consumption data, vessel fleet deployment schedules, planned dry-docking and maintenance windows, seasonal shipping volume patterns (peak seasons, Ramadan schedule adjustments, Indian monsoon impacts), and macroeconomic indicators affecting trade volumes in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent routes. For container repair materials, demand forecasts must correlate with container damage rates by trade route and seasonal weather patterns. For provisions and stores, forecasts must factor in crew rotation schedules and vessel manning levels. The system must measure forecast accuracy using MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) with a target accuracy of ±15% for 3-month forecasts and ±25% for 12-month forecasts. Procurement teams must be able to view AI-generated demand forecasts alongside actual procurement plans to identify gaps and optimize ordering schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R010	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Currency Exposure Analysis for Procurement Spend	The system must analyze procurement spend by transaction currency to quantify foreign exchange exposure across all entities. For each entity, the system must calculate net exposure by currency pair (e.g., QAR/USD, AED/EUR, INR/JPY) considering both committed purchase orders and forecasted spend. The AI engine must model currency risk scenarios using Monte Carlo simulation to estimate potential cost impacts under various exchange rate movements. The system must recommend natural hedging opportunities such as aligning payment currencies with revenue currencies on the same trade routes. For significant exposures exceeding configurable thresholds, the system must generate hedging recommendations including forward contracts, options, and currency swaps with estimated costs and break-even rates. Historical analysis must show the actual cost impact of currency movements on procurement spend over previous periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) compared to what costs would have been at budget exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R005	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Maintenance Budget vs Actual Tracking per Asset Category	The system must track maintenance budgets versus actual expenditures at the individual asset level, asset category level (e.g., quay cranes, yard equipment, reefer infrastructure, marine fenders), and entity level across all four jurisdictions. Budget amounts must be maintained in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with consolidated reporting in USD. Variance analysis must be automatically generated when actual maintenance spend exceeds 10% of the budgeted amount for any asset category. The system must support mid-year budget revisions with approval workflows and maintain a complete history of all budget amendments. Reports must be exportable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic headers and data labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R011	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Spend Dashboard with Executive Reporting	The system must provide an executive spend analytics dashboard with configurable KPI tiles showing total spend by period, spend by category, spend by supplier (top 10 and Pareto distribution), contract versus spot purchase ratio, savings achieved against budget, and year-over-year spend trends. The dashboard must support role-based views: CPO-level summary across all entities, entity procurement director view for single-entity deep-dive, and category manager view for specific spend category analysis. All visualizations must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with culturally appropriate color schemes. The dashboard must support date filtering in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Export functionality must generate board-ready PDF reports with company branding, bilingual headers, and multi-currency presentation. Automated report distribution must be schedulable (daily, weekly, monthly) with recipient lists configurable per report type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R012	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Spare Parts Pooling and Cross-Entity Optimization	The system must analyze spare parts inventory and consumption patterns across all entities to identify pooling opportunities where maintaining shared safety stock at a central location is more cost-effective than each entity holding independent stock. The AI engine must calculate optimal pooling configurations considering inter-port transit times (Jebel Ali to Hamad Port: 1 day, Jebel Ali to JNPT: 4-5 days), critical spare requirements based on vessel schedules, and the cost trade-off between holding cost reduction and emergency shipping premiums when parts are needed urgently from the pool location. The system must simulate various pooling scenarios and present the projected annual savings from inventory reduction, reduced obsolescence risk, and improved fill rates. Pooling recommendations must respect entity ownership structures and transfer pricing regulations, ensuring arm's-length pricing for cross-entity parts transfers. Implementation tracking must monitor actual savings against projected savings after pooling arrangements are activated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R013	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Supplier Payment Terms Optimization	The system must analyze actual payment patterns versus contracted payment terms across all suppliers and entities to identify working capital optimization opportunities. The AI engine must calculate the effective cost of early payment discounts offered by suppliers (e.g., 2/10 net 30 translates to approximately 36% annualized return) and recommend which discounts should be captured based on the company's cost of capital. The system must identify suppliers where negotiating extended payment terms (from 30 to 60 or 90 days) would improve working capital without jeopardizing the supplier relationship, based on the supplier's financial stability indicators and the entity's importance as a customer. For dynamic discounting programs, the system must model the optimal discount curve based on the company's available cash position and the supplier's eagerness for early payment. Analysis must consider entity-specific cash positions and regulatory constraints: for example, KSA's Mada system payment processing timelines and India's MSME payment compliance requirements mandating 45-day payment to micro and small enterprises.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R014	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Market Intelligence and Price Benchmarking	The system must integrate external market intelligence data to benchmark procurement prices against industry standards and market indices. For bunker fuel, benchmarking must reference Platts and Argus published prices at each relevant port. For container spare parts, benchmarking must use IICL cost tables and OEM published price lists. For general categories, the system must aggregate anonymized pricing data from industry procurement databases where available. The AI engine must automatically flag purchase prices that deviate more than configurable thresholds (default: 10%) above market benchmarks and generate supplier negotiation briefs with market price evidence. Price trend analysis must forecast expected price movements for key commodities over the next 3-6 months based on supply-demand fundamentals, geopolitical risk factors affecting Gulf shipping lanes, and seasonal patterns. Intelligence reports must be presented in both Arabic and English with data visualizations supporting RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R015	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Sustainability and ESG Spend Analytics	The system must track and analyze procurement spend through an environmental, social, and governance (ESG) lens aligned with IMO's strategy for reduction of GHG emissions from ships. The system must categorize spend on environmentally sustainable alternatives (low-sulphur fuels, shore power connections, energy-efficient equipment) versus conventional alternatives and track the trend of green procurement adoption. Carbon footprint estimation must be calculated for key procurement categories based on scope 3 emission factors: upstream transportation emissions for delivered goods, embodied carbon in manufactured equipment, and fuel lifecycle emissions. The system must track supplier ESG certifications (ISO 14001, EcoVadis ratings) and correlate with spend allocation to report the percentage of total spend directed to ESG-certified suppliers. The analytics must support mandatory ESG disclosure requirements under each jurisdiction: QSE (Qatar Stock Exchange) ESG guidelines, UAE Securities and Commodities Authority sustainability reporting, SEBI (India) BRSR requirements, and KSA Vision 2030 sustainability metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R016	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	AI-Generated Procurement Savings Opportunity Pipeline	The system must maintain an AI-curated pipeline of procurement savings opportunities ranked by estimated value and implementation feasibility. The AI engine must continuously analyze spend data, contract terms, supplier performance, and market conditions to identify actionable opportunities across categories including price renegotiation (based on market benchmarks or volume leverage), specification optimization (identifying over-specified items where lower-cost alternatives meet requirements), process efficiency (reducing rush orders, improving demand planning), supplier switching (where quality-equivalent alternatives exist at lower cost), and demand reduction (identifying departments with consumption above benchmark levels). Each opportunity must include estimated annual savings amount in local currency and USD, confidence level, implementation complexity (low/medium/high), and recommended next steps. The pipeline must track opportunities through stages from identification to validation to implementation to realized savings, with accountability assigned to specific procurement category managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R006	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Port Time Performance Analysis	The system must track and analyze time spent in port against CP-allowed laytime and demurrage/despatch terms for Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV). It must capture arrival at anchorage, NOR tendered time, all-fast time, operations commencement, completion time, and departure time. Laytime calculations must follow CP terms (SHINC, SHEX, or other specified working time definitions) including local public holidays and Eid observances per entity jurisdiction. Demurrage and despatch amounts must be computed in CP currency with multi-currency conversion to the operating entity's base currency using the applicable exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-003-R017	FEAT-038-2-003	MOD-038	Natural Language Query Interface for Spend Data	The system must provide a natural language query interface allowing users to ask questions about procurement spend in both Arabic and English. The AI must interpret queries such as 'What did we spend on container spare parts at Jebel Ali last quarter?' or 'Show me our top 5 suppliers in Qatar by total spend in 2025' and generate appropriate data visualizations. The NLP engine must understand shipping-industry terminology, port codes, INCOTERMS, and currency abbreviations. Query results must be presented as interactive charts and tables with the ability to export or save as custom reports. The system must maintain a query history per user and suggest related analyses based on previous queries. For Arabic language queries, the system must handle Arabic morphological complexity and support both formal (Modern Standard Arabic) and common business Arabic terminology. The system must disambiguate queries involving similar terms and ask clarifying questions when intent is uncertain rather than returning incorrect results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R001	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Multi-Dimensional Supplier Scoring Framework	The system must implement a configurable supplier scoring framework with weighted evaluation across at minimum five dimensions: Quality (defect rate, inspection pass rate, specification compliance), Delivery (on-time delivery rate, lead time accuracy, order completeness), Price (competitiveness versus market benchmark, price stability, total cost of ownership), Compliance (documentation accuracy, regulatory adherence, certification currency), and Service (responsiveness, issue resolution time, flexibility on urgent orders). Weights must be configurable at entity level and at category level, recognizing that weight priorities differ by procurement type: for example, quality weight may be 40% for safety equipment but only 20% for office supplies. The overall score must be calculated on a 0-100 scale with performance bands: Excellent (90-100), Good (75-89), Acceptable (60-74), Needs Improvement (40-59), and Unacceptable (below 40). Scoring methodology must be transparent and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R002	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Automated KPI Data Collection from Transactional Systems	The system must automatically collect supplier performance data from transactional systems without manual data entry. On-time delivery rates must be calculated by comparing goods receipt dates against PO-confirmed delivery dates with configurable grace periods per delivery location (e.g., 2-day grace for Jebel Ali local delivery, 5-day grace for JNPT imports due to customs variability). Quality metrics must be sourced from goods receipt inspection results, warranty claims, and non-conformance reports. Price competitiveness data must be computed by comparing actual purchase prices against catalog prices, contracted rates, and market benchmarks. Documentation accuracy must be measured by tracking rejection rates on supplier invoices during three-way matching. Responsiveness metrics must be captured from the supplier portal interaction logs measuring response times to RFQs, PO acknowledgments, and query resolutions. All data collection must operate across entities with the ability to evaluate a single supplier's performance separately by entity or consolidated across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R003	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	AI-Weighted Dynamic Scoring Algorithm	The system must employ an AI engine to dynamically adjust scoring weights based on business context and supply chain conditions. During periods of supply shortage or extended lead times (e.g., post-disruption recovery at a specific port), the AI must automatically increase the weight of delivery reliability and responsiveness dimensions. When quality incidents increase in a specific category, the AI must escalate quality dimension weights for all suppliers in that category. The AI must analyze correlations between individual KPI metrics and overall supply chain outcomes (e.g., vessel schedule reliability, container availability) to identify which supplier performance factors have the highest impact on business performance. Dynamic weight adjustments must be transparent, with the system logging the rationale for each adjustment and allowing procurement managers to override AI-suggested weights. The system must maintain both the AI-adjusted score and a static baseline score for comparison, enabling stakeholders to assess the impact of dynamic weighting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R004	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Supplier Scorecard Dashboard with Bilingual Presentation	The system must present supplier scorecards through an interactive dashboard supporting both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts. Each supplier scorecard must display the overall score with trend indicator (improving, stable, declining), dimension-level scores with radar/spider chart visualization, individual KPI metrics with targets and actuals, performance trend over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annually), ranking position among peers in the same supply category, and comparison against entity-specific and global averages. The dashboard must support drill-down from scores to underlying transactional evidence (specific late deliveries, specific quality rejections) so users can validate and understand scores. Date ranges must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection. The radar chart and all visual elements must properly mirror for RTL rendering. Color coding must follow culturally appropriate conventions with configurable color schemes for accessibility compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R005	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Supplier Segmentation and Classification	The system must automatically classify suppliers into strategic segments based on performance scores, spend volume, and supply criticality. The segmentation matrix must categorize suppliers along two axes: performance score (high/low) and business impact (high/low), resulting in four quadrants: Strategic Partners (high performance, high impact), Preferred Suppliers (high performance, lower impact), Development Suppliers (lower performance, high impact requiring improvement), and Transactional Suppliers (lower performance, lower impact). The AI engine must recommend segment-appropriate management strategies: Strategic Partners receive collaborative planning and innovation sharing; Development Suppliers receive improvement programs with milestone targets; Transactional Suppliers below minimum performance thresholds are flagged for replacement. Segment assignments must be reviewed quarterly with automated transition recommendations when a supplier's metrics cross segment thresholds. Each entity must be able to apply entity-specific segmentation overlays while maintaining a group-level consolidated view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R004	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Mark-to-Market Valuation of Open Hedge Positions	The system must perform daily mark-to-market (MTM) valuation of all open FX hedge positions using mid-market rates sourced from the configured rate feed provider (e.g., Reuters, Bloomberg, or Central Bank published rates from QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI). MTM valuations must calculate unrealized gain/loss per contract, per currency pair, and per legal entity. The valuation must use the present value of future cash flows discounted at the appropriate interbank rate (e.g., SOFR for USD, EIBOR for AED). Results must be stored with full audit trail and support both IFRS 9 and local GAAP reporting requirements for each jurisdiction. Arabic-language MTM summary reports must be available for QAR and SAR denominated positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R006	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Corrective Action and Improvement Plan Management	The system must support creation and tracking of supplier corrective action requests (SCARs) and performance improvement plans (PIPs) when supplier scores fall below acceptable thresholds. A SCAR must be automatically triggered when a critical KPI breaches a defined threshold (e.g., quality inspection fail rate exceeds 10% in a rolling 3-month period). The SCAR must specify the performance gap, root cause analysis fields, corrective actions required, implementation timeline, and verification criteria. PIPs must be structured with milestone targets over a defined improvement period (typically 90-180 days) with interim review checkpoints. The system must track SCAR and PIP progress, sending escalation notifications when milestones are missed. If a supplier fails to achieve PIP targets within the agreed timeframe, the system must automatically flag the supplier for potential disqualification review. All SCAR and PIP communications must support bilingual content in Arabic and English, with documents generated in the supplier's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R007	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Supplier Disqualification and Blacklisting Workflow	The system must manage a controlled supplier disqualification workflow for suppliers that consistently fail to meet performance standards or violate compliance requirements. Disqualification triggers must include: overall score below acceptable threshold for two consecutive quarters, critical quality failure resulting in safety incident, sanctions list match (OFAC, EU, UN sanctions), bankruptcy or financial distress indicators, and fraudulent activity detection. The disqualification workflow must require multi-level approval: entity procurement director for entity-level disqualification, group procurement head for group-wide disqualification. Upon disqualification, the system must block the supplier from appearing in vendor selection for new purchase orders across all affected entities, flag existing open POs for review and potential cancellation or transition, and notify all procurement users of the disqualification. A blacklist register must be maintained with disqualification reason, effective date, review date, and the approving authority. Reinstatement must require a separate approval process with evidence of corrective action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R008	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Supplier Self-Assessment Portal	The system must provide a secure supplier-facing portal where suppliers can view their own performance scorecards, access detailed KPI breakdowns, and submit self-assessment questionnaires. The portal must display the supplier's scores for each entity they serve along with benchmark comparisons (their score versus category average, without revealing specific competitor data). Suppliers must be able to submit improvement action plans in response to identified performance gaps. The self-assessment questionnaire must cover capability areas including quality management system certification status, environmental compliance certifications, financial stability indicators, workforce capacity, and business continuity planning. The portal must support document upload for certifications, audit reports, and evidence of improvement actions. Portal interface must be available in Arabic and English with the supplier selecting their preferred language. Access must be controlled through secure authentication with supplier-specific data isolation ensuring no cross-supplier data leakage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R009	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Delivery Performance Tracking with Port-Specific SLAs	The system must track supplier delivery performance against port-specific service level agreements that account for local logistics conditions. SLA parameters must be configurable per delivery location: Jebel Ali free-zone deliveries may have a 24-hour delivery window, Hamad Port deliveries may allow 48 hours, JNPT deliveries may allow 72 hours due to congestion and customs processing variability. The system must distinguish between supplier-attributable delays (production, dispatch, documentation) and non-supplier-attributable delays (customs hold, port congestion, force majeure) to ensure fair performance measurement. When a delivery delay is identified as non-supplier-attributable, the system must allow the procurement officer to exclude that instance from the supplier's delivery score with mandatory documentation of the exclusion reason. Early deliveries must also be tracked as they can cause warehousing and cash-flow issues, with excessive early delivery (more than configurable days before requested date) flagged as a performance concern.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R010	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Quality Performance Trend Analysis with Root Cause Categorization	The system must perform quality performance trend analysis for each supplier with detailed root cause categorization for all quality failures. Root cause codes must follow a hierarchical structure: Level 1 categories (Material Defect, Workmanship Defect, Specification Non-Compliance, Packaging/Shipping Damage, Documentation Error) with Level 2 sub-categories providing specific detail (e.g., under Material Defect: wrong grade, contamination, dimensional out-of-tolerance, surface finish defect). Pareto analysis must be automatically generated showing the most frequent root causes for each supplier. The AI engine must identify emerging quality trends such as increasing defect rates in specific product lines or seasonal quality variations correlating with supplier production capacity utilization. For maritime safety-critical items governed by SOLAS, IMO, and classification society requirements, quality failures must carry elevated severity weighting in the overall score. The system must calculate the cost of poor quality (COPQ) per supplier including inspection costs, rework costs, replacement costs, and operational disruption costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R011	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Price Competitiveness Benchmarking Against Market Indices	The system must continuously benchmark supplier pricing against relevant market indices and peer pricing data. For commodity items (fuel, steel, chemicals), pricing must be benchmarked against published commodity indices with configurable index sources per category. For manufactured goods (spare parts, equipment), pricing must be benchmarked against the average price achieved from recent competitive bids for the same or equivalent items. The system must calculate a price competitiveness index (PCI) for each supplier scoring their pricing relative to the market median: PCI above 100 indicates above-market pricing, PCI below 100 indicates below-market pricing. Price trend analysis must track how each supplier's pricing moves relative to market indices over time, identifying suppliers who pass through market increases faster than decreases. The AI engine must factor price competitiveness into overall scoring while correlating with quality and delivery scores to ensure low price does not compensate for poor quality. All benchmark prices must be presented in the relevant transaction currency and normalized to USD for cross-entity comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R012	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Compliance and Certification Monitoring	The system must track supplier compliance status including regulatory certifications, insurance coverage, trade compliance, and contractual obligations. Key certifications to monitor include ISO 9001, ISO 14001, ISO 45001, OHSAS 18001, classification society approvals (Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ABS, ClassNK), maritime equipment type approvals, and trade-specific licenses. The system must maintain certification expiry calendars and generate alerts at 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry with escalation if renewal evidence is not received by 30 days pre-expiry. Sanctions screening must be performed against all relevant lists (OFAC SDN, EU Consolidated, UN Security Council) at supplier onboarding and periodically thereafter (configurable, default monthly). For KSA operations, the system must verify ZATCA tax registration status and Saudization compliance. For UAE operations, economic substance verification must be tracked. For India, MSME registration status, GST registration validity, and compliance with local content requirements under the defense procurement procedure (where applicable to port security equipment) must be monitored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R013	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Supplier Risk Scoring and Early Warning System	The system must maintain a supplier risk score separate from the performance score, evaluating financial stability, operational resilience, geopolitical exposure, and concentration risk. Financial risk indicators must include publicly available financial data (revenue trends, profitability, debt ratios), payment behavior reports from credit agencies, and any available credit ratings. Operational risk must assess single-point-of-failure scenarios: suppliers operating from a single facility, suppliers with no disaster recovery capability, or suppliers dependent on a single sub-supplier for critical inputs. Geopolitical risk must evaluate supplier exposure to trade sanctions, regional conflicts affecting shipping lanes (Red Sea, Strait of Hormuz), and regulatory changes in supplier domicile countries. The AI engine must generate early warning alerts when risk indicators deteriorate beyond configurable thresholds, giving procurement teams advance notice to activate contingency sourcing plans. Concentration risk analysis must flag when a single supplier accounts for more than a configurable percentage (default: 40%) of total spend in any critical category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R014	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Scorecard Review Meeting Workflow and Minutes Recording	The system must support structured supplier review meeting workflows including meeting scheduling, agenda generation, minutes recording, and action item tracking. The system must auto-generate meeting agendas containing the supplier's current scorecard summary, KPI trends since the last review, open corrective actions and their status, contract utilization metrics, and upcoming contract milestones. Meeting frequency must be configurable by supplier segment: quarterly for Strategic Partners and Development Suppliers, semi-annually for Preferred Suppliers, and annually for Transactional Suppliers. Meeting minutes must be recorded within the system with structured fields for discussion topics, agreed actions, responsible parties, and target completion dates. The system must track action item completion rates from previous meetings and include unresolved items on the next meeting agenda. Meeting invitations and agendas must be generated in the supplier's preferred language (Arabic or English). AI-generated summary insights must be included in the agenda highlighting key discussion points based on recent performance data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R015	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Cross-Entity Supplier Performance Comparison	The system must enable comparison of a single supplier's performance across multiple entities where that supplier serves more than one operating entity. The comparison view must display side-by-side scorecards for each entity the supplier serves (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with the same dimensional breakdown and identical KPI calculations to ensure comparability. The system must highlight significant performance disparities across entities (e.g., the same supplier achieving 95% on-time delivery in Jebel Ali but only 78% at JNPT) and flag these for investigation. AI analysis must suggest potential causes for cross-entity performance variations such as logistics infrastructure differences, customs processing impacts, local representative quality, or prioritization bias. A consolidated global scorecard must aggregate cross-entity data with configurable weighting by entity spend volume. The comparison must also highlight pricing disparities across entities for equivalent items, enabling procurement teams to negotiate price harmonization using the best achieved price as the benchmark.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R016	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Preferred Supplier Award and Tier Management	The system must manage a formal preferred supplier program with tier-based recognition linked to scorecard performance. Supplier tiers must be configurable with defaults: Platinum (top 5% by overall score), Gold (next 10%), Silver (next 20%), and Standard (remaining qualified suppliers). Tier assignment must be automatically calculated based on rolling 12-month scorecard performance with minimum thresholds for each tier (e.g., Platinum requires minimum 92 overall score, zero critical quality failures, and 98% on-time delivery). Benefits associated with each tier must be configurable per entity and may include preferred evaluation status in competitive bids (e.g., 5% bid price preference for Platinum suppliers), streamlined approval processes for repeat orders, longer payment terms, and early sight of upcoming procurement opportunities. Tier changes must trigger notifications to both internal stakeholders and the supplier. The system must generate annual preferred supplier certificates in both Arabic and English with entity branding, suitable for the supplier's display and marketing use.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R017	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Scorecard Export and Regulatory Reporting	The system must support comprehensive export of supplier scorecard data in multiple formats: PDF reports with bilingual Arabic/English headers and branding for formal supplier communications, Excel workbooks with raw data and pivot-ready structures for ad-hoc analysis, and structured data extracts (CSV, JSON) for integration with external analytics tools. PDF reports must include the supplier's company details, scorecard period, overall and dimensional scores with trend charts, key KPI metrics with targets, benchmark comparisons, and outstanding action items. Reports must render correctly in both LTR and RTL formats based on the selected language. The system must support regulatory reporting requirements including Saudi Arabia's Tawteen (local content) reporting with supplier localization data, UAE In-Country Value reporting, Qatar's national procurement support metrics, and India's GEM (Government e-Marketplace) compliance data where applicable. All exports must include data classification markings (confidential, internal, public) and must maintain audit logs of who generated which reports and when.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-038-2-004-R018	FEAT-038-2-004	MOD-038	Historical Scorecard Archival and Performance Baseline Tracking	The system must maintain a complete historical archive of supplier scorecards with period-by-period snapshots that cannot be retroactively modified. Scorecard snapshots must be generated and locked at the end of each evaluation period (monthly, quarterly, or as configured) preserving all scores, underlying metrics, weights applied, and benchmark values used at the time of evaluation. The historical archive must support long-term trend analysis spanning a minimum of five years, enabling procurement teams to assess whether supplier performance trajectories are genuinely improving or showing cyclical patterns. Baseline performance levels must be established when a supplier is first onboarded or when a new contract begins, serving as the reference point for improvement measurement. The system must calculate compound annual improvement rates for each KPI dimension. For suppliers that have undergone ownership changes, mergers, or significant restructuring, the system must support performance timeline annotations marking these events to contextualize performance shifts. All historical data must be retained in compliance with the longest applicable data retention regulation across the four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R001	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Unique Asset Identification and Tagging	The system must generate and maintain a unique asset identification code for every fixed asset registered across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The asset code must follow a hierarchical scheme encoding the owning entity (QA/AE/SA/IN), asset class (CNT for containers, VSL for vessels, EQP for equipment, VEH for vehicles), location code (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNPT for JNPT), and a sequential number. The system must prevent duplicate asset codes across entities, support barcode and QR code generation for physical tagging, and maintain a complete audit trail of any asset code reassignments or mergers. Asset codes must be searchable in both Latin and Arabic character sets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R002	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Container Asset Classification and Attributes	The system must support detailed classification of container assets by ISO type codes (22G1 for 20ft dry, 42G1 for 40ft dry, 45G1 for 40ft HC, L5G1 for 45ft HC, 22R1 for 20ft reefer, 42R1 for 40ft reefer, 22U1 for 20ft open top, 42P1 for 40ft flat rack) in compliance with ISO 6346. Each container record must capture the BIC owner code, serial number, check digit, manufacturing date, tare weight, max payload, internal cubic capacity, CSC plate validity date, and current structural condition grade (A through D). The system must also track container-specific attributes such as reefer unit model and refrigerant type for temperature-controlled units, and must validate container numbers against the ISO 6346 check digit algorithm before registration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R003	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Vessel Asset Registration and Documentation	The system must maintain a complete vessel asset registry capturing IMO number, vessel name, flag state, classification society, call sign, MMSI number, gross tonnage, net tonnage, deadweight tonnage, LOA, beam, draft, TEU capacity (nominal and actual), year built, shipyard of construction, and hull material. Each vessel record must link to associated documentation including class certificates, safety management certificates, ISPS certificates, P&I club insurance, and registry of ownership. The system must track vessel ownership percentages for jointly owned vessels across multiple entities and must support vessel registration under different flag states with associated regulatory compliance requirements for each jurisdiction (Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority, Indian DG Shipping).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R004	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Port Terminal Equipment Registry	The system must register and classify all port terminal equipment assets including ship-to-shore (STS) gantry cranes, rubber-tyred gantry cranes (RTGs), rail-mounted gantry cranes (RMGs), reach stackers, empty container handlers, terminal tractors, chassis trailers, forklifts, and straddle carriers. Each equipment record must capture the manufacturer, model, serial number, year of manufacture, load capacity, fuel type (diesel, electric, hybrid, LNG), operating hours meter reading at acquisition, and assigned terminal location (Hamad Port Container Terminal 1 or 2, Khalifa Port Container Terminal, Jebel Ali Terminal 1-4, JNPT BMCT or NSIGT). The system must support parent-child asset relationships where attachments and major components such as spreaders, hoists, and engines are tracked as sub-assets under the parent equipment record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R005	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Multi-Entity Asset Ownership and Transfer	The system must support asset ownership across multiple legal entities operating in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, with the ability to record inter-entity asset transfers. When an asset is transferred from one entity to another (for example, a container fleet reallocation from the Qatar entity to the UAE entity), the system must generate the appropriate inter-company journal entries in both the transferring and receiving entity ledgers, calculate any transfer pricing adjustments in compliance with OECD transfer pricing guidelines, convert the net book value from the source currency (e.g., QAR) to the target currency (e.g., AED) using the exchange rate on the transfer date, and recalculate the depreciation schedule for the receiving entity from the transfer date based on the transferred net book value. All inter-entity transfers require dual authorization from both the releasing and receiving entity controllers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R006	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Acquisition Recording and Capitalization	The system must record asset acquisitions with full cost build-up including purchase price, import duties, freight charges, installation costs, professional fees, and any directly attributable costs required to bring the asset to working condition as per IAS 16 and IFRS requirements. For assets acquired in foreign currencies, the system must record both the original currency amount and the functional currency equivalent using the spot rate on the acquisition date for USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The capitalization threshold must be configurable per entity to accommodate different local regulatory requirements: minimum QAR 5,000 for Qatar, AED 5,000 for UAE, SAR 5,000 for KSA, and INR 50,000 for India. Assets below the threshold must be expensed immediately with appropriate expense account mapping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R006	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Spare Parts Inventory Integration for Asset Maintenance	The system must integrate with the inventory management module to track spare parts consumption against specific fixed asset maintenance activities. When a maintenance work order is created for equipment such as an RTG crane at Khalifa Port or a reach stacker at JNPT, the system must automatically check spare parts availability, reserve required components, and update inventory levels upon work order completion. If spare parts are unavailable, the system must trigger a procurement requisition with estimated lead time. Spare parts costs must be allocated to the correct asset's maintenance cost center and reflected in the asset's total cost of ownership calculation. Multi-currency costing must apply when parts are procured internationally in USD and consumed at local entity sites.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R007	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Location and Movement Tracking	The system must track the physical location of every registered asset with support for hierarchical location codes aligned with UN/LOCODE standards (QADOH for Doha/Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEAUH for Khalifa Port Abu Dhabi, INNSA for JNPT Nhava Sheva). For mobile assets such as containers and chassis, the system must integrate with GPS tracking and terminal operating systems to update current positions. Location history must be maintained with timestamps for each movement event. The system must support geofencing rules that trigger alerts when high-value assets move outside designated zones. For containers operating on international routes, the location tracking must distinguish between assets at port, on vessel, at inland depot, or at customer premises, and must record the associated voyage or transport reference number for each location change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R008	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Arabic RTL Interface for Asset Registry	The system must provide a fully functional Arabic right-to-left (RTL) user interface for the asset registry module, including all data entry forms, search screens, report outputs, and asset detail views. All field labels, dropdown values, validation messages, and help text must be available in both Arabic and English with seamless language switching without data loss. Asset descriptions and notes fields must support mixed Arabic-English content with proper bidirectional text rendering. Asset classification taxonomies must be maintained in both Arabic and English, with Arabic as the primary language for Qatar, KSA, and UAE entities. Printed asset tags and QR code labels must include bilingual text. The Arabic interface must properly handle right-to-left numeral display preferences configurable between Western Arabic numerals (0-9) and Eastern Arabic-Indic numerals, as preferred by the user or mandated by entity-level settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R009	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Hijri and Gregorian Dual Calendar Support	The system must support both Hijri (Islamic) and Gregorian calendar systems for all date fields in the asset registry including acquisition date, commissioning date, warranty expiry, and next inspection due date. Users in KSA and Qatar entities must be able to enter dates in either Hijri or Gregorian format with automatic bidirectional conversion using the Umm al-Qura calendar algorithm approved by the Saudi government. All date fields must store both Hijri and Gregorian equivalents in the database. Reports and exports must support date format selection between Hijri-only, Gregorian-only, or dual display. Fiscal year boundaries for depreciation calculations must respect entity-specific calendar configurations where KSA entities may operate on a Hijri fiscal year while other entities use the Gregorian calendar, and the system must correctly handle the variable length of Hijri months when computing daily depreciation rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R010	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Bulk Asset Import and Migration	The system must support bulk import of asset records from CSV, Excel (XLSX), and XML file formats to facilitate initial data migration from legacy systems and ongoing bulk registrations such as new container fleet deliveries. The import template must include all mandatory fields with validation rules enforced during import: asset code uniqueness, valid ISO container type codes, valid UN/LOCODE locations, numeric ranges for financial values, and date format validation for both Hijri and Gregorian dates. The import process must support a dry-run validation mode that reports all errors and warnings without committing data, followed by a confirmed import that creates asset records in batch. The system must handle imports of up to 50,000 records per batch and must provide a detailed import log showing records created, updated, skipped, and failed with specific error descriptions for each rejected record. Imported assets must be flagged as pending review until approved by an asset manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R011	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Image and Document Attachment	The system must allow multiple digital attachments per asset record including photographs, scanned documents, engineering drawings, manufacturer specifications, warranty certificates, and inspection reports. Supported file formats must include JPEG, PNG, PDF, TIFF, DWG, and MP4 for video inspections. Each attachment must be tagged with a document type classification, upload date, uploaded-by user, and optional expiry date for time-sensitive documents such as classification certificates and insurance policies. The system must send automated notifications 30, 60, and 90 days before document expiry to the responsible asset manager. Maximum file size per attachment must be configurable with a default of 50 MB. The system must support offline document capture on mobile devices with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored, which is critical for assets located in remote port areas at Hamad Port or JNPT where network coverage may be intermittent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R012	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Condition Assessment and Grading	The system must support periodic condition assessments for all registered assets with configurable assessment frequencies based on asset class (e.g., containers every 30 months per IICL standards, vessels per classification society survey schedules, equipment per manufacturer maintenance intervals). Each assessment must record the inspection date, inspector identity, condition grade on a standardized scale (Grade A: Excellent, Grade B: Good, Grade C: Fair, Grade D: Poor, Grade F: Failed/Condemned), detailed findings for each inspection checkpoint, estimated remaining useful life, and any recommended repairs or capital improvements. The system must maintain a complete history of all condition assessments per asset and must trigger automated workflows when an asset is graded below a configurable threshold, such as initiating a disposal evaluation when an asset is graded D or F. Condition assessment data must feed into the depreciation engine to support impairment reviews under IAS 36.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R013	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Warranty and Service Contract Tracking	The system must track warranty terms and service contracts associated with each asset, including warranty start and end dates, covered components, exclusion terms, service provider contact details, and claim submission procedures. For major equipment such as STS cranes, RTGs, and vessel main engines, the system must support multiple concurrent warranties covering different components (e.g., structural warranty, electrical warranty, software warranty). The system must generate automated alerts at configurable intervals before warranty expiry (default 90, 60, 30 days) to prompt procurement of extended warranties or service contracts. When a maintenance work order is raised for an asset under warranty, the system must flag the work order for warranty claim submission rather than internal cost absorption. The system must maintain a register of all warranty claims submitted, approved, rejected, and pending, with tracking of claim amounts in the original warranty currency and the entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R014	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Search and Advanced Filtering	The system must provide a comprehensive search and filtering interface for the asset registry supporting free-text search across asset codes, descriptions, serial numbers, and tag numbers with results ranked by relevance. Advanced filters must include asset class, sub-class, owning entity, current location (port/terminal/berth), acquisition date range (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian), net book value range with currency selection, condition grade, depreciation method, warranty status (active/expired/none), and custom attribute filters. Search results must be displayed in a configurable grid view supporting column sorting, grouping, and inline editing of selected fields. The system must support saved search configurations that users can name, store, and share with team members. Search must return results within 3 seconds for registries containing up to 500,000 asset records. Export of search results must be available in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with Arabic and English column headers based on user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R015	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Asset Registry Audit Trail and Access Control	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail for every change made to any asset record, capturing the timestamp, user identity, IP address, field changed, old value, new value, and reason for change where applicable. The audit trail must be queryable by asset, by user, by date range, and by change type (creation, modification, deletion, status change, transfer). Role-based access control must enforce granular permissions at the entity level, asset class level, and field level, such that a user in the Qatar entity cannot modify assets owned by the India entity unless explicitly granted cross-entity privileges. The system must support segregation of duties requiring different users for asset creation, asset approval, and asset disposal authorization. Audit trail records must be retained for a minimum of 10 years in compliance with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Commercial Transactions Law, Saudi ZATCA requirements, and Indian Companies Act 2013 provisions on books of accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-001-R016	FEAT-039-1-001	MOD-039	Offline Asset Registration and Synchronization	The system must support offline asset registration capabilities for field operations at port terminals and vessel locations where internet connectivity may be unavailable or unreliable. The offline mode must allow users to create new asset records, update existing records, capture photographs, and record condition assessments on mobile devices or ruggedized tablets. All offline data must be stored locally with encryption at rest using AES-256 and must queue for automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored. The synchronization engine must handle conflict resolution when the same asset record has been modified both offline and online, using a last-write-wins strategy with full conflict logging for manual review. The offline module must pre-cache the asset classification taxonomy, location master data, and user authorization profiles to enable full validation during offline data entry. The system must display a clear visual indicator of offline mode status and pending synchronization queue depth to the user.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R001	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Multiple Depreciation Method Support	The system must support the following depreciation methods configurable per asset or asset class: straight-line, reducing balance (declining balance), double declining balance, sum-of-years-digits, units-of-production (based on operating hours for cranes/equipment or TEU throughput for vessels), and MACRS for any US-reporting requirements. Each asset must support up to three concurrent depreciation books: a primary book for IFRS financial reporting, a secondary book for local statutory reporting (Qatar Financial Markets Authority, UAE IFRS adoption, KSA ZATCA, India Companies Act Schedule II), and a tertiary book for tax depreciation. The system must allow mid-life changes to depreciation method with proper prospective adjustment calculations as required by IAS 8 for changes in accounting estimates, recalculating remaining depreciation from the change date without restating prior periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R002	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Asset Useful Life Configuration by Class	The system must maintain configurable useful life tables by asset class and sub-class, with default values aligned to industry standards and local regulatory requirements. Default useful lives must include: dry containers 12-15 years, reefer containers 10-12 years, container vessels 25-30 years, STS gantry cranes 25 years, RTG cranes 15-20 years, reach stackers 8-10 years, terminal tractors 7 years, chassis 10-12 years, and office buildings 30-40 years. The useful life must be configurable per entity to accommodate different jurisdictional requirements: for example, India Companies Act Schedule II prescribes specific useful lives that may differ from IFRS estimates used by the Qatar entity. The system must allow individual asset-level overrides with mandatory documentation of the justification for deviation from the class default, and must track useful life revisions with effective dates for prospective depreciation recalculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R003	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Residual Value Estimation and Tracking	The system must record and track the estimated residual (salvage) value for each asset, which is deducted from the depreciable base before computing periodic depreciation charges. Residual values must be expressed as either a fixed monetary amount in the asset functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) or as a percentage of the original cost. For containers, the system must support market-based residual value estimation by referencing scrap steel prices and second-hand container market indices. The system must enforce annual review of residual value estimates as required by IAS 16 paragraph 51, generating a review task list for asset managers at entity fiscal year-end. When a residual value estimate is revised, the system must prospectively recalculate remaining depreciation from the revision date, record the change as a change in accounting estimate under IAS 8, and generate a disclosure note quantifying the impact of the revision on current and future period depreciation expense.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R004	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Monthly Depreciation Run Automation	The system must execute automated monthly depreciation calculations for all active assets across all entities on a configurable schedule, typically on the last business day of each month or the first day of the following month. The depreciation run must process assets by entity, computing depreciation in the entity functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and generating journal entries that debit the appropriate depreciation expense account and credit the accumulated depreciation account per the entity chart of accounts. The run must handle partial-month depreciation for assets acquired or disposed of mid-month using configurable conventions: full-month in month of acquisition, half-month convention, or exact-day proration. The system must generate a depreciation run summary report showing total depreciation by entity, asset class, and cost center, with drill-down to individual asset detail. Failed calculations must be isolated and reported without blocking the overall run completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R005	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Component-Level Depreciation for Major Assets	The system must support component-level (componentized) depreciation for major assets such as vessels and STS cranes in compliance with IAS 16 paragraphs 43-47, which require significant parts of an item of property, plant, and equipment with different useful lives to be depreciated separately. For a container vessel, the system must allow decomposition into components such as hull and superstructure (useful life 30 years), main engine (useful life 20 years), auxiliary engines (useful life 15 years), navigation and communication equipment (useful life 10 years), and dry-dock/special survey costs (useful life 2.5-5 years per survey cycle). Each component must have its own cost allocation, useful life, residual value, and depreciation method while rolling up to the parent asset net book value. Component replacement events must trigger derecognition of the old component carrying value and capitalization of the new component cost, with the difference recognized in profit or loss.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R006	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation Projection and Forecasting	The system must generate forward-looking depreciation projections for budgeting and financial planning purposes. Users must be able to run depreciation forecasts for periods of 1 to 10 years into the future, incorporating planned asset acquisitions from the capital expenditure budget, planned disposals, and expected useful life revisions. Projections must be generated per entity, per asset class, per cost center, or consolidated across all entities with multi-currency translation at projected exchange rates. The system must support scenario analysis allowing users to model the depreciation impact of alternative capital investment plans, for example comparing the purchase of 500 new 40ft containers versus leasing, or comparing the acquisition of a new STS crane versus refurbishment of existing cranes. Projection reports must include annual and monthly granularity and must be exportable in Excel format for integration with external financial planning tools.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R007	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Revaluation and Impairment Adjustment	The system must support asset revaluation under the IAS 16 revaluation model as an alternative to the cost model, applicable to entire classes of assets when elected by an entity. When an asset class is revalued, the system must record the revaluation surplus or deficit, update the gross carrying amount and accumulated depreciation proportionally or by elimination method as configured, post the revaluation surplus to other comprehensive income (revaluation reserve) in equity, and recalculate prospective depreciation based on the revalued amount and revised remaining useful life. The system must also support impairment testing under IAS 36, allowing users to record the recoverable amount (higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use), calculate any impairment loss, and post the impairment charge against the revaluation reserve (if any) with any excess recognized in profit or loss. Impairment reversal must be supported for non-goodwill assets subject to the original cost ceiling constraint.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R008	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Tax Depreciation Book for Each Jurisdiction	The system must maintain separate tax depreciation books for each operating jurisdiction with rates and methods prescribed by local tax authorities. For Qatar, the system must apply Qatar Tax Authority depreciation rates (buildings 5%, machinery 10%, vehicles 20%, furniture 10%). For UAE, the system must support the new UAE Corporate Tax Law depreciation provisions effective from June 2023. For KSA, the system must apply ZATCA-prescribed rates (buildings 5%, industrial equipment 25%, vehicles 25%, computers 25%, other assets 10%). For India, the system must apply Income Tax Act 1961 Written Down Value (WDV) depreciation rates as per Section 32 and the depreciation rate table (ships 20%, containers 20%, vehicles 15%, plant and machinery 15%, computers 40%). The system must compute deferred tax assets and liabilities arising from temporary differences between IFRS book values and tax book values per IAS 12, posting the deferred tax adjustments to the general ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R009	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation for Leased Assets under IFRS 16	The system must calculate depreciation for right-of-use (ROU) assets recognized under IFRS 16 for leased containers, vessels under time charter, leased port equipment, and leased office premises. The ROU asset must be depreciated over the shorter of the lease term and the useful life of the underlying asset, unless ownership transfers or a purchase option is reasonably certain to be exercised, in which case depreciation runs over the full useful life. The system must handle lease modifications that change the lease term or scope, recalculating the ROU asset value and prospective depreciation accordingly. For sale-and-leaseback transactions involving containers or vessels, the system must measure the ROU asset at the proportion of the previous carrying amount relating to the right of use retained, and compute depreciation on this retained amount. The depreciation entries for ROU assets must be clearly distinguished from owned asset depreciation in the general ledger and financial reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R010	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Capital Improvement and Subsequent Expenditure	The system must differentiate between capital improvements that extend the useful life or enhance the capacity of an asset and routine maintenance expenditures that are expensed in the period incurred, in accordance with IAS 16 recognition criteria. When a capital improvement is approved and capitalized (for example, a container refurbishment that extends useful life by 5 years, or a vessel engine retrofit that improves fuel efficiency), the system must add the improvement cost to the existing asset carrying amount or create a new component, reset or extend the remaining useful life as specified, and recalculate prospective depreciation. The system must enforce a configurable approval workflow for capitalization decisions requiring sign-off from the asset manager and finance controller. Subsequent expenditure below the capitalization threshold must be automatically routed to the appropriate maintenance expense account. The system must maintain a complete history of all capital improvements per asset with cost, date, description, and impact on useful life.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R011	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Multi-Currency Depreciation and Translation	The system must compute depreciation in the functional currency of the owning entity (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and support translation to the group reporting currency (USD) using the average exchange rate for the period as prescribed by IAS 21 for income statement items. Accumulated depreciation on the balance sheet must be translated at the closing rate, with the resulting exchange difference recognized in other comprehensive income as part of the foreign currency translation reserve. When an asset is acquired in a currency other than the entity functional currency, the initial cost must be recorded at the spot rate on the transaction date and depreciation must be computed on the functional currency amount. The system must handle the specific scenario of UAE entities holding USD-denominated assets where the AED/USD peg (3.6725) eliminates exchange differences, avoiding unnecessary translation adjustments for pegged currency pairs as configurable rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R012	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation Schedule Reporting and Disclosure	The system must generate depreciation schedule reports compliant with IAS 16 paragraph 73 disclosure requirements, showing for each class of assets: gross carrying amount at beginning and end of period, additions, disposals, acquisitions through business combinations, revaluation increases or decreases, impairment losses and reversals, depreciation charged for the period, net exchange differences, and net carrying amount at beginning and end of period. Reports must be generated per entity, per asset class, and consolidated. The system must produce these reports in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic reports. The reports must support both Hijri and Gregorian date headers based on entity fiscal year configuration. A fixed asset register detail report must list every asset with its cost, accumulated depreciation, net book value, depreciation method, useful life, and remaining useful life, sortable and filterable by any column.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R013	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation Suspension and Resumption	The system must support temporary suspension of depreciation for assets that are idle, held for sale under IFRS 5, or undergoing extended maintenance or dry-docking. When depreciation is suspended, the system must record the suspension start date, reason code (idle, held for sale, under repair, under construction, other), and expected resumption date. During the suspension period, no depreciation charges must be computed or posted for the affected asset. Upon resumption, the system must recalculate the remaining depreciation based on the remaining useful life without catch-up depreciation for the suspended period, unless the entity accounting policy specifies otherwise. For assets classified as held for sale under IFRS 5, the system must reclassify the asset from property, plant, and equipment to current assets, measure it at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell, and cease depreciation from the classification date. If the held-for-sale criteria are no longer met, the system must reverse the reclassification and record catch-up depreciation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R014	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Units of Production Depreciation for Operating Assets	The system must support units-of-production depreciation method for assets whose economic benefit consumption is more accurately measured by usage rather than time. For port equipment such as STS cranes and RTGs, the production unit must be configurable as crane operating hours, container lifts, or TEU moves. For vessels, the production unit must be configurable as revenue ton-miles, TEU-miles, or sailing days. The system must integrate with terminal operating systems and vessel voyage management systems to automatically capture actual production units per period. At each depreciation run, the system must calculate the depreciation charge as: (actual units in period / total estimated lifetime units) multiplied by the depreciable amount. The system must track cumulative production units versus estimated total lifetime units and alert asset managers when an asset approaches its estimated total production capacity, which may trigger a useful life extension review. If actual production data is not available for a period, the system must allow manual entry with supervisor approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R015	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation Run Reversal and Correction	The system must support reversal of a completed depreciation run in the event of errors discovered after posting, such as incorrect useful life parameters, wrong depreciation method assignment, or inclusion of disposed assets. The reversal must generate reversing journal entries that exactly offset the original depreciation entries for the affected period and assets. After reversal, the corrected parameters must be applied, and the depreciation run must be re-executed for the affected period. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all reversals showing the original run, reversal reason, approving user, and corrected run. Reversal of depreciation runs from prior fiscal years must generate prior period adjustment entries in compliance with IAS 8 for correction of errors, with appropriate restatement of comparative figures. The system must require finance controller authorization for any depreciation reversal and must prevent reversal of runs that have already been included in issued financial statements unless flagged as a prior period error correction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R016	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	Depreciation Integration with General Ledger	The system must automatically post depreciation journal entries to the general ledger module upon completion of each monthly depreciation run. Journal entries must be structured with configurable account mapping rules that determine the debit depreciation expense account and credit accumulated depreciation account based on asset class, cost center, business unit, and entity. The system must support segment reporting dimensions on depreciation entries, tagging each entry with operating segment (container shipping, port operations, logistics, corporate) per IFRS 8 requirements. Journal entries must include reference fields linking back to the depreciation run ID and individual asset ID for full traceability. The integration must support both real-time posting (immediate journal creation upon run completion) and batch posting (journal entries held in a staging table pending finance review and approval). The system must prevent duplicate posting of the same depreciation run and must validate that the target fiscal period is open in the general ledger before posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-002-R017	FEAT-039-1-002	MOD-039	AI-Driven Useful Life Optimization	The system must leverage artificial intelligence and machine learning models to analyze historical asset performance data, maintenance records, condition assessments, and operational intensity to recommend optimal useful life estimates for asset classes and individual high-value assets. The AI engine must process data from the asset registry, maintenance management system, and operational logs to identify patterns such as accelerated wear on containers operating in high-salinity Gulf port environments (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port) versus those in tropical monsoon conditions (JNPT). The system must generate useful life recommendation reports at least annually, showing the AI-recommended useful life, current configured useful life, confidence interval, and financial impact of adopting the recommendation (change in annual depreciation expense). All AI recommendations must be treated as advisory only, requiring human review and approval by the asset manager and finance controller before any parameter changes are applied to the depreciation engine. The model must be retrained quarterly with updated operational data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R002	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Configure trade lane corridor segments	The system must support the definition of trade lane segments representing origin-destination port pairs such as Hamad Port (QAHMD)–JNPT (INJNP), Jebel Ali (AEJEA)–Dammam (SAKDM), and Khalifa Port (AEKHL)–Mundra (INMUN). Each trade lane segment must capture freight revenue, bunker costs, port charges, THC (Terminal Handling Charges), and documentation fees. Trade lanes must be mappable to multiple entities when a single voyage crosses jurisdictions. The system must allow new trade lanes to be added without schema changes, supporting a minimum of 500 distinct trade lane corridor definitions with effective date ranges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R001	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Disposal Request Initiation and Approval Workflow	The system must support a configurable multi-level approval workflow for asset disposal requests. The disposal request must capture the asset identification, reason for disposal (end of useful life, obsolescence, damage beyond economic repair, surplus to requirements, regulatory non-compliance, sale opportunity), proposed disposal method (sale, scrap, trade-in, donation, abandonment), estimated disposal proceeds if applicable, and proposed disposal date. The approval workflow must be configurable by entity and asset value threshold: for example, assets with net book value below QAR 50,000 may require only department manager approval, while assets above QAR 50,000 require additional finance controller approval, and assets above QAR 500,000 require board-level authorization. The system must route approval requests to the appropriate approvers with email and in-app notifications, track approval status with timestamps, and prevent any disposal accounting entries until all required approvals are obtained. Rejected disposal requests must be returned to the initiator with mandatory rejection comments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R002	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Gain or Loss on Disposal Calculation	The system must accurately calculate the gain or loss on disposal of a fixed asset as the difference between the net disposal proceeds and the carrying amount (net book value) at the date of disposal, in accordance with IAS 16 paragraph 68. The carrying amount must be computed by running depreciation up to the exact disposal date, even if the disposal occurs mid-month, prorating the final month depreciation based on the entity disposal convention (exact day, half-month, or full month). Net disposal proceeds must be calculated as the gross sale price less directly attributable disposal costs including broker commissions, dismantling costs, site remediation costs, and transportation costs. When disposal proceeds are received in a currency different from the entity functional currency (e.g., container sold for USD but entity reports in QAR), the proceeds must be translated at the spot exchange rate on the date of receipt. The gain or loss must be classified as other income or other expense in the income statement and must not be included in revenue per IAS 16 paragraph 68A.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R003	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Container Scrap and Second-Hand Sale Processing	The system must support specialized disposal workflows for containers, which represent the highest-volume disposal category in container shipping operations. For scrap disposals, the system must record the scrap yard location, scrap weight in metric tons, negotiated scrap price per ton in the transaction currency (typically USD), and calculate total scrap proceeds. For second-hand container sales, the system must record the buyer details, sale price per unit, container condition grade at time of sale, and any refurbishment costs incurred to prepare the container for sale. The system must support batch disposal processing for fleet-wide container disposals, allowing simultaneous disposal of up to 1,000 containers in a single transaction with a common buyer and price but individual gain/loss calculations per container based on each container's unique net book value. The system must integrate with the container tracking system to update the container status to disposed and prevent further operational use of disposed containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R004	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Vessel Scrapping and Recycling Compliance	The system must manage the disposal process for vessels being scrapped or recycled, incorporating compliance requirements under the Hong Kong International Convention for the Safe and Environmentally Sound Recycling of Ships and the EU Ship Recycling Regulation. The disposal record must capture the ship recycling facility name and location (Alang in India, Gadani in Pakistan, Aliaga in Turkey, or other approved facilities), the recycling facility authorization status, the Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) document reference, and the International Ready for Recycling Certificate reference. For componentized vessels, the system must derecognize all components simultaneously upon vessel disposal, calculating individual gain or loss per component. The system must handle the scenario where vessel disposal proceeds are received in installments over several months from the recycling facility, recognizing the receivable at fair value on the disposal date and tracking subsequent collections. Environmental remediation provisions associated with vessel scrapping must be recorded as disposal costs reducing the net proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R005	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Asset Write-Off for Damaged or Lost Assets	The system must support write-off of assets that are damaged beyond economic repair, lost, stolen, or destroyed by force majeure events. The write-off process must record the incident date, incident description, supporting documentation (police reports, survey reports, insurance claim references, force majeure declarations), and the write-off authorization. For assets damaged in port operations (e.g., container crushed by crane mishap at Jebel Ali, equipment damaged during monsoon flooding at JNPT), the system must link the write-off to the incident management system and track any insurance recovery receivable separately from the asset derecognition. The carrying amount at the write-off date must be recognized as a loss in the income statement, with any subsequent insurance recovery recognized as other income when received or when the insurance claim is approved, whichever is earlier as per IAS 37 contingent asset guidance. The system must prevent premature write-off without proper investigation and must require supporting evidence documentation before processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R006	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Trade-In and Exchange Transaction Processing	The system must handle asset exchanges and trade-in transactions where an old asset is surrendered as partial payment for a new asset. The system must determine whether the exchange has commercial substance as defined in IAS 16 paragraph 25: if the exchange has commercial substance, the new asset must be recognized at fair value of the asset given up (adjusted for any cash paid or received), and a gain or loss must be recognized on the old asset. If the exchange lacks commercial substance, the new asset must be recorded at the carrying amount of the old asset with no gain or loss recognized. The system must record the trade-in allowance, any cash boot paid or received, the fair value determination basis (independent appraisal, market quotation, or present value of expected cash flows), and link the disposal of the old asset to the acquisition of the new asset in a single transaction. This is particularly relevant for port equipment trade-ins such as replacing older RTG cranes with newer electrified models at Hamad Port or upgrading reach stackers at JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R007	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Partial Disposal and Split of Asset	The system must support partial disposal of an asset where only a portion of the asset is sold, scrapped, or decommissioned while the remaining portion continues in use. This scenario commonly arises with land and buildings (selling a portion of port-adjacent land), container fleets registered as a single asset (disposing of a subset of containers), or equipment with separately tracked components. For partial disposals, the system must allocate the original cost and accumulated depreciation between the disposed portion and the retained portion based on a configurable allocation method: relative fair value, physical quantity ratio, or area ratio for land and buildings. The system must create a new asset record for the retained portion with the allocated cost and accumulated depreciation, calculate gain or loss only on the disposed portion, and update the depreciation schedule for the retained portion. The system must maintain a clear audit trail linking the original asset to both the disposed portion and the retained new asset record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R008	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Disposal Proceeds Multi-Currency Handling	The system must handle disposal proceeds received in currencies different from the entity functional currency, which is common in international container shipping where containers may be sold globally in USD regardless of the owning entity currency. The system must record the gross disposal proceeds in the original transaction currency, convert to the entity functional currency using the spot rate on the transaction date (or settlement date if materially different), and calculate the gain or loss on disposal in the entity functional currency. For disposal proceeds received in installments, each installment must be translated at the spot rate on the date of receipt, and any exchange differences between the initially recognized receivable and the actual cash received must be recognized as foreign exchange gains or losses separate from the disposal gain or loss. The system must support proceeds in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP, CNY, and any other configured currency with automatic rate lookup from the treasury exchange rate master maintained in the ERP system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R009	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Mass Disposal and Fleet Retirement Program	The system must support mass disposal programs for fleet retirement scenarios where large numbers of similar assets are disposed of simultaneously, such as retiring an aging container fleet of 2,000 units or decommissioning obsolete chassis at the end of their service life. The mass disposal function must allow selection of assets by class, age range, condition grade, location, and owning entity. Users must be able to apply a uniform disposal method and proceeds estimate to the entire batch while the system computes individual gain or loss per asset based on each asset unique net book value. The mass disposal must generate consolidated journal entries with individual asset-level detail available through drill-down. The system must provide a pre-disposal simulation report showing the total estimated gain or loss, impact on asset register totals by class, and effect on future period depreciation savings before the disposal is committed. Mass disposal must support a staged execution mode where assets are disposed in configurable batch sizes to manage general ledger posting volumes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R010	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Disposal Accounting Journal Entry Generation	The system must automatically generate the complete set of journal entries required to account for an asset disposal. The standard disposal journal must debit accumulated depreciation for the full amount accumulated to the disposal date, debit cash or receivables for the disposal proceeds, credit the asset cost account for the original cost (or revalued amount if under revaluation model), and debit loss on disposal or credit gain on disposal for the balancing difference. For assets that had a revaluation surplus, the remaining revaluation reserve must be transferred directly to retained earnings (not through profit or loss) upon disposal as per IAS 16 paragraph 41. For assets with associated provisions (such as decommissioning provisions for vessels), the disposal entries must include settlement or reversal of the provision. All journal entries must include segment coding for IFRS 8 reporting, cost center allocation, and inter-company coding for assets disposed across entities. The system must validate that the target accounting period is open and that the disposal date falls within the posting period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R011	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Disposal Tax Implications and Reporting	The system must calculate the tax impact of asset disposals for each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must determine if the disposal gives rise to a taxable gain under Qatar Income Tax Law for foreign-owned entities (noting that Qatari and GCC nationals are generally exempt). For UAE, the system must compute the taxable gain or loss under the UAE Corporate Tax Law, considering whether the participation exemption applies for vessel disposals by qualifying holding companies. For KSA, the system must calculate the capital gain treatment under ZATCA regulations, distinguishing between gains subject to standard corporate tax (20%) and gains by non-resident entities subject to withholding tax. For India, the system must distinguish between short-term capital gains (asset held less than 36 months, taxed at standard corporate rate) and long-term capital gains (held more than 36 months, taxed at preferential rate with indexation benefit under Section 48 of the Income Tax Act). The system must generate disposal tax impact reports for review by the tax compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R012	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Asset Disposal Register and History	The system must maintain a permanent disposal register recording all disposed assets with their full history, accessible for audit, regulatory inquiry, and management analysis. The disposal register must capture the asset code, description, asset class, owning entity, original acquisition date and cost, total depreciation charged over the asset life, net book value at disposal, disposal date, disposal method, buyer or recipient details (for sales and donations), disposal proceeds, gain or loss amount, approving authority, and all linked journal entry references. The register must be searchable by any field and filterable by entity, asset class, disposal date range, disposal method, and gain/loss threshold. The system must retain disposal records for a minimum of 15 years to satisfy the longest applicable statutory retention period across all operating jurisdictions. Reports from the disposal register must be available in both Arabic and English with export to Excel, PDF, and XML formats for regulatory submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R007	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Trade lane profitability dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing profitability metrics per trade lane including revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, margin percentage, and contribution margin. Dashboard must support filtering by date range (Gregorian and Hijri), container type, cargo category (dry, reefer, hazmat, OOG), and INCOTERM basis (CIF, FOB, CFR, FAS, EXW). Visual elements must include bar charts for revenue comparison, trend lines for margin evolution over 12-month rolling periods, and heat maps for trade lane density. The dashboard must load within 3 seconds for datasets covering up to 500,000 shipment records. Arabic UI must mirror chart axis labels and legends correctly in RTL mode.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R013	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Involuntary Conversion and Insurance Claim Linkage	The system must handle involuntary conversions where an asset is destroyed or condemned and the entity receives insurance proceeds or government compensation. Common scenarios include containers lost overboard during severe weather in the Arabian Sea, equipment damaged by port accidents at JNPT or Jebel Ali, or assets condemned by government authority for port expansion projects (eminent domain at Hamad Port). The system must record the involuntary conversion event, link to the insurance claim in the insurance tracking module, and handle the timing difference between asset derecognition (immediate upon loss or condemnation) and insurance or compensation receipt (which may be months later). If the insurance proceeds exceed the carrying amount, the excess must be recognized as a gain only when receipt is virtually certain. If the entity is compelled to acquire a replacement asset, the system must track the replacement to ensure proper disclosure of involuntary conversions in the financial statement notes as required by local regulations and IFRS guidance on government grants (IAS 20) for compensation receipts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R014	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Environmental and Decommissioning Obligation on Disposal	The system must track and account for environmental remediation and decommissioning obligations that are triggered or settled upon asset disposal, in accordance with IAS 37 for provisions and IFRIC 1 for changes in decommissioning obligations. For vessels being scrapped, the system must calculate the cost of hazardous material removal (asbestos, heavy metals, PCBs, ozone-depleting substances) as estimated in the decommissioning provision initially recognized at acquisition. For port equipment disposal, the system must account for any site restoration costs required by the port concession agreement (for example, returning a leased berth area at Khalifa Port or JNPT to its original condition). The system must compare the actual decommissioning costs incurred with the provision previously recognized, recording any shortfall as additional expense or any excess as provision reversal income. The decommissioning obligation must be unwound over the asset life using the discount rate established at initial recognition, with the unwinding recognized as finance cost.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R015	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Disposal Authorization and Segregation of Duties	The system must enforce strict segregation of duties throughout the disposal process to prevent fraud and unauthorized asset disposals. The following roles must be assigned to different users: disposal request initiator, asset valuation appraiser, disposal approver, disposal executor (who processes the physical removal or sale), proceeds collector (who records the cash receipt), and journal entry poster. The system must prevent any single user from performing more than two of these roles for the same disposal transaction. For high-value disposals (configurable threshold per entity, default QAR 1,000,000 or equivalent), the system must require independent valuation confirmation from a qualified external appraiser and must lock the disposal record for modification after approval. The system must log all access to disposal functions including view-only access for sensitive disposal details such as buyer identity and negotiated price, supporting the entity internal audit function and external auditor verification procedures. Any override of standard disposal controls must require system administrator intervention with documented business justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-003-R016	FEAT-039-1-003	MOD-039	Post-Disposal Asset Data Archival	The system must archive disposed asset records after a configurable retention period in the active database (default 2 years post-disposal) to maintain system performance while preserving historical data for regulatory compliance. The archival process must move the complete asset record including all transactions, depreciation history, condition assessments, attachments, and audit trail to a long-term archive storage with the same query and retrieval capabilities as the active database but with read-only access. Archived records must remain searchable through the standard asset search interface with a clear indicator that the record is archived. The archival must comply with data residency requirements for each jurisdiction: Qatar data must remain on servers within Qatar, KSA data must comply with Saudi National Data Governance Interim Regulations, UAE data must comply with DIFC or ADGM data protection rules as applicable, and India data must comply with the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023. The system must support automated archival runs on a scheduled basis with pre-archival validation that no open transactions, pending insurance claims, or unresolved tax matters exist for the assets being archived.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R001	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Policy Registry and Master Data	The system must maintain a comprehensive insurance policy registry capturing all active and historical insurance policies covering fixed assets. Each policy record must include the policy number, insurance provider name and contact details, broker name (if applicable), policy type classification (marine hull and machinery, P&I club, container all-risks, equipment all-risks, property all-risks, business interruption, third-party liability), coverage territory, policy inception and expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, total sum insured in the policy currency, annual premium amount, deductible or excess amounts, and any co-insurance arrangements with percentage splits among insurers. The system must support policies denominated in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR, GBP, and EUR. The registry must link each policy to the specific assets or asset classes it covers, supporting one-to-many and many-to-one relationships where a single policy covers multiple assets or a single asset is covered by multiple overlapping policies. Policy documents must be attached as PDF files with version control.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R002	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Vessel Hull and Machinery Insurance Tracking	The system must provide specialized tracking for vessel hull and machinery (H&M) insurance policies, which represent the highest-value insurance coverage in a container shipping operation. The H&M policy record must capture the agreed insured value of each vessel, the Institute Time Clauses (ITC) version applicable (Hulls 1983 or 2003), the trading warranty limits (geographical navigation limits beyond which coverage is void or requires additional premium), any classification society survey requirements that condition coverage, war risk and piracy extension details with geographical zones (especially relevant for Gulf of Aden, Arabian Sea, and Strait of Hormuz transits), and the lay-up return premium terms for vessels that are idle. The system must automatically flag vessels whose insured value deviates by more than a configurable percentage (default 15%) from the current net book value or last appraised market value, prompting review of insurance adequacy. For vessels with outstanding maritime liens or mortgages, the system must record the mortgagee interest endorsement details including the financing bank name and loss payee assignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R003	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Container Fleet Insurance Coverage Management	The system must manage insurance coverage for container fleets, which are typically insured under blanket container all-risks policies covering the entire fleet rather than individual units. The system must track the fleet insured quantity and total insured value, adjust these automatically as containers are added to or removed from the fleet through acquisitions and disposals, and calculate premium adjustments for fleet size changes. The policy must record coverage specifics including perils covered (physical damage, theft, mysterious disappearance, contamination), exclusions (inherent vice, ordinary wear, shipper-packed cargo damage), and geographical scope of coverage. The system must track the container damage claims process including notification to underwriters within the policy-required timeframe (typically 30 days of discovery), surveyor appointment, repair cost estimation, deductible application, and claim settlement. The system must maintain a loss ratio analysis per fleet segment showing claims incurred versus premiums paid, which is critical for annual insurance renewal negotiations. For reefer containers, the system must separately track machinery breakdown insurance covering the refrigeration unit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R004	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Premium Payment Scheduling and Tracking	The system must manage insurance premium payment schedules, supporting both annual lump-sum and installment payment plans (quarterly, semi-annual). For each premium payment, the system must record the due date, amount in policy currency, exchange rate and amount in entity functional currency, payment reference, and actual payment date. The system must generate premium payment reminders at configurable intervals before due dates (default 30, 15, 7, and 1 day). Overdue premium payments must trigger escalation alerts to the finance controller and risk manager, as late payment may result in policy cancellation. The system must allocate premium costs across the covered assets or cost centers on a pro-rata basis (by insured value, by asset count, or by risk-weighted allocation) for accurate management accounting. Premium prepayments spanning multiple accounting periods must be amortized on a straight-line basis with monthly accrual entries. The system must calculate and track premium taxes and levies applicable in each jurisdiction, such as insurance premium tax in KSA and stamp duty on insurance policies in India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R005	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Claim Filing and Settlement Tracking	The system must support the complete insurance claim lifecycle from incident notification through claim settlement or denial. The claim record must capture the incident date and time, location (port, at sea with coordinates, inland), affected asset details, nature of damage or loss, estimated claim amount, policy under which the claim is filed, deductible applicable, and claim reference number assigned by the insurer. The system must track claim status through stages: incident reported, insurer notified, surveyor appointed, surveyor report received, claim submitted, claim under review, claim approved, claim partially approved, claim denied, claim paid, and claim closed. Each status change must be timestamped and attributed to a user. For claims above a configurable threshold (default USD 100,000), the system must require attachment of a marine surveyor report, incident photographs, master mariner statement (for vessel claims), and relevant port authority reports. The system must track the time elapsed from claim submission to settlement and alert management when claims exceed the average settlement period for the insurer, supporting insurer performance evaluation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R006	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Asset Valuation Appraisal Management	The system must manage periodic asset valuations and independent appraisals required for insurance purposes, financial reporting (IAS 16 revaluation model), loan covenant compliance, and regulatory reporting. Each valuation record must capture the valuation date, appraiser name and qualifications, valuation methodology (market approach, income approach, cost approach, or combination), key assumptions (discount rate, market comparables, replacement cost indices), and the resultant fair value in the valuation currency. The system must support multiple concurrent valuations for the same asset from different appraisers for comparison purposes. For vessel valuations, the system must integrate with or accept imports from recognized vessel valuation services such as VesselsValue, Clarksons, or Braemar. Valuation results must be compared against the current insured value and the carrying amount (net book value) to identify assets that are over-insured (wasting premium) or under-insured (exposure to loss). The system must generate a valuation reconciliation report showing book value, insured value, and appraised fair value for each major asset with variance analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R007	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Coverage Gap Analysis	The system must perform automated insurance coverage gap analysis by comparing total insured values against current asset values across all asset classes and entities. The analysis must identify uninsured assets (assets in the registry with no linked insurance policy), under-insured assets (insured value less than a configurable percentage of book value or appraised value, default 80%), over-insured assets (insured value exceeding a configurable percentage of replacement value, default 120%), and policy gaps (periods where coverage lapses between policy expiry and renewal). The gap analysis must be run automatically on a monthly basis and on-demand when triggered by asset acquisitions, disposals, revaluations, or policy changes. Results must be presented in a dashboard showing coverage ratios by entity, asset class, and location with traffic-light indicators (green for adequate, amber for review needed, red for coverage gap). The system must generate a recommended action list for each identified gap, such as increasing sum insured, acquiring additional coverage, or reviewing the insured value downward to save premium costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R008	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Mandatory Insurance Compliance by Jurisdiction	The system must track compliance with mandatory insurance requirements imposed by regulatory authorities in each operating jurisdiction. For vessels, the system must verify that valid P&I club entry, war risk coverage, oil pollution liability insurance (CLC certificate), wreck removal insurance, and crew injury insurance are maintained as required by the Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority (Maritime), Saudi Ports Authority (MAWANI), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. For port operations, the system must track mandatory workmen compensation insurance, third-party liability insurance, and equipment insurance required by port concession agreements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must maintain a compliance calendar showing all mandatory insurance requirements per jurisdiction, their renewal dates, and the current compliance status. Non-compliance must trigger escalation alerts to the legal and compliance team with a countdown showing days until regulatory deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R009	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Renewal Management and Workflow	The system must manage the insurance renewal process with automated workflows triggered at a configurable lead time before policy expiry (default 90 days). The renewal workflow must include stages: renewal preparation (gathering updated asset valuations, claims history, risk assessments), market submission (distributing renewal terms to brokers or insurers), quotation receipt and comparison, management approval of selected terms, policy binding confirmation, and premium payment arrangement. The system must generate a renewal submission package containing the current policy summary, asset schedule with updated values, claims history for the past 5 years with loss ratios, and any material changes to the risk profile (new vessel acquisitions, route changes, port additions). The quotation comparison module must normalize quotes from different insurers into a comparable format showing premium rates per unit of exposure, deductible levels, coverage breadth score, and total cost of risk. The system must archive all renewal documentation and correspondence for each policy providing a complete renewal history for audit and market negotiation reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R010	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Fair Value Hierarchy and Measurement Disclosure	The system must classify and track asset valuations according to the IFRS 13 fair value hierarchy for entities applying the revaluation model under IAS 16. Level 1 valuations must be based on quoted prices in active markets for identical assets (applicable to standard container types and commodity vessels with published market indices). Level 2 valuations must be based on observable inputs other than quoted prices, such as comparable transaction prices adjusted for differences in condition, age, or specification. Level 3 valuations must use unobservable inputs such as discounted cash flow models or depreciated replacement cost approaches for specialized port equipment with no active market. The system must record the fair value hierarchy level for each valuation, the key inputs and assumptions used, and any sensitivity analysis performed for Level 3 valuations. For financial statement disclosure purposes, the system must generate the reconciliation tables required by IFRS 13 paragraphs 93-99, showing opening balance, total gains and losses (in profit or loss and in other comprehensive income), purchases, sales, transfers between levels, and closing balance for each class of assets measured at fair value.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R011	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance and Valuation Reporting in Arabic and English	The system must generate all insurance and valuation reports in both Arabic and English with proper formatting for each language. Arabic reports must use RTL layout with proper Arabic typography, Arabic numerals option (Eastern Arabic-Indic or Western Arabic as configured), and Hijri date format alongside Gregorian dates. Reports must include: insurance policy register listing all active policies with coverage details and premium summaries; claims register showing all open and closed claims with ageing analysis; valuation summary comparing book values, insured values, and appraised values across all asset classes; premium allocation report showing insurance costs by entity, asset class, and cost center; and coverage gap analysis report with risk exposure quantification. All reports must support PDF export with proper Arabic font embedding for printing, Excel export for data manipulation, and on-screen interactive viewing with drill-down from summary to policy and asset detail. Report headers must display the entity name and logo in the appropriate language and must include regulatory disclaimers required by each jurisdiction's insurance regulatory authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R012	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Co-Insurance and Reinsurance Arrangement Tracking	The system must track co-insurance and reinsurance arrangements where the risk on high-value assets is shared among multiple insurers. For co-insurance, the system must record each co-insurer's participation percentage, their share of the sum insured, their share of the premium, and their share of any claims. The lead insurer must be identified and claims communication must be routed through the lead insurer. For facultative reinsurance on specific high-value assets (such as individual vessels or STS cranes), the system must track the reinsurance treaty reference, ceding percentage, retention amount, and reinsurer details. The system must calculate the net retention exposure for the primary insured after considering all co-insurance and reinsurance layers, which is critical for the entity risk management and solvency assessment. When a claim is filed on a co-insured or reinsured asset, the system must automatically compute each party's share of the claim based on the co-insurance or reinsurance split percentages and generate individual recovery requests to each participant.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R013	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Asset Replacement Value Indexation	The system must support automatic indexation of asset replacement values using configurable inflation or cost indices to maintain insured values aligned with current replacement costs. For containers, the system must integrate with container price indices (such as the Drewry Container Census or Howe Robinson index) to adjust replacement values based on new-build container prices. For vessels, the system must reference newbuilding price indices from Clarkson Research or equivalent maritime intelligence providers. For port equipment, the system must apply manufacturer-specific or general industrial equipment cost indices. The indexation must be applied annually or upon policy renewal, and the system must allow manual override with justification for assets where the index does not accurately reflect the specific asset replacement cost. The indexed replacement values must feed into the insurance coverage gap analysis to ensure sums insured keep pace with rising replacement costs, which is particularly important in the current market environment where container prices and vessel newbuilding prices have experienced significant volatility. The system must maintain a historical record of all index adjustments applied with the source index reference and effective date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R014	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Total Cost of Risk Analysis and Dashboard	The system must calculate and present the total cost of risk (TCOR) for the organization's fixed asset portfolio, combining insurance premiums, retained losses (claims below deductibles), uninsured losses, risk management administrative costs, and loss prevention expenditure. The TCOR must be calculated per entity, per asset class, and consolidated, expressed both as an absolute amount in the reporting currency (USD) and as a ratio of total asset value. The system must present TCOR trends over time (minimum 5-year history) in an interactive dashboard with drill-down to contributing factors. The dashboard must include key risk metrics such as loss frequency (number of claims per 100 assets per year), loss severity (average claim amount), loss ratio (claims incurred divided by premiums paid), and risk-adjusted premium rate. The system must benchmark these metrics against configurable industry averages for container shipping operations in the GCC and Indian subcontinent region. The AI engine must analyze TCOR patterns and recommend risk mitigation strategies such as increasing deductibles, implementing loss prevention programs, or restructuring insurance programs to optimize the balance between premium cost and retained risk exposure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R015	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Valuation Impairment Indicator Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor for impairment indicators as defined in IAS 36 paragraph 12 for all fixed assets, triggering impairment review workflows when indicators are detected. External indicators monitored must include significant decline in asset market values (tracked through valuation feeds for vessels and containers), adverse changes in the technological, market, or economic environment (such as IMO 2020 sulfur regulations impacting non-scrubber-fitted vessel values), increases in market interest rates affecting value-in-use calculations, and situations where the entity market capitalization falls below its net assets. Internal indicators must include evidence of physical damage or obsolescence (from condition assessment data), plans to discontinue or restructure operations at specific ports, and actual economic performance of an asset falling below expectations. When an indicator is detected, the system must generate an impairment assessment task for the asset manager, pre-populated with the identified indicator details, current carrying amount, last known market value, and suggested recoverable amount calculation approach. The monitoring must run daily against external data feeds and trigger alerts in real-time for critical indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R016	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Insurance Certificate and Endorsement Management	The system must manage the issuance, tracking, and distribution of insurance certificates and endorsements required for operational and regulatory purposes. The system must generate certificates of insurance for presentation to port authorities (Hamad Port Authority, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World Jebel Ali, JNPT Port Trust) as a condition of vessel port entry and terminal operations. The system must track endorsements including additional insured endorsements (naming charterers, terminal operators, or financing banks as additional insureds), waiver of subrogation endorsements, and loss payee endorsements on financed assets. Each certificate and endorsement must be linked to the originating policy, have a defined validity period, and be automatically flagged for renewal when the underlying policy is renewed. The system must support digital distribution of certificates via email with encrypted PDF attachments and must maintain a log of all certificates issued showing the recipient, purpose, issuing date, and expiry date. For urgent operational needs such as a vessel arriving at Jebel Ali without current insurance documentation, the system must support expedited certificate generation with electronic verification capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-1-004-R017	FEAT-039-1-004	MOD-039	Offline Insurance and Valuation Data Access	The system must provide offline access to critical insurance and valuation data for users operating in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, which is common for surveyors conducting asset inspections at remote port areas, onboard vessels, or at inland container depots. The offline module must cache the insurance policy summary, coverage details, claim submission forms, asset valuation history, and contact information for insurers and brokers. Users must be able to initiate insurance claim notifications offline by capturing incident details, photographs, and preliminary damage assessments on mobile devices, with automatic submission to the insurance system when connectivity is restored. The offline data must be synchronized bidirectionally: policy updates made online must be pushed to offline devices during the next sync, and claim notifications created offline must be uploaded to the central system. Data cached on offline devices must be encrypted with AES-256 encryption and must be configurable for automatic purge after a defined period of inactivity (default 30 days) to protect sensitive insurance data on potentially lost or stolen devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R001	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Preventive Maintenance Calendar per Asset	The system must generate and manage a preventive maintenance calendar for every registered fixed asset, including quay cranes (STS), rubber-tyred gantry cranes (RTG), ship-to-shore cranes, and reefer monitoring units at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), and JNPT (INNPT). Each calendar entry must specify the maintenance type, estimated duration, required parts, assigned technician team, and cost estimate in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The calendar must support both Gregorian and Hijri date formats for scheduling in KSA and Qatar entities, and allow recurrence rules (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual) based on manufacturer recommendations or operational wear metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R002	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Corrective Maintenance Work Order Linkage	The system must allow creation of corrective maintenance work orders directly linked to a fixed asset record when unplanned breakdowns occur. Each work order must capture the asset tag, fault description, priority level (critical/high/medium/low), root cause classification, downtime hours, and actual repair cost in the local entity currency. For port-side equipment such as container spreaders and terminal tractors operating at Jebel Ali or JNPT, the system must flag any corrective maintenance exceeding 5% of the asset's net book value as a potential impairment trigger. Work orders must support attachment of photographic evidence and Arabic-language fault descriptions with RTL text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R003	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	AI-Predicted Maintenance Scheduling	The system must leverage AI/ML models to predict optimal maintenance windows for high-value container shipping assets based on historical breakdown patterns, utilization rates, environmental conditions (humidity, salinity levels at Gulf ports), and manufacturer lifecycle data. Predictions must be generated at least 30 days in advance with a confidence score. The AI engine must consider port operational schedules to avoid scheduling maintenance during peak vessel berthing windows at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali. Predicted maintenance events must be automatically converted into draft work orders with estimated costs in the asset's base currency, and the AI model must achieve at least 85% prediction accuracy within 12 months of deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R004	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Maintenance Cost Capitalization vs Expense Decision	The system must provide an AI-assisted decision engine to classify each maintenance expenditure as either a capitalizable improvement (increasing the asset's useful life or capacity) or a period expense under IAS 16 and local GAAP requirements for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Major overhauls of ship-to-shore cranes that extend useful life by more than 12 months must be flagged for capitalization review. The decision engine must consider the maintenance cost relative to the asset's original cost, remaining useful life, and net book value. All classification decisions must generate an audit trail with the AI confidence score, the applicable accounting standard reference, and must support override by authorized finance personnel with mandatory justification in English or Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R007	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Equipment Downtime Impact on Depreciation Calculation	The system must track equipment downtime hours resulting from maintenance activities and assess the impact on depreciation calculations under the units-of-production method where applicable. For container handling equipment at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali that uses activity-based depreciation (e.g., crane moves per year), extended downtime periods exceeding 30 consecutive days must trigger a review of the depreciation rate. The system must automatically recalculate the remaining useful life and adjusted depreciation schedule when significant downtime is recorded. All adjustments must be logged with the maintenance work order reference, the original and revised depreciation parameters, and must comply with IAS 16 paragraph 55 regarding changes in depreciation method reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R008	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Maintenance Compliance and Regulatory Certification Tracking	The system must track mandatory regulatory maintenance certifications and inspection schedules for port equipment as required by Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi GACA/port authorities, and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. Each asset must maintain a compliance register with certification expiry dates, inspection results (pass/fail/conditional), and remediation action items. The system must generate automated alerts 60, 30, and 7 days before certification expiry. Expired certifications must trigger an automatic operational hold flag on the asset preventing its deployment in vessel operations. All compliance documentation must support bilingual storage in English and Arabic with digital signature verification for regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R009	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Maintenance History and Lifecycle Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive maintenance lifecycle analytics dashboard for each fixed asset showing total maintenance cost over the asset's life, mean time between failures (MTBF), mean time to repair (MTTR), maintenance cost as a percentage of asset value, and remaining economic life projections. The dashboard must support filtering by port location (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), asset category, maintenance type (preventive vs corrective), and time period. AI-generated insights must highlight assets approaching end-of-life or those where cumulative maintenance costs suggest replacement is more economical than continued repair. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with currency amounts displayed in the selected entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R010	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Warranty Tracking and Claim Management for Assets	The system must maintain warranty records for all fixed assets including warranty start date, expiry date, coverage scope (parts only, parts and labor, comprehensive), and vendor warranty terms. When a maintenance work order is raised for an asset under active warranty at any port facility, the system must automatically flag the work order for warranty claim processing and prevent internal cost allocation until the warranty claim is resolved. Warranty claims must be tracked in the vendor's invoice currency (commonly USD or EUR) with automatic conversion to the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using the exchange rate on the claim date. The system must generate warranty utilization reports showing claims submitted, approved, rejected, and pending per vendor and asset category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R011	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Offline Maintenance Data Capture for Remote Port Locations	The system must support offline data capture for maintenance activities performed at remote port locations or anchorage areas where network connectivity may be intermittent. Maintenance technicians at Hamad Port outer berths or JNPT satellite yards must be able to record work order completion details, parts consumption, labor hours, and asset condition assessments on mobile devices without active internet connectivity. Upon network restoration, all offline records must automatically synchronize with the central system with conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recent update. Offline-captured data must include device timestamps and GPS coordinates to verify the maintenance location. The offline module must support both English and Arabic language interfaces for field technicians.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R012	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Vendor Service Level Agreement Monitoring for Maintenance Contracts	The system must track and monitor vendor service level agreements (SLAs) for outsourced maintenance contracts covering port equipment. Each SLA must define response time targets, resolution time targets, penalties for non-compliance, and performance metrics specific to container shipping equipment such as crane availability percentages and reefer unit uptime guarantees. The system must automatically calculate SLA adherence rates per vendor per asset category and generate penalty calculation reports when SLA breaches occur. SLA contracts must support multi-currency terms where the vendor invoices in USD but penalties are calculated in the local entity currency. Automated notifications must alert procurement and operations teams when vendor SLA compliance drops below 90% in any rolling 30-day period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R013	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Condition-Based Monitoring Integration for Critical Assets	The system must integrate with IoT sensor platforms and condition-based monitoring systems installed on critical container terminal assets such as STS cranes, RTG cranes, and automated guided vehicles (AGVs) at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali. Real-time sensor data including vibration levels, temperature readings, hydraulic pressure, and electrical load patterns must be ingested and correlated with asset maintenance records. When sensor readings exceed predefined thresholds, the system must automatically generate a condition-based maintenance alert and create a draft corrective work order with AI-recommended repair actions. Sensor data must be stored with the asset record for trend analysis, and the integration must handle data volumes of at least 10,000 sensor readings per asset per day without performance degradation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R014	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Maintenance Impact on Asset Insurance Valuation	The system must track the relationship between maintenance history and asset insurance valuations for port infrastructure and equipment. Well-maintained assets with complete preventive maintenance records must be flagged for favorable insurance premium negotiations, while assets with excessive corrective maintenance or deferred maintenance backlogs must trigger insurance risk alerts. The system must generate annual maintenance compliance certificates per asset for submission to marine and port equipment insurers. Insurance valuations must be maintained in the policy currency (typically USD) with cross-reference to the asset's net book value in local currency. The system must support attachment of third-party inspection reports and maintenance certifications required by P&I clubs and hull and machinery insurers for vessel-related port equipment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R015	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Multi-Entity Maintenance Cost Allocation and Intercompany Charging	The system must support maintenance cost allocation across multiple legal entities when shared equipment serves operations spanning different jurisdictions. For example, a specialized crane deployed temporarily from Jebel Ali (UAE entity) to Hamad Port (Qatar entity) must have its maintenance costs allocated proportionally based on utilization days at each location. Intercompany maintenance charges must be generated with proper transfer pricing documentation in compliance with OECD guidelines and local tax authority requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Cross-entity charges must be recorded in both the charging and receiving entity's local currency with the applicable intercompany exchange rate. All intercompany maintenance transactions must generate elimination entries for consolidated financial reporting in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-001-R016	FEAT-039-2-001	MOD-039	Environmental Compliance Maintenance for Port Equipment	The system must track maintenance activities related to environmental compliance for port equipment, including emissions testing for diesel-powered yard equipment, oil spill prevention system inspections on quay cranes, and noise level compliance checks. Each environmental maintenance task must reference the applicable regulation such as Qatar National Environmental Standards, UAE Federal Environment Agency requirements, Saudi Environmental Regulations, or Indian Environmental Protection Act provisions. The system must maintain a separate environmental maintenance register per port facility with automated scheduling based on regulatory inspection frequencies. Non-compliance findings must trigger escalation workflows to the entity's HSE department and must be trackable through to remediation completion with photographic evidence and regulatory sign-off documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R001	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	AI-Driven CAPEX/OPEX Auto-Classification Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven classification engine that automatically categorizes expenditure requests as CAPEX or OPEX based on the nature of the expenditure, amount thresholds, asset impact assessment, and applicable accounting standards. The engine must analyze expenditure descriptions (in English or Arabic), vendor quotes, and historical classification patterns to recommend the appropriate treatment. For container shipping specific expenditures such as crane rail replacements, reefer plug installations, berth dredging, and container yard resurfacing, the AI must reference IAS 16 paragraphs 7-22 for recognition criteria. The classification confidence score must be displayed, and recommendations below 90% confidence must be routed to a senior accountant for manual review. The engine must support entity-specific materiality thresholds: QAR 50,000 for Qatar, AED 50,000 for UAE, SAR 50,000 for KSA, and INR 500,000 for India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R002	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Materiality Threshold Configuration per Entity and Currency	The system must allow configuration of CAPEX materiality thresholds per legal entity in the entity's local currency. Expenditures below the threshold must be automatically classified as OPEX regardless of their nature. Thresholds must be configurable separately for different asset categories such as port infrastructure (higher threshold), IT equipment (lower threshold), and marine equipment (medium threshold). The system must support annual threshold reviews with effective dating so that historical classifications remain valid under the thresholds in effect at the time of classification. When a single expenditure is split across multiple entities (e.g., a shared IT system for Hamad Port and Jebel Ali), each entity's portion must be evaluated against its own materiality threshold independently. All threshold amounts must be stored in local currency with USD equivalent for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R003	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Component Approach Classification under IAS 16	The system must support the component approach to asset recognition under IAS 16 paragraph 13, allowing major components of a single asset to be classified and depreciated separately. For a ship-to-shore crane at Jebel Ali or Hamad Port, the system must enable separate classification of the boom assembly, trolley mechanism, electrical systems, spreader unit, and civil foundation as individual components with distinct useful lives and depreciation rates. When a component replacement occurs, the system must automatically derecognize the old component's carrying amount and capitalize the new component as CAPEX. The AI engine must recommend component-level classification when the replacement cost exceeds 15% of the total asset's net book value. All component classifications must maintain parent-child relationships with the master asset record across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R004	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Subsequent Expenditure Evaluation Workflow	The system must implement a structured workflow for evaluating subsequent expenditures on existing fixed assets to determine CAPEX or OPEX treatment. The workflow must capture whether the expenditure increases the asset's future economic benefits beyond its originally assessed standard of performance as required by IAS 16 paragraph 10. For container shipping assets, the evaluation criteria must include: increased container handling capacity (moves per hour), extended useful life beyond original estimate, improved safety ratings, enhanced energy efficiency, or ability to handle new container types (e.g., upgrading a yard crane to handle 45-foot containers). The workflow must route through entity-specific approval hierarchies with a minimum of three approval levels for CAPEX classifications exceeding the entity's threshold by more than 500%. All workflow steps must support Arabic and English language comments and attachments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R005	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	CAPEX Budget Control and Commitment Tracking	The system must integrate CAPEX classification with the capital budgeting module to enforce budget availability checks before any CAPEX classification is finalized. When an expenditure is classified as CAPEX, the system must verify that sufficient budget exists in the relevant CAPEX budget line for the entity and fiscal period. Budget checks must account for existing commitments including approved purchase orders, contracts in progress, and pending CAPEX approvals. The system must support CAPEX budgets in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic consolidation in USD. Budget overruns exceeding 10% must trigger escalation to the CFO level. The system must generate CAPEX commitment reports showing approved budget, committed amount, actual spend, and remaining balance per asset category per entity, with Hijri date support for KSA and Qatar fiscal period reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R004	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Per-Diem Rate Variance Detection and Resolution	The system must detect per-diem rate discrepancies where the lessor-billed daily rate differs from the contractually agreed rate stored in the lease master agreement. Detection must account for rate tiers based on container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, open-top, flat-rack), rate step-ups triggered by lease duration milestones, and promotional rate periods. The system must also detect billing for days beyond the contractual off-hire date as recorded in the off-hire survey or redelivery confirmation. All detected variances must generate a dispute record with supporting documentation automatically attached for lessor negotiation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R006	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Tax Treatment Differentiation for CAPEX and OPEX	The system must automatically apply the correct tax treatment based on the CAPEX or OPEX classification for each entity's jurisdiction. In UAE, where corporate tax was introduced in 2023, CAPEX items must follow the capital allowance schedules prescribed by the Federal Tax Authority. In KSA, the system must apply ZATCA depreciation rules for CAPEX items which may differ from IFRS depreciation. In India, the system must distinguish between Section 32 depreciation rates for tax purposes and accounting depreciation under Ind AS 16 for CAPEX items. In Qatar, the system must apply the Qatar Tax Authority's capital expenditure deduction rules. OPEX items must be immediately deductible in the period incurred under all four jurisdictions' tax laws. The system must maintain parallel CAPEX registers for accounting and tax purposes where the treatment diverges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R007	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Reclassification from OPEX to CAPEX and Vice Versa	The system must support reclassification of expenditures from OPEX to CAPEX or vice versa with a complete audit trail. Reclassification scenarios include: initial misclassification discovered during audit, scope changes in a project that convert a repair (OPEX) into an upgrade (CAPEX), or regulatory changes that affect classification criteria. The reclassification process must reverse the original accounting entries and generate new entries under the correct classification. For multi-currency scenarios where the original entry was in AED and the reclassification occurs in a later period, the system must use the original transaction exchange rate for the reversal and the current rate for the new entry, with any exchange difference recognized appropriately. Reclassifications must require approval from at least the entity's finance controller and must be flagged in period-end reporting for auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R008	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Project-Based CAPEX Accumulation and Asset Conversion	The system must support accumulation of multiple expenditures under a CAPEX project and convert the accumulated costs into a fixed asset upon project completion. For container terminal expansion projects at Khalifa Port or new berth construction at JNPT, the system must track all project-related costs including engineering, procurement, construction, supervision, and borrowing costs eligible for capitalization under IAS 23. Each cost element must be classified individually but accumulated under the project's capital work-in-progress (CWIP) account. The system must enforce a maximum CWIP duration policy (configurable per entity, default 36 months) after which the AI must flag the project for management review. Upon conversion from CWIP to a fixed asset, the system must automatically assign an asset tag, determine the depreciation start date, and allocate the total cost to asset components as applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R009	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Borrowing Cost Capitalization for Qualifying CAPEX Assets	The system must automatically identify qualifying assets under IAS 23 and capitalize borrowing costs incurred during the construction or acquisition period. For major port infrastructure CAPEX projects such as new terminal buildings, quay wall extensions, or container yard expansions, the system must calculate the capitalization rate based on the weighted average borrowing rate of the entity's outstanding loans. Borrowing costs must be tracked in the loan currency and converted to the asset's recording currency using the average exchange rate during the capitalization period. The system must cease capitalization when the asset is ready for its intended use and must handle suspension of capitalization during extended periods of interrupted construction. For multi-entity financed projects, borrowing costs from a parent company loan in USD must be allocated proportionally to the subsidiary entities in their local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R010	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	CAPEX Approval Workflow with Delegation of Authority Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable delegation of authority matrix for CAPEX approvals that varies by entity, expenditure amount, and asset category. The approval matrix must support at least five approval tiers: department manager, operations director, entity CFO, entity CEO, and group board. Approval thresholds must be configurable in local currency per entity, for example: up to QAR 100,000 requires department manager approval, QAR 100,001 to QAR 500,000 requires operations director, and above QAR 500,000 requires CFO approval at the Qatar entity. The system must support proxy approvers during absence periods, conditional approvals with spending caps, and emergency fast-track approval paths for critical port equipment failures. All approval actions must be captured with timestamps, approver identity, and comments in English or Arabic, and the workflow must function in offline mode with synchronization upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R011	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	CAPEX vs OPEX Classification Audit Report	The system must generate comprehensive audit reports detailing all CAPEX and OPEX classifications made during a reporting period. The report must include: the original expenditure description, amount in local and USD currency, AI classification recommendation, AI confidence score, final classification decision, approver details, applicable accounting standard reference (IAS 16 paragraph number), and any reclassifications that occurred. The report must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port location (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), asset category, classification type, and date range. Summary statistics must show total CAPEX and OPEX amounts with year-over-year comparison. The report must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with support for Arabic language output including RTL column ordering and Hijri date display for KSA and Qatar entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R012	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Lease vs Buy Analysis for CAPEX Decision Support	The system must provide an AI-powered lease-versus-buy analysis tool to support CAPEX decision-making for major container shipping equipment acquisitions. For assets such as new RTG cranes, reach stackers, or terminal tractors, the tool must calculate and compare the total cost of ownership under outright purchase (CAPEX), operating lease (OPEX), and finance lease (CAPEX under IFRS 16) scenarios. The analysis must consider entity-specific tax benefits, borrowing costs, residual values, maintenance obligations, and technology obsolescence risk for each scenario. Calculations must be performed in the entity's local currency with sensitivity analysis for exchange rate fluctuations against USD. The output must include net present value, internal rate of return, and payback period for each option, formatted in both English and Arabic with proper number formatting per locale conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R001	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Rate Master Configuration	The system must maintain a comprehensive THC rate master that stores charges per container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR, 20TK, 40TK) across all four terminal locations (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA). Each rate entry must support effective date ranges using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, currency denomination in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD, and must be associated with specific terminal operator codes. Rate records must include approval workflow status tracking and audit trail of all modifications with timestamps and user identifiers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R013	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Regulatory Compliance Validation for CAPEX Classifications	The system must validate CAPEX classifications against entity-specific regulatory requirements before finalization. In Qatar, CAPEX classifications for port infrastructure must comply with Qatar Financial Markets Authority reporting requirements. In UAE, classifications must align with UAE IFRS adoption requirements and free zone authority regulations applicable to Jebel Ali Free Zone and Khalifa Industrial Zone. In KSA, classifications must comply with SOCPA standards and Vision 2030 industrial investment reporting requirements. In India, classifications must satisfy Companies Act 2013 Schedule II requirements and Ind AS 16 provisions which may differ from full IFRS. The validation engine must flag any classification that conflicts with jurisdiction-specific rules and provide the specific regulatory reference and recommended corrective action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R014	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	CAPEX Return on Investment Tracking Post-Completion	The system must track the actual return on investment (ROI) for completed CAPEX projects against the projected ROI submitted during the approval process. For container terminal CAPEX investments such as new crane installations at Hamad Port or yard expansion at JNPT, the system must monitor actual throughput improvements, cost savings, revenue increases, and operational efficiency gains post-commissioning. Variance analysis between projected and actual ROI must be generated at 6-month, 12-month, and 24-month intervals after asset commissioning. The system must use AI to identify CAPEX projects that are significantly underperforming their projected returns (below 70% of target) and generate alerts to the entity finance team. All ROI calculations must be in the entity's local currency with USD consolidated views, and historical exchange rate adjustments must be applied for fair comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R015	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Intercompany CAPEX Transfer and Recharge Mechanism	The system must handle intercompany CAPEX transfers when assets are initially acquired by one entity and subsequently transferred or recharged to another entity within the group. For example, when the UAE entity procures a specialized container scanner in USD at Jebel Ali and deploys it at Hamad Port under the Qatar entity, the system must manage the intercompany recharge at arm's length pricing in compliance with transfer pricing regulations in both jurisdictions. The CAPEX classification must be evaluated at the receiving entity level against its materiality threshold and local accounting standards. The system must generate intercompany invoices in the appropriate currencies, record the transfer with proper derecognition at the sending entity and recognition at the receiving entity, and create elimination entries for group consolidation. Transfer pricing documentation must be automatically generated and stored for regulatory examination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R016	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	CAPEX Forecast and Pipeline Management Dashboard	The system must provide a CAPEX pipeline management dashboard showing all planned, approved, in-progress, and completed capital expenditure projects across all four entities. The dashboard must display project status, budget utilization, timeline adherence, and AI-predicted completion dates for in-progress projects. CAPEX forecasts must be presented in local currency and USD with monthly and quarterly breakdowns. The pipeline must categorize projects by strategic alignment (capacity expansion, replacement, compliance, efficiency improvement) and by port location. AI-generated recommendations must highlight optimal timing for CAPEX execution based on currency exchange rate trends, equipment market conditions, and port operational calendars. The dashboard must support both English and Arabic rendering with proper RTL layout, and must allow drill-down from summary level to individual expenditure line items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-002-R017	FEAT-039-2-002	MOD-039	Government Grant and Subsidy Treatment for CAPEX	The system must handle government grants and subsidies received for CAPEX projects in accordance with IAS 20. In KSA, industrial development grants under Vision 2030 for port infrastructure CAPEX must be accounted for using either the deferred income approach or the deduction from asset cost approach as selected by the entity's accounting policy. In India, government incentives under the Sagarmala port modernization program for JNPT-related CAPEX must be tracked separately with conditions monitoring. The system must track grant conditions, compliance milestones, and clawback provisions. Grant amounts must be recorded in the grant currency with translation to the entity's local currency. When grant conditions are not met and repayment is required, the system must reverse the original grant accounting and recalculate the affected asset's depreciation from the original capitalization date with appropriate catch-up adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R001	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Impairment Indicator Monitoring and AI Detection	The system must continuously monitor for impairment indicators as defined in IAS 36 paragraphs 12-14 for all fixed assets across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT entities. External indicators must include: significant decline in asset market value, adverse changes in the shipping industry (container volume drops exceeding 15%), changes in regulatory environment (new port licensing requirements), and increases in market interest rates affecting discount rates. Internal indicators must include: physical damage or obsolescence, significant underperformance versus budget, and changes in the manner or extent of asset use. The AI engine must analyze operational data, market feeds, and financial metrics to automatically flag assets with potential impairment indicators, scoring each indicator's severity on a 1-10 scale. Flagged assets must be queued for formal impairment testing with the identified indicator documented in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R002	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Cash-Generating Unit Definition and Asset Allocation	The system must support the definition and management of cash-generating units (CGUs) as required by IAS 36 paragraph 66. For container shipping operations, CGUs must be definable at various levels including individual terminal, berth cluster, port facility, or trade lane. The system must allow allocation of individual assets and shared corporate assets to CGUs with configurable allocation keys such as throughput volume, revenue contribution, or asset utilization rates. At Jebel Ali, separate CGUs might be defined for Container Terminal 1, Container Terminal 2, and the inland container depot. The system must validate that every depreciable asset is assigned to at least one CGU and must prevent orphaned assets. CGU definitions must be reviewable annually with change history maintained. The multi-entity structure must support cross-border CGU definitions where operations span Qatar and UAE entities, with carrying amounts consolidated in USD for testing purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R003	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Value-in-Use Calculation with Discounted Cash Flow Model	The system must calculate value-in-use for assets and CGUs using a discounted cash flow (DCF) model compliant with IAS 36 paragraphs 30-57. The model must project future cash flows for a maximum of five years based on management-approved budgets, with a terminal value calculation using a long-term growth rate not exceeding the industry average for container shipping. Cash flow projections must account for container volume forecasts per port, expected revenue per TEU, operating cost escalation rates, and planned maintenance expenditures. The discount rate must be a pre-tax rate reflecting current market assessments of the time value of money and asset-specific risks, calculated using the weighted average cost of capital adjusted for country risk premiums applicable to Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. All DCF calculations must be performed in the CGU's functional currency with sensitivity analysis showing impairment outcomes at discount rate variations of plus or minus 1% and 2%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R004	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Fair Value Less Costs of Disposal Determination	The system must support fair value less costs of disposal (FVLCD) measurement as an alternative to value-in-use under IAS 36. For container shipping assets, the system must maintain a fair value hierarchy: Level 1 using observable market prices from equipment auction databases and broker valuations for standard equipment like terminal tractors and reach stackers, Level 2 using adjusted comparable transactions for specialized equipment like STS cranes considering age, capacity, and condition adjustments, and Level 3 using income or cost approaches for unique port infrastructure with no observable market. Costs of disposal must include estimated dismantling costs, transportation costs for equipment relocation, and broker or agent fees. The system must store fair value assessments per asset in the valuation currency (typically USD for marine equipment) with conversion to the entity's local currency at the assessment date exchange rate. External valuation reports must be attachable and linked to specific assets or CGUs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R005	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Annual Impairment Test Scheduling and Execution	The system must enforce annual impairment testing schedules for goodwill and intangible assets with indefinite useful lives as required by IAS 36, and trigger-based testing for all other fixed assets. The annual test must be schedulable on a fixed date per entity, supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates for KSA and Qatar year-ends. The testing workflow must include: CGU carrying amount compilation, recoverable amount calculation (higher of value-in-use and FVLCD), comparison and impairment determination, management review and approval, and journal entry generation. The system must support batch processing of impairment tests across all CGUs within an entity and provide a consolidated impairment testing dashboard across all four entities. Test results must be stored with full supporting documentation for external auditor review, and the system must generate the IAS 36 disclosure requirements automatically including key assumptions, sensitivity analyses, and methodology descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R006	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Impairment Loss Recognition and Allocation to Assets	When an impairment loss is identified for a CGU, the system must allocate the loss to the assets within the CGU in the order prescribed by IAS 36 paragraph 104: first to reduce the carrying amount of any goodwill allocated to the CGU, then to the other assets of the CGU pro rata based on carrying amounts. The system must enforce the constraint that no asset is written down below the highest of its fair value less costs of disposal, its value in use, or zero. Impairment loss journal entries must be generated automatically in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with the corresponding debit to impairment loss expense and credit to accumulated impairment. For assets measured under the revaluation model, the impairment must first reduce the revaluation surplus before being recognized in profit or loss. All impairment entries must include the IAS 36 reference, CGU identification, calculation methodology, and approver details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R007	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Impairment Loss Reversal Processing	The system must support reversal of previously recognized impairment losses (except for goodwill) when indicators suggest the impairment may no longer exist or may have decreased, as required by IAS 36 paragraphs 109-125. The reversal must not increase the asset's carrying amount above the depreciated historical cost that would have been determined had no impairment loss been recognized. The system must maintain a shadow depreciation schedule showing the theoretical carrying amount without impairment to enforce this ceiling. Reversal journal entries must credit impairment loss recovery in profit or loss, or for revalued assets, increase the revaluation surplus. The AI engine must flag assets with improving performance metrics, increased utilization, or market value recovery at ports where container volumes have rebounded, as candidates for impairment reversal review. All reversals must require finance controller approval and must be documented with the changed circumstances justifying the reversal in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R008	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Goodwill Impairment Testing for Acquired Port Operations	The system must handle goodwill impairment testing for port operations acquired through business combinations under IFRS 3. Goodwill from acquisitions such as terminal operating concessions at Khalifa Port or container freight station operations at JNPT must be allocated to the CGU or group of CGUs expected to benefit from the synergies of the combination. The system must enforce annual goodwill impairment testing regardless of impairment indicators. The goodwill impairment test must be performed before the impairment test for the CGU to which it is allocated. The system must handle scenarios where goodwill is allocated to multiple CGUs across different entities and currencies, requiring translation to a common currency (USD) for comparison. Any goodwill impairment loss recognized must be irreversible, and the system must prevent attempts to reverse goodwill impairments with appropriate validation messages displayed in the user's selected language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R009	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Corporate Asset Allocation to CGUs for Impairment Testing	The system must handle the allocation of corporate assets that do not generate independent cash flows to CGUs for impairment testing purposes as required by IAS 36 paragraphs 100-103. Corporate assets in a container shipping context include shared IT infrastructure, group headquarters buildings, enterprise software platforms, and centralized logistics management systems. The system must support allocation methods including revenue-based, asset-based, headcount-based, and custom allocation keys. When a corporate asset cannot be allocated on a reasonable and consistent basis to individual CGUs, the system must support the bottom-up and top-down testing approach described in IAS 36 paragraph 102. Allocation keys must be reviewable and adjustable annually with proper audit trails. The system must handle corporate assets denominated in one currency (e.g., USD) being allocated to CGUs operating in different currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with appropriate translation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R010	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Discount Rate Determination and Country Risk Premium Calculation	The system must provide tools for determining the appropriate pre-tax discount rate for value-in-use calculations as required by IAS 36 paragraph 55. The system must calculate entity-specific weighted average cost of capital (WACC) incorporating: risk-free rate based on government bond yields for each jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), equity risk premium using industry beta for container shipping and logistics, country risk premium for each entity's operating jurisdiction, size premium for smaller operating entities, and asset-specific risk adjustments. The system must store discount rate components with source references and effective dates. AI-assisted rate recommendations must consider current market conditions and provide a defensible rate range. The system must support scenario testing with different discount rates and display the impairment sensitivity in both local currency and USD. Rate calculations must be auditable with all assumptions documented for external auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R011	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Impairment Impact on Depreciation Recalculation	The system must automatically recalculate depreciation schedules for impaired assets from the period following the impairment recognition date. The revised depreciation must spread the impaired carrying amount over the remaining useful life of the asset, which itself may be reassessed at the time of impairment. For a quay crane at Hamad Port impaired from QAR 15 million carrying amount to QAR 10 million recoverable amount with 10 years remaining life, the system must automatically adjust the annual depreciation from QAR 1.5 million to QAR 1 million from the next depreciation period. If the impairment is subsequently reversed, the system must recalculate depreciation based on the reversed carrying amount limited by the shadow depreciation ceiling. All depreciation adjustments must flow through to the entity's general ledger, tax depreciation registers (where different from accounting depreciation), and fixed asset subledger simultaneously with proper currency conversion for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R012	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	IAS 36 Disclosure Report Generation	The system must automatically generate IAS 36 disclosure reports for inclusion in the financial statements of each entity and the consolidated group. Required disclosures must include: the amount of impairment losses recognized in profit or loss and the line items in which they are included, the amount of reversals of impairment losses recognized in profit or loss, the events and circumstances leading to recognition or reversal, and for material CGUs with allocated goodwill, the carrying amount, recoverable amount basis, key assumptions, discount rate, and growth rate used. The system must generate sensitivity disclosures showing how reasonably possible changes in key assumptions would affect the impairment conclusion. All disclosure reports must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting, currency formatting per locale (e.g., QAR uses Arabic-Indic numerals in Arabic reports), and must support both Gregorian and Hijri date reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R013	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Multi-Currency Impairment Testing and Translation Effects	The system must handle multi-currency complexities in impairment testing for a multinational container shipping group. When a CGU's functional currency differs from the parent's presentation currency (USD), impairment testing must be performed in the CGU's functional currency first. The system must address scenarios where: cash flows are generated in multiple currencies (e.g., a Jebel Ali terminal earning revenue in AED and USD), the discount rate components are sourced from different currency markets, and assets within a CGU are recorded in different currencies due to foreign-sourced equipment purchases. Translation of impairment results for consolidated reporting must follow IAS 21 requirements using closing rates. The system must track exchange rate movements that may create apparent impairment or reversal triggers in the presentation currency and distinguish these from genuine economic impairment indicators requiring action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R014	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Impairment Testing Audit Trail and Approval Workflow	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every impairment test performed, capturing all inputs, assumptions, calculations, and conclusions. The audit trail must include: the impairment indicator that triggered the test, the CGU definition and asset composition, carrying amount calculations with supporting schedules, recoverable amount determination methodology and key assumptions, sensitivity analysis results, the impairment conclusion (loss amount or no impairment), all reviewer comments and approver actions, and the date and identity of each participant. The approval workflow must require sign-off from the entity finance controller, entity CFO, and group financial reporting manager before impairment journal entries are posted. The system must support attaching external valuation reports, market data evidence, and board resolution documents. All audit trail records must be immutable once the impairment test is finalized and must be accessible for external auditor review for a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R015	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Asset-Specific vs CGU-Level Impairment Testing Logic	The system must correctly determine whether impairment testing should be performed at the individual asset level or the CGU level based on IAS 36 requirements. An individual asset must be tested independently when it generates largely independent cash inflows, such as a container freight station leased to a third party at JNPT generating separate rental income. Assets that do not generate independent cash flows must be tested as part of their assigned CGU. The system must validate CGU assignment consistency and flag any asset that should be individually tested but is currently assigned to a CGU, or vice versa. When an individually tested asset shows impairment but its CGU does not, the system must recognize the individual asset impairment. The testing logic must handle assets shared across CGUs within the same entity and across entities, ensuring no double-counting of carrying amounts or cash flows in the multi-entity structure across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R016	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Scenario Modeling and What-If Analysis for Impairment	The system must provide scenario modeling capabilities allowing finance teams to perform what-if analyses on impairment outcomes before finalizing test results. Scenarios must include: optimistic and pessimistic container volume forecasts for each port, different discount rate assumptions reflecting varying economic conditions in Gulf and Indian markets, changes in terminal concession terms affecting cash flow projections, and impact of planned CAPEX on future cash generation capacity. Each scenario must calculate the resulting recoverable amount and impairment impact in the CGU's functional currency with automatic translation to USD for group-level analysis. The system must allow side-by-side comparison of up to five scenarios simultaneously. AI-generated probability weightings for each scenario must be available to support expected value calculations. Scenario results must be presentable in board-ready reports formatted in both English and Arabic with graphical representations of impairment sensitivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-003-R017	FEAT-039-2-003	MOD-039	Interim Impairment Testing Trigger for Significant Events	The system must support ad-hoc impairment testing triggered by significant events between annual testing dates. Triggering events in a container shipping context include: loss of a major shipping line customer representing more than 20% of terminal throughput, natural disasters or force majeure events affecting port operations (e.g., severe storms at Hamad Port), government regulatory changes such as new port concession terms or tariff revisions, significant unexpected equipment failure rendering major assets non-operational, and geopolitical events affecting trade routes through the Gulf region. When a trigger event is recorded, the system must automatically identify all potentially affected CGUs across all entities, initiate the impairment testing workflow with expedited timelines, and require management certification that all material trigger events have been considered. Interim test results must be reconciled with the next annual test to ensure consistency. All trigger event documentation must support Arabic and English language entry with Hijri and Gregorian date recording.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R001	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Contract Identification and IFRS 16 Scope Assessment	The system must provide an AI-assisted tool to evaluate whether a contract contains a lease under IFRS 16 paragraphs 9-11. For container shipping contracts, the system must analyze whether the contract conveys the right to control the use of an identified asset for a period of time in exchange for consideration. Specific contract types requiring evaluation include: port concession agreements at Hamad Port and Khalifa Port, terminal operating licenses, vessel time charter agreements, container chassis rental agreements, warehouse and office leases, and IT equipment rental contracts. The AI must assess the three criteria: identified asset existence, right to obtain substantially all economic benefits, and right to direct the use of the asset. Contracts must be uploadable in PDF format in English or Arabic, and the AI must extract key terms and provide a lease identification recommendation with confidence scoring. Low-confidence assessments (below 85%) must be routed to IFRS technical accounting specialists for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R002	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Classification as Finance or Operating for Lessor Accounting	For entities acting as lessors (e.g., sub-leasing container yard space at Jebel Ali to third-party logistics providers), the system must classify leases as either finance or operating leases per IFRS 16 paragraphs 61-66. The classification must assess whether the lease transfers substantially all risks and rewards of ownership by evaluating: whether the lease transfers ownership at end of term, whether there is a bargain purchase option, whether the lease term covers the major part of the asset's economic life, whether the present value of minimum lease payments approximates the asset's fair value, and whether the asset is specialized. The system must generate the appropriate accounting entries for each classification: for finance leases, derecognize the asset and recognize a lease receivable; for operating leases, continue recognizing the asset and record lease income on a straight-line basis. All calculations must support multi-currency where lease payments are in a different currency from the underlying asset's recording currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R003	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Right-of-Use Asset Initial Measurement and Recognition	The system must calculate the initial measurement of right-of-use assets in accordance with IFRS 16 paragraph 23. The ROU asset cost must comprise: the initial lease liability amount, any lease payments made at or before the commencement date less lease incentives received, any initial direct costs incurred by the lessee, and an estimate of dismantling or restoration costs. For port concession agreements at Hamad Port or Khalifa Port, restoration costs for returning the terminal to its original condition at concession end must be estimated and included. The system must handle complex initial measurement scenarios including: advance rent payments in one currency (USD) with ongoing payments in another (QAR), lease incentives provided as fit-out contributions by the lessor, and initial direct costs such as legal fees and commissions spread across multiple entities. All ROU assets must be automatically assigned asset tags, linked to the originating lease contract, and recorded in the lessee entity's functional currency with the exchange rate at the commencement date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R004	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Liability Initial Measurement with Present Value Calculation	The system must calculate the initial lease liability as the present value of lease payments not yet paid at the commencement date, discounted using the interest rate implicit in the lease or the lessee's incremental borrowing rate if the implicit rate is not determinable, per IFRS 16 paragraph 26. Lease payments must include: fixed payments less lease incentives, variable payments dependent on an index or rate (e.g., CPI-linked escalations common in Gulf port concessions), amounts expected to be payable under residual value guarantees, exercise price of purchase options reasonably certain to be exercised, and termination penalties if the lease term reflects exercise of a termination option. The system must support payment schedules with varying frequencies (monthly, quarterly, semi-annual, annual), multiple currencies within a single lease, and stepped payment structures common in long-term port concession agreements. The incremental borrowing rate must be determinable per entity and per currency with AI-assisted rate derivation based on the entity's credit profile and prevailing market rates in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R005	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Subsequent Measurement of ROU Assets with Depreciation	The system must apply the cost model for subsequent measurement of ROU assets per IFRS 16 paragraph 29, depreciating the asset from the commencement date to the earlier of the end of the useful life or the end of the lease term. If ownership transfers or a purchase option is reasonably certain to be exercised, depreciation must be over the asset's useful life. For port concession ROU assets at Khalifa Port with a 30-year concession term, the system must depreciate over the concession period. The system must also support the revaluation model where the entity applies IAS 16 revaluation to a class of property, plant and equipment, and the fair value model for ROU investment property under IAS 40. Depreciation must be calculated in the entity's functional currency with monthly posting to the general ledger. The system must handle mid-month commencement dates with configurable conventions (full month, half month, or exact days) and must generate depreciation schedules viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R006	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Liability Subsequent Measurement with Interest and Payments	The system must subsequently measure the lease liability by increasing the carrying amount for accrued interest using the effective interest method, reducing it for lease payments made, and remeasuring for lease modifications, reassessments, or revised lease payments per IFRS 16 paragraph 36. The effective interest calculation must produce a constant periodic rate of interest on the remaining balance. The system must generate monthly interest accrual journal entries in the entity's functional currency with proper allocation between interest expense and principal repayment. For leases with payments in foreign currency (e.g., USD payments for vessel charters by the Qatar entity with QAR functional currency), the lease liability must be retranslated at each reporting date closing rate per IAS 21, with exchange differences recognized in profit or loss. The system must produce an amortization schedule for each lease showing opening balance, interest charge, payment, closing balance, and cumulative interest, displayed in the entity's local currency with USD equivalent column for group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R007	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Modification Processing and Remeasurement	The system must process lease modifications in accordance with IFRS 16 paragraphs 44-46, determining whether the modification should be accounted for as a separate lease or a remeasurement of the existing lease. A modification granting an additional right of use at a commensurate standalone price must be treated as a separate lease. All other modifications must trigger a remeasurement of the lease liability using a revised discount rate, with the adjustment recognized against the ROU asset. Specific container shipping modification scenarios the system must handle include: extension of a port concession term at Hamad Port, addition of new berths to an existing terminal lease at Jebel Ali, change in variable rent formulas linked to container throughput volumes, partial termination of warehouse space at JNPT, and change in the assessment of a purchase option. The system must calculate the remeasurement impact in the entity's functional currency, generate adjustment journal entries, and update the ROU asset and lease liability balances simultaneously. Modification history must be maintained with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R008	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Short-Term Lease and Low-Value Asset Exemption Management	The system must support the IFRS 16 recognition exemptions for short-term leases (12 months or less at commencement with no purchase option) and leases of low-value assets (underlying asset value when new is below a configurable threshold, typically USD 5,000). For container shipping operations, common short-term leases include: temporary additional yard equipment during peak shipping seasons, short-term vessel charters for spot cargo, and event-specific generator rentals at port facilities. Low-value asset leases may include individual laptops, tablets, and small office equipment. The system must allow the entity to elect these exemptions on a lease-by-lease basis for short-term leases and on a class-by-class basis for low-value leases. Exempt lease payments must be recognized as an expense on a straight-line basis over the lease term. The system must monitor short-term leases for extensions or modifications that would cause them to exceed 12 months, automatically prompting reclassification under full IFRS 16 treatment with retrospective calculation of the ROU asset and liability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R009	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Variable Lease Payment Handling and Index-Linked Adjustments	The system must handle variable lease payments in accordance with IFRS 16, distinguishing between variable payments dependent on an index or rate (included in the lease liability) and variable payments linked to performance or usage (expensed as incurred). For port concession agreements common in the Gulf region, variable rent based on CPI escalation clauses or central bank reference rates must be included in the initial lease liability measurement using the index or rate prevailing at the commencement date. When the index or rate changes triggering a cash flow revision, the lease liability must be remeasured using the original discount rate per IFRS 16 paragraph 42. Variable payments based on container throughput volumes (e.g., per-TEU fees above a base threshold at Jebel Ali) must be recognized as expenses when incurred. The system must track both types separately within each lease, generate the appropriate journal entries in the entity's functional currency, and provide analytics showing the split between fixed and variable lease costs per entity and port location.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R010	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Sale and Leaseback Transaction Accounting	The system must handle sale and leaseback transactions under IFRS 16 paragraphs 98-103, determining whether the transfer of the asset qualifies as a sale under IFRS 15. If the transfer is a sale, the system must calculate the ROU asset as the proportion of the previous carrying amount that relates to the right of use retained, and recognize only the gain or loss relating to the rights transferred. If the transfer is not a sale, the system must treat the transaction as a financing arrangement, maintaining the asset on the balance sheet and recognizing a financial liability. Shipping-specific scenarios include: sale and leaseback of container handling equipment at JNPT to improve liquidity, sale of a purpose-built warehouse at Hamad Port with a 15-year leaseback, and sale of terminal infrastructure to a port authority with an operating leaseback concession. The system must handle multi-currency transactions where the sale proceeds are in USD but the leaseback payments are in local currency, with proper exchange rate application at each stage of the transaction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R011	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Portfolio Management and Centralized Lease Register	The system must maintain a centralized lease register across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) capturing every lease contract subject to IFRS 16. The register must record: lease identification number, counterparty details, underlying asset description and location, commencement and expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, lease term including optional extension and termination periods, payment schedule with amounts in payment currency and entity functional currency, discount rate applied, current ROU asset carrying amount, current lease liability balance, and modification history. The register must support bulk operations including: mass extension processing for leases with common landlords, portfolio-level remeasurement for index-linked adjustments, and batch reporting for regulatory submissions. Search and filter capabilities must support Arabic text for counterparty names and asset descriptions. The system must generate alerts for upcoming lease expiries at 180, 90, 30, and 7 days, enabling timely renewal negotiations for critical port infrastructure leases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R012	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	IFRS 16 Disclosure Report Automation	The system must automatically generate all IFRS 16 quantitative and qualitative disclosures required by paragraphs 47-60. Quantitative disclosures must include: depreciation charge for ROU assets by class of underlying asset, interest expense on lease liabilities, expense for short-term leases, expense for low-value asset leases, expense for variable lease payments not in the liability, total cash outflow for leases, additions to ROU assets, carrying amount of ROU assets by class, and a maturity analysis of lease liabilities showing undiscounted cash flows in annual bands. Qualitative disclosures must cover: the nature of leasing activities, extension and termination option assessments, and residual value guarantees. All disclosures must be generated per entity in local currency and consolidated in USD. Reports must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting, and must support Hijri fiscal year reporting for KSA and Qatar entities. The system must provide a disclosure reconciliation worksheet mapping each generated figure to underlying lease records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R013	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Accounting Tax Adjustment for Jurisdictional Differences	The system must handle the tax accounting implications of IFRS 16 lease accounting where the tax treatment differs from the accounting treatment in each jurisdiction. In many Gulf jurisdictions, tax deductions follow the actual lease payment pattern rather than the IFRS 16 split between depreciation and interest. In India, Ind AS 116 aligns with IFRS 16 but tax deductions under the Income Tax Act may follow operating lease treatment. The system must maintain parallel lease calculations: one for IFRS 16 accounting purposes and one for tax purposes per entity jurisdiction. Deferred tax assets or liabilities arising from the temporary differences between the IFRS 16 carrying amounts and tax bases must be automatically calculated per IAS 12. The system must generate tax adjustment journal entries at each reporting date and provide a reconciliation between the accounting and tax treatment of each lease. Entity-specific tax rate configurations must be maintained for accurate deferred tax calculations in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R014	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Lease Term Reassessment and Option Evaluation	The system must support reassessment of the lease term when there is a significant event or change in circumstances within the lessee's control that affects the assessment of extension or termination options per IFRS 16 paragraph 20. For container shipping operations, reassessment triggers include: significant leasehold improvements made to a leased terminal at Jebel Ali (increasing likelihood of extension), loss of a major customer reducing the operational need for leased warehouse space at JNPT, strategic decision to expand operations at Hamad Port making a port concession extension reasonably certain, or regulatory changes in KSA affecting the viability of a leased facility. The AI engine must monitor operational metrics and flag potential reassessment triggers automatically. Upon reassessment, the system must recalculate the lease liability using a revised discount rate at the reassessment date, adjust the ROU asset, and generate all necessary journal entries in the entity's functional currency. The system must maintain complete documentation of each reassessment decision with supporting rationale in English or Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R015	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Embedded Lease Identification in Service Contracts	The system must assist in identifying embedded leases within service contracts that may not be explicitly labeled as leases. In container shipping, embedded leases commonly occur in: dedicated vessel charter party agreements where a specific vessel is assigned, managed port service contracts where specific equipment is dedicated to the customer, IT managed services where specific server infrastructure is allocated, and logistics contracts where dedicated warehouse zones or yard blocks are assigned. The AI engine must analyze service contract terms uploaded in PDF format (English or Arabic) to identify clauses that indicate an identified asset, the right to control use, and the right to obtain substantially all economic benefits. When an embedded lease is identified, the system must prompt the user to separate the lease component from the service component, allocate the contract consideration between the components based on relative standalone prices, and apply full IFRS 16 accounting to the lease component. The identification analysis must be documented and stored for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R016	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Multi-Entity Lease Consolidation and Intercompany Elimination	The system must handle lease consolidation for group reporting when leases exist between entities within the container shipping group. Intercompany leases, such as the Qatar entity leasing equipment from the UAE entity, must be eliminated on consolidation with the underlying asset recognized on the group balance sheet at its original carrying amount. The system must identify all intercompany lease arrangements across the four entities, calculate elimination entries that reverse the lessee's ROU asset and lease liability and the lessor's lease receivable or asset, and ensure the group consolidated financial statements reflect the economic substance of intra-group arrangements. For sub-leases where an entity sub-leases externally a portion of space it leases from another group entity, the system must handle the partial elimination correctly. All elimination entries must handle multi-currency translation where the lessor and lessee operate in different functional currencies, applying IAS 21 translation requirements for the consolidated USD presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R017	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	COVID-19 and Force Majeure Rent Concession Accounting	The system must handle accounting for rent concessions granted as a result of force majeure events, following the IFRS 16 amendments for COVID-19-related rent concessions (extended through June 2022) and analogous treatment for future force majeure events in the Gulf region such as extreme weather events or geopolitical disruptions. The system must support the practical expedient allowing lessees to account for qualifying rent concessions without assessing whether they constitute lease modifications. Qualifying conditions must include: the revised payments being substantially the same or less than pre-concession payments, the concession affecting only payments originally due on or before a specified date, and no substantive change to other lease terms. The system must recognize rent forgiveness or reductions as variable lease payments (negative) in the period of the concession. For concessions that do not qualify for the practical expedient, the system must apply the standard lease modification accounting. All concession tracking must support multi-currency amounts across QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with proper documentation in English and Arabic for regulatory and audit review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-039-2-004-R018	FEAT-039-2-004	MOD-039	Transition and Opening Balance Adjustment Management	The system must support IFRS 16 transition adjustments for entities or newly acquired operations that have not previously applied the standard. Two transition approaches must be available: the full retrospective approach recalculating ROU assets and lease liabilities as if IFRS 16 had always been applied, and the modified retrospective approach measuring the ROU asset at an amount equal to the lease liability adjusted for prepaid or accrued lease payments. The system must provide transition calculation tools that take existing operating lease commitments (as disclosed under IAS 17) and convert them into IFRS 16 opening balances. For newly acquired port operations (e.g., a new terminal concession at a Gulf port), the system must calculate day-one IFRS 16 balances for all existing leases, apply the appropriate discount rate for each lease, and generate opening balance adjustment journal entries. The transition module must handle leases in multiple currencies across all four entity jurisdictions and must produce a reconciliation between the IAS 17 operating lease commitment note disclosure and the IFRS 16 opening lease liability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R001	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Multi-Entity Bank Account Registry	The system must maintain a centralized registry of all bank accounts across the four operating entities: Qatar (QA-ENT), UAE (AE-ENT), KSA (SA-ENT), and India (IN-ENT). Each account record must capture the bank name, branch, SWIFT/BIC code, IBAN (mandatory for GCC accounts), IFSC code (mandatory for Indian accounts), account number, account type (current, savings, call deposit, escrow, EEFC), currency denomination (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP), and opening/closing dates. The registry must enforce unique constraints on IBAN and account number combinations per entity to prevent duplicate entries. All account records must support bilingual storage in Arabic and English for bank name and branch fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R002	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Account Hierarchy and Grouping Structure	The system must support a hierarchical grouping structure for bank accounts, allowing treasury managers to organize accounts by entity, currency, bank relationship, and operational purpose. Operational purpose categories must include freight collection accounts (used for receiving ocean freight payments from shippers at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT), port disbursement accounts (for settling terminal handling charges, pilotage, and berth hire), payroll accounts, tax escrow accounts (VAT/GST), and intercompany settlement accounts. The hierarchy must support up to five nesting levels, and each node must display aggregated balances in both the local currency and a configurable reporting currency, defaulting to USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R003	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Signatory Mandate Management	The system must manage authorized signatory mandates for each bank account, capturing signatory name, designation, specimen signature image, authorization limit in the account currency, and effective date range. The mandate must support single, joint (any two), and panel (specific combinations) signatory rules with configurable thresholds. For shipping disbursement accounts used at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, where urgent port authority payments may arise, the system must support emergency single-signatory authorization with a reduced limit and mandatory post-facto approval workflow. All signatory changes must maintain a complete audit trail with timestamps, the user who made the change, and the reason for modification. Expired signatories must be automatically flagged 30 days before expiry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R004	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Facility and Credit Line Tracking	The system must track all banking facilities associated with each account including overdraft limits, letter of credit facilities, bank guarantee lines, trade finance limits, and working capital facilities. Each facility record must store the sanctioned limit, utilized amount, available balance, interest rate or margin, validity period, and collateral details. For container shipping operations, the system must specifically track LC facilities used for importing containers and spare parts through JNPT and Jebel Ali, including sub-limits for sight LCs versus usance LCs. Facility utilization must be recalculated in real-time as transactions are posted, and alerts must trigger when utilization exceeds configurable thresholds of 75%, 85%, and 95% of the sanctioned limit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R005	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Correspondent Banking Relationship Register	The system must maintain a register of correspondent banking relationships used for international freight payment routing, particularly for USD-denominated ocean freight settlements between GCC entities and Indian counterparts. Each correspondent record must include the correspondent bank name, SWIFT code, intermediary bank details if applicable, nostro/vostro account references, and the currencies supported through each correspondent chain. The system must validate that outward remittance instructions for freight settlements reference valid correspondent paths and must flag any transactions routed through sanctioned jurisdictions per OFAC and UN sanctions lists. The register must be updatable by authorized treasury users only, with all changes requiring dual approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R006	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Multi-Currency Account Balance Tracking	The system must track real-time balances for all bank accounts in their native currency and simultaneously convert them to the entity's functional currency and the group reporting currency (USD). Exchange rates must be sourced from the daily rate feed configured in the ERP's exchange rate module, with support for spot rates, contracted rates, and Central Bank reference rates from QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI. For EEFC (Exchange Earners Foreign Currency) accounts held by the India entity for retaining freight earnings in USD, the system must track both the USD balance and the INR equivalent using RBI reference rates. Balance discrepancies exceeding a configurable tolerance (default 0.01% of account balance) between the system ledger and bank-reported balance must trigger automatic reconciliation alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R007	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Account Opening and Closure Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for bank account opening and closure requests, requiring initiation by treasury operations, review by the treasury manager, and final approval by the CFO or designated authority. The account opening request must capture the business justification, expected transaction volume, required facilities, and the entity under which the account is opened. For container shipping entities operating across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INJNP), the workflow must validate that the requesting entity holds a valid trade license in the respective jurisdiction. Account closure requests must verify zero balance, no pending transactions, no outstanding bank guarantees, and no active LC facilities before allowing closure approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R008	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Account KYC and Compliance Document Store	The system must maintain a document repository for each bank account storing KYC documents, board resolutions, trade licenses, signatory identification documents, and regulatory filings. Each document must have a type classification, upload date, expiry date, and status (active, expired, pending renewal). For GCC entities, the system must track Commercial Registration (CR) certificates, Chamber of Commerce memberships, and VAT registration certificates as mandatory compliance documents. For the India entity, the system must track PAN, TAN, GST registration, IEC (Import Export Code) for JNPT customs operations, and FEMA compliance certificates. The system must generate automated alerts 60, 30, and 15 days before any compliance document expires and must prevent new facility requests if critical documents are expired.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R009	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Fee and Charges Configuration	The system must maintain a configurable schedule of bank fees and charges for each account, including transaction fees (per debit/credit), monthly maintenance charges, SWIFT message charges (MT103, MT202, MT700 for LC), cash handling fees, and foreign exchange conversion spreads. Fee schedules must support tiered pricing based on transaction volume bands commonly negotiated by shipping companies handling high-volume freight collections. The system must automatically calculate expected bank charges per statement period and compare them against actual charges debited by the bank, flagging discrepancies exceeding a configurable threshold (default AED 50 or currency equivalent). Overcharged fees must generate automatic dispute records routed to the bank relationship manager for follow-up and recovery.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R010	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Interest Rate and Yield Tracking	The system must track interest rates applicable to each bank account, supporting fixed rates, floating rates (linked to benchmarks such as EIBOR, SAIBOR, QIBOR, MCLR), and tiered rates based on balance slabs. For call deposit and fixed deposit accounts used by treasury to park surplus freight collection proceeds, the system must calculate accrued interest daily using the actual/365 or actual/360 day-count convention as configured per account. The system must project monthly and quarterly interest income for cash flow forecasting purposes. Interest rate changes must be logged with effective dates, and the system must compare earned interest against expected interest based on the agreed rate schedule, flagging any shortfalls exceeding 5 basis points for treasury review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R011	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Arabic RTL Interface for Account Management	The system must render the entire bank account management interface in Arabic right-to-left (RTL) layout when the user's language preference is set to Arabic. All labels, field names, navigation menus, dropdown options, and action buttons must display in Arabic. Numeric fields including account numbers, IBAN, balances, and amounts must remain left-to-right (LTR) within the RTL context as per standard Arabic numeric display conventions. Bank names and branch addresses must support dual-language entry with Arabic as the primary display in RTL mode. Date fields must support display in both Gregorian and Hijri (Um Al-Qura) calendar formats simultaneously, with Hijri dates calculated accurately for months including Ramadan and Dhul Hijjah which affect banking operational hours in GCC jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R012	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Account Access Control and Segregation	The system must enforce role-based access control on bank account data, segregating visibility and transactional authority by entity, department, and user role. A treasury analyst in the Qatar entity must not view account details of the India entity unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. The system must support at minimum the following roles: Treasury Clerk (view balances, initiate payments), Treasury Analyst (view and analyze, no payment authority), Treasury Manager (approve transactions, manage facilities), and CFO (full access including account opening/closure approval). Each role must be configurable at the entity level, and the system must log all access events including view, create, update, and delete actions with user identity, timestamp, IP address, and the specific account accessed for SOX compliance audit requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R013	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Statement Import Configuration	The system must support configurable bank statement import from multiple sources including SWIFT MT940/MT942 messages, BAI2 format files, CAMT.053 (ISO 20022) XML files, and CSV/Excel formats for banks that do not support standard electronic formats. Each bank account must have an individually configured import profile specifying the expected format, field mappings, character encoding (UTF-8 mandatory for Arabic content), and the automated import schedule. For banks serving the Hamad Port and Jebel Ali operations where statement delivery may be delayed during public holidays (including Eid Al-Fitr, Eid Al-Adha, and Qatar National Day), the system must handle gaps in statement sequences gracefully and generate alerts for missing statement periods exceeding two business days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R014	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Dormant and Inactive Account Monitoring	The system must automatically classify bank accounts as inactive if no transactions are recorded for 90 consecutive days, and as dormant if no transactions occur for 180 consecutive days. For container shipping operations, certain accounts such as seasonal charter hire escrow accounts or project-specific port development accounts at Khalifa Port or JNPT may legitimately have long periods of inactivity. The system must allow treasury managers to tag such accounts with a justified exception reason and an expected reactivation date, exempting them from dormancy alerts. Non-exempted dormant accounts must trigger escalating notifications: first to the treasury analyst at 90 days, then to the treasury manager at 150 days, and finally to the CFO at 180 days, recommending account closure to reduce the banking footprint and associated maintenance fees.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R015	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Relationship Scoring and Performance Dashboard	The system must calculate and display a relationship performance score for each banking partner based on configurable weighted metrics including: service quality (statement delivery timeliness, error rates), pricing competitiveness (fee benchmarking against market rates), facility responsiveness (time to sanction new limits), foreign exchange spread competitiveness (comparing offered rates for USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR conversions against mid-market rates), and digital capability maturity (API availability, host-to-host connectivity reliability). The dashboard must aggregate scores across all entities to provide a group-level view of each bank's performance. Scores must update monthly, trend over 12 months, and support export in PDF and Excel formats for quarterly banking relationship review meetings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R016	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Offline Account Data Access and Sync	The system must support offline access to critical bank account data including account details, current balances (as of last sync), signatory information, and facility summaries. This capability is essential for treasury managers traveling between port locations (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT) or operating from vessel agency offices where internet connectivity may be intermittent. The offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption on the local device. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any offline-created notes or draft payment instructions with the central server, applying conflict resolution rules that prioritize server-side data for balance fields and client-side data for user annotations. The offline cache must auto-expire after 72 hours without synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R017	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Regulatory Reporting Tags and Classification	The system must support tagging bank accounts with regulatory reporting classifications required by each jurisdiction's central bank. For Qatar, accounts must be tagged per QCB reporting categories for balance of payments submissions. For UAE, accounts must carry CBUAE classification codes for large-value transaction reporting. For KSA, accounts must be tagged for SAMA compliance including Zakat applicability flags. For India, accounts must carry RBI classification including NRO/NRE designations if applicable, FEMA compliance status, and purpose codes for outward remittances related to freight and charter hire payments. Each tag must have an effective date range, and the system must validate that all mandatory tags are present before allowing transactions to be processed through the respective account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-001-R018	FEAT-040-1-001	MOD-040	Bank Account Audit Trail and Change History	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for every bank account capturing all lifecycle events from opening request through closure. Auditable events must include account creation, detail modifications (any field change), signatory additions or removals, facility limit changes, status changes (active, suspended, dormant, closed), and all transactional postings referencing the account. Each audit entry must record the event type, previous value, new value, timestamp in UTC with millisecond precision, user identity, approval chain (who requested and who approved), and the source system or interface that triggered the change. The audit trail must be searchable by date range, event type, and user, and must support export in formats compliant with external audit requirements for KPMG, PwC, Deloitte, and EY firm templates commonly used by shipping conglomerates in the GCC region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R001	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Consolidated Multi-Entity Cash Position View	The system must display a single consolidated dashboard showing the aggregate cash position across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with the ability to drill down into individual entity positions. The consolidated view must present opening balance, total inflows, total outflows, and closing balance for the current business day. All amounts must be converted to the group reporting currency (USD) using the day's opening exchange rates, with the option to toggle to any other supported currency (AED, QAR, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP). The dashboard must auto-refresh at configurable intervals (default: every 15 minutes during banking hours) and must clearly indicate the timestamp of the last data refresh and any accounts where data is stale beyond 30 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R002	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Currency-Wise Cash Position Breakdown	The system must present the cash position broken down by currency, showing for each currency the total balance held across all entities, the number of accounts holding that currency, the largest single account balance, and the percentage of total group liquidity represented by that currency. For container shipping operations where USD is the primary trading currency for ocean freight, the dashboard must highlight the USD position prominently and show the percentage of non-USD balances that could be converted to USD if needed. The currency breakdown must include unrealized foreign exchange gains or losses based on current spot rates versus the weighted average booking rate, particularly for QAR, AED, and SAR positions where the USD peg provides relative stability versus INR positions which carry exchange rate volatility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R003	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Inflow and Outflow Transaction Categorization	The system must categorize all daily cash inflows and outflows into predefined shipping-specific categories. Inflow categories must include ocean freight collections (FCL and LCL), demurrage and detention charges received, terminal handling charge reimbursements, charter hire income, agency commission income, and intercompany transfers received. Outflow categories must include port disbursements (covering berth hire, pilotage, towage at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT), vessel operating expenses, bunker fuel payments, container lease rentals, customs duty and VAT/GST payments, payroll, and intercompany transfers sent. Each category must show the count of transactions, total amount, and comparison against the same day last week and the monthly daily average. Users must be able to add custom categories without developer intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R004	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Bank Account Level Cash Position Detail	The system must provide a detailed view at the individual bank account level showing opening balance, itemized inflows, itemized outflows, closing balance, available balance (net of holds and uncleared funds), and the float amount (difference between ledger and available balance). For accounts used in port disbursement operations at Hamad Port (QAHMD) and JNPT (INJNP), the system must display the value-dated balance alongside the booking-dated balance, as port authority payments often carry different value dates. Each account line must be clickable to expand into a full transaction listing for the day. Accounts with negative available balances or those exceeding overdraft limits must be highlighted in red with priority alert indicators. The detail view must also show the last five transactions chronologically for quick reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R005	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Short-Term Cash Forecast Overlay	The dashboard must overlay a rolling 7-day and 30-day cash forecast on top of the actual cash position, showing projected daily closing balances based on confirmed future-dated payments, expected receivables from freight invoices with known due dates, scheduled loan repayments, anticipated port disbursement agent funding requirements, and recurring fixed payments such as container lease rentals and office rents. The forecast must differentiate between confirmed cash flows (backed by approved payment instructions or confirmed receipt notifications) and estimated cash flows (derived from historical patterns and AI prediction). For each forecast day, the system must calculate the projected surplus or deficit against the minimum cash buffer threshold configured per entity, and must flag any projected deficit at least three business days in advance to allow treasury to arrange intercompany funding or short-term borrowing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R006	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	AI-Driven Anomaly Detection on Cash Flows	The system must employ AI/ML algorithms to detect anomalies in daily cash flows, including unusual transaction amounts (deviating more than two standard deviations from the 90-day rolling average for that category), unexpected timing of receipts or payments, duplicate payment patterns, and sudden concentration of outflows to a single beneficiary. For container shipping operations, the system must be trained on seasonal patterns such as increased freight collections during peak shipping seasons (typically Q3 and Q4 for GCC-India trade lanes) and reduced activity during Eid holidays and Diwali. Detected anomalies must be classified by severity (low, medium, high, critical) and presented on the dashboard with one-click drill-down to the underlying transactions. The AI model must continuously learn from treasury analyst feedback on flagged items to reduce false positive rates below 10% within six months of deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R007	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Multi-Currency Exposure Summary Widget	The dashboard must include a dedicated widget showing the organization's net currency exposure across all entities, calculated as the difference between assets (receivables plus cash balances) and liabilities (payables plus committed outflows) in each currency. For the container shipping context where freight is predominantly invoiced in USD but operational costs are incurred in local currencies (QAR for Hamad Port operations, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for Dammam/Jubail operations, INR for JNPT and domestic Indian operations), the widget must clearly show the natural hedge position and the residual unhedged exposure. The widget must support scenario analysis allowing treasury to simulate the impact of a 1%, 5%, and 10% adverse movement in each currency pair on the group's consolidated position, with results displayed in both tabular and graphical formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R008	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Liquidity Coverage Ratio and Buffer Indicators	The system must calculate and display key liquidity metrics including the Liquidity Coverage Ratio (total liquid assets divided by net cash outflows over the next 30 days), the Cash Buffer Ratio (current cash balance divided by average daily operating expenditure), and the Days of Cash on Hand metric. For each entity, the minimum acceptable thresholds must be configurable: default values of 120% for LCR, 1.5x for Cash Buffer Ratio, and 30 days for Days of Cash. The dashboard must use color-coded indicators (green for above threshold, amber for within 10% of threshold, red for below threshold) and must send automated email and in-app notifications to the CFO and treasury manager when any entity breaches its minimum liquidity threshold. Historical trend charts must show these ratios over the past 90 days to identify deteriorating liquidity patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R009	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Intercompany Position Netting Summary	The dashboard must display a real-time intercompany position matrix showing net receivable or payable balances between each pair of entities. For the four-entity structure (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), this produces six bilateral relationships that must be presented in a clear matrix format. The system must show both gross positions (total receivables and total payables) and net positions after bilateral netting. For container shipping operations, intercompany balances typically arise from slot exchange agreements, shared vessel costs, hub-and-spoke transshipment charges through Jebel Ali acting as the regional hub, and centralized procurement of bunker fuel. The matrix must highlight any intercompany balance that has remained unsettled beyond the group's intercompany settlement policy (configurable, default 30 days) and must calculate the notional interest cost of delayed settlements using the group's internal transfer pricing rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R010	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Hijri Calendar and GCC Business Day Awareness	The dashboard must incorporate full Hijri (Islamic) calendar awareness for accurate business day calculations across GCC jurisdictions. The system must recognize that Friday-Saturday is the standard weekend in Qatar, UAE, and KSA, while Saturday-Sunday is the weekend in India. Public holidays must be pre-loaded for each jurisdiction including Eid Al-Fitr (variable dates based on moon sighting), Eid Al-Adha, Qatar National Day (December 18), UAE National Day (December 2-3), Saudi National Day (September 23), and Indian holidays including Republic Day, Independence Day, Gandhi Jayanti, and Diwali. The cash position date selector must clearly indicate non-business days with greyed-out styling, and the forecast engine must skip non-business days when projecting payment and receipt dates. The dashboard header must display both Gregorian and Hijri dates simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R011	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Configurable Dashboard Layout and Widgets	The system must allow each user to configure their dashboard layout by selecting, arranging, and resizing widgets from a library of available components. Available widgets must include the consolidated position summary, currency breakdown chart, entity comparison bar chart, daily transaction volume timeline, forecast versus actual variance chart, anomaly alert feed, intercompany matrix, liquidity ratio gauges, and top pending payments list. Users must be able to save multiple dashboard configurations for different use cases (for example, a morning liquidity review layout versus a month-end closing layout) and switch between them. The dashboard must be responsive, functioning on desktop monitors with minimum 1280x720 resolution and on tablets with minimum 768x1024 resolution. Default layouts must be configurable by the system administrator per user role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R012	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Real-Time Alert Feed and Notification Center	The dashboard must include an integrated alert feed displaying real-time notifications relevant to the treasury function. Alert types must include low balance warnings, overdraft breaches, large transaction alerts (configurable threshold per entity, default USD 100,000 equivalent), failed payment notifications, bank statement import errors, reconciliation breaks, foreign exchange rate movements exceeding daily tolerance bands, and compliance-related alerts such as approaching sanctions screening deadlines. Each alert must show the severity level, timestamp, affected entity and account, a descriptive message in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English), and action buttons for acknowledge, investigate, or dismiss. Unacknowledged critical alerts older than 30 minutes must escalate to the treasury manager via SMS and email. The alert feed must support filtering by entity, severity, type, and date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R008	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Cost allocation rules engine	The system must provide a configurable rules engine for allocating shared costs (head office overhead, IT infrastructure, group insurance, shared vessel capacity) to segments. Allocation bases must include: headcount per entity, revenue share, TEU throughput, vessel days in port, and fixed percentage. Rules must support multi-tier allocation where costs first allocate to entities then to trade lanes within each entity. Each allocation rule must have an effective date range, approval status, and version history. The engine must process month-end allocations for all segments within a 15-minute batch window. Rules must be testable in simulation mode before activation to preview financial impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R013	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Historical Cash Position Comparison and Trending	The system must provide historical comparison capabilities allowing users to view the cash position for any past date and compare it against the current position or any other historical date. Trend analysis must be available showing daily closing balances over configurable periods (7 days, 30 days, 90 days, 365 days) with the ability to overlay multiple entities or currencies on the same chart. For container shipping operations, the trending feature must support correlation analysis between cash position fluctuations and key operational metrics such as vessel call volumes at Hamad Port and JNPT, TEU throughput, and freight rate indices. Moving averages (7-day and 30-day) must be available as chart overlays. All historical data must be retained for a minimum of seven years to support regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R014	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Cash Position Data Export and Reporting	The system must support exporting the cash position dashboard data in multiple formats including PDF (with company letterhead and bilingual Arabic/English headers), Microsoft Excel (with structured sheets per entity and a consolidation sheet), CSV (for integration with external treasury management systems), and JSON (for API consumers). Scheduled reports must be configurable to auto-generate and distribute via email at specified times, typically at 08:00 local time for each entity's treasury team showing the previous day's closing position and the current day's opening position. The export must preserve all formatting, currency symbols, and number formatting conventions (Arabian Standard: 1,234,567.89 for English; ١٬٢٣٤٬٥٦٧٫٨٩ for Arabic numeral display). Report templates must be customizable by the treasury manager without requiring IT support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R015	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Role-Based Dashboard Data Visibility	The dashboard must enforce strict role-based data visibility rules ensuring that users only see cash position data for entities and accounts they are authorized to access. A treasury clerk at the UAE entity must see only UAE account balances unless explicitly granted cross-entity visibility. The CFO role must have an unrestricted consolidated view across all entities. Entity-level treasury managers must see their own entity in full detail and a summary-only view of other entities. For shipping operations managers at specific ports (for example, a port captain at JNPT), the system must support a restricted view showing only the disbursement account balance and recent port-related transactions without exposing the full treasury position. All data visibility rules must be configurable by the system administrator and must cascade correctly through all dashboard widgets, drill-downs, and export functions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-002-R016	FEAT-040-1-002	MOD-040	Mobile-Responsive Cash Position Summary	The system must provide a mobile-responsive version of the cash position dashboard optimized for smartphone access, enabling treasury managers to monitor liquidity while visiting port facilities at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT. The mobile view must prioritize the most critical information: total group cash position in USD, entity-level summaries, critical alerts requiring action, and the top five pending high-value payments. The mobile interface must support biometric authentication (fingerprint or face recognition) as a login option, and all data transmitted to mobile devices must be encrypted using TLS 1.3. The mobile dashboard must function with degraded performance on 3G network connections common at port and maritime facilities, with a maximum initial load time of five seconds on a 3G connection and graceful degradation when offline by displaying the last cached position with a clear staleness indicator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R001	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Automated Bank Statement Ingestion	The system must automatically ingest bank statements from all configured bank accounts on a scheduled basis, supporting SWIFT MT940 (end-of-day), MT942 (interim intra-day), CAMT.053 (ISO 20022 end-of-day), CAMT.052 (ISO 20022 intra-day), BAI2, and proprietary CSV/Excel formats used by regional banks in Qatar (QNB, Commercial Bank), UAE (Emirates NBD, ADCB, Mashreq), KSA (Al Rajhi, NCB/SNB, Riyad Bank), and India (SBI, HDFC, ICICI, Axis). The ingestion engine must parse and normalize all formats into a common internal transaction structure capturing value date, booking date, amount, currency, debit/credit indicator, transaction reference, bank reference, counterparty details, and narrative text. Failed imports must be quarantined with detailed error logs and must not block the processing of other statements from different accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R002	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	AI-Powered Transaction Matching Engine	The system must employ AI/ML-based matching algorithms to automatically reconcile bank statement entries against ERP-recorded transactions (payments, receipts, journal entries, intercompany transfers). The matching engine must support one-to-one matching (single bank entry to single ERP entry), one-to-many matching (single bank entry against multiple ERP entries, common for bulk freight collection deposits), many-to-one matching (multiple bank entries against a single ERP entry, common for installment payments of charter hire), and many-to-many matching. Matching criteria must be configurable per account and must include amount (exact or within tolerance), date (exact or within configurable day range), reference number (exact, partial, or fuzzy), and counterparty name (fuzzy matching with transliteration support for Arabic names). The engine must achieve a minimum 90% automatic match rate within the first three months of deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R003	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Configurable Matching Rules and Tolerance Thresholds	The system must allow treasury administrators to define and prioritize matching rules per bank account, specifying which fields to compare, the matching tolerance for each field, and the confidence score threshold for automatic versus manual matching. Amount tolerance must be configurable as an absolute value (for example, QAR 0.50 for rounding differences) or as a percentage (for example, 0.1% for foreign exchange conversion variances). Date tolerance must be configurable in business days (for example, plus or minus two business days for value date matching at JNPT where bank processing can delay posting). Reference number matching must support regex patterns to extract relevant portions from verbose bank narratives. The system must support a minimum of 50 matching rule sets, allowing different rules for different banks and transaction types. Rules must be testable against historical data before activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R009	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment reporting period management	The system must manage reporting periods independently for each segment to accommodate different fiscal year-ends across jurisdictions: Qatar (December 31), UAE (December 31), KSA (Hijri year-end with Gregorian mapping), and India (March 31). Period open/close status must be controllable per segment, allowing the India entity to remain open for Q4 adjustments while other entities are closed. The system must support 12 monthly periods plus up to 4 adjustment periods (13–16) per fiscal year. Period closing must enforce completion of all allocations, eliminations, and currency translations before allowing lock. A period reopening workflow must require CFO-level approval with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R004	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Arabic and Bilingual Narrative Parsing	The matching engine must parse and interpret bank statement narratives in both English and Arabic, including mixed-language narratives commonly generated by GCC banks. The system must handle Arabic text normalization including ignoring diacritical marks (tashkeel), normalizing alef variants (alef with hamza above, alef with hamza below, alef with madda, and bare alef), and treating taa marbuta and haa as equivalent in matching contexts. For Indian bank statements, the system must handle Romanized transliterations of Hindi counterparty names. The narrative parser must extract structured data from free-text descriptions, identifying payment references (such as BL numbers, invoice numbers, voyage references), counterparty names, and purpose codes. A machine learning model must be trained on at least 12 months of historical bank statements from each bank to achieve optimal parsing accuracy exceeding 95% for structured data extraction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R005	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Unmatched Transaction Workflow and Resolution	All bank statement entries that cannot be automatically matched must be routed to a manual reconciliation worklist, prioritized by amount (highest first), age (oldest first), or entity (configurable). The worklist must present each unmatched item with the bank narrative, amount, date, and a list of suggested potential matches ranked by confidence score. Treasury analysts must be able to manually match, create a new ERP entry (for unexpected receipts such as insurance claim settlements or port authority refunds), or flag the item as under investigation. Items flagged as under investigation must have a mandatory comment field and a target resolution date. Unmatched items older than five business days must automatically escalate to the treasury manager. The system must track the average resolution time for unmatched items per entity and per bank, providing performance metrics visible on the reconciliation dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R006	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Multi-Currency Reconciliation with FX Variance Handling	The system must handle reconciliation of multi-currency transactions where the bank statement amount may differ from the ERP-recorded amount due to foreign exchange rate differences. For freight payments received in USD and converted to local currency by the receiving bank (for example, QAR at QNB for the Qatar entity), the system must match the original USD amount from the ERP against the converted QAR amount on the bank statement by applying the bank's conversion rate and calculating the FX variance. FX variances within a configurable tolerance (default: 0.5% of transaction value) must be automatically reconciled with the variance posted to a designated FX gain/loss general ledger account. Variances exceeding the tolerance must be flagged for manual review. The system must support triangulated currency conversions where the bank routes through an intermediate currency, commonly seen in INR to QAR conversions routed through USD at JNPT freight settlements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R007	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Bank Charges and Fee Reconciliation	The system must automatically identify and reconcile bank charges, fees, and interest entries on bank statements against the expected fee schedule configured in the bank account portfolio module. Transaction types to be identified include monthly maintenance fees, per-transaction charges, SWIFT charges (MT103 for wire transfers, MT700/MT710 for letters of credit), cash handling fees, cheque return charges, and interest debits or credits. The system must compare the actual charge against the expected amount based on the fee schedule and flag discrepancies. For container shipping companies handling high-volume transactions through ports like Jebel Ali which processes over 15 million TEUs annually, the cumulative bank charges can be significant, and the system must generate a monthly bank charges analysis report showing actual versus expected fees by bank, by charge type, and by entity, highlighting overcharges for recovery action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R008	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Reconciliation Status Dashboard and Metrics	The system must provide a comprehensive reconciliation status dashboard showing for each bank account and each entity: total statement entries received, number automatically matched (with percentage), number manually matched, number pending in worklist, number under investigation, and number of days since last fully reconciled statement. The dashboard must display trend charts showing the auto-match rate over the past 12 months to track improvement in AI matching accuracy. Key performance indicators must include the average time to fully reconcile each account (target: within one business day of statement receipt), the percentage of items matched within one hour of import, and the aging analysis of unmatched items. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, bank, currency, and date range, and must highlight accounts where reconciliation is falling behind the target SLA with red indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R009	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Automated Journal Entry Generation from Reconciliation	Upon completion of reconciliation, the system must automatically generate the necessary journal entries to bring the ERP cash book balance in line with the bank statement balance. Journal entries must be created for identified items including bank charges not previously recorded, interest income or expense, foreign exchange gains or losses from conversion variances, and direct debits or credits identified on the statement without a corresponding ERP entry. Each auto-generated journal entry must reference the originating bank statement line, the reconciliation batch, and the matching rule that triggered it. Journal entries exceeding a configurable threshold (default: USD 5,000 equivalent) must be routed for approval before posting. All auto-generated journals must use pre-configured GL account mappings maintained by the finance team, with separate mappings per entity to respect each entity's chart of accounts structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R010	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Reconciliation Period Management and Lock	The system must support formal reconciliation period management allowing treasury to open, work on, and close reconciliation periods per bank account. Once a reconciliation period is closed (typically monthly, aligned with financial reporting periods), no further changes may be made to matched transactions within that period without formal reopening approval from the treasury manager and the financial controller. The system must enforce that all bank accounts are reconciled and periods closed before the entity's monthly financial close process can proceed. For entities following the Hijri fiscal year (some KSA operations), the system must correctly handle reconciliation periods aligned with Hijri months, which vary in length between 29 and 30 days. The system must also support mid-month interim reconciliations for high-volume accounts such as freight collection accounts at Jebel Ali that may process hundreds of transactions daily.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R011	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Duplicate Transaction Detection and Prevention	The system must detect and prevent duplicate bank statement entries caused by re-import of statement files, overlapping intra-day and end-of-day statements, or bank errors. Duplicate detection must use a composite key of bank reference number, value date, booking date, amount, and debit/credit indicator. When a potential duplicate is detected, the system must quarantine the entry and alert the treasury analyst with details of both the existing and incoming entries. For banks that do not provide unique bank reference numbers (common with some regional Indian banks serving JNPT operations), the system must use an enhanced duplicate detection algorithm incorporating the narrative text, sequence number within the statement, and opening/closing balance cross-validation. The system must maintain a duplicate detection log showing all detected duplicates, the action taken (auto-rejected or manually reviewed), and the outcome, supporting audit requirements for all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R012	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Nostro and Vostro Account Reconciliation	The system must support reconciliation of nostro accounts (held with correspondent banks for international settlements) and vostro accounts (held by correspondent banks), which are critical for international freight payment routing between GCC entities and the India entity. The nostro reconciliation must compare the entity's internal nostro ledger against the correspondent bank's statement, identifying timing differences for in-transit funds. For container shipping operations, nostro reconciliation is particularly important for USD wire transfers from Indian importers paying freight to GCC-based shipping lines, where the payment chain may involve the importer's bank in India, a correspondent bank (typically in New York or London), and the beneficiary bank in Qatar or UAE. The system must track each payment through the correspondent chain and calculate the float days, providing analytics on average float time per correspondent route to optimize banking partner selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R013	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Reconciliation Audit Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive reconciliation reports for audit purposes, including the Bank Reconciliation Statement (BRS) showing the closing balance per books, add-back of credits in bank not in books, deduction of debits in bank not in books, add-back of debits in books not in bank, deduction of credits in books not in bank, and the resulting balance per bank. The report must be available in both summary and detailed formats, with the detailed version listing every reconciling item with its description, amount, age, and status. Reports must support dual-language output (Arabic and English) and must include digital signatures of the preparer and reviewer. The system must maintain archived copies of all reconciliation reports for seven years per QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI record retention requirements. Reports must be exportable in PDF with Arabic RTL formatting and in Excel for further analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R014	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Stale and Aged Reconciling Items Management	The system must identify and manage stale reconciling items that have remained unmatched or unresolved beyond configurable aging thresholds. Default aging buckets must be 0-7 days, 8-15 days, 16-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, and over 90 days. Items aged beyond 30 days must trigger mandatory review by the treasury manager with a documented action plan. Items aged beyond 90 days must require CFO approval to either write off (below a configurable threshold of USD 500 equivalent), escalate to the bank for investigation, or refer to internal audit. For container shipping operations, common sources of long-outstanding reconciling items include port authority refunds for overpaid terminal charges at Khalifa Port and JNPT that can take 60-90 days to process, and freight customer payment disputes where the shipper contests demurrage or detention charges. The system must provide an aging analysis report sortable by entity, bank, amount, and item type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R015	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Machine Learning Model Training and Feedback Loop	The system must implement a continuous learning feedback loop for the AI matching engine, incorporating treasury analyst actions on manually matched and corrected items to improve future automatic matching accuracy. When an analyst matches an item that the system failed to auto-match, the system must capture the matching criteria used and add it to the training dataset. When an analyst overrides an incorrect auto-match, the system must record the correction and retrain the model to avoid similar errors. The training pipeline must support incremental learning without requiring full model retraining, processing new feedback within 24 hours. Model performance metrics (precision, recall, F1 score) must be tracked per bank, per entity, and per transaction category. The system must maintain a minimum of three model versions for rollback capability and must alert the treasury administrator if model accuracy drops below the 90% auto-match target. All training data must be anonymized per PDPL (KSA) and DPDP Act (India) requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R016	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Reconciliation Exception Handling for Bulk Transactions	The system must handle reconciliation exceptions specific to bulk transactions common in container shipping, including consolidated freight collection deposits where a shipping agent deposits a single cheque or wire transfer covering multiple bills of lading, and bulk port disbursement payments where the port agent sends a single funding request covering multiple vessel calls. For consolidated deposits at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, the system must support splitting a single bank statement credit into multiple ERP freight receipt entries based on a remittance advice or deposit breakdown provided by the agent. For bulk disbursements at JNPT, the system must match a single outgoing wire against a proforma disbursement account covering pilotage, berth hire, channel dues, and port health charges for a specific vessel call. The splitting and grouping logic must be configurable per agent and per port, with templates pre-defined for recurring transaction patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-003-R017	FEAT-040-1-003	MOD-040	Inter-Branch and Inter-Bank Transfer Reconciliation	The system must reconcile inter-branch transfers (transfers between accounts at different branches of the same bank) and inter-bank transfers (transfers between accounts at different banks) where the debit and credit appear on separate bank statements with potentially different value dates and references. The system must correlate these paired transactions using the payment reference, amount, and expected settlement timeline. For example, when treasury transfers surplus funds from the freight collection account at Emirates NBD Jebel Ali branch to the operating account at Emirates NBD DIFC branch, the system must match the debit on the source account statement with the corresponding credit on the destination account statement. Unmatched transfers where one leg has appeared but the contra leg has not must be tracked as in-transit funds, with automatic alerts if the contra leg does not appear within the expected settlement period (same day for intra-bank, T+1 for inter-bank domestic, T+2 for international transfers).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R001	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Physical Cash Pool Structure Configuration	The system must support configuration of physical cash pooling structures where actual fund movements occur between participating accounts and a designated master (header) account. Each pool must define a master account, one or more participating subsidiary accounts, the sweep direction (upward to master, downward from master, or bidirectional), the sweep trigger type (zero-balance, target-balance, or threshold-based), and the sweep frequency (real-time, intra-day at specified times, or end-of-day). For the container shipping group, the typical structure involves a USD master account at the Qatar entity receiving upward sweeps from freight collection accounts across all four entities, with downward sweeps to operational disbursement accounts at each port. The system must support a minimum of 20 pool structures running simultaneously and must validate that no account participates in more than one physical pool to prevent circular sweeping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R002	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Notional Cash Pool Configuration and Interest Optimization	The system must support notional (virtual) cash pooling where balances are consolidated for interest optimization purposes without physical fund movement. The notional pool must calculate the net pool balance by aggregating all participating account balances (converting to the pool base currency using configured exchange rates) and must apply a tiered interest rate schedule based on the net balance. For GCC entities where Islamic banking principles may apply, the system must support Murabaha-based return calculations as an alternative to conventional interest for Sharia-compliant accounts at banks like Qatar Islamic Bank, Dubai Islamic Bank, and Al Rajhi Bank. The system must generate daily notional pooling statements showing each participant's contribution to the pool, the blended interest rate achieved, and the interest advantage gained compared to standalone account rates. The interest advantage must be allocated back to participating entities based on configurable methods: pro-rata by balance contribution, flat split, or custom allocation percentages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R003	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Zero-Balance Account Sweep Execution	The system must execute zero-balance sweeps that transfer the entire available balance from participating accounts to the master account at the configured frequency. The sweep must calculate the available balance net of any minimum balance requirements imposed by the bank, pending uncleared cheque deposits, and hold amounts for anticipated same-day payments. For freight collection accounts at Jebel Ali and Hamad Port that receive numerous intra-day deposits, the system must support multiple intra-day sweep cycles (configurable, default three times daily at 10:00, 14:00, and 16:00 local time) to maximize the concentration of funds in the master account for overnight investment. The zero-balance sweep must generate payment instructions in the appropriate format for each bank (MT101 for SWIFT-connected banks, host-to-host file formats for locally connected banks) and must obtain automated approval if the sweep amount is within pre-authorized limits, or route for manual approval if it exceeds the limit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R004	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Target-Balance Sweep Configuration and Execution	The system must support target-balance sweeping where funds are transferred to maintain a specified target balance in each participating account. The target balance must be configurable per account and must support fixed amounts (for example, QAR 500,000 retained in the Hamad Port disbursement account), dynamic amounts based on a rolling average of the past N days' outflows (configurable, default 5 business days), or formula-based amounts linked to scheduled upcoming payments within a configurable horizon (default 3 business days). When the actual balance exceeds the target, the surplus must be swept upward to the master account. When the actual balance falls below the target, the deficit must be funded downward from the master account. The system must enforce a minimum sweep amount threshold (configurable, default USD 10,000 equivalent) to avoid generating excessively small transfer instructions that incur bank charges disproportionate to the amount moved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R005	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Cross-Border Sweep Regulatory Compliance	The system must enforce regulatory constraints on cross-border fund sweeps between entities in different jurisdictions. For QAR and USD sweeps from Qatar, QCB regulations on capital outflows must be validated. For AED sweeps from UAE, CBUAE Large Value Transfer System (UAEFTS) rules must be followed for amounts exceeding AED 50,000. For SAR sweeps from KSA, SAMA regulations on intercompany fund transfers must be enforced, including purpose code documentation. For INR sweeps from India, RBI guidelines under FEMA must be strictly adhered to, particularly the requirement that outward remittances from India require valid purpose codes (P-codes) and supporting documentation such as freight invoices or charter party agreements. The system must automatically attach the appropriate regulatory purpose code to each cross-border sweep instruction, maintain a complete audit trail of compliance documentation, and prevent sweep execution if mandatory compliance fields are incomplete.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R006	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Multi-Currency Sweep with FX Conversion	The system must handle sweeps between accounts denominated in different currencies, automatically triggering foreign exchange conversion as part of the sweep process. For example, sweeping surplus INR from the JNPT freight collection account to the USD master account in Qatar requires INR-to-USD conversion. The system must source the FX rate from the configured rate provider (bank API, Reuters, or manual entry), apply the rate to calculate the destination amount, and include the FX rate details in the sweep instruction. The system must support configurable FX rate tolerance bands (default: 50 basis points from mid-market rate) and must reject sweep execution if the offered rate exceeds the tolerance, alerting treasury to negotiate a better rate. For pegged currencies (QAR/USD at 3.64, AED/USD at 3.6725), the system must validate that the conversion rate does not deviate from the peg rate by more than the configurable spread tolerance (default: 15 basis points).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R007	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Scheduling and Business Day Calendar Integration	The system must schedule sweep executions based on configurable timing parameters that respect the business day calendars of both the source and destination jurisdictions. A sweep from a QAR account in Qatar to a USD account in UAE must only execute on days that are business days in both Qatar and UAE. The system must handle the complexity of different weekends (Friday-Saturday in GCC versus Saturday-Sunday in India) and must not attempt sweeps involving Indian accounts on Indian bank holidays or GCC accounts on GCC holidays. For end-of-day sweeps, the system must respect each bank's cut-off times (typically between 13:00 and 15:00 local time for same-day value transfers) and must reschedule sweeps that miss the cut-off to the next valid business day with appropriate notification to treasury. The system must support holiday calendar overrides for ad-hoc bank closures such as those declared for extreme weather events or national mourning periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R008	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Instruction Generation and Bank Connectivity	The system must generate sweep fund transfer instructions in the appropriate format for each banking partner's connectivity channel. Supported formats must include SWIFT MT101 (Request for Transfer) for banks connected via SWIFT Alliance Lite2 or service bureau, ISO 20022 pain.001 (Customer Credit Transfer Initiation) for banks supporting the newer standard, and proprietary host-to-host file formats for banks like QNB (Qatar), Emirates NBD (UAE), Al Rajhi (KSA), and HDFC/ICICI (India). The system must support multiple transmission protocols including SFTP, SWIFT FileAct, API (REST and SOAP), and EBICS. Each bank's connectivity profile must be pre-configured with the endpoint URL, authentication credentials (encrypted), file format template, acknowledgment handling rules, and retry logic for failed transmissions (default: three retries with exponential backoff). The system must log all transmission attempts with timestamps and status for troubleshooting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R009	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Execution Monitoring and Status Tracking	The system must provide real-time monitoring of all sweep executions showing the current status of each sweep cycle: scheduled, initiated, instruction sent, acknowledged by bank, executed, confirmed, or failed. For each sweep transaction, the monitoring dashboard must display the source account, destination account, amount, currency, FX rate if applicable, instruction timestamp, bank acknowledgment timestamp, execution timestamp, and value date. Failed sweeps must display the failure reason (insufficient funds, bank rejection, connectivity error, compliance block, cut-off time exceeded) and must provide one-click retry or manual override options. The monitoring view must support filtering by pool, entity, bank, status, and date range. An aggregate view must show the total swept volume for the day by pool and by currency, comparing against historical daily averages. Email and SMS notifications must be sent to designated treasury personnel for failed sweeps and for sweeps exceeding configurable amount thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R010	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Accounting for Cross-Entity Sweeps	The system must automatically generate intercompany loan accounting entries for cross-entity sweeps to maintain proper legal and tax documentation between separate legal entities. When funds are swept from the UAE entity's collection account to the Qatar entity's master account, the system must create a receivable in the UAE entity's books and a corresponding payable in the Qatar entity's books. Interest must be calculated on outstanding intercompany balances using the group's transfer pricing rate (configurable, compliant with OECD arm's length principles and GCC transfer pricing regulations). The system must generate monthly intercompany loan statements showing opening balance, sweeps in, sweeps out, interest accrued, settlements made, and closing balance for each bilateral relationship. These statements must be auto-reconciled between entities and must flag any discrepancies for treasury review. The intercompany accounting entries must integrate with MOD-030 (General Ledger) posting rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R011	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Emergency Sweep Override and Manual Intervention	The system must support emergency manual sweep operations that bypass the scheduled automation, allowing treasury managers to initiate ad-hoc fund movements for urgent operational needs. Common scenarios in container shipping include emergency vessel disbursement funding when a vessel arrives at Hamad Port or JNPT outside the normal schedule requiring immediate berthing and port charges payment, urgent bunker fuel payments to avoid vessel detention, or sudden regulatory payment requirements such as customs duty demands at Khalifa Port. Emergency sweeps must require treasury manager authorization (or CFO authorization if exceeding the treasury manager's limit), must be flagged distinctly in all reports and audit trails, and must include a mandatory justification comment. The system must impose a daily limit on emergency sweep volumes per entity (configurable, default USD 2,000,000) to prevent misuse, with any breach of the daily limit requiring CFO approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R012	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Reversal and Error Correction	The system must support the reversal of executed sweeps when errors are identified, such as incorrect sweep amounts due to balance calculation timing, sweeps executed against the wrong accounts, or sweeps that should have been blocked by regulatory compliance rules. The reversal process must generate a contra fund transfer instruction to return funds from the destination to the source, must reverse all associated accounting entries (including intercompany entries for cross-entity sweeps and FX entries for cross-currency sweeps), and must update the cash position dashboard to reflect the corrected balances. Reversals must require dual authorization (initiator plus approver), must be time-bound (reversals must be requested within two business days of the original sweep execution), and must generate a detailed reversal audit trail linking the original sweep transaction, the reversal reason, the approver, and the reversal confirmation from the bank. The system must track reversal rates per pool and per bank for operational quality monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R013	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Cash Pool Performance Analytics and Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on cash pool performance, including daily, monthly, and quarterly reports showing total volume swept (by pool, by currency, by entity), average daily pool balance, peak and trough pool balances, interest earned or saved through pooling compared to standalone account management, foreign exchange impact of cross-currency sweeps, and bank charges incurred for sweep transactions. The analytics must calculate the total treasury value-add from cash pooling operations expressed in USD, comparing the actual interest earned on concentrated balances against the hypothetical interest that would have been earned on fragmented balances across individual accounts. Reports must support export in PDF with bilingual Arabic/English headers and in Excel for further analysis. The system must also generate regulatory reports required by QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI on intercompany fund movements, formatted per each regulator's specific template requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R014	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Rule Simulation and Testing Environment	The system must provide a simulation environment where treasury can model and test new sweep rules and pool configurations against historical actual data before activating them in production. The simulation must accept a date range of historical data, apply the proposed sweep rules, and generate a simulated result showing what sweeps would have been triggered, the amounts, the timing, and the resulting account balances compared to what actually occurred. For container shipping operations with seasonal volume variations (peak season from August to November for GCC-India trade and lean season from January to March), the simulation must be run across at least one full seasonal cycle to validate rule robustness. The simulation must output key metrics including projected auto-sweep count, projected manual intervention count, estimated interest optimization, and estimated bank charges. Simulation results must be saveable for comparison across multiple rule configurations and must support approval workflow for promoting tested rules to production.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R015	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Minimum and Maximum Sweep Threshold Controls	The system must enforce minimum and maximum sweep amount thresholds to prevent operationally inefficient or risk-excessive transfers. The minimum sweep threshold (configurable per pool and per account, default USD 10,000 equivalent) must prevent generation of small-value sweep instructions where the bank transfer charges would erode the interest benefit of concentration. The maximum sweep threshold (configurable per pool and per account, default USD 5,000,000 equivalent) must prevent excessively large single transfers that could trigger enhanced due diligence at correspondent banks or breach internal risk limits. When the calculated sweep amount exceeds the maximum threshold, the system must split the sweep into multiple transactions each within the threshold, staggered across the sweep window. For cross-border sweeps from India subject to RBI LRS (Liberalised Remittance Scheme) limits, the system must validate that individual and aggregate sweep amounts comply with the annual remittance limits applicable to the entity type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R016	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Sweep Failure Recovery and Retry Mechanism	The system must implement a robust failure recovery mechanism for sweep transactions that fail at any stage of execution. Failure scenarios must include bank connectivity timeouts (common during peak processing hours at Jebel Ali and JNPT), insufficient funds at the source account due to concurrent debits, bank system maintenance windows (typically Friday evenings for GCC banks and second/fourth Saturday for Indian nationalized banks), and rejection by the beneficiary bank due to compliance screening delays. The retry mechanism must support configurable retry attempts (default three), retry intervals (default 30 minutes, 60 minutes, and 120 minutes for successive retries), and alternate routing rules (for example, if the primary SWIFT path fails, attempt via the backup host-to-host channel). After all retries are exhausted, the system must mark the sweep as permanently failed, generate a critical alert to the treasury manager and operations team, and create a manual follow-up task with all relevant details including the failed instruction file and bank error responses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R017	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Intra-Day Liquidity Monitoring for Sweep Optimization	The system must monitor intra-day liquidity positions across all pool participant accounts to optimize sweep timing and amounts. The monitoring must track real-time incoming credits (freight collections arriving from shipping agents and direct customers), outgoing debits (scheduled and ad-hoc payments), and pending transactions (cheques deposited but not yet cleared, pre-authorized debits not yet executed). Based on this intra-day visibility, the system must dynamically adjust sweep amounts to avoid over-sweeping (which would require a costly reverse sweep later) or under-sweeping (which would leave idle balances in subsidiary accounts). For the Hamad Port and Jebel Ali operations where freight collections typically peak between 10:00 and 14:00 local time, the system must learn these patterns and schedule intra-day sweeps at optimal times to capture the majority of daily collections while leaving sufficient buffer for afternoon port charge payments. The intra-day monitoring must present a timeline visualization showing cumulative inflows, outflows, and net position by hour.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-1-004-R018	FEAT-040-1-004	MOD-040	Cash Pool Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance Log	The system must maintain a comprehensive, tamper-proof audit trail for all cash pooling and sweeping activities, capturing every configuration change, sweep execution, reversal, manual override, and approval action. Each audit entry must record the action type, timestamp in UTC, the user who performed the action, the user who approved it (if applicable), all monetary details (amounts, currencies, FX rates), source and destination accounts, the sweep rule that triggered the action, and any regulatory compliance codes applied. The audit trail must be stored immutably with cryptographic hashing to prevent retroactive modification, meeting the requirements of QCB Circular 79/2019 on electronic record-keeping, CBUAE regulatory framework for stored values, SAMA guidelines on interbank fund transfers, and RBI master directions on intercompany transactions. The audit data must be searchable by date range, entity, pool, account, amount range, and action type, and must support export in formats required by external auditors. Retention period must be a minimum of ten years for all pooling and sweeping records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R001	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	FX Exposure Auto-Discovery from Shipping Transactions	The system must automatically discover and aggregate foreign exchange exposures from all open shipping transactions including freight invoices, demurrage charges, port disbursement accounts at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV), container depot charges, bunker fuel procurement, and intercompany receivables/payables. Exposures must be categorized by currency pair (e.g., USD/QAR, USD/INR, AED/SAR), tenor bucket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 91-180, 181-365 days), and legal entity. The discovery engine must run at minimum every 4 hours and support on-demand refresh with full audit trail of exposure changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R002	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Forward Contract Booking and Lifecycle Management	The system must support booking FX forward contracts with counterparty banks specifying deal date, settlement date, currency pair, notional amount, forward rate, and premium or discount points. Each forward contract must be linked to one or more underlying shipping exposures such as a specific Bill of Lading freight receivable or a port disbursement payable at Jebel Ali or JNPT. The system must track the full lifecycle including initiation, confirmation matching with bank SWIFT MT300 messages, partial utilization against shipping settlements, rollover requests, early termination with breakage cost calculation, and final settlement with realized gain/loss booking to the general ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R003	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Natural Hedge Identification and Netting	The system must automatically identify natural hedging opportunities where receivables and payables in the same foreign currency can be netted to reduce gross exposure. For example, USD freight revenue from CIF shipments arriving at Hamad Port can offset USD bunker fuel costs and USD-denominated container lease payments. The netting engine must respect legal entity boundaries unless intercompany netting agreements are configured, consider settlement date alignment within a configurable tolerance window (default 7 days), and produce a net exposure report showing gross long, gross short, and net position per currency pair per entity. AI recommendations must suggest optimal netting strategies with projected savings on hedging costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R005	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Hedge Effectiveness Testing under IFRS 9	The system must support prospective and retrospective hedge effectiveness testing as required by IFRS 9 for hedge accounting qualification. Prospective testing must use the critical terms match method or the dollar offset method comparing the change in fair value of the hedging instrument against the hedged item. Retrospective testing must apply the hypothetical derivative method with a configurable effectiveness threshold (default 80%-125% range). The system must generate hedge documentation packages including risk management objective, hedging strategy description for shipping-specific risks such as freight rate FX exposure on FOB vs CIF INCOTERMS, identification of the hedged item and hedging instrument, and effectiveness assessment results. Failed effectiveness tests must trigger automatic de-designation alerts to treasury managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R006	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	FX Exposure Dashboard with AI Forecasting	The system must provide a real-time FX exposure dashboard displaying current net exposure by currency pair, entity, and time bucket with visual heat maps indicating concentration risk. The dashboard must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all monetary values formatted according to locale conventions (e.g., QAR uses Arabic-Indic numerals option, INR uses lakhs/crores grouping). An AI forecasting module must predict future FX exposure based on the vessel schedule, historical shipping volumes, seasonal freight rate patterns on the Qatar-India and UAE-India trade lanes, and contracted vs spot cargo mix. Forecasts must project 30, 60, 90, and 180-day forward exposures with confidence intervals and recommended hedging actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R007	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	FX Option and Structured Product Support	The system must support booking and managing FX options including vanilla European and American puts and calls, as well as structured products such as zero-cost collars, participating forwards, and knock-in/knock-out barriers commonly used in Gulf shipping treasury operations. Each option must record strike price, premium paid or received, expiry date, notional amount, and the underlying shipping exposure being hedged. The system must calculate option Greeks (delta, gamma, vega, theta) for portfolio risk management and support premium amortization over the option life in accordance with IFRS 9 time value accounting elections. Premium payments must be tracked in the originating entity's functional currency with appropriate FX translation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R008	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Regulatory Compliance for FX Transactions by Jurisdiction	The system must enforce jurisdiction-specific FX regulations: for Qatar, compliance with QCB Circular instructions on forward contract documentation and reporting to Qatar Financial Markets Authority; for UAE, adherence to CBUAE regulations on derivative transactions and DFSA rules if operating in DIFC; for KSA, SAMA guidelines on hedging instruments permissible under Islamic finance frameworks including Wa'ad-based structures; for India, RBI Master Direction on Risk Management and Inter-Bank Dealings covering permissible hedge tenors, underlying exposure documentation requirements for AD Category-I banks, and mandatory reporting via the RBI's trade repository. Each booking must validate regulatory eligibility before confirmation and flag non-compliant structures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R009	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Counterparty Bank Limit and Credit Risk Monitoring	The system must maintain counterparty credit limits for each bank with which FX hedging transactions are executed. Limits must be configurable by counterparty, product type (forwards, options, swaps), tenor bucket, and legal entity. Real-time utilization tracking must aggregate mark-to-market exposure plus potential future exposure using a configurable add-on percentage per tenor. The system must generate alerts when utilization exceeds configurable thresholds (e.g., 75%, 90%, 100%) and must hard-block new deal entry when the limit is fully utilized unless an authorized treasury manager provides override approval with documented justification. Counterparty limit data must support ISDA CSA margin call calculations for collateralized relationships.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R010	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	FX Deal Confirmation and Settlement Workflow	The system must implement a multi-step workflow for FX deal confirmation: deal capture by trader, verification by middle office against bank confirmation (SWIFT MT300 or email), approval by treasury manager based on configurable authority matrix (e.g., deals above USD 1 million require CFO approval), and settlement instruction generation to the payment module. Settlement must support gross settlement per deal or net settlement where bilateral netting agreements exist with the counterparty bank. The system must track settlement status, flag overdue settlements, and automatically reconcile settled amounts against bank statements received via MT940/MT950 or CAMT.053 formats. All workflow steps must capture timestamps, user IDs, and support Arabic-language approval comments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R011	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Exposure Reporting by Vessel and Voyage	The system must generate FX exposure reports segmented by vessel, voyage number, and trade lane to enable shipping operations managers to understand currency risk at the operational level. For each voyage, the report must show expected revenue currencies (based on freight contract terms and INCOTERMS — FOB typically USD, CIF may include local currency components), expected cost currencies (port charges in QAR at Hamad, AED at Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR at JNPT, bunker fuel in USD), net voyage-level currency exposure, and any hedges allocated against the voyage. Reports must be exportable in PDF (supporting Arabic RTL formatting) and Excel formats with drill-down capability from voyage summary to individual transaction detail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R012	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Hedge Ratio Optimization Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven hedge ratio optimization engine that recommends the optimal percentage of FX exposure to hedge per currency pair based on historical volatility analysis, cost of hedging (forward points and option premiums), the entity's risk appetite parameters configured by treasury policy, and cash flow certainty of the underlying shipping exposures. The engine must distinguish between highly probable exposures (e.g., contracted freight on confirmed vessel bookings) and forecast exposures (e.g., estimated spot cargo revenue) and recommend different hedge ratios accordingly. Optimization results must be presented with scenario analysis showing P&L impact under base case, stress case (e.g., 10% adverse FX move), and best case scenarios for treasury committee review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R002	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	Differential THC by Movement Type	The system must apply differential THC rates based on container movement type including import discharge, export loading, transshipment relay, and empty repositioning. Transshipment containers at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali must receive discounted THC rates as configured per terminal operator agreements. The system must automatically detect movement type from the vessel manifest or booking data, cross-referencing Bill of Lading direction codes (I for import, E for export, T for transshipment) and apply the correct THC schedule without manual intervention, achieving 95% or higher automated classification accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R013	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Multi-Entity Consolidated Exposure View	The system must provide a consolidated FX exposure view across all legal entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with the ability to drill down into individual entity positions. Consolidation must handle intercompany exposures by either including or excluding them based on user selection, and must translate all exposures into a common reporting currency (configurable, default USD) using current spot rates. The consolidated view must identify cross-entity natural hedges where, for example, the Qatar entity has USD receivables while the India entity has USD payables from the same trade lane. The system must respect entity-level access controls ensuring that India entity users cannot view Qatar entity hedge details unless granted explicit cross-entity visibility permissions in the role-based access control configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R014	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Hedge Accounting Journal Entry Generation	The system must automatically generate accounting journal entries for all hedge-related events including initial recognition of hedge instruments at fair value, periodic mark-to-market adjustments routed to either P&L or OCI depending on hedge designation (fair value hedge vs cash flow hedge under IFRS 9), de-designation entries when hedge relationships are discontinued, reclassification from OCI to P&L when the hedged forecast transaction affects earnings (e.g., when the hedged freight revenue is recognized), and settlement entries recording realized gains or losses. Journal entries must use entity-specific chart of accounts, support dual-dating with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates for KSA and Qatar entities, and include references to the underlying shipping transaction (Bill of Lading number, voyage ID) for audit traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R015	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	FX Policy Compliance Monitoring and Alerts	The system must continuously monitor compliance with the organization's FX risk management policy and generate alerts for policy breaches. Configurable policy parameters must include maximum unhedged exposure per currency pair, minimum hedge ratio for confirmed exposures, maximum tenor for forward contracts, approved hedging instruments (e.g., no exotic options without board approval), approved counterparty banks, and maximum single-deal size by user authority level. The system must produce a daily policy compliance dashboard showing green/amber/red status per parameter per entity, a weekly policy exception report listing all breaches with responsible treasury officer details, and a monthly board-level FX risk summary. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notifications, email, and optionally SMS for critical threshold breaches, with all alert text available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-001-R016	FEAT-040-2-001	MOD-040	Offline FX Deal Capture and Synchronization	The system must support offline capture of FX deal details when network connectivity to the central treasury system is unavailable, a scenario common during vessel port calls or when treasury staff operate from remote locations in the Gulf region. Offline-captured deals must be stored locally with full deal economics (currency pair, notional, rate, dates, counterparty) and flagged as provisional pending online synchronization. Upon reconnection, the system must automatically sync offline deals, perform validation against current market rates to flag any stale-rate deals (rate deviation exceeding configurable threshold, default 50 basis points from current mid-market), check counterparty limits, and route deals through the standard approval workflow. Conflict resolution must handle cases where the same exposure was hedged by two different users offline, presenting both deals for manual resolution by the treasury manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R001	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Import LC Issuance Request and Bank Submission	The system must enable creation of import Letter of Credit issuance requests linked to purchase orders or shipping contracts for cargo arriving at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), or JNPT (INNHV). The LC application must capture all UCP 600 required fields including applicant and beneficiary details, LC amount and currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), expiry date and place, latest shipment date, port of loading and discharge, goods description matching container cargo specifications (container type — 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer — quantity, commodity), required documents list (Bill of Lading, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, insurance certificate for CIF terms), and special conditions. The system must generate the LC application in SWIFT MT700 format for electronic submission to the issuing bank and track bank acknowledgment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R002	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Export LC Advising and Confirmation Tracking	The system must manage inbound export LCs received from overseas buyers' banks, parsing SWIFT MT700/MT710 messages to auto-populate LC details. The system must validate LC terms against the underlying sales contract or freight agreement, flagging discrepancies such as mismatched cargo descriptions, short LC validity periods that may not allow timely document presentation after vessel departure, or missing required documents. For each export LC, the system must track the advising bank, confirming bank (if confirmed), available with bank, and reimbursing bank. The system must support LC confirmation requests to the advising bank with associated confirmation fee tracking. All LC terms must be reviewable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering of bank names, addresses, and special conditions text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R003	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Amendment Processing and Version Control	The system must support LC amendment workflows for both import and export LCs. Amendment requests must capture the specific clause being modified (amount increase/decrease, expiry extension, shipment date change, document requirement addition/removal, port of discharge change) with before and after values and amendment reason. Each amendment must maintain version history so the complete evolution of LC terms is traceable. For import LCs, the system must generate SWIFT MT707 amendment messages for bank submission. For export LCs, the system must parse incoming MT707 amendments and present them for acceptance or rejection by the beneficiary. Amendment fees charged by banks must be automatically recorded and allocated to the relevant shipping transaction cost center. The system must enforce that partial shipment and transshipment clauses are correctly maintained through amendments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R004	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Document Preparation and Presentation Management	The system must provide a document preparation workspace for export LC presentations, listing all required documents per the LC terms and tracking preparation status of each. The system must validate document consistency: Bill of Lading details (shipper, consignee, notify party, port of loading, port of discharge, container numbers, seal numbers, goods description, weight) must match the LC requirements and the commercial invoice. The system must check for common discrepancies defined in ISBP 745 such as late presentation (documents presented after the LC presentation period, typically 21 days from B/L date), stale documents, inconsistent marks and numbers across documents, and missing endorsements. An AI-powered discrepancy checker must scan uploaded document images using OCR and flag potential discrepancies before submission to the negotiating bank, reducing rejection rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R005	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Discrepancy Resolution Workflow	The system must manage the discrepancy resolution process when the presenting or issuing bank identifies discrepancies in LC document presentations. The workflow must capture each discrepancy description, the bank's reference, and initiate a resolution path: request applicant waiver (for import LCs where the buyer agrees to accept discrepant documents), correct and re-present documents within the presentation period, or negotiate with the issuing bank. The system must track the 5-banking-day examination period per UCP 600 Article 14, generating alerts as the deadline approaches. Resolution history must be maintained for each presentation. For shipping-specific discrepancies such as on-deck B/L when LC requires shipped on board, claused B/L when clean B/L required, or transshipment when prohibited, the system must provide guidance text explaining the discrepancy and typical resolution approaches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R006	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Liability and Margin Tracking	The system must track LC-related liabilities and bank margin deposits for all open import LCs. When an import LC is issued, the system must record the contingent liability (off-balance-sheet) and any cash margin deposited with the issuing bank (typically 10-100% of LC value depending on the applicant's credit facility terms). Margin amounts must be classified as restricted cash on the balance sheet. As LC drawings occur, the system must reduce the contingent liability, release proportional margin if applicable, and create the corresponding trade payable. The system must generate an LC liability aging report showing outstanding LC commitments by maturity date, currency, issuing bank, and entity. For entities in Qatar and KSA, the system must support tracking margins in both conventional and Islamic banking structures (Murabaha-based LC facilities) with appropriate Sharia-compliant accounting treatment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R007	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Fee and Charge Management	The system must comprehensively track all fees and charges associated with each LC throughout its lifecycle: issuance commission, advising fee, confirmation fee (if confirmed LC), amendment fee, document examination fee, discrepancy fee, negotiation or acceptance commission, reimbursement charges, courier and SWIFT charges, and stamp duty or government fees applicable in each jurisdiction. Fees must be captured in the charging currency with automatic conversion to the entity's functional currency at the transaction date exchange rate. The system must validate bank fee debit advices against expected fee schedules configured per bank relationship. Fee allocations must be posted to the correct cost center (linked to the shipping voyage or container movement) and must support accrual of anticipated fees for LCs where final charges have not yet been confirmed. Fee comparison reports must enable treasury to benchmark charges across banking relationships.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R008	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Standby LC (SBLC) Management for Shipping Operations	The system must support Standby Letters of Credit commonly used in container shipping for performance guarantees to port authorities (e.g., guaranteeing minimum volume commitments at Jebel Ali or Hamad Port), securing container depot leases, and collateralizing freight payment obligations to liner principals. SBLC management must include issuance request workflow, tracking of SBLC terms (amount, beneficiary, expiry, auto-renewal clauses, drawing conditions), renewal and expiry management with advance alerts (configurable, default 60 and 30 days before expiry), and claim/drawing processing. The system must distinguish between financial SBLCs (ISP98 governed) and performance SBLCs and track the underlying obligation each SBLC secures. SBLC amounts must be included in the overall credit facility utilization monitoring and reported as contingent liabilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R009	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC-Linked Payment and Settlement Processing	The system must manage LC payment settlement types including sight payment (payment upon compliant presentation), deferred payment (payment at a specified future date, e.g., 90 days from B/L date), acceptance (bank accepts a time draft drawn under the LC), and negotiation. For deferred payment and acceptance LCs, the system must track the maturity date of each drawing, create the corresponding payable or receivable at the acceptance/deferred payment undertaking date, and generate payment instructions on the maturity date. The system must support LC discount/forfaiting workflows where the beneficiary sells accepted drafts to a bank at a discount, recording the discount charge and the accelerated receipt of funds. Settlement must integrate with the cash management module to update bank account balances and with the accounts payable/receivable modules to close the related trade transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R010	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Transferable and Back-to-Back LC Support	The system must support transferable LCs where the first beneficiary (typically a trading intermediary in the shipping chain) transfers all or part of the LC to a second beneficiary (the actual shipper or supplier). Transfer requests must capture the original LC reference, transfer amount (which may be less than the original), substitution of invoices, and any changes to shipment dates or presentation period. For back-to-back LC structures commonly used in Gulf-region commodity trading shipped via container, the system must link the master LC (received from the end buyer) to the child LC (issued to the supplier), ensuring the child LC terms are compatible with the master LC and tracking the margin between the two. Document flow must be managed so that the intermediary's documents are substituted before presentation under the master LC.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R011	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	Islamic LC (Murabaha/Wakala) Processing	The system must support Islamic trade finance structures for LC operations, as required by entities operating under Sharia-compliant banking arrangements in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. Supported structures must include Murabaha-based LC (where the bank purchases the goods and resells to the applicant at a markup), Wakala-based LC (where the bank acts as agent), and Musharakah-based LC for partnership arrangements. The system must correctly account for the Islamic financing structure: recording the bank's purchase and resale rather than a loan, tracking the profit element separately from the goods cost, and ensuring no interest (riba) components appear in the accounting entries. The system must generate Sharia-compliant documentation and reports with Arabic-language contract terminology. Profit rate benchmarks must reference Islamic interbank rates (e.g., EIBOR for Islamic facilities) rather than conventional interest rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R005	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Agreement Amendment and Version Control	The system must maintain a complete version history of every lease agreement, tracking all amendments with effective dates, changed fields, previous values, and new values. Amendments must go through the same approval workflow as new agreements. The system must support amendments to per-diem rates, depot lists, container type additions or removals, free-day allowances, and liability cap changes. Each version must be independently retrievable and printable in both Arabic and English. The system must prevent backdating amendments to periods that have already been invoiced unless a finance controller override is applied with documented justification stored in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R012	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Document Digitization and OCR Processing	The system must support digital upload and OCR processing of all LC-related documents including the LC instrument itself, amendments, Bills of Lading, commercial invoices, packing lists, certificates of origin, insurance certificates, and inspection certificates. OCR must extract key data fields from scanned documents and populate structured records for validation against LC terms. The system must handle documents in English and Arabic, including bilingual documents common in Gulf shipping trade. Extracted data must be presented for user verification before acceptance. The system must maintain both the original document image and the extracted structured data, with version tracking for document corrections. Document storage must comply with data residency requirements: Qatar entity documents stored in Qatar-based infrastructure, UAE in UAE, and so forth per local data protection regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R013	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Portfolio Risk and Exposure Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive LC portfolio reports including total outstanding LC commitments by currency, maturity, issuing/confirming bank, entity, and trade corridor (e.g., India-Qatar, UAE-India). Risk exposure reports must identify concentration risk by single counterparty beneficiary or applicant, single bank issuer, single country, and single commodity type shipped in containers. The system must calculate and report potential cash outflow exposure for import LCs (maximum drawing amount less amounts already drawn), confirmed export LC exposure by confirming bank, and unconfirmed export LC country risk. Reports must be available in both English and Arabic, with Hijri calendar date options for KSA and Qatar entities. The system must support regulatory reporting formats required by QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI for trade finance exposures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R014	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Expiry and Deadline Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive calendar of all LC-related deadlines including LC expiry date, latest shipment date, document presentation deadline (typically 21 days from shipment per UCP 600 Article 14(c) unless otherwise stated), amendment response deadlines, and deferred payment maturity dates. The system must generate proactive alerts at configurable intervals before each deadline (default: 30, 15, 7, 3, and 1 day(s) before). Overdue items must be escalated to the treasury manager and department head. The deadline calendar must integrate with the vessel schedule module so that if a vessel departure is delayed beyond the latest shipment date in an LC, the system immediately alerts the user to request an amendment. All deadline displays must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views, and must account for banking holidays in each jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) when calculating business day deadlines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R015	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	LC Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every LC transaction capturing all user actions (creation, modification, approval, rejection, document upload, presentation submission), system-generated events (status transitions, alerts triggered, deadline calculations), and external communications (SWIFT messages sent and received, bank correspondence). Each audit entry must record the timestamp (in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), user ID, user role, IP address, action description, and before/after values for any data changes. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using hash-chain verification. Compliance reports must support AML/CFT screening integration verifying that all LC parties (applicant, beneficiary, advising bank, issuing bank) are screened against sanctions lists (UN, OFAC, EU, local lists for Qatar/UAE/KSA/India). The system must flag and quarantine any LC involving a sanctioned party for compliance officer review before processing can continue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-002-R016	FEAT-040-2-002	MOD-040	AI-Powered LC Term Optimization and Risk Scoring	The system must provide AI-driven recommendations for LC term optimization based on historical transaction data, beneficiary payment behavior, trade lane risk profiles, and current market conditions. The AI engine must score each LC on a risk scale considering factors such as country risk of the beneficiary's jurisdiction, commodity price volatility for goods shipped in containers, historical discrepancy rates with the specific beneficiary, and the issuing bank's credit rating. For new LC issuance requests, the AI must recommend optimal LC type (sight vs deferred, confirmed vs unconfirmed), suggest appropriate document requirements based on the trade lane and INCOTERMS (e.g., CIF shipments from India to Qatar typically require specific insurance certificate clauses), and estimate total LC cost including all bank fees. Recommendations must be presented with confidence scores and supporting rationale in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R001	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Bank Guarantee Issuance Request Workflow	The system must support creation and approval of bank guarantee issuance requests with a multi-level approval workflow configurable by guarantee type, amount, and entity. The request must capture guarantee type (bid bond, performance guarantee, advance payment guarantee, retention money guarantee, customs guarantee, financial guarantee), beneficiary details, guarantee amount and currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), validity period with start and expiry dates, underlying contract or obligation reference (e.g., port terminal operating agreement, shipping agency contract, customs bond for container clearance at JNPT), guarantee text (in English and/or Arabic as required by the beneficiary), issuing bank preference, and any counter-guarantee requirements from a parent company entity. The approval workflow must enforce segregation of duties with separate requestor, reviewer, and approver roles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R002	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Type Classification and Template Management	The system must maintain a library of bank guarantee templates classified by type and jurisdiction. Shipping-specific guarantee types must include: customs bond guarantees for container clearance at GCC and Indian ports (with specific template variations for Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs ZATCA, and Indian Customs CBIC), performance guarantees for terminal handling agreements, bid bonds for port concession tenders (common at new port developments in Qatar and KSA), freight payment guarantees to liner principals, container deposit guarantees for equipment interchange agreements, and warehouse rent guarantees for container freight station operations. Each template must store standard text in both English and Arabic, configurable clauses, jurisdiction-specific legal requirements, and URDG 758 vs local law governing clause options. Templates must be version-controlled with approval workflow for template modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R003	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Lifecycle Status Tracking	The system must track each bank guarantee through its complete lifecycle with the following statuses: Draft (request initiated), Pending Approval (in approval workflow), Submitted to Bank (sent to issuing bank), Issued (guarantee instrument received from bank), Active (within validity period), Amendment Requested (modification in process), Amended (modification confirmed), Claim Received (beneficiary has made a demand), Claim Paid (demand honored), Expired (validity period ended), Cancelled (terminated before expiry), and Released (returned to issuing bank after obligation fulfillment). Status transitions must enforce business rules (e.g., cannot move to Cancelled without beneficiary release letter upload, cannot expire if an active claim exists). Each status change must record the date, user, and reason, and trigger configured notifications to relevant stakeholders including the treasury team, legal department, and the business unit responsible for the underlying obligation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R004	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Expiry and Renewal Management	The system must provide proactive management of guarantee expiry dates with configurable alert schedules (default: 90, 60, 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry). For guarantees with evergreen or auto-renewal clauses, the system must track the non-renewal notice period and generate alerts in advance of the notice deadline. The renewal workflow must support extending the guarantee validity with or without amount modification, generating the renewal request to the issuing bank with updated terms. For guarantees linked to shipping contracts with option periods (e.g., a 3-year terminal handling agreement with two 1-year extension options), the system must automatically calendar the potential guarantee renewal dates aligned with contract extension decisions. The system must report on guarantees expiring within configurable periods per entity, distinguish between those requiring renewal and those eligible for release, and calculate the estimated bank charges for renewals based on the fee schedule configured for each banking relationship.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R005	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Claim and Invocation Processing	The system must manage the guarantee claim process when a beneficiary invokes a bank guarantee. Claim processing must capture the claim date, claimed amount (which may be partial), claim basis (description of the alleged default), and supporting documentation uploaded by the beneficiary or forwarded by the issuing bank. The system must immediately notify treasury management, legal department, and the business unit responsible for the underlying obligation. A claim response workflow must be initiated with options to: accept the claim and authorize payment, dispute the claim with objection letter (capturing grounds for dispute such as non-compliance with guarantee terms, fraudulent demand, or expiry), negotiate a reduced settlement, or obtain a court injunction to prevent payment. The system must track the URDG 758 Article 20 five-business-day examination period for demand guarantees. All claim correspondence must be stored with the guarantee record for legal reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R006	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Customs Guarantee Management for Port Operations	The system must specifically support customs guarantee management for container shipping operations at each port jurisdiction. At Hamad Port (Qatar), the system must track customs bonds required by Qatar General Authority of Customs for temporary admission of containers and in-transit cargo. At Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE), customs guarantees issued to Dubai Customs and Abu Dhabi Customs must be managed with tracking of utilized vs available guarantee amounts for multiple clearance transactions against a single guarantee. At JNPT (India), the system must manage customs bank guarantees required by CBIC for provisional assessments, warehousing bonds, and container freight station operations. The system must monitor guarantee utilization percentages and generate alerts when remaining available amount falls below a configurable threshold (default 20%), triggering requests for guarantee amount increases to ensure uninterrupted container clearance operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R007	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Counter-Guarantee and Indirect Guarantee Tracking	The system must support counter-guarantee structures where a parent entity's bank issues a counter-guarantee to a local bank in another jurisdiction, which then issues the direct guarantee to the beneficiary. This is common in multi-entity shipping operations where, for example, the Qatar parent company's bank provides a counter-guarantee to an Indian bank to issue a customs guarantee at JNPT. The system must link the direct guarantee, counter-guarantee, and underlying obligation in a single view, tracking amounts, validity dates, and fees at each level. The system must ensure that counter-guarantee validity extends beyond the direct guarantee validity (minimum buffer configurable, default 15 days) and that amount coverage is adequate considering any currency differences between the counter-guarantee and direct guarantee. Amendment to either guarantee must trigger validation of consistency with the linked guarantee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R008	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Fee and Commission Tracking	The system must track all fees and commissions charged by issuing banks for guarantee facilities including issuance commission (typically charged quarterly or semi-annually as a percentage of guarantee amount), amendment fees, renewal fees, claim handling fees, SWIFT charges, and stamp duty or government levies applicable per jurisdiction. Fee accrual must be automated based on the fee schedule configured per banking relationship, with accrual entries posted to the general ledger on a monthly basis. The system must reconcile accrued fees against actual bank debit advices, flagging discrepancies for treasury review. Fee comparison reports must enable benchmarking across banking relationships per guarantee type and jurisdiction. For Islamic banking facilities in Qatar, UAE, and KSA, the system must handle guarantee fees structured as Kafalah fees (Sharia-compliant guarantee fee) with appropriate accounting treatment distinct from conventional interest-based commission structures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R009	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Credit Facility Utilization Monitoring	The system must monitor bank guarantee utilization against credit facility limits allocated for guarantee issuance. Facility limits must be configurable by bank, entity, guarantee type, and currency. Real-time utilization must show total facility limit, utilized amount (aggregate of all active guarantees), available amount, and pending requests that would further reduce availability if approved. The system must enforce that new guarantee issuance requests cannot exceed available facility limits unless explicitly overridden by an authorized treasury officer with documented justification. Facility limit reports must consolidate across all entities to provide a group-level view of guarantee facility utilization per banking relationship. The system must track facility expiry dates and renewal terms, generating alerts for upcoming facility renewals. Utilization data must feed into the overall banking relationship scorecard showing guarantee facilities alongside LC facilities, loan facilities, and other credit products.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R010	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Text Review and Legal Compliance Engine	The system must provide a guarantee text review engine that compares the issued guarantee instrument text against the approved template and the original request terms. Deviations must be flagged for legal review, including changes to governing law clause, demand conditions, expiry terms, amount, beneficiary details, and any non-standard clauses added by the issuing bank. The system must maintain a clause library of acceptable and unacceptable guarantee terms per jurisdiction: for example, guarantees governed by Qatar law must reference specific Qatar Civil Code articles, UAE guarantees may be subject to DIFC or onshore courts, KSA guarantees must comply with Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority requirements, and Indian guarantees must comply with Indian Contract Act provisions. The AI engine must highlight potentially problematic clauses such as unconditional demand clauses without fraud exception, joint and several liability extensions beyond the guaranteed obligation, and clauses that may conflict with the entity's corporate authority limitations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R011	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Received Guarantee Management and Beneficiary Tracking	The system must manage bank guarantees received from third parties where the shipping entity is the beneficiary, such as performance guarantees from sub-contractors, stevedoring companies, container depot operators, or freight forwarders. The system must record guarantee details including guarantor (issuing bank), principal (the obligor), guarantee amount and currency, validity period, governing rules (URDG 758 or local law), demand conditions, and the underlying contract reference. The system must track validity dates with expiry alerts, monitor the financial standing of the issuing bank, and manage the claim process if invocation becomes necessary. For guarantees received in Arabic (common from GCC-based banks), the system must support Arabic text storage and display with RTL rendering. The system must generate a report of all received guarantees with adequacy assessment comparing guarantee coverage against the outstanding obligation amount to identify under-secured positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R012	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Reporting and Contingent Liability Disclosure	The system must generate comprehensive guarantee reports for financial reporting and management purposes. The contingent liability report must list all outstanding guarantees issued, categorized by type, currency, maturity, issuing bank, beneficiary, and entity, with total contingent liability amounts for off-balance-sheet disclosure per IFRS 7 and IAS 37 requirements. The report must distinguish between guarantees where the probability of invocation is remote, possible, or probable, with provision amounts calculated for probable claims. Management reports must include guarantee portfolio analysis by expiry profile, currency distribution, bank concentration, and shipping operation linkage (by port, trade lane, or business segment). The system must support automated generation of notes to financial statements for guarantee disclosures, with output in both English and Arabic to support dual-language annual reports required for listed entities in Qatar (QSE) and KSA (Tadawul) stock exchanges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R013	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Multi-Entity Guarantee Consolidation and Intercompany Guarantees	The system must provide a consolidated view of all bank guarantees across all group entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with drill-down capability. Intercompany guarantees where one entity guarantees the obligations of another (e.g., Qatar parent guaranteeing India subsidiary's customs bond at JNPT, or UAE entity guaranteeing KSA entity's performance obligations at a Saudi port) must be specifically tracked with the guarantee chain visible in a hierarchical view. The consolidation must handle currency translation of guarantee amounts to a common reporting currency using period-end exchange rates. The system must identify and report on cross-entity guarantee dependencies to highlight group-level guarantee concentration risk. Elimination entries for intercompany guarantees in consolidated financial statements must be automatically suggested. Entity-level access controls must ensure users can only view guarantee details for entities within their authorized scope while group treasury can access the full consolidated portfolio.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R014	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Document Storage and Digital Vault	The system must provide a secure digital vault for storage of all guarantee-related documents including the original guarantee instrument (scanned copy or digital original), amendments, beneficiary acknowledgment letters, claim notices, release letters, bank correspondence, and internal approval documentation. Documents must be indexed by guarantee reference number, type, beneficiary, issuing bank, and entity for rapid retrieval. The vault must support full-text search across document content including Arabic-language documents using Arabic-aware text indexing. Document access must be controlled by role-based permissions with sensitive guarantee instruments (e.g., those containing confidential pricing or performance thresholds) restricted to authorized treasury and legal personnel. The system must maintain document retention in compliance with each jurisdiction's requirements: Qatar Commercial Companies Law record retention, UAE Commercial Transactions Law, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013 Section 128, with minimum retention of 10 years or as locally required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R015	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Amendment and Cancellation Processing	The system must support guarantee amendment workflows for modifications to amount (increase or decrease), validity period (extension or reduction), beneficiary details, guarantee text, and governing terms. Amendment requests must follow the same multi-level approval workflow as new guarantee issuance with the amendment reason documented. The system must generate amendment request letters to the issuing bank capturing the specific changes requested. For guarantee cancellations prior to expiry, the system must require upload of the beneficiary's release or no-objection letter before processing the cancellation request to the issuing bank. Upon cancellation confirmation from the bank, the contingent liability must be reversed, the guarantee status updated to Cancelled, and any prepaid guarantee commission must be evaluated for partial refund from the bank. The system must maintain the full amendment and cancellation history for each guarantee with timestamps, user actions, and bank confirmations for complete audit traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R010	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	IFRS 8 disclosure note generation	The system must auto-generate IFRS 8 Operating Segments disclosure notes for inclusion in annual and interim financial statements. Disclosures must include: basis of segmentation, measure of segment profit/loss, segment assets and liabilities, reconciliation of segment totals to consolidated totals, information about major customers (shipping lines or cargo owners exceeding 10% of total revenue), and geographic revenue breakdown by port of loading and discharge. Notes must be exportable in XBRL-tagged format for regulatory filing. The generation engine must support bilingual output (English primary with Arabic translation) and must reference the accounting policies applied to segment measurement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R016	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	AI-Driven Guarantee Optimization and Cost Reduction	The system must provide AI-driven analysis and recommendations for optimizing the guarantee portfolio. The AI engine must identify opportunities to reduce guarantee costs including: consolidating multiple small guarantees to the same beneficiary into a single umbrella guarantee, replacing cash-collateralized guarantees with facility-based guarantees when credit limits are available, identifying guarantees that can be released early because the underlying obligation has been fulfilled (e.g., a performance guarantee for a completed container terminal project), recommending optimal issuing banks based on fee comparison and relationship pricing across the group's banking relationships, and suggesting guarantee amount reductions where the underlying obligation has partially been performed. The system must also predict upcoming guarantee requirements based on the shipping operations pipeline (vessel bookings, port call schedules, contract renewal timelines) and recommend pre-arranging facility headroom. Optimization recommendations must quantify the projected cost savings in the entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-003-R017	FEAT-040-2-003	MOD-040	Guarantee Sanctions and AML Screening Integration	The system must integrate with sanctions screening systems to verify that all parties involved in a bank guarantee transaction (beneficiary, principal, issuing bank, counter-guarantor bank) are screened against applicable sanctions lists including UN Security Council consolidated list, OFAC SDN list, EU consolidated sanctions list, Qatar National Anti-Money Laundering Committee lists, UAE Executive Office of AML/CTF sanctions, KSA Designated list, and India's UAPA list. Screening must be performed at guarantee issuance, amendment, and periodically for active guarantees to catch newly designated parties. Any match or potential match must trigger an automatic hold on the guarantee processing, notification to the compliance officer, and creation of a compliance case file. The system must maintain screening history for audit purposes and generate periodic compliance reports for regulatory submissions. All screening must be performed without delaying customs guarantee issuance for time-sensitive container clearance operations, using a parallel processing approach with post-issuance verification for pre-approved beneficiary and bank combinations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R001	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Agreement Creation and Approval	The system must support creation of intercompany loan agreements between group entities with a structured approval workflow requiring sign-off from both the lending entity and borrowing entity treasury managers, group treasury, and legal department. The agreement must capture lender entity, borrower entity, loan amount and currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), interest rate and basis (fixed or floating, with reference rate such as SOFR, EIBOR, SAIBOR, or RBI repo rate plus spread), loan purpose (e.g., vessel acquisition financing, container fleet expansion, port terminal development, working capital for shipping operations), drawdown schedule (lump sum or multiple tranches), repayment schedule (bullet, amortizing, or custom), security or collateral if any, and governing law. The system must generate a standardized intercompany loan agreement document in both English and Arabic with entity-specific legal clauses per the jurisdiction of each party.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R002	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Transfer Pricing Compliance and Arm's Length Rate Validation	The system must enforce transfer pricing compliance for all intercompany loans by validating that the interest rate charged is at arm's length in accordance with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines and local regulations: Qatar Tax Authority requirements, UAE Corporate Tax Law transfer pricing rules (effective from 2024), KSA ZATCA transfer pricing regulations, and Indian Income Tax Act Section 92 and associated rules. The system must maintain a database of comparable arm's length interest rate ranges by currency, tenor, and credit quality benchmark, updated at least quarterly. When a new intercompany loan is created, the system must automatically compare the proposed interest rate against the arm's length range and flag any rate that falls outside the acceptable band. The system must generate transfer pricing documentation including functional analysis, economic analysis with comparable uncontrolled price or comparable uncontrolled transaction analysis, and the rationale for the selected interest rate, stored for audit and regulatory examination purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R003	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Loan Drawdown and Disbursement Processing	The system must manage loan drawdown requests from the borrowing entity, validating each drawdown against the approved loan facility terms including available undrawn amount, drawdown notice period (typically 3-5 business days for intercompany loans), minimum drawdown amount, and maximum number of tranches. Each drawdown request must follow an approval workflow with the lending entity's treasury confirming fund availability. Upon approval, the system must generate intercompany payment instructions specifying the source bank account of the lender entity, destination bank account of the borrower entity, amount, currency, value date, and payment reference linking to the loan agreement. For cross-border disbursements (e.g., Qatar entity lending to India entity), the system must handle FX conversion if the loan currency differs from the payment currency and record the applicable exchange rate. The system must update both entities' loan ledgers simultaneously, recording the drawdown as a receivable for the lender and a payable for the borrower with matching reference numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R004	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Interest Calculation and Accrual Engine	The system must calculate interest on intercompany loans using the agreed rate and day count convention (ACT/360, ACT/365, 30/360 as specified in the loan agreement). For floating rate loans, the system must automatically reset the interest rate on each reset date by fetching the current reference rate (SOFR, EIBOR, SAIBOR, or RBI repo rate) from the configured market data source and applying the contractual spread. Interest accrual must be computed daily and posted to the general ledger on a configurable frequency (daily, monthly, quarterly). The accrual engine must handle partial periods for new drawdowns and repayments mid-period, compounding interest where the loan terms specify it, and withholding tax deductions applicable to cross-border interest payments (e.g., India withholds tax on interest payments to non-resident entities under Section 195 of the Income Tax Act at rates modified by applicable DTAA provisions between India-Qatar, India-UAE, or India-KSA). Interest entries must be recorded simultaneously on both lender and borrower entity ledgers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R005	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Repayment Schedule Management and Tracking	The system must maintain and enforce repayment schedules for each intercompany loan, supporting bullet repayment (single principal payment at maturity), amortizing repayment (equal installments of principal plus interest), and custom repayment schedules with irregular amounts and dates. The system must generate repayment reminders to the borrowing entity at configurable intervals before each due date (default: 15, 7, and 3 days). Actual repayment receipts must be matched against scheduled payments, with the system tracking any overpayments (applied to future installments or returned), underpayments (recording the shortfall and generating follow-up alerts), and late payments (calculating late payment interest at the contractually agreed default rate). Early repayment (prepayment) must be supported with configurable prepayment notice periods and any applicable prepayment penalties. The repayment tracker must show a comprehensive loan amortization schedule with columns for date, opening balance, principal due, interest due, payment received, and closing balance, displayable in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R006	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Cross-Border Withholding Tax Management	The system must manage withholding tax obligations on cross-border intercompany interest payments in accordance with domestic tax laws and applicable Double Taxation Avoidance Agreements (DTAA). The system must store applicable withholding tax rates for each entity pair combination: India to Qatar (DTAA rate typically 10%), India to UAE (DTAA rate typically 12.5%), India to KSA (DTAA rate typically 10%), with default domestic rates applying where no DTAA exists or where treaty benefit conditions are not met. When processing an interest payment, the system must automatically calculate the withholding tax amount, generate the tax deduction certificate (Form 15CA/15CB for India outward remittances), record the gross interest, withholding tax deducted, and net interest paid on both entities' books, and track the borrower entity's obligation to remit the withheld tax to the relevant tax authority. The system must maintain a calendar of withholding tax deposit deadlines per jurisdiction and generate alerts for timely compliance. Tax credits must be tracked on the lender entity's records for offset against its own tax liability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R007	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Netting and Settlement	The system must support multilateral netting of intercompany loan obligations across group entities to minimize actual cross-border fund transfers. The netting engine must identify offsetting positions (e.g., Qatar entity has a loan receivable from UAE entity while UAE entity has a loan receivable from Qatar entity) and calculate net settlement amounts per entity pair and per currency. Netting cycles must be configurable (monthly, quarterly) with a formal netting statement generated for each cycle showing gross obligations, offsetting amounts, and net settlement due by each entity. The netting settlement must be approved by group treasury and recorded as a net payment instruction. The system must handle multi-currency netting where loans between entities are in different currencies, using the treasury-approved exchange rate for the netting date. Netting documentation must satisfy transfer pricing requirements by demonstrating that each underlying loan transaction maintains its arm's length terms independent of the netting arrangement. All netting calculations must produce audit-ready reports with complete traceability from net settlement back to individual loan transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R008	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Thin Capitalization and Debt Equity Ratio Monitoring	The system must monitor thin capitalization rules and debt-to-equity ratio limits for each borrowing entity to ensure intercompany loan levels remain within regulatory thresholds. For India, the system must track compliance with Section 94B of the Income Tax Act which limits interest deduction on borrowings from associated enterprises exceeding INR 10 million to 30% of EBITDA. For KSA, the system must monitor compliance with ZATCA thin capitalization rules. For UAE, the system must track the interest capping rules under the Corporate Tax Law. The system must calculate the current debt-to-equity ratio for each entity considering both external and intercompany borrowings, project future ratios based on planned drawdowns, and generate alerts when ratios approach or exceed the regulatory limit. The system must report on the tax-deductibility of intercompany interest expense per entity, identifying any non-deductible excess interest and its carry-forward amount where applicable. Reports must be available for each entity's tax return preparation and group tax planning review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R009	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Islamic Intercompany Financing Structures	The system must support Sharia-compliant intercompany financing structures as alternatives to conventional interest-bearing loans for entities operating under Islamic finance requirements in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. Supported structures must include Murabaha (cost-plus financing where the lending entity purchases an asset and sells it to the borrowing entity at a markup), Commodity Murabaha (Tawarruq, using commodity trading to generate a financing arrangement), Wakala (agency-based investment structure where the lender appoints the borrower as agent to invest funds for a target return), and Mudaraba (profit-sharing partnership). The system must correctly account for each structure: recording the purchase and sale transactions for Murabaha rather than a loan and interest, recognizing profit rather than interest income, and ensuring no riba components. The system must generate Sharia-compliant documentation including master agreements and transaction confirmations with Arabic-language terms. Transfer pricing compliance for Islamic structures must compare profit rates against arm's length benchmarks using Islamic finance equivalents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R010	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Regulatory Approval and RBI ECB Compliance for India Entity	The system must manage regulatory approval requirements for cross-border intercompany loans involving the India entity. For External Commercial Borrowings (ECB) received by the India entity from overseas group entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA), the system must validate compliance with RBI ECB Framework including: eligible borrower and lender categories, permitted end-use restrictions (e.g., shipping vessel acquisition and port infrastructure qualify, but general corporate purposes may not), all-in-cost ceiling (reference rate plus spread must not exceed the RBI-prescribed maximum), minimum average maturity period (typically 3-5 years depending on amount and end-use), and individual borrowing limit under the automatic route. The system must generate the Form ECB (formerly Form 83) for filing with the AD Category-I bank, track the ECB registration number (LRN - Loan Registration Number) assigned by RBI, and ensure timely filing of monthly ECB-2 returns reporting actual transactions. The system must alert if any proposed intercompany loan terms to/from the India entity would violate ECB norms, preventing non-compliant transactions from being processed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R011	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Reconciliation and Confirmation	The system must perform automated reconciliation of intercompany loan balances between lending and borrowing entities on a configurable frequency (default: monthly). The reconciliation must compare principal outstanding, accrued interest, and total balance per loan agreement on both entities' books, identifying any discrepancies due to timing differences in recording drawdowns, repayments, or interest accruals, FX translation differences for loans denominated in a currency different from either entity's functional currency, or errors in manual entries. Discrepancies exceeding a configurable threshold (default: USD 100 equivalent) must generate reconciliation exception reports requiring investigation and resolution by the treasury team. The system must support formal intercompany balance confirmation workflows where each entity's authorized signatory confirms the outstanding balance at period end (quarterly and annually at minimum). Confirmed balances must be locked to prevent unauthorized adjustments and must feed directly into the consolidation module for intercompany elimination entries during group financial statement preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R012	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Exposure and Limit Management	The system must enforce configurable exposure limits for intercompany lending to manage concentration risk and ensure prudent group treasury management. Limits must be configurable by lending entity (maximum total intercompany loans extended), borrowing entity (maximum total intercompany loans received), entity pair (maximum bilateral exposure), currency (maximum exposure in any single currency), and aggregate group level. The system must track real-time utilization against each limit including committed but undrawn facilities. When a new loan or drawdown request would breach any applicable limit, the system must block the transaction and require group treasury head approval to proceed with documented justification. The system must generate periodic limit utilization reports showing headroom across all dimensions. Limits must be reviewed and approved annually by the group CFO, with the approval workflow and limit history maintained in the system. The limit framework must also consider regulatory limits such as related-party transaction thresholds under Indian Companies Act Section 188 and SEBI LODR regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R013	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Currency Management and FX Impact	The system must manage currency aspects of intercompany loans including loans denominated in a currency different from the functional currency of the lender or borrower entity. For example, a USD-denominated loan from the Qatar entity (functional currency QAR) to the India entity (functional currency INR) creates FX exposure for both entities. The system must calculate and record FX translation gains/losses at each reporting date using the closing exchange rate per IAS 21, distinguish between monetary item translation differences (recognized in P&L) and net investment hedge treatments (recognized in OCI if designated), and provide analysis of the cumulative FX impact on each intercompany loan since inception. The system must integrate with the FX Hedging module (FEAT-040-2-001) to allow hedging of intercompany loan FX exposures with the hedge linked to the specific loan. Currency mismatch reports must be generated showing all intercompany loans where the loan currency differs from either party's functional currency with the current unrealized FX gain/loss position per loan.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R014	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Reporting and Group Consolidation Support	The system must generate comprehensive intercompany loan reports supporting group financial consolidation. Reports must include: a group-wide intercompany loan register listing all active loans with key terms, outstanding balances, and accrued interest per entity; an intercompany elimination schedule showing receivable and payable balances that must be eliminated on consolidation with automatic identification and flagging of mismatches; an interest income/expense elimination schedule ensuring symmetric recognition across entities; and a maturity analysis of intercompany loan obligations by time bucket (less than 1 year, 1-3 years, 3-5 years, over 5 years) for liquidity planning and IFRS 7 disclosure. Reports must support dual-language output (English and Arabic) and dual-calendar dates (Gregorian and Hijri). The system must also generate related-party transaction disclosure schedules as required by IAS 24, categorizing intercompany loans by relationship type and presenting the period's transactions (new loans, repayments, interest) and period-end balances for notes to the financial statements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R015	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Automated Intercompany Interest Invoicing and Payment	The system must automate the generation of intercompany interest invoices from the lending entity to the borrowing entity on each interest payment date as defined in the loan agreement. Interest invoices must detail the loan reference, interest period, applicable interest rate (including reference rate and spread for floating rate loans), day count, principal balance, calculated interest amount, any withholding tax deduction, and net amount due. Invoices must be generated in the currency of the loan with translations to both entities' functional currencies at the invoice date exchange rate. The system must automatically create the corresponding accounts receivable entry on the lender's books and accounts payable entry on the borrower's books. Payment instructions must be auto-generated for the borrower entity's approval, routed to the payment module for execution on the due date. For entities using Islamic financing structures, the invoice must reflect profit or markup charges rather than interest with appropriate Arabic terminology (e.g., Ribh for profit). Late payment penalties must be automatically calculated and invoiced if the payment is not received within the contractual grace period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R016	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	Intercompany Loan Audit Trail and Regulatory Examination Support	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all intercompany loan transactions supporting regulatory examinations by tax authorities (QTA, FTA UAE, ZATCA, Indian Income Tax), central banks (QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, RBI), and external auditors. The audit trail must capture every event in the loan lifecycle: agreement creation and approval chain, drawdown requests and approvals, interest calculations and accrual postings, repayment receipts and allocations, rate resets for floating rate loans, amendment processing, and loan closure. Each entry must record the timestamp (Gregorian and Hijri), user ID, user role, action description, system-calculated values, and any manual overrides with justification. The system must support generation of regulatory examination packages that bundle all documentation for a specific intercompany loan including the loan agreement, board resolutions authorizing the transaction, transfer pricing documentation, tax certificates, payment evidence, and correspondence. For the India entity, the system must generate CBDT-prescribed documentation under Section 92D for transfer pricing audit defense.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-040-2-004-R017	FEAT-040-2-004	MOD-040	AI-Powered Intercompany Funding Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven recommendations for optimizing intercompany funding structures within the shipping group. The AI engine must analyze each entity's cash flow patterns (considering seasonal shipping volumes on Qatar-India and UAE-India trade lanes, vessel payment schedules, port disbursement cycles), external borrowing costs per entity and currency, tax implications of different intercompany lending routes (considering withholding tax rates under applicable DTAAs, thin capitalization limits, and transfer pricing constraints), and regulatory restrictions (RBI ECB limits for India entity, QCB capital adequacy for Qatar entity). Based on this analysis, the system must recommend optimal funding routes: for example, routing a USD loan from Qatar to India via the UAE entity if this reduces overall group tax cost, or recommending a direct QAR-denominated loan instead of USD to avoid FX risk. The AI must also recommend optimal loan tenors and repayment structures aligned with the borrowing entity's projected shipping revenue cash flows. Recommendations must be presented with quantified cost-benefit analysis showing projected interest savings, tax savings, and FX risk reduction compared to alternative structures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R001	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Hierarchical Account Structure with Shipping Segments	The system must support a hierarchical chart of accounts with a minimum of six configurable segments: entity code (QA/AE/SA/IN), account category (asset, liability, equity, revenue, expense), natural account number, port code (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNP for JNPT), cost center, and voyage/vessel dimension. Each segment must allow alphanumeric values of configurable length between 2 and 12 characters. The hierarchy must support at least 10 levels of nesting for roll-up reporting. Parent-child relationships must enforce that child accounts inherit the classification of their parent and that postings are only allowed to leaf-level accounts. The system must validate segment combinations against a predefined matrix to prevent invalid account strings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R002	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Multi-Entity Chart of Accounts Synchronization	The system must maintain a global CoA template that serves as the master structure for all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each entity must be able to extend the global template with local accounts specific to their regulatory requirements — for example, India-specific GST input/output accounts or KSA-specific Zakat provision accounts. When the global template is modified, the system must propagate changes to all entity-level CoAs within a configurable synchronization window. Local extensions must never conflict with global account numbers. The AI engine must suggest account mappings when new entity-level accounts are created, comparing descriptions and usage patterns across entities to recommend the correct global roll-up parent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R003	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	IFRS and Local GAAP Dual Account Mapping	The system must support dual mapping of every account to both IFRS and local GAAP reporting standards simultaneously. For Qatar, mapping to QFC Authority standards is required. For UAE, mapping must support IFRS as adopted by the UAE Ministry of Finance. For KSA, mapping to SOCPA standards alongside IFRS is required. For India, mapping to Ind-AS standards is required. Each account must carry two classification tags — one for IFRS reporting and one for local statutory reporting. The mapping table must support many-to-many relationships so that a single local account can map to multiple IFRS accounts and vice versa, with configurable allocation percentages that sum to exactly 100% per source account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R004	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Shipping-Specific Revenue and Cost Account Templates	The system must provide pre-configured account templates specific to container shipping operations including but not limited to: ocean freight revenue (by trade lane such as Qatar-India, UAE-KSA), THC revenue and expense (separated by Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), demurrage and detention income, container leasing costs (distinguished by container type — 20ft standard, 40ft standard, 40ft high-cube, reefer, open-top, flat-rack), bunker fuel costs, port call expenses, vessel charter hire, agency commission expenses, B/L fee income, and customs brokerage expenses. Each template account must carry a default tax treatment flag, currency denomination, and intercompany elimination flag. Templates must be cloneable for new entity setup.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R005	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Multi-Currency Account Configuration	Each account in the chart of accounts must support configuration for its primary transactional currency from the supported set: USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The system must allow accounts to be designated as single-currency (accepting postings only in the designated currency) or multi-currency (accepting postings in any supported currency with automatic translation to the account's reporting currency). Every account must maintain balances in three currencies simultaneously: the transaction currency, the entity's functional currency, and the group reporting currency (USD). The system must flag any attempt to post a transaction in a non-permitted currency for a single-currency account and provide a clear error message indicating which currencies are allowed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R006	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Arabic and English Bilingual Account Descriptions	Every account in the chart of accounts must store both an Arabic name and an English name as mandatory fields. The Arabic name must support full RTL (right-to-left) text rendering including proper display of Arabic numerals (Eastern Arabic ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) alongside Western numerals when required. The system must validate that Arabic descriptions contain at least one Arabic Unicode character (U+0600 to U+06FF range) to prevent accidentally storing transliterated Latin text. Search functionality must operate across both language fields simultaneously so that a user searching in Arabic can find accounts described in English and vice versa. Bulk import/export via Excel must support both language columns with proper Unicode encoding (UTF-8 BOM) to preserve Arabic characters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R007	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	AI-Powered Account Classification and Suggestion	The system must provide an AI engine that automatically suggests the correct account classification when a user creates a new account. The AI must analyze the account name (in Arabic or English), its position in the hierarchy, and historical posting patterns from similar accounts across all entities to recommend: the IFRS category, the natural account grouping, the default tax treatment, and the appropriate reporting line on IFRS financial statements. The AI confidence score must be displayed alongside each suggestion with a threshold of 85% for auto-acceptance. Suggestions below this threshold must require manual review and approval by a qualified accountant. The AI must learn from corrections to improve future suggestions with a feedback loop that retrains the model on a weekly cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R008	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Account Activation, Deactivation, and Archival Lifecycle	The system must enforce a strict account lifecycle with three states: active, inactive, and archived. Active accounts accept postings and appear in all selection lists. Inactive accounts reject new postings but retain historical balances and appear in reports covering periods when they were active. Archived accounts are hidden from all user-facing interfaces but remain in the database for audit trail integrity with a minimum retention period of 10 years as required by QFC, DFSA, and Indian Companies Act 2013. Transitioning an account from active to inactive must require zero outstanding balance or explicit managerial approval with documented justification. The system must prevent reactivation of an archived account and instead require creation of a new account with an explicit link to the archived predecessor for audit continuity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R009	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Intercompany Account Pairing and Elimination Setup	The system must support automatic pairing of intercompany accounts between entities — for example, a receivable from Entity QA to Entity AE must be automatically paired with the corresponding payable from Entity AE to Entity QA. Each intercompany account pair must be flagged for consolidation elimination during group reporting. The system must validate that for every intercompany receivable account created in one entity, a corresponding intercompany payable account exists in the counterparty entity. If the counterparty account does not exist, the system must auto-generate it using AI-derived naming conventions and flag it for review. Intercompany accounts must carry the counterparty entity code as a mandatory segment to enable automated elimination journal generation during consolidation close.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R010	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Voyage and Container-Type Sub-Ledger Dimensions	The chart of accounts must support optional sub-ledger dimensions for voyage number and container type to enable profitability analysis at the voyage and equipment level. Voyage dimensions must accept standard voyage number formats including carrier prefix (e.g., MSC, CMA, HPL) followed by a numeric voyage identifier. Container-type dimensions must align with ISO 6346 container type codes including 22G1 (20ft general purpose), 42G1 (40ft general purpose), 45G1 (40ft high-cube), 22R1 (20ft reefer), and 42R1 (40ft reefer). These dimensions must be optional on all expense accounts and mandatory on revenue accounts classified under freight income. The system must validate container type codes against the ISO 6346 standard and reject invalid codes with a descriptive error message.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R011	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Account Code Validation and Duplicate Prevention	The system must enforce unique account codes across the entire chart of accounts within each entity and across the global template. Account code format must be validated against a configurable regex pattern defined per entity to ensure consistency — for example, Qatar entity may use pattern QA-NNNN-NNN while India entity uses IN-NNNN-NNN where N represents digits. The system must reject account creation if the proposed code matches any existing active or inactive account. Archived accounts must also be checked with a warning displayed if a match is found, requiring explicit user confirmation to proceed. Bulk upload of accounts via CSV or Excel must perform the same validation and reject the entire batch if any single account code violates the rules, providing a detailed error report listing all violations with row numbers and specific rule failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R012	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Account-Level Security and Access Control	The system must enforce role-based access control at the individual account level. Permissions must include: view balance, post transactions, modify account properties, deactivate, and archive. Each entity's finance team must only access their own entity's accounts by default, with explicit cross-entity access grants required for group accountants and consolidation teams. Sensitive accounts such as intercompany settlements, management fee allocations, and foreign currency revaluation reserves must require dual authorization for any posting. The system must log every access attempt (successful and failed) to account records with timestamp, user identity, IP address, and action type. Access control changes must require approval from at least one user with the entity CFO role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R013	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Account Balance Inquiry with Real-Time Currency Translation	The system must provide real-time balance inquiry for any account in any of the five supported currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). When a user queries an account balance, the system must display the balance in the account's native currency, the entity's functional currency, and the group reporting currency simultaneously. Users must be able to toggle the display to any other supported currency using the latest exchange rate from the Central Bank feeds (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI). For pegged currencies (QAR/USD at 3.64, AED/USD at 3.6725), the system must use fixed rates. Balance inquiries must support date-range filtering with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date pickers, allowing users to view balances as of any historical date within the current and previous five fiscal years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R014	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Bulk Account Import and Export with Validation	The system must support bulk import of chart of accounts data from Excel (XLSX) and CSV formats with a maximum file size of 50MB supporting up to 50,000 account records per import. The import process must execute in three phases: upload and parse, validation with detailed error reporting, and confirmation with preview. Validation must check all business rules including hierarchy consistency, segment format compliance, duplicate detection, mandatory field presence (Arabic name, English name, account type, currency), and IFRS mapping validity. The export function must generate files in the same formats with all account attributes including current balances, last posting date, and lifecycle status. Export must support filtering by entity, account type, status, and custom date range. Both import and export must maintain an audit log entry recording the user, timestamp, file hash, and record count.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R015	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Offline Chart of Accounts Access and Sync	The system must provide offline access to the chart of accounts for users operating in areas with unreliable connectivity, particularly relevant for port-based operations at Hamad Port and JNPT where network infrastructure may be intermittent. The offline mode must cache the complete CoA structure including all account details, hierarchies, and descriptions in both Arabic and English to the local device using encrypted storage (AES-256). Users must be able to browse and search the CoA offline but must not be able to create or modify accounts offline to prevent synchronization conflicts. When connectivity is restored, the local cache must automatically synchronize with the central database and notify the user of any changes made by other users during the offline period. The sync mechanism must use incremental updates based on a last-modified timestamp to minimize bandwidth consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R016	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Account Tagging for Regulatory and Tax Reporting	Each account must support multiple regulatory tags for automated inclusion in tax and regulatory reports. Required tags include: VAT treatment (standard rated 5% for UAE/KSA/Qatar, exempt, zero-rated, out-of-scope), GST treatment for India (CGST, SGST, IGST with applicable rates of 5%, 12%, 18%, 28%), Zakat base classification for KSA (current assets, current liabilities adjustments), and customs duty account classification for container shipping operations (import duty, anti-dumping duty, safeguard duty). Tags must be entity-specific so that the same global account can carry different tax treatments in different jurisdictions. The system must validate that all active revenue and expense accounts carry at least one tax tag and alert administrators of any untagged accounts on a weekly automated review cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-001-R017	FEAT-041-1-001	MOD-041	Chart of Accounts Audit Trail and Change History	The system must maintain a complete, immutable audit trail of every change made to the chart of accounts including account creation, modification, deactivation, archival, and any changes to account properties such as name, classification, tax tags, or currency configuration. Each audit record must capture: the previous value, the new value, the timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, the user who made the change, the approval chain if applicable, and the IP address and device identifier of the session. The audit trail must be searchable by account code, user, date range, and change type. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 15 years to comply with the longest applicable retention period across all four jurisdictions. The audit trail must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats for external auditor review with digital signature verification capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R001	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Manual Journal Entry Creation with Dual-Language Support	The system must allow authorized users to create manual journal entries with a minimum of two lines (debit and credit) and a maximum of 500 lines per single journal entry to support complex shipping transactions such as voyage cost allocations across multiple ports and cost centers. Each journal entry must capture: entry date (Gregorian with automatic Hijri conversion), posting period, entity code, currency, exchange rate, description in both Arabic and English (both mandatory), and supporting document reference. The debit and credit totals must balance exactly in the transaction currency before the entry can be submitted. The system must support memo fields on each line for additional narrative in either language, with a minimum of 200 characters allowed per line memo to accommodate detailed shipping descriptions including B/L numbers, container IDs, and voyage references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R002	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Multi-Currency Journal Entry with Automatic Translation	The system must support journal entries in any of the five operational currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and automatically compute translated amounts in the entity's functional currency and the group reporting currency. For each journal line, the system must store three amounts: transaction currency amount, functional currency amount, and reporting currency amount. Exchange rates must be sourced automatically from the integrated central bank feeds with the option for manual override with documented justification. For pegged currencies (QAR pegged to USD at 3.64, AED pegged to USD at 3.6725), the system must apply fixed rates by default. Cross-currency journal entries (e.g., a single entry with USD and QAR lines) must be supported with separate exchange rate application per line. Rounding differences must be automatically posted to a configurable FX rounding gain/loss account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R003	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	AI-Automated Recurring Journal Entry Generation	The system must provide AI-powered automation for generating recurring journal entries related to container shipping operations. The AI engine must identify patterns from historical postings and suggest recurring entry templates for: monthly vessel charter hire accruals, port call expense provisions for scheduled vessel arrivals at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, container lease amortization for owned and leased equipment, insurance premium amortization, and bunker fuel prepayment releases. Each AI-generated template must specify the frequency (daily, weekly, bi-weekly, monthly, quarterly), the accounts, the calculation method (fixed amount, percentage-based, formula-driven), and the expected variance tolerance. The AI must flag entries that deviate more than 15% from the expected amount for human review before posting. Auto-generated entries must be clearly tagged as AI-generated in the audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R004	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Intercompany Journal Entry with Auto-Balancing	The system must support intercompany journal entries that simultaneously create offsetting entries in two or more entities. When a user creates an intercompany entry — for example, management fee charged by Entity QA to Entity AE — the system must automatically generate the corresponding entry in the counterparty entity using the paired intercompany accounts. The originating entry and the offsetting entry must share a common intercompany transaction reference for consolidation tracking. Both entries must use the same exchange rate to prevent intercompany imbalances. The system must calculate and post any withholding tax implications automatically based on the nature of the intercompany transaction and the tax treaties between jurisdictions (e.g., Qatar-India DTAA, Qatar-UAE bilateral agreements). Intercompany entries must require approval from authorized users in both entities before final posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R005	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Journal Entry Approval Workflow with Configurable Thresholds	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for journal entries based on entry amount, entry type, and entity. At minimum, three approval levels must be supported: preparer, reviewer, and approver. Threshold-based routing must allow configuration such as: entries below USD 10,000 require single approval, entries between USD 10,000 and USD 100,000 require dual approval, and entries above USD 100,000 require CFO-level approval. Intercompany entries must always require at least dual approval regardless of amount. The workflow engine must support delegation of authority during absence periods with configurable start and end dates. Approval must be possible from mobile devices with biometric authentication. The system must escalate pending approvals that exceed a configurable SLA (default 48 hours) to the next level in the hierarchy with email and in-app notification in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R003	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Calculation for Reefer Containers	The system must calculate additional THC surcharges for refrigerated containers (ISO type codes 22R1, 42R1, 45R1) that require electrical plug-in monitoring and temperature management during terminal dwell time. The reefer THC surcharge must be computed as a base handling fee plus a per-hour or per-day plug-in charge depending on terminal operator policy. The system must track reefer plug-in and plug-out timestamps from terminal operating system integration, compute actual connected hours, and generate line-item detail showing base THC and reefer surcharge separately on invoices in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R006	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Voyage Cost Accrual Journal Automation	The system must automatically generate accrual journal entries for voyage costs based on vessel schedule data integrated from the operations module. When a vessel departs from or arrives at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), or JNPT (INJNP), the system must create accrual entries for expected port charges, pilotage, towage, berth hire, stevedoring, and terminal handling charges based on historical cost data and the vessel's specifications (LOA, GRT, NRT). The accrual amounts must be estimated using AI analysis of the last 12 months of actual costs for similar vessel types at each port. Upon receipt of the actual vendor invoice, the system must automatically reverse the accrual and post the actual cost, calculating and reporting the variance. Variances exceeding 20% of the accrued amount must trigger an alert to the finance controller with a detailed breakdown by cost component.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R007	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Journal Entry Reversal and Correction Mechanisms	The system must support two mechanisms for correcting posted journal entries: full reversal and partial correction. Full reversal must create an exact mirror image of the original entry with all debits becoming credits and vice versa, posted to the same period as the original or the current period based on user selection. Partial correction must allow modification of specific lines while maintaining the overall debit-credit balance. Both mechanisms must preserve a clear linkage to the original entry via a reversal reference field. Reversals of entries in closed periods must be posted to the first open period with a clear notation of the original period. The system must prevent reversal of an entry that has already been reversed (no double-reversal). Mass reversal must be supported for scenarios such as reversing all accrual entries at period end, with the ability to select entries by type, date range, or account and process up to 10,000 reversals in a single batch operation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R008	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Automated Foreign Currency Revaluation Journals	The system must automatically generate foreign currency revaluation journal entries at configurable intervals (daily, weekly, monthly, or on-demand). Revaluation must apply to all monetary accounts (receivables, payables, bank accounts, loans) denominated in a currency different from the entity's functional currency. The system must use closing rates from central bank feeds — Qatar Central Bank for QAR, UAE Central Bank for AED, SAMA for SAR, and RBI for INR — with USD as the common reference. Revaluation gains and losses must be posted to separate unrealized FX gain and unrealized FX loss accounts as required by IAS 21. The system must calculate revaluation for each individual open item (invoice, payment) rather than net balance to ensure accuracy for shipping receivables from agents across different countries. A detailed revaluation report must be generated alongside the journal showing the original rate, closing rate, and gain/loss per open item.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R009	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Batch Journal Entry Upload from External Systems	The system must support batch upload of journal entries from external systems including port community systems, vessel tracking platforms, customs brokers, and shipping agents. The upload must accept structured formats: CSV, Excel (XLSX), XML conforming to a published XSD schema, and JSON via REST API. Each batch must undergo a three-stage validation process: format validation (correct columns, data types, encoding), business rule validation (account existence, period open status, currency validity, debit-credit balance per entry), and duplicate detection (checking against existing entries within a configurable lookback window of 30 days). Failed batches must be quarantined with a detailed error log accessible to the uploader. Successful batches must enter the standard approval workflow. The system must support a maximum batch size of 50,000 journal lines per upload and process the validation within 5 minutes for batches up to 10,000 lines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R010	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Posting Validation Rules Engine	The system must enforce a configurable rules engine that validates every journal entry before posting. Rules must include: account-period combination validity (preventing posting to closed periods), account-entity authorization (ensuring the account belongs to the posting entity), balance validation (debits must equal credits in transaction currency), mandatory dimension validation (ensuring voyage number is populated for freight revenue accounts and container type is populated for equipment-related accounts), negative amount prevention on specified accounts, and maximum posting amount limits per account configurable by entity. The rules engine must execute all validations simultaneously and return a consolidated error list rather than failing on the first error. Custom rules must be definable by entity finance controllers using a no-code rule builder interface that supports conditions based on account attributes, entry attributes, and cross-referencing external data such as approved vendor lists and valid voyage numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R011	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Real-Time General Ledger Posting with Sub-Ledger Integration	Journal entries must post to the general ledger in real-time upon final approval, updating account balances immediately without requiring a batch posting process. The system must maintain synchronization between the general ledger and all sub-ledgers including accounts receivable (shipping agent and customer balances), accounts payable (vendor and port authority balances), fixed assets (vessel and container equipment), and inventory (bunker fuel and spare parts). Each general ledger posting must carry a sub-ledger source identifier to enable drill-down from the GL balance to the underlying sub-ledger transactions. The posting engine must process a minimum of 1,000 journal entries per minute to handle peak volumes during period-end close for all four entities. The system must implement optimistic concurrency control to prevent lost updates when multiple users post to the same account simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R012	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Journal Entry Attachment and Documentation Management	Each journal entry must support attachment of supporting documents in PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, Excel, and Word formats with a maximum individual file size of 25MB and maximum of 20 attachments per entry. The system must use AI-powered OCR to extract key data from attached documents including invoice numbers, amounts, dates, and vendor names from both Arabic and English documents. Extracted data must be cross-referenced against the journal entry details to flag discrepancies — for example, if the attached invoice amount does not match the journal entry amount, the system must generate a warning. Attachments must be stored with encryption at rest (AES-256) and linked immutably to the journal entry so that they cannot be deleted after the entry is posted. For shipping-specific documents such as bills of lading, delivery orders, and customs declarations, the AI must identify the document type and auto-classify it.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R013	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Statistical and Memo Journal Entries	The system must support statistical (non-financial) journal entries for tracking quantities and metrics that do not affect account balances but are essential for container shipping analytics. Statistical entries must support recording of: TEU counts by trade lane and port, container moves (lifts) at each terminal, vessel calls per port, weight (metric tons) of cargo handled, and fuel consumption in metric tons. Statistical entries must be linked to corresponding financial entries via a common transaction reference. The system must maintain separate statistical accounts that follow the same hierarchical structure as financial accounts but carry unit-of-measure instead of currency. Statistical balances must be available for key performance indicator calculations such as revenue per TEU, cost per container move, and fuel cost per nautical mile. These entries must follow the same approval workflow as financial entries but with configurable lower approval thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R014	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Automated Prepayment and Amortization Schedules	The system must automatically generate amortization journal entries for prepaid expenses common in container shipping operations: annual vessel insurance premiums, P&I club calls, port concession fees, container depot lease prepayments, and software license subscriptions. When a prepayment is recorded, the user must define the amortization method (straight-line or usage-based), the amortization period (start date, end date, number of periods), and the target expense account. The system must generate a complete amortization schedule viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. Each period's amortization entry must be auto-generated on the first day of the period and routed through the standard approval workflow. If the prepayment is partially refunded or written off, the system must recalculate the remaining amortization schedule and flag the change for approval. The amortization balance must reconcile to the prepaid asset account balance at all times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R015	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Journal Entry Search and Advanced Filtering	The system must provide a comprehensive search interface for journal entries supporting filters by: date range (Gregorian and Hijri), posting period, entity, account code or range, amount range, currency, entry type (manual, automated, recurring, reversal, intercompany), approval status, created-by user, approved-by user, description keyword search in both Arabic and English, document reference number, voyage number, and B/L number. Search results must display in a sortable, paginated grid with configurable columns. The system must support saving of frequently used search criteria as named filters accessible per user. Full-text search across journal descriptions and line memos must return results within 3 seconds for a dataset of up to 5 million journal entries. Export of search results to Excel and PDF must be available with a maximum of 100,000 rows per export. The search must respect entity-level access controls showing only entries the user is authorized to view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R016	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	AI-Powered Anomaly Detection on Journal Entries	The system must employ an AI-based anomaly detection engine that continuously monitors journal entries for unusual patterns indicative of errors or potential fraud. Detection rules must include: entries posted outside normal business hours for the entity's timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), entries significantly larger than historical averages for the same account (using a configurable z-score threshold defaulting to 3.0), round-number entries exceeding USD 50,000, unusual account combinations not seen in the prior 12 months, journal entries bypassing the standard sub-ledger flow, and entries posted by users who do not typically post to the affected accounts. Each flagged anomaly must generate an alert with a risk score (1-100), a natural language explanation in the user's preferred language, and a recommended action. Alerts must be routed to the entity's internal audit team and the finance controller simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R017	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Journal Entry Audit Trail and Immutability	Every journal entry must have a complete, immutable audit trail from creation through final posting and any subsequent reversals. The audit trail must record: creation timestamp, each edit before posting with before-and-after values, each approval action with approver identity and timestamp, posting timestamp, and any reversal or correction linkage. Once a journal entry is posted, its core financial data (accounts, amounts, currencies) must be immutable — corrections must be made via reversal and reposting only. The audit trail must be stored in append-only storage that prevents modification or deletion even by system administrators. Each audit record must be digitally signed using the system's PKI infrastructure to ensure tamper-evidence. The audit trail must be accessible for a minimum of 10 years for Qatar and UAE entities and 8 years for India entities as per the respective regulatory retention requirements. Export of audit trails must support formats required by external auditors including XBRL tagging for regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-002-R018	FEAT-041-1-002	MOD-041	Period-End Accrual Automation for Unbilled Shipping Services	The system must automatically identify and generate accrual journal entries for unbilled shipping services at period end. The AI engine must scan open bookings, completed voyages without final billing, delivered containers without demurrage settlement, and completed port operations without vendor invoices to calculate accrual amounts. For freight revenue accruals, the system must reference the applicable tariff or contract rate from the commercial module, the cargo manifest quantities, and the INCOTERM (CIF, FOB, CFR, FCA) to determine the revenue recognition point. For cost accruals, the system must use the last known rate card from each port authority (Mwani Qatar for Hamad Port, Abu Dhabi Ports for Khalifa Port, DP World for Jebel Ali, JNPT Port Authority) and apply vessel-specific parameters. All auto-generated accruals must be grouped into a single accrual batch per entity per period with a summary report showing the total accrual by category and the AI confidence level for each estimated amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R001	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Configurable Fiscal Period and Calendar Management	The system must support configurable fiscal periods for each entity independently. Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities must support both standard Gregorian fiscal years (January-December or April-March) and Hijri fiscal years with automatic period boundary calculation based on the Umm al-Qura calendar. India entity must support the statutory April-March fiscal year with quarterly sub-periods aligned to GST filing deadlines. Each entity must be able to define up to 16 periods per fiscal year (12 regular periods plus 4 adjustment periods for year-end audit adjustments). The system must maintain a clear mapping between Hijri and Gregorian periods for consolidation purposes when entities operate on different calendar bases. Period definitions must include configurable opening and closing dates, soft-close dates (warning only), and hard-close dates (posting blocked). The system must prevent creation of overlapping periods and ensure complete coverage of the fiscal year with no date gaps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R002	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Period Close Checklist with Dependency Management	The system must provide a configurable period-close checklist for each entity with task dependencies, assigned owners, due dates, and completion tracking. The checklist must include standard shipping-specific close tasks: voyage revenue recognition cutoff verification, container demurrage and detention income accrual, bunker fuel cost allocation, port expense accrual and reconciliation, intercompany transaction matching, foreign currency revaluation, bank reconciliation completion, sub-ledger to GL reconciliation, and management fee allocation. Each task must define prerequisites — for example, foreign currency revaluation cannot begin until all foreign currency transactions for the period are posted. The system must display the checklist in a visual dashboard showing task status (not started, in progress, completed, blocked), critical path highlighting, and estimated time to completion based on historical performance data. Tasks must be assignable to specific users or roles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R003	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	AI-Driven Period Close Status Monitoring	The system must employ an AI engine that monitors the period close process in real-time and predicts completion timeline based on current progress and historical close performance. The AI must analyze: the number of open tasks remaining, the average completion time for similar tasks in prior periods, the current workload of assigned personnel, and any blocking dependencies. The AI must generate a daily period-close status report in both Arabic and English that includes: percentage complete, predicted close date, tasks at risk of missing their deadline with specific reasons, and recommended actions to accelerate the close. The AI must learn from each period's close process to improve predictions — if a particular entity consistently delays on bank reconciliation, the AI must proactively suggest earlier start dates for that task in future periods. Critical path alerts must be sent to the group CFO when any entity's close is projected to miss the group consolidation deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R004	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Sub-Ledger to General Ledger Reconciliation Automation	The system must automatically reconcile all sub-ledger control account balances to their corresponding general ledger balances as a mandatory period-close step. Sub-ledgers include: accounts receivable (shipping agents, direct customers, freight forwarders), accounts payable (port authorities, vessel owners, container lessors, customs brokers), fixed assets (vessels, containers, port equipment, office assets), and inventory (bunker fuel, lubricants, spare parts). The reconciliation must compare the sum of all open items in each sub-ledger to the corresponding GL control account balance and identify discrepancies. For each discrepancy, the system must attempt AI-powered root cause analysis by examining timing differences, unposted batches, and misclassified entries. The reconciliation report must list each control account, the sub-ledger balance, the GL balance, the variance, and the AI-suggested cause. Variances exceeding a configurable threshold (default USD 100) must block period close until resolved or explicitly waived by the entity controller.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R005	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Automated Bank Reconciliation Integration	The system must integrate with bank reconciliation data to verify that all bank accounts are reconciled before period close. The integration must support electronic bank statement import in MT940 (SWIFT), BAI2, CAMT.053 (ISO 20022), and local formats used by Qatar National Bank, Emirates NBD, Al Rajhi Bank, and State Bank of India. The system must automatically match bank statement transactions to GL postings using AI-powered matching that considers: exact amount matches, date proximity (within 5 business days), reference number correlation, and payee name fuzzy matching in both Arabic and English. Unmatched items must be flagged as reconciling items and categorized as: timing differences, bank errors, book errors, or unknown. The period close checklist must show the reconciliation status of each bank account with the total unreconciled balance. Period close must be blocked if any bank account has unreconciled items older than 60 days unless explicitly waived.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R006	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Intercompany Transaction Matching and Reconciliation	The system must perform automated matching of intercompany transactions between all entity pairs (QA-AE, QA-SA, QA-IN, AE-SA, AE-IN, SA-IN) as a mandatory period-close step. The matching engine must compare intercompany receivables and payables by: transaction reference, amount (allowing a configurable tolerance of up to USD 50 for rounding differences), date, and currency. Matched pairs must be marked as confirmed for consolidation elimination. Unmatched items must be investigated and resolved before period close — the system must generate a discrepancy report for each entity pair showing unmatched items from both sides. For container shipping-specific intercompany charges such as slot charter fees, agency commissions, and equipment interchange fees, the system must cross-reference the operational data (vessel schedules, container movements, agent appointments) to validate the commercial basis of the charge. The system must calculate the net intercompany position per entity pair per currency and flag any position exceeding USD 500,000 for senior management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R007	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Revenue Recognition Cutoff Verification	The system must automate revenue recognition cutoff verification for container shipping operations in compliance with IFRS 15. The system must identify all bills of lading issued during the period and verify that freight revenue has been recognized according to the applicable INCOTERM and the percentage-of-voyage-completion method. For CIF and CFR shipments, revenue recognition must be prorated based on voyage progress from the port of loading to the port of discharge using vessel GPS position data where available. The system must flag any B/L where revenue has been recognized but the vessel has not departed the load port, or where the voyage is complete but revenue has not been fully recognized. Demurrage and detention revenue must be verified against container tracking data to confirm that the billing period aligns with actual container dwell time at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. A cutoff report must list all boundary transactions with their recognition status and any AI-recommended adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R011	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Multi-currency segment translation	The system must translate segment financial data from local functional currency to group reporting currency (USD) using the appropriate exchange rates: closing rate for balance sheet items, average rate for income statement items, and historical rate for equity components, per IAS 21 requirements. Translation differences must be captured in a Currency Translation Reserve (CTR) account within each segment. The system must support spot rates sourced from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA (Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority), and RBI (Reserve Bank of India). Rate overrides must be permitted with dual-approval workflow and audit log. Monthly translation must process all segments within a single batch run.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R008	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Automated Period-End Adjusting Journal Generation	The system must automatically generate standard period-end adjusting journal entries based on predefined templates and AI analysis. Required adjustments include: depreciation of fixed assets (vessels on a per-voyage or straight-line basis, containers on units-of-production or straight-line basis), amortization of prepaid expenses, accrual of unpaid salaries and wages (including end-of-service gratuity provisions as required by Qatar Labour Law, UAE Labour Law, KSA Labour Law, and Indian Gratuity Act), provision for doubtful receivables based on the IFRS 9 expected credit loss model applied to shipping agent receivable aging, and allocation of shared corporate costs from the Qatar head office to other entities based on revenue-weighted or TEU-weighted allocation keys. Each auto-generated adjusting entry must be queued in draft status for review and approval by the entity controller before posting. The system must highlight any adjustments that differ significantly from prior periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R009	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Soft Close and Hard Close Period Locking	The system must support a two-stage period close mechanism: soft close and hard close. During soft close, the period remains open for designated users (typically senior accountants and controllers) who have explicit soft-close posting permission, while all other users are blocked from posting. This allows late adjustments and corrections without risking unauthorized postings. During hard close, the period is locked for all users without exception — any required adjustments must be posted to an adjustment period or the next open period. The transition from open to soft-close and from soft-close to hard-close must require explicit approval from the entity controller with a digital signature. The system must provide a countdown dashboard showing days remaining until soft-close and hard-close deadlines. Emergency reopening of a hard-closed period must require approval from the group CFO and the external audit partner, with a full audit trail of the reopening reason, duration, and all postings made during the reopened window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R010	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Trial Balance Generation and Validation	The system must generate a trial balance for each entity at any point during the period close process, reflecting all posted entries including adjustments. The trial balance must be available in three views: transaction currency, functional currency, and group reporting currency (USD). Total debits must equal total credits in each currency view — any imbalance must be flagged as a critical error preventing period close. The trial balance must support filtering by account range, account type, cost center, and voyage dimension. A comparative trial balance showing current period versus prior period, current period versus same period prior year, and current period versus budget must be available. The system must provide an AI-driven trial balance review that highlights unusual fluctuations — defined as variances exceeding 20% from the prior period average for expense accounts and 15% for revenue accounts — with natural language explanations in Arabic or English suggesting possible causes such as seasonal shipping volume changes, new trade lane launches, or vessel dry-docking periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R011	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Consolidation Elimination Entry Automation	The system must automatically generate consolidation elimination journal entries for group reporting based on the confirmed intercompany balances from the matching process. Elimination entries must cover: intercompany receivable/payable balances, intercompany revenue/expense transactions (such as slot charter income/expense, management fees, agency commissions), unrealized profit on intercompany transactions where assets remain within the group (e.g., container transfers between entities at a markup), and intercompany dividend declarations. The elimination entries must be generated in the group reporting currency (USD) using the exchange rates applicable at the transaction date for income/expense items and the closing rate for balance sheet items as per IAS 21. The system must maintain a separate consolidation entity (CONS) that holds all elimination entries and produces the consolidated trial balance. Elimination entries must be automatically reversed at the start of the next period to maintain clean entity-level books.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R012	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Period Close Progress Dashboard and Notifications	The system must provide a real-time period close dashboard accessible to all finance team members showing the close status of all four entities simultaneously. The dashboard must display: a timeline view of each entity's close progress against target dates, completion percentage of the close checklist, outstanding items count by category (reconciliations, accruals, approvals, adjustments), blocking issues with their assigned owners, and the consolidation readiness status. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary to individual task detail. Notifications must be sent via email and in-app push notification in the user's preferred language when: a task is assigned, a task deadline is approaching (configurable reminder at 24 and 48 hours before deadline), a blocking issue is raised, and the entity close or group close is completed. The dashboard must be viewable on mobile devices with responsive design supporting both LTR and RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R013	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Year-End Close Specific Procedures	The system must support additional procedures specific to the annual year-end close beyond standard monthly close tasks. These procedures include: annual income and expense account closure with automatic balance transfer to retained earnings via a configurable closing entry, final Zakat calculation and provision posting for the KSA entity based on the Zakat base method, final corporate income tax calculation for the India entity based on the applicable tax rate from the Income Tax Act 1961, end-of-service benefit recalculation for all entities based on updated actuarial assumptions where required by IAS 19, and provision for audit fees across all entities. The year-end close must support the creation of separate adjustment periods for post-balance-sheet events discovered after the initial close but before financial statement issuance. The system must generate a year-end close package containing all supporting schedules, reconciliations, and adjusting entry documentation required by external auditors for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R014	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Parallel Period Close Across Entities	The system must support parallel period close processing across all four entities with a configurable entity close sequence that feeds into the group consolidation. Each entity must be able to progress through its close checklist independently without being blocked by other entities, except for intercompany reconciliation tasks which require coordination. The system must enforce a group-level close timeline that defines: entity-level close deadline (e.g., working day 5 after period end), intercompany matching deadline (working day 7), consolidation adjustment deadline (working day 9), and group close completion (working day 10). Working day calculations must respect each entity's local holiday calendar — Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, KSA National Day, and Indian public holidays. The system must display a cross-entity Gantt chart showing the parallel close progress and highlight any entity at risk of delaying the group timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R015	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Close Process Audit Trail and Compliance Documentation	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of the period close process including: who initiated each close task, when each task was completed, what entries were posted as part of the close, who approved each close step, and the final sign-off for entity close and group close. The audit trail must capture the state of all reconciliations at the point of close approval. The system must generate a period close compliance report that attests to the completeness of all required procedures and can be provided to external auditors and regulatory authorities (QFC Regulatory Authority, DFSA, CMA Saudi Arabia, and SEBI/MCA for India). The compliance report must include a digital signature from the entity controller and the group CFO. All close documentation must be archived in a tamper-proof document management system with retention of at least 10 years. The system must support retrieval of any historical close package within 30 seconds for audit and regulatory inquiry purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R016	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Automated Close Task Time Tracking and Performance Analytics	The system must automatically track the time spent on each period close task from assignment to completion. Time tracking must capture: elapsed time, active work time (based on system interaction logs), wait time (time spent pending approvals or dependencies), and block time (time spent waiting on resolution of issues). The system must generate period close performance analytics comparing: current period close duration versus prior periods (trend analysis over the last 12 months), close duration by entity identifying the slowest entity and bottleneck tasks, average task completion time by assigned user, and the impact of AI automation on close duration (comparing AI-automated tasks versus manual tasks). The analytics must be presented in graphical dashboards with the ability to export data to Excel for further analysis. The AI must use these analytics to recommend process improvements for the next period close, such as reassigning tasks, adjusting deadlines, or increasing automation coverage for consistently slow manual tasks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-003-R017	FEAT-041-1-003	MOD-041	Rollback and Recovery for Failed Close Procedures	The system must provide a controlled rollback mechanism for failed or incorrect period close procedures. If an error is discovered after soft-close but before hard-close, the system must allow the entity controller to reopen specific close tasks without unwinding the entire close process. If a critical error is discovered after hard-close, the system must support a controlled period reopening that: logs the reason for reopening, records the approver chain (entity controller plus group CFO), limits the scope of changes to specified accounts or entry types, enforces a maximum reopening window of 72 hours, and automatically re-executes all downstream close tasks (revaluation, reconciliation, consolidation) after the correction is posted. The system must create a complete before-and-after snapshot of all affected balances during the reopening window. Rollback of consolidation elimination entries must be automatically triggered when an entity period is reopened to prevent the group reporting from reflecting stale entity data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R001	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS Statement of Financial Position Generation	The system must generate a complete Statement of Financial Position (Balance Sheet) compliant with IAS 1 for each entity and the consolidated group. The statement must classify assets and liabilities as current or non-current based on the operating cycle applicable to container shipping operations (typically 12 months but configurable per entity). Shipping-specific line items must include: vessels and containers under property, plant and equipment; right-of-use assets for leased vessels, containers, and port facilities under IFRS 16; trade receivables from shipping agents and freight forwarders; bunker fuel inventory; and provisions for container repositioning costs. The statement must display comparative figures for the prior period and prior year-end. Generated output must be available in both Arabic (RTL formatted) and English with the option to produce a side-by-side bilingual format. Amounts must be displayable in the entity's functional currency and optionally in USD for group comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R002	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS Statement of Profit or Loss Generation	The system must generate a Statement of Profit or Loss and Other Comprehensive Income compliant with IAS 1 for each entity and the consolidated group. The statement must support both the nature-of-expense method and the function-of-expense (cost of sales) method, selectable per entity based on management preference. Shipping-specific revenue disaggregation must follow IFRS 15 requirements showing: ocean freight revenue by trade lane, terminal handling charge revenue by port, demurrage and detention income, container rental income, and agency commission income. Cost of sales for a container shipping company must include: vessel operating costs, port and terminal charges, bunker fuel costs, container handling and repair costs, and inland transportation costs. The statement must include other comprehensive income items such as foreign currency translation differences for foreign operations and cash flow hedge reserve movements for bunker fuel hedging. Comparative figures for the prior period must be presented alongside current period figures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R003	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS Statement of Changes in Equity Generation	The system must generate a Statement of Changes in Equity compliant with IAS 1 for each entity and the consolidated group. The statement must present movements in each component of equity: share capital, share premium, statutory reserves (as required by Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Companies Law, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013), retained earnings, foreign currency translation reserve, cash flow hedge reserve, and non-controlling interests for the consolidated statement. Each movement must be categorized as: profit or loss for the period, other comprehensive income items, transactions with owners (dividends, capital contributions), and transfers between reserves. For the KSA entity, the Zakat charge impact on retained earnings must be shown as a separate line item. The statement must display opening balance, each category of movement, and closing balance for both the current and comparative period. Dividends declared must show the per-share amount and total in the entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R012	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment KPI benchmarking	The system must calculate and display key performance indicators per segment and trade lane including: EBITDA margin, return on assets, working capital ratio, days sales outstanding (DSO) for freight receivables, container turnaround time cost impact, and berth productivity cost per vessel call. KPIs must be benchmarkable against user-defined targets, prior year actuals, and industry averages (configurable reference data). Threshold-based alerts must trigger when a segment's KPI deviates beyond configurable tolerance (e.g., margin drops below 12%). KPI cards must render in both English and Arabic with localized number separators (e.g., 1,234.56 for English; ١٬٢٣٤٫٥٦ for Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R004	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS Statement of Cash Flows Generation	The system must generate a Statement of Cash Flows compliant with IAS 7 for each entity and the consolidated group using both the direct method and the indirect method, selectable per entity. For the indirect method, the system must start with profit before tax and systematically adjust for non-cash items including: depreciation of vessels and containers, amortization of right-of-use assets, unrealized foreign exchange gains/losses on monetary items, provision movements, and impairment charges. Working capital changes must separately identify movements in trade receivables (shipping agents), trade payables (port authorities and vendors), bunker fuel inventory, and accrued voyage costs. Investing activities must include: vessel acquisition and disposal proceeds, container fleet capital expenditure, and port equipment purchases. Financing activities must include: vessel financing drawdowns and repayments, container lease payments separated into principal and interest per IFRS 16, dividend payments, and intercompany loan movements. Cash and cash equivalents must be reconciled by currency showing balances at each bank across all four jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R005	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Automated Notes to Financial Statements	The system must automatically generate comprehensive notes to the financial statements as required by IFRS. Notes must include: significant accounting policies specific to container shipping (revenue recognition policy referencing IFRS 15 for voyage revenue, lease accounting policy referencing IFRS 16 for vessel and container leases, hedge accounting policy for bunker fuel price risk), segment reporting per IFRS 8 using trade lanes or geographic regions as operating segments, related party disclosures per IAS 24 covering intercompany transactions between the four entities, and financial instrument disclosures per IFRS 7 including credit risk exposure from shipping agent receivables and currency risk from multi-currency operations. The AI engine must analyze the current period financial data and generate draft narrative disclosures describing significant changes, unusual items, and key judgments. Each AI-generated note must be flagged for review by the entity controller before inclusion in the final statement package.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R006	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Consolidated Financial Statements with Minority Interest	The system must generate consolidated financial statements for the group combining all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with full IFRS 10 compliance. The consolidation must: eliminate all intercompany balances and transactions, eliminate unrealized profits on intercompany transactions (e.g., markup on container transfers between entities), translate foreign operations' financial statements using the closing rate method for balance sheet items and average rate for income statement items as per IAS 21, and calculate and present non-controlling interests where applicable. The system must handle different functional currencies across entities — QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, and INR for India — with the group reporting currency being USD. Translation differences arising from the consolidation must be accumulated in the foreign currency translation reserve within equity. The system must produce a consolidation workbook showing each entity's trial balance, elimination entries, translation adjustments, and the final consolidated figures with full audit trail drill-down capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R007	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Segment Reporting per IFRS 8	The system must generate operating segment disclosures in accordance with IFRS 8 for the consolidated financial statements. Segments must be configurable to reflect how management reviews the container shipping business, with default segments based on geographic trade lanes: Middle East to Indian Subcontinent, Middle East to Far East, Middle East to Europe, Middle East to Africa, and Intra-Gulf. For each segment, the system must report: external revenue, inter-segment revenue, segment profit or loss, segment assets, segment liabilities, depreciation and amortization, capital expenditure, and material non-cash items. The system must derive segment data from the GL using the voyage dimension and port code segments on accounts. A reconciliation from total segment figures to consolidated financial statement totals must be automatically generated showing unallocated corporate costs, elimination of inter-segment transactions, and reconciling items. The AI must identify and flag segments that represent more than 10% of combined revenue, combined profit, or combined assets as reportable segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R008	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Financial Statement Output Formats and Templates	The system must generate financial statements in multiple output formats: PDF for formal distribution with professional formatting, Excel (XLSX) for working paper purposes with formulas preserved, Word (DOCX) for editing of narrative sections, XBRL for regulatory electronic filing with taxonomies mapped to IFRS Foundation taxonomy 2024 and local taxonomies (QFC, DFSA, MCA India), and HTML for web-based viewing on the company portal. PDF output must support corporate branding with configurable headers, footers, logos, and color schemes. Templates must be fully customizable by the finance team without developer involvement using a drag-and-drop report designer. Each template must support both portrait and landscape orientations with automatic page breaking at logical section boundaries. The system must maintain a library of at least 10 pre-built templates conforming to common presentation formats used by Big Four audit firms for container shipping clients operating in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent region.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R009	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Bilingual Arabic-English Financial Statement Rendering	The system must produce financial statements in Arabic, English, and a side-by-side bilingual format where Arabic text appears on the right page and English on the left page or in parallel columns. Arabic financial statements must be fully RTL formatted with: Arabic account descriptions, Arabic narrative notes, Eastern Arabic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) as a configurable option alongside Western numerals, proper RTL table layouts where the first column appears on the right, and Arabic date formatting using both Hijri and Gregorian dates. The bilingual format must ensure that corresponding line items are perfectly aligned between Arabic and English pages so that readers can cross-reference easily. All currency symbols must be displayed in their local forms — ر.ق for QAR, د.إ for AED, ر.س for SAR, ₹ for INR, and $ for USD. The system must support Arabic font selection with a minimum of three professional Arabic typefaces suitable for formal financial documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R008	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Spot Lease Contract Quick Entry	The system must provide a streamlined interface for creating spot lease contracts where containers are leased on a short-term or voyage basis outside of an existing MLA. The quick entry form must auto-populate known lessor details, suggest optimal container types based on the pending booking demand, and calculate estimated total lease cost. The system must support spot leases with minimum durations as short as 14 days. AI must flag when spot lease volumes suggest that negotiating a new MLA would be more cost-effective, providing a breakeven analysis showing the crossover point. The spot lease must be linkable to specific booking references or voyage numbers for cost traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R010	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Financial Statement Drill-Down to Source Transactions	The system must enable interactive drill-down from any line item on a generated financial statement to the underlying source transactions. From the Statement of Financial Position, a user must be able to click on any balance to see the account composition, then drill into each account to see the journal entries, and further drill into each journal entry to see the source document (invoice, payment, bank transaction). For the Statement of Profit or Loss, drill-down must trace revenue to individual bills of lading and voyage manifests, and costs to vendor invoices and port expense records. The drill-down must work across entity boundaries for consolidated statements — a user viewing consolidated freight revenue must be able to expand it by entity, then by trade lane, then by voyage, and finally to individual B/L-level revenue records. Each drill-down level must maintain the same currency view selected at the statement level. The drill-down path must be exportable as a PDF audit trail document for external auditor workpaper purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R011	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Comparative and Trend Financial Analysis	The system must generate comparative financial statements showing current period alongside multiple comparison columns. Supported comparisons include: prior period (month-over-month), same period prior year (year-over-year), year-to-date versus prior year-to-date, budget versus actual, and forecast versus actual. Each comparison must show both the absolute variance and the percentage variance. The system must support up to 5 comparison columns simultaneously in a single statement. Trend analysis must be available showing up to 24 months of historical data in a graphical format embedded within the financial statement package. The AI engine must automatically generate a management commentary section highlighting the top 5 most significant variances with plain-language explanations referencing known business events — for example, correlating a spike in bunker fuel costs with recorded bunker price movements or linking revenue decline to reduced vessel calls at specific ports based on operational data from the voyage management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R012	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS 16 Lease Disclosure Schedule Generation	The system must automatically generate IFRS 16 lease disclosure schedules for container shipping lease arrangements. The schedules must cover: vessel charter leases (time charter and bareboat charter categorized by lease term), container operating leases by container type (20ft, 40ft, 40ft HC, reefer), port facility and terminal concession leases, office and warehouse leases, and vehicle leases. For each lease category, the disclosure must present: carrying amount of right-of-use assets at period end with depreciation charge for the period, lease liability maturity analysis showing undiscounted cash flows in bands (less than 1 year, 1-2 years, 2-5 years, more than 5 years), interest expense on lease liabilities, short-term lease expense for leases of 12 months or less, low-value lease expense, and variable lease payments not included in the measurement of lease liabilities (such as contingent rent based on TEU throughput at port facilities). The system must reconcile total lease liabilities to the Statement of Financial Position and generate the movement schedule from opening to closing balance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R013	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Financial Statement Review and Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a formal review and approval workflow for financial statements before they are finalized and distributed. The workflow must include stages: draft generation (system-automated), first review (entity accountant), revision (based on review comments tracked with markup and version control), controller approval (entity controller sign-off), CFO approval (group CFO for consolidated statements), and board package preparation (final formatting for board of directors review). Each reviewer must be able to annotate specific line items or sections with comments in Arabic or English. The system must maintain a complete version history showing every iteration of the financial statements from first draft to final approved version with the ability to compare any two versions side-by-side highlighting all changes. Approval must be captured with digital signatures and timestamps. The system must prevent distribution of unapproved financial statements and watermark draft versions with 'DRAFT — NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION' in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R014	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Regulatory Financial Statement Packages	The system must generate entity-specific regulatory financial statement packages as required by local authorities. For Qatar: financial statements conforming to QFC Authority requirements including the QFC Regulatory Return format. For UAE: financial statements conforming to the UAE Ministry of Finance format and DFSA requirements for DIFC-registered entities, including the Ultimate Beneficial Ownership reporting supplement. For KSA: financial statements conforming to CMA requirements for listed entities and ZATCA Zakat return supporting schedules showing the Zakat base calculation. For India: financial statements in the Schedule III format of the Companies Act 2013 with additional disclosures required by MCA including Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) spending report, and the XBRL filing in MCA taxonomy. Each regulatory package must include all supplementary schedules, tax computation workpapers, and related party transaction summaries required by the respective authority. The system must track regulatory filing deadlines and send alerts 30, 15, and 7 days before each deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R015	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	AI-Powered Financial Statement Validation	The system must employ an AI engine that validates generated financial statements for completeness, accuracy, and IFRS compliance before submission for review. Validation checks must include: arithmetic consistency (all subtotals and totals recalculated), cross-statement consistency (net profit in P&L matches the movement in retained earnings in the Statement of Changes in Equity, which matches the starting point in the indirect method Cash Flow Statement), IFRS disclosure completeness (all mandatory notes present for the applicable standards — IAS 1, IAS 7, IAS 16, IAS 21, IAS 36, IAS 37, IFRS 7, IFRS 8, IFRS 9, IFRS 15, IFRS 16), sign conventions (assets are positive, liabilities are presented as positive under the equity side), and rounding consistency (all figures consistently rounded to the same precision). The AI must also compare current statements against the entity's prior year audited financials and flag any presentation changes that may require disclosure under IAS 8. Validation results must be presented as a scorecard with pass/fail for each check and detailed findings for failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R004	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	IMO Hazardous Cargo THC Surcharge	The system must apply IMO hazardous cargo surcharges on THC calculations when containers carry dangerous goods classified under IMO classes 1 through 9. The surcharge percentage or flat rate must be configurable per IMO class, per terminal, and per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The system must automatically extract IMO class information from the Dangerous Goods Declaration linked to the container booking and validate against the terminal's accepted hazmat classes. Rejected IMO classes must trigger an automatic hold notification to operations and compliance teams with bilingual Arabic-English alert messages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R016	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Financial Statement Distribution and Access Control	The system must provide controlled distribution of finalized financial statements to authorized recipients. Distribution channels must include: secure email with encrypted PDF attachments, secure download link with time-limited access (configurable expiry defaulting to 30 days), integration with the company's document management system, and direct upload to regulatory filing portals where API integration is available. Access control must enforce: board members receive consolidated group statements only, entity management receives their entity statements and consolidated statements, external auditors receive all entity and consolidated statements with full drill-down access, and regulators receive only the packages specific to their jurisdiction. The system must maintain a distribution log recording: recipient identity, document version distributed, channel used, timestamp of distribution, and confirmation of receipt where available. Password protection must be applied to all distributed PDF files with a separate secure channel for password delivery. Watermarking with the recipient's name must be applied to prevent unauthorized sharing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R017	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	IFRS 9 Expected Credit Loss Disclosure Generation	The system must automatically generate IFRS 9 expected credit loss (ECL) disclosures for trade receivables arising from container shipping operations. The disclosure must present: the aging analysis of trade receivables by category (shipping agents, freight forwarders, direct shippers, port authority rebates receivable) with buckets of current, 1-30 days past due, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, 91-180 days, and over 180 days. For each aging bucket, the system must calculate and display the historical loss rate, the forward-looking adjustment factor based on macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth, trade volumes, bunker fuel price index), and the resulting ECL provision. The provision matrix must be entity-specific reflecting the different credit risk profiles of customers in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. For the consolidated disclosure, the system must aggregate entity-level ECL calculations and present the group-wide credit risk exposure. Movement in the ECL provision from opening to closing balance must be disclosed showing: increases due to new receivables originated, decreases due to amounts written off, changes in forward-looking assumptions, and amounts recovered.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-1-004-R018	FEAT-041-1-004	MOD-041	Scheduled and On-Demand Financial Statement Generation	The system must support both scheduled automatic generation and on-demand manual generation of financial statements. Scheduled generation must be configurable to run at period-end close completion, producing a full set of entity-level and consolidated statements automatically once all close tasks are marked complete. On-demand generation must allow authorized users to produce interim financial statements at any point during an open period using current posted balances, with a clear watermark indicating 'INTERIM — SUBJECT TO PERIOD-END ADJUSTMENTS' in both Arabic and English. The generation engine must be capable of producing a complete set of financial statements (all four primary statements plus notes) for a single entity within 5 minutes and a consolidated group set within 15 minutes for a ledger containing up to 10 million journal lines. The system must queue generation requests and process them in priority order (scheduled generation takes priority over ad-hoc) with notification to the requesting user upon completion. Generation logs must record the exact data snapshot timestamp to ensure reproducibility of any previously generated statement set.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R001	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Define reportable segments by legal entity	The system must allow configuration of reportable financial segments aligned to each legal entity: Qatar (QA-ENT), UAE (AE-ENT), KSA (SA-ENT), and India (IN-ENT). Each segment must capture revenue, cost of goods sold, operating expenses, and net profit independently. Segment definitions must comply with IFRS 8 criteria for reportable segments, including the 10% quantitative thresholds for revenue, profit/loss, and assets. The system must store segment metadata including base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), jurisdiction tax regime, and regulatory body reference. Segments must be lockable during audit periods to prevent retroactive reclassification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R003	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Automated revenue allocation across shared voyages	When a single vessel voyage services multiple trade lanes (e.g., a vessel calling Jebel Ali then Hamad Port then JNPT), the system must automatically allocate voyage-level revenues and costs to each trade lane segment based on configurable allocation methods: TEU-proportional, weight-proportional, revenue-proportional, or slot-utilization-based. The allocation engine must handle both FCL (Full Container Load) and LCL (Less than Container Load) shipments, distinguishing 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, and 45HC container types. Reefer surcharges and hazardous cargo premiums must be allocated to the specific trade lane of the container, not distributed proportionally. Allocation results must be auditable with full drill-down capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R004	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Inter-segment elimination entries	The system must automatically generate inter-segment elimination journal entries when transactions occur between entities on different trade lanes. For example, when the Qatar entity provides feeder services to the UAE entity for cargo transshipped at Hamad Port destined for Khalifa Port, the inter-company revenue and corresponding cost must be eliminated in the consolidated segment view. Elimination entries must reference the original source transactions with full audit trail. The system must support both automatic rule-based elimination and manual override with approval workflow. Elimination journals must carry a distinct journal type code (ELIM) and must not affect individual entity statutory reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R005	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment P&L report generation	The system must generate Profit & Loss reports at the segment level showing revenue breakdown by freight income, demurrage, detention, documentation fees, and ancillary services. Cost categories must include vessel operating costs, port dues, stevedoring, inland haulage, customs brokerage, and administrative overhead. Reports must be producible in both USD (group reporting currency) and the segment's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Each line item must show current period, year-to-date, prior year comparative, and budget figures. Reports must render correctly in both English (LTR) and Arabic (RTL) layouts with proper number formatting per locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R006	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment balance sheet reporting	The system must produce balance sheet reports segmented by entity and trade lane, showing assets (vessels, containers, receivables, cash), liabilities (payables, accrued port charges, deferred revenue from prepaid freight), and equity attributable to each segment. Container assets must be tracked by type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer) and current location port (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT). Inter-segment balances must be clearly identified and reconcilable to elimination entries. The report must support Hijri date headers for KSA entity reports alongside Gregorian dates. Fixed asset segments must align with the vessel registry and container fleet management modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R014	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment reporting access controls	The system must enforce role-based access to segment reports such that entity-level finance staff can only view their own entity's segment data (e.g., Qatar finance team sees only QA-ENT segments), while group-level controllers and CFO can view all segments and consolidated views. Trade lane reports must be accessible to commercial managers only for trade lanes within their operational scope. Report export (PDF, Excel, XBRL) must be governed by the same access rules, preventing unauthorized data extraction. Access changes must be logged with effective dates. The system must support temporary elevated access grants for external auditors with automatic expiry after a configurable period (default 30 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R015	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	Segment reclassification and restatement	The system must support reclassification of transactions between segments when operational corrections are needed, such as a shipment originally booked to the Hamad Port–JNPT trade lane being corrected to the Hamad Port–Mundra lane due to a transshipment route change. Reclassification entries must create reversing and rebooking journals in both affected segments with cross-references. When a segment definition changes (e.g., merging two trade lanes), the system must support retrospective restatement of up to 3 prior years of comparative data. Restatement must be performed in a shadow ledger first for review before committing to the primary books. All reclassifications require documented business justification and manager approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-001-R016	FEAT-041-2-001	MOD-041	AI-driven segment anomaly detection	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to flag unusual segment-level financial patterns such as: a trade lane showing revenue growth but declining margins, disproportionate cost allocations compared to TEU volumes, sudden spikes in demurrage or detention charges at a specific port (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT), or inter-segment transactions deviating from historical norms by more than two standard deviations. Anomalies must be surfaced as prioritized alerts to segment controllers with recommended investigation actions. The AI model must retrain monthly on updated segment data. False positive rates must be trackable, with a user feedback loop to improve detection accuracy over time. Alerts must be bilingual (English and Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R001	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Define consolidation group structure	The system must allow definition of a multi-level consolidation group hierarchy representing the parent entity and all subsidiaries. The structure must capture: legal entity name (English and Arabic), country of incorporation, functional currency, ownership percentage, consolidation method (full consolidation, proportionate, equity method), and entity registration numbers per jurisdiction (Qatar Commercial Registration, UAE DED License, KSA CR Number, India CIN). The hierarchy must support intermediate holding companies and must be versionable to reflect changes in group structure over time. Each entity must be tagged with its applicable accounting framework (IFRS, Indian GAAP/Ind AS) and fiscal year-end date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R002	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Automated inter-company transaction matching	The system must automatically match inter-company transactions between group entities by comparing inter-company receivables against corresponding payables, inter-company revenue against corresponding costs, and inter-company loan balances. Matching must use transaction reference numbers, bill of lading numbers, and voyage identifiers as correlation keys. Unmatched items must be flagged for investigation with a tolerance threshold (configurable, default USD 100 or equivalent in QAR/AED/SAR/INR). The matching engine must process transactions across different currencies, converting at the transaction-date spot rate. A reconciliation report must list all matched pairs, tolerance-matched items, and unresolved differences with aging analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R003	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Inter-company elimination journal generation	Upon completion of inter-company matching, the system must automatically generate elimination journal entries that remove inter-company revenue/expenses, receivables/payables, dividends, and unrealized profits on inter-company asset transfers (e.g., containers transferred between entities at a markup). Elimination entries must be posted to a dedicated consolidation ledger (CONSOL-LED) separate from individual entity books. Each elimination must reference the matched transaction pair IDs. The system must handle partial eliminations when ownership is less than 100% (e.g., a 70%-owned KSA subsidiary). Elimination journals must support both English and Arabic descriptions and must be reversible if the source matching is corrected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R004	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Currency translation for consolidation	The system must translate each subsidiary's trial balance from its functional currency to the group presentation currency (USD) applying IAS 21 rules: assets and liabilities at the closing rate on the reporting date, income and expenses at the average rate for the period (weighted average or simple average configurable), and equity items at historical rates. The resulting foreign currency translation difference must be recorded in Other Comprehensive Income (OCI) under a Currency Translation Reserve. The system must source exchange rates from configured providers (Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI) with the ability to manually override rates subject to dual-authorization. Translation must handle the Indian entity's March 31 fiscal year-end through a stub period adjustment mechanism.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R005	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Minority interest calculation	For subsidiaries where the parent holds less than 100% ownership, the system must calculate Non-Controlling Interest (NCI) in both the consolidated balance sheet and income statement. NCI in net assets must equal the minority percentage applied to the subsidiary's net assets at fair value on the acquisition date plus the minority's share of post-acquisition changes in equity. NCI in profit/loss must be the minority percentage of the subsidiary's net income for the period. The system must handle changes in ownership percentage (e.g., additional share purchases increasing ownership from 70% to 85%) with appropriate adjustment entries. NCI must be presented separately in the consolidated balance sheet within equity and in the consolidated income statement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R006	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Goodwill and acquisition accounting	The system must support the recording and subsequent measurement of goodwill arising from business combinations under IFRS 3. Goodwill must be calculated as the excess of consideration paid plus NCI value over the net fair value of identifiable assets and liabilities acquired. The system must support both the full goodwill method and the partial goodwill method. Annual impairment testing must be facilitated by maintaining cash-generating unit (CGU) allocations for each goodwill amount. Impairment losses must be recorded in the consolidation ledger and must not be reversed in subsequent periods per IAS 36. The system must track the original acquisition date, purchase price allocation details, and contingent consideration fair value adjustments over the measurement period (up to 12 months post-acquisition).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R007	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidated balance sheet generation	The system must produce a consolidated balance sheet combining all group entities after applying eliminations, currency translations, and NCI adjustments. The balance sheet must present current and non-current classifications for both assets and liabilities. Shipping-specific asset categories must include: vessels (by class and registry), containers (by type: 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, tank), port infrastructure, and right-of-use assets for leased terminal equipment. The report must display current period, prior period comparative, and optionally two prior periods for trend analysis. Output must support dual-language headers (English and Arabic), Hijri date notation for KSA regulatory submissions, and export to PDF, Excel, and XBRL formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R008	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidated income statement generation	The system must generate a consolidated income statement showing group-level revenue (freight income, demurrage and detention, agency commissions, warehousing, CFS charges), cost of services (vessel operating, port charges, inland transport, bunker fuel), gross profit, administrative expenses, finance costs, foreign exchange gains/losses, and net profit after tax. Revenue must be disaggregated by trade lane category and service type per IFRS 15 disclosure requirements. The statement must include earnings per share (basic and diluted) if applicable. Prior period comparatives and year-to-date cumulative figures must be included. The income statement must reconcile to the segment reporting totals from FEAT-041-2-001 with a documented reconciliation trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R009	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidated cash flow statement	The system must produce a consolidated statement of cash flows using either the direct or indirect method (configurable per group policy). Operating activities must separately identify cash receipts from freight customers and cash payments to port authorities, vessel operators, and customs agencies. Investing activities must capture vessel acquisitions/disposals, container fleet purchases, and port infrastructure investments at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Financing activities must include loan drawdowns, repayments, lease payments under IFRS 16, and dividend distributions. The effect of exchange rate changes on cash held in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR must be separately disclosed. Intercompany cash flows must be eliminated from the consolidated presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R010	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidated statement of changes in equity	The system must generate a statement of changes in equity showing movement in share capital, retained earnings, foreign currency translation reserve, hedging reserve, revaluation surplus, and non-controlling interests for the reporting period. Each component must show opening balance, movements during the period (profit/loss, other comprehensive income, dividends, share transactions, changes in ownership interests), and closing balance. The statement must handle retrospective restatements under IAS 8 by adjusting opening balances with clear disclosure. Currency translation adjustments recycled to profit/loss on disposal of a subsidiary must be identifiable. The report must support Arabic language output with RTL formatting for all column headers, row labels, and footnote references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R011	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidation adjustment journals	The system must support manual consolidation adjustment entries for items that cannot be automated, such as fair value adjustments to subsidiary assets, alignment of accounting policies across entities (e.g., different depreciation methods for vessels between Qatar and India entities), and reclassification entries to conform subsidiary chart of accounts to group reporting structure. Each manual adjustment must carry a journal type of CONSOL-ADJ, require narrative justification in both English and Arabic, and be subject to reviewer approval before posting. Adjustments must be reversible and must not post to individual entity ledgers. The system must maintain a register of recurring consolidation adjustments that can be automatically proposed each period for reviewer confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R012	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Stub period alignment for differing year-ends	The system must handle the alignment of subsidiary financial data when fiscal year-ends differ from the group reporting date. Specifically, the India entity (March 31 year-end) must provide stub period financial data (April 1 to December 31 or whichever group year-end applies) for consolidation into the December 31 group accounts. The system must automatically identify the stub period, request or calculate the incremental financial data for the stub, and apply appropriate currency translation rates for the stub period. Significant transactions occurring between the subsidiary's year-end and the group year-end must be adjustable per IAS 10 requirements. The stub period handling must generate an audit trail documenting the periods used and any adjustments applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R013	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Regulatory reporting packages per jurisdiction	The system must produce statutory reporting packages tailored to each jurisdiction's regulatory requirements: Qatar Financial Markets Authority (QFMA) format, UAE Securities and Commodities Authority (SCA) format, Saudi Organization for Chartered and Professional Accountants (SOCPA) format with Hijri calendar compliance, and India Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA) Schedule III format under the Companies Act 2013. Each package must include the required financial statements, notes, directors' report template, and auditor's report template. Packages must be generated in the jurisdiction's official language(s) with English and Arabic as minimum. Export formats must include PDF for filing and XBRL for electronic submission where mandated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R014	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidation workflow and approval	The system must implement a structured consolidation workflow with defined stages: data collection from entities, inter-company matching, elimination processing, currency translation, manual adjustments, review, and final approval. Each stage must have assigned responsible roles and deadlines. The workflow must track completion percentage per entity and per stage. Escalation notifications must trigger when deadlines are at risk. The CFO or Group Financial Controller must provide final sign-off before the consolidated statements are marked as 'Approved.' Once approved, the consolidation must be locked from further changes unless formally reopened through a controlled process with full audit logging of the reopening justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R007	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Voyage P&L Impact Assessment	The system must calculate the financial impact of performance deviations on voyage profitability by comparing actual costs against CP-budgeted figures. Cost categories must include bunker consumption variance, off-hire deductions, speed claim exposure, port cost overruns, and canal transit delays (Suez Canal for Gulf-India routes). Revenue impact must account for freight rate adjustments, demurrage income, and despatch savings. All financial calculations must support multi-currency presentation in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with consolidation at entity and group levels. Voyage P&L variance reports must be generated within 24 hours of voyage completion with AI-generated narrative summaries in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R015	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Consolidation audit trail and version control	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every consolidation run, capturing: all individual entity trial balances used (with timestamps), elimination entries generated, currency rates applied, manual adjustments posted, NCI calculations, and the final consolidated figures. Each consolidation must be assigned a version number (e.g., V1 for initial, V2 for post-audit-adjustment). Prior versions must remain accessible for comparison. The system must generate a consolidation reconciliation report showing how individual entity figures map to the consolidated totals through a waterfall view (entity totals → eliminations → translations → adjustments → consolidated total). All audit records must be retained for a minimum of 7 years per regional regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R016	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	Offline consolidation data package	The system must support generation and consumption of offline data packages for scenarios where entity-level systems are temporarily disconnected (e.g., vessel-based offices or remote port locations with limited connectivity). Each entity must be able to export a signed and encrypted data package containing its trial balance, inter-company transaction listing, and supporting schedules. The group consolidation system must import these packages, validate data integrity via checksums, and incorporate the data into the consolidation process. The package format must include metadata such as reporting period, entity ID, functional currency, and generation timestamp. Conflict resolution rules must handle cases where an offline package is superseded by a later online submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-002-R017	FEAT-041-2-002	MOD-041	AI-assisted consolidation anomaly review	The system must utilize AI capabilities to review consolidated financial statements for anomalies before final approval. The AI engine must check for: unusual fluctuations in consolidated line items compared to prior periods (beyond configurable thresholds), elimination entries that do not net to zero indicating unmatched inter-company items, currency translation imbalances, NCI calculations that deviate from expected ownership percentages, and consolidated cash flow figures that are inconsistent with balance sheet movements. Detected anomalies must be presented in a prioritized review queue with AI-generated explanatory notes in English and Arabic. The system must learn from reviewer actions (accept/investigate/correct) to improve anomaly detection precision over successive consolidation cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R001	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget structure and hierarchy definition	The system must allow definition of budget structures that align with the chart of accounts and organizational hierarchy. Budgets must be definable at multiple levels: group consolidated, entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), department (operations, commercial, finance, HR), and trade lane. The hierarchy must support roll-up aggregation from the lowest level to consolidated group budget. Budget line items must map one-to-one to GL account codes to enable automated actual-vs-budget comparison. The structure must support both operational budgets (revenue, COGS, OPEX) and capital expenditure budgets (vessel acquisition, container purchases, port infrastructure). Each budget node must carry a unique identifier and be taggable with cost center and profit center codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R002	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Multi-currency budget entry and translation	The system must allow budget amounts to be entered in the local functional currency of each entity (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) and automatically translate to the group reporting currency (USD) using budgeted exchange rates. Budgeted exchange rates must be configurable per currency pair and per period (monthly or quarterly). The system must recalculate translated budget amounts when budgeted rates are updated, maintaining both local currency and translated currency versions. Bunker fuel budgets must be enterable in USD regardless of entity since bunker is globally priced in USD. The system must display budget figures in the user's preferred currency with the exchange rate used clearly disclosed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R003	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Top-down budget allocation	The system must support a top-down budgeting approach where group management sets consolidated budget targets that are then allocated downward to entities and trade lanes. Allocation methods must include: proportional to prior year actuals, proportional to forecast TEU volumes, equal distribution, or manual specification. The system must allow entity controllers to accept, modify (within a tolerance band configurable by group, e.g., +/- 5%), or reject allocated targets with documented justification. Rejected allocations must trigger a review workflow back to group management. The allocation process must handle shared cost pools (group IT, insurance, shared vessel capacity) using the same allocation rules defined in the segment reporting cost allocation engine (cross-reference FEAT-041-2-001-R008).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R004	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Bottom-up budget submission	The system must support a bottom-up budgeting approach where individual departments and trade lane managers prepare detailed budget submissions that aggregate upward. Each submission must include line-item details with supporting assumptions documented in structured comment fields. Shipping-specific assumptions must include: expected TEU volumes per trade lane, average freight rate per TEU (differentiated by container type: 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer), bunker price assumption per metric ton, expected vessel utilization percentage, port call frequency, and anticipated demurrage/detention revenue. Submissions must go through a multi-level approval chain: department head, entity CFO, group financial controller. The system must lock submitted budgets from editing once they enter the approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R005	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget versioning and scenario modeling	The system must support multiple budget versions including: initial budget, revised budget (mid-year revision), and latest estimate. Additionally, the system must support scenario modeling with at least three concurrent scenarios: base case, optimistic, and pessimistic. Scenarios must allow variation of key shipping-specific drivers such as freight rates (impact of rate fluctuations on Hamad Port–JNPT lane), bunker prices (Brent-linked projection models), port throughput volumes, currency exchange rates, and new trade lane launches. Each version and scenario must be independently stored and comparable side-by-side. The system must calculate the financial impact delta between scenarios and present it in a summary dashboard. Version history must be immutable once a version is formally approved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R006	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Rolling forecast integration	The system must support rolling forecasts that combine actual results for elapsed periods with forecast data for remaining periods, providing a continuously updated full-year projection. The rolling forecast must extend beyond the current fiscal year, supporting an 18-month rolling horizon. Forecast updates must be triggerable monthly or on-demand when significant market events occur (e.g., sudden freight rate changes, new IMO regulations, port congestion events). The system must auto-populate the actual portion from the GL module's posted transactions. Forecast cells must support both manual entry and formula-driven calculations linked to volume and rate assumptions. Rolling forecasts must be versioned and comparable to the approved annual budget.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R007	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Vessel and voyage budget templates	The system must provide specialized budget templates for vessel operating costs and individual voyage budgets. Vessel operating budget templates must include: crew costs, insurance (H&M and P&I), maintenance and dry-docking provisions, classification society fees, and flag state compliance costs. Voyage budget templates must capture: bunker consumption (based on vessel speed and route distance between ports like Hamad to JNPT), port charges at each call port, canal transit fees (if applicable), pilotage, towage, agency fees, and cargo handling costs (stevedoring, THC). Templates must auto-calculate total voyage cost and derive a break-even freight rate per TEU. Templates must be reusable across similar vessel classes and trade lane routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R008	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Capital expenditure budget management	The system must manage capital expenditure (CAPEX) budgets separately from operational budgets with specific tracking for shipping assets. CAPEX categories must include: new vessel acquisition or construction, second-hand vessel purchase, container fleet expansion (by type: 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, tank, flat-rack), port terminal equipment (cranes, reach stackers, chassis), IT infrastructure, and office premises. Each CAPEX item must have: estimated cost in local and group currency, expected commissioning date, useful life for depreciation calculation, funding source (internal, bank loan, lease), and ROI projection. CAPEX budget approval must follow a tiered authority matrix based on amount thresholds (e.g., items over USD 1M require board approval). The system must track committed vs. uncommitted CAPEX.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R009	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget approval workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-stage budget approval workflow supporting the following stages: draft, submitted, under review, revision requested, entity approved, and group approved. Each stage transition must be governed by role-based permissions: budget preparers can draft and submit, entity CFOs can approve at entity level, and the group financial controller and CEO must provide final group-level approval. The workflow must support parallel approval paths (e.g., all four entity budgets reviewed simultaneously). Approvers must be able to add comments, request specific line-item changes, and conditionally approve with noted exceptions. Email and in-app notifications must trigger at each stage transition. The workflow must support delegation of approval authority during approver absence with audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R010	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget calendar and timeline management	The system must manage the annual budget cycle through a configurable budget calendar defining key milestones: budget kick-off, assumption distribution, bottom-up submission deadline, review period, revision window, entity approval deadline, consolidation, and group approval deadline. The calendar must accommodate the India entity's different fiscal year (April–March) alongside the December year-end entities. Milestones must be trackable with completion status per entity. Automated reminders must be sent to responsible parties at configurable intervals before deadlines (e.g., 14 days, 7 days, 3 days, 1 day). The calendar must support Hijri date display for KSA users. A Gantt chart view must visualize the budget timeline with entity-specific progress indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R011	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget data import and integration	The system must support budget data import from external sources including Excel spreadsheets (XLSX format with predefined templates), CSV files, and direct API integration with operational planning systems (voyage planning, fleet management). Import templates must validate data against the budget structure, rejecting entries that do not map to valid GL accounts, cost centers, or budget periods. The system must handle bulk imports for all 12 monthly periods in a single upload. Imported data must be staged for review before committing to the budget. Error handling must provide row-level validation messages identifying invalid account codes, currency mismatches, or period conflicts. The import process must support incremental updates (adding to or replacing specific budget lines) without overwriting the entire budget.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R012	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget vs. actual monitoring dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard comparing budgeted figures against actual GL balances across all entities and trade lanes. The dashboard must display: total budget, actual to date, committed but not yet posted (purchase orders, approved voyage estimates), remaining budget, and projected year-end position. Visual indicators (color-coded status: green within 5%, amber 5-15% deviation, red over 15%) must highlight budget lines at risk of overrun. Users must be able to drill down from dashboard summaries to underlying transactions. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, department, trade lane, account category, and period. Data must refresh within 5 minutes of GL posting. Both English and Arabic UI must be supported with proper RTL layout for Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R013	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget reallocation and transfer	The system must allow authorized users to transfer budget between line items, departments, or trade lanes within the same entity subject to approval rules. Transfers must not change the entity's total budget unless explicitly approved by the entity CFO. Cross-entity budget transfers must require group-level approval. Each transfer must document: source budget line, destination budget line, amount, justification, and effective date. Transfer requests must go through an approval workflow appropriate to the transfer amount (e.g., transfers below USD 50,000 require department head approval; above USD 50,000 require entity CFO). The system must maintain a complete history of all budget movements showing the original budget, cumulative transfers in/out, and current working budget per line.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R014	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget commitment and encumbrance tracking	The system must track budget commitments (encumbrances) arising from approved purchase orders, signed contracts, and estimated voyage costs before actual expenses are recorded in the GL. Commitments must reduce available budget in real time, preventing over-commitment. Shipping-specific commitments must include: vessel charter agreements, port concession fees, long-term container lease contracts, bunker purchase contracts, and inland haulage service agreements. When an actual invoice is posted against a commitment, the encumbrance must be automatically released. Partial deliveries must proportionally release the encumbrance. The system must report: original budget, encumbrances, actuals, available balance (budget minus encumbrances minus actuals). Encumbrance data must be available for all currencies with automatic translation to group reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R015	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	AI-powered budget forecasting assistance	The system must provide AI-driven budget forecasting that analyzes historical financial data, seasonal shipping volume patterns, freight rate trends from market indices, bunker price trajectories, and macroeconomic indicators to suggest budget line-item values. The AI must generate confidence intervals for key budget assumptions (e.g., projected freight rate per TEU on the Jebel Ali–JNPT lane with 80% confidence band). Suggestions must be presentable to budget preparers as starting points that can be accepted, modified, or rejected. The AI must account for entity-specific patterns such as Ramadan-period volume dips in Gulf entities, monsoon-season impacts on Indian port operations at JNPT, and year-end cargo surges. AI-generated forecasts must include explainability narratives in both English and Arabic describing the key drivers behind each suggestion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-003-R016	FEAT-041-2-003	MOD-041	Budget reporting and export	The system must generate comprehensive budget reports including: budget summary by entity, detailed budget by GL account and cost center, budget-to-actual comparison, budget variance report, rolling forecast comparison, and CAPEX budget status. Reports must support multiple output formats: on-screen interactive view, PDF for distribution, Excel for further analysis, and structured JSON/XML for system integration. Each report must be producible in both English and Arabic with correct RTL layout, localized number formatting, and bilingual column headers. Hijri dates must be displayed alongside Gregorian dates in reports for KSA entity budgets. Reports must support parameterized execution (by entity, period range, currency, version) and must be schedulable for automatic generation and email distribution on configured dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R001	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Automated variance calculation engine	The system must automatically calculate financial variances by comparing actual GL balances against budget, forecast, prior period, and prior year for every GL account, cost center, trade lane, and entity combination. Variance must be computed as both absolute amount (actual minus budget) and percentage deviation. The engine must classify variances as favorable or unfavorable based on the account type (favorable when revenue exceeds budget or expense is below budget). Calculations must run in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and in the group reporting currency (USD). The engine must process full entity variance calculations within 60 seconds for datasets containing up to 50,000 GL account-period combinations. Variances must be calculable for any closed or current open period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R002	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Materiality threshold configuration	The system must allow configuration of materiality thresholds that determine which variances require mandatory explanation. Thresholds must be definable at multiple levels: group-wide default, entity-specific overrides, and account-category-specific settings. Thresholds must support both absolute amount (e.g., variances exceeding USD 50,000 or equivalent in QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and percentage (e.g., variances exceeding 10% of budget). A variance must be flagged as material if it exceeds either the absolute or percentage threshold. Threshold configurations must be versionable with effective dates so that audit trails reflect which thresholds applied during each reporting period. Different thresholds may apply for interim (quarterly) versus annual reporting. The system must support at least three severity levels: informational, requires explanation, and requires executive review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R003	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance commentary workflow	The system must implement a structured workflow for variance commentary where material variances are assigned to responsible managers for explanation. The workflow must include stages: variance identified, commentary requested, commentary submitted, commentary reviewed, and commentary approved. Assignments must be automatic based on the GL account ownership mapping (e.g., bunker cost variances to the vessel operations manager, freight revenue variances to the commercial manager for the relevant trade lane). Commentators must provide structured explanations including: root cause category (volume, price/rate, mix, timing, one-off, FX), quantified impact breakdown, and corrective action if unfavorable. Commentary must be enterable in English, Arabic, or both. Escalation notifications must trigger when commentary deadlines are missed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R004	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Shipping-specific variance decomposition	The system must decompose shipping revenue and cost variances into operationally meaningful components. Revenue variances must break down into: volume variance (actual vs. budgeted TEU), rate variance (actual vs. budgeted freight rate per TEU), mix variance (container type mix: 20GP vs. 40HC vs. reefer), and trade lane mix variance. Cost variances must decompose into: bunker price variance (actual vs. budgeted price per MT), bunker consumption variance (actual vs. budgeted MT consumed per nautical mile), port cost variance by port (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa, JNPT), vessel utilization variance, and demurrage/detention variance. Each component must be quantified in both local and USD terms with drill-down to supporting operational data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R005	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Currency impact variance isolation	The system must isolate the foreign exchange impact on variances by calculating what the actual results would have been at budgeted exchange rates (constant currency analysis). For each variance line, the system must present: variance at actual rates, variance at budgeted rates, and the pure FX impact (the difference). This analysis must cover all currency pairs relevant to the group: QAR/USD, AED/USD, SAR/USD, INR/USD, and cross rates between entity currencies. The FX isolation must be applied to both revenue and cost variances. For trade lanes crossing currency zones (e.g., cargo billed in AED with port costs in INR), the system must decompose the FX variance into its constituent currency pair effects. The constant currency analysis must be available at entity, trade lane, and consolidated group levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R006	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance trend analysis and patterns	The system must provide trend analysis showing how variances evolve over consecutive periods (monthly, quarterly, annually) to identify persistent patterns versus one-off deviations. Trend visualization must include line charts showing actual-vs-budget trajectories, cumulative variance build-up charts, and waterfall charts showing period-over-period variance movement. The system must flag recurring variances that appear in three or more consecutive periods as potential structural issues requiring budget revision. Seasonal patterns specific to container shipping must be recognizable: Ramadan volume dips in Gulf trade lanes, Chinese New Year cargo front-loading, Indian monsoon season disruptions at JNPT, and year-end cargo surges. Trend data must be exportable and must display correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R007	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	AI-generated variance narratives	The system must utilize AI to automatically generate draft variance commentary narratives based on the quantitative variance data and available operational context. AI narratives must: describe the variance in business terms (e.g., 'Freight revenue on the Jebel Ali–JNPT lane was USD 230K below budget primarily due to a 4% decline in average freight rate per TEU, partially offset by 2% higher volume'), identify probable root causes by correlating with operational data (vessel delays, port congestion, market rate indices), and suggest whether the variance is likely to recur. Narratives must be generated in both English and Arabic. Generated text must be clearly marked as AI-drafted and must be reviewed and approved by the responsible manager before inclusion in final reports. The AI must improve narrative quality over time by learning from manager edits to prior drafts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R008	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance reporting by entity and trade lane	The system must produce variance analysis reports at multiple levels: individual entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), individual trade lane (e.g., Hamad Port–JNPT), entity summary across all trade lanes, and consolidated group. Each report must present: account description, budget amount, actual amount, variance amount, variance percentage, favorability indicator, and commentary status (pending, submitted, approved). Reports must support sorting by largest absolute variance, largest percentage variance, or chart of accounts order. Filtering must allow selection by: variance type (favorable/unfavorable only), materiality level, commentary status, and account category. Reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual (English/Arabic) headers and Hijri date support for KSA reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R009	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Executive variance summary dashboard	The system must provide an executive-level dashboard summarizing key variances across the group for C-suite consumption. The dashboard must highlight: top 10 favorable and unfavorable variances by absolute amount, variance summary by entity with traffic-light indicators, revenue and margin variance waterfall chart (budget → volume → rate → mix → FX → actual), key cost category variances (bunker, port charges, crew, G&A), and a commentary completion progress tracker. The dashboard must load within 3 seconds and must support dynamic filtering by entity, period, and currency. Executive users must be able to click any variance item to access the detailed commentary and drill-down analysis. The dashboard must be accessible on tablet devices with responsive layout and must render correctly in Arabic RTL mode with mirrored chart elements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R010	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance drill-down to operational root causes	The system must enable drill-down from a financial variance to the underlying operational transactions and events that caused it. For example, a bunker cost overrun must drill down to: individual voyage fuel consumption records, vessel speed profiles, route deviations, bunker purchase records at specific ports (Hamad Port bunkering, Fujairah anchorage bunkering), and price per MT at each purchase. A demurrage revenue variance must drill through to: container-level detention events, customer invoices, waiver approvals, and container dwell time reports at each port. The drill-down must cross-reference data from the voyage management, container tracking, and billing modules. Each drill-down level must be accessible within 2 seconds. The system must support bookmarking frequently accessed drill-down paths for quick access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R011	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance commentary templates and categories	The system must provide configurable commentary templates categorized by variance type to standardize and accelerate the commentary process. Template categories must include: volume-driven (TEU throughput deviation), rate-driven (freight rate or bunker price movement), timing (revenue recognition or cost accrual differences), one-off items (vessel incident, port strike, regulatory penalty), foreign exchange impact, and new business/lost business. Each template must prompt the commentator for specific structured data fields: root cause description, quantified impact in local currency and USD, expected duration (one-off vs. recurring), and corrective action plan with responsible party and target date. Templates must be available in both English and Arabic. The system must suggest the most appropriate template based on the account type and variance characteristics using pattern matching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R012	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Prior period and year-over-year variance analysis	Beyond budget-to-actual comparison, the system must support variance analysis comparing current period actuals against: prior month, same month prior year, prior quarter, same quarter prior year, and full prior year. Year-over-year analysis must handle the India entity's April–March fiscal year correctly, aligning comparable periods across entities. The system must support normalization adjustments for periods with different working days, port holidays (Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, Indian Republic Day/Independence Day), and varying Ramadan impacts across years. Month-over-month variance trending must auto-adjust for seasonal patterns to prevent spurious variance flags. All comparative analyses must be available in entity local currency and group USD with consistent exchange rate treatment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R013	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance impact on forecast updates	The system must link variance analysis outputs to the rolling forecast process by allowing material variances to trigger forecast revision workflows. When a variance is identified as recurring (persisting for 3+ months or flagged by the commentator), the system must prompt the relevant budget owner to update the remaining-period forecast to reflect the new run-rate. The forecast update must calculate the projected full-year impact of the variance if it continues at the current rate. For example, if bunker costs have been running 8% over budget for three months across Hamad Port and JNPT voyages, the system must project the annualized overrun and propose a revised full-year forecast. Forecast adjustments linked to variances must carry a reference to the originating variance analysis. The system must distinguish between variances that have been incorporated into forecasts and those still pending forecast update.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R014	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance sign-off and audit trail	The system must enforce a formal sign-off process for variance commentaries where the entity financial controller reviews and approves all material variance explanations before they are included in management reports. The group financial controller must perform a secondary review for consolidated variances. Each sign-off must capture: reviewer identity, timestamp, approval status (approved, approved with modifications, rejected with revision instructions), and any amendments to the original commentary. The complete audit trail must be accessible for internal and external audit purposes. Signed-off variance reports must be tamper-protected with version hashing. The system must generate a sign-off completion report showing outstanding approvals per entity and period, enabling the group controller to track month-end close progress. All sign-off data must be retained for a minimum of 7 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R015	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Automated variance alert notifications	The system must send automated notifications when variances exceed configured materiality thresholds. Notifications must be delivered via in-app alerts, email, and optionally mobile push notification. Alert content must include: account description, budget amount, actual amount, variance amount and percentage, favorability classification, and the assigned commentator. Alerts must be tiered by severity: informational alerts for minor variances, action-required alerts for material variances needing commentary, and escalation alerts for critical variances exceeding a second higher threshold (e.g., 25% deviation or USD 500,000). Escalation alerts must notify the entity CFO and group controller directly. Alert frequency must be configurable (real-time as GL postings occur, daily digest, or weekly summary). Alerts must support bilingual content (English and Arabic) based on recipient language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R016	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance analysis offline capability	The system must support offline access to variance analysis data for users operating in environments with limited or no internet connectivity, such as vessel-based finance officers or port operations staff at remote terminals. The offline mode must cache the most recent variance report data, budget figures, actual balances, and commentary status for the user's assigned entity and trade lanes. Users must be able to enter and save variance commentaries offline, which automatically synchronize when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution must handle cases where the underlying variance data has been updated during the offline period by flagging stale commentaries for re-review. Offline data must be encrypted at rest on the device and must expire after a configurable period (default 7 days) requiring re-synchronization. The offline interface must support both English and Arabic with full RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-041-2-004-R017	FEAT-041-2-004	MOD-041	Variance analysis integration with board reporting	The system must produce board-ready variance analysis packages that summarize financial performance against budget for executive committee and board of directors presentations. The package must include: one-page executive summary with key metrics (group revenue, EBITDA, net profit vs. budget), entity-level performance scorecards, top variances with approved commentaries, visual charts (waterfall, trend lines, heat maps by trade lane), and management action items arising from variance analysis. The package must be auto-generated from approved variance data without manual reconstruction. Output format must support PowerPoint (PPTX) for board presentations and PDF for archival. Content must be producible in English and Arabic. Charts must use corporate-standard color schemes configurable per organization. The system must track which variance data was included in each board package for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R001	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Baseline Speed-Consumption Curve Registration	The system must allow registration and storage of baseline speed-consumption curves for each vessel in the fleet, capturing fuel consumption rates (in metric tonnes per day) at various speeds (in knots) for both laden and ballast conditions. Curves must support multiple fuel types including VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, and LNG. Each curve must record the vessel IMO number, date of sea trial or model test, reference draft, trim, and displacement. The system must support importing curves from CSV, Excel, or direct API integration with vessel design software. All curve data must be versioned so that historical baselines remain accessible for trend analysis across voyages between Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNHA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R002	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Real-Time Speed Over Ground vs Speed Through Water Tracking	The system must track and display both Speed Over Ground (SOG) and Speed Through Water (STW) for each vessel in real-time, sourcing data from AIS feeds, vessel IoT sensors, and noon reports. The differential between SOG and STW must be computed continuously to assess current and wind effects on vessel performance. When the differential exceeds configurable thresholds (default 1.5 knots), the system must generate an AI-driven alert recommending course adjustments. Data must be stored at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes) and displayed on dashboards supporting both English and Arabic (RTL) layouts with appropriate unit conversions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R003	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Fuel Consumption Rate Monitoring by Fuel Type	The system must monitor actual fuel consumption rates broken down by fuel type (VLSFO, HSFO, LSMGO, MGO, LNG, methanol) for main engine, auxiliary engines, and boilers separately. Consumption data must be sourced from flow meters via IoT integration or manual noon report entry. The system must compare actual consumption against the baseline curve at the current speed and loading condition, computing a performance deviation percentage. All fuel costs must be calculated in the vessel's operating currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) with real-time exchange rate conversion from a configurable FX rate source. Deviations exceeding configurable thresholds must trigger AI-generated alerts to vessel operations teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R004	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Hull and Propeller Fouling Performance Impact Assessment	The system must calculate hull and propeller fouling impact by comparing current speed-consumption performance against clean-hull baseline curves, normalized for draft, trim, sea state (Beaufort scale), wind force, current, and water temperature. The AI engine must apply ISO 19030 methodology for hull and propeller performance assessment, computing added resistance percentage attributable to fouling. The system must maintain a fouling trend timeline for each vessel and predict when fouling-related fuel penalty will exceed configurable thresholds (default 10% above baseline), recommending optimal hull cleaning schedules. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual English/Arabic headers conforming to entity-specific branding for Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R005	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Weather Normalization of Performance Data	The system must normalize all speed-consumption performance data by removing the effects of weather, sea state, current, and shallow water conditions using industry-standard correction methodologies including ISO 15016 and ITTC guidelines. Weather data must be sourced from integrated meteorological services or manual input covering wind speed and direction, wave height and period, swell height and direction, current speed and direction, and air and sea temperature. The normalization engine must produce corrected performance figures that reflect calm-water equivalent performance, enabling accurate trend analysis and degradation detection independent of environmental conditions encountered on routes between the Arabian Gulf and Indian subcontinent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R006	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	AI-Driven Optimal Speed Recommendation Engine	The system must provide AI-driven optimal speed recommendations for each voyage leg, considering the vessel's current speed-consumption curve, hull condition, weather forecast along the route, port ETAs, charter party requirements, fuel prices at bunkering ports (Fujairah, Hamad Port, JNPT), and CII rating targets. The recommendation engine must calculate the most cost-effective speed profile that meets the required arrival time while minimizing total voyage cost. Recommendations must factor in slow-steaming economics including the trade-off between fuel savings and time charter daily hire cost. Results must be presented in the vessel operator's preferred currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with sensitivity analysis showing fuel cost variation at plus or minus one and two knots.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R007	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Laden vs Ballast Performance Segregation	The system must automatically segregate performance data into laden and ballast voyage segments based on cargo loading status, draft readings, and voyage phase markers. Separate performance trend lines must be maintained for laden and ballast conditions for each vessel. The segregation logic must handle partial loading scenarios common in feeder services between Hamad Port and Jebel Ali or coastal runs to JNPT, where vessels may not be fully laden. The system must detect voyage phase transitions (loading, laden passage, discharge, ballast passage) automatically from draft sensor data or manual noon report entries and tag all performance records with the appropriate phase for accurate comparative analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R008	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Multi-Entity Fleet Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a consolidated fleet performance dashboard that displays key performance indicators for all vessels across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The dashboard must support entity-level filtering allowing operations managers to view only vessels belonging to their entity while senior management can view the consolidated fleet. KPIs must include average fleet speed, average fuel consumption per nautical mile, fleet-wide fouling penalty percentage, best and worst performing vessels ranked by efficiency, and total fleet fuel cost in the user's selected currency. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts and support Hijri date display alongside Gregorian dates for reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R009	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Performance Event Logging and Anomaly Detection	The system must maintain a comprehensive performance event log for each vessel capturing all significant performance-related events including speed changes, fuel type switches, engine mode changes, hull cleaning events, propeller polishing, dry docking, and equipment malfunctions. An AI-based anomaly detection engine must continuously analyze incoming performance data and flag deviations from expected patterns, such as sudden increases in fuel consumption not explained by weather or loading changes. Each anomaly must be assigned a severity level (info, warning, critical) and routed to the appropriate operations team via configurable notification channels including email, SMS, and in-app alerts. Anomaly detection models must be trainable on vessel-specific historical data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R010	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Voyage Performance Summary Report Generation	The system must automatically generate a voyage performance summary report at the end of each voyage, comparing actual performance against planned performance and baseline curves. The report must include total fuel consumed by type, average speed achieved versus instructed speed, weather conditions encountered, total distance sailed, cargo carried in TEU and metric tonnes, and calculated efficiency metrics including EEOI (Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator). Reports must be generated in PDF format with bilingual English/Arabic content, entity-specific letterhead and branding, and must be automatically distributed to configurable recipients including vessel managers, charterers, and the technical superintendent. Historical voyage reports must be searchable by vessel, route, date range, and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R011	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Trim Optimization Recommendations	The system must provide AI-driven trim optimization recommendations based on the vessel's hydrostatic data, current cargo and ballast distribution, speed-consumption characteristics at various trim angles, and planned voyage parameters. The trim optimization engine must calculate the optimal fore and aft drafts that minimize fuel consumption for the planned voyage speed and sea conditions. Recommendations must account for port draft restrictions at destination ports including Hamad Port (maximum 15.0m), Jebel Ali (17.0m), Khalifa Port (16.0m), and JNPT (14.0m). The system must display potential fuel savings in both metric tonnes and monetary value in the operating currency, with projected annual savings based on typical voyage patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R012	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Auxiliary Engine and Boiler Consumption Tracking	The system must track fuel consumption for auxiliary engines and boilers separately from main engine consumption, recording running hours, average load percentage, and fuel consumption rate for each auxiliary engine and boiler. The system must compute the auxiliary-to-total fuel consumption ratio and flag vessels where auxiliary consumption exceeds configurable norms (default 15% of total at sea, 85% in port). Port-stay auxiliary consumption must be benchmarked against similar vessel types and port facilities, considering shore power availability at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. AI models must identify patterns of excessive auxiliary consumption and recommend operational improvements such as optimized generator load sharing or boiler cycling schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R008	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	CP Claims Preparation Workflow	The system must provide an automated workflow for preparing charter party performance claims including under-performance speed claims, overconsumption claims, and off-hire claims. The workflow must compile supporting evidence packages containing noon reports, weather data, AIS track records, fuel receipts, and port logs. Claim documents must be generated in both English and Arabic with all monetary values shown in the CP currency and entity-local currency equivalents. The workflow must route claims through configurable approval hierarchies per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with role-based access controls and digital signature support for authorized signatories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R013	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Offline Performance Data Collection and Synchronization	The system must support offline data collection for vessel performance metrics when satellite connectivity is unavailable or limited, which is common in certain stretches of the Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean. The offline module must allow crew to enter speed, consumption, weather, and equipment status data on a local device or shipboard computer. Upon restoration of connectivity, the system must automatically synchronize offline records with the central ERP database, applying conflict resolution rules that prioritize the most recent manual entry while preserving all versions for audit purposes. The synchronization mechanism must handle data volumes of at least 30 days of accumulated noon reports and hourly sensor readings without data loss or corruption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R014	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Charter Party Speed and Consumption Warranty Tracking	The system must track vessel performance against charter party speed and consumption warranties, computing whether the vessel is over-performing or under-performing relative to the warranted figures. The system must apply weather exclusion criteria as defined in the charter party (typically Beaufort Force 5 and above, Douglas Sea State 4 and above) to exclude bad-weather periods from warranty calculations. Under-performance must be quantified in monetary terms using the charter party bunker price or current market bunker price in USD, with conversion to QAR, AED, SAR, or INR as needed. The system must generate claim-ready reports for speed and consumption disputes, formatted per industry standards (BIMCO format), with detailed voyage data supporting each calculation period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R015	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Performance Benchmarking Across Sister Vessels	The system must enable performance benchmarking across sister vessels or vessels of similar type and size within the fleet, comparing speed-consumption efficiency, fouling rates, auxiliary consumption patterns, and overall fuel efficiency. The benchmarking engine must normalize for differences in operating conditions including route, weather, cargo load, and hull cleaning intervals to provide a fair like-for-like comparison. Rankings must be updated weekly and displayed on the fleet dashboard with trend arrows indicating improving or declining performance. The system must generate quarterly benchmarking reports for fleet management, highlighting best practices from top-performing vessels and recommending operational improvements for underperformers. Reports must support Arabic and English bilingual formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-001-R016	FEAT-042-1-001	MOD-042	Integration with Vessel IoT Sensor Platforms	The system must integrate with major vessel IoT sensor platforms and data aggregation services including Kongsberg, Wartsila, ABB, and generic NMEA-2000 data feeds to receive real-time propulsion and consumption data. The integration layer must support secure data transmission protocols (MQTT, REST API, SFTP) and handle data quality issues such as sensor dropouts, out-of-range values, and timestamp misalignment. The system must apply configurable data quality filters that reject or flag readings outside physically plausible ranges (e.g., main engine consumption above 150% of MCR rating). All sensor data must be stored in a time-series database with a minimum retention period of five years to support long-term trend analysis and regulatory compliance across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R001	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Annual CII Reference Line and Rating Boundary Calculation	The system must calculate the CII reference line and rating boundaries (A through E) for each vessel based on the vessel's DWT capacity and ship type category as defined in IMO MEPC.364(79). The system must store and apply the annual reduction factors that tighten CII requirements each year from 2023 onwards, with the ability to update reduction factors when IMO revises them. Rating boundaries must be computed as dd-vectors applied to the reference line, with separate boundary values for each ship type (bulk carrier, tanker, container ship, gas carrier, general cargo, LNG carrier, ro-ro cargo, and combination carriers). The system must support manual override of reference values for novel vessel types not yet categorized by IMO regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R002	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Attained Annual CII Calculation per IMO Formula	The system must calculate the attained annual CII for each vessel using the IMO-prescribed formula: CII = (total CO2 emissions in grams) divided by (capacity in DWT multiplied by distance sailed in nautical miles). CO2 emissions must be derived from fuel consumption data using the fuel-specific carbon conversion factors defined in IMO MEPC.364(79) including 3.206 for diesel/gas oil, 3.151 for LFO, 3.114 for HFO, 2.750 for LNG, and 1.375 for methanol. The system must aggregate fuel consumption across all sources (main engine, auxiliary engines, boilers) and all fuel types consumed during the calendar year. Distance sailed must be sourced from AIS data or noon reports, excluding time at anchorage and in port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R003	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Voyage-Level CII Contribution Tracking	The system must calculate and display the CII contribution of each individual voyage, showing how much each voyage between ports such as Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other calling ports contributes to the vessel's cumulative annual CII. Each voyage CII contribution must show CO2 emitted, distance sailed, cargo carried, and the resulting CII intensity value. The system must enable operations planners to simulate the CII impact of planned voyages before committing to them, allowing what-if analysis of different speed profiles, routing options, and fuel types. Voyage-level CII data must be stored historically for trend analysis and must be viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R004	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Real-Time Year-to-Date CII Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing each vessel's year-to-date CII rating status, including the current attained CII value, the required CII value for each rating boundary (A through E), and the vessel's current projected year-end rating. The dashboard must use a visual gauge or traffic-light indicator showing the vessel's current position relative to rating boundaries. Fleet-wide summaries must show the distribution of vessels across rating categories and highlight vessels at risk of falling into D or E ratings. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel type, and trade route. All labels and legends must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R009	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Historical Vessel Performance Benchmarking Against CP	The system must maintain a historical database of vessel performance against CP terms spanning a minimum of 10 years. It must enable benchmarking of a vessel's current voyage performance against its own historical performance on similar trade routes (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Khalifa Port). The AI engine must identify performance degradation trends that may indicate hull fouling, engine deterioration, or propeller damage. Benchmark reports must present performance trends using interactive charts with data points filterable by vessel, route, season, and cargo type. All historical data must be queryable in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R005	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	AI-Powered CII Year-End Forecast and Scenario Modeling	The system must use AI and machine learning models to forecast each vessel's year-end CII rating based on current year-to-date performance, planned voyage schedule for the remainder of the year, historical seasonal patterns, and expected weather conditions on planned routes. The forecast engine must provide confidence intervals (e.g., 80% and 95% probability bands) around the predicted year-end CII value. The system must support scenario modeling allowing users to adjust variables such as average speed, fuel type mix, planned voyages, and lay-up periods to see their impact on the projected year-end rating. Scenario results must be exportable as PDF reports with charts showing the projected CII trajectory under each scenario.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R006	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Correction Factors and Voyage Adjustment Application	The system must apply all IMO-approved CII correction factors and voyage adjustments as defined in MEPC.355(78), including corrections for ice-class vessels, shuttle tankers, ships with STS operations, and voyages affected by force majeure events such as adverse weather, piracy risk routing, or port congestion causing extended anchorage. The system must support the electrical consumption correction for vessels fitted with cargo cooling or heating systems. Each correction factor must be configurable per vessel and per voyage, with full audit trail of when corrections were applied, by whom, and the justification. The system must flag voyages that may qualify for correction factors based on route and weather data, prompting operators to review and approve the correction application.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R007	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Improvement Plan Generation for D and E Rated Vessels	When a vessel receives or is projected to receive a D or E CII rating, the system must automatically generate a corrective action plan (Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan amendment) as required by IMO regulations. The plan must include AI-recommended measures such as speed reduction targets, route optimization suggestions for Middle East to India trades, hull cleaning scheduling, fuel type switching opportunities, and technical efficiency improvements. Each recommendation must include estimated CII improvement potential quantified in grams of CO2 per DWT-nautical mile, implementation cost estimates in the entity's operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD), and a projected payback period. The plan must be exportable in a format suitable for submission to the vessel's flag state administration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R008	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Multi-Year CII Trend Analysis and Regulatory Projection	The system must maintain and display multi-year CII trend data for each vessel, showing the annual attained CII, the applicable reference line, and rating boundaries for each year since 2023. The trend view must project forward at least five years, showing how tightening annual reduction factors will affect the vessel's future CII rating if current operational patterns continue. The system must highlight the year in which each vessel is projected to fall below a C rating under current operations, providing advance warning for fleet renewal or retrofit planning. Trend charts must be available per vessel, per entity, and fleet-wide, with export capability to PDF and Excel for board-level reporting in English and Arabic bilingual formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R009	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Fuel Type Mix Optimization for CII Compliance	The system must provide AI-driven recommendations for optimal fuel type mix to achieve or maintain target CII ratings, considering the different carbon conversion factors of available fuels (HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG, methanol, biofuels). The optimization engine must factor in fuel availability at key bunkering locations including Fujairah, Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Singapore, and Colombo, along with current and projected fuel prices in USD with conversion to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The system must calculate the cost differential between the current fuel mix and the recommended mix, presenting the incremental cost of CII compliance. Recommendations must respect vessel engine compatibility constraints and fuel tank segregation limitations stored in the vessel technical database.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R010	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Data Verification and Validation Engine	The system must implement a comprehensive data verification and validation engine for all CII calculation inputs, cross-referencing fuel consumption data from multiple sources (flow meters, BDN quantities, tank soundings, noon reports) to identify discrepancies. Validation rules must flag entries where daily fuel consumption deviates by more than a configurable percentage (default 15%) from expected values based on the vessel's speed-consumption curve and current operating conditions. Distance data must be validated against AIS track data and port-to-port geodesic distances. The system must generate a data quality score for each vessel's CII calculation, and vessels with quality scores below a configurable threshold must be flagged for manual review before year-end CII submission. All validation exceptions must be logged with timestamps and reviewer actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R011	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Flag State and Classification Society CII Reporting	The system must generate CII reports formatted for submission to major flag state administrations and classification societies relevant to the fleet, including Marshall Islands, Panama, Liberia, and the Indian Register of Shipping. Reports must comply with IMO DCS (Data Collection System) reporting requirements under MARPOL Annex VI, including vessel particulars, annual fuel consumption by type, total distance traveled, hours underway, and the calculated attained CII and rating. The system must support electronic submission via API integration where available (e.g., IHS Maritime, class society portals) and generate printable PDF reports where electronic submission is not supported. Report generation must include a review and approval workflow with digital signatures from the designated person ashore.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R012	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Impact Assessment for Commercial Decisions	The system must provide a CII impact assessment tool that calculates the CII effect of commercial decisions such as accepting a new voyage charter, extending a time charter, or agreeing to a specific speed instruction from a charterer. The tool must show how the proposed commercial arrangement would affect the vessel's projected year-end CII rating, quantifying the change in attained CII and the risk of rating downgrade. This information must be available to commercial teams during voyage estimation and chartering negotiations, integrated into the voyage estimation workflow. Results must be displayed in a clear format showing current projected rating, post-decision projected rating, and any financial implications of potential rating changes in USD and entity-local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R013	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Fleet CII Portfolio Management and Vessel Allocation	The system must support fleet-level CII portfolio management, enabling operations managers to optimize vessel allocation across trade routes to achieve the best overall fleet CII performance. The AI engine must recommend which vessels should be assigned to which routes considering each vessel's current CII status, efficiency characteristics, and the distance and speed profile of each route. For example, vessels approaching D rating limits may be recommended for shorter Arabian Gulf feeder routes (Hamad Port to Jebel Ali) rather than longer India runs (Jebel Ali to JNPT) where higher CII impact would occur. The portfolio optimization must balance CII targets with commercial revenue objectives and contractual obligations across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R014	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance Documentation	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all CII calculations, data inputs, corrections, overrides, and approvals to support regulatory inspections and port state control examinations at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other calling ports. Every data point used in the CII calculation must be traceable to its source (sensor reading, noon report entry, BDN document, AIS record) with timestamps and user identification. The audit trail must be immutable once a year-end CII calculation is finalized and submitted. The system must generate an audit-ready documentation package including data quality reports, correction factor justifications, and calculation methodology documentation. All audit records must be retained for a minimum of seven years as required by MARPOL Annex VI.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R015	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	Automated CII Alert and Notification System	The system must provide an automated alert and notification system that monitors each vessel's CII trajectory and sends proactive notifications when configurable thresholds are breached. Alert triggers must include: vessel projected to drop below C rating within the next 30, 60, or 90 days; vessel's year-to-date CII crossing a rating boundary; monthly CII trend showing deterioration beyond a configurable rate; and fleet average CII approaching a management-defined target. Notifications must be sent via email, SMS, and in-app push notifications to configurable recipient lists based on vessel, entity, and role. Alert messages must be generated in both English and Arabic based on recipient language preference. The system must support alert escalation rules where unacknowledged critical alerts are escalated to senior management after a configurable time period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-002-R016	FEAT-042-1-002	MOD-042	CII Benchmarking Against Industry Peers	The system must provide CII benchmarking capabilities that compare the fleet's CII performance against industry averages and peer group performance data sourced from publicly available IMO GISIS data, classification society databases, or third-party benchmarking services. Benchmarking must be segmented by vessel type, size category (DWT range), and trade route pattern to provide meaningful peer comparisons. The system must show where each vessel and the overall fleet rank in percentile terms relative to the global fleet and regional peers operating in the Middle East and Indian Ocean trades. Benchmarking reports must be generated quarterly and presented in bilingual English/Arabic format suitable for board-level presentations and investor ESG reporting requirements across all operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R001	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Reference Value Calculation per Ship Type	The system must calculate the required EEXI reference value for each vessel based on its ship type, DWT capacity, and the applicable regulatory formula as defined in IMO MEPC.350(78). The system must store the reference line parameters (a, b, c coefficients) for all ship type categories including container ships, bulk carriers, tankers, gas carriers, general cargo ships, LNG carriers, ro-ro cargo ships, and combination carriers. The calculated required EEXI must account for any applicable correction factors for ice-class notation, cargo-specific requirements, and common structural rules. Reference values must be automatically updated when IMO amends the regulatory parameters, and the system must maintain a version history of all reference value changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R002	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Attained EEXI Calculation and Documentation	The system must calculate the attained EEXI for each vessel using the IMO-prescribed methodology, incorporating installed main engine MCR, specific fuel oil consumption at 75% MCR, auxiliary engine power requirements, shaft generator and motor contributions, and any approved innovative energy efficiency technologies. The calculation must follow the EEXI calculation guidelines in MEPC.350(78) including all correction factors for weather (fw), voluntary structural enhancement (fi), and capacity correction (fc). The system must generate a complete EEXI Technical File for each vessel containing all input parameters, calculation steps, and supporting documentation. Technical files must be stored in PDF format with bilingual English/Arabic metadata and be accessible for classification society and flag state review at any time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R003	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Engine Power Limitation (EPL/SHaPoLi) Tracking	The system must track Engine Power Limitation (EPL) and Shaft Power Limitation (SHaPoLi) devices installed on vessels to achieve EEXI compliance, recording the overridable power limit setting, the maximum continuous rating of the engine before limitation, and the locked percentage reduction. For vessels using EPL, the system must log all instances of override activation (permitted only in emergency or adverse conditions), recording the date, time, duration, reason for override, GPS position, weather conditions, and the crew member who authorized the override. Override events occurring near Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT approaches must be cross-referenced with port authority emergency communications. The system must generate monthly EPL compliance reports showing total override duration versus total operating hours for flag state reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R004	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Compliance Status Dashboard and Fleet Overview	The system must provide a comprehensive EEXI compliance status dashboard showing the compliance status of every vessel in the fleet, with clear visual indicators (compliant, non-compliant, pending survey, exempted) for each vessel. The dashboard must display the required EEXI versus attained EEXI for each vessel, the compliance margin or gap, and the method used to achieve compliance (EPL, SHaPoLi, energy-saving device, fuel change, or combination). Fleet-wide statistics must show the percentage of vessels compliant, the average compliance margin, and vessels requiring attention before the next annual survey. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel type, flag state, and classification society, with data exportable to Excel and PDF in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R005	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Energy-Saving Technology Registry and EEXI Credit Tracking	The system must maintain a registry of all energy-saving technologies installed on fleet vessels that contribute to EEXI compliance, including but not limited to air lubrication systems, hull coatings, propeller boss cap fins, Mewis ducts, rotor sails, and waste heat recovery systems. Each technology entry must record the manufacturer, model, installation date, estimated energy savings percentage (as verified by classification society), the EEXI credit value approved by the flag state, maintenance requirements, and actual observed performance. The system must track whether each technology's EEXI credit has been approved by the vessel's classification society and flag state administration, and alert when credits are due for renewal or re-verification. Cost tracking for each technology must support multi-currency recording in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R006	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Survey and Certification Lifecycle Management	The system must manage the EEXI survey and certification lifecycle for each vessel, tracking the initial EEXI survey date, issuing authority (classification society), certificate number, certificate validity period, and next survey due date. The system must generate automated reminders for upcoming EEXI surveys at configurable intervals (default 90, 60, and 30 days before due date) sent to the vessel technical superintendent and fleet manager. Survey preparation checklists must be generated automatically, listing all documents and verifications required for the survey. The system must track survey findings, observations, and conditions of class related to EEXI compliance, with corrective action tracking and deadline monitoring. Survey scheduling must integrate with dry-docking and class survey windows for vessels calling at repair yards in Dubai, Bahrain, and Indian shipyards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R007	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Regulatory Change Monitoring and Impact Assessment	The system must monitor IMO regulatory developments related to EEXI and automatically alert fleet managers when proposed or adopted amendments may affect fleet compliance. The monitoring engine must parse IMO MEPC session outcomes, classification society circulars, and flag state notices to identify relevant changes. When a regulatory change is detected, the system must perform an automated impact assessment showing which vessels in the fleet would be affected, how their compliance status would change, and what actions would be required to maintain compliance. Impact assessments must include estimated costs in USD and entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for remediation measures. The system must maintain a regulatory change log accessible to all entities with entries in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R008	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Compliance Cost Analysis and Budgeting	The system must provide a comprehensive cost analysis module for EEXI compliance, tracking all costs associated with achieving and maintaining compliance for each vessel including EPL installation costs, energy-saving technology procurement and installation, naval architecture consultancy fees, classification society survey fees, and any operational penalties such as reduced speed capability. Costs must be recorded in the transaction currency and converted to each entity's reporting currency using ERP exchange rates. The system must generate EEXI compliance budgets and forecasts for fleet management, with variance tracking against approved budgets. Cost-benefit analysis tools must compare different compliance approaches (EPL vs. energy-saving devices vs. fuel switching) for vessels not yet compliant, presenting results in present value terms with configurable discount rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R009	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Speed Capability Impact Assessment of EEXI Measures	The system must calculate and display the impact of EEXI compliance measures on each vessel's maximum achievable speed and cargo-carrying capability. For vessels using EPL or SHaPoLi, the system must show the maximum speed achievable under the power limitation at various loading conditions (full laden, partial, ballast) and compare this against the speeds required to maintain commercial schedule adherence on key routes such as Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Khalifa Port, and Gulf-to-India services. The system must identify vessels where EEXI power limitations create a commercial risk of missing port windows or ETAs, especially during monsoon seasons in the Arabian Sea when additional power reserves may be needed for safe navigation. Alerts must be generated when a voyage plan requires speeds exceeding EPL-limited capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R010	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Documentation and Technical File Repository	The system must maintain a centralized digital repository for all EEXI-related documentation for each vessel, including EEXI Technical Files, IEE (International Energy Efficiency) Certificates, SEEMP (Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan) Part III documents, EPL/SHaPoLi verification certificates, energy-saving technology approval certificates, and all supporting calculation sheets. Documents must be version-controlled with full change history, and the system must prevent unauthorized modifications to finalized documents. The repository must support document tagging, full-text search, and categorization by vessel, document type, and regulatory reference. Access controls must enforce entity-level document segregation while allowing group-level administrators to view documents across all entities. Documents must be downloadable individually or as a compiled vessel compliance package for port state control inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R011	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI and CII Integration for Holistic Efficiency Management	The system must integrate EEXI technical efficiency data with CII operational efficiency tracking to provide a holistic view of each vessel's energy efficiency status. The integrated view must show how EEXI compliance measures (particularly EPL) affect the vessel's CII performance, since power-limited vessels operating at reduced speeds will generally have better CII ratings. The system must model the interplay between EEXI power limitations and CII targets, showing fleet managers the combined regulatory compliance position. When evaluating fleet decisions such as vessel acquisitions, disposals, or retrofits, the system must present the combined EEXI and CII implications. Reports must present technical (EEXI) and operational (CII) efficiency metrics side by side in bilingual format for regulatory submissions to authorities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R010	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Multi-Entity CP Performance Consolidation	The system must consolidate charter party performance data across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) into unified dashboards and reports. Consolidation must handle different CP currencies, converting all figures to a common reporting currency (USD) while preserving original currency amounts. Entity-specific regulatory requirements must be respected including KSA ZATCA compliance for financial reporting and India GST implications on demurrage charges. Access controls must enforce entity-level data segregation while permitting group-level consolidated views for authorized executive users. The consolidated view must support Arabic RTL layout for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R012	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Dual-Fuel and Alternative Fuel EEXI Calculation Support	The system must support EEXI calculations for vessels fitted with dual-fuel engines capable of operating on LNG, methanol, ethane, or other alternative fuels, applying the appropriate specific fuel oil consumption values and carbon conversion factors for each fuel mode. The calculation must handle the pilot fuel consumption required for gas mode operation and the different SFC curves for gas and diesel modes. For vessels with multiple fuel configurations, the system must calculate the EEXI for each operating mode and determine the weighted attained EEXI based on the expected fuel mix. The system must track regulatory developments regarding EEXI treatment of biofuels, synthetic fuels, and carbon capture technologies, updating calculation methodologies as new IMO guidelines are adopted. All alternative fuel parameters must be auditable and traceable to manufacturer specifications and class approvals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R013	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Compliance Verification for Vessel Acquisition and Sale	The system must provide EEXI compliance verification tools for use during vessel acquisition and sale processes, enabling commercial teams to assess the EEXI status of potential acquisitions and ensure proper compliance transfer during vessel sales. For acquisitions, the tool must calculate the required and estimated attained EEXI based on available vessel specifications, identify compliance gaps, and estimate remediation costs in USD with entity-currency equivalents. For sales, the system must generate a compliance documentation package including all EEXI technical files, survey records, and compliance history. The tool must verify that all EEXI-related certificates and documentation are valid and transferable to the new owner, flagging any items requiring renewal or update prior to delivery at handover ports including those in Dubai, Qatar, or India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R014	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Exemption and Waiver Management	The system must manage EEXI exemptions and waivers for vessels that qualify under IMO regulations, including vessels expected to be scrapped within a defined period, vessels operating exclusively in specific trades that may qualify for exemptions, and vessels undergoing major conversion. The system must track exemption application status, validity period, conditions attached to the exemption, and renewal requirements. When an exemption is approaching expiry, the system must generate automated alerts at configurable intervals (default 180, 90, 30 days before expiry) notifying the technical superintendent and compliance officer. The system must maintain records of all exemption applications (approved and rejected) for audit purposes, with document storage supporting bilingual English/Arabic content and Hijri date references where required by GCC maritime authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R015	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Port State Control EEXI Inspection Readiness Module	The system must provide a port state control (PSC) inspection readiness module that prepares vessel-specific EEXI compliance documentation packages for upcoming port calls at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and other ports in the fleet's trading area. The module must automatically compile the required documents including the IEE Certificate, EEXI Technical File, SEEMP Part III, EPL verification records, and engine logbooks showing no unauthorized EPL overrides. A pre-arrival compliance checklist must be generated for each port call, highlighting any items that may attract PSC attention such as recent EPL overrides, expired certificates, or outstanding conditions of class. The system must track PSC inspection outcomes related to EEXI, recording any deficiencies, detentions, or observations, and manage corrective action plans until deficiencies are rectified and verified by the classification society.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R016	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	EEXI Reporting to Classification Societies and Flag States	The system must generate standardized EEXI compliance reports for submission to the vessel's classification society and flag state administration, formatted according to each organization's specific requirements. The system must support electronic submission via API integration with major classification societies including Lloyd's Register, DNV, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, and Indian Register of Shipping. Reports must include vessel identification (IMO number, name, flag), EEXI calculation summary, compliance method details, survey history, and any correction factors applied. The system must track submission status (draft, submitted, under review, approved, rejected) and manage any queries or revision requests from the classification society or flag state. A submission calendar must track reporting deadlines for all vessels across all entities, with automated reminders and escalation for overdue submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-003-R017	FEAT-042-1-003	MOD-042	Offline EEXI Technical File Access for Vessel Crew	The system must provide offline access to EEXI Technical Files and compliance documentation for vessel crew when the vessel is at sea without reliable internet connectivity, which is common during Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean crossings. The offline module must synchronize the latest EEXI documentation package to a shipboard device or server before departure from port, including the EEXI Technical File, IEE Certificate, EPL verification records, SEEMP, and any applicable flag state or class correspondence. Updates made to documentation while offline (such as EPL override event logging) must be queued for synchronization upon connectivity restoration. The synchronization process must handle conflict resolution when shore-side updates have occurred during the offline period, applying last-write-wins for operational logs and requiring manual review for document revisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R001	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Structured Noon Report Digital Submission Form	The system must provide a structured digital noon report submission form accessible via web browser and mobile application for vessel crew to submit daily noon reports. The form must capture all standard noon report fields including vessel position (latitude/longitude), date and time (UTC and local), course and speed (SOG and STW), distance run since last report, slip percentage, weather conditions (wind force and direction, sea state, swell, visibility, air and sea temperature, barometric pressure), fuel consumption by type and consumer (main engine, auxiliary engines, boilers), fuel ROB (remaining on board) by type and tank, engine running hours, average RPM, cargo quantities on board in TEU and metric tonnes, and free-form remarks. The form must support both English and Arabic input with automatic transliteration capabilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R002	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Automated Line-Item Matching Against Internal Records	The system must automatically match each line item on a lessor statement to the corresponding internal lease contract record, on-hire certificate, and off-hire survey document. Matching must be performed using container number (ISO 6346 format with check digit validation), date range overlap, and rate agreement reference. The matching engine must handle discrepancies in date formats between Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and must support multi-entity matching where containers may have been sub-leased between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. Unmatched items must be queued for manual review with suggested matches ranked by confidence score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R002	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	AI-Powered Noon Report Data Validation Engine	The system must implement an AI-powered validation engine that checks each submitted noon report for data quality, consistency, and plausibility before acceptance. Validation rules must include: position consistency with previous report and expected vessel track based on reported speed and course; fuel consumption consistency with reported speed and the vessel's speed-consumption curve; distance run consistency with reported speed and time elapsed; ROB consistency with previous ROB minus reported consumption plus any bunkering; weather data consistency with meteorological service data for the reported position; and engine parameters within physically plausible ranges. Each validation failure must generate a specific error or warning message in the submitter's language (English or Arabic), with critical errors requiring correction before submission and warnings requiring acknowledgment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R003	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Submission via Multiple Communication Channels	The system must support noon report submission via multiple communication channels to accommodate varying connectivity conditions at sea, including direct web form submission over satellite internet, structured email submission (with defined subject line format and body template parsed by the system), API submission from shipboard software systems, and manual shore-side entry by operations staff based on radio or phone communication. Email-based submissions must be parsed by an AI-powered email processor that extracts noon report data from both structured templates and semi-structured free-text emails. The system must handle transmission delays of up to 24 hours for email submissions and properly sequence reports based on the report date-time rather than receipt time. Each submission channel must be tracked for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R004	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Type Classification and Handling	The system must support multiple noon report types with appropriate data requirements and processing rules for each: Sea Noon Report (daily at-sea report at local noon), Departure Report (submitted upon departure from port), Arrival Report (submitted upon arrival at port or anchorage), Port Noon Report (daily report while in port), Bunkering Report (submitted after completion of bunkering operations), Event Report (submitted for significant events such as weather deviation, main engine breakdown, or cargo operations). Each report type must have a configurable field template defining required and optional fields specific to that report type. The system must enforce sequential reporting (e.g., a Departure Report must follow an Arrival or Port report) and flag gaps in the reporting chain. Specific port fields must auto-populate for known ports including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R005	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Automatic Performance Metric Extraction from Noon Reports	The system must automatically extract and calculate key performance metrics from noon report data, including daily fuel consumption rates by fuel type and consumer, average speed over ground and through water, distance efficiency (actual distance versus great circle distance), slip percentage, specific fuel oil consumption per nautical mile, EEOI (Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator) for the reporting period, and weather performance loss estimates. Extracted metrics must be stored in a time-series database for trend analysis and must be tagged with the vessel identifier, voyage number, report type, and sea/port phase indicator. The extraction engine must handle unit conversions (e.g., long tons to metric tonnes, nautical miles to kilometers) based on the vessel's configured reporting units and the system's standard units.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R006	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Amendment and Correction Workflow	The system must provide a controlled amendment and correction workflow for submitted noon reports, allowing authorized users (vessel master, chief engineer, shore-side operations manager) to modify previously submitted reports with full audit trail. Each amendment must record the original value, new value, amendment reason, amending user, and timestamp. Amendments to fuel consumption or distance data must trigger recalculation of all derived metrics including voyage summaries, CII calculations, and charter party performance computations. The system must support a shore-side review process where operations staff can flag noon reports for correction by the vessel, with a communication mechanism to request clarification from the crew. Amendment rights must be time-limited (configurable, default 30 days from submission) after which reports become locked and require senior management approval to modify.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R007	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Voyage-Level Noon Report Aggregation and Analysis	The system must aggregate all noon reports for a given voyage into a comprehensive voyage analysis, computing total fuel consumed by type, total distance sailed, average and maximum speeds, total weather delay hours, cargo efficiency metrics, and overall voyage EEOI. The aggregation must handle the complete voyage cycle from departure to arrival including any intermediate port calls, deviations, and waiting time at anchorage. Voyage analysis must compare actual performance against the voyage plan/estimate, highlighting variances in fuel consumption, speed, and time. The analysis must be available as an interactive web dashboard and downloadable PDF report with bilingual English/Arabic content and entity-specific branding (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India). Historical voyage analyses must be searchable and comparable across similar routes and vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R008	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	AI-Driven Noon Report Anomaly Detection and Alerting	The system must apply AI-driven anomaly detection algorithms to incoming noon report data to identify unusual patterns that may indicate data quality issues, vessel performance problems, or operational concerns. Anomalies to detect include: sudden changes in fuel consumption not explained by speed or weather changes, position reports inconsistent with AIS data, unreported bunkering events detected from ROB changes, engine parameters outside normal operating ranges, and weather reports significantly different from meteorological service data for the vessel's position. Each detected anomaly must be classified by type and severity, logged in the vessel's performance event history, and routed as an alert to the appropriate shore-side team member. The anomaly detection model must be continuously improved using feedback from operators who confirm or dismiss flagged anomalies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R011	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	AI-Powered Performance Anomaly Detection	The system must employ machine learning models to detect anomalous performance patterns that deviate from expected vessel behavior based on vessel class, age, trade route, and environmental conditions. Anomaly detection must cover speed anomalies, consumption spikes, unusual port time patterns, and route deviations. The AI model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical noon report data per vessel. Detected anomalies must be classified by severity (informational, warning, critical) and trigger appropriate notifications to operations staff. The system must provide explainable AI outputs showing the factors contributing to each anomaly detection in both English and Arabic languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R009	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Data Integration with CII and EEXI Modules	The system must seamlessly integrate noon report data with the CII Carbon Intensity Rating Calculation (FEAT-042-1-002) and EEXI Energy Efficiency Compliance (FEAT-042-1-003) features, providing validated fuel consumption and distance data as primary inputs for regulatory calculations. The integration must ensure that any amendments to noon report data automatically trigger recalculation of affected CII values and update the vessel's year-to-date CII tracking. Data quality flags from the noon report validation engine must propagate to the CII module so that CII calculations can reflect the confidence level of underlying data. The integration must handle the mapping between noon report fuel type nomenclature and IMO-defined fuel categories for carbon conversion factor application, with configurable mapping tables maintained by the system administrator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R010	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Historical Noon Report Archive and Search Capability	The system must maintain a searchable archive of all noon reports for a minimum retention period of seven years, complying with MARPOL Annex VI data retention requirements and entity-specific record-keeping regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The archive must support advanced search and filtering by vessel, voyage number, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), report type, geographic area, weather conditions, and specific data field ranges (e.g., all reports where fuel consumption exceeded a specified threshold). Search results must be exportable to Excel and CSV formats for external analysis. The archive must support bulk data extraction via API for integration with external analytics and business intelligence tools. Archive storage must be optimized for cost efficiency while maintaining rapid retrieval for recent data (within the last two years) and reasonable retrieval times for older data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R011	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Compliance Monitoring for Reporting Obligations	The system must monitor noon report submission compliance across the fleet, tracking which vessels have submitted their daily reports, which are overdue, and which have outstanding validation errors or correction requests. A compliance dashboard must show real-time submission status for all vessels, with color-coded indicators for on-time, late (within grace period), and overdue reports. The system must send automated reminders to vessels that have not submitted their noon report by the configurable deadline (default 14:00 UTC daily for sea reports). Escalation rules must notify the vessel superintendent when a report is more than six hours overdue and the fleet manager when a report is more than 12 hours overdue. Monthly compliance statistics must be generated per vessel, per entity, and fleet-wide, with trend analysis showing improvement or deterioration in reporting discipline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R012	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Weather Routing Integration with Noon Report Analysis	The system must integrate noon report weather data with weather routing services to provide post-voyage weather analysis and routing performance assessment. The integration must compare the actual weather encountered (as reported in noon reports) against the forecast weather provided by the weather routing service at the voyage planning stage. Deviation analysis must quantify the accuracy of weather forecasts for routes commonly sailed between Middle East and Indian ports, building a historical accuracy database for each weather routing provider. The system must calculate weather-related fuel overconsumption and time delays for each voyage, attributing losses to forecast inaccuracies versus unavoidable conditions. This analysis must feed into AI models that improve future voyage planning and weather routing selections for the Arabian Sea, Persian Gulf, and Gulf of Oman regions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R013	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Bunker ROB Reconciliation via Noon Reports	The system must reconcile fuel Remaining On Board (ROB) quantities reported in noon reports against bunker delivery note (BDN) quantities, consumption figures, and tank sounding records to detect discrepancies that may indicate measurement errors, fuel theft, or quality issues. The reconciliation engine must compute expected ROB based on the last confirmed ROB plus any bunkering deliveries minus all reported consumption, comparing this against the reported ROB in each noon report. Discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds (default 2% of total ROB) must be flagged for investigation with alerts sent to the vessel operations team and bunker procurement department. The system must track ROB reconciliation accuracy trends per vessel and flag vessels with consistently poor reconciliation for potential flow meter calibration or tank measurement issues. All ROB figures must be maintained in metric tonnes with conversion support for barrels and cubic meters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R014	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Data Export for Charter Party Reporting	The system must generate charter party performance reports from noon report data, formatted according to common charter party reporting requirements. The export must support standard charter party formats including Poseidon Principles reporting format, BIMCO performance clause formats, and custom formats configurable per charter party agreement. Reports must include speed and consumption summaries, good weather day calculations (excluding days above specified Beaufort and Douglas sea state thresholds), and fuel consumption warranty compliance data. The system must identify and segregate weather-excluded periods, off-hire periods, and periods of port stay from performance calculations. Exported reports must include supporting data tables with individual noon report entries for the reporting period. All monetary calculations must support multi-currency presentation in USD and the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R015	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Dashboard with Geospatial Visualization	The system must provide an interactive geospatial dashboard that displays noon report data on a map visualization, showing vessel positions, tracks, and performance metrics plotted along the voyage route. The map must display color-coded indicators for fuel efficiency, speed compliance, weather severity, and any anomalies detected at each noon report position. Users must be able to click on individual noon report points to view the full report details in a popup. The map must support filtering by vessel, date range, entity, and performance metric. Track visualization must distinguish between laden and ballast legs, sea passages and port stays, and good weather and adverse weather segments. The geospatial dashboard must display commonly sailed routes in the Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Arabian Sea, and approaches to Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT with appropriate nautical chart underlays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R016	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Automated Noon Report Summary for Management Reporting	The system must generate automated daily, weekly, and monthly noon report summary reports for fleet management, consolidating key metrics across all vessels and entities. Daily summaries must highlight vessels with performance concerns, overdue reports, and significant events reported in the last 24 hours. Weekly summaries must include fleet fuel consumption trends, average speeds, CII trajectory updates, and notable weather impacts. Monthly summaries must provide comprehensive fleet performance analysis including vessel rankings, route efficiency comparisons, fuel cost summaries in USD and entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and regulatory compliance status updates. All summary reports must be generated in configurable bilingual formats (English and Arabic) and distributed automatically via email to configurable recipient lists based on entity and management level. Reports must include AI-generated narrative commentary explaining key trends and recommending actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R017	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Template Configuration per Vessel Type	The system must support configurable noon report templates that can be customized per vessel type, vessel, or trade route to capture relevant data fields while avoiding unnecessary data entry burden on crew. Container vessel templates must include container count fields (TEU on board, reefer containers connected, dangerous goods containers), while tanker templates must include cargo heating and tank washing fields. Templates for vessels on specific routes (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT feeder service) may include route-specific fields such as canal transit data or port-specific anchorage information. Template configuration must be accessible to authorized shore-side administrators only, with version control and effective dates to ensure vessels receive the correct template version. The template engine must support conditional fields that appear or are required based on the selected report type or previous field values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-1-004-R018	FEAT-042-1-004	MOD-042	Noon Report Offline Submission and Queuing System	The system must provide a robust offline noon report submission capability for vessels with intermittent or no internet connectivity, which is common during Indian Ocean crossings and certain Arabian Sea passages. The offline system must allow crew to complete and locally save noon reports on a shipboard device, with all validation rules executing locally to provide immediate feedback even without server connectivity. Completed reports must be queued for automatic transmission when connectivity is restored, with the queue supporting a minimum of 45 days of accumulated reports. The offline system must synchronize reference data (vessel speed-consumption curves, validation parameters, port databases) from the central server whenever connectivity is available. Conflict resolution must handle scenarios where shore-side staff have entered provisional data that conflicts with the vessel's actual submission, prioritizing the vessel's submission while preserving the shore-side entry for audit review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R001	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Charter Party Terms Ingestion and Parsing	The system must ingest and parse charter party agreements in both structured (JSON/XML) and unstructured (PDF/scanned document) formats using AI-powered OCR and NLP extraction. It must capture guaranteed speed in knots, fuel consumption guarantees in metric tons per day for laden and ballast conditions, weather exclusion clauses (Beaufort scale thresholds), off-hire trigger conditions, and deviation allowance parameters. The parser must handle English and Arabic language CP documents and store all extracted terms with confidence scores. When confidence falls below 85%, the system must flag the clause for manual review by the operations team with Arabic RTL support in the review interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R002	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Actual vs Guaranteed Speed Comparison	The system must continuously compare actual vessel speed-over-ground and speed-through-water against charter party guaranteed speed for each voyage leg. Calculations must account for current, wind, and sea-state corrections per the CP weather exclusion clause. The system must compute good-weather speed using Beaufort Force thresholds defined in the CP (typically BF 4-5). Deviations exceeding 0.5 knots below the CP guarantee must be flagged with severity levels (minor 0.5-1.0 knots, moderate 1.0-1.5 knots, major >1.5 knots). Results must be presented in both English and Arabic with Hijri and Gregorian date stamps for each measurement period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R003	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Fuel Consumption Variance Tracking	The system must track actual daily fuel consumption by fuel type (VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG) against charter party warranted consumption figures for each vessel operating across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India trade lanes. Variance calculations must separate laden, ballast, and port idle conditions. The system must apply slip corrections and displacement adjustments per CP terms. Overconsumption exceeding 5% of warranted figures must generate automated alerts to chartering managers. Fuel cost variance must be calculated in the vessel's CP currency (typically USD) and converted to entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using daily exchange rates from the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R004	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Off-Hire Event Detection and Quantification	The system must automatically detect off-hire events based on CP-defined triggers including mechanical breakdown, speed reduction below minimum threshold, deviation from agreed route, bunkering delays beyond allowance, and port authority detentions. Each off-hire event must be timestamped with start and end times in UTC and local port time, with duration calculated to the nearest minute. Financial impact must be computed using the daily hire rate from the CP, with amounts calculated in the CP currency and converted to entity-local currencies (QAR/AED/SAR/INR). The system must generate off-hire notices in both English and Arabic formats compliant with BIMCO standard off-hire clause templates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R005	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Weather Factor Exclusion Engine	The system must implement a configurable weather exclusion engine that filters voyage performance data per CP weather clauses. It must support multiple exclusion methodologies including Beaufort scale thresholds, Douglas sea state, combined wind and wave criteria, and adverse current thresholds. The engine must integrate with real-time meteorological data feeds and hindcast weather databases for post-voyage analysis. For voyages through the Arabian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, and Arabian Sea, the system must account for seasonal Shamal wind patterns and monsoon conditions. Excluded weather periods must be clearly annotated in performance reports with supporting meteorological evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R012	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Noon Report Data Validation and Integration	The system must ingest vessel noon reports from multiple sources including manual entry, email parsing, and direct API feeds from vessel reporting systems. Each noon report must be validated against plausibility rules including maximum speed thresholds, fuel consumption range checks per vessel class, position consistency with AIS data, and weather data cross-referencing. Invalid or suspicious reports must be quarantined for review with discrepancies highlighted. The system must support offline data entry for vessels in areas with limited connectivity (common in Indian Ocean passages) with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored. All data entry interfaces must support Arabic input and RTL text direction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R013	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Charter Party Clause Library Management	The system must maintain a searchable library of standard and custom charter party clauses with version control and audit trails. The library must include BIMCO standard clauses, ASBA forms, and company-specific amendments used across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. Each clause must be tagged with metadata including clause type, applicable vessel types, trade route restrictions, and regulatory jurisdiction. The system must support clause comparison tools to highlight differences between CP versions. Clause text must be stored and searchable in both English and Arabic. AI must suggest relevant clauses based on vessel type, trade route, and historical CP patterns for the specific entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R014	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Real-Time Voyage Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying current voyage performance against CP terms for all active voyages across the fleet. The dashboard must show vessel positions on an interactive map with color-coded status indicators for speed compliance (green/amber/red), consumption compliance, and schedule adherence. Key metrics must include current speed vs CP speed, cumulative fuel consumption vs CP allowance, estimated time of arrival vs CP laycan window, and projected voyage cost vs budget. The dashboard must auto-refresh at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes) and support both English and Arabic display with Hijri date overlays. Offline caching must allow dashboard viewing for up to 4 hours without connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R015	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Voyage Performance Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive voyage performance reports in PDF and Excel formats comparing actual performance against CP terms. Reports must include executive summary, speed and consumption analysis, weather factor assessment, port time analysis, financial impact summary, and claim recommendations. Each report section must include supporting data tables and charts. Reports must be generated in English, Arabic, or bilingual formats based on user preference. Financial figures must be presented in the CP currency with parallel columns showing entity-local currency equivalents (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Reports must comply with the entity's corporate branding guidelines and support digital distribution via email with configurable recipient lists per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-001-R016	FEAT-042-2-001	MOD-042	Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all charter party performance data, calculations, and claim activities. Every data modification, calculation parameter change, and claim status update must be logged with timestamp, user identity, entity context, and before/after values. Audit logs must be immutable and retained for a minimum of 7 years in compliance with maritime arbitration limitation periods and local regulatory requirements (QFCRA for Qatar, DFSA for UAE, CMA for KSA, and DG Shipping for India). Audit reports must be exportable in formats acceptable for maritime arbitration proceedings and must include chain-of-custody documentation for evidentiary purposes. All audit interfaces must support Arabic RTL display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R001	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Multi-Source Weather Data Integration	The system must integrate real-time and forecast weather data from multiple authoritative sources including ECMWF, GFS/NCEP, UK Met Office, and regional meteorological services (Qatar Met Department, India Meteorological Department, NCM UAE). Data ingestion must cover wind speed and direction, wave height and period, swell data, ocean currents, visibility, and tropical cyclone advisories. The system must maintain at least 14-day forecast horizons with 6-hourly resolution and 0.25-degree spatial resolution minimum. Data must be cached locally for offline access during vessel connectivity outages common in Indian Ocean passages, with automatic refresh when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R002	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	AI Route Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI-powered route optimization engine that calculates the optimal sailing route considering weather forecasts, vessel-specific performance characteristics, fuel consumption models, and schedule constraints. The engine must evaluate millions of potential waypoint combinations using dynamic programming and machine learning techniques. Optimization objectives must be configurable: minimum fuel consumption, minimum voyage time, maximum safety margin, or weighted multi-objective. The engine must account for restricted areas, traffic separation schemes, ECA zones, and piracy high-risk areas (Gulf of Aden, Somali Basin). Route calculations for Hamad Port to JNPT must complete within 60 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R003	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Vessel-Specific Performance Modeling	The system must maintain vessel-specific hydrodynamic performance models for each vessel in the fleet. Models must capture speed-power curves at various drafts and trim conditions, fuel consumption rates by engine load, hull fouling degradation factors, and propeller performance characteristics. Models must be continuously calibrated using actual noon report data and sensor feeds. The system must account for vessel aging effects and post-drydocking performance improvements. For each vessel, the model must predict fuel consumption within 3% accuracy for given speed, draft, trim, and environmental conditions. Model parameters must be version-controlled with full audit trail accessible to technical superintendents across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R004	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Dynamic Route Re-Optimization During Voyage	The system must continuously monitor weather conditions along the planned route and trigger automatic re-optimization when forecast changes exceed configurable thresholds. Re-optimization triggers must include wind speed forecast changes exceeding 10 knots, wave height changes exceeding 1.5 meters, tropical cyclone track updates, and port schedule changes at destination (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT). The system must present the master with up to three alternative route options ranked by optimization objective, with clear comparison of estimated arrival time, fuel consumption, and safety metrics. Route change recommendations must be communicated in both English and Arabic with graphical overlays on electronic chart displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R005	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Seasonal Weather Pattern Intelligence	The system must incorporate historical seasonal weather pattern analysis for key trade routes including Arabian Gulf (Shamal wind season December-March and June-August), Arabian Sea (Southwest monsoon June-September, Northeast monsoon November-February), Red Sea (northerly winds year-round with seasonal variations), and Bay of Bengal (cyclone season April-June and October-December). The AI engine must use at least 30 years of historical meteorological data to identify recurring patterns and anomalies. Seasonal routing advisories must be generated automatically for voyage planning teams, highlighting periods of elevated weather risk and recommended route alternatives. Advisories must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R006	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Fuel Savings Calculation and Reporting	The system must calculate and report fuel savings achieved through weather routing optimization compared to the great circle route and standard waypoint route for each voyage. Savings must be expressed in metric tons of fuel by type (VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG), monetary value in the vessel's operating currency and converted to entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and CO2 equivalent emissions avoided. The system must maintain cumulative savings records per vessel, per route, per entity, and fleet-wide. Monthly and quarterly savings reports must be generated automatically with trend analysis and AI-generated performance narratives. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R007	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Safety Constraint Configuration	The system must enforce configurable safety constraints during route optimization including maximum acceptable significant wave height (default 4.0m for container vessels), maximum beam sea angle duration, minimum under-keel clearance for shallow water passages, wind speed limits for container stack safety based on container weight distribution, and parametric rolling avoidance criteria. Safety thresholds must be configurable per vessel class and per operating entity. The system must never recommend a route that violates any active safety constraint. When all routes within the safety envelope result in significant delays (>12 hours), the system must alert the operations manager and suggest shelter anchorage options with estimated waiting times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R008	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	ECA Zone and Emissions Compliance Routing	The system must incorporate Emission Control Area boundaries into route optimization, calculating the optimal balance between route distance through ECAs and fuel cost differentials between compliant fuels (LSMGO) and standard fuels (VLSFO). The system must support current and planned ECA zones including the Mediterranean Sea ECA (effective 2025) relevant to Suez Canal transits. Fuel switching points must be calculated to ensure compliance before entering ECA boundaries with adequate safety margins. The cost optimization must account for multi-currency bunker pricing at different ports (Fujairah in AED, JNPT in INR, Hamad Port in QAR) and include scrubber-equipped vessel considerations where the vessel may continue using HSFO within ECAs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R009	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Piracy and Security Risk Zone Avoidance	The system must integrate maritime security risk data from UKMTO, MSCHOA, IMB Piracy Reporting Centre, and regional naval force advisories for route optimization. High-risk areas including the Gulf of Aden, Somali Basin, and Strait of Hormuz must be mapped with dynamic risk levels updated at least every 6 hours. Route optimization must offer options to avoid high-risk zones entirely, transit through recommended corridors (IRTC for Gulf of Aden), or minimize exposure time within risk zones. The system must calculate estimated cost impact of security routing diversions including additional fuel, time, and insurance premium adjustments. Security zone configurations must be manageable by each entity's security officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R010	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Integration with Voyage Planning Systems	The system must integrate with existing voyage planning and ECDIS systems through standard interfaces including S-421 route exchange format, RTZ route format, and proprietary API connections to major navigation systems (Transas, NAVTOR, StormGeo). Optimized routes must be exportable in formats compatible with onboard navigation systems for direct upload. The integration must support two-way communication: sending optimized routes to vessels and receiving actual route execution data for post-voyage analysis. API endpoints must be secured with OAuth 2.0 and support entity-specific authentication tokens. Integration logs must be maintained for audit purposes with all timestamps in UTC and local port time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R011	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Weather Routing Performance Analytics	The system must provide analytics comparing weather-routed voyages against non-weather-routed voyages for the same vessel and trade route combinations. Analytics must include average fuel savings percentage, average voyage time difference, safety incident reduction rates, and CP compliance improvement metrics. The AI engine must identify routes and vessel types that benefit most from weather routing optimization. Performance analytics must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel class, trade route, and seasonal period. Dashboards must present analytics in both English and Arabic with interactive drill-down capabilities. Historical trend data must span at least 36 months with Hijri calendar overlay support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R012	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Master Override and Feedback Mechanism	The system must provide a mechanism for vessel masters to override AI-recommended routes with documented justification. Override reasons must be selectable from a predefined list (safety concern, cargo sensitivity, local knowledge, port authority instruction) with free-text elaboration fields supporting English and Arabic input. All overrides must be logged with timestamp, master identification, original recommendation, chosen alternative, and stated reason. The system must analyze override patterns using AI to improve future recommendations and identify knowledge gaps in the optimization model. Override analytics must be reviewed monthly by fleet performance managers with entity-level aggregation reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R013	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Canal Transit Optimization	The system must optimize routing through major canal transit points including the Suez Canal (critical for Gulf-Mediterranean routes) and potentially the Strait of Hormuz chokepoint. For Suez Canal transits, the system must consider convoy schedules, canal authority booking windows, and transit fee calculations based on vessel SCNT. The optimization must factor in waiting time at canal anchorages versus speed adjustments to arrive at optimal convoy joining times. Transit fees must be calculated in SDR and converted to USD and entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must also evaluate Cape of Good Hope alternatives when Suez delays or security risks make the longer route economically viable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R014	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Fleet-Wide Route Coordination	The system must coordinate weather routing across multiple vessels in the fleet to optimize overall fleet efficiency. When multiple vessels are scheduled for the same port within a narrow window (e.g., three vessels arriving at Jebel Ali within 48 hours), the system must coordinate speed and routing to stagger arrivals and minimize port congestion and waiting time. Fleet coordination must account for berth availability data from port systems, tidal windows for draft-restricted vessels, and priority cargo discharge requirements. Coordination recommendations must be presented to fleet operations managers with impact analysis showing individual vessel and fleet-level fuel and time implications in multi-currency format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R015	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Offline Weather Routing Capability	The system must provide degraded-mode weather routing capability when vessels lose internet connectivity. The vessel-side module must cache the most recent 7-day weather forecast data and enable local route optimization using onboard computing resources. Cached data must include weather grids for the vessel's current ocean region with sufficient spatial and temporal resolution for meaningful route optimization. When connectivity is restored, the system must automatically synchronize locally computed routes with the shore-side system and trigger re-optimization using updated weather data. The offline mode must clearly indicate data freshness timestamps on all displays and warn users when cached forecast data exceeds 24 hours of age.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R016	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Weather Routing Accuracy Verification	The system must implement a post-voyage verification process that compares weather routing predictions against actual conditions encountered and actual vessel performance achieved. Verification metrics must include predicted vs actual wind and wave conditions along the route, predicted vs actual fuel consumption, predicted vs actual voyage duration, and predicted vs actual ETA accuracy. The AI model must use verification results as training feedback to improve future predictions. Accuracy reports must be generated for each completed voyage and aggregated monthly per vessel, per route, and per entity. Verification results below 80% accuracy must trigger model review alerts to the data science team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-002-R017	FEAT-042-2-002	MOD-042	Tropical Cyclone Evasion Planning	The system must provide specialized tropical cyclone evasion planning for vessels operating in cyclone-prone areas of the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal. When a tropical cyclone is detected within 1000 nautical miles of a vessel's planned route, the system must activate enhanced monitoring mode with route re-optimization every 6 hours. The system must implement the 1-2-3 rule (or Mariner's Rule) for cyclone avoidance, calculating the danger semicircle and recommended avoidance tracks. Evasion routes must prioritize vessel safety above all other optimization objectives. Cyclone advisory communications to vessel masters must be issued in both English and Arabic with clear graphical depictions of the cyclone track forecast and recommended evasion maneuver.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R001	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	IMO DCS Data Collection and Reporting	The system must collect and aggregate fuel consumption data for IMO Data Collection System (DCS) compliance as required under MARPOL Annex VI. Data collection must cover all fuel types consumed (HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG, methanol, ammonia) with quantities in metric tons and corresponding CO2 emission factors per IMO guidelines (MEPC.364(79)). The system must generate annual fuel consumption reports per vessel in the IMO-prescribed format for submission to the vessel's flag state administration. Data must be aggregated by voyage, calendar month, and calendar year. Reports must include distance traveled, hours underway, and transport work (deadweight tonnage × distance). Multi-entity consolidation must support fleet-wide reporting across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India registered vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R002	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) Calculation Engine	The system must calculate the annual operational Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) for each vessel in accordance with IMO resolution MEPC.352(78). CII must be computed as the ratio of total CO2 emissions to transport work (Annual Efficiency Ratio - AER) expressed in grams of CO2 per deadweight-ton nautical mile. The system must apply the appropriate reference line values and reduction factors for the current year per vessel type and size category. CII ratings (A through E) must be assigned based on the boundary values specified in MEPC.354(78). The system must project year-end CII ratings at monthly intervals to enable proactive operational adjustments. Vessels projected to receive D or E ratings must trigger automated improvement plan workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R003	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	CII Improvement Plan Management	The system must provide a structured workflow for creating, tracking, and reporting CII improvement plans for vessels rated D for three consecutive years or rated E in any single year as required by MEPC.355(78). Improvement plans must include specific corrective actions such as speed reduction targets, hull cleaning schedules, route optimization mandates, and energy efficiency technology installations. Each action must have assigned responsibility, target completion date (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), expected CII impact quantification, and estimated cost in the vessel's operating currency with entity-local currency equivalents. Plans must be reviewable and approvable by designated classification society surveyors and submitted to flag state administrations through the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R004	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	EEXI Compliance Monitoring	The system must track Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) compliance status for all vessels in the managed fleet per IMO resolution MEPC.350(78). The system must store verified EEXI values from classification society certificates, required EEXI threshold values per vessel type and size, and any applied correction factors (ice class, shuttle tanker, etc.). For vessels requiring engine power limitation (EPL) or shaft power limitation (SHaPoLi) to achieve EEXI compliance, the system must monitor that power limitations are being observed during operations. Non-compliance events where actual engine power exceeds the limited value must be flagged immediately. EEXI documentation must be maintained per vessel with certificate expiry tracking and renewal reminders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R005	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	EU ETS Maritime Emissions Tracking	The system must track CO2 emissions for voyages falling under the EU Emissions Trading System (EU ETS) as applicable to vessels calling at EU/EEA ports. For vessels transiting through the Suez Canal to European ports, the system must calculate the EU ETS liability covering 100% of emissions for intra-EU voyages and 50% for voyages between EU and non-EU ports. The system must track EU Allowance (EUA) requirements per vessel and per entity, calculate surrender obligations, and generate compliance reports. EUA costs must be tracked in EUR with conversion to entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD). The system must model the EU ETS phase-in schedule (40% in 2024, 70% in 2025, 100% in 2026) and project future EUA requirements and costs based on planned voyage schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R006	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	FuelEU Maritime GHG Intensity Compliance	The system must track well-to-wake greenhouse gas intensity of energy used onboard vessels per the FuelEU Maritime regulation for vessels calling at EU/EEA ports. The system must calculate the GHG intensity in grams of CO2 equivalent per megajoule for each fuel type considering upstream emissions (well-to-tank) and combustion emissions (tank-to-wake). Compliance assessment must compare actual GHG intensity against the reference value and annual reduction targets (2% reduction from 2025, escalating to 80% by 2050). The system must support pooling mechanisms allowing entities to pool compliance across vessels. Penalty calculations must compute the amount due in EUR for non-compliance with conversion to entity-local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R007	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Carbon Emissions Dashboard with IMO 2050 Trajectory	The system must present a real-time carbon emissions dashboard showing fleet-wide and per-vessel emissions against the IMO 2050 decarbonization trajectory. The dashboard must display current emissions levels, required reduction targets at key milestones (20% by 2030, 70% by 2040, net-zero by 2050 relative to 2008 baseline), and projected trajectory based on current operational patterns and planned fleet renewal. Visual indicators must show whether the fleet is on track, at risk, or off track for each milestone. The dashboard must support entity-level views (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and consolidated group view. All text and labels must be available in English and Arabic with RTL layout support. Time axes must offer Hijri calendar overlay option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R008	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Voyage-Level Carbon Accounting	The system must perform carbon accounting at the individual voyage level, calculating total CO2, CH4, and N2O emissions for each voyage from departure to arrival. Emissions must be calculated using fuel-specific emission factors from IMO's Fourth GHG Study and updated as new factors are published. For LNG-fueled vessels, methane slip must be accounted for using engine-type-specific slip rates. Voyage carbon accounts must attribute emissions to specific trade routes (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT: X tons CO2, Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port: Y tons CO2) enabling route-level carbon intensity analysis. Carbon costs must be monetized using configurable carbon pricing (internal carbon price, EU ETS price, or custom rates) in USD with multi-currency conversion support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R009	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Alternative Fuel Transition Planning	The system must provide decision support tools for planning the transition from conventional fossil fuels to low-carbon and zero-carbon alternatives in line with IMO 2050 targets. The tool must model scenarios for fleet-wide adoption of LNG, methanol, ammonia, hydrogen, and biofuels considering availability at key bunkering ports (Fujairah, Singapore, Rotterdam, and emerging facilities at Hamad Port and JNPT). Each scenario must project emissions reduction trajectory, capital expenditure requirements for vessel modifications or newbuilds, operating cost impacts with fuel price sensitivity analysis, and infrastructure readiness timelines. Financial projections must be presented in USD with entity-local currency equivalents and must support multi-year NPV analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R010	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Carbon Credit and Offset Management	The system must manage carbon credit inventories and offset purchases across all entities. It must track verified carbon credits from approved registries (Gold Standard, Verra VCS, CORSIA eligible units), recording credit type, vintage year, project origin, verification status, and retirement date. The system must match carbon credits to specific vessel emissions or entity-level obligations. Credit purchase transactions must be recorded in the purchase currency with conversion to entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must generate audit-ready reports of carbon credit usage and remaining balances. AI must recommend optimal credit purchasing strategies based on projected emission gaps, carbon price forecasts, and regulatory compliance deadlines across all four operating jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R011	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Speed Optimization for CII Management	The system must provide AI-driven speed optimization recommendations to manage vessel CII ratings within target bands. The optimizer must calculate the optimal speed profile for remaining voyages in the calendar year to achieve a target CII rating (typically B or C) while minimizing schedule disruption and fuel cost impact. Speed recommendations must consider charter party minimum speed requirements, cargo delivery deadlines, port berth window constraints at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and weather forecasts. The system must present trade-off analysis between speed reduction (fuel savings vs schedule impact) in both English and Arabic. Projected CII impact of each speed scenario must be displayed with confidence intervals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R012	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Emissions Reporting for Flag State Compliance	The system must generate emissions reports compliant with flag state requirements for all vessel registries represented in the fleet. Reports must follow the format and data requirements of major flag states including Marshall Islands, Panama, Liberia, and any vessels registered in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India. The system must track reporting deadlines per flag state and issue automated reminders 60, 30, and 14 days prior to submission deadlines. Verification workflows must support classification society review and approval with digital document exchange. Reports must include SEEMP Part III data as required by MEPC.346(78). All generated reports must maintain audit trails and be archived for a minimum of 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R013	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Supply Chain Scope 3 Emissions Calculation	The system must calculate Scope 3 emissions attributable to container shipping activities for shipper customers requiring supply chain carbon footprint data. Calculations must allocate vessel emissions to individual containers or TEUs based on weight or volume pro-rata methodology per the Global Logistics Emissions Council (GLEC) framework and ISO 14083 standard. Emission intensity must be expressed per TEU-kilometer and per ton-kilometer for different trade lanes. The system must generate emission statements for customer cargo movements between Gulf ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali) and Indian ports (JNPT) in customer-requested formats. Data must be exportable for integration into customer ESG reporting platforms with values in metric tons CO2e.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R014	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Regulatory Compliance Fuel Reporting	The system must generate fuel consumption reports compliant with regulatory requirements across all operating jurisdictions. Reports must satisfy IMO DCS requirements per MARPOL Annex VI regulation 22A, EU MRV requirements per Regulation (EU) 2015/757, flag state annual fuel consumption reporting requirements, and port state requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must track reporting deadlines per regulation and vessel, issue automated reminders, and maintain submission records. Reports must be verifiable by accredited verifiers with supporting evidence packages. Each regulatory report must include methodology descriptions, data source references, and uncertainty assessments. Reports must be archivable for minimum 10 years with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R014	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Regulatory Change Monitoring and Impact Assessment	The system must monitor upcoming and proposed changes to international and regional carbon emissions regulations using AI-powered regulatory intelligence feeds. Monitored sources must include IMO MEPC session outcomes, EU legislative developments, and regional regulations from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India maritime authorities. When new regulations are identified, the system must automatically assess the potential impact on the managed fleet including compliance gaps, required operational changes, and estimated financial impact in USD with entity-local currency conversions. Impact assessments must be generated within 72 hours of regulatory publication and distributed to designated compliance officers in English and Arabic. The system must maintain a forward-looking regulatory calendar with Hijri and Gregorian date support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R015	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Emissions Data Integrity and Verification	The system must implement multi-layer data verification to ensure the integrity of emissions data used for regulatory reporting. Verification layers must include automated cross-referencing of fuel consumption data against bunker delivery notes (BDN), comparison of reported fuel consumption against vessel performance model predictions (flagging variances exceeding 5%), reconciliation of fuel ROB measurements against calculated consumption, and AIS-based distance verification against reported distances. Data quality scores must be assigned to each reporting period at the vessel level. Periods with quality scores below 90% must be flagged for manual review. The verification audit trail must be maintained for a minimum of 7 years to support regulatory inspections and must be accessible through interfaces supporting Arabic RTL display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-003-R016	FEAT-042-2-003	MOD-042	Carbon Emissions Forecasting Engine	The system must provide AI-powered carbon emissions forecasting for the fleet over short-term (remaining calendar year), medium-term (1-5 years), and long-term (to 2050) horizons. Short-term forecasts must use confirmed voyage schedules, vessel performance models, and seasonal weather patterns to project year-end CII ratings and annual emissions. Medium-term forecasts must incorporate planned fleet changes (newbuilds, disposals, charter-in/charter-out), expected regulatory tightening, and fuel transition timelines. Long-term forecasts must model multiple decarbonization pathways aligned with IMO ambition levels. All forecasts must be entity-specific and consolidatable at the group level with financial projections in USD and entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R001	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Fleet-Wide Fuel Consumption Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time fleet-wide fuel consumption dashboard displaying total fuel consumption by type (HFO, VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG, biofuel blends), consumption rate trends per vessel, fleet average consumption metrics, and consumption vs budget variance. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel class, trade route, and time period (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annual). Time periods must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selections. Fuel costs must be displayed in the bunkering currency and converted to entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD using daily exchange rates. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout and load within 3 seconds for fleet sizes up to 200 vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R002	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Vessel-to-Vessel Peer Benchmarking	The system must enable benchmarking of fuel efficiency between vessels of the same class, size, and age within the managed fleet. Benchmarking metrics must include specific fuel consumption (grams per kilowatt-hour), fuel consumption per TEU-mile, fuel consumption per deadweight-ton nautical mile, and daily fuel consumption at reference speeds. The system must normalize comparisons for environmental conditions (wind, sea state, current), draft and trim differences, and cargo loading variations. Peer group definitions must be configurable by vessel managers. Vessels performing in the bottom quartile of their peer group must be automatically flagged with recommended improvement actions generated by the AI engine. Benchmark reports must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R003	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Industry Benchmark Comparison	The system must compare fleet fuel efficiency against published industry benchmarks from sources including IMO GHG studies, ICS performance standards, and commercial benchmarking databases (Sea/net, MariApps, or equivalent). Industry benchmarks must be updated at least quarterly and cover vessel categories relevant to the managed fleet (container vessels by TEU capacity, bulk carriers by DWT range). The system must display the fleet's percentile ranking against industry averages for key metrics. Where industry data is available by trade route (e.g., Gulf-India, Gulf-Europe via Suez), route-specific comparisons must be provided. Benchmark source, methodology, and data currency must be clearly disclosed. Reports must present monetary values in USD with entity-local currency equivalents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R004	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Historical Performance Trend Analysis	The system must maintain at least 10 years of historical fuel consumption data per vessel and generate trend analysis reports showing performance degradation or improvement over time. Trend analysis must identify patterns attributable to hull fouling (typically 1-2% per quarter degradation without cleaning), engine component wear, propeller roughness, and operational practice changes. The AI engine must correlate performance trends with maintenance events (hull cleaning, propeller polishing, engine overhaul) to quantify the effectiveness of each intervention. Trend visualizations must support interactive drill-down with date range selection in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. The system must project future performance based on current trends to support maintenance planning decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R005	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Bunker Consumption Reconciliation	The system must perform automated reconciliation between fuel consumed (as calculated from flow meters and noon reports), fuel purchased (from bunker delivery notes - BDNs), and fuel remaining on board (ROB measurements). Reconciliation must be performed at voyage level, monthly, and annually. Discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds (default 2% of total consumption) must be flagged for investigation. The system must track bunker quality parameters (density, viscosity, sulfur content, catalytic fines) from BDN and fuel analysis reports to correlate fuel quality with consumption patterns. Bunker costs must be tracked in the purchase currency (typically USD at Fujairah, INR at Indian ports, QAR at Hamad Port) with conversion to entity reporting currencies and group consolidation currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R006	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Trim and Draft Optimization Recommendations	The system must provide AI-generated trim and draft optimization recommendations to minimize fuel consumption for each voyage. Recommendations must be based on vessel-specific hull form data, CFD analysis results where available, and historical performance data correlating trim/draft combinations with actual fuel consumption. The system must calculate optimal trim for laden and ballast conditions at planned voyage speeds, accounting for cargo weight distribution from the loading computer. Expected fuel savings from optimal trim must be quantified in metric tons and monetary value in the vessel's operating currency with entity-local currency conversion. Recommendations must be communicated to vessels before departure from Gulf ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali) and Indian ports (JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R007	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Main Engine and Auxiliary Performance Monitoring	The system must monitor fuel efficiency of main engines and auxiliary engines separately, tracking specific fuel oil consumption (SFOC) against manufacturer's test bed values and shop trial results. Performance deviation from reference SFOC must be calculated considering engine load, ambient conditions, and fuel type. The system must flag excessive SFOC deviations (>5% above reference) as potential indicators of injector wear, turbocharger fouling, air cooler degradation, or fuel system issues. Monitoring must integrate with onboard sensor data where available (shaft power meters, flow meters, exhaust gas analyzers) and fall back to noon report data for vessels without automated monitoring. Alert notifications must be sent to technical superintendents in the responsible entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R008	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Speed-Consumption Curve Generation	The system must generate and maintain dynamic speed-consumption curves for each vessel based on actual operational data. Curves must be produced for laden and ballast conditions at representative drafts, with separate curves for different sea state conditions (calm, moderate, rough). The AI engine must fit curves using regression analysis on validated noon report data, filtering out outliers and data quality issues. Speed-consumption curves must be compared against charter party warranted curves and sea trial curves to identify performance gaps. The system must automatically update curves as new voyage data becomes available, maintaining version history for audit purposes. Curves must be exportable in tabular and graphical formats for use in voyage estimation and charter party negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R009	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Hull and Propeller Fouling Impact Assessment	The system must quantify the fuel consumption impact of hull and propeller fouling for each vessel by analyzing performance degradation trends between dry-docking and hull cleaning events. The AI engine must model fouling progression based on vessel operating profile, water temperature in operating areas (Arabian Gulf averages 25-35°C, promoting rapid fouling), idle time in port, and anti-fouling coating type and age. The system must estimate the added fuel consumption in metric tons per day attributable to fouling at reference speed and calculate the economic penalty in USD with entity-local currency conversion. When the estimated fouling penalty exceeds a configurable threshold, the system must recommend hull cleaning and project the ROI of cleaning versus continued operation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R010	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Fuel Efficiency KPI Scorecards	The system must generate monthly fuel efficiency KPI scorecards for each vessel, each entity, and the consolidated fleet. Scorecard KPIs must include total fuel consumption vs target, fuel cost per TEU transported, fuel cost per revenue dollar, CII operational rating, EEOI (Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator), and fleet ranking. Each KPI must show current period value, previous period comparison, year-to-date trend, and target attainment percentage. Scorecards must be generated automatically on the 5th business day of each month and distributed to designated recipients per entity. Scorecard formats must support both English and Arabic with monetary values in entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD. Distribution must support email and in-system notification channels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R011	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Fuel Cost Budget Variance Reporting	The system must generate fuel cost budget variance reports comparing actual fuel expenditure against budgeted amounts at vessel, entity, and fleet levels. Variance analysis must decompose total variance into price variance (actual vs budgeted fuel price), volume variance (actual vs budgeted consumption quantity), and exchange rate variance (for multi-currency entities). Budgets must be maintained in entity-local currencies (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with USD consolidation for group reporting. Monthly forecast revisions must be supported based on updated voyage plans and bunker price projections. Variance reports must be integrated with the ERP financial module for general ledger posting. Report generation must support Hijri calendar periods for KSA entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R012	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	AI Fuel Savings Opportunity Identification	The system must employ AI algorithms to proactively identify fuel saving opportunities across the fleet. Opportunity categories must include speed optimization (slow steaming potential), route optimization (alternative waypoints), trim optimization, hull cleaning scheduling, auxiliary engine load management, and port turnaround time reduction. Each identified opportunity must be quantified with estimated annual fuel savings in metric tons, monetary value in USD and entity-local currencies, CO2 reduction equivalent, and implementation difficulty rating. Opportunities must be ranked by ROI and presented in an actionable format to fleet performance managers. The AI engine must learn from implemented vs rejected opportunities to improve future recommendations across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R013	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Bunker Procurement Optimization Integration	The system must integrate fuel efficiency data with bunker procurement planning to optimize purchasing decisions. Integration must provide voyage-specific fuel requirement forecasts based on vessel performance models, optimal bunkering port recommendations considering fuel prices at Fujairah (USD/AED), Singapore (USD), Hamad Port (USD/QAR), and Indian ports (USD/INR), minimum and maximum bunkering quantities accounting for tank capacity and voyage requirements, and fuel quality recommendations based on engine performance data. The system must calculate the total cost of ownership for each bunkering option including fuel price, deviation cost, and time cost. Multi-currency cost comparisons must enable procurement teams to identify the most economical bunkering strategy for each voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R015	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Fuel Efficiency Benchmarking Data Export API	The system must expose RESTful API endpoints for exporting fuel efficiency benchmarking data to external systems and business intelligence platforms. API endpoints must support JSON and CSV response formats with configurable data granularity (voyage, daily, monthly aggregation). Data available through the API must include vessel fuel consumption metrics, benchmarking scores, CII ratings, emission calculations, and cost analyses. API authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with entity-specific access tokens ensuring data segregation between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. Rate limiting must be enforced at 1000 requests per hour per client. API documentation must follow OpenAPI 3.0 specification. All monetary values must include currency codes and exchange rate references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R016	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Offline Fuel Data Collection and Synchronization	The system must support offline fuel data collection for vessels and port offices with intermittent connectivity. The offline module must cache all reference data (vessel particulars, fuel types, port codes, exchange rates) and provide local data entry forms for noon reports, bunker receipts, and ROB declarations. Forms must validate input data locally using the same plausibility rules as the online system. When connectivity is restored, the module must synchronize collected data with the central system using conflict resolution rules (latest timestamp wins for single-user fields, merge for multi-user fields). Synchronization must handle bandwidth constraints common on maritime VSAT connections. The offline module must support Arabic input and RTL display for shore-side users in Qatar, UAE, and KSA offices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-042-2-004-R017	FEAT-042-2-004	MOD-042	Executive Fuel Efficiency Summary Reporting	The system must generate executive-level fuel efficiency summary reports for C-suite stakeholders providing a high-level overview of fleet fuel performance, cost management, and environmental compliance. Reports must include fleet fuel spend vs budget with variance explanation, top 5 fuel-saving achievements of the period with quantified impact, CII rating distribution across the fleet, emissions compliance status summary, fuel price trend analysis and forward-looking exposure, and year-over-year efficiency improvement metrics. Reports must be limited to 2-3 pages in PDF format with executive-quality visualizations. AI must generate narrative commentary explaining key trends and recommending strategic actions. Reports must be produced in English and Arabic bilingual format with all financial figures in USD and entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R006	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Supporting Document Upload and Validation	The system must allow claimants to upload supporting documents at registration including commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading copy, survey report, photographs of damage, temperature logs for reefer containers, and insurance policy. Each document must be validated for file type (PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF), maximum size (25 MB per file, 100 MB total per claim), and completeness. The system must use AI-powered OCR to extract key data points from uploaded documents—such as invoice value, container number from B/L, and commodity description—and cross-validate these against the manually entered claim data. Discrepancies exceeding a configurable threshold must trigger a validation warning. All documents must be stored with AES-256 encryption and tagged with metadata in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R011	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claim Type and Cause Code Classification	The system must classify each claim by type and cause using a hierarchical code structure aligned with International Union of Marine Insurance (IUMI) loss categories. Claim types must include but not be limited to: physical damage, shortage, contamination, temperature deviation (reefer), water damage, theft/pilferage, delay, and total loss. Each type must have sub-categories—for example, physical damage subdivided into crushing, puncture, breakage, and deformation. The AI triage engine must suggest type and cause codes based on the damage description analysis, with the claims handler able to accept, modify, or override the suggestion. The classification must also capture the stage at which damage likely occurred: pre-shipment, loading, sea transit, discharge, or inland transportation, mapped to the relevant port operations at QAHAM, AEKHL, AEJEA, or INJNP.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-001-R015	FEAT-043-1-001	MOD-043	Claim Status Notification Preferences	The system must allow claimants and internal users to configure notification preferences for claim status updates at the time of registration and subsequently. Notification channels must include email, SMS, in-app push notification, and WhatsApp (widely used in GCC and India). Users must be able to select which claim lifecycle events trigger notifications: registration confirmation, handler assignment, document request, survey scheduled, liability decision, settlement offer, and closure. Notification templates must be available in English and Arabic, with Arabic notifications using proper RTL formatting. The system must respect quiet hours configurable per entity timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India) and must batch non-urgent notifications to avoid excessive messaging. Failed notification delivery must be retried up to three times with exponential backoff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R003	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Carrier Defense Applicability Analysis	The system must systematically evaluate all 17 excepted perils under Hague-Visby Article IV Rule 2 against the facts of each claim. For each defense, the system must determine applicability (applicable, not applicable, or requires further investigation), provide reasoning based on the claim facts and supporting evidence, and suggest what additional evidence would strengthen or weaken the defense. The analysis must specifically account for common defense scenarios in the Gulf and Indian trade lanes—such as weather-related damage during monsoon season affecting JNPT-bound cargo, extreme heat damage to temperature-sensitive commodities at Jebel Ali or Hamad Port during summer months, and piracy risk assessments for vessels transiting the Gulf of Aden. Each defense evaluation must be presented in a structured format reviewable by claims handlers in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R006	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Contributory Negligence Assessment	The system must assess whether the claimant's own actions contributed to the cargo damage, which may reduce or eliminate carrier liability. The assessment must evaluate factors including: insufficiency of packing (Article IV Rule 2(n)), inherent vice or nature of goods (Article IV Rule 2(m)), act or omission of the shipper (Article IV Rule 2(i)), and failure to declare dangerous goods. The AI engine must cross-reference the commodity type against known packaging requirements, check whether the shipper provided special handling instructions on the booking, and verify if any shipper-packed container (SOC) declarations were made. For temperature-sensitive cargo in reefer containers, the system must compare the shipper's requested temperature settings against the actual settings recorded by the container monitoring system from MOD-002. The assessment must produce a contributory negligence percentage estimate with supporting reasoning accessible in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R009	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Historical Precedent Matching	The system must maintain a database of historical claim outcomes and maritime case law precedents relevant to the Gulf and Indian subcontinent trade lanes. When assessing liability for a new claim, the AI must search for and present up to five most relevant historical precedents based on similarity of cargo type, damage cause, trade route, and legal issues. Each precedent must include a summary of the facts, the liability outcome, the legal reasoning, and the jurisdiction. The system must support precedents from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian maritime courts and arbitration tribunals, as well as English High Court Admiralty Division decisions which are commonly referenced in the region. Precedent summaries must be available in English and Arabic, and the database must be updatable by legal team members with appropriate access controls and version history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R010	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Liability Assessment Workflow Approval	The system must enforce a configurable approval workflow for liability assessments before they become the official position on a claim. The workflow must support multiple approval levels based on claim value thresholds: claims under USD 10,000 require claims handler approval only, claims between USD 10,000 and USD 50,000 require senior claims handler approval, claims between USD 50,000 and USD 250,000 require claims manager approval, and claims exceeding USD 250,000 require director-level or legal counsel approval. Each approver must be able to accept, reject with comments, or modify the liability assessment. Rejections must return the assessment to the originator with specific revision instructions. The workflow must track SLA timers for each approval stage, send reminder notifications before SLA breach, and support delegation of approval authority during approver absence per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India office calendars).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R011	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Deviation and Deck Cargo Liability Analysis	The system must assess whether deviation from the agreed route or unauthorized deck stowage impacts the carrier's liability position. For deviation analysis, the system must compare the vessel's actual AIS track (from MOD-005 voyage data) against the contractually agreed route and recognized customary routes between the Gulf ports and Indian subcontinent. If an unreasonable deviation is detected, the system must flag that the carrier may lose the benefit of all Article IV defenses and limitation rights under Hague-Visby Rules. For deck cargo, the system must check whether the bill of lading contains a valid deck carriage clause, whether the cargo was in fact stowed on deck based on the vessel stowage plan, and whether the damage is consistent with deck stowage exposure (weather, sea spray, UV degradation). The analysis must distinguish between containers customarily carried on deck on container vessels and underdeck cargo improperly stowed on deck.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R012	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Dangerous Goods Liability Special Handling	The system must apply specialized liability assessment rules for claims involving dangerous goods classified under the IMDG Code. The assessment must verify whether the shipper properly declared the dangerous goods class, UN number, proper shipping name, and packing group on the booking and bill of lading. If the shipper failed to declare or misdeclared dangerous goods, the system must apply Hague-Visby Article IV Rule 6, under which the carrier may land, destroy, or render innocuous such goods without compensation. The system must cross-reference the DG manifest from MOD-007 (Dangerous Goods Management) and verify container placarding and labeling compliance. For claims where properly declared dangerous goods caused damage to adjacent cargo, the system must assess whether the carrier's stowage arrangements complied with IMDG Code segregation requirements, referencing the vessel stowage plan and IMDG segregation tables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R013	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Himalaya Clause and Sub-Contractor Protection	The system must evaluate the applicability of Himalaya clause protections when claims are brought against the carrier's servants, agents, or independent contractors such as stevedores at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT terminals. The system must parse the bill of lading to detect the presence and scope of a Himalaya clause, determine whether the defendant party falls within the clause's protection, and assess whether the party was acting within the scope of their engagement when the damage occurred. The analysis must account for local jurisdictional variations—notably that Indian courts have historically applied a stricter interpretation of Himalaya clause protections compared to GCC jurisdictions. The system must flag claims where Himalaya clause protection may be contested and recommend whether to include the terminal operator or stevedore as a co-defendant in any recovery action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R014	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Liability Assessment Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive liability assessment reports in PDF format for each claim, suitable for internal review, claimant communication, and legal proceedings. The report must include: executive summary with liability recommendation, detailed factual analysis, applicable legal framework, defense analysis with citations to specific Hague-Visby Articles, package limitation calculations with SDR conversion, contributory negligence findings, multi-party apportionment if applicable, historical precedent references, and recommended next steps. The report must be generatable in English and Arabic versions, with the Arabic version using proper RTL layout, Arabic legal terminology, and Hijri dates alongside Gregorian dates. The report must include the entity letterhead (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India entity) and must be digitally signed by the assessing officer. Report generation must complete within 30 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R015	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Without Prejudice Communication Framework	The system must enforce a 'without prejudice' communication framework for all liability-related correspondence generated during the assessment phase. All outgoing communications regarding liability must be automatically watermarked with 'WITHOUT PREJUDICE' in both English and Arabic (بدون إخلال بالحقوق). The system must maintain a strict separation between without-prejudice assessment documents and open (non-privileged) claim correspondence. Users must be prevented from attaching without-prejudice documents to open correspondence without explicit authorization from the legal department. The system must track the privilege status of every document associated with the claim and must support privilege log generation for litigation discovery purposes. Templates for without-prejudice settlement offers must be available in English and Arabic and must comply with the legal privilege rules applicable in each entity's jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-002-R016	FEAT-043-1-002	MOD-043	Automatic Subrogation Rights Identification	The system must automatically identify potential subrogation and recovery rights arising from the liability assessment. When the carrier is found liable and the claim is payable, the system must evaluate whether recovery can be pursued against third parties such as terminal operators, stevedoring companies, inland transporters, or the shipper for contributory negligence. The system must calculate the recoverable amount based on the liability apportionment and generate a recovery recommendation report. For claims where the claimant is a cargo insurer exercising subrogation rights, the system must verify the letter of subrogation, confirm the insurer has paid the assured, and validate that the subrogated claim does not exceed the amount actually paid by the insurer. The system must maintain a recovery tracking register with amounts in the original claim currency and USD equivalent, linking recovery actions to the parent claim for consolidated financial reporting across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R001	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Surveyor Panel Management	The system must maintain a panel of approved cargo surveyors categorized by port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), survey specialization (general cargo, reefer, dangerous goods, bulk, project cargo), language capability (English, Arabic, Hindi, Urdu), and professional accreditation (IIMS, BIMCO, Lloyd's Agency). Each surveyor record must include contact details, hourly rates in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), average response time, historical quality ratings based on past survey report assessments, and availability calendar. The system must support both in-house surveyors employed by the entity and external independent surveyors. Panel maintenance must include expiry tracking for professional certifications and insurance policies, with automatic alerts 60 days before expiry. Surveyor records must be searchable in both English and Arabic name formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R002	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	AI-Optimized Surveyor Appointment	The system must use an AI recommendation engine to suggest the optimal surveyor for each claim based on weighted criteria including: proximity to the survey location (port or inland depot), specialization match to the cargo type and damage type, availability within the required SLA timeframe, historical performance rating, cost efficiency, and language requirements. The AI must account for survey urgency—perishable cargo at JNPT during monsoon season or temperature-sensitive goods at Jebel Ali during summer require faster surveyor deployment. The system must present the top three recommended surveyors to the claims handler with a comparison matrix. Upon selection, the system must automatically generate and send the surveyor appointment letter via email and SMS in the surveyor's preferred language, including claim summary, survey scope, container location, and access instructions for the relevant port terminal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R003	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Joint Survey Coordination	The system must facilitate joint survey coordination when multiple parties require representation at the survey—typically the carrier's surveyor, the cargo owner's surveyor, and the insurer's surveyor. The system must send joint survey invitations to all parties specifying the proposed date, time, and location, and must collect acceptance or alternative date proposals. A scheduling algorithm must find the earliest mutually available time slot, accounting for port working hours at each facility (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and local weekend patterns (Friday-Saturday in Qatar/UAE/KSA, Saturday-Sunday in India). If agreement cannot be reached within the configurable timeframe (default 72 hours), the system must escalate to the claims handler and allow proceeding with a unilateral survey with proper notice to non-attending parties. All joint survey communications must be logged in the claim audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R004	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Mobile Survey Data Collection App	The system must provide a mobile application for surveyors to conduct and record surveys in the field at port terminals and container yards. The app must support: GPS-tagged photograph capture with timestamp overlay, video recording of damage (up to 5 minutes per clip), voice-to-text damage narration in English and Arabic, barcode and QR code scanning for container number and seal number verification, digital measurement recording (dimensions of damage area), temperature reading input for reefer surveys, and structured data entry forms for different cargo types. The app must function in offline mode with automatic data synchronization when connectivity is restored, as port terminal areas at Hamad Port and JNPT frequently experience connectivity issues. All locally stored survey data must be encrypted at rest. The app must capture the surveyor's digital signature upon survey completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R005	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	AI-Powered Damage Image Analysis	The system must apply computer vision AI to analyze photographs taken during cargo surveys to assist in damage quantification and classification. The AI must be trained to identify common cargo damage types including water damage, crushing, rust and corrosion, mold and mildew, breakage, deformation, contamination, and temperature damage. For each identified damage type, the AI must estimate the affected area as a percentage of the visible cargo, classify severity on a scale of 1 to 5, and suggest whether the damage is consistent with the claimed cause. The AI analysis must generate a structured output that pre-populates the survey report damage matrix. The AI confidence score must be displayed alongside each assessment, and surveyors must be able to override AI findings with manual input and justification. The system must continuously improve accuracy by incorporating surveyor feedback as training data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R006	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Container Condition Recording	The system must capture detailed container condition records as part of every survey, using the IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) damage coding standard. The survey must document external container condition including all six sides (front, rear, left, right, top, floor), door operation and gasket integrity, and structural member condition. For reefer containers, additional recording must cover refrigeration unit condition, pre-trip inspection results, data logger download, and set temperature versus actual temperature readings throughout the voyage. The system must compare the surveyed container condition against the container interchange receipt (EIR) at the port of loading to determine whether damage was pre-existing. IICL damage codes must be displayed in a visual container diagram interface allowing the surveyor to tap on the affected area and select the applicable code. All condition data must be stored with photographic evidence linked to each recorded defect.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R007	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Seal Integrity Verification	The system must enforce seal integrity verification as a mandatory component of every container survey. The surveyor must record: the seal number on the bill of lading, the seal number physically present on the container, whether the seal is intact or broken, the seal type (bolt seal, cable seal, padlock), and photographic evidence of the seal condition. The system must cross-reference the recorded seal number against the bill of lading seal number, the terminal gate-in seal record from the port operating system at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, and any intermediate seal change records from transshipment ports. Discrepancies in seal numbers or evidence of tampering must be automatically flagged as high-priority alerts on the claim, as seal integrity is critical evidence in theft and pilferage claims. The system must generate a seal verification certificate as part of the survey documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R008	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Survey Report Template Engine	The system must provide configurable survey report templates tailored to different survey types: initial damage survey, re-survey, pre-shipment survey, on-off hire survey, and salvage survey. Each template must define mandatory sections and optional sections, with structured fields for narrative description, quantified damage, photographs, measurements, and conclusions. Templates must be available in English and Arabic dual-language format with automatic translation assistance for narrative sections. The template engine must support entity-specific branding and legal disclaimers—Qatar entity reports must include QFC compliance statements, UAE reports must reference DMCA jurisdiction, and Indian reports must reference Mumbai High Court Admiralty jurisdiction. Templates must be version-controlled, and the system must ensure that reports generated under a specific template version reference that version for legal traceability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R009	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Damage Quantification and Valuation	The system must support structured damage quantification with monetary valuation for settlement purposes. The quantification must capture: quantity of damaged units versus total shipped units, weight of damaged goods versus total weight, percentage of damage per unit (partial damage), and whether the goods are a total loss, partial loss, or have suffered depreciation only. Valuation must be calculated based on the CIF value (cost, insurance, freight) as declared on the commercial invoice, with the system automatically converting to the claim currency if the invoice is in a different currency. The system must apply sound arrived value calculations where the goods have residual value, computing the claim as the difference between sound and damaged values. For commodities traded on exchanges (metals, agricultural products), the system must reference market prices on the date of arrival at the discharge port. All calculations must be transparent, auditable, and presentable in the survey report.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R010	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Survey Cost Tracking and Approval	The system must track all costs associated with cargo surveys including surveyor fees, travel expenses, laboratory testing fees, and special equipment hire. Each cost line must capture the amount in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent, the payee, invoice reference, and payment status. Survey cost approval must follow a configurable workflow: costs within the surveyor's pre-agreed rate card are auto-approved, costs exceeding rate card by up to 20% require claims handler approval, and costs exceeding rate card by more than 20% require claims manager approval. The system must flag survey costs that appear disproportionate to the claim value—for example, survey fees exceeding 15% of the claimed amount must trigger an alert. Approved survey costs must be automatically posted to the claim's cost ledger in the finance module (MOD-030) with the correct entity accounting treatment and currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R011	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Laboratory and Expert Analysis Integration	The system must support referral of samples to third-party laboratories and expert analysts when survey findings require specialized analysis such as metallurgical testing, chemical composition analysis, or contamination source identification. The system must maintain a panel of approved laboratories categorized by specialization and location in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Upon referral, the system must generate a chain of custody form documenting sample collection, handling, and transfer to the laboratory. Laboratory results must be uploadable as structured data (not just PDF attachments) with fields for test type, methodology, findings, and conclusion. The AI engine must cross-reference laboratory findings with the damage description and survey observations to validate consistency. Test results must be incorporated into the final survey report automatically, and the cost of laboratory analysis must be tracked under the claim's survey cost ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R012	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Survey Report Quality Assurance Review	The system must enforce a quality assurance review step before any survey report is finalized and shared externally. The QA review must be performed by a senior surveyor or claims technical lead and must evaluate: completeness of all mandatory report sections, adequacy of photographic evidence (minimum 10 photographs for standard surveys, minimum 25 for high-value claims), consistency between damage description and photographs, accuracy of damage quantification and valuation calculations, correct application of IICL codes, and proper attribution of causation. The QA reviewer must use a structured checklist that scores the report quality on a scale of 1-10. Reports scoring below 7 must be returned to the surveyor for revision with specific improvement instructions. QA review must be completed within 48 hours of report submission, with SLA tracking and escalation for overdue reviews. QA metrics must be tracked per surveyor for panel performance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R013	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Survey Scheduling Calendar Integration	The system must provide a visual survey scheduling calendar that displays all upcoming, in-progress, and completed surveys across all four port locations. The calendar must support day, week, and month views with color-coded status indicators (scheduled, in-progress, completed, cancelled, overdue). Each calendar entry must display the claim reference, container number, survey type, assigned surveyor, and location. The calendar must integrate with port terminal operating hours and berth schedules from MOD-003 (Terminal Operations) to suggest optimal survey times when the container is accessible. The system must account for Hijri calendar dates and local holidays in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India when scheduling, automatically avoiding non-working days per entity. Surveyors must be able to sync survey appointments to their personal calendar (iCal, Google Calendar, Outlook) and receive reminders 24 hours and 2 hours before the scheduled survey time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R014	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Salvage and Mitigation Action Tracking	The system must track salvage and damage mitigation actions taken during or after the survey to minimize loss. Mitigation actions must include: reconditioning of damaged goods, repacking, sorting of damaged from sound cargo, cold storage for perishables, and disposal of contaminated goods. Each action must record the date, description, service provider, cost in local currency and USD, and authorization details. The system must enforce that mitigation actions are authorized by the claims handler before incurring cost, except in emergency situations where the surveyor may authorize immediate action up to a configurable limit (default USD 5,000). The system must calculate the mitigation benefit by comparing the estimated loss without mitigation versus the actual loss after mitigation, supporting the principle of minimizing losses under Hague-Visby Rules. All salvage proceeds must be recorded and offset against the claim amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R015	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Digital Evidence Chain of Custody	The system must maintain an unbroken chain of custody for all digital evidence collected during surveys including photographs, videos, data logger downloads, and laboratory results. Each piece of evidence must be timestamped with collection date and time, GPS location coordinates, collecting party identity, device identifier, and cryptographic hash (SHA-256) computed at the time of capture. Any subsequent access, modification, or export of evidence must be logged in the chain of custody record. The system must detect and flag any evidence that has been modified after initial capture by re-computing and comparing hash values. Evidence must be stored in tamper-proof storage with write-once-read-many (WORM) compliance for legal admissibility. The system must generate chain of custody certificates suitable for submission to maritime courts and arbitration tribunals in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R016	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Reefer Container Data Logger Analysis	The system must provide specialized analysis tools for reefer container data logger downloads as part of temperature-sensitive cargo surveys. The system must import data logger files from major reefer unit manufacturers (Carrier PrimeLINE, Daikin, Thermo King, Star Cool) and parse temperature, humidity, atmosphere control (CA/MA), and alarm records. The analysis tool must generate time-series graphs showing set point versus actual temperature throughout the voyage, highlight any deviation events exceeding configurable thresholds (default plus or minus 1 degree Celsius from set point for more than 2 hours), and identify whether deviations occurred during pre-carriage, port storage, sea transit, or post-discharge. The system must compare deviations against the commodity's acceptable temperature range stored in the cargo commodity master. Analysis results must feed into the liability assessment for reefer claims, determining whether temperature deviation was due to carrier negligence, equipment failure, or shipper-caused factors such as incorrect set point instructions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-003-R017	FEAT-043-1-003	MOD-043	Third-Party Survey Report Ingestion	The system must accept and ingest survey reports produced by third-party surveyors who use their own reporting systems outside the platform. The ingestion process must support PDF and Word document uploads with AI-powered extraction of key data fields including: survey date, surveyor name, container number, damage description, damage quantification, and surveyor's opinion on cause. Extracted data must be presented to the claims handler for verification and manual correction before being committed to the claim record. The system must convert third-party survey findings into the platform's standardized damage coding and quantification structure for consistent reporting and analytics. Third-party reports must be stored with clear labeling distinguishing them from system-generated reports, and any discrepancies between the third-party report findings and the platform's AI damage analysis must be highlighted for the claims handler's attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R001	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Hague-Visby One-Year Time Bar Calculation	The system must automatically calculate the Hague-Visby time bar date for every registered cargo claim as one year from the date of delivery or the date when the goods should have been delivered, per Article III Rule 6. The calculation must correctly determine the delivery date by referencing the container discharge date from the terminal operating system, the consignee's collection date from the container yard, or the date of final delivery for door-to-door shipments. Where delivery has not occurred (non-delivery claims), the system must calculate from the expected delivery date based on the voyage schedule plus a reasonable time for discharge and collection. The calculated time bar date must be stored as an immutable reference date on the claim, with any extension agreements recorded separately. The system must handle timezone differences correctly—for example, a container delivered at JNPT at 23:30 IST on December 31 must use the local date, not UTC.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R002	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Multi-Jurisdiction Time Bar Rules Engine	The system must maintain a configurable rules engine containing the time limitation periods applicable under each jurisdiction's maritime law. At minimum, the engine must support: Hague-Visby Rules (one year from delivery), Qatar Maritime Law (one year from delivery with potential two-year extension for concealed damage), UAE Federal Maritime Law (one year per Article 295 with specific provisions for domestic coastal trade), KSA maritime regulations and their interaction with Sharia law limitation principles, and Indian Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1925 (one year per the Hague Rules schedule, with the Indian Limitation Act providing a residual three-year period for breach of contract claims). The rules engine must determine the applicable limitation period based on the claim's liability regime and jurisdiction, and must apply the most conservative (shortest) time bar when multiple regimes overlap. Rules must be maintainable by legal administrators without code changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R003	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Progressive Time Bar Alert System	The system must generate progressive alerts as a claim approaches its time bar deadline. The alert schedule must be configurable per entity and must default to the following intervals before the time bar date: 180 days (six months warning), 90 days (three months warning), 60 days, 30 days, 14 days, 7 days, 3 days, and 1 day (final warning). Each alert must include the claim reference, time bar date, days remaining, current claim status, and recommended actions. Alerts must be delivered via email, in-app notification, and SMS to the assigned claims handler, their supervisor, and the legal team. For claims within 30 days of time bar, alerts must additionally be sent to the entity's head of claims and general counsel. The alert must be displayed prominently in the claims handler's dashboard with color coding—green for more than 90 days, amber for 30 to 90 days, and red for fewer than 30 days. Alerts must account for the entity's local calendar including Hijri dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R004	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Contractual Notice Period Tracking	The system must track contractual notice periods separate from statutory limitation periods. Many bills of lading contain clauses requiring written notice of damage within specific timeframes—typically three days from delivery for apparent damage and 15 days for concealed damage under Article III Rule 6 of the Hague-Visby Rules. The system must parse the bill of lading terms or accept manual entry of notice period requirements. Upon claim registration, the system must calculate whether the claimant's notice was timely by comparing the notice date against the delivery date and the applicable notice period. Late notice must be flagged on the claim with an assessment of its impact on the carrier's liability position—under Hague-Visby, failure to give notice creates a presumption of delivery in good condition but does not extinguish the claim. The system must distinguish between apparent and concealed damage notice periods and must track notice dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R005	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Time Bar Extension Agreement Management	The system must support the recording and tracking of time bar extension agreements (also known as standstill agreements or time extension letters) between the carrier and the claimant. Each extension agreement must record: the original time bar date, the extended time bar date, the parties to the agreement, the date of agreement, and any conditions attached. The system must generate standardized extension agreement templates in English and Arabic, pre-populated with claim details. The system must recalculate alert schedules based on the extended time bar date while retaining the original time bar date for reference. Multiple successive extensions must be supported with a full chronological history. The system must track the status of extension requests (proposed, accepted, rejected, expired) and must alert the claims handler if an extension proposal has not been responded to within the configurable period (default 14 days). Extension agreements must be digitally signed by authorized representatives per the entity's signing authority matrix.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R006	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Protective Proceedings Deadline Tracking	The system must track deadlines for issuing protective proceedings (writs, arbitration notices) to preserve claims or counterclaims before time bar expiry. When a claim or recovery action approaches the 60-day warning threshold and settlement or extension agreement has not been reached, the system must automatically generate a protective proceedings recommendation alert to the legal team. The alert must specify the appropriate jurisdiction and court or arbitration forum based on the bill of lading dispute resolution clause—commonly London arbitration (LMAA), English High Court, Qatar International Court, DIFC courts, or Mumbai High Court Admiralty Division. The system must track whether protective proceedings have been issued, the date of issuance, the case reference number, and the court or tribunal. For recovery actions against third parties (terminal operators, stevedores), the system must track separate limitation deadlines which may differ from the main claim time bar.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R007	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Time Bar Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide a dedicated time bar monitoring dashboard accessible to claims handlers, supervisors, and legal team members. The dashboard must display all active claims sorted by proximity to time bar date, with visual indicators showing claims in green (more than 90 days remaining), amber (30-90 days), red (fewer than 30 days), and black (time-barred). The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, claims handler, claim severity, and claim value range. Summary statistics must include: total claims approaching time bar within 30/60/90 days, total value at risk from approaching time bar, number of claims where extension agreements are pending, and number of claims where protective proceedings have been recommended but not yet issued. The dashboard must support export to Excel and PDF in both English and Arabic formats with appropriate Hijri date columns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R008	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Automatic Time Bar Suspension Detection	The system must detect events that may suspend or toll the running of the limitation period under applicable law. Suspension events must include: filing of a lawsuit or arbitration claim (stops time bar in most jurisdictions), commencement of formal mediation under a contractual ADR clause, agreement to extend time bar, and jurisdiction-specific events such as court-ordered stays of proceedings. The system must also recognize that in certain GCC jurisdictions, the limitation period may be suspended during the holy month of Ramadan or by operation of force majeure declarations. Upon detecting a suspension event, the system must pause the time bar countdown, record the suspension reason with supporting documentation, and recalculate the remaining limitation period when the suspension is lifted. All suspension events must be logged in the claim audit trail with the date of suspension, reason, applicable legal basis, and the resumption date when known.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R009	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Carrier's Indemnity Claim Time Bar Tracking	The system must track time bar deadlines for the carrier's own indemnity and recovery claims against third parties separately from the main cargo claim timeline. Under Hague-Visby Article III Rule 6 bis, an action for indemnity may be brought even after the main one-year time bar has expired, provided it is brought within the time allowed by the law of the court seised, and not less than three months from when the person seeking indemnity has settled or been served with process. The system must calculate the indemnity claim deadline for each identified liable third party (terminal operator, stevedore, feeder operator, inland haulier) based on the applicable local law. The system must generate separate alert schedules for indemnity claims and must link indemnity time bar records to the parent cargo claim. Recovery amount tracking must support all five currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD equivalent calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R010	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Time Bar Compliance Audit Report	The system must generate periodic time bar compliance audit reports for management review. The report must list all claims that became time-barred during the reporting period (monthly and quarterly), the reason why the claim was not resolved or proceedings were not issued before time bar, the value of time-barred claims in USD and local entity currency, and the responsible claims handler and supervisor. The report must also identify claims that were saved from time bar by extension agreements or last-minute protective proceedings. A trend analysis section must show time bar compliance rates over time by entity, port, and claims team, identifying systemic issues. The report must be generated automatically on the first business day of each month and distributed to the head of claims, legal counsel, and entity managing directors. The report must be bilingual in English and Arabic with Hijri dates alongside Gregorian, and must include actionable recommendations generated by the AI for improving time bar compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R011	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Linked Claims Time Bar Synchronization	The system must synchronize time bar tracking across linked claims—such as a group of claims arising from the same incident, or a main claim and its associated counter-claim or recovery action. When multiple claims share a parent incident reference (as created in bulk claim registration), the system must display the time bar status of all linked claims in a consolidated view. If one claim in a linked group receives a time bar extension, the system must alert the claims handler to consider whether extensions should be sought for all related claims. The system must also detect scenarios where the time bar on a recovery action against a third party may expire before the main claim is settled, creating a risk of irrecoverable loss. Cross-entity linked claims must be visible regardless of which entity registered the claim, supporting the scenario where a claim filed in the UAE entity for Jebel Ali discharge is linked to a recovery action tracked by the India entity against JNPT terminal operators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R012	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Weekend and Holiday Adjustment for Deadlines	The system must correctly adjust all time bar deadlines and procedural dates that fall on non-business days according to the applicable jurisdiction's rules. In Qatar, the standard weekend is Friday-Saturday; in UAE it was changed to Saturday-Sunday from January 2022; in KSA the weekend is Friday-Saturday; and in India the weekend is Saturday-Sunday. Public holidays must be maintained per entity using an updatable calendar that includes both Gregorian holidays and Islamic holidays (calculated from Hijri calendar) such as Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, and Indian national holidays (Republic Day, Independence Day, Gandhi Jayanti). When a deadline falls on a non-business day, the system must extend it to the next business day per the local procedural rules. The system must also handle the variability of Hijri-based holidays whose Gregorian dates shift each year and may be confirmed only days before by official announcement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R013	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Time Bar Override and Manual Adjustment	The system must allow authorized users (claims manager level and above) to manually override or adjust time bar dates with mandatory justification. Override scenarios include: correction of an incorrectly calculated delivery date, application of a different limitation regime upon legal review, recording of a court order extending or shortening the limitation period, and recognition of a foreign judgment or arbitral award that affects the time bar calculation. Each override must record the original calculated date, the new date, the reason for override, the authorizing user, and any supporting document reference. The override must trigger a recalculation of all associated alert schedules. The system must maintain a complete history of all overrides for audit purposes, and overrides must require two-factor authentication by the authorizing user to prevent unauthorized modifications. A monthly report of all time bar overrides must be generated for legal department review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R014	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Claimant Time Bar Notification	The system must generate formal time bar warning notices to claimants when their claims are approaching statutory deadlines without resolution. The notice must be generated automatically at configurable intervals (default 90 days and 30 days before time bar) and must state the applicable limitation period, the calculated time bar date, and a recommendation that the claimant seek legal advice regarding preservation of their rights. The notice must be available in English and Arabic, with the Arabic version using formal legal Arabic terminology. Notices must be sent via the claimant's preferred communication channel (email, postal mail, or both) with proof of delivery tracking. For email notifications, the system must track delivery receipts and read receipts. For postal notices, the system must generate the formatted letter for printing with entity letterhead and must record the dispatch date and courier tracking reference. The without-prejudice status of these notices must be clearly marked per the jurisdiction's legal conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R015	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Arbitration and Jurisdiction Clause Parser	The system must parse the dispute resolution clause from bills of lading to determine the applicable forum and procedural rules for protective proceedings. The parser must identify: the governing law (English law, UAE law, Qatar law, KSA law, Indian law), the dispute resolution mechanism (court litigation, LMAA arbitration, ICC arbitration, SCCA arbitration in Saudi Arabia, QICCA arbitration in Qatar, ad hoc arbitration), and any tier requirements such as mandatory mediation before arbitration. The parser must use AI-based NLP to extract this information from uploaded B/L documents, with support for standard liner B/L formats from major carriers operating in the Gulf and Indian trade lanes. Parsed results must be validated against a database of known carrier B/L clause templates for accuracy. The identified forum and governing law must inform the applicable time bar rules and must be displayed on the claim record and time bar dashboard. Manual override must be available for non-standard or ambiguous clauses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R016	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Time Bar Analytics and Predictive Alerts	The system must provide analytics and AI-driven predictions related to time bar risk across the claims portfolio. The analytics must include: percentage of claims resolved before time bar, average resolution time by claim type and entity, claims at risk of time bar based on current resolution velocity, and projected time bar exposure value by month. The AI predictive model must analyze historical claim resolution patterns to identify claims that are progressing slower than similar claims and are therefore at elevated risk of reaching time bar without resolution. These at-risk claims must receive enhanced alerts and must be flagged on the claims handler's priority list. The analytics must support drill-down from portfolio level to entity level to individual claim, and must be visualizable in dashboard charts and exportable reports. All monetary values must be presentable in USD or any of the four local entity currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), with conversion using current exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-1-004-R017	FEAT-043-1-004	MOD-043	Integration with Legal Case Management	The system must integrate with external legal case management systems and the internal legal module to ensure seamless tracking when a claim transitions from administrative handling to legal proceedings. When protective proceedings are issued or a claim enters litigation or arbitration, the system must create a linked legal case record containing the claim reference, court or tribunal case number, appointed lawyers, key procedural dates (filing, response deadline, hearing dates, discovery deadlines), and the claim's current time bar status. The integration must support bi-directional synchronization so that updates in the legal case management system (such as court-ordered deadline extensions or adjournments) automatically update the time bar tracking in the claims module. The system must support legal case management integrations via REST API and must handle timezone differences between the claims operations (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and the legal team which may be based in London or Singapore.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R001	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Multi-Currency Settlement Calculation Engine	The system must calculate claim settlement amounts in the claimant's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) using real-time exchange rates sourced from the central treasury module. Settlement calculations must account for the original declared cargo value currency, the insurance policy currency, and any intermediate conversion spreads. The engine must support split settlements where partial amounts are paid in different currencies, for example when a claim involves cargo transshipped between Jebel Ali (AED) and JNPT Mumbai (INR). All currency conversions must be locked at the approval timestamp and recorded with the applicable exchange rate for audit purposes. Rounding rules must follow each jurisdiction's monetary conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R002	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Approval Workflow with Entity-Specific Thresholds	The system must enforce a multi-tier approval workflow for claim settlements with configurable monetary thresholds per legal entity. For the Qatar entity operating at Hamad Port, settlements exceeding QAR 50,000 must require regional director approval; for the UAE entity at Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, settlements above AED 75,000 must escalate to the VP of Operations. KSA entity settlements above SAR 100,000 require additional compliance review per Saudi maritime authority regulations. India entity settlements at JNPT above INR 500,000 must trigger Consumer Affairs compliance checks. Each approval tier must support delegation, time-bound auto-escalation after 48 hours of inactivity, and Arabic/English bilingual approval notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R003	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Partial and Staged Settlement Payments	The system must support partial settlement payments where a claim is resolved in multiple installments, common in high-value reefer container damage claims where repair costs are assessed incrementally. Each partial payment must be tracked against the total approved settlement amount with running balances displayed in both the settlement currency and the entity's base currency. Staged payments must support different payment methods per installment including wire transfer, demand draft, and digital payment channels. The system must prevent overpayment by blocking any partial payment that would cause the cumulative disbursed amount to exceed the approved settlement ceiling, with tolerance configurable per entity up to 2% for currency fluctuation adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R004	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Payment Integration with Banking Systems	The system must integrate with banking payment gateways for automated settlement disbursement, supporting SWIFT transfers for international payments and local payment rails including NEFT/RTGS for India (JNPT operations), UAEFTS for UAE operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SARIE for KSA operations, and QCB payment systems for Qatar operations at Hamad Port. Each payment instruction must include the claim reference number, bill of lading number, and container numbers in the payment narrative. The system must handle payment failures gracefully, automatically retrying up to three times with exponential backoff before flagging for manual intervention. Payment confirmation receipts must be stored against the claim record with timestamp and bank reference details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R005	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Liability Apportionment Among Multiple Parties	The system must support apportioning settlement liability among multiple responsible parties in a single claim, which is common in container shipping where damage may involve the carrier, terminal operator, freight forwarder, and stevedoring company. Liability percentages must be configurable per claim and must sum to exactly 100%. Each liable party's share must be calculated against the total settlement amount and tracked independently for payment processing. The system must generate separate settlement notices to each party in their preferred language (Arabic or English) with their specific liability share, supporting INCOTERMS-based liability determination where terms like CIF, FOB, and CFR affect the point at which risk transfers between seller and buyer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R006	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Tax and VAT Handling on Settlement Payments	The system must correctly apply tax and VAT rules to settlement payments based on the jurisdiction of the paying entity and the nature of the claim. UAE entities must apply 5% VAT on service-related settlement components per Federal Tax Authority guidelines. KSA entities must apply 15% VAT where applicable per ZATCA regulations. Qatar entities must handle settlements with zero VAT currently but the system must support future VAT implementation. India entity settlements at JNPT must account for GST at applicable rates (18% for services, variable for goods) and withhold TDS at 2% on settlements exceeding INR 30,000 per Section 194C of the Income Tax Act. Tax calculations must be itemized separately in settlement documentation and reported to the accounts payable module for statutory filing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R007	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Document Generation in Bilingual Format	The system must generate settlement agreements, payment vouchers, release letters, and settlement receipts in both Arabic and English simultaneously as dual-language documents. Arabic text must render correctly in RTL layout with proper handling of mixed Arabic-English content such as container numbers (e.g., MAEU1234567), vessel names, and port codes (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INJNP for JNPT). Documents must include the Hijri date alongside the Gregorian date as required by KSA and Qatar regulatory bodies. Settlement agreements must be generated as digitally signable PDFs with watermarking indicating draft or final status, and must include all referenced bill of lading numbers and container identifiers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R008	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Negotiation Tracking and Counter-Offer Management	The system must provide a structured negotiation tracking interface where claim adjusters can record initial settlement offers, claimant counter-offers, and subsequent rounds of negotiation until agreement is reached or the claim is escalated to arbitration. Each negotiation round must capture the offered amount, the currency, the date (in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), the negotiating parties, and any attached supporting documents such as revised survey reports or repair quotations. The system must maintain a complete negotiation history timeline viewable in both Arabic and English interfaces. Auto-calculated fields must show the percentage difference between the current offer and the original claim amount, helping adjusters assess the negotiation gap for container damage claims across all four operational ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R009	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Reserve Adjustment and Financial Impact Tracking	The system must automatically adjust claim reserves upon settlement approval, releasing any excess reserve back to the entity's claims provision account or requesting additional reserve allocation if the settlement exceeds the original reserve. Reserve adjustments must be posted to the general ledger in the entity's base currency with appropriate inter-company entries when the claim spans multiple entities, such as a shipment originating at JNPT and destined for Hamad Port involving both the India and Qatar entities. The system must calculate and display the settlement-to-reserve ratio for management reporting and flag any settlement that exceeds the original reserve by more than 20% for additional financial controller review. Historical reserve adequacy data must be retained for actuarial analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R010	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Offline Settlement Processing for Remote Locations	The system must support offline settlement processing capabilities for claim adjusters operating in remote port areas or on vessel surveys where internet connectivity is unreliable, particularly at anchorage areas outside Hamad Port and during container inspections at inland container depots near JNPT. Offline mode must allow adjusters to draft settlement offers, capture digital signatures on settlement agreements, and photograph settlement-related documents. All offline data must be stored locally with AES-256 encryption and automatically synchronized with the central system upon connectivity restoration, with conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recently modified record and flags any concurrent modifications by other users for manual review. Offline sessions must have a maximum duration of 72 hours before requiring re-authentication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R011	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Payment Reconciliation and Confirmation	The system must reconcile settlement payments against bank statements to confirm successful disbursement, matching by claim reference number, payment amount (with a tolerance of 0.5% for bank charges), and beneficiary account details. Reconciliation must run automatically every four hours for all active payment channels across all entities. Unmatched payments older than five business days must be flagged for investigation by the finance team. The system must generate a daily settlement payment reconciliation report per entity showing total settlements paid, outstanding payments, failed payments, and payments pending bank confirmation. For cross-border settlements between entities such as a UAE entity at Jebel Ali paying an Indian claimant, the system must track both the originating and receiving bank confirmations independently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R012	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Claimant Bank Account Verification and Sanctions Screening	The system must verify claimant bank account details before processing any settlement payment, validating IBAN format for GCC-based claimants (QA for Qatar, AE for UAE, SA for KSA) and IFSC codes for Indian claimants at JNPT. The system must screen all claimant beneficiaries against international sanctions lists including OFAC SDN, EU consolidated list, UN Security Council sanctions, and local regulatory watchlists for each jurisdiction. Sanctions screening must occur at the time of initial claim registration and again immediately before payment execution to catch any interim list updates. Any positive match must immediately halt the settlement payment, trigger a compliance alert to the entity's compliance officer, and create an audit record. Re-screening must be mandatory if the payment is delayed beyond 30 days from initial screening.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R013	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Interest Calculation on Delayed Settlements	The system must calculate and apply interest on settlement amounts when payment is delayed beyond the contractually agreed settlement period, which varies by jurisdiction: 30 days for UAE and Qatar entities, 45 days for KSA entity, and 60 days for India entity per the Carriage of Goods by Sea Act. Interest rates must be configurable per entity and per currency, with defaults aligned to each central bank's base rate plus a contractual spread. Interest calculations must use the actual/365 day count convention for GCC currencies and actual/365 for INR. The system must automatically compute accrued interest daily and add it to the settlement amount payable, with interest breakdowns shown separately in settlement documentation. Interest provisions must be posted to the appropriate financial accounts per entity accounting policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R014	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Write-Off and Bad Debt Processing	The system must support writing off uncollectable claim receivables and marking claims as bad debt when recovery efforts are exhausted, requiring approval from the entity CFO for write-offs exceeding entity-specific thresholds (QAR 25,000 for Qatar, AED 35,000 for UAE, SAR 40,000 for KSA, INR 300,000 for India). Write-off requests must include documentation of all recovery attempts, legal opinions where applicable, and the reason for write-off classification. The system must post write-off entries to the bad debt expense account in the general ledger with proper tax treatment per jurisdiction. Written-off claims must remain searchable and reportable for a minimum of seven years for regulatory compliance. If any recovery is subsequently received on a written-off claim, the system must automatically reverse the write-off proportionally and credit the recovery to the appropriate revenue account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R015	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Settlement Performance KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying key settlement performance indicators including average settlement cycle time (from claim registration to payment), settlement ratio (settled amount versus claimed amount), number of open settlements by aging bucket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, 90+ days), and total settlement outflow by entity and currency. The dashboard must support filtering by port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), by container type (dry, reefer, tank, flat rack, open top), by claim category (damage, shortage, delay, contamination), and by date range supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. All dashboard elements must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with proper numeral formatting (Eastern Arabic numerals for Arabic interface). The dashboard must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-001-R016	FEAT-043-2-001	MOD-043	Automated Settlement Notification and Communication Trail	The system must send automated notifications to all stakeholders at each settlement milestone including offer issuance, counter-offer receipt, approval pending, approval granted, payment initiated, and payment confirmed. Notifications must be sent via email and in-app alerts with content rendered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). SMS notifications must be supported for payment confirmations exceeding entity-defined thresholds. Each notification must include the claim reference number, bill of lading number, vessel name, voyage number, and container numbers for traceability. The system must maintain a complete communication audit trail per claim, recording all outbound and inbound communications with timestamps, recipient details, and delivery status. Failed notification deliveries must be retried three times before escalating to the claims supervisor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R001	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Subrogation Rights Identification and Registration	The system must automatically identify potential subrogation opportunities when a claim is settled by analyzing the liability chain including carrier, terminal operator, stevedore, inland transporter, and warehouse operator. Subrogation rights must be registered with a unique recovery case reference linked to the original claim, recording the settled amount, the target recovery party, their contact and legal details, and the legal basis for recovery (bill of lading terms, terminal handling agreement, INCOTERMS allocation). The system must support multiple subrogation targets per claim, which is common in multimodal shipments transiting through Jebel Ali for transshipment to Hamad Port or Khalifa Port. Each subrogation case must capture the applicable limitation period per the governing law jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R002	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Target Party Management and Classification	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of recovery target parties including shipping lines, terminal operators (DP World at Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi Ports at Khalifa Port, Mwani at Hamad Port, JNPT operators), stevedoring companies, freight forwarders, trucking companies, and warehouse operators. Each party record must include legal entity name in both Arabic and English, registered address, legal representative details, preferred communication language, historical recovery success rate, and average recovery duration. The system must classify recovery targets by responsiveness tier (cooperative, neutral, adversarial) based on historical interaction data. Party records must be linked to their active insurance policies and P&I club memberships to facilitate direct recovery from insurers where applicable under maritime law.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R003	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Demand Letter Generation and Tracking	The system must generate formal recovery demand letters in both Arabic and English with jurisdiction-appropriate legal language, referencing the applicable maritime convention (Hague-Visby Rules, Hamburg Rules, or Rotterdam Rules as applicable), the specific bill of lading terms, and the settlement details. Demand letters must include a response deadline calculated based on the target jurisdiction's legal requirements: 30 days for UAE and Qatar, 21 days for KSA, and 15 days for India. The system must track demand letter delivery via registered mail or courier with proof of delivery captured and stored. Automated follow-up reminders must be sent at 50% and 90% of the response deadline. If no response is received by the deadline, the system must automatically escalate the recovery case to the legal team and update the recovery probability score downward by a configurable percentage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R004	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Cross-Jurisdictional Recovery Proceedings Workflow	The system must manage recovery proceedings across multiple jurisdictions with jurisdiction-specific workflow configurations. For UAE recoveries related to Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port operations, the workflow must align with UAE Federal Maritime Law and DIFC courts where applicable. For Qatar recoveries at Hamad Port, proceedings must comply with Qatar Maritime Law and Qatar International Court processes. For KSA recoveries, the workflow must follow Saudi Maritime Commercial Law and the Board of Grievances procedures. For India JNPT recoveries, the system must support Admiralty Court filings under the Admiralty (Jurisdiction and Settlement of Maritime Claims) Act 2017. Each jurisdiction workflow must include configurable milestone tracking, document requirements checklist, and estimated timeline templates. The system must flag conflicting jurisdiction claims and recommend the most favorable forum based on historical recovery rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R005	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Amount Tracking and Financial Reconciliation	The system must track recovery amounts from initial demand through negotiation to final receipt, maintaining running totals in both the recovery currency and the entity's base currency. When a recovery payment is received, the system must automatically match it to the corresponding subrogation case, record the bank receipt details, and post the recovery to the appropriate general ledger account as a credit against the original claim expense. Partial recoveries must be tracked with remaining balances clearly displayed. The system must calculate the net recovery ratio (total recovered versus total settled) per recovery target, per port, and per entity. Inter-entity recovery scenarios where the paying entity differs from the recovering entity must generate proper inter-company accounting entries. Recovery amounts must be reconciled against bank statements with tolerance for bank charges and currency conversion differences up to 1%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R006	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Subrogation Time-Bar and Limitation Period Management	The system must track and enforce statutory limitation periods for subrogation claims based on the governing law and claim type. Under the Hague-Visby Rules the limitation period is one year from delivery of goods; under the Hamburg Rules it is two years. Local limitation periods must also be configured: three years for general commercial claims in UAE and Qatar, five years in KSA, and three years in India under the Limitation Act 1963. The system must calculate the time-bar date for each subrogation case at registration and provide automated warnings at 180 days, 90 days, 30 days, and 7 days before expiry. Cases approaching time-bar must be escalated to the legal team with urgent priority. The system must support tolling agreements that extend limitation periods and suspension events such as ongoing arbitration. A monthly time-bar exposure report must be generated showing all cases at risk of becoming time-barred within the next 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R007	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Negotiation and Settlement Tracking	The system must provide a structured interface for tracking recovery negotiations with target parties, recording each round of negotiation including the demanded amount, the counter-offered amount, supporting documents exchanged, and the negotiation timeline. The system must calculate the recovery efficiency percentage in real-time showing the ratio of the currently offered amount to the original demand amount. Negotiation communications must be logged with full audit trail including email correspondence, meeting notes, and telephone call summaries. The system must support settlement compromise agreements where the recovery party agrees to pay a reduced amount in exchange for a full and final release. Compromise settlements must require approval based on the write-down percentage: automatic approval for up to 10% discount, claims manager approval for 10-25%, and director approval for discounts exceeding 25% of the demanded amount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R008	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Arbitration and Litigation Case Integration	The system must integrate with external legal case management for subrogation cases that escalate to arbitration or litigation. Supported arbitration forums must include the DIFC-LCIA Arbitration Centre for UAE disputes, the Qatar International Centre for Conciliation and Arbitration for Qatar disputes, the Saudi Centre for Commercial Arbitration for KSA disputes, and the Mumbai Centre for International Arbitration for India disputes. The system must track arbitration milestones including filing date, tribunal constitution, document exchange deadlines, hearing dates, and award dates. Legal costs must be tracked separately from the recovery amount and allocated to the appropriate entity cost center. Arbitration awards must be recorded against the subrogation case and trigger automated recovery payment tracking workflows. The system must support London Maritime Arbitrators Association (LMAA) arbitration for international carriage disputes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R009	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	P&I Club and Insurance Recovery Coordination	The system must facilitate recovery actions directed at Protection and Indemnity (P&I) clubs and cargo insurers of liable third parties. The system must maintain a directory of major P&I clubs (Gard, Skuld, UK P&I, Standard Club, West of England, North P&I, Steamship Mutual, and others) with their claims handling contact details and correspondence addresses. Recovery demands to P&I clubs must follow the standard club correspondence format including vessel particulars, voyage details, bill of lading references, and survey report summaries. The system must track club responses, security provision (letters of undertaking), and settlement negotiations separately from direct party negotiations. When a liable vessel's P&I club provides a Letter of Undertaking as security, the system must record the LOU details, coverage limits, and expiry date, and generate alerts 30 days before LOU expiry to ensure renewal or claim filing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R010	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Cost-Benefit Analysis and Pursuit Decision Support	The system must provide an automated cost-benefit analysis for each potential subrogation case to support the pursuit or abandonment decision. The analysis must consider the recoverable amount, estimated legal costs by jurisdiction (with pre-configured cost templates for UAE, Qatar, KSA, and India courts), historical recovery success rate for the specific target party, the target party's financial standing and ability to pay, and the estimated time to recovery. The system must calculate the expected net recovery value using probability-weighted scenarios and recommend pursuit when the expected net recovery exceeds a configurable threshold (default 30% of the settled claim amount). Cases recommended for abandonment must still be reviewed by the claims manager before closure. The cost-benefit analysis must be recalculated whenever significant case developments occur such as new evidence or changes in the target party's financial status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R011	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Automated Recovery Aging and Escalation Rules	The system must automatically track the aging of each recovery case from the date of initial demand and apply escalation rules based on configurable aging buckets. Recovery cases with no response within 30 days of demand must be escalated to the senior claims adjuster. Cases with no substantive progress at 60 days must be escalated to the claims manager with a recommendation for legal action. Cases exceeding 90 days without resolution must be escalated to the regional director with a litigation recommendation and cost-benefit analysis. Each escalation must generate notifications in the escalation recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) with a summary of the case status, amounts involved, and recommended next steps. The system must maintain escalation history and track the average time-to-resolution per escalation tier for performance benchmarking across all four operational ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R012	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Portfolio Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive recovery portfolio reports showing total outstanding recovery amounts by entity, by port, by target party, and by aging bucket. Reports must include recovery velocity metrics showing the average time from demand to receipt, recovery success rates by claim category (damage, shortage, contamination, delay), and net recovery ratios by target party type (carrier, terminal operator, freight forwarder). The system must support comparative analysis across reporting periods using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. All monetary values must be displayable in any of the five supported currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with conversion at the period-end exchange rate. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English and exportable to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats. An executive summary dashboard must highlight the top ten recovery cases by value and the top five overdue recovery cases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R013	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Salvage and Cargo Disposal Recovery Integration	The system must integrate salvage and cargo disposal proceeds into the overall recovery tracking for a claim. When damaged cargo is sold through salvage, the salvage proceeds must be recorded against the claim and deducted from the net loss before calculating the subrogation demand amount. The system must track the salvage process including cargo condition assessment, auction or private sale arrangements, buyer details, and sale proceeds by currency. For perishable cargo in reefer containers at any of the four ports, the system must support emergency salvage workflows with expedited approval processes to minimize further deterioration losses. Salvage proceeds in currencies different from the claim settlement currency must be converted at the date-of-receipt exchange rate. The system must generate salvage certificates and disposal records as required by insurance underwriters and P&I clubs for claim documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R014	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Contribution and General Average Recovery Handling	The system must support General Average (GA) contribution recovery as a distinct subrogation type, handling cases where cargo interests must contribute to extraordinary sacrifices or expenditures made for the common safety of the maritime adventure under the York-Antwerp Rules. The system must track GA declarations, average adjuster appointments (with support for major adjusters like Richards Hogg Lindley), GA bonds and guarantees, contributory value calculations, and final GA adjustment statements. The system must calculate each container's contributory share based on its CIF value and apply the GA contribution percentage from the adjuster's statement. For containers transiting through the four operational ports, the system must track which containers are subject to GA contribution and generate notices to cargo interests in their preferred language. GA security requirements must be tracked to ensure containers are not released without proper GA bond or cash deposit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R015	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Recovery Case Closure and Final Accounting	The system must enforce a structured closure process for subrogation cases ensuring all financial entries are completed and reconciled before case closure. Closure requirements must include confirmation that all recovery payments have been received and reconciled, all legal costs have been captured and allocated, inter-entity recoveries have been properly journalized, and any remaining recovery provisions have been released back to the entity's general reserve. The closure process must generate a final recovery summary document showing the original claim amount, settlement paid, total recovered, legal costs incurred, and net financial impact per entity. Closed cases must be archived but remain searchable and reportable for a minimum of ten years. The system must prevent reopening of closed recovery cases without claims director approval and a documented justification. Post-closure recoveries must be handled through a separate windfall recovery process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-002-R016	FEAT-043-2-002	MOD-043	Offline Recovery Case Management for Field Adjusters	The system must support offline access to recovery case files for field adjusters conducting investigations at remote port locations, vessel inspections, or cargo surveys at inland depots where internet connectivity is limited. Offline functionality must include viewing recovery case details, recording investigation notes, capturing photographs of damaged cargo or containers, and drafting demand letters for later review and dispatch. The system must allow selective synchronization of specific recovery cases for offline use, downloading all associated documents including survey reports, bills of lading, packing lists, and previous correspondence. Data captured offline must be encrypted using AES-256 and synchronized upon reconnection with automatic conflict detection if the same case was modified by another user during the offline period. Offline sessions must log all actions with timestamps for audit compliance across all four operational jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R001	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Predictive Model Training on Historical Claims Data	The system must train and maintain machine learning models using a minimum of five years of historical claims data across all four operational entities and ports. Training features must include cargo commodity type (using HS codes), container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 20FR, 20TK), voyage route, vessel age and type, season and weather patterns, origin and destination ports, INCOTERMS, packaging type, cargo value bands, and historical claims frequency for each feature combination. The model must be retrained monthly with incremental learning on new claims data. Model performance must be evaluated using standard classification metrics including precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC-ROC with minimum acceptable thresholds configurable per entity. Training data must be anonymized to comply with data protection regulations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R002	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Real-Time Claim Risk Scoring for Active Shipments	The system must generate a real-time claim risk score on a scale of 0 to 100 for every active shipment at the point of booking, at container gate-in, at vessel loading, during transit, and at discharge. The risk score must be computed within 500 milliseconds of any triggering event to support real-time operational decisions. Risk factors must be weighted and combined using the trained model, with the top five contributing risk factors displayed alongside the score for explainability. Scores above 70 must be classified as high risk and trigger automated alerts to the operations team and claims department. Scores between 40 and 70 must be classified as medium risk. The risk score must be recalculated whenever material changes occur such as vessel deviation, transshipment changes, weather warnings affecting the route, or port congestion alerts at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R003	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Weather and Environmental Risk Factor Integration	The system must integrate real-time weather data feeds and environmental condition monitoring into the claim prediction model. Data sources must include sea state forecasts (wave height, wind speed, swell period) from maritime weather providers covering the Arabian Gulf, Arabian Sea, and Indian Ocean routes servicing the four operational ports. The system must correlate historical weather conditions with claims frequency to identify high-risk weather patterns, such as Shamal wind events in the Arabian Gulf affecting vessels at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali, and monsoon conditions affecting JNPT operations during June through September. Temperature extremes must be factored into reefer cargo risk predictions, particularly for the extreme summer heat in Gulf ports where ambient temperatures exceed 50°C. The model must update risk scores within one hour of receiving adverse weather warnings for any route segment in its operational coverage area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R004	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Container Condition and Age Risk Assessment	The system must integrate container condition data from pre-trip inspections and CSC (Convention for Safe Containers) plate information into the claim probability model. Risk factors must include container age, last survey date, number of repair cycles, known structural weaknesses, door seal condition, floor integrity for dry containers, and refrigeration unit service history for reefer units. The system must flag containers with higher claim probability based on their condition score, cross-referenced with the planned cargo type. For example, containers with borderline floor condition scores carrying heavy cargo such as machinery or steel must receive elevated risk scores. The system must integrate with container tracking databases to access real-time container condition data and must support manual condition override entries from surveyors at any of the four operational ports when automated data is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R005	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Carrier and Vessel Performance Risk Profiling	The system must maintain risk profiles for each carrier and vessel based on historical claims data, vessel inspection records, port state control detention history, and P&I club ratings. The carrier risk profile must include the carrier's overall claims frequency, average claim severity, most common claim types, and claims trend over the past three years. Vessel-specific risk factors must include vessel age, flag state, classification society, last dry dock date, and cargo hold or container bay condition ratings. The system must automatically elevate the claim risk score for shipments booked on carriers or vessels with deteriorating claims performance trends. Carrier risk profiles must be updated weekly with new claims data and must be viewable by the commercial team to inform rate negotiations. The system must support Arabic and English display of carrier and vessel names with proper transliteration between scripts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R006	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Port and Terminal Handling Risk Analysis	The system must analyze and score the cargo handling risk at each port and terminal based on historical damage claims attributed to port operations. Risk analysis must cover the four primary operational ports (Hamad Port with Mwani Qatar operations, Khalifa Port with Abu Dhabi Ports, Jebel Ali with DP World, and JNPT with multiple terminal operators including BMCT, GTI, and NSICT) and all connecting feeder ports and inland container depots. The analysis must consider terminal equipment age and maintenance records, stevedore workforce training levels, container yard congestion rates, and seasonal operational challenges such as Ramadan staffing reductions and monsoon disruptions at JNPT. The system must identify specific terminal areas with elevated risk such as particular berths or container yard blocks with higher reported damage incidence. Port risk scores must be refreshed weekly and factored into the overall shipment risk score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R007	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Commodity-Specific Claim Risk Classification	The system must maintain commodity-specific claim risk profiles based on HS code classification at the 6-digit level, with commodity risk scores derived from historical claims frequency and severity data. High-risk commodity categories must include perishable goods (HS chapters 02-08), chemicals (HS chapters 28-38), electronics (HS chapter 85), automotive parts (HS chapter 87), and pharmaceuticals (HS chapter 30). The system must consider commodity-specific risk factors such as temperature sensitivity for reefer cargo, moisture sensitivity for paper and textiles, breakage susceptibility for ceramics and glass, and contamination risk for food products. Commodity risk scores must be adjustable by route segment, recognizing that the same commodity may have different risk profiles when shipped via the Arabian Gulf versus the Indian Ocean. The model must support new commodity classifications being added by the risk management team without requiring full model retraining.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R008	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Claim Severity Prediction and Financial Impact Estimation	The system must predict not only the probability of a claim occurring but also the estimated severity in monetary terms, providing a financial impact forecast for each shipment. Severity prediction must consider the declared cargo value, the INCOTERMS (CIF values being the maximum exposure under CIF terms), deductible amounts from the applicable insurance policy, and average settlement ratios by claim type and commodity from historical data. The system must produce a predicted loss range with confidence intervals (50th, 75th, and 95th percentiles) displayed in the cargo's insured currency and convertible to any of the five supported currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The predicted severity must be used to automatically set initial claim reserves when a claim is eventually filed, reducing manual reserve estimation. The system must track prediction accuracy over time and report mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R009	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Route-Based Risk Corridor Mapping	The system must maintain a dynamic risk corridor map covering all major shipping routes between the four operational ports and their global trade partners. Risk corridors must be color-coded on an interactive map interface (green for low risk, yellow for medium, red for high) based on aggregated claim probability data. The map must display risk variations by route segment, identifying high-risk transit zones such as the Strait of Hormuz (piracy and geopolitical risk), the Gulf of Aden, the Indian Ocean monsoon belt, and congested anchorage areas outside major ports. The system must overlay real-time risk alerts including weather warnings, port closures, and security advisories onto the route risk map. The map interface must support both Arabic and English labels with proper RTL rendering for Arabic place names. Risk corridor data must be exportable for integration with voyage planning systems and must update dynamically as new claims data and environmental conditions are processed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R010	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Proactive Risk Mitigation Recommendation Engine	The system must generate automated risk mitigation recommendations when a shipment's claim risk score exceeds configurable thresholds. Recommendations must be specific and actionable, such as recommending additional lashing for deck-stowed containers on vessels transiting the Arabian Gulf during Shamal wind season, suggesting reefer pre-trip inspection for temperature-sensitive cargo during Gulf summer months, recommending cargo surveyor attendance at loading for high-value shipments at JNPT, or advising container fumigation for agricultural commodities susceptible to pest infestation. Each recommendation must include the estimated risk reduction if implemented, the estimated cost of implementation, and the responsible party. Recommendations must be presented in both Arabic and English and must be trackable to confirm implementation. The system must learn from implemented recommendations and their outcomes to improve future recommendation quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R011	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	AI Model Explainability and Audit Trail	The system must provide full explainability for every claim risk prediction, showing the contribution of each input feature to the final risk score using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values or equivalent interpretability methods. Each prediction must be accompanied by a natural language explanation in both Arabic and English describing why the risk score is elevated or reduced, for example: 'Risk elevated due to reefer cargo on aging vessel during monsoon season transit to JNPT with carrier having above-average claims history.' The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all predictions including the model version used, input features and their values, the computed risk score, and any subsequent manual overrides. Model performance metrics must be tracked over time with drift detection alerting data scientists when prediction accuracy degrades beyond acceptable thresholds. All model artifacts and training datasets must be versioned and reproducible for regulatory examination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R012	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Integration with Booking and Documentation Systems	The system must integrate with the booking module to inject claim risk scores into the booking workflow, enabling commercial teams to make risk-informed booking decisions. At the time of booking confirmation, the system must display the predicted claim risk score, top risk factors, and recommended mitigation actions. For high-risk bookings (score above 70), the system must require explicit risk acknowledgment from the booking team before confirmation. The risk score must be carried through to the bill of lading, shipping instruction, and manifest documentation for downstream visibility. Integration with the container allocation module must enable preferential assignment of newer, better-condition containers for high-risk cargo. The system must expose risk score data via REST APIs with JSON response format for integration with external partner systems and customer-facing portals, with API authentication supporting OAuth 2.0 and API key methods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R013	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Customer Risk Profile and Claims History Analysis	The system must build and maintain risk profiles for each customer (shipper and consignee) based on their historical claims behavior across all entities and ports. Customer risk factors must include claims frequency relative to shipment volume, average claim severity, most common claim types, packaging quality history, and documentation accuracy record. The system must identify customers who consistently generate above-average claims rates and flag their future bookings for enhanced cargo inspection or survey requirements. Customer risk profiles must be shared across entities with proper data governance controls, ensuring that a customer's claims history at Jebel Ali is visible when they book through the Qatar entity at Hamad Port. The system must support customer risk score overrides by authorized users with documented justification. Customer-facing risk reports must be available in Arabic and English and must comply with data privacy regulations in each jurisdiction including Qatar Data Privacy Law, UAE PDPL, and India DPDP Act.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R014	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Seasonal and Event-Based Risk Pattern Detection	The system must detect and model seasonal and event-based patterns that affect claim probability, incorporating both Gregorian and Hijri calendar events. Seasonal patterns must include the Gulf summer heat season (June-September) affecting reefer cargo and temperature-sensitive goods, the Indian monsoon season (June-September) affecting JNPT operations with port closures and vessel delays, and winter fog conditions in the northern Indian Ocean affecting vessel navigation. Event-based patterns must include Ramadan and Eid periods when reduced port staffing may increase handling damage frequency, Hajj season when KSA port volumes shift, and major sporting or cultural events in Qatar and UAE that alter cargo flow patterns. The system must automatically adjust baseline risk scores when seasonal or event-based risk periods are active, with adjustment factors learned from historical data spanning at least three complete annual cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R015	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Batch Risk Assessment for Vessel Cargo Manifests	The system must support batch risk assessment for entire vessel cargo manifests, processing all containers on a single vessel call to generate an aggregated vessel-level risk score and individual container-level risk scores. Batch processing must complete within 60 seconds for vessels carrying up to 20,000 TEU. The vessel-level risk score must consider cargo compatibility factors such as hazardous goods proximity to food products, reefer containers near heat sources, and heavy cargo stowed above fragile goods. The system must generate a risk heat map by container bay, row, and tier showing the spatial distribution of high-risk containers on the vessel. Results must be presented in a downloadable report format compatible with vessel stowage planning systems. For vessels calling at multiple operational ports (for example loading at JNPT, discharging at Jebel Ali, and transshipping to Hamad Port), the system must calculate risk scores for each port call segment independently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R016	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Continuous Model Performance Monitoring and Feedback Loop	The system must implement a continuous feedback loop comparing predicted claim probabilities with actual claim outcomes to monitor and improve model performance. Every settled claim must be matched back to its pre-shipment risk prediction, and the prediction accuracy must be evaluated. The system must track true positive rate (claims correctly predicted), false positive rate (high-risk predictions that did not result in claims), false negative rate (claims that occurred on low-risk predictions), and true negative rate. Monthly model performance reports must be generated showing accuracy trends, calibration curves, and feature importance shifts. The system must detect concept drift where the relationship between risk factors and claims outcomes changes over time, automatically triggering model retraining when drift exceeds configurable thresholds. Feedback data must be segregated by entity and port to allow regional model fine-tuning while maintaining a global base model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-003-R017	FEAT-043-2-003	MOD-043	Claim Prediction API Rate Limiting and Performance SLA	The system must enforce API rate limiting for claim prediction requests to ensure consistent performance across all consuming applications. Rate limits must be configurable per client application with defaults of 1,000 requests per minute for internal systems and 100 requests per minute for external partner integrations. The prediction API must maintain a 99.5% uptime SLA with automatic failover to a secondary model serving instance. Response latency must not exceed 500 milliseconds at the 95th percentile under normal load and 2,000 milliseconds at the 99th percentile during peak periods. The system must implement request queuing with priority levels where booking-time predictions receive highest priority, followed by real-time transit monitoring predictions, and batch manifest assessments receive lowest priority. API usage metrics must be tracked per entity and per client application, with monthly usage reports generated for capacity planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R001	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Cross-Entity Claims Portfolio Dashboard	The system must provide a consolidated claims portfolio dashboard showing the total claims position across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with drill-down capability by entity, port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), claim status (open, under investigation, in negotiation, settled, closed, litigated), and claim category (cargo damage, shortage, delay, contamination, temperature deviation, pilferage). The dashboard must display key metrics including total incurred (paid plus outstanding reserves), claims frequency per 1,000 TEU, average claim cost, and loss ratio by entity. All monetary values must be displayable in any of the five supported currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using the selected date's exchange rate. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts and must refresh data at configurable intervals with a minimum of every 15 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R002	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Claims Trend Analysis and Forecasting	The system must provide time-series trend analysis for claims volume, frequency, and severity over configurable time periods supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views. Trend charts must support granularity options of daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual views. The system must apply statistical forecasting models (exponential smoothing, ARIMA, or similar) to project future claims volumes and costs for the next 3, 6, and 12 months per entity and per port. Seasonal decomposition must identify recurring patterns such as increased claims during the Gulf summer heat season, Indian monsoon period, and Ramadan staffing reduction periods. Trend analysis must support comparison across entities, allowing management to benchmark Hamad Port claims performance against Jebel Ali or JNPT. Forecast confidence intervals must be displayed at 80% and 95% levels. Trend data must be exportable in CSV and Excel formats for external actuarial analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R003	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Reserve Adequacy Analysis and Actuarial Reporting	The system must provide reserve adequacy analysis tools comparing outstanding case reserves against actuarial projections of ultimate loss for each claim cohort (grouped by incident month). The system must support chain ladder, Bornhuetter-Ferguson, and Cape Cod actuarial methods for reserve estimation. Development triangles must be generated automatically from historical claims data, showing paid and incurred development patterns by claim type and by entity. The system must calculate IBNR (Incurred But Not Reported) reserves based on historical reporting lag patterns per port and claim category. Reserve adequacy reports must show the total required reserve (case reserves plus IBNR) against the actual booked reserve with surplus or deficiency clearly highlighted per entity and consolidated. Reports must be generated quarterly for management review and must comply with IFRS 17 insurance contract reporting requirements. All actuarial calculations must be auditable with methodology documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R004	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Loss Prevention Program Effectiveness Measurement	The system must measure and report on the effectiveness of loss prevention programs by correlating claims data with implemented prevention measures. The system must track prevention program categories including container inspection programs, cargo packaging standards, reefer monitoring initiatives, terminal handling improvements, and driver training programs across all four operational ports. Effectiveness metrics must include before-and-after claims frequency comparison, claims severity reduction, return on investment for prevention spend, and statistical significance testing of observed improvements. The system must support A/B testing frameworks where prevention measures are applied to a treatment group while a control group follows standard procedures, enabling rigorous measurement of program impact. Effectiveness dashboards must be filterable by port, entity, cargo type, and time period. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English for presentation to regional management and insurance underwriters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R005	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Claims Benchmarking Against Industry Standards	The system must support benchmarking of the organization's claims performance against configurable industry benchmarks and standards. Benchmark metrics must include claims frequency per 1,000 TEU handled, average claim cost as a percentage of cargo value, settlement cycle time, and recovery success rate. The system must allow manual input of industry benchmark data from sources such as the International Union of Marine Insurance (IUMI) statistics, TT Club publications, and regional maritime authority reports. Internal benchmarking must compare performance across the four operational ports and entities with ranking tables and variance analysis. The system must highlight areas where performance deviates significantly from benchmarks (more than one standard deviation) and generate automated recommendations for improvement. Benchmark reports must be generated quarterly and must support trend comparison showing whether the gap to benchmark is improving or deteriorating over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R006	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Root Cause Analysis and Pareto Reporting	The system must provide root cause analysis tools that categorize claims by their underlying cause using a configurable multi-level cause code hierarchy. Level 1 categories must include handling damage, weather damage, packaging failure, equipment failure, stowage error, contamination, and theft or pilferage. Each level 1 category must have detailed level 2 and level 3 subcategories (for example, handling damage subdivided into crane damage, forklift damage, lashing failure, and dropped container). The system must generate Pareto charts showing the top causes of claims by frequency and by cost, identifying the vital few causes that account for 80% of claims losses. Pareto analysis must be filterable by entity, port, container type, commodity, and time period. The system must support cause-and-effect diagram generation linking root causes to contributing factors. All cause codes and analysis labels must be available in both Arabic and English with consistent translations maintained across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R007	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Carrier and Partner Performance Scorecards	The system must generate performance scorecards for all carriers, terminal operators, freight forwarders, and other supply chain partners based on claims data attributed to their operations. Scorecards must include claims frequency per TEU handled or per voyage, average claim severity, response time to claim notifications, cooperation with claim investigations, and subrogation recovery success rate. Partner scorecards must be updated monthly and must show trend data over the most recent twelve months. The system must rank partners within each category (carrier, terminal operator, forwarder) by overall claims performance score. Scorecards must flag partners whose performance is deteriorating with three consecutive months of worsening metrics. The scoring methodology must be transparent and documented, with weighted criteria configurable by the claims management team. Scorecards must be exportable in PDF format for sharing during partner performance review meetings and must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R008	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Geographic Heat Map of Claims Distribution	The system must provide an interactive geographic heat map visualizing the distribution and concentration of claims across operational ports, terminals, container yards, and trade routes. The heat map must display claim density by location with color intensity representing claims frequency or severity (selectable by the user). Port-level heat maps must show claims concentration within terminal areas, specific berths, and container yard zones at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Route-level heat maps must show claims incidence along major shipping lanes between operational ports and their global trade partners. The heat map must support temporal animation showing how claims patterns shift over time across seasons. Users must be able to click on any hot spot to drill down into the underlying claims data. The map interface must support Arabic place name labels when the interface language is set to Arabic, with proper RTL rendering for map legends and controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R009	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Claims Aging Analysis and Open Claims Monitoring	The system must provide detailed aging analysis for all open claims categorized by aging buckets: 0-30 days, 31-60 days, 61-90 days, 91-180 days, 181-365 days, and over 365 days from the date of claim registration. Each aging bucket must display the number of claims, total incurred value (paid plus reserves), and average age in days. The system must flag claims that exceed entity-specific target resolution timelines: 60 days for simple claims, 120 days for complex claims, and 180 days for litigated claims. An open claims inventory report must list all claims exceeding their target resolution date with the assigned adjuster, last activity date, days since last activity, and next required action. The system must generate automated stale claim alerts when no activity is recorded on a claim for more than 14 consecutive days. Aging analysis must be viewable by entity, port, adjuster, claim type, and currency denomination, with Arabic interface support including Eastern Arabic numeral formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R010	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Financial Impact Analysis and P&L Reporting	The system must generate financial impact reports showing the effect of claims on each entity's profit and loss statement, including gross claims cost, reinsurance recoveries, subrogation recoveries, salvage proceeds, and net retained loss. Reports must break down financial impact by business line (import, export, transshipment), by trade lane, and by customer account. The system must calculate and report the claims cost as a percentage of freight revenue per entity, enabling profitability analysis at the trade lane and customer level. Intercompany claims between entities (for example, a claim originating at JNPT affecting cargo destined for the Qatar entity at Hamad Port) must be properly allocated and reported without double-counting. Financial impact reports must align with the entity's chart of accounts and be reconcilable with the general ledger. Reports must support both accrual and cash-basis views and must be available in all five currencies with period-end exchange rate conversions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R011	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Adjuster Workload and Performance Analytics	The system must provide analytics on claims adjuster workload distribution and individual performance metrics. Workload metrics must include the number of active claims per adjuster, total incurred value of assigned claims, number of new claims assigned in the current period, and claims closed in the current period. Performance metrics must include average claim cycle time, settlement ratio (amount settled versus amount claimed), customer satisfaction scores from claimant feedback, and adherence to documentation and investigation timelines. The system must identify workload imbalances where some adjusters carry significantly more claims than others and recommend redistribution. Performance benchmarking must compare adjusters within the same entity and across entities, controlling for claim complexity differences. The system must generate monthly adjuster performance reports for supervisory review, displaying results in the supervisor's preferred language (Arabic or English) with rankings and trend indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R012	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Container Type and Equipment Claims Analysis	The system must provide specialized analytics for claims segmented by container type and equipment category. Analysis must cover standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RF, 40HCRF), open top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat rack containers (20FR, 40FR), and tank containers (20TK). The system must identify which container types have the highest claims frequency and severity at each port, enabling targeted equipment improvement programs. Reefer container analysis must include temperature deviation claims correlated with unit age, manufacturer, and maintenance history. The system must track claims related to container structural defects versus operational damage, supporting fleet management decisions on container retirement and replacement cycles. Analysis must include container repositioning damage rates for empty containers moved between the four operational ports. All charts and tables must support Arabic and English labels with proper numeral formatting per the selected interface language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R013	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Insurance and Reinsurance Reporting Module	The system must generate reports specifically formatted for insurance underwriters and reinsurance treaty reporting requirements. Reports must include loss ratio calculations by underwriting year, premium sufficiency analysis, large loss listings (claims exceeding a configurable threshold per entity), and aggregate loss development triangles. The system must support bordereau reporting in formats required by major marine insurance markets including London, Singapore, and Dubai International Financial Centre. Treaty reinsurance reports must show ceded claims by layer, tracking excess-of-loss and quota share arrangements per entity. The system must calculate and report maximum probable loss (MPL) and probable maximum loss (PML) scenarios for catastrophe reinsurance treaties based on port concentration risk and natural catastrophe exposure at each of the four operational ports. Reports must be generatable in both Arabic and English with all monetary amounts displayed in USD as the common reinsurance reporting currency alongside the entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R014	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Custom Report Builder with Drag-and-Drop Interface	The system must provide a custom report builder allowing claims managers to create ad-hoc analytical reports without requiring IT support. The interface must support drag-and-drop selection of data dimensions (entity, port, claim type, commodity, carrier, time period, adjuster, container type, INCOTERMS) and measures (claim count, incurred amount, paid amount, reserve amount, recovery amount, cycle time). Users must be able to apply filters, define groupings, and select visualization types including bar charts, line charts, pie charts, scatter plots, and data tables. Created reports must be saveable as templates for recurring use and shareable with other users based on role-based access permissions. The report builder must support both Gregorian and Hijri date dimensions for time-based analysis. The interface must function correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR modes with all field labels, filter options, and chart legends rendered in the selected language. Generated reports must be exportable to PDF, Excel, CSV, and PowerPoint formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R015	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Automated Scheduled Report Distribution	The system must support automated scheduling and distribution of claims analytics reports to configured recipient lists. Reports must be schedulable at daily, weekly, monthly, and quarterly frequencies with delivery options including email attachment, secure portal upload, and SFTP delivery. Each scheduled report must support recipient-specific configurations including preferred language (Arabic or English), preferred currency for monetary values, and preferred date format (Gregorian, Hijri, or dual). The system must support conditional report generation where reports are only produced and distributed when certain thresholds are met, for example a daily alert report only when new high-value claims exceed AED 100,000 or equivalent. Distribution lists must be configurable per entity with role-based subscriptions. Report delivery must include a cover page summary with key metric highlights and period-over-period changes. Failed deliveries must be retried three times and then escalated to the system administrator. The system must maintain a log of all report distributions with recipient, delivery timestamp, and delivery status.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R011	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lease Agreement Document Management	The system must store and manage all documents associated with each lease agreement including signed contracts, addenda, rate schedules, depot lists, insurance certificates, and correspondence. Documents must be uploadable in PDF, DOCX, and image formats with a maximum individual file size of 25 MB. The system must support Arabic RTL document rendering and bilingual document generation. AI-powered OCR must extract key terms from uploaded scanned agreements and pre-populate agreement fields for user verification. Each document must have version control, retention policy tagging per regulatory jurisdiction, and access control based on user role and entity affiliation. Full-text search across all stored documents must be available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R016	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Regulatory and Compliance Analytics Reporting	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports required by maritime authorities and insurance regulators in each operational jurisdiction. For Qatar, reports must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA) requirements for claims reporting. For UAE, reports must meet Insurance Authority and CBUAE regulatory reporting standards. For KSA, reports must align with Saudi Central Bank (SAMA) insurance reporting requirements and the Council of Cooperative Health Insurance guidelines where applicable. For India, reports must comply with Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) claims reporting formats and the Directorate General of Shipping requirements. The system must track regulatory submission deadlines with automated reminders at 30, 15, and 5 days before due dates. Reports must be generated in the jurisdiction's required language (Arabic for GCC, English for India, or bilingual where required). The system must maintain an archive of all regulatory submissions with version control and submission acknowledgment records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-043-2-004-R017	FEAT-043-2-004	MOD-043	Data Export and Integration with Business Intelligence Tools	The system must provide comprehensive data export capabilities and integration interfaces for external business intelligence platforms. The system must support real-time data streaming via Apache Kafka or equivalent message broker to enterprise data warehouses and data lakes. Direct database connectivity must be available through read-only replica access with row-level security enforcing entity-based data isolation. The system must provide pre-built connectors or compatible data models for popular BI tools including Power BI, Tableau, and Qlik Sense. Data export APIs must support OData and GraphQL query interfaces with pagination for large datasets. Exported data must include all claim dimensions and measures with proper multi-currency support, Arabic text fields preserved in UTF-8 encoding, and Hijri date fields alongside Gregorian dates. Data export must respect role-based access controls ensuring users can only export data they are authorized to view within their entity scope. API access must be authenticated via OAuth 2.0 with rate limiting to prevent excessive resource consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R003	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lease Agreement Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for new lease agreements and amendments. The workflow must support at minimum three approval levels: operations manager, finance controller, and regional director. Each approval level must have configurable monetary thresholds in the entity's base currency, with automatic currency conversion for cross-entity agreements. The system must send bilingual notifications (Arabic and English) to approvers with full agreement details. Approval must be achievable via mobile interface. If an approver does not act within the configured SLA period (default 48 hours), the system must escalate to the next level and log the escalation event.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R004	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lease Renewal and Extension Processing	The system must automatically identify lease agreements approaching expiry within a configurable notice window (default 90 days) and trigger renewal workflows. AI must analyze current fleet utilization, projected trade demand, prevailing market per-diem rates, and container condition reports to generate a renewal recommendation with a confidence score. The recommendation must indicate whether to renew at current terms, renegotiate rates, reduce fleet size, or terminate and restitute containers. The system must support partial renewals where only a subset of container types or quantities under the MLA are renewed. All renewal decisions must be auditable with timestamps and the user or AI agent that initiated them.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R006	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Termination and Restitution Tracking	The system must manage the lease termination process including notice generation, restitution scheduling, and final settlement calculation. Upon termination initiation, the system must calculate the total number of containers to be restituted by type and location, cross-referencing current container positions at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). The system must generate a restitution plan optimizing drop-off at the nearest lessor-designated depot to minimize repositioning costs. AI must estimate the total termination cost including outstanding per-diem, repositioning expenses, pending MNR charges, and early termination penalties if the minimum lease duration has not been met.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R007	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lessor Master Data Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive lessor master registry with fields for legal entity name, registration country, tax identification numbers (VAT/GST), banking details for payment processing, primary and secondary contact persons, and communication preferences (email, EDI, API). Each lessor record must support multiple depot locations worldwide with UNLOC codes and GPS coordinates. The system must track lessor credit ratings and historical dispute resolution performance metrics. Integration with external lessor portals (e.g., ContainerFax, xChange) must be supported via configurable API endpoints. All lessor data must comply with data residency requirements specific to Qatar PDPL, UAE Federal Data Protection Law, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R009	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Free Day and Detention Clause Management	The system must record and enforce free-day allowances per lease agreement, specifying the number of combined or split free days at origin and destination. When a container exceeds the free-day allowance, the system must automatically calculate detention charges at the agreed contractual rate. Detention calculations must account for port-specific holidays (including Islamic holidays in Qatar, UAE, and KSA), weekends (Friday-Saturday in GCC, Saturday-Sunday in India), and force majeure periods. The system must distinguish between shipper-caused and carrier-caused detention and apply the correct billing party. AI must predict detention risk at the time of booking based on historical port dwell times and consignee pickup patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R010	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Multi-Currency Lease Financial Terms	The system must support lease agreements denominated in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR with the ability to specify settlement currency independently from the agreement currency. Currency conversion must use configurable exchange rate sources: Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, or RBI reference rates. The system must handle exchange rate fluctuation by supporting both fixed-rate agreements (locked at signing) and floating-rate agreements (converted at billing date). Each invoice line item must show both the original agreement currency amount and the settlement currency equivalent. The system must calculate and report foreign exchange gains or losses for each lease agreement per accounting period for IFRS 16 compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R012	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lease Portfolio Dashboard and Analytics	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying the complete lease portfolio across all entities, showing total leased TEU by lessor, by container type, by operating entity, and by status (active, expiring within 90 days, terminated). The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts. Financial summaries must show total monthly per-diem obligations, year-to-date lease expenditure versus budget, and projected annual lease cost. AI must generate alerts for agreements with unfavorable terms compared to current market benchmarks. The dashboard must support drill-down from portfolio level to individual agreement level and must export data in Excel and PDF formats with bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R013	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	IFRS 16 Lease Liability and ROU Asset Calculation	The system must automatically compute right-of-use (ROU) asset values and lease liabilities for each lease agreement in compliance with IFRS 16 Leases. Calculations must use the incremental borrowing rate configured per entity, apply the appropriate discount factor, and amortize the ROU asset on a straight-line basis over the lease term. The system must handle lease modifications including scope changes, term extensions, and rate adjustments by recalculating the liability at the modification date. Short-term leases (under 12 months) and low-value container leases must be eligible for the IFRS 16 recognition exemption. Monthly journal entries for depreciation, interest expense, and lease payment must be auto-generated and posted to the integrated general ledger module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R014	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Offline Lease Agreement Access and Sync	The system must allow users to view and create lease agreement drafts in offline mode when network connectivity is unavailable, particularly relevant for operations at remote depots or during vessel-side inspections at Hamad Port or JNPT. Offline-created drafts must be stored locally on the user device with AES-256 encryption and automatically synchronized to the central server upon connectivity restoration. Conflict resolution must follow a last-writer-wins policy with full conflict logging for manual review. The system must queue approval workflow triggers for offline-created agreements and process them in order upon sync. Maximum offline data retention must be configurable per entity security policy with a default of 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R015	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	AI-Driven Lease Optimization Recommendations	The system must employ AI models to continuously analyze the lease portfolio and generate optimization recommendations. The AI must evaluate fleet utilization by comparing leased container availability against booking demand forecasts per trade lane covering routes between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT, and connecting hub ports. Recommendations must include: rebalancing leased containers between entities to reduce repositioning, terminating underutilized leases, consolidating multiple spot leases into a single MLA, and timing renewals to align with seasonal demand patterns. Each recommendation must include projected cost savings in USD with confidence intervals, payback period, and implementation steps. The system must track recommendation acceptance rates and refine its models accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-001-R016	FEAT-044-1-001	MOD-044	Lease Agreement Compliance and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every action performed on a lease agreement including creation, viewing, editing, approval, rejection, renewal, amendment, and termination. Each audit entry must capture the user ID, timestamp in UTC, IP address, entity context, action type, and a structured before-and-after snapshot of changed data. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. The system must support compliance reporting for Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA), UAE Federal Tax Authority (FTA), Saudi ZATCA, and Indian GST authorities. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with the most stringent applicable jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R001	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	On-Hire Event Registration with Container Details	The system must record on-hire events when a leased container is picked up from a lessor depot or port facility. The on-hire record must capture container number (ISO 6346 format with check digit validation), container type and size code (ISO 6346 size/type), manufacturing year, tare weight, maximum gross weight, CSC plate expiry date, on-hire date and time, pickup location (UNLOC code), and the associated lease agreement reference. The system must validate that the container number is not already active on another lease or owned fleet record. AI must auto-detect container details from uploaded photos of the CSC plate or door-end markings using computer vision and populate the form fields for user confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R002	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Off-Hire Event Registration and Settlement	The system must record off-hire events when a leased container is returned to a lessor-designated depot. The off-hire record must capture return date and time, drop-off location (UNLOC code), container condition grade (A through E per IICL standards), and any noted damages referencing specific container components (panels, rails, floor, doors, refrigeration unit). The system must automatically calculate the final per-diem charges from on-hire date to off-hire date, deducting any pre-paid periods. Upon off-hire completion, the system must generate a settlement statement showing total per-diem, accrued detention if any, and pending MNR damage charges, presented in the agreement currency and the entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R003	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) Management	The system must generate and manage Equipment Interchange Receipts for every on-hire and off-hire event. The EIR must include a graphical container damage diagram following the IICL damage notation standard, with the ability to mark specific damage locations using a touch-enabled interface on tablets. Both the driver or trucker and the depot inspector must digitally sign the EIR. The system must store EIR documents in both Arabic and English with all mandatory fields bilingual. AI must compare on-hire and off-hire EIRs for the same container to automatically identify new damages incurred during the lease period and flag them for MNR processing. EIRs must be shareable with lessors via API or email in PDF format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R004	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Real-Time Leased Container Inventory Tracking	The system must maintain a real-time inventory of all leased containers showing their current status: on-hire at depot, in transit, at port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), loaded on vessel, at consignee premises, or awaiting off-hire. The inventory must be filterable by lessor, lease agreement, container type, size, age, entity, and geographic region. The system must integrate with terminal operating systems (TOS) at each port to receive gate-in and gate-out events that update container positions. Total leased TEU must be reconcilable against lease agreement quantities at any point in time. AI must predict containers approaching their minimum lease duration end date and flag those eligible for off-hire to optimize fleet cost.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R005	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Depot Network Integration and Management	The system must maintain a registry of all lessor-designated pickup and drop-off depots with their UNLOC codes, physical addresses, GPS coordinates, operating hours, accepted container types, and contact details. Integration with third-party depot operators (such as Leschaco, TCI, Universal Container Services) must be supported via EDI messages (COPARN, CODECO) or REST API. The system must track depot acceptance capacity and notify operations when a depot approaches full capacity for a specific container type. For the GCC region, depot operating hours must reflect Thursday half-day and Friday closures where applicable. The system must recommend the nearest available depot for off-hire based on container current position and lessor depot network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R006	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	On-Hire Container Condition Inspection	The system must support a structured on-hire inspection workflow where depot or port inspectors evaluate the container condition against IICL standards before accepting it into the operating fleet. The inspection checklist must cover structural integrity (walls, roof, floor, corner posts), door operation and gasket condition, interior cleanliness, cargo-worthiness certification status, and for reefer containers, refrigeration unit operational test results including set-point accuracy and defrost cycle timing. The system must prevent on-hire acceptance of containers failing critical inspection criteria without a manager override. AI must analyze inspection photos to automatically detect and classify damage types (dents, holes, corrosion, bent components) and assign preliminary severity ratings for inspector review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R007	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Bulk On-Hire and Off-Hire Processing	The system must support bulk processing of on-hire and off-hire events when large batches of containers are picked up or returned simultaneously, such as during a fleet expansion or lease termination at a major depot near Jebel Ali or JNPT. Users must be able to upload a CSV or Excel file containing container numbers, dates, and condition grades for batch processing. The system must validate each container number for ISO 6346 compliance, check for duplicates within the batch and against existing records, and report validation errors inline without rejecting the entire batch. Successfully processed containers must immediately update the lease inventory and trigger per-diem calculation. The system must support processing batches of up to 500 containers per upload.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R008	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Interchange Event Photo and Video Documentation	The system must allow inspectors to capture and attach photographic and video evidence to each on-hire and off-hire event. A minimum of four photos (front, back, left side, right side) must be required for each event, with optional additional photos for specific damage areas. Photos must be geotagged with GPS coordinates and timestamped to prove they were taken at the declared depot or port location. The system must support photo capture directly from the mobile app camera or upload from device gallery. Maximum photo resolution must be configurable with a default of 12 megapixels, and videos must be limited to 60 seconds per clip. AI must automatically categorize photos by container face and flag any that appear blurry, underexposed, or not showing the declared container.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R009	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Per-Diem Accrual Calculation Engine	The system must calculate per-diem charges daily for every on-hire container based on the applicable rate from the lease agreement. The calculation engine must handle rate changes mid-period by prorating charges at the old rate up to the effective date and the new rate from the effective date forward. Free-day allowances must be deducted before per-diem charges commence. The engine must support different rate tiers based on container age at the on-hire date and must apply the correct rate even if the container ages into a new tier during the lease period. Accruals must be computed in the agreement currency and converted to each entity's functional currency using the month-end exchange rate. The system must generate a monthly per-diem accrual summary per lessor, per entity, and per container type for financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R010	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Leased Container Movement History	The system must maintain a complete movement history for each leased container from on-hire to off-hire, recording every location change including gate-in and gate-out at ports, vessel loading and discharge events, rail and truck movements, and dwell time at each location. The movement history must integrate data from terminal operating systems, vessel tracking feeds (AIS), and inland transport management modules. Each movement record must include timestamp, location UNLOC code, movement type, and associated document references (bill of lading, delivery order). The movement history must be viewable on an interactive map with route visualization. AI must analyze movement patterns to identify containers that have been idle for more than a configurable threshold (default 14 days) and flag them for potential off-hire or repositioning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R011	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Lessor Portal Integration for Event Synchronization	The system must integrate with major lessor portals and platforms (Triton, Textainer, Beacon, SeaCube, CAI) to synchronize on-hire and off-hire events bidirectionally. When an on-hire or off-hire event is recorded in the ERP, the system must automatically transmit the event details to the lessor's platform via their supported integration method (API, EDI CODECO, or email with structured attachment). Conversely, events initiated by the lessor (such as pickup authorizations or off-hire confirmations) must be received and reflected in the ERP. The system must reconcile event data between both systems and flag discrepancies in dates, locations, or container conditions for manual review. Failed transmission attempts must be queued and retried with exponential backoff up to 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R012	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	On-Hire Off-Hire Event Notifications and Alerts	The system must generate configurable notifications for on-hire and off-hire events to relevant stakeholders. Notifications must be sent via email, SMS, and in-app push notification. Operations teams must be notified when containers are on-hired to plan equipment allocation. Finance teams must be notified of off-hire events to trigger final settlement processing. Lessors must receive automated event confirmations. Notifications must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the recipient's preferred language used as primary. The system must alert when on-hire volumes exceed the contracted maximum quantity under an MLA, when off-hire volumes suggest lease underutilization, or when containers are on-hire at non-designated depots. AI must predict upcoming off-hire surges based on seasonal trade patterns and notify logistics teams to arrange depot capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R013	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Reefer Container On-Hire Special Handling	The system must enforce additional on-hire inspection and documentation requirements for refrigerated containers (20RF, 40RF, 40HCRF). The reefer pre-trip inspection (PTI) results must be recorded including compressor operation, thermostat calibration, defrost timer settings, refrigerant gas type and charge level, power cable condition, and data logger download. The system must record the reefer unit manufacturer, model, and serial number (Carrier, Thermo King, Daikin, Star Cool). Containers failing PTI must be flagged as not cargo-worthy and must not be allocated to bookings until repairs are completed and a re-inspection is passed. The system must track reefer unit operating hours at on-hire for warranty and maintenance scheduling. AI must predict PTI failure probability based on unit age, manufacturer, and historical failure data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R014	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Cross-Entity Container Transfer Tracking	The system must track transfers of leased containers between entities, for example when a container on-hired by the Qatar entity is operationally moved to the UAE entity for a Jebel Ali export. The transfer must record the sending entity, receiving entity, transfer date, reason, and any inter-company cost allocation. The per-diem cost must be split between entities based on the number of days each entity held the container. The lease agreement must specify whether cross-entity transfers are permitted, and the system must enforce this restriction. Transfer events must generate inter-company accounting entries in both entities' books. The system must reconcile cross-entity transfers monthly to ensure no container is claimed by multiple entities simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R015	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	Historical Event Analytics and Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on on-hire and off-hire events including average lease duration by container type, average turnaround time between off-hire and re-on-hire, on-hire and off-hire volumes by month and by depot, and container utilization rate as a percentage of total leased days versus laden days. Reports must be filterable by entity, lessor, container type, port, and date range with support for both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date selection. The system must generate trend analysis charts showing lease portfolio growth or reduction over time. AI must identify seasonal patterns in on-hire and off-hire activity and recommend proactive fleet adjustments. All reports must be exportable in Excel, PDF, and CSV formats with bilingual column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-002-R016	FEAT-044-1-002	MOD-044	On-Hire Off-Hire Dispute Management	The system must support a dispute workflow for contested on-hire or off-hire events where the lessor and lessee disagree on event dates, container conditions, or applicable charges. Disputes must be logged with a unique dispute reference number, category (date dispute, condition dispute, location dispute, rate dispute), supporting evidence attachments, and escalation level. The system must track dispute aging and enforce resolution SLAs configurable per lessor agreement. Communication threads between the lessee and lessor regarding each dispute must be maintained within the system. AI must analyze historical dispute outcomes to predict resolution likelihood and recommended settlement amounts. Unresolved disputes exceeding 90 days must automatically escalate to senior management with a summary brief.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R001	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Damage Report Creation from Off-Hire Inspection	The system must allow inspectors to create detailed damage reports during off-hire inspections, documenting each damage item with the component affected (using IICL component codes), damage type (dent, hole, cut, corrosion, missing part), damage location on the container using a standardized grid system, severity classification, and dimensional measurements (length, width, depth in millimeters). Each damage item must be linked to photographic evidence. The system must distinguish between pre-existing damages noted on the on-hire EIR and new damages incurred during the lease period by comparing against the on-hire condition record. Only new damages must be included in the MNR claim. AI must auto-detect damage from off-hire photos and pre-populate damage report fields for inspector validation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R002	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	IICL Repair Estimate Generation	The system must generate repair cost estimates following IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) repair standards and pricing guidelines. The estimate must calculate material costs based on standard component pricing, labor hours based on repair complexity classification, and applicable shop rates at the repair depot location. The system must maintain an updatable IICL price list with regional variations for GCC (covering depots near Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali) and South Asia (covering depots near JNPT). Estimates must be generated in the currency of the lease agreement and must include separate line items for labor, materials, and any hazardous material handling surcharges. AI must optimize repair estimates by recommending the most cost-effective repair method while maintaining container cargo-worthiness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R003	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Lessor Repair Authorization Workflow	The system must submit MNR damage reports and repair estimates to the lessor for authorization before repairs are commenced. The authorization request must be transmittable via the lessor's preferred channel (API integration, email, or lessor portal upload). The system must track authorization status (submitted, under review, approved, partially approved, rejected) with timestamps for each status change. If the lessor partially approves or disputes specific damage items, the system must allow item-level acceptance or re-submission with additional evidence. The system must enforce a configurable authorization timeout (default 5 business days) after which repairs may proceed under a pre-authorized threshold amount defined in the lease agreement. All authorization communications must be logged for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R004	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Third-Party Repair Vendor Management	The system must maintain a registry of approved third-party repair vendors at each depot and port location, storing vendor name, capabilities (structural repair, reefer repair, tank container repair), certifications, insurance coverage, agreed labor rates, and historical quality performance metrics. The system must support vendor selection based on repair type, location proximity to the container, and vendor performance score. Purchase orders for repair work must be generated and tracked through the system with line items matching the approved MNR estimate. Upon repair completion, the vendor must submit actual costs which the system compares against the approved estimate, flagging variances exceeding a configurable threshold (default 10%). AI must recommend the optimal vendor for each repair job based on cost, quality history, and current workload capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R005	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Invoice Generation and Submission	The system must generate MNR invoices to bill the lessor for approved repair costs. Each invoice must reference the lease agreement number, container number, off-hire event reference, damage report number, and repair authorization number. Invoice line items must detail each damage repair with IICL component code, repair description, labor cost, material cost, and total per item. The invoice must comply with the tax requirements of the issuing entity: VAT at the applicable rate for Qatar (currently 0%), UAE (5%), KSA (15%), or GST for India (18% on repair services). The invoice must be generateable in both Arabic and English and must be submittable to the lessor via their preferred channel. The system must track invoice aging and payment status with automated reminders for overdue invoices exceeding 30, 60, and 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R006	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Damage Liability Determination Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable rules engine to determine damage liability between the lessee (operating company), the lessor, and third parties (truckers, terminal operators, consignees). Liability rules must consider the damage type, location on the container, container age, normal wear and tear allowances per IICL guidelines, and any special clauses in the lease agreement. The rules engine must distinguish between fair wear and tear (lessor's responsibility), operational damage (lessee's responsibility), and third-party damage (recoverable from the responsible party). For damages attributed to terminal operators at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT, the system must generate a separate damage recovery claim. AI must learn from historical liability determinations and dispute outcomes to improve automated classification accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R007	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Cost Accrual and Provisioning	The system must automatically accrue estimated MNR costs as a financial provision when damage reports are created, before the actual repair invoice is received. The accrual must be booked in the entity's functional currency using the exchange rate on the damage report date. When the actual repair invoice is processed, the system must reverse the accrual and book the actual cost, recognizing any variance as a gain or loss. Monthly MNR accrual reports must show the total provisioned amount by lessor, by entity, and by container type with aging analysis. The system must maintain a historical provision accuracy metric and use AI to refine future accrual estimates based on actual repair costs for similar damage types and locations. Provisions must be separately trackable for each operating entity for consolidated financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R008	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Dispute Resolution Tracking	The system must support a structured dispute resolution process for MNR claims where the lessor rejects or partially approves repair charges. Each dispute must be logged with the disputed damage items, lessor's rejection reason, lessee's counter-evidence, and proposed resolution. The system must track dispute through stages: initial submission, lessor response, negotiation, escalation, and settlement. Communication history between the parties must be maintained with timestamps and document attachments. The system must calculate the financial impact of each open dispute showing the potential write-off if the dispute is lost. AI must analyze dispute patterns by lessor, damage type, and depot to identify recurring issues and recommend process improvements. Settlement agreements must be recorded with the final agreed amount and any credit notes generated for the difference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R009	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Automated Damage Comparison Between On-Hire and Off-Hire	The system must automatically compare the container condition recorded at on-hire with the condition recorded at off-hire to identify damages incurred during the lease period. The comparison must operate at the individual damage item level using IICL component codes and damage locations to match pre-existing versus new damages. Damages recorded on the on-hire EIR must be excluded from MNR claims even if they have worsened during the lease period, unless the worsening exceeds IICL acceptable deterioration thresholds. The system must generate a comparison report highlighting new damages with their estimated repair costs. AI must use image comparison between on-hire and off-hire photos of the same container locations to identify damages that may have been missed during manual inspection, achieving a minimum 90% accuracy rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R010	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Repair Quality Verification	The system must support a post-repair inspection workflow to verify that MNR repairs have been completed to the required standard before the container is returned to service. The verification checklist must confirm structural integrity restoration, paint and markings compliance, door seal and gasket functionality, and for reefer units, full operational testing post-repair. Failed verifications must trigger rework orders to the repair vendor at no additional cost. The system must track first-time-pass rates for each repair vendor as a quality metric. Photos of completed repairs must be captured and stored alongside the original damage photos for comparison. The system must prevent a container from being allocated to bookings until the post-repair inspection is passed. AI must verify repair quality from post-repair photos by comparing against the original damage images and known good-condition reference images.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R011	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Cost Recovery from Third Parties	The system must support the process of recovering MNR costs from third parties responsible for container damage, including trucking companies, terminal operators at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, freight forwarders, and consignees. The system must generate damage recovery invoices or debit notes addressed to the responsible third party with supporting evidence including damage photos, inspection reports, and repair estimates. Recovery claims must be tracked separately from lessor MNR billing with their own aging and follow-up workflows. The system must calculate the net MNR cost position showing total repair costs minus lessor billing minus third-party recoveries for each period. AI must assess the likelihood of successful recovery based on the third party's historical payment behavior and the strength of the evidence, recommending whether to pursue or write off low-probability claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R012	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Analytics and Trend Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on MNR activity including total damage frequency by container type, most common damage types across the fleet, average repair cost per container per year, MNR cost as a percentage of total lease cost, and damage rates by trade lane. Reports must be filterable by entity, lessor, container type, depot, port, and time period with support for Hijri calendar date ranges. The system must generate Pareto charts showing the top damage categories contributing to the highest costs. Comparative analysis between lessors must be available to inform future leasing decisions. AI must identify anomalous damage patterns such as unusually high damage rates on specific trade lanes or at specific depots and generate investigative alerts. Reports must support Arabic RTL layout and bilingual export in Excel and PDF formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R013	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Reefer Unit Specific MNR Processing	The system must handle reefer container MNR separately from dry container MNR due to the specialized nature and higher cost of refrigeration unit repairs. Reefer MNR must include damage categories specific to the refrigeration system: compressor failure, condenser damage, evaporator coil leak, electronic controller malfunction, power cable damage, and refrigerant leak. The system must track reefer unit manufacturer warranty status (Carrier PrimeLINE, Thermo King Magnum Plus, Daikin, Star Cool) and apply warranty coverage to reduce MNR costs where applicable. Repair estimates for reefer units must use manufacturer-specific parts pricing rather than generic IICL rates. The system must maintain a log of reefer unit operating hours between on-hire and off-hire to correlate with damage claims. AI must predict reefer unit component failure probability based on operating hours, ambient temperature exposure history, and maintenance records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R014	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Budget Management and Forecasting	The system must support MNR budget planning and monitoring per entity, per lessor, and per container type. Budget allocation must be definable at annual and quarterly levels in each entity's functional currency. The system must track actual MNR expenditure against budget in real-time and generate variance reports. When actual spending exceeds a configurable budget threshold (default 80%), the system must alert the operations and finance managers. AI must forecast future MNR costs based on the current lease portfolio composition, container age distribution, historical damage rates, and trade lane damage profiles. The forecast must be updated monthly with a rolling 12-month projection showing expected MNR spend by category. Budget versus actual reports must be available in consolidated multi-entity view with currency conversion to a common reporting currency (default USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R015	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Photographic Evidence Chain of Custody	The system must maintain a secure chain of custody for all photographic evidence used in MNR claims to ensure admissibility in disputes and legal proceedings. Each photo must be cryptographically hashed at the time of capture with the hash value stored separately from the image. The system must record the capture device identifier, photographer user ID, GPS coordinates, timestamp, and any subsequent viewing or download events. Photos must not be editable once submitted as part of an MNR claim; any corrections require uploading a new photo with an explanation for the replacement. The system must detect and flag photos that appear to have been digitally manipulated using AI-based forensic analysis. Access to MNR evidence must be restricted by role, with view-only access for lessors and full access for authorized internal users only.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R016	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	Bulk MNR Claim Processing and Reconciliation	The system must support bulk processing of MNR claims when multiple containers are off-hired simultaneously, such as during a lease termination event involving hundreds of containers at a single depot near Jebel Ali or JNPT. The bulk processing interface must allow inspectors to batch-upload damage reports with container-specific damage items and photos. The system must generate consolidated MNR estimates and authorization requests to the lessor, grouped by damage category for efficient review. Upon lessor response, the system must split the consolidated authorization into individual container-level records. Reconciliation between the bulk MNR claim and individual repair invoices must be automated with variance reporting. The system must handle partial authorizations at both the batch level and individual container level without blocking the processing of approved items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-003-R017	FEAT-044-1-003	MOD-044	MNR Credit Note Processing from Lessors	The system must process credit notes received from lessors for MNR charges that were billed but subsequently reversed or reduced. Credit notes must be matched against the original MNR invoice using reference numbers and must reduce the outstanding receivable balance. The system must support partial credits where only some line items or amounts from the original invoice are credited. Each credit note must be recorded with the lessor's credit note number, date, currency, amount, and reason code (dispute settlement, goodwill, warranty claim, billing error). Currency conversion must apply the exchange rate from the credit note date. The system must update the MNR cost reports and accrual balances to reflect credit notes received. AI must flag unusual credit note patterns that may indicate systematic billing errors requiring process review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R001	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Per-Diem Cost Allocation to Bookings	The system must allocate daily per-diem lease costs to individual bookings based on the number of days each leased container is assigned to a specific booking. The allocation must span the full container usage period from pickup at origin depot through delivery to consignee and return to the depot or next assignment. When a container is used for consecutive bookings without an intervening off-hire, the idle days between bookings must be allocated to the trade lane of the subsequent booking or to a configurable overhead cost center. The allocation must use the per-diem rate from the applicable lease agreement and convert to the booking's revenue currency for margin analysis. The system must handle split-voyage scenarios where a container is transhipped at a hub port with the per-diem allocated proportionally across legs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R002	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Trade Lane Definition and Mapping	The system must maintain a configurable trade lane master registry defining each trade lane by origin port or port range, destination port or port range, direction (headhaul or backhaul), and trade region. Trade lanes must support multi-port definitions covering port clusters such as Arabian Gulf (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA) and Indian Subcontinent (JNPT INNSA, Mundra INMUN, Pipavav INPAV). Each booking must be automatically mapped to a trade lane based on its load port and discharge port. The system must support hierarchical trade lane groupings for consolidated reporting (e.g., Middle East to Far East, Intra-Gulf). AI must suggest new trade lane definitions when booking patterns indicate significant volume on routes not covered by existing trade lane mappings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R003	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Configurable Cost Allocation Methodology	The system must support multiple cost allocation methodologies selectable per entity or per reporting period. Supported methods must include: direct allocation (costs traced directly to the container and booking), weighted average (costs distributed across all bookings using a weighting factor such as TEU-days), activity-based costing (costs allocated based on the number of activities like lifts, moves, and dwell days attributable to each trade lane), and standard costing (using pre-determined per-TEU rates per trade lane). Each methodology must be fully documented with its calculation formula stored in the system. Users must be able to run parallel allocation simulations using different methodologies to compare results before committing. AI must recommend the most accurate methodology based on the entity's operational profile and data availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R004	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Repositioning Cost Allocation	The system must allocate the cost of repositioning empty leased containers to the trade lanes that create the demand for repositioning. The system must identify repositioning moves as any movement of an empty leased container from its off-hire or empty return location to a pickup location for the next laden booking. The full repositioning cost, including inland trucking, vessel slot cost for empty movement, port handling, and any associated per-diem during transit, must be calculated and allocated. The allocation may be to the trade lane that generated the imbalance (surplus at discharge port) or to the trade lane that benefits from the repositioned container (booking at load port). The system must support configurable allocation rules for how repositioning costs are attributed. AI must optimize repositioning routes to minimize cost and suggest triangulation opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R005	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	MNR Cost Allocation to Trade Lanes	The system must allocate MNR (Maintenance and Repair) costs to the trade lane on which the damage occurred. When a damage is attributable to a specific booking or voyage (e.g., cargo damage to container floor), the MNR cost must be allocated directly to that booking's trade lane. When damage cannot be attributed to a specific event (e.g., general wear-related corrosion), the MNR cost must be allocated proportionally across all trade lanes that the container operated on during the lease period, weighted by the number of days on each trade lane. The allocation must use the MNR cost in the entity's functional currency. The system must distinguish between recoverable MNR costs (billed to lessor or third party) and net MNR costs (borne by the lessee) for trade lane profitability purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R006	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Multi-Entity Cost Consolidation	The system must consolidate lease costs across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for group-level trade lane profitability analysis. Each entity's costs must be converted to a common reporting currency (configurable, default USD) using the period-end exchange rate from the applicable central bank. The consolidation must eliminate inter-company transfers where a container leased by one entity operates in another entity's trade to avoid double counting. The system must present both entity-level and consolidated group-level cost views. Drill-down from consolidated figures to individual entity details must be seamless. The system must handle scenarios where the same trade lane (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT) is operated by multiple entities, keeping costs separate unless explicit consolidation is requested. AI must flag inter-entity cost allocation imbalances that may indicate suboptimal container sharing arrangements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R007	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Voyage-Level Cost Attribution	The system must attribute lease costs at the individual voyage level, linking per-diem charges, proportional MNR costs, and handling charges to the specific vessel voyage on which the leased container was carried. The attribution must use the vessel voyage reference number and actual vessel schedule dates to determine the per-diem days attributable to each voyage. For containers carried on multiple voyage legs (e.g., feeder vessel from Hamad Port to Jebel Ali then mother vessel from Jebel Ali to destination), costs must be split across each leg proportionally by days. The system must integrate with the voyage management module to pull vessel schedule data and sailing dates. Voyage-level cost reports must support comparison against voyage revenue to calculate voyage-level contribution margins using leased container costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R008	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Idle Container Cost Allocation	The system must separately track and allocate the per-diem costs incurred during periods when leased containers are idle (not assigned to any booking or voyage). Idle periods must be categorized by reason: awaiting booking assignment, undergoing repair, in repositioning transit, quarantine or customs hold, or no demand. The system must allocate idle costs to trade lanes using configurable rules: proportional to booking volume by trade lane, allocated to the trade lane that last used the container, or charged to a central fleet overhead account. The system must generate an idle container cost report showing total idle days, idle cost, and idle rate (idle days as percentage of total leased days) by container type and by trade lane. AI must identify containers with excessive idle time and recommend actions such as off-hire, repositioning, or re-allocation to higher-demand trades.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R009	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Budget vs Actual Lease Cost Analysis by Trade	The system must support comparison of budgeted versus actual lease costs at the trade lane level. Budgets must be definable at annual and quarterly intervals per trade lane per entity in the entity's functional currency. Actual lease costs must be captured in real-time from per-diem accruals, MNR settlements, and repositioning allocations. The system must compute variances in absolute terms and as a percentage, highlighting unfavorable variances exceeding a configurable threshold (default 5%). Variance analysis must decompose the total variance into volume variance (more or fewer containers than budgeted), rate variance (higher or lower per-diem than budgeted), and mix variance (different container type mix than budgeted). AI must generate variance explanations by correlating lease cost deviations with operational events such as trade lane volume changes, new lease agreements, or vessel schedule disruptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R010	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Standard Cost Rate Maintenance	The system must maintain standard lease cost rates per TEU per trade lane for use in booking quotation and pricing processes. Standard rates must be reviewed and updated periodically (default quarterly) based on the latest actual cost data. The standard rate must incorporate per-diem cost, average MNR cost per TEU, estimated repositioning cost, and a configurable overhead allocation percentage. Separate standard rates must be maintained for each container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF) on each trade lane. The system must support rate approval workflows where proposed standard rate changes require finance manager sign-off. AI must recommend standard rates based on historical actual costs, current lease agreement terms, and projected trade volumes, flagging trade lanes where the current standard rate deviates from actual costs by more than a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R011	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Customer Profitability Impact Analysis	The system must enable analysis of how leased container costs impact customer-level profitability. For each customer booking, the system must calculate the total allocated lease cost (per-diem, MNR, repositioning) and compare it against the freight revenue to determine the customer-level contribution margin. The analysis must account for demurrage and detention charges collected from the customer as revenue offsets against lease detention costs. Customer profitability reports must aggregate across all bookings for a customer within a period, showing the total lease cost component. The system must support multi-currency analysis, converting all figures to the reporting currency. AI must identify customers whose booking patterns (long dwell times, remote delivery locations, high damage rates) disproportionately increase lease costs and recommend pricing adjustments or operational changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R012	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Allocation Adjustment and Manual Override	The system must allow finance users to manually adjust automated cost allocations when the default methodology produces incorrect or misleading results. Manual adjustments must be recorded with a mandatory justification reason, the original allocated amount, the adjusted amount, and the approving finance manager. Adjustments must flow through the same approval workflow as standard journal entries. The system must maintain a complete history of all adjustments distinguishing between system-calculated allocations and manual overrides. Adjusted allocations must be clearly marked in all reports with an indicator showing the original and adjusted values. Bulk adjustments must be supported for scenarios where an entire allocation run needs to be corrected. AI must flag frequent manual adjustments on the same trade lane or cost category as an indicator that the allocation methodology may need recalibration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R013	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Lease Cost Allocation Period Management	The system must manage cost allocation on a configurable period basis (monthly, quarterly) with a defined period open and close workflow. When a period is open, costs may be allocated and adjusted. When a period is closed, no further allocations or adjustments are permitted without a period reopen approved by a finance controller. Period close must trigger a reconciliation check ensuring that total per-diem costs for the period equal the sum of allocated costs across all trade lanes plus any unallocated overhead. The system must support retrospective allocation of costs received after period close (such as late MNR invoices) by booking them in the current open period with a reference to the originating period. Hijri calendar period boundaries must be supported for KSA and Qatar entities alongside Gregorian periods. AI must predict expected late-arriving costs to improve period-end accrual accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R014	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Lease vs Own Container Cost Comparison	The system must generate comparative cost analysis between leased containers and company-owned containers operating on the same trade lanes. The comparison must include per-TEU costs covering acquisition or per-diem, maintenance or MNR, insurance, depreciation (for owned), and repositioning. The system must normalize the comparison by using equivalent container types and trade lanes to ensure a meaningful like-for-like analysis. Reports must show the cost differential per TEU per trade lane and the fleet mix (percentage leased versus owned) that would minimize total container costs. AI must recommend the optimal lease-versus-own ratio per trade lane based on volume volatility, seasonal demand patterns, and current market lease rates versus purchase prices. The analysis must update dynamically as new cost data becomes available each month.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R015	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Trade Lane Profitability Dashboard with Lease Cost Overlay	The system must provide an interactive dashboard showing trade lane profitability with lease costs as a distinct cost layer. The dashboard must display revenue per TEU, total cost per TEU (broken down into vessel cost, port cost, inland cost, and lease cost), and resulting margin per TEU for each active trade lane. Lease cost must be further decomposable into per-diem, MNR, repositioning, and idle cost components. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, container type, time period (Gregorian and Hijri), and lessor. The visualization must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts. Trend lines must show how lease cost contribution to total trade lane cost evolves over time. AI must highlight trade lanes where the lease cost share is growing faster than revenue and recommend corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R016	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Automated Allocation Engine Scheduling and Execution	The system must support scheduling the cost allocation engine to run automatically at configurable intervals (daily, weekly, or monthly) with the ability to trigger manual runs on demand. Each allocation run must process all unallocated lease costs for the current period, apply the configured allocation methodology, and distribute costs to the mapped trade lanes and bookings. The engine must handle large volumes efficiently, processing allocations for up to 50,000 active leased containers across all entities within a 30-minute execution window. Execution logs must capture the start time, end time, number of costs processed, number of allocations created, any errors encountered, and total amounts allocated per entity. Failed allocations must be queued for review without blocking the remainder of the run. AI must monitor allocation engine performance and flag anomalies in allocation patterns compared to previous runs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-1-004-R017	FEAT-044-1-004	MOD-044	Cost Allocation Audit Trail and Regulatory Reporting	The system must maintain a full audit trail for every cost allocation transaction, recording the source cost record, allocation methodology applied, allocation basis (e.g., TEU-days, booking reference), calculated allocation amount, target trade lane, and the user or system process that initiated the allocation. The audit trail must be immutable and queryable for regulatory and internal audit purposes. The system must support generating allocation reports compliant with transfer pricing documentation requirements for inter-entity cost allocations, relevant for Qatar Financial Centre, UAE Corporate Tax (effective 2024), KSA ZATCA transfer pricing guidelines, and India Transfer Pricing regulations under the Income Tax Act. Reports must show the arm's-length basis for cost allocations between related entities. AI must detect allocation patterns that may raise transfer pricing concerns and recommend documentation enhancements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R001	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Lessor Statement Ingestion via EDI and PDF	The system must support automated ingestion of lessor billing statements in EDI COPARN/CODECO formats as well as structured and unstructured PDF invoices from major leasing companies such as Triton, Textainer, Beacon, Florens, SeaCube, and CAI International. AI-powered OCR must extract line items including container numbers, on-hire and off-hire dates, per-diem rates, damage charges, pickup and drop-off fees, and handling charges. The parser must handle Arabic-language invoices from regional lessors operating in Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Ingested statements must be stored with full audit trail and linked to originating lessor master records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R005	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Damage Charge Reconciliation with Survey Evidence	The system must reconcile lessor-claimed damage charges against off-hire survey reports, gate inspection photos, and pre-existing condition records from on-hire surveys. AI image comparison must be used to validate whether reported damages existed at on-hire versus off-hire. The system must apply IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) damage repair estimation guidelines to verify claimed repair costs. For operations at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system must integrate with port authority damage inspection systems. Disputed damage charges must be tracked through a structured workflow with configurable escalation to entity-level lease managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R006	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Pickup and Drop-Off Fee Validation	The system must validate lessor-billed pickup and drop-off fees against the agreed fee schedules in the lease contract, which may vary by depot location and container type. The system must cross-reference container movement records from TOS (Terminal Operating Systems) at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to confirm that the billed pickup or drop-off event actually occurred on the stated date at the stated location. Fees for repositioning between depots within the same port area must be flagged if the lease contract excludes inter-depot transfers. Each validated or disputed fee must update the container's cost ledger.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R007	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	AI Anomaly Detection on Billing Patterns	The system must employ machine learning models to detect anomalous billing patterns across lessor statements, including sudden per-diem rate increases without contract amendments, duplicate charges for the same container and period, billing for containers already redelivered and accepted, and statistically unusual damage claim frequencies from specific depots. The AI model must be trained on at least 24 months of historical reconciliation data per entity and must achieve a minimum precision of 85% on anomaly classification. Detected anomalies must be presented in a dashboard with severity scoring and recommended investigation actions in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R008	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Reconciliation Status Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The system must provide a real-time reconciliation status dashboard showing open statements, matched versus unmatched line items, total disputed amounts, and aging of unresolved items. The dashboard must support full Arabic RTL layout with Hijri date display alongside Gregorian dates and must allow filtering by lessor, entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), container type, charge category, and reconciliation status. Currency amounts must be displayed in the entity's functional currency with the option to toggle to the lessor's billing currency. Dashboard data must refresh within 30 seconds of any reconciliation action being completed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R009	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Dispute Management Workflow with Lessor Communication	The system must provide a structured dispute management workflow supporting dispute creation, evidence attachment, internal approval routing, and outbound dispute notification to the lessor. Dispute communications must be generated as formatted emails or EDI messages referencing the specific statement number, container number, and charge line. The workflow must support multi-level approval based on dispute value thresholds configurable per entity, with Arabic-language dispute templates for regional lessors and English templates for international lessors. Each dispute must track resolution status, settlement amount, credit note receipt, and final variance write-off approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R010	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Automated Accrual Reversal on Statement Receipt	The system must automatically reverse monthly per-diem accruals when the corresponding lessor statement is received and reconciled. Accrual entries posted to the lease liability account in the entity's general ledger must be reversed and replaced with the actual billed amount, with any variance posted to a lease cost variance account. The reversal process must handle multi-entity scenarios where a container accrued under the UAE entity is billed to the Qatar entity due to inter-company sub-lease arrangements. All accrual reversals must generate journal entries compliant with IFRS 16 and must be posted within the same accounting period as the statement date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R011	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Lessor Credit Note Tracking and Application	The system must track credit notes issued by lessors in response to accepted disputes, applying them against future statement balances or processing them as standalone refunds. Credit notes must be matched to the original disputed line item and must update the container's total cost of leasing. The system must alert when a promised credit note has not been received within a configurable number of days after dispute resolution and must automatically follow up with the lessor via email. Multi-currency credit notes must be converted at the exchange rate on the credit note date and any FX gain or loss must be separately recorded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R012	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Statement Payment Authorization and Integration	The system must generate payment authorization requests for reconciled and approved lessor statements, routing them to the appropriate entity's accounts payable system. Payment amounts must reflect the statement total minus disputed amounts and applied credit notes. The authorization must include a detailed reconciliation summary showing matched items, tolerated variances, open disputes, and net payable amount. Integration with treasury management must ensure that payments to international lessors in USD are scheduled to optimize FX conversion from QAR, AED, SAR, or INR. Payment due dates must respect the lessor's contractual payment terms, typically net 30 or net 45 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R013	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Historical Reconciliation Analytics and Lessor Scoring	The system must maintain a historical analytics module tracking reconciliation accuracy rates, average dispute values, dispute resolution times, and billing error frequencies per lessor over rolling 12-month and 24-month periods. A lessor reliability score must be computed based on billing accuracy, responsiveness to disputes, credit note timeliness, and rate compliance. These scores must be available to procurement teams during lease renewal negotiations. Analytics must be exportable in Excel and PDF formats with charts displaying trends. The module must support Arabic-language report generation for executive stakeholders in Qatar and KSA entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R014	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Offline Reconciliation Support for Connectivity Disruptions	The system must support offline reconciliation workflows for users at remote depots or port locations experiencing intermittent connectivity, particularly at JNPT where bandwidth may be limited. Users must be able to download pending statement data, perform manual matching and annotation on their local device, and synchronize results when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution logic must handle cases where the same statement line item is modified by multiple users during an offline period. The offline module must cache exchange rates and tolerance thresholds from the last successful sync to enable accurate reconciliation without server access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R005	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Multi-Currency Invoicing	The system must generate THC invoices in the billing currency specified by the customer contract or the default port currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT, SAR for KSA entities). When the tariff base currency differs from the invoice currency, the system must apply the exchange rate effective on the vessel arrival date or the invoice date as configured per entity policy. Exchange rates must be sourced from the central treasury rate table and locked at invoice generation. All invoices must display amounts in both the billing currency and USD equivalent for cross-entity reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R015	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail for every reconciliation action including statement ingestion, line-item matching, tolerance application, dispute creation, approval routing, payment authorization, and credit note application. The audit trail must capture the user identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, entity context, and before/after values for any data modification. Audit reports must be generatable for external auditors in formats compliant with Qatar Financial Centre Authority (QFCA), UAE Federal Tax Authority (FTA), Saudi ZATCA, and Indian GST regulatory requirements. All audit data must be retained for a minimum of seven years per entity jurisdictional requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-001-R016	FEAT-044-2-001	MOD-044	Bulk Statement Processing with Batch Controls	The system must support bulk processing of multiple lessor statements in a single batch operation, enabling month-end reconciliation of dozens or hundreds of statements simultaneously. Batch processing must include pre-validation checks for duplicate statements, period-mismatch detection where a statement covers dates outside the expected billing period, and currency validation against the lessor's default billing currency. Batch progress must be displayed with a real-time progress bar showing processed versus pending items. Failed items within a batch must not prevent remaining items from processing, and a batch summary report must be generated upon completion showing success counts, failure reasons, and aggregate variance amounts across all processed statements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R001	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Off-Hire Request Initiation and Approval Workflow	The system must provide a structured workflow for initiating container off-hire requests when a leased container is no longer needed or its lease term is approaching expiration. The request must capture the container number, current location (port/depot/yard), reason for return (lease expiry, surplus, damaged beyond economic repair, fleet right-sizing), and proposed redelivery date. Approval routing must be configurable per entity with multi-level authorization based on remaining lease term value. The system must validate against minimum lease duration commitments and calculate any early termination penalties per the lease contract terms before allowing the request to proceed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R002	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	AI-Optimized Redelivery Depot Selection	The system must use AI optimization to recommend the optimal redelivery depot from the lessor's approved depot network, considering factors including transportation cost from the container's current location, depot acceptance capacity and wait times, lessor-specific depot preferences and surcharges, proximity to future demand locations to avoid unnecessary repositioning by the lessor, and port-specific constraints at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The recommendation engine must present the top three depot options with cost comparison breakdowns and estimated redelivery processing times. The system must update recommendations in real-time as depot availability changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R003	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Pre-Redelivery Condition Assessment and Repair Decision	The system must facilitate a pre-redelivery condition assessment to determine whether the container meets the lessor's redelivery standards as specified in the lease agreement, typically referencing IICL-5 or IICL-6 criteria. AI-powered image analysis must evaluate gate photos and inspection images to identify damage items that would result in lessor rejection or damage billing. For each identified damage item, the system must calculate the estimated repair cost versus the expected lessor damage charge and recommend whether to repair before redelivery or accept the lessor charge. Repair decisions must factor in local repair costs at the container's current location across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India markets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R004	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Redelivery Appointment Scheduling with Depot Integration	The system must integrate with depot management systems at approved redelivery locations to schedule container drop-off appointments. Scheduling must respect depot operating hours including Friday closures in Qatar and KSA, Friday-Saturday reduced hours in UAE, and Indian national holiday schedules. The system must handle time zone differences for scheduling across the four country operations and must send appointment confirmations in the appropriate language (Arabic for Qatar/UAE/KSA depots, English or Hindi for Indian depots). Appointment modifications and cancellations must be communicated to the depot automatically. The system must track no-show events and their impact on future scheduling priority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R005	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Off-Hire Survey Document Generation and Management	The system must generate off-hire survey documents (Equipment Interchange Receipts) capturing the container's condition at the point of redelivery, including structural integrity, flooring condition, door operation, refrigeration unit status for reefer containers, and cleanliness. The document must be digitally signed by both the returning party's representative and the depot inspector. Survey data must include timestamped photographs with GPS coordinates confirming the redelivery location. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri date stamps on survey documents. Generated documents must be automatically linked to the container's lease record and transmitted to the lessor via EDI or API within four hours of survey completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R006	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Gate-Out Processing and Container Status Update	The system must integrate with terminal operating systems at Hamad Port (Navis N4), Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali (Navis/TOPS), and JNPT to process gate-out transactions for containers being redelivered to lessors. Gate-out must trigger automatic status updates changing the container from 'on-hire' to 'pending off-hire acceptance' in the lease management system. The system must capture the gate-out timestamp, truck and driver details, and seal numbers. For containers departing via barge or feeder vessel to reach the redelivery depot, the system must track the intermediate transport and update the expected redelivery arrival time accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R007	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Lessor Off-Hire Acceptance Tracking and Confirmation	The system must track the lessor's acceptance or rejection of each redelivered container through EDI message processing (CODECO off-hire confirmation), lessor portal API integration, or manual confirmation entry. For accepted containers, the off-hire effective date must be recorded and per-diem billing must cease from that date. For rejected containers, the rejection reason and required remediation actions must be captured. The system must alert operations when a container has been in 'pending off-hire acceptance' status beyond a configurable threshold (default 5 business days) to prevent continued per-diem accrual due to lessor processing delays. Acceptance confirmations must update the container's lifecycle status across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R008	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Last-Free-Day Monitoring and Detention Prevention	The system must continuously monitor the last free day for each leased container at every port and depot, alerting operations teams when containers are approaching their free-time expiry to prevent unnecessary detention and demurrage charges. Alerts must be generated at configurable intervals (e.g., 7 days, 3 days, 1 day before expiry) and must be prioritized based on the daily detention rate applicable after the free period expires. The monitoring must account for port-specific free-time rules at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, including any lessor-negotiated extensions. Alerts must identify the most cost-effective action: redelivery, on-carriage to next booking, or free-time extension request.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R009	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Multi-Entity Container Transfer Before Redelivery	The system must support inter-entity container transfers where a container leased by one entity (e.g., Qatar) is physically located in another entity's territory (e.g., UAE) at the time of planned redelivery. The transfer workflow must generate inter-company accounting entries to settle the remaining lease obligation, transportation costs, and any accrued charges. Transfer documentation must comply with customs transit requirements between GCC states and must handle the currency conversion between QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The system must determine whether it is more cost-effective to transfer the container to the originating entity for redelivery or to arrange redelivery at a depot in the entity where the container is physically located.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R010	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Cleaning and Washing Coordination Before Redelivery	The system must manage the cleaning and washing process required before container redelivery, particularly for containers that previously carried food-grade cargo, chemicals, or commodities requiring specialized cleaning at facilities near Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. The system must match the cleaning requirement to the lessor's redelivery standards and the container's last cargo type. Cleaning vendor selection must be automated based on location, cost, turnaround time, and quality ratings. The system must track cleaning certificates and attach them to the redelivery documentation. Costs must be allocated to the appropriate cost center and compared against the lessor's cleaning surcharge to determine the most economical approach.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R011	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Reefer Container Specific Redelivery Procedures	The system must implement specialized redelivery procedures for refrigerated containers (20RF, 40RF, 40HCRF) including pre-trip inspection (PTI) status verification, refrigeration unit run-hour logging, fuel level documentation, and temperature recorder data extraction. The system must verify that the reefer unit's run hours do not exceed the lessor's maintenance interval limits and must schedule pre-redelivery maintenance if needed. For containers with controlled atmosphere (CA) or modified atmosphere (MA) capabilities, additional system checks must be documented. The redelivery checklist must be dynamically generated based on the specific reefer unit make and model (Carrier, Thermo King, Daikin, Star Cool) and the lessor's technical requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R012	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Bulk Redelivery Planning for Fleet Right-Sizing	The system must support bulk redelivery planning when fleet right-sizing decisions require the simultaneous return of multiple containers of the same or different types. The planner must optimize the sequence and timing of redeliveries to minimize total cost considering depot capacity constraints, transportation logistics, and the impact on per-diem charges across all containers in the batch. The planning tool must generate a redelivery schedule with Gantt chart visualization showing dependencies and critical path items. The schedule must account for port-specific operational constraints such as Ramadan reduced working hours across Qatar, UAE, and KSA operations. Bulk redelivery plans must be approved by entity-level fleet managers before execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R013	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Redelivery Cost Tracking and Allocation	The system must track all costs associated with each container redelivery including transportation to depot, pre-redelivery repairs, cleaning, PTI for reefers, depot handling fees, and any lessor-assessed charges upon inspection. Costs must be captured in the transaction currency and converted to the entity's functional currency using the treasury-approved exchange rate. Total redelivery costs must be allocated to the appropriate voyage, trade lane, or cost center based on configurable allocation rules. The system must compare actual redelivery costs against budgeted redelivery allowances from the original lease financial model and flag any redeliveries exceeding budget by more than a configurable percentage threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R014	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Redelivery Documentation Archive and Compliance	The system must maintain a complete digital archive of all redelivery documentation including off-hire requests, depot appointment confirmations, equipment interchange receipts, inspection photographs, cleaning certificates, reefer PTI reports, lessor acceptance confirmations, and credit notes for returned deposits. Documents must be indexed by container number, lessor, entity, and redelivery date for rapid retrieval. The archive must comply with data retention requirements of QFCA (Qatar), UAE FTA, Saudi ZATCA, and Indian Companies Act, with a minimum retention of seven years. Documents must be accessible in both Arabic and English versions where applicable and must support digital signature verification for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R015	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Lessor Performance Tracking on Off-Hire Processing	The system must track lessor performance metrics related to the off-hire and redelivery acceptance process, including average days from redelivery to off-hire confirmation, rejection rate by depot and container type, frequency and value of disputed damage charges at redelivery, and responsiveness to pre-redelivery condition queries. These metrics must be maintained per lessor across all four country entities and must be weighted into the lessor reliability score used during lease renewal negotiations. Performance reports must be generated monthly with trend analysis over 6-month and 12-month periods. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English with currency figures shown in both the lessor's billing currency and the entity's functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R016	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Automated Notification to Lessor on Redelivery Intent	The system must automatically generate and transmit redelivery intent notifications to lessors within the contractually required notice period, typically 30 to 60 days before planned redelivery. Notifications must include the container number, current location, proposed redelivery depot, estimated redelivery date, and current condition summary. The system must track notification delivery confirmation and escalate if no acknowledgment is received within five business days. Notifications must be formatted per lessor communication preferences including EDI, email, or portal submission. For Arabic-speaking lessor contacts, the notification must be generated in Arabic with an English copy attached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-002-R017	FEAT-044-2-002	MOD-044	Deposit and Guarantee Recovery on Container Return	The system must track security deposits, bank guarantees, and advance payments made at lease inception and initiate recovery processes upon successful container redelivery and lessor off-hire acceptance. The recovery workflow must calculate the net refundable amount after deducting any outstanding per-diem charges, damage claims, and other lessor-assessed fees. For deposits held in currencies different from the entity's functional currency (common for USD deposits held by Qatar and KSA entities), the system must compute the FX gain or loss at the date of refund receipt. The system must generate reminders for deposits not returned within contractual timelines and escalate to legal if overdue beyond a configurable threshold, defaulting to 90 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R001	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Total Cost of Ownership Model for Container Purchase	The system must compute a comprehensive total cost of ownership (TCO) for purchasing containers, incorporating acquisition cost (new or used), delivery and positioning costs to Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, insurance premiums, annual maintenance and repair costs based on container age and type, periodic survey and inspection costs, storage costs during idle periods, and eventual disposal or residual value. The TCO model must support container types including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, open-top, and flat-rack. All cost inputs must be denominated in the entity's functional currency with the option to input in USD and auto-convert using configurable exchange rate assumptions over the analysis horizon.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R002	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Lease Cost Projection with Rate Escalation Modeling	The system must project total leasing costs over a configurable analysis period (typically 5 to 15 years) including per-diem lease rates, pickup and drop-off fees, insurance surcharges, damage and repair provisions based on historical rates, and administrative overhead. The projection must model rate escalation scenarios including fixed annual increases, CPI-linked adjustments specific to each country (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), and market-driven rate fluctuations based on historical container leasing market indices. The model must support master lease, spot lease, and long-term lease structures with different rate profiles. Projections must be computed in both nominal and real (inflation-adjusted) terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R003	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Net Present Value Comparison with Configurable Discount Rates	The system must calculate the net present value (NPV) of both lease and buy alternatives using entity-specific weighted average cost of capital (WACC) as the discount rate. The discount rate must be configurable per entity to reflect different capital structures and borrowing costs in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The NPV calculation must incorporate all cash flows including initial outlays, periodic operating costs, tax shields from depreciation (buy scenario) or lease expense deductions (lease scenario), maintenance reserves, and terminal value or redelivery costs. Sensitivity analysis must show NPV at plus/minus 1% and 2% discount rate variations. Results must be presented in the entity's functional currency alongside a USD-equivalent for cross-entity comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R004	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Internal Rate of Return Analysis for Purchase Option	The system must calculate the internal rate of return (IRR) for the container purchase option, treating the avoided lease payments as the revenue stream and the purchase-related cash outflows as the investment. The IRR calculation must handle non-conventional cash flow patterns where maintenance costs may create sign changes in the net cash flow stream. The system must also compute a modified internal rate of return (MIRR) using the entity's reinvestment rate for positive cash flows and financing rate for negative cash flows. IRR results must be compared against the entity's hurdle rate, which must be configurable per entity and container type. The analysis must clearly indicate whether the purchase option creates value above the minimum acceptable return.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R005	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Tax and Depreciation Impact Modeling by Jurisdiction	The system must model the tax implications of lease versus buy decisions specific to each entity's jurisdiction. For Qatar, the model must reflect Qatar Financial Centre tax rules and any applicable customs duty exemptions for containers imported through Hamad Port. For UAE, the model must incorporate UAE Corporate Tax (introduced June 2023) at 9% above the AED 375,000 threshold and relevant free zone exemptions. For KSA, the model must apply the 20% corporate income tax rate and ZATCA depreciation schedules. For India, the model must apply the applicable corporate tax rate, accelerated depreciation under the Income Tax Act Section 32, and customs duties on container imports through JNPT. Tax benefits of lease expense deduction versus depreciation must be compared on an after-tax NPV basis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R006	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	IFRS 16 Lease Accounting Impact Analysis	The system must model the impact of IFRS 16 on the entity's financial statements under both lease and buy scenarios. For leasing, the model must compute the right-of-use asset, lease liability, depreciation of the ROU asset, and interest expense on the lease liability over the lease term. For purchase, the model must compute the fixed asset carrying value and depreciation expense. The analysis must show the impact on key financial metrics including EBITDA, operating profit, net debt, return on assets, and debt-to-equity ratio. The model must handle lease modifications including term extensions, rate changes, and scope changes as defined under IFRS 16 paragraph 44-46. Results must be presented in a side-by-side comparison format suitable for CFO-level decision making.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R007	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Scenario Modeling with Monte Carlo Simulation	The system must support Monte Carlo simulation to model uncertainty in key input variables including container purchase prices, lease rates, maintenance costs, utilization rates, exchange rates, interest rates, and residual values. Users must be able to define probability distributions (normal, triangular, uniform) for each variable and run a minimum of 10,000 simulation iterations. The output must display probability distributions of NPV difference (buy minus lease), showing the likelihood of purchase being more economical than leasing. The simulation must produce percentile breakdowns (P10, P25, P50, P75, P90) and value-at-risk metrics for each scenario. Results must be visualized in histogram and tornado chart formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R011	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Dispute and Adjustment Workflow	The system must provide a structured dispute resolution workflow for THC charges where customers can raise disputes referencing specific invoice line items, container numbers, and vessel voyages. Each dispute must be assigned a unique case identifier, categorized by dispute type (rate error, duplicate charge, container type mismatch, movement type error), and routed to the appropriate billing team based on terminal location. The workflow must enforce SLA timers configurable per entity (default 5 business days for initial response, 15 business days for resolution) and generate automatic escalation notifications in Arabic and English when SLA thresholds are breached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R008	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Fleet-Level Aggregate Analysis Across Entities	The system must support fleet-level aggregate lease versus buy analysis spanning all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), consolidating entity-level analyses into a group-wide view. The aggregation must handle currency conversion from QAR, AED, SAR, and INR into a common reporting currency (configurable, typically USD) using projected exchange rates over the analysis horizon. The aggregate model must optimize the mix of leased and owned containers across the fleet to minimize group-wide total cost while respecting entity-specific constraints such as minimum ownership ratios, lessor diversification requirements, and regulatory restrictions on cross-border container ownership. The analysis must produce a recommended fleet composition with drill-down capability to each entity's contribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R009	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Financing Option Comparison for Purchase Scenarios	The system must model multiple financing options for container purchases including outright cash purchase, bank term loans, Islamic financing (Murabaha and Ijara structures common in Qatar, UAE, and KSA), export credit agency financing, and sale-leaseback arrangements. Each financing option must be modeled with its specific cash flow profile, effective interest rate, collateral requirements, and covenant restrictions. The comparison must show the effective cost of each financing option on an after-tax basis considering that Islamic financing structures may have different accounting treatments under IFRS. The system must rank financing options by total cost and present a recommendation with sensitivity to changes in base rate assumptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R010	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Residual Value Estimation Using Market Data	The system must estimate container residual values at various points in the analysis horizon using market data from container trading platforms, broker assessments, and historical transaction records. Residual value curves must be maintained for each container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF) accounting for age, condition grade, and market cycle position. The system must apply region-specific adjustments for containers positioned at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT based on local scrap metal prices and second-hand container demand. Residual values must be expressed in USD with conversion to entity functional currencies. The model must support user override of estimated residual values with documented justification for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R011	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Breakeven Utilization Analysis for Owned Containers	The system must calculate the minimum utilization rate (percentage of days on-hire or in revenue-generating service) at which owning a container becomes more economical than leasing. The breakeven analysis must account for all fixed and variable costs of ownership including storage during idle periods, repositioning costs between ports in the operating network, and the opportunity cost of capital invested in container assets. The analysis must produce breakeven utilization charts showing the crossover point for each container type and must compare the breakeven rate against the entity's historical utilization rates over the past 12 and 24 months. Results must be segmented by trade lane and seasonal pattern to reflect the cyclical nature of container demand.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R012	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Exchange Rate Sensitivity Analysis for Multi-Currency Decisions	The system must perform exchange rate sensitivity analysis showing how the lease versus buy decision changes under different FX rate scenarios for the USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, and USD/INR pairs. Since QAR, AED, and SAR are pegged to USD (with narrow bands), the sensitivity analysis for GCC entities must focus primarily on the impact of a potential peg adjustment or devaluation scenario. For the India entity, the model must incorporate historical INR/USD volatility patterns and project a range of depreciation scenarios. The analysis must quantify the FX exposure differential between leasing (where costs are typically USD-denominated) and buying (where the asset is held in the entity's functional currency). Hedging cost estimates for each scenario must be included.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R013	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Interactive Financial Dashboard with What-If Capabilities	The system must provide an interactive dashboard allowing users to adjust key assumptions (lease rates, purchase prices, discount rates, utilization, maintenance costs, tax rates, exchange rates) via sliders or input fields and see the lease versus buy comparison recalculate in real time. The dashboard must display NPV comparison, IRR, payback period, breakeven utilization, and annual cash flow charts that update dynamically. Arabic RTL layout must be fully supported with number formatting per locale (Arabic-Indic numerals where configured). The dashboard must support saving and naming scenarios for comparison, with the ability to overlay up to five scenarios on a single chart. Export to PDF and Excel must include all assumption inputs and computed outputs for documentation and board presentation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R014	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Historical Decision Audit and Outcome Tracking	The system must track the outcome of past lease versus buy decisions by comparing the projected financial model at the time of decision against actual realized costs over the container's lifecycle. For each decision made, the system must record the analysis assumptions, the chosen option (lease or buy), the approving authority, and the decision date. As actual costs accumulate, the system must compute the variance between projected and actual NPV and display whether the decision delivered the expected financial benefit. This retrospective analysis must inform future decision-making by identifying systematic biases in cost projections, such as consistently underestimating maintenance costs or overestimating utilization rates. Outcome reports must be generated quarterly per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R015	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Approval Workflow for Lease vs Buy Recommendations	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow for lease versus buy recommendations, routing the analysis to the appropriate decision-makers based on the total commitment value. The workflow must support entity-level approval thresholds (e.g., up to 500,000 USD approved by entity fleet manager, above 500,000 USD requires group fleet director approval). The approval package must include the complete financial analysis, risk assessment, sensitivity analysis summary, and AI recommendation with confidence score. Approvers must be able to add comments, request revisions, or approve with conditions. The workflow must support Arabic-language comments and must send approval notifications via email and in-app alerts. All approvals must be digitally recorded with timestamp and approver identity for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R016	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	Insurance Cost Differential Modeling	The system must model the insurance cost differential between leased and owned containers. For owned containers, the model must include hull and machinery insurance, third-party liability coverage, and cargo contamination insurance, with premiums estimated based on fleet size, container age, trade routes, and claims history. For leased containers, the model must reflect the insurance component embedded in the lease rate as well as any supplementary coverage required by the lessee. Insurance premiums must be sourced from broker quotations or estimated using market benchmarks for Gulf and Indian Subcontinent trade lanes. The model must account for self-insurance or captive insurance options available to larger fleet operators and must compute the net insurance cost difference on a per-TEU-day basis for inclusion in the overall lease versus buy comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-003-R017	FEAT-044-2-003	MOD-044	AI-Generated Recommendation with Confidence Scoring	The system must provide an AI-generated recommendation for each lease versus buy analysis, clearly stating whether leasing or buying is financially preferable under the base case assumptions and providing a confidence score (0-100%) reflecting the robustness of the recommendation across the simulated scenario range. The AI must explain the key drivers of its recommendation in natural language in both Arabic and English, highlighting the two or three most influential factors (e.g., low utilization favoring lease, favorable tax depreciation favoring buy, high residual value uncertainty favoring lease). The recommendation must include a risk-adjusted assessment noting scenarios under which the recommendation would reverse. The AI model must be retrained quarterly using actual outcome data from historical decisions to improve recommendation accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R001	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Demand Forecasting Engine for Container Requirements	The system must implement an AI-driven demand forecasting engine that predicts container requirements by type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, open-top, flat-rack, tank container), by location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and inland depots), and by time period (weekly forecast for 13 weeks, monthly for 12 months, quarterly for 3 years). The forecasting model must incorporate historical booking data, seasonal trade patterns (e.g., Ramadan period reductions in Gulf states, Indian festival season surges), macroeconomic indicators, trade agreement changes, and shipper contract pipeline data. Forecast accuracy must be measured using MAPE and must target below 15% error at the weekly level and below 10% at the monthly level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R002	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Multi-Objective Optimization Model for Fleet Mix	The system must implement a multi-objective optimization model that determines the optimal fleet composition minimizing total cost while simultaneously maximizing operational flexibility and ensuring service level compliance. The optimization must balance three sourcing strategies: owned containers (lowest per-unit cost, least flexible), long-term leases (moderate cost, moderate flexibility), and spot leases (highest cost, maximum flexibility). Decision variables must include the quantity of containers to own, long-term lease, and spot-lease by type and by entity. The model must use mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) or evolutionary algorithms capable of solving fleet composition problems with over 10,000 decision variables within a 30-minute computation window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R003	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Constraint Modeling for Operational and Financial Limits	The system must incorporate comprehensive operational and financial constraints into the fleet optimization model including maximum capital expenditure budgets per entity and consolidated, maximum lease commitment levels as a percentage of entity revenue, minimum container availability service levels by port (e.g., 98% availability at Jebel Ali, 95% at JNPT), lessor concentration limits (no single lessor exceeding 30% of leased fleet), container age distribution requirements (maximum average fleet age by type), and storage capacity constraints at each port and depot. Financial constraints must include debt covenants, IFRS 16 liability thresholds, and entity-specific balance sheet targets. All constraints must be configurable with soft and hard limit distinctions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R004	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Seasonal and Cyclical Demand Pattern Handling	The optimization model must account for seasonal and cyclical demand patterns affecting container requirements across the four-country operating network. The system must model Ramadan-related volume reductions in Qatar, UAE, and KSA (typically 15-25% decline in general cargo), Diwali and festive season surges in Indian exports through JNPT (typically 20-30% increase), summer peak season for reefer containers driven by Gulf perishable imports, and year-end budget-driven import spikes in government-related cargo. The model must recommend a base fleet level to cover sustained demand through owned and long-term leased containers, with a flexible buffer layer of spot leases to accommodate peaks. The seasonal strategy must be optimized annually with quarterly reviews.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R005	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Container Repositioning Cost Integration	The optimization model must incorporate container repositioning costs between ports and depots in the operating network when determining the optimal fleet composition. Repositioning costs must be modeled for each origin-destination pair including Hamad Port to Jebel Ali (road/barge), Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port (road), intra-India repositioning to/from JNPT (rail/road), and cross-regional repositioning via feeder vessels. The model must consider that a higher percentage of owned containers requires more repositioning investment to balance supply and demand across locations, while leased containers can be picked up and dropped off at different locations within the lessor's depot network. The repositioning cost model must use current freight rates and must be updated monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R006	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Real-Time Fleet Position Monitoring and Reoptimization Triggers	The system must continuously monitor the current fleet position showing the actual count of owned, long-term leased, and spot-leased containers by type and location, compared against the optimized target composition. Deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., 5% understock or 10% overstock at any location) must trigger automatic reoptimization runs. The monitoring dashboard must display a fleet health score (0-100) reflecting the degree of alignment between actual and optimized fleet composition. Real-time data feeds from terminal operating systems at all four ports must update container positions with a maximum latency of one hour. The dashboard must support Arabic and English interfaces with currency displays in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R007	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Lease Portfolio Optimization with Contract Staggering	The optimizer must recommend the optimal staggering of lease contract start and end dates to minimize concentration risk and maximize negotiating leverage. The system must ensure that no more than a configurable percentage (default 25%) of the total leased fleet comes up for renewal in any single quarter. The optimization must consider lease rate market cycle positioning, recommending longer-term commitments when rates are below historical averages and shorter-term or spot leases when rates are above average. The system must model the lessor relationship portfolio to maintain strategic diversification across at least three major international leasing companies and regional lessors operating at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R008	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Container Type Substitution and Interchangeability Logic	The optimizer must model container type substitutability where operationally feasible, for example using 40HC containers when 40GP containers are unavailable and the cargo dimensions permit. Substitution rules must be configurable per trade lane and cargo type, with cost adjustment factors for upsizing (e.g., 40HC replacing 40GP adds a cost premium) or downsizing (e.g., two 20GP replacing one 40GP adds handling cost). The optimizer must determine whether maintaining separate inventory pools for closely substitutable types is more efficient than allowing flexible substitution with a combined pool. The substitution logic must respect special equipment requirements such as temperature control for reefers, open-top loading clearance, and flat-rack structural load ratings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R009	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	What-If Scenario Analysis for Fleet Strategy	The system must support what-if scenario analysis allowing fleet managers to test the impact of strategic changes on optimal fleet composition. Scenarios must include adding or removing a trade route, changes in cargo volume projections (e.g., new shipper contract win or loss), acquisition of competitors or their container assets, entry into new markets beyond the current Qatar/UAE/KSA/India network, significant changes in lease market rates, and changes in container purchase prices or steel costs. Each scenario must show the change in optimal fleet mix, the associated cost delta, and the transition plan from current to scenario-optimal composition. Users must be able to save, compare, and present up to ten scenarios simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R010	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Transition Planning from Current to Optimal Fleet	The system must generate actionable transition plans to move from the current fleet composition to the AI-recommended optimal composition. The transition plan must specify which containers to purchase (by type, quantity, and preferred sourcing timeline), which lease contracts to renew or terminate at expiry, which spot leases to initiate or cease, and which owned containers to dispose of. The plan must be phased over a configurable transition period (typically 6 to 24 months) respecting lease contract notice periods, procurement lead times for new container manufacturing (typically 8-12 weeks from Chinese manufacturers), and capital expenditure phasing constraints. Each action in the plan must have a target completion date and assigned responsibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R011	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Carbon Footprint Optimization for Fleet Composition	The optimizer must incorporate carbon footprint considerations into the fleet composition decision, modeling the environmental impact of owning versus leasing based on container age, energy efficiency of reefer units, and repositioning distances. The system must quantify carbon emissions per TEU-move under different fleet compositions and must support optimization objectives that include minimizing carbon footprint alongside cost minimization. The model must account for evolving environmental regulations in Qatar (Qatar National Vision 2030), UAE (Net Zero 2050), KSA (Saudi Green Initiative), and India (National Action Plan on Climate Change). Carbon credit costs and benefits must be monetized and included in the total cost optimization where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R012	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	AI Model Training and Performance Monitoring	The system must provide a framework for training, validating, and monitoring the AI models underlying the fleet composition optimizer. Model training must use a minimum of 36 months of historical data covering demand patterns, lease rates, container purchase prices, utilization rates, and repositioning volumes across all four entities. Model validation must employ backtesting on held-out historical periods with performance metrics including cost savings versus actual decisions, forecast accuracy, and constraint satisfaction rates. Model performance must be monitored continuously with alerts when prediction accuracy degrades below configurable thresholds. Model retraining must be triggered automatically when performance drift is detected or manually by authorized data science personnel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R013	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Executive Reporting and Board Presentation Generation	The system must generate executive-level reports and board presentation materials summarizing the fleet composition optimization results and recommendations. Reports must include an executive summary in both Arabic and English, current fleet status versus optimal composition gap analysis, recommended actions with financial impact quantification, risk assessment of the recommended strategy, and projected 3-year fleet cost trajectory under the optimized composition. Reports must be formatted for direct inclusion in board presentations with professional charts, tables, and visualizations. Currency figures must be presented in USD for group-level and in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for entity-level reports. Hijri date stamps must appear alongside Gregorian dates on all report headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R014	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Integration with Container Procurement and Disposal Systems	The optimizer must integrate bidirectionally with the container procurement module for purchase recommendations and the container disposal module for end-of-life decisions. When the optimizer recommends increasing owned fleet, it must generate purchase requisitions with specifications including container type, grade (new/used), desired manufacturing specifications, and target delivery port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT). When the optimizer recommends fleet reduction, it must generate disposal recommendations including estimated sale value, recommended sales channel (direct sale, auction, broker), and optimal timing based on market conditions. Integration must ensure that optimization recommendations do not conflict with in-progress procurement or disposal transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R015	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Multi-Entity Budget Reconciliation and Allocation	The optimizer must reconcile its recommendations against the approved capital and operating budgets of each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and the consolidated group budget. Container purchases recommended by the optimizer must fit within the entity's approved capital expenditure budget for the fiscal period. Lease commitments must align with the entity's approved operating expenditure projections. When the optimal solution exceeds budget constraints, the system must identify the most cost-effective feasible solution within budget and quantify the additional cost of the budget constraint. The budget reconciliation must handle different fiscal year calendars across entities and must convert between QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD using approved budget exchange rates rather than spot rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R016	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Offline Optimization Capability for Remote Operations	The system must support an offline optimization capability allowing fleet managers at locations with limited connectivity to run simplified fleet composition analyses using locally cached data. The offline module must store the most recent demand forecasts, current fleet inventory, lease rate benchmarks, and container price indices. Optimization runs performed offline must be clearly marked as provisional and must be reconciled against the central system when connectivity is restored. The offline module must support core optimization functions including fleet gap analysis, lease renewal recommendations, and spot lease sizing for the local port's container requirements. Data synchronization upon reconnection must use conflict resolution logic to handle concurrent changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R017	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Competitor Fleet Intelligence and Market Positioning	The system must incorporate available market intelligence on competitor fleet strategies, container availability trends at key ports, and leasing market conditions to enhance optimization recommendations. Data sources must include industry publications, port authority statistics from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, container leasing market indices such as the Drewry Container Leasing Confidence Index and Howe Robinson Container Index, and publicly available competitor fleet data. The AI model must use this market intelligence to anticipate lease rate movements and container price trends, timing fleet composition adjustments to take advantage of favorable market conditions. Market intelligence must be refreshed weekly and its impact on recommendations must be traceable for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-044-2-004-R018	FEAT-044-2-004	MOD-044	Optimization Explainability and Decision Transparency	The system must provide full explainability for all optimization recommendations, showing the reasoning chain from input data and constraints to the final recommended fleet composition. Each recommendation must include a plain-language explanation in both Arabic and English describing why specific container types should be owned versus leased, why certain lessors are preferred, and why the recommended fleet size differs from the current size. The system must display the binding constraints that most influence the solution and quantify the cost of each active constraint. Sensitivity reports must show how the recommendation would change if specific inputs or constraints were modified. All optimization runs must be logged with complete input parameters and output results for governance and audit compliance across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R006	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Volume Discount Tiers	The system must support volume-based THC discount tiers negotiated with shipping lines and freight forwarders. Discount structures must accommodate cumulative monthly TEU thresholds (for example, 0-500 TEU at standard rate, 501-2000 TEU at 5% discount, 2001+ TEU at 10% discount). The system must track cumulative TEU counts per customer per terminal per calendar month and Hijri month, automatically apply the correct tier discount at invoice generation, and retroactively adjust prior invoices in the same period when a new tier threshold is crossed. Credit notes for retroactive adjustments must be generated automatically with full Arabic and English descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R007	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	AI-Driven THC Tariff Optimization	The system must provide AI-driven analytics to recommend optimal THC rate adjustments based on historical throughput data, competitor terminal pricing intelligence, seasonal volume patterns, and trade lane demand forecasting. The AI engine must analyze at least 24 months of historical transaction data per terminal and generate quarterly tariff revision recommendations with projected revenue impact analysis. Recommendations must be presented in a bilingual dashboard with Arabic RTL layout support, showing confidence scores for each recommendation and requiring management approval workflow before any rate changes are published to production tariff schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R008	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Exemption and Waiver Management	The system must support THC exemptions and waivers for specific scenarios including government-owned cargo, humanitarian aid shipments, diplomatic containers, and free zone transfers. Each exemption type must be configured with eligibility criteria, required supporting documentation (customs declarations, diplomatic notes, free zone certificates), maximum validity period, and approval authority levels. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all exemptions granted including approver identity, justification text in Arabic and English, exemption value in local and USD currency, and automatic expiration enforcement when validity periods lapse.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R009	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Integration with Terminal Operating Systems	The system must integrate with terminal operating systems (TOS) at each port via standardized API interfaces to receive real-time container move events including vessel discharge, vessel loading, gate-in, gate-out, yard shifts, and reefer plug events. The integration must support COPARN, BAPLIE, and COPINO EDI message formats as well as REST API payloads. For offline scenarios when TOS connectivity is interrupted, the system must queue pending move events locally, reconcile automatically upon reconnection, and flag any discrepancies between local queue and TOS confirmed moves for manual review by terminal billing staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R010	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Split Billing Between Parties	The system must support split THC billing where charges are divided between multiple parties such as the shipping line, consignee, freight forwarder, or slot charterer based on contractual agreements. Split ratios must be configurable as fixed percentages or fixed amounts per container. The system must generate separate invoice line items or separate invoices per party as configured, ensuring that the total split amounts equal the full THC charge with rounding differences allocated to the primary party. Each split invoice must reference the original container and voyage details and comply with local VAT or GST requirements for the billing entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R012	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Revenue Accrual and Recognition	The system must support THC revenue accrual based on container move completion events from the terminal operating system even before formal invoicing occurs. Accrued revenue entries must be posted to the general ledger per entity with proper multi-currency translation using the move date exchange rate. Upon invoice generation, accrued entries must be reversed and replaced with billed revenue entries. Month-end accrual reports must reconcile unbilled THC amounts by terminal, vessel, and customer with aging analysis. The system must support both IFRS 15 and local GAAP revenue recognition policies as configured per entity with Hijri calendar period support for KSA entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R013	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Tariff Version Control and Effective Dating	The system must maintain full version control of THC tariff schedules with effective date and expiry date management supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar systems. When a new tariff version is published, the system must automatically expire the previous version at one second before the new version's effective datetime. Historical tariff versions must remain queryable for audit, dispute resolution, and retroactive billing purposes. The system must prevent overlapping effective date ranges for the same terminal, container type, and movement type combination, raising validation errors in bilingual Arabic-English messages when conflicts are detected during tariff configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R014	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Benchmarking Across Terminals	The system must provide a comparative benchmarking dashboard that displays THC rates across all four terminal locations (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) normalized to USD for like-for-like comparison. The dashboard must support filtering by container type, movement type, and customer tier. Benchmark analysis must include historical trend charts showing rate evolution over configurable periods, variance highlighting where any terminal deviates more than a configurable percentage from the group average, and AI-generated narrative summaries in both Arabic and English explaining significant variances and recommending alignment actions for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R015	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Tax and Regulatory Compliance	The system must automatically apply applicable taxes on THC charges based on the billing entity jurisdiction: 5% VAT for Qatar, 5% VAT for UAE, 15% VAT for KSA, and 18% GST for India with correct CGST/SGST/IGST split based on place of supply rules. Tax calculations must reference the tax master maintained by the central taxation module and reflect any regulatory rate changes effective on the invoice date. Tax registration numbers (TRN for GCC, GSTIN for India) must be validated and displayed on all invoices. The system must generate tax-compliant e-invoice formats including ZATCA Phase 2 for KSA and GST e-invoice for India with proper QR code generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R016	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Bulk Upload and Mass Rate Update	The system must support bulk upload of THC rates via structured Excel or CSV templates with validation rules enforced at import time. The bulk upload must validate mandatory fields including terminal code, container type ISO code, movement type, effective date, currency, and rate amount. Validation must reject records with invalid terminal codes, unsupported currencies, overlapping effective dates, or negative rate amounts and produce a detailed bilingual error report. Successfully validated records must enter an approval workflow before activation. The system must support mass rate updates applying percentage increases or decreases across selected terminals, container types, or movement types with preview and confirmation before execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-001-R017	FEAT-045-1-001	MOD-045	THC Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive THC revenue reports segmented by terminal, shipping line, container type, movement type, and billing period with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period support. Reports must be available in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic output including right-aligned numeric columns with Arabic-Indic numerals as an option. Standard report templates must include monthly THC revenue summary, customer-wise THC analysis, container type mix analysis, and year-over-year growth comparison. All reports must be exportable to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with digital signature support for official document distribution to port authorities and regulatory bodies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R001	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Vessel Port Dues Tariff Configuration	The system must maintain a configurable vessel port dues tariff schedule for each port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA) with rates defined per gross registered tonnage (GRT) or net registered tonnage (NRT) bands. Each tariff entry must specify the port code, tonnage calculation basis (GRT or NRT), tonnage band ranges, rate per ton in local currency (QAR, AED, INR, SAR) and USD equivalent, effective date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and vessel type classification (container vessel, tanker, general cargo, Ro-Ro, passenger). The system must support multiple concurrent tariff schedules for different vessel flag states where preferential rates apply.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R002	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Automatic Vessel Tonnage Extraction	The system must automatically extract vessel particulars including GRT, NRT, LOA (length overall), beam, and draft from the vessel master database or incoming port call notification messages. When vessel data is received via EDI BERMAN message or API integration with port community systems, the system must validate tonnage figures against Lloyd's Register or equivalent vessel classification database. Discrepancies exceeding 5% must be flagged for manual verification. For vessels not found in the master database, the system must create a provisional record and trigger a data enrichment workflow requiring port authority confirmation before final port dues calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R003	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Dues Calculation Engine	The system must calculate vessel port dues automatically upon confirmed vessel arrival using the applicable tariff schedule, vessel tonnage, and duration of port stay. The calculation must support multiple charging methodologies including flat rate per GRT per port call, rate per GRT per day or part thereof, tiered rate structures with reducing rates for extended stays exceeding configurable day thresholds, and minimum charge enforcement regardless of vessel size. The engine must handle partial day rounding rules as configured per port authority regulation (round up to next full day, round to nearest half day, or charge actual hours). All calculations must produce detailed breakdowns viewable in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R004	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Wharfage Charge by Cargo Type	The system must calculate wharfage charges based on cargo classification with configurable rates per metric ton for break-bulk cargo, per TEU for containerized cargo, per cubic meter for volumetric cargo, and per unit for vehicles and heavy lift items. The wharfage tariff must support differential rates based on cargo commodity codes (at minimum HS code 4-digit level), direction (import or export), and whether cargo is transshipment or local delivery. At JNPT the system must additionally support Indian Customs tariff classification alignment. Wharfage charges must be billable to the vessel agent, shipping line, or cargo owner as configured per port authority rules and customer agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R005	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Berth Hire and Occupancy Charges	The system must calculate berth hire charges based on vessel LOA and actual berth occupancy duration from the time of first line ashore to last line cast off as reported by the port operations system. Berth hire rates must be configurable per berth location, berth type (container quay, multi-purpose, tanker jetty), and vessel LOA bands. The system must support penalty surcharge rates for vessels exceeding their allotted berth window as defined in the berth allocation schedule. Overstay surcharges must be calculated in configurable time increments (hourly, per 6 hours, per day) and automatically added to the vessel port call invoice with clear bilingual line-item descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R006	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Dues Exemptions and Preferential Rates	The system must support port dues exemptions and preferential rate schedules for qualifying vessels including government-owned vessels, naval vessels on courtesy calls, vessels in distress seeking safe harbor, vessels calling under bilateral trade agreement flags, and vessels participating in port authority loyalty programs. Each exemption category must have configurable eligibility criteria, required supporting documentation, approval workflow requirements, and maximum annual exemption budget limits per entity. The system must track cumulative exemptions granted per category per fiscal year and Hijri year, generating alerts when budget thresholds reach 80% and 95% consumption levels with notifications in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R007	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Multi-Port Call Consolidation	The system must support consolidated port dues billing for vessels making multiple calls at different terminals within the same port complex during a single voyage. For example, a vessel calling at both Container Terminal 1 and Container Terminal 2 at Jebel Ali within a single port stay must receive a single consolidated port dues invoice rather than duplicate charges. The system must identify consolidated port calls by matching vessel IMO number, voyage number, and arrival/departure date windows within a configurable tolerance period. Consolidation rules must be configurable per port authority and must properly allocate wharfage charges to each terminal while consolidating vessel-based port dues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R008	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Authority Tariff Gazette Integration	The system must support import of official port authority tariff gazette publications for each jurisdiction. Tariff gazette documents published by Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani Qatar), Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, and Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority must be parseable via AI-assisted document extraction to automatically populate tariff schedule drafts. Extracted tariff data must enter a verification workflow where billing administrators review and confirm rates before activation. The system must maintain a document repository linking each tariff version to its source gazette publication with reference numbers, publication dates in Gregorian and Hijri formats, and validity periods for regulatory audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R009	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Wharfage Revenue Sharing Calculation	The system must calculate wharfage revenue sharing amounts between port authorities and terminal operators based on concession agreement terms. Revenue sharing formulas must be configurable per terminal concession and may include fixed annual fees, percentage of gross wharfage revenue, tiered percentages based on throughput thresholds, or hybrid structures combining fixed and variable components. The system must generate monthly and quarterly revenue sharing statements with full transaction-level detail supporting reconciliation. Statements must be produced in both Arabic and English with amounts in local currency and USD equivalent, and must be digitally signed for official submission to port authority finance departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R010	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Light Dues and Navigation Charges	The system must calculate light dues and navigation aid charges levied per vessel call based on vessel NRT as mandated by each port's maritime authority. Light dues rates must be configurable per port with support for annual vessel exemption certificates where vessels that have paid annual light dues are exempt from per-call charges. The system must validate vessel light dues exemption certificates by checking certificate number, issuing authority, vessel IMO number, validity dates, and port coverage scope. Expired or invalid certificates must trigger automatic charge application with notification to the vessel agent. Light dues must appear as a separate line item on port call invoices in both Arabic and English with proper regulatory reference codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R011	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Dues Invoice Generation	The system must generate comprehensive port call invoices consolidating all vessel-related charges including port dues, berth hire, light dues, and vessel-based wharfage into a single invoice per port call. Invoice generation must be triggerable automatically upon vessel departure confirmation or manually by billing staff. Each invoice must display the vessel name, IMO number, flag state, GRT/NRT, LOA, voyage number, arrival and departure timestamps, berth numbers occupied, and itemized charge breakdown. Invoices must comply with local tax regulations per jurisdiction with proper VAT or GST treatment and must be producible in Arabic, English, or bilingual format based on customer preference settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R012	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Cargo Wharfage Declaration Processing	The system must process cargo wharfage declarations submitted by shipping lines or their agents detailing the containerized and non-containerized cargo discharged or loaded at each terminal. Declarations must be receivable via EDI IFTMIN or IFTMBF messages, API submissions, or manual entry through a bilingual Arabic-English web form. The system must validate declared quantities against vessel manifest data (BAPLIE and cargo manifest), flagging discrepancies exceeding configurable tolerance thresholds per cargo type. Validated declarations must automatically trigger wharfage charge calculations while discrepant declarations must enter a reconciliation workflow with the declaring party before charges are finalized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R013	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Dues Accrual and Forecasting	The system must maintain real-time port dues revenue accrual based on vessels currently in port with estimated charges calculated from vessel particulars and expected port stay duration. The accrual engine must update estimates as vessel departure times are revised by port operations. Monthly port dues revenue forecasts must be generated using AI analysis of vessel schedule data, historical calling patterns, seasonal trends, and new service announcements from shipping lines. Forecasts must be produced per port, per terminal, and per entity with confidence intervals, displayed in bilingual dashboards with Arabic RTL support, and exportable for integration with corporate financial planning systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R014	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Wharfage Reconciliation with Customs Data	The system must perform automated reconciliation of wharfage charges against customs clearance data to verify that all cargo passing through the port has been properly assessed for wharfage. For Indian operations at JNPT, reconciliation must cross-reference bills of entry and shipping bills from the ICEGATE customs system. For GCC operations, reconciliation must align with customs single window declarations at each respective port. Discrepancies where cargo appears in customs records but has no corresponding wharfage charge must be flagged as potential revenue leakage. The system must generate weekly reconciliation reports categorizing discrepancies by type, value, and aging with bilingual Arabic-English annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R015	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Offline Port Dues Calculation Mode	The system must support offline port dues and wharfage calculation when network connectivity to central servers is unavailable at port locations. The offline mode must cache the current tariff schedules, vessel master data for expected vessel calls, and customer account information locally on authorized workstations. Port dues calculations performed offline must be stored locally with provisional invoice numbers and synchronized to the central system upon connectivity restoration. The synchronization process must detect and resolve conflicts where the same vessel or cargo was billed both offline and online, applying configurable conflict resolution rules and flagging unresolvable conflicts for manual review by the billing supervisor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R016	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Port Dues Comparative Analytics	The system must provide comparative analytics of port dues revenue and vessel traffic across all four port locations with drill-down capabilities by vessel type, flag state, trade lane, and shipping line. Analytics must include average revenue per vessel call, average port stay duration, vessel size distribution trends, and port dues yield per GRT analysis. AI-powered anomaly detection must identify unusual patterns such as sudden drops in vessel calls from specific flag states, abnormal port stay durations suggesting operational inefficiencies, or revenue per vessel deviations from historical norms. Anomaly alerts must be delivered via dashboard notifications and email in both Arabic and English with suggested investigation actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-002-R017	FEAT-045-1-002	MOD-045	Environmental Port Dues Incentives	The system must support environmental incentive programs offering reduced port dues for vessels meeting specified environmental criteria such as Environmental Ship Index (ESI) scores above configurable thresholds, vessels using LNG fuel, vessels connecting to shore power during berth stay, or vessels with valid Green Award certification. Incentive discounts must be configurable as percentage reductions or fixed amount credits per port authority program. The system must validate environmental certifications against issuing authority databases where integration is available, track incentive utilization per vessel and per program period, and generate sustainability reporting showing emission reduction impact estimates in bilingual Arabic-English format for corporate ESG reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R001	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Pilotage Tariff Schedule Configuration	The system must maintain configurable pilotage tariff schedules for each port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA) with rates defined per vessel GRT or LOA bands. Tariff entries must support differential rates for inbound pilotage (sea to berth), outbound pilotage (berth to sea), and shifting pilotage (berth to berth within port). Each tariff entry must specify the port code, pilotage direction, vessel size band boundaries, rate per unit in local currency and USD, effective and expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and whether the rate is per service or per hour. The system must enforce that no overlapping tariff entries exist for the same port, direction, and vessel size combination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R002	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Pilotage Charge Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate pilotage charges upon receipt of pilot boarding and disembarkation events from the port operations or vessel traffic management system. The calculation must determine the applicable tariff based on vessel GRT or LOA, pilotage direction, and the effective tariff date. For time-based pilotage rates, the system must compute the chargeable duration from pilot boarding time to pilot disembarkation time, applying minimum charge rules (typically minimum one hour) and rounding rules as configured per port. Waiting time charges must be calculated separately when pilots are on standby due to vessel delays exceeding configurable free waiting periods, with distinct rates for daytime and nighttime waiting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R003	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Towage Service Rate Management	The system must maintain towage tariff schedules with rates configurable per tug type (harbor tug, escort tug, ASD tug, tractor tug), tug bollard pull capacity bands, vessel GRT or LOA bands, and service type (berthing assist, unberthing assist, escort, emergency). Each rate entry must define the charging unit (per service, per hour, or per tug-hour), minimum charge amount, and any applicable surcharges for night operations, weekend or holiday services, and adverse weather conditions. Rates must be stored in the local port currency with automatic cross-currency conversion capability. The system must support seasonal rate variations where towage demand patterns affect pricing at specific ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R004	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Automatic Tug Allocation Charge Computation	The system must calculate towage charges based on actual tug allocation data received from the port marine operations system. The calculation must account for the number of tugs assigned, individual tug bollard pull ratings, actual service start and end times per tug, and the applicable towage tariff schedule. When multiple tugs of different capacities are assigned to a single vessel movement, the system must calculate charges per tug individually and aggregate them on the vessel service invoice. The system must validate that tug allocation complies with port authority minimum tug requirements based on vessel size and type, flagging any under-allocation for safety compliance reporting in bilingual Arabic-English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R005	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Mooring and Unmooring Service Charges	The system must calculate mooring service charges for line handling operations during vessel berthing and unberthing. Mooring tariffs must be configurable per port, vessel LOA bands, and service type (mooring, unmooring, re-mooring for shifting operations). Charges must support both flat-rate per service and time-based calculation methods. The system must record mooring gang deployment times, number of mooring lines handled, and any additional services such as emergency line tending during adverse weather. Additional charges for mooring services outside standard working hours must be automatically applied based on configurable shift schedules per port, with separate rates for overtime, night shift, and public holidays observed in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R006	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Pilotage Exemption Certificate Management	The system must manage pilotage exemption certificates (PEC) issued to qualified vessel masters who have demonstrated sufficient knowledge of specific port approaches and channels. The PEC registry must store certificate holder details, qualifying vessel particulars, port and channel coverage, issue date, expiry date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and issuing authority reference. When a vessel with a PEC-holding master arrives, the system must verify PEC validity, match vessel particulars against PEC coverage, and either waive pilotage charges or apply reduced PEC-holder rates as configured per port authority rules. Expired PEC usage attempts must trigger automatic alerts to the harbor master office and billing department.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R007	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Marine Services Bundled Pricing	The system must support bundled pricing packages that combine pilotage, towage, and mooring services into a single composite rate per vessel call. Bundled packages must be configurable per port authority or terminal operator with package rates defined by vessel GRT or LOA bands. The system must determine whether a bundled package or individual service pricing yields a lower total charge for each vessel call and flag the cost comparison for vessel agents. Where contractual agreements specify bundled pricing, the system must automatically apply bundle rates and generate consolidated line items on invoices. Bundle pricing must show individual component value breakdowns for transparency, with all amounts displayed in local currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R008	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Cancellation and No-Show Charges	The system must calculate cancellation and no-show charges when scheduled pilotage, towage, or mooring services are cancelled or when vessels fail to arrive at scheduled service times. Cancellation charge rules must be configurable per service type and per port with tiered rates based on cancellation notice period (for example, full charge for less than 2 hours notice, 50% for 2-6 hours notice, no charge for more than 6 hours notice). The system must track the original service booking timestamp, cancellation request timestamp, and compute the notice period to determine the applicable cancellation tier. No-show charges must apply when services are mobilized but the vessel does not present, with charges covering actual resource deployment costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R009	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Overtime and Holiday Surcharge Computation	The system must automatically calculate overtime and holiday surcharges for marine services performed outside standard working hours. Standard working hours must be configurable per port and per entity, supporting different shift patterns for each location. Holiday calendars must include both Gregorian public holidays and Islamic holidays (Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Mawlid, National Days) with dates maintained in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar systems. Surcharge rates must be configurable as percentage markups on base service rates with different percentages for overtime (outside standard hours on working days), weekend (Friday-Saturday or Saturday-Sunday depending on jurisdiction), and public holiday categories. The system must automatically determine which surcharge category applies based on actual service execution timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R010	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Marine Services Invoice Consolidation	The system must consolidate all marine service charges (pilotage, towage, mooring) for a single vessel port call into a consolidated marine services invoice. The invoice must list each service as a separate line item with service type, service date and time, vessel movement reference, applicable tariff details, base charge amount, any surcharges (overtime, holiday, weather), and total per service. The consolidated invoice must include subtotals per service category, applicable VAT or GST per jurisdiction, and grand total in the billing currency with USD equivalent. Invoices must be generatable in Arabic, English, or bilingual format and must reference the vessel port call identifier for cross-referencing with port dues and terminal charges invoices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R011	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Pilot and Tug Resource Cost Tracking	The system must track resource costs associated with delivering pilotage and towage services for profitability analysis. Cost elements must include pilot remuneration per assignment, tug fuel consumption per service, tug crew costs, mooring gang labor costs, and equipment maintenance allocation. The system must calculate service-level profitability by comparing revenue generated per pilotage, towage, or mooring service against the direct costs incurred. Profitability dashboards must display margins by service type, vessel size category, time of day, and port location. Cost data must be maintained in local entity currency with multi-currency consolidation capabilities for group-level analysis and all dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R012	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Weather-Related Service Surcharges	The system must apply weather-related surcharges on towage and pilotage services when services are performed during adverse weather conditions. Weather condition thresholds triggering surcharges must be configurable per port and may include wind speed exceeding specified knots, wave height exceeding specified meters, visibility below specified nautical miles, and tidal current exceeding specified knots. The system must integrate with port meteorological data feeds to automatically determine whether weather surcharge conditions were met during actual service delivery windows. Weather surcharges must be calculated as configurable percentage additions to base service rates and must appear as distinct line items on invoices with weather condition references for billing transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R007	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Multi-Entity Cut-Off Calendar View	The system must provide a consolidated calendar view displaying all active cut-off deadlines across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with filtering by port, vessel, voyage, and cut-off type. The calendar must support both Gregorian and Hijri date display with toggle capability, respecting the user's locale preferences. Arabic language users must see the calendar in RTL layout with Arabic month names and numerals. The view must color-code cut-offs by status: green for open, amber for approaching (within warning window), red for passed, and grey for cancelled. The calendar must support export to PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic text rendering in exported documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R013	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Marine Services Demand Forecasting	The system must provide AI-driven demand forecasting for marine services based on expected vessel traffic, vessel schedule data from shipping lines, historical service patterns, and seasonal trends. Forecasts must predict daily pilotage, towage, and mooring service requirements for a rolling 30-day horizon with hourly granularity for the next 72 hours. The forecasting engine must consider vessel size mix to predict tug requirements by bollard pull category. Forecast outputs must feed into resource planning dashboards showing predicted demand against available capacity for pilots, tugs, and mooring gangs, highlighting potential resource shortfalls. Forecasts must be presented in bilingual dashboards with Arabic RTL support and exportable in standard report formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R014	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Service Delivery Confirmation Workflow	The system must implement a service delivery confirmation workflow where marine service providers confirm actual service delivery details including actual start and end times, resources deployed (pilot name, tug names and bollard pull), and any deviations from the original service order. Confirmation can be submitted via mobile application (supporting Arabic and English interfaces), web portal, or API integration with marine operations systems. Unconfirmed services must trigger escalation notifications after configurable time thresholds following expected service completion. The billing engine must not finalize charges until service delivery is confirmed, maintaining charges in provisional status. Disputed delivery details must enter a resolution workflow between the service provider and vessel agent before invoice finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R015	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Towage Contract and Standing Agreement Management	The system must manage towage contracts and standing agreements between terminal operators, port authorities, and towage service providers. Contract records must store agreed rate schedules, minimum service level commitments, response time guarantees, fleet availability requirements, penalty clauses for non-performance, and revenue sharing arrangements. The system must monitor actual service delivery against contractual commitments and calculate any performance penalties or bonuses due. Contract expiry notifications must be generated at configurable intervals (90, 60, 30 days before expiry) in Arabic and English. The system must support multi-currency contract values where towage providers operate across multiple port entities with automatic currency translation for consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R016	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Pilotage Distance and Zone-Based Charging	The system must support distance-based and zone-based pilotage charging models where pilotage fees vary based on the navigational distance or defined port zones traversed during the pilotage transit. Zone definitions must be configurable per port with named zones (approach channel, inner harbor, basin, terminal berth area) and corresponding rate multipliers. Distance-based models must calculate chargeable distance from pilot boarding station to berth using predefined route distances stored per berth combination. The system must automatically determine the applicable zones traversed based on the origin and destination berth pairing for each pilotage movement and compute the aggregate zone-based charge, displaying zone-by-zone breakdowns on invoices in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-003-R017	FEAT-045-1-003	MOD-045	Marine Services Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all marine service tariff changes, charge calculations, invoice modifications, and dispute resolutions. Every tariff rate modification must record the previous value, new value, effective date, modifying user, approval chain, and business justification in both Arabic and English. Charge calculation audit records must store all input parameters (vessel particulars, service timestamps, tariff version applied, surcharge conditions) enabling complete recalculation verification. The system must support regulatory audit exports in formats required by port state authorities including Qatar's Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Transport General Authority, and India's Directorate General of Shipping with data retention periods of minimum seven years per jurisdiction requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R001	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Storage Tariff Schedule Configuration	The system must maintain configurable storage tariff schedules for each terminal at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with escalating daily rates based on storage duration tiers. Each tariff entry must specify the terminal code, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, hazmat, out-of-gauge), cargo status (import, export, transshipment, empty), free-time days allowance, and escalating rate tiers (for example, days 1-3 free, days 4-7 at rate A, days 8-14 at rate B, days 15+ at rate C). Rates must be stored in local currency (QAR, AED, INR, SAR) with USD equivalents and effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Each tariff schedule must support version control with full modification audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R002	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Free-Time Period Management	The system must manage free-time periods during which no storage charges accrue, configurable per terminal, per container type, per cargo direction, and per customer contract. Standard free-time defaults must be maintainable per port authority regulation while customer-specific free-time extensions must be negotiable through commercial agreements linked to customer master records. Free-time calculation must commence from the container discharge completion timestamp for imports, gate-in timestamp for exports, and last vessel discharge for transshipment. The system must support free-time extensions for containers under customs examination hold, port authority inspection, or force majeure events with proper documentation requirements and approval workflows generating bilingual Arabic-English extension confirmations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R003	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Tiered Storage Rate Calculation Engine	The system must calculate storage charges using tiered escalating rate structures where the per-day rate increases as the storage duration extends beyond configured tier boundaries. The calculation engine must determine the chargeable storage days by subtracting free-time days and any approved extension days from the total calendar days of container presence at the terminal. Chargeable days must be allocated across rate tiers sequentially, computing the charge for each tier independently and summing for total storage cost. The engine must handle mid-tier tariff changes where a container spans a tariff effective date boundary, applying the old rate for days before the change and new rate for days after. Partial day charging rules must be configurable per terminal as full day, half day, or actual hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R004	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Demurrage Free-Time and Charge Calculation	The system must calculate demurrage charges for carrier-owned containers (shipping line equipment) detained beyond the contractually agreed free-time period at the consignee or shipper premises or at the port terminal. Demurrage free-time must be configurable per shipping line tariff, per trade lane, per container type, and per customer agreement with support for combined and separate free-time models (combined detention and demurrage versus separate calculation). The calculation must consider only calendar days or working days as configured, excluding Fridays and Saturdays for GCC operations, Sundays for India operations, and jurisdiction-specific public holidays including Islamic holidays. Demurrage charges must be billable in the shipping line's preferred currency with automatic conversion from local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R005	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Customs Hold Impact on Storage Charges	The system must automatically suspend or modify storage charge calculation when containers are placed under customs hold, port authority hold, or regulatory agency hold. The system must receive hold and release notifications from customs integration interfaces (ICEGATE for JNPT, Qatar Customs for Hamad Port, Dubai Customs for Jebel Ali, Saudi Customs for KSA operations). During hold periods, the system must apply one of three configurable hold charging policies per terminal: full storage charge freeze during hold period, reduced rate application during hold period, or standard rate continuation with hold days tracked for potential refund processing. Hold period documentation must be maintained for dispute resolution and regulatory audit with all timestamps recorded in both Gregorian and Hijri formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R006	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Reefer Container Storage Premium	The system must calculate additional storage premiums for refrigerated containers requiring electrical plug-in connection during terminal storage. Reefer storage premiums must be configurable per terminal with rates defined per day or per hour of plug-in connection, supporting different rates based on required temperature range (frozen below minus 18 degrees, chilled 0 to 5 degrees, controlled atmosphere, ambient ventilated). The system must integrate with terminal reefer monitoring systems to receive actual plug-in duration data, temperature compliance records, and pre-trip inspection status. Power consumption costs must be calculable based on metered kWh usage where available or estimated based on container type and temperature setting. Reefer monitoring failure alerts must trigger escalated storage rate application and notification to the cargo owner in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R007	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Hazardous Cargo Storage Surcharge	The system must apply elevated storage surcharges for containers carrying hazardous goods classified under IMO classes 1 through 9, reflecting the additional safety requirements and specialized storage area allocation. Hazardous cargo storage surcharge rates must be configurable per IMO class, per terminal, and per container type with certain high-risk IMO classes (such as Class 1 explosives and Class 7 radioactive) subject to mandatory accelerated removal rates that escalate more steeply than standard dangerous goods storage. The system must enforce maximum storage duration limits for hazardous containers as mandated by each port authority and generate automatic removal deadline notifications to the container owner, customs broker, and terminal safety officer at configurable intervals before the deadline, with all notifications issued in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R008	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Out-of-Gauge and Special Cargo Storage	The system must calculate storage charges for out-of-gauge (OOG) and special cargo containers that occupy additional yard space beyond standard container footprints. OOG storage rates must be configurable based on overhang dimensions (over-width, over-height, over-length) with multipliers applied to the base storage rate. Flat rack, open top, and platform containers carrying project cargo must be assessable at configurable premium rates reflecting their ground slot consumption. The system must support storage charges for break-bulk cargo stored in designated areas with rates per square meter per day or per metric ton per day. All OOG and special cargo storage charges must reference the original cargo survey report and container dimensions in the billing detail with amounts in local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R009	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Storage Charge Invoice Automation	The system must automatically generate storage charge invoices at configurable intervals including upon container gate-out, upon customs clearance completion, weekly for containers still in yard, or on-demand by billing staff. The invoice must detail each container with its container number, size and type code, vessel and voyage of arrival, discharge date, free-time expiry date, chargeable days per rate tier, rate applied per tier, subtotal per container, applicable taxes, and invoice total. For customers with multiple containers, the system must support consolidated invoices grouping containers by vessel, by customs declaration number, or by import license. Invoice generation must comply with local e-invoicing mandates including ZATCA Phase 2 XML format for KSA and GST e-invoice JSON format for India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R010	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Demurrage and Detention Combined Billing	The system must support combined demurrage and detention (D&D) billing models where shipping lines offer merged free-time pools covering both port demurrage and inland detention periods. The combined model must track the container lifecycle from vessel discharge through terminal gate-out through empty return, allocating days to demurrage or detention phases based on container location events. The system must support separate invoicing for the demurrage component (terminal phase) and detention component (inland phase) even under combined free-time agreements. Shipping line D&D tariff imports must be supported via structured file upload with validation against container type and trade lane matrices. D&D charge calculations must be transparent with day-by-day charge buildup viewable by the customer through a self-service portal in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R011	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	AI-Powered Dwell Time Prediction	The system must provide AI-powered container dwell time prediction based on historical patterns, customs clearance typical durations, consignee behavior profiles, cargo type, trade lane origin, and current port congestion levels. Predictions must be generated upon container discharge from the vessel, estimating the probable gate-out date with confidence intervals. Storage charge projections based on predicted dwell time must be available to terminal operators for yard planning and to customers for cost awareness. The AI model must be retrained at configurable intervals using at least 12 months of historical container movement data per terminal. Prediction accuracy metrics must be tracked and displayed in management dashboards with Arabic RTL support and English formatting options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R012	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Storage Charge Waiver and Discount Management	The system must support storage charge waivers and discounts for qualifying scenarios including first-discharge free storage for new service route promotions, loyalty customer extended free-time, government and humanitarian cargo exemptions, and force majeure event blanket waivers for all containers at affected terminals. Each waiver or discount must be configured with eligibility criteria, maximum discount percentage or amount, validity period, required documentation, and multi-level approval workflow. The system must track cumulative waiver values per customer, per terminal, and per fiscal period (Gregorian and Hijri) with budget limit enforcement. Waiver reports must show revenue impact analysis with full audit trail of approver identities and justification text maintained in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R013	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Empty Container Storage Tariff	The system must manage separate storage tariff schedules for empty containers with different rate structures than laden container storage. Empty container storage rates must be configurable per terminal, per container ownership type (shipping line owned, leased, shipper owned), and per container condition (serviceable, damaged, awaiting repair). The system must support empty container free storage pools negotiated with shipping lines based on throughput commitments where a specified number of empty TEU slots are provided at no charge or reduced rates. Excess empty storage beyond pool allocation must be charged at standard empty storage rates. The system must track empty container inventory aging by shipping line, generating automatic long-dwelling alerts when empties exceed configurable thresholds with notifications to shipping line equipment controllers in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R014	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Storage Revenue Accrual Engine	The system must perform daily storage revenue accrual calculations for all containers currently occupying terminal yard space beyond their free-time period. Accrual entries must be posted to the general ledger per entity and per terminal with proper multi-currency treatment using daily exchange rates from the treasury rate feed. Accrued storage revenue must be categorized by cargo status (import, export, transshipment, empty), container type, and customer account for management reporting granularity. Upon invoice generation, corresponding accrual entries must be reversed and replaced with billed revenue entries ensuring no double counting. Month-end accrual reconciliation reports must compare accrued versus invoiced amounts, identify variances exceeding configurable thresholds, and produce bilingual Arabic-English exception reports for finance department review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R015	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Demurrage Dispute Resolution Tracking	The system must provide a structured demurrage dispute management module where consignees, shipping lines, or freight forwarders can submit disputes against demurrage charges with supporting documentation such as proof of customs hold, evidence of port authority delays, or vessel schedule reliability data. Each dispute must be categorized by type (free-time extension request, charge calculation error, force majeure claim, container availability dispute), assigned a unique case number, and tracked through investigation, negotiation, and resolution phases with SLA timers. The system must maintain a dispute knowledge base using AI to suggest resolution approaches based on historical dispute outcomes for similar scenarios. All dispute correspondence, decisions, and financial adjustments must be recorded in bilingual Arabic-English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R016	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Congestion Surcharge and Peak Season Storage	The system must support congestion surcharges and peak season elevated storage rates that can be activated when terminal yard utilization exceeds configurable threshold percentages. Congestion surcharge activation must be triggerable manually by terminal management or automatically by the system when real-time yard occupancy data from the terminal operating system breaches defined levels (for example, 85% for warning, 90% for surcharge activation). Surcharge rates must be configurable as percentage additions to base storage rates or as separate flat-rate daily charges. The system must provide advance notification of congestion surcharge activation to all affected customers via email and portal announcements in Arabic and English at least 48 hours before effective time, with proper regulatory notification to port authority as required per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R017	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Storage and Demurrage Reporting Suite	The system must provide a comprehensive reporting suite for storage and demurrage operations including container dwell time analysis by terminal and container type, storage revenue reports by period and customer, free-time utilization analysis showing average free-time consumption patterns, demurrage collection efficiency reporting tracking billed versus collected amounts, and long-dwelling container exception reports. All reports must support dual calendar period selection (Gregorian and Hijri), multi-currency display with conversion to a selected reporting currency, Arabic RTL and English LTR layout options, and Arabic-Indic numeral display as a configurable option. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation and distribution via email with export capabilities to PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with proper digital signature support for regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-1-004-R018	FEAT-045-1-004	MOD-045	Intermodal Transfer Storage Charge Handoff	The system must manage storage charge responsibility handoff when containers transfer between terminal storage and intermodal facilities (rail yards, inland container depots, free zone warehouses). The handoff event must clearly delineate the timestamp when terminal storage charges cease and intermodal facility storage charges commence. The system must integrate with inland depot and rail terminal systems to receive container arrival confirmations, ensuring no gap or overlap in storage charge coverage. For containers transiting between Jebel Ali port and Jebel Ali Free Zone or between JNPT and adjacent ICDs, the system must apply agreed transfer protocols with configurable transit time allowances during which neither facility charges storage. Transfer documentation must be generated in bilingual Arabic-English format referencing both terminal and depot container tracking numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R001	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Multi-Port Tariff Data Ingestion	The system must ingest and normalize tariff schedules from Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA) in their native published formats including PDF, XML, and structured API feeds. Tariff data must be parsed into a canonical schema covering berth hire, wharfage, stevedoring, container handling (THC), storage/demurrage, and ancillary charges. The ingestion engine must support both English and Arabic tariff publications, correctly extracting line items from RTL-formatted documents. Each ingested tariff must be timestamped with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates, version-controlled, and linked to the originating port authority entity. Currency fields must capture the native currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Khalifa/Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) and an equivalent USD benchmark rate at ingestion time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R002	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Side-by-Side Tariff Comparison Matrix	The system must render a configurable comparison matrix displaying tariff line items across two or more selected ports simultaneously. Users must be able to select specific charge categories such as THC, wharfage, berth hire, reefer plug-in, hazardous cargo surcharges, and storage tiers for comparison. The matrix must normalize all amounts to a user-selected base currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR) using the ECB or central bank exchange rate for the selected comparison date. Each cell must show the original local currency amount alongside the converted value. The comparison must support filtering by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer, flat-rack, open-top, tank container) and cargo classification (general, hazardous IMO classes 1-9, reefer, overweight, over-dimensional).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R003	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Historical Tariff Trend Analysis	The system must maintain a historical archive of all ingested tariff versions per port spanning a minimum of five years and present year-over-year and quarter-over-quarter trend charts for each charge category. Trend visualization must support line charts, bar charts, and heat maps and allow overlay comparison of multiple ports on a single timeline. The AI engine must automatically detect anomalous tariff changes exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10% increase or decrease) and flag them for analyst review. Historical data must be filterable by Hijri or Gregorian calendar periods and exportable to Excel and PDF in both English and Arabic layouts. The trend analysis must account for currency fluctuations by offering both nominal local currency and real USD-adjusted trend lines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R004	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Regional Benchmarking Index Calculation	The system must compute a composite Port Cost Index (PCI) for each terminal based on weighted averages of THC, wharfage, berth hire, storage, and ancillary charges, using configurable weighting factors per charge category. The PCI must be calculated monthly and benchmarked against the GCC regional average (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, Dammam, Salalah, Sohar) and the broader Indian subcontinent average (JNPT, Mundra, Colombo, Chittagong). The index must support entity-specific views so that the Qatar entity sees benchmarks relevant to its trade lanes while the India entity sees South Asian comparators. Each index value must be accompanied by a confidence interval derived from data completeness and freshness metrics. The system must auto-publish the monthly PCI via dashboard widgets and email digests to configured stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R005	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Tariff Competitiveness Scoring per Trade Lane	The system must calculate a competitiveness score for each port on every active trade lane by comparing total port-call cost (pilotage, towage, berth hire, THC, wharfage, agency fees) against alternate ports serving the same hinterland. For example, for Qatar imports, the system must compare Hamad Port costs against the alternative of transshipment via Jebel Ali plus feeder to Hamad. The scoring algorithm must factor in vessel size categories (feeder, handymax, panamax, neo-panamax, ULCV), average dwell time, and free-storage-day allocations per terminal. Scores must be presented on a normalized 0-100 scale with color-coded indicators (green for competitive, amber for neutral, red for expensive relative to peers). The AI must update scores automatically when new tariff data is ingested or when exchange rates shift beyond a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R006	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Container-Type Specific Cost Breakdown	The system must provide granular cost breakdowns for each container type including 20-foot dry (20GP), 40-foot dry (40GP), 40-foot high-cube (40HC), 45-foot high-cube (45HC), 20-foot reefer (20RE), 40-foot reefer (40RE), flat-rack (20FR/40FR), open-top (20OT/40OT), and ISO tank containers across all benchmarked ports. Each breakdown must itemize charges for handling, wharfage, storage per day bands (free days, days 1-5, 6-10, 11-20, 21+), reefer monitoring and electricity, and special equipment surcharges. The system must highlight which port offers the lowest total cost per container type for a given standard dwell time scenario (e.g., 5-day, 10-day, 15-day). Breakdowns must be viewable in the user's preferred currency and language, with full Arabic RTL support for labels, totals, and footnotes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R007	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	AI-Driven Tariff Change Impact Simulation	The system must provide an AI simulation engine that allows users to model the financial impact of hypothetical tariff changes at any benchmarked port. Users must be able to adjust individual charge categories by percentage or absolute amount and see the cascading effect on total port-call cost, trade-lane competitiveness scores, and annual cost projections per entity. The simulation must support scenario naming, saving, versioning, and comparison of up to five scenarios side by side. The AI must auto-generate a narrative summary in both English and Arabic describing the key impacts, winners, and losers among the compared ports. Simulation results must be exportable as a board-ready PDF report including charts, tables, and executive summary formatted for C-suite stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R008	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Multi-Currency Normalization and FX Rate Management	The system must maintain a foreign exchange rate table covering USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with daily updates sourced from a configurable provider (default: central bank feeds from QCB, CBUAE, SAMA, and RBI). For benchmarking purposes, users must be able to select between spot rate, monthly average, or a user-defined fixed rate for currency conversion. The system must handle the AED and SAR USD pegs correctly (3.6725 AED/USD and 3.75 SAR/USD) while allowing override for scenario planning. All benchmark reports must display the FX rate used, its source, and effective date. When operating offline, the system must use the last cached exchange rate and clearly watermark outputs with a stale rate warning indicator showing the age of the cached rate in hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R009	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Peer Port Selection and Custom Benchmark Groups	The system must allow users to define custom benchmark groups beyond the default GCC and South Asia groups. Users must be able to create named groups containing any combination of supported ports and assign group-specific weighting schemes. The system must support a minimum of 20 custom benchmark groups per entity. Each group must be assignable to specific trade lanes, cargo types, or business units. The AI must suggest optimal peer groupings based on trade volume similarity, geographic proximity, and cargo mix analysis. Group definitions must be auditable with full change history, and modifications must require approval from a user with the Tariff Analyst or Finance Manager role. Custom groups must be shareable across entities with entity-level visibility controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R010	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Automated Tariff Publication Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor port authority websites and official gazette publications for tariff updates at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT using AI-powered web scraping and document analysis. When a new tariff circular or gazette notification is detected, the system must automatically parse the document, extract changed line items, and generate a change-delta report comparing old versus new rates with percentage differences. Notifications must be sent via email, in-app alert, and SMS to configured tariff analysts within 30 minutes of detection. The monitoring must handle Arabic-language publications from Qatari and Saudi port authorities and English publications from Indian and UAE ports. Each detected change must be queued for human review and confirmation before being incorporated into the live benchmarking dataset.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R011	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Benchmarking Dashboard with Drill-Down Capability	The system must provide a dedicated benchmarking dashboard presenting key metrics including Port Cost Index trends, charge-category heatmaps, competitiveness scores, and cost-per-TEU rankings. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary level to individual charge-category level and further to specific tariff line-item level. All dashboard widgets must support Arabic RTL rendering when the user's locale is set to Arabic, with proper right-to-left number formatting and Hijri date display. The dashboard must load within three seconds for up to 50,000 tariff records and support concurrent access by at least 100 users. Users must be able to configure personal dashboard layouts, pin favorite comparisons, and schedule automated dashboard snapshots for email distribution in PDF format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R012	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Entity-Specific Tariff Visibility and Access Control	The system must enforce entity-level data segregation so that the Qatar entity's negotiated tariff rates at Hamad Port are not visible to the India entity's users and vice versa. Published standard tariffs must be visible across all entities, but any negotiated, contracted, or volume-discounted rates must be restricted to the owning entity. Role-based access control must support roles including Tariff Viewer (read-only benchmarks), Tariff Analyst (full comparison and simulation access), and Tariff Administrator (data ingestion and group management). Cross-entity benchmark reports must use only published standard rates unless a specific inter-entity data sharing agreement is configured and approved by both entity administrators. All access attempts to restricted tariff data must be logged in the audit trail with user identity, timestamp, and IP address.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R013	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Export and Reporting for Benchmarking Results	The system must support exporting benchmarking results in Excel (XLSX), PDF, CSV, and JSON formats. Excel exports must include separate worksheets for summary comparison, detailed line items, trend data, and methodology notes. PDF exports must support both English left-to-right and Arabic right-to-left layouts with proper formatting of currency symbols, number groupings (e.g., Indian lakhs/crores for INR amounts, standard thousands for others), and bilingual headers. CSV exports must use UTF-8 encoding with BOM for Arabic character support. All exports must include metadata headers showing the comparison date range, ports included, exchange rates used, and the generating user's name and entity. The system must support scheduled report generation with configurable frequency (daily, weekly, monthly) aligned to either Gregorian or Hijri calendar periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R014	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Offline Benchmarking Data Cache	The system must support offline access to the most recent benchmarking dataset including the last 12 months of tariff snapshots, current competitiveness scores, and cached exchange rates. When network connectivity is unavailable, the user must still be able to view cached comparison matrices, historical trends, and saved simulation scenarios. The offline cache must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 and must not exceed 500 MB per user device. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize any offline-created simulation scenarios or benchmark group modifications with the central server using conflict resolution that preserves the most recent change. The offline mode must display a persistent visual indicator showing the cache freshness date and warn users when cached data is older than 48 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R015	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Integration with Shipping Line Tariff Portals	The system must integrate with major shipping line tariff portals and rate management systems to cross-reference terminal charges against ocean freight rates for total landed-cost benchmarking. The integration must support data feeds from at least the top 10 container shipping lines by TEU capacity (MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM, COSCO, Hapag-Lloyd, Evergreen, ONE, HMM, Yang Ming, ZIM). Terminal charges must be mappable to specific BL-level shipments to enable actual cost versus benchmark comparison at the consignment level. The system must handle rate validity periods, GRI (General Rate Increase) schedules, and peak-season surcharges in the cross-reference analysis. API connections must authenticate via OAuth 2.0 and support retry logic with exponential backoff for transient failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-001-R016	FEAT-045-2-001	MOD-045	Tariff Regulatory Compliance Tracking	The system must track regulatory frameworks governing port tariffs in each jurisdiction including Qatar's MWANI regulations, UAE Federal Transport Authority directives, Saudi MISA port pricing guidelines, and India's TAMP (Tariff Authority for Major Ports) orders. When a regulatory change affects tariff structures or ceiling rates, the system must flag impacted benchmark comparisons and alert users that underlying regulatory assumptions may have changed. The system must maintain a regulatory reference library linking each tariff line item to its governing regulation with effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Compliance status must be displayed on benchmark reports indicating whether compared tariffs are within regulatory ceilings or represent deregulated competitive rates. The AI must generate quarterly regulatory impact summaries for each entity's primary ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R001	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Automated Invoice Ingestion from Terminal Operators	The system must ingest terminal invoices from Hamad Port (QTerminals), Khalifa Port (KCST/Abu Dhabi Terminals), Jebel Ali (DP World), and JNPT (JNPCT/APM Terminals/NSIGT/BMCT) via electronic data interchange (EDI INVOIC D.16A), API integration, structured email attachment parsing (PDF with OCR), and manual upload. Each ingested invoice must be parsed to extract header fields (invoice number, date, terminal operator, vessel, voyage, port, currency) and line items (charge code, description, container number, quantity, unit rate, amount, tax). The system must handle invoices in AED, QAR, SAR, INR, and USD. Arabic-language invoices from Qatari and Saudi terminals must be parsed with Arabic OCR capability with a minimum accuracy of 95% for printed Arabic numerals and charge descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R002	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Three-Way Matching: Invoice vs Tariff vs Shipment	The system must perform automated three-way matching comparing each invoice line item against (1) the contracted or published tariff rate for the specific charge category, port, and container type, (2) the actual shipment record from the TOS (Terminal Operating System) or vessel operations module confirming the service was rendered (e.g., container was actually discharged, reefer was actually plugged in for N days, storage actually exceeded free days). Matching must account for container type-specific rates (20GP vs 40HC vs reefer), weight-based surcharges for overweight containers exceeding port-specific thresholds, and time-based charges calculated to the hour or day. The system must flag any line item where the invoiced amount deviates from the expected amount by more than a configurable tolerance (default: 1% or USD 5, whichever is greater). Each match result must be categorized as Matched, Overcharged, Undercharged, or Unmatched.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R003	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	AI-Powered Discrepancy Detection and Classification	The system must employ AI/ML models trained on historical invoice data to detect discrepancies beyond simple rate comparison, including duplicate charges for the same container/service combination, charges for services not contracted or not applicable to the vessel/cargo type, incorrect container count versus manifest data, storage charges computed from wrong gate-in date, reefer monitoring charges for non-reefer containers, and application of expired tariff rates when new rates are effective. The AI must classify each detected discrepancy by root cause category (rate error, quantity error, duplicate charge, phantom charge, date calculation error, tax computation error, wrong container type applied). Classification accuracy must exceed 90% as measured against human-reviewed validation samples. Each AI-detected discrepancy must include a confidence score and supporting evidence extracted from source documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R004	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Dispute Case Creation and Lifecycle Management	The system must automatically create a dispute case for each invoice containing one or more flagged discrepancies, assigning a unique dispute reference number following the pattern DSP-{PORT_CODE}-{YYYY}-{SEQ} (e.g., DSP-QAHMD-2026-00142). Each dispute case must track status through configurable workflow stages: Draft, Submitted to Terminal, Under Review, Counter-Offered, Accepted, Rejected, Escalated, and Closed. The system must enforce SLA timers for each stage with configurable durations per port and terminal operator (default: 7 calendar days for initial response, 14 days for resolution). SLA breach alerts must be sent to the assigned dispute analyst and their manager via email and in-app notification. The dispute case must consolidate all related evidence including the original invoice, tariff extracts, shipment records, gate-in/out timestamps, and any correspondence with the terminal operator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R005	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Automated Dispute Communication Generation	The system must auto-generate dispute letters and emails to terminal operators using AI-composed text based on the specific discrepancies detected. Generated communications must be available in English and Arabic, with the appropriate language selected based on the terminal operator's jurisdiction and communication preference (Arabic for Hamad Port and Saudi terminals, English for Jebel Ali and JNPT). Each communication must reference the specific invoice number, line items disputed, expected versus invoiced amounts, supporting tariff clause references, and requested resolution (credit note, rate correction, or additional documentation). The AI must adapt the tone and formality level based on the dispute stage (initial notification is formal, follow-ups are more assertive, escalations reference contractual SLA obligations). All generated communications must be reviewed and approved by the dispute analyst before sending.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R006	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Credit Note Tracking and Reconciliation	The system must track credit notes issued by terminal operators in response to successful disputes, matching each credit note to the original disputed invoice and specific line items. Credit note amounts must be validated against the disputed amount to ensure full resolution; partial credits must be flagged for further follow-up. The system must support credit notes in the original invoice currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) and handle FX differences when a credit note is issued in a different currency than the original invoice due to contractual terms. Reconciled credit notes must automatically update the accounts payable ledger in the ERP financial module and adjust the terminal operator's account balance. The system must generate a monthly credit note summary per terminal operator per entity showing total credits received versus total disputed amounts, providing a dispute recovery rate metric.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R007	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Invoice Validation Rules Engine	The system must provide a configurable rules engine allowing tariff analysts to define custom validation rules beyond standard three-way matching. Rules must support conditional logic including IF-THEN-ELSE constructs, mathematical operators, date arithmetic, and lookup functions against master data (container registry, vessel registry, tariff tables). Example rules include: if container dwell time is within free days per tariff schedule, then storage charge must be zero; if vessel LOA exceeds 300 meters at Hamad Port, then large vessel surcharge must apply; if cargo is classified IMO Class 3 at JNPT, then hazmat surcharge rate X must apply. Each rule must be versioned, have an effective date range, and be assignable to specific ports or terminal operators. The rules engine must execute all applicable rules per invoice line item within 2 seconds and produce a detailed rule-execution log for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R008	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Dispute Analytics and Recovery Dashboard	The system must provide a dispute analytics dashboard showing key performance indicators including total invoices processed, discrepancy detection rate, total disputed value, total recovered value (credit notes received), average dispute resolution time, disputes by root cause category, disputes by terminal operator, and dispute aging analysis. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port, terminal operator, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and currency. Trend charts must show month-over-month dispute volumes and recovery rates for the trailing 24 months. The AI must generate natural language insights highlighting significant changes such as a spike in rate errors at a specific terminal or a decline in recovery rate for a particular charge category. Dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R009	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Tax and VAT Validation on Terminal Invoices	The system must validate tax and VAT calculations on terminal invoices against jurisdiction-specific tax rules: Qatar (zero-rated for port services under current regime), UAE (5% VAT under Federal Tax Authority rules), KSA (15% VAT under ZATCA regulations), and India (18% GST with IGST/CGST/SGST split depending on intra-state vs inter-state classification at JNPT). The system must verify that the correct tax rate is applied per charge category, that the tax base amount is correctly computed (excluding non-taxable items), and that the total tax amount is arithmetically accurate. For Indian GST, the system must validate GSTIN numbers, HSN/SAC codes, and reconcile against GSTR-2B auto-populated returns. Tax validation errors must be classified separately from tariff rate errors in the dispute workflow to ensure proper routing to tax specialists versus operations analysts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R010	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Bulk Invoice Processing and Batch Validation	The system must support bulk processing of terminal invoices with the ability to ingest and validate up to 500 invoices in a single batch operation. Batch jobs must run asynchronously with progress tracking visible to the user in real time, showing invoices processed, matched, flagged, and errored. Each batch must produce a summary report categorizing invoices into fully matched (no discrepancies), partially matched (some line items flagged), and rejected (unable to parse or match). Users must be able to configure scheduled batch runs (daily, weekly) to automatically process invoices received via EDI or email integration. Batch processing must handle mixed-currency invoices within a single batch (e.g., QAR invoices from Hamad Port and INR invoices from JNPT in the same run). Failed invoice parsing must not block the processing of remaining invoices in the batch; errors must be quarantined for manual review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R011	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Dispute Escalation and Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a multi-level approval workflow for dispute escalation based on disputed amount thresholds configurable per entity. Default thresholds must be: Level 1 (Operations Analyst) up to USD 5,000, Level 2 (Operations Manager) USD 5,001 to USD 25,000, Level 3 (Finance Director) USD 25,001 to USD 100,000, Level 4 (CFO/VP) above USD 100,000. Currency conversion for threshold evaluation must use the same exchange rate applied to the original invoice. Escalation must automatically route the dispute to the next approval level if the current level does not act within the SLA period. Each approval action must capture the approver's decision (approve, reject, modify, return for more information), comments, and digital signature timestamp. The workflow must support delegation for approvers who are out of office, with configurable delegation rules per role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R012	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Terminal Operator Master Data Management	The system must maintain a master registry of terminal operators across all supported ports including QTerminals (Hamad Port), Abu Dhabi Terminals and KCST (Khalifa Port), DP World (Jebel Ali), and JNPCT, APM Terminals Gateway, NSIGT, and BMCT (JNPT). Each terminal operator record must store legal entity name (English and Arabic), trade license number, tax registration numbers (VAT/GST), bank account details for payment, contracted tariff schedule references, billing contact details, dispute contact details, preferred communication language, standard payment terms, and historical performance metrics (average invoice accuracy rate, average dispute resolution time). The master data must support effective dating so that changes to tariff schedules, tax registrations, or contact details are tracked with full audit history. Integration with the ERP vendor master module must ensure terminal operator records are synchronized bidirectionally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R013	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Supporting Document Attachment and Evidence Management	The system must allow users to attach supporting documents to dispute cases including scanned delivery orders, equipment interchange receipts (EIR), gate-in/gate-out tickets, reefer temperature logs, vessel bay plans, photographs of container condition, and email correspondence with terminal operators. Supported file formats must include PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, XLSX, DOCX, and MSG (Outlook email). Each attachment must be indexed with metadata including document type, upload date, uploader identity, and relevance description. The total attachment size per dispute case must support up to 100 MB. The AI must automatically extract relevant data points from attached documents (e.g., gate-in timestamp from a gate ticket scan, container number from an EIR) and cross-reference them against the disputed invoice line items. All attachments must be stored with encryption at rest and must be included in dispute case exports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R014	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Offline Invoice Review and Dispute Drafting	The system must support offline review of validated invoices and drafting of dispute cases when network connectivity is unavailable, a common scenario at port facilities with unreliable connectivity. Users must be able to view cached invoice validation results, add comments and annotations to flagged line items, draft dispute communications, and attach photos taken on-device. Upon reconnection, all offline actions must synchronize with the central server with conflict detection and resolution. If the same invoice has been modified by another user while offline, the system must present a side-by-side conflict resolution interface showing both versions. Offline capability must cover the last 30 days of invoice data with a maximum local storage footprint of 1 GB. The offline mode must clearly indicate which data is locally cached versus requiring network access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R015	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for all invoice validation and dispute management activities. Every action must be logged including invoice ingestion, validation rule execution results, discrepancy detection, dispute case creation, status transitions, approvals, communications sent, credit note receipt, and case closure. Each audit entry must capture the action type, timestamp (UTC and local time zone), user identity, IP address, entity context, and before/after state for any data modifications. The audit trail must be queryable by authorized compliance officers with filters for date range, user, entity, port, and action type. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law on Commercial Transactions, Saudi Commercial Court regulations, and Indian Companies Act requirements. Audit logs must be exportable in formats suitable for submission to external auditors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R016	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Automated Invoice Approval for Matched Invoices	The system must support a configurable auto-approval workflow for invoices that pass all validation checks with zero discrepancies detected. When enabled, fully matched invoices must be automatically approved for payment and forwarded to the accounts payable module without manual intervention, achieving the 95% AI automation target. Auto-approval must be configurable per entity, per terminal operator, and per charge-amount threshold (e.g., auto-approve only if total invoice amount is below USD 50,000). Invoices auto-approved must still be logged in the audit trail with an automated approval flag and the validation score. The system must track the auto-approval rate as a KPI and alert administrators if the rate drops below a configurable minimum (default 80%), which may indicate tariff data staleness or systematic billing changes by a terminal operator. Manual override must always be available to pause auto-approval for specific terminal operators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-002-R017	FEAT-045-2-002	MOD-045	Dispute Resolution SLA Reporting	The system must generate SLA compliance reports for dispute resolution measuring actual resolution times against contractual SLAs with each terminal operator. Reports must segment SLA performance by terminal operator, port, dispute category (rate error, quantity error, duplicate charge, etc.), and disputed amount range. The system must calculate and display metrics including percentage of disputes resolved within SLA, average days to resolution, median days to resolution, and total financial exposure of open disputes past SLA. Reports must highlight chronic SLA breaches by terminal operator to support contract renegotiation discussions. SLA reports must be available in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic, support Hijri calendar date grouping for Saudi and Qatari entities, and be distributable via scheduled email in PDF and Excel formats to entity-level finance leadership.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R001	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	AI Cost Optimization Engine Architecture	The system must implement an AI cost optimization engine that ingests data from multiple modules including port tariff benchmarks, historical invoice data, vessel schedules, container tracking, storage/demurrage records, and trade lane volumes. The engine must utilize gradient-boosted decision trees and neural network models trained on a minimum of 24 months of historical transaction data across all four port operations (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT). The engine must process optimization calculations in batch mode (nightly) and on-demand mode (triggered by user or event). All recommendations must include a confidence score (0-100%), estimated annual savings in the entity's base currency and USD equivalent, implementation complexity rating (low/medium/high), and a payback period estimate. The engine must support multi-entity operation, generating entity-specific recommendations while also identifying cross-entity optimization opportunities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R002	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Port Selection Optimization for Import/Export	The system must recommend optimal port selection for import and export shipments by comparing total port-call costs across feasible alternatives. For Qatar-bound cargo, the system must evaluate Hamad Port direct call versus Jebel Ali transshipment plus feeder, including transshipment handling charges, feeder freight differential, and additional transit time cost. For India-bound cargo, the system must compare JNPT against Mundra and other west coast alternatives where applicable. Recommendations must factor in total landed cost including ocean freight differential, terminal handling charges, inland transportation from port to final destination, storage risk based on historical dwell times, and customs clearance speed differences. The AI must weight factors based on cargo urgency (standard vs time-sensitive), cargo type (dry, reefer, hazmat), and container volume to produce shipment-specific or trade-lane-level recommendations. All currency comparisons must use consistent exchange rates with proper QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD conversions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R003	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Dwell Time Reduction Recommendations	The system must analyze container dwell time patterns at each terminal and generate recommendations to reduce storage and demurrage costs. The AI must identify root causes of excess dwell time including late documentation clearance, customs examination delays, consignee pickup delays, and container yard congestion. Recommendations must be specific and actionable, such as shifting customs pre-clearance submission from 2 days before arrival to 5 days before arrival at JNPT, or coordinating with QTerminals for priority delivery order processing at Hamad Port. The system must calculate the financial impact of each recommendation by multiplying the projected dwell time reduction (in days) by the applicable storage tariff rate per container type. Historical dwell time distributions must be analyzed using statistical process control to distinguish between common-cause variation and special-cause spikes requiring different interventions. Recommendations must be refreshed weekly and prioritized by potential savings magnitude.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R004	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Tariff Negotiation Strategy Recommendations	The system must generate data-driven tariff negotiation recommendations for use in annual contract renewals with terminal operators. The AI must analyze the entity's volume profile (TEU throughput by container type, cargo category, and seasonality), compare current contracted rates against published tariffs and benchmark indices, identify charge categories where the entity is paying above market rate, and propose target rates with supporting justification. Negotiation recommendations must include volume commitment scenarios (e.g., committing 10% more TEU in exchange for 5% THC discount) with break-even analysis. The system must generate negotiation briefing documents in both English and Arabic containing the entity's volume history, spend analysis, market benchmarks, and proposed rate card. The AI must learn from past negotiation outcomes (accepted/rejected rate proposals) to refine future recommendations. Recommendations must respect entity-level confidentiality so that one entity's negotiated rates are not used in another entity's strategy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R005	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Container Routing Optimization to Minimize Port Costs	The system must optimize container routing through the port network to minimize aggregate terminal costs considering multi-leg shipments and transshipment scenarios. The AI must model the full container journey including first port of discharge, transshipment ports, and final destination port to compute total terminal handling costs across the chain. For example, a container moving from Shanghai to Doha could be routed CMA CGM direct to Hamad Port or via Jebel Ali transshipment, and the system must compare total THC, wharfage, and handling costs for both routings. The optimization must respect constraints including shipping line service schedules, transit time limits per INCOTERM requirements (especially DDP and CIF obligations), and regulatory restrictions (e.g., certain cargo types prohibited at specific terminals). Multi-currency cost aggregation must correctly sum QAR, AED, and USD charges using consistent exchange rates, and present total cost in the user's preferred reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R006	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Seasonal Cost Pattern Detection and Advisory	The system must detect and predict seasonal cost patterns at each port using time-series analysis of historical tariff data, surcharge applications, and congestion-related cost spikes. The AI must identify recurring patterns such as Ramadan-period slowdowns affecting dwell times at GCC ports, Indian monsoon season port congestion at JNPT increasing storage charges, year-end volume surges triggering peak-season surcharges at Jebel Ali, and Hajj-period capacity constraints at Saudi ports. Based on detected patterns, the system must generate forward-looking advisories recommending shipment timing adjustments to avoid high-cost periods. Advisories must be generated at least 45 days in advance and must include both Gregorian and Hijri calendar references for Islamic seasonal events. The AI must quantify the potential cost differential between peak and off-peak periods per container type and present the information in the user's preferred language (English or Arabic) with full RTL support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R007	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Free Days Optimization Analysis	The system must analyze container free-day utilization across all terminals and recommend optimal free-day negotiation targets for contract renewals. The analysis must compare the entity's actual dwell time distribution against current free-day allocations to identify terminals where the entity is consistently under-utilizing free days (paying for unused benefit in the tariff rate) or over-utilizing free days (incurring significant storage charges). The AI must model the cost impact of different free-day scenarios (e.g., current 5 free days vs proposed 7 free days at Jebel Ali for 40HC containers) considering the trade-off between higher base THC rates that terminals may charge for extended free days versus reduced storage charges. Recommendations must be container-type-specific as reefer containers typically have different free-day structures due to plug-in charges. The system must present a break-even analysis showing the TEU volume threshold at which extended free days become cost-effective.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R008	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Equipment Type Optimization for Cost Reduction	The system must recommend optimal container equipment types to minimize port handling costs where cargo characteristics permit flexibility. For example, the AI should identify shipments using 2x20GP containers that could be consolidated into 1x40HC at lower total THC cost, or cargo in standard containers that could use flat-racks or open-tops at certain terminals where special equipment rates are paradoxically lower due to promotional tariffs. The system must analyze historical cargo dimensions, weights, and commodity codes against port-specific tariff structures for each equipment type to identify optimization opportunities. Recommendations must respect cargo constraints including weight limits per container type at each port (e.g., JNPT 30-ton limit for 20GP on certain berths), stacking restrictions for special containers, and shipping line equipment availability. Each recommendation must show the per-shipment and projected annual savings with calculations transparent and auditable, displayed in the relevant local currency and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R009	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Recommendation Approval and Implementation Tracking	The system must provide a workflow for reviewing, approving, and tracking implementation of AI-generated cost optimization recommendations. Each recommendation must be assignable to a responsible user with a target implementation date. The approval workflow must support entity-specific configurations with minimum two-level approval for recommendations involving contractual changes (e.g., tariff renegotiation) and single-level approval for operational recommendations (e.g., dwell time reduction actions). The system must track recommendation status through stages: Generated, Under Review, Approved, In Implementation, Implemented, Measuring Results, and Closed. Implemented recommendations must be measured against their projected savings using actual cost data from the invoice validation module for a configurable measurement period (default 6 months). The system must calculate actual versus projected savings and feed this accuracy data back into the AI model to improve future recommendation quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R010	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Cost Optimization Opportunity Dashboard	The system must display a dedicated cost optimization dashboard presenting all active recommendations ranked by estimated savings potential. The dashboard must show aggregate statistics including total identified savings opportunity, savings by category (port selection, dwell time, tariff negotiation, routing, equipment, timing), implementation progress, and realized savings to date. Recommendations must be filterable by entity, port, trade lane, container type, implementation status, confidence score, and savings magnitude. The dashboard must include a savings waterfall chart showing the progression from gross identified opportunity through approved, implemented, and verified savings. All dashboard elements must support Arabic RTL rendering and bilingual labels. The dashboard must display currency amounts in the user's preferred currency with entity-specific defaults (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India). Data refresh must occur within 5 minutes of any recommendation status change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R011	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Machine Learning Model Transparency and Explainability	The system must provide explainable AI output for all cost optimization recommendations, ensuring that users understand why each recommendation was generated and what data supports it. Each recommendation must include a plain-language explanation (in English and Arabic) of the key factors driving the recommendation, the relative weight of each factor, and the sensitivity of the projected savings to changes in key assumptions (e.g., exchange rate changes, volume fluctuations, tariff revisions). The system must support SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) or equivalent feature importance visualization showing which input variables most influenced the recommendation. Users must be able to drill down from a recommendation to the underlying data points including specific invoices, tariff records, and shipment records. Model performance metrics including prediction accuracy, recommendation acceptance rate, and savings realization rate must be visible to system administrators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R012	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Cross-Entity Cost Optimization Opportunities	The system must identify cost optimization opportunities that span multiple entities within the organization, such as consolidating volumes across the Qatar and UAE entities at Jebel Ali to negotiate better group rates, or coordinating container repositioning between JNPT (India entity) and Hamad Port (Qatar entity) to reduce empty container handling charges. Cross-entity recommendations must quantify the benefit to each participating entity separately and allocate projected savings using configurable apportionment rules (e.g., 50/50 split, volume-proportional, or custom). The system must enforce data privacy controls so that cross-entity recommendations are only visible to users with cross-entity access privileges, and entity-specific cost details are masked appropriately. Cross-entity recommendations must require approval from authorized representatives of all participating entities before implementation. The AI must flag potential conflicts where an optimization benefits one entity at the expense of another.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R013	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Real-Time Cost Alert and Threshold Monitoring	The system must monitor port cost metrics in real time and generate alerts when costs exceed configurable thresholds. Alert conditions must include: storage charges exceeding a per-container daily threshold, cumulative demurrage for a vessel call exceeding a total threshold, THC rates applied exceeding contracted rates, total weekly port spend per entity exceeding budget thresholds, and cost-per-TEU exceeding the benchmark index by a configurable percentage. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notification, email, SMS, and webhook integration with enterprise messaging platforms. Each alert must include the current metric value, the threshold breached, trend context (is the metric rising or falling), and an AI-generated suggested action. Alert configuration must support entity-specific thresholds with amounts in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must suppress duplicate alerts within a configurable cool-down period (default 4 hours) to prevent alert fatigue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R014	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	What-If Scenario Modeling for Cost Optimization	The system must provide a what-if scenario modeling tool allowing users to simulate the cost impact of proposed operational changes before implementation. Scenario parameters must include: changing the primary discharge port for a trade lane, modifying container equipment mix, adjusting shipment timing by days or weeks, renegotiating specific tariff line items, changing shipping line allocation affecting terminal assignment, and modifying free-day arrangements. The system must calculate projected annual cost impact across all affected charge categories and present results in a structured comparison format showing current baseline cost versus projected scenario cost with delta analysis. Users must be able to save, name, and share scenarios within their entity. The scenario engine must process calculations within 10 seconds for a single trade lane and within 60 seconds for a full entity portfolio. Scenario results must be exportable to Excel and PDF with bilingual English/Arabic formatting support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R015	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Optimization Recommendation Feedback and Learning Loop	The system must implement a structured feedback mechanism allowing users to rate and comment on AI recommendations to continuously improve model accuracy. Users must be able to rate each recommendation on a 5-point scale for relevance, feasibility, and accuracy of savings estimate. When a recommendation is rejected, the user must provide a rejection reason from a categorized list (not applicable, already implemented, savings overestimated, operationally infeasible, contractually restricted, other) with optional free-text explanation. The AI model must incorporate this feedback into its training pipeline, weighting recent feedback more heavily than older feedback using a time-decay function. The system must track recommendation acceptance rate over time as a model quality metric and alert administrators when the acceptance rate drops below a configurable threshold (default 60%). Feedback data must be aggregated across entities for model improvement while preserving entity-specific confidentiality in the feedback details.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R016	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Regulatory and Compliance Constraint Integration	The system must integrate regulatory and compliance constraints into all optimization recommendations to ensure that cost-saving suggestions do not violate applicable regulations. Constraints must include customs bonded area restrictions at JNPT limiting container movement options, Qatar Customs Authority requirements for direct port delivery versus container freight station routing, UAE Federal Customs Authority transit cargo regulations affecting transshipment optimization at Jebel Ali, Saudi Customs FASAH system requirements affecting port selection for KSA-bound cargo, and IMO dangerous goods segregation rules affecting terminal selection for hazardous cargo. The AI must validate every recommendation against the applicable constraint set before presenting it to users and must flag any recommendation that operates near a regulatory boundary with a compliance risk indicator. Constraint rules must be maintainable by compliance officers without requiring changes to the AI model, using a declarative rules configuration interface with effective date management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-003-R017	FEAT-045-2-003	MOD-045	Sustainability-Aware Cost Optimization	The system must incorporate environmental sustainability factors into cost optimization recommendations, aligning with the UAE Net Zero 2050 strategy, Saudi Vision 2030 green initiatives, Qatar National Vision 2030 environmental pillar, and India's maritime green port initiatives. The AI must calculate the carbon footprint impact of routing recommendations, factoring in vessel fuel consumption differentials between direct and transshipment routes, truck drayage distances from port to hinterland, and terminal energy sources (renewable vs fossil fuel). Recommendations must present a cost-carbon trade-off analysis showing whether a lower-cost option increases carbon emissions and by how much (measured in kg CO2e per TEU). Users must be able to configure a carbon cost shadow price (default USD 50/ton CO2e) to be factored into the total cost calculation for optimization purposes. The system must track and report the cumulative carbon impact of implemented optimization recommendations alongside financial savings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R001	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Multi-Entity Port Cost Budget Structure Definition	The system must support hierarchical budget structures for port and terminal costs across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The budget hierarchy must include levels for entity, port, terminal operator, charge category (THC, wharfage, storage, berth hire, ancillary), container type, and trade lane. Each budget node must support both annual total and monthly distribution with configurable seasonality profiles. The budget structure must allow entities to define budgets in their local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic consolidation to a group reporting currency (USD). The system must support multiple budget versions per fiscal year including original budget, revised budget (up to four revisions), and forecast. Budget structures must be templatable so that the prior year's structure can be rolled forward with adjustable growth rates applied to each node.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R002	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	AI-Assisted Budget Forecasting	The system must provide AI-powered budget forecasting that generates initial budget proposals based on historical cost data, projected trade volumes, committed tariff rates, and macroeconomic indicators. The AI model must analyze a minimum of 36 months of historical port cost data per entity and apply time-series forecasting (ARIMA, Prophet, or equivalent) to project costs by charge category, port, and container type. The forecast must incorporate known future changes including published tariff increases, contracted rate changes, projected TEU volume changes from the commercial plan, and planned new trade lane openings. The AI must generate low, base, and high budget scenarios with clearly stated assumptions for each. Forecasted amounts must be presented in local currency with USD equivalents using forward exchange rate projections. Users must be able to override any AI-generated figure with manual input while preserving the AI baseline for comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R003	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Hijri and Gregorian Fiscal Calendar Support	The system must support budget periods aligned to both Gregorian and Hijri fiscal calendars, as Saudi entities may operate on a Hijri fiscal year while Qatar, UAE, and India entities use Gregorian calendars. The system must correctly handle the Hijri calendar's variable month lengths (29-30 days) and the approximately 11-day annual shift relative to the Gregorian calendar when calculating monthly budget distributions and year-over-year comparisons. Budget reports must display dates in the user's preferred calendar system with the alternate calendar shown in parentheses. The system must support entity-specific fiscal year start months (e.g., January for UAE, April for India following the Indian government fiscal year, potentially Muharram for KSA). Period-end closing processes must accommodate both calendar systems and produce valid accrual calculations regardless of the fiscal calendar in use. Cross-entity consolidation must correctly align periods from different fiscal calendars into a unified group view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R004	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Approval Workflow with Multi-Level Authorization	The system must enforce a multi-level budget approval workflow with configurable approval chains per entity. The default workflow must include stages: Draft, Department Review, Finance Review, Entity Head Approval, and Group CFO Approval (for budgets exceeding a configurable threshold, default USD 5 million annual). Each approval stage must support actions including Approve, Reject (with mandatory reason), Return for Revision (with comments), and Conditional Approve (with conditions that must be addressed). The system must track the complete approval history with timestamps, approver identity, and decision rationale. Approval delegation must be supported for periods when primary approvers are unavailable, with delegation rules configurable per role. Notifications must be sent at each workflow transition via email and in-app alert. The budget must be locked from editing once it enters the approval workflow, with amendments requiring a formal revision process that creates a new budget version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R005	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Real-Time Budget Consumption Tracking	The system must track actual port cost expenditure against approved budgets in real time as terminal invoices are validated and approved for payment. Budget consumption must be calculated at every level of the budget hierarchy (entity, port, terminal, charge category, container type) and displayed as absolute amount spent, percentage consumed, remaining balance, and projected year-end position based on current run rate. The system must update consumption figures within 15 minutes of an invoice being approved for payment. Consumption tracking must handle multi-currency correctly, converting actual invoice amounts (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) to the budget currency using either the budget exchange rate (for budget-to-actual comparison) or actual exchange rate (for financial reporting), with both views available. Committed but not yet invoiced amounts (estimated from vessel discharge reports and expected terminal charges) must be shown as commitments against the budget to provide forward visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R006	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Variance Analysis with AI Insights	The system must perform automated variance analysis comparing actual port costs against budget at every hierarchy level with both amount and percentage variance calculations. Favorable variances (actual below budget) and unfavorable variances (actual above budget) must be clearly distinguished with color coding (green/red) and directional indicators. The AI engine must automatically decompose variances into contributing factors including volume variance (more or fewer TEU than planned), rate variance (actual tariff rate different from budgeted rate), mix variance (different container type or charge category mix than planned), and currency variance (exchange rate movements). Each variance factor must be quantified in the budget currency. The AI must generate natural language variance commentary in English and Arabic explaining the key drivers and their business context (e.g., rate variance at Jebel Ali driven by mid-year THC increase by DP World). Variance analysis must be available monthly, quarterly, and annually.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R007	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Transfer and Reallocation Controls	The system must support budget transfers between cost categories, ports, and periods within an entity, subject to configurable approval rules. Transfers must be classified as within-category (e.g., moving budget from Hamad Port storage to Hamad Port THC), cross-category (e.g., moving budget from wharfage to ancillary charges), or cross-port (e.g., moving budget from Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port). Each transfer type must have a separate approval workflow; default configuration must allow within-category transfers up to 10% of the source budget with manager approval, cross-category transfers with finance director approval, and cross-port transfers with entity head approval. All transfers must create an audit trail showing the source budget node, destination budget node, amount transferred, justification, and approving authority. The system must prevent transfers that would create a negative budget balance at the source node and must recalculate projected year-end positions after each transfer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R008	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Alert and Threshold Notification System	The system must generate proactive alerts when budget consumption reaches configurable threshold levels. Default thresholds must be set at 75% (amber warning), 90% (red warning), and 100% (critical - budget exhausted) of the approved budget at any hierarchy level. Alerts must be delivered to budget owners, their managers, and finance controllers via in-app notification, email, and SMS based on configurable notification preferences per role and alert severity. The system must also generate predictive alerts when the AI projects that a budget line will be exhausted before period end based on current consumption trajectory and known upcoming commitments. Predictive alerts must trigger when the projected exhaustion date is within 60 days. Alert suppression rules must be configurable to prevent repeat notifications within a cool-down period (default 24 hours for amber, 12 hours for red, no suppression for critical). Each alert must include the current budget balance, consumption rate, projected exhaustion date, and a link to the relevant budget detail view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R009	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Port Cost Accrual and Provision Management	The system must support month-end accrual processing for port costs where terminal invoices have not yet been received but services have been rendered. The accrual engine must estimate uninvoiced port costs based on vessel discharge data (containers discharged but not yet invoiced), known storage durations in progress, and historical average costs per port and charge category. Accruals must be calculated in the local currency of each entity and posted to the ERP general ledger via integration with the financial module. The system must support automatic accrual reversal at the start of the following period and re-accrual based on updated estimates. For Hijri fiscal calendar entities, accruals must align with the Hijri month-end dates which shift relative to the Gregorian calendar. The AI must learn from historical accrual-to-actual variance patterns to improve accrual estimate accuracy over time, targeting less than 5% variance between accrued and actual amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R010	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Group Consolidation and Intercompany Elimination	The system must consolidate port cost budgets and actuals across all four entities into a group-level view with proper intercompany elimination for transactions between related entities. For example, if the Qatar entity pays terminal charges at Jebel Ali on behalf of the UAE entity under an intercompany service agreement, the charge must not be double-counted in the group consolidation. The system must support configurable intercompany allocation rules and transfer pricing markup rates for port services provided between entities. Consolidation must convert all entity-level amounts to the group reporting currency (USD) using configurable exchange rate types (budget rate for budget consolidation, average rate for P&L consolidation, closing rate for balance sheet items). Elimination entries must be automatically generated and traceable to their source intercompany transactions. The consolidated view must provide drill-down capability from group total to entity, port, and individual transaction level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R011	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget vs Forecast Rolling Reconciliation	The system must maintain a rolling forecast that updates monthly alongside the static annual budget, enabling budget-to-forecast reconciliation. The rolling forecast must combine actual costs for elapsed months with AI-updated projections for remaining months, creating a latest best estimate (LBE) of full-year port costs. The system must quantify the gap between the original approved budget and the rolling forecast, decomposing the difference into known factors (tariff changes, volume updates, FX movements) and unexplained residual. The rolling forecast must be generated automatically by the AI each month after actual costs are loaded, with manual override capability for budget holders to incorporate business intelligence not captured in the model. Historical forecast accuracy must be tracked showing the forecast-to-actual variance for each month and a cumulative accuracy trend line. The reconciliation view must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period alignment and display amounts in entity local currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R012	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Capital vs Operational Expenditure Classification	The system must classify port costs into capital expenditure (CAPEX) and operational expenditure (OPEX) categories based on configurable rules aligned with IFRS and local accounting standards applicable in each jurisdiction (Qatar Financial Markets Authority standards, UAE IFRS adoption, Saudi SOCPA standards, Indian Accounting Standards). Standard port charges such as THC, wharfage, and storage must be classified as OPEX, while costs such as long-term berth lease payments, port infrastructure contributions, and terminal equipment investments must be classified as CAPEX. The classification must be applied at the budget line item level and must flow through to actual cost classification when invoices are validated. The system must generate separate CAPEX and OPEX budget summaries per entity with appropriate depreciation and amortization schedules for CAPEX items. Misclassification alerts must fire when an invoice charge code maps to a budget line with a different CAPEX/OPEX classification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R013	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Reporting Suite with Multilingual Support	The system must provide a comprehensive budget reporting suite including budget summary by entity, detailed budget by port and charge category, budget consumption report, variance analysis report, rolling forecast report, and budget transfer history. All reports must be available in English and Arabic with full RTL layout support for Arabic versions. Reports must support filtering by entity, port, terminal operator, charge category, container type, budget version, and period (monthly, quarterly, annual in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars). Currency display must follow entity conventions including proper thousand separators (comma for Western, Arabic-Indic numerals option for Arabic layout) and Indian lakh/crore formatting for INR amounts. Reports must be exportable to Excel (with formulas preserved for budget models), PDF (print-ready with entity letterhead), and CSV (UTF-8 with BOM for Arabic support). Scheduled report distribution must support email delivery to configurable recipient lists with entity-level access control.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R014	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Planning Collaboration and Workflow Tools	The system must provide collaboration tools enabling multiple budget contributors to work on different sections of the port cost budget simultaneously. Each budget node must support assignment to a specific budget owner who is responsible for input and justification. The system must implement record-level locking to prevent concurrent editing conflicts, with clear visual indicators showing which budget lines are currently being edited and by whom. Budget contributors must be able to add comments, attach supporting documents (volume forecasts, tariff change notifications, contract summaries), and tag other users for review. A built-in messaging system must allow threaded discussions on specific budget lines. The system must maintain a complete revision history for each budget cell showing all changes, the user who made each change, and the timestamp. Budget templates must support import from Excel for users who prefer to model in spreadsheets, with validation rules applied upon import to ensure data integrity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R015	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Scenario Comparison and Decision Support	The system must allow budget planners to create and compare multiple budget scenarios to support decision-making. At minimum, the system must support creating a conservative scenario (low volume, high cost assumptions), a base scenario (most likely assumptions), and an aggressive scenario (high volume, optimized cost assumptions). Each scenario must be a complete budget with all hierarchy levels populated. The system must provide a side-by-side comparison view showing the differences between scenarios at every budget level. The AI must generate a scenario analysis summary highlighting the key assumption differences and their financial impact in both English and Arabic. Scenarios must support sensitivity analysis showing how the budget changes if a key assumption (e.g., QAR/USD exchange rate, Jebel Ali THC rate, JNPT TEU volume) is varied within a specified range. The system must recommend the optimal scenario based on risk-adjusted expected value calculations, with the recommendation logic transparent and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R006	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	VGM Submission Cut-Off Tracking	The system must independently track VGM (Verified Gross Mass) submission cut-off per SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 requirements. VGM cut-off must be configurable separately from documentation and physical cut-offs, typically set 24-48 hours before ETD depending on the port. The system must validate that VGM data has been received for every container on a booking before allowing the booking to proceed to load list generation. Missing VGM containers must be automatically flagged and excluded from the load list with notifications sent to the booking party. The system must accept VGM submissions via EDI (VERMAS message), API integration, and manual entry with Method 1 (weighing) and Method 2 (calculation) indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R016	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Offline Budget Planning Support	The system must support offline budget planning for users who need to work on budgets during travel or in locations with unreliable connectivity, which is common for port operations staff. The offline mode must allow users to view the current budget structure and data, input and modify budget figures for their assigned budget nodes, add comments and justifications, and view historical cost data for reference. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline changes with the central server using optimistic concurrency control. If conflicts are detected (e.g., another user modified the same budget line while the first user was offline), the system must present a conflict resolution interface showing both versions with the option to accept either, merge, or escalate to a budget coordinator. Offline data must be encrypted on the local device using AES-256 encryption. The offline cache must include sufficient historical data (minimum 24 months) to support meaningful budget analysis without network access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R017	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Integration with Financial ERP and Treasury Modules	The system must integrate bidirectionally with the core financial ERP modules including general ledger, accounts payable, treasury, and management reporting. Budget data must be published to the general ledger module as budget journal entries mapped to the appropriate chart of accounts for each entity. Actual cost postings from validated terminal invoices must flow back to update budget consumption in real time. The system must integrate with the treasury module to obtain forward exchange rate projections for multi-currency budget consolidation and to communicate expected cash outflow schedules based on budgeted port costs and historical payment timing patterns. Management reporting must receive consolidated budget versus actual data for inclusion in entity and group management packs. All integrations must use the ERP's standard integration API with idempotent message handling, retry logic for transient failures, and dead-letter queuing for messages that fail after maximum retries. Integration mappings between port cost categories and GL account codes must be configurable per entity without code changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-045-2-004-R018	FEAT-045-2-004	MOD-045	Budget Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance	The system must maintain a comprehensive immutable audit trail for all budget planning and control activities. Every budget action must be logged including budget creation, data input, modification, approval, rejection, transfer, reallocation, version creation, and report generation. Each audit entry must record the action type, user identity, timestamp in both UTC and local time zone, entity context, the specific budget node affected, and the before and after values for any data changes. The audit trail must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 of 2021, Saudi Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013 record retention requirements. The system must support audit trail export in formats acceptable to external auditors including the Big Four firms (Deloitte, EY, KPMG, PwC) active in the GCC and India. Access to audit trail data must be restricted to users with Compliance Officer or Internal Auditor roles, and audit trail queries must not impact the performance of the operational budget system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R001	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Port-Specific Cut-Off Configuration	The system must allow configuration of distinct cargo cut-off windows for each port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA) with separate timelines for documentation cut-off, physical cargo receiving cut-off, and VGM (Verified Gross Mass) submission cut-off. Each cut-off type must be configurable in hours before ETD (Estimated Time of Departure) with minimum granularity of 30 minutes. The configuration must support port-specific overrides by terminal operator, with Jebel Ali DP World terminals having different rules than Khalifa Port ADPC terminals. All timestamps must be stored in UTC with display conversion to local port timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R002	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Documentation Cut-Off Enforcement	The system must enforce documentation cut-off deadlines by automatically blocking bill of lading instruction submissions, shipping instruction amendments, and manifest data changes after the configured documentation cut-off time for each port. When a booking's documentation cut-off is approaching within a configurable warning window (default 6 hours), the system must send automated alerts via email and in-app notification to the shipper, freight forwarder, and internal operations team. The enforcement must respect SOLAS VGM regulations requiring verified container weights before vessel loading. Late submission requests must trigger an exception workflow requiring operations manager approval with audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R003	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Physical Cargo Receiving Cut-Off	The system must track physical cargo gate-in cut-off times per terminal and enforce restrictions on container acceptance after the deadline. Integration with terminal operating systems (TOS) at each port must validate that containers arriving after the physical cut-off are flagged for rollover to the next available vessel. The system must differentiate between dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RH) which typically have earlier cut-offs due to plug-in requirements, and hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9) which may have separate cut-off windows mandated by port authority dangerous goods regulations. Out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo on flat racks and open tops must also have configurable earlier cut-offs due to special stowage planning requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R004	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	AI-Predicted Cut-Off Adjustment	The system must use AI/ML models to predict optimal cut-off time adjustments based on historical vessel schedule reliability, current port congestion levels, weather conditions, and terminal productivity rates. When a vessel's ETA/ETD is revised by the vessel operations module, the AI engine must automatically recalculate all associated cut-off times and propagate changes to affected bookings. The prediction model must consider seasonal patterns including Ramadan period operations where port working hours change, Eid holidays reducing terminal throughput, and monsoon season at JNPT causing berthing delays. Predictions must achieve at least 85% accuracy measured against actual operational outcomes over a rolling 90-day window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R005	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Cut-Off Exception Handling Workflow	The system must provide a structured exception handling workflow for late cargo acceptance after cut-off deadlines. Exception requests must capture the reason for lateness, the requesting party details, container numbers affected, and any additional costs incurred. The workflow must support configurable approval chains per entity: Qatar operations may require single-level approval, while India operations at JNPT may require dual approval from both operations and commercial teams due to customs bonding requirements. Each exception must generate a late-come surcharge calculation in the applicable currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) based on configurable rate tables per port and container type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R008	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Terminal Integration for Real-Time Gate Status	The system must integrate with terminal operating systems at Hamad Port (managed by QTerminals), Khalifa Port (managed by ADPC/KEZAD), Jebel Ali (managed by DP World), and JNPT (managed by multiple operators including APMT, DP World NSIGT, and BMCT) to receive real-time gate open/close status. When a terminal announces early gate closure or extension, the system must automatically adjust cut-off times and notify all affected parties. The integration must use industry-standard protocols including COPARN for booking confirmations, BAPLIE for bay plans, and COPINO for gate-in messages. Fallback to manual status updates must be available when terminal system integration is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R009	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Automated Shipper Notification System	The system must send automated notifications to shippers and freight forwarders at configurable intervals before each cut-off deadline. Default notification schedule must include alerts at 48 hours, 24 hours, 12 hours, and 6 hours before cut-off. Notifications must be sent via email, SMS, and in-app push notifications based on party communication preferences. All notifications must be bilingual (English and Arabic) with the recipient's preferred language displayed first. The notification content must include booking reference, vessel/voyage, port of loading, container details, cut-off type, deadline timestamp in both local time and UTC, and a direct link to submit required documentation or take corrective action. Notification delivery status must be tracked and logged.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R010	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Cut-Off Compliance Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate compliance reports showing cut-off adherence rates by port, terminal, shipper, and freight forwarder over configurable time periods. Reports must calculate KPIs including percentage of bookings meeting documentation cut-off, physical cut-off, and VGM cut-off. Late submissions must be categorized by root cause (shipper delay, transport delay, documentation error, customs hold, port congestion). The reporting engine must support multi-currency aggregation converting late-come surcharge revenue to a base reporting currency using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. Reports must be exportable in PDF with Arabic RTL support and Excel formats, and must support Hijri date ranges for KSA entity reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R011	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Customs and Regulatory Cut-Off Integration	The system must enforce customs-related cut-off deadlines specific to each country's regulatory framework. For Qatar, integration with Qatar Customs (TASMU) must validate that customs declarations are filed before documentation cut-off. For UAE, Dubai Trade and Khalifa Port Community System integration must be validated. For KSA, FASAH single-window platform submission deadlines must be tracked. For India, Indian Customs EDI System (ICES) and PCS1x portal filing deadlines must be enforced, including the mandatory 24-hour advance manifest filing for export shipments from JNPT. The system must automatically flag bookings where customs clearance status is incomplete as cut-off approaches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R012	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Reefer Cargo Pre-Trip Inspection Cut-Off	The system must manage separate pre-trip inspection (PTI) cut-off deadlines for refrigerated containers. PTI cut-off is typically 48-72 hours before vessel ETD to allow time for equipment testing, pre-cooling, and any necessary repairs. The system must track PTI status for each reefer booking and block reefer containers from the load list if PTI has not been completed and confirmed by the cut-off deadline. Integration with reefer monitoring systems must validate that temperature set-points, ventilation settings, and atmosphere control parameters match the booking requirements. Failed PTI containers must trigger automatic substitute equipment requests or rollover processing to the next available vessel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R013	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Hazardous Cargo Special Cut-Off Rules	The system must enforce specialized cut-off rules for hazardous cargo based on IMO classification (Classes 1 through 9) and port-specific dangerous goods regulations. Hamad Port and Jebel Ali have specific DG cut-off windows that are typically 24-48 hours earlier than standard cargo cut-offs. The system must validate that all required dangerous goods documentation including the IMO Dangerous Goods Declaration, Material Safety Data Sheet (MSDS), and Emergency Contact Information are submitted before the DG documentation cut-off. Segregation validation must be completed before the DG cut-off to ensure incompatible cargo classes are not booked on the same vessel in violation of IMDG Code stowage and segregation requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R014	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Offline Cut-Off Management Capability	The system must support offline operation for cut-off management when network connectivity to port systems or central servers is unavailable. Operations staff at port offices must be able to view current cut-off schedules, process late-come exception requests with local approval, and record gate-in timestamps using a locally cached dataset. When connectivity is restored, offline transactions must synchronize with the central system using conflict resolution rules that prioritize the most restrictive cut-off time in case of discrepancy. The offline module must cache at minimum 72 hours of forward-looking cut-off data for all vessels scheduled at the local port, including vessel details, booking manifests, and contact information for notification purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R015	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Cut-Off Audit Trail and Compliance Log	The system must maintain a comprehensive, immutable audit trail of all cut-off-related events including original cut-off setting, any modifications to cut-off times, exception approvals or rejections, late-come surcharge assessments, and notification delivery records. Each audit entry must capture the timestamp (UTC and local), user identity, entity context, IP address, and before/after values for any changes. The audit trail must be searchable by booking reference, vessel/voyage, port, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), and user. Data retention must comply with Qatar Financial Centre regulations (7 years), UAE commercial law (5 years), KSA ZATCA requirements (6 years), and Indian Customs Act retention rules (7 years), applying the longest applicable period per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-001-R016	FEAT-046-1-001	MOD-046	Vessel Schedule Change Impact Analysis	The system must automatically perform impact analysis when a vessel schedule change is received, identifying all bookings affected by the revised ETD and their corresponding cut-off deadline shifts. The impact analysis must classify affected bookings into categories: those still within cut-off compliance, those now past cut-off due to schedule advancement, and those gaining additional time due to schedule delay. For bookings negatively impacted by vessel advancement, the system must auto-generate shipper notifications with revised cut-off times and escalate to operations if the remaining time before cut-off falls below the minimum threshold (configurable per port, default 12 hours). The analysis must calculate financial exposure from potential rollovers in the applicable booking currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R001	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Configurable Overbooking Ratio per Trade Lane	The system must support configurable overbooking ratios expressed as a percentage above vessel nominal capacity, independently adjustable per trade lane, port pair, and equipment type. For example, the Jebel Ali to JNPT trade lane may allow 115% overbooking on 20GP containers but only 105% on 40HC due to different no-show patterns. Overbooking limits must be set separately for weight capacity (metric tonnes) and TEU slot capacity, with the system enforcing whichever limit is reached first. Configuration must be entity-specific, allowing Qatar entity to set different overbooking policies than UAE or India entities based on their commercial strategies and risk appetite.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R002	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	AI-Driven Overbooking Optimization	The system must employ AI/ML algorithms to recommend optimal overbooking levels based on historical booking cancellation rates, no-show percentages, short-shipment patterns, and seasonal demand fluctuations for each trade lane and port pair. The model must factor in Ramadan and Eid periods where cancellation patterns differ significantly, Diwali season peak demand from India, and year-end cargo rushes. The AI engine must continuously learn from actual outcomes, comparing predicted no-shows against actuals to refine its recommendations. Overbooking recommendations must be presented with confidence intervals and the estimated financial impact in USD of both under-booking (lost revenue from empty slots) and over-booking (rollover costs).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R003	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Rollover Candidate Prioritization Logic	When actual cargo exceeds vessel capacity due to overbooking, the system must apply configurable prioritization rules to determine which bookings are rolled over to the next available vessel. Prioritization criteria must include booking confirmation date (first-confirmed-first-loaded), shipper contract tier (premium, standard, spot), cargo type priority (reefer and hazardous before dry), revenue value per TEU, and trade lane strategic importance. The prioritization engine must respect contractual SLA commitments where specific shippers have guaranteed space allocations under their volume contracts. The system must calculate rollover cost impact per booking in the booking's original currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) including storage costs and potential penalty charges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R004	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Automated Rollover Processing Workflow	The system must automate the rollover process by identifying excess bookings against available vessel capacity, applying the prioritization logic, generating revised booking confirmations for rolled-over cargo with new vessel/voyage assignments, and notifying all affected parties. The workflow must automatically rebook rolled-over containers on the next available vessel serving the same port pair, considering equipment availability and special cargo requirements. If multiple subsequent vessels are available, the system must present options ranked by earliest arrival at destination, lowest cost, and fewest transshipment connections. Rolled-over bookings must retain their original booking reference with a rollover suffix and maintain linkage to the original booking for tracking and revenue accounting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R005	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Rollover Cost Tracking and Allocation	The system must track all costs associated with rollovers including additional terminal storage charges, container detention costs, reefer monitoring fees for refrigerated units during the extended stay, and any penalty payments to shippers under their service contracts. Costs must be captured in the local port currency and converted to the entity's reporting currency using the treasury exchange rate applicable on the date of the cost event. The system must allocate rollover costs to the responsible cost center, distinguishing between rollovers caused by overbooking decisions (commercial department responsibility), rollovers caused by operational issues like vessel delay or equipment shortage (operations department responsibility), and rollovers caused by shipper late delivery (no carrier cost). Monthly rollover cost reports must be generated per entity for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R006	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Guaranteed Space Protection Mechanism	The system must enforce guaranteed space allocations for shippers who hold volume commitment contracts with guaranteed loading provisions. When overbooking rollover processing is triggered, guaranteed space bookings must be excluded from the rollover candidate pool unless the shipper explicitly consents. The system must maintain a registry of active guaranteed space contracts per trade lane, linking them to the contract management module. If a guaranteed space booking must be rolled over due to force majeure (vessel mechanical failure, port closure, severe weather), the system must automatically calculate penalty charges payable to the shipper per contractual terms and create a credit note in the applicable currency. An alert must be sent to the commercial team when guaranteed space conflicts arise.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R007	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Vessel Capacity Utilization Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying current booking levels versus vessel capacity for all scheduled voyages, with visual indicators showing overbooking status per vessel, port of loading, and equipment type. The dashboard must display capacity in both TEU and metric tonnes with utilization percentages. Color-coded thresholds must indicate: green (below 90% capacity), amber (90-100% capacity), red (over 100% overbooking zone). The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with all labels and data values rendered correctly for Arabic language users. Drill-down capability must allow operations staff to view individual booking details for any vessel, and the dashboard must auto-refresh at configurable intervals (default 5 minutes) to reflect latest booking activity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R008	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Overbooking Alert and Escalation System	The system must generate proactive alerts when overbooking levels approach or exceed configured thresholds. Alert levels must be configurable in three tiers: advisory (approaching limit, e.g., 95% of overbooking cap), warning (at limit), and critical (exceeding limit). Each tier must trigger notifications to different recipient groups: advisory to booking desk, warning to operations supervisor, critical to operations manager and commercial head. Alerts must include vessel details, current booking vs. capacity figures, list of top rollover candidates, estimated rollover cost in USD equivalent, and recommended actions. For critical alerts, the system must automatically restrict new booking acceptance on the affected vessel until the overbooking situation is resolved or additional capacity is confirmed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R009	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Multi-Port Overbooking Coordination	The system must coordinate overbooking management across multiple ports of loading on the same voyage rotation. When a vessel calls at Jebel Ali, then Hamad Port, then proceeds to JNPT, the overbooking at each subsequent port must account for actual cargo loaded at previous ports. The system must dynamically recalculate available capacity after each port departure and adjust overbooking limits for remaining ports accordingly. If excess cargo loaded at Jebel Ali reduces available space at Hamad Port, the system must proactively identify at-risk bookings at Hamad Port and initiate early rollover assessment. This multi-port coordination must work across entity boundaries when a voyage serves ports in different entities (UAE, Qatar, India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R010	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Historical Rollover Pattern Analysis	The system must maintain and analyze historical rollover data to identify patterns and improvement opportunities. Analysis dimensions must include rollover frequency by trade lane, port, season, equipment type, and shipper. The system must calculate rolling 12-month averages for rollover rates and compare against targets. Trend analysis must identify whether rollover incidents are increasing or decreasing and correlate with overbooking policy changes. The analysis must produce a monthly AI-generated insight report highlighting the top five trade lanes with highest rollover rates, estimated revenue impact in each entity's base currency, and specific recommendations for overbooking ratio adjustments. Reports must be available in both English and Arabic with proper number formatting per locale conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R011	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Shipper Communication for Rollover Events	The system must manage all shipper communications related to rollover events through structured templates that comply with carrier-shipper contract terms. When a booking is rolled over, the system must generate an initial notification within 2 hours of the decision, including the reason for rollover, new vessel/voyage assignment, revised ETD and ETA at destination, and any applicable compensation or storage credit. Communications must be generated in both English and Arabic based on the shipper's language preference. Follow-up communications must be sent when the rolled-over container is confirmed loaded on the new vessel. All communications must be logged against the booking with timestamps and delivery confirmation for dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R012	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Equipment Type Specific Overbooking Rules	The system must apply different overbooking rules per equipment type reflecting varying no-show and cancellation patterns. Standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC) typically have higher no-show rates permitting more aggressive overbooking. Reefer containers (20RF, 40RH) must have more conservative overbooking limits due to specialized plug requirements on the vessel and higher rollover costs from spoilage risk. Special equipment types including flat racks (20FR, 40FR), open tops (20OT, 40OT), and tank containers (20TK) must generally not be overbooked due to limited availability and specialized stowage requirements. The system must prevent overbooking of out-of-gauge cargo beyond vessel cell guide capacity considering the specific dimensions registered for each OOG booking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R013	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Rollover Impact on Transshipment Connections	The system must evaluate the downstream impact of rollovers on transshipment connections when a rolled-over container is booked on a multi-leg routing. If a container rolled from vessel A at Jebel Ali was connecting to vessel B at a transshipment hub (e.g., Singapore, Colombo), the system must verify that the revised vessel assignment still provides a viable connection at the hub. If the connection is broken, the system must automatically search for alternative routings that deliver the container to the final destination within an acceptable delay threshold (configurable per trade lane, default 7 days). The system must calculate the total cost impact of re-routing including any additional transshipment handling charges, feeder costs, and potential demurrage at destination port in the applicable currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R014	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Seasonal and Event-Based Overbooking Profiles	The system must support the creation of seasonal and event-based overbooking profiles that automatically activate during defined periods. Profiles must be configurable for recurring events including Ramadan (dates shifting annually per Hijri calendar), Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Diwali, Chinese New Year (affecting feeder connections), and year-end peak season (October through December). Each profile must define adjusted overbooking ratios, modified prioritization rules, and special alert thresholds applicable during the event period. The system must use the Hijri calendar module to automatically determine Ramadan and Eid dates each year and activate corresponding profiles. Profile activation and deactivation must be logged in the audit trail with the responsible user and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R015	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Rollover SLA Monitoring and Penalty Calculation	The system must monitor rollover frequency against SLA targets defined in shipper contracts and service level agreements. When a specific shipper's bookings are rolled over more than the contractually allowed frequency (e.g., maximum 2 rollovers per quarter on a premium contract), the system must automatically flag the breach, calculate the applicable penalty amount per contract terms in the contract currency, and notify the commercial team. The penalty calculation must reference the contract rate tables and apply the correct penalty formula which may be a fixed amount per container, a percentage of freight, or free storage days at the destination port. All SLA breaches and penalty calculations must be reported monthly per entity for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R016	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Offline Rollover Decision Support	The system must provide offline capability for rollover decision-making at port operations offices when central system connectivity is unavailable. The offline module must cache current vessel capacity data, active bookings, overbooking parameters, and prioritization rules for all vessels scheduled within the next 7 days at the local port. Operations staff must be able to execute rollover candidate assessment, approve or reject rollover recommendations, and record decisions locally. When connectivity is restored, offline rollover decisions must synchronize with the central system with conflict detection alerting operations if the same booking was modified by another port office during the offline period. All offline actions must be recorded with timestamps for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-002-R017	FEAT-046-1-002	MOD-046	Overbooking Revenue Impact Simulation	The system must provide a simulation tool allowing commercial and operations managers to model the financial impact of different overbooking scenarios before implementing policy changes. The simulator must accept inputs including proposed overbooking ratio, trade lane selection, time period, and historical data range for baseline. Outputs must include projected additional revenue from improved utilization, estimated rollover costs, net revenue impact, and risk metrics (probability of rollover events, expected number of affected containers). The simulation must run calculations in each entity's base currency and provide a consolidated USD equivalent summary. Results must be presented in interactive charts and tables with export capability to Excel for further analysis by the commercial planning team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R001	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Equipment Substitution Rule Configuration	The system must allow configuration of permitted equipment substitution rules defining which container types can be substituted for others when the originally booked equipment is unavailable. Standard substitution paths must include 20GP to 20HC (upgrade), 40GP to 40HC (upgrade), and 20GP+20GP to 40GP (consolidation). Each substitution rule must specify the direction (upgrade only, downgrade only, or bidirectional), any additional cost or credit applicable, and whether the substitution requires shipper approval or can be executed automatically. Rules must be configurable per entity, port, and trade lane, as equipment availability patterns differ between Jebel Ali (high 40HC surplus) and JNPT (frequent 20GP shortages). The configuration must support seasonal rule variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R002	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Automatic Equipment Upgrade Processing	The system must automatically identify bookings eligible for free equipment upgrade when the originally booked container type is unavailable at the port of loading but a higher-grade equivalent is available. For example, when a shipper booked a 40GP but only 40HC units are available at Hamad Port, the system must automatically upgrade the booking to 40HC at no additional charge to the shipper. The automatic upgrade must generate a revised booking confirmation sent to the shipper and freight forwarder in their preferred language (English or Arabic). The system must track the revenue differential between the original booking rate and the upgraded equipment rate, recording this as an upgrade cost against the vessel operations budget in the applicable entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R003	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Commercial Upgrade Opportunity Detection	The system must use AI to identify commercial upgrade opportunities where offering a free or discounted upgrade to a higher container type would benefit the carrier by improving equipment repositioning. For example, if there is a surplus of 40HC containers at Jebel Ali that need to be repositioned to JNPT, the system should identify 40GP bookings on the Jebel Ali-JNPT route and recommend upgrading them to 40HC. The AI must calculate the net benefit considering the upgrade revenue loss versus the saved repositioning cost, presenting the recommendation only when the net benefit is positive. Recommendations must include the estimated savings in USD equivalent and the number of containers that can be optimized, allowing the commercial team to approve or reject batch upgrades.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R004	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Downgrade Management and Compensation	The system must handle scenarios where a booked container type must be downgraded due to equipment shortages, managing shipper compensation according to contractual terms. When a shipper booked a 40HC but only 40GP is available, the system must calculate the appropriate rate adjustment or compensation credit. Compensation rules must be configurable per entity and shipper contract tier: premium contract shippers may receive automatic rate reduction plus additional credit, while spot booking shippers may only receive a rate adjustment. The system must generate compensation credit notes in the booking currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) and link them to the original booking and invoice. Downgrade events must require explicit shipper acceptance before processing, with a rejection triggering rollover to the next vessel where the correct equipment is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R005	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Equipment Availability Forecasting	The system must forecast equipment availability at each port by analyzing inbound vessel manifests, expected container returns from import deliveries, empty container repositioning schedules, and historical equipment flow patterns. The forecast must project availability for each container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RH, special equipment) at each port over a rolling 14-day window. The AI forecasting model must consider trade imbalances typical in the region, such as the significant export deficit from Middle East ports requiring empty container imports, and the seasonal cargo surges from India during harvest seasons. When the forecast predicts equipment shortages, the system must proactively trigger rolling and upgrade recommendations for affected bookings before the cut-off deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R006	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Reefer Equipment Rolling Restrictions	The system must enforce special rules for rolling or substituting reefer containers due to their temperature-controlled nature and specialized requirements. Reefer rolling must verify that the substitute unit supports the required temperature range (standard reefer -25 to +25 degrees Celsius, super-freezer units down to -60 degrees Celsius), has compatible atmosphere control capabilities (controlled atmosphere CA, modified atmosphere MA) if required by the cargo, and has sufficient fuel/power capacity for the voyage duration. The system must not permit rolling a star-cool or controlled atmosphere booking to a standard reefer unit without explicit shipper approval and commodity verification. All reefer rolling events must capture the original and substitute unit numbers, PTI status of the new unit, and temperature set-point confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R007	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Hazardous Cargo Equipment Compliance	The system must validate that any equipment substitution for hazardous cargo containers complies with IMDG Code requirements and port-specific dangerous goods regulations. When rolling a hazardous cargo booking to a different container, the system must verify that the substitute unit has valid CSC (Container Safety Convention) certification, appropriate placarding capacity, and meets the structural requirements for the specific IMO class being transported. Tank containers carrying liquid hazardous cargo must only be substituted with units having compatible lining materials and pressure ratings. The system must block any equipment substitution that would violate dangerous goods transport regulations and must log all DG-related rolling decisions with full compliance documentation for regulatory audit purposes at each port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R008	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Rolling Impact on Weight and Dimensions	The system must validate weight and dimension compatibility when processing equipment rolling or upgrades. When substituting a 20GP (max payload approximately 28,200 kg) with a 40GP (max payload approximately 28,750 kg), the system must verify that the cargo weight and dimensions fit the substitute unit. For consolidation scenarios where two 20GP bookings are merged into one 40GP, the system must validate combined weight does not exceed the 40GP maximum payload and that the cargo dimensions are compatible. The system must also check road weight limits applicable at the destination country: UAE road limit of 40 tonnes gross for a 40-foot container, India varying by state. Any weight or dimension violation must block the rolling and alert the operations team with specific details of the incompatibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R009	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Shipper Approval Workflow for Equipment Changes	The system must implement a structured shipper approval workflow for equipment changes that require shipper consent before processing. The approval request must be sent electronically to the shipper or their nominated freight forwarder with full details of the original equipment, proposed substitute, any rate impact, and the deadline for response. The workflow must support approval via email reply, web portal click, or API response from integrated shipper systems. If no response is received within the configurable deadline (default 12 hours), the system must apply the configured default action per entity: auto-approve for upgrades, auto-reject for downgrades. All approval interactions must be logged with timestamps and digital consent records. The approval request and response must be available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R010	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Equipment Repositioning Cost Optimization	The system must integrate with the equipment management module to consider empty container repositioning costs when making rolling and upgrade decisions. The optimization engine must calculate the total system cost including the freight rate differential from the equipment change, the avoided or incurred repositioning cost, terminal handling charges at origin and destination for the substitute equipment, and any inland transportation cost differences due to equipment size change. Calculations must be performed in the local currency at each cost point and consolidated to USD for decision support. The system must rank available substitution options by net cost impact, presenting the most cost-effective option first. Repositioning cost savings from commercial upgrades must be tracked as a separate revenue line for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R011	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Bulk Rolling Processing for Vessel Changes	The system must support bulk rolling of multiple bookings simultaneously when a vessel change or cancellation affects a large number of containers. The bulk rolling interface must allow operations staff to filter affected bookings by equipment type, shipper, cargo type, and destination, then apply rolling rules in batch. The system must process bulk operations in priority order based on the configured prioritization rules and must validate each individual booking against applicable rolling rules before execution. Progress tracking must display real-time status of the bulk operation showing processed, succeeded, failed, and pending counts. A summary report must be generated upon completion listing all rolled bookings, new assignments, exceptions requiring manual intervention, and total financial impact per currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R012	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	SOC and COC Equipment Differentiation	The system must differentiate between Carrier-Owned Container (COC) and Shipper-Owned Container (SOC) equipment when processing rolling and upgrade decisions. SOC bookings must never be automatically rolled to carrier equipment without explicit shipper authorization, as the shipper has their own container for the shipment. Conversely, COC bookings can be rolled between carrier fleet units following standard substitution rules. When a SOC booking cannot be loaded on the planned vessel, the system must only offer rollover to the next vessel, not equipment substitution. The system must also handle NVO (Non-Vessel Operating) carrier containers with their specific handling rules. Equipment ownership type must be clearly displayed in all rolling decision interfaces and must be a mandatory filter in rolling candidate assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R013	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Rolling Decision Audit and Reporting	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all rolling and upgrade decisions with full traceability from initiation to completion. Each rolling event must record the trigger reason (equipment shortage, vessel change, commercial optimization, shipper request), the decision maker (AI automatic, operations staff, or commercial manager), the original and new equipment details, rate impact in original booking currency, shipper approval status, and outcome (completed, rejected, reversed). Monthly rolling activity reports must be generated per entity showing total rolling events by type, net revenue impact, equipment utilization improvement, and shipper satisfaction metrics. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri date formatting and must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering for Arabic reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R014	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Special Cargo Equipment Compatibility Validation	The system must validate equipment compatibility for special cargo types before allowing any rolling or substitution. For garment-on-hanger (GOH) shipments, the substitute container must have appropriate hanger bars installed. For bulk liquid in flexitanks, the container must be a one-trip unit meeting flexitank installation requirements. For livestock shipments (relevant for Middle East imports), the container must have ventilation modifications. For project cargo on flat racks, the substitute flat rack must have equivalent lashing ring capacity and maximum stack weight rating. The system must maintain a special cargo compatibility matrix that maps cargo types to required container features, and must block any substitution that fails the compatibility check with a clear explanation of the specific incompatibility to the operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R015	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Upgrade Cost Recovery and Revenue Accounting	The system must properly account for revenue impacts of all rolling and upgrade events in the financial module. Free upgrades must record the revenue differential as a commercial cost allocated to the vessel voyage P&L in the entity's base currency. Paid upgrades must generate supplementary invoices or credit notes linked to the original booking invoice. The revenue accounting must handle multi-currency scenarios where the original booking is in USD but the upgrade differential must be invoiced in the shipper's preferred currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) using the exchange rate on the date of the rolling decision. Month-end accrual entries must be generated for rolling events where the financial settlement is not yet completed. Integration with the general ledger must post rolling-related entries to configurable GL account codes per entity chart of accounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R016	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Offline Rolling Decision Capability	The system must support offline processing of equipment rolling decisions at port operations offices when connectivity to the central system is unavailable. The offline module must cache current equipment availability data, active bookings awaiting equipment assignment, substitution rule configurations, and shipper contact details for the local port. Operations staff must be able to assess rolling candidates, approve automatic upgrade recommendations, and record rolling decisions locally with full documentation. When connectivity is restored, offline rolling decisions must synchronize with the central system with conflict detection for scenarios where the same equipment unit was assigned to multiple bookings at different ports during the offline period. Priority resolution must follow the first-decision-timestamp rule with manual review for conflicts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-003-R017	FEAT-046-1-003	MOD-046	Equipment Interchange Agreement Compliance	The system must validate equipment rolling decisions against active equipment interchange agreements (EIAs) with other shipping lines and container leasing companies. When carrier-owned equipment is insufficient and rolling requires using leased or interchanged containers, the system must verify that the proposed substitute unit is covered under a valid EIA with the owning party. The system must calculate and track per-diem lease charges for interchanged equipment in the currency specified in the EIA (typically USD), ensuring that rolling to leased equipment accounts for the additional lease cost in the net benefit calculation. Off-hire notifications must be automatically generated when interchanged containers are returned. The system must maintain a registry of all active EIAs with their terms, covered equipment types, geographical validity zones, and expiry dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R001	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Automated Rate Validation at Booking	The system must automatically validate freight rates at the time of booking creation and amendment against the applicable rate source: contract rates for contracted shippers, tariff rates for spot bookings, and promotional rates for campaign bookings. Validation must check the base ocean freight rate per container type and trade lane, all applicable surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS, low sulphur surcharge, peak season surcharge, war risk), and any special cargo premiums for hazardous, reefer, or out-of-gauge shipments. The validation engine must identify any rate that deviates from the approved rate by more than a configurable tolerance threshold (default 2%) and flag it for review. Rates must be validated in the booking currency with cross-reference to the rate filing currency if different.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R002	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Contract Rate Compliance Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor that bookings from contracted shippers are rated using the correct contract rate and not inadvertently charged tariff or spot rates. The compliance engine must match each booking to the shipper's active service contract based on shipper identity, trade lane, equipment type, commodity code, and booking date falling within the contract validity period. When a booking rate does not match any applicable contract rate, the system must generate a discrepancy alert to the commercial team with details of the expected rate versus applied rate. The system must handle complex contract structures including tiered volume rates (rate decreases as committed volume thresholds are met), minimum quantity commitments (MQC), and named account designations where the contract rate applies only when cargo is from a specific beneficial cargo owner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R003	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Surcharge Accuracy Verification	The system must verify the accuracy of all surcharges applied to each booking against the currently effective surcharge tariff for the trade lane and port pair. Surcharge verification must cover Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF) tied to fuel price indices, Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF) tied to exchange rate fluctuations, Terminal Handling Charges (THC) which vary by port and terminal operator, ISPS security surcharge, low sulphur fuel surcharge per IMO 2020 regulations, and any locally mandated port charges. The system must detect when a surcharge has been updated in the tariff module but bookings created before the effective date are still using the old rate, and must determine the correct surcharge based on the booking's applicable date (booking date, sailing date, or bill of lading date per surcharge terms). All surcharges must be validated in their filing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R004	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Revenue Leakage Detection Engine	The system must employ AI-powered analytics to detect revenue leakage patterns across all bookings and entities. Revenue leakage categories must include: under-weight charges where actual container weight exceeds the declared weight used for rating, commodity misclassification where lower-rated commodity codes are used for higher-value cargo, free-time abuse where shippers consistently exceed detention and demurrage free-time without being charged, unapplied surcharges where mandatory surcharges are missing from bookings, and unauthorized discounts where rate reductions are applied without proper approval. The AI engine must analyze booking patterns per shipper and freight forwarder to identify systematic leakage versus occasional errors. Detected anomalies must be scored by estimated revenue impact in USD and prioritized for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R005	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Rate Audit Workflow and Resolution	The system must provide a structured workflow for investigating and resolving rate audit findings. Each audit finding must be assigned a unique case ID, categorized by type (undercharge, overcharge, missing surcharge, contract mismatch), and assigned to the appropriate team member based on configurable routing rules per entity. The workflow must track the finding through stages: identified, under investigation, root cause determined, corrective action proposed, approved, and resolved. For undercharges, the corrective action must generate a supplementary debit note or adjustment invoice in the original booking currency. For overcharges, a credit note must be issued. All corrective financial documents must flow through the appropriate approval chain, with amount thresholds determining the approval level required per entity's delegation of authority matrix.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R006	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Multi-Currency Rate Audit Handling	The system must handle rate audits across multiple currencies prevalent in the operational region. Bookings may be rated in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR, while the underlying contract or tariff rate may be filed in a different currency. The rate audit engine must perform currency conversion using the exchange rate applicable at the time of booking (or the contractually specified rate date) when comparing booking rates against filed rates. Exchange rate tolerance must be configurable to account for minor rounding differences during conversion. Audit findings involving currency discrepancies must clearly report the rate in both the booking currency and the rate filing currency, with the exchange rate used and the source of the exchange rate (treasury rate, central bank rate, or market rate). All monetary audit summaries must be consolidatable to a single reporting currency per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R007	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Tariff Compliance with Regulatory Bodies	The system must validate that applied rates comply with regulatory tariff filings required by maritime authorities in each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar, compliance with Qatar Transport and Communications Ministry shipping regulations must be verified. For UAE, Federal Transport Authority maritime tariff requirements must be met. For KSA, Transport General Authority regulations must be checked. For India, rates must comply with Shipping Ministry guidelines and any applicable Tariff Authority for Major Ports (TAMP) regulations at JNPT. The system must flag any booking where the applied rate falls below the minimum tariff floor or exceeds the maximum ceiling where such regulatory constraints exist. Anti-dumping rate checks must be performed for trade lanes where regulatory authorities have established minimum freight rate levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R008	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Detention and Demurrage Rate Audit	The system must audit detention and demurrage charges applied to import and export containers to ensure accuracy against published tariff schedules and any contract-specific free-time allowances. The audit must verify that free-time days are correctly calculated considering port-specific rules (e.g., Friday excluded at Middle East ports, holidays excluded per entity calendar including Hijri holidays), that the correct daily rate tier is applied as detention/demurrage rates typically increase in escalating tiers, and that any contract-specific free-time extensions or rate overrides are properly reflected. The system must identify containers where detention or demurrage charges should have been applied but were missed, and containers where charges were applied beyond the contractual or tariff maximum. Calculations must be performed in the applicable port currency with conversion to the billing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R009	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Freight Rate Benchmarking Analysis	The system must provide AI-driven freight rate benchmarking that compares the carrier's achieved rates against market indices and competitor intelligence for key trade lanes. The benchmarking must cover major routes in the network including Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Jebel Ali, and KSA ports to Indian subcontinent. Market rate data sources must be configurable and may include Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Freightos Baltic Index (FBX), and Drewry World Container Index. The system must present benchmarking results showing the carrier's average achieved rate per TEU versus the market average, with variance analysis by equipment type and commodity group. Benchmarking reports must highlight trade lanes where rates are significantly above or below market, indicating potential yield improvement or competitive risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R010	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Automated Invoice Reconciliation	The system must automatically reconcile generated invoices against booking rates and approved charges to catch any discrepancies introduced during the invoicing process. Reconciliation must verify that the invoiced ocean freight matches the booking rate, all applicable surcharges are included at the correct amounts, any adjustments from rolling or upgrade events are properly reflected, and the invoice currency and exchange rate match the booking terms. The reconciliation must run as a batch process before invoice finalization and as an on-demand check for individual invoices. Discrepancies must be classified by type and severity, with high-severity items (discrepancy exceeding USD 100 equivalent) blocking invoice release until resolved. Reconciliation results must be available per entity in both English and Arabic with amounts in local and USD equivalent currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R011	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Revenue Integrity KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard displaying key revenue integrity metrics across all entities. KPIs must include total revenue under audit, identified leakage amount by category and entity, recovery rate (percentage of identified leakage successfully recovered), average time to resolve audit findings, rate compliance percentage (bookings correctly rated on first pass), and top five leakage sources by trade lane. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, trade lane, time period (with both Gregorian and Hijri date selectors), and customer segment. All monetary values must be displayable in any of the operating currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with real-time conversion. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all charts, tables, and numerical values properly formatted per locale conventions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R012	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Contract Volume Commitment Tracking	The system must track actual cargo volumes against minimum quantity commitments (MQC) in shipper service contracts to ensure revenue integrity. The tracking must compare actual TEU, weight, and container count against contracted minimums on a rolling basis per the contract measurement period (monthly, quarterly, or annually). When a shipper is trending below their MQC commitment, the system must generate early warning alerts to the commercial team with projected shortfall and the applicable dead-freight or shortfall penalty calculation in the contract currency. Conversely, when a shipper exceeds volume thresholds that trigger rate tier changes, the system must flag that the revised rate should be applied to subsequent bookings. The MQC tracking must handle complex contract structures with multiple trade lane commitments and commodity-specific sub-commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R013	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Rate Amendment Impact Assessment	The system must provide impact assessment when rate amendments are proposed, calculating the projected revenue impact across all affected bookings before the amendment is implemented. When a surcharge change is announced (e.g., BAF increase of USD 50 per TEU), the system must identify all existing bookings within the effective date range, calculate the total revenue impact in each affected currency, and present the assessment to commercial management for approval. The impact assessment must differentiate between bookings where the rate change can be applied (per contract terms allowing floating surcharges) and bookings where the rate is locked (all-inclusive rate contracts or fixed-rate commitments). This enables informed decision-making on rate change timing and applicability, supporting revenue optimization across the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R014	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Commodity Code Verification and Audit	The system must verify that commodity codes (HS codes) declared on bookings match the actual cargo descriptions and detect potential commodity misclassification that could result in incorrect rating. The AI engine must cross-reference the declared HS code against the cargo description, shipper history, and trade lane commodity patterns to identify mismatches. For example, if a shipper historically ships electronics (HS 8471) but a new booking declares textiles (HS 6204) on the same trade lane with similar weight profiles, the system must flag this for verification. Commodity verification is critical for revenue integrity because different commodity codes may attract different freight rates, surcharges, and insurance premiums. The system must also validate that hazardous cargo commodity codes correctly map to their IMO classifications to prevent safety and compliance issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R015	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Intercompany Rate Transfer Pricing Audit	The system must audit intercompany freight charges between the four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to ensure transfer pricing compliance with each jurisdiction's tax regulations. When cargo moves on a routing involving multiple entities (e.g., booked by India entity but vessel operated by UAE entity), the intercompany rate must comply with arm's length principles and local transfer pricing documentation requirements. The system must verify that intercompany rates are within the approved transfer pricing policy bands and flag any deviations for review by the finance team. Qatar Financial Centre, UAE Corporate Tax (effective from 2023), KSA ZATCA transfer pricing rules, and India Transfer Pricing regulations under Section 92 of the Income Tax Act must all be considered. Audit findings must be reported per entity with supporting documentation for tax authority review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R016	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	AI Rate Recommendation Engine	The system must provide AI-powered rate recommendations for new bookings and contract renewals based on historical yield analysis, current market conditions, capacity utilization forecasts, and competitor rate intelligence. The recommendation engine must suggest optimal rates per TEU for each equipment type on each trade lane, considering the shipper's historical volume, contract loyalty tenure, payment reliability, and cargo characteristics. Recommendations must be presented as a rate range (minimum acceptable, target, and premium) in the applicable currency with justification for each level. The engine must factor in the carrier's capacity position, considering whether the vessel on the proposed sailing is under-booked (suggesting rate flexibility) or overbooked (suggesting rate firmness). Rate recommendations must comply with all regulatory constraints and must not recommend rates below filed tariff minimums.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R017	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Offline Rate Audit Capability	The system must support offline rate audit operations at port and branch offices when central system connectivity is unavailable. The offline module must cache the current rate tariff, active contract rates for locally managed accounts, surcharge schedules, and recent bookings awaiting audit at the local office. Auditors must be able to perform rate verification checks, flag discrepancies, and record audit findings locally with full notation and supporting evidence. When connectivity is restored, offline audit findings must synchronize with the central system and enter the standard audit workflow. The offline cache must be refreshed automatically when connected, with a staleness indicator showing the age of cached rate data to prevent auditing against outdated rates. All offline audit actions must be logged with timestamps, auditor identity, and the offline flag for compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-1-004-R018	FEAT-046-1-004	MOD-046	Rate Audit Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive rate audit compliance reports for management review and external regulatory submission as required. Reports must include total bookings audited per period, percentage of bookings with rate discrepancies, total value of identified undercharges and overcharges by entity and currency, recovery actions taken and their status, and trend analysis comparing current period performance against historical baselines. Reports must be generated per entity with consolidation at the group level in USD. The reporting module must produce outputs in PDF format with Arabic RTL support for Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities, and in Excel format for detailed data analysis. Hijri date ranges must be supported for KSA entity reports as required by ZATCA reporting standards. Automated monthly and quarterly report generation must be configurable with email distribution to designated management recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R001	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	VSA Partner Slot Swap Request Initiation	The system must allow authorized liner operations users to initiate a slot swap request specifying the originating vessel voyage, target partner vessel voyage, number of TEU and FEU slots requested, container type requirements (dry, reefer, open-top, flat-rack, tank), and the applicable trade route covering ports such as Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Each swap request must capture the requestor entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), the partner line code, and generate a unique swap reference number following the format SSW-{entity}-{YYYYMMDD}-{seq}. The request must support draft, submitted, and cancelled states before partner acknowledgment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R002	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Partner Acknowledgment and Counter-Offer Workflow	The system must support a structured negotiation workflow where the receiving partner can accept, reject, or counter-offer a slot swap proposal. Counter-offers must allow modification of slot quantities, container types, applicable voyage legs, and any financial compensation terms expressed in the partner's preferred settlement currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR). The workflow must enforce a configurable response SLA (default 48 hours) and automatically escalate unacknowledged requests to the liner operations manager. All negotiation history must be preserved as an immutable audit trail with timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R003	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Multilateral Slot Swap Chain Resolution	The system must support multilateral (three-party or more) slot swap chains where Entity A swaps with Entity B who simultaneously swaps with Entity C to satisfy capacity requirements across interconnected trade routes. The AI engine must identify and propose viable swap chains when direct bilateral swaps are unavailable, considering vessel capacity constraints at each port of call including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Chain resolution must validate that no single vessel exceeds its declared slot capacity at any point in the rotation and must compute net settlement amounts across all parties in the chain using real-time exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R004	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Slot Swap Financial Settlement Tracking	The system must track all financial obligations arising from slot swap agreements, including per-TEU swap fees, reefer surcharges, and imbalance penalties. Settlement records must be maintained in the transaction currency and the reporting currency of each participating entity (QAR for Qatar operations, AED for UAE operations, SAR for KSA operations, INR for India operations, with USD as the common interline settlement currency). The system must generate periodic settlement statements aligned with VSA agreement terms, support netting of reciprocal swap obligations, and interface with the accounts receivable and accounts payable modules for invoice generation and payment reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R005	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Real-Time Slot Availability Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying available and committed swap slots across all partner vessels on configured trade routes. The dashboard must show slot availability by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, 20TK), weight utilization percentage, and reefer plug availability per vessel per port call. Filters must include trade route, port pair, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri date selection), partner line, and entity. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with all labels, tooltips, and data columns rendering correctly in right-to-left orientation for Arabic-language users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R006	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	AI-Driven Swap Opportunity Recommendation	The AI engine must proactively analyze current booking forecasts, historical utilization patterns, and partner vessel capacities to recommend slot swap opportunities that would improve overall load factors for the operator's fleet. Recommendations must consider seasonal demand variations at Middle Eastern ports (Ramadan, Hajj season, Eid periods referenced via Hijri calendar), monsoon impacts on Indian subcontinent routes affecting JNPT operations, and known cargo flow imbalances between westbound and eastbound legs. Each recommendation must include a projected revenue impact in USD with entity-level breakdowns, a confidence score (0-100), and the underlying data points that informed the recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R007	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Slot Swap Capacity Validation Engine	Before confirming any slot swap, the system must validate that the receiving vessel has sufficient capacity considering declared maximum TEU, weight limits per hold and on-deck, reefer plug counts, dangerous goods segregation constraints per IMDG code, and port-specific draft restrictions at Hamad Port (15.0m), Jebel Ali (17.0m), Khalifa Port (16.0m), and JNPT (14.0m). Validation must account for already-confirmed bookings, existing swap commitments, and any embargo or equipment restrictions. The validation engine must return specific constraint violations with suggested remedial actions such as reducing swap quantity or shifting to an adjacent voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R008	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Partner Communication via EDI and API Integration	The system must support automated exchange of slot swap requests, confirmations, and cancellations with partner lines via EDIFACT BAPLIE and COPARN message standards as well as RESTful API endpoints conforming to DCSA (Digital Container Shipping Association) interface standards. Outbound messages must be queued and retried on transmission failure with configurable retry intervals. Inbound messages must be parsed, validated against the active swap request database, and routed to the appropriate workflow step. The system must maintain a complete message log per swap transaction and support manual override for partners who communicate via email or phone with structured data entry forms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R009	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Swap Agreement Template Management	The system must allow liner operations administrators to configure reusable slot swap agreement templates that define standard terms including validity period, slot quantity ranges, applicable trade routes, settlement currency preferences, cancellation penalties, and performance KPIs. Templates must be entity-specific (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and partner-specific, supporting different terms for different VSA partners. Each template must have version control with effective dates, and the system must prevent the use of expired templates. Template names and descriptions must support bilingual entry in both English and Arabic with proper storage of Unicode Arabic text.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R011	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Offline Slot Swap Request Queuing	The system must support offline slot swap request creation and queuing when network connectivity is unavailable, particularly relevant for operations personnel at port facilities with intermittent connectivity at Hamad Port and JNPT. Offline requests must be stored locally on the client device with all mandatory fields validated locally, timestamped with the local creation time, and automatically synchronized with the central server upon connectivity restoration. Conflict resolution rules must handle scenarios where the same slots were committed via another channel during the offline period, alerting the user to conflicts and suggesting alternative slots or voyages for resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R012	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Swap Slot Equipment Tracking Integration	The system must track the physical container equipment associated with swapped slots, integrating with the equipment management module to ensure that containers loaded under swap arrangements are properly flagged with the swap reference number, partner line operator code, and original booking reference. Equipment interchange receipts (EIR) generated at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT for swap-related containers must reference the swap agreement. The system must reconcile actual equipment movements against swap commitments and flag discrepancies such as wrong container type loaded, excess or shortfall against committed quantities, and unauthorized equipment substitutions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R013	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Regulatory Compliance Validation for Swap Cargo	The system must validate that cargo moved under slot swap arrangements complies with import and export regulations of all countries on the voyage rotation. This includes sanctions screening against OFAC, EU, and UN sanctions lists for cargo origin and destination, verification of required trade documentation (bill of lading, customs declarations, certificates of origin), and compliance with GCC Customs Union requirements for intra-GCC movements between Qatar, UAE, and KSA. For Indian operations at JNPT, the system must validate compliance with DGFT (Directorate General of Foreign Trade) regulations and Indian Customs Electronic Data Interchange requirements. Non-compliant swap cargo must be flagged and blocked from confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R014	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Swap Cancellation and Penalty Management	The system must handle slot swap cancellations initiated by either party, applying the penalty terms defined in the governing swap agreement or template. Cancellation penalties must be calculated based on the notice period provided relative to the vessel's estimated time of arrival (ETA) at the load port, the number of slots cancelled, and the container types affected (reefer cancellations typically carry higher penalties). Penalty amounts must be computed in the settlement currency specified in the agreement and converted to entity reporting currencies using the exchange rate on the cancellation date. The system must generate cancellation notices, update slot availability in real-time, and create debit or credit notes in the financial module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R015	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Multi-Entity Swap Authorization Matrix	The system must enforce an entity-specific authorization matrix for slot swap operations where different approval thresholds apply based on the initiating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the swap volume (number of TEU), and the financial value of the swap. For example, Qatar entity swaps exceeding 200 TEU or USD 50,000 in value may require regional director approval, while India entity swaps above 500 TEU may require country manager sign-off. The authorization matrix must be configurable per entity without code changes, support multi-level approval chains, and integrate with the ERP's role-based access control system. Delegation of authority during approver absence must be supported with configurable validity periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-001-R016	FEAT-046-2-001	MOD-046	Swap Utilization Forecasting with AI	The AI engine must forecast expected utilization of swapped slots based on historical booking patterns, current pipeline bookings, market intelligence feeds, and seasonal trends specific to the Middle East and Indian subcontinent corridors. The forecast must be generated at the time of swap negotiation to inform decision-making, updated daily as the vessel departure approaches, and must differentiate between dry cargo, reefer, and special equipment slots. Forecast accuracy must be tracked over time and used to calibrate the model. The system must alert operations when forecasted utilization of swapped slots falls below a configurable threshold (default 70%), recommending actions such as releasing slots back to the partner or sourcing additional cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R001	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Real-Time Schedule Deviation Detection	The system must continuously monitor vessel positions via AIS (Automatic Identification System) data feeds and compare actual positions against the planned schedule milestones (departure, arrival, berth, sail) for each port call in the rotation. When a vessel's projected arrival deviates from the published schedule by more than a configurable threshold (default 6 hours), the system must automatically generate a schedule deviation alert classified by severity: minor (6-12 hours), moderate (12-24 hours), major (24-48 hours), or critical (>48 hours). Alerts must be distributed to relevant stakeholders based on the affected ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT operations teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R002	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Deviation Root Cause Classification	The system must require classification of each schedule deviation by root cause category including weather and sea state conditions, port congestion and berth unavailability, mechanical breakdown or vessel defect, canal or strait transit delays (Suez Canal, Strait of Hormuz), regulatory hold or inspection, labor action or strike, and force majeure events. The AI engine must suggest probable root causes based on contextual data such as weather reports along the route, known port congestion at Jebel Ali or JNPT, and historical deviation patterns for the specific vessel class. Root cause data must feed into reliability analytics and be reportable in both English and Arabic for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R003	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	AI-Generated Schedule Recovery Plans	Upon detection of a significant schedule deviation (moderate or above), the AI engine must automatically generate one or more recovery plan options. Each recovery plan must specify recommended actions such as increased sailing speed (with estimated additional bunker fuel cost in USD), port call sequence modifications (port omission, order change), reduced port stay durations, and catch-up strategies over subsequent voyages. The AI must evaluate each plan against constraints including maximum vessel speed, minimum safe port stay for cargo operations, contractual port call commitments at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and bunker fuel availability. Plans must present a cost-benefit analysis comparing recovery cost versus schedule reliability penalty.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R004	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Transhipment Connection Impact Assessment	When a schedule deviation is detected, the system must automatically identify all transhipment cargo at risk of missing their connecting vessel at hub ports (Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port are primary transhipment hubs in this network). For each at-risk container, the system must display the booking reference, shipper and consignee details, commodity description, the connecting vessel and voyage, the original and revised connection window, and the financial exposure including potential cargo claim liability. The system must support automated re-routing proposals identifying alternative connections via other services or partner vessels, with cost implications displayed in the booking currency and entity reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R005	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Customer Proactive Notification Generation	The system must automatically generate customer-facing notifications when schedule deviations impact their cargo, including revised estimated times of arrival, alternative transhipment connection details if applicable, and any required action from the customer such as updated documentation. Notifications must be generated in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic) and delivered via configured channels (email, SMS, customer portal). For customers with contracts specifying schedule reliability guarantees, the system must flag potential SLA breaches and calculate provisional penalty amounts in the contract currency. Notification templates must be configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and support Hijri date formatting where required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R006	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Port Omission Decision Support	When a recovery plan recommends port omission as a schedule recovery strategy, the system must provide comprehensive impact analysis including the number of booked containers affected at the omitted port, the cargo value and freight revenue at risk in USD and local currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port, INR for JNPT), contractual obligations for port calls under service agreements and VSA commitments, alternative arrangements for affected cargo (feeder service, truck relay, next direct call), and estimated cost of each alternative. The decision must require explicit authorization from the designated approver per the entity authorization matrix. Port omission decisions must be logged with full justification for regulatory and commercial audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R007	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Weather Routing Integration for Deviation Prevention	The system must integrate with weather routing services to receive 7-day weather forecasts along planned vessel routes covering the Arabian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Arabian Sea, and Indian Ocean approaches to JNPT. Weather data must include wind speed and direction, wave height and period, current patterns, and visibility conditions. The AI engine must proactively recommend pre-emptive schedule adjustments when weather conditions are forecasted to cause delays exceeding the deviation threshold. Recommendations must balance schedule adherence against vessel safety requirements and must account for seasonal weather patterns such as the Indian Ocean monsoon season (June-September) affecting JNPT-bound vessels and Shamal wind events in the Arabian Gulf affecting Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R008	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Berth Window Rebooking on Deviation	When a vessel's revised ETA falls outside the originally booked berth window at any port in the rotation, the system must automatically initiate a berth window rebooking request to the port terminal operating system via EDI or API. For Hamad Port (QTerminals), Jebel Ali (DP World), Khalifa Port (Abu Dhabi Ports), and JNPT (multiple terminal operators including JNPCT, NSIGT, APMT), the system must interface with the respective terminal booking systems. The rebooking request must include the revised ETA/ETD, expected cargo volume (discharge and load), and any special requirements such as reefer plugs or hazardous cargo segregation. The system must track rebooking confirmations and alert operations when a suitable berth window cannot be secured within acceptable waiting time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R009	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Schedule Reliability KPI Tracking	The system must calculate and track schedule reliability KPIs in alignment with industry standards including vessel on-time arrival percentage (within 24-hour window of published schedule), average delay per port call in hours, schedule integrity index per service loop, and port-specific reliability metrics for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. KPIs must be computed at vessel, service, trade route, and entity levels. Reporting periods must support both Gregorian calendar quarters and Hijri calendar periods for KSA and Qatar entities. The system must benchmark performance against configurable targets and industry averages, with trend analysis displayed on dashboards supporting both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R010	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Cascading Delay Propagation Modeling	The system must model how a delay at one port propagates through subsequent port calls in the rotation, accounting for buffer time built into the schedule, potential speed increases between ports, and contractual minimum port stay requirements. The propagation model must consider port-specific constraints such as tidal windows at JNPT, daylight-only navigation requirements in certain channel approaches, berth availability patterns at Jebel Ali during peak season, and prayer time impacts on port operations in Qatar, UAE, and KSA. The model must present a visual timeline comparing the original schedule with the projected revised schedule, highlighting the ports where the delay is absorbed versus where it compounds. This cascading analysis must update dynamically as new position data and port information become available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R011	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Bunker Fuel Cost Impact of Recovery Actions	The system must calculate the additional bunker fuel consumption and cost associated with each proposed schedule recovery action, particularly speed increases. The calculation must use the vessel's specific fuel consumption curves (relating speed to daily consumption for main engine and auxiliary engines), current bunker fuel prices at relevant bunkering ports (Fujairah is the primary bunkering hub in the region), and the distance of each voyage leg affected by the speed change. Costs must be computed in USD (standard bunker trading currency) and converted to entity reporting currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using current exchange rates. The system must compare recovery fuel costs against potential penalties for late arrival, cargo claim exposure, and customer compensation to support cost-optimal recovery decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R012	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Historical Deviation Pattern Analysis	The AI engine must analyze historical schedule deviation data to identify recurring patterns such as specific ports with chronic congestion (known issue at JNPT during peak export season), seasonal weather-related delays, vessel-specific mechanical reliability issues, and route segments with persistent delay accumulation. The analysis must cover a minimum rolling 24-month period and present findings as actionable insights for schedule planning. Patterns must be classified by confidence level and statistical significance. The system must recommend schedule adjustments such as adding buffer time at specific ports, adjusting sailing speeds on specific legs, or modifying port call sequences to reduce future deviations. Reports must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R013	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Deviation Communication with Port Authorities	The system must generate and transmit required regulatory notifications to port authorities when vessel arrival times change, including pre-arrival notifications mandated by Qatar Port Authority (minimum 72 hours for Hamad Port), Dubai Maritime City Authority for Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi Ports for Khalifa Port, and Mumbai Port Trust and JNPT Port Authority for Indian calls. Notifications must comply with the FAL Convention (Convention on Facilitation of International Maritime Traffic) and include updated crew lists, cargo manifests, dangerous goods declarations, and health declarations as required. The system must track notification acknowledgments and flag instances where updated notifications were not transmitted within regulatory timeframes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R014	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Contractual Penalty Exposure Calculation	The system must calculate the financial exposure from schedule deviations against contractual commitments including COA (Contract of Affreightment) schedule guarantees specifying penalty rates per day of delay per TEU in the contract currency, volume commitment penalties where delays cause inability to fulfill contracted cargo volumes within the period, demurrage and detention liability for containers delayed beyond free time at destination ports, and reefer cargo claims for temperature-sensitive cargo where extended transit time may compromise cargo integrity. Calculations must account for force majeure exclusions defined in each contract. Exposure amounts must be computed in contract currencies and consolidated into entity reporting currencies with a group-level view in USD for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R015	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Multi-Service Deviation Coordination	When multiple liner services share vessels or have interconnected transhipment flows, the system must coordinate deviation management across services to prevent a recovery action on one service from negatively impacting another. For example, if a vessel operates on a pendulum service connecting Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, a port omission decision for Khalifa Port must consider the cargo feed implications for the connecting feeder service. The system must present a holistic view of all affected services, enable coordinated recovery planning, and track cross-service impact metrics. The AI engine must optimize recovery actions across the service network rather than optimizing each service in isolation, ensuring entity-level and group-level objectives are balanced.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R016	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Offline Deviation Recording and Sync	The system must support recording of schedule deviation observations and preliminary recovery actions in an offline mode for vessel operations staff and port superintendents who may have limited connectivity. Offline-capable functions must include deviation event logging with root cause classification, preliminary recovery action selection from pre-configured options, photo and document attachment for evidence of delay causes (port congestion photos, weather screenshots, mechanical issue documentation), and communication drafts for customer notifications. All offline entries must be timestamped locally, queued for synchronization, and merged with server-side data upon connectivity restoration. Conflict resolution must prioritize server-side vessel position data over manually entered timestamps while preserving all user annotations and classifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-002-R017	FEAT-046-2-002	MOD-046	Deviation Impact on Customs and Documentation Deadlines	The system must assess how schedule deviations affect customs documentation filing deadlines at each port, including Advance Cargo Information (ACI) requirements that mandate manifest submission 24 hours before arrival at GCC ports and the Indian Customs ICEGATE system requiring Import General Manifest filing before vessel arrival at JNPT. When a deviation causes the vessel to arrive earlier or later than planned, the system must recalculate documentation deadlines, alert the documentation team of revised submission windows, and flag any documents at risk of late filing. For earlier-than-expected arrivals, the system must prioritize documentation processing and, where possible, auto-submit pre-prepared documents. Penalties for late customs filings must be tracked per entity and included in the overall deviation cost assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R001	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Revenue-Maximizing Cargo Selection Algorithm	The AI engine must implement a multi-objective optimization algorithm that selects the revenue-maximizing combination of bookings for each vessel voyage from the pool of confirmed, waitlisted, and forecasted bookings. The algorithm must consider per-TEU freight rates in the booking currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), accessorial charges (THC, documentation fees, VGM fees), ancillary revenue (demurrage, detention, reefer monitoring), and contribution margin after variable costs. The optimization must respect vessel physical constraints including maximum TEU capacity, maximum deadweight, maximum stack weight per bay, reefer plug count, and dangerous goods capacity per IMDG class. The algorithm must produce a ranked priority list of bookings with clear accept/reject/waitlist recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R002	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Weight-Volume Balance Optimization	The AI engine must simultaneously optimize both weight utilization and volume utilization for each vessel voyage, recognizing that these objectives often conflict—heavy cargo (steel, minerals from India) fills weight before volume while light cargo (textiles, plastics from GCC) fills volume before weight. The optimizer must target a configurable ideal weight-volume ratio (default 14 tonnes per TEU) by recommending a cargo mix that balances heavy and light commodities. The system must model vessel stability requirements including metacentric height (GM), trim, and bending moments, ensuring the recommended cargo mix can be stowed within safe limits. Optimization results must show projected weight utilization percentage, volume utilization percentage, and average revenue per TEU for the recommended mix.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R003	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Reefer Cargo Allocation Optimization	The system must optimize reefer container slot allocation considering the limited number of reefer plugs per vessel (typically 200-800 depending on vessel class), the higher revenue yield of reefer cargo compared to dry cargo, power consumption constraints per electrical zone on the vessel, and temperature compatibility requirements for co-stowage. The AI must prioritize reefer bookings by contribution margin while ensuring temperature range compatibility (frozen at -18°C to -25°C versus chilled at 0°C to +5°C versus controlled atmosphere). For trade routes from JNPT, reefer exports (pharmaceuticals, seafood, agricultural products) form a significant cargo segment, and the system must account for Indian phytosanitary and FSSAI certification requirements when assessing booking eligibility for reefer allocation. Reefer allocation recommendations must specify plug positions by bay.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R004	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Dangerous Goods Mix Compliance Engine	The AI cargo mix optimizer must enforce IMDG Code segregation requirements when selecting bookings containing dangerous goods. The engine must validate that the proposed cargo mix does not violate any DG segregation rules including class-to-class segregation (e.g., Class 3 flammable liquids away from Class 5.1 oxidizers), package group compatibility, and maximum quantity limitations per vessel or per cargo hold. The system must reference the IMDG segregation table and compute stowage feasibility for each combination of DG bookings. For GCC ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port), the system must also validate compliance with local port authority DG handling regulations which may be more restrictive than IMDG minimums. The optimizer must flag when DG bookings must be rejected to accommodate higher-revenue DG cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R005	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Customer Contract Commitment Fulfillment	The cargo mix optimizer must ensure that volume commitments under Contracts of Affreightment (COA) and Service Contracts (SC) are prioritized in the cargo selection process. The system must track year-to-date shipped volumes against contracted minimum quantities per customer, trade route, and container type, and must boost the priority score of bookings from customers who are behind their contracted run-rate. Contract terms including minimum quantity commitments (MQC), maximum quantity commitments, and named accounts must be loaded from the contract management module. The system must balance MQC fulfillment across the remaining contract period (which may follow Hijri calendar for KSA/Qatar contracts) rather than front-loading or back-loading volumes, ensuring equitable distribution across voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R006	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Trade Imbalance-Aware Optimization	The AI engine must account for trade imbalances (directional asymmetry in cargo flows) when optimizing the cargo mix. On routes where one direction has significantly higher demand than the other—for example, export-heavy from JNPT to GCC or import-heavy from Far East to Jebel Ali—the optimizer must adjust acceptance criteria to maximize round-trip profitability rather than per-leg revenue. The system must model the cost of empty container repositioning when accepting low-yield cargo in the weak direction versus the alternative cost of repositioning empties. Repositioning costs must be calculated in USD and allocated to entities based on the origin of the empty repositioning movement. The optimizer must generate repositioning recommendations alongside cargo mix recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R007	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Dynamic Pricing Integration for Cargo Mix	The cargo mix optimizer must integrate with the dynamic pricing module to receive real-time spot rates and promotional pricing for under-utilized voyage legs. When the optimizer identifies surplus capacity on a specific leg (e.g., Khalifa Port to JNPT), it must trigger dynamic pricing recommendations to stimulate additional bookings at rates above variable cost but potentially below standard tariff rates. The system must compute the break-even rate per TEU considering vessel operating costs allocated to that leg, port costs at both ends, and incremental cargo handling costs. Dynamic pricing must respect minimum rate floors configured per entity (Qatar operations may have different floor rates than India operations), and any rate reductions must be approved through the entity-specific pricing authorization workflow before being offered to customers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R008	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Special Equipment Cargo Optimization	The optimizer must specifically handle special equipment types including open-top containers (20OT, 40OT) for over-height cargo, flat-rack containers (20FR, 40FR) for over-width and over-length cargo such as project cargo and machinery common in KSA and Qatar construction projects, and tank containers (20TK) for bulk liquids. For each special equipment type, the system must model the effective slot consumption (flat-racks may consume 2-3 standard slots depending on cargo dimensions), the limited fleet availability of special equipment at each port, and the premium freight rates achievable. The AI must determine the optimal number of special equipment slots to allocate per voyage considering equipment positioning costs and the opportunity cost of standard container slots displaced.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R009	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Embargo and Restriction-Aware Cargo Filtering	Before including any booking in the cargo mix optimization, the system must filter out bookings that violate active trade embargoes, commodity restrictions, or country-specific import/export prohibitions. The filter must reference an up-to-date database of restrictions covering Qatar embargo lists, GCC unified customs tariff restrictions, Indian DGFT prohibited and restricted commodity lists, OFAC sanctions, EU restrictive measures, and UN Security Council sanctions. For multi-stop voyages, the system must validate that cargo on board does not violate restrictions at any port of call, not just the origin and destination. Restriction checks must execute in real-time during optimization runs and must be updated within 24 hours of any published regulatory change. Filtered bookings must be flagged with the specific restriction reason and communicated to the commercial team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R010	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Cargo Mix Simulation and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a simulation environment where liner operations planners can run what-if scenarios to test alternative cargo mix strategies before committing. Scenarios must allow the user to adjust parameters such as freight rate assumptions (in any supported currency: USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), demand forecasts per trade lane, equipment availability at specific ports, vessel capacity constraints (simulating a vessel swap), and customer priority rankings. Each simulation must produce a full cargo mix recommendation with projected revenue, utilization metrics, and customer impact assessment. The simulation must support comparison of up to five scenarios side-by-side with variance highlighting. The interface must render correctly in Arabic RTL mode with all currency and numeric formatting respecting the selected locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R011	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Perishable Cargo Priority Scoring	The AI engine must implement a specialized priority scoring mechanism for perishable cargo that accounts for the time-sensitive nature of such commodities. Perishable cargo exported from JNPT (mangoes, grapes, marine products) and imported into GCC ports (fresh produce, dairy, meat) must receive priority scoring based on remaining shelf life, required transit time, reefer equipment availability, and the financial penalty for shipment delay or loss. The scoring must integrate with phytosanitary certificate validity periods and health certificate expiration dates. Cargo approaching documentation expiry must receive elevated priority to avoid wastage. The system must also consider Ramadan and Eid demand spikes (referenced via Hijri calendar integration) for food commodities when scoring perishable cargo in the GCC-bound direction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R012	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Multi-Entity Revenue Allocation for Shared Voyages	When a vessel voyage carries cargo booked by multiple entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), the system must allocate voyage revenue and costs to each entity based on configurable allocation rules. The optimization engine must consider entity-level profitability targets alongside group-level revenue maximization. Allocation methods must support per-TEU slot cost allocation, per-voyage proportional allocation based on utilized capacity, and activity-based costing for shared port costs. Each entity's allocated revenue and cost must be computed in its reporting currency using the exchange rate on the date of vessel sailing. The system must generate entity-level and consolidated voyage profit and loss statements, and the optimizer must flag scenarios where maximizing one entity's revenue significantly disadvantages another, recommending balanced alternatives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R013	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Cargo Mix Optimization Execution Monitoring	After the optimized cargo mix is approved and bookings are confirmed, the system must monitor actual loading progress against the optimized plan. The monitoring module must compare actual containers received at the container yard against the planned mix by container type, commodity category, weight range, and customer segment. Deviations from the optimized plan must be flagged in real-time—for example, if heavy cargo bookings arrive but the planned light cargo does not materialize, the system must alert operations to potential weight-over-volume or stability issues. The monitoring dashboard must display load progress as a percentage of the optimized plan, highlight at-risk bookings that have not yet gated in, and provide updated revenue projections based on actual versus planned mix. This dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R014	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Machine Learning Model Training and Calibration	The system must support continuous training and calibration of the AI cargo mix optimization model using historical voyage data including actual loaded quantities, actual revenue realized, booking no-show rates, overbooking outcomes, and actual weight versus declared weight discrepancies. Training datasets must span a minimum of 24 months of operational data across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and must be segmented by trade route, season, and vessel class. The system must support A/B testing of model versions where different optimization strategies are applied to comparable voyages to measure performance differences. Model performance metrics including revenue uplift versus baseline, utilization improvement, and customer satisfaction impact must be tracked and reported monthly to the AI governance committee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R015	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Overbooking Strategy Optimization	The AI engine must implement intelligent overbooking strategies that accept bookings beyond vessel nominal capacity to compensate for historical no-show and cancellation rates. The overbooking level must be calibrated per trade route, customer segment, commodity type, and season using historical data. For example, JNPT export voyages may have a 15% no-show rate for spot bookings but only 3% for contract bookings, requiring differentiated overbooking ratios. The system must compute the optimal overbooking level that maximizes expected revenue while keeping the probability of a roll (inability to load a confirmed booking) below a configurable threshold (default 2%). When rolls do occur, the system must prioritize bookings for loading based on contract status, freight rate, customer tier, and cargo urgency, with automatic rebooking of rolled cargo to the next available sailing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R016	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Environmental Impact Scoring for Cargo Mix	The cargo mix optimizer must include an environmental impact score as an optimization objective alongside revenue and utilization. The environmental score must account for estimated CO2 emissions per TEU based on cargo weight and voyage distance (using IMO CII methodology), the carbon footprint difference between alternative stowage plans (higher deck stowage increases wind resistance), and the environmental impact of empty repositioning movements required to support the cargo mix. The system must support configurable weighting between revenue maximization and environmental impact minimization, allowing entity-specific policies (e.g., Qatar entity may weight environmental factors more heavily in alignment with Qatar National Vision 2030). The environmental impact per voyage must be reported in tonnes of CO2 equivalent and included in sustainability reports formatted in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-003-R017	FEAT-046-2-003	MOD-046	Offline Cargo Mix Recommendation Access	The system must allow operations planners to access the most recently generated cargo mix optimization results and recommendations in offline mode when network connectivity is unavailable. The offline cache must include the full optimized cargo list with priority rankings, vessel capacity utilization summary, revenue projections in all relevant currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), and key constraint violations or warnings. Planners must be able to manually override booking priorities and acceptance decisions while offline, with all changes queued for synchronization upon reconnection. The system must prevent conflicting decisions when multiple users work offline simultaneously on the same voyage by implementing a last-write-wins strategy with full conflict audit trail visible to supervisors upon sync completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R001	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Voyage-Level Load Factor Calculation	The system must calculate load factors for each completed and in-progress voyage at both the full voyage level and per-leg level. Load factor must be computed as the ratio of utilized TEU to available TEU capacity, calculated separately for the vessel's total nominal capacity, practical capacity (accounting for stowage losses, hatch cover restrictions, and lashing gear limitations), and marketed capacity (after deducting partner swap allocations). Load factors must also be computed by weight (actual cargo weight versus maximum deadweight available for cargo after deducting constant weights, bunkers, and fresh water). Calculations must segment results by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer, special equipment) and by direction (head-haul versus back-haul). Each metric must be stored with the voyage record and available for trend analysis across configurable time periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R002	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Port-Pair Utilization Matrix Report	The system must generate a port-pair utilization matrix showing average load factors between every origin-destination port pair in the service network. The matrix must cover all ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), JNPT (INNSA), and any other ports in the configured rotations. For each port pair, the report must show average TEU utilization percentage, average weight utilization percentage, average revenue per TEU in USD, number of voyages in the reporting period, and the trend direction (improving, stable, declining) compared to the prior equivalent period. The matrix must be filterable by date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection), service loop, vessel class, and entity. The report layout must support Arabic RTL rendering with proper alignment of numeric data in right-to-left formatted tables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R003	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Revenue per Available TEU-Mile Metric	The system must compute Revenue per Available TEU-Mile (RATM) as a key utilization efficiency metric, calculated as total freight revenue divided by the product of available TEU capacity and voyage distance in nautical miles. RATM must be calculated at voyage, service, trade route, and entity levels, enabling comparison of revenue efficiency across routes of different lengths. Revenue must be normalized to USD for cross-entity comparison, with underlying data maintained in booking currencies (AED, QAR, SAR, INR). Distance calculations must use the actual sailed distance from vessel position data rather than standard port-to-port distances to account for weather routing and deviation. The system must maintain RATM benchmarks per trade route and flag voyages where RATM falls below the 12-month rolling average by more than a configurable threshold (default 15%).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R004	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Equipment Type Utilization Breakdown	The system must report utilization broken down by equipment type including standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RF, 40RFHC), special equipment (open-top, flat-rack, tank containers), and shipper-owned containers (SOC). For reefer equipment, utilization must include both slot utilization and reefer plug utilization as separate metrics since plug availability is often the binding constraint. For special equipment, the report must track equipment-specific metrics such as the ratio of special equipment slots allocated versus utilized, the average waiting time for special equipment at each port (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), and the revenue premium achieved versus standard containers. Reports must support drill-down from summary to voyage detail level and must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic RTL formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R002	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Multi-Currency Fee Structure Definition	The system must support defining GA agreement fee structures in multiple currencies including USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with automatic cross-currency conversion using daily Central Bank exchange rates. Each agreement must specify the base currency for settlement, acceptable billing currencies, and the exchange rate source (Qatar Central Bank, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, or RBI reference rates). The system must store historical exchange rates applied at the time of each transaction for audit purposes. Fee structures must accommodate fixed retainer fees, per-TEU handling charges, per-bill-of-lading processing fees, and percentage-based revenue sharing models commonly used in container shipping agency arrangements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R005	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	AI-Powered Utilization Forecasting	The AI engine must generate utilization forecasts for upcoming voyages based on current booking pipeline, historical booking velocity (bookings per day as sailing date approaches), seasonal demand patterns at Middle Eastern and Indian ports, known market events (Ramadan, Diwali, Chinese New Year impacting feeder connections), and macroeconomic indicators affecting trade volumes. Forecasts must be produced at 30, 14, 7, and 3 days before sailing with increasing accuracy expectations. The system must display forecast confidence intervals and track forecast accuracy over time (comparing predicted versus actual load factors). Forecasts must be segmented by container type, direction, and port pair. When forecasted utilization for an upcoming voyage falls below a configurable threshold (default 75%), the system must automatically trigger commercial alerts recommending actions such as promotional pricing or slot reallocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R006	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Benchmarking Against Industry Averages	The system must support benchmarking of the operator's load factor and utilization metrics against industry averages and competitor performance where data is available. Industry benchmarks must be configurable and updateable from market intelligence sources such as Alphaliner, Drewry, and Clarksons. Benchmarks must be segmented by trade route (Middle East to Indian Subcontinent, Intra-Gulf, Far East to Middle East), vessel size category (feeder, sub-panamax, panamax, post-panamax), and time period. The system must display the operator's performance relative to benchmarks using visual indicators (above/at/below benchmark) on dashboards. Benchmark comparison reports must be generated quarterly and annually, with entity-level performance highlighted for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Dashboard visualizations must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R007	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Empty Container Repositioning Impact Report	The system must quantify the impact of empty container repositioning on effective utilization by reporting the number and percentage of TEU capacity consumed by empty containers per voyage, per leg, and per port pair. The report must distinguish between laden (revenue-generating) moves and empty (cost-incurring) moves, calculating the net effective utilization (laden TEU divided by total capacity). Empty repositioning costs must be computed based on slot cost allocation, handling charges at origin and destination ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT), and inland transportation costs where applicable. Costs must be allocated to the requesting entity and presented in entity reporting currencies. The AI engine must identify routes with chronically high empty repositioning ratios and recommend strategies such as triangulation, carrier haulage, and off-hire of surplus equipment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R008	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Slot Cost and Breakeven Analysis	The system must calculate the slot cost per TEU for each voyage, representing the minimum freight rate needed to cover the allocated vessel operating cost, port charges, canal transit fees (Suez Canal tolls), bunker fuel cost, and overhead allocation. Slot costs must be computed per leg of the voyage, per direction, and per container type, recognizing that reefer slots have higher costs due to power consumption and that deck versus hold stowage affects cost allocation. The breakeven analysis must show the minimum load factor required at current average freight rates to achieve a zero-margin voyage, the load factor required to meet entity-level target margins, and the sensitivity of profitability to ±5%, ±10%, and ±15% load factor variations. All costs must be computed in USD with entity-level views in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R009	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Vessel Performance Comparison Dashboard	The system must provide a dashboard comparing utilization performance across all vessels in the fleet, enabling identification of consistently high-performing and underperforming vessels. The comparison must include average load factor by TEU, average load factor by weight, average revenue per TEU in USD, schedule reliability percentage, average cargo operations time per port call, and bunker efficiency (TEU-miles per tonne of fuel). Vessels must be groupable by vessel class, deployed service, and operating entity. The dashboard must support ranking and sorting by any metric and must highlight vessels whose performance deviates significantly from the fleet average. Vessel nameplates on the dashboard must display in both English and Arabic where vessel names have Arabic registrations. Interactive drill-down from vessel to voyage to port call detail must be supported.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R010	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Seasonal Utilization Pattern Analysis	The AI engine must identify and report seasonal utilization patterns across the network, correlating load factor trends with known seasonal factors including the Islamic calendar (Ramadan reduced activity, post-Eid cargo surges, Hajj season logistics demand in KSA), Indian festival seasons (Diwali, harvest seasons affecting JNPT export volumes), monsoon impacts on Indian coastal and ocean-going operations, and summer heat impacts on Arabian Gulf port productivity. Seasonal patterns must be analyzed using Hijri calendar alignment for Islamic events and Gregorian calendar for other seasonal factors. The system must generate a seasonal utilization index per trade route that predicts expected load factor variation by week of the year, enabling proactive capacity deployment and commercial strategy adjustments. Analysis must cover a minimum rolling 36-month historical dataset.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R011	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Customer Segment Utilization Contribution	The system must report utilization contribution by customer segment including BCO (Beneficial Cargo Owner) direct, NVOCC (Non-Vessel Operating Common Carrier), freight forwarder, and spot market. For each segment, the report must show TEU volume, revenue contribution in USD and entity currencies, average freight rate, load factor contribution (percentage of total loaded TEU), booking reliability (ratio of booked to actually shipped), and year-over-year trend. The report must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), trade route, and time period with Hijri calendar period support. The AI engine must identify shifts in segment mix and recommend commercial actions when high-yield BCO contribution declines or when over-reliance on a single segment creates revenue concentration risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R012	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Utilization Impact of Slot Swap Arrangements	The system must measure and report the net utilization impact of slot swap arrangements with partner lines. The report must show the number of slots given to partners versus received from partners, the utilization rate of given slots by partners and received slots by the operator, the net revenue impact of swap arrangements in USD and entity currencies, and the effective load factor with and without swap arrangements for comparison. The analysis must be performed per partner line, per service, per trade route, and per entity. The system must flag swap arrangements where the operator consistently provides high-demand slots to partners but receives low-demand slots in return, recommending renegotiation. Data must integrate with FEAT-046-2-001 (Slot Swap Coordination) to ensure consistent metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R013	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Capacity Waste Identification and Reporting	The system must identify and quantify sources of capacity waste including stowage losses (TEU capacity lost due to container mix and stacking constraints), weight waste (unused deadweight when volume capacity is exhausted), reefer plug waste (empty reefer slots when plugs are available), and booking no-shows (confirmed bookings that did not materialize). Each waste category must be reported as absolute TEU or tonnes and as a percentage of total available capacity. The system must compute the revenue opportunity cost of each waste category using average freight rates for the respective trade route and period. For each waste type, the AI must suggest specific mitigation actions such as improved stowage planning software for stowage losses, better booking screening for no-shows, and cargo mix optimization for weight-volume imbalances. Reports must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R014	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Executive Summary Report with AI Narrative	The system must generate a monthly executive summary report that combines key utilization metrics with AI-generated narrative insights explaining performance trends, anomalies, and recommended actions. The narrative must be generated in both English and Arabic, with the Arabic version being a proper Arabic composition (not a machine translation of the English text) using shipping industry terminology in Arabic. The report must include a group-level summary across all entities followed by entity-specific sections for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. KPI visualizations must include load factor trends, revenue per TEU trends, utilization by equipment type, and comparison against budget targets. Financial figures must be presented in entity currencies with a consolidated USD view. The report must be distributable as PDF with proper Arabic RTL page layout and as an interactive dashboard accessible via the ERP portal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R015	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Automated Report Scheduling and Distribution	The system must support automated scheduling of utilization reports at configurable frequencies including daily operational reports (vessel-level load factors for in-progress voyages), weekly tactical reports (service-level utilization summaries), monthly strategic reports (entity and group-level performance analysis), and ad-hoc reports triggered by specific events (e.g., utilization dropping below threshold on a specific service). Report distribution must support email delivery to configured recipient lists per entity, publication to the ERP dashboard portal, and export to shared network drives in PDF and Excel formats. Scheduling must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar-based frequencies (e.g., monthly reports aligned with Hijri months for KSA and Qatar entities). The system must log all report generation and distribution events for audit purposes and must retry failed deliveries with configurable retry policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R016	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Offline Report Viewing and Caching	The system must support offline viewing of previously generated utilization reports for users who may be traveling or at port locations with limited connectivity. The client application must cache the most recent version of each report type (daily, weekly, monthly) for offline access. Cached reports must include all charts, tables, and AI narrative sections with full functionality for drill-down within the cached dataset. The system must clearly indicate when the user is viewing cached data versus live data, displaying the cache timestamp prominently. Cache management must allow users to select which reports and which entities' data to cache locally, respecting device storage constraints. When connectivity is restored, the cache must synchronize to display the latest available reports. Cached reports must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts on tablets and desktop browsers commonly used by field operations staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R017	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Data Export and Integration API for BI Tools	The system must provide a RESTful API and bulk data export mechanism for integration with external Business Intelligence tools such as Power BI, Tableau, and Qlik. The API must expose all utilization metrics, load factor calculations, and supporting dimensional data (vessel, voyage, port, customer, entity, time period) in a structured format. API responses must support both JSON and CSV formats with configurable field selections. Data must be filterable by entity, date range, service, vessel, and port. The API must enforce entity-level data access controls ensuring that entity-specific users can only access their own entity's data while group-level users can access consolidated data. API authentication must use OAuth 2.0 tokens with configurable rate limiting. Numeric fields must include both raw values and formatted values respecting the requesting user's locale settings for decimal separators and thousand grouping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-046-2-004-R018	FEAT-046-2-004	MOD-046	Utilization Budget vs Actual Variance Reporting	The system must compare actual utilization metrics against budgeted targets at voyage, service, entity, and group levels. Budget data must be imported from the financial planning module or entered manually per service per period. Variance reports must show absolute variance (actual minus budget in TEU, revenue in USD and entity currencies) and percentage variance for load factor, revenue per TEU, total voyage revenue, and total TEU carried. Favorable and unfavorable variances must be visually distinguished using color coding that is accessible to color-blind users. The AI engine must provide commentary on significant variances, identifying root causes such as market rate declines, demand shortfalls on specific trade lanes, vessel capacity changes, or operational disruptions. Variance reporting must align with entity fiscal periods, which may follow Hijri calendar for KSA and Qatar or April-March fiscal year for India, with configurable period mappings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R001	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	GA Agreement Master Record Creation	The system must allow authorized users to create a General Agent agreement master record capturing agent legal entity name (Arabic and English), trade license number, chamber of commerce registration, operating territory (defined by port codes such as QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, INNHT for JNPT), agreement effective date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and the designated entity under which the agreement falls (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India). The system must validate that no overlapping active GA agreement exists for the same territory and service scope before allowing creation. AI must auto-suggest optimal agreement terms based on historical agent performance data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R003	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Territory Exclusivity and Scope Management	The system must allow definition of territory exclusivity clauses per GA agreement specifying whether the agent holds exclusive, semi-exclusive, or non-exclusive rights for specific port pairs, trade lanes, or geographic regions. Territory scope must be definable by port code (UN/LOCODE), country, customs zone, or free trade zone such as JAFZA (Jebel Ali Free Zone) or QFZ (Qatar Free Zone). The system must automatically detect and alert when a new GA agreement proposal conflicts with existing exclusivity clauses. For semi-exclusive arrangements, the system must enforce the maximum number of concurrent agents permitted per territory as defined in each agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R004	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement Approval Workflow Engine	The system must provide a configurable multi-level approval workflow for GA agreements with role-based routing that supports entity-specific approval hierarchies. Approval chains must be configurable per entity (e.g., Qatar operations may require Legal, Commercial Director, and CEO sign-off while India operations may require additional compliance officer approval due to FEMA regulations). The workflow must support parallel and sequential approval steps, delegation during absence using Hijri calendar-aware leave schedules, automatic escalation after configurable SLA periods, and must capture digital signatures with timestamps. The AI engine must flag agreements with unusual terms for additional review and provide risk scores to approvers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R005	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement Renewal and Expiry Management	The system must track GA agreement expiry dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and trigger automated renewal notifications at configurable intervals (e.g., 90, 60, and 30 days before expiry). The AI engine must generate renewal recommendations based on agent performance KPIs including cargo volume handled, revenue generated, customer satisfaction scores, and compliance record. The system must support automatic renewal for agreements with evergreen clauses, renegotiation workflows for modified terms, and grace period management where the agent continues operating under existing terms while renewal is pending. Expired agreements without renewal must automatically restrict agent access to booking and documentation systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R006	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement Suspension and Termination Processing	The system must support temporary suspension and permanent termination of GA agreements with full audit trail. Suspension must allow specifying the reason (regulatory hold, performance breach, force majeure, sanctions compliance), effective date, expected reinstatement date, and scope (full suspension vs. partial restriction on specific services). Upon suspension, the system must automatically restrict agent portal access, halt new booking acceptance, but allow completion of in-transit shipments and pending documentation. Termination processing must trigger handover workflows to transfer active bookings, pending B/Ls, and customer relationships to replacement agents. All outstanding financial obligations must be calculated and queued for settlement before final termination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R007	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Service Scope and Liner Representation Definition	The system must allow each GA agreement to specify the exact service scope including which liner services the agent represents, container types handled (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, ISO tanks), trade lanes covered (e.g., Arabian Gulf to Indian Subcontinent, Middle East to Far East), and ancillary services authorized (customs brokerage, inland transportation, container freight station operations, reefer monitoring). The service scope must reference standard shipping industry codes and INCOTERMS 2020 terms (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP) that the agent is authorized to quote. The AI must validate service scope consistency across overlapping agreements to prevent conflicts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R008	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Performance Bond and Financial Security Tracking	The system must track financial security instruments associated with each GA agreement including performance bonds, bank guarantees, letters of credit, and insurance policies. Each instrument must record the issuing bank, amount (in original currency and USD equivalent), validity period in both Gregorian and Hijri dates, beneficiary entity, and renewal status. The system must send automated alerts when security instruments approach expiry (configurable thresholds at 60, 30, and 15 days). If a performance bond expires without renewal, the system must automatically escalate to the commercial director and optionally trigger agreement suspension. The AI engine must recommend appropriate bond amounts based on projected cargo volumes and historical credit risk assessments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R009	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement Version Control and Amendment Tracking	The system must maintain full version history of each GA agreement capturing every amendment, addendum, and modification with the effective date, reason for change, initiating party, and approver details. Each version must be stored as an immutable snapshot allowing comparison between any two versions with highlighted differences. Amendments must follow the same approval workflow as new agreements but with a streamlined fast-track option for minor changes (e.g., contact detail updates). The system must support side-letter management where supplementary terms are linked to the master agreement. Arabic and English versions of the agreement must be maintained in parallel with the system tracking which language version is the legally binding version per the governing law clause.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R010	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Regulatory Compliance Validation per Jurisdiction	The system must validate GA agreements against jurisdiction-specific regulatory requirements including Qatar Commercial Agents Law (Law No. 8 of 2002), UAE Commercial Agencies Law (Federal Law No. 18 of 1981 as amended), KSA Commercial Agencies Regulation, and Indian Shipping Trade Practices regulations. The system must verify that required registrations (e.g., Qatar Ministry of Commerce agent registration, UAE Ministry of Economy commercial agency registration) are current and linked to the agreement. The AI engine must flag clauses that may conflict with local regulations, such as exclusivity terms that violate competition law or commission structures that breach anti-bribery regulations. Sanctions screening must be performed against OFAC, EU, and UN sanctions lists for all agent entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R011	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	AI-Powered Agreement Risk Scoring	The system must generate an automated risk score for each GA agreement on a scale of 1 to 100 using machine learning models trained on historical agreement performance data. Risk factors must include agent financial stability (based on submitted financial statements), territory market volatility, regulatory complexity of the jurisdiction, historical dispute frequency, currency exposure risk for multi-currency agreements, and competitive landscape assessment. The AI must provide explainable risk factor breakdowns in both Arabic and English, with specific recommendations for risk mitigation clauses. Risk scores must be recalculated monthly and whenever material changes occur (e.g., agent ownership change, regulatory update, significant volume deviation from projections). High-risk agreements (score above 75) must trigger mandatory additional review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R001	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Real-Time KPI Summary Panel	The system must render a real-time KPI summary panel on the dashboard landing page displaying total bookings processed, total TEU volume, gross revenue generated, average margin percentage, and on-time documentation submission rate for each agent. Data must refresh at configurable intervals (minimum every 60 seconds) and support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHV), date range supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and currency denomination in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, or INR with live exchange rate conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R012	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement KPI Dashboard and Performance Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time KPI dashboard for each GA agreement displaying cargo volume against targets (TEU actual vs. committed minimum), revenue performance, collection efficiency, booking conversion rate, documentation accuracy rate, customer complaint frequency, and port turnaround compliance. Dashboards must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selections and must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic-language users. The AI must generate automated monthly performance summaries with trend analysis and predictive forecasts for the next quarter. Performance data must be segmented by port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT), container type, and trade lane. Underperformance alerts must trigger at configurable thresholds with escalation to agreement managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R013	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Multi-Entity Agreement Consolidation View	The system must provide a consolidated view across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) showing the complete agent network with agreement status, territory coverage, and overlapping service scopes. This view must support filtering by entity, agreement status (active, pending, suspended, expired, terminated), territory, and service type. The consolidation engine must automatically identify gaps in territory coverage where no active GA agreement exists and recommend potential agents from a prospect database. Cross-entity agents (those operating under agreements with multiple entities) must be clearly identified with their aggregate performance metrics. The UI must support Arabic RTL rendering and allow switching between map-based and tabular views of the agent network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R014	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Offline Agreement Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to GA agreement records for field sales personnel visiting agents in areas with limited connectivity, particularly relevant for inland regions in India and remote port areas in KSA. Offline mode must cache essential agreement data including terms, KPIs, contact details, and recent performance metrics on the local device with AES-256 encryption. When connectivity is restored, the system must perform bidirectional synchronization resolving conflicts using last-write-wins with manual review for concurrent edits to the same agreement fields. Offline-created agreement drafts must be queued for server-side validation and approval workflow submission upon reconnection. The system must log all offline access events for security audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R015	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agreement Clause Library and Template Management	The system must maintain a centralized clause library containing pre-approved legal clauses in both Arabic and English for constructing GA agreements. Clauses must be categorized by type (commercial terms, liability, indemnification, force majeure, governing law, dispute resolution, termination, confidentiality) and tagged by applicable jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The AI must recommend relevant clauses based on the agreement parameters such as territory, service scope, and agent risk profile. Template management must support entity-specific agreement templates with mandatory and optional clause sections. The system must track clause usage frequency and flag outdated clauses when regulatory changes occur. Legal team users must have exclusive rights to add, modify, or deprecate clauses in the library.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-001-R016	FEAT-047-1-001	MOD-047	Agent Onboarding Checklist and Document Collection	The system must provide a structured onboarding checklist for new GA agreements that tracks completion of all prerequisite steps including trade license verification, bank guarantee submission, insurance certificate validation, staff competency certifications, IT system integration readiness, and port authority registration confirmation at applicable ports (Hamad Port authority, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World Jebel Ali, JNPT port trust). Each checklist item must have configurable mandatory/optional status per entity and jurisdiction. The AI must auto-populate checklist requirements based on the agreement territory and service scope. Progress must be tracked as a percentage with automated reminders for incomplete items. The agent must not be activated in operational systems until all mandatory onboarding items are verified and approved by the operations manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R001	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Registration and Hierarchy Setup	The system must allow General Agents to register sub-agents beneath their agency hierarchy with mandatory fields including sub-agent legal name (Arabic and English), trade license number, physical office address with geocoordinates, primary contact details, and the parent GA agreement reference. The hierarchy must support up to three levels (GA → Sub-Agent → Branch Office) with each level inheriting permission boundaries from its parent. The system must validate that the sub-agent registration territory falls within the parent GA's authorized territory as defined in the master agreement. Each sub-agent must be assigned a unique system identifier following the pattern SA-{EntityCode}-{PortCode}-{Sequence} for traceability across the multi-entity network.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R002	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Role-Based Access Control for Sub-Agents	The system must implement granular role-based access control (RBAC) for sub-agent users with predefined roles including Sub-Agent Administrator, Booking Clerk, Documentation Officer, Finance Viewer, and Customer Service Representative. Each role must have configurable permissions covering booking creation and modification, B/L preparation, freight rate visibility, customer account access, financial report viewing, and container tracking. Permissions must be constrained such that a sub-agent can never exceed the permission scope of its parent GA. The system must support custom role creation by GA administrators with an approval step from the principal's operations team. All permission changes must be logged with timestamp, initiating user, and justification for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R003	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Territory Sub-Allocation and Restriction	The system must allow General Agents to sub-allocate portions of their authorized territory to sub-agents, defined by port code (UN/LOCODE), inland city, free zone, or customs jurisdiction. Territory sub-allocation must not exceed the parent GA's territory scope and must prevent overlapping exclusive sub-allocations within the same GA hierarchy. The system must display a visual territory map showing allocated and unallocated areas with support for Arabic labeling and RTL layout. When a sub-agent attempts to create a booking for a port or destination outside their allocated territory, the system must block the action and display an appropriate error message in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English). AI must recommend optimal territory sub-allocation based on cargo flow patterns and sub-agent proximity to key logistics hubs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R004	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Portal Access Provisioning	The system must provision dedicated portal access for sub-agent users with entity-specific branding and language preferences (Arabic or English with proper RTL/LTR rendering). Portal provisioning must include single sign-on (SSO) integration capability, multi-factor authentication enforcement, password policy compliance per jurisdiction (Qatar NCSA cybersecurity framework, UAE TRA standards, KSA NCA requirements, Indian CERT-In guidelines), and session timeout configuration. The system must support bulk user provisioning via CSV upload for sub-agents with large teams. Each sub-agent portal instance must be isolated to show only data within their authorized scope including bookings, shipments, customers, and financial transactions. Deprovisioning must immediately revoke all access upon sub-agent deactivation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R005	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Service Scope Restriction	The system must allow GAs to restrict which shipping services each sub-agent can offer, including specific liner services, container types (limiting sub-agents to standard dry containers 20GP/40GP/40HC only, or extending to special equipment like 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR, ISO tanks), INCOTERMS they can quote (e.g., restricting a sub-agent to FOB and CIF only), and trade lane coverage. Service scope restrictions must cascade automatically to all users under the sub-agent. When a sub-agent attempts to perform an action outside their service scope, the system must log the attempt, deny the action, and notify both the sub-agent administrator and the parent GA. The AI engine must recommend service scope adjustments based on sub-agent demonstrated competency and training completion records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R006	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Financial Limit Configuration	The system must support configurable financial limits for each sub-agent including maximum booking value per transaction (in the sub-agent's local currency and USD equivalent), daily aggregate booking value ceiling, credit limit for outstanding receivables, and maximum allowable discount percentage from tariff rates. Financial limits must be denominated in the entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion for cross-currency bookings using the daily Central Bank exchange rate. When a sub-agent approaches 80% of any financial limit, the system must send warning notifications. Exceeding any limit must require real-time approval from the parent GA's finance controller. The AI must recommend financial limit adjustments based on sub-agent payment history and creditworthiness trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R007	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Performance Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide parent GAs with a real-time performance monitoring dashboard for all their sub-agents displaying key metrics including booking volume (TEU), revenue generated, booking conversion rate, documentation error rate, customer retention rate, and average response time to customer inquiries. The dashboard must support comparison between sub-agents with ranking tables and trend charts over configurable time periods in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Underperforming sub-agents must be automatically flagged with AI-generated diagnostic insights identifying likely causes (e.g., staff turnover, territory competition, seasonal decline). The dashboard must be accessible in both Arabic and English with proper RTL layout support and must export to PDF and Excel formats preserving bilingual content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R008	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Activation and Deactivation Workflow	The system must implement a controlled activation workflow for new sub-agents requiring completion of onboarding prerequisites (trade license verification, bank account setup, staff training acknowledgment, system access testing) before operational activation. Activation must be gated by an approval step from the parent GA administrator and the principal's regional operations manager. Deactivation must support immediate emergency deactivation (for fraud or compliance violations) and planned deactivation with a configurable transition period during which active bookings are reassigned. Upon deactivation, the system must archive all sub-agent data, revoke system access, transfer pending customer inquiries to the parent GA, and generate a final settlement statement covering outstanding commissions and receivables. Reactivation of previously deactivated sub-agents must follow a streamlined process preserving historical data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R009	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Communication Channel Management	The system must provide managed communication channels between the principal, GA, and sub-agents including in-system messaging, notification feeds, and circular distribution. Messages must support bilingual content (Arabic and English simultaneously) with automatic RTL/LTR formatting based on the primary language of each paragraph. The system must support targeted broadcast messaging to sub-agent groups filtered by entity, territory, service type, or performance tier. Critical notifications (rate changes, regulatory updates, service disruptions at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT) must require read-receipt acknowledgment from sub-agents. The AI must auto-translate urgent operational notices between Arabic and English and prioritize notification delivery based on relevance scoring. Communication history must be searchable and retained for a minimum of seven years for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R010	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Training and Certification Tracking	The system must track training and certification records for sub-agent staff including mandatory shipping operations training, dangerous goods handling certification (IMDG Code compliance), customs regulations training per jurisdiction, and system usage proficiency assessments. Each certification must record the issuing authority, date of issuance (Gregorian and Hijri), expiry date, and renewal requirements. The system must prevent sub-agent staff from performing functions requiring unexpired certifications (e.g., dangerous goods booking requires valid IMDG certification). Automated reminders must be sent 60 and 30 days before certification expiry. The AI must generate personalized training recommendations based on identified skill gaps from operational error patterns. Training completion status must be visible to both the parent GA and the principal's operations team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R011	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Customer Account Segregation	The system must enforce strict customer account segregation between sub-agents operating under the same GA to prevent unauthorized access to competitor sub-agents' customer data. Each customer account must be assigned to exactly one sub-agent with transfer capabilities requiring approval from the parent GA. When a customer has operations across multiple territories served by different sub-agents, the system must support shared-view access with configurable visibility rules (e.g., sub-agent A can see shipment status for customer X at Jebel Ali but not financial details managed by sub-agent B at JNPT). Customer data access must comply with Qatar Personal Data Protection Law, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act. The system must log all customer data access events for privacy audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R012	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Rate Visibility and Quoting Authorization	The system must control sub-agent visibility into freight rate structures with configurable levels: full tariff visibility, net rate only (hiding principal's buying rate), or markup-only visibility where the sub-agent sees only their margin above a floor rate. Rate quoting authorization must define whether sub-agents can issue binding freight quotations or only indicative quotes requiring GA confirmation. Maximum discount authority from published tariff must be configurable per sub-agent and per trade lane. The system must track all quotes issued by sub-agents with automatic comparison against authorized rate boundaries. Quotes exceeding authorized parameters must be flagged and routed to the parent GA for approval before customer confirmation. Rate visibility settings must be currency-aware, showing rates in the customer's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R013	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Booking Approval and Override Rules	The system must support configurable booking approval rules for sub-agents based on criteria including booking value threshold, special container types (reefer, open-top, flat-rack, ISO tank requiring additional handling expertise), hazardous cargo classifications per IMDG Code, transshipment bookings, and bookings to sanctioned or restricted destinations. Approval routing must direct to the parent GA for commercial review and optionally to the principal's operations team for high-value or sensitive cargo. The AI engine must auto-approve routine bookings matching historical patterns and within all configured parameters, flagging only exceptions for human review, targeting 95% automation rate for standard dry container bookings. Override capability must be available to GA administrators with mandatory justification capture and audit logging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R014	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Operational Hours and Holiday Calendar	The system must maintain operational hours and holiday calendars for each sub-agent reflecting local business hours and jurisdiction-specific holidays including Islamic holidays (Eid Al-Fitr, Eid Al-Adha, Mawlid Al-Nabi calculated per Hijri calendar), national days (Qatar National Day December 18, UAE National Day December 2, KSA National Day September 23, India Republic Day January 26 and Independence Day August 15), and Friday/Saturday weekends for GCC versus Saturday/Sunday for India. The system must use these calendars for SLA calculation, response time measurement, and escalation timing. Customers contacting a sub-agent outside operational hours must be automatically routed to the parent GA or an alternative coverage sub-agent. The AI must predict peak periods and recommend temporary capacity adjustments for sub-agents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R015	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent System Integration and API Access Control	The system must provide API access for sub-agents to integrate with their own local systems (warehouse management, transport management, accounting) with configurable API rate limits, endpoint access restrictions, and data scope filtering matching their RBAC permissions. API keys must be issued per sub-agent with separate keys for production and testing environments. The system must enforce OAuth 2.0 authentication with JWT tokens containing the sub-agent's permission scope. API usage must be monitored with automatic throttling when rate limits are approached and blocking upon threshold breach. Integration logs must capture all API calls with request/response payloads (excluding sensitive financial data) for troubleshooting and audit. The system must provide a sandbox environment for sub-agents to test integrations without affecting production data at any entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R016	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Data Retention and Archival Policy	The system must enforce entity-specific data retention policies for sub-agent records based on local regulatory requirements: Qatar (minimum 10 years per Commercial Law), UAE (minimum 5 years per Commercial Transactions Law), KSA (minimum 10 years per Commercial Court Law), and India (minimum 8 years per Companies Act and GST requirements). Upon sub-agent termination, the system must archive all associated data including bookings, documents, financial transactions, and communication history in a read-only state accessible to authorized principal and GA users. Archived data must remain searchable and retrievable for regulatory inquiries and dispute resolution. The system must support automated purging of data exceeding retention periods with approval from the legal compliance officer. All archival and purging actions must generate tamper-proof audit logs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-002-R017	FEAT-047-1-002	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Offline Operation and Data Sync	The system must support offline operation for sub-agents located in areas with unreliable internet connectivity, particularly relevant for inland sub-agents in India servicing cargo from manufacturing hubs to JNPT and sub-agents operating in developing industrial areas of KSA. Offline mode must allow booking creation (queued for server-side validation), customer inquiry logging, document draft preparation, and container tracking lookup from cached data. All offline actions must be timestamped and queued with conflict resolution logic applying when synchronization occurs upon connectivity restoration. The system must prioritize synchronization of time-sensitive data (booking confirmations, vessel cut-off updates, B/L amendments) over less urgent data. Offline data must be encrypted with AES-256 on the local device and wiped after 30 days of no synchronization to prevent stale data exposure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R001	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Structure Template Management	The system must support creation and management of commission structure templates defining calculation methods including flat rate per TEU, percentage of freight revenue, percentage of total invoice value, tiered volume-based rates (e.g., 5% for first 500 TEU/month, 6% for 501-1000 TEU, 7% above 1000 TEU), and hybrid structures combining fixed and variable components. Templates must be configurable per entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), trade lane, container type (with differential rates for standard dry containers vs. special equipment like reefers and open-tops), and cargo type (general cargo, hazardous goods per IMDG Code, project cargo). Each template must specify the commission base (freight charges only, freight plus surcharges, or total invoice including local charges) and must support both inclusive and exclusive commission models.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R003	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Revenue and Volume Trend Charts	The dashboard must display interactive trend charts showing agent revenue and booking volume over configurable time periods (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, yearly). Charts must support multi-currency overlay where revenue from Hamad Port bookings in QAR, Jebel Ali bookings in AED, and JNPT bookings in INR can be viewed individually or converted to a base currency (USD) for consolidated comparison. Chart types must include line charts, bar charts, and area charts with export capability to PDF and Excel. RTL rendering must mirror chart axis orientation when Arabic locale is active.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R002	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Automated Commission Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate agent commissions upon completion of configurable trigger events including vessel sailing, B/L release, invoice issuance, or payment receipt. The calculation engine must process all applicable commission rules in priority order handling exceptions such as house B/L vs. master B/L splits, co-loading arrangements where multiple agents share commission, transshipment bookings where port pairs span different agent territories, and NVOCC bookings with sub-contracted slot allocations. The engine must support retrospective recalculation when booking modifications occur after initial commission posting (e.g., container type change from 40GP to 40HC, weight adjustment affecting freight). All calculations must maintain precision to four decimal places before rounding to two decimal places in the settlement currency to prevent cumulative rounding errors across high-volume operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R003	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Multi-Currency Commission Settlement	The system must support commission settlement in the agent's preferred currency with automatic conversion from the booking currency using exchange rates as defined in the GA agreement (booking date rate, sailing date rate, settlement date rate, or monthly average rate). Commission accruals must be maintained in both the original booking currency and the settlement currency with exchange gain/loss tracking. For agents operating across multiple entities (e.g., a GA with agreements in both Qatar and UAE), the system must support consolidated commission statements in a single settlement currency or separate settlements per entity based on the agreement terms. The system must handle currency restrictions where applicable (e.g., INR settlement for India-based agents subject to RBI regulations on outward remittance) and maintain compliance with Central Bank reporting requirements for cross-border commission payments exceeding defined thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R004	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Accrual and Recognition Accounting	The system must maintain a commission accrual ledger that records commission liabilities at the point of trigger event occurrence and releases accruals upon actual payment. Accrual entries must follow entity-specific accounting standards (IFRS as adopted in Qatar, UAE, and KSA; Indian Accounting Standards Ind-AS for India operations). The system must support period-end commission accrual adjustments for estimated commissions on in-transit cargo where the final chargeable weight or container count is not yet confirmed. Reversal entries must be automatically generated when bookings are cancelled, amended, or when cargo is short-shipped. The accrual ledger must integrate with the general ledger module providing real-time commission liability balances per agent, entity, and currency. Month-end and year-end closing processes must include commission accrual reconciliation with automated variance analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R005	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Tax Withholding and Regulatory Deductions	The system must automatically apply jurisdiction-specific tax withholding on commission payments including Qatar withholding tax (5% on payments to non-resident agents per Qatar Tax Law), UAE corporate tax considerations (9% where applicable post-2023 CT regime), KSA withholding tax (5% on service fees to non-residents per ZATCA regulations), and Indian TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) at applicable rates under Section 194H of the Income Tax Act for commission payments. The system must track tax identification numbers (QID/TIN for Qatar, TRN for UAE, ZATCA TIN for KSA, PAN/GSTIN for India) for each agent and validate them before processing payments. Tax certificates and withholding statements must be auto-generated in the prescribed format for each jurisdiction. The system must support tax treaty benefit claims where applicable double taxation avoidance agreements exist between the entity's jurisdiction and the agent's country of residence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R006	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Approval and Payment Authorization Workflow	The system must implement a multi-level commission approval workflow with configurable thresholds and routing rules per entity. Standard commission runs within expected parameters must be auto-approved by the AI engine (targeting 95% automation). Commissions exceeding configurable deviation thresholds from historical averages, first-time agent payments, payments above absolute amount thresholds (configurable per entity in local currency), and payments flagged by the anomaly detection engine must require manual approval. The approval workflow must route to Finance Controller for amounts below entity threshold and to CFO/Finance Director for amounts above. The workflow must support batch approval for routine commission runs and individual approval for flagged items. Payment authorization must generate a payment instruction file compatible with the entity's banking platform (e.g., QNB for Qatar, FAB for UAE, Al Rajhi for KSA, HDFC/SBI for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R007	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Volume Incentive and Bonus Commission Processing	The system must support periodic volume incentive calculations separate from standard commissions, including quarterly volume bonuses (additional percentage on all commissions if TEU target met), annual performance bonuses (lump sum or percentage uplift for exceeding annual targets), growth incentives (bonus for year-over-year volume increase exceeding a threshold percentage), and new business acquisition bonuses (premium commission rate for cargo from newly onboarded customers). Incentive calculations must reference the same booking and shipment data as standard commissions but apply overlay rules defined in the GA agreement's incentive schedule. The AI must forecast incentive accruals based on current trajectory to help financial planning. Incentive settlements must follow the same multi-currency, tax withholding, and approval workflows as standard commissions but may have different payment timing (e.g., quarterly incentives paid 30 days after quarter-end).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R008	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Sub-Agent Commission Split Management	The system must support automatic commission splitting between GAs and their sub-agents based on configurable split rules defined in the sub-agent agreement. Split configurations must support fixed percentage splits (e.g., 70% GA / 30% sub-agent), variable splits based on the sub-agent's role in the booking (originating agent gets higher share), and tiered splits where the sub-agent's share increases with volume. The system must allow GAs to choose between principal-pays-both (system generates separate commission payments to GA and sub-agent) and GA-pays-sub-agent (system pays full commission to GA who settles with sub-agent independently with tracking). Commission split calculations must account for scenarios where multiple sub-agents contribute to a single shipment (e.g., origin sub-agent at JNPT and destination sub-agent at Jebel Ali). All split allocations must be auditable with complete trail from total commission to individual payments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R009	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Statement Generation and Distribution	The system must generate detailed commission statements for each agent and sub-agent at configurable frequencies (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly) containing a itemized list of all commissionable transactions, commission rates applied, gross commission amount, tax withholdings, deductions (outstanding advances, debit notes, penalty deductions), net payable amount, and payment reference details. Statements must be generated in both Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. Currency amounts must display both the original booking currency and the settlement currency with the applied exchange rate. The system must support electronic statement delivery via the agent portal, email with encrypted PDF attachment, and API for agents integrating statements into their own accounting systems. Statement data must be exportable in formats compatible with common accounting software (CSV, Excel, SAP IDoc, Tally XML for Indian agents). The AI must generate executive summary narratives highlighting notable commission trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R010	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Dispute and Adjustment Processing	The system must provide a formal commission dispute resolution workflow allowing agents to raise disputes against specific line items on their commission statements with supporting documentation. Disputes must be categorized (rate discrepancy, missing booking, incorrect container count, wrong exchange rate, missing incentive) and routed to the appropriate resolution team based on category. The system must track dispute aging with SLA-based escalation (initial response within 3 business days, resolution within 15 business days adjusted for entity-specific holidays using the Hijri-aware calendar). Approved adjustments must be automatically included in the next commission run with clear reference to the original disputed transaction. The AI must identify patterns in disputes that indicate systemic issues (e.g., recurring rate mismatches on a specific trade lane) and recommend process corrections. Dispute resolution history must be retained for the full agreement duration plus the jurisdiction-specific retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R011	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	AI-Powered Commission Fraud and Anomaly Detection	The system must deploy AI-powered anomaly detection models to identify potential commission fraud or errors including duplicate commission claims on the same shipment through different booking references, artificially split bookings to manipulate tier thresholds, commission claims on cancelled or no-show bookings, unusual spikes in commissionable revenue inconsistent with cargo volume, round-tripping where cargo is booked and cancelled repeatedly to generate commission accruals, and collusion patterns between agent staff and customer accounts. The AI must assign risk scores to each commission transaction and flag high-risk items for manual review before payment authorization. Detection models must be trained on entity-specific historical data accounting for legitimate seasonal variations (e.g., pre-Ramadan cargo surges, India festival season peaks). False positive rates must be monitored and models retrained quarterly to maintain detection accuracy above 95% with false positive rate below 5%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R012	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Budget and Forecast Management	The system must support annual commission budget creation per entity, agent, and trade lane with monthly phasing that accounts for seasonal cargo volume patterns. The AI engine must generate budget forecasts based on historical commission data, committed cargo volumes in active GA agreements, market growth projections, and planned rate changes. Actual commission expenses must be tracked against budget in real-time with variance analysis at entity, agent, and trade lane levels. Budget overrun alerts must trigger at configurable thresholds (e.g., 90% of monthly budget consumed before the 25th of the month) with notification to the commercial and finance teams. The system must support budget reforecasting mid-year with version tracking of original budget, revised forecasts, and actuals. Budget reports must display amounts in entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R013	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Advance and Guarantee Draw Processing	The system must support commission advance payments to agents against future earnings, tracking the advance balance, recovery schedule (fixed installment deduction from each commission run or percentage-based recovery), and outstanding exposure. Advance limits must be configurable per agent based on historical average commission and the performance bond/bank guarantee amount recorded in the GA agreement. The system must prevent advances exceeding the available guarantee amount and must track guarantee utilization as a percentage. When an agent's outstanding advance exceeds 80% of their guarantee, the system must alert the finance team and optionally halt further advances. Recovery entries must be automatically included in commission calculations with clear line-item visibility on commission statements. Interest on advances must be calculable where the GA agreement specifies interest-bearing advances, using entity-specific benchmark rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R014	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Payment Integration with Banking Systems	The system must generate payment files in formats compatible with banking platforms used by each entity including SWIFT MT103 for international transfers, local payment network formats (QPAY for Qatar, UAEFTS/IPP for UAE, SARIE for KSA, NEFT/RTGS for India), and bulk payment file formats for batch processing. Payment files must include all regulatory required fields including beneficiary bank SWIFT/BIC code, IBAN (for GCC entities), IFSC code (for India), payment purpose code per Central Bank requirements, and tax withholding references. The system must reconcile payment execution confirmations received from banking systems (via bank statement MT940/CAMT.053 import or API) against commission payment records, automatically matching by reference number and amount. Failed or returned payments must trigger automated investigation workflows with the agent's bank details flagged for verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R015	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Reporting and Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide comprehensive commission analytics dashboards for management reporting including total commission expense by entity, agent ranking by commission earned, commission as percentage of freight revenue by trade lane, commission cost per TEU trend analysis, budget variance visualization, and geographic heat maps showing commission distribution across the port network (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT). Dashboards must support drill-down from aggregate to individual transaction level. Time period selection must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar options with period comparison (current vs. prior period, year-over-year). All dashboards must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout. The AI must generate natural language insights highlighting significant trends, outliers, and actionable recommendations in both Arabic and English. Reports must be schedulable for automatic generation and email distribution to configurable recipient lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R016	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Clawback and Penalty Deduction	The system must support commission clawback mechanisms for scenarios including cargo booking cancellation after commission payment, freight rate credit notes issued to customers reducing the commissionable base, service level agreement penalties assessed against the agent for operational failures (documentation errors, missed cut-off deadlines at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali), and contractual penalty clauses triggered by underperformance against minimum volume commitments. Clawback calculations must follow the same commission structure logic as the original accrual, including reversing any applicable tax withholding. The system must support immediate deduction from the next commission run or structured recovery over multiple periods based on the clawback amount and the agent's current commission earnings. All clawback actions must be documented with the specific agreement clause reference, justification, and approval from the commercial director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R017	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Audit Trail and Regulatory Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for every commission transaction from accrual through payment including calculation inputs (booking data, rate applied, exchange rate), intermediate calculation steps, approval chain with timestamps, payment execution details, and any subsequent adjustments or clawbacks. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. Regulatory reports must be generatable per jurisdiction requirements including Qatar Tax Authority commission payment reports, UAE Federal Tax Authority CT return supporting schedules, KSA ZATCA withholding tax returns (monthly filing), and Indian TDS returns (quarterly Form 26Q filing with traces reconciliation). The system must support external auditor access with read-only permissions scoped to specific entities and periods. Commission data must be available for transfer pricing documentation where inter-entity commission arrangements exist between the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-003-R018	FEAT-047-1-003	MOD-047	Commission Reconciliation with Agent Statements	The system must support automated reconciliation between principal-generated commission statements and agent-submitted commission claims or invoices. The reconciliation engine must match at the individual shipment level using booking reference, B/L number, and container number as matching keys. Discrepancies must be categorized as amount differences (within or outside tolerance threshold), missing in principal records, missing in agent records, or timing differences (recognized in different periods). The AI must auto-resolve common discrepancy types such as timing differences caused by different cut-off dates and exchange rate variances within configurable tolerance bands (e.g., 0.5% for exchange rate differences). Unresolved items must be escalated to the commission disputes workflow. Reconciliation reports must be generated in both Arabic and English showing matched, auto-resolved, and outstanding items with aging analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R001	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Template Engine for Agreement Generation	The system must provide a document template engine capable of generating agency agreements from predefined templates with dynamic field population from the agreement master record. Templates must support bilingual output (Arabic and English) in a single document with proper RTL/LTR paragraph handling, Arabic typography (Tashkeel support for formal legal documents), and bilingual headers/footers. Templates must be configurable per entity and agreement type (GA appointment letter, sub-agent authorization, commission addendum, territory amendment, termination notice). The engine must support conditional sections that include or exclude clauses based on agreement parameters (e.g., exclusivity clause included only for exclusive agreements). Dynamic fields must include dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, currency amounts with proper formatting per locale, and entity-specific legal entity names and registration numbers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R002	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Electronic Signature Integration and Workflow	The system must integrate with electronic signature platforms supporting legally recognized e-signatures in all operating jurisdictions (Qatar Electronic Commerce and Transactions Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 46 of 2021 on Electronic Transactions, KSA Electronic Transactions Law, India IT Act 2000 Section 5). The workflow must support multi-party signature collection with configurable signing order (sequential or parallel), signature delegation for authorized representatives, and deadline-based reminders with escalation. Each signature must capture the signer's identity verification method, timestamp in UTC with local time zone reference, IP address, and device information. The system must support both simple electronic signatures for routine amendments and advanced/qualified electronic signatures using digital certificates for formal agreement execution where required by jurisdiction or company policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R003	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Version Control and Comparison	The system must maintain full version history for every agency agreement document with each version stored as an immutable object tagged with version number, creation timestamp, author, and change summary. The document comparison engine must support visual diff highlighting between any two versions showing additions, deletions, and modifications in both Arabic and English content with proper handling of RTL text comparison. Version branching must be supported for parallel negotiation tracks (e.g., legal team reviewing liability clauses while commercial team negotiates commission rates) with merge capability when both tracks are finalized. The system must prevent concurrent editing conflicts through document check-out/check-in mechanisms. Each version must be retrievable as a standalone document for regulatory submission or dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R004	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Classification and Metadata Tagging	The system must automatically classify uploaded agency agreement documents using AI-powered document recognition into categories including master agreements, addenda, amendments, side letters, performance bonds, bank guarantees, insurance certificates, trade licenses, commercial registrations, power of attorney documents, and correspondence. Metadata must be auto-extracted including document date (Gregorian and Hijri), parties involved, agreement reference number, jurisdiction, and key commercial terms. Users must be able to manually override AI classifications and add custom metadata tags. The classification model must be trained on both Arabic and English documents with accuracy above 95% for standard document types. Metadata must be searchable through both structured query (filter by type, date range, entity) and natural language search in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R005	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Secure Document Storage and Access Control	The system must store all agency agreement documents in an encrypted document repository with AES-256 encryption at rest and TLS 1.3 encryption in transit. Access control must be granular per document with permissions configurable at user, role, entity, and agent hierarchy levels. Sensitive documents (financial terms, commission schedules) must support additional access restrictions beyond standard RBAC such as time-limited access, watermarked viewing, and download prohibition. The system must maintain a comprehensive access log recording every document view, download, print, and share action with user identity, timestamp, and IP address. Document storage must comply with data residency requirements (Qatar data must reside in Qatar-based or approved data centers per Qatar PDPL, similarly for other jurisdictions). The system must support configurable retention periods aligned with each jurisdiction's legal requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R006	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Expiry Tracking and Renewal Alerts	The system must track expiry dates for all time-bound documents associated with agency agreements including the agreement itself, performance bonds, bank guarantees, insurance policies, trade licenses, commercial registrations, and staff certifications. Expiry tracking must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates with automated alert notifications at configurable intervals before expiry (standard intervals: 90, 60, 30, 15, and 7 days). Alerts must be sent to configurable recipients including the agent, GA relationship manager, and compliance officer. The AI must predict which documents are likely to face renewal delays based on historical processing times and proactively escalate those items earlier. Expired documents without renewal must trigger configurable business rules (e.g., expired trade license blocks new bookings, expired insurance certificate triggers agreement suspension review). The dashboard must display a consolidated expiry calendar view across all agents and entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R007	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Regulatory Document Compliance Checking	The system must automatically validate that each agency agreement file contains all documents required by the applicable jurisdiction's regulations. Required document checklists must be configurable per entity and include Qatar requirements (Ministry of Commerce commercial agent registration, Qatar Financial Centre license if applicable), UAE requirements (Ministry of Economy commercial agency registration, Emirates ID copies of authorized signatories), KSA requirements (Ministry of Commerce agent registration, ZATCA tax registration certificate, Saudization compliance documentation), and India requirements (IEC code certificate, GST registration, DGFT authorization). The AI must scan uploaded documents to verify they match the required document type and check for common issues such as expired validity, mismatched entity names, and missing mandatory fields. Non-compliant agreement files must be flagged with specific deficiency descriptions and blocked from activation until all required documents are provided.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R008	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Sharing with External Parties	The system must support secure document sharing with external parties (agents, sub-agents, legal counsel, regulatory authorities) through time-limited secure links with configurable permissions (view only, download permitted, print permitted). Shared documents must be watermarked with the recipient's name, access timestamp, and confidentiality classification. The system must support selective sharing of document sections, allowing sensitive commercial terms to be redacted before sharing with parties not authorized to view them. External access must require identity verification through email OTP, SMS OTP, or digital certificate authentication. All external sharing events must be logged with recipient details, documents accessed, duration of access, and actions performed. The system must support bulk document package sharing for regulatory submissions where multiple documents must be provided as a complete set with a cover letter and index.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R009	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	AI-Powered Document Content Analysis	The system must deploy AI models to analyze agency agreement document content for risk identification, obligation extraction, and compliance verification. The AI must extract key obligations (payment terms, performance commitments, exclusivity restrictions, termination conditions) and populate a structured obligation register linked to the agreement record. Risk analysis must identify unusual or unfavorable clauses by comparing against the standard clause library, flagging deviations such as unlimited liability exposure, one-sided termination rights, non-standard commission escalation triggers, and missing force majeure provisions. Content analysis must operate on both Arabic and English text with equal accuracy. The AI must generate obligation summaries highlighting upcoming deadlines (using both Gregorian and Hijri dates), financial commitments, and conditional triggers that require monitoring. Analysis results must be reviewable by legal team with accept/override capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R010	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Workflow and Collaboration	The system must provide a collaborative document workflow supporting agreement drafting, internal review, negotiation with counterparty, legal review, final approval, and execution stages. Each workflow stage must have configurable participants, SLA deadlines (calculated in business days using entity-specific holiday calendars including Hijri-based holidays), and escalation rules. The workflow must support threaded comments on specific document sections with @-mention notifications to relevant stakeholders. Track changes functionality must allow multiple reviewers to propose modifications simultaneously with a consolidation step to accept or reject each change. The system must maintain a workflow history showing time spent in each stage, bottlenecks, and participants' response times. The AI must predict workflow completion dates based on historical processing patterns and proactively notify stakeholders of potential delays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R011	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Physical Document Digitization and OCR	The system must support digitization of physical agency agreement documents through scanning integration with OCR (Optical Character Recognition) capability for both Arabic and English text. The OCR engine must achieve minimum 98% character accuracy for printed English text and 95% accuracy for printed Arabic text including documents with mixed Arabic/English content. Scanned documents must be automatically processed to extract text layers enabling full-text search. The AI must attempt to auto-classify scanned documents and extract metadata (date, parties, agreement reference) from the OCR output. For handwritten annotations or signatures on scanned documents, the system must flag these sections for manual verification. Digitized documents must be linked to their corresponding agreement records with the original scan preserved as the source document and the OCR-enhanced version used for search and analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R012	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Archival and Long-Term Preservation	The system must implement a document archival strategy ensuring long-term preservation and accessibility of agency agreement documents for the full regulatory retention period (up to 10 years in Qatar and KSA, 8 years in India, 5 years in UAE, or longer if specified in the agreement). Archived documents must be stored in PDF/A-3 format (ISO 19005-3) for long-term preservation with embedded fonts ensuring Arabic text renders correctly regardless of the viewing system. The archival process must include integrity verification using SHA-256 checksums with periodic validation to detect bit rot or unauthorized modification. Archived documents must remain searchable and retrievable through the same search interface as active documents with clear visual distinction of archived status. The system must support legal hold functionality preventing deletion or modification of documents subject to litigation or regulatory investigation regardless of retention policy schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R013	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Notification and Reminder Engine	The system must provide a configurable notification engine for document-related events including new documents pending review, review deadline approaching, document approved or rejected, signature required, signature completed, document expiring, and document compliance issue detected. Notifications must be deliverable through multiple channels (in-app notification center, email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API for agents in regions where WhatsApp is the primary business communication tool). Notification content must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the recipient's preferred language displayed prominently. Notification frequency must be configurable to prevent alert fatigue with options for immediate, daily digest, and weekly summary modes. The system must track notification delivery status and read receipts where supported. Critical document notifications (e.g., agreement termination notice, compliance violation) must enforce mandatory acknowledgment before the recipient can proceed with other system actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R014	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Bulk Document Operations and Batch Processing	The system must support bulk document operations for scenarios involving multiple agents or agreements simultaneously including bulk agreement renewal letter generation, mass notification distribution for tariff changes affecting all agents on a specific trade lane, batch document upload with AI-assisted classification for agent onboarding packages, and mass expiry report generation across all entities. Batch operations must support scheduling for off-peak execution to minimize system performance impact, particularly for operations involving Arabic document rendering which requires additional processing resources. Progress tracking must show completion percentage with the ability to pause and resume batch jobs. Failed items within a batch must be individually retryable without reprocessing successful items. The system must generate batch operation summary reports showing processed count, success count, failure count with error details, and total processing time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R015	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Integration with External Registries	The system must integrate with external government and regulatory registries to validate and cross-reference agent documents including Qatar Ministry of Commerce Commercial Registry, UAE Ministry of Economy commercial agency registry, KSA Ministry of Commerce (MC) commercial registration system, India Ministry of Corporate Affairs MCA21 portal for company verification, and port authority registration systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Integration must support automated periodic verification (configurable frequency) to detect if external registrations have expired, been suspended, or had ownership changes that affect the agency agreement validity. Where API integration is available, the system must perform real-time verification during document upload. Where API integration is not available, the system must support manual verification with auditor sign-off. Verification results must be stored with timestamp and used to update the compliance status of associated agreements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R016	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Offline Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to critical agency agreement documents for users in field locations or areas with limited connectivity. Users must be able to designate specific agreements and their associated document packages for offline access with selective synchronization to minimize storage requirements on mobile devices. Offline document packages must be encrypted with AES-256 and protected by biometric or PIN authentication on the device. The system must track which document versions are cached offline and alert users when newer versions are available upon connectivity restoration. Offline access must be time-limited (configurable, default 7 days) after which documents are automatically purged from the local device if not resynchronized. The system must log all offline document access events for security audit purposes. Annotations or comments added offline must be queued for synchronization and merged with server-side changes using conflict resolution rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-1-004-R017	FEAT-047-1-004	MOD-047	Document Analytics and Insights Dashboard	The system must provide a document analytics dashboard displaying key metrics including total documents by type and entity, documents pending review with aging analysis, average document processing time by workflow stage, document compliance rate across agents, upcoming expiry calendar heatmap, and storage utilization trends. The AI must identify document processing bottlenecks and recommend workflow optimizations such as suggesting parallel review stages where sequential review is causing delays. The dashboard must highlight agents with incomplete documentation packages ranked by business impact (e.g., high-volume agents with expiring guarantees prioritized over low-volume agents). Analytics must be filterable by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), agent, document type, and time period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selections). The system must generate automated weekly document status reports for compliance officers and monthly executive summaries for management in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R002	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Agent Ranking and Scorecard View	The system must generate an agent ranking scorecard that assigns a composite performance score (0-100) based on weighted KPIs including booking volume (configurable weight default 25%), revenue contribution (default 25%), documentation accuracy (default 20%), customer satisfaction rating (default 15%), and compliance adherence (default 15%). Rankings must be displayed as a sortable leaderboard supporting multi-entity comparison across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The scorecard must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all metric labels fully localized. Historical ranking trends must be viewable for up to 36 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R005	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Documentation Accuracy KPI Tracking	The system must track and display documentation accuracy KPIs per agent measuring the percentage of Bills of Lading, shipping instructions, customs declarations, and cargo manifests submitted without errors or requiring amendments. Error categorization must include incorrect container numbers, weight discrepancies exceeding 5% tolerance, missing INCOTERMS references (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP), invalid HS tariff codes, and incomplete consignee details. The KPI must calculate a rolling 30-day, 90-day, and 12-month accuracy rate. Agents falling below 95% accuracy threshold must be flagged automatically with configurable alert notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R006	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Customer Complaint and Dispute Metrics	The dashboard must display customer complaint and dispute metrics per agent including total complaints received, average resolution time in business hours, escalation rate to management, and repeat complaint percentage. Complaints must be categorized by type: cargo damage claims, documentation errors, billing disputes, communication delays, and missed vessel cutoffs. The system must correlate complaint data with specific shipments and container numbers. Multi-entity filtering must allow viewing complaints across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations simultaneously or individually. Arabic and English complaint descriptions must both be searchable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R007	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	AI-Powered Performance Prediction	The system must leverage AI models to predict agent performance trends for the upcoming 30, 60, and 90 days based on historical booking patterns, seasonal variations (Ramadan slowdown, Indian festival seasons, Qatar National Day), and market conditions. Predictions must include expected booking volume, revenue forecast in the agent's primary operating currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), and risk score for performance decline. The AI engine must generate automated recommendations for agent engagement actions when predicted performance drops below configurable thresholds. Prediction confidence intervals must be displayed alongside forecasts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R008	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Compliance and Regulatory Adherence Score	The system must calculate and display a compliance adherence score per agent based on timely submission of mandatory regulatory documents including Qatar Customs declarations, UAE Federal Customs Authority filings, Saudi ZATCA submissions, and Indian ICEGATE EDI transmissions. The score must account for sanctions screening completion rate, Know Your Customer document validity, trade license expiry monitoring, and adherence to local cabotage regulations. Agents operating across multiple entities must have separate compliance scores per jurisdiction. Non-compliant agents must be flagged with severity levels (warning, critical, suspended) and the dashboard must show days since last compliance review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R009	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Configurable Dashboard Widget Layout	The system must allow authorized users to configure the dashboard layout by adding, removing, repositioning, and resizing KPI widgets using a drag-and-drop interface. Predefined widget templates must include booking volume gauge, revenue bar chart, compliance score meter, customer satisfaction trend line, and ranking table. Users must be able to save multiple dashboard configurations as named profiles (e.g., Regional Manager View, Executive Summary, Port Operations View). The layout engine must support both LTR and RTL orientations and automatically adjust widget positioning when switching between English and Arabic locales without data loss or visual overlap.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R010	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Scheduled KPI Report Generation	The system must support scheduled generation and distribution of agent performance KPI reports via email and in-app notification. Reports must be configurable for daily, weekly, monthly, or custom Hijri calendar-based schedules (e.g., end of each Hijri month). Report formats must include PDF with company branding, Excel with raw data sheets, and CSV for data integration. Reports must support multi-currency presentation showing amounts in the agent's local currency with USD equivalent columns. Distribution lists must be configurable per entity with role-based access ensuring Qatar entity managers only receive Qatar agent reports unless granted cross-entity visibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R011	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Comparative Benchmarking Analysis	The system must provide comparative benchmarking allowing side-by-side comparison of up to five agents across all KPI dimensions. Benchmarking must include comparison against network averages, entity averages, and port-specific averages. The comparison view must highlight variances using color-coded indicators (green for above average, amber for within 10% of average, red for below average). Benchmarking data must account for agent size and market differences—for example, an agent primarily operating at Jebel Ali with high-volume transshipment cargo must be benchmarked against peers in similar markets rather than against agents handling only local Qatar import cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R012	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Vessel and Trade Lane Performance Attribution	The dashboard must attribute agent performance metrics to specific vessel services and trade lanes. The system must show which shipping lines and vessel strings each agent books most frequently, the on-time performance of vessels used by each agent, and the agent's market share on key trade lanes such as Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Khalifa Port, and intra-GCC routes. This attribution helps identify agents who consistently select poorly performing vessel services and allows management to provide targeted guidance. Data must be filterable by container type including dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC) and reefer containers (20RF, 40RF, 40RHHC).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R013	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Mobile-Responsive Dashboard Access	The KPI dashboard must be fully accessible and functional on mobile devices and tablets with responsive design that adapts to screen sizes from 320px to 2560px width. On mobile devices, KPI widgets must stack vertically with touch-friendly interactions including swipe navigation between KPI categories and pinch-to-zoom on charts. Critical KPI alerts must be delivered as push notifications on mobile devices. The mobile view must maintain full Arabic RTL support and Hijri date display. Offline capability must cache the last-viewed dashboard state and display data freshness timestamps so users reviewing agent performance during port visits without reliable connectivity can still access recent metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R014	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Agent Activity Heatmap Visualization	The system must display an activity heatmap visualization showing agent booking activity intensity across time periods (hours of day, days of week) and geographic distribution across ports. The heatmap must reveal patterns such as peak booking hours for agents operating in different time zones (GST for Gulf agents, IST for India agents), seasonal concentration of bookings during specific months, and geographic concentration at specific ports. The visualization must support overlay of multiple agents for pattern comparison. Color gradients must be configurable and must meet WCAG 2.1 AA contrast requirements for accessibility in both light and dark display modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R015	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	KPI Threshold Alert Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure KPI threshold alerts per agent, per agent group, or globally across the network. Configurable thresholds must include minimum monthly booking count, minimum revenue target in the agent's local currency, maximum documentation error rate percentage, minimum customer satisfaction score, and maximum average complaint resolution time in hours. When an agent breaches a threshold, the system must generate alerts via email, SMS, and in-app notification to designated recipients based on entity and role. Alert escalation rules must support multi-tier escalation if the breach is not acknowledged within configurable time periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-001-R016	FEAT-047-2-001	MOD-047	Audit Trail for Dashboard Data Access	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail logging every access to the agent performance dashboard including the user identity, timestamp in UTC with local timezone notation, filters applied, agents viewed, reports downloaded, and duration of session. The audit trail must capture data export events including the format exported and row count. This is required for compliance with Qatar Financial Centre regulations, UAE data protection requirements, and Indian data privacy guidelines. Audit logs must be retained for a minimum of seven years and must be searchable by date range, user, agent viewed, and action type. Logs must be immutable and tamper-evident.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R001	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Portal Authentication and SSO	The system must provide secure authentication for agent portal access supporting username/password login with mandatory multi-factor authentication (MFA) via SMS OTP or authenticator app. Single Sign-On (SSO) integration must be supported via SAML 2.0 and OAuth 2.0 protocols for agents who maintain enterprise identity providers. Password policies must enforce minimum 12 characters with complexity requirements and 90-day rotation. Failed login attempts must trigger account lockout after five consecutive failures with automatic unlock after 30 minutes. Session timeout must be configurable per entity with a default of 30 minutes of inactivity. All authentication events must be logged for security audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R002	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Role-Based Portal Access Control	The system must implement granular role-based access control within the agent portal defining permissions for agent administrator, booking operator, documentation clerk, finance viewer, and custom roles. Each role must have configurable permissions for booking creation, booking amendment, booking cancellation, document upload, document download, financial report viewing, and KPI dashboard access. Permissions must be entity-specific so that an agent operating in both UAE and Qatar entities can have different access levels per entity. Role assignments must require approval from the agent's designated principal and the shipping company's regional manager. Role changes must take effect immediately upon approval without requiring portal session restart.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R003	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Multi-Language Portal Interface	The agent portal must support full bilingual operation in English and Arabic with the ability to switch languages at any point during a session without data loss or page reload. Arabic interface must render in RTL layout with proper alignment of form fields, navigation menus, tables, and action buttons. All system-generated messages, error notifications, validation prompts, and help text must be available in both languages. Date fields must support display in Gregorian and Hijri formats based on user preference. Numeric formatting must follow locale conventions (e.g., Arabic-Indic numerals option for Arabic locale). Port names and shipping terminology must display localized equivalents while preserving international standard codes (UN/LOCODE).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R004	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Profile Self-Service Management	The portal must allow agents to view and update their company profile information including trade license details, contact information, bank account details for commission payments, authorized signatory list, and operational coverage areas. Profile updates to critical fields such as bank account numbers, trade license numbers, and authorized signatories must trigger a verification workflow requiring approval from the shipping company's agent management team before taking effect. The system must track profile completeness percentage and remind agents of missing mandatory fields. Profile data must be validated against entity-specific requirements—for example, Qatar agents must have a valid Qatar Commercial Registration number, UAE agents must have a valid DED trade license, and Indian agents must have a valid GSTIN.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R005	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Dashboard Customization per Agent	The system must allow each agent portal user to customize their landing dashboard by selecting from available widgets including active bookings summary, pending documentation tasks, financial statement overview, vessel schedule viewer, container tracking map, and announcement feed. Widget layout must be saved per user profile and persist across sessions. The system must provide a default dashboard template per agent role that can be overridden by user customization. Dashboard data must respect the user's entity context showing only data relevant to their authorized entities. Currency display on dashboard widgets must default to the agent's configured primary currency (QAR for Qatar agents, AED for UAE agents, SAR for KSA agents, INR for India agents) with option to switch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R006	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Portal Branding Configuration	The system must support configurable co-branding of the agent portal allowing the shipping company to apply entity-specific branding including company logo, color scheme (primary, secondary, accent colors), favicon, and login page background image. Branding must be configurable per legal entity so the Qatar entity portal displays different branding than the UAE or India entity portals. The portal header must display the agent's own company name alongside the shipping company brand. Email notifications sent from the portal must use the entity-specific email template with appropriate branding and contact details. Branding assets must support standard image formats (PNG, SVG, JPEG) with specified minimum resolution requirements for retina displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R007	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Notification Preferences and Channels	The portal must allow agents to configure their notification preferences for various event types including booking confirmation, booking amendment, vessel schedule change, documentation deadline reminder, payment receipt, and KPI alert. Notification channels must include email, SMS, in-portal notification center, and webhook for agents with integrated systems. Each event type must be independently configurable per channel (e.g., booking confirmation via email and SMS but vessel schedule change via email only). Notification frequency must support immediate, hourly digest, and daily digest options. SMS notifications must support international mobile numbers with country codes for Qatar (+974), UAE (+971), KSA (+966), and India (+91). Notification language must follow the user's portal language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R008	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	API Key Management for Agent Integration	The system must provide API key management functionality within the agent portal allowing authorized agent administrators to generate, view, rotate, and revoke API keys for system-to-system integration. Each API key must be scoped to specific permissions (booking API, tracking API, documentation API, financial API) and rate-limited based on the agent's subscription tier. API keys must have configurable expiry periods (30, 60, 90, 180, 365 days) with automated renewal reminders sent 14 days before expiry. The system must support IP whitelisting per API key to restrict usage to the agent's known server IP addresses. All API key operations must be logged in the audit trail with the user who performed the action and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R009	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Sub-User Account Management	The portal must allow agent administrators to create, modify, suspend, and delete sub-user accounts within their organization. Sub-user creation must capture full name, email address, mobile number, designation, and assigned role within the agent portal. The system must enforce a maximum number of concurrent portal users per agent based on their contractual agreement. Agent administrators must be able to view login activity of their sub-users including last login timestamp, active sessions, and failed login attempts. When an agent sub-user leaves the organization, the administrator must be able to immediately revoke access and reassign their pending tasks (draft bookings, incomplete documentation) to another sub-user without data loss.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R010	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Announcement and News Feed	The system must provide an announcement and news feed on the agent portal where the shipping company can publish operational notices, tariff updates, schedule changes, regulatory advisories, and system maintenance notifications. Announcements must support rich text formatting, file attachments (PDF, images), and priority levels (informational, important, urgent). Urgent announcements must display as a mandatory acknowledgment modal upon portal login. Announcements must be targetable by entity, port, agent group, or individual agent. All announcements must be publishable in both English and Arabic with the portal displaying the version matching the user's language preference. An announcement archive must retain all published notices for a minimum of three years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R011	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Document Upload and Template Management	The agent portal must provide a document management section where agents can upload required operational documents including trade licenses, insurance certificates, bank guarantees, and customs broker authorizations. The system must maintain a document template library providing downloadable templates for shipping instructions, packing lists, certificates of origin, and dangerous goods declarations in formats compliant with Qatar Customs, UAE Federal Customs Authority, Saudi Customs (ZATCA), and Indian Customs (ICEGATE) requirements. Uploaded documents must be virus-scanned before acceptance and stored with encryption at rest. File size limits must be configurable with a default maximum of 25MB per file. Supported formats must include PDF, JPEG, PNG, TIFF, and XLSX.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R012	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Session and Device Management	The system must allow agent users to view and manage their active portal sessions across multiple devices. The session management page must display device type (desktop, mobile, tablet), browser information, IP address, login timestamp, and geographic location derived from IP. Users must be able to terminate any active session remotely. The system must enforce a configurable maximum concurrent session limit (default three sessions per user). When the session limit is exceeded, the oldest session must be terminated with a notification to the user. Trusted devices can be registered to skip MFA for subsequent logins from that device for a configurable period (default 30 days). The portal must detect and warn about sessions from unusual geographic locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R013	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Portal Offline Mode Support	The agent portal must provide limited offline functionality allowing agents to view cached data including their recent bookings, downloaded vessel schedules, saved document templates, and draft booking forms when internet connectivity is unavailable. Offline mode must use service worker technology to cache essential portal resources and last-synchronized data. When connectivity is restored, the portal must automatically synchronize any offline-created draft bookings and upload queued documents. A clear visual indicator must show the portal's online/offline status and the timestamp of last successful data synchronization. Offline mode is particularly critical for agents operating at port locations such as JNPT where connectivity may be intermittent during customs clearance operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R014	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Usage Analytics and Tracking	The system must track and report agent portal usage analytics including login frequency per agent, most-used features, average session duration, peak usage hours, and feature adoption rates. Analytics must identify agents who have not logged in for configurable inactivity periods (default 30 days) and trigger engagement notifications to the account manager. Usage data must be aggregated per entity, per port region, and per agent tier for management reporting. The analytics engine must track the agent journey through common workflows (booking creation, documentation submission, payment processing) and identify drop-off points where agents abandon incomplete processes. All tracking must comply with applicable privacy regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R015	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Accessibility and Compliance Standards	The agent portal must comply with WCAG 2.1 Level AA accessibility standards ensuring usability for agents with visual, auditory, motor, or cognitive impairments. The portal must support screen reader compatibility with proper ARIA labels in both English and Arabic. Keyboard navigation must be fully functional for all portal features without requiring mouse interaction. Color contrast ratios must meet minimum 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Form inputs must have descriptive labels and error messages must be programmatically associated with the fields they describe. The portal must be tested with common assistive technologies including JAWS, NVDA, and VoiceOver. Accessibility compliance reports must be generated quarterly and made available to entity compliance officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R008	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Competitive Rate Benchmarking Integration	The system must integrate with external market rate data sources (such as Freightos Baltic Index, Drewry World Container Index, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index) to benchmark internal revenue per TEU against prevailing market rates for equivalent trade lanes. Benchmarking must be available for major port pairs including QAHMD-CNSGH, AEJEA-USLAX, INNSA-GBLGP, AEJEA-DEHAM, and QAHMD-SGSIN. The system must display benchmark comparisons on the revenue dashboard with variance indicators (above/below market) and must cache the latest market data locally for offline access. Data refresh frequency must be configurable per trade lane with a minimum daily update cycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R016	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Agent Portal Terms of Service and Consent Management	The system must manage agent acceptance of terms of service, privacy policy, and data processing agreements through the portal. When terms are updated, agents must be required to review and accept the new version upon their next login before accessing any portal features. The system must record the exact version accepted, timestamp of acceptance, IP address, and the user who accepted. Terms must be presented in both English and Arabic with legally binding versions configurable per entity jurisdiction. Consent for optional data processing (marketing communications, performance benchmarking sharing) must be separately manageable. Consent withdrawal must be processed within the timeframes required by applicable data protection regulations in each operating jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-002-R017	FEAT-047-2-002	MOD-047	Portal Help Center and Guided Tours	The agent portal must include an integrated help center with searchable knowledge base articles, frequently asked questions, and video tutorials covering all portal features. A guided tour system must provide interactive walkthroughs for first-time users highlighting key features such as booking creation, document upload, shipment tracking, and financial report access. Help content must be available in both English and Arabic with context-sensitive help links on every portal page. The system must provide a live chat widget connecting agents to support staff during business hours (Sunday to Thursday for GCC entities, Monday to Friday for India entity). A ticketing system must allow agents to submit support requests with categorization, priority levels, and SLA-tracked resolution timelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R001	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Authority Matrix Definition Interface	The system must provide an administrative interface for defining and maintaining the booking authority matrix that specifies the authorization levels required for different booking parameters. The matrix must support configuration dimensions including booking value threshold ranges in multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), container quantity limits per booking, cargo type restrictions (general cargo, hazardous DG classes 1-9, reefer, out-of-gauge, break-bulk), trade lane restrictions by origin-destination port pairs, and INCOTERMS applicability (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW, DDP, FCA, DAP). Each matrix cell must define whether the agent can book autonomously, requires supervisor approval, or is prohibited from booking that combination entirely.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R002	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Agent Tier Classification System	The system must support a multi-tier agent classification system (minimum four tiers: Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum) where each tier corresponds to specific booking authority levels within the matrix. Tier assignment must be based on configurable criteria including agent tenure (months active), cumulative booking volume (TEU per annum), revenue contribution (in base currency USD), documentation accuracy rate, and compliance score. The system must automatically evaluate agents for tier promotion or demotion on a configurable schedule (monthly or quarterly). Tier changes must trigger notification to the agent, their account manager, and the entity's commercial manager. Manual tier override must be available to authorized commercial directors with mandatory justification documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R003	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Booking Value Threshold Authorization	The system must enforce booking value threshold authorization based on the agent's tier and entity assignment. Value thresholds must be defined in the agent's primary operating currency with automatic conversion for cross-currency bookings using the system's daily exchange rate. For example, a Silver-tier agent in Qatar might be authorized to book up to QAR 500,000 per booking autonomously, while bookings between QAR 500,001 and QAR 1,000,000 require supervisor approval, and bookings exceeding QAR 1,000,000 require commercial director approval. The system must calculate booking value inclusive of freight, surcharges, and ancillary charges at the time of booking submission. Currency conversion thresholds must use the exchange rate locked at the moment of threshold evaluation to prevent rate fluctuation exploitation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R004	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Container Type and Quantity Restrictions	The authority matrix must define per-agent restrictions on container types and quantities per booking. Restrictions must cover standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RF, 40RHHC) with temperature range specifications, open-top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat-rack containers (20FR, 40FR), and tank containers (20TK). Quantity limits must be configurable per booking and per rolling period (weekly, monthly). For hazardous cargo requiring specialized containers, the matrix must enforce additional authorization levels based on IMO dangerous goods class (Classes 1 through 9) and UN number ranges. Agents must not be able to book container types outside their authorized list regardless of other authority levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R005	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Trade Lane Authorization Rules	The system must enforce trade lane authorization rules restricting agents to book only on approved origin-destination port combinations. Trade lanes must be defined using UN/LOCODE pairs (e.g., AEJEA-INNHV for Jebel Ali to JNPT, QAHMD-AEKHL for Hamad Port to Khalifa Port). The matrix must support wildcard configurations allowing an agent to book to any port within a country or region (e.g., AEJEA-IN*** for Jebel Ali to any Indian port). Restricted trade lanes must include sanctions-impacted routes that require additional compliance screening. When an agent attempts to book a trade lane outside their authorization, the system must block the booking and provide a clear message indicating the restriction and the escalation path to request trade lane authorization expansion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R006	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Approval Workflow for Exceeded Authority	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow triggered when a booking exceeds the agent's authority limits on any dimension (value, quantity, cargo type, trade lane). The workflow must route the approval request to the appropriate authority level based on the degree of exceedance. Approval requests must contain full booking details including shipper, consignee, cargo description, container requirements, requested rate, INCOTERMS, and the specific authority dimension exceeded. Approvers must be able to approve, reject, or approve with modifications from their dashboard or via email. Approval SLAs must be configurable per entity with default maximum response time of four business hours. Automated escalation must occur if the initial approver does not respond within the SLA period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R007	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Temporary Authority Elevation Mechanism	The system must support temporary authority elevation for agents during specific periods such as peak shipping seasons, promotional campaigns, or special project allocations. Temporary elevations must have a defined start date, end date (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates), elevated authority parameters, and the authorizing manager's approval. The system must automatically revert the agent's authority to their base level upon expiry of the temporary elevation without requiring manual intervention. A notification must be sent to the agent and their account manager seven days and one day before the elevation expires. Temporary elevations must be logged in the authority matrix audit trail and must be visible on the agent's profile for compliance review purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R008	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Multi-Entity Authority Segregation	The system must maintain separate authority matrices per legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) ensuring that an agent's authority level in one entity does not automatically grant equivalent authority in another entity. An agent registered with both the Qatar and UAE entities may have Gold-tier authority for Qatar operations from Hamad Port but only Silver-tier authority for UAE operations from Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port. Cross-entity bookings where cargo moves between two entities (e.g., Qatar origin to India destination) must evaluate authority against both the origin entity and destination entity matrices, applying the more restrictive of the two authority levels. Entity-specific regulatory requirements must be embedded in the authority rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R009	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Hazardous Cargo Special Authorization	The authority matrix must include a dedicated hazardous cargo authorization module that validates agent eligibility to book IMO dangerous goods. Authorization must verify that the agent holds valid DG handling certifications (IMDG Code training compliance), that the specific DG class and UN numbers are within the agent's approved scope, and that the origin and destination ports support the DG class (e.g., certain DG classes may be restricted at Hamad Port or JNPT during specific periods). The system must cross-reference the agent's DG authority against the vessel operator's DG acceptance matrix. Agents without valid DG authorization must be completely blocked from entering hazardous cargo bookings, with clear messaging directing them to the DG authorization application process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R010	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Credit Limit Integration with Booking Authority	The booking authority matrix must integrate with the agent's credit limit to prevent bookings that would exceed the agent's available credit balance. The system must calculate the cumulative outstanding amount from all unpaid invoices, pending booking values, and the current booking value to determine if the booking falls within the agent's credit limit. When a booking would cause a credit limit breach, the system must either block the booking or route it through a credit approval workflow to the finance department. Credit limit evaluation must account for multi-currency exposures converting all outstanding amounts to the entity's base currency at current exchange rates. The system must support prepaid agents who must maintain a positive cash balance, with bookings automatically deducting from their deposit account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R011	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Rate Authority and Discount Limits	The authority matrix must define the extent to which agents can negotiate rates and apply discounts. The system must specify a minimum rate floor per trade lane, container type, and cargo category below which the agent cannot book without commercial department approval. Discount authority must be tiered—for example, Gold-tier agents may apply up to 10% discount on published tariff rates autonomously, while Silver-tier agents may only apply up to 5%. Free time extensions for demurrage and detention must also be governed by the authority matrix with configurable limits in days per container type and port. All rate deviations from published tariffs must be logged with the agent's authority level and whether approval was obtained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R012	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Authority Matrix Version Control	The system must maintain full version control of the booking authority matrix with the ability to view, compare, and restore previous versions. Each modification to the matrix must create a new version with the modifier's identity, timestamp, change description, and effective date. The system must support scheduling future-effective matrix changes allowing commercial teams to prepare authority adjustments (e.g., for an upcoming tariff season starting on a specific Hijri or Gregorian date) without immediately impacting current operations. A comparison view must highlight differences between any two versions using visual diff indicators. Rollback to a previous version must require dual authorization from the commercial director and operations manager to prevent unauthorized changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R013	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Delegation of Authority During Absence	The system must support delegation of booking approval authority when an authorized approver is unavailable due to leave, travel, or other absence. The approver must be able to designate a delegate by specifying the delegate's identity, delegation start and end dates, and the scope of delegated authority (full delegation or limited to specific booking value ranges or cargo types). Delegated authority must be capped at the delegator's own authority level—a supervisor cannot delegate authority exceeding their own limits. All bookings approved by delegates must be clearly marked in the audit trail as delegate-approved with reference to the delegation authorization. Active delegations must be visible on the authority matrix dashboard for transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R014	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Automated Authority Compliance Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor all bookings for authority matrix compliance and flag any bookings that were processed in violation of authority rules due to system errors, manual overrides, or configuration gaps. A daily automated compliance report must identify bookings where the agent's authority was insufficient at the time of booking but was subsequently upgraded, creating a retroactive compliance gap. The monitoring engine must detect patterns of repeated authority limit boundary testing (agents consistently booking at 95-100% of their limit thresholds) which may indicate the need for tier reassessment. Compliance violations must be categorized by severity and routed to the entity compliance officer for review. Systemic violation patterns must trigger automatic authority suspension pending investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R015	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Authority Matrix Bulk Import and Export	The system must support bulk import and export of authority matrix configurations via structured file formats (CSV, Excel, JSON). The import function must validate all entries against business rules before application, providing a detailed validation report highlighting any conflicts, missing fields, or values outside acceptable ranges. Import must support dry-run mode where the system validates and reports without applying changes. Export must include the complete matrix with all dimensions (tiers, thresholds, trade lanes, container types, cargo restrictions) in a format that can be re-imported after modification. Bulk operations must be available for initial system setup, periodic matrix restructuring, and migration between environments. All bulk operations must be logged in the audit trail with before-and-after snapshots.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R016	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Real-Time Authority Validation API	The system must expose a real-time authority validation API that external booking systems and agent portal interfaces can call to verify whether a specific booking request falls within the agent's current authority before formal booking submission. The API must accept parameters including agent identifier, booking value with currency, container types and quantities, origin port, destination port, cargo type, DG classification if applicable, and INCOTERMS. The API must respond within 200 milliseconds with an authorization status (authorized, requires approval, prohibited) and, if applicable, the specific authority dimensions that are exceeded. The API must support rate limiting and must authenticate callers using the agent's API key. Response format must follow RESTful JSON conventions with proper HTTP status codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-003-R017	FEAT-047-2-003	MOD-047	Authority Exception Request Workflow	The system must provide a formal exception request workflow allowing agents to request one-time authority exceptions for specific bookings that exceed their normal authority limits. Exception requests must include business justification, supporting documentation (customer purchase order, letter of credit, prior approval correspondence), and the specific authority parameters requiring exception. The workflow must route to the appropriate authority level based on the magnitude of the exception. Approved exceptions must be valid only for the specific booking referenced and must not alter the agent's base authority matrix. Exception request history must be maintained per agent and factored into tier review evaluations. The system must limit the number of exception requests per agent per month to prevent systematic circumvention of authority controls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R001	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Incentive Program Definition and Configuration	The system must provide an administrative interface for defining and configuring agent incentive programs with parameters including program name, effective period (start and end dates supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), eligible agent tiers, eligible entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), target metrics (booking volume in TEU, revenue in specified currency, new customer acquisition count, trade lane development targets), achievement tiers (e.g., 80% target = base bonus, 100% = standard bonus, 120% = super bonus), and bonus calculation methodology (fixed amount, percentage of revenue, tiered slab rates). Programs must support multiple concurrent active programs with rules for handling overlapping eligibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R002	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Multi-Currency Bonus Calculation Engine	The system must calculate agent bonuses in the agent's designated payment currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) using the exchange rate applicable on the bonus calculation date. For agents operating across multiple entities, the system must aggregate performance across entities converting all booking revenues to a common base currency (USD) for target achievement evaluation, then convert the calculated bonus back to the agent's payment currency. The calculation engine must handle partial period scenarios where an agent joins or exits a program mid-period by prorating targets proportionally. Rounding rules must be configurable per currency (e.g., round to nearest fils for AED, round to nearest paisa for INR). All exchange rates used in calculations must be auditable and traceable to the source rate feed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R003	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Target Setting and Assignment Workflow	The system must support a structured target-setting workflow where commercial managers propose performance targets per agent or agent group, which are then reviewed and approved by the entity commercial director. Targets must be definable at multiple granularities: annual, semi-annual, quarterly, and monthly. Monthly targets may be distributed evenly across months or weighted to reflect seasonal patterns such as reduced activity during Ramadan, Eid holidays, and Diwali season. The system must support both top-down target allocation (entity target distributed across agents) and bottom-up target building (individual agent targets aggregated to entity level). Target amendments during the active period must follow an approval workflow with justification documentation and must maintain an audit trail of original and revised targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R004	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Real-Time Achievement Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time achievement tracking dashboard accessible to agents through the agent portal and to commercial managers through the internal application. The dashboard must display current period targets, actual achievement to date, achievement percentage, projected end-of-period achievement based on current run rate, and the bonus amount earned so far. Visual indicators (progress bars, gauges, traffic lights) must clearly communicate whether the agent is on track, at risk, or exceeding targets. The dashboard must break down achievement by sub-metrics (e.g., TEU from Hamad Port vs. Jebel Ali, revenue from import vs. export bookings). Achievement data must refresh within 15 minutes of booking confirmation. Both English and Arabic interfaces must be supported with proper RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R005	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Commission Structure Management	The system must support flexible commission structures that define the base commission rate percentage or fixed amount per TEU payable to agents on each booking. Commission structures must vary by trade lane (e.g., higher commission for developing new trade lanes from Khalifa Port), container type (reefer containers may carry higher commission than dry containers), cargo type (special cargo and project cargo may have negotiated rates), and INCOTERMS (CIF bookings may carry different commission than FOB). The system must support override commission rates for specific agents or specific customers. Commission calculations must account for cancellations, booking amendments that change the revenue, and short-shipped containers where actual loaded quantity differs from booked quantity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R006	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Bonus Approval and Authorization Workflow	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow for bonus payments. After the bonus calculation engine computes period-end bonuses, the results must be reviewed and approved through a configurable approval chain: first by the regional commercial manager, then by the entity commercial director, and finally by the entity finance controller. Each approver must be able to view the detailed calculation breakdown, adjust individual agent bonus amounts with mandatory justification, and approve or reject the bonus batch. Rejected bonuses must be returned to the calculation stage with reviewer comments. Partial batch approval must be supported allowing approval of individual agent bonuses while others remain pending. The workflow must enforce segregation of duties—the person who configures the incentive program cannot be the same person who approves the bonus payout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R007	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Clawback and Reversal Processing	The system must support bonus clawback and reversal scenarios where previously paid bonuses must be recovered due to booking cancellations after the bonus period, revenue adjustments from disputed invoices, or agent compliance violations discovered post-payment. The clawback engine must calculate the net adjustment amount considering the original bonus calculation parameters and the specific bookings that triggered the reversal. Clawback amounts must be deducted from the next bonus payment or invoiced separately if no future bonus is expected. All clawbacks must be approved through the bonus authorization workflow. The system must maintain a clear audit trail linking the original bonus payment, the triggering reversal event, and the clawback recovery. Agents must receive detailed clawback notifications in their preferred language with itemized breakdown.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R008	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Tax and Withholding Computation	The system must calculate applicable taxes and withholding amounts on agent bonuses and commissions per entity jurisdiction. For Qatar operations, the system must apply any applicable Qatar Financial Centre tax provisions. For UAE operations, corporate tax implications post-2023 UAE CT law must be considered. For KSA operations, withholding tax rates per ZATCA regulations for non-resident agents must be applied. For India operations, TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) under relevant sections of the Income Tax Act must be computed based on the agent's PAN status and applicable rates. The system must generate tax certificates and withholding statements per local regulatory requirements. Tax computation rules must be configurable and version-controlled to accommodate regulatory changes without system modifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R009	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Incentive Program Performance Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive analytics on incentive program effectiveness including total bonus outlay per program, percentage of agents achieving various target tiers, correlation between incentive spend and revenue growth, return on investment analysis comparing bonus costs against incremental revenue generated, and year-over-year program performance trends. Analytics must be segmented by entity, port, agent tier, and trade lane. AI-powered insights must identify which incentive structures drive the most behavioral change in agent booking patterns—for example, whether volume-based targets at Jebel Ali are more effective than revenue-based targets at Hamad Port. Analytics dashboards must support export to PDF and Excel with multi-currency presentation options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R010	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Agent Incentive Statement Generation	The system must generate detailed incentive statements for each agent at the end of every incentive period showing the target set, actual achievement per metric, achievement percentage, applicable bonus tier, gross bonus calculated, tax deductions, net bonus payable, and payment currency. Statements must be generated in PDF format with the entity's branding and must be available in both English and Arabic. The statement must include a detailed appendix listing every booking that contributed to the achievement calculation with booking reference, container count, revenue amount in original currency, and converted base currency amount. Statements must be accessible through the agent portal and optionally emailed to the agent's registered email address. Historical statements must be retrievable for a minimum of seven years for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R011	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Seasonal and Campaign-Based Incentive Programs	The system must support short-duration seasonal and campaign-based incentive programs that run alongside regular annual or quarterly programs. Campaign programs must be configurable for specific durations (as short as one week) targeting specific objectives such as filling vessel capacity on underutilized routes, promoting bookings from a new port facility, or driving volume during traditionally slow periods (post-Ramadan, Indian monsoon season). Campaign targets must be independent of regular program targets. The system must handle the scenario where a single booking qualifies for both a regular program bonus and a campaign bonus, with configurable rules for whether bonuses stack, the higher bonus applies, or a maximum cap is enforced. Campaign results must be reported separately from regular program results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R012	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Agent Group and Consortium Incentive Handling	The system must support incentive programs for agent groups and consortiums where multiple agents collaborate under a shared target. Group targets must aggregate the combined performance of all member agents. Bonus distribution within the group must be configurable: equal split, proportional to individual contribution, or custom allocation percentages defined by the group leader. The system must handle the complexity of multi-entity groups where member agents operate in different entities with different currencies—for example, a consortium with members in Qatar (QAR) and UAE (AED) must aggregate performance in a common currency and distribute bonuses in each member's local currency. Group membership changes during an active incentive period must be handled with prorated target and achievement adjustments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R013	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Integration with Financial Settlement System	The system must integrate with the ERP's financial settlement module to initiate bonus and commission payments upon approval. Payment instructions must include the agent's bank details (bank name, SWIFT/BIC code, IBAN for GCC agents, IFSC code for Indian agents, account number, beneficiary name), payment amount in the designated currency, payment reference number, and applicable tax deduction details. The system must support payment methods including wire transfer, cheque, and agent ledger credit. Payment status tracking must show stages including payment initiated, bank processing, payment confirmed, and payment failed. Failed payments must trigger alerts to the finance team with failure reason codes. Reconciliation reports must match approved bonus amounts against actual payments disbursed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R014	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Retroactive Incentive Adjustment Processing	The system must handle retroactive adjustments to incentive calculations when booking data changes after the incentive period closes. Scenarios requiring retroactive adjustment include late booking confirmations attributed to a closed period, revenue adjustments from rate corrections, container type changes affecting applicable commission rates, and disputed bookings resolved after period closure. The retroactive adjustment engine must recalculate the affected agent's achievement for the closed period, determine the incremental bonus difference (positive or negative), and generate an adjustment entry linked to the next open payment cycle. All retroactive adjustments must be clearly identified in the agent's incentive statement with references to the original period and the triggering adjustment events. A configurable lookback window must limit retroactive adjustments to a maximum period (default 90 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R015	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	AI-Driven Incentive Optimization Recommendations	The system must leverage AI analytics to provide data-driven recommendations for optimizing incentive program design. The AI engine must analyze historical program data to identify optimal target levels that maximize agent engagement without excessive bonus outlay, recommend commission rate adjustments per trade lane based on market competitiveness and margin analysis, suggest personalized incentive structures for individual agents based on their historical response patterns to different incentive types, and forecast the financial impact of proposed program changes before implementation. Recommendations must consider entity-specific market conditions—for example, the highly competitive Jebel Ali market may require different incentive strategies than the less saturated Hamad Port market. All AI recommendations must include confidence levels and supporting data citations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R016	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Incentive Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must provide a formal dispute resolution workflow for agents to challenge incentive calculations they believe are incorrect. Agents must be able to submit disputes through the portal specifying the incentive period, the disputed metric, the expected value versus the calculated value, and supporting evidence (booking references, email correspondence, screenshots). Disputes must be assigned a unique reference number and routed to the commercial operations team for investigation. SLA timers must track dispute resolution time with configurable escalation at 5, 10, and 15 business days. The resolution must be documented with a detailed explanation and any resulting adjustment must flow through the standard bonus approval workflow. Dispute analytics must identify recurring dispute patterns that may indicate systemic calculation issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R017	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Audit and Compliance Reporting for Incentive Programs	The system must generate comprehensive audit and compliance reports for all incentive and bonus activities. Reports must include total incentive expenditure per entity per period with currency breakdown (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), top ten agents by bonus earned, agents with zero achievement, bonus-to-revenue ratio analysis, and exception cases (manual adjustments, clawbacks, dispute resolutions). The system must maintain a complete audit trail for every incentive calculation including the input data snapshot (bookings, rates, exchange rates), calculation formula applied, intermediate computation steps, and final result. Audit reports must be exportable in formats acceptable to external auditors. The reporting module must support entity-specific regulatory reporting requirements including ZATCA compliance reports for KSA and RBI guidelines for India outward remittances.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-047-2-004-R018	FEAT-047-2-004	MOD-047	Agent Self-Service Incentive Simulator	The agent portal must include an incentive simulator tool allowing agents to model their potential bonus earnings under active incentive programs. The simulator must accept input parameters including projected booking volume by trade lane, estimated revenue by container type, and anticipated new customer acquisitions, then calculate the expected bonus based on current program rules. The simulator must show multiple scenarios (pessimistic, realistic, optimistic) with visual comparison charts. The tool must clearly indicate that simulated results are estimates and actual bonuses depend on confirmed booking data. The simulator must use the latest exchange rates and applicable tax withholding rates to show estimated net bonus in the agent's payment currency. This feature helps agents align their commercial efforts with the shipping company's strategic objectives for each port and trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R001	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Voyage-Level CO2e Computation Engine	The system must compute total CO2e emissions for each individual voyage by multiplying fuel consumption (metric tonnes) by the IMO-specified emission factor for each fuel type (HFO: 3.114 tCO2/tFuel, VLSFO: 3.151, LNG: 2.750, MDO: 3.206). The calculation must account for main engine, auxiliary engine, and boiler fuel consumption separately. The engine must support partial voyage segments where a vessel may bunker different fuel types at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), or JNPT (INNSA). Results must be stored in both metric tonnes CO2e and short tons for US-reporting entities. Multi-entity calculations must be isolated per legal entity in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R002	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Fuel Consumption Data Integration	The system must integrate fuel consumption data from multiple sources including noon reports, bunker delivery notes (BDNs), and flow meter readings. When discrepancies exist between noon report totals and BDN quantities exceeding a configurable threshold (default 3%), the system must flag the voyage for manual review. Integration must support automated ingestion from vessel ERP systems via API and manual CSV upload for chartered vessels. Data must be validated against expected consumption curves for vessel type and draft conditions. The system must handle both laden and ballast legs, accounting for different consumption profiles between Hamad Port and JNPT routes versus intra-Gulf routes between Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R003	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Distance and Route Factor Adjustment	The system must calculate actual voyage distance using AIS waypoint data and adjust carbon intensity metrics accordingly. When AIS data is unavailable, the system must fall back to standard port-to-port distances from recognized maritime distance tables. Route-specific adjustment factors must be configurable to account for weather routing, canal transits (Suez Canal), and traffic separation schemes. The system must distinguish between laden distance and ballast distance for CII and EEOI calculations. Distance calculations must support multi-port itineraries common in Gulf feeder services connecting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and onward ocean legs to JNPT. Each segment distance must be independently verifiable and auditable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R004	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Cargo Weight Attribution for Emission Allocation	The system must allocate voyage emissions to individual shipments based on cargo weight (TEU weight or actual gross weight from bill of lading) and distance carried. Allocation must follow the Clean Cargo methodology where emissions are distributed proportionally using tonne-kilometre shares. For FCL containers, actual declared weight from the verified gross mass (VGM) declaration must be used. For LCL consolidations, the system must prorate based on cargo manifest weights. The allocation engine must handle transshipment cargo that joins or leaves the vessel at intermediate ports such as Jebel Ali when the vessel operates a Hamad Port to JNPT routing via UAE. Empty container repositioning legs must be allocated to the repositioning entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R005	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Multi-Currency Emission Cost Valuation	The system must calculate the monetary value of carbon emissions per voyage using configurable carbon price scenarios in multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Carbon pricing must support EU ETS spot price integration, internal carbon price set by each entity, and shadow carbon pricing for scenario analysis. Currency conversion must use the exchange rate applicable on the voyage completion date sourced from the central treasury module. The system must generate cost projections under different carbon tax regimes relevant to each operating entity. For Indian entity operations from JNPT, the system must support the Indian Carbon Market pricing once enacted. All monetary values must be stored with full decimal precision and displayed according to locale-specific formatting rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R006	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Vessel CII Rating Tracking per Voyage	The system must compute the Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) attained value for each voyage and maintain a rolling annual CII calculation per vessel. The CII must be calculated as Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) using the formula: total CO2 emissions divided by (deadweight tonnage multiplied by total distance in nautical miles). The system must compare the attained CII against the IMO required CII rating boundaries (A through E) for the current year, accounting for the annual reduction factor. Vessels operating predominantly on short-sea Gulf routes between Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali may have different operational profiles than deep-sea vessels to JNPT, and the system must flag vessels at risk of receiving a D or E rating with sufficient lead time for corrective action planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R007	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	EEOI Calculation per Voyage	The system must calculate the Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) for each voyage according to IMO MEPC.1/Circ.684 guidelines. The EEOI formula must be implemented as: sum of (fuel consumption × carbon emission factor) divided by sum of (cargo carried × distance). Cargo carried must be expressed in tonnes for bulk and metric tonnes for containerized cargo using actual TEU weights. The system must support both single-leg and round-trip EEOI calculations. For multi-port voyages common in Gulf-India trade lanes, each leg must contribute to the voyage EEOI independently. The system must maintain historical EEOI trends per vessel and per trade lane to enable benchmarking against fleet averages and industry baselines published by the IMO.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R008	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Emission Factor Library Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive and auditable library of emission factors for all fuel types, refrigerants, and operational activities. The library must include IMO default factors, IPCC AR5 and AR6 GWP values, and region-specific factors where applicable. Each emission factor entry must record the source reference, effective date, and expiry date. When IMO or IPCC updates emission factors, administrators must be able to add new versions without overwriting historical data, ensuring past voyage calculations remain reproducible with the factors applicable at the time. The library must cover CO2, CH4, N2O, and black carbon factors for HFO, VLSFO, MGO, MDO, LNG, LPG, methanol, ammonia, and hydrogen fuels. Factor updates must trigger recalculation alerts for open reporting periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R009	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Well-to-Wake vs Tank-to-Wake Calculation Toggle	The system must support both well-to-wake (WtW) and tank-to-wake (TtW) emission calculation methodologies and allow users to toggle between them for reporting purposes. Tank-to-wake calculations must cover only combustion emissions onboard the vessel. Well-to-wake calculations must additionally include upstream emissions from fuel production, processing, and transportation to the bunkering port. The system must maintain separate upstream emission factor tables for fuel supply chains relevant to Gulf bunkering ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port) and Indian ports (JNPT). For LNG-fueled vessels, methane slip must be included in TtW calculations using engine-type-specific slip rates. Reports must clearly label which methodology is applied, and comparative views showing both WtW and TtW results must be available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R010	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Reefer Container Emission Surcharge Calculation	The system must calculate additional emissions attributable to refrigerated (reefer) containers powered by the vessel's auxiliary engines during the voyage. The calculation must use the average power draw per reefer unit (default 4.5 kW for frozen, 2.5 kW for chilled) multiplied by voyage duration and the auxiliary engine-specific fuel consumption rate. Reefer counts must be sourced from the vessel bay plan or cargo manifest for each voyage leg. The system must support configurable reefer power draw values per equipment type (20ft standard, 40ft high-cube reefer, super-freezer). For voyages between Jebel Ali and JNPT where reefer cargo is significant in perishable trade, the system must accurately attribute reefer-related emissions to the respective shippers for Clean Cargo reporting and carbon surcharge billing purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R011	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Arabic RTL Carbon Footprint Dashboard	The system must provide a fully functional Arabic RTL user interface for the carbon footprint dashboard displaying per-voyage emissions, fleet-level summaries, and trend charts. All numerical values must render correctly in RTL layout with appropriate digit formatting (Eastern Arabic numerals optional based on user preference). Chart axes, legends, and data labels must support Arabic text without truncation or overlap. Date selectors must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar (Um Al-Qura) for filtering voyage periods, with automatic conversion between the two. The dashboard must display port names in both Arabic and English (e.g., ميناء حمد / Hamad Port, ميناء جبل علي / Jebel Ali). PDF and Excel exports from the dashboard must preserve RTL formatting and Arabic text encoding using UTF-8. The dashboard must be responsive and functional on tablet devices used in port operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R012	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Offline Voyage Emission Data Capture	The system must support offline data entry for voyage emission parameters when vessel crew or port agents lack internet connectivity. Offline mode must allow entry of fuel consumption figures, cargo weights, and voyage distances via a progressive web application that caches data locally on the device. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline entries with the central database, performing conflict resolution using timestamp-based last-write-wins with manual override capability. Offline entries must be clearly marked as pending synchronization in the UI. The system must validate offline data against the same business rules applied online, queuing validation errors for review upon sync. This is critical for vessels at anchorage outside Hamad Port or JNPT where connectivity is intermittent, and for port operations at smaller terminals without reliable network infrastructure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R013	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	AI-Driven Emission Anomaly Detection	The system must employ machine learning models to detect anomalous emission values per voyage compared to historical baselines for the same vessel, trade lane, and cargo profile. Anomalies must be flagged when computed emissions deviate by more than two standard deviations from the rolling 12-month average for comparable voyages. The AI engine must consider variables including vessel speed, weather conditions, port congestion delays, and draft levels. Detected anomalies must generate alerts to the sustainability team with contextual explanations suggesting probable causes such as hull fouling, engine degradation, or data entry errors. The system must learn from user feedback on dismissed versus confirmed anomalies to improve detection accuracy over time. Anomaly detection must operate across all four operating entities and respect data isolation between legal entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R014	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Voyage Emission Audit Trail and Versioning	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all carbon footprint calculations including input data, emission factors used, calculation methodology version, and output results. Every modification to voyage emission data must be recorded with the user identity, timestamp, reason for change, and before/after values. When recalculations are triggered due to updated fuel data or emission factor changes, the system must store both the original and revised calculation as separate versions with clear version identifiers. Auditors must be able to retrieve any historical version of a voyage emission calculation and reproduce the result using the factors and methodology applicable at that time. The audit trail must comply with IMO DCS verification requirements and support third-party verification processes required by classification societies for vessels calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R015	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Multi-Entity Consolidation and Elimination	The system must consolidate carbon footprint data across all legal entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for group-level reporting while maintaining entity-level granularity. Intra-group voyages where cargo moves between entities (e.g., Qatar entity shipping to India entity via a UAE-flagged vessel) must be identified and tagged to avoid double-counting of emissions in consolidated reports. The consolidation engine must support both equity share and operational control approaches as defined by the GHG Protocol Corporate Standard. Entity-specific regulatory reporting (e.g., India's Business Responsibility and Sustainability Reporting for JNPT operations) must use entity-level data without consolidation adjustments. The system must generate elimination entries for inter-company cargo movements and provide a reconciliation report showing entity-level totals, eliminations, and consolidated group totals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-001-R016	FEAT-048-1-001	MOD-048	Carbon Footprint Benchmarking Against Trade Lane Averages	The system must benchmark each voyage's carbon footprint against industry averages for the same trade lane using data from the Clean Cargo Working Group, IMO, and publicly available fleet performance indices. Benchmarking must be expressed as a percentile rank and absolute deviation from the trade lane average in grams of CO2 per TEU-kilometre. The system must maintain a configurable library of benchmark values updated at least annually. For Gulf-India trade lanes (Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to JNPT), the system must use region-specific benchmarks where available. Benchmark comparisons must be displayed in dashboard widgets with color-coded performance indicators (green for top quartile, amber for average, red for bottom quartile). The benchmarking module must support what-if analysis allowing users to simulate emission reductions from speed optimization or fuel switching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R001	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 1 Direct Emission Inventory Compilation	The system must compile Scope 1 direct emissions from all owned and operationally controlled sources including vessel main engines, auxiliary engines, boilers, company-owned trucks and chassis at port facilities, emergency generators at offices and warehouses, and refrigerant leakage from company-owned reefer equipment. Each emission source must be categorized by type (stationary combustion, mobile combustion, fugitive emissions, process emissions). For the Qatar entity operating from Hamad Port, this includes portside equipment and vessel emissions during port stays. For the UAE entity at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, container handling equipment emissions must be included if operationally controlled. The system must aggregate Scope 1 emissions per entity and per source category for the selected reporting period using Gregorian or Hijri calendar boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R002	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 2 Electricity and Energy Indirect Emissions	The system must calculate Scope 2 emissions from purchased electricity, steam, heating, and cooling consumed at all company facilities including port offices at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, as well as inland container depots and warehouses. The system must support both the location-based method using grid average emission factors and the market-based method using supplier-specific factors, renewable energy certificates (RECs), and power purchase agreements (PPAs). Country-specific grid emission factors must be maintained for Qatar (KAHRAMAA), UAE (DEWA for Dubai, ADDC for Abu Dhabi), Saudi Arabia (SEC), and India (CEA published factors by regional grid). Shore power consumption by vessels at berth must be included in Scope 2 where the entity purchases the electricity. Dual reporting of both location-based and market-based Scope 2 figures is mandatory per GHG Protocol requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R009	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Revenue Per TEU Forecasting Model	The system must generate 30-day, 60-day, and 90-day forward revenue per TEU forecasts for each trade lane using machine learning models trained on historical booking data, market indices, fuel price trends, and seasonal patterns. Forecasts must be entity-specific and presented in the entity's functional currency with an option to view in USD. The model must provide a confidence interval (minimum 80% and 95% bands) and must be automatically retrained monthly with new data. Forecast accuracy must be tracked by comparing predictions against actuals, and a model performance dashboard must be accessible to revenue management analysts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R003	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Category 1 Purchased Goods and Services Emissions	The system must estimate Scope 3 Category 1 emissions from purchased goods and services including bunker fuel upstream emissions (well-to-tank), office supplies, IT equipment, and port service procurement. For bunker fuel, the system must use lifecycle emission factors covering extraction, refining, and transportation to the bunkering port. The spend-based method must be available as a fallback when supplier-specific data is unavailable, using EEIO (Environmentally Extended Input-Output) factors for the relevant country. The system must integrate with the procurement module to automatically pull purchase data categorized by UNSPSC codes. Supplier engagement scores tracking the percentage of procurement spend with emission data from suppliers must be maintained. For bunkering at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali where fuel supply chains differ from JNPT, region-specific upstream factors must be applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R004	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Category 3 Fuel and Energy Related Activities	The system must calculate Scope 3 Category 3 emissions covering fuel and energy-related activities not included in Scope 1 or Scope 2. This includes upstream emissions of purchased fuels (extraction, production, transportation) consumed in Scope 1 activities, upstream emissions of purchased electricity consumed in Scope 2, and transmission and distribution losses for purchased electricity. The calculation must use well-to-tank emission factors specific to each fuel type and supply chain. For electricity T&D losses, country-specific loss percentages must be applied: Qatar (approximately 7%), UAE (approximately 8%), KSA (approximately 9%), and India (approximately 19% for aggregate T&D losses). The system must avoid double-counting with Scope 3 Category 1 upstream fuel emissions by clearly delineating boundaries. All calculations must be automated from Scope 1 and Scope 2 source data with minimal manual intervention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R005	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Category 4 Upstream Transportation and Distribution	The system must compute Scope 3 Category 4 emissions from transportation and distribution services purchased by the company that are not operated by the reporting entity. This includes third-party trucking for container drayage from inland depots to Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, feeder vessel services operated by third-party lines, rail transportation for inland container movement in India from JNPT to inland container depots, and air freight for urgent spare parts shipments. Emissions must be calculated using either actual fuel consumption data from carriers (preferred) or distance-based emission factors from GLEC Framework or DEFRA. The system must track the percentage of Category 4 emissions covered by primary versus secondary data to support data quality scoring as required by the GHG Protocol Scope 3 Calculation Guidance document.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R006	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Category 6 Business Travel Emissions	The system must calculate Scope 3 Category 6 emissions from employee business travel across all entities. Air travel emissions must be computed based on distance, cabin class, and radiative forcing index using DEFRA or ICAO factors. Hotel night emissions must use country-specific factors for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must integrate with the travel management system or expense reporting module to automatically extract travel data. For road travel between offices and port facilities (e.g., Doha office to Hamad Port, Mumbai office to JNPT), the system must calculate emissions based on vehicle type and distance. The calculation must distinguish between short-haul and long-haul flights and apply appropriate emission factors. Travel emissions must be reportable by entity, department, and travel purpose to enable targeted reduction initiatives across the multi-entity group structure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R007	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Category 9 Downstream Transportation and Distribution	The system must estimate Scope 3 Category 9 emissions from downstream transportation and distribution of cargo after it leaves the company's operational control. This includes last-mile delivery from port to consignee warehouse, onward feeder connections arranged by the consignee, and inland transportation from JNPT to destinations across India. The system must use shipper-provided routing data where available or apply default distance-based estimates using standard road and rail distances from port to major consumption centres. Emission factors must follow the GLEC Framework for intermodal transport chains. The system must support both per-shipment and statistical estimation approaches, clearly labelling the data quality level. For shipments delivered under DDP or DAP INCOTERMS where the company arranges downstream transport, these emissions may need reclassification to Scope 1 or Category 4 based on operational control boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R008	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Organizational Boundary Definition and Control Approach	The system must allow each reporting entity to define its organizational boundary using either the equity share approach or the operational control approach as specified by the GHG Protocol Corporate Standard. The chosen approach must be consistently applied across all scopes and categories within an entity. For joint ventures at port facilities (common in Jebel Ali Free Zone and JNPT), the system must correctly apportion emissions based on the selected approach. The boundary configuration must specify which vessels, facilities, equipment, and activities fall within scope for each entity. Changes to organizational boundaries (e.g., acquisition of a new terminal, disposal of a vessel) must be tracked with effective dates and trigger base year recalculation if the change exceeds the significance threshold. The system must prevent reporting the same emission source under multiple entities without explicit inter-company allocation rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R009	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Base Year Emissions and Recalculation Policy	The system must maintain a base year emission inventory for each entity and the consolidated group, with a configurable base year recalculation policy compliant with the GHG Protocol. Structural changes such as mergers, acquisitions, divestitures, and outsourcing or insourcing of activities must trigger base year recalculation when the cumulative impact exceeds a configurable significance threshold (default 5% of base year emissions). Methodology changes including updated emission factors, improved data sources, and calculation method corrections must also be evaluated for base year recalculation. The system must maintain both the original and recalculated base year inventories with full documentation of changes. For a newly established entity in KSA, the system must support prorating the first partial year into a full-year equivalent for base year purposes. The recalculation engine must propagate changes through all affected reports and dashboards automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R010	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	GHG Inventory Data Quality Scoring	The system must assign data quality scores to each emission source and Scope 3 category using the GHG Protocol data quality indicators: representativeness, completeness, reliability, temporal correlation, geographical correlation, and technological correlation. Each indicator must be scored on a scale of 1 (highest quality) to 5 (lowest quality) with clear scoring criteria defined for container shipping contexts. For example, actual fuel consumption from vessel flow meters scores 1 for reliability, while spend-based estimates for purchased goods score 4 or 5. The system must calculate a weighted composite data quality score per scope and per category. Data quality scores must be disclosed in reports alongside emission figures. Year-over-year data quality improvement tracking must be supported with targets configurable per entity. The system must recommend specific actions to improve data quality for categories scoring below the target threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R011	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	GHG Report Generation in GRI and CDP Formats	The system must generate GHG inventory reports formatted according to GRI Standards (GRI 305-1 through 305-7) and CDP Climate Change questionnaire requirements. GRI-formatted reports must include all mandatory disclosures: gross Scope 1, Scope 2 (location and market-based), Scope 3 by category, GHG intensity ratios, base year inventory, and methodology descriptions. CDP-formatted reports must map emission data to the specific CDP questionnaire fields including C6.1, C6.3, and C6.5 for Scope 1/2/3 breakdowns. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and XBRL formats. Arabic language versions of all reports must be available with full RTL layout support. Report generation must support both Gregorian fiscal year and Hijri calendar year reporting periods. Each report must include a methodology appendix describing calculation approaches, emission factors, and data sources used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R012	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Emission Reduction Target Setting and Tracking	The system must support definition and tracking of emission reduction targets aligned with Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) methodologies for the maritime sector. Targets must be configurable as absolute targets (total tCO2e reduction) or intensity targets (gCO2e per TEU-km, per revenue USD, or per DWT-nm). The system must support both near-term (5-10 year) and long-term (net-zero by 2050) target trajectories. Progress against targets must be calculated automatically using actual emission data and displayed in dashboards with trend lines projecting whether the current trajectory will meet the target. For multi-entity groups, targets must be settable at group level and cascadable to individual entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India with entity-specific allocation. The system must alert stakeholders when progress is off-track and suggest corrective actions based on the highest-impact reduction opportunities identified by the AI engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R013	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Multi-Entity Scope 3 Intercompany Emission Boundary Management	The system must correctly handle Scope 3 emissions at intercompany boundaries to prevent double-counting or omission in consolidated group reporting. When the Qatar entity ships cargo via a UAE entity vessel, the vessel emissions are Scope 1 for the UAE entity and Scope 3 Category 4 for the Qatar entity. The system must automatically identify these intercompany transactions using booking and vessel ownership data. In consolidated group reports, intercompany Scope 3 emissions that correspond to another entity's Scope 1 must be eliminated. The system must generate an intercompany emission reconciliation report showing gross entity-level emissions, identified intercompany overlaps, elimination adjustments, and net consolidated emissions by scope. This reconciliation must be auditable with drill-down capability to individual voyages and bookings crossing entity boundaries at ports including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R014	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Automated Emission Factor Updates and Regulatory Alerts	The system must provide automated monitoring for updates to emission factors from key sources including IMO MEPC circulars, IPCC assessment reports, DEFRA annual conversion factors, US EPA emission factors, and country-specific publications from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India environmental agencies. When new factors are published, the system must alert the sustainability team and present a comparison of old versus new factors with the estimated impact on current year emissions. Administrators must be able to review, approve, and schedule factor updates with an effective date. Regulatory alerts must cover new ESG reporting mandates relevant to the operating jurisdictions such as Qatar National Vision 2030 sustainability requirements, UAE Net Zero 2050 strategy mandates, Saudi Green Initiative requirements, and SEBI BRSR mandates for Indian entities. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notification, email, and optionally SMS in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R015	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Scope 3 Supplier Emission Data Collection Portal	The system must provide a secure web portal for suppliers and subcontractors to submit their emission data for incorporation into Scope 3 calculations. The portal must support data submission for trucking companies providing drayage services to Hamad Port and JNPT, feeder line operators, terminal operators, and fuel suppliers. Each supplier must have a unique login with role-based access limited to their own data submission forms. Submitted data must be validated against expected ranges and flagged for review if anomalous. The portal must support Arabic and English interfaces with RTL layout for Arabic users. Suppliers must be able to upload supporting documentation such as fuel receipts and emission calculation spreadsheets. The system must track supplier response rates and data quality over time, generating scorecards that feed into the Category 1 and Category 4 data quality assessments. Non-responding suppliers must receive automated reminder notifications in their preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R016	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	Hijri Calendar Period Alignment for GHG Reporting	The system must support GHG inventory reporting aligned to Hijri calendar years in addition to Gregorian fiscal years to meet reporting requirements of Saudi Arabian government entities and certain Qatari regulatory bodies. Since the Hijri year is approximately 11 days shorter than the Gregorian year, the system must correctly prorate emission data when source data is recorded on Gregorian dates. Monthly and quarterly GHG summaries must be available in both Hijri and Gregorian periods. The system must handle the Hijri year boundary correctly for voyage emissions that span the year-end (a voyage departing in one Hijri year and arriving in the next must be allocated based on configurable rules: departure date, arrival date, or prorated by days at sea in each year). Calendar conversion must use the Um Al-Qura calendar standard. All period labels in Arabic reports must display Hijri month names in Arabic script.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-002-R017	FEAT-048-1-002	MOD-048	GHG Protocol Verification and Assurance Support	The system must provide comprehensive support for third-party verification and assurance of the GHG inventory as required by GHG Protocol and CDP. This includes generating detailed calculation workpapers showing every emission source, input data point, emission factor applied, and resultant emission figure. The system must support limited assurance (ISAE 3410) and reasonable assurance engagement workflows with configurable reviewer access for external auditors. Auditors must be granted read-only access to all supporting data, audit trails, and calculation methodologies without the ability to modify any records. The system must generate a verification-ready data package exportable in a standardized format including all evidence required by assurance providers. Verification findings and management responses must be tracked within the system. For entities operating from Hamad Port and JNPT, local assurance requirements from QSE and SEBI respectively must be supported with jurisdiction-specific report templates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R001	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Sea Cargo Charter Voyage Data Collection	The system must collect and structure all voyage-level data required for Sea Cargo Charter reporting including vessel IMO number, vessel type and size category, fuel type and consumption, cargo carried in metric tonnes, laden distance in nautical miles, and ballast distance. Data collection must cover all charter types: voyage charter, time charter, and contract of affreightment. For time-chartered vessels where the entity does not directly control fuel procurement, the system must obtain fuel consumption data from charterer reports or owner statements. The system must validate collected data for completeness and flag voyages with missing mandatory fields. Automated data extraction from voyage fixture recaps and charter party documents must be supported via OCR and NLP parsing for fixtures related to Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT trade routes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R002	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Demand-Based Carbon Intensity Calculation per Trade Lane	The system must calculate the demand-based carbon intensity metric for each voyage as defined by the Sea Cargo Charter methodology: CO2 emissions per unit of transport work (gCO2/tonne-nautical mile). The calculation must use actual cargo carried (not vessel capacity) and actual distance sailed (not shortest theoretical distance). For multi-port voyages where cargo is loaded or discharged at intermediate stops such as Jebel Ali on a Hamad Port to JNPT route, the system must correctly compute transport work for each cargo parcel over its respective carried distance. The system must aggregate demand-based intensity by trade lane, vessel type, and reporting period. Ballast legs must be allocated to the preceding or succeeding laden leg based on configurable allocation rules compliant with Sea Cargo Charter methodology version 3.0 or the latest published version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R003	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	IMO Decarbonization Trajectory Comparison	The system must compare calculated demand-based carbon intensity values against the IMO decarbonization trajectory as specified by the Sea Cargo Charter framework. The trajectory must be configurable to support multiple scenarios including the IMO Initial Strategy (50% reduction by 2050 versus 2008) and the IMO Revised Strategy (net-zero by or around 2050). The system must calculate an alignment delta showing the percentage above or below the required trajectory for the reporting year. Results must be expressed per vessel type category (container ship by size class, bulk carrier, tanker) using the appropriate reference lines. The system must display trajectory alignment in a time-series chart showing historical performance, current year position, and projected future trajectory based on fleet renewal and fuel switching plans. Alignment results must be generated per entity and consolidated for the group.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R004	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Charter Type Classification and Reporting Scope	The system must classify each voyage or vessel engagement by charter type to determine reporting scope under the Sea Cargo Charter. Voyage charters, time charters, bareboat charters, contracts of affreightment, and slot charters must each be identified and tagged with the appropriate party responsible for reporting. For time charters where the reporting entity is the charterer, the entity reports emissions associated with its cargo using the charterer's methodology. For voyage charters where the entity is the cargo owner, spot market fixture data must be used. The system must handle complex arrangements such as relet charters and space-sharing agreements common in Gulf-India container trades. Each charter engagement must link to the corresponding commercial contract in the operations module for data validation. The system must ensure no voyage is double-counted or omitted due to charter type misclassification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R005	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Annual Disclosure Report Generation	The system must generate the annual Sea Cargo Charter disclosure report in the format prescribed by the Sea Cargo Charter Secretariat. The report must include aggregate climate alignment results per vessel type, total transport work in tonne-nautical miles, total CO2 emissions in metric tonnes, weighted average carbon intensity versus the trajectory, and narrative sections describing methodology, data quality, and actions taken to improve alignment. The report must be generated in both English and Arabic versions with the Arabic version maintaining full RTL formatting and correct tabular data alignment. The report must support dual calendar presentation with Gregorian year as primary and Hijri year equivalent noted for Gulf-based stakeholders. PDF and machine-readable formats (JSON, CSV) must be supported for submission. The report generation workflow must include a review and approval chain with sustainability manager, CFO, and CEO sign-off stages tracked in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R006	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Voyage Exclusion and Inclusion Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable rules engine to determine which voyages are included in or excluded from the Sea Cargo Charter annual report. Standard exclusion rules must cover voyages below a configurable minimum distance threshold, ballast-only repositioning voyages without commercial cargo, voyages on vessels below the 5000 DWT reporting threshold, and voyages under force majeure conditions. The rules engine must support custom rules defined by the sustainability team such as excluding trial voyages for newly delivered vessels during their first operational month. Each exclusion must be logged with the specific rule applied and be auditable. The system must provide an inclusion/exclusion summary showing the number and percentage of total voyages excluded by each rule. Re-inclusion of previously excluded voyages must be supported with documented justification. The rules must be testable in a sandbox mode before applying to production reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R007	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Vessel Type and Size Category Mapping	The system must maintain a mapping of all vessels in the fleet and chartered fleet to the vessel type and size categories defined by the Sea Cargo Charter and IMO reference lines. Container vessels must be categorized by TEU capacity ranges (feeder: under 3000 TEU, intermediate: 3000-8000 TEU, neo-Panamax: 8000-15000 TEU, ULCV: over 15000 TEU) with corresponding reference trajectory lines. The mapping must be version-controlled to handle category definition changes across reporting years. For multi-purpose vessels that carry both containers and breakbulk cargo on Gulf-India routes, the system must assign the category based on the predominant cargo type by transport work share. The vessel mapping must link to the vessel registry master data and automatically update when new vessels are added to the fleet or charter portfolio. Each vessel's reference line assignment must be reviewable and overridable by authorized users with audit trail documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R008	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Data Quality and Coverage Metrics for Sea Cargo Charter	The system must calculate and report data quality and coverage metrics as required by the Sea Cargo Charter framework. Coverage must be expressed as the percentage of total transport work (tonne-nautical miles) for which actual emission data is available versus estimated or defaulted data. The system must track data quality at the voyage level using indicators such as primary data (actual fuel consumption from flow meters or BDNs), secondary data (estimated from vessel technical specifications), and default data (IMO default values). An overall data quality score must be computed for the annual report. The system must set year-over-year improvement targets for data coverage, with the goal of achieving at least 90% primary data coverage. For chartered vessels where fuel data availability depends on owner cooperation, the system must track owner response rates and data timeliness separately for vessels operating from Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R009	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Multi-Entity Charter Activity Consolidation	The system must consolidate Sea Cargo Charter reporting data across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for group-level disclosure while maintaining individual entity reports. When the Qatar entity charters a vessel that also carries cargo for the India entity on the same voyage from Hamad Port to JNPT, the system must correctly attribute transport work and emissions to each entity's report based on cargo ownership. Consolidation must eliminate double-counting for intercompany cargo movements. The system must support scenarios where different entities have different roles in the same charter arrangement (e.g., UAE entity as disponent owner, Qatar entity as sub-charterer). Entity-level reports must be reconcilable to the group consolidated report with a clear bridge showing entity contributions, intercompany eliminations, and any adjustments. The consolidation must respect fiscal year differences if entities operate on different year-ends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R010	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Trajectory Alignment Forecasting and Scenario Modeling	The system must provide forward-looking trajectory alignment forecasting based on planned fleet changes, fuel switching strategies, and operational efficiency measures. Users must be able to model scenarios including fleet renewal with LNG or methanol-fueled vessels, slow steaming policies, and adoption of shore power at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali. Each scenario must compute projected demand-based carbon intensity for future reporting years (up to 2050) and overlay results against the IMO trajectory. The system must calculate the residual gap between projected performance and the trajectory, quantifying the additional reduction needed in tCO2 and percentage terms. Scenario comparison views must display multiple scenarios side-by-side with NPV-adjusted cost estimates in USD and local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The AI engine must recommend optimal scenario combinations to minimize cost while achieving trajectory alignment, considering vessel age profiles and charter portfolio composition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R011	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Charterer Engagement Score and Feedback Loop	The system must calculate a charterer engagement score reflecting the quality and completeness of emissions data provided by vessel owners to the reporting entity acting as charterer. The score must consider timeliness of data submission (within 30 days of voyage completion), completeness of mandatory fields, use of primary versus estimated data, and responsiveness to clarification requests. Scores must be maintained per vessel owner and per vessel. The system must generate automated feedback reports to vessel owners highlighting data gaps and quality issues. For owners of vessels frequently calling at Hamad Port and JNPT, the system must benchmark their data quality against the fleet average. Low-scoring owners must be flagged for enhanced engagement by the sustainability and commercial teams. The engagement score must feed into charter party evaluation criteria for future fixture decisions, promoting a preference for owners with better emission data transparency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R012	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Fuel-Specific Emission Factor Application per Charter	The system must apply the correct emission factor for each fuel type consumed during each charter voyage as specified by the Sea Cargo Charter methodology. When multiple fuel types are used during a single voyage (e.g., VLSFO for main engine and MGO for auxiliary engines, or LNG dual-fuel with pilot fuel), the system must calculate emissions separately for each fuel type and aggregate. For voyages where the charterer does not know the specific fuel type, the system must apply a conservative default factor. Biofuel blends must be handled by apportioning the bio-fraction with a zero tank-to-wake CO2 factor and the fossil fraction with the standard factor, in accordance with the latest Sea Cargo Charter guidance on alternative fuels. The system must flag voyages using novel fuels (ammonia, hydrogen, methanol) for manual review of applicable emission factors until standardized factors are published. Emission factors must be version-controlled and linked to the Sea Cargo Charter methodology version used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R013	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Port-to-Port Segment Granularity for Multi-Stop Voyages	The system must decompose multi-stop voyages into port-to-port segments for granular Sea Cargo Charter reporting. A typical Gulf-India voyage may involve loading at Hamad Port (QAHMD), additional loading at Jebel Ali (AEJEA), discharge at JNPT (INNSA), and return via Khalifa Port (AEKHL). Each segment must record departure and arrival ports using UNLOCODE, segment distance, fuel consumed during the segment, and cargo onboard. When segment-level fuel data is unavailable, the system must prorate total voyage fuel consumption based on distance ratio or engine running hours per segment. Transport work must be calculated per segment and aggregated for the full voyage. The system must maintain segment-level detail for audit purposes while presenting aggregated voyage-level figures in the annual report. Segment decomposition must handle canal transits (Suez Canal) as distinct waypoints with associated fuel consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R014	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Historical Trend Analysis for Sea Cargo Charter Metrics	The system must maintain and display historical Sea Cargo Charter performance data for at least ten consecutive reporting years to enable trend analysis. Year-over-year comparisons must show changes in aggregate carbon intensity, transport work volumes, fleet composition, and trajectory alignment delta. The system must calculate compound annual growth rates for key metrics and extrapolate trend lines. Historical data must be presented in interactive charts with the ability to filter by entity, vessel type, trade lane, and charter type. When base year recalculations occur due to methodology changes, historical data must be restated consistently. The system must highlight structural shifts such as fleet expansion into new trade lanes (e.g., addition of KSA entity serving Dammam or Jeddah ports) that affect comparability. All historical data must be exportable in CSV and Excel formats with metadata including the methodology version used for each year.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R015	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Sea Cargo Charter Signatory Peer Benchmarking	The system must support benchmarking of the company's Sea Cargo Charter results against publicly available aggregate data from other signatories and industry averages. Benchmark data sources must include the Sea Cargo Charter annual aggregate report, IMO DCS aggregate statistics, and Clean Cargo Working Group published trade lane averages. The system must display the company's percentile rank among Sea Cargo Charter signatories for each vessel type category and overall. Benchmarking dashboards must show comparative charts with anonymized peer data ranges (minimum, maximum, median, quartiles). For Gulf-India specific trade lanes connecting Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must isolate relevant benchmark data where available. The benchmarking module must clearly distinguish between own-data (auditable) and external benchmark data (indicative only). All benchmark comparisons must be available in both English and Arabic with RTL-compatible chart rendering for Arabic users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R016	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Automated Data Validation and Completeness Checks	The system must perform automated validation checks on all Sea Cargo Charter data before annual report finalization. Validation rules must include: fuel consumption within expected range for vessel type and voyage distance (flag values exceeding two standard deviations from fleet mean), cargo carried not exceeding vessel deadweight capacity, distance consistent with port-to-port standard distances within a configurable tolerance (default 15%), and all mandatory fields populated. The system must generate a validation exception report listing every failed check with the affected voyage, field, expected range, and actual value. Voyages with critical validation failures must be blocked from inclusion in the final report until resolved. Warning-level exceptions must be includable with documented justification. The validation engine must process the full annual dataset within five minutes for a fleet of up to 200 vessels averaging 50 voyages each, covering all routes between Gulf ports and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-003-R017	FEAT-048-1-003	MOD-048	Integration with Classification Society DCS Submissions	The system must integrate with IMO Data Collection System (DCS) submissions managed through classification societies (DNV, Bureau Veritas, Lloyd's Register, ClassNK) to avoid duplication of data entry. The system must support importing verified DCS data packages in standard XML or JSON formats as received from classification societies. Imported DCS data must be mapped to the Sea Cargo Charter data schema with automatic field matching and exception reporting for unmapped fields. The system must reconcile Sea Cargo Charter calculations with DCS-reported figures and highlight discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds. For vessels flagged in registries common to Gulf operators (Bahamas, Marshall Islands, Panama), the system must support the specific DCS submission formats required by those flag states. Bidirectional data flow must be supported: DCS data informing Sea Cargo Charter reports, and Sea Cargo Charter validated data feeding back into DCS preparation for subsequent year submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R001	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	POSEIDON Principles Portfolio Boundary Definition	The system must allow users to define the portfolio boundary for POSEIDON Principles assessment, specifying which vessels and shipping assets are in scope based on financing arrangements. The boundary must include vessels under direct ownership, vessels financed through bilateral loans, syndicated loans, export credit facilities, and leasing arrangements. For the multi-entity structure, each entity in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India may have separate financing portfolios with different lenders. The system must support tagging vessels to one or more financing facilities identified by lender name, facility ID, and commitment amount in the original currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Joint financings where multiple entities guarantee a single vessel loan must be handled with configurable attribution percentages. The portfolio boundary must be version-controlled with effective dates to track changes due to vessel deliveries, disposals, and refinancing events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R002	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Annual Efficiency Ratio Calculation per Vessel	The system must calculate the Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) for each vessel in the POSEIDON Principles portfolio using the formula: total annual CO2 emissions (tonnes) divided by the product of deadweight tonnage (DWT) and total annual distance sailed (nautical miles). CO2 emissions must be sourced from verified IMO DCS data or calculated from fuel consumption records validated against bunker delivery notes. DWT must use the summer load line deadweight as per the vessel's International Tonnage Certificate. Distance must use actual distance sailed from AIS data or noon reports. For vessels operating between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the AER must reflect actual operational patterns including ballast voyages and port waiting times. The system must handle partial year reporting for vessels entering or leaving the portfolio mid-year, prorating the AER calculation based on the days the vessel was within the portfolio boundary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R003	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Climate Alignment Delta Computation	The system must compute the climate alignment delta for each vessel by comparing the vessel's AER against the required AER from the POSEIDON Principles decarbonization trajectory for the vessel's type and size category in the reporting year. A negative delta indicates the vessel is performing better than required (below the trajectory), while a positive delta indicates underperformance (above the trajectory). The trajectory values must be sourced from the POSEIDON Principles Technical Guidance and updated when new trajectory versions are published. The system must express the delta both as an absolute value (gCO2/DWT-nm) and as a percentage of the trajectory value. Climate alignment results must be color-coded: green for vessels at or below the trajectory, amber for vessels within 10% above, and red for vessels more than 10% above. The system must generate vessel-level alignment certificates suitable for submission to participating financial institutions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R004	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Portfolio-Level Climate Alignment Score	The system must calculate a portfolio-level climate alignment score by weighting individual vessel alignment deltas by their proportional contribution to the portfolio. Weighting must be configurable by outstanding loan amount, vessel DWT, or transport work share. The weighted portfolio alignment score must be expressed as a single number representing the average percentage above or below the decarbonization trajectory. The system must compute portfolio alignment separately by vessel type category and as an overall aggregate. For multi-entity portfolios, the system must produce entity-level alignment scores for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities as well as a consolidated group score. The portfolio score calculation must handle vessels with different reporting year-ends by aligning data to a common reporting period. Year-over-year portfolio alignment trends must be charted to show improvement or deterioration trajectory over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R005	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Vessel-Level Decarbonization Pathway Projection	The system must project each vessel's future AER trajectory based on current operational patterns and planned improvements to determine when the vessel may fall out of alignment with the POSEIDON Principles trajectory. The projection must account for the annual tightening of the trajectory and any planned measures such as engine power limitation (EPL), hull coatings, propeller upgrades, or fuel switching. The system must calculate the year in which each vessel is projected to exceed the trajectory if no additional measures are taken, termed the alignment horizon. For vessels operating predominantly on Gulf-India routes between Hamad Port and JNPT, route-specific operational profiles must inform the projections. The system must display vessel-level pathway charts showing historical AER, current year AER, projected future AER under baseline and improvement scenarios, and the POSEIDON trajectory line. Fleet renewal recommendations must identify which vessels to prioritize for replacement or retrofit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R006	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Lender Reporting Package Generation	The system must generate standardized reporting packages for submission to financial institutions participating in the POSEIDON Principles. Each reporting package must include: portfolio summary with overall alignment score, vessel-level detail sheets with AER, alignment delta, and underlying data, methodology description and data quality statement, and year-over-year comparison. The package must conform to the POSEIDON Principles annual disclosure template. Reports must be generated in PDF and Excel formats. For lenders in the Gulf region, Arabic versions of the reporting package must be available with full RTL support and Arabic numerical formatting. Currency amounts related to loan balances and vessel values must be displayed in the lender's preferred currency with exchange rates noted. The system must support generation of separate packages for each lending facility where a vessel is financed under multiple arrangements. Report distribution must be trackable with read receipts and acknowledgment workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R007	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Vessel Data Integration from IMO DCS and MRV	The system must integrate vessel emission and activity data from IMO DCS verified statements and EU MRV (Monitoring, Reporting and Verification) reports where applicable. DCS data import must support the verified statement format including total fuel consumption by type, total CO2 emissions, total distance traveled, and hours underway. The system must reconcile imported DCS/MRV data against internally calculated values and highlight discrepancies exceeding a configurable tolerance (default 5%). For vessels calling at EU ports from Gulf origins (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali via Suez to Mediterranean), EU MRV data must be captured and integrated. The system must maintain a mapping between internal vessel identifiers and IMO numbers to ensure correct data matching. Data import must support both manual file upload and automated API integration with classification society portals. The system must track DCS verification status and flag vessels with overdue or missing verified statements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R008	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Multi-Currency Vessel Financing Data Management	The system must maintain comprehensive vessel financing data required for POSEIDON Principles portfolio weighting in multiple currencies. Each financing facility must record the original currency and amount of the loan commitment, outstanding balance, drawdown schedule, and repayment profile. Supported currencies must include USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, EUR, and GBP. Currency conversion for portfolio weighting must use exchange rates from the central treasury module as of the reporting date. The system must handle multi-tranche facilities where different tranches may be denominated in different currencies. For Islamic finance structures common in Gulf markets (Ijara, Murabaha, Sukuk), the system must correctly represent the financing structure and outstanding exposure equivalent. Vessel valuations for portfolio weighting must be sourced from approved valuation providers (VesselsValue, Clarksons) and updated at least quarterly. Historical financing data must be maintained for trend analysis and auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R009	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	POSEIDON Principles Trajectory Version Management	The system must maintain a versioned library of POSEIDON Principles decarbonization trajectories, supporting updates as the framework evolves. Each trajectory version must specify the applicable vessel type categories, size classes, year-by-year required AER values, and effective date range. The system must support applying different trajectory versions to different reporting years to ensure historical reports remain reproducible with the trajectory applicable at the time. When a new trajectory version is published, the system must enable impact analysis showing how portfolio alignment scores change under the new trajectory compared to the previous one. The trajectory library must include IMO Initial Strategy pathways, IMO Revised Strategy pathways, and any POSEIDON Principles-specific modifications. Administrators must be able to add custom trajectories for internal scenario analysis (e.g., 1.5-degree aligned pathway). All trajectory data must be auditable with source references to POSEIDON Principles Technical Guidance documents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R010	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Covenant and Compliance Threshold Monitoring	The system must monitor vessel and portfolio alignment against covenants and compliance thresholds defined in financing agreements. Financial institutions may require borrowers to maintain a portfolio alignment score below a specified threshold (e.g., not more than 15% above trajectory) as a loan covenant. The system must track each covenant by facility, threshold value, measurement frequency (annual or semi-annual), and cure period. Automated alerts must be generated when a vessel or portfolio approaches within a configurable margin (default 5 percentage points) of a covenant threshold. Breach notifications must be escalated to the CFO and treasury team with recommended remedial actions. The system must maintain a covenant compliance history showing all measurement dates, scores, and compliance status. For entities in Qatar and UAE where Islamic finance covenants may differ in structure, the system must support Shariah-compliant covenant structures with equivalent monitoring logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R011	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	AI-Powered Portfolio Optimization Recommendations	The system must use artificial intelligence to recommend portfolio optimization actions that improve POSEIDON Principles alignment at minimum cost. The AI engine must analyze the fleet composition, individual vessel AERs, financing structures, charter commitments, and planned newbuilding deliveries to suggest actions such as vessel disposal, speed reduction for specific vessels, fuel switching priorities, and charter-in of more efficient tonnage. Recommendations must be ranked by cost-effectiveness expressed as USD per percentage point improvement in portfolio alignment score. The system must present recommendations with confidence intervals and sensitivity analysis showing how results change under different fuel price and carbon price scenarios. For the multi-entity group with operations across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, recommendations must consider entity-level alignment separately and flag when an entity-level improvement degrades group-level alignment or vice versa. Each recommendation must include an implementation timeline and responsible entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R012	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Vessel Operational Efficiency Measure Tracking	The system must track the implementation and impact of operational efficiency measures applied to each vessel in the POSEIDON Principles portfolio. Measures must include slow steaming programs, weather routing optimization, hull cleaning schedules, propeller polishing, trim optimization, and shore power utilization at equipped berths in Hamad Port and Jebel Ali. Each measure must record the implementation date, estimated annual CO2 reduction in tonnes, actual measured reduction (calculated by comparing pre- and post-implementation AER values with seasonal adjustment), and annual cost in the entity's local currency. The system must calculate the return on investment for each measure and rank measures by cost-effectiveness. Progress dashboards must display the cumulative emission reduction achieved through operational measures versus a no-action baseline. For vessels regularly trading between Gulf ports and JNPT, the system must account for seasonal variations in monsoon weather affecting operational efficiency when measuring improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R013	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Third-Party Verification Workflow for POSEIDON Principles Data	The system must provide a structured workflow for third-party verification of POSEIDON Principles data and calculations. The workflow must include data preparation, verifier assignment, document package generation, verifier access provisioning, finding management, and final sign-off stages. Verifiers must receive read-only access to all vessel data, fuel consumption records, distance calculations, AER computations, and trajectory comparisons for the reporting period. The system must generate a verification checklist aligned with POSEIDON Principles requirements. Verification findings must be categorized as major non-conformities, minor non-conformities, and observations, with required response deadlines. The system must track the resolution of all findings and generate a verification completion certificate once all major findings are closed. For multi-entity verification where different auditors verify different entities, the system must maintain separate verification tracks that feed into a consolidated group-level verification summary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R014	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	POSEIDON Principles Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The system must provide a comprehensive POSEIDON Principles dashboard displaying portfolio alignment scores, vessel-level details, trend charts, and key performance indicators in both English and Arabic with full RTL support. The dashboard must include a portfolio alignment gauge showing the weighted alignment score against the trajectory, a vessel scatter plot showing individual vessel AER versus the trajectory line, a time-series chart of historical alignment scores, and a drill-down capability from portfolio level to entity level to individual vessel level. All charts must render correctly in RTL mode with Arabic axis labels, legends, and tooltips. The dashboard must support Hijri calendar date ranges for filtering and display. Interactive features must include click-to-filter by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel type, and financing facility. Dashboard data must be exportable to PDF and Excel with preserved Arabic formatting. The dashboard must auto-refresh with the latest available data and display the data freshness timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R015	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Newbuilding Vessel Impact Simulation	The system must simulate the impact of planned newbuilding vessel deliveries on the POSEIDON Principles portfolio alignment score. Users must be able to input newbuilding specifications including expected EEDI/EEXI values, planned fuel type (LNG, methanol, ammonia, conventional), deadweight tonnage, anticipated trade deployment (Gulf-India routes from Hamad Port to JNPT or intra-Gulf services), and expected delivery date. The simulation must calculate the projected portfolio alignment score after delivery, accounting for the addition of the new vessel and optionally the disposal of an older vessel being replaced. Multiple newbuilding scenarios must be comparable in a side-by-side view showing alignment improvement, estimated financing requirement in USD and local currencies, and payback period. The simulation must also project the newbuilding's individual alignment through its expected economic life (25 years), showing when it may approach the trajectory. The AI engine must recommend optimal vessel specifications to maximize alignment improvement per dollar of investment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R016	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Integration with Shipping Finance and Treasury Module	The system must integrate bidirectionally with the shipping finance and treasury module to synchronize vessel financing data required for POSEIDON Principles portfolio construction. The integration must pull loan facility details, outstanding balances, drawdown history, vessel collateral assignments, and covenant terms. Changes in the finance module such as new loan drawdowns, prepayments, vessel sales, or refinancing events must be automatically reflected in the POSEIDON Principles portfolio within 24 hours. The system must push POSEIDON Principles alignment scores back to the finance module for inclusion in lender reporting packages and covenant monitoring dashboards. For multi-currency portfolios spanning QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD facilities, exchange rate synchronization must use the same rates applied in financial reporting to ensure consistency. The integration must handle Islamic finance instruments (Ijara, Murabaha) by mapping their structures to equivalent conventional finance metrics for portfolio weighting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R010	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Contract vs Spot Revenue Segmentation	The system must separately track and report revenue per TEU for contract (Named Account / Service Contract) bookings versus spot market bookings. For contract bookings, the system must reference the applicable freight agreement number, Minimum Quantity Commitment (MQC), and contracted rate tiers. For spot bookings, the system must capture the quoted rate, any negotiated discount from the published tariff, and the approving authority. Revenue segmentation reports must be filterable by entity, trade lane, time period (Gregorian and Hijri), container type, and commodity group, and must be exportable in CSV and PDF formats with Arabic language support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R017	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Annual Disclosure Submission Tracking and Compliance Calendar	The system must maintain a compliance calendar tracking all POSEIDON Principles reporting deadlines and submission requirements. The calendar must include data collection deadlines, internal review milestones, verification submission windows, and final disclosure dates. Automated reminders must be sent to responsible personnel at configurable intervals before each deadline (default: 90, 60, 30, and 7 days). The system must track the status of each disclosure submission: draft, under review, submitted for verification, verified, submitted to Secretariat, and published. For multi-entity submissions, the calendar must show entity-level and group-level deadlines separately. The system must accommodate different reporting timelines when entities have different fiscal year-ends. Historical submission records must be maintained showing all past disclosures with dates, versions, and any restatements. The compliance calendar must be viewable in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats and must integrate with the enterprise calendar system. Overdue submissions must trigger escalation alerts to senior management including the group CFO and sustainability director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-1-004-R018	FEAT-048-1-004	MOD-048	Sensitivity Analysis for Key Assumptions	The system must provide sensitivity analysis capabilities for key assumptions underlying POSEIDON Principles calculations. Users must be able to vary assumptions including fuel consumption rates (plus or minus 10%), distance measurements (accounting for AIS data gaps), DWT utilization assumptions, and emission factors. The system must display tornado charts showing the relative impact of each assumption on the portfolio alignment score. Monte Carlo simulation must be available for assessing the range of possible alignment scores given input data uncertainties, with configurable confidence intervals (default 90% and 95%). The sensitivity analysis must be conductable at vessel level and portfolio level. For trade lanes between Hamad Port and JNPT where seasonal weather patterns significantly affect fuel consumption, the system must model seasonal sensitivity separately. Results must be presentable to lenders as part of the POSEIDON Principles reporting package to demonstrate data robustness. All sensitivity analysis parameters and results must be stored for auditability and year-over-year comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R001	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Multi-Entity Decarbonization Baseline Configuration	The system must allow each legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to configure independent decarbonization baselines using a reference year selectable from 2008 to current year. Baselines must capture total CO2e emissions by scope (Scope 1, 2, 3), broken down by vessel class, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer), and trade lane. Baseline data must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar year references. Currency-denominated carbon intensity metrics (gCO2e per USD/AED/QAR/SAR/INR revenue) must be calculated automatically. Each entity must be able to set distinct reduction targets (absolute and intensity-based) aligned with their national NDC commitments under the Paris Agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R002	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	IMO GHG Strategy Milestone Tracking	The system must track progress against IMO Initial GHG Strategy milestones including the 2030 target of at least 40% carbon intensity reduction and the 2050 target of at least 50% total GHG reduction compared to 2008 levels. Each milestone must be linked to specific operational KPIs such as EEXI (Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index), CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) ratings, and EEDI (Energy Efficiency Design Index) values. The system must automatically flag vessels or routes falling behind trajectory with AI-generated corrective action recommendations. Port-specific emission factors for Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV) must be incorporated into trajectory calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R003	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	AI-Driven Decarbonization Scenario Modeling	The system must provide AI-powered scenario modeling that generates at least five alternative decarbonization pathways based on configurable variables including fleet renewal schedules, slow steaming adoption rates, alternative fuel adoption timelines, route optimization potential, and shore power availability at target ports. Each scenario must calculate cumulative emissions reduction, total investment required in multi-currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), NPV of carbon credit revenues, and compliance risk scores. Scenarios must account for regional regulatory differences such as Qatar's National Vision 2030 sustainability targets, UAE Net Zero 2050, Saudi Green Initiative, and India's updated NDC commitments. Results must be presented in both English and Arabic with RTL-compliant charts and graphs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R004	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Carbon Reduction Initiative Registry	The system must maintain a registry of all decarbonization initiatives across entities, categorized by type (operational efficiency, technical retrofit, fuel switching, digital optimization, modal shift). Each initiative must capture planned start date, completion date (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri dates), responsible entity, estimated annual CO2e reduction in tonnes, actual measured reduction, investment cost in local currency with automatic conversion to USD at daily exchange rates, and status (planned, in-progress, completed, deferred, cancelled). The registry must support attachment of supporting documents including feasibility studies, vendor proposals, and third-party verification certificates. AI must automatically prioritize initiatives by marginal abatement cost curve ranking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R005	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Vessel CII Rating Trajectory Dashboard	The system must display a real-time dashboard showing CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) ratings for every vessel in the fleet across all entities, plotted against the IMO annual reduction trajectory through 2030. The dashboard must show current rating (A through E), projected year-end rating based on YTD performance, and AI-predicted rating for the next three years based on planned voyages and cargo bookings. Vessels rated D for three consecutive years or E for one year must trigger automated corrective action plan generation per MEPC.339(76) guidelines. The dashboard must support Arabic language labels and RTL layout with Hijri date selectors for period filtering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R006	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Port-Specific Emission Factor Management	The system must maintain and regularly update emission factors specific to each port of operation including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), JNPT (INNHV), and any additional ports on served trade lanes. Emission factors must cover vessel hoteling, cargo handling equipment, truck drayage, reefer container power consumption, and administrative facilities. The system must support both default IMO/IPCC emission factors and custom factors derived from port authority measurement programs. AI must detect anomalies in reported emission factors exceeding two standard deviations from regional benchmarks and flag them for review. Factors must be versioned with effective dates to ensure historical calculations remain consistent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R007	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Supply Chain Scope 3 Emission Mapping	The system must calculate and track Scope 3 emissions across the full container shipping supply chain, including upstream emissions from fuel production and delivery, downstream emissions from last-mile delivery beyond port gates, and emissions from purchased goods and services by the shipping entity. The calculation must follow the GHG Protocol Corporate Value Chain (Scope 3) Standard and the GLEC Framework for logistics emissions. Multi-entity consolidation must eliminate double-counting when cargo moves between entities (e.g., Qatar entity hands off to India entity at transshipment). Each Scope 3 category (1-15) applicable to container shipping must be individually tracked with data quality scores indicating whether values are measured, calculated, or estimated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R008	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Decarbonization Investment Tracking and ROI Analysis	The system must track all capital and operational expenditures related to decarbonization initiatives across entities with amounts recorded in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and automatically converted to USD for consolidated reporting. Each investment must be linked to measurable emission reduction outcomes enabling calculation of cost per tonne CO2e abated. ROI analysis must incorporate carbon credit revenue projections using configurable carbon price scenarios (e.g., EU ETS price, voluntary market price), fuel cost savings from efficiency improvements, and regulatory penalty avoidance. The system must generate AI-powered investment recommendations ranking pending initiatives by risk-adjusted return considering both financial returns and strategic decarbonization value.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R009	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Regulatory Compliance Calendar and Alerts	The system must maintain a comprehensive calendar of all decarbonization-related regulatory deadlines across all operating jurisdictions including IMO MEPC sessions, EU ETS compliance cycles (applicable for vessels calling EU ports), Qatar MOENVIRONMENT reporting deadlines, UAE MOCCAE sustainability submissions, Saudi NCEC requirements, and Indian DG Shipping environmental mandates. Each deadline must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The system must generate automated alerts at 90, 60, 30, and 7 days before each deadline, distributed to responsible personnel via email, in-app notification, and SMS. AI must assess readiness for each upcoming submission and flag data gaps requiring resolution. Offline-cached alerts must sync when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R010	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Carbon Credit and Offset Portfolio Management	The system must manage the organization's carbon credit and offset portfolio across all entities, tracking purchased credits by standard (Gold Standard, VCS/Verra, CDM), vintage year, project type (renewable energy, methane capture, blue carbon), and retirement status. Credits must be valued in the purchasing entity's local currency with real-time mark-to-market valuation in USD. The system must calculate the net emission position (gross emissions minus retired offsets) for each entity and consolidated. AI must recommend optimal credit purchasing strategies based on price forecasts, upcoming compliance obligations, and portfolio diversification targets. All transactions must maintain a complete audit trail compliant with ISAE 3410 assurance requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R011	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Automated Progress Reporting and Stakeholder Communication	The system must generate automated decarbonization progress reports on configurable schedules (monthly, quarterly, annually) for different stakeholder audiences including board of directors, investors, regulators, and port authorities. Each report must be available in English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions. Reports must include executive summary with AI-generated narrative, progress against targets with traffic-light indicators, waterfall charts showing emission changes by driver, investment summary in relevant currencies, and forward-looking projections. The system must support digital signatures for report approval workflows and maintain version history. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and machine-readable XML/JSON formats for regulatory submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R012	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Slow Steaming and Speed Optimization Impact Tracking	The system must track the emission impact of slow steaming and speed optimization programs across the fleet, calculating fuel savings and CO2e reductions per voyage compared to design speed baselines. Integration with AIS data and vessel performance systems must provide actual vs. planned speed profiles for each voyage segment between ports (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port). The system must quantify the trade-off between emission reduction and schedule reliability, flagging instances where speed reduction causes cargo delays that trigger INCOTERMS-related penalties. AI must recommend optimal speed profiles balancing emission reduction targets with commercial commitments and port berth window constraints.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R013	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Weather Routing and Emission Optimization Integration	The system must integrate with weather routing services to track emission savings achieved through optimized routing decisions on key trade lanes including Arabian Gulf to Indian subcontinent, Gulf to East Africa, and Gulf to Southeast Asia routes. Each voyage must record the standard route distance and emission estimate versus the optimized route actually sailed, calculating the delta in CO2e, fuel consumption, and voyage cost in applicable currencies. The system must maintain historical weather routing performance data enabling AI to identify seasonal patterns and recommend standing route adjustments. Emission savings from weather routing must be credited to the relevant entity's decarbonization initiative tracker with verifiable calculation methodology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R014	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Third-Party Verification and Assurance Workflow	The system must support end-to-end workflow for third-party verification of decarbonization claims and emission data, including verifier selection and engagement tracking, data package preparation with required evidence documents, verifier portal access with read-only permissions to relevant system data, findings management with corrective action tracking, and final verification statement archival. The workflow must support multiple verification standards including ISO 14064-3, ISAE 3000/3410, and classification society verification protocols (DNV, Lloyd's Register, Bureau Veritas). Each entity must be able to manage independent verification engagements while supporting consolidated group-level assurance. Verification costs must be tracked in local currency with multi-currency consolidation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R015	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Offline Decarbonization Data Capture for Vessel Operations	The system must support offline data capture for decarbonization-relevant operational data when vessels or port facilities have limited or no internet connectivity, common in certain berths at JNPT and during open-sea transit. Offline-capable data points must include fuel consumption readings, engine performance parameters, cargo weight and TEU counts for carbon intensity calculations, and manual emission event logs. Data must be stored locally on the vessel or facility device with tamper-evident checksums, then automatically synchronized to the central system when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution must follow last-write-wins for non-critical fields and manual review for emission-critical measurements. The offline module must support both English and Arabic interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-001-R016	FEAT-048-2-001	MOD-048	Decarbonization Target Cascading Across Organizational Hierarchy	The system must enable top-down cascading of group-level decarbonization targets to individual entities, departments, vessels, and trade lanes, with configurable allocation methodologies including equal percentage reduction, grandfathering based on historical emissions, and ability-to-pay based on revenue contribution. Each organizational unit must have visibility into its allocated target versus actual performance with drill-down capability. Bottom-up aggregation must validate that entity-level targets sum to group targets with automatic variance detection. The system must support target revision workflows requiring multi-level approval when market conditions, fleet changes, or regulatory shifts necessitate rebalancing. Arabic and English bilingual target communication templates must be available for internal stakeholder dissemination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R001	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Alternative Fuel Type Master Data Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive master data registry of all alternative and green fuel types used or planned for use across the fleet, including LNG (liquefied natural gas), bio-LNG, e-LNG, green methanol, e-methanol, bio-methanol, green ammonia, blue ammonia, grey ammonia, green hydrogen, blue hydrogen, HVO (hydrotreated vegetable oil), FAME biodiesel, and synthetic diesel. Each fuel type must capture energy density (MJ/kg and MJ/L), well-to-wake emission factor (gCO2e/MJ), tank-to-wake emission factor, methane slip factor (for LNG variants), NOx and SOx emission profiles, and applicable regulatory classifications under IMO, EU FuelEU Maritime, and regional regulations. The registry must be AI-updatable as new fuel pathways receive regulatory certification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R002	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Fuel Procurement and Bunkering Transaction Tracking	The system must record every alternative fuel bunkering transaction including port of bunkering (with specific focus on availability at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT), fuel type and grade, quantity in metric tonnes and volumetric equivalent, energy content in gigajoules, supplier name and certification status, BDN (Bunker Delivery Note) number, unit price in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with automatic multi-currency conversion, total cost, and chain-of-custody documentation for biofuel sustainability certification. The system must validate that biofuels meet ISCC or RSB sustainability certification requirements before recording them as qualifying alternative fuels. AI must detect pricing anomalies compared to market benchmarks and flag potentially fraudulent sustainability claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R003	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Well-to-Wake Lifecycle Emission Calculation Engine	The system must calculate well-to-wake (WtW) lifecycle emissions for every fuel consumed, decomposing into well-to-tank (WtT) and tank-to-wake (TtW) components per IMO LCA guidelines adopted under MEPC resolution. Calculations must use fuel-specific emission factors from the registry, adjusted for actual fuel pathway (e.g., green methanol from renewable electricity vs. bio-methanol from agricultural waste), geographic origin of fuel production, and transportation distance to bunkering port. For LNG, the system must apply configurable methane slip rates based on engine type (low-pressure DFDE, high-pressure ME-GI). The calculation engine must support both default IMO factors and certified actual-pathway factors when third-party verification is available. Results must be stored per voyage leg for granular reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R004	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	EU FuelEU Maritime Compliance Tracking	The system must track compliance with the EU FuelEU Maritime regulation for any vessels in the fleet calling EU ports, calculating the GHG intensity of energy used on board against the annual reduction targets starting from 2025 (2% reduction) through 2050 (80% reduction). The system must compute the vessel's compliance balance in gCO2e/MJ and determine whether a surplus or deficit exists. Banking, borrowing, and pooling mechanisms between vessels must be managed within the system. For vessels operating primarily on Gulf-India trade lanes but occasionally calling EU ports, the system must calculate the EU-applicable portion of voyages and associated fuel consumption. Penalty exposure in EUR with conversion to entity local currencies must be calculated for non-compliance scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R005	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Alternative Fuel Availability and Port Infrastructure Mapping	The system must maintain and continuously update a database of alternative fuel availability and bunkering infrastructure at all ports on the network's trade lanes, with primary focus on Hamad Port (LNG bunkering capability status), Khalifa Port (planned green ammonia facilities), Jebel Ali (current LNG and planned methanol infrastructure), JNPT (biofuel availability and planned green hydrogen), and secondary ports. Each port entry must record available fuel types, maximum bunkering volumes per operation, bunkering method (ship-to-ship, truck-to-ship, pipeline), lead time requirements, and pricing indicative ranges in local currency. AI must generate fuel procurement optimization recommendations considering voyage schedules, fuel availability, and price differentials across ports to minimize total fuel cost while meeting emission targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R006	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Dual-Fuel and Multi-Fuel Vessel Configuration Management	The system must manage vessel-level fuel configuration profiles capturing each vessel's fuel capability (conventional HFO/VLSFO only, LNG dual-fuel, methanol dual-fuel, ammonia-ready, multi-fuel), tank capacities for each fuel type in cubic meters, maximum fuel switching rates during operation, minimum pilot fuel requirements, and operational restrictions by fuel type (e.g., ammonia usage restrictions in enclosed waters). Each configuration must link to the vessel's class notation (e.g., DNV LNG Ready, Lloyd's Register AF notation) and flag whether the vessel is eligible for green corridor participation. The system must track planned fuel capability upgrades and retrofits with projected costs in multi-currency, timeline, and expected emission reduction impact. Configuration changes must be version-controlled with audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R007	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Green Fuel Certificate and Proof of Sustainability Management	The system must manage the complete lifecycle of green fuel certificates and proofs of sustainability including ISCC certificates, RSB certificates, Guarantee of Origin (GoO) documents for e-fuels, and Book & Claim certificates for mass-balanced biofuels. Each certificate must capture issuing body, certificate number, validity period (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri dates), fuel type and pathway, quantity covered in tonnes and energy equivalent, GHG emission saving percentage versus fossil baseline, and chain-of-custody model (mass balance, physical segregation, book and claim). The system must prevent double-claiming by tracking certificate retirement against specific voyages or reporting periods. AI must validate certificate authenticity against known issuing body patterns and flag expired or soon-to-expire certificates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R008	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Alternative Fuel Cost-Benefit Analysis Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered cost-benefit analysis engine comparing alternative fuel options against conventional fuels for each vessel, route, and entity. Analysis must include fuel price differential (captured in entity local currency and USD), carbon price impact under various regulatory scenarios (IMO MBM, EU ETS, potential GCC carbon pricing), infrastructure investment requirements for vessel retrofits and port-side equipment, operational cost changes (crew training, safety equipment, maintenance), and revenue implications from green premium freight rates. The engine must generate multi-year NPV calculations using configurable discount rates and fuel price escalation scenarios. Results must be presentable in boardroom-ready format in both English and Arabic with executive summaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R009	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Fuel Consumption Monitoring and Anomaly Detection	The system must continuously monitor alternative fuel consumption rates across the fleet using data from vessel fuel flow meters, noon reports, and IoT sensors where available. AI-driven anomaly detection must identify consumption deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (default 10%) from expected values based on vessel speed, displacement, weather conditions, and engine load profile. Anomalies must be classified as potential data quality issues, equipment malfunction indicators, or unusual operational patterns. Each anomaly must generate an alert with severity rating and recommended investigation action. For vessels with limited connectivity (common during Gulf-India transit), the system must process batched consumption data upon reconnection and apply retrospective anomaly analysis. Fuel consumption trends must be visualized per vessel, per entity, and fleet-wide.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R010	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Green Corridor Participation and Reporting	The system must track participation in green shipping corridors relevant to Gulf-India trade lanes, including any established or planned corridors between Middle East and South Asian ports. For each corridor, the system must record participating vessels, qualifying fuel usage, emission performance versus corridor targets, and compliance with corridor-specific operational requirements (speed limits, emission zones). The system must generate corridor-specific performance reports for submission to corridor governance bodies and port authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. AI must identify opportunities for new corridor participation based on fleet capability, route patterns, and fuel availability. All corridor data must be available in English and Arabic for regional stakeholder reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R011	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Methane Slip Monitoring and Mitigation Tracking	The system must specifically track methane slip from LNG-fueled vessels, which is critical as methane has a global warming potential 28-36 times that of CO2 over 100 years. For each LNG-powered vessel, the system must record engine type and methane slip profile, actual measured methane emissions where onboard sensors are available, calculated methane emissions using engine-type-specific default factors when measurements are unavailable, and mitigation measures implemented (catalytic converters, operational protocols). The system must calculate the net GHG impact of LNG adoption accounting for methane slip, alerting when methane slip erodes the emission benefit below a configurable threshold (default: LNG must deliver at least 15% net GHG reduction versus VLSFO). Reports must present methane in both CO2e and absolute CH4 tonnes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R012	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Biofuel Blend Ratio Management and Compliance	The system must manage biofuel blend ratios for vessels using biodiesel (FAME, HVO) blends, tracking the bio-content percentage of each bunkering delivery (B5, B20, B30, B100), verifying compatibility with vessel engine specifications and OEM warranties, and calculating the emission credit for the bio-portion versus the fossil portion. The system must enforce maximum blend ratio limits per vessel engine type and flag blend ratios that would void manufacturer warranties or violate port state control requirements. For multi-entity operations, the system must track biofuel mandates and incentives in each jurisdiction (India's biofuel policy, potential GCC biofuel initiatives) and optimize blending strategies across the fleet. Sustainability criteria compliance per RED II/III for EU-relevant voyages must be validated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R013	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Fuel Transition Roadmap and Fleet Renewal Planning	The system must integrate with the decarbonization roadmap to manage the long-term fuel transition strategy, modeling scenarios for fleet-wide fuel mix evolution from current state (predominantly HFO/VLSFO) through interim states (increasing LNG and biofuel share) to target state (predominantly green methanol, ammonia, or hydrogen). Each scenario must project year-by-year fuel mix percentages, associated emission trajectories, cumulative investment requirements in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and vessel acquisition/retirement schedules. The system must account for fuel technology maturity timelines, regional infrastructure development plans at target ports, and second-hand vessel market dynamics. AI must recommend optimal transition timing balancing first-mover advantage against technology risk.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R014	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Safety and Hazard Management for Alternative Fuels	The system must maintain safety profiles for each alternative fuel type addressing specific hazards (cryogenic risks for LNG, toxicity for ammonia and methanol, flammability for hydrogen), required safety equipment and training certifications for crew, port-specific safety regulations and restricted zones for alternative fuel bunkering at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, and incident reporting templates for fuel-specific emergencies. The system must track crew certification status for alternative fuel handling per STCW amendments and flag vessels where crew certifications are expired or insufficient for planned fuel operations. Safety data sheets (SDS) for each fuel must be accessible offline in English and Arabic. AI must cross-reference planned bunkering operations against port safety clearance requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R015	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Alternative Fuel Subsidy and Incentive Tracking	The system must track government subsidies, tax incentives, and financial mechanisms available for alternative fuel adoption across all operating jurisdictions. For each incentive program, the system must record the granting authority, eligibility criteria, application deadlines (in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats), maximum benefit amount in local currency, required documentation, and application status (identified, applied, approved, received, rejected). Applicable programs include Qatar's green economy incentives, UAE clean energy subsidies, Saudi Arabia's green hydrogen incentives under Vision 2030, India's biofuel promotion schemes, and any IMO-linked financial mechanisms. The system must calculate total financial benefit realized and pending per entity, consolidated in USD. AI must proactively identify newly announced programs matching the organization's fuel transition profile.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R016	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Fuel Quality Testing and Laboratory Results Management	The system must manage fuel quality testing records for all alternative fuels bunkered, capturing sample identification, testing laboratory name and accreditation status, test date, fuel parameters tested (density, viscosity, flash point, sulfur content, methanol content for methanol fuels, methane number for LNG, bio-content verification for biofuels), results versus specification limits, and pass/fail determination. Off-specification fuel deliveries must trigger automated claims workflows against the fuel supplier with financial impact calculated in the bunkering transaction currency. The system must maintain trending analysis of fuel quality by supplier and port, enabling AI-driven supplier quality scoring that informs procurement decisions. Historical test records must be retained for a minimum of five years per regulatory archival requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-002-R017	FEAT-048-2-002	MOD-048	Hydrogen and Ammonia Readiness Assessment Module	The system must provide a readiness assessment framework for hydrogen and ammonia fuel adoption, which are expected to become commercially available at scale post-2030 for container shipping. The assessment must evaluate vessel structural readiness (tank space, safety zones, ventilation requirements), port infrastructure readiness at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT for hydrogen and ammonia bunkering, crew competency gaps against emerging STCW requirements, insurance and P&I coverage implications, and regulatory approval status in each operating jurisdiction. The system must maintain a technology readiness level (TRL) tracker for each fuel pathway with quarterly AI-updated assessments based on industry developments. Results must be available in English and Arabic for management reporting with investment requirement projections in multi-currency format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R001	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Configurable ESG KPI Framework and Taxonomy	The system must provide a configurable KPI framework supporting at least 150 ESG KPIs organized by pillar (Environmental, Social, Governance), category, and subcategory with mapping to major ESG frameworks including GRI Standards (2021), SASB Marine Transportation Standard, UN Sustainable Development Goals (particularly SDG 13 Climate Action, SDG 14 Life Below Water), CDP Climate and Water questionnaire items, and S&P Global CSA indicators. Each KPI must have configurable attributes including unit of measure, data type (quantitative, qualitative, binary), collection frequency (real-time, daily, monthly, quarterly, annual), target value, threshold ranges for RAG (Red/Amber/Green) status, and calculation methodology. The framework must support entity-specific KPI activation allowing each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to maintain its own active KPI set.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R002	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Real-Time Environmental KPI Monitoring Panel	The system must display a real-time environmental monitoring panel showing key metrics including total fleet CO2e emissions (YTD and rolling 12-month), carbon intensity (gCO2e per TEU-km and per tonne-km), Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) per vessel, SOx and NOx emissions versus MARPOL Annex VI limits, ballast water treatment compliance rate, waste generation and recycling rates, oil spill incidents count and volume, and shore power utilization rate at equipped berths. All metrics must refresh at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes for operational data). The panel must support drill-down from fleet level to entity level to individual vessel level. Visualization must include trend lines, year-over-year comparisons, and target variance indicators displayed correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R003	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Social KPI Tracking for Maritime Workforce	The system must track social KPIs specific to the container shipping and port operations workforce across all entities, including crew diversity metrics (nationality, gender distribution per MLC 2006 requirements), crew welfare indicators (average contract length, rest hour compliance per STCW, shore leave frequency), workplace safety metrics (LTIF - Lost Time Injury Frequency, TRCF - Total Recordable Case Frequency per million exposure hours), training hours per employee by category (safety, technical, ESG awareness), employee satisfaction survey scores, local content and nationalization metrics (Qatarization, Emiratization, Saudization, and Indian local hiring percentages), and supply chain labor standards compliance for contracted stevedoring and trucking at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. All social KPIs must support Arabic language labels and descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R004	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Governance KPI and Compliance Scorecard	The system must maintain a governance scorecard tracking board diversity and independence ratios, ESG committee meeting frequency and attendance, anti-corruption training completion rates, sanctions screening compliance (critical for Gulf region operations), beneficial ownership transparency, data privacy compliance across jurisdictions (Qatar Data Protection Law, UAE PDPL, Saudi PDPL, India DPDP Act), whistleblower case statistics, related-party transaction monitoring, and regulatory inspection results at each port of operation. The scorecard must calculate a composite governance score using configurable weighting and display trend direction. For multi-entity structures, the system must show both individual entity governance scores and consolidated group score. AI must identify governance risk patterns and generate recommendations for improvement prioritized by regulatory risk in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R003	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	AI damage classification engine with confidence scoring	The system shall employ a trained convolutional neural network model to classify container damage into categories defined by IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) repair standards including but not limited to: dents, holes, corrosion, panel deformation, door seal failure, floor damage, and roof puncture. Each classification result shall include a confidence score between 0 and 100 percent. Classifications with confidence below 75 percent shall be automatically routed to a human surveyor queue for manual review. The AI model shall be capable of detecting multiple damage types in a single image and shall return structured JSON output with bounding box coordinates for each identified defect area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R005	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Industry Peer Benchmarking Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven benchmarking engine comparing the organization's ESG performance against industry peers using publicly available data from sustainability reports, CDP disclosures, and ESG rating agency assessments for major container shipping lines (Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, COSCO, ZIM, and regional operators). Benchmarking must cover at minimum carbon intensity per TEU-mile, fleet average CII rating, alternative fuel adoption percentage, LTIF safety rate, and overall ESG rating scores. The system must normalize data for fleet size and trade lane mix differences. Benchmark data must be refreshable quarterly with AI-assisted data extraction from published reports. Results must be displayed in comparative charts supporting both English and Arabic with entity-specific peer group customization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R006	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Port-Specific ESG Performance Comparison	The system must enable ESG performance comparison at the port level across the four primary ports of operation: Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV). Comparable metrics must include vessel turnaround time and associated emissions per port call, shore power connection rate and duration, truck idling time at terminal gates, container dwell time and associated reefer energy consumption, waste management compliance, and community impact indicators. Each port comparison must account for port-specific variables such as berth depth (affecting vessel size and emission profile), terminal automation level, local emission regulations, and climate conditions affecting reefer energy requirements. The dashboard must highlight best-in-class performance across ports and generate AI recommendations for underperforming locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R007	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	AI-Powered ESG Predictive Analytics and Alerts	The system must leverage AI and machine learning models to provide predictive ESG analytics including forecasted year-end emission totals based on current trajectory and booked cargo, predicted CII rating downgrades for vessels requiring preemptive action, early warning for safety incident trends based on near-miss pattern analysis, predicted ESG rating movements based on current performance data, and anticipated regulatory non-compliance risks based on operational trends. Each prediction must include a confidence interval and the key driving factors. Alerts must be generated when predicted outcomes deviate from targets beyond configurable thresholds, delivered via in-app notification, email, and SMS in the user's preferred language (English or Arabic). AI models must be retrained quarterly using updated operational data to maintain prediction accuracy above 85%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R008	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Multi-Entity Consolidated and Entity-Level Dashboard Views	The system must support both consolidated group-level and individual entity-level dashboard views with seamless navigation between them. The consolidated view must aggregate KPIs across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with appropriate consolidation logic (sum for absolute metrics like total emissions, weighted average for intensity metrics, worst-case for compliance metrics). Entity-level views must show only that entity's data with its local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for financial ESG metrics. Inter-entity comparisons must be available showing relative performance rankings. Access control must restrict entity-level detail visibility based on user roles and entity affiliation. Dashboard layout must be responsive for desktop, tablet, and mobile displays with proper RTL support for Arabic-language users. Users must be able to save custom dashboard configurations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R009	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	ESG Data Collection Workflow and Quality Assurance	The system must provide structured data collection workflows for ESG KPIs that cannot be automatically sourced from operational systems, including survey-based metrics (employee satisfaction, community feedback), manually reported metrics (governance indicators, policy compliance assessments), and third-party sourced data (environmental monitoring results, external audit findings). Each workflow must assign data owners, set collection deadlines with reminders (supporting Hijri calendar scheduling), enforce data validation rules (range checks, consistency checks, year-over-year variance limits), and maintain approval chains before data is published to dashboards. AI must identify missing or overdue data submissions and escalate to responsible managers. Data quality scores must be calculated for each KPI based on timeliness, completeness, accuracy, and verification status, visible on the dashboard alongside KPI values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R010	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	UN SDG Alignment and Impact Measurement	The system must map ESG KPIs and business activities to relevant UN Sustainable Development Goals with specific focus on SDG 7 (Affordable and Clean Energy - alternative fuel adoption), SDG 8 (Decent Work and Economic Growth - crew welfare), SDG 9 (Industry Innovation and Infrastructure - port modernization), SDG 13 (Climate Action - emission reduction), SDG 14 (Life Below Water - ballast water and marine pollution), and SDG 17 (Partnerships for the Goals - green corridor participation). For each mapped SDG, the system must quantify contribution using recognized impact measurement methodologies and display progress towards entity-specific SDG targets. The dashboard must show SDG contribution by entity and consolidated, with the ability to generate SDG-specific impact reports in English and Arabic for stakeholder communication and national voluntary review contributions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R011	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	ESG Rating Agency Score Simulation	The system must provide simulation capabilities for major ESG rating agency methodologies including MSCI ESG Ratings, S&P Global CSA (used for DJSI), CDP Climate Change scoring, Sustainalytics ESG Risk Rating, and ISS ESG Corporate Rating. For each rating agency, the system must model the scoring methodology based on publicly available criteria, map internal KPI data to rating agency indicators, calculate estimated current scores, and simulate score changes based on hypothetical improvements in specific KPIs. AI must recommend the highest-impact improvement actions for each rating agency, prioritized by effort-to-impact ratio. Simulations must account for the multi-entity structure, showing both individual entity and parent company rating implications. Results must be presentable in English and Arabic with currency-specific financial thresholds in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R012	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Historical damage comparison with prior surveys	When a damage assessment is initiated for a container, the system shall automatically retrieve and display prior damage records for the same container number from the ERP database. A side-by-side comparison view shall allow the surveyor to compare current damage photos with historical photos to determine whether damage is pre-existing or new. The system shall highlight differences between current and prior assessments using the AI engine to detect changes in damage extent or new damage areas. This comparison is essential for determining liability during equipment interchange between carriers, terminal operators, and trucking companies at multi-user terminals like Jebel Ali Free Zone and JNPT gateway terminals where containers pass through multiple custodial handoffs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R012	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Water and Waste Management KPI Tracking	The system must track water and waste management KPIs across vessel operations and port facilities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT including freshwater consumption per vessel day and per TEU handled, ballast water treatment compliance rate per BWM Convention, bilge water and sludge disposal records with receiving facility documentation, solid waste generation and segregation rates (recyclable, hazardous, food waste), cargo residue management for container cleaning, and wastewater discharge compliance with local port regulations. Reefer container wash water management must be separately tracked due to potential chemical contamination. The system must compare waste metrics against MARPOL Annex V requirements and flag non-compliant disposals. Waste-related cost tracking must support multi-currency for port-specific waste reception charges. AI must identify waste reduction opportunities based on operational patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R013	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Biodiversity and Marine Ecosystem Impact Monitoring	The system must track biodiversity and marine ecosystem impact metrics relevant to container shipping operations including vessel strike risk assessments for protected marine species in the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean, underwater noise emission levels from vessel operations, anti-fouling system compliance with AFS Convention, invasive species risk from ballast water and hull fouling (particularly for vessels transiting between Gulf and Indian waters), and environmental impact assessments for any port expansion or infrastructure projects. The system must integrate with marine protected area databases to flag route segments passing through sensitive zones and track mitigation measures applied (speed reduction, route deviation). KPIs must include number of interactions with protected species, ballast water exchange compliance rate, and hull cleaning frequency versus biofouling risk scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R014	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Customizable ESG Report Builder for Stakeholders	The system must provide a drag-and-drop report builder enabling users to create customized ESG performance reports for different stakeholders (investors, regulators, customers, employees, communities) by selecting from available KPIs, charts, benchmarks, and narrative sections. Each report template must be saveable and schedulable for automatic generation and distribution. Reports must support both English and Arabic language output with proper RTL formatting, bidirectional text handling for mixed-language content, and Hijri date display options. Financial data in reports must be configurable by currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) based on target audience. The report builder must support interactive digital reports (HTML) and static formats (PDF, PowerPoint, Excel). AI must generate contextual narrative commentary for selected KPIs explaining trends, drivers, and recommended actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R015	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	ESG Data Integration Hub and API Management	The system must provide an integration hub for ingesting ESG-relevant data from internal operational systems (vessel tracking, fuel management, HR systems, procurement, port operations) and external sources (weather data, AIS vessel tracking, emission factor databases, ESG rating agency portals, regulatory databases). Each integration must be configurable with connection parameters, data mapping rules, transformation logic, synchronization frequency, and error handling procedures. The hub must support REST APIs, SFTP file transfers, database connectors, and IoT device protocols. API endpoints must be provided for external systems to query ESG KPI data with proper authentication and rate limiting. Data lineage must be maintained showing the complete provenance path from source system to dashboard KPI. Integration health monitoring must alert administrators to connection failures, data quality issues, or synchronization delays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R016	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Historical ESG Performance Trend Analysis	The system must maintain historical ESG KPI data for a minimum of ten years enabling long-term trend analysis and regulatory lookback requirements. Trend analysis must support configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annual), comparison of any two historical periods, compound annual growth rate (CAGR) calculations for improvement metrics, and seasonal decomposition for metrics affected by monsoon seasons (impacting Indian operations) or summer heat (impacting Gulf reefer energy consumption). Historical data must be version-controlled to ensure that KPI methodology changes do not distort trend comparisons, with the ability to restate historical values under updated methodologies. The system must support Hijri calendar year comparisons alongside Gregorian for entities where fiscal years follow Hijri dates. AI must identify statistically significant trend changes and correlate them with known operational events or regulatory changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-003-R017	FEAT-048-2-003	MOD-048	Offline Dashboard Access and Cached KPI Views	The system must provide offline access to ESG dashboards for users in locations with limited connectivity, including vessel-based personnel and field staff at port facilities. Offline capability must include cached versions of the last-synchronized dashboard with clear timestamp indicating data freshness, ability to view all KPI values, charts, and trend data from the cached snapshot, offline data entry for manual KPI submissions queued for synchronization upon reconnection, and configurable cache refresh schedules based on available bandwidth. The offline interface must support both English and Arabic with full RTL layout functionality. For vessel-based users, the system must prioritize caching of vessel-specific and entity-specific KPIs to minimize storage requirements. Upon reconnection, synchronization must handle conflicts between offline-entered data and centrally updated values with configurable resolution rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R001	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	TCFD Four-Pillar Disclosure Framework Configuration	The system must implement the complete TCFD disclosure framework organized across the four core pillars: Governance (board oversight, management role), Strategy (climate risks and opportunities, scenario impact, strategic resilience), Risk Management (risk identification, assessment, integration), and Metrics & Targets (climate metrics, Scope 1/2/3 emissions, targets and performance). Each pillar must be decomposed into recommended disclosures (a and b for each pillar) with detailed guidance notes specific to container shipping operations. The system must support configurable disclosure maturity levels (basic, intermediate, advanced) allowing entities at different readiness stages (e.g., India entity at basic, Qatar entity at advanced) to progressively improve their disclosures. Each disclosure item must track completion status, responsible owner, last update date (Gregorian and Hijri), and data quality rating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R002	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Climate Scenario Analysis for Container Shipping Operations	The system must support climate scenario analysis using at least three TCFD-recommended scenarios: orderly transition (1.5°C aligned, aligned with Paris Agreement), disorderly transition (delayed action leading to abrupt policy changes), and hot house world (3°C+ warming with severe physical risks). Each scenario must model impacts on the container shipping business across configurable time horizons (2030, 2040, 2050) including carbon pricing impact on operating costs in multi-currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), fuel availability and cost changes for key bunkering ports, trade flow shifts affecting Gulf-India and Gulf-East Africa routes, physical risks to port infrastructure at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT from sea-level rise and extreme weather, and market demand changes for containerized cargo. AI must generate scenario narratives and quantify financial impacts using Monte Carlo simulation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R003	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Physical Climate Risk Assessment for Port Operations	The system must assess physical climate risks specific to each port of operation using climate projection data from IPCC AR6 scenarios. For Hamad Port, risks must include extreme heat impacts on outdoor operations and reefer energy demand, sea-level rise projections for port infrastructure, and sandstorm frequency changes. For Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, risks must cover sea-level rise in the shallow Arabian Gulf, extreme temperature effects, and humidity-related equipment degradation. For JNPT, risks must address monsoon intensity changes, cyclone frequency and track shifts in the Arabian Sea, flooding from sea-level rise combined with subsidence, and heat stress impacts on labor productivity. Each risk must be quantified with probability, timeframe, and estimated financial impact in local currency with USD consolidation. AI must generate risk heat maps and recommended adaptation measures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R004	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Transition Risk Assessment and Regulatory Impact Modeling	The system must assess transition risks from the shift to a lower-carbon economy specific to the container shipping sector, categorized as policy and legal risks (IMO carbon levy/MBM, EU ETS expansion, national carbon pricing in GCC and India), technology risks (stranded asset risk from conventional-fuel vessels, disruptive alternative fuel technologies), market risks (changing customer preferences for green shipping, carbon-adjusted freight rates, trade flow shifts from border carbon adjustments), and reputation risks (ESG investor pressure, customer sustainability requirements, media and NGO scrutiny). Each risk must be rated by likelihood, severity, and velocity, with financial impact estimated across the scenario time horizons. The system must model the interaction between multiple risks (e.g., carbon pricing + fuel cost changes) and their compound effect on entity profitability. Risk assessments must be updatable quarterly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R005	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Climate Opportunity Identification and Quantification	The system must identify and quantify climate-related opportunities alongside risks, as required by TCFD, in categories including resource efficiency (fuel savings from vessel optimization, shore power adoption at equipped ports), energy source (competitive advantage from early alternative fuel adoption, green energy for port operations), products and services (green shipping products commanding premium freight rates, carbon-neutral container shipping services), markets (new trade lanes from Arctic route opening, increased demand for sustainable logistics), and resilience (climate-adapted port infrastructure, diversified fuel supply). Each opportunity must be linked to specific entities, trade lanes, or vessels, with estimated revenue or cost benefit calculated in entity local currencies and USD. AI must rank opportunities by risk-adjusted return and strategic alignment with each entity's business plan and national sustainability strategies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R006	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	TCFD-Aligned Governance Disclosure Documentation	The system must capture and manage governance-related TCFD disclosures including documentation of board-level oversight of climate risks and opportunities (meeting minutes, agenda items, board committee charters referencing climate), management's role in assessing and managing climate risks (organizational charts, responsibility matrices, escalation procedures), frequency and nature of climate-related briefings to the board, integration of climate considerations into strategic planning and risk management frameworks, and incentive structures linking executive compensation to climate targets. For multi-entity structures, the system must document governance arrangements at both group level and individual entity level (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). All governance documents must be storable in both English and Arabic. AI must assess governance disclosure completeness against TCFD implementation guidance and industry best practice.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R007	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Scope 1, 2, and 3 Emission Disclosure Generation	The system must automatically generate TCFD-compliant emission disclosures covering Scope 1 emissions (direct emissions from owned/controlled vessels, port equipment, and company vehicles), Scope 2 emissions (indirect emissions from purchased electricity for offices, warehouses, and shore power consumption, reported using both location-based and market-based methods), and Scope 3 emissions (all relevant categories per GHG Protocol including upstream fuel production, downstream cargo transportation beyond port gate, employee commuting, and business travel). Each scope must be reported in metric tonnes CO2e with breakdowns by entity, emission source, and geographic region. Historical emission data for at least three years must be presented showing trends. The system must apply the GHG Protocol's organizational boundary approaches (equity share, operational control, financial control) as configured per entity. Emission intensity metrics per TEU, per tonne-km, and per revenue unit must be calculated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R008	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Internal Carbon Price Implementation and Tracking	The system must support the implementation of internal carbon pricing as recommended by TCFD for strategic decision-making, configurable as either a shadow price (used in investment analysis) or an internal fee (charged to business units). The carbon price must be configurable per entity in local currency (QAR/tonne, AED/tonne, SAR/tonne, INR/tonne) with automatic USD conversion. The system must apply the internal carbon price to all capital expenditure proposals (vessel acquisitions, port equipment, facility investments) and operational decisions (route selection, fuel choice, speed optimization) to reveal the true climate-adjusted cost. Multiple carbon price scenarios (low, medium, high, aligned with IEA NZE pathway) must be supported for sensitivity analysis. The system must track the financial impact of the internal carbon price on entity profitability and investment decisions, feeding into TCFD strategy disclosures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R009	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	TCFD Report Generation and Assembly Automation	The system must automatically assemble TCFD-aligned reports by pulling data, narratives, and disclosures from across all four pillars into a structured document following TCFD recommended structure and guidance. The report generator must support standalone TCFD reports and integrated annual report sections, with configurable templates matching each entity's reporting style. Reports must be generated in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting, bilingual charts with dual-axis labels, and Hijri date references where required. The system must include cross-referencing to detailed disclosures in other reports (annual report, sustainability report, CDP response) to minimize duplication. AI must generate draft narrative sections for each disclosure area based on available data, flagging areas where qualitative input is needed from management. The report must include a TCFD disclosure index mapping each recommended disclosure to the report page number.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R010	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Climate-Related Financial Impact Quantification	The system must quantify the financial impact of identified climate risks and opportunities on the organization's income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow statement as recommended by TCFD. Impacts must be modeled across scenario time horizons and include revenue impacts (trade flow changes, green premium capture, stranded cargo risk), cost impacts (carbon pricing charges, fuel cost changes, adaptation expenditure), asset impacts (vessel impairment from stranded asset risk, port infrastructure damage from physical risks, insurance cost increases), and capital allocation impacts (required transition investment, opportunity cost of delayed action). All financial impacts must be calculated in entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD using scenario-specific exchange rate assumptions. The system must present impacts as ranges reflecting scenario uncertainty rather than point estimates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R011	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	TCFD Metrics and Targets Progress Dashboard	The system must provide a dedicated dashboard for TCFD Metrics and Targets pillar showing all climate-related metrics disclosed by the organization, current performance versus targets, historical trend, and forward projection. Required metrics must include absolute GHG emissions by scope, emission intensity ratios (per TEU-km, per revenue), percentage of revenue from low-carbon services, climate-related capital expenditure as percentage of total capex, internal carbon price applied, and proportion of fleet meeting CII ratings A or B. Each metric must show entity-level and consolidated values with currency amounts in configurable denominations. The dashboard must indicate alignment with Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) 1.5°C pathway where applicable. AI must generate automated commentary on metric movements and flag metrics trending away from targets with specific causal analysis and recommended corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R012	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Climate Risk Integration with Enterprise Risk Management	The system must demonstrate and document the integration of climate risk management with the organization's enterprise risk management (ERM) framework, as required by TCFD's Risk Management pillar. The system must map climate risks to the existing risk taxonomy and risk register, assign climate risk ratings using the organization's standard likelihood-impact matrix, embed climate risk triggers in operational risk monitoring dashboards, and document the escalation pathway from operational climate events to board-level risk reporting. For multi-entity operations, the system must show how climate risks are managed at entity level (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and escalated to group level. The system must track risk mitigation actions, their status, and effectiveness. AI must correlate climate risk events (extreme weather at JNPT, heat waves at Gulf ports) with operational and financial impacts to continuously improve risk quantification accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R013	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Regulatory Alignment Tracker for TCFD-Adjacent Mandates	The system must track alignment with TCFD-adjacent mandatory disclosure requirements across all operating jurisdictions, including ISSB IFRS S1 and S2 climate disclosure standards (expected mandatory in multiple jurisdictions), Qatar Financial Markets Authority ESG disclosure requirements, Abu Dhabi Securities Exchange ESG reporting guide, Saudi CMA ESG disclosure requirements, SEBI (India) Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR) framework, and EU Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) for any EU-linked reporting obligations. The system must maintain a regulatory matrix mapping TCFD disclosures to each jurisdiction's requirements, highlighting overlaps and gaps. AI must monitor regulatory developments and alert when new disclosure mandates are announced that affect any entity. Compliance status must be tracked with deadline management supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R014	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Stakeholder-Specific TCFD Disclosure Packaging	The system must generate stakeholder-specific versions of TCFD disclosures tailored to different audiences including institutional investors (detailed financial impact data, scenario analysis results, strategy resilience assessment), banks and lenders (Poseidon Principles alignment, climate VaR, transition plan credibility), insurance and P&I clubs (physical risk exposure, adaptation measures, loss history), customers and cargo owners (supply chain emission data, green service offerings, science-based target progress), regulators and port authorities (compliance status, emission data by port, mitigation measures at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), and rating agencies (comprehensive data packages formatted to each agency's methodology). Each package must be available in English and Arabic. AI must recommend which disclosures to prioritize based on stakeholder engagement analysis and upcoming investor meetings or regulatory submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R015	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Climate Value-at-Risk Calculation Module	The system must calculate Climate Value-at-Risk (Climate VaR) for the organization's asset portfolio including vessels, port infrastructure interests, and logistics facilities across all entities. The calculation must incorporate both physical risk VaR (potential asset value destruction from chronic and acute climate hazards) and transition risk VaR (potential asset value reduction from policy changes, technology shifts, and market responses). The model must use geo-referenced asset locations (vessels by operating region, facilities by specific port) mapped to climate hazard projections. Time horizons must cover 2030, 2040, and 2050 aligned with scenario analysis. Results must be expressed in entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated USD. The system must calculate VaR at entity level and consolidated, showing diversification effects across the multi-entity portfolio. AI must identify the assets contributing most to Climate VaR and recommend risk mitigation strategies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R016	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	TCFD Disclosure Audit Trail and Version Control	The system must maintain a complete audit trail for all TCFD disclosures including who created, reviewed, approved, and published each disclosure element, the data sources and calculation methodologies used, all changes between report versions with tracked markup capability, reviewer comments and management responses, and final sign-off by authorized personnel with digital signature support. Each disclosure version must be archived and retrievable for regulatory review or investor queries. The audit trail must support the independent assurance process by providing verifiers with systematic access to supporting evidence for each disclosure claim. Data lineage must be traceable from any reported figure back to source systems. All audit trail entries must record timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri formats with timezone awareness for multi-entity operations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India time zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R017	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Transition Plan Disclosure and Progress Tracking	The system must support detailed transition plan disclosure as increasingly expected alongside TCFD reporting, documenting the organization's strategy for transitioning to a low-carbon economy. The transition plan must cover decarbonization targets with interim milestones (2025, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050), planned actions by lever (fleet renewal, fuel switching, operational efficiency, shore power, digital optimization), capital allocation plan with investment amounts per year in multi-currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), governance and accountability structure for plan execution, sensitivity analysis showing plan viability under different carbon price and technology scenarios, and just transition considerations for workforce affected by operational changes at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must track actual progress against the transition plan quarterly, generating variance reports and AI-driven recommendations for plan adjustments when off-track.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-048-2-004-R018	FEAT-048-2-004	MOD-048	Offline TCFD Data Collection and Report Review	The system must support offline data collection and report review workflows for TCFD disclosures, enabling board members, senior executives, and subject matter experts to review and provide input on draft TCFD reports without requiring continuous internet connectivity. Offline capabilities must include downloadable report drafts in annotatable PDF and Word formats, offline data entry forms for qualitative governance and strategy disclosures with structured fields and guidance notes, cached climate scenario results and risk assessments for offline executive review sessions, and queued comment and approval actions that synchronize when connectivity is restored. The offline module must support both English and Arabic document formats with RTL rendering in offline viewers. Conflict resolution for simultaneous offline edits must flag discrepancies for manual resolution by the TCFD report coordinator. All offline actions must be logged to the audit trail upon synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R001	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Real-Time GPS Position Ingestion	The system must ingest GPS position data from IoT tracking devices attached to containers at a configurable polling interval ranging from 30 seconds to 15 minutes. Each position record must include latitude, longitude, altitude, speed, heading, horizontal dilution of precision (HDOP), timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the device battery level. The ingestion pipeline must support at minimum 50,000 concurrent container tracking devices across all four port jurisdictions (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHA). Data must be persisted with entity-level segregation for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations, and the system must handle out-of-order and duplicate messages gracefully using idempotent upsert logic keyed on device ID and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R002	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Multi-Network Connectivity Fallback	The system must support GPS data transmission over multiple network types including 4G/LTE cellular, satellite (Iridium, Globalstar), LoRaWAN for port yard coverage, and Wi-Fi for warehouse zones. When a container moves through areas with no cellular coverage such as open sea transit between Jebel Ali and JNPT or desert corridors in KSA, the system must automatically detect satellite-relayed position reports and reconcile them with cellular data upon reconnection. The fallback priority order must be configurable per route corridor. Each network handover event must be logged with timestamps, signal strength metrics, and the originating network type to support SLA compliance auditing for tracking continuity guarantees defined in shipping contracts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R003	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Geofence Zone Configuration and Management	The system must allow authorized users to define, edit, and delete geofence zones as polygonal or circular boundaries on a map interface. Geofences must be categorizable as port terminal zones, customs bonded areas, free trade zones (e.g., JAFZA in Dubai, QFZ in Qatar), inland container depots, and customer delivery zones. Each geofence must support metadata including zone name in both Arabic and English, operating entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), associated port code (UN/LOCODE), and applicable regulatory jurisdiction. The system must support a minimum of 10,000 active geofences globally and must validate that new geofences do not create conflicting overlapping boundaries within the same category without explicit administrator approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R004	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Geofence Entry Exit Event Triggering	The system must generate real-time geofence entry and exit events within 60 seconds of a container crossing a defined geofence boundary. Each event must record the container number (ISO 6346 format), geofence zone ID, crossing direction (entry or exit), exact crossing timestamp in UTC and local timezone of the geofence jurisdiction, GPS coordinates at the crossing point, and the speed of the container at boundary crossing. Events must trigger configurable downstream actions including automatic customs pre-arrival notifications for containers entering Hamad Port or JNPT customs zones, SMS/email alerts to consignees, and automatic milestone updates in the shipment tracking module. The system must implement hysteresis logic with a configurable buffer distance of 50 to 500 meters to prevent rapid toggling of events when a container is near a boundary.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R005	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Interactive Map Visualization with RTL Support	The system must provide an interactive map interface displaying real-time container positions using clustered markers that dynamically decluster as users zoom in. The map must support both left-to-right (English) and right-to-left (Arabic) layouts with all labels, tooltips, legends, and search controls properly mirrored for RTL rendering. Map layers must include satellite imagery, street maps, maritime charts for sea routes, and a custom port terminal overlay showing berth numbers, yard blocks, and gate positions for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Users must be able to filter visible containers by entity, port, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), booking reference, or bill of lading number. The map must render correctly on desktop and tablet devices with minimum resolution of 1024x768.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R006	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Historical Route Playback and Trail Rendering	The system must allow users to replay the historical route of any tracked container over a selectable date range up to 180 days in the past. The playback interface must render the container's path as a polyline on the map with color-coded segments indicating speed ranges (stationary in red, slow movement under 20 km/h in amber, normal transit in green). Users must be able to control playback speed from 1x to 100x real-time, pause, and skip to specific timestamps. Each point on the trail must display a tooltip with the timestamp (Gregorian and Hijri), coordinates, speed, and the geofence zone name if applicable. The route data must be retrievable in under 3 seconds for routes with up to 50,000 data points, and the system must support exporting route history as CSV, GeoJSON, or KML files with column headers in the user's selected language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R007	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Dwell Time Calculation and Alerting	The system must automatically calculate container dwell time within each geofence zone by computing the elapsed duration between entry and exit events. Dwell time must be reported in hours and minutes and categorized against configurable thresholds per zone type: for example, maximum 48 hours in a port customs inspection zone at JNPT, maximum 72 hours in a Jebel Ali free zone yard, or maximum 24 hours at an inland depot in KSA. When a container exceeds the configured dwell time threshold, the system must generate an escalating alert sequence: first a notification to the operations team, then after a second configurable period an alert to the logistics manager, and finally a critical alert to the entity compliance officer. Dwell time reports must be available in all five currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) when correlated with demurrage and detention cost calculations from the billing module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R008	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	AI-Powered ETA Prediction Engine	The system must leverage historical GPS movement data, current traffic conditions, port congestion indices, and weather data to generate AI-predicted estimated times of arrival (ETA) for containers in transit. The prediction model must be trained on at least 12 months of historical movement data per route corridor and must achieve an accuracy of within plus or minus 2 hours for land transit and plus or minus 6 hours for sea transit legs between Gulf ports and JNPT. ETA predictions must update dynamically as new GPS data arrives and must be exposed via API for consumption by the shipment visibility module. The system must display ETA in the local timezone of the destination port with both Gregorian and Hijri date formats and must log each prediction revision with a confidence score percentage for audit trail purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R009	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Container Yard Position Mapping	The system must integrate with port terminal operating systems (TOS) at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to display precise container yard positions using bay-row-tier coordinates. When a container is within a port terminal geofence, the system must switch from GPS-based tracking to TOS-reported yard position for higher accuracy, as GPS signals can be unreliable within dense stacking areas. The yard map must render container stacks visually with color coding by container status (import, export, transshipment, empty) and must support Arabic and English labeling of yard blocks and berth designations. The system must reconcile GPS and TOS positions and flag discrepancies greater than 100 meters for investigation by terminal operations staff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R010	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Multi-Entity Data Segregation and Access Control	The system must enforce strict data segregation so that GPS tracking data belonging to one legal entity (e.g., Qatar entity) is not accessible to users of another entity (e.g., India entity) unless explicitly authorized through cross-entity sharing permissions. Each GPS position record, geofence event, and route history entry must be tagged with the owning entity identifier. Role-based access control must support granular permissions including view-only tracking, geofence management, alert configuration, and report generation at entity, branch, and department levels. The system must support a super-admin role that can view consolidated cross-entity tracking data for group-level logistics coordination while maintaining audit logs of all cross-entity data access events in compliance with Qatar PDPL, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act data protection regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R011	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Offline GPS Data Buffering and Synchronization	The system must handle scenarios where IoT tracking devices lose network connectivity by buffering GPS position data locally on the device for a minimum of 7 days of continuous recording at 5-minute intervals. Upon network reconnection, the device must transmit all buffered data points in chronological order to the central platform. The system must process backfilled data and retroactively generate any geofence entry/exit events that occurred during the offline period, correctly timestamping them with the original GPS-recorded time rather than the upload time. The backfill processing must not disrupt real-time tracking operations for other containers and must complete within 30 minutes for a 7-day data backlog. The system must flag containers that experienced connectivity gaps exceeding 4 hours on the operations dashboard with an indicator showing the duration and last known position.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R012	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Automated Route Deviation Detection	The system must detect when a container deviates from its planned route corridor by more than a configurable distance threshold (default 5 kilometers for land routes, 50 nautical miles for sea routes). The planned route must be defined either by a predefined corridor template associated with the origin-destination pair or by an AI-generated optimal route based on historical patterns. Upon detecting a deviation, the system must immediately generate a high-priority security alert with the container number, current GPS position, deviation distance, planned versus actual bearing, and a map snapshot showing the divergence point. The alert must be sent to the security operations center, the booking party, and the customs authority of the jurisdiction where the deviation occurred. For containers carrying dangerous goods (IMO classes 1 through 9), route deviation alerts must additionally be escalated to the port state authority per IMDG Code requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R005	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Voyage Route Replay and History	The system must store complete AIS track histories for all monitored vessels and provide a route replay feature that allows users to visualize historical vessel movements along a timeline slider. Route history must be searchable by vessel name, IMO number, MMSI, voyage number, port of origin, and destination port. The replay must support variable playback speeds from 1x to 100x and allow users to export route data in GeoJSON and KML formats. Track data must be stored with a minimum position resolution of one point every 5 minutes and retained for at least 3 years. The replay interface must support Arabic RTL text direction for all labels and controls, with timestamps displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats where the user has enabled Hijri display in their locale preferences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R013	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	GPS Tracking API for External Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API and a WebSocket streaming API for external parties including shipping lines, customs brokers, and consignees to access real-time container location data. The REST API must support queries by container number, booking reference, bill of lading number, or geofence zone and must return responses in JSON format with coordinates in WGS84 datum. The WebSocket API must support subscription-based streaming of position updates for specified containers with configurable update frequency. All API endpoints must enforce OAuth 2.0 authentication with API key rate limiting of 1,000 requests per minute per client. API responses must include multi-currency freight cost-to-date fields (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) when the requesting party has billing visibility permissions. The API must comply with DCSA Track and Trace standards version 2.2 for interoperability with global shipping platforms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R014	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Battery and Device Health Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the health status of all GPS tracking devices including battery level percentage, last communication timestamp, firmware version, and internal temperature. When a device battery drops below 20 percent, the system must generate a low-battery warning to the asset management team with the container number, current location, estimated remaining battery life in hours, and the nearest depot where battery replacement can be performed. When battery drops below 5 percent, a critical alert must be raised. The system must maintain a device inventory registry tracking device serial number, assigned container, installation date, last maintenance date, and current entity assignment. A dashboard must display device health statistics aggregated by port, entity, and device manufacturer with Arabic and English toggle support and KPIs for device uptime percentage, average battery life, and communication failure rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R015	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Tracking Event Audit Trail and Compliance Logging	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail of all GPS tracking events, geofence crossings, alert triggers, configuration changes, and data access requests. Each audit record must include the event type, timestamp in UTC with Gregorian and Hijri representations, the acting user or system process identifier, the affected container number, the originating entity, and the source IP address. Audit logs must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with customs record-keeping requirements of Qatar Customs (General Authority of Customs), UAE Federal Authority for Identity Citizenship Customs and Port Security, KSA ZATCA, and Indian CBIC. The audit trail must be searchable by any field and exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers. Any attempt to modify or delete audit records must be blocked by the system and logged as a security incident for review by the compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-001-R016	FEAT-049-1-001	MOD-049	Container Movement Milestone Auto-Generation	The system must automatically generate standardized shipment milestones based on GPS tracking events and geofence crossings. Milestone types must include gate-out from origin, arrival at port of loading, loaded on vessel, vessel departure, vessel arrival at transshipment port, vessel arrival at discharge port, gate-out from discharge port, arrival at inland depot, and delivery to consignee. Each milestone must conform to UN/CEFACT and DCSA event naming conventions and must be published to the shipment visibility module within 2 minutes of the triggering GPS event. The milestone record must capture the container number in ISO 6346 format, the associated booking and bill of lading references, the port UN/LOCODE, and the timestamp in UTC plus local time. For multi-leg shipments involving transshipment at Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port, the system must correctly sequence milestones across vessel legs without duplicating or missing intermediate events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R001	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Sensor Data Ingestion Pipeline	The system must ingest temperature and humidity sensor data from IoT devices installed in refrigerated containers at a configurable interval ranging from 1 minute to 30 minutes. Each telemetry record must include the container number (ISO 6346), sensor device ID, internal cargo temperature in Celsius, external ambient temperature, relative humidity percentage, supply and return air temperatures, and the recording timestamp in UTC. The ingestion pipeline must support simultaneous data from at least 10,000 active reefer containers across all four port jurisdictions. Data must be validated on receipt to reject physically impossible values (e.g., temperature readings below minus 40 degrees Celsius or above plus 70 degrees Celsius, humidity outside 0 to 100 percent) and flag suspect readings for manual verification while continuing to process valid data from the same device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R002	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Temperature Set-Point Configuration per Booking	The system must allow operations staff to configure temperature and humidity set-points for each reefer container based on the booking instructions and commodity type. Set-point parameters must include target temperature, acceptable temperature range (minimum and maximum), target humidity, acceptable humidity range, ventilation setting (cubic meters per hour), and atmosphere composition for controlled atmosphere containers. The system must maintain a commodity-temperature reference database covering common perishable goods (fresh fruits, vegetables, frozen seafood, dairy, pharmaceuticals, chemicals) with recommended set-points per IATA Perishable Cargo Regulations and Carrier-specific reefer guidelines. Set-point configurations must be linked to the booking reference and bill of lading and must be immutable once the container is gated into the port terminal unless overridden by an authorized manager with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R003	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Real-Time Temperature Excursion Detection	The system must detect temperature excursions when the actual cargo temperature deviates from the configured acceptable range and must classify excursions by severity: minor (deviation of 1 to 3 degrees Celsius for less than 30 minutes), major (deviation of 3 to 5 degrees Celsius or minor deviation persisting beyond 30 minutes), and critical (deviation exceeding 5 degrees Celsius or any deviation persisting beyond 2 hours). Detection must occur within 2 minutes of receiving an out-of-range sensor reading. The system must apply configurable debounce logic requiring a minimum of 3 consecutive out-of-range readings before triggering an excursion event to prevent false alarms from transient sensor fluctuations during reefer defrost cycles. Each excursion event must record the start timestamp, peak deviation value, duration, container number, booking reference, commodity description, and the responsible entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R004	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Multi-Level Excursion Alert Escalation	The system must implement a configurable multi-level alert escalation workflow for temperature and humidity excursions. For minor excursions, an in-app notification must be sent to the reefer monitoring operator. For major excursions, SMS and email alerts must be sent to the reefer operations supervisor and the booking party contact. For critical excursions, the system must trigger voice call alerts via integrated telephony to the reefer operations manager, send push notifications to the shipper and consignee, and create an incident ticket in the operations workflow module. Escalation timing and recipients must be configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and per commodity category. Alert messages must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the language determined by the recipient's profile preference. All alert transmissions must be logged with delivery confirmation status for compliance audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R005	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Continuous Temperature Chart Recording	The system must generate a continuous temperature and humidity chart for each reefer container from the moment of sensor activation to deactivation, equivalent to a digital strip chart recorder. The chart must display time on the horizontal axis and temperature plus humidity on dual vertical axes with the configured set-point range shown as a shaded band. Excursion periods must be highlighted in red. The chart must support zoom levels from full journey overview down to 1-minute granularity. Users must be able to overlay multiple containers on a single chart for comparative analysis of containers in the same shipment. The chart must be exportable as a high-resolution PDF document suitable for submission to food safety authorities including Qatar MOPH, UAE MOCCAE, Saudi SFDA, and India FSSAI. The exported PDF must include the container number, voyage details, commodity description, set-point configuration, and a summary table of all excursion events with timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R006	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Cold Chain Compliance Certificate Generation	The system must automatically generate a Cold Chain Compliance Certificate upon container delivery confirming that temperature and humidity conditions were maintained within specified parameters throughout transit. The certificate must include the container number, booking and bill of lading references, origin and destination ports with UN/LOCODE, commodity description, configured set-points, actual minimum and maximum recorded temperatures and humidity, total excursion count and cumulative excursion duration, and a pass or fail compliance determination. The certificate must be rendered in both Arabic and English with the entity company logo, digital signature of the responsible operations manager, and a QR code linking to the full digital temperature record. Certificates must be generated in PDF/A format for long-term archival and must be automatically attached to the shipment document set in the document management module. Failed certificates must trigger a claims assessment workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R007	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Power Supply Status Monitoring	The system must monitor the electrical power supply status of each reefer container, detecting whether the unit is connected to shore power (at port terminal), generator power (during inland transit), or vessel power (during sea leg). Power disconnection events must be detected within 5 minutes and must generate immediate alerts to the reefer operations team with the container number, last known location, duration of disconnection, and the estimated time before internal temperature exits the acceptable range based on the commodity's thermal mass model. The system must track cumulative power-off duration per journey and flag containers exceeding configurable thresholds (default 2 hours cumulative) for inspection upon arrival. Power status data must be correlated with temperature readings to identify causal relationships between power interruptions and temperature excursions for claims investigation purposes across all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R008	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Pre-Trip Inspection Digital Checklist	The system must provide a digital pre-trip inspection (PTI) checklist for reefer containers that technicians complete via a mobile interface before the container is released for loading. The checklist must include verification of compressor operation, evaporator and condenser condition, door gasket integrity, drainage functionality, set-point programming accuracy, sensor calibration status, and firmware version of the IoT monitoring device. Each checklist item must support pass, fail, or not-applicable status with mandatory photo capture for failed items. The completed PTI record must be linked to the container number and booking reference and must include the technician name, certification number, inspection timestamp in Gregorian and Hijri formats, and the physical location (GPS coordinates and port name). A reefer container must not be assignable to a booking in the operations module unless a valid PTI record dated within the configurable validity period (default 30 days) exists in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R009	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Humidity Deviation and Condensation Risk Analysis	The system must monitor relative humidity inside reefer containers and detect deviations from the configured humidity set-point range. When humidity exceeds 95 percent or falls below the configured minimum for the commodity type, the system must flag a humidity excursion with the same severity classification and escalation logic as temperature excursions. Additionally, the system must calculate dew point based on current temperature and humidity readings and generate a condensation risk warning when the dew point approaches within 2 degrees Celsius of the cargo surface temperature. Condensation risk is particularly critical for pharmaceutical shipments transiting between the humid Gulf port environments (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali) and climate-controlled warehouse facilities. The system must recommend corrective actions such as adjusting ventilation settings or activating dehumidification based on the commodity type and current conditions, displayed in Arabic or English per user preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R010	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	AI-Based Predictive Temperature Failure Analysis	The system must employ machine learning models to predict potential reefer unit failures before they result in temperature excursions by analyzing patterns in sensor data including compressor cycling frequency, temperature oscillation amplitude, power consumption trends, and defrost cycle regularity. The predictive model must be trained on historical failure data and must generate early warning alerts at least 4 hours before an anticipated failure event with a minimum precision of 85 percent. Each prediction must include a confidence score, the predicted failure mode (compressor failure, refrigerant leak, thermostat malfunction, fan motor degradation), and recommended preventive action. Predictions must consider environmental factors such as extreme ambient temperatures common in Gulf summer months exceeding 50 degrees Celsius. The model must be retrained quarterly using updated failure data from all four entity operations and must support A/B testing of model versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R011	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Multi-Commodity Compartment Monitoring	The system must support monitoring of multi-compartment reefer containers and reefer containers carrying mixed commodities with different temperature requirements in partitioned zones. Each compartment or zone must have independently configurable temperature and humidity set-points, separate sensor assignments, and independent excursion detection logic. The monitoring dashboard must display a visual representation of the container cross-section showing each zone with its current readings and status. This capability is essential for shipments combining frozen and chilled goods in the same container, which is common for food distribution shipments from Jebel Ali to regional markets. The system must validate during booking assignment that the combined commodity temperature requirements are physically achievable by the reefer unit model and must reject incompatible combinations with a bilingual explanation message in Arabic and English referencing the reefer unit technical specifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R012	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Regulatory Temperature Logging Compliance	The system must comply with temperature logging requirements mandated by food safety and pharmaceutical regulatory authorities in all four operating jurisdictions. For Qatar, compliance with MOPH food import temperature requirements; for UAE, compliance with MOCCAE and Dubai Municipality cold chain regulations; for KSA, compliance with SFDA requirements for food and pharmaceutical cold chain; for India, compliance with FSSAI cold chain guidelines and CDSCO pharmaceutical storage requirements. The system must maintain unbroken temperature records at intervals not exceeding 15 minutes as required by most regulatory frameworks. Records must be tamper-evident with cryptographic hashing to ensure data integrity. The system must support automated submission of temperature logs to regulatory portals where electronic submission is available and must generate jurisdiction-specific formatted reports for manual submission where required.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R013	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Fleet Performance Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a reefer fleet performance analytics dashboard displaying key performance indicators including total active reefer count by entity and port, excursion rate as a percentage of monitored container-days, mean time between excursion events, average excursion duration, PTI pass rate, and reefer unit availability rate. The dashboard must support filtering by date range (with Hijri and Gregorian calendar selectors), entity, port, commodity category, shipping line, and reefer unit manufacturer. Trend charts must display monthly and weekly performance over configurable periods up to 24 months. The dashboard must include a cost impact analysis section showing estimated cargo damage costs from temperature excursions converted to the user's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) using daily exchange rates. The dashboard layout must be fully responsive and support Arabic RTL rendering with all chart labels, axis titles, and data tooltips properly mirrored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R014	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Monitoring Offline Mode and Data Recovery	The system must handle scenarios where reefer IoT devices lose connectivity during sea transit or in areas with poor network coverage. Devices must buffer temperature and humidity readings locally for a minimum of 30 days at 5-minute intervals. Upon reconnection, all buffered data must be uploaded and processed chronologically. The system must retroactively evaluate all buffered readings against the configured set-points and generate any excursion events that would have been triggered during the offline period, with timestamps reflecting the actual recording time. The cold chain compliance certificate must incorporate both real-time and backfilled data seamlessly without gaps. The operations dashboard must clearly indicate periods where data was received via backfill versus real-time with a visual timeline showing connectivity status. A data completeness score must be calculated for each container journey showing the percentage of expected readings that were successfully received.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R015	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Monitoring Integration with Vessel Systems	The system must integrate with vessel reefer monitoring systems to receive temperature data during the sea leg of container journeys. Integration must support standard maritime protocols including the vessel's integrated reefer monitoring system data export via API or SFTP file exchange. The system must correlate vessel-reported reefer data with the container's IoT device data and flag discrepancies exceeding 2 degrees Celsius between the two sources. During vessel transit between Gulf ports and JNPT, which typically takes 3 to 7 days, the vessel integration serves as the primary data source when container-mounted IoT devices cannot transmit directly. The system must map vessel bay-row-tier positions to container numbers and must handle vessel transshipment scenarios at Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port where a container's reefer data source switches from one vessel system to another without creating a gap in the temperature record.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R016	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Automated Reefer Set-Point Adjustment Recommendations	The system must use AI analysis of current environmental conditions, transit route weather forecasts, and commodity-specific thermal behavior models to recommend optimal reefer set-point adjustments during transit. For example, when ambient temperatures at Hamad Port exceed 45 degrees Celsius during summer months, the system may recommend lowering the set-point by 1 degree to compensate for increased thermal load on the reefer unit. Recommendations must include the rationale, expected energy consumption impact, and risk assessment for not implementing the adjustment. Recommendations must be presented to the reefer operations team for approval before any set-point change is executed and must never be applied automatically without human confirmation. All recommendations, approvals, and rejections must be logged in the audit trail with the operator ID, timestamp, and justification notes in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-002-R017	FEAT-049-1-002	MOD-049	Reefer Energy Consumption Tracking and Cost Allocation	The system must track electrical energy consumption of each monitored reefer container using power metering data from IoT devices or estimated consumption models based on reefer unit specifications, ambient temperature, and set-point differential. Energy consumption must be recorded in kilowatt-hours and allocated to the appropriate cost center based on the current journey leg: port terminal power charges for yard storage periods, vessel power charges during sea transit, and generator fuel costs during inland transport. The system must calculate energy costs using configurable tariff rates per entity and location, converting to the booking currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) at applicable exchange rates. Monthly energy consumption reports must be generated per entity with breakdowns by port, commodity type, and reefer unit manufacturer to support operational efficiency analysis and budgeting. Reports must support Arabic and English formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R001	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	E-Seal Device Registration and Assignment	The system must maintain a registry of all electronic seal devices with unique seal identifiers, manufacturer, model, firmware version, battery installation date, and current assignment status (available, assigned, active, deactivated, retired). When an e-seal is assigned to a container, the system must record the container number in ISO 6346 format, the associated booking reference, bill of lading number, seal application timestamp in Gregorian and Hijri formats, the GPS location of sealing, the identity of the person who applied the seal, and the supervising customs officer if the seal is applied under customs control. The system must validate that the e-seal has not been previously assigned to another active container and must prevent duplicate seal assignments. The seal assignment must be communicated to the destination customs authority electronically where supported (Qatar General Authority of Customs, UAE FAICCPS, KSA ZATCA, India CBIC ICEGATE) within 15 minutes of registration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R002	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Real-Time Seal Status Monitoring	The system must receive and process real-time status updates from electronic seals at a configurable interval ranging from 5 minutes to 60 minutes. Each status report must include the seal identifier, current seal state (locked, unlocked, tampered, removed, low-battery), GPS coordinates if the e-seal has integrated positioning, ambient light sensor reading indicating whether the container door area is exposed to light, accelerometer data for motion detection, and the timestamp of the reading. The monitoring engine must process status updates within 30 seconds of receipt and must correlate seal status with the container's current tracking position to detect spatial inconsistencies such as a seal reporting from a different location than the container GPS tracker. The system must support monitoring of at least 25,000 active e-seals concurrently across all four entity jurisdictions without performance degradation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R003	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Tamper Detection and Immediate Alert Generation	The system must detect seal tampering events including physical breach of the seal mechanism, abnormal force applied to the seal body, unauthorized unlock attempts, cutting or severing of the seal wire, and ambient light detection indicating door opening while the seal reports as locked. Upon detecting a tamper event, the system must generate an immediate critical security alert within 60 seconds containing the seal identifier, container number, tamper event type, GPS coordinates at the time of detection, the nearest known geofence zone, and the last known intact status timestamp. The alert must be sent simultaneously to the security operations center, the booking party, and the customs authority of the jurisdiction where the event occurred. For containers in transit between ports (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT), the alert must be sent to both the departure and arrival port customs authorities. The system must capture and store the raw sensor data surrounding the tamper event for forensic analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R004	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Customs Seal Verification Workflow	The system must support a customs seal verification workflow where authorized customs officers at arrival ports can electronically verify the integrity of an e-seal before granting cargo release. The verification process must compare the current seal status and unique cryptographic signature against the seal record created at the origin port. The system must display the complete seal history timeline showing all status transitions from sealing to current state, including any alerts or anomalies detected during transit. If the seal verification passes, the system must record the verification event with the customs officer identifier, timestamp, and port location. If verification fails due to seal status discrepancy, the system must automatically trigger a customs hold on the container, initiate a physical inspection workflow, and notify the importer, shipping line, and origin customs authority. The verification interface must support Arabic and English with quick-scan capability via barcode or NFC reader integration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R005	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Event Chain of Custody Tracking	The system must maintain an immutable chain of custody record for each electronic seal from manufacture or receipt into inventory through assignment, activation, transit monitoring, verification, removal, and retirement or disposal. Each custody transfer event must record the transferring party, receiving party, event type, timestamp in UTC with Gregorian and Hijri representations, GPS location, and the reason for the transfer. The chain of custody must be cryptographically secured using hash chaining so that any modification to a historical record is detectable. This chain of custody is critical for compliance with ISO 17712 high-security seal standards and the WCO SAFE Framework Authorized Economic Operator (AEO) requirements applicable in all four operating jurisdictions. The complete chain of custody must be retrievable as a printable bilingual report within 5 seconds for any seal by seal identifier or container number.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R006	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Multi-Seal Support per Container	The system must support the assignment and monitoring of multiple e-seals on a single container to accommodate scenarios where customs authorities at different jurisdictions require their own seal in addition to the carrier seal. For example, a container exported from Hamad Port may carry a Qatar Customs seal and a carrier e-seal, and upon arrival at JNPT, Indian Customs may affix an additional seal for bonded transit to an inland container depot. The system must track each seal independently with its own status history and chain of custody while associating all seals with the same container and booking. The monitoring dashboard must display all active seals for a container in a unified view with clear identification of the seal authority (carrier, Qatar Customs, UAE Customs, KSA Customs, India Customs). Alert thresholds and escalation workflows must be independently configurable per seal type to accommodate differing regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R007	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Authorized Seal Break Workflow	The system must support a controlled workflow for authorized seal breaking when legitimate access to container contents is required during transit, such as customs physical examination, quarantine inspection, or emergency retrieval of cargo. The workflow must require the requesting party to submit a seal break request specifying the reason, authorizing reference (customs examination order number, quarantine certificate reference), the planned break location, and estimated duration. The request must be approved by a designated authority before the seal can be broken. Upon seal break, the system must record the exact timestamp, GPS location, identity of the person who broke the seal, the supervising authority officer, and photographic evidence uploaded via mobile device. After the authorized access, a new seal must be assigned and the system must link the old and new seal records maintaining continuity of the security chain. The complete authorized break record must be available in Arabic and English for customs audit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R008	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	E-Seal Battery Life Management	The system must track battery life of all active electronic seals and predict remaining operational life based on the current reporting frequency, ambient temperature conditions, and cumulative transmission count. When estimated remaining battery life drops below 30 days, the system must generate a proactive replacement alert to the asset management team with the container number, current location, estimated battery depletion date, and the nearest facility where seal replacement can be performed without breaking the security chain. For containers on long sea voyages between Gulf ports and India where battery replacement is not possible, the system must automatically reduce the e-seal reporting frequency to extend battery life while maintaining minimum regulatory compliance intervals. Battery consumption analytics must be aggregated by seal manufacturer, model, and operating region to inform procurement decisions. Battery alerts must include cost implications in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) based on the replacement service rate schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R009	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Inventory Management and Procurement	The system must maintain an inventory of available e-seal devices at each location (port terminal, depot, warehouse) with minimum stock level thresholds that trigger automatic reorder notifications. The inventory must track seals by status (new, available, assigned, active, deactivated, damaged, retired), location, entity, and batch number. The system must support seal procurement workflows including purchase requisition generation when stock falls below minimum threshold, goods receipt upon delivery from the manufacturer, quality inspection recording, and batch activation. Inventory reports must show stock levels by location and entity with aging analysis for seals in storage beyond recommended shelf life. Inventory valuation must be maintained in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent for consolidated group reporting. The system must integrate with the procurement module for purchase order creation and with the finance module for seal cost allocation to specific shipments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R010	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Data Encryption and Security	The system must ensure all communications between e-seal devices and the central monitoring platform are encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent encryption standard. Each e-seal must authenticate with the platform using unique cryptographic certificates to prevent spoofing or replay attacks. The seal's unique identifier must be cryptographically signed at the factory and verified during registration to prevent counterfeit seal injection into the system. All tamper detection algorithms and threshold configurations must be protected from unauthorized modification with changes requiring multi-factor authentication and approval from the security operations manager. The system must implement intrusion detection to identify patterns suggestive of systematic seal compromise such as multiple tamper events on containers from the same origin or route. Security audit logs must be maintained separately from operational logs with restricted access limited to the information security team and must comply with data security requirements of ISO 28000 supply chain security management standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R011	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	E-Seal Integration with Gate Automation	The system must integrate with port terminal gate automation systems at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to automatically read and verify e-seal status during container gate-in and gate-out processes. When a container arrives at the terminal gate, the system must wirelessly read the e-seal status via RFID or Bluetooth and compare it against the expected seal record in the system. If the seal status matches and is intact, the gate transaction proceeds automatically. If there is a mismatch (wrong seal number, tamper alert, missing seal), the system must halt the gate transaction, alert the gate operator and security team, and require manual inspection before allowing the container to proceed. The gate verification must complete within 10 seconds to avoid causing truck queue delays. The system must support simultaneous multi-lane gate operations processing at least 6 lanes concurrently per terminal with Arabic and English display on the gate kiosk screens.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R012	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Event Correlation with Tracking Events	The system must correlate e-seal events with GPS tracking events and reefer monitoring events to provide a unified security timeline for each container. When a seal tamper event occurs, the system must automatically retrieve and display the container's GPS position, speed, heading, the nearest geofence zone, and any concurrent reefer temperature anomalies on a consolidated incident view. The correlation engine must detect suspicious patterns such as a seal tamper occurring while the container is stationary at an unauthorized location, or a seal break coinciding with a route deviation alert. The system must generate a composite security risk score for each container based on the weighted combination of seal integrity, route adherence, dwell time compliance, and environmental sensor normality. Containers with risk scores exceeding a configurable threshold must be flagged for priority customs inspection at the destination port. The risk scoring algorithm weights must be configurable per entity and trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R013	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Reporting for Customs and Regulatory Bodies	The system must generate standardized seal status reports for submission to customs authorities in all four operating jurisdictions. Reports must include the seal identifier, container number, sealing date and location, current seal status, complete transit history with intermediate verification points, and the final verification result at destination. The report format must comply with the electronic customs declaration requirements of Qatar General Authority of Customs, UAE FAICCPS MIRSAL 2 system, KSA ZATCA FASAH platform, and India CBIC ICEGATE system. Where electronic submission is supported, the system must transmit reports automatically via the respective customs integration API. Where manual submission is required, the system must generate PDF reports in the authority-prescribed format with content in Arabic and English as required by each jurisdiction. Reports must include digital signatures for authenticity verification and must reference the applicable ISO 17712 seal classification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R006	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Vessel Fleet Grouping and Watchlists	The system must allow users to create and manage vessel watchlists grouped by criteria such as shipping line, trade lane, vessel size category (feeder, Panamax, post-Panamax, ULCV), or custom business logic (e.g., vessels carrying specific commodity types or operating under specific INCOTERMS like CIF or FOB). Watchlists must support a maximum of 10,000 vessels per list and allow real-time filtering by flag state, vessel age, draught range, and current speed. Each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) must be able to maintain independent watchlists while a super-admin role can view consolidated cross-entity watchlists. Notifications must be configurable per watchlist to alert users when a watched vessel enters a geofence, deviates from expected route, or experiences AIS signal loss exceeding a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R014	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Analytics and Security Intelligence Dashboard	The system must provide a security intelligence dashboard displaying e-seal analytics including total active seals by entity and port, tamper event frequency by location and route, authorized versus unauthorized break statistics, seal verification pass and fail rates at each port gate, average seal battery life by manufacturer, and seal inventory turnover rates. The dashboard must include a geographic heat map showing tamper event density across the operational network highlighting hotspots that may indicate systematic security threats. Trend analysis must compare current period performance against historical baselines with anomaly detection flagging statistically significant deviations. All dashboard visualizations must support Arabic RTL rendering with bidirectional text support for mixed Arabic-English content such as container numbers and port codes. Dashboard data must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with all monetary values converted to the user's preferred currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R015	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	E-Seal Offline Operation and Reconnection Protocol	The system must handle scenarios where e-seals lose network connectivity during sea transit or passage through communication dead zones. E-seal devices must continue monitoring and recording seal status locally with timestamped entries for a minimum of 45 days. Upon network reconnection, all buffered seal status records must be transmitted to the central platform in chronological order. The system must process backfilled records and retroactively generate any tamper alerts that would have been triggered during the offline period, clearly marking them as retrospective alerts with both the event timestamp and the detection timestamp. If a tamper event is detected in backfilled data for a container that has already been delivered, the system must immediately alert the security team and the customs authority that cleared the container, as this may indicate a security breach that went undetected during transit. The offline operation protocol must be documented in compliance with AEO program requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-003-R016	FEAT-049-1-003	MOD-049	Seal Lifecycle Cost Analysis and Reporting	The system must track and report the total cost of ownership for e-seal operations including device procurement costs, battery replacement costs, activation and deactivation labor costs, IT infrastructure costs for monitoring, and insurance premiums related to seal-based cargo security. Costs must be allocated to individual shipments based on the seal assignment duration and must be included in the shipment profitability analysis. The system must calculate cost-per-seal-day metrics by entity and trade lane and must compare actual costs against budgeted amounts with variance reporting. Financial reports must be generated in the entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with USD consolidation for group-level analysis. The system must also quantify the financial benefit of e-seal usage by tracking the reduction in cargo theft claims, insurance premium savings, and customs processing time improvements attributable to the e-seal program, presenting ROI analysis in bilingual Arabic and English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R001	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Shock Impact Sensor Data Ingestion	The system must ingest accelerometer data from IoT shock sensors mounted on containers at a high-frequency sampling rate of at least 100 Hz during active events and a low-frequency monitoring rate of 1 Hz during dormant periods. Each shock event record must include the peak acceleration in g-force across three axes (X, Y, Z), the resultant vector magnitude, the duration of the impact in milliseconds, the timestamp in UTC, and the GPS coordinates at the time of the event. The ingestion pipeline must support simultaneous data from at least 30,000 active shock sensors across all four port jurisdictions. The system must distinguish between different shock signatures including single impact events (e.g., container dropped during crane operation), repeated impacts (e.g., rough road surface during trucking), and sustained vibration (e.g., vessel engine harmonics during sea transit). Raw waveform data must be stored for events exceeding configurable g-force thresholds for detailed forensic analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R002	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Configurable Shock Threshold Management	The system must allow configuration of shock detection thresholds based on commodity sensitivity classifications. The system must support a minimum of five sensitivity levels: ultra-sensitive (electronics, precision instruments: threshold 2g), high-sensitivity (glassware, ceramics: threshold 3g), medium-sensitivity (machinery, automotive parts: threshold 5g), low-sensitivity (bulk commodities, raw materials: threshold 8g), and robust (scrap metal, minerals: threshold 15g). Thresholds must be configurable per axis (vertical, lateral, longitudinal) to account for directional sensitivity of cargo packaging. The commodity sensitivity level must be automatically assigned based on the HS code of the goods declared in the booking, with manual override capability for operations staff. Threshold configurations must be entity-specific, as handling standards may vary between ports—for instance, Jebel Ali may have stricter thresholds for electronics transshipment than other ports. All threshold changes must be logged with the modifier's identity and timestamp for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R003	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Real-Time Shock Event Alert Generation	The system must generate real-time alerts when a shock event exceeds the configured threshold for the container's commodity sensitivity level. Alerts must be classified by severity: warning (threshold exceeded by 1 to 50 percent), major (threshold exceeded by 50 to 100 percent), and critical (threshold exceeded by more than 100 percent or multiple events within a 5-minute window). Each alert must include the container number, booking reference, commodity description, the measured g-force values, the threshold that was exceeded, GPS coordinates at the time of impact, the operational context derived from geofence zone (port yard, crane operation, truck transit, vessel, rail), and the timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. For critical shock events, the system must automatically create a damage inspection work order in the operations module and place a hold on the container preventing further handling until inspection is completed. Alert notifications must be bilingual in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R007	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Berth Window Coordination from AIS Data	The system must automatically correlate AIS-derived vessel positions and ETA predictions with the berth scheduling module to recommend optimal berth window assignments. When a vessel's AIS-predicted ETA deviates by more than a configurable threshold (default 4 hours) from its scheduled berth window, the system must generate an automated re-scheduling suggestion and notify the port operations team via in-app alert and optional SMS/email. The coordination logic must account for vessel LOA, beam, draught constraints at each berth, tidal windows at JNPT, and priority rules for vessels on confirmed booking versus those on waitlist. Multi-currency billing implications of berth window changes must reference applicable tariff rates in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR depending on the port entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R004	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Tilt Angle Monitoring and Alerting	The system must continuously monitor the tilt angle of containers using gyroscope sensor data and detect when a container is tilted beyond safe limits. Configurable tilt thresholds must be supported with defaults of 5 degrees for loaded containers with liquid cargo, 10 degrees for standard loaded containers, and 15 degrees for empty containers. The system must detect both sustained tilts (lasting more than 30 seconds) indicating improper stacking or uneven ground, and sudden tilts indicating potential toppling during crane operations. Tilt events must record the angle in degrees across pitch and roll axes, duration of tilt, the maximum angle reached, GPS coordinates, and the operational phase. For containers carrying dangerous goods (IMO classes 3, 5, 6, 8 liquids), tilt monitoring thresholds must be automatically reduced to 3 degrees, and alerts must be escalated to the port dangerous goods officer and the vessel operator. The system must provide a visual tilt indicator on the container tracking dashboard showing current orientation relative to level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R005	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Vibration Pattern Analysis and Classification	The system must analyze continuous vibration data from container-mounted sensors using frequency domain analysis (Fast Fourier Transform) to classify vibration patterns into categories including road surface vibration (5 to 25 Hz typical range), vessel engine harmonics (10 to 60 Hz), crane operation vibration (2 to 15 Hz), and rail transit vibration (5 to 40 Hz). The system must detect abnormal vibration patterns that deviate from expected profiles for the current transport mode, such as unusually high vibration during vessel transit that may indicate improper container securing or vessel mechanical issues. Vibration severity must be assessed using industry-standard metrics including root mean square (RMS) acceleration, peak velocity, and crest factor. The system must maintain vibration exposure history per container journey and calculate cumulative vibration dose value to assess the total handling quality, particularly important for fragile cargo transiting multiple legs through Gulf ports to the Indian subcontinent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R006	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Handling Quality Score Calculation	The system must calculate a composite handling quality score for each container journey on a scale of 0 to 100 based on the weighted combination of shock event count and severity, tilt event count and severity, vibration exposure metrics, and the commodity sensitivity level. The scoring algorithm must assign higher weight penalties to events occurring during port handling operations (crane loading, yard tractor transport) compared to transit vibrations, as port handling events are more likely to be controllable. The handling quality score must be calculated in real time and updated after each significant event. Scores below 70 must trigger a review flag, and scores below 50 must trigger a mandatory damage inspection. The scoring weights must be configurable per entity, and the system must provide AI-generated recommendations for improving handling quality based on pattern analysis of low-scoring containers. Historical handling quality scores must be available for trend analysis by port, carrier, route, and handling equipment type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R007	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Crane Operation Impact Monitoring	The system must specifically monitor and record shock and vibration events during container crane operations including ship-to-shore gantry crane loading and discharge, rubber-tired gantry (RTG) crane yard moves, and reach stacker operations. The system must integrate with terminal operating system crane event data to correlate sensor readings with specific crane movements and operator identifiers. When a shock event exceeds the commodity-specific threshold during a crane operation, the system must record the crane identifier, the operator ID, the specific move type (load, discharge, restack, or truck transfer), and the height from which the impact occurred if determinable from sensor data. This data must be aggregated to produce crane operator handling performance reports by terminal, supporting training interventions for operators with above-average impact rates. Reports must be available in Arabic and English and must be segregated by entity to respect operator privacy regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R008	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Transport Leg Shock Comparison Analytics	The system must provide comparative analytics of shock and vibration events across different transport legs of a container's journey, enabling identification of which segments contribute most to cargo damage risk. The analysis must break down the journey into distinct legs: origin pickup, trucking to port of loading, port yard storage, crane loading, sea transit, transshipment handling if applicable, crane discharge, port yard storage at destination, trucking to delivery point, and final delivery. Each leg must show the count and severity of shock events, cumulative vibration dose, maximum tilt angle recorded, and the handling quality score for that segment. The system must support cross-journey aggregation to identify systemic handling quality issues at specific ports, on specific routes, or with specific transport providers. Comparative reports must display monetary estimates of potential cargo damage risk in the booking currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) based on commodity value and damage probability models.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R009	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Insurance Claims Evidence Package Generation	The system must generate comprehensive evidence packages for cargo damage insurance claims based on recorded shock, tilt, and vibration data. The evidence package must include a complete timeline of all handling events exceeding 50 percent of the commodity-specific threshold, raw sensor waveform plots for significant events, GPS location and operational context for each event, the handling quality score breakdown by journey leg, photographic evidence if available from gate camera integrations, and the environmental conditions (temperature, humidity) from reefer monitoring if the container is refrigerated. The package must be formatted as a professional PDF report with bilingual Arabic and English content, entity company branding, and a digital signature for authenticity. The evidence package must be exportable in a format compatible with common insurance claim platforms and must include a machine-readable JSON data appendix. The system must calculate the estimated liability attribution percentage for each handling party based on the severity and timing of recorded events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R008	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	AIS Signal Loss Detection and Alerting	The system must continuously monitor AIS signal continuity for all tracked vessels and raise an alert when a vessel's AIS signal is lost for a duration exceeding a configurable threshold (default 60 minutes for open sea, 15 minutes within port geofence zones). Alerts must classify signal loss severity as informational, warning, or critical based on vessel proximity to managed ports, whether the vessel is carrying hazardous cargo (IMO classes 1-9), and whether the vessel is on an active watchlist. The alert must include the vessel's last known position, heading, speed, destination, and cargo summary. Signal loss events must be logged with timestamps in UTC and local port time, and the system must provide monthly signal reliability reports per vessel and per trade lane to support fleet performance analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R010	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Shock Sensor Device Health Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the operational health of shock, tilt, and vibration sensor devices including battery level, sensor calibration status, firmware version, internal device temperature, and last successful communication timestamp. The system must detect sensor malfunctions including stuck readings (constant value over extended period), noise floor degradation (increasing baseline vibration readings), and axis drift (systematic offset from zero in stationary conditions). When a sensor malfunction is detected, the system must generate a maintenance alert with the device serial number, container number, malfunction type, current location, and recommended corrective action. The system must maintain a sensor calibration schedule with reminders triggered 30 days before calibration expiry. A sensor health dashboard must display fleet-wide metrics aggregated by entity, port, and device manufacturer with Arabic and English toggle support, showing device uptime percentage, average battery life, mean time between failures, and calibration compliance rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R011	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	AI-Based Damage Probability Prediction	The system must employ machine learning models trained on historical shock, tilt, and vibration data correlated with actual cargo damage records to predict the probability of cargo damage for each active container in transit. The prediction model must consider the commodity type and packaging quality, cumulative shock exposure to date, vibration dose value, maximum tilt angle experienced, remaining journey distance and expected handling events, and environmental conditions. The predicted damage probability must be expressed as a percentage and updated in real time as new sensor data arrives. When the predicted damage probability exceeds a configurable threshold (default 30 percent), the system must alert the operations team with recommended preventive actions such as rerouting to avoid known rough road segments, requesting careful handling instructions to the destination terminal, or arranging pre-delivery inspection. The model must be retrained monthly using damage claim outcomes from all four entity operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R012	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Vibration-Sensitive Cargo Special Handling Protocols	The system must enforce special handling protocols for containers carrying vibration-sensitive cargo as identified by commodity HS codes and booking special handling instructions. For containers flagged as vibration-sensitive, the system must automatically apply stricter monitoring thresholds, increase sensor sampling frequency to maximum, and configure priority alerting to the operations supervisor. The system must validate that vibration-sensitive containers are assigned to stowage positions on vessels that minimize engine vibration exposure (typically away from the engine room and propeller shaft), integrated with the vessel stowage planning module. For inland transport, the system must recommend preferred routes with better road conditions based on historical vibration data from previous transits. The special handling flags must be visible on all tracking screens and documents in both Arabic and English with clear visual indicators. Non-compliance with special handling protocols must be logged and escalated to the entity quality manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R013	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Drop Detection and Height Estimation	The system must specifically detect container drop events using accelerometer free-fall signature analysis, identifying the characteristic zero-g period followed by a high-g impact. The system must estimate the drop height using the duration of the free-fall period and gravitational acceleration calculations, with an accuracy of plus or minus 0.5 meters for drops between 0.3 and 5 meters. Each drop event must record the estimated drop height, impact g-force, GPS location, timestamp, and the operational context (crane operation, forklift handling, chassis loading). Drop events must always be classified as critical severity regardless of the commodity sensitivity threshold and must immediately trigger a damage inspection work order, a notification to the shipper and consignee, and an incident report in the safety management module. For containers carrying dangerous goods per IMDG Code, drop events must additionally trigger emergency response notifications to the port dangerous goods officer and the local civil defense authority in the respective jurisdiction (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R014	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Multi-Entity Handling Performance Benchmarking	The system must provide cross-entity benchmarking of container handling quality performance across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Benchmarking metrics must include average handling quality score per port, shock event rate per 1,000 container moves, average peak g-force during crane operations, tilt violation rate, and cargo damage claim rate correlated with handling quality scores. The benchmarking dashboard must present performance rankings with anonymized peer comparison when viewed at entity level and detailed breakdowns when viewed at group level by authorized users. The system must calculate a performance improvement trend for each port showing month-over-month handling quality changes. Benchmark reports must be generated in the entity's functional currency for cost impact analysis (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD consolidation for group-level comparison. All benchmarking visualizations must support Arabic RTL rendering and Hijri calendar date selection alongside Gregorian dates. The system must generate automated monthly executive summary reports for distribution to entity managing directors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R015	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Shock and Vibration Data Offline Buffering	The system must handle scenarios where shock and vibration IoT sensors lose network connectivity by buffering all event data locally on the sensor device for a minimum of 60 days. The local buffer must store both event-triggered records (shock, tilt, drop events with full waveform data) and periodic status reports. Upon network reconnection, buffered data must be transmitted in chronological order with priority given to critical severity events. The system must process backfilled data and retroactively generate all alerts and update the handling quality score for the entire offline period. Critical events such as drops or severe shocks detected in backfilled data must generate retrospective alerts clearly marked with both the event timestamp and the processing timestamp. If a container has already been delivered when critical backfilled events are processed, the system must still generate alerts and flag the delivery record for post-delivery damage assessment. Data completeness metrics must be tracked per container journey and displayed on the tracking dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R001	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Dynamic TEU Revenue Baseline Calculation	The system must calculate a dynamic revenue baseline per TEU for each trade lane by analyzing historical booking data from the previous 24 months, factoring in seasonal fluctuations, port-pair combinations (e.g., QAHMD-INNSA, AEJEA-QAHMD), container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC), and cargo commodity classifications per HS code chapters. The baseline must be recalculated weekly and stored in multi-currency format (USD as base, with auto-converted values in QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using ECB or central bank reference rates. Historical baselines must be retained for audit trail purposes for a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R016	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Regulatory Compliance for Dangerous Goods Handling Monitoring	The system must enforce enhanced shock, tilt, and vibration monitoring requirements for containers carrying dangerous goods as classified under the IMDG Code (IMO classes 1 through 9). For Class 1 explosives, the system must apply shock thresholds not exceeding 2g and tilt thresholds not exceeding 3 degrees. For Class 3 flammable liquids, tilt monitoring must be active with a threshold of 5 degrees to prevent spillage. For Class 7 radioactive materials, the system must record and report all handling events exceeding 1.5g to the national radiation protection authority (Qatar MOEH, UAE FANR, KSA NRC, India AERB). The system must generate UN Dangerous Goods handling compliance reports per shipment showing all monitoring data and demonstrating adherence to IMDG Code stowage and handling requirements. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English and must be submittable electronically to the respective port state authority. Non-compliance events must trigger immediate operational holds and safety incident reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R017	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Real-Time Handling Event Notification to Stakeholders	The system must provide configurable real-time notifications of shock, tilt, and vibration events to all relevant stakeholders in the supply chain including the shipper, consignee, freight forwarder, customs broker, insurance provider, and carrier. Each stakeholder must be able to configure their notification preferences including the minimum severity level they wish to be notified about, preferred notification channels (email, SMS, push notification, webhook), preferred language (Arabic or English), and preferred timezone for timestamp display including Hijri calendar option. The system must support webhook integration for stakeholders who wish to receive event data programmatically in their own systems. Notification templates must include the container number, event summary, current location on a map thumbnail, recommended actions, and a link to the full event detail in the tracking portal. The system must respect data visibility permissions so that stakeholders only receive notifications for containers they are authorized to view based on their role in the booking. Notification delivery status must be tracked and undeliverable notifications must be retried with escalation to alternative channels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-1-004-R018	FEAT-049-1-004	MOD-049	Historical Shock Data Retention and Archival	The system must retain detailed shock, tilt, and vibration event data for a minimum of 7 years in compliance with maritime cargo claims limitation periods under the Hague-Visby Rules and Hamburg Rules applicable to international container shipping. High-frequency raw waveform data for significant events must be retained for 3 years in hot storage with sub-second query response time and subsequently archived to cold storage for the remaining 4 years with retrieval within 4 hours. Summary event data and handling quality scores must remain in hot storage for the full 7-year period. Archived data must be retrievable by container number, booking reference, bill of lading number, date range, event severity, port, and entity. The archival system must maintain data integrity through cryptographic checksums and must comply with electronic evidence admissibility requirements in the courts of Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Storage costs must be tracked per entity in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and allocated proportionally based on data volume for intercompany billing purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R001	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	AIS Data Feed Integration	The system must integrate with at least two independent AIS data providers (terrestrial and satellite-based) to ensure continuous vessel tracking coverage across the Arabian Gulf, Strait of Hormuz, Gulf of Oman, and Indian Ocean shipping corridors. The integration must support ITU-R M.1371 AIS message types 1, 2, 3, 5, 18, and 24 for Class A and Class B transponders. Data ingestion must handle a minimum of 50,000 position reports per minute with deduplication logic that reconciles MMSI numbers across providers. Failover between primary and secondary AIS feeds must occur automatically within 30 seconds of detecting data gaps, with an alert raised to the operations dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R002	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Real-Time Vessel Map Visualization	The system must render an interactive map displaying all tracked vessels with position updates at intervals no greater than 60 seconds for vessels within 200 nautical miles of any managed port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and 300 seconds for vessels beyond that radius. The map must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual labels (Arabic/English) for port names, anchorage zones, and navigational features. Vessel icons must indicate vessel type (container ship, tanker, bulk carrier), heading, speed over ground, and draught. Users must be able to toggle between satellite imagery, nautical chart overlay, and simplified schematic views. The map must load within 3 seconds on standard broadband connections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R003	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Geofence Zone Configuration	The system must allow authorized users to define custom geofence polygons around port approaches, anchorage areas, berth zones, pilot boarding stations, and traffic separation schemes for each managed port. Each geofence must support configurable entry and exit triggers that can initiate automated workflows such as pre-arrival notifications, customs document requests, or berth allocation processes. Geofences must support a minimum of 500 distinct zones across all entities with individual naming in both Arabic and English. The system must calculate vessel-to-geofence proximity using great-circle distance and trigger events within 10 seconds of a vessel crossing a boundary. Historical geofence crossing data must be retained for a minimum of 5 years per regulatory requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R004	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	AI-Powered ETA Prediction	The system must utilize machine learning models trained on historical AIS data, weather patterns, current sea conditions, and port congestion metrics to predict vessel estimated time of arrival at each managed port. ETA predictions must be generated for vessels within a configurable radius (default 500 nautical miles) and updated every 15 minutes. The AI model must achieve an accuracy within plus or minus 2 hours for vessels within 100 nautical miles and plus or minus 6 hours for vessels beyond 200 nautical miles. Predictions must account for known port closures (including Hijri calendar holidays such as Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha), tidal windows at JNPT, and traffic density at Jebel Ali. ETA confidence scores must be displayed alongside each prediction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R002	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	AI Slot Revenue Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI optimization engine that evaluates each vessel voyage and recommends an optimal cargo mix to maximize total revenue per TEU slot. The engine must consider weight utilization versus volume utilization ratios, hazardous cargo surcharges (IMO classes 1-9), reefer premium differentials, and out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo revenue uplift. The engine must run optimization simulations a minimum of 72 hours before estimated time of departure (ETD) and update recommendations every 6 hours as bookings change. Recommendations must be presented in both English and Arabic UI with RTL layout support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R009	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Weather and Sea Condition Overlay	The system must overlay real-time and forecast weather data on the vessel tracking map, including wind speed and direction, wave height, visibility, and current patterns sourced from recognized maritime weather services. Weather layers must be toggleable independently and support temporal animation showing forecast progression for up to 7 days. The system must automatically cross-reference weather conditions along a vessel's projected route and flag potential delays due to adverse conditions such as shamal winds in the Arabian Gulf, monsoon patterns affecting the Indian Ocean approach to JNPT, or fog conditions at Khalifa Port. Weather-induced ETA adjustments must be reflected in the AI prediction model and communicated to downstream logistics planning modules. All weather data labels must support Arabic and English bilingual display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R010	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Port Congestion Heatmap	The system must generate real-time port congestion heatmaps for each managed port by analyzing AIS data from vessels at anchor, at berth, and in approach channels. The heatmap must visualize vessel density using a color gradient and update at intervals no greater than 15 minutes. Historical congestion data must be available for trend analysis with daily, weekly, and monthly aggregation views. The system must calculate average anchorage waiting times and berth turnaround times from AIS-derived arrival and departure timestamps. Congestion metrics must be comparable across ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) in a consolidated dashboard view. AI-driven congestion predictions for the next 72 hours must be provided based on scheduled arrivals, historical patterns, and current vessel positions. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with Arabic and English bilingual headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R011	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Vessel Deviation and Anomaly Detection	The system must employ AI algorithms to detect vessel route deviations where a tracked vessel diverges more than a configurable distance (default 20 nautical miles) from its declared or historically typical route between origin and destination ports. Anomaly detection must also flag unusual speed changes (sudden deceleration or acceleration exceeding 30 percent of average speed), unexpected port calls not listed on the voyage plan, and prolonged loitering in non-anchorage zones. Each detected anomaly must be scored for severity and potential impact on scheduled port operations. The system must log all anomalies with full context including vessel identity, cargo manifest summary, and geospatial coordinates. Anomaly alerts must be routed to the relevant entity's security and operations teams based on the vessel's destination port jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R012	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Multi-Entity Access Control for Vessel Data	The system must enforce strict multi-entity data segregation ensuring that users associated with the Qatar entity can only view detailed vessel tracking data relevant to Hamad Port operations, UAE entity users see Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali data, KSA entity users see data relevant to their designated ports, and India entity users see JNPT data. A cross-entity super-admin role must exist to view consolidated data across all entities for group-level analytics. Data sharing agreements between entities must be configurable so that specific vessel tracking information can be shared on a per-voyage or per-trade-lane basis. All access control changes must be audit-logged with user identity, timestamp, and the specific data scope granted or revoked. Role-based permissions must integrate with the central identity management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R013	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Vessel Details and Particulars Database	The system must maintain a comprehensive vessel database synchronized with external maritime registries containing vessel particulars including IMO number, MMSI, call sign, flag state, vessel type, gross tonnage, net tonnage, deadweight tonnage, length overall (LOA), beam, maximum draught, year built, classification society, and P&I club. Vessel records must be automatically enriched from AIS static data messages (Type 5 and Type 24). Users must be able to manually supplement vessel records with operational notes, performance ratings, and compliance flags. The database must support searching and filtering in both Arabic and English with transliterated vessel names where applicable. Vessel records must link to related modules including booking, billing (with tariffs in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD), and customs documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R014	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Offline AIS Data Caching for Connectivity Loss	The system must implement a local caching mechanism that stores the most recent 4 hours of AIS position data on the client side to ensure continued vessel map display during temporary network or internet connectivity disruptions. When connectivity is lost, the map must display cached vessel positions with a clear visual indicator showing data staleness (time since last update) and an Arabic/English bilingual warning banner. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must automatically synchronize with the server, backfill any missing position data, and recalculate ETAs. The offline cache must prioritize vessels currently within managed port geofences and vessels on active user watchlists. Cache storage must not exceed 100 MB per client device and must be encrypted at rest to protect commercially sensitive vessel movement data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R015	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	AIS Data API for External System Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API and WebSocket streaming endpoint that allows authorized external systems (terminal operating systems, customs platforms, freight forwarders) to consume real-time and historical AIS vessel position data. The API must support filtering by geographic bounding box, vessel IMO or MMSI, port code (UN/LOCODE format such as QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNHA), and time range. Authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with API key rate limiting configurable per consumer. Response formats must include JSON and GeoJSON. The API must support pagination for historical queries returning more than 10,000 records. API usage must be metered and billable per entity with charges denominated in the entity's base currency. Comprehensive API documentation must be provided in both Arabic and English with interactive Swagger/OpenAPI specification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-001-R016	FEAT-049-2-001	MOD-049	Vessel Emissions Estimation from AIS Data	The system must calculate estimated CO2, SOx, and NOx emissions for tracked vessels based on AIS-derived speed profiles, vessel type, engine specifications from the vessel database, and applicable emission factors from the IMO Fourth GHG Study. Emissions must be calculated per voyage leg and aggregated per port call, per trade lane, and per reporting period. The system must support EU MRV and IMO DCS reporting formats and flag vessels operating in emission control areas (ECAs). Emission estimates must be presented in metric tonnes and displayed alongside vessel tracking data on the map interface. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date headers. Multi-entity consolidation must allow group-level sustainability reporting across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R001	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	IoT Device Registration and Management	The system must provide a centralized registry for all IoT-enabled port equipment assets including ship-to-shore (STS) cranes, rubber-tired gantry (RTG) cranes, rail-mounted gantry (RMG) cranes, reach stackers, empty container handlers, terminal tractors, and reefer monitoring units. Each device must be registered with a unique asset ID, equipment type, manufacturer, model, serial number, installation date, port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT), and assigned entity. The registry must support bulk import via CSV and API-based auto-registration for new devices. Device metadata must be maintainable in both Arabic and English. The system must support a minimum of 50,000 concurrent IoT device connections across all entities with device status indicators (online, offline, maintenance, decommissioned).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R002	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Real-Time Telemetry Data Ingestion	The system must ingest real-time telemetry data from IoT sensors via MQTT (version 5.0) and AMQP protocols with support for CoAP for constrained devices. Telemetry parameters must include engine RPM, hydraulic pressure, fuel consumption rate, electrical load, temperature, vibration amplitude, GPS coordinates, and operational cycle counts. The ingestion pipeline must handle a sustained throughput of 100,000 messages per second across all ports with message latency not exceeding 2 seconds from sensor to dashboard display. Each telemetry message must be timestamped in UTC with millisecond precision and tagged with device ID, port code, and entity identifier. Data validation rules must reject malformed messages and log them for diagnostic review without blocking the ingestion pipeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R003	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Equipment Health Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time equipment health dashboard displaying the operational status of all registered IoT-enabled equipment per port and per entity. The dashboard must use a traffic-light system (green for normal, amber for warning, red for critical) based on configurable threshold rules for each telemetry parameter. Users must be able to drill down from a port-level overview to individual equipment detail views showing live telemetry feeds, historical trend charts (1 hour, 24 hours, 7 days, 30 days), and active alerts. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with bilingual labels and must render within 5 seconds even when displaying data for more than 1,000 concurrent devices. Dashboard layouts must be customizable per user role with saved view preferences persisting across sessions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R004	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	STS Crane Performance Monitoring	The system must provide specialized monitoring for ship-to-shore cranes tracking key performance indicators including moves per hour (MPH), crane cycle time, hoist and trolley speeds, spreader twist-lock engagement status, anti-sway system activation frequency, and boom position. The monitoring must correlate crane performance with vessel operations to calculate gross and net crane productivity rates per vessel call. Alerts must be generated when crane MPH falls below a configurable threshold (default 25 MPH) or when cycle times exceed normal operating ranges by more than 20 percent. Crane downtime events must be automatically classified by cause code (mechanical failure, electrical fault, operator error, weather stoppage) and logged with duration in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar timestamps. Performance data must feed into the berth productivity module for vessel turnaround optimization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R005	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	RTG and Yard Equipment Utilization Tracking	The system must track utilization rates for rubber-tired gantry cranes, reach stackers, and empty container handlers operating in the container yard. Utilization must be calculated as the ratio of productive operating time to total available time, excluding scheduled maintenance windows. The system must record movement patterns, travel distances, idle times, and container handling cycles for each piece of equipment using GPS and accelerometer IoT data. Heatmaps must visualize equipment movement density across yard blocks to identify congestion points and inefficient routing patterns. Utilization reports must be generated per shift, per day, and per month, broken down by equipment type and yard zone. Reports must display costs in the local entity currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Khalifa and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) for fuel consumption and maintenance expense allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R006	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Reefer Container Monitoring Integration	The system must monitor refrigerated container power and temperature status through IoT sensors on reefer plugs and container-mounted data loggers. Monitored parameters must include supply temperature, return temperature, humidity, O2/CO2 levels for controlled atmosphere containers, power supply voltage and current, and defrost cycle status. Alerts must be generated immediately (within 60 seconds) when temperature deviates beyond the acceptable range for the commodity being carried (e.g., plus or minus 2 degrees Celsius for pharmaceuticals, plus or minus 5 degrees for frozen food). The system must maintain a continuous temperature log chain compliant with USDA, EU food safety, and GCC SFDA regulations. Reefer monitoring data must be accessible to the booking party via a customer portal with bilingual Arabic/English interface and exportable for customs and insurance claim documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R007	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Fuel and Energy Consumption Monitoring	The system must track real-time fuel consumption for diesel-powered equipment (RTGs, reach stackers, terminal tractors) and electrical energy consumption for electrified equipment (e-RTGs, STS cranes, RMG cranes, reefer yards). Fuel data must be captured from flow meter IoT sensors with accuracy within 2 percent. The system must calculate fuel efficiency metrics including liters per container move, kilowatt-hours per TEU handled, and total energy cost per vessel call. Cost calculations must use configurable fuel prices denominated in the local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated group reporting using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. Anomalous fuel consumption patterns exceeding 15 percent above the rolling 30-day average must trigger automated investigation alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R008	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Equipment Geolocation and Digital Twin Mapping	The system must display real-time GPS positions of all mobile port equipment on a digital twin map overlay of each terminal layout. The digital twin must include berth positions, yard block boundaries, gate lanes, rail sidings, and building structures rendered to scale. Equipment positions must update at intervals no greater than 10 seconds for actively operating equipment. The map must support zoom levels from full-terminal overview to individual yard row detail. Users must be able to click on any equipment icon to view live telemetry, current assignment, operator identification, and maintenance status. The digital twin must be configurable per terminal to reflect the actual physical layout of Hamad Port, Khalifa Port Container Terminal, Jebel Ali Terminal 1-4, and JNPT BMCT and NSIGT terminals. Arabic and English labels must be supported with dynamic switching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R009	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Automated Fault Detection and Classification	The system must employ rule-based and machine learning algorithms to detect equipment faults from telemetry data streams in real time. Fault detection must cover mechanical faults (abnormal vibration, hydraulic pressure drops, structural stress), electrical faults (insulation resistance degradation, motor current imbalance, power factor deviation), and operational faults (collision proximity events, overload conditions, safety system activations). Each detected fault must be automatically classified by severity (minor, major, critical), affected subsystem, and probable root cause. The system must generate a fault ticket in the maintenance management module with all relevant telemetry data attached. Critical faults on STS cranes or equipment near active vessel operations must trigger immediate audio-visual alerts on the operations dashboard and SMS notifications to the terminal duty manager within 30 seconds of detection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R010	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Equipment Operator Performance Tracking	The system must track individual equipment operator performance by correlating operator login sessions with equipment telemetry data. Key operator metrics must include average moves per hour, fuel consumption per move, idle time percentage, safety incident frequency, and smooth operation indices derived from accelerometer data indicating aggressive handling. Operator scorecards must be generated per shift with comparison against peer averages and historical personal bests. The system must enforce operator certification validation ensuring that only operators with valid, non-expired certifications for the specific equipment type are permitted to log in. Certification expiry warnings must be generated 30, 15, and 7 days before expiration. All operator data must comply with labor privacy regulations applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, with data retention policies configurable per entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R011	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Maintenance Schedule Integration with IoT Data	The system must integrate IoT telemetry data with the equipment maintenance scheduling module to enable condition-based maintenance triggers. When IoT sensors detect parameter drift approaching predefined thresholds (e.g., hydraulic oil temperature within 10 percent of critical limit, brake pad wear indicator activation, or bearing vibration exceeding ISO 10816 velocity limits), the system must automatically generate a maintenance work order with recommended actions and required spare parts. Scheduled preventive maintenance windows must be reflected on the equipment health dashboard with countdown timers. The system must calculate remaining useful life (RUL) estimates for critical components based on cumulative operational hours and sensor degradation trends. Maintenance costs must be tracked per equipment in the entity's local currency with budget variance alerts when actual costs exceed planned maintenance budgets by more than 10 percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R012	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	IoT Network Health and Connectivity Monitoring	The system must monitor the health of the IoT communication network itself, including gateway devices, edge computing nodes, network switches, and wireless access points deployed across each port terminal. Network monitoring must track signal strength, packet loss rate, latency, bandwidth utilization, and device connectivity status. The system must generate alerts when any IoT gateway loses connectivity for more than 60 seconds or when packet loss exceeds 5 percent on any network segment. A network topology map must be provided showing the hierarchical relationship between sensors, gateways, edge nodes, and the central cloud platform. The system must support network redundancy configurations and display failover status. Network health reports must be generated monthly per port with uptime SLA calculations. All monitoring interfaces must support Arabic and English bilingual display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R013	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Edge Computing for Local Data Processing	The system must support edge computing nodes deployed at each port terminal to perform local data preprocessing, filtering, aggregation, and time-critical decision making before transmitting summarized data to the central cloud platform. Edge nodes must be capable of running lightweight machine learning inference models for real-time fault detection with a maximum processing latency of 500 milliseconds from sensor input to alert output. Edge nodes must continue operating autonomously during cloud connectivity disruptions, buffering telemetry data locally for up to 72 hours and synchronizing automatically upon reconnection. Each edge node must support a minimum of 5,000 connected IoT devices. The system must provide remote management capabilities for edge node firmware updates, model deployments, and configuration changes. Edge node health status must be visible on the central IoT network monitoring dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R014	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Environmental Sensor Integration	The system must integrate environmental IoT sensors deployed across port terminals measuring ambient temperature, humidity, wind speed and direction, rainfall, visibility, dust/particulate levels, and noise levels. Environmental readings must be displayed on the equipment dashboard with configurable threshold alerts for conditions that affect equipment operations, such as wind speeds exceeding safe crane operating limits (typically 55 km/h for STS cranes). The system must automatically cross-reference environmental conditions with equipment operating rules and recommend or enforce operational restrictions (e.g., crane boom lowering during high wind events). Environmental data must be logged continuously and made available for incident investigation and insurance claim substantiation. Integration with Qatar Meteorology Department, UAE National Center of Meteorology, and India Meteorological Department feeds must be supported where available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R015	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Equipment IoT Data Security and Access Control	The system must enforce end-to-end encryption for all IoT telemetry data using TLS 1.3 for data in transit and AES-256 for data at rest. Device authentication must use X.509 certificates with automated certificate rotation on a configurable schedule (default 90 days). The system must implement device identity verification to prevent spoofed or unauthorized devices from injecting data into the telemetry pipeline. Multi-entity data isolation must ensure that IoT data from Hamad Port equipment is only accessible to Qatar entity users, Jebel Ali data to UAE entity users, and JNPT data to India entity users, with cross-entity access requiring explicit authorization. All IoT data access must be audit-logged with user identity, device ID, data scope, and timestamp. The system must comply with Qatar National Cyber Security Agency (NCSA) standards, UAE Information Assurance Regulation, and India CERT-In guidelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-002-R016	FEAT-049-2-002	MOD-049	Spare Parts Inventory Linkage from IoT Alerts	The system must automatically check spare parts inventory availability when an IoT-triggered maintenance work order is generated. The spare parts check must query the warehouse management module for required components based on the fault classification and equipment bill of materials. If required parts are in stock at the local port warehouse, the system must create a parts reservation and notify the maintenance team. If parts are out of stock, the system must trigger a procurement request with estimated lead times and costs in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). For critical equipment faults affecting vessel operations, the system must search across all entity warehouses for available parts and calculate inter-port transfer logistics including estimated delivery time and freight cost in USD. Parts consumption history per equipment must be tracked to optimize reorder points and safety stock levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R001	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Predictive Failure Model Training Pipeline	The system must provide an automated machine learning pipeline for training predictive failure models using historical IoT telemetry data, maintenance work order records, equipment inspection logs, and manufacturer-specified failure modes. The pipeline must support model architectures including gradient boosted trees, LSTM recurrent neural networks, and transformer-based time series models. Training data must be automatically curated from the IoT data lake with configurable feature engineering steps including rolling statistics, frequency domain transforms, and lag features. Model training must support GPU acceleration and handle datasets spanning a minimum of 3 years of historical data per equipment type. The pipeline must produce model performance metrics (precision, recall, F1 score, AUC-ROC) with a minimum acceptable F1 score of 0.85 for production deployment. Models must be trainable per equipment type and per port to account for environmental and operational differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R002	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Remaining Useful Life Estimation	The system must calculate and continuously update the estimated remaining useful life (RUL) for critical equipment components including crane hoist motors, hydraulic cylinders, wire ropes, spreader twist-locks, RTG wheel assemblies, and reach stacker mast chains. RUL estimates must be derived from the combination of cumulative operational hours, cycle counts, sensor degradation trends, and the predictive failure model outputs. Each RUL estimate must include a confidence interval and be presented as both a time-based estimate (days remaining) and a usage-based estimate (cycles or hours remaining). When RUL drops below a configurable threshold (e.g., 30 days or 500 operating hours), the system must automatically escalate to the maintenance planning team and create a draft work order. RUL trends must be visualizable on time-series charts with Arabic and English labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R003	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Intelligent Alert Prioritization and Routing	The system must employ an AI-driven alert management engine that prioritizes incoming IoT alerts based on multiple factors including equipment criticality score (STS cranes scoring higher than terminal tractors), impact on active vessel operations, cascading failure risk, historical false alarm rates for the specific sensor and equipment combination, and current port congestion levels. Alerts must be de-duplicated and correlated so that multiple sensor readings from the same root cause are grouped into a single actionable alert. Alert routing must be configurable per entity and per port, directing maintenance alerts to the appropriate maintenance team, safety alerts to the HSE department, and operational impact alerts to the terminal operations center. Each alert must include an AI-generated recommended action in both Arabic and English. The system must learn from alert resolution feedback to improve prioritization accuracy over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R004	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Condition-Based Maintenance Scheduling	The system must generate optimized condition-based maintenance schedules that replace or augment traditional time-based preventive maintenance programs. Scheduling must consider real-time equipment condition data from IoT sensors, predicted failure probabilities, operational workload forecasts (upcoming vessel calls, yard density), spare parts availability, and maintenance crew capacity. The scheduler must minimize total cost by balancing the risk of failure against the cost of premature maintenance intervention. Maintenance windows must avoid peak operational periods and coordinate with berth planning to schedule crane maintenance during low vessel traffic windows. The system must generate comparative cost analyses showing condition-based versus time-based maintenance savings per equipment type in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Schedule outputs must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R005	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Anomaly Detection in Telemetry Patterns	The system must implement unsupervised anomaly detection algorithms that continuously analyze IoT telemetry streams to identify patterns deviating from normal operational baselines without requiring pre-labeled training data. Anomaly detection must operate across multiple time scales: instantaneous spike detection (within 5 seconds), short-term trend shifts (hourly), and long-term degradation patterns (weekly to monthly). The algorithms must adapt to seasonal variations in operating conditions (e.g., higher ambient temperatures during Gulf summer months affecting hydraulic system temperatures) and distinguish between environmental factors and genuine equipment degradation. Detected anomalies must be scored from 0 to 100 indicating severity and confidence. Anomalies scoring above a configurable threshold must automatically generate investigation tickets. The system must support both univariate anomalies (single sensor deviation) and multivariate anomalies (correlated deviations across multiple sensors on the same equipment).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R006	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Predictive Model Performance Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the accuracy and drift of deployed predictive models by comparing predictions against actual outcomes. Key monitoring metrics must include prediction accuracy, false positive rate, false negative rate, mean time between predicted and actual failure, and model confidence calibration. When model performance degrades below configurable thresholds (e.g., F1 score drops below 0.80 or false positive rate exceeds 15 percent), the system must automatically trigger a model retraining workflow and alert the data science team. A model performance dashboard must display current and historical accuracy metrics per model, per equipment type, and per port. A/B testing capabilities must allow new model versions to run in shadow mode alongside production models before cutover. All model lifecycle events (training, deployment, retraining, retirement) must be logged in an immutable audit trail for regulatory compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R007	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Failure Impact Simulation Engine	The system must provide a simulation engine that calculates the operational and financial impact of a predicted equipment failure if no preventive action is taken. The simulation must model cascading effects including reduced vessel handling capacity, increased vessel waiting times, yard congestion buildup, and downstream schedule disruptions. Financial impact must be calculated in the entity's local currency including direct repair costs, vessel delay penalties (demurrage), cargo delay claims, alternative equipment rental costs, and revenue loss from reduced throughput. The simulation must present best-case, most-likely, and worst-case scenarios with probability-weighted expected costs. Results must inform maintenance prioritization decisions and support business case justification for capital expenditure on replacement equipment. Simulation reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual Arabic/English content and dual Gregorian/Hijri date formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R008	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Maintenance Crew Optimization and Dispatch	The system must optimize maintenance crew allocation and dispatch based on predicted maintenance demand, crew skills and certifications, geographic proximity within the port, work order priority, and estimated task duration. The optimization engine must generate shift-level crew assignment plans that minimize total response time for critical alerts while balancing workload across available technicians. Crew dispatch must be integrated with mobile devices showing real-time work order assignments with turn-by-turn navigation within the port terminal. The system must track actual versus planned maintenance task durations to improve future scheduling accuracy. Crew cost allocation must be tracked per work order and per equipment in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) for cost center reporting. Arabic and English language support must be available on all mobile interfaces for crew members across all entity locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R009	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Alert Escalation Workflow Engine	The system must implement a configurable multi-level alert escalation workflow that automatically escalates unacknowledged or unresolved alerts through a defined chain of responsibility. Level 1 escalation must occur if an alert is not acknowledged within a configurable time (default 15 minutes for critical, 60 minutes for major, 4 hours for minor). Level 2 escalation must occur if a Level 1 alert is acknowledged but not resolved within the target resolution time. Level 3 escalation must notify port management and, for vessel-affecting issues, the terminal operations director. Each escalation must include an enriched context package with the original alert, all related telemetry data, historical similar incidents, and AI-recommended resolution steps. Escalation rules must be independently configurable per entity and per port to reflect organizational structures in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Escalation notifications must support email, SMS, in-app push notifications, and integration with enterprise messaging platforms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R010	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Historical Failure Pattern Analysis	The system must maintain a comprehensive failure history database capturing all equipment failure events with full telemetry context from the 72 hours preceding each failure. The database must support pattern mining queries that identify common precursor signatures across equipment types, manufacturers, and operating environments. Users must be able to search failures by equipment type, failure mode, severity, port location, time period, and environmental conditions at the time of failure. The system must provide AI-assisted root cause analysis that clusters similar failures and identifies the most probable root causes based on telemetry patterns. Failure pattern reports must include mean time between failures (MTBF), mean time to repair (MTTR), and availability percentage per equipment class. Reports must be filterable by entity and exportable with bilingual Arabic/English headers, dual calendar date formats, and maintenance cost figures in local entity currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R011	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Predictive Spare Parts Demand Forecasting	The system must leverage predictive maintenance forecasts to generate forward-looking spare parts demand projections for a configurable planning horizon (default 90 days). Demand forecasts must consider predicted failure probabilities, scheduled condition-based maintenance work orders, historical consumption rates, equipment age profiles, and seasonal failure patterns (e.g., increased cooling system failures during Gulf summer). The forecast must produce recommended purchase quantities and timing per spare part per warehouse location across all ports. Integration with the procurement module must enable automated purchase order generation when forecasted demand exceeds available inventory plus in-transit stock. Cost projections must be denominated in the local entity currency with consolidated group views in USD. The system must track forecast accuracy by comparing predicted versus actual spare parts consumption monthly and automatically adjust forecasting models based on error analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R012	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Equipment Warranty and Service Contract Tracking	The system must track warranty terms and service contract coverage for all IoT-monitored equipment. When a predictive or actual fault is detected, the system must automatically check if the affected equipment or component is covered under an active warranty or service contract and include this information in the generated work order. Warranty coverage details must include the warranty provider, coverage scope (parts only, parts and labor, full service), remaining warranty period, claim process requirements, and maximum claim value in the contract currency. The system must alert procurement and maintenance teams 90, 60, and 30 days before warranty or service contract expiration. Warranty claim history must be tracked per equipment with total claimed value in the entity's local currency. Integration with the contract management module must provide direct links to scanned warranty certificates and service agreement documents stored in the document management system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R013	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Multi-Entity Maintenance KPI Benchmarking	The system must provide cross-entity benchmarking dashboards comparing maintenance performance KPIs across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. KPIs must include overall equipment effectiveness (OEE), mean time between failures (MTBF), mean time to repair (MTTR), planned versus unplanned maintenance ratio, maintenance cost per TEU handled, predictive model accuracy, and alert resolution time. Benchmarking must normalize metrics to account for differences in equipment fleet size, age profile, operational intensity, and environmental conditions. Each entity must be able to view their own KPIs in detail while cross-entity comparisons must be available only to authorized group-level users. KPI targets must be configurable per entity and per equipment type with automated red/amber/green status indicators. Cost-based KPIs must display in both local entity currency and USD for fair comparison. Dashboard text and labels must support Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R014	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	AI-Driven Maintenance Budget Forecasting	The system must generate AI-assisted maintenance budget forecasts for upcoming fiscal periods (quarterly and annually) based on equipment condition assessments, predicted failure rates, historical maintenance spend patterns, planned capital projects, and spare parts price trends. Budget forecasts must be broken down by cost category (labor, spare parts, external contractor, equipment rental) and by equipment type. The AI model must identify budget risk areas where actual spend is trending above forecast and recommend cost optimization actions such as deferred non-critical maintenance, bulk spare parts procurement, or warranty claim recovery opportunities. Budget forecasts must be generated in the entity's local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with consolidated group forecasts in USD. Forecasts must align with the fiscal calendar of each entity and support both Gregorian and Hijri reporting periods where required by local financial regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R015	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Offline Alert Processing and Sync	The system must ensure that predictive alerts and maintenance notifications continue to be generated and displayed on mobile devices used by field maintenance crews even during temporary network connectivity loss within port terminal areas. The mobile application must cache the most recent predictive model outputs and active alert queue locally, enabling maintenance technicians to view work orders, update task status, and record inspection findings offline. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize all offline-created records with the central system, resolving any conflicts using a last-write-wins strategy with manual review flagging for simultaneous edits. Offline mode must support a minimum operational period of 8 hours (one full shift) and all offline interface elements must function in both Arabic and English with the same layout quality as the online version. GPS tracking of crew movements during offline periods must continue and sync upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R016	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Regulatory Compliance Maintenance Tracking	The system must track mandatory regulatory inspection and maintenance requirements for port equipment as defined by the relevant authorities in each entity jurisdiction: Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), and Indian Directorate General of Shipping. The system must maintain a calendar of statutory inspection due dates per equipment item with automated reminders at 60, 30, and 14 days before each deadline. Overdue inspections must be flagged prominently on the equipment health dashboard and escalated to the port safety manager. Inspection records must capture the inspector identity, inspection authority, findings, corrective actions required, and compliance certificate number. The system must generate compliance status reports per port showing total equipment count, inspected count, overdue count, and compliance percentage. Reports must support Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date display and be exportable for submission to regulatory authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-003-R017	FEAT-049-2-003	MOD-049	Feedback Loop for Alert Accuracy Improvement	The system must implement a structured feedback mechanism where maintenance technicians and operations staff can rate the accuracy and usefulness of each predictive alert upon resolution. Feedback options must include confirmed accurate prediction, false alarm, partially accurate, and missed failure (where a failure occurred without a preceding alert). The system must aggregate feedback data per alert type, equipment type, and predictive model version to generate accuracy scorecards. When false alarm rates for a specific alert type exceed a configurable threshold (default 25 percent), the system must automatically flag the associated model for review and potential retraining. Feedback must be collectible through both the web dashboard and mobile application in Arabic and English. Monthly alert accuracy reports must be generated per entity and shared with the data science team to drive continuous model improvement cycles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R001	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Data Lake Architecture and Storage Management	The system must implement a scalable data lake architecture capable of storing raw, processed, and aggregated IoT telemetry data from all connected devices across all managed ports. The data lake must support a minimum retention of 5 years for raw telemetry data, 10 years for aggregated metrics, and indefinite retention for critical event records. Storage must be organized using a medallion architecture (bronze for raw, silver for cleaned, gold for business-ready) with automated data lifecycle management policies. The system must handle a minimum daily data ingestion volume of 50 TB across all ports. Data must be partitioned by entity, port, equipment type, and time period to optimize query performance. Storage costs must be tracked per entity in their local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automated tiering to move cold data to lower-cost storage classes after configurable periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R002	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Self-Service Analytics Dashboard Builder	The system must provide a drag-and-drop dashboard builder that enables authorized users to create custom analytics dashboards without requiring programming skills. The builder must offer a library of pre-built visualization widgets including time series charts, bar charts, pie charts, heatmaps, scatter plots, gauges, geographic maps, and data tables. Users must be able to connect widgets to data sources from the IoT data lake using a visual query builder that supports filtering, aggregation, grouping, and time range selection. Dashboards must be saveable, shareable with other users within the same entity, and schedulable for automatic email distribution as PDF or Excel reports. The dashboard builder must fully support Arabic RTL layout for all widget configurations, labels, and exported reports. Each user must be able to maintain up to 50 custom dashboards with version history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R003	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Pre-Built Operational Analytics Templates	The system must provide a minimum of 20 pre-built analytics dashboard templates covering common port operational scenarios including vessel turnaround time analysis, crane productivity benchmarking, yard utilization trends, reefer monitoring compliance, equipment downtime analysis, fuel consumption optimization, maintenance cost tracking, environmental condition impact assessment, and gate truck turnaround analysis. Each template must be pre-configured with appropriate data sources, visualizations, and KPI calculations relevant to container shipping operations. Templates must be customizable by users to adjust filters, time ranges, entity scope, and visual preferences. All templates must include bilingual Arabic/English labels and support date display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. Templates must automatically detect the user's entity assignment and pre-filter data accordingly while allowing authorized users to expand scope to other entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R004	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Real-Time Streaming Analytics	The system must support real-time streaming analytics that process IoT data as it arrives with a maximum processing latency of 5 seconds from data ingestion to dashboard visualization. Streaming analytics must support windowed aggregations (tumbling, sliding, and session windows) for calculating metrics such as rolling average crane moves per hour, running equipment utilization percentages, and live vessel count in port. Users must be able to configure streaming alerts that trigger when real-time metrics cross defined thresholds. The streaming pipeline must handle out-of-order and late-arriving data with configurable watermark policies. Real-time dashboards must support a minimum of 200 concurrent users per entity viewing live data simultaneously without performance degradation. The streaming analytics engine must be horizontally scalable to accommodate growth in IoT device count and data volume across ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R005	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Natural Language Query Interface	The system must provide an AI-powered natural language query interface that allows users to ask questions about IoT data in plain Arabic or English text. Example queries must include patterns like 'What was the average crane utilization at Jebel Ali last week' or 'Show me all reefer temperature alerts at Hamad Port this month' or the Arabic equivalents. The AI engine must translate natural language queries into appropriate data lake queries, execute them, and return results as auto-selected visualizations (charts, tables, or summary statistics). The system must handle ambiguous queries by presenting clarification options rather than returning incorrect results. Query history must be saved per user for easy re-execution. The NLQ engine must understand shipping domain terminology including equipment types, port names, container sizes (20ft, 40ft, 45ft, high-cube), and common abbreviations (TEU, STS, RTG, MTBF, OEE). Response time for NLQ queries must not exceed 10 seconds for standard aggregations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R006	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Cross-Port Comparative Analytics	The system must enable authorized group-level users to create comparative analytics views that benchmark operational metrics across Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Comparative views must support side-by-side and overlay chart modes for metrics including throughput per berth meter, equipment utilization rates, energy consumption per TEU, maintenance cost per container move, and average vessel turnaround time. The system must normalize metrics to account for differences in port size, equipment count, and operational hours to ensure fair comparisons. Cross-port analytics must respect multi-entity data access controls, only showing data the user is authorized to view. Trend analysis must highlight improving and declining performance areas with AI-generated narrative summaries explaining potential contributing factors. All comparative reports must display financial metrics in USD for normalization, with entity-local currency breakdowns available on drill-down.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R007	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Executive Summary Report Generation	The system must automatically generate executive summary reports on configurable schedules (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly) presenting high-level KPIs and trend narratives for port operations leadership. Reports must include sections on vessel traffic and throughput, equipment availability and performance, maintenance activities and costs, energy consumption and sustainability metrics, and significant incidents or anomalies. Each metric must include comparison to target, comparison to previous period, and trend direction indicator. The AI engine must generate narrative summaries in both Arabic and English explaining significant changes, deviations from targets, and recommended focus areas. Reports must be formatted as professional PDF documents with the entity's branding, bilingual headers, and dual Gregorian/Hijri calendar date formatting. Financial figures must be presented in the entity's local currency with USD equivalents. Reports must be automatically distributed to a configurable recipient list via email.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R008	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Data Export and Integration API	The system must provide comprehensive data export capabilities supporting CSV, Excel, JSON, and Parquet formats for analytics results and underlying data sets. Exports must support configurable column selection, filtering, date ranges, and entity scoping. For large exports exceeding 1 million rows, the system must support asynchronous export with notification upon completion and a download link valid for 72 hours. A RESTful analytics API must be available for external business intelligence tools to query the IoT data lake directly using SQL-compatible syntax. The API must enforce entity-level data isolation and respect the same access control rules as the web interface. API rate limiting must be configurable per consumer with usage tracking for cost allocation. All exported data must include metadata headers indicating the data source, extraction timestamp, entity scope, and currency denomination. API documentation must be provided in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R009	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Anomaly and Trend Detection in Analytics	The system must apply automated anomaly and trend detection algorithms to analytics dashboard data, highlighting data points that deviate significantly from historical patterns. Anomaly detection must operate on all numeric metrics displayed on dashboards and flag values exceeding configurable standard deviation thresholds (default 2 sigma). Trend detection must identify statistically significant upward or downward trends across configurable time windows (weekly, monthly, quarterly). Detected anomalies and trends must be visually highlighted on charts with clickable annotations explaining the detection rationale. The system must distinguish between positive anomalies (e.g., unexpectedly high crane productivity) and negative anomalies (e.g., sudden increase in equipment fault rates). Users must be able to acknowledge and annotate anomalies with explanatory notes in Arabic or English. The system must learn from user feedback to reduce false positive anomaly alerts over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R010	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Data Quality Monitoring and Governance	The system must implement data quality monitoring for all IoT data flowing into the analytics data lake. Quality checks must include completeness (percentage of expected data points received), accuracy (values within physically plausible ranges per sensor type), consistency (cross-sensor validation for related measurements), timeliness (data arriving within expected latency windows), and uniqueness (duplicate detection and handling). A data quality scorecard must be maintained per data source, per equipment, and per port, with quality scores visible on an administrative dashboard. When data quality drops below configurable thresholds (default 95 percent completeness, 99 percent accuracy), automated alerts must be sent to the IoT operations team. Data lineage tracking must record the full transformation chain from raw sensor reading to analytics-ready metric. All data governance documentation must be maintained in both Arabic and English to satisfy audit requirements in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R011	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Predictive Analytics for Operational Planning	The system must provide predictive analytics models that forecast key operational metrics for planning horizons of 7 days, 30 days, and 90 days. Forecasted metrics must include expected throughput (TEUs), equipment maintenance demand, energy consumption, spare parts requirements, and berth utilization. Forecasts must incorporate scheduled vessel arrivals, historical seasonal patterns, weather forecasts, known equipment maintenance windows, and ongoing capital projects. The predictive models must provide confidence intervals for each forecast and highlight key assumptions. Port planners must be able to create what-if scenarios by adjusting input parameters (e.g., adding a vessel call, removing a crane from service, simulating Ramadan operational schedule adjustments) and comparing scenario outcomes side by side. All forecast outputs must display dates in Gregorian and Hijri formats and financial projections in entity-local currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R012	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	User Access Control and Data Segregation	The system must enforce role-based access control on all analytics dashboards, reports, and data exports. Access levels must include entity administrator (full access to own entity data and dashboard configuration), operations manager (view and create dashboards for assigned port), analyst (view dashboards and run queries within assigned scope), and executive (view pre-built executive reports across authorized entities). Multi-entity data segregation must ensure that users can only query and visualize data for entities they are authorized to access. The system must prevent data leakage through export controls that strip unauthorized entity data from downloaded files even if a query inadvertently spans multiple entities. All analytics access must be audit-logged including dashboard views, query executions, data exports, and report downloads. Audit logs must capture user identity, timestamp, accessed data scope, entity, and action type. Access control configuration must support Arabic and English for role names and permission descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R013	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Scheduled Report Automation with Distribution	The system must provide a report scheduling engine that automates the generation and distribution of analytics reports at configurable intervals (hourly, daily, weekly, monthly) or triggered by specific events (e.g., vessel departure, equipment fault resolution). Scheduled reports must support parameterized templates where variables such as date range, port selection, and equipment type are dynamically populated at generation time. Distribution channels must include email with PDF or Excel attachment, upload to shared document management systems, and in-app notification with report link. Recipients must be configurable per report with support for both internal users and external stakeholders (shipping line contacts, regulatory authorities). Reports must be generated with the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) and date format (Gregorian or Hijri). The scheduling engine must handle timezone differences across entities and generate reports at the locally appropriate time for each recipient.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R014	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Dashboard Performance Optimization	The system must implement performance optimization strategies to ensure analytics dashboards load within 5 seconds for standard queries and within 15 seconds for complex cross-entity aggregations spanning multiple years of data. Optimization techniques must include pre-computed aggregation tables (materialized views) for common time-based rollups (hourly, daily, monthly), query result caching with configurable time-to-live per dashboard widget, and intelligent query planning that selects the optimal data tier (hot, warm, cold) based on the requested time range. The system must support dashboard auto-refresh intervals configurable from 30 seconds to 24 hours per widget. Heavy queries that would impact system performance must be queued and executed during off-peak hours with the user notified upon completion. The system must monitor query execution times and alert administrators when average dashboard response times exceed SLA thresholds. Performance metrics must be tracked per entity to ensure equitable resource allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R015	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	IoT Device Analytics and Fleet Management	The system must provide analytics specifically focused on the IoT device fleet itself, tracking metrics such as device uptime percentage, battery levels for wireless sensors, firmware version distribution, data transmission success rates, and sensor calibration drift. Device analytics must enable port IT teams to identify underperforming sensors, plan batch firmware updates, and forecast device replacement needs based on age and performance degradation trends. A device lifecycle dashboard must show the total cost of ownership per device type including procurement, installation, maintenance, and data transmission costs in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The system must identify devices transmitting anomalous data patterns that may indicate sensor malfunction versus genuine equipment issues. Device fleet reports must be generated per port with bilingual Arabic/English headers and exportable for asset management audits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R016	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Sustainability and Carbon Analytics	The system must provide a dedicated sustainability analytics dashboard aggregating energy consumption, fuel usage, emissions estimates, and environmental sensor data to support ESG (Environmental, Social, and Governance) reporting requirements. The dashboard must calculate carbon footprint metrics per TEU handled, per vessel call, and per equipment operating hour. Emissions must be categorized by Scope 1 (direct fuel combustion), Scope 2 (purchased electricity), and Scope 3 (indirect upstream and downstream activities where data is available). The system must track progress against configurable sustainability targets set per entity and per port. Trend analysis must highlight areas of improvement and areas requiring intervention. Reports must comply with GRI Standards, CDP reporting frameworks, and regional sustainability mandates including Qatar National Vision 2030, UAE Net Zero 2050, Saudi Vision 2030 green initiatives, and India's National Action Plan on Climate Change. Reports must be generated in Arabic and English with all date formats and financial figures localized per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R017	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Collaborative Analytics Workspace	The system must provide a collaborative workspace where analytics users can share insights, annotate dashboards, and discuss findings in context. Users must be able to add comments and annotations to specific data points, chart areas, or dashboard sections with mentions of other users to draw their attention. A shared investigation board must allow teams to compile related charts, data queries, and notes into a structured analysis for root cause investigations or operational reviews. All collaboration content must support Arabic and English text with proper RTL and LTR rendering within the same thread. Version history must be maintained for shared dashboards showing who made changes and when. The workspace must support real-time collaborative editing where multiple users can view and edit the same dashboard simultaneously with conflict-free concurrent editing. Collaboration activity must be logged for audit purposes with user identity, entity, and timestamp.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-049-2-004-R018	FEAT-049-2-004	MOD-049	Mobile Analytics Access and Responsive Design	The system must provide a fully responsive mobile-optimized interface for accessing analytics dashboards and reports on tablets and smartphones. The mobile interface must adapt visualizations for smaller screen sizes by converting complex multi-panel dashboards into scrollable single-column layouts with expandable sections. Touch-optimized interactions must include pinch-to-zoom on charts, swipe navigation between dashboard pages, and tap-to-drill-down on data points. The mobile interface must support offline access to the most recently viewed dashboards with cached data, displaying a clear indicator of data freshness. Push notifications for scheduled reports and anomaly alerts must be supported on both iOS and Android platforms. The mobile analytics interface must fully support Arabic RTL layout with the same quality as the desktop version. Performance on mobile must meet a target of first meaningful paint within 3 seconds on 4G connections. Dashboard favorites and recent history must sync across desktop and mobile devices per user account.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R001	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Mobile Gate Transaction Initiation	The system must allow gate operators to initiate a new gate-in or gate-out transaction from a mobile device by scanning the truck license plate using OCR camera capture or manual entry. The transaction must auto-populate carrier information from the registered trucking company database. The system must support license plate formats for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India including Arabic-script plates. Each transaction must be timestamped with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates, and tagged with the specific terminal gate number (e.g., Hamad Port Gate 1-12, Jebel Ali Gate A1-F6). The mobile interface must support both portrait and landscape orientations on devices with minimum 7-inch screens.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R002	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Container Number Recognition via Camera	The system must provide AI-powered container number recognition using the mobile device camera, capable of reading ISO 6346 container codes including the four-letter owner code, six-digit serial number, and check digit. The OCR engine must achieve a minimum 97% accuracy rate under varying lighting conditions including direct sunlight, nighttime with flood lighting, and rain. Recognition must work for standard 20ft (TEU), 40ft, 40ft HC, and 45ft HC containers. The system must validate the check digit per ISO 6346 algorithm and flag mismatches. Failed OCR attempts must fall back to manual entry with an Arabic/English soft keyboard. The recognized container number must be cross-referenced against the expected vessel manifest or booking reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R003	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Seal Verification and Recording	The system must enable gate operators to scan or photograph container seal numbers and record them against the gate transaction. The system must support mechanical bolt seals, cable seals, and electronic RFID seals. For gate-in transactions, the recorded seal must be validated against the bill of lading seal number from the shipping line's manifest data. Any seal discrepancy must trigger an automatic hold and generate an alert to the operations supervisor. The system must capture high-resolution photographs of each seal (minimum 2 megapixels) with embedded GPS coordinates and timestamps. For customs-sealed containers at JNPT, the system must validate against Indian Customs ICEGATE seal registry. For Qatar and UAE, the system must verify against GCC Customs Union electronic seal databases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R004	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Pre-Gate Appointment Validation	The system must validate truck arrivals against pre-booked terminal appointment slots before allowing gate-in processing. The validation must check the Vehicle Booking System (VBS) appointment window with a configurable tolerance of plus or minus 30 minutes by default. Trucks arriving outside their appointment window must be flagged with options to accept with override authorization, redirect to a holding area, or reject. The system must display the appointment status in both Arabic and English with color-coded indicators: green for on-time, amber for within tolerance, red for outside window. For Jebel Ali and JNPT terminals that enforce strict appointment systems, the rejection workflow must automatically notify the trucking company via SMS in the appropriate language. The mobile app must show real-time gate queue length and estimated wait times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R005	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Customs and Regulatory Document Verification	The system must verify that all required customs clearance documents are completed before allowing gate release for both import and export containers. For Qatar operations, the system must validate against Qatar Customs (Al Nadeeb system) clearance status. For UAE operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, the system must check Dubai Trade Portal and Abu Dhabi Customs clearance respectively. For KSA, the system must verify FASAH platform clearance. For JNPT India, the system must validate ICEGATE EDI clearance and check for any DPD (Direct Port Delivery) designations. The mobile interface must display document status with clear Arabic and English labels, and provide the gate operator with a one-tap option to view the detailed clearance status. Containers with pending customs holds must be automatically routed to customs examination areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R006	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Hazardous Cargo Gate Screening	The system must implement special gate processing workflows for containers carrying hazardous materials classified under the International Maritime Dangerous Goods (IMDG) Code. The mobile app must display IMDG class, UN number, proper shipping name, and segregation requirements upon scanning a DG container. Gate operators must confirm that the DG placard matches the declared class by selecting from a visual placard reference library on the device. The system must enforce that DG containers are not gated in if the yard has no available DG-compliant storage slots. Emergency contact information and Material Safety Data Sheets must be accessible offline on the device. The system must comply with Qatar Civil Defence, UAE NCEMA, KSA Civil Defense, and JNPT CISF requirements for DG handling notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R007	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Reefer Container Gate Processing	The system must provide specialized gate processing for refrigerated containers including temperature verification at gate-in and gate-out. Gate operators must record the reefer unit set temperature, actual supply temperature, and return air temperature using the mobile device. The system must flag any temperature deviation exceeding the threshold defined in the booking (typically plus or minus 2 degrees Celsius for most commodities, plus or minus 0.5 degrees for pharmaceutical cargo). For gate-in reefer containers, the system must verify that a powered reefer yard slot has been pre-assigned. The mobile app must display the reefer monitoring history graph from the vessel voyage if available. PTI (Pre-Trip Inspection) status must be verified for export reefer containers, and containers failing PTI must be blocked from gate-out. Currency for any reefer-related surcharges must reflect the operating entity: QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for KSA, INR for JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R008	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Multi-Entity Gate Configuration	The system must support distinct gate processing configurations for each operating entity across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Each entity must have independently configurable gate operating hours, mandatory document checklists, and approval hierarchies. The mobile app must automatically load the correct entity configuration based on the operator's login credentials and assigned terminal. Gate transaction records must be segregated by entity for accounting and reporting purposes, with each transaction recording the entity code, terminal code, and local currency. Inter-entity container transfers (e.g., a container moving from Hamad Port to Jebel Ali) must be processed as a gate-out at the origin entity and gate-in at the destination entity with linked transaction references. The system must enforce entity-specific VAT rules: 0% for Qatar currently, 5% for UAE and KSA, and applicable GST rates for India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R009	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Driver and Vehicle Identification	The system must capture driver identification using the mobile device camera to scan national ID cards, driver licenses, or port access passes. For GCC countries, the system must read and validate Qatar QID, UAE Emirates ID, and KSA National ID or Iqama numbers. For India, the system must support Aadhaar-based verification or JNPT-issued smart cards. The system must maintain a blacklist of banned drivers and vehicles, alerting the gate operator immediately if a match is found. Driver photographs must be captured and stored with the gate transaction for audit purposes. Vehicle details including truck registration, trailer number, and chassis number must be recorded. The system must verify that the driver has valid terminal access authorization for the specific gate and time period, with all UI prompts and validation messages displayed in the operator's preferred language of Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R010	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Real-Time Gate Throughput Dashboard	The mobile app must provide a real-time dashboard showing gate throughput metrics accessible to supervisors and terminal managers. The dashboard must display current transactions per hour, average gate processing time, queue lengths at each lane, and comparison against KPI targets. Data must refresh at minimum every 30 seconds. The dashboard must support drill-down from terminal level to individual gate lane level. Historical comparison must be available showing current shift versus previous shift and same day last week. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with all numeric formatting respecting locale conventions (e.g., Arabic-Indic numerals option for Arabic interface). Alerts must be triggered when gate processing time exceeds configurable thresholds, defaulting to 3 minutes for standard containers and 5 minutes for DG or reefer containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R011	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Weigh Bridge Integration	The system must integrate with terminal weigh bridges to capture and record container gross weight at gate-in and gate-out. The mobile app must display the Verified Gross Mass (VGM) as declared under SOLAS regulation and compare it with the actual weigh bridge reading. Any discrepancy exceeding the configurable tolerance (default 5% or 500 kg, whichever is greater) must trigger a hold for investigation. The system must record tare weight of the truck and trailer separately from the container cargo weight. For export containers, the VGM declaration must be validated against the SOLAS filing before gate-out is permitted. Weight data must be stored in kilograms as the base unit with conversion display available for metric tons. The system must support multiple weigh bridge hardware protocols including Avery Weigh-Tronix, Mettler Toledo, and locally used systems at each terminal. Overweight containers must be automatically flagged for re-planning in the yard module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R012	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Gate Transaction Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive immutable audit trail for every gate transaction capturing all operator actions, system validations, override decisions, and timestamps. Each audit entry must record the operator ID, device ID, GPS coordinates, action type, previous value, new value, and reason code for any manual overrides. The audit trail must be stored in compliance with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority data retention requirements (minimum 7 years), UAE Federal Law on electronic transactions, KSA NCA cybersecurity framework, and Indian Information Technology Act provisions. Audit records must be tamper-evident using cryptographic hashing. Supervisors must be able to search and filter the audit trail from the mobile app by date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), operator, container number, or transaction type. The system must support exporting audit reports in PDF format with bilingual Arabic-English headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R013	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Emergency Gate Override Processing	The system must provide an emergency gate override capability for authorized supervisors to process gate transactions when normal validation workflows cannot be completed. Emergency overrides must require two-factor authentication on the mobile device combining biometric verification (fingerprint or face recognition) and a one-time PIN sent to the supervisor's registered mobile number. Every emergency override must capture a mandatory text justification of at least 50 characters and automatically notify the terminal manager and compliance officer via push notification. The system must enforce a configurable maximum number of emergency overrides per shift (default 10) and per operator (default 3 per shift). Emergency override transactions must be flagged with a distinct visual indicator in all subsequent tracking screens and reports. The override reason codes must be configurable per entity and must include categories such as system downtime, customs system unavailability, and emergency vessel operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R014	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Container Damage Recording at Gate	The system must enable gate operators to record container damage observations during gate-in and gate-out processing using a standardized damage code system aligned with IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) guidelines. The mobile app must present an interactive container diagram where operators can tap on the damaged area (front, back, left, right, top, floor) and select the damage type from a predefined list including dents, holes, rust, broken doors, and damaged corner castings. High-resolution photographs must be captured and automatically tagged with the damage location and type. The system must compare gate-in damage records with gate-out records to determine if damage occurred during the container's stay at the terminal. Damage reports must be generated in both Arabic and English and automatically forwarded to the shipping line's equipment control department. For leased containers, the damage record must reference the applicable IICL repair standards and estimated repair costs in the entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R015	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Gate Notifications and Alerts Engine	The system must provide a configurable notifications engine that delivers real-time alerts to gate operators, supervisors, and external stakeholders via the mobile app push notifications, SMS, and email. Notification triggers must include: gate-in completion for customs brokers, gate-out completion for shipping lines, holds applied or released, appointment no-shows after a configurable grace period, and VGM discrepancies. Notifications must be delivered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) and must include the container number, transaction type, terminal, and timestamp. The system must support notification templates that can be customized per entity with variables for dynamic content insertion. External notifications to trucking companies and customs brokers must comply with UAE TRA, Qatar CRA, KSA CITC, and Indian TRAI regulations regarding commercial messaging. The notification delivery status must be tracked with retry logic for failed deliveries up to three attempts over 15 minutes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-001-R016	FEAT-050-1-001	MOD-050	Dual-Transaction Truck Processing	The system must support dual-transaction truck processing where a single truck performs both a container drop-off (gate-in) and a container pick-up (gate-out) in a single terminal visit. The mobile app must link both transactions under a single truck visit record and optimize the processing sequence to minimize total gate time. The system must validate that the pick-up container is available and released before allowing the dual transaction to proceed. Yard planning must be notified in real-time so that the pick-up container can be pre-positioned. Billing for dual transactions must apply any applicable discounts configured per entity, with amounts calculated in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The gate operator must be able to convert a single-transaction visit to a dual-transaction visit mid-process if conditions are met. Processing time KPIs for dual transactions must be tracked separately from single transactions with a default target of 5 minutes total.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R001	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspection Route Planning and Assignment	The system must generate optimized inspection routes for yard inspectors based on configurable criteria including block priority, time since last inspection, and container dwell time thresholds. Routes must be assigned to specific inspectors via the mobile app with estimated completion times calculated based on the number of bays, rows, and tiers to inspect. The system must support both AI-generated automatic route optimization and manual route creation by yard supervisors. Route assignments must account for inspector shift schedules and break times. Each route must be displayed on an interactive yard map with turn-by-turn navigation adapted to the terminal's internal road network. For multi-entity operations, routes must be scoped to the inspector's authorized terminal areas at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Route completion progress must be tracked in real-time with percentage indicators visible to supervisors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R002	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Container Position Verification	The system must enable inspectors to verify that containers are physically located in the yard positions recorded in the Terminal Operating System (TOS). The mobile app must display the expected container number for each bay-row-tier position and allow the inspector to confirm or report a mismatch by scanning the actual container number via camera OCR or manual entry. Position mismatches must be categorized as: container present but in wrong position, position empty but TOS shows occupied, position occupied but TOS shows empty, or different container found. Each verification must be GPS-tagged to confirm the inspector was physically at the location. The system must calculate and display a yard accuracy percentage per block updated in real-time. Discrepancies must be automatically queued for yard planning correction with priority levels based on the container's next planned move (vessel loading within 24 hours receiving highest priority).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R003	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Stack Height and Weight Compliance Check	The system must verify that container stacking complies with maximum stack height rules and weight-on-weight restrictions. The mobile app must display the maximum allowed stack height for each block (configurable per terminal, typically 5-high for standard and 4-high for reefer or DG blocks). Inspectors must verify that heavier containers are stacked below lighter ones by recording or confirming the verified gross mass of containers in each tier. The system must flag violations where a container with higher VGM is stacked above a lighter container by more than the configurable tolerance (default 2000 kg differential). For IMDG-class containers, the system must enforce segregation rules per the IMDG Code Amendment 41-22, preventing incompatible hazard classes from being stacked adjacent or above each other. Violations must generate immediate corrective action requests to the yard crane operators. Compliance reports must be generated per block with entity-specific regulatory references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R004	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Reefer Monitoring Inspection	The system must provide a specialized reefer inspection workflow where inspectors verify the operational status of all plugged-in refrigerated containers in reefer blocks. The mobile app must display the set temperature, actual supply temperature, and return air temperature for each reefer unit pulled from the terminal's reefer monitoring system. Inspectors must physically verify these readings against the reefer unit display and record any discrepancies. The system must flag reefer units where the temperature deviation exceeds the commodity-specific threshold, with stricter limits for pharmaceutical cargo (plus or minus 0.5 degrees Celsius) and standard limits for food cargo (plus or minus 2 degrees Celsius). Unplugged reefer containers that should be powered must trigger high-priority alerts. The inspection must record the reefer power connection status, any alarms displayed on the reefer unit, and fuel level for clip-on genset units. Reports must be generated in the entity's local currency for any resulting surcharges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R005	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Yard Infrastructure Condition Reporting	The system must enable inspectors to report on the condition of yard infrastructure including pavement surface integrity, block markings visibility, drainage systems, lighting, reefer power outlets, and fencing. Each infrastructure element must have a configurable inspection checklist with pass, fail, and needs-maintenance status options. Failed items must automatically generate maintenance work orders routed to the terminal engineering department. The mobile app must support geotagged photo and video capture for documenting infrastructure issues with a minimum of three photos required for any failed item. Infrastructure inspections must be scheduled on configurable recurring cycles (daily, weekly, monthly) separate from container inventory inspections. The system must maintain a historical condition log for each infrastructure element to track degradation trends. Reports must be available in both Arabic and English with maintenance cost estimates displayed in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R006	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Abandoned Container Identification	The system must automatically flag containers that have exceeded configurable dwell time thresholds as potential abandoned containers and include them in inspection priorities. Default thresholds must be 30 days for import containers and 14 days for empty containers, configurable per entity and container type. During yard inspection, the inspector must verify the physical condition of flagged long-dwelling containers and assess whether the container shows signs of abandonment such as excessive rust, vegetation growth, or structural degradation. The mobile app must display the container's complete history including arrival date, last status change, consignee information, and any outstanding charges in the entity's local currency. Inspectors must categorize the container as active-with-reason, potentially-abandoned, or confirmed-abandoned. Confirmed abandoned containers must trigger a workflow involving customs notification per the respective country's customs regulations, shipping line notification, and legal hold processing. The system must track abandonment cases through to resolution including auction or disposal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R007	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Yard Hazardous Materials Compliance Inspection	The system must provide a DG-specific yard inspection module that verifies compliance with IMDG Code segregation requirements, terminal DG storage limits, and local fire safety regulations. The mobile app must display an interactive DG yard map showing the location of all hazardous containers color-coded by IMDG class. Inspectors must verify that DG placard and marking visibility meets IMO standards, that emergency information panels are correctly displayed, and that no unauthorized containers are stored within the DG segregation zone. The system must calculate the current DG inventory against the terminal's maximum allowable quantities per hazard class and flag when capacity thresholds exceed 80 percent. Inspections must verify that firefighting equipment positions near DG blocks are accessible and within service dates. The system must generate compliance reports referencing Qatar Civil Defence Regulation, UAE Federal Fire Safety Code, KSA Saudi Civil Defense requirements, and JNPT CISF safety mandates as applicable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R008	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Empty Container Depot Inspection	The system must support specialized inspection workflows for empty container depot areas where shipping line equipment is stored. Inspectors must record container counts by shipping line, size, and type (standard dry, high cube, open top, flat rack, tank) and reconcile against the TOS inventory records. The system must identify and flag damaged empties requiring repair with damage categorization per IICL standards. The mobile app must display the depot allocation per shipping line and flag any overstock situations where actual counts exceed allocated space. Empty container grading must be supported with categories of food-grade, cargo-worthy, and needs-repair. The system must calculate empty container turnover rates and display average dwell times per shipping line. Depot inspection reports must be exportable in formats suitable for sharing with shipping line equipment controllers. All quantities and any associated storage charges must be recorded against the correct entity with costs in the applicable local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R009	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	AI-Assisted Anomaly Detection During Inspection	The system must leverage artificial intelligence to assist yard inspectors in detecting anomalies during their inspection rounds. The AI engine must analyze photos captured during inspections to automatically detect visible container damage, incorrect placards, missing seal indicators, and structural hazards. Machine learning models must be trained on terminal-specific imagery to account for local environmental conditions such as sand erosion at Gulf terminals and monsoon corrosion at JNPT. The AI must provide confidence scores for each detected anomaly with a minimum threshold of 85 percent before flagging to the inspector for confirmation. False positive rates must be tracked and used to continuously improve model accuracy. The AI must also correlate inspection findings with historical data to predict containers likely to have issues, enabling proactive inspection prioritization. All AI recommendations must be clearly labeled as suggestions requiring human confirmation, with the final determination always resting with the qualified inspector.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R010	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspection Scheduling and Frequency Management	The system must manage inspection schedules with configurable frequencies based on yard block type, container category, and regulatory requirements. DG blocks must default to twice-daily inspections, reefer blocks to three-times-daily, and standard dry container blocks to once-daily. The system must automatically adjust inspection frequency based on risk factors such as approaching vessel loading cutoffs, extreme weather conditions (sandstorms in Gulf terminals, heavy monsoon at JNPT), or elevated security threat levels. Inspection windows must account for terminal operating hours and prayer times as observed at each entity location. The scheduling engine must distribute inspection workload evenly across available inspectors considering their qualifications and certifications. Missed or overdue inspections must trigger escalating alerts: first to the inspector, then to the yard supervisor after 30 minutes, and to the terminal manager after 60 minutes. Schedule views must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar displays with full Arabic RTL interface support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R011	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspection Findings Workflow and Resolution Tracking	The system must implement a complete workflow for tracking inspection findings from identification through resolution. Each finding must be assigned a severity level: critical (safety hazard requiring immediate action), high (operational impact within 24 hours), medium (requires attention within 72 hours), or low (informational or cosmetic). Critical findings must trigger immediate push notifications to the terminal operations manager and safety officer. The mobile app must allow supervisors to assign corrective actions to specific personnel with target resolution dates. Resolution evidence must be captured including photos and inspector sign-off. The system must track resolution time metrics against SLA targets configurable per entity. Recurring findings at the same location must be automatically escalated to management review. A findings dashboard must display open, in-progress, and resolved items with filtering by severity, block, inspector, and date range. All workflow communications must be available in Arabic and English based on recipient preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R003	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Multi-Entity Revenue Target Configuration	The system must allow each legal entity (Qatar entity under QFC regulations, UAE entity under JAFZA/DMCC, KSA entity under MISA, India entity under Companies Act 2013) to configure independent revenue per TEU targets by trade lane, commodity group, and time period. Targets must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods for KSA and Qatar entities. Revenue targets must be denominated in the entity's local functional currency with real-time cross-rate visibility to USD. The system must enforce entity-level segregation such that one entity's target configuration cannot be viewed or modified by users of another entity without explicit cross-entity authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R012	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Yard Equipment Inspection Integration	The system must integrate yard equipment inspection capabilities allowing inspectors to report on the condition and positioning of yard handling equipment including rubber-tyred gantry cranes (RTGs), reach stackers, terminal tractors, and chassis. The mobile app must display equipment locations on the yard map and allow inspectors to record equipment status (operational, idle, maintenance-required, or out-of-service). Pre-operation inspection checklists must be available for each equipment type covering safety items such as tire condition, hydraulic leaks, warning lights, and fire extinguisher presence. Equipment defects must generate maintenance work orders linked to the terminal's asset management system. The system must track equipment utilization rates and idle time per shift. For leased equipment, the inspection records must reference the lease agreement and entity-specific accounting codes. Equipment inspection data must feed into predictive maintenance algorithms to forecast potential failures before they impact operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R013	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Security Perimeter Inspection	The system must support security perimeter inspection workflows covering terminal boundary fencing, CCTV camera positioning, access control points, and ISPS Code compliance requirements. Inspectors must verify that all CCTV cameras in their assigned zone are operational and correctly oriented by comparing live camera feeds on the mobile app against expected coverage zones. The system must maintain an ISPS Code compliance checklist aligned with the terminal's approved Port Facility Security Plan (PFSP) at each location. Security breaches or vulnerabilities identified during inspection must trigger immediate alerts to the terminal security officer and must be classified per the ISPS maritime security levels (MARSEC 1, 2, or 3). The mobile app must support discreet alert mode for reporting suspicious activities without drawing attention. All security inspection data must be encrypted at rest and in transit with access restricted to authorized security personnel. Reports must comply with Qatar MOI, UAE NCEMA, KSA Presidency of State Security, and Indian CISF security reporting requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R014	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspection Photo and Evidence Management	The system must provide comprehensive photo and evidence management capabilities for yard inspections. Each photo must be automatically tagged with GPS coordinates (accuracy within 3 meters), timestamp, inspector ID, container number or infrastructure element ID, and inspection route reference. Photos must be captured at a minimum resolution of 8 megapixels and stored with lossless compression. The mobile app must support annotation of photos with arrows, circles, and text labels in both Arabic and English before submission. A photo comparison feature must allow inspectors to view previous inspection photos of the same container or location side-by-side with current photos to identify changes. The system must enforce a configurable minimum number of photos per finding (default 3) and maximum storage per inspection (default 500 megabytes). Photos must be stored in a centralized document management system with retention periods aligned to each entity's regulatory requirements. Bulk photo export must be available for insurance claims, shipping line disputes, and regulatory audits with watermarked metadata.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R015	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspection Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate comprehensive inspection reports and analytics accessible through the mobile app and web dashboard. Reports must include yard accuracy rates per block over configurable time periods, inspection completion rates versus scheduled, average finding resolution times, and trend analysis of recurring issues. The analytics engine must produce heat maps showing areas with the highest discrepancy rates overlaid on the terminal yard map. KPI scorecards must be generated per inspector, per block, and per terminal with benchmarking across entities. The system must produce regulatory compliance reports formatted for submission to Qatar Port Authority, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World Jebel Ali, and JNPT administration as required. All reports must support dual language generation in Arabic and English within the same document. Financial impact analysis of inspection findings must be calculated in the entity's local currency with consolidated multi-currency views for group management. Reports must be exportable in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with scheduled automatic distribution to configurable recipient lists.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-002-R016	FEAT-050-1-002	MOD-050	Inspector Performance and Certification Tracking	The system must track individual inspector performance metrics including inspections completed per shift, average time per inspection, finding accuracy rate (confirmed findings versus false reports), and coverage completeness (percentage of assigned areas actually inspected based on GPS tracking). The mobile app must maintain a certification registry for each inspector recording qualifications such as IMDG DG handler certification, food safety inspection clearance, ISPS security officer certification, and equipment-specific training. The system must prevent assignment of inspectors to DG block inspections unless they hold valid IMDG certification. Certification expiry must trigger automatic alerts 30 days before expiration with renewal workflow initiation. Performance dashboards must be accessible to supervisors with configurable thresholds for performance warnings. Inspector availability must account for annual leave, training days, and Eid holidays as observed per entity country. All performance data must comply with labor law privacy requirements across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R001	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) Digital Survey	The system must provide a digital Equipment Interchange Receipt survey workflow that replaces paper-based EIR forms. The mobile app must present an interactive container schematic showing all six sides (front, back, left, right, top, floor) plus doors, corner castings, and cross-members. Surveyors must be able to tap on any component to record its condition using IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) standard damage codes. The EIR must capture container number validated per ISO 6346, survey date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, surveyor ID and certification number, location code per UN/LOCODE (QADOH for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEAUH for Khalifa Port, INNSA for JNPT), and the movement type (gate-in, gate-out, vessel discharge, or vessel load). The digital EIR must be electronically signed by both the surveyor and the truck driver using touch-screen signature capture.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R004	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Real-Time Revenue Dashboard per Voyage	The system must display a real-time revenue per TEU dashboard for each active voyage showing current achieved revenue versus target, broken down by container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 20FR, 45HC), port pair, and booking party. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual toggle. Currency display must default to the user's entity functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with the ability to switch to USD. Dashboard data must refresh within 30 seconds of any booking confirmation and must function in offline mode with the last-synced snapshot clearly timestamped.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R002	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	IICL Damage Code Library and Classification	The system must include a comprehensive IICL damage code library covering all container component groups including shell and panels, structural members, doors and hardware, floor and understructure, and refrigeration equipment. Each damage code must include the component identifier, damage type, location descriptor, and dimensional parameters. The mobile app must provide visual references for each damage type showing examples of acceptable versus rejectable damage levels based on IICL Repair Standards for Freight Containers 7th Edition. The damage code selection interface must support hierarchical navigation: first selecting the container component group, then the specific component, then the damage type. Frequently used damage codes must be accessible through a customizable quick-access panel. The system must support both IICL and UCIRC (Unified Container Inspection and Repair Criteria) classification systems, with the default standard configurable per shipping line preference. All damage descriptions must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R003	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Pre-Trip Inspection (PTI) Survey Workflow	The system must implement a complete Pre-Trip Inspection workflow for refrigerated containers being prepared for export loading. The PTI checklist must cover reefer unit mechanical inspection, electrical connections, thermostat calibration, defrost system operation, air circulation fans, door gasket seal integrity, and drainage system. Surveyors must record temperature set point, actual operating temperatures (supply and return air), and power consumption readings. The PTI result must be classified as pass, conditional pass with noted deficiencies, or fail. Failed PTI containers must be automatically flagged in the Terminal Operating System to prevent loading and must generate a repair estimate in the local currency of the operating entity. Conditional pass criteria must be configurable per shipping line requirements. The system must track PTI completion rates against vessel cutoff times and alert operations when pending PTIs risk missing the loading window. PTI certificates must be generated in a format acceptable to the shipping line's equipment control department.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R004	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	On-Hire and Off-Hire Survey Management	The system must support on-hire and off-hire container survey workflows used when containers are leased between shipping lines or between shipping lines and leasing companies. The on-hire survey must comprehensively document the container condition at the start of the lease period, establishing the baseline against which off-hire condition will be compared. The off-hire survey must identify all new damage incurred during the lease period by comparing against the on-hire record. The system must automatically calculate repair liability by comparing on-hire versus off-hire survey findings and applying the applicable repair standards (IICL or lessor-specific criteria). Repair cost estimates must be generated using configurable labor and material rate tables maintained per entity and currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The mobile app must support side-by-side comparison of on-hire and off-hire photos for each container component. Dispute management must be supported with workflow for lessor approval, lessee objection, and third-party arbitration referral.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R005	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Specialized Container Type Survey Templates	The system must provide specialized survey templates for non-standard container types including open-top containers, flat rack containers, tank containers, and platform containers. Each template must present the unique structural components and inspection points specific to that container type. For tank containers, the survey must include inspection of barrel integrity, valve assemblies, pressure relief devices, and IMO Type certification validity. For flat rack containers, the survey must cover the collapsible end wall mechanisms, lashing rings, and structural member integrity. For open-top containers, the survey must verify tarpaulin condition, top rail integrity, and corner casting accessibility. The system must automatically load the correct template based on the container type code (e.g., 22G1 for standard 20ft dry, 42R1 for 40ft reefer, 22T6 for tank container) as encoded in the ISO 6346 size and type code. Each template must be reviewed and updated at least annually to reflect changes in container manufacturing standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R006	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Damage Measurement and Dimensional Recording	The system must enable surveyors to record precise damage measurements using the mobile device. The app must support measurement input for damage dimensions including length, width, and depth in millimeters. The system should leverage the device camera and augmented reality capabilities to assist with measurement estimation, providing a visual measurement overlay feature. Damage measurements must be validated against IICL acceptable limits: dents must not exceed the configurable threshold (default 25mm depth for panels, 10mm for structural members). The system must calculate the affected area and automatically classify whether the damage exceeds repair-worthy thresholds based on the applicable standard. All measurements must be recorded in metric units as the base system with conversion display available for inches where required by certain shipping line standards. Measurement accuracy must be verified through calibration photos using a reference scale placed adjacent to the damage. The system must maintain measurement history for trend analysis of recurring damage patterns at specific terminal locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R007	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Survey Photo Documentation Standards	The system must enforce standardized photo documentation requirements for container surveys. Each survey must include mandatory overview photos of all four container sides, door end detail, and roof condition. For each recorded damage item, a minimum of two photos must be captured: one overview showing the damage in context on the container and one close-up showing the damage detail with a measurement reference. The mobile app must guide the surveyor through the required photo sequence with visual prompts indicating the expected angle and distance. Photos must be captured at minimum 12 megapixels resolution and must be automatically tagged with the container number, damage code, surveyor ID, GPS coordinates, and timestamp. The system must prevent survey submission if mandatory photos are missing. Photo metadata must be embedded using EXIF standards and must be tamper-evident. All photos must be stored with retention periods of minimum 5 years for standard surveys and 10 years for insurance claim-related surveys, complying with document retention laws across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R011	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Slot Cost Allocation per TEU	The system must calculate the cost per TEU slot for each voyage by allocating vessel operating costs (bunker, port dues at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, canal transit fees for Suez Canal, crew costs, insurance) proportionally across available TEU capacity. The cost allocation must distinguish between laden and empty repositioning slots. The resulting cost per TEU must be compared against revenue per TEU to derive a contribution margin per slot. All cost inputs must support multi-currency entry with conversion to the voyage base currency at the booking exchange rate. The margin analysis must be available at voyage, trade lane, and entity levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R008	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Repair Estimation and Cost Calculation	The system must automatically generate repair cost estimates based on survey findings using configurable rate tables. Rate tables must include labor rates per hour, material costs per component, and equipment rental rates, maintained separately for each operating entity in their local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India). The cost calculation engine must reference IICL Maximum Acceptable Repair Costs (MARC) guidelines as the default ceiling for repair estimates. The system must support both component-based pricing (cost per new door, per new panel) and dimensional pricing (cost per square meter of surface repair). Repair estimates must account for local factors including import duties on spare parts, local labor market rates, and applicable VAT or GST. For cross-entity comparison, the system must convert all estimates to a configurable base currency (default USD) using the daily exchange rate. The mobile app must allow surveyors to adjust estimates with documented justification for variations from standard rates. Approved estimates must integrate with the procurement and invoicing modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R009	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Shipping Line Equipment Control Integration	The system must integrate with major shipping line equipment control systems to exchange survey data, repair authorizations, and container status updates. Integration must support industry-standard EDI messages including CODECO (container gate-in/gate-out), COPARN (container pre-announcement), and DESTIM (damage and repair estimate) via UN/EDIFACT format. The system must also support REST API integration for shipping lines that have adopted modern interfaces. Survey findings must be automatically transmitted to the equipment owner's system within a configurable time window (default 2 hours after survey completion). Repair authorization requests must be routed through the shipping line's approval workflow with support for automatic approval below configurable cost thresholds. Container status updates must reflect survey outcomes including grade changes (food-grade to cargo-worthy to damaged). The system must maintain a directory of shipping line equipment department contacts per entity for escalation purposes. Integration must support the unique system requirements of the top 20 shipping lines operating at Gulf and Indian subcontinent ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R010	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Surveyor Certification and Authorization Management	The system must maintain a registry of surveyor certifications and enforce authorization controls based on qualification levels. Surveyor qualifications must include IICL container inspector certification, reefer technician certification (for PTI surveys), IMDG DG inspector certification, and tank container inspector certification (for ISO tank surveys). The mobile app must restrict access to specialized survey types based on the surveyor's active certifications. Certificate validity periods must be tracked with automated renewal reminders sent 60 days before expiry via push notification and email. The system must record continuing professional development hours for each surveyor as required by certification bodies. For multi-entity operations, surveyor authorization must be managed per terminal with a surveyor potentially certified for Jebel Ali operations but not for JNPT. Performance metrics must track survey accuracy through random quality audits where a senior surveyor re-inspects containers. The system must comply with professional qualification recognition agreements between GCC states and Indian maritime surveyor certification requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R011	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Container Cleanliness and Food-Grade Inspection	The system must provide a specialized cleanliness inspection workflow for containers being certified as food-grade or suitable for specific sensitive cargo. The inspection checklist must cover interior cleanliness (no residues, stains, or odors from previous cargo), structural integrity of interior walls and floor, absence of pest infestation, and verification of food-grade floor coating condition. Surveyors must perform and record the results of standardized tests including white cloth wipe tests, odor assessment using a standardized scale, and moisture readings of the floor and walls. The system must maintain a history of each container's cargo history to identify contamination risks from previous loads (e.g., chemicals, fishmeal, or strong-odored commodities). Food-grade certification must include a validity period (default 30 days) after which re-inspection is required. Certificates must be generated in formats acceptable to food safety authorities including Qatar MOPH, UAE FSSA, KSA SFDA, and FSSAI India. Inspection results must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R012	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Insurance Survey Documentation	The system must support insurance survey documentation workflows for containers involved in incidents such as vessel casualties, terminal accidents, or natural disasters. Insurance surveys must capture enhanced detail including estimated market value of the container, age and remaining useful life assessment, detailed photographic evidence from multiple angles, and surveyor's professional opinion on cause of damage. The mobile app must provide a structured insurance report template that meets the requirements of major marine insurance providers including P&I Clubs and cargo underwriters. Reports must reference applicable insurance terms including INCOTERMS 2020 delivery terms that determine insurance responsibility. The system must calculate depreciated replacement values using configurable depreciation schedules based on container age and type. Insurance survey reports must be generated in PDF format with digital signatures and must include a declaration of surveyor independence and qualifications. Costs must be presented in both local currency and USD equivalent for international insurance claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R013	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Container CSC Plate Verification	The system must enable surveyors to verify and record Container Safety Convention (CSC) plate information during surveys. The mobile app must capture CSC plate details including the date of manufacture, identification number, maximum operating gross mass, allowable stacking weight, racking test load value, and the date of the last examination. The system must flag containers where the CSC examination has expired (examinations are required within 5 years of manufacture and subsequently at intervals not exceeding 30 months under the Approved Continuous Examination Programme or ACEP, or within 5 years under the Periodic Examination Scheme). Expired CSC containers must be flagged as unfit for transport until re-examined. The mobile app must use OCR to read CSC plates and validate the data against the container database. Any discrepancies between the physical CSC plate and database records must be flagged for investigation. The system must maintain CSC examination schedules under ACEP programmes for participating shipping lines and generate examination due reports per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R014	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Survey Workflow Status and Handoff	The system must manage survey workflow status from initiation through completion and approval. Survey states must include requested, assigned, in-progress, completed-pending-review, approved, and disputed. The mobile app must support real-time status updates visible to all stakeholders including the operations team, shipping line representative, and surveyor. Survey assignment must consider surveyor availability, location proximity within the terminal, and specialization match (e.g., reefer surveys assigned to reefer-certified surveyors). Urgent survey requests for vessel-related operations must be flagged with a maximum response time SLA (default 30 minutes). The system must support survey handoff between surveyors when shift changes occur mid-survey, with the receiving surveyor acknowledging the current survey state. Quality review workflows must allow senior surveyors to approve, return for revision, or override findings. All status changes must be timestamped and recorded in the audit trail. Notifications must be sent in Arabic or English based on recipient preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R015	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Survey Report Generation and Distribution	The system must generate professional survey reports in configurable formats suitable for commercial and legal purposes. Reports must include a summary of findings, detailed damage descriptions with photos, repair estimates, surveyor credentials, and digital signature. The report layout must support bilingual generation with Arabic and English content in the same document, with Arabic text properly rendered in RTL format. Report templates must be customizable per entity with company logos, legal disclaimers, and terms and conditions specific to each operating entity. The system must support automatic distribution of completed survey reports to configurable recipient lists including the shipping line equipment department, terminal operations, insurance surveyor, and customs authorities where required. Reports must be generated in PDF/A format for long-term archival compliance. The distribution engine must track delivery confirmation and provide resend capability for failed deliveries. Report numbering must follow entity-specific sequences with configurable prefixes (e.g., QA-SRV for Qatar, AE-SRV for UAE, SA-SRV for KSA, IN-SRV for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R016	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Survey Analytics and Damage Trend Reporting	The system must provide analytics capabilities for identifying damage patterns and trends across the container fleet surveyed at each terminal. The analytics engine must generate reports showing the most common damage types by container age, shipping line, trade route, and terminal handling equipment. Heat maps must visualize damage frequency by container zone (e.g., showing that the right side panel at the 3-meter mark is a common damage location indicating a specific handling equipment issue). Trend analysis must compare damage rates across periods with statistical significance testing. The system must identify correlations between damage patterns and specific yard equipment, crane operators, or handling methods. Cost analytics must present total repair costs per period, average cost per survey, and shipping line comparison, all in the entity's local currency with consolidated USD views for group management. The mobile app must provide summarized analytics views while the full analytics suite is available through the web interface. Reports must support export in Excel, PDF, and BI tool-compatible formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-003-R017	FEAT-050-1-003	MOD-050	Third-Party Survey Integration and Vendor Management	The system must support integration with third-party survey companies that may be contracted to perform surveys on behalf of the terminal or shipping lines. External survey vendors must be registered in the system with their company details, insurance coverage verification, surveyor staff credentials, and authorized survey types. The mobile app must provide a vendor-specific login with restricted access limited to assigned survey tasks and relevant container information only. Third-party survey submissions must go through an additional review and approval step by terminal staff before being finalized. Vendor performance must be tracked including survey completion time, quality audit scores, and dispute rates. Invoicing for third-party survey services must be managed through the system with rate agreements maintained per vendor and entity, with costs recorded in the applicable local currency. The system must enforce data confidentiality by preventing vendor surveyors from accessing other vendors' survey data or terminal-sensitive information. Vendor contracts must be tracked with expiry alerts and renewal workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R001	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline-First Application Architecture	The system must implement an offline-first architecture where all core mobile operations (gate processing, yard inspection, and container survey) function fully without active network connectivity. The mobile app must use a local embedded database (such as SQLite or Realm) to store all operational data required for offline transactions. Upon app initialization, the system must download and cache the minimum required reference data including container inventory, booking references, vessel schedules, and operator configurations for the assigned terminal. The cache must be refreshable on demand and must automatically update when connectivity is available. The app must clearly indicate the current connectivity status (online, offline, or intermittent) using a persistent status bar visible across all screens. The offline architecture must support a minimum of 500 concurrent offline transactions before requiring synchronization. All mobile features must degrade gracefully with offline-appropriate alternatives for any functionality that inherently requires real-time server communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R002	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Local Data Storage and Encryption	The system must store all locally cached data with AES-256 encryption at rest on the mobile device. The encryption key must be derived from the user's authentication credentials and device-specific identifiers, ensuring that data is inaccessible if the device is lost or stolen. The local database schema must mirror the essential tables from the central server including containers, bookings, vessel visits, truck appointments, damage codes, and user authorizations. Sensitive data fields such as driver identification numbers, customs reference numbers, and financial amounts must use field-level encryption with separate key management. The system must enforce a configurable maximum local storage limit (default 2 gigabytes) with automatic pruning of the oldest synchronized data when the limit is approached. Data retention on the device must comply with the data protection regulations of all operating entities including Qatar Data Protection Law, UAE Federal Data Protection Law, KSA Personal Data Protection Law, and Indian Digital Personal Data Protection Act. Remote wipe capability must be available for lost or compromised devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R003	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Conflict Detection and Resolution Engine	The system must implement a robust conflict detection and resolution engine for handling scenarios where the same data is modified both offline on the device and online on the server during a period of disconnection. The conflict resolution strategy must use a configurable hierarchy: server-wins for reference data (container master, vessel schedule), client-wins for field observations (damage records, inspection findings, photos), and manual-resolution for transactional data (gate status changes, hold modifications). When conflicts are detected during synchronization, the system must present a clear conflict resolution interface showing both versions with timestamps and the originating user. Automatic resolution rules must be configurable per entity and data type. The system must maintain a complete conflict log recording both the winning and losing versions with resolution method (automatic or manual) and the resolving user's identity. The conflict rate must be tracked as a KPI with targets below 0.5 percent of all synchronized transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R004	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Incremental Data Synchronization Protocol	The system must implement an incremental synchronization protocol that transfers only changed data between the mobile device and the central server, minimizing bandwidth consumption. Each data record must carry a version vector or logical timestamp to enable precise change detection. The synchronization protocol must support both push (device to server) and pull (server to device) operations independently, allowing partial sync when bandwidth is limited. Delta synchronization must be used for large records where only modified fields are transmitted rather than the entire record. The sync engine must prioritize data by operational urgency: gate transactions and vessel-related operations sync first, followed by inspection findings, then photos and attachments. Synchronization must be resumable, meaning if connectivity is lost during a sync operation, it must continue from where it stopped rather than restarting. The system must compress sync payloads using gzip or equivalent achieving minimum 60 percent compression ratio. Sync progress must be displayed on the mobile app with estimated time to completion and data volume indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R005	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Authentication and Session Management	The system must support offline authentication allowing users to log in and access the mobile app without server connectivity. The last successful authentication token must be cached locally with a configurable validity period (default 72 hours) during which offline login is permitted using the cached credentials. Offline authentication must still require the user's PIN or biometric verification (fingerprint or face recognition) to protect against unauthorized device access. The system must enforce session timeout policies offline with configurable inactivity timeouts (default 15 minutes for gate operations, 30 minutes for yard inspections). When the cached authentication token expires, the user must be restricted to read-only mode until connectivity is restored and re-authentication is completed. The system must track all offline authentication events and report them to the security audit system upon reconnection. Multi-factor authentication challenges that require server validation must be handled gracefully offline by extending the existing session rather than forcing re-authentication. The offline session management must comply with security standards required by each entity's cybersecurity regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R006	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Photo and Attachment Queue Management	The system must implement a dedicated queue management system for photos and large attachments captured during offline operations. Photos captured offline must be stored in a prioritized upload queue that begins processing automatically when connectivity is restored. The queue must support configurable upload priorities: damage photos for active vessel operations at highest priority, gate transaction photos at medium priority, and routine inspection photos at standard priority. The system must implement adaptive upload strategies based on available bandwidth: full-resolution upload on WiFi, compressed upload on cellular with resolution reduction to a configurable level (default 4 megapixels on cellular), and upload suspension on metered connections below configurable speed thresholds. Queue status must be visible to the user showing total pending items, estimated upload time, and progress per item. Failed uploads must be retried with exponential backoff up to a configurable maximum of 5 attempts before flagging for manual intervention. The system must ensure photo integrity by calculating checksums before upload and verifying after server receipt.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R007	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Reference Data Pre-Caching Strategy	The system must implement an intelligent pre-caching strategy that downloads and stores reference data required for offline operations before the user enters areas of poor connectivity. The pre-cache must include vessel visit schedules for the next 48 hours, container inventory for the user's assigned terminal blocks, truck appointment schedules for the current and next shift, damage code libraries, shipping line configurations, and port tariff rate tables in the entity's local currency. The system must analyze historical connectivity patterns to identify poor-coverage zones within the terminal and proactively trigger pre-caching when the user approaches these areas (using GPS geofencing). Cache freshness must be managed with configurable time-to-live values per data type: vessel schedules cached for 4 hours, container positions for 1 hour, and static reference data for 24 hours. The pre-cache size must be optimized per terminal with Hamad Port requiring approximately 150 megabytes, Jebel Ali approximately 400 megabytes given its larger capacity, Khalifa Port approximately 200 megabytes, and JNPT approximately 350 megabytes. The system must alert the user if critical reference data is stale beyond the TTL threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R008	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Transaction Sequencing and Validation	The system must maintain proper transaction sequencing for operations performed offline to ensure business rule integrity upon synchronization. Gate transactions must be sequenced with monotonically increasing local sequence numbers that are reconciled with server-side sequences during sync. The system must validate offline transactions against locally cached business rules including container hold status, customs clearance status, and appointment validity. When business rules cannot be fully validated offline (e.g., real-time customs status check), the transaction must be flagged as provisionally-approved and require server-side confirmation during sync. The system must detect and prevent duplicate transactions such as a gate-in being processed twice for the same container during an offline period. Transaction dependency chains must be maintained: for example, a gate-in must be synced before a corresponding yard placement transaction. The system must handle time-zone aware sequencing for operations that span midnight or involve cross-entity transactions between different time zones (Qatar GMT+3, UAE GMT+4, KSA GMT+3, India GMT+5:30).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R009	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Background Synchronization Service	The system must implement a background synchronization service that operates independently of the foreground mobile application. The background service must detect connectivity changes and automatically initiate synchronization without requiring user intervention. Sync frequency must be configurable with defaults of every 30 seconds when online and immediate sync when transitioning from offline to online. The background service must respect device battery levels, reducing sync frequency when battery drops below 20 percent and suspending non-critical syncs below 10 percent. The service must operate within the platform constraints of both iOS and Android background execution models, using appropriate APIs (WorkManager for Android, BGTaskScheduler for iOS). The sync service must implement a priority queue processing critical gate transactions within 5 seconds of connectivity restoration. Server-side push notifications must be used to trigger immediate sync when urgent data updates are available (e.g., vessel schedule changes, hold status modifications). The background service must log all sync attempts, successes, and failures for troubleshooting connectivity issues at specific terminal locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R010	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Mode UI Indicators and Constraints	The system must provide clear and consistent visual indicators throughout the mobile app interface when operating in offline mode. A persistent banner must display the offline status with the duration of disconnection and the count of pending unsynced transactions. Features that require real-time server connectivity must be visually dimmed with an offline icon and a tooltip explaining the limitation in both Arabic and English. The system must display the age of cached data for each screen, for example showing that the container inventory was last updated 45 minutes ago. When the user attempts an action that cannot be completed offline, the system must queue the action and display a confirmation that it will be executed upon reconnection. The offline UI must adapt for Arabic RTL layouts maintaining the same indicator positioning logic mirrored appropriately. Color coding must follow the terminal's safety color standards with amber for offline advisory and red for critical data staleness warnings. The system must provide a dedicated sync status screen showing all pending transactions, queued actions, and their expected processing order upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R011	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Multi-Device Conflict Prevention	The system must prevent data conflicts arising from the same operation being performed on multiple devices simultaneously during offline periods. The system must implement an optimistic locking mechanism where each transaction acquires a soft lock on the container record when initiated. During offline mode, the system must use device-specific prefixes for transaction identifiers to prevent ID collisions across devices. When synchronization reveals that two devices have processed conflicting transactions for the same container (e.g., two different gate operators processing gate-in for the same container), the system must flag the conflict and present it to a supervisor for resolution. The system must maintain a device registry tracking all active mobile devices per terminal with their last known sync timestamps. Supervisors must have a dashboard view showing which containers have been modified on multiple offline devices. The system must enforce a configurable maximum number of simultaneous offline sessions per terminal (default 50 for Jebel Ali, 30 for Hamad Port, 30 for Khalifa Port, 40 for JNPT) to limit the potential conflict scope.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R012	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Data Integrity Verification	The system must implement data integrity verification mechanisms to ensure that offline transactions are not corrupted during storage or transmission. Each offline transaction must include a SHA-256 hash of its payload calculated at creation time and verified during synchronization. The local database must perform periodic integrity checks using checksums on critical tables, running at configurable intervals (default every 4 hours). If data corruption is detected, the system must isolate the affected records, notify the user, and attempt recovery from the local transaction log. The sync protocol must implement end-to-end integrity verification where the server acknowledges receipt of each transaction with a confirmation hash that the client verifies. The system must maintain a write-ahead log for all offline database operations enabling point-in-time recovery in case of device crash or forced app termination. Data integrity metrics must be tracked per device including corruption rate, recovery success rate, and sync verification failure rate. The system must support forensic export of local database state for troubleshooting persistent integrity issues at specific terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R013	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Bandwidth-Aware Sync Optimization	The system must intelligently adapt synchronization behavior based on the available network bandwidth and connection type. The sync engine must measure available bandwidth in real-time and classify the connection as high-bandwidth (greater than 10 Mbps, typically WiFi), medium-bandwidth (1-10 Mbps, typically 4G cellular), or low-bandwidth (less than 1 Mbps, typically poor cellular or satellite). On high-bandwidth connections, full synchronization including photos at original resolution must proceed. On medium-bandwidth, photos must be compressed to configurable quality (default 70 percent JPEG quality) and lower-priority data deferred. On low-bandwidth, only critical transactional data must sync with all media queued for later upload. The system must detect and handle network transitions gracefully (e.g., moving from WiFi to cellular as a gate operator walks into the yard). Bandwidth usage must be tracked per device and per entity for cost management, particularly for terminals in remote areas where cellular data costs are significant. Monthly bandwidth consumption reports must be generated per entity in their local currency for data plan cost allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R014	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Emergency Procedures	The system must provide emergency operational procedures for extended offline periods exceeding the normal offline capability window. When offline duration exceeds a configurable threshold (default 4 hours), the system must activate extended offline mode which relaxes certain validation requirements to prevent operational paralysis at the terminal. In extended offline mode, gate transactions must be allowed without real-time customs verification but must be flagged for mandatory post-sync customs validation. The system must generate a printable offline operations log in both Arabic and English that can be used as a manual backup if the device becomes inoperable. Emergency contact information for IT support must be cached and accessible offline for each entity. The system must maintain a local copy of critical standard operating procedures (SOPs) for common terminal operations. When reconnecting after an extended offline period, the system must perform a comprehensive reconciliation comparing all offline transactions against the current server state and presenting any operational implications to the supervisor for review before committing. Emergency mode usage must be reported to management with duration and frequency metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R015	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Selective Sync Scope Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure the synchronization scope per user role, terminal, and device type to optimize storage and bandwidth usage. Gate operators must sync only gate-related data for their assigned terminal and current shift. Yard inspectors must sync container inventory data only for their assigned blocks. Surveyors must sync survey-related reference data and their assigned survey queue. The configurable sync scope must include filters for date range (default current day plus or minus 1 day), container status (active containers only versus including historical), and vessel visits (upcoming vessels within a configurable window). Admin users must be able to define custom sync profiles that can be assigned to user groups. The system must calculate and display the estimated local storage requirement for each sync profile, enabling administrators to optimize settings for devices with limited storage capacity. Sync scope changes must take effect at the next sync cycle without requiring app restart. The configuration interface must support both Arabic and English with proper RTL layout for Arabic users. Each entity must be able to maintain independent sync scope configurations reflecting their terminal size and operational patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R016	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Analytics and Connectivity Reporting	The system must collect and report analytics on offline usage patterns and connectivity quality across all terminal locations. The mobile app must log connectivity events including signal strength, connection type, duration of offline periods, and GPS location at the time of disconnection. This data must be aggregated into connectivity heat maps for each terminal showing areas of poor coverage overlaid on the terminal layout map. Reports must identify the average number of offline transactions per shift per terminal, peak offline periods, and the mean time between connectivity losses. The analytics must be used to drive infrastructure investment decisions by quantifying the operational impact of connectivity gaps. The system must calculate an estimated cost of offline operations per entity by measuring the additional supervisor review time and conflict resolution effort required for offline transactions, with costs presented in the entity's local currency. Monthly connectivity quality reports must be generated for IT infrastructure teams with recommendations for coverage improvements. The reporting dashboard must support drill-down from group level through entity level to individual terminal and block level, with all reports available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R017	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Device Management and MDM Integration	The system must integrate with enterprise Mobile Device Management (MDM) solutions to manage the fleet of mobile devices used for terminal operations. The integration must support major MDM platforms including Microsoft Intune, VMware Workspace ONE, and Samsung Knox for ruggedized devices commonly used in port environments. The MDM integration must enable remote app deployment and updates, device configuration management, security policy enforcement, and remote lock and wipe of lost devices. The system must report device health metrics including battery health, storage utilization, operating system version, and app version to the MDM console. Device enrollment must be streamlined per entity with bulk provisioning support for large terminal deployments. The system must enforce minimum device specifications: 4 gigabytes RAM, 64 gigabytes storage, IP67 dust and water resistance rating for yard devices, and screen brightness minimum 500 nits for outdoor readability. Device inventory must be tracked per entity with asset tagging and cost allocation in the entity's local currency. The system must support both Android (minimum version 12) and iOS (minimum version 16) platforms with feature parity across both.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-1-004-R018	FEAT-050-1-004	MOD-050	Offline Notification Queuing and Delivery	The system must implement a notification queuing mechanism that ensures critical notifications are not lost during offline periods. Notifications generated by the server while a device is offline must be queued server-side and delivered in chronological order upon reconnection. The notification queue must have a configurable maximum depth (default 200 notifications) with priority-based retention: critical notifications (safety alerts, hold status changes) are never dropped, while informational notifications may be summarized or consolidated if the queue approaches capacity. Locally generated notifications (offline transaction confirmations, data staleness warnings) must use the device's native notification system to ensure delivery without server connectivity. The system must support notification categories with configurable offline handling: immediate-local-display for safety alerts, queue-for-sync for operational updates, and suppress-until-online for non-urgent communications. Notification delivery confirmation must be tracked with analytics showing the percentage of notifications delivered within target SLAs per entity. The notification system must handle Arabic and English content with proper text direction and must support the formatting of currency amounts (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) and date formats (Gregorian and Hijri) appropriate to the recipient's entity and language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R001	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Multi-angle photo capture with guided overlay	The system shall provide a guided camera interface that prompts the user to capture container damage photographs from at least four standardized angles: front panel, side panel close-up, damage perimeter wide shot, and interior view where applicable. Each capture screen shall display a semi-transparent overlay grid aligned to ISO 6346 container panel zones to ensure consistent framing. The interface shall support both 20-foot TEU and 40-foot FEU container types, adjusting overlay proportions accordingly. Photo resolution must be at minimum 12 megapixels to ensure AI analysis accuracy, and the camera module shall enforce minimum brightness thresholds before allowing capture to prevent unusable dark images in port yard conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R002	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Automatic geotagging and timestamp embedding	Every photograph captured through the damage upload module shall be automatically embedded with GPS coordinates accurate to within 5 meters, device compass heading, and a tamper-proof UTC timestamp. The system shall cross-reference the GPS location against known geofenced areas for Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA) to auto-populate the port code and terminal zone. If the device GPS is unavailable or the location falls outside any registered port geofence, the system shall flag the upload for manual location verification and require the user to select the port and zone from a dropdown before submission proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R004	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Automated repair cost estimation in local currency	Upon AI damage classification, the system shall automatically generate a repair cost estimate based on the IICL damage category, severity level, affected panel area in square centimeters, and the applicable repair labor rate matrix for the port location. Cost estimates shall be calculated and displayed in the local currency of the operating entity: QAR for Qatar operations, AED for UAE operations at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, SAR for KSA operations, and INR for JNPT India operations. The system shall also display a USD equivalent using the daily exchange rate from the ERP treasury module. Estimates shall distinguish between repair-in-place costs and depot repair costs, and shall reference the current container lessor rate schedule where the container is leased rather than owned.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R005	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Offline photo capture with queued synchronization	The mobile application shall support full offline functionality for damage photo capture when network connectivity is unavailable, which is common in remote yard sections of large ports such as Jebel Ali and JNPT. All captured photos, metadata, and user annotations shall be stored in encrypted local device storage with AES-256 encryption. Upon network restoration, the system shall automatically initiate a background synchronization queue that uploads pending damage records in chronological order with retry logic for failed uploads. The offline queue shall support a minimum of 200 damage photo sets before requiring synchronization, and shall display a clear badge indicator showing the count of pending uploads to the user at all times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R006	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Container identification via OCR and BIC code scanning	The system shall include an integrated OCR scanner capable of reading ISO 6346 BIC (Bureau International des Containers) codes directly from the container surface using the device camera. The OCR engine must achieve a minimum 97 percent accuracy rate for clean markings and 90 percent for weathered or partially obscured markings. Upon successful scan, the system shall validate the container number against the check digit algorithm specified in ISO 6346 Annex A and auto-populate the damage report with the container number, size-type code, and owner prefix. If OCR fails after three attempts, the user shall be prompted to enter the container number manually with real-time check digit validation to prevent transcription errors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R007	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Damage severity scoring with visual heat map	The AI assessment engine shall assign a severity score from 1 to 10 for each identified damage instance based on structural impact, cargo protection compromise, and regulatory compliance risk per IICL and CSC (International Convention for Safe Containers) standards. The mobile interface shall render a visual heat map overlay on a schematic container diagram showing damage locations color-coded by severity: green for minor cosmetic damage scoring 1 to 3, amber for moderate structural damage scoring 4 to 6, and red for critical safety-impacting damage scoring 7 to 10. The heat map shall be generated in real-time and stored as part of the damage assessment record for inclusion in survey reports and insurance claims documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R008	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Integration with Equipment Interchange Receipt workflow	Each damage assessment shall be linkable to an Equipment Interchange Receipt (EIR) record within the ERP system. When a damage photo upload is initiated during a gate-in or gate-out process, the system shall automatically associate the damage record with the active EIR transaction for that container at the specified terminal. The linked EIR shall be updated with damage classification codes, severity scores, and estimated repair costs. For containers under lease agreements, the system shall automatically trigger a damage notification to the leasing company via EDI COARRI message format. The integration shall support both real-time linking when online and deferred linking during offline operations, reconciling EIR associations upon synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R009	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Arabic RTL interface for damage annotation	The damage annotation interface shall fully support Arabic right-to-left text rendering for all labels, form fields, damage category descriptions, and user notes. When the device language is set to Arabic, the entire photo annotation toolbar shall mirror to RTL layout with annotation tools positioned on the right side of the screen. Bilingual damage category labels shall be displayed in both Arabic and English simultaneously to ensure consistency in multi-lingual port environments. Free-text damage description fields shall support mixed Arabic and English input with automatic text direction detection. All generated damage reports shall be available in Arabic, English, or bilingual format based on the recipient entity preference configured in the system.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R010	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Photo tampering detection and audit chain	The system shall implement digital watermarking and hash verification on all damage photographs to detect post-capture tampering or manipulation. Each photo shall be assigned a SHA-256 hash at the moment of capture, stored both locally and on the server upon synchronization. Any modification to the image file after capture shall invalidate the hash and flag the record as potentially tampered. The complete audit trail shall record the capturing user identity, device IMEI, capture timestamp, upload timestamp, GPS coordinates, and any subsequent viewing or downloading events. This audit chain is critical for insurance claim disputes, lessor damage liability negotiations, and regulatory compliance inspections conducted by port authorities at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R011	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Bulk damage assessment for multi-container surveys	The system shall support a bulk survey mode allowing surveyors to assess multiple containers in a single session without returning to the main menu between each unit. In bulk mode, the system shall maintain a running list of all containers surveyed with their damage status visible in a scrollable sidebar. The surveyor shall be able to swipe or tap to advance to the next container, with the OCR scanner automatically activating to read the next BIC code. Summary statistics including total containers surveyed, percentage with damage, and aggregate estimated repair costs in the local operating currency shall be displayed at the top of the bulk survey interface. Upon session completion, a consolidated survey report shall be generated and distributed to configured stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R012	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Revenue Maximization Scenario Simulator	The system must provide a scenario simulation tool that allows revenue managers to model what-if scenarios by adjusting variables such as freight rate levels, cargo mix percentages, surcharge amounts, vessel capacity allocation, and fuel price assumptions. The simulator must calculate the projected revenue per TEU outcome for each scenario and allow side-by-side comparison of up to five scenarios simultaneously. Scenarios must be saveable with unique identifiers and sharable across users within the same entity. The simulator must support input in all five system currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and display outputs in the user's selected currency with real-time conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R013	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Reefer container specialized damage assessment	The damage assessment module shall include specialized assessment criteria for refrigerated (reefer) containers covering mechanical unit damage, refrigerant leakage indicators, power cable connector integrity, thermostat display functionality, and insulation panel breaches. For reefer units, the AI model shall be trained to identify specific reefer component failures including compressor housing damage, condenser fin deformation, and drain port blockage. The system shall capture the current temperature reading either via manual input or Bluetooth integration with compatible reefer unit controllers. Reefer damage assessments shall be prioritized in the review queue given the perishable nature of cargo, and shall trigger immediate alerts to the operations control center for containers carrying temperature-sensitive goods such as pharmaceuticals or fresh produce routed through Hamad Port cold chain facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R014	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Insurance claim documentation package generation	The system shall automatically compile a comprehensive insurance claim documentation package from damage assessment data. The package shall include all geotagged photographs with embedded metadata, AI classification results with confidence scores, severity heat maps, repair cost estimates in both local currency and USD, the associated Equipment Interchange Receipt, historical damage comparison results, and the complete audit trail. The documentation shall be formatted as a PDF report with a standardized layout acceptable to major marine insurance underwriters including P&I clubs. The report header shall include entity details for the relevant operating company in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India, with company registration numbers, trade license references, and bilingual Arabic-English content where the operating entity is in an Arabic-speaking jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R015	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Role-based access control for damage workflows	Access to damage assessment functions shall be governed by role-based permissions synchronized from the central ERP identity management module. Roles shall include Gate Inspector with permission to capture and submit damage records, Senior Surveyor with permission to review and approve AI assessments, Operations Manager with permission to authorize repair work orders, and Finance Controller with permission to approve repair cost allocations. Each role shall have entity-level scoping so that a Gate Inspector at Hamad Port cannot view or modify damage records from JNPT operations. Permission escalation for high-value damage exceeding configurable thresholds per entity shall require multi-level approval with digital signature capture on the mobile device.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-001-R016	FEAT-050-2-001	MOD-050	Damage assessment KPI tracking and analytics	The system shall track and expose key performance indicators related to damage assessment operations directly on the mobile interface for supervisory users. KPIs shall include average time from photo capture to AI classification completion, percentage of AI assessments requiring human override, average repair cost per damage category, damage frequency by container owner and size type, and surveyor productivity measured in assessments completed per shift. KPI dashboards shall support filtering by port location, date range with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors, container type including standard dry, reefer, open-top, flat-rack, and tank containers, and operating entity. Data shall refresh in near real-time when the device is online and display cached values with a staleness indicator when operating in offline mode.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R001	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Driver assignment notification and acceptance workflow	The system shall push delivery assignments to registered drivers based on their availability status, current GPS location, and vehicle type compatibility with the container size and cargo requirements. Each assignment notification shall include the container number, pickup location with port terminal gate reference, delivery address, required delivery window, cargo type classification including hazardous goods indicators per IMDG code, and any special handling instructions. Drivers shall have the ability to accept, reject with mandatory reason code selection, or request reassignment within a configurable timeout period defaulting to 15 minutes. Unaccepted assignments shall automatically escalate to the dispatch controller and be re-queued for the next available driver matching the required criteria.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R002	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Electronic Proof of Delivery signature capture	The application shall provide a high-fidelity digital signature capture pad that records the consignee or authorized receiver signature directly on the mobile device touchscreen. The signature capture area shall be a minimum of 300 by 150 pixels with pressure sensitivity where supported by the device hardware. Along with the signature, the system shall record the signer full name as typed, their national ID or company authorization reference, the timestamp in both UTC and local timezone, and the device GPS coordinates at the moment of signing. The signed POD record shall be rendered as an immutable PDF document with the signature embedded as a vector graphic, and shall be cryptographically sealed with a digital certificate to prevent post-signing modification. This POD document serves as the legal delivery confirmation required under GCC commercial law and Indian Carriage of Goods by Road regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R003	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	GPS-based delivery geofence validation	The system shall validate that the driver is physically present at the designated delivery location before allowing POD capture by comparing the device GPS coordinates against a configurable geofence radius around the delivery address. Default geofence radius shall be 200 meters for port terminals and 500 meters for customer warehouse locations, with the ability for dispatchers to adjust per-location. If the driver attempts to capture a POD outside the geofence boundary, the system shall display a warning in both Arabic and English indicating the location mismatch, and shall require the driver to provide a mandatory exception reason such as alternate delivery point directed by consignee or GPS signal obstruction. All geofence validation results including pass or fail status and actual distance from target shall be recorded in the delivery audit log.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R004	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Offline POD capture with deferred transmission	The driver application shall support complete POD capture functionality in offline mode for areas with poor cellular coverage including industrial zones, free trade zones, and remote warehouse districts common around JNPT hinterland and KSA industrial cities. All POD data including signatures, photographs, timestamps, and GPS readings shall be stored in AES-256 encrypted local storage on the device. The offline queue shall support a minimum of 50 POD records before requiring synchronization. Upon connectivity restoration, the system shall automatically transmit queued POD records in FIFO order with exponential backoff retry logic for failed transmissions. A prominent badge on the main screen shall display the count of unsynchronized POD records, and the system shall emit an audible alert if the queue exceeds 24 hours without successful synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R005	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Multi-stop route display with real-time traffic overlay	The system shall display an interactive map showing the driver current assigned route with all pickup and delivery stops plotted as sequenced waypoints. The map shall integrate with a traffic data provider to overlay real-time traffic conditions, estimated travel times between stops, and alternative route suggestions when congestion is detected on the primary route. For routes involving port entry, the map shall display current port gate queue lengths where available from the terminal operating system integration. Route instructions shall be provided with turn-by-turn navigation support in both Arabic and English with automatic language switching based on the driver device language preference. The system shall recalculate ETAs dynamically as the driver progresses and push updated arrival estimates to the consignee notification system and dispatch control center.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R006	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Container pickup verification at port gate	When a driver arrives at a port terminal gate for container pickup, the system shall guide the driver through a verification checklist including confirming the container number matches the assignment via OCR scan or manual entry, verifying the customs release status is cleared by querying the ERP customs module in real-time, confirming the container seal number matches the bill of lading record, and capturing a photograph of the container condition at gate-out. The system shall block pickup confirmation if the customs release status shows the container is still held, under examination, or pending duty payment. For operations at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, the system shall verify Qatar Customs (DHAREEBA) or Dubai Customs clearance status respectively. For JNPT operations, Indian Customs ICEGATE clearance shall be validated before allowing gate-out confirmation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R007	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Delivery exception and rejection recording	The system shall provide a structured workflow for recording delivery exceptions when the consignee refuses delivery or when delivery cannot be completed. Exception categories shall include consignee refused due to cargo damage, consignee refused due to documentation discrepancy, premises closed or inaccessible, incorrect delivery address, container seal broken or tampered, and force majeure events. For each exception, the driver shall be required to capture a minimum of two photographs as evidence, provide a text description in Arabic or English, and select the applicable exception category. The system shall immediately notify the dispatch controller and the booking customer via push notification and email when a delivery exception is recorded. Exception records shall be linked to the original booking and shall trigger the appropriate workflow in the ERP claims or rebooking module depending on the exception type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R008	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Demurrage and detention timer display	The driver application shall display real-time demurrage and detention timers for each assigned container showing the elapsed free time, remaining free time before charges commence, and the applicable per-diem charge rate in the local currency. Free time calculations shall be based on the terms specified in the tariff schedule linked to the booking, which may vary by shipping line, port of discharge, container type, and INCOTERMS delivery basis such as CIF, FOB, or DDP. The timer shall visually escalate from green to amber when 75 percent of free time has elapsed and to red when free time has expired and charges are accruing. Accumulated demurrage and detention charges shall be displayed in both the local operating currency and USD equivalent. This visibility helps drivers prioritize urgent deliveries and assists dispatchers in minimizing demurrage exposure across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R009	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Driver document management and compliance verification	The system shall maintain a digital repository of driver compliance documents including commercial driving license, vehicle registration (Istimara in GCC countries), transport permit, hazardous goods handling certification where applicable per ADR or IMDG regulations, and port-specific access passes for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The application shall proactively alert drivers when any document is approaching expiry within a configurable lead time defaulting to 30 days. Expired or missing mandatory documents shall prevent the driver from accepting new assignments, with the restriction reason clearly displayed in both Arabic and English. Document uploads shall support camera capture and gallery selection with automatic cropping and enhancement. Port access pass numbers shall be validated against the terminal operator access control system where API integration is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R010	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Bilingual delivery note generation with QR code	Upon successful POD capture, the system shall automatically generate a delivery note document in both Arabic and English formatted as a dual-language PDF with Arabic text right-aligned and English text left-aligned on the same page. The delivery note shall include the container number, bill of lading reference, consignee name and address, delivery timestamp, driver name and vehicle registration, and the captured electronic signature. A QR code shall be embedded in the delivery note that when scanned links to the digital POD record in the ERP system for verification purposes. The QR code URL shall be time-limited and require authentication to access. The delivery note shall be automatically emailed to the consignee, the booking party, and the operating entity finance department for invoicing purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R011	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Vehicle pre-trip safety inspection checklist	Before accepting the first assignment of each shift, drivers shall be required to complete a digital pre-trip vehicle inspection checklist covering tire condition, brake function, lights and indicators, twist-lock mechanism integrity for container chassis, coupling pin security, and fire extinguisher presence and validity. Each checklist item shall require a pass or fail selection, and any failed item shall require a photograph and description of the deficiency. Vehicles with critical safety failures including brakes, tires, or twist-locks shall be automatically flagged as unserviceable and removed from the available fleet until a maintenance clearance is recorded. The inspection record shall include the vehicle registration number, odometer reading, and inspection timestamp, and shall be stored for a minimum retention period compliant with GCC road transport authority regulations and Indian Motor Vehicles Act requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R005	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Surcharge and Accessorial Revenue Tracking	The system must track and attribute all surcharge and accessorial revenue components to the per-TEU revenue calculation including but not limited to: Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF), Terminal Handling Charges (THC) at origin and destination, Peak Season Surcharge (PSS), Emergency Bunker Surcharge (EBS), Aden Gulf War Risk Surcharge, Suez Canal Transit surcharge, detention and demurrage charges, and documentation fees. Each surcharge must be individually identifiable in the revenue breakdown per TEU. The system must handle surcharge amounts in the billing currency and convert to the entity functional currency using the rate applicable on the bill of lading date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R012	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Weight bridge integration and overload prevention	The driver application shall integrate with weight bridge systems at port gates and customer premises to receive and display container gross weight readings. The system shall compare the measured gross weight against the maximum permissible weight for the assigned vehicle and chassis combination as well as the road weight limits applicable to the delivery route jurisdiction. For Qatar and UAE operations, the weight limit is governed by Ministry of Transport regulations, while for India the limits follow Motor Vehicles Act axle load specifications. If the measured weight exceeds the permissible limit, the system shall block departure and alert the dispatch controller to arrange for a higher-rated vehicle or partial destuffing. The weight reading shall be recorded on the delivery record and cross-referenced against the Verified Gross Mass declaration per SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R013	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Multi-entity job costing and driver settlement	Each delivery trip shall be costed against the appropriate legal entity based on the operating port and customer contract. The system shall track driver trip kilometers, waiting time at port gates and customer premises, toll charges, and any additional expenses submitted by the driver with receipt photographs. Trip costs shall be calculated in the entity local currency: QAR for Qatar Hamad Port trips, AED for UAE Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port trips, SAR for KSA operations, and INR for JNPT India trips. Inter-entity trips such as a container moving between Khalifa Port in Abu Dhabi and Jebel Ali in Dubai shall be allocated to the originating entity with an inter-company charge to the receiving entity. Driver settlement summaries shall be accessible from the app showing completed trips, earned amounts, pending approvals, and payment history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R014	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Hazardous cargo special handling procedures	For delivery assignments involving hazardous cargo containers classified under IMDG code classes 1 through 9, the driver application shall display mandatory special handling procedures specific to the cargo hazard class. Procedures shall include required personal protective equipment, emergency contact numbers for the relevant civil defense authority in Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India, spill containment instructions, and prohibited co-loading combinations. The driver shall be required to acknowledge reading and understanding the hazardous handling procedures before confirming pickup, with the acknowledgment recorded with timestamp and digital signature. Route restrictions for hazardous cargo including prohibited tunnels, bridge weight limits, and residential area transit bans shall be enforced in the navigation module, automatically routing around restricted zones based on the specific IMDG class and UN number of the cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R015	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Real-time driver location sharing with consignee	The system shall provide consignees with a real-time tracking link showing the driver current location and estimated time of arrival for their delivery. The tracking link shall be sent via SMS and email to the consignee contact registered on the booking when the driver departs the port terminal or previous stop. The tracking page shall display a map with the driver live position updated every 30 seconds, the ETA with dynamic recalculation based on traffic conditions, and the container number being delivered. The tracking interface shall be rendered in Arabic or English based on the consignee language preference stored in the customer master record. The tracking link shall expire automatically 2 hours after delivery confirmation or exception recording. No authentication shall be required to view the tracking page, but the URL shall contain a cryptographically random token to prevent unauthorized access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R016	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Empty container return workflow	The driver application shall support a dedicated workflow for returning empty containers to designated depots or port terminals after destuffing at the consignee premises. The workflow shall guide the driver to the correct return location based on the shipping line empty container return instructions, which may differ by shipping line and port. The system shall verify that the return depot is accepting containers of the specific size type and shipping line before dispatching the driver, querying the depot availability system in real-time where integration exists. Upon arrival at the return depot, the driver shall capture a gate-in photograph and record the container condition at return. The empty return confirmation shall automatically update the container tracking status in the ERP equipment module and stop any detention charges from further accruing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-002-R017	FEAT-050-2-002	MOD-050	Driver performance scoring and gamification	The system shall calculate a composite driver performance score based on weighted metrics including on-time delivery percentage, POD capture completeness and accuracy, pre-trip inspection compliance, fuel efficiency where telematics integration is available, delivery exception rate, and customer feedback ratings. Scores shall be calculated on a rolling 30-day basis and displayed to the driver on their home screen with trend indicators showing improvement or decline. A leaderboard shall display the top-performing drivers per operating entity, with anonymized competitor scores visible to each driver to encourage performance improvement. High-performing drivers shall be eligible for priority assignment of premium deliveries. Performance data shall be aggregated at the entity level for fleet management reporting, with drill-down capability by port location, delivery corridor, and container type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R001	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Multi-entity consolidated revenue dashboard	The executive dashboard shall display consolidated revenue figures across all operating entities with the ability to view in any supported currency: USD for group consolidation, QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, and INR for India entity. Revenue figures shall be broken down by revenue stream including ocean freight, inland transport, customs brokerage, warehousing, and value-added services. Currency conversion shall use the daily closing exchange rate from the ERP treasury module, with the rate timestamp clearly displayed. The dashboard shall show current month-to-date, quarter-to-date, and year-to-date comparisons against budget and prior year, with percentage variance calculations. Entity-level drill-down shall be available by tapping on any consolidated figure to reveal the contributing entity breakdown.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R006	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Cargo Weight vs Volume Revenue Yield Analysis	The system must analyze each booking to determine whether the cargo is weight-breaking or volume-breaking (measurement cargo) and calculate the effective revenue per TEU accordingly. For weight-breaking cargo exceeding the standard payload of 28,000 kg per 40GP or 21,700 kg per 20GP, the system must compute the revenue per weight-ton in addition to per-TEU metrics. The analysis must factor in vessel trim and stability constraints from the stowage plan. Results must be available per booking, per voyage leg, and aggregated per trade lane with multi-currency support and exportable to Excel and PDF formats in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R002	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Real-time vessel and shipment tracking map	The dashboard shall include an interactive world map displaying the real-time positions of all vessels carrying the company managed shipments, sourced from AIS vessel tracking data. Vessels shall be displayed as icons color-coded by status: on schedule in green, delayed by less than 24 hours in amber, and delayed by more than 24 hours in red. Tapping a vessel icon shall display a summary card showing vessel name, voyage number, current speed and heading, next port of call, ETA, and the count of company containers on board with aggregate TEU. The map shall highlight the four primary hub ports of Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with real-time port congestion indicators where available. Map data shall cache locally for offline viewing with a maximum staleness of 4 hours, with a visual indicator showing when data was last refreshed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R003	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	AI-generated daily operations briefing	Each morning at a configurable time per the executive timezone preference, the system shall generate and push an AI-composed daily operations briefing to the executive mobile dashboard. The briefing shall summarize key events from the prior 24 hours including vessels arrived and departed at hub ports, total TEU handled, significant delays or disruptions, revenue booked versus target, outstanding receivables aging alerts, and any compliance or regulatory issues flagged. The narrative shall be generated in the executive preferred language of Arabic or English and shall prioritize anomalies and exceptions over routine operations. The AI shall compare current metrics against historical baselines and highlight statistically significant deviations. The briefing shall include actionable recommendations where the AI identifies optimization opportunities, such as suggesting container repositioning to reduce empty container imbalances between ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R004	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Financial P&L summary with entity comparison	The dashboard shall present a profit and loss summary view displaying revenue, direct costs, gross margin, overhead allocation, and net profit for each operating entity and for the consolidated group. Figures shall be displayable in any of the five supported currencies with real-time conversion. The P&L view shall support period selection including current month, prior month, current quarter, year-to-date, and custom date range with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selector options. A horizontal bar chart shall enable visual comparison of entity performance with gross margin percentage highlighted. Tapping any P&L line item shall drill down to show the constituent cost or revenue categories. Budget variance shall be displayed with color coding: green for favorable variance exceeding 5 percent, amber for variance within plus or minus 5 percent, and red for unfavorable variance exceeding 5 percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R005	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Container throughput and utilization metrics	The dashboard shall display real-time container throughput metrics showing TEU volumes handled per port, per day, week, and month with trend lines and year-over-year comparison. Utilization metrics shall include average container dwell time at each port terminal, equipment utilization rates for owned and leased container fleets, truck fleet utilization percentage, and warehouse capacity utilization at company-operated facilities. Each metric shall show the current value, the trend direction with percentage change, and a sparkline chart showing the trailing 30-day pattern. Metrics shall be filterable by container type including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, and 40FR. Port-specific metrics shall be selectable via a horizontal scrollable tab bar showing Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and an All Ports consolidated view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R006	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Accounts receivable aging and cash flow indicators	The dashboard shall display accounts receivable aging buckets categorized as current, 1 to 30 days overdue, 31 to 60 days overdue, 61 to 90 days overdue, and over 90 days overdue, with amounts shown per entity in local currency and consolidated in USD. A donut chart shall visualize the aging distribution with segments clickable to drill down to the top 10 debtors in each bucket. Cash flow indicators shall show projected cash inflows and outflows for the next 30, 60, and 90 days based on outstanding invoices, scheduled payments, and known commitments. The system shall highlight any entity where the current ratio falls below a configurable threshold defaulting to 1.2, triggering a visual alert on the dashboard. Receivables data shall reflect the latest posted transactions with a maximum data latency of 15 minutes from the ERP general ledger module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R007	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Hijri and Gregorian dual calendar support	All date-related displays, filters, and selectors on the executive dashboard shall support both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar systems with a single-tap toggle between the two. When Hijri calendar mode is active, all date labels, axis labels on charts, period selectors, and report headers shall display dates in the Hijri format. The system shall correctly handle Hijri month boundaries which do not align with Gregorian months, recalculating period-based metrics accordingly. For fiscal year comparisons, the system shall support both Gregorian fiscal year configurations used by the UAE and India entities and Hijri fiscal year as used by KSA government reporting requirements. The Hijri calendar implementation shall use the Umm al-Qura calendar variant as mandated by Saudi Arabian government standards. Date range selections shall allow mixed entry where the start date is specified in Hijri and the end date in Gregorian or vice versa.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R008	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Executive alert escalation and action buttons	The dashboard shall display a prioritized alert feed showing critical operational and financial events requiring executive attention. Alert categories shall include shipment delays exceeding threshold, credit limit breaches by major customers, customs hold notifications, vessel schedule changes affecting hub port operations, driver safety incidents, and regulatory compliance deadlines. Each alert shall include an AI-generated impact assessment estimating the financial or operational consequence in the relevant currency. Action buttons shall be embedded within each alert enabling the executive to approve escalated requests, forward to a department head, request a detailed report, or dismiss with acknowledgment. Alert priority shall be calculated by the AI engine based on financial impact magnitude, time sensitivity, and affected customer tier. Dismissed alerts shall be archived but remain searchable in the alert history for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R009	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Customizable dashboard widget layout	Executives shall be able to customize their dashboard layout by selecting, repositioning, and resizing widgets from a catalog of available dashboard components. The widget catalog shall include all standard metric displays such as revenue charts, throughput gauges, map views, alert feeds, P&L summaries, and receivables aging charts. Widget positioning shall use a responsive grid system that adapts to different mobile device screen sizes and orientations including smartphone portrait, smartphone landscape, and tablet views. Each executive user shall have their layout preferences persisted to their profile and synchronized across devices. A reset to default option shall restore the standard layout. The system shall provide at least three pre-configured layout templates: CEO overview emphasizing financial metrics, COO overview emphasizing operational metrics, and CFO overview emphasizing cash flow and receivables.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R010	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Competitive benchmarking indicators	The dashboard shall display competitive benchmarking indicators comparing the company port throughput and operational efficiency against publicly available industry benchmarks for the Middle East and Indian Subcontinent container shipping corridor. Benchmarks shall include average vessel turnaround time at hub ports compared to published port authority statistics, average customs clearance duration compared to World Bank Doing Business indicators for each operating country, container dwell time compared to regional port averages, and freight rate indices sourced from the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index or Drewry World Container Index. Benchmark data shall be refreshed weekly from configured external data feeds. Where the company performance exceeds the benchmark, a positive indicator shall be displayed; where it falls below, the system shall flag the metric for management attention with a suggested improvement action generated by the AI analytics engine.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R011	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Secure biometric authentication for dashboard access	Access to the executive dashboard shall require biometric authentication using the device native capabilities including fingerprint scanner, facial recognition, or iris scanning where available. The biometric authentication shall be enforced at application launch and after any period of inactivity exceeding a configurable timeout defaulting to 5 minutes. For devices that do not support biometrics, a minimum 6-digit PIN with lockout after 5 failed attempts shall be required. The authentication layer shall integrate with the ERP central identity management system supporting SAML 2.0 and OAuth 2.0 protocols. Session tokens shall expire after 8 hours requiring re-authentication. All dashboard access events shall be logged with device identifier, authentication method, timestamp, and IP address. The authentication system shall comply with Qatar National Information Assurance Policy, UAE Information Assurance Standards, and India CERT-IN security guidelines.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R012	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Offline dashboard with cached data indicators	The executive dashboard shall remain fully navigable in offline mode, displaying the most recently cached data for all widgets and metrics. Each widget shall display a subtle timestamp indicator showing when the data was last refreshed, with the indicator turning amber when data is more than 1 hour stale and red when more than 4 hours stale. Charts, maps, and data tables shall render from locally cached datasets without requiring network connectivity. Upon connectivity restoration, the dashboard shall perform a differential synchronization updating only changed data points rather than reloading the entire dataset, to minimize bandwidth consumption. The offline cache shall be encrypted at rest using the device hardware security module where available. Maximum offline cache size shall be configurable with a default of 100 megabytes, with the system automatically purging the oldest cached data when the limit is approached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R013	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Voice-activated dashboard navigation	The dashboard shall support voice command navigation allowing executives to access specific metrics and views using spoken commands in Arabic or English. Supported voice commands shall include navigation phrases such as show me revenue for Qatar this month, display vessel positions on map, what is our JNPT throughput today, and open accounts receivable aging. The voice recognition engine shall support Arabic dialect variations common in the GCC region including Qatari, Emirati, and Saudi dialects, as well as Indian English accent patterns. Voice commands shall be processed locally on the device where possible to minimize latency, with cloud-based fallback for complex queries. A visual transcript of the recognized command shall be displayed before execution, with a cancel option within 3 seconds. Voice activation shall require a wake phrase or button press to prevent accidental activation during meetings or calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R014	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Scenario modeling and what-if analysis	The dashboard shall provide a scenario modeling tool allowing executives to perform what-if analyses on key business variables. Modifiable parameters shall include freight rate adjustments by trade lane, volume changes by percentage per port, currency exchange rate fluctuations for all five operating currencies, fuel surcharge bunker adjustment factor changes, and staffing level modifications. Upon adjusting parameters, the system shall recalculate projected revenue, costs, and profitability using the AI forecasting engine and display the results alongside the current baseline scenario. Up to five scenarios shall be saveable per user for comparison purposes. The scenario results shall be presentable as a side-by-side comparison table and as overlaid trend lines on revenue and profit charts. This capability enables executives to rapidly assess the financial impact of market changes such as Red Sea shipping disruptions or new trade route opportunities via Hamad Port expansion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R015	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Board and investor report generation from dashboard	The dashboard shall include a report generation function that compiles selected dashboard widgets and metrics into a formatted presentation-ready report suitable for board meetings and investor briefings. The report shall be exportable as PDF and PowerPoint formats with company branding including entity-specific logos and color schemes for each of the four operating entities. Financial figures in reports shall include proper currency formatting with the appropriate symbol and decimal precision for each currency: QAR and SAR with 2 decimals, AED with 2 decimals, INR with 2 decimals, and USD with 2 decimals. The report generator shall support bilingual output with Arabic and English on facing pages or in parallel columns. Charts and visualizations shall be rendered as high-resolution vector graphics suitable for projection display. A report scheduling function shall allow automatic generation and distribution to configured email recipients on a weekly or monthly cadence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-003-R016	FEAT-050-2-003	MOD-050	Real-time regulatory compliance status monitor	The dashboard shall include a compliance status widget displaying the current regulatory standing of each operating entity across key compliance domains. Domains shall include customs bond validity and utilization at Hamad Port Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs (ZATCA), and Indian Customs CBIC, trade license and commercial registration renewal status, VAT registration and filing compliance for UAE and KSA where VAT applies, GST compliance status for the India entity, IATA and FIATA membership renewals where applicable, and port operator license validity. Each compliance item shall show a traffic light status: green for compliant with more than 60 days to next deadline, amber for upcoming deadline within 60 days, and red for overdue or non-compliant. Tapping a compliance item shall show the detailed status including last filing date, next due date in both Gregorian and Hijri, responsible person, and any pending action items from the compliance management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R001	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification channel configuration per user role	The system shall support multiple notification delivery channels including push notification via Firebase Cloud Messaging for Android and Apple Push Notification Service for iOS, SMS for critical alerts, email for detailed notifications, and in-app notification center. Each user role shall have a default channel configuration: drivers shall receive push and SMS for assignment notifications, operations staff shall receive push and in-app for operational alerts, and executives shall receive push only with SMS escalation for critical financial alerts. Users shall be able to override default channel preferences within the bounds allowed by their role configuration. Administrators shall have the ability to enforce mandatory channels for specific notification categories that users cannot disable, ensuring critical safety and compliance notifications are always delivered regardless of user preferences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R002	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Bilingual notification content with dynamic language selection	All push notifications shall be generated and delivered in both Arabic and English, with the display language determined by the recipient user language preference stored in their profile. Notification content shall be dynamically composed using templates that support variable substitution for shipment-specific data such as container numbers, vessel names, port codes, amounts, and dates. Arabic notification text shall be rendered with proper RTL formatting including correct numeral display using Eastern Arabic numerals or Western Arabic numerals based on user preference. For notifications containing financial amounts, the currency symbol and amount formatting shall follow the locale conventions of the recipient: QAR and SAR use comma for thousands and period for decimals, while INR uses the Indian numbering system with lakh and crore groupings. Template management shall allow authorized administrators to modify notification text in both languages without requiring application redeployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R003	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Priority-based notification queuing and delivery	The notification engine shall categorize all notifications into four priority levels: critical for safety incidents and regulatory deadlines, high for shipment delays and financial threshold breaches, medium for routine operational updates and assignment notifications, and low for informational and marketing communications. Critical notifications shall bypass all quiet hour restrictions and be delivered immediately via all configured channels simultaneously. High priority notifications shall be delivered within 30 seconds with retry logic. Medium priority notifications may be batched into digest summaries at configurable intervals. Low priority notifications shall be queued for delivery during the next non-quiet-hour window. The queuing system shall handle a minimum throughput of 10000 notifications per minute across all channels and priority levels. Failed deliveries shall be retried with exponential backoff up to 3 attempts before being logged as undeliverable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R004	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Quiet hours and prayer time notification suppression	The system shall support configurable quiet hour periods during which non-critical notifications are suppressed and queued for later delivery. Quiet hours shall be configurable per user with defaults based on the operating entity timezone: 10 PM to 6 AM local time. Additionally, the system shall integrate with an Islamic prayer time calculation engine to automatically suppress non-critical notifications during the five daily prayer times based on the user GPS location or configured city. Prayer time windows shall be calculated using the Umm al-Qura method for users in KSA, the Qatar Calendar House method for users in Qatar, and the Dubai Astronomy Group method for users in UAE. Each prayer time suppression window shall be configurable in duration defaulting to 20 minutes. During Ramadan, the system shall extend Maghrib prayer suppression to 45 minutes to accommodate Iftar. Users shall have the ability to opt out of prayer time suppression if they choose.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R005	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Entity-aware notification routing and segregation	The notification routing engine shall enforce strict entity segregation ensuring that notifications generated by Qatar entity operations are routed only to users associated with the Qatar entity, UAE entity notifications to UAE users, and similarly for KSA and India entities. Cross-entity notifications shall only be generated for explicitly configured scenarios such as inter-entity container transfers, group-level financial alerts, and consolidated management reports. Each notification payload shall include the originating entity identifier, and the mobile application shall tag and categorize received notifications by entity for users who have access to multiple entities. The notification center in the mobile app shall provide entity-based filtering tabs allowing multi-entity users to view notifications from a specific entity or all entities. Notification routing rules shall be configurable by administrators at the entity level without affecting other entity configurations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R006	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Shipment milestone notification triggers	The system shall automatically generate push notifications for predefined shipment milestone events throughout the container shipping lifecycle. Milestone triggers shall include booking confirmation, shipping instruction submission, bill of lading release, vessel departure from origin port, vessel arrival at transshipment port, vessel departure from transshipment port, vessel arrival at destination port which may be Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, customs clearance completion, container available for pickup, container gate-out for delivery, proof of delivery confirmation, and empty container return confirmation. Each milestone notification shall include the booking reference, container number, current location, and next expected milestone with estimated date. Stakeholders shall be configurable per milestone type: customers may subscribe to all milestones while internal operations staff may subscribe only to exception milestones. Milestone triggers shall operate on data received from carrier EDI messages, terminal operating system events, and customs system status updates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R007	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification delivery confirmation and read tracking	The system shall track the delivery status of every notification sent through each channel. For push notifications, the system shall record delivery confirmation from FCM and APNS indicating the notification reached the device, and shall separately track whether the user opened or interacted with the notification. For SMS notifications, delivery receipts from the SMS gateway shall be recorded. For email notifications, open tracking pixels and click tracking on embedded links shall be implemented. The notification analytics dashboard shall display delivery rates, open rates, and interaction rates segmented by notification category, priority level, user role, and operating entity. Notifications that fail delivery across all configured channels shall trigger an escalation to the designated backup contact for the intended recipient. Unread high-priority notifications older than a configurable threshold defaulting to 2 hours shall trigger a follow-up reminder via an alternative channel.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R008	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Geofence-triggered contextual notifications	The system shall generate contextual push notifications triggered by the user mobile device entering or exiting predefined geofence zones. Geofence zones shall be configured for each port terminal including Hamad Port container yard, Khalifa Port KIZAD free zone, Jebel Ali terminals 1 through 4, and JNPT terminals NSIGT and BMCT. When a driver or field operative enters a port geofence, the system shall push relevant contextual information including their pending assignments at that port, current gate queue estimates, any active weather or safety advisories for the terminal, and customs office operating hours for the day. When exiting a port geofence, the system shall push a summary of completed activities and any pending items requiring follow-up. Geofence monitoring shall use a combination of GPS and cellular network triangulation to minimize battery drain while maintaining positional accuracy sufficient for port terminal zone identification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R009	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification template management with approval workflow	The system shall provide a template management interface allowing authorized content administrators to create, modify, and retire notification templates without requiring developer intervention or application redeployment. Each template shall contain the notification title and body text in both Arabic and English, variable placeholders for dynamic content insertion, the applicable notification category and default priority level, and the recommended delivery channels. Template changes shall go through an approval workflow requiring review by a second authorized user before activation, to prevent accidental mass communication of incorrect content. The template library shall be version-controlled with the ability to rollback to any previous version. Templates shall support conditional content blocks that display different text based on the recipient entity, role, or language preference. A template testing function shall allow administrators to send a preview notification to themselves before activating a template for production use.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R010	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Bulk notification broadcast for operational announcements	The system shall support bulk notification broadcasting for operational announcements that need to reach multiple users simultaneously. Broadcast target audiences shall be selectable by criteria including operating entity, user role, port location, department, and custom user groups. Use cases for bulk broadcasts include port closure announcements due to weather or security events, system maintenance windows, regulatory deadline reminders such as customs duty payment deadlines or VAT filing dates, and company-wide announcements. Bulk broadcasts shall include a scheduling function allowing administrators to set a future delivery time accounting for timezone differences across entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. A delivery progress tracker shall show real-time status of the broadcast including percentage delivered, percentage read, and any delivery failures. Bulk broadcasts shall be rate-limited to prevent overwhelming the notification infrastructure, with a configurable maximum of 5000 recipients per broadcast batch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R011	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	AI-powered notification relevance scoring	The notification engine shall employ an AI relevance scoring model to rank and filter notifications for each user based on their role, current activity context, historical interaction patterns, and the notification content. Notifications scoring below a configurable relevance threshold shall be automatically downgraded to the in-app notification center without triggering a push alert, reducing notification fatigue. The AI model shall learn from user interaction patterns including which notifications are opened immediately, which are dismissed without reading, and which trigger follow-up actions. The relevance model shall account for temporal patterns such as a user who consistently ignores low-priority notifications during peak operational hours but engages with them during off-peak periods. Relevance scores shall be entity-aware, recognizing that a user primarily working on Qatar operations may find JNPT notifications less immediately relevant unless they involve cross-entity shipments. The system shall provide a transparency view allowing users to see why specific notifications were deprioritized and to override the AI filtering for specific categories.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R012	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification history and searchable archive	The mobile application shall maintain a comprehensive notification history accessible through an in-app notification center with search and filter capabilities. The notification center shall display notifications in reverse chronological order with clear visual distinction between read and unread items. Filtering options shall include date range with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors, notification category such as shipment, financial, compliance, or system, priority level, originating entity, and read or unread status. Full-text search shall be supported across notification titles and body content in both Arabic and English. The notification history shall retain records for a minimum of 90 days on-device with older notifications archived to the server and retrievable on demand. Each notification in the history shall include a deep link that when tapped navigates the user directly to the relevant module or record in the ERP mobile application, such as opening the specific booking or delivery record referenced in the notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R007	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Revenue Leakage Detection and Alerts	The system must implement an AI-powered revenue leakage detection mechanism that identifies bookings priced below the dynamic baseline by more than a configurable threshold (default 10%). Alerts must be generated in real-time upon booking confirmation and routed to the revenue management team via in-app notification, email, and optionally SMS. Each alert must include the booking reference, trade lane, container type, quoted rate versus baseline rate, revenue shortfall amount in both USD and entity local currency, and the responsible sales agent. The system must maintain a leakage register with monthly aggregation by entity, trade lane, and sales team for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R013	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Do Not Disturb mode with exception rules	The system shall support a Do Not Disturb mode that users can activate manually or schedule to activate automatically during specified periods. When DND is active, all notifications except those matching configured exception rules shall be silently queued. Exception rules shall be configurable per user and shall support criteria including notification priority level where critical always breaks through, specific notification categories such as safety alerts, specific sender roles such as direct supervisor, and keywords in the notification content such as the word urgent or specific container numbers the user is actively tracking. The DND mode shall display a persistent subtle indicator on the app status bar to remind the user it is active. Queued notifications accumulated during DND shall be delivered as a batched digest when DND is deactivated, sorted by priority with critical items at the top. DND scheduling shall support recurring patterns such as every Friday or weekday evenings to align with weekend patterns that differ across operating entities where Friday is the weekend in Qatar and UAE while Sunday is the weekend in India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R014	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification-triggered quick actions and inline responses	Push notifications shall support actionable buttons allowing users to perform quick actions directly from the notification banner without opening the full application. Supported quick actions shall include approve or reject for workflow approval notifications, accept or decline for driver assignment notifications, acknowledge for alert notifications, and reply for communication notifications. The system shall support inline text response directly from the notification shade for notifications that require a brief text input such as providing a reason for declining an assignment. Quick action responses shall be transmitted to the server even if the full application is not running in the foreground, leveraging background processing capabilities of both Android and iOS platforms. Each quick action execution shall be logged with timestamp, action taken, and device state. For approval actions involving financial amounts exceeding a configurable threshold per entity and currency, the quick action shall require biometric confirmation before processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R015	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Cross-platform notification synchronization	For users who access the ERP system from multiple devices such as a mobile phone and a tablet or from both the mobile app and the web application, the notification system shall synchronize read status, dismissal status, and action completion across all devices in near real-time with a maximum synchronization delay of 10 seconds. When a user reads or dismisses a notification on one device, the notification shall be marked as read or dismissed on all other registered devices. Similarly, when a quick action is performed from a notification on one device, the notification on other devices shall reflect the completed action status and disable redundant action buttons. Device registration shall support a maximum of 5 concurrent devices per user account. The synchronization mechanism shall use WebSocket connections when the application is in the foreground and silent push notifications for background synchronization. Conflict resolution for simultaneous actions on different devices shall follow a last-write-wins strategy with the server timestamp as the authoritative source.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R016	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification compliance and data retention policies	The notification system shall enforce data retention policies that comply with the data protection regulations applicable in each operating jurisdiction including Qatar Data Privacy Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021 on Personal Data Protection, KSA Personal Data Protection Law, and India Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023. Notification records containing personal data such as recipient identity, device identifiers, and location data shall be retained for the maximum period required by any applicable regulation currently 5 years for financial transaction-related notifications and shall be automatically purged after the retention period expires. Users shall have the right to request export of their notification history in a machine-readable format as mandated by applicable data subject rights provisions. The system shall maintain an anonymized aggregate notification analytics dataset that persists beyond individual record deletion for business intelligence purposes. All notification content shall be encrypted in transit using TLS 1.3 and at rest using AES-256 encryption in the notification database.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R017	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Notification performance monitoring and SLA tracking	The system shall continuously monitor notification delivery performance against defined service level agreements. SLA targets shall include: critical notifications delivered within 5 seconds of trigger event, high priority notifications delivered within 30 seconds, medium priority delivered within 5 minutes, and low priority delivered within the next scheduled batch window. A monitoring dashboard accessible to system administrators shall display real-time metrics including current notification queue depth, average delivery latency by priority level and channel, delivery success rate percentage, and any channel-specific outages such as APNS or FCM service disruptions. When delivery latency for any priority level exceeds the SLA threshold for more than 5 consecutive minutes, the system shall generate an automated incident alert to the IT operations team via a separate alerting channel independent of the notification system itself. Monthly SLA compliance reports shall be automatically generated showing performance against targets segmented by operating entity and notification category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-050-2-004-R018	FEAT-050-2-004	MOD-050	Emergency broadcast override for safety-critical events	The system shall provide an emergency broadcast capability that overrides all notification preferences, DND settings, quiet hours, and prayer time suppressions to deliver safety-critical alerts to all users within a specified geographic zone or operating entity. Emergency broadcast triggers shall include port evacuation orders, hazardous material spill alerts at container yards, severe weather warnings such as cyclone alerts for JNPT or sandstorm warnings for Gulf ports, and security threat notifications issued by port authorities. Emergency broadcasts shall be delivered simultaneously via push notification, SMS, and audible alarm on the device regardless of the user notification channel preferences. The emergency notification shall display as a full-screen interstitial on the mobile device that cannot be dismissed without explicit user acknowledgment. The emergency broadcast function shall be restricted to users with the Emergency Coordinator role and shall require two-factor confirmation before transmission to prevent accidental activation. The system shall log complete delivery tracking for emergency broadcasts with response time analysis per recipient for post-incident review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R013	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Revenue KPI Threshold Alerts and Escalation	The system must support configurable revenue per TEU KPI thresholds at entity, trade lane, and voyage levels. When actual revenue per TEU falls below the configured minimum threshold, the system must trigger a tiered escalation workflow: Level 1 alert to the trade lane manager, Level 2 escalation to the regional revenue director if unresolved within 24 hours, Level 3 escalation to the entity CFO if unresolved within 48 hours. All alerts must include contextual data (current revenue per TEU, target, variance, top contributing bookings). Escalation workflows must respect entity working hours and Hijri/Gregorian calendar holidays for KSA and Qatar entities respectively.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R014	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Historical Revenue Trend Analysis	The system must provide historical revenue per TEU trend analysis with configurable time granularity (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually) spanning a minimum of five years of data. Trends must be visualizable as interactive charts with drill-down capability from entity aggregate to trade lane to voyage to individual booking. The system must support year-over-year and period-over-period comparisons with both Gregorian and Hijri period definitions. All historical amounts must be stored in original transaction currency with the ability to restate in any of the five system currencies at either historical or current exchange rates. Chart labels and axis titles must render correctly in both English and Arabic with RTL text direction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R015	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Revenue Data Export and Regulatory Reporting	The system must export revenue per TEU data in formats required by regulatory authorities across all four operating jurisdictions: Qatar Financial Markets Authority (QFMA), UAE Federal Tax Authority for VAT reporting, Saudi ZATCA for e-invoicing and Zakat calculations, and Indian GST reporting requirements under CGST/SGST/IGST framework for JNPT operations. Exports must include all mandatory fields per jurisdiction including tax registration numbers, SAC/HSN codes for India, and VAT treatment codes for GCC entities. The system must support automated scheduled exports (daily, weekly, monthly) and on-demand generation. All exported documents must be available in English and Arabic where jurisdictionally required, and must carry digital signatures conforming to ZATCA Phase 2 Fatoorah requirements for KSA entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-001-R016	FEAT-051-1-001	MOD-051	Offline Revenue Monitoring and Sync	The system must support offline revenue per TEU monitoring for users operating in low-connectivity environments such as onboard vessels or remote port terminals at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. In offline mode, the system must cache the latest revenue dashboards, voyage summaries, and booking-level revenue data locally with a clear indication of the last sync timestamp. Upon reconnection, the system must perform a differential sync to upload any locally created rate adjustments or annotations and download updated booking and revenue data. Conflict resolution for concurrent offline edits must follow a last-write-wins policy with full audit trail logging of all merge decisions. Offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R001	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Cargo Portfolio Template Configuration	The system must allow revenue managers to define cargo portfolio templates specifying target percentage allocations by commodity group (e.g., petrochemicals, consumer electronics, automotive, textiles, foodstuffs, building materials) and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 20FR, 45HC) for each trade lane. Templates must be entity-specific and support seasonal variations with effective date ranges aligned to both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Each template must include minimum and maximum tolerance bands per allocation category. Templates must be version-controlled with full change history showing the modifying user, timestamp, and reason for change. A minimum of three templates must be definable per trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R002	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	AI Cargo Mix Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI engine that analyzes pending booking requests against the defined cargo portfolio template and recommends acceptance, deferral, or rejection of individual bookings to maintain optimal cargo mix. The engine must consider revenue contribution per TEU, commodity compatibility (IMO segregation rules for hazardous goods classes 1-9), reefer plug availability, weight distribution constraints, and customer strategic importance scores. Recommendations must be generated within 5 seconds of a booking inquiry and must include an explanation in both English and Arabic. The engine must learn from historical acceptance decisions to improve future recommendations using reinforcement learning techniques.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R003	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Hazardous Cargo Portfolio Constraints	The system must enforce IMDG Code segregation requirements within the cargo mix by tracking the quantity and classification of hazardous cargo bookings per voyage. The system must prevent overbooking of hazardous cargo beyond vessel-specific capacity limits for each IMO class (1 through 9 including sub-classes 1.1 through 1.6). The portfolio view must display current hazardous cargo utilization as a percentage of maximum allowed per class, with amber warnings at 80% and red alerts at 95% capacity. Constraints must account for specific port restrictions at QAHMD (Qatar Civil Defence regulations), AEJEA (Jebel Ali free zone hazmat rules), and INNSA (Indian Explosives Act and PESO regulations). All hazardous cargo booking decisions must be logged for compliance audit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R004	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Reefer Cargo Allocation Management	The system must manage reefer container slot allocation as a distinct portfolio segment, tracking the number of reefer plugs available versus booked per vessel bay plan. The system must prioritize high-yield reefer commodities (pharmaceuticals, perishable food, chemicals requiring temperature control) based on revenue per reefer TEU contribution. Temperature range requirements (-25°C to +25°C) must be validated at booking against vessel reefer plant capacity. The system must flag potential reefer allocation conflicts when bookings from multiple entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) compete for the same vessel reefer slots. Allocation priority rules must be configurable per entity with override capability requiring manager-level authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R005	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Customer Segment Portfolio Balancing	The system must categorize customers into configurable segments (e.g., BCO - Beneficial Cargo Owners, NVOCCs, Freight Forwarders, Government/SOE, SME) and track the revenue contribution of each segment within the cargo portfolio per trade lane and voyage. The system must allow target segment mix percentages to be defined (e.g., 40% BCO, 25% NVOCC, 20% Freight Forwarder, 10% Government, 5% SME) with tolerance bands. When a segment exceeds its upper tolerance, the system must flag new bookings from that segment for review. Customer segment classification must be maintained per entity with the ability to classify the same customer differently across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities based on local business relationships.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R006	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Commodity Risk Diversification Analysis	The system must perform portfolio risk analysis by evaluating commodity concentration risk per trade lane. If a single commodity group (e.g., petrochemicals from QAHMD or AEJEA) exceeds a configurable percentage threshold (default 35%) of total booked TEUs for a voyage, the system must generate a concentration risk alert. The analysis must factor in commodity price volatility indices, geopolitical risk scores for origin and destination countries, and seasonal demand patterns. Risk reports must be generated in multi-currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) showing revenue-at-risk figures. The system must recommend mitigation actions such as diversifying into alternative commodity groups or adjusting rate levels to attract underrepresented cargo types.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R007	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Empty Container Repositioning Portfolio Impact	The system must integrate empty container repositioning costs into the cargo portfolio management view, showing the net revenue impact after accounting for repositioning costs on imbalanced trade lanes. For trade lanes with persistent imbalances (e.g., higher import volumes at INNSA versus exports), the system must calculate the effective revenue per TEU after netting repositioning costs for empty returns. The system must recommend cargo types and rate levels that could reduce imbalance (e.g., incentivized export rates from India). Repositioning cost calculations must use multi-currency inputs from vessel operating costs, inland transport costs, and depot handling charges at each port, converted to the entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R008	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Real-Time Cargo Mix Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing the current cargo mix for each active voyage compared against the target portfolio template. The dashboard must display percentage allocation by commodity group, container type, customer segment, and hazardous/non-hazardous split using visual indicators (pie charts, stacked bar charts, heatmaps). Deviations from target beyond tolerance bands must be highlighted with configurable color coding (green within tolerance, amber approaching limit, red exceeding limit). The dashboard must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual labels. Filtering must be available by entity, trade lane, vessel, and voyage number. Data must refresh within 60 seconds of booking status changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R009	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Seasonal Cargo Mix Pattern Recognition	The system must use machine learning to identify seasonal cargo mix patterns across trade lanes by analyzing a minimum of three years of historical booking data. Recognized patterns must include Ramadan period demand shifts (using Hijri calendar dates), Diwali peak season for India trade, Chinese New Year manufacturing slowdowns affecting Far East origin cargo, summer peak for reefer produce, and Q4 retail inventory build-up periods. The system must automatically suggest cargo portfolio template adjustments 45 days before identified seasonal shifts begin. Pattern recognition results must be reviewable by revenue managers before automatic template adjustments take effect. All seasonal pattern data must be entity-segregated while allowing cross-entity pattern comparison views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R010	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	INCOTERMS Impact on Cargo Portfolio Revenue	The system must track the INCOTERMS distribution (CIF, FOB, CFR, EXW, FCA, DAP, DPU, DDP) within the cargo portfolio and analyze the revenue impact of INCOTERMS mix. For CIF and CFR shipments where the shipper controls freight, the system must identify revenue optimization opportunities through bundled logistics services. For FOB shipments where the consignee nominates the carrier, the system must track the conversion rate of FOB nominations to actual bookings. The system must calculate the average revenue per TEU differential between shipper-controlled (CIF/CFR) and consignee-controlled (FOB) cargo for each trade lane. Reports must be filterable by entity and must display amounts in entity functional currency with USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R011	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Special Cargo Portfolio Category Management	The system must manage special cargo categories within the portfolio including out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo on flat racks and open tops, break-bulk items requiring special stowage, tank containers for liquid chemicals, and garment-on-hanger (GOH) containers. Each special cargo category must have a dedicated allocation target within the portfolio template with maximum slot limits per vessel. The system must calculate the revenue premium of special cargo versus standard dry containers and display the portfolio revenue uplift from special cargo acceptance. Special cargo booking approvals must follow a separate workflow requiring operations team validation of vessel capability and port handling capability at QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, and INNSA before revenue management approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R012	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Cargo Mix Compliance with Trade Sanctions	The system must validate all cargo within the portfolio against applicable trade sanctions and export control lists including US OFAC SDN list, EU consolidated sanctions list, UN Security Council sanctions, Qatar-specific trade restrictions, UAE Federal sanctions, KSA GDPCTS regulations, and Indian DGFT ITC-HS restricted items. Any booking involving a sanctioned entity, country, or controlled commodity must be automatically flagged and excluded from standard portfolio optimization until cleared by the compliance team. The system must maintain an updated sanctions database with automatic feeds and must log all screening results with timestamps. Portfolio reports must clearly separate cleared versus pending-compliance cargo volumes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R013	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Multi-Entity Cargo Portfolio Consolidation View	The system must provide a consolidated portfolio view across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) for senior management, aggregating cargo mix data while maintaining entity-level drill-down capability. The consolidated view must normalize all revenue figures to a common base currency (configurable, default USD) using daily exchange rates from the treasury module. Entity-specific regulatory restrictions (e.g., Qatar blockade legacy routing constraints, India cabotage rules under Merchant Shipping Act) must be annotated in the consolidated view. Access to the consolidated view must require a specific authorization role, and data must be filtered based on the user's entity permissions. The view must support export to executive presentation formats including PDF and PowerPoint with Arabic and English bilingual layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R002	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Detention and Demurrage Leakage Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor detention and demurrage calculations for all containers across all four operating regions to ensure free-time allowances match contractual terms. The engine must detect cases where free days were incorrectly extended, tariff rates were applied below contracted minimums, or detention charges were waived without proper authorization. For each port pair combination involving Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, the system must validate free-time rules against master service agreements. Multi-currency calculations must handle QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD with exchange rates locked at the booking date or bill of lading date per entity-specific policy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R014	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Cargo Portfolio Performance Benchmarking	The system must benchmark cargo portfolio performance against industry averages and competitor indicators for comparable trade lanes. Benchmarking metrics must include average revenue per TEU by commodity group, reefer utilization rate, special cargo percentage, customer segment diversification index (Herfindahl-Hirschman Index), and cargo mix volatility score. Benchmark data must be sourced from industry reports (Alphaliner, Drewry, Clarksons) with configurable manual override for proprietary intelligence. The system must generate monthly benchmarking scorecards per entity showing relative performance with trend arrows and must flag trade lanes where portfolio performance falls below the 25th percentile of the benchmark group. Scorecards must be available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R015	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Cargo Portfolio Audit Trail and Versioning	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all cargo portfolio management decisions including template changes, AI recommendation overrides, manual booking acceptance or rejection decisions outside portfolio targets, and allocation priority modifications. Each audit entry must record the user identity, entity, timestamp (in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), the before and after state, and a mandatory reason code selected from a configurable list. Audit records must be immutable and retained for a minimum of seven years to comply with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Commercial Companies Law, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013. Audit reports must be searchable and filterable by entity, user, date range, and decision type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-002-R016	FEAT-051-1-002	MOD-051	Offline Cargo Portfolio Access and Decision Logging	The system must support offline access to cargo portfolio dashboards and template configurations for users operating in connectivity-constrained environments. In offline mode, users must be able to view the latest cached portfolio status, record booking acceptance or rejection decisions against the portfolio template, and annotate portfolio deviations. All offline decisions must be queued locally with timestamps and synced to the central system upon reconnection using a conflict resolution mechanism that preserves all decisions chronologically. The offline cache must include the active portfolio template, current voyage booking summaries, and customer segment classifications. Offline data must be encrypted using AES-256 and must automatically purge after 72 hours without sync to prevent stale data usage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R001	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Trade Lane Demand Prediction Model	The system must implement a machine learning demand prediction model for each active trade lane that forecasts cargo volume in TEUs for 30-day, 60-day, 90-day, and 180-day horizons. The model must be trained on a minimum of three years of historical booking data, incorporating features such as seasonal trends, economic indicators (GDP growth rates for Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, and key trading partner countries), fuel prices, currency exchange rates (USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR), and port congestion indices. Predictions must include confidence intervals at 80% and 95% levels. The model must be retrained automatically on a monthly basis with the latest data and must support manual trigger for ad-hoc retraining.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R002	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Commodity-Level Demand Disaggregation	The system must disaggregate trade lane demand forecasts to the commodity group level (minimum 20 commodity groups aligned to HS code chapters: e.g., Chapter 27 Mineral Fuels, Chapter 39 Plastics, Chapter 72 Iron & Steel, Chapter 84 Machinery, Chapter 85 Electronics, Chapter 87 Vehicles). Each commodity forecast must show projected TEU volume, expected revenue per TEU range, and trend direction (growing, stable, declining). The system must identify emerging commodity trends such as increased LNG equipment exports from Qatar or automotive parts from India. Commodity forecasts must account for Ramadan and Eid seasonal impacts using Hijri calendar dates and Diwali/festival impacts for India trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R003	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Port-Pair Demand Heatmap Visualization	The system must generate an interactive demand heatmap displaying forecasted cargo volumes between all active port pairs. The heatmap must use a geographic map overlay showing trade lane routes with color-coded intensity indicating demand levels (low, medium, high, peak). Port pairs must include all combinations involving QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, and INNSA with major global ports (CNSGH, CNNGB, SGSIN, USLAX, USNYC, GBLGP, DEHAM, NLRTM, BEANR, JPYOK, KRPUS, AEJEA-feeder ports). The heatmap must be filterable by time period, commodity group, container type, and entity. Both English and Arabic port name labels must be displayed. The visualization must support touch interaction on tablets and must be printable in A3 format for boardroom presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R004	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	External Data Source Integration for Forecasting	The system must integrate external data sources to enhance demand forecasting accuracy including: global trade databases (UN Comtrade, ITC Trade Map), port statistics from Mwani Qatar, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, and Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority, vessel tracking data (AIS feeds), macroeconomic indicators (IMF World Economic Outlook, World Bank data), commodity price indices (Baltic Dry Index, crude oil futures, LNG spot prices), and weather pattern data affecting agricultural commodity flows. Data ingestion must be automated via APIs where available and support manual file upload (CSV, Excel, JSON) where API access is unavailable. Each external data source must have a configurable refresh schedule and must log data quality metrics including completeness, timeliness, and accuracy scores.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R005	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Forecast Accuracy Tracking and Model Governance	The system must continuously track demand forecast accuracy by comparing predicted TEU volumes against actual booked and shipped TEUs for each trade lane, commodity group, and time horizon. Accuracy metrics must include Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and directional accuracy percentage. The system must maintain a model performance dashboard showing accuracy trends over the past 12 months with entity-level filtering. If MAPE exceeds a configurable threshold (default 20%) for any trade lane for two consecutive periods, the system must automatically trigger a model review alert to the data science team. Model version history must be maintained with full reproducibility metadata including training data snapshots, hyperparameters, and feature importance rankings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R002	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Multi-Level Approval Routing Engine	The system must implement a configurable multi-level approval workflow for change requests that routes based on change type, priority, affected entity, and estimated impact scope. Critical changes affecting customs integration (Qatar Customs TASMU, Dubai Customs Mirsal, Saudi FASAH, Indian ICEGATE) must require approval from both the entity IT manager and the central ERP program director. Changes impacting financial modules involving multi-currency transactions (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) must additionally route through the finance controller of the affected entity. Parallel and sequential approval paths must be configurable per change category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R006	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Scenario Planning with Variable Inputs	The system must provide a scenario planning interface where users can adjust key input variables to generate alternative demand forecasts. Adjustable variables must include oil price (critical for Qatar and GCC economies), currency exchange rate movements, GDP growth rate changes, port capacity constraints (e.g., JNPT expansion, Hamad Port phase 2), new trade agreement impacts (e.g., India-GCC FTA, RCEP), and geopolitical disruption scenarios (e.g., Strait of Hormuz closure, Red Sea routing changes). The system must allow up to ten simultaneous scenarios with side-by-side comparison of demand volume and revenue impact. Scenarios must be saveable, sharable within entity, and presentable in both English and Arabic with amounts in all five system currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R007	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Short-Term Demand Sensing from Booking Pipeline	The system must implement a short-term demand sensing capability that analyzes the current booking pipeline (inquiries, quotations, confirmed bookings, waitlisted bookings) to generate a 7-day and 14-day demand outlook for each trade lane. The sensing algorithm must weight bookings by their pipeline stage (confirmed = 100%, quotation accepted = 80%, quotation pending = 40%, inquiry = 15%) and must adjust weights based on historical conversion rates per customer and trade lane. The demand sensing must run continuously and update every 4 hours. Results must be displayed on the revenue management dashboard with comparison against the medium-term ML forecast. Variances between short-term sensing and medium-term forecast exceeding 15% must trigger an investigation alert.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R008	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Event-Driven Demand Impact Assessment	The system must maintain a calendar of known events that impact shipping demand including trade exhibitions (e.g., Gulfood, Automechanika, GITEX in Dubai), religious observances (Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha based on Hijri calendar, Diwali, Chinese New Year), government fiscal year boundaries (Qatar April, UAE January, KSA January Hijri-aligned, India April), monsoon seasons affecting Indian west coast operations at JNPT, and major infrastructure events (port maintenance windows, canal closures). For each event, the system must estimate the demand impact (positive or negative percentage change) based on historical patterns and factor this into the baseline forecast. Events must be manageable through an admin interface supporting bilingual English and Arabic event descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R009	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Elasticity Modeling per Trade Lane	The system must calculate price-demand elasticity coefficients for each trade lane by analyzing the historical relationship between freight rate changes and booking volume responses. The elasticity model must segment results by customer type (BCO, NVOCC, Freight Forwarder), commodity group, and season. The system must use these elasticity coefficients to project the volume impact of proposed rate changes, enabling revenue managers to optimize pricing decisions. For trade lanes with low elasticity (price-insensitive cargo such as project cargo or time-critical reefer), the system must recommend premium pricing strategies. For high-elasticity trade lanes, the system must recommend competitive pricing to maximize volume. Elasticity reports must display currency-specific rate levels in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R010	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	New Trade Lane Demand Assessment	The system must provide a new trade lane demand assessment tool for evaluating potential new services from or to QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, or INNSA. The tool must estimate potential demand by analyzing bilateral trade statistics between origin and destination countries, existing competing liner service coverage, transit time competitiveness, transshipment hub connectivity (particularly Jebel Ali as a regional hub), and economic development indicators for the trade corridor. The assessment must produce a demand forecast with a 12-month projection at monthly granularity, an estimated revenue per TEU range, a required breakeven utilization percentage, and a risk score. Assessments must be generatable per entity and must support multi-currency revenue projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R011	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Forecast API for Downstream Systems	The system must expose a RESTful API that provides demand forecast data to downstream systems including pricing engines, capacity planning modules, vessel scheduling systems, and equipment management systems. The API must support filtering by trade lane, port pair, commodity group, container type, time horizon, and entity. Response payloads must include forecast TEU volumes, confidence intervals, model version, and forecast generation timestamp. The API must support both JSON and XML response formats. Authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with entity-scoped API keys. Rate limiting must be enforced at 1000 requests per minute per API key. The API must include comprehensive Swagger/OpenAPI documentation in English, and must return appropriate HTTP status codes for error scenarios including 429 for rate limit exceeded.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R012	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Competitor Capacity Impact on Demand Forecasts	The system must factor competitor liner service changes into demand forecasts by tracking competitor vessel deployments, new service launches, service suspensions, and capacity changes on overlapping trade lanes. Competitor data must be sourced from industry databases (Alphaliner, MDS Transmodal) and AIS vessel tracking. When a competitor adds or removes capacity on a trade lane served by the company, the system must adjust the demand forecast to reflect potential market share shifts. The adjustment magnitude must be calibrated using historical data from similar competitive events. The system must maintain a competitor service database with vessel names, capacities, port rotations, and frequency, updated at minimum weekly. Competitor intelligence must be entity-segregated where it pertains to local market conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R013	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Forecast Collaboration Workflow	The system must support a collaborative demand forecasting workflow where sales teams from each entity can submit local market intelligence adjustments to the AI-generated baseline forecast. Sales adjustments must include a volume modifier (positive or negative TEUs), affected commodity groups, justification text (supporting Arabic and English input), and a confidence level (high, medium, low). The system must combine AI baseline forecasts with sales adjustments using a configurable weighting methodology (default 70% AI, 30% sales input). All adjustments must be tracked with the submitting user, entity, timestamp, and approval status. A forecast consensus meeting workflow must be supported where regional managers review and approve the blended forecast for their trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R014	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Anomaly Detection and Root Cause Analysis	The system must implement an anomaly detection mechanism that identifies significant deviations between forecasted and actual demand in near real-time (within 24 hours of variance detection). When actual bookings deviate from the forecast by more than a configurable threshold (default plus or minus 25%), the system must generate an anomaly alert and initiate an automated root cause analysis. The root cause analysis must check for correlations with external factors including port congestion events at QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, or INNSA, competitor rate changes, currency fluctuations, regulatory changes, and weather disruptions. Results must be presented in a structured format with ranked probable causes, supporting evidence, and recommended corrective actions. All anomaly records must be stored for model improvement feedback loops.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R015	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Forecast Data Retention and Compliance	The system must retain all demand forecast data including model inputs, outputs, intermediate calculations, and human adjustments for a minimum of seven years to comply with commercial record retention requirements across all four jurisdictions (Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Commercial Transactions Law, KSA Commercial Court Law, Indian Information Technology Act and Companies Act 2013). Retained data must be stored in a tamper-evident format with cryptographic integrity verification. The system must support legal hold requests that prevent deletion of specified forecast records during litigation or regulatory investigation. Data retention must respect data localization requirements where applicable, particularly for KSA (PDPL) and India (DPDP Act 2023). Archived forecast data must remain queryable and reportable through the standard reporting interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R016	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Offline Demand Forecast Access	The system must allow users to download demand forecast summaries for offline access when traveling or operating in low-connectivity environments. Offline packages must include the latest demand forecasts for the user's assigned trade lanes, historical accuracy metrics, and event calendar data. The offline package must be downloadable in a compact encrypted format (AES-256) and accessible through the mobile application without an internet connection. The offline forecast view must clearly indicate the forecast generation date and must not allow offline users to submit forecast adjustments to prevent stale data conflicts. Offline packages must be automatically refreshed upon the next online connection. The offline interface must support both English and Arabic display with RTL layout switching.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-003-R017	FEAT-051-1-003	MOD-051	Demand Forecast Integration with Vessel Capacity Planning	The system must automatically feed demand forecasts into the vessel capacity planning module to recommend optimal vessel size deployment, sailing frequency adjustments, and blank sailing decisions per trade lane. When forecasted demand for a trade lane drops below 65% of deployed capacity for two consecutive periods, the system must recommend capacity reduction options (blank sailing, vessel swap to smaller tonnage, slot charter-out). When forecasted demand exceeds 90% of deployed capacity, the system must recommend capacity augmentation options (extra loader, vessel upsizing, slot charter-in). Recommendations must include financial impact analysis in the entity's functional currency showing revenue, cost, and margin implications. The integration must respect entity boundaries such that vessel deployment recommendations for Qatar-origin services are authorized by Qatar entity management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R001	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Freight Forward Agreement (FFA) Registration	The system must allow users to register Freight Forward Agreements including both over-the-counter (OTC) bilateral agreements and exchange-cleared FFAs. Each FFA registration must capture: trade lane (port pair using UN/LOCODE), settlement index reference (e.g., Baltic Exchange indices, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index), contract period (start and end dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), contract volume in TEUs, agreed forward rate per TEU in the contract currency, counterparty details, and the legal entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India) executing the contract. The system must validate that the FFA trade lane matches an active service trade lane. Each FFA must be assigned a unique system identifier and must be linked to the corresponding entity's general ledger accounts for financial reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R002	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Long-Term Contract of Affreightment (COA) Management	The system must support the creation and management of Contracts of Affreightment (COAs) that commit specific cargo volumes over multi-year periods (up to five years). Each COA must capture the minimum and maximum annual TEU commitment, rate structure (fixed, floating with index linkage, or hybrid with floor and ceiling), escalation clauses (annual CPI adjustment, bunker price linkage), nominated vessel class or size range, loading and discharge port ranges, laycan windows, and force majeure provisions. The system must track actual liftings against COA commitments and generate variance alerts when utilization falls below 80% or exceeds 110% of the period commitment. COA financial projections must be maintained in the contract currency with cross-rate visibility to the entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R003	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Mark-to-Market Valuation Engine	The system must perform daily mark-to-market (MTM) valuation of all open FFA positions and COA commitments by comparing the contracted rate against the current market forward curve for the corresponding trade lane and settlement period. The MTM engine must source market rate data from recognized indices (Baltic Exchange, Freightos, Platts) and calculate unrealized profit or loss for each position. MTM values must be calculated in the contract currency and converted to the entity functional currency using the daily treasury exchange rate. The system must generate MTM valuation reports that comply with IFRS 9 financial instrument reporting requirements, including hedge effectiveness testing where hedge accounting is applied. MTM reports must be available in both English and Arabic and must be exportable for external auditor review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R004	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Freight Derivatives Position Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive derivatives position dashboard showing all open FFAs, COAs, and any freight options or swaps, aggregated by trade lane, entity, counterparty, and settlement period. The dashboard must display for each position: notional volume (TEUs), contracted rate, current market rate, unrealized MTM gain or loss, remaining contract tenor, and settlement dates. Net position exposure must be calculated per trade lane showing whether the entity is net long (has more buy/charter-in positions) or net short (has more sell/charter-out positions). The dashboard must support multi-currency display with toggle between contract currency, entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and USD. Real-time refresh must occur within 5 minutes of market data updates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R005	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Counterparty Credit Risk Management	The system must maintain a counterparty credit risk register for all FFA and COA counterparties including credit rating (from agencies such as Moody's, S&P, Fitch), credit limit in USD, current exposure, available headroom, and payment history. Before any new FFA or COA is registered, the system must validate that the aggregate exposure to the counterparty does not exceed the approved credit limit. The system must monitor credit rating changes and trigger alerts when a counterparty is downgraded below investment grade (BBB- / Baa3). For counterparties without external ratings, the system must support internal credit scoring based on financial statement analysis. Credit limits must be entity-specific, allowing different exposure thresholds for the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities. Counterparty master data must comply with KYC and AML requirements applicable in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R006	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	FFA Settlement Processing	The system must automate the settlement processing for matured FFA contracts by calculating the settlement amount as the difference between the contracted rate and the settlement index rate, multiplied by the contract TEU volume. Settlement amounts must be calculated in the contract currency and converted to the paying or receiving entity's functional currency at the settlement date exchange rate. The system must generate settlement instructions for the treasury team including counterparty bank details, settlement amount, value date, and payment reference. For exchange-cleared FFAs, the system must record daily margin calls and variation margin payments. Settlement entries must automatically create corresponding accounting journal entries in the entity's general ledger with proper revenue or expense classification under IFRS 15 and IFRS 9.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R007	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Hedge Effectiveness Testing and Documentation	The system must support hedge accounting under IFRS 9 by providing tools for prospective and retrospective hedge effectiveness testing for FFA positions designated as hedging instruments. The system must document the hedging relationship including the hedged item (forecasted freight revenue on a specific trade lane), the hedging instrument (the FFA contract), the risk being hedged (freight rate variability), and the hedge ratio. Prospective effectiveness must be assessed using critical terms matching or regression analysis. Retrospective effectiveness must be measured using the dollar offset method or regression method with a threshold of 80% to 125% effectiveness ratio. If a hedge relationship fails effectiveness testing, the system must generate reclassification entries to move accumulated gains or losses from Other Comprehensive Income (OCI) to profit and loss per IFRS 9 requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R008	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Regulatory Compliance for Derivative Positions	The system must ensure all freight derivative positions comply with regulatory requirements in each operating jurisdiction. For Qatar entity, positions must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority (QFCRA) rules on OTC derivatives. For UAE entity, compliance with Securities and Commodities Authority (SCA) and DFSA regulations for DIFC-based activities. For KSA entity, compliance with Capital Market Authority (CMA) derivative regulations. For India entity, compliance with SEBI regulations on commodity derivatives and Forward Contracts Regulation Act provisions. The system must track regulatory reporting deadlines by jurisdiction, generate required regulatory submissions in the prescribed formats, and maintain an audit trail of all regulatory filings. Position limits per jurisdiction must be configurable and enforced with pre-trade validation checks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R009	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	FFA and COA Contract Lifecycle Workflow	The system must implement a configurable workflow for the full lifecycle of FFA and COA contracts including: deal origination and term sheet creation, internal pricing and risk assessment, legal review and compliance clearance, credit approval, counterparty negotiation tracking, contract execution and registration, ongoing monitoring, amendment processing, and contract expiry or early termination. Each workflow stage must support approval chains with entity-specific authorization matrices (e.g., Qatar entity may require CFO approval above USD 500,000 notional, while India entity threshold is INR 50,000,000). The workflow must support digital signatures for contract execution. Status transitions must be logged with timestamps, approving users, and any attached comments in English or Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R010	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Freight Rate Forward Curve Construction	The system must construct and maintain freight rate forward curves for all major trade lanes by interpolating and extrapolating from available market data points including spot rates, short-term contract rates, FFA settlement prices, and broker assessments. The forward curve must cover a minimum of 24 months forward with monthly granularity. Curve construction methodology must be configurable (linear interpolation, cubic spline, or bootstrapping) and must handle illiquid periods where market data points are sparse. The system must store historical forward curves to enable analysis of curve evolution over time. Forward curves must be used as inputs for FFA valuation, COA pricing, and demand forecasting models. Curve data must be exportable in CSV format and displayed in both tabular and graphical formats with multi-currency support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R011	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Freight Options and Swap Management	The system must support the registration and management of freight options (calls and puts) and freight rate swaps in addition to standard FFAs. For options, the system must capture the option type (call/put, European/Asian), strike price, premium paid or received, expiry date, and underlying index. Option valuation must use the Black-76 model or configurable alternative models with implied volatility sourced from market data. For freight swaps, the system must capture fixed versus floating legs, reset dates, and payment frequencies. The system must calculate the Greeks (delta, gamma, theta, vega) for option positions and display portfolio-level risk metrics. All derivative types must be supported across all four entities with appropriate general ledger mapping and must comply with IFRS 9 classification (fair value through profit or loss, or hedge accounting if designated).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R012	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Revenue Hedging Strategy Recommendation Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered hedging strategy recommendation engine that analyzes the entity's freight revenue exposure per trade lane and suggests optimal hedging strategies using available instruments (FFAs, options, swaps, COAs). The engine must consider the entity's risk appetite (configurable as conservative, moderate, or aggressive), the liquidity of available hedging instruments for each trade lane, the cost of hedging (option premiums, bid-ask spreads), and the correlation between the hedging instrument settlement index and the entity's actual freight rates (basis risk). Recommendations must include the recommended hedge ratio, instrument type, tenor, and estimated hedging cost. The engine must present recommendations in the entity's functional currency with a clear explanation suitable for presentation to entity CFOs and board risk committees, available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R013	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	FFA Portfolio Risk Analytics and Stress Testing	The system must provide portfolio-level risk analytics for all derivative positions including Value at Risk (VaR) calculation using historical simulation, Monte Carlo simulation, and parametric methods with configurable confidence levels (95% and 99%) and holding periods (1-day, 10-day). The system must support stress testing by applying predefined and custom shock scenarios to the portfolio, including scenarios such as a 30% freight rate collapse, Suez Canal closure, oil price spike to USD 150 per barrel, and simultaneous currency depreciation of emerging market currencies. Stress test results must quantify the portfolio-level profit or loss impact in USD and each entity functional currency. The system must generate risk reports conforming to Basel III market risk framework where applicable and must maintain a history of VaR breaches (backtesting) to validate model accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R014	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Multi-Currency Settlement and Conversion	The system must handle FFA and COA settlements in multiple currencies, supporting at minimum USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR, EUR, GBP, CNY, and JPY. For each settlement, the system must determine the settlement currency from the contract terms, calculate the settlement amount in that currency, and provide automatic conversion to the entity functional currency using the exchange rate sourced from the treasury module. The system must support settlement netting for multiple maturing contracts with the same counterparty and currency, generating a single net payment instruction. For cross-entity settlements where one entity's contract is settled through another entity's bank account, the system must generate intercompany settlement entries with proper transfer pricing documentation. All currency conversions must be logged with the rate used, rate source, and timestamp for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R015	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Contract Amendment and Novation Processing	The system must support amendments to existing FFA and COA contracts including rate adjustments, volume modifications, period extensions, port pair changes, and counterparty novations. Each amendment must go through an approval workflow mirroring the original contract approval chain with appropriate authority levels. For novations (transfer of contract from one counterparty to another), the system must perform credit checks on the incoming counterparty, update all position and exposure records, and generate novation confirmation documents. The system must maintain a complete amendment history linking each amendment to the original contract with before-and-after comparison of all modified terms. Amendment effective dates must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar inputs. Financial impact of amendments (change in MTM value, margin requirement changes) must be calculated and displayed before approval in the entity functional currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R016	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Freight Derivative Reporting and Disclosure	The system must generate comprehensive derivative reporting packages for financial statement disclosure under IFRS 7 (Financial Instruments: Disclosures) including: notional amounts outstanding by instrument type and maturity bucket, fair values of derivative assets and liabilities, gains and losses recognized in profit or loss and OCI, hedge accounting relationships and effectiveness results, and credit risk exposure analysis. Reports must be generated per entity for standalone financial statements and on a consolidated basis for group reporting, with intercompany derivative positions eliminated. All reports must be producible in both English and Arabic. The system must also generate board-level risk committee reports summarizing derivative portfolio performance, risk metrics, limit utilization, and policy compliance. Reports must be exportable to PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic numeral and RTL formatting support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R017	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Offline Contract Reference and Position Viewing	The system must support offline access to FFA and COA contract details, current position summaries, and MTM valuations for authorized users operating without internet connectivity. The offline cache must include all active contracts with full terms, the latest MTM valuations with valuation date, counterparty information, and settlement schedules. Users must be able to browse and search contracts offline by trade lane, counterparty, entity, and settlement period. Offline mode must be read-only for derivative positions to prevent unauthorized trade entry without proper connectivity to validation and approval systems. The offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and must require biometric or PIN authentication to access on mobile devices. The cache must auto-refresh upon connection restoration with a clear visual indicator showing the data freshness timestamp and any positions that changed since the last sync.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-1-004-R018	FEAT-051-1-004	MOD-051	Integration with Treasury and General Ledger Systems	The system must integrate bidirectionally with the treasury management module and general ledger system to ensure freight derivative positions are accurately reflected in financial records. Upon FFA or COA registration, the system must create initial recognition entries in the general ledger at fair value. Daily MTM revaluations must generate journal entries for unrealized gains or losses, posting to appropriate accounts based on whether hedge accounting is applied (OCI for effective hedges, profit or loss for trading positions). Upon settlement, the system must generate settlement journal entries reversing unrealized amounts and recognizing realized gains or losses. The integration must support multi-entity posting with intercompany elimination entries for intragroup transactions. All journal entries must include the derivative contract reference, trade lane, and entity code as posting dimensions. The integration must use idempotent posting to prevent duplicate entries during retry scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R001	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Automated Unbilled Charge Detection	The system must automatically scan all completed shipments across entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India to detect charges that were incurred but never invoiced. This includes terminal handling charges (THC), documentation fees, container cleaning fees, VGM verification charges, and hazardous cargo surcharges. The detection engine must compare actual service records from port operations at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA) against issued invoices. Any unbilled item exceeding 1 USD equivalent must be flagged within 24 hours of service completion with a severity classification of critical, high, medium, or low based on amount thresholds configurable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R003	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Surcharge Application Completeness Verification	The system must verify that all applicable surcharges defined in the active tariff schedule have been correctly applied to every shipment. Surcharges include but are not limited to Bunker Adjustment Factor (BAF), Currency Adjustment Factor (CAF), Peak Season Surcharge (PSS), Emergency Bunker Surcharge (EBS), Aden Gulf Surcharge, Low Sulphur Surcharge (LSS), and IMO 2020 compliance surcharges. The verification must occur at booking confirmation, bill of lading issuance, and invoice generation stages. Any missing surcharge must trigger an alert to the revenue management team with the surcharge code, applicable rate, estimated revenue impact in local currency and USD equivalent, and the responsible commercial team member.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R004	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Contract Rate vs Applied Rate Variance Analysis	The system must perform real-time variance analysis comparing the rates applied on each booking against the contracted rates stored in the customer service agreement. Variance thresholds must be configurable per trade lane, customer tier, and entity. For shipments between Gulf ports (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa) and Indian subcontinent (JNPT), tolerance bands must support percentage-based and absolute value thresholds in the billing currency. Any negative variance where the applied rate is below the contracted rate must be flagged immediately with a leakage classification. Positive variances exceeding 15% must also be flagged to prevent customer disputes. The analysis must cover base freight, all surcharges, and ancillary charges independently.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R005	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Revenue Leakage Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The system must provide a comprehensive revenue leakage dashboard displaying total identified leakage by entity, trade lane, customer segment, charge type, and time period. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with full support for Arabic numerals and Hijri calendar date displays alongside Gregorian dates. Currency amounts must display in the entity's local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with USD equivalent columns. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary KPIs to individual leakage incidents. Data refresh intervals must be configurable with a minimum of every 15 minutes. Export functionality must support Excel and PDF formats with proper RTL formatting for Arabic exports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R006	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Freight Rate Undercharge Detection Engine	The system must detect undercharges where the invoiced freight rate for any container movement is below the published tariff rate or the minimum rate floor defined by the commercial team. Detection must account for container types including 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, and 40FR with separate rate floors per equipment type. For reefer containers, the system must also verify that power supply charges, pre-trip inspection fees, and temperature monitoring charges are billed correctly. The engine must process historical data for the trailing 90 days upon activation and then operate in real-time for all new bookings. Results must be categorized by root cause: system error, manual override, contractual ambiguity, or unauthorized discount.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R007	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Automated Leakage Recovery Workflow	The system must provide an automated workflow for recovering identified revenue leakage through supplementary invoicing or credit note adjustments. Upon confirmation of a leakage incident by the revenue analyst, the system must generate a debit note or supplementary invoice in the correct entity's legal format with proper VAT/GST treatment per jurisdiction—5% VAT for UAE and KSA, no VAT for Qatar free zone operations, and applicable GST rates for India (IGST for interstate, CGST+SGST for intrastate). The workflow must include configurable approval chains based on recovery amount thresholds. Recovery invoices must reference the original invoice number, bill of lading number, and booking reference. The system must track recovery success rates and aging of unrecovered leakage per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R008	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Weight and Measurement Discrepancy Revenue Impact	The system must calculate the revenue impact of weight and measurement discrepancies between declared cargo details at booking and verified gross mass (VGM) at the port. For SOLAS VGM compliance at all four operating ports, the system must compare declared weight against VGM certificates and flag any shipment where the actual weight exceeds declared weight by more than a configurable threshold, typically 3%. The revenue impact must be calculated based on the applicable weight-break tariff, and if the higher weight bracket applies, the system must generate an adjustment charge. For measurement-based cargo, the system must compare declared CBM against actual measurements from port survey reports and calculate any revenue shortfall from under-declaration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R009	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Multi-Entity Leakage Consolidation and Reporting	The system must consolidate revenue leakage data across all four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) into a unified reporting framework while maintaining entity-level segregation for audit purposes. Consolidation must convert all local currency amounts to a common reporting currency (USD) using the entity-specified exchange rate source—Central Bank of Qatar for QAR, UAE Central Bank for AED, SAMA for SAR, and RBI reference rate for INR. Reports must present leakage by category, entity, trade lane, and customer with month-over-month and quarter-over-quarter trend analysis. Elimination of inter-company transactions must be handled to prevent double-counting of leakage on voyages where cargo moves between related entities such as Qatar-UAE transhipment operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R010	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Booking Amendment Revenue Impact Tracking	The system must track and quantify the revenue impact of every booking amendment from initial booking to final bill of lading issuance. Amendments that reduce revenue such as container type downgrade from 40HC to 20GP, cargo weight reduction, removal of special equipment requests, or destination port changes to shorter routes must be flagged with the revenue delta. The system must maintain an amendment audit trail showing the before and after values, the user who made the amendment, the timestamp, and the authorization reference if required. Amendments exceeding configurable revenue impact thresholds per entity must require supervisor approval before processing. The system must generate weekly amendment impact reports showing patterns of revenue-reducing amendments by customer, sales representative, and trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R011	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	No-Show and Cancellation Revenue Leakage	The system must track revenue leakage from booking no-shows and late cancellations where cancellation fees or dead-freight charges are not collected as per the contractual terms. For each booking that results in a no-show at the port of loading—whether at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT—the system must calculate the applicable cancellation penalty based on the customer's service agreement or the published tariff cancellation policy. The system must differentiate between force majeure cancellations documented with supporting evidence, customer-initiated cancellations within and outside the free cancellation window, and carrier-initiated cancellations. Revenue leakage from uncollected dead-freight charges for partial container load shipments where booked slots went unfilled must also be quantified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R012	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Port-Specific Charge Validation Rules Engine	The system must maintain a configurable rules engine for port-specific charges applicable at each operating port. For Hamad Port in Qatar, rules must cover Qatar Customs clearance fees, Mwani Qatar terminal charges, and QTerminals handling tariffs. For Jebel Ali, rules must validate DP World handling charges, Dubai Trade documentation fees, and JAFZA storage charges. For Khalifa Port, Abu Dhabi Ports tariff schedules and KIZAD logistics charges must be validated. For JNPT, rules must cover JNPT terminal operators' tariff schedules including BMCT, NSIGT, GTI, and APMT charges, Indian Customs EDI charges, and port health clearance fees. Each rule must specify the charge code, applicable rate, unit of measure, and effective date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R013	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Offline Revenue Leakage Detection Cache	The system must support offline revenue leakage detection for field operations teams at port locations where network connectivity may be intermittent. The offline module must cache the last 72 hours of booking data, applicable tariff schedules, and customer contract rates for the specific port location. When operating offline, the system must perform basic rate validation and flag potential leakage items that are queued for server-side confirmation upon reconnection. The offline cache must be encrypted using AES-256 encryption to protect commercially sensitive rate information. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all locally detected leakage flags with the central server, resolve any conflicts using a last-write-wins strategy with manual review for conflicting items, and update the local cache with any tariff or contract changes that occurred during the offline period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R014	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Customer Credit Note Abuse Detection	The system must detect patterns of excessive or suspicious credit note issuance that may indicate revenue leakage through improper adjustments. Detection rules must flag customers receiving credit notes exceeding a configurable percentage of their total billing in any rolling 30-day period. The system must also identify credit notes issued without corresponding service failure documentation, credit notes approved by the same person who raised the complaint, and clusters of credit notes just below approval thresholds that suggest threshold gaming. For each flagged pattern, the system must calculate the cumulative revenue impact across all entities, generate an investigation case with all supporting evidence, and route the case to the internal audit team. Historical pattern analysis must cover the trailing 12 months with the ability to extend to 24 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R015	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Automated Leakage Root Cause Classification	The system must automatically classify each identified revenue leakage incident into predefined root cause categories using rule-based logic enhanced by pattern recognition. Root cause categories must include: system configuration error, tariff update delay, contract interpretation gap, manual data entry error, unauthorized discount, process bypass, training deficiency, and third-party billing error. The classification must consider the charge type, the nature of the variance, the user involved, the port of origin and destination, and historical patterns for similar leakage types. Each classification must carry a confidence score. Classifications with confidence below 70% must be routed for manual review. Quarterly root cause analysis reports must be generated per entity showing the distribution of leakage by root cause with trend analysis and recommended corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-001-R016	FEAT-051-2-001	MOD-051	Real-Time Leakage Alert Notification System	The system must deliver real-time alerts for detected revenue leakage events through multiple notification channels including in-application notifications, email, SMS, and integration with Microsoft Teams or Slack. Alert severity levels must be configurable with separate thresholds per entity and currency—for example, critical alerts for leakage above 10,000 USD equivalent, high for 5,000 to 10,000 USD, medium for 1,000 to 5,000 USD, and low for below 1,000 USD. Each alert must contain the leakage type, affected booking or invoice reference, estimated revenue impact in local and USD currency, the responsible department, and a direct link to the leakage detail screen. Alert escalation rules must ensure unacknowledged critical alerts are escalated to the next management level within 4 hours. Arabic language alert templates must be maintained alongside English templates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R001	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Rate Deviation Approval Workflow Configuration	The system must provide a configurable multi-level approval workflow for any rate deviation from published tariffs or contracted rates. Approval levels must be defined based on the percentage deviation from the base rate with entity-specific thresholds—for example, deviations up to 5% may be auto-approved for senior sales managers, 5-10% requires regional commercial director approval, 10-20% requires entity head approval, and above 20% requires group commercial director approval. The workflow must support parallel and sequential approval chains. Each approval request must include the customer name, trade lane, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, etc.), proposed rate versus tariff rate, revenue impact projection, competitive justification, and volume commitment. Approval must be completed within configurable SLA timeframes before the booking cutoff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R002	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Tariff Rate Lock and Version Control	The system must enforce strict version control on all published tariff rates with full audit trail of every change. Each tariff version must record the effective date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, the user who published the change, the approver, and the business justification. Active tariff schedules must be locked against modification without going through the formal change management workflow. The system must support advance publishing of future tariff rates with scheduled activation dates, allowing the commercial team to prepare rate changes for upcoming seasons. Rollback capability must exist to revert to any previous tariff version within 15 minutes if errors are discovered. For GCC ports (Hamad, Jebel Ali, Khalifa), tariff currency is typically USD while for JNPT tariffs may be in INR with USD equivalents. The system must maintain tariff history for a minimum of 7 years per regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R003	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Discount Authority Matrix Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive discount authority matrix defining the maximum discount percentage or absolute amount each role can approve per trade lane, container type, and customer tier. The matrix must be configurable separately for each operating entity with the ability to set different authorities for Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, and India operations. Authority limits must be defined in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD conversion for cross-entity comparison. The system must prevent any user from approving a discount that exceeds their authorized limit regardless of manual overrides or system workarounds. Temporary authority delegation must be supported for leave coverage with a defined expiry date, and all delegated approvals must be logged with both the delegator and delegate identifiers. The matrix must be reviewed and re-approved quarterly by entity management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R004	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Spot Rate Control and Monitoring	The system must control and monitor all spot rate quotations issued outside of standard service agreements. Each spot rate must be assigned a unique reference number, tied to a specific vessel voyage and port pair, and given a validity period not exceeding 14 days unless extended by an authorized approver. Spot rates below the minimum rate floor must be blocked unless approved through the deviation workflow. The system must track the conversion rate from spot rate quotations to confirmed bookings and flag trade lanes where spot rate booking ratios exceed configurable thresholds, indicating potential rate integrity issues. For seasonal trade lanes such as the pre-Ramadan cargo surge from India to GCC ports, the system must enforce seasonal minimum rate floors that override standard floors. All spot rate activities must be visible on a centralized monitoring dashboard with filtering by entity, port pair, and sales team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R005	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Named Account Rate Protection	The system must protect contracted rates for named accounts by preventing any rate application below the agreed contract rate without explicit deviation approval. Named account contracts must specify rates per container type per trade lane with validity periods, volume commitments, and rate escalation clauses. The system must alert the commercial team 60 days before contract expiry to initiate renewal negotiations. When a booking is made for a named account, the system must automatically apply the contracted rate and block any manual rate entry below the contract floor. If the published tariff rate is lower than the contracted rate at the time of booking, the system must apply the lower rate only if the contract includes a most-favored-customer clause, otherwise the contracted rate must be applied. Rate protection must extend to all surcharges explicitly mentioned in the contract.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R006	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Volume Commitment Discount Tier Enforcement	The system must enforce volume-based discount tiers as defined in customer service agreements, applying the correct discount rate based on actual cumulative volume shipped during the contract period. Discount tiers must be calculated using TEU volumes with automatic conversion from container counts—one 20-foot container equals 1 TEU, one 40-foot container equals 2 TEU. The system must recalculate the applicable tier monthly and apply retroactive adjustments if a customer crosses a tier boundary. For multi-entity customers shipping through Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must support both consolidated volume counting across entities and entity-specific counting based on contract terms. If a customer falls below their committed volume at contract period end, the system must calculate the clawback amount representing the difference between the discount received and the discount earned based on actual volume, and generate a debit note for the shortfall.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R007	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Rate Audit Trail and Forensic Analysis	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail of every rate-related action including rate queries, quotations, approvals, rejections, modifications, and applications to bookings. The audit trail must capture the user identity, timestamp in UTC with local time zone conversion, IP address, action type, before and after values, and the business justification entered by the user. Forensic analysis tools must allow authorized auditors to search and filter rate actions by date range, user, customer, trade lane, and rate deviation magnitude. The system must support generation of audit reports showing all rate actions for a specific booking from initial quotation through final invoicing. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 10 years to meet regulatory requirements across all four operating jurisdictions and must be exportable in structured formats for external auditor consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R008	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Competitive Rate Intelligence Integration	The system must integrate competitive rate intelligence data from authorized market rate sources to provide context for rate deviation decisions. When a rate deviation request is submitted, the system must display the current market rate range for the specified trade lane and container type from sources such as Freightos Baltic Index, Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, or Drewry benchmarks. The competitive positioning of the proposed rate must be shown as a percentile within the market range. Rate approval authorities must be able to see whether the proposed discounted rate is above or below the market median. The system must update competitive rate data at least weekly and flag trade lanes where the entity's rates are more than 10% above market median, indicating potential volume loss risk, or more than 15% below market median, indicating potential revenue leakage from underpricing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R009	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Unauthorized Rate Override Prevention	The system must implement technical controls preventing unauthorized rate overrides at every point in the booking-to-invoice cycle. Direct database manipulation of rate tables must be detected through database audit triggers and reported immediately to IT security and commercial management. API-level rate submissions must validate against the approved rate for the booking reference and reject any discrepancy. The system must prevent rate changes after bill of lading issuance unless processed through a formal amendment workflow with approval. For EDI-integrated customers submitting bookings via INTTRA or CargoSmart, the system must validate submitted rates against contracted rates and reject bookings with rate discrepancies, returning appropriate EDI error codes. Screen-level controls must disable rate input fields for users without rate modification authority and log all attempts to access restricted rate functions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R010	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Blanket Rate Increase Management	The system must support blanket rate increase campaigns such as General Rate Increases (GRI) and Peak Season Surcharges (PSS) with configurable effective dates, applicable trade lanes, and customer notification workflows. When a GRI is announced, the system must automatically update all affected tariff schedules on the effective date, recalculate any open quotations or provisional bookings that fall after the effective date, and generate customer notification letters in English and Arabic. The notification must include the current rate, new rate, effective date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, and applicable trade lanes. Customers with rate protection clauses in their contracts must be automatically excluded from the GRI application and flagged for manual review. The system must track the revenue impact of each GRI campaign showing projected versus actual revenue uplift per trade lane and entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R011	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Intermodal Rate Component Integrity	The system must ensure rate integrity across all intermodal rate components including ocean freight, inland haulage origin, inland haulage destination, and any door-to-door service components. Each rate component must be independently validated against its respective floor rate, and the total door-to-door rate must also pass a combined floor validation. For shipments involving inland transportation to or from JNPT with ICD connections to locations such as ICD Tughlakabad, ICD Sabarmati, or ICD Nagpur, the inland component rates must reflect current trucking and rail tariffs. For GCC inland movements from Jebel Ali to Dubai warehouse districts or from Khalifa Port to Abu Dhabi industrial zones, last-mile delivery rates must be validated against contracted transporter rates. Margin analysis must be available for each rate component showing the contribution margin per component per container type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R012	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Special Cargo Premium Rate Enforcement	The system must enforce premium rate schedules for special cargo categories including hazardous goods (IMO classes 1-9), out-of-gauge cargo, reefer containers requiring temperature control, breakbulk on container vessels, and project cargo. Premium rates must be defined as a percentage uplift or absolute surcharge above the standard rate per container type and trade lane. The system must verify that every booking containing special cargo has the appropriate premium applied and must block bookings where the applied rate equals the standard cargo rate for the identified special cargo type. For hazardous cargo, the premium must be applied per IMO class with higher premiums for Class 1 (explosives) and Class 7 (radioactive) materials. Reefer premiums must account for the specific temperature setting requested, with deep-freeze cargoes below minus 18 degrees Celsius carrying higher premiums than standard chilled cargo.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R013	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Rate Parity Across Booking Channels	The system must enforce rate parity across all booking channels including direct sales, freight forwarder portals, NVOCC agreements, online booking platforms, and EDI submissions. A rate approved for a specific customer and trade lane must be consistently applied regardless of the booking channel used. The system must detect and flag cases where the same customer receives different rates for the same trade lane and container type through different channels within the same validity period. Rate parity exceptions must only be permitted through approved channel-specific discount programs with documented commercial justification. The system must generate a monthly rate parity analysis report per entity comparing rates across channels for the top 50 customers by revenue, highlighting any anomalies exceeding a configurable tolerance threshold of typically 2%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R014	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Freight Forwarder Override Rate Controls	The system must implement specific controls for freight forwarder rate management recognizing that freight forwarders negotiate on behalf of multiple shippers. Forwarder master rates must be defined at the forwarder account level, and the system must prevent forwarder sub-accounts from receiving rates below the master rate without explicit approval. The system must track the volume commitments made by each forwarder against actual utilization and flag forwarders whose utilization falls below 70% of committed volume for two consecutive months. For large forwarders operating across multiple entities such as those with offices in Dubai, Doha, and Mumbai, the system must support consolidated rate agreements with entity-specific execution. Rate confidentiality must be enforced such that forwarder-specific rates are never visible to competing forwarders or to the beneficial cargo owners who ship through the forwarder.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R015	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Rate Validity Period Enforcement	The system must strictly enforce rate validity periods ensuring that no booking can be confirmed using an expired rate quotation or a rate outside its defined validity window. The system must check the rate validity at booking confirmation time and again at bill of lading issuance, applying the rate valid at the earlier of the booking date or the vessel ETD as configured per entity policy. For quotations nearing expiry within 48 hours, the system must send automated reminders to both the sales team and the customer. Expired quotations must be archived but remain accessible for reference in rate negotiation history. When a customer requests extension of an expired rate, the system must route it through the standard rate approval workflow regardless of whether the rate was previously approved. Grace period functionality must allow a configurable extension of 24 to 72 hours for bookings in progress where the rate expires during the booking process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R016	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Rate Manipulation Pattern Detection	The system must employ pattern detection algorithms to identify potential rate manipulation behaviors by internal users. Patterns to detect include: a single user consistently approving rates at the maximum threshold of their authority suggesting systematic boundary exploitation, rates being lowered immediately before a specific customer's regular booking pattern, repeated rate corrections after invoice issuance for the same customer, and rates being set artificially high then discounted to appear as a customer benefit. The detection engine must analyze rate actions across a rolling 90-day window and generate anomaly scores for each user's rate management behavior. Users with anomaly scores exceeding the configurable threshold must be flagged for supervisory review. The system must maintain detection results confidentially, accessible only to authorized compliance and audit personnel, and must support export of evidence packages for disciplinary or legal proceedings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-002-R017	FEAT-051-2-002	MOD-051	Emergency Rate Floor Override Protocol	The system must provide a controlled emergency protocol for overriding rate floors in exceptional market conditions such as severe market downturns, trade route disruptions, or regulatory-mandated rate changes. The emergency override must require dual authorization from both the entity commercial head and the group CFO or their designated delegates. Each emergency override must specify the affected trade lanes, container types, revised floor rates, justification, and an automatic expiry date not exceeding 90 days. The system must log the override activation and automatically revert to standard rate floors upon expiry unless explicitly renewed through the same dual authorization process. During an active emergency override, the system must generate daily monitoring reports comparing booking volumes and revenue against the pre-override baseline. All emergency overrides must be reported to the board audit committee within 5 business days of activation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R001	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Voyage Percentage-of-Completion Revenue Accrual	The system must calculate revenue accruals for each active voyage based on the percentage-of-completion method as required by IFRS 15 for over-time revenue recognition in container shipping. The completion percentage must be computed using the ratio of voyage days elapsed to total estimated voyage days for each vessel on each route. For voyages between Hamad Port and JNPT, the system must track vessel position using AIS data integration to determine actual completion percentage rather than relying solely on scheduled dates. Revenue for each booking on the voyage must be accrued proportionally based on the voyage completion percentage at the accounting period end date. The system must handle voyages spanning two or more accounting periods, correctly splitting revenue between periods. Accrual amounts must be calculated in the booking currency and converted to the entity's functional currency using the period-end exchange rate for balance sheet items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R002	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Multi-Leg Voyage Revenue Allocation	The system must allocate revenue across multiple voyage legs for shipments involving transhipment at intermediate ports. For example, a shipment from JNPT to Hamad Port transhipping at Jebel Ali must have its revenue allocated between the JNPT-Jebel Ali leg and the Jebel Ali-Hamad Port leg based on a configurable allocation method. Supported allocation methods must include distance-based pro-ration, cost-based pro-ration, and management-defined fixed percentages per trade lane. When the shipper and transhipment carrier are different entities within the group, inter-company revenue allocation must follow transfer pricing policies defined per entity pair. The system must generate inter-company invoices or journal entries for each voyage leg with proper elimination entries for consolidated financial reporting. Each allocation must be auditable with the ability to trace from the total customer invoice amount back to individual leg accruals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R003	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Period-End Revenue Accrual Journal Generation	The system must automatically generate period-end revenue accrual journal entries for each entity at month-end, quarter-end, and year-end closing. Journal entries must be formatted according to each entity's chart of accounts structure and must include proper segmentation by trade lane, vessel, voyage number, and revenue type (freight, surcharges, ancillary). For the Qatar entity, journal entries must comply with Qatar Financial Markets Authority reporting requirements. For the UAE entity, entries must align with UAE Federal Tax Authority VAT reporting periods. For the KSA entity, entries must conform to ZATCA e-invoicing and Zakat requirements. For the India entity, entries must comply with Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS 115) with proper GST treatment. The system must support both auto-posting to the general ledger via ERP integration and export of journal entry files for manual import. Reversal entries for the subsequent period must be automatically generated alongside the accrual entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R004	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Unbilled Revenue Tracking and Aging	The system must track all unbilled revenue representing services performed but not yet invoiced across all four operating entities. Unbilled revenue must be categorized by reason: pending bill of lading release, pending destination charges confirmation, pending weight or measurement adjustment, and pending customer rate agreement finalization. An aging analysis must classify unbilled revenue into current (0-30 days), overdue (31-60 days), significantly overdue (61-90 days), and critical (over 90 days) buckets. For each aging bucket, the system must calculate the estimated collectibility percentage based on historical collection patterns per entity and customer tier. The system must generate daily unbilled revenue reports in both English and Arabic with amounts shown in entity local currency and USD equivalents. Automated alerts must be triggered when unbilled revenue for any single customer exceeds configurable thresholds or when the total entity unbilled revenue exceeds budget parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R005	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Deferred Revenue Management for Advance Payments	The system must manage deferred revenue arising from advance freight payments received before the performance obligation is satisfied—that is, before the vessel sails or before the cargo is delivered depending on the INCOTERMS delivery point. For prepaid freight terms common on CIF and CFR shipments, the advance payment must be recorded as deferred revenue upon receipt and recognized proportionally as the voyage progresses. The system must maintain a deferred revenue schedule per booking showing the total amount received, the amount recognized to date, and the remaining balance. For customers making lump-sum advance payments covering multiple bookings, the system must allocate the payment across individual bookings using the standalone selling price method per IFRS 15. Deferred revenue balances must be reported per entity with currency breakdowns showing QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD components. Month-end deferred revenue reconciliation reports must be generated automatically.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R006	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Revenue Recognition for Combined Service Contracts	The system must handle revenue recognition for combined service contracts that bundle ocean freight with additional services such as customs clearance, inland transportation, warehousing, and cargo insurance. Under IFRS 15, the system must identify distinct performance obligations within each combined contract and allocate the total transaction price to each obligation based on relative standalone selling prices. The system must maintain a standalone selling price database for all service components updated quarterly per entity. Revenue for each performance obligation must be recognized independently—ocean freight over the voyage duration, customs clearance at the point of clearance completion, inland transport upon delivery, and warehousing over the storage period. For contracts denominated in currencies different from the entity's functional currency, revenue must be translated at the exchange rate on the date each performance obligation is satisfied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R007	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Accrual Variance Analysis and Adjustment	The system must perform automated variance analysis comparing accrued revenue against actual invoiced revenue upon voyage completion and bill of lading finalization. Variances must be categorized as timing differences that self-correct in subsequent periods versus permanent differences requiring adjustment entries. The system must automatically generate adjustment journal entries for permanent variances with proper approval workflow based on the adjustment amount. Variance thresholds must be configurable per entity with typical tolerances of plus or minus 2% for timing differences and zero tolerance for permanent differences above a minimum materiality threshold. A monthly accrual accuracy report must show the percentage variance between accrued and actual revenue by voyage, trade lane, and entity. Persistent variances exceeding thresholds for three consecutive months must trigger a review of the accrual estimation methodology and parameters. Historical variance data must be retained for trend analysis and accrual model improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R008	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Multi-Currency Revenue Accrual Translation	The system must handle revenue accrual translation for multi-currency operations where bookings may be invoiced in USD, the entity's functional currency may be QAR, AED, SAR, or INR, and the group reporting currency is USD. Translation must follow IAS 21 requirements: revenue transactions must be translated at the exchange rate on the date of the transaction or a weighted average rate for the period if rates do not fluctuate significantly. Balance sheet items such as accrued revenue receivables must be translated at the period-end closing rate. Translation differences arising from the difference between transaction date rates and period-end rates must be recognized in other comprehensive income for foreign operations. The system must maintain exchange rate tables sourced from the entity-designated central bank with daily rate updates. For the Indian entity, where INR experiences greater volatility, the system must support hedged rate applications where forward contract rates are used instead of spot rates for designated hedged revenue streams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R009	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Revenue Accrual for Detention and Demurrage	The system must calculate and accrue revenue for detention and demurrage charges as they accumulate on a daily basis rather than only at the time of invoicing. For each container exceeding its free-time allowance at any of the four operating ports, the system must calculate the daily demurrage charge based on the applicable tariff rate for the container type and size, applying the correct currency (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, INR for JNPT). The accrual must begin on the first chargeable day after free-time expiry and continue until the container is returned or released. The system must apply an estimated collectibility factor to detention and demurrage accruals based on historical collection rates per port, per customer tier, and per container dwell time duration. Write-off provisions must be calculated for demurrage accruals exceeding 90 days uncollected using entity-specific provisioning rates approved by finance management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R010	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Voyage Cost Accrual for Margin Calculation	The system must accrue voyage costs alongside revenue accruals to enable real-time voyage margin calculation. Cost accruals must cover bunker fuel consumption estimated from vessel AIS speed and consumption curves, port charges at each call port based on vessel tonnage and published port tariff schedules, canal transit fees for Suez Canal transits common on Europe-Gulf-India routes, stevedoring charges based on container moves, and vessel charter costs pro-rated per voyage. The cost accrual percentage must match the revenue accrual percentage-of-completion to ensure proper matching under IFRS principles. For owned vessels, daily running costs including crew, insurance, and maintenance provisions must be accrued on a time-apportioned basis. For chartered vessels, charter hire must be accrued per the charter party terms. Voyage margin reports must be available in real-time showing revenue, cost, and margin at the individual booking, port pair, and total voyage levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R011	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Intercompany Revenue Elimination for Consolidation	The system must automatically generate intercompany revenue elimination entries required for consolidated financial reporting when cargo moves between related entities. For a shipment booked by the Dubai entity and transhipped through the Qatar entity's allocation at Hamad Port, the system must identify the intercompany revenue component, match it against the corresponding intercompany cost in the receiving entity, and generate elimination journal entries. The elimination process must handle currency differences when the originating entity invoices in AED and the receiving entity records costs in QAR. Elimination entries must be generated at each period-end closing and must balance to zero across the group after considering exchange rate differences which must be separately reported. The system must provide a full reconciliation report showing each intercompany transaction pair, the revenue and cost amounts in original currencies, the translated amounts, and any unmatched items requiring manual investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R012	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Revenue Accrual Audit and Compliance Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive audit-ready reports supporting revenue accrual compliance with IFRS 15 disclosure requirements. Reports must include a disaggregation of revenue by type of service, geographic region, timing of recognition (point-in-time versus over-time), and customer type. Contract balance disclosures must show opening and closing balances for contract assets (accrued revenue), contract liabilities (deferred revenue), and receivables with movements during the period. The system must produce these reports separately for each operating entity and on a consolidated basis. For the Qatar entity, reports must additionally comply with Qatar Exchange listing requirements if applicable. For the India entity, reports must include Ind AS 115 specific disclosures required by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs. Reports must be available in both English and Arabic and must support Hijri date headers for KSA regulatory submissions. All report data must be traceable to source transactions with drill-down capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R013	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Automated Revenue Reclassification Rules	The system must support automated revenue reclassification rules to ensure revenue is recorded in the correct general ledger account based on the nature of the service. Reclassification rules must map charge types to revenue accounts—ocean freight revenue, surcharge revenue, detention and demurrage revenue, inland transport revenue, and agency commission revenue must each be posted to separate accounts per the entity's chart of accounts. When a booking changes from a standard shipment to a special cargo classification such as hazardous or reefer, the system must automatically reclassify the accrued revenue to the correct account. Reclassification entries must be generated as journal entries with clear references to the original posting and the reclassification reason. Monthly reclassification activity reports must be generated for finance manager review, and any manual reclassification requests must go through an approval workflow to prevent misclassification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R014	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Revenue Accrual Reversal on Booking Cancellation	The system must automatically reverse any accrued revenue when a booking is cancelled, whether the cancellation occurs before vessel departure, during the voyage for cargo offloaded at an intermediate port, or upon customer dispute resolution. The reversal must be posted in the period in which the cancellation event occurs, not backdated to the original accrual period, to comply with accounting standards. Cancellation revenue reversals must be classified separately in financial reports to distinguish operational revenue changes from accrual adjustments. For partial cancellations where a multi-container booking is reduced, the system must proportionally reverse the accrued revenue for only the cancelled containers while maintaining accruals for the remaining containers. The system must reconcile total cancellation reversals against cancellation fee revenue to report the net revenue impact of cancellations per trade lane, per entity, and per period. Cancellation reversal entries must include the cancellation reason code for management analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R015	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Tax-Adjusted Revenue Accrual for VAT and GST	The system must calculate revenue accruals net of applicable indirect taxes and maintain separate accruals for the tax component. For UAE and KSA entities subject to 5% VAT, the system must accrue the net revenue and VAT liability separately based on the tax point which may differ from the revenue recognition point. For the India entity, GST accruals must distinguish between IGST for interstate movements and CGST plus SGST for intrastate movements, with rates varying by service type—ocean freight export is zero-rated, inland transport is 12% or 18% GST depending on service classification, and customs agency services are 18% GST. For the Qatar entity, which currently has no VAT, the system must be configurable to accommodate future VAT introduction. Tax accruals must be reconciled against tax return filing data monthly. The system must generate tax-adjusted revenue reports showing gross revenue, tax component, and net revenue per entity for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-003-R016	FEAT-051-2-003	MOD-051	Revenue Accrual Estimation Model Calibration	The system must provide tools for periodic calibration of revenue accrual estimation models to improve accuracy over time. Calibration must analyze historical accrual-to-actual variances across at least 12 months of data per trade lane, per container type, and per customer segment. The calibration engine must identify systematic biases in estimation such as consistent over-accrual on specific trade lanes or under-accrual for certain surcharge types and recommend parameter adjustments. Proposed calibration changes must be presented in a review interface showing the current parameters, proposed parameters, historical variance under current parameters, and projected variance under proposed parameters. Calibration changes must be approved by the entity finance controller before taking effect. The system must maintain a calibration change log recording all parameter changes with effective dates for audit trail purposes. Re-calibration must be prompted automatically when accrual variance exceeds entity-specific thresholds for two consecutive months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R001	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Dynamic Pricing Recommendation Engine	The system must implement an AI-driven dynamic pricing engine that recommends optimal freight rates for each trade lane, container type, and booking date combination. The engine must consider current vessel capacity utilization, historical demand patterns including seasonal surges such as pre-Ramadan import peaks to GCC ports and pre-Diwali export surges from JNPT, current fuel prices affecting cost base, competitive rate intelligence from market indices, and the customer's price sensitivity derived from historical booking behavior. Recommendations must be generated in real-time when a rate query is submitted and must include the recommended rate, a confidence interval, the projected acceptance probability, and the estimated revenue impact compared to the current tariff rate. The engine must support rate recommendations in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with automatic conversion. Each recommendation must include an explainability score showing the top three factors influencing the recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R002	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Vessel Capacity Yield Optimization	The system must optimize revenue yield per vessel voyage by dynamically adjusting rate recommendations based on remaining capacity and time to vessel cutoff. As the booking window closes and capacity fills, the engine must progressively increase recommended rates for remaining slots following a configurable yield curve. The yield curve must account for the specific vessel capacity in TEU, the number of port calls remaining where additional cargo can be loaded, and the weight versus volume utilization balance. For vessels calling at multiple ports such as JNPT, Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and continuing to other destinations, the engine must optimize across all port pair combinations on the voyage to maximize total voyage revenue rather than optimizing each leg independently. The system must model the opportunity cost of accepting a lower-value booking now versus the probability of receiving a higher-value booking later, using historical fill rate data per trade lane and vessel service.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R003	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Customer Lifetime Value Scoring Model	The system must maintain an AI-driven customer lifetime value (CLV) model that scores each customer based on their projected long-term revenue contribution. The CLV model must incorporate historical revenue generated, booking frequency and consistency, payment behavior and average days sales outstanding, volume growth trajectory, mix of cargo types with preference for higher-margin special cargo, geographic spread of shipments across entity ports, and responsiveness to rate increases. The CLV score must be available to sales teams during rate negotiations and integrated into the rate deviation approval workflow such that higher-CLV customers receive more favorable consideration for rate deviations. The model must be recalculated monthly using the latest 24 months of transactional data. CLV scores must be presented in entity local currency and USD with segmentation into platinum, gold, silver, and bronze tiers based on configurable score thresholds per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R004	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Demand Forecasting for Capacity Planning	The system must generate AI-powered demand forecasts for each trade lane on a weekly and monthly horizon to support proactive capacity management and pricing decisions. Forecasting models must incorporate historical booking volumes with at least three years of training data, seasonal patterns aligned with both Gregorian and Hijri calendars to capture Ramadan, Eid, and Hajj-related trade fluctuations, macroeconomic indicators for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India including GDP growth, trade balance data, and industrial production indices. The forecast must be disaggregated by container type (dry, reefer, special) and customer segment (direct shipper, freight forwarder, NVOCC). Forecast accuracy must be measured using mean absolute percentage error with a target of below 15% for weekly forecasts and below 10% for monthly forecasts. The system must present forecasts visually with confidence intervals and highlight weeks where demand is projected to exceed available capacity, triggering proactive rate increase recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R005	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Cargo Mix Optimization Algorithm	The system must implement a cargo mix optimization algorithm that recommends the optimal combination of cargo types per voyage to maximize revenue while respecting vessel operational constraints. The algorithm must consider revenue per TEU for each cargo type, stowage requirements including hazardous cargo segregation rules per IMDG Code, reefer plug availability on the vessel, weight distribution requirements for vessel stability, and special equipment needs such as flat racks and open tops. For vessels calling at Jebel Ali where transhipment cargo competes with local import cargo for slots, the algorithm must evaluate the total network revenue contribution of transhipment cargo versus the direct revenue from local cargo. The optimization must run automatically when bookings reach 60% of vessel capacity and produce a recommendation showing current versus optimized cargo mix with the projected revenue uplift. Results must be presented as actionable booking acceptance and rejection recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R006	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Competitor Rate Response Modeling	The system must model likely competitor rate responses to pricing changes on key trade lanes to prevent revenue-destructive rate wars. The model must analyze historical patterns of competitor rate movements following the entity's rate changes on trade lanes between India and GCC ports where multiple carriers compete. Input data must include published rate indices, market share estimates from port authority statistics, competitor vessel deployment patterns, and customer feedback captured by the sales team. When recommending a rate change, the engine must simulate potential competitor responses and estimate the resulting market equilibrium rate, market share impact, and net revenue effect over a 30, 60, and 90-day horizon. The system must flag trade lanes where initiating a rate decrease carries high risk of competitive retaliation and recommend alternative strategies such as service differentiation or targeted discounting to specific customer segments rather than broad rate reductions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R007	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Revenue Anomaly Detection and Alerting	The system must deploy AI-based anomaly detection models to identify unusual revenue patterns that may indicate emerging issues or opportunities. Anomalies to detect include sudden drops in booking volumes on a specific trade lane, unexpected shifts in cargo mix, individual high-value bookings significantly above normal patterns that may indicate one-time project cargo opportunities, deteriorating revenue per TEU trends, and unusual patterns in surcharge waivers or discounts. The anomaly detection must operate on real-time streaming data with detection latency not exceeding 30 minutes from the triggering event. Each detected anomaly must be classified by type, assigned an impact score in USD equivalent, and routed to the appropriate team—commercial for pricing anomalies, operations for volume anomalies, and finance for billing anomalies. The system must learn from user feedback on false positives to continuously improve detection accuracy with a target false positive rate below 15%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R008	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Seasonal Revenue Strategy Recommendation	The system must generate seasonal revenue strategy recommendations based on predictive analysis of cyclical trade patterns in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent markets. Key seasonal patterns must include Ramadan preparation periods with increased food and consumer goods imports to GCC from India, post-Eid furniture and household goods movements, monsoon season impacts on JNPT cargo volumes from June through September, Qatar National Day and UAE National Day related import surges, Indian festival season from Navratri through Diwali driving exports, and summer peak construction material imports to GCC. For each identified seasonal pattern, the system must recommend a pricing strategy including rate adjustment magnitude and timing, target customer segments, promotional offers for off-peak periods, and capacity redeployment suggestions. Recommendations must be generated at least 45 days before the anticipated seasonal shift to allow implementation. Historical seasonal strategy performance must be tracked showing the revenue achieved versus the recommended strategy projection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R009	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Overbooking Optimization Model	The system must implement an AI-powered overbooking optimization model that recommends the optimal overbooking percentage per vessel voyage to compensate for expected no-shows and cancellations while minimizing the risk of cargo rollover. The model must analyze historical no-show and cancellation rates per trade lane, customer segment, season, and booking lead time. The overbooking recommendation must balance the revenue opportunity from filling expected no-show slots against the cost of rolling over confirmed cargo including customer compensation, empty container repositioning, and relationship damage. The model must calculate the optimal overbooking level that maximizes expected net revenue per voyage with a configurable risk tolerance parameter. For premium customers identified through the CLV model, the system must guarantee slot protection even under overbooking scenarios. Overbooking alerts must be displayed to booking agents showing current booking level versus physical capacity and recommended capacity with color-coded risk indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R010	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Contract Renewal Revenue Impact Simulation	The system must provide AI-powered simulation tools for evaluating the revenue impact of service agreement renewal terms during customer contract negotiations. When a named account contract is up for renewal, the system must simulate multiple scenarios varying rate levels, volume commitments, discount tiers, surcharge inclusions, and contract duration. Each simulation must project the expected revenue over the contract period considering historical volume patterns, market trend forecasts, and the customer's CLV trajectory. The simulation must compare the proposed renewal terms against the current contract, against the published tariff rate, and against the average rate for similar customers on the same trade lanes. Negotiation guardrails must be established showing the minimum acceptable terms per entity commercial policy. The system must present simulation results in both Arabic and English with currency amounts in the contract currency and entity functional currency. Simulation scenarios must be saveable for collaborative review during negotiation meetings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R006	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Feeder Service Schedule Synchronization	The system must synchronize feeder service schedules with mainline mother vessel schedules to ensure valid transshipment connections at hub ports including Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), and Hamad Port (QAHMD). Minimum connection times must be configurable per hub port and per container type — for example, 24 hours for dry containers and 12 hours for reefer containers at Jebel Ali due to dedicated reefer handling infrastructure. The AI engine must continuously monitor feeder-mainline alignment and proactively alert when schedule changes on either side break established transshipment windows. The system must calculate total transit time from origin to destination including feeder legs, hub dwell time, and mainline transit, presenting results in both calendar days and working days excluding port-specific holidays for each entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R011	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Empty Container Repositioning Revenue Optimization	The system must optimize revenue from empty container repositioning by identifying revenue-generating backhaul opportunities on trade lanes with directional imbalance. For the India-to-GCC trade lane where exports from JNPT to Gulf ports typically exceed imports resulting in empty container surplus at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port, the system must recommend discounted rates that cover at least the marginal cost of repositioning while generating incremental revenue. The optimization must consider repositioning costs including vessel slot cost, terminal handling, and inland transport, the forecasted container demand at destination ports, the lead time for repositioning, and alternative repositioning options such as off-hiring leased containers. The system must generate weekly repositioning revenue opportunity reports per entity showing estimated revenue gain from offering promotional rates on imbalanced trade lanes versus the cost of deadheading empty containers. Promotional rates must be flagged as repositioning-driven and excluded from standard rate analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R012	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Revenue Impact Assessment for Service Changes	The system must automatically assess the revenue impact of proposed service changes such as adding or removing port calls, changing vessel deployment, adjusting service frequency, or modifying transit times. When a service change is proposed for routes involving any of the four operating ports, the AI engine must estimate the revenue impact by analyzing current booking volumes at affected ports, customer switching probability based on transit time changes, competitive service offerings on the affected trade lanes, and the reallocation of demand to alternative services. The assessment must produce a net revenue impact in USD and local currencies over a 6 and 12-month horizon with optimistic, baseline, and pessimistic scenarios. For each scenario, the system must identify the top 10 at-risk customers by revenue and the top 10 potential customer gains. The assessment must be generated within 48 hours of service change submission and must be updated weekly as booking patterns evolve post-implementation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R013	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	AI Model Performance Monitoring and Retraining	The system must implement comprehensive monitoring of all AI and machine learning model performance with automated retraining triggers. Key performance metrics must include prediction accuracy measured as mean absolute error for rate recommendations, forecast bias measured as the systematic tendency to over-predict or under-predict, model drift detection using statistical tests comparing recent prediction distributions against training data distributions, and business impact metrics showing actual revenue achieved versus model-recommended revenue. Each model must have a performance dashboard accessible to data science and commercial teams showing daily, weekly, and monthly metrics. When any model's performance degrades below configurable thresholds for three consecutive evaluation periods, the system must trigger an automated retraining pipeline using the latest available data. Model retraining must include validation against a holdout dataset before deployment. All model versions must be tracked with full lineage from training data to production deployment for audit and compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R014	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Revenue Scenario Planning Workspace	The system must provide an interactive scenario planning workspace where commercial managers can model revenue outcomes under different market assumptions. The workspace must support creation of multiple named scenarios with adjustable parameters including global trade growth rates, oil price ranges affecting bunker costs, currency exchange rate assumptions for QAR, AED, SAR, INR against USD, port congestion levels at each operating port, and competitive capacity additions or withdrawals. Each scenario must produce a projected revenue waterfall chart showing the impact of each parameter change on total entity and group revenue. Scenarios must be sharable across entity teams with role-based access control. The workspace must support side-by-side comparison of up to four scenarios with variance highlighting. The interface must be fully functional in both English and Arabic with RTL layout support for Arabic users. Scenario outputs must be exportable to Excel with proper formatting and to PDF for board presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R015	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Real-Time Revenue Optimization Recommendations Feed	The system must provide a real-time feed of AI-generated revenue optimization recommendations to commercial team dashboards, mobile applications, and notification channels. Recommendations must be categorized as pricing opportunities where rates can be increased on high-demand lanes, capacity alerts where vessel utilization triggers yield management actions, customer actions where specific customers should be targeted with tailored offers, and market intelligence where competitive movements create opportunities. Each recommendation must include a clear actionable suggestion, the estimated revenue impact in USD and local currency, a confidence level expressed as a percentage, the recommended implementation timeframe, and an accept or dismiss action for the user. The system must track recommendation acceptance rates and actual revenue outcomes to continuously improve recommendation quality. A maximum of 20 active recommendations per entity per day must be enforced to prevent alert fatigue. Recommendations must be prioritized by estimated revenue impact with the highest-impact items appearing first.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R016	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Trade Lane Profitability Prediction Model	The system must maintain a predictive model for trade lane profitability that forecasts the expected profit margin per TEU for each active trade lane over the next 30, 60, and 90-day horizons. The model must incorporate revenue projections from the demand forecasting engine, variable cost estimates including bunker at projected fuel prices, port charges per latest published tariffs, and canal dues. For trade lanes between India and GCC ports transiting the Arabian Sea, the model must factor in any war risk insurance premium adjustments affecting voyage costs. Profitability predictions must be generated separately for each container type and customer segment. Trade lanes predicted to become unprofitable within the forecast horizon must be flagged for management review with recommended actions including rate increases, service restructuring, or controlled volume reduction. The model must achieve a prediction accuracy of within 5% of actual profitability for the 30-day forecast horizon measured over rolling quarterly evaluations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R006	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Multi-Entity Voyage Cost Allocation	The optimization engine must support multi-entity cost allocation for voyage plans where the vessel is commercially operated by one entity but technically managed by another across the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India legal entities. The system must allocate voyage costs including bunker, port dues, canal tolls, and agency fees proportionally based on configurable allocation methods such as cargo TEU ratio, revenue share, or distance-based proration. Each entity cost allocation must be computed in the entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD as the base reference currency. Intercompany settlement entries must be automatically generated for approval when voyage cost allocations cross entity boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R017	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Revenue Maximization Explainability and Governance	The system must provide full explainability for every AI-generated revenue recommendation ensuring that commercial teams understand why a specific rate or action is recommended and that decisions can withstand regulatory scrutiny. Each recommendation must include a natural language explanation in both English and Arabic describing the key factors and their relative influence on the recommendation. The system must maintain a recommendation audit log capturing the model version, input features, model output, human decision taken, and actual outcome for every recommendation. A governance framework must enforce that no AI recommendation is automatically executed without human approval, ensuring the AI serves as a decision-support tool rather than an autonomous pricing agent. Bias monitoring must detect if recommendations systematically disadvantage certain customer segments, nationalities, or geographic regions in violation of competition law or anti-discrimination requirements applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Quarterly governance reports must be generated for senior management and regulatory compliance teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-051-2-004-R018	FEAT-051-2-004	MOD-051	Network Revenue Optimization Across Entities	The system must perform network-level revenue optimization across all four operating entities to maximize total group revenue rather than optimizing each entity independently. The network optimizer must evaluate scenarios where accepting a lower-margin booking at one entity's port generates higher-margin transhipment revenue at another entity's hub. For example, a competitively priced booking from JNPT to a European destination via Jebel Ali transhipment must be evaluated considering both the feeder revenue earned by the India entity and the transhipment and mainline revenue earned by the UAE entity. The optimizer must model slot allocation priorities across the network, recommending when capacity on a shared service should be allocated to direct cargo versus feeder cargo versus transhipment cargo based on total network revenue contribution. Transfer pricing between entities for shared services must be factored into the optimization to ensure each entity's reported profitability is fair. Network optimization results must be presented with entity-level revenue breakdowns and group-level consolidated metrics in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R001	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Service Loop Definition and Configuration	The system must allow operators to define and configure shipping service loops with unique service codes, trade lane identifiers, rotation sequences, and frequency patterns (weekly, bi-weekly, fortnightly, or ad-hoc). Each service loop definition must include the list of ports of call using UN/LOCODE format (e.g., QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, AEKLP for Khalifa Port, INNSA for JNPT), expected transit days between consecutive port pairs, applicable vessel class constraints, and the commercial trade lane grouping. The configuration must support both mainline and feeder service types with clear parent-child relationship mapping for transshipment connections. All labels and descriptions must support bilingual entry in English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering for Arabic text fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R002	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Alliance and Partner Schedule Ingestion	The system must ingest vessel schedules from alliance partners and slot-sharing agreements via standardized EDI messages (IFTMIN, BAPLIE) and API feeds. Ingested schedules must be parsed and mapped to internal service loop structures, resolving discrepancies in port code formats, timezone references, and vessel naming conventions. The AI engine must flag conflicts where partner schedules overlap with proprietary rotations at the same berth windows in Hamad Port or Jebel Ali. The system must maintain an audit trail of all ingested schedule versions, capturing the source system identifier, timestamp of receipt, and delta changes from the previous version. Failed ingestion attempts must trigger automated retry with exponential backoff and alert the operations team after three consecutive failures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R003	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	AI-Driven Schedule Conflict Detection	The AI engine must automatically analyze all active and proposed schedules to detect conflicts including overlapping berth windows at the same terminal, vessel double-booking across services, insufficient turnaround time between consecutive port calls, and violations of port authority operating hour restrictions. For Hamad Port, the system must enforce the Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani) berth allocation rules including minimum 4-hour buffer between vessel departures. For Jebel Ali, DP World terminal-specific constraints must be validated. Each detected conflict must be assigned a severity level (critical, warning, informational) and the AI must propose at least two resolution alternatives ranked by cost impact and schedule reliability score. Conflict detection must run within 30 seconds for schedules spanning up to 52 weeks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R004	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Schedule Version Control and Approval Workflow	The system must implement full version control for all schedule modifications, maintaining a complete history of changes with diff-level granularity showing exact fields modified between versions. Each schedule change must follow a configurable multi-level approval workflow supporting entity-specific rules — for example, Qatar entity changes require approval from Doha operations manager, while UAE entity changes route to Dubai or Abu Dhabi approvers based on the port involved. The workflow must support delegation during approver absence with automatic escalation after a configurable timeout period (default 24 hours). Approved schedules must be stamped with digital signatures, and the system must prevent publication of unapproved schedule versions. Both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates must be displayed in the approval interface based on user locale preferences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R005	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Automated Schedule Publication to External Parties	Upon final approval, the system must automatically publish schedules to configured distribution channels including the customer-facing web portal, EDI partners via IFTMIN messages, port community systems at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and freight forwarder platforms. Publication must support selective distribution rules — for example, publishing only the next 8 weeks to customers while providing 16 weeks to port authorities. The published schedule must render correctly in both English and Arabic interfaces, with port names displayed in the user's preferred language. Each publication event must generate a unique publication reference number and record the exact list of recipients, timestamps, and channel used. The system must support schedule publication in PDF format with entity-specific letterhead and branding for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R007	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Schedule Template Management and Cloning	The system must support the creation and management of schedule templates that can be cloned to rapidly generate new service schedules or seasonal variations. Templates must capture the full rotation pattern, standard transit days, default vessel assignments, and typical berth preferences at each port. Operators must be able to clone a template and apply date offsets, vessel substitutions, or port additions and removals without affecting the source template. The cloning operation must automatically recalculate all dependent ETAs and ETDs based on modified parameters. Templates must be tagged with metadata including applicable season (e.g., Hajj season for KSA ports, monsoon season for INNSA), trade lane, and entity ownership. The system must maintain a library of at least 500 active templates with full-text search in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R008	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Multi-Entity Schedule Segregation and Visibility	The system must enforce strict data segregation between entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while allowing configurable cross-entity visibility for shared services. Each schedule record must be tagged with the owning entity code and must be visible only to users with the appropriate entity-level permissions. Shared services operating across multiple entities (e.g., a rotation covering both Hamad Port and Jebel Ali) must appear in both entity views with entity-specific annotations such as local agent details, applicable tariff references, and currency-specific cost estimates in QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, and INR for India. Super-administrators must have a consolidated cross-entity view with the ability to filter by any combination of entities. Schedule reports must respect entity boundaries and display financial data in the entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R009	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Customer Schedule Search and Route Finder	The system must provide a customer-facing schedule search interface allowing users to query available sailings by origin port, destination port, date range, and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF). The search must return all viable routing options including direct services and transshipment routes with up to two intermediate hub connections. Results must display estimated transit time, vessel name, service loop code, cut-off dates for documentation and cargo, and expected arrival date at destination. The AI engine must rank results by reliability score computed from historical on-time performance data. The interface must fully support Arabic language with RTL layout, and date fields must allow input and display in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. Search response time must not exceed 3 seconds for queries spanning 12 weeks of schedules across all active services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R010	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Schedule Change Notification Engine	The system must implement a real-time notification engine that automatically alerts affected stakeholders whenever a published schedule is modified. Notifications must be triggered for changes including port omissions, additional port calls, sailing date shifts exceeding a configurable threshold (default 6 hours), and vessel substitutions. The notification engine must determine the affected parties by cross-referencing the modified schedule against active bookings, pending cargo, and registered interest lists. Notifications must be delivered via email, SMS, in-app push notification, and EDI where applicable. Each notification must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) and must include a summary of changes, impact assessment on booked cargo, and recommended actions. The system must log all notifications with delivery status tracking and support resend functionality for failed deliveries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R011	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Seasonal and Peak Period Schedule Adjustments	The system must support bulk schedule adjustments for known seasonal patterns and peak shipping periods relevant to the Middle East and Indian subcontinent trade. Predefined seasonal profiles must include Ramadan reduced port operating hours, Hajj season increased capacity to Jeddah (SAJED) and Dammam (SADMM), Indian monsoon season port restrictions at JNPT (INNSA), and year-end holiday slowdowns. The AI engine must recommend proactive schedule adjustments at least 8 weeks before each seasonal period based on historical patterns and configurable business rules. Bulk adjustments must propagate through all dependent calculations including transshipment connections, cut-off dates, and published customer schedules. The system must track the seasonal adjustment as a distinct schedule version type for reporting and performance analysis purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R012	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Schedule Reliability KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive dashboard displaying schedule reliability key performance indicators across all active services. Metrics must include on-time departure percentage, on-time arrival percentage, average delay in hours per port call, port omission frequency, and schedule integrity index computed as the ratio of actual versus planned port calls completed. The dashboard must support filtering by service loop, trade lane, vessel, port, entity, and date range with Hijri and Gregorian calendar selectors. AI-generated insights must highlight deteriorating reliability trends and correlate them with root causes such as port congestion at Jebel Ali, Suez Canal transit delays, or weather disruptions in the Arabian Sea. Data must refresh at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes) and support export to Excel and PDF with entity-specific branding and currency formatting for any financial metrics included.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R013	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Blank Sailing and Extra Loader Management	The system must support the declaration and management of blank sailings (cancelled port calls or full rotation cancellations) and extra loaders (additional unscheduled sailings inserted to handle demand surges). When a blank sailing is declared, the system must automatically identify all affected bookings and cargo, generate roll-over recommendations to the next available sailing, and notify all impacted customers and agents. For extra loaders, the system must allow rapid schedule creation using existing templates with expedited approval workflows. The financial impact of blank sailings and extra loaders must be estimated in the owning entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) including lost revenue for blanked cargo and additional vessel operating costs. Both blank sailings and extra loaders must be tracked as distinct event types in the schedule reliability analytics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R004	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Cargo Commitment Constraint Handling	The optimization engine must enforce hard constraints derived from cargo booking commitments when generating voyage plans. These constraints include guaranteed cut-off dates for export containers at origin ports, contractual delivery windows for import containers at destination ports, reefer container power supply duration limits, hazardous cargo segregation requirements per IMDG code affecting port call sequencing, and transshipment connection windows at hub ports such as Jebel Ali. The system must flag any voyage plan where cargo commitment constraints cannot be met and provide AI-generated alternative suggestions including potential cargo rollover recommendations with estimated cost impact in the booking entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R014	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Schedule Data Export and EDI Compliance	The system must export schedule data in industry-standard formats including EDIFACT IFTMIN (International Forwarding and Transport Message) for vessel schedule publication, CSV for integration with third-party planning tools, and JSON/XML for API consumers. Exported data must comply with DCSA (Digital Container Shipping Association) standards for schedule information exchange including proper use of facility codes, transport mode indicators, and timestamp formats in ISO 8601. The export must support selective field inclusion based on the recipient type — full operational detail for port authorities, commercial subset for customers, and planning-level aggregation for management reporting. Each export must be logged with a unique transaction reference, recipient identity, data scope, and timestamp. The system must support scheduled automated exports at configurable intervals (daily, weekly) and on-demand manual exports with preview capability before transmission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R015	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	Offline Schedule Access and Synchronization	The system must provide offline access to schedule data for users operating in areas with intermittent connectivity, particularly relevant for vessel-based users and remote port offices in the Indian subcontinent. The offline module must cache the latest published schedules for all services the user is authorized to view, supporting a minimum of 16 weeks of forward-looking data. Users must be able to search, view, and bookmark schedules while offline. Any schedule updates made during the online period must be incrementally synchronized to the offline cache within 5 minutes of reconnection using a delta-sync protocol that minimizes bandwidth consumption. The offline interface must maintain full bilingual support in English and Arabic with proper RTL rendering and must display a clear timestamp indicating when the cached data was last synchronized. Conflict resolution for any locally saved annotations must follow a last-write-wins policy with full audit logging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-001-R016	FEAT-052-1-001	MOD-052	AI-Powered Schedule Optimization Recommendations	The AI engine must continuously analyze historical schedule performance, demand patterns, bunker fuel price trends, and port congestion data to generate proactive schedule optimization recommendations. Recommendations must include suggestions for adjusting port stay durations, resequencing port calls to reduce empty repositioning, consolidating underutilized services, and optimizing vessel speed profiles for fuel efficiency. Each recommendation must include a quantified impact assessment showing projected cost savings in USD and the operating entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), estimated reliability improvement percentage, and CO2 emission reduction estimates in compliance with IMO CII (Carbon Intensity Indicator) requirements. Recommendations must be presented with confidence scores and supporting data visualizations. The system must learn from accepted and rejected recommendations to improve future suggestion relevance, targeting a minimum 70% acceptance rate within 6 months of deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R001	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Port Rotation Sequence Modeling	The system must model port rotation sequences as directed graphs where each node represents a port of call with associated attributes including UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKLP, INNSA, SAJED, SADMM), terminal code, available berth numbers, tidal restrictions, maximum vessel LOA and draft constraints, and operating hours. Edges between nodes must capture standard steaming distance in nautical miles, expected transit time at various speed profiles (eco speed 12-14 knots, normal 16-18 knots, full speed 20-22 knots), and applicable waterway transit requirements such as Suez Canal or Strait of Hormuz pilotage. The model must support rotations of up to 25 port calls and handle both clockwise and counter-clockwise directional variants. All port names and terminal descriptions must be stored in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R002	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	AI-Driven Port Sequence Optimization	The AI engine must evaluate alternative port call sequences within a rotation to minimize total voyage distance, bunker fuel consumption, and overall transit time while respecting commercial constraints such as minimum cargo volume thresholds at each port. The optimization algorithm must consider factors including great-circle sailing distances with waypoint routing through mandatory transit points (Suez Canal, Strait of Hormuz, Bab el-Mandeb), tidal windows at draft-restricted ports like JNPT where maximum permissible draft varies by tide state, and preferred arrival day-of-week at major ports to align with terminal peak and off-peak pricing schedules. The system must generate ranked alternatives with cost comparisons in USD and present fuel savings estimates in metric tons of VLSFO (Very Low Sulphur Fuel Oil). Optimization must complete within 60 seconds for rotations of up to 20 ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R003	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Berth Window Request and Reservation	The system must generate and submit berth window reservation requests to port community systems and terminal operating systems at Hamad Port (via Mwani Qatar integration), Jebel Ali (via DP World Dubaitrade), Khalifa Port (via Abu Dhabi Ports Maqta gateway), and JNPT (via PCS 1x Indian Port Community System). Each reservation request must specify the vessel IMO number, expected arrival date and time, requested berth or terminal preference, estimated cargo volume in TEUs, number of container moves (discharge and load), and special equipment requirements such as reefer plugs or out-of-gauge cargo handling capability. The system must track reservation status through the lifecycle (requested, confirmed, amended, cancelled) and automatically escalate unconfirmed reservations that are within 72 hours of the expected arrival. All timestamps must be stored in UTC with display conversion to the local port timezone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R004	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Berth Window Conflict Resolution	When a requested berth window conflicts with existing reservations or terminal maintenance windows, the system must automatically propose alternative berth windows ranked by proximity to the originally requested time, impact on the overall rotation schedule, and additional cost implications including overtime labor charges and tidal delay risks. The AI engine must consider terminal-specific rules — for example, at Jebel Ali Terminal 1 versus Terminal 3 berth interchangeability rules, or at Hamad Port the container terminal versus general cargo berth segregation requirements. The system must support manual override by authorized planners with mandatory reason capture for audit purposes. Conflict resolution proposals must be generated within 15 seconds and must include visual timeline representations showing the berth occupancy chart with the proposed slot highlighted. Financial impacts must be displayed in the port's local currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, INR for JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R005	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Tidal and Draft Constraint Management	The system must integrate real-time and forecast tidal data for draft-restricted ports, particularly JNPT (INNSA) where the approach channel depth limits vessel draft to approximately 14 meters at low tide. Tidal windows must be calculated based on the vessel's actual departure draft from the previous port, accounting for fuel consumption-driven draft changes during the voyage. The system must alert planners when a vessel's projected arrival draft exceeds the available water depth at the target berth during the planned arrival window, recommending either speed adjustments to arrive during a favorable tidal window or partial cargo discharge at an intermediate port. Tidal data must be sourced from official hydrographic authorities and updated at minimum every 6 hours. For ports in the Arabian Gulf (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali) where tidal range is typically less than 2 meters, the system must still validate under-keel clearance against port authority minimum requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R006	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Terminal Operating Hours and Cutoff Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive database of terminal operating hours for all ports in the rotation, accounting for standard working hours, extended hours, 24/7 operations, and scheduled closures for national holidays specific to each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must account for reduced operating hours during Ramadan and National Day (December 18). For UAE, operating hour adjustments for UAE National Day and Commemoration Day must be configured. For KSA, Hajj season terminal extensions at Jeddah Islamic Port must be supported. For India, JNPT terminal closures during Ganesh Chaturthi and Diwali must be tracked. Cargo cutoff times must be calculated backwards from the vessel departure time, with separate cutoffs for documentation submission (typically 24-48 hours before sailing), reefer container plug-in (12-24 hours), dangerous goods declarations (48-72 hours), and standard dry container gate-in (6-12 hours before sailing). All cutoff times must display in local time with Hijri date equivalents where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R007	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Multi-Terminal Port Call Splitting	The system must support scenarios where a single port call requires the vessel to work at multiple terminals within the same port — for example, at JNPT where cargo may be split between JNPT Container Terminal (operated by BMCT), Nhava Sheva International Container Terminal (NSICT), and APM Terminals Mumbai (GTI). The system must model inter-terminal shifting time, pilotage requirements for intra-port moves, and separate berth window reservations for each terminal visit. Cargo allocation across terminals must be optimized based on container destination, terminal handling rates, and applicable terminal handling charges in INR. The total port stay duration must be calculated as the sum of individual terminal working times plus inter-terminal shifting and waiting times. The system must generate a single unified port call plan that coordinates all terminal visits with a coherent timeline visible to the vessel master, port agents, and terminal operators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R008	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Port Congestion Monitoring and Avoidance	The system must continuously monitor port congestion levels at all ports in active rotations by integrating AIS (Automatic Identification System) vessel position data, port authority queue reports, and historical waiting time analytics. Congestion levels must be classified as normal (waiting time under 6 hours), elevated (6-24 hours), high (24-48 hours), and critical (over 48 hours). When congestion at an upcoming port exceeds the elevated threshold, the AI engine must recommend mitigation strategies including speed reduction to arrive after congestion clears (virtual arrival concept), port sequence swap with the next port in rotation if commercially feasible, or diversion to an alternative terminal within the same port complex. Congestion predictions must leverage machine learning models trained on at least 24 months of historical data per port. The financial impact of congestion-related delays must be estimated in USD and the operating entity's local currency, including vessel daily operating cost, bunker cost differential, and potential demurrage liabilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R009	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Berth Productivity and Port Stay Optimization	The system must calculate expected port stay duration based on planned container moves (discharge plus loading), the target terminal's historical crane productivity rate (moves per hour per crane), the number of cranes expected to be assigned (based on vessel size and terminal policy), and additional time allowances for hatch cover operations, lashing, and non-containerized cargo. The AI engine must identify opportunities to reduce port stay by recommending stowage plan adjustments that minimize crane repositioning, bay-swapping, and restow moves. Historical performance data must be maintained per terminal with minimum 12-month rolling statistics segmented by vessel size class. Port stay estimates must be accurate within a tolerance of plus or minus 2 hours for standard calls and plus or minus 4 hours for complex multi-terminal calls. The system must track planned versus actual port stay durations and feed deviations back into the AI model for continuous improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R010	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Anchorage and Waiting Area Management	The system must model designated anchorage areas and waiting zones for each port, capturing coordinates, maximum vessel capacity, depth restrictions, and port authority rules governing anchorage duration limits and priority queuing protocols. For Jebel Ali, the system must distinguish between inner anchorage and outer anchorage with respective draft limitations. For JNPT, the Mumbai Port Trust anchorage regulations including mandatory anchorage for vessels awaiting customs clearance must be enforced. The system must track vessels at anchorage with real-time AIS position updates and estimated time to berth based on current queue position and berth availability forecast. Anchorage costs including port authority anchorage dues, additional crew overtime, and bunker consumption at anchorage must be calculated in the applicable local currency (AED, QAR, INR) and consolidated into the voyage cost estimate. The system must alert planners when projected anchorage time exceeds 12 hours with an impact assessment on downstream schedule commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R011	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Port Rotation Change Impact Analysis	When a planner proposes a change to the port rotation sequence — such as adding a new port call, omitting a scheduled port, or resequencing ports — the system must perform a comprehensive impact analysis before the change is committed. The analysis must quantify the effect on total voyage distance in nautical miles, transit time in days and hours, bunker fuel consumption in metric tons, total voyage cost in USD and entity-specific currencies, transshipment connection viability at hub ports, and compliance with any contractual service commitments such as guaranteed weekly frequency at specific ports. The impact analysis must include a before-and-after comparison table and a visual route map overlay showing the original and proposed rotation. All affected bookings must be identified and categorized by severity of impact (no impact, minor delay, significant delay, missed connection). The analysis report must be generated within 30 seconds and be exportable as a bilingual PDF in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R012	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Weather and Sea State Integration for Port Planning	The system must integrate weather forecast data including wind speed and direction, wave height, swell period, visibility, and precipitation for all port approaches and coastal zones along the rotation route. Weather data must be sourced from recognized maritime meteorological services and updated at minimum every 6 hours with 7-day forecasts. The system must enforce port-specific weather operating limits — for example, Hamad Port berthing operations suspended when wind speed exceeds 35 knots, or JNPT pilot boarding suspended during monsoon season when swell height exceeds 3 meters. When adverse weather is forecast within the planning horizon, the AI engine must recommend schedule adjustments such as early departure from the current port, speed increase to arrive before weather deterioration, or delay to avoid arriving during unsafe conditions. Weather-related schedule impacts must be tagged distinctly from operational delays for KPI reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R013	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Bunker Optimization Within Port Sequence	The system must integrate bunker fuel availability and pricing data at each port in the rotation to recommend optimal bunkering ports and quantities. Bunker prices must be tracked in USD per metric ton with conversion to entity-specific currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using real-time exchange rates. The optimization must consider factors including fuel price differentials between ports, bunker barge availability and scheduling lead times, vessel tank capacity constraints, minimum and maximum fuel-on-board requirements for safe operation, and port-specific bunker quality regulations including IMO 2020 sulphur cap compliance for VLSFO and MGO. The AI engine must calculate the total bunkering cost for the rotation under multiple scenarios (single-port bunkering versus split bunkering) and recommend the most cost-effective strategy. Bunker planning must be recalculated automatically whenever the port sequence or vessel speed profile is modified, ensuring the fuel plan remains feasible and optimized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R014	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Port Priority and Commercial Weighting Configuration	The system must allow commercial teams to assign priority weights and business importance scores to each port in a rotation, influencing how the optimization algorithm trades off between competing objectives. High-priority ports such as Jebel Ali, which serves as a major transshipment hub for the GCC region, must receive scheduling preference to maintain reliable fixed-day arrival commitments. The weighting system must consider factors including cargo volume contribution measured in TEUs, revenue contribution in USD, strategic customer presence at the port, transshipment connectivity value, and contractual obligations to port authorities or terminal operators. Weights must be configurable per entity with appropriate access controls — Qatar entity managers can adjust weights for Hamad Port, UAE managers for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, and so forth. The system must prevent optimization outputs that would degrade service at high-priority ports below configurable minimum reliability thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R015	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Berth Window Calendar Visualization	The system must provide an interactive visual calendar showing berth window allocations across all active rotations for each port and terminal. The calendar must display a Gantt-style timeline view with horizontal bars representing vessel berth occupancy periods, color-coded by service loop. Users must be able to zoom from yearly overview down to hourly detail, filter by port, terminal, vessel, or service, and drag-and-drop berth windows for manual adjustment with automatic constraint validation. The calendar must support both Gregorian and Hijri date axes selectable by the user, and all vessel names and service descriptions must render correctly in both English and Arabic. The visualization must load within 5 seconds for a 4-week view across all terminals at a single port and must support concurrent access by multiple planners with real-time update propagation to prevent conflicting manual adjustments. Export to PNG and PDF must be supported for inclusion in operational reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R016	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Historical Port Call Performance Analytics	The system must maintain a comprehensive historical database of all port calls executed across all rotations, capturing planned versus actual arrival and departure times, berth allocation adherence, container moves achieved versus planned, crane productivity rates, delays with categorized root causes (weather, congestion, equipment breakdown, labor shortage, tidal restriction), and total port costs. Analytics must be available through configurable dashboards with drill-down capability from fleet-level summary to individual port call detail. The AI engine must identify statistically significant patterns such as recurring congestion at specific ports on certain days of the week, seasonal productivity variations, or systematic bias in planning estimates for particular terminals. Reports must support multi-currency display with amounts in both USD and the local port currency, and must be exportable in formats suitable for management presentation including bilingual English-Arabic PDF with proper RTL layout for Arabic sections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-002-R017	FEAT-052-1-002	MOD-052	Port Compliance and Regulatory Constraint Validation	The system must validate all port call plans against applicable regulatory constraints before allowing schedule publication or berth window submission. Regulatory checks must include vessel flag state port access restrictions, port state control inspection targeting based on vessel risk profile, security level requirements under ISPS Code with specific pre-arrival notification timeframes (typically 24-96 hours depending on port), customs pre-arrival manifest filing deadlines (e.g., India requires IGM filing 24 hours before arrival at JNPT), and environmental zone requirements including emission control area compliance. For KSA ports, the system must validate that the vessel and cargo comply with Saudi Arabia General Authority for Ports regulations. For Qatar ports, compliance with Qatar Maritime and Air Navigation Law requirements must be checked. The system must maintain a regulatory rules database updated at least quarterly and must generate a compliance checklist for each port call plan that can be reviewed and acknowledged by the operations team before finalizing the schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R001	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Transit Route Planning Integration	The system must integrate Suez Canal and Panama Canal transit requirements into the overall voyage route planning engine. When a rotation includes ports on both sides of either canal (e.g., Jebel Ali to Rotterdam via Suez, or Jebel Ali to US East Coast via Suez or alternatively via Panama), the system must automatically identify the canal transit requirement, model the transit as a distinct voyage segment with its own time and cost parameters, and factor it into the total voyage calculation. For routes where both Suez and Panama are viable alternatives (e.g., Middle East to US East Coast), the AI engine must compare both options considering transit fees, additional steaming distance, fuel cost differential, and total transit time. The system must support the Cape of Good Hope routing as a third alternative when canal transit disruptions occur, automatically recalculating voyage metrics for this significantly longer route.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R002	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Suez Canal Convoy Scheduling and Booking	The system must model the Suez Canal transit scheduling including northbound and southbound convoy departure times from Port Said and Suez respectively, transit duration through the canal (typically 12-16 hours depending on convoy position and vessel size), and the Great Bitter Lake passing protocol for convoy crossing. The system must submit transit booking requests to the Suez Canal Authority (SCA) via their electronic booking system, specifying the vessel particulars (IMO number, gross tonnage, net Suez Canal tonnage, LOA, beam, draft), requested transit date, convoy preference (first or second convoy), and any special requirements such as fresh water allowance for vessels arriving from the Indian Ocean. The system must track the booking confirmation status and automatically alert planners if the requested transit date is not confirmed within 48 hours of the planned transit. Canal transit slots must be integrated into the rotation schedule with appropriate buffer times for anchorage at Port Said or Suez waiting areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R003	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Suez Canal Transit Fee Estimation and Tracking	The system must calculate estimated Suez Canal transit fees based on the current SCA tariff structure, which uses Suez Canal Net Tonnage (SCNT) as the primary billing unit with additional surcharges for laden versus ballast transit, container count supplements, special drawing rights (SDR) currency adjustments, and applicable seasonal or promotional discounts. The fee calculation must account for the specific vessel's SCNT certificate values and apply the correct tariff bands. Estimated fees must be presented in SDR (the SCA billing currency), USD equivalent, and the operating entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The system must maintain a historical record of actual transit fees paid for variance analysis against estimates. When the SCA announces tariff revisions (typically annually), the system must allow administrators to update the tariff tables and automatically recalculate forward-looking estimates for all affected rotations. The AI engine must flag unusual fee variances exceeding 5% for investigation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R004	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Panama Canal Lock Scheduling and Neopanamax Support	The system must support Panama Canal transit planning for both the original Panamax locks and the expanded Neopanamax locks (Agua Clara and Cocoli), which accommodate container vessels up to approximately 14,000 TEU. Transit booking must specify the preferred lock system, vessel dimensions (including beam and draft relative to lock chamber constraints), requested transit direction (Pacific to Atlantic or Atlantic to Pacific), and preferred booking window. The system must enforce Panama Canal Authority (ACP) vessel dimension limits — maximum beam 32.31 meters for Panamax locks and 51.25 meters for Neopanamax locks, maximum draft 12.04 meters and 15.24 meters respectively. Transit time estimates must account for lock cycling times, Gatun Lake transit, and potential queuing delays. The system must integrate with the ACP Vessel Scheduling System for booking confirmation and must track transit slot auctions when regular booking slots are unavailable due to demand or maintenance restrictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R005	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Panama Canal Transit Fee Calculation	The system must calculate Panama Canal transit tolls based on the ACP tariff structure, which assesses fees per TEU capacity for container vessels with separate rates for laden and empty containers, plus a fixed per-transit fee component. The calculation must apply the correct tariff tier based on the vessel's declared TEU capacity and the number of laden versus empty containers being transited. Additional charges including tug assistance fees, locomotive (mule) charges for Panamax lock transits, and fresh water surcharge during drought-induced draft restrictions must be included. All fees must be presented in USD (the ACP billing currency) and converted to the operating entity's local currency using current exchange rates. The system must maintain the ACP tariff schedule with version control and must alert administrators when tariff updates published by the ACP require system updates. Historical transit cost data must be retained for at least 5 years for budgeting and benchmarking analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R006	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Transit Document Management	The system must manage all documentation required for canal transits including the Suez Canal transit declaration form, crew list, vessel certificates (tonnage certificate, SCNT certificate for Suez, loadline certificate), dangerous goods manifest for hazardous containers in transit, and pre-arrival security notifications. Documents must be generated from vessel and voyage master data with automatic population of known fields, reducing manual data entry. The system must enforce submission deadlines — for Suez Canal, the transit declaration must be submitted at least 48 hours before arrival at the canal, and for Panama Canal, documentation must be filed 96 hours before the scheduled transit date. Documents must be transmitted electronically to the respective canal authority systems via approved interfaces. The system must maintain a document checklist per transit that tracks completion status and automatically alerts the operations team when any required document is missing or approaching its submission deadline. All document templates must support both English and Arabic annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R007	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Disruption and Closure Contingency Planning	The system must monitor canal operational status in real-time and activate contingency planning workflows when disruptions occur. For the Suez Canal, the system must detect events including sandstorm-related closures, vessel grounding incidents (drawing from the 2021 Ever Given precedent), political instability affecting the Red Sea approach, and scheduled maintenance closures. For Panama Canal, drought-induced draft restrictions that reduce daily transit slots and lock maintenance closures must be tracked. When a disruption is detected or forecast, the AI engine must immediately assess the impact on all affected rotations, calculate alternative routing costs via Cape of Good Hope (for Suez disruptions) or Suez Canal (for Panama disruptions), estimate delay impacts on downstream port calls, and generate executive summary briefings. The system must support rapid re-routing decision workflows with multi-entity approval chains. Financial impact assessments must include additional bunker fuel costs, canal fee savings from avoidance, potential cargo delay penalties, and insurance implications, presented in USD and all entity-specific currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R005	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Port Congestion and Waiting Time Prediction	The system must incorporate AI-based port congestion predictions when computing voyage arrival times and berth availability windows. The prediction model must use historical vessel AIS data, current port queue lengths from integrated port community systems, tide windows for draft-restricted ports like JNPT, and seasonal congestion patterns including Hajj season surges at Saudi ports and monsoon-related delays at Indian ports. Predicted waiting times must be expressed as probability distributions with P50 and P90 estimates. The optimization engine must use these predictions to recommend speed adjustments that minimize fuel consumption while ensuring timely berth availability, known as virtual arrival or just-in-time arrival strategies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R008	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Real-Time Canal Transit Progress Tracking	The system must provide real-time tracking of vessels during canal transit using AIS position data correlated with known canal waypoints. For Suez Canal transits, the system must display the vessel's progress through defined segments: Port Said anchorage, northern approach channel, canal main channel, Great Bitter Lake, southern channel, and Suez exit. For Panama Canal transits, progress through Gatun Locks, Gatun Lake, Pedro Miguel Locks, Miraflores Locks (or Neopanamax lock sequence), and exit channel must be tracked. Transit progress must be displayed on an interactive map overlay with percentage completion, estimated time to canal exit, and current speed over ground. Any deviation from expected transit timeline exceeding 30 minutes must trigger automated alerts to the operations team, downstream port agents, and affected booking customers. The tracking interface must support Arabic language labels and must display local canal time alongside UTC and the user's local timezone.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R009	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Transit Speed and Draft Optimization	The system must calculate optimal vessel speed profiles for canal approach voyages to ensure arrival at the canal anchorage coincides with the booked convoy or transit slot, avoiding both premature arrival (incurring anchorage costs) and late arrival (missing the slot). For Suez Canal, the system must calculate the required speed from the last port of call (commonly Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, or JNPT for westbound transits, or Mediterranean ports for eastbound transits) to arrive at Port Said or Suez anchorage with a configurable buffer time (default 6 hours before convoy departure). For Panama Canal, transit draft restrictions during drought conditions must be factored into cargo planning at preceding ports — the system must alert when the vessel's projected transit draft exceeds the ACP's current maximum allowable draft, recommending either cargo reduction or ballast adjustment. Speed optimization must balance on-time arrival against bunker fuel cost, presenting the trade-off in USD per metric ton of fuel consumed and total CO2 emissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R010	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Transit Cost Allocation Across Entities	The system must allocate canal transit costs across multiple operating entities when the rotation serves ports belonging to different entities. The cost allocation methodology must be configurable with support for common shipping industry methods including allocation by cargo volume (TEU proportion per entity), allocation by revenue contribution, allocation by number of port calls per entity jurisdiction, and fixed percentage split based on inter-entity agreements. For a typical Suez Canal transit on a rotation covering Hamad Port (Qatar entity), Jebel Ali (UAE entity), and European ports, the transit fee must be apportioned and recorded in each entity's accounts in their respective base currencies (QAR, AED) with proper inter-company accounting entries. The system must generate monthly reconciliation reports showing canal transit costs per entity, per service loop, and per transit direction. Transfer pricing rules applicable to the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions must be respected in the allocation methodology. Audit trail must capture the allocation formula, input parameters, and resulting amounts for each transit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R011	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Suez Canal Northbound and Southbound Route Variants	The system must model both northbound (Asia/Middle East to Europe) and southbound (Europe to Asia/Middle East) Suez Canal transit variants with their distinct operational characteristics. Northbound transits from Suez to Port Said have different convoy formation points, pilot boarding locations, and canal entry sequencing compared to southbound transits. The system must account for the new Suez Canal parallel channel (opened 2015) which allows two-way traffic in the northern section, reducing transit times for some convoy configurations. Waiting time estimates must differ by direction based on historical data — southbound transits from Port Said typically experience different congestion patterns than northbound from Suez. The system must support convoy preference selection and must calculate the optimal departure time from the last port before the canal to align with the preferred convoy, considering that first convoy departures are typically at 0600 local time. All directional route models must include waypoint coordinates for accurate distance calculations and display on the planning map.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R012	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Dangerous Goods Canal Transit Compliance	The system must validate that dangerous goods containers loaded on the vessel comply with Suez Canal Authority and Panama Canal Authority regulations for canal transit. The SCA imposes specific restrictions on certain IMDG classes during transit, including quantity limits and stowage position requirements for Class 1 (explosives) and Class 7 (radioactive) materials. The system must cross-reference the vessel's dangerous goods manifest (generated from booking and container data) against canal-specific DG transit rules and flag any non-compliant shipments before the vessel departs from the last port prior to canal transit. For non-compliant cargo, the system must recommend remedial actions including offloading at the pre-canal port, restowing to compliant positions, or applying for special transit permits from the canal authority. The compliance check must be automated and run each time the stowage plan or dangerous goods manifest is updated for vessels with upcoming canal transits. Compliance reports must be available in English and Arabic for review by the vessel master and shore-side DG compliance officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R013	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Pilot and Service Provider Coordination	The system must manage the coordination of canal-specific service providers including pilots, tugboat operators, and canal authority inspectors. For Suez Canal transits, the system must track pilot boarding arrangements at Port Said or Suez, pilot assignment confirmation from the SCA, and required pilot provisions and accommodation during transit. For Panama Canal, locomotive (mule) operator assignments at Panamax locks and tug assignments at Neopanamax locks must be recorded. The system must generate service provider requirement notifications at configurable lead times (default 48 hours before transit) and track confirmation status. Costs for canal service providers must be captured separately from transit tolls for accurate cost analysis, recorded in the billing currency of each service and converted to the entity's local currency. The system must maintain a directory of canal service agents and emergency contacts at each canal, accessible both online and in the offline schedule cache for vessel-based users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R003	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Impact Analysis Assessment Template	The system must provide a structured impact analysis template for each change request that requires the analyst to document affected modules, dependent integrations (port community systems, customs gateways, carrier EDI connections), estimated effort in person-days, risk level, rollback plan, and testing requirements. The template must include specific sections for assessing impact on container tracking workflows (FCL/LCL), bill of lading processing, INCOTERMS-based cost allocation (CIF, FOB, CFR, EXW), and vessel schedule management. Impact on Arabic UI rendering and Hijri calendar date calculations must be explicitly evaluated as a mandatory checklist item.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R014	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Transit Analytics and Benchmarking	The system must collect and analyze historical canal transit data to support continuous improvement in transit planning and cost optimization. Key metrics must include average waiting time at anchorage before transit (segmented by canal, direction, and time of year), actual transit duration versus planned, transit fee trends over rolling 12-month and 36-month periods, frequency and duration of canal disruptions, and success rate of booking preferred convoy or transit slots. The AI engine must identify patterns such as seasonal congestion peaks (typically higher Suez traffic before Chinese New Year and European holiday seasons), correlation between booking lead time and slot confirmation success rate, and cost optimization opportunities through strategic transit timing. Benchmarking reports must compare the organization's canal transit performance against published industry averages from sources such as the Suez Canal Authority annual reports. All analytics must be available in multi-currency views (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and exportable as bilingual reports with proper Arabic RTL formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R015	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Red Sea and Gulf of Aden Security Risk Integration	The system must integrate maritime security risk data for the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden approaches to the Suez Canal, which have historically been affected by piracy threats and geopolitical disruptions. Security risk levels must be sourced from recognized maritime security advisory bodies including UKMTO (United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations), MSCHOA (Maritime Security Centre Horn of Africa), and the IMB Piracy Reporting Centre. When security risk levels exceed configurable thresholds, the system must alert planners and present alternative routing options including naval convoy corridors, recommended transit routes through the Internationally Recommended Transit Corridor (IRTC), and alternative routing via Cape of Good Hope with full voyage cost comparison. The system must factor in war risk insurance premium adjustments for high-risk transits, calculating the additional insurance cost in USD and entity-specific currencies. Security advisories must be displayed in both English and Arabic and must be linked to affected rotation schedules for rapid impact assessment across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R016	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Multi-Canal Transit Voyage Costing Summary	The system must generate a comprehensive voyage cost summary for rotations involving one or more canal transits, consolidating all canal-related expenses into a structured cost breakdown. The summary must include transit tolls (broken down by tariff components), anchorage charges at canal waiting areas, pilot and tug fees, canal agent disbursements, additional bunker consumption during canal transit and waiting, war risk insurance premiums for applicable zones, and any special permits or surcharges. The cost summary must be presented at both the voyage level and the per-TEU level for commercial benchmarking. Currency handling must support display in the canal's billing currency (SDR for Suez, USD for Panama), USD as the common reporting currency, and each operating entity's local currency with the applicable exchange rate and date clearly stated. The AI engine must compare the current voyage's canal costs against the previous 10 equivalent transits and highlight variances exceeding 10% with root cause annotations. The summary must be exportable as a formatted PDF with entity-specific letterhead and must support inclusion in broader voyage profit-and-loss reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-003-R017	FEAT-052-1-003	MOD-052	Canal Booking Cancellation and Rebooking Workflow	The system must manage the workflow for cancelling and rebooking canal transit slots when voyage plans change. Cancellation policies for both the Suez Canal Authority and Panama Canal Authority impose financial penalties based on the timing of cancellation relative to the booked transit date — the system must calculate applicable cancellation fees and present them to the planner before confirming the cancellation. Rebooking must check availability for the new requested date and automatically submit the revised booking through the canal authority's electronic system. The system must support common rebooking scenarios including date changes due to port delays, direction changes when a rotation is reversed, and vessel substitutions when the originally planned vessel is replaced. Each cancellation and rebooking must be recorded in the voyage audit trail with mandatory reason codes (port delay, weather, commercial decision, vessel breakdown, canal disruption). Financial impacts of cancellation fees and rebooking cost differentials must be tracked per entity in their local currency and included in the voyage cost reconciliation process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R001	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Dynamic ETA Calculation Engine	The system must implement a real-time ETA calculation engine that continuously updates arrival estimates for all upcoming port calls based on the vessel's current AIS-reported position, actual speed over ground, remaining distance via the planned route (including waypoints for canal transits, traffic separation schemes, and emission control areas), forecasted weather and sea state along the route, and known port approach constraints such as tidal windows at JNPT. The ETA must be recalculated at minimum every 30 minutes and whenever a significant event occurs (departure from a port, speed change exceeding 2 knots, route deviation). The calculation must account for planned canal transits including convoy scheduling at Suez and lock scheduling at Panama. ETA accuracy must target within plus or minus 4 hours at 72 hours before arrival and plus or minus 2 hours at 24 hours before arrival. All ETAs must be stored and displayed in UTC with automatic conversion to the destination port's local timezone and must support both Gregorian and Hijri date display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R002	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	ETD Calculation Based on Port Operations Progress	The system must calculate and continuously update the Estimated Time of Departure (ETD) for vessels currently in port based on real-time cargo operations progress. The ETD calculation must factor in remaining container moves (discharge and loading) at the current productivity rate, pending special operations such as reefer container plug-in/plug-out and out-of-gauge cargo handling, documentation completion status for the next port of call, bunker fuel loading completion status, and port authority departure clearance requirements. For vessels at Jebel Ali or Hamad Port, the system must also consider tidal window requirements for departure when the vessel's departure draft approaches the port's depth limitations. The ETD must be recalculated every 15 minutes while the vessel is in port and must reflect the later of the operational completion time and the earliest permitted departure time per the port authority. ETD changes exceeding a configurable threshold (default 2 hours) must trigger automated notifications to all downstream port agents and booked cargo stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R003	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	AI-Powered ETA Prediction with Machine Learning	The AI engine must employ machine learning models trained on historical voyage data to predict ETAs with higher accuracy than traditional distance-speed calculations alone. The ML model must incorporate features including vessel-specific speed profile patterns (accounting for hull fouling degradation over time between dry-dockings), seasonal weather pattern effects on specific route segments (e.g., monsoon impact on Arabian Sea transit times between June and September), historical port approach delay patterns at each destination port, and day-of-week effects on port accessibility. The model must be trained on a minimum of 24 months of historical AIS and port call data and must be retrained at configurable intervals (default monthly) to incorporate the latest operational data. The system must display both the ML-predicted ETA and the deterministic calculation-based ETA, with a confidence interval (e.g., 80% probability of arrival between time X and time Y). Model performance metrics including mean absolute error and prediction bias must be tracked and reported on the schedule reliability dashboard.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R004	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Proactive ETA Change Notification to Customers	The system must automatically notify affected customers and booking parties whenever the ETA at their port of interest changes beyond a configurable threshold (default 6 hours for changes more than 72 hours before arrival, 3 hours for changes within 72 hours of arrival, and 1 hour for changes within 24 hours of arrival). Notifications must be personalized per recipient, including their specific booking reference numbers, container numbers, the previous ETA, the updated ETA, the reason for the change (weather delay, port congestion, canal transit delay, speed reduction for fuel optimization, or upstream port delay), and any recommended actions such as adjusting trucking arrangements or warehouse receiving schedules. Notifications must be sent via the customer's preferred communication channel (email, SMS, in-app notification, or EDI IFTSTA message) and must be generated in the customer's preferred language (English or Arabic). The notification engine must support batching to avoid sending multiple updates within a configurable quiet period (default 4 hours) for the same booking when the ETA is fluctuating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R005	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Port Authority Pre-Arrival Notification Automation	The system must automate the generation and submission of pre-arrival notifications to port authorities based on current ETA calculations. Notification requirements differ by port jurisdiction — for JNPT (India), the system must file the vessel pre-arrival message through the Indian Port Community System (PCS 1x) at least 48 hours before arrival. For Hamad Port (Qatar), notification to Mwani Qatar must be submitted 72 hours before arrival with updated crew lists and security declarations. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port (UAE), pre-arrival notifications must comply with Federal Transport Authority (FTA) maritime regulations with 24-hour advance filing. For Saudi ports (Jeddah, Dammam), the Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani) requires 72-hour advance notification. The system must automatically regenerate and resubmit notifications when the ETA changes by more than 6 hours after the initial notification was sent. All notifications must include vessel particulars, cargo summary, dangerous goods declaration status, and crew health declarations in the format prescribed by each port authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R006	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Cascading ETA Impact Analysis Across Rotation	When the ETA at one port changes, the system must automatically cascade the impact analysis across all subsequent ports in the rotation, recalculating downstream ETAs and ETDs while respecting minimum port stay durations, berth window reservations, canal transit bookings, and speed envelope constraints. The cascading analysis must identify which downstream port calls can absorb the delay through schedule buffer consumption, which require speed increases (with associated additional bunker cost calculated in USD and entity-specific currencies), and which will experience unavoidable delays requiring berth window renegotiation or canal transit rebooking. The impact must be presented as a visual rotation timeline showing original versus revised schedule with delay propagation highlighted at each port. The system must calculate the total cost of schedule recovery options including speed increase bunker premium, overtime berth charges at ports where extended stays occur, and potential demurrage liabilities on affected cargo. The analysis must complete within 15 seconds for rotations of up to 20 port calls.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R007	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Speed Adjustment Recommendations for ETA Recovery	When a vessel is running behind schedule, the AI engine must calculate and recommend speed adjustment profiles to recover the delay and meet the planned ETA at the next port or a downstream high-priority port. The recommendation must present multiple scenarios — for example, increasing from eco speed (12 knots) to normal speed (16 knots) or full speed (20 knots) — with each scenario showing the estimated delay recovery in hours, additional bunker fuel consumption in metric tons, incremental fuel cost in USD and the entity's local currency, and the CO2 emission increase in metric tons (relevant to IMO CII compliance tracking). The system must enforce vessel-specific maximum speed constraints based on the vessel's engine capacity and current hull condition. Speed recommendations must not exceed the vessel's safe operating parameters and must account for weather conditions along the remaining route. The system must also identify scenarios where no feasible speed increase can recover the full delay, recommending instead a port omission or schedule replanning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R008	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	ETA Accuracy Tracking and Reporting	The system must track ETA prediction accuracy across all port calls, comparing the final confirmed ETA (issued 6 hours before arrival) against the initial ETA published at the time of schedule publication and at intermediate milestones (departure from the previous port, 72 hours before arrival, 48 hours before arrival, 24 hours before arrival). Accuracy metrics must be computed and reported per vessel, per service loop, per port, per trade lane, and per entity. The system must generate weekly and monthly ETA accuracy reports showing mean absolute deviation, percentage of arrivals within plus or minus 6 hours of the published ETA, and trend analysis comparing current period performance against the previous 6 and 12 months. Reports must be segmented by root cause of deviation (weather, port congestion, mechanical issue, canal delay, commercial speed reduction). All reports must support bilingual presentation in English and Arabic, display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and include entity-specific branding with financial metrics shown in the entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R009	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Agent and Terminal Operator ETA Communication	The system must provide dedicated communication channels for port agents and terminal operators to receive and acknowledge ETA and ETD updates. Port agents at each port of call must receive automated ETA updates at configurable milestones — default at 7 days, 3 days, 48 hours, 24 hours, and 12 hours before arrival. Each update must include the current ETA, expected cargo volume (discharge and load TEUs), special cargo requirements (reefer counts, dangerous goods, over-dimensional cargo), and any changes from the previous notification. Terminal operators must receive operational updates including expected number of container moves, preferred crane allocation, and special equipment requests. The system must track agent acknowledgment of each update and escalate to the operations manager if acknowledgment is not received within 4 hours for updates within 48 hours of arrival. Communication must support both English and Arabic, with port agents in Qatar and KSA receiving Arabic-primary notifications and agents in India receiving English-primary notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R010	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Vessel Tracking Dashboard with Live ETA Display	The system must provide an interactive vessel tracking dashboard displaying the real-time position of all vessels in active rotations on a world map, with current ETA information overlaid for each vessel's next port of call. The dashboard must show vessel icons color-coded by schedule status — green for on-time (ETA within plus or minus 6 hours of plan), amber for moderate delay (6-24 hours), red for significant delay (over 24 hours), and blue for ahead of schedule. Clicking on a vessel must display a detail panel showing the full rotation schedule with planned and current ETAs for all upcoming ports, the vessel's current speed and heading, distance to next port, and any active alerts or advisories. The dashboard must support filtering by service loop, entity, trade lane, and schedule status. Real-time AIS position data must refresh at configurable intervals (default every 5 minutes). The map must render port names and vessel names in the user's selected language (English or Arabic) and must be performant with at least 200 simultaneously tracked vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R011	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Automated Cutoff Time Recalculation on ETD Change	When the ETD for a vessel at a loading port changes, the system must automatically recalculate all associated cargo cutoff times and communicate the updated cutoffs to affected parties. Cutoff recalculation must maintain the configured time offset from the ETD for each cutoff type — documentation cutoff (e.g., ETD minus 48 hours), VGM (Verified Gross Mass) submission cutoff (e.g., ETD minus 24 hours), reefer container reception cutoff (e.g., ETD minus 18 hours), dangerous goods cutoff (e.g., ETD minus 72 hours), and standard dry container gate-in cutoff (e.g., ETD minus 8 hours). If a recalculated cutoff falls in the past (i.e., the ETD has advanced earlier than originally planned), the system must flag the affected cutoff type and alert operations to determine whether the cutoff can be extended or whether cargo acceptance must be restricted. Updated cutoffs must be published to the customer portal, communicated to freight forwarders via EDI or API, and displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats with local timezone conversion for each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R012	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Historical ETA Deviation Root Cause Classification	The system must maintain a structured root cause classification system for all ETA deviations, enabling systematic analysis of schedule reliability drivers. Root cause categories must include weather and sea state delays, port congestion and berth unavailability, terminal equipment breakdown, vessel mechanical issues, canal transit delays (Suez or Panama), security incidents, regulatory holds (customs, port state control), labor disputes or strikes, and commercial speed reductions (slow steaming for fuel savings). Each port call must be tagged with the applicable root cause when actual arrival deviates from the planned ETA by more than a configurable threshold (default 3 hours). The AI engine must suggest the most likely root cause based on contextual data (weather conditions, port congestion reports, vessel speed profile) and present it for operator confirmation. Root cause data must feed into the AI prediction models to improve future ETA accuracy. Monthly and quarterly reports must aggregate root cause statistics by entity, trade lane, and port, supporting both English and Arabic presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R013	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Multi-Leg Transshipment ETA Coordination	For cargo requiring transshipment at hub ports (Jebel Ali, Hamad Port, or Colombo), the system must coordinate ETAs across the feeding vessel and the connecting mother vessel to ensure cargo makes the planned transshipment connection. The system must track the ETA of the inbound feeder vessel, the minimum connection time required at the hub (configurable per hub port and per cargo type — typically 24-48 hours for standard containers, 12-24 hours for time-sensitive reefer cargo), and the ETD of the outbound connecting vessel. When the feeder vessel's ETA slips to a point where the minimum connection time can no longer be met, the system must immediately alert the transshipment planning team and recommend alternative connections — either a later sailing on the same service loop or an alternative service with a valid routing to the cargo's final destination. The financial impact of missed connections must be estimated including additional storage charges at the hub in the local currency (AED for Jebel Ali, QAR for Hamad Port), additional transit time, and customer compensation liability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R014	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	ETA-Based Yard Planning Integration	The system must provide ETA and cargo manifest data to terminal yard planning systems at sufficient lead times to enable efficient yard pre-planning. At minimum 48 hours before the vessel's ETA, the system must transmit the expected discharge and loading container lists with bay-level stowage positions to the terminal operating system via standard BAPLIE EDI messages or API integration. As the ETA is updated, the system must flag whether the update necessitates a yard plan revision — for example, if the ETA shifts to a different terminal operating shift, the yard plan may need to be reallocated to different yard blocks. For JNPT terminals, where yard congestion is a frequent constraint, the system must provide a 72-hour advance notification of expected import container volumes by size type (20-foot and 40-foot) and cargo category (dry, reefer, dangerous goods, over-dimensional). The integration must support both push notifications from the schedule system and pull queries from terminal systems, with all data exchanges logged for audit and dispute resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R015	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Customer Self-Service ETA Tracking Portal	The system must provide a customer-facing self-service portal where shippers, consignees, and freight forwarders can track the real-time ETA for their specific shipments without requiring manual inquiry to the shipping line's customer service team. Customers must be able to search by booking number, bill of lading number, or container number and view the current ETA at the port of discharge, the vessel name and current position on a map, the schedule status indicator (on-time, delayed, or ahead of schedule), and the history of ETA changes with timestamps and reason descriptions. The portal must support user registration with role-based access — shippers can see their export shipments, consignees can see their import shipments, and freight forwarders can see all shipments under their agency. The portal must be fully bilingual in English and Arabic with complete RTL support, must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats based on user preference, and must be accessible on mobile devices with responsive design. The portal must also allow customers to subscribe to push notifications for specific shipments with configurable alert thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R016	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	ETA Data Sharing via DCSA Track and Trace Standards	The system must expose ETA and vessel schedule data through APIs compliant with the Digital Container Shipping Association (DCSA) Track and Trace standard version 2.2 or later. The API must implement the DCSA event model including transport events for vessel arrival and departure at ports, equipment events for container gate-in and gate-out, and shipment events for documentation milestones. Event timestamps must use ISO 8601 format with timezone offset. The API must support both push (webhook subscription) and pull (polling) consumption models, with configurable webhook retry logic for failed deliveries. Authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with API key-based rate limiting. The API must serve responses within 500 milliseconds for single-shipment queries and within 3 seconds for bulk queries of up to 100 shipments. All API responses must include the event source, confidence level of ETA predictions, and links to related events. The system must maintain API versioning with a minimum 12-month deprecation notice for breaking changes, and must provide a developer portal with bilingual documentation in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R017	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	Offline ETA Access and Manual Override Capability	The system must support scenarios where automated ETA calculation is unavailable due to AIS signal loss, system maintenance, or connectivity issues at remote locations. Operations staff must be able to manually input or override ETA and ETD values with mandatory fields for the override reason, the source of the manual estimate (e.g., direct vessel communication via satellite phone, port agent report, or master's noon report), and the confidence level of the manual estimate. Manual overrides must be clearly flagged in the system interface with a distinct visual indicator differentiating them from AI-calculated values. When automated calculation resumes, the system must compare the manual override against the newly calculated value and alert the operator if they differ by more than a configurable threshold (default 4 hours), requiring the operator to either accept the system calculation or re-confirm the manual override. All manual overrides must be logged in the audit trail with the operator's identity, timestamp, previous value, new value, and stated reason. The offline capability must cache the last known positions and ETAs for all active vessels, allowing read-only access in the absence of network connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-1-004-R018	FEAT-052-1-004	MOD-052	ETA Integration with Inland Logistics Planning	The system must share ETA data with inland logistics planning modules and external trucking or rail coordination systems to enable end-to-end supply chain visibility. When the vessel ETA at the port of discharge is updated, the system must recalculate the estimated container availability date at the terminal, accounting for terminal discharge sequence, customs clearance lead time (which varies by jurisdiction — typically 24-48 hours at JNPT, 4-12 hours at Jebel Ali under pre-clearance, 24 hours at Hamad Port), and container inspection or scanning requirements. The updated container availability date must be transmitted to connected TMS (Transportation Management Systems) and customer logistics platforms via API or EDI (IFTMIN/IFTSTA messages). For Qatar and UAE free zone operations (e.g., JAFZA at Jebel Ali, QFZ at Hamad Port), the system must factor in free zone customs processing times specific to each zone. The integration must support multi-currency cost estimates for last-mile delivery, presented in the destination country's currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India), enabling customers to plan trucking dispatch aligned with the actual container readiness rather than the original schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R001	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Multi-Port Rotation Optimization Algorithm	The system must implement a constrained optimization algorithm that evaluates all feasible port rotation sequences for a given vessel across the configured trade lanes including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). The algorithm must consider minimum and maximum port stay durations, berth window availability from port authorities, cargo volume commitments per port, and contractual obligations such as fixed-day-of-week calls. The optimization must support up to 12 ports per rotation and return results ranked by total voyage cost, transit time, and cargo revenue potential within 30 seconds for standard rotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R002	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Bunker Cost Integration in Voyage Plans	The AI optimization engine must integrate real-time and forecasted bunker fuel prices from all configured bunkering ports including Fujairah, Singapore, and Jebel Ali when computing optimal voyage plans. The system must support VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, and LNG fuel types with per-port pricing feeds updated at minimum every 4 hours. Multi-currency pricing must be supported with automatic conversion between USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR using the platform exchange rate service. The engine must factor in bunker procurement lead times, tank capacity constraints, and minimum ROB (Remaining on Board) requirements per flag state regulation when recommending bunkering ports and quantities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R003	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Canal Transit Window Optimization	The system must optimize voyage plans to align with canal transit booking windows for Suez Canal and any other configured canal or strait passage. The engine must integrate Suez Canal Authority convoy schedules including northbound and southbound transit slots, calculate optimal arrival times at canal approaches factoring vessel draft and beam restrictions, and automatically compute canal toll estimates based on Suez Canal Net Tonnage (SCNT). When multiple transit window options exist, the engine must evaluate the cost trade-off between waiting at anchor versus adjusting speed on the preceding leg to arrive precisely at the optimal transit slot, presenting recommendations in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R007	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Real-Time Voyage Plan Re-Optimization	The system must support real-time re-optimization of active voyage plans when significant deviations occur from the original plan. Triggering events for re-optimization must include vessel speed deviation exceeding 1.5 knots from planned speed for more than 6 hours, unplanned port omission or additional port call requests, berth window changes communicated by port authorities, weather routing diversions exceeding 50 nautical miles from planned track, and bunker price changes exceeding 10 percent at planned bunkering ports. The re-optimization must preserve all existing cargo commitments as hard constraints and present the revised plan alongside the original for comparison with delta cost analysis displayed in the operating entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R008	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Voyage Plan Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for AI-generated voyage plans before they are activated and communicated to vessels and port agents. The workflow must support role-based approval chains with minimum two levels: operations planner and operations manager. Approval screens must display the complete voyage plan summary including all port calls, ETAs, ETDs, planned speeds, bunker plan, estimated voyage cost breakdown, and cargo utilization percentage. The approval interface must be fully functional in both English and Arabic with RTL layout support. Approved plans must be digitally timestamped with the approver identity, and the system must maintain a complete audit trail of all plan versions and approval decisions including Hijri calendar date references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R009	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	AI Confidence Scoring for Voyage Plans	Each AI-generated voyage plan must include a confidence score expressed as a percentage from 0 to 100, reflecting the engine's assessment of plan feasibility and cost accuracy. The confidence score must be decomposed into sub-scores covering schedule reliability based on historical port performance, bunker cost estimate accuracy based on fuel price volatility, weather risk exposure based on seasonal patterns for the planned route, and cargo commitment fulfillment probability. Plans with an overall confidence score below 70 percent must be flagged for manual review and cannot proceed through automated approval workflows. The scoring methodology documentation must be available to users in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R010	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Emission Compliance Constraint Integration	The voyage optimization engine must integrate IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) and Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) constraints as optimization parameters. The system must calculate the projected CII rating (A through E) for each proposed voyage plan and ensure vessel annual CII targets are not exceeded by any single voyage plan. EU ETS carbon cost projections must be included in voyage cost calculations for vessels trading in EU-applicable routes. The engine must support what-if analysis showing how different speed and routing choices affect the vessel annual CII trajectory, enabling planners to balance commercial objectives against environmental compliance requirements across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R011	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Historical Voyage Performance Benchmarking	The system must maintain a historical voyage performance database that the AI optimization engine uses for continuous model improvement and benchmarking. Each completed voyage must be compared against its original AI-optimized plan across key performance indicators including actual versus planned fuel consumption, actual versus planned port stay durations, actual versus planned voyage cost per TEU, and schedule reliability measured as arrival within 6 hours of planned ETA. Benchmarking reports must be generated per vessel, per trade lane, and per operating entity. Performance trends must be visualized on dashboards with data filterable by Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods, and reports must be exportable in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R012	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Vessel Technical Constraint Modeling	The optimization engine must incorporate vessel-specific technical constraints from the vessel master data module when generating voyage plans. These constraints must include maximum and minimum operational draft per port considering UKC (Under Keel Clearance) requirements at draft-restricted ports such as JNPT where tidal windows are critical, air draft limitations for passage under bridges, maximum cargo intake by weight and TEU capacity including on-deck and under-deck splits, reefer plug capacity, dangerous goods capacity per IMDG class, and vessel speed-fuel consumption curves specific to each vessel hull condition. The system must reject any voyage plan that violates vessel technical constraints and provide clear explanations of which constraints are binding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R013	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Parallel Scenario Comparison Interface	The system must provide a scenario comparison interface allowing operations planners to view up to four AI-generated voyage plan alternatives side by side. Each scenario must display total voyage cost, total voyage duration, fuel consumption, cargo utilization, CII impact, and port-by-port schedule details. The comparison must highlight differences between scenarios using color coding and delta values for all numeric fields. Users must be able to adjust individual parameters such as maximum speed, port stay duration, or bunkering port selection in any scenario and trigger a re-optimization. The comparison interface must support full Arabic RTL rendering and allow export of the comparison as a PDF report with the company letterhead and bilingual labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R014	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Integration with Vessel Stowage Planning	The voyage optimization engine must exchange data bidirectionally with the vessel stowage planning module to ensure voyage plans reflect actual cargo loading sequences and port rotation feasibility. When the stowage plan indicates that a specific port call sequence would require excessive container shifts (restows exceeding 5 percent of discharge volume), the optimization engine must flag the rotation and suggest alternative sequences that minimize restow moves. The integration must account for container weight distribution requirements, stability calculations, and lashing pattern constraints. Data exchange must occur via internal service APIs with sub-second response times and must support the container type codes used across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R015	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Offline Voyage Plan Access and Sync	The system must support offline access to approved voyage plans for vessel-side users operating in areas with limited or no internet connectivity, which is common during ocean passages in the Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean. Offline-capable data must include the complete voyage plan with all port call details, waypoints, speed instructions, bunker plan, and cargo summary. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize any vessel-reported deviations or position updates with the shore-side optimization engine and trigger re-optimization if deviation thresholds are exceeded. Conflict resolution for simultaneous shore-side and vessel-side edits must follow a last-write-wins policy with full audit logging of all changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-001-R016	FEAT-052-2-001	MOD-052	Regulatory Port Restriction Enforcement	The AI optimization engine must enforce all configured regulatory port restrictions when generating voyage plans. These restrictions include port state control detention risk assessments per vessel flag and age, sanction screening for ports and counterparties per OFAC, EU, and UN sanctions lists, cabotage restrictions that prevent foreign-flagged vessels from carrying domestic cargo between ports within the same country such as Indian coastal trade restrictions, and environmental zone speed limits such as the Particularly Sensitive Sea Area (PSSA) in the Arabian Gulf. The system must automatically exclude non-compliant port call options and log all regulatory constraint evaluations with timestamps and rule references for compliance audit purposes across all operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R001	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Vessel Speed-Consumption Curve Modeling	The system must maintain and utilize vessel-specific speed-consumption curves that define the relationship between sailing speed in knots and fuel consumption in metric tons per day for each fuel type (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO, LNG). Curves must be stored per vessel and per hull condition state (clean hull, 6-month fouled, 12-month fouled) to reflect real-world performance degradation. The system must support cubic polynomial curve fitting based on noon report data collected over the previous 12 months, with automatic curve recalibration triggered when more than 30 new data points are collected. Each curve must be validated against ISO 19030 hull and propeller performance standards, and the system must alert when actual consumption deviates more than 8 percent from the modeled curve.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R002	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Interactive Speed-Cost Slider Interface	The system must provide an interactive slider-based interface that allows planners to adjust vessel speed for individual voyage legs and immediately see the resulting impact on fuel cost, voyage duration, and estimated arrival time at the next port. The slider must operate in 0.1 knot increments between the vessel minimum maneuvering speed and maximum service speed. As the slider moves, the interface must dynamically update fuel consumption in metric tons, fuel cost in the operating entity currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), CO2 emissions in metric tons, voyage leg duration in hours and minutes, and CII impact. The interface must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with all numeric values formatted per the active locale settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R003	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Multi-Leg Speed Profile Optimization	The system must support optimization of speed profiles across multiple consecutive voyage legs simultaneously rather than optimizing each leg in isolation. Given a fixed arrival deadline at the final port of a multi-leg voyage, the engine must distribute available slack time across legs to minimize total fuel consumption, accounting for the non-linear relationship between speed and fuel consumption where slowing down on longer legs yields proportionally greater fuel savings. The optimization must respect per-leg minimum speed constraints such as minimum speed for reefer cargo temperature maintenance, port cut-off deadlines at intermediate ports, and canal transit timing windows. Results must be presented showing the per-leg recommended speed, fuel saving versus uniform speed, and total voyage cost comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R004	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Fuel Cost Sensitivity Analysis	The system must provide fuel cost sensitivity analysis showing how voyage economics change under different bunker price scenarios. The analysis must model at minimum five scenarios: current market price, plus 10 percent, minus 10 percent, 6-month forward curve price, and user-defined custom price. For each scenario, the system must calculate total voyage fuel cost, optimal speed profile that minimizes total cost, break-even speed at which slowing down no longer provides net savings after accounting for daily vessel operating costs and time charter rates, and the delta in per-TEU transport cost. All monetary values must be displayed in the operating entity base currency with the option to toggle between USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. Sensitivity charts must support export as PNG and PDF formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R005	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Time Charter Equivalent Impact Calculation	The system must calculate and display the Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) impact for each speed option evaluated in the trade-off analysis. TCE must be computed as gross freight revenue minus voyage costs including bunker, port dues, and canal tolls, divided by the number of voyage days. The system must show how TCE changes as speed varies, identifying the speed that maximizes TCE for the given cargo and freight rate. For vessels on time charter, the system must compare the charter hire daily rate against the marginal cost of additional voyage days to determine whether slow steaming generates net savings. Multi-currency support must allow TCE to be computed in USD as the industry standard while also displaying equivalent values in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR per the operating entity preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R006	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Weather Impact on Speed-Fuel Relationship	The system must adjust speed-fuel trade-off calculations based on forecasted weather conditions along the planned route. The adjustment model must account for head seas, beam seas, and following seas with Beaufort scale force levels, ocean current effects on speed over ground versus speed through water, wind resistance factors based on vessel windage area and container stack height, and sea state-induced added resistance using the Stawave method or equivalent hydrodynamic model. When weather conditions are expected to reduce vessel speed by more than 1 knot from calm water performance, the system must automatically recalculate the trade-off analysis and present revised optimal speed recommendations with explanatory notes available in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R007	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Emission Reduction Quantification per Speed Option	For each speed option in the trade-off analysis, the system must calculate and display total CO2 emissions in metric tons using IMO-standard emission factors per fuel type, SOx emissions based on fuel sulfur content and scrubber status, NOx emissions based on engine tier classification per MARPOL Annex VI, and equivalent CII operational carbon intensity in grams of CO2 per ton-nautical mile. The system must quantify the emission reduction achieved by each speed reduction increment compared to the maximum speed baseline. For routes subject to EU ETS, the system must estimate the carbon cost in EUR and convert to the operating entity currency. Emission data must be stored per voyage leg for regulatory reporting aggregation across the annual CII calculation period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R008	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Draft and Trim Impact on Fuel Efficiency	The system must incorporate vessel draft and trim conditions into the speed-fuel trade-off calculations. The model must account for increased resistance at deeper drafts and the fuel efficiency gains achievable through optimal trim settings. The system must reference vessel-specific trim optimization tables or CFD-derived data to recommend the optimal trim for each speed and draft combination. When the planned cargo loading results in a suboptimal trim condition, the system must estimate the additional fuel consumption penalty in metric tons per day and total additional cost for the voyage leg, suggesting ballast adjustments where operationally feasible. Draft restrictions at destination ports such as JNPT tidal berth limitations must be cross-referenced to ensure recommended loading conditions are compatible with port access requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R009	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Fleet-Wide Speed Policy Management	The system must support the definition and enforcement of fleet-wide speed policies that set maximum and minimum speed limits by trade lane, vessel class, and time period. Policies must be configurable per operating entity with examples including a Gulf-India trade lane maximum speed of 14 knots during low-demand periods and a minimum speed of 10 knots to maintain schedule frequency. When a planner attempts to set a voyage speed outside the active policy limits, the system must display a warning and require manager-level override approval. Speed policy changes must be logged with effective date, approver, and business justification. Policy dashboards must show fleet-wide compliance rates and estimated fuel savings achieved through speed management policies, with all text and date formats supporting English, Arabic, and Hijri calendar display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R010	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Historical Speed-Consumption Deviation Analysis	The system must provide analytical reports comparing planned versus actual speed and fuel consumption for completed voyages. The analysis must identify systematic deviations such as consistent over-consumption at specific speed ranges, performance degradation trends indicating hull fouling, and route-specific anomalies where actual consumption consistently exceeds model predictions. Reports must be filterable by vessel, trade lane, date range using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and operating entity. Statistical measures must include mean deviation, standard deviation, and 95th percentile deviation. The system must use deviation analysis results to automatically flag vessels requiring hull cleaning or propeller polishing and must trigger recalibration of the speed-consumption curve when cumulative deviation exceeds configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R011	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Auxiliary Engine Consumption Modeling	The speed-fuel trade-off analysis must include auxiliary engine fuel consumption in addition to main engine consumption to provide a complete fuel cost picture. Auxiliary consumption must be modeled separately for at-sea steaming, port approach and departure maneuvering, and in-port cargo operations including crane operations and reefer container power supply. The system must account for the fact that reefer-heavy voyages incur significantly higher auxiliary consumption, particularly on routes where reefer cargo percentages exceed 20 percent of total TEU such as certain India export trade lanes. Auxiliary consumption estimates must be adjustable based on ambient temperature forecasts, as higher temperatures in Gulf ports during summer months increase generator load for reefer containers and accommodation cooling systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R012	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Speed Optimization for Just-In-Time Arrival	The system must implement a just-in-time arrival speed calculator that determines the optimal speed to arrive at port precisely when the berth becomes available, thereby eliminating or minimizing anchorage waiting time. The calculator must integrate with port community system APIs for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to retrieve real-time berth availability updates. When a berth delay notification is received, the system must immediately recalculate the recommended approach speed and display the fuel savings achievable by slowing down versus maintaining current speed and waiting at anchor. The system must account for port-specific pilotage booking lead times, VTS reporting requirements, and tidal window constraints that may override the pure speed optimization recommendation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R013	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Charter Party Speed and Consumption Warranty Monitoring	For vessels operating under charter party agreements, the system must monitor actual performance against charter party warranted speed and consumption figures. The system must calculate the good weather performance by filtering out periods where wind exceeded Beaufort Force 4 or significant wave height exceeded 2.5 meters, consistent with standard charter party weather exclusion clauses. When actual performance falls below warranted levels, the system must calculate the overconsumption in metric tons and equivalent cost for potential off-hire or consumption claims. Conversely, when performance exceeds warranty, under-consumption credits must be tracked. All claim calculations must reference the charter party currency and include supporting noon report data extracts formatted for dispute resolution documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R014	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Speed Trade-Off Dashboard Visualization	The system must provide a dedicated dashboard for speed versus fuel trade-off visualization with interactive charts including a speed-consumption curve plot with current operating point highlighted, a Pareto frontier chart showing the trade-off between voyage duration and fuel cost with each point representing a feasible speed profile, a time-series chart of historical speed and consumption data for the selected vessel, and a bar chart comparing fuel cost per TEU at different speed options. All charts must support tooltips showing detailed data values on hover, must be responsive across desktop and tablet screen sizes, and must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic-language users. Dashboard data must refresh automatically every 15 minutes when connected and must display the last-updated timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R015	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Multi-Fuel Speed Optimization for Dual-Fuel Vessels	For dual-fuel vessels capable of operating on both conventional fuel oil and LNG, the system must extend the speed-fuel trade-off analysis to evaluate optimal fuel switching strategies at different speeds. The analysis must model the methane slip characteristics at various engine loads, the LNG boil-off rate and its impact on available fuel inventory, the price differential between LNG and VLSFO at bunkering ports in the Gulf region, and the emission compliance benefits of LNG operation including reduced SOx and PM emissions. The system must recommend the optimal combination of speed and fuel mode for each voyage leg that minimizes total cost while meeting emission constraints, presenting results in a unified comparison table with costs in the operating entity currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-002-R016	FEAT-052-2-002	MOD-052	Reporting and Export of Speed Analysis Results	The system must support comprehensive export of speed versus fuel trade-off analysis results in multiple formats including PDF, Excel, and CSV. PDF exports must include a formatted report with company branding, analysis parameters, charts, and recommendations in either English or Arabic based on user preference. Excel exports must include raw data tables with all speed increments evaluated, fuel consumption figures, cost calculations, and emission estimates to enable further offline analysis. All exported reports must include metadata such as analysis date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, vessel name and IMO number, voyage reference, analyst name, and a disclaimer noting that results are based on modeled performance curves. CSV exports must use the appropriate decimal separator and date format per the user locale configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R001	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Weather Data Provider Integration	The system must integrate with at minimum two independent weather data providers to source meteorological and oceanographic forecast data covering all operating regions including the Arabian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Arabian Sea, Indian Ocean, and Red Sea. Data must include wind speed and direction at 10-meter height, significant wave height and peak period, swell height direction and period, ocean surface currents, visibility, and precipitation. Forecast data must be ingested at minimum 6-hourly temporal resolution and 0.25 degree spatial resolution, with forecast horizons extending to at least 10 days. The system must implement automatic failover between providers if the primary source becomes unavailable and must log all data quality metrics including forecast age and coverage completeness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R002	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Dynamic Weather Overlay on Route Charts	The system must display weather forecast data as interactive overlays on the electronic chart display used for voyage planning. Overlays must include color-coded wind barbs, wave height contours, current vector arrows, visibility zones, and tropical cyclone track forecasts with uncertainty cones. Users must be able to animate the weather overlay through time using a timeline slider to visualize how conditions evolve along the planned route over the voyage duration. The overlay must support layer toggling to show or hide individual weather parameters. The chart rendering must function in both LTR and RTL interface modes and must display coordinate labels in the Arabic-Indic numeral system when the Arabic locale is active. Performance must maintain at least 30 frames per second during animation on standard workstation hardware.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R003	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Weather Risk Assessment per Voyage Leg	The system must generate a weather risk assessment for each leg of a planned voyage, categorizing risk as low, moderate, high, or extreme based on forecasted conditions along the route. Risk assessment criteria must include maximum expected wave height exceeding vessel operational limits, sustained wind speeds exceeding Beaufort Force 8, tropical cyclone proximity within 300 nautical miles of the planned track, monsoon-related conditions in the Arabian Sea during June through September affecting India-bound vessels, and Shamal wind events in the Arabian Gulf during winter and summer seasons. Each risk assessment must include a narrative summary generated by the AI engine describing the key weather threats and their expected timing, available in both English and Arabic. High and extreme risk assessments must trigger automatic notifications to the operations manager role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R004	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Alternative Route Generation for Weather Avoidance	When the weather risk assessment for any voyage leg is rated high or extreme, the system must automatically generate up to three alternative route options that avoid the adverse weather while still meeting cargo delivery commitments. Each alternative must be evaluated against the original route showing the additional distance in nautical miles, additional fuel consumption in metric tons, additional voyage time in hours, estimated cost impact in the operating entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR), and the resulting weather risk reduction. The route generation algorithm must respect navigational constraints including traffic separation schemes, restricted areas, territorial waters requiring permits, and the vessel draft limitations. Users must be able to select an alternative route and have it replace the original plan with a single approval action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R005	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Monsoon Season Planning Support	The system must provide specialized planning support for the Indian Ocean monsoon seasons that significantly impact vessel operations between Gulf ports and JNPT. For the southwest monsoon period from June through September, the system must apply enhanced safety margins to wave height and wind speed forecasts for vessels transiting the Arabian Sea, recommend route deviations south of the typical great circle track to avoid the worst monsoon conditions off the Indian west coast, adjust port stay estimates at JNPT to account for monsoon-related cargo handling delays and pilot availability restrictions, and factor in increased fuel consumption due to adverse sea states. The planning interface must display monsoon season indicators and historical monsoon severity data for the relevant years, with all textual information available in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R006	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Shamal Wind Event Detection and Routing	The system must specifically detect and respond to Shamal wind events that affect vessel operations in the Arabian Gulf, particularly impacting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali. The detection system must monitor weather forecasts for northwesterly wind events exceeding 25 knots sustained over 12 or more hours with associated sea states exceeding 2 meters significant wave height in the shallow Gulf waters. When a Shamal event is predicted, the system must assess the impact on port approach and berthing safety at affected ports, recommend schedule adjustments such as early departure before the event onset or delayed arrival after conditions moderate, and estimate the cargo handling disruption window. Historical Shamal event data covering at minimum the past 5 years must be maintained to support pattern recognition and seasonal planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R007	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Tropical Cyclone Tracking and Avoidance	The system must integrate tropical cyclone tracking data from recognized meteorological agencies including the India Meteorological Department (IMD) for the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal, and the Joint Typhoon Warning Center (JTWC). For each active or developing cyclone system, the system must display the current position, forecast track with 12 to 120 hour predictions, wind radii for 34-knot 50-knot and 64-knot winds, and the uncertainty cone. The voyage planning engine must automatically evaluate whether any planned route intersects with the forecast cyclone path including the uncertainty cone with a configurable safety buffer defaulting to 250 nautical miles. When intersection is detected, the system must prevent voyage plan approval until an alternative route is selected and must generate an alert to all relevant stakeholders with bilingual notifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R008	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Sea State Impact on Container Safety	The system must evaluate forecasted sea state conditions against container lashing safety thresholds to identify risks of container loss or damage during the voyage. The evaluation must consider parametric and synchronous rolling risk based on vessel stability characteristics and predicted wave encounter angles, green water loading risk on deck containers when forecasted wave heights approach vessel freeboard limits, and acceleration forces on container stacks at various positions (forward, aft, on-deck, under-deck) based on vessel motion predictions. When the analysis indicates container safety risk exceeding acceptable thresholds, the system must recommend speed reduction, course alteration, or route deviation and must flag specific bay positions on the vessel where container stacking height or weight should be limited for the affected voyage legs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R009	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Weather Window Scheduling for Port Operations	The system must integrate weather forecasts into port operation scheduling to identify optimal weather windows for cargo handling at ports where operations are weather-sensitive. For JNPT, the system must account for monsoon-season swell conditions that restrict container crane operations when wind speeds exceed 55 kilometers per hour or wave-induced vessel movement exceeds safe crane operating limits. For Gulf ports during Shamal events, the system must predict periods when sand and dust storms reduce visibility below port operational minimums. The system must recommend arrival timing that maximizes the overlap between berth availability and favorable weather windows, and must estimate the probability of weather-related cargo handling delays in hours for each port call. Delay probability estimates must be factored into the voyage schedule with appropriate buffer times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R010	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Ocean Current Routing Optimization	The system must incorporate ocean surface current data into route optimization calculations to leverage favorable currents and avoid adverse ones. Current data must cover the key current systems affecting the operating region including the Somali Current which reverses direction seasonally with the monsoon, the North Equatorial Current, and the circulation patterns within the Arabian Gulf. The routing algorithm must calculate speed over ground adjusted for current effects at each waypoint along the route and optimize the route to maximize favorable current assistance. The system must quantify the fuel saving or additional consumption attributable to ocean current effects for each route option, expressed in metric tons and equivalent cost in the operating entity currency. Current data must be updated at minimum daily and must include both analysis (hindcast) and forecast products.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R011	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Weather Forecast Accuracy Tracking	The system must continuously track the accuracy of weather forecasts used in voyage planning by comparing forecasted conditions against actual conditions reported via vessel noon reports and automatic weather station data. Accuracy metrics must be computed per weather parameter (wind speed, wave height, current), per forecast lead time (24h, 48h, 72h, 120h), and per geographic region. When forecast accuracy for a specific provider or region falls below configurable thresholds, the system must increase safety margins applied to that provider data in route optimization calculations and must alert the operations team. Accuracy reports must be generated monthly and stored for trend analysis over at least 36 months, with report formatting supporting both English and Arabic languages and dates displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R012	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Crew Safety Weather Alerts	The system must generate crew safety weather alerts when forecasted conditions along the planned route exceed predefined safety thresholds. Alert thresholds must be configurable per vessel class and must cover wind speed exceeding Beaufort Force 9, significant wave height exceeding 6 meters, visibility below 1 nautical mile, lightning storm probability exceeding 60 percent within 50 nautical miles of the vessel position, and extreme heat index above 50 degrees Celsius for deck operations in the Arabian Gulf during summer months. Alerts must be transmitted to the vessel Master via the integrated communication system and must include recommended precautionary actions such as securing deck cargo, restricting deck access, or adjusting heading to reduce vessel motion. Alert messages must be generated in English as the vessel operational language with Arabic translation available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R013	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Tidal Data Integration for Port Approach Planning	The system must integrate tidal prediction data for all configured ports to support draft-constrained port approach planning. Tidal data must include predicted high and low water times and heights for at least 30 days ahead, tidal stream rates and directions for port approaches and channels, and tidal window calculations for draft-restricted vessel arrivals. For JNPT where tidal range significantly affects channel depth and berth accessibility for deep-draft vessels, the system must calculate the earliest and latest safe transit times based on vessel arrival draft and required under-keel clearance. The system must present tidal windows graphically on the voyage timeline and must automatically adjust ETA recommendations to align with safe tidal windows. Tidal predictions must reference standard port chart datum and must be validated against published tide tables from the relevant hydrographic offices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R014	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Weather Routing Audit Trail and Compliance	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all weather routing decisions made during voyage planning and execution. The audit trail must record the weather data sources used and their forecast timestamps, the risk assessments generated for each route option, any route deviations recommended or implemented due to weather, the approver identity and timestamp for weather-related route changes, and the actual weather conditions encountered versus the forecast used for decision-making. This audit trail must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to support maritime casualty investigation requirements, Protection and Indemnity club inquiries, and regulatory compliance reviews. Audit reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual English and Arabic content and must include both Gregorian and Hijri dates for all recorded timestamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R015	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Offline Weather Data Caching for Vessel Systems	The system must support caching of weather forecast data on vessel-side systems to enable weather routing functionality during periods of limited satellite connectivity. The cache must store weather data for the vessel planned route corridor extending 200 nautical miles on either side of the planned track and covering the forecast period for the current and next two voyage legs. Cache updates must be prioritized during connectivity windows with differential updates to minimize bandwidth consumption over costly VSAT links. When operating from cached data, the system must clearly indicate the forecast age and must apply progressively wider safety margins as the cached data ages beyond 12, 24, and 48 hours. The total cache storage requirement must not exceed 500 megabytes per vessel to accommodate typical vessel IT infrastructure constraints.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R016	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Seasonal Weather Pattern Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a seasonal weather pattern analytics dashboard that aggregates historical weather data across the operating region to support long-term voyage planning and schedule design. The dashboard must display monthly and seasonal statistics for wave height, wind speed, visibility, and cyclone frequency across defined route corridors between Gulf ports and JNPT. Visualization must include heat maps showing geographic distribution of adverse weather frequency by month, trend charts showing year-over-year changes in monsoon onset and intensity, and probability distributions of voyage delay days attributable to weather by route and season. Data must cover at minimum the past 10 years of historical weather observations. The dashboard must support filtering by Gregorian months and Hijri months and must render all labels, legends, and tooltips in both English and Arabic based on the user language preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-003-R017	FEAT-052-2-003	MOD-052	Piracy and Maritime Security Zone Integration	The system must integrate maritime security risk zone data from recognized sources such as the United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO), the Maritime Security Centre Horn of Africa (MSCHOA), and the International Maritime Bureau Piracy Reporting Centre. Security risk zones must be displayed on the route planning chart with current threat levels and recommended transit corridors. The voyage planning engine must evaluate planned routes against active security advisories and must recommend route adjustments to avoid high-risk areas or to align with recommended transit corridors and group transit arrangements. When a planned route traverses a designated high-risk area, the system must flag the requirement for additional security measures such as citadel preparation, armed guards, or reporting to naval coordination centres. Security zone data must be updated at minimum daily.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R001	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Annual Fleet Deployment Plan Management	The system must support the creation, modification, and approval of annual fleet deployment plans that assign vessels to trade lanes for each month of the planning year. The deployment plan must accommodate both Gregorian and Hijri calendar year planning horizons to support entities operating on different fiscal calendars, such as KSA entities that may use the Hijri fiscal year. Each plan must specify the vessel, assigned trade lane or service loop, planned round voyage duration, planned number of voyages per month, and expected TEU capacity deployment. The system must support multiple plan versions with comparison capability and must enforce an approval workflow requiring sign-off from fleet management before a plan version becomes the active baseline. The plan must cover all vessels across the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operating entities with consolidated and per-entity views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R002	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Demand Forecast Integration for Capacity Planning	The system must integrate cargo demand forecasts from the commercial module to inform vessel deployment decisions. Demand forecasts must be ingested at the trade lane and port pair level with monthly granularity for at least 12 months forward. The system must compare projected demand in TEU per direction against planned vessel capacity deployment to identify periods and routes where demand exceeds capacity or where significant overcapacity exists. Gap analysis results must be displayed on a dashboard with color-coded indicators showing capacity utilization percentage per trade lane per month. When demand-capacity gaps exceed configurable thresholds such as utilization above 95 percent or below 60 percent, the system must generate redeployment recommendations. All capacity figures must account for vessel-specific TEU intake limitations based on trade-lane average cargo weights and container type mix.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R003	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Dry Dock and Maintenance Schedule Integration	The system must integrate vessel dry dock and major maintenance schedules into the deployment plan to ensure accurate capacity availability projections. Dry dock periods must be sourced from the technical management module and must include scheduled dry dockings per classification society requirements typically at 5-year intervals, intermediate surveys, and planned major equipment overhauls. The system must calculate the capacity impact of each vessel absence in TEU-weeks and must identify periods where multiple vessel absences from the same trade lane create unacceptable capacity shortfalls. When capacity shortfalls are identified, the system must suggest mitigation options including temporary vessel redeployment from other trade lanes, spot charter vessel procurement with estimated cost in USD and entity local currency, or service frequency reduction with estimated revenue impact. Dry dock schedule changes must trigger automatic re-evaluation of the deployment plan.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R004	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Seasonal Demand Variation Modeling	The system must model and account for seasonal demand variations in the deployment planning process. The model must capture recurring seasonal patterns including pre-Ramadan and Eid import surges into Gulf ports, Indian agricultural export seasons affecting container demand at JNPT and other Indian ports, summer peak season for Gulf imports of consumer goods and building materials, winter Shamal impact on Gulf port productivity reducing effective capacity, and Hajj season logistics surges at Saudi ports. The system must store at least 3 years of historical seasonal demand data and use machine learning models to predict seasonal patterns for the planning horizon. Seasonal adjustment factors must be applied per trade lane and per direction to produce realistic monthly capacity requirement estimates. All seasonal events must be displayed with both Gregorian and Hijri date references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R005	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Vessel Swap and Redeployment Scenario Planning	The system must provide a scenario planning tool that allows fleet managers to model vessel swap and redeployment options before committing to changes. The tool must support drag-and-drop vessel assignment changes on a visual timeline grid showing vessels on the vertical axis and time periods on the horizontal axis. When a vessel is moved between trade lanes, the system must immediately recalculate and display the impact on capacity coverage for both the origin and destination trade lanes, the change in vessel operating costs due to different voyage distances and port call patterns, the impact on fuel consumption based on different speed requirements per trade lane, and any cargo commitment conflicts arising from the redeployment. Multiple scenarios must be saveable for comparison with the ability to present up to four scenarios side by side, and the selected scenario must be convertible to the active deployment plan through the approval workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R006	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Charter-In and Charter-Out Planning	The system must support planning for charter-in and charter-out vessel requirements as part of the long-term deployment strategy. For charter-in planning, the system must allow planners to define required vessel specifications including minimum TEU capacity, maximum draft, reefer plug count, and gear requirements, specify the charter period start and end dates, estimate charter hire costs based on market rate databases or manual input in USD with conversion to entity currencies, and evaluate the cost-effectiveness of chartering versus redeploying owned tonnage. For charter-out planning, the system must identify periods where owned vessels are surplus to requirements and estimate potential charter-out revenue based on market conditions. The system must track all charter commitments against the deployment plan and must alert when charter expiry dates approach within 90 days to allow timely renewal or redelivery planning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R007	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Port Capacity and Berth Allocation Alignment	The deployment plan must be validated against known port capacity constraints and berth allocation agreements at key ports in the network. The system must store berth window allocations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT including allocated days of week, berth numbers, and maximum vessel LOA and beam restrictions per berth. When the deployment plan results in vessel calls that exceed allocated berth windows or when vessel specifications are incompatible with allocated berths, the system must flag conflicts and suggest resolution options including schedule adjustment, vessel swap with a size-compatible vessel, or berth allocation renegotiation request generation. The system must support multi-terminal configurations at ports with multiple container terminals and must account for terminal operator preferences and historical performance differences between terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R008	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Financial Performance Projection per Deployment Option	The system must generate financial performance projections for each deployment plan scenario to support investment and operational decision-making. Projections must include estimated annual revenue per trade lane based on demand forecasts and freight rate assumptions, estimated annual voyage costs including bunker, port, canal, and agency costs per vessel per trade lane, vessel operating costs including crew, insurance, maintenance, and overhead allocated per entity, contribution margin per trade lane per vessel, and return on invested capital for owned vessels. All financial projections must be computed in USD as the consolidation currency and must also be presented in entity local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using budgeted exchange rates. Projections must support sensitivity analysis on key assumptions including freight rates plus or minus 15 percent and bunker prices plus or minus 20 percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R009	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Vessel Lifecycle and Replacement Planning	The system must support long-term vessel lifecycle planning including vessel age tracking, remaining economic life estimation, and replacement timing analysis. For each vessel in the fleet, the system must display current age, classification society survey schedule, estimated remaining economic operating life based on vessel type and market conditions, and cumulative maintenance and dry dock cost trends. When a vessel approaches the end of its planned economic life typically 20 to 25 years for container vessels, the system must trigger a replacement planning workflow that evaluates the cost of continued operation including increased maintenance and insurance costs versus newbuilding or second-hand acquisition options. Replacement timing must be aligned with the deployment plan to minimize trade lane disruption. Financial analysis must be presented in the owning entity currency with cross-entity cost sharing if the vessel is deployed across multiple entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R010	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Regulatory Compliance Calendar Integration	The system must integrate regulatory compliance deadlines into the deployment planning timeline to ensure vessels meet all statutory requirements without unplanned off-hire. Compliance events must include classification society annual surveys and special surveys, flag state inspections, International Safety Management (ISM) audits, International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) certificate renewals, MARPOL compliance deadlines including ballast water management system installation cutoffs, and regional regulatory requirements such as Saudi Maritime Authority and Qatar Maritime Administration inspections. Each compliance event must be mapped to the relevant vessel and displayed on the deployment timeline with advance warning alerts at 90, 60, and 30 days before the deadline. The system must calculate the scheduling impact of compliance events and must prevent vessel deployment during periods when mandatory surveys or inspections are due.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R011	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	AI-Driven Deployment Recommendation Engine	The system must provide an AI-driven recommendation engine that analyzes all available data including demand forecasts, vessel technical characteristics, cost structures, seasonal patterns, and regulatory schedules to generate optimal fleet deployment recommendations. The engine must formulate the deployment problem as a mixed-integer optimization minimizing total fleet operating cost while satisfying demand coverage constraints, vessel technical compatibility constraints, maintenance schedule constraints, and minimum service frequency requirements per trade lane. Recommendations must be generated for the full planning horizon of at least 12 months with monthly granularity. The engine must explain its recommendations in natural language descriptions available in both English and Arabic, highlighting the key factors driving each vessel assignment and identifying the primary trade-offs involved in the recommended deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R012	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Multi-Entity Fleet Sharing Agreements	The system must support the management of fleet sharing agreements between operating entities within the group structure spanning Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Sharing agreements must define which vessels are shared between which entities, the allocation methodology for shared vessel capacity such as fixed TEU allocation per entity or proportional revenue sharing, cost allocation rules for shared vessel operating expenses, and priority rules when capacity demand from multiple entities conflicts on the same vessel. The system must generate intercompany invoices for fleet sharing costs based on agreed transfer pricing methodologies and must ensure compliance with each entity jurisdiction tax authority requirements for related-party transactions. Sharing agreement terms must be configurable with effective date ranges and must support both USD-denominated and local currency denominated settlement between entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R013	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Deployment Plan Change Impact Analysis	The system must provide a comprehensive impact analysis tool that evaluates the downstream effects of any proposed change to the active deployment plan before the change is approved. The impact analysis must cover cargo booking commitments that may be affected by vessel redeployment including booked cargo volumes and customer contract obligations, port agent and terminal operator notifications required for schedule changes, bunker procurement contracts that may need modification for quantity or delivery port changes, crew change plans and visa arrangements that depend on vessel port call schedules, and financial budget variances resulting from the deployment change. The impact analysis must be presented as a structured report with severity ratings for each impacted area and must be attached to the change approval workflow. Reports must be generated in both English and Arabic with currency values in the relevant entity local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R014	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Vessel Performance Comparison for Deployment Decisions	The system must provide vessel-to-vessel performance comparison tools to support deployment decisions when multiple vessels are candidates for assignment to a specific trade lane. Comparison metrics must include fuel efficiency measured as fuel consumption per TEU-nautical mile at the trade lane typical speed, cargo intake capacity at the ports in the trade lane accounting for draft and crane reach limitations, historical schedule reliability on similar trade lanes measured as percentage of port calls within 6 hours of scheduled time, maintenance history and predicted reliability for the planning period, and CII rating trajectory under the proposed deployment. The comparison must be presented in a tabular format with visual indicators highlighting the best-performing vessel for each metric. The system must generate a composite deployment suitability score that weights all metrics according to configurable business priorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R015	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Deployment Plan Visualization and Gantt Timeline	The system must provide an interactive Gantt-style timeline visualization of the fleet deployment plan. The timeline must display each vessel as a horizontal bar with color-coded segments indicating trade lane assignments, dry dock periods, idle periods, and charter-out periods. Users must be able to zoom from annual overview to weekly detail level using mouse scroll or pinch gestures. Clicking on any segment must display a detail panel showing the vessel name and IMO number, assigned trade lane with port rotation, planned voyages and round trip duration, expected utilization percentage, and estimated costs. The timeline must support drag-and-drop vessel reassignment between trade lanes for scenario modeling. The visualization must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts, and printed or exported PDF versions must maintain readability with appropriate color schemes and legends in the user selected language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R016	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Vessel Bunker Consumption Budgeting by Deployment	The system must generate bunker consumption budgets aligned with the deployment plan for each vessel over the planning horizon. The budget must calculate expected bunker consumption per vessel per month based on the assigned trade lane typical voyage profile, planned speed, vessel-specific speed-consumption curves, and seasonal weather factors. Bunker costs must be budgeted using configurable price assumptions per fuel type per quarter with the ability to lock in prices for contracted bunker volumes. The budget must distinguish between main engine, auxiliary engine, and boiler consumption. Total fleet bunker budget must be aggregated by operating entity with values in entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD. Budget versus actual tracking must be enabled once voyages commence with variance analysis reports generated monthly for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-052-2-004-R017	FEAT-052-2-004	MOD-052	Contingency and Disruption Response Planning	The system must support the creation and maintenance of contingency plans for major disruption scenarios that could affect the fleet deployment. Predefined disruption templates must include vessel breakdown or mechanical failure requiring emergency dry dock, port closure due to weather or geopolitical events affecting one or more of the four key ports, Suez Canal closure or transit delay exceeding 48 hours, piracy or maritime security escalation requiring route diversion, and pandemic-related crew change restrictions. For each disruption template, the system must allow planners to predefine response actions including vessel substitution chains, alternative port call sequences, cargo diversion protocols, and communication plans. When a disruption event is activated, the system must automatically apply the predefined contingency plan to the deployment schedule and must calculate the cost impact in the affected entity currencies and consolidated USD, enabling rapid management decision-making.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R001	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	AI-Driven Transshipment Plan Generation	The system must automatically generate transshipment plans for all inbound cargo flagged as T/S (transshipment) upon receipt of advance manifest data or EDI BAPLIE messages. The AI engine shall analyze vessel schedules, yard capacity, container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer, OOG, flat-rack, open-top, tank), and connecting service windows to produce an optimal transfer plan. Plans must consider minimum connection time (MCT) configured per hub port (e.g., Hamad Port MCT=8hrs, Jebel Ali MCT=6hrs) and include fallback options when primary connections are at risk. Each plan must be stored with a unique plan reference, creation timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and the originating entity identifier (QA/AE/SA/IN).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R002	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Multi-Hub Cargo Grouping and Consolidation	The system must support grouping transshipment containers into logical transfer batches based on connecting vessel, destination port, cargo compatibility, and hazardous goods segregation rules per IMDG Code. Grouping logic must handle multi-hub scenarios where cargo may transit through more than one hub (e.g., JNPT to Hamad Port to Jebel Ali to final destination). Each group shall have a unique batch ID, estimated transfer window, and priority classification (standard, express, time-critical). The AI shall optimize batch sizes to align with crane and yard equipment capacity at the receiving berth, supporting all four hub ports with port-specific equipment configuration profiles.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R003	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Berth-to-Berth Transfer Scheduling	The system must generate berth-to-berth transfer schedules for transshipment containers, specifying the discharge berth, intermediate yard block, and loading berth with time windows for each movement. Scheduling must account for internal transport equipment availability (ITV, straddle carriers, reach stackers), yard congestion levels, and reefer plug availability at intermediate storage locations. The schedule must integrate with the Terminal Operating System (TOS) via API and support real-time adjustments when vessel ETAs shift by more than 30 minutes. Transfer schedules must be viewable in both Arabic and English interfaces with RTL layout support for Arabic users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R004	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Dwell Time Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI-powered dwell time optimization engine that minimizes the time transshipment containers spend in the hub yard. The engine shall target hub-specific dwell time KPIs (e.g., Jebel Ali target ≤48hrs, Hamad Port target ≤36hrs, Khalifa Port target ≤42hrs, JNPT target ≤72hrs) and generate alerts when projected dwell times exceed thresholds. The optimization must consider free storage periods, detention cost accrual rates in applicable currencies (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali/Khalifa, INR for JNPT, USD for cross-entity billing), and re-routing options to alternative connecting services. Historical dwell time analytics must be maintained for AI model training.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R005	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	T/S Cargo Priority Classification	The system must classify all transshipment cargo into priority tiers based on configurable business rules including remaining connection time, cargo value declared on the Bill of Lading, contractual SLA commitments, hazmat classification, reefer temperature sensitivity, and customer loyalty tier. A minimum of five priority levels (Critical, High, Medium, Standard, Low) must be supported with color-coded visual indicators in the UI. Priority classification must automatically recalculate when vessel schedules change or when cargo approaches its minimum connection time threshold. The classification engine must support entity-specific priority rules, allowing Hamad Port operations to weight factors differently than JNPT operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R006	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Advance Manifest Integration for T/S Planning	The system must ingest advance cargo manifests via EDI (COPARN, BAPLIE, CUSCAR formats), API integration, and manual upload (Excel/CSV) to pre-plan transshipment operations before vessel arrival. Manifest data must include container number, ISO size-type code, weight, seal number, origin port (UN/LOCODE), destination port, hazmat class if applicable, reefer settings, and OOG dimensions. The system must validate manifest data against IMO container number check-digit algorithms and flag discrepancies. For each hub port entity, manifest data must be stored in compliance with local customs requirements including Qatar CDPS, UAE FTA, Saudi FASAH, and Indian ICEGATE regulatory frameworks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R007	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Multi-Entity Transshipment Cost Allocation	The system must allocate transshipment handling costs across entities when cargo transits through hubs operated by different legal entities within the group. Cost allocation must support inter-company transfer pricing rules, applying markup percentages configurable per entity pair and service type (stevedoring, storage, reefer monitoring, hazmat handling). All cost records must maintain dual-currency representation: the local hub currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and the group reporting currency (USD). Exchange rates must be sourced from the treasury module with effective date matching. The system must generate inter-company invoices and reconciliation reports with Arabic and English dual-language support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R008	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Yard Slot Pre-Allocation for T/S Cargo	The system must pre-allocate yard slots for inbound transshipment containers based on their connecting vessel and estimated transfer window. Pre-allocation logic must consider container size and type, hazmat segregation distances per IMDG Code Chapter 7.4, reefer plug requirements, weight stacking limits, and proximity to the outbound loading berth to minimize horizontal transport distance. The system must reserve capacity buffers (configurable percentage per hub, default 15%) for unplanned transshipment cargo. Yard slot assignments must be released automatically if the inbound vessel is cancelled or if cargo status changes from T/S to local delivery. All yard maps must render correctly in both LTR and RTL UI orientations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R009	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	T/S Plan Approval Workflow	The system must implement a configurable approval workflow for transshipment plans exceeding defined thresholds such as total TEU count above 500, hazmat cargo exceeding 50 TEU, or estimated handling cost exceeding USD 100,000. The workflow must support multi-level approvals with role-based authorization (Planner, Supervisor, Hub Manager, Regional Director). Approvals must be accessible via mobile interface for on-the-go decision-making and must include digital signature capture for audit compliance. The workflow must enforce entity-specific approval hierarchies and support delegation rules for periods when approvers are unavailable. Approval timestamps must record both Gregorian and Hijri dates per GCC regulatory requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R010	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Real-Time Vessel Schedule Integration	The system must integrate with vessel tracking services (AIS data providers, carrier schedule APIs) to maintain real-time ETA/ETD updates for all vessels calling at hub ports. Schedule changes must automatically trigger re-evaluation of affected transshipment plans, recalculating connection feasibility, dwell time projections, and priority classifications. The system must handle schedule data from multiple sources with configurable source priority (carrier direct feed > port authority > AIS prediction). When vessel delays exceed configurable thresholds per hub port, the system must proactively generate re-planning recommendations using the AI optimization engine. All schedule timestamps must be stored in UTC with display conversion to local time zones (AST, GST, IST).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R011	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Hazmat Transshipment Compliance Validation	The system must validate all hazmat transshipment plans against IMDG Code segregation requirements, port-specific hazmat handling regulations, and carrier acceptance policies before plan approval. Validation must check segregation between incompatible hazmat classes during yard storage and vessel loading, verify that hub port hazmat storage capacity limits are not exceeded, and ensure required emergency equipment is available. The system must maintain a hazmat compliance certificate reference for each transshipment movement. Compliance checks must integrate with Qatar Civil Defence requirements for Hamad Port, UAE NCEMA regulations for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, and Indian PESO regulations for JNPT. Validation failures must block plan execution with detailed Arabic and English error messages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R012	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Reefer Transshipment Monitoring Plan	The system must generate reefer monitoring plans for all transshipment refrigerated containers specifying plug-in location, temperature set-point verification schedule, ventilation settings, atmosphere control parameters (CA/MA), and pre-trip inspection (PTI) requirements before outbound loading. Monitoring plans must account for the dwell time at hub and schedule PTI activities when dwell time exceeds 24 hours. The system must integrate with reefer monitoring hardware (Emerson, Carrier, Daikin units) via IoT interfaces and generate alerts for temperature deviations exceeding ±2°C from set-point. Costs for reefer monitoring must be tracked per container in the applicable hub currency with automated inter-company billing when entities differ.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R013	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	OOG and Special Cargo Transshipment Planning	The system must support specialized transshipment planning for out-of-gauge (OOG) cargo including flat-racks, open-tops, and break-bulk items requiring special lifting equipment. Planning must account for over-height, over-width, and over-length dimensions with route clearance validation for internal transport within the terminal. The system must verify crane capacity (SWL) at both discharge and loading berths, check cell guide compatibility on the connecting vessel, and generate special stowage requests. For Jebel Ali and Hamad Port, the system must validate that OOG transport routes avoid restricted areas during specific time windows. Planning data must include photographic documentation attachments and must be shareable across entities in bilingual format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R014	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Transshipment Volume Forecasting	The system must provide AI-driven transshipment volume forecasting for each hub port using historical data, booked vessel schedules, trade lane trends, seasonal patterns, and economic indicators. Forecasts must be generated at daily, weekly, and monthly granularity with a minimum accuracy target of 85% at the weekly level. The forecasting model must account for Ramadan period adjustments, Hajj season impacts on Saudi ports, monsoon effects on JNPT operations, and regional holiday calendars (Hijri-based for GCC, Indian national holidays). Forecast outputs must feed into resource planning for crane allocation, yard capacity management, and manpower scheduling. Multi-currency revenue projections must accompany volume forecasts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R015	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	Offline Transshipment Planning Capability	The system must support offline transshipment planning when network connectivity to the central ERP is unavailable. The offline module must cache the latest vessel schedules, yard status, and container inventory for a configurable period (default 48 hours). Planners must be able to create, modify, and approve transshipment plans in offline mode with all changes queued for synchronization upon reconnection. Conflict resolution logic must handle cases where the same resources were allocated by different planners in offline mode, using timestamp-based priority with manual override capability. Offline mode must maintain full Arabic and English UI functionality and store all timestamps with timezone metadata for accurate sync reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-001-R016	FEAT-053-1-001	MOD-053	T/S Plan Performance Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time analytics dashboard showing transshipment planning KPIs including plan accuracy rate, average dwell time by hub, connection success rate, cost per TEU transshipped, crane utilization during T/S operations, and yard occupancy for T/S cargo. Dashboards must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), hub port, time period (Gregorian and Hijri date selectors), vessel operator, and trade lane. Visualization must include heat maps for yard utilization, Gantt charts for transfer timelines, and trend charts for volume analysis. All dashboard elements must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic users and support export to PDF and Excel with bilingual headers. Currency values must display in the selected entity's local currency with USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R001	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Service Master Data Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive feeder service master database containing all feeder routes, vessel rotations, carrier details, transit times, frequency, and port call sequences connecting to each transshipment hub. Each feeder service record must include the operating carrier, vessel name and IMO number, capacity in TEU, reefer plug count, hazmat acceptance policy, cut-off times (documentation, VGM, cargo gate-in), and commercial terms. The master data must support versioning with effective date ranges aligned to carrier schedule periods. Data must be maintainable per entity with shared visibility across the multi-entity network. Service descriptions must support Arabic and English text fields with proper RTL rendering for Arabic content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R002	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Connecting Service Window Matching	The system must automatically match transshipment cargo with available connecting services based on destination port, cargo type compatibility, available vessel capacity, and schedule alignment. The matching algorithm must consider carrier alliance agreements, slot charter arrangements, and preferential routing rules configured per trade lane. When multiple connecting options exist, the AI engine must rank them by transit time efficiency, cost optimization, service reliability score, and environmental impact (vessel carbon intensity indicator). The system must present the top three recommended connections to the planner with comparison metrics displayed in both the local hub currency and USD. Matching must execute within 5 seconds for batches of up to 1,000 containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R003	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Vessel Slot Booking and Confirmation	The system must enable slot booking on connecting feeder vessels for transshipment cargo through automated carrier EDI messaging (COPARN) or API integration with carrier booking systems. Bookings must capture container count by size-type, total weight, hazmat declarations, reefer requirements, and OOG dimensions. The system must track booking confirmation status (Requested, Confirmed, Waitlisted, Rejected) and manage booking amendments when cargo details change. Slot availability must be maintained in real-time with overbooking thresholds configurable per carrier and service. Booking costs must be recorded in the carrier's billing currency with automatic conversion to the hub entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using treasury-sourced exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R004	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Mother Vessel to Feeder Transfer Coordination	The system must coordinate the transfer of transshipment containers from mother vessels to feeder vessels including discharge sequencing, yard staging, and loading sequence optimization. Transfer coordination must account for feeder vessel stability requirements, maximum stack weights, and cell guide restrictions. The system must generate a transfer manifest linking each container from its mother vessel stowage position to its target feeder vessel slot position. When containers require repositioning in the yard for feeder loading optimization, the system must calculate the most efficient restow plan considering equipment availability and time constraints. Transfer manifests must be transmittable via EDI BAPLIE to feeder operators with bilingual remarks fields.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R001	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Multi-Leg Revenue Splitting Engine	The system must implement an automated revenue splitting engine that decomposes total transshipment revenue into first-leg inbound, hub handling, and second-leg outbound components. Revenue allocation must follow configurable split ratios defined per carrier agreement, trade lane (e.g., Asia-to-GCC via Jebel Ali AEJEA, India-to-Qatar via JNPT INMUN to Hamad Port QAHMD), and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, reefer). The engine must support multi-currency attribution in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with real-time FX conversion at the date of vessel discharge. Split calculations must be auditable with full traceability to the originating tariff schedule and carrier contract clause.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R005	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Schedule Deviation Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor feeder vessel schedules for deviations from published timetables using AIS tracking data, carrier notifications, and port authority vessel traffic information. Deviations must be classified by severity: Minor (delay ≤4 hours), Moderate (4-12 hours), Major (12-24 hours), and Critical (>24 hours or cancellation). For each deviation, the system must automatically assess impact on waiting transshipment cargo, calculate the number of affected containers and their value at risk, and generate re-routing recommendations when the deviation threatens connection windows. Notifications must be sent to affected stakeholders via configurable channels (email, SMS, in-app) in their preferred language (Arabic or English) with Hijri date references where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R006	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Carrier Coordination Communication Hub	The system must provide a centralized communication hub for coordinating with feeder carriers, mainline operators, and terminal operations teams. The hub must support structured message types (schedule update, capacity request, booking amendment, hazmat approval, special cargo notification) with template-based composition in Arabic and English. All communications must be linked to the relevant transshipment plan, container, or vessel record for audit traceability. The system must integrate with carrier EDI systems for automated message exchange and support manual communication logging for phone and in-person interactions. Message timestamps must be recorded in UTC with local time display and must support Hijri date references for GCC-based communications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R007	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Multi-Carrier Slot Swap and Exchange	The system must support slot swap and exchange arrangements between carriers at transshipment hubs where vessel sharing agreements (VSA) or slot charter agreements exist. The system must track slot entitlements per carrier per service, monitor actual utilization against entitlements, and calculate over/under usage for periodic settlement. Slot swap transactions must be recorded with reference to the governing commercial agreement, quantities exchanged by container size-type, and financial settlement terms. The AI engine must recommend optimal slot swap opportunities based on projected demand imbalances. All financial calculations must maintain multi-currency support with settlement in the agreed contract currency and reporting in each entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R008	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Cut-Off Time Management	The system must enforce and manage feeder vessel cut-off times for documentation submission, VGM declaration, and physical cargo gate-in. Cut-off times must be configurable per carrier, service, and hub port with override capability for authorized users. The system must generate escalating warnings as cut-off deadlines approach: advisory at 24 hours, warning at 12 hours, urgent at 6 hours, and final at 2 hours before cut-off. Containers that miss cut-off must be automatically flagged for re-routing to the next available connecting service. The system must track cut-off compliance statistics per carrier and trade lane for SLA monitoring. All cut-off times must display in local hub time with UTC reference and support both Gregorian and Hijri date formats in GCC entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R009	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Connecting Service Reliability Scoring	The system must calculate and maintain a reliability score for each connecting feeder service based on historical on-time performance, cancellation frequency, cargo damage incidents, documentation accuracy, and capacity honoring rate. Scores must be computed on a rolling 90-day window with configurable weighting per metric and normalized to a 0-100 scale. Reliability scores must influence the AI engine's service recommendation algorithm, deprioritizing unreliable services for time-critical cargo. The system must generate monthly reliability reports per carrier per hub port with trend analysis and benchmarking against industry standards. Reports must be available in Arabic and English with automatic currency conversion for cost-related metrics (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R010	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Capacity Forecasting and Pre-Booking	The system must forecast feeder capacity requirements per trade lane and time period using AI analysis of historical transshipment volumes, booked mainline vessel cargo, seasonal patterns, and known demand surges (e.g., pre-Ramadan cargo rushes, Diwali season volumes for India trade). Forecasts must trigger automated pre-booking of feeder slots when projected demand exceeds 75% of available capacity. Pre-booking rules must be configurable per entity and trade lane with maximum pre-booking limits to prevent over-commitment. The system must track forecast accuracy and continuously refine models. Financial exposure from pre-bookings must be calculated in local currency with USD equivalent and reported to the treasury module for cash flow forecasting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R011	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Direct Transshipment (Ship-to-Ship) Coordination	The system must support direct transshipment operations where containers are transferred directly from the mother vessel to the feeder vessel without intermediate yard storage. Direct T/S coordination must synchronize discharge and loading crane operations, manage the quayside buffer zone capacity, and track containers during the inter-vessel transfer. The system must validate that direct T/S is operationally feasible based on berth proximity, crane reach, and simultaneous vessel operations policies at each hub port. Direct T/S plans must include fallback to yard-via-transfer if the operation window is missed. Cost savings from direct T/S (avoided yard handling) must be calculated and recorded per container in applicable hub currency with inter-company allocation when entities differ.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R012	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Network Visualization and Route Map	The system must provide an interactive geographic visualization of the feeder network connected to each transshipment hub, displaying active feeder routes, vessel positions, port connections, and cargo flow volumes. The map must support zoom from regional overview to individual port view, with clickable routes showing service details (frequency, transit time, capacity, reliability score). Real-time vessel positions from AIS data must be overlaid on the network map. Users must be able to filter by carrier, trade lane, cargo type, and time period. The visualization must support Arabic labels and place names with RTL rendering for Arabic-language users. Map data must be cacheable for offline viewing for up to 24 hours with last-update timestamp prominently displayed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R013	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Vessel Berth Priority Coordination	The system must coordinate feeder vessel berth allocation at hub ports considering transshipment connection urgency, vessel size, cargo volume, carrier priority tier, and port authority scheduling constraints. When berth conflicts arise between multiple feeders, the system must apply priority scoring that weights time-critical transshipment connections highest. Berth priority requests must be communicable to the port authority or terminal operator via standardized interfaces. The system must maintain a berth reservation calendar with visual timeline display showing mother vessel and feeder vessel occupancy. Priority overrides must be logged with approval chain for audit purposes. Berth allocation data must synchronize with the TOS and be accessible in both Arabic and English interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R014	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Documentation Transfer Between Services	The system must manage the transfer of shipping documentation from the inbound mainline service to the outbound feeder service for transshipment cargo. Documentation transfer must include Bill of Lading details, customs transit declarations, hazmat documentation (IMDG DGD), phytosanitary certificates, origin certificates, and VGM declarations. The system must validate document completeness against feeder carrier requirements and flag missing documents with sufficient lead time for resolution. For GCC hub ports, documentation must comply with customs transit procedures (Qatar single window, UAE Mirsal-2, Saudi FASAH). Document transfer records must be maintained for regulatory audit with timestamps in both Gregorian and Hijri formats and support Arabic document descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R015	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Service Cost Comparison and Optimization	The system must provide a cost comparison engine for evaluating alternative connecting feeder services considering freight rates, terminal handling charges at origin and destination, surcharges (BAF, CAF, PSS, EBS, GRI), free time allowances, and overall transit cost per TEU. The comparison must normalize costs to a common currency (USD) while maintaining visibility of individual cost components in their native currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The AI engine must recommend the most cost-effective service option that meets the cargo's transit time requirement and service quality thresholds. Cost comparisons must be saveable as decision records with approval linkage. Reports must support bilingual output with Arabic RTL formatting for GCC entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R016	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Seasonal Schedule Change Management	The system must manage carrier seasonal schedule changes that affect feeder service patterns at transshipment hubs. The system must ingest new season schedules (typically published quarterly), compare against current schedules to identify changes in port calls, frequencies, transit times, and vessel deployments. Impact analysis must automatically identify all active transshipment bookings affected by schedule changes and generate re-routing recommendations. The system must support a transition period where both old and new schedules coexist with clear effective date handling. Schedule change notifications must be distributed to internal stakeholders and customers in their preferred language. Impact analysis must quantify financial exposure from re-routing in local entity currencies and consolidated USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-002-R017	FEAT-053-1-002	MOD-053	Feeder Vessel No-Show and Cancellation Handling	The system must handle feeder vessel no-show and service cancellation events with automated contingency workflows. Upon detecting a no-show (vessel fails to arrive within configurable window beyond scheduled ETA, default 12 hours) or receiving a cancellation notice, the system must immediately identify all affected transshipment cargo, calculate re-booking requirements, and trigger the AI engine to find alternative connecting services. Affected cargo must be re-prioritized based on original booking dates, cargo type urgency (perishable, time-critical), and contractual SLA obligations. The system must generate customer notifications in Arabic and English, create carrier claims for service failure penalties where contractually applicable, and update financial accruals in the affected entity's local currency. All no-show and cancellation events must be logged for carrier reliability scoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R001	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Real-Time Container Location Tracking	The system must track the real-time physical location of every transshipment container within the hub port from vessel discharge to outbound vessel loading. Location tracking must integrate with terminal operating systems, GPS/RFID tracking infrastructure, crane event logs, and yard management systems to provide position accuracy within a single yard bay. The system must record and display location as a combination of zone (berth, yard block, reefer area, hazmat zone, gate), specific position (bay-row-tier for yard, cell position for vessel), and movement status (static, in-transit). Location updates must propagate within 60 seconds of the physical event. Display must support Arabic and English with RTL-compatible yard coordinate notation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R002	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	T/S Milestone Event Tracking	The system must define and track a configurable set of transshipment milestone events for each container including: inbound vessel arrival, discharge from vessel, yard placement, customs scan completion, pre-loading inspection, loading onto connecting vessel, and connecting vessel departure. Each milestone must record the actual timestamp (UTC with local time conversion), the responsible operator, equipment used, and any exception notes. Planned versus actual milestone comparison must be computed automatically to identify delays. Milestones must align with UN/CEFACT standard event codes where applicable. The milestone timeline must be displayable as a visual journey map in the UI supporting both Arabic and English labels with Hijri date option for GCC entity operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R003	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Automated Stakeholder Notification Engine	The system must send automated notifications to configured stakeholders when transshipment milestones are achieved or when exceptions occur. Stakeholders include shipping line operations teams, freight forwarders, consignees, customs brokers, and internal hub operations staff. Notification channels must include email, SMS, webhook API callbacks, and in-app push notifications. Each stakeholder must have a configurable notification profile specifying which events trigger notifications, preferred channel, preferred language (Arabic or English), and time-zone for timestamp display. Notifications must include container number, current status, next expected milestone, and any action required. Bulk notification suppression rules must prevent notification storms when large vessel operations complete, batching updates into digest formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R004	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Container Status Dashboard with Multi-Hub View	The system must provide a real-time dashboard showing the status of all transshipment containers across all hub ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, JNPT) with drill-down capability to individual container detail. The dashboard must display aggregate KPIs including total T/S containers in hub, containers awaiting discharge, containers in yard, containers awaiting loading, and containers with connection risk. Filtering must be available by hub port, entity, carrier, trade lane, container type, and status. The dashboard must support configurable auto-refresh intervals (minimum 30 seconds) and provide both tabular and visual (map/graph) views. All text elements must support Arabic RTL rendering. Currency-related KPIs must display in the user's selected entity currency with USD toggle option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R005	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Customs Transit Status Integration	The system must integrate with customs systems at each hub port to track the customs transit status of transshipment containers. Integration must cover Qatar Customs (CDPS), UAE Federal Customs (Mirsal-2 for Jebel Ali, Al Dhafra for Khalifa Port), Saudi Customs (FASAH), and Indian Customs (ICEGATE) through their respective electronic interfaces. The system must display customs hold, release, examination, and clearance statuses alongside operational tracking data. Customs status changes must be captured within 15 minutes of the customs system update. When customs holds delay transshipment beyond the connection window, the system must automatically trigger re-routing evaluation. All customs reference numbers and status descriptions must be stored and displayed in both Arabic and English as required by local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R006	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Reefer Container Continuous Monitoring During T/S	The system must provide continuous temperature and operational monitoring for reefer transshipment containers throughout their hub dwell time. Monitoring data must be collected from IoT-enabled reefer units at intervals no greater than 15 minutes and must include supply temperature, return temperature, set-point, humidity, O2/CO2 levels for controlled atmosphere units, and power supply status. Temperature excursion alerts must trigger immediately when readings deviate beyond configurable thresholds per commodity type. The monitoring record must form a continuous chain from inbound vessel discharge through outbound vessel loading, providing an unbroken cold chain evidence trail. Monitoring data must be accessible per container with visual graphs and exportable for cargo insurance claims in multi-currency valuations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R007	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Hazmat Container Tracking and Compliance Trail	The system must maintain enhanced tracking for transshipment containers carrying hazardous goods, recording all movements with IMDG class, UN number, proper shipping name, and packing group. The system must verify that yard storage positions comply with IMDG segregation rules throughout the container's dwell time and generate alerts if a nearby container placement violates segregation distances. Emergency response information (EmS codes, emergency contact numbers) must be immediately accessible from the tracking interface. The system must generate a hazmat handling compliance trail document per container per hub for regulatory inspection purposes. Compliance documentation must satisfy requirements of Qatar Civil Defence, UAE NCEMA, Saudi Civil Defence, and Indian PESO authorities, with bilingual Arabic/English output.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R008	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	T/S Cargo Dwell Time Monitoring and Alerts	The system must continuously monitor the dwell time of each transshipment container from discharge completion to loading completion and generate progressive alerts as dwell time approaches and exceeds configured thresholds. Alert levels must include Approaching Threshold (at 75% of target dwell), At Threshold (100%), Exceeded Standard (125%), and Critical Overstay (150%). Each alert must identify the root cause category (vessel delay, customs hold, equipment shortage, documentation pending, weather, labor action) through AI-powered cause analysis. Dwell time must be calculated in hours with display in days:hours format. Financial impact of overstay must be calculated using hub-specific storage tariffs in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and flagged when free storage period is exhausted.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R009	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Container Damage and Exception Recording	The system must capture and track container damage reports and operational exceptions that occur during transshipment handling at the hub. Damage records must include damage type (structural, seal, reefer unit, cargo), location on container (ISO damage code positions), severity classification, photographic evidence (minimum 4 photos per incident), responsible party identification, and estimated repair cost in local currency. Exception types must include weight discrepancy, seal mismatch, documentation error, customs rejection, and misdirected container. All damage and exception records must be linked to the relevant transshipment plan and container journey record. Reports must be generatable in Arabic and English with standardized damage code references for insurance and carrier claims processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R010	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	End-to-End T/S Journey Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete, immutable audit trail for each transshipment container's journey through the hub, recording every status change, location update, handling event, and system interaction with timestamp, user/system identifier, and source system reference. The audit trail must be tamper-evident using sequential event hashing to detect any post-hoc modifications. Audit records must be retained for a minimum of 7 years in compliance with the most stringent applicable regulation across operating entities (Qatar Commercial Law, UAE Federal Law, Saudi Commercial Law, Indian Customs Act). The audit trail must be searchable and exportable in structured formats (JSON, XML, CSV) for regulatory submission. All audit entries must maintain bilingual descriptions where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R011	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Container Tracking API for External Stakeholders	The system must expose a secure RESTful API allowing authorized external stakeholders (shipping lines, freight forwarders, consignees) to query the real-time status and location of their transshipment containers. The API must support authentication via OAuth 2.0, rate limiting per API consumer, and granular permission control to restrict data visibility based on stakeholder role and commercial relationship. API responses must include container number, current location, current status, last milestone achieved, next expected milestone, estimated connection vessel, and estimated departure time. The API must support both JSON and XML response formats with optional Arabic or English text fields. API usage must be metered for billing purposes with rates configurable in each entity's currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R012	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Predictive Connection Risk Scoring	The system must calculate a real-time connection risk score for each transshipment container based on remaining time to connecting vessel cut-off, current container status, pending operational steps, historical processing times for similar cargo types, and current hub congestion levels. Risk scores must be expressed on a 0-100 scale with configurable thresholds for Green (low risk, score 0-30), Amber (moderate risk, 31-60), and Red (high risk, 61-100) classifications. The AI model must incorporate real-time factors including weather conditions, labor availability, equipment operational status, and customs processing backlogs. Risk scores must update at minimum every 15 minutes and trigger automatic escalation workflows when containers move from Amber to Red. Scoring algorithms must be tunable per hub port to account for local operational characteristics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R013	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Multi-Leg Transshipment Journey Tracking	The system must support tracking of containers undergoing multi-leg transshipment journeys involving two or more hub ports within the network (e.g., JNPT → Hamad Port → Jebel Ali → final destination). The full multi-leg journey must be visible as a single unified tracking record showing the status at each hub, completed and pending legs, and overall journey progress percentage. Inter-hub transit legs must track the connecting vessel, estimated and actual transit times, and any delays. The system must hand off tracking responsibility between hub entities automatically as the container moves between ports while maintaining a single consolidated view. Journey data must be accessible to all involved entities with role-based data visibility. Multi-entity cost accumulation must be tracked in each hub's local currency with consolidated USD total.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R014	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Mobile Tracking Application for Field Operations	The system must provide a mobile application (iOS and Android) for field operations staff to update container status, record exceptions, capture photos, and scan container numbers (OCR and barcode) during transshipment operations. The mobile app must function in offline mode when within terminal areas with poor connectivity, queuing updates for synchronization when connectivity is restored. The app must support Arabic and English interfaces with automatic RTL layout switching based on language selection. Field staff must be able to view the container's transshipment plan, pending milestones, and priority classification on their mobile device. GPS coordinates from the mobile device must be captured with each status update to validate location accuracy against terminal geofence boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R015	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	Container Weight Verification During T/S	The system must validate verified gross mass (VGM) compliance for transshipment containers per SOLAS Chapter VI Regulation 2. When containers are discharged at the hub, the system must compare the declared VGM with any available weighbridge or crane scale measurements and flag discrepancies exceeding the tolerance threshold (configurable per hub, default ±5% or 1 tonne whichever is greater). Weight discrepancies must be resolved before loading onto the connecting vessel. The system must generate weight discrepancy reports for the carrier and shipper, store original and verified weights, and update the BAPLIE data for the outbound vessel. Weight records must be maintained for customs compliance at all hub ports with values displayed in metric tonnes. Financial penalties for VGM discrepancies must be calculated per hub port tariff in local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-003-R016	FEAT-053-1-003	MOD-053	T/S Cargo Insurance and Liability Tracking	The system must track insurance coverage and liability transitions for transshipment cargo as it moves through the hub. The system must record the responsible party (carrier, terminal operator, hub entity) for each segment of the transshipment journey based on the applicable INCOTERMS and terminal handling agreements. When custody transfers occur (vessel to terminal, terminal to vessel), the system must capture the transfer event with condition assessment, timestamp, and responsible parties. Cargo value for insurance purposes must be maintained in the declared currency with equivalent values in the hub entity's local currency and USD. The system must generate liability exposure reports per entity showing total cargo value under custody segmented by cargo type, origin trade lane, and risk category, with bilingual output for GCC regulatory reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R001	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Automated Missed Connection Detection	The system must automatically detect missed connection events when a transshipment container fails to be loaded onto its planned connecting vessel. Detection must trigger when the connecting vessel departs the hub without the container being loaded, or when the container's operational status indicates it cannot reach the loading berth before the vessel's final cargo cut-off time. The detection engine must process events within 30 minutes of the connecting vessel's departure or cut-off time breach. Each missed connection event must be recorded with a unique incident reference, affected container list, planned connecting vessel, actual departure time, root cause category (AI-assigned from options: vessel delay, customs hold, equipment failure, weather, documentation, operational error), and financial impact estimate in the hub entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R002	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	AI-Powered Recovery Plan Generation	The system must automatically generate recovery plans for missed connection cargo within 60 minutes of the missed connection event detection. The AI engine must evaluate all available alternative connecting services within a configurable time window (default: next 7 days), considering destination port requirements, cargo type constraints (reefer, hazmat, OOG compatibility), available vessel capacity, transit time impact, and cost differential. Each recovery plan must present a minimum of three ranked alternatives with estimated new delivery date, additional cost breakdown, and service reliability score. Recovery plans must account for additional hub dwell time charges and any applicable detention or demurrage costs. Plan generation must work across all hub ports with entity-specific service databases and cost structures in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R003	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Root Cause Classification and Analysis	The system must classify missed connection root causes using AI analysis of operational event data, assigning primary and contributing cause categories from a standardized taxonomy. Primary categories must include: Inbound Vessel Delay, Customs Hold, Equipment Failure, Weather Disruption, Labor Action, Documentation Error, Carrier Booking Error, Terminal Operational Error, Carrier Schedule Change, and Force Majeure. The AI must analyze event sequences leading to each missed connection to identify systemic patterns and generate monthly root cause analysis reports per hub port. Reports must identify top causes by frequency and financial impact with trend analysis over rolling 12-month periods. Root cause data must feed into the carrier reliability scoring module. All reports must support Arabic and English output with currency values in both local entity currency and USD for cross-hub comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R004	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Customer Impact Assessment and Communication	The system must assess the customer impact of each missed connection event, identifying all affected Bills of Lading, consignees, contractual delivery commitments, and any penalty clauses at risk. The impact assessment must calculate the estimated delivery delay in days, financial exposure from late delivery penalties and additional charges, and reputational risk score based on customer tier and incident history. The system must generate customer-facing communication templates in Arabic and English explaining the delay, providing the recovery plan options, and offering a revised estimated time of arrival. Communications must be reviewable and approvable by the account manager before dispatch. Customer communications must reference the original booking in local terms with currency amounts displayed in the customer's preferred billing currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R005	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Financial Impact Calculation and Accrual	The system must calculate the complete financial impact of each missed connection event including additional storage charges, reefer monitoring extension costs, re-booking freight differentials, equipment detention charges, customer penalty liabilities, and internal operational costs. Financial calculations must apply the correct tariff rates per hub port and entity with currency-specific rounding rules (QAR/SAR to 2 decimal places, AED to 2 decimal places, INR to nearest rupee). The system must create automatic financial accruals in the accounting module for all estimated costs and liabilities. Cost responsibility must be assigned based on root cause analysis (terminal operator, carrier, customs, force majeure) following configurable allocation rules per entity. Financial summaries must be viewable in local currency and USD with automatic exchange rate application from the treasury module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R006	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Recovery Plan Approval and Execution Workflow	The system must implement an approval workflow for missed connection recovery plans with configurable authorization levels based on financial impact threshold, number of containers affected, cargo priority classification, and customer sensitivity tier. Approval workflow must support sequential and parallel approval paths with role-based routing (Operations Manager, Commercial Manager, Customer Service Manager). Approved recovery plans must trigger automated execution actions including re-booking on the selected alternative service, updating the transshipment plan, revising yard storage assignments, and notifying all affected stakeholders. The workflow must enforce SLA timers for each approval step with automatic escalation when response deadlines are exceeded. All workflow actions must be logged with timestamps in Gregorian and Hijri formats and support mobile approval for on-the-go decision-making.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R007	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Alternative Routing Across Hub Network	The system must evaluate alternative routing options that leverage other hubs in the network when recovery at the current hub is suboptimal. For example, if a container misses its connection at Hamad Port, the system must assess whether routing via Jebel Ali or Khalifa Port would provide a faster or more cost-effective path to the destination. Cross-hub routing evaluation must consider inter-hub transit times, available feeder connections at alternative hubs, total cost comparison including additional handling charges, and customs implications of redirecting cargo through a different country's jurisdiction. The system must handle the multi-entity commercial implications, generating inter-company service orders and cost allocations between the originating hub entity and the receiving hub entity. All financial comparisons must normalize costs to USD while preserving local currency detail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R008	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Missed Connection Escalation Matrix	The system must implement a configurable escalation matrix for missed connection incidents based on severity, cargo type, customer tier, and financial impact. Escalation levels must include Level 1 (Operations Supervisor, single container standard cargo), Level 2 (Hub Manager, multiple containers or priority cargo), Level 3 (Regional Operations Director, high-value or VIP customer), and Level 4 (Chief Operations Officer, critical incidents exceeding USD 500,000 impact). Each escalation level must have defined response time SLAs (Level 1: 2 hours, Level 2: 4 hours, Level 3: 8 hours, Level 4: 12 hours). The matrix must be independently configurable per entity to reflect organizational structures in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Escalation notifications must be sent in the recipient's preferred language with all financial data in their entity's reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R009	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Reefer Cargo Missed Connection Priority Handling	The system must implement special priority handling for reefer transshipment containers that miss their connecting vessel due to the time-sensitive nature of refrigerated cargo. The system must immediately verify continued power supply, check remaining cargo shelf life against recovery plan transit times, and escalate cargo where remaining shelf life is less than 150% of the shortest recovery option transit time. The system must coordinate with the reefer monitoring team to confirm temperature integrity and initiate pre-trip inspection if dwell time extension exceeds 24 hours. For controlled atmosphere (CA) and modified atmosphere (MA) containers, atmospheric condition verification must be mandatory before re-booking. Recovery plan ranking for reefer cargo must weight transit time above cost. Financial impact must include commodity value at risk calculated from cargo declarations with currency conversion to local hub currency and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R010	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Hazmat Cargo Missed Connection Regulatory Compliance	The system must ensure regulatory compliance for hazmat transshipment containers that miss their connecting vessel, particularly regarding maximum dwell time restrictions for specific hazmat classes imposed by port authorities. The system must check whether extended storage requires additional permits, whether yard position reassignment is needed due to changing segregation dynamics from other vessel operations, and whether notification to local hazmat authorities is required. For Hamad Port, maximum hazmat dwell limits per Qatar MOEI regulations must be enforced. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, UAE NCEMA thresholds apply. For JNPT, Indian PESO and DGCA (for air-sea intermodal) regulations must be checked. The system must block recovery plans that would cause regulatory threshold breaches and suggest compliant alternatives. All regulatory communications must be generated in Arabic for GCC ports and English for JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R004	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Status Tracking Dashboard	The system must display a real-time dashboard showing all change requests with filterable views by status (submitted, under review, approved, in development, testing, deployed, rejected, deferred), entity, priority, and assigned analyst. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper column alignment. Each CR card must show elapsed time since submission, current bottleneck stage, and SLA compliance indicator. Dashboard data must support export to Excel with proper Arabic character encoding (UTF-8) and currency formatting for cost estimates in the local currency of the requesting entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R011	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Carrier Claim Management for Missed Connections	The system must manage claim creation and tracking when missed connections are attributable to carrier failures such as vessel schedule changes, no-shows, overbooking, or documentation errors by the carrier. The system must compile the evidence package including original booking confirmation, vessel schedule records, operational event logs, financial impact calculations, and relevant contractual terms. Claims must be generated in the carrier's contract currency with cross-references to applicable service agreement clauses. The system must track claim lifecycle stages (Draft, Submitted, Acknowledged, Under Review, Settled, Disputed, Closed) with aging alerts. Settlement amounts must be recorded and reconciled against accrued costs. The claim module must support Arabic and English correspondence and maintain separate claim registers per entity with consolidated group reporting in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R012	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Missed Connection Pattern Recognition and Prevention	The system must use AI and machine learning to identify patterns in missed connection events and generate proactive prevention recommendations. Pattern analysis must correlate missed connections with factors including specific vessel services, trade lanes, time periods, weather seasons (e.g., Indian monsoon June-September, Gulf shamal winds), hub congestion levels, carrier performance, and specific equipment types. The AI must generate early warning alerts when conditions match historically problematic patterns, enabling preventive action before connections are missed. Prevention recommendations must include suggested buffer time adjustments to minimum connection time parameters, alternative default routing suggestions, and resource pre-positioning requests. Pattern analysis reports must be generated monthly per hub with cross-hub comparative analysis in bilingual Arabic/English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R013	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Customer Compensation and Goodwill Management	The system must manage customer compensation offers and goodwill gestures for missed connection incidents based on configurable business rules per customer tier, incident severity, and entity commercial policy. Compensation types must include freight discount on future shipment, storage fee waiver, priority handling upgrade, and monetary credit. The system must calculate recommended compensation amounts based on the financial impact to the customer, historical compensation precedents for similar incidents, and customer lifetime value. Compensation must be approved through a workflow with authorization limits per role (Customer Service up to USD 1,000, Manager up to USD 5,000, Director up to USD 25,000). All compensation records must be tracked for financial reporting in the entity's local currency with USD consolidation. Arabic and English customer-facing compensation letters must be auto-generated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R014	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Missed Connection Impact on Downstream Bookings	The system must assess the cascading impact of missed connections on downstream bookings and operations at the destination port and any subsequent transshipment hubs. When a container misses its connection, the system must identify and flag all downstream reservations (berth slots, yard allocations, delivery appointments, customs clearance slots, rail or truck connections) that are affected by the delay. Notifications must be sent to destination port operations, customs brokers, and inland logistics providers with revised arrival estimates. For multi-leg transshipment cargo, the system must re-evaluate connections at subsequent hubs and update the full journey plan. Financial impact assessment must include downstream charges in destination port local currency in addition to hub port costs. All downstream communication must support the destination country's language preferences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R015	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Force Majeure Missed Connection Handling	The system must provide specialized workflows for mass missed connection events caused by force majeure situations including severe weather (cyclones, fog, sandstorms), port closures, labor strikes, political disruptions, and pandemic-related restrictions. Force majeure mode must enable bulk processing of affected containers with simplified approval workflows and expedited recovery planning. The system must support declaration of a force majeure event by authorized users with configurable start and end dates, affected hub port, and scope definition. All containers affected during the force majeure period must be tagged for special reporting and exempt from standard SLA penalty calculations. The system must generate force majeure situation reports in Arabic and English for regulatory authorities and insurance claims. Financial impact aggregation must be available per entity in local currency with consolidated group impact in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R016	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Recovery Performance KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a KPI dashboard tracking missed connection recovery performance across all hub ports including metrics for: average detection-to-recovery time, recovery plan acceptance rate, average additional dwell time per missed connection, average cost per missed connection in local currency and USD, customer satisfaction impact score, root cause distribution, carrier attribution ratio, and recovery success rate (percentage of missed connections re-connected within 7 days). Dashboards must support filtering by entity, hub port, time period (with Gregorian and Hijri date selectors), carrier, trade lane, and cargo type. Visual elements must include trend charts, Pareto analysis of root causes, and geographic distribution maps of missed connection frequency. All dashboard elements must render correctly in RTL layout for Arabic users and support scheduled PDF export for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-1-004-R017	FEAT-053-1-004	MOD-053	Offline Missed Connection Management	The system must support missed connection detection, recording, and preliminary recovery planning in offline mode when hub port connectivity is disrupted. The offline module must cache connecting vessel schedules, container inventory, alternative service options, and recovery plan templates for the current and next 48-hour operational window. Operations staff must be able to record missed connection events, assign root causes, initiate customer notifications using cached templates, and create preliminary recovery plans in offline mode. All offline actions must be queued for synchronization and validation upon reconnection, with conflict resolution logic for concurrent updates. The offline module must maintain Arabic and English UI functionality, store timestamps with timezone metadata, and preserve financial calculations in local entity currency. Offline-created recovery plans must be flagged for supervisor review upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R002	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Transshipment Handling Charge Allocation	The system must allocate terminal handling charges (THC) for transshipment containers distinctly from gateway import/export containers. THC allocation must differentiate between quay crane lift-on/lift-off, internal transfer (ITV or straddle carrier), and yard storage components. Charges must be captured per container per move, with separate cost centers for each hub entity (Qatar entity for Hamad Port, UAE entity for Jebel Ali/Khalifa Port, India entity for JNPT). The allocation must support both bundled T/S THC rates and unbundled component-level pricing as defined in individual carrier service agreements, with override capability for spot rate negotiations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R003	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Reefer Transshipment Premium Tracking	The system must track and attribute reefer-specific revenue premiums for transshipment refrigerated containers including power monitoring charges, pre-trip inspection (PTI) fees, temperature deviation handling surcharges, and genset rental if applicable. Revenue must be attributed at the individual container level with timestamps for plug-in and plug-out at the hub yard. The system must calculate reefer dwell-time-based revenue tiers where storage charges escalate after configurable free-time thresholds (typically 3-5 days for T/S reefers). Currency must match the billing entity's base currency with cross-entity reefer transfer revenue reconciliation in consolidated reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R004	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Carrier-Level Profitability Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time carrier-level profitability dashboard showing net margin per transshipment TEU for each shipping line using the hub. The dashboard must display revenue components (THC, storage, reefer, special handling, hazardous surcharge) minus direct costs (equipment, labor, fuel, maintenance allocation) per carrier per month. Profitability must be segmented by trade lane corridor, container type, and vessel service loop. The dashboard must support both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with proper numeral rendering (Eastern Arabic numerals option). Drill-down capability must allow navigation from carrier summary to individual container-level P&L.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R005	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Route Corridor Margin Analysis	The system must calculate and display profitability margins by transshipment route corridor, defined as the combination of inbound trade lane origin region and outbound trade lane destination region transiting through the hub. Example corridors include Far East to East Africa via Jebel Ali, Indian Subcontinent to Mediterranean via Hamad Port, and Southeast Asia to Arabian Gulf via JNPT. Margin analysis must factor in vessel call costs apportioned to T/S volumes, berth utilization opportunity costs, and yard capacity consumption. The system must rank corridors by contribution margin and flag corridors operating below configurable minimum margin thresholds for commercial review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R006	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Multi-Entity Intercompany Revenue Settlement	The system must automate intercompany revenue settlement when a transshipment move spans entities, such as a container arriving at JNPT (India entity) and transshipping via Hamad Port (Qatar entity) before final discharge at Khalifa Port (UAE entity). Settlement must generate intercompany invoices with proper transfer pricing compliant with each jurisdiction's tax regulations (Qatar CIT, UAE CT, India GST/IGST, KSA VAT at 15%). The system must maintain an intercompany ledger with automatic reconciliation, aging reports, and dispute tracking. Settlement frequency must be configurable (daily, weekly, monthly) per entity pair agreement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R007	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Demurrage and Detention Revenue for T/S Containers	The system must calculate and attribute demurrage and detention charges specifically for transshipment containers that exceed their allotted free time at the hub. Demurrage applies to containers remaining in the terminal yard beyond the T/S free-time window (typically 5-7 days), while detention applies to carrier-owned equipment held beyond allowable turnaround. The system must distinguish between shipper-caused delays, carrier-caused delays (missed feeder connections), and force majeure events. Revenue must be attributed to the responsible party with automatic invoice generation. Charge escalation tiers (e.g., days 1-5 free, days 6-10 at USD 25/day, days 11+ at USD 50/day) must be configurable per carrier contract.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R008	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Hazardous Cargo Transshipment Surcharge Management	The system must manage surcharge revenue for transshipment of IMO-classified hazardous cargo containers (IMO classes 1-9) passing through the hub. Surcharges must be configurable per IMO class, segregation requirement complexity, and dwell duration. The system must track additional revenue from mandatory inspections, special stowage positioning in the yard (segregation from incompatible classes per IMDG Code), and enhanced monitoring fees. Revenue attribution must link to the specific hazmat permit and approval workflow. The system must maintain separate P&L tracking for hazmat T/S operations to assess whether the risk-adjusted return justifies continued acceptance of specific IMO classes at each hub.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R009	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Volume Commitment Rebate Calculation	The system must calculate volume-based rebates for carriers meeting or exceeding committed transshipment volumes as defined in their Minimum Quantity Commitment (MQC) agreements. Rebate tiers must be configurable with thresholds in TEU per quarter or per annum, with rebate percentages or fixed amounts per TEU above threshold. The system must track actual volumes against commitments in real time, project year-end rebate liability based on current run rates, and generate accrual entries for financial reporting. Rebate settlements must be processed in the carrier's contracted currency with proper FX handling. Shortfall penalties for under-commitment must also be tracked and invoiced according to contract terms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R010	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Transshipment Revenue Forecasting	The system must generate revenue forecasts for transshipment operations based on confirmed vessel schedules, historical T/S ratios per service loop, contracted tariff rates, and seasonal volume patterns. Forecasts must project revenue by week, month, and quarter for each hub port, broken down by carrier, trade lane, and container type. The AI engine must incorporate external signals such as global trade volume indices, regional economic indicators for GCC and Indian subcontinent, and known schedule changes or new service loop launches. Forecast accuracy must be tracked against actuals with variance analysis reports, and the model must self-calibrate using a minimum of 12 months historical data per hub.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R011	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Cost Allocation Model for Shared Hub Infrastructure	The system must implement a cost allocation model that distributes shared hub infrastructure costs (berth maintenance, yard equipment depreciation, IT systems, security, utilities) to transshipment operations proportionally. Allocation methods must be configurable: activity-based costing (per crane move, per yard move), volume-based (TEU throughput share), or time-based (berth hours consumed by T/S vessels). The model must recalculate allocations monthly and support what-if scenarios for evaluating the impact of T/S volume changes on per-TEU cost structures. Results must feed into the profitability dashboards and support budget vs. actual variance reporting for each entity's transshipment business unit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R012	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Wharfage and Port Dues Revenue Attribution	The system must separately track and attribute wharfage fees and port dues revenue generated from transshipment vessel calls. Wharfage must be calculated based on cargo tonnage or TEU count for T/S containers discharged and loaded, using tariff schedules specific to each port authority (Qatar Ports Management Company, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority). Port dues must be apportioned between T/S and gateway cargo based on the vessel's declared cargo manifest. The system must handle differential wharfage rates for T/S versus gateway cargo where applicable, and must reconcile attributed revenue against port authority invoices with automated discrepancy flagging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R013	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Bundled Service Package Revenue Tracking	The system must track revenue from bundled transshipment service packages offered to carriers, which may include guaranteed connection times, priority berthing, dedicated yard blocks, and value-added services (VGM weighing, container inspection, fumigation). Each bundle must be defined as a service catalog item with included components and overage charges. Revenue must be recognized per component when the bundle price is disaggregated for management reporting purposes, using stand-alone selling price methodology compliant with IFRS 15 revenue recognition standards. The system must track bundle utilization rates to identify underutilized components and support commercial teams in optimizing package offerings per carrier segment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R014	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Hijri Calendar Financial Period Support	The system must support financial period reporting aligned with both Gregorian and Hijri (Islamic) calendar systems for revenue attribution and profitability analysis. Saudi Arabia entity operations must support Hijri fiscal year reporting as required by ZATCA regulations, while other entities use Gregorian calendars. Period-end cutoffs, revenue accruals, and rebate calculations must correctly handle the variable-length Hijri months (29-30 days). Comparative reporting must properly align Hijri and Gregorian periods with clear labeling. The UI must display dates in both calendar systems simultaneously where configured, with Arabic month names rendered correctly in RTL layout. All financial period boundaries must be validated against the Umm al-Qura calendar used officially in Saudi Arabia.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R015	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Offline Revenue Capture and Synchronization	The system must support offline revenue data capture at hub terminals experiencing network connectivity issues, ensuring no revenue leakage during outages. Offline-capable terminals must cache container movement events, charge calculations, and carrier billing data locally with timestamps and operator authentication. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize cached records with the central revenue database using conflict resolution rules: latest-timestamp-wins for charge updates, additive-merge for new container events, and manual review queue for conflicting carrier rate changes. Synchronization must generate a reconciliation report comparing offline-calculated charges against centrally recalculated charges, flagging discrepancies exceeding configurable thresholds (e.g., 2% variance). All offline transactions must be audit-logged with the offline period identifier.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-001-R016	FEAT-053-2-001	MOD-053	Profitability Benchmark Comparison Across Hubs	The system must enable cross-hub profitability benchmarking comparing transshipment performance metrics across Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Benchmarks must normalize for volume differences, local cost structures, currency effects, and tariff variations to produce comparable efficiency-adjusted profitability scores. Key metrics must include revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, margin per TEU, revenue per berth meter per annum, and revenue per hectare of yard space. The system must present benchmarks in a unified dashboard with the ability to toggle between USD-equivalent and local currency views. Trend analysis must show quarter-over-quarter and year-over-year performance evolution for each hub, enabling management to identify best practices transferable between hubs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R001	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Quay Crane Productivity Metrics for T/S Operations	The system must calculate and display quay crane productivity metrics specific to transshipment vessel operations, including gross moves per hour (GMPH), net moves per hour (NMPH), crane intensity (cranes deployed per vessel), and T/S container ratio per crane shift. Metrics must distinguish between discharge and loading phases, and between T/S containers and gateway containers handled on the same vessel. The system must track delays attributed to T/S-specific factors such as restow moves required for T/S container extraction, hatch cover repositioning, and lashing/unlashing time. Productivity baselines must be configurable per berth, vessel size class (feeder, Panamax, post-Panamax, ULCV), and time of day to account for shift variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R002	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Yard Utilization Heatmap for Transshipment Blocks	The system must generate real-time yard utilization heatmaps showing occupancy levels in designated transshipment container blocks, segregated by container status (awaiting second-leg vessel, under customs hold, reefer plugged-in, hazmat segregated). Heatmaps must display TEU ground slot occupancy, stack height utilization, and weight distribution across yard bays. The system must highlight blocks exceeding 85% utilization in amber and 95% in red, triggering alerts to yard planners. Historical utilization data must be available for trend analysis with granularity down to hourly intervals. The heatmap must support touch-screen interaction on yard operations tablets and render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic display modes with proper coordinate mirroring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R005	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Advisory Board Virtual Meeting Integration	The system must support scheduling and tracking of Change Advisory Board (CAB) meetings where pending change requests are reviewed and dispositioned. Meeting agendas must be auto-generated from queued CRs, grouped by entity and priority, with links to impact analysis documents. The system must record CAB decisions (approve, reject, defer, request more info) against each CR and capture meeting minutes with attendee lists. Time zone handling must account for Qatar (AST/UTC+3), UAE (GST/UTC+4), KSA (AST/UTC+3), and India (IST/UTC+5:30) for cross-entity CAB sessions. Hijri and Gregorian dates must both display on meeting invitations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R003	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Vessel Turnaround Time Analysis	The system must track and analyze vessel turnaround time (VTT) for transshipment calls, decomposed into component phases: pilot boarding to berth (arrival maneuvering), berth to first crane lift (preparation), crane operations duration, last crane lift to unberth (departure preparation), and unberth to pilot disembarkation. Each phase must be measured against service-level agreement (SLA) targets defined per carrier and vessel size class. The system must calculate T/S-specific turnaround metrics by isolating the time attributable to T/S container handling versus gateway cargo. Trend analysis must show VTT improvement or degradation over rolling 30, 60, and 90-day windows. Comparative analysis across hub ports must normalize for vessel size and move count differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R004	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Inter-Terminal Transfer Time Monitoring	The system must monitor and report on inter-terminal transfer (ITT) times for transshipment containers moving between terminals within multi-terminal hub complexes (e.g., Jebel Ali Terminal 1 to Terminal 3, JNPT BMCT to NSIGT). Transfer time must be measured from container discharge at origin berth to container availability in destination terminal yard block. The system must track individual transfer legs: yard pickup, gate-out from origin terminal, road/rail transit, gate-in at destination terminal, and yard placement. GPS/RFID-based tracking of ITT vehicles must feed real-time position data. The system must flag transfers exceeding configurable time thresholds and correlate delays with root causes (traffic congestion, gate queue, equipment breakdown) for operational improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R005	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Transshipment Container Dwell Time Analytics	The system must provide comprehensive dwell time analytics for transshipment containers at each hub, measuring the duration from first-leg vessel discharge to second-leg vessel loading. Dwell time must be segmented into operational phases: discharge to yard placement, yard storage, yard extraction to quayside, and quayside to vessel loading. The system must calculate average, median, 90th percentile, and maximum dwell times by carrier, trade lane, container type, and hub port. AI-driven anomaly detection must identify containers with dwell times significantly exceeding the norm for their route corridor and flag potential causes (missed connections, documentation delays, customs holds). Dwell time KPIs must be reportable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods for KSA entity compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R006	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Berth Productivity Comparison Dashboard	The system must present a berth productivity comparison dashboard enabling hub managers to compare transshipment handling performance across berths within the same terminal and across hub ports. Metrics must include berth occupancy rate for T/S vessel calls, average moves per berth hour, berth time versus ship time ratio, and idle time between T/S vessel calls. The dashboard must support filtering by date range, vessel operator, vessel size class, and shift. Comparative rankings must highlight top-performing and underperforming berths with drill-down to contributing factors. The dashboard must render in responsive layouts for desktop monitors in control rooms and tablet devices used by berth supervisors, with full Arabic language support including proper right-to-left chart axis rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R007	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Equipment Utilization Tracking for T/S Operations	The system must track utilization rates of terminal equipment deployed for transshipment operations, including rubber-tired gantry cranes (RTG), rail-mounted gantry cranes (RMG), reach stackers, internal transfer vehicles (ITV/AGV), and terminal tractors. Utilization must be measured as productive hours versus available hours, with downtime categorized by cause (maintenance, breakdown, no-work, operator unavailability). The system must calculate equipment productivity ratios specific to T/S moves (moves per equipment hour) and compare against OEM benchmarks and historical baselines. Predictive maintenance alerts must be generated when equipment productivity drops below configurable thresholds, indicating potential mechanical degradation. Equipment utilization reports must support multi-entity consolidation across all four hub ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R008	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Gate Truck Turnaround for ITT Movements	The system must measure and report gate truck turnaround times for inter-terminal transfer movements, capturing arrival at gate, document verification duration, security screening time, container pickup/drop-off duration, and gate exit. The system must differentiate between self-hauled ITT (carrier-arranged transport) and terminal-operated ITT vehicles. Queue time at both origin and destination terminal gates must be tracked separately from processing time. The system must generate alerts when gate turnaround exceeds SLA thresholds (typically 30-45 minutes total). Analytics must identify peak congestion periods and recommend optimal scheduling windows for ITT movements to minimize wait times. Reports must be available in both English and Arabic with proper date and time formatting per locale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R009	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Connection Reliability Index	The system must calculate a Connection Reliability Index (CRI) measuring the percentage of transshipment containers that successfully connect to their planned second-leg vessel without requiring re-planning. The CRI must be computed per carrier, per service loop pair (inbound service to outbound service), per hub port, and overall. The system must track reasons for missed connections categorized as: first-leg vessel delay, second-leg vessel schedule change, terminal operational delay, customs/regulatory hold, and documentation incomplete. Historical CRI trends must be displayable over configurable time ranges with drill-down to individual container-level connection failure details. The system must benchmark CRI against industry standards and contractual SLA targets, generating exception reports when CRI falls below agreed thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R010	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	AI-Driven Anomaly Detection in Hub Operations	The system must implement AI-driven anomaly detection algorithms that continuously monitor hub operational metrics and automatically identify deviations from expected performance patterns. Anomalies must be detected across multiple dimensions simultaneously: crane productivity drops, unusual dwell time spikes, yard congestion patterns, equipment failure clusters, and vessel schedule deviation impacts. The AI engine must distinguish between one-time anomalies (weather events, vessel mechanical issues) and systemic issues (process bottlenecks, recurring equipment failures) using pattern recognition over rolling time windows. Each detected anomaly must generate an alert with severity classification (informational, warning, critical), probable root cause analysis, and recommended corrective actions. The system must learn from operator feedback on anomaly relevance to reduce false positive rates over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R011	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Capacity Planning Forecast Model	The system must provide an AI-powered capacity planning forecast model that predicts future transshipment throughput demand and identifies potential capacity constraints at each hub. The model must ingest vessel schedule data, carrier growth projections, trade lane volume forecasts, and infrastructure development timelines (e.g., new berth construction, yard expansion projects). Forecasts must project daily, weekly, and monthly throughput by container type and identify periods when demand is projected to exceed berth capacity, crane capacity, yard storage capacity, or gate processing capacity. Scenario modeling must allow planners to simulate the impact of adding equipment, extending operating hours, or re-allocating yard space. Output must include capital expenditure implications rendered in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R012	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Shift-Level Productivity Reporting	The system must generate shift-level productivity reports for transshipment operations, comparing performance across day shift, evening shift, and night shift for each hub terminal. Reports must include moves per shift, average crane rate per shift, equipment utilization per shift, incident count per shift, and T/S containers processed per shift. The system must account for Ramadan schedule adjustments where shift durations and break times change, as well as Friday prayer time impacts on productivity in GCC ports. Shift handover reports must summarize pending T/S operations, containers at risk of missing connections, and equipment status. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri date headers and be exportable in PDF format with proper Arabic typography rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R013	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Environmental Efficiency Metrics for T/S Operations	The system must track environmental efficiency metrics for transshipment operations including fuel consumption per TEU moved, electricity consumption for reefer monitoring, carbon emissions per transshipment move, and idle engine hours for yard equipment. Metrics must support reporting requirements for IMO Greenhouse Gas Strategy compliance and port-level environmental certifications (e.g., EcoPorts, Green Marine equivalent for Middle East ports). The system must calculate the environmental impact difference between direct shipping routes and transshipment routing to quantify hub efficiency in carbon terms. Reports must be generated per entity for inclusion in corporate sustainability disclosures and must support GCC-specific environmental reporting frameworks as mandated by respective national environmental agencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R014	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Real-Time Operational KPI Display Board	The system must support real-time KPI display boards for terminal control rooms showing live transshipment operational metrics. Display boards must show current vessel operations (T/S containers remaining to discharge/load per vessel), yard status (T/S block utilization), equipment deployment, and containers at risk of missing connections in the next 24 hours. KPIs must update at a minimum frequency of every 60 seconds. The display must support large-format screens (55-inch and above) with configurable layout panels. Color-coded status indicators must follow international standards (green/amber/red). The system must function in a degraded mode showing last-known-good data during temporary connectivity loss to the central system, with a clear visual indicator that data may be stale.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R015	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Benchmarking Against Global Hub Port Standards	The system must support benchmarking of hub efficiency metrics against configurable global hub port standards and industry benchmarks published by organizations such as Drewry, JOC, and the International Association of Ports and Harbors (IAPH). Benchmark categories must include vessel turnaround time per move, crane productivity (GMPH), yard density (TEU per hectare), berth utilization rate, and dwell time. The system must allow manual entry of benchmark values from industry reports and automatic adjustment for port-specific factors (climate, labor regulations, equipment age). Benchmark comparison reports must highlight areas where each hub meets, exceeds, or falls below global standards, with gap analysis quantifying the improvement potential in both operational and financial terms across all supported currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-002-R016	FEAT-053-2-002	MOD-053	Predictive Vessel Delay Impact Assessment	The system must perform predictive impact assessments when vessel delays are reported, calculating the cascading effect on transshipment operations at the hub. When a first-leg or second-leg vessel reports a delay via AIS data or carrier notification, the system must instantly identify all affected T/S containers, calculate revised connection timelines, flag containers at risk of missing their planned second-leg vessel, and estimate additional dwell time and associated costs. The assessment must propose mitigation actions such as expedited discharge priority, alternative second-leg vessel options, and yard repositioning to minimize re-handling. Impact assessments must be distributed to relevant stakeholders via configurable notification channels (email, SMS, in-app) in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R001	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Intelligent T/S Container Routing Algorithm	The system must implement an AI-powered routing algorithm that determines the optimal path for each transshipment container through the hub terminal, from discharge berth to yard storage location to loading berth. The algorithm must consider multiple optimization objectives: minimize total horizontal transport distance, minimize re-handling moves, maximize connection reliability, and balance workload across yard blocks. Input parameters must include vessel berthing positions, yard block availability, container weight and type (dry, reefer, hazmat, OOG), second-leg vessel ETA and berth assignment, and equipment availability. The algorithm must generate routing decisions within 500 milliseconds per container to support real-time discharge planning. Routing must comply with yard segregation rules for IMO hazardous cargo classes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R002	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Optimal Yard Slot Allocation for T/S Containers	The system must use AI to allocate optimal yard slots for transshipment containers, considering second-leg vessel discharge sequence, expected dwell duration, container weight category, and yard crane work distribution. The allocation algorithm must minimize the expected number of re-handles (shuffles) when extracting containers for second-leg vessel loading by predicting the loading sequence and pre-positioning containers accordingly. The system must maintain T/S container clusters by outbound vessel to reduce yard crane travel time during loading. Yard slot allocation must respect reefer plug availability for refrigerated T/S containers, hazmat segregation distances per IMDG Code, and weight stacking limits. The system must re-optimize allocations dynamically when vessel schedules change or unexpected yard congestion occurs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R003	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Connection Time Minimization Engine	The system must implement a connection time minimization engine that coordinates discharge and loading sequences across multiple vessels to achieve the shortest possible transshipment dwell time for time-critical containers. The engine must identify express transshipment opportunities where containers can be transferred directly from inbound vessel to outbound vessel via quayside or with minimal yard dwell (hot transfer or warm transfer). The system must coordinate crane sequencing on both inbound and outbound vessels to align discharge and loading windows for express T/S containers. Connection time targets must be configurable per carrier SLA, with the engine prioritizing containers approaching their connection deadline. The system must factor in customs clearance processing time requirements for containers transiting between different customs zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R004	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Dynamic Resource Allocation for T/S Peaks	The system must dynamically allocate terminal resources (cranes, yard equipment, labor gangs, ITT vehicles) based on AI-predicted transshipment workload peaks. The allocation engine must analyze vessel arrival clusters, T/S volume spikes by day of week, seasonal patterns (e.g., pre-Ramadan cargo surges, Diwali season for JNPT), and real-time operational status. Resource reallocation recommendations must be generated at least 4 hours ahead of predicted peaks, allowing operations managers to adjust staffing and equipment deployment. The system must balance resource allocation between T/S operations and gateway cargo handling to prevent service degradation for either. Resource allocation decisions must consider labor regulations specific to each jurisdiction (Qatar Labour Law, UAE Labour Law, India Dock Workers Act).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R005	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Stowage Coordination for Outbound T/S Vessels	The system must coordinate with vessel stowage planning systems to optimize the loading sequence and bay positions for transshipment containers on outbound vessels. The AI engine must communicate T/S container details (weight, type, IMO class, dimensions, destination port, onward connection at next hub) to the vessel planner, recommending stowage positions that facilitate efficient discharge at the next port of call. The system must flag stowage conflicts (weight distribution violations, hazmat segregation breaches, reefer plug availability on vessel) before loading commences. Integration must support BAPLIE (EDI message type for bayplan) version 2.2 and above for stowage data exchange. The system must handle multi-port discharge scenarios where T/S containers share the outbound vessel with gateway export containers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R006	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Predictive Connection Risk Scoring	The system must calculate a real-time connection risk score for every transshipment container in the hub, predicting the probability of successfully connecting to the planned second-leg vessel. The risk score must incorporate: current first-leg vessel ETA (from AIS data), discharge sequence position, yard transfer time estimate, required customs processing time, second-leg vessel ETD stability (historical on-time performance), and current terminal workload. Risk scores must be updated continuously and presented on a scale of 0-100 with configurable threshold bands: green (75-100, high confidence), amber (50-74, monitor), red (0-49, intervention required). Containers scoring below the red threshold must trigger automatic escalation workflows including alternative vessel identification and carrier notification. The scoring model must be trained on a minimum of 24 months of historical connection data per hub.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R007	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Multi-Hub Transshipment Route Optimization	The system must optimize transshipment routing for containers that may transit through multiple hub ports in the network (e.g., JNPT to Jebel Ali to Hamad Port). The AI engine must evaluate all feasible routing options through the hub network considering total transit time, total cost, connection reliability at each hub, available vessel services, and carrier preferences. Route optimization must account for customs and regulatory requirements at each transit point, including bonded cargo regulations in India (Customs Act Section 54), UAE free zone rules, Qatar customs transit procedures, and KSA ZATCA regulations. The system must present top-3 route options with cost-benefit analysis in the carrier's preferred currency and recommend the optimal route based on weighted scoring of time, cost, and reliability factors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R008	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Machine Learning Model Training and Retraining	The system must implement automated machine learning model training and retraining pipelines for all AI optimization components. Initial model training must use a minimum of 24 months of historical operational data from each hub port. Models must be retrained on a configurable schedule (default: weekly) incorporating recent operational data to capture evolving patterns such as new service loops, terminal layout changes, and equipment fleet modifications. The system must maintain model versioning with rollback capability, A/B testing framework for comparing model versions, and performance metrics tracking (prediction accuracy, optimization quality score, inference latency). Model training must be isolated from production systems, with validated models promoted to production through an approval workflow. Training data must be anonymized where required by data protection regulations applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R009	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Real-Time Optimization Decision Audit Trail	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail for all AI-generated optimization decisions, recording the input parameters, algorithm version, decision output, confidence score, and timestamp for each optimization action. The audit trail must capture whether the AI recommendation was accepted, modified, or overridden by a human operator, along with the operator's identity and reason for override. This trail must support regulatory compliance requirements for automated decision-making systems under applicable laws. Audit records must be immutable and retained for a minimum of 7 years. The system must generate periodic reports analyzing override patterns to identify opportunities for model improvement. Audit trail queries must support filtering by date range, container ID, vessel, operator, decision type, and hub port, with results exportable in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R010	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Weather and External Event Impact Modeling	The system must incorporate weather forecasts and external event data into transshipment optimization decisions. Weather data sources must include maritime weather services providing wind speed, wave height, visibility, and precipitation forecasts for each hub port's approaches and berth areas. The AI engine must predict weather-related operational impacts such as crane wind speed shutdowns (typically above 65 km/h for quay cranes), fog-related port closures, and extreme heat labor restrictions in GCC summer months (MOHRE midday work ban June-September in UAE, similar in Qatar and KSA). External events such as port authority maintenance windows, national holidays (including variable Eid dates calculated from Hijri calendar), and regulatory inspections must also be factored into optimization schedules. The system must proactively re-optimize T/S plans when weather or event conditions change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R011	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Container Prioritization Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable rules engine for prioritizing transshipment containers in optimization decisions. Priority rules must support multiple criteria including: carrier tier (premium vs. standard service agreements), cargo type (perishable reefer highest priority), connection time urgency (containers with tight connections prioritized), revenue value (high-value cargo prioritized), and contractual SLA penalties at risk. The rules engine must allow hub-specific priority configurations where each port can define its own priority weighting scheme. Conflict resolution logic must handle cases where multiple containers compete for the same resource (crane, yard slot, ITT vehicle) with different priority attributes. The system must log all priority-based decisions with justification for compliance review. Priority rules must be editable through an administrative interface with approval workflow and change history tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R012	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Simulation Engine for What-If Scenarios	The system must provide a simulation engine allowing operations planners to run what-if scenarios before committing to transshipment plans. Scenarios must support modifying parameters such as: vessel arrival times, T/S volume changes, equipment availability (crane breakdown simulation), yard block closures, weather events, and resource allocation strategies. The simulation must model the full transshipment workflow from vessel discharge through yard operations to vessel loading, producing predicted KPIs including throughput, dwell times, connection reliability, equipment utilization, and cost. Simulations must complete within 5 minutes for a 24-hour operational window. Results must be comparable side-by-side for up to 4 scenarios simultaneously. The simulation engine must use the same optimization algorithms as the production system to ensure representative results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R013	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Automated Feeder Vessel Slot Booking	The system must automate feeder vessel slot booking for transshipment containers requiring onward transport from the hub to final destination ports in the region. The AI engine must evaluate available feeder services based on destination port, required delivery date, available slot capacity, cost, and carrier alliance preferences. Automated booking must generate EDI booking requests (IFTMIN messages) to feeder operators with container details, requested sailing date, and special requirements (reefer settings, hazmat declarations, OOG dimensions). The system must handle booking confirmations, waitlist management, and automatic rebooking when preferred sailings are full. Booking decisions must consider multi-currency freight rate comparisons across feeder operators, converting rates to the hub entity's base currency for cost optimization. Manual override capability must be available for commercial or operational exceptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R014	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	AI Model Explainability and Transparency	The system must provide explainability features for all AI optimization decisions, enabling operations staff and management to understand why specific routing, allocation, or scheduling decisions were made. Each AI recommendation must include a human-readable explanation listing the top contributing factors and their relative weights in the decision. The system must support SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) or equivalent feature importance visualization for complex model outputs. Explanations must be available in both English and Arabic with shipping-domain-specific terminology. The system must provide a model performance dashboard showing accuracy metrics, prediction confidence distributions, and decision quality scores over time. Regulatory compliance documentation must be auto-generated describing model architecture, training data characteristics, fairness assessments, and known limitations for each deployed AI model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R015	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Graceful AI Degradation and Manual Fallback	The system must implement graceful degradation when AI optimization services are unavailable or producing low-confidence results. Fallback levels must include: Level 1 (AI with reduced feature set using cached data), Level 2 (rule-based heuristic optimization using predefined business rules), and Level 3 (manual planning mode with decision support tools). The system must automatically detect AI service degradation through health checks monitoring inference latency, prediction accuracy, and model drift metrics. Transition between levels must be automatic with operator notification, and manual override must allow operators to force a specific fallback level. All fallback modes must maintain full audit logging and support core transshipment operations including container routing, yard allocation, and connection tracking. The system must queue optimization requests during outages and process them when AI services recover, reconciling any manual decisions made during the fallback period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R016	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Cross-Hub Load Balancing Optimization	The system must optimize transshipment workload distribution across hub ports in the network when multiple hubs can serve the same trade lane. The AI engine must evaluate real-time capacity utilization, berth availability, yard congestion levels, equipment status, and vessel schedules at each hub to recommend the optimal hub for new transshipment bookings. Load balancing must consider commercial factors (carrier-specific hub preferences, tariff differentials, free zone benefits), operational factors (connection frequency, transit time competitiveness), and strategic factors (hub development targets, market share objectives per trade lane). The system must generate load balancing recommendations as part of the carrier booking response and support what-if analysis for commercial teams evaluating hub allocation strategies. All financial calculations must handle multi-currency conversions between QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD with daily exchange rate updates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-003-R017	FEAT-053-2-003	MOD-053	Integration with Terminal Operating Systems	The system must integrate bidirectionally with Terminal Operating Systems (TOS) at each hub port to exchange real-time operational data and deliver AI optimization instructions. Integration must support standard TOS platforms including Navis N4, TBA TOPS, Tideworks Spinnaker, and custom TOS implementations. Data exchange must include vessel schedules, berth allocations, container positions, equipment assignments, work orders, and gate transactions. The system must publish AI-generated optimization plans (yard allocation plans, discharge/load sequences, equipment deployment recommendations) to the TOS in executable format. Integration must use industry-standard protocols (REST API, MQ messaging, EDI) with configurable message mapping. The system must handle TOS version upgrades without service disruption through backward-compatible API versioning. Latency for real-time data exchange must not exceed 2 seconds end-to-end.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R001	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Configurable Dwell Time Threshold Definitions	The system must support configurable dwell time thresholds that define when a transshipment container transitions from standard storage to penalty-incurring long-stay status. Thresholds must be definable at multiple levels: global default, per hub port, per carrier contract, per container type (dry, reefer, hazmat, OOG), and per trade lane. The system must support multiple threshold tiers: warning threshold (e.g., 5 days), penalty commencement threshold (e.g., 7 days), escalated penalty threshold (e.g., 14 days), and forced removal threshold (e.g., 30 days). Thresholds must be expressed in calendar days or business days (excluding port-specific holidays and weekends as configured). Each threshold must be associated with specific actions: notification, penalty calculation start, escalation, or customs authority reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R002	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Automated Penalty Calculation Engine	The system must automatically calculate long transshipment penalties based on the number of days exceeding the applicable threshold, the penalty rate schedule, and the container type. Penalty rate schedules must support tiered structures (e.g., days 8-14 at USD 50/day, days 15-21 at USD 100/day, days 22+ at USD 200/day per TEU). Rates must be configurable in any supported currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) and converted to the billing currency using the exchange rate on the date of penalty assessment. The system must handle partial-day calculations for containers cleared on the final day. Penalty calculations must automatically exclude periods covered by force majeure declarations (port closures, severe weather, government-mandated shutdowns) and apply carrier-specific free-time extensions where contractually agreed. Calculations must be auditable with step-by-step breakdown visible to both terminal staff and carrier representatives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R003	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Proactive Warning Notification System	The system must send proactive warning notifications to carriers and internal stakeholders when transshipment containers approach penalty thresholds. Notifications must be triggered at configurable intervals before the penalty commencement date (e.g., 48 hours before, 24 hours before, at penalty commencement). Notification channels must include email, SMS, EDI IFTSTA status messages, and in-app alerts. Notifications must include container number, current dwell days, applicable threshold, estimated penalty amount if not cleared, second-leg vessel options with ETDs, and recommended actions. Notifications must be sent in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic) with amounts displayed in both local currency and USD. The system must track notification delivery status and maintain a log of all warnings sent per container for dispute resolution purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R004	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Customs Bond Expiry Monitoring	The system must monitor customs bond expiry dates for transshipment containers under customs transit bond and trigger alerts when bond expiry approaches. In India, transshipment cargo at JNPT operates under customs bond (Customs Act Section 54) with specific time limits. In GCC ports, customs transit guarantees have defined validity periods. The system must track bond/guarantee validity dates per container, calculate remaining validity, and alert operations staff when containers risk exceeding their bond period. The system must initiate bond extension requests where permitted or escalate to customs brokers for immediate action. Bond expiry violations must be reported to compliance officers and flagged in the penalty tracking system as they may attract customs fines and regulatory sanctions separate from commercial dwell penalties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R005	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Carrier Penalty Invoice Generation	The system must automatically generate penalty invoices to carriers for long transshipment dwell time charges. Invoices must include detailed line items showing container number, arrival date, discharge date from first-leg vessel, applicable free-time period, penalty commencement date, daily rate applied per tier, total penalty days, and total amount due. Invoices must be generated in the billing entity's base currency with the applicable tax treatment (zero-rated in Qatar Free Zone, UAE Free Zone, or standard VAT rate as applicable). Invoice formatting must comply with each jurisdiction's e-invoicing requirements (ZATCA Phase 2 e-invoicing for KSA, FTA requirements for UAE). The system must support both English and Arabic invoice templates with proper RTL formatting. Invoices must be transmittable via email, EDI INVOIC message, and downloadable from the carrier self-service portal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R006	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Penalty Dispute Management Workflow	The system must provide a structured dispute management workflow for carriers to contest long transshipment penalty charges. The workflow must support dispute submission with mandatory fields: container number, dispute reason category (force majeure, terminal delay, documentation error, contract interpretation, rate disagreement), supporting evidence upload, and requested resolution. Dispute resolution must follow a configurable multi-stage process: initial review by operations team, escalation to commercial manager, and final arbitration by senior management. Each stage must have defined SLA response times. The system must track dispute status, resolution history, financial adjustments (full waiver, partial credit, penalty confirmed), and pattern analysis identifying carriers with frequent disputes. Dispute communications must support bilingual correspondence (English and Arabic) and maintain a complete audit trail for regulatory and legal reference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R007	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Root Cause Analysis for Long Dwell Containers	The system must perform automated root cause analysis for containers incurring long transshipment penalties, categorizing delay reasons from a predefined taxonomy. Root cause categories must include: missed vessel connection due to first-leg delay, missed connection due to terminal operational issues, second-leg vessel schedule change or cancellation, customs hold (documentation deficiency, physical inspection required, regulatory clearance pending), carrier-requested hold, shipper-requested diversion, equipment damage requiring repair, hazmat documentation non-compliance, and port authority directive. The system must automatically assign the primary root cause based on operational event data (vessel movements, customs status updates, carrier instructions) with manual override capability. Root cause data must feed into aggregate analytics showing cause distribution trends per hub, per carrier, and per trade lane, enabling targeted corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R005	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Conversational Follow-Up and Context Retention	The AI assistant shall maintain conversational context across a session of up to 50 exchanges, enabling follow-up questions without requiring the user to restate context. For example, if a user asks about customs clearance procedures at Jebel Ali and then asks 'What about for dangerous goods?', the system must understand the implicit reference to Jebel Ali customs clearance for DG cargo. Session context shall persist for a configurable timeout period (default 30 minutes) and shall be entity-aware so that switching between Qatar and UAE operational contexts is handled gracefully. Users must be able to explicitly reset context with a command or button.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R008	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Abandoned Container Identification and Escalation	The system must identify potentially abandoned transshipment containers that have exceeded maximum dwell thresholds with no active second-leg vessel booking and no carrier communication for a configurable period (default: 30 days beyond penalty commencement). Identified containers must be escalated through a defined workflow: initial carrier contact attempt via multiple channels, formal notice to carrier with deadline for collection or instruction, notification to port authority and customs, and initiation of container disposition procedures per local regulations. In India, abandoned cargo at JNPT follows Customs Act Section 48 procedures. In GCC ports, the system must comply with respective port authority abandoned goods regulations. The system must track all communication attempts with timestamps and calculate accumulated charges (storage plus penalties) for carrier recovery or lien proceedings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R009	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Penalty Revenue Accounting Integration	The system must integrate penalty charges with the financial accounting system, creating appropriate journal entries for penalty revenue recognition, accounts receivable updates, and aged receivables tracking. Revenue must be recognized in the entity that operates the hub port where the container is stored. The system must handle multi-currency penalty billing with proper FX gain/loss recognition when penalties invoiced in one currency are settled in another. Penalty accruals must be calculated daily for financial reporting purposes, with the system generating accrual entries at period-end for containers still in penalty status. Bad debt provisions must be automatically calculated for penalties outstanding beyond configurable aging thresholds (e.g., 90 days past due). Integration must support the chart of accounts structure for each entity and comply with IFRS accounting standards for revenue recognition and receivables measurement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R010	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Regulatory Reporting for Long-Stay Containers	The system must generate regulatory reports for port authorities and customs agencies listing all transshipment containers exceeding mandated maximum dwell periods. Report formats must comply with specific regulatory requirements at each port: Qatar Customs General Authority reporting templates, UAE Federal Customs Authority requirements, Saudi ZATCA customs reporting formats, and Indian CBIC (Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs) reporting requirements. Reports must include container details, vessel information, carrier identity, dwell duration, bond status, and actions taken. The system must support automated submission of reports via approved electronic channels where available (e.g., ICEGATE in India, UAE FCA portal). Report generation must be schedulable (daily, weekly, monthly) and support ad-hoc generation for customs audit requests. All reports must maintain bilingual capability for Arabic and English as required by the receiving authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R011	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Carrier Performance Scorecarding for Dwell Compliance	The system must maintain carrier performance scorecards tracking dwell time compliance for transshipment containers. Scorecard metrics must include: percentage of containers cleared within free time, average dwell time, number of penalty-incurring containers, total penalty value, dispute rate, and payment timeliness. Scorecards must be calculated monthly and available in quarterly and annual aggregations. The system must rank carriers by dwell compliance and generate trend reports showing improvement or degradation over time. Scorecard data must feed into carrier relationship management processes, supporting annual contract negotiations with data-driven evidence. Poor-performing carriers must be flagged for commercial review when their compliance score falls below configurable thresholds. Scorecards must be viewable by carrier representatives through the self-service portal with export capability in PDF and Excel formats with proper Arabic language support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R012	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Grace Period and Waiver Management	The system must support configurable grace periods and penalty waivers to handle legitimate exceptions to standard dwell time penalties. Grace periods must be applicable for documented force majeure events (natural disasters, port closures, geopolitical disruptions, pandemics), vessel mechanical failures with classification society confirmation, port authority-directed holds, and Ramadan/Eid operational schedule extensions in GCC ports. Waiver requests must follow an approval workflow with authorization levels based on waiver value: operational manager up to USD 5,000 equivalent, commercial director up to USD 25,000, and regional VP for amounts above. The system must track total waivers granted per carrier per period, calculate revenue impact, and generate exception reports for management review. Grace period extensions must automatically adjust penalty calculation start dates and regenerate affected invoices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R013	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Container Relocation Trigger for Yard Optimization	The system must trigger automatic container relocation recommendations when long-stay transshipment containers occupy premium yard positions (near berth, reefer plug slots, or high-traffic lanes) that could be better utilized by short-dwell containers. The system must calculate the cost-benefit of relocating long-stay containers to designated long-term storage areas versus the operational value of freeing premium positions. Relocation triggers must fire when containers exceed configurable dwell thresholds (e.g., 10 days for prime positions, 14 days for standard positions). The system must generate relocation work orders for yard operations with optimal timing recommendations to minimize equipment repositioning overhead. Reefer containers must be relocated only to positions with available power connections. Relocation history must be tracked per container and factored into re-handling cost calculations that may be passed to the carrier as part of the long-stay penalty assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R014	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Historical Penalty Analytics and Trend Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive historical analytics on long transshipment penalties including total penalty revenue by period, average penalty duration, most penalized carriers, most affected trade lanes, and seasonal penalty patterns. Analytics must support multi-dimensional analysis with drill-down from hub-level summaries to individual container-level details. The system must identify correlations between penalty frequency and external factors such as weather disruptions, schedule reliability changes, trade volume fluctuations, and regulatory changes. Trend reports must compare penalty metrics across Gregorian calendar periods and optionally Hijri calendar periods for KSA entity reporting. All financial amounts in analytics must be displayable in both local entity currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD equivalent using period-average exchange rates. Reports must support export in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with proper Arabic text encoding and RTL formatting where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R015	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	SLA-Based Penalty Exemption Processing	The system must automatically identify and process penalty exemptions for transshipment containers covered by SLA-based exemption clauses in carrier contracts. When a carrier's contract includes provisions such as guaranteed connection windows or terminal-responsibility clauses where the terminal commits to specific T/S handling times, the system must evaluate whether the long dwell is attributable to terminal performance failure. If terminal KPIs (crane productivity, ITT transfer time, yard equipment availability) fell below SLA benchmarks during the container's handling window, the system must automatically exempt the container from penalties or reduce the penalty proportionally to the terminal's contribution to the delay. Exemption processing must generate a detailed calculation showing SLA benchmarks versus actual performance, terminal-attributable delay hours, and resulting penalty adjustment. All exemptions must be logged with financial impact quantification for management review and SLA renegotiation data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R016	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Real-Time Long-Stay Container Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying all transshipment containers currently in or approaching long-stay penalty status across all hub ports. The dashboard must show containers segmented by status: approaching warning threshold, in warning period, in penalty period, in escalated penalty period, and approaching forced removal. Each container entry must display key details: container number, carrier, first-leg vessel, discharge date, current dwell days, applicable threshold, accumulated penalty amount, second-leg vessel booking status, and assigned action owner. The dashboard must support filtering by hub port, carrier, container type, dwell duration range, and penalty amount range. Automatic refresh must occur every 5 minutes. The dashboard must be accessible in both English and Arabic with proper RTL layout, and amounts must be shown in the hub port's local currency with USD equivalent on hover. Color-coded urgency indicators must immediately draw attention to containers requiring immediate action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-053-2-004-R017	FEAT-053-2-004	MOD-053	Penalty Escalation Workflow with Multi-Level Approval	The system must implement a multi-level escalation workflow for managing long transshipment containers through defined penalty stages. Escalation levels must include: Level 1 (operations team awareness when warning threshold is reached), Level 2 (operations supervisor involvement when penalty commences), Level 3 (commercial manager engagement when escalated penalty tier triggers), Level 4 (hub director notification when forced removal threshold approaches), and Level 5 (regional VP notification for containers exceeding maximum dwell with no resolution). Each escalation level must have defined actions: mandatory carrier outreach, management report generation, yard repositioning assessment, and customs authority notification where required. Escalation timelines must be configurable per hub port to reflect local operational practices and regulatory frameworks. The workflow must track all escalation events with timestamps, responsible personnel, actions taken, and outcomes. Escalation notifications must support bilingual delivery in English and Arabic via email, SMS, and in-application alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R001	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Hierarchical SOP Repository Structure	The system must maintain a hierarchical SOP repository organized by operational domain (vessel operations, container yard management, customs clearance, freight forwarding, documentation, finance), by port (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA), and by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each SOP node must support metadata tags including applicable INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP), container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, OT, FR), and regulatory authority references. The tree structure must allow unlimited nesting depth with drag-and-drop reordering capability across all hierarchy levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R002	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Bilingual SOP Authoring with RTL Support	The system must provide a rich-text SOP editor supporting simultaneous Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR) content authoring within a single document. The editor must handle mixed-direction text blocks, embedded tables with bilingual headers, and inline terminology glossaries mapping English shipping terms (Bill of Lading, Delivery Order, Container Freight Station) to their Arabic equivalents. All formatting controls must mirror correctly in RTL mode including numbered lists, indentation, and flowchart annotations. The system must support automatic translation suggestions powered by AI with domain-specific shipping terminology for all four entity jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R003	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Version Control and Change Tracking	The system must implement full version control for every SOP document with immutable version history, diff comparison between any two versions, and rollback capability to any prior approved version. Each version must record the author, reviewer, approver, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the business justification for the change. When regulatory changes occur (e.g., Qatar Customs circular updates, ZATCA VAT rule amendments, Indian Customs IGST modifications, Dubai Trade portal changes), the system must flag all affected SOPs and initiate a mandatory review cycle within 48 hours of the regulatory change notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R004	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Multi-Level SOP Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for SOP creation, modification, and retirement. Minimum approval chain must include author, department reviewer, compliance officer, and final approver. For SOPs affecting customs operations at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, an additional customs compliance review step must be mandatory. Approval workflows must support parallel and sequential approval paths, delegation of authority during absence periods, automatic escalation after configurable SLA thresholds (default 72 hours), and digital signatures compliant with Qatar Digital Government standards and UAE PASS authentication frameworks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R005	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	AI-Powered SOP Recommendation Engine	The system must provide an AI recommendation engine that suggests relevant SOPs to users based on their current operational context including active shipment type (FCL, LCL, breakbulk, project cargo), origin-destination port pair, INCOTERM in use, cargo commodity classification (HS code based), and container type being handled. The engine must learn from user interaction patterns and continuously improve recommendation accuracy. When a user initiates a task such as creating a Bill of Lading for an export from Hamad Port to JNPT under CIF terms with 40HC reefer containers, the system must proactively surface the top five most relevant SOPs ranked by contextual relevance score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R006	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Compliance Tracking and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of SOP access, acknowledgment, and compliance across all four entities. Every staff member must formally acknowledge reading and understanding SOPs relevant to their role within configurable deadlines. The system must track compliance rates per department, per port location, and per entity, generating automated alerts when acknowledgment rates fall below 90 percent threshold. Audit reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual Arabic-English headers and must include Hijri and Gregorian dates. Compliance data must be available for external audit reviews by Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority, UAE Federal Tax Authority, and Indian DGFT as applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R007	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Embedded Process Flowchart Designer	The system must include an integrated visual flowchart designer for creating process flow diagrams within SOP documents. The designer must support standard BPMN 2.0 notation including swim lanes for multi-department processes such as the container discharge workflow involving vessel operations, yard planning, customs clearance, and delivery order issuance. Flowcharts must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts and must support bilingual labels on each process node. The designer must allow linking individual flowchart steps to detailed SOP sections, training modules, regulatory references, and system screen mockups for the ERP modules involved in each step.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R008	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Regulatory Cross-Reference Matrix	The system must maintain an automated cross-reference matrix linking each SOP to applicable regulatory requirements from Qatar Customs Authority, Dubai Customs and Dubai Trade, Saudi ZATCA, Indian CBIC and DGFT, IMO SOLAS regulations, IMDG Code for hazardous cargo, and ISPS Code for port security. When a regulatory body issues an update, the system must automatically identify all affected SOPs and generate a change impact assessment report. The matrix must track regulatory effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, support multi-currency fine and penalty references (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), and highlight compliance gaps requiring immediate SOP revision.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R009	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	Offline SOP Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to SOPs on mobile devices and tablets for field staff working in container yards at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT where network connectivity may be intermittent. Users must be able to download designated SOP packages for offline viewing including all embedded images, flowcharts, and reference documents. The offline cache must support selective synchronization by port location, department, and role. When connectivity is restored, the system must automatically sync acknowledgment records, reading progress, and any annotations made offline, resolving conflicts using a last-write-wins strategy with full conflict audit logging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R010	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Effectiveness Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide an analytics dashboard measuring SOP effectiveness through metrics including average time-to-compliance per SOP, frequency of SOP consultation during operational tasks, correlation between SOP adherence and operational KPIs (container dwell time, customs clearance time, documentation error rates), and user feedback ratings. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNSA), department, and time period with both Gregorian and Hijri date selectors. AI-generated insights must highlight SOPs with declining effectiveness scores and recommend content improvements based on operational performance data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R011	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Template Library for Shipping Operations	The system must provide pre-built SOP templates specific to container shipping operations including templates for vessel arrival procedures, container discharge and loading sequences, customs declaration filing (single and consolidated), dangerous goods handling per IMDG Code classes 1 through 9, reefer container monitoring and plug-in procedures, empty container depot operations, break-bulk cargo handling, transshipment container management, and freight invoice reconciliation. Each template must be pre-populated with industry best practices, configurable per port jurisdiction, and available in both Arabic and English. Templates must include placeholder sections for entity-specific regulatory requirements and local port authority mandates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R012	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Expiry and Periodic Review Management	The system must enforce mandatory periodic review cycles for all SOPs with configurable review intervals (default 12 months for standard SOPs, 6 months for safety-critical and regulatory SOPs). The system must generate automated review reminders 30, 14, and 7 days before expiry dates calculated in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Expired SOPs that have not completed review must be automatically flagged with a prominent visual warning banner in both Arabic and English and optionally restricted from new acknowledgments until re-approved. The review workflow must capture reviewer attestation that the SOP content remains current with respect to applicable regulations from Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R013	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Search with Natural Language Processing	The system must provide an AI-powered natural language search capability allowing users to find relevant SOPs using conversational queries in both Arabic and English. For example, a query such as 'How do I handle a reefer container with temperature deviation at Jebel Ali' must return the relevant reefer monitoring SOP, escalation procedures, and insurance claim filing instructions. The search engine must understand shipping domain terminology, port-specific jargon, INCOTERMS references, container type codes (ISO 6346), and HS code classifications. Search results must be ranked by relevance with highlighted matching excerpts and must support voice input for hands-free operation in yard environments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R014	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Integration with ERP Module Workflows	The system must integrate SOPs contextually within other ERP modules so that relevant procedures are accessible directly from operational screens. When a user is processing a customs declaration in the Customs module for Hamad Port, the applicable customs filing SOP must be accessible via a single click or hover panel. Similarly, when handling a hazardous cargo booking, the IMDG Code handling SOP must be surfaced automatically. The integration must cover all major ERP modules including vessel operations, yard management, customs, documentation, freight billing, and accounts receivable across all four entity jurisdictions. SOP access from integrated modules must be logged for compliance tracking purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R015	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Knowledge Gap Analysis Reporting	The system must generate periodic knowledge gap analysis reports identifying areas where SOP coverage is insufficient relative to operational activities. The AI engine must analyze operational transaction data across all four ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, JNPT) and cross-reference against existing SOP library coverage to identify processes that lack documented procedures. The report must quantify gap severity based on transaction volume, error frequency, and regulatory risk exposure. Reports must be segmented by entity and port with bilingual Arabic-English summaries and must recommend specific SOP creation priorities. Gap analysis must account for multi-currency transaction types and INCOTERM-specific workflow variations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-001-R016	FEAT-054-1-001	MOD-054	SOP Document Attachment and Media Embedding	The system must support rich media embedding within SOP documents including photographs of container handling procedures, video tutorials for equipment operation at port terminals, PDF attachments of regulatory circulars from Qatar Customs and Dubai Trade, and downloadable form templates in Excel and PDF formats. All media must support bilingual captions in Arabic and English. Video content must support Arabic subtitles and closed captions. Maximum individual attachment size must be 500MB to accommodate high-resolution training videos. The system must maintain a media asset library shared across SOPs to prevent duplication and must track media asset usage across all referencing SOP documents for lifecycle management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R001	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Module Builder with Drag-and-Drop Interface	The system must provide a visual training module builder with drag-and-drop content block assembly supporting lesson types including text-based instruction, video lectures, interactive simulations, knowledge check quizzes, and practical assignment submissions. The builder must support bilingual Arabic-English content within a single module with automatic RTL layout switching. Each module must be taggable by port (Hamad QAHMD, Khalifa AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA), department (operations, customs, documentation, finance), role level (entry, intermediate, senior, management), and topic category (vessel ops, yard management, customs procedures, dangerous goods, reefer handling, INCOTERMS).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R002	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	AI-Assisted Training Content Generation from SOPs	The system must provide an AI engine that automatically generates draft training module content from existing SOP documents in the knowledge library. Given an SOP for customs declaration filing at Hamad Port, the AI must produce a structured training module including learning objectives, instructional content broken into digestible lessons, knowledge check questions with correct answers and explanations, and practical scenario exercises. The AI must preserve all regulatory references, INCOTERM specifications, container type details, and port-specific procedures from the source SOP. Generated content must be available in both Arabic and English and must be flagged as AI-generated pending human review and approval before publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R003	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Learning Path Configuration and Prerequisites	The system must support the creation of structured learning paths that define the sequential or parallel completion order of training modules. Prerequisites must be configurable so that advanced modules (e.g., IMDG Code dangerous goods classes 1-9 handling) require completion of foundational modules (e.g., basic container handling safety). Learning paths must be configurable per entity and per port to reflect jurisdiction-specific requirements; for instance, Qatar entity staff must complete Qatar Customs Authority e-clearance training before accessing advanced customs documentation modules. The system must visually display learning path progress with completion percentages and estimated time to complete remaining modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R004	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Multi-Format Assessment Question Bank	The system must maintain a centralized question bank supporting multiple assessment formats including multiple choice, true-false, fill-in-the-blank, drag-and-drop matching (e.g., match INCOTERMS to their responsibility allocation), scenario-based case studies (e.g., determine correct customs duty calculation for a 40HC container of textiles imported via JNPT under CIF terms with INR/USD conversion), and image-based identification questions (e.g., identify container damage types from photographs). Each question must support bilingual Arabic-English presentation, difficulty level classification, topic tagging, and randomization pools. The question bank must track item discrimination indices and question difficulty statistics to support continuous assessment quality improvement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R005	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	SCORM and xAPI Compliance for External LMS Integration	The system must support exporting training modules in SCORM 1.2 and SCORM 2004 (4th Edition) formats for integration with external Learning Management Systems used by port authorities and shipping line partners. Additionally, the system must emit xAPI (Experience API formerly Tin Can API) statements for detailed learning activity tracking including module launch, page navigation, assessment attempts, and completion events. xAPI statements must include contextual extensions for shipping-specific metadata such as port location, entity, department, and applicable regulatory domain. Import of SCORM packages from external sources such as IMO e-learning modules and port authority safety courses must also be supported with automatic metadata mapping.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R006	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Module Scheduling and Calendar Integration	The system must provide a scheduling engine for instructor-led and blended training sessions supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views. Scheduling must account for entity-specific working days (Friday-Saturday weekend for Qatar and KSA, Saturday-Sunday for UAE free zones, Saturday-Sunday for India), public holiday calendars for all four jurisdictions, and Ramadan adjusted working hours. Training sessions must integrate with Microsoft Outlook and Google Calendar via API. The system must support recurring schedule patterns, automatic waitlist management when session capacity is reached, and SMS plus email notifications in Arabic or English based on employee language preference. Multi-currency training cost tracking must support QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R007	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Interactive Shipping Simulation Exercises	The system must support interactive simulation exercises that replicate real container shipping scenarios for hands-on training. Simulations must include vessel discharge planning at Hamad Port with container sequencing constraints, customs declaration filing with duty calculation under multiple INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, DAP), reefer container temperature monitoring alarm response procedures, dangerous goods segregation validation per IMDG Code stowage matrix, and Bill of Lading data entry with validation against shipping instruction discrepancies. Each simulation must provide real-time feedback, scoring based on accuracy and efficiency, and detailed performance reports. Simulations must operate in sandbox environments isolated from production ERP data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R008	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Module Version Control and Archival	The system must implement version control for training modules with full change history, diff comparison between versions, and the ability to rollback to any previously published version. When an underlying SOP is updated, the system must automatically flag dependent training modules for review and revision. Archived versions of training modules must remain accessible for audit purposes with records of which employees completed which version. The system must track version effective dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars and must prevent employees from being credited for completing outdated module versions unless explicitly exempted by a training administrator. Each version must carry entity-specific approval stamps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R009	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Content Localization and Translation Management	The system must provide a structured localization workflow for translating training content between Arabic and English with support for regional dialect variations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The translation workflow must include AI-assisted initial translation, human reviewer assignment, domain expert validation of shipping terminology accuracy, and final approval. The system must maintain a shipping-specific translation memory database mapping terms such as Bill of Lading to بوليصة الشحن, Letter of Credit to خطاب اعتماد, and Container Freight Station to محطة شحن الحاويات. Translation status must be tracked per module with visual indicators showing fully translated, partially translated, and pending translation states.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R010	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Offline Training Module Access for Field Staff	The system must enable offline access to training modules on mobile devices and tablets for port field staff at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT where reliable internet connectivity is not guaranteed in container yard and wharf areas. Offline packages must include all module content, embedded media, assessment questions, and interactive elements. Assessment responses completed offline must be queued and automatically synchronized when connectivity is restored with timestamp preservation showing actual completion time. The offline package size must be optimized through progressive media quality loading and must support selective download by department and role to minimize storage requirements on employee devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R011	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Effectiveness Analytics and ROI Measurement	The system must provide comprehensive analytics dashboards measuring training effectiveness through metrics including module completion rates, average assessment scores, time-to-competency per role, knowledge retention rates based on periodic reassessment, and correlation between training completion and operational performance KPIs such as customs declaration error rates, documentation turnaround times, and container dwell times. The dashboard must support multi-entity comparison views (Qatar vs UAE vs KSA vs India), per-port breakdowns, and department-level drill-downs. Training cost analytics must support multi-currency reporting in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD with configurable exchange rate sources for ROI calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R012	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Automated Training Assignment Based on Role and Events	The system must automatically assign training modules to employees based on configurable triggers including new role assignment, entity transfer (e.g., Qatar to UAE office), port location change, regulatory update affecting their function, incident occurrence requiring remedial training, and periodic recertification deadlines. When a customs clerk is transferred from the Hamad Port team to the Jebel Ali team, the system must automatically assign UAE-specific customs procedures training, Dubai Trade portal training, and UAE VAT compliance training while marking Qatar-specific modules as no longer required. Assignment rules must be configurable per entity with approval workflows and must generate notifications in the employees preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R013	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Instructor and Subject Matter Expert Management	The system must maintain a registry of internal and external training instructors and subject matter experts (SMEs) with profiles including areas of expertise (customs procedures, vessel operations, dangerous goods, freight commercial, finance), certified port jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), language capabilities (Arabic, English, Hindi, Urdu), availability calendar synchronized with Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and historical training delivery ratings from participant feedback. The system must support instructor workload balancing across entities, automatic instructor suggestion based on module topic and target audience location, and cost tracking for external instructors in multiple currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) with entity-specific budget allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R014	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Completion Certificates and Digital Credentials	The system must generate digital training completion certificates upon successful module or learning path completion. Certificates must be bilingual (Arabic and English), include the employee name in both scripts, training module title, completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, assessment score, issuing entity name and logo, and a unique verification QR code. Certificates must be digitally signed using entity-specific digital certificates compliant with Qatar National PKI, UAE PASS, or India IT Act 2000 digital signature standards. The system must support integration with blockchain-based credential verification platforms and must maintain a public verification endpoint where certificate authenticity can be validated by external parties such as port authorities and shipping line partners.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R015	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Budget Planning and Cost Allocation	The system must support annual training budget planning and cost tracking per entity, per department, and per port location. Budget entries must support multi-currency amounts in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD with automated conversion using configurable exchange rates. Cost categories must include instructor fees, venue rental, travel and accommodation for cross-entity training, external course subscriptions, certification exam fees, and training material production costs. The system must generate budget variance reports comparing planned versus actual expenditure with drill-down by cost category and entity. Budget approval workflows must follow entity-specific authorization hierarchies and must integrate with the ERP accounts payable module for payment processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R006	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request SLA Management and Escalation	The system must enforce configurable SLA timelines for each stage of the change request lifecycle based on priority level: critical CRs must be assessed within 4 hours and resolved within 48 hours; high priority within 1 business day assessment and 5 business days resolution; medium within 3 business days assessment and 15 business days resolution. Business day calculations must respect entity-specific working calendars including Friday/Saturday weekends for Qatar, UAE, and KSA, and Saturday/Sunday weekends for India. Automatic escalation notifications must trigger when SLA thresholds are breached at 75% and 100% of allocated time, routing to the next management level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R016	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Gamification and Engagement Features	The system must incorporate gamification elements to drive training engagement including achievement badges for module completion milestones, leaderboards ranked by department and port location, experience points accumulation with level progression, and streak rewards for consecutive training day participation. Leaderboards must be configurable to operate within entity boundaries to ensure fair comparison among peers in similar operational contexts. Badge designs must be culturally appropriate for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India audiences. The system must support custom badge creation by training administrators with bilingual Arabic-English badge names and descriptions. Gamification data must feed into the training analytics dashboard to measure engagement impact on completion rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-002-R017	FEAT-054-1-002	MOD-054	Training Notification and Reminder Engine	The system must provide a configurable notification engine sending training-related communications via email, SMS, in-app push notifications, and Microsoft Teams or Slack integrations. Notification types must include new assignment alerts, upcoming deadline reminders at configurable intervals (14 days, 7 days, 3 days, 1 day before due date), overdue training escalation to managers, session schedule confirmations, assessment result notifications, and certificate issuance alerts. All notifications must be delivered in the recipient preferred language (Arabic or English) and must respect entity-specific quiet hours and working day configurations. Escalation chains must support multi-level management notification with configurable escalation intervals per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R001	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Framework Definition by Role and Port	The system must support the definition of competency frameworks specific to each operational role within each entity and port location. A customs documentation clerk at Hamad Port (QAHMD) must have different competency requirements than the equivalent role at JNPT (INNSA) due to differing regulatory systems (Qatar Customs Al Nadeeb system versus Indian ICEGATE system). Competency frameworks must define skills across dimensions including technical shipping knowledge, regulatory compliance awareness, system proficiency (ERP module usage), language competency (Arabic and English), and soft skills. Each competency must have defined proficiency levels (novice, developing, competent, proficient, expert) with measurable behavioral indicators per level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R002	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	AI-Driven Adaptive Assessment Engine	The system must implement an AI-driven adaptive assessment engine that dynamically adjusts question difficulty and topic focus based on the examinee real-time performance during the assessment. The engine must use Item Response Theory (IRT) models calibrated against the shipping operations question bank to estimate competency levels with statistical confidence intervals. When an employee demonstrates mastery of basic container type identification (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF), the engine must automatically advance to more complex topics such as IMDG Code dangerous goods segregation rules or multi-currency freight rate calculation. The adaptive engine must reduce average assessment time by at least 30 percent compared to fixed-length assessments while maintaining measurement reliability above 0.85 coefficient.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R003	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Multi-Dimensional Competency Matrix Visualization	The system must provide interactive competency matrix visualizations displaying employee proficiency levels across all defined competency dimensions using radar charts, heat maps, and gap analysis views. The matrix must support individual employee views, team aggregate views by department and port, and entity-level organizational capability overviews. Comparison views must allow benchmarking an individual against role-specific competency targets, department averages, and cross-entity peers. All visualization labels and legends must support bilingual Arabic-English rendering with proper RTL layout for Arabic content. Export to PDF format must maintain visual fidelity with bilingual headers including both Gregorian and Hijri assessment dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R004	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Practical Skills Assessment with Checklist Scoring	The system must support practical skills assessment through configurable observation checklists that supervisors complete while observing employee task performance in real operational settings. Checklists must cover practical scenarios including container seal verification procedures at gate-in, dangerous goods documentation verification against IMDG Code requirements, reefer container pre-trip inspection at plug-in stations, customs declaration data entry accuracy using port-specific systems, and Bill of Lading amendment processing workflows. Each checklist item must support scored ratings (not observed, below standard, meets standard, exceeds standard), assessor comments in Arabic or English, and photographic evidence attachments. Checklists must work in offline mode for yard and terminal assessments where connectivity is limited.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R005	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Assessment Scheduling and Recertification	The system must manage assessment scheduling with configurable recertification intervals per competency area and per regulatory requirement. Safety-critical competencies such as dangerous goods handling per IMDG Code must require annual recertification, while general shipping knowledge may follow a 24-month cycle. The scheduling engine must account for entity-specific working calendars including Friday-Saturday weekends for Qatar and KSA, public holidays across all four jurisdictions, and Ramadan period scheduling restrictions. Assessment dates must display in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Automated reminders must be sent at 60, 30, 14, and 7 days before recertification expiry with escalation to department managers for overdue recertifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R006	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	360-Degree Competency Feedback Collection	The system must support 360-degree competency feedback collection gathering input from supervisors, peers, direct reports, and cross-functional stakeholders. Feedback forms must be configurable per role and must include both quantitative rating scales and qualitative comment fields supporting Arabic and English text input. For a customs operations supervisor at Jebel Ali, feedback sources must include their manager, peer supervisors, customs clerks they supervise, and stakeholders from documentation and freight commercial departments who interact with customs operations. Feedback must be anonymized for peer and subordinate responses while maintaining identifiable feedback from supervisors. The system must calculate weighted composite scores with configurable weight distributions per feedback source category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R007	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Skill Gap Analysis and Development Recommendation	The system must perform automated skill gap analysis comparing employee competency assessment results against their role-specific competency requirements and generate targeted development recommendations. When a freight commercial executive at the Qatar entity shows a gap in INCOTERMS knowledge (scoring developing on a competent requirement), the system must recommend specific training modules from the training library covering INCOTERMS 2020 rules, link relevant SOPs for freight quotation procedures under different INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DAP, DDP), and suggest mentoring pairings with higher-proficiency colleagues. Recommendations must be prioritized by gap severity, business impact, and available training resources with estimated time-to-competency projections per development activity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R008	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency-Based Access Control Integration	The system must integrate competency assessment results with ERP role-based access control to enforce competency prerequisites for accessing specific system functions. A user must not be granted access to process dangerous goods bookings in the vessel operations module unless they have achieved a minimum proficient competency level in IMDG Code knowledge assessment. Similarly, customs declaration filing access at each port must require demonstrated competency in the respective port authority system (Qatar Al Nadeeb, UAE Mirsal 2, KSA FASAH, India ICEGATE). When an employee competency certification expires, the system must automatically restrict access to the competency-gated functions and notify the employee and their manager of the required recertification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R009	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Assessment Integrity and Anti-Cheating Controls	The system must implement assessment integrity controls including randomized question selection from pooled question banks with a minimum pool-to-assessment ratio of 3:1, randomized answer option ordering for multiple choice questions, configurable time limits per question and per assessment section, browser lockdown mode preventing tab switching or external application access during proctored assessments, and IP-based access restriction to ensure assessments are taken from authorized locations (office or designated testing centers at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT facilities). The system must flag suspicious patterns such as unusually fast completion times or identical answer sequences between employees taking the same assessment and generate integrity incident reports for training administrator review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R010	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Assessment Results Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard for competency assessment results with drill-down capability from organization-wide overview to entity, port, department, team, and individual employee levels. Key metrics must include average competency scores by dimension, score distribution histograms, trend analysis showing competency improvement or decline over time, recertification compliance rates, and correlation analysis between competency scores and operational performance metrics such as container throughput, documentation accuracy, and customs clearance times. The dashboard must support bilingual Arabic-English display with Hijri and Gregorian date filtering. Exportable reports must include executive summaries suitable for entity board presentations with multi-currency training investment context in QAR, AED, SAR, INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R011	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Peer Assessment and Knowledge Sharing Tracking	The system must support formal peer assessment programs where experienced employees evaluate colleagues competencies through structured review processes. The system must track knowledge sharing activities including mentoring sessions, lunch-and-learn presentations, cross-entity knowledge transfer visits (e.g., Hamad Port team visiting Jebel Ali for best practice exchange), and internal workshop facilitation. Each knowledge sharing activity must be logged with participant lists, topic tags aligned to competency framework dimensions, duration, and effectiveness ratings from participants. The system must recognize knowledge sharing contributions in employee competency profiles and factor them into overall competency development scores using configurable weighting per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R012	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	External Certification Tracking and Validation	The system must track external professional certifications relevant to container shipping operations including FIATA Diploma in Freight Forwarding, ICC INCOTERMS certification, IMDG Code competency certificates issued by approved training providers, customs broker licenses issued by Qatar Customs Authority, Dubai Customs, ZATCA, and Indian CBIC, and ISO Lead Auditor certifications for ISO 9001, ISO 14001, and ISO 28000 supply chain security. Each certification record must capture issue date and expiry date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, issuing authority, certificate number, verification URL, and scanned certificate document. The system must generate automated alerts for upcoming certification expiries and must validate certifications against issuing authority databases where API integration is available.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R013	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Assessment Workflow Approval	The system must enforce approval workflows for competency assessment results before they are finalized and reflected in employee profiles. For assessments that result in competency level changes (promotion or demotion), a minimum two-level approval chain must be enforced including the assessing supervisor and the department head. For competency downgrades that would trigger access control restrictions on ERP functions, an additional HR department review step must be required. Approval workflows must support delegation of authority during absence periods respecting entity-specific delegation rules. All approval actions must be timestamped in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars with digital audit trails. Rejected assessments must require documented justification and may trigger a reassessment scheduling workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R014	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Bulk Assessment Campaign Management	The system must support bulk assessment campaign management allowing training administrators to schedule and execute organization-wide or entity-wide competency assessments. A campaign must define the target population by entity, port, department, and role filters, the competency areas to be assessed, the assessment timeline with start and end dates in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and the notification schedule. Campaign progress must be tracked in real-time with completion rate dashboards showing responded versus pending versus overdue counts segmented by entity and port. The system must support staggered campaign rollout across time zones (Qatar UTC+3, UAE UTC+4, KSA UTC+3, India UTC+5:30) to manage assessment server load and administrator oversight capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R015	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Data Privacy and Access Controls	The system must enforce strict data privacy controls on competency assessment data complying with Qatar Data Privacy Law, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act 2023 requirements. Assessment results must be accessible only to the assessed employee, their direct reporting chain, HR department personnel with appropriate role assignments, and designated training administrators. Cross-entity competency data access must require explicit authorization; a Qatar entity HR manager must not access UAE entity employee competency data without approved cross-entity access grants. All competency data access must be logged in an immutable audit trail. Data retention periods for assessment results must be configurable per entity jurisdiction with automated archival and purging workflows compliant with each countrys data protection regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-003-R016	FEAT-054-1-003	MOD-054	Competency Benchmarking Against Industry Standards	The system must support benchmarking employee and organizational competency levels against configurable industry standards and best practice frameworks. Benchmark sources must include IMO Model Courses for maritime operations competencies, FIATA minimum standards for freight forwarding personnel, WCO (World Customs Organization) competency frameworks for customs professionals, and port-specific competency standards published by Hamad Port Authority, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must allow training administrators to map internal competency framework dimensions to external benchmark frameworks and generate gap analysis reports comparing organizational competency profiles against industry benchmarks. Reports must be available in bilingual Arabic-English format with visual gap indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R001	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Entity-Specific Onboarding Workflow Templates	The system must provide configurable onboarding workflow templates specific to each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and port location (Hamad QAHMD, Khalifa AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA). Each template must define the complete sequence of onboarding activities including HR documentation collection, IT system access provisioning, entity-specific regulatory orientation (Qatar labor law, UAE labor law, KSA Nitaqat system, India labor compliance), port-specific security clearance processing (ISPS Code requirements per port), department introduction scheduling, and role-specific training path assignment. Templates must be versioned with approval workflows and must support branching logic based on employee attributes such as nationality, visa type, role level, and department.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R002	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Pre-Joining Document Collection and Verification	The system must automate pre-joining document collection from new employees through a secure portal supporting Arabic and English interfaces. Required documents must be configurable per entity and must include national ID or passport copy, educational certificates, professional certifications, previous employer references, medical fitness certificates, and entity-specific documents such as Qatar QID, UAE Emirates ID, KSA Iqama, or India Aadhaar and PAN card. The system must perform automated document verification including expiry date validation, format compliance checks, and OCR-based data extraction for auto-populating employee master records. Document upload must support offline capability with queue-based synchronization for candidates in locations with unreliable connectivity. All documents must be stored with AES-256 encryption compliant with each jurisdictions data protection requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R003	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Multi-Department Onboarding Task Orchestration	The system must orchestrate onboarding tasks across multiple departments including HR, IT, Operations, Finance, Compliance, and port-specific security departments with automated task assignment, progress tracking, and SLA monitoring. When a new customs documentation specialist is onboarded at the Qatar entity for Hamad Port, the system must simultaneously trigger HR to prepare the employment contract in Arabic and English, IT to provision ERP access with customs module permissions, Operations to schedule yard familiarization tours, Finance to set up payroll records in QAR, Compliance to initiate Qatar Customs Authority system access application, and Security to process ISPS port access credentials. Each task must have configurable SLA thresholds with automated escalation when deadlines approach or are breached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R004	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Automated Training Path Assignment Based on Role Profile	The system must automatically assign a role-specific training path to each new employee upon onboarding workflow initiation. The training path must be derived from the competency framework defined for the employees assigned role, entity, port, and department. For a new freight commercial executive joining the UAE entity at Jebel Ali, the assigned training path must include UAE trade regulations, Jebel Ali port procedures, Mirsal 2 customs system operation, INCOTERMS 2020 application in freight quotation, multi-currency freight rate calculation (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), Bill of Lading and shipping documentation procedures, and ERP freight commercial module training. The system must calculate estimated training completion timelines and flag any prerequisite gaps requiring immediate attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R005	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Progress Dashboard and Analytics	The system must provide a comprehensive onboarding progress dashboard showing real-time status of all active onboarding workflows across all four entities. The dashboard must display individual employee onboarding completion percentages, task-level status breakdowns by responsible department, SLA compliance rates, average onboarding duration by entity and role category, and bottleneck identification highlighting departments or tasks consistently causing delays. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, department, date range (with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selectors), and onboarding status (pre-joining, in-progress, completed, on-hold). AI-generated insights must identify patterns in onboarding delays and recommend process improvements. All dashboard text must render correctly in bilingual Arabic-English format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R006	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Buddy and Mentor Assignment Automation	The system must automate the assignment of onboarding buddies and mentors to new employees based on configurable matching criteria including same department, same port location, language compatibility (Arabic, English, Hindi, Urdu), relevant experience in the new employees role area, and current mentor workload capacity. The buddy must be assigned from the same entity and port to provide day-to-day operational guidance, while the mentor may be from any entity to provide broader career development support. The system must track buddy and mentor interaction milestones including initial introduction meeting, weekly check-in completion, and 30-60-90 day review meetings. Mentors must receive structured guides and checklists specific to the mentees role, department, and port location in their preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R007	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Port Security Clearance and Access Provisioning	The system must manage port security clearance workflows specific to each port authority and ISPS Code requirements. For Hamad Port, the system must track Qatar State Security clearance application status and Hamad Port Authority access card issuance. For Jebel Ali, the system must manage DP World security vetting and Jafza free zone access permits. For JNPT, the system must track Indian port trust security clearance and CISF approved personnel lists. Each security clearance workflow must have defined stages, estimated processing times, required document checklists, and automated status follow-up reminders. The system must prevent assignment of operational tasks requiring port access until security clearance is confirmed, and must alert supervisors when clearance processing exceeds expected timelines for any jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R008	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	IT System Access Provisioning Workflow	The system must automate IT system access provisioning as part of the onboarding workflow including ERP module access based on role-based access control profiles, email account creation, port authority system credentials (Qatar Customs Al Nadeeb, Dubai Trade Mirsal 2, ZATCA FASAH, India ICEGATE), shipping line EDI portal access for carrier bookings and tracking, and communication platform accounts (Microsoft Teams or Slack). Access provisioning requests must be auto-generated based on the employees role profile and entity assignment with appropriate approval routing to IT security and department managers. The system must enforce the principle of least privilege and must integrate with competency assessment results to gate access to specialized functions such as dangerous goods booking or customs declaration filing based on verified competency levels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R009	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Checklist with Mandatory and Optional Items	The system must support onboarding checklists with both mandatory and optional items, configurable per entity, port, department, and role. Mandatory items must be enforced as workflow gates preventing progression to subsequent onboarding phases until completed. For all entities, mandatory items must include HR documentation completion, code of conduct acknowledgment in Arabic or English, safety induction completion, and ERP basic navigation training. Entity-specific mandatory items must include Qatar entity QID processing verification, UAE entity Emirates ID registration confirmation, KSA entity Iqama issuance tracking, and India entity PF and ESI registration. Optional items may include cultural orientation programs, Arabic language basics for non-Arabic speaking staff, and inter-entity networking introductions. Checklist progress must be visible to the employee, their manager, and HR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R010	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Automated Onboarding Communication Sequences	The system must execute automated communication sequences throughout the onboarding journey delivering timely information to new employees, their managers, and involved departments. Pre-joining communications must include welcome messages with entity office location details and joining instructions, document submission reminders, and IT equipment readiness confirmations. Day-one communications must include orientation schedule, buddy introduction, and first-week agenda. Progressive communications during the onboarding period must include training schedule reminders, milestone achievement acknowledgments, and feedback survey invitations. All communications must be delivered via email and in-app notifications in the employees preferred language (Arabic or English) with entity-specific branding and sender identities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R011	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Probation Period Tracking and Assessment Integration	The system must integrate onboarding workflow completion with probation period tracking and assessment. Probation periods must be configurable per entity (Qatar standard 6 months, UAE standard 6 months, KSA standard 90 days, India as per employment contract). The system must schedule automated probation review assessments at configurable milestones (30, 60, 90 days and end of probation) with assessment forms that evaluate competency development progress, training completion status, operational performance metrics, and cultural integration. Probation assessment dates must be calculated respecting entity-specific working day calendars and displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. The system must generate automated alerts to managers 14 days before each probation milestone and must track probation outcome decisions (confirmed, extended, terminated) with mandatory documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R012	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Feedback and Continuous Improvement	The system must collect structured feedback from new employees at defined intervals during and after onboarding (day 7, day 30, day 90, and end of probation) through bilingual Arabic-English survey forms. Feedback dimensions must include clarity of role expectations, effectiveness of training provided, quality of buddy and mentor support, IT system readiness, and overall onboarding experience satisfaction. The system must aggregate feedback data across entities and ports to identify systematic improvement opportunities using AI-driven sentiment analysis on free-text comments in both Arabic and English. Trending issues must be automatically flagged to HR and training administrators with recommended corrective actions. Feedback response rates must be tracked as onboarding program effectiveness KPIs per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R013	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Cross-Entity Transfer Onboarding Workflow	The system must support a specialized onboarding workflow for employees transferring between entities (e.g., Qatar to UAE or India to KSA). The transfer onboarding must recognize previously completed training and competency assessments while identifying incremental requirements specific to the new entity and port jurisdiction. For an operations supervisor transferring from Hamad Port Qatar to Jebel Ali UAE, the system must retain core shipping operations competencies, assign UAE labor law orientation, Dubai Trade customs system training, Jebel Ali specific port procedures, and UAE VAT compliance training while waiving previously completed modules on vessel operations and container handling fundamentals. The workflow must coordinate offboarding from the source entity and onboarding to the target entity with synchronized timelines and multi-currency payroll transition from QAR to AED.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R014	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Document Template Management	The system must manage a library of onboarding document templates specific to each entity including employment contracts, offer letters, non-disclosure agreements, code of conduct documents, safety declaration forms, and entity-specific regulatory acknowledgment forms. All templates must be maintained in bilingual Arabic-English versions with configurable merge fields for employee-specific data auto-population (name, position, department, port, salary in local currency QAR/AED/SAR/INR, start date in Gregorian and Hijri formats). Template version control must track all modifications with approval workflows ensuring legal review for contract templates. The system must support digital signature collection on generated documents using entity-appropriate e-signature platforms compliant with Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India electronic transaction laws.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R015	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding SLA Management and Escalation Rules	The system must enforce configurable SLA targets for each onboarding task and overall onboarding workflow completion. Default SLA targets must include IT access provisioning within 2 business days of start date, port security clearance application within 1 business day, training path assignment within 3 business days, and overall onboarding completion within 90 calendar days. SLA calculation must respect entity-specific working day calendars and public holiday schedules across all four jurisdictions. The escalation engine must support multi-level escalation rules: first level notification to task assignee at 75 percent of SLA, second level escalation to department manager at 100 percent of SLA, and third level escalation to entity HR head at 150 percent of SLA. Escalation notifications must be delivered in the recipients preferred language with full context of the delayed task.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R016	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Workflow Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a complete immutable audit trail of all onboarding workflow activities including task assignments, completions, approvals, escalations, document submissions, system access grants, and assessment results. The audit trail must record actor identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, action performed, and associated evidence documents. Compliance reports must be generatable per entity demonstrating adherence to local labor law onboarding requirements including Qatar Labor Law articles on employment contract execution timelines, UAE Ministerial Resolution requirements for employment offer documentation, KSA Nitaqat system registration deadlines, and Indian Employment Exchanges (Compulsory Notification of Vacancies) Act requirements. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual headers and entity-specific legal reference annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R017	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Workflow Automation Rules Engine	The system must provide a configurable rules engine allowing HR administrators to define automation rules for onboarding workflow behavior without requiring developer intervention. Rules must support conditional logic based on employee attributes (entity, port, department, role, nationality, visa type, language preference), temporal conditions (business days elapsed since start date, days until probation end), and status triggers (document submitted, training completed, assessment passed). Example rules must include automatic assignment of Arabic language training for non-Arabic speaking employees joining Qatar or KSA entities, automatic scheduling of dangerous goods awareness training for all operations department joiners, and automatic notification to finance department for payroll setup in the appropriate local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) within 24 hours of employment contract execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-1-004-R018	FEAT-054-1-004	MOD-054	Onboarding Cost Tracking and Analysis	The system must track and analyze onboarding costs per employee including recruitment agency fees, visa processing costs, travel and relocation expenses, training material costs, IT equipment provisioning, port security clearance fees, and allocated HR administrative time. All costs must be captured in the relevant local currency (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity) with automated conversion to USD for consolidated cross-entity reporting using configurable exchange rates from the ERP treasury module. The system must generate average cost-per-hire reports segmented by entity, port, department, and role level with trend analysis over configurable time periods. Cost variance analysis must highlight onboarding processes that exceed budgeted amounts and identify cost optimization opportunities through AI-driven recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R001	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Natural Language Query Processing in Arabic and English	The AI Knowledge Assistant shall accept and process natural language queries in both Arabic and English, including mixed-language queries commonly used in Gulf shipping operations such as 'ما هو إجراء transhipment في Jebel Ali'. The system must support Arabic morphological analysis for root-based search, handle transliterated shipping terms (e.g., 'كونتينر' for container, 'بوليصة شحن' for bill of lading), and process queries containing INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, DDP), port codes (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INJNP), and container type references (20GP, 40HC, 45HQ). Query processing latency shall not exceed 2 seconds for standard queries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R002	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Semantic Search Across Multi-Entity Knowledge Repositories	The system shall perform semantic vector-based search across all knowledge repositories spanning the four operational entities in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Search results must respect entity-level access controls such that a user in the Qatar entity cannot access KSA-restricted HR policies unless explicitly granted cross-entity access. The search index must cover SOPs, regulatory documents, circulars from MOPH Qatar, FTA Dubai, ZATCA KSA, and CBIC India, historical case files, training materials, and internal memos. Results shall be ranked by semantic relevance with a minimum precision score of 85% as measured by quarterly evaluation benchmarks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R003	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Context-Aware Response Generation with Source Citations	The AI assistant shall generate responses using retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) that cites specific source documents including document title, section number, page reference, version, and last-updated date. For regulatory queries such as Qatar Customs Law Article references or India CBIC Circular numbers, the system must provide the exact regulatory citation. Responses must include a confidence score (high/medium/low) and when confidence is below 70%, the system shall explicitly state uncertainty and recommend consulting a subject matter expert. All generated responses must be logged with the source chunks used for auditability and compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R004	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Shipping Domain Knowledge Graph Integration	The system shall maintain and query a shipping domain knowledge graph that maps relationships between entities such as ports (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INJNP), shipping lines, container types (standard dry 20GP/40GP, high cube 40HC/45HQ, reefer 20RE/40RE, flat rack, open top, tank containers), INCOTERMS 2020 rules, HS codes, trade lane corridors, and regulatory bodies. The knowledge graph must support inference queries such as 'What documentation is required for reefer containers from JNPT to Hamad Port carrying pharmaceutical goods under CIF terms?' by traversing relationship edges across regulatory, operational, and commercial nodes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R006	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Role-Based Knowledge Access and Response Filtering	The AI assistant shall enforce role-based access control on both the knowledge sources queried and the responses generated. Operations staff shall only receive operational SOPs and procedures, while compliance officers shall access regulatory and audit-related knowledge. Finance team members querying about freight rate agreements or multi-currency settlement procedures (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) shall only see finance-authorized documents. The system must support at least 15 distinct role profiles aligned with shipping ERP roles including vessel operations, container yard management, customs brokerage, freight forwarding, documentation, finance, and executive management. Access violations must be logged for security audit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R007	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	RTL Arabic User Interface for Knowledge Assistant	The AI Knowledge Assistant interface shall fully support right-to-left (RTL) layout for Arabic language users, including proper RTL rendering of the chat interface, query input fields, response text, source citation panels, and navigation elements. The UI must correctly handle bidirectional text where Arabic sentences contain embedded English terms such as port codes, vessel names, container numbers (e.g., MSCU1234567), and INCOTERMS. Date displays in responses must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats based on user preference, and numeric formatting must respect locale settings (Arabic-Indic numerals ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩ versus Western Arabic numerals).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R008	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Offline Knowledge Cache for Field Operations	The system shall support an offline knowledge cache mode for field operations personnel working at port terminals, container yards, or vessel berths where network connectivity may be intermittent or unavailable. The offline cache must include the most recent versions of critical SOPs, emergency procedures, dangerous goods handling guides (IMDG Code references), and port-specific safety protocols for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The cache shall be synchronized automatically when connectivity is restored, with conflict resolution for any updates made during the offline period. Maximum offline cache size shall be configurable per device with a default of 2 GB.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R009	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Query Analytics and Knowledge Gap Identification	The system shall track and analyze all queries submitted to the AI assistant, categorizing them by topic, entity, user role, language, and resolution status (answered satisfactorily, partially answered, unanswered). The analytics engine must identify knowledge gaps where queries consistently receive low-confidence responses or no matching sources, generating weekly reports recommending new knowledge articles to be authored. Reports must be segmented by operational entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and by functional domain (customs, operations, finance, compliance). Trend analysis shall detect emerging query patterns that may indicate new regulatory requirements or operational challenges.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R010	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Document Ingestion Pipeline for Knowledge Base Updates	The system shall provide an automated document ingestion pipeline that processes new documents into the knowledge base from multiple sources including uploaded PDFs, scanned documents with OCR (supporting both Arabic and English text), emails from regulatory bodies, and API feeds from government portals such as Qatar Single Window, Dubai Trade, ZATCA Fasah, and ICEGATE India. Ingested documents must be automatically classified by type (regulation, SOP, circular, guideline, training material), tagged with relevant metadata (port, entity, effective date, expiry date), chunked for vector embedding, and made searchable within 15 minutes of ingestion. Duplicate detection must prevent redundant entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R011	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Expert Escalation Workflow from AI Assistant	When the AI assistant cannot provide a satisfactory answer or the user explicitly requests human assistance, the system shall initiate an expert escalation workflow. The escalation must capture the full conversation context, identified topic area, and route the query to the appropriate subject matter expert based on a configurable routing matrix (e.g., customs queries for Qatar route to Qatar customs SME, DG cargo queries route to IMDG specialist). The SME must receive the conversation transcript and relevant source documents. Response from the SME shall be captured and fed back into the knowledge base to improve future AI responses. Escalation SLA tracking must enforce response within 4 business hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R012	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Multi-Format Response Rendering with Visual Aids	The AI assistant shall render responses in multiple formats depending on content type, including formatted text with markdown, tabular data for comparative information (e.g., comparing documentation requirements across ports), flowcharts for procedural SOPs (e.g., customs clearance workflow at JNPT), and embedded images or diagrams from source documents. When a query relates to physical processes such as container inspection procedures or reefer plug-in sequences, the system shall link to relevant video training content from the Video Training Library (FEAT-054-2-004). Response rendering must adapt to both desktop and tablet form factors used in port terminal offices and mobile devices in the field.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R013	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Feedback Loop and Response Quality Improvement	The system shall provide a feedback mechanism on every AI-generated response allowing users to rate accuracy (thumbs up/down), flag incorrect information, and submit corrections. Flagged responses shall be queued for review by knowledge administrators who can update source documents, adjust search relevance weights, or add new training examples. The AI model fine-tuning pipeline must incorporate validated feedback on a weekly cycle. Quality metrics including user satisfaction rate, first-response accuracy, and escalation rate must be tracked per entity and per knowledge domain. A minimum user satisfaction threshold of 80% must trigger alerts to knowledge management administrators when breached.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R014	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Proactive Knowledge Suggestions Based on User Activity	The AI assistant shall proactively suggest relevant knowledge articles, SOPs, and regulatory updates based on the user's current operational context within the ERP system. For example, when a user is processing a bill of lading for dangerous goods shipment from JNPT to Hamad Port, the system shall proactively surface the latest IMDG Code amendments, Qatar MOPH import requirements for the specific UN number, and the relevant SOP for DG container handling at destination. Proactive suggestions must be non-intrusive, appearing as a sidebar panel or notification badge, and must be dismissible. Suggestion relevance shall be tracked and algorithms refined based on user engagement metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R015	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Multi-Language Glossary and Terminology Standardization	The system shall maintain a comprehensive bilingual (Arabic/English) glossary of container shipping terminology, port-specific terms, regulatory vocabulary, and organizational jargon used across all four operational entities. The glossary must map equivalent terms used differently across regions (e.g., 'customs duty' versus 'رسوم جمركية', 'CFS' versus 'محطة تجميع الحاويات') and standardize terminology in AI responses. The glossary shall support INCOTERMS 2020 definitions, HS code descriptions, container type specifications per ISO 6346, and port facility terminology per ISPS Code. Glossary entries must be searchable independently and used by the AI to normalize query interpretation across language variants.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-001-R016	FEAT-054-2-001	MOD-054	Audit Trail for AI Knowledge Interactions	The system shall maintain a comprehensive, tamper-proof audit trail of all AI knowledge assistant interactions including the original query, processed query interpretation, source documents retrieved, generated response, confidence score, user feedback, and any escalation actions. The audit trail must comply with data retention policies for each entity jurisdiction: Qatar (minimum 7 years per QCB regulations), UAE (minimum 5 years per FTA requirements), KSA (minimum 10 years per ZATCA requirements), and India (minimum 8 years per CBIC retention rules). Audit records must be exportable in CSV and PDF formats and accessible to compliance officers through a dedicated audit review interface with filtering by date range, user, entity, and topic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R001	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Automated Regulatory Feed Monitoring and Ingestion	The system shall continuously monitor and ingest regulatory updates from official government and regulatory body sources including Qatar General Authority of Customs, Qatar MOPH, Dubai Customs, Federal Tax Authority UAE, Abu Dhabi Customs, Saudi ZATCA, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani), India CBIC, Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) India, and International Maritime Organization (IMO). Monitoring shall cover official gazette publications, circulars, public notices, tariff amendments, and trade advisories. The system must support RSS feeds, API integrations, email subscription parsing, and web scraping of designated government portal pages. New updates must be detected and ingested within 1 hour of publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R002	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	AI-Powered Regulatory Change Classification and Tagging	Upon ingestion of a new regulatory update, the AI engine shall automatically classify the update by type (tariff change, procedural amendment, new requirement, exemption, deadline extension, prohibition), affected domain (customs clearance, port operations, dangerous goods, food safety, pharmaceutical imports, vehicle imports, trade sanctions), affected ports (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INJNP), affected container types (dry, reefer, DG, OOG), and applicable INCOTERMS. Classification accuracy must exceed 90% as validated by quarterly human review. Misclassified updates flagged by users must be corrected and used to retrain the classification model.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R003	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Impact Assessment Engine for Regulatory Changes	The system shall perform automated impact assessment for each regulatory update, analyzing its effect on current operations including active shipments in transit, pending customs declarations, existing contracts with INCOTERMS pricing implications, warehouse procedures, and documentation templates. The impact assessment must quantify affected shipments by count and value (in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR as applicable), identify specific customers and trade lanes impacted, and estimate compliance effort required. For tariff changes, the system must calculate the financial impact on pending and future shipments referencing the affected HS codes. Impact severity shall be rated as critical, high, medium, or low based on configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R004	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Multi-Channel Alert Distribution with Priority Routing	Regulatory update alerts shall be distributed through multiple channels including in-app notifications, email, SMS, and integration with Microsoft Teams or Slack. Alert routing must be configurable based on the update classification, affected entity, user role, and impact severity. Critical alerts (e.g., immediate trade sanctions, port closures, new prohibited goods lists) must trigger immediate push notifications to all affected department heads and compliance officers across relevant entities. High-priority alerts must be delivered within 30 minutes. Standard alerts shall be batched into daily digests. Users must be able to configure their alert preferences including channel, frequency, and topic filters while mandatory compliance alerts cannot be disabled.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R005	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Update Bilingual Content Presentation	All regulatory update alerts and their detailed content shall be presented in both Arabic and English. When the source regulatory document is published only in Arabic (common for Qatar, KSA, and UAE authorities), the system shall provide an AI-generated English translation with a disclaimer noting it is a machine translation and linking to the official Arabic source. Similarly, when Indian CBIC or DGFT updates are in English only, an Arabic summary shall be generated for Arabic-speaking users. The bilingual presentation must support RTL layout for Arabic content, Hijri calendar dates alongside Gregorian dates for effective and compliance deadline dates, and proper rendering of bilingual tables comparing old versus new regulatory provisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R006	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Compliance Action Item Generation and Tracking	For each regulatory update assessed as having operational impact, the system shall automatically generate specific compliance action items assigned to responsible departments and individuals. Action items must include a clear description of what needs to change, the regulatory reference, compliance deadline (in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), responsible entity and department, and priority level. For example, a ZATCA circular changing the HS code classification for specific petrochemical products must generate action items for the customs brokerage team to update declaration templates, the finance team to adjust duty rate calculations, and the documentation team to update commodity descriptions. Action item completion must be tracked with escalation for overdue items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R007	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Calendar with Compliance Deadlines	The system shall maintain a regulatory compliance calendar displaying all upcoming compliance deadlines, regulatory effective dates, transition periods, and recurring filing obligations across all four jurisdictions. The calendar must support both Gregorian and Hijri date views with the ability to toggle between them. Calendar entries must be filterable by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), regulatory body, domain, and impact severity. Automated reminders shall be sent at configurable intervals before deadlines (default: 30 days, 14 days, 7 days, 3 days, and 1 day). The calendar shall highlight conflicts where deadlines from different jurisdictions overlap or where a single shipment is affected by regulatory changes in both origin and destination countries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R008	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Historical Regulatory Change Repository and Search	The system shall maintain a searchable repository of all historical regulatory updates organized chronologically and by jurisdiction, with the ability to view the regulatory landscape as it existed at any point in time. This is critical for dispute resolution and customs audit responses where the applicable regulations at the time of a specific shipment's customs declaration must be referenced. The repository must store the original regulatory document, AI-generated summary, classification metadata, impact assessment, and compliance actions taken. Search functionality must support queries by date range, regulatory body, HS code, port, container type, and free text in both Arabic and English. Retention period must comply with the longest applicable jurisdiction requirement of 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R009	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Cross-Jurisdictional Regulatory Conflict Detection	The system shall automatically detect potential conflicts or inconsistencies between regulatory requirements across the four operational jurisdictions that may affect cross-border container shipping operations. For example, if Qatar introduces a new phytosanitary certificate requirement for agricultural imports while India's APEDA export regulations do not mandate the corresponding certificate at origin, the system must flag this gap and generate an advisory. Similarly, if UAE and KSA have conflicting requirements for transit cargo documentation on the same trade corridor, the conflict must be highlighted. Detection must cover documentation requirements, prohibited and restricted goods lists, packaging and labeling standards, and customs valuation methodologies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R010	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Update Impact on Active Bookings and BLs	When a regulatory update is assessed as impacting current operations, the system shall cross-reference the update against all active bookings, in-transit shipments, and pending bills of lading to identify specifically affected consignments. For tariff changes affecting specific HS codes, the system must list all active shipments carrying goods under those codes with their current status, ETA at affected ports, and estimated financial impact in the relevant transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). For documentation requirement changes, the system must identify shipments that may need amended documentation before arrival. Affected parties including shippers, consignees, and customs brokers shall be automatically notified with specific guidance on required actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R011	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Stakeholder Acknowledgment and Read Receipt Tracking	For critical and high-priority regulatory alerts, the system shall require explicit acknowledgment from designated stakeholders confirming they have read and understood the update. Acknowledgment must be tracked per individual with timestamp, and unacknowledged alerts shall trigger escalation to supervisors after a configurable period (default: 24 hours for critical, 72 hours for high-priority). The acknowledgment dashboard must display compliance rates by entity, department, and individual. For updates requiring training or procedure changes, acknowledgment alone is insufficient; the system must also track completion of associated training modules or SOP update confirmations. Monthly compliance reports on acknowledgment rates must be generated for each entity's management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R012	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Automated SOP and Template Update Recommendations	When a regulatory update necessitates changes to existing standard operating procedures or document templates, the system shall automatically identify the affected SOPs and templates and generate specific update recommendations. For example, if Dubai Customs issues a new circular requiring additional fields on import declarations, the system must identify the relevant customs declaration SOP and all associated templates, highlight the sections requiring modification, and generate draft updated language referencing the new circular number and effective date. Recommendations must be routed to the document owner for review and approval. The system shall track the time between regulatory publication and SOP/template update completion as a compliance KPI.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R013	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Update Integration with ERP Workflow Engine	The system shall integrate regulatory updates with the ERP workflow engine to automatically trigger process modifications when applicable. For example, if a new regulation requires an additional approval step for importing certain goods into KSA, the system must be capable of proposing a workflow modification to the customs clearance process for that goods category at Saudi ports. Integration must support automatic updates to validation rules (e.g., new mandatory document types), tariff rate tables, prohibited goods screening lists, and documentation checklists. All automated workflow modifications must go through an approval process by authorized administrators before activation to prevent operational disruption. Changes must be version-controlled and reversible.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R014	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Intelligence Dashboard and Analytics	The system shall provide a regulatory intelligence dashboard displaying key metrics including total updates received per period segmented by jurisdiction and domain, compliance action completion rates, average time-to-compliance, overdue action items, upcoming deadlines, and regulatory change velocity trends. The dashboard must support drill-down from summary metrics to individual updates and action items. Comparative views shall show regulatory activity across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India to identify which jurisdictions are most actively changing requirements. Predictive analytics shall forecast anticipated regulatory workload based on historical patterns and announced consultation periods. The dashboard must be accessible in both Arabic and English with RTL-compatible chart rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R015	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Offline Regulatory Alert Queue and Synchronization	The system shall support offline queuing of regulatory alerts for users operating in environments with intermittent connectivity, such as field inspectors at port terminals or remote customs clearance offices. When connectivity is unavailable, alerts shall be queued locally and delivered immediately upon reconnection with clear indicators showing which alerts were delayed and by how long. Users must be able to view previously downloaded regulatory updates offline, including the full document text, AI summary, and impact assessment. Acknowledgment actions taken offline must be timestamped locally and synchronized when connectivity resumes. The offline queue must prioritize critical alerts for first delivery upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R016	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Third-Party Regulatory Advisory Service Integration	The system shall support integration with third-party regulatory advisory services and legal databases commonly used in GCC and Indian maritime trade, such as Lexis Nexis Middle East, Gulf Customs Union regulatory feeds, World Customs Organization Harmonized System updates, and Indian Trade Portal notifications. Integration shall be via configurable API connectors or structured email parsing. Third-party content must be clearly distinguished from official government sources in the UI. The system must reconcile third-party advisory interpretations with official regulatory text and flag discrepancies for compliance officer review. Subscription management for third-party services must support cost tracking in multiple currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) allocated across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-002-R017	FEAT-054-2-002	MOD-054	Regulatory Update Version Control and Amendment Tracking	The system shall maintain full version control for regulatory documents, tracking all amendments, supersessions, and revocations over time. When a regulation is amended, the system must clearly display a diff view showing what changed between versions with additions highlighted in green and deletions in red, supporting both Arabic and English text comparison. Superseded regulations must be clearly marked as historical but remain accessible for reference. Chain-of-amendment tracking must show the complete lineage of a regulation from original issuance through all subsequent modifications. Cross-references between related regulations across jurisdictions must be maintained, for example linking Qatar customs tariff amendments to the corresponding GCC Common External Tariff changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R001	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Structured Lesson Capture with Shipping Context Fields	The system shall provide a structured lesson capture form that includes mandatory fields for incident category (customs delay, container damage, documentation error, port congestion, regulatory non-compliance, safety incident, financial loss, customer complaint), affected entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), affected port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), affected trade lane, container type involved (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RE, 40RE, OOG, tank), shipping line, root cause classification (human error, system failure, regulatory gap, communication breakdown, weather/force majeure), financial impact in transaction currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), and corrective/preventive actions taken. Each field must include tooltips with examples in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R002	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	AI-Assisted Root Cause Analysis and Classification	The system shall provide AI-assisted root cause analysis that guides users through a structured analysis process using techniques such as 5-Why analysis and fishbone diagrams adapted for container shipping contexts. When a user describes an incident (e.g., 'reefer container temperature excursion during transit from JNPT to Hamad Port'), the AI shall suggest potential root causes based on similar historical incidents, prompt for additional investigation data, and recommend a classification from the standardized root cause taxonomy. The AI must consider multi-factor causes common in international shipping such as handover gaps between shipping lines at transshipment ports, documentation mismatches between origin and destination country requirements, and communication delays across time zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R003	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Cross-Entity Lesson Dissemination and Relevance Matching	When a lesson is captured in one entity, the system shall automatically assess its relevance to other operational entities and disseminate accordingly. Relevance assessment must consider operational similarity (e.g., a customs documentation lesson from Dubai Customs is highly relevant to Qatar Customs given GCC alignment), trade lane overlap (e.g., lessons from the India-UAE corridor are relevant to India-Qatar operations), container type applicability, and regulatory framework similarity. Disseminated lessons must be translated to the recipient entity's primary language if different from the source. The system must track which entities have reviewed cross-entity lessons and report on knowledge transfer effectiveness. Sensitive lessons (e.g., involving specific customer disputes or financial losses) must support restricted dissemination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R004	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Automated Pattern Detection Across Lessons Repository	The AI engine shall continuously analyze the lessons learned repository to detect recurring patterns and systemic issues that may not be apparent from individual incidents. Pattern detection must operate across dimensions including time (increasing frequency of a specific issue type), geography (concentration of incidents at a particular port or on a specific trade lane), operational function (recurring documentation errors in a specific department), container type (higher damage rates for specific equipment types), and customer (repeated issues with specific shippers or consignees). When a pattern is detected meeting configurable thresholds (default: 3 related incidents within 90 days), the system shall generate an automatic pattern alert to the relevant entity's operations manager and quality assurance team with a consolidated analysis and recommended systemic corrective actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R005	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Corrective Action Tracking with Effectiveness Measurement	Each lesson learned must have associated corrective and preventive actions (CAPA) with assigned owners, target completion dates (displayed in both Gregorian and Hijri formats), and status tracking through stages of proposed, approved, in-progress, completed, and verified-effective. The system shall measure corrective action effectiveness by monitoring recurrence of similar incidents after implementation, tracking KPIs specified in the CAPA plan, and requiring a formal effectiveness review 90 days after CAPA completion. Overdue CAPAs must trigger escalation notifications following a configurable escalation matrix. The system shall generate monthly CAPA status reports per entity showing completion rates, overdue items, and effectiveness scores. Financial impact of corrective actions must be tracked in the relevant entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R006	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Lesson Approval and Quality Review Workflow	Submitted lessons shall go through a configurable approval workflow before publication to the general repository. The workflow must support multi-level review including departmental review (by the department manager of the submitting team), quality assurance review (by the QA team to ensure root cause analysis rigor and CAPA adequacy), and optional legal review (for lessons involving regulatory violations, customer disputes, or potential liability). Reviewers must be able to request additional information, return for revision, approve with modifications, or reject with justification. The workflow must support delegation and auto-escalation for reviewer absence. Average approval cycle time shall be tracked as a KPI with a target of 5 business days for standard lessons and 2 business days for critical safety-related lessons.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R007	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Searchable Repository with Advanced Filtering	The lessons learned repository shall support full-text search in both Arabic and English with advanced filtering by all structured fields including incident category, entity, port, trade lane, container type, root cause classification, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), financial impact range, severity level, and CAPA status. Search results must display a relevance-ranked list with AI-generated summaries highlighting why each result matches the query. The system must support saved searches and configurable alerts for new lessons matching saved criteria. Search analytics must track which lessons are most frequently accessed, enabling identification of high-value knowledge assets. Boolean search operators and wildcard patterns must be supported for advanced users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R008	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Integration with Incident Management and Claims Systems	The lessons learned repository shall integrate bidirectionally with the ERP's incident management module and cargo claims processing system. When an incident is resolved or a claim is settled, the system shall automatically prompt the responsible team to capture a lesson learned, pre-populating available fields from the incident or claim record including container numbers, booking references, shipping line, port, financial impact (in the claim currency: USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR), and timeline. Conversely, when a new lesson is captured, the system shall automatically link it to related incidents and claims for cross-reference. This integration must ensure that high-value claims (exceeding configurable thresholds per entity) always result in documented lessons learned.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R009	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Gamification and Incentive Framework for Knowledge Sharing	The system shall implement a gamification framework to incentivize active participation in the lessons learned process across all entities. Users shall earn points for submitting lessons (with higher points for critical lessons), reviewing and rating lessons, implementing CAPAs on time, and contributing follow-up observations. A leaderboard shall display top contributors by entity, department, and overall organization. Monthly and quarterly recognition badges shall be awarded for consistent participation. The system must track participation rates by entity and department, with low-participation areas receiving targeted prompts and manager notifications. The gamification framework must be culturally appropriate for the GCC and Indian context, avoiding elements that may conflict with local workplace norms.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R010	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Confidential and Anonymous Lesson Submission	The system shall support confidential and anonymous lesson submission modes to encourage reporting of sensitive issues such as near-misses, safety violations, regulatory non-compliance, or process failures that staff may hesitate to report under their name. Confidential submissions are visible to designated reviewers who know the submitter's identity but it is not published broadly. Anonymous submissions fully conceal the submitter's identity from all users including administrators, with a secure anonymous communication channel for reviewers to request additional information. The system must ensure anonymization is robust, stripping metadata that could identify the submitter including IP address, device ID, and writing style analysis. Anonymous lessons shall follow the same quality review workflow with accommodations for reviewer-submitter communication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R011	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Proactive Lesson Recommendations in Operational Context	The system shall proactively recommend relevant lessons learned to users based on their current operational activities within the ERP. For example, when a user is processing a booking for a reefer container shipment of pharmaceuticals from JNPT to Hamad Port, the system shall surface lessons related to pharmaceutical cold chain failures on similar trade lanes, documentation issues with Qatar MOPH drug import permits, and temperature monitoring best practices for the specific voyage duration. Recommendations must appear as non-intrusive contextual panels within the relevant ERP screens. The relevance algorithm must consider trade lane, cargo type, container type, involved ports, season (e.g., summer heat-related lessons during Gulf summer months), and the user's historical interaction patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R012	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Periodic Lessons Learned Review Meetings Support	The system shall support periodic lessons learned review meetings by generating automated agenda packages that include new lessons since the last review, outstanding CAPA items, pattern analysis findings, cross-entity knowledge transfer status, and participation metrics. Meeting frequency shall be configurable per entity (default: monthly) with the system sending calendar invitations to designated participants. The meeting support module must provide a presentation-ready format with charts and summaries viewable in both Arabic and English. Action items from review meetings must be captured directly in the system with assignment and tracking. Meeting minutes shall be auto-generated from structured notes taken during the session and published to all attendees and relevant stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R013	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Regulatory and Audit Compliance Linkage	Lessons learned involving regulatory non-compliance or audit findings must be tagged with the specific regulation, audit standard, or compliance requirement they relate to (e.g., ISPS Code Section 14, IMDG Code Chapter 7.4, Qatar Customs Law Article 93, ZATCA Executive Regulations Article 45). The system shall maintain a compliance heat map showing which regulations or standards have the most frequent associated lessons, indicating areas of persistent compliance challenge. When regulatory updates are received through the Regulatory Update Alert Management feature (FEAT-054-2-002), the system shall automatically cross-reference against existing lessons learned to identify whether previous incidents are affected by the new regulation. This linkage supports audit readiness by providing evidence of continuous improvement in compliance areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R014	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Multimedia Attachment and Evidence Management	Each lesson learned entry must support multimedia attachments including photographs (container damage, improper stowage, labeling defects), scanned documents (customs rejection notices, survey reports, regulatory correspondence), video clips (CCTV footage of operational incidents, process demonstrations), and audio recordings (interview notes from root cause investigations). The system must support file sizes up to 100 MB per attachment with a total limit of 500 MB per lesson entry. Attachments must be indexed for search where applicable (OCR for scanned documents, speech-to-text for audio in Arabic and English). Sensitive attachments such as customer correspondence or legal documents must support restricted access controls independent of the lesson's general visibility. Storage must be managed with configurable retention policies per entity jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R015	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Knowledge Base Article Generation from Lessons	The system shall support the transformation of validated lessons learned into formal knowledge base articles accessible through the AI Knowledge Assistant (FEAT-054-2-001). When a lesson has been verified as effective and its CAPA implemented successfully, the knowledge administrator shall be able to initiate an article generation workflow that uses AI to draft a structured knowledge article from the lesson content, including background, key findings, recommended practices, and references. The generated article must be reviewed and approved before publication to the knowledge base. The system must maintain the linkage between the original lesson and the derived knowledge article, ensuring that if the lesson is updated or superseded, the knowledge article is flagged for review. Article generation must support output in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-003-R016	FEAT-054-2-003	MOD-054	Offline Lesson Capture for Field Operations	The system shall support offline lesson capture for field operations personnel at port terminals, container yards, and vessel berths where network connectivity may be unavailable. The offline capture form must include all mandatory and optional fields available in the online version, with local storage of in-progress entries and draft attachments including photographs taken with the device camera. Upon connectivity restoration, offline entries must synchronize automatically with the central repository, handling conflicts where the same incident may have been reported by multiple field personnel. The offline mode must support Arabic text input with proper RTL rendering and must function on both tablet and mobile devices commonly used in port operations environments. Offline drafts must be protected against accidental data loss.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R001	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Video Content Upload and Metadata Management	The system shall support video content upload in standard formats including MP4, MOV, AVI, and WebM with file sizes up to 4 GB per video. Each video must be tagged with comprehensive metadata including title (Arabic and English), description (Arabic and English), category (operational procedure, safety training, regulatory compliance, system tutorial, soft skills), applicable entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India, or All), applicable port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, or All), target roles (configurable list matching ERP role definitions), difficulty level (beginner, intermediate, advanced), duration, language (Arabic, English, Hindi, Urdu), and related modules. The system must support bulk upload with CSV-based metadata import and automatic video transcoding to adaptive bitrate formats for streaming.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R002	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Automatic Video Transcription and Bilingual Subtitles	The system shall automatically generate transcriptions for all uploaded videos using AI speech-to-text technology supporting Arabic (Gulf dialect and Modern Standard Arabic), English (Indian English and British English accents common in the region), Hindi, and Urdu. Transcriptions must be time-stamped at the sentence level for subtitle synchronization. The system shall automatically generate translated subtitles: Arabic videos must receive English subtitles and vice versa. Transcription accuracy must exceed 90% for clear audio and 80% for operational environment recordings with background noise (port terminal, container yard). Human reviewers must be able to edit auto-generated transcriptions and translations before publication. Transcriptions must be indexed for full-text search enabling users to find specific content within videos by keyword.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R003	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	AI-Powered Video Content Search and Discovery	The system shall provide AI-powered search across the video library that searches not only metadata tags but also video transcriptions, enabling users to find specific segments within videos. Search queries in both Arabic and English must return results showing the specific timestamp where the relevant content appears, with a preview snippet of the transcription. For example, searching for 'إجراءات تفتيش حاويات المواد الخطرة في ميناء حمد' (dangerous goods container inspection procedures at Hamad Port) must return matching video segments with direct playback links starting at the relevant timestamp. Search must support filtering by all metadata fields, duration range, creation date, and completion status (watched/unwatched). Related video suggestions must appear alongside search results based on content similarity analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R004	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Role-Based Learning Path Configuration	The system shall support configuration of role-based learning paths that define mandatory and recommended video training sequences for each operational role within the container shipping ERP. Learning paths must be configurable per entity to accommodate local regulatory training requirements: Qatar operations staff may require MOPH food safety handling training, UAE staff may require FTA compliance modules, KSA staff may require ZATCA e-invoicing training, and India staff may require CBIC GST compliance training. Each learning path must define prerequisite relationships between videos (e.g., basic customs clearance must be completed before advanced tariff classification), estimated completion time, certification requirements, and renewal frequency. Learning paths must support branching based on assessment results, directing learners to remedial content when needed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R005	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Interactive Video Assessment and Competency Verification	The system shall support embedding interactive assessments within or after training videos to verify learner comprehension and competency. Assessment types must include multiple-choice questions, drag-and-drop exercises (e.g., matching container types to appropriate cargo), scenario-based simulations (e.g., identifying the correct customs documentation for a specific shipment scenario from JNPT to Jebel Ali), and practical task verification (e.g., confirming the learner has correctly completed a system workflow in the ERP sandbox). Questions must be presented in the learner's preferred language (Arabic or English) with shipping-specific context. Passing scores must be configurable per assessment (default 80%). Failed assessments must allow retry after a configurable cooling period with randomized question selection from a question bank.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R006	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Training Progress Tracking and Compliance Reporting	The system shall track individual learner progress across all assigned video training including completion percentage per video (based on actual watched segments, not just fast-forwarding), assessment scores, learning path completion status, and certification dates. Compliance reporting must show training completion rates by entity, department, role, and individual against mandatory training requirements. Reports must identify overdue training with escalation to supervisors for learners more than 14 days past deadline. Regulatory training compliance must be reported separately for each jurisdiction's requirements. Training completion data must be exportable for external audit purposes in both English and Arabic report formats. The system must support Hijri date formatting in all date fields within training reports for GCC entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R007	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Adaptive Bitrate Streaming and Low-Bandwidth Optimization	The system shall deliver video content using adaptive bitrate streaming that adjusts quality based on the viewer's available bandwidth, supporting resolutions from 360p to 1080p. This is essential for users at port terminals, container yards, and remote customs clearance offices in the GCC and India where bandwidth may be limited or variable. Videos must be encoded in multiple quality levels with seamless switching during playback. A progressive download option must be available for offline viewing, with content downloaded during periods of good connectivity. The video player must support resume playback from the last watched position across devices and sessions. Bandwidth usage must be monitored and reported to IT administrators per entity to manage network resource allocation at each operational location.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R008	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Video Content Versioning and Update Management	The system shall support video content versioning to manage updates when procedures, regulations, or systems change. When a new version of a training video is published, the system must automatically identify all learners who completed the previous version and determine whether recertification is required based on the nature of changes (minor update versus major revision). For major revisions, affected learners must be notified and the new version added to their learning path with a compliance deadline. Previous versions must be archived but accessible for reference, with clear labeling indicating superseded status. Version change logs must document what changed between versions. The system must track whether learners have viewed the updated content, distinguishing between those certified on current versus previous versions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R009	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Mobile-Responsive Video Player with RTL Support	The video player interface shall be fully responsive across desktop, tablet, and mobile devices commonly used in shipping operations environments. The player must support RTL layout for Arabic-language interfaces including right-aligned playback controls, RTL subtitle positioning, and Arabic chapter navigation. Player controls must include standard playback functions (play, pause, seek, volume, fullscreen, speed control from 0.5x to 2x), subtitle toggle with language selection, quality selection for bandwidth management, and picture-in-picture mode for users who need to reference the ERP system while watching training content. The player must support accessibility features including keyboard navigation, screen reader compatibility, and closed caption formatting compliant with WCAG 2.1 Level AA standards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R010	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Training Calendar and Scheduling Integration	The system shall integrate with the organizational calendar to schedule live training sessions, webinars, and training video deadlines. Scheduled sessions must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar views. The system must manage scheduling across the four entities' time zones (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India) with automatic time zone conversion for cross-entity training sessions. Calendar entries for mandatory training must include reminders at configurable intervals. The system must detect scheduling conflicts with operational peak periods (e.g., avoiding major training deadlines during Ramadan, Diwali, or peak shipping seasons) and recommend alternative scheduling. Integration with Microsoft Teams or Zoom shall support automatic meeting link generation for live webinar sessions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R011	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Video Analytics and Engagement Metrics	The system shall collect and analyze detailed video engagement metrics including play count, average watch time, completion rate, drop-off points (specific timestamps where viewers stop watching), replay frequency for specific segments, assessment pass/fail rates per question, and learner feedback ratings. Analytics must be segmented by entity, department, role, video category, and language preference. The system must identify videos with high drop-off rates and flag them for content quality review. Heatmap visualizations shall show which video segments receive the most and least engagement, informing content creators about effective and ineffective sections. Engagement data must be correlated with assessment performance to identify whether specific content gaps in videos correlate with knowledge gaps revealed in assessments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R012	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Content Creation Toolkit for Internal Subject Matter Experts	The system shall provide a lightweight content creation toolkit enabling internal subject matter experts to produce training videos without specialized video production skills. The toolkit must include screen recording capability (for system procedure demonstrations), webcam recording with basic editing (trim, cut, merge, add text overlays), template-based video creation using pre-designed slides with voiceover recording, and automated chapter marking based on content structure. The toolkit must support recording in environments typical of shipping operations including office settings, port terminals, and container yards. Output must be automatically formatted and transcoded for the video library with metadata fields pre-populated from the creator's profile. Arabic and English text overlays must render correctly in both LTR and RTL directions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R013	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Certification and Digital Badge Management	The system shall issue digital certificates and badges upon successful completion of learning paths and individual certification assessments. Certificates must include the learner's name (in both Arabic and English), certification title, completion date (Gregorian and Hijri), issuing entity, validity period, and a unique verification code. Certificates must be generated in PDF format with entity-specific branding and must be verifiable through a public verification URL. The system must track certification expiry and automatically enroll learners in recertification learning paths before expiry (configurable lead time, default 30 days). Certification records must be accessible to HR systems for compliance reporting. Badges earned must be displayable on the user's ERP profile. Certification costs, where applicable, must be tracked per entity in the relevant base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R014	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	External Training Content Integration	The system shall support integration of external training content from industry bodies and regulatory authorities including IMO e-learning modules, IATA dangerous goods training courses, port authority safety induction videos from Mwani Qatar, DP World training content, Mawani Saudi Arabia safety modules, and JNPT operational training content. Integration must support SCORM 1.2 and SCORM 2004 standards for course packaging, xAPI (Tin Can) for activity tracking, and direct video URL embedding with SSO authentication where supported. External content must be seamlessly incorporated into internal learning paths with unified progress tracking. Completion data from external platforms must be synchronized to the internal training record. License and subscription costs for external content must be managed per entity with allocation tracking across currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R015	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	AI-Generated Video Summaries and Key Takeaways	The system shall use AI to automatically generate concise text summaries and key takeaway bullet points for each training video based on the transcription content. Summaries must be generated in both Arabic and English regardless of the video's original language. The summary must capture the main topics covered, key procedures or rules explained, critical safety points, and any regulatory references mentioned. Summaries shall be displayed on the video detail page and shall be searchable through the AI Knowledge Assistant (FEAT-054-2-001). For lengthy training videos exceeding 30 minutes, chapter-level summaries must also be generated allowing learners to quickly identify which sections are most relevant to their immediate needs. Summary quality must be reviewed by content owners before publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R016	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Mandatory Safety Training Enforcement for Port Operations	The system shall enforce mandatory safety training requirements for all personnel involved in port operations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Safety training categories must include ISPS Code awareness, dangerous goods handling (IMDG Code compliance), container lashing and securing procedures, reefer container handling and monitoring, heavy lift and out-of-gauge cargo operations, and emergency response procedures. The system must prevent users from accessing certain ERP functions related to physical port operations unless their mandatory safety training is current. For example, a user with expired DG handling certification must be blocked from creating bookings for IMO class cargo until recertification is completed. Enforcement exceptions must require supervisor override with documented justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-054-2-004-R017	FEAT-054-2-004	MOD-054	Offline Video Download and Playback Management	The system shall support controlled offline download of training videos for playback in environments without reliable internet connectivity, such as vessel berths, remote terminal areas, and during travel between operational sites. Downloads must be DRM-protected to prevent unauthorized redistribution, with offline access expiring after a configurable period (default 30 days) requiring re-authentication. Downloaded content must track playback progress locally and synchronize completion data when connectivity is restored. The download manager must allow users to queue multiple videos for download during high-bandwidth periods, with configurable quality settings to manage device storage (low quality approximately 150 MB/hour, medium 400 MB/hour, high 800 MB/hour). Storage usage per device must be monitorable by IT administrators. Downloaded videos must support offline subtitle display in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R001	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Multi-Entity Project Plan Creation	The system must allow creation of hierarchical project plans spanning multiple legal entities across Qatar (Hamad Port QAHMD), UAE (Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA), KSA, and India (JNPT INJNP). Each entity-specific sub-plan must support localized configurations including currency denomination in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD for budget tracking. The plan must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar date systems for milestone scheduling, with automatic date conversion between the two calendars. Entity-level permissions must restrict visibility so that local project managers only see their entity's plan while global program managers see the consolidated view across all entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R002	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Work Breakdown Structure Management	The system must support creation and management of a work breakdown structure (WBS) with at least ten levels of hierarchy, covering all ERP modules including vessel scheduling, container tracking, customs clearance, freight billing, and port operations. Each WBS element must carry attributes for responsible entity, assigned team, estimated effort in person-days, actual effort logged, start and finish dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and budget allocation in the entity's local currency. The WBS must allow drag-and-drop reordering in the UI with full support for Arabic RTL layout rendering for Arabic-speaking team members at Hamad Port and KSA locations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R003	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Critical Path Analysis and Dependency Tracking	The system must compute and display the critical path across all implementation tasks using standard CPM (Critical Path Method) algorithms, identifying the longest sequence of dependent activities that determines the minimum project duration. Dependencies must support finish-to-start, start-to-start, finish-to-finish, and start-to-finish relationships with configurable lead and lag times. Cross-entity dependencies must be explicitly modeled, such as when JNPT India data migration depends on completion of master data harmonization at Jebel Ali. The critical path visualization must update in real time as task statuses change, and the system must send automated alerts when a critical path task is delayed beyond its baseline schedule by more than two business days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R004	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Milestone Definition with Shipping-Specific Templates	The system must provide pre-configured milestone templates specific to container shipping ERP implementations, including milestones for port interface testing with terminal operating systems (TOS), customs integration certification with local authorities (Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi ZATCA, Indian ICEGATE), INTTRA and COPARN messaging validation, container tracking system commissioning, and bill of lading workflow activation. Each milestone must support assignment to one or more entities, acceptance criteria definition, evidence attachment for sign-off documents, and approval workflows with configurable escalation paths. Milestones must be displayable in both English and Arabic with proper bidirectional text rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R005	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	AI-Driven Schedule Risk Prediction	The system must leverage AI models to analyze historical task completion patterns, resource utilization data, and current progress metrics to predict schedule risks and potential delays across all implementation workstreams. The AI engine must process at minimum the last 90 days of task completion velocity, factor in regional holiday calendars including Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, and Indian public holidays, and generate probabilistic forecasts for milestone completion dates. Risk predictions must be presented as confidence intervals with low, medium, and high probability scenarios, and the system must automatically flag milestones with greater than thirty percent probability of delay for management review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R006	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Resource Allocation and Capacity Planning	The system must provide resource allocation capabilities to assign implementation team members to project tasks across all four country entities, with visibility into resource availability, skill sets, and utilization rates. Resource calendars must account for entity-specific working hours, such as Sunday-to-Thursday workweeks in Qatar, UAE, and KSA versus Monday-to-Friday in India, and must properly handle Ramadan adjusted working hours. The capacity planning view must show resource loading in person-hours per week, highlight over-allocated resources in red, and support what-if scenario modeling to evaluate the impact of resource reassignment on the critical path and overall project timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R007	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Budget Tracking Per Entity and Currency	The system must track implementation budgets at the entity level with full multi-currency support for QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD. Budget line items must be linked to WBS elements and must capture planned versus actual expenditure with variance analysis. Currency conversion must use configurable exchange rates that can be updated daily or linked to a treasury feed, and all budget reports must be viewable in any of the five supported currencies with on-the-fly conversion. The system must enforce budget approval thresholds per entity, requiring escalation when actual spend exceeds planned budget by more than ten percent at any WBS level, and must generate automated alerts to the entity finance controller and program director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R008	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Gantt Chart Visualization with Bilingual Labels	The system must render interactive Gantt charts displaying all project tasks, milestones, and dependencies with full zoom capability from daily to yearly views. The Gantt chart must support bilingual display with task names and milestone labels shown in both English and Arabic simultaneously or toggled by user preference. Arabic labels must render correctly in RTL orientation without overlapping English LTR content. The chart must visually distinguish tasks by entity using color coding, show baseline versus actual progress with earned value indicators, highlight critical path tasks, and allow inline editing of task dates and durations directly on the chart with drag-and-drop functionality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R009	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Automated Progress Reporting and Dashboards	The system must generate automated weekly and monthly progress reports consolidating status across all entities, with drill-down capability from program level to entity level to individual workstream level. Reports must include key performance indicators such as Schedule Performance Index (SPI), Cost Performance Index (CPI), earned value metrics, milestone completion rates, and risk register summaries. Dashboards must be configurable per user role, with executive dashboards showing high-level RAG status across entities and detailed dashboards for project managers showing task-level progress. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with proper Arabic font rendering and in Excel format with multi-currency budget summaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R010	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Change Request Management for Scope Changes	The system must provide a formal change request workflow for managing scope changes during the implementation, including change request creation with impact analysis on schedule, budget, and resources. Each change request must capture the requesting entity, affected modules such as vessel operations, container yard management, customs clearance, or freight billing, estimated additional effort, cost impact in the entity's local currency, and risk assessment. The approval workflow must route change requests through entity project managers, program managers, and steering committee members with configurable approval thresholds based on cost and schedule impact magnitude. All change requests must maintain a complete audit trail with timestamps and approver comments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R011	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Integration with External Project Management Tools	The system must support bidirectional data synchronization with external project management tools including Microsoft Project, Jira, and Azure DevOps to accommodate implementation partners and consulting firms who may use their own tooling. The synchronization must map WBS elements, task statuses, resource assignments, and milestone dates between the ERP implementation tracker and the external tool. Conflict resolution rules must be configurable to determine which system is the master for each data element. The integration must support scheduled synchronization intervals as frequent as every fifteen minutes and must log all synchronization activities with error handling that alerts administrators when data mapping failures occur or when synchronization has not completed successfully within the configured interval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R012	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Offline Mode for Project Updates	The system must support offline mode for project plan updates to accommodate implementation team members working at port locations with intermittent connectivity, particularly during on-site commissioning activities at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT where network access inside container terminals may be restricted. Offline mode must allow task status updates, progress percentage changes, effort logging, and note additions to be captured locally and synchronized automatically when connectivity is restored. The system must implement conflict detection and resolution when offline changes conflict with updates made by other users during the offline period, presenting the user with a clear merge interface showing both versions and allowing manual resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R013	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Stakeholder Communication and Notification Engine	The system must provide a configurable notification engine that sends automated communications to project stakeholders based on milestone events, task assignments, status changes, and escalation triggers. Notifications must be delivered via email and in-app alerts, with SMS integration for critical escalations. All notification templates must support bilingual content in English and Arabic, with proper RTL formatting for Arabic recipients. The notification engine must respect entity-level working hours and time zones so that notifications for Qatar and UAE stakeholders are not sent outside Gulf Standard Time business hours, and notifications for India stakeholders respect Indian Standard Time. Stakeholders must be able to configure their notification preferences including frequency digest options.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R014	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Phase Gate Review and Approval Workflow	The system must enforce phase gate reviews at predefined implementation stages including project initiation, blueprint completion, system build, integration testing, user acceptance testing, and go-live for each entity. Each phase gate must require completion of defined deliverables checklist, sign-off from designated approvers at both entity and program levels, and formal documentation of open issues and risks carried forward. The system must prevent progression to the next phase until all mandatory gate criteria are satisfied, with an override mechanism available only to the program steering committee with full audit logging. Phase gate templates must be customizable per entity to accommodate local regulatory requirements such as Qatar MOTC approvals or India CBIC customs system certifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R015	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Implementation Lessons Learned Repository	The system must maintain a structured lessons learned repository that captures insights, challenges, and best practices discovered during the implementation process across all entities. Each lesson must be categorized by module area such as customs clearance, vessel operations, or container tracking, by entity, by implementation phase, and by type including technical, organizational, or process-related. The repository must support full-text search in both English and Arabic and must allow tagging and cross-referencing of related lessons. AI-powered recommendation must surface relevant lessons when team members begin work on tasks similar to those where lessons were previously recorded, helping to prevent repeated mistakes and accelerate knowledge transfer across the multi-entity implementation program.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-001-R016	FEAT-055-1-001	MOD-055	Steering Committee Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide a dedicated steering committee dashboard that presents a consolidated executive view of the entire implementation program across all entities. The dashboard must display overall program health using RAG indicators, budget utilization across all entities with multi-currency consolidation into a single reporting currency configurable as USD or QAR, milestone achievement rates, critical risk summary, and key decisions pending. The dashboard must support a presentation mode suitable for steering committee meetings with large-format display optimization. All dashboard content must be available in Arabic and English with one-click language switching, and the system must maintain a historical archive of dashboard snapshots taken at each steering committee meeting date for trend analysis and accountability tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R001	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Migration Strategy Document Generator	The system must provide an AI-assisted migration strategy document generator that produces entity-specific migration plans based on source system analysis, data volume assessments, and business continuity requirements. The generated strategy must cover all core shipping data domains including vessel schedules, booking records, bills of lading, container inventory, customer master data, tariff structures, customs declarations, and financial transactions. Each strategy document must identify data dependencies, migration sequence constraints such as migrating customer masters before booking records and tariffs before invoices, estimated migration windows accounting for port operational schedules at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, and rollback procedures for each migration phase.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R002	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Source System Connectivity and Data Extraction	The system must support data extraction from a wide range of source systems commonly used in container shipping operations including legacy ERP platforms such as SAP, Oracle E-Business Suite, and custom-built port management systems, terminal operating systems like Navis N4 and TOPS, customs integration platforms, and flat file exports in CSV, XML, JSON, and EDI formats including EDIFACT BAPLIE, COPARN, and IFTMIN message types. The extraction engine must handle character encoding for Arabic text data ensuring proper preservation of Arabic customer names, addresses, and cargo descriptions stored in legacy systems. Connection configurations must be storable per entity with encrypted credential management and must support both real-time API-based extraction and batch file-based extraction methods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R003	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Data Profiling and Quality Assessment	The system must perform automated data profiling on extracted source data to assess data quality across all migration domains. Profiling must evaluate completeness by identifying null and missing values, consistency by detecting format variations in fields such as container numbers which must follow ISO 6346 format, accuracy by cross-referencing port codes against UN/LOCODE standards for ports like QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, and INJNP, and uniqueness by identifying duplicate records in customer masters, vessel registries, and container inventories. The profiling engine must generate quantitative quality scores per data domain per entity, produce detailed exception reports listing every record that fails quality checks, and provide trend analysis showing quality improvement across successive extraction iterations. Reports must be viewable in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R004	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Data Mapping and Transformation Rule Engine	The system must provide a visual data mapping tool that allows migration analysts to define transformation rules mapping source system fields to target ERP data model fields. The mapping tool must support one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one, and calculated field mappings with a library of built-in transformation functions including date format conversion between Gregorian and Hijri calendars, currency conversion with historical exchange rates for QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD, unit of measurement conversion for container weights between metric tons and pounds, port code standardization to UN/LOCODE format, and INCOTERMS code normalization from legacy codes to INCOTERMS 2020 standard codes such as FOB, CIF, CFR, and DDP. All mapping rules must be version-controlled with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R005	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Container Master Data Migration Validation	The system must validate migrated container master data against ISO 6346 standards for container identification, ensuring that owner codes, serial numbers, and check digits are correctly formatted and consistent. Validation must verify container type codes against ISO 6346 size and type codes including standard types like 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, and 40RF, and must flag any container records with invalid or deprecated type codes. The migration tool must cross-reference container records with known container fleet registries to identify orphaned or phantom containers that exist in legacy data but are no longer in active service. Weight and tare data must be validated against typical ranges for each container type, with anomalies flagged for manual review by the entity migration team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R006	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Customer and Vendor Master Data Harmonization	The system must provide AI-powered deduplication and harmonization of customer and vendor master records across all four entities, identifying potential duplicates based on fuzzy matching of company names in both English and Arabic, tax identification numbers including Qatar QID, UAE TRN, KSA VAT registration, and India GSTIN, address matching accounting for transliteration variations between Arabic and English addresses, and contact information correlation. The harmonization tool must present potential duplicate groups to data stewards for manual review and merge decisions, with configurable merge rules that determine which entity's record serves as the golden master. The system must maintain a cross-reference table linking legacy entity-specific customer codes to the new unified customer identifier for post-migration audit and reconciliation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R007	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Financial Data Migration with Multi-Currency Balancing	The system must migrate financial data including open accounts receivable, accounts payable, general ledger balances, and bank reconciliation records with strict multi-currency balancing controls. Each financial record must carry both the transaction currency amount and the entity local currency equivalent using the historical exchange rate at the transaction date. The migration must preserve the original transaction currency for open items denominated in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, or INR, and must recalculate local currency equivalents where historical rates are available. Trial balance reconciliation must be performed automatically after each migration run, comparing source system trial balances with target system balances per entity, per currency, per GL account, with variance thresholds configurable by the entity finance controller and any variances exceeding the threshold blocking the migration from proceeding to the next phase.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R008	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Historical Shipment and Booking Data Archival Migration	The system must support migration of historical shipment and booking data with configurable retention periods per entity, accommodating regulatory requirements that vary by jurisdiction. Qatar and UAE entities may require seven years of historical booking data for customs audit purposes, KSA entities may require five years per ZATCA requirements, and India entities may require eight years per CBIC regulations. The migration tool must support tiered migration where recent active data within two years is migrated to the primary operational database while older historical data is migrated to an archival storage tier with on-demand retrieval capability. Historical bill of lading records must maintain their original reference numbers, vessel voyage details, container assignments, and INCOTERMS to support post-migration customs inquiries and dispute resolution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R009	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Tariff and Rate Card Migration with Effective Dating	The system must migrate tariff structures and rate cards from legacy systems preserving effective date ranges, currency denominations, and applicability rules based on trade lanes, container types, customer contracts, and INCOTERMS. The migration must handle complex tariff structures common in container shipping including base ocean freight rates, terminal handling charges at specific ports like Hamad Port and JNPT, bunker adjustment factors, currency adjustment factors, peak season surcharges, and dangerous goods surcharges. Each migrated tariff must be validated against its effective date range to ensure that only currently active and future-dated tariffs are migrated to the operational tier, while expired tariffs are archived. The system must detect and flag conflicting tariff records where multiple rates exist for the same trade lane, container type, and date range combination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R010	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Incremental and Delta Migration Support	The system must support incremental delta migration to handle the transition period between migration cutover rehearsals and actual go-live, during which legacy systems continue to process new transactions. The delta migration engine must identify records created or modified in the source system after the initial full extraction timestamp, extract only the changed records, apply the same transformation and validation rules as the full migration, and merge them into the target system without duplicating previously migrated data. Delta migration must support configurable scheduling to run at defined intervals such as hourly or daily during the transition period, with automated reconciliation reports showing the volume and nature of delta records processed per entity. The system must maintain a high-water mark timestamp per source table per entity to ensure no records are missed or duplicated across delta runs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R011	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Data Migration Dry Run and Rehearsal Management	The system must support multiple dry run migration rehearsals in isolated sandbox environments that replicate the production target system configuration for each entity. Each dry run must execute the complete migration pipeline including extraction, transformation, validation, and loading, with full logging and metrics collection to measure throughput, error rates, and elapsed time. The system must compare results across successive dry runs to identify improvements or regressions in data quality and migration performance. Dry run reports must capture record counts per migration object per entity, transformation error details, validation failure summaries, and estimated production migration window duration. The system must support at least three concurrent dry run environments to allow parallel rehearsals for different entities without interference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R012	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Rollback and Recovery Procedures	The system must implement comprehensive rollback procedures for each migration phase, enabling complete reversal of a failed migration batch without data loss or corruption. Rollback must be executable at multiple granularity levels including full entity rollback, migration object rollback such as rolling back only customer master migration while retaining completed vessel master migration, and individual record rollback for targeted corrections. The rollback mechanism must restore the target system to its exact pre-migration state for the affected scope, verified by automated checksum comparison. Recovery procedures must account for dependent data, automatically rolling back child records when parent records are rolled back, such as rolling back booking records when the associated customer master is rolled back. All rollback actions must be logged with full audit trail for compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R013	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Post-Migration Data Reconciliation	The system must perform automated post-migration reconciliation comparing source and target system data at multiple levels including record counts per migration object, field-level value comparison on a configurable sample basis of at least ten percent of migrated records, aggregate financial totals per entity per currency, and referential integrity validation ensuring all foreign key relationships are intact in the target system. Reconciliation must produce detailed variance reports highlighting any discrepancies between source and target, with severity classification as critical, major, or minor based on configurable rules. Critical discrepancies such as missing financial records or broken container-to-booking linkages must block go-live approval. The reconciliation engine must support re-execution after remediation actions are taken, maintaining a history of reconciliation runs showing progressive resolution of discrepancies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R014	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Migration Dashboard and Real-Time Monitoring	The system must provide a real-time migration monitoring dashboard displaying progress across all entities and migration objects with metrics including records extracted, records transformed, records loaded, records failed, throughput rates in records per second, estimated time remaining, and cumulative error counts by error category. The dashboard must support drill-down from entity level to migration object level to individual error record level. Visual indicators must clearly show migration health using color-coded status for each migration stream. The dashboard must refresh at least every thirty seconds during active migration runs and must support simultaneous monitoring of parallel migration streams running for different entities. Historical migration run statistics must be retained for comparison and trend analysis across dry runs and the final production migration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R015	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Sensitive Data Handling and Masking for Non-Production	The system must provide data masking and anonymization capabilities for migrated data used in non-production environments including development, testing, and training systems. Sensitive data fields including customer contact information, bank account details, personal identification numbers such as Qatar QID, UAE Emirates ID, KSA national ID, and Indian Aadhaar or PAN numbers, and commercial contract terms must be masked or replaced with realistic synthetic data that preserves data format and referential integrity. The masking rules must be configurable per data domain and must comply with data protection regulations applicable in each jurisdiction including Qatar data privacy law, UAE PDPL, KSA PDPL, and India DPDP Act. Masked datasets must be reproducible using seed values to ensure consistent test data across migration rehearsals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-002-R016	FEAT-055-1-002	MOD-055	Legacy System Decommission Readiness Assessment	The system must provide a legacy decommission readiness checklist and assessment tool that evaluates whether all critical data has been successfully migrated from each source system before it can be decommissioned. The assessment must verify that all mandatory migration objects have passed reconciliation with zero critical discrepancies, that all historical data required for regulatory compliance has been archived per entity-specific retention policies, that all active business processes have been successfully transitioned to the new ERP including open bookings, in-transit shipments, pending customs clearances, and outstanding invoices at each port location. The tool must generate a formal decommission readiness certificate per source system per entity, requiring digital sign-off from the entity data owner, migration lead, and IT director before legacy system shutdown is authorized.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R001	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Plan and Scope Definition	The system must support creation of entity-specific UAT plans that define the scope of testing for each implementation phase, covering all ERP modules relevant to the entity's container shipping operations. UAT plans must identify the business processes to be tested such as export booking, import delivery order, container tracking, freight invoicing, and customs declaration filing. Each plan must specify entry criteria that must be satisfied before UAT begins, including completion of integration testing, availability of migrated test data, and readiness of test environments. The UAT plan must support multi-entity consolidated views for program-level oversight while maintaining entity-specific test scope definitions that reflect the unique operational processes at each port location including Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R002	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Shipping-Specific Test Scenario Library	The system must provide a pre-built library of test scenarios specific to container shipping operations covering end-to-end business processes. The library must include scenarios for full container load FCL export booking from rate quotation through bill of lading issuance, less than container load LCL consolidation workflows, transshipment operations between hub ports such as Jebel Ali acting as transshipment hub for Hamad Port, reefer container temperature monitoring and exception handling for perishable cargo, dangerous goods DG booking with IMDG class validation, customs clearance workflows specific to each country including Qatar single window, Dubai Trade, Saudi FASAH, and India ICEGATE, and multi-currency invoice generation with tax calculations per entity jurisdiction including Qatar zero VAT, UAE five percent VAT, KSA fifteen percent VAT, and India GST.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R003	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Test Case Design with Bilingual Support	The system must allow test case creation and documentation in both English and Arabic, with each test case containing fields for test case identifier, linked business process, preconditions, detailed step-by-step execution instructions, expected results, actual results, pass or fail status, and evidence attachment capability for screenshots and documents. Test cases must support parameterization to enable data-driven testing where the same test logic is executed with different data sets such as testing customs declaration with different HS codes, different container types 20GP versus 40HC, or different INCOTERMS FOB versus CIF. The Arabic test case content must render correctly in RTL layout, and testers must be able to switch between English and Arabic views without losing any entered test data or results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R004	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Test Execution Workflow and Progress Tracking	The system must provide a structured test execution workflow that guides testers through assigned test cases in a defined sequence, recording execution start and end timestamps, tester identity, and execution environment details. Progress tracking must display real-time metrics including total test cases per entity, cases executed, cases passed, cases failed, cases blocked, and percentage completion. The workflow must enforce that test cases with dependencies are executed in the correct order, preventing execution of downstream cases when prerequisite cases have failed. A Kanban-style board view must be available showing test case status distribution across lanes for each entity, with filtering capabilities by module, business process, tester, and priority. Progress data must be accessible via dashboard widgets for inclusion in steering committee reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R005	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Defect Logging and Lifecycle Management	The system must integrate defect management capabilities allowing testers to log defects directly from failed test cases with automatic pre-population of test case context including module, business process, test environment, and test data used. Each defect must capture severity classification as critical, high, medium, or low, priority for resolution, detailed reproduction steps, expected versus actual behavior, screenshots and screen recordings as evidence, and affected entity. The defect lifecycle must follow a configurable workflow including states for new, assigned, in-progress, resolved, verification-pending, verified-closed, and reopened. Defects must be linkable to specific ERP modules and business processes, and the system must track defect aging to highlight defects that remain unresolved beyond entity-specific SLA thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R006	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Environment Management and Data Refresh	The system must track and manage UAT test environments for each entity, recording environment configuration details including server specifications, ERP version deployed, integrated external systems such as customs gateways and port community systems, and test data set version. The system must support scheduling and tracking of environment refresh cycles where test databases are restored to a known baseline state using migrated and masked production-equivalent data. Environment booking must be available to prevent conflicts when multiple test teams within the same entity need exclusive access, particularly for scenarios that modify shared master data like vessel schedules or tariff tables. Environment health checks must run automatically before each testing session to verify connectivity to dependent systems including customs test endpoints for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R007	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Role-Based Test Assignment and Tester Management	The system must support assignment of test cases to specific testers based on their business role and entity affiliation. Role definitions must align with shipping operational roles including booking clerk, documentation officer, customs broker, vessel planner, yard controller, finance officer, and operations manager. Test case assignment must consider tester availability accounting for entity-specific working days with Sunday-to-Thursday for Gulf entities and Monday-to-Friday for India, language proficiency for bilingual test cases, and module expertise. The system must provide a tester workload view showing assigned versus completed test cases per tester per day, enabling test coordinators to rebalance assignments when testers are overloaded or when absenteeism requires reassignment. Testers must receive automated notifications of newly assigned test cases via email and in-app alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R008	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	End-to-End Business Process Test Chains	The system must support definition and execution of end-to-end test chains that link individual test cases into complete business process flows spanning multiple ERP modules. For example, an export shipment chain must link booking creation in the commercial module, container allocation in yard management, customs export declaration in the regulatory module, vessel loading confirmation in operations, bill of lading generation in documentation, and freight invoice creation in finance. Each step in the chain must pass output data to the next step, such as passing the booking reference from booking creation to container allocation. Chain execution must track the overall business process completion status and must identify the exact point of failure when a chain breaks, flagging whether the issue is in data, configuration, integration, or application logic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R009	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Cycle Management and Iteration Tracking	The system must support multiple UAT cycles per entity, tracking the progression from UAT Cycle 1 through subsequent cycles as defects are resolved and retesting is required. Each cycle must maintain its own execution results, defect counts, and pass rates independent of other cycles while also providing cross-cycle trend analysis showing improvement in pass rates and reduction in open defects. The system must enforce entry criteria for each new UAT cycle, such as requiring that all critical and high-severity defects from the previous cycle have been resolved and verified before the next cycle begins. Cycle comparison reports must clearly show which previously failed test cases now pass in the current cycle, which remain failing, and which new failures have been introduced, enabling efficient focused retesting rather than complete re-execution of the full test suite.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R010	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	AI-Powered Test Coverage Gap Analysis	The system must leverage AI to analyze the current test case library against the ERP functional specification and identify gaps in test coverage. The AI engine must map test cases to functional requirements, business rules, and configuration items to calculate coverage percentages per module per entity. Gap analysis must highlight untested scenarios including edge cases specific to container shipping such as split bill of lading processing, container damage claims at transshipment ports, demurrage and detention charge calculations with free-time variations per port, and multi-leg intermodal routing involving rail connections from JNPT to inland container depots. The system must generate recommended test case outlines for identified gaps, prioritized by business risk and regulatory impact, and must update coverage metrics as new test cases are added to the library.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R011	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Performance and Load Testing Coordination	The system must coordinate performance and load testing activities alongside functional UAT, tracking test scripts that simulate realistic shipping operational loads such as concurrent booking creation during peak season at Jebel Ali which handles over fifteen million TEUs annually, high-volume customs declaration submissions during clearance windows, and mass container status update processing from terminal operating systems. Performance test results must capture response times, throughput rates, error rates, and resource utilization metrics for each tested scenario. The system must define acceptable performance thresholds per transaction type per entity, such as booking creation completing within three seconds and customs declaration submission completing within five seconds, and must flag any test results that breach these thresholds as performance defects requiring resolution before go-live.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R012	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Sign-Off and Acceptance Workflow	The system must enforce a formal UAT sign-off workflow requiring designated business process owners from each entity to review UAT results and provide explicit acceptance or rejection for their process area. Sign-off must be granular at the business process level, allowing the customs manager to sign off on customs processes independently of the finance manager signing off on billing processes. The sign-off interface must display a summary of test execution statistics, open defect counts by severity, and any accepted known issues with documented workarounds. Sign-off must require digital signature with timestamp and must generate a formal UAT acceptance certificate per entity that serves as a prerequisite for go-live readiness assessment. The system must prevent sign-off when critical defects remain open and unresolved for the specific business process area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R001	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Submission Form with Categorization	The system must provide a bilingual (Arabic/English) change request submission form that captures requester details, affected module, entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), priority level (critical, high, medium, low), change type (configuration, process, enhancement, bug fix), affected port operations (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKHL, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNHV), and detailed description with file attachments. The form must support RTL layout for Arabic users and validate that mandatory fields including business justification and expected outcome are completed before submission. Offline draft saving must be supported for field users at port terminals with intermittent connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R013	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Regression Test Suite Management	The system must maintain a regression test suite comprising test cases that verify previously working functionality has not been broken by defect fixes, configuration changes, or system patches applied during the UAT period. The regression suite must be automatically updated when defects are resolved by including the verification test case in the regression set. Regression test execution must be schedulable to run after each deployment to the UAT environment, with automated comparison of results against the previous regression run to identify newly introduced failures. The regression suite must cover cross-entity shared functionality such as global vessel schedule management, inter-entity container transfers between Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and consolidated financial reporting in multiple currencies. Regression test failure must automatically create defect records linked to the triggering deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R014	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Training Material and Tester Onboarding	The system must provide a structured onboarding workflow for UAT testers that includes access to training materials explaining the test management system usage, entity-specific business process documentation for the modules they will test, and test data preparation guides. Onboarding content must be available in both English and Arabic to serve testers across all entities. The onboarding workflow must track completion status per tester, ensuring that all assigned testers have reviewed mandatory materials before being granted access to execute test cases. The system must include a sandbox practice environment where new testers can execute sample test cases to familiarize themselves with the test execution interface, defect logging process, and evidence capture tools without affecting actual UAT results or metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R015	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	UAT Metrics and Executive Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive UAT metrics reports for executive stakeholders presenting testing progress, quality trends, and risk indicators across all entities. Reports must include test execution progress curves showing planned versus actual completion per entity, defect discovery and resolution trend charts, test pass rate progression across UAT cycles, defect density per module indicating code quality, and tester productivity metrics. The reporting engine must support automated generation on a daily basis during active UAT phases with distribution to configured stakeholder lists. Reports must be viewable on screen with interactive drill-down capability and exportable in PDF format with proper Arabic font rendering for Gulf entity stakeholders. The system must provide predictive analytics estimating the likely number of additional UAT cycles needed to achieve target pass rates based on current defect discovery and resolution trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-003-R016	FEAT-055-1-003	MOD-055	Offline Test Execution and Evidence Capture	The system must support offline test execution for testers working at port facilities with limited network connectivity, particularly during on-site validation of warehouse management, gate operations, and yard management functions at container terminals. Offline mode must allow testers to view assigned test cases, record execution results including pass or fail status and notes, and capture photographic evidence using mobile device cameras for scenarios requiring physical verification such as container seal number validation or reefer plug point connectivity testing. All offline data must synchronize automatically when the tester's device reconnects to the network, with conflict resolution handling if the same test case was updated by another user during the offline period. The offline client must support both English and Arabic interfaces with proper RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R001	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Configurable Go-Live Checklist Template	The system must provide a configurable go-live readiness checklist template with predefined categories covering technical readiness, data migration completeness, integration verification, user training completion, organizational change readiness, regulatory compliance, and operational continuity planning. Each checklist item must support assignment to a responsible person and entity, due date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, evidence attachment for completion proof, and status tracking through states of not-started, in-progress, completed, and waived-with-approval. The template must be customizable per entity to accommodate entity-specific requirements such as Qatar MOTC port system certifications, UAE FTA customs integration approvals, KSA ZATCA e-invoicing compliance verification, and India ICEGATE connectivity certification. The checklist must enforce that all mandatory items are completed before the go-live gate can be passed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R002	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Technical Infrastructure Readiness Verification	The system must include automated technical infrastructure readiness checks that verify production environment availability, server health, database readiness, network connectivity to all required external systems, and disaster recovery configuration. Infrastructure checks must validate connectivity to port community systems at each location including Hamad Port's PCS, Jebel Ali's DPWorld systems, and JNPT's PCS1x platform, customs authority gateways for all four countries, banking interfaces for payment processing in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD, and carrier EDI communication channels for INTTRA, CargoSmart, and direct carrier connections. Each check must produce a timestamped verification result with pass or fail status and diagnostic details for failures. The system must support scheduled re-execution of infrastructure checks to confirm continued readiness as the go-live date approaches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R003	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Data Migration Completeness Gate	The system must include a data migration completeness gate within the go-live checklist that aggregates migration reconciliation results from the Data Migration module and presents a consolidated view of migration status per entity. The gate must verify that all mandatory data objects have been migrated with reconciliation pass rates meeting or exceeding entity-specific thresholds, that financial trial balances match between source and target systems within acceptable tolerance per currency, that all open operational items including active bookings, in-transit containers, pending customs declarations, and outstanding invoices have been correctly migrated, and that the delta migration process has captured all transactions created in legacy systems since the last full extraction. The migration completeness gate must display entity-level RAG status and must block go-live approval if any entity shows red status on critical migration objects.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R004	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Integration Testing Certification	The system must require formal integration testing certification for all external system interfaces before go-live, covering customs authority integrations with Qatar single window, Dubai Trade, Saudi FASAH, and India ICEGATE, port community system integrations at each port location, carrier booking and documentation exchange via EDI and API, banking and payment gateway integrations for each entity's local currency, and vessel tracking data feeds from AIS providers. Each integration must have documented test results showing successful end-to-end message exchange in both directions where applicable, error handling verification for common failure scenarios such as customs rejection responses and payment gateway timeouts, and performance under expected transaction volumes. Integration certification must be signed off by both the ERP implementation team and the external system counterpart, with digital signatures stored as evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R005	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	User Training Completion Verification	The system must track and verify that all designated end users across every entity have completed their required training modules before go-live approval is granted. Training completion must be tracked per user, per module, and per entity, with minimum passing scores on training assessments configurable per entity. The system must identify users who have not completed mandatory training and generate automated reminder notifications with escalation to their line managers when training deadlines are approaching. Training completion metrics must be broken down by user role such as booking clerks, customs brokers, finance officers, and yard operators, and must distinguish between classroom training, e-learning completion, and hands-on system practice sessions. The go-live checklist must enforce a minimum training completion threshold of ninety-five percent per entity per role before the training readiness gate can be marked as passed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R006	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Business Continuity and Fallback Plan Verification	The system must require documented and tested business continuity plans and fallback procedures for each entity as a mandatory go-live checklist item. The fallback plan must define the conditions under which a rollback to legacy systems would be triggered, the maximum time window after go-live during which rollback remains feasible, the technical steps to revert to legacy systems, and the business process workarounds for maintaining container shipping operations during a potential system failure. Fallback procedures must specifically address critical operations that cannot tolerate interruption including vessel berthing schedules at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, customs clearance timelines subject to regulatory deadlines, reefer container monitoring for temperature-sensitive cargo, and financial transaction processing for pending invoices and payments. The system must verify that fallback procedures have been rehearsed and signed off by entity operations directors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R007	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Regulatory and Compliance Approval Tracking	The system must track all regulatory and compliance approvals required before go-live in each jurisdiction. For Qatar, this includes MOTC port operations system approval, Qatar Customs electronic declaration system certification, and Qatar Central Bank payment system registration. For UAE, this includes FTA VAT system compliance certification and Dubai Customs eDec approval. For KSA, this includes ZATCA e-invoicing phase two integration certification and Saudi Customs FASAH platform approval. For India, this includes CBIC ICEGATE registration, GSTIN compliance verification, and RBI payment gateway approval. Each regulatory item must track the application submission date, expected approval timeline, actual approval date, approval reference number, and validity period. The system must flag upcoming regulatory approval expirations and must prevent go-live for an entity if any mandatory regulatory approval is missing or expired.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R008	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Go-Live Cutover Plan with Timeline	The system must support definition and tracking of a detailed cutover plan for each entity specifying the exact sequence and timing of activities required to transition from legacy to the new ERP system. The cutover plan must be schedulable with hour-level granularity over the cutover weekend, covering activities such as legacy system freeze, final delta data migration, system configuration verification, integration endpoint switching from test to production, DNS and network routing changes, smoke testing of critical transactions, and user access activation. Each cutover activity must have an assigned owner, estimated duration, actual start and finish times, dependencies on prior activities, and a verification checkpoint confirming successful completion. The cutover plan must support execution tracking in real time during the actual cutover event, with a dedicated war room dashboard showing live progress against the planned timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R009	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Support Plan Definition	The system must require definition and approval of a hypercare support plan for each entity as a mandatory go-live checklist item. The hypercare plan must specify the duration of intensive post-go-live support, typically two to four weeks, the support team composition including functional consultants, technical support engineers, and business super users per module, escalation procedures with defined response time SLAs for severity levels from critical requiring thirty-minute response to low requiring next-business-day response, and communication channels for issue reporting including a dedicated support hotline, email distribution list, and in-app chat. The hypercare plan must account for time zone differences between Gulf entities operating in GMT plus three and the India entity operating in GMT plus five thirty, ensuring twenty-four-hour support coverage during the initial go-live week. The plan must also define criteria for transitioning from hypercare to steady-state BAU support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R010	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Performance Benchmark Verification	The system must include a performance benchmark verification gate that confirms the production ERP system meets defined performance thresholds under expected operational load before go-live is approved. Performance benchmarks must cover key shipping transactions including booking creation under three seconds, bill of lading generation under five seconds, customs declaration submission under four seconds, container status query under two seconds, and financial posting under three seconds. Benchmarks must be tested at peak load levels appropriate for each port, such as simulating simultaneous vessel arrivals processing at Jebel Ali which may involve thousands of container moves per vessel. The benchmark results must be compared against thresholds defined in the UAT performance testing phase, and any degradation greater than twenty percent from UAT results must be investigated and resolved before go-live authorization. Results must be documented with load profiles, response time distributions, and resource utilization data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R011	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Security and Access Control Verification	The system must verify that all security configurations and access controls are correctly implemented in the production environment before go-live. Verification must include confirmation that user roles and permissions are configured per the approved security matrix for each entity, segregation of duties rules are enforced to prevent conflicts such as the same user approving and processing payments, multi-factor authentication is enabled for privileged accounts, API security tokens and certificates for external integrations are installed and valid with expiration dates beyond the go-live period, data encryption at rest and in transit is configured for sensitive information including customer financial data and personal identification numbers, and audit logging is active and capturing all security-relevant events. The security verification must produce a formal security readiness report signed off by the information security officer for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R012	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Parallel Run Coordination and Comparison	The system must support coordination of a parallel run period where both legacy and new ERP systems process the same transactions simultaneously for a configurable duration per entity, typically one to two weeks before go-live. The parallel run tracking must record all transactions processed in both systems, compare results between legacy and new system outputs including booking confirmations, invoice amounts in entity local currency, customs declaration content, and container tracking updates, and highlight discrepancies for investigation. The system must provide a parallel run reconciliation dashboard showing match rates per transaction type per entity, with drill-down to individual discrepant transactions. Parallel run completion with acceptable match rates exceeding ninety-five percent must be a mandatory prerequisite for go-live approval, with the threshold configurable per entity and transaction type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R013	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Communication Plan and Stakeholder Notification	The system must track execution of the go-live communication plan ensuring all internal and external stakeholders are notified of the system transition. Internal communications must include go-live date announcements to all users, system access instructions for the new ERP, changed process notifications for workflows that differ from legacy, and support contact information for the hypercare period. External communications must cover notifications to customers about any changes to booking portals or documentation formats, notifications to customs authorities about system change for declaration processing, notifications to banking partners about payment system changes, and notifications to shipping line partners about EDI endpoint changes. Each communication must be tracked with intended recipients, send date, delivery confirmation, and available in both English and Arabic. The system must generate automated reminders for unsent communications as the go-live date approaches.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R014	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Go-Live Decision Gate and Authorization	The system must enforce a formal go-live decision gate that aggregates the status of all checklist categories and presents a consolidated readiness assessment to the go-live authorization committee. The decision gate must display the status of every mandatory checklist item across all entities, highlight any items that are incomplete or have been waived with documented justification, present a risk summary of proceeding with go-live given the current readiness state, and require explicit go or no-go votes from designated decision makers including the program director, entity managing directors, IT director, and chief financial officer. The go-live authorization must be recorded with digital signatures, timestamps, and any conditions attached to the approval. If a no-go decision is made, the system must capture the reasons and generate a remediation action plan with revised target dates for the deferred go-live.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R015	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Post-Go-Live Stabilization Tracking	The system must provide post-go-live stabilization tracking capabilities that monitor system health and operational metrics during the hypercare period for each entity. Stabilization metrics must include system availability and uptime percentage, transaction processing success rates by type such as bookings, customs declarations, invoices, and container moves, average response times compared to pre-go-live benchmarks, number of support tickets raised per day categorized by severity and module, and user login rates indicating adoption levels per entity. The tracking dashboard must display trends over the hypercare period with configurable alert thresholds that trigger notifications when metrics fall below acceptable levels, such as system availability dropping below ninety-nine point five percent or critical support ticket volume exceeding ten per day per entity. Stabilization reports must be generated daily for entity operations teams and weekly for steering committee review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R016	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Phased Rollout Entity Sequencing Management	The system must support phased go-live rollout planning where different entities go live on different dates according to a defined sequence, such as starting with the UAE entity at Jebel Ali as pilot followed by Qatar at Hamad Port, then KSA, and finally India at JNPT. The system must maintain independent go-live readiness checklists per entity while tracking cross-entity dependencies that could affect the rollout sequence. Lessons learned from earlier entity go-lives must be automatically flagged for review by subsequent entity implementation teams. The phased rollout tracker must display a consolidated timeline showing each entity's planned and actual go-live dates, current readiness percentage, and critical blocking issues. The system must support re-sequencing of the rollout order if an entity encounters delays, with impact analysis showing the ripple effects on dependent entities and the overall program timeline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-1-004-R017	FEAT-055-1-004	MOD-055	Go-Live Day Operational Command Center	The system must provide a go-live day operational command center interface that serves as the central coordination point during the actual go-live event for each entity. The command center must display real-time status of all cutover activities against the planned timeline, live monitoring of the first transactions processed through the new ERP system including the first booking, first customs declaration, first invoice, and first container gate-in at the respective port, an open issues log with real-time updates and resolution tracking, communication tools for the go-live war room team including integrated chat and announcement broadcast capability, and quick-access links to rollback procedures and escalation contacts. The command center must support simultaneous operation for multiple entities if overlapping go-live windows occur, with entity-specific views filtered by port location and a consolidated program view showing all active go-live events.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R007	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Dependency Mapping	The system must allow analysts to map dependencies between change requests, identifying CRs that must be deployed together or in a specific sequence. Dependency types must include technical dependency (shared code modules), data dependency (database schema changes), process dependency (workflow sequence), and integration dependency (EDI message format changes with shipping lines, port authorities, or customs systems). The system must prevent deployment approval for a CR if its prerequisite CRs have not been completed. Visual dependency graphs must render in both Arabic and English with proper directional flow for RTL and LTR layouts respectively.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R008	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Emergency Change Fast-Track Procedure	The system must support an emergency change fast-track workflow for critical issues affecting live port operations such as vessel berthing schedule disruptions, customs clearance system outages, container tracking failures, or payment processing errors involving multi-currency settlements. Emergency CRs must bypass standard CAB review and require only single-authority approval from the entity operations director or designated deputy. Post-implementation review must be automatically scheduled within 48 hours of emergency deployment. The system must maintain a separate audit trail for emergency changes with mandatory root cause analysis and justification documentation for regulatory compliance purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R009	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Cost Tracking and Budget Allocation	The system must track estimated and actual costs for each change request in the currency of the requesting entity (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated reporting using configurable exchange rates. Cost categories must include internal labor, external consultant fees, infrastructure costs, license fees, and testing expenses. Budget allocation must link to the entity-specific implementation budget with real-time remaining budget visibility. The system must prevent approval of CRs that would exceed the entity's allocated change management budget without explicit CFO-level override authorization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R010	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Deployment Scheduling and Release Management	The system must provide a deployment calendar that schedules approved changes into release windows, avoiding conflicts with peak shipping periods such as Hajj season cargo surges at Jeddah Islamic Port, Ramadan schedule adjustments across all Gulf entities, Diwali peak season at JNPT, and Qatar National Day logistics demands at Hamad Port. Deployment windows must be configurable per entity with blackout periods defined by operations management. The system must enforce that no more than one major change is deployed to production per entity per release window and must generate automated deployment checklists including pre-deployment verification, deployment steps, and rollback procedures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R011	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Post-Deployment Verification Checklist Automation	The system must auto-generate a post-deployment verification checklist for each deployed change request based on the modules and integrations affected. Verification items must include functional testing of container booking workflows, bill of lading generation with correct INCOTERMS references, customs declaration submissions to relevant authorities (Qatar Customs, Dubai Trade, Saudi FASAH, ICEGATE), financial transaction posting in correct currencies, and Arabic UI rendering validation. The checklist must require sign-off from the entity's operational lead and IT lead before the CR status transitions to completed. Failed verification items must automatically trigger a rollback assessment workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R012	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Communication and Notification Engine	The system must send automated notifications via email and in-app alerts at each stage transition of a change request to all stakeholders including the requester, assigned analyst, approvers, and affected end users. Notifications must be delivered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) with proper RTL formatting for Arabic emails. Notification templates must be configurable per entity and must include CR reference number, summary of change, current status, and required actions. For changes affecting port operations, the system must generate operational advisories that can be distributed to vessel agents, customs brokers, and freight forwarders through the port community system interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R013	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Analytics and Trend Reporting	The system must provide analytical reports showing change request volumes, approval rates, average cycle times, SLA compliance percentages, and cost summaries by entity, module, priority, and time period. Trend analysis must identify modules with highest change frequency indicating potential design issues or training gaps. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar period selections and must display currency values in entity-local currencies with a USD consolidated view. Exportable report formats must include PDF with proper Arabic typography support, Excel with multi-currency column formatting, and interactive dashboard views filterable by port (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNHV).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R014	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Knowledge Base Integration	The system must maintain a searchable knowledge base of all completed change requests with their impact analyses, implementation details, and lessons learned. When a new CR is submitted, the system must use AI-powered similarity matching to suggest related past CRs that may inform the analysis or reveal recurring issues. The knowledge base must support full-text search in both Arabic and English with proper morphological analysis for Arabic text stemming. Each knowledge base entry must be tagged with affected container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), trade lanes, and shipping processes to enable contextual retrieval during future change assessments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R015	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Regulatory Change Impact Assessment Workflow	The system must provide a specialized workflow for change requests triggered by regulatory or compliance updates from maritime authorities, customs departments, or government entities across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Regulatory CRs must capture the source regulation reference, effective date, compliance deadline, and penalties for non-compliance. The impact assessment must evaluate effects on documentation workflows (bill of lading, customs declarations, certificates of origin), financial processes (duty calculations, VAT/GST treatment across entities), and reporting obligations. The system must track regulatory change implementation against compliance deadlines and generate alerts when implementation timelines risk missing mandatory effective dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-001-R016	FEAT-055-2-001	MOD-055	Change Request Rollback Execution and Tracking	The system must maintain rollback plans for every deployed change request and provide a structured rollback execution workflow when post-deployment verification fails or production issues are detected. Rollback initiation must require approval from the entity IT manager and operations lead. The system must track rollback execution steps, duration, and data integrity verification results. For changes affecting financial transactions in multiple currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), the rollback plan must include specific procedures for reversing posted journal entries, recalculating exchange rate differences, and reconciling bank statement integrations. Rollback completion must trigger a mandatory incident review within 72 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R001	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Item Registry and Classification	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of all configuration items (CIs) organized by category: application parameters, integration endpoints, business rules, workflow definitions, report templates, user roles, and entity-specific settings. Each CI must be classified by scope (global, entity-specific, port-specific), sensitivity level (public, internal, restricted, confidential), and change frequency (static, periodic, dynamic). Entity-specific CIs must be tagged with the applicable legal entity (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations) and port code (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNHV). The registry must support bilingual CI names and descriptions in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R002	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Version Control and History	The system must version-control all configuration changes with complete history tracking including the previous value, new value, change timestamp in entity-local timezone, changed-by user, associated change request reference, and business justification. Version history must be immutable and tamper-evident for audit compliance. The system must support comparison between any two versions of a configuration item with visual diff highlighting. For configurations involving currency exchange rates (USD/QAR, USD/AED, USD/SAR, USD/INR), the version history must additionally capture the effective date range and the source of the rate (central bank, treasury, manual override) to support financial audit trails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R003	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Environment Configuration Baseline Management	The system must support defining and maintaining configuration baselines for each environment (development, testing, staging, production) across all four entities. Baseline snapshots must capture the complete configuration state at a point in time and must be restorable in case of configuration corruption or failed deployments. The system must enforce that production configuration changes are promoted through the environment pipeline (dev to test to staging to production) with validation gates at each stage. Baseline comparisons between environments must highlight discrepancies that could cause behavioral differences, particularly in customs integration parameters, tax calculation rules (Qatar zero VAT, UAE 5% VAT, KSA 15% VAT, India GST rates), and port-specific operational thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R004	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Drift Detection and Alerting	The system must continuously monitor production configurations against approved baselines and detect unauthorized or accidental configuration drift. Drift detection must run at configurable intervals (minimum every 4 hours) and must compare current values against the last approved baseline for each entity. Detected drifts must be classified by severity: critical (security settings, integration credentials, financial parameters), high (business rules, workflow thresholds), medium (UI preferences, report formats), and low (non-functional display settings). Critical drifts must trigger immediate alerts to the entity IT security officer and central ERP team. The system must support automated remediation for low-risk drifts by reverting to baseline values with audit logging.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R005	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Entity-Specific Configuration Segregation	The system must enforce strict segregation of entity-specific configurations ensuring that Qatar entity settings cannot be viewed or modified by UAE, KSA, or India entity administrators and vice versa. Global configurations that apply across all entities must be manageable only by the central ERP administration team. The segregation model must extend to port-specific configurations including berth allocation parameters at Hamad Port, container yard layouts at Khalifa Port, free zone processing rules at Jebel Ali, and gateway terminal configurations at JNPT. Role-based access control must be enforced at the configuration item level with separate permission sets for view, modify, approve, and export operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R006	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Import and Export with Validation	The system must support bulk import and export of configuration items in structured formats (JSON, XML) for migration between environments and disaster recovery purposes. Exported configurations must include metadata such as entity context, environment source, export timestamp, and schema version. Import operations must validate configuration items against the target environment schema, checking for data type mismatches, missing dependencies, invalid cross-references, and entity context conflicts. The system must prevent importing Qatar-specific customs integration parameters (TASMU system endpoints) into the UAE entity context (Dubai Trade endpoints) and must flag such cross-entity configuration conflicts for manual resolution before import proceeds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R007	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Business Rule Configuration Interface	The system must provide a user-friendly interface for configuring shipping business rules without requiring code changes, including container demurrage and detention calculation parameters, free time allowances per port and container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), tariff rate tables in entity-local currencies, INCOTERMS-based charge allocation rules (CIF, FOB, CFR, EXW, DDP, DAP), and documentation requirements per trade lane. The interface must support Arabic and English with proper RTL rendering for Arabic form layouts. Business rule configurations must be effective-dated allowing future-dated changes to be prepared in advance and automatically activated on the specified Gregorian or Hijri calendar date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R008	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Integration Endpoint Configuration Management	The system must manage all external integration endpoint configurations including port community system APIs (Qatar Mwani, Abu Dhabi Ports APCS, DP World systems, JNPT PCS1x), customs gateway connections (Qatar Customs Al Nadeeb, Dubai Trade Mirsal2, Saudi FASAH, Indian ICEGATE), carrier EDI endpoints (INTTRA, CargoSmart, individual shipping line connections), and banking interfaces for multi-currency payment processing. Each endpoint configuration must store connection URL, authentication credentials (encrypted), timeout parameters, retry policies, message format version, and health check endpoints. The system must support environment-specific endpoint URLs while maintaining consistent configuration structure across development, testing, and production environments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R009	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Change Impact Preview	The system must provide a configuration change impact preview before any modification is applied, showing all dependent configurations, affected modules, impacted workflows, and potentially affected user roles. For changes to financial configurations such as exchange rate sources, tax calculation parameters, or chart of accounts mappings, the preview must estimate the impact on pending transactions and in-progress shipments across entities. Changes to port-specific configurations at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT must show the impact on current vessel schedules, pending container movements, and active customs declarations. The preview must include a confidence indicator and recommend whether the change should be applied immediately or scheduled for a maintenance window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R010	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Audit Trail and Compliance Reporting	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all configuration accesses and modifications that is compliant with regulatory requirements across all four jurisdictions. The audit log must capture who accessed or changed the configuration, from which IP address and device, what was changed (before and after values), when the change occurred in both Gregorian and Hijri date formats, and the authorization reference (approved change request number). Audit reports must be generatable by entity, time period, configuration category, and user for internal audit reviews and regulatory examinations. The system must retain configuration audit data for a minimum of seven years in compliance with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R011	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Feature Flag and Toggle Management	The system must support feature flag management allowing new ERP capabilities to be deployed but activated selectively per entity, per port, or per user group. Feature toggles must support gradual rollout percentages enabling phased activation such as enabling a new container tracking feature for 10% of users at Jebel Ali before full rollout. Each feature flag must have a defined owner, expiration date, and cleanup plan. The system must track feature flag dependencies to prevent conflicts when multiple flags interact. Feature flags affecting financial calculations, customs submissions, or regulatory reporting must require additional approval from the entity compliance officer before activation to ensure continued adherence to local regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R012	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Template Management for New Entities	The system must maintain configuration templates that accelerate onboarding of new operational entities or port locations. Templates must capture the standard configuration baseline for a container shipping operation including chart of accounts structure, workflow definitions, user role hierarchies, integration patterns, and business rule defaults. Template instantiation must prompt for entity-specific values such as legal entity name in Arabic and English, local currency code, tax registration numbers, port authority credentials, customs system endpoints, and working calendar (Friday/Saturday weekend for Gulf entities, Saturday/Sunday for India). The system must validate template completeness before allowing a new entity to begin user acceptance testing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R013	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Sensitive Configuration Encryption and Access Control	The system must encrypt sensitive configuration items at rest and in transit, including integration credentials, API keys, database connection strings, encryption certificates, and payment gateway tokens. Access to sensitive configurations must require multi-factor authentication and must be restricted to designated security administrators per entity. The system must support hardware security module (HSM) integration for managing encryption keys used in customs digital signature processes at Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs, and Indian Customs. Decrypted values must never appear in audit logs, exports, or error messages. Sensitive configuration access must generate real-time security alerts to the entity information security officer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R014	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Dependency Graph Visualization	The system must provide an interactive visual dependency graph showing relationships between configuration items across modules and entities. The graph must display upstream and downstream dependencies, enabling administrators to understand the cascade effect of changing a specific configuration parameter. For example, changing the base currency exchange rate configuration must visually show impacts on freight rate calculations, demurrage billing, customs duty valuations, financial reporting consolidation, and inter-entity transfer pricing across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The visualization must support both Arabic and English labels with proper RTL/LTR directional rendering and must allow filtering by entity, module, and configuration category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R015	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Automated Configuration Testing and Validation	The system must support automated configuration testing by executing predefined validation rules against configuration sets before they are promoted to production. Validation rules must check for logical consistency (e.g., demurrage rates must be positive values), cross-reference integrity (e.g., referenced port codes must exist in the port master), regulatory compliance (e.g., VAT rate configurations must match current rates: UAE 5%, KSA 15%), and integration compatibility (e.g., EDI message format versions must match carrier requirements). Test results must be documented and attached to the associated change request. Configuration sets that fail validation must be blocked from production promotion until all critical validation errors are resolved.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R016	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Configuration Backup and Disaster Recovery	The system must perform automated daily backups of all configuration data with separate backup sets per entity to comply with data residency requirements (Qatar data in Qatar, UAE data in UAE, KSA data in KSA, India data in India). Backup integrity must be verified through automated restoration tests to a sandboxed environment at least monthly. The system must support point-in-time configuration recovery to restore the configuration state as it existed at any specific timestamp within the retention period. Recovery procedures must account for configuration interdependencies ensuring that related configurations across modules are restored consistently. Backup encryption must use entity-specific keys managed through the organization's key management infrastructure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-002-R017	FEAT-055-2-002	MOD-055	Offline Configuration Cache for Port Terminal Operations	The system must maintain a locally cached copy of essential operational configurations at each port terminal to enable continued container processing during network connectivity outages between the terminal and central ERP servers. Cached configurations must include container handling rules, tariff rate tables in local currency, customs document templates, hazardous cargo classification parameters (IMO classes), and reefer container temperature monitoring thresholds. The cache must synchronize with the central configuration store upon connectivity restoration with conflict detection for any configurations that were modified centrally during the offline period. Cache staleness alerts must trigger when the local cache has not synchronized for more than 4 hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R001	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Curriculum Definition by Role and Entity	The system must support defining training curricula mapped to ERP user roles (operations clerk, customs broker, finance officer, port supervisor, shipping line coordinator, warehouse manager, IT administrator) and entity context (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each curriculum must specify mandatory and elective courses, prerequisite sequences, estimated completion hours, and validity period before recertification is required. Curricula must account for entity-specific regulatory training requirements such as Qatar Customs procedures for Hamad Port, Dubai Trade portal operations for Jebel Ali, Saudi FASAH system training for Dammam operations, and ICEGATE compliance training for JNPT. Curriculum content must be available in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R002	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Individual Training Progress Dashboard	The system must provide each user with a personalized training dashboard showing their assigned curriculum, completed courses with dates and scores, in-progress courses with percentage completion, upcoming mandatory training deadlines, and overall readiness score as a percentage. The dashboard must render correctly in both RTL (Arabic) and LTR (English) layouts based on user language preference. Progress indicators must use color-coded status (green for completed, amber for in-progress, red for overdue) that are also distinguishable through icons for color-blind accessibility. The dashboard must function on mobile devices used by port terminal staff at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with responsive design supporting both portrait and landscape orientations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R003	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Manager Training Oversight and Team Readiness View	The system must provide managers with an aggregated view of their team's training completion status across all direct reports and organizational units. The oversight dashboard must show team-level completion percentages, identify individuals who are behind schedule or have overdue training items, and project team readiness dates based on current completion velocity. For multi-entity managers overseeing operations across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, the view must allow filtering by entity and port location. The system must generate automated weekly training status summaries emailed to managers in their preferred language (Arabic or English) highlighting at-risk individuals who may not complete mandatory training before their scheduled go-live date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R004	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Course Completion Recording and Verification	The system must record training course completions from multiple delivery channels including instructor-led classroom sessions at port facilities, virtual instructor-led training via video conference, self-paced e-learning modules, and on-the-job training observations by supervisors. Each completion record must capture the course identifier, completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, delivery method, instructor or supervisor name, assessment score if applicable, and time spent. For instructor-led sessions, attendance must be verified through digital sign-in with employee ID validation. Self-paced e-learning completions must be automatically recorded from the learning management system integration with minimum time-on-content enforcement to prevent rapid click-through without actual learning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R005	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Competency Assessment and Certification Management	The system must support competency assessments at the end of each training course with configurable passing thresholds per course (default 70%). Assessment types must include multiple-choice questions, practical scenario simulations (e.g., processing a container booking for a 40HC reefer from Jebel Ali to JNPT with CIF INCOTERMS), and observed task performance checklists. Upon passing all mandatory courses in a curriculum, the system must issue a digital certificate of competency with unique certificate number, issue date, expiry date, and QR code for verification. Certificates must be generated in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) with the entity's legal name and logo. Failed assessments must allow up to three retake attempts with a mandatory 48-hour waiting period between attempts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R006	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Prerequisite Enforcement and Sequencing	The system must enforce training prerequisite chains ensuring users cannot access advanced courses before completing foundational courses. For example, a customs broker must complete the basic ERP navigation course before accessing the customs declaration processing course, and must complete the customs declaration course before accessing the multi-entity customs reconciliation course. The prerequisite engine must consider entity-specific course variants, recognizing that completing Qatar customs procedures training satisfies the customs fundamentals prerequisite for Qatar-based staff but not for UAE or KSA staff who must complete their own entity-specific customs training. The system must visually display the prerequisite chain as a learning path diagram in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R007	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Go-Live Readiness Gate Based on Training Completion	The system must enforce a configurable go-live readiness gate that prevents entity activation until minimum training completion thresholds are met. Thresholds must be definable per role category: critical roles (customs officers, finance managers, port operations supervisors) must achieve 100% mandatory course completion, standard operational roles must achieve 90% completion, and support roles must achieve 80% completion. The readiness gate must generate a formal readiness report per entity showing compliance against thresholds with individual-level detail for non-compliant users. Exemption requests must be supported with approval from the entity general manager and the central program director, with compensating controls documented such as scheduled completion within the first two weeks post-go-live.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R008	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Schedule Management and Calendar Integration	The system must support creating and managing training session schedules across all four entities, accounting for entity-specific working hours, prayer time breaks, public holidays, and weekend patterns (Friday/Saturday for Qatar, UAE, KSA; Saturday/Sunday for India). Training sessions must avoid scheduling conflicts with peak port operations hours at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The schedule must integrate with enterprise calendar systems and send bilingual (Arabic/English) calendar invitations to participants. For Ramadan periods, the system must automatically adjust training session durations to accommodate reduced working hours across Gulf entities. Hijri calendar dates must appear alongside Gregorian dates on all schedule displays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R009	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Material Version Control and Distribution	The system must manage training material versions linked to specific ERP system versions ensuring users are always trained on current functionality. When a change request modifies ERP functionality, the system must flag affected training materials for review and update. Training materials must be distributable in multiple formats including PDF documents, video recordings, interactive simulations, and quick reference cards. All materials must be available in Arabic and English with proper RTL formatting for Arabic documents including correct rendering of shipping terminology, port codes, and container type designations. The system must support offline download of training materials for port terminal staff who may have limited connectivity during field-based training sessions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R010	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Effectiveness Measurement and Feedback	The system must collect training effectiveness data through post-course evaluation surveys, post-training performance metrics, and periodic knowledge retention assessments administered at 30, 60, and 90 days after course completion. Evaluation surveys must capture ratings for content relevance, instructor quality, training environment, and self-assessed confidence in applying learned skills to real container shipping operations. The system must correlate training completion with post-go-live operational metrics such as transaction error rates, processing time per container booking, customs declaration rejection rates, and help desk ticket volumes per user. Effectiveness reports must be generated per entity and per course to identify training content that needs improvement or delivery methods that are underperforming.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R011	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Cost Tracking and Budget Management	The system must track training costs per entity including instructor fees, venue rentals, travel expenses for cross-entity training sessions, e-learning platform licensing, and training material production costs. Costs must be recorded in entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD conversion for consolidated budget reporting using configurable exchange rates. Budget allocation must be managed per entity with variance tracking against planned training expenditure. The system must generate cost-per-user and cost-per-course metrics to support training investment optimization decisions. Cross-entity training sessions where participants from multiple entities attend the same session must allocate costs proportionally based on attendee count per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R012	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Recertification and Refresher Training Tracking	The system must track certification expiry dates and automatically generate recertification reminders at configurable intervals (90 days, 60 days, 30 days before expiry). Recertification requirements must be configurable per course, ranging from retaking the full course and assessment to completing a shortened refresher module and abbreviated assessment. For regulatory-mandated training such as dangerous goods handling (IMDG Code compliance), customs broker licensing requirements, and data protection awareness (Qatar PDPL, UAE DIFC Data Protection, KSA PDPL, India DPDP Act), recertification cycles must align with regulatory renewal periods. The system must automatically restrict ERP access for users with expired certifications in critical roles until recertification is completed, with a configurable grace period per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R013	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Attendance and No-Show Management	The system must track attendance for scheduled training sessions with support for marking attendees as present, absent (excused), absent (unexcused), late arrival, or early departure. No-show rates must be reported per entity, department, and course to identify patterns and improve scheduling effectiveness. The system must automatically reschedule no-show participants to the next available session and notify their manager. For instructor-led sessions at port facilities, the system must support QR code-based check-in using mobile devices to streamline attendance recording in busy terminal environments at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Repeated no-shows (three or more for the same course) must trigger escalation to the entity HR department and training coordinator.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R014	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Environment Provisioning and Access	The system must manage access to dedicated ERP training environments (sandboxes) that mirror production configuration for each entity. Training environment data must include realistic but anonymized container shipping data including sample vessel schedules, container bookings across trade lanes (Gulf-to-India, Gulf-to-Europe, India-to-Gulf), bills of lading with various INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, CFR, EXW), customs declarations for each entity's customs authority, and financial transactions in all supported currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD). Training environment access must be provisioned automatically when a user is enrolled in a course requiring hands-on practice and deprovisioned upon course completion. The system must reset training environment data on a configurable schedule to maintain clean datasets for each training cohort.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R015	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	AI-Powered Training Recommendation Engine	The system must leverage AI to analyze user behavior patterns within the ERP system and recommend targeted training based on identified skill gaps. The recommendation engine must consider transaction error rates by module, feature utilization patterns, help desk inquiry topics, time spent on specific tasks compared to benchmarks, and assessment score patterns across the user's training history. Recommendations must be personalized per user and prioritized by business impact, highlighting training that would most improve operational efficiency at the user's assigned port. The engine must account for the user's language preference (Arabic or English), available training delivery methods at their location, and their current workload schedule to suggest optimal training timing. Recommendations must be reviewable and approvable by the user's manager before enrollment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-003-R016	FEAT-055-2-003	MOD-055	Training Compliance Reporting for Regulatory Audits	The system must generate comprehensive training compliance reports suitable for submission to regulatory authorities across all four jurisdictions. Reports must demonstrate that personnel handling customs declarations have completed required customs authority training, dangerous goods handlers have current IMDG Code certification, financial system users have completed anti-money laundering awareness training as required by Qatar Financial Centre Regulatory Authority, UAE Central Bank, Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority, and Reserve Bank of India regulations. Reports must include individual completion records with dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, certificate references, assessment scores, and instructor qualifications. The system must maintain report archives for the regulatory retention period of each jurisdiction (minimum seven years) with tamper-evident storage and digital signature verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R001	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Period Definition and Activation per Entity	The system must support defining and activating hypercare periods independently for each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with configurable start and end dates aligned to each entity's go-live schedule. Hypercare duration must be configurable per entity with a default of 12 weeks, extendable based on stabilization metrics. The system must track the current hypercare phase (intensive support weeks 1-4, enhanced support weeks 5-8, transition support weeks 9-12) and adjust support level agreements accordingly. Phase transitions must be formally approved by the entity operations director and central program manager. The system must display hypercare status prominently on all support dashboards with countdown to phase transitions in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R002	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Issue Logging and Triage Workflow	The system must provide a dedicated hypercare issue logging portal accessible from all ERP screens via a persistent help button, enabling users to report issues without leaving their current workflow context. Issue submissions must capture the user's current screen, module, entity, port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), steps to reproduce, business impact assessment, and screenshot attachments. Automatic triage must classify issues as system defect, configuration issue, training gap, user error, or enhancement request. Priority must be assigned based on operational impact: P1 for issues blocking port operations or customs clearance, P2 for issues causing significant workarounds, P3 for issues with minor operational impact, and P4 for cosmetic issues. The portal must support Arabic and English submissions with bilingual issue descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R003	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare SLA Enforcement with Escalation Matrix	The system must enforce heightened SLAs during the hypercare period that are more stringent than steady-state support SLAs. P1 issues must have a 30-minute initial response time and 4-hour resolution target during hypercare intensive phase. P2 issues must have a 2-hour response and 24-hour resolution target. P3 issues must have a 4-hour response and 72-hour resolution target. SLA calculations must account for entity-specific business hours and working days (Friday/Saturday weekend for Gulf entities, Saturday/Sunday for India) while P1 issues during the intensive phase must be tracked on a 24/7 clock regardless of business hours. Automatic escalation must trigger at 50% and 80% of SLA elapsed time, routing to progressively senior support personnel and ultimately to the hypercare program director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R004	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	On-Site and Remote Support Team Coordination	The system must manage the allocation and scheduling of hypercare support team members across entities, tracking whether team members are deployed on-site at port locations or providing remote support. Resource allocation must account for required skill sets (functional consultants, technical developers, customs integration specialists, financial module experts, Arabic-speaking support staff) matched against entity-specific needs. The system must maintain a support team roster with shift schedules that ensure coverage during peak port operational hours at Hamad Port (06:00-22:00 AST), Khalifa Port (06:00-22:00 GST), Jebel Ali (24/7 operations), and JNPT (06:00-22:00 IST). Handover notes between shifts must be logged in the system with outstanding issue summaries and next-action items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R005	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	System Performance Monitoring During Hypercare	The system must provide real-time performance monitoring dashboards during the hypercare period tracking key technical metrics including system response times for critical transactions (container booking, bill of lading generation, customs declaration submission), database query performance, integration message processing times with port community systems and customs gateways, concurrent user load, and error rates by module. Performance baselines established during user acceptance testing must serve as comparison benchmarks. The system must generate automated alerts when performance degrades beyond configurable thresholds such as container booking response times exceeding 3 seconds or customs EDI message processing queues exceeding 100 pending messages. Performance data must be segmented by entity and port to isolate location-specific issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R006	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Business Process Stabilization Metrics Tracking	The system must track business process stabilization metrics comparing actual ERP-supported process performance against pre-go-live benchmarks and target KPIs. Metrics must include container booking processing time, bill of lading issuance accuracy rate, customs declaration first-submission acceptance rate per customs authority (Qatar Customs, Dubai Customs, Saudi Customs, Indian Customs), invoice generation accuracy with correct currency and tax calculations, daily transaction volumes versus projected volumes, and user adoption rates by module and entity. The system must generate daily stabilization scorecards during the intensive hypercare phase and weekly scorecards during enhanced and transition phases. Metrics trending below target thresholds must trigger corrective action workflows with assigned owners and target resolution dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R007	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Known Issues Registry and Workaround Documentation	The system must maintain a known issues registry documenting all identified defects and their workarounds during the hypercare period. Each known issue entry must include a clear description of the problem, affected module and entity, conditions that trigger the issue, business impact assessment, documented workaround steps in both Arabic and English, planned permanent fix timeline, and associated change request reference. Known issues must be searchable by module, entity, keyword, and affected container shipping process (booking, documentation, customs, billing, tracking). The system must proactively display relevant known issues to users when they navigate to affected screens, reducing redundant issue reports and enabling self-service workaround application by operations staff at port terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R008	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Daily Stand-Up and Status Reporting	The system must support daily hypercare stand-up meetings by automatically generating agenda reports that summarize new issues reported in the last 24 hours, issues resolved, issues approaching SLA breach, top five highest-priority open issues per entity, system performance trends, and key stabilization metrics movement. Stand-up reports must be generated for entity-level daily meetings and for cross-entity program-level meetings. Reports must be distributable in both Arabic and English via email and accessible through the hypercare dashboard. The system must track action items arising from stand-up meetings with assigned owners, due dates, and completion status. Meeting schedules must accommodate time zone differences across Qatar (UTC+3), UAE (UTC+4), KSA (UTC+3), and India (UTC+5:30).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R009	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	User Satisfaction Survey During Hypercare	The system must deploy configurable user satisfaction surveys at scheduled intervals during the hypercare period (weekly during intensive phase, bi-weekly during enhanced phase, monthly during transition phase). Surveys must capture user sentiment on system usability, response times, issue resolution quality, training adequacy, and overall confidence in using the ERP for their daily container shipping operations. Survey questions must be presented in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) with RTL support for Arabic respondents. The system must aggregate survey results by entity, department, role, and port location to identify areas requiring targeted intervention. Net Promoter Score (NPS) trends must be tracked throughout the hypercare period with alerts when entity-level NPS drops below the configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R010	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Data Quality Monitoring and Remediation Tracking	The system must monitor data quality in the live ERP system during hypercare, checking for data integrity issues that may arise from migration or early operational use. Quality checks must include duplicate container records, orphaned bill of lading references, mismatched currency codes in financial transactions (e.g., QAR amounts posted to AED-denominated accounts), incorrect port code assignments, INCOTERMS field validation (ensuring only valid codes such as CIF, FOB, CFR, EXW, DDP, DAP, FCA are stored), and master data completeness for vessel, customer, and vendor records. Data quality exceptions must generate remediation tasks assigned to entity data stewards with priority based on operational impact. The system must track remediation completion rates and trending data quality scores per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R011	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Knowledge Transfer to Steady-State Support	The system must track the knowledge transfer progress from the hypercare implementation team to the entity's permanent IT support and operations teams. Knowledge transfer items must be defined per module and must include system architecture understanding, configuration management procedures, common issue diagnosis and resolution procedures, integration monitoring and troubleshooting, and entity-specific customization documentation. Each knowledge transfer item must have a defined trainer, trainee, scheduled date, completion status, and competency verification method. The system must prevent hypercare team member demobilization until their assigned knowledge transfer items achieve a minimum completion threshold of 90% with trainee competency verification sign-off from the entity IT manager.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R012	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Exit Criteria and Transition Readiness Assessment	The system must evaluate and track hypercare exit criteria that must be met before transitioning an entity from hypercare to steady-state support. Exit criteria must include: all P1 and P2 issues resolved or formally accepted with permanent workarounds, system availability exceeding 99.5% for the last 30 consecutive days, all critical business processes (container booking, customs clearance, financial closing) successfully executed for at least two complete business cycles, user satisfaction NPS above configurable threshold, knowledge transfer completion at 90% or above, and data quality score above 95%. The system must generate a formal hypercare exit readiness report per entity with pass/fail status against each criterion, requiring sign-off from entity operations director, entity IT director, and central program director before transition.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R013	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Parallel Run Reconciliation During Initial Hypercare	The system must support parallel run reconciliation during the initial weeks of hypercare where legacy systems may still be operating alongside the new ERP. Reconciliation must compare transaction volumes, financial totals in entity-local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), container movement counts, customs declaration submissions, and bill of lading issuances between legacy and new systems. Discrepancies must be logged with investigation workflows assigned to functional consultants. The system must produce daily parallel run reconciliation reports per entity showing matched and unmatched transactions with variance analysis. Reconciliation completion criteria must define acceptable variance thresholds (e.g., financial variance below 0.1%) before legacy system decommissioning is approved for each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R014	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Emergency Rollback Readiness Tracking	The system must track emergency rollback readiness throughout the hypercare period, ensuring that rollback to legacy systems remains viable during the initial hypercare phase. Rollback readiness items must include legacy system maintenance status, data reverse-migration script testing results, legacy integration point availability (customs gateways, port community systems, carrier EDI connections), user access preservation to legacy systems, and documented rollback decision criteria and authorization chain. The system must perform automated rollback readiness checks daily during the intensive hypercare phase and weekly during subsequent phases. Rollback readiness status must be visible on the hypercare executive dashboard with clear go/no-go indicators per entity. Rollback readiness tracking may be deactivated only after formal approval based on stabilization metrics achievement.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R015	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Resource Utilization and Cost Tracking	The system must track hypercare support resource utilization including consultant hours by skill category, travel and accommodation expenses for on-site support staff, infrastructure costs for additional hypercare environments, and third-party support vendor charges. Costs must be recorded in entity-local currencies (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with consolidated USD reporting. Resource utilization rates must be monitored against planned allocations with variance alerts when actual spend exceeds budget by configurable thresholds. The system must forecast remaining hypercare costs based on current burn rate and remaining hypercare duration per entity. Cost reports must differentiate between planned hypercare activities and unplanned issue remediation to inform future implementation project budgeting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R016	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Hypercare Lessons Learned Capture and Analysis	The system must provide a structured framework for capturing lessons learned throughout the hypercare period, categorized by theme (technical, process, organizational, training, data, integration). Each lesson learned entry must document the situation encountered, root cause analysis, action taken, outcome achieved, and recommendation for future entity go-lives. Lessons must be tagged with affected module, entity, port location (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), and hypercare phase. The system must support collaborative review and validation of lessons by the hypercare team before finalizing. A lessons learned report must be automatically compiled at the end of each entity's hypercare period and must be searchable by subsequent entity implementations to proactively apply learnings. The report must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-055-2-004-R017	FEAT-055-2-004	MOD-055	Post-Hypercare Warranty Period Issue Tracking	The system must support a configurable warranty period following hypercare completion (default 6 months) during which implementation-related defects are tracked separately from steady-state support tickets. Warranty issues must be linked back to the original implementation scope and change requests to determine accountability between the implementation vendor and the entity's internal IT team. The system must differentiate between warranty-covered issues (defects in delivered functionality) and out-of-warranty items (new requirements, user-initiated configuration changes, regulatory changes enacted after go-live). Financial tracking must record warranty claim resolution costs in entity-local currencies with vendor chargeback management for defects attributable to implementation quality. Warranty period expiration must trigger a formal sign-off ceremony and final implementation closure report per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R001	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Checklist Generation	The system must auto-generate a comprehensive voyage closure checklist upon vessel arrival at the final discharge port. The checklist shall enumerate all outstanding items including pending bills of lading, unreconciled container moves, port disbursement accounts from Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEAUH), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA), bunker survey reports, off-hire claims, and cargo outturn discrepancies. Each checklist item must carry a mandatory/optional flag and a responsible entity assignment. The AI engine must pre-populate completion status by cross-referencing data from MOD-050 (Vessel Operations) and MOD-042 (Port Disbursements) with a confidence score for each auto-resolved item exceeding 95% accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R002	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Multi-Entity Voyage Closure Authorization Matrix	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level sign-off matrix for voyage closure based on the operating entity (Qatar LLC, UAE FZE, KSA WLL, India Pvt Ltd). Each entity shall define its own approval hierarchy with a minimum of three levels: Operations Manager, Finance Controller, and Commercial Director. Cross-entity voyages involving transshipment between Jebel Ali and JNPT must require concurrent sign-off from both originating and destination entity controllers. The authorization matrix must support delegation rules for absence management, time-bound approvals with automatic escalation after 48 hours, and digital signature capture compliant with Qatar MOTC and UAE TDRA electronic transaction regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R003	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Automated Cost Accrual Verification	Upon initiating voyage closure, the system must automatically verify all cost accruals against actual invoices received. For each cost category—port charges, stevedoring, bunker consumption, canal transit fees, agency fees, and container handling—the system shall compute variance between accrued and actual amounts in the voyage's base currency (USD) and local settlement currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Variances exceeding a configurable threshold (default 5% or USD 5,000 whichever is lower) must trigger a mandatory review workflow. The AI engine must classify variances into categories: timing differences, rate changes, volume discrepancies, and unrecorded costs, providing recommendations for adjustment entries with supporting documentation references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R004	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Status Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The system must provide a real-time voyage closure status dashboard displaying all voyages in various stages of closure across the four operating entities. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts, with all labels, status indicators, numerical formats, and date displays (Gregorian and Hijri) properly mirrored. Numeric values must remain LTR within RTL context per Unicode bidirectional algorithm. The dashboard shall support filtering by entity, vessel, trade lane, closure status (initiated, pending review, disputed, approved, closed), and aging buckets (0-7 days, 8-14 days, 15-30 days, 30+ days). KPI tiles must show average closure cycle time, outstanding accrual value, and percentage of AI-auto-resolved items.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R005	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Document Completeness Validation	The system must validate documentary completeness before allowing voyage closure sign-off. Mandatory documents include signed bills of lading, cargo manifests, port disbursement accounts, bunker delivery notes, statement of facts for each port call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, crew wage settlement confirmations, and container interchange receipts. Each document must be linked to the voyage record with metadata including document type, upload timestamp, source entity, and verification status. The AI engine must perform OCR-based content extraction to verify document authenticity and cross-reference key data points (vessel name, voyage number, port codes, dates) against the voyage master record, flagging mismatches for human review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R006	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Partial Voyage Closure for Multi-Leg Voyages	The system must support partial voyage closure for multi-leg voyages where individual legs can be financially closed independently while the overall voyage remains open. Each leg closure must capture leg-specific revenue and costs including freight revenue allocated by leg via the voyage allocation model, port costs at the specific load/discharge ports, bunker consumption for the sea passage, and proportional time charter hire. The system must maintain a consolidated voyage view showing closed legs with finalized figures and open legs with accrued estimates. Inter-leg cost transfers such as repositioning empty containers between Jebel Ali and JNPT must be handled through inter-entity transfer pricing rules compliant with OECD guidelines and local transfer pricing regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R007	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	AI-Driven Anomaly Detection at Voyage Closure	The AI engine must perform comprehensive anomaly detection during voyage closure by comparing the voyage's financial and operational metrics against historical benchmarks for the same trade lane, vessel class, and season. Anomalies to detect include abnormal bunker consumption rates per nautical mile, port stay durations exceeding statistical norms for Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, unexplained cost spikes in stevedoring or terminal handling charges, revenue per TEU falling below corridor thresholds, and demurrage/detention patterns inconsistent with cargo mix. Each detected anomaly must be scored by severity (low/medium/high/critical) and presented with root cause hypothesis and recommended investigation actions. The system must learn from user feedback on false positives to improve detection accuracy over successive voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R008	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Reversal and Reopening Controls	The system must provide a controlled mechanism to reopen a previously closed voyage subject to strict authorization. Reopening shall require approval from the entity CFO or designated authority and must capture a mandatory reason code (late invoice received, audit adjustment, regulatory requirement, error correction) and free-text justification. Upon reopening, the system must create a reversal snapshot preserving the original closure state including all P&L figures, accruals, and sign-off timestamps. Any modifications during the reopened period must be tracked as adjustment entries clearly distinguished from original postings. The system must enforce a maximum reopening window configurable per entity (default 90 days post-closure) and prevent reopening of voyages already included in completed financial period closures without explicit period unlock.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R009	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Offline Voyage Closure Capability	The system must support voyage closure initiation and checklist completion in offline mode for scenarios where vessel or port operations personnel lack reliable internet connectivity, particularly relevant for anchorage operations at Hamad Port or during canal transits. Offline capability must include downloading the voyage closure package (checklist, pending items, document attachments up to 500MB) to the local device, allowing data entry and document capture offline, queuing approval requests, and synchronizing all changes upon connectivity restoration. The offline sync engine must implement conflict resolution logic using last-write-wins for non-critical fields and mandatory manual review for financial figures where concurrent edits are detected. Offline sessions must be time-limited to 72 hours before requiring re-authentication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R010	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Audit Trail and Compliance Log	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for every voyage closure action including checklist item completion, document uploads, approval grants or rejections, financial figure modifications, and status transitions. Each audit entry must record the acting user, entity, timestamp in UTC and local timezone, IP address or device identifier, action type, and before/after values for any data changes. The audit trail must be queryable by voyage, user, entity, date range, and action type. For regulatory compliance, the audit log must satisfy Qatar Financial Markets Authority record retention requirements (minimum 10 years), UAE Commercial Companies Law provisions, KSA ZATCA audit requirements, and India Companies Act 2013 provisions. The log must be exportable in PDF and CSV formats with Arabic and English column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R011	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Automated Voyage Closure Notifications and Escalations	The system must generate automated notifications at each stage of the voyage closure process. Notification triggers include voyage arrival at final port (closure initiation reminder), checklist items overdue beyond configurable SLA (default 48 hours), approval requests pending action, variance alerts requiring investigation, and successful closure confirmation. Notifications must be delivered via in-app alerts, email, and configurable webhook integrations for enterprise messaging platforms. All notification content must be bilingual (Arabic and English) with the user's preferred language displayed first. Escalation rules must support up to four escalation levels with configurable time intervals, and the AI engine must predict closure delays based on historical patterns and proactively alert stakeholders before SLA breaches occur.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R012	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Bunker ROB Reconciliation at Voyage Closure	The system must reconcile bunker remaining-on-board (ROB) quantities at voyage closure by comparing the opening ROB from the previous voyage, bunker stems received during the voyage at ports including Hamad Port and Jebel Ali bunkering anchorages, daily consumption reports from noon positions, and the closing ROB from the final bunker survey. The reconciliation must account for multiple fuel grades (VLSFO, LSMGO, HSFO) with quantities in metric tons and values in USD using weighted average cost methodology. Variances exceeding 2% of total consumption must trigger investigation workflows. The system must integrate with bunker supplier invoices to validate delivery quantities and prices, and automatically generate bunker adjustment entries for the voyage P&L. IMO 2020 sulphur cap compliance data must be captured and validated for each fuel grade consumed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R013	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Container Movement Reconciliation at Closure	The system must reconcile all container movements for the voyage against the original booking manifest, bills of lading, and terminal operating system records from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Reconciliation must cover loaded containers (full and empty), discharged containers, transshipped units, short-shipped cargo, over-landed containers, and containers remaining on board for the next voyage. Each discrepancy must be categorized by type (documentation error, operational variance, system sync delay) and linked to the responsible party for resolution. The system must calculate revenue impact of container movement variances including short-shipment freight adjustments, dead freight claims, and container imbalance costs. For reefer containers, the system must additionally verify power consumption charges against actual plug-in records at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R014	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Integration with Financial Periods	The system must enforce alignment between voyage closure dates and financial reporting periods for each operating entity. When a voyage spans multiple financial periods (monthly or quarterly), the system must support period-end accrual generation for open voyages based on percentage-of-completion methodology calculated from voyage distance completed versus total planned distance. Upon actual voyage closure, the system must automatically generate accrual reversal entries and post final actual figures to the correct financial period. For entities following Hijri fiscal calendars (relevant for KSA operations), the system must correctly map voyage dates to Hijri periods while maintaining parallel Gregorian period alignment. Cross-entity voyages must respect each entity's distinct financial calendar and period-end cutoff procedures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R015	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Performance Benchmarking	The system must generate a voyage closure performance benchmark report comparing the completed voyage's key metrics against fleet averages, trade lane benchmarks, and the same vessel's historical performance. Benchmark metrics must include total voyage cost per TEU, revenue per TEU, bunker cost per nautical mile, port cost per container move, voyage duration versus scheduled duration, cargo utilization percentage, and overall voyage contribution margin. The report must display trend analysis over the last twelve months with visual charts supporting both LTR and RTL rendering. The AI engine must provide narrative commentary in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English) explaining significant deviations from benchmarks and suggesting operational improvements for future voyages on the same trade lane between Gulf ports and Indian subcontinent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-001-R016	FEAT-056-1-001	MOD-056	Voyage Closure Currency Gain/Loss Calculation	The system must calculate realized and unrealized foreign exchange gains or losses at voyage closure arising from multi-currency transactions. Revenue booked in USD, costs incurred in QAR at Hamad Port, AED at Jebel Ali, SAR at Dammam, and INR at JNPT must be converted using the exchange rates applicable at the transaction date for realized items and the voyage closure date rate for unrealized accruals. The system must separately identify translation gains/losses (functional to reporting currency) and transaction gains/losses (foreign currency to functional currency) per IAS 21 requirements. Net FX impact must be allocated proportionally across voyage cost centers and displayed as a separate line item in the voyage P&L. The AI engine must flag voyages where FX impact exceeds 3% of voyage revenue and recommend hedging strategies for future similar voyages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R001	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Time Charter Settlement Initiation and Workflow	The system must initiate a Time Charter TA settlement workflow upon vessel redelivery or at configurable periodic intervals (monthly, quarterly) for long-term charters. The settlement workflow must capture the charter party reference, vessel details, delivery and redelivery dates and locations (including ports such as Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT), charter party rate per day in the agreed currency (typically USD), and any special clauses affecting settlement such as ballast bonus, address commission, and brokerage. The workflow must support both inward charters (vessel chartered in) and outward charters (vessel chartered out) with appropriate accounting treatment for each direction. AI must pre-populate settlement parameters from the charter party fixture note stored in the contract management module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R002	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Hire Calculation with Off-Hire Deductions	The system must calculate gross hire for the settlement period based on the charter party daily rate multiplied by the number of on-hire days, computed to the precision of hours and minutes using the charter party's agreed time counting methodology (port-to-port or berth-to-berth). Off-hire periods must be automatically identified from vessel event logs including machinery breakdowns, drydocking, deviation for owner's purposes, and periods where the vessel fails to maintain warranted speed or consumption. Each off-hire event must reference the applicable charter party off-hire clause, capture start and end timestamps with supporting documentation (master's notices, surveyor reports), and calculate the deduction amount. The system must handle partial off-hire scenarios where the vessel operates at reduced capacity and compute proportional deductions based on the performance warranty parameters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R003	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Bunker Valuation at Delivery and Redelivery	The system must compute bunker adjustments at charter delivery and redelivery based on physical bunker surveys and agreed pricing methodology from the charter party. Bunker quantities for each fuel grade (VLSFO, LSMGO, HSFO) must be recorded in metric tons from independent surveyor reports. Pricing methodology must support charter party options including fixed price per grade, market price at delivery/redelivery port (referencing Platts or Argus benchmarks for Fujairah, Hamad Port, or Mumbai anchorage), and last purchase price. The net bunker adjustment (redelivery value minus delivery value) must be computed in USD and added to or deducted from the hire settlement. The system must flag quantity discrepancies exceeding 3% between surveyor reports and vessel's declared ROB, triggering a dispute workflow for resolution between owner and charterer.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R004	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Address Commission and Brokerage Computation	The system must automatically calculate address commission and brokerage deductions from gross hire as specified in the charter party terms. Address commission (typically 2.5%-3.75% of hire) must be computed on gross hire before off-hire deductions but after any charter party-specified exclusions. Brokerage (typically 1.25%-2.5%) must be allocated to one or more brokers as specified in the fixture note with configurable split percentages. The system must generate commission payment instructions in the broker's preferred currency (USD, AED, or GBP) with appropriate bank details from the broker master data. Commission calculations must respect entity-specific withholding tax requirements: India TDS under Section 194H at applicable rates, Qatar withholding provisions, and UAE/KSA tax-free treatment. The AI engine must validate commission rates against market norms and flag outliers for review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R005	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Speed and Consumption Performance Claims	The system must support calculation of speed and consumption performance claims under the charter party warranty provisions. For each laden and ballast voyage leg, the system must compare actual vessel performance (speed in knots and bunker consumption in MT/day by fuel grade) against warranted parameters from the charter party, excluding periods of adverse weather (Beaufort scale exceeding the charter party's good weather warranty, typically Force 4-5), port approaches, and traffic separation scheme transits. Weather data must be sourced from integrated meteorological feeds for the Arabian Sea, Persian Gulf, and Indian Ocean routes. The system must compute underperformance claims as the monetary value of excess bunker consumed or time lost due to speed deficiency, applying the agreed bunker price and daily hire rate. Claims must be presented with detailed leg-by-leg analysis and supporting weather data for charterer or owner review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R003	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Automated voyage result variance detection	The system must automatically compare actual voyage results against the original voyage estimate and flag variances exceeding configurable thresholds per cost category. Threshold defaults must be set at 5% for bunker costs, 10% for port disbursements at Hamad Port and Khalifa Port, 8% for canal transit fees, and 15% for miscellaneous charges. Variance alerts must be generated in both USD base currency and local currency equivalents (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) using the exchange rate locked at the voyage completion date. Each flagged variance must require explicit acknowledgment with a written explanation before the workflow can advance to the next approval stage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R006	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Time Charter Settlement Statement Generation	The system must generate a comprehensive Time Charter settlement statement in a standardized format acceptable to both owners and charterers in the Gulf and Indian shipping markets. The statement must itemize gross hire, off-hire deductions with clause references, bunker adjustments at delivery and redelivery, address commission, brokerage, speed and consumption claims, hold cleaning costs, port expenses for owner's account, and any other adjustments specified in the charter party. The statement must display amounts in the charter party currency (USD) with equivalent values in the entity's functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The statement must be renderable in both English and Arabic with proper RTL formatting for Arabic versions, and must include a reconciliation summary showing the net amount payable or receivable. PDF export must support both A4 and letter page sizes with entity letterhead branding.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R007	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Owner-Charterer Balance Tracking and Payment Scheduling	The system must maintain a running balance account between the operating entity and each vessel owner or charterer, tracking all hire payments made, deductions applied, and settlement adjustments across multiple charter periods. The balance must be denominated in the charter party currency with real-time conversion to entity functional currencies at prevailing exchange rates sourced from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, or RBI as applicable. Payment schedules must be generated based on charter party payment terms (typically 15 days in advance for hire, 30 days for settlement balances) with automated reminders and SWIFT payment instruction generation. The system must support netting of counter-claims between owner and charterer where both parties have amounts payable, subject to configurable netting agreement flags. Outstanding balance aging must be tracked in 30/60/90/120+ day buckets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R008	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Charter Party Clause Interpretation Engine	The AI engine must parse and interpret key charter party clauses relevant to TA settlement including off-hire clauses (NYPE Clause 17 variants), performance warranty clauses, bunker adjustment clauses, and payment terms. The engine must extract structured parameters from semi-structured clause text: daily hire rate, off-hire triggers, speed warranties (laden and ballast), consumption warranties by fuel grade, weather exclusion thresholds, and commission percentages. Extracted parameters must be presented to the user for validation before being applied to settlement calculations. The engine must maintain a library of standard charter party forms (NYPE 2015, BOXTIME 2004, GENTIME) and identify deviations from standard wording. Confidence scores must be displayed for each extracted parameter, with any score below 90% triggering mandatory human verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R009	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Multi-Currency Hire Payment Reconciliation	The system must reconcile hire payments received or made against the expected hire schedule defined in the charter party. Each payment must be matched to the corresponding hire period with tolerance for timing differences up to the charter party's grace period (typically 3-5 banking days). Payments received in currencies other than the charter party currency must be converted at the exchange rate on the date of receipt, and any FX differential must be recorded as a separate settlement item. The system must handle scenarios common in Gulf shipping where hire is invoiced in USD but settled via QAR or AED transfers through local banks, requiring reconciliation of conversion rates applied by intermediary banks against agreed benchmark rates. Unmatched or partially matched payments must be flagged for investigation with AI-suggested matching candidates based on amount proximity and timing analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R010	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Off-Hire Event Documentation and Evidence Management	The system must provide a structured repository for off-hire event documentation supporting each deduction in the TA settlement. Required documentation per off-hire event includes master's notice of off-hire commencement and completion, chief engineer's technical report, classification society or surveyor reports where applicable, port authority records for port-related delays at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, and photographs or video evidence for physical damage events. Each document must be tagged with the off-hire event reference, uploaded with version control, and linked to the corresponding charter party off-hire clause. The system must enforce minimum documentation requirements before an off-hire deduction can be finalized, with configurable rules per charter party type. AI must auto-classify uploaded documents by type and extract key dates and amounts for cross-validation against the off-hire log.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R011	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	TC Settlement Dispute Resolution Workflow	The system must provide a structured dispute resolution workflow for contested items in the TA settlement. Either party (owner or charterer) must be able to flag specific line items as disputed with a mandatory reason code (calculation error, clause interpretation disagreement, missing documentation, rate dispute) and supporting commentary. Disputed items must be segregated from agreed items, allowing partial settlement of undisputed amounts while disputes are resolved. The workflow must support multiple resolution stages: initial review, counter-proposal, negotiation, and final determination (including arbitration reference where applicable under London or Singapore arbitration rules commonly used in Gulf shipping). The system must track dispute aging, estimated financial exposure, and resolution history. The AI engine must suggest resolution approaches based on precedent analysis from previously resolved disputes in the organization's history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R012	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Voyage Charter Equivalent Comparison	The system must calculate the Voyage Charter Equivalent (VCE) or Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) for each settlement period to enable comparison between time charter arrangements and hypothetical voyage charter alternatives. The calculation must derive net daily earnings by deducting voyage costs (bunkers, port charges at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, canal tolls) from gross freight revenue and dividing by voyage days. TCE must be computed at both the individual voyage level and aggregated across the charter period. The system must benchmark the achieved TCE against market rates sourced from Baltic Exchange indices or Clarksons data for comparable vessel classes and trade routes. The AI engine must generate advisory insights on whether the charter arrangement is outperforming or underperforming market rates, supporting commercial decisions on charter extensions or redelivery timing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R013	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Ballast Bonus and Delivery Expense Settlement	The system must handle settlement of ballast bonus payments and delivery/redelivery expenses as specified in the charter party. Ballast bonus amounts (lump sum or calculated based on ballast distance and daily rate) must be amortized over the charter period for accounting purposes per IFRS 16 or entity-applicable standards. Delivery expenses including canal transit fees, bunker costs for positioning voyages, and agency fees at the delivery port must be allocated between owner and charterer accounts based on charter party terms. For vessels delivering at Gulf ports (Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port) or Indian ports (JNPT, Mundra), the system must capture port-specific delivery inspection costs and pre-delivery condition survey fees. The settlement must clearly segregate one-time delivery/redelivery items from recurring hire charges for correct financial period allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R014	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Hold Cleaning and Condition Settlement	The system must manage settlement of hold cleaning and vessel condition costs arising at charter redelivery. The system must record the vessel's condition at delivery (on-hire survey) and redelivery (off-hire survey) with structured condition reports covering cargo holds, tank coatings, deck equipment, and hull condition. Cleaning costs must be allocated based on charter party terms—typically charterer bears cleaning costs if the vessel is redelivered in a condition below the on-hire standard. The system must support common Gulf and Indian port scenarios where hold cleaning between cargo grades (e.g., from sulphur to grain) requires specialized cleaning at facilities in Jebel Ali or Mumbai. Cost estimates from multiple cleaning service providers must be comparable within the system, and the AI engine must recommend the most cost-effective option based on historical data, current port availability, and vessel schedule constraints.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R015	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Time Charter Settlement Accounting Integration	The system must generate accounting entries for all TA settlement components and post them to the appropriate general ledger accounts in the entity's chart of accounts. Hire expense or income must be recognized on a straight-line basis over the hire period per IFRS 16 lease accounting requirements for time charters meeting the lease definition. Off-hire deductions must be posted as reductions to hire expense/income in the period they relate to, not the period of settlement. Bunker adjustments must be posted to bunker inventory and consumption accounts with appropriate cost center and voyage allocation tags. Commission and brokerage must be accrued at the time of hire recognition and settled against the accrual upon payment. All postings must carry the charter party reference, settlement period, and voyage linkage as mandatory dimensions. Multi-entity settlements must generate inter-company entries where the chartering entity differs from the vessel-operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R016	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	Hijri Calendar Settlement Period Alignment	The system must support settlement period definition and reporting aligned to both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, with Hijri alignment being particularly critical for KSA operations where financial reporting may follow the Hijri fiscal year. Settlement periods must be definable in either calendar system with automatic date conversion for cross-referencing. Hire calculations spanning Hijri month boundaries must correctly compute the number of days using the Umm al-Qura calendar officially adopted in Saudi Arabia. Reports must display dates in both calendar systems simultaneously with the primary calendar determined by entity configuration (Hijri primary for KSA, Gregorian primary for Qatar, UAE, and India). Weekend definitions must respect entity-specific working days: Friday-Saturday for Qatar/KSA/UAE and Saturday-Sunday for India, affecting banking day calculations for payment due dates and grace periods in the settlement schedule.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-002-R017	FEAT-056-1-002	MOD-056	AI-Powered Settlement Anomaly Detection	The AI engine must analyze each TA settlement for anomalies and potential errors before finalization. Detection rules must include: hire rate deviations from charter party terms, off-hire duration inconsistencies with vessel tracking data (AIS-based position verification for Gulf waters), bunker pricing outside market corridors for Fujairah and Gulf bunkering hubs, commission calculations deviating from charter party percentages, duplicate deduction entries, and settlement amounts inconsistent with historical patterns for the same vessel or charterer. Each anomaly must be scored by financial impact and confidence level, with high-impact anomalies (exceeding USD 10,000) requiring mandatory review before settlement finalization. The system must present anomalies in a prioritized review queue with one-click access to supporting data and recommended corrective actions, available in both Arabic and English interfaces.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R001	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Structure and Chart of Accounts Mapping	The system must generate a Voyage P&L statement following a standardized shipping industry structure with configurable account mapping to each entity's chart of accounts. The P&L structure must include revenue sections (freight revenue by cargo type, demurrage income, detention charges, container leasing income, reefer monitoring fees), voyage cost sections (bunker costs, port charges, canal dues, stevedoring, cargo claims provisions), and vessel operating cost allocations (daily running cost, insurance, crew wages proportional allocation). Each P&L line item must map to one or more general ledger accounts specific to the operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, or India). The mapping must support entity-specific cost categorizations required by local tax authorities (ZATCA in KSA, CBDT in India) while maintaining consolidated group reporting consistency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R002	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Revenue Recognition and Allocation per Voyage	The system must finalize revenue recognition for each voyage in compliance with IFRS 15 (Revenue from Contracts with Customers) principles applicable to container shipping. Freight revenue must be recognized over the voyage duration using the output method (percentage of voyage completion based on distance sailed). For voyages spanning multiple reporting periods, the system must compute period-specific revenue based on the vessel's position at period-end relative to the total voyage distance. Revenue must be allocated across voyage legs for multi-port itineraries (e.g., Hamad Port to Jebel Ali to JNPT) using the relative standalone selling price methodology. The system must handle revenue adjustments for short-shipped cargo, container weight discrepancies, and freight rate amendments post-booking. All revenue figures must be recorded in the booking currency and converted to entity functional currency at the transaction date exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R003	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Cost Allocation Methodology for Shared Voyages	The system must implement configurable cost allocation methodologies for voyages involving multiple entities or cost-sharing arrangements. Allocation bases must include TEU-miles (container volume multiplied by distance carried), revenue proportional allocation, time-based allocation for vessel operating costs, and activity-based costing for port-specific charges. For joint service agreements common on Gulf-India trade lanes, the system must allocate slot costs and revenues between consortium partners based on the agreed slot allocation matrix. Transfer pricing for inter-entity cargo movements between Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities must comply with arm's length principles per OECD guidelines and local regulations. The AI engine must recommend the most appropriate allocation methodology based on the voyage characteristics and flag scenarios where different methodologies produce materially different results (variance exceeding 5% of voyage contribution).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R004	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Variance Analysis Against Estimate	The system must perform automated variance analysis comparing the finalized Voyage P&L against the pre-voyage estimate (voyage proforma) and any mid-voyage revised estimates. Variances must be computed at each P&L line item level and categorized by cause: volume variance (actual vs estimated cargo volume), rate variance (actual vs estimated freight rates or cost rates), efficiency variance (actual vs estimated bunker consumption or port time), and exchange rate variance. Each material variance (exceeding configurable thresholds per line item) must be annotated with an AI-generated explanation referencing specific operational events from the voyage log. The analysis must present both absolute and percentage variances, with drill-down capability from summary to individual transaction level. Historical variance patterns must be analyzed to identify systematic estimation biases that should be corrected in future voyage proformas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R005	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Multi-Level P&L Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for Voyage P&L finalization, with approval levels determined by the voyage's financial magnitude and variance significance. Default approval levels must include: Level 1—Trade Manager approval for voyages within budget tolerance (variance within 10%); Level 2—Commercial Director approval for voyages with material negative variance (10-25% below estimate); Level 3—CFO approval for voyages with significant loss or variance exceeding 25%. Each approver must be able to approve, reject with comments, or conditionally approve with adjustment requests. The workflow must support parallel approval paths for multi-entity voyages where each entity's finance team must independently approve their entity-specific P&L components. Approval delegation rules must allow temporary authority transfer during absence periods with full audit trail of delegated approvals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R006	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Consolidation for Fleet and Trade Lane Reporting	The system must support consolidation of individual Voyage P&Ls into aggregate views by vessel, trade lane, service string, entity, and fleet. Consolidation must eliminate inter-entity transactions such as feeder charges between Jebel Ali hub and Hamad Port spokes, slot swap arrangements, and internal container repositioning costs. The consolidated P&L must present figures in both entity functional currencies and a common group reporting currency (USD) using month-end exchange rates from entity-designated central banks. Trade lane profitability views must aggregate all voyages on specific corridors (e.g., Gulf-India, Intra-Gulf) showing revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, and contribution margin trends over rolling 12-month periods. The AI engine must identify underperforming trade lanes and suggest commercial actions such as rate adjustments, service frequency changes, or capacity redeployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R007	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Cargo Claims Provision in Voyage P&L	The system must incorporate cargo claims provisions into the Voyage P&L based on known claims, IBNR (incurred but not reported) estimates, and historical claims experience for the trade lane. Known claims must be valued at the current best estimate of settlement amount plus legal and survey costs. IBNR provisions must be calculated using actuarial methods based on the entity's claims development triangle, with separate factors for different cargo types (dry containers, reefer cargo, hazardous goods, high-value commodities transiting Jebel Ali Free Zone). The claims provision methodology must comply with IFRS 37 (Provisions, Contingent Liabilities) and local insurance regulatory requirements. For voyages with P&I Club coverage, the system must track deductible thresholds and identify claims likely to exceed the deductible for separate accounting treatment. Claims recovery receivables from sub-contractors, terminals, or other carriers must be separately tracked and netted against gross claims expense.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R008	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Report Generation in Bilingual Format	The system must generate the finalized Voyage P&L report in both English and Arabic with full RTL support for the Arabic version. The report must include a header section with voyage details (vessel name, voyage number, trade lane, load and discharge ports using UN/LOCODE format), a P&L summary section with revenue, costs, and contribution margin, a detailed breakdown section with individual line items, a variance analysis section with commentary, and an approval history section with digital signatures. Numeric formatting must follow locale conventions: English format (1,234,567.89) and Arabic format with Eastern Arabic numerals option (١٬٢٣٤٬٥٦٧٫٨٩). Currency symbols must display correctly in both LTR and RTL contexts. The report must be exportable as PDF (bilingual side-by-side or separate documents), Excel (with formulas preserved for further analysis), and structured JSON for system integration. Hijri dates must appear alongside Gregorian dates in the report header.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R009	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	AI-Driven P&L Optimization Recommendations	The AI engine must analyze the finalized Voyage P&L and generate actionable optimization recommendations for future voyages on the same trade lane. Recommendations must cover: bunker procurement strategies based on price differentials between Fujairah, Hamad Port, and Indian bunkering ports; optimal speed profiles to minimize combined bunker and time costs; port rotation optimization to reduce ballast legs and maximize cargo intake; terminal selection based on cost efficiency and productivity at alternative terminals within the same port complex (e.g., multiple terminals at JNPT); and cargo mix optimization to maximize revenue per slot. Each recommendation must quantify the estimated financial impact in USD per voyage and annualized savings for the trade lane. Recommendations must be ranked by feasibility (easy/medium/hard implementation) and payback period. The AI must learn from which recommendations are accepted or rejected by commercial managers and adjust future suggestions accordingly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R010	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Demurrage and Detention Revenue Finalization	The system must finalize demurrage and detention revenue for the voyage based on container tracking data from all ports of call. Free time allocations must be applied per the tariff applicable to each port and customer agreement, with separate calculations for import demurrage (at discharge ports like JNPT), export demurrage (at load ports like Hamad Port), and detention (off-terminal container usage). The system must reconcile calculated demurrage against amounts invoiced and collected, identifying write-offs, waivers granted by commercial teams, and disputed amounts. Waiver approvals must be linked to the authorizing manager with the waiver reason code and financial impact recorded in the voyage P&L. For multi-entity operations, demurrage revenue must be attributed to the entity responsible for the port operation where the demurrage was incurred. The AI engine must flag unusual patterns such as excessive waivers for specific customers or ports that may indicate commercial policy issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R011	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Lock and Period Close Integration	The system must implement a P&L locking mechanism that freezes the Voyage P&L once approved, preventing further modifications without a formal unlock and re-approval cycle. The lock must be synchronized with the entity's financial period close process—voyages closing within a financial period must have their P&L locked before the period can be closed. For voyages spanning multiple periods, the system must generate and lock period-specific P&L snapshots representing the revenue and cost recognized in each period. The locking mechanism must support entity-specific lock dates that may differ across the Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Post-lock adjustments must be processed as prior period items in the subsequent open period, clearly tagged as voyage P&L amendments. The system must provide a pre-lock validation report listing all incomplete items, unreconciled amounts, and pending approvals that would prevent P&L finalization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R012	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Data Retention and Archival	The system must implement a data retention policy for finalized Voyage P&L records compliant with the most stringent applicable regulation across operating jurisdictions. Retention periods must meet or exceed: Qatar Commercial Law (10 years), UAE Commercial Companies Law (5 years from last entry), KSA ZATCA requirements (retention until assessment finality plus appeal period), and India Companies Act 2013 (8 financial years). Archived P&L records must remain queryable and retrievable with all supporting documentation including invoices, accrual calculations, approval chains, and variance analyses. The archival mechanism must support both online (immediate access for recent 3 years) and nearline (retrieval within 4 hours for older records) storage tiers. Archived records must preserve the exact P&L presentation as at the time of finalization, including exchange rates used, approval timestamps, and any post-lock amendments. Data sovereignty requirements must be respected—KSA entity data must reside within KSA borders per NDMO regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R013	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Intermodal Cost Integration in Voyage P&L	The system must integrate intermodal transportation costs into the Voyage P&L for door-to-door shipments where the shipping entity provides inland logistics. Inland transport costs must cover truck haulage from ICD (Inland Container Depot) to port for export cargo at JNPT or from port to ICD for import cargo, rail freight charges for container movement on dedicated freight corridors in India, and last-mile delivery costs in Doha, Dubai, Jeddah, and Mumbai metropolitan areas. Each intermodal cost must be matched to the originating bill of lading and voyage, with cost allocation to the correct voyage leg. For CIF/DDP INCOTERMS shipments, the system must ensure all seller-borne costs including customs clearance, inland freight, and insurance are captured in the voyage P&L. Multi-modal carrier invoices must be split between ocean and inland components using activity-based cost drivers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R014	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Vessel Operating Cost Daily Rate Allocation	The system must allocate vessel operating costs to individual voyages using a configurable daily rate methodology. The daily operating cost (OPEX) rate must be derived from the vessel's annual budget divided by expected operating days (365 minus planned off-hire days for drydocking and maintenance). OPEX components allocated must include crew wages and provisions, insurance premiums (H&M, P&I Club calls), maintenance and repairs, lubricants, stores and spares, and vessel management fees. For owned vessels, depreciation and finance costs must be separately allocated as below-the-line items in the voyage P&L. For time-chartered vessels, the daily hire rate replaces the OPEX allocation. The system must handle mid-voyage rate changes due to crew rotations (common at Jebel Ali crew change anchorage), insurance renewals, or budget revisions. The AI engine must compare allocated OPEX against industry benchmarks for vessel type and age class, flagging vessels with above-average operating costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R015	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Tax Provision Computation in Voyage P&L	The system must compute applicable tax provisions for the voyage P&L based on the operating entity's tax jurisdiction and the voyage's trading pattern. Tax computations must handle: India corporate tax provisions for voyages earning Indian-source income with tonnage tax scheme election per Section 115V-115VZC of the Income Tax Act, KSA Zakat provisions for Saudi entity voyage profits calculated per ZATCA guidelines, Qatar tax provisions under the Income Tax Law (Law No. 24 of 2018) with reduced rates for listed entities, and UAE corporate tax provisions effective from June 2023 including free zone qualifying income rules applicable to Jebel Ali Free Zone entities. The system must identify voyages with profit splits across multiple tax jurisdictions and compute the tax impact of each jurisdiction's claim on voyage income. Transfer pricing adjustments from inter-entity transactions must be reflected in the tax provision computation. The AI engine must optimize voyage allocation to minimize group tax exposure within legal boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-003-R016	FEAT-056-1-003	MOD-056	Voyage P&L Comparison and Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide an interactive dashboard for comparing Voyage P&L results across multiple dimensions with full bilingual support (Arabic and English). Comparison views must include: voyage-over-voyage for the same vessel showing performance trends, trade lane comparison showing relative profitability of Gulf-India vs Intra-Gulf vs other corridors, vessel class comparison identifying the most efficient vessel type for each trade lane, and budget-vs-actual comparison for fleet-wide financial performance monitoring. The dashboard must support dynamic filtering by entity, vessel, trade lane, cargo type, time period (Gregorian and Hijri), and P&L line item. Visualization must include contribution margin waterfall charts, cost composition treemaps, and trend sparklines for key KPIs (revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, TCE, voyage margin percentage). All charts must render correctly in both LTR and RTL layouts with culturally appropriate color schemes. The AI engine must highlight statistically significant outliers and provide natural language summaries in the user's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R001	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Hire Statement Ingestion and Parsing	The system must ingest hire statements received from vessel owners or charterers in multiple formats including PDF, Excel, CSV, and EDI (BAPLIE/IFTMCS message types used in container shipping). The AI-powered parser must extract key data fields from unstructured or semi-structured hire statements: charter party reference, vessel name and IMO number, hire period (from/to dates), daily hire rate, total hire amount, off-hire deductions with dates and reasons, bunker adjustments, commission deductions, and net amount due. The parser must handle varying statement formats from different owners and charterers without requiring format-specific templates, achieving a minimum 92% field extraction accuracy on first pass. Extracted data must be presented for user validation with AI-highlighted fields where confidence is below 95%. Statements in Arabic must be parsed with equivalent accuracy to English-language statements using bilingual NLP models trained on Gulf shipping terminology.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R002	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Automated Three-Way Hire Reconciliation	The system must perform automated three-way reconciliation matching the owner's hire statement against the charterer's internal hire calculation and the underlying charter party terms. Reconciliation must be performed at the line-item level covering: hire days (comparing owner's count vs charterer's count vs calendar days), daily rate (comparing stated rate vs charter party rate vs any agreed escalation formula), off-hire periods (comparing owner's off-hire log vs charterer's off-hire records vs vessel AIS tracking data for position verification), bunker quantities and prices (comparing owner's figures vs charterer's records vs surveyor reports), and commission rates (comparing applied rate vs charter party terms). Each line item must be classified as: matched (within tolerance), matched with variance (within acceptable threshold), or mismatched (requiring investigation). Tolerance thresholds must be configurable per entity and charter party type with defaults of 0.5% for amounts and 2 hours for time periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R003	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Discrepancy Identification and Classification	The system must automatically identify and classify all discrepancies found during hire statement reconciliation. Discrepancy categories must include: rate discrepancies (hire rate applied differs from charter party terms), period discrepancies (hire days counted differently due to delivery/redelivery timing disputes), off-hire discrepancies (disagreement on off-hire events, duration, or deduction calculation), bunker discrepancies (quantity, price, or grade valuation differences at delivery/redelivery), commission discrepancies (percentage or base amount differences), and calculation errors (arithmetic mistakes in the statement). Each discrepancy must be valued in both the charter party currency (typically USD) and the entity functional currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR). The AI engine must assess whether each discrepancy likely favors the owner or charterer, compute the net financial impact, and prioritize discrepancies by materiality. Discrepancies below a configurable de minimis threshold (default USD 500) must be auto-resolved in favor of the least administrative effort.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R004	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Dispute Initiation and Tracking Workflow	The system must provide a structured dispute initiation workflow for hire statement discrepancies that cannot be resolved through automated reconciliation. The dispute record must capture: the original hire statement reference, specific disputed items with line-item granularity, the entity's position with supporting calculation and documentation, the counterparty's position as stated in their hire statement, the financial value in dispute in both charter party and functional currencies, the applicable charter party clause reference, and the assigned dispute handler from the chartering or finance team. Disputes must be categorized by type and severity with configurable SLA timelines for resolution (default: minor disputes within 30 days, major disputes within 90 days). The system must generate a unique dispute reference number following entity-specific numbering conventions and maintain a dispute register accessible to authorized users across all four operating entities for cross-referencing related disputes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R005	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Counterparty Communication Management	The system must facilitate structured communication with counterparties (vessel owners, disponent owners, or head charterers) regarding hire statement discrepancies and disputes. Communication must be managed through templated correspondence in English and Arabic, with templates covering initial discrepancy notification, request for supporting documentation, counter-proposal submission, and settlement confirmation. Each communication must be linked to the specific dispute record and stored in the system with full version history. The system must support communication via email integration (with sent/received tracking), in-platform messaging for counterparties with system access, and formal letter generation with entity letterhead for legal correspondence. Communication templates must include entity-specific disclaimers, legal jurisdiction references (typically English law with London arbitration for Gulf shipping or Indian law for JNPT-related disputes), and without-prejudice markers where appropriate. The AI engine must draft initial response communications based on the dispute type and entity's historical resolution approach.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R006	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Hire Statement Reconciliation Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive hire statement reconciliation dashboard showing the status of all hire statements across the four operating entities. The dashboard must display: total hire statements received and pending reconciliation, statements reconciled with no discrepancies, statements with identified discrepancies pending review, statements with active disputes, and statements fully reconciled and settled. Financial summary tiles must show total hire value under reconciliation, total discrepancy value, total disputed amount, and aging analysis of open items. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel, owner/charterer, charter type (time charter in/out), and date range in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. All dashboard elements must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with properly mirrored charts, right-aligned labels, and Eastern Arabic numeral options. The AI engine must provide trend analysis commentary and early warnings for deteriorating reconciliation cycle times.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R004	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Voyage result status lifecycle management	The system must enforce a strict status lifecycle for voyage results: Draft, Under Review, Operations Approved, Finance Approved, Management Approved, Closed, and Archived. Transitions between statuses must be governed by role-based permissions where only users with the VOYAGE_OPS_APPROVER role can move from Under Review to Operations Approved, and only VOYAGE_FIN_APPROVER can advance to Finance Approved. The system must prevent any cost or revenue modifications once a voyage reaches Closed status. Reopening a Closed voyage must require C-level authorization and generate a high-priority audit event with notification to all entity finance controllers across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R007	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Off-Hire Dispute Evidence Correlation	The system must support evidence correlation for off-hire disputes by cross-referencing multiple data sources to establish or refute off-hire claims. Data sources for correlation must include: vessel AIS position data confirming whether the vessel was stationary or moving during claimed off-hire periods in Gulf waters or approaching Indian ports, engine room log extracts from vessel reporting systems showing machinery status, port authority records from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT confirming berthing and unberthing times, weather data from meteorological services for the Arabian Sea and Persian Gulf validating weather delay claims, classification society records for scheduled and unscheduled surveys, and independent surveyor reports for condition-related off-hire. The AI engine must automatically correlate these sources, identify supporting and contradicting evidence for each off-hire claim, and generate an evidence strength assessment scored from 1-10 for both the owner's and charterer's positions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R008	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Partial Settlement Processing for Disputed Statements	The system must support partial settlement of hire statements where some line items are agreed while others remain in dispute. The partial settlement mechanism must allow the finance team to approve payment of the undisputed portion while ring-fencing the disputed amount in a suspense account. Accounting entries for partial settlement must clearly distinguish between settled amounts (posted to hire expense/income accounts) and suspended amounts (posted to dispute suspense accounts with charter party and dispute references). The system must track the running balance of disputed amounts per counterparty and per charter party, updating the suspense balance as disputes are resolved. For multi-currency scenarios where hire is in USD but payment is in QAR or INR, the system must manage FX exposure on disputed amounts separately, marking them at fair value using period-end exchange rates per IAS 21. The AI engine must calculate the financial impact of settlement timing delays and recommend whether early settlement at a discount might be commercially advantageous.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R009	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Arbitration and Legal Proceedings Tracking	The system must track hire statement disputes that escalate to formal arbitration or legal proceedings. The tracking record must capture: arbitration forum (London Maritime Arbitrators Association, Singapore Chamber of Maritime Arbitration, or Indian Maritime Arbitration as applicable), appointed arbitrators, key procedural dates (submission deadlines, hearing dates, award dates), legal counsel details for each party, estimated legal costs and provisions, and interim orders or awards. The system must integrate with the entity's legal case management for document sharing and cost tracking. Financial provisions for potential adverse arbitration outcomes must be computed based on the disputed amount, legal assessment of outcome probability (strong/moderate/weak case), and estimated legal costs. Provisions must be reflected in the Voyage P&L and entity financial statements per IAS 37. The system must alert relevant stakeholders when arbitration deadlines approach, with a minimum 30-day advance warning for submission deadlines and configurable reminder intervals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R010	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Historical Dispute Pattern Analysis	The AI engine must analyze historical hire statement disputes to identify patterns and provide predictive insights. Pattern analysis must cover: dispute frequency by counterparty identifying owners or charterers with consistently high dispute rates, common dispute categories (off-hire interpretation, bunker measurement, rate application) by trade lane and port, average dispute value and resolution duration by category, seasonal patterns in dispute frequency correlated with monsoon seasons affecting Indian port operations or summer heat affecting Gulf port productivity, and correlation between charter party clause complexity and dispute likelihood. The system must generate a counterparty dispute risk score that can inform chartering decisions—flagging owners or charterers with historically high dispute rates when new fixture negotiations commence. Trend reports must be presentable in both Arabic and English with appropriate data visualization supporting RTL rendering. The AI must recommend charter party clause amendments that could reduce future disputes based on historical resolution outcomes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R011	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Hire Statement Reconciliation Audit Compliance	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all hire statement reconciliation activities to satisfy internal and external audit requirements across all four operating jurisdictions. Every reconciliation action must be logged: statement receipt, data extraction, matching attempts (automated and manual), discrepancy identification, dispute initiation, communication with counterparties, resolution decisions, and final settlement approval. The audit trail must capture the acting user, entity affiliation, timestamp in UTC with local time equivalent, action description, and before/after data snapshots for any modifications. For Indian operations, the audit trail must support GST audit requirements under Section 65 of the CGST Act. For KSA operations, records must be structured to support ZATCA e-invoicing audit requirements. The system must generate audit-ready reconciliation reports that map hire statement amounts to general ledger postings with complete transaction lineage. Audit reports must be available in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers and compliant with each jurisdiction's record-keeping language requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R012	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Multi-Entity Hire Statement Consolidation	The system must support consolidation of hire statement reconciliation across multiple entities for group-level reporting and management oversight. Consolidation views must aggregate open reconciliation items, disputed amounts, and settlement balances across Qatar LLC, UAE FZE, KSA WLL, and India Pvt Ltd entities with elimination of inter-entity charter arrangements (e.g., where the Qatar entity charters a vessel from the UAE entity). Consolidated reports must present figures in the group reporting currency (USD) with entity-level breakdowns in respective functional currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The consolidation must handle scenarios where the same vessel's hire statement is relevant to multiple entities due to sub-chartering arrangements, ensuring no double-counting of hire expense or income. Cross-entity dispute exposure must be aggregated with clearly identified contingent liabilities per IAS 37 for group financial statement disclosure purposes. Access controls must respect entity-level data segregation while allowing authorized group-level users to view consolidated information.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R013	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Automated Reconciliation Matching Rules Engine	The system must implement a configurable matching rules engine for automated hire statement reconciliation. Matching rules must be definable at multiple levels: global defaults, entity-specific overrides, counterparty-specific rules, and charter party-specific rules. Rule parameters must include: amount matching tolerance (absolute and percentage), date matching tolerance (days and hours), rounding convention alignment (owner vs charterer rounding practices), commission calculation methodology matching (gross hire vs net hire base), and bunker pricing source precedence (Platts vs Argus vs actual invoice). The rules engine must support conditional logic: for example, if the counterparty is a Japanese owner, apply their typical rounding convention of truncating to whole dollars; if the charter party references NYPE 2015 form, apply the standard off-hire clause interpretation. The AI engine must learn from manual reconciliation decisions to suggest new matching rules that could automate future similar cases. Rule effectiveness must be measured by the percentage of statement value auto-reconciled and presented in monthly performance reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R014	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Interest Calculation on Late Hire Payments	The system must calculate interest on late hire payments based on charter party provisions and applicable law. Interest computation must support multiple methodologies: simple interest at a fixed rate specified in the charter party, compound interest where contractually agreed, LIBOR/SOFR-based floating rate plus spread as commonly used in shipping charter parties, and statutory interest rates per jurisdiction (Qatar Civil Code, UAE Federal Law No. 5, KSA Commercial Court Practice, or Indian Interest Act 1978). The system must automatically determine when a hire payment becomes overdue by comparing the charter party payment due date (typically 15 days in advance of the hire period) against actual payment receipt date from the bank reconciliation module. Interest must be calculated from the due date to the actual payment date, accounting for grace periods specified in the charter party and excluding non-banking days based on the payment currency's banking calendar. The system must generate interest debit/credit notes in the charter party currency with supporting calculation schedules and maintain a running interest balance per charter party.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R015	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Hire Statement Reconciliation Offline Mode	The system must support hire statement reconciliation workflows in offline mode for scenarios where finance team members need to work during travel or in locations with unreliable connectivity, particularly relevant for staff traveling between Gulf offices in Doha, Dubai, and Jeddah. Offline mode must allow downloading a configurable reconciliation package containing: the owner/charterer's hire statement, the entity's internal hire calculation, charter party terms extract, vessel event log for the hire period, and supporting documentation. Users must be able to perform matching, flag discrepancies, add comments, and draft dispute communications offline. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline work with conflict detection for scenarios where multiple users reconciled the same hire statement concurrently. Critical financial data (amounts, dates, rates) must use last-write-wins with mandatory reviewer notification for conflicting changes. Offline work packages must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption and require biometric or PIN authentication for access on mobile devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R016	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Dispute Resolution Financial Impact Reporting	The system must generate comprehensive financial impact reports for hire statement disputes at individual, counterparty, entity, and group levels. Individual dispute reports must show the disputed amount, provision held, legal costs incurred, settlement amount (if resolved), and net financial impact classified as gain or loss. Counterparty-level reports must aggregate all disputes with a specific owner or charterer showing total exposure, resolution rate, average settlement as a percentage of claimed amount, and relationship impact assessment. Entity-level reports must present total dispute exposure by currency (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), dispute provisions as a percentage of total hire expense, and trend analysis over rolling quarters. Group-level reports must consolidate across entities with appropriate eliminations. All reports must be available in Arabic and English with Hijri and Gregorian date support. The AI engine must forecast expected dispute outcomes and timing based on historical resolution patterns, enabling more accurate financial provisions and cash flow projections for each operating entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-1-004-R017	FEAT-056-1-004	MOD-056	Hire Statement Version Control and Comparison	The system must maintain full version control of hire statements throughout the reconciliation lifecycle. When a counterparty submits a revised hire statement, the system must store the new version while preserving all previous versions with their complete reconciliation state. A visual comparison tool must highlight differences between statement versions at the line-item level, showing added, removed, and modified items with the monetary impact of each change. The comparison must support side-by-side display of up to three versions simultaneously (e.g., original owner statement, revised owner statement, and charterer's calculation). Version metadata must capture submission date, source (email, portal upload, EDI), submitter identity, and stated reason for revision. The AI engine must analyze version changes to detect patterns such as owners systematically revising statements to reduce off-hire deductions after initial charterer pushback. The system must alert reconciliation teams when a new statement version is received for a hire period currently under dispute, as the revision may affect the dispute resolution strategy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R001	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Multi-stage voyage result approval workflow	The system must implement a configurable multi-stage approval workflow for voyage results comprising at minimum: operations review, finance review, management approval, and final closure. Each stage must capture the approver identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, approval comments in Arabic or English, and digital signature hash. The workflow must support parallel approvals for multi-entity voyages where Hamad Port (QAHMD), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNHV) operations may require simultaneous sign-off from different entity controllers. Rejected results must return to the originating stage with mandatory rejection reason codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R002	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Comprehensive voyage audit trail logging	The system must maintain an immutable audit trail for every voyage result modification, capturing the before-state and after-state of all changed fields including revenue allocations, cost entries, bunker adjustments, and demurrage charges. Each audit entry must record the user ID, entity code (QA-ENT, AE-ENT, SA-ENT, IN-ENT), IP address, browser fingerprint, timestamp in UTC with entity-local timezone conversion, and the business justification for the change. Audit logs must be retained for a minimum of seven years in compliance with Qatar Financial Centre Authority and UAE Federal Tax Authority requirements and must be exportable in PDF and CSV formats with Arabic RTL layout support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R005	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Automatic revenue recognition trigger on closure	Upon voyage result closure, the system must automatically trigger revenue recognition entries in the general ledger module for each participating entity. Revenue must be allocated based on the proportional leg contribution method where each port pair (e.g., Hamad Port to JNPT, JNPT to Jebel Ali) receives its proportional share of total voyage freight revenue. The system must generate separate journal entries per entity with the correct local currency denomination, applying the closing exchange rate from the Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, or Reserve Bank of India as applicable. IFRS 15 five-step revenue recognition compliance checks must be validated before posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R006	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Voyage result document attachment management	The system must support attachment of supporting documents to voyage results including scanned port disbursement accounts, bunker delivery notes, statement of facts, cargo manifests, bills of lading copies, and surveyor reports. Each attachment must be tagged with document type, port code (QAHMD, AEKHL, AEJEA, INNHV), voyage leg reference, and upload timestamp. The system must enforce mandatory document checklists per port where voyage closure cannot proceed without all required documents attached. Documents must support Arabic filenames and metadata, with OCR-based content extraction for AI-assisted validation of amounts against entered voyage cost figures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R007	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Delegation and escalation rules for approvals	The system must support configurable delegation rules allowing approvers to designate temporary delegates during absence periods defined by Gregorian or Hijri date ranges. Delegation must be restricted to users within the same entity and with equivalent or higher authorization levels. If an approval request remains pending beyond a configurable SLA period (default 48 hours for operations review, 72 hours for finance review), the system must automatically escalate to the next-level manager and send notifications via email and in-app alert in the user's preferred language (Arabic or English). Escalation chains must be entity-specific, supporting different hierarchies for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R008	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Voyage result locking and concurrent edit prevention	The system must implement optimistic locking on voyage result records to prevent concurrent edits by multiple users. When a user opens a voyage result for editing, the system must acquire a soft lock displaying the lock holder name, entity, and lock acquisition time. If a second user attempts to edit the same voyage result, the system must display a bilingual warning (Arabic and English) identifying the current editor and offering a read-only view option. Locks must automatically expire after 30 minutes of inactivity. The system must handle offline scenarios where users at remote port locations may have intermittent connectivity, queuing changes for synchronization upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R009	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Configurable voyage result templates per trade lane	The system must allow configuration of voyage result templates specific to trade lanes such as Arabian Gulf to Indian Subcontinent, Intra-Gulf (Hamad Port to Jebel Ali to Khalifa Port), and India coastal routes. Each template must pre-populate expected cost categories, default port disbursement structures, typical bunker consumption rates, and applicable regulatory surcharges (e.g., Indian Goods and Services Tax, UAE VAT at 5%, KSA VAT at 15%, Qatar customs duties). Templates must define which cost line items are mandatory versus optional and establish default approval workflows. Template management must be restricted to users with the VOYAGE_TEMPLATE_ADMIN role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R010	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Automated email notifications for workflow transitions	The system must send automated email notifications at each workflow stage transition to all relevant stakeholders. Notification recipients must be configurable per entity and per approval stage, with support for CC and BCC distribution lists. Email content must be rendered in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English) with proper RTL formatting for Arabic recipients. Notifications must include a summary of voyage key metrics: vessel name, voyage number, total revenue, total costs, net result in USD and local currency, and a deep link to the voyage result detail page. The system must maintain a notification delivery log and retry failed deliveries up to three times with exponential backoff.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R011	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Voyage result comparison across estimate revisions	The system must provide a side-by-side comparison view showing the original voyage estimate, any mid-voyage estimate revisions, and the final actual voyage result. The comparison must be available at both summary and line-item detail levels, with color-coded variance indicators (green for favorable, red for unfavorable) applied consistently across cost categories including port charges at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Currency conversions must use the exchange rates applicable at each estimate revision date and the final result date. The comparison report must be exportable in Excel format with Arabic column headers for GCC entity users and English headers for India entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R012	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Regulatory compliance validation before closure	Before allowing voyage result closure, the system must validate compliance with jurisdiction-specific regulatory requirements. For Qatar operations, the system must verify that Qatar Customs Authority clearance references are recorded for all cargo discharged at Hamad Port. For UAE operations, FTA tax invoice references must be linked. For KSA operations, ZATCA e-invoicing compliance must be confirmed. For India operations, JNPT customs duty payment references and GST reconciliation status must be verified. The system must present a compliance checklist dashboard showing pass or fail status for each regulatory requirement, blocking closure if any mandatory check fails and providing remediation guidance in Arabic or English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R013	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Bulk voyage result processing capability	The system must support bulk operations for processing multiple voyage results simultaneously, enabling operations managers to advance up to 50 voyage results through a single workflow stage in one action. Bulk processing must validate each voyage result individually and report per-voyage success or failure outcomes. Failed voyages must not block successful ones in the batch. The bulk operation must generate a consolidated audit trail entry referencing all affected voyage numbers, with individual audit entries created per voyage. The UI must display a progress indicator during bulk processing and support Arabic and English status messages. Bulk operations must respect entity-level permissions, preventing cross-entity bulk approvals unless the user holds a multi-entity authorization role.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R014	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Voyage result amendment after closure process	The system must provide a controlled amendment process for voyage results that have already been closed, required when late-arriving invoices from port agents at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT necessitate cost adjustments. Amendments must create a new version of the voyage result linked to the original, with all changes tracked in the audit trail. The amendment must trigger a differential journal entry in the general ledger reflecting only the changed amounts in the appropriate local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Amendments must go through an abbreviated approval workflow requiring finance controller and management approval. The system must maintain a complete version history accessible through a timeline view showing all amendments with their business justifications.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R015	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Role-based voyage result visibility and data masking	The system must enforce role-based access control determining which voyage result data elements are visible to each user role. Operations staff must see operational metrics (port days, bunker consumption, container counts) but not financial margins. Finance staff must see all cost and revenue figures but may have restricted access to operational details of other entities. Management must have full visibility across all entities. Sensitive fields such as commission rates, rebate percentages, and intercompany transfer pricing must be masked for users without the VOYAGE_CONFIDENTIAL_ACCESS role. The system must apply data masking consistently across all views, exports, and API responses, with Arabic and English field labels appropriately masked.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-001-R016	FEAT-056-2-001	MOD-056	Offline voyage result data entry and synchronization	The system must support offline data entry for voyage results when users are operating at port locations with limited internet connectivity, particularly relevant for field operations at JNPT and smaller feeder ports. The offline module must cache the current voyage result state locally, allow cost and revenue entry modifications, and queue all changes for synchronization when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution must follow a last-write-wins policy with mandatory user review of any conflicting changes detected during sync. The offline module must store data encrypted at rest using AES-256 and must automatically purge cached data after successful synchronization. Offline capability must support both Arabic and English interface languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R001	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Automated intercompany cost allocation engine	The system must automatically allocate shared voyage costs across participating entities based on configurable allocation methodologies including: proportional TEU volume, proportional revenue share, equal split, and custom weighted allocation. For a voyage calling at Hamad Port (Qatar entity), Jebel Ali (UAE entity), and JNPT (India entity), the system must calculate each entity's share of common voyage costs such as bunker fuel, vessel hire, insurance, and crew costs. Allocation results must be computed in USD as the base currency and converted to each entity's local currency (QAR, AED, INR) using the exchange rate locked at the voyage completion date. Each allocation must generate a detailed breakdown traceable to the source cost entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R002	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Transfer pricing policy enforcement	The system must enforce transfer pricing policies compliant with OECD guidelines and local regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. Intercompany charges must be validated against configured arm's length price ranges for each service category including slot chartering, feeder transportation, container repositioning, and agency services. The system must flag any intercompany transaction where the applied rate deviates by more than a configurable percentage (default 10%) from the arm's length benchmark. Flagged transactions must require transfer pricing officer approval before settlement. The system must maintain a transfer pricing documentation package per fiscal year per entity pair, exportable in both Arabic and English for tax authority submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R003	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany settlement invoice generation	The system must generate intercompany settlement invoices for each entity pair involved in a voyage. Invoices must comply with the invoicing requirements of the billing entity's jurisdiction: UAE FTA tax invoice format with TRN number for UAE entities, ZATCA-compliant e-invoice for KSA entities, GST tax invoice with GSTIN for India entities, and Qatar commercial invoice format. Each invoice must itemize all allocated cost components with voyage reference, vessel name, port pair, container counts by type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), and applicable tax calculations. Invoices must be generated in dual language (Arabic and English) for GCC entities and in English for India entities, with amounts shown in both USD and local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R004	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Multilateral netting of intercompany balances	The system must support multilateral netting of intercompany balances across all entity pairs to minimize the number of actual cash settlements required. The netting engine must calculate the net receivable or payable position for each entity considering all outstanding intercompany invoices across multiple voyages. Netting cycles must be configurable (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly) with support for both Gregorian and Hijri calendar scheduling. The netting statement must present each entity's gross receivables, gross payables, and net settlement amount in USD and local currency. The system must generate a netting confirmation document requiring electronic sign-off from the finance controller of each participating entity before triggering payment instructions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R005	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany reconciliation dashboard	The system must provide a real-time intercompany reconciliation dashboard showing the status of all intercompany transactions per voyage and per entity pair. The dashboard must display matched entries (where both entities have recorded the transaction), unmatched entries (recorded by one entity but not the counterparty), and disputed entries (where amounts differ between entities). For each discrepancy, the system must show the variance amount in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR as applicable. The dashboard must support filtering by voyage number, entity pair, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), cost category, and settlement status. Arabic RTL layout must be fully supported with bidirectional text rendering for mixed Arabic-English voyage references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R003	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Fleet Mix Optimization Scenario Engine	The system must provide a scenario modeling engine that allows users to define multiple fleet mix scenarios varying the number and type of vessels (container feeder 500-2000 TEU, intermediate 2000-5000 TEU, Panamax, Post-Panamax) assigned to trade routes between Gulf and Indian subcontinent ports. Each scenario must calculate projected revenue, operating costs, capital requirements, and return on invested capital (ROIC) over configurable time horizons. The engine must support at least 50 concurrent scenarios per user session, with AI-driven ranking of scenarios by net present value, internal rate of return, and payback period, accounting for multi-currency cash flows in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R006	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Automated intercompany journal entry generation	Upon settlement approval, the system must automatically generate corresponding journal entries in both the billing and receiving entity's general ledgers. The debit entry in the receiving entity must reference the intercompany payable account mapped to the billing entity, while the credit entry in the billing entity must reference the intercompany receivable account. Journal entries must use the entity's local chart of accounts and currency, with the exchange rate and any foreign exchange gain or loss recorded separately. For India entity transactions, the system must generate TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) entries where applicable under Section 194C or 194J of the Indian Income Tax Act. All journal entries must carry the voyage number, vessel code, and intercompany settlement reference as tracking dimensions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R007	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Slot charter cost sharing calculation	The system must calculate slot charter costs between entities sharing vessel capacity on a voyage. When the Qatar entity charters slots on a vessel operated by the UAE entity for the Hamad Port to Jebel Ali leg, the system must compute the slot cost based on the agreed slot rate per TEU multiplied by the actual number of slots utilized. The calculation must account for different container types with conversion factors (one 40-foot container equals two TEU, one 45-foot container equals 2.25 TEU). Empty repositioning containers must be allocated at a configurable discount rate (default 50% of laden rate). The system must reconcile slot utilization against the slot charter agreement terms and flag any over-allocation or under-utilization beyond agreed minimum quantity commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R008	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Currency conversion and FX exposure management	The system must handle multi-currency intercompany settlements with support for USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. Exchange rates must be sourced from configurable rate providers (Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, Saudi Central Bank, Reserve Bank of India) with daily automatic rate imports. The system must calculate foreign exchange gains and losses arising from timing differences between the transaction date and settlement date for each intercompany invoice. FX exposure reports must be generated per entity showing outstanding intercompany balances by currency with mark-to-market valuations. The system must support forward rate booking for planned settlements and must apply the QAR/USD fixed peg rate of 3.64 and AED/USD fixed peg rate of 3.6725 where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R009	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany dispute resolution workflow	The system must provide a structured dispute resolution workflow for intercompany settlement disagreements. When an entity rejects an intercompany charge, the system must create a dispute record capturing the disputed amount, reason code (rate disagreement, quantity disagreement, allocation methodology disagreement, missing documentation), and supporting evidence attachments. Disputes must be routed to a configurable resolution authority, typically the regional finance director. The workflow must enforce resolution SLAs (default 15 business days) with automatic escalation to the group CFO if unresolved. Resolution outcomes must include: accept as charged, accept with adjustment, split the difference, or write off. All dispute communications must support Arabic and English with full audit trail preservation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R010	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Tax withholding calculation for cross-border settlements	The system must automatically calculate applicable tax withholding on intercompany payments based on the jurisdiction of the paying and receiving entities and the nature of the service. For payments from the India entity to any GCC entity, the system must apply Indian withholding tax rates under the applicable Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA), currently 10% for technical services and 15% for royalties under the India-Qatar DTAA. For payments within GCC entities, the system must verify the applicability of GCC common market provisions. The system must generate withholding tax certificates in the format required by each jurisdiction, calculate the gross-up amount when the intercompany agreement specifies net-of-tax pricing, and maintain a tax credit tracking register per entity for annual tax return preparation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R011	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany cost settlement approval chain	The system must enforce a configurable approval chain for intercompany settlements with thresholds based on settlement amount in USD equivalent. Settlements below USD 50,000 must require entity finance manager approval only. Settlements between USD 50,000 and USD 250,000 must require entity finance controller and regional finance director approval. Settlements exceeding USD 250,000 must additionally require group CFO approval. The approval interface must display settlement details including voyage references, cost breakdowns, allocation methodology applied, exchange rates used, and tax withholding calculations. Approvers must be able to partially approve settlements, holding disputed line items for separate resolution while releasing undisputed amounts for payment processing.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R012	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Automated settlement payment instruction generation	Upon final approval of intercompany settlements, the system must generate payment instructions in the format required by each entity's banking partner. Payment files must support SWIFT MT101 format for cross-border payments, UAE UAEFTS format for domestic UAE transfers, NEFT and RTGS formats for India domestic transfers, and QPAY format for Qatar domestic transfers. Each payment instruction must include the beneficiary entity bank details, settlement reference number, voyage numbers covered, and remittance advice details. The system must support payment batching to consolidate multiple settlement invoices into a single payment instruction per entity pair per netting cycle, reducing banking transaction costs and reconciliation effort.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R013	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany agreement master data management	The system must maintain a master repository of intercompany agreements defining the commercial terms between each entity pair. Agreement records must capture effective dates (Gregorian and Hijri), service types covered (slot chartering, agency services, equipment leasing, management fees), pricing methodology (cost plus markup, fixed fee, variable rate per TEU), applicable markup percentages, payment terms in calendar days, settlement currency preferences, and tax treatment. The system must enforce that no intercompany transaction can be processed without a valid active agreement between the involved entities. Agreement expiry alerts must be generated 60 days before expiration. Version control must track all agreement amendments with approval trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R014	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Consolidated intercompany elimination reporting	The system must generate intercompany elimination entries required for consolidated financial statement preparation. For each intercompany transaction, the system must produce the corresponding elimination journal entry that removes intercompany revenue and expense from the consolidated profit and loss statement and eliminates intercompany receivables and payables from the consolidated balance sheet. Elimination entries must handle currency translation adjustments when entity functional currencies differ (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) against the USD group reporting currency. The elimination report must reconcile to zero for each intercompany account pair and flag any imbalances exceeding the configurable tolerance threshold of USD 100.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R015	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany settlement audit and compliance reporting	The system must generate comprehensive audit reports for intercompany settlements supporting tax authority inquiries and transfer pricing audits in each jurisdiction. Reports must include transaction-level detail showing the original cost, allocation basis, markup applied, arm's length price benchmark, and final invoiced amount for each intercompany charge. The system must produce country-by-country reporting data in OECD CbCR XML format for entities exceeding the consolidated revenue threshold. Reports must be available in Arabic for GCC tax authority submissions and English for India tax authority submissions. The system must retain all intercompany settlement data and supporting documentation for the statutory retention period of each jurisdiction, minimum seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R016	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Feeder vessel cost sharing between entities	The system must calculate and allocate feeder vessel costs when multiple entities share feeder services connecting hub ports to spoke ports within the Arabian Gulf network. For example, when a feeder vessel operates between Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali serving cargo from Qatar, Abu Dhabi, and Dubai entities respectively, the system must allocate feeder costs based on actual cargo volumes loaded and discharged per entity at each port. The allocation must consider container weights for heavy cargo surcharges, reefer container power consumption costs allocated proportionally, and hazardous cargo handling premiums. The system must reconcile feeder allocations against the feeder operator invoice and distribute any over or under recovery across entities proportionally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-002-R017	FEAT-056-2-002	MOD-056	Intercompany settlement period closing controls	The system must enforce period-end closing controls for intercompany settlements aligned with each entity's financial calendar. Before an accounting period can be closed, the system must verify that all intercompany transactions for voyages completed within that period have been fully settled, reconciled, and posted to the general ledger. The system must generate a period-end intercompany balance confirmation request to each counterparty entity requiring electronic acknowledgment of the outstanding balance. Any unconfirmed balances must be flagged and reported to entity finance controllers. The system must support different fiscal year-ends across entities (March for India entity aligned with Indian fiscal year, December for GCC entities) and handle the resulting timing differences in period-end reconciliation processes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R001	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	AI-driven voyage profitability scoring model	The system must implement a machine learning model that assigns a profitability score (0-100) to each completed voyage based on multiple performance dimensions including: gross margin percentage, TCE (Time Charter Equivalent) rate achieved versus market benchmark, bunker efficiency ratio, port time optimization, cargo utilization factor, and container mix optimization. The model must be trained on historical voyage data spanning at least 24 months across all trade lanes served from Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Scores must be computed in real-time upon voyage closure and displayed alongside financial results in both Arabic and English dashboards with contextual explanations of score drivers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R002	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Market rate benchmarking integration	The system must integrate with external market rate indices including the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Drewry World Container Index (WCI), and Freightos Baltic Index (FBX) to benchmark achieved freight rates against prevailing market conditions. For each voyage leg (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Jebel Ali), the system must compare the blended freight rate achieved with the applicable market index rate on the voyage departure date. The AI model must normalize comparisons accounting for container type mix (dry versus reefer), cargo weight profiles, contract versus spot ratio, and INCOTERMS distribution (CIF, FOB, CFR). Benchmark results must be presented in USD per TEU with currency conversion to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR for entity-specific reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R003	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Predictive voyage profitability forecasting	The system must provide AI-generated profitability forecasts for planned voyages before vessel departure. The forecasting model must consider historical performance on the same trade lane, current bunker price trends from Singapore and Fujairah benchmarks, seasonal demand patterns for the Arabian Gulf to Indian Subcontinent trade, expected port congestion levels at Hamad Port and JNPT based on vessel queue data, and current freight rate market conditions. Forecasts must provide a probability-weighted range (optimistic, expected, pessimistic scenarios) with confidence intervals. The system must present forecasts in a visual dashboard supporting Arabic RTL layout with interactive drill-down into each cost and revenue assumption driving the forecast.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R004	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Trade lane profitability ranking and optimization	The AI engine must continuously rank all active trade lanes by profitability metrics and generate optimization recommendations. Rankings must consider net voyage result per TEU, return on deployed capital, contribution margin per vessel day, and cargo volume sustainability. For the core trade network connecting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, the system must identify underperforming legs and suggest remediation strategies such as freight rate adjustments, cargo mix optimization (increasing high-yield reefer cargo share), or schedule modifications to reduce port idle time. Recommendations must be generated in both Arabic and English and must quantify the estimated financial impact in USD and local currency for each suggested action.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R005	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Bunker consumption efficiency benchmarking	The system must benchmark bunker fuel consumption against expected performance curves for each vessel in the fleet, adjusted for weather conditions, sea state, draft, and speed profile. The AI model must identify voyages where actual bunker consumption deviates significantly from the expected consumption, distinguishing between controllable factors (speed management, route optimization) and uncontrollable factors (adverse weather, port delays at Hamad Port or JNPT). The system must calculate bunker efficiency in metric tons per TEU-mile and compare against fleet average, vessel class average, and industry benchmarks. Efficiency trends must be tracked over rolling 12-month periods with alerts generated when a vessel shows a declining trend exceeding configurable thresholds, potentially indicating hull fouling or engine performance degradation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R006	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Container utilization optimization analysis	The AI system must analyze container utilization patterns across voyages to identify revenue optimization opportunities. Analysis must cover slot utilization rates per leg, empty container repositioning costs, container dwell time at ports, and the mix of laden versus empty containers. For the Arabian Gulf to India trade, the system must identify trade imbalance patterns where outbound utilization from Jebel Ali to JNPT significantly exceeds return utilization, and recommend strategies such as triangulation, off-hire of surplus equipment, or short-term leasing. The system must quantify the cost of empty repositioning in USD per TEU and benchmark against industry averages. Utilization reports must support Arabic and English with visual heat maps showing utilization by port pair and container type.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R007	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Peer comparison benchmarking with anonymized data	The system must support peer comparison benchmarking allowing the organization to compare its voyage profitability metrics against anonymized industry peer data. The AI model must normalize peer comparisons for vessel size class, trade lane geography, and operational profile to ensure meaningful comparisons. Benchmarking dimensions must include TCE rate, operating cost per TEU, port productivity (containers per hour), bunker cost per TEU-mile, and overall voyage margin percentage. The system must present peer comparison results as percentile rankings (e.g., 75th percentile for TCE rate on Arabian Gulf to India trade). Data privacy must be enforced through differential privacy techniques ensuring no individual peer company's data can be reverse-engineered. Reports must be available in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R008	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Seasonal profitability pattern recognition	The AI engine must identify and quantify seasonal patterns in voyage profitability across different trade lanes. The system must analyze at minimum three years of historical data to detect recurring seasonal trends such as pre-Ramadan cargo surges on the India to Gulf trade, post-Diwali export peaks from JNPT, summer reefer cargo demand for perishables through Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, and year-end inventory building patterns. The model must output seasonal adjustment factors per trade lane per month in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats. The system must generate forward-looking seasonal alerts recommending proactive capacity adjustments, rate strategy changes, or equipment positioning actions at least 60 days before anticipated seasonal shifts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R009	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	AI-generated voyage performance narrative reports	The system must use natural language generation to produce human-readable voyage performance narrative reports in both Arabic and English. Each report must summarize the voyage's financial outcome, compare it to the pre-voyage estimate and AI forecast, explain the key variance drivers, and provide forward-looking recommendations. The Arabic narrative must use proper maritime terminology in formal Arabic suitable for executive presentation. Reports must incorporate contextual market intelligence such as prevailing freight rate trends, bunker price movements at Fujairah and Singapore, and port congestion status at key ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. The system must support automated distribution of narrative reports to configurable stakeholder lists upon voyage result closure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R010	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Anomaly detection in voyage cost patterns	The AI system must implement anomaly detection algorithms to identify unusual cost patterns in voyage results that may indicate errors, fraud, or operational inefficiencies. The system must establish dynamic baseline cost ranges for each cost category per port using historical data and flag entries that fall outside the expected range with a configurable sensitivity threshold (default two standard deviations). Specific anomaly checks must include: unusually high port disbursements at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali relative to vessel size, bunker consumption significantly exceeding expected rates, excessive overtime charges, and abnormal agency fees. Each detected anomaly must generate an alert with severity classification (low, medium, high, critical) and recommended investigation actions in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R011	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Voyage profitability impact simulation engine	The system must provide an interactive simulation engine allowing users to model the profitability impact of hypothetical changes to voyage parameters. Users must be able to adjust variables including freight rates per TEU, bunker price per metric ton, port stay duration at each port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), vessel speed, cargo volume, and container mix. The simulation must recalculate the voyage result in real-time showing the marginal impact of each variable change on the net voyage result in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The system must support saving simulation scenarios for comparison and sharing with colleagues. The simulation interface must support Arabic RTL layout with interactive sliders and graphical sensitivity analysis charts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R012	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Machine learning model training and retraining pipeline	The system must implement an automated pipeline for training and retraining the voyage profitability AI models. The pipeline must ingest historical voyage data from all four operating entities, perform data quality validation, feature engineering (computing derived metrics like TEU-miles, vessel utilization days, cargo yield per slot), and model training using gradient boosted trees and neural network architectures. Model retraining must be triggered automatically when new voyage data accumulates beyond a configurable threshold (default 100 completed voyages) or on a scheduled basis (monthly). The pipeline must evaluate model performance using mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) with a target of less than 8% for profitability prediction accuracy. Model versioning must track all trained models with their performance metrics and training data provenance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R013	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Profitability benchmarking data export and API	The system must provide RESTful API endpoints and bulk data export capabilities for voyage profitability benchmarking data. API endpoints must support querying benchmark scores by voyage number, vessel, trade lane, date range, and entity with responses in JSON format. Bulk exports must be available in CSV and Excel formats with configurable column selection and filtering. All API endpoints must enforce entity-level data access controls ensuring Qatar entity users cannot access India entity voyage details without cross-entity authorization. API responses must support localization with Arabic and English field labels and descriptions. Rate limiting must be implemented at 100 requests per minute per API key. The API must be documented using OpenAPI 3.0 specification with Arabic and English descriptions for each endpoint.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R014	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Demurrage and detention cost impact analysis	The AI system must analyze the impact of demurrage and detention charges on overall voyage profitability and identify patterns contributing to excessive container dwell times. The analysis must cover free time utilization rates at each port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), average demurrage cost per TEU by trade lane and customer segment, correlation between vessel arrival delays and demurrage incurrence, and the effectiveness of demurrage collection from consignees. The system must provide predictive alerts when AI models detect conditions likely to result in demurrage exposure, such as customs clearance delays at JNPT or documentation issues at Hamad Port. Recommendations must quantify potential savings from operational improvements in both USD and local currency with Arabic and English report generation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R015	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	Voyage carbon emission cost impact on profitability	The system must integrate carbon emission cost calculations into voyage profitability benchmarking to prepare for upcoming IMO carbon pricing mechanisms and EU ETS requirements for shipping. The AI model must estimate CO2 emissions per voyage based on actual bunker consumption, fuel type (VLSFO, LSMGO, LNG), and emission factors per fuel grade. The system must calculate the carbon cost impact at configurable carbon price scenarios (USD 25, 50, 75, 100 per metric ton of CO2) and present the adjusted voyage profitability after carbon costs. Benchmarking must compare fleet-level carbon intensity (grams CO2 per TEU-mile) against IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) ratings. The system must generate carbon cost projections for future voyages to support decarbonization strategy planning and report in Arabic and English formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-003-R016	FEAT-056-2-003	MOD-056	AI model explainability and transparency dashboard	The system must provide an explainability dashboard for all AI-generated profitability benchmarks and recommendations, ensuring users understand the basis for each AI output. For each profitability score, the dashboard must display the top contributing factors ranked by importance using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values, showing how each factor pushed the score above or below the baseline. The dashboard must present confidence levels for all predictions with clear uncertainty bands. Users must be able to drill into the training data subset that most influenced a specific prediction. The explainability interface must be available in Arabic and English with maritime domain-specific terminology. The system must log user interactions with AI explanations for continuous improvement of the model explanation quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R001	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Executive voyage performance summary dashboard	The system must provide an executive-level dashboard presenting key voyage performance metrics in a consolidated view across all entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The dashboard must display total voyage count, aggregate revenue, aggregate costs, net profit or loss, average margin percentage, fleet utilization rate, and TCE rate trend over a selectable time period. All monetary values must be displayable in USD as the group currency or in any entity local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with real-time currency toggle. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with properly formatted Arabic numerals and currency symbols. Comparative indicators must show period-over-period performance change (month-over-month, quarter-over-quarter, year-over-year) with color-coded arrows indicating improvement or decline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R002	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Interactive trade lane profitability heat map	The system must render an interactive geographical heat map visualization showing voyage profitability by trade lane overlaid on a map of the Arabian Gulf, Indian Ocean, and Indian Subcontinent region. Each trade lane connecting port pairs (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT and other served ports) must be color-coded by profitability performance (green for above target, yellow for meeting target, red for below target). Users must be able to click on any trade lane to drill down into detailed voyage-level data for that route. The heat map must support time-range animation showing profitability evolution over selected periods. Map labels and tooltips must display in the user's selected language (Arabic or English) with proper bidirectional text rendering for port names.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R003	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Customizable KPI tracking with threshold alerts	The system must allow users to configure personalized KPI dashboards with selectable metrics from a library of at least 30 voyage performance indicators. Core KPIs must include: revenue per TEU, cost per TEU, net margin percentage, bunker cost per TEU-mile, port productivity (moves per hour), vessel utilization percentage, empty repositioning ratio, demurrage incidence rate, customer claim ratio, and on-time delivery percentage. Each KPI must support configurable alert thresholds with automatic notifications when values breach upper or lower bounds. KPI definitions must be standardized across entities with the option for entity-specific overrides. Dashboard layouts must be saveable as personal or shared views with Arabic and English naming support. Historical KPI trend lines must span up to five years of data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R004	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Multi-dimensional voyage data pivot analysis	The system must provide a pivot table interface enabling users to analyze voyage data across multiple dimensions simultaneously. Supported dimensions must include: entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel, trade lane, port, customer, cargo type, container type (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF), INCOTERMS (FOB, CIF, CFR, EXW), time period (month, quarter, year in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars), and cost category. Users must be able to drag and drop dimensions into row, column, and filter areas. Aggregation functions must support sum, average, count, minimum, maximum, and weighted average. The pivot table must handle datasets of up to one million voyage records without degradation in response time. Export to Excel must preserve the pivot structure with Arabic column headers for GCC users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R005	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Voyage cost breakdown waterfall visualization	The system must present voyage costs in an interactive waterfall chart showing the progression from gross revenue to net voyage result. The waterfall must display sequential deductions for each cost category: bunker costs, port disbursements (itemized per port call at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT), canal transit fees, cargo handling charges, vessel operating costs, overhead allocations, insurance, and intercompany charges. Each bar in the waterfall must be clickable to reveal sub-category detail. The chart must support comparison mode showing two voyages or two time periods side-by-side. All monetary values must be displayable in USD or entity local currency. The waterfall must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper label alignment and text direction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R006	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Vessel performance comparison analytics	The system must provide comparative analytics across vessels in the fleet showing performance metrics side-by-side. Comparison dimensions must include: average voyage margin, TCE rate achieved, bunker efficiency (metric tons per TEU-mile), port time as percentage of total voyage time, cargo utilization rate, on-schedule performance, and maintenance downtime ratio. The system must support grouping vessels by class, size category, or entity to identify systematic performance differences. Trend charts must show each vessel's performance trajectory over selectable time periods. The analytics must highlight top-performing and underperforming vessels with AI-generated explanatory commentary in Arabic or English. Users must be able to select any subset of vessels for comparison with a minimum of two and maximum of ten vessels displayed simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R007	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Customer profitability analysis by voyage contribution	The system must analyze customer-level profitability based on their cargo contributions across voyages. For each customer, the system must calculate total revenue generated, direct costs attributable (cargo handling, documentation, special equipment), contribution margin, average revenue per TEU, payment collection performance, and claim history impact. The analysis must segment customers by profitability tiers (platinum, gold, silver, bronze) based on configurable thresholds. For multi-entity customers who ship through multiple ports (e.g., a customer using both Hamad Port and JNPT), the system must provide a consolidated cross-entity view. Customer names must support Arabic script storage and display. Reports must be exportable with customer-specific data access controls preventing commercial team members from viewing competitor customer data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R008	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Time series trend analysis with forecasting overlay	The system must provide time series charts for all key voyage metrics with configurable trend lines and AI-generated forecast overlays. Trend analysis must support daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual granularity with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar options. Moving averages (7-day, 30-day, 90-day) must be toggleable as overlay lines. The AI forecasting overlay must project metric values forward for up to 12 months using ensemble methods combining ARIMA, Prophet, and gradient boosted models. Forecast confidence intervals (80% and 95%) must be displayed as shaded bands around the central forecast line. Users must be able to annotate specific points on the time series with event markers (e.g., Suez Canal disruption, new service launch, Ramadan period) to contextualize performance changes. Charts must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R009	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Port performance comparative dashboard	The system must provide a dedicated dashboard comparing operational and financial performance across all served ports. For each port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and other configured ports), the dashboard must display average vessel turnaround time, average container handling rate, average port disbursement cost per vessel call, frequency of berth delays, customs clearance average duration, and total cargo volume throughput. The dashboard must support temporal comparison showing how each port's performance metrics have evolved over configurable time periods. Port rankings must be automatically generated based on composite performance scores. The dashboard must incorporate external data where available such as port authority published statistics. All port names must display correctly in both Arabic and English with proper transliteration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R010	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Scheduled report generation and distribution	The system must support scheduling of automated report generation and distribution for all dashboard views and analytics outputs. Users must be able to configure report schedules using daily, weekly, monthly, or custom cron expressions with support for Hijri calendar date triggers (e.g., first day of each Hijri month). Reports must be generated in PDF, Excel, or PowerPoint formats with configurable page layout, letterhead branding per entity, and language selection (Arabic or English). Distribution lists must support email delivery with configurable recipient lists per entity, SFTP upload to partner systems, and storage in a document management repository. The system must maintain a report generation log tracking successful and failed deliveries. Report content must reflect the data access permissions of the report owner, not the recipients.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R011	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Ad-hoc query builder for voyage data exploration	The system must provide a visual query builder interface allowing business users without technical skills to construct custom queries against the voyage analytics data warehouse. The query builder must support selection of data fields from categorized field lists (voyage details, financial metrics, operational metrics, port data, customer data), application of filters with standard comparison operators, grouping and aggregation, sorting, and result limiting. The interface must display a real-time preview of query results updating as the user builds the query. Queries must be saveable, shareable with other users, and schedulable for periodic execution. The query builder must enforce entity-level data access controls and must support Arabic field labels and filter values. Maximum query execution time must be capped at 60 seconds with timeout notification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R012	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Voyage result data warehouse ETL pipeline	The system must implement a robust ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) pipeline to populate the analytics data warehouse from operational voyage result data. The pipeline must extract data from the voyage result module, intercompany settlement records, port operations systems, and external market data feeds. Transformations must include currency normalization to USD using applicable exchange rates, time dimension enrichment with both Gregorian and Hijri calendar attributes, entity dimension mapping, and calculation of derived metrics such as TEU-miles, per-unit costs, and margin percentages. The pipeline must run incrementally with configurable frequency (minimum every four hours) and maintain data lineage tracking. Error handling must quarantine failed records without blocking the pipeline, with automated alerts to the data operations team. Full historical reprocessing must be supported for backfilling corrections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R013	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Dashboard performance optimization and caching	The system must ensure dashboard page load times do not exceed three seconds for standard views and five seconds for complex analytical queries involving aggregation across more than 10,000 voyage records. The system must implement a multi-tier caching strategy with pre-computed aggregations for common dashboard views refreshed every four hours, query result caching with configurable TTL per dashboard widget, and browser-level caching of static visualization assets. Cache invalidation must be triggered automatically when new voyage results are closed or existing results are amended. The system must support horizontal scaling of the analytics query engine to handle concurrent dashboard access by up to 200 simultaneous users across all entities. Dashboard loading must display a skeleton UI with Arabic or English placeholder text during data fetch.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R014	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Mobile-responsive analytics dashboard access	The system must provide a mobile-responsive version of the analytics dashboard optimized for tablet and smartphone access. The mobile interface must adapt chart visualizations for smaller screen sizes using responsive breakpoints at 768 pixels for tablets and 375 pixels for smartphones. Touch-friendly interactions must replace hover-based tooltips with tap-to-reveal data points. The mobile dashboard must support offline access to the most recently viewed dashboard with cached data, particularly useful for executives reviewing voyage analytics during travel. Gesture support must include pinch-to-zoom on charts and swipe navigation between dashboard sections. Arabic RTL layout must be fully supported on mobile devices with proper touch target sizing meeting WCAG 2.1 accessibility guidelines. Push notifications for KPI threshold breaches must be delivered to mobile devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R015	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Voyage analytics data access control and row-level security	The system must implement row-level security on the analytics data warehouse ensuring users can only query and visualize voyage data they are authorized to access. Entity-level security must restrict Qatar entity users to Qatar voyage data unless they hold a cross-entity analytics role. Within entities, data access must be further filterable by trade lane, vessel group, customer segment, and cost confidentiality level. Security policies must be enforced at the database query layer, not merely at the UI layer, preventing unauthorized data access through direct API calls or exported datasets. The system must log all analytics data access events for security audit purposes. Security policy administration must provide a UI for defining and managing access rules with Arabic and English rule descriptions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R016	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Comparative period analysis with normalization	The system must support comparative period analysis allowing users to compare voyage performance metrics between any two time periods with appropriate normalization. Comparison types must include year-over-year for the same Gregorian or Hijri month, quarter-over-quarter, current period versus budget, and current period versus a user-selected custom baseline period. Normalization must adjust for differences in voyage count, vessel deployment days, and fleet composition between compared periods to ensure like-for-like comparison. The system must highlight statistically significant changes versus changes within normal variation bounds using configurable confidence levels. Comparative analysis results must be presentable in tabular format with absolute and percentage variance columns and in graphical format with side-by-side or overlay bar and line charts. All outputs must support Arabic and English presentation with proper number and currency formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-056-2-004-R017	FEAT-056-2-004	MOD-056	Natural language query interface for analytics	The system must provide a natural language query interface allowing users to ask questions about voyage analytics in plain Arabic or English text. Example queries must include: 'What was the average margin for Jebel Ali to JNPT voyages last quarter?', 'Show me the top 5 most profitable vessels in 2025', and the Arabic equivalent queries using proper maritime terminology. The NLP engine must parse the query, identify the requested metrics, filters, groupings, and time ranges, translate the query into the appropriate data warehouse query, and return results in a formatted visualization. The system must display the interpreted query parameters back to the user for confirmation before execution. Query history must be maintained per user and frequently asked queries must be suggested as shortcuts. The system must handle ambiguous queries by presenting clarification options rather than returning incorrect results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R001	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Multi-Criteria Scoring Engine for Vessel-Route Pairing	The system must implement a weighted multi-criteria scoring engine that evaluates each vessel against candidate trade routes using at minimum the following dimensions: TEU capacity utilization potential, fuel consumption per nautical mile, draft restrictions at destination ports (Hamad Port max 16m, Jebel Ali max 17m, JNPT max 14m), crane compatibility, reefer plug capacity, and dangerous goods certification. Each criterion must carry a configurable weight between 0.0 and 1.0, with weights summing to 1.0 per evaluation profile. Scores must be normalized on a 0-100 scale and stored with full audit trail including the scoring timestamp, user or AI agent that triggered the evaluation, and the specific weight profile version used.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R002	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Trade Lane Definition and Route Parameter Management	The system must allow authorized users to define trade lanes as ordered sequences of port calls with associated parameters including port UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), estimated transit times in hours between consecutive ports, canal transit requirements (Suez Canal for India-Gulf routes), bunker fuel zones, and Emission Control Area (ECA) applicability. Each trade lane definition must support versioning so historical deployment decisions reference the exact route parameters that were active at decision time. The system must validate that port sequences form geographically plausible routes and flag circular or redundant port calls during lane creation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R003	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Vessel Technical Profile Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive vessel technical profile registry capturing IMO number, vessel name, flag state, classification society, gross tonnage, net tonnage, deadweight tonnage, LOA (length overall), beam, maximum draft, TEU nominal capacity, TEU capacity at homogeneous 14-tonne loading, reefer plug count, dangerous goods class certifications (IMDG classes 1-9), crane specifications (if geared), main engine type and fuel consumption curves, and auxiliary engine specifications. Each profile must support effective dating so that vessel modifications (e.g., scrubber retrofits, capacity upgrades) are tracked historically. The registry must integrate with vessel master data from MOD-012 (Asset Management) and flag any discrepancies between locally stored and externally sourced vessel particulars.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R004	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Decision Matrix Visualization with Arabic RTL Support	The system must render the vessel deployment decision matrix as an interactive grid where rows represent candidate vessels and columns represent evaluation criteria, with composite scores displayed in a summary column. The visualization must fully support Arabic RTL layout, reversing the column order so that the summary score appears on the left side in Arabic mode while remaining on the right in English LTR mode. Column headers, vessel names, and all tooltips must render in the user's selected language (Arabic or English). Numeric values must respect locale formatting: Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) when the UI locale is set to Arabic, and Western Arabic numerals in English mode. Color-coded heatmap overlays must use a configurable palette that remains accessible under WCAG 2.1 AA contrast requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R005	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Port Draft and Tidal Window Constraint Integration	The system must integrate real-time and forecast tidal data for each port in the network to enforce draft constraints during vessel deployment scoring. For JNPT (INNSA), where tidal range can exceed 5 meters, the system must calculate available sailing draft windows based on vessel loaded draft, under-keel clearance requirements (minimum 1.0m for Hamad Port, 1.2m for JNPT as per local VTS requirements), and predicted tide levels. Vessels whose maximum summer draft exceeds the port's available depth at mean low water spring must receive a penalty score or be flagged as requiring lighterage or part-loading. The constraint engine must pull tidal data from configured hydrographic APIs or accept manual uploads in standard IHO formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R006	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Commercial Viability Scoring per Trade Lane	The system must calculate a commercial viability score for each vessel-route combination by estimating voyage revenue potential versus voyage cost. Revenue estimation must factor in the average freight rate per TEU for the trade lane (sourced from MOD-045 Rate Management or manual input), expected load factor based on historical booking data, and surcharge applicability (BAF, CAF, THC, ISPS). Cost estimation must include bunker fuel cost using current prices in USD with conversion to the operating entity's base currency (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity), port dues, canal tolls (Suez Canal charges based on SCNT), pilotage, and agency fees. The resulting margin percentage must be displayed alongside the composite matrix score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R007	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Multi-Entity Deployment Authority and Approval Workflow	The system must enforce entity-specific approval workflows for vessel deployment decisions. When a vessel registered under one legal entity (e.g., Qatar entity) is proposed for deployment on a route primarily serving another entity's commercial operations (e.g., UAE entity Jebel Ali feeder service), the system must trigger a cross-entity approval workflow requiring sign-off from both the vessel-owning entity's fleet manager and the route-operating entity's commercial director. Approval requests must display cost allocation splits, inter-company charter rate implications, and tax jurisdiction impacts. The workflow must support configurable escalation timers (default 48 hours) and must log all approval or rejection decisions with timestamps, approver identity, and comments in both Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R008	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Seasonal and Monsoon Pattern Adjustment Factors	The system must incorporate seasonal adjustment factors into the decision matrix that reflect known weather patterns affecting Gulf-India trade routes. During the Southwest Monsoon season (June through September), the system must automatically apply increased transit time estimates (configurable, default +15% for Arabian Sea crossings), elevated fuel consumption factors due to adverse weather steaming, and reduced cargo intake recommendations for vessels with lower metacentric height. For the Shamal wind season (June-August in the Arabian Gulf), port productivity reduction factors must be applied to Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, and Khalifa Port. These seasonal factors must be stored as named profiles with Gregorian and Hijri calendar date ranges, and users must be able to override or adjust them per voyage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R009	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Emissions Compliance Scoring for IMO Regulations	The system must include an emissions compliance dimension in the decision matrix that evaluates each vessel's Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) rating (A through E) and Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) compliance status against IMO 2023+ requirements. Vessels rated D or E on CII must receive a penalty in the matrix scoring, with the penalty weight configurable by the fleet planner. The system must estimate voyage-specific CO2 emissions based on vessel engine specifications, planned speed, and route distance, and compare them against the vessel's annual CII trajectory. For routes transiting emission-sensitive zones near Qatar and UAE waters, additional scoring penalties must apply to non-scrubber-fitted vessels burning high-sulphur fuel oil. All emissions calculations must be traceable and exportable for regulatory reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R010	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Historical Deployment Performance Feedback Loop	The system must maintain a historical record of all vessel deployment decisions made through the matrix and their actual operational outcomes. After each completed voyage, the system must compare predicted matrix scores (capacity utilization, fuel consumption, commercial margin, schedule reliability) against actual performance metrics sourced from MOD-058 (Voyage Management) and MOD-034 (Financial Accounting). Deviations exceeding configurable thresholds (default ±10%) must be flagged and used to recalibrate the scoring model weights. A feedback dashboard must display trend analysis of prediction accuracy over rolling 6-month and 12-month periods, segmented by trade lane, vessel class, and season. This feedback data must be accessible via API for consumption by the AI Fleet Deployment Optimizer (FEAT-057-1-004).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R011	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Offline Decision Matrix Access and Synchronization	The system must support offline access to the vessel deployment decision matrix for fleet planners operating in environments with limited connectivity, such as vessel-side planning offices or remote port locations in the Indian subcontinent. The offline module must cache the most recent vessel profiles, trade lane definitions, scoring weights, and the last 30 days of matrix evaluations. Users must be able to create new matrix evaluations offline, which are queued for synchronization when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution logic must handle cases where vessel availability or route parameters changed during the offline period, presenting the user with a side-by-side comparison of their offline evaluation versus the updated online state. Offline data must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R012	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Vessel Availability and Dry-Dock Schedule Integration	The system must integrate vessel availability data including current position, estimated time of arrival at next open port, ongoing charter commitments, and planned dry-dock periods from MOD-012 (Asset Management). Vessels currently in dry-dock or scheduled for dry-dock within the deployment period must be excluded from the decision matrix or flagged with a non-availability indicator showing the dry-dock start date, end date, and shipyard location. For vessels on time-charter to third parties, the system must check charter party redelivery dates and geographic redelivery ranges to determine if the vessel can be redeployed to the candidate route within the required timeframe. The availability check must account for ballast voyage time from the vessel's current or projected position to the route's first load port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R013	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Configurable Weight Profiles by User Role and Trade Lane	The system must support multiple named scoring weight profiles that can be associated with specific user roles or trade lane categories. For example, a commercial-focused profile may weight revenue potential and load factor heavily, while an operations-focused profile may prioritize schedule reliability and fuel efficiency. Each profile must define weights for all scoring dimensions summing to 1.0, and the system must prevent saving profiles with invalid weight sums. Profiles must be assignable as defaults at the entity level (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) or trade lane level, with user-level overrides permitted. A comparison mode must allow planners to view the same vessel-route matrix evaluated under two different weight profiles side by side, highlighting rank changes and score deltas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R014	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Container Type Mix Compatibility Assessment	The system must evaluate vessel suitability for each route based on the expected container type mix derived from historical booking patterns and forward cargo forecasts. Assessment must cover standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RE, 40RE, 40REHC), flat-racks (20FR, 40FR), open-tops (20OT, 40OT), tank containers (20TK), and out-of-gauge cargo. Vessels must be scored on their ability to accommodate the projected mix, considering reefer plug availability versus projected reefer demand (critical for perishable exports from JNPT and Khalifa Port), below-deck cell guide compatibility with non-standard container heights, and deck strength ratings for heavy-lift flat-rack stowage. A mismatch between vessel capability and route cargo mix must reduce the composite matrix score proportionally.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R015	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Matrix Export and Regulatory Audit Trail	The system must provide export functionality for decision matrix evaluations in PDF, Excel (XLSX), and CSV formats. PDF exports must support bilingual rendering with Arabic and English on the same document, using right-to-left paragraph direction for Arabic sections. Excel exports must include separate worksheets for the matrix summary, detailed per-criterion scores, vessel profiles, and route parameters. All exports must include a header with the evaluation timestamp, evaluating user or AI agent identifier, entity name, and a unique evaluation reference number. For regulatory audit purposes (Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Indian DG Shipping), the system must maintain immutable snapshots of each finalized deployment decision including the complete matrix state, selected vessel, and approver chain, retained for a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-001-R016	FEAT-057-1-001	MOD-057	Sanctions and Trade Compliance Screening in Matrix	The system must integrate sanctions and trade compliance screening into the vessel deployment decision matrix. Before scoring a vessel for a route, the system must verify the vessel's flag state, registered owner, beneficial owner, and operator against OFAC SDN lists, EU consolidated sanctions lists, and UN Security Council sanctions. Vessels flagged by any sanctions list must be automatically excluded from deployment consideration on affected routes, with a clear audit log entry explaining the exclusion reason. For routes calling at ports in countries with specific trade restrictions (e.g., certain cargo types restricted for import into KSA), the system must cross-reference the expected cargo profile. The sanctions database must be updated at least daily, and the system must alert fleet planners if a previously cleared vessel becomes sanctioned during an active deployment period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R001	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Real-Time Fleet Capacity Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying the aggregate and per-vessel capacity status of the entire owned and chartered fleet. The dashboard must show total nominal TEU capacity, deployed TEU capacity (vessels on active service), idle TEU capacity (vessels awaiting deployment, in layup, or between charters), and TEU capacity under maintenance or dry-dock. Capacity figures must be segmented by operating entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and by vessel size class (feeder under 3,000 TEU, handy 3,000-8,000 TEU, sub-Panamax 8,000-13,000 TEU, neo-Panamax 13,000+ TEU). The dashboard must refresh at configurable intervals (default every 15 minutes) using vessel position and status feeds, and must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R002	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Demand Forecast Integration for Capacity Planning	The system must integrate cargo demand forecasts from MOD-046 (Demand Forecasting) or accept manual demand inputs per trade lane per week. Demand data must be expressed in TEU (both laden and empty reposition) and in weight (metric tonnes) segmented by container type (dry, reefer, special). The system must compare forecasted demand against deployed capacity for each service loop over a configurable planning horizon (default 12 weeks, extendable to 52 weeks). Surplus or deficit calculations must be displayed per week per route, with color-coded indicators: green for balanced (utilization 70-95%), amber for underutilized (below 70%), and red for overbooked (above 95%). All demand figures must be convertible between entity-specific currencies for commercial valuation of capacity gaps.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R003	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Vessel Utilization Tracking per Voyage and Annualized	The system must track vessel utilization at both the individual voyage level and annualized level. Per-voyage utilization must capture: TEU loaded versus TEU capacity (slot utilization), weight loaded versus deadweight capacity (weight utilization), reefer containers loaded versus reefer plug capacity, and revenue per TEU versus break-even revenue per TEU. Annualized utilization must aggregate voyage-level data into rolling 12-month averages. The system must calculate vessel operating days versus idle days, sea days versus port days, and productive days versus non-productive days (including off-hire, dry-dock, waiting for berth). Utilization metrics must be reportable per vessel, per service loop, per entity, and fleet-wide, with drill-down capability from summary to voyage-level detail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R004	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Charter Market Integration for Capacity Adjustment	The system must interface with charter market data sources to provide current time-charter and spot-charter rates for vessels of relevant size classes operating in the Gulf-India region. When a capacity shortfall is identified on a specific trade lane, the system must automatically suggest charter-in options with estimated costs in USD and converted to the operating entity's base currency using exchange rates from MOD-033 (Treasury Management). Conversely, when overcapacity is detected, the system must suggest charter-out opportunities with estimated revenue. Charter cost and revenue estimates must factor in positioning (ballast) voyage costs, charter hire duration (minimum and maximum), and brokerage commissions. The system must track charter inquiry status through stages: identified, contacted broker, offer received, negotiating, fixed, and rejected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R005	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Capacity Planning Scenario Modeling Tool	The system must provide a scenario modeling tool that allows fleet planners to create and compare up to five simultaneous what-if scenarios for fleet capacity allocation. Each scenario must allow modification of vessel deployments, addition or removal of chartered vessels, changes to service frequency, and adjustments to demand assumptions. For each scenario, the system must calculate total fleet operating cost (in all entity currencies: USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR), projected revenue based on demand fill rates, fleet-wide utilization percentage, CO2 emissions per TEU-mile, and schedule reliability score. Scenarios must be saveable with descriptive names, timestamps, and the creating user's identity. A comparison view must present all scenarios in a tabular format with variance highlighting against the baseline (current deployment) scenario.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R006	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Empty Container Repositioning Capacity Allocation	The system must account for empty container repositioning requirements when calculating effective capacity utilization. Trade lane imbalances (e.g., high export volumes from JNPT to Gulf ports with lower return cargo volumes) generate empty repositioning needs that consume vessel slot capacity. The system must integrate empty repositioning forecasts from MOD-041 (Equipment Management) and deduct projected empty slots from available laden capacity. Planners must be able to set maximum empty-to-laden ratios per service loop (e.g., maximum 30% empty slots on Gulf-to-India legs). When empty repositioning demand threatens to exceed allocated capacity, the system must alert planners and suggest alternative repositioning routes, timing adjustments, or third-party slot purchase arrangements to rebalance container flows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R007	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Port Productivity and Turnaround Time Impact Analysis	The system must model the impact of port productivity variations on effective fleet capacity. For each port in the network (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA, and others as configured), the system must maintain average crane moves per hour, average vessel turnaround time by vessel size class, and berth availability probability based on historical data. Delays at ports reduce the number of round voyages a vessel can complete in a given period, effectively reducing annualized capacity. The system must calculate the sensitivity of fleet capacity to port productivity changes, showing planners how a 10% increase or decrease in turnaround time at specific ports affects annual TEU throughput. During known congestion events (e.g., Ramadan schedule changes, monsoon-related closures at JNPT), the system must apply temporary productivity adjustment factors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R008	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Hijri Calendar Integration for Planning Milestones	The system must support Hijri (Islamic) calendar alongside Gregorian calendar for all capacity planning timelines and milestone dates. Users in Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities must be able to toggle between Hijri and Gregorian date display, and the system must correctly handle Hijri-specific planning periods such as Ramadan (reduced port operating hours at Gulf ports), Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha (cargo surge patterns for consumer goods), and Hajj season (increased demand for specific cargo types to Jeddah Islamic Port SAJED). Capacity planning views must align weekly and monthly buckets to the selected calendar system. Date range filters, export headers, and printed reports must display dates in both Hijri and Gregorian formats when the user's locale preference includes dual-calendar display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R009	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Bunker Consumption Forecasting for Capacity Costing	The system must forecast bunker fuel consumption for each capacity deployment scenario to enable accurate cost-per-TEU calculations. Fuel consumption estimates must be based on vessel-specific consumption curves (from the vessel technical profile in FEAT-057-1-001-R003), planned sailing speeds, route distances including canal transits, and expected weather factors. The system must support multiple fuel types: VLSFO (Very Low Sulphur Fuel Oil), LSMGO (Low Sulphur Marine Gas Oil), and LNG where applicable. Bunker prices must be sourced from MOD-052 (Bunker Procurement) or entered manually per bunkering port. Total voyage bunker cost must be allocated proportionally across TEU capacity to derive fuel cost per TEU, displayed in USD and convertible to QAR, AED, SAR, or INR based on the operating entity's reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R010	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Slot Swap and Vessel Sharing Agreement Capacity Tracking	The system must track capacity exchanged through slot swap agreements (also known as vessel sharing agreements or VSAs) with partner carriers. For each VSA, the system must record the agreement identifier, partner carrier name, service loop, slot allocation per leg (both slots provided by the company and slots received from the partner), effective date range, and commercial terms. Net capacity impact of all active VSAs must be reflected in the capacity dashboard: slots given to partners must reduce available capacity, and slots received must increase it. The system must alert planners when actual slot usage by partners approaches the contractual limit (configurable threshold, default 90%) and when company usage of partner slots is significantly below entitlement, indicating potential commercial renegotiation opportunity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R011	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Multi-Currency Capacity Cost Reporting	The system must produce capacity cost reports in multiple currencies to serve the multi-entity organizational structure. Each entity must see capacity costs in its base currency: Qatar entity in QAR, UAE entity in AED, KSA entity in SAR, and India entity in INR, with USD as the consolidated reporting currency. Exchange rates must be sourced from MOD-033 (Treasury Management) and applied using the rate type appropriate for the reporting context: spot rate for real-time dashboards, monthly average rate for period reports, and budget rate for planning scenarios. Cost components must include vessel daily operating cost, voyage variable costs (fuel, port charges, canal tolls), and allocated overhead. Reports must include currency conversion footnotes showing the exact exchange rates applied, and must support re-statement at alternative rates for sensitivity analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R012	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Capacity Alert and Notification Engine	The system must implement a configurable alert engine that monitors capacity utilization metrics and triggers notifications when predefined thresholds are breached. Alert conditions must include: vessel utilization falling below minimum threshold for two consecutive voyages, trade lane demand exceeding deployed capacity by more than a configurable percentage (default 10%), vessel idle time exceeding configurable maximum days (default 7 days), and fleet-wide utilization dropping below annual budget target. Notifications must be delivered via in-app alerts, email, and SMS (for critical alerts). Alert messages must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). Each alert must include a direct link to the relevant capacity dashboard view and suggested corrective actions generated by the system based on the alert type and current fleet state.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R013	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Berth Window and Terminal Capacity Constraint Modeling	The system must model terminal-side capacity constraints that affect vessel deployment feasibility. For each port terminal, the system must maintain: number of berths suitable for each vessel size class, maximum quay crane allocation per vessel, container yard storage capacity in TEU ground slots, gate throughput capacity in truck moves per hour, and rail connectivity capacity where applicable (relevant for JNPT intermodal connections). When fleet capacity planning proposes multiple vessel calls at the same terminal within a given week, the system must validate that the terminal can physically accommodate all proposed calls without berthing conflicts. Terminal capacity data must be sourced from MOD-027 (Port Operations) or maintained locally with periodic reconciliation. Planning views must highlight weeks where terminal constraints limit the deployment of additional capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R014	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Fleet Age and Replacement Planning Integration	The system must incorporate fleet age profile data into long-term capacity planning (1-5 year horizon). For each vessel, the system must display current age, expected economic life remaining (based on vessel type and class society survey cycle), and scheduled major survey dates (special survey every 5 years, intermediate survey at 2.5-year intervals). When vessels approach end-of-economic-life thresholds (configurable, default 25 years for container vessels), the system must flag them in the capacity plan and prompt planners to model replacement scenarios. Replacement modeling must account for newbuilding delivery lead times (typically 2-3 years), secondhand market acquisition timelines, and the capacity gap during transition periods. The system must also factor in IMO environmental regulations that may make older vessels non-compliant before their physical end-of-life.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R015	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Capacity Data API for External System Integration	The system must expose RESTful API endpoints that provide fleet capacity data to external systems including the company's commercial booking platform, customer-facing schedule portals, and partner carrier data exchange systems. API endpoints must include: current deployed capacity per service loop (GET /api/v1/capacity/deployed), available slot inventory per voyage (GET /api/v1/capacity/slots/{voyage-id}), and capacity forecast per trade lane per week (GET /api/v1/capacity/forecast). All endpoints must require OAuth 2.0 authentication and support JSON response format. Data must be filterable by entity, trade lane, vessel, and date range. Rate limiting must be enforced at 1,000 requests per minute per API client. The API must return capacity figures in TEU and weight, with optional currency-denominated cost data available to authorized internal consumers only.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-002-R016	FEAT-057-1-002	MOD-057	Reefer Capacity Specialized Planning Module	The system must provide a specialized planning view for reefer (refrigerated) container capacity given the high-value nature of perishable cargo on Gulf-India routes. This module must track reefer plug availability per vessel per voyage leg, forecast reefer demand based on seasonal agricultural export patterns (e.g., mango and grape season from JNPT March-June, dates from Gulf ports August-October), and identify reefer capacity gaps or surpluses. Power generation capacity aboard each vessel must be validated against simultaneous reefer connection demands, including safety margins for power fluctuation. The system must support temperature zone categorization (frozen below -18°C, chilled 0-5°C, controlled atmosphere 12-15°C) and alert planners when a vessel's reefer system capabilities do not match the projected cargo temperature requirements for a given trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R001	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Service Loop Definition and Port Rotation Builder	The system must provide an interactive service loop builder that allows planners to define a new service by specifying the ordered sequence of port calls forming the rotation. Each port call must include the port UN/LOCODE (e.g., QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, SAJED, INNSA), terminal name within the port, planned day of the week for arrival, estimated hours in port for cargo operations, and the transit time in hours to the next port. The builder must calculate total round-voyage duration and determine the minimum number of vessels required to maintain a weekly, bi-weekly, or other configured frequency. The interface must display an interactive map visualization showing the port rotation with route lines and distance annotations, supporting both Arabic and English port name labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R002	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Network Connectivity Analysis for Port Pairs	The system must analyze and display the connectivity provided by the current service network for all origin-destination port pairs. For each port pair (e.g., QAHMD to INNSA), the system must show: direct service availability (if the pair is served by a single service loop), transshipment options (if connection requires cargo transfer at an intermediate hub such as Jebel Ali), total transit time for each routing option, service frequency (weekly, bi-weekly), and cut-off-to-delivery time. Connectivity must be scored on a scale that considers transit time competitiveness versus market benchmarks, frequency adequacy versus demand volumes, and transshipment risk (additional handling increases cargo damage probability). Gaps in the network where demand exists but no competitive routing is offered must be highlighted for new service consideration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R003	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Transit Time Competitiveness Benchmarking	The system must maintain a database of competitor transit times for key origin-destination pairs in the Gulf-India trade and benchmark the company's service network against them. Data sources must include published competitor schedules (manually entered or scraped from public schedule databases with appropriate licensing), and industry reports. For each port pair, the system must display the company's transit time, the market best transit time, the market average transit time, and the company's ranking. Transit time comparisons must account for both port-to-port and door-to-door where inland transportation data is available. The system must alert network planners when a competitor launches a new service or modifies an existing one that significantly changes the competitive landscape on routes involving QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, or INNSA.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R004	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Service Profitability Estimation Model	The system must estimate the profitability of each service loop on a per-voyage and annualized basis. Revenue estimation must use the average freight rate per TEU by cargo type and trade lane direction, multiplied by the expected cargo volume per voyage derived from demand forecasts and historical load factors. Cost estimation must include vessel daily operating cost (crew, insurance, maintenance, administration), voyage costs (bunker fuel, port dues at each call including Hamad Port tariffs, Jebel Ali tariffs, JNPT tariffs, canal tolls), and cargo handling costs (THC, stevedoring). The model must produce per-voyage contribution margin, annual service profitability, return on deployed capital, and cost per TEU-mile. All financial figures must be calculated in USD and translatable to entity-specific currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) at applicable exchange rates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R005	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Feeder Network Design for Hub-and-Spoke Operations	The system must support the design and evaluation of feeder networks that connect smaller ports to the main hub ports (Jebel Ali as the primary Gulf transshipment hub, JNPT as the primary Indian gateway). Feeder service design must account for mother vessel schedules at the hub to ensure cargo connection windows (minimum connection time configurable, default 24 hours at Jebel Ali, 36 hours at JNPT). The system must calculate the percentage of mainline cargo that depends on feeder connectivity, and model the impact of feeder schedule changes on overall network transit times. For ports like Hamad Port and Khalifa Port that may serve as both direct call and feeder destinations, the system must compare the total logistics cost and transit time of direct versus feeder service options and recommend the optimal configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R006	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Schedule Reliability Simulation and Buffer Planning	The system must simulate schedule reliability for proposed and existing service loops by modeling variability in transit times, port productivity, weather delays, and canal transit queuing. Using Monte Carlo simulation or equivalent statistical methods, the system must generate probability distributions for arrival times at each port in the rotation. Planners must be able to set target on-time reliability percentages (e.g., 90% within 12-hour window) and the system must calculate the buffer time required at each port to achieve the target. The simulation must account for known disruption patterns: Suez Canal congestion peaks, monsoon-season delays on Arabian Sea crossings, and tidal window restrictions at JNPT. Results must be displayed as probability curves with confidence intervals and exportable to PDF reports with bilingual (Arabic/English) headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R007	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Intermodal Connectivity and Hinterland Reach Assessment	The system must evaluate the hinterland reach of each port in the service network by modeling intermodal connections (road and rail) from the port to major cargo origins and destinations. For JNPT, the system must model rail connections to the Dedicated Freight Corridor reaching inland container depots (ICDs) across western and northern India. For Gulf ports, the system must model road freight connections to major industrial zones: Ras Laffan and Mesaieed from Hamad Port, KIZAD and ICAD from Khalifa Port, and JAFZA and DWC from Jebel Ali. Transit time and cost from door-to-port must be calculable for major origin-destination points, enabling network planners to evaluate total supply chain competitiveness rather than just ocean transit. Hinterland data must be updateable by local operations teams in each entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R008	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Service Frequency Optimization Based on Demand Elasticity	The system must model the relationship between service frequency and cargo demand to help planners determine optimal calling frequency for each trade lane. Using historical data on booking volumes at different service frequencies and industry elasticity benchmarks, the system must estimate how increasing frequency (e.g., from bi-weekly to weekly) or decreasing it would affect cargo volumes and revenue. The model must account for the additional vessel cost required to increase frequency, the competitive response likelihood, and the customer retention impact of frequency reduction. Optimization outputs must include the recommended frequency, expected volume change, marginal revenue versus marginal cost analysis, and payback period for any additional vessel deployment. Recommendations must be entity-aware, considering that Qatar entity customers may have different frequency sensitivity than India entity customers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R009	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Port Cost Comparison and Optimization for Rotations	The system must maintain a comprehensive port cost database for all ports in the service network and use it to optimize port rotation sequences. Port costs must include: port authority charges (based on vessel GT or NT), pilotage and towage fees, berth hire per hour or per meter of LOA, container handling charges (THC) per move differentiated by container size and type, ISPS security surcharges, and anchorage charges where applicable. For Gulf ports, costs must be maintained in both USD and local currency (QAR for Hamad, AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa, SAR for King Abdullah Port). For JNPT, costs must be in INR and USD. The system must compare rotation alternatives where port substitution is feasible (e.g., Khalifa Port versus Jebel Ali for Abu Dhabi cargo) and present the cost differential to support rotation optimization decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R010	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Network Overlap and Cannibalization Detection	The system must detect and quantify service overlap and potential revenue cannibalization when multiple company-operated services cover similar origin-destination pairs. For each port pair served by more than one service loop, the system must calculate the degree of overlap by comparing transit times, sailing days, and target cargo segments. Cannibalization risk must be scored based on the proportion of shared addressable market between overlapping services. The system must distinguish between healthy overlap (providing customers with frequency and choice) and detrimental cannibalization (services competing for the same thin cargo base). Alerts must be generated when a proposed new service or service modification increases cannibalization risk beyond a configurable threshold. Recommendations must include options for service differentiation such as targeting different cargo types, adjusting sailing days, or merging overlapping services.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R004	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Bunker Cost Sensitivity Analysis	The system must perform bunker cost sensitivity analysis across the fleet, modeling the impact of fuel price variations (VLSFO, HSFO with scrubber, LNG, methanol) on per-voyage and annualized fleet economics. The analysis must incorporate bunker price differentials at key bunkering locations including Fujairah (AEFUJ), Singapore (SGSIN), and Colombo (LKCMB). Sensitivity ranges must be configurable from -50% to +200% of current spot prices. Results must show break-even freight rates per TEU for each vessel class and route combination, with outputs available in all five operational currencies (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and exportable to Excel with proper Arabic column headers for RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R011	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Regulatory and Trade Agreement Impact Modeling	The system must model the impact of regulatory changes and trade agreements on service network viability. Planners must be able to define regulatory scenarios such as new customs facilitation agreements between GCC countries, changes in Indian cabotage rules affecting feeder operations at JNPT, IMO speed reduction mandates, and new environmental regulations requiring operational adjustments. For each scenario, the system must recalculate affected service loop parameters: transit times (if speed changes are mandated), port call sequences (if new customs corridors enable direct clearance), and cost structures (if new levies or incentives apply). The GCC Customs Union implications on transshipment cargo handling between Qatar, UAE, and KSA ports must be specifically modelable. Scenario results must be comparable against the baseline network design to quantify the net impact on service competitiveness and profitability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R012	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Service Network Versioning and Change History	The system must implement full version control for the service network design, allowing planners to track every modification to service loops, port rotations, and vessel assignments over time. Each version must capture the complete network state including all active service loops with their port rotations, vessel assignments, frequency, and effective dates. Changes between versions must be diffable, showing exactly which services were added, removed, or modified. The system must maintain a minimum of five years of network version history to support long-term trend analysis. Each version must be tagged with the change reason (commercial, operational, regulatory, seasonal), the authorizing user, and the effective date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars. Planners must be able to revert to or branch from any historical version to create a new planning scenario.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R013	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Environmental Impact Assessment per Service Loop	The system must calculate and display the environmental impact of each service loop including total CO2 emissions per round voyage, CO2 emissions per TEU transported, SOx and NOx emissions based on fuel type and engine specifications, and estimated particulate matter generation. Emissions must be calculated using IMO-approved methodologies (Fourth IMO GHG Study methodology) and vessel-specific emission factors derived from engine data in the vessel technical profile. The system must compare emissions across alternative network designs to enable selection of the most environmentally sustainable configuration. For each service loop, the system must generate an Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) and track its trend over time. Environmental reports must be exportable in formats required by Qatar National Environment and Climate Change Committee, UAE Ministry of Climate Change, and Indian Maritime Administration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R014	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Customer Service Level Agreement Impact Analysis	The system must evaluate how proposed network changes affect service level agreements (SLAs) with key customers. The system must integrate customer SLA data from MOD-044 (Customer Management) including committed transit times, schedule reliability targets, equipment availability guarantees, and cargo acceptance cut-off commitments. When a planner modifies a service loop (changes port rotation, adjusts speed, adds or removes a port call), the system must identify all affected customer SLAs and calculate the impact: whether the modified service still meets committed transit times, whether reliability targets remain achievable, and whether booking cut-off times need adjustment. A summary report must list all SLAs affected by the proposed change, categorized as compliant (still met), at-risk (marginally affected), or breached (no longer achievable), enabling planners to communicate proactively with affected customers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R015	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Network Visualization and Interactive Map Display	The system must provide an interactive geographic map visualization of the entire service network. The map must display all ports of call with their UN/LOCODE identifiers, service loop routes rendered as colored polylines with directional indicators, vessel current positions (from AIS integration via MOD-012), and port capacity indicators. Users must be able to toggle visibility of individual service loops, filter by entity or trade lane region, and click on any port to view detailed metrics (call frequency, average utilization, cost per call). The map must support zoom levels from global overview down to individual terminal layout level. For Arabic-locale users, all labels, tooltips, and legend text must render in Arabic with RTL text direction. The visualization must perform smoothly with up to 50 simultaneous service loops and 200 port calls displayed, with WebGL rendering for complex overlays.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R016	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Transshipment Hub Efficiency and Dwell Time Analysis	The system must analyze transshipment hub efficiency for ports serving as connection points in the service network, primarily Jebel Ali (AEJEA) as the Gulf hub. Analysis must include average container dwell time at the hub (time between discharge from feeder or mainline vessel and loading onto connecting vessel), connection reliability (percentage of containers making their intended connecting vessel), missed connection rate and root cause classification (late arriving feeder, early departing mainline, terminal handling delay, customs hold). The system must model the impact of schedule changes on transshipment connection quality, alerting planners when a proposed modification increases average dwell time beyond configurable thresholds (default 72 hours) or reduces connection reliability below target (default 95%). Dwell time cost must be calculated including terminal storage charges and opportunity cost of delayed delivery.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-003-R017	FEAT-057-1-003	MOD-057	Blank Sailing and Service Suspension Management	The system must support the planning and management of blank sailings (cancelled voyages) and temporary service suspensions as capacity management tools. Planners must be able to designate specific voyages as blank sailings within a service loop, specifying the affected port calls and the reason (demand shortfall, vessel repositioning, maintenance, weather). The system must calculate the impact of each blank sailing on customer bookings (identifying bookings that need to be rolled to the next sailing), network connectivity (identifying transshipment connections that are disrupted), and financial performance (savings from avoided voyage costs versus lost revenue and penalty costs). A blank sailing calendar view must display all planned blank sailings across the network with color coding by reason, filterable by service loop and entity. Notifications must be automatically generated for affected customers and partner carriers with configurable lead time (default 14 days before scheduled sailing).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R001	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	AI-Driven Vessel-Route Assignment Optimization Engine	The system must implement an AI optimization engine that determines the optimal assignment of vessels to service routes across the entire network simultaneously. The engine must use mixed-integer programming or equivalent optimization techniques enhanced by machine learning models trained on historical deployment outcomes. The objective function must be configurable to maximize fleet contribution margin, maximize utilization, minimize emissions, or apply a weighted combination of multiple objectives. Constraints must include: vessel technical compatibility with routes (draft, capacity, speed), vessel availability windows, crew rest and manning regulations, and contractual obligations (existing charter commitments, customer SLAs). The engine must process the full fleet of up to 100 vessels and up to 50 service routes within a maximum computation time of 30 minutes, providing the top three recommended deployment configurations ranked by objective score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R002	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Demand Prediction Model with Machine Learning	The system must implement a machine learning-based demand prediction model that forecasts container shipping demand per trade lane per week for a configurable horizon (default 26 weeks). The model must ingest historical booking data, macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth rates for Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), trade flow statistics, commodity price indices, seasonal patterns (including Hijri calendar events such as Ramadan demand surges and Hajj-related cargo peaks), and competitor capacity changes. The prediction model must support multiple container types (dry, reefer, special equipment) and differentiate between import and export directions for each trade lane. Prediction accuracy must be tracked using MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) with a target of below 15% for 4-week-ahead forecasts. Model retraining must occur automatically on a configurable schedule (default weekly) using the latest actual booking data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R003	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Dynamic Speed Optimization for Fuel and Schedule Balance	The system must include an AI-powered speed optimization component that recommends optimal sailing speeds for each voyage leg to balance fuel cost, schedule adherence, and emissions targets. The optimizer must consider current bunker fuel prices at relevant bunkering ports (Fujairah, Singapore, Colombo), vessel-specific fuel consumption curves at various speeds, weather and current forecasts for the planned route, and the schedule buffer available at each port call. The system must dynamically adjust speed recommendations as conditions change during the voyage (weather deterioration, port congestion reports, bunker price movements). Speed recommendations must respect minimum speed limits for main engine operation and maximum speed limits from charter party agreements. For each voyage, the system must calculate the monetary value of speed changes in the operating entity's currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD) showing the trade-off between fuel savings and potential schedule delay costs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R004	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Anomaly Detection for Fleet Performance Deviations	The system must implement anomaly detection algorithms that continuously monitor fleet performance metrics and flag statistically significant deviations from expected patterns. Monitored metrics must include: vessel fuel consumption versus expected consumption at current speed and load (detecting potential hull fouling or engine degradation), port turnaround time versus historical average for the vessel class at that port, schedule adherence deviation trends, and cargo utilization patterns that diverge from seasonal norms. Anomalies must be classified by severity (informational, warning, critical) and by domain (mechanical, commercial, operational). Each detected anomaly must be presented with contextual data explaining what the expected value was, what the actual value is, the statistical confidence of the anomaly classification, and suggested investigation actions. Anomaly detection models must self-calibrate using rolling historical windows to adapt to gradual operational changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R005	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	What-If Scenario Generation and AI Recommendation	The system must enable users to define what-if scenarios and receive AI-generated deployment recommendations for each scenario. Scenario parameters must include: addition or removal of vessels from the fleet, opening or closing of trade lanes, changes in demand assumptions (e.g., +20% India export demand due to new trade agreement), bunker price changes, new port access (e.g., addition of King Abdullah Port SAKAP), and regulatory changes (e.g., IMO speed limits). For each scenario, the AI engine must generate an optimized deployment plan and compare it against the current baseline, quantifying differences in profitability (in all entity currencies: QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD), utilization, emissions, and service quality. The system must support running up to ten scenarios in parallel and present results in a ranked comparison dashboard. Scenario execution must complete within 15 minutes for standard complexity scenarios.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R006	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Market Intelligence Integration for Competitive Positioning	The system must integrate market intelligence data to inform AI deployment recommendations with competitive context. Data sources must include: published competitor sailing schedules and capacity deployments on Gulf-India trades, freight rate indices (Shanghai Containerized Freight Index, Drewry World Container Index), charter market rates from recognized brokers, and port throughput statistics from Qatar Ports Management Company (Mwani), Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, and Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority. The AI engine must identify market opportunities where competitors have reduced capacity or where demand growth outpaces supply, and recommend deployment adjustments to capture incremental cargo. Intelligence data must be timestamped and sourced, with data freshness indicators visible to planners. The system must distinguish between confirmed intelligence and estimated or projected data, weighting confirmed data more heavily in optimization calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R007	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	AI Model Explainability and Recommendation Justification	The system must provide human-readable explanations for all AI-generated deployment recommendations to build trust and enable informed decision-making by fleet planners. Each recommendation must include: a natural language summary of the key factors driving the recommendation (in both Arabic and English), a ranked list of the top five most influential input variables with their contribution to the recommendation score, a sensitivity analysis showing how the recommendation would change if key inputs varied by plus or minus 10%, and a comparison against the planner's most recent manual deployment decision highlighting improvements and trade-offs. Explanations must be generated using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values or equivalent interpretability techniques. The system must maintain explanation logs for audit purposes, retained for a minimum of three years, accessible by compliance officers and senior management.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R008	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Continuous Learning from Planner Feedback and Decisions	The system must implement a continuous learning loop where fleet planner feedback on AI recommendations is captured and used to improve future recommendations. When a planner accepts, modifies, or rejects an AI recommendation, the system must record the decision along with the planner's reason (selected from a categorized list or entered as free text in Arabic or English). Accepted recommendations must reinforce the model's learned patterns, while rejected recommendations with stated reasons must be analyzed for systematic biases or gaps in the model. Monthly model performance reports must show acceptance rate, modification rate, and rejection rate segmented by recommendation type, trade lane, and planner. The AI engine must detect when its acceptance rate falls below a configurable threshold (default 60%) and trigger an automated model review process that includes retraining with recent planner decision data. Planner expertise preferences (e.g., a planner specializing in Gulf routes) must be factored into personalized recommendation presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R009	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Multi-Objective Optimization with Pareto Frontier Display	The system must support multi-objective optimization that simultaneously considers conflicting objectives such as profit maximization, emission minimization, schedule reliability maximization, and fleet utilization balancing. The optimization engine must generate a Pareto frontier showing the set of non-dominated solutions where improving one objective requires sacrificing another. The Pareto frontier must be visualized as an interactive chart where planners can select different points to view the corresponding deployment configuration details. The system must highlight the knee point of the Pareto frontier (the solution offering the best balance across objectives) as the default recommendation while allowing planners to explore trade-offs by adjusting objective priorities using slider controls. Each Pareto solution must display its performance across all objectives in both absolute values and percentile ranks relative to historical deployment performance. The visualization must support Arabic RTL rendering with axis labels and legends in the user's selected language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R010	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Weather and Disruption Risk Integration for Proactive Planning	The system must integrate weather forecasts and disruption risk assessments into the AI fleet deployment optimization process. Weather data sources must include maritime weather services covering the Arabian Sea, Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, and Indian Ocean regions relevant to Gulf-India shipping routes. The system must model the impact of forecasted adverse weather (cyclones in the Arabian Sea during pre-monsoon and post-monsoon seasons, Shamal dust storms in the Gulf, fog events at JNPT during December-January) on vessel transit times, fuel consumption, and schedule reliability. Disruption risk scores must be calculated for each service route per week, incorporating weather forecasts, historical disruption frequency, and current events (canal closures, port strikes, geopolitical events). The AI optimizer must proactively recommend deployment adjustments such as speed changes, route diversions, or schedule revisions at least 72 hours before forecasted disruptions to minimize operational impact.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R011	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Automated Deployment Recommendation Scheduling	The system must support automated scheduling of AI deployment optimization runs at configurable intervals. By default, the system must execute a full fleet deployment optimization every Monday at 06:00 UTC and an incremental update every day at 06:00 UTC. Full optimization must re-evaluate the entire fleet-to-route assignment from scratch, while incremental optimization must adjust the current deployment based on updated demand forecasts, vessel position changes, and newly received market intelligence since the last full run. Each automated run must generate a deployment recommendation report that is distributed to configured recipients (fleet managers, commercial directors) via email with an executive summary in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must support time zone-aware scheduling so that reports arrive during business hours in Doha (AST), Dubai (GST), Riyadh (AST), and Mumbai (IST). Automated runs must be pausable during system maintenance windows without losing scheduled configurations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R012	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Fleet Deployment Simulation with Digital Twin Integration	The system must support digital twin simulation of fleet deployment scenarios where the entire service network is modeled as an event-driven simulation over a configurable time horizon (default 90 days). The simulation must model vessel movements along assigned routes at planned speeds, cargo loading and discharge operations at each port with realistic productivity rates, bunker consumption and bunkering stops, weather-induced delays sampled from historical distributions, and random disruption events (equipment breakdowns, port closures). Simulation results must include projected financial performance per vessel and per service loop, fleet utilization metrics, schedule reliability statistics, and container flow patterns across the network. Planners must be able to run simulations of AI-recommended deployments before committing to them, comparing simulated outcomes of multiple deployment options. Each simulation run must complete within 10 minutes for a 90-day horizon covering up to 50 vessels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R013	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Constraint Relaxation Suggestions for Infeasible Deployments	The system must detect when the AI optimizer cannot find a feasible deployment solution that satisfies all constraints and provide intelligent suggestions for constraint relaxation. When infeasibility is detected, the system must identify the minimal set of constraints that, if relaxed, would enable a feasible solution. For each constraining factor, the system must explain in natural language (Arabic and English) why it blocks feasibility, quantify the degree of relaxation needed (e.g., allowing one additional day of port idle time, accepting 5% lower utilization on a specific route), and estimate the cost or impact of the relaxation. Common constraint conflicts in the Gulf-India context must be handled, including: insufficient vessels for all committed service loops, draft-restricted vessels assigned to shallow-draft ports like JNPT, and scheduling conflicts at congested terminals. The system must present relaxation options ranked by least commercial impact, allowing planners to approve relaxations selectively.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R005	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Port Cost Comparative Analysis	The system must maintain and analyze port cost structures for all operational ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, Mundra (INMUN), Dammam (SADMM), and Jubail (SAJUB). Port costs must include berth hire, pilotage, towage, mooring, wharfage, container handling charges (per lift for 20ft and 40ft standard, high-cube, and reefer containers), and anchorage fees. The system must compute cost differentials between ports for identical vessel calls and flag these for route optimization. All tariffs must be stored in local currency with automated conversion, and the system must track tariff revision dates with alerts 30 days before known scheduled increases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R014	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	AI Optimizer Performance Monitoring and SLA Compliance	The system must monitor the performance of the AI fleet deployment optimizer itself, tracking key metrics including: optimization run execution time, solution quality score (how close the solution is to the theoretical optimum), recommendation accuracy (predicted versus actual outcomes after implementation), model drift indicators (degradation in prediction accuracy over time), and system resource consumption (CPU, memory, GPU if applicable). Performance metrics must be displayed on a dedicated AI operations dashboard accessible to system administrators and data science teams. The system must enforce SLA compliance for optimization runs: full optimization must complete within 30 minutes and incremental optimization within 5 minutes. If an optimization run exceeds its time limit, the system must return the best feasible solution found so far with a warning indicating suboptimal completion. Monthly AI performance reports must be generated automatically and archived for trend analysis over a minimum retention period of two years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R015	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Role-Based Access Control for AI Recommendations	The system must enforce role-based access control (RBAC) on AI fleet deployment recommendations and optimization functions. Defined roles must include: Fleet Planner (can view and accept or reject recommendations for assigned entity), Senior Fleet Manager (can view recommendations across all entities, approve cross-entity deployments, and modify optimization parameters), Commercial Director (can view recommendations and financial impact analysis, approve commercial threshold overrides), Data Scientist (can access model training data, modify algorithm parameters, initiate model retraining), and System Administrator (can manage optimization schedules, monitor system performance, configure infrastructure). Entity-level segregation must ensure that India entity planners cannot view detailed vessel cost data for Qatar entity vessels unless explicitly authorized via a cross-entity data sharing agreement. All access to AI recommendations and optimization parameters must be logged with user identity, timestamp, action performed, and IP address for security audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R016	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Integration with Voyage Execution for Closed-Loop Optimization	The system must integrate with MOD-058 (Voyage Management) to create a closed-loop optimization cycle where AI deployment recommendations flow into voyage execution and actual voyage outcomes feed back into the optimization models. When a deployment recommendation is approved, the system must automatically generate voyage plan templates in MOD-058 with pre-populated route waypoints, speed profiles, bunker plans, and port call schedules derived from the optimization output. During voyage execution, the system must receive real-time position reports, actual fuel consumption data, and port operation updates to compare against the AI-predicted outcomes. Post-voyage, the system must automatically compute prediction accuracy metrics for each voyage parameter and feed the variance data into the machine learning model's training pipeline. This closed-loop must operate continuously without manual intervention, with exception alerts generated only when prediction variances exceed configurable thresholds (default ±15% for fuel, ±12 hours for transit time).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R017	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Natural Language Interaction for Deployment Queries	The system must provide a natural language interface allowing fleet planners to query the AI optimizer using conversational questions in both Arabic and English. Example queries the system must handle include: 'What is the most profitable vessel to deploy on the Hamad-Jebel Ali-JNPT route next month?', 'Show me the impact of adding a weekly call at Khalifa Port to Service Loop Gulf-Express', 'Why did the optimizer recommend vessel X instead of vessel Y for the India feeder?', and the Arabic equivalent queries. The natural language processor must understand shipping domain terminology including port names, vessel names, trade lane references, and container shipping jargon in both languages. Responses must be generated in the same language as the query with the option to switch. The system must handle ambiguous queries by requesting clarification rather than making assumptions, and must maintain conversation context across multiple related queries within a session. Query-response pairs must be logged for model improvement and audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-1-004-R018	FEAT-057-1-004	MOD-057	Offline AI Recommendation Cache and Degraded Mode	The system must support a degraded operational mode when the AI optimization engine is unavailable due to infrastructure issues, maintenance, or connectivity problems. In degraded mode, the system must serve cached recommendations from the most recent successful optimization run, clearly marked with a timestamp indicating when the recommendation was generated and a warning that it may not reflect current conditions. Fleet planners must be able to use the decision matrix (FEAT-057-1-001) and manual planning tools without dependency on the AI engine. When the AI engine comes back online, the system must automatically trigger an incremental optimization to update recommendations based on any changes that occurred during the downtime. The system must maintain a local cache of the last five full optimization results and the last 30 incremental updates, occupying no more than 5 GB of local storage. Cache data must be encrypted and accessible only to authenticated users with appropriate role permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R001	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Total Cost of Ownership Model per Vessel Class	The system must compute a total cost of ownership (TCO) model for each vessel class in the fleet, including capital costs (acquisition or charter hire), operating expenses (crew, insurance, stores, lubricants), voyage costs (bunker, port dues at Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLP, Jebel Ali AEJEA, and JNPT INJNP), maintenance and dry-docking reserves, and regulatory compliance costs (SOLAS, MARPOL Annex VI). TCO must be expressed in the entity's base currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with USD equivalent, applying real-time exchange rates. The model must support projection horizons of 5, 10, 15, and 25 years with configurable discount rates per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R002	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Charter vs Own Break-Even Analysis	The system must provide a break-even analysis comparing vessel ownership against time-charter for each vessel type and trade lane. The analysis must factor in capital expenditure, financing costs (including Islamic finance structures such as Ijara and Murabaha commonly used in Qatar, UAE, and KSA), operating cost differentials, tax advantages per jurisdiction (Qatar free zone benefits, UAE corporate tax, KSA VAT, Indian tonnage tax scheme), and residual value assumptions. Results must be displayed in both tabular and graphical formats with Arabic and English labels, supporting Hijri and Gregorian date axes on all time-series charts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R006	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Revenue Projection per Vessel Deployment	The system must generate revenue projections for each vessel deployed on a specific route, factoring in slot capacity (TEU nominal and intake), historical utilization rates by trade lane and season, contracted vs spot cargo mix, freight rate assumptions per container type (dry 20GP, 40GP, 40HC, reefer 20RE, 40RE, special equipment flat rack, open top), and surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, peak season surcharge, Suez Canal surcharge). Projections must account for round-trip economics including laden and ballast legs, repositioning empty containers, and cabotage restrictions applicable under Indian and Saudi regulations. Revenue must be calculated in booking currency and converted to entity reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R007	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Fleet Renewal Financial Planning	The system must support fleet renewal planning by modeling the financial impact of phasing out aging vessels and introducing newbuilds or second-hand acquisitions over a 10-25 year horizon. The model must incorporate vessel age profiles, class survey and dry-docking schedules (every 2.5 and 5 years per classification society rules), increasing maintenance costs as vessels age beyond 15 years, emission regulation compliance costs (IMO CII ratings, EU ETS if applicable for transshipment cargo), and scrap value estimates based on lightweight tonnage and prevailing demolition market rates at Alang (India). Financing structures must include conventional loans, Islamic finance (Sukuk, Ijara), and lease-back arrangements with tax treatment per jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R008	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Multi-Entity Consolidated Fleet Economics	The system must produce consolidated financial analysis across all operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) while maintaining entity-level detail. Intercompany vessel charter arrangements must be modeled with proper transfer pricing documentation requirements. The consolidation must handle elimination of intercompany revenues and costs, currency translation adjustments using closing rate for balance sheet items and average rate for income statement items per IAS 21, and minority interest calculations where applicable. Reports must be generated in both IFRS and local GAAP formats (Qatar Financial Markets Authority standards, UAE Federal Tax Authority requirements, ZATCA standards for KSA, and Indian Companies Act 2013 Schedule III format).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R009	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	AI-Driven Fleet Size Recommendation Engine	The system must provide an AI-powered recommendation engine that analyzes historical trade volumes, contracted cargo commitments, seasonal demand patterns across the Gulf-India trade corridor, and forward-looking economic indicators to recommend optimal fleet size. The engine must consider port capacity constraints (Hamad Port berth availability, Jebel Ali gate capacity, JNPT draft restrictions during monsoon season), competitor fleet deployments visible through AIS data analysis, and pipeline infrastructure projects (NEOM port development, Vizhinjam port commissioning). Recommendations must include confidence intervals and must be presented in both Arabic and English with supporting data visualizations. The AI model must be retrained quarterly using the latest 24 months of operational data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R010	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Insurance Cost Modeling per Fleet Configuration	The system must model hull and machinery (H&M) insurance, protection and indemnity (P&I) club calls, war risk premiums, and freight demurrage and defense (FD&D) costs for each fleet configuration scenario. Insurance premiums must be calculated based on vessel age, flag state, classification society, trading area (including Gulf of Oman, Arabian Gulf, and Indian Ocean war risk zone definitions), claims history, and fleet size discount tiers offered by P&I clubs. The system must account for sanctions compliance screening for vessels trading to all Gulf states, Indian ports, and transshipment hubs, ensuring no fleet configuration inadvertently breaches OFAC, EU, or UN sanctions lists that would void insurance coverage.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R011	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Crew Cost Analysis by Flag State and Vessel Type	The system must compute crew costs for each vessel type based on flag state manning requirements, minimum safe manning certificates, and applicable maritime labor convention (MLC 2006) standards. The analysis must cover officer and rating salary scales, overtime provisions, leave and travel allowances, training and certification costs (STCW compliance), victualing, and crew insurance. For vessels under Qatar, UAE, or Saudi flags, Qatarization, Emiratization, and Saudization crew quotas must be factored into cost modeling with premium salary differentials. Indian-flagged vessels must account for DG Shipping manning norms. The system must present crew cost comparisons across flag options to support optimal fleet registration decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R012	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Emission Compliance Cost Forecasting	The system must forecast emission compliance costs per vessel and aggregated across the fleet, incorporating IMO Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) trajectory requirements through 2030, EEXI technical efficiency requirements, and potential regional carbon pricing mechanisms. For vessels with CII ratings at risk of degradation to D or E, the system must model remediation costs including slow steaming revenue impact, energy efficiency technology retrofits (shaft generators, air lubrication, wind-assisted propulsion), and alternative fuel conversion costs. Forecasts must be generated in USD with automatic conversion to entity currencies. The system must alert fleet planners when projected emission profiles threaten charter party compliance clauses or port access restrictions at environmentally sensitive terminals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R013	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Offline Financial Analysis Capability	The system must support offline financial analysis for users operating in areas with limited connectivity, such as onboard vessels or remote port locations. The offline module must cache the latest vessel financial data, port tariff schedules, exchange rates (with timestamp of last refresh), and scenario parameters locally. Users must be able to create and run fleet mix scenarios, generate TCO comparisons, and produce break-even analyses while offline. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize offline work with the central database, detecting and flagging conflicts where underlying data (exchange rates, tariffs) changed during the offline period. Offline sessions must not exceed 72 hours before mandatory resynchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R014	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Financial Dashboard with KPI Scorecards	The system must provide a financial dashboard displaying key fleet economics KPIs including daily running cost per vessel, time-charter equivalent (TCE) earnings per vessel and route, fleet-wide EBITDA margin, return on assets, and cost per TEU-mile. KPI scorecards must support threshold-based color coding (green/amber/red) with configurable targets per entity. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper mirroring of charts, legends, and navigation elements. Hijri calendar dates must appear alongside Gregorian dates when the user locale is set to Arabic. Dashboards must refresh automatically every 15 minutes or on-demand, and must be exportable to PDF format preserving bilingual formatting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R015	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Audit Trail for Financial Analysis Assumptions	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all assumptions, parameters, and input data used in every financial analysis and scenario run. The audit log must capture the user identity, timestamp (in both Hijri and Gregorian), entity context, exchange rates applied, discount rates selected, fuel price assumptions, port tariff versions, and any manual overrides to AI-generated recommendations. Audit records must be immutable and retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32/2021, KSA ZATCA record retention requirements, and Indian Companies Act 2013 Section 128 mandates. The trail must be searchable and exportable for internal and external audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-001-R016	FEAT-057-2-001	MOD-057	Sensitivity Analysis on Currency Fluctuation Impact	The system must perform multi-currency sensitivity analysis showing the impact of exchange rate fluctuations on fleet financial performance. The analysis must model simultaneous movements in QAR/USD, AED/USD, SAR/USD, and INR/USD pairs, incorporating the pegged nature of QAR, AED, and SAR to USD and the floating nature of INR. Stress test scenarios must include INR depreciation of 5%, 10%, 15%, and 20% against USD, and potential de-pegging scenarios for Gulf currencies. The system must calculate the net exposure per entity considering natural hedges from revenue and cost currency matching, and must recommend hedging strategies where unhedged exposure exceeds configurable thresholds. Results must be presented with both tabular data and tornado chart visualizations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R001	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Vessel Substitution Trigger and Reason Classification	The system must support initiation of vessel substitution workflows triggered by defined events including mechanical breakdown, dry-docking schedule conflict, weather-related delay exceeding 48 hours, port congestion at Hamad Port or JNPT causing cascading schedule disruption, regulatory detention, sanctions-related trading restriction, and commercial redeployment decisions. Each substitution must be classified with a primary reason code and optional secondary codes from a configurable taxonomy. The classification must support Arabic and English reason descriptions and must be mandatory before the substitution workflow can proceed. Historical substitution reason data must be available for trend analysis to identify recurring fleet reliability issues.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R002	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Substitute Vessel Eligibility Matching	The system must automatically identify eligible substitute vessels based on matching criteria including vessel capacity (TEU nominal ±15% of original), container type compatibility (dry, reefer plug count, dangerous goods certification, IMO class approvals), draft restrictions at destination ports (JNPT channel depth 14.0m, Hamad Port 16.0m), crane compatibility requirements, flag state acceptability, and current geographic position for timely deployment. The matching algorithm must rank candidates by estimated repositioning cost and time, considering current vessel commitments and contractual obligations. AI must recommend the top three substitution options with a cost-benefit comparison. Vessels under different operating entities must be included with intercompany charter rate implications clearly shown.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R003	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Cargo Rebooking and Transfer Workflow	The system must provide an automated cargo rebooking workflow when a vessel substitution occurs, transferring all confirmed bookings from the original vessel to the substitute vessel. The workflow must handle capacity differences where the substitute vessel has fewer slots by applying a priority ranking based on booking contract tier (committed volume accounts first), cargo type priority (reefer before dry, hazardous cargo compatibility), and revenue per TEU. Bookings that cannot be accommodated must be flagged for manual intervention with recommended alternative sailings. Bill of lading references must be preserved, and all booking amendments must generate automatic notifications to shippers, consignees, and freight forwarders in their preferred language (Arabic or English).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R004	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Schedule Impact Assessment and Propagation	The system must calculate the schedule impact of a vessel substitution on downstream port calls, including estimated arrival and departure time changes at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, and all intermediate ports on the rotation. The assessment must consider the substitute vessel's speed capabilities, current fuel consumption profile, canal transit schedules (Suez Canal convoy timings), and port berth window availability. Schedule changes must automatically propagate to all integrated systems including port community systems, terminal operating systems, customs pre-arrival manifests (Qatar Single Window, Dubai Trade, ICEGATE India), and container tracking platforms. The impact must be communicated in both Hijri and Gregorian calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R005	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Commercial Impact and Demurrage Liability Calculation	The system must calculate the commercial impact of each vessel substitution including additional voyage costs (bunker differential, extra port calls, canal transit fees), potential demurrage and detention liabilities arising from delayed delivery of containers at destination, dead-freight claims where substitute vessel capacity is smaller, and contractual penalty exposures under service contracts and volume commitments. Calculations must apply the correct currency for each commercial relationship (typically USD for international freight, local currency for domestic charges at Indian ports in INR). The system must generate a substitution cost summary report itemizing all incremental costs and potential revenue impacts, with approval workflow routing to the appropriate entity finance controller.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R006	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Vessel Swap Agreement Documentation	The system must generate vessel swap agreement documents when substitution involves vessels between different operating entities or between owned and chartered tonnage. The agreement must capture the swap period, hire rate differential, off-hire provisions, bunker adjustment terms (quantity and price at delivery and redelivery), condition survey requirements, and insurance coverage transfer terms. Documents must be generated in dual-language format (Arabic and English) for swaps involving Gulf entities, with English and Hindi supplements for Indian entity swaps. The system must apply the correct legal jurisdiction and arbitration clause based on the entities involved (Qatar Maritime Law, UAE Maritime Code Federal Law No. 26/1981, Saudi Maritime Commercial Law, or Indian Merchant Shipping Act 1958).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R007	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Real-Time Substitution Status Tracking Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard tracking all active vessel substitution events across the fleet, showing the substitution stage (initiated, vessel identified, cargo transfer in progress, schedule updated, completed, closed), percentage of bookings successfully transferred, outstanding issues requiring manual intervention, and estimated financial impact. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, trade lane, vessel class, and substitution reason. Map visualization must show the original vessel position, substitute vessel position, and affected port rotation with ETA updates. The dashboard must function in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with full bidirectional text support for vessel names and port names that may contain mixed-script content.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R008	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Container Equipment Rebalancing on Substitution	The system must assess and manage container equipment imbalances created by vessel substitutions. When a substitute vessel has different equipment capacity (fewer reefer plugs, different cell guide dimensions restricting 45ft containers, reduced dangerous goods stowage zones), the system must identify affected containers and generate repositioning plans. Empty container availability at each port in the rotation must be checked against the revised vessel equipment profile. The system must calculate additional equipment repositioning costs, including inland transport from depot to port at JNPT (where depots may be in ICD Tughlakabad or ICD Dadri) and equipment interchange fees at Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port. Imbalance alerts must be issued to equipment control teams across all affected entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R009	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Regulatory and Documentation Compliance Check	The system must perform automated compliance checks when a substitute vessel is proposed, verifying that the vessel meets all regulatory requirements for each port in the rotation. Checks must include vessel flag state acceptance (certain flags may be restricted at specific Gulf ports), ISM and ISPS Code certification validity, class society certificates (hull, machinery, load line, safety equipment) with minimum 3 months validity remaining, crew certification for the trade area, and port state control inspection history (targeting coefficient below threshold for Tokyo MOU and Indian Ocean MOU). The system must verify the substitute vessel is not on any sanctions blacklist (OFAC SDN, EU consolidated list, UN Security Council) and that its P&I club provides coverage for all ports in the rotation including Indian coastal calls requiring specific Indian P&I cover.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R010	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Stakeholder Notification Management	The system must manage automated multi-channel notifications to all stakeholders affected by a vessel substitution. Notification recipients must include shippers, consignees, freight forwarders, customs brokers, port authorities (Mwani Qatar, Abu Dhabi Ports, DP World, Jawaharlal Nehru Port Authority), terminal operators, feeder service operators, inland transport providers, and insurance underwriters. Notifications must be dispatched via configurable channels (email, SMS, EDI IFTMIN/IFTSTA messages, API webhooks). Content must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English), include the new vessel name and IMO number, revised schedule with ETAs, and any required action items. Notification templates must be customizable per entity and must comply with GDPR and local data protection regulations in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R011	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Historical Substitution Performance Analytics	The system must maintain comprehensive analytics on vessel substitution history, tracking metrics including substitution frequency per vessel and route, average time to identify and deploy substitute vessel, cargo transfer completion rate, schedule recovery time (days to return to normal rotation), total financial impact per substitution event, and customer satisfaction impact measured through subsequent booking behavior. Analytics must be segmentable by entity, vessel class, trade lane, and time period (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian calendar groupings). AI-powered trend analysis must identify patterns such as seasonal substitution peaks, vessel reliability degradation curves, and trade lanes most vulnerable to disruption, generating proactive recommendations for fleet planners to mitigate future substitution risks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R012	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Automated Voyage Estimate for Substitution Scenarios	The system must generate automated voyage estimates for each substitution option, calculating the complete financial picture including bunker costs based on the substitute vessel's actual consumption profile and current fuel prices at Fujairah or other bunkering points, port disbursements at each port using the latest tariff data, canal dues (Suez Canal tolls based on SCNT and container count), agency fees, and any additional insurance premiums for deviation from the substitute vessel's normal trading pattern. The voyage estimate must compare the substitute voyage economics against the original voyage estimate to quantify the incremental cost. All calculations must handle multi-currency with proper exchange rate date stamping and must be auditable to support post-voyage financial reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R013	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Charter Party Compliance Verification for Swaps	The system must verify charter party compliance when a substitution involves chartered vessels, checking that the proposed swap does not violate charter party terms including trading limits (geographic exclusions, war zone restrictions, sanctioned country calls), speed and consumption warranties, cargo exclusion clauses (no hazardous cargo, no scrap metal), sub-charter consent requirements, and maintenance obligations. For time-chartered vessels, the system must calculate off-hire implications and whether the substitution event qualifies as an off-hire trigger under the specific charter party form (NYPE 2015, Boxtime 2004). The system must flag any potential disputes and route them to the chartering department with the relevant charter party clauses highlighted in both the original language and translated to the user's interface language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R014	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Contingency Vessel Pool Pre-Configuration	The system must allow fleet planners to pre-configure contingency vessel pools for each major trade route, designating vessels that can serve as immediate substitutes with minimal commercial disruption. Pool configuration must specify priority order, maximum repositioning distance (in nautical miles), cost thresholds for automatic substitution approval, and required lead time for activation. The system must continuously monitor pool vessel positions via AIS integration and update availability status in real-time. When a substitution need arises, the contingency pool must be evaluated first, with AI providing a readiness score based on current position, cargo load status, and crew rest compliance. Pool configurations must be reviewable and approvable per entity, with cross-entity pools requiring bilateral approval from both entity fleet managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R015	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Post-Substitution Review and Lessons Learned Workflow	The system must enforce a post-substitution review workflow within 14 days of completing each vessel substitution event. The review must capture actual vs estimated costs, schedule recovery performance, customer impact assessment, root cause analysis of the substitution trigger, and actionable recommendations for future prevention. Reviews must be completed by the fleet operations manager and approved by the entity operations director. The system must store all reviews in a searchable knowledge base accessible to all entities, supporting full-text search in both Arabic and English. AI must analyze the cumulative review database to generate quarterly fleet reliability reports with predictive maintenance and deployment recommendations, identifying systemic issues across the multi-entity fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-002-R016	FEAT-057-2-002	MOD-057	Offline Substitution Initiation and Approval	The system must support offline initiation and preliminary approval of vessel substitutions for users with limited connectivity, particularly operations staff aboard vessels or at remote anchorage locations. The offline module must cache the current fleet position data, vessel specifications, basic port requirements, and approval workflow routing rules. Users must be able to initiate a substitution request, select a preliminary substitute from cached vessel data, and obtain first-level operational approval offline. Upon connectivity restoration, the system must synchronize the offline substitution with the central system, triggering full compliance checks, financial calculations, and remaining approval steps. The offline workflow must enforce a maximum staleness threshold of 24 hours for cached vessel position data before requiring resynchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R001	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Type and Structure Definition	The system must support creation and management of multiple long-term deployment contract types including time charter parties (TCP), bareboat charters (BBC), contracts of affreightment (COA), volume commitment agreements (VCA), dedicated vessel service agreements (DVSA), and slot charter arrangements. Each contract type must have configurable templates with mandatory and optional clause libraries. Templates must support Arabic and English dual-language versions with clause-level translation mapping. The system must enforce minimum required fields per contract type aligned with BIMCO standard forms (NYPE 2015 for TCP, Barecon 2017 for BBC, GENCOA for COA) while allowing entity-specific customizations for Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi MISA, and Indian DG Shipping requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R002	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Financial Terms and Payment Scheduling	The system must manage comprehensive financial terms for each deployment contract including hire rate (daily, monthly, or lump sum), payment frequency (semi-monthly in advance per NYPE, monthly, quarterly), currency of payment (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR or multi-currency splits), escalation clauses (annual percentage, CPI-linked, bunker adjustment), profit-sharing mechanisms for pool arrangements, and address commission and brokerage percentages. Payment schedules must be auto-generated based on contract terms with invoice creation triggers. The system must handle Islamic finance structures where applicable, including Ijara (lease) payment schedules with profit rate instead of interest. All financial terms must be stored with effective dates to track amendments over the contract lifecycle.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R003	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Duration and Option Period Management	The system must manage contract duration including firm period, optional extension periods (with notice requirements), early termination provisions, and redelivery windows. For time charters, the system must track minimum and maximum contract duration with tolerance margins (typically ±30 days), and calculate the earliest and latest redelivery dates. Option declaration deadlines must trigger automated alerts at configurable intervals (90, 60, 30 days before declaration deadline). The system must maintain a contract timeline visualization showing firm periods, option periods, and key milestone dates in both Hijri and Gregorian calendars. When contracts span multiple fiscal years, the system must project annual financial commitments per entity for budgeting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R004	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Performance Warranty Monitoring and Claims	The system must continuously monitor vessel performance against contractual warranties including speed (laden and ballast), fuel consumption at various speeds and drafts, and cargo capacity. Performance data must be sourced from noon reports, AIS data, and vessel performance monitoring systems. The system must calculate weather-adjusted performance using the Beaufort scale exclusion method (typically excluding observations above Beaufort Force 4-5 as defined in the charter party). When performance falls below warranty thresholds, the system must automatically calculate under-performance claims in the contract currency, applying the contractual claim methodology (speed claim, consumption claim, or combined). Claims must be generated with supporting evidence packages including voyage data, weather records, and current/tide information for Gulf and Indian Ocean waters.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R005	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Off-Hire Event Tracking and Calculation	The system must track all off-hire events during the contract period, classifying them per the applicable off-hire clause (typically Clause 17 NYPE 2015). Off-hire categories must include breakdown of machinery, dry-docking, crew deficiency, deviation for vessel's purposes, strike of crew, and detention by authorities. The system must record off-hire start and end times to the nearest minute, calculate hire deductions including bunker consumption adjustment during off-hire periods, and maintain a running off-hire balance per contract. Dispute flags must be available for contested off-hire periods with supporting document attachments. The system must generate periodic off-hire summaries (monthly and per voyage) with trend analysis to identify vessels with deteriorating reliability, and must express all amounts in the contract currency with entity reporting currency equivalents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R006	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Bunker Management Under Charter Contracts	The system must manage bunker quantities and values at delivery, during the charter period, and at redelivery. At delivery, the system must record bunker quantities on board (VLSFO, HSFO, MGO/MDO, LNG where applicable) by tank with prices agreed in the charter party. During the charter period, the system must track bunker purchases, consumption, and remaining on board (ROB) quantities. At redelivery, the system must calculate the bunker adjustment based on contractual terms (typically same quantity ±10% of delivery quantity, priced at delivery price or current market price as per charter party). Bunker clauses referencing specific price benchmarks (Platts Fujairah, Singapore, Mumbai) must be configurable. All bunker financial transactions must be processed in the contract currency with appropriate entity-level accounting entries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R007	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Compliance and Obligation Tracking	The system must track all contractual obligations for both parties (owner and charterer) with compliance status indicators. Obligations must include trading limits (geographic restrictions, excluded ports, war zone definitions per contract), cargo restrictions (IMO class exclusions, commodity blacklists), maintenance obligations (class maintenance, hull cleaning frequency, propeller polishing schedule), insurance requirements (minimum H&M value, P&I club rating, war risk coverage), and reporting obligations (noon reports, voyage reports, monthly statements). The system must generate automated compliance alerts when obligations are approaching due dates or when violations are detected. Non-compliance events must be logged with severity classification and must trigger configurable escalation workflows to entity fleet managers and legal departments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R008	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Amendment and Addendum Management	The system must support contract amendments and addenda with full version control, maintaining the complete amendment history for each contract. Each amendment must capture the effective date, clauses affected, old and new values, reason for amendment, and approving parties from both sides. The system must generate amendment documents in the contract language (Arabic, English, or dual-language) with proper legal referencing to the original clause numbers. Amendment approval workflows must require sign-off from the entity's legal, commercial, and finance departments, with configurable escalation to entity general manager for amendments exceeding financial impact thresholds. The system must maintain a consolidated view of the current effective contract terms incorporating all amendments, enabling users to view the contract as-amended or trace the evolution of any specific clause.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R009	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Renewal and Renegotiation Pipeline	The system must maintain a contract renewal pipeline tracking all deployment contracts approaching expiry within a configurable horizon (default 12 months). The pipeline must classify contracts by renewal likelihood (certain, probable, possible, unlikely) based on performance history, market conditions, and relationship factors. For each expiring contract, the system must generate a renewal brief containing current contract terms, historical performance metrics, market benchmarking data (current time-charter rates for equivalent vessels from Baltic Exchange or similar indices), counterparty relationship history, and AI-generated recommended renewal terms. The pipeline must support Kanban-style workflow stages (identified, contacted, negotiating, terms agreed, documentation, signed) with drag-and-drop progression. Dashboard views must be available in both Arabic and English with proper RTL Kanban board rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R010	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Multi-Entity Contract Allocation and Cost Sharing	The system must manage deployment contracts that span multiple operating entities, allocating costs and revenues based on configurable allocation methodologies. For vessels deployed on routes serving multiple entities (e.g., a vessel on a Gulf rotation calling Hamad Port for Qatar entity and Jebel Ali for UAE entity), the system must calculate per-entity allocation based on TEU-miles, port call days, revenue contribution, or negotiated fixed percentages. Intercompany settlement records must be generated automatically with proper transfer pricing documentation compliant with OECD guidelines and local transfer pricing regulations in Qatar, UAE (Federal Decree-Law No. 47/2022), KSA (ZATCA transfer pricing rules), and India (Section 92 Income Tax Act). Allocation methodology changes must require multi-entity approval and must be effective from the start of a fiscal period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R011	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Document Repository and Digital Signatures	The system must provide a secure document repository for all contract-related documents including the main charter party, addenda, condition surveys, performance claims, correspondence, and supporting calculations. Documents must be organized hierarchically by contract with full-text search capability in both Arabic and English. The system must support digital signature workflows compliant with Qatar Law No. 16/2010 on electronic transactions, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 46/2021, KSA Anti-Cyber Crime Law and e-transaction regulations, and India Information Technology Act 2000. Digital signatures must support multi-party signing sequences (owner, charterer, broker) with timestamps and certificate-based authentication. Signed documents must be tamper-evident with SHA-256 hash verification. Document access must be controlled by role-based permissions with entity-level isolation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R012	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Delivery and Redelivery Condition Survey Management	The system must manage the delivery and redelivery survey process for chartered vessels, tracking on-hire and off-hire survey scheduling, surveyor appointment, condition findings, and dispute items. The system must maintain a standardized checklist covering hull condition (paint, anodes, marine growth), machinery condition (main engine hours, auxiliary engine hours, boiler condition), cargo holds/tanks cleanliness, navigation and communication equipment, safety equipment validity, and spare parts inventory. Survey findings must be recorded with photographic evidence attachments and categorized as acceptable, noted, or disputed. Disputed items must enter a resolution workflow with financial quantification of the dispute value in the contract currency. The system must generate on-hire and off-hire bunker survey records and compare delivery vs redelivery conditions to calculate fair wear and tear versus damage claims.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R013	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Financial Forecasting and Accruals	The system must generate financial forecasts for all active deployment contracts, projecting hire payments, estimated off-hire deductions, bunker adjustment estimates, and performance claim provisions over the remaining contract duration. Forecasts must be generated monthly and must feed into entity-level financial planning and budgeting systems. The system must calculate accounting accruals for hire expense/revenue on a daily pro-rata basis, handling period-end cut-offs correctly across different entity fiscal year-ends (Qatar: March or December, UAE: December, KSA: December aligned with Hijri, India: March). Foreign currency contract forecasts must apply forward exchange rates where available and spot rates otherwise. The system must support IFRS 16 lease accounting treatment for qualifying charter arrangements, calculating right-of-use assets and lease liabilities per entity requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R001	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Real-Time Empty Inventory Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time dashboard displaying the count and status of all empty containers across Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). The dashboard must refresh at intervals no greater than 5 minutes and support both card-view and tabular-view layouts. Each port location must display container counts broken down by ISO container type codes (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 45HC, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR). The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), depot code, and container ownership type (owned, leased, shipper-owned). All numeric values must be formatted according to the user's locale settings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R014	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Dispute Resolution and Arbitration Tracking	The system must manage dispute resolution processes arising from deployment contracts, tracking dispute items from identification through negotiation, mediation, arbitration, and settlement. Each dispute must be classified by type (off-hire, performance, bunker, damage, early termination) and severity (minor under USD 50,000, moderate USD 50,000-500,000, major over USD 500,000). The system must track arbitration proceedings including appointed arbitrators, hearing dates, submission deadlines, and award amounts. Applicable arbitration venues must be configurable per contract (London LMAA, Singapore SIAC, Qatar International Court and Dispute Resolution Centre, DIAC Dubai, Riyadh Chamber of Commerce). Financial provisions for ongoing disputes must be maintained and reported per entity with currency-appropriate reserve amounts. The system must generate dispute timeline reports for legal counsel and management review in the appropriate entity language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R015	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Contract Portfolio Risk Assessment	The system must provide portfolio-level risk assessment across all active deployment contracts, evaluating exposure to counterparty credit risk, market rate risk, currency risk, geopolitical risk, and concentration risk. Counterparty risk must be assessed using financial indicators (credit ratings, payment history, publicly available financial statements) with automated monitoring for credit deterioration signals. Market rate risk must compare contracted rates against current market indices to quantify above-market and below-market contract positions. The system must calculate portfolio-level Value at Risk (VaR) for the deployment contract book using Monte Carlo simulation with configurable confidence intervals (95%, 99%). Risk reports must be generated per entity and consolidated, supporting both Arabic and English formats, with entity-specific risk appetite thresholds configurable by the entity risk committee.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R016	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Automated Contract Expiry Notifications and Handover	The system must generate automated notifications at configurable intervals before contract expiry (180, 120, 90, 60, 30, 14, and 7 days). Notifications must be routed to the entity commercial manager, fleet operations manager, finance controller, and legal counsel. Each notification must include a contract summary, remaining obligations (outstanding claims, pending surveys, bunker adjustment estimates), and a redelivery preparation checklist. For vessels being redelivered, the system must initiate a redelivery preparation workflow including scheduling condition surveys, arranging bunker measurement, preparing final hire statements, and notifying port agents at the agreed redelivery port. The system must generate final settlement calculations including last hire payment proration, bunker adjustment, outstanding off-hire deductions, and any performance claim settlements, all expressed in the contract currency with entity reporting currency conversion at the settlement date exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-003-R017	FEAT-057-2-003	MOD-057	Audit and Regulatory Compliance for Contract Management	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all contract management activities including creation, amendment, approval, financial transactions, and dispute resolution actions. Audit records must capture user identity, action timestamp (Hijri and Gregorian), entity context, and before/after values for all data changes. The system must support regulatory compliance requirements including QFMA reporting for Qatar-registered entities, ADGM and DFSA requirements for UAE entities, CMA requirements for KSA-registered entities, and SEBI and RBI compliance for Indian entities. Contract data must be retainable for the maximum statutory period across all jurisdictions (minimum 10 years). The system must generate compliance reports on demand for regulatory examinations and must support data extraction in formats required by each jurisdiction's regulatory authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R001	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Market Data Feed Integration Framework	The system must support integration with major freight market data providers including Baltic Exchange (SCFI, BDI component routes), Shanghai Shipping Exchange (CCFI), Drewry (WCI), Freightos (FBX), Xeneta, and Container Trade Statistics (CTS). Integration must use provider-specific APIs with configurable polling intervals (real-time where available, minimum daily refresh). The framework must handle data normalization across different index methodologies, unit conversions (per TEU, per FEU, per slot), and currency standardization to USD with local currency conversion to QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. Data ingestion must include quality validation checks for outliers, stale data detection (flagging data older than 24 hours for daily indices), and automated fallback to secondary providers when primary feeds are unavailable. Historical data must be retained for a minimum of 10 years to support long-term trend analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R002	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Trade Lane Rate Benchmarking Dashboard	The system must provide a benchmarking dashboard comparing the company's achieved freight rates against market indices for each major trade lane. Trade lanes relevant to Gulf-India operations must include Gulf to Indian subcontinent (Jebel Ali/Hamad to JNPT/Mundra), Gulf to Far East (transshipment via Colombo or Singapore), Gulf to Europe (via Suez), Gulf to East Africa, and intra-Gulf feeder routes. Benchmarking must show spot rate comparisons, contract rate positioning (above/below market percentile), and trend analysis over configurable periods (weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually). The dashboard must support both Gregorian and Hijri date selectors, render correctly in RTL Arabic layout with proper chart axis mirroring, and allow entity-level filtering to compare performance across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R003	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	AI-Powered Freight Rate Forecasting	The system must provide AI-powered freight rate forecasting for key trade lanes using machine learning models trained on historical rate data, macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth, trade volumes, manufacturing PMI for GCC and India), supply-side factors (fleet orderbook, scrapping rates, vessel deliveries), seasonal patterns (Ramadan demand shifts, Indian festival season, Chinese New Year), and disruption factors (Suez Canal delays, port congestion indices, geopolitical events in the Gulf region). Forecasts must be generated for 1-week, 1-month, 3-month, and 6-month horizons with confidence intervals and probability distributions. The system must track forecast accuracy over time, reporting Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) per trade lane and horizon. Models must be retrained monthly with the latest data, and forecasts must be available in USD with automatic conversion to entity currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R004	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Competitor Fleet Deployment Tracking	The system must track and analyze competitor fleet deployments on routes overlapping with the company's operations. Using AIS data integration and published liner service schedules, the system must identify competitor vessel deployments, capacity changes (vessel upsizing or downsizing on routes), new service launches, and service withdrawals on Gulf-India and connected trade lanes. Competitor analysis must include capacity share calculations per trade lane, vessel utilization estimates based on draft readings, and deployed fleet age and efficiency profiles. The system must maintain competitor profiles for major container lines operating in the Gulf-India market including MSC, Maersk, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, and regional operators. AI must generate competitive intelligence briefs highlighting significant deployment changes that may impact the company's market position and rate environment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R005	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Trade Flow Volume Analysis and Visualization	The system must analyze and visualize container trade flow volumes between origin-destination pairs relevant to the company's operations. Data sources must include customs statistics from Qatar (General Authority of Customs), UAE (Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs and Port Security), KSA (ZATCA), and India (DGCI&S), supplemented by commercial data from CTS and IHS Markit. Trade flow visualization must include animated flow maps showing volume movement between ports with thickness proportional to TEU volume, time-series charts of trade volume trends with seasonality decomposition, and commodity breakdown by HS code chapter. The system must support drill-down from country-level to port-level flows and must display data in both English and Arabic with proper bilingual labeling of trade partners, commodities, and geographic entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R006	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Supply-Demand Balance Analysis	The system must calculate and display the supply-demand balance for each trade lane, comparing deployed vessel capacity (supply measured in TEU-miles) against estimated cargo demand (measured in TEU volume and ton-miles). Supply analysis must factor in vessel fleet list (ordered, under construction, active, laid up, scrapped) from sources like Clarksons or IHS Maritime, speed adjustments affecting effective supply (slow steaming reducing capacity), and seasonal deployment shifts. Demand analysis must incorporate forward booking indicators, economic forecasts for GCC and Indian markets, infrastructure project cargo pipelines (Saudi Vision 2030, Qatar FIFA legacy projects, Indian Sagarmala program), and e-commerce growth projections. The system must generate a supply-demand ratio index per trade lane and project forward balance for 6, 12, and 24 months, alerting fleet planners to anticipated over-supply or under-supply conditions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R007	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Bunker Market Intelligence Integration	The system must integrate bunker fuel market intelligence including daily price assessments from Platts, Argus, and Ship & Bunker for key bunkering locations relevant to Gulf-India operations (Fujairah AEFUJ, Singapore SGSIN, Colombo LKCMB, Mumbai INBOM, Jeddah SAJED). Price tracking must cover VLSFO 0.5%, HSFO 3.5%, MGO, LSMGO, and emerging alternative fuels (LNG bunker, methanol, ammonia where available). The system must calculate bunker price spreads between locations to optimize bunkering strategies, model the financial impact of fuel choice (VLSFO vs HSFO with scrubber) on fleet economics, and generate forward bunker price curves using commodity futures data. Bunker cost projections must feed directly into fleet deployment scenario models, with prices available in USD and convertible to all entity currencies for budgeting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R008	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Port Congestion and Efficiency Intelligence	The system must provide real-time port congestion intelligence for all operational ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, JNPT, Mundra, Dammam, and Jubail, as well as key transshipment hubs (Colombo, Singapore, Salalah). Metrics must include average vessel waiting time at anchorage, berth productivity (moves per hour per crane), average turnaround time by vessel size category, and yard utilization percentage where available. Data must be sourced from AIS vessel tracking (measuring time between arrival at anchorage and departure from berth), port authority published statistics, and terminal operator data feeds. The system must calculate congestion impact on schedule reliability and fleet deployment efficiency, generate congestion trend charts, and trigger alerts when waiting times at any port exceed configurable thresholds that could impact deployment schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R009	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Seasonal Demand Pattern Library	The system must maintain a library of seasonal demand patterns for each trade lane, derived from analysis of at least five years of historical booking data, trade flow statistics, and market indices. Patterns must capture regular seasonal variations including Ramadan period demand shifts (reduced Gulf import volumes, pre-Ramadan inventory build-up), Indian festival season surges (Diwali, Dussehra driving consumer goods imports), Chinese New Year factory shutdowns affecting Far East transshipment volumes, summer peak seasons for specific commodities, and winter reefer season patterns. Each seasonal pattern must include statistical confidence measures and year-over-year trend adjustments. The library must support Hijri calendar alignment for Islamic seasonal events and Gregorian calendar for fixed-date events. Fleet planners must be able to overlay seasonal patterns on deployment scenarios to optimize vessel positioning for anticipated demand peaks and troughs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R010	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Geopolitical Risk Assessment for Trade Routes	The system must integrate geopolitical risk intelligence affecting maritime trade routes relevant to Gulf-India operations. Risk categories must include military conflict and piracy zones (Gulf of Aden, Strait of Hormuz, Red Sea), sanctions regime changes affecting port calls or cargo types, canal transit disruption risks (Suez Canal blockage, operational restrictions), diplomatic tensions affecting bilateral trade volumes between Gulf states and India, and regulatory changes impacting vessel operations (emission control areas, ballast water management zones). The system must source data from recognized maritime security providers (UKMTO, MSCHOA, IMB Piracy Reporting Centre) and geopolitical intelligence feeds. Risk assessments must update daily and must be presented on a route map overlay with risk level indicators (low, moderate, elevated, high, critical). Fleet deployment recommendations must factor in current risk levels, and the system must calculate insurance cost impacts of routing through elevated-risk zones.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R011	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Market Intelligence Report Generation	The system must generate automated market intelligence reports at configurable frequencies (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly) consolidating freight rate movements, trade flow changes, fleet deployment shifts, bunker market developments, port congestion updates, and geopolitical risk changes. Reports must be customizable by entity and user role with sections relevant to their operational scope. The weekly report must include AI-generated market commentary summarizing key developments and their implications for fleet deployment decisions. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English versions with proper RTL formatting, charts with mirrored axes, and bilingual data tables. Distribution must be via email, in-app notification, and PDF download. The system must maintain a report archive searchable by date, topic, and trade lane, supporting historical market research across both Hijri and Gregorian calendar periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R012	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Rate Volatility and Risk Metrics	The system must calculate and display freight rate volatility metrics for each trade lane including standard deviation of daily/weekly rate changes, coefficient of variation, maximum drawdown from peak rates, and Value at Risk (VaR) for the company's rate exposure on contract vs spot mix. Volatility calculations must use a minimum of 52 weeks of historical data with configurable lookback periods up to 5 years. The system must classify trade lanes by volatility regime (low, moderate, high) using statistical breakpoints. Risk metrics must include the company's revenue sensitivity to rate changes per trade lane, scenario analysis showing impact of 10%, 20%, and 30% rate declines on entity profitability, and correlation analysis between trade lane rate movements to identify diversification benefits. All monetary amounts must be calculable in USD, QAR, AED, SAR, and INR with consistent exchange rate dating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R013	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Newbuilding and Secondhand Market Intelligence	The system must integrate vessel sale and purchase market intelligence including newbuilding prices by vessel type and size from major shipyards (Korean, Chinese, Japanese), secondhand vessel prices by age and type from brokers and platforms (VesselsValue, Clarksons), demolition prices per LDT at Indian recycling yards (Alang), Bangladeshi yards (Chittagong), and Turkish yards (Aliaga). Price data must be updated weekly at minimum and must cover container vessel categories from feeder (500 TEU) to ultra-large (24,000+ TEU). The system must generate asset value trend charts, calculate depreciation curves against purchase price for owned fleet, and estimate current market value of each owned vessel for balance sheet and fleet renewal planning. Vessel valuation reports must be generated per entity in the entity's reporting currency with USD equivalents, supporting financial reporting and loan covenant compliance monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R014	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Charterer and Shipper Market Behavior Analysis	The system must analyze charterer and shipper market behavior patterns to inform deployment decisions. Analysis must include cargo booking lead times by trade lane and customer segment, spot vs contract cargo ratios and trends, cargo type mix evolution (standard dry, reefer, hazardous, out-of-gauge, special equipment), and directional imbalance trends affecting empty repositioning costs. The system must identify emerging trade patterns such as new commodity flows between Gulf and Indian ports (e.g., petrochemical product growth from Ras Laffan/Jubail to Indian west coast), shifts in INCOTERMS usage affecting shipping line selection (CIF vs FOB trends), and changes in shipper consolidation patterns. AI must generate customer behavior insights highlighting opportunities for deployment adjustments, new service introductions, or capacity reallocation, with recommendations presented in both Arabic and English for respective entity commercial teams.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R015	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Offline Market Data Access and Caching	The system must provide offline access to market intelligence data for users operating in environments with limited or intermittent connectivity. The offline cache must include the latest 90 days of freight rate indices, bunker prices, port congestion metrics, and vessel position data, along with the most recent market intelligence reports and seasonal pattern library. Cache synchronization must occur automatically when connectivity is available, with incremental updates to minimize bandwidth usage. The offline module must clearly display the last synchronization timestamp and flag data freshness warnings when cached data exceeds 48 hours for rate indices or 7 days for market reports. Users must be able to run rate benchmarking comparisons, view historical trends, and access previously generated forecasts while offline. Upon reconnection, any offline-generated analysis bookmarks or annotations must be synchronized to the central system for sharing across entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R016	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Market Data API for External System Integration	The system must expose a RESTful API allowing other ERP modules and external systems to consume market intelligence data. The API must provide endpoints for current and historical freight rates by trade lane, bunker prices by location, port congestion metrics, vessel valuations, and trade flow volumes. API authentication must use OAuth 2.0 with entity-level API keys and rate limiting per consumer. Response formats must include JSON and XML with proper Unicode support for Arabic content. The API must support query parameters for date ranges, currency conversion (to any of the five operational currencies), and aggregation levels (daily, weekly, monthly). Webhook subscriptions must be available for real-time alerts when rates cross configurable thresholds or when significant market events are detected. API usage must be metered and reportable per consuming system and entity for cost allocation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-057-2-004-R017	FEAT-057-2-004	MOD-057	Audit Trail for Market Data and Analysis Activities	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all market data ingestion, user analysis activities, and system-generated recommendations. The audit log must record data feed timestamps and source identifiers, any manual data corrections or overrides with justification, user access to market intelligence reports, AI forecast parameters and model versions used for each prediction, and deployment decisions influenced by market intelligence outputs. Audit records must include entity context, user identity, timestamps in both Hijri and Gregorian formats, and must be immutable once written. The audit trail must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with the most stringent regulatory requirement across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. Audit data must be searchable and exportable for compliance examinations, with access restricted to authorized compliance and audit personnel via role-based permissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R002	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Container Condition Classification Tracking	The system must classify each empty container into one of the following condition grades: Grade A (cargo-worthy, ready for immediate use), Grade B (requires minor repair such as patch welding or floor repair, estimated turnaround under 48 hours), Grade C (requires major repair such as structural damage, estimated turnaround over 48 hours), and Grade D (beyond economic repair, candidate for disposal or sale). Each grade must display the estimated repair cost in the entity's local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion to USD as the base reporting currency using daily exchange rates. The condition grade must be updateable by depot inspectors via mobile interface.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R003	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Multi-Entity Inventory Segregation	The system must segregate empty container inventories by legal entity so that Qatar-registered containers, UAE-registered containers, KSA-registered containers, and India-registered containers are tracked under their respective entity books. Inter-entity container movements must generate automatic intercompany transfer records. Each entity must maintain its own inventory ledger with container counts reconciled against the consolidated group view. Users with multi-entity access must be able to toggle between entity-specific views and a consolidated group-level view. The segregation must comply with each jurisdiction's customs bonding requirements, ensuring containers under customs hold are flagged and excluded from repositioning availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R004	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Geospatial Container Location Map	The system must render an interactive geospatial map displaying the physical location of empty container clusters at each depot, port terminal, inland container depot (ICD), and container freight station (CFS). The map must support zoom levels from regional overview (entire Middle East and Indian subcontinent) down to individual terminal yard block level. Container clusters must be color-coded by surplus (green, where supply exceeds 120% of forecast demand), balanced (amber, between 80% and 120%), and deficit (red, below 80% of forecast demand). Clicking a cluster must open a detail panel showing container type breakdown, average dwell time in days, and the oldest container's last gate-in date. The map must support Arabic RTL labeling for location names when the user interface language is set to Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R005	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Empty Container Dwell Time Monitoring	The system must calculate and display the dwell time of each empty container from its last gate-in event at the current location. Dwell time must be measured in calendar days and displayed with color-coded severity thresholds: green for 0-14 days, amber for 15-30 days, red for over 30 days. The system must generate automatic alerts when any container exceeds 21 days dwell time at Hamad Port or Jebel Ali, or 14 days at JNPT where demurrage and ground rent charges escalate more rapidly. Alerts must be sent via in-app notification, email, and optionally SMS. The dwell time calculation must account for Hijri calendar weekends (Friday-Saturday in Qatar/KSA/UAE) and Indian public holidays when computing business-day equivalents for SLA tracking purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R006	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Leased Container Tracking and Return Deadlines	The system must separately track leased containers from lessors such as Triton, Beacon, Textainer, Florens, and SeaCube, recording the master lease agreement number, per-diem rate in USD, and drop-off location restrictions. For each leased empty container, the system must display the remaining free days before per-diem charges commence, the nearest approved drop-off depot, and the accumulated per-diem liability in both USD and the local entity currency. The system must generate alerts 7 days, 3 days, and 1 day before free-day expiry. When a leased container is idle beyond free days, the system must automatically calculate the accrued per-diem cost and post it to the appropriate cost center in the financial module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R007	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Container Survey and Inspection Records	The system must store and display the most recent survey or inspection record for each empty container, including the inspection date, inspector name, survey type (on-hire, off-hire, periodic, or pre-trip for reefers), and photographic evidence attachments. Survey records must capture damage codes following IICL (Institute of International Container Lessors) standards and estimated repair costs. For refrigerated containers (20RF, 40RF), the system must additionally track the last PTI (Pre-Trip Inspection) date and result (pass or fail), compressor hours, and refrigerant gas level. Containers with failed PTI must be flagged as unavailable for booking until a passing PTI record is uploaded. All inspection dates must be displayable in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R008	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Surplus and Deficit Forecasting Integration	The system must integrate with the booking and vessel schedule modules to compute a rolling 30-day forecast of empty container supply and demand at each location. The forecast must factor in expected import container returns (based on import manifest data and average free-time utilization), confirmed export bookings requiring empty pick-up, and scheduled repositioning movements already in the pipeline. The forecast must be broken down by container type (dry, reefer, open-top, flat-rack, tank) and size (20-foot, 40-foot, 45-foot). Forecast accuracy must be tracked by comparing predicted versus actual availability on a weekly basis, with variance reports accessible to planning managers. The AI engine must self-calibrate its forecasting model when variance exceeds 15% for any location-type combination over a 4-week rolling window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R009	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Offline Inventory Capture for Remote Depots	The system must support offline inventory capture for remote inland depots in India (such as ICD Tughlakabad, ICD Dadri, or CFS locations near JNPT) and KSA inland locations (such as Riyadh Dry Port) where internet connectivity may be intermittent or unavailable. Depot operators must be able to record gate-in, gate-out, and survey events on a mobile application that stores data locally. Upon reconnection, the application must automatically synchronize all buffered transactions to the central system with conflict resolution logic that prioritizes the most recent timestamp. The offline module must support a minimum of 500 container transactions before requiring synchronization. All offline-captured data must include the device GPS coordinates and the operator's digital signature for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R010	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Container Ownership Type Filtering	The system must allow users to filter the empty container inventory by ownership type: company-owned (COC), shipper-owned (SOC), and leased (LSE). For SOC containers, the system must display the shipper name, the original booking reference, and the contractual return deadline. For leased containers, the lease agreement reference and lessor name must be visible. The filter must be applicable across all inventory views including the dashboard, map view, and tabular reports. When computing repositioning availability, only COC and LSE containers with valid lease repositioning rights must be included by default; SOC containers must be excluded unless the user explicitly overrides this filter. The ownership type filter must persist across user sessions as a saved preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R011	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Customs Hold and Regulatory Status Display	The system must display the customs and regulatory hold status for each empty container. Containers under customs examination, fumigation hold, or quarantine (particularly relevant for containers returning from agricultural cargo at Indian ports subject to Plant Quarantine Order) must be flagged with the hold type, authority reference number, and expected release date. Containers under hold must be visually distinguished in all inventory views using a lock icon and red border. The system must prevent any repositioning order from being created for containers with an active customs or regulatory hold. When a hold is released by the customs authority, the container status must update to available within one synchronization cycle. The hold status must be auditable with a complete history log of all status changes, timestamps, and the user or system that initiated each change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R012	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Arabic RTL Interface for Inventory Screens	All empty container inventory screens must fully support Arabic right-to-left (RTL) layout when the user selects Arabic as their interface language. This includes mirroring of navigation panels, table column ordering (rightmost column first), chart axis labels, and map control placements. Port names, depot names, and location descriptions must be displayed in Arabic where Arabic translations are available in the master data, with English fallback when no Arabic translation exists. Numeric fields (container counts, costs, dwell times) must remain in Western Arabic numerals (0-9) unless the user explicitly enables Eastern Arabic numeral display (٠-٩) in their profile preferences. Date fields must display in Hijri format (e.g., 15 Sha'ban 1447) with Gregorian equivalent shown in parentheses. All data export functions (PDF, Excel) must also respect the RTL layout setting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R013	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Inventory Reconciliation with Terminal Operating Systems	The system must perform automated daily reconciliation of empty container counts against data feeds from terminal operating systems (TOS) at Hamad Port (using Navis N4), Jebel Ali (using Navis N4), Khalifa Port, and JNPT. Discrepancies between the ERP inventory and the TOS-reported inventory must be flagged in a reconciliation exception report, categorized by type: missing in ERP, missing in TOS, status mismatch, or location mismatch. Each discrepancy must be assigned a severity level based on the number of days it remains unresolved, with automatic escalation to the inventory control manager after 48 hours. The reconciliation must run as a scheduled batch job at 02:00 local time for each port's time zone and must complete processing within 30 minutes. A reconciliation audit trail must be maintained for a minimum of 24 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R014	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Empty Stock Report Generation and Distribution	The system must generate a standardized Empty Stock Report on a daily, weekly, and monthly basis, summarizing empty container positions across all locations. The report must include columns for location code, location name, container type, size, condition grade, ownership type, total count, available count (excluding holds and repairs), and average dwell time. The report must be exportable in PDF, Excel (XLSX), and CSV formats. Automated distribution must be configurable so that designated recipients receive the report via email at scheduled times. The report must support multi-currency display, showing local currency values alongside USD equivalents. For the Arabic-language version of the report, all headers, labels, and location names must be rendered in Arabic with RTL formatting. The monthly report must include a trend analysis chart comparing current month inventory levels against the previous three months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R015	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Role-Based Inventory Access Control	The system must enforce role-based access control (RBAC) on empty container inventory data. Depot-level operators must only see inventory at their assigned depot or port. Country managers must see all inventory within their entity (e.g., Qatar entity sees Hamad Port and associated inland depots). Regional managers must see inventory across multiple entities within their assigned region (e.g., GCC region covering Qatar, UAE, and KSA). Global administrators must have unrestricted access to all entities and locations. The access control must apply to all views including dashboard, map, reports, and API endpoints. Attempting to access inventory data outside the user's authorized scope must return an HTTP 403 response and log the unauthorized access attempt. The RBAC configuration must be manageable through an administration interface without requiring code deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-001-R016	FEAT-058-1-001	MOD-058	Container Age and Depreciation Visibility	The system must display the manufacturing year and calculated age in years for each company-owned empty container, sourced from the BIC (Bureau International des Containers) container database or manual master data entry. For containers older than 12 years, the system must display a visual aging indicator (yellow for 12-15 years, orange for 15-18 years, red for over 18 years) to support fleet renewal planning. The system must integrate with the fixed asset module to display the current net book value (NBV) of each owned container in both the entity's local currency and USD. Depreciation method (straight-line over 13 years with 15% residual value is the default) must be configurable per entity. Containers that have reached zero NBV must be flagged for review by the fleet management team for potential disposal, sale, or continued use assessment. The age and NBV data must be included as optional columns in all inventory reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R001	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Demand Calculation Engine	The system must calculate repositioning demand by comparing forecast empty container surplus at origin locations against forecast deficit at destination locations over configurable time horizons of 7, 14, 30, and 60 days. The calculation must consider container type compatibility, ensuring that a surplus of 40HC containers at Jebel Ali is not matched against a deficit of 20GP containers at JNPT. The engine must output a prioritized list of recommended repositioning moves ranked by urgency (days until stock-out at deficit location), volume (number of TEUs required), and cost-efficiency (estimated repositioning cost per TEU). The demand calculation must run automatically every 24 hours and be triggerable on-demand by authorized planners. Results must be stored with a version history allowing comparison of demand snapshots over time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R002	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Multi-Leg Repositioning Plan Creation	The system must allow planners to create multi-leg repositioning plans that chain together ocean, truck, rail, and barge transport segments. For example, a plan may involve trucking empty containers from an inland depot in Riyadh to Jebel Ali, then shipping via feeder vessel to Hamad Port. Each leg must capture the transport mode, carrier or haulier name, estimated transit time in hours, estimated cost in the origin entity's currency, and capacity constraints (maximum TEU per movement). The plan must support splitting a single repositioning order across multiple vessels or conveyances when the volume exceeds single-shipment capacity. Each leg must have configurable lead times for booking cutoffs, and the system must validate that the end-to-end plan timeline meets the deficit location's required-by date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R003	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Vessel Slot Allocation for Empty Repositioning	The system must integrate with the vessel schedule and space management module to allocate slots for empty container repositioning on company-operated or alliance partner vessels. The planner must be able to search available vessel voyages between origin and destination port pairs, view remaining slot capacity after laden cargo allocation, and reserve slots for empties. Slot reservations must be tentative until approved by the vessel operations team, with automatic expiry after 48 hours if not confirmed. The system must enforce a configurable maximum percentage of vessel capacity allocable to empty repositioning (default 15% of total TEU capacity) to prevent excessive displacement of revenue-generating laden cargo. Slot allocation must respect vessel stability requirements by distributing empty container weight across bays as specified by the vessel's stowage planner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R004	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Partner Line Slot Swap Management	The system must support slot-swap arrangements with partner shipping lines whereby empty containers are repositioned using partner vessel space in exchange for reciprocal slot access on company vessels. Each slot-swap agreement must be recorded with the partner line name, SCAC code, agreement validity period, swap ratio (e.g., 1:1 or 1:1.2), covered trade lanes (defined by origin-destination port pairs), and maximum TEU per month. The system must track utilization against each agreement and alert planners when 80% and 95% of the monthly swap quota has been consumed. Overage beyond the agreed quota must require management approval before booking. Settlement of swap imbalances must be calculated monthly in USD using agreed per-TEU rates and integrated with the accounts payable and receivable modules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R005	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Inland Transport Coordination for Repositioning	The system must coordinate inland transport (truck and rail) required as part of repositioning plans. For truck movements, the system must generate transport orders specifying the pickup depot, delivery depot or port gate, required pickup date and time window, container type and quantity, and assigned haulier from the approved vendor list. For rail movements within India (e.g., ICD Tughlakabad to JNPT via Indian Railways CONCOR service), the system must capture the rail booking reference, rake allocation number, estimated transit time, and rail freight cost in INR. The system must validate that inland transport scheduling aligns with the vessel cut-off time at the load port, providing a minimum buffer of 24 hours for truck deliveries and 48 hours for rail deliveries. Transport orders must be trackable through statuses: created, dispatched, in-transit, delivered, and cancelled.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R006	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Approval Workflow	The system must enforce a configurable multi-level approval workflow for repositioning plans based on the total estimated cost and number of TEUs involved. Plans below 50 TEU and costing under USD 10,000 may be auto-approved by the system if AI confidence score exceeds 90%. Plans between 50-200 TEU or costing USD 10,000-50,000 must require approval by the regional equipment manager. Plans exceeding 200 TEU or USD 50,000 must require additional approval by the VP of Operations. The approval workflow must support delegation so that an approver can designate a substitute during leave periods. Pending approvals must be accessible via a mobile-responsive interface and push notifications. Approval or rejection must capture the approver's comments and timestamp, and all approval actions must be immutably logged for audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R007	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Cross-Entity Repositioning Transfer Accounting	When a repositioning plan moves containers from one legal entity to another (e.g., from UAE entity at Jebel Ali to Qatar entity at Hamad Port), the system must automatically generate intercompany transfer records. The transfer must record the sending entity, receiving entity, number and type of containers, transfer date, and agreed transfer price. The transfer price must default to the net book value of the containers but be overridable by authorized finance personnel within a configurable tolerance band (default ±10% of NBV). The system must generate corresponding intercompany journal entries in both entities' general ledgers: a receivable in the sending entity's books and a payable in the receiving entity's books, denominated in USD as the intercompany settlement currency. VAT and customs duty implications for cross-border transfers must be flagged for review by the tax compliance team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R008	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Timeline Gantt View	The system must provide a Gantt chart visualization of each repositioning plan showing all legs, milestones, and dependencies on a timeline. The Gantt view must display key milestones including: plan creation date, each transport leg start and end, vessel booking cutoff, vessel ETD (estimated time of departure) from origin, vessel ETA (estimated time of arrival) at destination, and final delivery to the deficit depot. Dependencies between legs must be visually linked so that delays in one leg are immediately visible in their impact on subsequent legs. The timeline must support both Gregorian and Hijri date display. Critical path legs (those with zero float where any delay impacts the final delivery date) must be highlighted in red. Users must be able to drag-and-drop legs to adjust timing, with the system automatically revalidating feasibility after each adjustment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R009	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Container Selection for Repositioning Orders	The system must allow planners to select specific containers or specify selection criteria for containers to be included in a repositioning order. Selection criteria must include container type, size, condition grade (only Grade A and Grade B containers should be eligible for repositioning by default), maximum age in years, ownership type (COC or LSE with repositioning rights), and maximum dwell time at origin. The system must validate that selected containers are not already allocated to an export booking, not under customs or regulatory hold, and not scheduled for survey or repair. When specific containers are not pre-selected, the system must auto-select containers meeting the criteria using a FIFO (first-in-first-out) prioritization based on gate-in date to minimize dwell time charges. The selection must be lockable to prevent other processes from allocating the same containers during the plan approval window.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R010	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Volume and Frequency Analytics	The system must provide analytics dashboards showing historical repositioning volumes and frequencies across all trade lanes. The dashboard must display monthly TEU volumes repositioned between each origin-destination pair, average cost per TEU repositioned, average transit time, and on-time delivery percentage. Trend charts must cover a minimum of 12 months of historical data. The system must identify seasonal patterns in repositioning demand (e.g., increased empty build-up at Indian ports during monsoon season affecting JNPT, or Ramadan-related volume fluctuations in GCC ports) and present them as annotations on the trend charts. All analytics must be filterable by entity, container type, trade lane, and time period. Data must be exportable to Excel and PDF formats with Arabic-language headers available for GCC entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R011	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Template Library	The system must support the creation and storage of repositioning plan templates for frequently used trade lanes and container type combinations. A template must capture the standard route legs, preferred carriers or hauliers for each leg, typical transit times, benchmark costs, and default approval routing. Planners must be able to instantiate a new repositioning plan from a template, automatically populating all template fields while allowing overrides for the specific execution. Templates must be versioned so that updates to a template do not affect previously created plans. Each template must have an owner (the creating user) and be shareable with specific user roles or all users within an entity. The system must track template usage frequency and last-used date to identify obsolete templates that should be archived. A minimum of 50 templates must be supportable per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R012	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Demand Alerts and Notifications	The system must generate proactive alerts when empty container imbalances reach configurable threshold levels. Alert triggers must include: a location's available empty stock falling below 7 days of forecast demand (critical deficit), a location's empty stock exceeding 30 days of forecast demand (excessive surplus), a specific container type reaching zero availability at any port location, and leased containers approaching free-day expiry at a surplus location where repositioning could avoid per-diem costs. Alerts must be delivered via in-app notification center, email, and optional SMS and WhatsApp integration for the GCC and India markets where WhatsApp is a primary business communication channel. Each alert must include a one-click link to initiate a repositioning plan pre-populated with the relevant origin, destination, container type, and suggested quantity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R013	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Regulatory and Customs Compliance for Cross-Border Repositioning	The system must validate that cross-border repositioning plans comply with customs regulations at both the origin and destination countries. For repositioning from UAE to Qatar, the system must verify that containers cleared under temporary import at Jebel Ali have valid re-export documentation. For repositioning to India via JNPT, the system must ensure compliance with Indian Customs ICES (Indian Customs EDI System) requirements including filing of an Import General Manifest (IGM) for empty containers. For KSA-bound repositioning, the system must verify SABER certification is not required for empty equipment and that FASAH customs platform pre-clearance data is prepared. The system must maintain a regulatory requirements matrix by country pair and flag any missing documentation or permits before the repositioning plan can be submitted for approval. Non-compliant plans must be blocked from execution with a clear indication of the missing regulatory element.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R014	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Status Tracking and Updates	The system must track the end-to-end execution status of each repositioning plan through defined lifecycle stages: Draft, Pending Approval, Approved, In Execution (with sub-statuses for each leg: Booked, Picked Up, In Transit, Arrived, Delivered), Completed, and Cancelled. Status updates must be captured from integrated transport management systems, vessel tracking feeds, and manual updates by operations personnel. Each status change must record the timestamp, updating user or system source, and any remarks. The system must calculate and display the percentage completion of each plan based on the number of containers that have reached the destination versus the total planned quantity. Deviations from the planned timeline exceeding 24 hours must trigger automatic alerts to the plan owner and the destination depot manager. A plan-level audit trail must be accessible showing the complete history of all status changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R015	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Cancellation and Reversal	The system must support partial and full cancellation of repositioning plans with appropriate financial and operational reversal handling. Cancellation before any physical container movement must release all reserved vessel slots and inland transport bookings and reverse any tentative accounting entries. Cancellation after partial execution must handle the split scenario where some containers have already been shipped and others have not, creating a revised plan for the in-transit containers and cancelling the remainder. Cancellation must require a reason code selected from a configurable list (e.g., demand change at destination, vessel schedule change, cost overrun, regulatory issue) and approval by the original plan approver. The system must calculate and display any cancellation penalties from carriers or hauliers based on their contractual terms. All cancellation actions must be logged with full traceability for audit and financial reconciliation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R016	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Feeder and Mother Vessel Transshipment Planning	The system must support repositioning plans that involve transshipment, where empty containers are loaded onto a feeder vessel at the origin port, transshipped at a hub port onto a mother vessel (or vice versa), and then delivered to the final destination port. The plan must capture each vessel segment separately with its own voyage reference, vessel name, IMO number, ETD, and ETA. The system must validate that the transshipment connection time at the hub port (time between feeder arrival and mother vessel departure) meets a minimum configurable dwell time (default 24 hours for Jebel Ali hub, 36 hours for other ports) to account for terminal handling. If the connection time is insufficient, the system must flag the plan as high-risk for missed connection and suggest alternative vessel combinations. Transshipment handling costs at the hub port must be included in the total repositioning cost calculation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-002-R017	FEAT-058-1-002	MOD-058	Repositioning Plan Bulk Import via Spreadsheet	The system must support bulk creation of repositioning plans via spreadsheet upload in XLSX and CSV formats. The upload template must include columns for origin location code, destination location code, container type, container size, quantity, required delivery date, preferred transport mode for each leg, and priority level. The system must validate all uploaded data against master data references (valid location codes, valid container types) and business rules (no repositioning to a surplus location, no repositioning of restricted container types). Validation errors must be returned in a downloadable error report specifying the row number, column, and error description in both English and Arabic. Successfully validated rows must be converted into draft repositioning plans and presented for review before submission to the approval workflow. The bulk import must support a maximum of 500 rows per upload and must complete processing within 60 seconds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R001	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Comprehensive Cost Component Breakdown	The system must capture and display a detailed breakdown of all cost components for each repositioning movement. Cost components must include: ocean freight (per TEU or per container), terminal handling charges at origin (THC-O), terminal handling charges at destination (THC-D), inland haulage at origin, inland haulage at destination, rail freight (where applicable), transshipment handling fees, documentation fees, customs clearance charges, container survey or inspection fees, and any applicable port-specific surcharges (e.g., Jebel Ali terminal congestion surcharge, JNPT infrastructure development cess). Each cost component must be recorded with the original currency amount, the exchange rate applied, and the USD equivalent. The total repositioning cost must be calculable at both per-container and per-TEU levels for comparison across different container sizes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R002	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Multi-Currency Cost Recording and Conversion	The system must support cost recording in multiple currencies reflecting the actual invoiced currency from each vendor or port authority. Supported currencies must include USD (base currency), QAR (Qatari Riyal), AED (UAE Dirham), SAR (Saudi Riyal), and INR (Indian Rupee). Each cost entry must record the original transaction currency and amount, the conversion rate to USD, the rate source (Central Bank rate, market rate, or contracted rate), and the conversion date. The system must apply the daily exchange rate from an integrated rate feed for automatic conversions, while allowing finance users to override with a manually entered rate for specific transactions where a contracted rate applies. All cost reports must display amounts in both the original currency and USD equivalent, with an option to display in the reporting entity's local currency as a third column.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R003	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Budget Allocation and Variance Monitoring	The system must allow finance and operations managers to set annual and quarterly repositioning budgets by entity, trade lane (origin-destination pair), and container type. Budgets must be denominated in USD and automatically converted to local entity currencies using the budgeted exchange rate set at the start of each fiscal period. The system must track actual repositioning expenditure against budget in real-time, displaying utilization percentage, remaining budget amount, and projected year-end or quarter-end spend based on current run-rate. Variance alerts must trigger when actual spend reaches 75%, 90%, and 100% of the budget for any budget line. Budget overruns must require explicit approval from the entity CFO before additional repositioning plans can be approved for that budget line. Monthly budget versus actual variance reports must be generated automatically and distributed to designated finance stakeholders.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R004	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Vendor Invoice Matching for Repositioning Costs	The system must support three-way matching of repositioning cost invoices against the repositioning plan, the contracted rate card, and the actual service delivered. When a vendor (shipping line, haulier, terminal operator, or rail carrier) submits an invoice, the system must compare the invoiced amount against the expected cost from the repositioning plan and the contracted rate. Discrepancies exceeding a configurable tolerance (default 5% or USD 100, whichever is greater) must be flagged for dispute resolution. The system must support invoice upload in PDF format with AI-assisted data extraction to auto-populate invoice fields including vendor name, invoice number, invoice date, line items, amounts, and currency. Matched and approved invoices must be automatically forwarded to the accounts payable module for payment processing. The system must maintain an aging report of unmatched invoices by vendor, entity, and age bracket (0-30, 31-60, 61-90, over 90 days).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R005	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Cost Approval Matrix Configuration	The system must provide an administration interface for configuring the cost approval matrix that determines which approvers are required based on the repositioning cost amount, entity, and trade lane. The matrix must support multiple approval levels with configurable thresholds. For example: Level 1 (Equipment Controller) for costs up to USD 5,000, Level 2 (Equipment Manager) for USD 5,001 to USD 25,000, Level 3 (Regional Director) for USD 25,001 to USD 100,000, and Level 4 (VP Operations) for costs exceeding USD 100,000. The matrix must support AND and OR logic for multi-approver scenarios (e.g., both Equipment Manager AND Finance Manager required for Level 2). Matrix configurations must be entity-specific to accommodate different organizational structures across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Changes to the approval matrix must be audited with an effective date, preventing retroactive changes to already-submitted approvals.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R006	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Repositioning Cost per TEU Benchmarking	The system must calculate and maintain benchmark cost-per-TEU metrics for each repositioning trade lane based on historical actuals. Benchmarks must be computed as the rolling 6-month weighted average cost per TEU for each origin-destination-container type combination. When a new repositioning plan is created, the system must compare the estimated cost per TEU against the benchmark and display the variance as both an absolute amount in USD and a percentage. Plans with estimated costs exceeding the benchmark by more than 20% must be flagged with a cost advisory warning visible to the planner and all approvers. The benchmarking module must also compare costs against external market indices for empty repositioning (where available from data providers such as Drewry or Alphaliner) and display the company's positioning relative to the market average. Benchmark data must be refreshable on a monthly basis and the historical benchmark trend must be viewable for up to 24 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R007	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Leased Container Per-Diem Cost Accrual	The system must automatically calculate and accrue per-diem costs for leased empty containers that are idle beyond their contractual free days. The accrual must be computed daily by multiplying the number of excess days by the per-diem rate specified in the master lease agreement for each container. Accruals must be posted to the appropriate cost center and general ledger account in the entity's local currency with USD equivalents. The system must distinguish between on-hire per-diem (charged while the container is in the company's possession and idle) and off-hire per-diem (charged during the return journey to a lessor-designated drop-off location). When a container is repositioned to reduce per-diem exposure, the system must calculate the per-diem cost saved as a benefit metric for the repositioning plan's cost-benefit analysis. Monthly per-diem accrual reports must be generated by lessor, entity, and container type for reconciliation with lessor invoices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R008	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Cost Center and GL Account Mapping	The system must map each repositioning cost component to the correct cost center and general ledger (GL) account based on the entity, trade lane, and cost type. The mapping must be configurable through an administration interface and must support the chart of accounts structures used by each entity: Qatar entity using IFRS-compliant chart of accounts, UAE entity aligned with UAE CT (Corporate Tax) requirements effective from 2024, KSA entity aligned with ZATCA requirements, and India entity aligned with Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS). Each cost posting must carry the repositioning plan ID as a reference for drill-down traceability from financial statements to operational transactions. The system must support split cost allocation when a repositioning movement benefits multiple entities, with configurable allocation keys based on container count, TEU volume, or custom percentages agreed upon by the involved entities. GL postings must be reversible through a controlled journal reversal process requiring finance manager approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R009	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Repositioning ROI and Cost-Benefit Analysis	The system must calculate the return on investment (ROI) for each repositioning movement by comparing the repositioning cost against the revenue opportunity enabled by making the container available at the deficit location. The revenue opportunity must be estimated based on the average freight rate for the most likely laden export booking from the destination port, sourced from the historical booking data for the same trade lane and container type over the past 90 days. The cost-benefit analysis must also factor in avoided costs such as per-diem charges saved for leased containers, demurrage or ground rent avoided at surplus locations, and potential dead-freight charges avoided at deficit locations. The net benefit must be displayed in USD alongside a traffic-light indicator: green for ROI exceeding 150%, amber for ROI between 100% and 150%, and red for ROI below 100%. Plans with negative ROI (cost exceeding total benefit) must require mandatory justification text from the planner before submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R010	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Tax and Duty Calculation for Cross-Border Repositioning	The system must calculate applicable taxes and customs duties for cross-border empty container repositioning movements. For imports into India, the system must compute the Integrated Goods and Services Tax (IGST) at the applicable rate on the declared value of the container as empty equipment, plus Basic Customs Duty (BCD) where applicable, and any anti-dumping or safeguard duties in force. For movements into KSA, the system must account for the 15% Value Added Tax (VAT) on service charges and any customs processing fees levied by Saudi Customs (ZATCA). For movements within the GCC (Qatar-UAE, UAE-KSA), the system must apply the appropriate VAT treatment based on the GCC VAT framework, distinguishing between zero-rated international transport services and standard-rated local handling services. All tax calculations must be stored with the applicable tax code, rate, base amount, and tax amount for integration with the VAT return filing module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R011	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Repositioning Cost Forecast vs Actual Report	The system must generate a report comparing the forecasted (estimated) repositioning costs at the plan creation stage against the actual costs incurred upon plan completion. The report must display each cost component side by side with the estimated amount, actual amount, and variance in both absolute value and percentage terms. The report must be filterable by entity, trade lane, container type, time period, and planner. An executive summary section must show the aggregate forecast accuracy across all completed repositioning plans for the selected period, calculated as the mean absolute percentage error (MAPE). Plans with actual costs exceeding estimates by more than 25% must be individually listed in a variance exception section with a mandatory explanation field that the plan owner must complete within 10 business days of plan completion. The report must be available in English and Arabic with multi-currency display supporting all five operational currencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R012	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Approval Escalation and SLA Enforcement	The system must enforce service level agreements (SLAs) for repositioning cost approvals to prevent delays in plan execution. Approval SLAs must be configurable by approval level: default 4 hours for Level 1, 8 hours for Level 2, 24 hours for Level 3, and 48 hours for Level 4. SLA measurement must exclude non-business hours based on each entity's working calendar, accounting for Friday-Saturday weekends in Qatar, KSA, and UAE, and Saturday-Sunday weekends in India, as well as Hijri-based holidays (Eid Al Fitr, Eid Al Adha, National Days) and Indian public holidays (Republic Day, Independence Day, Diwali). When an approval SLA is breached, the system must automatically escalate to the next-level approver and send an escalation notification to both the original and escalated approver. If a plan remains unapproved after two escalation levels, the system must notify the entity General Manager. Escalation timestamps and outcomes must be logged for SLA compliance reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R013	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Historical Cost Data Retention and Archival	The system must retain all repositioning cost records, including plan estimates, actual costs, invoices, approvals, and variance analyses, for a minimum of 7 years in the active database to comply with the longest statutory retention period across the four jurisdictions (India requires 8 assessment years under Income Tax Act; KSA and UAE require 5-7 years under VAT regulations; Qatar requires 10 years under commercial law). Records older than 7 years must be archived to a cost-effective storage tier while remaining searchable and retrievable within 24 hours upon request. Archived records must maintain full referential integrity so that drill-down from summary reports to archived detail records functions seamlessly. The archival process must run as a scheduled annual job and must generate an archival certificate listing the number of records archived, the date range covered, and a checksum for data integrity verification. Archived data must comply with data residency requirements, ensuring Qatar entity data remains in Qatar or GCC-approved data centers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R014	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Cost Dashboard with Real-Time Aggregation	The system must provide an executive cost dashboard displaying real-time aggregated repositioning costs across all entities and trade lanes. The dashboard must include key performance indicator (KPI) tiles for: total repositioning spend month-to-date in USD, average cost per TEU repositioned, number of repositioning plans in progress, budget utilization percentage, and top 5 most expensive trade lanes. A cost trend line chart must show daily repositioning expenditure for the current month with a comparison overlay of the same month in the prior year. A treemap visualization must display cost distribution by entity, with drill-down capability to trade lane and then individual plan level. All dashboard components must respect the user's entity access permissions, showing only data for authorized entities. The dashboard must load within 3 seconds and support real-time updates via WebSocket connection. Arabic-language dashboard labels must be available with full RTL layout support.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R015	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Repositioning Cost Allocation to Laden Voyages	The system must support allocation of repositioning costs to subsequent laden voyages to enable accurate profitability analysis at the voyage and trade lane level. When a repositioned empty container is subsequently used for a laden export booking, the system must allocate the repositioning cost to that booking's cost structure as a pre-carriage or positioning cost. The allocation must be proportional when a repositioning plan covers multiple containers that are subsequently used for different bookings. The allocated cost must flow through to the voyage P&L statement, the trade lane profitability report, and the customer profitability analysis if the cost is not absorbed and is partially recovered through equipment positioning surcharges. The allocation logic must be configurable: FIFO (allocate to the next booking chronologically), specific assignment (manual linking by the planner), or weighted average (spread evenly across all bookings at the destination within 30 days of container arrival). Allocation records must be auditable and reversible.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R016	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Carrier and Vendor Rate Card Management	The system must maintain a rate card database for all carriers, hauliers, terminal operators, and other vendors involved in empty container repositioning. Each rate card entry must specify the vendor name, vendor code, service type (ocean, truck, rail, handling, customs clearance), origin-destination pair (or zone-based pricing), container type and size, rate amount and currency, validity period (start and end dates), volume tier discounts (if applicable), and any surcharges or accessorial fees. The system must automatically select the most cost-effective rate when estimating repositioning plan costs, with the ability for planners to manually override if a preferred vendor is selected for service quality reasons. Rate cards must support bulk upload via XLSX template. Expiring rate cards (within 30 days of validity end) must trigger renewal alerts to the procurement team. Rate card history must be maintained for benchmarking and audit purposes, and all rate changes must be tracked with the effective date and the user who made the modification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-003-R017	FEAT-058-1-003	MOD-058	Cost Anomaly Detection by AI	The system must employ AI-based anomaly detection to identify unusual or potentially erroneous repositioning costs. The AI engine must analyze each cost entry against historical patterns for the same trade lane, container type, vendor, and cost component to detect outliers that deviate by more than two standard deviations from the historical mean. Detected anomalies must be flagged in the cost approval workflow with a visual indicator and an AI-generated explanation describing why the cost appears anomalous (e.g., ocean freight rate 45% above 6-month average for Jebel Ali to JNPT 40HC repositioning). The system must also detect patterns indicative of potential fraud or billing errors such as duplicate invoices, invoices for non-existent repositioning plans, or costs booked against cancelled plans. Anomaly detection results must be reviewable in a dedicated queue by the finance control team, with options to dismiss (with reason), investigate further, or escalate. The AI model must retrain monthly using the latest 12 months of cost data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R001	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Multi-Objective Optimization Engine	The system must implement a multi-objective optimization engine that simultaneously minimizes total repositioning cost (including ocean freight, inland transport, handling, and per-diem charges), minimizes total transit time from origin to destination, and maximizes container utilization rate across the network. The engine must use a configurable weighting scheme allowing planners to prioritize cost versus speed versus utilization based on current business conditions. For example, during peak season when container availability is critical, the weight for transit time minimization may be increased to 60% while cost minimization is reduced to 30% and utilization maximization set to 10%. The optimization must be formulated as a mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) problem or equivalent and must produce near-optimal solutions within 120 seconds for a network of up to 50 locations and 200 active repositioning demands. The engine must output a ranked list of recommended repositioning plans with Pareto-optimal trade-offs clearly displayed.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R002	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Real-Time Vessel Schedule Integration	The optimizer must integrate with real-time vessel schedule data to identify available vessel voyages for empty container repositioning. The integration must consume schedule data from the company's own vessel fleet management system, alliance partner schedule feeds, and common carrier schedules via INTTRA, CargoSmart, or equivalent maritime data platforms. Schedule data must include vessel name, IMO number, voyage reference, port rotation with ETD and ETA for each port call, remaining slot capacity, and vessel type constraints (e.g., some feeders may not accept out-of-gauge containers). The optimizer must refresh schedule data at least every 6 hours and must account for schedule reliability metrics (historical on-time performance percentage) when calculating expected transit times. When a vessel schedule change is detected that impacts a recommended repositioning route, the optimizer must automatically recalculate and present updated alternatives to the planner.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R004	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Seasonal and Event-Based Adjustment Factors	The forecast model must incorporate seasonal adjustment factors including Ramadan periods (calculated dynamically from the Hijri calendar using Umm al-Qura standards), Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha, Diwali, Chinese New Year, and other regional holidays that impact shipping volumes in the GCC and Indian subcontinent. The system must allow manual override adjustment factors expressed as percentage multipliers for specific trade lanes and date ranges. Event-based adjustments must include port congestion alerts, vessel schedule disruptions, and known infrastructure changes such as terminal expansions at Hamad Port or JNPT. All adjustments must be auditable with the user who applied them, the timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and a mandatory justification note supporting both Arabic and English text input.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R003	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Port Congestion and Dwell Time Prediction	The optimizer must incorporate AI-predicted port congestion levels and expected container dwell times at each port and terminal in the network. The prediction model must use historical data on vessel waiting times, berth occupancy rates, yard utilization percentages, and gate turn times for Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. External data sources such as MarineTraffic or VesselFinder AIS data showing vessel anchorage queues must be integrated where available to enhance congestion prediction accuracy. The congestion prediction must output a daily congestion index (1-10 scale) for each port for the next 14 days, with confidence intervals. The optimizer must factor congestion predictions into transit time calculations, adding buffer time for congested ports. During predicted high-congestion periods (index above 7), the optimizer must prefer alternative routing through less congested ports even if the direct route would nominally be cheaper, unless the cost differential exceeds a configurable threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R004	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Demand Forecasting Using Machine Learning	The optimizer must incorporate a machine learning-based demand forecasting model that predicts empty container demand and supply at each location for the next 30, 60, and 90 days. The model must be trained on a minimum of 24 months of historical data including import and export booking volumes, seasonal patterns, macroeconomic indicators (GDP growth rates, trade indices for GCC and India), Ramadan and Diwali seasonal effects on trade volumes, and historical repositioning movements. The model must use an ensemble approach combining time-series forecasting (ARIMA or Prophet), gradient boosting (XGBoost or LightGBM), and a recurrent neural network (LSTM) with the final prediction being a weighted average of the ensemble members. Forecast accuracy must be measured using MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error) with a target of below 15% at the location-container type level for 30-day forecasts. The model must retrain weekly using the latest available data and must log all retraining events, feature importance rankings, and accuracy metrics for model governance review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R005	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Weather and Disruption Event Integration	The optimizer must integrate weather forecast data and maritime disruption event feeds to adjust repositioning routes dynamically. Weather data must include tropical cyclone tracks in the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal (particularly relevant for JNPT during monsoon season from June to September), sandstorm forecasts affecting GCC port operations, and fog advisories impacting port approach channel navigability. Disruption events must include port closures, labor strikes, security incidents, and canal or strait restrictions (e.g., Strait of Hormuz transit delays). The optimizer must assess the impact of each weather or disruption event on the affected route segments and either reroute around the affected area, delay the movement to a post-event window, or flag the plan for manual review by the operations team. Each weather or disruption adjustment must be logged with the event type, source, severity, affected route legs, and the optimization decision taken.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R006	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Intermodal Route Generation	The optimizer must generate intermodal route options combining ocean, road, and rail transport modes for each repositioning movement. For India, the optimizer must consider rail options via CONCOR (Container Corporation of India) connecting inland container depots such as ICD Tughlakabad (Delhi), ICD Dadri, ICD Whitefield (Bangalore), and ICD Tondiarpet (Chennai) to JNPT and other gateway ports. For GCC movements, the optimizer must consider the Etihad Rail network connecting UAE ports to KSA, and truck routes between Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi (Khalifa Port), and Doha (Hamad Port) via the Saudi land border crossing at Salwa or Abu Samra. Each generated route must include total distance in kilometers, estimated transit time, number of handover points, carbon emission estimate in kg CO2, and total cost. The optimizer must present a minimum of 3 alternative routes for each origin-destination pair, ranked by the user's selected optimization priority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R007	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Street Turn and Triangulation Optimization	The optimizer must identify street turn and triangulation opportunities to reduce empty container repositioning needs. A street turn occurs when an import container that would normally return empty to the port is instead redirected directly to a nearby exporter for laden use, bypassing the port entirely. Triangulation extends this concept across three parties. The optimizer must match import delivery locations with export pickup locations using geospatial proximity analysis, with a configurable maximum radius (default 50 km for truck movements). Matching must also consider container type and size compatibility, import container condition (must be Grade A), timing alignment (import delivery and export pickup must be within a configurable window, default 48 hours), and shipper or consignee consent requirements. Each identified opportunity must display the estimated savings versus the standard empty repositioning via port in both cost and CO2 emissions. Accepted street turns must be tracked through completion with actual savings calculated and reported monthly.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R008	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Network Flow Optimization Model	The optimizer must solve a minimum-cost network flow problem across the entire container logistics network, treating each port, depot, and ICD as a node and each available transport connection as an arc with associated cost, capacity, and transit time attributes. The network model must account for node-specific constraints including maximum storage capacity at each depot, maximum throughput rate (gate moves per day), and working hour restrictions. Arc constraints must include vessel slot capacity, truck fleet availability from contracted hauliers, and rail rake scheduling at Indian depots. The model must solve for the optimal flow of empty containers across all arcs simultaneously, producing a global repositioning plan that is superior to optimizing individual trade lanes independently. The network flow model must be solvable within 300 seconds for the full network and must handle up to 100 nodes, 500 arcs, and 1,000 demand-supply pairs. The solution must be decomposable into individual executable repositioning plans.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R005	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	External Economic Indicator Integration	The system must integrate external economic indicators that correlate with container demand including crude oil prices (Brent and WTI), regional GDP growth indices for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, purchasing manager indices (PMI), port throughput statistics published by respective port authorities, and commodity price indices relevant to major trade lane commodities. Data feeds must be configurable with API endpoints, polling frequencies, and data transformation rules. The system must gracefully handle feed failures by using the last known good values with a staleness indicator displayed to users. Each external factor must have a configurable weight and the system must display correlation coefficients between each indicator and actual demand to help analysts understand forecast drivers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R009	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Carbon Emission Calculation for Repositioning Routes	The optimizer must calculate the estimated carbon emissions (CO2 equivalent in metric tons) for each proposed repositioning route. Emission calculations must follow the IMO EEOI (Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator) methodology for ocean segments and the GLEC (Global Logistics Emissions Council) framework for inland transport segments. Emission factors must be configurable and must default to: container vessel ocean transport at 15 grams CO2 per TEU-km (varying by vessel size and speed), diesel truck at 62 grams CO2 per TEU-km, and diesel rail at 22 grams CO2 per TEU-km. The optimizer must provide a carbon-optimized route option alongside the cost-optimized and time-optimized options, allowing planners to select a higher-cost route with lower emissions when the company's sustainability targets require it. Monthly and annual carbon emission reports for empty repositioning must be generated for inclusion in the company's ESG (Environmental, Social, and Governance) disclosures, compliant with GHG Protocol Scope 3 Category 4 (upstream transportation) requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R010	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Optimization Constraint Configuration Interface	The system must provide an administration interface for configuring all optimization constraints and parameters without requiring code changes. Configurable parameters must include: minimum and maximum repositioning batch sizes per movement (default minimum 10 TEU, maximum 500 TEU), maximum percentage of vessel capacity allocable to empties per voyage, maximum road transit distance for truck legs (default 500 km), preferred and blacklisted carriers or routes, time-of-day restrictions for truck operations at specific depots, and seasonal adjustment factors for demand forecasting. Each parameter must have an effective date range so that seasonal constraints (e.g., monsoon period adjustments for Indian ports from June 1 to September 30 each year, or Ramadan period adjustments in GCC based on Hijri calendar) can be pre-configured. Changes to optimization parameters must require approval from the Head of Equipment Planning and must be version-controlled with the ability to roll back to any previous configuration version.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R011	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	AI Explanation and Recommendation Transparency	The optimizer must provide transparent, explainable recommendations for each suggested repositioning plan. Each recommendation must include a natural language summary (generated in both English and Arabic) explaining why this route was selected over alternatives, the key factors that influenced the decision (e.g., 35% weight to cost saving, 40% to transit time, 25% to vessel schedule reliability), and the sensitivity analysis showing how the recommendation would change if key input parameters varied by ±10%. The explanation must identify the top 3 risk factors for the recommended plan (e.g., vessel schedule reliability at 82%, monsoon impact probability at 30%, Jebel Ali congestion index at 6/10). The system must provide a what-if simulation capability allowing planners to adjust input parameters (e.g., change vessel, modify quantity, shift date) and see the immediate impact on cost, time, and risk scores. All AI recommendations must be logged with the model version, input features, and output scores for regulatory compliance and model audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R012	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Continuous Learning from Plan Execution Outcomes	The optimizer must implement a continuous learning feedback loop that incorporates actual repositioning plan execution outcomes to improve future recommendations. After each repositioning plan is completed, the system must compare the actual transit time, actual cost, and actual container condition at arrival against the optimizer's predictions. Prediction errors must be categorized by root cause (vessel delay, port congestion underestimation, inland transport delay, cost escalation, weather event) and fed back into the respective prediction models. The learning module must track the optimizer's recommendation acceptance rate (percentage of AI-recommended plans accepted by planners without modification), modification patterns (which parameters planners most frequently override), and outcome quality (whether planner-modified plans outperform AI recommendations). A monthly model performance report must be generated showing prediction accuracy trends, recommendation acceptance rates, and identified areas for model improvement. The system must support A/B testing of model versions to validate improvements before full deployment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R013	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Batch Optimization Scheduling	The system must support scheduled batch optimization runs that compute repositioning plans for the entire network at configurable intervals. The default schedule must be a daily run at 06:00 UTC processing all open repositioning demands with a horizon of 30 days. Additional batch runs must be triggerable on-demand by authorized planners for immediate re-optimization in response to significant events such as major vessel schedule changes, port closures, or sudden demand spikes at specific locations. Each batch run must produce a set of recommended repositioning plans ranked by priority, with plans grouped by execution urgency: immediate (within 3 days), near-term (4-14 days), and forward planning (15-30 days). The batch run must complete within 30 minutes for the full network and must generate an execution summary report including total TEUs planned, total estimated cost in USD, number of plans generated, and comparison against the previous batch run's output. Historical batch run results must be retained for 12 months for trend analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R014	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Scenario Planning and Simulation Mode	The system must provide a scenario planning mode that allows planners and managers to simulate hypothetical repositioning scenarios without creating actual plans. Users must be able to define scenarios by modifying inputs such as: adding or removing a port from the network, changing demand forecasts at specific locations, simulating a vessel withdrawal from a trade lane, introducing a new rail connection, or applying a fuel surcharge increase. The simulator must run the optimization engine on the modified inputs and display the resulting repositioning plans alongside the baseline (current actual) plans for comparison. Comparison metrics must include total network repositioning cost, average transit time, network container utilization rate, and carbon emissions. Users must be able to save, name, and share scenarios for collaborative review. The simulation must operate on a sandboxed copy of the data, ensuring that no scenario inputs or outputs affect the live operational system. Each scenario must be completable within 5 minutes of user input completion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R015	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Integration with External Container Marketplace Platforms	The optimizer must integrate with external empty container marketplace platforms such as xChange, Container xChange, or similar digital platforms where surplus empty containers can be offered for interchange with other shipping lines or where deficit containers can be sourced. The integration must allow the system to publish available surplus containers at specified locations with offered interchange rates and to query available containers at deficit locations with requested rates. The optimizer must evaluate whether interchanging containers via the marketplace is more cost-effective than physical repositioning and must include marketplace options alongside internal repositioning options in its recommendations. Marketplace transactions must be recorded with the counterparty shipping line name, interchange rate in USD, container details (type, size, condition), pickup and return location, free-day terms, and per-diem rates for excess days. All marketplace interactions must be subject to the same approval workflow as internal repositioning plans.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R016	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Optimizer Performance Monitoring Dashboard	The system must provide a performance monitoring dashboard for the AI optimization engine, accessible to system administrators and data science teams. The dashboard must display: model inference latency (average, P95, and P99 response times), optimization solver execution time by problem size, demand forecast accuracy by location and container type (MAPE, RMSE), recommendation acceptance rate over time, cost prediction accuracy (estimated versus actual), and transit time prediction accuracy. Health indicators must flag when any performance metric degrades beyond acceptable thresholds: forecast MAPE exceeding 20%, solver time exceeding 300 seconds, or recommendation acceptance rate falling below 60%. The dashboard must also display the current model version in production, the date of last retraining, the training data date range, and the number of features used. All metrics must be queryable over custom date ranges and exportable in CSV format for further analysis. The dashboard must support both English and Arabic interface languages.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R017	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Fallback Manual Planning Mode	The system must provide a fully functional manual planning mode that allows authorized users to create and execute repositioning plans without AI assistance, serving as a fallback when the AI engine is unavailable, undergoing maintenance, or when planners determine that unique circumstances make AI recommendations inappropriate. The manual mode must provide access to all the same data inputs (inventory visibility, vessel schedules, cost rates, demand forecasts) but allow the planner to freely select routes, carriers, quantities, and timing without optimization constraints. Manual plans must still be subject to the same cost approval workflow and regulatory compliance checks as AI-generated plans. When the AI engine is restored, the system must compare recent manual plans against what the optimizer would have recommended and generate an efficiency gap analysis report. The system must automatically detect AI engine unavailability and switch to manual mode with a prominent banner notification indicating that AI optimization is temporarily offline. All manual plans must be tagged for identification in analytics to separate AI-assisted and manual performance metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-1-004-R018	FEAT-058-1-004	MOD-058	Optimizer Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance Logging	The system must maintain a comprehensive audit trail of all AI optimization activities for regulatory compliance and model governance. Each optimization run must log: the run timestamp, trigger type (scheduled, on-demand, event-driven), input data snapshot (demand matrix, available inventory, vessel schedules, cost rates), model version and configuration parameters used, all candidate solutions evaluated with their objective function scores, the selected recommended solution(s), and any constraints that were binding (actively limiting the solution). When a planner accepts, modifies, or rejects an AI recommendation, the action and rationale must be logged. The audit trail must be tamper-proof using append-only storage with cryptographic hashing for data integrity verification. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with the most stringent applicable regulation across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India jurisdictions. The audit system must support queries by date range, optimization run ID, planner name, trade lane, and decision outcome, and must produce compliance reports suitable for review by internal audit teams and external regulators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R001	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Trade Lane Definition and Configuration	The system must allow configuration of trade lanes as directed origin-destination port pairs with associated metadata including average transit time, typical vessel services, and historical volume bands. Each trade lane must reference valid UN/LOCODE port codes such as QAHMD (Hamad Port), AEJEA (Jebel Ali), AEKHL (Khalifa Port), and INNSA (JNPT Nhava Sheva). Trade lanes must be tagged with directional imbalance indicators (surplus or deficit) and must support grouping into corridor families such as Arabian Gulf to Indian Subcontinent, Intra-GCC, and GCC to Far East. Configuration must support multi-entity visibility where Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India entities each see trade lanes relevant to their operations while a consolidated group view aggregates all lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R002	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Historical Data Ingestion Pipeline	The system must ingest and normalize at minimum 36 months of historical empty container movement data including pickup dates, drop-off dates, container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40FR), origin and destination depots, associated booking references, and vessel voyage identifiers. Data ingestion must handle multi-currency freight records converting amounts to a base currency (USD) using historical exchange rates for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The pipeline must run as a scheduled batch process with support for incremental updates and must flag data quality anomalies such as missing port codes or inconsistent date sequences for manual review before inclusion in forecast models.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R003	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Machine Learning Forecast Model Execution	The AI engine must execute demand forecast models using ensemble methods combining at minimum time-series decomposition (ARIMA/Prophet), gradient boosted trees, and neural network approaches. The model must generate point forecasts and confidence intervals (80% and 95%) for empty container demand by trade lane, container type, and week for a rolling 12-week horizon. Model retraining must occur automatically on a configurable schedule (default weekly) using the latest available data. Each forecast run must be versioned with a unique identifier, timestamp, model parameters used, and training data window. The system must store forecast accuracy metrics (MAPE, RMSE, bias) and flag when accuracy degrades below configurable thresholds per trade lane.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R006	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Forecast Dashboard with Arabic RTL Support	The forecast dashboard must display demand predictions in an interactive visualization showing trade lanes on a geographic map overlay with color-coded surplus and deficit indicators. Charts must render correctly in both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper axis label mirroring, legend positioning on the correct side, and Arabic numeral formatting options (Eastern Arabic or Western Arabic numerals based on user preference). The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), container type, trade lane corridor, and forecast horizon. Date selectors must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar inputs. All chart tooltips, data labels, and export headers must be fully bilingual. The dashboard must load within 3 seconds for the default 4-week view even with 500+ active trade lanes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R007	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Forecast Accuracy Tracking and Model Comparison	The system must continuously track forecast accuracy by comparing predicted demand against actual empty container movements at weekly granularity for each trade lane and container type combination. Accuracy metrics must include Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), directional accuracy (percentage of correctly predicted increase or decrease), and bias (systematic over or under prediction). The system must maintain a leaderboard comparing current model performance against baseline models including naive persistence, seasonal naive, and simple moving average. When the primary model underperforms the baseline for three consecutive weeks on any trade lane, the system must automatically generate an alert to the data science team and optionally switch to the better-performing model pending human approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R008	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Container Type Disaggregation Forecasting	Demand forecasts must be disaggregated by ISO container type codes including standard dry containers (20GP, 40GP, 40HC), reefer containers (20RF, 40RF), open top containers (20OT, 40OT), flat rack containers (20FR, 40FR), and tank containers (20TK). The system must model substitution elasticity between container types where applicable, such as using 2x20GP in place of 1x40GP when 40-foot supply is constrained. Reefer container forecasts must additionally account for perishable commodity seasons specific to the region such as date exports from KSA and UAE and seafood exports from India. Each container type must have independently configurable minimum safety stock levels per depot and the forecast must flag when predicted demand exceeds available plus in-transit supply.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R009	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Multi-Entity Consolidated and Segmented Forecasting	The system must generate forecasts at both the individual entity level (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations) and at the consolidated group level. Entity-level forecasts must respect each entity's specific trade lane portfolio and container fleet allocation. The consolidated view must identify inter-entity repositioning opportunities where one entity's surplus on a trade lane can satisfy another entity's deficit. Transfer pricing rules configured in the ERP master data must be applied when calculating the cost-benefit of inter-entity container sharing. Access control must enforce that entity-level users see only their own forecasts while group-level planners see the consolidated view. All monetary values must display in the entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with a toggle to show USD equivalent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R010	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Demand Signal Capture from Booking Pipeline	The system must capture real-time demand signals from the booking pipeline including confirmed bookings, provisional bookings, booking amendments, cancellations, and rollover requests. Each signal must be weighted by its conversion probability: confirmed bookings at 95%, provisional bookings at historical conversion rate per customer segment, and spot inquiries at a configurable base rate. The demand signal processor must update the near-term forecast (0-2 weeks) in near real-time with a maximum latency of 15 minutes from booking system event to forecast adjustment. The system must detect unusual booking surges or drops that deviate more than two standard deviations from the forecast and generate alerts with recommended repositioning actions. Booking data must include INCOTERMS (CIF, FOB, EXW, etc.) as these affect which party controls container logistics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R011	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Vessel Schedule Integration for Supply Projection	The forecast engine must integrate with vessel schedule data to project the inbound supply of empty containers arriving at each port. This includes empties discharged from arriving vessels, expected container releases from import deliveries based on average detention periods, and empties returned from export stuffing. Vessel schedule changes such as port omissions, delays, or extra loaders must automatically trigger forecast recalculation. The system must model vessel-specific empty container capacity constraints and must account for terminal-specific handling capabilities at Hamad Port Container Terminal 2, Jebel Ali Terminal 1-4, Khalifa Port KPCT, and JNPT BMCT and NSIGT terminals. The projection must update within 30 minutes of receiving a vessel schedule change notification from the operations module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R012	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Forecast Export and API Availability	All forecast data must be available through a RESTful API with endpoints for retrieving forecasts by trade lane, port, container type, date range, and entity. The API must support JSON and CSV response formats with proper Unicode handling for Arabic text in trade lane names and port descriptions. Pagination must be supported for large result sets. The system must also provide scheduled export functionality generating Excel reports with bilingual headers (Arabic and English) suitable for management review. API authentication must use OAuth 2.0 tokens scoped to entity-level permissions. Rate limiting must be configurable per API consumer with a default of 100 requests per minute. The API must include forecast metadata such as model version, confidence interval, and last training date in every response.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R013	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	What-If Scenario Simulation for Demand Forecasting	The system must provide a what-if scenario simulation capability allowing planners to adjust input parameters and observe the impact on demand forecasts without affecting the production forecast. Adjustable parameters must include trade lane volume multipliers, new trade lane additions, vessel service changes (new string, service withdrawal, frequency change), economic scenario toggles (recession, boom, sanctions), and port capacity constraints. Each scenario must be saveable with a unique name, creator, description in Arabic or English, and creation date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats. The system must allow side-by-side comparison of up to four scenarios against the base forecast. Scenario results must include the delta in empty container demand, estimated repositioning cost impact in USD and local currencies, and fleet utilization change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R014	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Offline Forecast Access and Sync	The system must support offline access to the most recent forecast data for field personnel and depot managers who may experience intermittent connectivity at port locations. The offline cache must store at minimum the current 12-week forecast for all trade lanes within the user's entity scope, the last 4 weeks of actual versus forecast comparison, and any active alerts. When connectivity is restored, the system must synchronize any manual adjustments or notes entered offline using a last-write-wins conflict resolution strategy with full audit trail of offline changes. The offline data package must not exceed 50 MB per entity to support low-bandwidth environments. Offline mode must clearly indicate the staleness of cached data with a prominent banner showing the last sync timestamp in the user's local timezone and preferred calendar format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R015	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Automated Forecast Alert and Notification Rules	The system must support configurable alert rules that trigger notifications based on forecast conditions. Alert types must include demand spike detection (forecast exceeds historical average by configurable percentage), supply shortage warning (forecasted demand exceeds projected supply within configurable weeks), accuracy degradation (MAPE exceeds threshold for configurable consecutive periods), and imbalance escalation (trade lane surplus or deficit exceeds configurable TEU threshold). Notifications must be deliverable via in-app notification, email, SMS, and Microsoft Teams webhook. Alert messages must be generated in both Arabic and English based on recipient language preference. Each alert must include the affected trade lane, port codes, container types, forecast values, recommended actions generated by the AI, and a direct deep-link to the relevant forecast dashboard view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R016	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Competitor and Market Intelligence Overlay	The system must allow manual or automated ingestion of market intelligence data including competitor empty repositioning patterns observed at shared ports, industry-wide container availability indices published by container trading platforms such as xChange or Container xChange, and port authority statistics on empty container throughput. This data must be displayed as an overlay on the demand forecast dashboard with clear visual distinction between proprietary forecast data and market intelligence. The system must calculate the company's estimated market share of empty repositioning volumes at each port and flag when competitor activity suggests changing demand patterns. All market intelligence data must be tagged with source, reliability rating, and ingestion date. Access to competitor intelligence must be restricted to authorized roles with separate permission from standard forecast access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-001-R017	FEAT-058-2-001	MOD-058	Forecast Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance	Every forecast generation, modification, manual override, and consumption event must be recorded in an immutable audit trail. The audit record must include the event type, timestamp in UTC and local timezone with Hijri equivalent, the user or system process that initiated the event, the before and after state of affected data, and the business justification for manual changes. The audit trail must be retained for a minimum of 7 years to comply with commercial record retention requirements under Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 32 of 2021, KSA Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013. The audit data must be exportable in formats acceptable to external auditors. The system must support audit queries by date range, user, trade lane, and event type with response times under 5 seconds for queries spanning up to 12 months of data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R001	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Cost Model for Repositioning Owned Containers	The system must calculate the total cost of repositioning owned or master-leased empty containers from a surplus location to a deficit location. The cost model must include ocean freight or feeder charges per TEU on the applicable routing, port handling charges at origin terminal (liftoff, gate-out) and destination terminal (gate-in, grounding) referencing the tariff schedules of Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, inland transportation from depot to port and port to depot at destination, container insurance during transit, estimated carbon emission cost based on IMO guidelines for the voyage distance, and opportunity cost of the container being in transit and unavailable for booking. All costs must be calculated in the origin entity's local currency and converted to USD for comparison using daily exchange rates for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R002	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Cost Model for Leasing Containers at Demand Point	The system must calculate the total cost of leasing containers at the demand location as an alternative to repositioning. The leasing cost model must include one-way or round-trip per-diem rates from major lessors, pickup charges at the leasing depot, drop-off charges at the designated return location, damage protection or insurance premiums, estimated off-hire survey and repair costs based on historical averages per container type, and any minimum lease duration penalties. The system must maintain a database of current leasing rates from at least the top 10 global container lessors with rates specific to pickup locations at or near QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, and INNSA. Leasing rates must be refreshable via API integration with leasing platforms or manual bulk upload in Excel format with Arabic and English column headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R003	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	AI Decision Recommendation Generation	The decision engine must generate a recommendation for each identified empty container deficit specifying whether to reposition from a specific surplus point, lease locally, or apply a hybrid approach (partial reposition and partial lease). The recommendation must include a quantified cost comparison showing the total cost of each option in USD and local currency, the estimated time to fulfill the deficit under each option, risk factors such as transit time variability and leasing availability uncertainty, and a confidence score for the recommendation. The AI must consider fleet composition strategy targets including minimum owned container ratios per trade lane, maximum leasing exposure limits, and container age profile goals. Recommendations must be generated automatically when demand forecasts identify deficits exceeding configurable TEU thresholds and must be reviewable by planners before execution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R005	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Annex II Noxious Liquid Substance Cargo Tracking	The system must classify and track all noxious liquid substance (NLS) cargoes carried in bulk according to MARPOL Annex II categories (X, Y, Z, and Other Substances). For each NLS cargo operation at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must record the substance name, UN number, MARPOL category, quantity loaded or discharged, tank washing procedures applied, and residue disposal method. Pre-wash requirements for Category X substances must be enforced automatically, blocking cargo completion sign-off until pre-wash confirmation is recorded. The system must maintain a Cargo Record Book equivalent in digital form, with entries sequentially numbered and tamper-proof. All substance names must be displayed in both English and Arabic with correct RTL rendering for Arabic chemical nomenclature.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R004	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Fleet Composition Strategy Constraints	The system must enforce configurable fleet composition strategy constraints that influence the lease versus reposition decision. Constraints must include a target ratio of owned to leased containers per entity and trade lane (e.g., minimum 60% owned for core trade lanes), maximum leasing spend as a percentage of total container logistics cost per fiscal period, preferred container age profile with maximum average fleet age targets, container type mix targets ensuring adequate supply of specialized equipment such as reefers and open tops, and strategic minimum presence requirements at key ports. Constraints must be configurable at the group level and overridable at the entity level with approval workflow. When a recommendation violates a strategic constraint, the system must flag the deviation and present the cost of compliance versus the cost of the optimized recommendation to enable informed override decisions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R005	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Multi-Leg Repositioning Route Optimization	When repositioning is the recommended option, the system must evaluate multiple routing alternatives from the surplus location to the deficit location. Route evaluation must consider direct vessel services, transshipment options via hub ports such as Jebel Ali or Singapore, feeder connections, and multi-modal options including land bridge routes within the GCC (e.g., truck from Jebel Ali to Hamad Port or rail from JNPT to ICD locations). Each route must be scored on total cost, transit time, reliability (based on historical on-time performance of the vessel services), carbon footprint, and alignment with existing vessel space allocations. The system must recommend the optimal route and present the top three alternatives with comparative analysis. Route optimization must update dynamically as vessel schedules and freight rates change.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R006	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Leasing Contract Terms Evaluation	The decision engine must evaluate and compare leasing contract terms beyond simple per-diem rates. Evaluation criteria must include minimum and maximum lease duration flexibility, off-hire location options and associated charges, damage allowance thresholds and excess damage billing rates, container substitution rights (ability to swap container types or sizes during the lease), renewal and extension terms, early termination penalties, and currency of billing with FX risk implications. The system must maintain a structured repository of active master leasing agreements per entity, parsing key commercial terms to automatically apply the correct rates and conditions to each decision scenario. When multiple lessors offer containers at the same location, the system must rank offers considering total cost of the lease lifecycle not just the headline per-diem rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R007	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Decision Approval Workflow with Multi-Entity Rules	Each lease versus reposition recommendation must pass through a configurable approval workflow before execution. The workflow must support entity-specific approval hierarchies where decisions below a configurable cost threshold (e.g., USD 5,000) are auto-approved, decisions between configurable thresholds require single-level approval from the entity logistics manager, and decisions above the upper threshold require multi-level approval including regional management. For inter-entity repositioning decisions where containers move from one entity's fleet to another, approval must be required from both the sending and receiving entity managers. The workflow must support delegation during absence, escalation timers for pending approvals (configurable per approval level), and mobile approval capability. All approval screens must be fully bilingual in Arabic and English with approval history showing both Gregorian and Hijri dates.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R008	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Real-Time Market Rate Benchmarking	The system must benchmark repositioning costs and leasing rates against real-time market data to validate the cost assumptions in each decision. Benchmarking sources must include container freight rate indices such as the Shanghai Containerized Freight Index (SCFI), Drewry World Container Index, and Freightos Baltic Index (FBX) for repositioning cost validation, and container leasing indices from platforms like Container xChange for leasing rate validation. The system must display the percentile ranking of the proposed cost against market rates (e.g., proposed repositioning cost is at the 35th percentile of current market rates for this route). When proposed costs deviate more than a configurable percentage from market benchmarks, the system must flag the anomaly for review. Market rate data must refresh at minimum daily with staleness indicators displayed when data is more than 48 hours old.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R009	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Carbon Emission Cost Integration in Decision Making	The decision engine must incorporate carbon emission costs as a factor in the lease versus reposition comparison in alignment with IMO 2023 Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) regulations and EU Emissions Trading System (ETS) requirements for voyages touching EU ports. The system must calculate estimated CO2 emissions for each repositioning route based on vessel type, fuel type, voyage distance, and expected speed using the IMO Fourth GHG Study methodology. A configurable carbon price per ton of CO2 equivalent must be applied, with the ability to use either a shadow carbon price set by corporate policy or the actual ETS allowance market price. The carbon cost must be displayed as a separate line item in the cost comparison and must be included in the total cost calculation. The system must also track cumulative carbon emissions from repositioning decisions per entity for ESG reporting purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R010	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Decision History and Pattern Analysis	The system must maintain a complete history of all lease versus reposition decisions including the recommendation, the actual decision taken, the approval chain, and the post-decision outcome (actual cost versus estimated cost, actual timeline versus estimated timeline). The AI engine must analyze decision patterns to identify systematic biases such as planners consistently overriding AI recommendations for specific trade lanes or container types, decisions that resulted in significantly higher actual costs than estimated, and recurring deficit situations at the same locations suggesting structural fleet deployment issues. Pattern analysis results must be presented in a monthly summary report available in both Arabic and English with visualizations showing decision outcome distributions, override rates by entity and planner, and cumulative cost impact of deviations from AI recommendations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R011	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Container Condition and Age Factor in Decisions	The decision engine must factor in individual container condition and age when determining whether to reposition. Containers approaching their end-of-economic-life (typically 12-15 years for standard dry containers, 10-12 years for reefers) should be deprioritized for long-distance repositioning as the remaining revenue-generating life may not justify the repositioning cost. The system must integrate with the container maintenance module to access the latest survey status, CSC plate expiry date, and pending repair estimates for each container in the surplus pool. Containers with pending repairs exceeding a configurable percentage of their book value should be flagged for sell or scrap consideration rather than repositioning. The system must calculate a repositioning ROI metric that divides the expected revenue contribution of the repositioned container by the repositioning cost, adjusted for remaining useful life.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R012	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Sensitivity Analysis for Key Decision Variables	The system must provide sensitivity analysis for each lease versus reposition recommendation showing how the optimal decision changes as key variables fluctuate. Variables for sensitivity testing must include ocean freight rate (plus or minus 20% in 5% increments), leasing per-diem rate (plus or minus 30% in 5% increments), transit time (plus or minus 5 days), exchange rates for QAR/USD, AED/USD, SAR/USD, and INR/USD (plus or minus 10%), and carbon price per ton (plus or minus 50%). The sensitivity analysis must present results as a tornado chart showing which variables have the greatest impact on the decision outcome. Breakeven points must be calculated for each variable showing the exact value at which the recommendation switches from reposition to lease or vice versa. The analysis must be generated within 10 seconds and must be exportable as a PDF report with bilingual labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R013	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Integration with Container Procurement Module	When the decision engine identifies a sustained deficit on a trade lane that requires repeated leasing or repositioning over a configurable number of consecutive periods (default 8 weeks), the system must automatically generate a procurement suggestion to the container procurement module recommending purchase of additional owned containers. The procurement suggestion must include the recommended quantity by container type, the estimated annual cost savings of owning versus continued leasing or repositioning, the payback period calculation using the entity's weighted average cost of capital, and the suggested allocation by depot. The suggestion must account for current container purchase market prices, factory lead times from major manufacturers in China, and the entity's capital expenditure budget availability. Procurement suggestions must be routed to the fleet strategy team and must track the status of each suggestion through to approval, order, delivery, or rejection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R014	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Batch Decision Processing for Weekly Planning Cycle	The system must support batch processing of lease versus reposition decisions aligned with the weekly planning cycle. Each week the system must automatically generate a decision package containing all identified deficits for the upcoming 4-week window with prioritized recommendations. The batch must sequence decisions to avoid conflicts such as two deficits competing for the same surplus pool and must optimize across the entire portfolio rather than making isolated point-to-point decisions. The batch processing must complete within 30 minutes for up to 200 simultaneous deficit-surplus pairs across all four entities. Results must be presented in a summary dashboard showing total recommended repositioning moves, total leasing volumes, aggregate cost by entity in local currencies and USD, and net fleet balance after execution. The dashboard must support Arabic RTL layout with proper table formatting and right-aligned numeric columns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R015	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Offline Decision Support for Depot Managers	The system must provide an offline-capable decision support interface for depot managers at locations with unreliable internet connectivity. The offline module must cache the latest decision recommendations, leasing rate cards, repositioning cost estimates, and container availability data for the depots under the manager's responsibility. Depot managers must be able to accept, reject, or modify recommendations offline with mandatory reason codes and comments supporting Arabic and English text. When connectivity is restored, offline decisions must synchronize with the central system using timestamp-based conflict detection. If a conflicting decision was made centrally during the offline period, the system must present both decisions to a supervisor for resolution. The offline data package must be refreshable on demand and must auto-refresh when connected, with a maximum cache staleness of 24 hours before the system displays a mandatory refresh warning.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R016	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Regulatory and Customs Compliance Validation	Before finalizing any repositioning recommendation, the system must validate compliance with customs regulations at both the origin and destination countries. Validation checks must include empty container customs declaration requirements for Qatar (Qatar General Authority of Customs), UAE (Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs and Port Security), KSA (Zakat, Tax and Customs Authority - ZATCA), and India (Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs - CBIC). The system must verify that container types being repositioned are permitted at the destination port, that any phytosanitary or fumigation requirements for empty containers on specific trade lanes are flagged, and that temporary import or re-export bond requirements are identified. For containers entering India, the system must check compliance with the Indian Customs Act 1962 provisions for empty container movements and flag any applicable IGST implications. Non-compliance must block the recommendation from proceeding to approval.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-002-R017	FEAT-058-2-002	MOD-058	Decision Engine Performance and Scalability Requirements	The decision engine must generate individual lease versus reposition recommendations within 5 seconds for a single deficit-surplus pair evaluation including all cost calculations, constraint checks, and route optimizations. The system must support concurrent decision generation for up to 50 users across all four entities without degradation below the 5-second threshold. The decision engine API must maintain 99.5% uptime measured monthly with automatic failover to a read-only mode that serves cached recommendations when the optimization engine is unavailable. All decision engine computations must be deterministic given the same inputs, ensuring reproducibility for audit purposes. The system must log execution times for each decision component (cost calculation, route optimization, constraint validation, AI scoring) to enable performance profiling and optimization. Database queries supporting the decision engine must use appropriate indexing strategies for trade lane, container type, port code, and date range dimensions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R001	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Program Definition and Configuration	The system must allow creation and configuration of empty return incentive programs with the following parameters: program name in both Arabic and English, effective date range in Gregorian and Hijri formats, eligible container types (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 20RF, 40RF, and other ISO types), eligible return depots identified by depot code and linked to port UN/LOCODE (QAHMD, AEJEA, AEKHL, INNSA), eligible participant categories (consignee, freight forwarder, trucker, shipping agent), incentive rate structure (fixed amount per container or per TEU, tiered rates based on volume, or time-based rates with early return bonuses), currency of incentive payment (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD), maximum program budget, and auto-suspension threshold when budget utilization reaches a configurable percentage. Each program must be entity-specific with the owning entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) clearly identified.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R002	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Dynamic Incentive Rate Adjustment by AI	The AI engine must dynamically adjust incentive rates based on real-time supply-demand conditions at each depot. The adjustment algorithm must consider current empty container inventory at the depot versus the target stock level, inbound empty supply projected from vessel schedules and forecasted returns, outbound demand from confirmed and forecasted export bookings, the urgency of need expressed as days until projected stockout, and the historical responsiveness of participants to different incentive levels at that location. Rate adjustments must occur at configurable intervals (minimum hourly) and must respect configured minimum and maximum rate boundaries per program. Each rate change must be logged with the triggering factors and the AI confidence score. Sudden rate increases exceeding 50% of the base rate within a 24-hour period must require human approval before taking effect to prevent erroneous or manipulated incentive spikes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R003	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Participant Registration and Eligibility Management	The system must maintain a registry of eligible incentive program participants linked to their master data records in the ERP (customer, vendor, or partner records). Registration must capture the participant's legal entity name in Arabic and English, trade license number, tax registration number (QAR TIN, UAE TRN, KSA VAT number, India GSTIN), bank account details for incentive payments including IBAN for GCC entities and IFSC code for Indian entities, preferred communication language (Arabic or English), and authorized contact persons. Participants must be able to self-register through a portal with document upload for verification, subject to approval by the entity's commercial team. The system must enforce a blacklist check against participants with outstanding debts, unresolved disputes, or compliance violations before activating their enrollment. Participant status must be clearly shown as pending, active, suspended, or terminated.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R004	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Claim Processing and Validation	The system must process incentive claims automatically when an eligible empty container is returned to a qualifying depot by a registered participant. Claim validation must verify that the container was returned within the eligible timeframe of the active incentive program, the container type matches the program eligibility criteria, the return depot is a qualifying location, the participant is in active status, the container passes gate-in inspection (not rejected for damage beyond acceptable thresholds), and the same container return has not already been claimed under another program (duplicate detection). The system must calculate the applicable incentive amount based on the rate in effect at the time of actual gate-in (not the time of container pickup or return booking). For tiered rate structures, the system must accumulate the participant's period-to-date volume to determine the correct tier. Claims must be auto-approved when all validation checks pass and routed to exception handling when any check fails.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R005	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Payment Processing and Multi-Currency Settlement	Approved incentive claims must be batched for payment processing on a configurable cycle (default weekly). The payment batch must group claims by participant and currency, calculate applicable taxes or withholding per jurisdiction (VAT in UAE and KSA, GST in India, and applicable levies in Qatar), generate payment instructions in the participant's configured currency using the exchange rate on the payment processing date, and create the corresponding accounting entries in each entity's books. Payment methods must support bank transfer (SWIFT for international, local clearing for domestic), credit note against outstanding freight invoices, and prepaid wallet top-up for frequent participants. Each payment must generate a remittance advice in Arabic and English detailing the container numbers, return dates, incentive rates applied, and any deductions. The system must handle partial payments when budget constraints require prorated distribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R006	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Program Budget Tracking and Controls	The system must provide real-time budget tracking for each incentive program showing committed spend (approved but unpaid claims), actual spend (paid claims), remaining budget, and projected spend based on current claim run rate. Budget tracking must be available in the program's base currency and in USD for group-level consolidation. The system must enforce hard budget caps that automatically suspend new claim approvals when the budget ceiling is reached, with immediate notification to the program manager via in-app alert and email. Soft warning thresholds must be configurable at 70%, 80%, and 90% of budget utilization, each triggering escalating notifications. Budget reallocation between programs within the same entity must be supported through a transfer workflow requiring entity finance manager approval. Cross-entity budget transfers must additionally require group treasury approval. Monthly budget variance reports must be generated automatically comparing actual spend against planned budget with variance explanations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R007	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Effectiveness Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive analytics dashboard measuring the effectiveness of each incentive program. Key performance indicators must include the number of additional container returns attributed to the incentive versus the baseline return rate, cost per additional return (incentive spend divided by incremental returns above baseline), impact on container availability at the target depot measured as days of stock improvement, reduction in emergency repositioning or leasing costs attributable to incentive-driven returns, and participant engagement metrics including claim frequency and average time from container release to return. The dashboard must support drill-down from program level to depot level to individual participant level. All visualizations must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with proper chart axis mirroring. Comparison views must allow benchmarking across entities (Qatar versus UAE versus KSA versus India) using normalized metrics to account for market size differences.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R008	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Participant Communication and Notification System	The system must manage automated communications to incentive program participants including program launch announcements, rate change notifications, claim approval or rejection notices, payment confirmations, and program expiry warnings. Communications must be generated in the participant's preferred language (Arabic or English) and delivered through configurable channels including email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API, and in-app notifications via the participant portal. Rate change notifications must be sent at least 24 hours before the new rate takes effect to ensure fair treatment. The notification templates must be configurable by entity with mandatory fields including program name, effective dates, incentive rates, eligible depots, and terms and conditions. All communications must include the entity's legal name, commercial registration number, and contact details as required by consumer protection regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R009	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Fraud Detection and Anomaly Monitoring	The system must implement fraud detection mechanisms to identify suspicious patterns in incentive claims. Detection rules must include monitoring for the same container being returned and picked up repeatedly within short intervals (round-tripping), participants claiming returns for containers they did not originally receive, unusual concentration of claims from a single participant exceeding three standard deviations from the peer group average, claims clustered immediately before known rate increases suggesting insider knowledge, and geographic anomalies where the claimed return depot is inconsistent with the participant's normal operating area. Detected anomalies must generate alerts categorized by severity (low, medium, high, critical) and routed to the entity's compliance team. High and critical severity alerts must automatically suspend the participant's incentive eligibility pending investigation. The system must maintain a fraud case management workflow tracking investigation status, findings, and resolution actions with full audit trail.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R010	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Tax and Regulatory Compliance for Incentive Payments	The system must ensure all incentive payments comply with tax regulations in each operating jurisdiction. For UAE operations, incentive payments must be treated as consideration for a supply of services subject to 5% VAT under Federal Decree-Law No. 8 of 2017, with proper tax invoice generation. For KSA operations, the system must apply 15% VAT under the KSA VAT implementing regulations and generate ZATCA-compliant tax invoices in the mandated XML e-invoicing format (FATOORAH). For India operations, the system must determine the applicable GST rate and generate GST-compliant invoices with proper HSN or SAC codes, handle TDS deductions under Section 194C of the Income Tax Act 1961 where applicable, and support GSTR filing data export. For Qatar operations, the system must comply with applicable tax regulations as they evolve. All tax calculations must use the rates effective on the transaction date and the system must maintain a tax rate master configurable by jurisdiction, transaction type, and effective date range.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R011	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Geo-Targeted Incentive Zone Management	The system must support definition of geographic incentive zones around depots and ports to enable location-based incentive targeting. Each zone must be defined as a polygon or radius around a depot coordinate with configurable distance bands (e.g., 0-20 km premium zone, 20-50 km standard zone, 50-100 km extended zone). Incentive rates can vary by zone with higher rates for returns to depots in high-deficit areas and lower or zero rates for adequately stocked locations. The system must integrate with GPS tracking data from container transport to verify that the return was made to the claimed depot. Zone definitions must account for actual road network distances rather than straight-line distances at ports with complex access routes such as JNPT where the JNPT road connectivity project affects trucking routes. Zone maps must be displayed on the participant portal with Arabic and English labels and must be printable for distribution to trucking companies without digital access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R012	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Program Versioning and Audit History	Every modification to an incentive program configuration must create a new version while preserving the complete history of all prior versions. Version-tracked attributes must include rate structures, eligible container types, eligible depots, eligible participant categories, budget allocations, and terms and conditions. The system must record the user who made each change with their entity affiliation, the timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, and a mandatory change justification note in Arabic or English. Active claims must continue to be processed under the program version that was effective at the time of the container return, not the current version, to prevent retroactive rate changes. A version comparison view must allow administrators to see the differences between any two versions of a program in a side-by-side diff format. The audit history must be retained for a minimum of 7 years and must be exportable for external audit review in PDF and CSV formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R013	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Integration with Gate-In Operations and EDI	The incentive system must integrate in real-time with depot gate-in operations to automatically trigger incentive claim creation when an eligible empty container is returned. Integration must support electronic data interchange (EDI) messages including CODECO (container gate-in/gate-out report) messages per UN/EDIFACT standards. The system must also support API-based integration with terminal operating systems (TOS) at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT terminals. For depots without electronic integration, the system must support manual gate-in entry with container number, condition survey results, return date and time, and participant identifier. Manual entries must be flagged for additional verification. The integration must handle high-volume scenarios with up to 500 gate-in events per hour per depot without queuing delays. Container condition data from gate-in surveys (clean, damaged, requires repair) must automatically determine incentive eligibility based on program-specific condition acceptance rules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R014	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Participant Self-Service Portal	The system must provide a web-based self-service portal for incentive program participants accessible in both Arabic and English with full RTL support for Arabic including mirrored navigation, right-aligned form fields, and Arabic date formatting in Hijri and Gregorian. The portal must allow participants to view active incentive programs with current rates and eligible depots, track their submitted claims with real-time status updates, view payment history and download remittance advices, update their bank account and contact information subject to verification, view their performance metrics including total returns, total incentives earned, and ranking among anonymized peers, and submit disputes for rejected claims with document attachment capability. The portal must be responsive for mobile access as many trucking company operators use smartphones as their primary device. Authentication must support OTP-based login via SMS for users without email accounts, accommodating the participant demographic in the Indian and GCC trucking industry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R015	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Seasonal and Campaign-Based Incentive Programs	The system must support creation of short-term campaign-based incentive programs overlaying the standard incentive structure. Campaign triggers must include seasonal demand surges such as pre-Ramadan inventory build-up periods, peak reefer demand seasons for date and fruit exports from the GCC, monsoon season impacts on Indian port operations at JNPT, and Diwali-related consumer goods import spikes in India. Campaign programs must have distinct budgets, rate multipliers applied on top of base incentive rates, and targeted participant segments. The system must allow scheduling campaigns in advance with automatic activation and deactivation based on configured date ranges using both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates. Campaign performance must be tracked separately from base programs with dedicated ROI metrics. The AI engine must recommend campaign parameters based on historical effectiveness of similar past campaigns, including optimal rate levels, duration, and target depots.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R016	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Cross-Entity Incentive Coordination	The system must coordinate incentive programs across entities to prevent conflicts and optimize group-level empty container positioning. Coordination mechanisms must include mutual recognition of participant registrations across entities so a trucker registered in UAE can claim incentives in Qatar without re-registration, harmonized rate floors and ceilings to prevent entities from bidding against each other for returns at shared hinterland depots, consolidated participant blacklists shared across all entities, and group-level budget optimization that allocates incentive spending to the locations with the highest marginal return on incentive investment. The system must detect when incentive programs at nearby depots controlled by different entities create adverse incentive gradients that could redirect returns away from the location with greater need. When conflicts are detected, the system must alert group-level coordinators and recommend harmonized rate adjustments. Inter-entity settlement for participants claiming incentives from an entity other than their registration entity must be handled through the intercompany reconciliation module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-003-R017	FEAT-058-2-003	MOD-058	Incentive Impact on Container Detention and Demurrage	The system must analyze and display the relationship between incentive programs and container detention and demurrage performance. The analysis must show whether incentive programs are effectively reducing the average free time utilization by encouraging faster empty returns, track the number of containers returned within free time versus those incurring detention charges with and without incentive programs active, calculate the net financial impact considering detention revenue foregone versus incentive cost versus repositioning cost savings, and identify participants who consistently return containers late despite incentive availability suggesting the incentive level is insufficient or the participant has structural constraints. The system must provide a breakeven calculator showing the incentive rate at which the cost of the incentive equals the detention revenue that would otherwise be earned, helping program managers set rates that optimize total container cycle time without eroding detention revenue beyond the offsetting repositioning savings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R001	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Repositioning Cost Capture and Classification	The system must capture and classify all costs associated with empty container repositioning movements. Cost categories must include ocean freight or feeder charges for sea-leg repositioning, port terminal handling charges at origin and destination referencing actual tariff schedules from Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, inland transportation costs for truck or rail movements between depots and ports, container lift-on and lift-off charges at intermediate handling points, container insurance during repositioning transit, container repair and maintenance costs incurred specifically to make a container reposition-ready, customs and documentation fees for cross-border repositioning, and allocated carbon emission costs per the company's internal carbon pricing policy. Each cost must be captured in the currency of the invoicing entity with automatic conversion to USD for consolidated reporting using the exchange rate on the transaction date for QAR, AED, SAR, and INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R002	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Benefit Attribution from Repositioned Containers	The system must attribute revenue benefits to repositioning activities by tracking the subsequent utilization of repositioned containers. When a repositioned container is used for an export booking, the system must link the repositioning cost to the revenue generated from that booking. Benefit calculation must include the freight revenue from the first laden voyage post-repositioning, the avoided cost of leasing a container locally had the repositioning not occurred (calculated using current leasing rates for the container type at the destination), the avoided cost of customer compensation for equipment non-availability that would have resulted without the repositioning, and the detention and demurrage revenue earned during the container's subsequent use. The system must track the time from repositioning arrival to first laden use (idle time) as a key efficiency metric. Containers that remain idle for more than a configurable number of days (default 14) after repositioning must be flagged as potentially unnecessary repositioning moves for post-hoc analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R003	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Trade Lane Level P&L Aggregation	The system must aggregate repositioning costs and benefits at the trade lane level to produce a repositioning P&L statement for each origin-destination pair. The trade lane P&L must show total repositioning volume in TEU, total repositioning cost broken down by cost category, total attributed revenue benefit, net repositioning P&L (benefit minus cost), repositioning cost per TEU, and return on repositioning investment percentage. Trade lanes must be identifiable by their UN/LOCODE origin and destination codes and must be filterable by corridor groups such as Intra-GCC, GCC to Indian Subcontinent, and GCC to Far East. The aggregation must be available for configurable time periods including weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual with support for both Gregorian calendar periods and Hijri calendar periods for entities operating on Hijri fiscal calendars. Comparative analysis must show current period versus prior period and current period versus budget with variance explanations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R006	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Annex III Harmful Substances in Packaged Form	The system must identify and flag containerized cargo containing harmful substances as defined under MARPOL Annex III, cross-referencing the IMDG Code classifications. When a booking or container manifest includes hazardous goods with marine pollutant markings, the system must verify that packaging, labeling, documentation, and stowage comply with Annex III requirements. The AI engine must automatically parse container manifest data to detect potential Annex III substances based on commodity codes (HS codes), UN numbers, and shipper declarations. For multi-entity shipments where cargo is booked through the India entity (JNPT) but transshipped via Jebel Ali to the Qatar entity (Hamad Port), the system must ensure Annex III compliance documentation travels with the container through all legs of the journey.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R004	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Customer-Level Repositioning Cost Allocation	The system must allocate repositioning costs to specific customers based on configurable allocation methodologies. Allocation methods must include direct allocation where a repositioning move was triggered by a specific customer booking with identifiable demand, proportional allocation where repositioning costs for a trade lane are distributed among customers proportional to their booking volume on that lane, and tiered allocation where key account customers receive a different cost allocation treatment than spot customers. The customer-level allocation must integrate with the customer profitability analysis module to provide a true cost-to-serve metric that includes repositioning costs alongside direct freight costs, terminal handling, and documentation. The system must support what-if analysis showing how customer profitability changes under different repositioning cost allocation scenarios. Allocation results must be stored at the individual booking level enabling drill-down from customer total to individual shipment repositioning cost contribution.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R005	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Multi-Entity Intercompany Repositioning Settlement	When containers are repositioned between entities (e.g., from UAE fleet surplus to Qatar fleet deficit), the system must generate intercompany settlement transactions reflecting the transfer of container value between entities. The settlement must comply with transfer pricing regulations applicable in each jurisdiction including Qatar tax authority requirements, UAE corporate tax provisions under Federal Decree-Law No. 47 of 2022, KSA transfer pricing regulations under ZATCA guidelines, and Indian transfer pricing rules under Section 92 of the Income Tax Act 1961. Transfer prices must be set using the arm's length principle with supporting documentation generated automatically. The system must create paired accounting entries in both the sending and receiving entity's books with automatic elimination at the group consolidation level. Settlement currency, timing, and netting rules must be configurable per entity pair. Monthly intercompany reconciliation reports must be generated automatically with discrepancy alerts for unmatched transactions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R006	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Vessel Service Level Cost Attribution	The system must attribute repositioning costs to specific vessel services to enable profitability analysis at the service string level. When empty containers are loaded onto a vessel as repositioning cargo, the slot cost must be calculated based on the vessel operating cost allocation model which considers total vessel daily operating cost, the proportion of vessel capacity consumed by repositioning empties versus laden containers, port-specific costs at each call port on the rotation, and bunker fuel cost attributable to the additional weight of empty containers. The system must distinguish between repositioning on own-operated vessels (where slot cost is an internal allocation) and repositioning on partner or slot-chartered vessels (where slot cost is an actual freight charge). Attribution must flow into the vessel service P&L enabling identification of services that are net contributors to repositioning efficiency versus services that primarily consume repositioning resources. The analysis must cover services calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R007	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Budget and Forecast Integration for Repositioning Costs	The system must integrate with the financial planning module to support budgeting and forecasting of repositioning costs. The budget module must allow entry of planned repositioning volumes by trade lane, container type, and month with costs calculated using budgeted rates for ocean freight, terminal handling, and inland transportation. Actual repositioning costs must be compared against budget at configurable aggregation levels (entity, trade lane, container type, cost category) with automatic variance calculation. The forecast module must use AI-generated demand forecasts from FEAT-058-2-001 to project future repositioning requirements and costs over a rolling 12-month horizon. Budget data must support input in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automatic USD consolidation using budgeted exchange rates. Budget approval workflows must route through entity finance managers with final approval by group CFO for consolidated repositioning budgets. The system must flag forecast repositioning costs that exceed budget by more than a configurable threshold, generating early warning alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R008	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Cost Center and GL Account Mapping	The system must map repositioning costs to the appropriate general ledger accounts and cost centers in each entity's chart of accounts. The mapping must be configurable per entity to accommodate different accounting structures across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. Default GL account mapping must distinguish between domestic repositioning costs (movements within the same country), international repositioning costs (cross-border movements), leasing costs for containers leased as an alternative to repositioning, incentive program costs (from FEAT-058-2-003), and repositioning revenue offsets from benefit attribution. Cost center mapping must support hierarchical structures with allocations cascading from department to division to entity to group. The system must validate GL mappings against the active chart of accounts and reject postings to closed or inactive accounts. Journal entries must be generated in the format required by each entity's ERP general ledger with proper Arabic and English descriptions, entity-specific fiscal period assignment, and both Gregorian and Hijri posting dates where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R009	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Repositioning P&L Reporting Suite	The system must provide a comprehensive suite of P&L reports for repositioning activities. Required reports must include an Entity Repositioning P&L Summary showing total costs, benefits, and net result per entity per period; a Trade Lane Profitability Matrix showing repositioning economics for each origin-destination pair in a heat map format; a Container Type P&L Breakdown comparing repositioning costs and benefits across standard dry, reefer, open top, flat rack, and tank containers; a Trend Analysis Report showing repositioning P&L evolution over rolling 12 months with moving average and trend lines; and a Management Executive Summary condensing key metrics into a single-page dashboard. All reports must support Arabic and English rendering with proper RTL layout, Hijri and Gregorian date headers, and multi-currency display. Reports must be exportable to Excel with formatted worksheets, PDF for management distribution, and CSV for data extraction. Scheduled report generation and email distribution must be configurable by recipient and frequency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R010	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Variance Analysis and Root Cause Identification	The system must perform automated variance analysis comparing actual repositioning P&L against budget, forecast, and prior period benchmarks. Variance decomposition must separate the total variance into volume variance (more or fewer TEU repositioned than planned), rate variance (actual cost per TEU different from planned rate), mix variance (different container type or trade lane mix than planned), and currency variance (FX movements between QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD affecting consolidated results). For each material variance exceeding configurable thresholds, the AI must generate a root cause hypothesis based on correlation analysis with operational events such as vessel schedule changes, port congestion incidents, demand forecast misses, or market rate movements. Variance analysis must be available at entity, trade lane, and container type granularity. The analysis must be generated within 24 hours of period close and presented in both Arabic and English with drill-down capability from summary to individual transaction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R011	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Activity-Based Costing for Repositioning Overhead	The system must implement activity-based costing (ABC) to allocate repositioning overhead costs that cannot be directly attributed to specific moves. Overhead cost pools must include repositioning planning team labor costs, container tracking and monitoring system costs, empty container depot operating costs (rent, utilities, security, maintenance), repositioning-related IT infrastructure costs, and management overhead. Cost drivers must be configurable and may include number of repositioning moves planned, number of containers monitored, depot throughput volume, and system transaction count. The ABC model must allocate overhead costs to individual repositioning moves producing a fully-loaded cost per TEU repositioned. Overhead allocation must run on a monthly cycle aligned with each entity's financial closing calendar. The system must support parallel calculation of repositioning costs with and without overhead allocation enabling users to analyze both direct and fully-loaded cost perspectives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R012	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Real-Time P&L Dashboard with Drill-Down	The system must provide a real-time P&L dashboard that updates continuously as repositioning transactions are processed. The dashboard must display current period-to-date repositioning P&L at the group level with one-click drill-down to entity, trade lane, container type, and individual move levels. Key metrics on the dashboard must include total repositioning cost MTD and YTD in USD and local currencies, cost per TEU repositioned with trend sparkline, repositioning ROI percentage, budget utilization percentage with color-coded status (green under 90%, amber 90-100%, red over 100%), and top five highest-cost trade lanes. The dashboard must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with proper numeric formatting per locale. Data refresh latency must not exceed 15 minutes from transaction posting. The dashboard must support saved filter configurations per user and must be embeddable in executive portal pages. Performance must allow the dashboard to load within 4 seconds even when displaying 12 months of data across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R013	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Leasing Cost Integration and Comparison	The P&L attribution system must integrate leasing costs from the Leasing vs Repositioning Decision Engine (FEAT-058-2-002) to provide a unified view of total container availability costs. The integrated view must show for each trade lane and period the cost of repositioning owned containers, the cost of leasing containers locally, the total container availability cost (repositioning plus leasing), the unit cost comparison (cost per TEU made available through repositioning versus leasing), and the cumulative decision impact showing actual total cost versus the counterfactual cost if all decisions had gone the opposite way. This integration must account for multi-entity scenarios where one entity repositions while another leases on the same trade lane, enabling identification of group-level optimization opportunities. The comparison must adjust for differences in container quality and age between repositioned owned containers and leased containers to ensure like-for-like comparison. Monthly reconciliation between the P&L attribution system and the general ledger must be automated with discrepancy reports for items exceeding configurable materiality thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R014	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Transfer Pricing Documentation Generation	For all intercompany repositioning transactions, the system must automatically generate transfer pricing documentation packages compliant with OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines and local regulations in each jurisdiction. The documentation must include a functional analysis describing each entity's role in the repositioning transaction, a comparability analysis benchmarking the intercompany rates against arm's length market rates using data from freight rate indices and industry databases, the selected transfer pricing method (typically Comparable Uncontrolled Price or Cost Plus method) with justification, financial analysis showing the profitability impact on both entities, and a master file and local file structure per OECD BEPS Action 13. The system must generate documentation in English for group purposes and in Arabic where required by local tax authorities in Qatar, KSA, and UAE. Documentation must be generated automatically at year-end and must be available for ad-hoc generation when requested during tax audits. The system must store all supporting data and calculations for a minimum of 10 years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R015	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Profitability Simulation for Strategic Scenarios	The system must provide a simulation capability allowing strategic planners to model the P&L impact of different repositioning strategies before implementation. Simulation scenarios must include changing the fleet deployment pattern (e.g., allocating more owned containers to high-volume trade lanes), adjusting the lease versus reposition decision thresholds, adding or removing vessel services affecting repositioning routing options, changing incentive program parameters to influence empty return patterns, and implementing hub-and-spoke repositioning models versus point-to-point. Each simulation must produce a complete projected P&L statement comparable to the actual P&L format enabling direct comparison. The system must allow saving named scenarios with full parameter sets and must support scenario comparison dashboards showing up to four scenarios side by side. Simulation calculations must complete within 60 seconds for a 12-month projection across all four entities. Results must be available in all supported currencies with Arabic and English labels.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R016	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Automated Period-End Closing for Repositioning Accounts	The system must automate period-end closing procedures for repositioning-related accounts. Closing procedures must include accrual of repositioning costs for moves in transit at period end where the cost has been incurred but the invoice has not been received, reversal of prior period accruals upon receipt of actual invoices, provision for disputed invoices related to repositioning charges with aging analysis, reconciliation of repositioning cost subledger balances to general ledger control accounts, generation of closing journal entries with proper descriptions in Arabic and English, and production of period-end reconciliation schedules for auditor review. The closing process must support different fiscal period calendars across entities (Gregorian January-December for UAE and India, potentially Hijri-based for KSA, and configurable for Qatar). Closing must be executable per entity independently and must include a group-level consolidation step with intercompany elimination entries. The system must track closing status per entity per period and prevent posting to closed periods without authorized reopening.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R017	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	Audit Trail and Regulatory Compliance for P&L Data	All P&L attribution calculations, adjustments, and postings must be recorded in an immutable audit trail. The audit record must include the transaction identifier linking to the source repositioning move, the calculation methodology applied with all input parameters and intermediate results, the user or automated process that initiated or approved the transaction, the timestamp in UTC with Gregorian and Hijri equivalents, and the before and after state for any manual adjustments. The audit trail must support compliance requirements under International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) as adopted by Qatar, UAE, and KSA, Indian Accounting Standards (Ind AS), and local statutory reporting requirements in each jurisdiction. The system must support audit data extraction in formats compatible with major audit firm tools (ACL, IDEA, CaseWare). Access to audit trails must be controlled by role with read-only access for external auditors configurable with time-limited credentials. Audit data must be retained for a minimum of 10 years with archival to cold storage after 3 years while maintaining query accessibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-058-2-004-R018	FEAT-058-2-004	MOD-058	KPI Scorecards and Performance Benchmarking	The system must generate KPI scorecards for repositioning performance benchmarking across entities, trade lanes, and time periods. Scorecards must include repositioning cost as a percentage of total container logistics cost, repositioning cost per TEU of laden cargo carried on the trade lane (measuring the cost of maintaining equipment availability per unit of productive output), container turnaround time from discharge empty to next laden loading, percentage of repositioning decisions aligned with AI recommendations, forecast accuracy contribution to repositioning efficiency measured as the correlation between forecast accuracy improvement and repositioning cost reduction, and carbon emissions per TEU repositioned. Each KPI must have configurable target thresholds with red, amber, and green status indicators. Entity-level scorecards must be comparable with group-level benchmarks and anonymized industry benchmarks where available. Scorecards must be generated monthly and distributed automatically to entity general managers and group operations leadership. The scorecard layout must support Arabic RTL rendering with bilingual KPI labels and must export cleanly to PowerPoint format for inclusion in board presentation materials.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R001	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	MARPOL Annex Master Registry	The system must maintain a master registry of all six MARPOL Annexes with their specific sub-regulations, amendments, and effective dates. Each annex entry must store the IMO resolution reference, date of adoption, date of entry into force, and latest amendment date. The registry must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar dates for KSA and Qatar entities. Each regulation must be tagged with applicable vessel types (bulk carriers, container vessels, tankers, ro-ro), applicable gross tonnage thresholds, and geographical applicability including MARPOL Special Areas such as the Persian Gulf (also known as Arabian Gulf) Special Area under Annex I and Annex V. The system must allow authorized users to update regulation entries when IMO issues new amendments, with full audit trail tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R002	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Vessel Compliance Profile Management	The system must create and maintain a compliance profile for every vessel registered in the ERP, linking IMO number, flag state, classification society, and vessel type to applicable MARPOL requirements. The profile must auto-determine which annexes apply based on vessel characteristics such as gross tonnage, build date, cargo type capability, and engine specifications. For container vessels calling at Jebel Ali (AEJEA), Hamad Port (QAHAM), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), and JNPT (INNSA), the system must pre-populate applicable Annex VI Emission Control Area requirements. The compliance profile must support multi-entity ownership structures where a vessel may be owned by the Qatar entity but commercially managed by the UAE entity, maintaining separate compliance responsibilities per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R003	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Certificate Validity Tracking and Expiry Alerts	The system must track all MARPOL-related certificates including IOPP Certificate (Annex I), NLS Certificate (Annex II), ISPP Certificate (Annex IV), IAPP Certificate (Annex VI), and Ballast Water Management Certificate. Each certificate record must store issue date, expiry date, issuing authority (flag state or recognized organization), endorsement dates, and survey due dates. The AI engine must generate automated alerts at 90, 60, 30, and 14 days before certificate expiry, dispatched via email and in-app notification in both English and Arabic. If a certificate expires while the vessel is en route to any of the four ports, the system must flag the vessel as non-compliant and notify the port operations team and the designated person ashore (DPA) immediately.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R004	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Annex I Oil Discharge Monitoring	The system must record and monitor all oil discharge events from vessels, capturing discharge location (latitude/longitude), discharge rate in liters per nautical mile, oil content in parts per million, and timestamp. For discharges within MARPOL Special Areas including the Persian/Arabian Gulf, the system must enforce the stricter 15 ppm oil content limit and zero discharge of sludge. The system must integrate with Oil Discharge Monitoring Equipment (ODME) data feeds where available, and support manual entry with mandatory photographic evidence upload for vessels without automated systems. All discharge records must be cross-referenced against the Oil Record Book Part I and Part II entries. Currency amounts for any fines or penalties must be recorded in the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R007	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Annex IV Sewage Discharge Compliance	The system must track sewage treatment plant (STP) status and sewage discharge compliance for all vessels above 400 GT or carrying more than 15 persons. For each vessel calling at the four operational ports, the system must verify that the sewage treatment plant certificate is valid, the plant is operational, and discharge records comply with Annex IV distance and treatment requirements. The system must enforce that untreated sewage is not discharged within 12 nautical miles of the nearest land, and comminuted and disinfected sewage not within 3 nautical miles. Within port limits of Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT, all sewage must be retained onboard or discharged to port reception facilities, and the system must generate a sewage holding tank capacity warning when estimated capacity falls below 25 percent.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R008	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Annex VI Sulphur Cap and Emission Monitoring	The system must monitor and enforce the IMO 2020 global sulphur cap of 0.50% m/m for all fuel oil used onboard vessels. For each bunkering operation at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must record the fuel type (VLSFO, ULSFO, MGO, LSMGO, HFO with scrubber), sulphur content as stated on the bunker delivery note (BDN), quantity in metric tons, and supplier details. If a vessel uses HFO with an exhaust gas cleaning system (scrubber), the system must verify a valid SECC (SOx Emission Compliance Certificate) and approved EGCS technical file. AI-driven anomaly detection must flag discrepancies between reported fuel sulphur content and actual emission readings from continuous emission monitoring systems (CEMS) where integrated. Fuel cost records must support multi-currency entry in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR with real-time exchange rate conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R009	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	NOx Emission Tier Compliance Verification	The system must verify that each vessel's marine diesel engines comply with the applicable NOx emission tier (Tier I, Tier II, or Tier III) based on the engine's build date and the keel-laying date of the vessel. For engines installed on vessels constructed on or after 1 January 2016, Tier III compliance must be verified when operating in designated NOx Emission Control Areas. The system must store the Engine International Air Pollution Prevention (EIAPP) Certificate for each engine, including engine manufacturer, model, rated power output in kW, and NOx-critical components. If an engine undergoes major conversion, the system must trigger a re-assessment of the applicable NOx tier and require updated EIAPP documentation. The AI engine must predict NOx compliance risks based on engine age and maintenance history data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R010	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Energy Efficiency EEXI and CII Tracking	The system must calculate and track the Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index (EEXI) and Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII) ratings for all applicable vessels in the fleet. EEXI must be calculated based on the vessel's reference speed, installed power, and applicable correction factors as per IMO MEPC.351(78). Annual CII ratings (A through E) must be computed from annual fuel consumption data, distance traveled, and deadweight tonnage, applying the reduction factors specified for each compliance year. Vessels rated D for three consecutive years or E in any single year must trigger a corrective action plan workflow. The system must generate the Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan (SEEMP) Part III reports and submit data compatible with the IMO Data Collection System (DCS). All tracking dashboards must support Arabic RTL display and Hijri date selectors for KSA and Qatar entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R011	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Port State Control Inspection Readiness	The system must maintain a Port State Control (PSC) inspection readiness score for each vessel, aggregating compliance status across all six MARPOL Annexes, certificate validity, equipment condition, and crew training records. The AI engine must analyze historical PSC detention data from Paris MOU, Indian Ocean MOU, and Riyadh MOU databases to predict inspection likelihood and identify high-risk deficiency areas. Before a vessel calls at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT, the system must generate a pre-arrival PSC readiness checklist with identified gaps and recommended corrective actions. The readiness score must be displayed as a color-coded dashboard widget (green, amber, red) with drill-down capability into each annex compliance area. Reports must be exportable in PDF format with bilingual English-Arabic headers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R012	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Compliance Violation and Penalty Management	The system must record and manage all MARPOL compliance violations detected by port state authorities, flag state inspectors, or internal audits. Each violation record must include the vessel IMO number, port of detection (UN/LOCODE), inspection date, deficiency code per IMO Res. A.1155(32), corrective action required, responsible entity, and deadline for rectification. Financial penalties must be recorded in the currency imposed by the issuing authority (QAR for Qatar Maritime Authority, AED for UAE Federal Transport Authority, SAR for Saudi Transport General Authority, INR for Indian DG Shipping) with automated USD equivalent calculation. The system must support appeal workflow processes with document attachment capability for evidence submission. An AI-driven root cause analysis module must identify systemic compliance failures across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R013	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Multi-Entity Compliance Responsibility Matrix	The system must define and enforce a compliance responsibility matrix across the four operating entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), specifying which entity is responsible for each MARPOL obligation based on vessel registration, operational area, and commercial management arrangement. For chartered vessels, the system must differentiate between owner compliance obligations (structural and equipment-related under Annex I, II, IV) and charterer compliance obligations (operational matters under Annex V, VI fuel selection). The responsibility matrix must be configurable by the compliance manager and support scenarios where a vessel registered under the UAE entity operates commercially under the KSA entity in Saudi territorial waters. Entity-level compliance dashboards must aggregate only the obligations relevant to that entity while a group-level dashboard must consolidate all obligations with inter-entity drill-down capability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R014	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Offline Compliance Data Capture and Sync	The system must support offline data capture for MARPOL compliance events when vessels or port personnel operate without reliable internet connectivity, particularly during anchorage operations at JNPT outer anchorage or during passage through areas with limited satellite coverage. The offline module must allow recording of oil record book entries, garbage record book entries, fuel changeover logs, and equipment malfunction reports on mobile devices. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize all offline entries to the central database with conflict resolution logic that preserves the earliest timestamp record and flags discrepancies for manual review. Offline-captured entries must be digitally signed with the recording officer's credentials and timestamped using the device's GPS-verified clock to prevent backdating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R015	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	Regulatory Update Automation and Impact Assessment	The system must monitor IMO MEPC (Marine Environment Protection Committee) resolutions and circulars for updates to MARPOL regulations, using AI-powered document parsing to extract regulatory changes from published IMO documents. When a new amendment is detected, the system must automatically assess its impact on the fleet by cross-referencing the amendment scope (affected vessel types, tonnage thresholds, geographical areas) against the registered vessel profiles. An impact assessment report must be generated showing affected vessels, compliance gaps introduced by the new regulation, estimated cost of compliance in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR), and recommended implementation timeline. The report must be routed to the designated compliance officers of each affected entity for review and action planning. Historical regulatory change tracking must maintain a complete audit trail of all amendments applied.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R016	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	MARPOL Special Area Geofencing and Alerts	The system must implement geofencing boundaries for all MARPOL Special Areas relevant to the operational region, including the Persian/Arabian Gulf Special Area (Annex I and Annex V), the Gulf of Aden (Annex I), and any future designated Emission Control Areas in the Indian Ocean region. When a vessel's AIS position enters a geofenced Special Area, the system must automatically activate the stricter discharge and emission requirements applicable to that area and send real-time notifications to the vessel master, fleet operations center, and entity compliance officer. The system must calculate estimated time of entry into and exit from Special Areas based on voyage plan data to allow proactive preparation of equipment changeover such as fuel switching for Annex VI compliance. Geofence boundary data must be updated automatically when IMO designates new Special Areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-001-R017	FEAT-059-1-001	MOD-059	AI-Driven Compliance Risk Scoring and Prediction	The AI engine must compute a composite MARPOL compliance risk score for each vessel on a 0-100 scale, aggregating weighted factors including certificate expiry proximity, historical violation frequency, vessel age, last dry-dock date, crew competency records, and flag state performance on port state control white/grey/black lists. The model must be trained on historical PSC inspection outcomes and detention records to predict the probability of a MARPOL-related detention at each of the four operational ports. Risk scores must be recalculated daily and displayed on the fleet compliance dashboard with trend lines showing 30, 60, and 90-day trajectories. High-risk vessels scoring above 75 must trigger automated escalation to the entity-level DPA and group compliance director. The scoring algorithm weights must be configurable by authorized administrators and documented for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R001	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Management Plan Registry	The system must maintain a digital repository of approved Ballast Water Management Plans (BWMP) for each vessel in the fleet, storing the plan approval date, approving authority (flag state or classification society), applicable BWMS type code, and revision history. Each BWMP must be linked to the vessel's IMO number and the installed ballast water management system (BWMS) details including manufacturer, model, type approval certificate number, and maximum treatment capacity in cubic meters per hour. The system must alert operations teams 180 days before the vessel's compliance date for transitioning from D-1 (ballast water exchange) to D-2 (ballast water performance) standard. BWMP documents must be stored in both English and Arabic versions where required by Qatar and KSA maritime authorities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R002	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Exchange Recording D-1 Standard	The system must record all ballast water exchange (BWE) operations performed under the D-1 standard, capturing exchange start and end positions (latitude/longitude), water depth at exchange location (minimum 200 meters required), exchange method (sequential, flow-through, or dilution), volume exchanged per tank in cubic meters, and exchange efficiency percentage (minimum 95% volumetric exchange required for sequential method). For vessels transiting from Indian ports (JNPT) to Gulf ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali), the system must verify that exchange was performed in open ocean waters at least 200 nautical miles from the nearest land and in water depth exceeding 200 meters. If exchange conditions were not met due to safety concerns, the master's declaration of exemption must be recorded with weather data as supporting evidence.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R003	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Treatment System D-2 Performance Monitoring	The system must continuously monitor the performance of installed Ballast Water Management Systems (BWMS) against D-2 standard discharge limits: fewer than 10 viable organisms per cubic meter for organisms greater than or equal to 50 micrometers, fewer than 10 viable organisms per milliliter for organisms between 10 and 50 micrometers, and indicator microbe limits as specified in Regulation D-2. Treatment system operational data must be ingested from BWMS control systems where API integration is available, or entered manually by the vessel's engineering officer. The system must flag any treatment cycle where D-2 discharge standards are not met, immediately notifying the vessel master and shore-side compliance team. AI-driven trend analysis must predict BWMS component failure based on operating hours, treatment cycles completed, and UV lamp or filter replacement schedules.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R004	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Record Book Digital Management	The system must provide a digital Ballast Water Record Book compliant with BWM Convention Regulation B-2, recording every ballast water operation including uptake, circulation, treatment, exchange, and discharge. Each entry must capture the date and time (UTC and local), vessel position, ballast tank identification, volume in cubic meters, water temperature, and salinity. The digital record book must enforce sequential entry numbering with no gaps allowed, and each entry must be digitally signed by the responsible officer. The system must support export of the record book in a format acceptable to port state control officers at all four operational ports. Entries must display dates in both Gregorian and Hijri formats for inspections conducted by Qatar and KSA authorities, and all field labels must support Arabic RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R005	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Appetite and Tolerance Thresholds	The system must allow each entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to define independent risk appetite statements and numeric tolerance thresholds per risk category. When any risk score exceeds the entity-specific tolerance threshold, the system must automatically trigger escalation workflows including email notifications, SMS alerts, and in-app push notifications to designated risk committee members. Thresholds must be configurable in monetary values using the entity's local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion to USD equivalents using daily exchange rates from the central treasury module. Breach notifications must include the risk details, current score, threshold value, and recommended mitigation actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R005	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Pre-Arrival Ballast Water Reporting	The system must generate and electronically submit pre-arrival ballast water reporting forms to port authorities at least 24 hours before vessel arrival, complying with the specific format requirements of each port. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, the system must submit forms to the UAE Federal Transport Authority (FTA) Maritime portal. For Hamad Port, forms must comply with Qatar Maritime Declaration requirements. For JNPT, the system must submit to the Indian Directorate General of Shipping via the eFiling system. Each form must include last port of ballast water uptake, ballast water management method used (exchange or treatment), volume to be discharged at the destination port, and BWMS type approval details. The AI engine must auto-populate forms from voyage data and ballast water record book entries, requiring only officer verification before submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R006	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Sampling and Analysis Integration	The system must record and manage ballast water sampling events conducted by port state control authorities or third-party sampling agents at the four operational ports. Each sampling record must capture the sampling date and time, sampled tank identification, sampling method (indicative or detailed), laboratory name and accreditation number, analysis results for each D-2 parameter, and pass/fail determination. The system must support integration with external laboratory information management systems (LIMS) for automated result ingestion. If sampling results indicate non-compliance with D-2 standards, the system must trigger a compliance violation workflow including vessel hold notification, corrective action assignment, and financial provision for potential fines. Sampling costs must be recorded in the local port currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with automated USD conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R007	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Invasive Species Risk Assessment Engine	The AI engine must perform an invasive species risk assessment for each ballast water discharge operation, evaluating the biogeographic risk based on the ballast water uptake port environment compared to the discharge port environment. Risk factors must include water temperature differential, salinity differential, known invasive species presence at the uptake port (referencing GloFouling and GloBallast databases), and whether the uptake port is in the same biogeographic region as the discharge port. For routes between Indian Ocean ports (JNPT) and Persian/Arabian Gulf ports (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali), the system must apply elevated risk weighting due to the documented sensitivity of Gulf marine ecosystems. Risk assessment results must be stored for each voyage and accessible for review by environmental compliance officers across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R008	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	BWMS Maintenance and Spare Parts Tracking	The system must track the maintenance schedule and spare parts inventory for each installed Ballast Water Management System, referencing the manufacturer's maintenance manual requirements and classification society survey intervals. Maintenance tasks must include UV lamp replacements (tracked by operating hours), filter element replacements, chemical dosing system calibration, and sensor calibration events. The system must generate predictive maintenance alerts based on AI analysis of operating hours, treatment cycle count, and historical failure patterns. Spare parts procurement must integrate with the ERP's purchasing module, supporting multi-currency purchase orders (USD for international suppliers, AED/QAR/SAR/INR for regional suppliers). Critical spare parts falling below minimum stock levels must trigger automated reorder requests to the designated procurement entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R009	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Contingency Planning	The system must support the creation and management of ballast water contingency plans for scenarios where the primary BWMS fails during a voyage. Contingency options must include emergency ballast water exchange procedures, diversion to an alternate port with reception facilities, use of backup treatment systems, and retention of ballast water onboard. For each contingency scenario, the system must calculate the operational and financial impact including potential voyage delays, additional fuel costs, port deviation charges, and regulatory penalties, all denominated in USD with entity-level cost allocation in local currencies. The AI engine must recommend the optimal contingency action based on vessel position, remaining voyage distance to the destination port (Hamad, Khalifa, Jebel Ali, or JNPT), weather conditions, and available ballast tank capacity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R010	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Same Location Ballast Exception Management	The system must handle exceptions under BWM Convention Regulation B-3.6, where ballast water uptake and discharge occur at the same location and no mixing with unmanaged ballast water has taken place. The system must verify same-location exceptions by comparing uptake position coordinates with discharge position coordinates within a configurable tolerance radius (default 1 nautical mile). For vessels performing ballast operations within a single port complex (for example, moving between berths at Jebel Ali's multiple container terminals), the system must automatically apply the same-location exception and document the justification in the ballast water record book. The exception must be flagged for review by the shore-side compliance officer and supported with AIS track data as evidence of the vessel remaining within the port area throughout the ballast retention period.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R011	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Regional BWM Regulation Variance Management	The system must accommodate regional variations in ballast water management requirements that may differ from or exceed the BWM Convention baseline. For India, the system must enforce the Indian Maritime Administration's additional requirements under Merchant Shipping (Prevention of Pollution by Harmful Substances Carried by Sea in Packaged Form) Rules. For UAE ports, compliance with FTA Maritime Circular requirements for ballast water reporting must be enforced. For Qatar, Hamad Port Authority-specific ballast water discharge restrictions within port limits must be configured. For KSA, Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani) environmental regulations must be applied. The system must maintain a regulation variance matrix that automatically selects the most stringent applicable requirement when a vessel moves between jurisdictions of the four operational countries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R012	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Volume and Stability Integration	The system must integrate ballast water management operations with vessel stability calculations to ensure that ballast water exchange or treatment operations do not compromise vessel stability or structural integrity. For sequential ballast water exchange, the system must verify that the planned exchange sequence maintains positive stability (GM above minimum required), does not exceed hull stress limits (shear force and bending moment), and keeps the vessel within acceptable trim parameters. Integration with the vessel's loading computer data must be supported via standardized data exchange formats. For container vessels calling at JNPT with tidal restrictions, the system must factor in arrival draft limitations when planning ballast water discharge volumes to ensure the vessel meets the maximum permissible draft for channel transit.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R013	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	BWM Convention Compliance Date Tracker	The system must track the BWM Convention implementation timeline for each vessel, determining the applicable compliance date based on the vessel's IOPP Certificate renewal date and build date as specified in BWM Convention Regulation B-3. For existing vessels, the system must calculate the D-2 standard compliance deadline based on the IOPP renewal survey schedule. The system must generate a fleet-wide compliance timeline dashboard showing all vessels sorted by their D-2 compliance deadline, with color coding (green for compliant, amber for within 12 months of deadline, red for overdue). Capital expenditure planning for BWMS installation must be supported with cost estimates in USD and entity-level budget allocation in QAR, AED, SAR, and INR. The AI engine must recommend optimal BWMS installation scheduling to minimize vessel off-hire time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R014	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Sediment Management	The system must track ballast tank sediment management activities as required under BWM Convention Regulation B-4, including sediment removal during dry-docking, sediment trap maintenance, and sediment disposal records. For each sediment removal event, the system must record the volume of sediment removed in cubic meters, disposal method (approved shore reception facility or other approved method), disposal location, and cost. The system must enforce that sediment is not disposed of at sea and only at approved reception facilities. For vessels dry-docking at regional shipyards (Dubai Drydocks, Nakilat shipyard in Ras Laffan, or Indian shipyards near JNPT), the system must verify that the shipyard's sediment reception facility is approved and compliant. All sediment disposal costs must be recorded with multi-currency support and allocated to the owning entity's maintenance budget.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R015	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Ballast Water Discharge Monitoring at Port Reception	The system must manage and record ballast water discharges to port reception facilities where vessels cannot meet D-1 or D-2 standards and must use shore-based reception as an alternative compliance method. The system must track reception facility availability at each of the four operational ports, including facility capacity in cubic meters per hour, operating hours, advance booking requirements, and cost per cubic meter of ballast water received. For each discharge to a reception facility, the system must generate a waste delivery receipt cross-referenced with the vessel's ballast water record book entry. Costs must be invoiced to the appropriate entity and recorded in the local currency of the port (QAR for Hamad Port, AED for Khalifa Port and Jebel Ali, INR for JNPT) with automated conversion to the vessel-owning entity's reporting currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R016	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	Offline Ballast Water Operations Recording	The system must enable offline recording of ballast water operations on mobile devices when vessel connectivity is unavailable, which is common during ocean passage between Indian and Gulf ports. The offline module must support recording of all Ballast Water Record Book entries including uptake, exchange, treatment, and discharge events with full data fields (position, volume, tank ID, timestamps). Data must be stored locally on the device with AES-256 encryption and synchronized to the central server upon connectivity restoration. The synchronization engine must handle conflict resolution when the same tank record has been updated both onboard and ashore, using a last-writer-wins strategy with full conflict audit log. GPS coordinates captured offline must be validated against the vessel's AIS track upon synchronization to detect any positional data anomalies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-002-R017	FEAT-059-1-002	MOD-059	BWM Compliance Reporting and Analytics Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive BWM compliance analytics dashboard with fleet-wide and vessel-level views, showing D-1/D-2 compliance status, BWMS operational performance metrics, upcoming compliance deadlines, sampling results history, and violation trends. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), vessel type, flag state, and time period. AI-generated insights must highlight systemic risks such as recurring BWMS failures across a specific equipment model, ports with high sampling frequency, and vessels approaching compliance deadlines without installed BWMS. The dashboard must be fully bilingual with Arabic RTL layout support, and all date filters must accommodate both Gregorian and Hijri calendar selection. Dashboard data must be exportable to Excel and PDF formats with entity-specific branding and letterhead.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R001	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Certificate and Declaration Registry	The system must maintain a registry of International Anti-Fouling System (IAFS) Certificates and Anti-Fouling System Declarations for all vessels in the fleet and all third-party vessels calling at the four operational ports. For vessels of 400 GT and above engaged in international voyages, the system must track the IAFS Certificate including issue date, expiry date, issuing authority, survey type (initial, renewal, or additional), and details of the anti-fouling system applied. For vessels below 400 GT or those not required to carry an IAFS Certificate, a signed Declaration on Anti-Fouling System must be stored. The system must enforce that no vessel without a valid IAFS Certificate or Declaration is cleared for berth allocation at Hamad Port (QAHAM), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), or JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R002	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Organotin Compound Prohibition Enforcement	The system must enforce the AFS Convention prohibition on the use of organotin compounds (particularly tributyltin or TBT) as biocides in anti-fouling systems. For vessels with a keel-laying date before 1 January 2003, the system must verify that either the organotin-based anti-fouling system has been removed, or an approved barrier coat has been applied over the existing organotin paint to prevent leaching. The system must record AFS survey results including XRF (X-ray fluorescence) sampling data where conducted, coating thickness measurements, and surveyor observations. If a vessel is found to have non-compliant anti-fouling during a port state inspection at any of the four ports, the system must initiate a compliance violation workflow with mandatory remediation planning including estimated coating removal and reapplication costs in USD and the entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R003	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Anti-Fouling Coating Lifecycle Management	The system must track the complete lifecycle of anti-fouling coatings applied to each vessel, from initial application during newbuilding or dry-docking through to the next scheduled recoating. Coating records must include the product name, manufacturer, active biocide type and concentration, application date, dry film thickness (DFT) measurements per hull zone, expected service life in months, and next recommended application date. The AI engine must predict coating degradation based on the vessel's trading pattern (tropical waters between India and Gulf accelerate fouling), speed profile, and idle time in port. When a vessel's coating is approaching end of service life, the system must generate a dry-docking planning recommendation with cost estimates for hull preparation and recoating, budgeted in USD with entity-level allocation in QAR, AED, SAR, or INR depending on the owning entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R004	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Cybutryne Controls Compliance Tracking	The system must enforce the 2021 amendment to the AFS Convention (MEPC.331(76)) which prohibits the use of cybutryne (Irgarol 1051) as an anti-fouling biocide, effective from 1 January 2023. For vessels with anti-fouling systems applied before the prohibition date, the system must track whether the cybutryne-containing coating has been removed or sealed with an approved barrier coat by the vessel's next scheduled renewal survey of the IAFS Certificate. The system must cross-reference the anti-fouling coating product database to identify any coatings containing cybutryne and flag affected vessels. Compliance timeline tracking must include cost projections for remediation work in multi-currency format. All cybutryne compliance status displays must support Arabic RTL rendering and bilingual English-Arabic labeling for compliance officers in Qatar and KSA entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R005	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Survey Scheduling and Management	The system must manage the scheduling of AFS surveys in alignment with the vessel's IAFS Certificate cycle, including initial surveys before the vessel enters service, renewal surveys at intervals not exceeding 60 months, and additional surveys when the anti-fouling system is changed or replaced. Survey scheduling must be coordinated with other statutory surveys (IOPP, load line, safety equipment) to minimize off-hire time. The system must maintain a list of recognized organizations (classification societies) authorized to conduct AFS surveys on behalf of each flag state represented in the fleet. For dry-docking at regional facilities, the system must integrate with the drydock booking module to align AFS survey dates with available dry-dock slots at Dubai Drydocks, Bahrain's ASRY, or Indian shipyards near JNPT. Survey cost estimates must be tracked in multi-currency with entity-level budget allocation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R006	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Hull Fouling Inspection and Reporting	The system must record in-water hull fouling inspections conducted at the four operational ports, capturing inspection date, inspector credentials, inspection method (diver, ROV, or drone), fouling rating per the IMO Biofouling Guidelines (MEPC.207(62)) scale from FR-0 (no fouling) to FR-5 (heavy calcareous fouling), and photographic or video evidence. The system must support inspection reports segmented by hull zone (flat bottom, vertical sides, boot-top, niche areas including sea chests, bow thruster tunnels, and rudder). If fouling rating exceeds FR-3 on any hull zone, the system must recommend hull cleaning before the vessel's next voyage and calculate the estimated fuel penalty of operating with the detected fouling level. Inspection costs must be recorded in the port's local currency with automated USD equivalent conversion.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R007	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	In-Water Hull Cleaning Compliance Management	The system must manage in-water hull cleaning operations at the four operational ports, ensuring compliance with local port authority regulations that may restrict or regulate hull cleaning activities. For Jebel Ali, the system must enforce Dubai Maritime City Authority regulations on in-water cleaning including approved cleaning methods, capture system requirements, and waste handling procedures. For JNPT, Indian maritime environmental regulations governing hull cleaning discharge must be applied. The system must record cleaning date, cleaning company, method used (brush, cavitation, or other), hull zones cleaned, waste water capture method, and volume of waste generated and disposed. If the port does not permit in-water cleaning (as may be the case at certain berths in Hamad Port), the system must recommend alternative solutions such as dry-docking or transit to an approved cleaning location.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R008	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Biofouling Management Plan Digital Repository	The system must store and manage vessel Biofouling Management Plans as recommended by IMO Biofouling Guidelines (MEPC.207(62)), linking each plan to the vessel profile by IMO number. The plan must document the vessel's anti-fouling system details, niche area protection measures, operational practices to minimize fouling (such as speed management and port idle time reduction), and scheduled inspection and maintenance activities. The system must generate automated reminders for biofouling management plan review at intervals not exceeding 36 months. For vessels trading exclusively between the Gulf and Indian subcontinent (warm tropical waters year-round), the AI engine must recommend more aggressive fouling management schedules due to accelerated biofouling growth rates in these conditions. Plans must be accessible in both English and Arabic for regulatory inspection purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R009	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Convention Regional Regulation Variance	The system must accommodate regional variations in anti-fouling regulations that may exceed AFS Convention requirements. For the UAE, the system must track compliance with Federal Law requirements on marine environment protection that may impose additional restrictions on anti-fouling substances. For India, compliance with the Indian Maritime Administration's environmental regulations regarding hull coatings must be monitored. For Qatar, Qatar Environmental and Climate Change Regulations applying to vessels in Qatari waters must be tracked. For KSA, the General Authority of Meteorology and Environmental Protection (now known as the National Center for Environmental Compliance) regulations must be applied. The system must maintain a regulation variance matrix and automatically apply the most stringent standard when a vessel operates across multiple jurisdictions within the four-country operational area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R010	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Anti-Fouling Product Database and Approval Status	The system must maintain a database of anti-fouling coating products with their approval status under the AFS Convention, recording product name, manufacturer, active biocide substances and their CAS numbers, type approval certificate reference, approved application methods, and environmental classification. The database must distinguish between copper-based biocidal coatings, biocide-free silicone or fluoropolymer coatings, and other emerging technologies such as ultrasonic anti-fouling systems. Each product must be tagged with its approval status in each of the four operational countries (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India), as some jurisdictions may impose additional restrictions beyond the AFS Convention. The system must alert procurement teams when a previously approved product is banned or restricted in any jurisdiction, preventing non-compliant purchasing. Product data sheets must be stored in English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R007	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Review Scheduling and Workflow	The system must automate periodic risk review scheduling based on configurable frequencies per risk severity level: critical risks reviewed weekly, high risks reviewed bi-weekly, moderate risks reviewed monthly, and low risks reviewed quarterly. Review workflows must route to appropriate risk owners and committee members based on the entity's organizational hierarchy. The system must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar-based scheduling, respecting entity-specific holidays (Qatar National Day, UAE National Day, Saudi National Day, Indian Republic Day and Independence Day). Overdue reviews must trigger automated escalation to the next management level after a configurable grace period, defaulting to 3 business days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R011	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Non-Compliance Impact on Port Operations	The system must assess and enforce operational restrictions when a vessel is found to have non-compliant anti-fouling systems during port state control inspection or pre-arrival screening. If a vessel lacks a valid IAFS Certificate, the system must restrict berth allocation and notify terminal operations at the affected port (Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT). The system must calculate the commercial impact of AFS non-compliance including estimated delay costs per day in USD, container handling disruption costs, and potential penalties from port state authorities. Delay and penalty costs must be allocated to the responsible entity and recorded in the appropriate local currency. The system must support a remediation workflow where the vessel can be conditionally cleared for berth subject to a time-bound corrective action plan approved by both the flag state and port state authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R012	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Hull Performance Monitoring and Fouling Impact Analysis	The system must integrate hull performance monitoring data to assess the relationship between hull fouling levels and vessel energy efficiency, calculating the speed-power penalty attributable to hull condition. Data inputs must include noon report data (speed, fuel consumption, draft, trim, weather), hull and propeller inspection results, and cleaning event records. The AI engine must calculate hull fouling impact as a percentage increase in fuel consumption compared to the vessel's baseline clean-hull performance, expressed in additional metric tons of fuel per day and associated additional cost in USD. The system must generate hull performance trend reports showing fouling accumulation rates between cleaning events and recommend optimal cleaning intervals to minimize the total cost of ownership (cleaning cost versus fuel penalty). Reports must support entity-level cost allocation across the four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R013	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Compliance Audit Trail and Documentation	The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all AFS compliance activities for each vessel, including certificate issuance and renewal, survey reports, coating application records, hull inspection reports, cleaning event records, and any non-compliance incidents with corrective actions. The audit trail must be tamper-proof with each entry timestamped, user-attributed, and sequentially indexed. For vessels that change ownership or management between entities (for example, from the India entity to the Qatar entity), the full AFS compliance history must transfer with the vessel record. The system must support audit trail export in a format suitable for flag state inspections, port state control reviews, and charterer vetting inspections (SIRE, CDI, or RightShip). All audit trail entries must display timestamps in UTC and local time of the recording entity, with Hijri date display available for Qatar and KSA users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R014	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Environmental Impact Assessment of Anti-Fouling Systems	The system must calculate and report the environmental impact of anti-fouling systems used across the fleet, including estimated biocide release rates based on coating type and hull surface area, copper or zinc leaching rates in micrograms per square centimeter per day, and cumulative environmental load per port call. For environmentally sensitive areas such as the coral reef ecosystems in Qatar and UAE waters, the AI engine must assess whether the fleet's anti-fouling systems pose elevated ecological risk and recommend lower-impact alternatives. The system must generate annual environmental impact reports per entity and per vessel, supporting Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) reporting and ESG disclosure requirements. Reports must present financial data in entity-local currencies with group-level USD consolidation, and must be formatted in bilingual English-Arabic for Gulf entity submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R015	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Niche Area Fouling Protection Management	The system must specifically track fouling protection and management for vessel niche areas as defined in IMO Biofouling Guidelines, including sea chests, bow thruster tunnels, stern tube seals, rudder hinges, propeller shafts, anchor chains and lockers, and hull appendages. For each niche area, the system must record the protection method applied (anti-fouling coating, marine growth prevention system (MGPS), cathodic protection, or physical barrier), installation or application date, inspection history, and condition assessment. The AI engine must identify niche areas with recurring fouling issues and recommend targeted interventions. For vessels operating predominantly in the warm waters of the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean, the system must apply enhanced monitoring schedules for niche areas due to accelerated biofouling growth rates in water temperatures consistently above 25 degrees Celsius.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R016	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	AFS Data Sharing with Charterer Vetting Systems	The system must support data export and API integration with major charterer vetting platforms including SIRE (Ship Inspection Report Programme), RightShip, and CDI (Chemical Distribution Institute) for sharing AFS compliance information. Exportable data must include IAFS Certificate validity, last hull inspection date and fouling rating, last cleaning date, anti-fouling coating type and age, and any AFS-related deficiencies from port state control inspections. The system must automatically prepare vetting questionnaire responses related to anti-fouling compliance, pulling data from the vessel's AFS compliance profile. For commercially sensitive data, the system must enforce entity-level access controls ensuring that the Qatar entity's vessel data is not accessible to the India entity's commercial team without explicit authorization from the fleet compliance director.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-003-R017	FEAT-059-1-003	MOD-059	Emerging Anti-Fouling Technology Evaluation Module	The system must provide an evaluation module for emerging anti-fouling technologies, allowing the technical team to assess new products and methods against criteria including environmental compliance under the AFS Convention, performance in warm-water conditions typical of the Gulf and Indian Ocean, cost-effectiveness, and compatibility with existing hull coating systems. The module must store technology evaluation records including trial vessel assignment, trial start and end dates, performance measurements during the trial period, and final recommendation. Cost-benefit analysis must compare new technology costs against current anti-fouling expenditure, calculating payback periods in months and net present value in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). The AI engine must monitor maritime industry publications and patent databases to proactively identify emerging anti-fouling solutions for evaluation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R001	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Garbage Management Plan Digital Repository	The system must maintain a digital repository of vessel-specific Garbage Management Plans as required by MARPOL Annex V Regulation 10.2 for every vessel of 100 GT and above and every vessel certified to carry 15 or more persons. Each plan must document waste segregation procedures, collection and storage arrangements, onboard processing equipment (compactor, incinerator, comminuter), designated crew responsibilities, and procedures for discharge to port reception facilities. The system must enforce that every vessel in the fleet has a current, approved Garbage Management Plan linked to its profile by IMO number. Plans must be available in the working language of the crew and in English, with Arabic translation available for vessels operating under Qatar, UAE, or KSA entity management. The AI engine must analyze plan completeness against MARPOL Annex V requirements and flag any deficiencies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R002	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Waste Category Classification and Segregation Tracking	The system must classify and track vessel waste according to the MARPOL Annex V garbage categories: Category A (plastics), Category B (food wastes), Category C (domestic wastes), Category D (cooking oil), Category E (incinerator ashes), Category F (operational wastes), Category G (animal carcasses), Category H (fishing gear), and Category I (e-waste). For container vessels operating between the four ports, Category F operational wastes (cargo residues, cargo dunnage) are particularly relevant and must be sub-classified by associated cargo type. The system must enforce proper waste segregation by requiring category selection for every waste entry, and the AI engine must detect anomalous categorization patterns (such as unusually low plastic waste volumes which may indicate improper segregation). All waste category labels must be displayed in both English and Arabic with correct RTL rendering.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R003	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Garbage Record Book Digital Management	The system must provide a digital Garbage Record Book compliant with MARPOL Annex V Regulation 10.3, recording every garbage discharge, disposal, or incineration operation. Each record must capture the date and time (UTC and local), vessel position (latitude and longitude), garbage category per MARPOL classification, estimated volume in cubic meters, disposal method (discharged to sea, delivered to port reception facility, or incinerated onboard), and for discharge to sea the distance from nearest land in nautical miles. Entries must be sequentially numbered with no gaps, digitally signed by the responsible officer, and countersigned by the vessel master. The digital record book must support export in formats acceptable to port state control officers at all four operational ports. Date displays must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats for KSA and Qatar port inspections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R004	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Discharge to Sea Compliance Validation	The system must validate all garbage discharge-to-sea operations against MARPOL Annex V discharge requirements, applying the correct restrictions based on the garbage category, vessel location, and whether the vessel is within a MARPOL Annex V Special Area. Outside Special Areas, the system must enforce that food waste is comminuted to particles less than 25mm before discharge and vessel is more than 12 nautical miles from nearest land. Within the Persian/Arabian Gulf Special Area (designated under Annex V), the system must enforce the near-total prohibition on garbage discharge to sea, permitting only food waste discharge when the vessel is more than 12 nautical miles from land. The system must integrate with the vessel's GPS position to perform real-time validation against Special Area geofence boundaries and nearest land distance calculations. Any attempted non-compliant discharge entry must be blocked and logged as an attempted violation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R005	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Port Reception Facility Waste Delivery Management	The system must manage waste delivery to port reception facilities at the four operational ports, tracking advance waste notification submissions, delivery schedules, waste types and volumes delivered, and waste delivery receipts. For Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, the system must comply with UAE port waste delivery notification requirements (minimum 24 hours before arrival). For Hamad Port, Qatar port authority waste notification procedures must be followed. For JNPT, Indian Maritime Administration waste declaration requirements must be met. The system must record waste delivery costs per category in the local port currency (AED for Jebel Ali and Khalifa Port, QAR for Hamad Port, INR for JNPT) with automated USD equivalent calculation. If a port reception facility is unavailable or has insufficient capacity, the system must log the inadequacy report as required under MARPOL Annex V Regulation 8 and recommend the next available port with adequate facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R006	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Onboard Waste Processing Equipment Monitoring	The system must track the operational status, maintenance history, and certification of onboard waste processing equipment including incinerators, compactors, comminuters, and food waste treatment systems. For incinerators, the system must verify compliance with IMO MEPC.244(66) resolution requirements including maximum flue gas outlet temperature limits, and track approved waste types that may be incinerated (noting that incineration of MARPOL Annex I, II, and III substances is prohibited). Equipment maintenance records must include last service date, operating hours since last service, next scheduled maintenance, and any outstanding deficiencies. If waste processing equipment is inoperative, the system must calculate the increased waste storage requirement and predict whether onboard storage capacity is sufficient until the next port call where reception facilities are available. Equipment maintenance costs must be recorded in the procuring entity's local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R007	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Waste Volume Estimation and Storage Capacity Management	The system must estimate daily waste generation rates per vessel based on crew size, passenger count (if applicable), voyage duration, and cargo type carried. The AI engine must build vessel-specific waste generation models using historical garbage record book data, improving prediction accuracy over time. The system must track onboard waste storage capacity per garbage category, calculating remaining capacity and estimated days until storage is full. For long voyages between JNPT and Gulf ports (typically 3-7 days), the system must predict whether onboard storage will be sufficient for the entire voyage considering Special Area restrictions that limit discharge to sea. If storage capacity is predicted to be insufficient, the system must recommend waste delivery at an intermediate port or additional onboard waste processing. Storage capacity alerts must be generated at 75% and 90% utilization thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R008	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Cargo-Related Waste Management for Container Operations	The system must specifically manage cargo-related waste generated during container operations at the four ports, including cargo residues (Category F under MARPOL Annex V), dunnage materials, lashing equipment waste, damaged packaging, and container cleaning waste. For hazardous cargo residues classified as Harmful to the Marine Environment (HME), the system must enforce the prohibition on discharge to sea and require delivery to port reception facilities. The system must link cargo waste generation to specific booking numbers, container numbers, and bill of lading references to enable cost allocation to the responsible commercial entity. For transshipment containers moving between JNPT and Gulf ports, waste generated during container inspection or re-stuffing must be tracked and allocated to the correct entity. Waste disposal costs must support multi-currency recording in QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R009	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Placards and Waste Signage Compliance Tracking	The system must track compliance with MARPOL Annex V Regulation 10.1 requiring display of placards notifying crew and passengers of garbage disposal requirements. For vessels of 12 meters or more in length, the system must verify that placards are displayed in the working language of the crew and additionally in English, French, or Spanish for international voyages. For vessels operating under Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities, the system must verify that Arabic language placards are also displayed in compliance with local maritime authority requirements. The system must maintain a placard inventory record per vessel, tracking placard locations (mess rooms, galley, accommodation areas, bridge, cargo areas), placard condition from last inspection, and replacement dates. Inspection checklists for placard compliance must be generated as part of the pre-port-call compliance readiness assessment and must support digital photo attachment for evidence documentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R010	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Special Area Discharge Restriction Automation	The system must automatically detect when a vessel enters or exits a MARPOL Annex V Special Area and enforce the applicable discharge restrictions. For the Persian/Arabian Gulf Special Area, the system must enforce that no garbage except food waste (under specified conditions) may be discharged into the sea. The system must integrate with AIS position data and voyage plan waypoints to determine Special Area entry and exit times. Upon entering the Gulf Special Area (which encompasses the operational waters between JNPT transits and the three Gulf ports), the system must automatically switch the vessel's garbage disposal options to restrict available disposal methods to onboard storage, incineration (where permitted), or retention for delivery to port reception facilities. The system must calculate whether the vessel has sufficient onboard storage and processing capacity to comply with Special Area restrictions for the planned duration within the Special Area.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R011	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Waste Minimization AI Recommendations	The AI engine must analyze waste generation data across the fleet and generate actionable recommendations for waste minimization at vessel and fleet levels. Recommendations must address procurement practices (reducing single-use packaging in vessel stores), operational practices (optimizing food provisioning to reduce food waste based on crew size and voyage duration), and cargo handling practices (minimizing dunnage waste through reusable securing methods). The system must calculate the environmental benefit of each recommendation in terms of estimated waste volume reduction in cubic meters per year and the financial benefit in terms of waste disposal cost savings in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies. The AI engine must benchmark each vessel's waste generation rate against fleet averages and industry benchmarks, identifying outlier vessels that may benefit from targeted waste reduction programs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R012	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Waste Delivery Receipt Reconciliation	The system must reconcile waste delivery receipts from port reception facilities against the vessel's Garbage Record Book entries to ensure completeness and accuracy of waste management documentation. Each waste delivery receipt must be digitally captured (scanned or photographed) and linked to the corresponding Garbage Record Book entry by date, port, and waste category. The system must detect discrepancies between the waste volume recorded in the Garbage Record Book and the volume stated on the port reception facility receipt, flagging variances exceeding 10% for investigation. For multi-entity operations where the vessel's owning entity differs from the port entity processing the waste delivery (for example, a Qatar entity vessel delivering waste at JNPT managed by the India entity), the system must ensure proper cost allocation and inter-entity invoicing in the appropriate currencies (QAR to INR with USD reference).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R013	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Marine Plastic Litter Reporting and Tracking	The system must support IMO's Action Plan to Address Marine Plastic Litter from Ships by tracking all plastic waste generated onboard, collected from cargo operations, and delivered to port reception facilities. The system must record plastic waste volumes separately from other domestic waste, tracking both weight in kilograms and volume in cubic meters. For plastic waste retrieved from the sea during operations (such as plastic debris caught in sea chests or cooling water intakes), the system must provide a reporting mechanism aligned with IMO MEPC guidelines on reporting marine plastic litter. The system must generate annual plastic waste reports per vessel and per entity, supporting environmental reporting obligations to the Qatar Ministry of Environment, UAE Ministry of Climate Change, KSA National Center for Environmental Compliance, and India's Ministry of Earth Sciences. Reports must be bilingual English-Arabic for Gulf entity submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R014	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Food Waste Discharge Management	The system must specifically manage food waste (Category B) discharge operations with distinct rules based on vessel location and food waste treatment. Outside Special Areas, the system must permit food waste discharge when the vessel is more than 3 nautical miles from nearest land (if comminuted to less than 25mm) or more than 12 nautical miles from nearest land (if not comminuted). Within the Persian/Arabian Gulf Special Area, food waste discharge must only be permitted when the vessel is more than 12 nautical miles from nearest land and the food waste has been comminuted. The system must verify comminuter operational status before allowing food waste discharge entries in Special Areas. Food waste volumes must be estimated using the AI engine based on crew size, provisioning data, and meal planning records. For vessels during Ramadan (determined by Hijri calendar), the AI must adjust food waste predictions to account for altered meal patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R015	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Waste Management KPIs and Environmental Dashboards	The system must provide waste management key performance indicators (KPIs) and environmental dashboards at vessel, entity, and group levels. KPIs must include waste generation rate per person per day (kg), waste recycling rate as a percentage, port reception facility utilization rate, waste disposal cost per vessel per port call, and MARPOL Annex V compliance score. The dashboard must display waste volume trends over time with drill-down by waste category, port, and vessel. AI-generated benchmarking must compare each vessel's performance against fleet averages, entity averages, and industry standards. All dashboard elements must support Arabic RTL layout and bilingual English-Arabic labels. Date range selectors must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar inputs. Dashboard data must be exportable to Excel and PDF with entity-specific branding, and automatic monthly email reports must be distributable to designated environmental compliance officers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R016	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Offline Waste Record Entry and Synchronization	The system must enable offline recording of waste management operations on mobile devices when vessel connectivity is unavailable during ocean passages or at anchorage without reliable communications. The offline module must support all Garbage Record Book entry types including discharge to sea, delivery to reception facility, incineration, and accidental loss. Each offline entry must capture all mandatory fields including position, volume, waste category, disposal method, and officer signature. Data must be stored locally with encryption and synchronized to the central server upon connectivity restoration. The synchronization process must validate all offline entries against MARPOL discharge rules using the recorded GPS positions, flagging any entries that would constitute non-compliant discharges for compliance officer review. Conflict resolution for simultaneous shore-side and vessel-side updates to the same waste record must follow a configurable merge strategy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R017	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Port Reception Facility Adequacy Reporting	The system must support the reporting of inadequate port reception facilities as required under MARPOL Annex V Regulation 8 and IMO MEPC.1/Circ.834/Rev.1. When a vessel is unable to deliver waste to a port reception facility at any of the four operational ports due to facility unavailability, insufficient capacity, unreasonable cost, or undue delay, the system must generate a standardized inadequacy report containing vessel details (IMO number, flag, agent), port details (UN/LOCODE, facility operator), waste types and volumes that could not be delivered, reason for inadequacy, and the date and time of the attempted delivery. The report must be submitted electronically to the port state administration and flagged in the vessel's compliance record. The AI engine must analyze inadequacy reports across all ports to identify systemic reception facility gaps and recommend infrastructure improvement priorities. Reports must support bilingual English-Arabic formatting for Gulf port submissions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-1-004-R018	FEAT-059-1-004	MOD-059	Crew Waste Management Training Compliance	The system must track crew training compliance for waste management responsibilities under MARPOL Annex V, ensuring that all crew members receive initial and refresher training on the vessel's Garbage Management Plan, waste segregation procedures, discharge requirements, and record-keeping obligations. Training records must capture trainee name, rank, training date, training provider, topics covered, assessment results, and certificate validity period. The system must generate training due alerts 30 days before certificate expiry and prevent a vessel from being cleared as compliant if designated waste management officers have expired training certificates. For multi-national crews common on vessels in the Gulf-India trade, training materials must be available in English, Arabic, Hindi, and other common seafarer languages. Training costs must be recorded against the employing entity's budget in the appropriate local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) with USD equivalent tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R001	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Bunker Delivery Note Digital Ingestion	The system must ingest and digitize Bunker Delivery Notes (BDNs) per MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 18.6 from bunker suppliers at all operational ports including Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKHL), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), and JNPT (INNSA). Each BDN record must capture supplier name, vessel IMO number, delivery date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, fuel grade (VLSFO, ULSFO, MGO, MDO), sulphur content percentage, quantity delivered in metric tonnes, seal numbers, and delivery port UNLOCODE. The AI engine must auto-extract fields from scanned or PDF BDNs with at least 97% accuracy and flag any sulphur content reading above 0.50% m/m for immediate review. All BDN records must be retained for a minimum of three years as required by MARPOL.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R002	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Fuel Oil Non-Availability Report (FONAR) Management	The system must support creation, submission tracking, and archival of Fuel Oil Non-Availability Reports (FONARs) per MEPC.320(74) when compliant fuel is unavailable at a bunkering port. The FONAR workflow must capture the vessel IMO number, port of intended bunkering (UNLOCODE), date range of non-availability, list of contacted suppliers with quotations in local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, or USD), evidence of attempts to procure compliant fuel, and planned next bunkering port. The AI must auto-generate draft FONARs from supplier communication logs and pre-fill vessel particulars from the fleet register. Each FONAR must be submitted to the relevant flag state and IMO GISIS database, with status tracking for acknowledgment and acceptance. Multi-entity support must ensure the correct legal entity appears as the submitting party.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R003	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Fuel Sample Retention and Laboratory Analysis Tracking	The system must maintain a chain-of-custody register for MARPOL-mandated fuel samples retained onboard each vessel for at least 12 months from delivery date. Each sample record must link to the originating BDN, store the sample seal number, storage location onboard, retention expiry date, and laboratory analysis status. When samples are sent for independent verification at accredited laboratories, the system must track dispatch date, laboratory name, analysis reference number, and results including sulphur content, density, viscosity, and flash point. The AI must automatically flag any laboratory result showing sulphur content exceeding 0.50% m/m (or 0.10% m/m for ECA fuels) and initiate a non-compliance workflow. Results must be viewable in both English and Arabic interfaces with RTL layout support for Arabic users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R004	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Exhaust Gas Cleaning System (Scrubber) Monitoring	The system must integrate with onboard Exhaust Gas Cleaning System (EGCS) continuous monitoring systems to receive real-time washwater discharge parameters and exhaust emission data per MEPC.259(68) guidelines. Data points must include washwater pH at overboard discharge, PAH concentration, turbidity levels, SO2/CO2 ratio from exhaust monitoring, and scrubber operational status (open-loop, closed-loop, hybrid mode). The system must enforce scrubber discharge restrictions in ports and jurisdictions that prohibit open-loop scrubber operation, including Fujairah anchorage and specific berths at Jebel Ali. AI-driven alerts must trigger when washwater parameters approach or exceed IMO thresholds, and the system must log all scrubber mode changes with GPS coordinates and timestamps for port state control inspection readiness.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R005	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Sulphur Compliance Dashboard with Port-Specific Rules	The system must provide a real-time compliance dashboard showing each vessel's current sulphur compliance status across the fleet. The dashboard must display active fuel type in use, latest BDN sulphur reading, scrubber operational mode, and compliance risk score calculated by the AI engine. Port-specific sulphur regulations must be maintained in a configurable rules engine, including ECA limits (0.10% m/m in designated areas), EU MRV port requirements, China domestic ECA limits, and any regional restrictions at Middle East ports such as DMCA requirements at Jebel Ali. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations), vessel type, and compliance risk level. Data must refresh at minimum every 15 minutes and support both English LTR and Arabic RTL display modes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R006	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	AI-Predicted Fuel Procurement Optimization	The AI engine must analyze historical bunker prices, vessel voyage schedules, and fuel consumption patterns to recommend optimal bunkering ports and fuel quantities that minimize cost while maintaining sulphur compliance. Recommendations must factor in price differentials between VLSFO and HSFO-plus-scrubber economics, port-specific fuel availability, vessel tank capacity and segregation capability, and upcoming voyage routing through ECAs. Cost calculations must be performed in the vessel's operating currency and converted to the entity's reporting currency (QAR for Qatar entity, AED for UAE entity, SAR for KSA entity, INR for India entity, USD for consolidated reporting) using daily exchange rates. The system must generate procurement advisories at least 7 days before anticipated bunkering and integrate with the bunker procurement module for purchase order creation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R007	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Port State Control Inspection Readiness Package	The system must auto-generate a Port State Control (PSC) inspection readiness package for sulphur compliance per the IMO Sulphur Inspection Guidance (MEPC.1/Circ.864/Rev.1). The package must include the last five BDNs with corresponding sample records, current EGCS Technical Manual and SECP Scheme A/B approval certificates, fuel changeover procedures and logs for ECA transitions, Oil Record Book Part I excerpts related to fuel operations, and the International Air Pollution Prevention (IAPP) Certificate. All documents must be compiled into a single downloadable PDF bundle with bilingual headers in English and Arabic. The AI must pre-check the package for completeness gaps and alert the vessel master and designated person ashore (DPA) of any missing or expired documents at least 48 hours before port arrival at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R008	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Fuel Changeover Procedure Logging for ECA Compliance	The system must record and validate fuel changeover procedures when vessels transit into or out of Emission Control Areas where the 0.10% sulphur limit applies. Each changeover log entry must capture start and completion timestamps, initial and final fuel sulphur content, tank volumes before and after changeover, engine and boiler fuel line flushing confirmation, and GPS coordinates at changeover initiation. The AI must validate that changeover was completed before the vessel crossed the ECA boundary using AIS position data and flag any late changeovers as compliance exceptions. The changeover log must be formatted to satisfy MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 14.6 requirements and be exportable as a supplement to the Oil Record Book. Offline data entry must be supported for vessels with intermittent satellite connectivity, with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R009	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Multi-Entity Compliance Reporting by Flag State	The system must generate sulphur compliance reports segmented by vessel flag state and operating entity, as different flag state administrations may have varying reporting requirements and enforcement mechanisms. For each entity (Qatar Maritime Authority, UAE Federal Transport Authority, Saudi Transport General Authority, Indian DG Shipping), the system must produce flag-state-specific compliance summaries including total compliant vs non-compliant fuel operations, FONAR submissions and outcomes, PSC detention records related to sulphur violations, and corrective action status. Reports must support Hijri date ranges for QAR and SAR entity reports as required by local government fiscal calendars. The AI must identify cross-entity compliance trends and recommend fleet-wide corrective actions when systemic issues are detected across multiple vessels or entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R010	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Sulphur Emission Penalty and Fine Tracking	The system must record and track financial penalties, fines, and detention costs resulting from sulphur non-compliance events at any port worldwide. Each penalty record must capture the issuing authority, port of detention or inspection (UNLOCODE), vessel IMO number, violation details referencing the specific MARPOL regulation breached, penalty amount in local currency with USD equivalent, payment deadline, appeal status, and resolution outcome. The system must calculate the total financial exposure per entity in their reporting currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR/USD) and maintain a running compliance cost ledger that feeds into the ERP's general ledger module. AI must correlate penalty patterns with specific root causes such as fuel supplier quality issues, scrubber maintenance gaps, or crew procedural failures and recommend targeted preventive measures.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R011	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Crew Competency Tracking for Sulphur Compliance	The system must maintain records of crew competency and training related to sulphur compliance operations including fuel changeover procedures, scrubber operation and maintenance, fuel sampling protocols, and BDN verification. Each crew member's training record must link to their seafarer certificate number, training provider, completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, certificate validity period, and next refresher due date. The AI must flag vessels where key personnel (Chief Engineer, Second Engineer, Chief Officer) lack current sulphur compliance training and recommend training schedules aligned with vessel dry-dock or port layover periods at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or JNPT. Training completion rates must be reportable by entity and vessel, with automatic escalation alerts to the fleet superintendent when compliance training coverage falls below 95%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R012	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Automated Flag State and IMO Notification Submission	The system must automate the submission of mandatory sulphur-related notifications to flag state administrations and the IMO through their designated electronic portals. This includes FONAR submissions through the IMO GISIS Fuel Oil Non-Availability module, fuel oil consumption reporting under IMO DCS (Data Collection System) per MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 22A, and any voluntary emission reduction commitments under the Poseidon Principles or Sea Cargo Charter frameworks. The system must maintain API integrations or secure file transfer mechanisms for each flag state portal and track submission acknowledgments. Where electronic submission is not available, the system must generate pre-formatted notification documents for manual submission. All submissions must include the correct legal entity details, vessel registration information, and authorized signatory based on the multi-entity configuration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R013	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Fuel Quality Dispute and Claim Management	The system must manage fuel quality dispute workflows when laboratory analysis reveals off-specification bunker fuel with sulphur content exceeding the supplier's stated BDN value. The dispute record must capture the originating BDN reference, laboratory analysis report, variance between stated and actual sulphur content, financial claim amount in the bunker purchase currency (typically USD) with entity currency equivalent, supplier response timeline, and arbitration status. The AI must auto-generate claim letters referencing ISO 8217 fuel specifications, MARPOL Annex VI compliance obligations, and applicable supply contract terms. Claim amounts must include direct fuel replacement costs, off-hire time if the vessel was delayed, any fines incurred, and scrubber chemical consumption if emergency exhaust gas cleaning was required. Integration with the accounts receivable module must track claim recovery amounts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R014	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	ECA Zone Geofence and Automatic Compliance Triggering	The system must maintain geofenced boundaries for all designated Emission Control Areas (North American ECA, US Caribbean ECA, North Sea ECA, Baltic Sea ECA) and any regional low-sulphur zones such as the China Domestic ECA. When a vessel's AIS-reported position enters a geofenced ECA approach zone (configurable, default 12 nautical miles from ECA boundary), the system must automatically trigger a fuel changeover reminder to the vessel's bridge and engine room, notify the shore-based operations team, and begin enhanced monitoring of fuel type in use. The AI must calculate the estimated time to ECA boundary entry based on current speed and heading, and validate whether sufficient compliant fuel is onboard for the planned ECA transit duration. All geofence trigger events must be logged with timestamp and GPS coordinates for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R015	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Historical Compliance Trend Analysis and Benchmarking	The system must provide AI-driven trend analysis of sulphur compliance performance across the fleet over configurable time periods (monthly, quarterly, annual, Hijri fiscal year). Key performance indicators must include average fuel sulphur content by vessel and entity, FONAR frequency and root causes, PSC inspection outcomes related to sulphur compliance, fuel quality dispute frequency and recovery rates, and total compliance cost per vessel per voyage. The AI must benchmark fleet performance against industry averages where data is available from IMO or class society publications, and identify statistically significant deviations that warrant management attention. Trend reports must be exportable in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual English/Arabic headers and support multi-currency aggregation with entity-level and consolidated group-level views in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-001-R016	FEAT-059-2-001	MOD-059	Offline Sulphur Compliance Data Capture on Vessels	The system must support offline data capture for sulphur compliance records on vessels operating in areas with limited or no satellite connectivity, particularly during Indian Ocean crossings and in-port periods where shore WiFi may be restricted. Offline capability must cover BDN data entry, fuel changeover logging, scrubber parameter recording, fuel sample chain-of-custody updates, and daily fuel consumption entries. All offline records must be timestamped with the vessel's local time and GPS position at entry, queued for synchronization, and automatically uploaded to the central ERP when connectivity is restored. Conflict resolution logic must handle cases where shore-side personnel have updated the same vessel record during the offline period, with vessel-side entries taking precedence for operational data and shore-side entries taking precedence for administrative corrections. The offline module must function for up to 30 consecutive days without connectivity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R001	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Annual CII Attained Value Calculation Engine	The system must calculate the attained Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) for each vessel in accordance with IMO guidelines MEPC.352(78) using the formula: AER = (total annual CO2 emissions in grams) / (deadweight tonnage × total distance sailed in nautical miles). CO2 emissions must be derived from actual fuel consumption data across all fuel types (HFO, VLSFO, MGO, LNG) using the IMO-prescribed carbon conversion factors (CF values per MEPC.364(79)). The calculation must handle voyage exclusions per MEPC.355(78) such as time spent in dry dock, force majeure events, and ice class operation. The system must support calculation for all vessel types in the fleet (bulk carriers, container ships, tankers, general cargo) using the applicable DWT or GT-based formulas. Calculations must be performed in real-time as fuel consumption data is ingested and finalized at calendar year-end.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R002	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	CII Reference Line and Reduction Factor Configuration	The system must maintain configurable CII reference lines and annual reduction factors as defined in MEPC.353(78) and updated through subsequent MEPC resolutions. For each vessel type and size category, the system must store the reference line parameters (a, c coefficients) and the annual reduction factor (Z%) which increases progressively from 2023 through 2030 and beyond. The required CII value must be automatically calculated as: Required CII = Reference CII × (1 - Z%/100). The system must allow administrators to update these parameters when IMO issues revised reduction factors without requiring code changes. The AI must project future required CII values for each vessel through 2030 and alert fleet managers when vessels are trending toward non-compliance in future years, enabling proactive chartering and fleet renewal decisions across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R003	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	CII Rating Band Assignment (A through E)	The system must assign each vessel a CII rating from A (major superior) to E (inferior) based on the ratio of attained CII to required CII, using the boundary values (d-vectors) specified in MEPC.354(78) for each ship type and size. Rating band boundaries must be configurable and updated when IMO revises the d-vectors. The rating assignment must be performed at calendar year-end for the official annual rating and continuously throughout the year as a projected year-end rating based on year-to-date performance. The AI must calculate the probability of each vessel achieving each rating band (A through E) at year-end based on remaining voyage schedule and historical consumption patterns. Vessels projected to receive a D or E rating must trigger automatic corrective action workflows. The rating must be displayed prominently on vessel dashboards in both English and Arabic with color-coded indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R004	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan (SEEMP Part III) Generation	The system must auto-generate the required SEEMP Part III document per MEPC.346(78) for each vessel, incorporating the vessel-specific CII target, implementation plan for achieving the target, and self-evaluation and improvement mechanisms. The SEEMP Part III must include vessel particulars (IMO number, flag state, gross tonnage, DWT), the required and target CII values, planned operational and technical measures (speed optimization, hull cleaning schedule, propeller polishing, route optimization), timeline for implementation, and responsible persons. The AI must draft the narrative sections based on the vessel's historical performance data, planned voyages between Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT, and available technical improvement options. The document must be exportable in PDF format for submission to the vessel's flag state administration and classification society. Both English and Arabic versions must be generatable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R005	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Voyage-Level CII Contribution Analysis	The system must decompose the annual CII attained value into voyage-level contributions, showing how each individual voyage between ports (e.g., Jebel Ali to JNPT, Hamad Port to Khalifa Port) contributed to the overall annual carbon intensity. Each voyage contribution must display departure and arrival ports (UNLOCODE), cargo carried in TEU or metric tonnes, distance sailed in nautical miles, fuel consumed by type, CO2 emitted, and the marginal CII impact. The AI must identify the top 10 worst-performing voyages by CII contribution and recommend specific improvements such as speed reduction, weather routing adjustments, or cargo consolidation that could have reduced the carbon intensity for those voyages. This analysis must support drill-down by entity, trade lane, and vessel class to identify systemic operational patterns affecting CII performance across the fleet.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R006	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	AI-Driven CII Improvement Simulation Tool	The system must provide an AI-powered simulation engine that allows fleet managers to model the CII impact of various operational and technical improvement measures before implementation. Simulation scenarios must include speed reduction (slow steaming) with configurable speed profiles per trade lane, hull and propeller cleaning with estimated fouling penalty reduction percentages, route optimization with alternative waypoint selection, cargo optimization to maximize DWT utilization, shore power usage at equipped ports, and alternative fuel adoption (LNG, biofuels, methanol). Each simulation must calculate the projected CII improvement in AER units and rating band change, the associated cost impact in the entity's operating currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and USD equivalent, and the estimated revenue impact from schedule changes. The AI must recommend the optimal combination of measures to achieve a target rating at minimum cost.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R007	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Corrective Action Plan for D and E Rated Vessels	The system must enforce the creation and tracking of a mandatory Corrective Action Plan (CAP) for any vessel that receives a D rating for one year or an E rating in any year, per MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 28.6. The CAP must include root cause analysis of the poor CII performance, specific measurable corrective actions with responsible owners and target dates, interim milestone checkpoints, and a target CII value for the next reporting period. The AI must auto-generate a draft CAP based on the vessel's voyage-level CII analysis and available improvement measures, prioritized by cost-effectiveness. The CAP workflow must route through the fleet superintendent, technical director, and entity general manager for approval. Progress against each corrective action must be tracked monthly with automated status updates. Overdue corrective actions must escalate to the DPA and company CEO. The CAP document must be submitted to the flag state as part of the SEEMP Part III update.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R008	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Real-Time CII Monitoring and Speed Advisory	The system must continuously monitor each vessel's year-to-date CII trajectory and provide real-time speed advisories to optimize the year-end CII rating. The monitoring engine must ingest noon report data (daily fuel consumption, distance, cargo onboard) and AIS position data to calculate the current cumulative AER. Based on the remaining scheduled voyages for the calendar year, the AI must calculate the maximum permissible average speed for each upcoming voyage to achieve the target CII rating. Speed advisories must be delivered to the vessel's bridge via the ERP's vessel application and to the chartering desk shore-side. The advisory must include the recommended speed in knots, projected fuel savings in metric tonnes and cost savings in USD, and the CII rating impact. Advisories must update when voyage schedules change, new cargo is booked, or vessels deviate from planned routes between Gulf and Indian subcontinent ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R009	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	IMO DCS Annual Data Submission Workflow	The system must automate the preparation and submission of annual fuel consumption data to the IMO Data Collection System per MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 22A. The submission must include total fuel consumption by fuel type, total distance traveled, total hours underway, and transport work for each vessel. Data must be aggregated from verified noon reports, BDN records, and tank sounding logs with reconciliation checks to ensure consistency. The AI must identify and flag data quality issues such as missing noon reports, fuel consumption anomalies exceeding two standard deviations from vessel baselines, and distance discrepancies between AIS track and reported distances. The submission package must be routed to the recognized organization (classification society) for verification before flag state submission. The system must track verification status, queries from the RO, and final Statement of Compliance issuance date for each vessel. Multi-entity support must ensure each vessel's submission is processed through the correct legal entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R010	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	EU MRV and FuelEU Maritime CII Integration	The system must maintain parallel CII tracking for vessels calling at EU/EEA ports under the EU MRV Regulation (2015/757) and the FuelEU Maritime Regulation, in addition to the IMO CII framework. The EU MRV module must calculate CO2 emissions per transport work on a per-voyage basis for voyages to, from, and between EU ports, using EU-specific monitoring methodology. The FuelEU Maritime module must track the greenhouse gas intensity of energy used onboard against the reference value, including well-to-wake emissions. The system must identify vessels in the fleet that call at EU ports during Middle East to Mediterranean trade rotations and automatically apply dual reporting obligations. Compliance balances, surplus units, and deficit penalties under FuelEU Maritime must be tracked in EUR with USD equivalent. The AI must optimize voyage planning to balance IMO CII and EU FuelEU obligations when they create conflicting optimization incentives.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R011	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Weather and Current Impact on CII Performance	The system must account for weather and ocean current impacts on CII performance by integrating meteorological and hydrographic data feeds for the Arabian Sea, Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, and Indian Ocean trade routes. The AI must correlate adverse weather events (monsoon seasons affecting JNPT approaches, shamal winds in the Persian Gulf affecting Hamad Port and Jebel Ali operations) with increased fuel consumption and higher CII attained values. Voyage correction factors per MEPC.355(78) for adverse conditions must be applied where applicable. The system must build vessel-specific weather performance models that predict fuel consumption adjustments for seasonal conditions on each trade lane. During CII improvement planning, the AI must factor in seasonal weather patterns to set realistic monthly CII targets rather than uniform linear targets, acknowledging that monsoon-period voyages between the Gulf and India will inherently have higher carbon intensity than fair-weather periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R012	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Fleet CII Portfolio Dashboard and Benchmarking	The system must display a fleet-wide CII portfolio dashboard showing the distribution of vessel ratings across the A-E bands for each operating entity and the consolidated group. The dashboard must include year-over-year trend charts, projected year-end rating distribution, and a compliance risk heatmap. Benchmarking capabilities must compare the fleet's CII performance against published industry averages from IMO annual reports and class society fleet statistics. The AI must identify outlier vessels performing significantly better or worse than fleet averages and recommend knowledge transfer or investigation actions. The dashboard must support filtering by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), vessel type, vessel age, trade lane, and charter type (owned, time-chartered-in, voyage-chartered). All visualizations must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts, with Hijri calendar date selectors available for QAR and SAR entity users.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R013	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Charter Party CII Clause Management	The system must manage CII-related charter party clauses for time-chartered-in and time-chartered-out vessels, tracking the allocation of CII responsibility between owner and charterer. For each charter party, the system must record the agreed CII target rating, speed and consumption warranties relevant to CII, the contractual mechanism for sharing CII improvement costs and benefits, and any CII-contingent hire adjustment formulas. The AI must monitor actual vessel performance against charter party CII warranties and calculate any hire adjustment amounts in the charter currency (typically USD) with entity currency conversion. When a chartered vessel's CII performance deviates from the warranted level, the system must generate claim documentation referencing the specific charter party clause, vessel performance data, and calculated financial impact. Integration with the chartering and demurrage modules must ensure CII-related financial adjustments flow through to the correct entity's accounts receivable or payable ledgers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R014	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	Technical Upgrade CII Impact Assessment	The system must maintain a registry of available technical upgrades and their estimated CII improvement potential for each vessel in the fleet. Technical upgrades must include but not be limited to energy-saving devices (Mewis duct, propeller boss cap fins, hull air lubrication), engine power limitation (EPL/SHaPoLi), waste heat recovery systems, alternative fuel conversion (dual-fuel LNG, methanol-ready), wind-assisted propulsion, and advanced hull coatings. Each upgrade record must include the estimated CII reduction percentage based on manufacturer specifications and fleet experience, capital expenditure in USD and entity currency equivalent, installation timeline and required dry-dock period, classification society approval status, and payback period calculation factoring in fuel savings and potential CII-linked commercial benefits. The AI must rank upgrades by cost-effectiveness for each vessel and generate investment business cases for fleet management approval, considering the vessel's remaining economic life and charter commitments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R015	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	CII Data Quality Assurance and Audit Trail	The system must implement comprehensive data quality assurance checks on all input data feeding the CII calculation to ensure accuracy and auditability. Validation rules must include cross-referencing fuel consumption from noon reports against BDN quantities and tank soundings, validating distance sailed against AIS track data with a configurable tolerance (default 5%), checking for missing or duplicate noon reports, verifying cargo quantities against bills of lading and port loading/discharge records, and ensuring all voyage exclusion claims (dry dock, force majeure) are supported by documentary evidence. The AI must assign a data quality confidence score (0-100%) to each vessel's annual CII calculation and highlight low-confidence data points requiring manual verification. A complete audit trail must record all data inputs, corrections, exclusions, and calculation versions with user identification, timestamp, and change justification. The audit trail must satisfy classification society verification requirements and be exportable for flag state inspection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R016	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	CII Notification and Regulatory Deadline Management	The system must manage all regulatory deadlines associated with CII compliance including SEEMP Part III submission dates, annual DCS data submission windows, classification society verification appointments, and flag state Statement of Compliance renewal dates. A centralized regulatory calendar must display upcoming deadlines for all vessels across all four entities with Hijri date display for Middle Eastern regulatory deadlines. The AI must generate automated reminders at configurable intervals (default: 90, 60, 30, and 14 days before deadline) routed to the responsible vessel manager, technical superintendent, and entity compliance officer. Overdue items must escalate to the fleet director and entity general manager. The system must track the actual submission and receipt dates for all regulatory filings and maintain a compliance scorecard showing on-time vs late submissions by entity. The offline vessel module must cache upcoming deadline alerts for display even when connectivity is unavailable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-002-R017	FEAT-059-2-002	MOD-059	CII Impact on Vessel Valuation and Commercial Strategy	The system must integrate CII ratings into vessel commercial assessment workflows, reflecting the growing market impact of carbon intensity performance on vessel earnings and asset values. The AI must analyze the correlation between CII rating and achieved time charter rates from market data, project the commercial impact of CII rating changes on each vessel's earning capacity, and estimate the asset value premium or discount associated with the vessel's CII trajectory through 2030. This analysis must feed into fleet renewal planning, vessel sale and purchase decisions, and charter rate negotiations. Commercial impact assessments must be reported in USD for group-level analysis and in entity-specific currencies (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) for local management reporting. The system must generate scenario analyses showing the net present value impact of CII improvement investments versus early vessel disposal for aging vessels with deteriorating CII performance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R001	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Sea Cargo Charter Signatory Profile Management	The system must maintain signatory profiles for each legal entity that is a signatory to the Sea Cargo Charter, storing the entity name, signatory date, designated reporting contact, classification of signatory role (charterer, shipowner, or both), and the applicable reporting year. Each entity (Qatar operations, UAE operations, KSA operations, India operations) may have different signatory statuses and reporting obligations. The system must track the SCC version applicable to each reporting year (initial 2020 framework vs subsequent amendments) and maintain the corresponding methodology parameters. The AI must alert compliance officers 120 days before the annual disclosure deadline and initiate the data collection workflow. Entity profiles must display correctly in both English and Arabic interfaces, with the Arabic entity names using the official commercial registration names in Arabic script.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R002	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Voyage-Level Carbon Intensity Calculation per SCC Methodology	The system must calculate voyage-level carbon intensity for all charter voyages using the Sea Cargo Charter methodology, which requires computing the Annual Efficiency Ratio (AER) and the Energy Efficiency Operational Indicator (EEOI) for each voyage. The AER calculation must use actual fuel consumption converted to CO2 using IMO emission factors, divided by deadweight tonnage multiplied by distance. The EEOI calculation must use actual cargo carried (in tonnes or TEU-equivalent) instead of DWT capacity. Both metrics must be calculated for each voyage leg between loading and discharge ports across the Gulf-India trade lanes (Hamad Port to JNPT, Jebel Ali to Mundra, Khalifa Port to Nhava Sheva). The system must handle ballast voyages, multi-port cargo operations, and transshipment legs per SCC guidance on voyage boundary definitions. Results must be stored at voyage granularity with full traceability to source fuel consumption and distance data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R003	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Climate Alignment Trajectory Comparison	The system must compare each vessel's and the overall fleet's carbon intensity performance against the Sea Cargo Charter climate alignment trajectories, which are based on the IMO's greenhouse gas reduction strategy. The system must store and apply the SCC-prescribed decarbonization trajectories for each vessel type (bulk carriers, container ships, tankers, general cargo) including the trajectory reference values and annual reduction rates. For each charter voyage and annually, the system must calculate the percentage deviation from the applicable trajectory (positive for above-trajectory, negative for below-trajectory). The AI must classify each vessel and the portfolio as aligned, partially aligned, or misaligned with the trajectory and project forward alignment status through 2030 and 2050. Trajectory data must be updatable when SCC revises its methodology to reflect the 2023 IMO GHG Strategy revision targeting net-zero by or around 2050.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R004	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Charter Portfolio Emissions Aggregation by Trade Lane	The system must aggregate charter portfolio emissions data by trade lane, cargo type, and vessel category for SCC disclosure reporting. Trade lane aggregation must cover the primary routes operated by the four entities including Middle East Gulf to Indian Subcontinent, Middle East Gulf to Far East, Middle East Gulf to Mediterranean, Middle East Gulf to East Africa, and intra-Gulf (Hamad Port to Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port to Dammam). For each trade lane, the system must calculate total CO2 emissions, transport work (tonne-miles), average AER, average EEOI, and trajectory alignment percentage. Cargo type breakdown must include containerized cargo in TEU, dry bulk in metric tonnes, liquid bulk in metric tonnes, and breakbulk/project cargo. The AI must identify the highest-emitting trade lanes and recommend portfolio rebalancing strategies to improve overall climate alignment while maintaining commercial viability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R005	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Annual SCC Disclosure Report Generation	The system must auto-generate the annual Sea Cargo Charter disclosure report in the prescribed format, including all required data tables, charts, and narrative sections. The report must contain the signatory entity information, reporting period and methodology version, total chartered voyages and transport work, aggregate and vessel-type-specific AER and EEOI values, trajectory alignment analysis with visualization of actual vs required carbon intensity curves, discussion of significant operational or technical measures taken during the reporting year, and forward-looking improvement commitments. The AI must draft the narrative sections based on the quantitative analysis, highlighting positive trends, explaining deviations, and articulating credible improvement plans referencing specific fleet initiatives at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT operations. The report must be generated in English for international publication and optionally in Arabic for regional stakeholder distribution. Output formats must include PDF for publication and Excel for data verification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R006	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Third-Party Charter Data Collection and Validation	The system must collect and validate carbon intensity data from third-party vessel operators for voyages chartered by the reporting entity but operated by external shipowners. Data collection workflows must support electronic data exchange via API for integrated partners and manual upload via standardized Excel templates for non-integrated operators. Required data fields must include vessel IMO number, voyage dates, fuel consumption by type and quantity, distance sailed, cargo quantity and type, and applicable BDN references. The AI must validate submitted data against expected ranges based on vessel type and size, flag implausible values (e.g., fuel consumption more than 30% above or below expected for the vessel type and distance), and request corrections from the data provider. Where third-party data is unavailable or unreliable, the system must apply SCC-prescribed default values or proxy calculation methods and clearly mark these data points in the disclosure report as estimated rather than actual.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R007	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Data Scope Filtering and Voyage Eligibility Rules	The system must apply the correct scope and eligibility rules to determine which voyages and vessels fall within the Sea Cargo Charter reporting boundary for each entity. Scope rules must cover the signatory's role-specific reporting obligations: charterers report on voyages they charter regardless of vessel ownership, while shipowners report on all vessel operations regardless of charter status. The system must handle complex multi-party charter chains (head charter, sub-charter, voyage charter) and correctly attribute emissions to the appropriate reporting entity. Voyage eligibility filters must exclude non-cargo voyages (positioning ballast with no forward cargo commitment), laid-up periods, dry dock transits, and other non-commercial operations per SCC guidance. The AI must automatically classify each voyage's eligibility based on voyage fixture records from the chartering module and highlight borderline cases for manual compliance officer review. Voyage scope tagging must persist across reporting periods for consistent year-over-year comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R008	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Poseidon Principles Alignment and Cross-Framework Reporting	The system must support cross-framework reporting between the Sea Cargo Charter and the Poseidon Principles for Marine Insurance and Poseidon Principles for Ship Finance, as many stakeholders participate in multiple climate alignment frameworks. The system must map the SCC methodology outputs (AER, EEOI, trajectory alignment) to the corresponding Poseidon Principles metrics and produce consolidated reporting packages. Where methodology differences exist between frameworks (e.g., trajectory reference lines, voyage scope definitions), the system must calculate and store separate metric sets and clearly annotate the reconciliation differences. The AI must generate a unified climate alignment dashboard showing the entity's portfolio performance across all applicable frameworks, enabling consistent messaging to charterers, lenders, and insurers. Multi-currency financial exposure to carbon pricing scenarios (USD, QAR, AED, SAR, INR) must be integrated to quantify the commercial risk of climate misalignment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R009	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Disclosure Peer Benchmarking and Market Intelligence	The system must enable benchmarking of the entity's SCC disclosure results against published disclosures from peer shipping companies and industry aggregate data. When SCC publishes its annual aggregate results, the system must ingest the aggregate statistics and enable comparison of the entity's AER and EEOI values, trajectory alignment percentages, and fleet composition against industry averages. The AI must analyze publicly available SCC disclosures from competitor shipping lines and charterers to identify competitive positioning. Benchmarking visualizations must show the entity's position within the peer group distribution (quartile ranking) for each metric and vessel type. The system must track year-over-year changes in peer positioning and alert management when the entity's relative performance deteriorates. All benchmarking data must be accessible in both English and Arabic interfaces with supporting charts rendering correctly in RTL layout.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R010	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Carbon Offset and Inset Integration for SCC Reporting	The system must track carbon offset and carbon inset (within-value-chain reduction) purchases applied to the SCC reporting scope, where entities choose to supplement operational emission reductions with market-based mechanisms. Each offset record must capture the offset standard (Gold Standard, Verra VCS, etc.), project reference, vintage year, quantity in tonnes CO2e, purchase price in transaction currency with USD equivalent, retirement registry confirmation, and the specific voyages or annual scope to which the offset is allocated. The AI must validate that offsets meet SCC-recognized quality criteria and flag any offsets from standards not accepted by the SCC framework. The system must clearly distinguish between operational emission reductions and offset-adjusted figures in the disclosure report, as SCC methodology requires transparency on the use of offsets. Financial tracking of offset expenditure must flow to the correct entity's cost center with multi-currency support (QAR/AED/SAR/INR/USD).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R011	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Stakeholder Communication and Disclosure Distribution	The system must manage the distribution of the finalized SCC annual disclosure report to all relevant stakeholders including the SCC Secretariat for official submission, charterer counterparties with climate reporting requirements, lenders and insurers under Poseidon Principles obligations, investors and ESG rating agencies, and internal board and management committees. Distribution workflows must track which version was sent to which stakeholder, delivery confirmation, and any stakeholder queries or feedback received. The AI must generate tailored executive summaries for different stakeholder audiences: a technical summary for the SCC Secretariat, a commercial impact summary for charterers and trading partners, and a governance summary for board reporting. All communications must be available in English and Arabic, with the Arabic version suitable for submission to Gulf Cooperation Council regulatory authorities. The system must maintain a complete audit trail of all disclosure versions and distributions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R012	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Methodology Version Control and Historical Comparison	The system must maintain version-controlled copies of the SCC methodology and reference trajectories for each reporting year, enabling accurate historical comparison even when the methodology is updated. When the SCC issues revised guidance, the system must store the new methodology parameters alongside the previous version and clearly indicate which methodology version applies to each reporting year. Historical data must be recalculable under updated methodologies for indicative comparison purposes, with the AI generating a reconciliation showing the impact of methodology changes on previously reported figures. The system must display trajectory charts with clearly marked methodology transition points. For multi-entity reporting, the system must handle cases where different entities may have adopted the SCC framework in different years and therefore have different reporting history lengths. All methodology documentation must be stored with bilingual annotations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R006	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Mitigation Action Plans	The system must support creation and tracking of risk mitigation action plans linked to specific risk register entries. Each action plan must include a unique plan ID, description in Arabic and English, assigned responsible person, target completion date in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, estimated cost in local currency with USD equivalent, current status (not started, in progress, delayed, completed, cancelled), and effectiveness rating post-implementation. The system must track multiple mitigation actions per risk with dependency mapping between actions. AI must recommend mitigation strategies based on historical effectiveness data from similar risks across all four entities, suggesting the most cost-effective approaches first.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R013	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Charterer Preference Scoring for Low-Carbon Vessels	The system must generate a carbon performance score for each vessel that can be shared with charterers during fixture negotiations, showing the vessel's SCC trajectory alignment and projected performance for the proposed voyage. The scoring algorithm must consider the vessel's historical AER and EEOI performance, CII rating, available technical efficiency features (EEDI/EEXI compliance margin), fuel flexibility, and planned improvement measures. The AI must calculate the expected carbon intensity for a specific proposed voyage based on the vessel's performance model, the route distance and expected weather conditions, and the cargo type and quantity. Charterers increasingly request this information as part of their own SCC reporting obligations and commercial decarbonization targets. The score must be presentable in a standardized one-page vessel carbon profile document, exportable as PDF in English with Arabic translation option, and include the methodology disclosure required by SCC for transparent data sharing between counterparties.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R014	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Disclosure Approval and Governance Workflow	The system must implement a multi-level approval workflow for the annual SCC disclosure report before publication. The workflow must include data verification by the fleet performance analyst, technical review by the sustainability manager, financial sign-off by the entity CFO for any cost or investment figures disclosed, legal review for regulatory compliance and liability implications, and final approval by the entity CEO or designated board committee. Each approval stage must capture the approver identity, timestamp, comments, and approval or rejection decision. Rejected reports must cycle back to the appropriate stage with specific revision instructions. The AI must track the approval timeline against the SCC submission deadline and escalate when the approval process is at risk of causing a late filing. Multi-entity governance must allow each entity to complete its own approval cycle while the group sustainability officer reviews and approves the consolidated group disclosure. Approval workflows must support digital signatures compliant with Qatar, UAE, KSA, and Indian electronic transaction laws.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R015	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	Voyage Emission Attribution for Shared Charter Operations	The system must accurately attribute CO2 emissions for shared charter operations where multiple charterers share vessel capacity on a single voyage, which is common in container shipping liner operations and slot charter arrangements. The emission attribution methodology must follow SCC guidance on allocation approaches including distance-based allocation, cargo-weight-based allocation, and TEU-capacity-based allocation for container vessels. For each shared voyage, the system must calculate the total voyage emissions and then allocate to each charterer based on the agreed or default allocation method. The AI must identify the most appropriate allocation method based on the charter party type and cargo characteristics, and flag cases where different allocation methods produce materially different results (variance exceeding 10%). Allocated emissions must be traceable from the vessel's total fuel consumption through to each charterer's SCC reporting scope. The allocation logic must correctly handle complex multi-leg voyages calling at Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port, and JNPT with cargo loaded and discharged at multiple ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-003-R016	FEAT-059-2-003	MOD-059	SCC Future Scenario Modeling and Decarbonization Pathway	The system must provide scenario modeling tools that project the entity's SCC portfolio alignment under different decarbonization strategies through 2030 and 2050. Scenarios must include fleet renewal with newbuild energy-efficient vessels, retrofitting existing vessels with emission reduction technologies, transitioning to alternative fuels (LNG, methanol, ammonia, hydrogen), implementing operational efficiency measures (speed optimization, weather routing, just-in-time arrivals), and adjusting the charter portfolio toward lower-carbon vessels. The AI must model each scenario's impact on the portfolio's trajectory alignment, total annual CO2 emissions, transport work efficiency, and associated capital and operational expenditure in multi-currency (USD for consolidated, entity currencies for local planning). Monte Carlo simulation must account for uncertainty in fuel prices, technology maturity, regulatory stringency, and carbon pricing. Results must be presented in interactive dashboards with scenario comparison views and sensitivity analysis, supporting strategic investment decisions by the board across all four operating entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R001	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Environmental Incident Initial Report Creation	The system must enable rapid creation of an initial environmental incident report within 15 minutes of incident detection, capturing essential information required for immediate regulatory notification. The report must include incident timestamp in UTC and vessel local time (with Hijri date equivalent), vessel identification (IMO number, name, flag state, operating entity), GPS coordinates at time of incident, incident category (oil spill, chemical release, cargo loss, air emission breach, ballast water violation, garbage discharge, other), estimated quantity of pollutant released with unit of measure, weather and sea conditions at incident time, immediate containment actions taken, and initial cause assessment. The AI must auto-populate vessel and voyage details from the ERP database and suggest the incident category based on the reported description using natural language processing. The initial report form must be accessible in both English and Arabic, with the Arabic interface fully supporting RTL data entry for narrative descriptions. Offline incident reporting must be supported for vessels at sea with queued synchronization.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R002	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Regulatory Notification Matrix and Automated Dispatch	The system must maintain a configurable regulatory notification matrix that determines which authorities must be notified for each type of environmental incident based on the incident location, vessel flag state, and coastal state jurisdiction. For incidents occurring in Qatari waters, notifications must route to the Qatar Ministry of Transport and Communications and Qatar Environment Agency. For UAE waters, notifications must route to the UAE Federal Transport Authority and relevant emirate environmental agency (e.g., Dubai Municipality for Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi Environment Agency for Khalifa Port). For Indian waters, notifications must route to the Indian DG Shipping and the relevant port's Pollution Control Board. The system must also notify the vessel's flag state administration, classification society, and P&I Club per their respective notification requirements. The AI must auto-generate notification drafts in the required format for each authority and track dispatch timestamps, delivery confirmations, and response acknowledgments. Notification deadlines (typically 24 hours for initial report) must be enforced with escalation alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R003	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Oil Spill Quantity Estimation and Trajectory Modeling	The system must provide tools for estimating oil spill quantities using standard methods including visual appearance estimation tables (Bonn Agreement oil appearance codes), tank ullage differential calculation, flow rate and duration estimation for ongoing releases, and aerial or drone survey area-based estimation. The AI must integrate with weather and current data to project the oil slick trajectory for the next 24, 48, and 72 hours using simplified Lagrangian particle tracking, considering wind-driven surface drift, tidal currents in confined waters (particularly relevant for the shallow Persian Gulf), and oil weathering processes (evaporation, emulsification). For spills near Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, or Khalifa Port, the trajectory model must account for the specific coastal geography and environmentally sensitive areas (coral reefs, mangroves, desalination plant intakes). Quantity estimates and trajectory projections must update as new observation data is entered and be shareable with response coordinators and port authorities in map-based visual format.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R004	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Incident Investigation Workflow and Root Cause Analysis	The system must implement a structured incident investigation workflow based on the IMO Casualty Investigation Code (Resolution MSC.255(84)) and ISM Code Section 9 requirements. The investigation workflow must include assignment of a lead investigator and investigation team, evidence collection and preservation checklist (photographs, samples, log book entries, VDR data, crew statements), timeline reconstruction of events leading to the incident, root cause analysis using approved methodologies (Five Whys, Fishbone Diagram, TapRooT, or equivalent), identification of immediate causes, contributing factors, and systemic root causes, and formulation of corrective and preventive actions (CAPA). The AI must assist the investigation by analyzing historical incident data to identify similar past events, correlating maintenance records with equipment-related failures, and suggesting probable root causes based on the incident circumstances. Investigation reports must be generated in bilingual English/Arabic format and stored with full audit trail for flag state, P&I Club, and classification society review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R005	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	MARPOL Annex I Oil Discharge Monitoring and Recording	The system must monitor and record oil discharge operations in compliance with MARPOL Annex I Regulations 15 and 34, integrating with onboard Oil Discharge Monitoring Equipment (ODME) where available. The system must track all oil discharge events including oily water separator discharges (with oil content in ppm), slop tank decanting operations, clean ballast discharges from oil cargo tanks, and any accidental or operational oil releases. Each discharge record must capture the date, time (UTC and local with Hijri equivalent), GPS coordinates, discharge rate, total quantity, oil content reading, and the name of the responsible officer. The AI must validate that discharges comply with MARPOL Annex I requirements: oil content below 15 ppm for machinery space bilge water, discharge rate within ship-approved limits, not in special areas or within 50 nautical miles of land, and that the vessel is proceeding en route. Non-compliant discharge events must immediately trigger the environmental incident reporting workflow and regulatory notification process.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R006	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Ballast Water Management Incident Tracking	The system must track ballast water management incidents including failure to comply with the BWM Convention (D-1 or D-2 standards), ballast water treatment system malfunctions, unauthorized ballast water discharge in port or prohibited areas, and ballast water sampling failures during port state control inspections. For each incident, the system must record the vessel's ballast water management plan status, treatment system type and approval certificate reference, ballast water exchange or treatment log data, and the specific violation or equipment failure details. The AI must cross-reference the vessel's ballast water record book (electronic equivalent) with port state requirements at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT to proactively identify compliance gaps before port arrival. The system must track the status of ballast water sampling and analysis at Indian ports where JNPT requires mandatory ballast water testing, and record the results with laboratory reference numbers. Corrective actions for treatment system failures must integrate with the planned maintenance system module.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R007	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Environmental Incident Cost Tracking and P&I Claims	The system must track all financial costs associated with environmental incidents including cleanup costs, containment equipment hire, third-party contractor charges, port authority fines and penalties, class society survey fees, legal defense costs, compensation claims from affected parties, and vessel off-hire time. Each cost item must be recorded in the transaction currency with automatic conversion to the entity's reporting currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and USD equivalent using the exchange rate applicable on the transaction date. The system must manage P&I Club claims by recording the Club name, claim reference number, date of notification, initial reserve amount, interim payments, and final settlement. The AI must estimate total incident cost exposure based on the incident type and severity using historical claims data and flag incidents where the estimated cost exceeds the entity's insurance deductible or P&I Club retention. Integration with the ERP's accounts payable module must enable tracking of cleanup vendor invoices and payment status. Cost reports must be segmented by entity and aggregable at group level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R008	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) Management	The system must manage corrective and preventive actions arising from environmental incident investigations through their complete lifecycle from identification to verification of effectiveness. Each CAPA must have a unique reference number linked to the originating incident, a clear description of the action required, classification as corrective (addressing the immediate cause) or preventive (addressing the root cause to prevent recurrence), assigned responsible person with their organizational role and entity, target completion date with progress milestones, actual completion date, and verification of effectiveness assessment. The AI must recommend CAPAs based on the root cause analysis findings and cross-reference with industry best practices from IMO circulars, P&I Club loss prevention bulletins, and classification society guidance. CAPA progress must be tracked on a dashboard with RAG (Red-Amber-Green) status indicators, overdue action escalation to entity management, and fleet-wide CAPA statistics. Where a CAPA applies across multiple vessels or entities, the system must support fleet-wide CAPA deployment with vessel-specific implementation tracking.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R009	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Garbage Record Book Part I and II Digital Management	The system must digitize the Garbage Record Book Parts I (for all ships) and II (for ships carrying solid bulk cargoes) per MARPOL Annex V Regulation 10.3. Digital entries must capture each garbage discharge, incineration, or delivery to reception facility event with the date and time (Gregorian and Hijri), vessel position (GPS coordinates or port name and UNLOCODE), garbage category per the MARPOL Annex V classification (plastics, food waste, domestic waste, cooking oil, operational waste, cargo residues, animal carcasses, e-waste, fishing gear), estimated quantity in cubic meters, and method of disposal. For discharges at sea, the system must validate compliance with MARPOL Annex V discharge requirements including minimum distance from shore, special area restrictions (the Persian Gulf is a MARPOL Special Area under Annex V), and cargo residue classification. The AI must flag non-compliant entries and alert the vessel's environmental officer. Garbage management plans must be linked, and the system must calculate per-voyage and per-capita waste generation metrics for benchmarking against fleet reduction targets.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R010	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Air Emission Incident Detection and NOx/SOx Breach Alerts	The system must detect and record air emission incidents involving breaches of MARPOL Annex VI limits for sulphur oxides (SOx), nitrogen oxides (NOx), and volatile organic compounds (VOCs). SOx breach detection must integrate with the 0.5% Sulphur Fuel Cap Compliance feature (FEAT-059-2-001) for fuel-related violations and with continuous emission monitoring systems where installed. NOx breach records must capture the engine identification (manufacturer, model, rated power, Tier classification), NOx emission reading versus the applicable Tier limit (Tier I, II, or III depending on ship construction date and operating area), and the technical cause of the exceedance (SCR malfunction, EGR failure, engine overload). The AI must correlate emission breaches with engine operational parameters from the engine monitoring system and maintenance records to identify whether the breach resulted from equipment failure, incorrect operation, or fuel quality issues. Each air emission incident must be linked to the vessel's IAPP Certificate and engine NOx Technical File. Incidents occurring in designated ECAs must be flagged with higher severity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R011	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Environmental Incident Evidence Management and Document Repository	The system must provide a secure document repository for storing all evidence related to environmental incidents, maintaining chain-of-custody integrity for potential legal proceedings. Evidence types must include photographs and video with embedded GPS coordinates and timestamps, laboratory analysis reports for water, soil, or air samples, crew witness statements with digital signatures, electronic log book extracts (Oil Record Book, Garbage Record Book, Ballast Water Record Book), Voyage Data Recorder (VDR) data extracts, AIS track data, weather and current data for the incident period, port authority inspection reports, and classification society condition of class records. Each evidence item must be timestamped, tagged with the incident reference, classified by evidence type, and access-controlled to prevent unauthorized modification. The AI must organize evidence chronologically and cross-reference items to support the investigation timeline. Storage must comply with data retention requirements of all four jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and the longest applicable retention period must be applied (typically 10 years for environmental liability). Evidence must be exportable in a legal-admissible format for court or arbitration proceedings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R012	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Near-Miss Environmental Event Reporting and Analysis	The system must support reporting and analysis of near-miss environmental events where a potential pollution incident was narrowly avoided, fostering a proactive safety culture per ISM Code Section 9. Near-miss categories must include near-spill events (valve errors, hose failures contained before release), potential ballast water violations detected before discharge, fuel quality concerns identified before bunkering completion, waste management procedural deviations caught before discharge, and equipment failures on pollution prevention systems detected during routine checks. The reporting interface must encourage no-blame reporting with an option for anonymous submission while still capturing sufficient technical detail for analysis. The AI must analyze near-miss reports to identify leading indicators and trending risk patterns across the fleet, correlating near-miss frequency with vessel age, crew experience levels, trade routes, and seasonal factors. Near-miss data must feed into the risk assessment model to prioritize preventive investments. Monthly near-miss trend reports must be generated per entity and fleet-wide, displayed in bilingual English/Arabic dashboards.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R013	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Port Reception Facility Usage Tracking and Documentation	The system must track the use of port reception facilities for delivery of ship-generated waste at all operational ports including Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Each delivery record must capture the waste type per MARPOL classification (oily waste, noxious liquid substances, sewage, garbage by category), quantity delivered in appropriate units (cubic meters or metric tonnes), port reception facility operator name and license number, waste delivery receipt reference number, delivery date and time in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, and the cost charged for the reception service in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with USD equivalent. The system must maintain a database of port reception facility availability, capacity, and advance notification requirements for each port. The AI must pre-plan waste delivery schedules for upcoming port calls based on accumulated waste quantities onboard and port facility availability, generating advance notification forms per port-specific requirements. Non-availability of adequate port reception facilities must trigger MARPOL inadequacy reporting to the flag state and IMO per MEPC.1/Circ.834/Rev.1.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R014	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Environmental KPI Dashboard and Fleet Performance Metrics	The system must display a comprehensive environmental KPI dashboard aggregating incident and compliance data across the fleet for management oversight. Key metrics must include total environmental incidents by category and severity (year-to-date and trailing 12 months), incident frequency rate (incidents per 1000 operating days), total oil spilled (litres), CAPA completion rate and overdue percentage, near-miss reporting rate per vessel per month, port state control environmental deficiency rate, total environmental incident costs by entity in local currency and USD, and waste delivery volumes versus waste generation rates. The dashboard must support drill-down from fleet level to entity level to individual vessel level. Trend indicators must show improvement or deterioration against the previous period and against annual targets set in each entity's Environmental Management System. The AI must generate monthly environmental performance commentaries highlighting significant events, emerging risks, and recommended management actions. Dashboard visualization must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with Hijri calendar date range selectors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R015	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Shipboard Emergency Response Plan Integration	The system must integrate with each vessel's Shipboard Oil Pollution Emergency Plan (SOPEP) per MARPOL Annex I Regulation 37 and Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plan (SMPEP) per MARPOL Annex II Regulation 17. The digital plan must contain up-to-date emergency contact lists for port authorities, national maritime administrations, P&I Clubs, salvage companies, and oil spill response organizations (OSRO) specific to the vessel's current operating area (Gulf region contacts for Hamad Port, Jebel Ali, Khalifa Port; Indian coast contacts for JNPT). The AI must dynamically update the relevant emergency contacts based on the vessel's real-time AIS position, ensuring the crew always has the correct regional contacts immediately accessible. The system must provide guided checklists for initial response actions based on the incident type, including notification sequences, containment measures, and evidence preservation steps. Drill and exercise records linked to SOPEP/SMPEP must be tracked with dates, participants, scenario descriptions, and lessons learned. The emergency plan module must function fully offline on the vessel's local installation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R016	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Regulatory Inspection and Audit Finding Management	The system must track environmental findings and observations from port state control inspections, flag state audits, classification society surveys, ISM Code audits, and ISPS Code inspections that pertain to environmental compliance. Each finding must be recorded with the inspection type, inspection date (Gregorian and Hijri), inspecting authority or organization, finding reference code (PSC deficiency code per IMO Resolution A.1155(32)), finding description in English and Arabic, required corrective action, deadline for rectification, and current status. For PSC detainable deficiencies related to environmental systems (ODME malfunction, OWS bypass detection, BWMS inoperability), the system must immediately trigger an incident escalation workflow to the DPA and entity management. The AI must analyze inspection finding patterns across the fleet to identify systemic issues (e.g., recurring OWS maintenance failures on a particular vessel class) and recommend targeted preventive measures. Finding resolution must be verified by the classification society or inspector and documented with closure evidence. Statistics on finding rates and recurrence must be tracked per entity and benchmarked against industry averages from Paris MOU and Indian Ocean MOU annual reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-059-2-004-R017	FEAT-059-2-004	MOD-059	Environmental Incident Lessons Learned and Fleet-Wide Communication	The system must facilitate the capture and fleet-wide dissemination of lessons learned from environmental incidents and near-misses to prevent recurrence across the organization. After each incident investigation is completed, the system must generate a structured lessons-learned bulletin containing the incident summary (anonymized if required), root causes identified, CAPAs implemented, and key takeaways for crew and shore staff. The AI must categorize lessons by topic (oil pollution prevention, air emissions, waste management, ballast water, cargo operations), relevance by vessel type and trade area, and priority level. Distribution must be targeted to relevant vessel types and departments, with read-receipt tracking to confirm crew acknowledgment. The system must maintain a searchable lessons-learned knowledge base accessible in English and Arabic, with the AI able to retrieve relevant past lessons when a new incident exhibits similar characteristics. Quarterly fleet safety committee meeting agendas must be auto-generated incorporating the period's environmental lessons learned. The lessons-learned module must be accessible offline on vessels for crew review during safety meetings and toolbox talks.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R001	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Register CRUD Operations	The system must support full create, read, update, and delete operations for risk register entries across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Each risk entry must capture risk title in both Arabic and English, risk category (operational, financial, regulatory, environmental, reputational, strategic), risk owner assignment from the organizational hierarchy, date of identification in both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats, and the originating port code (QAHMD for Hamad Port, AEKHL for Khalifa Port, AEJEA for Jebel Ali, INJNP for JNPT). The system must enforce mandatory fields and prevent submission of incomplete entries while supporting offline creation with automatic synchronization upon reconnection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R002	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	AI-Powered Risk Scoring Engine	The system must implement an AI-driven risk scoring engine that automatically calculates risk scores based on a configurable likelihood-impact matrix (minimum 5x5 grid). The engine must analyze historical incident data, near-miss reports, and external threat intelligence feeds to generate a composite risk score from 1 to 100. Scores must factor in port-specific variables such as weather patterns for Arabian Gulf ports, monsoon seasonality affecting JNPT operations, geopolitical risk indices for the Middle East corridor, and currency volatility across USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The AI model must retrain weekly using newly ingested data and provide confidence intervals for each score.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R003	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Categorization Taxonomy	The system must provide a hierarchical risk categorization taxonomy with at least three levels of depth supporting both Arabic and English labels. Categories must include but not be limited to: operational risks (vessel delays, port congestion, equipment failure, container damage), financial risks (currency fluctuation across QAR/AED/SAR/INR/USD, credit default, freight rate volatility), regulatory risks (customs compliance in Qatar/UAE/KSA/India, sanctions screening, INCOTERMS misapplication for FOB/CIF/CFR/DDP terms), environmental risks (oil spill, emissions non-compliance with IMO 2020), and security risks (piracy, theft, cyber threats). Administrators must be able to extend the taxonomy without code changes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R004	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Heat Map Visualization	The system must generate interactive risk heat maps that display all registered risks plotted on a likelihood-vs-impact grid with color-coded severity zones (green for low, yellow for moderate, amber for high, red for critical). Heat maps must be filterable by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port (Hamad/Khalifa/Jebel Ali/JNPT), risk category, risk owner, and time period. The visualization must support Arabic RTL layout with mirrored axis labels and Arabic numerals when the user's locale is set to Arabic. Drill-down capability must allow users to click any risk point to view full details, linked incidents, and historical score trends over the past 24 months.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R008	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Multi-Entity Risk Consolidation Dashboard	The system must provide a consolidated enterprise-wide risk dashboard aggregating risk data across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) with the ability to drill down to individual entity, port, or department levels. The dashboard must display key risk indicators (KRIs) including total active risks, risks by severity, overdue mitigation actions, top 10 risks by score, risk trend over time, and cost of risk in USD. Financial values from different entities must be automatically converted to a common reporting currency (USD) using real-time exchange rates. The dashboard must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts with all chart labels, legends, and data tables properly mirrored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R009	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Register Import and Export	The system must support bulk import of risk register entries from Excel (.xlsx) and CSV files with a predefined template that includes all mandatory fields. The import process must validate data integrity, flag duplicate entries based on risk title similarity using AI-powered fuzzy matching, and provide a preview screen showing proposed changes before committing. Export functionality must generate reports in PDF, Excel, and CSV formats with options to filter by entity, port, category, severity, and date range. PDF exports must support bilingual Arabic-English layouts with proper RTL formatting, company letterhead, and Hijri date stamps. All imports and exports must maintain a complete audit trail logging the user, timestamp, and record count.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R010	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Regulatory Risk Compliance Mapping	The system must map risk register entries to applicable regulatory frameworks and compliance requirements specific to each jurisdiction. For Qatar, this includes QP HSE regulations and Qatar Civil Defense requirements. For UAE, this includes Abu Dhabi EHSMS and Dubai Maritime City Authority regulations. For KSA, this includes GACA and Saudi Ports Authority standards. For India, this includes DGMS, Indian Ports Act, and Major Port Trusts Act requirements. Each risk must be taggable with one or more regulatory references, and the system must alert risk owners when regulatory changes affect their registered risks. AI must scan regulatory update feeds and automatically suggest new risk entries when emerging regulations are detected.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R011	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Interdependency Mapping	The system must support mapping of interdependencies between risk register entries including parent-child relationships, causal chains, and correlation links. When a risk score changes, the system must automatically recalculate scores for all linked dependent risks using configurable propagation rules. A visual network graph must display risk interdependencies with node sizes proportional to risk scores and edge thickness proportional to dependency strength. The graph must be interactive, allowing users to simulate what-if scenarios by adjusting one risk score and observing the cascade effect on connected risks across multiple entities and ports. This capability is critical for understanding systemic risks in the Qatar-Dubai-India container shipping corridor.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R012	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Key Risk Indicator (KRI) Configuration	The system must allow administrators to define custom Key Risk Indicators (KRIs) with configurable data sources, calculation formulas, threshold values, and alert rules. KRIs must support both leading indicators (predictive metrics such as vessel schedule reliability, port congestion index, weather severity forecasts) and lagging indicators (historical metrics such as incident frequency rate, cargo damage percentage, claims ratio). Each KRI must be assignable to one or more risk register entries and must display trend data with RAG (Red/Amber/Green) status on the risk dashboard. KRI thresholds must be independently configurable per entity to account for varying risk profiles across Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R013	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Register Versioning and Audit Trail	The system must maintain a complete version history of every risk register entry, recording all changes including field-level modifications, score recalculations, status transitions, and ownership transfers. Each version must capture the modifying user's identity, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, IP address, entity context, and a mandatory change reason. The audit trail must be immutable and tamper-proof, stored with cryptographic hash chaining to prevent unauthorized modification. Authorized auditors must be able to view the full change history of any risk, compare any two versions side by side with differences highlighted, and generate audit reports filtered by date range, user, entity, or change type for internal and external audit compliance.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R014	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk-Based Insurance and Claims Linkage	The system must link risk register entries to corresponding insurance policies and active claims within the ERP. When a risk materializes into an incident, the system must automatically identify applicable insurance coverage based on risk category, affected port, cargo type (dry container, reefer, tank container, flat rack, open top), and INCOTERMS delivery terms (FOB, CIF, CFR, FCA, DDP). Financial exposure calculations must consider deductibles, coverage limits, and co-insurance percentages denominated in the policy currency with conversion to the entity's local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) and USD. The system must track residual risk exposure after insurance offset and flag under-insured risks exceeding configurable thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R015	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Offline Risk Register Access and Sync	The system must provide offline access to the risk register for field personnel operating in areas with limited connectivity at port terminals, vessel berths, or remote warehouse locations. The offline module must cache the user's assigned risks, pending review tasks, and mitigation action items with all associated documents and images. Users must be able to create new risk entries, update existing entries, and complete review tasks while offline. Upon reconnection, the system must perform intelligent conflict resolution using timestamp-based merging with manual override options for conflicting changes. Synchronization must be atomic at the record level to prevent partial updates, and the system must display a clear sync status indicator showing pending uploads and last successful sync time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R016	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	AI Risk Prediction and Early Warning	The system must implement an AI-based predictive analytics engine that forecasts emerging risks by analyzing patterns across operational data, external threat feeds, weather forecasts, geopolitical indices, and market data for the Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean shipping routes. The engine must generate early warning alerts at least 72 hours before predicted risk materialization with a minimum accuracy target of 75 percent. Predictions must be port-specific, distinguishing between risks at Hamad Port (limited berth capacity risks), Jebel Ali (high-volume congestion risks), Khalifa Port (petrochemical handling risks), and JNPT (monsoon and infrastructure risks). Each prediction must include a confidence score, contributing factors, recommended preventive actions, and estimated financial impact in USD.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R017	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Role-Based Access Control for Risk Data	The system must enforce granular role-based access control (RBAC) for all risk register data with permissions configurable at entity, port, department, risk category, and individual risk levels. Predefined roles must include Risk Administrator (full access), Risk Committee Member (view and approve), Risk Owner (edit assigned risks), Department Manager (view department risks), and Auditor (read-only with export). Cross-entity visibility must be restricted by default, requiring explicit authorization for users to view risks from entities other than their own. The system must support delegation of risk ownership during absence periods with automatic reversion upon return. All access attempts to restricted risk data must be logged for security audit purposes with alerts on unauthorized access patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-001-R018	FEAT-060-1-001	MOD-060	Risk Register Integration with External Systems	The system must provide RESTful API endpoints and webhook capabilities for bidirectional integration with external risk management systems, port community systems (PCS), and maritime authorities. Inbound integrations must accept risk alerts from vessel tracking systems (AIS data), weather services (Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean forecasts), and regulatory bodies (Qatar Ministry of Transport, UAE FTA, Saudi Customs, Indian CBIC). Outbound integrations must publish risk notifications to enterprise communication platforms and business intelligence tools. All API communications must use TLS 1.3 encryption, OAuth 2.0 authentication, and support both JSON and XML payload formats. Rate limiting must be configurable per integration partner with a default of 1000 requests per minute.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R001	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Policy and Procedure Repository	The system must maintain a centralized digital repository for all HSSE policies, procedures, work instructions, and safety data sheets (SDS) across all four entities. Each document must support bilingual content in Arabic and English with versioning, approval workflows, and automatic distribution tracking. Documents must be categorized by entity (Qatar/UAE/KSA/India), port facility, operation type (vessel operations, yard operations, gate operations, reefer monitoring, dangerous goods handling), and regulatory framework. The system must enforce periodic review cycles with automated reminders to document owners and must track acknowledgment receipts from all designated personnel. Expired or superseded documents must be archived but remain searchable for audit and investigation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R002	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Inspection Scheduling and Execution	The system must support planning, scheduling, and execution of HSSE inspections across all port facilities and operational areas. Inspection types must include routine scheduled inspections, unannounced spot checks, pre-operation safety inspections, and regulatory compliance audits. Each inspection must use configurable checklists specific to the inspection type, facility, and applicable regulations (NEBOSH standards, OSHA equivalents, local port authority requirements). Scheduling must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendars with entity-specific working days. Inspectors must be able to capture findings using mobile devices with offline capability, attaching photographs, GPS coordinates of the finding location within the port, and severity classifications. AI must analyze inspection patterns to recommend optimal inspection frequencies for different risk areas.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R003	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Permit to Work (PTW) Management	The system must implement a digital Permit to Work system covering all high-risk activities at port terminals including hot work (welding, cutting near containers), confined space entry (tank container inspection), working at height (crane maintenance, container stacking), electrical isolation, excavation, and radiography. Each PTW must capture the work description, location within the port facility, risk assessment reference, required PPE, isolation requirements, gas test results where applicable, authorized personnel, validity period, and emergency contacts. The system must enforce sequential approval workflows with entity-specific authorization hierarchies and prevent permit issuance when prerequisite conditions are not met. Active permits must display on a real-time facility map showing all concurrent high-risk activities to prevent conflicting operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R004	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Safety Observation and Behavioral Safety Tracking	The system must enable all employees and contractors to submit safety observations categorized as safe behaviors, unsafe behaviors, unsafe conditions, or improvement suggestions. Each observation must capture the observer's identity (with anonymous option where permitted by entity policy), observation date and time, location (port, terminal, zone), activity being observed, description in Arabic or English, and photographic evidence. The system must track behavioral safety metrics including safe-to-unsafe observation ratios per department, port, and entity. AI must analyze observation trends to identify emerging safety culture patterns, predict areas likely to experience incidents, and generate weekly behavioral safety dashboards with trend analysis visible to HSSE managers across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R005	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Environmental Monitoring and Compliance	The system must track environmental compliance metrics including air quality measurements, noise levels, water discharge quality, waste generation and disposal records, and carbon emissions per vessel call at each port. Environmental data must be mapped to applicable regulations: Qatar Ministry of Environment standards, UAE Federal Environmental Agency requirements, Saudi NCEC regulations, and Indian MoEFCC guidelines. The system must calculate Scope 1, 2, and 3 emissions for each entity and port facility, tracking against entity-specific reduction targets. Automated alerts must trigger when any monitored parameter approaches or exceeds regulatory limits, with escalation to the environmental compliance officer. AI must predict environmental compliance risks based on operational forecasts such as increased vessel traffic or hazardous cargo bookings.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R006	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Training Matrix and Competency Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive training matrix mapping required HSSE competencies to each job role across all entities and port facilities. Training requirements must cover mandatory safety inductions, equipment-specific certifications (crane operator, forklift driver, reach stacker operator), hazardous materials handling (IMDG Code compliance), first aid and emergency response, fire safety, and entity-specific regulatory training. The system must track individual training records including course name, provider, completion date, expiry date, and certification number. Automated alerts must notify employees and their supervisors 30, 14, and 7 days before certification expiry. The system must prevent assignment of personnel to tasks requiring certifications they do not hold or that have expired, enforcing this at the work order and permit-to-work level.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R007	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Emergency Response Plan Management	The system must store and manage emergency response plans (ERPs) for each port facility covering scenarios including fire, explosion, chemical spill, man overboard, vessel collision, structural collapse, security breach, natural disaster (sandstorms for Gulf ports, cyclones and flooding for JNPT), and medical emergencies. Each ERP must include response procedures, assembly points mapped on facility layouts, emergency contact directories, resource requirements (firefighting equipment, spill kits, medical supplies), and communication protocols. The system must support drill scheduling with Gregorian and Hijri date options, drill execution recording, and post-drill evaluation workflows. AI must analyze drill performance data and real incident response times to recommend ERP improvements and identify gaps in emergency preparedness across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R008	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Dangerous Goods HSSE Compliance	The system must enforce HSSE compliance for dangerous goods handling at all port terminals in accordance with the IMDG Code, ADR/RID regulations, and local authority requirements for each jurisdiction. The system must validate segregation requirements for container stacking in the yard, ensuring incompatible dangerous goods classes are not placed adjacent to each other per IMDG Table 7.2.4. Handling restrictions must be enforced based on the specific UN number, packing group, and container type (standard dry, tank container, flexitank). The system must generate real-time dangerous goods manifests showing current dangerous goods inventory at each terminal with proximity alerts when thresholds are exceeded. Emergency information sheets must be automatically linked to dangerous goods locations for first responder access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R009	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE KPI Dashboard and Reporting	The system must provide configurable HSSE KPI dashboards displaying leading and lagging safety indicators across all entities and ports. Lagging indicators must include Lost Time Injury Frequency Rate (LTIFR), Total Recordable Incident Rate (TRIR), fatalities, environmental incidents, and property damage costs in local currency and USD. Leading indicators must include training completion rates, inspection completion rates, safety observation submission rates, corrective action closure rates, and permit compliance rates. Dashboards must support drill-down from enterprise level to entity, port, department, and individual team levels. All charts and tables must render correctly in both English LTR and Arabic RTL layouts. The system must generate automated monthly, quarterly, and annual HSSE reports in PDF format with bilingual Arabic-English content for board-level presentation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R010	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Corrective and Preventive Action (CAPA) Management	The system must implement a structured CAPA workflow for managing corrective and preventive actions arising from inspections, incidents, audits, and observations. Each CAPA must capture the source finding, root cause classification, proposed action description in Arabic and English, responsible person, target completion date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, estimated cost in local currency, verification method, and effectiveness criteria. The system must support multi-level approval workflows based on CAPA cost and risk severity. Overdue CAPAs must trigger automated escalation emails at configurable intervals. AI must analyze CAPA data to identify recurring root causes, recommend systemic corrective actions, and predict which open CAPAs are at risk of becoming overdue based on historical completion patterns for similar actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R011	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Occupational Health Surveillance	The system must manage occupational health surveillance programs for port workers exposed to specific hazards including noise exposure (crane operators, yard equipment operators), chemical exposure (fumigation teams, dangerous goods handlers), heat stress (outdoor workers at Gulf ports where temperatures exceed 50 degrees Celsius), and ergonomic risks (manual handling of lashing equipment). Health surveillance records must capture pre-employment medical assessments, periodic examinations, fitness-for-duty certifications, and workplace injury treatment records. All health data must be encrypted and access-restricted to authorized occupational health personnel in compliance with data protection regulations in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India. The system must track return-to-work programs for injured workers and flag personnel due for medical assessments based on their exposure profile and risk category.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R012	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Contractor HSSE Management	The system must manage HSSE requirements for all contractors and subcontractors operating within port facilities across all four entities. Contractor management must include pre-qualification HSSE assessments, insurance verification (with amounts validated against minimum thresholds in local currency and USD), safety plan review and approval, personnel competency verification, and ongoing performance monitoring. Each contractor must have a HSSE scorecard tracking incident rates, inspection findings, training compliance, and permit violations. The system must enforce gate access restrictions preventing contractor personnel from entering port facilities without valid safety inductions, required certifications, and approved PPE. Contractor HSSE performance data must feed into the procurement module to inform future tendering decisions with automatic disqualification triggers for contractors falling below minimum safety performance thresholds.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R013	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	Security Threat Assessment and Management	The system must support security threat assessments for port facilities aligned with the International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code requirements. Threat assessments must evaluate risks from unauthorized access, cargo theft, smuggling, sabotage, cyber attacks on port systems, and terrorism for each port facility at the three defined MARSEC security levels. The system must maintain security plans for each facility with escalation procedures, access control configurations, and surveillance requirements per MARSEC level. When the MARSEC level changes, the system must automatically trigger the corresponding security measures checklist and notify all relevant security personnel. AI must analyze access control logs, CCTV anomaly reports, and regional threat intelligence to provide real-time security risk scores for each port facility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R014	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Document Acknowledgment and Digital Signatures	The system must support digital acknowledgment and electronic signature capture for HSSE documents including safety briefings, toolbox talk attendance, risk assessment sign-offs, permit approvals, and incident report validations. Electronic signatures must comply with electronic transaction laws in Qatar (Law No. 16 of 2010), UAE (Federal Decree-Law No. 46 of 2021), KSA (Electronic Transactions Law), and India (Information Technology Act Section 3A). The system must capture the signer's full name, employee ID, timestamp in both Gregorian and Hijri formats, IP address, and device identifier. Signatures must be cryptographically secured to prevent tampering. Bulk acknowledgment tracking must allow HSSE managers to monitor compliance rates and send automated reminders to personnel who have not acknowledged mandatory documents within the specified deadline.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R015	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Mobile Application for Field Operations	The system must provide a mobile application for HSSE field operations supporting both iOS and Android platforms with full offline capability for use in port terminal areas with intermittent connectivity. The mobile app must support safety observation submission, inspection checklist completion, permit-to-work initiation and approval, incident reporting with photo and video capture, and emergency contact directory access. The interface must support Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with automatic switching based on device language settings. GPS-enabled location tagging must automatically identify the port facility and zone based on geofence configurations. The app must support barcode and QR code scanning for equipment identification during inspections. Data synchronization must occur automatically when connectivity is restored with conflict resolution for concurrent edits.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R016	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Regulatory Change Management	The system must track regulatory changes affecting HSSE compliance across all four jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and international maritime regulations (IMO conventions, ILO Maritime Labour Convention, SOLAS, MARPOL). AI must monitor regulatory feeds and government gazette publications to identify changes relevant to container shipping terminal operations. Each identified change must be assessed for impact on existing policies, procedures, and compliance status. The system must generate gap analysis reports comparing current compliance posture against new requirements, with recommended action plans and implementation timelines. Change tracking must maintain a complete history of regulatory evolution per jurisdiction, enabling auditors to verify compliance at any historical point in time.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R017	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	PPE Inventory and Compliance Tracking	The system must track personal protective equipment (PPE) inventory, issuance, and compliance across all port facilities and entities. PPE categories must include hard hats, safety boots, high-visibility vests, safety glasses, hearing protection, respiratory protection, chemical-resistant gloves, fall arrest harnesses, and life jackets. The system must record PPE issuance to individual personnel including item type, manufacturer, batch number, issuance date, expected replacement date, and condition assessments during inspections. Automated alerts must notify warehouse managers when PPE stock levels fall below reorder thresholds per facility. PPE cost tracking must be maintained in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with USD consolidated reporting. The system must flag non-compliant PPE usage identified during inspections and link findings to corrective action workflows.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-002-R018	FEAT-060-1-002	MOD-060	HSSE Benchmarking Across Entities	The system must enable benchmarking of HSSE performance across all four entities and port facilities using standardized metrics normalized for operational volume (per TEU handled, per vessel call, per employee hour worked). Benchmarking dashboards must display comparative performance for key indicators including incident rates, inspection scores, training compliance, CAPA closure rates, and environmental metrics. The system must identify statistically significant performance variations between entities and ports, highlighting best practices from top-performing facilities for cross-entity adoption. AI must generate quarterly benchmarking reports with natural language narrative summaries in both Arabic and English, recommending specific improvement actions for underperforming areas. Historical benchmarking data must be retained for a minimum of seven years to support long-term trend analysis and regulatory reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R001	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Report Submission Interface	The system must provide an intuitive submission interface for near-miss reports accessible via web browser and mobile application in both Arabic and English. The submission form must capture event date and time, location (entity, port, terminal, zone, specific area via map pin-drop), activity being performed, description of what happened and what could have happened, immediate actions taken, and suggested preventive measures. The interface must support photo and video attachments with a maximum file size of 50MB per attachment and up to 5 attachments per report. Voice-to-text input must be available for description fields supporting both Arabic and English speech recognition. The form must be completable within 3 minutes for simple reports to minimize barriers to reporting among frontline port workers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R002	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Anonymous Reporting Option	The system must provide an anonymous reporting option that completely anonymizes the reporter's identity while still capturing all event details. When anonymous mode is selected, the system must not store any identifying information including user ID, IP address, device identifier, or session tokens that could be used to trace the reporter. Anonymous reports must be processed through the same workflow as identified reports but with an auto-assigned handler rather than direct feedback to the reporter. Entity-specific configuration must control whether anonymous reporting is enabled, as some jurisdictions may require identified reporting for certain categories. The system must display a clear privacy notice in Arabic and English explaining data handling practices before submission, and the anonymous reporting rate must be tracked as a safety culture metric per entity and port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R003	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Classification and Severity Assessment	The system must classify near-miss reports using a multi-dimensional taxonomy including potential consequence severity (fatality, major injury, minor injury, property damage, environmental impact), event type (fall from height, struck by moving equipment, caught between containers, electrical contact, chemical exposure, vehicle collision, marine incident), equipment involved (STS crane, RTG, reach stacker, terminal tractor, forklift, vessel), and contributing factors (human error, equipment failure, procedural gap, environmental condition, communication breakdown). AI must automatically suggest classifications based on the text description and attached media, achieving a minimum 80 percent accuracy rate. The severity assessment must use a potential risk matrix evaluating what the worst credible outcome could have been, not just what actually happened, to prioritize follow-up actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R003	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Trade Lane Risk Corridor Mapping	The system must maintain and continuously update risk corridor maps for all trade lanes connecting Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT with global destinations. Risk corridors must incorporate piracy risk zones (Gulf of Aden, Strait of Hormuz, Malacca Strait), war-risk areas as defined by the Joint War Committee (JWC) Listed Areas, weather severity indices from IMD and Qatar Meteorology, and port congestion risk levels. Each corridor must carry a dynamic risk premium expressed in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR using real-time exchange rates. The AI must predict corridor risk changes 14 days in advance with at least 78% accuracy based on geopolitical event feeds and AIS vessel tracking data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R004	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Unsafe Act Categorization and Recording	The system must support dedicated reporting of unsafe acts observed during port operations with categories aligned to the port terminal operating environment. Categories must include failure to use required PPE, unauthorized entry into restricted zones, improper container lashing techniques, bypassing safety interlocks on cranes or equipment, using mobile phones in operational zones, exceeding speed limits within the terminal, improper lifting techniques, working under suspended loads, and failure to follow lockout-tagout procedures. Each unsafe act report must capture the observer's details (or anonymous flag), the person or group performing the unsafe act (identifiable by role only, not by name, to maintain a non-punitive culture), the specific location within the port facility, and photographic evidence where safely obtainable. The system must link recurring unsafe acts to training needs assessment and behavioral safety programs.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R005	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	AI-Powered Trend Analysis for Near-Misses	The system must implement AI-driven trend analysis that continuously monitors near-miss and unsafe act reports to identify emerging patterns, clusters, and escalation risks. The AI engine must detect spatial clusters (recurring near-misses in the same port zone), temporal patterns (time-of-day or shift-based correlations), equipment-related trends (specific crane or vehicle types), and activity-based patterns (particular operations generating disproportionate near-misses). When a statistically significant trend is identified, the system must automatically generate a trend alert to the relevant HSSE manager with a natural language summary in Arabic or English based on user preference. The system must calculate the Heinrich ratio (near-misses to minor incidents to major incidents) per entity and port, and trigger enhanced surveillance alerts when ratios deviate from expected norms for container terminal operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R006	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Triage and Assignment Workflow	The system must implement an automated triage workflow that routes near-miss reports to appropriate handlers based on configurable rules including report severity, location, event type, and equipment involved. High-severity near-misses (potential fatality or major injury) must be immediately escalated to the entity HSSE director and port facility manager with SMS and push notification alerts, requiring acknowledgment within 1 hour. Medium-severity reports must be assigned to department HSSE supervisors with a 24-hour response target. Low-severity reports must be assigned to the nearest area safety officer with a 72-hour response target. The workflow must support reassignment, delegation, and escalation with full audit trail. AI must recommend the optimal assignee based on workload balancing, expertise match, and historical resolution effectiveness across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R007	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Feedback Loop to Reporters	The system must implement a closed-loop feedback mechanism that keeps identified reporters informed about the status and outcome of their near-miss reports. Notifications must be sent at each workflow stage: report received, investigation initiated, corrective action planned, action completed, and case closed. Each notification must be bilingual (Arabic and English) and delivered via the reporter's preferred channel (in-app notification, email, or SMS). The final closure notification must include a summary of actions taken and a thank-you message reinforcing the positive safety reporting behavior. For anonymous reporters, the system must publish anonymized case summaries on a safety notice board within the mobile app and web portal so that all reporters can see that their submissions result in tangible safety improvements. The average feedback loop closure time must be tracked as a KPI per entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R008	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Gamification and Reporting Incentive Program	The system must implement a configurable gamification framework to encourage near-miss and unsafe act reporting across all entities and ports. The framework must support points-based rewards for report submissions, quality bonuses for reports that lead to corrective actions, milestone badges for consistent reporting behavior, and entity-wide or port-wide reporting challenges with leaderboards. Point values must be configurable per entity and may vary by report type and quality. The leaderboard must display department-level rankings (not individual) to promote team-based safety culture. Accumulated points must be convertible to tangible rewards managed through the HR module with monetary values tracked in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR). The system must prevent gaming through AI-based detection of low-quality or duplicate submissions and must exclude flagged submissions from point calculations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R009	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Photographic and Multimedia Evidence Management	The system must manage multimedia evidence attached to near-miss and unsafe act reports including photographs, videos, audio recordings, and annotated diagrams. The system must automatically extract metadata from uploaded media including capture timestamp, GPS coordinates, and device information. Image annotation tools must allow reporters and investigators to highlight hazards, mark locations, and add text callouts directly on photographs. Video evidence must support playback with frame-by-frame analysis capability for detailed incident reconstruction. All media must be stored in a secure, encrypted repository with retention periods configurable per entity (minimum 3 years). Media storage costs must be tracked per entity in local currency with automated archival of older evidence to lower-cost storage tiers. The system must strip personal identification metadata from media attached to anonymous reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R010	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Cross-Entity Near-Miss Knowledge Sharing	The system must facilitate knowledge sharing of near-miss learnings across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) through a structured safety alert and lessons-learned distribution system. When a significant near-miss occurs at one entity, the HSSE team must be able to create a safety alert that is automatically translated into Arabic and English and distributed to relevant personnel at all other entities based on applicability criteria (similar operations, equipment types, or risk categories). The system must maintain a searchable lessons-learned database indexed by event type, root cause, port facility type, and equipment category. AI must proactively recommend relevant historical near-misses and lessons learned when a new report is submitted, helping investigators identify systemic issues across the multi-entity operation and preventing recurrence of similar events at other ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R001	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Login Page	Email + password login form with client-side validation, show/hide password toggle, remember-me checkbox (30-day JWT), and CAPTCHA after 3 failed attempts. Arabic RTL + English LTR toggle on login screen.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R011	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Reporting Metrics and Analytics	The system must calculate and display comprehensive near-miss reporting metrics including total reports per period, reports per employee, reports per 1000 TEU handled, reports per vessel call, reporting frequency by port zone, average time from event to report submission, average investigation closure time, percentage of reports resulting in corrective actions, and repeat near-miss rates for the same hazard. All metrics must be available at enterprise, entity, port, department, and team levels with period-over-period comparison capability. Reports must support both Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods. Metrics dashboards must render correctly in Arabic RTL layout with Arabic numeral display options. The system must generate automated monthly reporting metric summaries distributed to HSSE leadership with AI-generated narrative insights highlighting significant trends and areas requiring management attention.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R012	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Integration with Operational Systems for Context Enrichment	The system must integrate with operational systems to automatically enrich near-miss reports with contextual data at the time of the event. Integration sources must include the Terminal Operating System (TOS) for container movement and equipment activity data, the vessel scheduling system for vessel presence and cargo operations status, the gate management system for truck traffic volumes, the weather monitoring system for environmental conditions (temperature, wind speed, visibility, humidity), and the workforce management system for shift details and personnel on-site. When a near-miss is reported, the system must automatically attach relevant contextual data including what operations were active, what equipment was operating nearby, weather conditions, and staffing levels. This enrichment eliminates manual data gathering by investigators and provides immediate situational context for faster and more accurate root cause analysis.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R013	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Recurring Hazard Identification and Tracking	The system must automatically identify and track recurring hazards by analyzing near-miss reports for patterns of similar events at the same location, involving the same equipment, or related to the same activity type. When a hazard recurs more than a configurable number of times (default: 3 occurrences within 90 days), the system must escalate it to a persistent hazard record that requires a comprehensive risk assessment and systemic corrective action plan. Persistent hazard records must be linked to the enterprise risk register for ongoing monitoring and must be visible on facility safety maps as permanent warning markers. AI must calculate a recurrence probability score for each identified hazard based on historical frequency, seasonal factors, operational changes, and effectiveness of previous corrective actions. The system must generate automatic alerts when a near-miss matches a known persistent hazard pattern at any entity or port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R014	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Safety Stand-Down and Toolbox Talk Generation	The system must automatically generate safety stand-down briefing materials and toolbox talk content based on recent near-miss reports and trending hazards. Generated content must include a summary of the near-miss event (anonymized), hazard description, potential consequences, preventive measures, and discussion questions for team engagement. Content must be generated in both Arabic and English with appropriate reading level for frontline port workers. The system must schedule and track toolbox talk delivery across all shifts and departments with attendance recording and comprehension verification through simple quiz questions. AI must select the most relevant recent near-misses for each department based on their operational profile, ensuring that yard operations teams receive yard-relevant briefings while marine operations teams receive berth-relevant content. Toolbox talk completion rates must be tracked as a leading safety indicator per entity and port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R015	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Near-Miss Report Quality Assurance	The system must implement quality assurance mechanisms for near-miss reports to ensure data integrity and analytical value. AI must evaluate report quality at submission time based on completeness of description, specificity of location information, clarity of hazard identification, and relevance of attached evidence. Reports scoring below a configurable quality threshold must prompt the reporter with specific suggestions for improvement before submission. Post-submission quality reviews must be conducted by HSSE coordinators who can request additional information from identified reporters, reclassify events, merge duplicate reports, and link related reports. The system must track quality metrics including average report quality score, percentage of reports requiring revision, and correlation between report quality and corrective action effectiveness. These quality metrics must be factored into the gamification scoring system to incentivize thorough reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R016	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Offline Near-Miss Reporting Capability	The system must support complete near-miss report submission while the device is offline, critical for port terminal areas with poor cellular coverage near large metal container stacks and ship hulls. The offline module must cache the full submission form, location data from GPS, and allow photo and video capture stored locally on the device. Reports created offline must be queued with a visible pending count indicator and automatically submitted when network connectivity is restored. The system must handle conflicts when the same user creates multiple offline reports by assigning sequential temporary IDs that are replaced with permanent system IDs upon synchronization. Offline mode must support both Arabic and English interfaces without requiring network access for language resources. The maximum offline queue must support at least 20 pending reports per device with a total media attachment size of up to 500MB.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R017	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Management of Change Safety Impact Assessment	The system must integrate near-miss reporting with the Management of Change (MOC) process to assess safety impacts of operational changes at port facilities. When operational changes are proposed (new equipment installation, procedure modifications, layout changes, staffing adjustments), the system must automatically query the near-miss database for historical events related to the affected area, equipment type, or activity. The impact assessment must present a risk profile of the change area including near-miss frequency, severity distribution, and trending patterns. Post-implementation, the system must monitor for changes in near-miss patterns in the affected area and alert HSSE managers if near-miss rates increase following the change. This creates a closed-loop feedback mechanism between operational changes and safety outcomes across all four entities and their port facilities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-003-R018	FEAT-060-1-003	MOD-060	Regulatory Near-Miss Reporting Compliance	The system must support regulatory near-miss reporting requirements specific to each jurisdiction where the entity operates. For Qatar, reports meeting certain severity thresholds must be formatted for submission to the Ministry of Labour. For UAE, reportable near-misses at Khalifa Port must comply with Abu Dhabi Ports HSSE reporting requirements while Jebel Ali must comply with Dubai Maritime City Authority standards. For KSA, reporting must align with the Ministry of Human Resources and Social Development requirements. For India, JNPT near-misses must comply with the Factories Act and Dock Workers Safety Health and Welfare Act reporting requirements. The system must automatically identify reports meeting regulatory reporting thresholds, pre-populate authority submission forms in the required format and language, track submission deadlines, and maintain proof of submission. Failure to meet regulatory submission deadlines must trigger executive-level alerts with potential penalty exposure calculations in local currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R001	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Incident Report Creation and Initial Classification	The system must support comprehensive incident report creation capturing all essential details including incident date and time in Gregorian and Hijri formats, exact location within the port facility using GPS coordinates and zone mapping, incident type (injury, illness, property damage, environmental release, security breach, cargo damage, vessel incident, near-miss escalation), involved personnel details, involved equipment and container numbers, weather conditions at the time, shift information, and immediate response actions taken. AI must auto-classify incident severity using the ICAM (Incident Cause Analysis Method) classification system and suggest the appropriate investigation level: Level 1 (simple investigation for low-severity), Level 2 (formal investigation for medium-severity), or Level 3 (full team investigation for high-severity and fatalities). Classification must be reviewable and overridable by authorized HSSE managers.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R002	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Investigation Team Assignment and Management	The system must support formation and management of incident investigation teams based on incident severity, type, and location. For Level 3 investigations, the system must enforce minimum team composition requirements including a trained lead investigator, subject matter experts relevant to the incident type, a worker representative, and an HSSE professional. The system must maintain a registry of qualified investigators across all four entities with their competencies, certifications (ICAM, TapRooT, or equivalent), availability, and investigation history. AI must recommend optimal team composition based on incident characteristics, investigator expertise, workload, and entity affiliation. For cross-entity incidents (such as cargo damage discovered at a destination port originating from a source port), the system must support multi-entity investigation teams with shared access to evidence and findings while respecting entity-specific data governance policies.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R003	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Evidence Collection and Chain of Custody	The system must provide a structured evidence management framework maintaining chain of custody for all investigation materials. Evidence types must include photographs, video recordings, CCTV footage references, witness statements, equipment inspection records, maintenance logs, training records, operating procedures, weather data, vessel traffic data, container tracking data, and communication records. Each evidence item must be timestamped, tagged with a unique evidence ID, linked to the investigator who collected it, and stored in an encrypted tamper-proof repository. The system must log all evidence access events including who viewed, downloaded, or modified evidence and when. For physical evidence that cannot be digitized, the system must track physical custody transfers with digital sign-off by both the releasing and receiving parties. Evidence must be retained for a minimum period configurable per entity and incident severity, with default retention of 7 years for serious incidents.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R004	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Witness Statement Management	The system must support structured collection and management of witness statements with bilingual Arabic and English support. Each statement must capture the witness's identity (with confidentiality protections configurable per entity policy), role and department, relationship to the incident, a free-text narrative of their observations, a structured questionnaire section with investigation-specific questions, and the date and time of statement collection. The system must support audio recording of verbal statements with AI-powered transcription in both Arabic and English, allowing investigators to review and annotate transcripts. Witness statements must be digitally signed by the witness to confirm accuracy. The system must track which witnesses have been interviewed and which are still pending, with automated reminders to investigation team members. Access to witness statements must be restricted to the investigation team and authorized reviewers to protect witness confidentiality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R005	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	5-Why Root Cause Analysis Tool	The system must implement an interactive 5-Why analysis tool that guides investigators through the iterative questioning process to identify root causes of incidents. The tool must present a structured template where each why question and answer is documented with supporting evidence references. The system must support branching analysis where a single why level may have multiple contributing causes, each requiring its own branch of further investigation. AI must suggest potential answers at each level based on similar incidents in the database, historical near-miss patterns, and industry knowledge of common failure modes in container terminal operations (crane malfunctions, lashing failures, reefer unit breakdowns, yard collision scenarios). The completed 5-Why analysis must be exportable as a visual diagram and PDF report in both Arabic and English with all evidence cross-references maintained.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R006	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Fishbone (Ishikawa) Diagram Builder	The system must provide an interactive fishbone diagram builder for structured root cause analysis using the Ishikawa methodology. The default category headings must be customizable but pre-populated with container shipping terminal relevant categories: People (training, competency, fatigue, communication), Process (procedures, planning, scheduling, supervision), Equipment (maintenance, design, condition, suitability), Environment (weather, visibility, noise, temperature, sea state), Materials (cargo type, container condition, lashing equipment, PPE), and Management (policies, resourcing, oversight, safety culture). Investigators must be able to add, edit, and remove branches interactively with drag-and-drop functionality. Each cause must be linkable to supporting evidence from the evidence repository. The diagram must render correctly in both LTR English and RTL Arabic layouts with proper text direction. AI must suggest potential causes for each category based on the incident description and similar historical incidents across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R007	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Fault Tree Analysis (FTA) Tool	The system must implement a fault tree analysis tool for complex incidents requiring systematic top-down analysis of failure paths. The tool must support standard FTA logic gates including AND gates (all sub-events must occur), OR gates (any sub-event can cause the parent event), and inhibit gates (conditional events). Each basic event in the fault tree must be linkable to evidence and assignable a probability estimate based on historical data or expert judgment. The system must automatically calculate the minimum cut sets identifying the smallest combinations of basic events that can cause the top event. AI must suggest fault tree structures based on the incident type and location, pre-populating common failure modes for port terminal operations such as crane failure trees, vehicle collision trees, and cargo damage trees. The completed fault tree must be exportable as a scalable vector graphic and included in the investigation report.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R008	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Barrier Analysis and Bow-Tie Methodology	The system must provide a barrier analysis tool using the bow-tie methodology to evaluate the effectiveness of preventive and mitigating controls for incident scenarios. The bow-tie diagram must display the hazard at center, threat pathways on the left with preventive barriers, and consequence pathways on the right with mitigating barriers. Each barrier must be characterized by type (engineering control, administrative control, PPE, emergency response), effectiveness rating, ownership, and current operational status. When barriers are identified as failed or degraded during investigation, the system must flag them for immediate corrective action and update the barrier status across all related risk assessments in the enterprise risk register. AI must evaluate barrier effectiveness based on incident history and recommend barrier improvements, new barriers, or independent protection layers (IPLs) specific to container terminal hazard scenarios including dropped containers, crane collisions, and hazardous cargo releases.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R009	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Investigation Timeline Reconstruction	The system must provide an interactive timeline reconstruction tool that plots the sequence of events leading up to, during, and after an incident on a visual chronological timeline. The timeline must support multiple parallel tracks showing activities of different personnel, equipment movements, and environmental conditions simultaneously. Events on the timeline must be linkable to evidence items (photographs, CCTV timestamps, vessel tracking data, equipment logs, witness statements) for corroboration. The tool must support zooming into specific time periods and expanding or collapsing detail levels. Time entries must display in the entity's local timezone with UTC reference and support both Gregorian and Hijri date display. AI must assist in timeline construction by automatically extracting time-stamped events from uploaded evidence and suggesting chronological ordering. Gaps in the timeline must be highlighted for investigators to focus additional evidence-gathering efforts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R010	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Corrective Action Recommendation and Tracking	The system must generate corrective action recommendations from investigation findings and track their implementation to closure. Each recommendation must be classified by the hierarchy of controls: elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, and PPE (least preferred). Recommendations must include a description in Arabic and English, responsible entity and person, target implementation date in Gregorian and Hijri formats, estimated cost in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR) with USD equivalent, priority level, and verification criteria. AI must suggest corrective actions based on root causes identified during investigation, drawing from a knowledge base of proven interventions for similar incidents in container terminal operations. The system must track implementation progress with milestone updates, verification audits, and effectiveness reviews at 30, 60, and 90 days post-implementation. Overdue corrective actions must trigger automated escalation through the entity's management hierarchy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R011	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Investigation Report Generation	The system must generate comprehensive investigation reports in a standardized format configurable per entity. Reports must include executive summary, incident description, investigation methodology, evidence summary, timeline of events, root cause analysis results (5-Why, fishbone, fault tree, or barrier analysis as applicable), findings, recommendations, and appendices with supporting evidence. Reports must be generated in bilingual Arabic-English format with proper RTL layout for Arabic sections. The system must support a structured review and approval workflow with configurable levels: investigator draft, lead investigator review, HSSE manager approval, and entity management sign-off. Each review level must allow tracked comments and revision requests. Final approved reports must be digitally signed and stored in an immutable archive. AI must assist in drafting report sections by synthesizing investigation data, generating narrative summaries, and ensuring completeness against the entity's reporting standard checklist.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R012	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Regulatory Incident Notification and Reporting	The system must manage mandatory incident notifications to regulatory authorities across all four jurisdictions. For Qatar, this includes notifications to the Ministry of Labour and Qatar Port Management Company. For UAE, this includes Abu Dhabi Ports Authority for Khalifa Port and Dubai Maritime City Authority for Jebel Ali. For KSA, this includes the General Authority for Ports (Mawani) and Ministry of Human Resources. For India, JNPT incidents must be reported to the Directorate General of Factory Advice Service and Dock Safety (DGFASLI). The system must automatically identify reportable incidents based on jurisdiction-specific criteria (fatality, hospitalization, dangerous occurrence, specified injury), pre-populate notification forms in the required format and official language, track notification submission deadlines (typically within 24 hours for serious incidents), and maintain proof of timely submission. Failure to meet notification deadlines must trigger immediate executive-level alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R013	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Cross-Entity Incident Learning and Safety Alerts	The system must facilitate cross-entity learning from incident investigations through structured safety alert distribution and lessons-learned sharing. When a significant investigation is concluded at one entity, the lead investigator must be able to create a safety alert containing the incident summary (anonymized as appropriate), root causes, key findings, and recommended preventive actions for other entities. Safety alerts must be automatically translated into Arabic and English and distributed to relevant personnel at all entities based on configurable distribution rules (matching by operation type, equipment category, or risk profile). The system must track alert receipt, acknowledgment, and action implementation at each receiving entity. AI must proactively identify investigations from one entity that are relevant to other entities based on operational similarity and risk profile matching, automatically suggesting cross-entity alert creation to investigators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R014	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	AI-Assisted Causal Factor Identification	The system must implement an AI engine that assists investigators in identifying causal factors by analyzing the incident description, evidence, witness statements, and contextual data. The AI must suggest potential causal factors categorized as immediate causes (substandard acts, substandard conditions), underlying causes (personal factors, job factors), and organizational factors (management system deficiencies, cultural issues). Suggestions must be drawn from a knowledge base trained on historical incidents across all four entities and supplemented with industry-wide container shipping incident data. The AI must calculate confidence scores for each suggested causal factor and provide supporting reasoning with references to similar historical incidents. Investigators must be able to accept, modify, or reject AI suggestions with documented rationale. The AI model must continuously improve by learning from investigator feedback, with accuracy metrics tracked and reported quarterly to the HSSE leadership team.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R015	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Incident Cost Tracking and Financial Impact Analysis	The system must track all costs associated with incidents and their investigation including direct costs (medical expenses, equipment repair, environmental cleanup, cargo claims, regulatory fines) and indirect costs (operational delays, overtime, temporary labor, investigation resources, legal fees, reputation impact). Costs must be recorded in the entity's local currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with automatic conversion to USD at the transaction date exchange rate for consolidated reporting. The system must calculate cost ratios including cost per incident by type, cost per TEU, and cost as a percentage of revenue. Insurance recovery must be tracked against each cost item, showing net financial impact after claims settlement. AI must project total incident costs at an early investigation stage based on incident characteristics and historical cost data, helping management allocate appropriate resources for investigation and recovery. Financial impact reports must integrate with the ERP finance module for general ledger posting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R016	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Investigation Quality Assurance and Peer Review	The system must implement a quality assurance process for incident investigations to ensure thoroughness, objectivity, and consistency across all entities. Each completed investigation must undergo a structured quality review evaluating evidence adequacy, root cause analysis depth, finding specificity, recommendation practicality, and report completeness against a configurable scorecard with minimum 20 criteria. For Level 3 investigations, a mandatory peer review by an independent investigator from a different entity must be conducted before report finalization. The system must track investigation quality scores over time per investigator, entity, and port to identify training needs and best practices. AI must pre-screen investigation reports for common quality gaps such as stopping at symptoms rather than root causes, insufficient evidence for findings, and recommendations that do not address identified root causes. Quality metrics must include average investigation duration, evidence-to-finding traceability ratio, and recommendation implementation success rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R017	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Incident Trend Analysis and Predictive Analytics	The system must perform continuous trend analysis on incident data across all entities identifying patterns by incident type, location, time period, equipment, personnel demographics, operational conditions, and root cause categories. Trend analysis must calculate statistical significance to differentiate genuine trends from random variation. Predictive analytics must forecast future incident probabilities based on leading indicators including near-miss frequency trends, inspection finding rates, maintenance backlog levels, training compliance gaps, and operational volume changes. Predictions must be specific to each port and entity with confidence intervals displayed on trend dashboards. AI must generate quarterly trend reports with natural language narratives in both Arabic and English, highlighting the most significant patterns and recommending targeted interventions. The system must support Gregorian and Hijri calendar periods for all trend analysis with the ability to overlay religious and national holiday periods to assess their correlation with incident patterns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-1-004-R018	FEAT-060-1-004	MOD-060	Integration with Insurance and Claims Module	The system must integrate bidirectionally with the insurance and claims management module within the ERP to streamline the post-incident claims process. When an incident is reported, the system must automatically identify applicable insurance policies based on incident type, location, affected assets (containers, equipment, cargo, vessels), and entity. The investigation report, evidence repository, and cost tracking data must be shareable with the claims management team through controlled access permissions. Key investigation milestones (root cause determination, liability assessment, corrective action status) must automatically update the related insurance claim file. For cargo damage incidents, the system must link to specific container numbers, bill of lading references, INCOTERMS delivery terms (CIF, FOB, CFR, FCA, DDP), and cargo valuation in the original transaction currency with USD equivalent. Subrogation opportunities must be flagged when investigation findings indicate third-party liability, with supporting evidence packaged for legal review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R001	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Multi-Factor Vessel Risk Score Computation	The system must compute a composite vessel risk score on a 0-100 scale by evaluating at minimum twelve discrete factors: vessel age, flag state risk tier, classification society rating, ISM Code compliance status, Port State Control detention history (last 36 months), crew certification validity, P&I club tier membership, hull condition survey recency, machinery breakdown frequency, SIRE/CDI inspection grades, trade area war-risk exposure, and sanctions screening results. Each factor must carry configurable weighting per P&I club (e.g., Gard, Skuld, Standard Club, West of England). The AI model must retrain weekly using updated casualty and claims data from Lloyd's List Intelligence. Scores must be recalculated upon any change event such as vessel detention at Hamad Port (QAHMD), Khalifa Port (AEKLP), Jebel Ali (AEJEA), or JNPT (INNSA).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R002	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Cargo-Specific Risk Classification Engine	The system must classify cargo risk using IMO Dangerous Goods classes (1-9), IMDG Code special provisions, reefer cargo sensitivity indices, and cargo value tiers. For container shipping specifically, the engine must differentiate risk profiles for 20ft (TEU), 40ft (FEU), 40ft high-cube, open-top, flat-rack, tank containers, and reefer units. Cargo declared under INCOTERMS 2020 terms CIF, CIP, and DDP must automatically receive elevated risk scores due to seller-borne insurance obligations. The system must maintain a cargo risk database covering at minimum 8,000 HS code categories with historical loss ratios sourced from P&I club circulars and TT Club loss prevention bulletins.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R004	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Claims History Pattern Recognition	The AI must analyze historical P&I claims data across all four entity jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) to identify recurring loss patterns by vessel type, trade route, season, cargo category, and terminal operator. The pattern recognition engine must detect statistical anomalies indicating potential fraud (e.g., repeated cargo damage claims on the same vessel-route combination exceeding three standard deviations from the mean). Claims data must be stored with Hijri and Gregorian date dual-indexing to support Saudi and Qatari regulatory reporting requirements. The system must generate pattern reports in both Arabic and English with RTL-compliant chart rendering and support export to PDF and Excel formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R005	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Premium Optimization Recommendation Engine	The system must generate AI-driven recommendations to reduce P&I insurance premiums by suggesting operational changes such as route modifications, vessel substitutions, cargo stowage plan adjustments, and enhanced survey schedules. Recommendations must quantify the expected premium reduction in all supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) and project annual savings across each legal entity. The engine must consider each entity's claims-to-premium ratio, deductible structures, and pool contribution tiers. Recommendations must comply with UAE Insurance Authority Regulation, SAMA insurance directives for KSA, QCB guidelines for Qatar, and IRDAI circulars for India. Each recommendation must include a confidence score and an implementation difficulty rating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R006	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Real-Time Risk Score Event Triggers	The system must automatically recalculate risk scores in real-time when triggered by specific events including: Port State Control inspection results, vessel casualty reports from IMO GISIS, cargo damage notifications, weather alerts for Arabian Gulf and Indian Ocean regions, JWC listed area updates, sanctions list changes (OFAC, EU, UN), crew change events, and class society condition-of-class notations. Event triggers must be processed within 60 seconds of receipt. The system must support webhook-based event ingestion from external maritime data providers and must queue events for processing when operating in offline mode at remote port locations, automatically reconciling scores upon connectivity restoration.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R007	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	P&I Club API Integration Layer	The system must integrate via secure APIs with at least five major P&I clubs (Gard, Standard Club, Britannia, West of England, Steamship Mutual) for bidirectional data exchange including policy details, claims submissions, loss prevention circulars, and fleet risk assessments. API communication must use mutual TLS authentication with certificate rotation every 90 days. Data payloads must conform to ACORD messaging standards for marine insurance where applicable. The integration layer must handle API rate limiting, implement exponential backoff retry logic, and maintain a local cache of policy data for offline operations at Hamad Port and JNPT where connectivity may be intermittent. All transmitted data must be encrypted at rest and in transit complying with Qatar PDPL and India DPDP Act requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R008	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Multi-Entity Risk Aggregation View	The system must provide a consolidated risk aggregation dashboard that displays combined and individual entity risk profiles for Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India operations. The aggregation must calculate group-level risk metrics including total insured value, aggregate deductible exposure, combined claims frequency, and pooled risk retention levels. Currency conversion for aggregation must use configurable rate sources (Central Bank of Qatar, UAE Central Bank, SAMA, RBI) with intraday refresh capability. The view must support drill-down from group level to entity level to individual vessel or voyage level. All numerical displays must support Arabic-Indic numeral rendering (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) alongside Western numerals based on user locale preference.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R009	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Predictive Loss Modeling with Monte Carlo Simulation	The system must implement Monte Carlo simulation capabilities to model potential loss scenarios across the fleet and cargo portfolio. Simulations must run at minimum 10,000 iterations per scenario, incorporating variables for vessel casualty probability, cargo damage rates, third-party liability exposure, pollution incident likelihood, and crew injury frequency. The model must account for regional-specific risks including shamal wind events in the Arabian Gulf, monsoon disruption for JNPT-bound voyages, and Strait of Hormuz transit risks. Results must be presented as probability distribution curves with P10, P50, P90, and P99 loss estimates denominated in the user's preferred currency. Simulation parameters must be auditable with full version history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R010	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Underwriting Data Automated Submission	The system must automate the preparation and submission of annual P&I club underwriting data packages including fleet lists, entered tonnage declarations, trading patterns, crew matrices, ISM audit results, and five-year claims records. Submission packages must be generated in formats required by each P&I club with auto-populated fields from the ERP master data. The system must track submission deadlines using both Gregorian and Hijri calendars, sending reminders at 60, 30, and 14 days before renewal. For the KSA entity, packages must include Arabic-language translated summaries as required by SAMA. Data validation must ensure completeness against each club's minimum data requirements before allowing submission.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R011	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Risk Score Audit Trail and Explainability	Every risk score generated by the AI must include a full explainability report detailing which input factors contributed to the score, their individual weights, and how the final composite score was derived. The audit trail must capture the model version used, input data snapshot, scoring timestamp in UTC and local timezone (AST for Qatar, GST for UAE, AST for KSA, IST for India), and the identity of any user who manually overrode the AI score. Explainability reports must comply with UAE AI governance principles and India's forthcoming AI regulatory framework. Reports must be retrievable for a minimum of seven years to satisfy P&I club record retention requirements and must be exportable in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R012	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Sanctions and Compliance Risk Layer	The risk scoring engine must incorporate a dedicated sanctions and compliance risk layer that screens all vessels, beneficial owners, charterers, cargo shippers, and consignees against OFAC SDN list, EU consolidated sanctions, UN Security Council sanctions, Qatar National Anti-Money Laundering Committee lists, UAE Executive Office of AML/CFT lists, and Indian MHA entity lists. Screening must occur at booking creation, bill of lading issuance, and cargo release stages. Any positive or fuzzy match (Levenshtein distance ≤ 2) must automatically elevate the risk score to critical level, block the transaction, and generate an alert to the compliance officer. The system must maintain screening audit logs for seven years minimum.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R013	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Weather and Environmental Risk Integration	The system must integrate real-time weather data feeds from at least three meteorological services including Qatar Meteorology Department, India Meteorological Department, and a global maritime weather provider such as DTN or StormGeo. Weather risk factors must be incorporated into vessel and voyage risk scores including tropical cyclone tracks, shamal wind forecasts, fog probability at port approaches, sea state indices, and extreme temperature alerts affecting reefer container cargo integrity. The system must calculate estimated weather delay probabilities for each active voyage and adjust risk scores accordingly. Weather data must be cached locally for 24-hour offline operation and displayed on map overlays with bilingual Arabic/English legends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R014	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Risk Score Threshold Alert Configuration	The system must allow administrators to configure risk score thresholds at entity, port, vessel, and cargo category levels with associated alert actions. Threshold levels must include Low (0-25), Medium (26-50), Elevated (51-75), and Critical (76-100) with each level triggering configurable actions such as email notification, SMS alert, workflow escalation, booking hold, or automatic P&I club notification. Threshold configurations must support multi-entity inheritance where group-level thresholds cascade to entity-level unless overridden. Alert messages must be generated in the recipient's preferred language (Arabic or English). The system must support alert fatigue management by suppressing duplicate alerts within a configurable time window (default 4 hours).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R015	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Historical Risk Score Trend Analysis	The system must maintain a complete historical record of all risk scores and provide trend analysis capabilities showing risk score evolution over configurable time periods (weekly, monthly, quarterly, annually) with support for both Hijri and Gregorian calendar periodization. Trend analysis must identify statistically significant risk score changes using CUSUM and EWMA control chart methodologies. The system must generate automated narrative summaries in Arabic and English explaining risk trend drivers such as fleet age progression, trade pattern shifts, or regulatory environment changes. Trend data must be exportable in CSV, Excel, and JSON formats and must support API access for integration with external business intelligence platforms used by the board of directors.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-001-R016	FEAT-060-2-001	MOD-060	Peer Benchmarking Risk Comparison	The system must provide peer benchmarking capabilities comparing the organization's risk profile against anonymized industry averages sourced from P&I club annual reports, IUMI statistics, and Cefor Nordic marine insurance data. Benchmarking dimensions must include claims frequency per entered tonnage, average claim cost by category (cargo, collision, pollution, crew), loss ratio trends, and risk score distributions. The system must normalize benchmarking data to account for fleet composition differences (container vs. bulk vs. tanker) and regional trading pattern variations. Benchmarking dashboards must display data in the user's preferred currency and language, with trend comparisons over three-year and five-year horizons.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R001	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Document Lifecycle Management	The system must manage the full lifecycle of business continuity plan documents including creation, review, approval, publication, periodic revision, and retirement. Each BCP document must follow a versioning scheme with major and minor version numbers, track all changes with detailed revision history, and require multi-level approval workflows configurable per entity (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). Documents must support dual-language content in Arabic and English with synchronized version control across both languages. The system must enforce mandatory review cycles (minimum annually or upon significant operational change) and generate automated reminders using both Hijri and Gregorian calendar scheduling. Retired documents must be archived with full traceability for a minimum of ten years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R002	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Business Impact Analysis Module	The system must provide a structured Business Impact Analysis (BIA) framework that identifies and quantifies the impact of disruptions to critical container shipping processes including vessel operations, terminal handling, customs clearance, documentation processing, and customer service. The BIA must calculate Recovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) for each critical process at every port location (Hamad Port QAHMD, Khalifa Port AEKLP, Jebel Ali AEJEA, JNPT INNSA). Financial impact must be quantified in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and consolidated in USD. The BIA must identify interdependencies between processes and entities, mapping upstream and downstream impacts of each disruption scenario on container throughput and revenue.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R003	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Scenario-Based BCP Template Library	The system must provide a pre-built library of at minimum 25 BCP scenario templates specific to container shipping operations in the Gulf and Indian subcontinent region. Templates must cover scenarios including port closure due to shamal storms, Strait of Hormuz blockage, terminal equipment failure, cyber-attack on port community systems, pandemic-related crew restrictions, sanctions-related trade disruption, customs system outage at JNPT, labor strike, vessel collision in port approach channel, and dangerous goods incident. Each template must include predefined response procedures, communication trees, resource requirements, and recovery milestones. Templates must be customizable per entity and port while maintaining a minimum standard structure compliant with ISO 22301 Business Continuity Management Systems.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R004	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Exercise and Drill Scheduling	The system must manage the scheduling, execution, and evaluation of BCP exercises including tabletop exercises, functional drills, and full-scale simulations. Exercise scheduling must support recurring patterns using both Hijri and Gregorian calendars with conflict detection against port operational schedules and Islamic holidays (Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha). The system must track participant attendance, record exercise observations, capture lessons learned, and generate post-exercise reports with improvement action items. Each entity must complete a minimum of two tabletop exercises and one functional drill per year as tracked by the system. Exercise evaluation criteria must be configurable and must measure response time, communication effectiveness, decision-making quality, and resource mobilization efficiency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R003	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Email Verification Flow	On registration, send verification email with signed token (24h TTL). On click, verify token, activate account, redirect to onboarding. Resend option with 60s cooldown. Track email_verified_at timestamp.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R005	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Activation and Escalation Workflow	The system must provide a one-click BCP activation mechanism with predefined escalation workflows that vary by incident severity level (Level 1: Localized, Level 2: Entity-Wide, Level 3: Multi-Entity, Level 4: Enterprise-Critical). Upon activation, the system must automatically notify all designated personnel via SMS, email, push notification, and WhatsApp integration based on the communication tree. Escalation must follow time-based rules where if a Level 1 incident is not resolved within the defined RTO, it automatically escalates to Level 2. The system must support activation from mobile devices in offline mode with synchronization upon connectivity restoration. Activation authority must be role-based with entity-specific delegation chains supporting acting-authority designations during Hajj and annual leave periods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R006	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Critical Resource and Alternate Site Registry	The system must maintain a comprehensive registry of critical resources required for business continuity including alternate office locations, backup IT infrastructure, emergency equipment, key personnel with specialized skills, critical vendor contacts, and alternate port facilities. Each resource must be tagged with geographic location, availability status, capacity limits, and activation lead time. For container shipping operations, the registry must include alternate vessel berth allocations at nearby ports (e.g., Hamad Port as alternate for Jebel Ali and vice versa), backup container yard facilities, emergency crane and handling equipment sources, and alternate customs broker contacts. Resource availability must be validated quarterly with automated status checks and the registry must support Arabic and English data entry.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R007	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Regulatory Compliance Mapping for BCP	The system must map BCP requirements against applicable regulatory frameworks for each operating jurisdiction including Qatar Civil Defence requirements, UAE National Emergency Crisis and Disaster Management Authority (NCEMA) guidelines, Saudi Civil Defense regulations, Indian National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA) guidelines, and port-specific requirements from Mwani (Qatar), AD Ports (UAE), Saudi Ports Authority, and JNPT administration. The system must track compliance status against each requirement, highlight gaps, and generate remediation action plans. Compliance status must be reportable by entity, by port, and by regulatory framework. The system must automatically monitor for regulatory updates and flag new or changed requirements within 48 hours of publication, notifying the BCP coordinator in the relevant entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R008	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Supply Chain Continuity Impact Mapping	The system must model and visualize the container shipping supply chain to identify continuity risks at each node including origin ports, transshipment hubs, destination ports, inland container depots, and last-mile delivery networks. The impact map must show real-time container flow volumes, identify single points of failure, and calculate the cascading impact of disruptions at any node on overall supply chain performance. For the Gulf-India corridor specifically, the system must model the impact of Strait of Hormuz disruption on container rerouting via Suez or Cape of Good Hope, including cost implications in USD, AED, QAR, SAR, and INR. The map must integrate with AIS vessel tracking data and port congestion indices to provide dynamic risk assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R009	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Communication Plan Management	The system must manage structured communication plans for each BCP scenario including internal stakeholder notification sequences, external party communication protocols (customers, port authorities, P&I clubs, regulatory bodies), and media response guidelines. Communication templates must be pre-drafted in both Arabic and English with placeholder fields for incident-specific details. The system must support automated message dispatch via multiple channels (email, SMS, WhatsApp Business API, satellite phone for vessel communication) with delivery confirmation tracking. Communication plans must define spokesperson authority levels per entity and scenario severity, with escalation to group-level communication for Level 3 and Level 4 incidents. The system must log all communications with timestamps for post-incident review and regulatory reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R010	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	IT Disaster Recovery Plan Integration	The system must integrate with IT disaster recovery plans covering all critical shipping ERP modules, ensuring that technology recovery procedures align with business recovery priorities defined in the BIA. The integration must track RPO and RTO for each IT system component including the container booking system, bill of lading processing, customs EDI interfaces (Qatar Single Window, UAE Mirsal-2, KSA FASAH, Indian ICEGATE), vessel planning systems, and financial transaction processing. The system must validate that IT DR test results demonstrate recovery within stated RTO/RPO targets and flag any gaps. Backup and recovery procedures must account for data sovereignty requirements where Qatar entity data must be recoverable from within Qatar, and Indian entity data from within India as per respective data localization regulations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R011	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Pandemic and Health Emergency BCP Module	The system must provide specialized BCP procedures for pandemic and health emergency scenarios reflecting lessons learned from COVID-19 impact on container shipping. Procedures must address crew change restrictions at all four port locations, shore leave policy modifications, remote working arrangements for office-based staff, reduced terminal workforce operations, and cargo disinfection protocols. The module must track entity-specific government health directives and quarantine requirements, maintaining a regulatory timeline with both Hijri and Gregorian dates. The system must model the impact of workforce reduction scenarios (25%, 50%, 75% capacity) on container throughput at each port and generate staffing contingency plans. Integration with port health authority systems at Hamad Port and JNPT must be supported for real-time health alert monitoring.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R012	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Maturity Assessment Framework	The system must implement a BCP maturity assessment framework based on ISO 22301 with five maturity levels: Initial, Managed, Defined, Quantitatively Managed, and Optimizing. The assessment must evaluate BCP capabilities across dimensions including governance, risk assessment, strategy, plan development, exercise program, and continuous improvement. Each entity must be independently assessed with results aggregated at group level. The assessment must generate gap analysis reports identifying specific improvement actions required to advance to the next maturity level. Assessment questionnaires must be available in Arabic and English, with scoring algorithms that weight dimensions based on entity-specific risk profiles. Assessment history must be maintained to demonstrate maturity progression over time for board reporting and P&I club due diligence purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R013	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Vendor and Third-Party BCP Assessment	The system must manage the assessment and monitoring of business continuity capabilities of critical third-party vendors and service providers including terminal operators, customs brokers, freight forwarders, trucking companies, container depot operators, and IT service providers across all four jurisdictions. Assessment questionnaires must evaluate vendor BCP maturity, financial stability, geographic diversification, and recovery capabilities. The system must maintain a vendor risk register with continuity risk scores and trigger reassessment when vendor risk indicators change (e.g., financial distress, ownership change, regulatory action). For each critical vendor, the system must track alternate vendor identification with pre-negotiated contingency agreements. Vendor assessments must comply with Qatar Financial Centre Authority outsourcing guidelines and RBI outsourcing directives for the India entity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R014	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Incident Log and Post-Incident Review Tracking	The system must maintain a comprehensive incident log recording all business continuity events including near-misses, partial activations, and full BCP activations. Each incident record must capture incident description, severity classification, affected entities and ports, activation timeline, response actions taken, resources deployed, financial impact in local currency and USD, root cause analysis findings, and corrective actions identified. The system must support post-incident review workflows with mandatory participation tracking and action item assignment with due dates. Lessons learned must be automatically cross-referenced with existing BCP documents to identify required updates. Incident data must be analyzable across entities, time periods (supporting both Hijri and Gregorian grouping), incident categories, and root causes to identify systemic weaknesses.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R015	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	BCP Document Access Control and Distribution	The system must enforce granular access control on BCP documents based on user role, entity affiliation, security clearance level, and need-to-know principle. Sensitive BCPs containing security-related procedures must support classification levels (Unrestricted, Internal, Confidential, Strictly Confidential). Document distribution must support controlled digital copies with read-receipt tracking and must prevent unauthorized printing, downloading, or forwarding of classified plans. Offline access must be provided for designated emergency response team members through a secure mobile application that caches encrypted BCP documents locally with automatic expiry after 30 days without server synchronization. The system must maintain a distribution matrix showing which personnel have access to which plans and must automatically revoke access upon role change or employment termination.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R016	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	AI-Powered BCP Gap Detection	The system must use AI analysis to continuously evaluate existing BCPs against current operational reality and identify coverage gaps. The AI must compare BCP assumptions (staffing levels, system dependencies, vendor relationships, port operating procedures) against actual operational data from the ERP to detect misalignments. For example, if a new customs EDI interface has been deployed at Khalifa Port but the IT DR plan still references the legacy system, the AI must flag this gap. The AI must also analyze industry incident reports and maritime casualty databases to identify emerging threat scenarios not covered by existing BCPs. Gap detection results must be presented with severity ratings and remediation recommendations in both Arabic and English, with automated workflow creation for gap closure actions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-002-R017	FEAT-060-2-002	MOD-060	Cross-Entity BCP Coordination Protocol	The system must manage cross-entity coordination protocols for incidents that affect multiple operating entities simultaneously, such as a regional geopolitical event impacting both Qatar and UAE operations or a global shipping line disruption affecting all four entities. The coordination protocol must define a unified command structure, shared resource allocation rules, inter-entity communication channels, and consolidated stakeholder reporting. The system must handle timezone differences across entities (AST/GST for Gulf, IST for India) when scheduling coordination calls and setting response deadlines. Cross-entity financial impact must be consolidated in USD with entity-level breakdowns in local currencies. The protocol must address potential conflicts of interest where continuity actions for one entity may adversely impact another entity's operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R001	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Procedure Document Repository	The system must maintain a structured repository of emergency response procedures organized by category (vessel emergency, terminal emergency, cargo emergency, security threat, natural disaster, IT/cyber incident), severity level, and applicable port location. Each procedure document must contain a title, scope, applicability criteria, step-by-step response actions, required resources, escalation contacts, and regulatory references. All procedures must be available in both Arabic and English with synchronized updates across languages. The repository must support full-text search with Arabic morphological analysis and English stemming. Document storage must comply with data residency requirements where Qatar-specific procedures are stored on Qatar-hosted infrastructure and India-specific procedures on India-hosted infrastructure.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R002	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Dangerous Goods Emergency Response Integration	The system must provide immediate access to emergency response procedures for all nine IMO Dangerous Goods classes with specific guidance linked to IMDG Code Emergency Schedules (EmS). When a dangerous goods incident is reported, the system must automatically retrieve the relevant EmS based on the UN number, proper shipping name, and container position from the vessel stowage plan or terminal yard plan. The system must integrate with the ERP's container tracking module to identify all containers carrying dangerous goods within a defined radius of the incident location. Emergency response procedures must include MFAG (Medical First Aid Guide) references for crew exposure scenarios. The system must support offline access to DG emergency data at all port locations, with the complete IMDG Code supplement cached locally on emergency response team mobile devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R003	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Port-Specific Emergency Procedure Customization	The system must support port-specific customization of emergency procedures to reflect the unique operational characteristics, regulatory requirements, and emergency service contacts at each port location. For Hamad Port (QAHMD), procedures must reference Qatar Civil Defence coordination protocols and Mwani emergency contact chains. For Khalifa Port (AEKLP) and Jebel Ali (AEJEA), procedures must align with AD Ports Group and DP World emergency management frameworks respectively. For JNPT (INNSA), procedures must integrate with the JNPT Disaster Management Plan and reference Indian Coast Guard coordination procedures. Each port procedure must include site maps, muster point locations, emergency equipment inventories, and local emergency service response times. Customization must be controlled via approval workflows with port-level safety officer sign-off.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R004	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Interactive Emergency Response Flowcharts	The system must provide interactive digital flowcharts that guide emergency responders through decision-based response procedures step by step. Each flowchart must present binary or multiple-choice decision points that lead to the appropriate next action based on the responder's input about the situation. Flowcharts must be accessible on mobile devices with touch-optimized interfaces and must support offline operation. All flowchart text must be available in Arabic and English with the ability to switch languages mid-procedure without losing progress. Flowcharts must capture the responder's path through the decision tree for post-incident analysis. The system must support flowchart creation and editing through a visual drag-and-drop designer accessible to safety officers without programming knowledge, with mandatory review and approval workflow before publication.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R005	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Contact Directory Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive emergency contact directory covering all internal and external contacts required during emergency response across all four jurisdictions. Contact categories must include internal emergency response team members, port authority emergency desks (Mwani, AD Ports, DP World, JNPT), coast guard (Qatar Coast Guard, UAE Critical Infrastructure and Coastal Protection Authority, Saudi Border Guard, Indian Coast Guard), P&I club emergency hotlines, classification society surveyors, salvage companies, oil spill response organizations (MEMAC for Gulf region), customs authorities, and medical facilities near each port. Each contact must include multiple communication channels (phone, mobile, satellite phone, email, radio frequency). The directory must be validated quarterly with automated outreach to confirm contact accuracy and must support one-click dialing from mobile devices.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R006	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Response Team Role Assignment	The system must manage emergency response team composition with defined roles including Incident Commander, Operations Chief, Safety Officer, Communications Lead, Logistics Coordinator, and liaison roles for port authority, regulatory bodies, and media. Role assignments must be configurable per entity and port location with primary, secondary, and tertiary designees for each role. The system must automatically check role holder availability based on duty roster integration, leave management data, and travel schedules, escalating to the next designee if the primary is unavailable. Role-specific procedure checklists must be presented to each team member upon emergency activation. The system must track qualifications and training certifications required for each role and alert when certifications are expiring, with training records maintained in both Hijri and Gregorian date formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R007	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Container Spill and Pollution Response Procedures	The system must provide specialized response procedures for container cargo spills and pollution incidents at terminal and vessel locations. Procedures must be differentiated by pollutant type (chemical, petroleum, biological, radioactive) with specific containment, cleanup, and disposal instructions referencing OPRC Convention requirements and regional agreements such as the Kuwait Regional Convention (ROPME). The system must calculate potential pollution spread models based on wind, current, and tide data for each port location, integrating with meteorological data feeds. Financial liability estimation must be provided in real-time showing potential fines and cleanup costs in local currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and USD, cross-referencing CLC and Bunkers Convention limits. Procedures must include regulatory notification requirements with jurisdiction-specific reporting timelines and forms for Qatar MOEI, UAE MOCCAE, Saudi GAMEP, and Indian CPCB.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R008	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Security Threat Response Procedures (ISPS Compliance)	The system must maintain security threat response procedures compliant with ISPS Code requirements at all three MARSEC levels. Procedures must cover scenarios including bomb threat, unauthorized access, stowaways, piracy and armed robbery, cyber-attack on port systems, and suspicious cargo detection. Each procedure must reference the port facility security plan (PFSP) and vessel security plan (SSP) requirements. The system must support rapid MARSEC level change notifications across all entities and ports with automatic procedure set switching based on the declared security level. Security procedures must integrate with port CCTV and access control systems where API access is available. Access to security procedures must be restricted to security-cleared personnel with multi-factor authentication, and procedure content must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption. Procedure review must occur every six months or upon any security incident.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R009	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Fire and Explosion Emergency Response Procedures	The system must provide comprehensive fire and explosion response procedures specific to container terminal and vessel environments. Procedures must differentiate between container yard fires (addressing stacked container access challenges), vessel hold fires, reefer container electrical fires, dangerous goods fires requiring specialized suppression agents, and office/warehouse structure fires. Each procedure must reference the specific fire suppression systems available at each port location with equipment locations mapped on interactive terminal layouts. The system must integrate with fire detection sensor data where available to provide automatic incident detection and procedure triggering. Response procedures must include evacuation route guidance with real-time crowd management considering container yard layouts at Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, Jebel Ali, and JNPT. Procedures must comply with NFPA standards and local fire safety regulations in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R010	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Natural Disaster Response Procedures for Regional Hazards	The system must maintain response procedures for natural disaster scenarios specific to the Gulf and Indian subcontinent region including shamal dust storms, tropical cyclones (particularly for JNPT during Arabian Sea cyclone season), extreme heat events exceeding 50°C affecting terminal operations, flooding from unseasonal rainfall, and seismic events. Each natural disaster procedure must include pre-event preparation checklists, during-event response actions, and post-event recovery procedures. The system must integrate with regional meteorological services for early warning alerts and automatically pre-activate relevant procedures when warning thresholds are reached. Procedures must address container securing protocols for high-wind events including maximum safe wind speeds for crane operations and container stacking limits, referencing terminal operator specifications at each port.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R011	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Response Training Record Management	The system must track all emergency response training completed by personnel across all four entities including STCW certifications for maritime staff, first aid qualifications, fire warden training, dangerous goods handling (IMDG Code), ISPS security awareness, and oil spill response (OPRC Level 1/2/3). Training records must capture certification date, expiry date (in both Hijri and Gregorian formats), certifying body, and training provider. The system must generate automated alerts 90, 60, and 30 days before certification expiry and block assignment of personnel to emergency response roles if mandatory certifications have expired. Training compliance must be reportable by entity, port, role, and certification type. The system must identify training gaps by comparing role requirements against current certification status and generate training needs analysis reports in Arabic and English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R012	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Mobile Emergency Response Application	The system must provide a dedicated mobile application (iOS and Android) for emergency response team members that provides instant access to all emergency procedures, contact directories, and interactive flowcharts. The mobile app must function fully offline with automatic synchronization when connectivity is restored, supporting environments at JNPT and remote port areas where cellular coverage may be intermittent. The app must support push notifications for emergency activations with priority override of device do-not-disturb settings. Location services must enable the app to automatically present procedures relevant to the user's current port location. The app interface must support Arabic RTL layout and English LTR layout with instant language switching. The app must include a panic button feature that immediately notifies the port emergency coordinator with the user's GPS location and allows voice recording of situation reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R013	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Regulatory Notification Automation	The system must automate regulatory notification requirements during emergency events by pre-populating notification forms with incident details and submitting to the appropriate authorities via their prescribed channels. Notification recipients must be automatically determined based on incident type, severity, and location. For maritime incidents, the system must generate and submit IMO casualty investigation notifications. For pollution incidents, notifications must be sent to the applicable environmental authority within the jurisdiction-specific mandatory timeframe (e.g., within 1 hour for Qatar MOEI, within 30 minutes for certain DG incidents at JNPT). The system must track notification acknowledgment receipts and escalate if acknowledgment is not received within defined timeframes. All notifications must be bilingual (Arabic and English) where required by the receiving authority and must be archived with full audit trail for a minimum of ten years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R014	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Resource Deployment Tracking	The system must track the deployment and status of emergency resources during an active incident including personnel assignments, equipment deployment (fire trucks, ambulances, hazmat suits, oil spill booms, portable pumps), and external service provider mobilization. Resource tracking must support real-time status updates via mobile app check-ins showing deployed, en-route, on-scene, and released states. The system must calculate resource utilization rates and identify resource shortfalls against the procedure requirements in real-time. For multi-entity incidents, resource sharing across entities must be tracked with cost allocation recorded in each entity's local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, INR). Post-incident, the system must generate resource consumption reports including equipment usage hours, consumables expended, and replacement requirements. Resource deployment data must feed into the post-incident review and BCP improvement processes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R015	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Emergency Procedure Version Control and Compliance Audit	The system must enforce strict version control on all emergency response procedures with change tracking showing additions, modifications, and deletions between versions in a diff-view format. Each procedure version must go through a mandatory review and approval workflow involving the port safety officer, entity risk manager, and group safety director before publication. The system must maintain a compliance audit schedule ensuring all procedures are reviewed against current regulations, industry best practices (OCIMF, ICHCA, TT Club guidelines), and lessons learned from recent incidents at minimum annually. Audit findings must be tracked with corrective action plans and due dates. The system must generate a compliance matrix showing each procedure's alignment status against applicable regulations in each jurisdiction. All audit records must be available in Arabic and English and stored for a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R016	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Mutual Aid Agreement Management	The system must manage mutual aid agreements with neighboring port facilities, emergency services, and industry partners for shared emergency response capabilities. For the Gulf region, the system must track agreements between Hamad Port, Khalifa Port, and Jebel Ali for mutual assistance during major incidents including shared firefighting resources, oil spill response equipment, and hazmat response teams. Agreement records must include scope of assistance, activation procedures, cost-sharing arrangements in the relevant currencies (QAR, AED, SAR), response time commitments, and liability allocation. The system must maintain agreement expiry tracking with renewal reminders at 90 and 60 days before expiry. During emergency activation, the system must identify available mutual aid resources and initiate activation requests through the prescribed communication channels. Agreement performance must be tracked against committed response times and resource availability.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-003-R017	FEAT-060-2-003	MOD-060	Post-Emergency Demobilization and Recovery Procedures	The system must provide structured demobilization procedures for transitioning from emergency response mode back to normal operations. Demobilization checklists must cover personnel release, equipment return and inspection, temporary safety measures removal, environmental clearance verification, and regulatory close-out requirements. The system must manage recovery action plans that track the restoration of normal container operations including berth reopening, yard block clearance, equipment recertification, and customer communication. Recovery milestones must be tracked against target dates with progress visible on dashboards accessible to entity management. Financial impact assessment must be compiled during recovery, capturing all direct and indirect costs in local currencies and USD for insurance claim preparation. The system must generate final incident reports in Arabic and English summarizing timeline, response effectiveness, costs incurred, and improvement recommendations for P&I club and board review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R001	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Executive Risk Dashboard Layout and Navigation	The system must provide a configurable executive risk dashboard with a grid-based layout supporting drag-and-drop widget placement. The dashboard must display key risk metrics including aggregate risk score, active incidents count, insurance claims status, BCP readiness level, and compliance posture across all four entities (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India). The dashboard must support multiple saved layouts per user role (CEO, CFO, Risk Director, Entity Manager, Board Member) with entity-level filtering. Navigation must support drill-down from group-level metrics to entity-level to port-level to individual vessel or shipment level. The dashboard must render correctly in both Arabic RTL and English LTR modes with automatic layout mirroring. Loading time must not exceed 3 seconds for initial render and 1 second for drill-down interactions, even when aggregating data across all four entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R002	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk KPI Definition and Calculation Engine	The system must support the definition, calculation, and monitoring of at minimum 40 risk KPIs organized into categories including Insurance Performance (loss ratio, claims frequency, premium adequacy), Operational Risk (vessel detentions, cargo damage rate, safety incidents per TEU handled), Compliance Risk (regulatory violations, sanctions screening hit rate, audit findings closure rate), Financial Risk (credit exposure, currency hedging effectiveness, accounts receivable aging), and Strategic Risk (market share volatility, customer concentration, trade lane dependency). Each KPI must have configurable target values, warning thresholds, and critical thresholds with entity-specific targets where applicable. KPI calculations must support both Gregorian and Hijri period definitions and must automatically convert between currencies using configurable exchange rate sources for cross-entity aggregation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R003	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Real-Time Risk Heat Map Visualization	The system must generate interactive risk heat maps displaying risk concentrations across multiple dimensions including geographic (port locations, trade lanes, country corridors), operational (vessel fleet, terminal operations, cargo categories), financial (currency exposure, counterparty risk, insurance coverage gaps), and temporal (seasonal patterns, trend trajectories). Heat maps must use a standardized color scheme with five risk levels (Very Low-green, Low-yellow-green, Medium-yellow, High-orange, Critical-red) and must be accessible to color-blind users through pattern overlays. Geographic heat maps must display on interactive maps with zoom capability from global view to individual port terminal level. Heat map data must refresh at configurable intervals (minimum every 15 minutes) and must support snapshot comparisons between current and historical states. All labels and legends must be bilingual Arabic/English.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R004	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Automated Board Risk Report Generation	The system must automatically generate board-ready risk reports on configurable schedules (monthly, quarterly, annually) aligned with each entity's board meeting calendar which may use Hijri or Gregorian date references. Reports must include executive summary, risk score trends, top 10 risk items with mitigation status, insurance portfolio performance, claims analysis, BCP readiness assessment, regulatory compliance status, and risk outlook. Report format must comply with corporate governance requirements in each jurisdiction including Qatar Commercial Companies Law, UAE Companies Law, Saudi Companies Law, and Indian Companies Act 2013. Reports must be generated in both Arabic and English as parallel documents with consistent data and formatting. The system must support automated distribution to board members via secure email with read-receipt tracking and must archive all generated reports with version control for a minimum of seven years.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R005	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk Appetite Statement Management	The system must manage the organization's risk appetite statements at group and entity levels, translating qualitative appetite expressions into quantitative thresholds that drive KPI alerting and dashboard visualizations. Risk appetite must be definable across categories including maximum acceptable loss ratio, maximum single-event loss tolerance, minimum insurance coverage levels, maximum counterparty credit exposure, and acceptable regulatory finding counts. Each entity's risk appetite must be independently configurable while remaining within group-level boundaries set by the board. The system must continuously monitor actual risk metrics against appetite thresholds and generate breach notifications when metrics approach (warning at 80%) or exceed appetite limits. Risk appetite statements must be version-controlled with board approval dates tracked in both Hijri and Gregorian calendars and must be reviewable at minimum annually.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R006	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Claims Analytics and Trend Visualization	The system must provide comprehensive claims analytics dashboards showing P&I and H&M insurance claims data across all entities with visualizations including claims frequency trends, average claim cost evolution, claims by category (cargo damage, collision, pollution, crew injury, third-party liability), claims by vessel, claims by trade lane, and claims by port. All monetary values must be displayable in any supported currency (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR) with conversion at historical rates for trend accuracy. The system must calculate and display combined ratios, incurred-but-not-reported (IBNR) estimates, and claims development triangles in actuarial standard format. Trend analysis must support period-over-period comparisons using both Hijri and Gregorian periods. The system must identify statistically significant claim trends using regression analysis and present automated narrative insights explaining trend drivers in the user's preferred language.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R007	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Regulatory Risk Disclosure Report Generation	The system must generate risk disclosure reports compliant with regulatory requirements in each jurisdiction including Qatar Financial Markets Authority (QFMA) disclosure rules, Securities and Commodities Authority (SCA) of UAE listing requirements, Capital Market Authority (CMA) of Saudi Arabia risk reporting standards, and Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) risk management framework disclosures. Reports must be formatted according to each regulator's prescribed template and must include risk management framework description, principal risks and uncertainties, risk mitigation strategies, and material risk events during the reporting period. The system must track reporting deadlines against both Hijri and Gregorian calendars with automated reminders. Reports must be generated in the regulator's required language (Arabic for QFMA, CMA; English acceptable for SCA, SEBI) and must support digital submission where regulator portals provide API access.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R008	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk Register Integration and Visualization	The system must integrate with the enterprise risk register to display risk items on the dashboard with their current risk rating (likelihood × impact), mitigation status, risk owner, and next review date. The risk register visualization must support standard risk matrix (5×5 grid) and bow-tie diagram views showing risk causes, preventive controls, risk event, mitigating controls, and consequences. Each risk must be linkable to associated KPIs, BCP plans, and emergency procedures for comprehensive risk context. The system must track risk rating changes over time with automated trend indicators (increasing, stable, decreasing risk). Risk register data must support multi-entity views with filtering by entity, risk category, risk owner, and rating level. The visualization must support Arabic and English risk descriptions simultaneously and must allow export to PowerPoint format for board presentations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R009	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Insurance Portfolio Performance Dashboard	The system must provide a dedicated insurance portfolio performance dashboard displaying all marine insurance policies across entities including P&I, H&M, cargo, loss of hire, war risk, and freight demurrage and defence (FDD) coverages. The dashboard must show policy periods, premium paid (in original currency and USD equivalent), coverage limits, deductible levels, and claims-to-premium ratios for each policy. Premium benchmarking must compare current rates against market indices and historical trends. The system must display coverage gap analysis identifying uninsured or underinsured exposures across the fleet and cargo portfolio. Policy renewal timelines must be tracked with 120, 90, 60, and 30-day reminders aligned to P&I club renewal dates (typically February 20). The dashboard must calculate total cost of risk (TCOR) per TEU handled at each port to enable inter-port performance comparison.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R010	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	AI-Generated Risk Narrative and Insights	The system must use AI to generate natural language narrative summaries of risk data, trends, and anomalies for inclusion in board reports and executive briefings. Narratives must be generated in both Arabic and English with shipping industry-appropriate terminology. The AI must identify and highlight the most significant risk developments, emerging risk patterns, and recommended management attention areas based on KPI movements and incident data. Narrative generation must contextualize data against industry benchmarks, regulatory developments, and geopolitical factors affecting Gulf and Indian subcontinent shipping operations. Each AI-generated narrative must include a confidence indicator and must be reviewable and editable by the risk manager before publication. The system must maintain a library of generated narratives for consistency analysis and must learn from risk manager edits to improve future narrative quality.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R011	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Multi-Currency Risk Exposure Reporting	The system must provide comprehensive multi-currency risk exposure reporting showing the organization's financial risk positions across all supported currencies (USD, AED, QAR, SAR, INR). Reports must display gross exposure by currency, hedging positions, net exposure, and value-at-risk (VaR) calculations at 95% and 99% confidence levels. Currency exposure must be broken down by entity, trade lane, and time horizon (30, 60, 90, 180, 360 days). The system must model the impact of currency movements on insurance costs, claims reserves, and operational expenses using scenario analysis (base case, stress case, worst case). Exchange rate assumptions must be sourced from configurable providers (Reuters, Bloomberg, central bank rates) with the ability to override with management assumptions for forward-looking reports. All currency displays must support both Western and Arabic-Indic numeral formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R012	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk Committee Meeting Management	The system must support risk committee meeting management including agenda preparation, document packaging, minutes recording, and action item tracking. Meeting agendas must be auto-populated with risk items requiring committee attention based on KPI breaches, new high-rated risks, overdue mitigation actions, and upcoming regulatory deadlines. Meeting document packages must be compiled from dashboard snapshots, risk reports, and supporting analytics and distributed securely to committee members at a configurable lead time before the meeting. The system must support meeting scheduling across multiple timezones (AST, GST, IST) with participant availability checking. Minutes must capture decisions, action items with assigned owners and due dates, and risk acceptance declarations. Action item tracking must include automated follow-up reminders and overdue escalation. Meeting records must be archived for governance compliance in each jurisdiction.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R013	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Comparative Entity Risk Performance Scorecards	The system must generate comparative risk performance scorecards that benchmark each entity's risk management effectiveness against the others and against group-level targets. Scorecards must evaluate dimensions including claims management efficiency (average claim cycle time, recovery rate), loss prevention program effectiveness (incident rate per TEU, near-miss reporting rate), insurance cost efficiency (premium per TEU, TCOR trend), compliance posture (audit findings, regulatory submissions timeliness), and BCP readiness (exercise completion rate, plan currency, maturity level). Each dimension must be scored on a standardized scale with visual indicators (traffic lights or star ratings). Scorecards must be generated quarterly and must support both Hijri and Gregorian quarter definitions. Entity rankings must be presented with context explaining performance drivers, avoiding simplistic league table comparisons that may not account for entity-specific operational complexity.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R014	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk Data Export and API Access	The system must provide comprehensive data export capabilities supporting CSV, Excel (XLSX with Arabic-compatible encoding), PDF, and JSON formats for all dashboard data, KPI results, and report content. Excel exports must preserve formatting, charts, and bilingual headers. PDF exports must support both LTR (English) and RTL (Arabic) layouts with proper font embedding for Arabic text rendering. The system must expose a RESTful API with OAuth 2.0 authentication providing programmatic access to all risk KPI data, dashboard metrics, and report content for integration with external business intelligence platforms (Power BI, Tableau, Qlik). API responses must support JSON and XML formats with pagination for large datasets. API rate limiting must be configurable per consumer with usage analytics. Data exports must respect access control permissions ensuring users can only export data they are authorized to view.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R015	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Predictive Risk Trend Forecasting Dashboard	The system must provide AI-powered predictive risk trend forecasting displaying projected risk metric trajectories for 3, 6, and 12-month horizons. Predictions must cover claims frequency forecasts, premium renewal projections, loss ratio estimates, regulatory compliance risk trajectories, and operational risk indicator trends. The forecasting model must incorporate leading indicators including fleet age progression, trade pattern changes, regulatory pipeline analysis, geopolitical risk indices for the Gulf and South Asian regions, and macroeconomic indicators affecting container shipping volumes. Forecast accuracy must be tracked by comparing predictions against actual outcomes with model performance metrics displayed on the dashboard. Forecasts must be segmented by entity with group-level consolidation in USD. The dashboard must support scenario overlays where users can adjust assumptions and visualize the impact on forecasted risk metrics.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R016	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Board Member Secure Mobile Access	The system must provide board members with secure mobile access to risk dashboards and reports through a dedicated mobile interface optimized for tablets and smartphones. Authentication must use multi-factor methods including biometric (fingerprint, face recognition) and PIN/password. The mobile interface must support offline access to the most recent board report package with encrypted local storage that auto-purges after 7 days without server contact. Board members must be able to annotate reports, flag items for discussion, and submit questions to the risk management team through the mobile interface. Push notifications must alert board members when new reports are published or when critical risk events occur. The mobile interface must support Arabic RTL and English LTR layouts with dynamic switching. Device management must allow remote wipe of cached data if a device is reported lost or stolen.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R017	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Risk Dashboard Audit and Access Logging	The system must maintain comprehensive audit logs of all dashboard access, report generation, data export, and configuration change activities. Audit logs must capture user identity, timestamp (in UTC with local timezone notation for AST, GST, IST), action performed, data accessed, filters applied, and client device/IP information. Dashboard configuration changes including KPI threshold modifications, risk appetite updates, and report template changes must require approval workflows and must be logged with before-and-after state snapshots. Audit logs must be immutable and stored for a minimum of seven years to comply with the most stringent jurisdiction requirement. The system must generate periodic access review reports for the IT security team and internal audit function. Anomalous access patterns (e.g., bulk data export, access outside normal hours, access from unusual locations) must trigger automated security alerts.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-060-2-004-R018	FEAT-060-2-004	MOD-060	Stakeholder-Specific Report Personalization	The system must support report personalization based on stakeholder role and information needs. For the CEO, reports must emphasize strategic risk outlook, enterprise risk score trends, and material risk events. For the CFO, reports must focus on insurance cost analysis, claims reserves, currency exposure, and financial impact of risk events. For entity managers, reports must highlight entity-specific operational risks, local regulatory compliance, and port-level KPIs. For P&I club relationship managers, reports must present fleet risk profiles, claims data, and loss prevention program status. Each personalized view must draw from the same underlying data to ensure consistency. The system must support creation of custom report templates by authorized users with a visual report designer supporting both Arabic and English content blocks, configurable chart types, and conditional formatting rules. Report scheduling must support timezone-aware delivery ensuring recipients receive reports during their local business hours.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R001	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	AI-Driven Import Declaration Auto-Generation	The system must automatically generate import customs declarations (Single Administrative Document equivalent) by extracting data from scanned bills of lading, commercial invoices, packing lists, and certificates of origin using AI-powered OCR and NLP. The AI engine must achieve a minimum 95% field extraction accuracy across English and Arabic documents. For Hamad Port (QAHMD), the system must generate Qatar Customs Form 1 declarations; for Jebel Ali (AEJEA) and Khalifa Port (AEKLP), UAE Customs Declaration forms; for JNPT (INNSA), Indian Bill of Entry forms. The system must support HS code auto-classification at the 8-digit level based on goods description, with confidence scoring and human review triggers when confidence drops below 85%.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R002	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Multi-Entity Customs Broker Assignment	The system must support assignment of licensed customs brokers per entity and per port jurisdiction. Each legal entity (Qatar WLL, UAE LLC, KSA CR, India Pvt Ltd) must maintain its own roster of approved customs brokers with valid license numbers, expiration dates, and port authorizations. The system must automatically select the optimal broker based on port of discharge, commodity type, broker performance score (calculated from historical clearance times and success rates), and current workload. When a broker license is within 30 days of expiry, the system must raise alerts in both English and Arabic. Broker assignments must respect entity-level segregation to prevent cross-entity data leakage in multi-tenant operations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R003	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Regulatory Document Validation Engine	The system must validate all import clearance documents against the specific regulatory requirements of each destination port before submission. For Qatar imports, the system must verify conformity certificates (QS mark compliance), health certificates for food items, and CICPA permits for restricted goods. For UAE imports, the system must validate Emirates Conformity Assessment Scheme (ECAS) certificates and halal certificates where applicable. For KSA imports, SASO conformity certificates and SFDA approvals for food and drug products must be checked. For India JNPT imports, BIS certificates, FSSAI licenses, and plant quarantine certificates must be validated. Each document must be checked for completeness, validity dates (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri calendar formats), and cross-referenced against the declared HS codes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R004	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Import Duty Pre-Assessment Calculator	The system must pre-calculate estimated import duties and taxes before formal declaration submission, enabling importers to plan cash flow and arrange payment. The calculator must apply the correct duty rates from the GCC Common External Tariff for Qatar, UAE, and KSA imports, and the Indian Customs Tariff Act schedules for JNPT imports. Calculations must handle ad valorem duties, specific duties, and compound duty structures. The system must support multi-currency assessment where CIF values may be denominated in USD, EUR, GBP, CNY, or JPY and must convert to the local currency (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) using the official customs exchange rates published by respective central banks. Anti-dumping duties and safeguard duties must be applied where applicable based on country of origin and HS code combinations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R005	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Customs Channel Selection and Risk Profiling	The system must integrate with port customs risk management systems to receive and display channel assignments (Green/Yellow/Red) for each import consignment. For Green channel shipments, the system must auto-trigger cargo release workflows without physical inspection. For Yellow channel, the system must schedule document review appointments and track reviewer assignments. For Red channel, the system must coordinate physical inspection scheduling including X-ray scanning slots at Hamad Port and Jebel Ali container examination facilities. The AI engine must maintain a predictive risk scoring model trained on historical clearance data to pre-estimate likely channel assignment with at least 80% accuracy, enabling proactive preparation of additional documentation for high-risk consignments. Risk scores must be entity-specific and not shared across organizational boundaries.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R006	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Inspection Coordination and Results Tracking	The system must manage the full inspection lifecycle for import consignments flagged for physical examination or document review. This includes scheduling inspection appointments with customs authorities, recording inspector assignments, tracking container unstuffing and restuffing operations, and capturing inspection outcomes with photographic evidence attachments. For Hamad Port, the system must integrate with Qatar Customs ASYCUDA World system for inspection scheduling. For Jebel Ali, integration with Dubai Trade Mirsal 2 platform is required. For JNPT, integration with ICEGATE examination module is mandatory. The system must track demurrage and detention charges accruing during inspection delays, calculate financial impact in local currency, and generate alerts when inspection holds exceed configurable SLA thresholds (default 48 hours for GCC ports, 72 hours for JNPT).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R007	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Cargo Release Order Generation	The system must automatically generate and transmit cargo release orders upon successful completion of all customs clearance steps including duty payment confirmation, inspection clearance (if applicable), and regulatory hold releases. Release orders must be formatted according to port-specific requirements: Qatar Customs release format for Hamad Port, Dubai Customs e-release for Jebel Ali, Abu Dhabi Customs release for Khalifa Port, and ICEGATE Out of Charge order for JNPT. Each release order must contain the unique customs reference number, container numbers (supporting 20ft, 40ft, 40ft HC, and 45ft container types per ISO 6346), seal numbers, and authorized delivery location. The system must distribute release orders electronically to terminal operators, shipping lines, and transport companies simultaneously.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R008	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Free Zone and Bonded Warehouse Clearance	The system must support specialized clearance procedures for cargo destined for free zones and bonded warehouses at each port jurisdiction. For Qatar Free Zones (QFZ Authority areas), the system must handle duty suspension declarations and maintain bond tracking. For JAFZA, KIZAD, and other UAE free zones, the system must generate appropriate free zone entry permits with duty deferral documentation. For KSA special economic zones, ZATCA-specific bonded warehouse entry forms must be produced. For JNPT, the system must support clearance into SEEPZ, MEPZ, and other Indian SEZs with appropriate customs bond executions. The system must track the duty-suspended status of all bonded inventory, calculate potential duty liability in local currency (QAR/AED/SAR/INR), and trigger re-assessment when goods are removed from bonded status for domestic consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R009	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Import Permit and License Verification	The system must verify that all required import permits and licenses are valid and on file before allowing customs declaration submission. This includes trade licenses, commercial registration numbers, importer codes (IEC for India, customs code for GCC entities), and commodity-specific permits such as arms and ammunition permits, pharmaceutical import licenses, telecommunications equipment type approvals, and food import registrations. The system must maintain a centralized permit registry per legal entity with expiration tracking, auto-renewal reminders sent 60 days before expiry, and blocking rules that prevent declaration submission when a mandatory permit has expired. Permit data must be searchable in both English and Arabic, with Arabic RTL rendering in all UI screens including the permit dashboard and search results.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R010	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Customs Clearance Status Dashboard	The system must provide a real-time customs clearance status dashboard showing all active import clearances across all four port jurisdictions. The dashboard must display clearance stage (Document Preparation, Submitted, Under Assessment, Under Inspection, Duty Payment Pending, Cleared, Released), estimated completion times based on AI predictions, and financial summaries of pending duty payments. The dashboard must support filtering by entity, port, clearing agent, date range (Gregorian and Hijri), container number, bill of lading number, and HS code chapter. All dashboard labels, column headers, and status indicators must be fully localized in English and Arabic with proper RTL layout switching. The dashboard must support offline caching of the last-synced state with visual indicators showing data freshness and auto-sync when connectivity is restored.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R011	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Customs Tariff Database Management	The system must maintain a comprehensive and up-to-date customs tariff database covering the GCC Common External Tariff, KSA national tariff amendments, UAE local tariff schedules, Qatar-specific duty rates, and the Indian Customs Tariff Act First and Second Schedules. The database must store HS codes at 8-digit granularity with effective dates, duty rates (basic customs duty, additional duty, special additional duty for India), preferential rates under applicable FTAs (GCC-Singapore FTA, India-UAE CEPA, India-GCC FTA, SAFTA), and any temporary duty modifications or anti-dumping measures. The system must auto-update tariff rates from official gazette notifications and provide version-controlled audit trails showing rate changes with effective dates. Tariff descriptions must be stored in both English and Arabic for GCC jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R012	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Prior Import Declaration and Advance Ruling	The system must support filing of prior import declarations (advance customs entries) before vessel arrival to expedite clearance. For Qatar Customs, the system must submit pre-arrival processing requests at least 48 hours before ETA at Hamad Port. For UAE, the system must support Dubai Trade advance declaration filing for Jebel Ali and Abu Dhabi Ports advance manifesting for Khalifa Port. For JNPT, the system must file advance Bill of Entry under the Indian Customs ACP (Accredited Client Programme) or AEO (Authorised Economic Operator) schemes. The system must also manage applications for advance tariff rulings, storing ruling references, binding period dates, and linking rulings to specific HS codes for automated application during declaration generation. Advance ruling requests and responses must be tracked with full audit history.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R013	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Customs Payment Processing and Reconciliation	The system must process customs duty and tax payments through authorized payment channels for each jurisdiction. For Qatar, the system must support payment through Qatar Central Bank approved channels and maintain customs deposit account balances. For UAE, integration with Dubai Customs e-payment gateway (ePay) and Abu Dhabi Customs payment portal is required. For KSA, ZATCA SADAD payment integration must be supported. For India JNPT, ICEGATE e-payment through authorized banks must be facilitated. The system must support payment in local currency only (QAR, AED, SAR, INR) and reconcile payments against duty assessments, tracking any differential duty demands or refunds. Payment receipts must be stored with customs declaration cross-references and made available in both English and Arabic formats.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R014	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Import Clearance SLA Monitoring and Escalation	The system must track clearance processing times against configurable SLA targets per port and per clearance type. Default SLA targets must be: Hamad Port Green channel 4 hours, Yellow channel 24 hours, Red channel 72 hours; Jebel Ali Green channel 2 hours, Yellow channel 12 hours, Red channel 48 hours; Khalifa Port Green channel 3 hours, Yellow channel 18 hours, Red channel 60 hours; JNPT Green channel 6 hours, Yellow channel 48 hours, Red channel 96 hours. When SLA thresholds are breached, the system must auto-escalate to the assigned customs broker supervisor and entity operations manager via in-app notifications and email alerts in the recipient's preferred language (English or Arabic). The system must generate weekly SLA compliance reports per entity showing clearance performance trends.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R015	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Import Amendment and Cancellation Handling	The system must support post-submission amendments to import declarations when errors are identified or cargo details change. Amendment workflows must comply with the specific regulatory procedures at each port: Qatar Customs amendment request through ASYCUDA World, UAE Customs amendment through Mirsal 2 or Dhabi portal, KSA ZATCA amendment through FASAH platform, and India JNPT amendment through ICEGATE. The system must track amendment reasons categorized by type (quantity change, value correction, HS code reclassification, consignee change), maintain a complete version history of each declaration showing before and after states, and calculate any differential duty arising from amendments. Cancellation of declarations must require multi-level approval within the entity hierarchy and generate appropriate reversal entries for any payments already made. All amendment and cancellation records must be retained for the statutory audit period of each jurisdiction (minimum 5 years for GCC, 7 years for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R016	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Offline Import Clearance Capability	The system must support offline operation for import clearance workflows when internet connectivity to customs authority systems is unavailable. In offline mode, users must be able to prepare and validate import declarations locally, attach supporting documents, calculate estimated duties using the locally cached tariff database, and queue submissions for automatic transmission when connectivity is restored. The offline data store must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 encryption to protect sensitive commercial and customs data. Upon reconnection, the system must synchronize queued declarations in chronological order, handle any conflicts arising from tariff rate changes during the offline period, and notify users of any declarations that failed submission with specific error details. Offline capability must be tested and validated for connectivity outages lasting up to 72 hours across all four port jurisdictions.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-001-R017	FEAT-027-1-001	MOD-027	Preferential Origin and FTA Certificate Management	The system must manage certificates of origin for preferential tariff treatment under applicable free trade agreements. For GCC ports, the system must validate and apply preferential rates under GCC internal trade arrangements, GCC-Singapore FTA, and other bilateral agreements. For India JNPT, the system must handle CEPA certificates for UAE-origin goods, certificates under SAFTA for South Asian trade, and AIFTA certificates for ASEAN-origin goods. The system must verify certificate authenticity fields including issuing authority, certificate number, validity dates, and origin criteria (wholly obtained, substantial transformation, or regional value content). When preferential rates are applied, the system must flag any anomalies such as transshipment through non-FTA countries that could invalidate preferential treatment. Certificate images must be stored and linked to the corresponding customs declaration for audit purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R001	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	AI-Powered Export Declaration Generation	The system must automatically generate export customs declarations by extracting data from commercial invoices, proforma invoices, sales contracts, and letters of credit using AI document processing. For Hamad Port (QAHMD) exports, the system must generate Qatar Customs export declarations in ASYCUDA World format. For Jebel Ali (AEJEA) exports, Dubai Customs Mirsal 2 export declarations must be produced. For Khalifa Port (AEKLP), Abu Dhabi Customs export forms are required. For JNPT (INNSA), Indian Shipping Bills must be generated in ICEGATE-compatible format. The AI must auto-populate HS codes, FOB values, INCOTERMS (EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP per Incoterms 2020), and container details from source documents with at least 93% accuracy.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R002	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Control and Sanctions Screening	The system must screen all export shipments against applicable export control lists and international sanctions regimes before allowing declaration submission. Screening must cover the Qatar National Export Control List, UAE Strategic Goods List, KSA dual-use goods regulations under ZATCA, Indian SCOMET (Special Chemicals Organisms Materials Equipment and Technologies) list, and international sanctions lists including UN Security Council consolidated list, US OFAC SDN list, and EU consolidated sanctions list. The system must perform fuzzy matching on consignee names, end-user names, and intermediary entities with configurable match threshold (default 85%). Any potential matches must block export filing and trigger a compliance review workflow requiring sign-off from the entity's designated export control officer before proceeding or escalating.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R007	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	OAuth2 SSO Google and Microsoft	Social login via OAuth2: Google Workspace and Microsoft 365. On callback: match by email, link to existing account or auto-provision. Store provider and provider_user_id. Scopes: email and profile only. No posting permissions.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R003	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Electronic Filing Integration with Customs Systems	The system must electronically submit export declarations to the respective customs authority systems through certified API integrations or approved EDI channels. For Qatar, direct integration with ASYCUDA World via XML messaging must be supported. For UAE, integration with Dubai Trade single window platform for Jebel Ali and Maqta Gateway for Khalifa Port is required. For KSA, integration with ZATCA FASAH single window platform must be implemented. For India, ICEGATE EDI integration for electronic Shipping Bill filing at JNPT must be supported. Each integration must handle authentication, message queuing, acknowledgment processing, and error handling. The system must retry failed submissions up to 3 times with exponential backoff and alert operations staff if all retries fail. Submission timestamps must be recorded in UTC with local timezone display.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R004	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Incentive and Drawback Tracking	The system must track and manage export incentive claims and duty drawback entitlements for eligible export shipments. For Indian exports through JNPT, the system must support Duty Drawback scheme claims under Section 74 and Section 75 of the Customs Act 1962, RoDTEP (Remission of Duties and Taxes on Exported Products) claims, and MEIS/SEIS scheme claims where applicable. For GCC exports, the system must track re-export duty refund eligibility for goods originally imported with duty payment. The system must calculate drawback amounts based on the All Industry Rates or Brand Rate as applicable, link export declarations to original import declarations for re-export scenarios, and generate claim submission files in the format required by each authority. Drawback receivables must be tracked in the local currency of the exporting entity (QAR, AED, SAR, or INR) with aging reports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R005	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Let Export Order and Shipping Bill Processing	The system must manage the export customs clearance workflow from Shipping Bill or export declaration submission through to Let Export Order (LEO) issuance. For JNPT exports, the system must track the progression through stages: Shipping Bill filing, customs officer assignment, document verification, examination order (if applicable), Let Export Order, and container handover to terminal. For GCC port exports, equivalent stages must be tracked: declaration submission, assessment, examination (if flagged), release for export, and gate-out confirmation. The system must auto-capture LEO numbers and dates from customs system responses, update the shipment status in real-time, and notify the shipping line and freight forwarder of export clearance completion. Container stuffing reports and ARE-1 forms for excisable goods in India must be managed within the workflow.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R006	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Documentation Package Assembly	The system must automatically assemble complete export documentation packages tailored to the destination country requirements and the applicable INCOTERMS. Standard export document sets must include: commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or sea waybill, certificate of origin (preferential or non-preferential), insurance certificate (for CIF/CIP terms), inspection certificates, and fumigation certificates where required. For exports to EU destinations, the system must include EUR.1 movement certificates or origin declarations. For exports to countries requiring legalized or attested documents, the system must track Chamber of Commerce attestation, Ministry of Foreign Affairs legalization, and embassy attestation status. Document templates must support dual-language output (English/Arabic for GCC-origin exports) with proper RTL formatting for Arabic content sections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R007	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Valuation and FOB Verification	The system must validate export declaration values against commercial documentation and flag discrepancies for review. FOB (Free on Board) values must be calculated from the commercial invoice value adjusted for the applicable INCOTERMS. When the invoice term is CIF or CFR, the system must subtract freight and insurance components to derive FOB value. When the term is EXW, the system must add inland transport and port handling charges to derive FOB. The system must maintain historical export value databases per commodity per destination to enable AI-powered anomaly detection, flagging declarations where the declared value deviates by more than 15% from the rolling 90-day average for similar goods. All values must be captured in both the transaction currency and converted to the local reporting currency using the prevailing central bank exchange rate.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R008	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Permit and NOC Management	The system must verify that all required export permits and No Objection Certificates (NOCs) are valid before allowing export declaration filing. For Qatar, the system must check Ministry of Commerce and Industry export permits and CICPA clearances for controlled items. For UAE, export permits from the Ministry of Economy and relevant free zone authority NOCs must be validated. For KSA, ZATCA export permits and Ministry of Commerce licenses must be verified. For India, DGFT (Directorate General of Foreign Trade) export licenses, RBI approvals for high-value exports, and Wildlife Trade Control permits for CITES-listed goods must be checked. The system must maintain a permit registry per entity showing permit type, issuing authority, validity period, permitted commodities, and quantity limits. Permits nearing exhaustion of quantity limits must trigger alerts at 80% utilization threshold.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R009	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Container Packing and Seal Verification	The system must record and verify container packing details and seal numbers as part of the export customs filing process. For each export container, the system must capture container number (validated against ISO 6346 format including check digit verification), container type and size (20GP, 40GP, 40HC, 45HC, 20RF, 40RF, 20OT, 40OT, 20FR, 40FR), gross weight, net weight, tare weight, number of packages, and customs seal number. For Hamad Port and Jebel Ali, electronic seal (e-seal) tracking numbers must be recorded where applicable. The system must validate that total declared package count and weight across all containers matches the commercial invoice and packing list totals, flagging any discrepancy exceeding 2% for weight or any count mismatch. Seal numbers must be unique within each export declaration and cross-checked against port terminal records.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R008	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Session Management	JWT access token (15 min TTL) + refresh token (30 days, httpOnly cookie). Refresh rotation on each use. Maintain active sessions table: device name, IP, last_seen, user-agent. User can view and revoke individual sessions. Force-logout all sessions option. On password change: invalidate all refresh tokens.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R010	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Declaration Amendment Workflow	The system must support amendments to export declarations after submission but before vessel departure, following the regulatory amendment procedures at each port. Amendment types must include: quantity changes, value corrections, container number updates, consignee changes, and HS code modifications. For each amendment, the system must capture the reason for change, original values, amended values, and supporting documentation. Qatar Customs and UAE Customs amendments must be filed electronically through the same single window platforms used for original submission. For JNPT, amendments must follow the ICEGATE amendment module procedures. The system must enforce a maker-checker workflow where the preparer cannot approve their own amendment. All amendment audit trails must be retained for the statutory period (5 years GCC, 7 years India) and must be available for customs audit in both English and Arabic.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R011	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Multi-Entity Export Consolidation Rules	The system must enforce strict entity segregation in export customs filings while supporting consolidated shipment scenarios where multiple group entities may have goods in the same container. Each export declaration must be filed under the correct legal entity with its own customs registration number, trade license, and authorized signatory. When a single container carries goods from multiple group entities (co-loading), the system must generate separate export declarations per entity and link them via a common container reference. The system must prevent users from viewing or modifying export declarations belonging to other entities unless explicitly granted cross-entity access permissions. Entity-specific customs registration details including Qatar Customs Code, UAE Customs Client Code, KSA ZATCA registration, and India IEC must be auto-populated based on the filing entity selection.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R012	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Compliance Reporting and Analytics	The system must generate regulatory compliance reports for export activities per entity and per jurisdiction. Monthly export statistics reports must be produced showing total export value by HS chapter, destination country, and port of loading in the entity's base currency. For UAE entities, the system must generate Federal Customs Authority statistical returns. For Qatar entities, Qatar Customs statistical submissions must be auto-generated. For Indian entities, the system must produce data for DGFT annual returns and RBI reporting on foreign exchange realization. The system must maintain a compliance scorecard per entity tracking metrics such as declaration accuracy rate, amendment frequency, export control screening hit rate, and average clearance processing time. Reports must be available in PDF and Excel formats with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) for GCC entities.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R013	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Dangerous Goods Export Declaration Handling	The system must handle specialized export declaration requirements for dangerous goods containers in compliance with IMDG Code and local port authority regulations. For each DG export, the system must capture UN number, proper shipping name, class and division, packing group, marine pollutant status, flashpoint (where applicable), and Emergency Schedule (EmS) number. The system must validate that the declared DG information matches the DG declaration (IMO Dangerous Goods Form) and the container packing certificate. For Hamad Port, Qatar General Authority of Customs DG-specific fields must be populated. For Jebel Ali, Dubai Municipality and Dubai Customs DG approval references must be included. For JNPT, Petroleum and Explosives Safety Organisation (PESO) approvals and Chief Controller of Explosives certificates must be verified. The system must block export filing if required DG documentation is missing or expired.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R014	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Filing Deadline Management	The system must track and enforce export declaration filing deadlines relative to vessel estimated time of departure (ETD). The system must apply jurisdiction-specific cutoff rules: Qatar Customs requires export declarations filed at least 24 hours before vessel ETD; UAE Customs at Jebel Ali requires filing before the terminal's export receiving cutoff (typically 24-48 hours before ETD); Khalifa Port follows Abu Dhabi Customs cutoff schedules; JNPT requires Shipping Bill filing and Let Export Order before the shipping line's container yard cutoff. The system must display countdown timers for each pending export shipment showing time remaining until filing deadline, with escalating alerts at 48 hours, 24 hours, and 6 hours before deadline. Late filing risks must be highlighted with estimated financial impact including potential vessel rollover costs. Filing deadlines must be displayed in the user's local timezone with Hijri date equivalents for GCC ports.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R015	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Certificate of Origin Issuance Workflow	The system must manage the application, issuance, and tracking of certificates of origin for export shipments. The system must support both preferential certificates of origin (under applicable FTAs such as India-UAE CEPA, GCC internal trade, and Pan-Arab Free Trade Area agreements) and non-preferential certificates. For GCC-based entities, applications must be routed to the relevant Chamber of Commerce (Qatar Chamber, Dubai Chamber, Abu Dhabi Chamber, or Riyadh Chamber). For Indian entities, applications must be filed with the relevant Export Inspection Council or authorized agency. The system must auto-populate certificate applications from export declaration data, track application status, store issued certificate images, and link certificates to corresponding export declarations. For electronic certificates of origin (eCO), the system must transmit data in the format required by each issuing authority.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R016	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Offline Export Declaration Preparation	The system must allow users to prepare, validate, and queue export declarations for submission while operating in offline mode without connectivity to customs authority platforms. In offline mode, the system must provide access to the locally cached tariff database, export control screening lists (cached with maximum 24-hour staleness), customer and consignee master data, and HS code lookup functionality. All export declaration validations except customs system-specific checks must be executable offline. The system must clearly indicate which declarations are queued for submission and display a prominent offline status indicator in both English and Arabic. Upon reconnection, the system must submit queued declarations in chronological order, handling any customs system rejections by routing failed declarations back to the user with specific error details. Offline-prepared declarations must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 to protect commercially sensitive export data.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-002-R017	FEAT-027-1-002	MOD-027	Export Customs Audit Trail and Record Retention	The system must maintain comprehensive audit trails for all export customs filing activities to support regulatory audits and investigations. Every action on an export declaration must be logged including creation, modification, submission, amendment, cancellation, and document attachment with the user identity, timestamp (UTC and local), IP address, and entity context. The system must support customs authority audit requests by providing filtered export records by date range, HS code, destination country, consignee, or value range. Audit data must be exportable in structured formats (CSV, JSON, XML) compatible with customs authority audit tools. Records must be retained for the mandatory statutory period: 5 years from date of export for Qatar and UAE, 5 years for KSA per ZATCA regulations, and 7 years for India per Customs Act requirements. Archived records must remain searchable and retrievable within 24 hours of an audit request.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R001	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Customs Transit Declaration Generation	The system must generate customs transit declarations for containers moving between ports or customs zones without entering domestic commerce. For GCC internal transit (e.g., Jebel Ali to Hamad Port via road, or Khalifa Port to KSA border), the system must generate GCC Common Transit Document declarations. For transit through India (e.g., transshipment at JNPT), the system must generate Indian Customs Transit declarations under the Customs Act provisions for goods in transit. Transit declarations must include origin port, destination port, transit route, estimated transit duration, container details (number, type, seal), goods description with HS codes, and value declaration. The system must support multi-modal transit combining sea and road segments and track each segment independently with separate customs references.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R002	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Bond and Guarantee Management	The system must manage customs transit bonds and bank guarantees required to secure duty-suspended movement of goods. For each transit movement, the system must calculate the bond amount based on the potential duty liability of the goods being transited, applying the destination country's tariff rates. The system must maintain a bond register per entity showing bond type (single transit bond, comprehensive guarantee, or customs deposit), issuing bank, bond reference number, face value, utilized amount, and available balance. For GCC transit, bonds must be denominated in the currency of the customs authority issuing the transit permit (QAR, AED, or SAR). For Indian transit, bonds must be in INR. The system must automatically check available bond capacity before allowing transit declaration submission and alert operations when bond utilization exceeds 75% of face value, enabling timely bond top-up or renewal.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R003	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transshipment Container Tracking	The system must track containers undergoing transshipment at hub ports (primarily Jebel Ali and Hamad Port) from arrival on the inbound vessel through to loading on the outbound vessel. The system must capture inbound vessel details, container discharge confirmation, storage location within the terminal, planned outbound vessel and voyage, and actual loading confirmation. For Jebel Ali transshipment operations, integration with DP World terminal operating system is required to receive real-time container movement updates. For Hamad Port, integration with QTerminals systems must be supported. The system must flag containers at risk of missing their planned outbound connection when the inbound vessel delay exceeds a configurable threshold (default 12 hours) and suggest alternative outbound vessel options. Transshipment containers must be tracked separately from import and export containers in all reporting and dashboard views.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R004	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Re-Export Declaration and Workflow Management	The system must manage the complete re-export workflow for goods originally imported into a customs territory and subsequently exported without substantial transformation. The system must link re-export declarations to the original import declarations to establish the chain of custody and calculate any duty refund eligibility. For UAE re-exports (a major trade flow through Jebel Ali), the system must generate re-export declarations referencing the original import declaration number and customs duty receipt. For Qatar re-exports from free zones, duty-suspended re-export procedures must be followed with appropriate QFZ Authority approvals. For KSA re-exports, ZATCA re-export declaration formats must be supported. The system must validate that re-exported quantities do not exceed originally imported quantities per HS code and container, accounting for any portions already cleared for domestic consumption.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R005	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Temporary Admission (ATA Carnet) Processing	The system must support temporary admission procedures for goods entering a customs territory for a defined period with re-export obligation, including ATA Carnet processing. The system must record the temporary admission permit details including admission date, permitted duration (typically 6-12 months, jurisdiction-dependent), goods description, declared value, and duty security (bond or deposit). The system must track the re-export deadline for each temporarily admitted consignment and generate escalating alerts at 90 days, 60 days, 30 days, and 7 days before the re-export deadline. If the deadline is missed, the system must calculate the full import duty liability and generate a duty demand notice. For GCC jurisdictions, temporary admission under the GCC Common Customs Law provisions must be supported. For India, the system must handle temporary imports under Customs Notification provisions with appropriate re-export bonds in INR.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R006	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	GCC Inter-State Transit Movement Tracking	The system must track container movements between GCC member states (Qatar, UAE, KSA) under the GCC Common Customs Transit regime. Each inter-state movement must be recorded with the departure customs post, arrival customs post, transit route, escort requirements (if applicable for sensitive goods), and GPS tracking reference where available. The system must generate the required GCC transit documents including the T1 transit declaration equivalent and manifest. Border crossing events must be logged with timestamps as containers pass through customs checkpoints such as Abu Samra (Qatar-KSA border), Al Ghuwaifat (UAE-KSA border), and Al Batha (UAE-Oman-KSA). The system must reconcile departure and arrival records within a configurable tolerance window (default 48 hours beyond estimated transit time) and escalate unreconciled movements that may indicate diversion or smuggling.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-001-R010	FEAT-000-1-001	\N	Passwordless Magic Link Login	Optional: user requests magic link via email. Link contains signed JWT (10 min TTL, single-use). On click: verify, create session, redirect to dashboard. Auto-expire on use. Rate-limit: 3 magic links per email per hour.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R007	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Seal Integrity Verification	The system must manage seal integrity verification throughout the transit movement lifecycle. At each transit checkpoint (departure, intermediate, and arrival), the system must record seal inspection results including seal number verification against the transit declaration, physical seal condition (intact, damaged, broken, replaced), and inspector identity. If a seal is found broken or tampered with during transit, the system must trigger an immediate alert to the transit bond holder, the departure customs authority, and the arrival customs authority. A replacement seal procedure must be initiated requiring supervisor authorization and photographic evidence. The system must maintain a complete seal history per container per transit movement showing all inspection events chronologically. For electronic seals (e-seals) used on high-value or sensitive cargo, the system must integrate with e-seal tracking platforms to provide real-time tamper alerts during GCC road transit segments.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R008	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Free Zone to Domestic Market Transfer Procedures	The system must manage the customs procedures for transferring goods from free zone storage to the domestic customs territory (duty area) for local consumption. This transfer triggers full import duty and tax liability. For UAE free zones (JAFZA at Jebel Ali, KIZAD at Khalifa Port), the system must generate import declarations for goods exiting the free zone into UAE domestic territory, applying the UAE customs tariff rates on the declared value. For Qatar Free Zones, the system must handle QFZ Authority exit permits and Qatar Customs import assessment. For KSA special economic zones, ZATCA import declaration for zone-to-domestic transfers must be generated. The system must recalculate duty liability based on current tariff rates at the time of transfer (not the original import date), apply any applicable depreciation allowances for goods stored for extended periods, and process duty payments in the respective local currency (AED, QAR, or SAR).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R009	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Time and Route Optimization AI	The system must provide AI-powered transit route optimization for inter-port and cross-border container movements. The AI engine must analyze historical transit time data across different routes, border crossing congestion patterns, customs processing times at various checkpoints, and seasonal variations to recommend the optimal transit route for each movement. For example, a container moving from Jebel Ali to Hamad Port could transit via road through Al Ghuwaifat-Abu Samra or via sea feeder, and the system must recommend the faster and more cost-effective option based on current conditions. Route recommendations must factor in transit bond costs, fuel costs, border crossing fees, and potential delay penalties. The system must present recommendations in both English and Arabic, showing estimated transit time, cost breakdown in the relevant currencies (AED, QAR, SAR), and risk assessment for each route option.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R010	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Re-Export Duty Refund Claim Processing	The system must manage duty refund claims for re-exported goods where import duties were paid upon original entry. The system must link the re-export declaration to the original import declaration and customs duty payment receipt, verify that the goods being re-exported match the originally imported goods (by HS code, quantity, and description), and calculate the eligible refund amount based on jurisdiction-specific refund rules. For UAE, the system must file refund claims with Dubai Customs or Abu Dhabi Customs within the permitted refund period (typically 12 months from original import). For Qatar, refund claims must be submitted to Qatar Customs with supporting documentation. For KSA, ZATCA refund procedures must be followed. The refund amount must account for any depreciation deductions applied by customs authorities and must be tracked as a receivable in the entity's base currency until actual refund receipt and bank reconciliation.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R011	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Insurance and Liability Tracking	The system must track insurance coverage and liability allocation for goods in customs transit. For each transit movement, the system must record the insurance policy reference, insurer name, coverage amount, deductible, and covered perils (all risks, named perils, or war and strikes). The system must verify that insurance coverage is adequate to satisfy customs authority requirements for transit bond reduction where applicable. For GCC road transit, minimum insurance coverage must meet the requirements of the transit country's customs regulations. The system must calculate and display the total liability exposure per transit movement including customs duty liability (secured by bond), cargo value liability (secured by insurance), and any additional liabilities such as transit country environmental or security bonds. All financial values must be displayed in the transaction currency and the entity's reporting currency, supporting QAR, AED, SAR, INR, and USD denominations.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R012	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transshipment Regulatory Compliance Validation	The system must validate that transshipment operations comply with the regulatory requirements of the hub port jurisdiction and any applicable international sanctions. For Jebel Ali hub transshipments, the system must verify that the cargo, origin, destination, and parties comply with UAE export control regulations even though the goods do not enter UAE domestic commerce. Similarly, for Hamad Port transshipments, Qatar customs regulations regarding transshipment of restricted goods must be validated. The system must screen transshipment consignments against the same export control and sanctions lists used for export declarations (UN, OFAC, EU sanctions). The system must flag transshipment routings that may constitute sanctioned trade patterns (e.g., goods of certain origin transshipping through Jebel Ali to a sanctioned destination). All screening results must be logged with the transshipment reference for audit purposes, and any hits must block the transshipment pending compliance officer review.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R013	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Multi-Leg Transit Declaration Chaining	The system must support complex transit movements involving multiple customs territories and transport legs by chaining individual transit declarations into a single end-to-end transit workflow. For example, a container originating from JNPT transshipping at Jebel Ali then transiting by road to Hamad Port requires three separate customs interactions: Indian export, UAE transshipment, and Qatar transit/import. The system must create and link these declarations maintaining referential integrity across all legs. Each leg must be independently trackable with its own customs reference, bond allocation, and status, while the parent transit workflow provides a consolidated view of the entire movement. The system must automatically trigger the next leg's declaration preparation when the previous leg reaches a configurable trigger status (e.g., vessel departure for sea legs, gate-out for road legs). Arabic and English documentation must be generated per leg based on the jurisdiction requirements.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R014	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Movement Exception Handling	The system must handle exceptions and anomalies during transit movements including route deviations, delays beyond permitted transit time, seal discrepancies, and cargo quantity mismatches at arrival. For each exception type, the system must define configurable workflows: route deviation alerts must be sent to the transit bond holder and customs authorities within 1 hour of detection; transit time overruns must trigger bond extension requests before the original transit permit expires; seal discrepancies must initiate inspection and reporting procedures. The system must support customs authority intervention actions such as transit suspension, diversion to examination, and bond forfeiture proceedings. Exception records must include the nature of the exception, detection method, corrective actions taken, financial impact assessment in local currency, and resolution status. All exception handling must comply with the GCC Common Customs Law transit exception procedures and Indian Customs transit regulations respectively.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R015	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit Document Archival and Retrieval	The system must archive all transit-related documents including transit declarations, bond certificates, seal records, tracking logs, and exception reports for the statutory retention period. For GCC transit movements, documents must be retained for a minimum of 5 years from the date of transit completion. For Indian transit movements, the retention period must be 7 years per Customs Act requirements. Archived documents must be indexed by transit declaration number, container number, origin, destination, date range, and entity for rapid retrieval. The archival system must support full-text search across document content in both English and Arabic. Documents must be stored in tamper-evident format with digital signatures to ensure integrity for customs audit purposes. The system must support bulk export of transit records for customs audit requests, generating compressed archives with manifest files listing all included documents and their metadata.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R016	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Offline Transit Declaration Capability	The system must support offline preparation and tracking of transit declarations for use during connectivity outages at border crossings and remote customs checkpoints. Border crossing points such as Abu Samra and Al Ghuwaifat may have intermittent connectivity, requiring the system to function with locally cached data. In offline mode, users must be able to record seal inspections, log border crossing timestamps, capture photographic evidence of container condition, and update transit status. All offline-captured data must be timestamped using the device's local clock and synchronized with the server upon reconnection, with conflict resolution applying a last-writer-wins strategy for status updates and an append-only strategy for inspection logs. The offline data store must be encrypted at rest and must handle synchronization of data accumulated over up to 48 hours of offline operation without data loss.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R017	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Hijri Calendar Transit Date Management	The system must fully support Hijri (Islamic) calendar dates alongside Gregorian dates for all transit document dates, bond validity periods, and deadline calculations. Transit permits issued by GCC customs authorities may specify validity periods in Hijri dates, and the system must accurately convert between Hijri and Gregorian calendars for deadline tracking and SLA calculations. The system must handle the Umm al-Qura calendar variant used in Saudi Arabia and the standard Hijri calendar used in Qatar and UAE. Date display preferences must be configurable per user, with options for Gregorian only, Hijri only, or dual display showing both calendars. All date input fields in transit-related forms must accept both Hijri and Gregorian input with automatic conversion. Transit deadline alerts and countdown timers must calculate correctly regardless of which calendar system the original deadline was specified in, accounting for the fact that Hijri months are 29-30 days.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-003-R018	FEAT-027-1-003	MOD-027	Transit and Re-Export KPI Dashboard	The system must provide a comprehensive KPI dashboard for transit and re-export operations across all entities and jurisdictions. Key metrics must include: average transit clearance time by route and port pair, transit bond utilization rate per entity, re-export volume and value trends (monthly and quarterly), duty refund claim success rate and average processing time, transshipment dwell time at hub ports, transit exception rate and resolution time, and seal integrity compliance rate. The dashboard must support drill-down from aggregate metrics to individual transit movements. Data must be filterable by entity, port, route, date range (supporting both Gregorian and Hijri), and commodity type. Financial KPIs must be displayed in the user's selected currency with multi-currency conversion using prevailing exchange rates. The dashboard must render correctly in both English and Arabic with full RTL layout support, and must function in a degraded mode when operating offline showing last-synced data with staleness indicators.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R001	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	GCC Common External Tariff Calculation Engine	The system must implement the GCC Common External Tariff (CET) rate structure as the base calculation for imports into Qatar, UAE, and KSA. The CET applies a standard 5% ad valorem duty on most goods, with specific exemptions and higher rates for goods competing with local production. The calculation engine must apply the correct CET rate based on the HS code at 8-digit level, referencing the GCC tariff schedule effective date. The engine must handle the CET exemption list including basic foodstuffs, medicines, and educational materials that carry 0% duty. For goods subject to national exceptions (where individual GCC states may apply rates different from CET), the system must apply the country-specific rate. All calculations must use the CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight) value as the duty base, converted to the local currency (QAR, AED, or SAR) at the customs exchange rate prevailing on the declaration date.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R002	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Indian Customs Duty Multi-Component Calculation	The system must calculate the multi-layered Indian customs duty structure for imports through JNPT. The calculation must include: Basic Customs Duty (BCD) per the First Schedule rates at HS 8-digit level, Social Welfare Surcharge (SWS) at 10% on BCD, Integrated GST (IGST) calculated on the assessable value plus BCD plus SWS, and GST Compensation Cess where applicable (e.g., on automobiles, tobacco, and aerated beverages). The system must correctly compute the cascading duty structure where each component builds upon previous components. For goods subject to anti-dumping duty or safeguard duty under Indian trade defense measures, these additional components must be calculated and added. The total landed cost calculation must include all duty components plus port handling and terminal charges. All calculations must be performed in INR with the exchange rate notified by CBIC on a fortnightly basis for converting foreign currency invoice values.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R003	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Customs Valuation Method Application	The system must apply the WTO Customs Valuation Agreement (based on GATT Article VII) methodology for determining the customs value of imported goods. The system must implement all six valuation methods in sequential hierarchy: (1) Transaction Value of imported goods, (2) Transaction Value of identical goods, (3) Transaction Value of similar goods, (4) Deductive Method, (5) Computed Method, and (6) Fall-back Method. For the primary transaction value method, the system must adjust the invoice price by adding dutiable additions (commissions, brokerage, container costs, royalties, assists) and deducting non-dutiable elements (post-importation transport, construction costs, duties). The system must flag cases where customs authorities may reject the declared transaction value, such as related-party transactions where the buyer-seller relationship may have influenced the price, or when the declared value falls below the customs reference price database for similar goods.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R004	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Multi-Currency CIF Value Computation	The system must compute CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight) values from commercial invoices denominated in any trade currency and convert to the duty-assessment currency. Supported invoice currencies must include at minimum: USD, EUR, GBP, JPY, CNY, INR, AED, QAR, SAR, KWD, BHD, OMR, SGD, KRW, and THB. The system must apply the official customs exchange rates published by: Qatar Central Bank for QAR conversions, UAE Central Bank for AED conversions, Saudi Arabian Monetary Authority (SAMA) for SAR conversions, and CBIC for INR conversions. When the invoice INCOTERMS is not CIF, the system must adjust to derive CIF value: for FOB terms, add estimated freight and insurance; for EXW terms, add inland transport, freight, and insurance; for CFR terms, add insurance only. Freight estimates must be sourced from the freight module or entered manually, and insurance must be calculated at the standard customs rate (typically 1.125% of CFR value for GCC, as notified for India).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R005	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Preferential Duty Rate Application Engine	The system must identify and apply preferential (reduced) duty rates under applicable free trade agreements and bilateral trade arrangements. For GCC imports, the system must apply zero-duty or reduced rates for intra-GCC trade where goods meet GCC origin rules, and preferential rates under the GCC-Singapore FTA and other GCC external agreements. For India JNPT imports, the system must apply preferential rates under India-UAE CEPA (effective May 2022), SAFTA (South Asian Free Trade Area), AIFTA (ASEAN-India FTA), India-Japan CEPA, India-Korea CEPA, and other notified FTAs. The system must verify eligibility by checking the certificate of origin against the FTA rules of origin requirements including product-specific rules, regional value content thresholds, and change in tariff classification criteria. When preferential rates are applied, the duty savings compared to MFN rates must be calculated and displayed for management reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R006	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Anti-Dumping and Trade Defense Duty Calculation	The system must calculate anti-dumping duties, countervailing duties, and safeguard duties imposed by customs authorities in each jurisdiction. The system must maintain a database of all active trade defense measures applicable in Qatar, UAE, KSA, and India, indexed by HS code and country of origin. For Indian imports, the system must apply anti-dumping duties notified by the Directorate General of Trade Remedies (DGTR) which may be specified as ad valorem percentages, specific amounts per unit, or as a reference price mechanism. For GCC jurisdictions, the system must apply any GCC-wide or national anti-dumping measures. The calculation engine must correctly identify the country of origin (not the country of export) for trade defense duty application, as goods transshipped through intermediate countries remain subject to anti-dumping duty based on manufacturing origin. Trade defense duty amounts must be calculated separately from basic customs duty and displayed as distinct line items in the duty assessment.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R007	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	VAT and GST Computation on Imports	The system must calculate Value Added Tax or Goods and Services Tax applicable on imports in each jurisdiction. For Qatar imports, VAT is not currently applicable but the system must support future VAT implementation with configurable rates per HS chapter. For UAE imports, 5% VAT must be calculated on the CIF value plus customs duty amount per Federal Tax Authority regulations, with exemptions for designated free zone imports. For KSA imports, 15% VAT must be calculated per ZATCA regulations on the duty-inclusive value. For India JNPT imports, Integrated GST (IGST) must be calculated at the applicable rate (0%, 5%, 12%, 18%, or 28%) based on the HS code classification, computed on the assessable value plus all customs duties plus Social Welfare Surcharge. The system must generate VAT/GST payment records that feed into the entity's periodic VAT/GST returns for input tax credit recovery where applicable.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R008	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Excise Tax and Selective Tax Calculation	The system must calculate excise taxes and selective taxes applicable to specific categories of imported goods. For UAE imports, the Excise Tax (Selective Tax) must be applied at 50% on carbonated beverages, 100% on tobacco products, 100% on energy drinks, and 100% on electronic smoking devices per Federal Tax Authority schedules. For KSA imports, similar selective tax rates under ZATCA regulations must be applied. For Qatar, excise duties on tobacco and applicable products must be calculated per Qatar Tax Authority rates. For India JNPT imports, GST Compensation Cess must be calculated on applicable goods (automobiles, tobacco, aerated waters, coal) at notified rates which vary by HS code. The calculation must correctly sequence excise tax computation in the overall duty cascade, as excise taxes may be calculated on the CIF value or on the duty-paid value depending on the jurisdiction. Results must be displayed in the entity's local currency with itemized breakdowns.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R009	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Duty Calculation Simulation and What-If Analysis	The system must provide a duty simulation tool allowing users to estimate total duty liability before committing to an import or export transaction. The simulator must accept inputs including HS code, goods description, country of origin, CIF value, currency, destination port, and applicable INCOTERMS, and produce a detailed duty breakdown showing each duty and tax component. Users must be able to run multiple scenarios simultaneously comparing duty costs across different destination ports (e.g., importing through Jebel Ali versus JNPT) or different origin countries (to evaluate FTA savings). The simulation must also support comparison of landing costs when importing as a specific entity versus another group entity, showing the financial impact of different customs registrations. Simulation results must be exportable as PDF reports with bilingual headers (English/Arabic) showing duty amounts in all relevant currencies (QAR, AED, SAR, INR, USD) for management decision-making.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R010	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Tariff Rate Effective Date Management	The system must manage tariff rate changes with proper effective date handling to ensure the correct duty rate is applied based on the customs declaration date. The tariff database must maintain historical rates with start and end dates, supporting the ability to calculate duties retrospectively for amendment scenarios. When a tariff rate change is notified, the system must allow pre-loading of new rates with a future effective date and automatic cutover at midnight on the effective date (in the relevant jurisdiction's local timezone). For India, mid-year tariff changes via Finance Act, Union Budget notifications, and ad hoc customs notifications must be supported with same-day loading capability. For GCC, CET amendments agreed by the GCC Customs Union committee must be applied across Qatar, UAE, and KSA entities simultaneously. The system must provide impact analysis reports showing how pending tariff changes will affect duty costs for open purchase orders and in-transit shipments, with financial impact calculated in each entity's base currency.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R011	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Customs Duty Payment Scheduling and Cash Flow Forecasting	The system must forecast customs duty payment obligations for upcoming shipments and generate cash flow projections to support treasury planning. The forecast must be based on confirmed purchase orders, in-transit shipments with known ETA, and historical import patterns analyzed by AI for recurring shipments. Duty payment projections must be generated per entity and per currency, showing expected payment dates aligned with vessel ETAs and port-specific clearance time estimates. For entities with customs duty deferral arrangements (e.g., UAE Customs deferred payment accounts, Indian AEO deferred duty payment facility), the system must calculate the deferred payment schedule and track due dates. The cash flow forecast must display amounts in the entity's base currency and be aggregated across entities for group-level treasury visibility in USD. Forecasts must be refreshable on demand and auto-updated when vessel schedule changes affect ETA projections.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R012	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	HS Code Classification AI Assistant	The system must provide an AI-powered HS code classification assistant that recommends the most appropriate tariff classification for imported and exported goods. The AI engine must analyze goods descriptions from commercial invoices, product specifications, material composition data, and product images to suggest HS codes at the 8-digit level with confidence scores. The system must present the top 3 classification suggestions with explanatory notes referencing the relevant General Rules of Interpretation (GRI), Section Notes, Chapter Notes, and Explanatory Notes of the Harmonized System. When the AI confidence is below 85%, the system must flag the classification for human expert review. The assistant must support queries in both English and Arabic, mapping Arabic goods descriptions to the English HS nomenclature. Classification decisions must be stored in a precedent database for consistency, and the system must detect when a new classification contradicts a previous ruling for the same product, requiring explicit override justification.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R013	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Duty Exemption and Concession Application	The system must apply duty exemptions and concessions for eligible imports based on the specific provisions of each jurisdiction's customs law. For Qatar, the system must apply exemptions for government imports, diplomatic imports, and goods imported under specific Qatar Investment Authority incentive schemes. For UAE, exemptions for goods imported into designated free zones, industrial inputs under specific customs tariff notifications, and GCC-origin goods must be handled. For KSA, ZATCA exemption notifications including industrial development exemptions and SAGIA (now MISA) investment license concessions must be applied. For India JNPT, the system must apply exemption notifications under Section 25 of the Customs Act, including project import concessions, concessional rates for actual users, and end-use based exemptions. Each exemption application must verify eligibility criteria, record the legal basis (notification number and date), and maintain audit documentation proving exemption eligibility.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R014	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Duty Calculation Audit Trail and Dispute Support	The system must maintain a complete and immutable audit trail for every duty calculation performed, supporting customs dispute resolution and post-clearance audit scenarios. Each calculation record must capture: the HS code applied, tariff database version used, exchange rate applied with source and date, CIF value computation breakdown, each duty and tax component with rate and base amount, any exemptions or preferential rates applied with legal basis, and the final total duty assessed. When a customs authority issues a revised assessment (higher duty demand or refund), the system must record the original assessment alongside the revised assessment with the differential amount. The system must support generation of duty calculation review submissions for customs appeals, formatted per the requirements of Qatar Customs Dispute Committee, UAE Customs Objection procedures, KSA ZATCA grievance mechanism, and Indian Customs Commissioner (Appeals). All monetary amounts must be stored in the assessment currency with cross-reference to USD equivalent for group reporting.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R015	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Country of Origin Determination Rules Engine	The system must implement rules of origin determination logic for both preferential and non-preferential origin purposes. The rules engine must evaluate whether goods qualify for preferential treatment under applicable FTAs by checking product-specific rules of origin which may include: change in tariff classification (CTC) at the chapter, heading, or subheading level; regional value content (RVC) thresholds (typically 35-40% for most FTAs); specific manufacturing process requirements; and cumulation provisions allowing inputs from FTA partner countries. For non-preferential origin, the system must apply the WCO Kyoto Convention rules for determining substantial transformation. The system must maintain the origin rules matrix for each active FTA relevant to the four operating jurisdictions, cross-referenced by HS code. When goods undergo processing in a free zone before import into the domestic territory, the system must evaluate whether the processing constitutes sufficient transformation to confer origin, or whether the goods retain their original country of origin for duty calculation purposes.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-000-1-002-R001	FEAT-000-1-002	\N	System Roles Definition	Built-in non-deletable roles: SUPER_ADMIN (full system access), COMPANY_ADMIN (full company access), FINANCE_MANAGER, OPERATIONS_MANAGER, SALES_MANAGER, CUSTOMER_SERVICE_AGENT, READONLY_VIEWER. Roles are hierarchical: COMPANY_ADMIN inherits all module manager permissions.	must_have	\N	\N	[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R016	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Duty Calculation Offline Mode	The system must support comprehensive duty and tax calculations while operating in offline mode without connectivity to central servers. The offline calculation engine must use a locally cached copy of the tariff database, exchange rate tables (with a maximum staleness of 24 hours at the time of going offline), FTA preferential rate schedules, and exemption notification registers. When calculating duties offline, the system must clearly display the effective date of the cached tariff data and exchange rates being used, with a warning indicator if the data is older than 24 hours. Offline calculation results must be flagged as provisional and automatically recalculated against the latest server-side data upon reconnection, with any differences highlighted for user review. The offline tariff cache must cover all four jurisdictions (Qatar, UAE, KSA, India) and must be compressed to minimize storage requirements on mobile devices. Cache synchronization must be incremental, downloading only changed tariff entries since the last sync.	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R017	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Landed Cost Total Calculation and Comparison	The system must calculate the total landed cost for imported goods incorporating all customs duties, taxes, port charges, and ancillary costs to provide a complete cost picture for procurement and accounting purposes. The landed cost must include: CIF value, basic customs duty, additional duties and surcharges, VAT/GST/IGST, excise or selective tax, anti-dumping or safeguard duties, customs broker fees, terminal handling charges, documentation fees, examination fees (if applicable), demurrage and detention charges, and inland transport to final destination. The system must support per-unit landed cost calculation by dividing total costs across declared units, packages, or container-level as selected by the user. Landed cost comparison reports must be available showing the same goods imported through different ports (e.g., Jebel Ali vs JNPT) or under different entities, with all costs normalized to USD for comparability. Reports must render in both English and Arabic with proper number formatting per locale (e.g., Indian numbering system lakhs/crores for INR amounts).	must_have			[]	pending
FEAT-027-1-004-R018	FEAT-027-1-004	MOD-027	Customs Duty Reconciliation with Financial Accounts	The system must reconcile customs duty calculations and payments with the enterprise financial accounting system to ensure accurate booking of import costs and tax credits. Each duty payment must generate accounting entries: customs duty expense debited to the relevant cost center or inventory account, VAT/GST input credit recorded as a recoverable tax asset, and bank account credited for the payment amount. The system must support multi-entity accounting where the importing entity, paying entity, and goods-receiving entity may differ within the group structure. For each entity, the duty reconciliation must operate in the entity's functional currency (QAR for Qatar, AED for UAE, SAR for KSA, INR for India) with foreign exchange differences on duty payments recognized as per the entity's FX accounting policy. Monthly reconciliation reports must compare the total duties assessed per customs declarations against the total duties posted in the general ledger, flagging any discrepancies exceeding a configurable threshold (default 0.5%) for investigation. The reconciliation process must account for deferred duty payments, provisional assessments pending finalization, and duty refund receivables.	must_have			[]	pending
